const CHAPTERS_SEARCH_DATA = [{"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Introduction", "title": "Introduction", "subtitle": "Introduction to Volume 1", "path": "Volume 1/Introduction/Introduction_explanation.html", "word_count": 27916, "read_time": "140 min read", "tags": ["Dialectical Method & Critique of Political Economy", "Historical Specificity of Capital"], "keywords": ["Dialectical Method in Economics", "Critique of Political Economy", "Abstract to Concrete Progression", "Appearance vs Essence", "Historical Specificity of Capital"], "text": "Introduction - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 27,753 words ⏱️ ~139 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER ANALYSIS: Ernest Mandel's Introduction to Capital , Volume I 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Mandel's Introduction is far more than a conventional preface. It is a systematic defence , reconstruction , and mapping of Karl Marx's Capital Volume I as a rigorously scientific project whose object is not \"the economy\" in general but one historically specific social form—the capitalist mode of production—and whose central achievement is the discovery of the mechanism of surplus-value. The chapter's core thesis unfolds along three interlocking axes. First , Capital is not \"pure\" economic theory but a scientific anatomy of a historically limited social organism, governed by its own laws of motion that cannot be subsumed under \"eternal\" economic laws. Second , those laws of motion have been strikingly confirmed by a century and a half of history, and today's Western world is closer to Marx's \"pure model\" of capitalism than was the world of 1867. Third , the famous \"breakdown theory\" ( Zusammenbruchstheorie ) is a misreading: capitalism's overthrow is not an automatic economic event but the product of conscious class struggle—a \"subjective factor\" that Marx did not systematically theorise and which his followers (Lenin, Trotsky, Luxemburg) had to develop. The chapter's central problem is simultaneously methodological and political . Methodologically, Mandel asks: what is the difference between the method of investigation and the method of exposition ? What does it mean that Capital begins with the commodity rather than with value as a concept? How does Marx's materialist dialectic, \"turned right side up again\" from Hegel, allow one to move from appearance to essence and back, while treating both as equally real ? Politically, Mandel asks: if Marx \"predicted the collapse of capitalism,\" why does capitalism survive? His answer—that between economic contradictions and collapse there is a necessary mediation in the development of working-class consciousness, organisation, and revolutionary capacity—reframes the entire question. The Introduction's intellectual objective, in short, is to establish Capital as the highest expression of the proletariat's class consciousness, a work whose scientific integrity is inseparable from (though not reducible to) its revolutionary intention. The chapter's broader framework is Ernest Mandel's position within the tradition of classical Marxism, itself distinctive. Mandel is writing in 1976 (the second half of the 20th century), a period of capitalist crisis—the Vietnam War, the collapse of Bretton Woods, worker insurgencies since 1968, ecology and energy crises, recurring recessions—in which the \"stone of wisdom\" claimed by bourgeois ideologues (Keynesianism, \"integration of the working class\") has visibly failed. So Mandel's Introduction is not a neutral academic exercise: it is a militant theoretical intervention on behalf of a specific reading of Marx—one that is dialectical, non-determinist, \"monistic\" in its adherence to the labour theory of value, and open to integrating the insights of Rosa Luxemburg, Rosdolsky, Hilferding, and the disputed question of the \"law of value\" in precapitalist and post-capitalist societies. The Introduction also functions as a prolegomenon to the three volumes: it explains why Volume I deals with production and not circulation; why it abstracts from competition and prices of production; why it analyses \"wage-labour\" but not \"wage fluctuations\"; and why the \"trade cycle\" and \"crises\" problems are reserved for later volumes. The thesis is threaded throughout eleven sections: (I) The Purpose of Capital; (II) The Method of Capital; (III) The Plan of Capital; (IV) The Plan of Volume I; (V) The Marxist Labour Theory of Value; (VI) Marx's Key Discovery: The Theory of Surplus-Value; (VII) Marx's Theory of Capital; (VIII) The Theory of Accumulation of Capital; (IX) The Theory of Wages; (X) The Theory of Money; and (XI) Capital and the Destiny of Capitalism. Together these form a single argument: that Capital is a scientific, historically grounded, internally coherent analysis of a mode of production whose fundamental crisis-proneness and tendency toward collapse remain operative today, and whose transcendence depends on the conscious action of the revolutionary subject it both presupposes and helps to constitute. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS I. THE PURPOSE OF CAPITAL: Against \"Eternal Laws,\" Toward Historically Relative Science Mandel opens with a striking historical claim: when Volume I of Capital was published (1867), capitalist industry was still \"an isolated island encircled by a sea of independent farmers and handicraftsmen\" covering even most of Europe. What Marx explained was not this empirical world but rather \"the ruthless and irresistible impulse to growth\" inherent in production for private profit and in the predominant use of profit for capital accumulation. Since Marx, that impulse has spread across the globe, deepening two tendencies simultaneously: the growth of material wealth and freed human possibilities on the one hand, and the polarisation of society into \"fewer and fewer owners of capital and more and more workers of hand and brain, forced to sell their labour-power to these owners.\" Concentration of wealth and power in a small number of giant industrial and financial corporations has brought with it \"an increasingly universal struggle between Capital and Labour.\" This opening frames the dialectical hinge of Mandel's entire argument. Periodically, the bourgeois class and its ideologues have believed they had found \"the stone of wisdom\"—the end of crises and socio-economic contradictions. Keynesian techniques and integration of the working class notwithstanding, for over a decade the system has appeared \"if anything more crisis-ridden than when Marx wrote Capital .\" The list of symptoms—from Vietnam to the turmoil of the world monetary system, from the upsurge of radical workers' struggles in Western Europe since 1968 to the rejection of bourgeois values by youth, from ecology and energy crises to recurrent economic recessions—supports the claim that \"capitalism's heyday is over.\" This leads to a pivot: Capital explains why these sharpening contradictions were as inevitable as capitalism's impetuous growth. Hence \"contrary to a generally accepted belief, Marx is much more an economist of the twentieth century than of the nineteenth. Today's Western world is much nearer to the 'pure' model of Capital than was the world in which it was composed.\" The argument thus immediately stakes a claim about Capital 's continued relevance—one Mandel will ultimately defend against all critiques of its \"inevitability\" and \"unscientific\" character. The decisive conceptual move in Section I is the denial of universal economic laws . Marx's fundamental aim was to \"lay bare the laws of motion which govern the origins, the rise, the development, the decline and the disappearance of a given social form of economic organization: the capitalist mode of production.\" He was not seeking universal laws—and indeed, one of Capital 's essential theses is that no such laws exist . The only exception is trivial: no society can consume more than it produces without reducing its stock of wealth. Otherwise, each specific social form of economic organization has its own specific economic laws . Capital limits itself to examining the laws of the capitalist mode of production. This is why Capital is \"not 'pure' economic theory at all.\" For Marx, \"pure\" economic theory—theory abstracted from a specific social structure—is impossible, analogous to \"pure\" anatomy abstracted from the specific species being examined. Mandel develops this anatomical analogy with care. Comparative anatomy is a useful branch of natural science, but it \"can be only a by-product of the development of the anatomical understanding of specific given species.\" Similarly, Marx's historical materialism does include comparative economic analysis—examining the evolution of human labour, productivity, social surplus product, and economic growth from slavery through feudalism to capitalism—but such comparison \"can result only from the analysis of specific modes of production.\" These cannot be \"superseded by or subsumed under 'eternal' economic laws.\" If one tries to find a \"basic common kernel in 'all' anatomy,\" one leaves anatomy for biology or biochemistry; likewise, if one tries to discover working hypotheses valid for \"all\" economic systems, one passes from economic theory to \"the science of social structures: historical materialism.\" This distinction between economic theory (specific to a mode of production) and historical materialism (the general science of social structures) is a foundational methodological claim that runs through the entire Introduction. The consequence, Mandel argues, is a doctrine of the \" relativity, social determination and historical limitation of all economic laws .\" Commodity production, market economy, and the distribution of social resources \"behind the back of the producers\"—these do not correspond to \"human nature,\" have not always existed, and will not always exist. Capital , explaining the origins of capitalism, points to its \"inevitable historical decline and fall.\" An economic theory based on the historical relativity of every economic system \"tactlessly reminds Messrs the capitalists, their hangers-on and their apologists that capitalism itself is a product of history. It will perish in due course as it once was born.\" But Capital does not deal exclusively with the capitalist mode of production. The crucial insight is that \"capitalist production is generalized commodity production.\" Generalized commodity production \"fully unfolds trends and contradictions which are latent in every one of its basic 'cells', the commodities.\" It is no accident that Marx starts Volume I neither with \"the capitalist mode of production\" nor with capital, nor with wage-labour, nor even with the relation between them—for \"it is impossible to analyse any of these basic concepts or categories... without a previous analysis of value, exchange-value and surplus-value.\" And these latter categories in turn hinge upon an analysis of the commodity and of commodity-producing labour. Just as surplus-value and capital emerge logically from value and exchange-value, so the capitalist mode of production emerges historically from the growth of commodity production: \"without simple commodity production no capitalism can come into existence.\" This is where Mandel advances one of his most contested arguments: that Capital contains analyses of simple commodity production, which existed \"in manifold ways for nearly 10,000 years before modern capitalism was born,\" but found its fullest flowering \"between the thirteenth and sixteenth centuries A.D. in the Low Countries, northern Italy, and later Britain (and to a lesser degree in Japan before the Meiji revolution).\" This view, originating with Engels and held by Rosa Luxemburg, is contested by Bogdanov , Rubin , Colletti , and Althusser . Mandel argues these objections rest on a \"double confusion.\" First, while it is true that capitalism alone implies generalized commodity production, it would be \"completely mistaken\" to consider Hellenistic slave society or the classical Islamic Empire—both with developed petty commodity production, money economy, and international trade—as \"ruled by the 'law of value'.\" In these precapitalist modes, commodity production is \"intertwined with, and in the last analysis subordinated to,\" non-capitalist organizations of production (first and foremost agriculture) that follow \"a different economic logic.\" However, this does not imply that in societies where petty commodity production has become the predominant mode , the laws governing commodity exchange and money circulation do not influence the economic dynamic—\"indeed, it is precisely the unfolding of the law of value which leads in such societies to the separation of the direct producers from their means of production.\" The birth-process of modern capitalism is influenced by a whole series of social and political developments that hasten, slow down, or divert it. Note: Mandel quotes Marx's 'Chapter 6' of Capital Volume 1 (in the Appendix): \"within certain limits both goods and money were circulated and hence there was a certain evolution of trade: this was the premiss and point of departure for the formation of capital and the capitalist mode of production.\" The second confusion: while \"fully-fledged 'economic accounting based upon quantities of socially equalized labour'\" comes into its own only under capitalism—and only as an objective economic law, not as conscious decisions of commodity owners—it does not follow that \"labour quantities accounting\" cannot begin to appear in precapitalist societies where commodity production is a regular institution. On the contrary, precisely where petty commodity production is largely developed but still intertwined with traditional \"natural\" forms (implying conscious allocations of resources through customs, habits, rites, religion, deliberation of elders, assemblies), the need for conscious accounting of \"labour quantities\" can and must appear \"to avoid basic injustices and inequalities in social organisations still based upon a high degree of social equality and coherence.\" Mandel claims to have proved this empirically (citing his Marxist Economic Theory , pp. 59–65). But this does not mean the law of value is a \"product of precapitalist history,\" nor that these societies were burdened with the \"manic pursuit of material rewards, and measurement of labour-time expenditure down to fractions of seconds\" characteristic of bourgeois society. It only means \"the embryonic forms of the 'law of value' can be discovered in the embryonic developments of commodity production, just as the 'elementary cell' of capital, the commodity, contains in an embryonic way all the inner qualities and contradictions of that social category.\" To deny this historical dimension, Mandel warns, \"is to transform the origins of capitalism into an insoluble mystery.\" Mandel then extends this argument dramatically: if Capital includes elements valid for \"the whole historical epoch encompassing economic organizations in which commodity production exists,\" then parts of Capital remain valid both into the past and into the future . Phenomena of commodity production obviously survive, at least partially, in post-revolutionary but not-yet-classless societies: \"the USSR and the People's Republics of Eastern Europe, China, North Vietnam, North Korea and Cuba.\" Capital , Mandel is careful to say, is \"no more a guide to understanding the laws of motion of these societies than it is a guide to understanding the laws of motion of developed late medieval society based upon petty commodity production.\" But it can tell us about \"the dynamics (and disintegrating logic) of commodity production and money economy in such non-capitalist societies, and the contradictions which these introduce into the specific and 'pure' laws of motion of the latter.\" This is a remarkable extension— Capital aids analysis of commodity production even where capital does not rule. The last major theme of Section I concerns the scientific status of Capital . Some Marxists—foremost Karl Korsch —have denied that Capital contains a science of the capitalist economy, viewing it \"essentially [as] an instrument for the revolutionary overthrow of capitalism by the proletariat.\" For them, as for \"so many bourgeois critics of Marx,\" it is impossible to separate the \"scientific\" from the \"revolutionary\" content, as Rudolf Hilferding tried to do. Mandel responds by invoking Marx's and Engels's distinction between utopian and scientific socialism. Marx remained a revolutionary his whole adult life after 1843, but he considered it essential to base socialism on a scientific foundation—the scientific analysis of the capitalist mode of production being \"the cornerstone of that foundation.\" The claim: Marx strove to analyse capitalism \"in an objective and strictly scientific way,\" precisely as a function of his revolutionary intention , not in contradiction to it. He did not simply vent \"aggressive hostility towards a particular form of economic organization\"—nor was he motivated by \"personal spite, material failure or psychotic imbalance.\" Marx sought \"objective laws of motion.\" And \"there was nobody—not even the typical bourgeois Spiesser—whom he despised more than the man with scientific pretensions who nevertheless deliberately twists empirical data or falsifies research results to suit some subjective purpose.\" Because Marx was convinced of the decisive importance of the proletariat's cause, he wanted \"not a flimsy platform of rhetorical invective or wishful thinking, but the rock-like foundation of scientific truth.\" This rebuttal sets up the entire scientific presentation Mandel will defend against Popperian and neo-Ricardian critics. II. THE METHOD OF CAPITAL: The Materialist Dialectic, Method of Investigation vs. Method of Exposition, and the Essence/Appearance Distinction Mandel's second section is his methodological charter. Marx left no doubt that he applied the materialist dialectic to economic problems—writing to Maurice Lachâtre (editor of the first French edition) that he was the first to apply this method to economic problems, and in his postface to the second German edition, specifying the dialectical method as the differentia specifica of Capital . When applied to economic problems, says Mandel, \"economic phenomena are not viewed separately from each other, by bits and pieces, but in their inner connection as an integrated totality, structured around, and by, a basic predominant mode of production.\" This totality is analysed in all its aspects and manifestations as \"determined by certain given laws of motion, which relate also to its origins and its inevitable disappearance.\" These laws of motion \"are discovered to be nothing but the unfolding of the inner contradictions of that structure, which define its very nature.\" The given economic structure is characterized simultaneously by \"the unity of these contradictions and by their struggle, both of which determine the constant changes which it undergoes.\" Mandel then draws the classic dialectical distinction between quantitative changes—adaptation, integration of reforms, self-defence (evolution)—and qualitative changes that \"by sudden leaps, produce a different structure, a new mode of production (revolution).\" This is the dialectical law of the transformation of quantity into quality applied to social forms. Marx opposes his dialectical method to Hegel's, though recognising his debt to a philosopher who, \"spurred on by the French Revolution, catapulted dialectical thought back into the modern world.\" Hegel's dialectic was idealist : the basic motion was the Absolute Idea, material reality only its outward appearance. For Marx, \"the dialectic is materialist, 'the ideal is nothing but the material world reflected in the mind of man, and translated into forms of thought'.\" The basic laws of motion of history \"are those of real men, themselves producing their own material existence in a given social framework.\" The development of thought corresponds \"in the final analysis\" to that basic movement, reflecting it \"through many mediations.\" Thus the scientific thought process through which Marx understood the capitalist mode of production was itself a product of that mode of production, of bourgeois society and its contradictions. Only secondarily can it be seen as a product of prior human sciences and ideologies: classical German philosophy, English political economy, French historiography and political science, pre-Marxian socialism. Only \"the growth of bourgeois society and its contradictions, above all the struggle between capital and labour,\" enabled Marx to \"assimilate, combine and transform these sciences in the specific way and the specific direction he did.\" Nonetheless, Mandel concedes, while the materialist dialectic is Hegel's idealist dialectic \"turned right side up again,\" both share common traits. Dialectics as the \"logic of motion\" presupposes that all motion—whether of nature, society, or human thought—adopts certain general forms. Engels and Lenin both saw in Capital Volume I's construction a striking application of this general dialectical method: Lenin wrote that although Marx never wrote his projected short treatise on dialectics, \"he had nevertheless left us Capital , which is the application of the materialist dialectic in the field of economic phenomena.\" The pivotal methodological distinction—one Mandel stresses repeatedly—is between the method of investigation and the method of exposition . Because Marx's dialectic is materialist, \"it does not start from intuition, preconceptions or mystifying schemes, but from a full assimilation of scientific data.\" Empirical facts must be gathered first; the given state of knowledge must be fully grasped; only then can a dialectical reorganisation of the material be undertaken. If successful, the result is a \"reproduction\" in man's thought of the material totality—the capitalist mode of production. This is a critical defence against the charge that Capital is speculative or Hegelian in the pejorative sense. Mandel then identifies the \"main danger for any scientist involved in the study of social phenomena\": taking anything for granted, \"problem-blindness.\" The distinction between appearance and essence —inherited from Hegel—\"is nothing but a constant attempt to pierce farther and farther through successive layers of phenomena, towards laws of motion which explain why these phenomena evolve in a certain direction and in certain ways.\" Marx quotes himself to Engels (27 June 1867): the vulgar economist is trapped in \"the direct form of manifestation of relations... not their inner connection\"—and \"if the latter were the case what need would there be of science?\" Constant searching for questions—\"calling into question!\"—where others see ready-made answers and vulgar \"evidence\": this is \"one of Marx's main merits as a revolutionary innovator in economic science.\" Crucially, however, Mandel insists that for Marx the materialist dialectician, the distinction between \"essence\" and \"appearance\" in no sense implies that \"appearance\" is less \"real\" than \"essence.\" Movements of value determine \"in the last analysis\" movements of prices; but Marx \"would have laughed at any 'Marxist' who suggested that prices were 'unreal', because in the last analysis determined by value movements.\" The distinction refers to different levels of determination —that is, to the process of cognition , not to different degrees of reality . To explain the capitalist mode of production in its totality, it is \"wholly insufficient to understand simply the 'basic essence', the 'law of value'.\" One must integrate essence and appearance \"through all their intermediate mediating links, to explain how and why a given 'essence' appears in given concrete forms and not in others.\" These appearances \"are neither accidental nor self-evident. They pose problems, they have to be explained in their turn.\" To deny the need to reintegrate essence and appearance is \"as un-dialectical and as mystifying as to accept 'appearances' as they are.\" This distinction between levels of determination and degrees of reality is the philosophical core of Mandel's method and is critical to his later refutation of Joan Robinson and others. Turning to the starting point of Capital , Mandel argues that the analysis begins with the \"basic unit (fundamental cell) of capitalist economic life, the commodity\"—a \"model application\" of the materialist dialectic. Marx does not start from a basic concept —value—but from an elementary material phenomenon —the commodity—which is the basis of capitalism as \"the only economic organization based upon generalized commodity production.\" Hence it is \"correct but incomplete, strictly speaking, to say that Marx's method consists of 'rising from the abstract to the concrete'.\" Rather, he \"starts from elements of the material concrete to go to the theoretical abstract, which helps him then to reproduce the concrete totality in his theoretical analysis.\" In its full richness, the concrete is always \"a combination of innumerable theoretical 'abstractions'.\" But the material concrete —real bourgeois society—\"exists before this whole scientific endeavour, determines it in the last instance, and remains a constant practical point of reference to test the validity of the theory.\" Only if the reproduction of the concrete totality in thought \"comes nearer to the real material totality is thought really scientific.\" Mandel reinforces this with a note on Lenin, who argued that thought proceeding \"from the concrete to the abstract ... does not get away from the truth but comes closer to it,\" and with D. I. Rosenberg's point that Marx's abstractions are themselves \"concrete\" in the sense that they are historically determined and not arbitrary. This is a major intervention against the abstract-concrete schema attributed to Marx by later readers (including the Grundrisse's own editorship). Mandel then confronts a central interpretive question about Volume I's structure: is the movement from commodity to capital accumulation a purely abstract synopsis of capitalism's essence, or a simplified reflection of real history —the real history that led from the first appearance of commodity production to full-scale capitalist production in the West, \"purified of all secondary and combined forms\"? His answer is dialectical: \"It is impossible to answer this question simply with a 'yes' or a 'no'.\" Commodities produced accidentally in precapitalist societies \"obviously cannot trigger off the striking and terrifying logic of the 'law of value' which Marx majestically unfolds in Capital .\" Commodity production as a basic and dominant feature of economic life presupposes capitalism . In that sense, \"the analysis of Volume 1 of Capital is logical (based upon dialectical logic) and not historical.\" But dialectics implies that every phenomenon has \"an origin and an end, that nothing is either eternal or finished once and for all.\" Hence \"the historical cell of capital is at the same time the key to the logical analysis of capital: phylogenesis and embryology cannot be completely separated.\" Within capital accumulation in contemporary everyday life, some aspects of primitive capital accumulation are reproduced—without which there would be no capitalist mode of production. So the logical analysis does reflect some basic trends of historical development after all. \"The simplest forms of appearance of the 'economic categories' ... are often also their primitive, that is their original, form.\" Mandel admits this interpretation is controversial, but insists it is difficult to deny that this unity of historical and logical analysis \"is the way in which Marx and Engels understood their own method.\" The section closes with a fierce polemical counterargument against those who attack the dialectical method as \"useless,\" \"metaphysical,\" or \"mystifying\" (Bernstein to Popper and contemporary academic economists). Mandel inverts the attack: \"The positivist narrowness of outlook of these critics themselves generally bears eloquent testimony to the contrary—that is, to the broad historical vision and the piercing lucidity which the dialectical method helped Marx to achieve.\" Thanks to the dialectic, Capital \"appears as a giant compared to any subsequent or contemporary work of economic analysis.\" It was never intended as a handbook for balance-of-payments deficits, nor as \"a learned, if somewhat trite, explanation of all the exciting happenings in the market place when Mr Smith finds no buyer for the last of his 1,000 tons of iron.\" It was intended as an explanation of \"what would happen to labour, machinery, technology, the size of enterprises, the social structure of the population, the discontinuity of economic growth, and the relations between workers and work, as the capitalist mode of production unfolded all its terrifying potential.\" It is precisely because of Marx's capacity to discover long-term laws of motion, \"irrespective of thousands of 'impurities' and of secondary aspects,\" that his predictions have been \"so strikingly confirmed by history\": laws of accumulation, technological progress, accelerated productivity and intensity of labour, growing concentration and centralization of capital, transformation of the majority of economically active people into sellers of labour-power, declining rate of profit, increased rate of surplus value, recurrent recessions, class struggle, and revolutionary attempts. Mandel quotes Leontief's famous tribute (\"an unsurpassed series of prognostications fulfilled, against which modern economic theory with all its refinements has little to show indeed\") as authoritative counter-evidence. He then refutes two standard critiques. The crude one (represented by Samuelson) reduces Marx's laws of motion to two \"misstated and oversimplified formulae\": \"progressive immiseration of the working class\" and \"ever-worsening economic crisis.\" Samuelson concludes \"it simply never took place\" for the first, and for the second claims Marx was right for thirty years and wrong for seventy. The sophisticated one (Popper) denies the very scientific nature of such laws, calling them \"unconditional historical prophecies\" to be distinguished from \"scientific predictions.\" Popper: \"Ordinary predictions in science are conditional. They assert that certain changes (say, of the temperature of water in a kettle) will be accompanied by other changes (say the boiling of the water).\" Popper denies the scientific nature of Capital by asserting that, unlike scientific theories, its hypotheses cannot be scientifically tested. Mandel's rejoinder is twofold. First, the materialist dialectic, \"as Lenin pointed out, requires constant verification through praxis to increase its cognition content.\" Second, and more devastatingly, Mandel offers a counterfactual test : it would be \"very easy to 'prove' Marx's analysis to have been wrong, if experience had shown\" that: (a) the more capitalist industry develops, the smaller and smaller the average factory becomes ; (b) the less it depends on new technology; (c) the more its capital is supplied by the workers themselves; (d) the more workers become owners of their factories; (e) the less the part of wages taken by consumer goods becomes (and the greater becomes the part used for buying the workers' own means of production); and (f) decades passed without economic fluctuations and with a \"full-scale disappearance of trade unions and employers' associations.\" Had these occurred, \"then one could indeed say that Capital was so much rubbish.\" But \"it is sufficient to compare the real history of the period since 1867 on the one hand with what Marx predicted it would be, and on the other with any such alternative 'laws of motion'.\" The verdict: Marx's theoretical achievement is \"remarkable indeed.\" Mandel sprinkles in an amusing aside: Pareto's \"critique\" of the labour theory of value, in which he posited a seamstress hiring her machine to prove the machine \"produced\" the surplus-value—which Mandel mocks as \"utterly absurd\" while showing Pareto was blind to its absurdity, \"all the more underlin[ing] the profundity of Marx's insight into the operations of capitalism.\" III. THE PLAN OF CAPITAL: The Architecture of a Work, Four Volumes, and the Concept of \"Capital in General\" The third section turns from method to textual history. Capital was \"not the result of spontaneous generation nor... the product of a sudden interest of Marx in economic problems.\" Marx had been a communist since the 1840s under the pressure of social problems (treatment of wood-thieves in the Rhine provinces, the Silesian textile workers' uprising, British strikes, French class struggle), and had turned toward economic studies. His first encounter with modern political economy—leaving its main results in the Economic and Philosophical Manuscripts , The Poverty of Philosophy , Wage Labour and Capital , and The Communist Manifesto —was interrupted by the 1848 revolutions. After counter-revolutionary reaction submerged Europe, Marx emigrated to London and had to struggle for a livelihood as a journalist. These pressures, plus émigré politics, delayed a systematic presentation of his economic theory \"for a whole decade.\" Only when, through Lassalle, a publisher pressed him, did Marx return to a full-scale encounter with Adam Smith, Malthus, Ricardo, J.-B. Say, Sismondi, Tooke, together with the British government Blue Books —\"an invaluable source of factual material about the conditions of British industry, trade, finance and working-class life.\" The systematic study, resumed around 1857, produced six basic works: (a) the Grundrisse , written 1857–8 (first rough draft of Capital , published posthumously); (b) the Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy , published 1859 (uncompleted); (c) the 1861–3 manuscripts (twenty-three notebooks, from which Kautsky extracted Theories of Surplus-Value , \"Volume 4 of Capital\"—encompassing only notebooks VI–XV; notebooks I–V deal with matters encompassed in Volume 1; notebooks XVI–XVIII with Volume 3; notebooks XIX–XXIII again with Volume 1, including lengthy treatment of the history of techniques and machinofacture); (d) a manuscript of 1864–5 mostly on matters taken up in Volume 3; (e) four manuscripts written 1865–70 from which Engels extracted most of Volume 2; and (f) the final version of Volume 1, written 1866–7. Mandel emphasises: of the six basic economic writings of the mature Marx, Volume 1 is the only one which the author completed and edited himself , and \"of which he even made available corrected editions in German and in French.\" Volumes 2 and 3 were \"posthumously and laboriously published by Marx's life-long friend Friedrich Engels.\" Theories of Surplus-Value was rearranged by Kautsky. The Grundrisse was presented to the public only in 1939. A considerable part of the 1861–3 manuscripts still remains unpublished. Mandel then notes the two most accurate scientific editions of Volume 1—MEW 23 (Institute for Marxism-Leninism of the SED) and the Lieber–Kautsky edition (Stuttgart, 1962)—both indicating variations across editions. Mandel says he counted at least 100 textual variations in the Lieber edition, \"some of which are important,\" and that the present translation was made from MEW 23. This textual-philological underpinning matters: the dialectical argument's rigour depends on textual fidelity. The initial plan of Capital (drawn up in 1857) was extraordinarily ambitious: it read as follows: Volume on Capital — (a) Capital in general: {1) Process of production of capital; 2) Process of circulation of capital; 3) Profit and interest}; (b) On competition; (c) On credit; (d) On joint-stock companies Volume on landed property Volume on wage labour Volume on the State Volume on international trade Volume on the world market and crises The 1865–6 version, however, falls into only four volumes: Volume 1: Process of production of capital; Volume 2: Process of circulation of capital; Volume 3: Forms of the process in its totality; Volume 4: History of the theory. Roman Rosdolsky, \"who has made the most extensive study to date of this problem,\" isolated \"no less than fourteen different versions of the plan for Capital \" between September 1857 and April 1868. Mandel identifies two questions raised by these changes. First: why did Marx modify the initial plan, and what implications do the modifications have for understanding Marx's method and the content of Capital ? Second: does the 1865–6 version imply that the four volumes represent the full—though, all save Volume 1, unedited—work as finally intended? What we call Capital today, Mandel explains, is \"the third attempt by Marx to present his views... in its totality.\" The first attempt—the Grundrisse (1857–8)—follows exactly the initial plan but stops at point 1(a)(3). The second attempt (1861–3) is still unpublished except for the part on Theories of Surplus-Value . The third attempt is the 1865–6 one. As early as January 1863, Marx had decided to deal with land rent as an element of distribution of total surplus-value among different sectors of the ruling classes. However, he still seemed to stick at that time to a separate volume on wage-labour, a separate volume on landed property, and separate volumes on credit, competition, and joint-stock companies. Mandel explains the logic of this plan: it implied \"the desire to deal with the basic social classes of bourgeois society in a separate way: first the industrial capitalists; then the landowners; finally the proletariat.\" It also implied the desire to \"separate sharply the problems of production of value, surplus-value and capital from the problems of capitalist competition, which can only be understood as arising out of processes of redistribution of previously produced surplus-value.\" But this original plan, though a \"necessary stepping stone,\" increasingly proved \"an obstacle to a rigorous and consistent expose of the laws of motion of that mode of production. It had therefore to be discarded in the end.\" The volume on wage-labour became integrated into Volume 1 (\"The Process of Production of Capital\"). It appeared impossible to deal with wage-labour separately from the production of surplus-value. (Marx probably intended to deal with wage fluctuations in Volume 6 on the world market and crises.) The volume on landed property became integrated, together with profit, interest, competition, and joint-stock companies, into the new Volume 3. Mandel draws an important conceptual distinction. Volumes 1 and 2 can be subsumed under \"Capital in General\"; only Volume 3, like the originally planned 4, 5, and 6 (never written), falls under \"many capitals.\" This distinction explains why certain problems—like the origin and mechanics of the \"trade cycle\" (over-production crises)— have no place in Volumes 1 and 2 . They can be dealt with only \"when one descends from the highest level of abstraction, where capital is dealt with in its global relationship to wage-labour, to an examination of the interactions of various capitals upon each other.\" This leads Mandel to identify a major methodological error: Rosa Luxemburg , \"because she did not take this specific structure of the successive volumes of Capital into account,\" was \"methodologically mistaken in accusing Marx of having constructed his reproduction schemes of Volume 2 without solving the 'realization problem' or without formulating a theory of crises.\" Mandel reserves the fuller argument for his introduction to Volume 2. A similar mistake is attributed to Joan Robinson in her Preface to the second edition of An Essay on Marxian Economics , where she constructs a contradiction between the assumptions on real wages of Volume 1 and Volume 3. In Volume 1, she says, Marx assumes rising labour productivity leads to rising rate of exploitation; in Volume 3, he assumes rising productivity could lead, through a stable rate of exploitation, to a rising rate of real wages and a declining rate of profit. Mandel's rebuttal: Robinson \"does not understand that Volumes 1 and 3 of Capital are at different levels of abstraction, deal with different questions, and make different assumptions in order to clarify the specific dynamics which allow answers to these questions.\" In Volume 1, Marx examines the relations between Capital and Labour in general , abstracting from competition among capitalists; he therefore assumes initially stable real subsistence wages \"in order to show through what mechanics surplus-value is produced, appropriated and increased by capital.\" In Volume 3, he examines the effects of competition upon distribution and redistribution of surplus-value, \"and therefore has to integrate into the analysis the effects of this competition on the rate of exploitation (for example in periods of boom, with a high level of employment).\" It is perfectly logical to abstract initially from fluctuations in the rate of profit and wages in Volume 1, and to assume initially a stable rate of exploitation in Volume 3, \"but subsequently to abandon these simplifying hypotheses (Volume 1, Chapter 17; Volume 3, Chapter 14).\" So contrary to Robinson's asserted contradiction, the two volumes operate at different levels of abstraction and pose different questions , requiring different assumptions. Mandel next considers whether Marx intended further volumes. It seems clear from remarks throughout the Volume 3 manuscript that Marx maintained his intention of completing Capital with volumes on the state, foreign trade, the world market, and crises, though he placed these problems \"clearly outside the final plan of Capital itself.\" Only when the unpublished 1861–3 manuscript becomes available will we know whether \"some rough draft of what he intended to develop in these three books does indeed exist somewhere,\" or whether it was intended as a completely new development of his study of bourgeois society. From the place of Volume 1 in the total final plan, Mandel draws an answer to two misconceptions. First: it is true that capitalists do not exchange the commodities they own on the basis of their value, whereas under petty commodity production exchange is roughly based on value. But it does not follow that Volume 1, which assumes exchange of commodities according to value, \"is concerned with pre-capitalist commodity production and exchange, and that only in Volume 3 do we start to examine what capitalist commodity circulation is all about.\" On the contrary, Marx abstracts from the problem of redistribution of surplus-value among competing capitalists—the equalization of the rate of profit—\"precisely in order to isolate and demonstrate the laws of capitalist commodity production and circulation in their 'purest', most fundamental way.\" Second: it is wrong to assume Volume 1 deals only with \"essence\" or \"abstractions\" whereas \"concrete\" capitalism is analysed only in Volume 3. \"Nothing could be more 'concrete' and closer to immediately perceived economic data ('appearances') than the analysis of the working day, of wages and of machinery in Volume 1.\" Commentators confuse \"the type of question solved in Volume 1 with the method of answering.\" Volume 1 abstracts from capitalist competition, from uneven and combined development, and therefore from prices of production and equalization of the rate of profit, and even more from market prices, \"in order to reveal the basic origin of surplus-value in the process of production, which is a process of consumption of labour-power by capital.\" But this problem is dealt with \"by a combination of theoretical insight and empirical verification, by a constant attempt to discover the mediating links between 'essence' and 'appearance', by a thorough analysis of how and why the 'essence' (the value of labour-power) is manifesting itself through the 'appearances' (the fluctuations of real wages).\" IV. THE PLAN OF VOLUME I: The Logical-Historical Unfolding of the Analysis Mandel now turns to Volume 1's internal architecture, which \"presents itself as a rigorously logical construction.\" He walks through the sequence: We start from the elementary form of capitalist wealth—the commodity—and its inner contradiction : the contradiction between use-value and exchange-value . Because it is produced by private labour , whose social character can no longer be recognized automatically, immediately and directly by society, the commodity can exist only together with a necessary corollary: money , a universal means of exchange. But the analysis of the circulation of commodities accompanied by circulation of money leads to the unfolding of the inner potentialities and contradictions of money: (a) the possibility of exchange-value embodied in money becoming an autonomous economic agent ; (b) of money appearing as starting and final point, and not simply intermediary, of a process of circulation; (c) of money bent upon accretion of money—that is, actually becoming capital . In precapitalist societies, \"capital appears outside the sphere of production, and hardly ever enters that sphere. It feeds parasitically upon the social surplus product produced and originally appropriated by non-capitalist classes.\" Here Marx comes to his central point. A basic difference between the capitalist and precapitalist modes of production is that under capitalism capital not only appropriates surplus-value; it produces surplus-value. Because he considered this fundamental to understanding all aspects of bourgeois society—\"incidentally, not only the economic but also the political\"—Marx starts Capital with a whole volume devoted to the process of production. For the capitalist process of production is at one and the same time a process of production of value, of surplus-value, of capital, and of production and constant reproduction of \"the basic antagonistic social relations: the relation between wage-labour and capital, the compulsion for the proletariat to sell its labour-power to the capitalists, the compulsion for the capitalists to accumulate capital and therefore to maximize the extortion of surplus-value from the workers.\" Volume 1 is \"centred around Marx's basic discovery, the explanation of the 'secret' of surplus-value.\" There exists one commodity— labour-power —whose use-value for the capitalist is its ability to produce new value larger than its own exchange-value . The \"process of production\" Marx analyses in Volume 1 is thus essentially the process of production of surplus-value . The production of surplus-value can be examined in more detail only if capital itself is subdivided into constant capital and variable capital . Constant capital represents \"that part of the wealth of the capitalist class with which it acquires and maintains a monopoly of property and access to the material means of production.\" Thereby it \"cuts the working class off from any possibility of producing its own livelihood in an independent way.\" It is a necessary precondition for the production of surplus-value, \"but it does not produce that surplus-value in and by itself.\" Only the labour-power of living labour produces additional value, including surplus-value. That is why Marx calls \"that portion of capital by which the capitalists buy the labour-power of the workers\" variable capital —\"for only that portion actually produces surplus-value.\" This provides a direct answer to the \"productivity of capital\" critique which Mandel later attacks. The next step in the analysis: the distinction between the production of absolute surplus-value and of relative surplus-value . Absolute surplus-value \"is produced by a lengthening of the working day beyond that number of hours during which the worker produces the value which is only the equivalent of his wages.\" Relative surplus-value \"is produced by increasing the productivity of labour in the wage-goods industry sector, which enables the worker to reproduce the equivalent of his wages in a shorter portion of the working day, thereby increasing surplus-value without a lengthening of the working day.\" Marx notes that while the production of absolute surplus-value predominated in the early centuries of the capitalist mode of production (in England, roughly speaking, between the sixteenth century and the first half of the nineteenth), the production of relative surplus-value \"becomes predominant once the logic of the industrial revolution (of the development of machinery) and the logic of the class struggle between labour and capital fully unfold themselves.\" A central section of Part Four of Volume 1 (\"The Production of Relative Surplus-Value\") is taken up by a lengthy and minute analysis of manufacture and of the modern factory (Chapters 14 and 15). Here the production of surplus-value takes on an important additional dimension. During the stage of manufacturing industry, capital exploits the fruits of an increase in the productivity of labour born from \"more and more advanced forms of the division of labour.\" But \"the technique of production remains fundamentally the same. Labour is subdivided in function of the subdivision of the final product produced by manufacture. But beyond these subdivisions no changes occur in the labour process.\" The main interest for the capitalist during the stage of manufacture is therefore \"the constant direct control of capital over labour in order to secure a maximum expenditure of surplus labour with a given level of technique.\" It is \"like a workhouse in which the workers lose their freedom to determine their own work rhythm, in which work becomes unfree, forced labour from that point of view also.\" Many initial manufacturing concerns were in fact literally workhouses, \"filled with labourers who to various degrees had lost their individual freedom.\" With the industrial revolution and the emergence of the modern factory, \"this process of the submission of labour to capital in the course of the process of production is rooted, not only in the hierarchical forms of labour organization, but in the very nature of the production process itself.\" Inasmuch as production becomes mechanized, it becomes reorganised around machinery. The work rhythm and work content of living labour are subordinated to \"the mechanical needs of machinery itself.\" Alienation of labour is no longer only alienation of the products of labour, \"but alienation of the forms and contents of the work itself\"—a key distinction between the worker's estrangement from what he produces and from how and what he does. The explosive potentialities of modern machinery are developed by Marx \"in three directions simultaneously.\" First: machines are capital's main weapon for subordinating labour to capital in the process of production. Second: machines are the main weapon for increasing the production of relative surplus-value , \"thereby relentlessly spurring on the process of accumulation of capital.\" Third: labour-saving machines are the main weapon for producing and reproducing the industrial 'reserve army of labour' , \"through which wages are kept fluctuating around the value of the commodity labour-power, and through which the appropriation of surplus-value is normally guaranteed to the capitalists.\" Marx therefore logically integrates the development of the class struggle into his analysis of the production of surplus-value, \"inasmuch as he sees that class struggle as originating in that process of production.\" The extortion of surplus-value from living labour means \"a struggle by the capitalists to lengthen the working day, to increase the work-load of the workers without increasing wages, to appropriate for capital all the benefits of increased productivity of labour.\" Conversely, the struggle against capitalist exploitation means, for the workers, \"a struggle to reduce the working day without any reduction of wages, a struggle for cuts in the work-load, a struggle for increased real wages.\" How the class struggle against the immediate aspects of exploitation transforms itself into a struggle for the overthrow of the system is briefly taken up in Part Eight. Part Seven deals basically with the accumulation of capital, \"the goal of the whole infernal logic which Marx has laid bare so far.\" Capital produces surplus-value which is largely transformed into additional capital, which then produces additional surplus-value, and so on, \"with all its subsequent contradictory effects for mankind.\" Mandel then presents an outline that \"subdividing Part One into its three constituent chapters, we can see how this flawless logic of the analysis unfolds and how it roughly corresponds to the historical process 'stripped of the historical form and diverting chance occurrences'.\" I. Starting point: elementary form of capitalist wealth: the commodity: (a) the commodity and the realization of its exchange-value, or the process of exchange; (b) the process of exchange and the means of exchange: money; (c) money, necessary mediator of the process of circulation of commodities. II. Money transforming itself into capital , i.e., value searching for an accretion of value, surplus-value; the nature of surplus-value. III. The production of surplus-value: absolute surplus-value. IV. The production of surplus-value: relative surplus-value (from manufacturing to the modern factory system). V. Relations between wages, productivity of labour and surplus-value; the rate of surplus-value. VI. How the value of labour-power is transformed into wages, their different forms and variations. VII./VIII. The accumulation of capital , i.e., capitalist wealth in its totality: its consequences for labour. The origins of capitalism (the 'primitive accumulation of capital'). At the end of Volume 1, \"we are back where we started from: capitalist wealth. But now we no longer understand it simply as a sum of 'elementary elements,' a mountain of commodities (although it is this mountain also!). We see it now also as the result of a gigantic process of value production, of surplus-value extraction, out of living labour; as a gigantic movement constantly revolutionizing the means of production, the organization of production, the labour process and the producers themselves.\" The formula \"capital-value in search of additional value\" is now understood as \"capital organizing a process of self-valorization ( Verwertung ), a process of constant searching for increases in its own value through the unity of the labour process and the process of production of increased value ( Einheit von Arbeitsprozess und Verwertungsprozess ).\" Thus we understand more fully why an analysis of capitalism has first to clarify everything which happens in the process of production . Mandel adds a translator's caveat of great significance. The Pelican Marx Library edition of the Grundrisse \"contains a grave and regrettable error of translation.\" Marx's concept of Verwertung (valorization, process of accretion of value) is translated throughout as \"realization of capital.\" Marx uses realization generally only in relation to the realization of the value of commodities (containing surplus-value). But this problem has its place in the realm of the circulation of commodities and capital, whereas \"the problem of valorization of capital (the problem of surplus-value or profit in relation to, or as a proportion of, capital) is a basic aspect of the capitalist process of production.\" This is a crucial conceptual distinction Mandel is defending: valorization belongs to production , realization belongs to circulation . Finally, Mandel takes up Marx's attitude toward technology. He was obviously more aware than any contemporary economist, sociologist, or philosopher of \"the long-range revolutionary effects of machinery upon all aspects of life in bourgeois society.\" His indictment of the inhuman results of the capitalist use of machinery cannot escape \"anyone who reads Chapters 10, 15 and 25 of Capital Volume 1 with a minimum of attention.\" But should we see in Marx \"a latter-day Luddite, a forerunner of the zero-growth prophets\"? Or was Marx—as some argue—\"a deep admirer of capitalist technology and put all his hopes in the long-run emancipatory effects of that technology, alone capable of reducing the unavoidable work-load and work-fatigue to which man is condemned?\" Mandel insists that \"Marx the dialectician, bent upon an all-sided analysis,\" avoids both pitfalls—\"the conservatively romantic as well as the inhumanly mechanistic one.\" In classic Grundrisse passages, Marx underlines \"the civilizing and progressive aspects of capitalism, its giant impulse to develop the social forces of production, its relentless search for new ways and means to economize on labour, for new needs and new sectors of mass production, which help to unfold man's unlimited possibilities.\" But simultaneously he shows how the specifically capitalist form of this development \"increases tenfold the inhuman potentiality of technology, machinery and exchange-value 'gone mad' (that is, becoming goals in themselves).\" Capitalism subordinates men to machines instead of using machines to liberate men from repetitive work. It subordinates all social activities to \"the imperatives of an incessant drive for individual enrichment in terms of money, instead of gearing social life to the development of rich individualities and their social relations.\" The contradiction between use-value and exchange-value, inherent in every commodity, \"fully unfolds itself in this contradictory nature of capitalist machinery.\" And—crucially—\"When capitalism is not overthrown once it has created the material and social preconditions for a classless society of associated producers, this contradiction implies the possibility of a steadily increasing transformation of the forces of production into forces of destruction, in the most literal sense of the word: not only forces of destruction of wealth (crises and wars), of human wealth and human happiness, but also forces of destruction of life tout court.\" This locates Marx squarely in the tradition of \"socialism or barbarism\"—a theme Mandel will develop at the end of the Introduction. V. THE MARXIST LABOUR THEORY OF VALUE: The Quantitative and Qualitative Dimensions, and the Triple Function of the Law of Value The fifth section is the theoretical core of Mandel's defence of Marx's value theory against its critics (Böhm-Bawerk, Pareto, Tugan-Baranovsky, Samuelson, Schumpeter, Robinson). Mandel asserts that \"no part of Marx's theory has been more assaulted in the academic world during the last seventy-five years than his theory of value. His bourgeois critics show a sharp class instinct here, for this theory is indeed the corner-stone of the whole system.\" But no contemporary intellectual endeavour \"has been so obviously based upon a basic misunderstanding as the repeated attacks on the Marxist labour theory of value.\" Marx's theory recognises two aspects of the problem of value: a quantitative and a qualitative one . Quantitatively: the value of a commodity is \"the quantity of simple labour (skilled labour being reduced to simple labour through a given coefficient) socially necessary for its production (that is, at a given average productivity of labour).\" Qualitatively: the value of a commodity is \"determined by abstract human labour\"—commodities produced by private labour become commensurate \"only inasmuch as society abstracts from the concrete and specific aspect of each individual private craft or branch of industry and equalizes all these labours as abstract social labour, regardless of the specific use-value of each commodity.\" Mandel then formulates the actual problem Marx's theory was designed to answer. Man must work to satisfy material needs, to \"produce his material life.\" The way labour is divided among branches of material production determines the extent to which needs can be fulfilled. Hence, given a set of needs, \"a rough equilibrium between needs and output requires a distribution of labour (of 'labour inputs') between these various branches of production in a given proportion, and in that only.\" In a primitive society or in a fully developed socialist one, \"this distribution of labour inputs occurs in a consciously planned way: in a primitive society, on the basis of habits, custom, tradition, magico-ritual processes, decisions by elders etc.; in a socialist one, on the basis of a democratic selection of priorities by the mass of the associated producers-consumers themselves.\" But under capitalism, \"where labour has become private labour, where products of labour are commodities produced independently from each other by thousands of independent firms, no conscious decision pre-establishes such an equilibrium of inputs of labour and socially recognized needs (under capitalism this implies, of course, that only those needs expressed through effective demand are socially recognized).\" Equilibrium is reached \"only accidentally, through the operation of blind market forces.\" Price fluctuations—\"to which academic economists remain glued—are in the most favourable hypothesis only signals which indicate whether this equilibrium is being shaken, by what pressure and in what direction. They do not explain what is being equilibrated and which is the driving force behind all these myriad fluctuations. It is precisely this question which Marx tried to answer with his perfected labour theory of value.\" From this, Mandel draws the crucial refutation of the standard critique. Contrary to what Böhm-Bawerk and so many others assumed, \"Marx never intended to explain short-term price fluctuations on the market with his theory of value.\" (Probably he intended to raise some problems of short-term price fluctuations in the never-written Volume 6.) Nor is it meaningful to speak of Capital Volume 1's value theory as \"a 'micro-economic theory' allegedly in contrast with the 'macro-economic' labour theory of value in Volume 3.\" What Marx tried to discover was \"a hidden key behind price fluctuations, the atoms inside the molecule so to speak. He moved the whole economic analysis to a different and higher level of abstraction. His question was not: how does Sammy run (what movements do his legs and body make while running), but what makes Sammy run.\" From this, \"99 per cent of the criticism directed against the Marxist labour theory of value is entirely beside the point,\" especially when it tries to \"refute\" the first pages of Chapter 1 of Capital Volume 1 (sometimes construed as a \"proof\" of the theory). Mandel's illustrative counter: \"To say that commodities have qualities in common other than the fact that they are products of social labour transforms an analysis of social relations into a logical parlour game.\" The \"other qualities\" (like scarcity) \"have nothing to do with the nexus between members of society in an anarchic market economy.\" The fact that both bread and aeroplanes are \"scarce\" does not make them commensurable. Even when thousands of people are dying of hunger and \"the 'intensity of need' for bread is certainly a thousand times greater than the 'intensity of need' for aeroplanes, the first commodity will remain immensely cheaper than the second, because much less socially necessary labour has been spent on its production.\" This is a direct attack on the marginalist foundations of value theory. Mandel then anticipates the next objection: \"Why bother at all with this type of inquiry? Why can one not restrict 'economics' to the analysis of what actually occurs in day-to-day economic life ... the ups and downs of prices, wages, interest rates, profits etc., instead of trying to discover mysterious 'forces beneath the surface of the economy' ... only on a very high level of abstraction and in the very final analysis?\" His answer is that this \"neo-positivist approach is curiously and typically unscientific\": \"Nobody dealing with medicine... would dare for fear of becoming a laughing stock to ask: 'Why bother to look for the \"deeper causes\" of diseases, when one can collect symptoms to establish a diagnosis?'\" And he gives a vivid empirical example: \"one kilogram of gold buys in 1974 nearly twice as many given baskets of American consumer commodities as seventy years ago (the average consumer price index has risen somewhat more than fivefold compared to 1904, whereas the price of gold on the free market has risen nine times).\" The explanation lies in the different dynamics of long-term social productivity of labour in different consumer industries versus gold-mining—\"that is, with the laws of value as formulated by Marx.\" The famous \"invisible hand\" is thus \"nothing but the operation of that same law of value.\" Money born out of exchange can serve as a universal equivalent \"only because it is itself a commodity with its own intrinsic value (or, in the case of paper money, represents a commodity with its own intrinsic value).\" Monetary theory is \"re-united with the theory of value and the theory of capital accumulation.\" The ups and downs of the trade cycle appear \"as the mechanism through which upheavals in the value of commodities end by asserting themselves\"—with the painful devalorization (loss of value) this entails, not only for \"the 'infantry' of the commodity army, the individual mass of finished consumer goods sold on a day-to-day basis, but also for its 'heavy artillery', that is, large-scale machinery, fixed capital.\" Thus, \"The theory of economic growth, of the 'trade cycle', of capitalist crises, the theory of the rate of profit and of its tendency to decline—everything flows in the last analysis from this operation of the law of value.\" The question whether it has any use in economic analysis is therefore \"as meaningless as the question whether you need the concept of basic particles (atoms, etc.) in physics.\" Mandel then specifies the triple function of the law of value in Marxist economic theory. First , \"it governs (which does not mean that it determines here and now) the exchange relations between commodities; that is to say, it establishes the axis around which long-term changes in relative prices of commodities oscillate.\" (This includes the exchange relation between capital and labour.) Second , \"it determines the relative proportions of total social labour (and this implies, in the last analysis, total material resources of society) devoted to the output of different groups of commodities\"—distributing material resources over different branches of production according to the division of \"effective demand,\" always \"within the framework of antagonistic class relations of production and distribution.\" Third , \"it rules economic growth, by determining the average rate of profit and directing investment towards those firms and sectors of production where profit is above average, and away from those firms and sectors where profit is below average.\" These movements \"correspond in the final analysis to conditions of 'economy' and 'waste' of social labour, that is to the workings of the law of value.\" Marx's labour theory of value, Mandel explains, is \"a further development and perfection of the labour theory of value as it emanated from the 'classical' school of political economy, and especially of Ricardo's version.\" But Marx's innovations were manifold. One was decisive: \"the use of the concept of abstract social labour as the foundation of his theory of value.\" It is for this reason that \"Marx cannot be considered as in any way an 'advanced neo-Ricardian'.\" \"'Labour quantities as the essence of value' is something quite different from 'labour quantities as numeraire'—a common measuring rod of the value of all commodities.\" The distinction between concrete labour (which determines use-value) and abstract labour (which determines value) is \"a revolutionary step forward beyond Ricardo of which Marx was very proud; indeed he considered it his main achievement, together with the discovery of the general category of surplus-value.\" It is based on \"an understanding of the peculiar structure of a society of commodity-producers, that is of the key problem of how to relate to each other the segments of the global labour potential of society which have taken the form of private labour. It represents, therefore, together with Marx's concept of necessary labour and surplus labour (necessary product and surplus product), the key nexus between economic theory and the science of social revolution, historical materialism.\" This is a powerful argument: the labour theory of value is not merely an economic organon but a hinge connecting economic theory to revolutionary theory. Mandel then corrects another misconception. The way the labour theory of value \"sharply excludes use-value from any direct determination of value and exchange-value\" has often been interpreted as a rejection of use-value beyond the boundary of economic analysis. This \"does not correspond at all to the rich dialectical complexity of Capital .\" For Marx, \"the commodity was understood as encompassing both a unity and a contradiction between use-value and exchange-value: a good with no use-value for any potential buyer could not realize its exchange-value; and the specific use-value of two categories of commodities, means of production and labour-power, played a key role in his analysis of the capitalist mode of production.\" Here Mandel signals he will develop this in the introduction to Volume 2 on reproduction. Mandel then works out in fine detail the meaning of the law of value under capitalism, using a case study of Mr Jones (an English shoe manufacturer). Suppose Mr Jones has his workers produce 100,000 pairs of shoes a year: \"he knows that people need shoes and buy them; he even knows, if he bothers to do his homework, that the annual number of shoes sold in the United Kingdom (and all those countries to which he intends to export his output) vastly outdistances the modest figure of 100,000 pairs. But he has no way of knowing whether the specific 100,000 pairs of shoes he owns will find specific customers willing and able to buy them.\" Only after selling his shoes and receiving their equivalent can he say (provided he has realized the average rate of profit on his invested capital): \"my workers have truly spent socially necessary labour in my factory.\" If part of the produced shoes remain unsold, or are sold at a loss or below-average profit, \"this means that part of the labour spent on their production has not been recognized by society as socially necessary labour, has in fact been wasted labour from the point of view of society as a whole.\" But this recognition or refusal of recognition \"occurs exclusively in function of meeting effective demand on the market, that is it is independent of the use-value or social usefulness of the specific physical qualities of a given commodity.\" Society recognizes quantities of labour spent, \"making abstraction of these considerations. That is why Marx called these quantities, quantities of abstract socially necessary labour.\" If a pound of opium, a box of dum-dum bullets, or a portrait of Hitler find customers, \"the labour which has been spent on their output is socially necessary labour; its production has been value-production.\" If, on the contrary, an exquisite piece of china or a new pharmaceutical product does not find customers, \"its production has created no value, has been equivalent to a waste of social labour—even if, in some distant future, their creators will be celebrated as geniuses or benefactors of mankind.\" Mandel then makes the crucial ethical point: \"The labour theory of value has nothing to do with judgements on the usefulness of things from the point of view of human happiness or social progress. It has even less to do with establishing 'conditions for justice in exchange.' It simply recognizes the deeper meaning of the actual act of exchange and of the output of commodities under capitalism, and what governs the distribution of income between social classes which results from these acts, independently of any moral, aesthetic or political judgement.\" Indeed, \"if one were to look for such 'judgements', one would have to say that Marx, while understanding why the law of value has to operate as it does under commodity production, did not at all strive to 'defend' that law, but on the contrary to build a society in which its operations would be totally abolished.\" Mandel next addresses a common objection: \"if prices are governed in the last analysis by value (socially necessary quantities of abstract labour), how can goods have prices if they are not products of labour, that is if they have no value?\" Marx himself answered this long before drafting Capital Volume 1. Products of nature (\"free goods\"), which have no value since no social labour has been spent on their production, \"can get a price through private appropriation, through the social institution of private property.\" Land on which no human hand has ever worked has no value. \"But it can get a price if it is surrounded by a fence upon which is put a placard 'Private property: Trespassing forbidden,' and if people are ready to pay that price because they need that land as a source of livelihood. This price will in reality be the capitalization of the net income (land rent) accruing to its owner, income produced by those who will farm it and draw material resources from it through their toil.\" This is a beautiful illustration that price and value are distinct but related categories. Mandel then defends the quantitative measurability of value against those who say it is purely qualitative and \"unmeasurable.\" \"This bends the stick too far in the other direction.\" It is true that \"the quantitative measurement of the labour quantities in commodities is difficult. But the difficulty is not so much a conceptual one... as one stemming from a lack of accurate information.\" He sketches a macroeconomic methodology : start from macro-aggregates, \"the total sum of man-hours spent in the whole realm of material production—industry, agriculture and commodity transport—in a given country, its division between different branches of industry and key groups of commodities, their interrelationship through an input-output table, the labour spent for the average unit produced in 'autarchic' branches where no raw material has to be imported from foreign lands, and so climb up towards an estimate of total labour expenditure per branch and per commodity produced...\" This requires that it \"will be necessary to 'open the books' of all capitalist enterprises and to verify these figures on the basis of shop-floor evidence.\" This is a striking and concrete methodological proposal—essentially a call for empirical socialist accounting. Finally, Mandel adds a note responding to the objection that the labour theory of value \"assumes\" labour is the only scarce factor. This is \"obviously nonsense.\" Leontief makes the correct point that \"Marx was probably the first economist to give fixed capital a central importance in the process of production, as against, for example, Böhm-Bawerk.\" What Marx does assume is that \"machines cannot in and by themselves 'command' portions of the total available labour-power of society to be additionally expended or to move from one sector of production to another—a proposition which is rather self-evident, besides having been scientifically proved by Marx.\" Once one understands that \"for Marx, value is in the last analysis assignment of portions of the socially available labour-power, total value newly produced being equal to total expenditure of living labour in a given period, one solves the riddle.\" And Marx, \"advancing beyond classical economy, did not 'dissolve' the value of the annual product into wages and surplus-value (profits, rents and interests), but added to this the value of raw materials and machinery used up in the process of production. His only point was that this part of the annual product's value did not increase in the process of production but was only maintained, the only source of new value being living labour.\" This is a key doctrinal clarification of Marx's departure from Smith and Ricardo on the composition of the annual product's value. VI. MARX'S KEY DISCOVERY: THE THEORY OF SURPLUS-VALUE Mandel opens his sixth section by contrasting Marx with the classical and post-classical schools. The classical school, including Ricardo, \"saw profits as a residual net income, once wages had been paid.\" So strict was their adherence to this concept that Ricardo \"believed that only increases or decreases in production costs in the wage-good industries could influence the rate of profit.\" Whatever happened to luxury goods industry, or even to raw materials, \"would not affect the global rate of profit.\" This view is \"incomplete and therefore incorrect,\" though at least it was \"an attempt to come to grips with the problem of income distribution between social classes as a function of what happens in the course of production.\" By contrast, \"the exponents of post-Ricardian 'vulgar' economic theory, and especially the neo-classical marginalists, do not bother to ask the question 'why?', they are content just to answer the question 'how?'.\" They simply note that \"factors\" (labour, capital, land) get different \"prices\" on the market, and limit themselves to studying how these prices fluctuate. It was left to Marx \"to unravel these basic questions about the capitalist mode of production.\" Mandel then presents a historical sequence to make the theory of surplus-value intuitive. The origin of income and consumption of the ruling classes in precapitalist societies is no matter of speculation. When the medieval serf \"worked half the week for his own livelihood on the land of his manse, and the other half of the week without remuneration on the estate of the noble or the church,\" one could argue (from a \"moral\" point of view) that he was offering unpaid labour \"in exchange\" for \"service\" of protection. But \"nobody would confuse this 'exchange' with what goes on in the market place. It was in fact no economic exchange at all... The 'service of protection' is not 'bought' by the serf any more than a small Chicago businessman 'buys a service' from a gang of hoodlums. It is an extortion imposed upon him by the social set-up, whether he likes it or not.\" Hence the origin of the social surplus product accruing to the precapitalist ruling class is \"obviously unpaid labour (whether in the form of labour services, or of physical products of these labour services, or even of money-rent) expended by the producers.\" In slavery, \"the context is as clear if not clearer, especially in those extreme examples where even the miserable pittance of the slave was not provided by the masters, but had to be provided by the slave himself on the seventh day of the week.\" Regarding slave plantations, even the most sceptical critics of historical materialism \"will find it hard to doubt that the whole social product, the part which fed the slaves as well as the part which fed the masters, had but one origin: social labour expended by the slaves and by them alone.\" When we look at the capitalist mode of production, \"everything seems much more complicated and much more obscure.\" No brutal force, personified by an overseer with a whip, appears to force the worker to give up anything he has produced or owns. The relationship with the capitalist appears to be based on an act of exchange identical to that of a small artisan or farmer meeting in the market place. The worker appears to sell his \"labour\" for a wage; the capitalist \"combines\" that labour with machines, raw materials, and the labour of other men to produce finished products. As the capitalist owns the machines and raw materials and the money to pay wages, \"is it not 'natural' that he should also own the finished products which result from the 'combination of these factors'?\" Mandel's answer: \"This is what appears to occur under capitalism. However, probing below the surface, Marx comes up with a series of striking observations.\" He organises them into three points , each a defeat of the appearance of equal exchange. First: an institutional inequality of conditions between capitalists and workers. The capitalist is not forced to buy labour-power on a continuous basis. He does it \"only if it is profitable to him. If not, he prefers to wait, to lay off workers, or even to close his plant down till better times.\" The worker, on the other hand, \"is under economic compulsion to sell his labour-power.\" As he has no access to the means of production (including land), no access to large-scale free stock of food, and no reserves of money enabling him to survive for any length of time while doing nothing, \"he must sell his labour-power to the capitalist on a continuous basis and at the current rate.\" Without such institutionalized compulsion, \"a fully developed capitalist society would be impossible.\" Indeed, \"once such compulsion is absent (for example where large tracts of free land subsist), capitalism will remain dwarfed until, by hook or by crook, the bourgeois class suppresses access to that free land.\" The last chapter of Capital Volume 1, on colonization, develops this point to great effect. Mandel cites the history of Africa, especially South Africa, and the Portuguese, Belgian, French, and British colonies—large-scale appropriation of land by white settlers and colonial companies, herding of Africans into \"reserves,\" imposition of money taxes in essentially non-monetary economies, imposition of large-scale money fines, and even direct forced labour penalties for innumerable transgressions of laws \"specially designed to furnish the settlers with labour-power.\" The principle: \"If people are living under conditions where there is no economic compulsion to sell their labour-power, then repressive juridical and political compulsion has to deliver the necessary manpower to the entrepreneurs; otherwise capitalism could not survive under these circumstances.\" Mandel draws an immediate and striking corollary concerning trade unions : \"Workers who combine to set up a reserve fund can be freed at least for some weeks from the compulsion to sell their labour-power on a continuous basis at the given market rate. Capitalism does not like that at all. It is contrary to 'nature'; if not to human nature, then at least to the deeper nature of bourgeois society. That is why, under robust nascent capitalism, trade unions were simply banned. That is also why, under senile capitalism, we are gradually returning to a situation in which workers are denied the right to strike—the right to abstain from selling their labour-power at the offered price whenever they like.\" Mandel's punchline: \"In this instance, Marx's insight is clearly confirmed by the highest authorities of the bourgeois state: under capitalism, labour is fundamentally forced labour. Whenever possible, capitalists prefer hypocritically to cloak the compulsion under a smokescreen of 'equal and just exchange' on the 'labour market'. When hypocrisy is no longer possible, they return to what they began with: naked coercion.\" Mandel then addresses the \"factors of production\" objection. Marx was perfectly aware that \"in order to organize production in modern factories, it was not enough to combine the social labour-power of manual and intellectual workers. It was necessary to provide for land, buildings, energy, infrastructural elements like roads and water, machinery, a given fabric of organized society, means of communication, etc.\" But \"it is obviously absurd to presume that, because factory production is impossible without these conditions of production, roads and canals therefore 'produce value'.\" It is \"no more logical to assume that machines 'produce' any value, in and by themselves. Of all these 'factors' it can be said only that their given value has to be maintained and reproduced, through incorporation of part of it in the current output of living labour, during the production process.\" Mandel concedes: property titles (private appropriation rights) to land and machinery lead to a situation where these \"factors\" will not be incorporated into the process of production \"without their proprietors receiving an expected 'return' over and above the compensation for the wear and tear.\" This is \"obviously true.\" But \"it does not follow at all that such 'returns' are then 'produced' by the property titles.\" Nor does it imply \"that owners of such property titles meet the owners of labour-power on an equal footing.\" Only if we were in \"a 'capitalistic slave society', where owners of slaves hired out labour-power to owners of factories renting land from landed proprietors, could one say that institutional equality existed between all owners—though, of course, not between owners and slaves!\" And in that case, \"the slave owners would hire out their slaves only if they received a 'net return' over and above the upkeep of the slaves.\" Second: the social situation in which a small part of society has monopolized property and access to the means of production \"is in no way a product of 'natural inequality of talents and inclinations' among human beings.\" Indeed, \"it did not exist for tens of thousands of years of social life on the part of homo sapiens.\" Even 150 years ago, \"nine-tenths of the producers on this planet—who were in their overwhelming majority agricultural producers—did have direct access, in one way or another, to their means of production and livelihood. The separation of the producer from his means of production was a long and bloody historical process, analysed in detail by Marx in Part Eight of Capital Volume 1, 'So-Called Primitive Accumulation'.\" Third: the worker does not sell the capitalist his labour , but his labour-power —his capacity to work for a given period of time. This labour-power becomes a commodity under capitalism. As such it has a value (exchange-value), as any other commodity does: \"the quantity of social labour necessary to reproduce it—that is to say the value of the consumer goods necessary to keep the worker and his children in condition to continue to work at a given level of intensity of effort.\" But it has a \"special quality, a special 'use-value' for the capitalist.\" When the capitalist \"consumes\" labour-power in the process of production, \"the worker produces value. His labour has the double capacity to conserve value —that is, to transfer into the finished product the value of the raw material and of a fraction of the machinery used up in this process of production—and to create new value , by spending itself.\" The whole mystery of the origin of profits and rents is over once one understands that \"in the process of production, the workers can (and must—otherwise the capitalist would not hire them) produce value over and above the value of their own labour-power, over and above the equivalent of the wages which they receive.\" This is the crucial double character of labour: at one and the same time , the labour that preserves value (transfers constant capital) and the labour that creates new value (including surplus-value). Mandel makes the historical comparison explicit: \"We are back where we started in pre-capitalist societies, and we have been able to eliminate the cobweb of apparent 'exchange equality': like feudal rent or the slave-owner's livelihood, capitalist profits, interests and rents originate from the difference between what the workers produce and what they receive for their upkeep. Under capitalism this difference appears in the form of value, and not of physical output. This fact prevents the process from being immediately transparent. But it does not make it fundamentally different from the 'exchange' taking place between feudal lord and serf.\" Mandel then clarifies a doctrinal point: \"It is therefore incorrect to state, as does Blaug, following other academic critics of Marx, that Marx's theory of surplus-value is a theory of 'unearned increment'.\" Rather, \"It is an appropriation or deduction theory of the capitalists' income, as was the classical labour theory of value. Capitalists appropriate value which the workers have already produced, prior to the process of circulation of commodities and of distribution of income. No value can be distributed—from a macro-economic point of view... which has not been previously produced.\" This distinction—unearned increment (a Ricardian idea of windfall gains) versus appropriation/deduction theory (the fundamental class mechanism)—is important: the surplus-value theory is not about exceptional gains but about the regular class mechanism. Marx himself considered the discovery of the concept of surplus-value, \"representing the sum total of profits, interests and rents of all parts of the bourgeois class, as his main theoretical discovery.\" Mandel quotes Marx's letter to Engels of 24 August 1868: \"Das Beste an meinem Buch ist 1. (darauf beruht alles Verständnis der facts) der gleich im Ersten Kapitel hervorgehobne Doppelcharakter der Arbeit, je nachdem sie sich in Gebrauchswert oder Tauschwert ausdrückt; 2. die Behandlung des Mehrwerts unabhängig von seinen besondren Formen als Profit, Zins, Grundrente etc.\" (The best point in my book is: 1) —this is the basis of all understanding of facts—the double character of labour, according to whether it is expressed in use-value or exchange-value, emphasised in the very first chapter; 2. the treatment of surplus-value independently of its particular forms as profit, interest, ground-rent, etc.)—especially, Marx adds, this will show itself in Volume 2. Mandel emphasises: the concept of surplus-value \"ties together the historical science of society and the science of the capitalist economy, explaining both the origins and content of the class struggle and the dynamic of capitalist society.\" For once we understand that surplus-value is produced by workers—that surplus-value is nothing but \"the age-old social surplus product in money form, in the form of value\"—we understand \"the historical leap which occurred when that social surplus product no longer appeared essentially in the form of luxury goods (of which consumption is necessarily limited, even under conditions of such extreme extravagance as during the Roman Empire or in the eighteenth-century French court) but in the form of money.\" More money means not only additional purchasing power for luxury goods, \"but additional purchasing power for more machines, more raw materials, more labour-power.\" Here Marx discovers another economic compulsion : \"Private property, the fragmentation of social labour among various firms, that is, the very nature of generalized commodity production—capitalism—implies a compulsion to compete for shares of the market.\" That is: \"the need to accumulate capital, the need to increase the extraction of surplus-value, the unquenchable thirst for surplus-value which characterizes capitalism, it is all here: the accumulation of capital = the transformation of surplus-value into additional capital.\" Mandel reiterates: \"Again, as for value, we should note what this is all about: command over fractions of the total disposable quantity of social labour.\" It is sufficient to recall this \"to understand how misplaced are criticisms of the theory of surplus-value which speak about the 'productivity of capital', capital being understood as machines.\" Machines \"can never, in and by themselves, hire any fraction of the disposable social labour force, except in science fiction. In the more prosaic world in which we live, men owning machines can, for that reason, hire and fire other men. How the product of the labour of these men is then divided, and why, is what Marx seeks to explain.\" Mandel then returns to the question of increased productivity. Marx \"did not 'deny' that machinery could increase the social productivity of labour.\" On the contrary, in Chapter 15 of Capital Volume 1 \"he was more aware of that potential of technology than any economist among his contemporaries.\" But the overlooked question is: \"why should the results of the increased productivity of labour be appropriated by the capitalist? Why should the combined productivity of many men working together—the famous 'collective labour potential of the factory' to which a key analysis is devoted in the original Part Seven ('Chapter Six') omitted from the published version of Capital Volume 1 (see appendix)—the combined productivity of scientists and technologists, workers by hand and brain, inventors of machinery and flexers of muscle, increase the profit of the owners of machinery? Surely not because that machinery has some mysterious quality of 'creating' value.\" Mandel answers: \"Surely rather because the owners are in a position to appropriate the products of that combination. So we are back to Marx's theory of surplus-value.\" Finally, Mandel takes up a specific contemporary apologetics: Alchian and Demsetz's theory of the firm (1972), \"an interesting, if somewhat astounding, innovation in apologetics for capitalist profits.\" In this theory, owners of different \"co-operating inputs\" are supposed to have a natural tendency to shirk , because they give preference to \"non-pecuniary goods\" (!) such as leisure, attractive working conditions, and time to converse with fellow workers. It follows that if shirking is to be checked \"someone must have both the right to monitor the performance of team members and the disinclination to shirk himself. To this end he must have the right to receive the residue after all other inputs have been paid contractual amounts, the right to terminate membership of the team and the right to sell these rights.\" Mandel's ironic conclusion: \"After having received with great joy the good tidings that he has now been promoted to the status of member of a 'co-operative team', on an equal footing with the capitalist, the average worker cannot fail to wonder for what mysterious reason the 'someone' who gets all these 'economically necessary rights' is always the owner of the 'input—means of production' and never the owner of the 'input—labour-power'.\" He asks: \"Would it be because the capitalist is free from the human vice of shirking, or has no inclination to leisure or attractive working conditions? Or is it perhaps because Messrs Apologists for Capitalism are trying to argue away the fact of surplus-value appropriation through monopoly ownership of the means of production?\" This is a sharp illustration of the survival of vulgar apologetics in modern guise. VII. MARX'S THEORY OF CAPITAL: Capital as a Social Relation, the Distinction Between Capital and Capitalism, and the Sraffa Critique Mandel's seventh section opens with a definition: \"Capital is thus, from the Marxist point of view, a social relation between men which appears as a relation between things or between men and things.\" Flowing logically from Marx's labour theory of value and theory of surplus-value, \"this is another of the key discoveries which opposes his economic theory radically to all forms of academic 'economics'.\" This formulation captures the Marxian doctrine of fetishism as an objective social reality, not merely an illusion—a point Mandel developed in his discussion of money. Marx \"energetically rejects the idea, as expounded by 'vulgar' and neo-classical economists, that 'capital' is just 'any stock of wealth' or 'any means to increase labour productivity'.\" Mandel gives a biting illustration: \"A chimpanzee using a stick to get at bananas is no more the first capitalist than a tribal community learning to accrue its wealth through animal husbandry or land irrigation is 'accumulating capital'.\" Capital presupposes the following conditions: \"that goods are not being produced for direct consumption by the producing communities, but are sold as commodities; that the total labour potential of society has become fragmented into private labours conducted independently of each other; that commodities therefore have value; that this value is realized through exchange with a special commodity called money; that it can therefore start an independent process of circulation, being property of a given class of society whose members operate as owners of value looking for increments of value.\" If, as Adam Smith explained, the productive (technical) division of labour is a source of increased labour productivity—\"to a large extent independently from the specific social form of organization of the economy\"—then capital \"is not a product of that division of labour, but of a social division of labour, in which owners of accumulated value face non-owners.\" This distinction between technical and social division of labour is critical to the definition of capital. Mandel takes up Schumpeter's reproach that Marx \"elaborated a theory of capital which was unable to explain the origins of capital.\" \"Nothing is further from the truth.\" Marx the dialectician \"perfectly understood the difference between, on the one hand, the production and reproduction of capital on the basis of the capitalist mode of production and, on the other, the origins and development of capital in pre-capitalist modes of production.\" Indeed, \"one of the essential objections to the imprecise and unscientific handling of categories by 'vulgar' economists was their undifferentiated use of the terms 'capital' and 'capitalism' as more or less synonymous.\" Mandel now gives his own distinctions: Capitalism = \"the capitalist mode of production, the seizure of the means of production by capital, which has become predominant in the sphere of production.\" Capital = \"value (initially in the form of money) becoming an independent operator in the pores of a non-capitalist mode of production. Capital appears initially as usury and merchant (long-distance trade) capital.\" Capital \"after a long historical process, and only under specific social conditions, does capital victoriously penetrate the sphere of production in the form of manufacturing capital. (This occurred in the late fifteenth and sixteenth centuries in Western Europe; in the eighteenth century in Japan. In China, isolated elements of manufacturing capital had probably already appeared more than a thousand years earlier.)\" This distinction has dramatic analytical implications. In simple commodity production, capital does not produce surplus-value. It \"simply transforms into surplus-value parts of current output and revenue which originate independently from capital.\" It can appropriate part of the social surplus product normally passing into the hands of precapitalist ruling classes (for instance the appropriation, through usury, of part of the feudal land rent). It can appropriate part of the product which normally serves as a consumption fund for the producers themselves. The basic characteristic of these operations of capital under precapitalist relations of production \"is that it will barely increase the global wealth of society ; it will neither significantly develop productive forces nor stimulate economic growth. It can only have a disintegrating effect on the given precapitalist social order, precipitating the ruin of several social classes. However, by accelerating the transformation of goods produced and consumed as use-values only into commodities, that is by accelerating the spread of money economy, it can historically prepare the ground for an eventual appearance of the capitalist mode of production.\" Mandel draws an explicit correspondence: \"Capital operating in pre-capitalist modes of production refers essentially to a theory of money circulation and appropriation. This is why in Volume 1 of Capital Marx first introduces capital in Part Two, after having explained the nature of money. Indeed, Part Two is entitled 'The Transformation of Money into Capital'. Here again, the logical analysis corresponds to the historical process, to which Marx continually refers, albeit for the most part in footnotes.\" By contrast, \"capital operating in the capitalist mode of production, the real object of study of Capital , refers obviously to a theory of production and appropriation of value and surplus-value.\" Marx explains in Volume 1, Chapter 24, how \"the law of appropriation of commodities is transformed when we pass from a society of petty commodity producers to a capitalist society. In the first case, the direct producers are owners of the products of their labour; in the second, the owners of capital become the owners of the products of the labour of the direct producers.\" Apologists for capitalism try to justify this fact by arguing that capitalists \"place at the disposal\" of the workers the tools with which production occurs. But again history pierces the hypocrisy: \"For capitalism was not born—in the days of manufacturing—with the capitalist 'putting at the disposal of the producers' any new machinery. It was born with the capitalists expropriating the tools owned by the producers themselves and assembling these very tools under a common roof.\" Mandel cites N. W. Posthumus's history of the Leiden cloth industry as evidence. So, Mandel concludes: \"Capital, under the capitalist mode of production, is therefore value constantly increased by surplus-value, which is produced by productive labour and appropriated by capitalists through the appropriation of the commodities produced by the workers in factories owned by capitalists.\" Mandel then turns to the thorny question of property. \"The way in which this analysis of capital and capitalism hinges on the institution of private property has often been misunderstood or (and) misrepresented, both by critics and by disciples of Marx.\" Historically and logically, \"capitalism is tied to the private ownership of the means of production, which allows private appropriation of produced commodities, thus private appropriation of surplus-value, and thus private accumulation of capital.\" Hence \"rights of private property\" are \"at the bottom of the whole constitutional and juridical superstructure\" centuries of law-making have erected upon commodity production. But what we confront \"when we examine the social relations which lie behind these juridical forms is, of course, something which is not simply formal private property; otherwise the analysis would be reduced to simple tautology.\" When Marx states that commodity production is possible only because \"social labour has been fragmented into private labours conducted independently from each other,\" he refers to \"a socio-economic and not a juridical reality; the latter is only a reflection—and sometimes a very imperfect one!—of the former.\" What capitalism is about, then, is \"a specific relation between wage-labour and capital, a social organization in which social labour is fragmented into firms independent of each other, which take independent decisions about investment, prices and forms of financing growth, which compete with each other for shares of markets and profits (of the total surplus-value produced by productive labour in its totality), and which therefore buy and exploit wage-labour under specific economic conditions, compulsions and constraints.\" It is \"not simply a general relationship between 'producers' and 'accumulators', or 'producers' and 'administrators', for such a relationship is in the last analysis characteristic of all class societies and not specific to capitalism at all.\" Thus: \"The content of the economic institution of private capital is therefore the independent firm (whether a small manufacturer or a giant multi-national corporation).\" Whether the juridical form strictly conforms to that content or not is irrelevant, \"and often poses complex legal problems.\" Mandel cites the example of bankruptcy laws—differing across countries—and the question of whether stockholders are owners of income titles or fractions of the firm's assets or property. But \"the vital economic decisions (key investment decisions, for example) are taken by all those firms which are really independent and not subordinate companies. The basic fact of life of the capitalist economy is the fact that these vital decisions are not taken by society as a whole or by the 'associated producers'.\" Mandel adds a crucial clarification: \"the content of this economic institution of private property (fragmented social labour) should not be confused with the question of the precise agents who take the independent firms' decisions. Whether those who take the decisions are individual owners, or representatives of stockholders, or so-called managers, does not in the least change the fact that they are working under the same previously analysed economic compulsion.\" This directly addresses the Galbraithian thesis. \"Some economists today, such as Galbraith and even some Marxists, contend that the contemporary giant corporation has largely freed itself from these constraints. This is an illusion, born of an extrapolation from conditions prevailing during a rather lengthy boom. In fact, the idea that any giant corporation, whatever its dimensions or power, could emancipate itself definitively from the compulsion of (monopolistic) competition, that is, could have a guaranteed specific demand for its products, independently of the trade cycle and from technological innovation, could make sense only if it were insulated both from economic fluctuations and from economic uncertainty, that is if the very nature of its output as commodity production was denied. Experience does not confirm such a contention.\" Mandel then tackles the Galbraith–Baumol–Kaysen distinction between profit maximization and growth maximization . \"The basic distinction which Galbraith, following Baumol, Kaysen and others, introduces between compulsion to profit maximization (true for yesterday's firms) and compulsion to growth maximization (true for today's corporations) becomes devoid of practical long-term significance once we understand that growth remains essentially a function of profit, that capital accumulation can result in the last analysis only from surplus-value production and realization.\" The only \"kernel of truth\" is \"the difference between short-term and long-term profit maximization, which is indeed one of the basic differences between competitive capitalism and monopoly capitalism.\" Finally, Mandel brings the discussion up to date with the Sraffa–Cambridge critique of the theory of the marginal productivity of capital . The Cambridge school has demonstrated convincingly that \"the measurement of capital inputs in the neo-classical 'production function' is based upon circular reasoning.\" For if the effect of marginal increases or decreases of capital inputs upon output has to be measured, \"this can only be done in money terms, given the heterogeneous nature of so-called 'capital goods'.\" Mandel quotes Maurice Dobb: \"But this process of pricing or valuation of capital inputs presupposes a rate of return on the plant and equipment in question, of which the latter value is the capitalization; that is 'one has to assume a rate of interest in order to demonstrate how this equilibrium rate of return is determined'.\" The Cambridge critique thus dismantles the neoclassical theory by showing it cannot measure capital independently of the rate of return which is itself supposed to be explained by capital productivity. The way out, \"obviously, is to look for a common substance in all the 'capital goods' independent of money, that is to return to socially necessary labour as the measurable substance of the value of all commodities.\" Mandel adds a cautious and important note: \"One should note, however, that, to use the Schumpeterian jargon, Dobb thus only justifies the use of labour as a numeraire (a unit of account), in a typically neo-Ricardian way, and not at all on the basis of the Marxist labour theory of value.\" Mandel is careful not to overclaim: the Cambridge critique is a negative demonstration of the incoherence of marginalism, but its \"solution\" remains neo-Ricardian and does not itself establish the Marxian theory of abstract labour. VIII. MARX'S THEORY OF ACCUMULATION OF CAPITAL The eighth section opens with the dialectical reciprocity of capital and surplus-value: \"Capital is thus, by definition, value looking for accretion, for surplus-value. But if capital produces surplus-value, surplus-value also produces additional capital. Under capitalism, economic growth therefore appears in the form of accumulation of capital. The basic drive of the capitalist mode of production is the drive to accumulate capital.\" This is not because of \"some mysterious and tautological 'accumulative passion' or inclination on the part of capitalists. It is essentially explained by competition, that is by the phenomenon of 'various capitals'. Without competition, Marx states categorically, the 'driving fire' of growth would become extinguished. Totally monopolized capital ('a single world trust') would essentially be stagnating capital.\" But competition is combined with \"the trend to replace labour by machinery\" as a driving force for capital accumulation and economic growth under capitalism. If the extension of output \"maintained the given relationship between inputs of living labour and inputs of dead labour (machinery and raw materials), it would rapidly reach both a physical limit (the total available manpower) and hence a profit limit. Under conditions of permanent full employment, wages would tend to increase and erode profits to the point where capital accumulation and economic growth would gradually disappear.\" Under capitalism, however, economic growth is not \"neutral\" with respect to the relationship between living and dead labour inputs (between variable and constant capital). \"It is heavily loaded in favour of an expansion of labour-saving devices. Indeed, a permanent tendency to increase the social productivity of labour is the main civilizing by-product of capital accumulation, the main objective service which capitalism has rendered mankind.\" Mandel gives a quantitative indication: \"Capital accumulation takes on the primary form of an increase in the value of plant and equipment, as well as of the stock of raw materials available in industrialized capitalist countries. On a long-term basis, this accumulation is as impressive as Marx could have imagined. The value of all accumulated private non-farm producer durables multiplied more than tenfold in constant dollars between 1900 and 1965 in the U.S.A., and this estimate is certainly undervalued as it is based upon official records biased for reasons of tax evasion.\" This is one of the few direct empirical statistics Mandel provides. Mandel distinguishes capital accumulation from the behaviour of precapitalist ruling classes: \"If all surplus-value were to be consumed in the form of luxury goods, no capital accumulation would take place at all. Capital would then be maintained at the level it had already reached. This special 'limiting' case was indeed presented by Marx under the name of 'simple reproduction', for purely analytical reasons. It does not, of course, correspond to any 'real' stage or situation of a normally functioning capitalist mode of production.\" Mandel adds: \"As we pointed out, what characterizes capitalism is precisely the compulsion to accumulate, that is 'enlarged reproduction'.\" Enlarged reproduction presupposes that \"not all surplus-value produced by productive labour, and appropriated by the capitalist class, is unproductively consumed. Part of it is transformed into luxury goods and disappears from the process of reproduction. Part of it is transformed into additional capital by being used to buy additional plant and equipment, additional raw materials and additional labour-power.\" This, then, is the process of accumulation of capital: \"the transformation of surplus-value into additional capital, which can produce new increments of surplus-value, leading to new increments of capital. The movement develops in the form of a spiral, as Simonde de Sismondi, one of the early 'romantic' critics of capitalism, whom Marx quotes approvingly on this question, already understood.\" Mandel then addresses the \"abstinence\" theory of profits . The fact that capital accumulation is possible only because part of the surplus-value is not socially squandered in luxury goods \"constitutes the starting point for the so-called 'abstinence' theory (more accurately, justification) of profits and capitalist exploitation.\" Against this: \"Historically, there is not an atom of evidence for the assumption that capital somehow grew out of the 'frugal habits' of some members of the community, as opposed to the 'improvidence' of others... On the contrary, all historical evidence confirms that the sudden appearance of large amounts of 'capital' (in the form of a stock of precious metals and other treasure) in a society previously confined almost exclusively to natural economy... was the result not of 'frugality' and 'thrift' but of large-scale piracy, robbery, violence, theft, enslavement of men and trade in slaves.\" The history of the origins of West European usury and merchant capital between the tenth and thirteenth centuries—\"from the piracy in the Mediterranean through the plundering of Byzantium by the Fourth Crusade to the regular plundering razzias into the Slav territories\"—is \"very eloquent in this respect.\" Mandel's second-level refutation is analytic: \"What is unconfirmed by history is even more absurd in the light of contemporary economic analysis. Nobody could seriously argue that Messrs Rockefeller, Morgan and Mellon have to be compensated for their virtue in not squandering tens of billions of dollars on additional yachts, mansions and private jets—the vulgar version of the abstinence theory. But its more sophisticated version, namely the idea that the profits of the owners of capital are just the way in which their 'fund' is transformed into the 'flow' of long-term capital investment, provides a nice piece of circular reasoning. For whence does the 'fund' originate, if not precisely from the 'flow', that is to say what else is capital if not accumulated profits?\" The verdict: \"To deny that profits originate in the process of production flies in the face of all scientific as well as practical observation of what goes on in a capitalist economy. Once we understand this, there is no room left for any abstinence theory of profit—only for a subtraction one.\" Mandel notes that even Schumpeter defended the \"abstinence\" theory, giving it \"a less vulgar character than in the case of Senior,\" but this does not rescue it. Mandel then distinguishes the two levels of abstraction at which accumulation is analysed: \"In Volume 1, in the framework of 'Capital in general', he examines it essentially in the light of what occurs in and flows from the exchange between wage-labour and capital. In Volume 3, he examines capital accumulation (economic growth under capitalism) in the light of what occurs in the sphere of 'many capitals', that is of capitalist competition.\" Mandel reserves the Volume 3 issues for a later introduction and confines himself to the basic effects of capital accumulation on wage-labour. Mandel begins with a crucial corrective: \"Unlike many of his contemporaries, including some of the sternest non-Marxist critics of capitalism, Marx did not consider that capital accumulation had a simple and unequivocally detrimental effect upon the situation of wage-labour.\" Marx had studied the movement of real wages during the trade cycle, \"and the fact that wages were at their highest level when capital accumulation was progressing at the quickest pace by no means escaped him.\" But \"once again, he tried to go beyond such evident facts to study the fundamental modifications in value terms which capital accumulation would exercise upon labour.\" Marx's contention: \"the very way in which capital accumulation proceeds, the very motive force of capitalist progress—the development of fixed capital, of machinery—contains a powerful dynamic to reduce the value of labour-power .\" For as value of labour-power is \"the equivalent of the value of a given quantity of consumer goods, supposed to be necessary to restore the capacity of a worker to produce at a given level of intensity, a decrease in the value of these consumer goods resulting from an increase in the productivity of labour in the consumer goods industry leads to a decrease in the value of labour-power, all other things remaining equal.\" Mandel stresses: \"This argument implies neither any tendency to a decrease in real wages (on the contrary, it is based upon the assumption of stable real wages in the short and medium term), nor any trend towards 'growing absolute misery' of the working class.\" (He will deal with the false theory \"in the next section.\") But it does imply \"that the favourable results of the increase in productivity of labour end by falling, to a large extent, into the hands of the capitalist class, by transforming themselves into supplementary 'relative surplus-value', provided that the long-term trend of the industrial reserve army of labour is either stable or increasing.\" This distinction—between the value of labour-power and real wages —is one of Mandel's most important analytical hinges, and will be developed fully in Section IX. Mandel notes the world scale: \"As Marx predicted, capitalism spread not only by creating new jobs but also by creating new unemployed (by destroying employment of previous wage earners, and especially of previously self-employed small farmers and handicraftsmen).\" But \"to calculate a 'world average value of labour-power' is of course a meaningless abstraction. Indeed, ever since industrial capitalism in the West started to swamp the rest of the world with its cheap, mass-produced commodities, and at the latest since the eighteen-seventies, a divergent trend has appeared in the world economy: a long-term decline of the reserve army of labour in Western Europe (as a result of exports of both emigrants and commodities) and a rise in the reserve army of labour in the underdeveloped countries. (This latter process included, of course, the transformation of masses of precapitalist farmers, cattle-raisers and artisans into uprooted 'marginalized' vagrants, migrant seasonal labourers, and forced labourers, following a pattern similar to what had happened a few centuries earlier in Western Europe.)\" Mandel insists: \"The dynamics of 'capital accumulation on a world scale' have therefore to be seen as those of an organic whole, and not as the simple sum of capital accumulation processes in distinct countries. The operation of the world market as a gigantic syphon to transfer value from the south to the north of our planet (from the countries with lower to the countries with higher productivity of labour) lies at the very root of the imperialist system.\" This is a striking formulation of the theory of unequal exchange —the transfer of value from low- to high-productivity countries. Mandel admits: \"While the debate on the theoretical explanation of this phenomenon is still in its initial stages, it is important to note that the phenomenon itself is based upon uneven movements (uneven mobility) of capital and labour, and introduces all those dimensions into the analysis of capitalism which Marx reserved for the never-written Volumes 4, 5 and 6 in the original plan of Capital .\" The section then turns to the human consequences of accumulation. \"The accumulation of capital is the accumulation of wealth in the form of commodities, of value. Value production becomes a goal in itself. Work is degraded to the level of a means by which to receive money incomes.\" Mandel notes: \"One of the most striking and most 'modern' parts of Capital is that which examines the inhuman consequences of capital accumulation for the workers and for work itself.\" Marx added to the second German edition of Volume 1 a note that \"under capitalism, labour-power not only becomes a commodity for the capitalist but also receives this form for the worker himself, implying that this degradation of work is both objectively and subjectively the fate of the industrial proletariat.\" It took \"'official' political economy a long time, indeed until after the growing revolt of the workers against assembly line speed-ups, to discover what Marx had anticipated.\" The critique proceeds: \"Because capital accumulation presupposes production for profit, because it has profit maximization as its very rationale, exact and minute cost calculations entail constant reorganizations of the production process with the single purpose of reducing costs. From the point of view of the single capitalist firm, a worker cannot be seen as a human being endowed with elementary rights, dignity, and needs to develop his personality. He is a 'cost element' and this 'cost' must be constantly and exclusively measured in money terms, in order to be reduced to the utmost. Even when 'human relations' and 'psychological considerations' are introduced into labour organization, they are all centred in the last analysis upon 'economies of cost' (of those 'overhead costs' called excessive labour turnover, too many work interruptions, absenteeism, strikes, etc.).\" Mandel's footnote adds: \"The most extreme case is that of 'globalization of costs' in cost-benefit analysis, in which human illness and death are likewise computed in the form of money costs.\" The conclusion Mandel draws is devastating: \"Capitalist economy is thus a gigantic enterprise of dehumanization, of transformation of human beings from goals in themselves into instruments and means for money-making and capital accumulation. It is not the machine, nor any technological compulsion, which inevitably transforms workers and men and women in general into appendices and slaves of monstrous equipment. It is the capitalist principle of profit maximization by individual firms which unleashes this terrifying trend. Other types of technology and other types of machine are perfectly conceivable—provided that the guiding principle of investment is no longer 'cost-saving' by individual competing firms, but the optimum development of all human beings.\" This is an important dialectical correction to both technophobic and technophilic readings of Marx: the problem is not technology per se, but the specific social form of technology under profit-maximizing firms. IX. MARX'S THEORY OF WAGES: Against the \"Iron Law,\" Toward an Accumulation Theory of Wages, and the Relative Impoverishment Thesis Section IX is perhaps the most doctrinally delicate in the Introduction, because it confronts the widespread attribution to Marx of a \"theory of absolute impoverishment.\" Mandel opens with the historical correction: \"Strangely enough, the idea of an ever-increasing decline in the standard of living of the working class, which has often been falsely attributed to Marx, originated with those economists against whom he maintained a constant barrage of polemics after perfecting his own economic theories. It originated with Malthus and, via Ricardo, reached several socialists of Marx's generation, such as Ferdinand Lassalle. Whether under the guise of a 'stable wage fund' or under the guise of an 'iron law of wages', it is essentially a population growth theory of wages . Whenever wages rise sufficiently above the physiological minimum, labourers are supposed to have more children, who then in turn create large-scale unemployment and depress wages back to the minimum.\" Mandel's critique of this theory is methodical: \"The logical shortcomings of this theory are glaring. It examines only what happens on the supply side of labour-power; it does not examine at all what happens on the demand side. It presupposes that the potential working population is a linear function of population increase, and that the demographic movement is in turn a linear function of real income. All the intermediate links—like the effects of increases of income not only upon the child mortality rate but also upon birth rates, not to speak of the effects of increases of income and of the organized strength of the working class on the length of the working week, the duration of training and the moment of retiring from the work process—are eliminated from the chain of reasoning, thereby leading to wrong and indeed absurd results.\" This is a rigorous methodological critique of the Malthusian wages theory. Mandel then contrasts Marx's theory of wages: Marx \"points out not only that labour-power, having been transformed by capitalism into a commodity, has a value which is objectively determined like the value of all other commodities, but also that the value of labour-power has a characteristic distinct from that of all other commodities—to wit that it is dependent on two elements: the physiological needs and the historical-moral needs of the working class.\" This distinction is linked to \"the peculiar nature of labour-power: a commodity inseparable from and integrated with human beings, who are not only endowed with muscles and a stomach, but also with consciousness, nerves, desires, hopes and potential rebelliousness.\" The physical capacity to work can be measured by calory inputs. \"But the willingness to work at a given rhythm, a given intensity, under given conditions, with a given equipment of higher and higher value and increasing vulnerability, presupposes a level of consumption which is not simply equivalent to a sum-total of calories, but is also a function of what is commonly considered by the working class to be its 'current', 'habitual' standard of living.\" Marx notes these habitual standards \"differ greatly from country to country, and are generally higher in those countries which have an advanced, developed capitalist industry than in those which are still at pre-industrial levels, or are going through the throes of 'primitive' industrial capital accumulation.\" Mandel draws an \"unexpected conclusion\" from this aspect of Marx: \"according to this aspect of Marx's work, real wages would actually have to be higher in more advanced capitalist countries—and therefore also in more advanced stages of capitalism—than in less developed countries. This would also imply that they would tend to increase in time, as the level of industrialization increases.\" On the other hand, Mandel recalls that Marx \"explained fluctuation of wages during the trade cycle, that is of the price and not of the value of labour-power, as being governed essentially by the movements of the industrial reserve army. Real wages would tend to increase in times of boom and full employment and to decline in times of depression and large-scale unemployment.\" But \"he indicated, however, that there was nothing automatic about this movement, and that the actual class struggle—including trade-union action, which he considered indispensable for this very reason—was the instrument through which workers could take advantage of more favourable conditions on the 'labour market' somewhat to increase their wages, whereas the main effect of depression was that it would weaken the resistance of the working class to wage-cuts.\" Mandel then stresses that Marx \"stuck to his theory of value with regard to wages. Wages are the prices of the commodity labour-power. Like all other prices, they do not fluctuate at random, but around an axis which is the value of that commodity.\" So the trade-cycle movements explain \"only short-term fluctuations: these have to be integrated within a wider analysis, explaining the long-term fluctuations of wages in function of the changes in the value of labour-power.\" Mandel then formulates \"Marx's theory of wages as an accumulation of capital wage theory, in opposition to the crude demographic wage theory of the Malthus-Ricardo-Lassalle school.\" He spells out the fivefold sense in which long-term movements of wages are a function of capital accumulation: (1) Accumulation of capital implies a decline in value of a given basket of consumer goods included in the given standard of living of the working class (with the given reproduction costs of labour-power). \"In this sense, the development of capitalism tends to depress the value of labour-power, all other things remaining equal. Let us repeat: such a decline in the value of labour-power does not imply a decline, but only a stability, of real wages.\" This is a crucial point that Mandel has repeated. (2) Accumulation of capital implies a decline in the value and an expansion of the output (mass production) of consumer goods previously not included in the reproduction costs of labour-power. \"If objective and subjective conditions are favourable, the working class can force the inclusion of these goods into the accepted minimum standard of living, can expand the 'moral-historical' component of the value of labour-power, thereby increasing its value. This again does not happen automatically, but essentially as a result of the class struggle.\" (3) Accumulation of capital will favour the increase in value of labour-power if the long-term structural supply of labour-power does not strongly exceed demand, or is even below demand. This \"explains why wages in the U.S.A. were from the beginning significantly higher than in Europe, why wages started rising significantly in the latter part of the nineteenth century in Europe as a result of massive overseas emigration of the reserve army of labour, and why persistent massive unemployment and underemployment in the underdeveloped countries has implied a tendentially declining value of labour-power (often even accompanied by declining real wages) in the last two decades.\" (4) Accumulation of capital forms the upper barrier which no increase in the value or the price of labour-power can break under capitalism. \"If and when the increase in the value of labour-power implies a strong decline in surplus-value, accumulation of capital slows down, large-scale unemployment reappears, and wages are 'readjusted' to a level compatible with capital accumulation. In other words, under capitalism, wages can fall to the point where the 'historical-moral' ingredient of the value of labour-power completely disappears, where they are actually reduced to the bare physiological minimum. They cannot rise to the point where the 'historical-moral' ingredient of the value of labour-power wipes out surplus-value as the source of capital accumulation.\" This is the Marxian version of an upper limit to wages—not because of population but because of the requirements of accumulation. (5) Accumulation of capital implies increased exploitation of the workers, including an increased attrition of labour-power, especially through intensification of the production process. \"But this in turn implies the need for higher consumption just to reproduce labour-power even physiologically. So one can say that, in this sense, capitalism increases the value of labour-power by making its exploitation more intensive.\" Mandel notes: \"One can especially find negative confirmation of this effect of the accumulation of capital on the value of labour-power. Once wages decline below a certain level (especially under the effects of wars or reactionary dictatorships), the productive effort of the workers will decline and labour-power will not be reconstituted to its full productive capacity, as a result of too low a level of wages.\" Mandel adds a footnote citing Rosdolsky and Otto Bauer on the distinction between \"physiological needs\" born from the simple life process of the worker and those needs born from the work process, the second expanding progressively with the growing intensification of work. Mandel then asks: \"How, then, has it been possible for so many writers, for so long, to have attributed to Marx a 'theory of absolute impoverishment of the workers under capitalism' which obviously implied a theory of tendential fall in the value not only of labour-power but even of real wages?\" He gives three reasons. First: \"because Marx, in his youthful writings, did in fact hold such a theory—for example, in the Communist Manifesto . But this was formulated before he had brought his theoretical understanding of the capitalist mode of production to its final, mature conclusion. It is only in the years 1857-8 that we have the birth of Marx's economic theory in its rounded, consistent form. After he had written A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy and the Grundrisse , there was no longer a trace of any such historical trend towards absolute impoverishment in his economic analysis.\" The \"mature Marx\" argument—a critical hinge of Mandel's reading. Second: \"because so many writers confuse Marx's treatment of the value of labour-power (which depends upon the value of the consumer goods the worker buys with his wages) with the category of real wages (determined by the mass of consumer goods his wages buy). Under capitalism, given the constant increase in the productivity of labour, these categories can move in opposite directions.\" Mandel quotes Capital Volume 1, Chapter 17: \"In this way it is possible, given increasing productivity of labour, for the price of labour-power to fall constantly and for this fall to be accompanied by a constant growth in the mass of the worker's means of subsistence.\" He also quotes Wages, Price and Profit : \"consequently the general tendency of capitalistic production is not to raise but to sink the average standard of wages, or to push the value of labour to its minimum limit,\" adding that the whole argument \"applies to the trend of the value of labour-power, not to that of real wages.\" Third: \"because two famous passages in Capital Volume 1 have been consistently misinterpreted.\" In both passages, Marx speaks about \"increasing misery\" and pauperism, and about \"accumulation of misery.\" But \"the context indicates clearly that what he is referring to is the poverty and misery of the 'surplus population', of the 'Lazarus layer of the working class', that is, of the unemployed or semi-employed poor.\" Mandel cites Michael Harrington's The Other America (1963) and Brian Abel-Smith and Peter Townsend's The Poor and the Poorest (1963), which \"estimates that 14 per cent of the British population (7 million people!) were living in, or on the margin of, poverty twenty years after the establishment of the welfare state!\" Mandel's conclusion: \"The truth is simply that in passages such as these Marx uses formulations that are ambiguous and so lend weight to confusion on the question.\" He adds: \"To have revealed that such poverty is rooted in the system of wage-labour, and that no permanent elimination of it (i.e. a guaranteed standard of living for all human beings, irrespective of how much they work or indeed whether they work at all) is possible without upsetting the economic compulsion to sell the proletarian's labour-power, is one of Marx's most epoch-making discoveries and fundamental to his economic theory.\" Mandel then formulates the positive doctrine: \"What we in fact have here is a theory of a tendency towards relative impoverishment of the working class under capitalism in a double sense .\" First: \"in the sense that productive workers tend to get a smaller part of the new value they produce: in other words there is a trend towards an increase in the rate of surplus-value.\" Second: \"in the sense that even when wages rise the needs of the workers as human beings are denied.\" Mandel elaborates: \"This applies even to their additional consumer needs that grow out of the very increase in the productivity of labour which results from the accumulation of capital. One has only to think of the unfulfilled needs of workers in the fields of education, health, skill acquisition and differentiation, leisure, culture, housing, even in the richest capitalist countries of today, to see how this assumption remains accurate in spite of the so-called 'consumer society'. But it applies much more to the needs of the worker as a producer and a citizen—his need to develop a full personality, to become a rich and creative human being, etc.; these needs are brutally crushed by the tyranny of meaningless, mechanical, parcellized work, alienation of productive capacities and alienation of real human wealth.\" Mandel notes: \"In addition to this law of general relative impoverishment of workers under capitalism, Marx also notes a trend towards periodic absolute impoverishment , essentially in function of the movement of unemployment. This is closely linked to the inevitability of cyclical fluctuations under capitalism, that is the inevitability of periodic crises of overproduction, or 'recessions' as they are called today with less provocative connotations.\" Mandel then turns to a controversy that has raged \"for almost a century\": the question of the different values of 'skilled labour-power' and 'unskilled labour-power' (and whether Marx gives a satisfactory explanation of the fact that skilled labour produces more value in an hour than unskilled labour). \"Starting with Böhm-Bawerk, some critics have claimed to discover here one of the basic inconsistencies in Marx's economic theory. For if the greater productivity, in value terms, of skilled as opposed to unskilled workers is a function of the higher wages of the former, are we not back at Adam Smith's famous circular argument, in which the 'price of labour' determines the 'natural price' of goods but is in turn determined by the 'natural price' of one category of goods, so-called wage goods, that is food?\" Mandel's answer: \"Marx avoided such circular reasoning, contrary to what his critics mistakenly assume. He never explained the higher value content of an hour of skilled labour as compared to an hour of unskilled labour by the higher wages which skilled labour receives. This higher content is explained strictly in terms of the labour theory of value, by the additional labour costs necessary for producing the skill, in which are also included the total costs of schooling spent on those who do not successfully conclude their studies.\" Mandel then articulates the solution: \"The higher value produced by an hour of skilled labour, as compared to an hour of unskilled labour, results from the fact that skilled labour participates in the 'total labour-power' ( Gesamtarbeitsvermögen ) of society (or of a given branch of industry) not only with its own labour-power but also with a fraction of the labour-power necessary to produce its skill. In other words, each hour of skilled labour can be considered as an hour of unskilled labour multiplied by a coefficient dependent on this cost of schooling. Marx speaks in this context of 'composite labour' as against 'simple labour'. The skill, by analogy, can be compared to an additional tool, which is in itself not value-producing, but which transfers part of its own value into the value of the product produced by the skilled worker.\" Mandel attributes the solution first to Hilferding's answer to Böhm-Bawerk, then worked out by Hans Deutsch ( Qualificierte Arbeit und Kapitalismus , 1904) and Otto Bauer (1905–6). The controversy: Hilferding held that only the cost of production of skill (the work of the teacher, etc.) adds to the value of skilled labour-power, while Deutsch held that the time spent by the apprentice himself while learning must be added to those costs. Bauer supported Deutsch's thesis that \"the 'labour' of the apprentice (student) creates supplementary value and enters the process of value production of the skilled worker,\" but (contrary to Deutsch and together with Hilferding) contended that \"this value increases the surplus-value produced by the skilled worker, not the value of his own labour-power.\" This debate is documented in Rubin and Rosdolsky. X. MARX'S THEORY OF MONEY: Commodity Money, the Rejection of the Quantity Theory for Metallic Money, and the Logic of Paper Money Inflation Section X is Mandel's systematic account of Marx's monetary theory. It opens from a \"significant flaw in Ricardo's economic system.\" While Ricardo adheres to a strict labour theory of value concerning commodities, he contends that this is true for gold \"only if the quantity in circulation remains in exact proportion to the mass and prices of other commodities.\" Increases or decreases in this money circulation \"would provoke an increase or decrease in commodity prices and this in turn would provoke a further decrease or increase in the value of gold.\" Marx tries to overcome this inconsistency \"by integrating his theory of money into his general explanation of value, value production and autonomous value circulation (money circulation, capital circulation), on the basis of a rigorous application of the labour theory of value.\" Mandel stresses that \"as with the theory of value, the most important aspect of this monetary theory is the qualitative one, which has hitherto received too little attention from either the critics or the disciples of Marx.\" The fact that social labour, in a society based on generalized commodity production, is fragmented into many segments of private labour executed independently of each other, leads to the result that its social character can only be recognized post festum , through the sale of the commodity and depending upon the amount of equivalent it receives in this sale. \"The social character of the labour embedded in the commodity, therefore, can only appear as a thing outside the commodity—that is, money.\" Mandel explicitly connects this to the fetishism chapter: \"The fact that relations between human beings appear under capitalism (generalized commodity production) as relations between things—a phenomenon which Marx analysed at length in the fourth section on 'The Fetishism of the Commodity' of Chapter I of Capital Volume I—should, therefore, not be understood in the sense that people under capitalism, being in the grip of false consciousness, have the illusion of being confronted with things when in reality they are confronted with specific social relations of production. It is also an objective necessity and compulsion . Under conditions of generalized commodity production, social labour cannot be immediately recognized otherwise than through its exchange against money. The circulation of commodities cannot but produce its own counterpart in the circulation of the medium of exchange, money.\" Hence: \"Money is the necessary materialization of abstract social labour: that is the qualitative determinant in Marxist monetary theory.\" This is a crucial anti-idealist account of fetishism: the \"thing-like\" appearance of social relations is not merely an error of consciousness but a real structural necessity. Mandel then warns against the temptation to give money and money-creation functions \"which they cannot fulfil in a society based upon private property.\" \"To assume an 'automatic' realization of the exchange-value of commodities through the creation of an 'adequate' volume of money presupposes that that value is pre-established, that all labour expended on the production of these commodities was socially necessary labour. In other words, it presupposes that there exists a permanent equilibrium of supply with effective demand and, therefore, that there is no commodity production at all but a priori adaptation of production to consciously registered needs. Under capitalism, including monopoly capitalism, this can never be achieved.\" This directly targets Hilferding's proposal (in Das Finanzkapital ) for a category called \"socially necessary value of circulation\" ( gesellschaftlich notwendiger Zirkulationswert ), established by dividing the sum of values of all commodities by the velocity of circulation of money. Mandel criticizes this: \"Hilferding does not notice the incongruity of dividing quantities of value, i.e. socially necessary labour quanta, by the velocity of circulation media. Only prices (the monetary expression of value) can, of course, be so divided. Commodities cannot enter the circulation process except with (preliminary) prices.\" This is a fine analytical point about the distinction between value and price. Mandel then develops the commodity theory of money . \"Money born from the process of exchange, from the circulation of commodities, can realize the value of these commodities only because it itself has value, because it itself is a commodity produced by socially necessary abstract labour. Marx's theory of money is, therefore, above all a commodity theory of money in which the monetary standards (precious metals) enter the process of circulation with an intrinsic value of their own.\" From this point of view, \"Marx must reject any quantity theory of money applied to money based upon a gold or gold-and-silver basis.\" Mandel elaborates: \"When, with a given velocity of circulation, a given amount of gold has a value higher than that of the total mass of commodities against which it exchanges itself, it can no more 'lose' value (that is, provoke an increase of prices through abundance of bullion) in the circulation process than any other commodity. What happens is simply that part of it will be withdrawn from circulation and hoarded, until such time as the need for circulation again increases.\" The theory is thus rigorously consistent with the labour theory of value: gold cannot \"overflow\" into higher prices because excess gold is hoarded. But this changes with paper money: \"But if such a commodity theory of money implies a straight rejection of the quantity theory, as long as money is directly based upon precious metals, it points in the opposite direction as soon as we are faced with paper bank notes which function in reality as representatives of, and tokens for, precious metals.\" In this case, \"quite independently of whether or not there is legal convertibility of paper into gold, emission of paper money to the amount which, at a given value of gold and a given velocity of circulation of the bank notes, enables it to realize the prices of all the commodities in circulation, will leave these prices unaffected. But if this amount of paper money in circulation is doubled at its face value, all other things remaining equal, prices expressed in that currency will also double, not in contradiction with, but in application of, the labour theory of value.\" Mandel offers a concrete numerical illustration: \"To simplify, if we presume that each unit of gold circulates only once a year, the equation 1,000,000 tons of steel = 1,000 kilos of gold means that the same quantity of socially necessary abstract labour (say 100,000,000 man hours) has been necessary to produce the respective quantities of steel and gold. If £1,000,000 represents 1,000 kilos of gold, then the fact that the price of 1 ton of steel is £1 is just a straight expression of the equality in value (in quantities of abstract labour) between 1 ton of steel and 1 kilo of gold. But if, through additional issuing of paper money, 1,000 kilos of gold is now represented by £2,000,000 instead of £1,000,000, then, all other things remaining equal, the price of steel will rise from £1 to £2 in strict application of the labour theory of value.\" This is a beautifully compact exposition of Marx's theory of paper money inflation as a phenomenon derivable from the labour theory of value. But Mandel is careful: \"This does not mean that, with regard to paper money, Marx was the proponent of any mechanistic quantity theory. There is an evident analogy between his theory and the traditional forms of the quantity of money; but this analogy is limited by two essential factors.\" First: \"for Marx, with paper money as with metallic money, it is the movement of the value of commodities, that is fluctuations of material production and of productivity of labour, which remains the primum movens of price fluctuations, not the ups and downs of the quantity of paper money in circulation.\" Mandel cites Volume 3, where Marx \"examines the need to increase money circulation at the moment of the outbreak of the crisis,\" and \"sharply criticizes the role which the Bank of England played, through the application of the 'currency principle', in accentuating money panics and monetary crises as accelerators of crises of overproduction when these coincided with an outflow of gold from England.\" But Marx also \"denied any possibility of preventing recessions by issuing additional money.\" Second: \"Marx understood perfectly that the dialectical interrelationship of all the elements of a mechanistic quantity theory equation excludes the possibility of simply deriving conclusions from independent variations of a single one of these elements. He knew, for example, that the velocity of circulation of money was co-determined by the trade cycle, and could not be considered stable in a given phase when only the quantity of money was supposed to change.\" The volume-velocity interdependence means the quantity theory is only a first approximation. Mandel notes he will deal with the fuller analysis in the introduction to Volume 3. Mandel then observes the transformation of money into money capital: \"With the development and generalization of commodity production, money becomes more and more transformed into money capital. It is more and more replaced by 'monetary signs' in the process of circulation, and becomes more and more transformed from a means of exchange into a means of payment, that is into the counterpart of debts, into an instrument of credit.\" But \"in examining the credit role of money Marx maintains a rigorous adherence to the labour theory of value, so that his whole economic system is thoroughly 'monistic'.\" Here is the monism of Marx's system: \"Money as the general equivalent of the exchange value of all commodities and money as the means to settle debts (resulting out of the generalization of sales on credit) are both claims on a given fraction of the total labour expenditure of society in a given period. Whatever the 'nominal' value of the currency, and whatever the 'standard of measurement' of prices, it is obviously impossible to distribute more labour quantities than have been produced and stocked within the same period of time.\" This is a striking and rigorous statement of the real limits of credit creation. \"On the other hand, given the very nature of commodity production, no general increase of money circulation (no increase of 'aggregate demand') can in the long run prevent the eventuality that a whole series of commodities produced will not meet the 'specific demand' they need to allow their proprietors to realize at least the average rate of profit. Technological changes, differences in productivity between different plants and firms, changes in real wages and in the structure of consumer expenditures, changes in the rate of profit entailing changes in the direction and structure of investment: all these complex movements which make the trade cycle and periodic recessions possible and indeed unavoidable under conditions of generalized commodity production cannot be eliminated by manipulation of currency volume or currency units.\" Mandel concludes with the verdict: \"Experience since Marx's death, and especially since the 'Keynesian revolution', fully confirms the correctness of this diagnosis, although it also confirms that, under specific conditions and within specific limits, monetary policies can reduce the amplitude of economic fluctuations, a fact of which Marx was perfectly aware.\" This is an important nuance: Mandel does not deny that monetary policy can do something , only that it cannot abolish the cycle. Mandel then turns to Marx's comments on gold as world currency. \"Marx's short comments on the dual nature of gold, as the basis 'in the last resort' of all paper money systems and as the only possible 'world currency' acceptable for final settlement of accounts between the central banks (and bourgeois classes) of different nations, make especially interesting reading today, when the Bretton Woods monetary system has broken down because of the inconvertibility of the dollar into gold.\" Mandel notes that Marx, \"while rejecting all theories which explain the 'value' of money by convention or state compulsion, does relate this role of gold as a means of final settlement of accounts on an international scale to the specific role of the bourgeois state. Among the functions of the state is that of creating the 'general conditions for capitalist production'. A coherent and accepted currency certainly belongs to these 'general conditions'.\" But: \"Paper money with a fixed rate of exchange ( Zwangskurs ) can be imposed only through the authority of the state within given limits. But where this authority is absent, proprietors of commodities cannot be forced to accept in exchange for their goods paper money whose rate they consider inflated.\" Hence: \"'Paper gold' as a universal means of exchange and payment on the world market presupposes, therefore, a world government, in other words the absence of inter-imperialist competition and, therefore, in the last analysis the withering away of private property. To expect such a situation to occur under capitalism is utopian.\" This is one of the most prescient passages in the Introduction, anticipating the end of Bretton Woods and the difficulties of any global monetary regime under capitalism. Finally, Mandel sketches the history of Marxist debates on money: \"Marx's theory of money has been much less analysed, criticized and discussed by later Marxists than other parts of his economic theory. An interesting discussion did, however, occur on the eve of the First World War between Hilferding, Kautsky and Varga, on the possibility of deducing from the value of commodities a 'socially necessary volume of money'—a hypothesis which is obviously mistaken since it confuses the value of commodities with their price. Varga, moreover, in a series of polemics which were continued in the early twenties, persisted in maintaining that, as central banks bought gold at a fixed price, the fluctuations of the intrinsic value of gold would not influence the general level of prices, but only govern the ups and downs of the differential rent commanded by gold mines with a productivity above the level allowing the average rate of profit at the given price of gold.\" Mandel concludes: \"Subsequent developments, especially in the last four or five years, have confirmed that both these attempted corrections of Marx's theory of money were unfounded and wrong.\" This historical-doctrinal perspective is important: even respected Marxist theorists erred in trying to \"correct\" Marx on money. XI. CAPITAL AND THE DESTINY OF CAPITALISM: Integration of Theory and History, the Definition of Capitalism, and the \"Subjective Factor\" The final section is Mandel's grand summary and his most directly political terrain. He opens: \"It is above all through its integration of theory and history that Marxism manifests its superiority in the economic domain over classical and neo-classical political economy. It is through its ability to foresee correctly long-term trends of capitalist development, including the main inner contradictions of the capitalist mode of production which propel this long-term development forward, that Capital continues to fascinate friend and foe alike.\" Mandel addresses the critics: \"Those who, generation after generation, continue to accuse Marx of 'unscientific' parti pris or speculative excursions into the realms of prophecy cannot escape the burden of proof. It is up to them to account for the mysterious fact that a thinker according to them so devoid of analytical tools should have been able unfailingly to work out the long-term laws of motion that have determined the development of capitalism for a century and a half.\" This is the \"argument from the success of the theory,\" reinforcing the earlier Popper critique. He then identifies the two theoretical claims most under attack: \"Apart from the so-called law of increasing absolute impoverishment of the working class wrongly attributed to Marx, the aspect of the latter's theoretical conclusions concerning the capitalist mode of production which has been most consistently under attack since Capital Volume 1 first appeared has been the so-called 'theory of the inevitable collapse of capitalism' ( Zusammenbruchstheorie ). First strongly challenged by the Bernsteinian 'revisionists' within the socialist movement, and only weakly defended by most orthodox Marxists of the epoch, the theory has been exposed to ridicule by a monotonous succession of authors in the last decades. All have asked the ritual rhetorical question: has not the capitalist mode of production shown a capacity of adaptation and self-reform far beyond anything which Marx foresaw?\" Mandel notes Hilferding's own proposal in Das Finanzkapital of the theoretical possibility of an \"organized\" capitalism without crises through a \"general cartel.\" Mandel argues that these critiques \"contain a basic flaw: they try to prove too much. They contend that capitalism has survived so many crises that nobody can seriously challenge its capacity to survive future ones. But at the same time they also contend that the present economic system in the West cannot any longer be characterized as 'capitalist'; and that through successive self-reform and adaptation, in order to overcome crises which threatened to wreck it, capitalism has transformed itself into a new social organization of the economy. This they most often characterize by the term 'mixed economy', although a host of other formulas such as 'managerial capitalism', 'organized capitalism', 'managerial society', 'technostructure rule', etc. have at times been devised to describe it.\" The critique seems unable to consistently maintain both that capitalism persists and that it has been abolished. This is a sharp logical point. Mandel then offers his definition of capitalism , drawn from Capital : Capitalism \"is neither a society of 'perfect competition', nor a society of 'increasing pauperism', nor a society where 'private entrepreneurs rule the factories', nor even a society in which 'money is the one and only master'. Vague and imprecise definitions of this type, which allow evasion of the basic issues, lead to endless confusion about the relationship of today's economic system in the West with the economic system analysed by Capital .\" Instead: \" Capital shows that the capitalist mode of production is fundamentally determined by three conditions and three only: (1) the fact that the mass of producers are not owners of the means of production in the economic sense of the word, but have to sell their labour-power to the owners; (2) the fact that these owners are organized into separate firms which compete with each other for shares of the market on which commodities are sold, for profitable fields of investment for capital, for sources of raw materials, etc. (that is, the institution of private property in the economic sense of the word); (3) the fact that these same owners of the means of production (different firms) are, therefore, compelled to extort the maximum surplus-value from the producers, in order to accumulate more and more capital—which leads, under conditions of generalized commodity production and generalized alienation, to constantly growing mechanization of labour, concentration and centralization of capital, growing organic composition of capital, the tendency for the rate of profit to fall, and periodically recurrent crises of over-production.\" Mandel adds: \"If these are the criteria, there can be no question that Western society is still capitalist; that wage-labour and capital are still the two antagonistic classes of society; that accumulation of capital is more than ever the basic motive force of that society; and that the extortion and realization of private profit governs the basic drive of separate firms.\" He then anticipates possible objections: \"Such aspects of contemporary Western society as the fact that some of these firms are nationalized; that there is growing state intervention in the economy; that competition has become 'imperfect' (that it is no longer essentially fought by cutting prices, but rather by reducing production costs and increasing distribution and sales); that workers have strong trade unions (except when, under conditions of violent social crisis, bourgeois democratic freedoms are abolished) and that their standard of living has risen far more than Marx expected it to rise—all this in no way abolishes or reduces the relevance of the basic structural features of capitalism, as defined by Capital , from which all the basic laws of motion of the system flow. These basic laws of motion thus continue to remain valid.\" Mandel then makes his strongest comparative claim: \"Without courting paradox one could even contend that, from a structural point of view, the 'concrete' capitalism of the final quarter of the twentieth century is much closer to the 'abstract' model of Capital than was the 'concrete' capitalism of 1867, when Marx finished correcting the proofs of Volume 1.\" He gives three reasons: First: \"because the intermediate class of small independent producers, proprietors of their own means of production, which was still a significant social layer a century ago, has today nearly been eroded out of existence; dependent wage and salary earners, compelled to sell their labour-power, now amount to over 80 per cent of the economically active population in most Western countries and in several to over 90 per cent.\" Second: \"because concentration and centralization of capital has led to a situation where not only do a couple of hundred giant corporations dominate the economy of each imperialist country, but a few hundred multi-national corporations also concentrate in their hands one third of all the wealth of the capitalist world economy.\" Third: \"because the productivity and the objective socialization of labour have increased to such an extent that production of value for private enrichment has become absurd beyond anything Marx could have foreseen a century ago and the world cries out so compellingly for a planned husbanding of resources to satisfy needs on the basis of consciously and democratically chosen priorities that even opponents of socialism cannot fail to understand the message.\" Mandel then confronts the obvious question: \"Why then, one might ask, have the expropriators not yet become the expropriated, and why does capitalism still survive in the highly industrialized countries?\" His answer: \"The answer to that question would involve a detailed critical review of twentieth-century political and social history. But the whole point is, of course, that Marx never predicted any sudden and automatic collapse of the capitalist system in one 'final' crisis, due to a single economic 'cause'. In the famous Chapter 32 of Capital Volume 1, 'The Historical Tendency of Capitalist Accumulation', Marx describes economic tendencies provoking a reaction from social forces. The growth of the proletariat, of its exploitation, and of organized revolt against that exploitation, are the main levers for the overthrow of capitalism. Centralization of the means of production and objective socialization of labour create the economic preconditions for a society based upon collective property and free co-operation by associated producers. But they do not automatically produce such a society on some universal day of victory. They have to be consciously utilized, at privileged moments of social crisis, to bring about the revolutionary overthrow of the system.\" Mandel insists: \"Marx was as far removed from any fatalistic belief in the automatic effects of economic determinism as any social thinker could be. He repeated over and over again that men made and had to make their own history, only not in an arbitrary way and independently from the material conditions in which they found themselves.\" Hence: \"Any theory of the collapse of capitalism, therefore, can only present itself as Marxist if it is a theory of conscious overthrow of capitalism, that is, a theory of socialist revolution. Chapter 32, at the end of Capital Volume 1, only indicates in very general terms how and why objective inner contradictions of the capitalist mode of production make this overthrow both possible and necessary. The rest has to result, in Marx's words, from the growth of 'the revolt of the working class, a class constantly increasing in numbers, and trained, united and organized by the very mechanism of the capitalist process of production'.\" Mandel then introduces his central theoretical construct: \"In other words, between the growing economic contradictions of the capitalist mode of production on the one hand, and the collapse of capitalism on the other hand, there is a necessary mediation : the development of the class consciousness, organized strength and capacity for revolutionary action of the working class (including revolutionary leadership). That chapter of Marxist theory is not incorporated into Capital . Perhaps Marx intended to discuss it in the book on the State which he wanted to write but never came even to draft. At all events, he left no systematic exposition of his thought in this respect, although many ideas on the subject are to be found scattered throughout his articles and letters. It was up to his most gifted followers, foremost among them Lenin, Trotsky and Rosa Luxemburg, to deal systematically with what one might call 'the Marxist theory of the subjective factor '.\" Mandel quotes Marx's famous letter to Friedrich Bolte (23 November 1871) on the necessity for previous organization of the working class and for systematic education through propaganda, agitation and action. He also invokes Rosa Luxemburg's 1899 formulation of the dilemma: \"The production relations of capitalist society approach more and more the production relations of socialist society. But on the other hand, its political and juridical relations [and, one might add, their ideological reflections in the minds of men as well] establish between capitalist society and socialist society a steadily rising wall. This wall is not overthrown but on the contrary strengthened and consolidated by the development of social reforms and the course of [bourgeois parliamentary] democracy.\" Mandel then reviews the empirical history of the twentieth century: \"The survival of capitalism to this day in the most industrialized countries has certainly given it a life-span far beyond what Marx expected. But this is not because the system has developed in essentially other directions than those predicted by Capital . Nor is it because it has been able to avoid a periodic repetition of explosive social crises. On the contrary, since the Russian revolution of 1905, and certainly since the outbreak of the First World War, such crises have become recurrent features of contemporary history.\" \"In the course of such crises, capitalism has indeed been overthrown in many countries, Russia and China being the most important. But contrary to what Marx expected, this overthrow occurred not so much where the proletariat was most strongly developed numerically and economically, as a result of the greatest possible extension of capitalist industry, that is, in those countries which also have a powerful bourgeois class. It occurred rather in those countries where the bourgeoisie was weakest and where, therefore, the political relationship of forces was favourable for a young proletariat capable of gaining the support of a strongly rebellious peasantry.\" Mandel says this \"historical detour can be understood only if one integrates into the analysis two key factors: on the one hand, the development of imperialism and its effect on the large part of the human race which lives in socially and economically underdeveloped societies (the law of uneven and combined development ); on the other hand, the interrelationship between the lack of revolutionary experience on the part of the Western working class during the long period of 'organic growth' of imperialism (1890-1914) and the growing reformism and integration of social democracy into bourgeois society and the bourgeois state which were responsible for the failure of the first large-scale revolutionary crises in the West, in 1918-23 (above all in Germany and Italy). As a result of this failure, the victorious Russian revolution itself became isolated, and the international working-class movement went through the dark interlude of Stalinism, from which it only slowly began to emerge in the nineteen-fifties. This brings us back to... the Marxist theory of the subjective factor—and incidentally explains why, after the rich flowering of Marxist economic theory in the period 1895-1930, a quarter of a century of almost total stagnation occurred in that field too.\" The final paragraph addresses the \"breakdown\" debate once and for all. \"The debate around the Zusammenbruchstheorie has suffered from a confusion between two different questions: the question whether the replacement of capitalism by socialism is inevitable (an inevitable result of the inner economic contradictions of the capitalist mode of production); and the question whether, in the absence of a socialist revolution, capitalism would live on for ever. A negative answer to the first question in no way implies a positive one to the second. Indeed, classical Marxists, following the young Marx, formulated their prognosis in the form of a dilemma: socialism or barbarism .\" Mandel's closing thought: \"The social catastrophes which mankind has witnessed since Auschwitz and Hiroshima indicate that there was nothing 'romantic' in such a prognosis, but that it expressed a clear insight into the terrifying destructive potential of exchange-value production, capital accumulation, and the struggle for personal enrichment as ends in themselves. The concrete mechanics of the economic breakdown of capitalist economy may be open to conjecture. The interrelationship of the downturn of value production (decline of the total number of labour hours produced as a result of semi-automation), of the increasing difficulty of realizing surplus-value, of increasing output of waste not entering the reproduction process, of increasing depletion of national resources and, above all, of long-term decline of the rate of profit, is still far from clear. But a very strong case can be made for the thesis that there are definite limits to the adaptability of capitalist relations of production, and that these limits are being progressively attained in one field after another.\" Mandel concludes: \"It is most unlikely that capitalism will survive another half-century of the crises (military, political, social, monetary, cultural) which have occurred uninterruptedly since 1914. It is most probable, moreover, that Capital and what it stands for—namely a scientific analysis of bourgeois society which represents the proletariat's class consciousness at its highest level—will in the end prove to have made a decisive contribution to capitalism's replacement by a classless society of associated producers.\" This is Mandel's final formulation of the unity of science and revolution that has run through the entire Introduction. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles and Systemic Laws Revealed Mandel's Introduction establishes several foundational principles that run through his reading of Marx. First , the historical relativity of economic laws : there is no \"pure\" economic theory abstracted from a specific social structure, just as there is no \"pure\" anatomy. Each mode of production has its own laws of motion; the attempt to find universal laws crosses over from economic theory to historical materialism. This principle has an unusually wide application—from methodological debates in economics to comparative-historical analysis—and it grounds Mandel's claim that Capital remains valid for analysing commodity production even in postcapitalist societies (USSR, China, etc.), where the law of value continues to operate in its \"disintegrating logic.\" Second , the centrality of the labour theory of value as the corner-stone of the whole Marxian system . Mandel explicitly warns that the theory's critics \"show a sharp class instinct\" in attacking value, because \"this theory is indeed the corner-stone of the whole system.\" But he does not rest on this defensive claim. He shows the theory's explanatory functions : (1) it governs exchange relations as the axis around which long-term relative prices oscillate; (2) it determines the proportional distribution of total social labour across branches of production; (3) it rules economic growth by channelling investment through average-profit signals. From this \"monistic\" foundation flow monetary theory, capital accumulation theory, the trade-cycle theory, the theory of the rate of profit and its tendency to decline, and ultimately the theory of crises. The labour theory of value is thus not just an abstract position but a research programme with systematic consequences. Third , the qualitative dimension of value and money . Marx's distinctive contribution is not merely to have specified a quantity (socially necessary abstract labour) but to have shown that the form of value—its appearance as a thing, as money, as capital—is itself a real material necessity of a society in which labour is private. Fetishism, on Mandel's reading, is not a false consciousness problem but an objective structural determination. This rescues Marx from purely \"mentalistic\" or \"superstructural\" readings of his theory of value. Fourth , the double nature of labour (concrete/useful and abstract/value-creating) as the key to the double nature of the commodity (use-value/exchange-value), and hence to the possibility of both conserving and creating value in the same labour act. This is the differential specifica of the capitalist mode of production: capital not only appropriates surplus-value but produces it. Fifth , the theory of surplus-value as appropriation/deduction theory, not unearned increment . This distinction (directed at Blaug) is crucial: the profits, rents, and interest of the whole bourgeois class are not windfall gains but the systematic conversion of social surplus labour into the form of value. Sixth , the \"various capitals\" and competition as the primum movens of accumulation . Capital is not accumulative by psychological propensity but by structural compulsion. Total monopolization (\"a single world trust\") would mean stagnation—an important, non-tautological grounding of Marx's theory of accumulation. Seventh , the distinction between the value of labour-power and real wages . This is perhaps the single most important analytical distinction in Mandel's Introduction, and it is the hinge on which his refutation of the \"absolute impoverishment\" thesis rests. Long-term capital accumulation tends to reduce the value of labour-power (because it reduces the value of wage goods) while permitting stability or growth in real wages , provided the working class can expand the \"moral-historical\" component of its standard of living through class struggle. Eighth , the distinction between technical and social division of labour, and between capital and capitalism . The technical division of labour is a transhistorical source of productivity; capital is a social relation between owners of accumulated value and non-owners. Capitalism is the mode of production in which capital seizes the sphere of production; capital as merchant's and usurer's capital is a precapitalist phenomenon. Ninth , the \"subjective factor\" as necessary mediation between economic contradictions and socialist transformation . This is Mandel's most distinctive contribution to the Zusammenbruchstheorie debate: between the economic contradictions of capital and its overthrow stands the development of working-class consciousness and organization. Without this theory of the subjective factor, no theory of collapse can be Marxist. Theoretical, Practical, and Philosophical Implications The Introduction's practical and political implications are inseparable from its theoretical ones. Mandel's central political conclusion—socialism or barbarism—is not rhetorical but follows from his analysis of the destructive potentialities of the capitalist mode of production once it has created the material preconditions for emancipation and yet has not been overthrown. As Mandel puts it, the contradiction between use-value and exchange-value \"fully unfolds itself in this contradictory nature of capitalist machinery\"—a contradiction that, unremedied, implies the transformation of productive forces into forces of destruction \"of life tout court.\" Theoretically, the Introduction makes several important contributions to Marxology. (1) It defends the dialectical method against positivistic (Popper) and romantic (Luxemburgian) critiques while insisting that the dialectic is materialist, empirically grounded, and reproducible-testable through praxis. (2) It clarifies the different levels of abstraction at which the three (or four) volumes of Capital operate, thereby refuting the supposed contradictions attributed to Marx by Joan Robinson and others. (3) It defends the historical dimension of Capital —its unity of logical and historical analysis—against readings that reduce it to a purely \"logical\" or purely \"essentialist\" work. (4) It provides a rigorous distinction between Verwertung (valorization, a production concept) and Realisation (realization, a circulation concept), and points to a translation error in the Pelican Grundrisse . (5) It outlines a specific methodology for empirically measuring the labour content of commodities under capitalism (\"opening the books\" of firms). (6) It clarifies Marx's theory of skilled labour in a way that frees it from the circularity of the Smithian conception. (7) It formulates a rigorous commodity theory of money that distinguishes Marx from quantity-theory accounts even for paper money, and anticipates the collapse of Bretton Woods and the impossibility of a stable world currency under capitalism. (8) It lays out a theory of \"imperialism as value transfer\"—the world market as \"a gigantic syphon to transfer value from the south to the north\"—which is one of Mandel's recurring contributions to Marxist theory. Philosophically, the Introduction stakes out a distinctive position. Against deterministic readings of Marx, Mandel insists on the necessity of the \"subjective factor\" in the transition from capitalism to socialism. Against voluntaristic readings, he insists that men \"make their own history, only not in an arbitrary way and independently from the material conditions in which they found themselves.\" Against rationalist reconstruction (the neo-Ricardian \"correctors\"), he insists on the abstract social labour foundation of Marx's theory, not merely labour as numeraire . Against empiricist reductionism (the neo-positivist emphasis on studying observed prices alone), he insists on the scientific necessity of theory and on the practical impossibility of doing without \"elementary principles\" like value. Against the fetishism-as-false-consciousness interpretation, he argues that the appearance of social relations as relations between things is a real, structural necessity of generalized commodity production. For contemporary readers, Mandel's Introduction is thus both an introduction to and a defence of a scientific research programme and a political project at once. It leaves us with a set of analytical tools—the labour theory of value, the theory of surplus-value, the theory of accumulation, the theory of wages as an accumulation theory, the theory of money—that permit the study of contemporary capitalist societies as continuing to be determined by the same laws of motion Marx discovered. It leaves us, equally, with a picture of Marx as neither a fatalist nor a mere moralist, but a scientific analyst whose revolutionary commitment motivated rather than compromised his scientific rigour. And it leaves us with the closing thought—at once analytic and existential—that the question at the end of the twentieth century is not \"whether capitalism will collapse\" but \"socialism or barbarism.\" The chapter's enduring lesson is that the analysis of Capital and the fate of capitalism are not two separate things: \"the proletariat's class consciousness at its highest level\" is the theory of that class's destiny, and vice versa. In Mandel's own words, \"The question whether it has any use at all in economic analysis is, therefore, as meaningless as the question whether you need the concept of basic particles (atoms, etc.) in physics. Indeed, no coherent and consistent analysis of the capitalist economy in its totality, explaining all the basic laws of motion of that system, is possible without 'elementary principles' organized around the value of commodities.\" The final wager of the Introduction is that, just as those elementary particles made possible a science of matter, the elementary principle of abstract social labour makes possible a science of value—and, through it, of revolution, of the destiny of capitalism, and of the classless society of associated producers that—Marx and Mandel both insist—can only be won by conscious, organized human action. Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 1"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 1", "title": "Chapter 1", "subtitle": "The Commodity", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 1/Chapter 1  The Commodity_explanation.html", "word_count": 18968, "read_time": "95 min read", "tags": ["Value & Commodity Form", "Fetishism & Mystification of Capital", "Dialectical Method & Critique of Political Economy"], "keywords": ["Use-Value vs Exchange-Value", "Dual Character of Labour (Concrete vs Abstract)", "Socially Necessary Labour Time (SNLT)", "Value-Form Analysis (Elementary, Expanded, General, Money)", "Commodity Fetishism & Reification", "Substance and Magnitude of Value"], "text": "Chapter 1 The Commodity - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 18,946 words ⏱️ ~95 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION: MARX, CAPITAL , VOLUME I, CHAPTER 1 — \"THE COMMODITY\" 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Core Thesis Chapter 1 of Capital , Volume I, \"The Commodity,\" is the foundational analytic keystone upon which the entire architecture of Marx's critique of political economy rests. Its central thesis is deceptively simple but extraordinarily dense in its implications: the wealth of capitalist societies manifests as an \"immense collection of commodities,\" and the individual commodity is the \"elementary form\" ( Elementarform ) of that wealth. Consequently, the entire capitalist mode of production must be understood by first dissecting the commodity itself — its dual nature as both use-value and value, the dual character of the labour that produces it, the necessary development of its value-form into money, and the peculiar \"fetishism\" that attaches to the products of labour as soon as they are produced as commodities. Marx's intellectual objective in this chapter is not merely to define terms or to offer a static taxonomy of economic categories. He is performing a genealogical and dialectical reconstruction of the most basic categories of bourgeois economic thought. He begins from the most immediate, empirically given phenomenon — the commodity as a \"trivial thing\" — and demonstrates that this seemingly obvious object is \"a very strange thing, abounding in metaphysical subtleties and theological niceties.\" The chapter's trajectory is therefore from surface appearance (the commodity as an obvious object of exchange) to hidden essence (value as congealed abstract labour), and then back again to a higher level of surface appearance (the money-form as the necessary and historically specific mode of expression of value), now understood in its full complexity. The final movement — the analysis of commodity fetishism — reveals that the categories of bourgeois economics are themselves historically specific forms of thought, socially valid for commodity-producing societies but falsely absolutized as eternal truths. The Central Problem The central problem motivating the chapter is a question Marx formulates explicitly near its conclusion: Why does labour express itself in value, and why does the measurement of labour by its duration express itself in the magnitude of the value of the product? This is the question classical political economy — even in its best representatives, Adam Smith and Ricardo — never once asked. Classical economy analysed value and its magnitude, however incompletely, and \"uncovered the content concealed within these forms. But it has never once asked the question why this content has assumed that particular form.\" The failure to pose this question is, for Marx, not an incidental oversight but a symptom of a deeper theoretical imprisonment: the inability to see the value-form as historically specific rather than as \"the eternal natural form of social production.\" The chapter therefore operates simultaneously on three levels: Analytical-descriptive : defining and elaborating the categories of use-value, value, abstract and concrete labour, socially necessary labour-time, relative and equivalent forms of value. Critical-genealogical : showing how these categories necessarily develop from the simplest form of value (the accidental exchange relation between two commodities) to the money-form, and demonstrating that each stage of this development resolves the internal contradictions of the previous stage. Critical-ideological : exposing the fetishism of commodities — the process whereby social relations between persons appear as social relations between things — and demonstrating that the categories of bourgeois political economy are \"forms of thought which are socially valid, and therefore objective, for the relations of production belonging to this historically determined mode of social production.\" Fit Within the Broader Theoretical Framework The chapter serves as the point of departure for all three volumes of Capital and for Marx's broader critique of political economy. Its placement is strategic: the commodity is the \"cell-form\" of bourgeois society, and its analysis must yield the categories — value, money, capital, surplus-value — that will subsequently be developed. The chapter's conclusion explicitly points forward: the commodity-form, \"the most general and the most undeveloped form of bourgeois production,\" unfolds into more concrete forms — money, capital, the wage-form, ground rent — whose fetishistic character is correspondingly more difficult to penetrate. The chapter thus functions as the methodological foundation for the entire work: it establishes the dialectical materialist method of analysing social forms, demonstrates the procedure of ascending from the abstract to the concrete, and provides the conceptual vocabulary (value, value-form, fetishism) that will be presupposed throughout the rest of the text. Significantly, the chapter also positions itself critically against both \"bourgeois\" economics and \"vulgar\" economics. The classical economists (Petty, Smith, Ricardo) grasped the substance and magnitude of value but treated the form as a matter of indifference. The vulgar economists (Bailey, Broadhurst, Macleod) elevated the form alone — the quantitative relation of exchange — to the status of value itself, thereby obliterating any conceptual determination of value. Marx's own position is that value must be analysed in its unity of substance, magnitude, and form; every attempt to isolate one of these dimensions from the others produces theoretical incoherence. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Point of Departure: The Commodity as the Elementary Form of Capitalist Wealth Marx opens the chapter with one of the most celebrated sentences in all of economic literature: \"The wealth of societies in which the capitalist mode of production prevails appears as an 'immense collection of commodities'; the individual commodity appears as its elementary form.\" Several crucial conceptual choices are embedded in this single sentence. First, Marx does not say that the wealth of all societies appears this way — he specifies \"societies in which the capitalist mode of production prevails.\" This is an immediate signal that the commodity is not a transhistorical category but a historically specific form of social wealth. The category is indexed to a particular mode of production. Second, the word \"appears\" ( erscheint ) signals from the outset that surface appearance is precisely what the investigation must penetrate. What appears as an \"immense collection of commodities\" is actually, beneath the surface, a system of social relations. Third, the reference to the commodity as the \"elementary form\" ( Elementarform ) establishes the methodological principle of beginning the analysis with the simplest, most abstract unit of the social formation — analogous to the cell in biology. The investigation of the whole must begin with the analysis of this elementary particle. The reference to the commodity as \"first of all an external object, a thing which through its qualities satisfies human needs of whatever kind\" establishes the initial moment of the dialectic: the commodity must satisfy a need to count as a commodity at all, and the nature of the need — whether it arises \"from the stomach, or the imagination\" — is declared irrelevant. This is a deliberate abstraction. Marx is not concerned with the phenomenological or anthropological specificity of needs; he is concerned with the formal conditions under which objects of utility become commodities. Similarly, it does not matter whether the thing satisfies needs directly (as an object of consumption) or indirectly (as a means of production). What matters is that it is \"useful.\" Use-Value and Its Complexities Every useful thing can be considered from the point of view of quality and quantity. This dual perspective on utility is not merely a logical distinction but is governed by a historical dynamic: \"The discovery of these ways and hence of the manifold uses of things is the work of history. So also is the invention of socially recognized standards of measurement for the quantities of these useful objects.\" The development of use-value is thus itself historically conditioned, a fact Marx emphasizes with his reference to the magnet: \"The magnet's property of attracting iron only became useful once it had led to the discovery of magnetic polarity.\" This example illustrates a crucial point: a natural property becomes a use-value only when it is incorporated into a social practice, into the historical development of human needs and productive capacities. Use-value is therefore not simply a brute natural property but a historically mediated one. The definition of use-value is precise: \"The usefulness of a thing makes it a use-value. But this usefulness does not dangle in mid-air. It is conditioned by the physical properties of the commodity, and has no existence apart from the latter. It is therefore the physical body of the commodity itself, for instance iron, corn, a diamond, which is the use-value or useful thing.\" This material grounding of use-value is essential. It cannot be reduced to subjective preference or utility in the utilitarian sense; it is bound to the physical being of the thing. Consequently, \"this property of a commodity is independent of the amount of labour required to appropriate its useful qualities.\" Use-value is the material content of wealth, and as such, it \"constitutes the material content of wealth, whatever its social form may be.\" But in the specific form of society Marx is analysing, use-values are also \"the material bearers ( Träger ) of — exchange-value.\" This term Träger is significant. In Marx's vocabulary, Träger (bearer, or support) designates an agent or vehicle that carries a social relation without being reducible to it. The commodity as a material thing is a bearer of exchange-value; it supports and carries a social relation that is not itself material. This distinction between the material substrate and the social relation is fundamental to the chapter's central argument about value. When Marx says that \"when examining use-values, we always assume we are dealing with definite quantities, such as dozens of watches, yards of linen, or tons of iron,\" he introduces the dimension of quantity that will reappear in the analysis of value magnitude. The commercial knowledge of commodities is the branch of science devoted to use-values; it is a special branch precisely because use-values are qualitatively heterogeneous. Exchange-Value and Its Apparent Relativity The transition from use-value to exchange-value is effected with the sentence: \"Exchange-value appears first of all as the quantitative relation, the proportion, in which use-values of one kind exchange for use-values of another kind. This relation changes constantly with time and place.\" The phenomena to be explained are the fluctuating ratios of exchange. Initially, exchange-value appears \"accidental and purely relative,\" and consequently \"an intrinsic value, i.e. an exchange-value that is inseparably connected with the commodity, inherent in it, seems a contradiction in terms.\" This is the philosophical problem: if exchange-value appears as a purely relational, accidental, and fluctuating magnitude, how can it be an intrinsic property of the commodity? Marx cites Barbon — \"Nothing can have an intrinsick value\" — and the popular verse from Samuel Butler: \"The value of a thing / Is just as much as it will bring.\" The vulgar and nominalist conception that value is simply the exchange relation is thus immediately present as a position to be overcome. The resolution of this contradiction proceeds through a mathematical and logical argument. Consider a given commodity, such as a quarter of wheat, exchanged for x boot-polish, y silk, or z gold, etc. The wheat has many exchange values instead of one. But if x boot-polish, y silk, and z gold are all exchange-values of the same quarter of wheat, then \"these valid exchange-values of a particular commodity express something equal,\" and \"exchange-value cannot be anything other than the mode of expression, the 'form of appearance' ( Erscheinungsform ), of a content distinguishable from it.\" The multiple empirical exchange ratios of a single commodity are thus not a sign of the arbitrariness of value but a sign that each ratio expresses the same underlying content in a different material form. If a quarter of wheat can be equated with x boot-polish and also with y silk, then the wheat must have something in common with both, and the boot-polish and silk must also have this something in common between themselves. Marx formalizes this: \"Let us now take two commodities, for example corn and iron. Whatever their exchange relation may be, it can always be represented by an equation in which a given quantity of corn is equated to some quantity of iron, for instance 1 quarter of corn = x cwt of iron. What does this equation signify? It signifies that a common element of identical magnitude exists in two different things, in 1 quarter of corn and similarly in x cwt of iron. Both are therefore equal to a third thing, which in itself is neither the one nor the other.\" The Geometrical Analogy and the Reduction to Common Substance Marx illustrates this logical operation with a geometrical analogy that has important methodological implications: \"In order to determine and compare the areas of all rectilinear figures we split them up into triangles. Then the triangle itself is reduced to an expression totally different from its visible shape: half the product of the base and the altitude. In the same way the exchange values of commodities must be reduced to a common element, of which they represent a greater or a lesser quantity.\" The analogy underscores that the common element sought is not visible on the surface; it is a conceptual abstraction achieved through analysis, just as the area of a triangle is not visible as such but is derived analytically from base and altitude. The reduction of diverse figures to a common measure is precisely what is at stake. The common element \"cannot be a geometrical, physical, chemical or other natural property of commodities. Such properties come into consideration only to the extent that they make the commodities useful, i.e. turn them into use-values. But clearly, the exchange relation of commodities is characterized precisely by its abstraction from their use-values.\" The exchange relation is characterized by an abstraction from use-value; therefore the common element cannot be use-value itself. This is a crucial point. Marx cites Barbon's assertion: \"One sort of wares are as good as another, if the value be equal. There is no difference or distinction in things of equal value... One hundred pounds worth of lead or iron, is of as great a value as one hundred pounds worth of silver and gold.\" The equality of value implies the equivalence of qualitatively different use-values; therefore, the quality of the use-value cannot be what constitutes value. \"As use-values, commodities differ above all in quality, while as exchange-values they can only differ in quantity, and therefore do not contain an atom of use-value.\" Abstract Human Labour as the Substance of Value Having abstracted from use-value, \"only one property remains, that of being products of labour.\" But Marx immediately qualifies this: \"even the product of labour has already been transformed in our hands. If we make abstraction from its use-value, we abstract also from the material constituents and forms which make it a use-value. It is no longer a table, a house, a piece of yarn or any other useful thing. All its sensuous characteristics are extinguished. Nor is it any longer the product of the labour of the joiner, the mason or the spinner, or of any other particular kind of productive labour. With the disappearance of the useful character of the products of labour, the useful character of the kinds of labour embodied in them also disappears; this in turn entails the disappearance of the different concrete forms of labour. They can no longer be distinguished, but are all together reduced to the same kind of labour, human labour in the abstract.\" The \"residue\" that remains is \"the same phantom-like objectivity; they are merely congealed quantities of homogeneous human labour, i.e. of human labour-power expended without regard to the form of its expenditure.\" This is the substance of value. \"As crystals of this social substance, which is common to them all, they are values — commodity values ( Warenwerte ).\" The metaphor of the crystal is significant: value is a social substance crystallized in the product, invisible to the senses but socially objective. The conclusion of this analytical sequence is stated with precision: \"We have seen that when commodities are in the relation of exchange, their exchange-value manifests itself as something totally independent of their use-value. But if we abstract from their use-value, there remains their value, as it has just been defined. The common factor in the exchange relation, or in the exchange-value of the commodity, is therefore its value.\" The further development of the investigation will return to exchange-value as \"the necessary mode of expression, or form of appearance, of value.\" The Magnitude of Value and Socially Necessary Labour-Time The next movement of the argument addresses the magnitude of value. \"A use-value, or useful article, therefore, has value only because abstract human labour is objectified ( vergegenständlicht ) or materialized in it. How, then, is the magnitude of this value to be measured? By means of the quantity of the 'value-forming substance,' the labour, contained in the article. This quantity is measured by its duration, and the labour-time is itself measured on the particular scale of hours, days etc.\" Marx immediately anticipates a potential misunderstanding: \"It might seem that if the value of a commodity is determined by the quantity of labour expended to produce it, it would be the more valuable the more unskilful and lazy the worker who produced it, because he would need more time to complete the article.\" The solution to this problem is the concept of socially necessary labour-time . \"The labour that forms the substance of value is equal human labour, the expenditure of identical human labour-power. The total labour-power of society, which is manifested in the values of the world of commodities, counts here as one homogeneous mass of human labour-power, although composed of innumerable individual units of labour-power. Each of these units is the same as any other, to the extent that it has the character of a socially average unit of labour-power and acts as such, i.e. only needs, in order to produce a commodity, the labour time which is necessary on an average, or in other words is socially necessary.\" Socially necessary labour-time is defined as \"the labour-time required to produce any use-value under the conditions of production normal for a given society and with the average degree of skill and intensity of labour prevalent in that society.\" This is a crucial concept. It is not the actual labour-time expended by any particular producer, but the socially determined average — the labour-time that would be required by a producer of average skill, using average technology, under average conditions. The concept is simultaneously quantitative and social: it establishes the measure of value while recognizing that this measure is determined by social relations, not by individual idiosyncrasy. Marx illustrates this with a compelling historical example: \"The introduction of power-looms into England, for example, probably reduced by one half the labour required to convert a given quantity of yarn into woven fabric. In order to do this, the English hand-loom weaver in fact needed the same amount of labour-time as before; but the product of his individual hour of labour now only represented half an hour of social labour, and consequently fell to one half its former value.\" This example demonstrates that value is determined not by the individual labour-time actually expended but by the socially necessary labour-time. The hand-loom weaver who continues to work with obsolete technology is penalized by the social determination of value: his product loses half its value because the socially necessary labour-time has been halved by the power loom. This is a fundamental mechanism of capitalist competition and technological change. Marx draws the general conclusion: \"What exclusively determines the magnitude of the value of any article is therefore the amount of labour socially necessary, or the labour-time socially necessary for its production.\" And he adds: \"The individual commodity counts here only as an average sample of its kind.\" The commodity is treated as a representative unit; individual variations are averaged out. Productivity, Value, and the Contradiction Between Wealth and Value The section continues with a systematic analysis of the relationship between productivity (or productive power) of labour and value. \"The value of a commodity would therefore remain constant, if the labour-time required for its production also remained constant. But the latter changes with every variation in the productivity of labour.\" Productivity is determined by a wide range of circumstances: \"the workers' average degree of skill, the level of development of science and its technological application, the social organization of the process of production, the extent and effectiveness of the means of production, and the conditions found in the natural environment.\" Marx provides several examples. \"The same quantity of labour is present in eight bushels of corn in favourable seasons and in only four bushels in unfavourable seasons. The same quantity of labour provides more metal in rich mines than in poor.\" Diamonds are particularly instructive: \"Diamonds are of very rare occurrence on the earth's surface, and hence their discovery costs, on an average, a great deal of labour-time. Consequently much labour is represented in a small volume.\" Marx cites Jacob's question whether gold has ever been paid for at its full value, and adds that this applies still more to diamonds. He cites Eschwege's data: \"the total produce of the Brazilian diamond mines for the eighty years ending in 1823 still did not amount to the price of 1½ years' average produce of the sugar and coffee plantations of the same country, although the diamonds represented much more labour, therefore more value.\" The counterfactual is illuminating: \"With richer mines, the same quantity of labour would be embodied in more diamonds, and their value would fall. If man succeeded, without much labour, in transforming carbon into diamonds, their value might fall below that of bricks.\" The imagined case of synthetic diamond production illustrates the social determination of value: if the labour-time socially necessary to produce diamonds were drastically reduced, their value would collapse, regardless of their physical properties or their traditional prestige. Value is a social relation, not a natural property. The general law is stated: \"In general, the greater the productivity of labour, the less the labour-time required to produce an article, the less the mass of labour crystallized in that article, and the less its value. Inversely, the less the productivity of labour, the greater the labour-time necessary to produce an article, and the greater its value. The value of a commodity, therefore, varies directly as the quantity, and inversely as the productivity, of the labour which finds its realization within the commodity.\" This is the fundamental quantitative law of value. Marx adds a parenthetical remark that occurs only in the first edition: \"Now we know the substance of value. It is labour. We know the measure of its magnitude. It is labour-time. The form, which stamps value as exchange-value, remains to be analysed.\" The Conditions of Being a Commodity Marx then specifies the conditions under which a thing is a commodity — a crucial clarification that prevents the concept from being overextended. \"A thing can be a use-value without being a value. This is the case whenever its utility to man is not mediated through labour. Air, virgin soil, natural meadows, unplanted forests, etc. fall into this category. A thing can be useful, and a product of human labour, without being a commodity. He who satisfies his own need with the product of his own labour admittedly creates use-values, but not commodities.\" For a product to be a commodity, it must be produced for others, and it must be transferred through exchange. Marx emphasizes: \"And not merely for others. The medieval peasant produced a corn-rent for the feudal lord and a corn-tithe for the priest; but neither the corn-rent nor the corn-tithe became commodities simply by being produced for others. In order to become a commodity, the product must be transferred to the other person, for whom it serves as a use-value, through the medium of exchange.\" Engels' note to the fourth German edition emphasizes this passage because its omission led to the misconception that Marx regarded every product consumed by someone other than the producer as a commodity. Finally, \"nothing can be a value without being an object of utility. If the thing is useless, so is the labour contained in it; the labour does not count as labour, and therefore creates no value.\" This last condition establishes the dialectical relationship between use-value and value: use-value is the necessary presupposition of value, but not its sufficient condition. A thing must be useful to be a value, but usefulness alone does not make it a value. The Dual Character of Labour: The Pivot of Political Economy Section 2 introduces what Marx himself considered the most crucial discovery of his economic analysis: the twofold nature of the labour contained in commodities. He writes: \"Initially the commodity appeared to us as an object with a dual character, possessing both use-value and exchange-value. Later on it was seen that labour, too, has a dual character: in so far as it finds its expression in value, it no longer possesses the same characteristics as when it is the creator of use-values. I was the first to point out and examine critically this twofold nature of the labour contained in commodities. As this point is crucial to an understanding of political economy, it requires further elucidation.\" The claim to originality is significant and historically accurate. No previous economist had systematically distinguished between concrete useful labour and abstract human labour as two aspects of the same labour process. The classical economists treated labour quantitatively, as the source of value, without recognizing that this quantitative treatment presupposed a qualitative abstraction — the reduction of all concrete labours to abstract human labour. Marx's discovery is that labour has a dual character : it is simultaneously concrete (producing specific use-values) and abstract (producing value). The argument proceeds through the celebrated example of the coat and linen. Let it be assumed that the value of the coat is twice that of 10 yards of linen, so that if 10 yards of linen = W, the coat = 2W. The coat satisfies a particular need and requires a specific kind of productive activity — tailoring — determined by its aim, mode of operation, object, means, and result. \"We use the abbreviated expression 'useful labour' for labour whose utility is represented by the use-value of its product, or by the fact that its product is a use-value.\" The coat and linen are qualitatively different use-values, and the forms of labour through which their existence is mediated — tailoring and weaving — are correspondingly different. \"If the use-values were not qualitatively different, hence not the products of qualitatively different forms of useful labour, they would be absolutely incapable of confronting each other as commodities. Coats cannot be exchanged for coats, one use-value cannot be exchanged for another of the same kind.\" The Social Division of Labour \"The totality of heterogeneous use-values or physical commodities reflects a totality of similarly heterogeneous forms of useful labour, which differ in order, genus, species and variety: in short, a social division of labour. This division of labour is a necessary condition for commodity production, although the converse does not hold; commodity production is not a necessary condition for the social division of labour.\" Marx gives two examples: the primitive Indian community, where labour is socially divided but products do not become commodities; and the factory, where labour is systematically divided but workers do not exchange their individual products. \"Only the products of mutually independent acts of labour, performed in isolation, can confront each other as commodities.\" This is a crucial condition: commodity exchange presupposes the independence and isolation of producers. The division of labour within a workshop or community does not produce commodities; only the division of labour between independent private producers does. Marx summarizes: \"In a society whose products generally assume the form of commodities, i.e. in a society of commodity producers, this qualitative difference between the useful forms of labour which are carried on independently and privately by individual producers develops into a complex system, a social division of labour.\" The emphasis on \"independently and privately\" is essential. The social division of labour that underlies commodity production is not a consciously planned division but a spontaneous, unplanned differentiation of private labours. The section continues with the observation that the relation between the coat and the labour that produced it is not altered when tailoring becomes a special trade. \"Men made clothes for thousands of years, under the compulsion of the need for clothing, without a single man ever becoming a tailor. But the existence of coats, of linen, of every element of material wealth not provided in advance by nature, had always to be mediated through a specific productive activity appropriate to its purpose.\" Labour as the creator of use-values, as useful labour, \"is a condition of human existence which is independent of all forms of society; it is an eternal natural necessity which mediates the metabolism between man and nature, and therefore human life itself.\" This is a critical point: useful labour, considered as the producer of use-values, is transhistorical. It is a metabolic necessity of human existence, not a historically specific social form. This transhistorical character of useful labour distinguishes it sharply from abstract labour, which is historically specific to commodity-producing societies. The Material Substratum of Wealth Marx emphasizes that use-values \"are combinations of two elements, the material provided by nature, and labour. If we subtract the total amount of useful labour of different kinds which is contained in the coat, the linen, etc., a material substratum is always left. This substratum is furnished by nature without human intervention.\" He quotes Verri: \"All the phenomena of the universe, whether produced by the hand of man or indeed by the universal laws of physics, are not to be conceived of as acts of creation but solely as a reordering of matter.\" And he cites Petty's famous aphorism: \"Labour is the father of material wealth, the earth is its mother.\" This passage establishes that labour is not the sole source of material wealth; nature contributes the material substratum. This is important for Marx's critique of the labour theory of value in its cruder forms: value is created by labour, but use-value — material wealth — is a combination of labour and nature. Abstract Labour and the Reduction of Complex to Simple Labour The transition from useful labour to abstract labour is effected through the thought experiment of disregarding the determinate quality of productive activity. \"If we leave aside the determinate quality of productive activity, and therefore the useful character of the labour, what remains is its quality of being an expenditure of human labour-power. Tailoring and weaving, although they are qualitatively different productive activities, are both a productive expenditure of human brains, muscles, nerves, hands etc., and in this sense both human labour.\" Human labour-power must have attained a certain level of development before it can be expended in this or that form, but \"the value of a commodity represents human labour pure and simple, the expenditure of human labour in general.\" Marx then introduces the concept of simple average labour . \"Simple average labour, it is true, varies in character in different countries and at different cultural epochs, but in a particular society it is given. More complex labour counts only as intensified, or rather multiplied simple labour, so that a smaller quantity of complex labour is considered equal to a larger quantity of simple labour. Experience shows that this reduction is constantly being made. A commodity may be the outcome of the most complicated labour, but through its value it is posited as equal to the product of simple labour, hence it represents only a specific quantity of simple labour.\" The reduction of complex to simple labour is a social process that \"goes on behind the backs of the producers; these proportions therefore appear to the producers to have been handed down by tradition.\" For the sake of simplification, Marx henceforth treats every form of labour-power directly as simple labour-power. This reduction is crucial. It means that the heterogeneity of concrete labours is systematically reduced to a homogeneous unit — abstract human labour — and that this reduction is performed by the social process of exchange itself, not by any conscious decision. \"The various proportions in which different kinds of labour are reduced to simple labour as their unit of measurement are established by a social process that goes on behind the backs of the producers.\" This \"behind the backs\" formulation recurs throughout the chapter and is central to the theory of fetishism: social processes that are not consciously controlled by the producers appear to them as natural facts. The Quantitative Determination of Value and the Twofold Character of Labour Marx returns to the coat and linen example to illustrate the difference between the qualitative and quantitative aspects of labour. \"Coats and linen, however, are not merely values in general, but values of definite magnitude, and, following our assumption, the coat is worth twice as much as the 10 yards of linen. Why is there this difference in value? Because the linen contains only half as much labour as the coat, so that labour-power had to be expended twice as long to produce the second as to produce the first.\" The distinction is then formalized: \"While, therefore, with reference to use-value, the labour contained in a commodity counts only qualitatively, with reference to value it counts only quantitatively, once it has been reduced to human labour pure and simple. In the former case it was a matter of the 'how' and the 'what' of labour, in the latter of the 'how much,' of the temporal duration of labour.\" The section then demonstrates the contradictory movement between material wealth and value. \"In itself, an increase in the quantity of use-values constitutes an increase in material wealth. Two coats will clothe two men, one coat will only clothe one man, etc. Nevertheless, an increase in the amount of material wealth may correspond to a simultaneous fall in the magnitude of its value. This contradictory movement arises out of the twofold character of labour.\" Productivity is an attribute of concrete useful labour; it determines the quantity of use-values produced in a given time. But it has no impact on the labour represented in value. \"The same labour, therefore, performed for the same length of time, always yields the same amount of value, independently of any variations in productivity. But it provides different quantities of use-values during equal periods of time; more, if productivity rises; fewer, if it falls. For this reason, the same change in productivity which increases the fruitfulness of labour, and therefore the amount of use-values produced by it, also brings about a reduction in the value of this increased total amount, if it cuts down the total amount of labour-time necessary to produce the use-values.\" This is a key theoretical result: the same cause (increased productivity) simultaneously increases material wealth and decreases value. The contradiction is resolved only by recognizing the dual character of labour: concrete labour produces use-values, abstract labour produces value. The two aspects are not additive but dialectically related. The section concludes with the formal statement: \"On the one hand, all labour is an expenditure of human labour-power, in the physiological sense, and it is in this quality of being equal, or abstract, human labour that it forms the value of commodities. On the other hand, all labour is an expenditure of human labour-power in a particular form and with a definite aim, and it is in this quality of being concrete useful labour that it produces use-values.\" This is the pivotal theoretical result of Section 2. It is here that Marx establishes the fundamental categorial distinction upon which the rest of his analysis depends. The footnote at this point is notable: Marx criticizes Adam Smith for confusing the determination of value by the quantity of labour expended in production with the determination of value by the value of labour. Smith, according to Marx, \"has a suspicion that, in so far as labour manifests itself in the value of commodities, it only counts as an expenditure of labour-power; but then again he views this expenditure merely as the sacrifice of rest, freedom and happiness, not as also man's normal life-activity.\" Smith thus misses the dual character of labour, viewing it only as a burden rather than as both a burden and a creative activity. Engels' note adds a linguistic observation: English has two words for these two aspects — \"work\" for labour that creates use-values and is qualitatively determined, and \"labour\" for labour that creates value and is quantitatively measured. This terminological distinction, Engels suggests, corresponds to the theoretical distinction Marx has drawn. The Value-Form: The Most Difficult Section Section 3, \"The Value-Form, or Exchange-Value,\" is the most technically demanding and conceptually intricate part of the chapter. Its function is to demonstrate the necessary development of the value-form from its simplest expression to the money-form. Marx describes this task as one \"never even attempted by bourgeois economics\": \"we have to show the origin of this money-form, we have to trace the development of the expression of value contained in the value-relation of commodities from its simplest, almost imperceptible outline to the dazzling money-form. When this has been done, the mystery of money will immediately disappear.\" The Riddle of the Value-Form and the Objectivity of Value Marx begins by noting that commodities come into the world in their \"plain, homely, natural form\" as use-values. But they are commodities only because they have a dual nature — they are at the same time objects of utility and bearers of value. \"The objectivity of commodities as values differs from Dame Quickly in the sense that 'a man knows not where to have it.' Not an atom of matter enters into the objectivity of commodities as values; in this it is the direct opposite of the coarsely sensuous objectivity of commodities as physical objects. We may twist and turn a single commodity as we wish; it remains impossible to grasp it as a thing possessing value.\" The value of a commodity is purely social; it \"can only appear in the social relation between commodity and commodity.\" The value-form is therefore the necessary mode of appearance of value: \"we started from exchange-value, or the exchange relation of commodities, in order to track down the value that lay hidden within it. We must now return to this form of appearance of value.\" The Simple, Isolated, or Accidental Form of Value: The Two Poles The simplest value-relation is that of one commodity to another commodity of a different kind. The equation is: x commodity A = y commodity B, or, in Marx's example, 20 yards of linen = 1 coat. The \"whole mystery of the form of value lies hidden in this simple form.\" Two commodities play two different roles. The linen expresses its value in the coat; the coat serves as the material in which that value is expressed. The first commodity plays an active role, the second a passive one. The value of the first is represented as relative value ; the commodity is in the relative form of value . The second commodity fulfils the function of equivalent ; it is in the equivalent form . These two forms are \"inseparable moments, which belong to and mutually condition each other; but, at the same time, they are mutually exclusive or opposed extremes, i.e. poles of the expression of value.\" The same commodity cannot simultaneously appear in both forms. \"I cannot, for example, express the value of linen in linen. 20 yards of linen = 20 yards of linen is not an expression of value.\" The equation states rather the contrary: 20 yards of linen are nothing but 20 yards of linen, a definite quantity of linen considered as an object of utility. The value of the linen can only be expressed relatively, in another commodity. \"The relative form of the value of the linen therefore presupposes that some other commodity confronts it in the equivalent form. On the other hand, this other commodity, which figures as the equivalent, cannot simultaneously be in the relative form of value.\" The equation 20 yards of linen = 1 coat also includes its converse: 1 coat = 20 yards of linen. But in this case, the equation must be reversed to express the value of the coat relatively, and the linen becomes the equivalent. \"The same commodity cannot, therefore, simultaneously appear in both forms in the same expression of value. These forms rather exclude each other as polar opposites.\" Whether a commodity is in the relative or equivalent form depends on its actual position in the expression of value — whether it is the commodity whose value is being expressed or the commodity in which value is being expressed. The Content of the Relative Form of Value The usual mode of procedure in analysing value is the precise opposite of Marx's own. \"Nothing is seen in the value-relation but the proportion in which definite quantities of two sorts of commodity count as equal to each other. It is overlooked that the magnitudes of different things only become comparable in quantitative terms when they have been reduced to the same unit.\" The quantitative comparison presupposes a qualitative equation. \"Only as expressions of the same unit do they have a common denominator, and are therefore commensurable magnitudes.\" Whether 20 yards of linen = 1 coat or = 20 coats or = x coats, the linen and the coat are \"expressions of the same unit, things of the same nature.\" The equation \"linen = coat\" is the basis. But the two qualitatively equated commodities do not play the same part. Only the value of the linen is expressed. It is expressed by being related to the coat as its \"equivalent,\" as \"the thing exchangeable\" with it. \"In this relation the coat counts as the form of existence of value, as the material embodiment of value, for only as such is it the same as the linen. On the other hand, the linen's own existence as value comes into view or receives an independent expression, for it is only as value that it can be related to the coat as being equal in value to it, or exchangeable with it.\" Marx illustrates this with a chemical analogy: butyric acid and propyl formate are different substances, but both are composed of the same chemical substances — carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen — combined in the same proportions (C₄H₈O₂). If butyric acid were equated with propyl formate, the latter would count only as a form of existence of C₄H₈O₂, and the equation would express their chemical composition as opposed to their physical formation. \"If we say that, as values, commodities are simply congealed quantities of human labour, our analysis reduces them, it is true, to the level of abstract value, but does not give them a form of value distinct from their natural forms. It is otherwise in the value relation of one commodity to another. The first commodity's value character emerges here through its own relation to the second commodity.\" The crucial point is that the value-relation actively expresses the abstract character of value-creating labour. \"By equating, for example, the coat as a thing of value to the linen, we equate the labour embedded in the coat with the labour embedded in the linen.\" The act of equating tailoring with weaving reduces the former to what is really equal in the two kinds of labour — their common character of being human labour. \"This is a roundabout way of saying that weaving too, in so far as it weaves value, has nothing to distinguish it from tailoring, and, consequently, is abstract human labour. It is only the expression of equivalence between different sorts of commodities which brings to view the specific character of value-creating labour, by actually reducing the different kinds of labour embedded in the different kinds of commodity to their common quality of being human labour in general.\" But it is not enough to express the specific character of the labour that goes to make up the value of the linen. \"Human labour-power in its fluid state, or human labour, creates value, but is not itself value. It becomes value in its coagulated state, in objective form.\" The value of the linen as a congealed mass of human labour can be expressed only as an \"objectivity\" ( Gegenständlichkeit ), a thing materially different from the linen itself and yet common to the linen and all other commodities. The coat, in the value-relation, counts qualitatively as the equal of the linen, because it is a value. \"Here it is therefore a thing in which value is manifested, or which represents value in its tangible natural form. Yet the coat itself, the physical aspect of the coat-commodity, is purely a use-value. A coat as such no more expresses value than does the first piece of linen we come across.\" The coat's capacity to express value is not inherent in its physical being; it is a social capacity conferred by its position in the value-relation. Marx's joke is apt: \"just as some men count for more when inside a gold-braided uniform than they do otherwise.\" The crucial result is that the natural form of commodity B becomes the value-form of commodity A. \"The physical body of commodity B becomes a mirror for the value of commodity A.\" Commodity A, by entering into a relation with commodity B as an object of value, \"makes the use-value B into the material through which its own value is expressed.\" The value of commodity A, expressed in the use-value of commodity B, has the form of relative value. The Quantitative Determinacy of the Relative Form of Value Marx then analyses the quantitative aspect of the relative form. Every commodity whose value is to be expressed is a useful object of a given quantity — for instance, 15 bushels of corn or 100 lb. of coffee. A given quantity of any commodity contains a definite quantity of human labour. Therefore, the form of value must express not only value in general but also quantitatively determined value — the magnitude of value. The equation 20 yards of linen = 1 coat presupposes that both commodities contain the same amount of labour-time. But the labour-time necessary for production varies with every change in productivity. Marx then systematically examines the possible combinations of changes in the values of the two commodities. Case I: The value of the linen changes while the value of the coat remains constant. If the labour-time necessary for the production of linen is doubled (for instance, due to the increasing infertility of flax-growing soil), the value of linen doubles. Instead of 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, we have 20 yards of linen = 2 coats, since 1 coat now contains only half as much labour-time as 20 yards of linen. If the necessary labour-time is reduced by one half (due to improved looms), the value of linen falls by one half, and the equation becomes 20 yards of linen = ½ coat. \"The relative value of commodity A, i.e. its value expressed in commodity B, rises and falls in direct relation to the value of A, if the value of B remains constant.\" Case II: The value of the linen remains constant while the value of the coat changes. If the labour-time necessary for the production of a coat is doubled (due to a poor crop of wool), the equation becomes 20 yards of linen = ½ coat. If the value of the coat sinks by one half, then 20 yards of linen = 2 coats. \"If the value of commodity A remains constant, its relative value, as expressed in commodity B, rises and falls in inverse relation to the change in the value of B.\" Comparison of Cases I and II. The same change in the magnitude of relative value may arise from entirely opposed causes. \"Thus the equation 20 yards of linen = 1 coat becomes 20 yards of linen = 2 coats, either because the value of the linen has doubled or because the value of the coat has fallen by one half.\" The relative expression of value is therefore ambiguous: a change in the relative value can be caused by a change in the value of either commodity. This ambiguity is inherent in the simple form of value and is only resolved in the general form. Case III: The quantities of labour necessary for production vary simultaneously in the same direction and the same proportion. In this case, 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, as before, whatever change may have taken place in their respective values. \"Their change of value is revealed only when they are compared with a third commodity, whose value has remained constant. If the values of all commodities rose or fell simultaneously, and in the same proportion, their relative values would remain unaltered. The change in their real values would be manifested by an increase or decrease in the quantity of commodities produced within the same labour-time.\" Case IV: The labour-time necessary for production varies simultaneously in the same direction but to an unequal degree, or in opposite directions. The influence of all possible combinations of this kind can be worked out by applying cases I, II, and III. The general conclusion is drawn: \"Thus real changes in the magnitude of value are neither unequivocally nor exhaustively reflected in their relative expression, or, in other words, in the magnitude of the relative value. The relative value of a commodity may vary, although its value remains constant. Its relative value may remain constant, although its value varies; and finally, simultaneous variations in the magnitude of its value and in the relative expression of that magnitude do not by any means have to correspond at all points.\" Marx notes that \"vulgar economists\" have exploited this lack of congruence to attack Ricardo. He quotes Broadhurst's argument and refutes it with a mathematical analogy: \"Consider the fractions 10/20, 10/50, 10/100 etc. The number 10 remains unchanged, and yet its proportional magnitude, its magnitude in relation to the numbers 20, 50, 100 continually diminishes. Therefore, the great principle that the magnitude of a whole number, such as 10, is 'regulated' by the number of times the number 1 is contained in it falls to the ground.\" The analogy shows that the relativity of the expression does not undermine the determinacy of the value itself. The Equivalent Form and Its Three Peculiarities Marx now turns to the equivalent form, which he characterizes as having a \"mysteriousness\" that \"only impinges on the crude bourgeois vision of the political economist when it confronts him in its fully developed shape, that of money.\" The equivalent form is the form in which a commodity is directly exchangeable with other commodities. The first peculiarity is that \"use-value becomes the form of appearance of its opposite, value.\" The natural form of the commodity becomes its value-form. This substitution occurs only when some other commodity enters into a value-relation with it, and only within the limits of this relation. \"Since a commodity cannot be related to itself as equivalent, and therefore cannot make its own physical shape into the expression of its own value, it must be related to another commodity as equivalent, and therefore must make the physical shape of another commodity into its own value-form.\" Marx illustrates this with the example of weight. \"A sugar-loaf, because it is a body, is heavy and therefore possesses weight; but we can neither take a look at this weight nor touch it. We then take various pieces of iron, whose weight has been determined beforehand. The bodily form of the iron, considered for itself, is no more the form of appearance of weight than is the sugar-loaf. Nevertheless, in order to express the sugar-loaf as a weight, we put it into a relation of weight with the iron. In this relation, the iron counts as a body representing nothing but weight.\" The analogy is precise: iron serves as the form of appearance of weight in relation to the sugar-loaf, just as the coat serves as the form of appearance of value in relation to the linen. But \"here, however, the analogy ceases. In the expression of the weight of the sugar-loaf, the iron represents a natural property common to both bodies, their weight; but in the expression of value of the linen the coat represents a supra-natural property: their value, which is something purely social.\" The equivalent form is mysterious precisely because it attributes a social property — value — to a natural body. \"The equivalent form consists precisely in this, that the material commodity itself, the coat for instance, expresses value just as it is in its everyday life, and is therefore endowed with the form of value by nature itself. Admittedly, this holds good only within the value-relation... However, the properties of a thing do not arise from its relations to other things, they are, on the contrary, merely activated by such relations. The coat, therefore, seems to be endowed with its equivalent form, its property of direct exchangeability, by nature, just as much as its property of being heavy or its ability to keep us warm.\" This is the source of the fetishism of the equivalent form. The social relation is mistaken for a natural property. Marx notes that the crude bourgeois political economist, confronted with the fully developed equivalent form in money, \"seeks to explain away the mystical character of gold and silver by substituting for them less dazzling commodities, and, with ever-renewed satisfaction, reeling off a catalogue of all the inferior commodities which have played the role of the equivalent at one time or another. He does not suspect that even the simplest expression of value, such as 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, already presents the riddle of the equivalent form for us to solve.\" The second peculiarity of the equivalent form is that \"concrete labour becomes the form of manifestation of its opposite, abstract human labour.\" The body of the commodity that serves as the equivalent always figures as the embodiment of abstract human labour, and is always the product of some specific useful and concrete labour. \"This concrete labour therefore becomes the expression of abstract human labour. If the coat is merely abstract human labour's realization, the tailoring actually realized in it is merely abstract human labour's form of realization.\" In the expression of value of the linen, the usefulness of tailoring consists not in making clothes but in making a physical object which we at once recognize as value. \"In order to act as such a mirror of value, tailoring itself must reflect nothing apart from its own abstract quality of being human labour.\" Marx observes that there is \"nothing mysterious\" in the fact that human labour-power is expended in the form of tailoring as well as in the form of weaving, and that both possess the general property of being human labour. But in the value expression of the commodity, \"the question is stood on its head.\" To express the fact that weaving creates the value of linen through its general property of being human labour rather than in its concrete form as weaving, we contrast it with the concrete labour that produces the equivalent — tailoring. \"Tailoring is now seen as the tangible form of realization of abstract human labour.\" The third peculiarity of the equivalent form is that \"private labour takes the form of its opposite, namely labour in its directly social form.\" Because the concrete labour, tailoring, counts exclusively as the expression of undifferentiated human labour, it possesses the characteristic of being identical with other kinds of labour. \"Consequently, although, like all other commodity-producing labour, it is the labour of private individuals, it is nevertheless labour in its directly social form. It is precisely for this reason that it presents itself to us in the shape of a product which is directly exchangeable with other commodities.\" Aristotle's Insight and Its Historical Limits Marx illustrates the peculiarities of the equivalent form through a discussion of Aristotle, whom he calls \"the great investigator who was the first to analyse the value-form.\" Aristotle states clearly that the money-form of the commodity is only a more developed aspect of the simple form of value. He sees that the value-relation which provides the framework for this expression requires that the house should be qualitatively equated with the bed, and that these things, being distinct to the senses, could not be compared as commensurable magnitudes if they lacked this essential identity. \"There can be no exchange,\" Aristotle says, \"without equality, and no equality without commensurability.\" Here, however, he falters. \"It is, however, in reality, impossible that such unlike things can be commensurable,\" i.e., qualitatively equal. This form of equation can only be something foreign to the true nature of the things, \"a makeshift for practical purposes.\" Marx explains Aristotle's failure: \"Aristotle therefore himself tells us what prevented any further analysis: the lack of a concept of value. What is the homogeneous element, i.e. the common substance, which the house represents from the point of view of the bed, in the value expression for the bed? Such a thing, in truth, cannot exist, says Aristotle. But why not? Towards the bed, the house represents something equal, in so far as it represents what is really equal, both in the bed and the house. And that is — human labour.\" Aristotle was unable to extract this fact from the form of value \"because Greek society was founded on the labour of slaves, hence had as its natural basis the inequality of men and of their labour-powers.\" The secret of the expression of value — the equality and equivalence of all kinds of labour because and in so far as they are human labour in general — could not be deciphered until the concept of human equality had already acquired the permanence of a fixed popular opinion. \"This however becomes possible only in a society where the commodity-form is the universal form of the product of labour, hence the dominant social relation is the relation between men as possessors of commodities.\" Aristotle's genius is displayed precisely by his discovery of a relation of equality in the value-expression of commodities, but \"only the historical limitation inherent in the society in which he lived prevented him from finding out what 'in reality' this relation of equality consisted of.\" This passage is crucial for Marx's historical materialist method: the categories of thought are historically conditioned by the social relations of the society in which the thinker lives. Aristotle could not discover the concept of abstract human labour because the social conditions for that discovery — a society based on the equality of commodity producers — did not yet exist. The Simple Form of Value as a Whole The simple form of value is contained in the value-relation of one commodity with another. The value of commodity A is qualitatively expressed by the direct exchangeability of commodity B with commodity A, and quantitatively expressed by the exchangeability of a specific quantity of B with a given quantity of A. \"The value of a commodity is independently expressed through its presentation ( Darstellung ) as 'exchange-value.'\" Marx corrects his own initial formulation: \"When, at the beginning of this chapter, we said in the customary manner that a commodity is both a use-value and an exchange-value, this was, strictly speaking, wrong. A commodity is a use-value or object of utility, and a 'value.' It appears as the twofold thing it really is as soon as its value possesses its own particular form of manifestation, which is distinct from its natural form.\" Exchange-value is the form of manifestation of value; it is not an independent property of the commodity but a form that emerges only in the value-relation with another commodity. \"Our analysis has shown that the form of value, that is, the expression of the value of a commodity, arises from the nature of commodity-value, as opposed to value and its magnitude arising from their mode of expression as exchange-value.\" The second view — that value arises from exchange-value — is the delusion of both the Mercantilists and their antipodes, the free-traders. \"The Mercantilists place their main emphasis on the qualitative side of the expression of value, hence on the equivalent form of the commodity, which in its finished form is money. The modern pedlars of free trade, on the other hand, who must get rid of their commodities at any price, stress the quantitative side of the relative form of value. For them, accordingly, there exists neither value, nor magnitude of value, anywhere except in its expression by means of the exchange relation, that is, in the daily list of prices current on the Stock Exchange.\" The internal opposition between use-value and value, hidden within the commodity, is represented on the surface by an external opposition — by a relation between two commodities such that one counts directly only as a use-value, and the other directly only as exchange-value. \"Hence the simple form of value of a commodity is the simple form of appearance of the opposition between use-value and value which is contained within the commodity.\" The Insufficiency of the Simple Form and the Transition to the Expanded Form \"The product of labour is an object of utility in all states of society; but it is only a historically specific epoch of development which presents the labour expended in the production of a useful article as an 'objective' property of that article, i.e. as its value. It is only then that the product of labour becomes transformed into a commodity. It therefore follows that the simple form of value of the commodity is at the same time the simple form of value of the product of labour, and also that the development of the commodity-form coincides with the development of the value-form.\" Marx perceives \"straight away the insufficiency of the simple form of value: it is an embryonic form which must undergo a series of metamorphoses before it can ripen into the price-form.\" The expression of the value of commodity A in terms of any other commodity B merely distinguishes the value of A from its use-value, and therefore merely places A in an exchange-relation with any particular single different kind of commodity, instead of representing A's qualitative equality with all other commodities and its quantitative proportionality to them. \"To the simple relative form of value of a commodity there corresponds the single equivalent form of another commodity.\" Nevertheless, the simple form of value \"automatically passes over into a more complete form.\" The simple form expresses the value of commodity A in one commodity of another kind. But what this second commodity is — whether a coat, iron, corn, etc. — is a matter of complete indifference. Therefore, different simple expressions of the value of one and the same commodity arise according to whether that commodity enters into a value-relation with this second commodity or another kind of commodity. \"The number of such possible expressions is limited only by the number of the different kinds of commodities distinct from it. The isolated expression of A's value is thus transformed into the indefinitely expandable series of different simple expressions of that value.\" The Total or Expanded Form of Value The expanded form is expressed as: z commodity A = u commodity B or = v commodity C or = w commodity D or = x commodity E or = etc. In Marx's example: 20 yards of linen = 1 coat or = 10 lb. tea or = 40 lb. coffee or = 1 quarter of corn or = 2 ounces of gold or = ½ ton of iron or = etc. Here, \"the value of a commodity, the linen for example, is now expressed in terms of innumerable other members of the world of commodities. Every other physical commodity now becomes a mirror of the linen's value.\" The value of the linen first shows itself as being \"in reality, a congealed quantity of undifferentiated human labour. For the labour which creates it is now explicitly presented as labour which counts as the equal of every other sort of human labour, whatever natural form it may possess.\" The linen, by virtue of the form of value, \"no longer stands in a social relation with merely one other kind of commodity, but with the whole world of commodities as well. As a commodity it is a citizen of that world.\" In the first form, 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, it might well be a purely accidental occurrence that these two commodities are exchangeable in a specific quantitative relation. In the second form, \"the background to this accidental appearance, essentially different from it, and determining it, immediately shines through. The value of the linen remains unaltered in magnitude, whether expressed in coats, coffee, or iron, or in innumerable different commodities, belonging to as many different owners. The accidental relation between two individual commodity-owners disappears. It becomes plain that it is not the exchange of commodities which regulates the magnitude of their values, but rather the reverse, the magnitude of the value of commodities which regulates the proportion in which they exchange.\" Each commodity figures in the expression of value of the linen as an equivalent, hence as a physical object possessing value. \"The specific natural form of each of these commodities is now a particular equivalent form alongside many others. In the same way, the many specific, concrete, and useful kinds of labour contained in the physical commodities now count as the same number of particular forms of realization or manifestation of human labour in general.\" Marx then lists the defects of the expanded form. First, \"the relative expression of value of the commodity is incomplete, because the series of its representations never comes to an end. The chain, of which each equation of value is a link, is liable at any moment to be lengthened by a newly created commodity, which will provide the material for a fresh expression of value.\" Second, \"it is a motley mosaic of disparate and unconnected expressions of value.\" Third, \"if, as must be the case, the relative value of each commodity is expressed in this expanded form, it follows that the relative form of value of each commodity is an endless series of expressions of value which are all different from the relative form of value of every other commodity.\" The defects of the expanded relative form are reflected in the corresponding equivalent form. Since the natural form of each particular kind of commodity is one particular equivalent form amongst innumerable other equivalent forms, \"the only equivalent forms which exist are limited ones, and each of them excludes all the others.\" Similarly, the specific, concrete, useful kind of labour contained in each particular commodity-equivalent is only a particular kind of labour and therefore not an exhaustive form of appearance of human labour in general. The expanded relative form of value is \"nothing but the sum of the simple relative expressions or equations of the first form.\" Each of these equations implies the identical equation in reverse. When a person exchanges his linen for many other commodities, and thus expresses its value in a series of other commodities, it necessarily follows that the other owners of commodities exchange them for the linen, and therefore express the values of their various commodities in one and the same third commodity, the linen. By reversing the series, we get the general form of value. The General Form of Value The general form is expressed as: 1 coat = 20 yards of linen 10 lb. of tea = 20 yards of linen 40 lb. of coffee = 20 yards of linen 1 quarter of corn = 20 yards of linen 2 ounces of gold = 20 yards of linen ½ ton of iron = 20 yards of linen x commodity A = 20 yards of linen etc. The commodities now present their values in a simple form (in a single commodity), in a unified form (in the same commodity each time), and their form of value is \"simple and common to all, hence general.\" Marx explains the change: \"The two previous forms... only amounted to the expression of the value of a commodity as something distinct from its own use-value or its physical shape as a commodity. The first form, A, produced equations like this: 1 coat = 20 yards of linen, 10 lb. of tea = ½ ton of iron. The value of the coat is expressed as comparable with linen, that of the tea as comparable with iron. But to be comparable with linen and with iron, these expressions of the value of coat and tea, is to be as different as linen is from iron. This form, it is plain, appears in practice only in the early stages, when the products of labour are converted into commodities by accidental occasional exchanges.\" The second form, B, \"distinguishes the value of a commodity from its own use-value more adequately than the first, for the value of the coat now stands in contrast with its natural form in all possible shapes, in the sense that it is equated with linen, iron, tea, in short with everything but itself. On the other hand any expression of value common to all commodities is directly excluded; for, in the expression of value of each commodity, all other commodities now appear only in the form of equivalents.\" The expanded form comes into actual existence \"for the first time when a particular product of labour, such as cattle, is no longer exceptionally, but habitually, exchanged for various other commodities.\" The general form expresses the values of the world of commodities through one single kind of commodity set apart from the rest. \"Through its equation with linen, the value of every commodity is now not only differentiated from its own use-value, but from all use-values, and is, by that very fact, expressed as that which is common to all commodities. By this form, commodities are, for the first time, really brought into relation with each other as values, or permitted to appear to each other as exchange-values.\" The two earlier forms express the value of each commodity either in terms of a single commodity of a different kind, or in a series of many commodities which differ from the first one. \"In both cases it is the private task, so to speak, of the individual commodity to give itself a form of value, and it accomplishes this task without the aid of the others, which play towards it the merely passive role of equivalent. The general form of value, on the other hand, can only arise as the joint contribution of the whole world of commodities.\" A commodity only acquires a general expression of its value if, at the same time, all other commodities express their values in the same equivalent; and every newly emergent commodity must follow suit. \"It thus becomes evident that because the objectivity of commodities as values is the purely 'social existence' of these things, it can only be expressed through the whole range of their social relations; consequently the form of their value must possess social validity.\" In this form, all commodities appear not only as qualitatively equal, as values in general, but also as values of quantitatively comparable magnitude. \"Because the magnitudes of their values are expressed in one and the same material, the linen, these magnitudes are now reflected in each other. For instance, 10 lb. of tea = 20 yards of linen, and 40 lb. of coffee = 20 yards of linen. Therefore 10 lb. of tea = 40 lb. of coffee. In other words, 1 lb. of coffee contains only a quarter as much of the substance of value, that is, labour, as 1 lb. of tea.\" The general relative form of value imposes the character of universal equivalent on the linen, which is the commodity excluded, as equivalent, from the whole world of commodities. \"Its own natural form is the form assumed in common by the values of all commodities; it is therefore directly exchangeable with all other commodities. The physical form of the linen counts as the visible incarnation, the social chrysalis state, of all human labour.\" Weaving, the private labour which produces linen, acquires a general social form, the form of equality with all other kinds of labour. \"The innumerable equations of which the general form of value is composed equate the labour realized in the linen with the labour contained in every other commodity in turn, and they thus convert weaving into the general form of appearance of undifferentiated human labour.\" The labour objectified in the values of commodities is thereby presented not just negatively, as labour in which abstraction is made from all concrete forms, but positively, as \"the reduction of all kinds of actual labour to their common character of being human labour in general, of being the expenditure of human labour-power.\" The general value-form \"shows by its very structure that it is the social expression of the world of commodities. In this way it is made plain that within this world the general human character of labour forms its specific social character.\" The Interdependence of the Relative and Equivalent Forms \"The degree of development of the relative form of value, and that of the equivalent form, correspond. But we must bear in mind that the development of the equivalent form is only the expression and the result of the development of the relative form.\" The simple or isolated relative form of value converts some other commodity into an isolated equivalent. The expanded form imprints those other commodities with the form of particular equivalents of different kinds. Finally, a particular kind of commodity acquires the form of universal equivalent, because all other commodities make it the material embodiment of their uniform and universal form of value. \"The antagonism between the relative form of value and the equivalent form, the two poles of the value-form, also develops concomitantly with the development of the value-form itself.\" In the first form, 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, the antagonism is already contained but not yet fixed. In form B, only one commodity at a time can completely expand its relative value, and it only possesses this expanded relative form because all other commodities are, with respect to it, equivalents. \"Here we can no longer reverse the equation 20 yards of linen = 1 coat without altering its whole character, and converting it from the expanded form into the general form of value.\" In form C, \"the last form gives to the world of commodities a general social relative form of value, because, and in so far as, all commodities except one are thereby excluded from the equivalent form. A single commodity, the linen, therefore has the form of direct exchangeability with all other commodities, in other words it has a directly social form because, and in so far as, no other commodity is in this situation.\" The commodity that figures as universal equivalent is excluded from the uniform and therefore universal relative form of value. \"If the linen, or any other commodity serving as universal equivalent, were, at the same time, to share in the relative form of value, it would have to serve as its own equivalent. We should then have: 20 yards of linen = 20 yards of linen, a tautology in which neither the value nor its magnitude is expressed.\" In order to express the relative value of the universal equivalent, we must reverse form C: the equivalent has no relative form of value in common with other commodities; its value is expressed relatively in the infinite series of all other physical commodities. \"Thus the expanded relative form of value, or form B, now appears as the specific relative form of value of the equivalent commodity.\" Marx adds a crucial footnote on the antagonistic character of the universal equivalent form: \"It is by no means self-evident that the form of direct and universal exchangeability is an antagonistic form, as inseparable from its opposite, the form of non-direct exchangeability, as the positivity of one pole of a magnet is from the negativity of the other pole. This has allowed the illusion to arise that all commodities can simultaneously be imprinted with the stamp of direct exchangeability, in the same way that it might be imagined that all Catholics can be popes.\" This illusion is the basis of Proudhon's socialism, which Marx dismisses as a \"philistine utopia\" that \"does not even possess the merit of originality, but was in fact developed far more successfully long before Proudhon by Gray, Bray and others.\" The Transition to the Money Form \"The universal equivalent form is a form of value in general. It can therefore be assumed by any commodity. On the other hand, a commodity is only to be found in the universal equivalent form (form C) if, and in so far as, it is excluded from the ranks of all other commodities, as being their equivalent. Only when this exclusion becomes finally restricted to a specific kind of commodity does the uniform relative form of value of the world of commodities attain objective fixedness and general social validity.\" The specific kind of commodity with whose natural form the equivalent form is socially interwoven becomes the money commodity, or serves as money. \"It becomes its specific social function, and consequently its social monopoly, to play the part of universal equivalent within the world of commodities.\" Among the commodities which in form B figure as particular equivalents of the linen, and in form C express in common their relative values in linen, \"there is one in particular which has historically conquered this advantageous position: gold.\" If we replace the linen by gold in form C, we get the money form. \"Fundamental changes have taken place in the course of the transition from form A to form B, and from form B to form C. As against this, form D differs not at all from form C, except that now instead of linen gold has assumed the universal equivalent form. Gold is in form D what linen was in form C: the universal equivalent. The advance consists only in that the form of direct and universal exchangeability, in other words the universal equivalent form, has now by social custom finally become entwined with the specific natural form of the commodity gold.\" Gold confronts the other commodities as money only because it previously confronted them as a commodity. \"Like all other commodities it also functioned as an equivalent, either as a single equivalent in isolated exchanges or as a particular equivalent alongside other commodity-equivalents. Gradually it began to serve as universal equivalent in narrower or wider fields. As soon as it had won a monopoly of this position in the expression of value for the world of commodities, it became the money commodity, and only then, when it had already become the money commodity, did form D become distinct from form C, and the general form of value come to be transformed into the money form.\" The simple expression of the relative value of a single commodity, such as linen, in a commodity which is already functioning as the money commodity, such as gold, is the price form . The price form of the linen is therefore: 20 yards of linen = 2 ounces of gold, or, if 2 ounces of gold when coined are £2, 20 yards of linen = £2. \"The only difficulty in the concept of the money form is that of grasping the universal equivalent form, and hence the general form of value as such, form C. Form C can be reduced by working backwards to form B, the expanded form of value, and its constitutive element is form A: 20 yards of linen = 1 coat or x commodity A = y commodity B. The simple commodity form is therefore the germ of the money-form.\" This is the culmination of the value-form analysis. The money-form is not an arbitrary convention or a historical accident but the necessary outcome of the development of the value-form itself. Money is the \"dazzling\" form in which the social character of private labour appears as a natural property of a thing. The mystery of money is resolved when we understand that it is the necessary form of appearance of the value-relation of commodities — the form in which the general equivalent character of labour is socially recognized and fixed. The Fetishism of the Commodity and Its Secret Section 4, \"The Fetishism of the Commodity and Its Secret,\" is the philosophical culmination of the chapter. It explains why the categories analysed in the previous sections appear to the participants in commodity production as natural, eternal, and thing-like rather than as historical, social, and relational. Marx begins with the paradox: \"A commodity appears at first sight an extremely obvious, trivial thing. But its analysis brings out that it is a very strange thing, abounding in metaphysical subtleties and theological niceties.\" As a use-value, there is nothing mysterious about it. \"It is absolutely clear that, by his activity, man changes the forms of the materials of nature in such a way as to make them useful to him. The form of wood, for instance, is altered if a table is made out of it. Nevertheless the table continues to be wood, an ordinary, sensuous thing. But as soon as it emerges as a commodity, it changes into a thing which transcends sensuousness. It not only stands with its feet on the ground, but, in relation to all other commodities, it stands on its head, and evolves out of its wooden brain grotesque ideas, far more wonderful than if it were to begin dancing of its own free will.\" The table, as a commodity, becomes a sensuous-supersensible thing — it has a social property (value) that is not perceptible to the senses but is objective nonetheless. \"The mystical character of the commodity does not therefore arise from its use-value. Just as little does it proceed from the nature of the determinants of value.\" The determinants of value — labour, labour-time, the social form of labour — are not in themselves mysterious. \"It is a physiological fact that they are functions of the human organism.\" The quantitative determination of value by labour-time is also not mysterious in itself: \"In all situations, the labour-time it costs to produce the means of subsistence must necessarily concern mankind, although not to the same degree at different stages of development.\" And \"as soon as men start to work for each other in any way, their labour also assumes a social form.\" \"Whence, then, arises the enigmatic character of the product of labour, as soon as it assumes the form of a commodity? Clearly, it arises from this form itself.\" The equality of the kinds of human labour takes on a physical form in the equal objectivity of the products of labour as values; the measure of the expenditure of human labour-power by its duration takes on the form of the magnitude of the value of the products of labour; and \"the relationships between the producers, within which the social characteristics of their labours are manifested, take on the form of a social relation between the products of labour.\" \"The mysterious character of the commodity-form consists therefore simply in the fact that the commodity reflects the social characteristics of men's own labour as objective characteristics of the products of labour themselves, as the socio-natural properties of these things. Hence it also reflects the social relation of the producers to the sum total of labour as a social relation between objects, a relation which exists apart from and outside the producers.\" Through this substitution, \"the products of labour become commodities, sensuous things which are at the same time supra-sensible or social.\" Marx draws an analogy with the physiology of vision: \"the impression made by a thing on the optic nerve is perceived not as a subjective excitation of that nerve but as the objective form of a thing outside the eye. In the act of seeing, of course, light is really transmitted from one thing, the external object, to another thing, the eye. It is a physical relation between physical things.\" But the commodity-form, and the value-relation of the products of labour within which it appears, \"have absolutely no connection with the physical nature of the commodity and the material relations arising out of this. It is nothing but the definite social relation between men themselves which assumes here, for them, the fantastic form of a relation between things.\" The analogy with religion is then introduced: \"In order, therefore, to find an analogy we must take flight into the misty realm of religion. There the products of the human brain appear as autonomous figures endowed with a life of their own, which enter into relations both with each other and with the human race. So it is in the world of commodities with the products of men's hands. I call this the fetishism which attaches itself to the products of labour as soon as they are produced as commodities, and is therefore inseparable from the production of commodities.\" The Origin of Fetishism in the Social Character of Labour \"This fetishism of the world of commodities arises from the peculiar social character of the labour which produces them.\" Objects of utility become commodities only because they are the products of the labour of private individuals who work independently of each other. The sum total of the labour of all these private individuals forms the aggregate labour of society. \"Since the producers do not come into social contact until they exchange the products of their labour, the specific social characteristics of their private labours appear only within this exchange.\" The labour of the private individual manifests itself as an element of the total labour of society only through the relations which the act of exchange establishes between the products, and, through their mediation, between the producers. \"To the producers, therefore, the social relations between their private labours appear as what they are, i.e. they do not appear as direct social relations between persons in their work, but rather as material relations between persons and social relations between things.\" It is only by being exchanged that the products of labour acquire a socially uniform objectivity as values, distinct from their sensuously varied objectivity as articles of utility. This division of the product of labour into a useful thing and a thing possessing value appears in practice only when exchange has already acquired a sufficient extension and importance. \"From this moment on, the labour of the individual producer acquires a twofold social character.\" On the one hand, it must, as a definite useful kind of labour, satisfy a definite social need, and thus maintain its position as an element of the total labour, as a branch of the social division of labour. On the other hand, it can satisfy the manifold needs of the individual producer himself only in so far as every particular kind of useful private labour can be exchanged with every other kind. \"Equality in the full sense between different kinds of labour can be arrived at only if we abstract from their real inequality, if we reduce them to the characteristic they have in common, that of being the expenditure of human labour-power, of human labour in the abstract.\" The private producer's brain reflects this twofold social character of his labour only in the forms which appear in practical intercourse, in the exchange of products. \"Hence the socially useful character of his private labour is reflected in the form that the product of labour has to be useful to others, and the social character of the equality of the various kinds of labour is reflected in the form of the common character, as values, possessed by these materially different things, the products of labour.\" Marx then formulates the crucial inversion: \"Men do not therefore bring the products of their labour into relation with each other as values because they see these objects merely as the material integuments of homogeneous human labour. The reverse is true: by equating their different products to each other in exchange as values, they equate their different kinds of labour as human labour. They do this without being aware of it.\" Value \"does not have its description branded on its forehead; it rather transforms every product of labour into a social hieroglyphic. Later on, men try to decipher the hieroglyphic, to get behind the secret of their own social product: for the characteristic which objects of utility have of being values is as much men's social product as is their language.\" The belated scientific discovery that the products of labour, in so far as they are values, are merely the material expressions of the human labour expended to produce them, \"marks an epoch in the history of mankind's development, but by no means banishes the semblance of objectivity possessed by the social characteristics of labour.\" The value-form remains objective in appearance even after its secret has been discovered, just as the atmosphere remains unchanged after its chemical composition has been analysed. The Practical Consciousness of Commodity Producers \"What initially concerns producers in practice when they make an exchange is how much of some other product they get for their own; in what proportions can the products be exchanged? As soon as these proportions have attained a certain customary stability, they appear to result from the nature of the products, so that, for instance, one ton of iron and two ounces of gold appear to be equal in value, in the same way as a pound of gold and a pound of iron are equal in weight, despite their different physical and chemical properties.\" The value character of the products of labour becomes firmly established only when they act as magnitudes of value. \"These magnitudes vary continually, independently of the will, foreknowledge and actions of the exchangers. Their own movement within society has for them the form of a movement made by things, and these things, far from being under their control, in fact control them.\" The production of commodities must be fully developed before the scientific conviction emerges, from experience itself, that all the different kinds of private labour are continually being reduced to the quantitative proportions in which society requires them. \"The reason for this reduction is that in the midst of the accidental and ever-fluctuating exchange relations between the products, the labour-time socially necessary to produce them asserts itself as a regulative law of nature. In the same way, the law of gravity asserts itself when a person's house collapses on top of him.\" The determination of the magnitude of value by labour-time is therefore \"a secret hidden under the apparent movements in the relative values of commodities. Its discovery destroys the semblance of the merely accidental determination of the magnitude of the value of the products of labour, but by no means abolishes that determination's material form.\" Marx notes the methodological point that \"reflection on the forms of human life, hence also scientific analysis of those forms, takes a course directly opposite to their real development. Reflection begins post festum, and therefore with the results of the process of development ready to hand.\" The forms which stamp products as commodities already possess the fixed quality of natural forms of social life before man seeks to give an account of their content and meaning. \"Consequently, it was solely the analysis of the prices of commodities which led to the determination of the magnitude of value, and solely the common expression of all commodities in money which led to the establishment of their character as values.\" The finished form of the world of commodities — the money form — \"conceals the social character of private labour and the social relations between the individual workers, by making those relations appear as relations between material objects, instead of revealing them plainly.\" The Historical Specificity of Fetishism: Robinson Crusoe, Medieval Europe, the Peasant Family, and Free Association Marx then demonstrates the historical specificity of fetishism by contrast with other forms of production. \"The whole mystery of commodities, all the magic and necromancy that surrounds the products of labour on the basis of commodity production, vanishes therefore as soon as we come to other forms of production.\" Robinson Crusoe. \"As political economists are fond of Robinson Crusoe stories, let us first look at Robinson on his island.\" Robinson has needs to satisfy and must perform useful labours of various kinds. \"Despite the diversity of his productive functions, he knows that they are only different forms of activity of one and the same Robinson, hence only different modes of human labour. Necessity itself compels him to divide his time with precision between his different functions.\" Robinson \"learns this by experience, and having saved a watch, ledger, ink and pen from the shipwreck, he soon begins, like a good Englishman, to keep a set of books.\" His stock-book contains a catalogue of useful objects, the operations necessary for their production, and the labour-time that specific quantities of these products have on average cost him. \"All the relations between Robinson and these objects that form his self-created wealth are here so simple and transparent that even Mr Sedley Taylor could understand them. And yet those relations contain all the essential determinants of value.\" The point is that the determinants of value (labour-time, useful labour) are present in Robinson's economy, but they do not take a fetishistic form because the relations are direct and transparent. Robinson knows that he himself performs all the labours and that the products are his own. Medieval Europe. \"Here, instead of the independent man, we find everyone dependent — serfs and lords, vassals and suzerains, laymen and clerics. Personal dependence characterizes the social relations of material production as much as it does the other spheres of life based on that production. But precisely because relations of personal dependence form the given social foundation, there is no need for labour and its products to assume a fantastic form different from their reality.\" They take the shape of services in kind and payments in kind. \"The natural form of labour, its particularity — and not, as in a society based on commodity production, its universality — is here its immediate social form. The corvée can be measured by time just as well as the labour which produces commodities, but every serf knows that what he expends in the service of his lord is a specific quantity of his own personal labour-power.\" The social relations between individuals appear as their own personal relations, \"and are not disguised as social relations between things, between the products of labour.\" The Patriarchal Peasant Family. \"For an example of labour in common, i.e. directly associated labour, we do not need to go back to the spontaneously developed form which we find at the threshold of the history of all civilized peoples. We have one nearer to hand in the patriarchal rural industry of a peasant family which produces corn, cattle, yarn, linen and clothing for its own use.\" These things confront the family as products of its collective labour, but they do not confront each other as commodities. \"The different kinds of labour which create these products — such as tilling the fields, tending the cattle, spinning, weaving and making clothes — are already in their natural form social functions; for they are functions of the family, which, just as much as a society based on commodity production, possesses its own spontaneously developed division of labour.\" The distribution of labour within the family and the labour-time expended by individual members are regulated by differences of sex and age as well as by seasonal variations. \"The fact that the expenditure of the individual labour-powers is measured by duration appears here, by its very nature, as a social characteristic of labour itself, because the individual labour-powers, by their very nature, act only as instruments of the joint labour-power of the family.\" An Association of Free Men. \"Let us finally imagine, for a change, an association of free men, working with the means of production held in common, and expending their many different forms of labour-power in full self-awareness as one single social labour force.\" All the characteristics of Robinson's labour are repeated, but with the difference that they are social instead of individual. The total product is a social product. One part serves as fresh means of production and remains social; another part is consumed by the members of the association as means of subsistence. \"The way this division is made will vary with the particular kind of social organization of production and the corresponding level of social development attained by the producers.\" Marx assumes, \"but only for the sake of a parallel with the production of commodities,\" that the share of each individual producer in the means of subsistence is determined by his labour-time. \"Labour-time would in that case play a double part. Its apportionment in accordance with a definite social plan maintains the correct proportion between the different functions of labour and the various needs of the associations. On the other hand, labour-time also serves as a measure of the part taken by each individual in the common labour, and of his share in the part of the total product destined for individual consumption. The social relations of the individual producers, both towards their labour and the products of their labour, are here transparent in their simplicity, in production as well as in distribution.\" Religion and the Social Order Marx then draws a parallel between the fetishism of commodities and religious forms of thought. \"For a society of commodity producers, whose general social relation of production consists in the fact that they treat their products as commodities, hence as values, and in this material form bring their individual, private labours into relation with each other as homogeneous human labour, Christianity with its religious cult of man in the abstract, more particularly in its bourgeois development, i.e. in Protestantism, Deism, etc., is the most fitting form of religion.\" The ancient Asiatic, Classical-antique, and other such modes of production, in which the transformation of the product into a commodity plays a subordinate role, are \"much more simple and transparent than those of bourgeois society. But they are founded either on the immaturity of man as an individual, when he has not yet torn himself loose from the umbilical cord of his natural species-connection with other men, or on direct relations of dominance and servitude. They are conditioned by a low stage of development of the productive powers of labour and correspondingly limited relations between men within the process of creating and reproducing their material life, hence also limited relations between man and nature. These real limitations are reflected in the ancient worship of nature, and in other elements of tribal religions.\" The religious reflections of the real world \"can, in any case, vanish only when the practical relations of everyday life between man and man, and man and nature, generally present themselves to him in a transparent and rational form. The veil is not removed from the countenance of the social life-process, i.e. the process of material production, until it becomes production by freely associated men, and stands under their conscious and planned control.\" This requires a material foundation, \"a series of material conditions of existence, which in their turn are the natural and spontaneous product of a long and tormented historical development.\" The Failure of Political Economy Marx concludes the chapter with a critique of political economy. \"Political economy has indeed analysed value and its magnitude, however incompletely, and has uncovered the content concealed within these forms. But it has never once asked the question why this content has assumed that particular form, that is to say, why labour is expressed in value, and why the measurement of labour by its duration is expressed in the magnitude of the value of the product.\" The formulas of political economy, \"which bear the unmistakable stamp of belonging to a social formation in which the process of production has mastery over man, instead of the opposite, appear to the political economists' bourgeois consciousness to be as much a self-evident and nature-imposed necessity as productive labour itself.\" Hence the pre-bourgeois forms of social organization are treated \"in much the same way as the Fathers of the Church treated pre-Christian religions.\" Marx's critique of the classical economists is precise: \"It is one of the chief failings of classical political economy that it has never succeeded, by means of its analysis of commodities, and in particular of their value, in discovering the form of value which in fact turns value into exchange-value. Even its best representatives, Adam Smith and Ricardo, treat the form of value as something of indifference, something external to the nature of the commodity itself.\" The explanation for this is not simply that their attention is absorbed by the analysis of the magnitude of value. \"It lies deeper. The value-form of the product of labour is the most abstract, but also the most universal form of the bourgeois mode of production; by that fact it stamps the bourgeois mode of production as a particular kind of social production of a historical and transitory character. If then we make the mistake of treating it as the eternal natural form of social production, we necessarily overlook the specificity of the value-form, and consequently of the commodity-form together with its further developments, the money form, the capital form, etc.\" This is why economists who agree that labour-time is the measure of value have \"the strangest and most contradictory ideas about money.\" The failure to analyse the value-form leads directly to theoretical incoherence in the analysis of money, capital, and the more concrete forms of bourgeois economy. Marx defines classical political economy as \"all the economists who, since the time of W. Petty, have investigated the real internal framework of bourgeois relations of production,\" as opposed to the vulgar economists \"who only flounder around within the apparent framework of those relations, ceaselessly ruminate on the materials long since provided by scientific political economy, and seek there plausible explanations of the crudest phenomena for the domestic purposes of the bourgeoisie.\" Vulgar economists \"confine themselves to systematizing in a pedantic way, and proclaiming for everlasting truths, the banal and complacent notions held by the bourgeois agents of production about their own world, which is to them the best possible one.\" Finally, Marx exposes the fetishism of the economists who attribute exchange-value to things as natural properties. \"So far no chemist has ever discovered exchange-value either in a pearl or a diamond. The economists who have discovered this chemical substance, and who lay special claim to critical acumen, nevertheless find that the use-value of material objects belongs to them independently of their material properties, while their value, on the other hand, forms a part of them as objects.\" What confirms them in this view is \"the peculiar circumstance that the use-value of a thing is realized without exchange, i.e. in the direct relation between the thing and man, while, inversely, its value is realized only in exchange, i.e. in a social process.\" The chapter closes with a quotation from Dogberry in Much Ado About Nothing : \"To be a well-favoured man is the gift of fortune; but reading and writing comes by nature.\" The irony is pointed: the economists treat social, historical properties as natural, and natural properties as social gifts. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed Chapter 1 of Capital reveals several fundamental principles that operate throughout Marx's entire theoretical system. First , the principle of the historicity of economic categories . The commodity, value, money, and the fetishism that attaches to them are not eternal features of human economic life but historically specific forms of social organization. They arise under specific conditions — private, independent producers who exchange their products — and they will vanish under different conditions, such as the \"association of free men\" Marx imagines at the end of the chapter. This historicization is not merely a theoretical stance; it has direct political implications. If the categories of bourgeois economy are historical, then the bourgeois mode of production is itself historical and transitory, not natural and eternal. Second , the principle of the dual character of labour . This is the theoretical pivot of the chapter. Labour is simultaneously concrete useful labour (producing use-values) and abstract human labour (producing value). This duality is not a logical trick but a real characteristic of labour in commodity-producing societies. It explains the apparently paradoxical phenomenon that increasing productivity simultaneously increases material wealth and decreases value. It also provides the foundation for the theory of surplus-value developed later in Capital : the distinction between the labour that produces use-values and the labour that produces value is essential to understanding how surplus-value arises from the exploitation of labour-power. Third , the principle of the necessary development of the value-form . The value-form is not arbitrary or conventional but develops through a series of logically necessary stages from the simple form (A) through the expanded form (B) to the general form (C) and finally to the money form (D). Each stage resolves a contradiction inherent in the previous stage. The money-form is thus not an invention or a convenience but the necessary form of appearance of value in a developed commodity economy. This analysis demonstrates that money is not a neutral medium of exchange but a specific social form that arises from the value-relation of commodities. Fourth , the principle of fetishism . The social relations between producers appear as social relations between things. This is not a subjective illusion or a false consciousness that could be dispelled by education, but an objective feature of commodity production. The value-character of products \"becomes firmly established only when they act as magnitudes of value,\" and this action is independent of the will, foreknowledge, and actions of the exchangers. The producers' own movement within society has for them the form of a movement made by things, and \"these things, far from being under their control, in fact control them.\" Fetishism is thus not simply a theoretical error but a real appearance — an appearance that corresponds to the actual social relations of commodity production. Systemic Laws and Essential Takeaways The chapter establishes several systemic laws that operate within the capitalist mode of production. The law of value — that the magnitude of value is determined by socially necessary labour-time — is not merely a price theory but a regulative law of social production. It asserts itself \"as a regulative law of nature\" in the midst of accidental and fluctuating exchange relations. It operates \"behind the backs\" of the producers, who are unaware of its operation. The law of value thus functions as a kind of invisible hand, but unlike Smith's invisible hand, it is not a harmonious coordination of interests but a blind, coercive force that imposes itself through crises and through the ruin of producers who cannot meet the socially necessary labour-time. The law of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall is not yet developed in this chapter, but the contradiction between material wealth and value that Marx identifies — the fact that the same increase in productivity increases wealth and decreases value — is the germ of that later analysis. The contradiction is intrinsic to the twofold character of labour: the more productive labour becomes, the more use-values it produces, but the less value each unit of the product contains. This contradiction is the fundamental dynamic of capitalist accumulation. The theory of the social division of labour established in this chapter is also foundational. The social division of labour under commodity production is not a consciously planned division but a spontaneous, unplanned differentiation of private labours. \"Only the products of mutually independent acts of labour, performed in isolation, can confront each other as commodities.\" This independence and isolation of producers is the presupposition of commodity exchange, and it explains why the social character of labour takes the form of a relation between things. The anarchy of production inherent in commodity production is thus not an aberration but a structural feature of the mode of production itself. Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications Theoretical Implications for Political Economy. Marx's analysis in this chapter fundamentally reorients political economy. Classical political economy was concerned with the magnitude of value — the quantitative determination of exchange ratios. Marx shifts the focus to the form of value — the qualitative question of why labour assumes the form of value in the first place. This shift has profound consequences. It means that political economy cannot be a positivist science of prices and exchanges; it must be a critical theory of social forms. The categories of economic analysis — value, money, capital, profit, rent — are not natural facts to be measured but social forms to be explained. The failure of classical political economy to pose the question of the value-form led it into theoretical incoherence, particularly in its treatment of money and capital. Philosophical Implications. The chapter engages with fundamental philosophical questions about the relationship between appearance and essence, the relationship between social being and social consciousness, and the possibility of objective knowledge of social phenomena. Marx's method is neither empiricist (taking appearance as reality) nor idealist (deriving reality from concepts). It is dialectical: it begins from appearance, penetrates to essence, and then returns to appearance at a higher level of comprehension. The appearance of the commodity as a simple, trivial thing is not false in the sense of being illusory; it is the necessary form in which the social relations of commodity production present themselves. The essence — value as abstract labour — is not hidden behind appearance but is expressed in and through appearance. The task of science is to trace the necessary connection between essence and appearance. The chapter also engages with the question of social objectivity . Value is a \"phantom-like objectivity,\" a \"purely social existence\" that is yet objective. It is not a physical property of things, but neither is it a subjective projection of the observer. It is a social fact, produced and reproduced through the actual exchange relations of commodities. Marx's analysis thus offers a theory of social objectivity that avoids the twin errors of positivism (which reduces social facts to physical facts) and subjectivism (which reduces social facts to individual beliefs or conventions). Practical Implications. The chapter has direct implications for the understanding of contemporary capitalism. The fetishism of commodities — the perception of social relations as relations between things — is not a historical curiosity but a continuing feature of capitalist societies. The dominance of the money-form, the treatment of labour as a commodity, the apparent autonomy of the market over against the producers — all of these phenomena analysed by Marx in this chapter remain central to the experience of life under capitalism. The chapter's analysis of the contradiction between wealth and value, between use-value and exchange-value, also has contemporary relevance: the capitalist system's capacity to produce enormous material wealth coexists with — and in some ways depends upon — the destruction of value, the devaluation of labour-power, and the immiseration of producers. The chapter's utopian moment — the vision of an \"association of free men\" whose social relations are transparent — points toward a socialist transformation of society in which the fetishism of commodities would be overcome. But Marx is careful to note that this requires \"a series of material conditions of existence, which in their turn are the natural and spontaneous product of a long and tormented historical development.\" The overcoming of fetishism is not a matter of intellectual enlightenment alone but of the material transformation of the relations of production. The \"veil is not removed from the countenance of the social life-process\" until production \"becomes production by freely associated men, and stands under their conscious and planned control.\" This is the practical horizon toward which the entire analysis points, but which the analysis itself can only anticipate, not realize. The Chapter's Place in the History of Social Thought Chapter 1 of Capital represents one of the most sustained and rigorous analyses of the categories of economic thought ever written. Its influence extends far beyond economics, into philosophy, sociology, anthropology, literary theory, and cultural studies. The concept of fetishism, in particular, has been enormously influential in disciplines far removed from political economy — in the analysis of advertising, consumer culture, and ideology. The chapter's demonstration that social relations take the form of relations between things has become one of the foundational insights of critical social theory. But the chapter's enduring significance lies not merely in its specific conclusions but in its method. Marx demonstrates that the analysis of social forms requires a distinctive mode of thought — one that is neither purely empirical nor purely conceptual, but dialectical; one that traces the necessary development of forms from their simplest to their most complex manifestations; one that reveals the hidden connections between appearance and essence, between the abstract and the concrete. This method is Marx's most important contribution to social science, and Chapter 1 of Capital is its most brilliant demonstration. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Introduction Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 2"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 2", "title": "Chapter 2", "subtitle": "The Process of Exchange", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 2/Chapter 2  The Process of Exchange_explanation.html", "word_count": 4415, "read_time": "22 min read", "tags": ["Value & Commodity Form", "Money, Currency & Circulation"], "keywords": ["Process of Exchange", "Commodity Guardians & Juridical Relations", "Crystallization of the Money-Form", "Contradictions of Direct Barter", "Universal Alienability of Commodities"], "text": "Chapter 2 The Process Of Exchange - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 4,337 words ⏱️ ~22 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 2 of Capital , \"The Process of Exchange,\" performs a deceptively narrow task that is actually the hinge of Marx's entire theory of money. Its central problem is this: Chapter 1 analyzed the commodity in isolation and deduced that it is a unity of use-value and exchange-value, that its value is congealed abstract human labour, and that its value can only be expressed in a relative form against some other commodity functioning as equivalent . But that analysis was purely conceptual — it left the commodity suspended in logical space, never asking how the \"value-form\" comes to be stamped onto a real, physical object in the real world of buying and selling. Chapter 2 solves that problem. It explains how, out of the historically developing practice of exchange itself, one particular commodity is socially expelled from the ranks of all others and set up as the universal equivalent — money. The thesis, in a single sentence, is that money is not an invention, a convention, or a symbol imposed on commodities from outside; money is a necessary crystallization of the commodity's own internal contradiction between use-value and value, worked out through the blind, unplanned \"social action\" of commodity owners. The chapter's intellectual objective is therefore genetic and anti-idealist . Marx wants to show that the money-form is \"merely the reflection thrown upon a single commodity by the relations between all other commodities\" — it is a social relation that appears as a natural property of a thing (gold, silver). This appearance is the money fetish , and Marx closes the chapter by declaring it to be identical with the commodity fetish of Chapter 1, \"now become visible and dazzling to our eyes.\" Within the broader architecture of Volume I, Chapter 2 is the bridge between the analysis of the commodity (Chapter 1) and the analysis of money (Chapter 3). It is the moment when the theory of value transitions into the theory of circulation. Crucially, it also performs a permanent polemical function: Marx uses the genesis of money to demolish (a) Proudhonist and petty-bourgeois socialism, which fantasizes abolishing money while retaining commodity production; (b) the \"money is a mere symbol\" tradition running from medieval lawyers to the Enlightenment; and (c) the eclectic muddles of academic economists like Roscher. The chapter is thus simultaneously a piece of historical reconstruction, a logical deduction, and a weapon of ideological critique — all compressed into a narrative of how two people, standing face to face with things they own, are compelled by the logic of their own situation to create a god. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Starting Predicament: Why Commodities Need Guardians Marx opens with a deliberately deflationary image. Commodities \"cannot themselves go to market and perform exchanges in their own right.\" A commodity is a thing , and things \"lack the power to resist man.\" So we must \"have recourse to their guardians, who are the possessors of commodities.\" Here Marx introduces his characteristic legal-economic vocabulary: the commodity owner must \"place himself in relation to another as a person whose will resides in those objects.\" The will of the owner is somehow inside the thing he owns — this is the elementary form of the property relation. The rule that follows is precise: each owner must not seize another's commodity, nor surrender his own, \"except through an act to which both parties consent.\" This yields the chapter's first conceptual artifact — the juridical relation , whose form is the contract . Marx is careful to say it may exist \"as part of a developed legal system or not,\" because the contract is not fundamentally a legal convention imposed from above; it is \"a relation between two wills which mirrors the economic relation.\" And, decisively, \"the content of this juridical relation... is itself determined by the economic relation.\" This is a foundational move for historical materialism: law is not autonomous — it is the mirror-image, in the realm of will and recognition, of the material exchange of things. The startling conclusion of this opening is that juridical persons are not free-standing subjects . \"The characters who appear on the economic stage are merely personifications of economic relations ; it is as the bearers of these economic relations that they come into contact with each other.\" The translators' footnote flags the German Träger — the holder, repository, or bearer of a force different from the person themselves — noting that this concept \"appears repeatedly in Capital .\" The owner is therefore a mask : the market is not populated by agents but by roles — the personification of the buyer, the personification of the seller. The Twisted Perspective of the Commodity Owner Marx then gives the commodity itself a mock-anthropomorphic personality. Every commodity \"counts for\" every other commodity \"only as the form of appearance of its own value\" — it is a \" born leveller and cynic ,\" ready to exchange \"not only soul, but body\" with any other commodity, \"be it more repulsive than Maritornes herself\" (a reference to a famously coarse character in Don Quixote ). The commodity has no respect for concrete qualities; it sees only value. But the commodity lacks sense organs , and so the owner must \"make up for this lack of a sense of the concrete, physical body of the other commodity, by his own five and more senses.\" This yields the crucial asymmetry: for the owner, his own commodity has no direct use-value — \"otherwise he would not bring it to market.\" Its use-value exists for others , but for himself the only use it has is as \"a bearer of exchange-value, and consequently, a means of exchange.\" Hence: \"All commodities are non-use-values for their owners, and use-values for their non-owners.\" They must all change hands. The dialectical conclusion is a two-sided circle: commodities \"must be realized as values before they can be realized as use-values\" — but equally, \"they must stand the test as use-values before they can be realized as values.\" The reason the second half holds is Marx's labour theory of value in its social form: \"labour expended on them only counts in so far as it is expended in a form which is useful for others.\" And \"only the act of exchange can prove whether that labour is useful for others.\" This is the famous doctrine that socially necessary labour is validated (or invalidated) only post hoc, on the market . Private labour must demonstrate its social character through sale — or fail to. The Contradiction of Simple Barter Marx now sets up the central logical crisis of the chapter. Consider an owner's two simultaneous attitudes: 1. Individual : he will part with his commodity only against others whose use-value satisfies his own need . 2. Social/General : he wants to realize his commodity \"as a value, in any other suitable commodity of the same value \" — it does not matter whether his own commodity has use-value for the other's owner. Here is the contradiction, stated bluntly: \"the same process cannot be simultaneously for all owners of commodities both exclusively individual and exclusively social and general.\" Marx then works the contradiction out step by step. If each owner treats every other commodity as \"the particular equivalent of his own commodity,\" then logically his own commodity is the universal equivalent for all others. \"But since this applies to every owner, there is in fact no commodity acting as universal equivalent.\" This is a decisive result: in pure, pre-monetary barter, there is no general relative form of value under which commodities can be equated as values and their magnitudes compared. Therefore, \"they definitely do not confront each other as commodities, but as products or use-values only.\" Barter, taken in its pure form, collapses the commodity relation back into mere usefulness . The value dimension is present in each commodity but cannot be socially expressed . This is the brilliantly rigorous way Marx demonstrates the logical impossibility of consistent universalized barter — a proof that is simultaneously a refutation of any \"fair exchange\" utopia. The Practical Resolution: \"In the Beginning Was the Deed\" Marx's resolution is dialectical and deliberately anti-intellectualist. \"In their difficulties our commodity-owners think like Faust: 'In the beginning was the deed.'\" (The footnote cites Goethe's Faust , Part I: 'Im Anfang war die Tat.' ) They \"have therefore already acted before thinking.\" Before anyone reasoned out the necessity of money, human practice had already produced it. Marx's formula is that \"the natural laws of the commodity have manifested themselves in the natural instinct of the owners.\" This is a profound methodological claim: economic categories are discovered through practice , not contemplation. The mechanism is stated exactly: owners \"can only bring their commodities into relation as values... by bringing them into an opposing relation with some one other commodity , which serves as the universal equivalent.\" Marx notes that \"we have already reached that result by our analysis of the commodity\" — an elegant backwards reference to Chapter 1, which had already shown that the general form of value demands a single excluded equivalent. But now comes the crucial social addition: \"only the action of society can turn a particular commodity into the universal equivalent.\" No private logic can do it. \"The social action of all other commodities... sets apart the particular commodity in which they all represent their values.\" Through this social act, the commodity's \"natural form... becomes the socially recognized equivalent form,\" and it becomes — \"money.\" Money as Necessary Crystallization Marx formalizes this with the striking Apocalypse epigraph (Revelation 17:13, 13:17): no one may buy or sell \"save he had the mark, or the name of the beast.\" The scriptural allusion underscores money's coercive universality. He then states the genetic law: \"Money necessarily crystallizes out of the process of exchange.\" The mechanism is historical: \"the historical broadening and deepening of the phenomenon of exchange develops the opposition between use-value and value which is latent in the nature of the commodity.\" The need to give \"an external expression to this opposition\" produces \"the drive towards an independent form of value,\" which \"finds neither rest nor peace until an independent form has been achieved\" — the differentiation of commodities into commodities and money . And: \"At the same rate... as the transformation of the products of labour into commodities is accomplished, one particular commodity is transformed into money.\" Direct Exchange vs. the Value-Form Marx now contrasts the logical forms. In Chapter 1, the simple expression of value was: x commodity A = y commodity B . The form of direct exchange of products is different: x use-value A = y use-value B . The difference is not cosmetic. In barter, A and B \"are not as yet commodities, but become so only through the act of exchange.\" This is a genuinely subtle point: the exchange act creates the commodity character , rather than presupposing it. In barter, each commodity is \"a direct means of exchange to its owner,\" and \"the articles exchanged do not acquire a value-form independent of their own use-value, or of the individual needs of the exchangers.\" The historical genesis of exchange is then reconstructed. The first condition for a thing becoming exchange-value is for it to exist as \"a non-use-value ... a quantum of use-value superfluous to the immediate needs of its owner.\" Things are \"external to man, and therefore alienable \" — Marx uses Veräusserung (alienation), a term he will later load with philosophical weight — and reciprocal alienation requires only that men \"agree tacitly to treat each other as the private owners of those alienable things, and, precisely for that reason, as persons who are independent of each other.\" Now comes a passage essential to Marx's historical argument: this relationship of \"reciprocal isolation and foreignness\" does not exist within a primitive community — whether \"a patriarchal family, an ancient Indian commune or an Inca state.\" Exchange therefore \"begins where communities have their boundaries, at their points of contact with other communities.\" This is a decisive claim about the origin of value: commodity exchange is born on the frontier , in the encounter between mutually alien groups, and only then , \"by reaction,\" penetrates the internal life of the community. Initially, exchange relations are \"determined purely by chance,\" driven by \"mutual desire.\" Over time \"the constant repetition of exchange makes it a normal social process,\" some products are \"produced intentionally for the purpose of exchange,\" and the distinction between consumption-usefulness and exchange-usefulness \"becomes firmly established.\" Finally, \"custom fixes their values at definite magnitudes\" — exchange-value becomes determinate and regular only after long practice. Crystallization of the Universal Equivalent into Money Marx traces the emergence of a general equivalent. As commodities multiply in number and variety, \"the problem and the means for its solution arise simultaneously\" — a compact statement of dialectical immanence. Commercial intercourse \"never takes place unless different kinds of commodities belonging to different owners are exchanged for, and equated as values with, one single further kind of commodity .\" That commodity becomes the \"universal or social equivalent, if only within narrow limits.\" At first it is volatile: the universal equivalent form \"comes and goes with the momentary social contacts which call it into existence\" — it is \"transiently attached to this or that commodity in alternation.\" But with development, \"it fixes itself firmly and exclusively onto particular kinds of commodity, i.e. it crystallizes out into the money-form.\" Which commodity gets chosen is at first \"a matter of accident,\" but two circumstances are broadly decisive: (i) the money-form attaches either to \"the most important articles of exchange from outside\" — the first spontaneous manifestations of exchange-value of local products — or (ii) to \"the object of utility which forms the chief element of indigenous alienable wealth, for example cattle.\" Marx adds the historical observation that nomadic peoples are the first to develop the money-form because their possessions are \"in a movable and therefore directly alienable form\" and their way of life brings them into continuous contact with foreign communities. He then notes that \"men have often made man himself into the primitive material of money, in the shape of the slave, but they have never done this with the land and soil.\" This distinction is pointed: land could only be conceived as a money-commodity in a developed bourgeois society — and indeed the idea dates from \"the last third of the seventeenth century,\" first attempted on a national scale during the French Revolution (the footnote specifies the 1789 assignats , backed by confiscated Church lands). Why Precious Metals: The Natural Fit of Gold and Silver As exchange \"bursts its local bonds\" and value becomes \"the material embodiment of human labour as such,\" the money-form transfers to commodities \"by nature fitted to perform the social function of a universal equivalent\" — the precious metals . Marx quotes his own earlier work (the Zur Kritik der Politischen Ökonomie ): \"although gold and silver are not by nature money, money is by nature gold and silver.\" He also cites Galiani: \"The metals... are by their nature money.\" The justification is grounded in physical properties. Money, so far, has only one function — to be \"the form of appearance of the value of commodities... the material in which the magnitudes of their values are socially expressed.\" Two requirements follow: 1. Uniform quality : only a material \"whose every sample possesses the same uniform quality can be an adequate form of appearance of value\" — i.e., an embodiment of \"abstract and therefore equal human labour.\" Homogeneity is essential because abstract labour is qualitatively identical in all its instances. 2. Purely quantitative differentiation : since differences between magnitudes of value are purely quantitative, the money commodity must be \"divisible at will\" and reassemblable from its parts. \"Gold and silver possess these properties by nature.\" This is where Marx introduces the dual use-value of the money commodity: alongside its special use-value (gold fills hollow teeth, forms raw material for luxury goods), it acquires \"a formal use-value , arising out of its specific social function.\" The dialectical summary is: since all other commodities are \"merely particular equivalents for money, the latter being their universal equivalent,\" they relate to money \"as particular commodities relate to the universal commodity\" (a formula he attributes to Verri). Money Is a Commodity — and the Errors That Obscure This Marx now cautions against the misleading immediacy of the money-form. The money-form \"is merely the reflection thrown upon a single commodity by the relations between all other commodities.\" Therefore \"that money is a commodity is... only a discovery for those who proceed from its finished shape in order to analyse it afterwards.\" In other words, the insight that money is a commodity is trivial for anyone who starts from the result ; the real difficulty was in genesis . Here Marx deploys a decisive conceptual distinction: the process of exchange gives the commodity converted into money \" not its value but its specific value-form .\" Confusing the two attributes has produced two classic errors: - \"Money's value is imaginary\" — held by writers Marx cites (Locke's \"imaginary value\"; Law's valeur additionnelle ; Galiani's concession that gold has value \"as metals before they are money\"). - \"Money is a mere symbol\" — arising from the fact that money can , in certain functions, be replaced by \"mere symbols of itself\" (Forbonnais, Montesquieu, Hegel's Philosophy of Right §63, Le Trosne's counter-view). Marx grants the error \"did contain the suspicion that the money-form of the thing is external to the thing itself, being simply the form of appearance of human relations hidden behind it.\" In a strict sense every commodity is a symbol — \"as value, it is only the material shell of the human labour expended on it.\" But if one then declares that \"the social characteristics assumed by material objects... are mere symbols,\" one collapses into declaring those characteristics \"the arbitrary product of human reflection.\" This was the characteristic Enlightenment manoeuvre: explaining away the mystery by attributing it to human convention, thereby \"remov[ing] the appearance of strangeness from the mysterious shapes assumed by human relations whose origins they were unable to decipher.\" Two historical footnotes reinforce the critique. The medieval lawyers, serving royal power, made fashionable the idea that money is a mere symbol with imaginary value, \"supporting the right of the latter to debase the coinage.\" Marx cites the Roman-law maxim Pecunias vero nulli emere fas erit, nam in usu publico constitutas oportet non esse mercem (\"it shall not be lawful for anyone to buy money, for it was created for public use... it is not permissible for it to be a commodity\"), and the decree of Philip of Valois (1346) claiming the exclusive royal right to fix the currency's rate — a doctrine, Marx notes, drawn from the Digest and Imperial practice, designed to legitimize debasement. Pagnini's Saggio sopra il giusto pregio delle cose (1751) is praised for polemicizing against these legal gentlemen. Value-Form vs. Value-Magnitude Marx then re-emphasizes a point already made: \"the equivalent form of a commodity does not imply that the magnitude of its value can be determined.\" Knowing that gold is money tells us nothing about how much 10 lb. of gold is worth. \"Money, like every other commodity, cannot express the magnitude of its value except relatively in other commodities.\" Its value is determined by \"the labour-time required for its production,\" and is expressed in the quantity of any other commodity embodying equal labour-time. The crucial timing rule is: \"This establishing of its relative value occurs at the source of its production by means of barter. As soon as it enters into circulation as money, its value is already given.\" A helpful footnote quotes William Petty ( A Treatise of Taxes and Contributions , 1667), giving a concrete illustration of the labour-time equivalence between silver from Peru and corn. Marx draws the historiographical moral: the discovery that money is a commodity was made in the last decades of the seventeenth century, but \"this was merely the first step, and nothing more.\" The real difficulty is stated in one line: \"The difficulty lies not in comprehending that money is a commodity, but in discovering how, why and by what means a commodity becomes money.\" The footnote savages Wilhelm Roscher , whose catalogue of \"false definitions of money\" wallows in pseudo-philosophical equivocation (\"More! Less! Not sufficiently! So far! Not altogether!\"), a \"professorial twaddle\" he modestly dubs the \"anatomico-physiological method.\" The mockery is sharpened by praise for Roscher's \"one discovery,\" namely that money is \"a pleasant commodity.\" The Money Fetish as the Consummated Commodity Fetish The chapter's closing movement returns to the simplest expression of value, x commodity A = y commodity B , and to a phenomenon first noted in Chapter 1: the commodity that serves as the equivalent appears to possess the equivalent form independently, as \"a social property inherent in its nature,\" rather than as a product of the relation. Marx recapitulates what happened: \"We followed the process by which this false semblance became firmly established, a process which was completed when the universal equivalent form became identified with the natural form of a particular commodity, and thus crystallized into the money-form.\" The inversion is now stated with full force: \"What appears to happen is not that a particular commodity becomes money because all other commodities express their values in it, but, on the contrary, that all other commodities universally express their values in a particular commodity because it is money.\" The mediation — the whole historical process of exchange — \"vanishes in its own result, leaving no trace behind.\" The commodities \"find their own value-configuration ready to hand,\" as a physical object existing \"outside but also alongside them.\" This is why gold, \"in its crude state, becomes, immediately on its emergence from the bowels of the earth, the direct incarnation of all human labour.\" Marx names this: \"Hence the magic of money.\" The final sociologico-philosophical consequence: \"Men are henceforth related to each other in their social process of production in a purely atomistic way .\" Their own relations of production \"assume a material shape which is independent of their control and their conscious individual action\" — the core of Marx's doctrine of alienation/reification ( Versachlichung ). This \"situation is manifested first by the fact that the products of men's labour universally take on the form of commodities.\" And so the chapter concludes with its climactic identification: \"The riddle of the money fetish is therefore the riddle of the commodity fetish, now become visible and dazzling to our eyes.\" Two earlier footnotes deserve integration here. Footnote 1 on the opening page notes that in \"the twelfth century, so renowned for its piety,\" a French poet (Guillot de Paris, in the satirical Dit du Lendit ) could list among commodities at the fair of Lendit, alongside clothing and leather, \"'femmes folles de leur corps'\" — \"wanton women.\" This concretizes the alienability of persons as commodities. Footnote 2 (on Proudhon) attacks the attempt to derive an \"ideal of justice, of ' justice éternelle ',\" from \"the juridical relations that correspond to the production of commodities.\" Marx's analogy is scathing: Proudhon \"proves... that the production of commodities is a form as eternal as justice,\" then wants to reform actual production in light of this eternal ideal — like \"a chemist who, instead of studying the actual laws governing molecular interactions,\" tried to regulate them by \"eternal ideas\" of \"'naturalité' and 'affinité'.\" Similarly, footnote 4 attacks petty-bourgeois socialism — and specifically John Gray's labour-money scheme ( The Social System , 1831) later taken up by Proudhon — for wanting \"to perpetuate the production of commodities while simultaneously abolishing the 'antagonism between money and commodities', i.e. abolishing money itself.\" Marx's verdict: \"One might just as well abolish the Pope while leaving Catholicism in existence.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS This chapter establishes several principles that govern the rest of Capital . First: money is a social relation masquerading as a natural thing. The money-form arises not from the intrinsic properties of gold but from the total system of relations among commodities; gold's physical properties merely make it a suitable vessel for that social function. The chapter's methodological core is that material objects can bear social determinations ( Träger ) that are not inherent in them. This is the general law Marx will apply to capital, machinery, and wages throughout the rest of the work. Second: the genesis of money is dialectical, not voluntarist. Money cannot be abolished by decree or replaced by \"labour-money\" (Gray) or reformed into \"eternal justice\" (Proudhon), because money is not an external nuisance but the only possible external expression of the commodity's internal contradiction between use-value and value. To abolish money while preserving commodity production is, in Marx's image, to \"abolish the Pope while leaving Catholicism in existence.\" This is the deep logical refutation of every utopian socialist monetary scheme. Third: the necessity of \"social action.\" Individuals can desire money to exist, but only \"the action of society\" can make it exist. This reflects Marx's fundamental position that economic categories are collectively produced and collectively opaque — no individual design created them, and no individual can simply redesign them. Fourth: the doctrine of fetishism reaches its visible climax. Where Chapter 1 revealed the commodity fetish in the abstract, Chapter 2 shows how it becomes \"dazzling to our eyes\" in the form of money. The whole mediatory history — the frontier exchanges, the accidental choices of cattle and metals, the slow fixing of custom — vanishes, leaving a thing that appears naturally to be the incarnation of value. Users of money see the result, not the process. Finally, the philosophical lesson. \"Men are henceforth related to each other... in a purely atomistic way,\" while their own social relations take on \"a material shape which is independent of their control and their conscious individual action.\" This is the alienation thesis in its economic form: the more human beings produce a social world, the more that world confronts them as an alien power beyond their design. Money is thus not a neutral tool but the paradigm of a self-produced but uncontrolled social object — the visible, dazzling surface of a mode of production whose inner laws no individual agent comprehends. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 1 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 3"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 3", "title": "Chapter 3", "subtitle": "Money, or the Circulation of Commodities", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 3/Chapter 3  Money, or the Circulation of Commodities_explanation.html", "word_count": 15243, "read_time": "76 min read", "tags": ["Value & Commodity Form", "Money, Currency & Circulation"], "keywords": ["Measure of Values & Price-Form", "Medium of Circulation (C-M-C)", "Currency Velocity & Quantity of Money", "Hoarding & Money as Independent Value", "Means of Payment & Debt Relations", "World Money / Universal Equivalent"], "text": "Chapter 3 Money, Or The Circulation Of Commodities - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 15,233 words ⏱️ ~76 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 3 — Money, or the Circulation of Commodities: An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem and Its Stakes Chapter 3 of Capital Volume One addresses a problem that appears deceptively simple but contains the genetic code of the entire capitalist system: What is money, and what does it do? The chapter's intellectual objective is nothing less than to derive money's every function from the commodity-form itself — to demonstrate that money is not an external convention, not a clever invention, not a neutral lubricant, but the necessary and logically compelled form of appearance of a specific social relation: the universal equivalent that arises when commodity production becomes general. Where Chapter 1 derived value from socially necessary labour-time and Chapter 2 derived the exchange-relation from the value-relation, Chapter 3 shows that money is the crystallized form of the value-relation , the \"social pledge\" ( nexus rerum ) which every commodity-owner must obtain before he can eat. The chapter's thesis can be stated crisply: Money is the necessary form of appearance of the measure of value immanent in commodities — labour-time — and its functions (measure of value, means of circulation, hoarding, means of payment, world money) develop dialectically out of the contradictory unity of use-value and value that constitutes the commodity itself. This is the pivot of the entire work. Before Chapter 3, Marx has analyzed production and exchange in their immediacy; after Chapter 3, he will analyze the transformation of money into capital (Volume One, Part II). The chapter thus serves as the conceptual bridge: it shows how the simple circulation of commodities, which appears to be a realm of freedom, equality, and property, already contains — in the contradictory unity of its own forms — the possibility of crisis, the potentiality of capital, and the actuality of social domination. The Dialectical Method and the Problem of \"Reading\" the Text To understand this chapter, one must grasp Marx's method. He is not merely describing monetary institutions; he is deducing categorical forms from the internal contradictions of the commodity . The movement is Hegelian in structure but materialist in content: each new function of money arises not because someone invented it, but because a contradiction in the previous function requires resolution. Money as measure of value produces a contradiction (the standard of price must be stable but the measure of value is variable); this is resolved by the separation of measure of value from standard of price. Money as means of circulation produces a contradiction (the identity of sale and purchase implies their separation); this resolves into money as hoard. Money as means of payment produces a contradiction (the chain of debts implies the possibility of general default); this resolves into money as world money and into the credit system. The reader must therefore resist the temptation to read Chapter 3 as a taxonomy of monetary functions. It is instead a genealogy of monetary forms , each stage of which is logically entailed by the contradictions of the previous stage. Marx's footnotes — densely packed with references to Petty, Locke, North, Vanderlint, Hume, Ricardo, Fullarton, Barbon, Boisguillebert, and dozens of others — are not merely scholarly apparatus; they are the record of a polemical engagement with the entire history of political economy, showing how bourgeois economists confused the functions of money (most notoriously, confusing measure of value with standard of price, and thereby arriving at the absurd conclusion that the quantity of money determines prices). The Broader Theoretical Framework The chapter sits at the intersection of three grand theoretical projects: First, the critique of political economy. Marx engages the entire tradition of monetary theory — from the Mercantilists (who fetishized the balance of trade), through the Classical school (Smith, Ricardo, and the Currency School), to the utopian socialists (Owen and his \"labour money\"). His critique operates on two levels: he demonstrates the logical errors in these theories (e.g., Hume's confusion of the measure of value with the circulating medium), but more importantly, he shows that these errors are symptomatic — they reflect the fetishized consciousness of a society in which social relations appear as relations between things. Second, the theory of crisis. Chapter 3 contains the famous argument that the possibility of crisis is immanent in the commodity-form itself — specifically, in the separation of sale from purchase that money makes possible. \"Circulation bursts through all the temporal, spatial and personal barriers imposed by the direct exchange of products\" — and in doing so, it creates the possibility that sales will not be followed by purchases, that the \"internal unity\" of exchange will assert itself \"through external antitheses.\" This is one of the most important theoretical innovations in the chapter: crisis is not an accident, not a policy error, but a formal possibility of commodity circulation as such , which becomes actual only when credit and the division of labour have developed sufficiently. Third, the theory of the world market. The chapter's final section on world money opens the door to the international sphere, showing that the national circulation of commodities has a limit — the world market — where money \"falls back into its original form as precious metal in the shape of bullion.\" This sets up the analysis of international trade, exchange rates, and the global hierarchy of production that dominates later parts of Marx's work. The Chapter's Position in Capital Within the architecture of Volume One, Chapter 3 completes the analysis of \"Commodities and Money\" (Part I), preparing the way for \"The Transformation of Money into Capital\" (Part II). The crucial theoretical achievement is the demonstration that money, fully analyzed, is not merely the servant of exchange but the independent form of value — \"the absolute form of existence of exchange-value.\" Once money has attained this independence, the question inevitably arises: can this independent form of value increase itself ? The answer — M-C-M′, the general formula for capital — is the subject of the next part. But the possibility of that increase is already latent in the contradictions of simple circulation that Chapter 3 unveils. The chapter's thesis, in sum: Money is the form of appearance of the value-relation, and its development through measure of value, means of circulation, hoarding, means of payment, and world money is the dialectical unfolding of the contradictions of the commodity-form — contradictions that contain the possibility of crisis, the presupposition of capital, and the reality of social domination. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS Introduction: The Assumption of Gold and the Derivation of Money Marx begins with a deliberately simplifying assumption: \"Throughout this work I assume that gold is the money commodity, for the sake of simplicity.\" This is not an arbitrary choice but a methodologically motivated abstraction. Gold serves as the money commodity par excellence because it is the commodity that historically imposed itself as the universal equivalent — not by convention, but through the logic of exchange itself. The assumption allows Marx to analyze the form of money without being distracted by the historical multiplicity of monetary materials (silver, copper, cattle, shells, etc.). Yet he is careful to note that this is a simplification: in the notes and asides throughout the chapter, he repeatedly references silver, copper, and paper money, showing that the logic he develops applies to all monetary forms. The first main function of gold, Marx writes, is \"to supply commodities with the material for the expression of their values, or to represent their values as magnitudes of the same denomination, qualitatively equal and quantitatively comparable.\" This is gold's function as measure of value . The crucial point — which Marx will hammer home repeatedly — is that \"it is not money that renders the commodities commensurable. Quite the contrary.\" Commodities are commensurable because they are all objectifications of human labour ; money merely provides the material for expressing this commensurability. The equation \"x commodity A = y money commodity\" is the money-form or price — and it is socially valid because gold has already established itself as the universal equivalent. This is the pivotal theoretical move: money does not create value; it expresses it. The value of a commodity is determined by socially necessary labour-time; money is the \"necessary form of appearance of the measure of value which is immanent in commodities, namely labour-time.\" This directly refutes the utopian socialism of Owen and others, who imagined that \"labour money\" — certificates representing hours of labour — could replace gold and eliminate exploitation. Marx's footnote is scathing: Owen's labour money \"is no more 'money' than a theatre ticket is.\" Owen presupposes directly socialized labour, a form of production \"diametrically opposed to the production of commodities.\" The point is that money is not a dispensable convention but an essential form of commodity-producing society: to abolish money without abolishing commodity production is to attempt to \"circumvent the necessary conditions of that form of production.\" I. The Measure of Values The Ideal Character of Money as Measure of Value Having established that money expresses value, Marx turns to the specific form in which it does so. The price or money-form of commodities is \"quite distinct from their palpable and real bodily form; it is therefore a purely ideal or notional form.\" When a commodity-owner says his linen is worth £2, he does not need to have any actual gold; the price exists \"only in their heads, so to speak.\" This is the function of money as ideal or imaginary measure of value. The guardian of the commodities \"must therefore lend them his tongue, or hang a ticket on them, in order to communicate their prices to the outside world.\" (Marx's footnote here is characteristically ethnographic: Captain Parry describes Baffin Bay inhabitants who licked articles twice to seal a bargain, while among the Eastern Eskimo the exchanger licked each article — a delightful illustration that price-communication is a social act, mediated by the body.) The ideal character of money as measure of value has given rise to \"the wildest theories\" — a theme Marx will develop throughout the chapter. The essential point, however, is that \"although the money that performs the functions of a measure of value is only imaginary, the price depends entirely on the actual substance that is money.\" If gold, silver, or copper serve as the measure of value, the value of a ton of iron will be expressed \"by very different prices, or will be represented by very different quantities of those metals.\" The ideal character of the measure does not mean that the measure is arbitrary; it means that the material of the measure must be a real commodity with real value. The Contradiction of Dual Measures and the History of Bimetallism Marx then introduces a crucial historical argument. If two different commodities — gold and silver — serve simultaneously as measures of value, \"all commodities will have two separate price-expressions, the price in gold and the price in silver.\" These can coexist as long as the ratio of their values remains unchanged. But \"every alteration in this ratio disturbs the ratio between the gold-prices and the silver-prices of commodities, and thus proves in fact that a duplication of the measure of value contradicts the function of that measure.\" The footnote to this passage is a masterpiece of historical-economic analysis. Marx traces the monetary history of England from Edward III to George II as \"one long series of perturbations caused by the clash between the legally fixed ratio between the values of gold and silver, and the fluctuations in their real values.\" When gold was overvalued by law, silver was withdrawn from circulation, melted down, and exported; when silver was overvalued, gold suffered the same fate. This is an early formulation of what would later be called Gresham's Law — \"bad money drives out good\" — but Marx's formulation is more precise: the metal that is overvalued by law remains in circulation as the real measure, while the undervalued metal is withdrawn. As Marx summarizes the entire historical experience: \"where two commodities perform by law the functions of a measure of value, in practice only one maintains that position.\" This historical digression is not merely illustrative; it establishes a fundamental theoretical point: the measure of value must be single and uniform . The contradiction between the ideal character of money and the multiplicity of possible monetary materials is resolved only by the social establishment of a single measure. Measure of Value versus Standard of Price The next move is one of the most important conceptual distinctions in the chapter — indeed, in all of Marx's monetary theory. \"As measure of value, and as standard of price, money performs two quite different functions.\" The distinction is subtle but decisive: As measure of value , money is \"the social incarnation of human labour.\" It converts the values of commodities into prices — into imaginary quantities of gold. It measures commodities considered as values . As standard of price , money is \"a quantity of metal with a fixed weight.\" It measures quantities of gold by a unit quantity of gold — \"not the value of one quantity of gold by the weight of another.\" The distinction has three crucial implications: First , the standard of price requires stability of measurement. \"For the standard of price, a certain weight of gold must be fixed as the unit of measurement... the stability of the measurement is of decisive importance.\" Hence \"the less the unit of measurement (here a quantity of gold) is subject to variation, the better the standard of price fulfils its office.\" Second , the measure of value cannot be stable in the same way, because \"gold can serve as a measure of value only because it is itself a product of labour, and therefore potentially variable in value.\" This is a profound point: the very property that makes gold capable of measuring value — that it is itself a product of labour — makes it variable in value, and therefore an imperfect measure of value. Third , and most importantly, a change in the value of gold does not impair its function as a standard of price. \"No matter how the value of gold varies, different quantities of gold always remain in the same value-relation to each other.\" If the value of gold falls by 1,000 per cent, 12 ounces of gold still have twelve times the value of one ounce. And since an ounce of gold does not change in weight when its value changes, \"gold always renders the same service as a fixed measure of price, however much its value may vary.\" Nor does a change in the value of gold prevent it from functioning as a measure of value: \"The change affects all commodities simultaneously, and therefore, other things being equal, leaves the mutual relations between their values unaltered, although those values are now all expressed in higher or lower gold-prices than before.\" This analysis is the foundation of Marx's critique of the quantity theory of money . If the value of gold changes, prices change proportionally (assuming commodity values constant); but this is a change in the measure of value , not in the quantity of circulating medium . The quantity theory — held by Hume, Ricardo, and the Currency School — confuses the two, arguing that an increase in the quantity of money causes a proportional rise in prices. Marx will address this confusion systematically in Section II(b). The Laws of Commodity Price Fluctuations Marx then derives the laws of price fluctuation from the simple relative form of value developed in Chapter 1. A general rise in prices can result either from a rise in commodity values (with money value constant) or from a fall in the value of money (with commodity values constant). The reverse holds for a general fall. Crucially: \"It therefore by no means follows that a rise in the value of money necessarily implies a proportional fall in the prices of commodities.\" This would hold only for commodities whose value remains constant. Commodities whose value rises in proportion to money retain the same price; commodities whose value rises slower or faster will see prices determined by \"the difference between the path described by their value and that described by the value of money.\" This is a refutation of any mechanical quantity theory: the relation between money and prices is mediated by the values of both commodities and money, which can vary independently and disproportionately. The Separation of Money-Name from Weight-Name Marx next traces the historical process by which the money-names of metal weights \"are gradually separated from their original weight-names.\" He identifies three historically decisive reasons: The introduction of foreign money among less developed peoples — foreign coins circulated with different names from indigenous weights (e.g., Rome). The development of material wealth — the more precious metal extrudes the less precious from its function as measure of value (silver drives out copper, gold drives out silver). The word \"pound\" was originally the money-name for an actual pound weight of silver; once gold drove out silver, the same name was attached to roughly one-fifteenth of a pound of gold. Centuries of continuous debasement by kings and princes — \"have in fact left nothing behind of the original weights of gold coins but their names.\" The result is that \"the standard of money is on the one hand purely conventional, while on the other hand it must possess universal validity\" — hence it is \"in the end regulated by law.\" A given weight of gold becomes officially divided into aliquot parts, \"baptized by the law as a pound, a thaler, etc.\" The prices of commodities are then expressed in these money-names or legal names of the subdivisions of the gold standard. In England, instead of saying a quarter of wheat is worth an ounce of gold, people say it is worth \"£3 17s. 10½d.\" Money thus serves as money of account whenever it is a question of fixing a thing as a value. Marx's footnote warns against attributing \"a hidden meaning to these cabalistic signs.\" The money-names \"express both the values of commodities and, simultaneously, aliquot parts of a certain weight of metal.\" This dual character has generated confusion — specifically, the notion that gold is \"estimated in its own material\" and that \"its price is fixed by the State.\" Marx quotes his own earlier work: \"The establishing of names of account for definite weights of gold was mistaken for the establishing of the value of these weights.\" Sir William Petty's Quantulumcunque is cited to refute the notion that a proclamation can decuple a nation's wealth. Price as Money-Name and the Possibility of Divergence Having established the money-name, Marx now defines price : \"Price is the money-name of the labour objectified in a commodity.\" The expression of the equivalence of a commodity with the quantity of money whose name is that commodity's price is a tautology — like the expression of relative value generally. But — and here is a crucial theoretical move — \"although price, being the exponent of the magnitude of a commodity's value, is the exponent of its exchange-ratio with money, it does not follow that the exponent of this exchange-ratio is necessarily the exponent of the magnitude of the commodity's value.\" The possibility of a quantitative incongruity between price and magnitude of value is \"inherent in the price-form itself.\" This is \"not a defect, but, on the contrary, it makes this form the adequate one for a mode of production whose laws can only assert themselves as blindly operating averages between constant irregularities.\" The example: two equal quantities of socially necessary labour are represented by 1 quarter of wheat and £2. If circumstances raise the price to £3 or reduce it to £1, these are still prices of the wheat — \"for they are, in the first place, the form of its value, i.e. money, and, in the second place, the exponents of its exchange-ratio with money.\" Even if £1 or £3 is \"too small or too large to give proper expression to the magnitude of the wheat's value,\" they are still prices. The magnitude of value expresses \"a necessary relation to social labour-time which is inherent in the process by which its value is created\"; but with the transformation of value into price, this necessary relation appears as the exchange-ratio between a single commodity and money — a relation that \"may express both the magnitude of value of the commodity and the greater or lesser quantity of money for which it can be sold under the given circumstances.\" This is a subtle and important argument. The price-form is adequate to a mode of production characterized by \"constant irregularities\" precisely because it allows for the divergence of price from value — a divergence that appears as the \"blindly operating averages\" of market competition. The possibility of crisis is already latent here: the price-form is the form in which the value-relation appears, but it is also the form in which that relation can be misrepresented . The Qualitative Contradiction: Price Without Value The price-form \"may also harbour a qualitative contradiction, with the result that price ceases altogether to express value.\" Things that are not commodities — \"conscience, honour, etc.\" — can be offered for sale and acquire \"the form of commodities through their price.\" Thus \"a thing can, formally speaking, have a price without having a value.\" The expression of price is in this case \"imaginary, like certain quantities in mathematics.\" Conversely, the imaginary price-form may conceal a real value-relation or one derived from it — as with the price of uncultivated land, \"which is without value because no human labour is objectified in it.\" This passage is crucial for understanding Marx's theory of fictitious capital and rent , though he does not develop those themes here. The point is that the price-form is purely formal : it can attach to anything, regardless of whether that thing has value. This is the root of the fetishism of the price-form — the illusion that value is a property of the price-tag rather than of the social relation of labour. The Exchangeability of Commodities and the Necessity of Exchanges Price asserts that a given quantity of the equivalent (gold) is directly exchangeable with the commodity. But \"it by no means asserts the converse, that iron is directly exchangeable with gold.\" For a commodity to \"operate effectively as exchange-value, it must divest itself of its natural physical body and become transformed from merely imaginary into real gold.\" Marx's famous image: this \"act of transubstantiation\" may be \"more 'troublesome' for it than the transition from necessity to freedom for the Hegelian 'concept', the casting of his shell for a lobster, or the putting-off of the old Adam for Saint Jerome.\" A commodity cannot simultaneously be both real iron and real gold. \"To establish its price it is sufficient for it to be equated with gold in the imagination. But to enable it to render its owner the service of a universal equivalent, it must be actually replaced by gold.\" The Dante quotation — \"Right well hath now been tested this coin's alloy and weight; but tell me if thou hast it in thy purse\" — drives the point home: the ideal measure of value must be backed by real gold. \"The price-form therefore implies both the exchangeability of commodities for money and the necessity of exchanges. On the other hand, gold serves as an ideal measure of value only because it has already established itself as the money commodity in the process of exchange. Hard cash lurks within the ideal measure of value. \" II. The Means of Circulation (a) The Metamorphosis of Commodities Marx opens this section with a dialectical principle: \"We saw in a former chapter that the exchange of commodities implies contradictory and mutually exclusive conditions. The further development of the commodity does not abolish these contradictions, but rather provides the form within which they have room to move.\" The analogy is the ellipse: \"it is a contradiction to depict one body as constantly falling towards another and at the same time constantly flying away from it. The ellipse is a form of motion within which this contradiction is both realized and resolved.\" This is a general statement of Marx's dialectical method: contradictions are not eliminated but realized in a form that allows them to move. The process of exchange is a process of social metabolism ( Stoffwechsel ) — a term Marx introduces here for the first time. It transfers commodities from hands in which they are non-use-values to hands in which they are use-values. \"The product of one kind of useful labour replaces that of another.\" Once a commodity serves as a use-value, it \"falls out of the sphere of exchange into that of consumption.\" Marx is concerned here only with the formal aspect — the change in form or metamorphosis through which the social metabolism is mediated. He notes that this change of form \"has been very imperfectly grasped as yet\" because of the failure to distinguish the material aspect (exchange of commodity for gold) from the formal aspect (what happens to the form of the commodity). \"We do not see that gold, as a mere commodity, is not money, and that the other commodities, through their prices, themselves relate to gold as the medium for expressing their own shape in money.\" The key dialectical point: \"Commodities first enter into the process of exchange ungilded and unsweetened, retaining their original home-grown shape. Exchange, however, produces a differentiation of the commodity into two elements, commodity and money, an external opposition which expresses the opposition between use-value and value which is inherent in it.\" The commodity is in reality a use-value; its existence as a value appears only ideally, in its price. Gold, inversely, ranks only as the materialization of value; its use-value appears only ideally in the series of expressions of relative value. \"These antagonistic forms of the commodities are the real forms of motion of the process of exchange.\" The Circuit C-M-C Marx then accompanies \"our old friend the linen weaver\" to the market. The weaver has 20 yards of linen, priced at £2. He exchanges it for £2, then parts with the £2 for a family Bible. The process is accomplished through \"two metamorphoses of opposite yet mutually complementary character — the conversion of the commodity into money, and the re-conversion of the money into a commodity.\" The two moments are distinct transactions (selling and buying) and a unity (\"selling in order to buy\"). The formula: C-M-C As far as material content is concerned, the movement is C-C — the exchange of one commodity for another. The result, for the weaver, is that he has exchanged the product of his labour for the product of someone else's — \"nothing more than an exchange of products.\" The Salto Mortale of the Commodity C-M : the first metamorphosis of the commodity, or sale . Marx calls this the commodity's salto mortale (death-leap). \"If the leap falls short, it is not the commodity which is defrauded but rather its owner.\" The social division of labour makes the producer's labour as one-sided as his needs are many-sided. His product must be a use-value for the owner of money — the labour expended on it must be \"of a socially useful kind, i.e. it must maintain its position as a branch of the social division of labour.\" But the division of labour is \"an organization of production which has grown up naturally, a web which has been, and continues to be, woven behind the backs of the producers of commodities.\" Marx then enumerates the specific hazards of the salto mortale : The commodity may be the product of a new kind of labour, claiming to satisfy a newly arisen need — or trying to create a new need. A particular operation may tear itself out of a larger framework and establish itself as an independent branch of labour — but the circumstances may not be ripe. The product may satisfy a social need today, but tomorrow be expelled by a similar product. Even if the labour is a recognized branch of the social division of labour, the society's need for linen may already be satisfied by rival weavers — making the product \"superfluous, redundant and consequently useless.\" Even if the use-value maintains itself, the quantity of socially necessary labour-time may have changed: \"What was yesterday undoubtedly labour-time socially necessary to the production of a yard of linen ceases to be so today.\" Even if every piece of linen contains socially necessary labour-time, \"all these pieces taken as a whole may contain superfluously expended labour-time.\" If the market cannot absorb the whole quantity at the normal price, \"too great a portion of the total social labour-time has been expended in the form of weaving.\" As the German proverb has it: \"caught together, hung together.\" The conclusion is memorable: \"commodities are in love with money, but 'the course of true love never did run smooth.'\" The quantitative and qualitative articulation of society's productive organism is \"as haphazard and spontaneous\" as its qualitative articulation. The owners of commodities find that \"the same division of labour which turns them into independent private producers also makes the social process of production and the relations of the individual producers to each other within that process independent of the producers themselves.\" And \"the independence of the individuals from each other has as its counterpart and supplement a system of all-round material dependence.\" The Identity of Sale and Purchase When the commodity is sold, \"a commodity and gold, 20 yards of linen and £2, have changed hands and places.\" What is the commodity exchanged for? \"For the universal shape assumed by its own value.\" What is gold exchanged for? \"For a particular form of its own use-value.\" The realization of a commodity's price is \"at the same time, and inversely, the realization of the merely ideal use-value of money.\" This single process is two-sided: from one pole it is a sale, from the other a purchase. \"A sale is a purchase, C-M is also M-C.\" The first metamorphosis of one commodity — its transformation from commodity-form into money — is \"invariably the second, and diametrically opposite, metamorphosis of some other commodity\" — the retransformation of the latter from money into a commodity. This interlocking is the essence of circulation. M-C: The Second Metamorphosis M-C : the concluding metamorphosis of the commodity — purchase . \"Money is the absolutely alienable commodity, because it is all other commodities divested of their shape, the product of their universal alienation.\" Money \"reads all prices backwards, and thus as it were mirrors itself in the bodies of all other commodities.\" The prices define the limit of money's convertibility — its own quantity. Since every commodity disappears when it becomes money, \"it is impossible to tell from the money itself how it got into the hands of its possessor, or what article has been changed into it. Non olet , from whatever source it may come.\" (The reference is to Vespasian's reply to Titus about the tax on public lavatories: \"It has no smell.\") M-C is at the same time C-M: the concluding metamorphosis of one commodity is the first metamorphosis of another. \"Since the producer of the commodity offers only a single product, he often sells it in large quantities, whereas the fact that he has many needs compels him to split up the price realized... into numerous purchases. Hence a sale leads to many purchases of different commodities. The concluding metamorphosis of a commodity thus constitutes an aggregate of the first metamorphoses of other commodities.\" The Complete Metamorphosis and Its Implications The complete metamorphosis of a commodity \"implies four denouements and three dramatis personae.\" First, the commodity-owner confronts the money-owner. Once the commodity is changed into money, the money becomes its vanishing equivalent-form, whose use-value exists in the bodies of other commodities. The seller in the first transaction becomes a buyer in the second, meeting a third commodity-owner as seller. The two inverted phases constitute a circuit: commodity-form, stripping off of this form, and return to it. The commodity is subject to contradictory determinations: at the starting-point it is a non-use-value to its owner; at the end a use-value. Money appears in the first phase as \"a solid crystal of value\" into which the commodity has been transformed; afterwards it dissolves into \"the mere equivalent-form of the commodity.\" The circulation of commodities differs from direct exchange not only in form but in its essence. The weaver has exchanged his linen for a Bible, but \"this phenomenon is only true for him.\" The Bible-pusher had no intention of exchanging linen for his Bible; the weaver did not know that wheat had been exchanged for his linen. \"B's commodity replaces that of A, but A and B do not mutually exchange their commodities.\" It may happen that A and B buy from each other, but \"a particular relationship of this kind is by no means the necessary result of the general conditions of the circulation of commodities.\" On the one hand, exchange breaks through all individual and local limitations; on the other, \"there develops a whole network of social connections of natural origin, entirely beyond the control of the human agents.\" The process of circulation, unlike direct exchange, \"does not disappear from view once the use-values have changed places and changed hands. The money does not vanish when it finally drops out of the series of metamorphoses undergone by a commodity. It always leaves behind a precipitate at a point in the arena of circulation vacated by the commodities.\" In the metamorphosis linen-money-Bible, the linen first falls out of circulation, and money steps into its place; then the Bible falls out, and again money takes its place. \" Circulation sweats money from every pore. \" The Impossibility of Equilibrium and the Possibility of Crisis Marx now attacks the \"dogma that because every sale is a purchase, and every purchase a sale, the circulation of commodities necessarily implies an equilibrium between sales and purchases.\" To say that the number of sales equals the number of purchases is \"a flat tautology.\" Its real intention is \"to show that every seller brings his own buyer to market with him.\" Sale and purchase are one identical act considered as the alternating relation between two persons in polar opposition; but they constitute two acts of polar and opposite character considered as the transactions of one and the same person. The identity of sale and purchase implies that \"the commodity is useless if, when it is thrown into the alchemist's retort of circulation, it does not come out again as money.\" It further implies that the process, if it reaches fruition, \"constitutes a point of rest, an interval, long or short, in the life of the commodity.\" Since the first metamorphosis is at once a sale and a purchase, \"this partial process is at the same time an independent process in itself.\" The buyer has the commodity, the seller has the money — \"a commodity which remains in a form capable of circulating.\" \"No one can sell unless someone else purchases. But no one directly needs to purchase because he has just sold.\" Circulation \"bursts through all the temporal, spatial and personal barriers imposed by the direct exchange of products\" by splitting the direct identity between the exchange of one's own product and the acquisition of someone else's into \"the two antithetical segments of sale and purchase.\" The two processes \"lack internal independence because they complement each other. Hence, if the assertion of their external independence proceeds to a certain critical point, their unity violently makes itself felt by producing — a crisis .\" This is the key theoretical innovation. There is \"an antithesis, immanent in the commodity, between use-value and value, between private labour which must simultaneously manifest itself as directly social labour, and a particular concrete kind of labour which simultaneously counts as merely abstract universal labour, between the conversion of things into persons and the conversion of persons into things; the antithetical phases of the metamorphosis of the commodity are the developed forms of motion of this immanent contradiction. These forms therefore imply the possibility of crises, though no more than the possibility. For the development of this possibility into a reality a whole series of conditions is required, which do not yet even exist from the standpoint of the simple circulation of commodities.\" The footnote attacks \"the method of the bourgeoisie's economic apologists\": first, \"the identification of the circulation of commodities with the direct exchange of products, achieved simply by abstracting from their differences\"; second, \"the attempt to explain away the contradictions of the capitalist process of production by dissolving the relations between persons engaged in that process of production into the simple relations arising out of the circulation of commodities.\" J. B. Say is singled out: \"he knows that a commodity is a product\" — the triviality of Say's Law (\"products are exchanged against products\") is the foundation of the denial of crisis. Marx quotes Ricardo's adoption of Say: \"The conception adopted by Ricardo from the tedious Say, that over-production is not possible or at least that no general glut of the market is possible, is based on the proposition that products are exchanged against products.\" (b) The Circulation of Money The change of form C-M-C \"requires that a given value shall form the starting-point of the process, in the shape of a commodity, and that it shall return to the same point in the shape of a commodity. This movement of commodities is therefore a circuit. On the other hand, the form of this movement excludes money from the circuit.\" The result of the movement is not the return of money but \"its continued removal further and further away from its starting-point.\" As long as the seller sticks to his money, the commodity is \"still at the stage of the first metamorphosis.\" Once the process of selling in order to buy is complete, \"the money again leaves the hands of its original possessor.\" If the weaver sells more linen, money returns — but \"this return is not a result of the circulation of the first 20 yards of linen.\" It results only from \"the renewal or repetition of the same process of circulation with a fresh commodity.\" Hence the movement directly imparted to money takes the form of \"a constant removal from its starting-point, a path followed from the hands of one commodity-owner into those of another. This path is its circulation (currency, cours de la monnaie ).\" The Appearance of Money as the Mover \"The circulation of money is the constant and monotonous repetition of the same process. The commodity is always in the hands of the seller; the money, as a means of purchase, always in the hands of the buyer.\" Money serves as a means of purchase by realizing the price of the commodity. \"That this one-sided form of motion of the money arises out of the two-sided form of motion of the commodity is a circumstance which is hidden from view. The very nature of the circulation of commodities produces a semblance of the opposite.\" In the first metamorphosis, the money's movement is visible as also the commodity's movement; in the second, \"the movement appears to us as the movement of the money alone.\" The commodity falls out of circulation into consumption; \"its value-shape or monetary larva steps into its shoes.\" The continuity of the movement depends entirely on the money. Hence \"the result of the circulation of commodities, namely the replacement of one commodity by another, appears not to have been mediated by its own change of form, but rather by the function of money as means of circulation.\" As means of circulation, money \"circulates commodities, which in and for themselves lack the power of movement.\" Although \"the movement of money is merely the expression of the circulation of commodities, the situation appears to be the reverse of this, namely the circulation of commodities seems to be the result of the movement of money.\" Marx notes that money functions as a means of circulation only because \"in it the value possessed by commodities has taken on an independent shape.\" This must make itself \"plainly visible\" in the circulation of money. The linen first changes its commodity-form into money-form; the final term of C-M becomes the first term of M-C. \"The same pieces of coin come into the seller's hand as the alienated form of the commodity and leave it as the commodity in its absolutely alienable form. They are displaced twice.\" The two opposite changes undergone by the same commodity are reflected in \"the displacement, twice repeated but in opposite directions, of the same pieces of coin.\" If only a one-sided metamorphosis takes place — only sales or only purchases — then \"a given piece of money changes its place only once.\" Its second change of place \"always expresses the second metamorphosis of the commodity, its reconversion from money.\" The frequently repeated displacement of the same coins reflects \"not only the series of metamorphoses undergone by a single commodity, but also the mutual entanglement of the innumerable metamorphoses in the whole world of commodities.\" The Quantity of Money in Circulation Every commodity, when it first enters circulation, \"does so only to fall out of circulation once more and be replaced again and again by fresh commodities. Money, on the contrary, as the medium of circulation, haunts the sphere of circulation and constantly moves around within it. The question therefore arises of how much money this sphere continuously absorbs.\" The answer is developed through a series of steps: Step 1 : In a given country, numerous one-sided metamorphoses take place simultaneously — simple sales on one hand, simple purchases on the other. In their prices, commodities \"have already been equated with definite but imaginary quantities of money.\" Since money and commodities \"always come into physical confrontation with each other,\" the amount of means of circulation required \"is determined beforehand by the sum of the prices of all these commodities.\" The money is \"only the representation in real life of the quantity of gold previously expressed in the imagination by the sum of the prices.\" Step 2 : Given that the sum of prices rises or falls with the value of gold (inversely), \"the quantity of money in circulation must fall or rise to the same extent.\" But this change is \"caused by the money itself, yet not in virtue of its function as a medium of circulation, but rather in virtue of its function as a measure of value.\" First the price varies inversely as the value of money; then the quantity of the medium varies directly as the price. The same phenomenon would arise if silver replaced gold as the measure of value (more silver would circulate) or if gold replaced silver (less gold would circulate). Step 3 : \"We have already seen that the sphere of circulation has a gap in it, through which gold (or silver, or the money material in general) enters as a commodity with a given value.\" When money functions as a measure of value, its value is presupposed. If that value falls, the fall \"first shows itself in a change in the prices of those commodities which are directly exchanged with the precious metals at their source.\" Other commodities \"will continue for a long time to be estimated in terms of the former value of the measure of value, which has now become antiquated and illusory.\" But \"one commodity infects another through their common value-relation,\" and prices gradually settle into the proportions determined by comparative values. This process is accompanied by \"a continued increase in the quantity of the precious metals.\" The critique of the quantity theory : \"A one-sided observation of the events which followed the discovery of fresh supplies of gold and silver led some people in the seventeenth and more particularly in the eighteenth century to the false conclusion that the prices of commodities had risen because there was more gold and silver acting as the means of circulation.\" Marx will henceforth assume the value of gold as given. \"On this assumption, then, the quantity of the medium of circulation is determined by the sum of the prices to be realized.\" Step 4 : If the price of each commodity is given, the sum of prices depends on the total amount of commodities in circulation. \"If our quarter of wheat costs £2, 100 quarters will cost £200, 200 quarters £400... the quantity of money which changes places with the wheat, when it is sold, must increase as the quantity of the wheat increases.\" Step 5 : If the mass of commodities remains constant, \"the quantity of money in circulation surges up or down according to the fluctuations in the prices of the commodities.\" It is not necessary that all prices rise or fall simultaneously; \"a rise or a fall in the prices of a number of leading articles is sufficient.\" Whether the change reflects an actual change in value or a fluctuation in market prices, \"the effect on the quantity of the medium of circulation remains the same.\" The Velocity of Circulation and the Equation of Exchange Marx now introduces the crucial concept of the velocity of circulation through a numerical example. If there are \"unconnected and simultaneous sales\" of 1 quarter of wheat, 20 yards of linen, 1 Bible, and 4 gallons of brandy, each priced at £2, the sum of prices is £8, and £8 must enter circulation. But if the same articles are links in a chain of metamorphoses (wheat-£2-linen-£2-Bible-£2-brandy-£2), then the £2 \"causes the different commodities to circulate after realizing their prices successively, and therefore realizing the sum of those prices, which is £8.\" The £2 \"has turned over four times. It has performed four acts of circulation.\" This repeated change of place corresponds to the double change of form undergone by the commodities and \"the intertwining of the metamorphoses of different commodities.\" The antithetical and complementary phases \"cannot take place alongside each other. They must follow in temporal succession.\" Hence \"the velocity of the circulation of money is measured by the number of times the same piece of money turns over within a given period.\" The equation: Quantity of money functioning as circulating medium = Sum of prices of commodities ÷ Number of times coins of the same denomination turn over Or, more formally, where M is the quantity of money, P the sum of prices, and V the average velocity of circulation: M = P / V This law \"holds generally.\" The process of circulation is made up, on the one hand, of \"numerous isolated and simultaneous partial metamorphoses\" (each coin changes position once), and on the other hand, of \"many distinct series of metamorphoses\" (each coin turns over a number of times). Given the total number of times all coins turn over, we can arrive at the average velocity of circulation. The quantity of money thrown into circulation at the beginning of each day is determined by the sum of prices of commodities circulating simultaneously. But within the process, \"coins are, so to speak, made responsible for each other. If one increases its velocity of circulation, the other slows down or completely leaves the sphere of circulation.\" The sphere of circulation \"can absorb only the amount of gold which, multiplied by the average number of times its basic unit turns over, is equal to the sum of prices to be realized.\" Hence \"if the number of acts of circulation performed by the separate pieces increases, the total number of those pieces in circulation diminishes.\" This is why \"one has only to throw a given quantity of £1 notes into circulation in order to extract the same number of sovereigns from it. This trick is well known to all banks.\" Velocity as the Reflection of Commodity Metamorphosis \"Just as the circulation of money is in general merely a reflection of the process of circulation of commodities... so too the velocity of circulation of money is merely a reflection of the rapidity with which commodities change their forms, the continuous interlocking of the series of metamorphoses, the hurried nature of society's metabolic process, the quick disappearance of commodities from the sphere of circulation, and their equally quick replacement by fresh commodities.\" In the velocity of circulation \"there appears the fluid unity of the antithetical and complementary phases.\" Inversely, \"when the circulation of money slows down, the two processes become separated, they assert their independence and mutual antagonism; stagnation occurs in the changes of form, and hence in the metabolic process.\" The circulation itself \"gives no clue to the origin of this stagnation; it merely presents us with the phenomenon.\" Popular opinion attributes stagnation to \"a quantitative deficiency in the circulating medium\" — a delusion, though Marx concedes in a footnote that \"an actual shortage of the circulating medium resulting from, say, bungling government interference... may not for its part give rise to stagnation.\" The Three Factors and Their Variations The total quantity of money functioning as circulating medium is determined \"on the one hand by the sum of the prices of the commodities in circulation, and on the other hand by the rapidity of alternation of the antithetical processes of circulation.\" The three factors — \"the movement of prices, the quantity of commodities in circulation, and the velocity of circulation of money\" — can all vary in various directions. Marx outlines the most important combinations: Prices constant : Quantity of money may increase with more commodities or slower velocity; may decrease with fewer commodities or faster velocity. General rise in prices : Quantity may remain constant if the number of commodities decreases proportionally, or if velocity increases at the same rate; may decrease with more rapid decrease in commodities or more rapid increase in velocity. General fall in prices : Quantity may remain constant if the number of commodities increases proportionally or velocity decreases proportionally; may increase if commodities increase more quickly or velocity decreases more quickly. The variations \"may be mutually compensatory,\" so that \"notwithstanding their continued instability, the sum of the prices to be realized and the quantity of money in circulation remains constant.\" Hence, over long periods, \"the quantity of money in circulation in each country diverges far less from its average level than we should at first sight have expected, with the exception of the violent perturbations which arise periodically, either from crises in production and commerce, or, more rarely, from changes in the value of money itself.\" The Law of Circulation Restated and the Critique of the Quantity Theory The law \"may also be stated as follows: given the sum of the values of commodities, and the average rapidity of their metamorphoses, the quantity of money or of the material of money in circulation depends on its own value.\" The illusion \"that it is, on the contrary, prices which are determined by the quantity of the circulating medium, and that the latter for its part depends on the amount of monetary material which happens to be present in a country\" — the quantity theory — \"had its roots in the absurd hypothesis... that commodities enter into the process of circulation without a price, and money enters without a value, and that, once they have entered circulation, an aliquot part of the medley of commodities is exchanged for an aliquot part of the heap of precious metals.\" Marx's footnotes cite Petty's correct view (quoted with approval), Adam Smith's ambiguous position (correct in criticizing earlier systems, but \"quietly eliminates the question about the amount of coin in circulation by quite improperly regarding money as a simple commodity\"), Hume's and Vanderlint's quantity theory, and Montesquieu's absurd calculation of \"the total commodity = x cwt of gold.\" He also mocks John Stuart Mill's \"eclectic logic\" — holding both his father's view and the opposite — and notes that \"in no science other than political economy does there prevail such a combination of great self-importance with the mouthing of elementary commonplaces.\" (c) Coin. The Symbol of Value \"Money takes the shape of coin because of its function as the circulating medium.\" The weight of gold represented in the imagination by prices \"has to confront those commodities, within circulation, as coins or pieces of gold of the same denomination.\" The business of coining, like the establishing of a standard measure of prices, \"is an attribute proper to the state.\" The different national uniforms worn by gold and silver as coins, \"but taken off again when they appear on the world market, demonstrate the separation between the internal or national spheres of commodity circulation and its universal sphere, the world market.\" \"The only difference, therefore, between coin and bullion lies in their physical configuration, and gold can at any time pass from one form to the other. For a coin, the road from the mint is also the path to the melting pot.\" In circulation, coins wear down; \"the denomination of the gold and its substance, the nominal content and the real content, begin to move apart.\" Coins of the same denomination become different in value because they are different in weight. \"The weight of gold fixed upon as the standard of prices diverges from the weight which serves as the circulating medium, and the latter thereby ceases to be a real equivalent of the commodities whose prices it realizes.\" The history of these difficulties \"constitutes the history of the coinage throughout the Middle Ages and in modern times down to the eighteenth century.\" The \"natural and spontaneous tendency of the process of circulation to transform the coin from its metallic existence as gold into the semblance of gold\" is recognized by modern laws on the degree of metal loss which demonetizes a gold coin. \"The fact that the circulation of money itself splits the nominal content of coins away from their real content, dividing their metallic existence from their functional existence, this fact implies the latent possibility of replacing metallic money with tokens made of some other material, i.e. symbols which would perform the function of coins.\" Silver and copper tokens replace gold where coins circulate most rapidly; the law determines the minute proportions in which they can be accepted as payment. \"The metallic content of silver and copper tokens is arbitrarily determined by law.\" In circulation they wear down even more rapidly. \"Their function as coins is therefore in practice entirely independent of their weight, i.e. it is independent of all value. In its form of existence as coin, gold becomes completely divorced from the substance of its value. Relatively valueless objects, therefore, such as paper notes, can serve as coins in place of gold. This purely symbolic character of the currency is still somewhat disguised in the case of metal tokens. In paper money it stands out plainly. But we can see: everything depends on the first step.\" Inconvertible Paper Money and Its Law Marx is concerned here only with \"inconvertible paper money issued by the state and given forced currency.\" This money \"emerges directly out of the circulation of metallic money. Credit-money on the other hand implies relations which are as yet totally unknown, from the standpoint of the simple circulation of commodities.\" (But he notes in passing: \"just as true paper money arises out of the function of money as the circulating medium, so does credit-money take root spontaneously in the function of money as the means of payment.\") Pieces of paper with money-names \"are thrown into the circulation process from outside by the state.\" In so far as they actually circulate in place of the same amount of gold, \"their movement is simply a reflection of the laws of monetary circulation itself.\" The law peculiar to paper money: \"the issue of paper money must be restricted to the quantity of gold (or silver) which would actually be in circulation, and which is represented symbolically by the paper money.\" The quantity of gold absorbed by circulation constantly fluctuates, but \"the mass of the circulating medium in a given country never sinks below a certain minimum.\" This minimum can therefore be replaced by paper symbols. \"If however all the channels of circulation were today filled with paper money to the full extent of their capacity for absorbing money, they might the next day be over-full owing to the fluctuations in the circulation of commodities. There would no longer be any standard.\" \"If the paper money exceeds its proper limit... then, quite apart from the danger of becoming universally discredited, it will still represent within the world of commodities only that quantity of gold which is fixed by its immanent laws. No greater quantity is capable of being represented. If the quantity of paper money represents twice the amount of gold available, then in practice £1 will be the money-name not of ¼ of an ounce of gold, but ⅛ of an ounce. The effect is the same as if an alteration had taken place in the function of gold as the standard of prices. The values previously expressed by the price of £1 would now be expressed by the price of £2.\" Paper money is a symbol of gold, a symbol of money. Its relation to the values of commodities consists only in this: \"they find imaginary expression in certain quantities of gold, and the same quantities are symbolically and physically represented by the paper. Only in so far as paper money represents gold, which like all other commodities has value, is it a symbol of value.\" Why Gold Can Be Replaced by Symbols Marx addresses the question: why is gold capable of being replaced by valueless symbols? The answer: \"it is capable of being replaced in this way only if its function as coin or circulating medium can be singled out or rendered independent.\" This function \"does not attain an independent position as far as the individual gold coins are concerned\" — a piece of money is a mere coin \"only as long as it is actually in circulation.\" But \"what is not valid for the individual gold coin is valid for that minimum mass of gold which is capable of being replaced by paper money. That mass constantly haunts the sphere of circulation, continually functions as a circulating medium, and therefore exists exclusively as the bearer of this function.\" Its movement represents \"nothing but the continued alternation of the inverse phases of the metamorphosis C-M-C, phases in which the commodity's shape as a value confronts it only to disappear again immediately.\" The presentation of exchange-value \"is here only a transient aspect of the process. The commodity is immediately replaced again by another commodity. Hence in this process which continually makes money pass from hand to hand, it only needs to lead a symbolic existence. Its functional existence so to speak absorbs its material existence.\" \"One thing is necessary, however: the symbol of money must have its own objective social validity. The paper acquires this by its forced currency. The state's compulsion can only be of any effect within that internal sphere of circulation which is circumscribed by the boundaries of a given community, but it is also only within that sphere that money is completely absorbed in its function as medium of circulation.\" The footnote cites Nicholas Barbon, who deduced from the fact that gold and silver become their own symbols \"the right of governments 'to raise money'\" — to give to the quantity of silver called a shilling the name of a greater quantity, such as a crown, and so to pay back shillings to creditors instead of crowns. This is the theory of debasement dressed up as monetary theory. III. Money \"The commodity which functions as a measure of value and therefore also as the medium of circulation, either in its own body or through a representative, is money. Gold (or silver) is therefore money.\" It functions as money, on the one hand, \"when it has to appear in person as gold\" — as the money commodity, neither merely ideal nor capable of being represented. On the other hand, \"when its function... causes it to be fixed as the sole form of value, or, in other words, as the only adequate form of existence of exchange value in the face of all the other commodities, here playing the role of use-values pure and simple.\" (a) Hoarding \"The continuous circular movement of the two antithetical metamorphoses of commodities, or the repeated alternating flow of sale and purchase, is reflected in the unceasing turnover of money, in the function it performs of a perpetuum mobile of circulation. But as soon as the series of metamorphoses is interrupted, as soon as sales are not supplemented by subsequent purchases, money is immobilized. In other words, it is transformed, as Boisguillebert says, from 'meuble' into 'immeuble', from coin into money.\" \"When the circulation of commodities first develops, there also develops the necessity and the passionate desire to hold fast to the product of the first metamorphosis. This product is the transformed shape of the commodity, or its gold chrysalis.\" Commodities are sold \"not in order to buy commodities, but in order to replace their commodity-form by their money-form. Instead of being merely a way of mediating the metabolic process, this change of form becomes an end in itself. The form of the commodity in which it is divested of content is prevented from functioning as its absolutely alienable form... The money is petrified into a hoard, and the seller of commodities becomes a hoarder of money.\" \"In the very beginnings of the circulation of commodities, it is only the excess amounts of use-value which are converted into money. Gold and silver thus become of themselves social expressions for superfluity or wealth. This naive form of hoarding is perpetuated among those peoples whose traditional mode of production... corresponds to a fixed and limited range of needs. This is true of the Asiatics, particularly the Indians.\" Vanderlint's question — why Indian commodities are so cheap — is answered: because the Indians bury their money. From 1602 to 1734, they buried 150 million pounds' worth of silver. From 1856 to 1866, England exported to India (and China) £120,000,000 in silver. \"With more developed commodity production, every producer is compelled to secure for himself the nexus rerum , the 'social pledge.'\" His needs are ceaselessly renewed, necessitating continual purchases, whereas the production and sale of his own commodity costs time and is subject to accidents. \"In order then to be able to buy without selling, he must have sold previously without buying.\" At the sources of production, the precious metals are directly exchanged for other commodities — sales without purchases. \"And later sales, again without subsequent purchases, merely bring about a further distribution of the precious metals among all the owners of commodities. In this way, hoards of gold and silver of the most various sizes are piled up at all the points of commercial intercourse.\" \"With the possibility of keeping hold of the commodity as exchange-value, or exchange-value as a commodity, the lust for gold awakens. With the extension of commodity circulation there is an increase in the power of money.\" Marx quotes Columbus: \"Gold is a wonderful thing! Its owner is master of all he desires. Gold can even enable souls to enter Paradise.\" Since money does not reveal what has been transformed into it, \"everything, commodity or not, is convertible into money. Everything becomes saleable and purchaseable. Circulation becomes the great social retort into which everything is thrown, to come out again as the money crystal. Nothing is immune from this alchemy, the bones of the saints cannot withstand it.\" Just as money extinguishes all qualitative differences between commodities, \"so too for its part, as a radical leveller, it extinguishes all distinctions.\" \"But money is itself a commodity, an external object capable of becoming the private property of any individual. Thus the social power becomes the private power of private persons. Ancient society therefore denounced it as tending to destroy the economic and moral order. Modern society... greets gold as its Holy Grail, as the glittering incarnation of its innermost principle of life.\" The hoarding drive is \"boundless in its nature.\" Qualitatively, money is \"independent of all limits... the universal representative of material wealth because it is directly convertible into any other commodity. But at the same time every actual sum of money is limited in amount, and therefore has only a limited efficacy as a means of purchase. This contradiction between the quantitative limitation and the qualitative lack of limitation of money keeps driving the hoarder back to his Sisyphean task: accumulation. He is in the same situation as a world conqueror, who discovers a new boundary with each country he annexes.\" The hoarder \"sacrifices the lusts of his flesh to the fetish of gold. He takes the gospel of abstinence very seriously.\" But \"he cannot withdraw any more from circulation, in the shape of money, than he has thrown into it, in the shape of commodities. The more he produces, the more he can sell. Work, thrift and greed are therefore his three cardinal virtues, and to sell much and buy little is the sum of his political economy.\" \"Alongside the direct form of the hoard there runs its aesthetic form, the possession of commodities made out of gold and silver.\" This grows with the wealth of civil society. \"Let us be rich, or let us appear rich\" (Diderot). There is formed \"a constantly extending market for gold and silver which is independent of their monetary functions\" and \"a latent source of monetary inflow which is used particularly in periods of social disturbance.\" Hoarding serves various purposes in an economy where metallic circulation prevails. Its first function arises from the conditions of circulation: owing to fluctuations in the extent and rapidity of circulation and in prices, \"the quantity of money in circulation unceasingly ebbs and flows. This quantity must therefore be capable of expansion and contraction. At one time money must be attracted as coin, at another time coin must be repelled as money.\" To ensure that the mass of money in circulation corresponds to the saturation level of circulation, \"it is necessary for the quantity of gold and silver available in a country to be greater than the quantity required to function as coin. The reserves created by hoarding serve as channels through which money may flow in and out of circulation, so that the circulation itself never overflows its banks.\" (b) Means of Payment \"In the direct form of commodity circulation hitherto considered, we found a given value always presented to us in a double shape, as a commodity at one pole, and money at the opposite pole.\" But with the development of circulation, \"conditions arise under which the alienation of the commodity becomes separated by an interval of time from the realization of its price.\" Marx enumerates these conditions: One commodity requires a longer, another a shorter time for production. Production depends on different seasons. One commodity may be born in the market place, another must travel to a distant market. One commodity-owner may step forth as a seller before the other is ready to buy. When transactions are continually repeated between the same persons, \"the conditions of sale are regulated according to the conditions of production.\" In the case of houses, the use of the commodity is sold for a definite period; the buyer receives the use-value only after the lease expires. \"He therefore buys it before he pays for it. The seller sells an existing commodity, the buyer buys as the mere representative of money, or rather as the representative of future money. The seller becomes a creditor, the buyer becomes a debtor.\" Money receives a new function: it becomes the means of payment . \"The role of creditor or of debtor results here from the simple circulation of commodities. The change in its form impresses this new stamp on seller and buyer. At first, therefore, these new roles are just as transient as those of seller and buyer.\" Nevertheless, \"this opposition now looks less pleasant from the very outset, and it is capable of a more rigid crystallization.\" Marx notes that \"the same characteristics can emerge independently of the circulation of commodities. The class struggle in the ancient world, for instance, took the form mainly of a contest between debtors and creditors, and ended in Rome with the ruin of the plebeian debtors, who were replaced by slaves. In the Middle Ages the contest ended with the ruin of the feudal debtors.\" \"The two equivalents, commodities and money, have ceased to appear simultaneously at the two poles of the process of sale.\" Money functions first as a measure of value in determining the price of the commodity sold; the price fixed by contract measures the obligation of the buyer. Second, it serves as \"a nominal means of purchase. Although existing only in the promise of the buyer to pay, it causes the commodity to change hands.\" Only when payment falls due does the means of payment actually enter circulation. The circulating medium was transformed into a hoard \"because the process stopped short after the first phase\"; the means of payment enters circulation \"but only after the commodity has already left it.\" Money \"no longer mediates the process. It brings it to an end by emerging independently, as the absolute form of existence of exchange-value, in other words the universal commodity.\" The seller turned his commodity into money to satisfy a need; the hoarder to preserve the monetary form; the indebted purchaser \"in order to be able to pay. If he does not pay, his goods will be sold compulsorily.\" The value-form of the commodity \"has now become the self-sufficient purpose of the sale, owing to a social necessity springing from the conditions of the process of circulation itself.\" \"The buyer converts money back into commodities before he has turned commodities into money: in other words, he achieves the second metamorphosis of commodities before the first. The seller's commodity circulates, and realizes its price, but only as a title to money in civil law. It is converted into a use-value before it has been converted into money.\" The Chain of Payments and Its Laws \"The obligations falling due within a given period of the circulation process represent the sum of the prices of the commodities whose sale gave rise to those obligations. The quantity of money necessary to realize this sum depends in the first instance on the rapidity of circulation of the means of payment.\" This quantity is conditioned by two factors: first, \"the way in which relations between creditors and debtors interlock, as when A receives money from B, who is in debt to him, and then pays it out to his creditor C\"; second, \"the length of time between the different days in which the obligations fall due.\" \"The chain of payments, or retarded first metamorphoses, which participate in the process, is essentially different from that intertwining of the series of metamorphoses considered earlier. The flow of the circulating medium does not merely express the connection between buyers and sellers: the connection itself arises within, and exists through, the circulation of money. The movement of the means of payment, however, expresses a social connection which was already present independently.\" \"The fact that sales take place simultaneously and side by side limits the extent to which the rapidity of turnover can make up for the quantity of currency available. On the other hand, this fact gives a new impulse towards the economical use of the means of payment.\" With the concentration of payments in one place, \"special institutions and methods of liquidation develop spontaneously\" — for instance, the virements in medieval Lyons. \"The debts due to A from B, to B from C, to C from A, and so on, have only to be brought face to face in order to cancel each other out, to a certain extent, as positive and negative amounts. There remains only a single debit balance to be settled. The greater the concentration of the payments, the less is this balance in relation to the total amount, hence the less is the mass of the means of payment in circulation.\" The Contradiction of the Means of Payment and the Monetary Crisis \"There is a contradiction immanent in the function of money as the means of payment. When the payments balance each other, money functions only nominally, as money of account, as a measure of value. But when actual payments have to be made, money does not come onto the scene as a circulating medium, in its merely transient form of an intermediary in the social metabolism, but as the individual incarnation of social labour, the independent presence of exchange-value, the universal commodity. This contradiction bursts forth in that aspect of an industrial and commercial crisis which is known as a monetary crisis .\" \"Such a crisis occurs only where the ongoing chain of payments has been fully developed, along with an artificial system for settling them. Whenever there is a general disturbance of the mechanism, no matter what its cause, money suddenly and immediately changes over from its merely nominal shape, money of account, into hard cash. Profane commodities can no longer replace it. The use-value of commodities becomes valueless, and their value vanishes in the face of their own form of value. The bourgeois, drunk with prosperity and arrogantly certain of himself, has just declared that money is a purely imaginary creation. 'Commodities alone are money,' he said. But now the opposite cry resounds over the markets of the world: only money is a commodity.\" \"In a crisis, the antithesis between commodities and their value-form, money, is raised to the level of an absolute contradiction. Hence money's form of appearance is here also a matter of indifference. The monetary famine remains whether payments have to be made in gold or in credit-money, such as bank-notes.\" The Total Quantity of Money in Circulation \"If we now consider the total amount of money in circulation during a given period, we find that, for any given turnover rate of the medium of circulation and the means of payment, it is equal to the sum of prices to be realized, plus the sum of the payments falling due, minus the payments which balance each other out, and, finally, minus the number of circuits in which the same piece of coin serves alternately as medium of circulation and means of payment.\" The farmer sells wheat for £2 (medium of circulation); uses it to pay for linen (means of payment); the weaver buys a Bible (medium of circulation again). \"Therefore, even when prices, speed of monetary circulation and economies in the use of the means of payment are given, the quantity of money in circulation no longer corresponds with the mass of commodities in circulation during a given period, such as a day. Money which represents commodities long since withdrawn from circulation continues to circulate. Commodities circulate, but their equivalent in money does not appear until some future date. Moreover, the debts contracted each day, and the payments falling due on the same day, are entirely incommensurable magnitudes.\" Credit-Money and the Expansion of the Means of Payment \"Credit-money springs directly out of the function of money as a means of payment, in that certificates of debts owing for already purchased commodities themselves circulate for the purpose of transferring those debts to others. On the other hand, the function of money as a means of payment undergoes expansion in proportion as the system of credit itself expands. As the means of payment money takes on its own peculiar forms of existence, in which it inhabits the sphere of large-scale commercial transactions. Gold and silver coin, on the other hand, are mostly relegated to the sphere of retail trade.\" Marx's footnote provides a striking empirical illustration: a statement from Morrison, Dillon & Co., a large London merchant bank, showing its yearly receipts and payments for 1856, reduced to the scale of one million pounds. The vast majority of transactions are conducted through bills and cheques; actual gold and silver coin constitute a tiny fraction (under 4% combined). The Transformation of Payments in Kind into Money Payments \"When the production of commodities has attained a certain level and extent, the function of money as means of payment begins to spread out beyond the sphere of the circulation of commodities. It becomes the universal material of contracts. Rent, taxes and so on are transformed from payments in kind to payments in money.\" Marx gives historical examples: the twice-repeated failure of the Roman Empire to levy all contributions in money; the misery of the French agricultural population under Louis XIV; the resistance of Asia, where ground rent paid in kind \"forms one of the secrets of the self-preservation of the Ottoman Empire.\" If Japan's rents in kind are transformed into money rents by European trade, \"the exemplary agriculture of that country will be done for.\" \"In every country, certain days become established as the dates on which general settlements are made.\" These depend partly on natural conditions of production (seasons) and partly regulate payments with no direct connection to commodity circulation (taxes, rents). The concentration of payments on certain days causes \"periodic, but entirely superficial, perturbations in the economy of the means of payment.\" Marx cites a striking example from Scotland: on Whitsuntide 1824, the demand for notes was so great that by 11 a.m. no notes were left; by 3 p.m. the whole of the notes were returned — \"a mere transfer from hand to hand.\" Although the average effective circulation of bank-notes in Scotland was less than £3m., on certain settlement days every single note (about £7m.) was called into activity. \"The development of money as a means of payment makes it necessary to accumulate it in preparation for the days when the sums which are owing fall due. While hoarding, considered as an independent form of self-enrichment, vanishes with the advance of bourgeois society, it grows at the same time in the form of the accumulation of a reserve fund of the means of payment.\" (c) World Money \"When money leaves the domestic sphere of circulation it loses the local functions it has acquired there, as the standard of prices, coin, and small change, and as a symbol of value, and falls back into its original form as precious metal in the shape of bullion. In world trade, commodities develop their value universally. Their independent value-form thus confronts them here too as world money. It is in the world market that money first functions to its full extent as the commodity whose natural form is also the directly social form of realization of human labour in the abstract. Its mode of existence becomes adequate to its concept.\" \"Within the sphere of domestic circulation, there can only be one commodity which by serving as a measure of value becomes money. On the world market a double standard prevails, both gold and silver.\" Marx's footnote attacks \"the absurdity of all legislation laying down that the banks of a country should form reserves only of the particular precious metal circulating within the country as money.\" He cites the Bank of England's \"pleasant difficulties\" and Peel's Bank Act of 1844, which allowed the Bank to issue notes against silver bullion on condition that the silver reserve never exceed one-fourth of the gold reserve. Engels' addition to the fourth German edition provides an extended analysis of the change in the relative values of gold and silver — from 15½:1 twenty-five years earlier to approximately 22:1 — as a result of a revolution in the mode of production of both metals. Gold was formerly obtained by washing from alluvial deposits; now it requires processing quartz lodes, where \"1,000,000 grammes of quartz barely yield 1-3 grammes of gold.\" Silver, on the other hand, occurs in special quartz that is separated \"with comparative ease\" and contains \"mostly 40-90 per cent silver.\" Moreover, huge new silver deposits were discovered in North America and opened up by railways. \"From this alone it is apparent that the labour expended on the production of gold is tending to increase, while that expended on silver production has decidedly decreased.\" Engels concludes by dismissing \"the utopianism of the bimetallist idea that compulsory international quotation will raise silver again to the old value ratio of 1: 15½. It is more likely that silver will forfeit its money function more and more in the world market.\" The Three Functions of World Money \"World money serves as the universal means of payment, as the universal means of purchase, and as the absolute social materialization of wealth as such (universal wealth). Its predominant function is as means of payment in the settling of international balances. Hence the slogan of the Mercantile System: balance of trade.\" \"Gold and silver serve essentially as international means of purchase when the customary equilibrium in the interchange of products between different nations is suddenly disturbed. And, lastly, world money serves as the universally recognized social materialization of wealth, whenever it is not a matter of buying or paying, but of transferring wealth from one country to another, and whenever its transfer in the form of commodities is ruled out, either by the conjuncture of the market, or by the purpose of the transfer itself.\" (Examples: subsidies, money loans for wars, enabling banks to resume cash payments.) \"Just as every country needs a reserve fund for its internal circulation, so too it requires one for circulation in the world market. The functions of hoards, therefore, arise in part out of the function of money as medium of payment and circulation internally, and in part out of its function as a world currency. In this latter role it is always the genuine money-commodity, gold and silver in their physical shape, which is required. For that reason Sir James Steuart expressly characterizes gold and silver as 'money of the world' in order to distinguish them from their merely local representatives.\" The Twofold Motion of Gold and Silver \"The stream of gold and silver has a twofold motion. On the one hand, it spreads out from its sources all over the world, and is absorbed to various extents into the different national spheres of circulation, where it enters into the various channels of internal circulation. There it replaces abraded gold and silver coins, supplies the material for articles of luxury, and petrifies into hoards. This first movement is transmitted through the medium of the direct exchange of the labour of individual countries... for the labour realized in the precious metals by the gold- and silver-producing countries. On the other hand, gold and silver continually flow backwards and forwards between the different national spheres of circulation, and this movement follows the unceasing fluctuations of the rate of exchange.\" \"Countries with developed bourgeois production limit the hoards concentrated in the strong rooms of the banks to the minimum required for the performance of their specific functions. Whenever these hoards are strikingly above their average level, this is, with some exceptions, an indication of stagnation in the circulation of commodities, i.e. of an interruption in the flow of their metamorphoses.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles This chapter reveals several foundational principles of Marx's analysis of money and circulation: 1. Money is a social relation, not a thing. The most important lesson is that money is not a physical object with intrinsic properties but \"the necessary form of appearance of the measure of value which is immanent in commodities, namely labour-time.\" Its power derives not from its material substance (gold) but from the social relations it embodies. This is why paper money can replace gold: because the material substance was always secondary to the social function. Yet — and this is crucial — the materiality of gold matters insofar as it provides an \"objective social validity\" that no mere convention can. The chapter thus avoids both crude materialism (money is just gold) and crude idealism (money is just convention). 2. The dialectical development of monetary functions. Money's functions — measure of value, means of circulation, hoarding, means of payment, world money — are not a list but a dialectical sequence. Each function arises from a contradiction in the previous one. The measure of value requires stability but is based on a variable commodity (gold); this is resolved by the separation of measure of value from standard of price. The means of circulation implies the identity of sale and purchase, but this identity can be broken; the result is hoarding. Hoarding as self-enrichment gives way to accumulation as a reserve fund; the means of payment emerges when the alienation of the commodity is separated from the realization of its price; the contradiction of the means of payment produces the monetary crisis; and the whole system culminates in world money, where money \"becomes adequate to its concept.\" 3. The possibility of crisis is immanent in the commodity-form. This is perhaps the most important theoretical innovation. The separation of sale and purchase — made possible by money — creates the formal possibility of crisis. \"These forms therefore imply the possibility of crises, though no more than the possibility.\" The development of this possibility into reality requires \"a whole series of conditions... which do not yet even exist from the standpoint of the simple circulation of commodities.\" This refutes Say's Law and all those who argue that exchange is inherently equilibrating. It also sets up the analysis of capitalist crisis in later volumes. 4. The critique of the quantity theory of money. Marx systematically demolishes the notion that prices are determined by the quantity of circulating medium. The correct relation is: quantity of money = sum of prices ÷ velocity of circulation. Prices are determined by the values of commodities and the value of money, not by the quantity of money. The quantity theory arises from \"a one-sided observation\" of the effect of new gold discoveries and rests on \"the absurd hypothesis... that commodities enter into the process of circulation without a price, and money enters without a value.\" 5. The fetishism of money. Money embodies the most extreme form of commodity fetishism: \"Since money does not reveal what has been transformed into it, everything, commodity or not, is convertible into money... Nothing is immune from this alchemy, the bones of the saints cannot withstand it.\" Money is the \"radical leveller\" that \"extinguishes all distinctions\" — a social power that becomes the private power of private persons. This is the root of the \"lust for gold\" and the \"hoarding drive.\" Broader Theoretical Implications For the theory of value : The chapter demonstrates that value is not a static magnitude but a dynamic social relation that must constantly prove itself in exchange. The salto mortale of the commodity — the leap from commodity to money — is the moment of truth for value. A commodity that cannot be sold has no value, no matter how much socially necessary labour-time it embodies. This is a crucial qualification of the labour theory of value: value is realized only through exchange. For the theory of crisis : The chapter establishes that crisis is not an accident of capitalism but a necessary possibility inherent in commodity circulation. The monetary crisis — where \"money suddenly and immediately changes over from its merely nominal shape, money of account, into hard cash\" — is the most dramatic manifestation of the contradiction between commodities and their value-form. This analysis would be developed in Volume Three's theory of crisis and in the Grundrisse 's analysis of the credit system. For the theory of the state : The chapter shows that the state plays a crucial role in monetary circulation — coining, setting the standard of price, giving paper money \"forced currency.\" Yet the state's power is limited: \"The state's compulsion can only be of any effect within that internal sphere of circulation which is circumscribed by the boundaries of a given community.\" On the world market, state fiat is helpless; only gold and silver count. For the theory of world economy : The section on world money opens the door to the analysis of international trade, exchange rates, and the global hierarchy of production. The \"twofold motion\" of gold and silver — from sources to circulation, and back and forth between nations — is the material basis of the international monetary system. For the critique of utopian socialism : Marx's critique of Owen's \"labour money\" is devastating: it presupposes \"directly socialized labour, a form of production diametrically opposed to the production of commodities.\" To abolish money without abolishing commodity production is to attempt the impossible. The lesson is clear: monetary reform cannot solve the problems of capitalism; only the abolition of commodity production can. Practical Implications For the contemporary reader, Chapter 3 offers several enduring insights: Money is not neutral. It is not a lubricant that facilitates exchange but a social relation that shapes production and distribution. Crises are inherent in market economies. The separation of sale and purchase creates the formal possibility of crisis; credit and the division of labour develop this possibility into reality. The quantity theory of money is wrong. Inflation is not simply \"too much money chasing too few goods\"; it depends on the values of commodities and the value of money, which are determined by labour-time. Paper money depends on gold. Even in a system of inconvertible paper money, the symbol must represent a quantity of gold determined by the \"immanent laws\" of circulation. If it exceeds this quantity, it depreciates. The world market is the arena of real money. National currencies are local representatives; on the world market, only the \"genuine money-commodity\" counts. Conclusion Chapter 3 of Capital is a masterpiece of dialectical analysis. It derives the entire system of monetary functions from the contradictions of the commodity-form, demonstrates the possibility of crisis inherent in the separation of sale and purchase, and lays the foundation for the analysis of capital, credit, and the world market. Its central thesis — that money is the necessary form of appearance of the value-relation — remains one of the most powerful arguments against both the crude materialism that sees money as just a thing and the crude idealism that sees it as just a convention. Money is a social relation, and the contradictions of that relation are the contradictions of the society that produces it. The chapter ends with the image of hoards accumulating in the strong rooms of banks, \"strikingly above their average level\" — an indication of \"stagnation in the circulation of commodities, i.e. of an interruption in the flow of their metamorphoses.\" This image — the frozen gold, the interrupted circulation, the crisis in waiting — is the perfect conclusion to a chapter that has shown how the free flow of exchange contains, within itself, the necessity of its own interruption. The next chapter will show how money, accumulated in hoards, becomes capital — and how circulation, interrupted, resumes on a new and more dangerous basis. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 2 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 4"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 4", "title": "Chapter 4", "subtitle": "The General Formula for Capital", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 4/Chapter 4  The General Formula for Capital_explanation.html", "word_count": 5519, "read_time": "28 min read", "tags": ["Circuits of Capital (M-C-M')", "Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["General Formula for Capital (M-C-M')", "Self-Valorizing Value", "Differences between Commodity Circulation and Capital", "Quantitative Infinity of Valorization", "Movement of Merchant and Interest Capital"], "text": "Chapter 4 The General Formula For Capital - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,435 words ⏱️ ~27 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 4 of Capital, Volume I , entitled \"The General Formula for Capital,\" constitutes the opening salvo of Part Two, \"The Transformation of Money into Capital.\" Its central thesis is deceptively simple but philosophically immense: capital is not a thing but a movement . Specifically, capital is the movement of value through the circulatory form M–C–M′ , in which money (M) is advanced to purchase a commodity (C), which is then sold for more money (M′), the increment being surplus-value . The chapter's intellectual objective is to isolate, with surgical precision, the purely formal difference between the circulation of money as mere money (C–M–C) and the circulation of money as capital (M–C–M), and to demonstrate that this formal difference conceals a difference in content — namely, that C–M–C terminates in consumption (use-value) whereas M–C–M terminates in exchange-value itself and is therefore, by its very form, boundless . Marx's problem is situated at a critical hinge in his argument. Having analyzed in Part One the commodity and money in their \"simple\" forms, he must now cross the threshold into the analysis of capital . He does so by asking a deceptively ordinary question: What distinguishes money that is simply money from money that becomes capital? His answer — that the distinction lies in the form of circulation — provides the scaffolding for the rest of the volume. The chapter thus functions as a formal deduction of capital's general formula , which will in Chapter 5 reveal a contradiction (the famous contradiction of the general formula ) resolvable only by the discovery of the peculiar commodity labour-power , treated in Chapter 6. Everything in Marx's theory of surplus-value — exploitation, the working day, the labor process, accumulation — is anticipated here in embryo. The chapter's ambition is therefore double. First, it is a phenomenology of capital , tracing capital to its \"first form of appearance\" in money. Second, and more profoundly, it is a Hegelian-flavored ontology of value , in which value is progressively revealed not as a passive relation but as an \"automatic subject\" that \"puts itself into circulation,\" \"preserves and multiplies itself,\" and \"throws off surplus-value from itself\" — a self-moving substance. This is where Marx's critique of political economy becomes, in the fullest sense, a critique of the categories of bourgeois thought . The reader must therefore hold two registers simultaneously: the technical-economic (the difference between the two circulatory forms) and the philosophical (the constitution of value as subject). The chapter's dramatic arc moves from empirical observation (merchants and peasants buying and selling) to logical deduction (the formal distinction) to speculative confrontation (the God-Father/God-Son metaphor) — and closes by reclaiming the general formula as the true form not of merchants' capital alone but of all capital, including industrial and interest-bearing capital. Its final sentence is its thesis: \"M–C–M′ is in fact therefore the general formula for capital, in the form in which it appears directly in the sphere of circulation.\" 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS I. The Historical Presupposition and the Everyday Appearance of Capital Marx opens with a genealogical claim that doubles as a logical one: \"The circulation of commodities is the starting-point of capital.\" Two historical conditions must be met before capital can arise — the production of commodities and their circulation \"in its developed form, namely trade.\" Both crystallize historically in the sixteenth century, when world trade and the world market emerge and \"the modern history of capital starts to unfold.\" This is not ornamental history. It establishes that capital is neither a natural given nor a trans-historical category, but a specific historical formation whose presuppositions were themselves the product of a prior development — the enclosure, colonization, and long-distance commerce that forged a global market. The methodological pivot follows immediately. If we \"disregard the material content of the circulation of commodities\" (i.e., the exchange of use-values) and consider only \"the economic forms brought into being by this process,\" we find that its ultimate product is money . Money is thus both the empirical residue of commodity circulation and the \"first form of appearance of capital.\" Marx distinguishes two registers here. Historically , capital \"invariably first confronts landed property in the form of money — monetary wealth, merchants' capital and usurers' capital.\" In footnote 1 he sharpens this with two French proverbs: \"Nulle terre sans seigneur\" (\"No land without its lord\") and \"L'argent n'a pas de maître\" (\"Money has no master\"). The first expresses landed property's power as personal domination and servitude ; the second money's power as impersonal, masterless power . The historical rise of capital is thus the victory of the abstract, impersonal power of money over the personal, concrete power of the land — a theme that will recur throughout Marx's historical work. Logically and synchronically , however, \"we do not need to look back at the history of capital's origins.\" Every day new capital \"steps onto the stage\" as money, whether in the commodity market, the labour market, or the money market, \"money which has to be transformed into capital by definite processes.\" This \"definite processes\" is the entire business of the book. II. The Formal Antithesis: C–M–C Contrasted with M–C–M The first distinction between money as money and money as capital, Marx insists, \"is nothing more than a difference in their form of circulation.\" He then names the two forms: C–M–C: the \"direct form of the circulation of commodities\" — commodity into money, money back into commodity; selling in order to buy. M–C–M: the \"quite distinct\" second form — money into commodity, commodity back into money; buying in order to sell. Money that describes this second course is \"transformed into capital, becomes capital, and, from the point of view of its function, already is capital.\" This is the chapter's first theorem: capital is defined formally, by the shape of its circuit . Marx then anatomizes M–C–M. It \"passes through two antithetical phases\": the purchase M–C (money into commodity) and the sale C–M (commodity back into money). Taken as a unity, the movement exchanges money for a commodity and the same commodity for money — or, \"if one neglects the formal distinction between buying and selling, buys a commodity with money and then buys money with a commodity.\" (Here he cites footnote 2, Mercier de la Rivière: \"With money one buys commodities, and with commodities one buys money.\") The result, \"in which the whole process vanishes,\" is the exchange of money for money, M–M . His concrete illustration is decisive: purchase 2,000 lb. of cotton for £100, resell for £110 — and you have \"in fact exchanged £100 for £110, money for money.\" III. The Problem of Absurdity and the Anatomy of Reflux Having stated the form, Marx confronts its apparent absurdity. \"It is evident that the circulatory process M–C–M would be absurd and empty if the intention were... to exchange two equal sums of money, £100 for £100.\" The miser would have been wiser to keep the £100 rather than expose it to \"the dangers of circulation.\" Yet Marx refuses to reduce M–C–M to mere folly: whether the merchant sells at £110, £100, or even £50, \"his money has at all events described a characteristic and original path, quite different in kind from the path of simple circulation\" — for example, the peasant who sells corn and buys clothes. So Marx returns to the formal comparison to uncover the content hidden behind it. He first establishes what the two forms have in common . Both divide into the same two antithetical phases, C–M (sale) and M–C (purchase) . In each phase the same material elements confront each other — a commodity and money — and the same economic dramatis personae : a buyer and a seller. Each circuit is \"the unity of the same two antithetical phases,\" mediated through three contract participants — one who only sells, one who only buys, and one who both buys and sells. The differences then emerge with precision: Inverted order of succession. C–M–C begins with a sale and ends with a purchase; M–C–M begins with a purchase and ends with a sale. Inverted extremes and mediation. In C–M–C both starting and terminating points are commodities ; the process is mediated by money . In M–C–M both extremes are money ; the process is mediated by a commodity . Spent vs. advanced. In C–M–C, money is \"in the end converted into a commodity which serves as a use-value; it has therefore been spent once and for all.\" In M–C–M, the buyer \"lays out money in order that, as a seller, he may recover money.\" He releases money \"only with the cunning intention of getting it back again.\" The money \"is not spent, it is merely advanced .\" Marx anchors this conceptual distinction in footnote 3 (James Steuart): \"When a thing is bought in order to be sold again, the sum employed is called money advanced; when it is bought not to be sold, it may be said to be expended.\" The twofold displacement and the reflux. In C–M–C \"the same piece of money is displaced twice\" — received from one buyer, paid to another — effecting \"its definitive transfer from one hand into another.\" In M–C–M \"it is not the piece of money which is displaced twice, but the commodity\": the buyer takes it from the seller and passes it to another buyer, and \"the twofold displacement of the same commodity causes the money to flow back to its initial point of departure.\" This reflux of money to its starting point is the \"palpable difference\" between money circulating as capital and money circulating as mere money. Crucially, \"this reflux does not depend on the commodity's being sold for more than was paid for it. That only has a bearing on the amount of money which flows back.\" Reflux occurs as soon as the commodity is resold , i.e., as soon as the cycle M–C–M completes — regardless of profit or loss. The contrast is then sharpened against C–M–C. In simple circulation, money is \"irreversibly spent\" — if I sell a quarter of corn for £3 and buy clothes, the money \"belongs to the clothes merchant.\" If I sell a second quarter, money flows back to me, \"not however as a result of the first transaction, but of its repetition.\" So in C–M–C \"the expenditure of money has nothing to do with its reflux,\" whereas in M–C–M \"the reflux of the money is conditioned by the very manner in which it is expended. Without this reflux, the operation fails, or the process is interrupted and incomplete.\" IV. Teleological Inversion: Use-Value vs. Exchange-Value as the Motion's Final Cause Having established the mechanical difference (reflux), Marx now reveals the difference in purpose . C–M–C \"proceeds from the extreme constituted by one commodity, and ends with the extreme constituted by another, which falls out of circulation and into consumption.\" Its final goal is therefore \"Consumption, the satisfaction of needs, in short use-value.\" M–C–M \"proceeds from the extreme of money and finally returns to that same extreme. Its driving and motivating force, its determining purpose, is therefore exchange-value .\" This telescoping of purpose out of form is one of the chapter's most elegant moves. In C–M–C the two extremes \"have the same economic form\" — they are both commodities of equal value — but they are \"qualitatively different use-values\" (corn and clothes). The content of the movement is \"the exchange of products, the interchange carried out between the different materials in which social labour is embodied\" — that is, the material metabolism of society , the distribution of concrete labor's products. Here Marx shows he is not, as vulgar critics claimed, indifferent to use-value; the use-value interchange is the entire point of C–M–C. M–C–M is otherwise. \"At first sight this appears to lack any content, because it is tautological.\" Both extremes are money — \"the converted form of commodities, in which their particular use-values have been extinguished.\" To exchange £100 for cotton and then the same cotton for £100 is \"merely a roundabout way of exchanging money for money, the same for the same.\" Because both extremes are money, they cannot differ qualitatively. \"One sum of money is distinguishable from another only by its amount.\" Therefore, \"the process M–C–M does not... owe its content to any qualitative difference between its extremes... but solely to quantitative changes. More money is finally withdrawn from circulation than was thrown into it at the beginning.\" The full form is therefore M–C–M′, where M′ = M + ΔM — \"the original sum advanced plus an increment.\" And here Marx introduces, for the first time in the work, the category that will rule the rest of Capital : \"This increment or excess over the original value I call 'surplus-value.'\" (The translator's footnote flags that both Mehrwert and Verwertung are introduced at this juncture; English lacks a word for Verwertung adequate to Marx's meaning, hence \"valorization.\") Worth noting is the irony in footnote 4, where Marx assembles the political economists against themselves. Mercier de la Rivière exclaims to the Mercantilists, \"One does not exchange money for money.\" Corbet ( An Inquiry into the Causes and Modes of the Wealth of Individuals , 1841) insists \"trade is advantageously contrasted with gambling, which consists in a mere exchange of money for money\" — yet Corbet concedes that M–M \"is the characteristic form of circulation, not only of merchants' capital, but of all capital.\" Then \"MacCulloch comes on the scene, and asserts that to buy in order to sell is to speculate, and thus the distinction between speculation and trade vanishes.\" Pinto, \"the Pindar of the Amsterdam Stock Exchange,\" remarks: \"Trade is a game... If one won everything from everybody for long, it would be necessary to give back voluntarily the greater part of the profit in order to begin the game again.\" Marx's real target here is the apologetic tendency to dissolve the formal specificity of capital — the very move that the chapter reverses. V. Valorization, the Boundlessness of Capital, and the Aristotelian Precedent With surplus-value on the table, Marx draws out its consequences for the whole process: \"The value originally advanced, therefore, not only remains intact while in circulation, but increases its magnitude, adds to itself a surplus-value, or is valorized [ verwertet sich ]. And this movement converts it into capital.\" The word \" verwertet sich \" — valorizes itself — is reflexive: the value doesn't merely receive an increment, it does something to itself. This is the pivot on which Marx's idealist-sounding ontology of value turns. Marx takes care to confront a potential counterexample. Could not C–M–C also involve quantitative inequality — the peasant sells corn above value, or buys clothes below value, or is cheated by the merchant? \"Yet, for this particular form of circulation, such differences in value are purely accidental.\" Equivalence is \"rather a necessary condition of its normal course\" — the exchange of use-values is intelligible even when equivalents are exchanged. But in M–C–M, \"The fact that the corn and the clothes are equivalents does not deprive the process of all sense and meaning, as it does in M–C–M\" — this sentence is surely a typographic slip in the text for C–M–C at the end; what Marx means is that equivalence destroys the meaning of M–C–M (as it does not destroy that of C–M–C). The distinction therefore lies not in the empirical presence or absence of unequal exchange but in the logical requirement of the form itself . The consequence is the famous limitlessness of capital . The repetition of C–M–C \"finds its measure and its goal... in a final purpose which lies outside it, namely consumption, the satisfaction of definite needs.\" But in M–C–M \"the end and the beginning are the same, money or exchange-value, and this very fact makes the movement an endless one.\" £100 becomes £110 — but £110, qualitatively, \"is the same as £110, namely money\"; quantitatively, \"£110 is, like £100, a sum of definite and limited value.\" If £110 \"is now spent as money, it ceases to play its part. It is no longer capital. Withdrawn from circulation, it is petrified into a hoard\" — and could stay so until the Last Judgment without \"a single farthing accruing to it.\" Both £100 and £110 \"have the same vocation, to approach, by quantitative increase, as near as possible to absolute wealth.\" The momentary distinction between original capital (£100) and profit (£10) \"vanishes immediately\" at the end of the process: what emerges is a value of £110, \"in exactly the same form... as the original £100.\" Engels' Umrisse zu einer Kritik der Nationalökonomie (1844) is cited in footnote 5 on this point — that capital divides into original capital and profit \"although in practice profit is immediately lumped together with capital and set into motion with it.\" The logical result follows: \"The simple circulation of commodities — selling in order to buy — is a means to a final goal which lies outside circulation, namely the appropriation of use-values... As against this, the circulation of money as capital is an end in itself, for the valorization of value takes place only within this constantly renewed movement. The movement of capital is therefore limitless.\" Marx localizes this insight in the Western philosophical tradition through the extended footnote 6 on Aristotle . Aristotle distinguishes economics (household management, aimed at procuring the articles necessary to existence, whose wealth is limited by use) from chrematistics (the art of acquiring wealth, aimed at money as such, with no limit). Aristotle writes that \"there appear to be no limits to riches and property\" in chrematistics, because \"circulation is the source of riches,\" \"money is the beginning and the end of this kind of exchange,\" and \"just as every art which is not a means to an end, but an end in itself, has no limit to its aims... so with chrematistics there are no bounds to its aims, these aims being absolute wealth.\" The editor's note to the translation adds that Marx \"quotes so as to bring out the meaning relevant to his argument\" — turning Aristotle's \"gaining wealth through exchange\" into \"circulation,\" \"the art of household management\" into \"economics,\" and \"the art of getting wealth\" into \"chrematistics.\" Marx is not merely citing Aristotle; he is making Aristotle say the structural distinction he himself has just derived formally. There is a deeper point: Aristotle had glimpsed the form of unbounded acquisitiveness, but could not explain it, because he was confined to a society in which the use of money as capital was subordinate to household economy. Only with capitalism does the sheer form of chrematistics become the dominant, universalizing form of wealth. Footnote 10 underscores the family resemblance of hoarding and capitalism through language: the Greek σωζειν (\"to save\") is \"a characteristic Greek expression for hoarding,\" and English \"to save\" means both to rescue and to spare . The miser and the capitalist inhabit the same linguistic universe. VI. The Capitalist as Bearer: Personification of the Movement Marx now shifts registers from the form of movement to the human bearer of that form . \"As the conscious bearer [ Träger ] of this movement, the possessor of money becomes a capitalist.\" This is the chapter's celebrated doctrine of personification . The capitalist is not an autonomous agent who happens to pursue profit; rather, \"the objective content of the circulation... the valorization of value — is his subjective purpose, and it is only in so far as the appropriation of ever more wealth in the abstract is the sole driving force behind his operations that he functions as a capitalist, i.e. as capital personified and endowed with consciousness and a will.\" The capitalist is thus the mask capital wears; his will is a function of the movement. From this follows the rationale for capital's cultural forms. \"Use-values must therefore never be treated as the immediate aim of the capitalist; nor must the profit on any single transaction. His aim is rather the unceasing movement of profit-making.\" Marx cites footnote 7 (Chalmers: \"Commodities are not the terminating object of the trading capitalist, money is his terminating object\") and footnote 8 (Genovesi: \"Though the merchant does not count the profit he has just made as nothing, he nevertheless always has his eye on his future profit\"). The distinction between capitalist and miser is then famously cast: \"This boundless drive for enrichment, this passionate chase after value, is common to the capitalist and the miser; but while the miser is merely a capitalist gone mad, the capitalist is a rational miser.\" The miser \"seeks to attain by saving his money from circulation\" what the capitalist achieves \"by means of throwing his money again and again into circulation\" — hence the citation of Galiani (footnote 11): \"Things possess an infinite quality when moving in a circle which they lack when advancing in a straight line.\" In footnote 9, Marx inserts a barbed aside about MacCulloch, who invokes the auri sacra fames (\"accursed hunger for gold\") to explain the capitalist's drive — but who, \"when in theoretical difficulties, as for example in the treatment of over-production,\" transforms that same capitalist \"into a good citizen, whose sole concern is for use-values, and who even develops an insatiable hunger for boots, hats, eggs, calico and other extremely common kinds of use-value.\" The point is methodological: apologetic economics oscillates between a capital driven by boundless value-greed (in good times) and a capital motivated by consumption needs (when defending the system against crises). The philosophical culmination now comes: In M–C–M both money and commodity function \"only as different modes of existence of value itself, the money as its general mode of existence, the commodity as its particular or, so to speak, disguised mode.\" Value perpetually \"chang[es] from one form into the other, without becoming lost in this movement; it thus becomes transformed into an automatic subject .\" Hence the famous proposition: \"capital is money, capital is commodities.\" This is not a mere tautology but the expression of a deeper structure. \"In truth, however, value is here the subject of a process in which, while constantly assuming the form in turn of money and commodities, it changes its own magnitude, throws off surplus-value from itself considered as original value, and thus valorizes itself independently.\" The word \"itself\" is doing real philosophical work: \"its valorization is therefore self-valorization [ Selbstverwertung ]. By virtue of being value, it has acquired the occult ability to add value to itself. It brings forth living offspring, or at least lays golden eggs.\" The irony of \"occult ability\" and \"golden eggs\" is the mark of Marx's critique: this self-generating power belongs, for political economy, to value as such; Marx's project will be to explain how this appears to be so by explaining the labor process that secretly underlies it. (Footnote 12 cites J.B. Say — \"It is not the material which forms capital, but the value of that material\" — and footnote 13 cites Macleod and James Mill.) Value is then named \"the dominant subject [ übergreifendes Subjekt ]\" of the process. Because it \"alternately assumes and loses the form of money and the form of commodities, but preserves and expands itself through all these changes,\" it requires \"an independent form by means of which its identity with itself may be asserted. Only in the shape of money does it possess this form. Money therefore forms the starting-point and the conclusion of every valorization process.\" Money is thus not merely the beginning but the logical home of capital — the form in which value can recognize itself as the same across its metamorphoses. The chapter reaches its speculative climax in the famous theological analogy . Value \"enters into a private relationship with itself.\" It \"differentiates itself as original value from itself as surplus-value, just as God the Father differentiates himself from himself as God the Son, although both are of the same age and form, in fact one single person; for only by the surplus-value of £10 does the £100 originally advanced become capital, and as soon as this has happened, as soon as the son has been created and, through the son, the father, their difference vanishes again, and both become one, £110.\" This is a deliberate parodic appropriation of the Hegelian logic of the Trinity (as read by Feuerbach and, in Marx's day, by the young Hegelians): the metaphysical self-differentiation of a subject that remains identical with itself through its \"other.\" Marx is making two points: (1) the structure of value is speculative — it constitutes itself by producing its own other; (2) this constitution is fantastic , i.e., a form of appearance whose real ground lies elsewhere. The subsequent volume of Capital will unmask the \"golden eggs\" as the product of exploited labor-power. This chapter deliberately stops at the surface . In footnote 14, Sismondi's \"capital... permanent self-multiplying value\" is cited as an expression of this same truth from a different direction. VII. The General Formula Applied: Mercantile, Industrial, and Interest-Bearing Capital The chapter now generalizes. \"Value therefore now becomes value in process, money in process, and, as such, capital.\" Its rhythm: \"It comes out of circulation, enters into it again, preserves and multiplies itself within circulation, emerges from it with an increased size, and starts the same cycle again and again.\" The Mercantilists, \"its first interpreters,\" described this as \"M–M, 'money which begets money.'\" Marx then defends the generality of the formula. \"Buying in order to sell, or, more accurately, buying in order to sell dearer, M–C–M, seems admittedly to be a form peculiar to one kind of capital alone, merchants' capital.\" But, he insists, \"industrial capital too is money which has been changed into commodities, and re-converted into more money by the sale of these commodities. Events which take place outside the sphere of circulation, in the interval between buying and selling, do not affect the form of this movement. \" This parenthesis is decisive, and it is the hinge on which the coming chapters turn. It means that the form M–C–M′ is sufficient to define capital as capital, even though the actual production of surplus-value occurs in the interval — in production — not in exchange. Marx is preparing the move: the formula is universally correct as a form , but internally self-contradictory if we try to explain surplus-value from within circulation alone. Finally, \"in the case of interest-bearing capital , the circulation M–C–M′ presents itself in abridged form, in its final result and without any intermediate stage, in a concise style, so to speak, as M–M′ — i.e. money which is worth more money, value which is greater than itself.\" This is the most \"mystified\" and shortest rendering of capital's formula, the form Marx will later call the \"mother of all insane forms.\" Here it serves to demonstrate the universality of the formula precisely by showing its most compressed, least transparent embodiment. The chapter's final sentence performs the closure: \"M–C–M′ is in fact therefore the general formula for capital, in the form in which it appears directly in the sphere of circulation.\" The qualifier — \"in the form in which it appears directly in the sphere of circulation\" — is a promissory note. What appears on the surface as self-valorizing value must still be explained ; the surface is not yet the substance. A textual note demands attention: on page 12 of the chapter, Marx writes that the capitalist knows all commodities are \"in faith and in truth money, are by nature circumcised Jews , and, what is more, a wonderful means for making still more money out of money.\" The phrase is one of the notorious antisemitic figures in Marx's text — historically significant as evidence of the antisemitic idiom inherited by nineteenth-century German socialism, and one Marx deploys as a metaphor for the convertibility of commodities into money . It should be read both as an analysis of a rhetorical figure and as a mark of the ideological milieu in which the book was written; its theoretical content is that, for capital, all commodities are functions of money, \"money in disguise.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Three fundamental principles emerge from this chapter. First, capital is defined formally, by its circulation. Marx's breakthrough is to distinguish two circuits that superficially involve the same phases (buying, selling, money, commodity) but have inverted orders and opposite purposes . C–M–C terminates in use-value and is therefore bounded by consumption. M–C–M′ terminates in exchange-value itself and is therefore, by its logical form, limitless . Once this formal distinction is grasped, the moralizing debates about greed, luxury, or usury become secondary — the boundlessness is built into the form , not the character , of the agent. This is what Marx means by \"capital personified\": the capitalist is the historical bearer of a structural logic he does not invent. Second, the chapter makes surplus-value a formally necessary category. Once circulation is understood as M–C–M′, the name for ΔM — surplus-value — forces itself upon us. The very form of capital contains, as an internal demand, the production of an increment. From this follows the self-valorization ( Selbstverwertung ) of value, the \"automatic subject,\" and the Trinity of Father, Son, and £110. Marx takes seriously but criticizes the surface appearance that value begets value \"as naturally as trees bear fruit.\" The rest of Capital will be the systematic demonstration that this apparently self-grounded productivity rests on the exploitation of living labor — the substance that alone can create more value than it costs. Third, the chapter initiates the discovery of the contradictions of the general formula. Because Marx explicitly brackets production (\"events... outside the sphere of circulation... do not affect the form of this movement\"), he has isolated a form that cannot yet be explained by its own terms. This is the pivot of Chapter 5: if equivalents exchange, how is surplus-value possible? The answer lies outside circulation but only via a commodity whose use-value is the source of value — labor-power. The elegant analytic economy of Chapter 4 is thus a deliberate aporia that structures the entire argument. Theoretical and philosophical implications are enormous. Methodologically, Marx demonstrates the power of form analysis : the difference between two arrows in an equation (C–M–C vs. M–C–M) is used to generate a theory of surplus-value, of the historical specificity of capitalism, of the capitalist's subjective motive, and of the surface forms of credit and interest. His method is neither empirical induction from isolated facts nor abstract deduction from axioms, but a dialectical exposition of forms in which each form reveals its own immanent contradictions and drives toward its successor. Historically and politically, the identification of capital with boundless self-valorization connects to Aristotle's chrematistics and to the auri sacra fames of the miser. Where the ancients saw a limit imposed by household need, and where moralists decried the excess of greed, Marx sees a structural unlimitedness that no moral restraint can tame. This has profound implications: capital cannot be reformed by making capitalists more moderate, because moderation contradicts capital's concept. The very form of the movement is a demand for infinite expansion — a category no longer available to pre-capitalist societies. Philosophically, the chapter stages a critique of the categories of political economy as self-mystifying . The value that \"brings forth living offspring\" and \"enters into a private relationship with itself\" is the appearance generated by a social relation (the wage relation) that the economists treat as a natural property of things. The \"occult ability\" of value is the fetishism of Part One re-emerging at the level of capital. The theological analogy — Father/Son, one single person — is not merely rhetorical ornament: it is Marx's diagnosis that the logic of capital is structurally speculative, a self-positing subject whose apparent self-sufficiency conceals its dependence on living labor. The chapter thus secures the hermeneutic key to the whole of Capital : to read the surface of economic forms as rigorous self-misrecognition, whose correction must be effected by theoretical work penetrating from the self-moving value of circulation to the exploited labor of production. Finally, the practical implication is stark. Because capital is defined by M–C–M′, every institutional form of capital — merchant, industrial, financial — is a permutation of the same formula. The \"abridged form M–M′\" of interest-bearing capital, far from being a mere abbreviation, is capital's most confessional moment, laying bare in a single stroke the pretense of productivity without production. Marx's chapter therefore not only frames Part Two but supplies the vocabulary — capital, surplus-value, valorization, personification, the automatic subject — through which the entire anatomy of bourgeois society will be dissected. Its thesis sentence, \"M–C–M′ is in fact therefore the general formula for capital, in the form in which it appears directly in the sphere of circulation,\" is not just a summary but a commission : we are only at the surface — the depths remain to be sounded. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 3 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 5"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 5", "title": "Chapter 5", "subtitle": "Contradictions in the General Formula", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 5/Chapter 5  Contradictions in the General Formula_explanation.html", "word_count": 5076, "read_time": "25 min read", "tags": ["Circuits of Capital (M-C-M')", "Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["Contradictions of the General Formula", "Critique of Equal Exchange Producing Surplus-Value", "Critique of Unequal Exchange / Cheating", "Origin of Surplus-Value: Inside and Outside Circulation", "Commodity Circulation as Zero-Sum Valorization"], "text": "Chapter 5 Contradictions In The General Formula - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,058 words ⏱️ ~25 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS This chapter — Chapter 5 of Capital , Volume I, titled \"Contradictions in the General Formula\" — is the analytic pivot of Part II, \"The Transformation of Money into Capital.\" Its task is purely negative and devastating: to prove that the value-form which defines capital, the circuit M–C–M′ (money buying commodities in order to sell them for more money), cannot be explained by anything that happens within circulation. The chapter subjects the general formula to a merciless thought-experiment that closes off every escape route the \"vulgar economists\" might take, and in doing so it converts a puzzle into an antinomy — a genuine contradiction that will drive the entire remaining argument of the book. The central problem is stated in the opening sentence: the circulation-form in which money becomes capital \"contradicts all the previously developed laws bearing on the nature of commodities, value, money and even circulation itself.\" The distinguishing feature of M–C–M′ is the inverted order of succession of the two antithetical acts, sale and purchase (buy first, sell second — the reverse of simple circulation C–M–C). Marx asks the foundational question of the whole chapter: \"How can this purely formal distinction change the nature of these processes, as if by magic?\" The thesis he will establish is that it cannot — no mere reordering can conjure surplus-value out of a realm whose immanent law is the exchange of equivalents. The chapter proceeds by exhaustion. Marx demonstrates, one by one, that surplus-value cannot arise from: The formal inversion of sale and purchase (which has no existence for the other parties, A and B, and collapses back into ordinary circulation); The exchange of equivalents in its \"pure form\"; Selling commodities above their value (the gain is lost again as a buyer); Buying commodities below their value (mirror-image failure); A special class of buyers-who-do-not-sell (whose money must have been given for nothing in the first place); Individual cunning or cleverness (a mere redistribution, like theft); Merchants' capital and usurers' capital (the \"antediluvian,\" derivative forms); Production outside circulation (labour creates value but cannot create self-valorizing value). The double result — the chapter's two-fold conclusion — is the celebrated antinomy: capital cannot arise from circulation, and it is equally impossible for it to arise apart from circulation. \"It must have its origin both in circulation and not in circulation.\" The money-owner \"must buy his commodities at their value, sell them at their value, and yet at the end of the process withdraw more value from circulation than he threw into it at the beginning.\" He must, and yet must not, \"emerge as a butterfly\" in the sphere of circulation. Marx closes with the challenge Hic Rhodus, hic salta! — \"Rhodes is here; leap here and now.\" Intellectual objective and fit: This chapter is the dialectical hammer-blow that shatters the illusionistic view of capital (circulation as a value-generating realm) in order to relocate the source of surplus-value to the sphere of production — specifically, to the \"background which is not visible in the circulation itself.\" It is the indispensable bridge between Part I (commodity and money) and the solution to follow: the discovery of a commodity whose use-value is itself the source of value, namely labour-power. Without Chapter 5, Chapter 6 would be arbitrary; with it, the purchase of labour-power becomes a logical necessity rather than a device. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Formal Inversion of Sale and Purchase — and Its Instant Disappearance Marx opens by isolating what appears to be the unique signature of capital: the inverted order of succession of sale and purchase. In simple commodity circulation (C–M–C) the owner sells first and buys afterward; in the capital circuit (M–C–M′) the money-owner buys first and sells afterward. The \"magic\" thesis of capital's apologists is that this purely formal reversal can transform the nature of the acts themselves — that beginning with a purchase and ending with a sale somehow generates value. Marx dismantles this immediately with a social-relational argument. The inversion \"has no existence for two of the three persons who transact business together.\" Consider three parties: the capitalist, A (from whom he buys), and B (to whom he sells). For A and B the distinction is invisible. A only knows that he sold to the capitalist; B only knows that he bought from the capitalist. The capitalist confronts A purely as a buyer (money) and B purely as a seller (commodities). Neither A nor B confronts him as \"capital or a capitalist\" — only as money or commodities. The \"series\" — the connection between the two acts — exists \"for me alone.\" Worse for the thesis: A and B, if the capitalist tried to explain his \"meritorious\" inversion, would protest that he had the order exactly backward. From A's standpoint the capitalist began with a sale (A's sale of commodities to him); from B's standpoint the capitalist concluded with a purchase (B's purchase from him). And beyond that, A and B would declare the whole circuit \"superfluous and nothing but hocus-pocus\": henceforth A would buy direct from B and B sell direct to A. The entire three-party operation would \"shrink down to a single, one-sided phase of the ordinary circulation of commodities.\" The crucial conclusion: the inversion of the order of succession does not take us outside the sphere of simple circulation. Therefore we must investigate whether simple circulation, \"by its nature,\" could permit valorization. The Test Case: Simple Exchange of Equivalents Marx reduces the analysis to its purest form: barter between two commodity-owners whose mutual debts balance on settlement day. In this model money serves only as money of account (a measuring device expressing values in prices) and never confronts commodities \"in a material shape.\" Here a sharp distinction between use-value and exchange-value does the work. With respect to use-value , both parties may genuinely gain. Marx cites Destutt de Tracy: \"Exchange is an admirable transaction by which both sides gain — always (!).\" The division-of-labor example makes this concrete: A, a wine-grower, may produce more wine in a given labour-time than B could, and B produces more corn than A could. So A gets more corn for the same exchange-value, and B more wine, than either would obtain by producing both goods himself. With respect to use-value, \"exchange is a transaction by which both sides gain.\" But \"It is otherwise with exchange-value.\" Marx deploys Mercier de la Rivière: exchanging corn worth 50 for wine worth 50 \"produces no increase of exchange-value either for the one or the other; for each of them already possessed, before the exchange, a value equal to that which he acquired.\" This is the first statement of the equivalence principle : pure exchange merely transfers equal values. Critically, introducing money as a medium of circulation — splitting the transaction into two physically distinct acts (sale, then purchase) — \"does not alter this situation.\" Why? Because the value of a commodity is expressed in its price before it enters circulation; it is therefore a precondition of circulation, not a result. The price is fixed prior to the act; circulation merely moves an already-determined magnitude. The Change of Form and the Immanence of Equivalence Marx now generalizes. Considering exchange \"in the abstract,\" i.e., disregarding everything that does not follow from the immanent laws of simple commodity circulation, all that happens (setting aside the replacement of one use-value by another) is a metamorphosis — a mere change in the form of the commodity. The same value, \"the same quantity of objectified social labour,\" remains in the owner's hands: first as his commodity, then as money, then again as commodity. \"This change of form does not imply any change in the magnitude of the value.\" Marx's decisive analogy: the transformation is no more value-changing than \"the changing of a £5 note into sovereigns, half-sovereigns and shillings.\" (A £5 note equals 5 sovereigns equals 10 half-sovereigns equals 100 shillings — same value, different form.) The conclusion is stated with the force of a theorem: \"in so far as the circulation of commodities involves a change only in the form of their values, it necessarily involves the exchange of equivalents, provided the phenomenon occurs in its purity.\" This is the lemma on which everything rests. Marx pauses to flay the vulgar economists, who \"have practically no inkling of the nature of value\" and who, \"whenever they wish to consider the phenomenon in its purity, after their fashion, assume that supply and demand are equal, i.e. that they cease to have any effect at all.\" That is — the very abstraction Marx is performing rigorously, they perform accidentally and then forget. From use-value, both may gain; from exchange-value, \"Where equality exists there is no gain\" (Galiani). Prices may diverge from values, but such divergence \"appears as an infringement of the laws governing the exchange of commodities.\" In its pure form, the exchange of commodities is an exchange of equivalents, and thus it is not a method of increasing value. The Condillac Fallacy: Substituting Use-Value for Exchange-Value Marx now names the specific intellectual error that generates the illusion of circulation-born profit: \"an inadvertent substitution, a confusion of use-value and exchange-value.\" The exemplar is Condillac, whom Marx quotes at length. Condillac's claim: \"It is not true that in an exchange of commodities we give value for value. On the contrary, each of the two contracting parties in every case gives a less for a greater value… If we really exchanged equal values, neither party could make a profit… The value of a thing consists solely in its relation to our needs.\" The trick here is sliding from \"value-in-use\" (where both parties can gain) to \"value-in-exchange\" (where they cannot). Marx's critique has two layers. First, Condillac \"confuses use-value with exchange-value.\" Second, and more devastatingly, Condillac \"in a really childish manner assumes that, in a society in which the production of commodities is well developed, each producer produces his own means of subsistence, and throws into circulation only what is superfluous.\" Le Trosne's answer is decisive: \"In a developed society absolutely nothing is superfluous.\" And Le Trosne teases Condillac's tautology: if both parties receive more for an equal amount and part with less, \"they both get the same.\" Marx notes the political stake: Condillac's error is \"frequently repeated by modern economists, especially when the point is to show that the exchange of commodities in its developed form, commerce, is productive of surplus-value.\" He cites S. P. Newman: \"Commerce… adds value to products, for the same products in the hands of consumers are worth more than in the hands of producers.\" Marx's rebuttal is elegant and cutting: \"commodities are not paid for twice over, once on account of their use-value, and a second time on account of their value.\" It is true that use-value is more serviceable to the buyer than the seller — but \"its money-form is more so to the seller. Would he sell it otherwise?\" The buyer \"converting stockings into money\" is no more an \"act of production\" than the seller is. Marx also fires a footnote broadside at Wilhelm Roscher, who had enlisted Condillac as guarantor of his own \"childish notions.\" Consequence: \"If commodities… of equal exchange-value, and consequently equivalents, are exchanged, it is plain that no one abstracts more value from circulation than he throws into it.\" Surplus-value formation does not occur. But since real processes do not occur in their pure form, Marx grants a test: let us assume an exchange of non-equivalents. The First Deception Hypothesis: Selling Above Value Marx sets up the sphere rigorously. The market is frequented only by commodity-owners; the power they exercise over each other \"is no other than the power of their commodities.\" The material variety of commodities is the driving force of exchange and makes buyers and sellers mutually dependent. Apart from this material (use-value) variety, the only distinction between commodities is the distinction between their natural form and their converted form — between commodities and money. Consequently, owners \"can be differentiated only into sellers, those who own commodities, and buyers, those who own money.\" The dramatis personae are thus exhausted by two roles. Now the first deception hypothesis: suppose an \"inexplicable privilege\" lets the seller sell above value — sell a commodity worth 100 for 110, a 10% nominal increase. The seller pockets a surplus-value of 10. But — and this is the whole argument — \"after he has sold he becomes a buyer.\" A third owner now confronts him as seller, and enjoys the same privilege. \"Our friend gained 10 as a seller only to lose it again as a buyer.\" Net result: all owners sell to each other at 10% above value, \"which is exactly the same as if they sold them at their true value.\" Marx's analogy: \"A universal and nominal price increase of this kind has the same effect as if the values of commodities had been expressed for example in silver instead of in gold.\" All money-names rise; the relations between values remain unchanged. He cites John Gray: sellers \"are not enriched… since what they gain as sellers, they precisely expend in the quality of buyers.\" The Mirror Hypothesis: Buying Below Value Marx now runs the mirror-image test: suppose the buyer has the privilege of purchasing below value. Does this differ? No — because the buyer \"was a seller before he became a buyer; he had already lost 10 per cent as a seller before he gained 10 per cent as a buyer.\" Every buyer was, before his purchase, someone who had to sell to obtain the money. Quoting Le Trosne: whoever must sell a product worth 24 livres for 18 livres will, when spending those 18 livres, receive only what 18 livres would have bought — the loss simply reasserts itself on the buying side. \"Everything remains as it was before.\" Therefore: \"The formation of surplus-value, and therefore the transformation of money into capital, can consequently be explained neither by assuming that commodities are sold above their value, nor by assuming that they are bought at less than their value.\" Marx footnotes Mercier de la Rivière to the same effect: a seller can raise his prices only if he accepts higher prices for what he buys, and a consumer pays less only if he accepts less for what he sells. Torrens, \"Effectual Demand,\" and the Parasite Consumer Class Marx anticipates a sophisticated evasion — Colonel Torrens's \"effectual demand,\" defined as the power and inclination of consumers \"to give for commodities… some greater portion of… capital than their production costs.\" Marx reduces this to its kernel: it merely disguises \"the following simple phrase: the owner of commodities possesses, as a seller, the privilege of selling too dear.\" In circulation \"producers and consumers confront each other only as buyers and sellers.\" One producer confronts another; the only difference is that one buys and one sells. Selling dear as producer and overpaying as consumer \"does not carry us a single step further\" (citing Ramsay: \"Who are the consumers?\"). The consistent upholders of the nominal-price theory are therefore forced into an absurd implication: they must assume \"a class of buyers who do not sell, i.e. a class of consumers who do not produce.\" Marx leaps ahead to anticipate this and demolish it: such a class's purchase-money \"must constantly flow into its coffers without any exchange, gratis, whether by might or by right, from the pockets of the commodity-owners themselves.\" To sell commodities above value to such a class \"is only to get back again, by swindling, a part of the money previously handed over for nothing.\" His historical illustration is the tribute economy of antiquity: the towns of Asia Minor paid a yearly money tribute to Rome; Rome then bought commodities from them, and bought them too dear; the provincials \"cheated the Romans, and in this way swindled back from their conquerors a portion of the tribute.\" Yet — and this is the punchline — \"the provincials remained the ones who had been cheated. Their goods were still paid for with their own money.\" No wealth was created; the same money simply circled back. \"That is not the way to get rich or to create surplus-value.\" The Clever Individual: The Robinsonade of the Sharp Trader Marx now considers whether his perplexity stems from treating people \"merely as personified categories, instead of as individuals.\" Perhaps individual cleverness — not an institutional privilege — is the source. Suppose A is \"clever enough to get the advantage of B and C without their being able to take their revenge.\" A sells wine worth £40 and obtains corn worth £50 — \"has converted his £40 into £50… and has transformed his commodities into capital.\" Marx examines this with arithmetical precision. Before the exchange: £40 of wine (A) + £50 of corn (B) = a total value of £90. After the exchange: still £90. \"The value in circulation has not increased by one iota; all that has changed is its distribution between A and B.\" What appears on one side as loss appears on the other as surplus — \"what appears on one side as a minus appears on the other side as a plus.\" Marx's conclusion is blunt: \"The same change would have taken place if A, without the disguise provided by the exchange, had directly stolen the £10 from B.\" The general theorem follows: \"The sum of the values in circulation can clearly not be augmented by any change in their distribution.\" Marx's illustration here employs a currency-dealer trope (\"no more than a Jew can increase the quantity of the precious metals in a country by selling a farthing from the time of Queen Anne for a guinea\") — a rhetorical flourish reflecting the period's idiom rather than any part of the logical argument, but it graphically makes his point: an advantageous trade is a redistribution of fixed value, not an augmentation of it. Therefore: \"The capitalist class of a given country, taken as a whole, cannot defraud itself.\" Marx cites Destutt de Tracy, who (ironically, as a member of the Institut) held the opposite view — that industrialists profit because \"they all sell for more than it has cost to produce. And to whom do they sell? In the first instance to one another.\" Marx has just shown this to be circular. The Two Theorems: Equivalence and Non-Equivalence Marx now states the double conclusion in its sharpest form, supported by J. B. Say (who borrowed the point, \"almost word for word,\" from the Physiocrats, just as his celebrated \"Products can only be bought with products\" reproduces Le Trosne's \"Products can only be paid for with products\"): \"If equivalents are exchanged, no surplus-value results, and if non-equivalents are exchanged, we still have no surplus-value. Circulation, or the exchange of commodities, creates no value.\" He cites Wayland: \"Exchange confers no value at all upon products.\" This is the exhaustion of the possibilities within circulation — the two logical branches (equal or unequal exchange) are both dead ends for surplus-value. Merchants' Capital and Usurers' Capital: The \"Antediluvian\" Forms This result explains why Marx has so far excluded \"the well-known and so to speak antediluvian forms\" of capital: merchants' capital and usurers' (interest-bearing) capital . His treatment here is a compressed but crucial digression. Merchants' capital is the form M–C–M′ \"at its purest\" — buying in order to sell dearer. But the entire movement takes place \"within the sphere of circulation.\" Since it is impossible, by circulation alone , to explain the transformation of money into capital, merchants' capital \"appears to be an impossibility, as long as equivalents are exchanged.\" Its only apparent escape is to be \"derived from the twofold advantage gained, over both the selling and the buying producers, by the merchant who parasitically inserts himself between them.\" Marx cites Franklin's aphorism: \"war is robbery, commerce is cheating.\" Should valorization of merchants' capital not rest on frauds practised on producers, \"a long series of intermediate steps would be necessary, which are as yet entirely absent,\" given the present assumption of simple circulation. (Opdyke's dictum — \"Under the rule of invariable equivalents commerce would be impossible\" — and Engels's note that trade's \"equivalent\" is not a true equivalent are marshalled to show that merchants' capital presupposes precisely the divergence Marx has bracketed.) Usurers' capital is worse. In merchants' capital, the two extremes (money thrown out and augmented money withdrawn) are at least mediated through a purchase and a sale. But in usurers' capital, M–C–M′ \"is reduced to the unmediated extremes M–M′ , money which is exchanged for more money, a form incompatible with the nature of money and therefore inexplicable from the standpoint of the exchange of commodities.\" Marx invokes Aristotle's Politics (c. 10): chrematistics is a double science — commerce and household economics; the latter is \"necessary and praiseworthy,\" the former \"based on circulation and with justice disapproved (for it is not based on Nature, but on mutual cheating).\" The usurer \"is most rightly hated, because money itself is the source of his gain, and is not used for the purposes for which it was invented.\" Money \"originated for the exchange of commodities, but interest makes out of money, more money. Hence its name\" — the Greek τόκος signifying both interest and offspring — \"for the offspring resembles the parent.\" Aristotle: of all modes of making a living, usury \"is the most contrary to Nature.\" Marx closes the digression with a forward-looking promise: in the course of the investigation he will show that merchants' capital and interest-bearing capital are derivative forms , and it will become clear \"why, historically, these two forms appear before the modern primary form of capital.\" This is a dense historical materialist claim — that the \"antediluvian\" forms are parasitic on and presuppose a productive base that they did not themselves create. Outside Circulation: The Impotence of Production Alone The penultimate movement turns the proposition upside down. Having shown surplus-value cannot arise from circulation — so that \"for it to be formed, something must take place in the background which is not visible in the circulation itself\" (citing Ramsay: \"Profit… is not made by exchanging. Had it not existed before, neither could it after\") — Marx asks: can surplus-value originate anywhere else than in circulation? He examines the only remaining sphere: the commodity-owner standing in relation to his own commodity, outside any contact with other owners. His relation to his own commodity \"is limited to this, that the commodity contains a quantity of his own labour which is measured according to definite social laws.\" That quantity is expressed by the magnitude of value and, reckoned in money of account, by the price — say £10. But his labour does not receive a double representation: it is not represented both in the value of the commodity and in an excess over and above that value — \"it is not represented in a price of 10 which is simultaneously a price of 11.\" The essential formula: labour creates value, but not self-valorizing value. \"The commodity-owner can create value by his labour, but he cannot create values which can valorize themselves.\" Marx's concrete illustration is the metamorphosis of leather into boots. The owner adds fresh labour to the leather, and the boots have more value than the leather did, \"because it contains a greater quantity of labour.\" But — crucially — \"the value of the leather remains what it was. It has not valorized itself, it has not annexed surplus-value during the making of the boots.\" The increased value of the boots equals exactly the labour newly added; the original value was merely transferred , not expanded. Hence: \"It is therefore impossible that, outside the sphere of circulation, a producer of commodities can, without coming into contact with other commodity-owners, valorize value, and consequently transform money or commodities into capital.\" The Double Result and the Statement of the Problem All strands now converge on the chapter's governing antinomy: \"Capital cannot therefore arise from circulation, and it is equally impossible for it to arise apart from circulation. It must have its origin both in circulation and not in circulation.\" Marx announces \"a double result.\" First, the transformation of money into capital \"has to be developed on the basis of the immanent laws of the exchange of commodities, in such a way that the starting-point is the exchange of equivalents.\" Second — the conditions of the problem in their full, contradictory formulation: \"The money-owner, who is as yet only a capitalist in larval form, must buy his commodities at their value, sell them at their value, and yet at the end of the process withdraw more value from circulation than he threw into it at the beginning. His emergence as a butterfly must, and yet must not, take place in the sphere of circulation. These are the conditions of the problem. Hic Rhodus, hie salta!\" The larva/butterfly metaphor captures the developmental logic of Part II: the money-owner begins as potential capital, and his transformation into actual capital (the butterfly) is precisely what remains to be explained. The Latin tag — from Aesop's fable of the boaster who claimed to have made an immense leap at Rhodes, challenged to \"leap here and now\" — functions simultaneously as a defiance to rival theorists (produce the explanation here and now, on these exact conditions) and, as Marx's footnote notes, an allusion to the Preface of Hegel's Philosophy of Right , where the phrase marks the philosopher's task of comprehending what is rather than what ought to be. Footnote 24 — a substantial methodological note — clarifies the precise meaning of the equivalence starting-point. It states that the formation of capital must be possible \"even though the price and the value of a commodity be the same, for it cannot be explained by referring to any divergence between price and value.\" If prices do differ from values, we must first reduce the former to the latter, disregarding the divergence as \"accidental,\" so as to observe capital-formation against the pure exchange of commodities without \"disturbing incidental circumstances.\" Marx then makes the realist case: \"The continual oscillations in prices, their rise and fall, compensate each other, cancel each other out, and carry out their own reduction to an average price which is their internal regulator.\" This average price is \"the guiding light of the merchant or the manufacturer in every undertaking of a lengthy nature.\" A rational manufacturer formulates the problem thus: How can we account for the origin of capital on the assumption that prices are regulated by the average price, i.e. ultimately by the value of the commodities? And here Marx adds a further precision — the word \"ultimately\" is necessary because \"average prices do not directly coincide with the values of commodities, as Adam Smith, Ricardo, and others believe.\" A sub-footnote reinforces this: Ricardo \"accepts Smith's confusion or identification of exchange-value with cost-price or natural price,\" based on the notion that exchange-value is formed by adding together wages, profit, and rent. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental principle — the sterility of circulation — is here established with the rigor of a mathematical theorem. The chapter's most durable lesson is the equivalence theorem : any act of exchange, considered in its pure form, transfers value without creating it, and any deviation from equivalence (over-selling or under-buying) merely redistributes an unchanged total. This is the \"no-free-lunch\" theorem of value theory, and it stands at the logical foundation of the entire critique of political economy. The systemic law it reveals is that capital is a contradictory category, not a simple or self-evident one. The antinomy \"in and not in circulation\" cannot be resolved by refinement of the circulation sphere; it can only be resolved by discovering a hidden hinge that belongs simultaneously to circulation (it is a contract, a purchase) and to production (its consumption generates value). That hinge — the commodity labour-power, whose use-value is its capacity to create more value than it costs — is the solution Marx spends the next chapter unveiling. Thus Chapter 5's negativity is generative : it forces the very discovery that constitutes the foundation of surplus-value theory. The chapter also functions as a polemical clearinghouse . It systematically invalidates the pre-Marxian and contemporary explanations of profit: the mercantilist concern with selling dear (the \"balance of trade\" logic), the Physiocratic and Condillacian conflation of use-value and exchange-value, Torrens's \"effectual demand,\" Malthus's and Chalmers's glorification of non-producing consumers, and the naive apologia of commerce as an \"act of production.\" Marx exposes a common structural flaw: each illegitimately transfers to exchange-value a property (mutual gain) that belongs only to use-value. Broader theoretical implications. First, the chapter's insistence on starting from the exchange of equivalents (rather than from price-value divergences) establishes the methodological priority of value over price and of production over exchange — the germ of the distinction between surplus-value and its phenomenal forms (profit, rent, interest) treated in Volume III. Second, the dialectic of the larva and the butterfly, and the placement of an apparently paradoxical thesis (\"must, and yet must not, take place in circulation\"), demonstrate the systematic rather than eclectic character of the analysis: contradictions are not to be evaded but carried forward until the categories that resolve them are discovered. Third, the digression on merchants' and usurers' capital embeds a historical-materialist chronology : the \"antediluvian\" parasitic forms precede the modern primary form precisely because they presuppose forces they do not generate — a claim Marx will vindicate in the historical sections of Capital and in the Grundrisse . Finally, the chapter's footnote 24, with its insistence that average prices do not directly coincide with values (against Smith and Ricardo), signals that Marx's value theory is not the classical \"cost-of-production\" theory but a theory of the social labour concealed beneath price — the theoretical seed of the transformation problem and of Marx's distinctive critique of classical political economy. The essential takeaway: Circulation is the sphere of appearance, not of origin. Whatever surplus-value may be, it is not made by the act of exchange; the question of its origin drives us, with mathematical compulsion, \"into the hidden abode of production.\" That is the lasting bequest of Chapter 5 — and its Hic Rhodus, hic salta! remains one of the most consequential challenges ever posed in economic thought. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 4 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 6"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 6", "title": "Chapter 6", "subtitle": "The Sale and Purchase of Labour-Power", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 6/Chapter 6  The Sale and Purchase of Labour-Power_explanation.html", "word_count": 6179, "read_time": "31 min read", "tags": ["Surplus-Value & Exploitation", "Wages & Value of Labour-Power"], "keywords": ["Sale and Purchase of Labour-Power", "Free Worker in the Double Sense", "Value of Labour-Power & Historical Subsistence", "The Hidden Abode of Production", "Sphere of Circulation as Eden of Innate Rights"], "text": "Chapter 6 The Sale And Purchase Of Labour-Power - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,107 words ⏱️ ~31 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 6, \"The Sale and Purchase of Labour-Power,\" is the pivotal hinge of Capital , Volume One—the chapter in which Marx resolves the paradox he has so carefully constructed across the preceding chapters of Part Two (\"The Transformation of Money into Capital\"). Its core thesis is deceptively simple: the self-expansion of value (the generation of surplus-value) cannot arise within the sphere of circulation itself, since all exchange there is exchange of equivalents; it can arise only from the consumption of a uniquely peculiar commodity whose use-value is itself a source of value. That commodity is labour-power. The chapter is therefore the answer to the riddle stated in its famous opening sentence—the riddle of where the \"change in value\" of money destined to become capital can possibly originate. The central problem the chapter confronts is this: the general formula of capital, M–C–M′, appears to contradict the laws of commodity exchange (equal values exchanged for equal values), yet the capitalist emerges from circulation with more value than he entered with. Marx's objective is to show that this apparent miracle is no violation of the law of value at all, but rather its fulfillment—provided one commodity exists whose value is determined like any other commodity's, but whose use-value is the capacity to create more value than it costs. The intellectual objective is thus to locate surplus-value's source without abandoning the principle of equivalence, and to do so by identifying the specific historical conditions under which such a commodity can appear. Within the broader theoretical framework of Capital , this chapter functions as a necessary bridge. Chapters 4 and 5 established the general formula of capital and demonstrated its internal contradictions—proving that surplus-value cannot be explained by cheating, by circulation alone, or by the mere act of exchange. Chapter 6 supplies the missing term: the commodity labour-power, examined in two respects—the social conditions of its saleability and the determination of its value . This analysis then hands off directly to Chapter 7, where capital enters \"the hidden abode of production\" and the labour process itself is dissected for the extraction of surplus-value. The chapter is thus simultaneously a conclusion (it solves the circulation riddle) and a prologue (it opens the door to production). Beyond its structural role, the chapter advances two of Marx's most consequential theoretical innovations. First, it distinguishes rigorously between labour and labour-power —a distinction Marx treats as one of his most important contributions to political economy, and one his predecessors (Ricardo, Torrens, the classical school) systematically conflated. Second, it introduces the concept of the historical and moral element in the value of labour-power, insisting that wages are not determined by mere physiological subsistence but by historically contingent standards of necessity—thereby rejecting both a crude \"iron law of wages\" and the moralizing sentimentality of bourgeois economists like Rossi. The chapter also performs a crucial ideological unmasking. Its closing pages—the famous \"Eden of the innate rights of man\"—expose the sphere of circulation as a \"very Eden\" of Freedom, Equality, Property, and Bentham, only to reveal that this paradise of free contract conceals the compulsion that drives the worker to market bearing \"his own hide.\" The chapter thus operates at three levels at once: a logical resolution of the valeur problem, a historical account of capital's preconditions, and a biting critique of bourgeois ideology that mistakes the appearance of free exchange for the reality of exploitation. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Puzzle: Where Can the Change in Value Originate? Marx opens by restating the logical impasse reached at the end of the previous chapter. The \"change in value of the money which has to be transformed into capital\" cannot occur in the money itself, because in its function as means of purchase and payment money does no more than realize (in German, realisieren ) the price of the commodity it buys—it simply equates itself to an equal value, and when it \"sticks to its own peculiar form,\" it \"petrifies into a mass of value of constant magnitude.\" This phrasing is precise: money as money is inert, value fixed in a rigid, gelid form. A footnote here cites Ricardo's Principles of Political Economy (p. 267): \"In the form of money... capital is productive of no profit.\" Marx is aligning himself with the classical insight while preparing to go beyond it. Nor can the change originate in the second act of circulation , the resale of the commodity (C–M′), for that act \"merely converts the commodity from its natural form back into its money-form.\" Selling at full value simply returns you the equivalent of what you had. So the change cannot come from the act of buying (money is just an equivalent) nor from the act of selling (again, equivalence). It must therefore reside in the commodity bought in the first act, M–C —\"but not in its value, for it is equivalents which are being exchanged, and the commodity is paid for at its full value.\" The only remaining locus is the commodity's use-value , \"i.e. in its consumption.\" This is the decisive logical maneuver: value creation must be sought not in exchange but in use, not in the realm of equivalence but in the realm of consumption. To \"extract value out of the consumption of a commodity,\" the money-owner must find on the market a commodity \"whose use-value possesses the peculiar property of being a source of value, whose actual consumption is therefore itself an objectification [ Vergegenständlichung ] of labour, hence a creation of value.\" The term Vergegenständlichung —objectification or embodiment—is loaded: it means that in consuming this commodity, labour becomes thing-like , crystallized into value. And this commodity exists: the \"capacity for labour\" ( Arbeitsvermögen ), or \"labour-power\" ( Arbeitskraft ). Defining Labour-Power Marx now supplies his formal definition: \"We mean by labour-power, or labour-capacity, the aggregate of those mental and physical capabilities existing in the physical form, the living personality, of a human being, capabilities which he sets in motion whenever he produces a use-value of any kind.\" Note the deliberate comprehensiveness— mental and physical —and the crucial anchoring in \"the living personality.\" Labour-power is not an abstraction floating free of bodies; it is inseparable from a living human being. This definitional move matters enormously for what follows, because everything about the peculiarity of this commodity—its non-transferability in a lump, its dependence on the worker's continued life, its historical-moral valuation—flows from the fact that it is embodied in a person. The First Condition: Free Proprietorship and the Time-Bound Sale For labour-power to appear on the market as a commodity , certain conditions must hold. Marx notes that \"the exchange of commodities implies no other relations of dependence than those which result from its own nature\"—i.e., pure commodity exchange, on its own terms, presupposes only buyer and seller. The first essential condition is that the possessor of labour-power must offer it for sale himself, which requires that \"he must have it at his disposal, he must be the free proprietor of his own labour-capacity, hence of his person.\" The buyer and seller thus meet \"on a footing of equality as owners of commodities,\" differing only in that one buys and one sells; \"both are therefore equal in the eyes of the law.\" But this freedom carries an internal limit. For the relation to continue —for the worker to remain a seller rather than becoming merchandise—he \"must always sell it for a limited period only.\" If he sold his labour-power \"in a lump, once and for all,\" he \"would be selling himself, converting himself from a free man into a slave, from an owner of a commodity into a commodity.\" The legal identity of the wage-worker thus stands or falls on the temporality of the contract. He \"must constantly treat his labour-power as his own property, his own commodity,\" which he can do only by placing it at the buyer's disposal \"for a definite period of time, temporarily.\" In this way he \"manages both to alienate [ veräussern ] his labour-power and to avoid renouncing his rights of ownership over it.\" The verb veräussern (to alienate) is carefully distinguished from full sale: the worker alienates the use for a time while retaining ownership of the capacity itself. Two footnotes deepen this. Footnote 2 mocks the \"nonsense\" found in classical encyclopedias claiming the ancient world had developed capital \"except for the absence of the free worker and of a system of credit,\" and criticizes Mommsen's History of Rome for committing \"one blunder after another\" on this point—Marx's signal that the free worker is not an incidental detail but the defining precondition of capital. Footnote 3 notes that legislation in various countries fixes a maximum length for labour contracts, and where free labour rules, the law regulates contract termination. It then exposes how \"slavery is hidden under the form of peonage\" in Mexico (and pre-Civil War US territories seized from Mexico, and the Danubian Principalities until Cuza's coup d'état): through advances repayable in labour handed down across generations, \"not only the individual worker, but also his family, become in fact the property of other persons.\" Juarez abolished peonage; the puppet \"Emperor\" Maximilian re-established it—aptly denounced in the US House of Representatives as a decree reintroducing slavery. The footnote closes with a substantial citation from Hegel's Philosophy of Right (§67, p. 104): one can alienate \"single products\" of one's particular skill and grant the use of one's abilities \"for a restricted period,\" but \"by alienating the whole of my time... and everything I produced, I would be making into another's property the substance of my being, my universal activity and actuality, my personality.\" This is a philosophically rich corroboration: the temporal limit is not merely legal convenience but the boundary separating personhood from thinghood. The Second Condition: The \"Doubly Free\" Worker The second essential condition completes the picture: the possessor of labour-power must be someone who \"instead of being able to sell commodities in which his labour has been objectified, must rather be compelled to offer for sale as a commodity that very labour-power which exists only in his living body.\" The logic is crisp. To sell commodities other than labour-power, one must possess means of production —\"raw materials, instruments of labour, etc.\"—for \"no boots can be made without leather.\" One must also possess means of subsistence. And crucially, one cannot live on the products of the future: \"Nobody—not even a practitioner of Zukunftsmusik —can live on the products of the future.\" The footnote glosses Zukunftsmusik literally as \"music of the future,\" i.e., \"castles in the air, or dreams which may or may not be realised.\" The joke is double-edged: the term (borrowed from Wagnerian aesthetics) ridicules any economic scheme premised on consuming as-yet-unproduced use-values. The worker, like everyone, \"must still consume every day, before and while he produces.\" And if products are produced as commodities , they must be sold before they can satisfy the producer's needs—so \"the time necessary for sale must be counted as well as the time of production.\" This is a subtle but essential point: the worker's need to eat now while his product can only be sold later is precisely the lever by which he is forced to sell labour-power rather than its products. The famous formulation follows: \"the owner of money must find the free worker available on the commodity-market; and this worker must be free in the double sense\"—(1) as a free individual he can dispose of his labour-power as his own commodity, and (2) he has \"no other commodity for sale,\" being \"rid of them, he is free of all the objects needed for the realisation [ Verwirklichung ] of his labour-power.\" This is the celebrated \"double freedom\": freedom to sell (legal emancipation, ownership of self) and freedom from (expropriation from all means of production). The term Verwirklichung (realisation/actualization) again signals that labour-power can only become actual when it is applied to objective conditions of production—conditions the worker lacks. His freedom is thus freedom from property, which in the marketplace is indistinguishable from the compulsion to sell the only thing he owns. The Historicity of the Relation: \"Nature Does Not Produce...\" Marx now addresses the question of why this free worker confronts the money-owner. He deliberately brackets it: \"a question which does not interest the owner of money, for he finds the labour-market in existence as a particular branch of the commodity-market. And for the present it interests us just as little. We confine ourselves to the fact theoretically, as he does practically.\" This methodological suspension is important—Marx is operating at the level of the appearance through which the capitalist sees the world, deferring the fuller historical treatment (the so-called \"primitive accumulation,\" which he will supply in Part Eight) to later. But he immediately undercuts any naturalization of the relation: \"nature does not produce on the one hand owners of money or commodities, and on the other hand men possessing nothing but their own labour-power.\" This relation \"has no basis in natural history, nor does it have a social basis common to all periods of human history.\" It is \"clearly the result of a past historical development, the product of many economic revolutions, of the extinction of a whole series of older formations of social production.\" Here Marx lands one of the chapter's central blows against bourgeois apologetics: the \"free worker\" is no natural category but the product of a violent, contingent history. The Historical Imprint of Economic Categories and Capital's New Epoch This historicity is then generalized. \"The economic categories already discussed similarly bear a historical imprint.\" For a product to become a commodity, it \"must cease to be produced as the immediate means of subsistence of the producer himself.\" Marx admits that a fuller investigation of when all or even most products take commodity form \"would have been foreign to the analysis of commodities\"—but the answer would be: \"only on the basis of one particular mode of production, the capitalist one.\" This is a methodological self-consciousness unusual in political economy. Commodity production and circulation can exist without dominating society—where most objects are made for the producers' own needs and are not turned into commodities, exchange-value \"by no means dominates\" the process. The appearance of products as commodities requires a developed social division of labour such that \"the separation of use-value from exchange-value, a separation which first begins with barter, has already been completed\"—but such development \"is common to many economic formations of society.\" Likewise, money's existence implies a definite stage of exchange; its various functions (equivalent, means of circulation, means of payment, hoard, world currency) \"indicate very different levels\"—yet \"a relatively feeble development of commodity circulation suffices for the creation of all these forms.\" Capital is different. \"The historical conditions of its existence are by no means given with the mere circulation of money and commodities.\" Capital \"arises only when the owner of the means of production and subsistence finds the free worker available, on the market, as the seller of his own labour-power. And this one historical pre-condition comprises a world's history. Capital, therefore, announces from the outset a new epoch in the process of social production.\" Footnote 4 makes the reciprocal point: the capitalist epoch is characterized by labour-power taking, \"in the eyes of the worker himself, the form of a commodity which is his property,\" so that his labour takes \"the form of wage-labour\"; and only from this moment does the commodity-form of labour's products become universal. The argument is dialectically rich: capital and wage-labour are mutually constituting; the free worker is the condition of capital and is produced by it. Determining the Value of Labour-Power \"Like all other commodities it has a value. How is that value determined?\" The answer follows the same principle as for every commodity: by the labour-time necessary for its production and reproduction . \"In so far as it has value, it represents no more than a definite quantity of the average social labour objectified in it.\" But labour-power is peculiar because it \"exists only as a capacity of the living individual\"—so its \"production\" presupposes the individual's existence, and \"the production of labour-power consists in his reproduction of himself or his maintenance.\" For maintenance he requires a quantity of means of subsistence; therefore \"the labour-time necessary for the production of labour-power is the same as that necessary for the production of those means of subsistence; in other words, the value of labour-power is the value of the means of subsistence necessary for the maintenance of its owner .\" Marx then refines this with the expenditure argument. Labour-power \"becomes a reality only by being expressed; it is activated only through labour.\" In activation, \"a definite quantity of human muscle, nerve, brain, etc. is expended, and these things have to be replaced. Since more is expended, more must be received.\" If the owner works today, \"tomorrow he must again be able to repeat the same process in the same conditions as regards health and strength.\" His means of subsistence \"must therefore be sufficient to maintain him in his normal state as a working individual.\" A footnote (6) illustrates the reverse with the Roman vilicus (the overseer of slaves), who received \"more meagre fare than working slaves, because his work was lighter\"—an archaic demonstration that consumption is calibrated to expenditure. The Historical and Moral Element Here Marx introduces his signature qualification—one of the most theoretically consequential claims in the chapter. Natural needs—\"food, clothing, fuel and housing\"—vary \"according to the climatic and other physical peculiarities of his country.\" But beyond that, \"the number and extent of his so-called necessary requirements, as also the manner in which they are satisfied, are themselves products of history, and depend therefore to a great extent on the level of civilisation attained by a country; in particular they depend on the conditions in which, and consequently on the habits and expectations with which, the class of free workers has been formed.\" Hence: \"In contrast, therefore, with the case of other commodities, the determination of the value of labour-power contains a historical and moral element .\" This is decisive. It means the value of labour-power is not fixed by bare physiology, but floats on a socially and historically constructed standard of \"necessity.\" A footnote (7) cites W. T. Thornton's Over-Population and Its Remedy (1846). Yet Marx guards against indeterminacy: \"Nevertheless, in a given country at a given period, the average amount of the means of subsistence necessary for the worker is a known datum\"—a determinate, measurable magnitude for any given moment. Replacement, Procreation, and Education Marx then notes that \"the owner of labour-power is mortal.\" Since capital requires the continuous transformation of money into capital, \"the seller of labour-power must perpetuate himself 'in the way that every living individual perpetuates himself, by procreation'\" (footnote 8 attributes this phrase to Petty). The labour-power withdrawn \"by wear and tear, and by death, must be continually replaced by, at the very least, an equal amount of fresh labour-power.\" Therefore the sum of means of subsistence \"must include the means necessary for the worker's replacements, i.e. his children, in order that this race of peculiar commodity-owners may perpetuate its presence on the market.\" Footnote 9 quotes Torrens (1815): the labourer's natural price is that quantity of necessaries and comforts \"which are necessary to support the labourer, and to enable him to rear such a family as may preserve, in the market, an undiminished supply of labour\"—with Marx's corrective that \"the word labour is here wrongly used for labour-power.\" Marx thus integrates the reproduction of the class into the value of labour-power, converting a seemingly individual wage question into a question of generational class maintenance. To this is added the cost of skill formation . To modify the human organism so that it gains \"skill and dexterity in a given branch of industry,\" becoming \"labour-power of a developed and specific kind,\" \"a special education or training is needed, and this in turn costs an equivalent in commodities of a greater or lesser amount.\" These education costs \"vary according to the degree of complexity of the labour-power required,\" and—while \"exceedingly small in the case of ordinary labour-power\"—\"form a part of the total value spent in producing it.\" This lays the groundwork for the later distinction between simple and complex labour (skilled labour as multiplied simple labour). The Value Formula and Numerical Illustration Marx now formalizes. The value of labour-power \"can be resolved into the value of a definite quantity of the means of subsistence,\" so it \"varies with the value of the means of subsistence, i.e. with the quantity of labour-time required to produce them.\" He then introduces a rate-of-replacement formula to handle goods of differing durability. Some commodities (food, fuel) are consumed daily; others (clothes, furniture) last longer. Let: A = total commodities required every day, B = those required every week, C = those required every quarter, and so on. Then the daily average of these commodities is: $$\\frac{365A + 52B + 4C + \\dots}{365}$$ ...where 365 = days per year, 52 = weeks per year, and 4 = quarters per year; each term weights the periodic quantity by its frequency so that the total annual requirement is averaged over the year. This formula mathematically expresses the principle that \"in whatever way the sum total of these outlays may be spread over the year, they must be covered by the average income, taking one day with another.\" Marx then supplies a concrete numerical illustration. Suppose this mass of commodities \"required for the average day contains 6 hours of social labour.\" Then \"every day half a day of average social labour is objectified in labour-power\"—i.e., half a day of labour is required for the daily production of labour-power . \"This quantity of labour forms the value of a day's labour-power.\" If half a day of average social labour is present in 3 shillings , then \"3 shillings is the price corresponding to the value of a day's labour-power.\" If the owner offers it at 3 shillings a day, \"its selling price is equal to its value,\" and the money-owner intent on turning 3 shillings into capital \"pays this value.\" The example is deliberately stripped down yet decisive: the value paid for a day's labour-power is half a day , while the use of that labour-power will yield a whole day . That gap—between the value of labour-power and the value it can create—is the secret of surplus-value, though Marx does not yet name it here; the example both honors the equivalence principle and sets the trap for capital. The Minimum Limit and the \"Crippled\" Condition \"The ultimate or minimum limit of the value of labour-power is formed by the value of the commodities which have to be supplied every day to the bearer of labour-power, the man, so that he can renew his life-process\"—i.e., \"the value of the physically indispensable means of subsistence.\" But crucially, if the price of labour-power \"falls to this minimum, it falls below its value, since under such circumstances it can be maintained and developed only in a crippled state, and the value of every commodity is determined by the labour-time required to provide it in its normal quality .\" This is a vital distinction often misread. The physiological minimum is not the value of labour-power; it is the floor below value . Value is defined by the cost of reproducing the worker in his normal condition—which, given the historical-moral element, is typically above bare survival. Any wage at the bare minimum represents the sale of labour-power below its value, with the \"crippling\" of the worker's capacity as the consequence. Polemical Interlude: Rossi, the Digestion Analogy, and Sismondi Marx pauses to attack what he calls \"an extraordinarily cheap kind of sentimentality\" that declares this method of valuation \"brutal.\" He targets Rossi, who argued that to conceive \"capacity for labour ( puissance de travail ) in abstraction from the workers' means of subsistence during the production process is to conceive a phantom ( être de raison )\"—insisting that \"when we speak of labour, or capacity for labour, we speak at the same time of the worker and his means of subsistence, of the worker and his wages.\" Marx's rebuttal is the celebrated digestion analogy : \"When we speak of capacity for labour, we do not speak of labour, any more than we speak of digestion when we speak of capacity for digestion. As is well known, the latter process requires something more than a good stomach.\" The point is analytical: labour-power is a capacity ; the means of subsistence are the conditions that maintain that capacity. To conflate the capacity with its material prerequisites is exactly the error that makes surplus-value invisible—by smuggling the worker's consumption into the definition of what is sold, Rossi obscures the distinction between the value of labour-power and the value labour-power creates . Marx adds that the value of the means of subsistence is expressed in the value of labour-power, not abstracted away. He then turns the tables affectively: if the capacity for labour \"remains unsold, this is of no advantage to the worker\"—the worker experiences it as \"a cruel nature-imposed necessity\" that his capacity required a definite quantity of means of subsistence. He will \"like Sismondi\" discover that \"the capacity for labour... is nothing unless it is sold.\" The citation of Sismondi (footnote 11) reinforces the pathos: the worker whose labour-power is unsold is destitute; his \"property\" is worthless unless it can be alienated. The Peculiar Time-Structure: Money as Means of Payment Marx then identifies a distinctive temporal feature of this commodity. Labour-power \"does not in reality pass straight away into the hands of the buyer on the conclusion of the contract.\" Its value , like all values, \"is already determined before it enters into circulation\"—a definite quantity of social labour has been spent on producing it. But its use-value \"consists in the subsequent exercise of that power.\" Thus \"the alienation [ Veräusserung ] of labour-power and its real manifestation [ Äusserung ], i.e. the period of its existence as a use-value, do not coincide in time.\" In cases where the \"formal alienation by sale of the use-value of a commodity is not simultaneous with its actual transfer to the buyer, the money of the buyer serves as means of payment .\" Footnote 12 supports this with two citations: \"All labour is paid after it has ceased\" (from An Inquiry into Those Principles Respecting the Nature of Demand ) and Ganilh's observation that the system of commercial credit \"had to start at the moment when the worker... could, thanks to his savings, wait for his wages until the end of the week, the fortnight, the month, the quarter, etc.\" The Worker's Credit to the Capitalist Marx draws the striking conclusion: \"In every country where the capitalist mode of production prevails, it is the custom not to pay for labour-power until it has been exercised for the period fixed by the contract, for example, at the end of each week.\" Therefore \"the worker advances the use-value of his labour-power to the capitalist. He lets the buyer consume it before he receives payment of the price. Everywhere the worker allows credit to the capitalist.\" This reframes the wage relation in a way that inverts capitalist self-understanding: the worker, not the capitalist, is the creditor. Marx insists that this \"credit is no mere fiction,\" citing not only \"the occasional loss of the wages the worker has already advanced, when a capitalist goes bankrupt\" (footnote 13 cites Storch's coy claim that the worker \"risks nothing\"—except the loss of his wages) but \"a series of more long-lasting consequences.\" Footnote 14 is a tour de force of empirical documentation for these consequences. In London there are \"full priced\" bakers who sell bread at its full value and \"undersellers\" who sell below value—comprising \"more than three-quarters of the total number of bakers.\" The undersellers \"almost without exception, sell bread adulterated with alum, soap, pearl-ash, chalk, Derbyshire stone-dust and other similar agreeable, nourishing and wholesome ingredients.\" Sir John Gordon testified before the 1855 committee that \"in consequence of these adulterations, the poor man, who lives on two pounds of bread a day, does not now get one-fourth part of nourishing matter, let alone the deleterious effects on his health.\" Tremenheere explains that the working class accepts the adulteration \"as a matter of necessity to take from their baker or from the chandler's shop such bread as they choose to supply\"—because, being unpaid until week's end, they cannot pay for the week's bread before week's end, so \"bread composed of those mixtures is made expressly for sale in this manner.\" In English agricultural districts (and still more in Scottish), wages are paid fortnightly or even monthly, forcing agricultural labourers to buy on credit at higher prices, \"tied to the shop which gives him credit\"—e.g., at Horningham, Wilts., flour buyable at 1s. 10d. per stone elsewhere costs the labourer 2s. 4d. The Paisley and Kilmarnock block-printers struck in 1833 to reduce payment periods from monthly to fortnightly. Coal-owners pay monthly and advance \"sums on account... often in goods\" through the tonmy-shop (truck system), where \"the men take it on one side and lay it out on the other.\" The empirical density here transforms an abstract point about payment timing into a documented mechanism of exploitation and dependency. From Circulation to Production: Crossing the Threshold Marx now argues that the form of payment \"does not alter the nature of the exchange of commodities.\" The price \"is fixed by the contract, although it is not realised till later, like the rent of a house.\" Labour-power \"is sold, although it is paid for only at a later period.\" For clarity of the \"pure form,\" he proposes a methodological presupposition: \"that the possessor of labour-power, on the occasion of each sale, immediately receives the price stipulated in the contract.\" With value determination now settled, Marx states what remains: the use-value the buyer gets \"manifests itself only in the actual utilisation, in the process of the consumption of the labour-power.\" The money-owner \"buys everything necessary for this process, such as raw material, in the market, and pays the full price for it. The process of the consumption of labour-power is at the same time the production process of commodities and of surplus-value.\" Consumption is \"completed, as in the case of every other commodity, outside the market or the sphere of circulation.\" Hence the famous transition: letting us \"leave this noisy sphere, where everything takes place on the surface and in full view of everyone,\" and follow the two parties \"into the hidden abode of production, on whose threshold there hangs the notice 'No admittance except on business.' Here we shall see, not only how capital produces, but how capital is itself produced. The secret of profit-making must at last be laid bare.\" The spatial metaphor—surface vs. hidden abode—encodes the epistemological thesis: the truth of capital is invisible in circulation and revealed only in production. The Eden of the Rights of Man: The Closing Irony The chapter's final movement is its most rhetorically powerful. The sphere of circulation \"within whose boundaries the sale and purchase of labour-power goes on, is in fact a very Eden of the innate rights of man. It is the exclusive realm of Freedom, Equality, Property and Bentham.\" Marx unpacks each: Freedom , \"because both buyer and seller of a commodity... are determined only by their own free will\"—they contract as free persons equal before the law, their contract \"the final result in which their joint will finds a common legal expression.\" Equality , \"because each enters into relation with the other, as with a simple owner of commodities, and they exchange equivalent for equivalent.\" Property , \"because each disposes only of what is his own.\" And Bentham (the utilitarian philosopher of self-interest), \"because each looks only to his own advantage. The only force bringing them together... is the selfishness, the gain and the private interest of each.\" In a passage of sardonic wit, Marx writes: \"And precisely for that reason, either in accordance with the pre-established harmony of things, or under the auspices of an omniscient providence, they all work together to their mutual advantage, for the common weal, and in the common interest.\" The ironic invocation of Leibniz's \"pre-established harmony\" and providentialism mocks the harmony-of-interests doctrine. Then comes the dramatic reversal: \"When we leave this sphere of simple circulation... a certain change takes place, or so it appears, in the physiognomy of our dramatis personae .\" The former money-owner \"now strides out in front as a capitalist; the possessor of labour-power follows as his worker. The one smirks self-importantly and is intent on business; the other is timid and holds back, like someone who has brought his own hide to market and now has nothing else to expect but—a tanning.\" The theatrical metaphor ( dramatis personae ) and the skinning image (\"a tanning\") are deliberately brutal. They reveal that the vaunted freedom and equality of the market are the surface glitter of a relation whose substance is compulsion and exploitation: the worker sells his hide; the capitalist takes it. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS This chapter yields several fundamental principles that resonate far beyond its immediate logical function. The first is the law of the self-expansion of value through use, not exchange . Marx demonstrates rigorously that if surplus-value is to be generated without violating the principle of equivalence, its source must lie in a commodity whose value is purchased at full value but whose use-value creates more value than its price—and that commodity is labour-power. This reframes the entire problem of profit: profit is not theft in the marketplace, not a markup, not the cheating of buyers, but the consequence of a specific use-value consumed in production. The corollary is that exploitation is not an aberration of exchange but its logical outcome under determinate social conditions. The second principle is the distinction between labour and labour-power , which Marx treats as foundational. Labour-power is the capacity ; labour is its exercise . The wage buys the former, but the capitalist consumes the latter. Confusing them—as Rossi, Torrens, and the classical school did—renders surplus-value invisible. This conceptual clarification is the pivot on which the entire theory of surplus-value turns, and its analytical clarity remains a benchmark for economic thought. The third is the historicity of economic categories . The \"free worker\" is not a natural phenomenon but the product of \"many economic revolutions\" and the \"extinction of a whole series of older formations.\" Capital \"announces from the outset a new epoch.\" This historicizing move denies capital any claim to naturalness or universality; it makes the wage relation a contingent, transitory social form rather than a permanent feature of human society. The \"one historical pre-condition comprises a world's history\"—a claim of sweeping scope that situates economic theory within historical narrative. The fourth is the historical-moral element in the value of labour-power. By refusing to reduce wages to bare subsistence and insisting on the historically and culturally constructed character of \"necessary requirements,\" Marx both anticipates modern discussions of a \"living wage\" and social wage and defeats any deterministic \"iron law.\" Yet he preserves determinacy for \"a given country at a given period,\" avoiding relativist dissolution. The fifth is the ideological unmasking of the sphere of circulation . The \"very Eden of the innate rights of man\"—Freedom, Equality, Property, and Bentham—is exposed as the surface appearance that conceals the compulsion beneath. This is the chapter's most enduring contribution to critical social theory: the demonstration that the realm of free contract (the market) systematically produces and requires a realm of unfreedom (the hidden abode of production). The dramatis personae who \"smirk\" and \"hold back\" are figures of a relation in which the worker has \"nothing else to expect but—a tanning.\" The broader implications are profound. For economic theory, the chapter grounds the labor theory of value in a concrete institutional analysis of the labor market, making the value of labor-power the linchpin connecting value theory to distribution. For political philosophy, it dissolves the liberal myth of the free and equal market by exposing the double freedom of the worker as freedom to sell and freedom from property. For social history, it insists that the emergence of capital is not a natural evolution but a violent, contingent, world-historical rupture. And for method, it demonstrates Marx's characteristic procedure: to accept the surface categories of bourgeois economy (free contract, equivalent exchange, equal rights) and then show, by rigorous internal logic, that these categories both presuppose and conceal their opposite—the compulsion, inequality, and expropriation that constitute the hidden abode into which the next chapter will take us. The sale and purchase of labour-power is thus not a minor chapter but the theoretical fulcrum of Capital , the point at which the laws of value and the laws of exploitation are shown to be one and the same. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 5 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 7"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 7", "title": "Chapter 7", "subtitle": "The Labour Process and the Valorization Process", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 7/Chapter 7  The Labour Process and the Valorization Process_explanation.html", "word_count": 8981, "read_time": "45 min read", "tags": ["Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["The Labour Process (Purposive Activity, Object, Instruments)", "The Valorization Process", "Creation of Value vs Creation of Surplus-Value", "Productive Consumption", "Dead Labour vs Living Labour"], "text": "Chapter 7 The Labour Process And The Valorization Process - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 8,948 words ⏱️ ~45 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 7: The Labour Process and the Valorization Process — An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 7 of Capital, Volume One opens Part Three, \"The Production of Absolute Surplus-Value,\" and constitutes one of the most consequential pivots in Marx's entire theoretical architecture. Its central problem is deceptively simple but devastating in its implications: How is it possible for money to be transformed into capital in a way that obeys, rather than violates, the laws of commodity exchange? The chapter's core thesis is that this apparent paradox — the secret of profit under conditions of fair exchange — is resolvable only by recognizing that the commodity the capitalist purchases, labour-power, has a use-value qualitatively unlike any other: its consumption creates more value than it costs to reproduce . Everything else in the chapter is the rigorous working-out of this discovery. The chapter performs its argument in two tightly bound movements. The first movement ( Section 1, \"The Labour Process\" ) abstracts entirely from social form to deliver a trans-historical account of what labour is — the \"everlasting nature-imposed condition of human existence\" through which humanity metabolizes with nature. Marx deliberately strips away all historically specific determinations (slave-owner, serf-master, capitalist) to isolate the pure anatomy of purposeful activity, object of labour, and instrument of labour. This abstraction is not ornamental: it establishes the conceptual baseline against which the specifically capitalist distortions of the labour process — supervision, property in the product, and the extraction of surplus — can later be measured as departures from, and appropriations of, a universal human activity. The second movement ( Section 2, \"The Valorization Process\" ) reintroduces capital and shows how the same material process, viewed under the aspect of value, becomes the engine of self-expanding wealth. Marx produces a meticulous numerical demonstration — the famous cotton-spinning example — that exhausts every \"moral\" and \"service\"-based defence of profit and lands on the sole remaining explanation: the difference between the value of labour-power and the value that labour-power can be made to create in a day's work . This gap is the seed of all surplus-value, and Part Three of Capital (and ultimately the entire critique of political economy) unfolds from it. Intellectually, this chapter belongs to the deepest level of Marx's critical project. Having established in Chapter 1 the dual nature of the commodity (use-value and exchange-value), and in Chapters 4–6 the laws of exchange and the circulation of capital (the elusive M–C–M′ formula whose \"+\" Marx had promised to explain), Marx now locates the site where that mysterious increment is actually produced. The chapter thereby links the abstract theory of value to the concrete labour process of the workshop, dissolving the false separation between \"circulation\" and \"production\" that both vulgar economics and, in different ways, the Physiocrats and Ricardo had maintained. It is also where Marx deploys his most sustained philosophical anthropology (the architect-versus-bee argument), his clearest statement of productive consumption, and the definitive distinction between labour as creator of use-values and labour as creator of value — a distinction, he says, that \"resolves itself into a distinction between two aspects of the production process.\" The chapter's rhetorical strategy is as important as its logic. It is a drama in three acts: an apparently serene technical account of production, a psychological collapse of the capitalist (\"stares in astonishment\") when his calculations yield no surplus, and a triumphant resolution in which the capitalist \"laughs\" because he has all along understood — like the practical man he is — what the professors of political economy are paid to obscure. The chapter is thus simultaneously an economics treatise, a piece of materialist philosophy, and a polemic against apologetics. Its objective is nothing less than to make visible, in plain daylight and without appeal to fraud, theft, or unequal exchange, the mechanism by which capital feeds on living labour. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS Framing: Part Three and the Absolute Form of Surplus-Value The chapter is placed under the banner of Part Three, \"The Production of Absolute Surplus-Value.\" The adjective absolute is the key to the entire section's logic. Surplus-value, Marx will argue, can be increased in two fundamental ways: by lengthening the working day beyond the time needed to reproduce the worker (absolute surplus-value), or by shortening that necessary time itself through increased productivity (relative surplus-value). Chapter 7 lays the conceptual groundwork for the absolute form because it isolates, for the first time, the pure mechanics of how any surplus arises at all — the length of the working day versus the value of labour-power. Everything about relative surplus-value (developed later, in Parts Four and beyond) presupposes this baseline. The chapter is therefore foundational not merely chronologically but structurally. 2.1 The Labour Process in its Simple, Abstract Elements The Use of Labour-Power as Labour Itself Marx opens by completing a transaction begun in the preceding chapters. The capitalist has purchased labour-power on the market. But a purchased commodity must be consumed to be of use, and the consumption of labour-power is labour itself. \"The purchaser of labour-power consumes it by setting the seller of it to work.\" In working, the seller becomes \"in actuality what previously he only was potentially, namely labour-power in action, a worker.\" This is a crucial dialectical move: potentiality (the capacity to work, purchased as a commodity) is actualized only through the act of working. To embody labour in commodities, the worker must first embody it in use-values — because value cannot exist except as the bearer of some use-value. Hence the capitalist sets the worker to produce a specific article. Crucially, Marx adds that the fact that this production is carried on \"under the control of a capitalist and on his behalf does not alter the general character of that production.\" This is the justification for his abstraction: we can study the labour process \"independently of any specific social formation.\" Labour as Metabolism Between Man and Nature Marx then delivers his celebrated definition of labour: \"Labour is, first of all, a process between man and nature, a process by which man, through his own actions, mediates, regulates and controls the metabolism between himself and nature.\" The term metabolism (in German, Stoffwechsel , literally \"material exchange\") is central. Man \"confronts the materials of nature as a force of nature,\" setting in motion \"the natural forces which belong to his own body, his arms, legs, head and hands.\" By acting on external nature and changing it, he \"simultaneously changes his own nature.\" Labour is thus not a one-way extraction from nature but a reciprocal transformation: humanity develops \"the potentialities slumbering within nature\" while subjecting \"the play of its forces to his own sovereign power.\" This passage is deeply grounded in a materialist anthropology. Marx explicitly excludes \"those first instinctive forms of labour which remain on the animal level.\" He notes the \"immense interval of time\" separating the fully human labour presupposed here from humanity's earliest instinctive forms — a historical-developmental claim. The labour he analyzes is \"an exclusively human characteristic.\" The Architect and the Bee: Purpose and Ideality Here Marx deploys his most famous illustration of what distinguishes human from animal production. A spider performs operations resembling a weaver's; a bee puts many a human architect to shame in building its honeycomb. \"But what distinguishes the worst architect from the best of bees is that the architect builds the cell in his mind before he constructs it in wax.\" At the end of every labour process, a result emerges that \"had already been conceived by the worker at the beginning, hence already existed ideally .\" The German verwirklichen (realize) is literally \"to make real\" — the worker realizes his own purpose in the material. This purpose is conscious, determining \"the mode of his activity with the rigidity of a law,\" and the worker \"must subordinate his will to it.\" This subordination is not momentary; it requires purpose, will, and \"close attention\" for the whole duration of the work. Marx adds a psychological-register observation: the less the worker is attracted to the work and the less he \"enjoys it as the free play of his own physical and mental powers, the closer his attention is forced to be.\" This is a subtle foreshadowing of alienation — where labour is not intrinsically enjoyable, attention becomes a disciplinary imposition rather than spontaneous absorption. The Three Simple Elements of the Labour Process Marx reduces the labour process to three \"simple elements\": (1) purposeful activity, that is work itself ; (2) the object on which that work is performed ; and (3) the instruments of that work . This tripartite scheme is the chapter's structural skeleton for everything that follows. The Object of Labour and the Definition of Raw Material The object of labour is whatever human activity works upon. Marx begins with the most basic case: the land (economically including water) in its original state, which supplies \"necessaries or means of subsistence ready to hand... as the universal material for human labour.\" Things that labour \"merely separates from immediate connection with their environment\" are objects of labour \"spontaneously provided by nature\" — fish caught and separated from water, timber felled in virgin forests, ores extracted from veins. Marx's footnote here quotes James Steuart to the effect that nature's spontaneous productions are small in quantity and independent of man, appearing \"as it were, to be furnished by Nature, in the same way as a small sum is given to a young man, in order to put him in a way of industry, and of making his fortune.\" But if the object of labour has \"been filtered through previous labour,\" Marx calls it raw material (a technical use of the term, narrower than standard English, as the editors note). His precise formulation: \"All raw material is an object of labour, but not every object of labour is raw material; the object of labour counts as raw material only when it has already undergone some alteration by means of labour.\" The logic is a matter of position in the process , not intrinsic property. The Instrument of Labour The instrument of labour is \"a thing, or a complex of things, which the worker interposes between himself and the object of his labour and which serves as a conductor, directing his activity onto that object.\" The worker uses the mechanical, physical, and chemical properties of some substances to set them to work on other substances \"as instruments of his power, and in accordance with his purposes.\" Marx here cites Hegel's Logic on \"cunning\" (the List der Vernunft ): cunning lies in the \"intermediative action which, while it permits the objects to follow their own bent and act upon one another till they waste away, and does not itself directly interfere in the process, is nevertheless only working out its own aims.\" This is an extraordinary philosophical resource: Marx borrows from Hegel the idea that the tool is a form of mediated power, wherein the worker's purpose is realized indirectly through the tool's own natural propensities. Marx distinguishes between ready-made means of subsistence (like fruit, gathered by bodily organs alone — the body itself as instrument) and the general case, where the object the worker directly takes possession of is \"not the object of labour but its instrument.\" Nature thus becomes \"one of the organs of his activity,\" which he annexes to his bodily organs, \"adding stature to himself in spite of the Bible.\" The famous image: \"As the earth is his original larder, so too it is his original tool house.\" The earth supplies stones for throwing, grinding, pressing, cutting. The earth itself is an instrument of labour (as in agriculture), but using it in this way \"presupposes a whole series of other instruments and a comparatively high stage of development of labour-power.\" Marx cites Ganilh (in his otherwise \"miserable work\") for enumerating the long series of labour processes that agriculture presupposes, and footnotes the Physiocrats' error that the productivity of labour appeared as a \"gift of nature,\" so that surplus-value appeared as a \"gift of nature.\" Even at the smallest stage of development, the labour process requires \"specially prepared instruments\" — stone implements and weapons in the oldest caves. In earliest human history, domesticated animals (themselves modified by labour) play the chief role as instruments, alongside worked stones, wood, bones, and shells. Marx cites Turgot on the importance of domesticated animals for the beginnings of civilization. The use and construction of instruments of labour, though present \"in germ\" among certain animals, is \"characteristic of the specifically human labour process,\" prompting Benjamin Franklin's definition of man as \"a tool-making animal.\" Instruments as Indices of Social Epochs Marx now makes a sweeping theoretical claim of major importance: \"Relics of bygone instruments of labour possess the same importance for the investigation of extinct economic formations of society as do fossil bones for the determination of extinct species of animals.\" His argument: \"It is not what is made but how, and by what instruments of labour, that distinguishes different economic epochs.\" Instruments of labour not only measure the development of human labour but \"indicate the social relations within which men work.\" This is a proto-archaeological, materialist methodology — the basis for what later becomes the mode-of-production thesis. He subdivides instruments into mechanical ones (\"the bones and muscles of production\"), which offer \"much more decisive evidence of the character of a given social epoch,\" versus those that merely hold materials (pipes, tubs, baskets, jars) — the \"vascular system of production,\" which first becomes important in the chemical industries. His footnote laments that historians have paid \"very little attention to the development of material production, which is the basis of all social life, and therefore of all real history.\" He notes that prehistory is classified by tools (Stone Age, Bronze Age, Iron Age) rather than by \"so-called historical research.\" Marx extends the definition of instruments of labour \"in a wider sense\" to include \"all the objective conditions necessary for carrying on the labour process,\" which do not directly enter but without which the process is impossible or partial. The earth is again the universal instrument of this kind (the \"ground beneath his feet\" and a \"field of employment\"). Mediated versions of such conditions include \"workshops, canals, roads, etc.\" The Product: Objectification of Labour Marx now completes the circuit: \"In the labour process, therefore, man's activity, via the instruments of labour, effects an alteration in the object of labour which was intended from the outset. The process is extinguished in the product.\" The product is a use-value, \"a piece of natural material adapted to human needs by means of a change in its form.\" Crucially: \"Labour has become bound up in its object: labour has been objectified, the object has been worked on.\" Marx stages this as a transformation from Unruhe (unrest, the restless activity of living labour) to Sein (being, the fixed, immobile characteristic of the finished product). \"The worker has spun, and the product is a spinning.\" This is the philosophical grammar of objectification — later the hinge of alienation theory, though Marx does not use that word here. Means of Production and Productive Labour Viewing the process from its result (the product), \"both the instruments and the object of labour are means of production \" and \"the labour itself is productive labour .\" Marx has a footnote cautioning that \"this method of determining what is productive labour, from the standpoint of the simple labour process, is by no means sufficient to cover the capitalist process of production.\" This is a critical caveat: what counts as \"productive\" in the abstract labour process is not yet what counts as productive for capital , a distinction that will matter enormously later. Marx notes that although a use-value emerges as product, other use-values (products of previous labour) enter as means of production. \"The same use-value is both the product of a previous process, and a means of production in a later process.\" Extractive vs Transformative Industries With the exception of the extractive industries (mining, hunting, fishing, and agriculture only insofar as it \"starts by breaking up virgin soil\"), where the material comes directly from nature, all branches of industry deal with raw material — an object of labour \"already filtered through labour.\" Even animals and plants we consider \"products of nature\" may be, in their present form, the result of \"a gradual transformation continued through many generations under human control.\" As for instruments of labour, they \"show traces of the labour of past ages, even to the most superficial observer.\" Principal Substance and Accessory Materials Raw material may form a product's principal substance or enter only as an accessory . Accessories may be consumed by the instruments (coal by a steam-engine, oil by a wheel, hay by draft-horses); added to the raw material to produce a physical modification (chlorine to unbleached linen, coal to iron, dye to wool); or help accomplish the work itself (materials for heating and lighting). The distinction between principal substance and accessory \"vanishes in the chemical industries proper,\" where none of the raw material reappears in its original composition in the product. Marx cites Storch (who distinguishes matière from materiaux ) and Cherbuliez (who calls accessories matières instrumentales ). The Relativity of Functional Categories Marx insists that any object can shift roles. Corn is raw material for millers, starch-manufacturers, distillers, cattle-breeders; it also enters its own production as seed. Coal emerges from mining as product and enters as means of production. An animal fattening for beef is simultaneously raw material and instrument (producing manure). A product ready for consumption can become raw material (grapes into wine) or be released only as raw material (cotton, thread, yarn — semi-manufactured , better described as \"manufactured up to a certain level\"). The decisive principle: \"whether a use-value is to be regarded as raw material, as instrument of labour or as product is determined entirely by its specific function in the labour process, by the position it occupies there: as its position changes, so do its determining characteristics.\" These categories are therefore relational , not substantial — a point of great methodological importance. The Extinguishing of Past Labour in Successful Production When products enter as means of production into new labour processes, \"they lose their character of being products and function only as objective factors contributing to living labour.\" A spinner treats spindles only as means for spinning, flax as material. In the process itself, the fact that they are products of past labour is \"as irrelevant as, in the case of the digestive process, the fact that bread is the product of the previous labour of the farmer, the miller and the baker.\" On the contrary, it is \"by their imperfections\" that means of production reveal their past-labour character: \"A knife which fails to cut, a piece of thread which keeps on snapping, forcibly remind us of Mr A, the cutler, or Mr B, the spinner. In a successful product, the role played by past labour in mediating its useful properties has been extinguished.\" This is a remarkable epistemic point: success conceals labour's history; failure reveals it. Living Labour as Vivifier A machine not active in the labour process is useless and falls prey to natural destruction: \"Iron rusts; wood rots. Yarn with which we neither weave nor knit is cotton wasted.\" Living labour must \"seize on these things, awaken them from the dead, change them from merely possible into real and effective use-values.\" Marx's near-lyrical language: \"Bathed in the fire of labour, appropriated as part of its organism, and infused with vital energy for the performance of the functions appropriate to their concept and to their vocation in the process, they are indeed consumed, but to some purpose.\" Finished products thus are both results and conditions of the labour process; their contact with living labour is \"the sole means by which they can be made to retain their character of use-values, and be realized.\" Productive vs Individual Consumption Labour \"uses up its material elements... consumes them, and is therefore a process of consumption.\" Marx distinguishes productive consumption (using up products as means of subsistence for labour) from individual consumption (using up products as means of subsistence for the living individual). The product of individual consumption is the consumer himself; the result of productive consumption is \"a product distinct from the consumer.\" Labour consumes products to create products — turning one set of products into means of production for another. And even now, production still employs many means provided directly by nature, \"not representing any combination of natural substances with human labour.\" The Labour Process as Universal Condition Marx summarizes his abstraction: the labour process, in its \"simple and abstract elements,\" is \"purposeful activity aimed at the production of use-values... an appropriation of what exists in nature for the requirements of man. It is the universal condition for the metabolic interaction between man and nature, the everlasting nature-imposed condition of human existence, and it is therefore independent of every form of that existence, or rather it is common to all forms of society in which human beings live.\" This is the theoretical payoff of the abstraction. He then explicitly notes what he has not introduced: the worker's relationship to other workers. It sufficed to present \"man and his labour on one side, nature and its materials on the other.\" His memorable illustration: \"The taste of porridge does not tell us who grew the oats, and the process we have presented does not reveal the conditions under which it takes place, whether it is happening under the slave-owner's brutal lash or the anxious eye of the capitalist, whether Cincinnatus undertakes it in tilling his couple of acres, or a savage, when he lays low a wild beast with a stone.\" This last example invites Marx's delicious polemical footnote targeting Colonel Torrens, who claimed to find \"the origin of capital\" in the first stone the savage flings at his prey, or the first stick used to strike down fruit. Marx ironizes: \"No doubt this 'first stick' [ Stock ] would also explain why 'stock' in English is synonymous with capital.\" This is the chapter's first direct assault on the capitalist apologetics that treat capital as a natural, trans-historical category rather than a social relation. The Capitalist's Two Characteristic Phenomena Marx now \"returns to our would-be capitalist,\" who has purchased all the factors of production. He has selected them \"with the keen eye of an expert.\" He proceeds to consume labour-power by causing the worker to consume the means of production. Marx reiterates that \"the general character of the labour process is evidently not changed\" by the fact that the worker works for the capitalist. The methods of bootmaking or spinning are \"not immediately altered by the intervention of the capitalist.\" He must \"begin by taking the labour-power as he finds it in the market\" — labour of a kind that arose \"in a period when there were as yet no capitalists.\" Marx signals that the transformation of the mode of production itself (via subordination to capital) \"can only occur later on.\" The labour process, when it becomes the capitalist's consumption of labour-power, exhibits two characteristic phenomena : The worker works under the control of the capitalist , to whom his labour belongs. The capitalist ensures the work is done properly, that means of production are applied directly to purpose, raw material is not wasted, and instruments are spared (worn only as necessary). The product is the property of the capitalist, not the worker. By paying for a day's labour-power, the capitalist has the right to use it for a day — \"just as much as the right to use any other commodity, such as a horse he had hired for the day.\" From the moment the worker \"steps into the workshop,\" the use-value of his labour-power — and therefore its use, which is labour — belongs to the capitalist. The capitalist incorporates labour \"as a living agent of fermentation\" into the lifeless constituents of the product, which also belong to him. The product belongs to him \"just as much as the wine which is the product of the process of fermentation going on in his cellar.\" Marx supports this with a battery of citations: Cherbuliez (\"Products are appropriated before they are transformed into capital\"), the same author on the law of appropriation whose \"fundamental principle was the exact opposite, namely that every worker has an exclusive right to the ownership of what he produces,\" and James Mill (the term \"capital\" as commonly used absurdly \"includes labour and capital both\"). These citations establish that the appropriation of the product by the capitalist follows rigorously from the law of commodity exchange — not from theft — even though it inverts the classical labour-theory-of-property principle. 2.2 The Valorization Process Two Objectives of the Capitalist Marx now shifts register. The product — the capitalist's property — is a use-value (yarn or boots). \"But although boots are, to some extent, the basis of social progress... he does not manufacture boots for their own sake. Use-value is certainly not la chose qu'on aime pour lui-même in the production of commodities.\" Use-values are produced \"only because and in so far as they form the material substratum of exchange-value.\" The capitalist has two objectives: (1) to produce a use-value that has exchange-value , i.e., an article destined for sale, a commodity; and (2) to produce a commodity greater in value than the sum of the values of the commodities used to produce it (means of production plus labour-power). \"His aim is to produce not only a use-value, but a commodity; not only use-value, but value; and not just value, but also surplus-value.\" The Unity of Labour Process and Value-Creation Marx signals the structure of the coming analysis: \"Just as the commodity itself is a unity formed of use-value and value, so the process of production must be a unity, composed of the labour process and the process of creating value [ Wertbildungsprozess ].\" Thus the same process has two aspects, mirroring the twofold nature of the commodity established in Chapter 1. The Value Calculation: The Spinning Example Marx now performs his meticulous numerical demonstration. He assumes the production of yarn, with these figures: Raw material: 10 lb. of cotton, bought at its full value = 10 shillings (this price already expresses the labour needed to produce the cotton in terms of average social labour). Instrument of labour: wear and tear of the spindle = 2 shillings . Labour-time necessary to produce the gold representing 12 shillings: 24 hours (two working days). From these, the value of the yarn: two days of labour are objectified in 12 shillings' worth of constant capital (cotton + spindle). The value of the product must include both the raw material and the worn portion of the spindle, because \"the labour-time required for the production of the cotton... is part of the labour necessary to produce the yarn, and is therefore contained in the yarn,\" and \"the same applies to the labour embodied in the spindle.\" Marx cites Ricardo's authority: \"Not only the labour applied immediately to commodities affects their value, but the labour also which is bestowed on the implements, tools, and buildings with which such labour is assisted.\" The Transmigration of Value into the Product Marx insists that despite the transformation of forms (cotton into yarn, spindle worn away and disappeared), the value is unaffected: \"The spindle and cotton, instead of resting quietly side by side, join together in the process, their forms are altered, and they are turned into yarn; but their value is no more affected by this fact than it would be if they had been simply exchanged for their equivalent in yarn.\" This is a crucial conservation-of-value principle: value does not originate in the labour process as such, but is transferred — the means of production preserve their value by reappearing in the product. Using an elegant grammatical metaphor, Marx notes that all the labour contained in the yarn is past labour; the labour producing the constituent elements lies \"further back in the past\" than the spinning, standing \"as it were, in the pluperfect, the latter in the perfect tense, but this does not matter.\" His house example: if thirty days of labour are needed to build a house, the total labour incorporated in it is not altered by the fact that the work of the last day was done 29 days later than that of the first. \"Therefore the labour contained in the raw material and instruments of labour can be treated just as if it were labour expended in an earlier stage of the spinning process, before the labour finally added in the form of actual spinning.\" The Two Conditions for Value Transfer Marx adds two conditions for the means of production to transfer value properly: The means of production must genuinely have served to produce a use-value — they must become yarn. \"Value is independent of the particular use-value by which it is borne, but a use-value of some kind has to act as its bearer.\" If they do not enter into a real use-value, their value is not transferred. The labour-time expended must not exceed what is socially necessary under given social conditions of production. If no more than 1 lb. of cotton is needed to spin 1 lb. of yarn, no more may be consumed. If the capitalist has \"a foible for using golden spindles instead of steel ones, the only labour that counts for anything in the value of the yarn remains that which would be required to produce a steel spindle, because no more is necessary under the given social conditions.\" Labour as Value-Creating vs Labour as Useful Activity Marx now distinguishes two standpoints on the spinner's labour. In the labour process, we viewed it as \"the activity which has the purpose of changing cotton into yarn\" — a specifically qualitative activity, differing from other kinds of productive labour, revealed subjectively (purpose of spinning) and objectively (special operations, special means of production, special use-value of product). Cotton and spindles are necessities for spinning but useless for making rifled cannon. In the value-creating process, on the contrary, the spinner's labour differs \"in no respect from the labour of the man who bores cannon, or... from the labour of the cotton-planter and the spindle-maker.\" It is only by reason of this identity (abstract, homogeneous human labour) that cotton-planting, spindle-making, and spinning can form the component parts of one whole — the value of the yarn. Here we are \"no longer concerned with the quality, the character and the content of the labour, but merely with its quantity.\" The Transformation of Labour from Unrest to Being During the labour process, the worker's labour \"constantly undergoes a transformation, from the form of unrest [ Unruhe ] into that of being [ Sein ], from the form of motion [ Bewegung ] into that of objectivity [ Gegenständlichkeit ].\" At the end of one hour, \"the spinning motion is represented in a certain quantity of yarn; in other words, a definite quantity of labour, namely that of one hour, has been objectified in the cotton.\" Note the terminology: labour , not spinning labour , because spinning counts here only as \"the expenditure of labour-power in general.\" The Socially Necessary Condition Maintained Again, Marx stresses the socially necessary condition: no more time may be consumed than is necessary under given social conditions. If x pounds of cotton are made into y pounds of yarn in one hour, then a day's labour does not count as 12 hours' labour unless 12x lb. of cotton have been made into 12y lb. of yarn; \"for only socially necessary labour-time counts towards the creation of value.\" Raw Material and Product in a New Light Under the value-creating aspect, raw material \"merely serves to absorb a definite quantity of labour\"; by being soaked in labour, it is changed into yarn. The product (yarn) is \"now nothing more than a measure of the labour absorbed by the cotton.\" If 1⅔ lb. of cotton can be spun into 1⅔ lb. of yarn per hour, then 10 lb. of yarn indicates the absorption of 6 hours of labour. Definite quantities of product now represent \"nothing but definite quantities of labour, definite masses of crystallized labour-time\" — \"the material shape taken by a given number of hours or days of social labour.\" Marx notes the irrelevance of the specific character (spinning, cotton, yarn): if the worker were in a coal-mine, a definite quantity of coal would represent a definite quantity of absorbed labour. The Value of Labour-Power Assumed Marx now assumes the value of a day's labour-power is 3 shillings, and that 6 hours of labour are incorporated in that sum (the amount needed to produce the worker's average daily means of subsistence). If the spinner converts 1⅔ lb. of cotton per hour, then in 6 hours he converts 10 lb. of cotton into 10 lb. of yarn. During the spinning process, the cotton absorbs 6 hours of labour. The same quantity of labour is embodied in a piece of gold worth 3 shillings. \"A value of 3 shillings, therefore, is added to the cotton by the labour of spinning.\" The Total Value of the Product — and the Absence of Surplus Now: the total value of 10 lb. of yarn. Two and a half days of labour are objectified in it: two days contained in the cotton and worn spindle, half a day absorbed during spinning. Two and a half days = 15 shillings. So 15 shillings is an adequate price for 10 lb. of yarn, and the price per pound is 1s. 6d. At this point Marx returns to the capitalist — and here the drama peaks. \"Our capitalist stares in astonishment. The value of the product is equal to the value of the capital advanced. The value advanced has not been valorized, no surplus-value has been created, and consequently money has not been transformed into capital.\" The 15 shillings spent (10 for cotton, 2 for spindle wear, 3 for labour-power) equal the 15 shillings' worth produced. \"The swollen value of the yarn is of no avail, for it is merely the sum of the values formerly existing in the cotton, the spindle and the labour-power: out of such a simple addition of existing values, no surplus-value can possibly arise.\" These values are concentrated in one thing, but so they were \"in the sum of 15 shillings, before it was split up into three parts by the purchase of the commodities.\" Marx gives a mundane illustration: \"whether a man buys his house ready built, or has it built for him, neither of these operations will increase the amount of money laid out on the house.\" He also footnotes the Physiocrats, who held that all non-agricultural labour is unproductive — a doctrine this result would seem to confirm (\"For the professional economist it is irrefutable\"), quoting Mercier de la Rivière: addition is not multiplication. This is a dialectical setup : Marx demonstrates the Physiocrat conclusion holds if we assume the value of labour-power equals the value it creates — the very assumption he is about to shatter. The Capitalist's Apologetic Interlude Marx now has the capitalist resort to every \"moral\" and apologetic argument, satirizing them all: \"Intention\": The capitalist says he intended to make more money. Marx comments: \"The road to hell is paved with good intentions, and he might just as well have intended to make money without producing at all.\" He threatens to buy commodities in the market instead of manufacturing them — but \"if all his brother capitalists were to do the same, where would he find his commodities?\" And \"he cannot eat his money.\" Footnote: capitalists have withdrawn capital from production to gamble (railways 1844–47, Liverpool cotton exchange during the American Civil War) — the practical refutation of the \"intention\" argument. \"Abstinence\": The capitalist recites the catechism \"Consider my abstinence. I might have squandered the 15 shillings, but instead I consumed it productively and made yarn with it.\" Marx mocks: \"Very true; and as a reward he is now in possession of good yarn instead of a bad conscience.\" He adds: \"whatever the merits of his abstinence there is no money there to recompense him, because the value of the product is merely the sum of the values thrown into the process... Let him therefore console himself with the reflection that virtue is its own reward.\" Marx jibes at MacCulloch, whose \"proven remedy\" for over-production was to tell capitalists to produce only what they need themselves. \"Service\": The capitalist protests that he provided materials, instruments, and the worker's means of subsistence, so surely he deserves a return. Marx rebuts: has the worker not performed an equivalent service by turning cotton and spindle into yarn? \"In any case, here the question of service does not arise. A service is nothing other than the useful effect of a use-value.\" He footnotes Luther's blistering satire on \"service\" (adulterers do each other great service; a horseman serves a robber; \"the devil himself does his servants a great, inestimable service\"). The productive-contribution argument collapses: the capitalist paid 3 shillings and received an exact equivalent of 3 shillings in value added. \"Superintendence labour\": The capitalist asks, \"Have I myself not worked?... does not this labour, too, create value?\" His own overseer and manager \"shrug their shoulders.\" Marx notes drolly: \"with a hearty laugh, he recovers his composure. The whole litany he has just recited was simply meant to pull the wool over our eyes... He leaves this and all similar subterfuges and conjuring tricks to the professors of political economy, who are paid for it. He himself is a practical man, and although he does not always consider what he says outside his business, within his business he knows what he is doing.\" The Real Solution: The Value of Labour-Power vs the Value it Creates Now Marx delivers the decisive argument. The value of a day's labour-power is 3 shillings, because \"half a day's labour is objectified in that quantity of labour-power\" — the means of subsistence required daily for producing labour-power cost half a day's labour. But: \"the past labour embodied in the labour-power and the living labour it can perform, and the daily cost of maintaining labour-power and its daily expenditure in work, are two totally different things. The former determines the exchange-value of the labour-power, the latter is its use-value.\" The fact that half a day's labour keeps the worker alive for 24 hours \"does not in any way prevent him from working a whole day.\" Therefore \"the value of labour-power, and the value which that labour-power valorizes [ verwertet ] in the labour-process, are two entirely different magnitudes; and this difference was what the capitalist had in mind when he was purchasing the labour-power.\" This is the kernel of the entire chapter . The useful quality of labour-power (that it makes yarn or boots) was to the capitalist \"merely the necessary condition for his activity.\" What was really decisive was \"the specific use-value which this commodity possesses of being a source not only of value, but of more value than it has itself. This is the specific service the capitalist expects from labour-power, and in this transaction he acts in accordance with the eternal laws of commodity-exchange.\" The seller of labour-power \"realizes its exchange-value, and alienates its use-value.\" He cannot take the one without giving the other. The use-value of labour-power belongs as little to its seller as the use-value of sold oil belongs to the dealer. The buyer has paid for a day's labour-power and therefore has the use of it for a day. \"On the one hand the daily sustenance of labour-power costs only half a day's labour, while on the other hand the very same labour-power can remain effective, can work, during a whole day, and consequently the value which its use during one day creates is double what the capitalist pays for that use; this circumstance is a piece of good luck for the buyer, but by no means an injustice towards the seller.\" — This last clause is the chapter's most explosive ideological move: the exploitation of labour is not a violation of market justice but its perfect realization. The Extended Labour Process \"Our capitalist foresaw this situation, and that was the cause of his laughter.\" The worker now finds \"the means of production necessary for working not just 6 but 12 hours.\" If 10 lb. of cotton absorbed 6 hours, now 20 lb. absorbs 12 hours and becomes 20 lb. of yarn. Now five days of labour are objectified in the 20 lb. of yarn: four days due to cotton and the lost steel of the spindle, the remaining day absorbed during spinning. Expressed in gold, five days = 30 shillings. That is the price of 20 lb. of yarn (still 1s. 6d. per lb.). But the sum of values of the commodities thrown into the process = 27 shillings . The value of the yarn = 30 shillings . \"Therefore the value of the product is one-ninth greater than the value advanced to produce it; 27 shillings have turned into 30 shillings; a surplus-value of 3 shillings has been precipitated. The trick has at last worked: money has been transformed into capital.\" The Theoretical Reconciliation Marx savors the elegance: \"Every condition of the problem is satisfied, while the laws governing the exchange of commodities have not been violated in any way. Equivalent has been exchanged for equivalent.\" The capitalist paid full value for cotton, spindle, and labour-power. He then did what every purchaser does: consumed the use-value. The process yielded 20 lb. of yarn worth 30 shillings. The capitalist, \"formerly a buyer, now returns to the market as a seller,\" sells at its exact value — and yet withdraws 3 shillings more than he put in. \"This whole course of events, the transformation of money into capital, both takes place and does not take place in the sphere of circulation.\" It takes place through circulation (because conditioned by the purchase of labour-power in the market); it does not take place in circulation (because what happens there is only an introduction to the valorization process, which is entirely confined to the sphere of production). \"And so 'everything is for the best in the best of all possible worlds.'\" (A Voltairean allusion to Candide — Marx's irony at the expense of Panglossian optimism about the market.) Value as a Self-Expanding Subject Marx summarizes: by turning money into commodities that serve as building materials for a new product and as factors in the labour process, \"by incorporating living labour into their lifeless objectivity, the capitalist simultaneously transforms value, i.e. past labour in its objectified and lifeless form, into capital, value which can perform its own valorization process, an animated monster which begins to 'work', 'as if its body were by love possessed.'\" (The Goethe quotation from Faust .) This is a key philosophical culmination: value itself becomes an active subject, a self-moving, self-valorizing entity — capital as \"M–C–M′\" personified. It also carries a strong Burkean-Gothic flavor: capital is an \"animated monster\" — an undead force driven by a vampiric compulsion. Value-Creation vs Valorization Marx now clarifies the relation between the two processes: \"If we now compare the process of creating value with the process of valorization, we see that the latter is nothing but the continuation of the former beyond a definite point. If the process is not carried beyond the point where the value paid by the capitalist for the labour-power is replaced by an exact equivalent, it is simply a process of creating value; but if it is continued beyond that point, it becomes a process of valorization.\" So the two are not distinct processes but phases of one process, separated by a threshold — the point at which the value equivalent to labour-power has been reproduced. Value-Creation vs Labour Process Then: \"If we proceed further, and compare the process of creating value with the labour process, we find that the latter consists in the useful labour which produces use-values. Here the movement of production is viewed qualitatively... But if it is viewed as a value-creating process the same labour process appears only quantitatively.\" In the value-creating view, commodities no longer count as functionally determined and material elements on which labour-power acts with a purpose; \"they count merely as definite quantities of objectified labour.\" Whether the labour was already in the means of production or has just been added, \"that labour counts only according to its duration. It amounts to so many hours, or days, etc.\" The Socially Necessary Conditions Restated Marx reiterates: \"the time spent in production counts only in so far as it is socially necessary for the production of a use-value.\" This has several consequences: Normal objective conditions: Labour-power must be functioning under normal conditions. If a self-acting mule is the socially predominant instrument, it is \"impermissible to supply the spinner with a spinning-wheel.\" The cotton must be of suitable quality. Otherwise the spinner spends more than socially necessary time, and the excess \"would create neither value nor money.\" Whether the objective factors are normal \"does not depend on the worker, but rather on the capitalist.\" Normal subjective conditions: Labour-power must itself be of normal effectiveness — average skill, dexterity, and speed for that trade; capitalist took care to buy it so. It must be expended with average exertion and usual intensity. The capitalist is \"as careful to see that this is done, as he is to ensure that his workmen are not idle for a single moment. He has bought the use of the labour-power for a definite period, and he insists on his rights. He has no intention of being robbed.\" No wasteful consumption: All wasteful consumption of raw material or instruments is strictly forbidden — \"our friend has a penal code of his own\" — because what is wasted represents \"a superfluous expenditure of quantities of objectified labour, labour that does not count in the product or enter into its value.\" Marx footnotes the case of slavery: the slave distinguishes himself as instrumentum vocale , an animal as instrumentum semi-vocale , and a lifeless implement as instrumentum mutum (Varro). Under slavery, only the \"rudest and heaviest implements\" are used because slaves damage them deliberately; this is confirmed by Cairnes ( The Slave Power ) and Olmsted ( A Journey in the Seaboard Slave States ), who describes the clumsy tools and the mule-over-horse preference (mules \"will bear cudgelling... and they do not take cold or get sick, if neglected or overworked\"). The Two Unities Marx draws the synthesis: \"The difference between labour, considered on the one hand as producing utilities, and on the other hand as creating value, a difference which we discovered by our analysis of a commodity, resolves itself into a distinction between two aspects of the production process.\" He gives two definitive definitions: \"The production process, considered as the unity of the labour process and the process of creating value, is the process of production of commodities; considered as the unity of the labour process and the process of valorization, it is the capitalist process of production, or the capitalist form of the production of commodities.\" This is the chapter's structural climax — the pairing maps perfectly onto the commodity's dual unity, closing the loop opened in Chapter 1. Skilled vs Simple Labour — the Final Nuance The chapter closes with an important qualification. Marx earlier assumed the labour appropriated was simple, average social labour. But the argument holds equally for more complex labour. Higher, more complicated labour is \"expenditure of labour-power of a more costly kind, labour-power whose production has cost more time and labour... and which therefore has a higher value.\" Being of higher value, it expresses itself in higher sorts of labour, and so objectifies in proportionally higher values during equal time. \"Whatever difference in skill there may be between the labour of a spinner and that of a jeweller, the portion of his labour by which the jeweller merely replaces the value of his own labour-power does not in any way differ in quality from the additional portion by which he creates surplus-value. In both cases, the surplus-value results only from a quantitative excess of labour, from a lengthening of one and the same labour-process.\" An extensive footnote unpacks why \"skilled\" and \"unskilled\" are shaky categories: partly \"pure illusion\" or \"traditional convention,\" partly the product of the \"helpless condition of some sections of the working class.\" Accidental circumstances cause the two forms sometimes to change places. Where the working class's physique has deteriorated (as in highly developed capitalist countries), heavy-muscle labour is considered \"higher\" and delicate labour sinks to \"simple\" (bricklaying vs damask-weaving; the fustian-cutter's labour counts as simple despite greater exertion). Marx cites Laing's estimate that in England and Wales, 11,300,000 people lived on unskilled labour (out of 18 million), with Laing's numbers criticized for including better-paid factory workers and middle-class categories to swell the \"skilled\" side. James Mill: \"The great class who have nothing to give for food but ordinary labour, are the great bulk of the people.\" The final technical point: \"in every process of creating value the reduction of the higher type of labour to average social labour, for instance one day of the former to x days of the latter, is unavoidable.\" Marx therefore \"saves himself a superfluous operation\" by assuming the worker's labour is average simple labour. His closing footnote cites Cazenove: \"Where reference is made to labour as a measure of value, it necessarily implies labour of one particular kind... the proportion which the other kinds bear to it being easily ascertained.\" The reduction of complex labour to simple is thus treated as a technical operation of value-theory, not a claim about the dignity or difficulty of work — a subtle but necessary component of the value-creating analysis. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter yields a set of principles that operate at every level of Marx's system. First, the principle of the two aspects : the same labour process is simultaneously qualitative (creating use-values) and quantitative (creating value). This resolves the apparent contradiction of a production process that both transfers old value and creates new value — the value of the means of production is preserved in the product, while the living labour adds a new quantum. Second, the principle of the threshold : value-creation becomes valorization the moment labour is extended beyond the point at which it has reproduced the equivalent of its own price. Surplus-value is thus not a discrete \"thing\" produced at some point in the day, but a quantitative excess that accrues continuously after the threshold is crossed. Third, the principle of the conservation and transfer of value — Marx's answer to the Physiocrats: production adds nothing to the value of the means of production except what living labour adds; the rest is transferred, not created. Fourth, the principle of exchange-conformity : capital's exploitation of labour does not require breaking the laws of the market. It is realised through fair exchange, not despite it. The broader philosophical implication is Marx's materialist anthropology : labour is the \"everlasting nature-imposed condition of human existence,\" the universal metabolism between humanity and nature. This trans-historical baseline allows Marx to make a devastating critique of apologetic political economy, which takes the specific historical form of the labour process (capitalist control, property in product, surplus extraction) and presents it as natural and universal . By contrast, Marx shows that the form of the labour process under capital — supervision, discipline, product appropriation, the extraction of surplus beyond the value paid for labour-power — is a historically specific shape imposed on a universal activity. The \"practical man\" of the chapter understands what the \"professors of political economy\" are paid to obscure. The chapter also establishes the ideological mechanism of capitalism. The capitalist's apologies (intention, abstinence, service, superintendence labour) are all shown to be incoherent, yet they survive because the operation of the market presents exploitation as fair exchange. The \"good luck\" of the buyer is not an injustice to the seller — and it is precisely this legal-ethical shield that makes the extraction of surplus-value appear natural, indeed just . The vampire image — capital as an \"animated monster\" driven by a self-expanding compulsion — announces the central theme of the rest of Capital : the self-valorization of value as a social force that subordinates living labour to its own dead logic. Practically, the chapter's conclusions reverberate through the entire political economy that follows. The length of the working day becomes a contested terrain (Parts Three and Eight, on absolute surplus-value and the factory legislation), because the only way to increase surplus without raising productivity is to lengthen the day. The value of labour-power becomes the pivot of class struggle, because any cheapening of the worker's subsistence (or any intensification of work without compensation) shifts the threshold in capital's favor. And the socially necessary labour-time standard becomes the normative yardstick against which both productivity and exploitation are measured. In short, Chapter 7 is where Marx converts the abstract logic of value into a living conflict: between the capitalist, who buys labour-power to consume it for a day, and the worker, who sells it but retains the human capacity to resist its full expenditure. The chapter that begins with the architect's ideal plan and the bee's instinctive construction ends with the discovery that the very same human purposiveness that distinguishes labour is what capital captures, commercializes, and turns against the labourer. That paradox is the engine of the rest of Marx's critique. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 6 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 8"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 8", "title": "Chapter 8", "subtitle": "Constant Capital and Variable Capital", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 8/Chapter 8  Constant Capital and Variable Capital_explanation.html", "word_count": 5447, "read_time": "27 min read", "tags": ["Surplus-Value & Exploitation", "Organic Composition of Capital (OCC)"], "keywords": ["Constant Capital vs Variable Capital", "Preservation of Old Value vs Addition of New Value", "Transfer of Means of Production Value", "Dual Action of Living Labour", "Raw Materials and Depreciation Transfer"], "text": "Chapter 8 Constant Capital And Variable Capital - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,420 words ⏱️ ~27 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation CHAPTER 8: CONSTANT CAPITAL AND VARIABLE CAPITAL — EXHAUSTIVE DECONSTRUCTION 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 8 of Capital, Volume One marks the precise moment where Marx's discovery of the twofold character of labour — laid out in Chapter 7 — crystallizes into the analytical apparatus that will govern the rest of the entire work: the distinction between constant capital and variable capital . The chapter's central problem is deceptively simple but conceptually treacherous: how is it that the same act of labour both adds new value to a product and simultaneously preserves the old value of the means of production consumed in making it? Marx opens by observing that the various factors of the labour process \"play different parts in forming the value of the product.\" The worker's living labour adds fresh value; the means of production (raw cotton, spindles, machinery) have their values preserved and transferred into the product. The intellectual challenge the chapter solves is explaining this twofold result without invoking two separate acts of labour — the worker, Marx insists, \"does not perform two pieces of work simultaneously.\" The unifying solution is that labour possesses two properties inhering in a single indivisible act: as abstract social labour it creates new value; as concrete, useful labour (spinning, weaving, forging) it transfers and preserves the value of the means of production. This distinction has a two-tier architecture. First, Marx rigorously demonstrates the mechanics of value transfer: means of production never transfer more value than they themselves possess or lose; free gifts of nature (land, wind, water, virgin timber) transfer nothing because they embody no labour. He introduces several celebrated analytical devices — the \"metempsychosis\" of value, the difference between entering a process \"as a whole\" versus \"piece by piece,\" the \"'devil's dust'\" of cotton waste — to show how the labour process (production of use-values) and the valorization process (production of value) can diverge within the identical act. Second, he translates these functional distinctions into the categorical vocabulary of capital itself. That portion of capital embodied in means of production — raw material, auxiliary material, instruments of labour — \"does not undergo any quantitative alteration of value in the process of production,\" and is therefore named constant capital . That portion laid out in labour-power does alter its value: it reproduces its own equivalent and produces a surplus, \"which may itself vary.\" Hence variable capital — the part \"continually being transformed from a constant into a variable magnitude.\" The chapter's intellectual objective is thus to give capitalism's value-generating mechanism a scientific anatomy. It fits into the broader architecture of Capital as the hinge between the abstract theory of value (Parts I–II) and the concrete analysis of surplus-value extraction, the working day, and the composition of capital. Critically, Marx pre-emptively wards off misreadings: the term \"constant\" does not deny that the prices of means of production may change; it only asserts that such changes originate outside the production process in which they serve. The chapter closes by insisting that even a vast revolution in the technical ratio of constant to variable capital \"has not in the least degree affected the essential difference between the two\" — a forward-looking gesture toward the organic composition of capital and the falling rate of profit. The whole argument is repeatedly sharpened against bourgeois apologists — J. B. Say and Wilhelm Roscher — who attempt to derive surplus-value from the \"productive services\" of nature and materials, a mystification Marx treats with withering sarcasm. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Problem: Two Results from One Labour Marx begins (p. 307) with an observation that is simultaneously economic and philosophical: the \"various factors of the labour process play different parts in forming the value of the product.\" On the one hand the worker — spinner, weaver, smith — \"adds fresh value to the material of his labour by expending on it a given amount of additional labour,\" irrespective of the specific content and purpose of that labour. On the other hand, the values of the means of production — cotton and spindle — \"re-appear again in the value of the yarn.\" This is the phenomenon of value transfer : \"The value of the means of production is therefore preserved by being transferred to the product.\" Marx specifies that this transfer occurs during the conversion of means into product — that is, during the labour process — and that it is \"mediated through labour.\" The word mediated is doing heavy lifting here: it flags that labour is not merely the cause of transfer but the vehicle through which dead past labour (crystallized in materials) re-enters living circulation. The immediate analytical puzzle: the worker does not perform two separate acts. He does not spin to add value and then separately work \"to preserve the value of the means of production.\" And yet two entirely distinct results emerge. The conclusion Marx draws is that \"this twofold nature of the result can be explained only by the twofold nature of his labour; it must at the same time create value through one of its properties and preserve or transfer value through another.\" This is the load-bearing move of the chapter — a direct application of the twofold-labour analysis established in Chapter 7. Concrete useful labour (the specific, purposeful form — spinning, weaving, forging) is the property that transfers and preserves value; abstract social labour (expenditure of human labour-power in general, measured by duration) is the property that creates new value. The Mechanics of Transfer: Productive Form as the Condition On p. 308, Marx makes the mechanism explicit. How does any worker add fresh labour-time and fresh value? \"Evidently, only by working productively in a particular way.\" The spinner adds value by spinning, the weaver by weaving, the smith by forging. These operations \"add labour as such, and therefore new values\" — but here comes the crucial qualification: it is \"only through the agency of labour directed to a particular purpose\" that cotton, yarn, iron, etc., \"become constituent elements of the product, of a new use-value.\" The old form of use-value disappears but is \"taken up again in a new form of use-value.\" Marx then invokes the conclusion of the earlier analysis of value-creation: if a use-value is effectively consumed in producing a new use-value, then the quantity of labour needed to produce the consumed article forms part of the labour necessary to produce the new use-value; \"this portion is therefore labour transferred from the means of production to the new product.\" The decisive formulation follows: the worker \"preserves the values of the already consumed means of production or transfers them to the product as portions of its value, not by virtue of his additional labour as such, but by virtue of the particular useful character of that labour, by virtue of its specific productive form. \" This is the chapter's conceptual hinge, and Marx reinforces it with a vivid image: concrete, purposeful labour \"raises the means of production from the dead merely by entering into contact with them, infuses them with life so that they become factors of the labour process, and combines with them to form new products.\" The counterfactual proof is elegant: \"If the specific productive labour of the worker were not spinning, he could not convert the cotton into yarn, and therefore he could not transfer the values of the cotton and spindle to the yarn.\" A spinner who switched to joinery would still add value by a day's labour — but he would no longer transfer the value of cotton into yarn, because he would no longer be spinning. The concrete form is what determines which use-values get transformed and therefore which values get transferred; the abstract form is what determines how much new value is added, since the value added depends only on the duration of the labour, not its content. Marx crystallizes this in the summary passage: \"it is by virtue of its general character as expenditure of human labour-power in the abstract that spinning adds new value...; and on the other hand, it is by virtue of its special character as a concrete, useful process that the same labour of spinning both transfers the values of the means of production to the product and preserves them in the product.\" Footnote 1 cites an 1821 tract: \"Labour gives a new creation for one extinguished\" — a neat pre-Marxian intuition of the phenomenon. Four Thought-Experiments in Value and Productivity Marx then conducts four controlled variations to prove that the two properties of labour — preserving and creating value — are \"different in their very essence\" (p. 309). Experiment 1 — Rising productivity. Suppose an invention allows the spinner to spin in 6 hours as much cotton as before in 36 hours. His labour is now six times as effective as useful productive activity . The product rises sixfold, from 6 lb. to 36 lb. of yarn. But the new labour absorbed by each pound of cotton falls to one-sixth of the former amount, so the value added per pound is one-sixth of what it was. Conversely, the value transferred from the cotton to the product is six times as great, because six times as much cotton has been worked up. Thus the same 6 hours of spinning, within one indivisible process, transfers six times as much preserved value while adding one-sixth as much new value per pound. The two functions move in opposite directions — proof of their distinct essence. Marx states the general law: the longer the time needed to spin a given weight of cotton, the greater the fresh value added; the greater the weight spun in a given time, the greater the value preserved by transfer. Experiment 2 — Changing value of raw material. Productivity is held constant; the spinner still needs the same time per pound; but the exchange-value of cotton rises sixfold or falls to one-sixth . The spinner puts the same quantity of labour into the pound and adds the same value. Yet the value transferred from the cotton to the yarn is now either six times or one-sixth as much. The same result holds if the value of the instruments of labour rises or falls \"while their usefulness in the labour process remains unaltered.\" Experiment 3 — Unchanged conditions. If technical conditions and the values of means of production are constant, the spinner consumes in equal working-times equal quantities of material and machinery of unvarying value. Then \"the value preserved in the product is directly proportional to the new value added to the product.\" In two weeks he adds twice as much value as in one and preserves twice as much. But Marx immediately qualifies: \"this does not happen because he adds new value, but because the addition of new value takes place under unvaried conditions which are independent of his own labour.\" This is an important anti-teleological caveat — the correlation between added and preserved value is not causal but contingent on unchanged external conditions. Experiment 4 — Labour-time proportionality. Despite all variation, Marx concedes a relative sense in which the worker \"always preserves old value in proportion to the added quantity of new value\": whether cotton's value rises to two shillings or falls to sixpence, the worker preserves half as much value in one hour as in two. The proportionality is to labour-time , not to productivity or value levels. Use-Value as the Substratum of Value Before proceeding, Marx inserts a philosophical reminder (pp. 310–11): \"Value exists only in use-values, in things, if we leave aside its purely symbolic representation in tokens.\" Even man, \"viewed merely as the physical existence of labour-power,\" is \"a natural object, a thing, although a living, conscious thing.\" He notes the German phrase dingliche Äusserung — the physical, thing-like manifestation of labour-power. The consequence: if an article loses its use-value, it also loses its value. Means of production escape this fate because they \"lose in the labour process the original form of their use-value only to assume in the product the form of a new use-value.\" Yet what particular object serves as value's bearer \"is still a matter of complete indifference.\" The general law: \"in the labour process the means of production transfer their value to the product only in so far as they lose their exchange-value along with their independent use-value. They only give up to the product the value they themselves lose as means of production.\" Divergent Behaviours: Auxiliary Materials vs. Instruments But the objective factors \"do not all behave in the same way\" (p. 311). Marx distinguishes three types: Coal and lubricating oil : vanish entirely, leaving no trace. Dyestuffs and auxiliary substances : vanish as independent entities but re-appear in the properties of the product. Raw material : forms the substance of the product, but only after a change of form. All three — raw material and auxiliary substances — lose the independent form with which they entered the labour process. Instruments of labour behave differently. Tools, machines, factory buildings, containers are useful \"only as long as they keep their original shape.\" During their lifetime they retain their shape independently of the product, and so too after death: \"The mortal remains of machines, tools, workshops etc., always continue to lead an existence distinct from that of the product they helped to turn out.\" Over the whole period of service, the instrument's use-value is completely consumed and its exchange-value completely transferred. A spinning machine lasting ten years transfers its total value gradually to the product of ten years. Its lifetime is \"spent in the repetition of a greater or lesser number of similar operations.\" The famous analogy: \"The instrument suffers the same fate as the man.\" Every day brings a man twenty-four hours nearer the grave, yet life insurance companies use the theory of averages to draw \"very accurate, and what is more, very profitable conclusions\" — so too with machines. Marx operationalizes this (p. 312): if a machine's use-value lasts six days, it loses one-sixth per day and parts with one-sixth of its value to each day's product. \"The deterioration of all instruments, their daily loss of use-value, and the corresponding quantity of value they part with to the product, are accordingly calculated on this basis.\" This is the conceptual foundation of depreciation . The Iron Ceiling on Value Transfer From this Marx extracts a general law: \"means of production never transfer more value to the product than they themselves lose during the labour process by the destruction of their own use-value.\" The corollary is decisive for the critique of capitalism: if an instrument \"has no value to lose, i.e. if it is not the product of human labour, it transfers no value to the product.\" It helps create use-value without contributing to exchange-value. This applies to \"all those means of production supplied by nature without human assistance, such as land, wind, water, metals in the form of ore, and timber in virgin forests.\" The Two Divergences: Whole vs. Piece, Piece vs. Whole Marx introduces \"another interesting phenomenon\" (p. 312). A machine worth £1,000 wears out in 1,000 days; one-thousandth of its value is transferred daily, yet the machine as a whole \"continues to take part in the labour process, though with diminishing vitality.\" This gives the signature structural insight: \"one factor of the labour process, a means of production, continually enters as a whole into that process, while it only enters in parts into the valorization process. The distinction between the labour process and the valorization process is reflected here in their objective factors, in that one and the same means of production, in one and the same process of production, counts in its totality as an element in the labour process, but only piece by piece as an element in the creation of value.\" Footnote 2 clarifies that repairs are excluded — a machine under repair \"is no longer an instrument of labour, but its material\"; work is done upon it, not with it. Marx permits assuming repair labour counts as part of original production labour, but the text deals only with \"that kind of wear which cannot be repaired from time to time,\" wear that \"little by little brings about death,\" quoting an anonymous writer on the knife that \"is not worth a new blade.\" This same footnote contains a polemical aside against the author of Observations on Certain Verbal Disputes in Political Economy , who mocked Ricardo by arguing that the whole labour of the engineer making stocking-machines is contained in each pair of stockings. Marx concedes the author's confusion is justified only to the extent that \"neither Ricardo nor any other economist before or since has accurately distinguished the two aspects of labour.\" The Inverse Case: Devil's Dust On p. 313 Marx presents the mirror image: a means of production \"may enter as a whole into the valorization process, although it enters only piece by piece into the labour process.\" Example: in spinning, 15 lb. out of every 115 lb. of cotton becomes \"'devil's dust'\" — not yarn, but unavoidable waste. Yet the value of the 15 lb. is transferred to the yarn just as surely as that of the 100 lb. that become yarn substance. The reason is strictly functional: \"The use-value of 15 lb. of cotton must vanish into dust before 100 lb. of yarn can be made. The destruction of this cotton is therefore a necessary condition for the production of the yarn. And because it is a necessary condition, and for no other reason, the value of that cotton is transferred to the product.\" The same applies to refuse generally; Marx notes Manchester machine-building factories where mountains of iron turnings are carted to the foundry at night, only to re-appear the next morning as solid iron. The Quantitative Ceiling and the \"Metempsychosis\" of Value Marx re-states the limit (p. 314): the maximum value loss is limited by the original value with which means entered, or \"the labour-time required to produce them.\" Hence a machine costing £150 (or 500 days of labour) \"cannot under any circumstances add more than £150 to the value of the product.\" Its value \"is determined not by the labour process into which it enters as a means of production, but by that out of which it has issued as a product.\" Footnote 3 is a substantial polemic. Marx demolishes J. B. Say's doctrine that surplus-value (interest, profit, rent) derives from the \"services productifs\" rendered by means of production (land, instruments, raw material) via their use-values. He mocks Roscher's citation of Say's oil-mill example: \"The oil produced by the oil mill is indeed something very different from the labour expended in constructing the mill!\" — a deliberate expose of Roscher's confusion between value and stuff (oil). Marx ridicules Roscher's \"nature\" as resembling \"the foolish virgin who admitted she had had a child, but 'only a very little one.'\" He then quotes Roscher's defence of the capitalist's \"abstinence,\" describing it as an \"anatomico-physiological method of political economy, which converts a mere 'demand' into a source of value!\" This is a crucial theoretical boundary: nature and materials produce use-values but no exchange-value — a point that will become the axis of the critique of \"productivity-of-capital\" theories. The chapter's key metaphor appears here: while productive labour changes means of production into constituents of a new product, \"their value undergoes a metempsychosis . It deserts the consumed body to occupy the newly created one. But this transmigration takes place, as it were, behind the back of the actual labour in progress.\" The Pythagorean image is deliberate: value appears to migrate on its own, invisibly, while the living labour is busy doing something else. The Gift of Nature and the Revelatory Function of Crisis On p. 315 Marx draws out the consequence: the worker cannot add new labour without simultaneously preserving old values, because his labour must be of a useful kind, and useful labour requires employing products as means of production. Therefore: \"The property which labour-power in action, living labour, possesses of preserving value, at the same time that it adds it, is a gift of nature which costs the worker nothing, but is very advantageous to the capitalist since it preserves the existing value of his capital.\" The phrase \"gift of nature\" is critical — value-preservation is a gratuitous service that capital appropriates without payment. And its concealed character is revealed by crisis : \"As long as trade is good, the capitalist is too absorbed in making profits to take notice of this gratuitous gift of labour. Violent interruptions of the labour process, crises, make him painfully aware of it.\" Footnote 5 (The Times, 26 November 1862) gives a concrete indictment: a manufacturer with 800 workers complains of £6,000 annual overhead when the mill is idle, including £150 for coal and £1,200 depreciation, because \"the weather and the natural principles of decay do not suspend their operations because the steam-engine ceases to revolve.\" Here Marx shows the living-labour dependence of capital's self-preservation: without labour's preservation function, capital decays. Consumed Use-Value vs. Preserved Value Marx refines the vocabulary (p. 315): as regards means of production, what is really consumed is use-value; labour's consumption of that use-value results in the product. There is \"in fact no consumption of their value,\" so \"it would therefore be inaccurate to say that it is reproduced. It is rather preserved .\" It is preserved not through any operation the means itself undergoes, but because the use-value in which it existed \"vanishes (although when it vanishes, it does so into another use-value).\" Footnote 6 cites S. P. Newman's Productive Consumption , confirming: \"In these instances there is no consumption of value.\" Then the key sentence (p. 316): \"the value of the means of production re-appears in the value of the product, but it is not strictly speaking reproduced in that value. What is produced is a new use-value in which the old exchange-value re-appears.\" This terminology distinguishes value reproduction (genuine creation of new value) from value re-appearance (mere preservation by transfer). The Subjective Factor: Labile Reproduction \"It is otherwise with the subjective factor of the labour process, labour-power, which sets itself in motion independently.\" While concrete labour preserves value, \"at the same time, throughout every instant it is in motion, it is creating an additional value, a new value.\" Marx gives a concrete numeric illustration: suppose the process stops when the worker has produced an equivalent for his own labour-power — say six hours of labour adding three shillings. This three shillings is \"the excess of the total value of the product over the portion of its value contributed by the means of production. It is the only original value formed during this process, the only portion of the value of the product created by the process itself.\" Marx concedes: this new value only replaces the money advanced for labour-power — the money the worker spends on subsistence — so it appears as mere reproduction. \"Nevertheless, it is a real reproduction, and not, as in the case of the value of the means of production, simply an apparent one. The replacement of one value by another is here brought about by the creation of new value.\" Footnote 7 lampoons F. Wayland's American compendium, whose slogan \"'It matters not in what form capital re-appears'\" leads to the absurd claim that the value of food and clothing \"re-appears in that new vigour imparted to his body and mind, forming fresh capital.\" Marx retorts: what re-appears in the man's vigour is not the bread's price but its body-building substance; what re-appears in the value of the vigour is not the substance but the value. Cheap bread at half price would build just as much muscle — but not muscle of the same value. \"This confusion of 'value' and 'vigour'... conceals an attempt, an inevitably vain attempt, to squeeze an explanation of surplus-value out of the mere re-appearance of pre-existing values.\" Here Marx intercepts a specifically crude variant of the same error he attacked in Say and Roscher: substituting physical productivity for value creation. Surplus-Value and the Naming of the Categories On p. 317 Marx shows why the labour process continues beyond the reproduction-equivalent. Six hours suffice to reproduce labour-power's value; but the process lasts twelve. \"The activity of labour-power, therefore, not only reproduces its own value, but produces value over and above this. This surplus-value is the difference between the value of the product and the value of the elements consumed in the formation of the product, in other words the means of production and the labour-power.\" And now the categorical consolidation — the moment the chapter names its subject: \"That part of capital, therefore, which is turned into means of production, i.e. the raw material, the auxiliary material and the instruments of labour, does not undergo any quantitative alteration of value in the process of production. For this reason, I call it the constant part of capital , or more briefly, constant capital .\" \"On the other hand, that part of capital which is turned into labour-power does undergo an alteration of value in the process of production. It both reproduces the equivalent of its own value and produces an excess, a surplus-value, which may itself vary, and be more or less according to circumstances. This part of capital is continually being transformed from a constant into a variable magnitude. I therefore call it the variable part of capital , or more briefly, variable capital .\" The parallel structure is underscored: the same elements distinguishable as objective/subjective in the labour process are distinguishable as constant/variable in the valorization process. This is the taxonomy — c and v — that will structure the analysis of the rate of surplus-value (s/v) and organic composition (c/v) in later chapters. The Value-Change Caveat Marx immediately guards against a pervasive misreading (pp. 317–18): \"The definition of constant capital given above by no means excludes the possibility of a change of value in its elements.\" Example: cotton rises from sixpence to a shilling a pound due to crop failure. Each pound bought at sixpence and worked up after the rise transfers a shilling's worth; cotton already spun and circulating as yarn similarly transfers twice its original value. \"It is plain, however, that these changes of value are independent of the valorization of the cotton in the spinning process itself.\" If the old cotton had never been spun, it could have been resold at a shilling. This isolates the source of change: the value change \"originates not in the process in which the cotton plays the part of a means of production, and in which it therefore functions as constant capital, but in the process in which the cotton itself is produced.\" Marx leverages the point for an empirical aside: speculators, when sudden revaluations occur, \"speculate in the raw material in its least worked-up form\" — in cotton rather than yarn, yarn rather than cloth. This grounds a crucial meta-claim about value theory: \"The value of a commodity is certainly determined by the quantity of labour contained in it, but this quantity is itself socially determined.\" If the labour-time socially necessary for production alters — after a bad harvest a given weight of cotton represents more labour — this \"reacts back on all the old commodities of the same type, because they are only individuals of the same species.\" Their value is measured by the labour socially necessary \"under the social conditions existing at the time.\" Footnote 8 cites Le Trosne: \"all products of the same kind form a single mass, and their price is determined in general and without regard to particular circumstances.\" This is the law of socially necessary labour-time restated in dynamic terms: capital's book-values are continuously revalued by changing social productivity — a harbinger of Marx's later treatment of moral depreciation and technical obsolescence. Parallel reasoning applies to instruments of labour. If a new invention allows machinery of a given kind to be produced with less labour, old machinery \"undergoes a certain amount of depreciation\" and transfers proportionately less value. \"But here too the change in value originates outside the process in which the machine is acting as a means of production. Once engaged in this process the machine cannot transfer more value than it possesses independently of the process.\" Composition Changes Do Not Alter Functional Distinctions The chapter's closing move (p. 319) anticipates a potential objection: does a change in the ratio of constant to variable capital compromise the distinction? Marx shows it does not. The technical conditions may be revolutionized: where \"ten men using ten implements of small value worked up a relatively small quantity of raw material, one man may now, with the aid of one expensive machine, work up one hundred times as much raw material.\" This produces \"an enormous increase in the constant capital... and at the same time a great reduction in the variable part.\" But: \"This change however alters only the quantitative relation between the constant and the variable capital, or the proportion in which the total capital is split up into its constant and variable constituents; it has not in the least degree affected the essential difference between the two.\" This final sentence is programmatic. The qualitative distinction between c and v — between value-preserving dead labour and value-creating living labour — is invariant under any change in their quantitative proportion. This is the analytical bedrock for the later concepts of the organic composition of capital and the tendency of the rate of profit to fall . 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental principle unveiled by Chapter 8 is that value can only be created by living labour, but it can be transferred only through the concrete form of that labour's productive activity . This is not a metaphysical claim but a functional one, and it yields several systemic laws. First, the conservation-and-creation law : means of production never transfer more value than they possess or lose; they are value-conserving while living labour alone is value-generating. Second, the free-gift law : the preservation of capital's value is a gratuitous by-product of labour's concrete usefulness, appropriated by capital without compensation — a hidden subsidy whose existence is typically revealed only when crisis interrupts production and decay proceeds unmitigated. Third, the composition-invariance law : however drastic the technical re-organization of production, the qualitative distinction between constant and variable capital persists. The broader theoretical implications are enormous. This chapter is the birthplace of the c/v schema that anchors the entire subsequent analysis of Capital . Without it, the rate of surplus-value (s/v) would have no denominator, the organic composition of capital (c/v) no numerator, and the theory of the falling rate of profit no foundation. The distinction also resolves a foundational paradox of classical political economy: how capital can be \"productive\" without being a source of value. Marx's answer — capital's apparent productivity is the loan of living labour's creative and preserving power — directly repudiates Adam Smith's resolution of value into wages, profit, and rent into cost components, and demolishes Say's and Roscher's attempts to derive surplus-value from nature's bounty or capital's \"abstinence.\" Philosophically, the chapter's insistence that \"value exists only in use-values, in things\" and that its transference is a \"metempsychosis\" occurring \"behind the back of the actual labour in progress\" articulates a powerful critique of fetishism in motion: value appears self-moving, migrating from material to material, but this self-motion is an illusion sustained by the unacknowledged labor-power that animates dead matter. The analogy between machines and human mortality — \"The instrument suffers the same fate as the man\" — alongside the actuarial reference, underscores that depreciation, like life, is a social accounting over natural decay. Practically, the chapter implies that crises of profitability during production halts are not moral failures of businessmen but structural exposures of the capital-form's reliance on continuously flowing living labour. Theoretically, it supplies the analytical tool by which Marx shows that capitalism's growing technological power — increasing constant capital and shrinking variable capital in proportion — does not liberate capital from its dependence on living labour but deepens it. The distinction drawn here between apparent and real reproduction of value thus carries the entire theoretical weight of surplus-value: only value produced by labour-power constitutes genuine new value; the \"re-appearance\" of material value is a passive preservation. In this single chapter, Marx thereby establishes the closed circuit — dead labour preserves, living labour creates, capital appropriates — that drives the whole machinery of the capitalist mode of production. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 7 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 9"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 9", "title": "Chapter 9", "subtitle": "The Rate of Surplus-Value", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 9/Chapter 9  The Rate of Surplus-Value_explanation.html", "word_count": 6002, "read_time": "30 min read", "tags": ["Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["Rate of Surplus-Value (s/v)", "Surplus Labour Time vs Necessary Labour Time", "Degree of Exploitation of Labour", "Critique of Nassau Senior's 'Last Hour' Dogma", "Surplus Product Proportion"], "text": "Chapter 9 The Rate Of Surplus-Value - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,989 words ⏱️ ~30 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 9 of Capital , Volume One, occupies a pivotal position in Marx's architecture. It sits within Part Three, \"The Production of Absolute Surplus-Value,\" immediately after the chapters that established the labour process, the valorization process, and the crucial distinction between constant and variable capital, and immediately before the long historical chapter on the working day. Its function is to convert a qualitative insight — that surplus-value arises exclusively from the variable portion of capital — into a precise, quantitative measuring instrument. The chapter's core thesis is announced bluntly on its opening pages and defended relentlessly thereafter: the rate of surplus-value (s/v) is \"an exact expression for the degree of exploitation of labour-power by capital, or of the worker by the capitalist.\" Everything else in the chapter — the algebraic manipulation, the farm and factory examples, the demolition of Nassau Senior's \"last hour,\" the definition of the surplus product — exists to establish, illustrate, and defend that single proposition. The central problem the chapter confronts is twofold. First, there is a formal/analytical problem: surplus-value presents itself initially as the mere excess of a product's value over the value of its constituent elements, an excess that seems to belong to the total capital advanced (C = c + v). To isolate its true source, Marx must show that the constant capital c contributes nothing to the creation of new value but merely re-appears in the product, and must therefore be \"posited as zero.\" This is not sleight of hand but a legitimate analytical abstraction, defended by analogy with the chemist ignoring his retorts. Second, and more consequentially, there is an ideological problem : because the surplus is habitually compared to the total capital rather than its variable part, the measured degree of exploitation is systematically understated. In Marx's example, the true rate of 100 percent appears as a mere 18 percent when mis-computed as s/C — a \"pleasant surprise to Mr Carey and other harmonizers.\" The chapter thus doubles as an exposé of how a superficially technical comparison conceals a social relation. The chapter's intellectual objectives are fourfold: (i) to derive the rate of surplus-value from the transformations C → C′, (v + s) = v + Δv; (ii) to demonstrate the equivalence of two languages for the same ratio — objectified labour (s/v) and living labour (surplus labour/necessary labour); (iii) to work through two detailed empirical illustrations (a Manchester spinning mill and Jacob's 1815 agricultural data) to train the reader in the \"novel principles\"; and (iv) to expose the \"last hour\" fallacy as the canonical ideological abuse of the category — the claim that profits depend entirely on the final hour of the working day. Within the broader framework of Marx's project, this chapter supplies the ratio whose multiplication by other determinants will later generate the theory of the rate of profit (explicitly deferred to Volume Three). It also, crucially, links the abstract categories of value to the physical working day: the working day is defined as the sum of necessary labour and surplus labour, and the surplus product as the material embodiment of surplus-value. The chapter thus bridges the pure theory of value with the concrete, historical struggles over the length of the working day — setting the stage for everything that follows. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Algebraic Frame: From C = c + v to C′ = (c + v) + s Marx opens by stating how surplus-value \"presents itself to us first\": as the amount by which the value of the product exceeds the value of its constituent elements. He then decomposes the capital advanced, C, into its two components: c , the sum of money laid out on means of production (raw material, auxiliary material, instruments of labour) — the portion \"turned into constant capital\" — and v , the sum expended on labour-power — the portion \"turned into variable capital.\" The initial identity is therefore C = c + v . His worked example: £500 = £410 constant + £90 variable. When the production process concludes, a commodity emerges whose value is (c + v) + s , where s is surplus-value. With the same figures: value = (£410 + £90) + £90 = £590. The original capital has changed from C to C′ , from £500 to £590, and the difference, £90, is the surplus-value. Since the value of the constituent elements of the product equals the value of the capital advanced, \"it is a mere tautology to say that the excess of the value of the product over the value of its constituent elements is equal to the valorization of the value of the capital advanced.\" Marx immediately announces that this tautology nonetheless requires closer inspection — and the entire first section is that inspection. 2.2 The Tautology, Fixed Capital, and the Malthus Footnote The first complication is fixed capital . Not all of the value of the instruments of labour passes into the product in any given cycle; only the fraction worn away transfers. Marx distinguishes the value of the means of production actually consumed from the total value of the machinery employed . In his illustrative breakdown, c = £410 consists of £312 raw material, £44 auxiliary material, and £54 for machinery wear; but the machinery's total value is £1,054. Only the £54 is \"advanced for the purpose of turning out the product.\" Should one also count the remaining £1,000 that continues to exist in the machinery in its old form, it would have to appear on both sides of the calculation — as advance and as product — yielding £1,500 on one side and £1,590 on the other. The difference, and therefore the surplus-value, would still be £90. This is why Marx stipulates that \"when we refer to constant capital advanced for the production of value, we always mean the value of the means of production actually consumed in the course of production.\" He footnotes Malthus's Principles of Political Economy (2nd edn, 1836, p. 269) to the same effect: \"If we reckon the value of the fixed capital employed as a part of the advances, we must reckon the remaining value of such capital at the end of the year as a part of the annual return.\" The principle is one of consistency — a value that persists must never be counted only on one side. 2.3 The Value-Product and the Legitimacy of Setting c = 0 Returning to C′ = (c + v) + s , Marx draws the decisive distinction between the value of the product and the value-product (\"the new value actually created in the process\"). The value of the product is £590; the value-product is only v + s = £180 (£90 variable + £90 surplus). The £410 constant capital is not created; it \"merely re-appears.\" To isolate the creation of value, Marx posits c = 0 . He defends this through a limiting thought-experiment: imagine branches of industry where the capitalist could dispense with all previously produced means of production — no raw material, no auxiliary material, no instruments — employing only labour-power and materials supplied by nature. Then C = (0 + v) = v, C′ = v + s, and C′ − C = s, \"as before.\" Conversely, if s = 0 — if labour-power produced only its equivalent — then C′ = (c + v) + 0, so C′ = C; the capital would not have valorized itself. The justification for the abstraction is explicitly mathematical: \"This is merely an application of a mathematical rule, employed whenever we operate with constant and variable magnitudes, related to each other only by the symbols of addition and subtraction.\" And it is an everyday practice: to calculate England's profit from the cotton industry, one first deducts what is paid for cotton to the United States, India, Egypt — positing the merely-reappearing capital as a zero magnitude. He reinforces the point with a methodological analogy of remarkable staying power: the fact that retorts and other vessels are necessary to a chemical process does not prevent the chemist from ignoring them when analysing the results . The means of production provide only \"the material in which fluid, value-creating labour-power has to be incorporated\"; neither their nature nor their value matters to the creation of value. All that is needed is \"a sufficient supply of material to absorb the labour expended.\" That material may rise or fall in value, or be valueless in itself, \"like the land and the sea\" — with no influence on the variation in the quantity of value. This is underscored by footnote 2, citing Lucretius — nil posse creari de nihilo (\"out of nothing, nothing can be created\") — and stating the materialist premise: \"'Creation of value' is the transposition of labour-power into labour. Labour-power itself is, above all else, the material of nature transposed into a human organism.\" 2.4 The Metamorphosis of v: Dead Labour into Living Labour A subtler difficulty emerges. In the formula C′ = £410c + £90v + £90s, the £90 figure is \"a given and therefore a constant quantity,\" so treating it as variable seems absurd. Marx's resolution is vital: the £90 is \"merely a symbol for the process undergone by this value.\" What is purchased is a definite quantity of objectified labour — a constant value. But within production, \"the place of the £90 is taken by labour-power which sets itself in motion, dead labour is replaced by living labour, something stagnant by something flowing, a constant by a variable.\" The result is the reproduction of v plus an increment of v. Hence the crucial expression v + s = v + Δv . From the standpoint of capitalist production, \"the whole process appears as the independent motion of what was originally constant value, but has now been transformed into labour-power.\" Marx's dense closing remark — that expressions like \"£90 variable capital\" or \"self-valorizing value\" seem contradictory — dissolves into the observation that they \"express a contradiction immanent in capitalist production.\" This is the germ of the fetishism critique: the capital relation inverts living labour into a predicate of value. 2.5 From Absolute to Relative Magnitude: The Rate of Surplus-Value With c set to zero, the capital advanced reduces from c + v to v , and the value of the product (c + v) + s becomes the value produced v + s . Of the £180 new value, subtracting the £90 variable capital leaves £90 surplus. This £90 is the absolute quantity of surplus-value. The relative quantity — \"the ratio in which the variable capital has valorized its value\" — is given by s/v , here 90/90, or 100 percent. This relative magnitude is what Marx christens the rate of surplus-value (with a footnote noting the English terms \"rate of profit\" and \"rate of interest,\" and forecasting Volume Three's demonstration that \"the rate of profit is no mystery, when one knows the laws of surplus-value\"). 2.6 Necessary Labour and Surplus Labour: Two Languages for One Ratio Marx now re-derives the ratio from the side of labour-time. The worker does not directly produce his own means of subsistence; within a system of social division of labour he produces a particular commodity — yarn, say — whose value equals the value of his means of subsistence (or the money for it). If the value of his daily means of subsistence represents on average 6 hours of objectified labour, he must work 6 hours to produce that value. During that time he produces only an equivalent for the value of his labour-power already advanced — which is why the production of new value looks like mere reproduction . Marx defines: Necessary labour-time : the portion of the working day during which reproduction of the equivalent takes place. Necessary labour : the labour expended in that time — necessary \"for the worker, because independent of the particular social form of his labour; necessary for capital and the capitalist world, because the continued existence of the worker is the basis of that world.\" The second period, in which labour is no longer necessary, is surplus labour-time , and its labour is surplus labour . It creates surplus-value, which \"for the capitalist, has all the charms of something created out of nothing.\" Marx insists that conceiving surplus-value as \"merely a congealed quantity of surplus labour-time, as nothing but objectified surplus labour,\" is as important as conceiving value generally as congealed labour-time. He then delivers the chapter's second foundational equivalence: s/v = surplus labour / necessary labour This states \"the same thing in different ways; in the one case in the form of objectified labour, in the other in the form of living, fluid labour.\" From this it follows immediately that \"the rate of surplus-value is therefore an exact expression for the degree of exploitation of labour-power by capital, or of the worker by the capitalist.\" Footnote 5 acknowledges a terminological doubling: the term \"necessary labour-time,\" previously used for the time required to produce any commodity, is now also used for the time needed to produce the specific commodity labour-power. Marx concedes the inconvenience \"cannot be entirely avoided in any science,\" comparing the higher and lower branches of mathematics. Footnote 4 (added by Engels) reminds the reader that \"in reality it is the worker who does the 'advancing' to the capitalist, not the capitalist to the worker.\" And footnote 6 attacks Wilhelm Roscher — \"Thucydides\" of political economy by his own claim — who held that in the lowest stages of civilization \"it is the strong who compel the weak to be thrifty.\" Marx retorts: thrifty with what — with labour? with surplus products \"which are not even available\"? Such apologies arise, he says, from \"their real ignorance\" and \"an apologetic dread of a scientific analysis of value and surplus-value which might produce a result unpalatable to the powers that be.\" 2.7 The Degree versus the Amount of Exploitation, and the Confusion with the Rate of Profit Two critical caveats now appear. Footnote 7 draws a distinction that must not be glossed: the rate of surplus-value measures the degree of exploitation, but \"it is in no sense an expression for the absolute magnitude of the exploitation.\" If necessary labour = 5 hours and surplus labour = 5 hours, the rate is 100 percent and the amount is 5 hours. If necessary = 6 and surplus = 6, the rate remains 100 percent while the actual amount has increased by 20 percent. The point is that the rate and the absolute magnitude vary independently. The second caveat is the chapter's most politically charged analytic move. With s = £90 and C = £500, \"the usual way of reckoning\" would yield 18 percent — \"a rate so low it might well cause a pleasant surprise to Mr Carey and other harmonizers.\" (Carey is identified in the footnote as a proponent of the view that bourgeois relations of production are inherently harmonious; Marx devoted a section of the Grundrisse to a critique of such \"harmonizers.\") But the true rate is not s/C = s/(c + v) = 90/500, but s/v = 90/90 = 100 percent , \"more than five times the apparent degree of exploitation.\" Marx then notes that even without knowing the length of the working day, the number of workers, or the duration of the labour process, the rate s/v still discloses — through its equivalent expression, surplus labour/necessary labour — the relation between the two parts of the working day: here an equality, 100 percent, meaning the worker works half the day for himself and half for the capitalist. He concludes the methodological recipe: take the total value of the product, posit the constant capital re-appearing in it as zero; what remains is the value actually created. If s is given, deduct it to find v; if v is given, find s; if both are given, compute s/v. 2.8 The Two Numerical Illustrations Example One: The Manchester Spinning Mill (April 1871 data). Marx reports figures supplied by a Manchester manufacturer, chosen (per footnote 8) to replace erroneous data in the first edition. The mill runs 10,000 mule spindles spinning No. 32 yarn from American cotton, producing 1 lb. of yarn per spindle per week — 10,000 lb. weekly. With waste at 6 percent, 10,600 lb. of cotton are consumed (600 lb. wasted). The price of cotton in April 1871 was 7¾d. per lb., so the raw material costs approximately £342 . 10,000 spindles, including preparation machinery and motive power, at £1 per spindle = £10,000 . Depreciation at 10 percent = £1,000 a year = £20 a week . Rent of the building £300 a year = £6 a week . Coal: 100 h.p. indicated, at 4 lb. of coal per horse-power per hour over 60 hours, plus heating coal = 11 tons a week at 8s. 6d. per ton = about £4¾ a week ; gas £1 a week ; oil etc. £4¾ a week . Total auxiliary materials = £10 a week . The constant part of the weekly product's value is therefore £378 . Wages are £52 a week . Yarn at 12¼d. per lb. gives £510 for the 10,000 lb. Surplus-value = £510 − £430 = £80 (where £430 = £378 constant + £52 wages). Setting the constant part to zero leaves £132 as the weekly value created, which equals £52 variable + £80 surplus . The rate of surplus-value is therefore 80/52 = 153 11/13 percent . In a 10-hour working day with average labour this corresponds to necessary labour of 3 11/13 hours and surplus labour of 6 2/13 hours. Example Two: Jacob's Agricultural Calculation (1815). Marx next takes William Jacob's figures for the year 1815 (wheat at 8s. a quarter, average yield 22 bushels per acre), acknowledging in the footnote that they are \"very imperfect\" but sufficient for his purpose. He stipulates the standard assumption: price = value, and surplus-value is distributed under the headings of profit, interest, rent, etc. — headings he declares irrelevant, simply adding them together. The table runs: Value Produced Per Acre Seed £1 9s. 0d. Manure £2 10s. 0d. Wages £3 10s. 0d. Total £7 9s. 0d. Tithes, rates and taxes £1 1s. 0d. Rent £1 8s. 0d. Farmer's profit and interest £1 2s. 0d. Total £3 11s. 0d. Seed (£1 9s.) + manure (£2 10s.) = £3 19s., which is constant capital, posited as zero. Remaining is £3 10s., the variable capital advanced. A new value of £3 10s. + £3 11s. has been produced in its place. Therefore s/v = £3 11s./£3 10s. = 71/70 ≈ 101 percent — \"more than 100 per cent.\" The worker employs more than half his working day producing surplus-value, which various persons then share \"on different pretexts.\" A footnote warns these calculations are merely illustrative, since in Volume Three it will emerge that even with average prices the simple price-equals-value assumption cannot be made. 2.9 The Representation of the Value of the Product by Proportional Parts of the Product Section 2 shifts from measurement to representation . Marx returns to the earlier spinning example: 12-hour day, 20 lb. of yarn, value 30s. Of this, 24s. (eight-tenths) consists of the re-appearance of the value of means of production — 20 lb. of cotton worth 20s. plus 4s. of worn spindle — i.e. constant capital. The remaining 6s. (two-tenths) is new value: 3s. replacing the day's labour-power (variable capital) and 3s. surplus-value: 30s. value of yarn = 24s. constant + 3s. variable + 3s. surplus Since the whole value is contained in the 20 lb. of yarn, its components can be represented as contained in proportional parts of the product . Eight-tenths of the value (24s.) is contained in eight-tenths of the product (16 lb.); within that, 13⅓ lb. represents the 20s. of cotton and 2⅔ lb. the 4s. of spindle wear. The poetic result is that \"the whole of the cotton used up in spinning the 20 lb. of yarn is represented by 13⅓ lb. of yarn,\" and it is as though those 16 lb. contained no labour spent in spinning at all — \"just as if the cotton had converted itself into yarn without any help.\" Conversely, the remaining 4 lb. yam (two-tenths) represents nothing but the new value of 6s. created in the 12-hour process; \"all the value transferred to those 4 lb. from the raw material and instruments of labour consumed was so to speak intercepted in order to be incorporated in the 16 lb. first spun.\" Of this 4 lb., half (2 lb.) represents the 3s. variable, half the 3s. surplus. Marx then translates the representation from the spatial into the temporal sphere. Since 12 hours yield 6s., 60 hours of labour are objectified in 30s. worth of yarn: 16 lb. materialize 48 hours of prior labour; 4 lb. materialize the 12 hours expended in the process. The spinner produces 1⅔ lb. per hour: in 8 hours, 13⅓ lb. (the value of the day's cotton); in the next 1 hour 36 minutes, 2⅔ lb. (the value of the instruments consumed); in the following 1 hour 12 minutes, 2 lb. worth 3s. (equal to the value created in his 6 hours of necessary labour); and in the final 1 hour 12 minutes, another 2 lb. worth 3s. (the surplus-value). The method is \"perfectly correct\" in the abstract — it is the spatial method transferred to the temporal sphere. But Marx warns that it can be \"accompanied by very barbaric notions,\" especially in the heads of those practically interested in valorization and theoretically interested in misunderstanding it. It can suggest that the spinner produces or replaces the value of the cotton in the first 8 hours, the wear of instruments in the next, his wages in the next, and devotes only the \"famous 'last hour'\" to surplus-value. This would make the spinner perform a \"twofold miracle\": producing cotton, spindles, steam-engine, coal, oil while using them to spin, and turning one working day into five. For in the example, producing the raw material and instruments requires 24/6 = 4 working days of 12 hours each, and their conversion into yarn another one — five days in all. 2.10 Senior's \"Last Hour\": Anatomy of an Apologetic Fallacy Section 3 is the chapter's rhetorical and polemical summit. In 1836 Nassau W. Senior — \"the Clauren of the English economists,\" summoned from Oxford to Manchester to learn the political economy he taught — was chosen by the manufacturers as their \"prize-fighter\" against the newly passed Factory Act (1833) and the Ten Hours' Agitation. His lecture was embodied in Letters on the Factory Act, as it Affects the Cotton Manufacture (London, 1837). Senior's \"analysis\": under the law, no mill employing persons under 18 could work more than 11½ hours a day. A manufacturer investing £100,000 — £80,000 in mill and machinery, £20,000 in raw material and wages — with capital turned once a year and gross profits of 15 percent, should return goods worth £115,000. Each of the 23 half-hours of work produces 5/115ths, or 1/23. Of the 23 twenty-thirds constituting £115,000, twenty (= £100,000) simply replace capital; one 23rd (£5,000) makes up for the deterioration of the mill and machinery; and \"the remaining two 23rds... that is the last two of the twenty-three half-hours\" produce the net profit of 10 percent. Extend the day to 13 hours with about £2,600 added circulating capital, and \"the net profit would be more than doubled.\" Reduce by one hour, and net profit is destroyed; reduce by 1½ hours, and even gross profit is destroyed. Footnote 10 reconstructs what Senior really intended: the manufacturer employs the worker for 11½ hours (23 half-hours) multiplied by the working days of the year, yielding an annual product of £115,000. Twenty half-hours yield £100,000, replacing the capital; three half-hours yield £15,000 gross profit; of these, one yields £5,000 for wear and tear; the remaining two — \"the last hour\" — yield £10,000, the net profit. Senior's fallacy is that he \"converts the last 2/23 of the product into portions of the working day itself.\" The footnote also commends Leonard Horner , Factory Inquiry Commissioner in 1833 and Inspector until 1859, whose A Letter to Mr Senior (1837) showed the figures were worth no more than the \"analysis.\" Horner is praised for a life-long contest \"not only with the embittered manufacturers, but also with the Cabinet.\" Marx's rebuttal proceeds in stages: The superfluous-analysis argument. If Senior accepted the manufacturers' outcry that workers spend the best part of the day reproducing the value of buildings, machinery, cotton, and coal, then his analysis was superfluous. There was a simpler answer: work 10 hours instead of 11½, consume proportionally less cotton and machinery — \"You gain just as much as you lose.\" The separation of categories. A question turning exclusively on the relation of net profit to the length of the working day required the manufacturers to place constant capital (buildings, machinery, raw material) on one side of the account and wage capital on the other. The dissection of the \"last hour.\" If the worker produces his wages in the last hour but one and surplus-value in the last hour, and equal periods produce equal values, then since wages and surplus-value are of equal value, he produces his wages in 5¾ hours and net profit in the other 5¾ hours — a rate of 100 percent. The apparent paradox — that the yarn produced in one hour embodies 5¾ hours of labour — dissolves: the use-value produced in an hour is a definite quantity of yarn, but the value of that yarn is measured by 5¾ hours, of which 4¾ were previously embodied in the means of production (without assistance from him) and only one hour is added by him. There is \"no witchcraft\": the worker does not lose a moment reproducing the values of cotton and machinery; rather, \"it is because his labour converts the cotton and the spindles into yarn, because he spins, that the values of the cotton and spindles go over to the yarn of their own accord. This is a result of the quality of his labour, not its quantity.\" He transfers more value in an hour than in half an hour only because he spins up more cotton. The reduction to truism. Senior's claim \"amounts to no more than this, that in the yarn produced by him in 2 working hours... there are incorporated 11½ working hours.\" And Marx's own claim \"that in the first 5¾ hours he produces his wages, and in the last 5¾ hours your net profit, amounts only to this, that you pay him for the former, but not for the latter.\" The class relation is not a technical fact about machines but a fact about payment : \"in speaking of payment of labour, instead of payment of labour-power, I am only using your own slang expression.\" The counterfactual arithmetic. Compare paid with unpaid working time: \"they are related to each other as half a day is to half a day; this gives a rate of 100 per cent, and a very pretty percentage it is.\" Extend the day to 13 hours and surplus labour rises from 5¾ to 7¼ hours, giving a rate of 126 2/23 percent — not the \"more than doubled\" 200 percent the manufacturers anticipated. Reduce the day to 10 hours and surplus labour falls from 5¾ to 4¼ hours, \"a period that still gives a very profitable rate of surplus-value\" (given in the text as 82 14/17 percent). The \"fateful 'last hour'\" — the object of \"more stories than the millenarians about the Day of Judgement\" — \"is 'all bosh.'\" Footnotes 11 and 12 extend the polemic. Footnote 11 contrasts Senior's claim with Dr Andrew Ure's parallel argument in The Philosophy of Manufactures (1835): if children under 18 were turned out an hour sooner from the \"warm and pure moral atmosphere of the factory,\" they would be deprived, through \"idleness and vice,\" of all hope of salvation. It also reports Mr Howell's factory report of 21 May 1855 — \"Had the following ingenious calculation been correct, every cotton factory in the United Kingdom would have been working at a loss since the year 1850\" — and the 1848 flax-spinning petition in which parents claimed that \"an additional hour of leisure will tend more to demoralize the children than otherwise, believing that idleness is the parent of vice.\" The factory report of 31 October 1848 rebuts this with a harrowing description of the flax mills' dust-laden atmosphere, the \"feverish haste of the machinery,\" and children \"fettered for 10 whole hours to such an occupation,\" branding the parents' talk of idleness \"the purest cant, and the most shameless hypocrisy.\" The same report describes the tricks, threats, and falsifications used to obtain such petitions. Footnote 12 notes, sardonically, that Senior did profit from his Manchester journey: in Letters on the Factory Act he makes net gain depend on a single hour of unpaid labour, while a year earlier, in his Outline of Political Economy , he had \"discovered,\" against Ricardo, that profit derives from the labour of the capitalist and interest from his \"abstinence\" — a word Roscher correctly translated as Enthaltung , though others produced the monkish Entsagung (\"renunciation\"). The chapter closes the section by noting that the battle-cry was raised again by James Wilson in The Economist of 15 April 1848 against the Ten Hours' Bill. 2.11 The Surplus Product and its Measure Section 4, brief but conceptually load-bearing, defines the surplus product : the portion of the product that represents surplus-value — one-tenth of the 20 lb., or 2 lb. of yarn, in the earlier example (German Mehrprodukt , French produit net ). The parallel with the rate is exact: just as the rate of surplus-value is determined by its relation not to the total capital but to its variable part, so \"the relative amount of the surplus product is determined by its ratio, not to the remaining part of the total product, but to that part of it in which necessary labour is incorporated.\" Because the production of surplus-value is the determining purpose of capitalist production, the size of a given quantity of wealth must be measured not by its absolute quantity but by the relative magnitude of the surplus product. Finally, Marx establishes the bridge to the following chapters: \"the sum of the necessary labour and the surplus labour, i.e. the sum of the periods of time during which the worker respectively replaces the value of his labour-power and produces the surplus-value, constitutes the absolute extent of his labour-time, i.e. the working day.\" Footnote 13 shows that Ricardo had already grasped the point in an arithmetical register — \"To an individual with a capital of £20,000, whose profits were £2,000 per annum, it would be a matter quite indifferent whether his capital would employ a hundred or a thousand men\" — and quotes Arthur Young's Political Arithmetic (1774): of what use would a province of small independent peasants be, \"except for the mere purpose of breeding men, which taken singly is a most useless purpose?\" It adds T. Hopkins's observation ( On Rent of Land , 1828) about \"the strong inclination... to represent net wealth as beneficial to the labouring class... though it is evidently not on account of being net.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter yields several fundamental principles. First, and decisively: surplus-value arises exclusively from the variable capital , and the rate of surplus-value (s/v) — identical to the ratio of surplus labour to necessary labour — is the exact expression of the degree of exploitation. This is not one metric among others but the measure of a social relation : the rate tells us the proportion of the working day during which the worker labours without payment. In Marx's example, the worker works half the day for himself and half for the capitalist. Second, the chapter exposes a systematic optical illusion built into capitalist accounting. Because the surplus is habitually compared to the total capital advanced, the true rate of 100 percent appears as 18 percent — a fivefold understatement — and this understatement flatters the system and produces \"harmonizers.\" The confusion of s/v with s/C is not innocent error but a structural misrecognition; it is why Marx says Volume Three's theory of the rate of profit is a mystery only if one works in the wrong direction. Third, the chapter establishes a vital distinction between degree and absolute magnitude of exploitation (footnote 7). A constant rate may conceal an expanding absolute extraction. Any theory that reads the rate as a proxy for the total burden will misjudge the historical trajectory. Fourth, the chapter performs a piece of methodological instruction of enduring value: the legitimacy of abstraction. Setting c = 0 is not falsification but analytical isolation — the chemist ignoring his retorts. Marx draws a sharp line between what must be accounted for in the calculation (all value that appears on both sides cancels; the surplus is invariant to the treatment) and what must be accounted for in the explanation (the material precondition — the adequate supply of means of production). The abstraction is rigorous because the omitted term performs no causal work in the creation of new value. Fifth, the chapter contributes to historical materialism a criterion of differentiation: what distinguishes economic formations — slave society, wage-labour society — is \"the form in which this surplus labour is in each case extorted from the immediate producer, the worker.\" The rate and the product are described in the language of value and volume, but they are simultaneously moral-historical categories, and the Roscher footnote makes clear that the apologetic impulse to found surplus-value on the capitalist's \"thrift\" (or, later, his \"abstinence\") is a feature of all ruling-class economies, not an accident of 1836. Sixth, the chapter's critique of the spatial/temporal metaphor of value leaves a general warning: representational devices that are analytically correct can be ideologically weaponized. The mental habit of allocating the working day into blocks that \"replace\" this or that element of capital is the same operation that generates the \"last hour.\" The physician of this disease is the distinction between created value and transferred value — between the labour that adds new value and the labour that merely preserves old value by converting raw materials into products. This is the same principle later dramatized in the \"Trinity Formula\" and the fetishism discussions. Seventh, the section on Senior remains one of the most durable set pieces of political economy. Its method — accepting the opponent's premises, then showing that they fail to support the conclusion — anticipates the entire edifice of the critique of political economy. The concept of \"the last hour\" has become a shorthand for the way interested reasoning mystifies the sources of profit. And Marx's conclusion — that Senior's own numbers, properly read, show a rate of 100 percent that would fall gracefully, not catastrophically, from the loss of one hour — remains, as a piece of immanent refutation, exemplary. Finally, the chapter establishes the conceptual bridge to the working day . By defining the working day as the sum of necessary and surplus labour, and the surplus product as the material measure of wealth, Marx converts a technical ratio into the object of political struggle. The \"Ten Hours' Bill,\" the factory inspections, the petitions, the inspectors' reports — all of the historical material that follows — are the practical form taken by the abstract relations established here. The rate of surplus-value is not merely a way of calculating; it is the numerical face of the class relation, and it is precisely because of this that the \"last hour\" was fabricated — and why it had to be destroyed. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 8 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 10"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 10", "title": "Chapter 10", "subtitle": "The Working Day", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 10/Chapter 10  The Working Day_explanation.html", "word_count": 17465, "read_time": "87 min read", "tags": ["The Working Day & Factory Acts", "Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["The Working Day Limits (Physical & Moral)", "Antinomy of Right against Right: Force Decides", "Capital's Greed for Surplus Labour (Vampire Metaphor)", "Factory Acts & English Working-Class Struggle", "Relay System & Parliamentary Inquiries", "Sanitary Regulations and Capitalist Resistance"], "text": "Chapter 10 The Working Day - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 17,467 words ⏱️ ~87 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER ANALYSIS: MARX, CAPITAL VOL. I, CHAPTER 10 — THE WORKING DAY 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem Chapter 10 of Capital Volume I — \"The Working Day\" — is the longest single chapter in the entire work, and it pivots on a deceptively simple question that the previous nine chapters had deliberately bracketed: once we know that labour-power is bought and sold at its value, and once we know the mechanics by which surplus-value is extracted, how long is the worker actually made to work? Where Chapters 7–9 established the qualitative logic of surplus-value production (the distinction between necessary and surplus labour-time, the rate of surplus-value), Chapter 10 turns to the quantitative frontier: the length of the working day becomes the terrain on which the whole system of exploitation is contested. Marx's core thesis is that the working day is not a fixed, naturally given magnitude but a variable, contested one whose determination is the outcome of class struggle, not of economic law . The nature of commodity exchange itself imposes no limits on the length of the working day; the capitalist, in buying labour-power for a day, has the same right to extract the maximum use-value from it as any buyer of any commodity. Yet the peculiar nature of this commodity — that its consumption produces value — means that the worker also has a legitimate claim to insist that it not be consumed beyond its normal duration. Between equal rights, force decides. The Intellectual Objective The chapter has four major objectives: To demonstrate the antinomy of the working day : two parties with reciprocal rights, both derived from the law of exchange, come into collision; the resolution is historically contingent and political, not economic. To show that surplus labour is not an invention of capitalism — it predates capital, and the comparison with corvée and slavery reveals capitalism's distinctive form of the appetite for surplus labour (unlimited extension of the working day rather than direct physical coercion). To document — with overwhelming empirical evidence from factory inspectors' reports, parliamentary inquiries, and medical testimony — the actual state of the working day in nineteenth-century England , focusing especially on unregulated branches (potteries, matches, baking, lace, wallpaper) and on the barbarism of the unregulated labour process. To trace the historical struggle over the working day from the fourteenth-century Statutes of Labourers (which forcibly extended the working day) through the Factory Acts of 1833–64 (which forcibly limited it), demonstrating that \"force\" — state power — was necessary on both sides, and that the legally limited working day is a hard-won collective achievement of the working class. Fit Within the Broader Framework Chapter 10 belongs to Part III of Volume I (\"The Production of Absolute Surplus-Value\"). Where Chapters 7–9 established that surplus-value can be increased by extending the working day beyond necessary labour-time (absolute surplus-value), Chapter 10 explores the strategy, limits, and struggle surrounding that extension. It is simultaneously: The bridge to Part IV (relative surplus-value), by showing that over-work and industrial decimation force capital to seek productivity increases as an alternative; A historical materialist case study of the transition from pre-capitalist to capitalist exploitation; A foundational statement on the working-class movement : the normal working day is \"the product of a protracted and more or less concealed civil war between the capitalist class and the working class,\" and the English factory workers are \"champions, not only of the English working class, but of the modern working class in general.\" The chapter is also where Marx most explicitly shows that capital is not merely an economic category but a social relation of force , and that capitalism's vaunted \"freedom of labour\" resolves, upon inspection, into the worker's legal freedom to sell his entire active life — \"his very capacity for labour\" — for a subsistence wage. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Fundamental Antinomy: The Working Day as Variable and Contested Marx opens with a return to first principles. The starting assumption is that labour-power is bought and sold at its value , which is determined, like that of all commodities, by the labour-time necessary to reproduce it. If it takes 6 hours to produce the worker's average daily means of subsistence, then 6 hours is the \"necessary\" component of the working day. But — and this is the crux — the extent of the working day itself is not thereby given . The necessary labour-time AB is a fixed quantity; the day itself, AC, is not. Marx formalizes this with a simple but decisive schema: Working day I: A ——— B–C (7 hours, surplus = 1 hour) Working day II: A ——— B——C (9 hours, surplus = 3 hours) Working day III: A ——— B————C (12 hours, surplus = 6 hours) BC represents surplus labour-time , and the ratio $BC / AB$ determines the rate of surplus-value . Since AB = 6 hours: Working day I: $BC/AB = 1/6$, rate = 10%; Working day II: $BC/AB = 3/6$, rate = 50%; Working day III: $BC/AB = 6/6$, rate = 100%. This is why the rate of surplus-value alone cannot tell us the length of the working day. If the rate is 100%, the day might be 8, 10, 12, or more hours — the rate indicates that the two parts are equal in extent but says nothing about their absolute magnitude. The working day is therefore \"capable of being determined, but in and for itself indeterminate.\" The limits of variation. Although variable, the working day has bounds. The minimum limit cannot be determined in any strict sense: if BC = 0, we have the theoretical minimum — pure necessary labour — but under the capitalist mode of production, this is impossible. The working day can never be reduced to bare necessary labour, because that would abolish surplus-value and with it capitalism itself. The maximum limit is conditioned by two factors : Physical limits : within 24 hours, a person can expend only a certain quantum of vital force. Just as a horse can work regularly for only 8 hours a day, so the human organism requires rest, sleep, feeding, washing, clothing. Moral (social) limits : the worker needs time for intellectual and social requirements, whose extent is \"conditioned by the general level of civilization.\" These limits are, however, \"of a very elastic nature\" — and so we find \"working days of many different lengths, of 8, 10, 12, 14, 16 and 18 hours.\" The rights-based antinomy. Marx now develops the dialectic that gives the chapter its dramatic force. The capitalist has bought labour-power at its daily value; the use-value belongs to him throughout one working day. As \"capital personified,\" his soul is \"the soul of capital,\" and capital's sole driving force is the drive to valorize itself — to absorb the greatest possible quantity of surplus labour. Capital is \"dead labour which, vampire-like, lives only by sucking living labour, and lives the more, the more labour it sucks.\" If the worker consumes his disposable time for himself, \"he robs the capitalist.\" The capitalist thus takes his stand on the law of commodity-exchange : like all buyers, he seeks maximum benefit from the use-value of his purchase. \"The time during which the worker works is the time during which the capitalist consumes the labour-power he has bought.\" But then arises the voice of the worker , previously \"stifled in the sound and fury of the production process.\" Marx gives this voice an extended, rhetorically magnificent hearing. The worker's argument runs: The commodity you bought is peculiar: its use creates value, a greater value than it costs. That is why you bought it. What appears to you as self-valorization of capital is, on my side, excess expenditure of labour-power. On the market we know only one law: exchange of commodities. The consumption of the commodity belongs to the buyer, yes — but by the price you pay daily, I must be able to reproduce my labour-power daily, so as to sell it again. Apart from natural deterioration through age, I must be able to work tomorrow with the same normal strength, health and freshness as today. You preach \"saving\" and \"abstinence\" — very well, I will husband my sole wealth, my labour-power. I will expend only as much as is compatible with its normal duration and healthy development. The arithmetic of the argument : if the average worker can live (with reasonable work) 30 years, then the daily value of my labour-power is $1/(365 \\times 30)$ of its total value — i.e., $1/10{,}950$. If you consume it in 10 years, you pay only $1/3{,}650$ instead of $3/3{,}650$ — i.e., only one-third of its daily value — and you therefore rob me every day of two-thirds of the value of my commodity. You pay me for one day's labour-power while you use three days of it. \"That is against our contract and the law of commodity exchange.\" The worker demands a normal working day, not on grounds of sentiment — \"in money matters sentiment is out of place\" — but because, like every other seller, he demands the value of his commodity. He may concede that the capitalist is a model citizen, a member of the R.S.P.C.A., in the odour of sanctity — but \"the thing you represent when you come face to face with me has no heart in its breast. What seems to throb there is my own heartbeat.\" The resolution: force. Both parties are right by the standards of commodity exchange. There is \"an antinomy, of right against right, both equally bearing the seal of the law of exchange. Between equal rights, force decides. Hence, in the history of capitalist production, the establishment of a norm for the working day presents itself as a struggle over the limits of that day, a struggle between collective capital, i.e., the class of capitalists, and collective labour, i.e., the working class.\" This is the theoretical keystone of the chapter: the working day is the locus where the purely economic analysis of surplus-value is revealed as inadequate, and where class struggle — a political, historical, and ultimately coercive force — enters as the determinant. 2.2 Surplus Labour Across Modes of Production: The Boyar and the Manufacturer Having established the antinomy, Marx pivots to a comparative and historical analysis. Capital did not invent surplus labour. Wherever a part of society monopolizes the means of production, the worker — \"free or unfree\" — must add to the labour-time necessary for his own maintenance an extra quantity in order to produce the means of subsistence for the owner. Marx lists the historical forms of this appropriation: \"an Athenian aristocrat, an Etruscan theocrat, a civis romanus, a Norman baron, an American slave-owner, a Wallachian boyar, a modern landlord or a capitalist.\" The principle of variation. In economic formations where use-value rather than exchange-value predominates , surplus labour is restricted by a \"more or less confined set of needs\" — no boundless thirst for surplus labour arises from the character of production itself. Hence in antiquity over-work becomes \"frightful\" only when the aim is to obtain exchange-value in its independent monetary form — i.e., in the production of gold and silver. There, the recognized form of over-work is \"forced labour until death\" (Marx cites Diodorus Siculus on the Egyptian gold mines, where \"no indulgence, no forbearance for the sick, the feeble, the aged... all, forced by blows, must work on until death\"). The world-market catalyst. As soon as peoples whose production still moves within the lower forms of slave-labour, corvée, etc., are drawn into a world market dominated by the capitalist mode of production — whereby the sale of their products for export becomes their principal interest — \"the civilized horrors of over-work are grafted onto the barbaric horrors of slavery, serfdom, etc.\" Marx's key example is Negro labour in the southern United States . As long as production was chiefly directed to local needs, it preserved a \"moderately patriarchal character.\" But as the export of cotton became vital, the over-working of the Negro — \"and sometimes the consumption of his life in seven years of labour\" — became \"a factor in a calculated and calculating system.\" It was no longer a question of obtaining a certain quantity of useful products, but \"the production of surplus-value itself.\" The logic of the corvée in the Danubian Principalities is the same. The corvée as analytically transparent surplus labour. Marx turns to a sustained comparison between the Danubian Principalities and English factories, because the corvée presents surplus labour in \"an independent and immediately perceptible form.\" If the working day consists of 6 hours necessary + 6 hours surplus, the free worker gives the capitalist $6 \\times 6 = 36$ hours of surplus labour per week — equivalent to working 3 days for himself and 3 days gratis. But this fact is not directly visible : surplus and necessary labour are mingled. One can express the relation by saying that in every minute the worker works 30 seconds for himself and 30 seconds for the capitalist — but the two moments are inseparable in time and space. With the corvée , by contrast, the necessary labour the Wallachian peasant performs for his own maintenance is distinctly marked off from his surplus labour for the boyar. One is performed on his own field, the other on the seignorial estate. Both parts of labour-time exist independently, side by side. In the corvée, surplus labour is \"accurately marked off\" from necessary labour. This changes nothing in the quantitative ratio — three days' surplus labour in the week remain three days yielding no equivalent — but it makes it visible . The different form of the appetite. In the capitalist, the appetite for surplus labour appears as the drive for an unlimited extension of the working day . In the boyar, it appears more simply as \"a direct hunt for days of corvée.\" This is a crucial analytical distinction: the quantitative relation of surplus to necessary labour can be identical while the form of exploitation differs. The specific difference of capitalism is that it does not need direct coercion to secure surplus labour — the wage relation, embedded in a mature market society, does the work — but it requires constant state intervention to limit the working day. The Romanian case. Marx traces the historical development of the corvée in the Romanian provinces. Their original mode of production was based on communal property — but not in the Slav or Indian form. Part of the land was cultivated independently as free private property by commune members; another part — the ager publicus — was cultivated in common. The products of common labour served partly as a reserve fund against bad harvests, partly as a \"state treasury\" for war, religion, and communal expenses. In time, military and clerical dignitaries usurped the communal land and, along with it, the obligations owed to it. The labour of free peasants on communal land was transformed into corvée performed for the thieves who had taken that land . This corvée soon developed into a servile relationship in point of fact though not legally, until Russia — \"the liberator of the world\" — \"raised it to the level of a law on the pretext of abolishing serfdom.\" The Reglement organique of 1831, proclaimed by the Russian General Kiselev (who was the Russian viceroy during the occupation of 1828–1834, and himself a boyar-chosen instrument), codified the corvée. Marx's ironic note: \"Thus, at one stroke, Russia both conquered the magnates of the Danubian Principalities and earned the applause of cretinous liberals throughout Europe.\" The arithmetic of the corvée. According to the Reglement organique, every Wallachian peasant owes the \"so-called landlord,\" besides payments in kind: (1) 12 days of labour in general; (2) 1 day of field labour; (3) 1 day of wood-carrying. This appears to be 14 days per year. But \"with deep insight into political economy, the working day is not taken in its ordinary sense, but as the working day necessary to the production of an average daily product\" — and that average daily product is determined \"in such a sly manner that even a Cyclops would be unable to finish the job within 24 hours.\" The Reglement dryly declares that by 12 working days one must understand the product of 36 days of manual labour; by 1 day of field labour , 3 days ; by 1 day of wood-carrying , likewise 3 times as much . The sum total is now 42 days of corvée . To this is added the jobbagio — service due to the lord for emergency requirements. Every village must furnish annually a definite contingent proportional to population. This additional corvée is estimated at 14 days per Wallachian peasant. Thus the prescribed corvée amounts to 56 working days per year . But because of the severe climate, the agricultural year in Wallachia numbers only 210 days , of which: 40 for Sundays and holidays, 30 on average for bad weather, total 70 days not counted, 140 working days remain. The ratio of the corvée to necessary labour is $56/84$ — that is, $66\\tfrac{2}{3}$ percent . Marx notes this is a much smaller rate of surplus-value than that regulating the English agricultural labourer or factory worker. But this is only the legally prescribed corvée. The liberal spirit of evasion. The Reglement organique was \"able to facilitate its own evasion\" in a spirit \"yet more liberal than the English Factory Acts.\" After making 56 days out of 12, the nominal day's work of each of the 56 corvée days is so arranged that a portion must fall on the next day. For instance, an amount of land must be weeded in one day which would require twice as much time. The legal day's work for some kinds of agricultural labour can be interpreted so that \"the day begins in the month of May and ends in the month of October.\" For Moldavia the regulations are even stricter: \"'The 12 corvée days of the Reglement organique,' cried a boyar, drunk with victory, 'amount to 365 days in the year.'\" The English Factory Acts as negative expression. If the Reglement organique is the positive expression of the appetite for surplus labour — every paragraph legalizing it — the English Factory Acts are the negative expression of the same appetite. These laws curb capital's drive toward limitless draining of labour-power by forcibly limiting the working day \"on the authority of the state, but a state ruled by capitalist and landlord.\" Why did the state intervene? Marx identifies three forces: The daily more threatening advance of the working-class movement; The same necessity that forced the manuring of English fields with guano — the exhaustion of the soil in one case, the seizure of the vital force of the nation at its roots in the other; Periodical epidemics and the diminishing military standard of height in France and Germany — evidence cited from Liebig and others that conscript fitness was declining. Marx gives the data: in France the minimum height for infantry fell from 165 cm (pre-1789) to 157 cm (1818) to 156 cm (1832); in Saxony from 178 cm (1780) to 155; in Prussia 157. In Prussia, over nine years, 716 of every 1,000 conscripts were unfit — 317 for deficiency of height, 399 for bodily defects. The specific form of the English Factory Act of 1850 (in force when Marx wrote, 1867). The Act allows 10 hours for the average working day: the first five days, 12 hours from 6 a.m. to 6 p.m., including half an hour for breakfast and an hour for dinner, leaving $10\\tfrac{1}{2}$ working hours; and 8 hours for Saturday, 6 a.m. to 2 p.m., minus half an hour for breakfast. Total: 60 working hours — $10\\tfrac{1}{2}$ for each of the first five days, $7\\tfrac{1}{2}$ for the last. Factory inspectors as statistical witnesses. Marx notes that the guardians of these laws — factory inspectors directly under the Home Secretary, whose reports are published every six months by order of Parliament — provide \"regular and official statistics of the voracious appetite of the capitalists for surplus labour.\" He quotes Inspector L. Horner's famous computation of \"petty pilferings of minutes\": Before 6 a.m.: 15 minutes After 6 p.m.: 15 minutes At breakfast time: 10 minutes At dinner time: 20 minutes Total for five days: 60 minutes per day → 300 minutes On Saturday before 6 a.m.: 15 minutes At breakfast time: 10 minutes After 2 p.m.: 15 minutes Saturday total: 40 minutes Weekly total: 340 minutes = 5 hours 40 minutes Multiplied by 50 working weeks per year (allowing two for holidays and stoppages), this equals 27 working days of stolen time. Or, as another inspector notes, \"Five minutes a day's increased work, multiplied by weeks, are equal to two and a half days of produce in the year\"; and \"an additional hour a day gained by small instalments... is nearly equivalent to working 13 months in the year.\" The counter-cyclical intensification. Marx observes a counterintuitive phenomenon: crises, during which production is interrupted and factories work \"short time,\" do not affect the tendency to extend the working day. On the contrary — \"the less business there is, the more profit has to be made on the business done. The less time spent in work, the more of that time has to be turned into surplus labour-time.\" During the crisis of 1857–58, Horner reported: \"It may seem inconsistent that there should be any over-working at a time when trade is so bad; but that very badness leads to the transgression by unscrupulous men, they get the extra profit of it.\" In Horner's district, 122 mills were given up, 143 were found standing, \"yet over-work is continued beyond the legal hours.\" The same occurred during the cotton crisis of 1861–65. The excuse offered was that workers would not leave the mill at the appointed hour, and compulsion was necessary — especially on Saturday afternoons, when machinery had to be cleaned. Marx's rebuttal: \"if the hands remain in a factory after the machinery has ceased to revolve... they would not have been so employed if sufficient time had been set apart specially for cleaning, etc., either before 6 a.m. or before 2 p.m. on Saturday afternoons.\" The legal arithmetic of evasion. Marx quotes the inspectors' summary: the small amounts of penalty and costs mean that manufacturers calculate \"upon the chance of not being found out; and when they see the small amount of penalty and costs, which those who have been convicted have had to pay, they find that if they should be detected there will still be a considerable balance of gain.\" Where the additional time is gained \"by a multiplication of small thefts in the course of the day, there are insuperable difficulties to the inspectors making out a case.\" These small thefts — described by inspectors as \"petty pilferings of minutes,\" \"snatching a few minutes,\" and in workers' technical language as \"nibbling and cribbling at meal-times\" — are the micro-physics of surplus-value extraction. The famous maxim: \"Moments are the elements of profit.\" The classification of workers as persons. Marx notes the characteristic designation of workers as \"full-timers\" and children under 13 as \"half-timers\" — the worker \"is here nothing more than personified labour-time. All individual distinctions are obliterated in that between 'full-timers' and 'half-timers.'\" 2.3 Branches of English Industry Without Legal Limits to Exploitation Marx now turns from the regulated textile districts to the \"free\" branches — those where exploitation was still (or had recently been) unfettered. These sections provide the most horrifying empirical evidence of the chapter. Lace trade (Nottingham, 1860). Reading from the Daily Telegraph , Marx quotes the statement of Mr Broughton Charlton, county magistrate, chairman of a meeting at the Assembly Rooms, Nottingham, 14 January 1860: \"there was an amount of privation and suffering among that portion of the population connected with the lace trade, unknown in other parts of the kingdom, indeed, in the civilized world... Children of nine or ten years are dragged from their squalid beds at two, three, or four o'clock in the morning and compelled to work for a bare subsistence until ten, eleven, or twelve at night, their limbs wearing away, their frames dwindling, their faces whitening, and their humanity absolutely sinking into a stone-like torpor.\" The Rev. Montagu Valpy called the system \"one of unmitigated slavery, socially, physically, morally, and spiritually.\" The meeting petitioned \"that the period of labour for men shall be diminished to eighteen hours a day\" — a demand Marx contrasts bitterly with the British horror at Virginian and Carolinian cotton planters: \"Is their black-market, their lash, and their barter of human flesh more detestable than this slow sacrifice of humanity which takes place in order that veils and collars may be fabricated for the benefit of capitalists?\" Potteries of Staffordshire. Three parliamentary inquiries over 22 years (Scriven 1841, Greenhow 1860, Longe 1862/63) documented the conditions. Marx quotes the depositions of child workers: William Wood, 9 years old (began at 7 years 10 months), ran moulds from 6 a.m. to about 9 p.m., six days a week — \"I work till 9 o'clock at night six days in the week. I have done so for the last seven or eight weeks.\" Fifteen hours of labour for a child of 7½. J. Murray, 12 : \"I turn jigger and run moulds. I come at 6. Sometimes I come at 4. I worked all night last night, till 6 o'clock this morning. I have not been in bed since the night before last. There were eight or nine other boys working last night. All but one have come this morning. I get 3 shillings and sixpence. I do not get any more for working at night. I worked two nights last week.\" Fernyhough, 10 : \"I have not always an hour [for dinner]. I have only half an hour sometimes: on Thursday, Friday, and Saturday.\" The medical verdict. Dr Greenhow states that the average life-expectancy in the pottery districts of Stoke-on-Trent and Wolstanton is \"extraordinarily low.\" Although only 36.6% of the male population over 20 are employed in the potteries in Stoke, and 30.4% in Wolstanton, more than half the deaths among men of that age in the first district, and nearly two-fifths in the second, are the result of pulmonary diseases among the potters. Dr Boothroyd (Hanley): \"Each successive generation of potters is more dwarfed and less robust than the preceding one.\" Mr McBean: \"Since I began to practise among the potters 25 years ago, I have observed a marked degeneration, especially shown in diminution of stature and breadth.\" Dr J. T. Arledge (senior physician, North Staffordshire Infirmary): \"The potters as a class, both men and women, represent a degenerated population, both physically and morally. They are, as a rule, stunted in growth, ill-shaped, and frequently ill-formed in the chest; they become prematurely old, and are certainly short-lived; they are phlegmatic and bloodless, and exhibit their debility of constitution by obstinate attacks of dyspepsia, and disorders of the liver and kidneys, and by rheumatism. But of all diseases they are especially prone to chest-disease, to pneumonia, phthisis, bronchitis, and asthma. One form would appear peculiar to them, and is known as potter's asthma, or potter's consumption . Scrofula attacking the glands, or bones, or other parts of the body, is a disease of two-thirds or more of the potters... That the 'degenerescence' of the population of this district is not even greater than it is, is due to the constant recruiting from the adjacent country, and intermarriages with more healthy races.\" Mr Charles Parsons, House Surgeon: \"my indignation has been aroused again and again at the sight of poor children whose health has been sacrificed to gratify the avarice of either parents or employers.\" He sums up the causes of disease in the phrase \"long hours.\" Match manufacture. Dates from 1833 (discovery of applying phosphorus to the match itself). Since 1845 it developed rapidly in England. It brought with it tetanus — discovered in 1845 by a Vienna doctor to be peculiar to match makers. Half the workers are children under 13 and young persons under 18. The trade has \"such a bad reputation that only the most miserable part of the working class, half-starved widows and so forth, deliver up their children to it\" — \"ragged, half-starved, untaught children.\" Of the witnesses examined by Commissioner White (1863), 270 were under 18, fifty under 10, ten only 8, and five only 6 years old. Working day: 12 to 14 or 15 hours, night-labour, irregular meal-times, meals mostly taken in the workrooms themselves, \"pestilent with phosphorus.\" Marx: \"Dante would have found the worst horrors in his Inferno surpassed in this industry.\" Wallpaper manufacture. Coarser sorts printed by machine, finer by hand (block printing). Most active months: October to April. Work often lasts, almost uninterruptedly, from 6 a.m. to 10 p.m. or further into the night. Depositions: J. Leach : \"Last winter six out of nineteen girls were away from ill-health at one time from over-work. I have to bawl at them to keep them awake.\" W. Duffy : \"I have seen when the children could none of them keep their eyes open for the work; indeed, none of us could.\" J. Lightbourne, 13 : \"We worked last winter till 9 [evening], and the winter before till 10. I used to cry with sore feet every night last winter.\" G. Apsden : \"That boy of mine... when he was 7 years old I used to carry him on my back to and fro through the snow, and he used to have 16 hours a day... I have often knelt down to feed him as he stood by the machine, for he could not leave it or stop.\" The managing partner's own words. Mr Smith, of a Manchester factory: \"We work on, with no stoppage for meals, so that the day's work of 10½ hours is finished by 4.30 p.m., and all after that is overtime.\" Marx's sarcastic aside: \"(Does this Mr Smith take no meals himself during 10½ hours?)\" Smith adds: \"we seldom leave off working before 6 p.m.... we are really working overtime the whole year round... For all these, children and adults alike (152 children and young persons and 140 adults), the average work for the last 18 months has been at the very least 7 days, 5 hours, or 78½ hours a week. For the six weeks ending 2 May this year (1862), the average was higher — 8 days or 84 hours a week.\" And yet Smith smirks: \"Machine-work is not great.\" Similarly, employers in block printing: \"Hand labour is more healthy than machine-work.\" And Mr Otley, manager of a wallpaper factory: a clause allowing work between 6 a.m. and 9 p.m. \"would suit us(!) very well, but the factory hours, 6 a.m. to 6 p.m., are not suitable. Our machine is always stopped for dinner.\" Marx: \"(What generosity!)\" The logic of the meal-time. Marx exposes the manufacturers' argument that children may eat \"as coal and water are supplied to the steam-engine, soap to wool, oil to the wheel — namely during the process of production itself, as merely auxiliary material for the instruments of labour.\" The Commission's report counters that the fear of \"losing time\" — that is, the time for appropriating the labour of others — is not a sufficient reason. Baking. Marx calls baking \"the most archaic, pre-Christian\" of English trades — a method of production unchanged since the Roman Empire (the practice of making bread by machinery had only recently begun to make its way). Capital, in its early stages, \"at first indifferent towards the technical character of the labour process it seizes control of. At the outset, it takes it as it finds it.\" The adulteration of bread , especially in London, was revealed by the Committee on the adulteration of articles of food (1855–56) and Dr Hassall's Adulterations Detected . The consequence was the Act of 6 August 1860, \"an inoperative law.\" Marx notes that \"alum, either finely powdered or mixed with salt, is a normal article of commerce bearing the significant name of 'baker's stuff.'\" And in a footnote on the general prevalence of \"sophistication,\" he cites Chevallier's catalogue: 6 kinds of adulteration of sugar, 9 of olive oil, 10 of butter, 12 of salt, 19 of milk, 20 of bread, 23 of brandy, 24 of meal, 28 of chocolate, 30 of wine, 32 of coffee. \"Even God Almighty does not escape this fate.\" The journeymen bakers' conditions. From Tremenheere's report (1862) and the Second Report (1863): The London journeyman baker begins work at about 11 p.m. He \"makes the dough\" — a laborious process lasting from half an hour to three-quarters of an hour. He then lies down on the kneading-board and sleeps for about two hours. He then engages in rapid, continuous labour for about five hours — throwing out the dough, \"scaling it off,\" moulding, putting into the oven, taking out the batch, etc. The bakehouse temperature ranges from 75° to 90°+ F. After the bread is made, distribution begins — many hours on foot carrying baskets or wheeling hand-carts. During \"the London season,\" full-priced West End bakers generally begin at 11 p.m. and work through to 8 a.m.; the men then carry bread until 4, 5, 6, or even 7 p.m. \"They may have, after they have done their work, sometimes 5 or 6, sometimes only four or five hours' sleep before they begin again.\" On Fridays they begin at 10 p.m., continuing to 8 p.m. Saturday, or more generally to 4 or 5 a.m. Sunday. On Sundays they must attend twice or three times to make preparations. The \"underselling masters\" (three-fourths of London bakers) have it worse: the men begin Thursday night at 10 p.m. and continue with only slight intermission until late Saturday evening. The classical political economy of the baking trade. Marx quotes George Read's History of Baking (1848): \"The unpaid labour of the men was made the source whereby the competition was carried on.\" And a full-priced baker denounces his underselling competitors: \"They only exist now by first defrauding the public, and next getting 18 hours' work out of their men for 12 hours' wages.\" The adulteration of bread and the formation of a class of underselling bakers developed in England since the beginning of the eighteenth century — as soon as the corporate character of the trade was lost and \"the capitalist stepped behind the nominal master baker in the shape of a miller or a flour factor.\" This laid the foundation for capitalist production, the unlimited extension of the working day, and night work. The verdict. The Commission's report classes journeymen bakers among \"the short-lived workers, who, having by good luck escaped the normal decimation of the children of the working class, rarely reach the age of 42.\" Yet the trade is always \"overwhelmed with applicants\" — from Scotland, the agricultural districts of the West of England, and Germany. The bakeries of Ireland. Marx turns to the Irish bakers' agitation (1858–60). Their movement — supported by the public with \"typically Irish warmth\" — successfully established a rule of exclusive day-labour in Wexford, Kilkenny, Clonmel, Waterford. In Limerick, however, the master bakers (especially the miller bakers) defeated the movement. The Limerick example led to retrogression in Ennis and Tipperary. In Cork, the masters exercised their power of turning the men out of employment. In Dublin, the master bakers \"have succeeded in leading the men into acquiescence in Sunday work and night work, contrary to the convictions of the men.\" The English government's Committee — a government \"armed to the teeth\" in Ireland — merely \"remonstrates, in funereal tones.\" Its argument is worth quoting at length, because it summarizes the bourgeois rationale for a somewhat limited working day: \"The Committee believe that the hours of labour are limited by natural laws, which cannot be violated with impunity. That for master bakers to induce their workmen, by the fear of losing employment, to violate their religious convictions and their better feelings, to disobey the laws of the land, and to disregard public opinion... is calculated to provoke ill-feeling between workmen and masters... and affords an example dangerous to religion, morality, and social order... any constant work beyond 12 hours a day encroaches on the domestic and private life of the working man, and so leads to disastrous moral results... That work beyond 12 hours has a tendency to undermine the health of the working man, and so leads to premature old age and death.\" Scotland and the railways. Marx moves north: the Scottish agricultural labourer protests against his 13–14 hours' work in a very severe climate, with 4 hours' additional work on Sunday — \"in that land of Sabbatarians!\" In London, simultaneously, three railwaymen — a guard, an engine-driver, a signalman — are up before a coroner's jury after a tremendous railway accident that dispatched hundreds of passengers to the next world. They testified that ten or twelve years before, their labour lasted only 8 hours a day. In the last five or six years it had been \"screwed up to 14, 18 and 20 hours,\" and during holiday pressure often \"40 or 50 hours without a break.\" Marx: \"They are ordinary men, not Cyclops. At a certain point their labour-power ran out. Torpor seized them. Their brains stopped thinking, their eyes stopped seeing.\" The \"respectable British juryman\" sent them to the Assizes on a charge of manslaughter, while piously hoping the capitalist railway magnates would in future be more \"extravagant\" in purchasing labour-power. Marx cites the reply of a North Staffordshire worker: a fireman on duty 14 hours 50 minutes, then recalled before he could drink his tea, then on duty 14 hours 25 minutes — total 29 hours 15 minutes without intermission; the week's total: 88 hours 40 minutes. He was paid for 6½ days and refused extra compensation beyond 10d. The milliner and the blacksmith. Two final figures \"whose striking contrast proves that all men are alike in the face of capital.\" Mary Anne Walkley, 20 years old , employed in a \"highly respectable\" dressmaking establishment kept by \"a lady with the pleasant name of Elise.\" In the last week of June 1863, the London papers published the story: \"Death from simple over-work.\" The girls work on average 16½ hours without a break, during the season often 30. The height of the season required magnificent dresses for the ball in honor of the newly imported Princess of Wales. Mary Anne Walkley had worked uninterruptedly for 26½ hours, with sixty other girls, thirty in each room. The rooms provided only ⅓ of the necessary quantity of air. At night the girls slept in pairs in \"stifling holes\" divided by wooden partitions. She fell ill Friday, died Sunday — without, to the astonishment of Madame Elise, having finished the bit of finery. The doctor, Mr Keys, deposed: \"Mary Anne Walkley died from long hours of work in an overcrowded work-room, and a too small and badly ventilated bedroom.\" The coroner's jury brought in a verdict that she died of apoplexy, \"but there was reason to fear that her death had been accelerated by over-work.\" Even the Morning Star — organ of Cobden and Bright — exclaimed: \"Our white slaves, who are toiled into the grave, for most part silently pine and die.\" The blacksmith. \"If the poets were true, there is no man so hearty, so merry as the blacksmith\" — rising early, striking sparks before the sun, eating, drinking, sleeping. Working in moderation he is in one of the best of human positions, physically. But in the city, \"the stress of work on that strong man\" — in Marylebone, blacksmiths die at 31 per thousand per annum, or 11 above the mean for male adults of the country. \"He can strike so many blows per day, walk so many steps, breathe so many breaths, produce so much work, and live an average, say, of fifty years; he is made to strike so many more blows, to walk so many more steps, to breathe so many more breaths per day, and to increase altogether a fourth of his life. He meets the effort; the result is, that producing for a limited time a fourth more work, he dies at 37 for 50.\" 2.4 Day-Work, Night-Work, and the Shift System Marx now turns to the question of the natural day and the drive to appropriate labour throughout the entire 24 hours. The economic logic. Constant capital — the means of production — exists, from the standpoint of valorization, only in order to absorb labour and, with every drop of labour, a proportional quantity of surplus labour. If the means of production fail to do this, \"their mere existence forms a loss for the capitalist\" — they represent a useless advance of capital while they lie fallow. This loss becomes positive as soon as interruption necessitates additional outlay when work begins again. The impossibility of individual 24-hour labour. Capitalist production therefore drives, \"by its inherent nature, towards the appropriation of labour throughout the whole of the 24 hours in the day.\" But since it is physically impossible to exploit the same individual labour-power constantly, night and day, capital must overcome this obstacle through alternation between labour-powers used by day and by night. This is the shift-system — \"this alternation of two sets of workers.\" Marx notes that the shift-system \"predominated in the full-blooded springtime of the English cotton industry\" and still flourished in the cotton-spinning factories of the Moscow gubernia. The 24-hour process exists as a system in many \"as yet 'free'\" branches of industry in Britain — in blast-furnaces, forges, rolling mills, and other metallurgical establishments. Here the labour process includes a great part of the 24 hours of Sunday, in addition to the 24 hours of the six working days. Workers include men and women, adults and children of both sexes; ages run from 8 (in some cases 6) to 18. In some branches, girls and women work through the night with the male personnel. The medical evidence on night-work. Marx quotes a steel manufacturer: \"It seems but natural that boys who work at night cannot sleep and get proper rest by day, but will be running about.\" Dr W. Strange on sunlight: \"Light also acts upon the tissues of the body directly in hardening them and supporting their elasticity. The muscles of animals, when they are deprived of a proper amount of light, become soft and inelastic, the nervous power loses its tone from defective stimulation, and the elaboration of all growth seems to be perverted... In the case of children, constant access to plenty of light during the day, and to the direct rays of the sun for a part of it, is most essential to health.\" And in a letter to Commissioner White: \"I have had opportunities formerly, when in Lancashire, of observing the effects of night-work upon children, and I have no hesitation in saying, contrary to what some employers were fond of asserting, those who were subjected to it soon suffered in their health.\" The over-work beyond the already-excessive norm. Marx quotes the official English report on boys of 9 to 12: \"It is impossible for any mind to realize the amount of work described in the following passages as being performed by boys of from 9 to 12 years of age... without coming irresistibly to the conclusion that such abuses of the power of parents and of employers can no longer be allowed to exist.\" Examples: A boy at a rolling-mill where the proper hours were 6 a.m. to 5.30 p.m. worked four nights every week till 8.30 p.m. at least — for six months. Another, at 9 years old, sometimes made three 12-hour shifts running; at 10, two days and two nights running. A boy of 10 worked from 6 a.m. to 12 p.m. three nights, and till 9 p.m. the other nights. Another, 13, worked from 6 p.m. till 12 noon next day, for a week at a time, sometimes three shifts together, e.g., Monday morning to Tuesday night. Another, 12, worked in an iron foundry at Staveley from 6 a.m. to 12 p.m. for a fortnight on end; \"could not do it any more.\" George Allinsworth, age 9 , came as cellar-boy Friday; next morning began at 3, \"so I stopped here all night. Live five miles off. Slept on the floor of the furnace, over head, with an apron under me, and a bit of a jacket over me. The two other days I have been here at 6 a.m.\" The illiteracy and degraded mind of the \"labour-powers.\" Marx quotes the disturbing dialogues with one of the Commissioners. Jeremiah Haynes, 12: \"Four times four is eight; four fours are sixteen. A king is him that has all the money and gold. We have a King (told it is a Queen), they call her the Princess Alexandra... A Princess is a man.\" William Turner, 12: \"Don't live in England. Think it is a country, but didn't know before.\" John Morris, 14: \"Have heard say that God made the world, and that all the people was drowned but one; heard say that one was a little bird.\" William Smith, 15: \"God made man, man made woman.\" Edward Taylor, 15: \"Do not know of London.\" Henry Matthewman, 17: \"Had been to chapel, but missed a good many times lately. One name that they preached about was Jesus Christ, but I cannot say any others, and I cannot tell anything about him. He was not killed, but died like other people.\" \"The devil is a good person. I don't know where he lives.\" \"Christ was a wicked man.\" One girl spelt God as dog and did not know the name of the queen. In the paper factories, night-work is the rule for all processes except rag-sorting. In some cases night-work is carried on incessantly through the whole week, by means of shifts, from Sunday night until midnight of the following Saturday. Various relay patterns exist — the 12-hour system, the 24-hour system, the \"intermediate\" system where all workers work 15 or 16 hours every day. Children under 13, young persons under 18, and women work under this night system. In glazing, girls of 12 work 14 hours a day for the whole month, \"without any regular relief or cessation beyond two, or, at most, three breaks of half an hour each for meals.\" The self-representation of capital. Marx now gives what he calls capital's own \"regard\" for the 24-hour system — in its \"normal\" form. Messrs Naylor and Vickers, steel manufacturers (600–700 workers): \"The boys do not suffer from the heat. The temperature is probably from 86 degrees to 90 degrees... About twenty of the boys under the age of 18 work in the night sets... We could not well do without lads under 18 working by night. The objection would be the increase in the cost of production... of lads we could get any number... But from the small proportion of boys that we employ, the subject is of little importance or interest to us.\" Messrs John Brown & Co. (Mr J. Ellis): \"I do not think it would be very objectionable to require that no person under the age of 18 should work more than 12 hours in the 24. But we do not think that any line could be drawn over the age of 12, at which boys could be dispensed with for night-work. But we would sooner be prevented from employing boys under the age of 13, or even so high as 14, at all, than not be allowed to employ boys that we do have at night.\" Marx's gloss: \"the increase of expense... would be more than the trade, with due regard to its being successfully carried out, could fairly bear.\" Marx translates: \"Labour is scarce here, and might fall short if there were such a regulation\" — \"(In other words, Ellis, or Brown & Co., might be subjected to the fatal embarrassment of having to pay labour-power at its full value.)\" Cammell & Co.'s \"Cyclopean Steel and Iron Works\" — the managing director's written evidence, later suppressed on revision, recalled by Mr White: prohibition of night-labour of children and young persons \"would be impossible, it would be tantamount to stopping their works,\" and yet their business employs little more than 6% of boys under 18, and less than 1% under 13. Sanderson's confession of the real reason. E. F. Sanderson: \"Great difficulty would be caused by preventing boys under 18 from working at night. The chief would be the increase of cost from employing men instead of boys... probably it would not be enough to enable the manufacturers to raise the price of steel, and consequently it would fall on them, as of course the men (how wrong-headed these people are!) would refuse to pay it.\" Boys get 4s. to 5s. a week. \"The boys' work is of a kind for which the strength of boys is generally... quite sufficient, and consequently there would be no gain in the greater strength of the men to counterbalance the loss... The men would not like so well not to have boys under them, as men would be less obedient. Besides, boys must begin young to learn the trade. Leaving day-work alone open to boys would not answer the purpose.\" Why could the boys not learn by day? \"Owing to the men working days and nights in alternate weeks, the men would be separated half the time from their boys, and would lose half the profit which they make from them. The training which they give to an apprentice is considered as part of the return for the boys' labour, and thus enables the men to get it at a cheaper rate. Each man would want half of this profit.\" In other words, Messrs Sanderson would have to pay part of the adult men's wages out of their own pockets instead of by the night-work of the boys — their profit would fall. Sanderson's final argument. Night-work on men alone would be intolerable; the difficulties would lead to total suppression of night-work. \"As far as the work itself is concerned, this would suit as well, but —\" Marx comments: \"But Messrs Sanderson have something else to make besides steel. Steel-making is simply a pretext for profit-making. The steel furnaces, rolling-mills, etc., the buildings, machinery, iron, coal, etc., have something more to do than transform themselves into steel. They are there to absorb surplus labour, and they naturally absorb more in 24 hours than in 12... as soon as there is an interruption in their function of absorbing labour, they lose their character as capital, and are therefore a pure loss for the Sandersons.\" Sanderson: \"there would be the loss from so much expensive machinery, lying idle half the time, and to get through the amount of work which we are able to do on the present system, we should have to double our premises and plant, which would double the outlay.\" Marx replies: \"But why should these Sandersons pretend to a privilege not enjoyed by the other capitalists who only work during the day, and whose buildings, machinery, raw material, therefore lie 'idle' during the night?\" Sanderson's answer: \"It is true that there is this loss from machinery lying idle in those manufactories in which work only goes on by day. But the use of furnaces would involve a further loss in our case. If they were kept up these would be a waste of fuel (instead of the present waste of the living substance of the workers) and if they were not, there would be loss of time in laying the fires and getting the heat up (whereas a loss of sleeping time, even that of 8-year-olds, is a gain of working time for the Sanderson clan), and the furnaces themselves would suffer from the changes of temperature (whereas those same furnaces suffer nothing from the alternation of day-work and night-work).\" The glass manufacturers' tenderness. Marx quotes Commissioner White's rebuttal of the glass manufacturers, who maintained that \"regular meal-times\" for children were impossible because they would cause a \"pure loss\" or a \"waste\" of heat radiated by the furnaces: \"A certain amount of heat beyond what is usual at present might also be going to waste, if meal-times were secured in these cases, but it seems likely not equal in money-value to the waste of animal power now going on in glass-houses throughout the kingdom from growing boys not having enough quiet time to eat their meals at ease, with a little rest afterwards for digestion.\" Marx: \"And this in 1865, 'the year of progress'!\" Children in the glass-sheds walk 15 to 20 miles in every 6 hours, performing their work continuously; often the work lasts for 14 or 15 hours. In the 6-hour shift system, the unbroken period is at most 6 hours, from which must come time spent in coming and going, washing, dressing, and meals — \"leaving a very short period indeed for rest, and none for fresh air and play, unless at the expense of the sleep necessary for young boys.\" The vignette closes with a brilliant satirical image: \"Meanwhile, late at night perhaps, Mr Glass-Capital, stuffed full with abstinence, and primed with port wine, reels home from his club, droning out idiotically 'Britons never, never shall be slaves!'\" 2.5 The Historic Battle: From Statutory Extension to Statutory Limitation (14th–17th c.) Marx now turns to the historical struggle, and identifies a central paradox: Section 5 establishes the opposite tendency of early legislation. From the middle of the fourteenth to well into the middle of the eighteenth century, English Labour Statutes compulsorily lengthened the working day. Modern Factory Acts compulsorily shorten it. \"While the modern Factory Acts compulsorily shorten the working day, the earlier statutes tried forcibly to lengthen it.\" Why the difference? In its embryonic state, when capital \"cannot yet use the sheer force of economic relations to secure its right to absorb a sufficient quantity of surplus labour,\" it must be aided by the power of the state. In its adult condition, the state becomes an obstacle. \"Centuries are required before the 'free' worker, owing to the greater development of the capitalist mode of production, makes a voluntary agreement, i.e., is compelled by social conditions to sell the whole of his active life, his very capacity for labour, in return for the price of his customary means of subsistence, to sell his birthright for a mess of pottage.\" Hence the longer working day imposed by state power from the mid-fourteenth to the late seventeenth centuries is approximately the same length as the shorter working day which, in the second half of the nineteenth century, the state has here and there interposed as a barrier. The Massachusetts comparison. \"What has now been proclaimed, for instance in the State of Massachusetts, until recently the freest state of the North American republic, as the statutory limit of the labour of children under 12, was in England, even in the middle of the seventeenth century, the normal working day of able-bodied artisans, robust ploughmen and gigantic blacksmiths.\" The Statutes of Labourers. - 23 Edward III, 1349 — the first \"Statute of Labourers.\" Its immediate pretext was the Black Plague, but its cause is deeper. A Tory writer: \"The difficulty of getting men to work on reasonable terms\" (i.e., at a price yielding the employer a reasonable quantity of surplus labour) \"grew to such a height as to be quite intolerable.\" Reasonable wages were fixed by law, as were the limits of the working day. - Statute of 1496 (Henry VII) : the working day for all craftsmen and field labourers from March to September was supposed to last from 5 a.m. to between 7 and 8 p.m. Meal-times: 1 hour for breakfast, 1½ hours for dinner, half an hour for \"noon-meate\" — exactly twice as much as under the Factory Acts now in force . In winter, work from 5 a.m. until dark, with the same intervals. - Statute of Elizabeth, 1562 : leaves the length of the working day untouched but limits intervals to 2½ hours in summer and 2 in winter. Dinner only 1 hour; the \"afternoon-sleep of half an hour\" only between mid-May and mid-August. Every hour of absence: 1d. subtracted. The gap between statute and practice. Marx notes that in practice conditions were much more favourable to labourers than in the statute-book. William Petty, in the last third of the seventeenth century, says labouring men work ten hours per diem and make twenty meals per week — three on working days, two on Sundays — \"whereby it is plain, that if they could fast on Friday nights, and dine in one hour and an half, whereas they take two, from eleven to one; thereby thus working 2½ more, and spending 2½ less, the above-mentioned tax might be raised.\" Marx notes drily: \"Was Dr Andrew Ure not right when he deplored the Twelve Hours' Bill of 1833 as a retrogression to the age of darkness?\" Child labour at the end of the seventeenth century. The complaint: \"Our youth, here in England, do absolutely nothing before they come to be apprentices, and then they naturally require a long time — seven years — to be formed into complete craftsmen.\" Germany, on the other hand, is praised because children are educated from their cradle \"at least to something of employment.\" The eighteenth-century controversy over the working week. Still, until the epoch of large-scale industry, capital in England had not gained control of the worker's whole week. The fact that workers could live for a week on four days' wages did not seem to them a sufficient reason to work the other two days for the capitalist. Postlethwayt's defense of the workers. Marx recounts the polemic. Postlethwayt (in Great Britain's Commercial Interest Explained and Improved ): the workers' \"relaxation of their own way\" is what preserved their ingenuity and dexterity. \"Many of them will execute as much work in four days as a Frenchman will in five or six. But if Englishmen are to be eternal drudges, 'tis to be feared they will degenerate below the Frenchmen.\" The anonymous author of the Essay on Trade and Commerce (1770) replies : \"That mankind in general, are naturally inclined to ease and indolence, we fatally experience to be true.\" If a bushel of wheat costs 5 shillings and the worker earns a shilling a day, he would work five days only; but as wages are higher relative to necessaries, the manufacturer who labours four days has a surplus to live idle the rest of the week. \"I hope I have said enough to make it appear that the moderate labour of six days in a week is no slavery.\" And: \"The labouring people should never think themselves independent of their superiors... The cure will not be perfect, till our manufacturing poor are contented to labour six days for the same sum which they now earn in four days.\" The 'Ideal Workhouse' proposal. To this end, and for \"extirpating idleness, debauchery and excess,\" promoting a spirit of industry, \"lowering the price of labour in our manufactories, and easing the lands of the heavy burden of poor's rates,\" the author proposes to lock up paupers in an \"Ideal Workhouse\" — a \"House of Terror.\" \"The poor shall work 14 hours in a day, allowing proper time for meals, in such a manner that there shall remain 12 hours of neat labour.\" Marx's punchline: Twelve working hours a day in the \"Ideal Workhouse\" of 1770! Sixty-three years later, in 1833, when Parliament reduced the working day for children of 13 to 18 to 12 full hours, in four branches of industry, \"the Day of Judgement seemed to have dawned for English industry!\" In 1852, when Louis Bonaparte sought to secure his position by tampering with the legal working day, the people of France cried: \"the law that limits the working day to 12 hours is the one good that has remained to us of the legislation of the Republic.\" At Zurich, work of children over 10 is limited to 12 hours; in Aargau in 1862, children between 13 and 16 had their hours reduced from 12½ to 12; in Austria in 1860, the same reduction for children between 14 and 16. \"'What progress since 1770,' Macaulay might shout 'with exultation'.\" The \"House of Terror\" realized. \"The 'House of Terror' for paupers, only dreamed of by the capitalist mind in 1770, was brought into being a few years later in the shape of a gigantic 'workhouse' for the industrial worker himself. It was called the factory. And this time the ideal was a pale shadow compared with the reality.\" 2.6 The Factory Legislation of 1833–64: Struggle, Relay System, Capital's Revolt Marx now narrates the century-long civil war over the working day in England. The period of unregulated orgies. After capital had taken centuries to extend the working day to its normal maximum (12 hours), the birth of large-scale industry in the last third of the eighteenth century produced \"an avalanche of violent and unmeasured encroachments. Every boundary set by morality and nature, age and sex, day and night, was broken down.\" Even the ideas of day and night became confused: as late as 1860, an English judge needed \"the penetration of an interpreter of the Talmud\" to explain judicially what was day and what was night. \"Capital was celebrating its orgies.\" The first resistance. As soon as the working class had recovered from the initial shock, it began to resist — first in England, the native land of large-scale industry. But for three decades (1802–1833) the concessions wrung from industry remained \"purely nominal.\" Parliament passed five Labour Laws, \"but was shrewd enough not to vote a penny for their compulsory implementation.\" They remained a dead letter. \"The fact is, that prior to the Act of 1833, young persons and children were worked all night, all day, or both ad libitum.\" The Factory Act of 1833. This is the first factory act \"of any real substance.\" It covers cotton, wool, flax, and silk factories. Its provisions: Ordinary factory working day: 5.30 a.m. to 8.30 p.m. (15 hours); Within these limits, it is lawful to employ young persons (13–18) at any time of the day, provided no individual young person works more than 12 hours in any one day; Sixth chapter: \"There shall be allowed in the course of every day not less than one and a half hours for meals\"; Employment of children under 9 forbidden; Work of children between 9 and 13 limited to 8 hours a day; Night-work (between 8.30 p.m. and 5.30 a.m.) forbidden for all persons between 9 and 18. The \"system of relays\" introduced. The law-makers did not wish to interfere with adult male labour — \"or, as they called it, 'the freedom of labour'\" — so they created a special system to prevent the Factory Acts from having \"such a frightful consequence\" (the limitation of adult labour). The Central Board of the Commission (28 June 1833): \"The great evil of the factory system as at present conducted has appeared to us to be that it entails the necessity of continuing the labour of children to the utmost length of that of the adults. The only remedy for this evil, short of the limitation of the labour of adults, which would, in our opinion, create an evil greater than that which is sought to be remedied, appears to be the plan of working double sets of children.\" This \"plan of relays\" — one set of children 5.30 a.m. to 1.30 p.m., another 1.30 p.m. to 8.30 p.m. — was intended to permit the adult working day to remain 15 hours. The gilded pill. To reward the manufacturers for having ignored all previous child-labour acts, Parliament decreed that after 1 March 1834 no child under 11, after 1 March 1835 no child under 12, and after 1 March 1836 no child under 13 was to work more than 8 hours — i.e., delayed the effective age limit. \"This 'liberalism,' so full of consideration for 'capital,'\" Marx writes, \"was the more noteworthy in that Dr Farre, Sir A. Carlisle, Sir B. Brodie, Sir C. Bell, Mr Guthrie, etc., in a word, the most distinguished physicians and surgeons in London, had declared in their evidence before the House of Commons that there was danger in delay.\" Dr Farre was blunter: \"Legislation is necessary for the prevention of death, in any form in which it can be prematurely inflicted, and certainly this (the factory method) must be viewed as a most cruel mode of inflicting it.\" The hypocrisy of the Emancipation Act. The same \"reformed\" Parliament, which in its delicate consideration for manufacturers condemned children under 13 to 72 hours of factory labour every week, in the Emancipation Act \"forbade the planters, from the very beginning, to work any Negro slave for more than 45 hours a week.\" Capital's agitation on the age limit. Capital began a noisy, long-lasting agitation against the age-limit of \"children\" (restricted to 8 hours and subject to compulsory education). \"According to the anthropology of the capitalists, the age of childhood ended at 10, or, at the outside, 11.\" By 1835 they had intimidated the government into proposing to lower the age of childhood from 13 to 12. But \"the 'pressure from without' became more threatening\" — the House of Commons refused, and the Act of 1833 came into full operation, remaining unaltered until June 1844. The new \"relay system\" of evasion. Under the 1833 Act, the timing of each \"young person's\" or \"child's\" work within the 15-hour day was left to the free decision of the lords of capital. This permitted a new \"system of relays\" — \"the work-horses were not changed at fixed stations, but were always re-harnessed at different stations.\" Marx: \"It annulled the whole Factory Act, not only in the spirit, but in the letter.\" The factory inspectors, with complex bookkeeping for each individual child, could not enforce the legal hours nor compel the legal meal-times. In 1844, the inspectors demonstrated to the Home Secretary the impossibility of any control under the newly invented relay system. Changing circumstances and the 1844 Act. Meanwhile, the factory workers had made the Ten Hours' Bill their economic, as they had made the Charter their political, election cry. Some manufacturers who had complied with the 1833 Act complained of the \"immoral competition\" of \"false brethren.\" The spokesmen of the manufacturing class began to change their language and attitude towards the workers — they had started their campaign to repeal the Corn Laws and needed workers' help. They promised not only that the loaf of bread would be twice its size but that the Ten Hours' Bill would be enacted in the free trade millennium. The Tories, threatened in their interest in the rent of land, \"thundered with philanthropic indignation against the 'nefarious practices' of their foes.\" The Factory Act of 7 June 1844 (effective 10 September 1844): Placed under protection a new category — women over 18 — on the same footing as young persons: working hours limited to 12, night-work forbidden; For the first time, the labour of adults was directly and officially controlled by legislation. The Factory Report of 1844–45 states ironically: \"No instances have come to my knowledge of adult women having expressed any regret at their rights being thus far interfered with\"; Working hours of children under 13 reduced to 6½, in certain circumstances to 7; To abolish the abuses of the \"relay system,\" the law established: working hours to be reckoned from the time when any child or young person begins work in the morning (so that if A begins at 8 and B at 10, B's day must end with A's); time regulated by a public clock; a printed notice stating hours for beginning/ending work and pauses; children beginning before 12 noon may not again be employed after 1 p.m. (afternoon shift must consist of different children); of the 1½ hours for meals, \"one hour thereof at the least shall be given before three of the clock in the afternoon... and at the same period of the day\"; no child or young person shall be employed more than 5 hours before 1 p.m. without an interval of at least 30 minutes; no child or young person or female shall be employed or remain in any room where manufacturing is being carried on during meal-times. Marx's central methodological point: these highly detailed specifications, regulating times, limits, and pauses by the stroke of the clock, \"were by no means a product of the fantasy of Members of Parliament. They developed gradually out of circumstances as natural laws of the modern mode of production. Their formulation, official recognition and proclamation by the state were the result of a long class struggle.\" One of their first consequences: the working day of adult males in factories became subject to the same limitations, since in most processes the cooperation of children, young persons, and women is indispensable. Thus, from 1844 to 1847, the 12-hour working day became universal in all branches under the Factory Act. Capital's compensating \"retrogression\". At the manufacturers' instigation, the House of Commons reduced the minimum age for exploitable children from 9 to 8, \"in order to ensure that 'additional supply of factory children' which is owed to the capitalists.\" The Ten Hours' Bill enacted (1847). The years 1846–47 are \"epoch-making.\" The Corn Laws were repealed; duties on cotton and other raw materials removed; free trade proclaimed. In the same years, the Chartist movement and the ten hours' agitation reached their peak. \"Despite the fanatical opposition of the army of perjured Free traders, headed by Bright and Cobden, the Ten Hours' Bill, so long struggled for, made its way through Parliament.\" The Factory Act of 8 June 1847 provided: - 1 July 1847: preliminary reduction of the working day for \"young persons\" (13–18) and all females to 11 hours; - 1 May 1848: definite limitation to 10 hours. Capital's campaign to prevent implementation. Capital undertook a preliminary campaign to prevent the Act coming into full force on 1 May 1848. The moment was cleverly chosen: after two years of great suffering (the crisis of 1846–47), factory operatives were in narrow circumstances, many in debt. Manufacturers aggravated the situation by a general 10% reduction in wages as a \"celebration\" of the new free-trade era, then a further reduction of 8⅓% when hours were shortened to 11, then a reduction of twice that amount when shortened to 10. Wherever circumstances permitted, a reduction in wages of at least 25% took place. The failure of the wage-reduction strategy. Under these prepared conditions, the agitation for repeal of the 1847 Act began. But no method of deceit, seduction, or intimidation could bring the workers to demand their own over-working. In relation to the half-dozen petitions complaining of \"oppression by the Act,\" the petitioners declared under oral examination that their signatures had been extorted. \"They felt themselves oppressed, but by something different from the Factory Act.\" The inspectors' inquiries. Leonard Horner conducted many examinations. Of the workers examined (10,270 adult male labourers in 181 factories in Horner's district), about 70% declared in favour of 10 hours , a smaller percentage for 11, and an insignificant minority for the old 12. On the \"friendly\" trick of making adult males work 12–15 hours to fake a demonstration of workers' preferences: the majority of \"overtimers\" declared: \"They would much prefer working 10 hours for less wages, but they had no choice; so many were out of employment... that if they refused to work the longer time, others would immediately get their places, so that it was a question with them of agreeing to work the longer time, or of being thrown out of employment altogether.\" The Ten Hours' Act in force (1 May 1848) and capital's revolt. The Chartist fiasco (leaders imprisoned, organization dismembered) shattered the self-confidence of the English working class. The June insurrection in Paris and its bloody suppression united all fractions of the ruling classes in Britain and on the Continent under the common slogan of \"the salvation of property, religion, the family and society.\" Everywhere the working class was outlawed, anathematized, placed under a loi des suspects . Capital's revolt. The manufacturers broke out in open revolt — not only against the Ten Hours' Act but against all legislation since 1833. Marx calls this \"a pro-slavery rebellion in miniature, carried on for over two years with a cynical recklessness and a terroristic energy which were so much the easier to achieve in that the rebel capitalist risked nothing but the skin of his workers.\" The steps of the revolt. 1. Dismissal of young persons and women — in many cases half of them. 2. Restoration of night-work for adult males, which had almost disappeared. The Ten Hours' Act \"leaves us no other alternative.\" 3. On meal-times: manufacturers claimed that they could fulfil the statutes by allowing an hour before 9 a.m. and half an hour after 7 p.m. — i.e., workers should eat their dinner before 9 in the morning or at home! The Crown lawyers decided that prescribed meal-times \"must be in the interval during the working-hours, and that it will not be lawful to work for 10 hours continuously, from 9 a.m. to 7 p.m., without any interval.\" 4. On the letter of the law: the Act of 1844 prohibited employment after 1 p.m. of children aged 8–13 who had been employed before noon — but it did not regulate the 6½ hours' work of children whose day began at 12 midday or later. Children of 8 might, if they began at noon, be employed from 12–1 (1 hour), 2–4 (2 hours), and 5–8.30 (3½ hours) — a legal 6½ hours! Or, better, by being kept entirely from work till 2 p.m., they could then be kept until 8.30 p.m. without intermission. Marx quotes Shylock: \"My deeds upon my head! I crave the law, / The penalty and forfeit of my bond.\" And later: \"Ay, his breast, / So says the bond.\" Statistics presented to the House of Commons on 26 July 1850: 3,742 children still subjected to this \"practice\" in 257 factories. 5. The open revolt against the regulation of \"young persons and women\": the abolition of the \"false relay system\" was the main aim of the 1844 Act. Manufacturers declared that the sections prohibiting the unrestricted use of young persons and women in fractions of the 15-hour day had been \"comparatively harmless\" with 12 hours but were now a \"grievous hardship.\" They informed the inspectors they would set themselves above the letter of the law and introduce the old system on their own account. Pretexts: this would allow them to pay higher wages; it was the only way to maintain Britain's industrial supremacy; what did the inspectors' trouble matter compared to \"the great manufacturing interest of this country\"? The Home Secretary's capitulation and the courts. Factory Inspector J. Stuart, following Sir George Grey's circular of 5 August 1848, allowed the relay system for the 15-hour period to be restored throughout Scotland. English inspectors declared the Home Secretary had no dictatorial power to suspend the laws and continued their legal proceedings. But the courts — county magistrates — acquitted the manufacturers. The Eskrigge case. A cotton-spinner named Eskrigge submitted a relay plan to the factory inspector, was refused, kept quiet. Months later, a spinner named Robinson (Eskrigge's relative) appeared before the borough magistrates of Stockport on a charge of introducing the very plan. The bench consisted of four Justices, three of them cotton-spinners , headed by Eskrigge himself. Eskrigge acquitted Robinson, then decided that what was right for Robinson was fair for Eskrigge, and introduced the plan into his own factory. Marx notes that the composition of this tribunal was itself a blatant violation of the law (by Section 10 of 1 & 2 William IV, c. 24 — Sir John Hobhouse's Factory Act — no owner of a cotton mill, nor the father, son, or brother of such an owner, could act as justice in factory inquiries). Horner's despair. Horner reports: 10 prosecutions in 7 magisterial divisions, supported by magistrates in only one case. He considered further prosecution useless. The part of the 1848 Act framed for securing uniformity of hours is \"no longer in force\" in his district. The number of mill-owners working by shifts amounts to 114 and is rapidly increasing. Working time is extended to 13½ hours (6 a.m. to 7½ p.m.), in some instances to 15 hours (5½ a.m. to 8½ p.m.). Horner already possessed a list of 65 manufacturers and 29 overseers who unanimously declared that no system of supervision could prevent the most extensive over-work under this relay system. The absurdity of the relay system. Sometimes the same children were shifted from the spinning-room to the weaving-room; sometimes shifted from one factory to another in the course of 15 hours. \"How was it possible to control a system which 'under the guise of relays, is some one of the many plans for shuffling \"the hands\" about in endless variety, and shifting the hours of work and of rest for different individuals throughout the day, so that you may never have one complete set of hands working together in the same room at the same time'?\" Fourier comparison. Marx compares the relay system to Fourier's humorous sketches of the courts é sêances — \"except that the 'attraction of labour' is here transformed into the attraction of capital.\" The working personnel was divided into 12–15 categories, constantly changing composition. During the 15 hours of the factory day, capital dragged the worker in for 30 minutes, then an hour, then pushed him out, then in afresh, \"hounding him hither and thither, in scattered shreds of time, without ever letting go until the full 10 hours of work was done.\" Like actors committed to the theatre for the whole play, workers were committed to the factory for the whole 15 hours. Thus \"the hours of rest were turned into hours of enforced idleness, which drove the young men to the taverns and the young girls to the brothels.\" Every new trick for keeping machinery going for 12 or 15 hours without increasing personnel meant the worker had to gulp his meals in a different fragment of time. The wage trick. \"During the 10 hours' agitation, the manufacturers cried out that the mob of workers were petitioning in the hope of obtaining 12 hours' wages for 10 hours' work. Now they reversed the medal. They paid 10 hours' wages for 12 or 15 hours' disposition over the workers' labour-power. This was the heart of the matter, this was the manufacturers' edition of the ten hours' law!\" The Court of Exchequer's verdict (8 February 1850). This \"revolt of capital\" was crowned with victory by a decision of the Court of Exchequer: the manufacturers were \"certainly acting against the sense of the Act of 1844, but that this Act itself contained certain words that rendered it meaningless.\" Marx: \"This verdict was tantamount to an abrogation of the Ten Hours' Bill.\" Many manufacturers who had been afraid to use the shift system now seized on it enthusiastically. Marx adds a footnote comparing this to the same Court's decision in the Alexandra case (1863), which reversed the meaning of the Foreign Enlistment Act against the arming of pirate ships. The workers' counter-stroke. The victory of capital was immediately followed by a counter-stroke. The workers protested in Lancashire and Yorkshire in threatening meetings. \"The so-called Ten Hours' Act, they said, was thus mere humbug, a parliamentary fraud. It had never existed!\" Factory inspectors warned the government that class antagonisms had reached an unheard-of degree of tension. Manufacturers grumbled about contradictory decisions producing \"anarchic\" conditions: one law in Yorkshire, another in Lancashire. \"The manufacturer in large towns could evade the law, the manufacturer in country districts could not find the people necessary for the relay system.\" Marx's analytical point: \"the most fundamental right under the law of capital is the equal exploitation of labour-power by all capitalists.\" The compromise: Factory Act of 5 August 1850. The working day for \"young persons and women\" was lengthened from 10 to 10½ hours for the first five days, and shortened to 7½ hours on Saturdays. Work had to take place between 6 a.m. and 6 p.m. (7 a.m. to 7 p.m. in winter), with pauses of not less than 1½ hours for meal-times, at exactly the same time for all. The relay system was ended once and for all. For child labour, the Act of 1844 remained in force. The silk manufacturers' privilege. Silk manufacturers secured special \"seigniorial rights over the children of the proletariat.\" In 1833 they had howled that if the liberty of working children of any age for 10 hours a day were taken away, it would stop their works. They extorted the privilege. Subsequent investigation showed the pretext was a deliberate lie. Throughout the following decade they spun silk for 10 hours a day \"out of the blood of little children who had to be put on stools to perform their work.\" The 1844 Act \"robbed\" the silk manufacturers of the liberty of employing children under 11 for longer than 6½ hours, but secured them the privilege of working children 11–13 for 10 hours, and annulled compulsory education. Their pretext: \"the delicate texture of the fabric in which they were employed, requiring a lightness of touch, only to be acquired by their early introduction to these factories.\" Marx: \"The children were quite simply slaughtered for the sake of their delicate fingers, just as horned cattle are slaughtered in southern Russia for their hides and their fat.\" In 1850 the privilege was limited to silk-twisting and silk-winding, but to compensate capital the hours for children 11–13 were increased from 10 to 10½. Official medical inquiries proved that \"the average death-rate is exceedingly high in the silk districts, and amongst the female part of the population is higher even than it is in the cotton districts of Lancashire.\" The Greenhow table. Marx reproduces (in a compressed statistical table) Dr Greenhow's 1861 data comparing mortality in manufacturing districts to \"eight healthy agricultural districts.\" Manufacturing districts show 100% higher mortality than the agricultural baseline of 340 per thousand for male adults. Even after the Factory Acts had improved conditions, the manufacturing districts lagged badly in health. Marx adds: \"In general, the working population has greatly improved physically under the regime of the Factory Act. All medical testimony agrees on this point, and my own personal observation on various occasions has convinced me this is true.\" The 1850 Act's loopholes and their closure. The Act of 1850 replaced the 15-hour period (6 a.m. to 8.30 p.m.) with a 12-hour period (6 a.m. to 6 p.m.), but only for young persons and women. It did not affect children, who could still be employed for half an hour before this period and 2½ hours after it, provided the total did not exceed 6½ hours. During the bill's discussion, the factory inspectors laid before Parliament statistics on the abuses arising from this anomaly. In vain. \"In the background lurked the intention of using the children to force the working day of adult males up to 15 hours, in years of prosperity. The experience of the three years which followed demonstrated that such an attempt was bound to fail in face of the resistance of the adult male workers.\" The Act was finally completed in 1853 by the prohibition of \"the employment of children in the morning before and in the evening after young persons and women.\" By then \"half a century had elapsed since the passing of the first Factory Act.\" The Printworks Act of 1845. The first extension of factory legislation beyond its original sphere. Marx calls it a \"parliamentary abortion\": it limits the working day for children 8–13 and for women to 16 hours between 6 a.m. and 10 p.m., without any legal pause for meal-times. It allows males over 13 to be worked at will day and night. He quotes inspectors: \"The Printworks Act is admitted to be a failure.\" Children of 8 and upwards were still employed from 6 a.m. to 9 p.m. within the inspector's district. The 1860s expansion and the fruits of regulation. The very manufacturers from whom the legal limitation of the working day had been wrung \"now pointed boastfully to the contrast with the areas of exploitation which were still 'free'.\" The Pharisees of political economy \"now proclaimed that their newly won insight into the necessity for a legally regulated working day was a characteristic achievement of their 'science'.\" Marx refers to W. Newmarch (editor of Tooke's History of Prices ). He asks: \"Is it a scientific advance to make cowardly concessions to public opinion?\" The extension of legislation. Once the factory magnates had resigned themselves, capital's power of resistance gradually weakened, while the working class's power of attack grew with the number of its allies in social layers not directly interested. Hence the comparatively rapid progress since 1860: 1860 : Dye-works and bleach-works brought under the Act of 1850 (12 hours provisional 1 Aug 1861, definitively 10 hours on 1 Aug 1862); 1861 : Lace and stocking factories; 1863 (following the first report of the Commission on the Employment of Children, 1863): manufacturers of all earthenware products (not just potteries), matches, percussion-caps, cartridges, carpets and fustian cuttings, and employers of people engaged in the many processes included under \"finishing\"; 1863 : bleaching in the open air and baking placed under special Acts. In the first case, the labour of young persons and women at night was forbidden (from 8 p.m. to 6 a.m.); in the latter, the employment of journeymen bakers under 18 between 9 p.m. and 5 a.m. was forbidden. Marx notes the great inadequacy even of these Acts — the bleachers' evasions, the loopholes, the \"failure\" of even the 1863 Act. The manufacturers again tried the trick of demanding 12 hours \"for the sake of the workers\" during the cotton famine — but \"it was mainly due to the action of the operative bleachers in Scotland that the bill was abandoned.\" Marx's close on this section : \"We shall return to the later proposals of the same Commission, which threaten to deprive all the important branches of English industry of their 'freedom,' with the exception of agriculture, mining and transport.\" (In a footnote he notes: \"Since 1866, when I wrote the above passages, a reaction has set in once again.\") 2.7 International Impact: France, America, the First International The final section draws out the general conclusions of the historical sketch. Preliminary clarifications. Marx reminds the reader that \"the production of surplus-value, or the extraction of surplus labour, forms the specific content and purpose of capitalist production\" — independent of any reconstruction of the mode of production that may arise from the subordination of labour to capital. The independent worker, legally qualified to act for himself, enters into a contract with the capitalist as seller of a commodity. If the historical sketch has shown the prominent part played by modern industry on the one hand and the labour of the physically and legally minor on the other, \"the former is still for us only a particular department of the exploitation of labour, and the latter only a particularly striking example of it.\" Two general conclusions can be derived by connecting the historical facts together: First. Capital's drive towards boundless extension of the working day begins in the industries first revolutionized by water-power, steam, and machinery — the earliest creations of the modern mode of production (spinning and weaving of cotton, wool, flax, silk). \"The changed material mode of production, and the correspondingly changed social relations of the producers, first gave rise to outrages without measure, and then called forth, in opposition to this, social control, which legally limits, regulates and makes uniform the working day and its pauses.\" Thus, for the first half of the nineteenth century, this control appears simply as legislation for exceptions . As soon as the Factory Acts had conquered the original domain, it was found that many other branches had entered the factory system — manufactures with more or less obsolete methods (potteries, glass-making), old-fashioned handicrafts (baking), and scattered domestic industries (nail-making) — all as completely under capitalist exploitation as the factories themselves. \"Factory legislation was therefore compelled gradually to strip itself of its exceptional character, or to declare that any house in which work was done was a factory, as in England, where the law proceeds in the manner of the Roman Casuists.\" Second. The isolated worker, the worker as 'free' seller of his labour-power, succumbs without resistance once capitalist production has reached a certain stage of maturity. Therefore the establishment of a normal working day is the product of \"a protracted and more or less concealed civil war between the capitalist class and the working class.\" Since the contest takes place in the arena of modern industry, it is fought out first in England, the homeland of that industry. \"The English factory workers were the champions, not only of the English working class, but of the modern working class in general, just as their theorists were the first to throw down the gauntlet to the theory of the capitalists.\" Ure's outrage. Marx recalls that \"the philosopher of the factory, Ure, considers it a mark of inextinguishable disgrace on the part of the English working class that they wrote 'the slavery of the Factory Acts' on their banners, as opposed to capital, which was striving manfully for the 'perfect freedom of labour'.\" France. \"France limps slowly behind England. The French twelve hours' law needed the February revolution to bring it into the world, and it has far more loopholes than its English model. Nevertheless, the French revolutionary method has its own peculiar advantages. At one stroke it dictates the same limits to the working day in all shops and factories without distinction, whereas the English legislation yields reluctantly to the pressure of circumstances, now on this point, now on that, and is well on the way to creating an inextricable tangle of contradictory enactments.\" Moreover, \"the French law proclaims as a principle what in England was only won in the name of children, minors and women, and has only recently been claimed, for the first time, as a universal right.\" Marx cites the Compte Rendu of the International Statistical Congress (Paris, 1855): the French law limiting daily labour to 12 hours does not confine work to definite fixed hours; for children's labour only, the working time is prescribed as between 5 a.m. and 9 p.m. \"Therefore, some of the manufacturers use the right which this fatal silence gives them to keep their works going without intermission, day in, day out, with the possible exception of Sunday. For this purpose they use two different sets of workers... the work of the establishment lasts day and night. The law is satisfied, but is humanity?\" The report also stresses \"the destructive influence of night-labour on the human organism\" and \"the fatal influence of the association of the two sexes by night in the same badly lighted workshops.\" The Belgian model. Belgium \"shows no trace of this movement.\" The workers, \"in perfect 'freedom',\" are consumed at any period and through any length of time. Out of every 1,000 persons employed in coal and metal mines: 733 men, 88 women, 135 boys, 44 girls under 16. In blast-furnaces: 668 men, 149 women, 98 boys, 85 girls under 16. Average daily pay: 2s. 8d. for a man, 1s. 8d. for a woman, 1s. 2½d. for a boy. As a result, Belgium nearly doubled the amount and value of its coal, iron, etc. exports between 1850 and 1863. Owen's example. Robert Owen, soon after 1810, not only maintained the necessity of limiting the working day in theory, but introduced the 10-hour day into his factory at New Lanark. \"This was laughed at as a communist utopia; so was his 'combination of children's education with productive labour,' as well as the workers' co-operative societies he was the first to set up. Today, the first utopia is a Factory Act, the second figures as an official phrase in all Factory Acts, and the third is already being used as a cloak for reactionary swindles.\" The United States. \"In the United States of America, every independent workers' movement was paralysed as long as slavery disfigured a part of the republic. Labour in a white skin cannot emancipate itself where it is branded in a black skin. However, a new life immediately arose from the death of slavery. The first fruit of the American Civil War was the eight hours' agitation, which ran from the Atlantic to the Pacific, from New England to California, with the seven-league boots of the locomotive.\" The General Congress of Labour held at Baltimore in August 1866 declared: \"The first and great necessity of the present, to free the labour of this country from capitalistic slavery, is the passing of a law by which eight hours shall be the normal working day in all States of the American Union. We are resolved to put forth all our strength until this glorious result is attained.\" The International. At the same time (beginning of September 1866), the Congress of the International Working Men's Association, held at Geneva, passed the following resolution, proposed by the London General Council (and drafted by Marx himself): \"We declare that the limitation of the working day is a preliminary condition without which all further attempts at improvement and emancipation must prove abortive... the Congress proposes eight hours as the legal limit of the working day.\" The final synthesis. Marx closes with a powerful restatement of the chapter's central theme: \"It must be acknowledged that our worker emerges from the process of production looking different from when he entered it. In the market, as owner of the commodity 'labour-power,' he stood face to face with other owners of commodities, one owner against another owner. The contract by which he sold his labour-power to the capitalist proved in black and white, so to speak, that he was free to dispose of himself. But when the transaction was concluded, it was discovered that he was no 'free agent,' that the period of time for which he is free to sell his labour-power is the period of time for which he is forced to sell it, that in fact the vampire will not let go 'while there remains a single muscle, sinew or drop of blood to be exploited.'\" For \"protection\" against the serpent of their agonies, workers must put their heads together and, as a class, compel the passing of a law — \"an all-powerful social barrier\" — by which they can be prevented from selling themselves and their families into slavery and death by voluntary contract with capital. \"In the place of the pompous catalogue of the 'inalienable rights of man' there steps the modest Magna Carta of the legally limited working day, which at last makes clear 'when the time which the worker sells is ended, and when his own begins.'\" The final Latin phrase: Quantum mutatus ab illo! — \"What a great change from that time\" (Virgil, Aeneid , Bk 2, line 274). The \"illo\" is both the worker as he entered the process and the historical era before the working-class movement had won the legal limitation of the working day. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed 1. The working day is a social and political determination, not an economic one. Marx's central analytic achievement in this chapter is to show that the economic categories established in earlier chapters — value, surplus-value, the rate of surplus-value — are insufficient to determine the length of the working day. Economic law fixes the value of labour-power and the necessary labour-time, but it leaves the length of the working day indeterminate . That indeterminacy is resolved by force, by class struggle, and ultimately by state legislation. This is a profound methodological point: the capitalist economy cannot be understood purely in economic terms . 2. The antinomy of equal rights. The dialectical structure of the chapter rests on the collision between two equally valid claims derived from the law of commodity-exchange: the buyer's right to maximize the use-value of his purchase, and the seller's right to preserve the value of his commodity from premature consumption. \"Between equal rights, force decides.\" This formula has enormous implications: it means that the \"freedom of contract\" which appears as the legal foundation of capitalism is, in fact, the legal form of an underlying relation of force. 3. Surplus labour is transhistorical; its specific form under capitalism is the drive to extend the working day. The comparative analysis of corvée and wage-labour shows that the quantitative ratio of surplus to necessary labour is not unique to capitalism, but that its specific form differs: the corvée makes surplus labour visible (distinct fields, distinct days), while wage-labour conceals it under the unity of a single working day. The capitalist's distinctive appetite appears as the drive for an unlimited extension of the working day, because, unlike the boyar, the capitalist cannot simply command more corvée days — he can only extend the day. 4. Capital is indifferent to the health and life of the worker. \"By extending the working day, therefore, capitalist production... not only produces a deterioration of human labour-power... but also produces the premature exhaustion and death of this labour-power itself.\" \"Capital asks no questions about the length of life of labour-power. What interests it is purely and simply the maximum of labour-power that can be set in motion in a working day. It attains this objective by shortening the life of labour-power, in the same way as a greedy farmer snatches more produce from the soil by robbing it of its fertility.\" The watchword is \" Après moi le déluge! \" — a formula still cited today in ecological and labor-degradation critiques. 5. The \"reserve army\" mechanism replaces the immediate self-interest of capital in preserving labour-power. Marx makes the crucial observation: even though capital's own long-term interest would seem to require the preservation of labour-power, the availability of a constant surplus population from the countryside, from Ireland, from Germany, and from the natural reproduction of the working class means that capital does not need to care for any individual worker — \"when his place can at once be supplied from foreign preserves, the duration of his life becomes a matter of less moment than its productiveness while it lasts.\" This is the essence of the later concept of the industrial reserve army, deployed here to explain the self-destructive dynamic of capital. 6. The state is not neutral; it acts as an instrument of whichever class can bring more force to bear. The history of the working day demonstrates two opposite uses of state power: in the fourteenth through seventeenth centuries, the state forcibly extended the working day on behalf of nascent capital; in the nineteenth century, it forcibly limited the working day under working-class pressure. The state's class character is not fixed by its form (Parliament, courts, inspectors) but by the balance of forces operating on it. Note especially the complicity of the courts — county magistrates (the \"Great Unpaid,\" \"patrimonial jurisdiction of the ruling classes\") acquitting manufacturers, the Court of Exchequer twisting the law's plain meaning to favor capital, the Home Secretary instructing inspectors not to prosecute. 7. The legally limited working day is the \"modest Magna Carta\" of the working class. The \"inalienable rights of man\" are dismissed as a \"pompous catalogue,\" while the legally limited working day is \"the modest Magna Carta\" that \"at last makes clear 'when the time which the worker sells is ended, and when his own begins.'\" This is a profoundly important statement about the strategic orientation of the working-class movement: the working day is not just one demand among many, but a preliminary condition for all further improvement. \"Further steps towards a reformation of society can never be carried out with any hope of success, unless the hours of labour be limited, and the prescribed limit strictly enforced.\" Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications Practical implications for labor movements. The chapter is a \"how-to\" of class struggle in many ways. It shows that: - Workers' resistance to over-work is not a moral preference but a defense of their own commodity; - Isolated workers cannot defend themselves; only collective action (\"collective labor\" against \"collective capital\") can secure limits; - The struggle is not won once and for all — capital constantly finds new \"dodges\" (the relay system, the letter of the law, meal-times, wage reductions); - Workers' organizations must be ready to make alliances with other social forces when capital's own internal divisions appear; - The example of English factory workers is offered to workers internationally as a model of struggle. Theoretical implications. The chapter does not merely illustrate an economic theory — it transforms it. It establishes that: - The category of surplus-value cannot be fully analyzed without a theory of the state , of class struggle, and of legislation ; - Historical materialism must integrate the relative autonomy of legal and political institutions (Parliament, inspectorates, courts) with the underlying economic relation; - The distinction between \"formal\" and \"real\" subsumption is implicit in the argument that capital \"takes the labour process as it finds it\" but transforms working conditions through over-work; - The analysis of the working day is the essential precondition for understanding relative surplus-value (Part IV) — because relative surplus-value emerges in response to limits on the working day. Philosophical implications. The chapter is a moral indictment of political economy's \"freedom of labour,\" but the indictment is not moralistic. Marx is careful to argue that the capitalist is not personally responsible: \"this does not depend on the will, either good or bad, of the individual capitalist. Under free competition, the immanent laws of capitalist production confront the individual capitalist as a coercive force external to him.\" The individual capitalist may be a model citizen, \"in the odour of sanctity\" — but \"the thing you represent when you come face to face with me has no heart in its breast. What seems to throb there is my own heartbeat.\" The critique is structural, not moral. This is a foundational statement of Marx's method: the critique of capital is a critique of a system , and the system's \"laws\" — competition, valorization, the drive for surplus-value — are the effective causal forces, not the character of individuals. The final image. Quantum mutatus ab illo — the worker who enters the market as a free agent, the \"owner of the commodity 'labour-power',\" emerges from the production process \"looking different from when he entered it.\" He discovers that the period for which he is \"free\" to sell his labour-power is, in fact, the period for which he is forced to sell it. The \"freedom of contract\" is revealed as the form of his subjection. The legally limited working day is thus not a reformist compromise but a genuine conquest: it establishes, for the first time, a boundary between \"the worker's\" time and \"the master's\" time. The Magna Carta of the working class is not the Magna Carta of the barons, but the historical form of a broader claim — that human life should not be entirely absorbed into the process of capital's self-valorization. \"The vampire will not let go 'while there remains a single muscle, sinew or drop of blood to be exploited.'\" The workers must put their heads together and, as a class, compel the passing of a law — \"an all-powerful social barrier.\" The chapter thus ends with the theoretical articulation of a political program: the legally limited working day, the eight-hour day, as the precondition of any further human emancipation. That program, articulated by Marx in the Geneva resolution of the First International and echoed in the Baltimore declaration of the American workers in 1866, is not merely a moral aspiration but the necessary outcome of the historical analysis that Marx has conducted in the preceding 76 pages. This concludes the exhaustive analysis of Chapter 10 of Marx's Capital , Volume I. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 9 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 11"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 11", "title": "Chapter 11", "subtitle": "The Rate and Mass of Surplus-Value", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 11/Chapter 11  The Rate and Mass of Surplus-Value_explanation.html", "word_count": 6481, "read_time": "32 min read", "tags": ["The Working Day & Factory Acts", "Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["Rate and Mass of Surplus-Value", "Laws of Mass of Surplus-Value", "Limit of Exploitation per Worker", "Minimum Capital Required for Capitalist Exploitation", "Total Variable Capital Determination"], "text": "Chapter 11 The Rate And Mass Of Surplus-Value - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,397 words ⏱️ ~32 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 11, \"The Rate and Mass of Surplus-Value,\" is the culminating theoretical chapter of Part IV of Capital Volume I, the section devoted to the production of absolute surplus-value . It occupies a strategic hinge-point in the architecture of the work. Having spent Chapters 7–10 establishing the labour-process, the distinction between constant and variable capital, the rate of surplus-value (s/v, or surplus labour/necessary labour), and the historical struggle over the working day, Marx now poses a fresh and deceptively simple question: how is the rate of exploitation related to the mass of value extracted? The rate is an intensive magnitude—a ratio, a \"degree.\" The mass is an extensive magnitude—a quantity of value, a sum of money. The chapter's central intellectual objective is to demonstrate rigorously that the second is governed by the first through a determinate algebraic relationship, and to draw out that relationship's three corollary laws. The chapter's core thesis is that, once three magnitudes are fixed—(1) the value of labour-power, (2) the degree of exploitation, and (3) the number of workers employed—the mass of surplus-value is wholly determined by the product of the variable capital advanced and the rate of surplus-value. In the notation Marx himself sets out, S = v × (a'/a) × n , equivalently S = P × (a'/a) × n , equivalently S = (s/v) × V . From this single equation, Marx derives a set of consequences that carry the whole weight of the volume's subsequent argument: the substitutability of the number of workers for their intensity of exploitation; the absolute barrier imposed by the length of the working day on any such substitution; the direct proportionality between variable capital and surplus-value mass (together with the corollary that constant capital contributes nothing to newly created value); the social significance of the total working day as a national quantity bounded by population and by the biologically limited day; and the threshold concept that not every sum of money can function as capital at all. Methodologically, the chapter is a masterclass in what we might call the dialectic of appearance and law . Its third law—that surplus-value mass varies directly with variable capital—\"clearly contradicts all experience based on immediate appearances,\" because the cotton-spinner with a vast constant capital and a tiny variable capital pockets no less profit than the baker who employs almost entirely variable capital. Marx does not resolve this contradiction here; he explicitly defers it, insisting that \"many intermediate terms are still needed,\" anticipating the transformation of surplus-value into profit (Book III) and the disintegration of the Ricardian school (Book IV). This deferral is not evasion but architectural precision: the chapter marks where the analysis must later go. Finally, the chapter pivots from pure quantitative algebra to a historical and philosophical register . The emergence of a capitalist from a mere money-owner is shown to require a minimum capital, and that minimum crosses over—by what Marx, invoking Hegel, calls a dialectical inversion —from a merely quantitative accumulation into a qualitative social transformation: the birth of a class wholly released from manual labour. In its closing pages the chapter unveils the formal subsumption of labour under capital, describes capital as a coercive relation surpassing all prior systems of compulsory labour in ruthless energy, and then stages a remarkable inversion: under the valorization process, it is not the worker who employs the means of production but \"the means of production which employ the worker.\" The chapter ends not with an equation but with a case study in bourgeois ideology—the letter of a Scottish mill-owner who genuinely believes his spindles mechanically contain a \"power of self-valorization\"—as an illustration of how deeply the fetish-form of capital penetrates capitalist consciousness. Thus Chapter 11 completes Part IV and opens the door both to relative surplus-value (Part V) and to the concept of capital's self-expansion as a social totality. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Presupposition: Fixed Labour-Power Value and the Direct Translation of Rate into Mass The chapter opens by stating its controlling presupposition, one inherited from all preceding analysis: \"the value of labour-power, and therefore the part of the working day necessary for the reproduction or maintenance of that labour-power, is assumed to be a given, constant magnitude.\" This is the necessary labour-time —the fraction of the day the worker spends reproducing the value of his own subsistence. By holding it constant, Marx isolates one variable at a time, allowing a clean algebra to emerge. He immediately illustrates with a numerical example: if necessary labour is 6 hours, expressed as 3 shillings of gold, then 3 shillings is the daily value of one labour-power and hence the value of the capital advanced to purchase it. If the rate of surplus-value is 100 per cent, this 3-shilling variable capital yields 3 shillings of surplus-value—six hours of unpaid surplus labour. The rate is thus demonstrably equivalent to a specific, physical quantity of surplus-labour time. From this starting point Marx generalizes along two axes. Aggregation across workers : variable capital is the \"monetary expression for the total value of all the labour-powers the capitalist employs simultaneously.\" Given a fixed value per labour-power, the magnitude of variable capital varies directly with the number of workers. If one labour-power costs 3 shillings daily, then 100 labour-powers require 300 shillings, and n labour-powers require n × 3 shillings. Scaling of surplus-value : if 3 shillings produces 3 shillings of surplus-value, then 300 shillings produces 300 shillings, and n × 3 shillings produce n × 3 shillings of surplus-value. The mass of surplus-value is therefore the surplus-value produced by one worker's working day multiplied by the number of workers employed . This yields the first law : the mass of surplus-value equals the variable capital advanced multiplied by the rate of surplus-value—i.e., it is determined by the product of (a) the number of labour-powers simultaneously exploited by the same capitalist and (b) the degree of exploitation of each individual labour-power. The Algebra of Exploitation: Formalizing the Rate–Mass Relationship Marx then renders the argument symbolically—a rare and deliberate move that marks how much weight the equation is intended to bear. He defines: S — the mass of surplus-value s — the surplus-value supplied by an individual worker in the average day v — the variable capital advanced daily for the purchase of one individual labour-power (equivalently, the daily value of one labour-power) V — the sum total of variable capital P — the value of an average labour-power a'/a — the degree of exploitation, i.e. surplus labour divided by necessary labour (the rate of surplus-value) n — the number of workers employed The famous formula then appears as three equivalent expressions bracketed together: S = v × (a'/a) × n = P × (a'/a) × n = (s/v) × V Each expression repays close reading, because the equivalence is not trivial—it encodes the entire argument of the chapter in miniature. The first form, S = v × (a'/a) × n , says: take the daily value of a single labour-power (v), multiply by its degree of exploitation (a'/a), and multiply by the number of workers (n). It is the arithmetic of the individual capitalist's payroll. The second form, S = P × (a'/a) × n , is formally identical but reframes v as P, \"the value of an average labour-power.\" This substitution is critical: it introduces an explicit averaging assumption, allowing Marx to abstract from workers who are faster or slower than the norm. The third form, S = (s/v) × V , is the most theoretically significant, because (s/v) is precisely the rate of surplus-value and V is total variable capital . This form announces the general result: mass of surplus-value = total variable capital × rate of surplus-value , or S = V × (a'/a) . The mass of surplus-value is a product of one extensive quantity (V, the total wage-bill or value of labour-power purchased) and one intensive ratio (the degree of exploitation). Immediately after setting out the equation, Marx adds a crucial caveat: \"We assume throughout, not only that the value of an average labour-power is constant, but that the workers employed by a capitalist are reduced to average workers.\" He concedes that \"there do exist exceptional cases in which the surplus-value produced does not increase in proportion to the number of workers being exploited\"—but then notes that in such cases \"the value of the labour-power does not remain constant.\" The law is thus stated conditionally: it holds ceteris paribus , and the exception proves the assumption by violating it. This is a characteristic Marxian move—laying bare the counterfactuals on which a law's validity depends. The Compensation Theorem: Substitution Between Number of Workers and Degree of Exploitation The derivation of the first law immediately generates a striking secondary result, which we may call the compensation theorem . If the mass of surplus-value is the product of two factors—variable capital and rate of exploitation—then a decrease in one factor can be compensated for by a proportionate increase in the other , leaving the mass unchanged. Marx illustrates arithmetically. Suppose the capitalist must advance 300 shillings to exploit 100 workers daily, and the rate of surplus-value is 50 per cent. This produces 150 shillings of surplus-value, or \"100 × 3 working hours.\" Now suppose the rate of surplus-value doubles —which, given a necessary labour of 6 hours, means the working day expands from 6 to 12 hours (6 necessary + 6 surplus)—while variable capital is halved to 150 shillings, i.e. 50 workers. This too yields 150 shillings, or \"50 × 6 working hours.\" The mass is identical. The inverse also holds: \"a fall in the rate of surplus-value leaves the mass of surplus-value which has been produced unaltered, if the amount of the variable capital, i.e. the number of workers employed, increases in the same proportion.\" The theorem's strategic significance crystallizes in a single sentence: \"Within certain limits, therefore, the supply of labour exploitable by capital is independent of the supply of workers.\" This is a claim of enormous political-economic weight. It means that capital's demand for labour —for labour-time extracted—is not simply a demand for labourers . Capital can, up to a point, substitute intensity for breadth. This is precisely the lever by which Marx anticipates and refutes the vulgar economist who imagines that the market price of labour is determined by supply and demand. In a delicious footnote Marx writes that this elementary law \"appears to be unknown to the vulgar economist, who imagines, like an inverted Archimedes, that in the determination of the market price of labour by supply and demand he has found the fulcrum by means of which he cannot so much move the world, as bring it to a standstill.\" The image—an Archimedes who claims a fulcrum but who only stands still—mocks the pretension of supply-and-demand theory to explain wages when it has failed to notice that the mass of exploitable labour is itself manipulable through the length of the working day. The Second Law: The Absolute Barrier Set by the Working Day Compensation, however, is not unlimited. Marx immediately insists that \"there are limits, which cannot be overcome, to the compensation.\" He states the reason with mathematical clarity: whatever the value of labour-power, the total value a worker can produce in a day is always less than the value in which 24 hours of labour are objectified. If 12 shillings expresses 24 hours of objectified labour, no worker can produce 12 shillings of new value in a day, because no worker can work 24 hours. So the lengthening of the working day has a hard ceiling. He illustrates with numbers. Take the necessary labour = 6 hours, so the 12-hour day yields 6 surplus hours. A capital of 1,500 shillings employing 500 workers at a 100 per cent rate produces 1,500 shillings of surplus-value , or 6 × 500 = 3,000 working hours. Now take a capital of 300 shillings employing 100 workers at 200 per cent (an 18-hour day: 6 necessary + 12 surplus). This produces 600 shillings of surplus-value , or 12 × 100 = 1,200 working hours. Crucially, \"its total value-product, the equivalent of the variable capital advanced plus the surplus-value, can, day in, day out, never reach the sum of 1,200 shillings or 24 × 100 working hours.\" That is, even pushing the day to its 18-hour point, the total value-product (v + s = 300 + 600 = 900 shillings) falls short of the absolute maximum conceivable (24 hours × 100 workers × 0.5 shillings/hour = 1,200 shillings). The absolute limit of the average working day—\"by nature always less than 24 hours\"—fixes an absolute limit to any compensation scheme. Marx calls this the \"self-evident second law,\" but insists it is \"of importance for the explanation of many phenomena.\" Specifically, it illuminates a contradiction within capital itself : capital has \"a tendency to reduce as much as possible the number of workers employed, i.e. the amount of its variable component\"—a tendency Marx says will be developed later (see Chapter 25, Sections 2–3, on the general law of capitalist accumulation)—while it simultaneously has \"the other tendency to produce the greatest possible mass of surplus-value.\" These two drives collide precisely because of the second law: cutting the workforce while raising the rate of exploitation can compensate only within the bounds of the physically possible day. Beyond that bound, shrinking variable capital does shrink surplus-value. Marx adds the converse: if the mass of labour-power or the variable capital increases but not in proportion to the fall in the rate of surplus-value, the surplus-value mass diminishes . The Third Law: Variable Capital as the Exclusive Determinant of Surplus-Value Mass The third law follows from the same equation. Given the rate of surplus-value (degree of exploitation), the value of labour-power, and a fixed limit to the working day and its necessary component, \"the mass of value and surplus-value produced by the individual capitalist is clearly exclusively dependent on the mass of labour that he sets in motion.\" And since—under the assumptions—the mass of labour set in motion depends on the mass of labour-power, i.e., the number of workers he exploits, and that number is determined by \"the amount of the variable capital advanced,\" it follows that with a given rate of surplus-value and a given value of labour-power, the masses of surplus-value produced vary directly as the amounts of variable capital advanced . Here Marx pauses for a critical clarification that will govern his later treatment of capital composition. A capitalist divides his capital into a constant part (outlay on means of production) and a variable part (outlay on living labour-power). Marx notes that the proportion between these parts—the composition of capital—differs between branches of production and, within a branch, changes with the \"technical foundations\" and the \"ways of linking together the processes of production in society.\" Yet \"whatever the proportion between the constant and the variable part of a given capital, whether it is 1:2, or 1:10 or 1:x, the law just laid down is not affected by this.\" Why? Because, by the prior analysis of Chapter 8, the value of constant capital re-appears in the value of the product, but does not enter into the newly produced value —the newly created value-product. More spinners certainly require more raw material, spindles, and machinery, but the value of these additional means of production, whether it \"may rise, fall, remain unaltered, be large or small; it has no influence on the valorization process performed by the labour-powers which set the means of production in motion.\" This sharpens the third law into its mature statement: \"the masses of value and of surplus-value produced by different capitals—the value of labour-power being given and its degree of exploitation being equal—vary directly as the amounts of the variable components of these capitals, i.e. the parts which have been turned into living labour-power.\" Labour Process versus Valorization Process: The Methodological Pivot The interplay just described between labour process and valorization process is the conceptual hinge of the chapter. Marx distinguishes two viewpoints on the same production process. From the standpoint of the labour process , the worker relates to the means of production per his own purposeful activity—tanning skins, he deals with \"the skins as his simple object of labour.\" In that view, means of production are the raw material and instruments of his work. But from the standpoint of the valorization process , the very same means of production \"are at once changed into means for the absorption of the labour of others.\" It is this dual character that later allows Marx to describe the conversion of money into means of production as the conversion of dead labour into a \"title, both by right and by might, to the labour and surplus labour of others.\" The distinction is the theoretical engine by which the chapter moves from the algebraic laws of Parts IV's opening chapters toward the qualitative-historical conclusion of its closing ones. The Contradiction with Appearances and the Method of Intermediate Terms Marx now confronts the fact that his third law—the direct proportionality of surplus-value to variable capital— \"clearly contradicts all experience based on immediate appearances.\" The example is vivid and durable: \"a cotton spinner, who... employs much constant capital and little variable capital, does not, on account of this, pocket less profit or surplus-value than a baker, who sets in motion relatively much variable capital and little constant capital.\" Immediate experience already knows that profits do not track the wage bill—they track the total capital advanced . Marx treats this contradiction with unusual methodological candour, refusing a cheap resolution. He writes that \"for the solution of this apparent contradiction, many intermediate terms are still needed, just as, from the standpoint of elementary algebra, many intermediate terms are needed before we can understand that 0/0 may represent an actual magnitude.\" The reference to 0/0 is technical and important: it is the mathematical figure for an indeterminate form that can contain a determinate result once the proper mediating operations are performed. The implication is that the apparent discord between law and appearance is not a refutation of the law but a signal that further mediating categories—price of production, general rate of profit, transformation—must be developed. Marx then situates classical economics in relation to this problem. Classical political economy \"holds instinctively to this law, although it has never actually formulated it, because it is a necessary consequence of the law of value.\" Its response to the discordance with experience is \"by making a violent abstraction\"—an ad hoc retreat. And \"we shall see later how the school of Ricardo came to grief on this stumbling-block.\" A footnote reinforces this, quoting the direct statement of the contradiction: capitals of equal magnitude but unequal organic composition \"set in motion unequal quantities of unpaid labour; consequently they cannot appropriate equal quantities of surplus-value... But capitals of equal magnitude, no matter what their organic composition, yield equal profits.\" Marx points to Book IV, Chapter 20, \"Disintegration of the Ricardian School,\" as the place where this intellectual impasse is traced. By contrast, vulgar economics —\"which like the Bourbons 'has really learnt nothing'\"—simply refuses to look behind the appearance, clinging to \"the mere semblance as opposed to the law which regulates and determines the phenomena.\" Marx's philosophical counterpoint is sharp and playful: \"In antithesis to Spinoza, it believes that 'ignorance is a sufficient reason.'\" Spinoza had argued that ignorance of true causes is not a sufficient reason for believing in final causes; the vulgar economist, in Marx's inverted paraphrase, elevates ignorance itself to an explanation. The essayistic sneer is more than ornament—it names a methodological failure: the refusal to reason beyond appearances. The Social Working Day and Population as the Mathematical Limit The chapter then scales the analysis from the individual capitalist to society as a whole . \"The labour which is set in motion by the total capital of a society, day in, day out, may be regarded as a single working day.\" If, say, a million workers each work a ten-hour day, the social working day consists of ten million hours. Given the length of that day—whether its limits are \"fixed physically or socially\"—the mass of surplus-value can be increased only in one of two ways: (1) by increasing the number of workers, i.e. the size of the working population, or (2) by lengthening the working day itself. \"The growth of population here forms the mathematical limit to the production of surplus-value by the total social capital. And, inversely, with a given population this limit is formed by the possible lengthening of the working day.\" Marx ties this to a striking historical source, citing in footnote 3 an 1821 tract, An Essay on the Political Economy of Nations , which already spoke of the \"Labour, that is the economic time, of society\" as \"a given portion, say ten hours a day of a million people, or ten million hours,\" noting that \"capital has its boundary of increase\" attained \"in the actual extent of economic time employed.\" This citation serves a double function: it demonstrates that even bourgeois writers had glimpsed the natural limits to valorization, and it grounds Marx's abstraction of the \"social working day\" in real economic literature. Marx concludes the passage by foreshadowing the next chapter: \"It will however be seen in the next chapter that this law holds only for the form of surplus-value dealt with up to the present\"—that is, only for absolute surplus-value, produced by extending the working day. Once relative surplus-value is introduced—through productivity gains and the shortening of necessary labour—the population limit can be partly circumvented. The Qualitative Leap: Minimum Capital and the Genetic Origins of the Capitalist The chapter's argument now pivots from extensive magnitude to social threshold . A crucial consequence, which Marx here announces as the chapter's final thematic development, is this: \"not every sum of money, or value, can be transformed into capital at will.\" This is a direct attack on the idea that capital is merely \"money that begets money\"—a purely quantitative property of any sufficiently patient hoarder of cash. Rather, the transformation presupposes \"that a certain minimum of money or of exchange-value is in the hands of the individual possessor.\" The minimum equals at least the cost price of one labour-power employed year-round for surplus-value production. Marx then reason through an example that inverts the standard picture of capitalist affluence. If a self-employed worker owned his own means of production and were satisfied to live as a worker, he would need only the labour-time necessary to reproduce his subsistence—say 8 hours a day—plus means of production sufficient for 8 working hours. The would-be capitalist, however, who makes him work these 8 hours plus, say, 4 surplus hours, must additionally furnish the means of production for those extra 4 hours. And crucially: if the capitalist is to live on the appropriated surplus-value as well as—and \"no better than\"—an ordinary worker, he must employ not one but two workers to simply subsist. If he wishes to live twice as well and turn half the surplus back into capital, he must multiply both the number of workers and the minimum capital advanced by eight . At the low end, any capitalist who continues to work alongside his employees is a \"hybrid, a man between capitalist and worker, a ' small master '.\" A more developed stage of capitalist production requires the capitalist to devote his full time to functioning \"as capital personified\"—to the \"appropriation and therefore the control of the labour of others [ fremde Arbeit ]\" and to the sale of products. The historical warrant for this threshold claim is supplied by the guild system of the Middle Ages , which \"tried forcibly to prevent the transformation of the master of a craft into a capitalist, by limiting the number of workers a single master could employ to a very low maximum.\" Hence the possessor of money or commodities \"actually turns into a capitalist only where the minimum sum advanced for production greatly exceeds the known medieval maximum.\" The theoretical pay-off is one of the most philosophically charged passages in the volume. Marx declares that \"here, as in natural science, is shown the correctness of the law discovered by Hegel, in his Logic , that at a certain point merely quantitative differences pass over by a dialectical inversion into qualitative distinctions.\" Accumulating more shillings is a quantitative process; but at a threshold, it becomes a different kind of thing —the capacity to command and supervise others' labour full-time, to be a capitalist rather than a worker. Engels' footnote amplifies this with a scientific illustration from the molecular theory of chemistry , in particular the homologous series of carbon compounds named by C. Gerhardt in 1843: paraffins (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂), normal alcohols (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂O), and normal fatty acids (CₙH₂ₙO₂). In these series, adding a single CH₂ unit to a molecule's formula—\"a simply quantitative addition\"—produces a qualitatively different body . The Hegelian law of the transformation of quantity into quality thereby receives an empirical, chemical analogue, and Marx plants a marker for the later concept of capital's \"thresholds of production.\" Historical Mediation: Guild Limits, State Subsidies, Joint-Stock Companies Marx then deepens the historical dimension of the minimum-capital argument. The threshold \"changes with the different stages of development of capitalist production, and is at given stages different in different spheres of production, according to their special technical conditions.\" Certain spheres, even at the dawn of capitalist production, required \"a minimum of capital which is not yet to be found in the hands of single individuals.\" This gave rise to two historical responses. First, state subsidies to private persons—as in France in the time of Colbert and in some German states down to Marx's own epoch. Second, the formation of companies with a legally secured monopoly over particular branches—\"the forerunners of the modern joint-stock companies.\" Marx footnotes Martin Luther , who in Von Kaufshandlung und Wucher (1524) called these institutions \"the Company Monopolia\" and thundered: \"Who is so stupid that he cannot see that the trading companies are nothing but pure monopolies?\" The passage thus quietly inaugurates the historical problematic of the joint-stock company as a response to capital thresholds—a theme Marx will resume in Volume III. Capital as Command and Coercion: Formal Subsumption and the Surpassing of Earlier Systems The chapter's last movement is its most historically sweeping and theoretically general. Marx announces he will not here examine \"in detail the changes which take place in the relation between the capitalist and the wage-labourer in the course of the process of production, nor... the characteristics of capital itself,\" but will emphasize \"certain main points.\" First, capital as command : \"Capital developed within the production process until it acquired command over labour, i.e. over self-activating labour-power, in other words the worker himself.\" The capitalist, \"who is capital personified,\" now ensures that the worker performs his labour \"regularly and with the proper degree of intensity.\" Second, capital as coercive relation : capital \"compels the working class to do more work than would be required by the narrow circle of its own needs.\" As \"an extractor of surplus labour and an exploiter of labour-power,\" it \" surpasses all earlier systems of production, which were based on directly compulsory labour , in its energy and its quality of unbounded and ruthless activity.\" This is a critical historical claim. Earlier systems—slavery, serfdom, corvée—extracted surplus labour directly , by force of law or personal domination. Capitalist exploitation, ironically, appears more \"free,\" yet surpasses them in intensity and inexorability, because it operates through the apparently voluntary wage contract and the legal compulsion of the market. Third, formal subsumption : \"At first capital subordinates labour on the basis of the technical conditions within which labour has been carried on up to that point in history. It does not therefore directly change the mode of production.\" The production of absolute surplus-value by the simple extension of the working day appeared \"independently of any change in the mode of production itself\"—and \"was no less effective in the old-fashioned bakeries than in the modern cotton factories.\" This is the theoretical formulation of what Marx elsewhere calls the formal subsumption of labour under capital : capital takes over an existing labour process, changes only its social form (who commands whom, over what duration), not its technical content. The passage is crucial for Part V, which will examine the real subsumption —when capital transforms the technical process itself through cooperation, manufacture, and machinery. The Grand Inversion: Dead Labour Commanding Living Labour The chapter's grandest theoretical passage is its articulation of the inversion of subject and object in capitalist production. Reusing the labour process/valorization process distinction, Marx writes: \"It is no longer the worker who employs the means of production, but the means of production which employ the worker. Instead of being consumed by him as material elements of his productive activity, they consume him as the ferment necessary to their own life-process, and the life-process of capital consists solely in its own motion as self-valorizing value.\" This is the very core of Marx's account of capital as self-expanding value —value which, having become autonomous (\"capital personified\"), treats the worker as an input rather than a subject. The passage then extends to the physical plant. Idle furnaces and workshops \"that stand idle by night, and absorb no living labour, are 'a mere loss' to the capitalist. Hence furnaces and workshops constitute 'lawful claims upon the night-labour' of the labour-powers.\" Dead labour—machinery, buildings—makes legal-moral demands on living labour to be fed with activity. As soon as \"a certain sum of money is transformed into means of production... the means of production themselves are transformed into a title, both by right and by might, to the labour and surplus labour of others.\" The title is de jure (ownership) and de facto (control); \"right\" and \"might\" operate in tandem. The Carlile & Co. Episode: Bourgeois Consciousness as Fetishised Form Marx closes the chapter with a concrete historical episode that makes the inversion visible in the mind of an actual capitalist. The setting is the English manufacturers' revolt of 1848–50 —the agitation against the Ten Hours Bill. The documentary source is a letter printed in the Glasgow Daily Mail of 25 April 1849 , under the heading \"The Relay System,\" written by the head of \"one of the oldest and most respectable houses in the West of Scotland\"— Messrs Carlile Sons & Co. of the linen and cotton thread factory at Paisley, a company existing \"for about a century,\" in operation since 1752, conducted by four generations of the same family. The letter, as Marx quotes it, bemoans the limiting of the working day to 10 hours: \"They amount to the most serious damage to the mill-owner's prospects and property. If he\"—i.e. his \"hands\"—\"worked 12 hours before, and is limited to 10, then every 12 machines or spindles in his establishment shrink to 10, and should the works be disposed of, they will be valued only as 10, so that a sixth part would thus be deducted from the value of every factory in the country.\" Marx comments with surgical sarcasm. In this \"West of Scotland bourgeois brain, which has inherited the capitalist qualities of 'four generations',\" the value of the means of production (spindles, etc.) is \"so inextricably confused with the quality they possess, as capital, of valorizing themselves\"—i.e. of \"swallowing up every day a definite quantity of the unpaid labour of others\"—that Carlile & Co.'s head \"actually imagines that if he sells his factory, not only will the value of the spindles be paid to him, but, in addition, their power of self-valorization, not only the labour contained in them... but also the surplus labour which they help to pump out daily from the brave Scots of Paisley.\" The fetish is total: the industrialist believes the valorizing power is a physical property of the machines, capitalised into the sales-price and diminished proportionately by any shortening of the working day. Marx's analysis had already shown why the belief is incoherent: constant capital contributes to the product's value only the value already congealed in it (its own reproduction cost), never any new value; the surplus-value is produced by living labour, not by the machinery. Yet in the mind of the capitalist, the social relation appears as a natural property of things . This is the fundamental form of the capital fetish —and Marx uses the episode to close the chapter not with an equation but with a diagnosis of consciousness. Marx then quotes the reaction of Factory Inspector Stuart —himself \"a Scotsman and, unlike the English factory inspectors, a complete prisoner of the capitalist mode of thought\"—who praises Carlile's letter in his own report as \"the most useful of the communications which any of the factory-owners working with relays have given to those engaged in the same trade, and which is the most calculated to remove the prejudices of such of them as have scruples respecting any change of the arrangement of the hours of work.\" The inspector's endorsement is the final ironic touch: even the state's supervisory apparatus fails to see through the fetish. The official enforcer of the factory acts shares the misrecognition of the very capitalists he regulates. The chapter thus terminates not with a mathematical conclusion but with a demonstration of the ideological consequences of the value-form : that capital's self-valorizing character appears to those who own it as an inherent, quasi-physical property of the machinery they possess, and is therefore misrecognised as an object rather than understood as a social relation. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Chapter 11 yields a set of fundamental principles that structure the rest of Marx's project. The first principle is the analytic decomposition of capital's expansion into one extensive and one intensive magnitude. Mass of surplus-value is total variable capital × rate of surplus-value. This forbids the naive view that capital's growth is simply a matter of accumulating more money; the intensity of exploitation—how hard a given wage-bill is worked—is equally constitutive. The corollary, that the supply of exploitable labour is partly independent of the supply of workers , dismantles the vulgar supply-and-demand theory of wages. Wage-fund arguments—which pretend that a fixed pool of capital determines a fixed wage level—miss the precise mechanism by which capital can raise its surplus without increasing its wage bill. The second principle is the absolute barrier . Because the working day is physically less than 24 hours, compensation between breadth and intensity exhausts itself at a hard limit. From this follows the structural contradiction within capital itself : its drive to economise on variable capital (throw workers out) collides with its drive to maximise surplus-value mass. Crises, chronic unemployment, and the pressure to intensify labour are all downstream of this tension. The third principle is the composition-neutrality of newly created value . Constant capital, however vast, transfers only its own value to the product and creates no new value. This yields the third law—surplus-value mass varies directly as variable capital, given equal rates of exploitation—and immediately sets up the great apparent contradiction with the empirical equality of profit rates across industries of differing composition. Marx's refusal to resolve this here, and his insistence on \"many intermediate terms,\" serves as a template for the relation between Volume I and Volume III: Volume I analyses value and surplus-value ; Volume III will analyse price of production and profit , in which the law of value remains operative but only through mediations. The chapter is thus a bridge between the abstract analysis of capitalist production and the concrete analysis of capitalist competition and distribution. The fourth principle is the threshold concept. Not every quantum of money is capital. There is a minimum below which money cannot become capital, and this minimum is historically variable, technically conditioned, and socially enforced (via guild limits, state subsidies, monopoly charters, and eventually joint-stock companies). The dialectical overtones—Hegel's transformation of quantity into quality, illustrated by organic chemistry's homologous series—signal that the emergence of a capitalist class is not a smooth accumulation but a qualitative leap that reconstitutes social relations. The fifth principle is the concept of formal subsumption . At its origins, capital does not need to transform the labour process itself; it merely takes over existing techniques and extends and intensifies them. Absolute surplus-value is thus historically the \"first\" form of capitalist exploitation, \"no less effective in the old-fashioned bakeries than in the modern cotton factories.\" This concept will be the baseline against which the \"real subsumption\" of Part V is measured. The sixth and culminating principle is the inversion of subject and object : it is the means of production that employ the worker, not the worker who employs the means of production. Dead labour commands living labour. Idle machinery exerts a \"lawful claim\" upon the night-labour of the workers. The means of production become a title —by right and by might—to the labour of others. From this inversion follows the fetish-form of capital, beautifully exhibited in the Carlile & Co. episode: the capitalist cannot distinguish the machinery's value from its capacity to pump out surplus labour, and therefore thinks any diminution of the working day physically shrinks the value of his plant. This failure of recognition is not a simple error of an ignorant businessman; it is the necessary form in which the capitalist social relation appears to those enmeshed in it. Even the state inspector shares the illusion. The broader implications are threefold. Theoretically , the chapter roots the later transformation problem in a single rigorous accounting: mass of surplus-value, total value, value-product, price of production, and profit are all internally linked, and the apparent contradictions between the value-theoretic law and competitive appearances are resolvable only through a chain of intermediate categories (Book III) and a critical history of political economy (Book IV). Historically , the chapter locates the emergence of the capitalist within a specific institutional matrix—the breakdown of guild limits, the granting of charters and subsidies, and the eventual emergence of joint-stock capital—showing that the \"free\" wage-labour relation was constructed, not discovered. Ideologically , the chapter demonstrates that the surface form of capital—machinery, plant, money \"begetting\" money—systematically conceals the underlying social relation, generating the fetishised consciousness in which even the machinery appears to labour on its own behalf and the shortening of the working day appears as a destruction of value rather than the liberation of life-time from capital's grip. The chapter thus closes Part IV not merely with a set of economic laws but with a theory of how those laws are misrecognised—setting the stage for the analysis of real subsumption, the production of relative surplus-value, and, eventually, the critique of political economy as a whole. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 10 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 12"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 12", "title": "Chapter 12", "subtitle": "The Concept of Relative Surplus-Value", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 12/Chapter 12  The Concept of Relative Surplus-Value_explanation.html", "word_count": 6614, "read_time": "33 min read", "tags": ["Surplus-Value & Exploitation", "Machinery & Technology"], "keywords": ["Concept of Relative Surplus-Value", "Reduction of Necessary Labour Time", "Increase in Productive Power of Labour", "Cheapening of Wage Goods / Labour-Power", "Individual Value vs Social Market Value"], "text": "Chapter 12 The Concept Of Relative Surplus-Value - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,560 words ⏱️ ~33 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Exhaustive Chapter Analysis: Marx, Capital Vol. I, Chapter 12 — \"The Concept of Relative Surplus-Value\" 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 12 opens Part Four of Capital , Volume One , and it is a hinge chapter in the strict architectural sense: it does not merely add a new category to the theory of surplus-value, it re-founds the theory on a new terrain. Everything preceding it — the analysis of the commodity and money, the transformation of money into capital, the labour process, the production of surplus-value, and the long treatment of the working day — had operated with one technique of exploitation: the prolongation of the working day beyond the point at which the worker reproduces the value of his own labour-power . Chapter 12 announces that this technique, now retrospectively christened absolute surplus-value , is only the first and cruder of capital's two fundamental modes of extracting surplus labour. The second, relative surplus-value , is the subject of Part Four, and this chapter supplies its concept — its formal definition, its necessary presuppositions, its scope conditions, and the precise mechanism by which it is generated. The central problem is posed with quasi-geometric precision. Given a working day of fixed length (line AC, twelve hours) whose internal division is also fixed (AB = ten hours of necessary labour, BC = two hours of surplus labour), how can surplus labour be prolonged without any extension of AC ? Marx's answer is that the surplus segment can grow only by pushing back its own starting point B toward A — that is, by curtailing necessary labour-time. But since the value of labour-power is determined by the labour-time required to reproduce it, a fall in that value is impossible without a rise in the productivity of labour , and a rise in productivity is impossible without a revolution in the mode of production itself (tools, methods of working, technical and social conditions of the labour process). Hence the chapter's thesis: relative surplus-value arises from the curtailment of necessary labour-time consequent upon a revolution in the productive forces, occurring within a working day of unchanged length. The chapter's definition is thus: \"I call that surplus-value which arises from the curtailment of the necessary labour-time, and from the corresponding alteration in the respective lengths of the two components of the working day, relative surplus-value .\" The intellectual objective of the chapter is threefold. First, to derive rather than assert the category, by showing that no other route to increased surplus labour is available under the assumption of equivalent exchange. Second, to specify the scope condition under which the mechanism operates at the level of the total social capital: the productivity revolution must \"seize upon those branches of industry whose products determine the value of labour-power\" — the wage-good industries and the industries supplying their means of production — and the resulting gain in the general rate of surplus-value is proportional to the weight of each commodity in the worker's necessary consumption. Third, and most subtly, to reconcile the individual capitalist's behaviour with the class interest of capital , by showing that an innovating capitalist captures a transient extra surplus-value (the difference between the individual value of his cheapened commodity and its social value) quite independently of whether his commodity enters the worker's subsistence — thereby providing a universal, structural motive for raising productivity, over and above the collective cheapening of labour-power. Its place in the broader framework: Part Four (Chapters 12–15) moves from concept (12) to the concrete historical methods by which relative surplus-value is produced — co-operation (13), the division of labour and manufacture (14), and machinery and large-scale industry (15). Chapter 12 is therefore the conceptual gateway to capital's specifically modern form of domination, in which capital no longer merely \"takes over the labour process in its given or historically transmitted shape\" and lengthens it, but actively transforms the labour process. It also plants the seed of two later theoretical structures: the theory of competition and market value (the chapter's treatment of individual vs. social value and the coercive law of competition), and the law of the tendential fall in the value of commodities alongside rising surplus-value — the apparent paradox Marx resolves through Quesnay's riddle. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Formal Problem: Setting Up the Working-Day Diagram Marx begins by retrospectively characterizing the terrain of Chapters 7–11. The portion of the working day that \"merely produces an equivalent for the value paid by the capitalist for his labour-power\" — necessary labour-time — has until now been treated \"as a constant magnitude.\" Marx immediately qualifies this: it is constant \"under given conditions of production and at a given stage in the economic development of society.\" The constancy is therefore relative to a historical stage , not absolute. This qualification is essential because the whole chapter will consist in showing what happens when the condition is lifted. The recap of the prior results is precise: the worker could work 2, 3, 4, 6, or more hours beyond his necessary labour-time; both the rate of surplus-value (surplus labour ÷ necessary labour) and the length of the working day depended on how far this extra time was prolonged. So while necessary labour-time was constant, \"the total working day was variable.\" The variable element in the earlier analysis was the length of the day; the constant element was its floor . Marx then imposes a new set of givens: a working day whose length and whose division between necessary and surplus labour are both fixed. He renders this in a line diagram: A----------B--C where the whole line AC = a 12-hour working day, AB = 10 hours of necessary labour, BC = 2 hours of surplus labour. The question that drives the entire chapter: \"How can the production of surplus-value be increased, i.e. how can surplus labour be prolonged, without any prolongation, or independently of any prolongation, of the line AC?\" The geometric solution is to move the internal partition point rather than the endpoints: A---------B' -B--C If B'B = half of BC = 1 hour, then moving B to B' gives B'C in place of BC: surplus labour rises by half, from 2 to 3 hours, while the working day remains 12 hours . Marx immediately draws the decisive structural consequence: this extension of surplus labour from BC to B'C is \"evidently impossible without a simultaneous contraction of the necessary labour-time from AB to AB', from 10 hours to 9 hours.\" The prolongation of surplus labour corresponds to a shortening of necessary labour; a portion of the labour-time previously consumed \"in reality, for the worker's own benefit would be converted into labour-time expended for the capitalist.\" The alteration, then, is \"not in the length of the working day, but in its division.\" This is the formal skeleton of relative surplus-value: a purely internal redistribution of a constant day , achieved by moving the boundary between the two parts. 2.2 The First Route and Its Exclusion: Cutting Wages Below the Value of Labour-Power Marx now asks whether the surplus could be enlarged by another route — and here the chapter performs one of its most important methodological exclusions. The duration of surplus labour is given when the length of the working day and the value of labour-power are given; the value of labour-power \"i.e. the labour-time necessary to produce labour-power, determines the labour-time necessary for the reproduction of the value of labour-power.\" He works an example that also functions as an illustration of the classical subsistence-wage doctrine, backed by a chain of citations in footnote 1: 1 hour of work = 6d (sixpence); the daily value of labour-power = 5s (shillings) . Hence the worker must work 10 hours a day to replace the value paid by capital for his labour-power, i.e. to produce an equivalent for the value of the means of subsistence he consumes daily. Given the value of those means of subsistence → the value of labour-power → the duration of necessary labour-time. Surplus labour is then the residual: 12 − 10 = 2 hours. \"It is not easy to see how, under the given conditions, the surplus labour can possibly be prolonged beyond 2 hours.\" But, Marx concedes, the capitalist could pay 4s. 6d. instead of 5s. — or even less. Nine hours' labour-time would suffice to reproduce 4s. 6d., so 3 hours of surplus labour would accrue and surplus-value would rise from 1s. to 1s. 6d. This, however, \"could be attained only by pushing the wage of the worker down below the value of his labour-power.\" The consequences are spelled out materially: with the 4s. 6d. produced in 9 hours, the worker commands one-tenth less of the means of subsistence; \"the reproduction of his labour-power can take place only in a stunted form.\" Surplus labour would be prolonged \"only by transgressing its normal limits\"; its domain extended \"only by a usurpation of part of the domain of necessary labour-time.\" The crucial exclusion: \"Despite the important part which this method plays in practice, we are excluded from considering it here by our assumption that all commodities, including labour-power, are bought and sold at their full value.\" The assumption of equivalent exchange — the postulate that all commodities, labour-power included, are bought and sold at their full value — is the analytical price of admission for the theory of relative surplus-value in its pure form. Under that assumption, the labour-time necessary for the reproduction of labour-power \"cannot be lessened by a fall in the worker's wages below the value of his labour-power, but only by a fall in this value itself.\" The argument is then closed with a directional, almost logical point: given the length of the working day, \"the prolongation of the surplus labour must of necessity originate in the curtailment of the necessary labour-time; the latter cannot arise from the former.\" In the example chosen, the value of labour-power had to fall in fact by one-tenth — from 10 hours to 9 — for surplus labour to be prolonged from 2 to 3 hours. This is a crucial nuance for readers: Marx is not denying that wage-cutting occurs \"in practice\"; he is bracketing it to isolate a mechanism whose logic is independent of cheating, fraud, or the deviation of price from value. The chapter's scientific claim is that relative surplus-value is possible under the strictest honesty of exchange . That is what makes it a systemic category rather than a moral one. 2.3 The Necessary Presupposition: Revolutionizing the Mode of Production A fall in the value of labour-power implies that the same means of subsistence formerly produced in 10 hours can now be produced in 9. \"But this is impossible without an increase in the productivity of labour.\" Marx's illustrative example is deliberately artisanal: a cobbler with a given set of tools makes one pair of boots in a working day of 12 hours; to make two pairs in the same time, \"the productivity of his labour must be doubled; and this cannot be done except by an alteration in his tools or in his mode of working, or both.\" Hence \"the conditions of production of his labour, i.e. his mode of production, and the labour process itself, must be revolutionized.\" He then supplies his formal definition: \"By an increase in the productivity of labour, we mean an alteration in the labour process of such a kind as to shorten the labour-time socially necessary for the production of a commodity, and to endow a given quantity of labour with the power of producing a greater quantity of use-value.\" Two elements should be parsed separately. First, productivity is defined socially , not individually — it is measured by a change in socially necessary labour-time , the average labour-time required under prevailing social conditions. Second, it is defined as a change in the use-value productivity of labour : the same quantity of labour yields more use-values. It is emphatically not defined as producing more value: value depends on the quantity of labour-time, not on the productivity with which that time is expended. This definitional duality — more goods, same value — is the pivot on which the whole chapter turns. The contrast with the earlier analysis is then drawn explicitly. In the production of absolute surplus-value, the mode of production was assumed \"given and invariable\"; capital could take over the labour process \"in its given or historically transmitted shape, and then simply to prolong its duration.\" But \"when surplus-value has to be produced by the conversion of necessary labour into surplus labour, it by no means suffices for capital to take over the labour process in its given or historically transmitted shape.\" The technical and social conditions of the process, \"and consequently the mode of production itself must be revolutionized before the productivity of labour can be increased.\" Only then does the value of labour-power fall and the portion of the day necessary for reproducing it shrink. This passage is, in the architecture of Capital , the point at which capital ceases to be merely the formal appropriator of a pre-existing labour process and becomes its active transformer — the move from what Marx elsewhere calls formal to real subsumption of labour under capital. 2.4 The Formal Definition of Absolute and Relative Surplus-Value With the mechanism established, Marx coins the terminology: \"I call that surplus-value which is produced by the lengthening of the working day, absolute surplus-value . In contrast to this, I call that surplus-value which arises from the curtailment of the necessary labour-time, and from the corresponding alteration in the respective lengths of the two components of the working day, relative surplus-value .\" Three points of precision are worth extracting. (i) The distinction rests on which variable changes : under absolute surplus-value, the working day lengthens while the division between necessary and surplus labour remains (or need not) change; under relative surplus-value, the working day stays fixed and the boundary point moves. (ii) Both are forms of surplus-value, i.e. both are objectifications of unpaid surplus labour — the difference is the method , not the substance. (iii) The definition of relative surplus-value is relational : it specifies that the two components of the day change in opposite directions, with their sum constant. Analytically, relative surplus-value is a pure redistribution . 2.5 The Scope Condition: Which Branches Must Be Seized? Marx now asks a question that a careless reader might overlook: if raising productivity cheapens commodities, does any increase in productivity anywhere cheapen labour-power? The answer is an emphatic no, and the reasoning yields a precise scope condition. \"In order to make the value of labour-power go down, the rise in the productivity of labour must seize upon those branches of industry whose products determine the value of labour-power, and consequently either belong to the category of normal means of subsistence, or are capable of replacing them.\" But the scope condition has a second, technically subtle layer. \"The value of a commodity is determined not only by the quantity of labour which gives it its final form, but also by the quantity of labour contained in the instruments by which it has been produced.\" Thus a pair of boots embodies the cobbler's labour plus the labour in the leather, wax, thread, etc. Consequently, a fall in the value of labour-power is also brought about by productivity increases in \"those industries which supply the instruments of labour and the material for labour, i.e. the physical elements of constant capital which are required for producing the means of subsistence.\" Hence the scope condition must be stated recursively : the wage-goods industries, plus the industries supplying their means of production. And the negative corollary: \"an increase in the productivity of labour in those branches of industry which supply neither the necessary means of subsistence nor the means by which they are produced leaves the value of labour-power undisturbed.\" Luxury industries, for instance, can be revolutionized without touching the rate of surplus-value. 2.6 Proportionality, Aggregation, and the \"Cheapening of Shirts\": The Sum of Reductions The chapter then addresses the magnitude of the effect, and here a second major nuance appears. \"The cheapening of the commodity, of course, causes only a relative fall in the value of labour-power, a fall proportional to the extent to which that commodity enters into the reproduction of labour-power.\" Shirts, for example, are a necessary means of subsistence, \"but are only one out of many.\" The total sum of necessary means of subsistence consists of various commodities, each the product of a distinct industry, and the value of each enters as a component part into the value of labour-power. Crucially, \"the total decrease of necessary labour-time is equal to the sum of all the different reductions in labour-time which have occurred in those various distinct branches of production.\" This is an aggregative law: no single cheapening has more than a fractional effect, but the effects are additive across the relevant industrial system. Marx then makes a methodological move of considerable importance, which he flags as deliberate: \"Here we treat this general result as if it were the direct result and the direct purpose in each individual case.\" That is, he is aware that no individual capitalist who cheapens shirts \"necessarily aims to reduce the value of labour-power and shorten necessary labour-time in proportion to this.\" The individual capitalist contributes to the general rate of surplus-value \"only in so far as he ultimately contributes to this result.\" Then the principle: \"The general and necessary tendencies of capital must be distinguished from their forms of appearance.\" This is the first appearance of the essence/appearance distinction that governs the chapter's methodology, and it recurs twice more. 2.7 The Methodological Excursus: Essence, Competition, and the Real vs. Apparent Motion of the Heavenly Bodies Marx then explicitly declines to undertake a full analysis of competition — \"it is not our intention here to consider the way in which the immanent laws of capitalist production manifest themselves in the external movement of the individual capitals, assert themselves as the coercive laws of competition, and therefore enter into the consciousness of the individual capitalist as the motives which drive him forward.\" Nevertheless he states a principle: \"a scientific analysis of competition is possible only if we can grasp the inner nature of capital, just as the apparent motions of the heavenly bodies are intelligible only to someone who is acquainted with their real motions, which are not perceptible to the senses.\" This is a striking epistemological analogy — invoking the distinction between geocentric apparent motion and heliocentric real motion — and it does double duty: it justifies the abstraction from competition performed in this chapter (which analyzes what competition must express even when the agents are unconscious of it), and it pre-announces that competition will later be analyzed as the form of appearance of the immanent laws. On the strength of the results already achieved, but \"merely on the basis of the results already achieved,\" Marx then adds a set of remarks — which are in fact the analytical heart of the chapter. 2.8 The Numerical Core: Twelve Articles, Individual Value, Social Value, and the Genesis of Extra Surplus-Value The argument now proceeds through a sustained arithmetical construction. Stipulations: 1 hour of labour = 6d; a 12-hour working day produces a value of 6s. Under currently prevailing productivity, 12 articles are produced in those 12 hours. The value of the means of production used up in each article is 6d. Under these circumstances each article costs 1s. — 6d. for the value of the means of production and 6d. for the value newly added. (Check: 12 articles × 6d. means of production = 6s. constant capital; 12 articles × 6d. new value = 6s. new value; total 12s. = 12 articles × 1s. ✓) Now let one capitalist double the productivity of labour, producing 24 articles instead of 12 in a 12-hour day . The value of the means of production per article is unchanged (6d). Hence the value of each article falls to 9d : 6d. for means of production + 3d. for the value newly added by labour. Note the crucial invariance: the day's labour \"creates, as before, a new value of 6s. and no more,\" which is now spread over twice as many articles. Each article embodies 3d. instead of 6d.; equivalently, \"only half an hour of labour-time, instead of a whole hour, is now added to the means of production while they are being transformed into each article.\" Then the decisive theoretical step: \"The individual value of these articles is now below their social value.\" Definitionally, the individual value of a commodity is the labour-time it actually costs this producer; the social value is \"the labour-time socially required for its production\" — the average under prevailing social conditions. Each article \"costs, on an average, 1s., and represents 2 hours of social labour; but under the altered mode of production it costs only 9d., or contains only 1½ hours' labour.\" Marx states the principle baldly: \"The real value of a commodity, however, is not its individual, but its social value.\" The payoff follows immediately. If the innovating capitalist sells at the social value of 1s., he sells 3d. above the individual value of each article, \"and thus he realizes an extra surplus-value of 3d.\" Here is the birth of the concept later elaborated as super-profit (and, in Volume III, as differential rent-like phenomena): the innovator pockets the gap between individual and social value. 2.9 The Market Constraint and the Price Cut: Why Extra Surplus-Value Survives at 10d. Marx does not let the argument rest at the social-value price, because a quantitative obstacle appears. The working day of 12 hours is now represented \"for him\" by 24 articles instead of 12. Therefore \"in order to get rid of the product of one working day, the demand must be double what it was, i.e. the market must become twice as extensive.\" Since, other things equal, a capitalist can command a more extensive market only if his prices fall, \"he will therefore sell them above their individual but below their social value, say at 10d. each. By this means he still squeezes an extra surplus-value of one penny out of each.\" This is a genuinely important piece of analysis, and it produces a general motive: \"This augmentation of surplus-value is pocketed by the capitalist himself, whether or not his commodities belong to the class of necessary means of subsistence, and therefore participate in determining the general value of labour-power .\" Therefore — and Marx italicizes the inference logically rather than typographically — \"quite independently of this, there is a motive for each individual capitalist to cheapen his commodities by increasing the productivity of labour.\" In other words: the incentive to revolutionize production does not depend on the commodity being a wage-good. Relative surplus-value in the strict sense (a rise in the general rate) requires wage-goods; but the drive to cheapen is universal across all branches because of extra surplus-value. This is how Marx links the micro-rationality of the individual capitalist to the macro-tendency of capital as a whole — without collapsing the two. 2.10 Recomputing the Partition: 5:1 Becomes 5:3, and the \"Intensified Labour\" Question Marx now insists — against any reading that would treat the innovator's gain as a mere market accident or a species of unequal exchange — that \"even in this case, the increased production of surplus-value arises from the curtailment of the necessary labour-time, and the corresponding prolongation of the surplus labour.\" He re-derives the partition of the day under the innovated conditions. Stipulations: necessary labour-time = 10 hours; value of a day's labour-power = 5s.; surplus labour-time = 2 hours; daily surplus-value = 1s. Now the capitalist produces 24 articles and sells them at 10d. each, making 20s. in all . Since the value of the means of production is 12s., \"14⅖ of these articles merely replace the constant capital advanced.\" (Check: 12s. ÷ 10d. per article = 12s. ÷ (10/12)s. = 14.4 = 14⅖ articles ✓.) \"The labour of the 12-hour working day is represented by the remaining 9⅗ articles.\" Since the price of labour-power is 5s., 6 articles represent the necessary labour-time (5s. ÷ 10d. = 6 ✓) and 3⅗ articles the surplus labour . \"The ratio of necessary labour to surplus labour, which under average social conditions was 5:1, is now only 5:3.\" Marx then offers an alternative derivation. The value of the product of the 12-hour day is 20s.; of this, 12s. represent the value of the means of production, \"a value that merely re-appears in the finished product.\" There remain 8s. , \"which are the expression in money of the value newly created during the working day.\" This sum is greater than that in which average social labour of the same kind is expressed: 12 hours of the latter are expressed by only 6s. Hence: \"The exceptionally productive labour acts as intensified labour; it creates in equal periods of time greater values than average social labour of the same kind.\" (Footnote 4 flags that intensified labour was discussed in Chapter 1, Section 2, pp. 135–6.) The worker, still paid 5s. as the daily value of labour-power, now needs to work only 7½ hours to reproduce this value (not 10). His surplus labour is therefore increased by 2½ hours (from 2 to 4½), and the surplus-value he produces grows from 1s. into 3s. (Check: 8s. new value − 5s. wages = 3s. surplus; necessary share = 5/8 × 12 = 7.5 hours; surplus share = 3/8 × 12 = 4.5 hours; ratio 7.5:4.5 = 5:3 ✓; surplus-value rate rises from 20% to 60%.) A nuance the reader must register: there is a subtle tension in the text between the earlier statement that \"the day's labour creates, as before, a new value of 6s. and no more\" and the later statement that the value newly created during the working day is 8s. Marx reconciles these by the \"intensified labour\" formula: the innovator sells above the individual value of the commodity (9d.) but below its social value (12d.), at 10d., and the excess over individual value is realized as value that the exceptionally productive labour has, in effect, added. Analytically the extra 2s. is a realized surplus attributable to the innovation, and Marx's framework treats exceptionally productive labour as equivalent to intensified labour — labour of a higher degree of intensity, which by the Chapter 1 analysis creates more value per unit of time. The mechanism by which the deviation between individual and social value is converted into value remains one of the most debated passages in Volume I; but within the chapter, the conclusion is unambiguous: the result is still a curtailment of necessary labour-time, not an extension of the working day. The methodological punchline follows: \"Hence the capitalist who applies the improved method of production appropriates and devotes to surplus labour a greater portion of the working day than the other capitalists in the same business. He does as an individual what capital itself taken as a whole does when engaged in producing relative surplus-value.\" 2.11 The Self-Dissolution of Extra Surplus-Value and Competition as a Coercive Law The innovation, however, is self-undermining at the level of the individual. \"This extra surplus-value vanishes as soon as the new method of production is generalized, for then the difference between the individual value of the cheapened commodity and its social value vanishes.\" When all producers use the new method, the socially necessary labour-time falls to the new level, social value falls to individual value, and the extra 3d. (or 2s.) disappears. But the law of value does not disappear. It operates with two faces: \"The law of the determination of value by labour-time makes itself felt to the individual capitalist who applies the new method of production by compelling him to sell his goods under their social value; this same law, acting as a coercive law of competition , forces his competitors to adopt the new method.\" Footnote 5 is a remarkable pre-Marxian formulation of this compulsion from a 1720 pamphlet: if a neighbour \"by doing much with little labour, can sell cheap, I must contrive to sell as cheap as he… every art, trade, or engine, doing work with labour of fewer hands, and consequently cheaper, begets in others a kind of necessity and emulation, either of using the same art, trade, or engine, or of inventing something like it.\" The class-level conclusion is then drawn with care: \"The general rate of surplus-value is therefore ultimately affected by the whole process only when the increase in the productivity of labour has seized upon those branches of production and cheapened those commodities that contribute towards the necessary means of subsistence, and are therefore elements of the value of labour-power.\" Thus the analysis separates three levels: (a) the individual capitalist, who transitorily gains extra surplus-value wherever he innovates; (b) the branch , from which the extra surplus-value vanishes upon generalization; and (c) social capital as a whole , whose rate of surplus-value rises only if the cheapened commodities enter the value of labour-power. This three-level architecture is the analytical spine of the chapter. 2.12 The Proportionality Laws and Capital's Immanent Drive Marx now summarizes in the form of proportionalities. \"The value of commodities stands in inverse ratio to the productivity of labour.\" Since the value of labour-power depends on the values of commodities, so too does it stand in inverse ratio. But \"relative surplus-value, however, is directly proportional to the productivity of labour. It rises and falls together with productivity.\" This is the chapter's central proportional statement: productivity up → commodity values down → value of labour-power down → relative surplus-value up. He then restates the arithmetic of value conservation within the day: \"The value of money being assumed to be constant, an average social working day of 12 hours always produces the same new value, 6s., no matter how this sum may be apportioned between surplus-value and wages.\" But if productivity raises the value of labour-power's determinants, and the daily value of labour-power falls from 5s. to 3s., then surplus-value rises from 1s. to 3s.; \"10 hours were necessary for the reproduction of the value of the labour-power; now only 6 are required. 4 hours have been set free, and can be annexed to the domain of surplus labour. \" Hence the chapter's most quoted formulation of capital's dynamic: \"Capital therefore has an immanent drive, and a constant tendency, towards increasing the productivity of labour, in order to cheapen commodities and, by cheapening commodities, to cheapen the worker himself.\" Footnote 6 assembles eighteenth- and nineteenth-century sources asserting precisely this: that whatever lowers the price of provisions lowers the price of labour, that \"wages are decreased in the same proportion as the powers of production increase,\" and that \"machinery, it is true, cheapens the necessaries of life, but it also cheapens the labourer.\" 2.13 The Quesnay Riddle and Its Solution The chapter then poses its paradox in the form of a historical riddle, attributing it to Quesnay , \"one of the founders of political economy,\" who \"used to torment his opponents with this question, and they could find no answer to it.\" The substance: the capitalist's \"sole concern is to produce exchange-value,\" yet he \"continually strives to bring down the exchange-value of commodities.\" Quesnay's own formulation, quoted in French in footnote 7, notes that reducing the costs of labour in manufacture lowers the price of the finished article, yet the physiocrats believed the production of wealth through artisans' labour consisted in augmenting the exchange-value of their products — an apparent contradiction. Marx's solution is the simultaneity theorem established over the preceding sections: since the same process both cheapens commodities and augments the surplus-value contained in them , there is no paradox. The capitalist does not care about the absolute value of the commodity. \"All that interests him is the surplus-value present in it, which can be realized by sale. Realization [ Realisierung ] of the surplus-value necessarily carries with it the replacement of the value advanced.\" The falling absolute value of the product is not merely compatible with, but the means to , a rising surplus-value. What looks like the capitalist working against his own interest (producing exchange-value, yet depreciating it) is in fact the very expression of his interest — because he is interested not in value as such but in unpaid value , in the surplus-value residue. Note that Marx does not deny the real-world phenomenon that cheapening may also arise from competition among capitalists selling at a loss; he locates the systemic logic in the identity of the cheapening and surplus-augmenting process. The solution is also a correction of the physiocrats and of the classical school: they saw cheapening and could not square it with wealth-as-exchange-value because they lacked the distinction between the value of a commodity and the surplus-value it contains. 2.14 The Real Objective: Shortening Necessary Labour-Time, Not the Working Day The chapter closes with a sharp polemic that also serves as a conceptual boundary-marking. \"The shortening of the working day, therefore, is by no means what is aimed at in capitalist production, when labour is economized by increasing its productivity.\" What is aimed at is only \"the shortening of the labour-time necessary for the production of a definite quantity of commodities.\" The distinction is drawn with a concrete image: if the worker's productivity rises tenfold and he produces ten times as many commodities, spending one-tenth as much labour-time on each, he may still work 12 hours and produce 1,200 articles instead of 120; \"indeed, his working day may simultaneously be prolonged, so as to make him produce say 1,400 articles in 14 hours.\" This yields the polemical target: \"in the treatises of economists of the stamp of MacCulloch, Ure, Senior and the like, we may read on one page that the worker owes a debt of gratitude to capital for developing his productivity, because the necessary labour-time is thereby shortened, and on the next page that he must prove his gratitude by working in future for 15 hours instead of 10.\" The two claims — the boon of productivity and the demand for longer hours — are not contradictions in the ideology because both serve the same master. The chapter's final sentence states the objective precisely: \"The objective of the development of the productivity of labour within the context of capitalist production is the shortening of that part of the working day in which the worker must work for himself, and the lengthening, thereby, of the other part of the day, in which he is free to work for nothing for the capitalist.\" And the final line defers the concrete study: \"How far this result can also be attained without cheapening commodities will appear from the following chapters, where we examine the particular methods of producing relative surplus-value.\" That forward pointer to Chapters 13–15 (co-operation, manufacture, machinery) sets the agenda of the rest of Part Four: the channels of relative surplus-value that operate through organization and technology rather than through cheapening of wage-goods per se. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS First principle — the internalization of class conflict into the working day. The chapter establishes that capital's exploitation does not require the arbitrary lengthening of the day (though it pursues that too). It can install itself inside a day of constant legal and physiological length by continuously shifting the internal boundary between necessary and surplus labour. This yields a systemic law of capital: surplus labour can grow without limit in principle within a fixed working day, because its ceiling is set not by clock-time but by the productivity of the wage-good sectors. The working day is thus at once a fixed container and a moving frontier. Second principle — the primacy of the mode of production over the act of exchange. By excluding wage-cutting below the value of labour-power and assuming equivalent exchange throughout, the chapter demonstrates that relative surplus-value is a phenomenon of production , not of circulation. No fraudulent exchange is required; the innovating capitalist buys labour-power at its value and sells the product below its former social value, yet realizes extra surplus-value. The source of all surplus-value is unpaid labour time; the method of relative surplus-value is the technological reorganization of the labour process so that the time needed to reproduce labour-power shrinks. Third principle — the recursive scope of exploitation. Relative surplus-value at the level of social capital requires productivity gains in wage-good industries and in the industries producing their means of production — a recursively expanding industrial system. Thus even industries that produce nothing the worker consumes contribute to the cheapening of labour-power if they supply constant capital to wage-good industries. This is the chapter's most technically dense contribution to the theory: it integrates the analysis of constant capital into the theory of surplus-value, showing that the value of the instruments and materials of production is as determinant of the value of labour-power as the labour spent finishing the goods. Fourth principle — the concrete universal of the productivity drive. Extra surplus-value supplies a universal motive for cheapening across all branches — including luxury goods and capital goods — because any innovator can sell above individual and below social value. The chapter thereby explains, without recourse to the intentions of capitalists, why capital is structurally, continuously technological and competitive: the law of value, acting as the coercive law of competition, forces generalization; generalization erases the extra surplus-value; the race begins again. Capital is here revealed as an internally driven growth machine whose competitive dynamics are the phenomenal form of the law of value. Fifth principle — the essence/appearance distinction as scientific method. The chapter's repeated insistence — general and necessary tendencies versus forms of appearance; the real versus the apparent motions of the heavenly bodies; treating the aggregate result as if it were the direct purpose — provides the methodology for the rest of Capital . Competition is not the origin of the laws but the mode in which they assert themselves as coercive necessities. This explains why Marx can analyze relative surplus-value without a full theory of market prices, competition, or credit: those belong to a later level of analysis. Broader practical and theoretical implications. The chapter retroactively repositions the earlier analysis of absolute surplus-value as the historically earlier, more primitive form, and it positions relative surplus-value as capital's mature and specifically \"modern\" mode — one that will be developed concretely through co-operation, manufacture, and machinery. It thereby supplies the analytical bridge from the theory of value to the theory of industrial organization. Its immediate political-economic implication is the refutation of the \"gratitude\" ideology of MacCulloch, Ure, and Senior: the shortening of necessary labour-time is not a benefit conferred on the worker but the objective of capital, whose counterpart is either the intensification of labour, the lengthening of the day, or the lowering of the value of labour-power through the recursive cheapening of the worker's own subsistence. And its deepest philosophical implication is that the capitalist's seeming irrationality — producing exchange-values while systematically destroying them — is no irrationality at all, once one understands that his object is not value in its absolute magnitude but surplus-value, the immanent social relation of exploitation embedded in the commodity. The \"Quesnay riddle\" is thus resolved not by a tweak to doctrine but by the completed distinction between value, use-value, and surplus-value — the distinction that makes the whole of Capital possible. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 11 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 13"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 13", "title": "Chapter 13", "subtitle": "Co-operation", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 13/Chapter 13  Co-operation_explanation.html", "word_count": 7360, "read_time": "37 min read", "tags": ["Division of Labour & Manufacture", "Machinery & Technology"], "keywords": ["Co-operation & Collective Worker", "Social Productive Power of Combined Labour", "Command & Despotism in the Capitalist Workplace", "Economy in Means of Production", "Simultaneous Work by Multiple Hands"], "text": "Chapter 13 Co-Operation - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 7,274 words ⏱️ ~36 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 13: Co-operation — An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 13, \"Co-operation,\" is the opening salvo of Part Four of Capital , Volume I — the section devoted to the production of relative surplus-value . Its placement is deliberate and structural. The preceding part (on absolute surplus-value) had analyzed how capital lengthens the working day to extract surplus labour. But Marx here pivots from the duration of labour to its social organization . The chapter's central problem is deceptively simple: what happens when capital gathers many workers under one roof and sets them to work together? Marx's answer is that nothing changes quantitatively in the production of value — yet everything changes qualitatively in the production of use-values and in the social relations of production. This dialectical tension between the quantitative equivalence of aggregated value-production and the qualitative novelty of combined labour is the chapter's beating heart. The core thesis is threefold. First , simple co-operation — the simultaneous employment of numerous wage-labourers by a single capital on the same labour process — is the historically and conceptually primordial form of capitalist production. It is \"the starting-point of capitalist production,\" the moment \"the birth of capital itself.\" Manufacture, at its earliest, is nothing but an enlargement of the guild master's workshop; the initial difference is \"purely quantitative.\" Second , and crucially, this quantitative expansion secretly generates a new qualitative power . When workers co-operate in a planned way, they produce a social productive power of labour that is not the mere sum of individual powers. This collective force — arising from the fusion of many forces, from the division of a complex operation into simultaneous phases, from the temporal compression enabled by many hands, from the stimulation of rivalry and \"animal spirits,\" and from the joint economization of the means of production — is a free gift to capital. It costs capital nothing, and because it only materializes once labour has been subsumed into capital, it appears as a power inherent in capital itself. Third , the chapter anatomizes the political and coercive form that this co-operation assumes. Because co-operation brings workers into contact and hence into potential resistance, and because the means of production confront the wage-labourer as \"another's property\" ( fremdes Eigentum ), capital's direction of the labour process is twofold in content (a technical function of social labour plus a function of exploitation) but purely despotic in form . The scale of co-operation necessitates a specialized hierarchy of managers and overseers — an \"industrial army\" with its officers and NCOs — who exercise supervision in the name of capital. Marx's decisive inversion of bourgeois ideology is crystallized here: \"It is not because he is a leader of industry that a man is a capitalist; on the contrary, he is a leader of industry because he is a capitalist.\" Intellectually, the chapter sits at the hinge of Marx's entire architectonic. It prepares the ground for the division of labour in manufacture (Chapter 14) and machinery and modern industry (Chapter 15) by establishing simple co-operation as the \"fundamental form of the capitalist mode of production\" that persists beneath all its more developed incarnations. It also advances the theory of relative surplus-value by showing how capital raises productive power without intensifying exploitation one worker at a time, and it deepens the theory of formal versus real subsumption of labour under capital — the shift from capital command as a mere \"formal result\" of the wage-relation to capital command as a \"real condition of production,\" as indispensable as a general's command on a battlefield. Finally, the chapter situates capitalist co-operation in a materialist history of labour's social forms, contrasting it with the communal co-operation of tribal and Indian village life (grounded in common ownership) and with the slave-based co-operation of ancient empires and plantations (grounded in relations of domination and servitude). The historical specificity is the point: co-operation is not inherently capitalist, but capital appropriates and transforms it, so that from the standpoint of the peasant and artisan, co-operation itself appears as the distinctive, epochal form of the capitalist process of production. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Quantitative Threshold: Large-Scale Simultaneous Employment as the Genesis of Capitalist Production Marx opens by recalling an established conclusion: \"Capitalist production only really begins... when each individual capital simultaneously employs a comparatively large number of workers,\" such that the labour-process is carried on \"on an extensive scale\" and yields \"relatively large quantities of products.\" The precise definition he supplies is worth pausing over, because every subsequent argument depends on it. A large number of workers working together, at the same time, in one place (or in the same field of labour), in order to produce the same sort of commodity , under the command of the same capitalist — this constitutes the starting-point of capitalist production. The three operative criteria are simultaneity , spatial co-presence , and unity of command (one capital, one capitalist). All further distinctions — of technique, division of labour, machinery — are, at this threshold, absent. The historical-conceptual claim is emphasized: this starting-point is true \"both historically and conceptually.\" Historically, because manufacture ( Manufaktur ), in its earliest stages, \"can hardly be distinguished\" from the handicraft trades ( Handwerksindustrie ) of the guilds except by the greater number of workers simultaneously employed by the same individual capital . Conceptually, because the logical category of capitalist production presupposes the aggregation of labour-powers under one command before any qualitative transformation of the labour process can be theorized. The workshop of the guild master, \"merely an enlargement,\" is thus the chrysalis of the factory. \"At first, then, the difference is purely quantitative.\" The Value-Theoretic Puzzle: Why Numbers Don't Change Value — And How They Do Here Marx inserts a crucial recapitulation from the theory of value, and it functions as the chapter's provocation. \"We have shown that the surplus-value produced by a given capital is equal to the surplus-value produced by each worker multiplied by the number of workers simultaneously employed.\" In formal terms, if s is the surplus-value produced by one worker and n is the number of workers, the total surplus-value S = s × n . The immediate implication is that the number of workers \"does not in itself affect either the rate of surplus-value [s/v] or the degree of exploitation of labour-power.\" The rate is a ratio internal to each labour-power; multiplying the workers multiplies the numerator and denominator alike, leaving the rate untouched. Marx illustrates with a value-production calculation. \"If a working day of 12 hours is objectified in 6 shillings, 1,200 working days of 12 hours will be objectified in 1,200 times 6 shillings.\" Whether we express this as 12 × 1,200 working-hours or as twelve working-hours multiplied 1,200-fold, the aggregate value produced is identical. \"In the production of value a number of workers merely rank as so many individual workers,\" he concludes, \"and it therefore makes no difference in the value produced whether the 1,200 men work separately or united under the command of one capitalist.\" This is the abstract labour side of the twofold nature of labour: value is congealed homogeneous labour-time, indifferent to how it is socially packaged. It is essential to grasp that Marx is not backtracking here; he is setting up a contrast. The identity holds for value — but not for use-value , and not for the social relations of production. The Emergence of Average Social Labour: The Statistical Logic of the Collective Working Day The moment of transition is signaled by the phrase \"Nevertheless, within certain limits, a modification does take place.\" Marx's argument turns on a statistical insight: \"The labour objectified in value is labour of an average social quality, it is an expression of average labour-power.\" But an average is \"merely the average of a number of separate magnitudes all of one kind, but differing in quantity.\" In every industry, each individual worker deviates from the average. These individual differences are \"errors\" in the mathematical sense — deviations that \"compensate each other and vanish whenever a certain minimum number of workers are employed together.\" Marx invokes Edmund Burke, \"that famous sophist and sycophant,\" who from his practical observations as a farmer asserted that in a platoon as small as five farm labourers, \"all individual differences in the labour vanish,\" so that any five adult farm labourers will do as much work as any other five. Marx neither endorses the precise number nor dismisses the principle; \"however that may be,\" he grants the statistical logic . The mechanism is then formalized through the collective working day . Let each individual work 12 hours. The collective working day of twelve simultaneously employed workers therefore consists of 144 hours. Although each man's labour may diverge from average social labour — each requiring a different amount of time for the same operation — the working day of each \"possesses the qualities of an average social working day, because it forms one-twelfth of the collective working day of 144 hours.\" Each individual day is an aliquot part of the collective day. And this holds \"no matter whether the twelve men help each other in their work, or whether the connection between their operations consists merely in the fact that the men are all working for the same capitalist.\" This is a critical nuance: the averaging effect does not require technical interaction. Mere aggregation of labour-powers under one capital, at a certain minimum scale, suffices to render the labour socially average. The footnote to Quetelet and his \"average man\" situates Marx's argument within contemporaneous statistical theory — Marx is appropriating the mathematics of probabilities to explain a social result of combined labour. The Counterfactual of the Six Small Masters: How Co-operation Secures the Law of Valorization The argument is then sharpened by a thought-experiment. Suppose the twelve men are employed in six pairs by six different \"small masters.\" It becomes \"entirely a matter of chance\" whether each master produces the same value, and hence whether he secures the general rate of surplus-value. Divergences occur in individual cases. If one worker requires considerably more time than is socially necessary, \"the duration of the necessary labour-time would, in his case, diverge significantly from the labour-time socially necessary,\" and his labour \"would therefore not count as average labour.\" His labour-power \"would not count as average labour-power\"; it would be unsaleable, or saleable only below the average value of labour-power. Marx notes that capitalist production supplies the means of fixing this minimum of efficiency — foreshadowing the disciplinary apparatus examined later — yet \"this minimum diverges from the average,\" even as the capitalist pays the average value of labour-power. The consequence for the six small masters: one \"would squeeze out more than the average rate of surplus-value, another less.\" The inequalities \"would cancel out for the society as a whole, but not for the individual masters.\" The theoretical payoff is stated with precision: \"The law of valorization therefore comes fully into its own for the individual producer only when he produces as a capitalist and employs a number of workers simultaneously, i.e. when from the outset he sets in motion labour of a socially average character.\" This is a profound result. The law of value — that commodities exchange at their socially necessary labour-time — is realized at the level of the individual enterprise only through the scale of co-operation. Competition among small masters distributes the gains randomly; the collective working day, by internalizing the average, makes the general rate of surplus-value a reliable result. Co-operation thus converts the statistical average into an organizational reality for the individual capitalist. The Revolution in the Objective Conditions: Economy in the Means of Production A second, independent consequence is then introduced: \"Even without an alteration in the method of work, the simultaneous employment of a large number of workers produces a revolution in the objective conditions of the labour process.\" The focus shifts from labour-power to constant capital . Buildings, store-houses for raw material, implements and utensils used simultaneously or in turns — \"a portion of the means of production\" — are now \"consumed jointly.\" Marx makes a two-sided argument about the exchange-value of these means. On the one hand, their exchange-value is not increased , \"for the exchange-value of a commodity is not raised by any increase in the exploitation of its use-value.\" On the other hand, they are \"used in common, and therefore on a larger scale.\" A room where twenty weavers work at twenty looms must be larger than that of a single weaver with two assistants; but \"it costs less labour to build one workshop for twenty persons than to build ten to accommodate two weavers each.\" Hence the value of concentrated means of production \"does not increase in direct proportion to their extent and useful effect.\" This produces an economy in constant capital with a determinate effect on commodity value. When means of production are consumed in common, they give up a smaller part of their value to each single product — for two reasons: (i) the total value they part with is spread over a greater number of products ; (ii) their value, though greater in absolute terms, is relatively less \"from the point of view of their sphere of action\" than the value of separate means. Consequently \"the value of a part of the constant capital falls, and, in proportion to the size of this fall, the total value of the commodity also falls.\" The effect is \"the same as if the means of production had cost less.\" Marx emphasizes that this economy \"arises entirely out of their joint consumption in the labour process by many workers,\" and — importantly — this social character attaches to the instruments of labour \"even when the numerous workers assembled together do not assist each other but merely work side by side.\" He notes: \"A portion of the instruments of labour acquires this social character before the labour process itself does so.\" Marx then distinguishes two points of view on this economy. Firstly, \"in so far as it cheapens commodities, and thereby brings about a fall in the value of labour-power\" — this directly feeds the theory of relative surplus-value, since a cheaper wage-good lowers the value of labour-power and thus raises surplus-value without lengthening the working day. Secondly, \"in so far as it alters the ratio of surplus-value to the total capital advanced,\" that is, the ratio s/(c+v) , which is the rate of profit . The footnote clarifies this: s /( c + v ) is the rate of profit (since c + v = C ), as opposed to the rate of surplus-value s / v . Marx explicitly defers this second aspect to the first section of Volume 3, Chapter 2 (\"The Rate of Profit\"), conceding that the analysis \"forces this tearing apart of the object under investigation.\" He adds an ideological observation: this fragmentation \"corresponds also to the spirit of capitalist production,\" because the worker encounters the instruments of labour as another man's property, so \"economy in their use appears, from his standpoint, to be a separate operation,\" one disconnected from the methods that raise his own productivity. The Definition of Co-operation and the Emergence of a New Collective Productive Power Marx now delivers the formal definition: \"When numerous workers work together side by side in accordance with a plan, whether in the same process, or in different but connected processes, this form of labour is called co-operation .\" Two criteria are embedded: plan (conscious direction) and simultaneity (side-by-side labour), across either a single or several connected processes. The definition is immediately followed by the chapter's most famous claim — the emergence of a new productive power . Marx deploys a military analogy: \"Just as the offensive power of a squadron of cavalry, or the defensive power of an infantry regiment, is essentially different from the sum of the offensive or defensive powers of the individual soldiers taken separately, so the sum total of the mechanical forces exerted by isolated workers differs from the social force that is developed when many hands co-operate in the same undivided operation.\" Examples include raising a heavy weight, turning a winch, clearing an obstacle. In such cases \"the effect of the combined labour could either not be produced at all by isolated individual labour, or it could be produced only by a great expenditure of time, or on a very dwarf-like scale.\" The claim is emphatic: \"Not only do we have here an increase in the productive power of the individual, by means of co-operation, but the creation of a new productive power, which is intrinsically a collective one.\" This is the qualitative leap — the whole genuinely greater than the sum of its parts, a social force that has no individual analogue. The Mechanical and Psychological Augmentation of Force: The Social Animal and \"Animal Spirits\" The argument then broadens from the mechanical to the psychological . \"Apart from the new power that arises from the fusion of many forces into a single force, mere social contact begets in most industries a rivalry and a stimulation of the 'animal spirits', which heightens the efficiency of each individual worker.\" Hence a dozen people working together produce \"far more\" in a 144-hour collective day than twelve isolated men each working 12 hours, and \"far more than one man who works 12 days in succession.\" The source: \"man, if not as Aristotle thought a political animal, is at all events a social animal.\" Marx's footnote corrects Aristotle: the real meaning of the definition is that man is by nature a citizen of a town ( polis ), characteristic of classical antiquity — just as Franklin's \"tool-making animal\" is characteristic of Yankeedom. This is a small but telling materialist-historical aside: definitions of human nature themselves bear the stamp of their epoch. Temporal Compression: Simultaneity, Phases, and Critical Moments Next, Marx develops the temporal dimension of co-operation's productivity. Even when workers perform the same task, that labour \"may correspond to a distinct phase of the labour process,\" and \"as a result of the system of co-operation, the object of labour passes through the phases of the process more quickly than before.\" His illustration: a dozen masons in a row passing stones from the foot of a ladder to its summit. Each does the same thing, \"and yet their separate acts form connected parts of one total operation; these acts are particular phases which each stone must go through, and the stones are thus carried up more quickly by the twenty-four hands of the row of men than they could be if each man went separately up and down the ladder with his load.\" The object of labour \"is carried over the same distance in a shorter time.\" (The Skarbek footnote confirms this is a \"partial division of labour\" even under identity of task.) The spatial-temporal point is reinforced: a building taken in hand \"on different sides simultaneously\" means co-operating masons do the same work, yet twelve masons in 144 collective hours \"make much more progress with the building than one mason could make working for 12 days, or 144 hours.\" The reason: \"a body of men working together have hands and eyes both in front and behind, and can be said to be to a certain extent omnipresent. The various parts of the product come to fruition simultaneously.\" Marx stresses that this elementary identity of work \"plays a great part in co-operation, even at its most fully developed stage.\" And crucially, when the labour process is complicated , \"the sheer number of the co-operators permits the apportionment of various operations to different hands, and consequently their simultaneous performance,\" shortening the total time (with a Destutt de Tracy footnote on fishing — one rows, one holds the rudder, a third casts the net). Marx then introduces the concept of critical moments — \"periods of time determined by the nature of the labour process itself, during which certain definite results must be obtained.\" Examples: sheep-shearing, wheat harvest, herring fishing. Here \"the quantity and quality of the product depend on the initiation and the completion of the work at certain definite points in time,\" and \"the time the labour process may take is laid down in advance.\" The arithmetic of temporal compulsion is stark: \"A single person cannot carve a working day of more than, say, 12 hours out of the natural day, but 100 men co-operating can extend the working day to 1,200 hours.\" The \"shortness of the time allowed for the work is compensated for by the large mass of labour thrown into the field of production at the decisive moment.\" Completion within the deadline \"depends on the simultaneous application of numerous combined working days\"; useful effect \"depends on the number of workers\"; and this number \"is always smaller than the number of isolated workers that would be required to do the same amount of work in the same period.\" The empirical cost of its absence is given: corn wasted annually in the western United States, and cotton in eastern India \"where English rule has destroyed the old communities\" — a pointed historical-materialist footnote about the destruction of pre-capitalist co-operation. Spatial Dialectics: Extension of the Arena and Contraction of the Field ( Faux Frais ) Marx turns to the spatial dimension, framed as a dialectic. On the one hand, co-operation \"allows work to be carried on over a large area; for certain labour processes, therefore, it is required simply by the physical constitution of the object of labour.\" Examples: draining marshes, constructing dykes, irrigation, canals, roads, railways. On the other hand, \"while extending the scale of production it renders possible a relative contraction of its arena.\" This seemingly paradoxical \"simultaneous restriction of space and extension of effectiveness\" allows \"a large number of incidental expenses ( faux frais ) to be spared,\" and results \"from the massing together of workers and of various labour processes, and from the concentration of the means of production.\" The R. Jones footnote supplies the historical illustration: capital and labour that once \"loosely occupied 500 acres\" are \"concentrated for the more complete tillage of 100\" — space is concentrated, yet the sphere of production is enlarged relative to that of a single independent agent. The gloss on faux frais explains it as a technical French expression (Garnier, Say) for costs not directly incurred in production, originating in Adam Smith's distinction between productive and unproductive labour — a small lexical gesture that ties the analysis to the political-economy tradition Marx is both using and criticizing. The Consolidated Theorem: The Social Productive Power of Labour Marx now gathers every previously enumerated cause into a single synthesis. The combined working day produces \"a greater quantity of use-values than an equal sum of isolated working days,\" and \"consequently diminishes the labour-time necessary for the production of a given useful effect.\" He lists the causes of this increased productivity: whether the combined day (i) heightens mechanical force, (ii) extends the sphere of action over a greater space, (iii) contracts the field of production relative to scale, (iv) sets large masses of labour to work at the critical moment, (v) excites rivalry and raises animal spirits, (vi) impresses on similar operations \"the stamp of continuity and many-sidedness,\" (vii) performs different operations simultaneously, (viii) economizes the means of production by use in common, or (ix) lends individual labour \"the character of average social labour.\" The exhaustive catalogue matters because Marx insists: \"whichever of these is the cause of the increase, the special productive power of the combined working day is, under all circumstances, the social productive power of labour , or the productive power of social labour. This power arises from co-operation itself.\" The chapter's philosophical summit follows: \"When the worker co-operates in a planned way with others, he strips off the fetters of his individuality, and develops the capabilities of his species.\" Here Marx smuggles in an almost Feuerbachian-humanist formulation — co-operation as species-being ( Gattungswesen ) realized — which coexists uneasily with, and is immediately subordinated to, the appropriation argument that follows. The Capitalist Condition: Concentration of Capital as Material Presupposition From the social nature of co-operation, Marx derives its dependence on capital . \"As a general rule, workers cannot co-operate without being brought together: their assembly in one place is a necessary condition for their co-operation.\" Hence \"wage-labourers cannot co-operate unless they are employed simultaneously by the same capital, the same capitalist, and therefore unless their labour-powers are bought simultaneously by him.\" The total value of these labour-powers, the day's or week's wages, \"must be ready in the pocket of the capitalist before the workers themselves are ready to start the process of production.\" This establishes a scale threshold : \"The payment of 300 workers at once, even though only for one day, requires a greater outlay of capital than the payment of a smaller number of men, week by week, during a whole year.\" Therefore \"the number of the workers that co-operate, or the scale of co-operation, depends in the first instance on the amount of capital that the individual capitalist can spare for the purchase of labour-power\" — i.e., on \"the extent to which a single capitalist has command over the means of subsistence of a number of workers.\" The same logic applies to constant capital. \"For example, the outlay on raw material is thirty times as great for the capitalist who employs 300 men as it is for each of the thirty capitalists who employ ten men.\" The value and quantity of instruments used in common \"does not, it is true, increase at the same rate as the number of workers, but it does increase very considerably.\" Marx's conclusion: \"Hence concentration of large masses of the means of production in the hands of individual capitalists is a material condition for the co-operation of wage-labourers, and the extent of co-operation, or the scale of production, depends on the extent of this concentration.\" He links this back to an earlier finding: a minimum capital is needed so that the number of workers, and the surplus-value produced, \"might suffice to liberate the employer himself from manual labour, to convert him from a small master into a capitalist, and thus formally to establish the capital-relation.\" Now that minimum is shown to be \"a material condition for the conversion of numerous isolated and independent processes into one combined social process.\" Formal Subsumption Becomes Real Command: The Genesis of the Directing Authority A pivotal theoretical distinction is now advanced. Earlier, \"the subjection of labour to capital was only a formal result of the fact that the worker, instead of working for himself, works for, and consequently under, the capitalist.\" But \"through the co-operation of numerous wage-labourers, the command of capital develops into a requirement for carrying on the labour process itself, into a real condition of production .\" This is the transition from formal to real subsumption in embryo: capital's command ceases to be an external legal relation superimposed on a self-sufficient labour process and becomes technically woven into production itself. \"That a capitalist should command in the field of production is now as indispensable as that a general should command on the field of battle.\" Marx then offers a general theory of direction . \"All directly social or communal labour on a large scale requires, to a greater or lesser degree, a directing authority, in order to secure the harmonious co-operation of the activities of individuals, and to perform the general functions that have their origin in the motion of the total productive organism, as distinguished from the motion of its separate organs.\" The metaphor: \"A single violin player is his own conductor: an orchestra requires a separate one.\" Thus \"the work of directing, superintending and adjusting becomes one of the functions of capital, from the moment that the labour under capital's control becomes co-operative.\" And \"as a specific function of capital, the directing function acquires its own special characteristics.\" The Despotic Form and the Twofold Content: The Industrial Army and Its Officers The \"special characteristics\" are now unpacked. The driving motive of capitalist production is \"the self-valorization of capital to the greatest possible extent, i.e. the greatest possible production of surplus-value, hence the greatest possible exploitation of labour-power by the capitalist.\" As the number of co-operating workers increases, \"so too does their resistance to the domination of capital, and, necessarily, the pressure put on by capital to overcome this resistance.\" Therefore the capitalist's control is twofold in content : \"not only a special function arising from the nature of the social labour process, and peculiar to that process, but it is at the same time a function of the exploitation of a social labour process, and is consequently conditioned by the unavoidable antagonism between the exploiter and the raw material of his exploitation.\" A further ground for control is added: as the means of production extend, \"the necessity increases for some effective control over the proper application of them, because they confront the wage-labourer as the property of another [ fremdes Eigentum ].\" The footnote's Spectator citation — on the Manchester wirework company where partnership led to \"a sudden decrease in waste,\" and on the Rochdale co-operatives that \"did not leave a clear place for masters\" (\" Quelle horreur! \") — is a sardonic illustration of how capitalist ideology recoils from co-operation without a master. Marx then states the ultimate expropriation of agency: \"the co-operation of wage-labourers is entirely brought about by the capital that employs them. Their unification into one single productive body, and the establishment of a connection between their individual functions, lies outside their competence. These things are not their own act, but the act of the capital that brings them together and maintains them in that situation.\" The social interconnection therefore confronts the workers \"in the realm of ideas, as a plan drawn up by the capitalist, and, in practice, as his authority, as the powerful will of a being outside them, who subjects their activity to his purpose.\" Hence the celebrated formulation: content is twofold; form is purely despotic . As co-operation extends, \"this despotism develops the forms that are peculiar to it.\" Just as the capitalist was earlier relieved of actual labour once his capital reached a minimum, \"so now he hands over the work of direct and constant supervision of the individual workers and groups of workers to a special kind of wage-labourer.\" The metaphor expands into a full political anatomy: \"An industrial army of workers under the command of a capitalist requires, like a real army, officers (managers) and N.C.O.s (foremen, overseers), who command during the labour process in the name of capital. The work of supervision becomes their established and exclusive function.\" Marx then exposes a bourgeois double standard. Political economists, comparing \"the mode of production of isolated peasants or independent artisans with the plantation economy which rests on slavery,\" count supervisory labour as part of the faux frais de production (the Cairnes footnote notes that the peasant proprietor \"appropriating the whole produce of his toil, needs no other stimulus to exertion. Superintendence is here completely dispensed with\"). But when considering the capitalist mode of production, \"they on the contrary identify the function of direction which arises out of the nature of the communal labour process with the function of direction which is made necessary by the capitalist and therefore antagonistic character of that process.\" Marx's counter-thesis: \"It is not because he is a leader of industry that a man is a capitalist; on the contrary, he is a leader of industry because he is a capitalist. The leadership of industry is an attribute of capital, just as in feudal times the functions of general and judge were attributes of landed property.\" The Steuart footnote — \"Why do large undertakings in the manufacturing way ruin private industry, but by coming nearer to the simplicity of slaves?\" — and Marx's jab at Auguste Comte's school (which might \"just as well have shown that feudal lords are an eternal necessity\") complete the ideological critique: the \"eternal necessity\" of the capitalist director is a class apologetic dressed as a functional necessity. The Free Gift: How Social Productivity Becomes a Power of Capital The chapter's theoretical climax — the doctrine of the \"free gift\" — now unfolds through a careful legal-economic argument. \"The worker is the owner of his labour-power until he has finished bargaining for its sale with the capitalist, and he can sell no more than what he has — i.e. his individual, isolated labour-power.\" This relation \"is in no way altered by the fact that the capitalist, instead of buying the labour-power of one man, buys that of 100, and enters into separate contracts with 100 unconnected men instead of with one.\" The capitalist \"can set the 100 men to work, without letting them co-operate. He pays them the value of 100 independent labour-powers, but he does not pay for the combined labour-power of the 100 .\" This is decisive: co-operation's product is not purchased; it arises only from the workers' combination , and combination is imposed by capital. \"Being independent of each other, the workers are isolated. They enter into relations with the capitalist, but not with each other. Their co-operation only begins with the labour process, but by then they have ceased to belong to themselves. On entering the labour process they are incorporated into capital.\" The consequence is the dissociation of productive power from its producer . \"As co-operators, as members of a working organism, they merely form a particular mode of existence of capital. Hence the productive power developed by the worker socially is the productive power of capital.\" Marx repeats and sharpens the point: \"The socially productive power of labour develops as a free gift to capital whenever the workers are placed under certain conditions, and it is capital which places them under these conditions. Because this power costs capital nothing, while on the other hand it is not developed by the worker until his labour itself belongs to capital, it appears as a power which capital possesses by its nature — a productive power inherent in capital.\" This is the ideological inversion the chapter exists to expose: a socially produced, historically specific capacity is misrecognized as a natural, intrinsic property of capital. It is the same structure of fetishism that Marx anatomized in the commodity and in the wage-form, now operating on the productivity of the collective worker. Historical Excursus: Ancient Cooperation, Asiatic Despotism, and Pre-Capitalist Forms Marx widens the frame historically. \"The colossal effects of simple co-operation are to be seen in the gigantic structures erected by the ancient Asiatics, Egyptians, Etruscans, etc.\" He quotes R. Jones at length: Oriental States, after supplying their civil and military establishments, retained a surplus applied to \"works of magnificence or utility,\" and their \"command over the hands and arms of almost the entire non-agricultural population has produced stupendous monuments.\" The Nile's teeming valley fed \"a swarming non-agricultural population,\" and this food, belonging to monarch and priesthood, \"afforded the means of erecting the mighty monuments.\" In moving colossal statues, \"human labour almost alone was prodigally used... The number of the labourers and the concentration of their efforts sufficed.\" Jones's coral-reef metaphor is quoted: although each depositor is puny, \"their number is their strength,\" and \"the power of directing these masses gave rise to the palaces and temples, the pyramids, and the armies of gigantic statues.\" The key line: \"It is that confinement of the revenues which feed them, to one or a few hands, which makes such undertakings possible.\" Marx then draws the modern parallel: this power of Asiatic and Egyptian kings, of Etruscan theocrats, \"has in modern society been transferred to the capitalist, whether he appears as an isolated individual or, as in the case of joint-stock companies, in combination with others.\" The form of concentrated command persists across epochs; only its class bearer changes. This leads to a contrast of historical forms. Co-operation at the beginning of civilization (hunting peoples) and as a predominant feature of the agriculture of Indian communities rests on two foundations: \"the common ownership of the conditions of production,\" and the fact that \"the individual has as little torn himself free from the umbilical cord of his tribe or community as a bee has from his hive.\" Both characteristics distinguish it from capitalist co-operation. By contrast, the \"sporadic application of co-operation on a large scale in ancient times, in the Middle Ages, and in modern colonies, rests on direct relations of domination and servitude, in most cases on slavery.\" The capitalist form, uniquely, \"presupposes from the outset the free wage-labourer who sells his labour-power to capital.\" Historically it develops \"in opposition to peasant agriculture and independent handicrafts, whether in guilds or not.\" A substantial footnote situates peasant and artisan production as the economic foundation both of the feudal mode (on the one hand) and of classical antiquity's best period (on the other) — a lapidary materialist genealogy of the two great pre-capitalist formations. The Historical Specificity and Pervasiveness of Simple Cooperation Marx concludes with a reflection on historical appearance . \"From the standpoint of the peasant and the artisan, capitalist co-operation does not appear as a particular historical form of co-operation; instead, co-operation itself appears as a historical form peculiar to, and specifically distinguishing, the capitalist process of production.\" That is, the pre-capitalist producer, lacking a comparative perspective, mistakes a historically specific social arrangement for co-operation as such — a form of epochal myopia. Symmetrically, just as the social productive power of labour \"appears to be the productive power of capital,\" so \"co-operation itself... appears to be a specific form of the capitalist process of production.\" It is \"the first change experienced by the actual labour process when subjected to capital,\" and it \"takes place spontaneously and naturally.\" The simultaneous employment of many wage-labourers, the necessary condition for this change, \"also forms the starting-point of capitalist production. This starting-point coincides with the birth of capital itself.\" The dialectical summary is balanced: \"If then, on the one hand, the capitalist mode of production is a historically necessary condition for the transformation of the labour process into a social process, so, on the other hand, this social form of the labour process is a method employed by capital for the more profitable exploitation of labour, by increasing its productive power.\" Marx here refuses one-sidedness: capitalism is both a historically progressive socializer of labour and an exploitative appropriator of that socialization. Finally, Marx delimits the epochal status of simple co-operation. \"In its simple shape, as investigated so far, co-operation is a necessary concomitant of all production on a large scale, but it does not in itself represent a fixed form characteristic of a particular epoch in the development of the capitalist mode of production.\" At most it appears as such — \"and then only approximately\" — in the handicraft-like beginnings of manufacture, and in large-scale agriculture corresponding to the manufacture period, distinguished from peasant agriculture \"mainly by the number of workers simultaneously employed and the mass of means of production concentrated for their use.\" Simple co-operation \"has always been, and continues to be, the predominant form in those branches of production in which capital operates on a large scale, but the division of labour and machinery play only an insignificant part.\" The chapter closes with a lapidary statement of persistence: \"Co-operation remains the fundamental form of the capitalist mode of production, although in its simple shape it continues to appear as one particular form alongside the more developed ones.\" The elementary sociality of combined labour is thus the substratum that manufacture and machinofacture will reorganize but never abolish. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter yields several fundamental principles of lasting theoretical weight. The first is the dialectic of quantity and quality under capital . Marx accepts that multiplying labour-powers leaves the rate of surplus-value and the value produced per unit of abstract labour unchanged — yet he demonstrates that a sufficient quantity of combined workers generates a qualitatively new social productive power . This is not mysticism but the logic of emergence: whole systems possess properties their elements lack. The \"new productive power\" of co-operation is the paradigm case, and it becomes the template for Marx's later analyses of manufacture's \"collective worker\" ( Gesamtarbeiter ) and of machinery's vast powers. The second principle is the statistical-social logic of the average . The collective working day renders each participant's labour socially average by the mathematics of compensation of errors. This insight — that capital, by internalizing many workers, manufactures the conditions under which the law of value is reliably realized for the individual enterprise — dissolves the classical political-economy puzzle of how competition enforces general rates while individual capitals deviate. Co-operation is thus not merely productive but normalizing : it disciplines the workforce into social average labour-power. The third principle is the economization of constant capital through joint consumption, which lowers the value of commodities, cheapens labour-power, and thereby raises relative surplus-value. This establishes a material feedback loop: the scale of co-operation feeds the rate of surplus-value, which feeds accumulation, which feeds further scale. The fourth, and perhaps most consequential, principle is the \"free gift\" thesis and its ideological counterpart, the fetishism of capital's productivity . Social co-operation, because it is organized by and for capital and arises only within the capital-relation, is expropriated as though it were capital's intrinsic power. This is the precise mechanism by which a social and historical relation is naturalized and eternalized. The corollary is the twofold content / despotic form doctrine: capital's direction is simultaneously a technical coordination of social labour and a political apparatus of exploitation, and it is because of the latter that it takes the despotic, hierarchical form of managers, foremen, and overseers. The industrial army metaphor is not decorative; it theorizes the firm as a command structure whose necessity is grounded in antagonism, not technique. The fifth principle is the historical-materialist genealogy of co-operation . By contrasting tribal/communal co-operation (grounded in common ownership), slave-based co-operation (grounded in domination), and capitalist co-operation (grounded in free wage-labour), Marx shows that co-operation as such is trans-historical while its forms are epoch-specific. The capitalist form is neither natural nor eternal; it is the historical product of the dissolution of feudal and petty production. The broader implications reach across political economy, social theory, and political practice. For economic theory , the chapter demolishes the bourgeois identification of management with technical necessity, exposing it as an apology for class domination — and demonstrates that co-operation's productivity, far from vindicating capital's \"contribution,\" is a social property capital merely commands. For social theory , it offers a rigorous account of how structure and agency interpenetrate: workers create a collective power greater than their individual selves, yet that very power is organized as an alien will outside them — an early and profound statement of what later theory would call reification and alienation . The workers' co-operation is real, but its \"soul\" is capital's plan. For politics , the analysis yields a decisive evaluation of co-operatives and worker self-management. The Spectator 's horror that the Rochdale pioneers \"did not leave a clear place for masters\" reveals the system's dependency on the master-function. Yet Marx's own argument implies that because co-operation's productive power is socially produced , it need not be capitalistically commanded — a possibility latent in the chapter and later developed in his remarks on associated labour. The chapter thus plants a seed: if the \"free gift\" is socially generated, it can in principle be socially reappropriated . And for historical perspective , the persistence of simple co-operation as \"the fundamental form of the capitalist mode of production\" that survives beneath manufacture and machinery means that the elementary reality of people working together, in a planned way, remains the irreducible human substratum of all production — a substratum whose liberation from the despotic form of capital's command stands as the chapter's enduring challenge. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 12 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 14"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 14", "title": "Chapter 14", "subtitle": "The Division of Labour and Manufacture", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 14/Chapter 14  The Division of Labour and Manufacture_explanation.html", "word_count": 16614, "read_time": "83 min read", "tags": ["Division of Labour & Manufacture"], "keywords": ["Division of Labour and Manufacture", "Heterogeneous vs Organic Manufacture", "Detail Labourer and Specialization", "Division of Labour in Workshop vs Society", "Mutilation of the Detail Worker", "Handicraft Foundation of Manufacture"], "text": "Chapter 14 The Division Of Labour And Manufacture - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 16,572 words ⏱️ ~83 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 14: The Division of Labour and Manufacture — An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem and Intellectual Objective Chapter 14 of Capital, Volume I is Marx's sustained investigation into manufacture —the historically specific form of capitalist production that dominated the period stretching \"roughly speaking, from the middle of the sixteenth century to the last third of the eighteenth century.\" The chapter is not a neutral history of craft production; it is a rigorous, dialectical anatomy of how a particular organization of labour—the division of labour within the workshop—became the vehicle for generating relative surplus-value and for entrenching the domination of capital over labour. Marx's objective is threefold: (1) to trace the dual origin of manufacture out of pre-capitalist handicrafts; (2) to dissect its internal anatomy, its two fundamental forms (heterogeneous and organic), its qualitative and quantitative principles, and its specialized machinery of human organs; and (3) to draw the decisive theoretical distinction between the division of labour in society (which can exist across the most diverse economic formations) and the division of labour in the workshop (which is an entirely specific creation of the capitalist mode of production). The chapter thus sits at the hinge of Marx's theory of surplus-value: it explains how cooperation, organized through the division of labour, augments the productive power of labour while simultaneously subordinating the worker body and soul to capital. The Broader Theoretical Framework This chapter belongs to the section of Capital titled \"The Production of Relative Surplus-Value.\" Where absolute surplus-value arises from lengthening the working day, relative surplus-value arises from raising the productivity of labour so that the labour-time necessary to reproduce the worker's means of subsistence is reduced, thereby extending the domain of surplus labour. Chapter 14 shows that manufacture is a historically specific method of producing relative surplus-value—one that does not yet rely on machinery, but on the dissection of handicraft activity itself . It is the missing middle term between simple cooperation (Chapter 13) and large-scale industry based on machinery (Chapter 15). Manufacture both perfects the handicraft basis and creates the material conditions for its own supersession: \"the narrow technical basis on which manufacture rested came into contradiction with requirements of production which it had itself created.\" The chapter therefore is simultaneously an economic history, a theory of the labour process, a critique of political economy (especially Adam Smith and the apologists of the factory system), and a foundational statement of the alienation of the worker from intellectual and social productive powers. It culminates in the claim that the specialized worker and his tools are the simplest elements of manufacture, that these elements are combined by capital into a collective worker, and that this collective worker is \"the item of machinery specifically characteristic of the manufacturing period\"—machinery made of human beings. Core Thesis Marx's central thesis can be stated as follows: Manufacture originates in two opposite ways—by combining independent handicrafts and by dividing up a single handicraft—but in both cases it produces a productive mechanism whose organs are human beings. This mechanism rests on a technically narrow handicraft basis, which excludes a truly scientific division of the production process. Manufacture reproduces and drives to an extreme, within the workshop, the naturally developed differentiation found in society; it converts the partial task into the life-long destiny of the worker, develops a hierarchy of labour-powers corresponding to a scale of wages, creates for the first time a class of unskilled labourers, and lowers the value of labour-power by reducing or eliminating apprenticeship costs. The result is the crippling of the individual worker, the concentration of knowledge, judgement, and will in capital, and a new, more refined and \"civilized\" means of exploitation. Manufacture is therefore a specific method of creating relative surplus-value—one that increases the socially productive power of labour \"for the benefit of the capitalist instead of the worker\" and \"by crippling the individual worker.\" Its internal division of labour stands in inverse ratio to the authority regulating the division of labour in society: anarchy in the social division of labour and despotism in the manufacturing division of labour mutually condition each other. How the Chapter Fits Into the Broader Work The chapter builds directly on the concept of cooperation developed in the preceding chapter. Simple cooperation already shows that the collective working organism is a form of capital's existence, but it \"leaves the mode of the individual's labour for the most part unchanged.\" Manufacture, by contrast, \"thoroughly revolutionizes it, and seizes labour-power by its roots.\" It is the first form of capitalist production to attack the individual worker's mode of labour at its foundation. At the same time, the chapter prepares the way for the analysis of large-scale industry: manufacture creates the differentiated instruments of labour that become the material conditions for machinery, and it creates the \"workshop for the production of the instruments of labour themselves,\" which in turn produces machines. Machines then abolish the handicraftsman as the regulating principle of social production—sweeping away the technical reason for the lifelong attachment of the worker to a partial function and removing the barriers that handicraft skill placed in the way of capital's domination. The chapter also intervenes decisively in the history of economic thought. Marx positions himself against Adam Smith, who treated the division of labour as the pivotal fact of economic life while assigning machinery a subordinate role, and who failed to distinguish the division of labour in manufacture from the division of labour in society except as a subjective difference for the observer. Marx insists the difference is real and objective : the specialized worker in manufacture produces no commodity; only the common product of all the specialized workers becomes a commodity. The social division of labour is mediated through the purchase and sale of commodities; the manufacturing division of labour is mediated through the sale of labour-power to a single capitalist. One presupposes the dispersal of the means of production among independent producers; the other presupposes their concentration in the hands of one capitalist. This distinction is not scholastic—it is the axis on which the political economy of the manufacturing period turns, and it exposes the apologetic character of bourgeois thought, which celebrates the despotism of the workshop while denouncing every conscious attempt to regulate production socially as a violation of \"the rights of property, freedom and the self-determining 'genius' of the individual capitalist.\" Finally, the chapter is a study in historical materialism's method. Marx moves from the simplest elements (the specialized worker and his tool) to the whole mechanism (the collective worker, the hierarchy of labour-powers, the quantitative proportionality), from the genesis of manufacture to its consolidation as a \"conscious, methodical and systematic form of capitalist production,\" and from its internal contradictions to its historical supersession. The famous examples—the carriage, the watch, the needle, the glass bottle, the Indian community—are not decorative; they are empirical instantiations of a theoretical logic that moves from the abstract to the concrete and back again. By the end of the chapter, the reader understands that manufacture is not merely a technique but a social relation, and that the division of labour under capital is simultaneously a source of social productive power and a machine for the mutilation of the human individual. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Classical Shape of Manufacture and the Dual Origin Marx opens with a definitional and periodizing move. \"That form of co-operation which is based on division of labour assumes its classical shape in manufacture.\" Manufacture is thus a species of cooperation, distinguished by the presence of division of labour. As a characteristic form of the capitalist process of production, it prevails throughout \"the manufacturing period properly so called,\" which Marx dates from roughly the middle of the sixteenth century to the last third of the eighteenth century. This periodization matters: manufacture is not an eternal category but a historically bounded mode of production, and Marx will argue that its internal contradictions and technical limits eventually make it obsolete. He then identifies two origins of manufacture, which are logical opposites but historically intertwined. First origin: combination of independent handicrafts. Workers belonging to various independent crafts—wheelwrights, harness-makers, tailors, locksmiths, upholsterers, turners, fringe-makers, glaziers, painters, polishers, gilders—are assembled in one workshop under a single capitalist. In the manufacture of carriages, the unfinished product passes from hand to hand. Marx notes a crucial temporal paradox: \"It is true that a carriage cannot be gilded before it has been made. But if a number of carriages are being made simultaneously, some may be in the hands of the gilders while others are going through an earlier process.\" This simultaneity is the first form of cooperation, and it is what makes the concentration of heterogeneous crafts possible. \"So far, we are still on the footing of simple co-operation, which finds its materials ready to hand in the shape of men and things.\" But then an \"important change\" occurs. The tailor, the locksmith, and the other craftsmen are now exclusively occupied in making carriages; they \"gradually lose the habit, and therefore the ability, of carrying on their old trade in all its ramifications.\" Their activity becomes one-sided, and that one-sidedness is precisely what makes it more effective. \"At first, the manufacture of carriages appeared as a combination of various independent handicrafts. But it gradually began to signify the splitting-up of carriage production into its various detailed operations, and each single operation crystallized into the exclusive function of a particular worker, the manufacture as a whole being performed by these partial workers in conjunction.\" Marx notes that cloth manufacture arose in the same way from combining different handicrafts under one capitalist. The footnote adds a modern example from A. Blanqui: the silk spinning and weaving of Lyons and Nîmes, which employs women and children without exhausting them, allows them to remain in their valleys cultivating silkworms, and yet applies the principle of the division of labour to a high degree through winders, throwsters, dyers, sizers, and weavers who are independent rather than gathered in one workshop. Engels' addition notes that since Marx wrote, the power-loom has invaded these factories. Second origin: the opposite path. One capitalist simultaneously employs in one workshop a number of craftsmen who all do the same work—papermaking, type-founding, needlemaking. This is cooperation in its simplest form. Each craftsman makes the entire commodity and performs in succession all the necessary operations. But \"very soon external circumstances cause a different use to be made of the concentration of the workers on one spot and the simultaneousness of their work.\" Perhaps an increased quantity must be delivered within a given time. The work is therefore divided up. \"Instead of each man being allowed to perform all the various operations in succession, these operations are changed into disconnected, isolated ones, carried on side by side; each is assigned to a different craftsman, and the whole of them together are performed simultaneously by the co-operators.\" This \"accidental division is repeated, develops advantages of its own and gradually ossifies into a systematic division of labour.\" The commodity, \"from being the individual product of an independent craftsman, becomes the social product of a union of craftsmen, each of whom performs one, and only one, of the constituent partial operations.\" Marx illustrates with the papermaker and the needlemaker. The operations that in a German guild papermaker \"merged into each other as the successive acts of one craftsman\" became in Dutch paper manufacture \"a number of partial operations performed side by side by numerous workers acting in co-operation.\" The needlemaker of Nuremberg, organized in his guild, performed a series of perhaps twenty operations one after another; in England, \"it was not long before there were twenty needlemakers side by side, each performing only one operation of the twenty.\" Finally, each of those twenty operations was again split up, isolated, and made entirely independent, \"so that it became the exclusive function of a separate worker.\" Marx then states the twofold character of manufacture's genesis with precision: \"On the one hand it arises from the combination of various independent trades, which lose that independence and become specialized to such an extent that they are reduced to merely supplementary and partial operations in the production of one particular commodity. On the other hand, it arises from the co-operation of craftsmen in one particular handicraft; it splits up that handicraft into its various detailed operations, isolating these operations and developing their mutual independence to the point where each becomes the exclusive function of a particular worker.\" Whatever the starting-point, \"its final form is always the same—a productive mechanism whose organs are human beings.\" Here Marx inserts a critical set of theoretical qualifications. Firstly , the analysis of a process of production into its particular phases coincides completely with the decomposition of a handicraft into its different partial operations. Every operation remains manual, retains the character of a handicraft, and depends on \"the strength, skill, quickness and sureness with which the individual worker manipulates his tools.\" Handicraft is the basis—\"a technically narrow basis which excludes a really scientific division of the production process into its component parts, since every partial process undergone by the product must be capable of being done by hand, and of forming a separate handicraft.\" This is the crucial limitation: manufacture cannot decompose labour according to the objective logic of chemistry or physics; it can only decompose it according to what human hands can do. It is precisely because \"the skill of the craftsman thus continues to be the foundation of the production process\" that every worker becomes exclusively assigned to a partial function and that his labour-power \"becomes transformed into the life-long organ of this partial function.\" Secondly , this division of labour is a particular sort of cooperation, and many of its advantages spring from the nature of cooperation in general, not from this particular form of it. This distinction—between the generic effects of cooperation and the specific effects of the manufacturing division of labour—will structure much of the subsequent argument. The Specialized Worker, His Tools, and the Dissection of Handicraft Activity Marx now descends to the level of the individual worker and his instrument. \"A worker who performs the same simple operation for the whole of his life converts his body into the automatic, one-sided implement of that operation.\" The consequence is reduced time: he takes less time than the craftsman who performs a whole series of operations. The \"collective worker, who constitutes the living mechanism of manufacture, is made up solely of such one-sidedly specialized workers.\" Hence, compared with independent handicraft, \"more is produced in less time, or in other words the productivity of labour is increased.\" Marx quotes The Advantages of the East-India Trade (1720): \"The more any manufacture of much variety shall be distributed and assigned to different artists, the same must needs be better done and with greater expedition, with less loss of time and labour.\" Once partial labour is established as the exclusive function of one person, its methods become perfected. \"The worker's continued repetition of the same narrowly defined act and the concentration of his attention on it teach him by experience how to attain the desired effect with the minimum of exertion.\" Since several generations of workers live and work together at the manufacture of a given article, \"the technical skill, the tricks of the trade thus acquired, become established, and are accumulated and handed down.\" Hodgskin's aphorism captures this: \"Easy labour is transmitted skill.\" Marx then makes a double theoretical claim. On the one hand , manufacture \"produces the skill of the specialized worker by reproducing and systematically driving to an extreme within the workshop the naturally developed differentiation which it found ready to hand in society.\" On the other hand , \"the conversion of a partial task into the life-long destiny of a man corresponds to the tendency shown by earlier societies towards making trades hereditary.\" Trades either \"became petrified into castes,\" or where historical conditions produced individual variability incompatible with caste, \"they hardened into guilds.\" Caste and guild arise \"from the action of the same natural law that regulates the differentiation of plants and animals into species and varieties,\" except that once a certain degree of development is reached, \"the heredity of castes and the exclusiveness of guilds are ordained as a law of society.\" The footnote quotes Diodorus Siculus on Egypt, where craftsmen could not interfere in the affairs of other classes, had to follow the calling hereditary in their clan, and were severely punished if they meddled with affairs of State or carried on several trades at once. The famous Indian weaver passage illustrates the opposite pole—virtuosity without division of labour. \"The muslins of Dacca in fineness, the calicoes and other piece goods of Coromandel in brilliant and durable colours, have never been surpassed. Yet they are produced without capital, machinery, division of labour, or any of those means which give such facilities to the manufacturing interest of Europe.\" The weaver is \"a detached individual, working a web when ordered of a customer, and with a loom of the rudest construction.\" There is no expedient for rolling up the warp, so the loom must be kept stretched full-length, becoming so large it cannot fit in the hut—hence the weaver works in the open air, interrupted by every vicissitude of the weather. \"It is only the special skill accumulated from generation to generation, and transmitted from father to son, that gives to the Hindu, as it gives to the spider, this virtuosity. And yet the work of such a Hindu weaver is very complicated, in comparison with that of the majority of workers under the system of manufacture.\" This comparison is dialectical: the very absence of division of labour preserves a complex, whole skill that manufacture destroys. Marx then analyzes the temporal mechanics of the gain. A craftsman performing various partial operations in succession \"must at one time change his place, at another time his tools.\" The transition interrupts the flow of labour and \"creates gaps in his working day, so to speak.\" These gaps close when he is tied to the same operation all day long; they vanish \"in the same proportion as the changes in his work diminish.\" The resulting increase of productivity is due either to \"an increased expenditure of labour-power in a given time—i.e. increased intensity of labour—or to a decrease in the amount of labour-power unproductively consumed.\" The extra expenditure of power required by every transition from rest to motion \"is compensated for by prolonging the duration of the normal speed of work, when once acquired.\" But Marx immediately adds a countervailing consideration: \"As against this, however, constant labour of one uniform kind disturbs the intensity and flow of a man's vital forces, which find recreation and delight in the change of activity itself.\" This is a rare moment where Marx acknowledges a natural human need for variety—one that manufacture suppresses. The section on tools is pivotal. \"The productivity of labour depends not only on the proficiency of the worker, but also on the quality of his tools.\" Tools of the same kind—knives, drills, gimlets, hammers—may be employed in different processes, and the same tool may serve various purposes in a single process. But \"as soon as the different operations of a labour process are disconnected from each other, and each partial operation acquires in the hands of the worker a suitable form peculiar to it, alterations become necessary in the tools which previously served more than one purpose.\" The direction of change is determined by \"the particular difficulties put in the worker's way by the unchanged form of the old tool.\" Manufacture is thus characterized by \"the differentiation of the instruments of labour—a differentiation whereby tools of a given sort acquire fixed shapes, adapted to each particular application—and by the specialization of these instruments, which allows full play to each special tool only in the hands of a specific kind of worker.\" In Birmingham alone, 500 varieties of hammer are produced, each adapted to a particular process, with several varieties often serving exclusively for different operations in the same process. The manufacturing period \"simplifies, improves and multiplies the implements of labour by adapting them to the exclusive and special functions of each kind of worker.\" It thereby \"creates at the same time one of the material conditions for the existence of machinery, which consists of a combination of simple instruments.\" The footnote quotes Darwin's Origin of Species on the natural organs of plants and animals: \"As long as the same part has to perform diversified work, we can perhaps see why it should remain variable... In the same way that a knife which has to cut all sorts of things may be of almost any shape; whilst a tool for some particular purpose must be of some particular shape.\" Marx is drawing a homology between natural selection and the differentiation of tools—not to naturalize capitalist production, but to show that the same principle of adaptation to specialized function operates in both domains, and that manufacture, like nature, works through differentiation and specialization. The specialized worker and his instruments are, Marx concludes, \"the simplest elements of manufacture.\" The Two Fundamental Forms: Heterogeneous and Organic Manufacture Marx now ascends from the elements to the whole. Manufacture has \"two fundamental forms of articulation which, although occasionally intertwined, are essentially different in kind, and moreover play very different roles in the later transformation of manufacture into large-scale industry carried on by machinery.\" The double character arises from the nature of the article produced: it either \"results from the merely mechanical assembling of partial products made independently,\" or it \"owes its completed shape to a series of connected processes and manipulations.\" Heterogeneous manufacture. A locomotive consists of more than 5,000 independent parts, but it is a creation of large-scale industry and cannot serve as an example of genuine manufacture. A watch can, and William Petty used it to illustrate the division of labour. Formerly the individual creation of a Nuremberg craftsman, the watch has become \"the social product of an immense number of specialized workers\": mainspring makers, dial makers, spiral-spring makers, jewelled hole makers, ruby lever makers, hand makers, case makers, screw makers, gilders. Then there are numerous subdivisions—wheel makers (with a further division between brass and steel), pin makers, movement makers, acheveurs de pignon (who fix wheels on axles and polish the facets), pivot makers, planteurs de finissage (who put wheels and springs in the works), finisseurs de barillet (who cut teeth, make holes the right size), escapement makers, cylinder makers for cylinder escapements, escapement wheel makers, balance-wheel makers, makers of the raquette (the regulating apparatus), planteurs d'echappement , then repasseurs de barillet , steel polishers, wheel polishers, screw polishers, figure painters, dial enamellers (who melt enamel on copper), fabricants de pendants (who make the ring), finisseurs de charniere (who put brass hinges in the cover), graveurs , ciseleurs , polisseurs de boite , and \"last of all the repasseurs , who fit together the whole watch and hand it over in a going state.\" Only a few parts pass through several hands; \"all these membra disjecta come together for the first time in the hand that binds them into one mechanical whole.\" This external relation between the finished product and its diverse elements makes it \"a matter of chance in this case as in the case of all similar finished articles, whether the specialized workers are brought together in one workshop or not.\" The subdivided operations may be carried on as independent handicrafts, as in the Cantons of Vaud and Neuchâtel; while in Geneva there exist large watch factories where specialized workers directly cooperate under one capitalist. Even there, the dial, springs, and case are seldom made in the factory itself. To carry on the trade as a manufacture with concentration of workers is profitable only under exceptional conditions, because competition is greatest between those who desire to work at home, because the splitting-up into heterogeneous processes \"scarcely permits the use of instruments of labour common to all,\" and because \"the capitalist, by scattering the work around, saves any outlay on workshops, etc.\" The footnote provides quantitative evidence: in 1854 Geneva produced 80,000 watches, not one-fifth of the Canton of Neuchâtel's production; La Chaux-de-Fonds alone produces twice as many as Geneva every year; from 1850 to 1861 Geneva produced 720,000 watches. When production consists of parts fitted together, the lack of connection makes it very difficult to convert such manufacture to large-scale industrial production; in watches, two extra impediments are \"the minuteness and delicacy of its parts, and its character as an article of luxury.\" In the best London houses, scarcely a dozen watches are made alike in a year; Vacheron and Constantin's factory, which uses machinery successfully, produces at most three or four varieties of size and form. The footnote also notes that watchmaking is \"that classical example of heterogeneous manufacture\" where one may study \"the above-mentioned differentiation and specialization of the instruments of labour which arises from the decomposition of the craftsman's activity.\" Marx adds that the position of the specialized worker who works at home for a capitalist ( manufacturer, etablisseur ) is \"very different from that of the independent craftsman, who works for his own customers.\" Organic manufacture. The second kind, \"its perfected form,\" produces articles that \"go through connected phases of development, go step by step through a series of processes, like the wire in the manufacture of needles, which passes through the hands of seventy-two, and sometimes even ninety-two, different specialized workers.\" In so far as such manufacture combines scattered handicrafts, \"it lessens the space by which the various phases of production are separated from each other. The time taken in passing from one stage to another is shortened, and so is the labour by means of which these transitions are made.\" Compared with handicraft, productive power is gained, and this gain arises from \"the general co-operative character of manufacture.\" But on the other hand, division of labour, \"which is the principle peculiar to manufacture, requires the isolation of the various stages of production and their independence of each other.\" The establishment and maintenance of a connection between isolated functions requires that the article \"be transported incessantly from one hand to another, and from one process to another.\" From the standpoint of large-scale industry, this requirement \"emerges as a characteristic and costly limitation, and one that is inherent in the principle of manufacture.\" The footnote quotes The Industry of Nations (1855): \"The isolation of the different stages of manufacture, consequent upon the employment of manual labour, adds immensely to the cost of production, the loss mainly arising from the mere removals from one process to another.\" Marx then articulates the crucial transformation of time into space . If we confine attention to a determinate quantity of raw material—a heap of rags in paper manufacture, a length of wire in needle manufacture—it \"passes successively through a series of stages in the hands of the various specialized workers, until it takes on its final shape.\" But if we look at the workshop as a complete mechanism, \"we see the raw material in all stages of its production at the same time.\" The collective worker \"draws the wire with one set of tooled-up hands, straightens the wire with another set, armed with different tools, cuts it with another set, points it with another set, and so on.\" The different stages of the process, \"previously successive in time, have become simultaneous and contiguous in space. Hence a greater quantity of finished commodities is produced within the same period.\" This simultaneity \"arises from the general co-operative form of the process as a whole; but manufacture not only finds the conditions for co-operation ready to hand; it also, to some extent, creates them by subdividing handicraft labour. On the other hand, it only accomplishes the social organization of the labour process by riveting each worker to a single fraction of the work.\" The footnote quotes Dugald Stewart: the division of labour \"produces also an economy of time by separating the work into its different branches, all of which may be carried on into execution at the same moment... By carrying on all the different processes at once, which an individual must have executed separately, it becomes possible to produce a multitude of pins completely finished in the same time as a single pin might have been either cut or pointed.\" The interdependence of the partial processes produces a new technical law . \"Since the product of each specialized worker is, at the same time, only a particular stage in the development of a finished article which is the same in each case, each worker, or group of workers, prepares the raw material for another worker or group of workers. The result of the labour of the one is the starting-point for the labour of the other. One worker therefore directly sets the other to work.\" The labour-time necessary in each partial process is learned by experience, and \"the mechanism of manufacture, taken as a whole, is based on the assumption that a given result will be obtained in a given time. It is only on this assumption that the various supplementary labour processes can proceed uninterruptedly, simultaneously, and side by side.\" The direct mutual interdependence \"compels each one of them to spend on his work no more than the necessary time.\" This creates \"a continuity, a uniformity, a regularity, an order, and even an intensity of labour, quite different from that found in an independent handicraft or even in simple co-operation.\" Marx then draws a decisive contrast between the social enforcement of the law of value and its technical enforcement in manufacture. \"The rule that the labour-time expended on a commodity should not exceed the amount socially necessary to produce it is one that appears, in the production of commodities in general, to be enforced from outside by the action of competition: to put it superficially, each single producer is obliged to sell his commodity at its market price. In manufacture, on the contrary, the provision of a given quantity of the product in a given period of labour is a technical law of the process of production itself.\" The footnote concedes that \"in many branches of production the system of manufacture attains this result only very imperfectly, owing to the absence of the knowledge necessary to control with certainty the general chemical and physical conditions of the production process.\" The footnote from The Advantages of the East-India Trade (p. 68) adds: \"The more variety of artists to every manufacture... the greater the order and regularity of every work, the same must needs be done in less time, the labour must be less.\" Quantitative proportionality. Different operations require unequal lengths of time and therefore yield unequal quantities of specialized products. \"Thus if the same worker has to perform the same operation day after day, there must be a different number of workers for each operation; for instance, in type manufacture there are four founders and two breakers to one rubber: the founder casts 2,000 type an hour, the breaker breaks up 4,000, and the rubber polishes 8,000.\" Here we have \"again the principle of co-operation in its simplest form, the simultaneous employment of many people doing the same thing; only now this principle is the expression of an organic relation.\" The division of labour under manufacture \"not only simplifies and multiplies the qualitatively different parts of society's collective worker, but also creates a fixed mathematical relation or ratio which regulates the quantitative extent of those parts—i.e. the relative number of workers, or the relative size of the group of workers, for each special function. Thus alongside the qualitative articulation, the division of labour develops a quantitative rule and a proportionality for the social labour process.\" Once the most fitting proportion has been established, \"that scale can be extended only by employing a multiple of each particular group.\" Babbage's principle is quoted in the footnote: \"When (from the peculiar nature of the produce of each manufactory) the number of processes into which it is most advantageous to divide it is ascertained, as well as the number of individuals to be employed, then all other manufactories which do not employ a direct multiple of this number will produce the article at a greater cost... Hence arises one of the causes of the great size of manufacturing establishments.\" Moreover, \"the same individual can do certain kinds of work just as well on a large as on a small scale; for instance the labour of superintendence, the transportation of the parts of the product from one stage to the next, etc. The isolation of such functions, their allotment to a particular worker, becomes advantageous only with an increase in the number of workers employed; but this increase must affect every group proportionally.\" The glass bottle example illustrates the group as an organized body. Glass bottle manufacture resolves into three essentially different stages: first, the preliminary stage of preparing the components of the glass, mixing sand and lime, and melting them into a fluid mass; second, the glass-melting proper; third, the final stage of removing bottles from the drying furnace, sorting, and packing. In the middle stage, at each mouth of the furnace there works a group called \"the hole,\" consisting of one bottle-maker or finisher, one blower, one gatherer, one putter-up or whetter-off, and one taker-in. \"These five specialized workers are special organs of a single working organism that only acts as a whole, and therefore can operate only by the direct co-operation of all five. The whole body is paralysed if only one of its members is missing.\" But a glass furnace has several openings (in England from four to six), each containing an earthenware melting-pot full of molten glass and employing a similar five-man group. \"The organization of each group is based on the division of labour, but the bond between the different groups is simple co-operation, which, by using in common one of the means of production, namely the furnace, causes it to be consumed more economically. A furnace of this kind, with its four to six groups, constitutes a glass house; and a glass factory comprises a number of such glass houses, together with the apparatus and the workers required for the preparatory and the final phases of production.\" The footnote notes that in England the melting furnace is different from the glass furnace in which the glass is manipulated; in Belgium the same furnace serves for both processes. Combination of manufactures. Just as manufacture arises partly from the combination of various handicrafts, \"so too it develops into a combination of various manufactures.\" The larger English glass manufacturers make their own earthenware melting-pots, because success depends on their quality. \"The manufacture of one of the means of production is here united with that of the product. On the other hand, the manufacture of the product may be united with other manufactures, in which the very same product serves in turn as raw material, or with whose products the original product is itself subsequently mixed.\" Thus flint glass manufacture is combined with glass-cutting and brass-founding, brass being needed for metal settings. \"The various manufactures which have been combined together in this way form more or less separate departments of a complete manufacture, but they are at the same time independent processes, each with its own division of labour. In spite of the many advantages offered by this combination of manufactures, it never attains a complete technical unity on its own foundation. This unity only arises when it has been transformed into an industry carried on by machinery.\" Machinery's subordinate role. Early in the period of manufacture, \"the principle of lessening the labour-time necessary for the production of commodities was consciously formulated and expressed; and the use of machines also appeared sporadically, especially for certain simple primary processes that have to be conducted on a very large scale and with the application of great force.\" Thus in paper manufacture the tearing-up of rags was done by paper-mills; in metal works the pounding of ores was done by stamping-mills. The Roman Empire handed down the elementary form of all machinery in the shape of the water-wheel. The handicraft period bequeathed the compass, gunpowder, type-printing, and the automatic clock. \"But on the whole, machinery played that subordinate part which Adam Smith assigns to it in comparison with division of labour.\" In the seventeenth century, the sporadic use of machinery was of the greatest importance, \"because it supplied the great mathematicians of that time with a practical basis and an incentive towards the creation of modern mechanics.\" The footnote observes that the whole history of machinery can be traced in the corn mill; the factory is still described in English as a \"mill,\" and in German technological writings of the early nineteenth century \"Mühle\" is still used for all machinery driven by natural forces and all manufactories where machine-like apparatus is employed. Another footnote argues that Adam Smith said nothing new about the division of labour; what characterizes him as \"the quintessential political economist of the period of manufacture\" is \"the stress he lays on it.\" The subordinate part he assigned to machinery provoked a polemic from Lauderdale in the early days of large-scale industry and from Ure later. Smith also confuses \"the differentiation of the instruments of labour, in which the specialized workers of the manufacturing epoch themselves took an active part, with the invention of machinery; in the latter case it is not the workers but men of learning, artisans and even peasants (Brindley)\" who play the main role. The collective worker as machinery. \"The collective worker, formed out of the combination of a number of individual specialized workers, is the item of machinery specifically characteristic of the manufacturing period.\" The various operations performed in turn by the producer of a commodity \"make demands of various kinds upon him. In one operation he must exert more strength, in another more skill, in another more attention; and the same individual does not possess all these qualities in an equal degree.\" After the operations are separated, made independent and isolated, \"the workers are divided, classified and grouped according to their predominant qualities. If their natural endowments are the foundation on which the division of labour is built up, manufacture, once introduced, develops in them new powers that are by nature fitted only for limited and special functions. The collective worker now possesses all the qualities necessary for production in an equal degree of excellence, and expends them in the most economical way by exclusively employing all his organs, individualized in particular workers or groups of workers, in performing their special functions.\" Babbage is quoted: \"The master manufacturer, by dividing the work to be executed into different processes, each requiring different degrees of skill or of force, can purchase exactly that precise quantity of both which is necessary for each process; whereas, if the whole work were executed by one workman, that person must possess sufficient skill to perform the most difficult, and sufficient strength to execute the most laborious of the operations into which the art is divided.\" The footnote adds: \"The one-sidedness and even the deficiencies of the specialized individual worker become perfections when he is part of the collective worker. The habit of doing only one thing converts him into an organ which operates with the certainty of a force of nature, while his connection with the whole mechanism compels him to work with the regularity of a machine.\" Another footnote records the answer of Mr. William Marshall, general manager of a glass works, to the Inquiry Commissioners' question \"How are the young persons kept steadily to their work?\": \"They cannot well neglect their work; when they once begin, they must go on; they are just the same as parts of a machine.\" Hierarchy of labour-powers. \"Since the various functions performed by the collective worker can be simple or complex, high or low, the individual labour-powers, his organs, require different degrees of training, and must therefore possess very different values. Manufacture therefore develops a hierarchy of labour-powers, to which there corresponds a scale of wages. The individual workers are appropriated and annexed for life by a limited function; while the various operations of the hierarchy of labour-powers are parcelled out among the workers according to both their natural and their acquired capacities.\" The footnote clarifies that \"labour-powers\" is deliberately employed to show that, for capital, \"the worker is merely the repository of labour-power.\" Every process of production requires certain simple manipulations that every man is capable of doing. \"These actions too are now separated from their constant interplay with those aspects of activity which are richer in content, and ossified into the exclusive functions of particular individuals.\" Creation of unskilled labour. \"Hence in every craft it seizes, manufacture creates a class of so-called unskilled labourers, a class strictly excluded by the nature of handicraft industry. If it develops one-sided speciality to perfection, at the expense of the whole of a man's working capacity, it also begins to make a speciality of the absence of all development. Alongside the gradations of the hierarchy, there appears the simple separation of the workers into skilled and unskilled. For the latter, the cost of apprenticeship vanishes; for the former, it diminishes, compared with that required of the craftsman, owing to the simplification of the functions. In both cases the value of labour-power falls.\" The footnote quotes Ure: \"Each handicraftsman being... enabled to perfect himself by practice in one point, became... a cheaper workman.\" Marx notes an exception: \"whenever the decomposition of the labour process gives rise to new and comprehensive functions, which either did not appear at all in handicrafts or not to the same extent.\" The relative devaluation of labour-power \"caused by the disappearance or reduction of the expenses of apprenticeship directly implies a higher degree of valorization of capital; for everything that shortens the necessary labour-time required for the reproduction of labour-power, extends the domain of surplus labour.\" The Division of Labour in Manufacture and the Division of Labour in Society Marx now turns to the relation between the division of labour in manufacture and the social division of labour that forms the foundation of all commodity production. He begins with a threefold classification. \"If we keep labour alone in view, we may designate the division of social production into its main genera such as agriculture, industry, etc. as division of labour in general, and the splitting-up of these broad divisions into species and sub-species as division of labour in particular. Finally, we may designate the division of labour within the workshop as division of labour in detail.\" The footnotes quote Storch and Skarbek to the same effect: Storch distinguishes the separation of the most widely different professions from the division where several workers divide between them the preparation of one and the same product; Skarbek distinguishes \"general\" (agriculturalists, manufacturers, shopkeepers), \"particular\" (division of each branch into species), and \"division of tasks, or of labour properly so called\" (which grows up in individual crafts and trades and takes root in the majority of workshops and factories). Two starting-points. \"The division of labour within society develops from one starting-point; the corresponding restriction of individuals to particular vocations or callings develops from another starting-point, which is diametrically opposed to the first. This second starting-point is also that of the division of labour within manufacture.\" Within a family and, after further development, within a tribe, \"there springs up naturally a division of labour caused by differences of sex and age, and therefore based on a purely physiological foundation.\" More material is provided by \"the expansion of the community, the increase of its population and, in particular, conflicts between the different tribes and the subjugation of one tribe by another.\" Engels' note to the third German edition observes that subsequent and very thorough investigations into the primitive condition of man led the author to the conclusion that \"it was not the family that originally developed into the tribe, but that, on the contrary, the tribe was the primitive and spontaneously developed form of human association, based on consanguinity, and that out of the first incipient loosening of the tribal bonds, the many and various forms of the family were afterwards developed.\" On the other hand, \"the exchange of products springs up at the points where different families, tribes or communities come into contact; for at the dawn of civilization it is not private individuals but families, tribes, etc. that meet on an independent footing. Different communities find different means of production and different means of subsistence in their natural environment. Hence their modes of production and living, as well as their products, are different. It is this spontaneously developed difference which, when different communities come into contact, calls forth the mutual exchange of products and the consequent gradual conversion of those products into commodities. Exchange does not create the differences between spheres of production but it does bring the different spheres into a relation, thus converting them into more or less interdependent branches of the collective production of a whole society.\" In this case, \"the social division of labour arises from the exchange between spheres of production which are originally distinct from and independent of one another. In the other case, where the physiological division of labour is the starting-point, the particular organs of a compact whole become separated from each other and break off. This process of disintegration receives its main impetus from the exchange of commodities with foreign communities. Afterwards, these organs attain such a degree of independence that the sole bond still connecting the various kinds of work is the exchange of the products as commodities. In the one case, what was previously independent has been made dependent; in the other case, what was previously dependent has been made independent.\" Town and country. \"The foundation of every division of labour which has attained a certain degree of development, and has been brought about by the exchange of commodities, is the separation of town from country. One might well say that the whole economic history of society is summed up in the movement of this antithesis. However, for the moment we shall not go into this.\" The footnote credits Sir James Steuart with the best treatment of this question, noting that his Inquiry into the Principles of Political Economy (1767) appeared ten years before the Wealth of Nations (1776) and is little known—Malthus's admirers do not know that the first edition of his work on population contains, except in the purely declamatory part, very little but extracts from Steuart, as well as from Wallace and Townsend. Population density and means of communication. \"Just as a certain number of simultaneously employed workers is the material pre-condition for the division of labour within manufacture, so the number and density of the population... is a pre-condition for the division of labour within society.\" Nevertheless, \"this density is more or less relative. A relatively thinly populated country, with well-developed means of communication, has a denser population than a more numerously populated country with badly developed means of communication. In this sense, the northern states of the U.S.A. for instance, are more thickly populated than India.\" The footnote illustrates with the cotton famine after 1861: cotton production was extended at the expense of rice cultivation in some otherwise thickly populated districts of eastern India, causing local famines because, owing to deficiencies in the means of communication and hence the absence of physical links, failures of the rice crop in one district could not be compensated by importing supplies from other districts. Reciprocal reaction. \"Since the production and the circulation of commodities are the general prerequisites of the capitalist mode of production, division of labour in manufacture requires that a division of labour within society should have already attained a certain degree of development. Inversely, the division of labour in manufacture reacts back upon that in society, developing and multiplying it further. With the differentiation of the instruments of labour, the trades which produce these instruments themselves become more and more differentiated.\" The footnote notes that the manufacture of shuttles formed a special branch of industry in Holland as early as the seventeenth century. \"If the system of manufacture seizes upon a trade which was previously carried on in connection with others, either as a chief or a subordinate trade, and by one producer, these trades immediately break their connection and assert their independence of each other. If it seizes upon a particular stage in the production of a commodity, the other stages of its production become converted into as many independent trades.\" Where the finished article consists merely of parts fitted together, \"the specialized operations may re-establish themselves as genuine and separate handicrafts. In order to accomplish the division of labour in manufacture more completely, a single branch of production is split up into numerous and to some extent entirely new manufactures, according to the varieties of its raw material or the various forms that the same piece of raw material may assume.\" Thus in France alone, in the first half of the eighteenth century, over 100 different kinds of silk stuffs were woven, and in Avignon it was legally required that \"every apprentice should devote himself to only one sort of fabrication, and should not learn the preparation of several kinds of stuff at once.\" The territorial division of labour, \"which confines special branches of production to special districts of a country, acquires fresh stimulus from the system of manufacture, which exploits all natural peculiarities.\" Berkeley's The Querist (1750) is quoted: \"Whether the Woollen Manufacture of England is not divided into several parts or branches, appropriated to particular places, where they are only or principally manufactured; fine cloths in Somersetshire, coarse in Yorkshire, long ells at Exeter, saies [serges] at Sudbury, crapes at Norwich, linseys at Kendal, blankets at Witney, and so forth?\" The colonial system and the extension of the world market \"furnish us with rich materials for displaying the division of labour in society.\" Marx adds: \"This is not the place, however, for us to show how division of labour seizes upon, not only the economic, but every other sphere of society, and everywhere lays the foundation for that specialization, that development in a man of one single faculty at the expense of all others, which already caused Adam Ferguson, the master of Adam Smith, to exclaim: 'We make a nation of Helots, and have no free citizens.'\" The decisive distinction. \"But in spite of the numerous analogies and links connecting them, the division of labour in the interior of a society, and that in the interior of a workshop, differ not only in degree, but also in kind.\" The analogy appears most indisputable where there is \"an invisible bond uniting the various branches of trade.\" For instance, the cattle-breeder produces hides, the tanner makes the hides into leather, and the shoemaker makes the leather into boots. \"Here the product of each man is merely a step towards the final form, which is the combined product of their specialized labours. There are, besides, all the various trades which supply the cattle-breeder, the tanner and the shoemaker with their means of production.\" It is possible to imagine, with Adam Smith, that the difference is \"merely subjective, exists merely for the observer, who in the case of manufacture can see at a glance all the numerous operations being performed on one spot, while in the instance given above, the spreading-out of the work over great areas and the great number of people employed in each branch of labour obscure the connection.\" Marx quotes Smith at length: \"In manufacture proper, the division of labour appears to be greater, because 'those employed in every different branch of the work can often be collected into the same workhouse, and placed at once under the view of the spectator. In those great manufactures(!), on the contrary, which are destined to supply the great wants of the great body of the people, every different branch of the work employs so great a number of workmen that it is impossible to collect them all into the same workhouse... the division is not near so obvious.'\" Marx notes that the famous passage beginning \"Observe the accommodation of the most common artificer or day-labourer in a civilized and thriving country\" is \"copied almost word for word from the 'Remarks' added by B. de Mandeville to his Fable of the Bees, or Private Vices, Publick Benefits \" (first edition 1706; with remarks 1714). But Marx insists on the real, objective distinction. \"What is it that forms the bond between the independent labours of the cattle-breeder, the tanner and the shoemaker? It is the fact that their respective products are commodities. What, on the other hand, characterizes the division of labour in manufacture? The fact that the specialized worker produces no commodities. It is only the common product of all the specialized workers that becomes a commodity.\" Hodgskin is quoted: \"There is no longer anything which we can call the natural reward of individual labour. Each labourer produces only some part of a whole, and each part, having no value or utility in itself, there is nothing on which the labourer can seize, and say: It is my product, this I will keep to myself.\" The footnote about the Yankees during the Civil War illustrates the practical consequences of this distinction: a 6 per cent tax \"on all industrial products\" raised the question \"What is an industrial product?\" The answer was that a thing is produced \"when it is made,\" and it is made when it is ready for sale. Umbrellas, a composite mixture of heterogeneous parts, became products of various separate industries carried on independently in different places; they entered as separate commodities into the manufacture of umbrellas, so that all the umbrella manufacturers had to do was fit them together. The Yankees called these \"assembled articles,\" and they allow taxes to be assembled: an umbrella \"assembles\" first 6 per cent on the price of each of its elements, and a further 6 per cent on its own total price. Mediation and presupposition. \"The division of labour within society is mediated through the purchase and sale of the products of different branches of industry, while the connection between the various partial operations in a workshop is mediated through the sale of the labour-power of several workers to one capitalist, who applies it as combined labour-power. The division of labour within manufacture presupposes a concentration of the means of production in the hands of one capitalist; the division of labour within society presupposes a dispersal of those means among many independent producers of commodities.\" Within the workshop, \"the iron law of proportionality subjects definite numbers of workers to definite functions\"; in society outside, \"the play of chance and caprice results in a motley pattern of distribution of the producers and their means of production among the various branches of social labour.\" Equilibrium and anarchy. \"It is true that the different spheres of production constantly tend towards equilibrium, for the following reason. On the one hand, every producer of a commodity is obliged to produce a use-value, i.e. he must satisfy a particular social need (though the extent of these needs differs quantitatively, and there exists an inner bond which attaches the different levels of need to a system which has grown up spontaneously); on the other hand, the law of the value of commodities ultimately determines how much of its disposable labour-time society can expend on each kind of commodity. But this constant tendency on the part of the various spheres of production towards equilibrium comes into play only as a reaction against the constant upsetting of this equilibrium. The planned and regulated a priori system on which the division of labour is implemented within the workshop becomes, in the division of labour within society, an a posteriori necessity imposed by nature, controlling the unregulated caprice of the producers, and perceptible in the fluctuations of the barometer of market prices.\" Authority and despotism. \"Division of labour within the workshop implies the undisputed authority of the capitalist over men, who are merely the members of a total mechanism which belongs to him. The division of labour within society brings into contact independent producers of commodities, who acknowledge no authority other than that of competition, of the coercion exerted by the pressure of their reciprocal interests, just as in the animal kingdom the 'war of all against all' more or less preserves the conditions of existence of every species. The same bourgeois consciousness which celebrates the division of labour in the workshop, the lifelong annexation of the worker to a partial operation, and his complete subjection to capital, as an organization of labour that increases its productive power, denounces with equal vigour every conscious attempt to control and regulate the process of production socially, as an inroad upon such sacred things as the rights of property, freedom and the self-determining 'genius' of the individual capitalist. It is very characteristic that the enthusiastic apologists of the factory system have nothing more damning to urge against a general organization of labour in society than that it would turn the whole of society into a factory.\" The inverse ratio. \"If, in the society where the capitalist mode of production prevails, anarchy in the social division of labour and despotism in the manufacturing division of labour mutually condition each other, we find, on the contrary, in those earlier forms of society in which the separation of trades has been spontaneously developed, then crystallized, and finally made permanent by law, on the one hand, a specimen of the organization of the labour of society in accordance with an approved and authoritative plan, and, on the other, the entire exclusion of division of labour in the workshop or, at the least, its development on a minute scale, sporadically and accidentally.\" The footnote quotes Marx's own Misère de la philosophie : \"It can... be laid down as a general rule that the less authority presides over the division of labour inside society, the more the division of labour develops inside the workshop, and the more it is subjected there to the authority of a single person. Thus authority in the workshop and authority in society, in relation to the division of labour, are in inverse ratio to each other.\" The Indian communities. The ancient Indian communities, \"some of which continue to exist to this day,\" are based on \"the possession of the land in common, on the blending of agriculture and handicrafts and on an unalterable division of labour, which serves as a fixed plan and basis for action whenever a new community is started.\" They occupy areas of from 100 up to several thousand acres, each forming \"a compact whole producing all it requires.\" Most products are destined for direct use and are not commodities. \"Hence production here is independent of that division of labour brought about in Indian society as a whole by the exchange of commodities. It is the surplus alone that becomes a commodity, and a part of that surplus cannot become a commodity until it has reached the hands of the state, because from time immemorial a certain quantity of the community's production has found its way to the state as rent in kind.\" In the simplest communities, land is tilled in common and the produce divided among members; spinning and weaving are carried on in each family as subsidiary industries. Alongside the mass of people occupied in the same way, there are: the \"chief inhabitant,\" who is judge, police authority, and tax-gatherer in one; the book-keeper, who keeps accounts of the tillage; another official, who prosecutes criminals and protects strangers; the boundary man, who guards boundaries against neighbouring communities; the water-overseer, who distributes water from the common tanks for irrigation; the Brahmin, who conducts religious services; the schoolmaster, who teaches children reading and writing on the sand; the calendar-Brahmin, or astrologer, who makes known the lucky or unlucky days for seed-time and harvest; a smith and a carpenter, who make and repair agricultural implements; the potter; the barber; the washerman; the silversmith; here and there the poet, who in some communities replaces the silversmith, in others the schoolmaster. \"This dozen or so of individuals is maintained at the expense of the whole community. If the population increases, a new community is founded, on the pattern of the old one, on unoccupied land. The whole mechanism reveals a systematic division of labour; but a division like that in manufacture is impossible, since the smith, the carpenter, etc. find themselves faced with an unchanging market, and at the most there occur, according to the sizes of the villages, two or three smiths or carpenters, instead of one.\" \"The law that regulates the division of labour in the community acts with the irresistible authority of a law of nature, while each individual craftsman, the smith, the carpenter and so on, conducts in his workshop all the operations of his handicraft in the traditional way, but independently, and without recognizing any authority. The simplicity of the productive organism in these self-sufficing communities which constantly reproduce themselves in the same form and, when accidentally destroyed, spring up again on the same spot and with the same name—this simplicity supplies the key to the riddle of the unchangeability of Asiatic societies, which is in such striking contrast with the constant dissolution and refounding of Asiatic states, and their never-ceasing changes of dynasty. The structure of the fundamental economic elements of society remains untouched by the storms which blow up in the cloudy regions of politics.\" The footnotes quote Mark Wilks's Historical Sketches of the South of India and George Campbell's Modern India ; and Raffles's History of Java : \"Under this simple form... the inhabitants of the country have lived from time immemorial. The boundaries of the villages have been but seldom altered, and though the villages themselves have been sometimes injured, and even desolated by war, famine, and disease, the same name, the same limits, the same interests, and even the same families, have continued for ages. The inhabitants give themselves no trouble about the breaking up and division of kingdoms; while the village remains entire, they care not to what power it is transferred, or to what sovereign it devolves; its internal economy remains unchanged.\" The guilds. \"The rules of the guilds, as I have said before, deliberately hindered the transformation of the single master into a capitalist, by placing very strict limits on the number of apprentices and journeymen he could employ. Moreover, he could employ his journeymen only in the handicraft in which he was himself a master. The guilds zealously repelled every encroachment by merchants' capital, the only free form of capital which confronted them. A merchant could buy every kind of commodity, but he could not buy labour as a commodity. He existed only on sufferance, as a dealer in the products of the handicrafts. If circumstances called for a further division of labour, the existing guilds split themselves up into subordinate sections, or founded new guilds by the side of the old ones. But they did this without concentrating different handicrafts in one workshop. Hence the guild organization, however much it may have contributed to creating the material conditions for the existence of manufacture by separating, isolating and perfecting the handicrafts, excluded the kind of division of labour characteristic of manufacture. On the whole, the worker and his means of production remained closely united, like the snail with its shell, and therefore the principal basis of manufacture was absent, namely the autonomy of the means of production, as capital, vis-à-vis the worker.\" The decisive conclusion. \"While the division of labour in society at large, whether mediated through the exchange of commodities or not, can exist in the most diverse economic formations of society, the division of labour in the workshop, as practised by manufacture, is an entirely specific creation of the capitalist mode of production.\" The Capitalist Character of Manufacture Marx now turns to the specifically capitalist character of manufacture. \"An increased number of workers under the control of one capitalist is the natural starting-point, both of co-operation in general and of manufacture in particular. But the division of labour in manufacture makes this increase in the number of workers a technical necessity. The minimum number that any given capitalist is bound to employ is here prescribed by the previously established division of labour. On the other hand, the advantages of further division can be obtained only by adding to the number of workers, and this means adding not single individuals but multiples.\" The law of increasing capital. \"However, an increase in the variable component of the capital employed necessitates an increase in its constant component too, i.e. both in the available extent of the conditions of production, such as workshops, implements, etc., and, in particular, in raw material, the demand for which grows much more quickly than the number of workers. The quantity of it consumed in a given time, by a given amount of labour, increases in the same ratio as does the productive power of that labour through its division. Hence it is a law, springing from the technical character of manufacture, that the minimum amount of capital which the capitalist must possess has to go on increasing. In other words, the transformation of the social means of production and subsistence into capital must keep extending.\" The footnote quotes Storch: \"It is not sufficient that the capital\" (he should have said the necessary means of subsistence and of production) \"required for the subdivision of handicrafts should be in readiness in the society: it must also be accumulated in the hands of the employers in sufficiently large quantities to enable them to conduct their operations on a large scale... The more the division increases, the more does the constant employment of a given number of workers require a greater outlay of capital in tools, raw material, etc.\" And Marx's own Misère de la philosophie : \"The concentration of the instruments of production and the division of labour are as inseparable one from the other as are, in the political sphere, the concentration of public authority and the division of private interests.\" The collective worker as capital. \"In manufacture, as well as in simple co-operation, the collective working organism is a form of existence of capital. The social mechanism of production, which is made up of numerous individual specialized workers, belongs to the capitalist. Hence the productive power which results from the combination of various kinds of labour appears as the productive power of capital. Manufacture proper not only subjects the previously independent worker to the discipline and command of capital, but creates in addition a hierarchical structure amongst the workers themselves.\" The crippling of the worker. \"While simple co-operation leaves the mode of the individual's labour for the most part unchanged, manufacture thoroughly revolutionizes it, and seizes labour-power by its roots. It converts the worker into a crippled monstrosity by furthering his particular skill as in a forcing-house, through the suppression of a whole world of productive drives and inclinations, just as in the states of La Plata they butcher a whole beast for the sake of his hide or his tallow. Not only is the specialized work distributed among the different individuals, but the individual himself is divided up, and transformed into the automatic motor of a detail operation, thus realizing the absurd fable of Menenius Agrippa, which presents man as a mere fragment of his own body.\" The footnote quotes Dugald Stewart: manufacturing workers are \"living automatons... employed in the details of the work.\" The Menenius Agrippa footnote explains the Roman patrician's fable: the patricians represented the stomach, the plebeians the limbs; the limbs were required to feed the stomach, and if the stomach were not fed, the limbs themselves would soon wither. Marx's footnote adds: \"In corals, each individual is, in fact, the stomach of the whole group; but it supplies the group with nourishment instead of extracting it, like the Roman patrician.\" The worker's dependence on capital. \"If, in the first place, the worker sold his labour-power to capital because he lacked the material means of producing a commodity, now his own individual labour-power withholds its services unless it has been sold to capital. It will continue to function only in an environment which first comes into existence after its sale, namely the capitalist's workshop. Unfitted by nature to make anything independently, the manufacturing worker develops his productive activity only as an appendage of that workshop. As the chosen people bore in their features the sign that they were the property of Jehovah, so the division of labour brands the manufacturing worker as the property of capital.\" The footnote quotes Storch: \"The worker who is the master of a whole craft can work and find means of subsistence anywhere: the other\" (the manufacturing worker) \"is only an appendage who, when he is separated from his fellows, possesses neither capability nor independence, and finds himself forced to accept any law it is thought fit to impose.\" The separation of mental and manual labour. \"The knowledge, judgement and will which, even though to a small extent, are exercised by the independent peasant or handicraftsman, in the same way as the savage makes the whole art of war consist in the exercise of his personal cunning, are faculties now required only for the workshop as a whole. The possibility of an intelligent direction of production expands in one direction, because it vanishes in many others. What is lost by the specialized workers is concentrated in the capital which confronts them.\" Ferguson is quoted: \"The former may have gained what the other has lost.\" \"It is a result of the division of labour in manufacture that the worker is brought face to face with the intellectual potentialities [geistige Potenzen] of the material process of production as the property of another and as a power which rules over him. This process of separation starts in simple co-operation, where the capitalist represents to the individual workers the unity and the will of the whole body of social labour. It is developed in manufacture, which mutilates the worker, turning him into a fragment of himself. It is completed in large-scale industry, which makes science a potentiality for production which is distinct from labour and presses it into the service of capital.\" The footnote quotes W. Thompson: \"The man of knowledge and the productive labourer come to be widely divided from each other, and knowledge, instead of remaining the handmaid of labour in the hand of the labourer to increase his productive powers... has almost everywhere arrayed itself against labour. Knowledge becomes an instrument, capable of being detached from labour and opposed to it.\" Ignorance as the mother of industry. \"In manufacture, the social productive power of the collective worker, hence of capital, is enriched through the impoverishment of the worker in individual productive power.\" Ferguson is quoted: \"Ignorance is the mother of industry as well as of superstition. Reflection and fancy are subject to err; but a habit of moving the hand or the foot is independent of either. Manufactures, accordingly, prosper most where the mind is least consulted, and where the workshop may... be considered as an engine, the parts of which are men.\" Marx adds: \"As a matter of fact, in the middle of the eighteenth century some manufacturers preferred to employ semi-idiots for certain operations which, though simple, were trade secrets.\" The footnote cites J. D. Tuckett. Adam Smith on the stupidity of the specialized worker. Marx quotes Smith at length: \"The understandings of the greater part of men... are necessarily formed by their ordinary employments. The man whose whole life is spent in performing a few simple operations... has no occasion to exert his understanding... He generally becomes as stupid and ignorant as it is possible for a human creature to become.\" After describing the stupidity of the specialized worker, Smith goes on: \"The uniformity of his stationary life naturally corrupts the courage of his mind... It corrupts even the activity of his body and renders him incapable of exerting his strength with vigour and perseverance in any other employments than that to which he has been bred. His dexterity at his own particular trade seems in this manner to be acquired at the expense of his intellectual, social, and martial virtues. But in every improved and civilized society, this is the state into which the labouring poor, that is, the great body of the people, must necessarily fall.\" Marx notes that Smith, as a pupil of Adam Ferguson, who had pointed out the harmful effects of the division of labour, \"was perfectly clear on this point.\" In the Introduction to his work, \"where he professedly praises the division of labour, he indicates only in passing that it is the source of social inequalities. It is not till the fifth book, on the 'Revenue of the State', that he reproduces Ferguson.\" Marx adds: \"In my Misère de la philosophie , I have sufficiently explained the historical relation between Ferguson, Adam Smith, Lemontey and Say as regards their criticisms of the division of labour, and also shown, for the first time, that the division of labour in manufacture is a specific form of the capitalist mode of production.\" Garnier's opposition to education. \"For preventing the complete deterioration of the great mass of the people which arises from the division of labour, Adam Smith recommends education of the people by the state, but in prudently homoeopathic doses. Garnier, his French translator and commentator, who quite naturally developed into a senator under the first French Empire, is entirely consistent in opposing Smith on this point. Education of the people, he urges, violates the first law of the division of labour, and with it 'our whole social system would be proscribed'. 'Like all other divisions of labour,' he says, 'that between hand labour and head labour is more pronounced and decided in proportion as society' (he rightly uses this word to describe capital, landed property and the state that belongs to them) 'becomes richer. The division of labour, like every other, is an effect of past, and a cause of future progress... ought the government then to work in opposition to this division of labour, and to hinder its natural course? Ought it to expend a part of the public money in the attempt to confound and blend together two classes of labour which are striving after division and separation?'\" Ferguson's remark is quoted: \"And thinking itself, in this age of separations, may become a peculiar craft.\" Industrial pathology. \"Some crippling of body and mind is inseparable even from the division of labour in society as a whole. However, since manufacture carries this social separation of branches of labour much further, and also, by its peculiar division, attacks the individual at the very roots of his life, it is the first system to provide the materials and the impetus for industrial pathology.\" The footnote cites Ramazzini's De morbis artificum (1713), translated into French in 1777 and reprinted in 1841; the period of large-scale industry has very much enlarged this catalogue of workers' diseases. Other works cited include Fonteret's Hygiène physique et morale de l'ouvrier dans les grandes villes en général, et dans la ville de Lyon en particulier (Paris, 1858), Rohatzsch's Die Krankheiten, welche verschiedenen Ständen, Altern, und Geschlechtern eigenthümlich sind (6 vols., Ulm, 1860), and Eduard Reich's Über die Entartung des Menschen (Erlangen, 1868). In 1854 the Society of Arts appointed a Commission of Inquiry into industrial pathology; the list of documents collected is in the catalogue of the Twickenham Economic Museum. The official Reports on Public Health are also important. Urquhart is quoted: \"To subdivide a man is to execute him, if he deserves the sentence, to assassinate him if he does not... The subdivision of labour is the assassination of a people.\" The footnote notes that Hegel held \"very heretical views on the division of labour\": in his Philosophy of Right he says, \"By educated men we may prima facie understand those who... can do what others do.\" Manufacture as a conscious, methodical form. \"Co-operation based on the division of labour, in other words manufacture, begins as a spontaneous formation. As soon as it attains a degree of consistency and extension, it becomes the conscious, methodical and systematic form of capitalist production. The history of manufacture proper shows how the division of labour which is peculiar to it acquires the most appropriate form at first by experience, as it were behind the backs of the actors, and then, like the guild handicrafts, strives to hold fast to that form when once it has been found, and here and there succeeds in keeping it for centuries. Any alteration in this form, except in trivial matters, never results from anything but a revolution in the instruments of labour. Modern manufacture—I am not referring here to large-scale industry, which is based on machinery—either finds the disjecta membra poetae ready to hand, and only waiting to be collected together, as is the case in the manufacture of clothes in large towns, or it can easily apply the principles of division, simply by exclusively assigning the various operations of a handicraft (such as book-binding) to particular men. In such cases, a week's experience is enough to determine the proportion between the numbers of the 'hands' necessary for the various functions.\" The footnote mocks \"the pleasant belief in the inventive genius displayed a priori by the individual capitalist in the division of labour,\" which \"exists nowadays only among German professors of the stamp of Herr Roscher, who, to recompense the capitalist from whose Jovian head the division of labour sprang ready formed, dedicates to him 'various wages'. The more or less extensive application of the division of labour depends on the length of the purse, not on the magnitude of the genius.\" The summary of manufacture's historical role. \"By dissection of handicraft activity into its separate components, by specialization of the instruments of labour, by the formation of specialized workers and by grouping and combining the latter into a single mechanism, the division of labour in manufacture provides the social process of production with a qualitative articulation and a quantitative proportionality. It thereby creates a definite organization of social labour and at the same time develops new, and social, productive powers of labour. As a specifically capitalist form of the process of social production—and, on the foundations available to it, it could not develop in any other form than a capitalist one—the division of labour in manufacture is merely a particular method of creating relative surplus-value, or of augmenting the self-valorization of capital—usually described as social wealth, 'wealth of nations', etc.—at the expense of the worker. Not only does it increase the socially productive power of labour for the benefit of the capitalist instead of the worker; it also does this by crippling the individual worker. It produces new conditions for the domination of capital over labour. If, therefore, on the one hand, it appears historically as an advance and a necessary aspect of the economic process of the formation of society, on the other hand, it appears as a more refined and civilized means of exploitation.\" Political economy and the standpoint of use-value. \"Political economy, which first emerged as an independent science during the period of manufacture, is only able to view the social division of labour in terms of the division found in manufacture, i.e. as a means of producing more commodities with a given quantity of labour, and consequently of cheapening commodities and accelerating the accumulation of capital. In most striking contrast with this accentuation of quantity and exchange-value is the attitude of the writers of classical antiquity, who are exclusively concerned with quality and use-value.\" The footnote notes that older writers like Petty and the anonymous author of The Advantages of the East-India Trade bring out the capitalist character of the division of labour more clearly than Adam Smith. \"As a result of the separation of the social branches of production, commodities are better made, men's various inclinations and talents select suitable fields of action, and without some restriction no important results can be obtained anywhere. Hence both product and producer are improved by the division of labour. If the growth of the quantity produced is occasionally mentioned, this is only done with reference to the greater abundance of use-values. There is not a word alluding to exchange-value, or to the cheapening of commodities. This standpoint, the standpoint of use-value, is adopted by Plato, who treats the division of labour as the foundation on which the division of society into estates is based, and also by Xenophon, who with his characteristic bourgeois instinct already comes closer to the division of labour within the workshop. Plato's Republic , in so far as the division of labour is treated in it as the formative principle of the state, is merely an Athenian idealization of the Egyptian caste system, Egypt having served as the model of an industrial country to others of his contemporaries, e.g. Isocrates. It retained this importance for the Greeks even at the time of the Roman Empire.\" The footnotes elaborate. Beccaria and James Harris are exceptions among the moderns who follow the ancients. Beccaria: \"Everyone knows from experience that if the hands and the intelligence are always applied to the same kind of work and the same products, these will be produced more easily, in greater abundance, and in higher quality than if each individual makes for himself the things he needs... In this way, men are divided up into various classes and conditions, to their own advantage and to that of the community.\" James Harris (the philosopher, not his son the diplomat): \"The whole argument to prove society natural (i.e. by division of employments)... is taken from the second book of Plato's Republic .\" Greek quotations from the Odyssey and Archilochus illustrate the ancient view: \"For different men take joy in different works\"; \"Men differ as to what things cheer their hearts.\" Thucydides has Pericles say that the Athenians are more ready for war with their persons than the Spartans with their money. \"Nevertheless, even with regard to material production, autarkeia [self-sufficiency], as opposed to the division of labour, remained their ideal.\" The footnote notes that at the fall of the Thirty Tyrants (404 B.C.) there were still less than 5,000 Athenians without landed property. Plato's insistence that \"the work will not wait for the leisure of the workman, but the workman must attend to it as his main affair, and not treat it as a subsidiary occupation\" leads to the conclusion that \"more things are produced, and better, and more easily, when one man performs one task according to his nature, at the right moment, and at leisure from other occupations.\" Marx adds the modern parallel: English bleachers protested against a Factory Act clause providing for fixed meal-times for all workers, saying their business cannot await the convenience of the workmen, because \"in the various operations of singeing, washing, bleaching, mangling, calendering, and dyeing, none of them can be stopped at a given moment without risk of damage... to enforce the same dinner hour for all the workpeople might occasionally subject valuable goods to the risk of damage by incomplete operations.\" Marx's aside: \"Le platonisme ou va-t-il se nicher!\" (\"Where will Platonism be found next!\"). Xenophon, in the Cyropaedia , explains that the Persian king's food is more tasty because in great towns each man can find many buyers and one trade is sufficient; indeed, not even one complete trade is needed—one man makes shoes for men, another for women; one gets a living by sewing, another by cutting out shoes; one cuts out clothes, another sews the pieces together. \"It follows necessarily then, that he who does the simplest kind of work undoubtedly does it better than anyone else. So it is with the art of cooking.\" Marx notes that Xenophon \"lays stress exclusively on the excellence to be attained in the quality of the use-value, although he is already aware that the degree of division of labour reached is dependent on the extent of the market.\" The obstacles to manufacture's full development. \"During the manufacturing period proper, i.e. the period in which manufacture is the predominant form taken by capitalist production, the full development of its own peculiar tendencies comes up against obstacles from many directions. Although, as we have already seen, manufacture creates a simple division of the workers into skilled and unskilled, at the same time as it inserts them into a hierarchical structure, the number of unskilled workers remains very limited owing to the preponderant influence of the skilled. Although it adapts the particular operations to the various degrees of maturity, strength and development of the living instruments of labour, and thus tends towards the exploitation of women and children in production, this tendency is largely defeated by the habits and the resistance of the male workers. Although the splitting-up of handicrafts lowers the cost of forming the worker, and thereby lowers his value, a long period of apprenticeship is still necessary for certain more difficult kinds of work; moreover, even where it would be superfluous, the workers jealously retain it. In England, for instance, we find the laws of apprenticeship, with their seven years' probation, in full force down to the end of the manufacturing period; they are not entirely thrown aside until the advent of large-scale industry. Since handicraft skill is the foundation of manufacture, and since the mechanism of manufacture as a whole possesses no objective framework which would be independent of the workers themselves, capital is constantly compelled to wrestle with the insubordination of the workers.\" Ure is quoted: \"By the infirmity of human nature, it happens that the more skilful the workman, the more self-willed and intractable he is apt to become, and of course the less fit a component of a mechanical system in which... he may do great damage to the whole.\" Hence \"the complaint that the workers lack discipline runs through the whole of the period of manufacture.\" Marx adds: \"Even if we did not have the testimony of contemporary writers on this, we have two simple facts which speak volumes: firstly, during the period between the sixteenth century and the epoch of large-scale industry capital failed in its attempt to seize control of the whole disposable labour-time of the manufacturing workers, and secondly, the manufactures are short-lived, changing their locality from one country to another with the emigration or immigration of workers. 'Order must in one way or another be established,' exclaims in 1770 the often-cited author of the Essay on Trade and Commerce . 'Order,' echoes Dr Andrew Ure sixty-six years later, was lacking in the system of manufacture, based as it was on 'the scholastic dogma of the division of labour', and 'Arkwright created order'.\" The narrow technical basis and its supersession. \"At the same time, manufacture was able either to seize upon the production of society to its full extent, or to revolutionize that production to its very core. It towered up as an artificial economic construction, on the broad foundation of the town handicrafts and the domestic industries of the countryside. At a certain stage of its development, the narrow technical basis on which manufacture rested came into contradiction with requirements of production which it had itself created.\" The machine-factory is the resolution. \"One of its most finished products was the workshop for the production of the instruments of labour themselves, and particularly the complicated pieces of mechanical apparatus already being employed. 'A machine-factory,' says Ure, 'displayed the division of labour in manifold gradations—the file, the drill, the lathe, having each its different workmen in the order of skill.' This workshop, the product of the division of labour in manufacture, produced in its turn—machines. It is machines that abolish the role of the handicraftsman as the regulating principle of social production. Thus, on the one hand, the technical reason for the lifelong attachment of the worker to a partial function is swept away. On the other hand, the barriers placed in the way of the domination of capital by this same regulating principle now also fall.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed The chapter yields several systemic laws and principles that extend far beyond the historical period it describes. First, the division of labour is not a technical given but a social relation. Marx's decisive move is to distinguish the division of labour in society from the division of labour in the workshop, and to show that they differ \"not only in degree, but also in kind.\" The former is mediated through the exchange of commodities and presupposes the dispersal of the means of production among independent producers; the latter is mediated through the sale of labour-power to a single capitalist and presupposes the concentration of the means of production in one pair of hands. The social division of labour can exist in the most diverse economic formations; the manufacturing division of labour is \"an entirely specific creation of the capitalist mode of production.\" This distinction demolishes the Smithian reduction of all division of labour to a single, quasi-natural phenomenon and exposes the ideological function of that reduction: by presenting the workshop as a model of society, bourgeois political economy naturalizes the despotism of capital. Second, the division of labour under capital is a method of producing relative surplus-value. Manufacture does not merely increase output; it increases the rate of surplus-value by lowering the value of labour-power. The mechanism is precise: the splitting-up of handicrafts reduces or eliminates apprenticeship costs, thereby shortening the necessary labour-time required to reproduce the worker's means of subsistence, thereby extending the domain of surplus labour. \"Everything that shortens the necessary labour-time required for the reproduction of labour-power, extends the domain of surplus labour.\" The productivity gains of manufacture thus accrue to capital, not to the worker, and they do so by crippling the worker's individual productive capacity. \"The social productive power of the collective worker, hence of capital, is enriched through the impoverishment of the worker in individual productive power.\" Third, the division of labour in manufacture rests on a technically narrow basis that generates its own contradiction. Because every partial process must be capable of being done by hand, manufacture \"excludes a really scientific division of the production process into its component parts.\" It can only decompose labour according to the limits of human dexterity, not according to the objective logic of chemistry and physics. This narrowness produces a structural antagonism: because handicraft skill remains the foundation of production, and because the mechanism of manufacture \"possesses no objective framework which would be independent of the workers themselves,\" capital is \"constantly compelled to wrestle with the insubordination of the workers.\" The skilled worker remains self-willed and intractable; apprenticeship laws persist; manufactures are short-lived and migrate in search of docile labour. The resolution comes only with machinery, which abolishes the handicraftsman as the regulating principle of social production and removes both the technical reason for the lifelong attachment of the worker to a partial function and the barriers that handicraft skill placed in the way of capital's domination. Fourth, the separation of mental and manual labour is a historical product, not a natural fact. \"The knowledge, judgement and will which, even though to a small extent, are exercised by the independent peasant or handicraftsman... are faculties now required only for the workshop as a whole. The possibility of an intelligent direction of production expands in one direction, because it vanishes in many others. What is lost by the specialized workers is concentrated in the capital which confronts them.\" This process begins in simple cooperation, develops in manufacture, and is completed in large-scale industry, where science becomes \"a potentiality for production which is distinct from labour and pressed into the service of capital.\" The famous Ferguson quotation—\"Ignorance is the mother of industry as well as of superstition\"—and the Adam Smith passage on the stupidity of the specialized worker show that even bourgeois political economy recognized the phenomenon; what Marx adds is the explanation of its structural necessity and its function in the valorization of capital. Fifth, the historicality of the forms of production. Manufacture is not an eternal technique but a historically bounded mode of production with a beginning, a period of dominance, and an end. Its \"final form is always the same—a productive mechanism whose organs are human beings\"—but that form is unstable. Manufacture creates the material conditions for its own supersession: the differentiation and specialization of the instruments of labour, the workshop for the production of the instruments of labour, and the machines that abolish the role of the handicraftsman. The chapter thus illustrates the general method of historical materialism: forms of production arise, develop, and are superseded through the working-out of their internal contradictions. Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications The critique of political economy. Marx's engagement with Adam Smith, Ferguson, Steuart, Petty, Blanqui, Storch, Skarbek, Babbage, Ure, and the ancients (Plato, Xenophon, Isocrates, Thucydides, Diodorus) is not antiquarian. It is a demonstration that political economy, from its emergence as an independent science in the manufacturing period, has been unable to think the division of labour except through the lens of the workshop. The ancients, concerned with quality and use-value, treated the division of labour as the foundation of the state and the improvement of the producer. The moderns, concerned with quantity and exchange-value, treat it as a means of producing more commodities with a given quantity of labour, cheapening commodities, and accelerating accumulation. Marx's recovery of the ancient standpoint is not a romantic nostalgia for self-sufficiency but a way of denaturalizing the modern one: by showing that the division of labour has been understood differently in different epochs, he shows that it need not be understood as it is under capital. The alienation of the worker. The chapter provides one of Marx's most vivid accounts of alienation. The worker is \"converted into a crippled monstrosity\"; the individual himself is \"divided up, and transformed into the automatic motor of a detail operation\"; he is \"branded as the property of capital.\" The Menenius Agrippa fable, in which the patrician stomach extracts nourishment from the plebeian limbs, becomes a \"fable\" realized in manufacture—except that Marx notes the corals do the opposite, supplying nourishment to the group rather than extracting it. The Urquhart line—\"To subdivide a man is to execute him, if he deserves the sentence, to assassinate him if he does not... The subdivision of labour is the assassination of a people\"—captures the human cost. Manufacture is \"the first system to provide the materials and the impetus for industrial pathology,\" and Ramazzini's De morbis artificum (1713) is cited as the founding text of a tradition that would catalogue the diseases of workers. The historical role of manufacture. Marx is careful to avoid a one-sided condemnation. Manufacture \"appears historically as an advance and a necessary aspect of the economic process of the formation of society\" and also \"as a more refined and civilized means of exploitation.\" It develops productive powers—continuity, uniformity, regularity, order, intensity; it provides the social process of production with \"a qualitative articulation and a quantitative proportionality\"; it creates a definite organization of social labour. But these gains are appropriated by capital and purchased at the cost of the worker's crippling. The dialectic of progress and exploitation is the chapter's central historical judgment. The theory of the state and of social authority. Marx's contrast between the \"undisputed authority of the capitalist\" in the workshop and the \"no authority other than that of competition\" in society generates a theory of the modern state. The same bourgeois consciousness that celebrates workshop despotism denounces social regulation as an inroad upon \"the rights of property, freedom and the self-determining 'genius' of the individual capitalist.\" The apologists of the factory system have \"nothing more damning to urge against a general organization of labour in society than that it would turn the whole of society into a factory.\" Against this, Marx notes that in earlier forms of society where the separation of trades had been \"crystallized, and finally made permanent by law,\" there was \"a specimen of the organization of the labour of society in accordance with an approved and authoritative plan,\" combined with \"the entire exclusion of division of labour in the workshop.\" The inverse ratio between authority in the workshop and authority in society is a general law: \"the less authority presides over the division of labour inside society, the more the division of labour develops inside the workshop, and the more it is subjected there to the authority of a single person.\" The Asiatic mode of production and the theory of history. Marx's analysis of the Indian communities, with their \"unalterable division of labour,\" their self-sufficiency, their reproduction in the same form, and their indifference to political storms, supplies \"the key to the riddle of the unchangeability of Asiatic societies.\" The simplicity of the productive organism explains why \"the structure of the fundamental economic elements of society remains untouched by the storms which blow up in the cloudy regions of politics.\" This is not a theory of Asian stagnation but a theory of the relationship between the economic base and political superstructure: where the economic elements are self-reproducing and self-sufficient, political changes do not transform the fundamental structure. The contrast with the guilds is instructive: the guilds, by keeping worker and means of production \"closely united, like the snail with its shell,\" also lacked \"the principal basis of manufacture... the autonomy of the means of production, as capital, vis-à-vis the worker.\" The historical emergence of the working class. Manufacture creates \"a class of so-called unskilled labourers, a class strictly excluded by the nature of handicraft industry.\" It develops a \"hierarchy of labour-powers\" with a corresponding \"scale of wages,\" and it separates workers into skilled and unskilled. The worker who once \"could work and find means of subsistence anywhere\" now \"possesses neither capability nor independence\" and \"finds himself forced to accept any law it is thought fit to impose\" unless he sells his labour-power to capital. The manufacturing worker is thus the historical precursor of the fully proletarianized worker of large-scale industry—a worker whose labour-power \"withholds its services unless it has been sold to capital.\" The long shadow of the chapter. The themes developed here—the separation of mental and manual labour, the degradation of work under capital, the appropriation of the collective productive power by capital, the contrast between the anarchy of the market and the despotism of the workshop, the historicality of productive forms—reverberate throughout Marx's later work and throughout subsequent social theory. The chapter's insistence that manufacture is neither a natural technique nor a neutral advance, but a specific social relation with specific costs and specific beneficiaries, remains its most enduring lesson. The specialized worker and his tools are the simplest elements of manufacture; but the whole mechanism they compose is a human machinery, and its product is not only commodities but also a crippled humanity and a consolidated domination. To understand manufacture is to understand how capital, before it invented the machine, first made a machine of the human being. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 13 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 15"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 15", "title": "Chapter 15", "subtitle": "Machinery and Large-Scale Industry", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 15/Chapter 15  Machinery and Large-Scale Industry_explanation.html", "word_count": 40414, "read_time": "202 min read", "tags": ["Machinery & Technology", "Division of Labour & Manufacture", "Subsumption of Labour (Formal vs Real)", "The Working Day & Factory Acts", "Industrial Reserve Army & Surplus Population"], "keywords": ["Machinery and Modern Industry", "Development of Machinery (Motor, Transmitting, Tool)", "Formal vs Real Subsumption of Labour", "Female and Child Labour Exploitation", "Intensification and Prolongation of Labour", "Displacement of Workers & Compensation Theory", "Creation of Industrial Reserve Army through Mechanization", "Factory Legislation & Education Clauses", "Revolution in Agriculture and Urban Migration"], "text": "Chapter 15 Machinery And Large-Scale Industry - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 40,380 words ⏱️ ~202 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 15: Machinery and Large-Scale Industry — An Exhaustive Scholarly Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem and Intellectual Objective Chapter 15 of Marx's Capital , Volume 1, stands as perhaps the most technically dense and historically exhaustive treatment of the material foundations of capitalist production. The chapter's core thesis is devastatingly simple in its formulation yet endlessly complex in its ramifications: machinery, under capitalism, is not a neutral instrument of progress but a means for producing surplus-value —a weapon in the hands of capital for the extraction of unpaid labour. Marx opens with John Stuart Mill's melancholic observation that it is \"questionable if all the mechanical inventions yet made have lightened the day's toil of any human being,\" and immediately counters that this was \"by no means the aim of the application of machinery under capitalism.\" The machine's purpose, like every other instrument for increasing the productivity of labour, is to \"cheapen commodities and, by shortening the part of the working day in which the worker works for himself, to lengthen the other part, the part he gives to the capitalist for nothing.\" This chapter constitutes the culmination of Marx's analysis of relative surplus-value , which he had begun developing in previous chapters on co-operation, the division of labour, and manufacture. The production of relative surplus-value—surplus-value extracted not by lengthening the working day (absolute surplus-value) but by increasing the productivity of labour so that the value of labour-power falls—finds its most mature and revolutionary expression in the factory system based on machinery. Here, for the first time in human history, the instruments of labour acquire a material mode of existence—the machine—that necessitates the replacement of human force by natural forces and the replacement of the rule of thumb by the conscious application of natural science. The chapter's intellectual objective is threefold. First, Marx seeks to establish the material-technical foundations of large-scale industry, distinguishing the machine from the tool in a historically rigorous manner, and tracing the development from the individual machine to the organized system of machinery that constitutes the modern factory. Second, he aims to demonstrate how this technical revolution transforms the social relations of production —how it revolutionizes the worker's relationship to capital, the composition of the working class, the nature of labour, and the conditions of existence for the masses of producers. Third, and perhaps most importantly, he undertakes a polemical demolition of bourgeois political economy's \"compensation theory\"—the claim that machinery, though initially displacing workers, ultimately sets free capital that re-employs them. Against this apologetic doctrine, Marx demonstrates that machinery under capitalism produces a permanent surplus population, intensifies exploitation, and creates contradictions that point toward the transcendence of the capitalist mode of production itself. How This Chapter Fits Into the Broader Theoretical Framework Chapter 15 occupies a pivotal position in Capital 's architecture. It completes Part IV on the production of relative surplus-value and serves as the immediate foundation for Part VII on the accumulation of capital. The chapter's analysis of machinery explains how the capitalist mode of production acquires its specifically modern character—its industrial basis in the factory system, its cyclical crises, its creation of an industrial reserve army, and its revolutionary impact on all pre-existing forms of production. The chapter also prepares the ground for critical concepts developed later in Marx's work: the organic composition of capital (the ratio of constant to variable capital), which is first mentioned here; the industrial reserve army and its role in capital accumulation; the tendency of the rate of profit to fall (hinted at in the discussion of the contradiction between the two factors of surplus-value); and the metabolic rift between humanity and nature, which Marx introduces in the chapter's final section on agriculture. The analysis of machinery thus functions as the material hinge upon which Marx's entire theory of capitalist development turns. Moreover, the chapter contains some of Marx's most extended historical and empirical research—drawing extensively from Blue Books, factory inspector reports, parliamentary commissions, and contemporary economic literature. This historical material is not merely illustrative but constitutive of the argument: Marx demonstrates that the laws he derives theoretically are verified in the actual historical experience of English industry, the most advanced capitalist economy of his time. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Development of Machinery: From Tool to Machine Marx begins Section 1 with a methodological intervention that deliberately distinguishes his approach from that of mathematicians, mechanics, and English economists. The latter call a tool a simple machine and a machine a complex tool, seeing no essential difference between them. They even give the name of machine to simple mechanical aids such as the lever, the inclined plane, the screw, and the wedge. From the economic standpoint , however, Marx asserts that this explanation \"is worth nothing, because the historical element is missing from it.\" Every machine is indeed a combination of these simple mechanical powers, but the crucial distinction lies not in physical complexity but in the historical transformation of the instrument of labour from a manual implement into a mechanism. A second, equally inadequate attempt to distinguish tool from machine proposes that in the case of the tool, man is the motive power, whereas the machine is powered by a natural force independent of man—animal, water, wind, or steam. Marx demolishes this criterion with devastating clarity. A plough drawn by oxen, common to the most diverse epochs of production, would under this definition be a machine, while Claussen's circular loom, which weaves 96,000 picks per minute though set in motion by a single worker's hand, would be a mere tool. Yet the same loom, if powered by steam, would become a machine. Since animal power is one of humanity's earliest inventions, this definition would imply that production by machinery preceded production by handicrafts—an absurdity. In 1735, when John Wyatt announced his spinning machine and initiated the industrial revolution, he nowhere mentioned that a donkey would provide the motive power instead of a man, yet this is what actually happened. His programme called it a machine \"to spin without fingers.\" The significance of this methodological critique cannot be overstated. Marx is insisting that the distinction between tool and machine is not physical or mechanical but historical and social . The machine emerges at a particular moment in the development of the productive forces and the social relations of production. The industrial revolution of the eighteenth century began with the tool or working machine—the part of the machinery that seizes on the object of labour—not with the motor mechanism. To this day, whenever a handicraft or manufacture is converted into an industry carried on by machinery, the working machine serves as the starting-point. Marx then presents his analytical decomposition of all fully developed machinery into three essentially different parts : the motor mechanism , which acts as the driving force of the mechanism as a whole (whether generating its own motive power like the steam-engine or receiving its impulse from an existing natural force like a water-wheel); the transmitting mechanism , composed of fly-wheels, shafting, toothed wheels, pulleys, straps, ropes, bands, pinions, and gearing of the most varied kinds, which regulates motion, changes its form where necessary, and divides and distributes it among the working machines; and finally, the tool or working machine , which seizes on the object of labour and modifies it as desired. These first two parts exist solely to impart motion to the working machine. On closer examination of the working machine proper, we rediscover, though often in highly modified forms, the very apparatus and tools used by the handicraftsman or manufacturing worker. The difference is that instead of being the tools of a man, they are the implements of a mechanism—mechanical implements. Either the entire machine is a more or less altered mechanical edition of the old handicraft tool (as with the power-loom), or the working parts fitted in the frame of the machine are old acquaintances (spindles in a mule, needles in a stocking-loom, saw-blades in a sawing-machine, knives in a chopping-machine). A crucial observation follows: the distinction between these tools and the actual framework of the working machine exists from their moment of entry into the world, because they continue for the most part to be produced by handicraft or by manufacture, and are afterwards fitted into the framework of the machine, which is produced by machinery. It was only since about 1850 that a constantly increasing portion of machine tools were made in England by machinery. The machine, therefore, is \"a mechanism that, after being set in motion, performs with its tools the same operations as the worker formerly did with similar tools.\" Marx emphasizes that whether the motive power is derived from man or from a machine makes no difference here . From the moment that the tool proper is taken from man and fitted into a mechanism, a machine takes the place of a mere implement. The difference strikes one at once even where man himself continues to be the prime mover. The number of implements that a worker can use simultaneously is limited by the number of his own natural instruments of production—his bodily organs. In Germany they tried at first to make one spinner work two spinning-wheels with both hands and both feet, but this proved too exhausting. A treadle spinning-wheel with two spindles was later invented, but adepts who could spin two threads at once were \"almost as scarce as two-headed men.\" The Jenny, on the other hand, even at its very beginning, spun with twelve to eighteen spindles, and the stocking-loom knits with many thousand needles at once. The number of tools that a machine can bring into play simultaneously is from the outset independent of the organic limitations that confine the tools of the handicraftsman. In many manual implements, the distinction between man as mere motive power and man as worker or operator is very striking. With the spinning-wheel, the foot is merely the prime mover while the hand, working with the spindle, performs the real operation of spinning. It is the spindle that is first seized on by the industrial revolution, leaving the worker the merely mechanical role of acting as the motive power. Conversely, where man has always acted as a simple motive power—turning a crank, pumping, moving a bellows, pounding with a mortar—there is soon a call for the application of animals, water, and wind as motive powers. These implements may attain the stature of machines long before the period of manufacture, but without creating any revolution in the mode of production. Marx illustrates this with striking examples: the pumps used to empty the Lake of Harlem in 1836-7 were constructed on the principle of ordinary pumps but driven by \"Cyclopean steam-engines\" instead of men; the blacksmith's bellows was occasionally converted into a blowing-engine by connecting its arm with a steam-engine. The steam-engine itself, as it existed from its invention at the close of the seventeenth century until 1780, \"did not give rise to any industrial revolution.\" It was, on the contrary, the invention of machines that made a revolution in the form of steam-engines necessary. As soon as man, instead of working on the object of labour with a tool, becomes merely the motive power of a machine, it is purely accidental that the motive power happens to be clothed in the form of human muscles; wind, water, or steam could just as well take man's place. The machine that is the starting-point of the industrial revolution replaces the worker who handles a single tool by a mechanism operating with a number of similar tools and set in motion by a single motive power, whatever the form of that power. Here we have the machine, but in its first role as a simple element in production by machinery. An increase in the size of the machine and the number of its working tools calls for a more massive mechanism to drive it, and this mechanism, to overcome its own inertia, requires a mightier moving power than that of man—quite apart from the fact that man is a very imperfect instrument for producing uniform and continuous motion. Of all the great motive forces handed down from the period of manufacture, horse-power is the worst, partly because a horse has a head of his own, partly because he is costly, and partly because the extent to which he can be used in factories is very limited. Marx quotes John C. Morton's 1861 paper to the Society of Arts on \"The Forces Employed in Agriculture,\" which reduced steam-power, horse-power, and man-power to a common unit—the force required to raise 33,000 lb. one foot in one minute—and calculated that one horse-power from a steam-engine cost 3d. per hour, while a horse cost 5½d. and could only work eight hours a day. To do the work of a steam-engine would require sixty-six men at a total cost of 15s. an hour, and to do the work of a horse, thirty-two men at a total cost of 8s. an hour. The wind was too inconstant and uncontrollable, and in England, the birthplace of large-scale industry, water-power preponderated even during the period of manufacture. The increased size of the transmitting mechanism came into conflict with water-power, which was insufficient, and this was one of the factors that gave the impulse for a more accurate investigation of the laws of friction. Similarly, the irregularity caused by the motive power in mills set in motion by pushing and pulling a lever led to the theory and application of the fly-wheel, which later played such an important part in large-scale industry. In this way, the first scientific and technical elements of large-scale industry were developed during the period of manufacturing. Arkwright's throstle-spinning mill was from the very first turned by water, but water-power brought various added difficulties: the flow of water could not be increased at will, it failed at certain seasons, and above all it was essentially local. Not until the invention of Watt's second and so-called double-acting steam-engine was a prime mover found which drew its own motive power from the consumption of coal and water, was entirely under man's control, was mobile and a means of locomotion, was urban and not—like the water-wheel—rural, permitted production to be concentrated in towns instead of being scattered over the countryside, and was of universal technical application. The greatness of Watt's genius showed itself in the specification of the patent he took out in April 1784, in which his steam-engine is described not as an invention for a specific purpose but as \"an agent universally applicable in industry.\" Many of the applications he points out, such as the steam-hammer, were not introduced until half a century later. As soon as tools had been converted from being manual implements of man into the parts of a mechanical apparatus, the motive mechanism also acquired an independent form, entirely emancipated from the restraints of human strength. Thereupon the individual machine sinks to a mere element in production by machinery. One motive mechanism was now able to drive many machines at once. The motive mechanism grows with the number of machines turned simultaneously, and the transmitting mechanism becomes an extensive apparatus. We now come to the crucial distinction between the co-operation of a number of machines of one kind and a complex system of machinery . In the first case, the product is entirely made by a single machine that performs all the various operations previously done by one handicraftsman with his tool, or by several handicraftsmen successively. In the modern manufacture of envelopes, for example, one man folded the paper, another laid on the gum, a third turned over the flap, a fourth embossed the emblem, and on each occasion the envelope had to change hands. One single envelope machine now performs all these operations at once and makes more than 3,000 envelopes in an hour. At the London exhibition of 1862, an American machine for making paper cornets cut, pasted, folded, and finished 300 in a minute. Here the whole process, which under the manufacturing system was split up into a series of operations, is completed by a single machine operating a combination of different tools. Whether such a machine is merely a reproduction of a complicated manual implement or a combination of various simple implements specialized by manufacture, in both cases we meet again with simple co-operation in the factory —the assembling in one place of similar and simultaneously acting machines. A weaving factory consists of a number of power-looms working side by side; a sewing factory consists of a number of sewing-machines all in the same building. But there is here a technical unity in that all the machines receive their impulse simultaneously, and in an equal degree, from the pulsations of the common prime mover, which are imparted to them by the transmitting mechanism. Just as a number of tools form the organs of a machine, so a number of machines of one kind constitute the organs of the motive mechanism. A real machine system , however, does not take the place of these independent machines until the object of labour goes through a connected series of graduated processes carried out by a chain of mutually complementary machines of various kinds. Here we have again the co-operation by division of labour peculiar to manufacture, but now it appears as a combination of machines with specific functions. The tools peculiar to the various specialized workers—beaters, combers, shearers, spinners—are now transformed into the tools of specialized machines, each machine forming a special organ with a special function in the combined mechanism. In those branches in which the machine system is first introduced, manufacture itself provides a natural basis for the division and organization of the process of production. Nevertheless, an essential difference at once appears. In manufacture, it is the workers who, either singly or in groups, must carry on each particular process with their manual implements. The worker has been appropriated by the process; but the process had previously to be adapted to the worker. This subjective principle of the division of labour no longer exists in production by machinery. Here the total process is examined objectively, viewed in and for itself, and analysed into its constitutive phases. The problem of how to execute each particular process and to bind the different partial processes together into a whole is solved by the aid of machines, chemistry, etc. But in this case too, the theoretical conception must be perfected by accumulated experience on a large scale. Each particular machine supplies raw material to the machine next in line; and since they are all working at the same time, the product is always going through the various stages of its formation and is constantly in a state of transition from one phase to another. Just as in manufacture the direct co-operation of specialized workers establishes a numerical proportion between the different groups, so in an organized system of machinery, where one machine is constantly kept employed by another, a fixed relation is established between their number, their size, and their speed. The collective working machine, which is now an articulated system composed of various kinds of single machines and groups of single machines, becomes all the more perfect the more the process as a whole becomes a continuous one —the less the raw material is interrupted in its passage from the first phase to the last, the more its passage from one phase to another is effected not by the hand of man but by the machinery itself. In manufacture, the isolation of each special process is a condition imposed by the division of labour itself; in the fully developed factory, the continuity of the special processes is the regulating principle. A system of machinery, whether based simply on the co-operation of similar machines (as in weaving) or on a combination of different machines (as in spinning), constitutes in itself a vast automaton as soon as it is driven by a self-acting prime mover. But although the whole system may be driven by a steam-engine, some of the individual machines may require the aid of the worker for some of their movements (such aid was necessary for the insertion of the mule carriage before the invention of the self-acting mule). As soon as a machine executes, without man's help, all the movements required to elaborate the raw material, and needs only supplementary assistance from the worker, we have an automatic system of machinery , capable of constant improvement in its details. Marx illustrates the continuity of production and the implementation of the automatic principle with the example of the modern paper-mill. In the paper industry, we may study in detail not only the distinctions between modes of production based on different means of production, but also the connection between the social relations of production and those modes of production. The old German paper-making trade provides an example of handicraft production; Holland in the seventeenth century and France in the eighteenth century provide examples of manufacture proper; and modern England provides the example of automatic fabrication. Besides these, there still exist in India and China two distinct ancient Asiatic forms of the same industry. An organized system of machines to which motion is communicated by the transmitting mechanism from an automatic centre is the most developed form of production by machinery. Here we have, in place of the isolated machine, a \"mechanical monster whose body fills whole factories, and whose demonic power, at first hidden by the slow and measured motions of its gigantic members, finally bursts forth in the fast and feverish whirl of its countless working organs.\" Marx then addresses the historical development of machine production. There were mules and steam-engines before there were any workers exclusively occupied in making them, just as men wore clothes before there were tailors. However, the inventions of Vaucanson, Arkwright, Watt, and others could be put into practice only because each inventor found a considerable number of skilled mechanical workers available, placed at their disposal by the period of manufacture. Some of these workers were independent handicraftsmen of various trades, others were grouped together in manufactures where a division of labour of particular strictness prevailed. As inventions increased and the demand for newly discovered machines grew larger, the machine-making industry increasingly split up into numerous independent branches, and the division of labour within these manufactures developed accordingly. Here, therefore, in manufacture, we see the immediate technical foundation of large-scale industry. Manufacture produced the machinery with which large-scale industry abolished the handicraft and manufacturing systems in the spheres of production it first seized hold of. The system of machine production therefore grew spontaneously on a material basis which was inadequate to it. When the system had attained a certain degree of development, it had to overthrow this ready-made foundation, which had meanwhile undergone further development in its old form, and create for itself a new basis appropriate to its own mode of production. Just as the individual machine retains a dwarf-like character as long as it is worked by the power of man alone, so too large-scale industry was crippled in its whole development as long as its characteristic instrument of production, the machine, owed its existence to personal strength and personal skill, and depended on the muscular development, keenness of sight, and manual dexterity with which specialized workers wielded their dwarf-like implements. Thus, apart from the high cost of machines made in this way—a circumstance that forms the dominant motive for the capitalist's actions—the expansion of industries carried on by machinery and the invasion of fresh branches of production by machinery were dependent on the growth of a class of workers who, owing to the semi-artistic nature of their employment, could increase their numbers only gradually. But at a certain stage of its development, large-scale industry also came into conflict with the technical basis provided for it by handicrafts and manufacture. A number of technical problems arose naturally from the very course of development: the size of the prime movers, of the transmitting mechanism, and of the machines properly so called increased; the components of the machines became more complicated and diverse in form; they had to operate with stricter regularity; and they accordingly diverged more and more from the model that originally determined their construction under the handicraft system, acquiring an independent form restricted only by their mechanical task. At the same time, the automatic system was perfected, and it became more and more unavoidable to use materials difficult to work with, such as iron in place of wood. In every case the solution of these problems met with a stumbling-block in the personal restrictions which even the collective worker of manufacture could not break through except to a limited extent. The transformation of the mode of production in one sphere of industry necessitates a similar transformation in other spheres. This happens at first in branches of industry connected together by being separate phases of a process, yet isolated by the social division of labour, in such a way that each produces an independent commodity. Thus machine spinning made machine weaving necessary, and both together made a mechanical and chemical revolution compulsory in bleaching, printing, and dyeing. The revolution in cotton-spinning called forth the invention of the gin for separating the seeds from the cotton fibre; it was only by means of this invention that the production of cotton became possible on the enormous scale required. But the revolution in the modes of production of industry and agriculture also made necessary a revolution in the general conditions of the social process of production—in the means of communication and transport. In a society whose pivot was small-scale agriculture with its subsidiary domestic industries and urban handicrafts, the means of communication and transport were utterly inadequate to the needs of production in the period of manufacture. Hence, quite apart from the immense transformation in shipbuilding, the means of communication and transport gradually adapted themselves to the mode of production of large-scale industry by means of a system of river steamers, railways, ocean steamers, and telegraphs. But the huge masses of iron that had now to be forged, welded, cut, bored, and shaped required for their part machines of Cyclopean dimensions, which the machine-building trades of the period of manufacture were incapable of constructing. Large-scale industry therefore had to take over the machine itself, its own characteristic instrument of production, and to produce machines by means of machines. It was not till it did this that it could create for itself an adequate technical foundation and stand on its own feet. The most essential condition for the production of machines by machines was a prime mover capable of exerting any amount of force while retaining perfect control—the steam-engine already fulfilled this condition. But at the same time it was necessary to produce the geometrically accurate straight lines, planes, circles, cylinders, cones, and spheres for the individual parts of the machines. Henry Maudslay solved this problem in the first decade of the nineteenth century by the invention of the slide-rest , a tool that was soon made automatic and was applied in a modified form to other machines for constructing machinery besides the lathe for which it was originally intended. This mechanical appliance replaces not some particular tool but the hand itself, which produces a given form by holding and guiding the cutting tool along the iron or other material of labour. Thus it became possible to produce the geometrical shapes of the individual parts of machinery \"with a degree of ease, accuracy, and speed, that no accumulated experience of the hand of the most skilled workman could give.\" Marx quotes The Industry of Nations (1855) on the significance of the slide-rest: \"Simple and outwardly unimportant as this appendage to lathes may appear, it is not, we believe, averring too much to state that its influence in improving and extending the use of machinery has been as great as that produced by Watt's improvements of the steam-engine itself. Its introduction went at once to perfect all machinery, to cheapen it, and to stimulate invention and improvement.\" If we look at the part of the machinery employed in the construction of machines, we find that the manual implements reappear, but on a Cyclopean scale. The operating part of the boring machine is an immense drill driven by a steam-engine; without this machine, the cylinders of large steam-engines and hydraulic presses could not be made. The mechanical lathe is only a Cyclopean reproduction of the ordinary foot-lathe; the planing machine is \"an iron carpenter\" that works on iron with the same tools as the human carpenter employs on wood; the instrument that cuts veneers on the London wharves is a gigantic razor; the tool of the shearing machine is a monster pair of scissors; and the steam-hammer works with an ordinary hammer head, but of such a weight that even Thor himself could not wield it. These steam-hammers are an invention of Nasmyth, and there is one that weighs over 6 tons and strikes with a vertical fall of 7 feet on an anvil weighing 36 tons. It is mere child's play for it to crush a block of granite into powder, yet it is no less capable of driving a nail into a piece of soft wood with a succession of light taps. Marx concludes Section 1 with a crucial theoretical summary: As machinery, the instrument of labour assumes a material mode of existence which necessitates the replacement of human force by natural forces, and the replacement of the rule of thumb by the conscious application of natural science. In manufacture, the organization of the social labour process is purely subjective: it is a combination of specialized workers. Large-scale industry, on the other hand, possesses in the machine system an entirety objective organization of production, which confronts the worker as a pre-existing material condition of production. In simple co-operation and even in the more specialized form based on the division of labour, the extrusion of the isolated worker by the associated worker still appears to be more or less accidental. Machinery, with a few exceptions, operates only by means of associated labour, or labour in common. Hence the co-operative character of the labour process is in this case a technical necessity dictated by the very nature of the instrument of labour. The Value Transferred by the Machinery to the Product Section 2 addresses a question of fundamental importance for Marx's theory of value: how does machinery, as a component of constant capital, transfer its value to the product? Marx begins by recalling that the productive forces resulting from co-operation and the division of labour cost capital nothing—they are natural forces of social labour. Other natural forces appropriated to productive processes, such as steam and water, also cost nothing. But just as a man requires lungs to breathe with, so he requires something that is the work of human hands in order to consume the forces of nature productively. A water-wheel is necessary to exploit the force of water, and a steam-engine to exploit the elasticity of steam. Once discovered, the law of the deflection of a magnetic needle in the field of an electric current, or the law of the magnetization of iron by electricity, cost absolutely nothing. But the exploitation of these laws for the purposes of telegraphy necessitates costly and extensive apparatus. Marx notes that science generally costs the capitalist nothing, a fact that by no means prevents him from exploiting it. \"Alien\" science is incorporated by capital just as \"alien\" labour is. But \"capitalist\" appropriation and \"personal\" appropriation, whether of science or of material wealth, are totally different things. Dr. Ure himself deplores the gross ignorance of mechanical science among his beloved machinery-exploiting manufacturers, and Liebig can tell us about the astounding ignorance of chemistry displayed by English chemical manufacturers. As we have seen, the machine does not drive out the tool. Rather does the tool expand and multiply, changing from a dwarf implement of the human organism to the implement of a mechanism created by man. Capital now sets the worker to work, not with a manual tool, but with a machine which itself handles the tools. Therefore, although it is clear at first glance that large-scale industry raises the productivity of labour to an extraordinary degree by incorporating into the production process both the immense forces of nature and the results arrived at by natural science, it is by no means equally clear that this increase in productive force is not, on the other hand, purchased with an increase in the amount of labour expended. Machinery, like every other component of constant capital, creates no new value but yields up its own value to the product it serves to beget. In so far as the machine has value and, as a result, transfers value to the product, it forms an element in the value of the latter. Instead of being cheapened, the product is made dearer in proportion to the value of the machine. And it is crystal clear that machines and systems of machinery, large-scale industry's characteristic instruments of labour, are incomparably more loaded with value than the implements used in handicrafts and in manufacture. In the first place, Marx observes, machinery, while always entering as a whole into the labour process, enters only piece by piece into the process of valorization . It never adds more value than it loses, on an average, by depreciation. Hence there is a great difference between the value of a machine and the value transferred in a given time by the machine to the product. Equally, there is a great difference between the machine as a factor in the formation of value and as a factor in the formation of the product. The longer the period during which the machine serves in the same labour process, the greater are those differences. It is no doubt true that every instrument of labour enters as a whole into the labour process, while only piecemeal, in proportion to its average daily depreciation, into the process of valorization. But this difference between the utilization of the instrument and its depreciation is much greater in the case of machinery than with a tool, because the machine, being made from more durable material, has a longer life; because it can be employed more economically, from the point of view both of the deterioration of its own components and of its consumption of materials, as its use is regulated by strict scientific laws; and finally, because its field of production is incomparably larger than that of a tool. Both in the case of the machine and of the tool, we find that after allowing for their average daily cost—the value they transmit to the product by their average daily wear and tear, and their consumption of auxiliary substances such as oil and coal—they do their work for nothing, like the natural forces already available without the intervention of human labour. The greater the productive effectiveness of the machinery compared with that of the tool, the greater is the extent of its gratuitous service. Only in large-scale industry has man succeeded in making the product of his past labour, labour which has already been objectified, perform gratuitous service on a large scale, like a force of nature. When we considered co-operation and manufacture, we found that certain general conditions of production such as buildings could be consumed more economically than the scattered conditions of production of isolated craftsmen, because they could be consumed in common, and that they therefore made the product cheaper. In a system of machinery, not only is the framework of the machine consumed in common by its numerous working parts, but the prime mover, together with a part of the transmitting mechanism, is consumed in common by a large number of operating machines. Marx quotes Ricardo on this effect of machinery: \"Adam Smith nowhere undervalues the services which these natural agents and machinery perform for us, but he very justly distinguishes the nature of the value which they add to commodities [by adding to value in use]... As they perform their work gratuitously... the assistance which they afford us adds nothing to value in exchange.\" Marx notes that this observation by Ricardo is correct in so far as it is directed against J.B. Say, who \"drivels on about the 'service' performed by machines when they create value which forms a part of 'profits'.\" Given the difference between the value of the machinery and the value transferred by it in a day to the product, the extent to which this latter value makes the product dearer depends in the first instance upon the size of the product, on its area, so to speak. Mr. Baynes of Blackburn, in a lecture of 1857, estimates that \"each real mechanical horse-power will drive 450 self-acting mule spindles, with preparation, or 220 throstle spindles, or 15 looms for 40-inch cloth with the appliances for warping, sizing, etc.\" In the first case, it is the daily product of 450 mule spindles, in the second of 200 throstle spindles, and in the third of 15 power-looms, over which is spread the daily cost of one horse-power and the deterioration of the machinery set in motion by that power. Hence this deterioration transfers only a minute amount of value to a pound of yarn or a yard of cloth. Similarly with the steam-hammer mentioned earlier. Since its daily deterioration and its consumption of coal are spread over the immense masses of iron hammered by it in a day, only a small value is added to a hundredweight of iron; but that value would be very great if the Cyclopean instrument were used to drive in small nails. Given a machine's capacity for work—the number of its operating tools or, where it is a question of force, their size—the amount of its product will depend on the velocity of its working parts, on the speed of the spindles, or on the number of blows given by the hammer in a minute. Many of these colossal hammers strike seventy times in a minute, and Ryder's patent machine for forging spindles with small hammers gives as many as 700 strokes per minute. Given the rate at which machinery transfers its value to the product, the amount of value so transferred depends on the total value of the machinery. The less labour it contains, the less value it contributes to the product. The less value it gives up, the more productive it is, and the more its services approach those rendered by natural forces. But the production of machinery lessens its value in relation to its extension and efficacy. A comparative analysis of the prices of commodities produced by handicrafts or manufacture, and of the prices of the same commodities produced by machinery, shows in general that in the product of machinery the value arising out of the instrument of labour increases relatively, but decreases absolutely . In other words, its absolute amount decreases, but its amount in relation to the total value of the product—of a pound of yarn, for instance—increases. Marx adds a note addressing the reader \"imbued with capitalist notions\" who will naturally miss here the \"interest\" added by the machine to the product in proportion to its capital value. It is easily seen that since a machine no more creates new value than any other part of constant capital, it cannot add any value under the heading of \"interest.\" This will be explained in the third volume of Capital . Marx then introduces a crucial quantitative analysis of the productivity of machinery. It is evident that whenever it costs as much labour to produce a machine as is saved by the employment of that machine, all that has taken place is a displacement of labour. Consequently, the total labour required to produce a commodity has not been lessened; in other words, the productivity of labour has not been increased. However, the difference between the labour a machine costs and the labour it saves—the degree of productivity the machine possesses—does not depend on the difference between its own value and the value of the tool it replaces. As long as the labour spent on a machine is such that the portion of its value added to the product remains smaller than the value added by the worker to the product with his tool, there is always a difference of labour saved in favour of the machine. The productivity of the machine is therefore measured by the human labour-power it replaces. According to Mr. Baynes, 2½ workers are required for the 450 mule spindles, including preparation machinery, driven by one horse-power; each self-acting mule spindle, working 10 hours, produces 13 ounces of yarn (average thickness); consequently 2½ workers spin 365⅝ lb. of yarn per week. Hence, if we disregard waste, 366 lb. of cotton absorb only 150 hours of labour during their conversion into yarn, or 15 working days of 10 hours each. But with a spinning-wheel, assuming that the hand-spinner produces 13 ounces of yarn in 60 hours, the same weight of cotton would absorb 2,700 working days of 10 hours each, or 27,000 hours of labour. Where block-printing, the old method of printing calico by hand, has been driven out by machine-printing, a single machine, with the aid of one man or boy, prints as much calico of four colours in one hour as it formerly took 200 men to do. Before Eli Whitney invented the cotton gin in 1793, the separation of the seed from a pound of cotton cost an average day's labour. By means of his invention it became possible for one black woman to clean 100 lb. a day. A pound of raw cotton which previously cost 50 cents to produce could subsequently be sold for 10 cents at a greater profit. In India they use an instrument called a churka, half machine and half tool, for separating the seeds from the cotton wool; with this one man and one woman can clean 28 lb. a day. With the churka invented by Dr. Forbes, one man and a boy produce 250 lb. a day. If oxen, steam, or water are used for driving it, only a few boys and girls are required as feeders. Sixteen of these machines driven by oxen do as much work in a day as 750 people did before. Marx returns to the steam-plough example to clear up an erroneous notion. A steam-plough does as much work in one hour at a cost of 3d. as 66 men at a cost of 15s. The 15s. are by no means the expression in money of all the labour expended in one hour by the 66 men. If the ratio of surplus labour to necessary labour were 100 per cent, these 66 men would produce in one hour a value of 30s., although their wages, 15s., represented only their labour for half an hour. Let us suppose, then, that a machine costs as much as the wages for a year of the 150 men it displaces, say £3,000; this £3,000 is by no means the expression in money of the labour provided by these men and added to the object of labour before the introduction of the machine, but only the expression of that portion of their year's labour which was expended for themselves and is represented by their wages. On the other hand, the £3,000, the monetary value of the machine, expresses all the labour expended to produce it, whatever the proportion between the worker's wages and the capitalist's surplus-value. Therefore, even if the machine costs as much as the labour-power displaced by it, the labour objectified in it is still much smaller in quantity than the living labour it replaces. Marx quotes Ricardo: \"These mute agents (the machines) are always the produce of much less labour than that which they displace, even when they are of the same money-value.\" The use of machinery for the exclusive purpose of cheapening the product is limited by the requirement that less labour must be expended in producing the machinery than is displaced by the employment of that machinery. For the capitalist, however, there is a further limit on its use. Instead of paying for the labour, he pays only the value of the labour-power employed; the limit to his using a machine is therefore fixed by the difference between the value of the machine and the value of the labour-power replaced by it. Since the division of the day's work into necessary labour and surplus labour differs in different countries, and even in the same country at different periods or in different branches of industry; and further, since the actual wage of the worker sometimes sinks below the value of his labour-power and sometimes rises above it, it is possible for the difference between the price of the machinery and the price of the labour-power replaced by that machinery to undergo great variations, while the difference between the quantity of labour needed to produce the machine and the total quantity of labour replaced by it remains constant. But it is only the former difference that determines the cost to the capitalist of producing a commodity and influences his actions through the pressure of competition. Hence the invention nowadays in England of machines that are employed only in North America; just as in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries machines were invented in Germany for use exclusively in Holland, and just as many French inventions of the eighteenth century were exploited only in England. In the older countries, machinery itself, when employed in some branches of industry, creates such a superfluity of labour (\"redundancy of labour\" is how Ricardo puts it) in other branches that the fall of wages below the value of labour-power impedes the use of machinery in those other branches and, from the standpoint of the capitalist, makes the use of machinery superfluous and often impossible, because his profit comes from a reduction in the labour paid for, not in the labour employed. In some branches of the wool industry in England, the employment of children has been considerably lessened during recent years, and in some cases entirely abolished. Why? Because the Factory Acts made two sets of children necessary, one set working six hours, the other four, or both sets working five hours. But the parents refused to sell the \"half-timers\" cheaper than the \"full-timers.\" Hence the substitution of machinery for the \"half-timers.\" Before the labour of women and children under 10 years old was forbidden in mines, the capitalists considered the employment of naked women and girls, often in company with men, so far sanctioned by their moral code, and especially by their ledgers, that it was only after the passing of the Act that they had recourse to machinery. The Yankees have invented a stone-breaking machine. The English do not make use of it because the \"wretch\" who does this work gets paid for such a small portion of his labour that machinery would increase the cost of production to the capitalist. In England women are still occasionally used instead of horses for hauling barges, because the labour required to produce horses and machines is an accurately known quantity, while that required to maintain the women of the surplus population is beneath all calculation. Hence we nowhere find a more shameless squandering of human labour-power for despicable purposes than in England, the land of machinery. The Most Immediate Effects of Machine Production on the Worker Section 3 begins Marx's analysis of the revolution in the worker's condition brought about by machine production. Before inquiring how human material is incorporated with the objective organism of the factory, Marx considers some general effects of the revolution on the worker himself. (a) Appropriation of Supplementary Labour-Power by Capital: The Employment of Women and Children In so far as machinery dispenses with muscular power, it becomes a means for employing workers of slight muscular strength, or whose bodily development is incomplete, but whose limbs are all the more supple. The labour of women and children was therefore the first result of the capitalist application of machinery. That mighty substitute for labour and for workers, the machine, was immediately transformed into a means for increasing the number of wage-labourers by enrolling, under the direct sway of capital, every member of the worker's family, without distinction of age or sex. Compulsory work for the capitalist usurped the place, not only of the children's play, but also of independent labour at home, within customary limits, for the family itself. The value of labour-power was determined not only by the labour-time necessary to maintain the individual adult worker, but also by that necessary to maintain his family. Machinery, by throwing every member of that family onto the labour-market, spreads the value of the man's labour-power over his whole family. It thus depreciates it. To purchase the labour-power of a family of four workers may perhaps cost more than it formerly did to purchase the labour-power of the head of the family, but, in return, four days' labour takes the place of one day's, and the price falls in proportion to the excess of the surplus labour of four over the surplus labour of one. In order that the family may live, four people must now provide not only labour for the capitalist but also surplus labour. Thus we see that machinery, while augmenting the human material that forms capital's most characteristic field of exploitation, at the same time raises the degree of that exploitation. Marx notes Thomas de Quincey's observation that \"three girls of 13, at wages of from 6 shillings to 8 shillings a week, have replaced the one man of mature age, at wages varying from 18 shillings to 45 shillings.\" Since certain family functions, such as nursing and suckling children, cannot be entirely suppressed, the mothers who have been confiscated by capital must try substitutes of some sort. Domestic work, such as sewing and mending, must be replaced by the purchase of ready-made articles. Hence the diminished expenditure of labour in the house is accompanied by an increased expenditure of money outside. The cost of production of the working-class family therefore increases, and balances its greater income. In addition, economy and judgement in the consumption and preparation of the means of subsistence become impossible. Machinery also revolutionizes, and quite fundamentally, the agency through which the capital-relation is formally mediated—the contract between the worker and the capitalist. Taking the exchange of commodities as our basis, our first assumption was that the capitalist and the worker confronted each other as free persons, as independent owners of commodities, the one possessing money and the means of production, the other labour-power. But now the capitalist buys children and young persons. Previously the worker sold his own labour-power, which he disposed of as a free agent, formally speaking. Now he sells wife and child. He has become a slave-dealer. Notices of demand for children's labour often resemble in form the inquiries for Negro slaves that were formerly to be read among the advertisements in American journals. An English factory inspector quotes an advertisement: \"Wanted, 12 to 20 young persons, not younger than what can pass for 13 years. Wages, 4 shillings a week. Apply, etc.\" The phrase \"what can pass for 13 years\" refers to the fact that, according to the Factory Act, children under 13 years old may only work 6 hours a day. An officially appointed doctor (the \"certifying surgeon\") must certify their age. The manufacturer, therefore, asks for children who look as if they are already 13 years old. The decrease in the number of children under 13 years employed in factories was, for the most part, according to the evidence of the factory inspectors themselves, the work of the certifying surgeons, who adjusted the children's ages in a manner appropriate to the capitalist's greed for exploitation and the parents' need to engage in this traffic. In the notorious London district of Bethnal Green a public market is held every Monday and Tuesday morning, at which children of both sexes, from 9 years of age upwards, hire themselves out to the silk manufacturers. \"The usual terms are 1s. 8d. a week (this belongs to the parents) and '2d. for myself and tea'. The contract is binding only for the week. The scene and language while this market is going on are quite disgraceful.\" It still happens in England that women \"take children from the workhouse and let any one have them out for 2s. 6d. a week.\" In spite of legislation, the number of boys sold in Great Britain by their parents to act as live chimney-sweeping machines (although machines exist to replace them) is at least 2,000. The revolution effected by machinery in the legal relationship between buyer and seller of labour-power, causing the transaction as a whole to lose the appearance of a contract between free persons, later offered the English Parliament an excuse, founded on juristic principles, for state intervention into factory affairs. Whenever the law limits the labour of children to 6 hours in industries not previously touched, the complaints of the manufacturers resound yet again. They allege that numbers of parents withdraw their children from the industries brought under the Act in order to sell them where \"freedom of labour\" still prevails, i.e. where children under 13 years are compelled to work like adults, and for that reason can be sold at a higher price. But since capital is by its nature a leveller, since it insists upon equality in the conditions of exploitation of labour in every sphere of production as its own innate right, the limitation by law of children's labour in one branch of industry results in its limitation in others. Marx dwells on the enormous mortality of the children of the workers during the first few years of their life. In 16 of the registration districts into which England is divided, there are, for every 100,000 children alive under the age of one year, only 9,085 deaths in a year on an average (in one district only 7,047); in 24 districts the deaths are over 10,000 but under 11,000; in 39 districts over 11,000 but under 12,000; in 48 districts over 12,000 but under 13,000; in 22 districts over 20,000; in 25 districts over 21,000; in 17 over 22,000; in 11 over 23,000; in Hoo, Wolverhampton, Ashton-under-Lyne and Preston, over 24,000; in Nottingham, Stockport and Bradford, over 25,000; in Wisbeach, 26,000; and in Manchester, 26,125. An official medical inquiry in 1861 showed that the high death-rates are, apart from local causes, principally due to the employment of the mothers away from their homes, and to the neglect and maltreatment arising from their absence, which consists in such things as insufficient nourishment, unsuitable food, and dosing with opiates; besides this, there arises an unnatural estrangement between mother and child, and as a consequence intentional starving and poisoning of the children. In those agricultural districts \"where a minimum in the employment of women exists, the death-rate is on the other hand very low.\" However, the 1861 Commission of Inquiry arrived at the unexpected conclusion that in some purely agricultural districts bordering on the North Sea, the death-rate of children under one year old almost equalled that of the worst factory districts. Dr. Julian Hunter was commissioned to investigate this phenomenon. His report, incorporated into the Sixth Report on Public Health, showed that \"the same cause which drove away malaria, the conversion of the land from a morass in winter and a scanty pasture in summer into fruitful corn land, created the exceptional death-rate of the infants.\" The revolution in cultivation had led to the introduction of the industrial system. \"Married women, who work in gangs along with boys and girls, are, for a stipulated sum of money, placed at the disposal of the farmer by a man called the 'undertaker', who contracts for the whole gang.\" All the phenomena of the factory districts are reproduced here, including a yet higher degree of disguised infanticide and stupefaction of children with opiates. Dr. Simon, the medical officer of the Privy Council, declared: \"My knowledge of such evils may excuse the profound misgiving with which I regard any large industrial employment of adult women.\" Mr. Baker, the factory inspector, exclaimed: \"Happy indeed will it be for the manufacturing districts of England, when every married woman having a family is prohibited from working in any textile works at all.\" The moral degradation arising out of the exploitation by capitalism of the labour of women and children has been so exhaustively presented by Engels in his Condition of the Working Class in England that a mere mention will suffice here. But the intellectual degeneration artificially produced by transforming immature human beings into mere machines for the production of surplus-value finally compelled even the English Parliament to make elementary education a legal requirement before children under 14 years could be consumed \"productively\" by being employed in industries subject to the Factory Acts. The spirit of capitalist production emerges clearly from the ludicrous way the so-called education clauses of the Factory Acts have been drawn up, from the absence of any administrative machinery whereby this compulsory education is once again made for the most part illusory, from the opposition of the manufacturers themselves to these education clauses, and from the tricks and dodges they use to evade them. Leonard Horner, the factory inspector, described the situation: \"For this the legislature is alone to blame, by having passed a delusive law, which, while it would seem to provide that the children employed in factories shall be educated, contains no enactment by which that professed end can be secured. It provides nothing more than that the children shall on certain days of the week, and for a certain number of hours (three) in each day, be inclosed within the four walls of a place called a school, and that the employer of the child shall receive weekly a certificate to that effect signed by a person designated by the subscriber as a schoolmaster or schoolmistress.\" Before the passing of the amended Factory Act of 1844, the certificates of attendance at school were sometimes signed by the schoolmaster or schoolmistress with a cross, as they themselves were unable to write. One inspector related: \"On one occasion, on visiting a place called a school, from which certificates of school attendance had issued, I was so struck with the ignorance of the master, that I said to him: 'Pray, sir, can you read?' His reply was 'Aye, summat!' and as a justification of his right to grant certificates, he added: 'At any rate, I am before my scholars.'\" Sir John Kincaid, factory inspector for Scotland, related similar experiences. \"The first school we visited was kept by a Mrs. Ann Killin. Upon asking her to spell her name, she straightway made a mistake, by beginning with the letter C, but correcting herself immediately, she said her name began with a K. On looking at her signature, however, in the school certificate books, I noticed that she spelt it in various ways, while her handwriting left no doubt as to her unfitness to teach. She herself also acknowledged that she could not keep the register... In a second school I found the schoolroom 15 feet long, and 10 feet wide, and counted in this space 75 children, who were gabbling something unintelligible.\" Machinery, by this excessive addition of women and children to the working personnel, at last breaks the resistance which the male workers had continued to oppose to the despotism of capital throughout the period of manufacture. Marx quotes Lord Ashley's 1844 speech: \"Thus are the virtues, the peculiar virtues of the female character to be perverted to her injury—thus all that is most dutiful and tender in her nature is made a means of her bondage and suffering.\" (b) The Prolongation of the Working Day If machinery is the most powerful means of raising the productivity of labour, i.e. of shortening the working time needed to produce a commodity, it is also, as a repository of capital, the most powerful means of lengthening the working day beyond all natural limits in those industries first directly seized on by it. It creates, on the one hand, new conditions which permit capital to give free rein to this tendency, and on the other hand, new incentives which whet its insatiable appetite for the labour of others. In the first place, in machinery the motion and the activity of the instrument of labour assert their independence vis-à-vis the worker. The instrument of labour now becomes an industrial form of perpetual motion. It would go on producing for ever, if it did not come up against certain natural limits in the shape of the weak bodies and the strong wills of its human assistants. Because it is capital, the automatic mechanism is endowed, in the person of the capitalist, with consciousness and a will. As capital, therefore, it is animated by the drive to reduce to a minimum the resistance offered by man, that \"obstinate yet elastic natural barrier.\" This resistance is moreover lessened by the apparently undemanding nature of work at a machine, and the more pliant and docile character of the women and children employed by preference. The productivity of machinery is, as we saw, inversely proportional to the value transferred by it to the product. The longer the period during which it functions, the greater is the mass of the products over which the value transmitted by the machine is spread, and the smaller is the portion of that value added to each single commodity. The active lifetime of a machine, however, is clearly dependent on the length of the working day, or the duration of the daily labour process multiplied by the number of days for which the process is carried on. The amount of deterioration suffered by a machine does not by any means exactly correspond to the length of time it has been in use. Even if it were so, a machine working 16 hours a day for 7½ years covers as long a working period as the same machine working only 8 hours a day for 15 years and transmits to the total product no more value. Notwithstanding this, the value of the machine would be reproduced twice as quickly in the first case as in the second, and the capitalist, using the same machine, would absorb in 7½ years as much surplus-value as he would in 15 in the second case. The physical deterioration of the machine is of two kinds. The one arises from use, as coins wear away by circulating; the other from lack of use, as a sword rusts when left in its scabbard. This second kind is its consumption by the elements. Deterioration of the first kind is more or less directly proportional, and that of the second kind to a certain extent inversely proportional, to the use of the machine. But in addition to the material wear and tear, a machine also undergoes what Marx calls moral depreciation . It loses exchange-value, either because machines of the same sort are being produced more cheaply than it was, or because better machines are entering into competition with it. In both cases, however young and full of life the machine may be, its value is no longer determined by the necessary labour-time actually objectified in it, but by the labour-time necessary to reproduce either it or the better machine. It has therefore been devalued to a greater or lesser extent. The shorter the period taken to reproduce its total value, the less is the danger of moral depreciation; and the longer the working day, the shorter that period in fact is. When machinery is first introduced into a particular branch of production, new methods of reproducing it more cheaply follow blow upon blow, and so do improvements which relate not only to individual parts and details of the machine but also to its whole construction. It is therefore in the early days of a machine's life that this special incentive to the prolongation of the working day makes itself felt most acutely. Given the length of the working day, and in otherwise identical circumstances, the exploitation of double the number of workers requires not only a doubling of that part of constant capital invested in machinery and buildings but also a doubling of the part laid out in raw material and auxiliary substances. The lengthening of the working day, on the other hand, permits an expansion of the scale of production without any change in the amount of capital invested in machinery and buildings. Not only does surplus-value increase therefore, but the outlay necessary to obtain it diminishes. It is true that this takes place, more or less, with every lengthening of the working day; but here the change is of far greater importance because the part of the capital that has been converted into the instruments of labour now falls more decisively into the balance. The development of machine production ties a constantly increasing portion of the capital to a form in which, on the one hand, it is constantly capable of valorization, and in which, on the other hand, it loses both use-value and exchange-value whenever it is deprived of contact with living labour. Mr. Ashworth, an English cotton magnate, imparted the following lesson to Professor Nassau W. Senior: \"When a labourer lays down his spade, he renders useless, for that period, a capital worth eighteenpence. When one of our people leaves the mill, he renders useless a capital that has cost £100,000.\" Marx comments with heavy irony: \"Just imagine that! Making 'useless', if only for a single moment, a piece of capital that has cost £100,000! It is in truth monstrous that a single one of our people should ever leave the factory!\" The increased use of machinery makes a constantly increased prolongation of the working day \"desirable.\" Machinery produces relative surplus-value, not only by directly reducing the value of labour-power, and indirectly cheapening it by cheapening the commodities that enter into its reproduction, but also, when it is first introduced sporadically into an industry, by converting the labour employed by the owner of that machinery into labour of a higher degree, by raising the social value of the article produced above its individual value, and thus enabling the capitalist to replace the value of a day's labour-power by a smaller portion of the value of a day's product. During this transitional period, while the use of machinery remains a sort of monopoly, profits are exceptional, and the capitalist endeavours to exploit thoroughly \"the sunny time of this his first love\" by prolonging the working day as far as possible. The magnitude of the profit gives him an insatiable hunger for yet more profit. As machinery comes into general use in a particular branch of production, the social value of the machine's product sinks down to its individual value, and the following law asserts itself: surplus-value does not arise from the labour-power that has been replaced by the machinery, but from the labour-power actually employed in working with the machinery. Surplus-value arises only from the variable part of capital, and the amount of surplus-value depends on two factors: the rate of surplus-value and the number of workers simultaneously employed. Given the length of the working day, the rate of surplus-value is determined by the relative duration of the necessary labour and the surplus labour performed in the course of a working day. The number of workers simultaneously employed depends, for its part, on the ratio of the variable to the constant capital. Now, however much the use of machinery may increase surplus labour at the expense of necessary labour by raising the productive power of labour, it is clear that it attains this result only by diminishing the number of workers employed by a given amount of capital. It converts a portion of capital which was previously variable, i.e. had been turned into living labour, into machinery, i.e. into constant capital which does not produce surplus-value. It is impossible, for instance, to squeeze as much surplus-value out of two as out of twenty-four workers. If each of these twenty-four men gives only 1 hour of surplus labour in 12, the twenty-four men give together 24 hours of surplus labour, while 24 hours is the total labour of the two men. Hence there is an immanent contradiction in the application of machinery to the production of surplus-value, since, of the two factors of the surplus-value created by a given amount of capital, one, the rate of surplus-value, cannot be increased except by diminishing the other, the number of workers. This contradiction comes to light as soon as machinery has come into general use in a given industry, for then the value of the machine-produced commodity regulates the social value of all commodities of the same kind; and it is this contradiction which in turn drives the capitalist, without his being aware of the fact, to the most ruthless and excessive prolongation of the working day, in order that he may secure compensation for the decrease in the relative number of workers exploited by increasing not only relative but also absolute surplus labour. The capitalist application of machinery on the one hand supplies new and powerful incentives for an unbounded prolongation of the working day, and produces such a revolution in the mode of labour as well as the character of the social working organism that it is able to break all resistance to this tendency. But on the other hand, partly by placing at the capitalists' disposal new strata of the working class previously inaccessible to him, partly by setting free the workers it supplants, machinery produces a surplus working population , which is compelled to submit to the dictates of capital. Hence that remarkable phenomenon in the history of modern industry, that machinery sweeps away every moral and natural restriction on the length of the working day . Hence too the economic paradox that the most powerful instrument for reducing labour-time suffers a dialectical inversion and becomes the most unfailing means for turning the whole lifetime of the worker and his family into labour-time at capital's disposal for its own valorization. Marx quotes Aristotle's dream: \"If every tool, when summoned, or even by intelligent anticipation, could do the work that befits it, just as the creations of Daedalus moved of themselves, or the tripods of Hephaestus went of their own accord to their sacred work, if the weavers' shuttles were to weave of themselves, then there would be no need either of apprentices for the master craftsmen, or of slaves for the lords.\" And Antipater, a Greek poet of the time of Cicero, hailed the water-wheel for grinding corn as the liberator of female slaves and the restorer of the golden age. Marx comments with sardonic wit: \"Oh those heathens! They understood nothing of political economy and Christianity, as the learned Bastiat discovered, and before him the still wiser Macculloch. They did not, for example, comprehend that machinery is the surest means of lengthening the working day.\" (c) Intensification of Labour The immoderate lengthening of the working day produced by machinery in the hands of capital leads later on to a reaction on the part of society, which is threatened in the very sources of its life; and from there to a normal working day whose length is fixed by law. On the foundation laid by the latter, something we have already met with—the intensification of labour —develops into a phenomenon of decisive importance. Marx's analysis of absolute surplus-value dealt primarily with the extensive magnitude of labour, its duration, while its intensity was treated as a given factor. We have now to consider the inversion of extensive magnitude into intensive magnitude, or magnitude of degree . It is self-evident that in proportion as the use of machinery spreads, and the experience of a special class of worker—the machine-worker—accumulates, the rapidity and thereby the intensity of labour undergoes a natural increase. Thus in England, in the course of half a century, the lengthening of the working day has gone hand in hand with an increase in the intensity of factory labour. Nevertheless, Marx emphasizes, we are dealing here not with temporary paroxysms of labour but with labour repeated day after day with unvarying uniformity. Hence a point must inevitably be reached where extension of the working day and intensification of labour become mutually exclusive, so that the lengthening of the working day becomes compatible only with a lower degree of intensity, and inversely, a higher degree of intensity only with a shortening of the working day. As soon as the gradual upsurge of working-class revolt had compelled Parliament compulsorily to shorten the hours of labour, and to begin by imposing a normal working day on factories properly so called, from the moment that it was made impossible once and for all to increase the production of surplus-value by prolonging the working day, capital threw itself with all its might, and in full awareness of the situation, into the production of relative surplus-value, by speeding up the development of the machine system. At the same time a change took place in the nature of relative surplus-value. In general, relative surplus-value is produced by raising the productivity of the worker, and thereby enabling him to produce more in a given time with the same expenditure of labour. The same amount of labour-time adds the same value as before to the total product, but this unchanged amount of exchange-value is spread over more use-values. Hence the value of each single commodity falls. But the situation changes with the compulsory shortening of the hours of labour. This gives an immense impetus to the development of productivity and the more economical use of the conditions of production. It imposes on the worker an increased expenditure of labour within a time which remains constant, a heightened tension of labour-power, and a closer filling-up of the pores of the working day, i.e. a condensation of labour , to a degree which can only be attained within the limits of the shortened working day. This compression of a greater mass of labour into a given period now counts for what it really is, namely an increase in the quantity of labour. In addition to the measure of its \"extensive magnitude,\" labour-time now acquires a measure of its intensity, or degree of density. The denser hour of the 10-hour working day contains more labour, i.e. expended labour-power, than the more porous hour of the 12-hour working day. Thus the product of one of the 10 hours has as much value as the product of 1⅕ of the 12 hours, or even more. Apart from the increased yield of relative surplus-value which results from the heightened productivity of labour, the same mass of value is now produced for the capitalist by, say, 3½ hours of surplus labour and 6½ hours of necessary labour, as was previously produced by 4 hours of surplus labour and 8 hours of necessary labour. Marx then addresses how labour is intensified. The first effect of shortening the working day results from the self-evident law that the efficiency of labour-power is in inverse ratio to the duration of its expenditure . Hence, within certain limits, what is lost by shortening the duration of labour is gained by increasing the degree of power exerted. Moreover, the capitalist ensures by his method of payment—especially by piece-wages—that the worker really does expend more labour-power. In manufactures like potteries, where machinery plays little or no part, the introduction of the Factory Act has strikingly shown that the mere shortening of the working day increases to a wonderful degree the regularity, uniformity, order, continuity, and energy of labour. It seemed, however, doubtful whether this effect could be produced in the factory proper, because there the dependence of the worker on the continuous and uniform motion of the machinery had already created the strictest discipline. Hence, when in 1844 the reduction of the working day to less than twelve hours was being debated, the manufacturers declared almost unanimously \"that their overlookers in the different rooms took good care that the hands lost no time,\" that \"the extent of vigilance and attention on the part of the workmen was hardly capable of being increased,\" and therefore, assuming the speed of the machinery and other conditions remained constant, \"to expect in a well-managed factory any important result from increased attention of the workmen was an absurdity.\" This assertion was controverted by means of experiments. Mr. Robert Gardner reduced the hours of work in his two large factories at Preston, on and after 20 April 1844, from 12 to 11 hours a day. The result of about a year on this system was that \"the same amount of product for the same cost was received, and the workpeople as a whole earned in 11 hours as much wages as they did before in 12.\" In the weaving department, where many sorts of figured fancy articles were woven, there was not the slightest alteration in the objective conditions of production. The result was: \"From 6th January to 20th April 1844, with a 12 hours' day, average weekly wages of each hand 10s. 1½d.; from 20th April to 29th June 1844, with a day of 11 hours, average weekly wages 10s. 3½d.\" Here we have more produced in 11 hours than previously in 12, entirely as a result of steadier application to the work and a more economical use of time on the part of the workers. While they got the same wages and gained one hour of spare time, the capitalist got the same amount produced and saved the cost of coal, gas, and other such items for one hour. The shortening of the working day creates, to begin with, the subjective condition for the condensation of labour, i.e. it makes it possible for the worker to set more labour-power in motion within a given time. As soon as that shortening becomes compulsory, machinery becomes in the hands of capital the objective means, systematically employed, for squeezing out more labour in a given time. This occurs in two ways: the speed of the machines is increased, and the same worker receives a greater quantity of machinery to supervise or operate. Improved construction of the machinery is necessary, partly to allow greater pressure to be put on the worker, partly because it is an inevitable concomitant of intensification of labour, since the legal limitation of the working day compels the capitalist to exercise the strictest economy in the cost of production. The improvements in the steam-engine have increased the piston speed and at the same time have made it possible, by means of a greater economy of power, to drive more machinery with the same engine, while consuming the same amount of coal, or even a smaller amount. The improvements in the transmitting mechanism have lessened friction and reduced the diameter and weight of the shafts to a constantly decreasing minimum. Finally, the improvements in the operative machines have, while reducing their size, increased their speed and efficiency, as in the modern power-loom; or, while increasing the size of their frames, they have also increased the extent and number of their working parts, as in spinning-mules, or added to the speed of those working parts by imperceptible alterations of detail. Marx provides extensive evidence of the intensification of labour. In 1836 a manufacturer stated: \"The labour now undergone in the factories is much greater than it used to be... compared with thirty or forty years ago... owing to the greater attention and activity required by the greatly increased speed which is given to the machinery.\" In 1844, Lord Ashley made in the House of Commons the following statements, supported by documentary evidence: \"The labour performed by those engaged in the processes of manufacture, is three times as great as in the beginning of such operations. Machinery has executed, no doubt, the work that would demand the sinews of millions of men; but it has also prodigiously multiplied the labour of those who are governed by its fearful movements... In 1815, the labour of following a pair of mules spinning cotton of No. 40—reckoning 12 hours to the working day—involved a necessity of walking 8 miles. In 1832, the distance travelled in following a pair of mules, spinning cotton yarn of the same number, was 20 miles, and frequently more. In 1825 the spinner put up daily, on each of these mules, 208 stretches, making a total of 1,640 stretches in the course of the day. In 1832, the spinner put up on each mule 2,200 stretches, making a total of 4,400. In 1844, 2,400 stretches, making a total of 4,800; and in some cases the amount of labour required is even still greater... In the carding-room there has also been a great increase of labour. One person there does the work formerly divided between two. In the weaving-room... the labour has increased within the last few years fully 10 per cent, owing to the increased speed of the machinery in spinning. In 1838, the number of hanks spun per week was 18,000, in 1843 it amounted to 21,000. In 1819, the number of picks in power-loom weaving per minute was 60—in 1842 it was 140, showing a vast increase of labour.\" Leonard Horner, the factory inspector, initially believed that a further reduction of the working day below 12 hours would necessarily diminish the quantity of the product. He cited his opinion of 1845 ten years later in order to show how much at that time he still under-estimated the elasticity of machinery and of human labour-power, both of which are simultaneously stretched to their utmost by the compulsory shortening of the working day. Marx then presents data from the period following the introduction of the Ten Hours' Act in 1847. \"The speed of the spindles has increased upon throstles 500, and upon mules 1,000 revolutions a minute, i.e. the speed of the throstle spindle, which in 1839 was 4,500 times a minute, is now (1862) 5,000; and of the mule spindle, that was 5,000, is now 6,000 times a minute, amounting in the former case to one-tenth; and in the second case to one-fifth additional increase.\" James Nasmyth, the eminent civil engineer, explained in a letter to Leonard Horner in 1852 the nature of improvements in the steam-engine made between 1848 and 1852: \"I am confident that from the same weight of steam-engine machinery, we are now obtaining at least 50 per cent more duty or work performed on the average, and that in many cases the identical steam-engines which in the days of the restricted speed of 220 feet per minute, yielded 50 horse-power, are now yielding upwards of 100... Although the same number of hands are employed in proportion to the horse-power as at former periods, there are fewer hands employed in proportion to the machinery.\" In 1850, the factories of the United Kingdom employed 134,217 nominal horse-power to move 25,638,716 spindles and 301,445 looms. By 1856, the number of spindles and looms had increased to 33,503,580 and 369,205 respectively, which, if we reckon the nominal horse-power required to move them to be the same as in 1850, would call for a total horse-power of 175,000. But according to the official return for 1856 the actual horse-power was 161,435, over 10,000 horse-power less than the result arrived at by calculating on the basis of the return of 1850. The factory system was increasing rapidly; although the same number of hands were employed in proportion to the horse-power as at former periods, there were fewer hands employed in proportion to the machinery; the steam-engine was enabled to drive an increased weight of machinery by economy of force and other methods; and an increased quantity of work could be turned off by improvements in machinery, by increase of speed, and by a variety of other causes. \"The great improvements made in machines of every kind have raised their productive power very much. Without any doubt, the shortening of the hours of labour... gave the impulse to these improvements. The latter, combined with the more intense strain on the workman, have had the effect that at least as much is produced in the shortened working day (shortened by two hours or one-sixth) as was previously produced during the longer one.\" Marx provides statistics showing the enormous increase in the wealth of manufacturers along with the more intensive exploitation of labour-power. From 1838 to 1850 the average annual increase in English cotton and other factories was 32; from 1850 to 1856 it was 86. In silk factories, there were 1,093,799 spindles in 1856 and 1,388,544 in 1862; 9,260 looms in 1856 and 10,709 in 1862. The number of workers, however, was 56,131 in 1856 and 52,429 in 1862. The increase in spindles was therefore 26.9 per cent, and in looms 15.6 per cent, while the number of workers decreased by 7 per cent. In worsted mills, the number of power-looms was 32,617 in 1850, 38,956 in 1856, and 43,048 in 1862. The number of workers was 79,737 in 1850, 87,794 in 1856, and 86,063 in 1862. But the number of children under 14 years old included in these figures was 9,956 in 1850, 11,228 in 1856, and 13,178 in 1862. Thus in spite of the greatly increased number of looms in 1862 compared with 1856, the total number of workers employed decreased, and the number of children exploited increased. On 27 April 1863, Mr. Ferrand said in the House of Commons: \"I have been informed by delegates from sixteen districts of Lancashire and Cheshire, in whose behalf I speak, that the work in the factories is, in consequence of the improvements in machinery, constantly on the increase. Instead of as formerly one person with two helps tenting two looms, one person now tents three looms without helps, and it is no uncommon thing for one person to tent four. 12 hours' work, as is evident from the facts adduced, is now compressed into less than 10 hours. It is therefore self-evident, to what an enormous extent the toil of the factory operative has increased during the last ten years.\" Thus, although the factory inspectors unceasingly commend the results of the Acts of 1844 and 1850, they admit that the shortening of the working day has already produced such an intensification of the labour itself as is injurious to the health of the worker and therefore to his labour-power as well. \"In most of the cotton, worsted, and silk mills, an exhausting state of excitement necessary to enable the workers satisfactorily to mind the machinery, the motion of which has been greatly accelerated within the last few years, seems to me not unlikely to be one of the causes of that excess of mortality from lung disease, which Dr. Greenhow has pointed out in his recent report on this subject.\" Capital's tendency, as soon as a prolongation of the hours of labour is once for all forbidden, is to compensate for this by systematically raising the intensity of labour, and converting every improvement in machinery into a more perfect means for soaking up labour-power. There cannot be the slightest doubt that this process must soon lead once again to a critical point at which a further reduction in the hours of labour will be inevitable. On the other hand, the rapid advance of English industry between 1848 and the present time, i.e. during the period of the 10-hour working day, surpasses the advance made between 1833 and 1847, during the period of the 12-hour working day, by far more than the latter surpasses the advance made during the half century after the first introduction of the factory system, i.e. during the period of the unrestricted working day. The Factory Section 4 turns to the factory as a whole in its most developed form. Dr. Ure, \"the Pindar of the automatic factory,\" describes it, on the one hand, as \"combined co-operation of many orders of work-people, adult and young, in tending with assiduous skill a system of productive machines continuously impelled by a central power\"; and on the other hand as \"a vast automaton, composed of various mechanical and intellectual organs, acting in uninterrupted concert for the production of a common object, all of them being subordinate to a self-regulated moving force.\" Marx observes that these two descriptions are far from being identical. In one, the combined collective worker appears as the dominant subject, and the mechanical automaton as the object; in the other, the automaton itself is the subject, and the workers are merely conscious organs, co-ordinated with the unconscious organs of the automaton, and together with the latter subordinated to the central moving force. The first description is applicable to every possible employment of machinery on a large scale, the second is characteristic of its use by capital, and therefore of the modern factory system. Ure therefore prefers to present the central machine from which the motion comes as not only an automaton but an autocrat: \"In these spacious halls the benignant power of steam summons around him his myriads of willing menials.\" Along with the tool, the skill of the worker in handling it passes over to the machine. The capabilities of the tool are emancipated from the restraints inseparable from human labour-power. This destroys the technical foundation on which the division of labour in manufacture was based. Hence, in place of the hierarchy of specialized workers that characterizes manufacture, there appears, in the automatic factory, a tendency to equalize and reduce to an identical level every kind of work that has to be done by the minders of the machines ; in place of the artificially produced distinctions between specialized workers, it is natural differences of age and sex that predominate. In so far as the division of labour re-appears in the factory, it takes the form primarily of a distribution of workers among the specialized machines, and of quantities of workers, who do not however form organized groups, among the various departments of the factory, in each of which they work at a number of similar machines placed together; only simple co-operation therefore takes place between them. The organized group peculiar to manufacture is replaced by the connection between the head worker and his few assistants. The essential division is that between workers who are actually employed on the machines (among whom are included a few who look after the engine) and those who merely attend them (almost exclusively children). More or less all the \"feeders\" who supply the machines with the material to be worked up are counted as attendants. In addition to these two principal classes, there is a numerically unimportant group whose occupation it is to look after the whole of the machinery and repair it from time to time, composed of engineers, mechanics, joiners, etc. This is a superior class of workers, in part scientifically educated, in part trained in a handicraft; they stand outside the realm of the factory workers, and are added to them only to make up an aggregate. This division of labour is purely technical. All work at a machine requires the worker to be taught from childhood upwards, in order that he may learn to adapt his own movements to the uniform and unceasing motion of an automaton. Since the machinery, taken as a whole, forms a system of machines of various kinds, working simultaneously and in combination, co-operation based upon it requires the distribution of various groups of workers among the different kinds of machine. But machine production abolishes the necessity of fixing this distribution in the manner of manufacture, i.e. by constantly appropriating the same worker to the same function. Since the motion of the whole factory proceeds not from the worker but from the machinery, the working personnel can continually be replaced without any interruption in the labour process. The most striking proof of this is afforded by the relay system, put into operation by the manufacturers during their revolt of 1848 to 1850. Lastly, the speed with which machine work is learnt by young people does away with the need to bring up a special class of worker for exclusive employment by machinery. Thus although, from a technical point of view, the old system of division of labour is thrown overboard by machinery, it hangs on in the factory as a tradition handed down from manufacture, and is then systematically reproduced and fixed in a more hideous form by capital as a means of exploiting labour-power. The lifelong speciality of handling the same tool now becomes the lifelong speciality of serving the same machine. Machinery is misused in order to transform the worker, from his very childhood, into a part of a specialized machine. In this way, not only are the expenses necessary for his reproduction considerably lessened, but at the same time his helpless dependence upon the factory as a whole, and therefore upon the capitalist, is rendered complete. In handicrafts and manufacture, the worker makes use of a tool; in the factory, the machine makes use of him. There the movements of the instrument of labour proceed from him, here it is the movements of the machine that he must follow. In manufacture the workers are the parts of a living mechanism. In the factory we have a lifeless mechanism which is independent of the workers, who are incorporated into it as its living appendages. \"The wearisome routine of endless drudgery in which the same mechanical process is ever repeated, is like the torture of Sisyphus; the burden of toil, like the rock, is ever falling back upon the worn-out drudge.\" Factory work exhausts the nervous system to the uttermost; at the same time, it does away with the many-sided play of the muscles, and confiscates every atom of freedom, both in bodily and intellectual activity. Even the lightening of the labour becomes an instrument of torture, since the machine does not free the worker from the work, but rather deprives the work itself of all content. Every kind of capitalist production, in so far as it is not only a labour process but also capital's process of valorization, has this in common, but it is not the worker who employs the conditions of his work, but rather the reverse, the conditions of work employ the worker. However, it is only with the coming of machinery that this inversion first acquires a technical and palpable reality. Owing to its conversion into an automaton, the instrument of labour confronts the worker during the labour process in the shape of capital, dead labour, which dominates and soaks up living labour-power. The special skill of each individual machine-operator, who has now been deprived of all significance, vanishes as an infinitesimal quantity in the face of the science, the gigantic natural forces, and the mass of social labour embodied in the system of machinery, which, together with those three forces, constitutes the power of the \"master.\" This \"master,\" therefore, in whose mind the machinery and his monopoly of it are inseparably united, contemptuously tells his \"hands,\" whenever he comes into conflict with them: \"The factory operatives should keep in wholesome remembrance the fact that theirs is really a low species of skilled labour; and that there is none which is more easily acquired, or of its quality more amply remunerated, or which by a short training of the least expert can be more quickly, as well as abundantly, acquired... The master's machinery really plays a far more important part in the business of production than the labour and the skill of the operative, which six months' education can teach, and a common labourer can learn.\" The technical subordination of the worker to the uniform motion of the instruments of labour, and the peculiar composition of the working group, consisting as it does of individuals of both sexes and all ages, gives rise to a barrack-like discipline, which is elaborated into a complete system in the factory. This brings the labour of superintendence to its fullest development, thereby dividing the workers into manual labourers and overseers, into the private soldiers and the N.C.O.s of an industrial army. Ure described the main difficulty in the automatic factory as \"above all in training human beings to renounce their desultory habits of work, and to identify themselves with the unvarying regularity of the complex automaton. To devise and administer a successful code of factory discipline, suited to the necessities of factory diligence, was the Herculean enterprise, the noble achievement of Arkwright! Even at the present day, when the system is perfectly organized and its labour lightened to the utmost, it is found nearly impossible to convert persons past the age of puberty into useful factory hands.\" In the factory code, the capitalist formulates his autocratic power over his workers like a private legislator, and purely as an emanation of his own will, unaccompanied by either that division of responsibility otherwise so much approved of by the bourgeoisie, or the still more approved representative system. This code is merely the capitalist caricature of the social regulation of the labour process which becomes necessary in co-operation on a large scale and in the employment in common of instruments of labour, and especially of machinery. The overseer's book of penalties replaces the slave-driver's lash. All punishments naturally resolve themselves into fines and deductions from wages, and the law-giving talent of the factory Lycurgus so arranges matters that a violation of his laws is, if possible, more profitable to him than the keeping of them. Marx provides extensive examples of the despotic factory code. In Sheffield at the end of 1866, a worker engaged for two years in a steelworks left after a dispute and was prosecuted for breach of contract, condemned to two months' imprisonment. After serving his sentence, the master invited him to return; the worker refused, saying he had already been punished for the breach. The master prosecuted again, and the court condemned again, although one of the judges publicly denounced it as a legal monstrosity that a man can periodically, as long as he lives, be punished over and over again for the same offence. In Wiltshire at the end of November 1863, around thirty power-loom weavers employed by one Harrup struck work because Harrup made deductions from their wages for being late in the morning: 6d. for two minutes, 1s. for three minutes, and 1s. 6d. for ten minutes—at the rate of 9s. per hour, and £4 10s. 0d. per day, whereas the annual average wage of the weavers never exceeded 10s. to 12s. a week. Harrup summoned nineteen women and girls before the magistrates for breach of contract; they were each fined 6d. and 2s. 6d. for costs. Marx describes the material conditions under which factory labour is performed: \"Every sense organ is injured by the artificially high temperatures, by the dust-laden atmosphere, by the deafening noise, not to mention the danger to life and limb among machines which are so closely crowded together, a danger which, with the regularity of the seasons, produces its list of those killed and wounded in the industrial battle. The economical use of the social means of production, matured and forced as in a hothouse by the factory system, is turned in the hands of capital into systematic robbery of what is necessary for the life of the worker while he is at work, i.e. space, light, air and protection against the dangerous or the unhealthy concomitants of the production process, not to mention the theft of appliances for the comfort of the worker. Was Fourier wrong when he called factories 'mitigated jails'?\" The Struggle Between Worker and Machine Section 5 addresses the worker's struggle against the instrument of labour itself. The struggle between the capitalist and the wage-labourer starts with the existence of the capital-relation itself. It rages throughout the period of manufacture. But only since the introduction of machinery has the worker fought against the instrument of labour itself, capital's material mode of existence. He is in revolt against this particular form of the means of production because it is the material foundation of the capitalist mode of production. In the seventeenth century nearly all Europe experienced workers' revolts against the ribbon-loom, a machine for weaving ribbons and lace trimmings called in Germany Bandmühle, Schnurmühle, or Mühlenstuhl. In the 1630s, a wind-driven saw-mill, erected near London by a Dutchman, succumbed to the rage of the mob. Even as late as the beginning of the eighteenth century, saw-mills driven by water overcame the opposition of the people only with great difficulty, supported as this opposition was by Parliament. No sooner had Everett constructed the first wool-shearing machine to be driven by water-power (1758) than it was set on fire by 100,000 people who had been thrown out of work. Fifty thousand workers, who had previously lived by carding wool, petitioned Parliament against Arkwright's scribbling mills and carding engines. The large-scale destruction of machinery which occurred in the English manufacturing districts during the first fifteen years of the nineteenth century, largely as a result of the employment of the power-loom, and known as the Luddite movement , gave the anti-Jacobin government a pretext for the most violent and reactionary measures. It took both time and experience before the workers learnt to distinguish between machinery and its employment by capital, and therefore to transfer their attacks from the material instruments of production to the form of society which utilizes those instruments. Marx notes that the struggles over wages within the manufacturing system presuppose manufacture, and are in no sense directed against its existence. The opposition to the establishment of manufactures proceeds from the guild-masters and the privileged towns, not from the wage-labourers. Hence the writers of the manufacturing period treat the division of labour predominantly as a means of virtually making up for a shortage of workers, and not of actually displacing them. If someone says that 100 million people would be required in England to spin with the old spinning-wheel the cotton that is now spun with mules by 500,000 people, this does not mean that the mules took the place of those millions who never existed. It means only that many million workers would be required to replace the spinning machinery. If, on the other hand, we say that in England the power-loom threw 800,000 weavers onto the streets, we do not refer to existing machinery, which would have to be replaced by a certain number of workers, but to an actually existing number of workers who were in fact replaced or displaced by the looms. The instrument of labour, when it takes the form of a machine, immediately becomes a competitor of the worker himself. The self-valorization of capital by means of the machine is related directly to the number of workers whose conditions of existence have been destroyed by it. The whole system of capitalist production is based on the worker's sale of his labour-power as a commodity. The division of labour develops this labour-power in a one-sided way, by reducing it to the highly particularized skill of handling a special tool. When it becomes the job of the machine to handle this tool, the use-value of the worker's labour-power vanishes, and with it its exchange-value. The worker becomes unsaleable, like paper money thrown out of currency by legal enactment. The section of the working class thus rendered superfluous by machinery, i.e. converted into a part of the population no longer directly necessary for the self-valorization of capital, either goes under in the unequal contest between the old handicraft and manufacturing production and the new machine production, or else floods all the more easily accessible branches of industry, swamps the labour-market, and makes the price of labour-power fall below its value. It is supposed to be a great consolation to the pauperized workers that, firstly, their sufferings are only temporary (\"a temporary inconvenience\") and, secondly, machinery only gradually seizes control of the whole of a given field of production, so that the extent and the intensity of its destructive effect is diminished. Marx comments: \"The first consolation cancels out the second.\" When machinery seizes on an industry by degrees, it produces chronic misery among the workers who compete with it. Where the transition is rapid, the effect is acute and is felt by great masses of people. World history offers no spectacle more frightful than the gradual extinction of the English hand-loom weavers; this tragedy dragged on for decades, finally coming to an end in 1838. Many of the weavers died of starvation, many vegetated with their families for a long period on 2½d. a day. In India, on the other hand, the English cotton machinery produced an acute effect. The Governor General reported in 1834-5: \"The misery hardly finds a parallel in the history of commerce. The bones of the cotton-weavers are bleaching the plains of India.\" Of course, in turning the weavers out of this \"temporal\" world, the machinery caused them a \"temporary inconvenience.\" But since machinery is continually seizing on new fields of production, its \"temporary\" effect is really permanent. Hence the character of independence from and estrangement towards the worker, which the capitalist mode of production gives to the conditions of labour and the product of labour, develops into a complete and total antagonism with the advent of machinery. It is therefore when machinery arrives on the scene that the worker for the first time revolts savagely against the instruments of labour. The instrument of labour strikes down the worker. The direct antagonism between the two is at its most apparent whenever newly introduced machinery enters into competition with handicrafts or manufactures handed down from former times. But within large-scale industry itself the continual improvement of machinery and the development of the automatic system has an analogous effect. \"The object of improved machinery is to diminish manual labour, to provide for the performance of a process or the completion of a link in a manufacture by the aid of an iron instead of the human apparatus.\" \"The adaptation of power to machinery heretofore moved by hand is almost of daily occurrence... the minor improvements in machinery having for their object economy of power, the production of better work, the turning off more work in the same time, or in supplying the place of a child, a female, or a man, are constant, and although sometimes apparently of no great moment, have somewhat important results.\" \"Whenever a process requires peculiar dexterity and steadiness of hand, it is withdrawn, as soon as possible, from the cunning workman, who is prone to irregularities of many kinds, and it is placed in charge of a peculiar mechanism, so self-regulating that a child can superintend it.\" \"On the automatic plan skilled labour gets progressively superseded.\" \"The effect of improvements in machinery, not merely in superseding the necessity for the employment of the same quantity of adult labour as before, in order to produce a given result, but in substituting one description of human labour for another, the less skilled for the more skilled, juvenile for adult, female for male, causes a fresh disturbance in the rate of wages.\" \"The effect of substituting the self-acting mule for the common mule, is to discharge the greater part of the men spinners, and to retain adolescents and children.\" Marx notes the machine system's extraordinary capacity for expansion as a result of accumulated practical experience, the extent of the mechanical instruments already available for use, and the constant advance of technology. The stimulus of the American Civil War called forth galloping pace of improvements in machinery and corresponding displacement of manual labour in the following three years. A Manchester manufacturer stated: \"We formerly had seventy-five carding engines, now we have twelve, doing the same quantity of work... We are doing with fewer hands by fourteen, at a saving in wages of £10 a week. Our estimated saving in waste is about 10 per cent in the quantity of cotton consumed.\" In another fine-spinning mill in Manchester, through increased speed and the adoption of some self-acting processes, a reduction had been made, in number, of a fourth in one department, and of above half in another. Messrs Gilmour, spinners at Manchester, stated: \"In our blowing-room department we consider our expense with new machinery is fully one-third less in wages and hands... in the jack-frame and drawing-frame room, about one-third less in expense, and likewise one-third less in hands; in the spinning-room about one-third less in expenses. But this is not all; when our yarn goes to the manufacturers, it is so much better by the application of our new machinery, that they will produce a greater quantity of cloth, and cheaper than from the yarn produced by old machinery.\" Marx provides a table showing the total result of the mechanical improvements in the English cotton industry resulting from the American Civil War: Number of Factories: - 1858: England and Wales 2,046; Scotland 152; Ireland 12; United Kingdom 2,210 - 1861: England and Wales 2,715; Scotland 163; Ireland 9; United Kingdom 2,887 - 1868: England and Wales 2,405; Scotland 131; Ireland 13; United Kingdom 2,549 Number of Power-Looms: - 1858: England and Wales 275,590; Scotland 21,624; Ireland 1,633; United Kingdom 298,847 - 1861: England and Wales 368,125; Scotland 30,110; Ireland 1,757; United Kingdom 399,992 - 1868: England and Wales 344,719; Scotland 31,864; Ireland 2,746; United Kingdom 379,329 Number of Spindles: - 1858: England and Wales 25,818,576; Scotland 2,041,129; Ireland 150,512; United Kingdom 28,010,217 - 1861: England and Wales 28,352,152; Scotland 1,915,398; Ireland 119,944; United Kingdom 30,387,494 - 1868: England and Wales 30,478,228; Scotland 1,397,546; Ireland 124,240; United Kingdom 32,000,014 Number of Persons Employed: - 1858: England and Wales 341,170; Scotland 34,698; Ireland 3,345; United Kingdom 379,213 - 1861: England and Wales 407,598; Scotland 41,237; Ireland 2,734; United Kingdom 451,569 - 1868: England and Wales 357,052; Scotland 39,809; Ireland 4,203; United Kingdom 401,064 Hence, between 1861 and 1868, 338 cotton factories disappeared; more productive machinery on a larger scale was concentrated in the hands of a smaller number of capitalists. The number of power-looms decreased by 20,663; but since their product increased in the same period, an improved loom yielded more than an old one. Finally, the number of spindles increased by 1,612,541, while the number of workers employed decreased by 50,505. The \"temporary\" misery inflicted on the workers by the cotton crisis was therefore heightened and made permanent by the rapid and continuous progress of machinery. But machinery does not just act as a superior competitor to the worker, always on the point of making him superfluous. It is a power inimical to him, and capital proclaims this fact loudly and deliberately, as well as making use of it. It is the most powerful weapon for suppressing strikes, those periodic revolts of the working class against the autocracy of capital. According to Gaskell, the steam-engine was from the very first an antagonist of \"human power,\" an antagonist that enabled the capitalists to tread underfoot the growing demands of the workers, which threatened to drive the infant factory system into crisis. Marx suggests it would be possible to write a whole history of the inventions made since 1830 for the sole purpose of providing capital with weapons against working-class revolt. Above all, the self-acting mule opened up a new epoch in the automatic system. Nasmyth, the inventor of the steam-hammer, gave evidence before the Commission on Trades Unions regarding the improvements in machinery he himself introduced as a result of the widespread and long-lasting strikes of the engineers in 1851: \"The characteristic feature of our modern mechanical improvements, is the introduction of self-acting tool machinery. What every mechanical workman has now to do, and what every boy can do, is not to work himself but to superintend the beautiful labour of the machine. The whole class of workmen that depend exclusively on their skill, is now done away with. Formerly, I employed four boys to every mechanic. Thanks to these new mechanical combinations, I have reduced the number of grown-up men from 1,500 to 750. The result was a considerable increase in my profits.\" Ure says of the colouring machines used in calico printing: \"At length capitalists sought deliverance from this intolerable bondage (namely the terms of their contracts with the workers, which they saw as burdensome) in the resources of science, and were speedily re-instated in their legitimate rule, that of the head over the inferior members.\" Of an invention for dressing warps whose immediate occasion was a strike: \"The combined malcontents, who fancied themselves impregnably intrenched behind the old lines of division of labour, found their flanks turned and their defences rendered useless by the new mechanical tactics, and were obliged to surrender at discretion.\" Of the invention of the self-acting mule: \"A creation destined to restore order among the industrious classes... This invention confirms the great doctrine already propounded, that when capital enlists science into her service, the refractory hand of labour will always be taught docility.\" Although Ure's work appeared in 1835, it remains the classical expression of the spirit of the factory, not only because of its undisguised cynicism but also because of the naivete with which it blurts out the thoughtless contradictions of the capitalist brain. The Compensation Theory Section 6 addresses the \"compensation theory\" of bourgeois political economy. A whole series of bourgeois political economists, including James Mill, MacCulloch, Torrens, Senior, and John Stuart Mill, assert that all machinery that displaces workers simultaneously, and necessarily, sets free an amount of capital adequate to employ precisely those workers displaced. Marx subjects this theory to a devastating critique. Let us assume that a capitalist employs 100 workers at £30 a year each in a carpet factory. The variable capital annually laid out therefore amounts to £3,000. Let us then assume that he dismisses fifty of his workers, and employs the remaining fifty with machinery that costs him £1,500. To simplify matters, we take no account of buildings, coal, etc. Finally, let the raw material annually consumed cost £3,000, both before and after the change. Is any capital \"set free\" by this metamorphosis? Before the change, the total sum of £6,000 consisted half of constant and half of variable capital. After the change it consists of £4,500 constant (£3,000 raw material and £1,500 machinery) and £1,500 variable capital. The variable capital, instead of being one-half, is only one-quarter of the total capital. Instead of being set free, a part of the capital is here locked up in such a way as to cease to be exchanged for labour-power; variable has been changed into constant capital. Other things being equal, the capital of £6,000 can now employ no more than fifty men. With each improvement in the machinery, it will employ fewer people. If the newly introduced machinery had cost less than the labour-power and implements displaced by it—if, for instance, instead of costing £1,500, it had cost only £1,000—a variable capital of £1,000 would have been converted into constant capital, and locked up in it, and a capital of £500 would have been set free. The latter sum, given the same annual wage-bill, would form a fund sufficient to employ about sixteen out of the fifty men dismissed, or rather less than sixteen, for, in order to be employed as capital, a part of this £500 must in its turn be transformed into constant capital, thus leaving only the remainder to be laid out in the purchase of labour-power. But suppose, in addition to this, that the making of the new machinery employs an increased number of mechanics. Can this be regarded as compensation for the carpet-makers who have been thrown on the streets? At best, the construction of the machinery will still employ fewer men than its application displaces. The sum of £1,500, which previously represented the wages of the dismissed carpet-makers, now represents in the shape of machinery (1) the value of the means of production used in the construction of that machinery, (2) the wages of the mechanics who constructed it, and (3) the surplus-value falling to the share of their \"master.\" Moreover, the machinery need not be renewed until it is worn out. Hence, in order to keep the increased number of mechanics in constant employment, one carpet manufacturer after another must replace workers with machines. In fact, the apologists for capitalism do not have in mind this sort of liberation of capital. They are thinking more of the means of subsistence of the workers who have been \"set free.\" It cannot be denied in the above instance that the machinery not only liberates fifty men, thus placing them at the disposal of other capitalists, but also, at the same time, withdraws from their consumption, and sets free, £1,500 worth of means of subsistence. The simple and by no means new fact that machinery sets the workers free from their means of subsistence is expressed in economic language by saying that machinery sets free means of subsistence for the workers, or converts those means of subsistence into capital with which to employ them. \"Everything, as you see, depends on the way things are put. Nominibus mollire licet mala.\" This theory implies that the £1,500 worth of means of subsistence was capital that was being valorized by the labour of the fifty men dismissed. Accordingly, the capital ceases to be employed as soon as the workers begin their forced holiday, and never rests until it has found a new \"placing\" in which the above-mentioned fifty can again consume it productively. On this theory, the capital and the workers must sooner or later come together again, and that is when the compensation will appear. Hence the sufferings of the workers displaced by machinery are as transient as worldly wealth. But the £1,500 worth of means of subsistence never confronted the dismissed workers as capital. This role was reserved for the sum of £1,500 later on, when it had been transformed into machinery. If we look more closely, it will be seen that the initial sum of £1,500 represented only a portion of the carpets produced in a year by the fifty dismissed men, and they received this part as wages from their employer, paid in money instead of in kind. With the carpets thus transformed into £1,500 they bought means of subsistence to the same value. These means, therefore, were to them not capital but commodities, and they, as regards these commodities, were not wage-labourers but buyers. The circumstance that they were \"set free\" by the machinery from the means of purchase changed them from buyers into non-buyers. Hence a lessened demand for those commodities. If this diminution of demand is not compensated for by an increase in demand from another direction, the market price of the commodities falls. If this state of things lasts for some time, and increases in extent, there follows the displacement of the workers employed in the production of those commodities. A part of the capital which previously produced the necessary means of subsistence is now reproduced in another form. While prices are falling, and capital is being displaced, the workers employed in the production of the necessary means of subsistence are in turn \"set free\" from a part of their wages. Instead, therefore, of proving that when machinery frees the worker from his means of subsistence, it simultaneously converts those means into capital for his further employment, our friends the apologists, with their well-tried law of supply and demand, prove the opposite, namely that machinery throws workers onto the streets, not only in that branch of production into which it has been introduced, but also in branches into which it has not been introduced. The real facts, which are travestied by the optimism of the economists, are these: the workers, when driven out of the workshop by the machinery, are thrown onto the labour-market. Their presence in the labour-market increases the number of labour-powers which are at the disposal of capitalist exploitation. Workers who have been thrown out of work in a given branch of industry can no doubt look for employment in another branch. If they find it, and thus renew the bond between them and the means of subsistence, this takes place only through the agency of a new, additional capital which is seeking investment, and in no way through the agency of the capital that was already functioning previously and was then converted into machinery. And even if they do find employment, what a miserable prospect they face! Crippled as they are by the division of labour, these poor devils are worth so little outside their old trade that they cannot find admission into any industries except a few inferior and therefore over-supplied and under-paid branches. Furthermore, every branch of industry attracts each year a new stream of men, who furnish a contingent from which to fill up vacancies, and to draw a supply for expansion. As soon as machinery has set free a part of the workers employed in a given branch of industry, the reserve men are also diverted into new channels of employment, and become absorbed in other branches; meanwhile the original victims, during the period of transition, for the most part starve and perish. It is an undoubted fact that machinery is not as such responsible for \"setting free\" the worker from the means of subsistence. It cheapens and increases production in the branch it seizes on, and at first leaves unaltered the quantity of the means of subsistence produced in other branches. Hence, after the introduction of machinery, society possesses as much of the necessaries of life as before, if not more, for the workers who have been displaced, not to mention the enormous share of the annual product wasted by non-workers. And this is the point relied on by our economic apologists! The contradictions and antagonisms inseparable from the capitalist application of machinery do not exist, they say, because they do not arise out of machinery as such, but out of its capitalist application! Therefore, since machinery in itself shortens the hours of labour, but when employed by capital it lengthens them; since in itself it lightens labour, but when employed by capital it heightens its intensity; since in itself it is a victory of man over the forces of nature but in the hands of capital it makes man the slave of those forces; since in itself it increases the wealth of the producers, but in the hands of capital it makes them into paupers, the bourgeois economist simply states that the contemplation of machinery in itself demonstrates with exactitude that all these evident contradictions are a mere semblance, present in everyday reality, but not existing in themselves, and therefore having no theoretical existence either. Thus he manages to avoid racking his brains any more, and in addition implies that his opponent is guilty of the stupidity of contending, not against the capitalist application of machinery, but against machinery itself. Marx compares this reasoning to Bill Sikes, the celebrated cut-throat: \"Gentlemen of the jury, no doubt the throat of this commercial traveller has been cut. But that is not my fault, it is the fault of the knife. Must we, for such a temporary inconvenience, abolish the use of the knife? Only consider! Where would agriculture and trade be without the knife? Is it not as salutary in surgery, as it is skilled in anatomy? And a willing assistant at the festive table? If you abolish the knife—you hurl us back into the depths of barbarism.\" Although machinery necessarily throws men out of work in those industries into which it is introduced, it may, despite this, bring about an increase in employment in other industries. This effect of machinery, however, has nothing in common with the so-called theory of compensation. Since every article produced by a machine is cheaper than a similar article produced by hand, we deduce the following absolute law: if the total quantity of the article produced by machinery is equal to the total quantity of the article previously produced by a handicraft or by manufacture, and now made by machinery, then total labour expended is diminished. The increase in the labour required to produce the instruments of labour themselves, the machinery, coal, etc., must be less than the reduction in labour achieved by the employment of machinery; otherwise the product of the machine would be as dear as, or dearer than, the product of the manual labour. But as a matter of fact, the total quantity of the article produced by machinery with a diminished number of workers, instead of remaining equal to the total quantity of the hand-made article that has been displaced, exceeds this by far. Suppose that 400,000 yards of cloth have been produced on power-looms by fewer weavers than could weave 100,000 yards by hand. The quadrupled product contains four times as much raw material. Hence the production of raw material must be quadrupled. But as regards the instruments of labour consumed, such as buildings, coal, machinery, and so on, it is different; the limit of the possible increase in the amount of additional labour required to produce them varies with the difference between the quantity of the machine-made article and the quantity of the same article that the same number of workers could make by hand. Hence, as the use of machinery extends in a given industry, the immediate effect is to increase production in the other industries that provide the first with means of production. How far employment is thereby found for an increased number of workers depends, given the length of the working day and the intensity of labour, on the composition of the capital employed, i.e. on the ratio of its constant to its variable component. This ratio, in its turn, varies considerably with the extent to which machinery has already penetrated, or is engaged in penetrating, those trades. The number of men condemned to work in coal and metal mines has been enormously swollen by the progress of machine production in England, although the growth in numbers has been slowed down during the last few decades by the introduction of new machinery into the mining industries. Along with the machine, a new type of worker springs to life: the machine-maker. Machinery is seizing control even of this branch of production on an ever-increasing scale. As to raw materials, there can be no doubt that the rapid advance of cotton spinning not only promoted as if in a hothouse the growing of cotton in the United States, and with it the African slave trade, but also made slave-breeding the chief business of the so-called border slave states. In 1790, when the first census of slaves was taken in the United States, their number was 697,000; in 1861 it had nearly reached four millions. On the other hand, it is no less certain that the blossoming of the English woollen factories, together with the progressive transformation of arable land into sheep pasture, brought about the conversion of agricultural labourers into \"supernumeraries\" and drove them in their masses from the land. Ireland, having during the last twenty years reduced its population by nearly one-half, is at this moment undergoing the process of still further reducing the number of its inhabitants to a level which will correspond exactly with the requirements of its landlords and the English woollen manufacturers. When machinery penetrates into any of the preliminary or intermediate stages through which an object of labour has to pass on its way to its final form, there is an increased yield of material in those stages, and simultaneously an increased demand for labour in the handicrafts or manufactures supplied by the machines. Spinning by machinery, for example, supplied yarn so cheaply and so abundantly that the hand-loom weavers were at first able to work full-time without increased outlay. Their earnings accordingly rose. This produced a flow of people into the cotton-weaving trade, until at length the 800,000 weavers called into existence by the jenny, the throstle, and the mule were overwhelmed by the power-loom. So also, owing to the abundance of clothing materials produced by machinery, the number of tailors, seamstresses, and needle-women went on increasing until the appearance of the sewing-machine. In proportion as machinery, with the aid of a relatively small number of workers, increases the mass of raw materials, half-finished products, and instruments of labour, the working-up of these raw materials and half-finished products becomes split up into innumerable subdivisions. There is thus an increase in the number of the branches of social production. Machine production drives the social division of labour immeasurably further than manufacture does, because it increases the productive power of the industries it seizes upon to a much greater degree. The immediate result of machinery is to augment surplus-value and the mass of products in which surplus-value is embodied. It also increases the quantity of substances for the capitalists and their dependants to consume, and therefore the size of these social strata themselves. Their growing wealth, and the relatively diminished number of workers required to produce the means of subsistence, begets both new luxury requirements and the means of satisfying them. A larger portion of the social product is converted into surplus product, and a larger portion of the surplus product is reproduced and consumed in a multitude of refined shapes. In other words, the production of luxuries increases. The products are also made more refined and more varied by the new world market relations created by large-scale industry. Not only are greater quantities of foreign luxury articles exchanged for home products, but a greater mass of foreign raw materials, ingredients, and half-finished articles are used as means of production in the home industries. Owing to these relations with the world market, the demand for labour increases in the transport industry, and splits the latter into numerous extra subdivisions. The increase in means of production and subsistence, accompanied by a relative diminution in the number of workers, provides the impulse for an extension of work that can only bear fruit in the distant future, such as the construction of canals, docks, tunnels, bridges, and so on. Entirely new branches of production, creating new fields of labour, are also formed as the direct result either of machinery or of the general industrial changes brought about by it. But the place occupied by these branches in total production is far from important, even in the most developed countries. The number of workers they employ is directly proportional to the demand created by these industries for the crudest form of manual labour. The chief industries of this kind are, at present, gas-works, telegraphy, photography, steam navigation, and railways. According to the census of 1861 for England and Wales, we find in the gas industry 15,211 persons; in telegraphy, 2,399; in photography, 2,366; in steam navigation, 3,570; and in railways, 70,599. The total number of persons employed in these five new industries amounts to 94,145. Lastly, the extraordinary increase in the productivity of large-scale industry, accompanied as it is by both a more intensive and a more extensive exploitation of labour-power in all other spheres of production, permits a larger and larger part of the working class to be employed unproductively. Hence it is possible to reproduce the ancient domestic slaves, on a constantly extending scale, under the name of a servant class, including men-servants, women-servants, lackeys, etc. According to the census of 1861, the population of England and Wales was 20,066,224; 9,776,259 of these were males and 10,289,965 females. If we deduct from this population all who are too old or too young for work, all \"unproductive\" women, young persons and children; then the \"ideological\" groups, such as members of the government, priests, lawyers, soldiers, etc.; then all the people exclusively occupied in consuming the labour of others in the form of ground rent, interest, etc.; and lastly, paupers, vagabonds, and criminals, there remain in round numbers eight millions of the two sexes of every age, including in that number every capitalist who is in any way engaged in industry, commerce, or finance. These eight millions are distributed as follows: Agricultural labourers (including shepherds, farm servants, and maidservants living in the houses of farmers): 1,098,261 Those employed in cotton, woollen, worsted, flax, hemp, silk, and jute factories, in stocking-making and lace-making by machinery: 642,607 (of these, only 177,596 are males above 13 years of age) Those employed in coal-mines and metal mines: 565,835 Those employed in metal works (blast-furnaces, rolling-mills, etc.) and metal manufactures of every kind: 396,998 (of these, 30,501 are females) The servant class: 1,208,648 (of these, 137,447 are males) All the persons employed in textile factories and in mines, taken together, number 1,208,442; those employed in textile factories and metal industries, taken together, number 1,039,605; in both cases less than the number of modern domestic slaves. What an elevating consequence of the capitalist exploitation of machinery! Repulsion and Attraction of Workers Through the Development of Machine Production. Crises in the Cotton Industry Section 7 addresses the apparent contradiction that, despite machinery displacing workers, the number of factory workers may increase over time. All the more respectable representatives of political economy concede that the introduction of new machinery has a baneful effect on the workers in the old handicrafts and manufactures with which this machinery at first competes. Almost all of them bemoan the slavery of the factory worker. And what is the great trump-card they play? That machinery, after the horrors of the period of its introduction and development have subsided, in the final analysis increases, rather than diminishing, the number of wage-slaves! Yes, political economy joyfully proclaims the theory, which is hideous to every \"philanthropist\" who believes that the capitalist mode of production is an eternal necessity ordained by nature, that after a period of growth and transition, and even when it is already founded on production by machinery, the factory system grinds down more workers than it originally threw onto the streets. It is true that in some cases, as we saw from the example of the English worsted and silk factories, an extraordinary extension of the factory system may, at a certain stage of its development, be accompanied not only by a relative, but by an absolute decrease in the number of workers employed. In 1860, when a special census of all the factories in the United Kingdom was taken by order of Parliament, the factories in those parts of Lancashire, Cheshire, and Yorkshire included in the district of Mr. R. Baker, the factory inspector, numbered 652; 570 of these contained 85,622 power-looms and 6,819,146 spindles (not including doubling-spindles), utilized 27,439 horse-power in steam-engines and 1,390 horse-power in water-wheels, and employed 94,119 persons. In 1865, the same factories contained 95,163 looms and 7,025,031 spindles, utilized 28,925 horse-power in steam-engines and 1,445 horse-power in water-wheels, and employed 88,913 persons. Between 1860 and 1865, therefore, the increase in looms was 11 per cent, in spindles 3 per cent, and in engine-power 3 per cent, while the number of persons employed decreased 5½ per cent. Between 1852 and 1862 considerable growth occurred in English woollen manufacture, while the number of workers employed in that industry remained almost stationary. \"This shows to what a great extent the introduction of new machines had superseded the labour of preceding periods.\" In certain cases, the increase in the number of factory workers employed is only apparent, i.e. it is not due to the extension of industries already based on machine production but to the gradual annexation of neighbouring branches of industry. For instance, the increase in power-looms and in the number of factory workers employed by them between 1838 and 1856 was, in the cotton trade, simply a result of the extension of this branch of industry; but in the other trades it resulted from the application of steam-power to the carpet-loom, the ribbon-loom, and the linen-loom, which had previously been driven by muscle-power. Hence the increase in the number of workers in these latter trades was merely an expression of the reduction in the total number of workers employed. Finally, we have considered this question entirely apart from the fact that everywhere, except in the metallurgical industries, young persons (under 18), women, and children form by far the most preponderant element in the factory personnel. Nevertheless, in spite of the mass of workers actually driven out and virtually replaced by machinery, we can understand how workers in factories may become more numerous than the manufacturing artisans and handicraftsmen they have displaced; their numbers grow through the building of more factories or the extension of old factories in a given industry. Suppose, for example, that under the old method of running the factory a capital of £500 is employed every week, two-fifths being constant and three-fifths variable capital, i.e. £200 being laid out in means of production, and £300, say £1 per man, in labour-power. With the introduction of machinery the composition of the total capital is altered. We will assume that it consists now of four-fifths constant and one-fifth variable, which means that only £100 is now laid out in labour-power. Consequently, two-thirds of the workers are dismissed. If now the business expands, and the total capital employed grows to £1,500, the other conditions of production remaining the same, the number of workers employed will increase to 300, just as many as before the introduction of the machinery. If the capital employed grows some more, to £2,000, 400 men will be employed, or one-third more than under the old system. Their numbers have in fact increased by 100, but in relative terms, i.e. in proportion to the total capital advanced, they have diminished by 800, for the £2,000 of capital would under the old method of running the enterprise have employed 1,200 instead of 400 workers. Hence a relative decrease in the number of workers employed is consistent with an actual increase in that number. We assumed above that while the total capital increased, its composition remained the same, because the conditions of production remained constant. But we have already seen that every advance in the use of machinery entails an increase in the constant component of capital, that part which consists of machinery, raw material, etc., and a decrease in its variable component, the part laid out in labour-power. We also know that in no other system of production is improvement so continuous and the composition of the capital employed so subject to variation as in the factory system. This constant variation is however equally constantly interrupted by periods of rest, during which there is a merely quantitative extension of factories on the existing technical basis. During such periods the number of workers employed increases. Thus in 1835 the total number of workers in the cotton, woollen, worsted, flax, and silk factories of the United Kingdom was only 354,684; while in 1861 the number of power-loom weavers alone (of both sexes and all ages, from 8 years old upwards) was 230,654. Admittedly, this growth appears less significant when we consider that in 1838 the hand-loom weavers with their families still numbered 800,000, not to mention those thrown out of work in Asia and on the European Continent. As long as machine production expands in a given branch of industry at the expense of the old handicrafts or of manufacture, the result is as certain as is the result of an encounter between an army with breech-loading rifles and one with bows and arrows. This first period, during which machinery conquers its field of operations, is of decisive importance, owing to the extraordinary profits it helps to produce. These profits not only form a source of accelerated accumulation, they also attract into the favoured sphere of production a large part of the additional social capital that is constantly being created, and is always seeking out new areas of investment. The special advantages of this initial period of furious activity are felt in every branch of production when it is newly penetrated by machinery. However, as soon as the factory system has attained a reasonable space to exist in, and reached a definite degree of maturity, and in particular as soon as the technical basis peculiar to it, machinery, is itself produced by machinery, as soon as coal-mining and iron-mining, the metallurgical industries, and the means of transport have been revolutionized; in short, as soon as the general conditions of production appropriate to large-scale industry have been established, this mode of production acquires an elasticity, a capacity for sudden extension by leaps and bounds, which comes up against no barriers but those presented by the availability of raw materials and the extent of sales outlets. On the one hand, the immediate effect of machinery is to increase the supply of raw material: thus, for example, the invention of the cotton gin increased the production of cotton. On the other hand, the cheapness of the articles produced by machinery and the revolution in the means of transport and communication provide the weapons for the conquest of foreign markets. By ruining handicraft production of finished articles in other countries, machinery forcibly converts them into fields for the production of its raw material. Thus India was compelled to produce cotton, wool, hemp, jute, and indigo for Great Britain. By constantly turning workers into \"supernumeraries,\" large-scale industry, in all countries where it has taken root, spurs on rapid increases in emigration and the colonization of foreign lands, which are thereby converted into settlements for growing the raw material of the mother country, just as Australia, for example, was converted into a colony for growing wool. A new and international division of labour springs up, one suited to the requirements of the main industrial countries, and it converts one part of the globe into a chiefly agricultural field of production for supplying the other part, which remains a pre-eminently industrial field. The factory system's tremendous capacity for expanding with sudden immense leaps, and its dependence on the world market, necessarily give rise to the following cycle: feverish production, a consequent glut on the market, then a contraction of the market, which causes production to be crippled. The life of industry becomes a series of periods of moderate activity, prosperity, over-production, crisis, and stagnation. The uncertainty and instability to which machinery subjects the employment, and consequently the living conditions, of the workers becomes a normal state of affairs, owing to these periodic turns of the industrial cycle. Except in the periods of prosperity, a most furious combat rages between the capitalists for their individual share in the market. This share is directly proportional to the cheapness of the product. Apart from the rivalry this struggle gives rise to in the use of improved machinery for replacing labour-power, and the introduction of new methods of production, there also comes a time in every industrial cycle when a forcible reduction of wages beneath the value of labour-power is attempted so as to cheapen commodities. A necessary condition for the growth of the number of factory workers is thus a proportionally much more rapid growth in the amount of capital invested in factories. But this process of growth takes place only within the ebbs and flows of the industrial cycle. It is, in addition, constantly interrupted by the technical progress that at one time virtually takes the place of additional workers, and at another time actually drives workers out of employment. This qualitative change in machine production continually removes workers from the factories, or closes their doors to the fresh stream of recruits, while the purely quantitative extension of the factories absorbs not only the men thrown out of work but also fresh contingents of workers. The latter are thus continually repelled and attracted, slung backwards and forwards, while, at the same time, constant changes take place in the sex, age, and skill of the industrial conscripts. Marx then provides a detailed chronological survey of developments in the English cotton industry from 1770 to 1863, demonstrating the cyclical nature of capitalist production. From 1770 until 1815 the cotton trade underwent only five years of depression or stagnation. During this period of forty-five years the English manufacturers had a monopoly of machinery and a monopoly of the world market. From 1815 to 1821 depression; 1822 and 1823 prosperity; 1824, repeal of the Combination Laws, great extension of factories everywhere; 1825, crisis; 1826, great misery and riots among the factory workers; 1827, slight improvement; 1828, great increase in power-looms and in exports; 1829, exports, especially to India, surpass all former years; 1830, glutted markets, great distress; 1831 to 1833, continued depression, the monopoly of the trade with India and China withdrawn from the East India Company; 1834, great increase of factories and machinery, shortage of hands; 1835, great prosperity, simultaneous starvation of the hand-loom weavers; 1836, great prosperity; 1837 and 1838, depression and crisis; 1839, revival; 1840, great depression, riots, the military called out to intervene; 1841 and 1842, frightful suffering among the factory workers; 1842, the manufacturers lock their hands out of the factories in order to enforce the repeal of the Corn Laws; 1844, revival; 1845, great prosperity; 1846, continued improvement at first, then reaction; 1847, crisis, general reduction of wages by 10 and more per cent; 1848, continued depression; 1849, revival; 1850, prosperity; 1851, falling prices, low wages, frequent strikes; 1852, the situation begins to improve; 1853, increasing exports, strike for three months and great misery at Preston; 1854, prosperity, glutted markets; 1855, news of bankruptcies streams in from the United States, Canada, and the markets of the Far East; 1856, great prosperity; 1857, crisis; 1858, improvement; 1859, great prosperity, increase in factories; 1860, zenith of the English cotton trade; 1861, prosperity continues for a time, reaction, the American Civil War, cotton famine; 1862 to 1863, complete collapse. Marx then dwells on the cotton famine as a typical example. From indications as to conditions in the world market in 1860 and 1861, the cotton famine came in the nick of time for the manufacturers, and was to some extent advantageous to them. Of the 2,887 cotton mills in the United Kingdom in 1861, 392 out of the 2,109 mills included in Redgrave's district, or 19 per cent, employed less than 10 horse-power each; 345, or 16 per cent, employed between 10 and 20 horse-power; while 1,372 employed upwards of 20 horse-power. The majority of the small mills were weaving sheds built during the period of prosperity after 1858, for the most part by speculators. These small manufacturers mostly went to the wall. In October 1862, 60.3 per cent of the spindles, and 58 per cent of the looms, were at a standstill. Even in those few cases where full time was worked, the weekly wages of the workers necessarily shrank, because good cotton was replaced by bad, Sea Island by Egyptian, American and Egyptian by Surat, and pure cotton by a mixture of waste and Surat. The shorter fibre of the Surat cotton and its dirty condition, the greater fragility of the thread, the substitution of all sorts of heavy ingredients for flour in sizing the warps, all these things lessened the speed of the machinery or the number of looms that could be superintended by one weaver, increased the amount of labour necessitated by defects in the machinery, and reduced the piece-wage by reducing the amount of the product actually leaving the mill. Where Surat cotton was used, the loss to the workers when on full time amounted to 20 per cent, 30 per cent, or more. But on top of this, the majority of the manufacturers reduced the piece-rate by 5, 7½, and 10 per cent. Even in 1863, after a comparative improvement had set in, the weekly wages of spinners and weavers were 3s. 4d., 3s. 10d., 4s. 6d., and 5s. 1d. Even in this miserable state of affairs, the inventive spirit of the manufacturer did not stand still, but was exercised in making deductions from wages. These were to some extent inflicted as a penalty for defects in the finished article that were really due to his own bad cotton and his unsuitable machinery. Moreover, where the manufacturer owned the workers' cottages, he paid himself his rents by deducting the amount from these miserable wages. Mr. Redgrave tells us of self-acting minders earning at the end of a fortnight's full work 8s. 11d., and from this sum was deducted the rent of the house, the manufacturer, however, returning half the rent as a gift. The minders took away the sum of 6s. 11d. In many places the self-acting minders ranged from 5s. to 9s. per week, and the weavers from 2s. to 6s. per week, during the latter part of 1862. Even when short time was being worked, the rent was frequently deducted from the workers' wages. No wonder that in some parts of Lancashire a kind of famine fever broke out. But more characteristic than all this was the revolution that took place in the production process at the expense of the workers. \"Experiments in corpore vili,\" like those of anatomists on frogs, were actually being made. \"Although,\" says Mr. Redgrave, \"I have given the actual earnings of the operatives in the several mills, it does not follow that they earn the same amount week by week. The operatives are subject to great fluctuation from the constant experimentalizing of the manufacturers... the earnings of the operatives rise and fall with the quality of the cotton mixings; sometimes they have been within 15 per cent of former earnings, and then, in a week or two, they have fallen off from 50 to 60 per cent.\" These experiments were not made just at the expense of the worker's means of subsistence. His five senses also had to pay the penalty. \"The people who are employed in making up Surat cotton complain very much. They inform me, on opening the bales of cotton there is an intolerable smell, which causes sickness... In the mixing, scribbling and carding rooms, the dust and dirt which are disengaged, irritate the air passages, and give rise to cough and difficulty of breathing. A disease of the skin, no doubt from the irritation of the dirt contained in the Surat cotton, also prevails... Bronchitis is more prevalent owing to the dust. Inflammatory sore throat is common, from the same cause. Sickness and dyspepsia are produced by the frequent breaking of the weft, when the weaver sucks the weft through the eye of the shuttle.\" On the other hand, these flour-substitutes were a Fortunatus's purse to the manufacturers because they increased the weight of the yarn. They caused \"15 lb. of raw material to weigh 26 lb. after it was woven.\" In the Reports of the Inspectors of Factories for the half-year ending 30 April 1864, we read: \"The trade is availing itself of this resource at present to an extent which is even discreditable. I have heard on good authority of a cloth weighing 8 lb. which was made of 5¼ lb. cotton and 2¾ lb. size; and of another cloth weighing 5¼ lb., of which 2 lb. was size. These were ordinary export shirtings. In cloths of other descriptions, as much as 50 per cent size is sometimes added; so that a manufacturer may, and does truly boast, that he is getting rich by selling cloth for less money per pound than he paid for the mere yarn of which they are composed.\" But the workers did not just have to suffer from the experiments of the manufacturers inside the mills, and of the municipalities outside, from reduced wages and absence of work, from want and from charity, and from the eulogies uttered in the Lords and the Commons. \"Unfortunate females, in consequence of the cotton famine, were at its commencement thrown out of employment, and have thereby become outcasts of society; and now, though trade has revived, and work is plentiful, continue members of that unfortunate class, and are likely to continue so.\" Marx summarizes: We find, then, in the first forty-five years of the English cotton industry, from 1770 to 1815, only five years of crisis and stagnation; but this was the period of monopoly. The second period from 1815 to 1863 counts, during its forty-eight years, only twenty years of revival and prosperity against twenty-eight of depression and stagnation. Between 1815 and 1830 competition with the continent of Europe and with the United States sets in. After 1833, the extension of the Asiatic markets is enforced by \"destruction of the human race.\" After the repeal of the Corn Laws, from 1846 to 1863, there are eight years of moderate activity and prosperity against nine years of depression and stagnation. The Revolutionary Impact of Large-Scale Industry on Manufacture, Handicrafts, and Domestic Industry Section 8 examines how large-scale industry revolutionizes all pre-existing forms of production. (a) Overthrow of Co-operation Based on Handicrafts and on the Division of Labour Machinery does away with co-operation based on handicrafts, and with manufacture based on the handicraft division of labour. An example of the first sort is the reaping-machine; it replaces co-operation between reapers. A striking example of the second kind is the needle-making machine. According to Adam Smith, ten men in his time, using the system of the division of labour, made 48,000 sewing-needles every day. A single needle-making machine, however, makes 145,000 needles in a working day of 11 hours. One woman or one girl superintends four such machines, and so produces nearly 600,000 needles in a day, and over 3,000,000 in a week. A single machine, when it takes the place of co-operation or of manufacture, may itself serve as the basis of an industry of a handicraft character. But this reproduction of the handicraft system on the basis of machinery only forms a transition to the factory system, which, as a rule, makes its appearance as soon as human muscles are replaced, for the purpose of driving the machines, by a mechanical motive power such as steam or water. In the Coventry ribbon industry the experiment of \"cottage factories\" was a quite natural and spontaneous development. In the centre of a square surrounded by rows of cottages, an engine-house was built and the engine was connected by shafts with the looms in the cottages. The power was hired out at so much per loom. The rent was payable weekly, whether the looms were working or not. The struggle between these cottage factories and the factory proper lasted over twelve years. It ended with the complete ruin of the 300 cottage factories. Wherever the nature of the process has not necessitated production on a large scale, the new industries that have sprung up in the last few decades, such as envelope making, steel pen making, etc., have, as a general rule, first passed through the handicraft stage, and then the manufacturing stage, as short phases of transition to the factory stage. The first steel pens were supplied by the handicraft system, in the year 1820, at £7 4s. the gross; in 1830 they were supplied by manufacture at 8s., and today the factory system supplies them at a wholesale price of from 2d. to 6d. the gross. (b) The Impact of the Factory System on Manufacture and Domestic Industries With the development of the factory system and the revolution in agriculture that accompanies it, production in all the other branches of industry not only expands but also alters its character. The principle of machine production, namely the division of the production process into its constituent phases, and the solution of the problems arising from this by the application of mechanics, chemistry, and the whole range of the natural sciences, now plays the determining role everywhere. Hence machinery penetrates into manufacture for one specialized process after another. The solid crystallization of a hierarchy of specialized processes, which arose from the old division of labour, ceases to exist; it is dissolved, and makes way for constant changes. In contrast with the period of manufacture, the division of labour is now based, wherever possible, on the employment of women, of children of all ages, and of unskilled workers, in short, of \"cheap labour,\" as the Englishman typically describes it. This is true not only for all large-scale production, whether machinery is employed or not, but also for the so-called domestic industries, whether carried on in the private dwellings of the workers or in small workshops. This modern \"domestic industry\" has nothing except the name in common with old-fashioned domestic industry, the existence of which presupposes independent urban handicrafts, independent peasant farming, and, above all, a dwelling-house for the worker and his family. That kind of industry has now been converted into an external department of the factory, the manufacturing workshop, or the warehouse. Besides the factory worker, the workers engaged in manufacture, and the handicraftsmen, whom it concentrates in large masses at one spot and directly commands, capital also sets another army in motion, by means of invisible threads: the outworkers in the domestic industries, who live in the large towns as well as being scattered over the countryside. An example: the shirt factory of Messrs. Tillie at Londonderry, which employs 1,000 workers in the factory itself, and 9,000 outworkers spread over the country districts. The exploitation of cheap and immature labour-power is carried out in a more shameless manner in modern manufacture than in the factory proper. This is because the technical foundation of the factory system, namely the substitution of machines for muscular power and the light character of the labour, is almost entirely absent in manufacture, and at the same time women and excessively young children are subjected quite unscrupulously to the influence of poisonous substances. In the so-called domestic industries this exploitation is still more shameless than in modern manufacture, because the workers' power of resistance declines with their dispersal; because a whole series of plundering parasites insinuate themselves between the actual employer and the worker he employs; because a domestic industry has always to compete either with the factory system or with manufacturing in the same branch of production; because poverty robs the worker of the conditions most essential to his labour, of space, light, and ventilation; because employment becomes more and more irregular; and, finally, because in these last places of refuge for the masses made \"redundant\" by large-scale industry and agriculture, competition for work necessarily attains its maximum. Economical use of the means of production, first systematically carried out in the factory system and coinciding there, from the very beginning, with the most reckless squandering of labour-power and the theft of the normal requirements for the labour-function, now, in a given branch of industry, turns uppermost its antagonistic and murderous side; and the less the social productivity of labour and the technical basis for the combination of labour processes are developed in that branch, the more does the murderous side of this economy emerge. (c) Modern Manufacture Marx illustrates the principles laid down above with a few examples. In the hardware manufactures of Birmingham and the neighbourhood, there are employed, mostly in very heavy work, 30,000 children and young persons, besides 10,000 women. They are to be found in a range of unhealthy jobs: in brass-foundries, button factories, and enamelling, galvanizing, and lacquering works. Owing to the excessive labour performed by their workers, both adult and non-adult, certain London firms where newspapers and books are printed have gained for themselves the honourable name of \"slaughter-houses.\" Similar excesses occur in book-binding, where the victims are chiefly women, girls, and children; young persons have to do heavy work in rope-works, and night-work in salt mines, candle factories, and chemical works; young people are worked to death at turning the looms in silk weaving, when it is not carried on by machinery. One of the most shameful, dirtiest, and worst paid jobs, a kind of labour on which women and young girls are by preference employed, is the sorting of rags. It is well known that Great Britain, apart from its own immense store of rags, is the emporium for the rag trade of the whole world. The rags flow in from Japan, from the most remote countries of South America, and from the Canary Islands. But the chief sources of supply are Germany, France, Russia, Italy, Egypt, Turkey, Belgium, and Holland. They are used for manure, for making bed-flocks, for shoddy, and they serve as the raw material of paper. The rag-sorters are carriers for the spread of small-pox and other infectious diseases, and they themselves are the first victims. A classic example of over-work, of hard and unsuitable labour, and of its brutalizing effects on the worker from his childhood upwards, is afforded not only by coal-mining and mining in general, but also by tile and brick making, in which industry the recently invented machinery is, in England, used only here and there. Between May and September the work lasts from 5 in the morning till 8 in the evening, and where the drying is done in the open air, it often lasts from 4 in the morning till 9 in the evening. Work from 5 in the morning till 7 in the evening is considered \"reduced\" and \"moderate.\" Boys and girls of 6 and even 4 years of age are employed. They work for the same number of hours as the adults, often longer. The work is hard and the summer heat increases the exhaustion. In a brickfield at Moxley, for example, a young woman (24 years old) was in the habit of making 2,000 bricks a day, with the assistance of two little girls who carried the clay for her and stacked the bricks. Every day these girls carried 10 tons up the slippery sides of the clay pits, from a depth of 30 feet, and then for a distance of 210 feet. \"It is almost impossible as things are for a child to pass through the ordeal of a brickfield without great moral degradation... the low language, which they are accustomed to hear from their tenderest years, the filthy, indecent, and shameless habits, amidst which, unknowing, and half wild, they grow up, make them in after-life lawless, abandoned, dissolute... A frightful source of demoralization is the mode of living. Each moulder, who is always a skilled labourer, and the chief of a group, supplies his seven subordinates with board and lodging in his cottage. Whether members of his family or not, the men, boys, and girls all sleep in the cottage, which contains generally two, exceptionally three rooms, all on the ground floor, and badly ventilated. These people are so exhausted after the day's hard work, that neither the rules of health, of cleanliness, nor of decency are in the least observed.\" There is a rich collection of official material in the fourth and sixth Public Health Reports (1862 and 1864) on the way in which capital economizes on the requirements for labour in modern manufacture. The descriptions of the workshops, more especially those of the London printers and tailors, surpass the most loathsome fantasies of the novelists. Dr. Simon, the chief medical officer of the Privy Council, says among other things: \"In my fourth report (1861) I showed, how it is practically impossible for the work-people to insist upon that which is their first sanitary right, viz., the right that, no matter what the work for which their employer brings them together, the labour, so far as it depends upon him, should be freed from all avoidably unwholesome conditions. I pointed out, that while the workpeople are practically incapable of doing themselves this sanitary justice, they are unable to obtain any effective support from the paid administrations of the sanitary policy... The life of myriads of workmen and workwomen is now uselessly tortured and shortened by the never-ending physical suffering that their mere occupation begets.\" Dr. Simon provides a table of mortality comparing different industries: Death-rate per 100,000 men in the respective industries between the stated ages: - Agriculture in England and Wales (958,265 persons): Age 25-35: 743; Age 35-45: 805; Age 45-55: 1,145 - London tailors (22,301 men, 12,379 women): Age 25-35: 958; Age 35-45: 1,262; Age 45-55: 2,093 - London printers (13,803 persons): Age 25-35: 894; Age 35-45: 1,747; Age 45-55: 2,367 (d) Modern Domestic Industry Marx then examines so-called domestic industry. In order to get an idea of the horrors of this sphere, in which capital conducts its exploitation against the background of large-scale industry, one might look at the apparently idyllic trade of nail-making, carried on in a few remote villages of England. Here, however, it will be enough to give a few examples from industries, some of whose branches are either not yet carried on with the aid of machinery, or do not as yet compete against machine and factory products: lace-making and straw-plaiting. Of the 150,000 persons employed in England in the production of lace, about 10,000 fall within the sphere of the Factory Act of 1861. Almost the whole of the remaining 140,000 are women, young persons, and children of both sexes, although the male sex is only weakly represented here. The state of health of this \"cheap\" material for exploitation can be seen from the following table, worked out by Dr. Trueman, physician to the Nottingham General Dispensary. Out of 686 female patients who were lace-makers, most of them between the ages of 17 and 24, the proportion of consumptives was: 1852: 1 in 45 1853: 1 in 28 1854: 1 in 17 1855: 1 in 18 1856: 1 in 15 1857: 1 in 13 1858: 1 in 15 1859: 1 in 9 1860: 1 in 8 1861: 1 in 8 \"This advance in the rate of consumption ought to suffice for the most optimistic advocate of progress, or for the most mendacious free-trade bagman in Germany.\" The Factory Act of 1861 regulates the actual making of the lace, in so far as it is done by machinery. The branches Marx examines, solely with regard to those who work at home, and are not concentrated in workshops or warehouses, fall into two categories: (1) finishing, and (2) mending. The former gives the finishing touches to the machine-made lace, and includes numerous subdivisions. The lace finishing is done either in what are called \"mistresses' houses,\" or by women in their own houses, with or without the help of their children. The women who keep the \"mistresses' houses\" are themselves poor. The workroom is in a private house. The mistresses take orders from manufacturers, or from warehousemen, and employ as many women, girls, and young children as the size of their rooms and the fluctuating demand of the business will allow. The number of women employed in these workrooms varies from twenty to forty in some, and from ten to twenty in others. The average age at which the children start work is 6 years, but in many cases it is below 5. The usual working hours are from 8 in the morning until 8 in the evening, with 1½ hours for meals, which are taken at irregular intervals, and often in the stinking workrooms. When business is brisk, the labour frequently lasts from 8 or even 6 o'clock in the morning until 10, 11, or 12 o'clock at night. In English barracks the regulation space allotted to each soldier is 500 to 600 cubic feet, and in the military hospitals 1,200 cubic feet. But in those finishing sties there are between 67 and 100 cubic feet for each person. At the same time the oxygen of the air is consumed by gas-lights. In order to keep the lace clean, the children are often compelled to pull off their shoes, even in winter, although the floor is tiled or flagged. \"It is not at all uncommon in Nottingham to find fourteen to twenty children huddled together in a small room, of, perhaps, not more than 12 feet square, and employed for 15 hours out of the 24, at work that of itself is exhausting, from its weariness and monotony, and is besides carried on under every possible unwholesome condition... Even the very youngest children work with a strained attention and a rapidity that is astonishing, hardly ever giving their fingers rest or slowing their motion. If a question is asked them, they never raise their eyes from their work from fear of losing a single moment.\" The \"long cane\" is increasingly used as a stimulant by the mistresses as the working hours drag towards their close. \"The children gradually tire and become as restless as birds towards the end of their long detention at an occupation that is monotonous, eye-straining, and exhausting from the uniformity in the posture of the body. Their work is like slavery.\" When women and their children work at home, which nowadays means in a hired room, often in a garret, their situation is, if that is possible, still worse. This sort of work is given out within a radius of 80 miles from Nottingham. On leaving the warehouse at 9 or 10 o'clock at night, the children are often given a bundle of lace to take home with them and finish. The pharisee of a capitalist, represented by one of his paid lackeys, accompanies this action, of course, with the unctuous phrase \"That's for mother,\" yet he knows very well that the poor children must sit up and help. Pillow lace-making is chiefly carried on in England in two agricultural districts; one, the Honiton lace district, extending from 20 to 30 miles along the south coast of Devonshire, and including a few places in North Devon; the other comprising a great part of the counties of Buckingham, Bedford, and Northampton, and also the adjoining portions of Oxfordshire and Huntingdonshire. The cottages of the agricultural labourers are the places where the work is usually carried on. Many manufacturers employ upwards of 3,000 of these lace-makers, who are chiefly children and young persons, and always female. The conditions we have just described for the lace-finishing trade also prevail here, except that instead of the \"mistresses' houses\" we find what are called \"lace-schools\" kept by poor women in their cottages. From their fifth year and often earlier, until their twelfth or fifteenth year, the children work in these schools; during the first year the very young ones work from four to eight hours, and later on, from six in the morning till eight and ten o'clock at night. \"These rooms are generally the living rooms of small cottages, with the fireplace stopped up to prevent draught, and sometimes even in winter, the animal heat of the inmates being thought sufficient; in other cases, they are small pantry-like rooms without any fireplaces... The crowding in these rooms and the foulness of air produced by it are sometimes extreme. Added to this is the injurious effect of drains, privies, decomposing substances, and other filth usual in the purlieus of the smaller cottages.\" With regard to space: \"In one lace-school eighteen girls and a mistress, 33 cubic feet to each person; in another, where the smell was unbearable, eighteen persons and 24½ cubic feet per head. In this industry are to be found employed children of 2 and 2½ years.\" Where lace-making ends in the counties of Buckingham and Bedford, straw-plaiting begins, and extends over a large part of Hertfordshire and the westerly and northerly parts of Essex. In 1861, there were 40,043 persons employed in straw-plaiting and straw-hat making; of these, 3,815 were males of all ages, the rest females, of whom 14,913, including about 7,000 children, were under 20 years of age. In the place of the lace-schools we find here the \"straw-plait schools.\" The children generally start to be instructed in straw-plaiting at the age of 4, often between 3 and 4 years. They get no education, of course. The children themselves call the elementary schools \"natural schools,\" distinguishing them in this way from these blood-sucking institutions, in which they are kept at work simply to get through the task, generally 30 yards a day, which is prescribed by their half-starved mothers. These same mothers often make them work at home, after school is over, till 10, 11, and 12 o'clock at night. The straw cuts their mouths, with which they constantly moisten it, and their fingers. Dr. Ballard gives it as the general opinion of the whole body of medical officers in London that 300 cubic feet is the minimum space proper for each person in a bedroom or workroom. But in the straw-plait schools space is more sparingly allotted than in the lace-schools, \"12½, 17, 18½ and below 22 cubic feet for each person.\" The smaller of these numbers, says one of the commissioners, Mr. White, represents less space than the half of what a child would occupy if packed in a box measuring 3 feet in each direction. Thus do the children enjoy life till the age of 12 or 14. The wretched half-starved parents think of nothing but getting as much as possible out of their children. The latter, as soon as they are grown up, do not care a farthing, and naturally so, for their parents, and leave them. \"It is no wonder that ignorance and vice abound in a population so brought up... Their morality is at the lowest ebb... a great number of the women have illegitimate children, and that at such an immature age that even those most conversant with criminal statistics are astounded.\" And the native land of these model families is itself the model Christian country in Europe; at least, this is what Count Montalembert says, and he is certainly a competent authority on Christianity! Wages in the above industries, miserable as they are (the maximum wages of a child in the straw-plait schools rising in rare cases to 3 shillings), are reduced far below their nominal amount by the prevalence of the truck system everywhere, but especially in the lace districts. (e) Transition from Modern Manufacture and Domestic Industry to Large-Scale Industry. The Hastening of this Revolution by the Application of the Factory Acts to those Industries The cheapening of labour-power, by sheer abuse of the labour of women and children, by sheer robbery of every normal condition needed for working and living, and by the sheer brutality of over-work and night-work, finally comes up against certain insuperable natural obstacles. This is also true of the cheapening of commodities, and of capitalist exploitation in general, which rest on these foundations. When this point has at last been reached—and this takes many years—the hour has struck for the introduction of machinery, and for a thenceforth rapid transformation of the scattered domestic industries, as well as the manufactures, into factory industries. An example of this process, on the most colossal scale, is afforded by the production of \"wearing apparel.\" This industry, according to the classification of the Children's Employment Commission, comprises straw-hat makers, ladies'-hat makers, cap-makers, tailors, milliners and dressmakers, shirtmakers, corset-makers, glove-makers, shoemakers, besides many minor branches, such as the making of neck-ties, collars, etc. In 1861 the number of females employed in these industries, in England and Wales, amounted to 586,299, of whom 115,242 at least were under 20, and 16,650 under 15 years of age. The number of these working women in the United Kingdom in 1861 was 750,334. The number of males employed in England and Wales, in hat-making, shoemaking, glove-making, and tailoring was 437,969; of these, 14,964 were under 15 years, 89,285 between 15 and 20, and 333,117 over 20 years. Many of the smaller branches are not included in these figures. But take the figures as they stand; we then have for England and Wales alone, according to the census of 1861, a total of 1,024,277 persons, about as many as are taken up by agriculture and cattle-breeding. We begin to understand the purpose for which machinery conjures up such immense quantities of goods, and \"sets free\" such enormous masses of workers. The production of \"wearing apparel\" is carried on partly in manufacturing workshops within which there is merely a reproduction of the division of labour whose scattered elements were already to hand; partly by small master-craftsmen, who do not, however, work as before for individual consumers, but for factories and warehouses, and to such an extent that often whole towns and stretches of country carry on certain branches, such as shoemaking, as a speciality; finally, on a very large scale, by the so-called domestic workers, who form an external department of the factories and warehouses, and even of the workshops of the smaller masters. The raw material for this labour, either in its raw shape or already semi-fabricated, is supplied by large-scale industry, and the mass of cheap human material (\"taxable at pleasure and mercy\") consists of the individuals \"set free\" by large-scale industry and agriculture. The manufactures of this sphere owed their origin chiefly to the capitalists' need to have at hand an army equipped to respond to any increase in demand. These manufactures, nevertheless, allowed the scattered handicrafts and domestic industries to continue to exist as a broad foundation. The great production of surplus-value in these branches of labour, and the progressive cheapening of their articles, were and are chiefly due to the minimum wages paid, which just sufficed for a miserable, vegetable existence, and to the extension of the hours of labour to the maximum endurable by the human organism. It was in fact the cheapness of the human sweat and the human blood which were converted into commodities, which permitted the constant extension of the market; this was especially true of England's colonial market where, besides, English tastes and habits prevail. At last the critical point was reached. The basis of the old method, sheer brutality in the exploitation of the workers, accompanied by a more or less systematic division of labour, no longer sufficed for the extending markets and for the still more rapidly extending competition of the capitalists. The hour of the machine had struck. The decisively revolutionary machine, the machine which attacks in an equal degree all the innumerable branches of this sphere of production, such as dressmaking, tailoring, shoemaking, sewing, hat-making and so on, is the sewing-machine. Its immediate effect on the workers is like that of all machinery, which, during the epoch of large-scale industry, has seized on new branches of trade. Children who are too young are removed. The wage of those who work with machines rises compared with that of the domestic workers, many of whom belong among the \"poorest of the poor.\" The wage of the better situated handicraftsmen sinks, however, since the machine is in competition with them. The new machine-minders are exclusively girls and young women. With the help of mechanical force, they destroy the monopoly that male labour had of the heavier work, and they drive off from the lighter work numbers of old women and very young children. The overpowering competition crushes the weakest manual workers. The fearful increase in death from starvation during the last ten years in London runs parallel with the extension of machine sewing. The new female workers turn the machines by hand and foot, or by hand alone, sometimes sitting, sometimes standing, according to the weight, size, and special make of the machine, and expend a great deal of labour-power. Their occupation is unwholesome, owing to the long hours, although in most cases these are not so long as under the old system. Wherever the sewing-machine is located in narrow and already over-crowded workrooms, it adds to the unwholesome influences. \"The effect,\" says Mr. Lord, \"of entering a low pitched room where thirty to forty machinists are working under such conditions... is almost overpowering... the heat, partly owing to gas stoves for heating irons, was dreadful... Even when moderate hours of work, i.e., from 8 in the morning till 6 in the evening, prevail in such places, yet three or four persons fall into a swoon regularly every day.\" The revolution in the social mode of production which is the necessary product of the revolution in the means of production is accomplished through a variegated medley of transitional forms. These forms vary according to the extent to which the sewing-machine has become prevalent in one branch of industry or the other, the time during which it has been in operation, the previous condition of the workers, the degree to which manufacture, handicrafts, or domestic industry preponderates, the level of rent of the workrooms, and so on. In dress-making, for instance, where the labour for the most part was already organized, chiefly by simple co-operation, the sewing-machine at first formed nothing but a fresh element in the already existing system of manufacture. In tailoring, shirtmaking, shoemaking, etc., all the forms are intermingled. Here we see the factory system proper. We see middlemen receiving the raw material from the capitalist en chef, and setting to work at sewing-machines, in \"chambers\" and \"garrets,\" groups of from ten to fifty or more female workers. Finally, as is always the case with machinery when not organized into a system, and when it can also be used on a very small scale, handicraftsmen and domestic workers, along with their families, or with a little extra labour from outside, make use of their own sewing-machines. The system actually prevalent in England is this: the capitalist concentrates a large number of machines on his premises, and then distributes the product of those machines amongst the domestic workers to work it up into its finished form. The variety of these transitional forms does not, however, conceal the tendency operating to transform them into the factory system proper. This tendency is nurtured by the very nature of the sewing-machine, the manifold uses of which tend to compel the concentration, under one roof and one management, of previously separated branches of a trade. It is also favoured by the circumstance that preparatory needlework and certain other operations are most conveniently done on the premises where the machine is at work, as well as by the inevitable expropriation of the hand sewers, and the domestic workers who work with their own machines. This fate has already in part overtaken them. The constantly increasing amount of capital invested in sewing-machines spurs on the production of machine-made articles and gluts the market with them, thereby signalling to the domestic workers that they must sell their machines. The over-production of sewing-machines themselves causes their producers, who need to sell at all costs, to let them out for so much a week, thus crushing the small sewing-machine owners by their deadly competition. Constant changes in the construction of the machines, and their ever-increasing cheapness, cause the older makes to depreciate daily and compel their sale in great numbers, at absurd prices, to large capitalists, who are now the only people who can employ them at a profit. Finally, the substitution of the steam-engine for man strikes the final blow in this, as in all similar processes of transformation. Initially the use of steam-power meets with certain technical difficulties, such as unsteadiness in the machines, difficulty in controlling their speed, rapid wear and tear of the lighter machines, etc. But these are all soon overcome by experience. If, on the one hand, the concentration of many machines in large factories leads to the use of steam power, on the other hand the competition of steam with human muscles hastens on the concentration of workers and machines in large factories. Thus England is at present experiencing, not only in the colossal industry of making \"wearing apparel,\" but also in most of the other trades mentioned above, the conversion of manufacture, handicrafts, and domestic work into the factory system, after each of those forms of production, totally changed and disorganized under the influence of large-scale industry, has long ago reproduced and even overdone all the horrors of the factory system, without reproducing any of the positive aspects of its development. This industrial revolution, which advances naturally and spontaneously, is also helped on artificially by the extension of the Factory Acts to all industries in which women, young persons, and children are employed. The compulsory regulation of the working day, as regards its length, pauses, beginning, and end, the introduction of the relay system for children, the exclusion from the factory of all children under a certain age, etc., necessitate on the one hand more machinery and the substitution of steam as a motive power in the place of muscles. On the other hand, in order to make up for the loss of time, an expansion occurs of the means of production used in common, of the furnaces, buildings, etc., in one word, a greater concentration of the means of production and a corresponding increase in the number of workers conglomerated in one place. The chief objection, raised repeatedly and passionately on behalf of each manufacture threatened with the Factory Act, is in fact this, that in order to continue the business on the old scale a greater outlay of capital will be necessary. But, as regards labour in the so-called domestic industries and the intermediate forms between them and manufacture, as soon as limits are set to the working day and to the employment of children, those industries go to the wall. Unlimited exploitation of cheap labour-power is the sole foundation of their ability to compete. One of the essential conditions for the existence of the factory system, especially when the length of the working day is fixed, is certainty in the result , i.e. the production in a given time of a given quantity of commodities, or of a given useful effect. The statutory pauses in the working day, moreover, imply the assumption that periodic and sudden cessations in the work do no harm to the article undergoing the process of production. This certainty in the result, and this possibility of interrupting the work, are of course more easily attained in the purely mechanical industries than in those in which chemical and physical processes play a part; as, for instance, in the earthenware trade, in bleaching, dyeing, baking, and in most of the metallurgical industries. Wherever there is a working day whose length is not restricted, wherever there is night-work and unrestricted waste of human life, there the slightest obstacle presented by the nature of the work to a change for the better is soon looked on as an eternal \"natural barrier\" inherent in production. No poison kills vermin with more certainty than the Factory Act removes such \"natural barriers.\" Marx provides examples. In the earthenware manufacture, no one made a greater outcry over \"impossibilities\" than the gentlemen involved. In 1864, however, they were brought under the Act, and within sixteen months every \"impossibility\" had vanished. \"The improved method,\" called forth by the Act, \"of making slip by pressure instead of by evaporation, the newly-constructed stoves for drying the ware in its green state, etc., are each events of great importance in the pottery art, and mark an advance which the preceding century could not rival... It has even considerably reduced the temperature of the stoves themselves with a considerable saving of fuel, and with a readier effect on the ware.\" In spite of every prophecy, the cost price of earthenware did not rise, but the quantity produced did, and to such an extent that the export for the twelve months ending December 1865 exceeded in value by £138,628 the average of the preceding three years. In the manufacture of matches it was thought to be an indispensable requirement that boys, even while bolting their dinner, should go on dipping the matches in melted phosphorus, whose poisonous vapour rose into their faces. The Factory Act (1864) made the saving of time a necessity, and so forced into existence a dipping machine, whose vapour could not come into contact with the workers. Marx notes that the British Parliament, \"which no one will reproach with being excessively endowed with genius, has been led by experience to the conclusion that a simple compulsory law is sufficient to enact away all the so-called impediments opposed by the nature of the process to the restriction and regulation of the working day.\" Hence, on the introduction of the Factory Act into a given industry, a period varying from six to eighteen months is fixed within which it is incumbent on the manufacturers to remove all technical impediments to the working of the Act. Mirabeau's \"Impossible! ne me dites jamais ce bête de mot!\" is particularly applicable to modern technology. But though the Factory Acts thus artificially ripen the material elements necessary for the conversion of the manufacturing system into the factory system, yet at the same time, because they make it necessary to lay out a greater amount of capital, they hasten the decline of the small masters, and the concentration of capital. Apart from the purely technical impediments, which can be removed by technical means, the irregular habits of the workers themselves obstruct the regulation of the hours of labour. This is especially the case where piece-wages predominate, and where loss of time in one part of the day or week can be made good by subsequent overtime or by night-work, a process which brutalizes the adult worker and ruins his wife and children. Although this absence of regularity in the expenditure of labour-power is a natural and crudely spontaneous reaction against the tedium of monotonous drudgery, it also originates, and to a much greater degree, from the anarchy in production itself, an anarchy that in its turn presupposes unbridled exploitation of labour-power by the capitalist. Alongside the general and periodic changes in the industrial cycle, and the special fluctuations in the markets to which each industry is subject, we may also reckon what is called \"the season,\" dependent either on the periodicity of favourable seasons of the year for navigation, or on fashion, and the sudden placing of large orders that have to be executed in the shortest possible time. The habit of giving such orders becomes more frequent with the extension of railways and telegraphs. In factories and places of manufacture which are not yet subject to the Factory Acts, the most fearful over-work prevails periodically during what is called the season, as a result of sudden orders. In the outside departments of factory, workshop, and warehouse, the so-called domestic workers, whose employment is at best irregular, are entirely dependent for their raw material and their orders on the caprice of the capitalist, who, in this industry, is not hampered by any regard for depreciation of his buildings and machinery, and risks nothing by a stoppage of work but the skin of the worker himself. Here then he sets himself systematically to work to form an industrial reserve force that shall be ready at a moment's notice; during one part of the year he decimates this force by the most inhuman toil, during the other part he lets it starve for lack of work. \"Employers... avail themselves of the habitual irregularity (in work at home) when any extra work is wanted at a push, so that work goes on till 11 and 12 p.m., or 2 a.m., or as the usual phrase is, 'all hours.'\" \"They are curious men,\" said one of the witnesses, a shoemaker, speaking of the masters, \"and think it does a boy no harm to work too hard for half the year, if he is nearly idle for the other half.\" Like the technical impediments, these \"usages which have grown with the growth of trade,\" or business customs, were also proclaimed by interested capitalists (and still are proclaimed) to be \"natural barriers\" inherent in production. This was a favourite cry of the cotton lords at the time when they were first threatened with the Factory Acts. Although their industry depends, more than any other, on the world market, and therefore on shipping, experience showed they were lying. Since then, every pretended \"obstruction to business\" has been treated by the Factory Inspectors as a mere sham. The thoroughly conscientious investigations of the Children's Employment Commission prove that the effect of the regulation of the hours of work, in some industries, was to spread the mass of labour previously employed more evenly over the whole year; that this regulation was the first rational bridle on the murderous, meaningless caprices of fashion; that the development of ocean navigation and of the means of communication in general has destroyed the actual technical foundation of seasonal labour; and that all other so-called uncontrollable circumstances are swept away by the increased size of the buildings, the additional machinery, the increased number of workers employed simultaneously, and the automatic impact of all these changes on the mode of conducting the wholesale trade. But for all that, capital never becomes reconciled to such changes—and this is admitted over and over again by its own representatives—except \"under the pressure of a General Act of Parliament\" for the compulsory regulation of the hours of labour. The Health and Education Clauses of the Factory Acts. The General Extension of Factory Legislation in England Section 9 examines factory legislation as \"that first conscious and methodical reaction of society against the spontaneously developed form of its production process,\" which is \"just as much the necessary product of large-scale industry as cotton yarn, self-actors and the electric telegraph.\" Marx first notices certain clauses contained in the Factory Acts which do not relate to the hours of work. Quite apart from their wording, which makes it easy for the capitalist to evade them, the clauses relating to health are extremely meagre, and in fact limited to provisions for whitewashing the walls, for insuring cleanliness in some other matters, for ventilation, and for protection against dangerous machinery. Marx notes the \"fanatical opposition of the manufacturers\" to those clauses which imposed on them a slight expenditure on appliances for protecting the arms and legs of their \"hands.\" One example suffices. During the last twenty years the flax industry expanded considerably, and with that expansion the number of scutching mills in Ireland increased. In 1864 there were 1,800 of these mills. Regularly, in autumn and winter, women and \"young persons,\" the wives, sons, and daughters of the neighbouring small farmers, a class of people entirely unaccustomed to machinery, are taken from field labour to feed the rollers of the scutching mills with flax. The accidents, both as regards number and kind, are wholly unparalleled in the history of machinery. In one scutching mill, at Kildinan, near Cork, there occurred between 1852 and 1856 six fatal accidents and sixty mutilations. Every one of these might have been prevented by the simplest appliances, costing a few shillings. Dr. W. White, the certifying surgeon for factories at Downpatrick, stated in his official report, dated 15 December 1865: \"The serious accidents at the scutching mills are of the most fearful nature. In many cases a quarter of the body is torn from the trunk, and either involves death or a future of wretched incapacity and suffering. The increase of mills in the country will of course extend these dreadful results, and it will be a great boon if they are brought under the legislature. I am convinced that by proper supervision of scutching mills a vast sacrifice of life and limb would be averted.\" What could be more characteristic of the capitalist mode of production than the fact that it is necessary, by Act of Parliament, to force upon the capitalists the simplest appliances for maintaining cleanliness and health? In the potteries the Factory Act of 1864 \"has whitewashed and cleansed upwards of 200 workshops, after a period of abstinence from any such cleaning, in many cases of 20 years, and in some entirely\" (this is the \"abstinence\" of the capitalist!), \"in which were employed 27,800 artisans, hitherto breathing through protracted days and often nights of labour, a mephitic atmosphere, and which rendered an otherwise comparatively innocuous occupation, pregnant with disease and death. The Act has improved the ventilation very much.\" At the same time, this part of the Act strikingly demonstrates that the capitalist mode of production, by its very nature, excludes all rational improvement beyond a certain point. The English doctors are unanimous in declaring that where the work is continuous 500 cubic feet is the very smallest space that should be allowed for each person. Now, just as the Factory Acts, owing to their compulsory provisions, indirectly hasten the conversion of small workshops into factories, thus indirectly attacking the proprietary rights of the smaller capitalists, and assuring a monopoly to the big ones, so too, in the same way, thousands of small employers would be expropriated directly, at a single stroke, if it were made obligatory to provide the proper space for each worker in each workshop. This would strike at the very roots of the capitalist mode of production, i.e. the self-valorization of capital, whether on a small or a large scale, by means of the \"free\" purchase and consumption of labour-power. Factory legislation is therefore brought to a dead halt before these 500 cubic feet of breathing space. The health officers, the industrial inquiry commissioners, the factory inspectors, all repeat, over and over again, that it is both necessary for the workers to have these 500 cubic feet, and impossible to impose this rule on capital. They are, in reality, declaring that consumption and the other pulmonary diseases of the workers are conditions necessary to the existence of capital. Marx notes that it has been found out by experiment that with each breath of average intensity taken by an average, healthy individual, about 25 cubic inches of air are consumed, and that about twenty breaths are taken in each minute. Hence the air inhaled in 24 hours by each individual is about 720,000 cubic inches, or 416 cubic feet. It is clear, however, that air which has once been breathed cannot serve again for the same process until it has been purified in the great workshop of nature. According to the experiments of Valentin and Brunner, it appears that a healthy man gives off about 1,300 cubic inches of carbonic acid per hour; this would give about 8 ounces of solid carbon thrown off from the lungs in 24 hours. \"Every man should have at least 800 cubic feet\" (Huxley). Paltry as the education clauses of the Act appear on the whole, they do proclaim that elementary education is a compulsory precondition for the employment of children. The success of those clauses proved for the first time the possibility of combining education and gymnastics with manual labour, and consequently of combining manual labour with education and gymnastics. The factory inspectors soon found out, by questioning the schoolmasters, that the factory children, although they received only one half the education of the regular day students, yet learnt quite as much and often more. \"This can be accounted for by the simple fact that, with only being at school for one half of the day, they are always fresh, and nearly always ready and willing to receive instruction. The system on which they work, half manual labour, and half school, renders each employment a rest and a relief to the other; consequently, both are far more congenial to the child, than would be the case were he kept constantly at one. It is quite clear that a boy who has been at school all the morning, cannot (in hot weather particularly) cope with one who comes fresh and bright from his work.\" Further evidence of this will be found in Senior's speech at the Social Science Congress at Edinburgh in 1863. He shows there, amongst other things, how the monotonous, unproductive and long school day undergone by the children of the upper and middle classes uselessly adds to the labour of the teacher, \"while he not only fruitlessly but absolutely injuriously, wastes the time, health, and energy of the children.\" As Robert Owen has shown us in detail, the germ of the education of the future is present in the factory system; this education will, in the case of every child over a given age, combine productive labour with instruction and gymnastics, not only as one of the methods of adding to the efficiency of production, but as the only method of producing fully developed human beings. Marx notes that large-scale industry sweeps away by technical means the division of labour characteristic of manufacture, under which each man is bound hand and foot for life to a single specialized operation. At the same time, the capitalist form of large-scale industry reproduces this same division of labour in a still more monstrous shape; in the factory proper, by converting the worker into a living appendage of the machine; and everywhere outside the factory by the sporadic use of machinery and machine workers, or by the introduction of the labour of women, children, and unskilled men as a new foundation for the division of labour. The contradiction between the division of labour under manufacture and the essential character of large-scale industry makes itself forcibly felt. It appears, for example, in the frightful fact that a great part of the children employed in modern factories and manufactures are from their earliest years riveted to the most simple manipulations, and exploited for years, without being taught a single kind of skill that would afterwards make them of use, even in the same factory. In the English letter-press printing trade, for example, there formerly existed a system, corresponding to that in the old manufactures and handicrafts, of advancing the apprentices from easy to more and more difficult work. They went through a course of teaching till they were finished printers. To be able to read and write was for every one of them a requirement of their trade. All this was changed by the printing machine. It employs two sorts of worker. On the one hand there are adults, tenters, and on the other hand there are boys, mostly from 11 to 17 years of age, whose sole occupation is either to spread the sheets of paper under the machine, or to take from it the printed sheets. They perform this weary task, in London especially, for 14, 15, and 16 hours at a stretch, during several days in the week, and frequently for 36 hours, with only 2 hours' rest for meals and sleep. A great proportion of them cannot read, and they are, as a rule, \"utter savages and very extraordinary creatures.\" \"To qualify them for the work which they have to do they require no intellectual training; there is little room in it for skill, and less for judgement; their wages, though rather high for boys, do not increase proportionately as they grow up, and the majority of them cannot look for advancement to the better paid and more responsible post of machine minder, because, while each machine has but one minder, it has at least two, and often four boys attached to it.\" As soon as they get too old for such children's work, that is at about 17 years old, at the latest, they are discharged from the printing establishments. They become recruits for crime. Attempts to procure them employment elsewhere come to grief owing to their ignorance and brutality, their mental and bodily degradation. What is true of the division of labour within the workshop under the system of manufacture is also true of the division of labour within society. As long as handicrafts and manufacture form the universal basis of social production, the subjection of the producer to a single branch, the breaking-up of the originally multifarious parts of his employment, is a necessary aspect of the process of development. It is on that basis that each separate branch of production acquires its technically appropriate shape empirically, and slowly perfects it. Then, as soon as a particular degree of maturity has been reached, it rapidly crystallizes. Once this has happened, the only thing that here and there gives rise to a change, apart from the provision of new materials for labour through the medium of trade, is the gradual alteration of the instruments of labour. But their form, too, once it has been definitively laid down by experience, undergoes a process of petrifaction, as is proved by their frequent transmission from one generation to another, unaltered, through thousands of years. It is characteristic of this situation that, right down to the eighteenth century, the different trades were called \"mysteries,\" into whose secrets none but those initiated by their profession and their practical experience could penetrate. Large-scale industry tore aside the veil that concealed from men their own social process of production and turned the various spontaneously divided branches of production into riddles, not only to outsiders but even to the initiated. Its principle, which is to view each process of production in and for itself, and to resolve it into its constituent elements without looking first at the ability of the human hand to perform the new processes, brought into existence the whole of the modern science of technology. The varied, apparently unconnected and petrified forms of the social production process were now dissolved into conscious and planned applications of natural science, divided up systematically in accordance with the particular useful effect aimed at in each case. Modern industry never views or treats the existing form of a production process as the definitive one. Its technical basis is therefore revolutionary, whereas all earlier modes of production were essentially conservative. By means of machinery, chemical processes, and other methods, it is continually transforming not only the technical basis of production but also the functions of the worker and the social combinations of the labour process. At the same time, it thereby also revolutionizes the division of labour within society, and incessantly throws masses of capital and of workers from one branch of production to another. Thus large-scale industry, by its very nature, necessitates variation of labour, fluidity of functions, and mobility of the worker in all directions . But on the other hand, in its capitalist form it reproduces the old division of labour with its ossified particularities. We have seen how this absolute contradiction does away with all repose, all fixity, and all security as far as the worker's life-situation is concerned; how it constantly threatens, by taking away the instruments of labour, to snatch from his hands the means of subsistence, and, by suppressing his specialized function, to make him superfluous. We have seen, too, how this contradiction bursts forth without restraint in the ceaseless human sacrifices required from the working class, in the reckless squandering of labour-powers, and in the devastating effects of social anarchy. This is the negative side. But if, at present, variation of labour imposes itself after the manner of an overpowering natural law, and with the blindly destructive action of a natural law that meets with obstacles everywhere, large-scale industry, through its very catastrophes, makes the recognition of variation of labour and hence of the fitness of the worker for the maximum number of different kinds of labour into a question of life and death. This possibility of varying labour must become a general law of social production, and the existing relations must be adapted to permit its realization in practice. That monstrosity, the disposable working population held in reserve, in misery, for the changing requirements of capitalist exploitation, must be replaced by the individual man who is absolutely available for the different kinds of labour required of him; the partially developed individual, who is merely the bearer of one specialized social function, must be replaced by the totally developed individual, for whom the different social functions are different modes of activity he takes up in turn. Marx identifies two aspects of this transformative process that have developed spontaneously from the foundation provided by large-scale industry: the establishment of technical and agricultural schools , and the foundation of schools for vocational teaching , in which the children of the workers receive a certain amount of instruction in technology and in the practical handling of the various implements of labour. Though the Factory Act, that first and meagre concession wrung from capital, is limited to combining elementary education with work in the factory, there can be no doubt that, with the inevitable conquest of political power by the working class, technological education, both theoretical and practical, will take its proper place in the schools of the workers. There is also no doubt that those revolutionary ferments whose goal is the abolition of the old division of labour stand in diametrical contradiction with the capitalist form of production, and the economic situation of the workers which corresponds to that form. However, the development of the contradictions of a given historical form of production is the only historical way in which it can be dissolved and then reconstructed on a new basis. \"Ne sutor ultra crepidam,\" a phrase which was the absolute summit of handicraft wisdom, became sheer nonsense from the moment the watchmaker Watt invented the steam-engine, the barber Arkwright the throstle, and the jeweller Fulton the steamship. Marx notes that John Bellers, \"a veritable phenomenon in the history of political economy,\" already saw very clearly, at the end of the seventeenth century, the need to abolish the present system of education and division of labour, which gives rise to hypertrophy and atrophy at the two opposite extremities of society. Amongst other things he says: \"An idle learning being little better than the learning of idleness... Bodily labour, it's a primitive institution of God... Labour being as proper for the bodies' health as eating is for its living; for what pains a man saves by ease, he will find in disease... Labour adds oil to the lamp of life, when thinking inflames it... A childish silly employ leaves the children's minds silly.\" As long as factory legislation is confined to regulating the labour done in factories, etc., it is regarded only as an interference with capital's rights of exploitation. But when it comes to regulating so-called \"domestic labour,\" this is immediately viewed as a direct attack on the patria potestas, or, in modern terms, parental authority. The tender-hearted English Parliament long affected to shrink from taking this step. The power of facts, however, at last compelled it to acknowledge that large-scale industry, in overturning the economic foundation of the old family system, and the family labour corresponding to it, had also dissolved the old family relationships. The rights of the children had to be proclaimed. The concluding report of the Children's Employment Commission, published in 1866, says: \"It is, unhappily, to a painful degree apparent throughout the whole of the evidence, that against no persons do the children of both sexes so much require protection as against their parents.\" The system of unlimited exploitation of children's labour in general and so-called domestic labour in particular is \"maintained only because the parents are able, without check or control, to exercise this arbitrary and mischievous power over their young and tender offspring... Parents must not possess the absolute power of making their children mere machines to earn so much weekly wage... The children and young persons, therefore, in all such cases may justifiably claim from the legislature, as a natural right, that an exemption should be secured to them, from what destroys prematurely their physical strength, and lowers them in the scale of intellectual and moral beings.\" Marx comments: It was not however the misuse of parental power that created the direct or indirect exploitation of immature labour-powers by capital, but rather the opposite, i.e. the capitalist mode of exploitation, by sweeping away the economic foundation which corresponded to parental power, made the use of parental power into its misuse. However terrible and disgusting the dissolution of the old family ties within the capitalist system may appear, large-scale industry, by assigning an important part in socially organized processes of production, outside the sphere of the domestic economy, to women, young persons, and children of both sexes, does nevertheless create a new economic foundation for a higher form of the family and of relations between the sexes. It is of course just as absurd to regard the Christian-Germanic form of the family as absolute and final as it would have been in the case of the ancient Roman, the ancient Greek, or the Oriental forms, which, moreover, form a series in historical development. It is also obvious that the fact that the collective working group is composed of individuals of both sexes and all ages must under the appropriate conditions turn into a source of humane development, although in its spontaneously developed, brutal, capitalist form, the system works in the opposite direction, and becomes a pestiferous source of corruption and slavery, since here the worker exists for the process of production, and not the process of production for the worker. The necessity for a generalization of the Factory Acts, for transforming them from exceptional laws relating to mechanical spinning and weaving—those first creations of machinery—into the general law for all social production, arose from the path of historical development taken by large-scale industry. For, in its wake, the traditional shape of manufacture, handicrafts, and domestic industry is entirely revolutionized; manufactures are constantly passing over into the factory system, and handicrafts into manufactures; and, at the end, the spheres of handicrafts and domestic industry become, in what is relatively an amazingly short time, dens of misery where capitalist exploitation is given free rein to commit the most frightful iniquities. There are two circumstances which finally turn the scale: first, the constantly recurring experience that as soon as capital is subjected to state control, even at a handful of points on the periphery of society, it seeks compensation all the more unrestrainedly at all other points ; and second, the cry of the capitalists for equality in the conditions of competition, i.e. for equality of restraint on the exploitation of labour. Marx provides examples. Messrs. W. Cooksley of Bristol, nail and chain manufacturers, voluntarily introduced the regulations of the Factory Acts into their business. \"As the old irregular system prevails in neighbouring works, the Messrs. Cooksley are subject to the disadvantage of having their boys enticed to continue their labour elsewhere after 6 p.m. 'This', they naturally say, 'is an injustice and loss to us, as it exhausts a portion of the boy's strength of which we ought to have the full benefit.'\" Mr. J. Simpson (paper box and bagmaker, of London) stated before the commissioners of the Children's Employment Commission: \"He would sign any petition for it\" (legislative intervention)... \"As it was, he always felt restless at night, when he had closed his place, lest others should be working later than him and getting away his orders.\" Summing up, the commissioners declare: \"It would be unjust to the larger employers that their factories should be placed under regulation, while the hours of labour in the small places in their own branch of business were under no legislative restriction. And to the injustice arising from the unfair conditions of competition, in regard to hours, that would be created if the smaller places of work were exempt, would be added the disadvantage to the larger manufacturers of finding their supply of juvenile and female labour drawn off to the places of work exempt from legislation. Further, a stimulus would be given to the multiplication of the smaller places of work, which are almost invariably the least favourable to the health, comfort, education, and general improvement of the people.\" In its concluding report, the Children's Employment Commission proposes to subject to the Factory Act more than 1,400,000 children, young persons, and women, of whom about one-half are exploited in small industries and in so-called domestic labour. The branches they propose to regulate include lace-making, stocking-weaving, straw-plaiting, the manufacture of wearing apparel with its numerous subdivisions, artificial flower-making, shoe-making, hat-making, glove-making, tailoring, all metal works from blast-furnaces down to needle factories, paper-mills, glass-works, tobacco factories, india-rubber works, and many others. The Tory government announced on 5 February 1867, in the Speech from the Throne, that it had incorporated the proposals of the Children's Employment Commission into Bills. To get that far, \"an extra twenty years of experimenta in corpore vili had been required.\" Already in 1840 a Parliamentary Commission of Inquiry into child labour had been appointed. Its report, issued in 1842, unfolded, in the words of Nassau W. Senior, \"the most frightful picture of avarice, selfishness and cruelty on the part of masters and parents, and of juvenile and infantile misery, degradation and destruction ever presented... It may be supposed that it describes the horrors of a past age. But there is unhappily evidence that those horrors continue as intense as they were.\" The Factory Acts Extension Act received the royal assent on 15 August 1867, the Workshops Regulation Act on 21 August 1867; the first Act regulated the large industries, the second the small. The Factory Acts Extension Act applies to blast-furnaces, iron and copper mills, foundries, machine shops, metal works, gutta-percha works, paper-mills, glass-works, tobacco factories, letter-press printing works, book-binding works—in short, to all industrial establishments of this kind in which fifty individuals or more are occupied simultaneously, and for not less than 100 days a year. The Workshops Regulation Act defines its terms: - \"Handicraft shall mean any manual labour exercised by way of trade, or for purposes of gain in, or incidental to, the making any article or part of an article, or in, or incidental to, the altering, repairing, ornamenting, finishing, or otherwise adapting for sale any article.\" - \"Workshop shall mean any room or place whatever in the open air or under cover, in which any handicraft is carried on by any child, young person, or woman, and to which and over which the person by whom such child, young person, or woman is employed, has the right of access and control.\" - \"Employed shall mean occupied in any handicraft, whether for wages or not, under a master or under a parent as herein defined.\" - \"Parent shall mean parent, guardian, or person, having the custody of, or control over, any... child or young person.\" The Factory Acts Extension Act, which affects the large establishments, regresses from the Factory Act by a mass of vicious exceptions and cowardly compromises with the masters. The Workshops Regulation Act, wretched as far as its detailed provisions were concerned, remained a dead letter in the hands of the municipal and local authorities who were charged with its execution. When in 1871, Parliament withdrew this power from them, in order to confer it on the factory inspectors, to whose province it thus added at a single stroke more than 100,000 workshops, as well as 300 brickworks, care was taken at the same time not to add more than eight assistants to their already under-manned staff. What strikes us, then, in the English legislation of 1867, is, on the one hand, the necessity imposed on the Parliament of the ruling classes of adopting, in principle, such extraordinary and extensive measures against the excesses of capitalist exploitation; and, on the other hand, the hesitation, the unwillingness and the bad faith with which it actually put these measures into practice. The 1862 Inquiry Commission also proposed a renewed regulation of the mining industry, an industry distinguished from all others by the fact that in it the interests of the landowner and the capitalist coincide. The antagonism between these two interests had been favourable to factory legislation, and the absence of that antagonism is sufficient to explain the delays and the chicanery surrounding the legislation on mines. The 1840 Inquiry Commission had made revelations so terrible and shocking, and created such a scandal throughout Europe, that to salve its conscience Parliament passed the Mining Act of 1842. But this did no more than forbid the employment underground of children under 10 years of age and of females. Then, in 1860, there came the Mines Inspection Act, which provides that mines shall be inspected by public officials appointed specially for that purpose, and that boys between the ages of 10 and 12 years shall not be employed, unless they have a school certificate, or go to school for a certain number of hours. This Act was a complete dead letter, owing to the ridiculously small number of inspectors, the meagreness of their powers, and other causes. One of the most recent Blue Books on mines is the Report from the Select Committee on Mines, together with... Evidence, 23 July 1866. This is the work of a Parliamentary Committee selected from members of the House of Commons, and authorized to summon and examine witnesses. It is a thick folio volume, but the Report itself occupies only five lines, to this effect: the committee has nothing to say, and more witnesses must be examined! The mode of examining the witnesses reminds one of the cross-examination of witnesses in English courts of justice, where the advocate tries, by means of impudent, confusing, and unexpected questions, to intimidate and confound the witness, and to give a forced meaning to the answers thus extorted. In this inquiry the members of the committee themselves are the cross-examiners, and among them are to be found both mine-owners and mine-exploiters; the witnesses are mining workers, mostly coal miners. Marx provides extensive extracts from this Report, classified under headings: Employment in mines of boys of 10 years and upwards. In the mines the work, including the obligatory journey to and from the mine, usually lasts 14 or 15 hours, in exceptional cases even longer, from 3, 4, and 5 a.m. until 5 and 6 p.m. in the evening. The adults work in two shifts of 8 hours each; but there is no such alternation with the boys, on account of the expense. The younger boys are chiefly employed in opening and shutting the ventilating doors in the various parts of the mine; the older ones are employed on heavier work, in carrying coal, etc. They work these long hours underground until their 18th or 22nd year, when they are put to miner's work proper. Children and young persons are at present worse treated and harder worked than at any previous period. The miners almost unanimously demand an Act of Parliament prohibiting the employment of children under 14 in mines. Education. The mining workers want a law for the compulsory education of their children, as in factories. They declare that the clauses of the Act of 1860 which require a school certificate to be obtained before employing boys of 10 and 12 years of age are quite illusory. \"The majority of the men themselves cannot read.\" Employment of women. Since 1842, women are no longer employed underground, but occupied on the surface in loading the coal, etc., in drawing the tubs to the canals and railway wagons, in sorting, etc. The numbers have increased considerably during the past three or four years. They are mostly the wives, daughters, and widows of colliers, and their ages range from 12 to 50 or 60 years. The miners generally condemn the employment of women as \"degrading to the sex.\" The secret of the bourgeois' \"sympathy\" for widows and poor families emerges: \"The coal proprietor appoints certain gentlemen to take the oversight of the workings, and it is their policy, in order to receive approbation, to place things on the most economical basis they can, and these girls are employed at from 1s. up to 1s. 6d. a day, where a man at the rate of 2s. 6d. a day would have to be employed.\" Coroner's Juries. The workmen have no confidence in the proceedings at coroner's inquests when accidents occur. The men who are generally chosen are men who know nothing about mines. Workmen are never summoned except as witnesses. The juries are generally tradesmen in the neighbourhood who are sometimes liable to be influenced by their employers—the owners of the works. False weights and measures. The workers demand to be paid by the week instead of by the fortnight, and by weight instead of by the cubic content of the tubs; they also demand protection against the use of false weights. Inspection of mines. The workers complain of the bad ventilation of the collieries. Men are afraid to apply to the inspector because there have been cases of men being sacrificed and losing their employment in consequence of applying. In 1865 there were 3,217 coal mines in Great Britain, and twelve inspectors. A Yorkshire mine-owner himself calculates that, leaving aside the purely bureaucratic activities which absorb the whole of their time, each mine can be visited only once in ten years by an inspector. No wonder mining disasters have increased progressively, both in number and extent (sometimes with a loss of 200 to 300 men) during the last ten years. These are the beauties of \"free\" capitalist production! Defective as it is, the Act passed in 1872 is the first that regulates the hours of labour of children employed in mines, and makes exploiters and owners, to a certain extent, responsible for so-called accidents. The Royal Commission appointed in 1867 to inquire into the employment in agriculture of children, young persons, and women has published some very important reports. Several attempts have been made to apply the principles of the Factory Acts, in a modified form, to agriculture, but so far these attempts have failed totally. However, all Marx wishes to point out here is that there exists an irresistible tendency towards the general application of those principles. If the general extension of factory legislation to all trades for the purpose of protecting the working class both in mind and body has become inevitable, on the other hand, as we have already pointed out, that extension hastens on the general conversion of numerous isolated small industries into a few combined industries carried on upon a large scale; it therefore accelerates the concentration of capital and the exclusive predominance of the factory system. It destroys both the ancient and the transitional forms behind which the dominion of capital is still partially hidden, and replaces them with a dominion which is direct and unconcealed. But by doing this it also generalizes the direct struggle against its rule. While in each individual workshop it enforces uniformity, regularity, order, and economy, the result of the immense impetus given to technical improvement by the limitation and regulation of the working day is to increase the anarchy and the proneness to catastrophe of capitalist production as a whole, the intensity of labour, and the competition of machinery with the worker. By the destruction of small-scale and domestic industries it destroys the last resorts of the \"redundant population,\" thereby removing what was previously a safety-valve for the whole social mechanism. By maturing the material conditions and the social combination of the process of production, it matures the contradictions and antagonisms of the capitalist form of that process, and thereby ripens both the elements for forming a new society and the forces tending towards the overthrow of the old one. Marx notes that Robert Owen, \"who was the father of the co-operative factories and stores, but who... in no way shared the illusions of his followers about the field of effectiveness of these isolated elements of transformation, not only made the factory system in practice the sole foundation of his experiments, but also declared that system to be theoretically the point of departure for the social revolution.\" Large-Scale Industry and Agriculture Section 10, the chapter's final section, examines the revolution called forth by large-scale industry in agriculture and in the social relations of agricultural producers. If the use of machinery in agriculture is for the most part free from the injurious physical effect it has on the factory worker, its effect in making workers \"redundant\" is more intense and comes up against less resistance. In the counties of Cambridgeshire and Suffolk, for example, the area of cultivated land has been much extended within the last twenty years, while in the same period the rural population has diminished, not only relatively, but absolutely. In the United States of America the workers are as yet only virtually replaced by agricultural machinery, i.e. the machines allow the producer to cultivate a larger area, but do not actually expel any agricultural labourers employed. In 1861 the number of persons occupied in England and Wales in the manufacture of agricultural machinery was 1,034, while the number of agricultural labourers employed in using agricultural machinery and steam-engines was only 1,205. In the sphere of agriculture, large-scale industry has a more revolutionary effect than elsewhere, for the reason that it annihilates the bulwark of the old society, the \"peasant,\" and substitutes for him the wage-labourer. Thus the need for social transformation, and the antagonism of the classes, reaches the same level in the countryside as it has attained in the towns. A conscious, technological application of science replaces the previous highly irrational and slothfully traditional way of working. The capitalist mode of production completes the disintegration of the primitive familial union which bound agriculture and manufacture together when they were both at an undeveloped and childlike stage. But at the same time it creates the material conditions for a new and higher synthesis, a union of agriculture and industry on the basis of the forms that have developed during the period of their antagonistic isolation. Capitalist production collects the population together in great centres, and causes the urban population to achieve an ever-growing preponderance. This has two results. On the one hand it concentrates the historical motive power of society; on the other hand, it disturbs the metabolic interaction between man and the earth, i.e. it prevents the return to the soil of its constituent elements consumed by man in the form of food and clothing; hence it hinders the operation of the eternal natural condition for the lasting fertility of the soil. Thus it destroys at the same time the physical health of the urban worker and the intellectual life of the rural worker. But by destroying the circumstances surrounding that metabolism, which originated in a merely natural and spontaneous fashion, it compels its systematic restoration as a regulative law of social production, and in a form adequate to the full development of the human race. In agriculture, as in manufacture, the capitalist transformation of the process of production also appears as a martyrology for the producer; the instrument of labour appears as a means of enslaving, exploiting, and impoverishing the worker; the social combination of labour processes appears as an organized suppression of his individual vitality, freedom, and autonomy. The dispersal of the rural workers over large areas breaks their power of resistance, while concentration increases that of the urban workers. In modern agriculture, as in urban industry, the increase in the productivity and the mobility of labour is purchased at the cost of laying waste and debilitating labour-power itself. Moreover, all progress in capitalist agriculture is a progress in the art, not only of robbing the worker, but of robbing the soil; all progress in increasing the fertility of the soil for a given time is a progress towards ruining the more long-lasting sources of that fertility. The more a country proceeds from large-scale industry as the background of its development, as in the case of the United States, the more rapid is this process of destruction. Capitalist production, therefore, only develops the techniques and the degree of combination of the social process of production by simultaneously undermining the original sources of all wealth—the soil and the worker. Marx quotes David Urquhart: \"You divide the people into two hostile camps of clownish boors and emasculated dwarfs. Good heavens! a nation divided into agricultural and commercial interests, calling itself sane; nay, styling itself enlightened and civilized, not only in spite of, but in consequence of this monstrous and unnatural division.\" Marx comments that this passage demonstrates both the strengths and the weaknesses of the kind of criticism which knows how to judge and condemn the present, but not how to comprehend it. Marx references Liebig's Die Chemie in ihrer Anwendung auf Agricultur und Physiologie (7th edn, 1862), especially the \"Introduction to the Natural Laws of Agriculture\" in Volume I. To have developed from the point of view of natural science the negative, i.e. destructive, side of modern agriculture, is one of Liebig's immortal merits. Marx notes, however, that Liebig makes erroneous attributions regarding the law of diminishing returns, incorrectly crediting John Stuart Mill with a theory first published by James Anderson in the days of Adam Smith, and subsequently developed by Malthus, West, Ricardo, and James Mill. Marx comments that \"the second Mill owes his certainly 'remarkable' authority almost entirely to such mistaken attributions.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles and Systemic Laws Revealed Chapter 15 of Capital reveals several fundamental principles that operate as systemic laws of the capitalist mode of production. First, the principle of the dialectical inversion of machinery : the same instrument that could liberate humanity from toil—shortening the working day, lightening labour, increasing the wealth of producers—becomes under capitalist control the most powerful means for lengthening the working day, intensifying labour, and impoverishing the masses. Marx captures this in his observation that machinery \"sweeps away every moral and natural restriction on the length of the working day\" and becomes \"the most unfailing means for turning the whole lifetime of the worker and his family into labour-time at capital's disposal for its own valorization.\" This is not an accidental or contingent outcome but a necessary result of the machine's subsumption under the capital-relation. The machine, as constant capital, is animated by the capitalist's will to extract surplus-value; its technical capacities are thereby subordinated to the drive for profit, not the satisfaction of human need. Second, the principle of the immanent contradiction in machine production : machinery creates a contradiction within the valorization process itself. Surplus-value arises only from the variable part of capital, from living labour. Yet machinery, by displacing living labour with constant capital, diminishes the number of workers employed by a given amount of capital. The rate of surplus-value can be increased only by diminishing the number of workers exploited. This contradiction drives the capitalist to compensate for the relative decrease in worker numbers by intensifying exploitation—prolonging the working day (before legal limitation) and intensifying labour (after legal limitation). The capitalist is thus driven \"without his being aware of the fact\" to the most ruthless and excessive prolongation of the working day. This contradiction, Marx notes, will be further developed in Volume 3 in the analysis of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall. Third, the principle of the industrial reserve army : machinery, by throwing workers out of employment, creates a surplus working population that is compelled to submit to the dictates of capital. This reserve army serves as a lever for the intensification of exploitation—it keeps wages down, disciplines the employed workers, and provides a pool of labour for the expansion of production during periods of prosperity. The \"temporary inconvenience\" of displacement is permanent in its effects, since machinery is continually seizing on new fields of production and continually revolutionizing the technical basis of existing industries. The worker's life-situation is characterized by \"constant repulsion and attraction,\" by being \"slung backwards and forwards,\" with no security, no fixity, no repose. Fourth, the principle of the revolutionary character of large-scale industry : modern industry never treats the existing form of the production process as definitive. Its technical basis is revolutionary, whereas all earlier modes of production were conservative. By means of machinery and chemical processes, it continually transforms not only the technical basis of production but also the functions of the worker and the social combinations of the labour process. It thereby revolutionizes the division of labour within society and incessantly throws masses of capital and workers from one branch of production to another. This revolutionary character necessitates variation of labour, fluidity of functions, and mobility of the worker in all directions. But in its capitalist form, it reproduces the old division of labour with its ossified particularities, creating an absolute contradiction that \"does away with all repose, all fixity, and all security as far as the worker's life-situation is concerned.\" Fifth, the principle of the metabolic rift : capitalist production \"disturbs the metabolic interaction between man and the earth\"—it prevents the return to the soil of its constituent elements consumed by man in the form of food and clothing, thereby hindering \"the operation of the eternal natural condition for the lasting fertility of the soil.\" Moreover, \"all progress in capitalist agriculture is a progress in the art, not only of robbing the worker, but of robbing the soil.\" Capitalist production \"only develops the techniques and the degree of combination of the social process of production by simultaneously undermining the original sources of all wealth—the soil and the worker.\" This insight, developed in the chapter's final pages, anticipates the ecological critique of capitalism that has become increasingly central to contemporary Marxist analysis. Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications The implications of Chapter 15 extend far beyond the specific historical period Marx analyzes. Theoretically , the chapter completes Marx's analysis of relative surplus-value and establishes the material foundations for the theory of accumulation developed in subsequent parts of Capital . It demonstrates that the capitalist mode of production is not a static system but a dynamic, revolutionary one that constantly transforms its own material basis. The factory system, based on machinery, is not merely one form of production among others but the characteristic form of capitalist production, the form that gives the capitalist mode of production its distinctive historical character. Practically , the chapter provides a devastating critique of the \"compensation theory\" and the broader apologetics of bourgeois political economy. Marx demonstrates that machinery under capitalism does not compensate displaced workers but creates a permanent surplus population. The workers thrown out of employment by machinery are not \"set free\" capital that will re-employ them; they are thrown onto the labour market, where their presence depresses wages and intensifies the exploitation of those still employed. The \"temporary inconvenience\" of displacement is permanent in its effects, since machinery continually revolutionizes production and continually displaces labour. This critique remains relevant today, as capitalist globalization continues to displace workers, create surplus populations, and intensify exploitation. The chapter also provides a powerful analysis of the role of the state in regulating capitalist production. The Factory Acts, Marx shows, were the \"first conscious and methodical reaction of society against the spontaneously developed form of its production process.\" They represent a concession wrung from capital by the struggle of the working class. But Marx also shows the limits of such legislation: the health clauses are \"extremely meagre,\" the education clauses are made \"illusory\" by the absence of administrative machinery, and the Factory Act is \"brought to a dead halt before these 500 cubic feet of breathing space.\" The state, under capitalism, can regulate the worst excesses of exploitation but cannot abolish exploitation itself. As long as the capital-relation persists, the machine will continue to be used as a means for extracting surplus-value. Philosophically , the chapter raises profound questions about the relationship between technology and human freedom. Marx's analysis is not a critique of machinery as such but of its capitalist application. He envisions a society in which \"the individual man who is absolutely available for the different kinds of labour required of him\" replaces \"the disposable working population held in reserve, in misery, for the changing requirements of capitalist exploitation\"; in which \"the totally developed individual, for whom the different social functions are different modes of activity he takes up in turn,\" replaces \"the partially developed individual, who is merely the bearer of one specialized social function.\" This vision of human emancipation through the abolition of the old division of labour is grounded in the material possibilities created by large-scale industry itself. The factory system, Marx argues, contains \"the germ of the education of the future\"—an education that will combine productive labour with instruction and gymnastics, not only as a method of increasing production but \"as the only method of producing fully developed human beings.\" The chapter thus ends on a note of revolutionary optimism that does not ignore the horrors of capitalist industrialization but sees in those horrors the material conditions for their own transcendence. Large-scale industry, by maturing \"the material conditions and the social combination of the process of production,\" matures \"the contradictions and antagonisms of the capitalist form of that process,\" and thereby \"ripens both the elements for forming a new society and the forces tending towards the overthrow of the old one.\" The machine, which under capitalism has been the instrument of the worker's enslavement, contains within itself the possibility of the worker's liberation. The task of the working class is to seize the machine and place it in the service of humanity rather than profit. The chapter's enduring significance lies in its demonstration that the most powerful instruments of production humanity has ever created can serve either as means of domination or means of liberation—and that the choice between these alternatives is not technical but social, not given by the nature of the machine but determined by the relations of production within which it operates. Marx's analysis of machinery and large-scale industry thus provides not only a historical account of the industrial revolution but a permanent framework for understanding the relationship between technology, labour, and human freedom—a framework that remains indispensable for anyone seeking to comprehend the dynamics of contemporary capitalism and the possibilities for its transformation. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 14 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 16"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 16", "title": "Chapter 16", "subtitle": "Absolute and Relative Surplus-Value", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 16/Chapter 16  Absolute and Relative Surplus-Value_explanation.html", "word_count": 6221, "read_time": "31 min read", "tags": ["Surplus-Value & Exploitation", "Subsumption of Labour (Formal vs Real)"], "keywords": ["Absolute and Relative Surplus-Value Interaction", "Natural Conditions of Labour Productivity", "Subsumption of Labour under Capital", "Productive Labour Defined", "Absolute Working Day Limits"], "text": "Chapter 16 Absolute And Relative Surplus-Value - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,170 words ⏱️ ~31 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 16 of Capital , Volume I, bears the same title as Part Five which it opens: The Production of Absolute and Relative Surplus-Value . That doubling is not redundant but programmatic. Having spent Part III dissecting absolute surplus-value (Chapters 7–11, culminating in the titanic historical sweep on the working day) and Part IV dissecting relative surplus-value (Chapters 12–15, on co-operation, manufacture, machinery and large-scale industry), Marx must now demonstrate that his readers have not been presented with two species of surplus-value, but two moments of a single dynamic . The chapter is therefore a hinge—a transitional synthesis that welds the two halves of the analytic framework into one dialectical unity and prepares the ground for the discussion of wages (Part VI) and accumulation (Part VII). The chapter's core thesis is fourfold. First, the very category \"productive labour\" is not a technical description of work but a historically determined social relation : the worker is productive only because he produces surplus-value for capital, a relation which \"stamps the worker as capital's direct means of valorization\" and makes productivity \"not a piece of luck, but a misfortune.\" Second, absolute surplus-value is the general foundation of the capitalist system and the presupposition of relative surplus-value; the latter does not replace the former but is built upon it, presupposing a working day already divided into necessary and surplus labour. Third, the apparent logical identity of the two forms—each contains the other— dissolves once we track the movement of surplus-value : once the capitalist mode of production has become universal, the difference becomes practical and forceful whenever the rate of surplus-value is to be raised, and the two paths (prolonging the day, or shortening the necessary component) are sharply distinct. Fourth, and most polemically, the origin of surplus-value lies not in nature, not in productivity as such, but in a specific historical social relation; nature supplies only the possibility of surplus labour, never its reality . This fourth thesis is what allows Marx to expose Ricardo's evasion, the Mercantilists' error, and Mill's \"wretched evasions.\" The intellectual objective of the chapter is thus threefold: to consolidate the theoretical result of the previous two Parts; to establish the natural and historical presuppositions of the surplus-labour relation so as to deny it any \"occult\" or trans-historical status; and, with these weapons in hand, to open the long subterranean polemic against bourgeois political economy that culminates in Theories of Surplus-Value . In the broader architecture of Capital , the chapter is both an overture to Part Five's remaining chapters (on changes in the magnitude of the price of labour-power and on the various formulae for the rate of surplus-value) and a decisive step toward the theory of the wage and the theory of accumulation. The brief invocation of the concepts of formal and real subsumption —with the footnote pointing forward to Marx's separate treatment of that topic—quietly announces the historical typology that will structure the whole account of capitalist development. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Return to Productive Labour: The Abstract Labour Process versus the Capitalist Process Marx opens by reaching back to his earlier, deliberately abstract handling of the labour process, \"independently of its historical forms, as a process between man and nature.\" The reader is reminded of a formula already offered: viewed from the standpoint of its result , the product, \"both the instruments of labour and the object of labour are means of production, and that the labour itself is productive labour.\" This is the pivot on which the chapter turns. In the earlier analysis—the exposition of the simple labour process—Marx had already salted the argument with a caveat, relegated to a note: \"This method of determining what is productive labour, from the standpoint of the simple labour process, is by no means sufficient to cover the capitalist process of production.\" Chapter 16 openly declares that \"we must now develop this point further.\" The methodological point is subtle and deserves emphasis. The original definition of productive labour was an abstraction—an analytical convenience, not a historical truth. Marx treats labour \"in the abstract\" not because labour ever existed in such form, but because the simple labour process is a necessary mental isolation of one aspect of reality: the relation between man and nature. The chapter's opening move is to break down that abstraction. The repetition of the abstraction was necessary; now its inadequacy must be exposed. 2.2 The Collective Labourer: Extension of the Concept of Productive Labour The first move in that breakdown is the concept of the collective labourer . In \"purely individual\" labour, the worker \"unites in himself all the functions that later on become separated.\" The solitary producer supervises his own activity; under developed social production, \"he is supervised by others.\" Marx draws an anatomical analogy: \"Just as head and hand belong together in the system of nature, so in the labour process mental and physical labour are united. Later on they become separate; and this separation develops into a hostile antagonism.\" Note that phrase \"hostile antagonism\"—it registers a claim that will not be cashed in fully here but that anticipates the whole argument on manufacture and machinery. The separation of mental from physical labour is not a neutral division of tasks; it is a relation of opposition , which, under capitalism, is realized in the subordination of the detail worker to the direction of capital. The consequence is a broadening of the concept. The product is no longer the direct product of the individual producer but \"a social product, the joint product of a collective labourer, i.e. a combination of workers, each of whom stands at a different distance from the actual manipulation of the object of labour.\" With the cooperative character of the labour process thus accentuated, \"it is no longer necessary for the individual himself to put his hand to the object; it is sufficient for him to be an organ of the collective labourer, and to perform any one of its subordinate functions.\" Marx is explicit about the principled status of this extension: the original definition \"remains correct for the collective labourer, considered as a whole,\" but \"it no longer holds good for each member taken individually.\" 2.3 The Narrowing: Productive Labour as Surplus-Value Production At precisely the point where the concept has been broadened, Marx narrows it again—this time on a wholly different axis. \"Yet the concept of productive labour also becomes narrower. Capitalist production is not merely the production of commodities, it is, by its very essence, the production of surplus-value.\" The worker \"produces not for himself, but for capital.\" Therefore \"it is no longer sufficient ... for him simply to produce. He must produce surplus-value. The only worker who is productive is one who produces surplus-value for the capitalist, or in other words contributes towards the self-valorization of capital.\" This is the operative definition of the chapter. To ram home its universality, Marx deploys the celebrated schoolmaster analogy: \"a schoolmaster is a productive worker when, in addition to belabouring the heads of his pupils, he works himself into the ground to enrich the owner of the school. That the latter has laid out his capital in a teaching factory, instead of a sausage factory, makes no difference to the relation.\" The sphere of material production is thus shown to be a red herring. The distinguishing mark of productive labour is not the useful effect of the work, not the content of the product, but a \"specifically social relation of production, a relation with a historical origin which stamps the worker as capital's direct means of valorization.\" This is a definition that is simultaneously an indictment : \"To be a productive worker is therefore not a piece of luck, but a misfortune.\" The irony is bitter but precise—the term \"productive\" is a reward the worker earns only by producing his own subordination. 2.4 Classical Political Economy and the Historical Determination of the Concept Marx concludes the concept's genealogy with a historical point. In the appendix volume on the history of theory—Volume 4, which he announces he will write (and which his manuscripts of 1862–63 in fact constitute; the editorial note tells us the work was published posthumously by Kautsky between 1905 and 1910, and first translated into English in three parts between 1963 and 1972 as Theories of Surplus-Value )—he will show that \"the classical political economists always made the production of surplus-value the distinguishing characteristic of the productive worker.\" Their definition is thus internally variable : \"Hence their definition of a productive worker varies with their conception of the nature of surplus-value.\" Marx illustrates with the Physiocrats , who \"insist that only agricultural labour is productive, since that alone, they say, yields a surplus-value. For the Physiocrats, indeed, surplus-value exists exclusively in the form of ground rent.\" The insight is that the concept of productive labour is not merely social-historically determined; even the theory of it shifts with the theory of surplus-value. 2.5 The Two Forms Defined The definitions proper now appear, and Marx's phrasing is exact. Absolute surplus-value is \"the prolongation of the working day beyond the point at which the worker would have produced an exact equivalent for the value of his labour-power, and the appropriation of that surplus labour by capital.\" It \"forms the general foundation of the capitalist system, and the starting-point for the production of relative surplus-value.\" Relative surplus-value , by contrast, \"presupposes that the working day is already divided into two parts, necessary labour and surplus labour. In order to prolong the surplus labour, the necessary labour is shortened by methods for producing the equivalent of the wage of labour in a shorter time.\" The distinguishing feature of the two processes is captured in a single, lapidary contrast: \"The production of absolute surplus-value turns exclusively on the length of the working day, whereas the production of relative surplus-value completely revolutionizes the technical processes of labour and the groupings into which society is divided.\" The first is extensive , requiring no transformation of the labour process; the second is intensive and revolutionary , transforming the mode of production itself. 2.6 Formal and Real Subsumption From this contrast Marx derives a historical typology. Relative surplus-value \"requires a specifically capitalist mode of production, a mode of production which, along with its methods, means and conditions, arises and develops spontaneously on the basis of the formal subsumption [ Subsumtion ] of labour under capital. This formal subsumption is then replaced by a real subsumption.\" The footnote directs the reader \"see below, pp. 1019–38, for Marx's own exposition of the concepts of formal and real subsumption\"—a future appendix ( Results of the Immediate Process of Production ), in which the full argument was later elaborated. Formal subsumption is the bare fact of wage-labour under the direct control of capital, without any transformation of the labour process itself. Real subsumption involves the thoroughgoing reorganization of the labour process on a specifically capitalist basis. The distinction is decisive: absolute surplus-value requires only formal subsumption; relative surplus-value requires real subsumption. Marx then qualifies this with a discussion of hybrid forms —\"certain hybrid forms, in which although surplus labour is not extorted by direct compulsion from the producer, the producer has not yet become formally subordinate to capital. In these forms, capital has not yet acquired a direct control over the labour process.\" These are the cases in which independent handicraftsmen or peasants continue to labour \"in the inherited, traditional way,\" while alongside them \"there steps the usurer or merchant with his usurer's capital or merchant's capital, which feeds on them like a parasite.\" Such parasitism is pre-capitalist: \"The predominance of this form of exploitation in a society excludes the capitalist mode of production, although it may form the transition to capitalism, as in the later Middle Ages.\" Marx also notes the reappearance of such hybrid forms under modern conditions—\"as in the case of modern 'domestic industry'\"—but with a crucial qualification: \"though their physiognomy is totally changed.\" Their outward appearance has been transformed even where a formal similarity persists. And crucially: \"A merely formal subsumption of labour under capital suffices for the production of absolute surplus-value\" (e.g., independent handicraftsmen becoming wage-labourers under a capitalist). 2.7 Interpenetration: Methods of Relative Surplus-Value as Methods of Absolute Surplus-Value Now the chapter's central dialectical claim begins to unfold. \"But we have seen how methods of producing relative surplus-value are, at the same time, methods of producing absolute surplus-value.\" The clearest instance: \"the unrestricted prolongation of the working day turned out to be a very characteristic product of large-scale industry.\" Machinery, formally a means of raising productivity and thus of relative surplus-value, becomes in practice a device for lengthening the working day. The two are not separable processes, but fused. Marx then tracks the historical trajectory: \"The specifically capitalist mode of production ceases in general to be a mere means of producing relative surplus-value as soon as it has conquered an entire branch of production; this tendency is still more powerful when it has conquered all the important branches of production.\" Once it becomes \"the universal, socially predominant form of the production process,\" it takes on a double character: it \"only continues to act as a special method of producing relative surplus-value in two respects: first, in so far as it seizes upon industries previously only formally subordinate to capital, that is, in so far as it continues to proselytize, and second, in so far as the industries already taken over continue to be revolutionized by changes in the methods of production.\" The metaphor \"proselytize\" captures the missionary expansion of the capitalist mode of production from branch to branch; the second clause captures its immanent dynamism , its need for continual technical revolution. 2.8 The Apparent Illusoriness of the Distinction Marx now poses the crucial dialectical problem. \"From one standpoint the distinction between absolute and relative surplus-value appears to be illusory.\" Why? Because each contains the other: \"Relative surplus-value is absolute, because it requires the absolute prolongation of the working day beyond the labour-time necessary to the existence of the worker himself. Absolute surplus-value is relative, because it requires a development of the productivity of labour which will allow the necessary labour-time to be restricted to a portion of the working day.\" The paradox is exact: each is the other's condition of possibility. There is no absolute surplus-value that does not presuppose a certain level of productivity, and no relative surplus-value that does not presuppose an extension of the working day beyond necessary labour. 2.9 Raising the Rate of Surplus-Value: The Real Distinction But the \"semblance of identity\" is only a semblance. \"If we keep in mind the movement of surplus-value, this semblance of identity vanishes.\" Once the capitalist mode of production is universal, the difference asserts itself \"whenever there is a question of raising the rate of surplus-value.\" Assuming labour-power is paid at its value: if productivity and intensity of labour are given, the rate of surplus-value can be raised only by prolonging the working day in absolute terms ; if the length of the working day is given, the rate can be raised only by changing the relative magnitudes of the components of the working day , i.e., necessary labour and surplus labour—and \"if wages are not to fall below the value of labour-power, this change presupposes a change in either the productivity or the intensity of the labour.\" This is the analytic cores the chapter delivers: two distinct levers, an extensive one (length of the day) and an intensive one (productivity/intensity, given the day). The clause \"if wages are not to fall below the value of labour-power\" quietly excludes a third, illegitimate path—the reduction of wages below value—which will become central to the theory of wages in Part VI. 2.10 The Natural Basis of Surplus-Value Marx now pivots to the natural and historical presuppositions of surplus labour. \"If the worker needs to use all his time to produce the necessary means of subsistence for himself and his family, he has no time left in which to perform unpaid labour for other people.\" Surplus labour presupposes free time , which presupposes a certain level of labour productivity: \"Unless labour has attained a certain level of productivity, the worker will have no such free time at his disposal, and without superfluous time there can be no surplus labour, hence no capitalists, as also no slave-owners, no feudal barons, in a word no class of large-scale landed proprietors.\" This is a remarkable bracketing—capitalists, slave-owners and feudal lords are grouped together as appropriators of surplus labour , materially distinct but formally identical in this respect. But Marx immediately restricts the scope of the \"natural\" claim. Surplus-value rests on a natural basis \"but only in the very general sense that there is no natural obstacle absolutely preventing one man from lifting from himself the burden of the labour necessary to maintain his own existence, and imposing it on another, just as there is no unconquerable natural obstacle to the consumption of the flesh of one man by another.\" The grisly cannibalism comparison (note: \"According to a recent calculation there are still at least 4,000,000 cannibals in those parts of the earth which have so far been explored\") is not rhetorical excess; it makes a rigorous point: both cannibalism and surplus appropriation have a natural possibility and no natural necessity . Marx explicitly warns against attaching \"mystical notions\" to spontaneously developed productivity—a barb aimed at those who would naturalize the social relation. It is \"only when men have worked their way out of their initial animal condition, when therefore their labour has been to some extent socialized,\" that surplus labour of one person becomes a condition of existence for another. At the dawn of civilization, productive powers are small, \"but so too are the needs which develop with and upon the means of their satisfaction.\" The share of society living on the labour of others is \"infinitely small compared with the mass of direct producers\" but grows \"both absolutely and relatively\" with the advance of social productivity (note 3 cites: \"Among the wild Indians in America, almost everything is the labourer's, 99 parts of a hundred are to be put upon the account of labour. In England, perhaps, the labourer has not two thirds\"). The decisive conclusion: \"The existing productivity of labour, from which it proceeds as its basis, is a gift, not of nature, but of a history embracing thousands of centuries.\" 2.11 Natural Conditions of the Productivity of Labour Even setting aside the level of social development, the productivity of labour \"remains fettered by natural conditions. These conditions can all be traced back to the nature of man himself (his race, etc.) and to the natural objects which surround him.\" External natural conditions divide into two great classes: (1) natural wealth in the means of subsistence —\"a fruitful soil, waters teeming with fish, etc.\"; (2) natural wealth in the instruments of labour —\"waterfalls, navigable rivers, wood, metal, coal, etc.\" At the dawn of civilization it is the first class that dominates; at a higher stage, the second. Marx's examples: England versus India; Athens and Corinth versus the shores of the Black Sea. The smaller the number of natural requirements and the greater the natural fertility of the soil and kindness of climate, \"the smaller the amount of labour-time necessary for the maintenance and reproduction of the producer,\" and hence the greater the quantity of excess labour he can perform for others. This is the principle the ancient Egyptians illustrate; Marx cites Diodorus Siculus: Egyptian children cost their parents no more than twenty drachmas to raise, hence the enormous population and hence \"why so many great works can be undertaken.\" But Marx immediately qualifies: \"Nevertheless, the gigantic building projects of ancient Egypt owed less to the size of the population than to the large proportion of it that was freely disposable.\" The analogy between the individual worker and the working population is exact: less necessary labour-time means more surplus labour; a smaller portion of the population devoted to producing subsistence means a greater portion available for other work. 2.12 The Temperate Zone, Soil Differentiation, and the Domination of Nature This section delivers one of the chapter's most brilliant counter-intuitive claims: fertility does not make a region fit for capitalism . The most fertile soil, precisely because it is fertile, may \"keep him in hand, like a child in leading-strings\"—man's own development \"is not in that case a nature-imposed necessity.\" The note cites Thomas Mun's England's Treasure by Forraign Trade (1669): natural wealth \"doth make the people careless, proud, and given to all excesses; whereas the second [wealth acquired through labour] enforceth vigilancy, literature, arts, and policy.\" And N. Forster (1767): \"A soil incapable of produce by labour is quite as bad as a soil that produces plentifully without any labour.\" Marx concludes: \"The mother country of capital is not the tropical region, with its luxuriant vegetation, but the temperate zone.\" The reason is that \"it is not the absolute fertility of the soil but its degree of differentiation, the variety of its natural products, which forms the natural basis for the social division of labour\"—the variety of nature, not its abundance, \"spurs man on to the multiplication of his needs, his capacities, and the instruments and modes of his labour.\" The argument now turns explicitly to the domination of nature: \"It is the necessity of bringing a natural force under the control of society, of economizing on its energy, of appropriating or subduing it on a large scale by the work of the human hand, that plays the most decisive role in the history of industry.\" Marx's examples are the regulation of water in Egypt, Lombardy and Holland; irrigation in India and Persia, where artificial canals also \"carry down mineral fertilizers from the hills, in the shape of sediment.\" The footnote on Egypt is notable for its materialist claim about ideology: \"The necessity for predicting the rise and fall of the Nile created Egyptian astronomy, and with it the domination of the priests as the directors of agriculture\" (citing Cuvier's Discours sur les révolutions du globe ). Another footnote makes the same point about India: \"One of the material foundations of the power of the state over the small and unconnected producing organisms of India was the regulation of the water supply. Its Mohammedan rulers understood this better than their English successors\"—with the Orissa famine of 1866 as the devastating evidence (\"more than a million Hindus in the district of Orissa, in the Bengal Presidency\"). The Spanish and Sicilian industry under Arab rule is likewise located in irrigation works. 2.13 Possibility versus Reality: The Sago Gatherer The chapter's most decisive natural-history passage now consolidates the argument: \"Favourable natural conditions can provide in themselves only the possibility, never the reality of surplus labour, nor, accordingly, the reality of surplus-value and a surplus product.\" Natural conditions affect surplus labour only as limits —\"by determining the point at which labour for others can begin\"—and \"in proportion as industry advances these natural limits recede.\" Marx then offers the sago-gatherer example: an East Indian who bores a hole into a sago tree, cuts down and divides the trunk, extracts and filters the pith—\"one tree commonly yields 300 lb., and occasionally 500 to 600 lb.\"—goes into the forest and \"cuts bread for himself, just as with us they cut firewood.\" Suppose this bread-cutter requires 12 working hours a week for all his needs. \"Nature's direct gift to him is plenty of leisure time.\" But \"before he can apply this leisure time productively for himself, a whole series of historical circumstances is required; before he spends it in surplus labour for others, compulsion is necessary.\" Under capitalist production \"the good fellow would perhaps have to work six days a week, in order to appropriate to himself the product of one working day. In that case, the bounty of nature would not explain why he now has to work six days a week, or why he must provide five days of surplus labour. It explains only why his necessary labour-time would be limited to one day a week. But in no case would his surplus product arise from some innate, occult quality of human labour.\" The word \"occult\" is the clue: Marx is polemicizing against any naturalization of surplus-value. His target is Proudhon, quoted in note 9: \"All labour must leave a surplus\"—which Marx sarcastically caps with the aside \"apparently this is also part of the 'rights and duties of the citizen'.\" 2.14 Productive Forces of Labour as Productive Forces of Capital The natural-historical section of the chapter closes with a single, compressed thesis: \"Thus both the historically developed productive forces of labour in society, and its naturally conditioned productive forces, appear as productive forces of the capital into which that labour is incorporated.\" This is the fetishism of capital in its most abstract form: the productive powers of labour, whatever their origin in nature or history, present themselves in the capitalist mode of production as attributes of capital itself. It also sets up the theoretical critique that follows. 2.15 Ricardo's Evasion: Origin versus Magnitude The final movement of the chapter is a polemic in the history of economic thought. Ricardo \"never concerns himself with the origin of surplus-value. He treats it as an entity inherent in the capitalist mode of production, and in his eyes the latter is the natural form of social production.\" In his discussion of the productivity of labour, \"he seeks in it not the cause of the existence of surplus-value, but the cause that determines the magnitude of that value\"—a crucial and precise distinction. Ricardo's school , by contrast, \"has loudly proclaimed that the productive power of labour is the originating cause of profit (read: surplus-value).\" Marx grants this is at least an advance over the Mercantilists , \"who derive the excess of the price of a product over its cost of production from the act of exchange, from the sale of the product above its value.\" But Ricardo's school ultimately \"merely evaded the problem rather than solving it.\" And Marx adds one of his most cutting observations: \"In fact, these bourgeois economists instinctively and rightly saw that it was very dangerous to penetrate too deeply into the burning question of the origin of surplus-value.\" 2.16 The Critique of John Stuart Mill The chapter's polemical climax takes down John Stuart Mill , who \"half a century after Ricardo, solemnly claims superiority over the Mercantilists by clumsily repeating the wretched evasions of Ricardo's earliest vulgarizers.\" Marx subjects Mill's argument to a step-by-step demolition. (a) The cause of profit. Mill: \"The cause of profit is that labour produces more than is required for its support.\" Marx: \"So far, nothing but the old story.\" (b) The duration confusion. Mill continues: \"the reason why capital yields a profit, is because food, clothing, materials and tools, last longer than the time which was required to produce them.\" Marx identifies the error immediately: \"Here he confuses the duration of labour-time with the duration of its products.\" The reductio: \"a baker, whose products last only a day, could never extract the same profit from his workers as a machine manufacturer, whose products last for twenty years or more.\" And the beautifully absurd bird's-nest aside: \"it is very true that if a bird's nest did not last longer than the time it takes to build, the birds would have to do without nests.\" (c) The exchange error. Mill: \"profit arises, not from the incident of exchange, but from the productive power of labour ... If there were no division of employments, there would be no buying or selling, but there would still be profit.\" Marx's response is withering: \"For Mill then, exchange, buying and selling, i.e. the general conditions of capitalist production, are a mere incident, and there would always be profits even without the purchase and sale of labour-power!\" (d) The 20 per cent fallacy. Mill: \"If the labourers of the country collectively produce 20 per cent more than their wages, profits will be 20 per cent, whatever prices may or may not be.\" Marx concedes this is \"in one respect, a rare piece of tautology\"—since the rate of surplus-value, s/v, is definitionally the ratio of surplus-value to wages (20:100)—but then exposes the confusion between the rate of surplus-value and the rate of profit . \"It is absolutely false to say that 'profits will be 20 per cent'. They will always be less, because they are calculated upon the sum total of the capital advanced.\" Marx's numerical illustration is exact: if the capitalist has advanced £500, of which £400 is laid out in means of production and £100 in wages, and the rate of surplus-value is 20 per cent, then s = £20, and \"the rate of profit will be 20:500, i.e. not 20 per cent but 4 per cent.\" This anticipates the machinery of Volume III, where the rate of profit is s/(c + v) as distinct from the rate of surplus-value, s/v. (e) The \"optical illusion.\" Mill: \"I assume, throughout, the state of things which ... prevails, with few exceptions, universally; namely, that the capitalist advances the whole expenses, including the entire remuneration of the labourer.\" Marx: \"Strange optical illusion, to see everywhere a situation which as yet exists only exceptionally on our earth!\" The editorial note is important here: Marx would certainly have altered this passage; the addition in square brackets—\"where the labourers and capitalists are separate classes\"—was omitted inadvertently by Marx and \"clears Mill, at least formally, from the charge of being guilty of an 'optical illusion'.\" In his letter of 28 November 1878 to N. F. Danielson, Marx proposed replacing the passage beginning \"Strange optical illusion\" with: \"Mr Mill is willing to concede that it is not absolutely necessary for it to be so, even under an economic system where workers and capitalists confront each other as separate classes.\" (f) The worker as capitalist. Mill concedes that the capitalist's advancing of the whole remuneration \"is not a matter of inherent necessity,\" and that \"the labourer might wait, until the production is complete, for all that part of his wages which exceeds mere necessaries; and even for the whole, if he has funds in hand sufficient for his temporary support. But in the latter case, the labourer is to that extent really a capitalist in the concern, by supplying a portion of the funds necessary for carrying it on.\" Marx's reductio: \"Mill might just as well have said that the worker who advances to himself not only the means of subsistence but also the means of production is in reality his own wage-labourer, or, indeed, that the American peasant is his own slave, because he does forced labour for himself instead of doing it for someone who is his master.\" (g) Capitalist production \"would still continue to exist even if it did not exist.\" Marx's summary of Mill's procedure: \"After thus proving clearly that capitalist production would still continue to exist even if it did not exist, Mill now proceeds, quite consistently, to show that it would not exist even if it did exist.\" Mill's final absurdity: even where the worker is a wage-labourer to whom the capitalist advances the whole of his subsistence, \"he ... may be looked upon in the same light [i.e., as a capitalist] since, contributing his labour at less than the market price (!), he may be regarded as lending the difference (?) to his employer and receiving it back with interest, etc.\" Marx's response: \"In reality, the worker advances his labour gratuitously to the capitalist during, say, one week, in order to receive its market price at the end of the week, etc.: according to Mill this makes him into a capitalist!\" (h) The closing image. Marx ends the chapter: \"On a level plain, simple mounds look like hills; and the insipid flatness of our present bourgeoisie is to be measured by the altitude of its 'great intellects'.\" Mill, in other words, is a \"great intellect\" only because the surrounding landscape is so flat. 2.17 The Editorial Note and Marx's Revised Passage The chapter's penultimate note (Footnote * on p. 653) informs us that Marx—had he had the opportunity—would have altered the \"Strange optical illusion\" passage to acknowledge Mill's formal anticipation. The letter to Danielson is a fascinating glimpse into Marx's willingness to revise even the most polemical passages of Capital in the interest of accuracy. The editorial apparatus also points us to MEW 23, p. 540 for the textual source. The passage is included here as one of the few places where the reader is shown Marx's own awareness of the limits of his polemic. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental lessons of Chapter 16 may be summarized as follows. First, categories of political economy are historical, not natural. The concept of \"productive labour\" is shown to have no stable trans-historical content: under capitalism, to be productive is to produce surplus-value, a definition that both extends (to the collective labourer) and narrows (to the servant of capital). Marx's method here is exemplary: he takes a term that appears technical and neutral—\"productive labour\"—and demonstrates that it is a garment worn by a social relation. The schoolmaster who enriches the owner of the school is \"productive\"; the worker who produces only his own subsistence is not. The lesson is that the grammar of economic \"productiveness\" is a class grammar. Second, the two forms of surplus-value are dialectically unified but practically distinct. Neither absolute nor relative surplus-value can exist without the other—each presupposes the other's conditions—yet once the capitalist mode of production is universal, the distinction is operative whenever the rate of surplus-value is to be raised. The path of prolonging the working day and the path of increasing productivity/intensity, given a fixed working day, are not interchangeable; they correspond to different historical moments, different technical regimes, and different political conflicts. The dialectic of identity-in-difference is therefore not a scholastic nicety but a tool of concrete analysis. Third, surplus-labour rests on a historical, not a natural, foundation. Nature provides only possibility—fertile soil, abundant waters, \"leisure time\"—never the reality of surplus-value. The reality is produced by compulsion , by the social relation that separates the producer from the means of production, and by an \"economic soil that is the product of a long process of development.\" The existing productivity of labour, from which capitalism proceeds, \"is a gift, not of nature, but of a history embracing thousands of centuries.\" This is the theoretical ground on which Marx rejects both naturalism (the physiocrats' land, Proudhon's \"all labour must leave a surplus\") and the reduction of surplus-value to exchange (the Mercantilists). Fourth, the critique of political economy is immanent. Ricardo never asks the origin question; he asks only the magnitude question, and he treats the capitalist mode of production as the natural form of social production. His school asserts the productivity of labour as the \"originating cause\" of profit but thereby only evades the problem. Mill, in turn, converts evasion into positive blunder: he confuses duration of labour with duration of product; he confuses the rate of surplus-value with the rate of profit (the £500/£400/£100/20% example yields 4 per cent, not 20 per cent); he imagines that \"in the latter case, the labourer is to that extent really a capitalist in the concern\"; and he manages the double miracle of showing that capitalist production \"would still continue to exist even if it did not exist\" and \"would not exist even if it did exist.\" The polemic is not merely ad hominem : the errors of Mill are systematic, arising from the prior refusal to pose the origin question, and Marx's formal demolitions (the baker, the bird's nest, the American peasant as his own slave, the mounds that look like hills) are weapons in a war over categories, not merely targets of ridicule. Broader implications. For the theory of Capital as a whole, the chapter secures the dual mechanism of surplus-value extraction that will inform the theory of accumulation (Part VII) and the theory of the wage (Part VI). It also clarifies that the development of capitalism is not a single-track process: the formal subsumption of labour under capital (merely wage-labour under a capitalist) suffices for absolute surplus-value, whereas the real subsumption (a specifically capitalist mode of production) is the condition of relative surplus-value—a distinction that will be of the first importance for the historical account of the transition to capitalism and, in the twentieth century, for debates on the \"labour process.\" Finally, the chapter quietly demonstrates Marx's method: to move from abstract to concrete, from the simple labour process to the capitalist process of production, from natural possibility to historical reality, and from the appearance of identity to the reality of difference. Each of these movements is a model of the dialectical presentation that Capital as a whole enacts, and each carries within it a sharp political-indictment: the \"productive worker\" is a \"misfortune,\" the \"great intellects\" of the bourgeoisie are mounds mistaken for hills, and the \"felicity\" of nature's bounty is a veil over the compulsion of surplus labour. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 15 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 17"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 17", "title": "Chapter 17", "subtitle": "Changes of Magnitude in the Price of Labo", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 17/Chapter 17  Changes of Magnitude in the Price of Labo_explanation.html", "word_count": 7348, "read_time": "37 min read", "tags": ["Wages & Value of Labour-Power", "Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["Magnitude Changes in Price of Labour-Power and Surplus-Value", "Length of Working Day, Intensity, and Productivity", "Simultaneous Variations in Value of Labour-Power", "Upper and Lower Bounds of Real Wages", "Productivity Gains Counterbalanced by Intensity"], "text": "Chapter 17 Changes Of Magnitude In The Price Of Labo - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 7,308 words ⏱️ ~37 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 17, \"Changes of Magnitude in the Price of Labour-Power and in Surplus-Value,\" is the analytic keystone of Marx's theory of surplus-value production in Capital , Volume 1. It is not a narrative or historical chapter but a work of rigorous, almost geometric comparative statics. Its central problem is deceptively simple: given that a working day produces value, and that this value is divided between the worker's wage (the value of labour-power) and the capitalist's surplus-value, how do changes in the underlying determinants of production redistribute that value between the two classes? Marx answers by constructing a formal combinatorial system around exactly three variables—the length of the working day (its extensive magnitude), the intensity of labour (its intensive magnitude), and the productivity of labour (its efficiency in yielding use-values). By holding two variables constant at a time and allowing the third to vary, then by examining simultaneous variations, he derives a complete taxonomy of capitalist exploitation dynamics. The chapter's core thesis is that the relationship between the value of labour-power and surplus-value is governed by strict, law-like regularities that can be specified with mathematical precision—but only once Marx has disentangled surplus-value \"as such\" from its phenomenal forms (profit, rent). In the Introduction to the chapter Marx defines the value of labour-power as determined by the value of the means of subsistence habitually required by the average worker, treating the quantity of those means as a constant for any given epoch and society, so that only their value varies. He explicitly brackets two complicating factors: the cost of developing labour-power (which varies with the mode of production) and the natural diversity of labour-power between men and women, adults and children—factors that, as Engels' footnote 1 notes, also exclude the case treated on pp. 433–6 where wages are forced below the value of labour-power. He then imposes two foundational assumptions: (1) commodities are sold at their value, and (2) the price of labour-power may occasionally rise above its value but never sinks below it. These assumptions quarantine the analysis from market-price noise and from the violent history of wage depression, isolating the pure inner logic of exploitation. The intellectual objective is threefold. First, Marx wants to demonstrate that the confrontation between capital and labour is not a matter of contractual accident or moral failing but a structural necessity : the zero-sum logic of a fixed working day (Section I) and the positive-sum logic of lengthened or intensified labour (Sections II–III) are both built into the capital relation itself. Second, he wants to expose the analytical defects of classical political economy—specifically Ricardo's failure to distinguish the rate of surplus-value (s/v) from the rate of profit (s/C), and his unexamined assumption that the working day and labour intensity are fixed. Third, and most importantly for the architecture of Capital , the chapter prepares the ground for the theory of accumulation and for Volume 3's analysis of the rate of profit by establishing once and for all that the laws of surplus-value are not the laws of profit. Within the broader theoretical framework, Chapter 17 functions as the synthesis of Parts III and IV of Volume 1. Part III developed absolute surplus-value (lengthening the working day); Part IV developed relative surplus-value (raising productivity); Chapter 17 unifies them by showing how the three determinants interact in all possible combinations. It is the hinge upon which the entire subsequent argument of Capital turns: without these laws, neither the historical analysis of the working day (Chapter 10) nor the theory of accumulation (Part VII) nor the falling tendency of the profit rate (Volume 3) can be properly understood. The chapter's final pages—touching on the \"universality of labour\" and the conversion of the masses' lifetime into labour-time—also carry an unmistakable emancipatory horizon, connecting the analytic machinery to Marx's vision of a society beyond capitalist production. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Architectonic Setup: Assumptions, Variables, and the Analytical Frame Marx opens by defining the value of labour-power : it \"is determined by the value of the means of subsistence habitually required by the average worker.\" Crucially, the quantity of these means of subsistence is \"given at any particular epoch in any particular society, and can therefore be treated as a constant magnitude.\" What varies is the value of this quantity —i.e., the socially necessary labour-time required to produce the subsistence basket. This distinction between a constant physical basket and a variable value of that basket is the foundational move that allows the subsequent analysis: productivity changes alter the value of the basket, not its size, and thus alter the value of labour-power without altering the worker's material standard of living in kind. He then identifies two factors that enter into the determination of the value of labour-power but are excluded from the analysis. The first is the cost of developing that power , which \"varies with the mode of production\"—the historical and skill-formation costs embedded in producing a particular type of worker. The second is the natural diversity of labour-power , \"the difference between the labour-power of men and women, children and adults.\" The utilization of these different sorts of labour-power is \"in turn conditioned by the mode of production,\" and it \"makes for great variations in the cost of reproducing the worker's family, and in the value of the labour-power of the adult male.\" Both are excluded. Engels' footnote 1 adds that the case considered earlier on pp. 433–6—where wages are depressed below the value of labour-power—is also excluded \"of course.\" On top of these exclusions Marx imposes two assumptions: \"(1) that commodities are sold at their value, (2) that the price of labour-power occasionally rises above its value, but never sinks below it.\" This is a critical methodological choice. By assuming commodities sell at value, he neutralizes market-price deviations; by forbidding the price of labour-power from falling below its value, he brackets the systematic wage-depression that characterized early capitalism and primitive accumulation. The analysis that follows is therefore not a description of empirical wage levels but a specification of the inner laws that govern the capital–labour relation once these contingent distortions are set aside. Given these assumptions, Marx restates the three circumstances that determine \"the relative magnitudes of surplus-value and of price of labour-power\": (1) the length of the working day , or the extensive magnitude of labour; (2) the normal intensity of labour , or its intensive magnitude, \"whereby a given quantity of labour is expended in a given time\"; and (3) the productivity of labour , \"whereby the same quantity of labour yields, in a given time, a greater or a smaller quantity of the product, depending on the degree of development attained by the conditions of production.\" He notes the combinatorial richness: one factor can be constant while the other two vary; two can be constant while one varies; all three can vary. Moreover, when all three vary simultaneously, \"the amount and direction of their respective variations may differ.\" He announces that only \"the chief combinations alone are considered\"—a signal of analytical economy, not incompleteness, since the method developed in the first three sections can handle any combination. Section I: Law One — The Constant Working Day as a Constant Magnitude of Value Section I holds the length of the working day and the intensity of labour constant and allows productivity to vary. Under these assumptions three laws govern the value of labour-power and the magnitude of surplus-value. The first law states: \"a working day of a given length always creates the same amount of value, no matter how the productivity of labour, and, with it, the mass of the product and the price of each single commodity produced may vary.\" Marx illustrates: \"If the value created by a working day of 12 hours is, say, 6 shillings, then, although the mass of the use-values produced varies with the productivity of labour, the value represented by 6 shillings will simply be spread over a greater or a less number of commodities.\" The underlying principle is the fundamental distinction from Chapter 1 between value and use-value: productivity changes the quantity of use-values produced but not the value embodied in them, because the total labour-time expended remains the same. Section I: Law Two — The Inverse Variation of Labour-Power's Value and Surplus-Value The second law declares: \"the value of labour-power and surplus-value vary in opposite directions. A variation in the productivity of labour, its increase or diminution, causes the value of labour-power to move in the opposite direction, while surplus-value moves in the same direction.\" The mechanics are strictly arithmetical. The value created by a 12-hour day is a constant quantity, say 6 shillings, which \"is the sum of the surplus-value plus the value of the labour-power.\" Because the worker replaces the value of labour-power with an equivalent, the 6 shillings is partitioned into two parts. \"It is self-evident that if a constant quantity consists of two parts, neither of them can increase without the other diminishing.\" Marx gives a numerical demonstration: if v = 3s and s = 3s, then \"the value of the labour-power cannot rise from 3 shillings to 4 without the surplus-value falling from 3 shillings to 2; and the surplus-value cannot rise from 3 shillings to 4 without the value of the labour-power falling from 3 shillings to 2.\" Hence under these conditions \"no change can take place in the absolute magnitude either of the surplus-value or of the value of the labour-power without a simultaneous change in their relative magnitudes. It is impossible for them to rise or fall simultaneously.\" Marx then tightens the causal chain: \"the value of the labour-power cannot fall, and consequently surplus-value cannot rise, without a rise in the productivity of labour.\" The concrete mechanism is given: \"the value of the labour-power cannot sink from 3 shillings to 2 unless an increase in the productivity of labour makes it possible to produce in 4 hours the same quantity of means of subsistence as could only be produced previously in 6 hours.\" Conversely, \"the value of the labour-power cannot rise from 3 shillings to 4 without a decrease in the productivity of labour, so that 8 hours are required to produce the same quantity of means of subsistence as were previously produced in 6 hours.\" The general conclusion: \"an increase in the productivity of labour causes a fall in the value of labour-power and a consequent rise in surplus-value, while, on the other hand, a decrease in the productivity of labour causes a rise in the value of labour-power and a fall in surplus-value.\" Section I: Ricardo's Oversight — The Asymmetry of Proportional Change Here Marx inserts a crucial corrective against Ricardo. In formulating this law, \"Ricardo overlooked one thing: although a change in the magnitude of the surplus-value or surplus labour causes a change in the opposite direction in the magnitude of the value of labour-power, or in the quantity of necessary labour, it by no means follows that they vary in the same proportion.\" They do increase or diminish by the same quantity —but their proportional change depends on their original magnitudes before the productivity change. Marx works a concrete example. Suppose the value of labour-power is 4 shillings (= 8 hours necessary labour) and surplus-value is 2 shillings (= 4 hours surplus labour). If productivity rises so that v falls to 3 shillings (= 6 hours) then s rises to 3 shillings (= 6 hours), a uniform quantity of 1 shilling or 2 hours. But \"the proportional change of magnitude is different in the two cases. The value of the labour-power falls from 4 shillings to 3, i.e. it falls by ¼, or 25 per cent, but the surplus-value rises from 2 shillings to 3, i.e. it rises by ½, or 50 per cent.\" The general rule: \"the proportional increase or diminution in surplus-value resulting from a given change in the productivity of labour depends on the original magnitude of that portion of the working day which is embodied in surplus-value; the smaller that portion, the greater the proportional change; the greater that portion, the less the proportional change.\" The footnote attaches Ricardo's original formulation—\"There can be no rise in the value of labour without a fall of profits\"—and offers Marx's reformulation: \"There can be no rise in the value of labour-power without a fall in surplus-value,\" directing readers to the fuller discussion in Theories of Surplus-Value , Part 2. Section I: Law Three — Consequence, Not Cause The third law states: \"Increase or diminution in surplus-value is always the consequence, and never the cause, of the corresponding diminution or increase in the value of labour-power.\" The derivation is a compressed chain of reasoning: because the working day is constant and represented by a constant value, because every variation in surplus-value has an inverse variation in the value of labour-power, and because the value of labour-power \"cannot change except as a result of a change in the productivity of labour,\" it follows that every change in surplus-value arises from an inverse change in the value of labour-power. And if no change in absolute magnitudes can occur without a change in relative magnitudes, \"it now follows that no change in their relative magnitudes is possible without a change in the absolute magnitude of the value of labour-power.\" The altered value of labour-power sets the limit of the change. Section I: The Subsidiary Movement and the Class-Struggle Residual Marx immediately qualifies the pure law with a crucial empirical intrusion: \"even when circumstances allow the law to operate, subsidiary movements may occur.\" If productivity increase drives the value of labour-power down from 4 shillings to 3, \"the price of labour-power might well fall only to 3s. 8d., 3s. 6d. or 3s. 2d., thus allowing the amount of surplus-value to rise only to 3s. 4d., 3s. 6d., or 3s. 10d.\" The amount of the fall—whose lowest limit is the new value of 3 shillings—\"depends on the relative weight thrown into the scale by the pressure of capital on the one side, and the resistance of the worker on the other.\" This is a decisive insertion: the laws determine the boundaries within which the wage can move, but where within those boundaries it actually settles is a function of class struggle. The analysis is thus both deterministic and open-ended. Marx then dispatches a rival theory. MacCulloch added \"the following absurd addition to this third law: he says that a rise in surplus-value which is not accompanied by a fall in the value of labour-power can occur as a result of the abolition of taxes formerly payable by the capitalist.\" Marx's refutation is surgical: \"the abolition of such taxes makes no change whatever in the quantity of surplus-value extorted by the capitalist at first hand from the worker. It only alters the proportion in which that surplus-value is divided between the capitalist himself and third persons. It therefore produces no change whatsoever in the relation between surplus-value and the value of labour-power.\" MacCulloch's exception \"proves only his failure to understand the rule\"—a misfortune Marx archly notes \"as often happens to him in the vulgarization of Ricardo as it does to J. B. Say in the vulgarization of Adam Smith.\" Section I: The Paradox of Simultaneous Improvement — Rising Above Value A subtle and important case follows. Marx notes that it is possible \"owing to an increase in the productivity of labour both the worker and the capitalist may simultaneously be able to appropriate a greater quantity of means of subsistence, without any change in the price of labour-power or in surplus-value.\" He sets up: v = 3s (6 hours necessary), s = 3s (6 hours surplus). If productivity doubles \"without any alteration in the ratio between necessary labour and surplus labour, there would be no change in the magnitude either of the surplus-value or of the price of labour-power.\" Each would represent twice as many use-values and each use-value would be half as cheap. \"Although labour-power would be unchanged in price, it would have risen above its value.\" The value of labour-power has fallen to 1s. 6d. (since productivity halved the labour-time needed to reproduce the subsistence basket), yet its price remains at 3s. The worker is being paid above value. Now suppose the price falls, \"not as far as 1s. 6d., the lowest possible point consistent with its new value, but to 2s. 10d. or 2s. 6d.\" Even this lower price \"would still represent an increased quantity of means of subsistence.\" The dialectical conclusion is one of Marx's most powerful: \"it is possible, given increasing productivity of labour, for the price of labour-power to fall constantly and for this fall to be accompanied by a constant growth in the mass of the worker's means of subsistence. But in relative terms, i.e. in comparison with surplus-value, the value of labour-power would keep falling, and thus the abyss between the life-situation of the worker and that of the capitalist would keep widening.\" This is the formal, quantitative grounding of the thesis of absolute improvement combined with relative immiseration . The Cazenove footnote (note 3) supports the distinction between the proportion of wages and the quantity they represent. Section I: Ricardo's Two Defects — The Rate of Surplus-Value versus the Rate of Profit Marx concludes Section I by acknowledging that \"Ricardo was the first to give an accurate formulation of the three laws we have just stated,\" but his presentation suffers two defects. First , \"he views the special conditions under which these laws hold good as the self-evident, universal, and exclusive conditions of capitalist production. He recognizes no change either in the length of the working day or in the intensity of labour, so that with him the productivity of labour becomes the only variable factor.\" Second, and more serious , \"he has not, any more than the other economists, investigated surplus-value as such, i.e. independently of its particular forms, such as profit, ground rent, etc. He therefore fails to differentiate between the laws governing the rate of surplus-value and those governing the rate of profit.\" Marx then provides the crucial definitions. The rate of profit is \"the ratio of the surplus-value to the total capital advanced\"; the rate of surplus-value is \"the ratio of the surplus-value to the variable part of that capital.\" Using C = £500, of which c (raw materials, instruments of labour) = £400 and v (wages) = £100, and s = £100, he computes: rate of surplus-value = s/v = £100/£100 = 100 per cent; rate of profit = s/C = £100/£500 = 20 per cent. The rate of profit \"may depend on circumstances which in no way affect the rate of surplus-value.\" Volume 3 will demonstrate \"that the same rate of surplus-value may be expressed in the most diverse rates of profit, and that different rates of surplus-value may, under certain circumstances, be expressed in the same rate of profit.\" The footnote references Volume 3, Chapter 3. This distinction is foundational: it isolates the exploitation relation (s/v) from the appearance of profitability within which class interests are typically articulated. Section II: The Intensive Magnitude — Why Intensity Is Not Productivity Section II holds the length of the working day and productivity constant and varies intensity . Marx defines: \"Increased intensity of labour means increased expenditure of labour in a given time. Hence a working day of more intense labour is embodied in more products than is one of less intense labour, the length of each working day being the same.\" The key analytical distinction from productivity is then drawn with precision. An increase in productivity will also supply more products in a given day, \"but in that case the value of each single product falls, for it costs less labour than before, whereas in the case mentioned here that value remains unchanged, because each article costs the same amount of labour as before.\" Hence: \"an increase in the number of products unaccompanied by a fall in their individual prices: as their number increases, so does the sum of their prices, whereas in the case of an increase in productivity, a given value is spread over a greater mass of products.\" The consequence: with constant length of day, \"a day's labour of increased intensity will be incorporated in an increased amount of value, and, assuming no change in the value of money, in an increased amount of money.\" The value created varies with intensity's divergence from the normal social level. \"A given working day, therefore, no longer creates a constant value, but a variable one; in a day of 12 hours of ordinary intensity, the value created is, say, 6 shillings, but, with increased intensity, the value created may be 7, 8 or more shillings.\" If the value created rises from 6 to 8 shillings, then \"the two parts into which this value is divided, namely the price of labour-power and surplus-value, may both increase simultaneously, and either equally or unequally. They may both simultaneously increase from 3 shillings to 4.\" Crucially, \"here, the rise in the price of labour-power does not necessarily imply that it has risen above the value of labour-power. On the contrary, this rise in price may be accompanied by a fall below its value. This always occurs when the rise in the price of labour-power does not compensate for its more rapid deterioration.\" Section II: The Dissolution of the Consumption-Article Limitation Marx then notes a crucial structural implication. We know that \"with exceptions which are purely temporary, a change in the productivity of labour does not cause any change in the value of labour-power, nor, consequently, in the magnitude of surplus-value, unless the products of the industries affected are articles habitually consumed by the workers.\" Productivity only affects the value of labour-power through the value of the wage-goods bundle. But with intensity this limitation \"falls to the ground. Whether the magnitude of the labour changes in extent or in intensity, there is always a corresponding change in the magnitude of the value created, independent of the nature of the article in which that value is embodied.\" Intensity is therefore a more generally applicable lever of exploitation than productivity: it works regardless of what is being produced, because it changes the amount of value created within the working day itself. Section II: The International Dimension — Intensity and the Law of Value Between Nations Marx adds a geopolitical corollary. \"If the intensity of labour were to increase simultaneously and equally in every branch of industry, then the new and higher degree of intensity would become the normal social degree of intensity, and would therefore cease to count as an extensive magnitude.\" The norm would simply shift. \"But even so, the intensity of labour would still be different in different countries, and would modify the application of the law of value to the working days of different nations.\" A more intensive working day \"would be represented by a greater sum of money than the less intensive day of another nation.\" Footnote 4 cites the Reports of the Inspectors of Factories of 31 October 1855: \"All things being equal, the English manufacturer can turn out a considerably larger amount of work in a given time than a foreign manufacturer, so much as to counterbalance the difference of the working days, between 60 hours a week here, and 72 or 80 elsewhere.\" Marx adds, with characteristic polemical irony: \"The most infallible means of reducing this difference between the product of the English and of the Continental working hour would be a law shortening the length of the working day in Continental factories.\" What appears as a technical comparison of international productivity is thus a covert argument for factory legislation. Section III: The Three Laws of the Variable Working Day Section III holds productivity and intensity constant and varies the length of the working day . The working day may be shortened or lengthened. Marx lays out three laws. First : \"The working day creates a greater or lesser amount of value in proportion to its length—thus, a variable and not a constant quantity of value.\" Second : \"Every change in the relation between the magnitude of surplus-value and the value of labour-power arises from a change in the absolute magnitude of the surplus labour, and consequently of the surplus-value.\" Third : \"The absolute value of labour-power can change only in consequence of the reaction exercised by the prolongation of surplus labour upon the wear and tear of labour-power. Every change in this absolute value is therefore the effect, but never the cause, of a change in the magnitude of surplus-value.\" Note the structural inversion: in Section I, the value of labour-power was the independent variable (via productivity) and surplus-value the dependent one; here, surplus-value is the independent variable and the value of labour-power reacts only through wear and tear. A parenthetical note (present only in the 1872 French translation, presumably inserted by Engels into the first English translation) frames the two cases: shortening first, then lengthening. Section III: Shortening the Day — The Capitalist's Compensation Problem Case (1): a shortening of the working day under the given conditions \"leaves the value of labour-power, and therefore the necessary labour-time, as it was before. It reduces the surplus labour and the surplus-value. Along with the absolute magnitude of the latter, its relative magnitude also falls, i.e. its magnitude in relation to the magnitude of the value of labour-power, which remains constant. Only by reducing the price of labour-power below its value could the capitalist compensate himself for this fall.\" This is a crucial point: a shorter working day at the same wage is an unambiguous loss to capital, and capital's only way to recover is to push the wage below the value of labour-power—i.e., to violate Assumption 2, which is exactly what happens historically. Marx then adds a dialectical reversal: \"All the usual arguments against the shortening of the working day depend on the assumption that the phenomenon occurs under the conditions presupposed here. But in reality the very opposite is the case: a shortening of the working day either follows upon, or immediately precedes, a change in the productivity and the intensity of labour.\" Footnote 5 cites the Reports of the Inspectors of Factories of 31 October 1848 on the \"compensating circumstances\" that the Ten Hours' Act brought to light. In other words, the static model's prediction of loss is empirically falsified by the dynamic model, in which shortening is bundled with productivity and intensity gains. Section III: Lengthening the Day — Relative Fall Without Absolute Fall Case (2): lengthening of the working day. Marx sets v = 3s (6 hours necessary), s = 3s (6 hours surplus), total day 12 hours = 6s. \"If the working day is lengthened by 2 hours and the price of labour-power remains the same, the surplus-value increases both absolutely and relatively. Although there is no absolute change in the value of labour-power, it suffers a relative fall.\" This is a critical structural difference from Section I: \"Under the conditions assumed in I, there could not be a change of relative magnitude in the value of labour-power without a change in its absolute magnitude. Here, on the contrary, the change of relative magnitude in the value of labour-power is the result of the change of absolute magnitude in surplus-value.\" Since the value-product increases with the day's length, \"the surplus-value and the price of labour-power may simultaneously increase, either by equal or unequal quantities.\" Marx identifies two cases in which simultaneous increase is possible: \"an absolute increase in the length of the working day,\" and \"an increase in the intensity of labour unaccompanied by an increase in length.\" Section III: The Commensurability Break — Wear and Tear Beyond the Threshold Marx then introduces a profound qualification. \"When the working day is prolonged, the price of labour-power may fall below its value, although that price nominally remains unchanged, or even rises.\" Why? Because \"the value of a day's labour-power is estimated, as will be remembered, on the basis of its normal average duration, or the normal duration of the life of a worker, and on the basis of the appropriate normal standard of conversion of living substances into motion as it applies to the nature of man.\" Footnote 6 cites Grove's On the Correlation of Physical Forces on the chemical changes that measure bodily labour. \"Up to a certain point, the increased deterioration of labour-power inseparable from a lengthening of the working day may be compensated for by making amends in the form of higher wages. But beyond this point deterioration increases in geometrical progression, and all the requirements for the normal reproduction and functioning of labour-power cease to be fulfilled. The price of labour-power and the degree of its exploitation cease to be commensurable quantities.\" This is a subtle and chilling point: nominal wage gains may mask a real fall below value, and beyond a threshold, the wage relation itself loses its coherence as a measure of exploitation. The physical limits of the worker's body override the logic of equivalence. Section IV: The Method of Analysis and the Two Chosen Cases Section IV addresses simultaneous variations in duration, productivity, and intensity. Marx notes the combinatorial explosion—\"Any two of the factors may vary and the third remain constant, or all three may vary at once\"; variations may be equal or unequal, same or opposed in direction, and \"the variations cancel each other out, either wholly or in part.\" His methodological claim is that \"every possible case can easily be analysed by using the results obtained in cases I, II and III. The effect of every possible combination may be found by treating each factor in turn as variable, and the other two as constant for the time being.\" He then selects two important cases: (1) diminishing productivity with simultaneous lengthening of the working day, and (2) increasing intensity and productivity with simultaneous shortening of the working day. Section IV: Case One — Diminishing Productivity with a Lengthening Day Marx limits the case \"particularly\" to \"those industries whose products determine the value of labour-power; we have in mind, for example, diminishing productivity resulting from the decreasing fertility of the soil, and from the corresponding increase in the prices of its products.\" He sets up: a 12-hour day, a value-product of 6s, half replacing v (=3s), half s (=3s). Then \"let a rise in the prices of the products of the soil occur, so that the value of labour-power goes up from 3 shillings to 4, and the necessary labour-time therefore rises from 6 hours to 8.\" Three sub-cases follow: Day unaltered (12 hours): surplus labour falls from 6 to 4 hours, surplus-value falls from 3s to 2s. Day lengthened by 2 hours (to 14): necessary labour = 8 hours, surplus labour remains at 6 hours. The text states \"the surplus-value will remain at 6 shillings\"—this is almost certainly a transcription error for 3 shillings , since the value-product of a 14-hour day is 7s, and 7 − 4 = 3. The subsequent claim \"the relative magnitude of the surplus-value will decrease in comparison with the magnitude of the value of labour-power\" only makes sense if s = 3s relative to v = 4s (a ratio of 3:4, down from 1:1). The apparent inconsistency is worth flagging: the logic of the passage requires the corrected figure. Day lengthened by 4 hours (to 16): value-product = 8s; v = 4s; s = 4s. \"The proportional magnitudes of surplus-value and value of labour-power, of surplus labour and necessary labour, will continue unchanged [8:8 = 1:1], but the absolute magnitude of surplus-value will rise from 3 shillings to 4, and that of the surplus labour from 6 hours to 8, an increment of ⅓, or 33⅓ per cent.\" The general conclusion: \"with diminishing productivity of labour and a simultaneous lengthening of the working day, the absolute magnitude of surplus-value may continue unaltered, at the same time as its relative magnitude diminishes; its relative magnitude may continue unaltered at the same time as its absolute magnitude increases; and, finally, if the working day is lengthened to a sufficient extent, both may increase.\" Section IV: The Historical Intervention — 1799–1815 and the Ricardo–West Error Marx grounds the abstract case in a historical refutation. \"In the period between 1799 and 1815 an increase in the prices of the means of subsistence led in England to a nominal rise in wages, although there was a fall in real wages, as expressed in the quantity of the means of subsistence they would purchase.\" From this fact \"West and Ricardo drew the conclusion that the diminution in the productivity of agricultural labour had brought about a fall in the rate of surplus-value, and they made this assumption (which was purely a product of their own imagination) the starting-point of important investigations into the relative magnitudes of wages, profits and ground rent.\" The footnote cites West's Essay on the Application of Capital to Land (1815) and Ricardo's An Essay on the Influence of a Low Price of Corn on the Profits of Stock (1815)—\"contributions to the controversy of 1815 over the Corn Laws.\" Marx's counter-claim: \"as a matter of fact surplus-value had at that time increased both in absolute and in relative magnitude, thanks to the increased intensity of labour, and to the prolongation of the working day which had been forced upon the workers.\" He paints the period as one in which \"the right to prolong the working day without any restriction at all became accepted as one of the basic rights of the citizen,\" characterized by \"a rapid growth of capital\" and \"a rapid growth of pauperism.\" Footnote 7 quotes Malthus: \"Corn and labour rarely march quite abreast; but there is an obvious limit, beyond which they cannot be separated. With regard to the unusual exertions made by the labouring classes in periods of dearness... they are most meritorious in the individuals, and certainly favour the growth of capital. But no man of humanity could wish to see them constant and unremitted.\" Marx praises Malthus for drawing attention to the lengthening of hours, noting that Ricardo and others \"flying in the face of the most notorious facts, make invariability in the length of the working day the groundwork of all their investigations.\" But he immediately criticizes Malthus: \"the conservative interests Malthus served prevented him from seeing that unlimited prolongation of the working day, combined with an extraordinary development of machinery and the exploitation of the labour of women and children, inevitably made a great portion of the working class 'redundant', particularly after the cessation of wartime demand and the ending of the English monopoly of the world market.\" Malthus preferred to explain \"over-population\" by \"the eternal laws of nature\" rather than \"the merely historical laws of the nature of capitalist production.\" Footnote 8 quotes the Essays on Political Economy (1830) on how the increase of capital during the war proceeded \"from the greater exertions, and perhaps the greater privations of the labouring classes.\" Section IV: Case Two — Rising Intensity and Productivity with a Shortening Day The second chosen case pairs increasing intensity and productivity with a shortening of the working day. Marx observes: \"Increased productivity and greater intensity of labour both have a similar effect. They both augment the mass of articles produced in a given time. Both therefore shorten that portion of the working day which the worker needs to produce his means of subsistence or their equivalent.\" The minimum length of the working day \"is fixed by this necessary component, which is however itself capable of further contraction. If the whole working day were to shrink to the length of its necessary component, surplus labour would vanish, something which is impossible under the regime of capital.\" This is the absolute limit internal to capitalist production: the system cannot allow the working day to collapse into necessary labour, because surplus labour is its raison d'être . \"Only the abolition of the capitalist form of production would permit the reduction of the working day to the necessary labour-time.\" But Marx immediately complicates the counterfactual: \"even in that case the latter would expand to take up more of the day, and for two reasons: first, because the worker's conditions of life would improve, and his aspirations become greater, and second, because a part of what is now surplus labour would then count as necessary labour, namely the labour which is necessary for the formation of a social fund for reserve and accumulation.\" This is a critical theoretical move. The distinction between necessary and surplus labour is not a physical constant but a social-historical category. Under socialism, necessary labour expands both because the worker's needs are historically elevated and because a portion of what was surplus is reclassified as socially necessary (for reserve and accumulation). The boundary between necessary and surplus is thus a function of the mode of production, not of nature—a point that anticipates the critique of \"naturalizing\" the wage relation. Section IV: The Emancipatory Horizon — Necessity, Freedom, and the Universality of Labour The chapter's final paragraphs pivot from analytic to normative-historical. Marx writes: \"The more the productivity of labour increases, the more the working day can be shortened, and the more the working day is shortened, the more the intensity of labour can increase.\" This reciprocal dynamic is the historical trajectory of capitalist development in compressed form. He adds: \"From the point of view of society the productivity of labour also grows when economies are made in its use. This implies not only economizing on the means of production, but also avoiding all useless labour.\" Capitalist production achieves economy within each individual business, yet \"begets, by its anarchic system of competition, the most outrageous squandering of labour-power and of the social means of production, not to mention the creation of a vast number of functions at present indispensable, but in themselves superfluous.\" This is the contradiction between private economy and social waste —a theme that runs through Marx's critique of capitalism. The final formulation is classically Marxian: \"The intensity and productivity of labour being given, the part of the social working day necessarily taken up with material production is shorter and, as a consequence, the time at society's disposal for the free intellectual and social activity of the individual is greater, in proportion as work is more and more evenly divided among all the able-bodied members of society, and a particular social stratum is more and more deprived of the ability to shift the burden of labour (which is a necessity imposed by nature) from its own shoulders to those of another social stratum.\" The key variable, then, is the distribution of labour across the population : the more evenly work is shared, the more free time for free intellectual and social activity becomes possible for all. The conclusion strikes a definitive chord: \"The absolute minimum limit to the shortening of the working day is, from this point of view, the universality [Allgemeinheit] of labour. In capitalist society, free time is produced for one class by the conversion of the whole lifetime of the masses into labour-time.\" The German term Allgemeinheit —universality or generality—names the historical condition in which labour is shared among all and no group can offload its burden onto another. In capitalism, free time for the few is literally purchased by the lifetime labour of the many. The chapter thus closes by returning the reader to the historical and moral stakes of the analytic machinery: what looked like a technical taxonomy of variable magnitudes turns out to be an anatomy of the deepest structural antagonism of modern society. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Chapter 17 yields several fundamental principles that together form a kind of \"conservation law\" of capitalist exploitation. The first is the zero-sum logic of the fixed working day : when length and intensity are constant, the value produced is a fixed magnitude, so any gain to the worker is a loss to the capitalist, and vice versa. This is the structural reason why wage bargaining under fixed hours is a class struggle, and why capital has an intrinsic interest in shifting to the second and third dimensions—intensity and length—where the pie can grow. The second principle is the positive-sum logic of lengthened or intensified labour : when the working day is extended or its intensity increased, the total value produced grows, so both the wage and surplus-value can rise simultaneously. This reconciles the seemingly paradoxical historical record (rising wages alongside rising profits) without abandoning the theory of exploitation. It also explains why capital historically favored lengthening the working day over raising wages, and why factory legislation was so fiercely resisted. The third principle is the asymmetric logic of proportional change : the same quantity change produces a larger proportional effect on the smaller portion. Since surplus-value is typically the smaller portion of the working day relative to necessary labour in the empirical cases Marx analyses, productivity and intensity gains tend to increase surplus-value more rapidly (in proportional terms) than they reduce the value of labour-power. This is the quantitative core of Marx's critique of Ricardo. The fourth principle is the distinction between the value and the price of labour-power , and the resulting possibility that wages can rise above value (when productivity outpaces wage adjustment) or fall below value (when intensity or length increase beyond the threshold of normal reproduction). The price of labour-power is thus a floor-and-ceiling variable bounded by value on one side and by class struggle on the other. The fifth principle is the social-historical character of the necessary/surplus boundary . Whether a given hour of labour counts as necessary or surplus is not fixed by biology or technology but by the historically conditioned standard of the worker's needs and the social organization of the mode of production. This is why capitalism cannot tolerate the collapse of the working day into necessary labour, and why socialism would not simply preserve that collapse but redefine necessary labour to include the social fund for reserve and accumulation. The broader implications are profound. Politically, the chapter grounds the strategic logic of the labor movement: since capital can offset shorter hours by intensification or productivity gains, workers must fight on all three dimensions simultaneously—which is exactly what the historical Ten Hours' movement and later struggles over the length, intensity, and productivity of work effectively did. Theoretically, the chapter delivers the definitive critique of classical political economy: Ricardo's identification of \"profits\" with surplus-value, and his conflation of the rate of surplus-value with the rate of profit, are shown to be category errors with cascading consequences. Marx's separation of the two rates—demonstrated numerically with the £500 example (100% rate of surplus-value, 20% rate of profit)—lays the foundation for Volume 3 and for the entire theory of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall. Philosophically, the chapter enacts Marx's method: rigorous abstraction (isolating variables), concrete differentiation (distinguishing productivity from intensity, value from price, rate of surplus-value from rate of profit), and historical specification (grounding abstraction in the 1799–1815 Corn Law controversy, the Ten Hours' Act, and the class struggles of the period). The dialectical tension between absolute improvement and relative immiseration—where workers can consume more than before while their social position deteriorates relative to the capitalist class—captures the peculiar character of modern capitalism: it does not necessarily impoverish workers in absolute material terms, but it reproduces and deepens class inequality as a structural feature of accumulation. And the closing invocation of Allgemeinheit —the universality of labour—points beyond capitalism toward a society in which free time is no longer the privilege of one class purchased by the lifetime labour of another. The chapter thus achieves what few technical treatises achieve: it is at once a precise analytic specification of exploitation and a compressed historical and political argument for its abolition. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 16 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 18"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 18", "title": "Chapter 18", "subtitle": "Different Formulae for the Rate of Surplu", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 18/Chapter 18  Different Formulae for the Rate of Surplu_explanation.html", "word_count": 5883, "read_time": "29 min read", "tags": ["Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["Different Formulae for Rate of Surplus-Value", "Critique of Classical Economics Value Ratios", "Surplus Product vs Surplus-Value", "Surplus Labour / Necessary Labour Equivalents", "Unpaid Labour vs Paid Labour"], "text": "Chapter 18 Different Formulae For The Rate Of Surplu - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,834 words ⏱️ ~29 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 18, \"Different Formulae for the Rate of Surplus-Value,\" is the closing chapter of Part V of Capital , Volume I — the part devoted to \"The Production of Absolute and Relative Surplus-Value.\" It is a short chapter (five pages, roughly 1,770 words) and it introduces no new empirical material, no new historical investigation, and no new factory report. What it does instead is something rarer and, structurally, something more consequential: it formalizes . It gathers up the conceptual labour of the preceding chapters — the working day, necessary and surplus labour, the value of labour-power, variable capital, the value-product — and asks a deceptively simple question: in what mathematical shape should the exploitation of labour be written down? The central problem is that one and the same social relation admits of several distinct symbolic representations, and these representations are not interchangeable in their effects. Marx presents three sets of formulae. Formula I — surplus-value over variable capital; surplus-value over the value of labour-power; surplus labour over necessary labour — is, he says, \"rigorously definite and correct,\" and it captures the real degree of exploitation . Formula III — surplus-value over the value of labour-power; surplus labour over necessary labour; unpaid labour over paid labour — is a restatement of the same truth in \"popular\" idiom, and it carries a conceptual trap that must be disarmed. Formula II — surplus labour over the working day; surplus-value over the value-product; surplus product over total product — is derivative , and although it expresses the identical proportion, it \"falsely\" expresses the rate of surplus-value when it is treated as a direct expression of capital's self-valorization. From that diagnosis follows the chapter's polemical core. If Formula II is read as the measure of exploitation, an \"erroneous law\" results: surplus labour or surplus-value can never reach 100 per cent . And a measure that structurally forbids its own object from exceeding a ceiling of one hundred has three downstream effects, all of them intellectual and political at once: it fixes the working day as a constant magnitude (thereby naturalizing a historically contested, class-determined variable); it conceals the specific character of the capital-relation — namely that variable capital is exchanged for living labour-power and that the worker is therefore excluded from the product ; and it substitutes for exploitation the \"false semblance of a relation of association,\" in which worker and capitalist appear as partners dividing the proceeds in proportion to their respective contributions. The chapter's intellectual objective is thus threefold. First, to fix the correct measure — the rate of surplus-value — as the index of the \"degree of exploitation,\" and to demonstrate its unboundedness (Marx cites Lavergne's estimate of English agricultural wages to show a real-world rate of 300 per cent). Second, to expose the ideological mechanism by which the derivative formulae invert essence into appearance. Third, and most ambitiously, to convert the analysis of the rate into a definition of its object: \"Capital ... is not only the command over labour, as Adam Smith thought. It is essentially the command over unpaid labour.\" All surplus-value — whether it later crystallizes as profit, interest, or rent — is \"the materialization of unpaid labour-time,\" and the secret of capital's self-valorization is nothing more than its \"disposal over a definite quantity of the unpaid labour of other people [ fremder Arbeit ].\" Positioned as it is, the chapter functions as a hinge. It closes the theory of surplus-value production and opens onto the theory of wages (Part VI) by insisting on the distinction between the value of labour-power and the value of labour; it prepares the theory of accumulation (Part VII) by fixing the rate whose magnitude accumulation will presuppose; and it pre-announces the later analysis of the forms of surplus-value — profit, interest, rent — as so many disguises of a single substance. Methodologically, it is also a compact demonstration of Marx's general procedure: the modes of appearance generated by capitalist production itself are not mere errors of economists, they are real representational forms — \"this mode of presentation ... arises, by the way, out of the capitalist mode of production itself\" — whose inversion of the essence must be scientifically undone. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS I. The Opening Move: One Relation, Three Symbolic Registers Marx opens by simply laying out Formula I as an established result: $$\\frac{\\text{Surplus-value }(s)}{\\text{Variable capital}} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\text{Surplus-value}}{\\text{Value of labour-power}} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\text{Surplus labour}}{\\text{Necessary labour}}$$ The first two members are ratios of values ; the third is a ratio of the times during which those values are produced. His claim is that these are \"mutually replaceable\" and \"rigorously definite and correct.\" The replaceability is the crucial theoretical assertion: because value is congealed labour-time, and because the value of labour-power is itself determined by the labour-time socially necessary to reproduce the worker, a ratio of values and a ratio of times are two notations for one thing. The equality is not a convenience; it is the direct expression of the labour theory of value in the domain of exploitation. This is why the formula can be written indifferently as a money ratio (3s./3s.), a time ratio (6 hours/6 hours), or a percentage (100 per cent), without any loss. The historical note attached here is important and characteristically generous in form, sharp in substance: classical political economy \"worked out\" these formulae \"in substance, but not in a conscious form.\" Smith and Ricardo possessed the content — they knew that profit derived from the unpaid labour of the worker, that rent was a deduction — but they never isolated the rate as a formal category, and so they drifted into the derivative formulae of Set II. This is the Marxian theory of the relationship to predecessors in miniature: not invention but the elevation of an implicit content into conscious, explicit form. The French-translation footnote attached to the third member of Formula I reinforces this claim: Marx there placed parentheses around the \"surplus labour/necessary labour\" expression \"because the concept of surplus labour is not found clearly expressed in bourgeois political economy.\" The parenthesis is a small, almost pedantic editorial gesture, and it is also a precise historical judgement: the third term is Marx's own contribution, and by bracketing it he indicates where the classics stopped. II. Why Formula I Is \"Rigorously Definite and Correct\" It is worth unpacking why Formula I earns the description \"rigorously definite.\" Consider variable capital . It is capital advanced for the purchase of labour-power; its magnitude equals the value of that labour-power, i.e. the value of the means of subsistence required to reproduce the worker at a given historical and moral standard. But this value is realized only in the necessary labour portion of the working day — the portion in which the worker reproduces the equivalent of his own wage. Hence variable capital and necessary labour are the same magnitude in two units. The denominator of Formula I therefore measures the cost of the worker to capital , and the numerator measures the surplus value obtained beyond that cost . The ratio is thus precisely a rate of return on the one capital outlay whose consumption generates new value. Two properties follow, and both will matter. First, the denominator is strictly positive — necessary labour cannot be zero, because a worker who receives nothing cannot reproduce himself and the system cannot continue. Second, the ratio itself has no upper bound. Nothing in the definition of $s/v$ restricts it to 1 or any other value; it can be 50 per cent, 100 per cent, 300 per cent, or higher, depending on the relation between the length of the working day and the value of labour-power. Formula I is \"definite\" in the sense that it names the thing it measures without distortion: it is the rate at which capital expands itself by consuming the labour-power it has purchased. III. The Derivative Formulae (II) and the Handling of Constant Capital Marx then presents the second set, which he calls derivative : $$\\frac{\\text{Surplus labour}}{\\text{Working day}} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\text{Surplus-value}}{\\text{Value of the product}} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\text{Surplus product}}{\\text{Total product}}$$ Here the same proportion is expressed \"alternately in the form of labour-times, of the values in which those labour-times are embodied, and of the products in which those values exist.\" Three registers again — time, value, and physical product — but with a different denominator. Under I the denominator was the worker's share (necessary labour, variable capital); under II the denominator is the whole (working day, value-product, total product). The numerator is unchanged; only the base has been shifted from a part to the whole. A clarification immediately follows, and it is easy to miss its significance: by \"value of the product\" the political economists mean \"only the value newly created in a working day, the constant part of the value of the product being excluded.\" This is a technical but decisive restriction. The value of the product in the full sense would be $c + v + s$, including the value transferred from raw materials and machinery. What Formula II's middle term uses is the value-product , i.e. $v + s$ — the new value added by living labour in the day. This same exclusion is carried into the empirical footnote: Lavergne's estimate of the agricultural labourer's share \"of course\" leaves out \"that part of the product which merely replaces the constant capital advanced.\" Without this exclusion the ratio would be meaningless as a measure of the division between wages and surplus, because constant capital is neither wage nor surplus. The restriction also matters because it shows that Formula II is not simply careless arithmetic — it is a correct description of a different thing , namely the division of the freshly created value between the two classes. IV. The Arithmetical Demonstration: Twelve Hours, Six and Six, Three Shillings and Three Shillings Marx then makes the argument numerically, using the same assumptions as the earlier chapters: a twelve-hour working day, a labour-power value of 3 shillings corresponding to six hours, and therefore six hours of surplus labour producing 3 shillings of surplus-value. Under Formula I: $$\\frac{6\\text{ hours surplus labour}}{6\\text{ hours necessary labour}} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\text{surplus-value of 3s.}}{\\text{variable capital of 3s.}} \\;=\\; 100 \\text{ per cent}$$ Under Formula II, on the same facts: $$\\frac{6\\text{ hours surplus labour}}{12\\text{ hours working day}} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\text{surplus-value of 3s.}}{\\text{value-product of 6s.}} \\;=\\; 50 \\text{ per cent}$$ The same day, the same exploitation, two answers: 100 per cent and 50 per cent. This is the pivot of the whole chapter. The formal relation between the two measures is worth stating explicitly, because Marx's arithmetic implies it even where he does not write the algebra. Let $e = s/v$ be the rate of surplus-value. Then the Formula II ratio is $s/(v+s) = e/(1+e)$. Conversely, $e = [s/(v+s)] / [1 - s/(v+s)]$. So a Formula I rate of 100 per cent ($e = 1$) corresponds to a Formula II ratio of $1/2 = 50$ per cent; a Formula I rate of 300 per cent ($e = 3$) corresponds to $3/4 = 75$ per cent; and as $e \\to \\infty$, the Formula II ratio approaches but never exceeds 1. The two measures are related by a monotone transformation — which is precisely why the confusion is so seductive and so dangerous. Nothing is arithmetically wrong. The meaning has been replaced. V. The Erroneous Law: Why the Share Can Never Reach 100 Per Cent From the misreading follows the \"erroneous law\": surplus labour or surplus-value can never reach 100 per cent . Marx's derivation of it is a small model of rigorous inference. The surplus labour is only \"an aliquot part\" of the working day — that is, an exact fractional portion of a whole — and surplus-value is likewise only an aliquot part of the value-product. A part must be less than its whole. Therefore surplus labour must always be less than the working day, and surplus-value always less than the value-product. For the ratio to reach $100:100$ — unity — the two terms would have to be equal . They cannot be, because one is contained in the other. Q.E.D. The \"law\" is thus not an empirical generalization but a definitional artefact of the wrong denominator. It is worth stressing that Marx is not accusing the political economists of an arithmetic mistake. He grants that Formula II expresses \"in reality, only the proportion in which the working day, or the value produced by it, is divided between the capitalist and the worker.\" That proportion is real, it is measurable, and it does cap out at 100 per cent — as any proportional share must. The error is categorical: to mistake a share for a rate, to treat a distribution ratio as \"a direct expression of the degree of capital's self-valorization.\" VI. The Self-Dissolving Limit: Why the Whole-Day Asymptote Cannot Be Attained Marx then pushes the argument one step further and produces a genuinely dialectical result. Having said that surplus labour must be less than the working day, he asks what would be required for the gap to close: \"In order for the surplus labour to absorb the whole day (i.e. an average day of any week or year) the necessary labour would have to sink to zero. But if necessary labour vanishes, surplus labour does as well, since it is only a function of the necessary [labour].\" The limit is not merely unattained; it is self-dissolving . Surplus labour is not an independent magnitude that could in principle swallow the day; it is defined in relation to necessary labour, and it exists only as the excess over that necessary labour. Annihilate the base and the excess vanishes with it. Hence: \"The ratio $\\frac{\\text{surplus labour}}{\\text{working day}}$, or $\\frac{\\text{surplus-value}}{\\text{value-product}}$, can therefore never reach the limit of $100/100$, still less rise above it.\" The Formula II measure is asymptotically bounded above by unity and structurally prevented from reaching it. Note the parenthetical \"(i.e. an average day of any week or year)\" — a small but characteristic precaution: the comparison must be made over an average period, since daily hours fluctuate, and the point holds for the average as much as for any single day. Contrast this with the rate of surplus-value, which, \"however, the real degree of exploitation of labour, is able to do this\" — is able to reach and exceed 100 per cent. The two measures thus differ not merely in magnitude but in their structural ceilings : one is bounded by 1 and cannot attain it; the other is unbounded. That is the formal statement of the chapter's central contrast. VII. The Empirical Refutation: Lavergne and the 300 Per Cent Rate Marx does not leave the unboundedness of the rate as a purely formal possibility. He supplies an empirical instance: the estimate of Léonce de Lavergne, according to which the English agricultural labourer receives only one-quarter of the product (or of its value), while the capitalist farmer takes three-quarters, \"whatever the subsequent division of the booty between the capitalist, the landowner and others.\" On that calculation, the labourer's surplus labour stands to his necessary labour as 3:1, \"which gives a rate of exploitation of 300 per cent.\" This figure is doing several jobs at once. First, it demonstrates concretely that rates above 100 per cent occur in the real world, so the \"erroneous law\" is not merely internally arbitrary but empirically false. Second, it illustrates the relationship between the two measures on real data: a 300 per cent rate of exploitation corresponds under Formula II to a share of three-quarters — a number comfortably under 100 per cent, and therefore perfectly consistent with the false law. Third, it introduces a distinction Marx will build on later: the decomposition of the surplus itself among capitalist, landowner, and others. The \"booty\" is one quantity, differently divided; the fact of appropriation is prior to the distribution of appropriated value. In a footnote Marx adds his own critical caveat about the source: Lavergne, \"as a blind admirer of England, is inclined to estimate the share of the capitalist too low, rather than too high.\" Even the empirical illustration is handled with methodological scepticism. VIII. The Political Effect: Fixing the Working Day as a Constant Having exposed the erroneous law, Marx draws out its first consequence: \"Through the use of the formulae given under II, the political economists' favourite method of treating the working day as constant in magnitude became a fixed usage, because in those formulae surplus labour is always compared with a working day of a given length.\" This is a superb observation about how the shape of a formula governs thought. Under Formula II, the denominator is the working day, taken as a fixed quantum; the measure can only register movements in the numerator. The formula therefore cannot, in and of itself, express a change in the length of the working day — precisely the variable that was the central object of class struggle in the nineteenth century and the central object of Marx's own Chapters 8 and 10. By writing the relation this way, the political economists embedded a presupposition — a given working day — into their analytic apparatus, so that the historical and political determination of the working day's length simply dropped out of view. It is worth underlining that this is precisely the criticism Marx developed at length in the earlier chapters on the struggle over the working day: the length of the working day is not a natural constant but a contested, historically variable magnitude won through legislation and struggle. Formula II, whatever its author's intentions, renders the outcome of that struggle invisible — it appears as an unargued premise, not as a battleground. IX. The Ideological Effect: From Exploitation to \"Association\" The second consequence is deeper and is stated with Marx's characteristic move from logic to social form. When political economy treats surplus-value and the value of labour-power as fractions of the value-product , it \"conceal[s] the specific character of the capital-relation, namely the fact that variable capital is exchanged for living labour-power, and that the worker is accordingly excluded from the product.\" Three distinct concealments are being named. First, the object of exchange is obscured: capital buys labour-power, not labour, and the formula's tidy partitioning of \"the value-product\" whispers that the worker is a participant in the output. Second, the exclusion of the worker from the product is obscured: the product belongs, legally and economically, to the capitalist; the worker never owned it, and receives a wage, not a share. Third, and most insidiously, \"instead of revealing the capital-relation they show us the false semblance of a relation of association, in which worker and capitalist divide the product in proportion to the different elements which they respectively contribute towards its formation.\" The phrase \"false semblance\" ( Schein in the Hegelian register) is precisely chosen. It is not a lie told by dishonest economists; it is the appearance generated by the surface of the capitalist process, in which two parties appear to meet, exchange, and part with their respective returns. Marx flags this explicitly: this mode of presentation \"arises, by the way, out of the capitalist mode of production itself, and whose significance we shall unearth later on.\" He does not discharge the promissory note in this chapter; he records it, so that the apologetic capacity of the surface-form can be traced back to its source in the real relations rather than to mere error. The footnote to this passage is a small masterpiece of sarcasm. Since \"all the developed forms of the capitalist process of production are forms of co-operation, nothing is easier ... than to make abstraction from their specifically antagonistic character, and, merely by verbal alterations, make them sound like forms of free association.\" He names Count A. de Laborde's De l'esprit d'association dans tous les intérêts de la communauté (Paris, 1818), and then H. Carey \"the Yankee,\" who \"occasionally performs this conjuring trick, with similar success, even with the relations prevailing under slavery.\" The point is aimed at a whole tradition — the harmonious-association school that reaches from the Restoration through Carey's American protectionism and beyond — and it makes explicit that the ideological payoff of the derivative formulae is not incidental but essential. X. The Rodbertus Footnote: Marx's Judgement of Predecessors, and Engels's Correction The first footnote of the chapter opens a different front. Marx cites \"for example\" Rodbertus's Sociale Briefe an von Kirchmann (Third Letter, Berlin, 1851) as a locus of the erroneous law, and adds a characteristically generous appraisal: in spite of its erroneous theory of rent, the pamphlet \"sees through the nature of capitalist production,\" and Marx promises to return to it later — a promise discharged, as the editorial note points out, in Theories of Surplus-Value , Part 2, Chapters 8 and 9. Engels, preparing the third German edition, appended a correction that is historically and theoretically revealing. He observes that \"it may be seen from this how favourably Marx judged his predecessors, whenever he found in them a real advance, or new and sound ideas,\" and then reports that the publication of Rodbertus's letters to Rudolf Meyer showed \"that the above acknowledgement needs to be restricted to some extent.\" In those letters Rodbertus wrote that \"capital must be rescued not only from labour, but from itself,\" best achieved \"by treating the acts of the industrial capitalist as economic and political functions that have been delegated to him with his capital, and by treating his profit as a form of salary\"; he added that \"the irruption of Marx into society ... must be warded off,\" accusing Marx's book of being \"not so much an investigation of capital as a polemic against the present form of capital, a form which he confuses with the concept of capital itself.\" Engels's verdict on this collapse is dry: \"The bold onslaught mounted by Rodbertus in his 'social letters' finally dwindled down to ideological commonplaces of this kind.\" The footnote thus accomplishes three things simultaneously. It documents the prevalence of the \"erroneous law\" (Rodbertus held it). It exhibits Marx's method of scholarly acknowledgement — credit for the genuine insight, criticism for the error, no retrospective settling of scores. And through Engels's addition, it administers a sharp lesson about the difference between a critique of capital and a reformist proposal to pay off capital with a salary — thereby insulating the chapter's own argument (that capital is command over unpaid labour) against precisely the misreading that Rodbertus exemplifies. XI. Reconversion: How Formula II Collapses Back into Formula I After the critique, Marx shows the escape route. \"But in any case, the formulae given under III can always be reconverted into the original formulae.\" (The text's \"III\" here appears to be a typographical artefact for \"II\" — the formulae under discussion are exhausted by I and II; this is the reading consistent with what follows.) The demonstration is arithmetic: $$\\frac{\\text{Surplus labour of 6 hours}}{\\text{Working day of 12 hours}} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\text{necessary labour} = 12 - 6 = 6 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\frac{\\text{Surplus labour of 6 hours}}{\\text{Necessary labour of 6 hours}} = 100 \\text{ per cent}$$ The reversal is exactly the algebraic inverse noted above: since necessary labour is the working day minus surplus labour, the whole is decomposable into its two parts, and the ratio can be rewritten on the correct (part-to-part) base. Two things follow. First, the error of Formula II is remediable — it is not a mathematical dead end but a representational choice, and the choice can be unmade. Second, and importantly for the political argument: the reconversion shows that nothing is actually concealed from the analyst who knows how to read the whole. The concealment operates only for those who take the share for the rate. XII. Formula III: Unpaid Labour and the Trap of Popular Language Marx then returns to a formulation he says he has \"occasionally anticipated\": $$\\frac{\\text{Surplus-value}}{\\text{Value of labour-power}} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\text{Surplus labour}}{\\text{Necessary labour}} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\text{Unpaid labour}}{\\text{Paid labour}}$$ The first two members are Formula I restated. The third is a popular gloss, and Marx immediately warns against its dangers: \"After the analysis given above, it is no longer possible to be misled by the formula unpaid labour / paid labour into concluding that the capitalist pays for labour and not for labour-power .\" The trap is elementary but consequential. If capital paid for labour , then all labour would be paid for, and surplus-value would be inexplicable. The formula unpaid labour / paid labour is safe only when the reader already understands that the wage is the price of labour-power, and that \"unpaid labour\" is not a refused wage but a description of that portion of the working day in which the worker produces value for which no equivalent is returned. Hence: \"The capitalist pays the value of the labour-power (or, if the price diverges from this, he pays the price) and receives in exchange the right to dispose of the living labour-power itself.\" The distinction between value and price is admitted without fuss in the parenthesis, but the essential point is the object of the exchange: not the labour, but the capacity to labour. The popular phrase thus becomes, in the hands of a careless reader, exactly the formulation that destroys the theory it was meant to express. XIII. The Two Periods of the Working Day: The Equivalent and the Surplus Marx then spells out in plain narrative why \"unpaid labour\" is the right popular phrase, and in doing so recapitulates the whole mechanism of surplus-value. The time during which the capitalist utilizes the labour-power \"is divided into two separate periods.\" In the first, \"the worker produces a value that is only equal to the value of his labour-power, i.e. he produces its equivalent. Thus the capitalist receives, in return for advancing the price of the labour-power, a product of the same price. It is the same as if he had bought the product ready-made in the market.\" This is the necessary-labour phase — pure exchange, no surplus, a transaction that would satisfy the most rigorous standard of equivalent exchange. In the second period, \"the period of surplus labour, the utilization of the labour-power creates a value for the capitalist without costing him any value in return. He is thus able to set labour-power in motion without paying for it. It is in this sense that surplus labour can be called unpaid labour.\" The phrasing \"It is the same as if he had bought the product ready-made in the market\" is doing defensive work. It shows that the first phase contains no injustice in the terms of commodity exchange; the appropriation occurs entirely in the second phase, where a purchased capacity is made to yield more than it cost. The inference \"It is in this sense that surplus labour can be called unpaid labour\" is therefore carefully hedged: \"in this sense \" — not in the sense that a wage has been withheld, but in the sense that the value it produced has no counterpart in what was paid for it. XIV. The Two Complementary Footnotes: The French Parentheses and the Physiocrats Two footnotes round out the chapter's scholarly apparatus and both bear on the historical argument. One, attached to the \"surplus labour/necessary labour\" member of Formula I, records that Marx placed parentheses round that expression in the French translation \"because the concept of surplus labour is not found clearly expressed in bourgeois political economy.\" As noted earlier, this marks the third member of Formula I as Marx's own theoretical addition, distinguishing his formula from what the classics actually wrote. The other footnote, appended to the analysis of unpaid labour, concedes a partial foresight to the Physiocrats: \"Although the Physiocrats could not penetrate the mystery of surplus-value, this much was clear to them, that it was 'independent and disposable wealth, which he' (the possessor of the surplus value) 'has not bought, and nevertheless sells' (Turgot, Réflexions sur la formation et la distribution des richesses , p. 11).\" Turgot's phrase captures the whole logic in a single image: a wealth that was never purchased yet is freely disposed of and sold . That is the essence of surplus-value, seen through the eyes of an eighteenth-century Frenchman who nonetheless believed agriculture alone produced it. The footnote thus works the same way as the reference to the classics at the chapter's opening: it credits real perception where it existed, while noting the limit of the form in which it was held. XV. The Climax: Capital as Command over Unpaid Labour The chapter ends not with arithmetic but with a redefinition. \"Capital, therefore, is not only the command over labour, as Adam Smith thought. It is essentially the command over unpaid labour.\" The reference to Smith is pointed. In Smith's account, capital dominates labour because the accumulation of stock and the division of labour place the wage-earner under the direction of the employer; \"command over labour\" is a structural feature of advanced exchange. Marx accepts the description and then sharpens it: the defining power of capital is not command over labour as such but command over unpaid labour — its power consists in setting labour in motion and appropriating the surplus product without equivalent. \"All surplus-value, whatever particular form (profit, interest or rent) it may subsequently crystallize into, is in substance the materialization of unpaid labour-time.\" This sentence is the seed of the entire later analysis of the forms of surplus-value. Profit, interest, and rent, which appear in the economic consciousness as three independent sources of income and as three distinct revenues of three distinct classes (enterprise, finance, landownership), are here declared to be one substance in three crystallized shapes: materialized unpaid labour-time. The dissolution of that appearance into its essence is the project of the volumes to come. And the chapter closes by naming the resolution of its central problem: \"The secret of the self-valorization of capital resolves itself into the fact that it has at its disposal a definite quantity of the unpaid labour of other people [ fremder Arbeit ].\" Every term is loaded. \"A definite quantity\" — quantification matters, because the amount of unpaid labour is what the rate of surplus-value measures and what the working day limits. \"Others'\" — the German fremder registers at once \"of others\" and \"alien,\" so that the alienated labour of the worker is the very material of capital's autonomy. And \"self-valorization\" — the appearance that value magically expands of its own accord is dispelled: the expansion is nothing but the absorption of alien labour-time. The rate of surplus-value, then, was never a technical curiosity. It was, from the beginning, the arithmetic of a social relation. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The first and most portable principle this chapter establishes is that the measure is not neutral . Two ratios drawn from the same facts — 100 per cent and 50 per cent — are the same proportion of the same day, and yet they license opposite conclusions about the nature and the limits of exploitation. A formula is a theory in shorthand: its denominator encodes what the relation is being measured against , and with that choice come commitments about what the phenomenon is. To divide by the worker's share is to ask how much surplus is extracted per unit of the worker's cost, and to get an unbounded rate. To divide by the whole day is to ask what fraction of the product is taken, and to get a bounded share. Both questions are legitimate; only the first answers the question \"what is the degree of exploitation?\" Marx's lesson is that the choice of denominator is not a matter of taste but a theoretical decision with ideological consequences. The second principle is the structural distinction between rate and share . The share is bounded by 1; the rate is not. Any habit of thought that treats the worker's proportional share of the value-product as the measure of exploitation will be blind to rising rates whenever the working day lengthens or the value of labour-power falls — because a higher rate simply moves the share asymptotically toward, but never beyond, 100 per cent. This is a general analytic trap, not a nineteenth-century curiosity: any bounded distributional indicator is a systematically misleading proxy for an unbounded relational magnitude. The third principle is dialectical, and it emerges from the as-yet-unreachable limit: surplus labour is \"only a function of the necessary [labour].\" Exploitation is not an independent quantum that might in principle consume the day; it exists only as the excess over reproduction. Necessary labour and surplus labour are therefore relational, not additive, magnitudes — destroy one and the other collapses with it. This is why the concept of a \"limit\" to exploitation cannot be modelled as a mere upper bound on a share; the limit dissolves itself when approached. Exploitation is structurally dependent on the very element it appears to threaten. The fourth principle is definitional: capital is command over unpaid labour . The chapter corrects Adam Smith, but not by rejection — it accepts Smith's description of capital as command over labour and identifies what that command is for . The command is not the essence; the appropriation without equivalent is. This is the conceptual foundation for the treatment of profit, interest, and rent as forms of one substance, and it is the answer to the puzzle of self-valorization: value expands because it acquires a claim on the labour-time of others. From these principles follow the broader implications. Politically, the naturalization critique matters: the working day can appear as a constant magnitude only because the formula presupposes it as fixed. Once the presupposition is exposed, the length of the working day reappears for what it was — a variable struggled over, legislated, and enforced. Analytically, the distinction between the value of labour-power and the value of labour, adumbrated in the warning against the phrase \"unpaid labour,\" is the hinge on which the subsequent critique of the wage-form turns; without it the wage appears to pay for the entire working day, and surplus-value becomes unthinkable. Ideologically, the \"false semblance of a relation of association\" is a durable trope: from Laborde's esprit d'association and Carey's harmonious treatment of slavery, through the twentieth-century language of partnership and stakeholder value, the presentation of the wage relation as co-operation in production recurs because the surface of the capitalist process continues to generate it. Marx's contribution is to show that this appearance is not a lie but an effect , arising out of the mode of production itself — and therefore traceable, analysable, and refutable. Finally, methodologically, the chapter models the relation of critical theory to its predecessors: to find in the classics \"in substance, but not in a conscious form\" what they had not isolated as such, and to mark with a parenthesis (as Marx did in the French translation) exactly where the addition begins. The chapter thus closes Part V by doing three things at once: it fixes the rate, it exposes the form that inverts it, and it names the substance that all the forms — profit, interest, rent — will turn out to conceal. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 17 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 19"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 19", "title": "Chapter 19", "subtitle": "The Transformation of the Value (and Respectively the Price) of Labour-Power into Wages", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 19/Chapter 19  The Transformation of the Value (and Respectively the Price) of Labour-Power into Wages_explanation.html", "word_count": 5099, "read_time": "25 min read", "tags": ["Wages & Value of Labour-Power", "Fetishism & Mystification of Capital"], "keywords": ["Transformation of Value/Price of Labour-Power into Wages", "The Wage Form as Mystification of Exploitation", "Extinction of the Trace of Unpaid Labour", "Critique of the Category 'Value of Labour'", "Wage Contract Ideology"], "text": "Chapter 19 The Transformation Of The Value (And Respectively The Price) Of Labour-Power Into Wages - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,067 words ⏱️ ~25 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS This chapter — the opening salvo of Part Six (\"Wages\") of Capital , Volume 1 — is a surgical intervention into the most self-evident-looking fact of bourgeois society: that the worker sells his labour and is paid a wage for it. Marx's central thesis is that this everyday appearance is a systematic illusion. What the worker actually sells is never labour but labour-power — the capacity to work, embodied in his person — and the wage is the converted form (the money expression) of the value and price of that labour-power, not of labour itself. The category \"value of labour\" is, Marx insists, an irrational expression , as imaginary as the \"value of the earth,\" yet one that arises necessarily from the surface relations of capitalist production and reproduces itself spontaneously as ordinary common sense. The intellectual objective is double-edged. First, analytically , Marx must demonstrate that the phrase \"value of labour\" is not merely imprecise but internally self-destructive: if value is measured by the labour contained in a commodity, then the \"value of a 12-hour working day\" would be twelve hours — a tautology — and any attempt to have living labour exchange directly against objectified labour would either abolish the law of value or abolish capitalism itself. Second, critically , he must explain why this impossible category nonetheless dominates both bourgeois political economy and the consciousness of workers and capitalists alike, and trace how the wage-form conceals the division of the working day into paid and unpaid labour — a concealment that grounds \"all the notions of justice held by both the worker and the capitalist,\" \"all capitalism's illusions about freedom,\" and \"all the apologetic tricks of vulgar economics.\" Situated within the broader architecture of Capital , the chapter is the hinge between the theory of surplus-value (developed in Part Three, where Marx already established that surplus-value arises from the use-value of labour-power) and the concrete analysis of wages in subsequent chapters. It performs the crucial ideological work of the whole volume: showing that the mechanisms generating surplus-value are not only economically necessary but socially invisible , and that classical political economy — Ricardo, Smith, the Physiocrats — inadvertently discovered the truth (value of labour-power) while staying trapped in the false category (\"value of labour\"), thereby handing vulgar economists a \"secure base of operations\" for worshipping appearances. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Illusory Surface: Wage as the \"Price of Labour\" Marx opens not with a refutation but with an act of descriptive fidelity to the surface. \"On the surface of bourgeois society the worker's wage appears as the price of labour, as a certain quantity of money that is paid for a certain quantity of labour.\" This is the form of appearance — the phenomenal level at which everyday economic actors live and reason. From this surface there flow a whole vocabulary: people speak of the \"value of labour,\" call its money expression its \"necessary or natural price,\" and speak of \"market prices of labour\" oscillating above or below that necessary price. The structure of this vocabulary mirrors exactly the way we speak of any commodity (cotton, corn, iron): a natural price gravitationally anchoring fluctuating market prices. The apparent symmetry is precisely what Marx will demolish. The demolition begins with a deceptively simple logical probe. What is the value of a commodity? \"The objective form of the social labour expended in its production.\" How is the quantity of that value measured? \"By the quantity of the labour contained in it.\" Now apply this definition to labour itself: how is the value of, say, a 12-hour working day to be determined? \"By the 12 working hours contained in a working day of 12 hours, which is an absurd tautology.\" Here Marx deploys the classical labour theory of value against the classical category \"value of labour.\" If value = socially necessary labour time, then labour has no value in the technical sense; it is the substance of value, the measure of value, but it cannot be measured by itself as a magnitude that it possesses. This is the first crack. He reinforces this with a second, deeper objection grounded in the temporal structure of sale . \"In order to be sold as a commodity in the market, labour must at all events exist before it is sold. But if the worker were able to endow it with an independent existence, he would be selling a commodity, and not labour.\" This is the pivot of the whole chapter. A commodity must have independent existence before it circulates. Labour, however, is activity ; it does not pre-exist its own performance. What is brought to market before the act of working must therefore be something else — a capacity , embedded in the living worker. The footnote (2) sharpens this to a paradox: labour \"is created the moment it is brought to market; nay, it is brought to market before it is created.\" The very ontology of the supposed commodity \"labour\" is incoherent, because it would have to be sold prior to existing. Footnote 1 (Bailey on Ricardo) supplies the historical preview: Ricardo \"ingeniously enough, avoids a difficulty\" by making the value of labour depend on \"the quantity of labour required to produce wages.\" Marx's gloss is that this is \"similar to saying, that the value of cloth is estimated, not by the quantity of labour bestowed on its production, but by the quantity of labour bestowed on the production of the silver for which the cloth is exchanged.\" Ricardo, in other words, tacitly substituted wages (the value of what the worker consumes) for the value of labour — an unwitting step toward the labour-power concept — but without recognizing what he had done. The Impossibility of Exchange Between Objectified and Living Labour Marx next frames the problem as a fork in the road with only two possible branches, both fatal to the appearance. \"Apart from these contradictions, a direct exchange of money, i.e. of objectified labour, with living labour, would either supersede the law of value, which only begins to develop freely on the basis of capitalist production, or supersede capitalist production itself, which rests directly on wage-labour.\" This is a compressed statement of the foundational contradiction: capitalism requires that exchanges be governed by value (equivalents), yet also requires that the capitalist extract surplus — i.e., that a non-equivalent exchange occur somewhere. If labour itself is the commodity, no such reconciliation is possible. The numeric example makes the dilemma concrete. Suppose the working day is 12 hours and is represented in a monetary value of, say, 6 shillings. Then: Alternative A — Equivalents are exchanged. The worker receives 6 shillings for 12 hours of labour; \"the price of his labour would be equal to the price of his product.\" But then \"he produces no surplus-value for the buyer of his labour, the 6 shillings are not transformed into capital, and the basis of capitalist production vanishes.\" The very premise that he sells his labour as wage-labour presupposes a capitalist existence that this alternative destroys. Alternative B — Non-equivalents are exchanged. The worker receives \"less than 6 shillings,\" i.e., 12 hours of labour are exchanged for 10, 6, etc. hours of labour. But this is worse: \"to equate unequal quantities in this way does not just do away with the determination of value.\" A law that declares 12 hours equal to 6 hours is not a law but the negation of law — \"a self-destructive contradiction cannot be in any way even enunciated or formulated as a law.\" Marx then pre-empts the obvious Hegelian-sounding escape: \"It is no use deducing the exchange of more labour against less from the differences in form in each case, one piece of labour being objectified, the other living.\" That is, one cannot rescue the appearance by arguing that objectified labour is qualitatively different from living labour and therefore commands a premium. This \"way out\" is \"even more absurd,\" because it presupposes the very thing Marx has just refuted: that the value of a commodity is determined by the objectified form of the labour in it. In fact, the opposite is true — and this is his crucial correction, developed on the following page. Value Is Determined by Living Labour, Not Its Objectified Form Here Marx states a decisive principle: \"the value of a commodity is determined not by the quantity of labour actually objectified in it, but by the quantity of living labour necessary to produce it.\" He illustrates with a productivity shock. A commodity \"represents, say, 6 working hours. If an invention is made by which it can be produced in 3 hours, the value, even of the commodity already produced, falls by half. It now represents 3 hours of socially necessary labour instead of the 6 formerly required.\" Note the key phrasing: \"even of the commodity already produced.\" This is the point — the social labour-time required (the socially necessary labour time) is a constantly revalued magnitude, not a fixed quantum crystallized at the moment of production. The determining variable is the living expenditure society must currently make, not the dead labour stored up in the object. This refutes the \"differences in form\" escape route definitively: since value is a function of living labour, you cannot appeal to the objectified character of one side of the exchange to justify a permanent quantitative inequality. From here Marx delivers the chapter's clearest formulation of the corrected category: \"It is not labour which directly confronts the possessor of money on the commodity-market, but rather the worker. What the worker is selling is his labour-power.\" And immediately, the temporal argument returns to seal it: \"As soon as his labour actually begins, it has already ceased to belong to him; it can therefore no longer be sold by him.\" Finally, the crispest possible summary: \"Labour is the substance, and the immanent measure of value, but it has no value itself.\" (Footnote 5 cites Hodgskin: \"Labour the exclusive standard of value ... the creator of all wealth, no commodity.\") The Inverted Category: \"Value of Labour\" as Imaginary Expression Marx now moves from logical impossibility to ideological inversion . \"In the expression 'value of labour', the concept of value is not only completely extinguished, but inverted, so that it becomes its contrary. It is an expression as imaginary as the value of the earth.\" The value of the earth is imaginary because land has no labour embodied in it but nonetheless commands a price — a parallel Marx treats as instructional: both are \"imaginary expressions\" that nonetheless have real social force. Crucially, he insists (against those who would dismiss him as a pedant) that these expressions \"arise, nevertheless, from the relations of production themselves. They are categories for the forms of appearance of essential relations.\" This is a philosophical point of great importance: the illusions are not arbitrary errors but necessary appearances . \"That in their appearance things are often presented in an inverted way is something fairly familiar in every science, apart from political economy.\" The analogy is to optics or astronomy — where the sun's apparent motion around the earth is not a mistake we can simply wish away but a phenomenal necessity that science must explain. This rebuts both the naïvely empiricist economist and the radical who thinks the problem is merely a \"grammatical ellipsis.\" In footnote 6 Marx attacks Proudhon precisely for this: Proudhon calls the \"value of labour\" a \"figurative expression,\" and Marx retorts that in \"labour as a commodity, which is a grim reality, he sees nothing but a grammatical ellipsis. Thus the whole of existing society, founded on labour as a commodity, is henceforth founded on a poetic licence.\" The sarcastic cure — \"change the language\" and apply to the Académie for \"a new edition of its dictionary\" — signals Marx's contempt for reforming the names rather than the relations . He adds a jab at J. B. Say's circular definitions (\"value is what a thing is worth\"), showing how a shallow nominalism collapses into vacuity. How Classical Political Economy Stumbled: From Market Price to Natural Price to Labour-Power Marx now narrates the history of the science as a detective story. Classical political economy \"borrowed the category 'price of labour' from everyday life without further criticism, and then simply asked the question, how is this price determined?\" It then correctly recognized that supply and demand explain \"nothing, with regard to the price of labour or any other commodity, except those changes themselves\" — the oscillations. Once demand and supply balance, \"the oscillation of prices ceases,\" and by the same token \"demand and supply also cease to explain anything.\" So a third thing — the natural price — must be posited independently of supply and demand. The classical economists then tried to find this natural price either as the equilibrium point or as the moving average over a period: \"a longer period of oscillation in the market price was taken, for example a year, and the oscillations were found to cancel each other out, leaving a mean average quantity, a constant magnitude.\" This is a genuine methodological achievement — recognizing that price must be determined \"otherwise than by its own mutually compensatory variations.\" The Physiocrats called it the \"necessary price\"; Adam Smith the \"natural price.\" But both could only conclude that it is \"its value expressed in money, as with all other commodities.\" The fatal step: \"this value was then further determined by the cost of production. But what is the cost of production ... of the worker, i.e. the cost of producing or reproducing the worker himself?\" Note Marx's precise wording: the political economists \"unconsciously substituted this question for the original one, for the search after the cost of production of labour as such turned in a circle, and did not allow them to get any further forward at all.\" This is the moment of unconscious discovery . To escape a circular definition (labour determined by the labour producing it), they slid into asking the cost of reproducing the worker — which is exactly the value of labour-power. \"Therefore what they called the 'value of labour' is in fact the value of labour-power, as it exists in the personality of the worker, and it is as different from its function, labour, as a machine is from the operations it performs.\" The machine simile is central: you hire the machine, not its operation, though the hire price appears to attach to the work done. Yet they never recognized what their analysis had accomplished. Because they focused on \"the difference between the market price of labour and its so-called value,\" and on the relation of that value to profit and to the values of other commodities, \"they never discovered that the course of the analysis had led not only from the market prices of labour to its presumed value, but also to the resolution of this value of labour itself into the value of labour-power.\" Their \"unconsciousness of this result of its own analysis\" and their \"uncritical acceptance of the categories 'value of labour', 'natural price of labour'\" led them into \"inextricable confusions and contradictions\" — and, critically, \"offered a secure base of operations to the vulgar economists who, in their shallowness, make it a principle to worship appearances only.\" The Conversion Mechanism: How Value of Labour-Power Becomes Wages Marx now performs the positive analysis, showing the transformation of the hidden essence into its surface form. He sets up the arithmetic: \"Assume that the usual working day is 12 hours and the daily value of labour-power 3 shillings, which is the expression in money of a value embodying 6 hours of labour.\" So 6 hours of the 12-hour day reproduce the value of the labour-power (3 shillings), and 6 hours create surplus value for the capitalist. Now the crucial sleight-of-hand that the form performs: \"If this value of a day's labour-power is now expressed as the value of a day's labour itself, we have the formula: 12 hours of labour has a value of 3 shillings.\" The value of labour-power has here been projected onto the whole day's labour . Hence: \"The value of labour-power thus determines the value of labour, or, expressed in money, its necessary price. If, on the other hand, the price of labour-power differs from its value, the price of labour will similarly differ from its so-called value.\" This is the mechanism of converted form : the wage is derived from the value of labour-power, but the derivation is transposed onto its phenomenal appearance as the value of the worker's whole day's toil. The Absurd Result and the Wage-Form's Concealment From this transformation there follows the chapter's most famous paradox. Because \"the value of labour is only an irrational expression for the value of labour-power,\" it follows \"that the value of labour must always be less than its value-product,\" since \"the capitalist always makes labour-power work longer than is necessary for the reproduction of its own value.\" In the running example: the value of the labour-power functioning through 12 hours is 3 shillings (6 hours to reproduce); yet the value it produces is 6 shillings, because it functions for 12 hours, and \"its value-product depends, not on its own value, but on the length of time it is in action.\" Hence: \"labour which creates a value of 6 shillings possesses a value of 3 shillings.\" Marx flags that this is \"at first sight absurd\" — but it is exactly the absurdity that would remain invisible under the naturalistic appearance. The masking is then spelled out with precision: \"the value of 3 shillings, which represents the paid portion of the working day, i.e. 6 hours of labour, appears as the value or price of the whole working day of 12 hours, which thus includes 6 hours which have not been paid for.\" The wage therefore pays, or appears to pay, for unpaid as well as paid labour. \"The wage-form thus extinguishes every trace of the division of the working day into necessary labour and surplus labour, into paid labour and unpaid labour. All labour appears as paid labour.\" Comparative Forms: Corvée, Slavery, Wage-Labour To make this concealment vivid, Marx triangulates across three modes of extracting surplus labour: Corvée (serfdom). \"There the labour of the serf for himself, and his compulsory labour for the lord of the land, are demarcated very clearly both in space and time.\" The unpaid component is visible as such. Slavery. \"Even the part of the working day in which the slave is only replacing the value of his own means of subsistence, in which he therefore actually works for himself alone, appears as labour for his master. All his labour appears as unpaid labour.\" Here the opposite distortion occurs: the paid (self-sustaining) portion is hidden inside the general appearance of total servitude. Wage-labour. \"Even surplus labour, or unpaid labour, appears as paid.\" The structural contrast is drawn with algebraic neatness: \"In the one case, the property-relation conceals the slave's labour for himself; in the other case the money-relation conceals the uncompensated labour of the wage-labourer.\" This symmetry is theoretically important: it shows that both precapitalist and capitalist forms distort the paid/unpaid division, but in opposite directions and by different mechanisms — property versus money. The wage-relation does not merely permit exploitation; it transforms exploitation into its own appearance of freedom and full payment . Footnote 8 gives a polemical illustration: The Morning Star , a \"London free-trade organ which is so naive as to be positively foolish,\" indignantly claimed during the American Civil War that the Negroes in the \"Confederate States\" worked \"absolutely for nothing.\" Marx's sardonic rebuttal — the paper \"should have compared the daily cost of a Negro in the southern states with that of a free worker in the East End of London\" — punctures the moral complacency by suggesting that the \"free\" East End worker's compensation may be equally illusory once the actual cost of his reproduction is examined. The Decisive Importance of the Transformation Marx now states the widest significance of the entire analysis: \"All the notions of justice held by both the worker and the capitalist, all the mystifications of the capitalist mode of production, all capitalism's illusions about freedom, all the apologetic tricks of vulgar economics, have as their basis the form of appearance discussed above, which makes the actual relation invisible, and indeed presents to the eye the precise opposite of that relation.\" This is a strong claim. The chapter's central object is thus not a semantic quibble; it is the ideological cornerstone of capitalism. The wage-form is what makes exploitation appear as exchange, dependence as freedom, and unpaid labour as paid. Why the Illusion Persists: The Naturalness of the Appearance Marx — remarkably, given his critical stance — then says \"nothing is easier to understand than the necessity, the raison d'être, of this form of appearance.\" He enumerates four reasons the illusion feels entirely natural: The mirror of commodity exchange. \"The exchange between capital and labour at first presents itself to our perceptions in exactly the same way as the sale and purchase of all other commodities. The buyer gives a certain sum of money, the seller an article which is something other than money.\" The legal mind sees only \"a material difference,\" captured in the hollow reciprocity of the Roman formulas: Do ut des, do ut facias, facio ut des, facio ut facias (\"I give that you may give; I give that you may do; I do that you may give; I do that you may do\"). Incommensurability of value and use-value. \"Since exchange-value and use-value are in themselves incommensurable magnitudes, the expressions 'value of labour', 'price of labour', do not seem more irrational than the expressions 'value of cotton', 'price of cotton'.\" The absurdity hides behind the innocuousness of ordinary commodity language. Money as deferred payment. \"The worker is paid after he has given his labour\" — so money functions as a means of payment that realizes retroactively the value of the article \"supplied.\" This temporal sequence (work first, pay later) reinforces the belief that the money pays for the work performed. The use-value delivered is congealed into a specific craft. What the worker actually hands over to the capitalist is, in everyday perception, \"a specific form of useful labour, such as tailoring, cobbling, spinning\" — not the abstract capacity that is the \"universal value-creating element\" with properties unlike all other commodities. \"That this same labour is ... the universal value-creating element ... is something which falls outside the frame of reference of the everyday consciousness.\" The Worker's Perspective and the Capitalist's Perspective Marx then adopts a dual standpoint, in what is a masterful exercise in critical phenomenology. From the worker's standpoint: he receives for 12 hours of labour the value-product of 6 hours (Marx here gives 2 shillings, a numerical slip or variant relative to the earlier 3-shilling example, but the logic is identical). His 12 hours are for him the means of buying the 3 shillings he needs. His labour-power's value may fluctuate (3–4 or 3–2 shillings), or its price may rise or fall with demand and supply; but \"he always gives 12 hours of labour. Every change in the amount of the equivalent that he receives therefore necessarily appears to him as a change in the value or price of his 12 hours of labour.\" This is the worker's phenomenological trap. Marx notes explicitly: \"This circumstance misled Adam Smith, who treated the working day as a constant quantity, into the opposite assertion that the value of labour is constant\" — i.e., Smith held that the value of labour stays fixed even as the value of subsistence goods varies (footnote 9 notes Smith only incidentally alludes to variation of the working day, when discussing piece-wages). From the capitalist's standpoint: he \"wishes to receive as much labour as possible for as little money as possible,\" and so \"the only thing that interests him is the difference between the price of labour-power and the value which its function creates.\" But he \"tries to buy all commodities as cheaply as possible,\" and thus \"his own invariable explanation of his profit is that it is a result of mere sharp practice, of buying under the value and selling over it.\" Here is the capitalist's counterpart illusion: he doesn't recognize that the very wage-form he operates within is the condition of his profit. \"Hence he never comes to see that if such a thing as the value of labour really existed, and he really paid this value, no capital would exist, and his money would never be transformed into capital.\" The worker and the capitalist are thus locked in asymmetric but mutually reinforcing misrecognitions. The Two Classes of Phenomena; Appearance vs. Essence; the Limits of Classical Political Economy Marx closes with an empirical-observational challenge: \"the actual movement of wages presents phenomena which seem to prove that it is not the value of labour-power which is paid, but the value of its function, of labour itself.\" Two classes of these phenomena: Changes in wages owing to changes in the length of the working day. Longer days bring higher pay, which superficially looks like payment for the quantity of labour performed. Marx replies with the machine analogy: \"One might as well conclude that it is not the value of a machine which is paid, but that of its operation, because it costs more to hire a machine for a week than for a day.\" The higher weekly hire-charge on the machine is entirely compatible with paying for the machine's value rather than its \"operation.\" Individual wage differences between workers performing the same function. These also occur under slavery, but \"there they do not give rise to any illusions, for labour-power is in that case itself sold frankly and openly, without any embellishment.\" The decisive contrast: \"in the slave system, the advantage of a labour-power above the average, and the disadvantage of a labour-power below the average, affects the slave-owner; whereas in the system of wage-labour it affects the worker himself, because his labour-power is, in the one case, sold by himself, in the other, by a third person.\" The same differential is interpreted as a property of the owner under slavery and of the worker under wage-labour — a perfect illustration of how forms of appearance invert essential relations. The chapter's final paragraph generalizes into epistemology: what holds for all forms of appearance holds for \"'value and price of labour', or 'wages', as contrasted with the essential relation manifested in it, namely the value and price of labour-power. The forms of appearance are reproduced directly and spontaneously, as current and usual modes of thought; the essential relation must first be discovered by science.\" This is a conciliatory but severe verdict on classical political economy: it \"stumbles approximately onto the true state of affairs, but without consciously formulating it.\" Its limitation is not intellectual capacity but class determination: \"It is unable to do this as long as it stays within its bourgeois skin.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Chapter 19 establishes a fundamental principle with implications far beyond political economy: the most obvious surface facts of a social system can be systematically inverted representations of the mechanisms that sustain it. The wage appears to be payment for labour; it is in reality payment for the capacity to labour , and it simultaneously conceals the unpaid labour that generates surplus-value. This is not a minor terminological correction; it is the epistemological key to the whole of capitalism. To mistake appearance for essence — to speak of the \"value of labour\" — is not just an economic error but the operating ideology of bourgeois society, shared by worker, capitalist, and economist alike. The chapter reveals several systemic laws . First, the law of equivalent exchange is preserved at the surface of capitalism precisely by displacing the extraction of surplus-value to the hidden dimension of labour-power's use-value — the market remains a realm of formal equality, while the labor process is a realm of unequal appropriation. Second, the convertibility of essence into appearance is a structural requirement , not an accidental error: because the wage-form must be applied to the entire working day, its very definition extinguishes the trace of the paid/unpaid division. Third, the asymmetry of the worker's and capitalist's illusions — the worker thinks he is paid for his labour, the capitalist thinks his profit comes from buying cheap and selling dear — shows that the ideology of capital is multiply distributed and self-reinforcing across class positions. Theoretically, the chapter sews up the argument of Volume 1's Part Three by answering the question Marx had posed in A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy (footnote 7): how can a regime of exchange-values determined by labour-time produce the result that \"the exchange-value of labour is less than the exchange-value of its product\"? Answer: because labour is not what is exchanged; labour-power is. Once the category is corrected, the apparent contradiction disappears and the law of value stands intact. The critique of classical political economy is also carefully calibrated. Marx does not say Ricardo and Smith were stupid; he shows they almost got there — discovered the value of labour-power through the cost-of-reproduction question — but could not name their own discovery because doing so would transcend \"bourgeois skin.\" This produces a striking verdict on the limits of scientific thought under class conditions: even genuine analytic progress can be arrested by the categories one refuses to question. And it explains the rise of \"vulgar economics,\" which does not accidentally worship appearances but makes a principle of it, precisely because classical political economy left the appearance-categories intact. Philosophically, the chapter's method prefigures the \"forms of appearance\" logic later elaborated in Volume 3: the task of science is not to abolish appearances but to explain what they conceal. The wage-form is the paradigm case — spontaneous, necessary, socially reproduced, and therefore not refutable by mere linguistic reform. Its practical implication is that any politics built on the wage-form's promises (fair wages, just exchange, freedom of contract) operates within the very terms that hide exploitation — a lesson aimed equally at reformist economists, juridical socialism, and Proudhonian nominalism. The task, for Marx, is not to correct the language of wages but to grasp the relation that the language conceals. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 18 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 20"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 20", "title": "Chapter 20", "subtitle": "Time-Wages", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 20/Chapter 20  Time-Wages_explanation.html", "word_count": 5210, "read_time": "26 min read", "tags": ["Wages & Value of Labour-Power", "The Working Day & Factory Acts"], "keywords": ["Time-Wages", "Nominal vs Real Wages", "Overtime Work and Day-Wages", "Price of the Working Hour", "Deterioration of Worker Health from Overwork"], "text": "Chapter 20 Time-Wages - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,176 words ⏱️ ~26 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 20, \"Time-Wages,\" is the opening chapter of Part VI of Capital Volume One and the first of two chapters (the second being \"Piece-Wages\") devoted to the forms of appearance of the wage. Its core problem is a problem of form . Marx begins by asserting that \"wages themselves again take many forms,\" and that this plurality is invisible to \"the ordinary economic treatises, which, in their crude obsession with the material side [ Stoff ], ignore all differences of form.\" The neglect of form is not a minor taxonomic failing; it is precisely through form that the essential relation — the extraction of unpaid labour — is concealed, and, more importantly for this chapter, it is through form that new mechanisms of exploitation become possible. The chapter's central thesis is that in time-wages, the price of labour and the amount of the wage are two distinct quantities that can move independently, and even in opposite directions . Because the sale of labour-power \"always takes place for definite periods of time,\" its value is necessarily presented as day-wages, week-wages — \"time-wages.\" From this it follows that the same daily or weekly sum can represent radically different prices per hour of labour, and that wages may stay constant while the price of labour falls, or rise while the price of labour stays constant or falls. Marx's intellectual objective is to demonstrate that this formal slippage is not an academic curiosity but the heartbeat of a cumulative spiral: a low price of labour compels longer hours; longer hours lower the price of labour further; competition among workers and among capitalists transmits and normalizes the result, until \"an abnormally low selling price of the commodity ... becomes the constant basis of a miserable wage for excessive hours of work.\" The chapter's placement is deliberate. It functions as the hinge between the abstract laws of Chapter 17 (on changes in the relative magnitudes of the price of labour-power and surplus-value) and the historical material of the working-day analysis and the 1860s factory reports. Marx states explicitly that Chapter 17's laws \"can be transformed, by a simple alteration in their form, into laws of wages,\" and that the difference between the exchange-value of labour-power and the sum of means of subsistence into which it converts now \"appears as the distinction between nominal and real wages.\" He refuses to repeat the essential analysis at the level of appearance — a methodological signature of the whole work — and restricts himself to \"a few points which characterize time-wages.\" What those few points reveal is a form of payment that, left unregulated, converts the wage contract into an instrument of unlimited and self-reinforcing intensification of labour. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Formal Problem: Why Wages Require a Theory of Form Marx opens by conceding a limitation of scope: an exposition of all wage forms belongs to \"the special study of wage-labour, and not, therefore, to this work.\" He will nonetheless treat \"the two fundamental forms\" — time-wages and, in the following chapter, piece-wages. The methodological target is announced immediately: the \"ordinary economic treatises,\" in their \"crude obsession with the material side [ Stoff ],\" ignore \"all differences of form.\" The bracketed German term Stoff signals the Stoff/Form distinction that organizes Marx's critical method: the classical economists treat wages as a quantity of stuff (a sum of money, a basket of goods) and therefore miss that the form in which that stuff is paid — by the hour, by the day, by the piece — has its own causal powers. Form is not a wrapper around an invariant content; it is a determinant of the content. This is the reason the chapter cannot be read as mere description. If the form of payment is causally efficacious, then the same \"value of labour-power\" can support wildly different lived outcomes and wildly different rates of exploitation depending on the temporal unit in which it is contracted. From Laws of Value to Laws of Wages: Nominal and Real Wages The transition into the substantive argument is compressed into a few sentences. Because \"the sale of labour-power ... always takes place for definite periods of time,\" the converted form in which the daily or weekly value of labour-power is \"directly presented\" is time-wages — \"therefore day-wages, etc.\" Two transformations follow. First, the laws of Chapter 17, governing changes in the relative magnitudes of the price of labour-power and surplus-value, can be re-expressed \"by a simple alteration in their form\" as laws of wages . Second, the distinction worked out at the level of essence — between the exchange-value of labour-power and the sum of means of subsistence into which that value is converted — now reappears at the level of appearance as the distinction between nominal wages and real wages . Here a definition is embedded rather than announced: nominal wages are \"the sum of money which the worker receives for his daily or weekly labour,\" i.e., \"his wages estimated in value\"; real wages are the sum of means of subsistence into which that money converts. Marx adds a founding assumption in footnote 1: \"Here we always assume that the value of money itself remains constant.\" Without that assumption, every comparison below would be contaminated by monetary variation. Crucially, Marx explicitly declines to repeat the essential analysis: \"It would be useless to repeat here, when dealing with the form of appearance, what we have already worked out in relation to the essential form.\" This is a discipline of exposition, not an evasion. The chapter's whole force depends on the reader already knowing that wages are the converted form of the value of labour-power, and that the \"price of labour\" is an irrational expression concealing unpaid labour. The Unit of Measurement: Price of Labour as Daily Value Divided by Hours Because the same daily wage can represent very different prices of labour, Marx must distinguish \"between the sum total of the daily or weekly wages ... and the price of labour.\" He defines the latter with the help of Sir Edward West (footnote 2): \"The price of labour is the sum paid for a given quantity of labour\" ( Price of Corn and Wages of Labour , London, 1826, p. 67). Marx names West's earlier anonymous 1815 Essay on the Application of Capital to Land an \"epoch-making work in the history of political economy,\" yet in a later footnote accuses him of dismissing the central question — \"How is the price of labour determined?\" — \"with mere banalities.\" Marx supplies the answer West failed to give. The average price of labour is the average daily value of labour-power divided by the average number of hours in the working day. His worked example: if the daily value of labour-power is 3 shillings, which is \"the value-product of 6 working hours,\" and the working day is 12 hours, then the price of one working hour is 3/12 of a shilling = 3d. This hourly rate \"serves as the unit measure for the price of labour.\" The formula is thus: price of labour (per hour) = daily value of labour-power ÷ hours in the working day. Two structural features are already latent here. The numerator is a value (the reproduction cost of a day's labour-power); the denominator is a duration . The ratio therefore behaves strangely under change: it is not a market price equilibrating supply and demand, but an arithmetic quotient whose components can be manipulated by the capitalist's control over hours. Two-Way Divergence: Constant Wages, Falling Price of Labour — and the Reverse Marx now works both directions of the divergence. (a) Wages constant, price of labour falling. Suppose the usual working day is 10 hours and the daily value of labour-power is 3 shillings (= 36d). Then the hourly price is 3.6d (3 3/5 d). If the working day rises to 12 hours, the hourly price falls to 3d; at 15 hours it falls to 2.4d (2 2/5 d). \"Despite all this, daily or weekly wages remain unchanged.\" The worker receives the same 3 shillings for a longer working day. (b) Wages rising, price of labour constant or falling. With a 10-hour day and 3 shillings daily value, the hourly price is 3 3/5 d. If the worker, \"owing to an increase in the number of orders,\" works 12 hours and the price of labour remains the same, his daily wage rises to 3s 7 1/5 d (12 × 3.6d = 43.2d) \"without any variation having taken place in the price of labour.\" The same result follows if \"instead of the extensive magnitude of labour, its intensive magnitude increased\" — that is, if the worker works harder rather than longer. Marx draws out two consequences. First, \"the rise of nominal daily or weekly wages may therefore be unaccompanied by any change in the price of labour, or may even be accompanied by a fall in the latter.\" Second, the same logic applies to family income: when \"the quantity of labour provided by the head of the family is augmented by the labour of the members of his family,\" the household's total money income can rise while the price per hour of labour falls. The conclusion: \"There are therefore methods of lowering the price of labour which are independent of any reduction in the nominal daily or weekly wage.\" This is where the nuance of rival positions enters. Footnote 3 quotes West himself recognizing the phenomenon — \"An increase in the wages of labour does not necessarily imply an enhancement of the price of labour. From fuller employment, and greater exertions, the wages of labour may be considerably increased, while the price of labour may continue the same\" — yet Marx immediately undercuts him: West \"dismisses the main question, 'How is the price of labour determined?', with mere banalities.\" Footnote 4 adds two more witnesses. The anonymous author of the Essay on Trade and Commerce — \"the most fanatical representative of the eighteenth-century industrial bourgeoisie\" — saw it, confusedly: \"It is the quantity of labour and not the price of it ... that is determined by the price of provisions ... Master-manufacturers know that there are various ways of raising and falling the price of labour, besides that of altering its nominal amount.\" And N. W. Senior, in Three Lectures on the Rate of Wages (1830), which \"uses West's work without mentioning it,\" writes: \"The labourer is principally interested in the amount of wages.\" Marx's rejoinder is acid: Senior means \"the worker is principally interested in what he receives, the nominal sum of his wages, not in what he gives, the quantity of labour!\" The General Law of Time-Wages, and Under-Employment Marx then states the general law in two halves: Given the amount of daily/weekly labour , the daily/weekly wage depends on the price of labour , which varies either with the value of labour-power or with the divergences between its price and its value. Given the price of labour , the daily/weekly wage depends on the quantity of labour expended daily or weekly. From here Marx derives a phenomenon distinct from overwork: under-employment . Repeating the unit of measurement (12-hour day, 3 shillings daily value, 3d per hour, 6d of value produced per hour), he notes that if the worker is employed for less than 12 hours — \"for instance only for 6 or 8 hours\" — he receives, at the same 3d rate, only 1s 6d or 2s a day. The arithmetic is decisive: to produce a day's wage equal to the value of his labour-power, he must work on average 6 hours; and since he works \"only half of every hour for himself, and half for the capitalist,\" he cannot obtain the value-product of 6 hours unless he is employed for the full 12. \"In previous chapters we saw the destructive consequences of over-work; but here we come upon the origin of the sufferings which arise for the worker out of his being insufficiently employed.\" Footnote 5 sharpens the nuance by contrasting abnormal under-employment with a legally reduced working day. The former \"has nothing to do with the absolute length of the working day, and may just as well occur in a working day of 15 hours as in one of 6.\" The normal hourly price is calculated on a 15-hour basis in one case and a 6-hour basis in the other, and \"the result is therefore the same if the worker is employed in the one case for only 7 1/2 hours, and in the other case for only 3 hours\" — in both instances he receives only 1s 6d, half the daily value. The absolute length of the normal day is irrelevant to the proportional shortfall. Hour-Wages at the Capitalist's Will: Destruction of the Paid/Unpaid Nexus The chapter now reaches its theoretical pivot. If the hour's wage is fixed so that \"the capitalist does not bind himself to pay a day's or a week's wage, but only to pay wages for the hours during which he chooses to employ the worker,\" then the capitalist can employ the worker for less time than the average that served as the basis for computing the hourly rate. Since the unit of measurement is \"determined by the ratio of the daily value of labour-power to the working day of a given number of hours,\" it \"naturally loses all meaning as soon as the working day ceases to contain a definite number of hours.\" The consequence is stated in a phrase of great formal precision: \"The connection between the paid and the unpaid labour is destroyed.\" What this means is that the legal form of the wage contract no longer guarantees the worker even the equivalent of his subsistence, let alone a determinate proportion of paid to unpaid time. The capitalist can \"wring from the worker a certain quantity of surplus labour without allowing him the labour-time necessary for his own subsistence.\" He can \"annihilate all regularity of employment\" and, \"according to his own convenience, caprice, and the interest of the moment, make the most frightful over-work alternate with relative or absolute cessation of work.\" He can abnormally lengthen the day \"without giving the worker any corresponding compensation, under the pretence of paying 'the normal price of labour.'\" This is why Marx calls the London building workers' resistance \"the perfectly rational revolt\" — the strike of 1859–60 (the footnote corrects the text: it began in July 1859 and ended in February 1860) was a revolt against the hourly-wage form as such. Marx also marks the limits of reform: \"The legal limitation of the working day puts an end to mischievous acts of this kind, though it does not of course end the diminution of employment caused by the competition of machinery, by changes in the quality of the workers employed, and partial or general crises.\" Prolongation Without Compensation: The Rising Numerator and the Fractional \"Normal Day\" A second, subtler mechanism follows. Even when the daily or weekly wage increases , the price of labour may remain nominally constant \"and yet fall below its normal level\" — this happens \"every time the working day is prolonged beyond its customary length, while the price of labour (reckoned per working hour) remains constant.\" Marx's reasoning is expressed in the terms of the ratio: daily value of labour-power ÷ working day If the denominator increases, \"the numerator increases still more rapidly. The amount of deterioration in labour-power, and therefore its value, increases with the duration of its functioning, and to a more rapid degree than the increase of that duration.\" In other words, the longer the day, the greater the wear and tear that must be repaired for labour-power to reproduce itself; the reproduction cost rises faster than the hours. A constant hourly rate therefore understates the true value of the labour actually expended. This is the theoretical justification for overtime premia — and simultaneously the demonstration that customary premia are systematically inadequate. Marx documents the spontaneous emergence of a normative division of the day in industries without legal limits. A habit grows up \"of regarding the working day as normal only up to a point in time, for instance up to the expiration of the tenth hour ('normal working day', 'the day's work', 'the regular hours of work').\" Beyond that point, time becomes \"overtime,\" paid at a \"better hourly rate ('extra pay'), although often in a proportion which is ridiculously small.\" Footnote 6 records that in lace-making, overtime paid from 1/2 d. to 2d. per hour stands \"in painful contrast to the amount of injury produced to the health and stamina of the workpeople,\" and that the small sum earned \"is also often obliged to be spent in extra nourishment.\" The sting is structural: \"The normal working day exists here as a fraction of the actual working day, and over the year as a whole the latter is often more common than the former.\" Footnote 7 gives the paper-staining case: \"the day's work of 10 hours is finished by 4.30 p.m., and all after that is overtime, and we seldom leave off working before 6 p.m., so that we are really working overtime the whole year round.\" The mechanism is perverse: the low price of labour during the so-called normal time \"compels the worker to work during the better paid overtime, if he wishes to obtain a sufficient wage at all.\" Footnote 8's Scottish bleaching-works illustrate: ten hours a day at a nominal 1s 2d, plus 3 or 4 hours of daily overtime at 3d per hour — roughly double the regular hourly rate. The effect, in the inspector's words, was that \"a man could not earn more than 8s. per week when working the ordinary hours ... without overtime they could not earn a fair day's wages.\" The same footnote records that \"The higher wages, for getting adult males to work longer hours, are a temptation too strong to be resisted,\" and describes London book-binders' indentured girls of 14–15 working until 10, 11, 12 or 1 o'clock in the last week of each month, \"tempted\" by extra pay and supper in neighbouring public houses — a \"great debauchery among these 'young immortals' \" whose compensation Marx dryly notes lies partly in binding \"many Bibles and other edifying books.\" Again: legal limitation \"puts an end to this pastime.\" The Empirical Regularity: Longer Hours, Lower Wages Having built the mechanism, Marx now appeals to observation: \"It is a generally known fact that the longer the working day in a branch of industry, the lower the wages are.\" Footnote 10 supplies the inspectors' generalization — \"where long hours are the rule, small wages are also so\" (31 October 1863, p. 9), and \"The work which obtains the scanty pittance of food is, for the most part, excessively prolonged\" ( Public Health, Sixth Report , 1864, p. 15). The decisive evidence is comparative and quantitative: the factory inspector Alexander Redgrave , in a comparative review of the twenty years from 1839 to 1859, showed that \"wages rose in the factories under the Ten Hours' Act, while they fell in the factories where the work went on for 14 and 15 hours every day\" (footnote 11). This is a natural-experiment style comparison: same country, same period, same industry, differing legal regimes — and the shorter day coincides with the higher wage. The Cumulative Spiral of Low Wages and Overwork From the second half of the general law — price of labour given, the wage depends on the quantity of labour — Marx deduces a feedback loop in two steps. Step one: \"the lower the price of labour, the greater must be the quantity of labour, or the longer must be the working day, for the worker to secure even a miserable average wage. The low level of the price of labour acts here as a stimulus to the extension of the labour-time.\" Footnote 12 documents the English hand nail-makers, forced to work 15 hours a day (6 a.m. to 8 p.m.) at low prices in order to hammer out a wretched weekly wage: to earn 11d. or 1s. a day, the worker must work hard all the time, and from that must be deducted \"the wear of the tools, the cost of firing, and something for waste iron ... which takes off altogether 2 1/2 d. or 3d.\" The women, working the same length of time, earn a weekly wage of only 5 shillings. Step two: \"the extension of the period of labour produces in its turn a fall in the price of labour, and with this a fall in the daily or the weekly wage.\" The circle closes: low price → long hours → lower price. And note the asymmetry Marx identifies in the ratio: \"the determination of the price of labour by use of the ratio ... shows that a mere prolongation of the working day lowers the price of labour, if no compensatory factor enters. But the same circumstances which allow the capitalist in the long run to prolong the working day also allow him at first, and compel him finally, to reduce the price of labour nominally as well until the total price of the increased number of hours goes down, and therefore the daily or weekly wage falls.\" The Two Competitive Mechanisms and the Underselling of Commodities Marx then specifies \"two kinds of circumstance\" through which the nominal price is driven down. First, competition among workers. \"If one man does the work of 1 1/2 or 2 men, the supply of labour increases, although the supply of labour-power on the market remains constant.\" Here is a delicate distinction: the headcount of workers is unchanged, but the quantity of labour supplied rises with the intensity and duration of each worker's effort — so the effective supply of labour services increases. \"The competition thus created between the workers allows the capitalist to force down the price of labour, while the fall in the price of labour allows him, on the other hand, to force up the hours of work still further.\" Footnote 13 quotes the inspectors: a factory worker who refused the customary long hours \"would very shortly be replaced by somebody who would work any length of time, and thus be thrown out of employment.\" Senior, despite himself, concurs: \"If one man performs the work of two ... the rate of profits will generally be raised ... in consequence of the additional supply of labour having diminished its price.\" Second, competition among capitalists. Command over abnormal quantities of unpaid labour — \"quantities in excess of the average social amount\" — becomes a weapon. Since \"a part of the price of the commodity consists of the price of labour,\" and since \"the unpaid part of the price of labour does not need to be reckoned as part of the price of the commodity,\" that unpaid portion \"may be given to the buyer as a present.\" The first step under the impulse of competition is to donate the unpaid surplus to the purchaser. The second step, \"also compelled by competition, is the exclusion from the selling price of the commodity of at least a part of the abnormal surplus-value created by the extension of the working day.\" Thus \"an abnormally low selling price of the commodity arises, at first sporadically, and becomes fixed by degrees; this lower selling price henceforward becomes the constant basis of a miserable wage for excessive hours of work, just as originally it was the product of those very circumstances.\" The exception becomes the norm. Marx flags the methodological boundary: \"This movement is simply indicated here, as the analysis of competition does not belong to this part of the investigation.\" He then \"lets the capitalist speak.\" The Birmingham manufacturer complains: \"there is so much competition of masters one against another, that many are obliged to do things as employers that they would otherwise be ashamed of; and yet no more money is made, but only the public gets the benefit.\" (Footnote 14.) The Bakers' Jeremiad: The Capitalist's Semblance-Consciousness The London bakers supply the chapter's richest case. Two sorts of bakers existed — the \"full-priced,\" selling bread at its full price, and the \"undersellers,\" selling below its normal price (cross-referenced to the earlier discussion of the bakers' night-work). The full-priced denounced their rivals before the Parliamentary Committee of Inquiry: the undersellers \"only exist now by first defrauding the public, and next getting 18 hours' work out of their men for 12 hours' wages.\" The unpaid labour of the men \"was made ... the source whereby the competition was carried on, and continues so to this day.\" Competition among the master-bakers \"is the cause of the difficulty in getting rid of night-work,\" since an underseller \"must make it up by getting more out of the labour of the men\": \"If I got only 12 hours' work out of my men, and my neighbour got 18 or 20, he must beat me in the selling price. If the men could insist on payment for overwork, this would be set right.\" A large number of the undersellers' employees \"are foreigners and youths, who are obliged to accept almost any wages they can obtain.\" (Footnote 15.) Marx adds, from the same testimony, that the full-priced bakers themselves — as their spokesman Bennett admits — start their men at 11 p.m. and run them through the following day until as late as 7 in the evening. Marx's interpretation is the theoretical heart of the passage. This \"jeremiad\" is interesting because \"it shows how it is only the semblance of the relations of production which is reflected by the brain of the capitalist.\" The capitalist \"does not know that the normal price of labour also includes a definite quantity of unpaid labour, and that this very unpaid labour is the normal source of his profits. The category of surplus labour-time does not exist at all for him, since it is included in the normal working day, which he thinks he has paid for in the day's wages.\" But overtime — prolongation beyond the limits corresponding to the usual price of labour — \"certainly does exist for him,\" and against an underselling competitor he even insists on extra pay for it. Yet \"he does not know that this extra pay also includes unpaid labour, just as much as the price of the customary hour of labour does.\" This is not hypocrisy but a structural form of consciousness: the wage form itself, by presenting the value of labour-power as the price of a full day's labour, makes the unpaid portion invisible. The capitalist is not lying when he believes he has paid for the whole day; he is describing the semblance faithfully. The Final Arithmetic: One-Half versus One-Third The chapter closes with a numerical demonstration of the point that even \"extra pay\" is a wage for unpaid labour. Take a 12-hour day whose hourly price is 3d. Since the daily value of labour-power is 3 shillings and it is the value-product of 6 hours, one working hour produces 6d of new value. The paid 3d is therefore \"the value-product of half a working hour,\" and \"the capitalist appropriates one-half of the working hour\" without payment. Now suppose overtime is paid at 4d per hour — \"the value-product of 2/3 of a working hour.\" Here the capitalist \"appropriates one-third\" of the hour, again \"without making any payment in return.\" The structure of the argument is exact. Under the normal hour, the rate of exploitation is 100% (3d unpaid against 3d paid). Under the overtime hour, the rate of exploitation falls to 50% (2d unpaid against 4d paid) — yet the absolute mass of unpaid labour still rises, because an additional hour is worked and because the total day is extended. The premium is real but insufficient, and it never reaches the point of compensating the increased depreciation of labour-power that Marx identified earlier in the rising numerator. The worker trades a longer life for a slightly better-paid hour; the capitalist pockets the difference in both directions. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental lesson of Chapter 20 is that the wage is not a thing but a relation expressed in a temporal form , and that this form has autonomous causal force. Three systemic principles emerge. First, the two-quantity principle : the price of labour (money per unit of time) and the amount of the wage (money per day or week) are distinct variables capable of opposite movement. This dissolves the ordinary intuition that higher wages mean better-paid labour, and it dismantles the standard of \"a fair day's wage\" as anything self-evident — as Senior's complacent formula (\"the labourer is principally interested in the amount of wages\") shows, the ideology of the wage form is precisely to fix attention on the amount while the price is quietly eroded. Second, the reflexivity principle : the determinants of wages are mutually reinforcing, not equilibrating. Low hourly price drives longer hours; longer hours drive the hourly price lower, and increase labour-power's own reproduction cost faster than the hours increase; competition among workers and competition among capitalists then convert a sporadic advantage into a settled norm — the abnormally low selling price becomes \"the constant basis of a miserable wage for excessive hours of work.\" There is no tendency to a natural wage floor within this form; the spiral runs downward until an external limit is imposed. Third, the juridical-indeterminacy principle : when the contract fixes an hourly rate without fixing the number of hours, \"the connection between the paid and the unpaid labour is destroyed.\" The worker loses the guarantee of even subsistence, and the capitalist acquires the power to alternate \"the most frightful over-work\" with \"relative or absolute cessation of work.\" This is why the London building workers of 1859–60 demanded, as conditions for accepting hourly wages, (1) a legally defined normal day of 9 or 10 hours, with the hourly price for the 10-hour day set higher than for the 9-hour day, and (2) proportional extra pay for every hour beyond the normal day. Their two demands are the exact political translation of the chapter's two theoretical findings. The broader implications are epistemological and political. Epistemologically, Marx shows that the capitalist's consciousness is a faithful mirror of \"the semblance of the relations of production\" — the employer genuinely does not perceive the unpaid labour embedded in the normal wage, because the wage form itself performs that concealment. Ideology here is a structural effect of the form of appearance, not a delusion to be corrected by argument alone. Politically, the chapter establishes the necessity and the limits of the legal limitation of the working day: it ends the specific mischiefs of hourly wage-by-caprice but cannot end unemployment generated by machinery, changes in the quality of workers employed, or crises. Above all, it establishes that wage struggles are never merely about the amount of money; they are struggles over the form of payment, the length of the day, and the rate at which the worker's life is exchanged for a price. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 19 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 21"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 21", "title": "Chapter 21", "subtitle": "Piece-Wages", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 21/Chapter 21  Piece-Wages_explanation.html", "word_count": 6902, "read_time": "35 min read", "tags": ["Wages & Value of Labour-Power"], "keywords": ["Piece-Wages", "Measure of Labour Intensity by Output", "Supervision and Parasitic Sub-Contracting", "Competition among Workers under Piece-Rates", "Piece-Wages as Fertile Ground for Wage Cuts"], "text": "Chapter 21 Piece-Wages - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,858 words ⏱️ ~34 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 21 — Piece-Wages: An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 21 is the third and shortest of the three chapters that Marx devotes to the form of wages in Volume I of Capital , and it performs what is, in the architecture of the book, an exercise in systematic demystification. Its opening sentence states the thesis with lapidary economy: \"The piece-wage is nothing but a converted form of the time-wage, just as the time-wage is a converted form of the value or price of labour-power.\" That is, the chapter's intellectual objective is to strip away a specific, historically durable illusion — the illusion, endemic to piece-work, that the capitalist buys and pays for labour embodied in a product rather than labour-power as a capacity . Marx's central problem is not to add a new category to political economy but to demonstrate that an apparently distinct and even \"more rational\" payment system is at bottom the same relation of exploitation, only transposed into a different phenomenal guise. The stakes are exactly the stakes of Part VI as a whole: to show that wage-forms, whatever their variety, systematically conceal the division of the working day into paid and unpaid labour, and thereby conceal the extraction of surplus-value itself. The chapter therefore has two halves, a conceptual and an operational one. The conceptual half (roughly pp. 692–694) dissolves the piece-wage into the time-wage by showing that the two are mutually convertible via a fixed equation — the value of a day's labour equals the daily value of labour-power — and by demonstrating, through a worked numerical example, that a per-piece price is simply a translated hourly price. The operational half (pp. 694–700) examines the characteristic peculiarities that this form of wage produces when it becomes general: it polices the quality of labour through the product itself; it supplies an exact measure of labour-intensity; it renders direct superintendence largely superfluous; it thereby provides the material basis for \"domestic labour,\" for subletting (the \"sweating system\"), and for a stratified system in which one worker supervises and exploits another; it aligns the worker's immediate private interest with over-exertion and over-long hours; and it individualises wages in a way that simultaneously rewards the strong and depresses the average. It concludes with a historical claim of considerable weight: that piece-wages are \"the form of wage most appropriate to the capitalist mode of production,\" whose great proving-ground was the period of manufacture and whose most devastating deployment came in the \"stormy youth of large-scale industry,\" 1797–1815. In relation to the broader theoretical framework, this chapter sits between the analysis of time-wages, whose \"hour-system\" piece-wages are said to support, and the analysis of national differences in wages, which presupposes precisely the concept here analysed — the wage as the converted price of labour-power. It closes a circuit of appearance versus essence : it takes the most \"self-evidently\" product-based form of payment, one that even sympathetic observers mistook for a form of artisan independence, and shows it to be an intensification, not a mitigation, of the wage-relation. It is also, as the chapter's closing pages make plain, a political chapter: its object is not merely a category but a site of struggle, \"constant struggles between the capitalist and the worker\" over the meaning of the piece-rate. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Illusion at First Sight Marx begins by specifying the false appearance that piece-wages generate. In time-wages, however mystified, the purchased object at least appears to be a duration of living labour. In piece-wages, the appearance shifts: it seems \"at first sight as if the use-value bought from the worker is not his labour-power as it actually functions, living labour, but labour already objectified in the product.\" The distinction is precisely the distinction between labour-power (the capacity to labour, sold for a period) and labour objectified (labour congealed in a commodity, already realised as value). The second half of the illusion follows from the first: it also seems \"as if the price of this labour is determined not … by the daily value of labour-power / working day of a given number of hours, but by the producer's capacity for work\" — that is, not by the value of the commodity labour-power (which resolves into the socially necessary means of subsistence for its reproduction) but by the worker's individual efficiency, his dexterity, strength, and application. This appearance has consequences that extend well beyond academic confusion. Marx cites, in the footnote appended to this passage, the work of John Watts ( Trade Societies and Strikes, Machinery and Co-operative Societies , Manchester, 1865, pp. 52–3), who asserts that \"the system of piece-work illustrates an epoch in the history of the working-man; it is halfway between the position of the mere day-labourer depending upon the will of the capitalist and the co-operative artisan, who in the not distant future promises to combine the artisan and the capitalist in his own person.\" For Watts, the piece-worker is his own master, \"even whilst working upon the capital of the employer.\" Marx's treatment of this is brutal and dismissive: he quotes the book \"because it is a veritable gutter full of long-decayed and apologetic commonplaces,\" and he convicts its author of a reactionary volte-face, noting that Watts had previously published, in 1842, a pamphlet ( Facts and Fictions of Political Economy ) in which \"property is robbery\" was declared — \"but that is already in the distant past.\" The lesson here is a methodological one about how to read the phenomenal form of a social relation: the piece-wage's appearance of independence is not a neutral fact but an ideological resource that apologetics seize upon. The Empirical Shock: Both Forms Coexist Simultaneously The first solvent Marx pours onto the illusion is empirical. The claim that piece-wages reflect a different purchase — labour objectified rather than labour-power — cannot survive the fact that \"both forms of wages exist side by side, at the same time, in the same branches of industry.\" He supplies concrete, documented examples: the compositors of London \"as a general rule, work by the piece, time-work being the exception,\" whereas the compositors of the countryside \"work by the day, the exception being work by the piece.\" The shipwrights of the port of London work by the job or piece, \"while those of all other ports work by the day\" (cited from T. J. Dunning, Trades' Unions and Strikes , London, 1860, p. 22). In the saddlery shops of London, \"often for the same work,\" Frenchmen are paid piece-wages while Englishmen are paid time-wages. Even inside factories, where piece-wages are the general rule, \"certain specific operations have to be excepted from this form of evaluation on technical grounds,\" and these are paid by time-wages. If the two forms were expressions of genuinely distinct purchase-contracts — embodied labour versus living labour — such arbitrary and geographically contingent substitutions would be unintelligible. Their very interchangeability proves that the difference is one of form of payment and not substance of exchange . The coexistence, however, is not merely a logical embarrassment to the illusion; it is, Marx shows, an instrument of fraud. The footnote to this passage cites the Reports of the Inspectors of Factories … 31 October 1860 , p. 9, to the effect that a factory employing 400 people, half paid by the piece and half by the day, is so arranged that the piece-workers have \"a direct interest in working longer hours,\" while the other 200, \"paid by the day, work equally long with the others, and get no more money for their overtime.\" The inspectors calculate: \"The work of these 200 people for half an hour a day is equal to one person's work for 50 hours, or 6/100 of one person's labour in a week, and is a positive gain to the employer.\" The mixed system thus converts the piece-workers into the unpaid instruments by which the day-workers' labour-time is extended without compensation. Leonard Horner, in the Reports … 30 April 1859 , pp. 8–9, is quoted to the same purpose: over-working \"to a very considerable extent still prevails; and, in most instances, with that security against detection and punishment which the law itself affords.\" Marx's conclusion is though measured: \"it is in itself obvious that the difference of form in the payment of wages in no way alters their essential nature, although the one form may be more favourable to the development of capitalist production than the other.\" That last clause is the hinge of the whole chapter — the two forms are identical in essence, but not in consequence. The Numerical Anatomy: Twelve Hours, Twenty-Four Pieces, Six Shillings The decisive proof that piece-wages are a converted time-wage is arithmetical, and Marx is at pains to make the conversion explicit and transparent. He stipulates: an ordinary working day of 12 hours, of which 6 are paid and 6 unpaid; the value-product of the day is 6 shillings; therefore the value-product of one hour of labour is 6d. He assumes a worker of average intensity and skill, working only the \"amount of labour-time socially necessary,\" produces in 12 hours \"twenty-four pieces, either distinct products or measurable parts of some integral construction.\" The value of those 24 pieces, once the constant capital consumed in them is deducted, is 6 shillings — so the value of a single piece is 3d. The worker, however, receives 1½d per piece, \"and thus earns 3 shillings in 12 hours.\" Two structural equivalences then fall out. First, at the level of the working day: with time-wages it is a matter of indifference whether we say the worker works 6 hours for himself and 6 for the capitalist, or works the first half of every hour for himself and the second half for the capitalist; the partition is conventional and distributive. Exactly the same indifference applies here: \"it does not matter whether we say that each individual piece is half paid for, and half unpaid for, or that the price of only twelve of the pieces is the equivalent of the value of the labour-power, while in the other twelve pieces surplus-value is incorporated.\" Twelve pieces at 3d apiece equal 3 shillings — the value of labour-power — and the remaining twelve pieces carry pure surplus-value. Surplus labour is thus distributed across every unit of output rather than confined to a block of time, but the rate of exploitation (here 100 per cent) is unaltered. The Irrationality of the Form and the Inversion of Measurement Marx then makes his sharpest conceptual move. Having established the equivalence, he declares: \"The form of piece-wages is just as irrational as that of time-wages.\" The irrationality is this — in the example, two pieces, after subtracting the value of the means of production consumed in them, are worth 6d, being the product of one hour of labour, yet the worker receives for them a price of 3d. This is the same rent-like concealment as in time-wages: the wage-form presents the price of the whole hour as though it were the price of labour, whereas half of it is unpaid. From this it follows that \"Piece-wages are not in fact a direct expression of any relation of value. It is not, therefore, a question of measuring the value of the piece by the labour-time incorporated in it. It is rather the reverse: the labour the worker has expended must be measured by the number of pieces he has produced.\" This is the inversion of measurement , and it is the theoretical core of the chapter. In time-wages, labour is measured by its immediate duration. In piece-wages, labour is measured by the quantity of products in which it has become embodied during a given time. But because the price of labour-time is itself finally fixed by the equation value of a day of labour = daily value of labour-power , the product-measure is simply an indirect route back to the same quantity: hours. The measurement runs backwards through the commodity, but it terminates at the same place. Marx cites in support a 1796 work, the Abrégé élémentaire des principes de l'économie politique (Paris, p. 32), by G. Garnier: \"Wages can be measured in two ways: either by the duration of the labour, or by its product.\" Hence the chapter's conclusion: \"The piece-wage is therefore only a modified form of the time-wage.\" Peculiarity One — The Quality of Labour Policed by the Work Itself There follows a taxonomic survey of what Marx calls \"the characteristic peculiarities of piece-wages.\" The first is that \"the quality of the labour is here controlled by the work itself, which must be of good average quality if the piece-price is to be paid in full.\" The product becomes its own inspector. Where the worker's output is defective, the penalty falls on him — the piece-price is docked, or the goods are rejected. Marx cites Andrew Ure's Philosophy of Manufactures (pp. 316–17) for the classic illustration from spinning: \"So much weight of cotton is delivered to him [the spinner] and he has to return by a certain time, in lieu of it, a given weight of twist or yarn, of a certain degree of fineness, and he is paid so much per pound for all that he so returns. If his work is defective in quality, the penalty falls on him; if less in quantity than the minimum fixed for a given time, he is dismissed and an abler operative procured.\" Two consequences are thus fused in a single mechanism: quality assurance is shifted from paid supervision to unpaid worker self-discipline, and the threshold of \"acceptable\" output becomes a moving frontier that can be used to dismiss the weak. Marx's verdict is unsparing: \"Piece-wages become, from this point of view, the most fruitful source of reductions in wages, and of frauds committed by the capitalists.\" Peculiarity Two — Intensity Measured with Precision The same mechanism which polices quality also, and more importantly, calibrates intensity. Because the piece-price presumes a definite quantity of output per unit of time, \"only the labour-time which is embodied in a quantity of commodities laid down in advance and fixed by experience counts as socially necessary labour-time and is paid as such.\" Marx gives a fascinating ethnographic detail from the London tailoring trade: \"a certain piece of work, a waistcoat for instance, is called an hour, or half an hour, the hour being valued at 6d.\" That is, the unit of account itself becomes a unit of product — the \"hour\" is a garment. Practice, not the clock, determines \"the size of the average product of one hour.\" And because the standard is conventional rather than natural, every change in fashion or design opens a contest: \"a contest arises between the employer and the worker as to whether a particular piece of work is one hour, and so on, until here also experience decides.\" The same practice is documented for London furniture workshops. The sanction attached to failing the standard is dismissal: the worker who \"cannot provide labour of an average degree of efficiency, and if he cannot therefore supply a certain minimum of work per day,\" is let go. Intensity, in short, is not merely measured; it is enforced by the wage-form itself . Peculiarity Three — The Euthanasia of Supervision, Subletting, and Hierarchical Exploitation From the first two peculiarities Marx draws the third, which is the chapter's most structurally significant claim. \"Since the quality and intensity of the work are here controlled by the very form of the wage, superintendence of labour becomes to a great extent superfluous.\" The capitalist no longer needs to stand over the worker; the piece-rate does it. This has two far-reaching consequences. First, piece-wages become \"the basis for the modern 'domestic labour'\" described in an earlier chapter of Capital — the system in which the worker, nominally independent, works in his own dwelling on materials supplied by a merchant-capitalist, and in which the wage-relation is concealed beneath the appearance of a simple purchase of finished goods. Because no supervision is required and overhead is externalised onto the worker's own household, this \"domestic\" form becomes viable and widespread. Second, piece-wages become the basis for \"a hierarchically organized system of exploitation and oppression,\" of which Marx identifies two fundamental forms. The first is subletting : piece-wages \"make it easier for parasites to interpose themselves between the capitalist and the wage-labourer,\" and \"the profits of these middlemen come entirely from the difference between the price of labour which the capitalist pays, and the part of that price they actually allow the worker to receive.\" In England, Marx notes, this is \"characteristically\" called the sweating system. The Children's Employment Commission, Second Report (p. lxx, n. 424) is cited: \"It is when work passes through several hands, each of which is to take its share of profits, while only the last does the work, that the pay which reaches the workwoman is miserably disproportioned.\" The chain of subcontractors is a chain of deductions, each link living off the gap between what capital pays and what the actual producer receives. The second form is the group-contracting or \"butty\" arrangement: \"piece-wages allow the capitalist to make a contract for so much per piece with the most important worker — in manufacture, with the chief of some group, in mines with the extractor of the coal, in the factory with the actual machine-worker — at a price for which this man himself undertakes the enlisting and the payment of his assistants.\" Here the essential relation is not merely concealed; it is doubled . \"Here the exploitation of the worker by capital takes place through the medium of the exploitation of one worker by another.\" The foreman, the hewer, the senior operative becomes a sub-capitalist, whose own interests lie in squeezing his fellows. Even the apologist Watts is forced to concede, as Marx notes with some relish: \"It would be a great improvement to the system of piece-work, if all the men employed on a job were partners in the contract, each according to his abilities, instead of one man being interested in over-working his fellows for his own benefit.\" Marx adds, from the Children's Employment Commission, Third Report , the phrase \"the vile nature of the piece-work system\" as the considered judgment of the commissioners themselves. Peculiarity Four — Self-Exploitation and the Lengthening of the Working Day The fourth peculiarity concerns the alignment of the worker's immediate private interest with the most exhausting rhythms of capital. \"Given the system of piece-wages, it is naturally in the personal interest of the worker that he should strain his labour-power as intensely as possible; this in turn enables the capitalist to raise the normal degree of intensity of labour more easily.\" The worker, paid per unit, discovers that more units mean more money within the same hours — so he polices his own effort. Marx is careful to note that this spontaneous tendency is often artificially cultivated : in the London engineering trade, the customary trick is \"the selecting of a man who possesses superior physical strength and quickness, as the principal of several workmen, and paying him an additional rate,\" with the explicit understanding that he is \"to exert himself to the utmost to induce the others, who are only paid the ordinary wages, to keep up to him.\" Dunning, the trade-union secretary from whom Marx takes this passage, notes that \"without any comment this will go far to explain many of the complaints of stinting the action, superior skill, and working-power, made by the employers against the men\" — and Marx observes that even though Dunning is a worker and union official, and might therefore be suspected of exaggeration, the same technique is recommended to farmers as an approved one in the \"highly respectable\" Cyclopaedia of Agriculture edited by J. C. Morton. The same logic extends the working day itself. \"The lengthening of the working day is now in the personal interest of the worker, since with it his daily or weekly wages rise.\" The inspectors document the willingness: \"All those who are paid by piece-work … profit by the transgression of the legal limits of work.\" The Reports of the Inspectors of Factories … 30 April 1858 , p. 9, singles out women employed as weavers and reelers as especially prone to this self-overworking. The Children's Employment Commission, First Report , p. xiii, records that the piece-work system \"tends directly to encourage the young potter greatly to over-work himself during the four or five years during which he is employed in the piece-work system, but at low wages,\" and identifies this as \"another great cause to which the bad constitutions of the potters are to be attributed.\" But Marx immediately notes a reaction, \"like that already described in time-wages.\" As hours lengthen and workers compete, the piece-rate is pressured downward, so the increased effort purchases no permanent increase in the daily wage. And he adds the crucial structural point, which is independent of any such downward drift: \"quite apart from the fact that the prolongation of the working day, even if the piece-wage remains constant, includes of necessity a fall in the price of the labour.\" The reason is definitional — a fixed piece-price for a longer day means the same daily value of labour-power is purchased with more labour, so the price of labour (the wage per unit of labour-time) falls. The worker's apparent self-advancement is a self-undermining. Peculiarity Five — Individualisation, the Sense of Independence, and the Lowering of the Average The final peculiarity is the most dialectically interesting. \"In time-wages, with few exceptions, the same wage is paid for the same function, while in piece-wages, although the price of the labour-time is measured by a definite quantity of the product, the daily or weekly wage will vary with the individual differences between the workers,\" one supplying only the minimum, another the average, a third more than the average. There is therefore \"great variety among the individual workers, according to their different degrees of skill, strength, energy and staying-power\" — a point Dunning also makes (op. cit., p. 17): in piece-work \"wages may very materially differ in amount,\" whereas in day-work there is generally \"an uniform rate … recognized by both employer and employed as the standard of wages for the general run of workmen in the trade.\" Marx then insists — twice — that this variation \"does not alter the general relation between capital and wage-labour,\" and he gives two reasons. First , individual differences cancel out in the aggregate: \"the individual differences cancel each other out in the workshop as a whole, which thus supplies the average product within a given period of labour, and the total wages paid will be the average wage of that particular branch of industry.\" The capitalist pays, in total, the average; the dispersion is internal to the wage-bill. Second , and more fundamentally, \"the proportion between wages and surplus-value remains unaltered, since the mass of surplus labour supplied by each particular worker corresponds with the wage he receives.\" The man who produces more is paid more, and for that very reason also generates more surplus-value; the rate of exploitation is invariant across the distribution. Nevertheless, the form has a dynamic effect on that distribution, and here Marx identifies one of the most consequential tendencies of piece-work. \"The wider scope that piece-wages give to individuality tends to develop both that individuality, and with it the worker's sense of liberty, independence and self-control, and also the competition of workers with each other.\" This is a genuinely two-sided observation. Piece-work produces a subjective figure — the self-reliant, competitive, self-managing worker, proud of his output — and it is precisely this subjectivity that Watts and others mistook for genuine independence. But the objective consequence is the reverse of what it feels like: \"The piece-wage therefore has a tendency, while raising the wages of individuals above the average, to lower this average itself.\" The exemplar raises the collective standard; the collective standard then becomes the new normal against which everyone is measured; and the reward for the exemplar is converted into a uniform intensification. The illusion of freedom is the mechanism of its own dissolution. Marx notes, with a telling historical illustration, that where this downward adjustment has been blocked by custom, capital turns to outright compulsion: where \"a particular rate of piece-wage has for a long time been a fixed tradition, and its lowering, therefore, has presented especial difficulties, in such exceptional cases the masters have sometimes had recourse to the forcible transformation of piece-wages into time-wages.\" In 1860 this action set off a big strike among the ribbon-weavers of Coventry. The direction of the manoeuvre is significant: time-wages, normally the older and less \"advanced\" form, can be imposed as a weapon precisely because they permit a de facto rate reduction without the appearance of altering an established price. And finally, \"the piece-wage is one of the chief supports of the hour-system described in the preceding chapter\" — the practice whereby employers engage workers only when work is available, so that idle time is at the workers' expense. The Les Typographes devant le tribunal correctionnel de Bruxelles (Brussels, 1865, p. 9), by H. Grégoir, is quoted on this: employers take on \"many more workers than were needed actually to do the work\" in the expectation of uncertain or \"even completely imaginary\" work, and since they are paid piece-wages, \"the employers say to themselves that they run no risk, because any loss of working time will be at the expense of the workers who are unoccupied.\" The piece-form transfers the entire risk of market fluctuation onto the workforce. Historical Vindication and the Cantillon Excursus At this point Marx turns to history, and the argument acquires a developmental dimension. \"From what has been shown so far, it is apparent that the piece-wage is the form of wage most appropriate to the capitalist mode of production.\" He is careful, however, to deny novelty to the form: \"Although by no means new — it figures officially side by side with time-wages in the French and English labour statutes of the fourteenth century — it only conquered a larger field of action during the period of manufacture properly so-called.\" Its great trauma came later: \"In the stormy youth of large-scale industry, and particularly from 1797 to 1815, it served as a lever for the lengthening of the working day and the lowering of wages.\" Marx then adduces what he calls \"very important material bearing on the movement of wages during that period,\" drawn from two Blue Books: the Report and Evidence from the Select Committee on Petitions Respecting the Corn Laws (Parliamentary Session 1813–14), and the Report from the Lords' Committee, on the State of the Growth, Commerce, and Consumption of Grain (Session 1814–15). These contain \"documentary evidence of the constant lowering of the price of labour from the beginning of the Anti-Jacobin War.\" In weaving, \"piece-wages had fallen so low that in spite of the very great lengthening of the working day, the daily wage was then lower than it had been before.\" A contemporaneous pamphlet ( Remarks on the Commercial Policy of Great Britain , London, 1815, p. 48) is quoted: \"The real earnings of the cotton weaver are now far less than they were; his superiority over the common labourer, which at first was very great, has now almost entirely ceased. Indeed … the difference in the wages of skilful and common labour is far less now than at any former period.\" Two things are happening at once: the absolute wage is falling even as hours lengthen, and the wage differential between skilled and common labour is being compressed — a direct consequence, in Marx's framework, of the intensification and homogenisation of labour that generalised piece-work produces. For agriculture, Marx quotes a pamphlet written in defence of the landlords and farmers ( A Defence of the Landowners and Farmers of Great Britain , London, 1814, pp. 4–5): \"By far the greater part of agricultural operations is done by people, who are hired for the day or on piece-work. Their weekly wages are about 12 shillings, and although it may be assumed that a man earns on piece-work under the greater stimulus to labour, 1 shilling, or perhaps 2 shillings more than on weekly wages, yet it is found, on calculating his total income, that his loss of employment, during the year, outweighs this gain.\" This is the risk-transfer point made empirically: the piece-premium is more than cancelled by the unpaid idle time that the piece-form permits. The pamphlet continues, in a passage Marx quotes without comment: wages \"bear a certain proportion to the price of the necessary means of subsistence, so that a man with two children is able to bring up his family without recourse to parish relief\" — a statement which, in Marx's framework, is simply the classical confirmation that the wage hovers around the value of labour-power, no more. The chapter's historical section concludes with a quotation from Malthus's Inquiry into the Nature and Progress of Rent (London, 1815, p. 49, note): \"I confess that I see, with misgiving, the great extension of the practice of piece-wage. Really hard work during 12 or 14 hours of the day, or for any longer time, is too much for any human being.\" Marx's deployment of the arch-defender of population doctrine as a witness against the piece-wage is a characteristic rhetorical move: the system is condemned even by those whose economic convictions lie wholly on the other side. Productivity, the Falling Piece-Rate, and the Contradiction Made Visible The final analytic section examines what happens to the piece-rate when the productivity of labour changes. Marx's reasoning is rooted in the labour theory of value: \"Changes in the productivity of labour mean that the same quantity of a given product represents an amount of labour-time which varies. Therefore, the piece-wage also varies, for it is the expression of the price of a definite amount of labour-time.\" He returns to his example. Twenty-four pieces in 12 hours; value-product of 12 hours 6 shillings; daily value of labour-power 3 shillings; price of an hour of labour 3d; wage per piece 1½d; half an hour of labour absorbed in one piece. Now suppose productivity doubles, so that the same working day yields 48 pieces instead of 24, \"all other circumstances remaining unchanged.\" Then \"the piece-wage falls from 1½d. to ¾d., as every piece now only represents ¼ instead of ½ a working hour. 24 × 1½d. = 3s., and, similarly, 48 × ¾d. = 3s.\" The arithmetic is exact and the implication is total: the daily wage is unchanged at 3 shillings, the value-product is unchanged at 6 shillings (since value is fixed by labour-time and not by output), and the entire gain from increased productivity accrues to capital in the form of cheaper unit costs. The piece-wage, as Marx says, \"is lowered in the same proportion as the number of pieces produced in the same time rises, and therefore in the same proportion as the amount of labour-time employed on the same piece falls.\" This is where Marx engages Ure most directly, and the engagement is a study in how to read apologetics. Ure writes ( Philosophy of Manufactures , p. 317): \"The productive power of his spinning-machine is accurately measured, and the rate of pay for work done with it decreases with, though not as, the increase of its productive power.\" Marx notes two things. First, Ure himself later contradicts the apologetic qualification: he admits that lengthening the mule increases the quantity of labour required, so that \"the amount of labour does not, therefore, diminish in the same ratio as its productivity increases.\" Second, Ure's own further evidence shows the spinner's position deteriorating even where his money earnings nominally rise. Marx quotes (p. 321): \"By this increase the productive power of the machine will be augmented one-fifth. When this event happens the spinner will not be paid at the same rate for work done as he was before, but as that rate will not be diminished in the ratio of one-fifth, the improvement will augment his money earnings for any given number of hours of work\" — but, Ure immediately adds, \"the foregoing statement requires a certain modification … The spinner has to pay something additional for juvenile aid out of his additional sixpence.\" And Ure also concedes that improvements in machinery \"displace a portion of adults,\" which, Marx notes dryly, \"certainly does not tend to raise wages.\" The pattern is revealing: the apologist's own casuistry is undone by the apologist's own data. The Final Struggle: The Worker's \"Gross Error\" and the Capitalist's Rejoinder The chapter closes on the terrain of class struggle. The change in the piece-wage, though \"so far purely nominal\" (since the daily wage is unchanged when productivity rises), \"leads to constant struggles between the capitalist and the worker.\" Marx identifies three distinct grounds of friction. First, the capitalist \"uses it as a pretext for actually lowering the price of labour\" — the nominal adjustment is opportunistically extended into a real one. Second, an increase in the productivity of labour is \"accompanied by an increase in its intensity,\" so the worker's real expenditure of effort rises without commensurate compensation. Third, and most revealingly, \"the worker takes the outward appearance of piece-wages seriously, i.e. he thinks his product is being paid for and not his labour-power, and he therefore resists any reduction of wages which is not accompanied by a reduction in the selling price of the commodity.\" This is the practical consequence of the theoretical illusion with which the chapter began. The worker's own tacit theory of the wage — that he is paid for the product — leads him to demand a share in any increase in the product's value in the market. H. Fawcett's The Economic Position of the British Labourer (Cambridge and London, 1865, p. 178) is cited for the workers' practice: \"The operatives … carefully watch the price of the raw material and the price of manufactured goods, and are thus enabled to form an accurate estimate of their master's profits.\" From the appearance that the product is being paid for, it is a short step to the claim that worsening sale conditions, or improving ones, ought to be reflected in the rate — a type of claim that shades into profit-sharing. Marx's comment on the capitalist's response is one of the most ironic passages in the chapter: \"The capitalist rightly rejects such pretensions as being gross errors as to the nature of wage-labour. He cries out against this presumptuous attempt to lay taxes on the progress of industry, and declares roundly that the productivity of labour does not concern the worker in the least.\" The word \"rightly\" is doing enormous work. From the standpoint of the concept of wage-labour — the purchase of a definite labour-power for a definite price — the capitalist is correct: the productivity of labour is none of the worker's business, because the worker is not selling a product but a capacity. The worker's error is not moral but conceptual, and it is an error manufactured by the wage-form itself , which presents the product as the object of exchange. The capitalist's advantage consists precisely in being able to switch between the two readings as convenience dictates: the form says \"you are paid for your product,\" but the substance says \"your product is not your concern.\" This duplicity is documented in the chapter's final footnotes. The London Standard of 26 October 1861 (note 19) reports proceedings brought by the firm of John Bright and Co. before the Rochdale magistrates \"to prosecute for intimidation the agents of the Carpet Weavers' Trades' Union.\" Bright's partners had installed new machinery \"which would turn out 240 yards of carpet in the time and with the labour (!) previously required to produce 160 yards.\" The law and the capitalists held that \"the workmen had no claim whatever to share in the profits made by the investment of their employer's capital in mechanical improvements.\" Accordingly Bright proposed to lower the rate of pay from 1½d per yard to 1d — precisely the productivity-proportional reduction Marx's arithmetic predicted — \"leaving the earnings of the men exactly the same as before for the same labour. But there was a nominal reduction, of which the operatives, it is asserted, had not fair warning beforehand.\" The struggle is thus not over the level of the wage but over whether the piece-rate is a price of a product (subject to renegotiation when the product becomes cheaper to make) or a price of labour-power (fixed by the cost of reproducing the worker). The capitalists answered: the latter — while the workers, taking the form at its word, answered: the former. And the final note (note 20), from On Combinations of Trades (new edn, London, 1834, p. 42), gives the definitive statement of the capitalist position: \"'Trades' Unions, in their desire to maintain wages, endeavour to share in the benefits of improved machinery!' ( Quelle horreur! ) 'The demanding higher wages, because labour is abbreviated, is in other words the endeavour to establish a duty on mechanical improvements.'\" With that, the chapter closes: the wage-form's illusion, the worker's resistance to its dissolution, and the capitalist's insistence on the harshest version of the truth. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The most fundamental lesson of Chapter 21 is a principle of form-analysis as such: the phenomenal form of an economic relation is not a neutral wrapper around its content but a historically active mechanism. Marx's entire procedure here consists in distinguishing essence from appearance without ever treating the distinction as inert. Piece-wages are time-wages in essence — the same 3 shillings, the same 6 shillings of value-product, the same 100 per cent rate of exploitation. But the form is not therefore epiphenomenal. It changes the distribution of surplus-value among capitals (via subletting), the organisation of the labour process (supervision becomes \"superfluous,\" domestic labour becomes viable, internal hierarchies emerge), the risk-bearing of production (idle time falls on the worker, the \"hour-system\" is sustained), the subjective disposition of the worker (independence, competition, self-exploitation), and the dynamic path of the wage (the average is lowered even as individuals rise above it). Essence without form is a mere abstraction; form without essence is ideology. The second lesson is that this chapter supplies the missing link in the theory of surplus-value's concealment . Value theory explains how surplus-value is produced; the wage-form chapters explain how it is rendered invisible . Time-wages conceal the division of the day by presenting a price for \"labour.\" Piece-wages conceal it twice over, because they additionally present a price for the product , thereby transferring the relation into the seemingly neutral and voluntarist domain of market exchange — the worker sells goods, and gets paid for them in proportion to his efficiency. The worker's own spontaneous consciousness, as Marx notes, is formed by this appearance; his resistance to a rate cut takes the form of a claim about the product's price, and he is thereby placed in the position of someone making a \"gross error\" about his own condition. The form of wages is thus not merely an economic category but an ideological one: it generates the very \"trade-union consciousness\" that capital can then denounce as ignorance of economics. The third lesson is historical and developmental. Piece-wages are the form \"most appropriate to the capitalist mode of production\" — not because they are exploitative in a merely local sense, but because they are the form in which capital's drive to intensify and extend labour is transferred from external coercion to the worker's own self-interest, and because they are uniquely suited to the period of manufacture's subcontracting hierarchies, to the prolongation of the working day in 1797–1815, and, under the Factory Acts, to capital's need to increase output \"only by intensifying labour,\" since hours are legally capped. The empirical dossier from the Blue Books of 1813–15 corroborates the theoretical prediction: falling real wages for weavers, compression of the skilled-unskilled differential, an agricultural proletariat whose piece-work premium is cancelled by unemployment. Marx's structural claim — that piece-wages give capital a leverage for lengthening the working day and lowering wages — is therefore not a rhetorical flourish but a stated and partly documented historical thesis. The broader implications extend in two directions. For political economy, the chapter exposes the apologetic use of the illusion, exemplified by Watts and unmasked by the exposure of his earlier position — a case study in how an intellectual converts the appearance of worker independence into a defence of the piece-work system. For workers' practice, the chapter explains why the piece-rate is an inevitable and perpetual site of conflict: it is a price attached to a product whose value is not the worker's, and both parties can play the double character of the form against each other. Marx's closing irony — that the capitalist \"rightly\" rejects the workers' claim — is a precise statement that within the wage-relation as legally defined, the worker's moral conviction is a category mistake, and that the only remedy lies not in correcting the category but in transcending the relation that generates it. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 20 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 22"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 22", "title": "Chapter 22", "subtitle": "National Differences in Wages", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 22/Chapter 22  National Differences in Wages_explanation.html", "word_count": 5550, "read_time": "28 min read", "tags": ["Wages & Value of Labour-Power"], "keywords": ["National Differences in Wages", "Relative Wages in World Market", "Comparative Intensity and Productivity of Nations", "Value of Money across Countries", "Nominal vs Real Purchasing Power across Borders"], "text": "Chapter 22 National Differences In Wages - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,511 words ⏱️ ~28 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Core Thesis. Chapter 22 of Capital , Volume I, is a short but extraordinarily dense hinge-chapter that performs a conceptual translation. Its central proposition is that the very laws which Chapter 17 established concerning the value of labour-power — the manifold combinations that raise or lower that value, whether measured absolutely or relatively against surplus-value — do not vanish when we step from one country to another. Instead, they reappear \"as a set of simultaneous differences in national wage-levels.\" What is a temporal succession of varying combinations inside a single nation becomes a spatial array of coexisting national wage structures. Marx's task is therefore to specify precisely what an honest international comparison of wages would require, why the naive comparison (comparing money wages directly) is fraudulent, and what the true relationship is between the poverty or wealth of a nation's workers and the apparent height of their wages. The chapter's decisive conclusion, stated early and defended empirically and polemically thereafter, is a double one. First, nominal wages are typically higher in the more developed capitalist nation than in the less developed one , but this \"by no means proves that the same can be said of real wages.\" Second — and more explosively — the relative price of labour (the price of labour measured against surplus-value and against the value of the product) frequently stands higher in the poorer, less-developed nation than in the richer one. In other words, labour can be dear to the capitalist precisely where the worker is paid least, and cheap to the capitalist where the worker's money wage is highest. This is the theoretical bombshell the chapter exists to deliver. The Central Problem and Intellectual Objective. The problem Marx confronts is that the category \"wages\" is, as he established in the immediately preceding chapters, an exoteric form — an outward appearance that conceals the underlying value-relation of labour-power. Because the wage-form hides the value of labour-power, comparing wages across nations on the basis of their surface appearance is guaranteed to mislead. The chapter's objective is therefore twofold: Methodological : to establish the conditions under which a valid international comparison is even possible — specifically, the reduction of average daily wages in the same trades to a uniform working day , followed by the translation of the time-wage into the piece-wage , \"as only the latter can be a measure both of the productivity and of the intensity of labour.\" Theoretical and polemical : to show how the law of value is modified — not abolished — in its international application, and to demolish the optimistic doctrine of Henry Charles Carey that national wage differences are directly proportional to differences in the productivity of labour, a doctrine Marx exposes as both logically absurd (given his own analysis of surplus-value) and empirically falsified by the evidence of factory inspectors. Fit within the Broader Framework. The chapter sits at the culmination of Part VI (\"Wages\") of Capital Volume I. Having analysed in Chapter 17 the transformation of the value (and price) of labour-power into wages, and in Chapters 19–21 the forms of wages (time-wage, piece-wage) and their national peculiarities, Marx now pushes the analysis onto the world market . In doing so he foreshadows the later, more fully developed treatment of the world market and international exchange, and anticipates themes that would become central to theories of unequal exchange and uneven development. Structurally, the chapter functions as a bridge: it connects the micro-logic of the wage-form to the macro-logic of international economic hierarchy, and it simultaneously serves as a platform for ideological combat against the \"harmonious wisdom\" of the free-trade optimists. It is at once a technical chapter on comparative wage measurement and a political intervention into the ideological self-understanding of capitalist political economy. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Point of Departure — Chapter 17 and the Transformation of Value into Wages The chapter opens by explicitly recalling its own foundation. In Chapter 17, Marx had been concerned with \"the manifold combinations which may bring about a change in the magnitude of the value of labour-power,\" considering that magnitude \"either absolutely or relatively (i.e. as compared with surplus-value).\" The phrase \"value of labour-power\" is the load-bearing concept here and must be held firmly: it is not the same as wages. The value of labour-power is the value of the means of subsistence required to produce, maintain, and reproduce the worker — determined, like all value, by the socially necessary labour-time embodied in those means of subsistence, but conditioned by what Marx elsewhere calls the \"natural and historical\" elements of a given society's standard of life. Marx then reminds us of a subtle finding from Chapter 17: the quantity of the means of subsistence in which the price of labour-power is realized \"might again undergo fluctuations independent of, or different from, the changes in this price.\" This is the wedge that opens the entire chapter. The price of labour-power (its money expression) and the mass of use-values that price can command are two distinct variables that can move in different directions. This non-identity is precisely what makes cross-national comparison treacherous. The pivot of the whole chapter is stated in one sentence: \"the simple translation of the value, or respectively of the price, of labour-power, into the exoteric form of wages transforms all these laws into laws governing the movement of wages.\" The term exoteric form is critical — it means the outward, surface, publicly visible form , as opposed to the inner essential relation. Wages are the phenomenal form in which the value of labour-power presents itself on the market. Because that surface form is what everyone sees, the underlying laws of value \"appear\" ( erscheinen ) as laws of wage movement. When we compare nations, this translation effect produces the illusion that we are comparing like with like, when in fact we are comparing differently-constituted surface appearances of different underlying value-relations. Hence the methodological pivot from time to space: what \"appears within the movement of wages as a series of varying combinations may appear for different countries as a set of simultaneous differences in national wage-levels.\" A single nation runs through a sequence ; the international arena displays the same variety as a simultaneous array . This insight reframes the comparative problem entirely: the differences between nations are not exotic anomalies but the same laws playing out under different conditions. 2.2 The Comparative Method — Uniform Working Day and the Primacy of Piece-Wage From the reformulation of the problem, Marx immediately derives the conditions of any valid comparison. To compare wages in different nations, \"we must therefore take into account all the factors that determine changes in the amount of the value of labour-power.\" He enumerates them with precision: the price and the extent of the prime necessities of life in their natural and historical development — that is, both the cost of the subsistence basket and the historically formed standard of what counts as necessary; the cost of training the workers — the reproduction cost of skilled labour; the part played by the labour of women and children — the composition of the labouring family unit whose combined wage reproduces the working class; the productivity of labour — output per unit of labour-time; its extensive and intensive magnitude — the length of the working day (extensive) and the density/concentration of effort per unit of time (intensive). Then comes the first technical prescription: \"Even the most superficial comparison requires the prior reduction of the average daily wage for the same trades, in different countries, to a uniform working day.\" This is indispensable because comparing, say, a 10-hour English day with a 14.5-hour Continental day at face value conflates the price per day with the price per hour of labour . Marx had already established in the analysis of the time-wage that the daily wage is the product of the price of labour-power and the length of the working day; a longer day at the same daily wage conceals a lower hourly price. Without normalization, the comparison is meaningless. But even this reduction is insufficient. After wages are reduced to a common time-basis, \"the time-wage must again be translated into the piece-wage, as only the latter can be a measure both of the productivity and of the intensity of labour.\" This is a profound methodological claim. The piece-wage (payment per unit produced) is, in Marx's analysis, the form of wages that most directly registers both how much is produced (productivity) and how hard labour is expended (intensity). A time-wage hides these differences because it pays for time spent rather than work done ; a piece-wage, by contrast, is a direct index of output, and therefore a comparative instrument capable of capturing the very variables — productivity and intensity — that make national wage levels incommensurable on the surface. 2.3 The National Average Intensity of Labour and the World Market The next beat introduces the pivotal concept of national average intensity of labour . Within any given country, Marx explains, \"there is a certain average intensity of labour, below which the labour for the production of a commodity requires more than the time socially necessary, and therefore does not count as labour of normal quality.\" This is a crucial refinement of the theory of socially necessary labour-time: intensity is not merely a psychological or physiological matter but is socially standardized. In a given country, \"only a degree of intensity which is above the national average alters the measurement of value by the mere duration of labour-time.\" In other words, inside a nation, the average intensity is tacitly treated as the norm, and only deviations above the norm create more value per unit of time. But \"it is otherwise on the world market, whose integral parts are the individual countries.\" Here the critical move is made: \"The average intensity of labour changes from country to country; here it is greater, there less. These national averages form a scale whose unit of measurement is the average unit of universal labour.\" The concept of universal labour (or abstract, general human labour) supplies the measuring unit for the international scale. This means that the different national intensities are not private facts sealed within borders but are ranked on a single comparative scale established by the world market. The consequence is stated without qualification: \"The more intense national labour, therefore, as compared with the less intense, produces in the same time more value, which expresses itself in more money.\" Thus the labor-time that is \"socially necessary\" economically is, at the international level, a weighted magnitude. A day of labour in a high-intensity nation counts for more than a day of labour in a low-intensity nation, because the world market imposes a single metric onto the national averages. This is the first major theoretical platform for the chapter's conclusion: nations with more intense labour create more value per hour and hence would command higher money returns, independently of any difference in workers' real standard of living. 2.4 Productivity as Intensity — The International Modification of the Law of Value Marx then deepens the argument by folding productivity into the same logic: \"the law of value is yet more modified in its international application by the fact that, on the world market, national labour which is more productive also counts as more intensive, as long as the more productive nation is not compelled by competition to lower the selling price of its commodities to the level of their value.\" This is a subtle and qualified claim. The qualifier — the conditional clause — is essential: the more productive nation's labour counts as more intensive only if competition does not force it to sell at value . If competitive pressure forces the price down to the value of the commodity, the productivity advantage is surrendered to consumers rather than retained as higher value per unit of labour. The conditionality here is Marx's recognition that the world market is a contested terrain, not a mechanically fixed hierarchy. The argument proceeds with a key proposition: \"In proportion as capitalist production is developed in a country, so, in the same proportion, do the national intensity and productivity of labour there rise above the international level.\" This ties the international scale directly to the degree of capitalist development. Development and intensity/productivity are posited as rising in lockstep. From this it follows that \"the different quantities of commodities of the same kind, produced in different countries in the same working time, have, therefore, unequal international values, which are expressed in different prices, i.e. in sums of money varying according to international values.\" We can render the implicit relation the text is working with in a compact reconstruction: Let $v_i$ be the international value produced by one unit of working time in nation $i$. Comparing two nations, $A$ (more developed) and $B$ (less developed): $v_A > v_B$, because the labour of $A$ counts as both more intense and more productive. Hence the money expression of one unit of working time is larger in $A$: $M_A > M_B$, where $M$ is the money sum representing that value. This formalization should be read strictly as a reconstruction of the text's stated logic, not as an equation supplied by Marx; the chapter contains no algebraic apparatus. The crucial conclusion follows: \"The relative value of money will therefore be less in the nation with a more developed capitalist mode of production than in the nation with a less developed capitalism.\" Because the labour of the developed nation embodies more value per unit of time, the commodities it produces confront money as being of higher value , so a unit of money represents a smaller quantum of value — the relative value of money (its value against commodities) is lower. The mechanism Marx is driving at is that a higher money wage can be nothing but the necessary expression of a lower value of money , not evidence of greater worker welfare. Therefore, \"nominal wages, the equivalent of labour-power expressed in money, will also be higher in the first nation than in the second; but this by no means proves that the same can be said of real wages, i.e. the means of subsistence placed at the disposal of the worker.\" This is the pivot on which the chapter's critical punch rests. Nominal wages are money wages — the sum of money the worker receives. Real wages are the mass of means of subsistence (use-values) that money commands. The two can diverge systematically across nations because the value of money itself is nation-specific. The reader should note the deliberate caution of Marx's footnote 2, where he defers the fuller investigation: \"We shall inquire, in another place, what circumstances in relation to productivity may modify this law for individual branches of production.\" This is an admission that the argument is stated at the level of national averages and that branch-specific complications remain to be treated. 2.5 The Relative Price of Labour — The Decisive Category The chapter now introduces and insists upon the category that carries its most important message: the relative price of labour , defined as \"the price of labour as compared both with surplus-value and with the value of the product.\" This is labour's cost to capital , its share in the value produced. Marx states the thesis flatly: \"even apart from these relative differences in the value of money in different countries, it will frequently be found that the daily or weekly wage in the first nation is higher than in the second while the relative price of labour ... stands higher in the second than in the first.\" In other words, the \"cheap labour\" nation is, from capital's standpoint, dear , and the \"expensive labour\" nation is cheap . This seeming paradox is the theoretical heart of the chapter. It is not a curiosity but a structural tendency. The reason is that the high-productivity, high-wage nation produces so much more value per worker that the worker's share of that value (the relative price of labour) can be smaller even when the absolute money paid is larger. Conversely, in the low-wage nation each worker produces little, so the small absolute wage can represent a large fraction of what the worker produces. Here Marx draws support from a historical authority in footnote 3: James Anderson , in a polemic against Adam Smith, distinguished the apparent price of labour (the daily wage) from the real price — \"that which a certain quantity of work performed actually costs the employer.\" Anderson argued that \"labour is in almost all cases cheaper in rich countries than in those that are poorer,\" and that \"Labour by the piece is generally cheaper in England.\" Anderson even notes the reciprocal process: \"a low level of wages produces, in its turn, dearness of labour,\" citing the observation that \"Labour being dearer in Ireland than it is in England ... because the wages are so much lower.\" This gives Marx an eighteenth-century authority for a central contention, and it also introduces the crucial concept of cost per unit of work performed as distinct from wage per day — precisely the distinction footnoted again in footnote 1, where David Buchanan observes that it is inaccurate to say wages rise merely because the same money \"purchases more of a cheaper article.\" 2.6 The Empirical Evidence — Cowell and Redgrave on British vs. Continental Labour Marx now substantiates the theory with the empirical investigations of official British factory inspectors — a hallmark of his method in this part of Capital . He cites J. W. Cowell , a member of the Factory Commission of 1833, who concluded after careful investigation of the spinning trade that \"in England wages are virtually lower to the capitalist, though higher to the operative than on the Continent of Europe.\" This single sentence crystallizes the chapter's thesis: the wage is simultaneously higher for the worker (in nominal terms) and lower for the capitalist (in relative terms). The two perspectives do not contradict; they describe different measures of the same fact. Marx then introduces Alexander Redgrave , an English factory inspector, whose Report of 31 October 1866 yields the chapter's richest empirical material. Redgrave \"proves, by comparing the statistics of Continental states, that in spite of lower wages and much longer hours of work, Continental labour is, in proportion to the product, dearer than English.\" The structure of the demonstration is important: lower wages and longer hours do not make Continental labour cheaper to capital; when measured against output, it is dearer . This is the empirical vindication of the \"relative price of labour\" category. The mechanism is revealed through a concrete case. The English manager of a cotton factory in Oldenburg declared \"that the working hours there were from 5.30 a.m. to 8 p.m., Saturdays included\" — an extraordinarily long day (14.5 hours, six days a week) — \"and that the workers, when under English overseers, did not supply during this time quite so much of the product as the English in 10 hours, while they supplied much less when under German overseers.\" Here intensity and productivity are isolated with striking clarity: even with the same supervision (English overseers), the Continental workers could not match English output per hour; under local supervision, they fell further behind. Wages are \"much lower than in England, in many cases lower by 50 per cent,\" yet the number of \"hands\" in proportion to the machinery is much greater — \"in certain departments in the proportion of 5 : 3.\" To operate the same machinery, more than one and a half times as many workers were required. The low-wage nation thus employs more labour-power per unit of capital, another dimension of labour's relative dearness. The next case, Russia, sharpens the point. Redgrave relayed precise details on Russian cotton factories from an English manager until recently employed there. On Russian soil, \"so fruitful of all infamies, the old horrors of the early days of English factories are in full swing.\" The managers are English, since \"the native Russian capitalist is incapable of handling factory business.\" Despite over-work \"which continues throughout the day and the night\" and \"the most shameful under-payment of the workers, Russian manufacturing only manages to vegetate thanks to the prohibition of foreign competition\" — i.e., protective tariffs. This is doubly significant: it shows that a regime of the very lowest wages and the very longest hours still fails to be competitive on its own terms, and it introduces the theme of state intervention (protection) that Marx will deploy against Carey. Cheap labour, far from guaranteeing capitalist viability, correlates with an inability to survive international competition without tariff shelter. 2.7 The Spindle Statistics — Quantifying Productivity and Intensity Differentials Redgrave's comparative tables provide the chapter's most quantitative evidence. The tables report two measures: average number of spindles per factory and average number of spindles per person across European countries. Average number of spindles per factory: England 12,600; France 1,500; Prussia 1,500; Belgium 4,000; Saxony 4,500; Austria 7,000; Switzerland 8,000. Average number of spindles per person (one person to): France 14; Russia 28; Prussia 37; Bavaria 46; Austria 49; Belgium 50; Saxony 50; Switzerland 55; Smaller German states 55; Great Britain 74 . These figures quantify two dimensions of the productivity/intensity differential. Spindles per factory measure the scale and concentration of capital; spindles per worker measure the technical productivity of labour — how much machinery each worker operates. Great Britain, the most developed capitalist nation, has both by far the largest factories (12,600 spindles, dwarfing France's and Prussia's 1,500) and the highest number of spindles per worker (74, against France's 14). Redgrave is careful about the reliability of the data: the figures \"were collected a few years ago, and ... since that time the size of the factories and the number of spindles per worker in England has increased,\" but he assumes \"an approximately equal advance has been made in the Continental countries,\" so the comparison retains its value. Redgrave adds a crucial caveat about the direction of bias: the comparison is \"yet more unfavourable to Great Britain, inasmuch as there is so large a number of factories in which weaving by power is carried on in conjunction with spinning\" — while, in the table, the weavers are not deducted , and \"the factories abroad are chiefly spinning factories.\" Since the British figures include weaving as well as spinning labour, the true British advantage in spinning productivity is understated. To drive this home, Redgrave offers a concrete illustration: \"many cotton spinning factories in my district in which mules containing 2,200 spindles are minded by one man (the 'minder') and two assistants only, turning off daily 220 lb. of yarn, measuring 400 miles in length.\" This single statistic — three workers, 2,200 spindles, 220 pounds and 400 miles of yarn daily — gives the productivity differential a tangible form and grounds the abstract category of intensity in measurable industrial practice. 2.8 Railway Construction and the Inverse Relationship Confirmed The chapter extends its empirical base beyond cotton. Marx notes that \"in Eastern Europe, as well as in Asia, English companies have undertaken the construction of railways, and have, in making them, employed a certain number of English workers side by side with the locals.\" This created a natural experiment in which English and local labour worked on the same projects. \"Practical necessity has thus compelled them to take into account the national differences in the intensity of labour, but this has not brought them any loss.\" The capitalists had to adjust their expectations and wage offers to the differing intensities of the national labour forces, and doing so proved profitable. The lesson is stated conceptually: \"Their experience shows that even if the level of wages more or less corresponds with the average intensity of labour, the relative price of labour (i.e. the price of labour in relation to the product) generally varies in the inverse direction.\" Wages may track intensity roughly, but the relative price of labour moves inversely — the higher the intensity (and wage), the lower labour's relative cost; the lower the intensity (and wage), the higher labour's relative cost. The railway example thus generalizes the finding beyond textiles and across continents. 2.9 The Critique of Carey — From False Premise to Ideological Apologetics The final movement of the chapter turns to open polemic against H. C. Carey and his Essay on the Rate of Wages (Philadelphia, 1835) — footnote 4 gives the full, revealing title: Essay on the Rate of Wages: With an Examination of the Causes of the Differences in the Condition of the Labouring Population throughout the World. Carey \"tries to prove that differences in national wage-levels are directly proportional to the degree of productivity of the working day of each nation, in order to draw from this international ratio the deduction that wages everywhere rise and fall in proportion to the productivity of labour.\" This is the doctrine Marx must demolish, because it underwrites the free-trade optimist's claim that the market harmonizes the interests of capital and labour: high productivity produces high wages automatically. Marx's refutation operates on three levels: logical, evidentiary, and ideological. Logical. \"The whole of our analysis of the production of surplus-value shows that this deduction would be absurd even if Carey himself had demonstrated his premises.\" The reduction is fatal: even granting Carey his premises, the conclusion does not follow. This is because the analysis of surplus-value establishes that higher productivity lowers the value of labour-power and raises surplus-value; it does not mechanically raise wages in proportion. The direct proportionality thesis is therefore internally void. Evidentiary. Marx accuses Carey of \"shuffling a confused mass of statistical material to and fro in his usual uncritical and superficial manner.\" Carey had not demonstrated his premises; he had merely assembled statistics without method. The comparison with Marx's own careful use of Cowell, Redgrave, and the spindle tables is implicit but pointed: Marx weighs his sources, distinguishes like from like, flags biases; Carey does none of this. Ideological. Marx then exposes Carey's confession-within-the-claim. Carey \"does not assert that things actually are as they ought to be according to his theory. For state intervention has falsified the natural economic relation.\" Carey must explain away reality by appeal to an external distorting force: protective tariffs (state intervention) have prevented the natural proportionality from materializing. He therefore proposes that \"the different national wages must therefore be calculated on the assumption that the part of them that goes to the state in the form of taxes was received by the worker himself\" — a hypothetical readjustment that removes the taxes and restores the imagined harmony. Marx's rejoinder is a single devastating question: \"Ought not Mr Carey to consider further whether these 'state expenses' are not the 'natural fruits' of capitalist development?\" If protection and state expenditure are products of capitalist development itself, then the \"natural\" harmony Carey posits is a fiction, and the intervention he deplores is internal to the system, not an external accident. 2.10 The Polemic Climax — Carey, Bastiat, and the Free-Trade Optimists The chapter closes with a sustained, almost invective passage that escalates the critique into an ideological unmasking. Marx constructs a portrait of Carey as a thinker whose contradictions reveal the general bankruptcy of harmony economics. Carey first \"declared that capitalist relations of production were eternal laws of nature and reason, whose free and harmonious working was only disturbed by the intervention of the state.\" He then \"discovered afterwards that state intervention, i.e. the defence of those laws of nature and reason by the state, alias the system of protection, was necessitated by the diabolical influence of England on the world market\" — an influence which, absurdly, \"does not spring from the natural laws of capitalist production.\" The escalation continues: Carey \"discovered, further, that the theorems of Ricardo and others, in which existing social antagonisms and contradictions are formulated, are not the ideal product of the real economic movement, but, on the contrary, that the real antagonisms of capitalist production in England and elsewhere are the result of the theories of Ricardo and others!\" This is the inversion Marx finds laughable — as if reality conformed to theory rather than the reverse, as if the class antagonisms of capitalism were caused by an economist's writings . Finally, Carey \"discovered that it is, in the last resort, trade which destroys the inborn beauties and harmonies of the capitalist mode of production.\" Marx's sarcasm peaks: \"A step further, and he will perhaps discover that the one evil in capitalist production is capital itself.\" The sentence is a trap: Carey's logic, if pursued consistently, would dissolve into the recognition that capitalism's contradictions are intrinsic — the very opposite of the harmony he preaches. Marx concludes that \"only a man with such an atrocious lack of the critical faculty, and such spurious erudition, deserved, in spite of his Protectionist heresy, to become the secret source of the harmonious wisdom of a Bastiat, and of all the other free-trade optimists of the present day.\" Bastiat stands here as the emblem of harmony economics — the doctrine that capitalism's free operation produces universal benefit. Marx's verdict is that Carey, despite being a protectionist (a \"heresy\" from the free-trade standpoint), nevertheless supplied the ideological raw material for the free-traders' \"harmonious wisdom.\" The polemic thus binds the technical argument to its political stakes: the illusion of harmonious international wage relations is not a neutral error but a class instrument. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed. The chapter yields several enduring systemic principles. First, the wage-form is a concealment , and any comparison built on surface appearance is necessarily distorted. The exoteric form of wages hides the value of labour-power, so the analyst must penetrate beneath nominal money sums. Second, the law of value is not transcended but modified on the world market : national averages of labour intensity and productivity are ranked on a single international scale measured in universal labour, so that the same working time represents unequal quantities of value across nations. Third, and most importantly, labour's cheapness is measured by its relative price, not its absolute wage . The high-wage, high-productivity nation can have cheap labour in the capitalist sense (a small share of a large product), while the low-wage nation can have dear labour (a large share of a small product). This inverts the commonsense equation of low wages with cheap labour — a reversal that Marx attributes in the older literature to James Anderson, and which he vindicates with the factory statistics of Cowell and Redgrave. The Inverse Relation and the \"Dearness of Cheap Labour.\" A subsidiary law repeatedly surfaces: a low level of wages itself produces the \"dearness of labour\" (Anderson), because low wages are bound up with low intensity, low productivity, and consequently high cost per unit of output. The Oldenburg and Russian cases demonstrate this concretely: longer hours and 50-percent-lower wages coexist with a greater number of hands per machine and a higher relative cost of labour to the capitalist. This reciprocal mechanism is a precursor of later analyses of the self-reinforcing character of underdevelopment. The Ideological Lesson. The critique of Carey is not ornamental — it is the payoff. Carey's proportionality thesis supplies the ideological justification for free-trade optimism: if wages rise proportionally with productivity, then the international market harmonizes interests and the worker's poverty in a less-developed nation is merely a productivity problem to be solved automatically by development. Marx's demolition shows this to be both logically void (surplus-value analysis refutes it a priori) and empirically false (the inspectors' data refute it a posteriori). Equally significant is the exposure of the move by which harmony theorists explain away contrary evidence: they invoke state intervention as an external distortion of an otherwise harmonious system. Marx's counter — that state expenses and protection are themselves \"natural fruits\" of capitalist development — refuses the externalization and relocates the contradiction inside the system. Reality does not deviate from a natural harmony; the \"harmony\" was a fiction all along. Broader Implications. Practically, the chapter warns against reading international wage statistics naively — a caution that remains potent in modern debates about global labour standards, offshoring, and \"cheap labour\" competition. Theoretically, it lays groundwork for later theories of unequal exchange and uneven and combined development: the international scale of value means that exchanges between nations of unequal productivity and intensity can systematically transfer value, and the \"cheapness\" of labour in the periphery is a relational, not an absolute, fact. Philosophically, the chapter enacts Marx's method at its most characteristic: it takes an apparently simple empirical question (why do wages differ between countries?) and reveals beneath it a structure of concealed value relations, contested categories, and class-serving ideologies. The lesson is that economic appearances are not merely imperfect reflections of reality to be corrected by better statistics — they are systematically produced forms that invert the underlying relations. To understand the wages of nations, one must first understand the value of labour-power, and one must refuse to mistake the visible measure for the hidden magnitude. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 21 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 23"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 23", "title": "Chapter 23", "subtitle": "Simple Reproduction", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 23/Chapter 23  Simple Reproduction_explanation.html", "word_count": 12417, "read_time": "62 min read", "tags": ["Capital Accumulation & Crises", "Social Reproduction Schemes (Dept I & II)"], "keywords": ["Simple Reproduction", "Continuity of the Production Process", "Reproduction of Variable Capital as Worker's Own Product", "Reproduction of the Wage-Labour Relation Itself", "Individual Consumption of Worker as Production of Capital"], "text": "Chapter 23 Simple Reproduction - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 12,351 words ⏱️ ~62 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS: Chapter 23, \"Simple Reproduction,\" is the opening substantive chapter of Part Seven of Capital , Volume 1 — \"The Process of Accumulation of Capital\" — and it performs an intellectual operation of extraordinary economy and violence. Its central thesis is deceptively simple in its statement and radical in its consequences: if you look at capitalist production not as an isolated act but as a continuous, self-renewing process — a process of reproduction — then several of the most cherished \"apparent characteristics\" of the isolated act dissolve, and the relation between capitalist and worker is revealed as one of systematic, self-perpetuating dependence rather than contractual exchange. The chapter's problem is the problem of the whole : how does the individual transaction between a buyer of labour-power and a seller of labour-power look once it is embedded in an endlessly repeated social process that reproduces its own preconditions? Marx's objective is not yet to analyse accumulation proper — that is, the expansion of capital through the reinvestment of surplus-value — but to establish its logical and conceptual foundation. Simple reproduction is the limiting case: a capitalist who consumes the entire surplus-value and keeps the capital intact. Yet even in this limiting case, Marx argues, decisive transformations occur. The worker's wages turn out to be a share of the product he himself has already produced; variable capital turns out to be only a historical form of appearance of the universal \"labour-fund\" that workers must in any society produce for themselves; the capitalist's \"original\" capital, after a finite number of years of consuming his surplus-value, turns out to be nothing but capitalised surplus-value — unpaid labour appropriated without equivalent; and the process itself turns out to produce and reproduce the capital-relation itself , on the one hand the capitalist, on the other the wage-labourer. Intellectual objective. The chapter seeks to defeat a way of seeing. The way of seeing it defeats is atomistic and juridical: the notion that capitalist wealth rests on the capitalist's own labour or frugality, that wages are an advance drawn from the capitalist's own funds, that the worker's independence is real because he freely contracts with, and can freely leave, an individual employer. Marx's method is to show that the continuity of the process, and the move from the standpoint of \"a single capitalist and a single worker\" to \"the capitalist class and the working class,\" causes these appearances to evaporate. He is building, piece by piece, the conceptual apparatus — labour-fund, variable capital, capitalised surplus-value, reproduction of the capital-relation — that will be required for the theory of accumulation in the chapters that follow. Place in the broader framework. The chapter opens Part Seven, whose task is accumulation — the subject that gives Volume 1 its ultimate point. It is the first of a small cluster of chapters (23, \"Simple Reproduction\"; 24, \"The Conversion of Surplus-Value into Capital\"; 25, \"The General Law of Capitalist Accumulation\"; 26–33 on primitive accumulation) that together constitute Marx's account of how the capital-relation expands, deepens, and universalises itself. Chapter 23 is the hinge on which the earlier analysis of production and the later analysis of the historical genesis of capitalism turn: it is where the \"starting point\" of the analysis (the separation of the worker from the means of production) is shown to be the \"result\" that the process continually re-establishes. It also serves as a bridge to Volume 2 (the sphere of circulation, dealt with only abstractly here) and Volume 3 (the fragmentation of surplus-value into profit, interest, rent, etc.). The chapter's intellectual ancestry is visible in its footnotes: Sismondi, Ramsay, James Mill, Adam Smith, Ricardo, Malthus, Cazenove, Richard Jones, Hodgskin, Liebig. Marx is intervening in classical political economy's own debates about whether wages are \"advanced\" out of capital or are a share of the product, and about what \"productive consumption\" means — and he is showing that the classical economists either glimpsed the truth and lost it (James Mill's \"remarkably peculiar property\"), or fetishised the money-form and missed it (the bourgeois economist who cannot separate \"the form of appearance from the thing which appears within that form\"). The chapter is thus simultaneously an exposition of a positive theory and a critique of a rival one. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS: The Prelude: The Circuit of Capital and the Two Abstractions Before the chapter's named subject begins, Marx sets the scene with three pages (vv. 709–710 in the standard pagination) that establish both the object and the method. He begins by restating the circuit of capital that was worked out earlier in the volume. The transformation of a sum of money into means of production and labour-power is \"the first phase\" of the movement of the value that is going to function as capital — and it takes place in the market, \"within the sphere of circulation.\" The second phase — the process of production — is complete as soon as the means of production have been converted into commodities whose value exceeds that of their component parts, and therefore contains the originally advanced capital plus a surplus-value . Those commodities must then be thrown back into circulation: they must be sold, their value realised in money, and that money transformed once more into capital, \"and so on, again and again.\" This cycle, in which the same phases are continually gone through in succession, is the circulation of capital . Two logical points are extracted from this circuit. First, the \"first condition of accumulation\" is that the capitalist must have contrived to sell his commodities and to reconvert into capital the greater part of the money received from their sale. Marx announces that in the following pages he will assume that capital passes through its circulation in the normal way — and he defers the detailed analysis of circulation to Volume 2. Second, he notes a division within surplus-value: the capitalist who directly extracts unpaid labour and fixes it in commodities is admittedly the first appropriator of surplus-value, \"but he is by no means its ultimate proprietor.\" He must share it with capitalists performing other functions in social production taken as a whole, with the owner of the land, and with others still. Surplus-value is thus split into fragments — profit, interest, gains of trade, ground rent — which take on \"various mutually independent forms.\" The analysis of these modified forms is deferred to Volume 3. On the basis of these two deferrals, Marx makes his method explicit. On the one hand, he assumes the capitalist sells his commodities at their value and does not concern himself with their later return to the market, the new forms capital assumes in circulation, or \"the concrete conditions of reproduction hidden within those forms.\" On the other hand, he treats the capitalist producer as the owner of the entire surplus-value , or, better, as \"the representative of all those who will share the booty with him.\" Therefore, he writes, \"we shall begin by considering accumulation from an abstract point of view, i.e. simply as one aspect of the immediate process of production.\" The justification offered is crucial and merits close attention, because it is a methodological claim about why abstraction is not falsification . In so far as accumulation actually takes place, the capitalist must have succeeded in selling and reconverting; moreover, the break-up of surplus-value into fragments \"does not affect either its nature or the conditions under which it becomes an element in accumulation.\" Whatever the proportion of surplus-value the capitalist retains or yields up, \"he is the one who in the first instance appropriates it.\" Marx's claim, then, is that in assuming the whole surplus-value for the capitalist he \"assume[s] no more than is assumed by the actual process of accumulation itself.\" But there is a cost to this abstraction, and Marx names it: \"the simple, fundamental form of the process of accumulation is obscured both by the splitting-up of surplus-value and by the mediating movement of circulation.\" Hence — and this is the epistemologically decisive sentence — \"An exact analysis of the process, therefore, demands that we should, for a time, disregard all phenomena that conceal the workings of its inner mechanism.\" Place this alongside the chapter's own later statement that simple reproduction makes \"the disappearance of some apparent characteristics possessed by the process in isolation,\" and you have the methodological signature of the whole chapter: abstraction is a technique for dissolving concealment. Marx is not simplifying for convenience; he is stripping away the layers (money-form, commodity-form, split surplus-value, the contingency of individual transactions) that hide the underlying social relation. Note too the word \"for a time\" — the abstractions are provisional, to be lifted in Volumes 2 and 3. This is a constructive, not a reductive, abstraction. Reproduction in General: The Universal Form of All Social Production The chapter proper opens with a general proposition of almost banal obviousness that Marx proceeds to load with consequence: Whatever the social form of the production process, it has to be continuous, it must periodically repeat the same phases. A society can no more cease to produce than it can cease to consume. When viewed, therefore, as a connected whole, and in the constant flux of its incessant renewal, every social process of production is at the same time a process of reproduction. The argument is a simple one. Production is not a one-off event; it must be repeated. But the repetition has material requirements. The conditions of production are at the same time the conditions of reproduction. No society can go on producing unless it constantly reconverts a part of its products into means of production, or elements of fresh products. All other things being equal, society can reproduce or maintain its wealth on the existing scale only by replacing the used-up means of production — instruments of labour, raw material, auxiliary substances — \"with an equal quantity of new articles.\" These must be separated from the mass of the yearly product and incorporated once again into the production process. Hence, Marx concludes, \"a definite portion of each year's product belongs to the sphere of production. Destined for productive consumption from the very first, this portion exists, for the most part, in forms which by their very nature exclude the possibility of individual consumption.\" This passage performs several tasks at once. It establishes reproduction as a material necessity independent of social form — the point being to forestall any suggestion that capitalist reproduction is natural and non-capitalist reproduction artificial. It establishes the division of the social product into that destined for productive consumption and that available for individual consumption — a distinction that will be essential to the whole argument. And it prepares the definition of productive consumption by which the worker's (and the capitalist's) expenditure is later classified. The phrase \"this portion exists, for the most part, in forms which by their very nature exclude the possibility of individual consumption\" is a reminder that the physical form of capital goods (machines, raw materials) is not merely incidental; it prises open the analytical separation between productive and individual consumption that the chapter's second half exploits. The Capitalist Form of Reproduction and the Emergence of Revenue Having established reproduction as a universal necessity, Marx draws the decisive consequence: \"If production has a capitalist form, so too will reproduction.\" And just as, in the capitalist mode of production, the labour process appears only as a means towards the process of valorisation, so in reproduction the labour process appears \"only as a means of reproducing the value advanced as capital, i.e. as self-valorizing value.\" This is the crucial functional identification: in the capitalist mode of production, production is not for the sake of the product but for the sake of the self-expansion of value; correspondingly, reproduction is not for the sake of maintaining the stock of use-values but for the sake of preserving and re-expanding value. From this follows the fixing of the capitalist character on a person. \"The economic character of capitalist becomes firmly fixed to a man only if his money constantly functions as capital.\" The example: £100 converted into capital this year, producing a surplus-value of £20, must continue next year and in subsequent years to repeat the same operation. In this repetition, surplus-value \"acquires the form of a revenue arising out of capital\" — \"a periodic increment of the value of the capital, or a periodic fruit borne by capital-in-process.\" Marx cites Sismondi's Nouveaux Principes in support: the rich, who consume the products of the labour of others, seem exposed to exhaustion of their reserve funds, \"but, in the social order, wealth has acquired the power of reproducing itself through the labour of others… Wealth, like labour, and by means of labour, bears fruit every year, but this fruit can be destroyed every year without making the rich man any poorer thereby. This fruit is the revenue which arises out of capital.\" The citation marks Marx's engagement with the political economy of his time: the concept of revenue (as distinct from capital ) is being borrowed from the sources, but given a new, critical, systematic place. Simple Reproduction Defined — and Why Its \"Mere Repetition\" Matters Marx now defines his object: \"If this revenue serves the capitalist only as a fund to provide for his consumption, and if it is consumed as periodically as it is gained, then, other things being equal, simple reproduction takes place.\" The definition is deliberately exact. Simple reproduction is not the absence of surplus-value, but its consumption rather than its capitalisation . It is the case of a stationary capital. And then the chapter's central engine is announced: \"although this reproduction is a mere repetition of the process of production, on the same scale as before, this mere repetition, or continuity, imposes on the process certain new characteristics, or rather, causes the disappearance of some apparent characteristics possessed by the process in isolation.\" The two formulations — \"imposes new characteristics\" and \"causes the disappearance of apparent characteristics\" — are not alternative phrasings of the same thing. They mark two distinct kinds of result, and the chapter will deliver on both. New characteristics : for instance, that capital becomes capitalised surplus-value. Disappearance of apparent characteristics : for instance, that wages appear as an advance out of the capitalist's own funds. First Transformation: Wages as a Share in the Product the Worker Himself Reproduces The first transformation concerns the wage. Marx begins from the material facts of the labour contract. \"The purchase of labour-power for a fixed period is the prelude to the production process; and this prelude is constantly repeated when the period of time for which the labour-power has been sold comes to an end, when a definite period of production, such as a week or a month, has elapsed.\" But — and this is the pivotal observation — \"the worker is not paid until after he has expended his labour-power, and realized both the value of his labour-power and a certain quantity of surplus-value in the shape of commodities.\" So the worker has produced, before he is paid, not only the surplus-value (here regarded as a fund to meet the capitalist's private consumption), but also the variable capital — the very fund out of which he himself is paid. \"His employment lasts only as long as he continues to reproduce this fund.\" This is the material basis for a formula Marx had already encountered in Chapter 18: the economists' presentation of wages as \"a share in the product itself.\" He cites Ramsay (\"Wages as well as profits are to be considered, each of them, as really a portion of the finished product,\" op. cit. , p. 142) and James Mill (\"The share of the product which comes to the worker in the form of wages\"). Marx's gloss: \"What flows back to the worker in the shape of wages is a portion of the product he himself continuously reproduces. The capitalist, it is true, pays him the value of the commodity in money, but this money is merely the transmuted form of the product of his labour.\" While the capitalist is converting a portion of the means of production into products, a portion of his former product is being turned into money. \"It is his labour of last week, or of last year, that pays for his labour-power this week or this year.\" The argument, in its exact logic, is this: the appearance in the isolated act is that the capitalist advances wages out of his own funds, making possible the worker's subsistence and labour. The reality, once the act is repeated, is that the wage is drawn from a fund constituted by the worker's own prior production. The capitalist has no original fund of subsistence advanced independently; he is perpetually a period behind, paying the worker with the converted product of the worker's own labour. To dissolve the money-appearance, Marx shifts standpoint — the same shift he will make several times in the chapter. \"The illusion created by the money-form vanishes immediately if, instead of taking a single capitalist and a single worker, we take the whole capitalist class and the whole working class.\" The picture that emerges: \"The capitalist class is constantly giving to the working class drafts, in the form of money, on a portion of the product produced by the latter and appropriated by the former. The workers give these drafts back just as constantly to the capitalists, and thereby withdraw from the latter their allotted share of their own product. The transaction is veiled by the commodity-form of the product and the money-form of the commodity.\" Note the metaphor: the wage is a draft — an order of payment — that the capitalist class draws on the product, which the working class then redeems. The \"advance\" is a bill on labour's own output, not a transfer from capital's store. Variable Capital as a Historical Form of the Labour-Fund From this first transformation Marx extracts a definitional conclusion: \"Variable capital is therefore only a particular historical form of appearance of the fund for providing the means of subsistence, or the labour-fund, which the worker requires for his own maintenance and reproduction, and which, in all systems of social production, he must himself produce and reproduce.\" This is a strong claim, and the reader must register what it asserts. The labour-fund is the store of means of subsistence required by any producer in any society; it is not specific to capitalism. What is specific to capitalism is the form in which this fund reaches the worker: not as his own direct product, but as money paid for his labour-power. \"If the labour-fund constantly flows to him in the form of money that pays for his labour, it is because his own product constantly moves away from him in the form of capital.\" And: \"this form of appearance of the labour-fund makes no difference to the fact that it is the worker's own objectified labour which is advanced to him by the capitalist.\" Marx cites Cazenove's note to his edition of Malthus: \"When capital is employed in advancing to the workman his wages, it adds nothing to the funds for the maintenance of labour.\" The point of the citation is that even a bourgeois economist could see that the \"advance\" is not a net addition to the subsistence fund. The Corvée Peasant: Form of Appearance Versus the Thing That Appears To nail down the distinction between form of appearance and substance, Marx now constructs a thought-experiment — one of the most instructive in the chapter. \"Let us take a peasant liable to do compulsory labour services.\" He works on his own land with his own means of production for, say, three days a week. The other three days are devoted to forced labour on the lord's domain. He \"constantly reproduces his own labour-fund, which never, in his case, takes the form of a money payment for his labour, advanced by another person.\" But — and this is the symmetry Marx insists on — \"in return his unpaid and forced labour for the lord never acquires the character of voluntary and paid labour.\" Now the thought-experiment's second step. \"If one fine morning, the landowner appropriates to himself the plot of land, the cattle, the seed, in short, the means of production of the peasant,\" then the peasant must \"thenceforth… sell his labour-power to the former.\" Marx then itemises the continuities : \"He will, other things being equal, labour six days a week as before, three for himself, three for his former lord, who thenceforth becomes a wage-paying capitalist. As before, he will use up the means of production as means of production, and transfer their value to the product. As before, a definite portion of the product will be devoted to reproduction.\" The only change is a change of form: \"from the moment that forced labour is changed into wage-labour, the labour-fund, which the peasant himself continues as before to produce and reproduce, takes the form of a quantity of capital advanced in the form of wages by the lord of the land.\" The conclusion is a reductio of the bourgeois economist: \"The bourgeois economist, whose limited mentality is unable to separate the form of appearance from the thing which appears within that form, shuts his eyes to the fact that even at the present time the labour-fund only crops up exceptionally on the face of the globe in the form of capital.\" The footnote appends Richard Jones's Textbook of Lectures : \"The wages of labour are advanced by capitalists in the case of less than one-fourth of the labourers of the earth.\" Marx reinforces through the reference to Jones the empirical claim in the reductio : the wage-form is a minority form globally, and it should not therefore be mistaken for the universal \"labour-fund\" itself. The thought-experiment thus does double duty: it isolates form from substance conceptually, and it hints at the historical contingency of the form. The Primitive Accumulation Parenthesis and the Dissolution of the \"Original\" Capital Marx then turns to the historical dimension, in a brief but vital parenthesis. He concedes: \"Variable capital, it is true, loses its character of a value advanced out of the capitalist's funds only when we view the process of capitalist production in the flow of its constant renewal. But that process must have had a beginning of some kind.\" He then records what \"from our present standpoint\" it therefore seems likely: \"that the capitalist, once upon a time, became possessed of money by some form of primitive accumulation [ ursprüngliche Akkumulation ] that took place independently of the unpaid labour of other people, and that this was therefore how he was able to frequent the market as a buyer of labour-power.\" (The footnote — actually a translator's note — records the preference for \"primitive accumulation\" over \"original accumulation.\") Note the hedge: \"seems likely.\" Marx does not argue the historical case here; he defers it to the later chapters of Part Seven (which will demolish the notion that capital was ever acquired independently of others' labour). His immediate task is different: \"However this may be, the mere continuity of the process of capitalist production, or simple reproduction, brings about other remarkable transformations which seize hold of not only the variable, but the total capital.\" The hedge is thus a setup : the historical question of the origin of capitalism is bracketed, and Marx proceeds to show that, whatever the origin , the working of the process ensures that the capital now functioning is not what it was. He then deploys a numerical argument. \"If a surplus-value of £200 is generated every year by the use of a capital of £1,000, and if this surplus-value is consumed every year, it is clear that when this process has been repeated for five years, the surplus-value consumed will amount to 5 × £200, or the £1,000 originally advanced. If only a part were consumed, say one-half, the same result would follow at the end of ten years, since 10 × £100 = £1,000.\" The general formulation is then stated: \"the value of the capital advanced divided by the surplus-value annually consumed gives the number of years, or periods of reproduction, at the expiration of which the capital originally advanced has been consumed by the capitalist and has disappeared.\" In symbols, if C is the value of the capital originally advanced and s the surplus-value annually consumed, the number of years is n = C / s . With C = £1,000 and s = £200, n = 5. With s = £100, n = 10. Marx then anticipates the capitalist's counter-consciousness. \"The capitalist thinks that he is consuming the produce of the unpaid labour of others, i.e. the surplus-value, and is keeping intact the value of his original capital; but what he thinks cannot alter the actual situation. After the lapse of a certain number of years, the value of the capital he possesses is equal to the sum total of the surplus-value he has appropriated during those years, and the total value he has consumed is equal to the value of his original capital.\" He then pre-empts the obvious empirical objection: \"It is true that he has in hand a quantity of capital whose magnitude has not changed, and that part of it, such as buildings, machinery, etc., was already there when he began to conduct his business operations. But we are not concerned here with the material components of the capital. We are concerned with its value.\" This is the crux of the value -theoretic nature of the argument. The physical stock persists, but the value that stock represents is, after n years, no longer the value originally advanced. Marx's analogy: \"When a person consumes the whole of his property, by taking upon himself debts equal to the value of that property, it is clear that his property represents nothing but the sum total of his debts. And so it is with the capitalist; when he has consumed the equivalent of his original capital, the value of his present capital represents nothing but the total amount of surplus-value appropriated by him without payment. Not a single atom of the value of his old capital continues to exist.\" This is an important analytical move, and it is worth pausing on the logic. The argument does not say that the thing (building, machine) has been consumed; it says that the value of the thing has been offset by consumption of surplus-value, so that the remaining stock of value must be attributed , in value-accounting terms, to surplus-value rather than to the original advance. The debt analogy makes the point vivid: if I consume my estate while acquiring debts equal to its value, the estate in my hands \"represents nothing but the sum total of my debts\" — the estate is still physically present, but it is no longer mine in the relevant sense; in value terms it belongs to my creditors. By analogy, the capitalist's surviving stock of capital, after he has consumed an amount of surplus-value equal to his original advance, is no longer the \"value of the old capital\" — it is unpaid labour appropriated without equivalent. The conclusion is then generalised, with two phrases that must not be overlooked: sooner or later and necessarily . \"Therefore, entirely leaving aside all accumulation, the mere continuity of the production process, in other words simple reproduction, sooner or later, and necessarily, converts all capital into accumulated capital, or capitalized surplus-value.\" The \"sooner or later\" and \"necessarily\" register the argument's status: it is a claim about what happens with certainty given enough repetitions, whatever the initial historical facts. Marx then extends the conclusion to the case where capital was originally the fruit of the owner's own labour — a case that classical political economy (Locke, Smith, the \"abstinence\" theorists) relied upon to justify property. \"Even if that capital was, on its entry into the process of production, the personal property of the man who employs it, and was originally acquired by his own labour, it sooner or later becomes value appropriated without an equivalent, the unpaid labour of others materialized either in the money-form or in some other way.\" This is a targeted polemic: the labour-theory of property, which was used by the classical economists and by Locke to justify private property as the fruit of the owner's own labour, cannot survive the temporal argument; the same labour theory now turns property into its opposite — appropriated unpaid labour. Recall from Chapter 4: for the transformation of money into capital, \"something more was required than the production and circulation of commodities.\" Two poles must confront each other: \"the possessor of value or money,\" on the one hand, and \"the possessor of the value-creating substance\" — the possessor of means of production and subsistence, and the possessor of nothing but labour-power — on the other. \"A division between the product of labour and labour itself, between the objective conditions of labour and subjective labour-power, was therefore the real foundation and the starting-point of the process of capitalist production.\" From Starting-Point to Result: Reproduction of the Capital-Relation The chapter's pivot comes in the next paragraph: \"But what at first was merely a starting-point becomes, by means of nothing but the continuity of the process, by simple reproduction, the characteristic result of capitalist production, a result which is constantly renewed and perpetuated.\" This is the theoretical heart of the chapter. Marx unpacks the result in a passage of concentrated power. \"On the one hand, the production process incessantly converts material wealth into capital, into the capitalist's means of enjoyment and his means of valorization. On the other hand, the worker always leaves the process in the same state as he entered it — a personal source of wealth, but deprived of any means of making that wealth a reality for himself.\" This is the reproduction of the poles of the relation . Note the asymmetry: the capitalist is reproduced with more (means of enjoyment plus means of valorisation); the worker is reproduced with less — with nothing but himself as labour-power. He then traces the process in its subjective dimension, using two German terms whose untranslatability matters: entfremdet (\"alienated\") and fremder Produkt (\"a product alien to him\"). \"Since, before he enters the process, his own labour has already been alienated [ entfremdet ] from him, appropriated by the capitalist, and incorporated with capital, it now, in the course of the process, constantly objectifies itself so that it becomes a product alien to him [ fremder Produkt ].\" The worker enters the process already dispossessed — his capacity to labour has been alienated by sale; and he leaves it with a product that objectifies his labour in a form foreign to him, because it belongs to another. Because the process is also \"the process of the consumption of labour-power by the capitalist,\" the worker's product is not only converted into commodities but into capital — into \"value that sucks up the worker's value-creating power, means of subsistence that actually purchase human beings, and means of production that employ the people who are doing the producing.\" The resulting image is the systematic reproduction of two mutually opposed social roles. \"Therefore the worker himself constantly produces objective wealth, in the form of capital, an alien power that dominates and exploits him; and the capitalist just as constantly produces labour-power, in the form of a subjective source of wealth which is abstract, exists merely in the physical body of the worker, and is separated from its own means of objectification and realization; in short, the capitalist produces the worker as a wage-labourer. This incessant reproduction, this perpetuation of the worker, is the absolutely necessary condition for capitalist production.\" This is one of the most famous sentences in Capital — \"the capitalist produces the worker as a wage-labourer\" — and its logic deserves emphasis. It is a thesis about reproduction , not about production . Any given act of production might be described as the capitalist buying labour and using it. But the continuity of acts produces something that no single act produces: two classes , with distinct functional positions, sustained over time. The worker does not merely sell his labour-power; the process reproduces him in the condition of being a seller of labour-power — which is why, in the chapter's later formulation, \"the worker belongs to capital before he has sold himself to the capitalist.\" A footnote attaches the observation of James Mill: \"This is a remarkably peculiar property of productive labour. Whatever is productively consumed is capital, and it becomes capital by consumption.\" Marx adds with irony: \"James Mill, however, was never able to track down this 'remarkably peculiar property'.\" Mill has sighted the phenomenon — productive consumption transforms the consumed thing into capital — but cannot explain it, because he lacks the labour theory of value and the theory of surplus-value. Marx's footnote is thus a compressed critical history of political economy's self-incomprehension. Two more footnotes in this stretch function as empirical adornments to the theoretical claim of the worker's perpetuation. The first (from Reasons for a Limited Exportation of Wool , London, 1677): \"It is true indeed, that the first introducing a manufacture emploies many poor, but they cease not to be so, and the continuance of it makes many.\" The second (from Reasons for the late Increase of the Poor Rates , London, 1777): \"The farmer now absurdly asserts, that he keeps the poor. They are indeed kept in misery.\" Both citations from the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries show that the phenomenon of the reproduction of poverty along with the reproduction of capital was visible to contemporaries before Marx's theory — visible, but not explained. The Worker's Two Kinds of Consumption Marx now changes register and develops a classification that is formally economic and polemically loaded. \"The worker's consumption is of two kinds. While producing he consumes the means of production with his labour, and converts them into products with a higher value than that of the capital advanced. This is his productive consumption. It is at the same time consumption of his labour-power by the capitalist who has bought it. On the other hand, the worker uses the money paid to him for his labour-power to buy the means of subsistence; this is his individual consumption.\" The two are \"totally distinct.\" The distinction is defined not by the objects consumed (in both cases the objects are consumed by the worker's activity) but by the function the consumption performs: \"In the former, he acts as the motive power of capital, and belongs to the capitalist. In the latter, he belongs to himself, and performs his necessary vital functions outside the production process.\" The result: \"of the first kind of consumption is that the capitalist continues to live, of the second, that the worker himself continues to live.\" A supplement acknowledges a complication already treated in the chapter on the working day: \"the worker is often compelled to make his individual consumption into a merely incidental part of the production process. In such a case, he provides himself with means of subsistence in order to keep his labour-power in motion, just as coal and water are supplied to the steam-engine, and oil to the wheel. His means of consumption are then merely the means of consumption of a means of production; his individual consumption is directly productive consumption.\" Marx qualifies this: \"This, however, appears to be an abuse, rather than an essential attribute of the capitalist process of production.\" The footnote (Rossi) defends the theoretical distinction: \"Rossi would not have declaimed so emphatically on this point if he had really penetrated the secret of 'productive consumption'.\" The Rossi citation: \"To conceive capacity for labour in abstraction from the workers' means of subsistence during the production process is to conceive a phantom.\" The distinction between the \"abuse\" and the \"essential attribute\" is a genuine caveat and a mark of Marx's care. He is not claiming that in every capitalist workplace the worker's consumption is literally part of the production process (as, say, ore and fuel are); he is claiming that, at the social level, the worker's individual consumption is nevertheless functionally an aspect of the reproduction of capital, whatever the worker's own aims and experience . The \"abuse\" versus \"essential attribute\" distinction anticipates the shift to the social standpoint that will establish the latter claim without relying on the former. The Social Standpoint: Reproduction of the Worker as Means of Production Marx now makes the move that defines the chapter's most disturbing claims: \"The matter takes quite another aspect if we contemplate not the single capitalist and the single worker, but the capitalist class and the working class, not an isolated process of production, but capitalist production in full swing, and on its actual social scale.\" At the class level, the worker's individual consumption becomes an aspect of the reproduction of capital . \"By converting part of his capital into labour-power, the capitalist valorizes the value of his entire capital. He kills two birds with one stone. He profits not only by what he receives from the worker, but also by what he gives him.\" The \"advance\" to the worker, which looked like a cost or an act of providence, turns out to be a means of valorisation: the wage buys means of subsistence which \"have to be consumed to reproduce the muscles, nerves, bones and brains of existing workers, and to bring new workers into existence.\" Therefore: \"Within the limits of what is absolutely necessary, therefore, the individual consumption of the working class is the reconversion of the means of subsistence given by capital in return for labour-power into fresh labour-power which capital is then again able to exploit. It is the production and reproduction of the capitalist's most indispensable means of production: the worker.\" Here we arrive at one of the most consequential formulations in the chapter: the reproduction of the worker is the reproduction of the means of production . The worker is classified, functionally, as one of the conditions of production — and not metaphorically but economically. The argument does not rest on the capitalist's intention (or the worker's). Marx will insist on this precisely: The individual consumption of the worker, whether it occurs inside or outside the workshop, inside or outside the labour process, remains an aspect of the production and reproduction of capital, just as the cleaning of machinery does, whether it is done during the labour process, or when intervals in that process permit. The fact that the worker performs acts of individual consumption in his own interest, and not to please the capitalist, is something entirely irrelevant to the matter. The consumption of food by a beast of burden does not become any less a necessary aspect of the production process because the beast enjoys what it eats. The beast-of-burden analogy is deliberate and brutal. It is designed to forestall a natural objection: surely, the worker who eats and sleeps in his own home, spending his wages on what he pleases, is outside capital, and is thereby reproducing himself as a free individual? Marx's answer: the subjective experience and intention of the worker are irrelevant to the functional question of what his consumption accomplishes in the total process. The maintenance and reproduction of the working class remains a necessary condition for the reproduction of capital. But there is a striking qualification, and it preserves the distinction between the theoretical claim and the capitalist's practical attitude. \"But the capitalist may safely leave this to the worker's drives for self-preservation and propagation. All the capitalist cares for is to reduce the worker's individual consumption to the necessary minimum, and an immense distance separates his attitude from the crudeness of the South American mine-owners, who force their workers to consume the more substantial, rather than the less substantial, kind of food.\" The footnote is from Liebig's Organic Chemistry in its Application to Agriculture and Physiology : \"The workers in the mines of South America, whose daily task (the heaviest perhaps in the world) consists in bringing to the surface on their shoulders a load of metal weighing from 180 to 200 pounds, from a depth of 450 feet, live on bread and beans only; they themselves would prefer the bread alone for food, but their masters, who have found out that the men cannot work so hard on bread, treat them like horses, and compel them to eat beans; beans are relatively much richer in bone-ash than is bread.\" The detail is remarkable: the mine-owners are \"crude\" not because they force greater consumption but because they force a chemically disciplined consumption, precisely calibrated by the demands of muscle work. Liebig's physiological data are the empirical substrate for the claim that capitalist \"necessary minimum\" consumption is a functional minimum determined by the reproduction requirements of the wage-worker as a means of production. The Ideological Mirror: Political Economy on Productive and Unproductive Consumption Marx now turns to the reflection of this structure in bourgeois political economy. \"Hence both the capitalist and his ideologist, the political economist, consider only that part of the worker's individual consumption to be productive which is required for the perpetuation of the working class, and which therefore must take place in order that the capitalist may have labour-power to consume. What the worker consumes over and above that minimum for his own pleasure is seen as unproductive consumption.\" The footnote is James Mill, op. cit. , pp. 238ff. Marx then adds a second corroborating citation. \"If the accumulation of capital were to cause a rise of wages and an increase in the worker's consumption unaccompanied by an increase in the consumption of labour-power by capital, the additional capital would be consumed unproductively.\" The footnote is Ricardo: \"If the price of labour should rise so high that, notwithstanding the increase of capital, no more could be employed, I should say that such increase of capital would be still unproductively consumed\" ( op. cit. , p. 163). The move here is analytically subtle. Marx is not endorsing Ricardo's or Mill's categories; he is showing that these categories are the ideological expression of the functional relation he has just described. The bourgeois economists' \"productive consumption\" is exactly what the capitalist process itself recognises as productive: consumption that reconstitutes labour-power, the means of production par excellence . Consumption that exceeds that minimum — consumption for the worker's pleasure — is \"unproductive\" from the standpoint of capital, because it does not valorise capital. The classification of consumption by its function-for-capital, not by its function-for-human-beings, is thus revealed as the ideology of the situation. Marx then adds his own re-description, which is both analytically sharp and rhetorically devastating: \"In reality, the individual consumption of the worker is unproductive even from his own point of view, for it simply reproduces the needy individual; it is productive to the capitalist and to the state, since it is the production of a force which produces wealth for other people.\" The footnote is Malthus's Definitions in Political Economy : \"The only productive consumption, properly so called, is the consumption or destruction of wealth\" (he means the using up of the means of production) \"by capitalists with a view to reproduction… The workman… is a productive consumer to the person who employs him, and to the state, but not, strictly speaking, to himself.\" Marx's re-description exploits the standpoint-relativity of the productive/unproductive distinction. From the worker's own standpoint, his individual consumption is not \"productive\" — he does not emerge from it with a product, only with the recovered capacity to begin again. From the standpoint of the capitalist and the state, it is productive — it produces the labour-power on which their wealth depends. The footnote from Malthus both corroborates and, in a sense, concedes the point: the workman is \"a productive consumer to the person who employs him, and to the state, but not, strictly speaking, to himself.\" Marx's critical move is to reverse the usual normative evaluation embedded in the terms: what is \"productive\" from the standpoint of capital is precisely what leaves the worker \"needy\"; the criterion itself is a criterion of the exploitation of one class for another. The Invisible Threads Marx now generalises. \"From the standpoint of society, then, the working class, even when it stands outside the direct labour process, is just as much an appendage of capital as the lifeless instruments of labour are. Even its individual consumption is, within certain limits, a mere aspect of the process of capital's reproduction.\" And: \"That process, however, takes good care to prevent the workers, those instruments of production who are possessed of consciousness, from running away, by constantly removing their product from one pole to the other, to the opposite pole of capital.\" The phrase \"instruments of production who are possessed of consciousness\" is a compressed restatement of the chapter's central paradox: the worker is at once a thing (means of production) and a person (conscious being), and the whole system of wage-labour is, in effect, a device for holding in place a thing that is aware of its situation. Marx then offers a two-sided account of individual consumption. \"Individual consumption provides, on the one hand, the means for the workers' maintenance and reproduction; on the other hand, by the constant annihilation of the means of subsistence, it provides for their continued re-appearance on the labour-market.\" The second aspect is often overlooked, and it is essential to the argument: the worker's consumption depletes the means of subsistence and therefore produces the need to return to work. If the worker could hoard his wage indefinitely, the coercion of wage-labour would be looser; the fact that consumption is continuous, and requires renewal, ensures the worker's continuous re-appearance on the market. The system works not despite the worker's consumption but through it. The image that follows is one of the most quoted in Marx: \"The Roman slave was held by chains; the wage-labourer is bound to his owner by invisible threads. The appearance of independence is maintained by a constant change in the person of the individual employer, and by the legal fiction of a contract.\" Two mechanisms, then, sustain the appearance of freedom even as the substance is dependence: (a) the worker changes employers, so his tie to this capitalist seems contingent and personal rather than structural and necessary; (b) the contract form — a mutual, juridical, voluntary agreement — presents the relation as one of exchange between equals, however unequal the substantive conditions. The phrase \"legal fiction\" is not overheated; it names the technical fact that the legal form (contract) does not record the social content (dependence). The bond is invisible because it is social , not personal — and universal , not this transaction. The historical footnote drives the point home: \"In former times, capital resorted to legislation, whenever it seemed necessary, in order to enforce its proprietary rights over the free worker. For instance, down to 1815 the emigration of mechanics employed in machine-making was forbidden in England, on pain of severe punishment.\" So the \"invisible threads\" are sometimes supplemented by visible, statutory chains; the laws against the emigration of skilled mechanics are historical instances of the same proprietary claim being asserted openly, when informal means were insufficient. The theme of \"proprietary rights over the free worker\" also expands a definitional point about variable capital as the real existence of capital's condition of production . \"The reproduction of the working class implies at the same time the transmission and accumulation of skills from one generation to another.\" The footnote is Hodgskin's Labour Defended : \"The only thing, of which one can say, that it is stored up and prepared beforehand, is the skill of the labourer… The accumulation and storage of skilled labour, that most important operation, is, as regards the great mass of labourers, accomplished without any capital whatever.\" Marx's gloss: \"The capitalist regards the existence of such a skilled working class as one of the conditions of production which belong to him, and in fact views it as the real existence of his variable capital.\" What Hodgskin emphasises as a theoretical puzzle — skill is accumulated \"without any capital whatever,\" yet capital functions as if skill were its own store — Marx turns into a critique : capital treats the socially produced skill of the working class, built up across generations at no cost to capital, as its private asset. The Cotton Famine: Empirical Demonstration Marx at once supplies an empirical demonstration of this proprietary attitude at work. \"This becomes very clear as soon as a crisis threatens him with its loss.\" And so begins the longest empirical set-piece in the chapter: the American Civil War cotton famine in Lancashire. \"As a result of the American Civil War and the accompanying cotton famine, the majority of the cotton workers of Lancashire were, as is well known, thrown out of work. Both from the working class itself, and from other social strata, there arose a cry for state aid, or voluntary national subscriptions, in order to make possible the emigration of those who were 'redundant' to the English colonies or to the United States.\" Marx then quotes at length from a letter to The Times of 24 March 1863 by Edmund Potter, a former President of the Manchester Chamber of Commerce — a letter \"rightly described in the House of Commons as 'the manifesto of the manufacturers'\" (the footnote cites Ferrand's motion on the Cotton Famine, House of Commons, 27 April 1863). Potter's letter is quoted to expose the legal-fiction of independence. Potter writes (as quoted): the man out of work \"may be told the supply of cotton-workers is too large… and… must… in fact be reduced by a third, perhaps, and that then there will be a healthy demand for the remaining two-thirds… Public opinion… urges emigration… The master cannot willingly see his labour supply being removed; he may think, and perhaps justly, that it is both wrong and unsound… But if the public funds are to be devoted to assist emigration, he has a right to be heard, and perhaps to protest.\" Marx's gloss immediately after the quotation: Potter \"then proceeds to point out how useful the cotton industry is, how 'it has undoubtedly drawn the surplus population from Ireland and from the agricultural districts', how immense is its extent, how it yielded 7/13ths of total English exports in the year 1860, how, after a few years, it will again expand by the extension of the market, particularly of the Indian market, and by calling forth a plentiful supply of cotton at 6d. per lb.\" The centrepiece of the citation is the passage in which Potter's two machineries argument emerges — a passage Marx will pick apart. Potter: \"[It is not to be denied that] time — one, two, or three years it may be — will produce the quantity… The question I would put then is this — Is the trade worth retaining? Is it worth while to keep the machinery\" (Marx interpolates: \"he means the living labour-machines\") \"in order, and is it not the greatest folly to think of parting with that? I think it is. I allow that the workers are not a property, not the property of Lancashire and the masters; but they are the strength of both; they are the mental and trained power which cannot be replaced for a generation; the mere machinery which they work might much of it be beneficially replaced, nay improved, in a twelvemonth. Encourage or allow(!) the working-power to emigrate, and what of the capitalist?\" Marx appends an ironic literary allusion: \"This cry from the heart reminds one of Lord Chamberlain Kalb.\" The footnote explains: Kalb is a character in Schiller's Kabale und Liebe — a Lord Chamberlain of a petty German princely court, who, upon hearing of another official's resignation, exclaims: \"But what of me? You can at least talk well… you are a man of learning. But what would become of me if his highness dismissed me?\" The allusion is to self-serving pathos: Potter's \"what of the capitalist?\" is a decapitation of the very idea we are discussing — the capitalist is the one whose fate hangs on the continued existence of others' labour-power. Potter's letter continues (as quoted by Marx): \"Take away the cream of the workers, and fixed capital will depreciate in a great degree, and the floating will not subject itself to a struggle with the short supply of inferior labour… We are told the workers wish it\" (emigration). \"Very natural it is that they should do so… Reduce, compress the cotton trade by taking away its working power and reducing their wages expenditure, say one-fifth, or five millions, and what then would happen to the class above, the small shopkeepers; and what of the rents, the cottage rents… Trace out the effects upward to the small farmer, the better householder, and… the landowner, and say if there could be any suggestion more suicidal to all classes of the country than enfeebling a nation by exporting the best of its manufacturing population, and destroying the value of some of its most productive capital and enrichment… I advise a loan (of five or six millions sterling)… extending it may be over two or three years, administered by special commissioners added to the Boards of Guardians in the cotton districts, under special legislative regulations, enforcing some occupation or labour, as a means of keeping up at least the moral standard of the recipients of the loan… can anything be worse for landowners or masters than parting with the best of the workers, and demoralizing and disappointing the rest by an extended depletive emigration, a depletion of capital and value in an entire province?\" Several features of this letter deserve analytical marking. First, Potter openly admits \"the workers are not a property, not the property of Lancashire and the masters\" — yet insists they are \"the strength of both.\" Marx's point: the same functional role (labour as a condition of capital) survives the disappearance of the legal form (slavery, serfdom, statute labour). Second, Potter's argument is explicitly politically economic : he traces the \"depletion\" not merely of capital but of rents , of the small shopkeepers, of the landowner's estate, of the whole social fabric. The argument for keeping workers is an argument for holding the social conditions of accumulation. Third — and this is what Marx mainly hammers — Potter's claim that the trained worker cannot be replaced \"for a generation\" directly contradicts the claim made by factory owners and their apologists whenever the question is wages . Marx's footnote 15 spells out the contradiction: \"It will not be forgotten that this same capital sings quite another tune under ordinary circumstances, when it is a question of reducing wages. Then the 'masters' exclaim with one voice…: 'The factory operatives should keep in wholesome remembrance the fact that theirs is really a low species of skilled labour; and that there is none which is more easily acquired, or of its quality more amply remunerated, or which, by a short training of the least expert, can be more quickly, as well as abundantly, acquired… The master's machinery' (which we now learn can advantageously be replaced and improved within twelve months) 'really plays a far more important part in the business of production than the labour and skill of the operative' (who cannot now be replaced in less than thirty years), 'which six months' education can teach, and a common labourer can learn.'\" The point is a methodological one about ideology : the capitalists' description of the worker's skill is not a description at all; it is a weapon that changes with the argumentative weather. No formulation can be trusted that is generated by the exigencies of the wage-bargain; both the dismissal of the worker as unskilled and the discovery of the worker as a generation-in-reproduction \"mental and trained power\" are ideological positions , not scientific statements. The Times's Reply and the Actual Outcome Marx then paraphrases the argument that follows: \"Potter, the chosen mouthpiece of the cotton manufacturers, distinguishes between two sorts of 'machinery'. Both belong to the capitalist, but one stands in his factory, while the other is housed in cottages outside the factory at night-time and on Sundays. The one is inanimate, the other living. The inanimate machinery not only wears out and loses value from day to day, but also much of it becomes out of date so quickly, owing to constant technical progress, that it can be replaced with advantage by new machinery after a few months. With the living machinery the reverse is true: it gets better the longer it lasts, and in proportion as the skill handed down from one generation to another accumulates.\" The italics-laden locution \"two sorts of 'machinery'\" carries the entire critical charge: Marx has now made the industrialist's own vocabulary perform the reduction of worker to means of production, without a word of editorialising. The mode here is the classical-Marxist one of letting the document speak : Potter has literally called the workers \"the machinery\" and has distinguished them only by their physical location at night and on Sundays. That is the chapter's thesis, in the capitalist's own voice. Marx then gives the reply of The Times (24 March 1863) at length — a \"jeu d'esprit\" Marx describes as such only after quoting it. The Times : \"Mr Edmund Potter is so impressed with the exceptional and supreme importance of the cotton masters that, in order to preserve this class and perpetuate their profession, he would keep half a million of the labouring class confined in a great moral workhouse against their will. 'Is the trade worth retaining?' asks Mr Potter. 'Certainly by all honest means it is,' we answer. 'Is it worth while keeping the machinery in order?' again asks Mr Potter. Here we hesitate. By the 'machinery', Mr Potter means the human machinery, for he goes on to protest that he does not mean to use them as an absolute property. We must confess that we do not think it 'worth while', or even possible, to keep the human machinery in order — that is to shut it up and keep it oiled till it is wanted. Human machinery will rust under inaction, oil and rub it as you may. Moreover, the human machinery will, as we have just seen, get the steam up of its own accord, and burst or run amuck in our great towns. It might, as Mr Potter says, require some time to reproduce the workers, but, having machinists and capitalists at hand, we could always find thrifty, hard, industrious men wherewith to improvise more master-manufacturers than we can ever want. Mr Potter talks of the trade reviving 'in one, two, or three years', and he asks us not 'to encourage or allow (!) the working power to emigrate'. He says that it is very natural the workers should wish to emigrate; but he thinks that in spite of their desire, the nation ought to keep this half million of workers with their 700,000 dependants, shut up in the cotton districts; and as a necessary consequence, he must of course think that the nation ought to keep down their discontent by force, and sustain them by alms — and upon the chance that the cotton masters may some day want them… The time is come when the great public opinion of these islands must operate to save this 'working power' from those who would deal with it as they would deal with iron, and coal, and cotton.\" The Times reply is important for two reasons. First, it is irony : by adopting Potter's own machinery metaphor and pushing it to its absurd conclusions (\"we could always find thrifty, hard, industrious men wherewith to improvise more master-manufacturers than we can ever want\"), the writer exposes the metaphor's instability. The Times even flirts — momentarily — with siding with the workers' right to emigrate. Second, its conclusion (\"save this 'working power' from those who would deal with it as they would deal with iron, and coal, and cotton\") is precisely the critical insight of the chapter. Marx's own assessment is deflationary: \"This article was only a jeu d'esprit . The 'great public opinion' was in fact of Mr Potter's own opinion; it too thought that the factory workers were movable accessories to the factories. Their emigration was prevented.\" The \"great public opinion\" of the cotton districts, and of England, has converged on Potter's position: labour-power is an accessory of capital. The footnote appends a bitter comparison. \"Parliament did not vote a single farthing in aid of emigration, but simply passed some Act empowering the municipal corporations to keep the workers in a state of semi-starvation, i.e. to exploit them without paying the normal wages.\" And contrast: \"when the cattle disease broke out three years later, Parliament hastened to break even its own normal customs, and immediately voted millions for the indemnification of the millionaire landlords, whose farmers in any event came out of it without losing anything, owing to the rise in the price of meat.\" Marx's remark — \"The bestial howls of the landed proprietors at the opening of Parliament, in 1866, showed that a man can worship the cow Sabala without being a Hindu, and can change himself into an ox without being a Jupiter\" — is a satirical flourish, but it makes a serious point: the willingness of the British state to vote millions for the indemnification of landlords when their property is threatened, while refusing a farthing for the emigration of redundant workers, reveals the differential valuation of kinds of property under the capitalist state. Landed capital is protected as property; labour-power is left to the \"drives for self-preservation and propagation\" or to semi-starvation . Marx's summing-up of the episode: \"They were locked up in that 'moral workhouse', the cotton districts, and they form, as before, 'the strength' of the cotton manufacturers of Lancashire.\" The workers are, in the technical sense of the chapter, means of production retained on site; not killed, not freed, but locked up until wanted . Conclusion: Reproduction of the Relation Itself The chapter's concluding paragraphs generalise what the cotton episode showed. \"Capitalist production therefore reproduces in the course of its own process the separation between labour-power and the conditions of labour. It thereby reproduces and perpetuates the conditions under which the worker is exploited. It incessantly forces him to sell his labour-power in order to live, and enables the capitalist to purchase labour-power in order that he may enrich himself. It is no longer a mere accident that capitalist and worker confront each other in the market as buyer and seller. It is the alternating rhythm of the process itself which throws the worker back onto the market again and again as a seller of his labour-power and continually transforms his own product into a means by which another man can purchase him.\" Notice the form of this conclusion. What looked contingent in the isolated act — that this worker and this capitalist meet in the market — is, once the process is seen as continuous, revealed as a systematic and reproduced condition. The worker does not stumble onto the market; he is thrown back onto it by the process. His product does not merely fail to be his; it is what purchases him. The phrase \"the alternating rhythm of the process itself\" identifies the causal agency: not this or that event, but the process's own periodicity. Then the chapter's most concise formulation of the whole: \"In reality, the worker belongs to capital before he has sold himself to the capitalist. His economic bondage is at once mediated through, and concealed by, the periodic renewal of the act by which he sells himself, his change of masters, and the oscillations in the market-price of his labour.\" Each of the three mediating phenomena is named precisely: the periodic renewal (which makes the sale look like a fresh free choice each time); the change of masters (which makes the relation look personal and transitory rather than structural); the oscillations of the wage (which make the relation look market-determined rather than class-determined). All three conceal — and one could add, all three are necessary to the concealment — while none alters the substance. A footnote brings us back to the \"invisible threads\" of the earlier passage, with a concrete case of visible bondage. \"A clumsy agricultural form of this bondage exists in the county of Durham.\" Marx describes the \"hinds' houses.\" \"This is one of the few counties in which circumstances do not secure to the farmer undisputed proprietary rights over the agricultural labourers. The existence of the mining industry allows the latter some freedom of choice.\" Where workers have an alternative (mining), they can escape; where they do not, feudal-style arrangements survive. \"The farmer rents only such farms as have on them labourers' cottages. The rent of the cottage forms part of the wage. These cottages are known as 'hinds' houses'. They are let to the labourers in consideration of certain feudal services, under a contract called 'bondage', which, among other things, binds the labourer, during the time he is employed elsewhere, to leave someone, say his daughter, etc., to fill his place. The labourer himself is called a 'bondsman'.\" Marx then adds the characteristically grotesque detail, drawn from the Public Health. Seventh Report (1864): \"This relationship also shows, from an entirely new angle, how individual consumption by the worker is consumption on behalf of capital, in other words productive consumption: 'It is curious to observe that the very dung of the hind and bondsman is the perquisite of the calculating lord… and the lord will allow no privy but his own to exist in the neighbourhood, and will rather give a bit of manure here and there for a garden than bate any part of his seigneurial right'.\" The detail is extreme, but it is not incidental: it shows the principle in its most concrete form — the worker's every product of consumption, even his bodily waste, is, in this \"clumsy\" form, seized as an input to capital. The \"essence\" of the relation, considered as productive consumption, is not abolished but made visible when the \"form\" of the wage is supplemented by the \"form\" of feudal service. A short note appends the case of child labour: \"It will not be forgotten that, where the labour of children is concerned, even the formality of a voluntary sale vanishes.\" The footnote, together with footnote 18 (Sismondi: \"The worker asked for means of subsistence in order to live, the master asked for labour in order to make a profit\"), rounds out the chapter's catalogue of empirical exceptions that prove the rule: where the wage-form is absent (feudal service, child labour) the substance of the relation is exposed rather than concealed, which is why the wage-form is the characteristic form of \"free\" labour under capital. The final lines of the chapter resume the whole argument in a single sentence — and they are, in the strict sense, the chapter's thesis: \"The capitalist process of production, therefore, seen as a total, connected process, i.e. a process of reproduction, produces not only commodities, not only surplus-value, but it also produces and reproduces the capital-relation itself; on the one hand the capitalist, on the other the wage-labourer.\" The footnote is self-citation: from Marx's 1847 Brussels lectures, published in 1849 under the title \"Wage Labour and Capital\" in the Neue Rheinische Zeitung (No. 266, 7 April 1849): \"Capital presupposes wage-labour; wage-labour presupposes capital. They reciprocally condition each other's existence; they reciprocally bring forth each other. Does a worker in a cotton factory merely produce cotton goods? No, he produces capital. He produces values which serve afresh to command his labour and by means of it to create new values.\" The self-citation marks the continuity of Marx's position from 1847 to 1867, but also the development : what in 1847 was a slogan is in 1867 a demonstrated result , established by the specific analyses of the labour-fund, the wage, individual consumption, and the reproduction of the separated poles. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS: The Fundamental Principles Several principles of far-reaching generality emerge from the chapter. First: continuity transforms. This is the chapter's master principle, and it recurs. A single act of production and exchange looks one way; the same act, indefinitely repeated, is another thing. The repetition is not an aggregate of independent acts — it is a process with properties of its own. From this principle Marx derives nearly everything else in the chapter: the wage as a share in the product, variable capital as a historical form of the labour-fund, the original capital as capitalised surplus-value, the worker's reproduction as a means of production. Second: value accounting and its standpoint. Marx's argument about the \"original\" capital depends on the distinction between the material and the value components of capital, and on the demonstration that after n = C/s years the value-perspective reveals the surviving capital as capitalised surplus-value. This is one of the earliest and clearest statements of a principle Marx will develop throughout the remainder of the book: that the standpoint of value — not the standpoint of things, nor of the individual transaction — is the standpoint at which capitalist production must be analysed. Third: the standpoint-shift. From \"single capitalist and single worker\" to \"capitalist class and working class,\" from \"isolated process of production\" to \"capitalist production in full swing, and on its actual social scale.\" This is a method, not a rhetorical flourish. The illusions that the isolated act generates (independence of the worker, generosity of the capitalist, contingency of the meeting) dissolve at the class level, where the process is visible as the reproduction of two mutually dependent and opposed positions. Fourth: the primacy of function over form. The form of appearance of the labour-fund (wages) is not the same as the labour-fund itself. The form of appearance of the worker's independence (contract, change of masters, wage-oscillation) is not the same as his dependence. The form of appearance of \"productive consumption\" (bread, beans, dung) is not the same as its functional reality (reproduction of the means of production). Marx's critical apparatus throughout is the separation of form from substance. Fifth: the reproduction of the relation. Production under capital is not just the production of commodities and surplus-value; it is the production of the relation itself — the capitalist and the wage-labourer as ongoing social positions. This thesis has been the most influential in the chapter, and the most disputed. It is what makes \"simple reproduction\" not merely a limiting case but a fundamental demonstration. Theoretical Implications For Marx's own project, the chapter is a hinge. It establishes that the capital-relation contains within itself the mechanisms of its own perpetuation, and thereby sets up the analysis of accumulation proper (Chapter 24) on a foundation that no merely distributive account can shake. When, in Chapter 24, Marx shows that surplus-value converted into capital becomes new means of production and new labour-power, and that the process redounds to the benefit of capital, he builds on the demonstrated persistence of the relation . When, in Chapter 25, he develops the general law of capitalist accumulation and its polarisation of wealth and misery, he builds on the reproduction of the two classes established here. And when, in Chapters 26–33, he recounts the historical genesis of capitalism in primitive accumulation, he does so on the ground prepared by the \"sooner or later, and necessarily\" argument — that whatever the origin, the working of the process converts capital into appropriated surplus-value. For later theory, the chapter contributes several durable notions. The labour-fund as a category (distinct from variable capital) nourishes a critique of the \"wages-fund\" doctrine. The functional classification of the worker as a means of production nourishes twentieth-century work on the \"social factory\" and on the reproduction of labour-power. The distinction between productive and unproductive consumption as standpoint-relative nourishes analyses of the ideological character of economic categories. Most importantly, the thesis \"the capitalist produces the worker as a wage-labourer\" has been foundational for all theories that treat class not as a distributional outcome but as a social relation reproduced in and through the process of production. Practical and Philosophical Implications The chapter's practical implications are direct and unsettling. If the first condition of accumulation is the continuous production of the wage-labourer, then any politics that addresses only the conditions of this contract, this employer, this wage — without addressing the reproduction of the relation as a whole — is a politics of appearances. The chapter's arguments imply that reforms (better wages, better working conditions, the legalisation of unions) can mitigate the worker's situation without abolishing the relation; the relation will continue to reproduce itself through the \"periodic renewal of the act by which he sells himself.\" Whether Marx is right that this is inherent in accumulation, or whether it is a tendency that can be countered by the systematic organisation of labour, remains a live dispute — but the chapter has made the framing of the dispute sharper, and by introducing the class-level standpoint it has made the frame itself part of the object of contention. The philosophical implications concern the nature of freedom. If contract, choice, and legal equality coexist with substantive dependence, then freedom of the kind the wage-form claims to provide is, in Marx's analysis, freedom in the form of its opposite . This is what the chapter's most famous image — the invisible threads — captures. It is not that wage-labour is slavery in disguise, nor that the wage-labourer is less free than the Roman slave; it is that the specific form of capitalist freedom consists in the renewed, market-mediated, and individually-chosen entry into a relation of dependence . Marx's claim is that this is not a perversion of freedom but its form under capitalism — and, therefore, that any aspiration to substantial freedom must move beyond the wage-form as such. The chapter does not say how; it establishes that what it calls \"simple reproduction\" is, at the social level, anything but simple. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 22 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 24"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 24", "title": "Chapter 24", "subtitle": "The Transformation of Surplus-Value into Capital", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 24/Chapter 24  The Transformation of Surplus-Value into Capital_explanation.html", "word_count": 10015, "read_time": "50 min read", "tags": ["Capital Accumulation & Crises", "Critique of the Smithian Dogma"], "keywords": ["Transformation of Surplus-Value into Capital", "Accumulation on an Expanded Scale", "Inversion of Property Laws of Commodity Production", "Division of Surplus-Value: Capital vs Revenue", "Critique of Abstinence / Sacrificial Theory", "Classical Dogma of Consuming Surplus-Value Productively"], "text": "Chapter 24 The Transformation Of Surplus-Value Into Capital - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 10,000 words ⏱️ ~50 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 24 of Marx's Capital , Volume 1, constitutes one of the most philosophically explosive and dialectically intricate chapters in the entire work. Its title — \"The Transformation of Surplus-Value into Capital\" — announces a problem that is at once technical, historical, and juridical: not merely how a portion of the surplus product is reinvested, but how the very legal architecture of bourgeois society — the property rights allegedly grounded in commodity exchange — is, through its own inner logic, inverted into a system of class appropriation that is its structural opposite. The chapter's central thesis is that accumulation of capital is not an external addition to capitalist production but the very mechanism through which capitalist property relations reproduce and expand themselves while continuing to invoke the juridical forms of equality and equivalence . Where the preceding chapters established how surplus-value arises from capital, this chapter reverses the lens: how does capital arise from surplus-value? The intellectual objective is threefold. First, to demonstrate, at the level of the individual capitalist and then of the class as a whole, the material and value mechanics of accumulation — how a £10,000 spinning enterprise generates £2,000 of surplus-value, which, when capitalized, produces £400 more, and so on in the \"spiral\" that Sismondi named. Second, to expose and demolish a set of apologetic doctrines — Adam Smith's reduction of accumulation to the consumption of surplus product by productive workers, Senior's theology of \"abstinence,\" Bentham's dogma of a fixed \"labour fund\" — that obscure the exploitative content of accumulation behind the language of saving, self-denial, and natural limits. Third, and most crucially, to demonstrate what Marx calls a \"dialectical inversion\" : the property laws of commodity production, which began as the right of the producer to own his own product, transform — through the immanent, inexorable working out of their own principles — into the laws of capitalist appropriation, which is the right of the non-producer to own the product of others' labour. This is the beating theoretical heart of Volume 1's final section. The earlier parts of the book anatomized the origin of surplus-value (the purchase of a peculiar commodity, labour-power, whose use-value exceeds its exchange-value). Chapter 24 performs the complementary movement: it shows that this origin-relation, repeated and capitalized, is self-reproducing and self-expanding. Capital is not a thing but a social relation that generates the conditions of its own perpetuation on an ever-larger scale. The mysterious \"original accumulation\" that political economy assumed as a primal fact — the capitalist's putative \"own labour and that of his forefathers\" — is here exposed as a vanishing quantity, a magnitudo evanescens , smothered by the accumulated mass of unpaid labour that constitutes capital's true substance. The chapter also functions as a hinge into the broader architecture of Capital . It anticipates the analysis of the circulation of social capital in Volume 2 (which Marx promises to provide in Part Three of that volume) and the analysis of the trinity formula in Volume 3. It sets the stage for the historical account of primitive accumulation (Chapter 26 onwards in the Penguin edition and Chapters 26–33 in older editions), by demonstrating that the \"so-called primitive accumulation\" is not — as political economy would have it — the pre-history of capitalist accumulation but rather the historical precondition that allowed the system of self-expanding appropriation to get underway. The chapter thus sits between the micro-logic of surplus-value production and the macro-logic of capitalist totality: it is the bridge from the exchange relation taken in isolation to the class relation taken as a whole. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The inaugural movement: capital arising from surplus-value and the spinning example Marx opens with a compact statement of the problem's inversion: \"Earlier we considered how surplus-value arises from capital; now we have to see how capital arises from surplus-value.\" The technical term for this — the \"employment of surplus-value as capital, or its reconversion into capital\" — is accumulation of capital . Marx immediately marks his intellectual debts and their limits by quoting Malthus (\"Accumulation of capital; the employment of a portion of revenue as capital\") and the second edition of Malthus's Principles of Political Economy (\"Conversion of revenue into capital\"). The turn to Malthus is no idle courtesy: Malthus, a political economist of the Ricardian generation, had hit upon the vocabulary that would become the apologetic shell of the concept, while missing its class content entirely. To ground the analysis materially, Marx introduces his running example: a master-spinner with an advanced capital of £10,000, four-fifths (£8,000) in constant capital — cotton, machinery, etc. — and one-fifth (£2,000) in wages. Assume a rate of surplus-value of 100 per cent. The spinners produce 240,000 lbs. of yarn with a total value of £12,000. The surplus-value of £2,000 is \"contained in the surplus, or net product, of 40,000 lbs. of yarn\" — one-sixth of the gross product. Marx adds a philosophically pointed observation: \"£2,000 is £2,000. Neither seeing nor smelling will tell us that this sum of money is surplus-value.\" Money is indifferent to its origin; value, once it takes the money form, erases the historical trace of exploitation. This is a motif running through the entire Marxian corpus: the scandal is not that theft is visible but that it is invisible, that the exchange form homogenizes unpaid labour with paid, surplus with the original capital. Accumulation proceeds: the £2,000 is now laid out in the same proportions as the original capital — £1,600 for constant capital and £400 for additional spinning workers. The new capital of £2,000 \"then functions in the spinning-mill and in its turn brings in a surplus value of £400.\" The process scales: \"It is the old story: Abraham begat Isaac, Isaac begat Jacob and so on.\" Original capital produces surplus-value; that surplus-value becomes additional capital, which produces its own surplus-value; the second £400 becomes additional capital producing £80, and so on in geometric descent. Marx deliberately brackets several complications: the portion of surplus-value consumed by the capitalist (analyzed later); whether the additional capital is attached to the original or separated from it; whether the same capitalist employs the additional capital or hands it to others. The point is structural: \"by the side of the newly formed capital, the original capital continues to reproduce itself and to produce surplus-value, and this is true of all accumulated capital in relation to the additional capital engendered by it.\" The mass of capital shares a common quality — every increment traces back to unpaid labour, but each increment also continues, in its turn, to extort further unpaid labour. The interrogation of \"original capital\" and the dialectic of appropriation Now Marx inserts his famous juridical interrogation. The original capital of £10,000 — where did its owner get it from? \"From his own labour and that of his forefathers,\" say \"the spokesmen of political economy.\" Marx concedes that this assumption \"appears to be the only one consonant with the laws of commodity production\" — a crucial concession that sets up the dialectical reversal. \"But it is quite otherwise with regard to the additional capital of £2,000.\" Its origin is transparent: \"There is not one single atom of its value that does not owe its existence to unpaid labour.\" The means of production and the subsistence of workers, which the £2,000 purchases, are nothing but \"parts of the tribute annually exacted from the working class by the capitalist class.\" Marx then deploys one of the chapter's most memorable images: the conqueror who buys commodities from the conquered with money stolen from them. The exchange of equivalent for equivalent at the point of purchase of labour-power is perfectly consistent with — indeed is the medium of — a systematic appropriation without equivalent. Even if the additional capital employs the producer of that very capital, the worker \"must buy back the fruits of his previous labour with more labour than they cost.\" Property relations have become a cycle of expropriation masquerading as exchange. The \"spiral\" of reproduction and the dialectical inversion Sismondi's phrase \"the cycle of simple reproduction alters its form and ... changes into a spiral\" is invoked to capture the qualitative difference between mere repetition and accumulation. Marx then spells out the cumulative logic: The first additional £2,000 presupposes a value of £10,000 \"advanced by the capitalist, and belonging to him by virtue of his 'original labour.'\" The second additional £400 presupposes only the prior accumulation of the £2,000 \"of which the £400 is the capitalized surplus-value.\" Therefore \"the ownership of past unpaid labour is thenceforth the sole condition for the appropriation of living unpaid labour on a constantly increasing scale.\" And: \"The more the capitalist has accumulated, the more is he able to accumulate.\" This is the positive feedback loop — the \"dialectical inversion\" — that Marx will now name explicitly. Every individual transaction conforms to the laws of commodity exchange: the capitalist buys labour-power, the worker sells it \"at what we shall assume is its real value\"; equivalents are exchanged. And yet \"the laws of appropriation or of private property, laws based on the production and circulation of commodities, become changed into their direct opposite through their own internal and inexorable dialectic.\" Marx analyzes the exchange relation between capitalist and worker in three moments of inversion: The form is exchange, the content is appropriation. The social relation appears as a succession of free exchanges, but what it accomplishes is \"the constant appropriation by the capitalist, without equivalent, of a portion of the labour of others which has already been objectified, and his repeated exchange of this labour for a greater quantity of the living labour of others.\" The form mystifies the content; it is \"a mere semblance.\" The laws of property invert. Originally, \"the rights of property seemed to us to be grounded in a man's own labour.\" Now, \"property turns out to be the right, on the part of the capitalist, to appropriate the unpaid labour of others or its product, and the impossibility, on the part of the worker, of appropriating his own product.\" The identity of property and labour gives way to their separation as \"the necessary consequence of a law that apparently originated in their identity.\" The inversion arises from the application, not the violation, of commodity laws. This is the most important and most subtle point: \"however much the capitalist mode of appropriation may seem to fly in the face of the original laws of commodity production, it nevertheless arises, not from a violation of these laws but, on the contrary, from their application.\" There is no fraud, no cheating of the worker, no paying below value. The dialectic is immanent. This is a direct refutation of any theory of exploitation grounded in unequal exchange, cheating, or monopoly pricing — a refutation that separates Marx from the \"utopian socialism\" of Proudhon and others. Marx then walks through the \"consecutive phases of motion\" of this dialectic, in the passage added by Engels to the fourth German edition (based on the French edition of 1872). The original transformation of values into capital proceeded \"in complete accordance with the laws of exchange\": the worker sold his labour-power, the capitalist bought it; the worker received the value of his commodity and alienated its use-value. The resulting product contains (a) the value of the means of production consumed, (b) the equivalent of wages, and (c) a surplus-value. But this surplus-value arises not because the seller was defrauded but because the buyer used the commodity — a purchase of labour-power is the purchase of a use-value capable of producing value, and its consumption is legitimate. From this, Marx draws three juridical corollaries: (1) The product belongs to the capitalist, not the worker. (2) The value of the product includes a surplus-value \"which costs the worker labour but the capitalist nothing, and which none the less becomes the legitimate property of the capitalist.\" (3) The worker has retained his labour-power and may sell it again. The laws are not broken; they are fulfilled to their logical conclusion — and that conclusion is the transformation of private property based on labour into private property based on appropriation of others' labour. Simple reproduction and the perpetual legitimization of appropriation Simple reproduction, Marx continues, is \"only the periodic repetition of this first operation; each time, money is freshly transformed into capital.\" The law \"gains the opportunity to operate continuously.\" Sismondi's formulation — \"Several successive acts of exchange have only made the last represent the first\" — captures the temporal disguise: each successive sale obscures the accumulated history behind it. Sismondi's distinction between workers who \"acquire each year a fresh right to their share by fresh labour\" and capitalists who \"have already acquired, by their original labour, a permanent right to their share\" is quoted, and Marx adds his mocking observation: \"It is indeed a well-known fact that the sphere of labour is not the only one in which primogeniture works miracles.\" The point — cutting to the bone — is that the capitalist's apparent \"permanent right\" to a share of the annual product is nothing but the cumulative sediment of past appropriations, now trumped up as inheritance. Nor does the argument change under expanded reproduction. Whether the capitalist squanders all surplus-value in dissipation (simple reproduction) or consumes only part and converts the rest to money (accumulation), the surplus-value \"is his property; it has never belonged to anyone else.\" This is the surface-level truth — under the rules of commodity exchange, the capitalist does own the surplus-value, just as he owns the capital advanced. If worker B is paid from the surplus-value produced by worker A, that is no concern of B's; what B has a right to claim is that the capitalist pay him the value of his labour-power. Marx acidifies this logic by re-writing a passage from Sismondi: \"Both of them still benefited: the worker because he was advanced the fruits of his labour (should read: of the unpaid labour of other workers) before the work was done (should read: before his own labour had borne fruit); the employer, because the labour of this worker was worth more than his wages (should read: produced more value than the value of his wages).\" The isolation of transactions and the inadmissibility of class categories A crucial methodological passage follows. If we shift perspective from individual transactions to \"capitalist production in the uninterrupted flow of its renewal\" — from individual capitalist and worker to capitalist class and working class — \"the matter looks quite different.\" But \"in so doing we should be applying standards entirely foreign to commodity production.\" Because commodity production knows only \"mutually independent buyer and seller\"; relations between them cease when the contract expires. Each transaction is discrete; a repeated transaction is \"the result of a new agreement which has nothing to do with the previous one.\" Marx's dialectical genius lies in his refusal to simply refute the bourgeois standpoint. He grants it completely on its own ground: \"If, therefore, commodity production, or one of its associated processes, is to be judged according to its own economic laws, we must consider each act of exchange by itself, apart from any connection with the act of exchange preceding it and that following it.\" And, \"since sales and purchases are negotiated solely between particular individuals, it is not admissible to look here for relations between whole social classes.\" In other words: taken in isolation, every transaction is just; the injustice is systemic, arising from the repetition and the totality that commodity exchange cannot see. The dialectical punchline: \"However long a series of periodic reproductions and preceding accumulations the capital functioning today may have passed through, it always preserves its original virginity.\" As long as the laws of exchange are observed in each isolated act, \"the mode of appropriation can be completely revolutionized without in any way affecting the property rights which correspond to commodity production.\" This is why the same legal rights persist across epochs of wildly different appropriation — from the artisan who enriched himself only through his own labour to the modern capitalist who appropriates unpaid labour \"over and over again.\" The generalization follows: \"This result becomes inevitable from the moment there is a free sale, by the worker himself, of labour-power as a commodity.\" And this generalization — wage-labour as the basis of society-wide commodity production — is the moment when commodity production \"unfolds all its hidden potentialities.\" Marx's retort to those who say wage-labour \"adulterates\" commodity production: \"To say that the intervention of wage-labour adulterates commodity production is to say that commodity production must not develop if it is to remain unadulterated.\" Thus, \"to the extent that commodity production, in accordance with its own immanent laws, undergoes a further development into capitalist production, the property laws of commodity production must undergo a dialectical inversion so that they become laws of capitalist appropriation.\" A footnote added by Engels (based on the French edition) targeting Proudhon is worth flagging: \"We may well, therefore, feel astonished at the cleverness of Proudhon, who would abolish capitalist property — by enforcing the eternal laws of property which are themselves based on commodity production!\" This is one of Marx's most devastating critiques of petty-bourgeois socialism: the project of restoring \"property\" against \"capitalist property\" is incoherent because property in the Marxian sense is already, immanently, capitalist property in embryo. Vanishing original capital and the \"accumulated wealth\" of political economy The section closes with the magnitudo evanescens argument. In simple reproduction, all capital — whatever its origin — becomes \"accumulated capital, or capitalized surplus-value.\" In the \"flood of production,\" the original capital advanced becomes \"a vanishing quantity (magnitudo evanescens in the mathematical sense)\" compared to the directly accumulated capital. Whether it functions in the hands of the original accumulator or of others is immaterial. Hence political economy's intuition (Malthus: \"Capital, viz., accumulated wealth employed with a view to profit\"; R. Jones: \"Capital... consists of wealth saved from revenue, and used with a view to profit\") is correct as description, but political economy fails to see what class relation is being described. The formula \"all existing capital is accumulated or capitalized interest\" (from an 1851 issue of The Economist ) is technically correct, since interest is a fragment of surplus-value — but it conceals the origin in appropriation. Transition: political economy's erroneous conception of reproduction on an increasing scale Section 2 clears away a semantic confusion in classical political economy. The confusion concerns the phrase \"capital is consumed by productive workers.\" Marx grants that the consumption of surplus product by productive rather than unproductive workers is characteristic of accumulation — the classical economists are right about that much. But they draw the wrong conclusion. Adam Smith made it \"the fashion to present accumulation as nothing more than the consumption of the surplus product by productive workers,\" which amounts to saying \"the capitalization of surplus-value consists merely in turning surplus-value into labour-power.\" Ricardo follows Smith in this: \"'When we say that revenue is saved, and added to capital, what we mean is, that the portion of revenue, so said to be added to capital, is consumed by productive instead of unproductive labourers. There can be no greater error than in supposing that capital is increased by non-consumption.'\" Marx objects: \"There can be no greater error than the one repeated after Adam Smith by Ricardo and all subsequent political economists.\" In fact, surplus-value — like any value advanced — divides into constant and variable capital, into means of production and labour-power. To say that the whole surplus is consumed by productive workers is to identify accumulation with the hiring of more workers , ignoring the fact that a substantial part of the additional capital must be laid out as constant capital (machinery, raw materials). Adam Smith's error culminates in \"the absurd conclusion\" that while each individual capital divides into a constant and variable part, \"the capital of society can be entirely resolved into variable capital.\" The absurdity: Smith argues that a cloth manufacturer's £2,000 flows to weavers, but also to wool and machinery sellers, who in turn pay their own workers, \"and so on until the whole £2,000 is spent in the payment of wages.\" Marx's cutting rejoinder: \"It is evident that the entire thrust of this argument lies in the words 'and so on,' which send us from pillar to post. In fact, Adam Smith breaks off the investigation just where the difficulties begin.\" Marx then explains the material reality. \"The annual process of reproduction is easily understood, as long as we look solely at the sum total of the year's production. But every single component of this annual product must be brought into the market as a commodity, and there the difficulties begin.\" The movements of individual capitals and personal revenues \"cross and intermingle\" in a general circulation that confuses the observer — and Marx promises a full analysis in Part Three of Volume 2 (the reproduction schemas). He credits the Physiocrats with the Tableau Économique , the first attempt \"to depict the year's production in the shape in which it emerges from circulation.\" Marx notes acidly that \"political economy has not failed to exploit, in the interests of the capitalist class, Adam Smith's doctrine that the whole of that part of the net product which is transformed into capital is consumed by the working class.\" The apologetic payoff of Smith's error: if the entire surplus reinvested is consumed by workers, then accumulation is a boon to labour, and there is nothing extractive about it. The Smithian error is functional to class apologetics. The footnote on John Stuart Mill — \"One of the great merits...\" [actually Marx's criticism] — targets Mill's inability to spot the error even when \"it cries out for rectification from a purely technical standpoint, entirely within the bourgeois field of vision.\" Mill reproduces Smith: \"The capital itself in the long run becomes entirely wages, and when replaced by the sale of produce becomes wages again.\" Marx notes Mill \"registers the confusion of his masters with the dogmatism of a disciple.\" A further footnote notes the related error — that the price of commodities resolves into wages, profit and rent — and quotes Storch's confession: \"It is impossible to resolve the necessary price into its simplest elements.\" Marx finds this confession \"a fine kind of economic science.\" The point: Smith's confusion between fixed/circulating capital and constant/variable capital (which Marx claims to be the first to have properly distinguished — see footnote 55) leads to the \"dogma that the price of commodities is made up of wages, profit (interest) and ground rent.\" Section 3: Division of surplus-value into capital and revenue — the abstinence theory Having established that surplus-value is both a fund for consumption and a fund for accumulation, Marx turns to the division between them. \"With a given mass of surplus-value, then, the larger the one part, the smaller the other.\" The ratio determines the magnitude of accumulation. And \"it is the owner of the surplus-value, the capitalist, who makes this division. It is an act of his will.\" Marx notes the double meaning of the English/French term \"revenue\" — sometimes meaning surplus-value as a whole, sometimes only the portion consumed — and retains it for consistency. He then gives the celebrated description of the capitalist as \"capital personified\": \"Except as capital personified, the capitalist has no historical value, and no right to that historical existence which, to use Lichnowsky's amusing expression, 'ain't got no date.' It is only to this extent that the necessity of the capitalist's own transitory existence is implied in the transitory necessity of the capitalist mode of production.\" The German prince Lichnowsky's 1848 Frankfurt Assembly outburst against Polish independence — \"keinen Datum nicht hat\" — is repurposed as a witty jab at the historical groundlessness of capitalist existence. The capitalist, as a private person, has no raison d'être ; only as capital incarnate does he have one. Marx then articulates the historical mission of the capitalist: \"in so far as he is capital personified, his motivating force is not the acquisition and enjoyment of use-values, but the acquisition and augmentation of exchange-values. He is fanatically intent on the valorization of value; consequently he ruthlessly forces the human race to produce for production's sake. In this way he spurs on the development of society's productive forces, and the creation of those material conditions of production which alone can form the real basis of a higher form of society, a society in which the full and free development of every individual forms the ruling principle.\" This is a critical passage for understanding Marx's dialectical evaluation of capitalism: the capitalist is not merely a villain; he is a historically necessary instrument for the development of the productive forces that will eventually enable a higher social form. \"Only as a personification of capital is the capitalist respectable.\" Marx compares him to the miser: what in the miser is individual mania, in the capitalist is \"the effect of a social mechanism in which he is merely a cog.\" The capitalist is compelled by competition — \"external and coercive laws\" — to accumulate: \"It compels him to keep extending his capital, so as to preserve it, and he can only extend it by means of progressive accumulation.\" The ascetic injunctions follow in Marx's characteristic satirical mode: \"his own private consumption counts as a robbery committed against the accumulation of his capital, just as, in double-entry book-keeping, the private expenditure of the capitalist is placed on the debit side of his account against his capital.\" Accumulation is \"the conquest of the world of social wealth... the extension of the area of exploited human material and, at the same time, the extension of the direct and indirect sway of the capitalist.\" A footnote quotes Luther's magnificent invective against usurers — \"the heathen were able, by the light of reason, to conclude that a usurer is a double-dyed thief and murderer\" — reminding us that moral condemnation of finance long predates Marx's political economy. The Faustian conflict and Dr Aikin's Manchester Now Marx introduces a historical dimension: \"With the development of the capitalist mode of production, with the growth of accumulation and wealth, the capitalist ceases to be merely the incarnation of capital. He begins to feel a human warmth towards his own Adam.\" The \"classical\" capitalist brandished consumption as \"abstinence\" from accumulating; the \"modernized\" capitalist can entertain accumulation as \"renunciation\" of pleasure. Marx quotes Goethe's Faust — \"Two souls, alas, do dwell within his breast; The one is ever parting from the other\" — to capture the Faustian conflict between the passion for accumulation and the desire for enjoyment. (This is the chapter's central image for the capitalist psychology: internalized asceticism versus the reality of growing luxury.) Dr Aikin's Description of the Country from Thirty to Forty Miles round Manchester (1795) is then invoked to trace four historical phases of the Manchester trade: First period : manufacturers worked hard for a livelihood; enrichment came chiefly through robbing parents who bound their children as apprentices to them — high premiums, starved apprentices — while the manufacturers themselves \"lived like misers\" and \"were far from consuming even the interest on their capital.\" Second period : they acquired little fortunes but worked as hard and lived as plainly as before. Third period : \"luxury began\"; the trade was pushed by riders sent out for orders into every market town. Before 1690, \"few or no capitals of £3,000 to £4,000 acquired by trade existed here\"; but around then, \"traders... began to build modem brick houses, instead of those of wood and plaster.\" Even in the early eighteenth century, a Manchester manufacturer who placed a pint of foreign wine before his guests exposed himself to neighbors' headshakings. It was not until 1758 that a person actually engaged in business was seen with his own carriage. Fourth period (last 30 years of the eighteenth century): \"expense and luxury have made great progress, supported by a trade extended by means of riders and factors through every part of Europe.\" Marx adds: \"What would the good Dr Aikin say if he could rise from the grave and see the Manchester of today'?\" The \"accumulate, accumulate!\" imperative and Malthus's compromise The celebrated slogan: \"Accumulate, accumulate! That is Moses and the prophets!... Therefore save, save, i.e. reconvert the greatest possible portion of surplus-value or surplus product into capital! Accumulation for the sake of accumulation, production for the sake of production: this was the formula in which classical economics expressed the historical mission of the bourgeoisie in the period of its domination.\" Classical economics did not deceive itself over \"the nature of wealth's birth-pangs\" — but this honesty is cold comfort: \"If, in the eyes of classical economics, the proletarian is merely a machine for the production of surplus-value, the capitalist too is merely a machine for the transformation of this surplus-value into surplus capital.\" Marx quotes J.B. Say — \"The savings of the rich are made at the expense of the poor\" — and Sismondi's more radical variant: \"The Roman proletarian lived almost entirely at the expense of society... One might almost say that modern society lives at the expense of the proletarians, on the portion of the wages of labour which it withdraws from their pockets.\" The Sismondi passage reveals that even within bourgeois political economy, the class-exploitation content was recognized; the question was what to do about it. Malthus, \"around the beginning of the 1820s,\" proposed a division of labor: the actual production-capitalist accumulates, while the landed aristocracy, place-men, and beneficed clergy do the spending. The rationale, in Malthus's own words: \"It is of the highest importance... to keep separate the passion for expenditure and the passion for accumulation.\" The proposal triggered a protest from Ricardian spokesmen: \"What, exclaimed one of their spokesmen, does Mr Malthus preach high rents, heavy taxation, etc. so that the industrious may constantly be kept up to the mark by the pressure of unproductive consumers?\" But — as Marx notes — this same Ricardian, hostile to Malthus's high-rent proposal, was perfectly prepared to keep wages low. \"'Increased demand on the part of the labourers means nothing more than their disposition to take less of their own product for themselves, and leave a greater part of it to their employers; and if it be said, that this begets glut, by lessening consumption (on the part of the workers) I can only reply that glut is synonymous with large profits.'\" The intra-class dispute — \"how the booty pumped out of the workers may most advantageously be divided for the purposes of accumulation\" — had to fall silent, Marx observes, before the class struggle: the July Revolution (1830), the Lyon uprising of the silk-workers, English rural arson (the Swing Riots), Owenism in England, Saint-Simonism and Fourierism in France. \"The hour of vulgar economics had arrived.\" Senior's abstinence theory and its demolition Enter Nassau W. Senior — just a year before he discovered at Manchester that profit is \"the product of the unpaid 'last hour of the twelve hours of labour'\" (the target of Marx's \"Senior's Last Hour\" in Chapter 7 of this volume) — announced another discovery: \"I substitute,\" he proudly says, \"for the word capital, considered as an instrument of production, the word abstinence.\" Marx's verdict: \"An unparalleled example of the 'discoveries' of vulgar economics! It replaces an economic category with a sycophantic phrase, and that is all.\" Senior's argument: \"When the savage makes bows, he exercises an industry, but he does not practise abstinence.\" Hence in earlier states of society the instruments of labour were constructed \"without the abstinence of the capitalist.\" And: \"The more society progresses, the more abstinence is demanded\" — namely, \"from those whose business it is to appropriate the industry and the products of others.\" Marx enumerates the reductio ad absurdum: if the corn is not all eaten but in part sown, \"abstinence of the capitalist\"; if the wine gets time to mature, \"abstinence of the capitalist\"; the capitalist \"robs himself whenever he 'lends (!) the instruments of production to the worker.'\" This last move — the interpolation of \"(!!)\" — mocks the identification of lending to a worker with victimization of the lender. Even the conservation of a capital requires \"a constant effort to resist the temptation of consuming it,\" Courcelle-Seneuil claims. The reductio: \"The simple dictates of humanity therefore plainly enjoin the release of the capitalist from his martyrdom and his temptation, in the same way as the slave-owners of Georgia, U.S.A., have recently been delivered by the abolition of slavery from the painful dilemma over whether they should squander the surplus product extracted by means of the whip from their Negro slaves entirely in champagne, or whether they should reconvert a part of it into more Negroes and more land.\" A footnote extends the mockery: \"It has never occurred to the vulgar economist to make the simple reflection that every human action may be conceived as an 'abstinence' from its opposite. Eating is abstinence from fasting, walking is abstinence from standing still, working is abstinence from idling, idling is abstinence from working, etc.\" Spinoza's \"Determinatio est negatio\" is invoked — and the reduction absorbs every determination into an infinite regress of abstinences, rendering the concept worthless. Another footnote quotes Scrope on sow-wheat-abstinence and de Molinari's ludicrous characterization of the capitalist as lending \"his instruments of production to the worker, instead of devoting their value to his own consumption.\" The famous footnote on John Cazenove is also present: Cazenove objected that \"It is not the capital, but the use of the capital productively, which is the cause of profits.\" Marx adds that \"John Stuart Mill, on the contrary, both copies Ricardo's theory of profit, and annexes to it Senior's 'remuneration of abstinence.' He is as much at home with absurd and flat contradictions as he is at sea with the Hegelian 'contradiction,' which is the source of all dialectics.\" Richard Jones and the universal reality of expanded reproduction without the abstaining capitalist Marx now makes the historically crucial point that expanded reproduction — production on a scale that expands — is not unique to capitalism; it occurs \"in economic formations of society of the most diverse kinds.\" What is unique to capitalism is that this expanded reproduction appears as the function of a capitalist. The distinction: in non-capitalist forms, the worker's means of production, product, and means of subsistence do not confront him as capital . Richard Jones, former occupant of the East India College Haileybury chair (successor of Malthus), provides the empirical study: the Indian peasantry cultivating their own land never see their product take \"the shape of a fund saved from revenue, which fund has, therefore, gone through a previous process of accumulation.\" In provinces least disturbed by English rule, non-agricultural workers are directly employed by magnates to whom tribute or rent is rendered; some of this is consumed in kind, some converted into luxuries, and the remainder forms the wage of workers who own their own implements. \"Here, production and reproduction on an increasing scale go on their way without any intervention from that peculiar saint, that knight of the woeful countenance, the 'abstaining' capitalist.\" The allusion is to Quixote — the \"knight of the woeful countenance\" — a fitting image for the sanctimonious investor celebrated by political economy. A footnote from Jones: \"Profits... unimportant source of accumulation, compared with wages and rents, in the earlier stages of society... When a considerable advance in the powers of national industry has actually taken place, profits rise into comparative importance as a source of accumulation.\" Section 4: Circumstances determining the extent of accumulation Assuming the ratio of capitalization to consumption is given, the absolute magnitude of accumulation depends on the absolute magnitude of surplus-value. Marx enumerates several factors. First, the degree of exploitation of labour-power. Political economy confuses the acceleration of accumulation due to increased productivity with that due to increased exploitation . Marx notes that the previous chapters assumed wages equal to the value of labour-power, but the \"forcible reduction of the wage of labour beneath its value plays too important a role in the practical movement of affairs for us not to stay with this phenomenon for a moment. In fact, it transforms the worker's necessary fund for consumption, within certain limits, into a fund for the accumulation of capital.\" Marx quotes John Stuart Mill: \"Wages have no productive power; they are the price of a productive power... If labour could be had without purchase, wages might be dispensed with.\" Marx's reply: \"But if the workers could live on air, it would not be possible to buy them at any price. This zero cost of labour is therefore a limit in a mathematical sense, always beyond reach, although we can always approximate more and more nearly to it. The constant tendency of capital is to force the cost of labour back towards this absolute zero.\" The historical evidence: the anonymous author of the Essay on Trade and Commerce (1770) declares England's historical mission to be to force down English wages to the French and Dutch level, complaining: \"But if our poor... will live luxuriously... then labour must, of course, be dear.\" The Northamptonshire manufacturer laments that \"Labour is one-third cheaper in France than in England; for their poor work hard, and fare hard.\" Marx notes the \"pious fraud\" of the manufacturer, who compares English manufacturing workers to French agricultural labourers, as he himself later confesses \"in his confused way.\" Engels adds a footnote from the third German edition: \"Today, thanks to the competition on the world market which has grown up since then, we have advanced much further. 'If China,' says Mr Stapleton, M.P., to his constituents, 'should become a great manufacturing country, I do not see how the manufacturing population of Europe could sustain the contest without descending to the level of their competitors.'... The desired goal of English capital is no longer Continental wages, oh no, it is Chinese wages!\" The Benjamin Thompson (Count Rumford) episode follows: his Essays are \"a cookery book with recipes of all kinds for replacing the ordinary, but expensive food of the worker with various surrogates.\" Marx quotes the \"particularly successful recipe\": \"5 lb. of barley-meal, 7½d.; 5 lb. of Indian corn, 6½d.; 3d. worth of red herring, 1d. salt, 1d. vinegar, 2d. pepper and sweet herbs, in all 20¾d.; make a soup for 64 men, and at the medium price of barley and of Indian corn... this soup may be provided at ¼d. the portion of 20 ounces.\" The biographical footnote on Rumford (Massachusetts-born, fought for England, Bavarian service, created Count in 1790) invites the reader to recognize the obscene recycling of a Yankee in the service of industrial profit. Marx adds that \"with the advance of capitalist production, the adulteration of food has rendered Thompson's ideal superfluous\" — and the footnote documents thirty-one of thirty-four specimens of opium from London chemists adulterated with poppy heads, wheat-flour, gum, clay, sand, some containing no morphine at all. The Speenhamland system of 1795 — parish relief to top up wages — is documented. Burke's quip: \"The squires of Norfolk had dined... when they fixed the rate of wages; the squires of Berks evidently thought the labourers ought not to do so.\" The magistrate-farmer A. Bennett's testimony before the House of Lords inquiry of 1814 — that the weekly income of every family is made up to the gallon loaf (8 lb. 11 oz.) and 3d. per head, deducted if clothes are found — is quoted. Marx concludes with a bourgeois writer's lament: \"For years... they (the farmers) have degraded a respectable class of their countrymen, by forcing them to have recourse to the workhouse... the farmer, while increasing his own gains, has prevented any accumulation on the part of his labouring dependants.\" And a note on \"domestic industry\" as a case of direct robbery of the worker's consumption fund. Second, the growing difference between capital employed and capital consumed. Even if the number of workers is fixed, capital has an elasticity. To increase labour by 50%, one can either hire 50 more workers (requiring additional constant capital) or extend the working day from 8 to 12 hours, in which case existing instruments of labour suffice, being merely consumed more rapidly. In extractive industries (mines, etc.), raw materials form no part of the capital advanced; the object of labour is provided by nature free of charge. Constant capital consists \"almost exclusively of instruments of labour which can very easily absorb an increased quantity of labour (day and night shifts, for example).\" In agriculture, land once prepared produces \"a marvellous effect\" from additional labour. In manufacturing, raw material must be expanded, but the additional products of extractive industry and agriculture redound to manufacturing's advantage. The general result: \"by incorporating with itself the two primary creators of wealth, labour-power and land, capital acquires a power of expansion that permits it to augment the elements of its accumulation beyond the limits apparently fixed by its own magnitude.\" Third, the productivity of social labour. \"The mass of the products in which a certain value, and therefore a surplus-value of a given magnitude is embodied, increases along with the productivity of labour.\" Even at a constant rate of surplus-value, the mass of surplus product grows. The relative magnitude of the accumulation fund can increase at the expense of the consumption fund while the capitalist's real enjoyment increases. The increasing productivity of labour is accompanied by a cheapening of the worker , and thus a higher rate of surplus-value even when real wages rise — and real wages \"never rise in proportion to the productivity of labour.\" Hence \"same value in variable capital therefore sets in motion more labour-power,\" and \"same value in constant capital is embodied in more means of production... It therefore supplies both more product-creating agencies and more value-creating agencies, in other words absorbers of labour.\" Thus \"the production of surplus-value increases more rapidly than the value of the additional capital.\" Productivity also impacts the original capital. Fixed capital (machinery, etc.) is replaced periodically; if the productivity of labour in its construction has risen, the replacement machines are \"more efficient and (considering their increased efficiency) cheaper.\" Chemistry multiplies useful materials, applications, and recycling opportunities: \"it also teaches capital how to throw back the waste from the processes of production and consumption into the cycle of the process of reproduction, and thus, without any previous outlay of capital, it creates fresh materials for it.\" Science and technology give capital \"a power of expansion which is independent of the given magnitude of the capital actually functioning.\" This development is accompanied by \"a partial depreciation of the functioning capital; but in so far as this depreciation makes itself acutely felt in competition, the main burden falls on the worker, in whose increased exploitation the capitalist seeks compensation for his loss.\" Marx then carefully distinguishes value from mass: \"Labour transmits to the product the value of the means of production consumed by it.\" The same quantity of labour adds the same sum of new value; but the old value transmitted grows with productivity. The English spinner versus the Chinese spinner — both working the same hours at the same intensity, both creating equal values in a week — but the Englishman's automatic machine, allowing him to spin hundreds of pounds where the Chinese spins one, transmits \"a sum of old values, many hundred times as great.\" Engels's Condition of the Working Class in England is quoted: \"In 1782 the whole wool crop of the preceding three years lay unused for want of workers, and would have continued so to lie if the newly invented machinery had not come to its assistance and spun it.\" The natural property of living labour — to preserve old value while creating new — becomes a power of capital for \"its own self-preservation.\" This is the crucial fetishism point: \"All the powers of labour project themselves as powers of capital, just as all the value-forms of the commodity do as forms of money.\" The free service of past labour (the \"free service\" of means of production, akin to the free service of water, steam, air, electricity) is progressively accumulated. Marx's footnote here takes a swipe at Ricardo's failure to grasp this: \"a million men always produce in manufactures the same value\" applies only to the new value added; the mass of transmitted old value varies widely with productivity. Say's attempt to resolve the \"difficulty\" by conflating use-value with exchange-value is quoted at length — \"the difficulty is met by calling use-value exchange-value, if you please\" — and an English critic's dismissal of Say's conjuring tricks is quoted. Marx generalizes the fetishism: \"Since past labour always disguises itself as capital, i.e. since the debts owed to the labour of A, B, C etc. are disguised as the assets of the non-worker X, bourgeois citizens and political economists are full of praise for the services performed by past labour.\" MacCulloch is criticized for wanting payment for these \"services\" in the form of interest and profit; his formula — \"The profits of capital are only another name for the wages of accumulated labour\" — is pithily rebutted: \"MacCulloch took out a patent on the 'wages of past labour' long before Senior patented the 'wages of abstinence.'\" The most cutting passage on fetishism: \"The practical agents of capitalist production and their ideological word-spinners are as incapable of thinking of the means of production separately from the antagonistic social mask they wear at present as a slave-owner is of thinking of the worker himself as distinct from his character as a slave.\" Fourth, the magnitude of the capital advanced. With a given degree of exploitation, the mass of surplus-value depends on the number of workers simultaneously exploited, which corresponds, in varying proportions, with the magnitude of the capital. Hence \"the more that capital increases by means of successive accumulations, the more does the sum of value increase that is divided into a fund for consumption and a fund for accumulation.\" The capitalist can both live better and \"renounce\" more. Section 5: The so-called labour fund The chapter's final section demolishes the dogma that capital is a fixed magnitude or that there is a \"labour fund\" fixed by Nature. Marx's summary of the preceding inquiry: \"capital is not a fixed magnitude, but a part of social wealth which is elastic, and constantly fluctuates with the division of surplus-value into revenue and additional capital.\" Further, \"even with a given magnitude of functioning capital, the labour-power, science and land... incorporated in it form elastic powers of capital, allowing it, within certain limits, a field of action independent of its own magnitude.\" Marx reminds the reader he has ignored all circulation relations that could produce different efficiencies, and that he has presupposed limits set by capitalist production in its spontaneous development, disregarding \"any more rational combination which could be effected directly and in a planned way with the means of production and the labour-power at present available.\" This last point is significant: the analysis is not an apology for unplanned production but an argument that even the existing potential is unrecognized by capitalist dogmas. Classical political economy \"has always liked to conceive social capital as a fixed magnitude of a fixed degree of efficiency. But this prejudice was first established as a dogma by the arch-philistine, Jeremy Bentham, that soberly pedantic and heavy-footed oracle of the 'common sense' of the nineteenth-century bourgeoisie. Bentham is among philosophers what Martin Tupper is among poets. Both could only have been manufactured in England.\" A lengthy footnote savages Bentham: \"The principle of utility was no discovery made by Bentham. He simply reproduced in his dull way what Helvetius and other Frenchmen had said with wit and ingenuity in the eighteenth century.\" Marx's criticism: \"To know what is useful for a dog, one must investigate the nature of dogs... Applying this to man... [Bentham] assumes that the modern petty bourgeois, especially the English petty bourgeois, is the normal man. Whatever is useful to this peculiar kind of normal man, and to his world, is useful in and for itself. He applies this yardstick to the past, the present and the future. The Christian religion, for example, is 'useful,' 'because it forbids in the name of religion the same faults that the penal code condemns in the name of the law.' Art criticism is 'harmful' because it disturbs worthy people in their enjoyment of Martin Tupper, etc.\" The valuation: \"If I had the courage of my friend Heinrich Heine, I should call Mr Jeremy a genius in the way of bourgeois stupidity.\" Bentham's dogma \"renders the commonest phenomena of the production process, for instance its sudden expansions and contractions, and even accumulation itself, absolutely incomprehensible.\" It was used \"by Bentham himself, as well as by Malthus, James Mill, MacCulloch, etc., for apologetic purposes\" — specifically to represent variable capital (or its material form, the labour fund ) as a fixed size. Variable capital \"was turned by this fable into a separate part of social wealth, confined by natural chains and unable to cross the boundary to the other parts.\" Marx then quantifies the variability. To set a definite mass of constant capital in motion, a definite mass of living labour is required, given by technology. But the number of workers required to embody this mass of labour-power is not given, because it varies with the degree of exploitation of individual labour-power. Nor is the price of labour-power given — only a minimum limit, \"which is moreover very elastic.\" The actual facts underlying the dogma are: (1) \"the worker has no right to interfere in the division of social wealth into means of enjoyment for the non-worker and means of production\"; (2) \"it is only in favourable and exceptional cases that he can enlarge the so-called 'labour fund' at the expense of the 'revenue' of the rich.\" A footnote from John Stuart Mill concedes: \"The produce of labour is apportioned at present in an inverse ratio to the labour — the largest portions to those who have never worked at all, the next largest to those whose work is almost nominal, and so in a descending scale...\" — Marx notes this honestly, and adds that while Mill should be rebuked for the contradiction between his dogmas and his modern tendencies, \"it would be very unjust to lump them together with the herd of vulgar economic apologists.\" The reductio of the labour fund dogma comes from Fawcett: \"The circulating capital of a country is its wage-fund. Hence, if we desire to calculate the average money wages received by each labourer, we have simply to divide the amount of this capital by the number of the labouring population.\" Marx's riposte: \"That is to say, we first add together the individual wages actually paid, and then we assert that the sum thus obtained forms the total value of the 'labour fund' handed down to us by the grace of God and Nature. Lastly, we divide the sum thus obtained by the number of workers, in order to find out how much each is permitted to receive on the average. A very shrewd way of proceeding, this is.\" The circularity is devastating: the \"labour fund\" is simply the sum of wages already paid, dressed up as a natural datum, then divided to produce the average wage — tautology masquerading as law. The final blow against the fixed-fund doctrine: Fawcett himself admits that much of the annual saving \"is used as capital, not in England, but in foreign countries.\" Marx: \"with the additional capital thus exported, a part of the 'labour fund' invented by God and Bentham naturally also flows out of the country.\" The labour fund can cross national borders; it is not chained to any natural magnitude. A footnote qualifies: \"It might be said that not only capital but also workers, in the shape of emigrants, are annually exported from England.\" Marx responds that the emigrants are \"in great part not workers\" — \"most of them are the sons of farmers\" — and that the exported capital is a much greater proportion of annual accumulation than emigration is of annual population increase. Marx closes the chapter (implicitly) by having cleared the ground: the argument that the labour fund is elastic, capable of expanding across the boundaries of \"revenue\" and \"capital,\" cross-nationally mobile, and subject to change with the degree of exploitation — demolishes the last bastion of the apologetic claim that wages are limited by a natural, immovable fund. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter's central lesson is the immanent, self-unfolding dialectic of capitalist appropriation . Marx does not argue that capitalism violates its own laws of exchange — he argues that it fulfils them, and that this fulfilment inverts them. The property right grounded in a man's own labour becomes the right to appropriate the labour of others; the exchange of equivalents becomes the systematic exchange of the worker's living labour for a smaller quantity of objectified labour; the freedom of contract becomes the compulsion to sell one's labour-power on pain of starvation. This is not a critique from outside the bourgeois horizon but a demonstration that the bourgeois horizon, followed to its necessary conclusions, produces the very inversion it denies. Property and labour, initially identified, are now separated as structural opposites — and this separation is the necessary consequence of a law that originated in their identity. From this follows a profound methodological lesson : the isolated transaction is always just, but the systemic repetition of just transactions produces systematic injustice. The bourgeois economist's insistence on judging each exchange on its own terms is not a mistake of analysis but a structural feature of the commodity form: the form abstracts from history, from accumulation, from class. Marx grants the abstraction in order to overcome it: only when the transactions are viewed in their temporal succession, in their material interconnection, in their reproduction of the class relation, does the injustice become visible. This methodological move — taking the isolated transaction and the connected totality as two opposite but equally necessary standpoints — is the dialectical core of the chapter. The demonstration that the \"original capital\" becomes a magnitudo evanescens is a decisive blow to the ideology of the \"frugal capitalist\" who earned his initial stock by labour and abstinence. Even granting the possibility of such an origin (which Marx neither affirms nor denies here, since the historical question belongs to \"primitive accumulation\"), the accumulated mass of capital is overwhelmingly unpaid labour; whatever the first atom of capital was, its present existence is the capitalized subjugation of living labour. The ownership of past unpaid labour is \"thenceforth the sole condition for the appropriation of living unpaid labour on a constantly increasing scale.\" The critique of political economy provided in the chapter has three main axes: The Smithian confusion (accumulation as mere conversion of surplus into wages) whose apologetic payoff is that accumulation benefits workers directly. The abstinence theory (Senior, Mill, Cazenove, Scrope, de Molinari) whose function is to convert appropriation into sacrifice, exploitation into ascetic virtue. The mockery of \"abstinence from fasting\" and \"abstinence from standing still\" dissolves the concept into linguistic nonsense. The labour fund dogma (Bentham, Fawcett) whose function is to represent wages as bounded by a natural, fixed magnitude, thus denying workers any legitimate claim to alter the distribution. The positive content of the chapter is equally important. Marx identifies the elasticity of capital: the power to expand beyond the limits of the value magnitude through (a) increased exploitation, (b) increased productivity, (c) the incorporation of nature (land, mines, air, water, chemistry, science) as a free productive force, (d) the difference between capital employed and consumed, (e) the magnitude of advance. Capital is not a thing but a power-laden social relation that generates, from within itself, forms of expansion not derivable from any static equality between inputs and outputs. The historical-theoretical dimension is developed through the reading of Richard Jones and Dr Aikin. Jones supplies the empirical demonstration that expanded reproduction occurs without the \"abstaining capitalist\" in non-capitalist modes, thus undercutting the claim that capitalist property relations are naturally required for economic development. Aikin shows that asceticism and luxury are historical stages of the capitalist class's self-formation — the miser-capitalist gives way to the luxury-capitalist as accumulation deepens. This historical dialectic is essential to Marx's argument: capitalist psychology is not a natural feature of human nature but a function of the position within the accumulation process. The Faustian conflict between accumulation and enjoyment is produced , not pre-given. The broader philosophical implications reach beyond political economy. The chapter's treatment of fetishism — \"All the powers of labour project themselves as powers of capital, just as all the value-forms of the commodity do as forms of money\" — extends Chapter 1's commodity fetishism to the accumulated capital relation. The slave-owner's inability to see the worker apart from his slave character is Marx's model for the capitalist's inability to see the means of production apart from their capitalist form. This recognition of the social mask as constitutive of the perceived object connects Marx to the Hegelian critique of immediacy: what appears as a simple thing (capital) contains a hidden social history (expropriation), and critique consists in detecting the smuggled history behind the naturalized present. The final image — that part of the \"labour fund invented by God and Bentham\" flows out of the country with capital export — punctures the naturalistic apologetics with a concrete international dynamic. Capital is mobile, elastic, and internationalist; the wage-fund doctrine is provincial. The chapter thus lays the conceptual groundwork for the analysis of the world market and the colonial dimension that Marx will develop in the polemical material on Wakefield's colonization theory (Chapter 33). The chapter's political implications are direct. If the property laws of commodity production invert into laws of capitalist appropriation, then appeals to the \"sanctity of property\" and appeals to \"restoring the identity of property and labour\" (Proudhon) are incoherent within the capitalist order. Only a transformation of the property relations themselves — a revolutionary supersession, not a \"reform\" or \"enforcement of eternal laws\" — can dissolve the inversion. At the same time, Marx's insistence that capitalism is historically necessary for the development of the productive forces heads off any romantic or moralistic rejection: the socialist project is not to return to a prior simplicity but to appropriate, on a new basis, the productive powers capitalism itself has developed. \"The creation of those material conditions of production which alone can form the real basis of a higher form of society, a society in which the full and free development of every individual forms the ruling principle.\" In sum, Chapter 24 is a masterpiece of dialectical exposition. It takes the apparently innocuous concepts of saving, reinvestment, and property and shows that, followed rigorously through their own logic, they reveal a systematic relation of appropriation that is invisible to the individual look but unavoidable to the total view. It demonstrates that the critique of political economy is not external moralizing but the unfolding of the very categories of political economy against themselves. And it establishes that the accumulation of capital is not a matter of the capitalist's virtue or vice — his frugality or his extravagance, his abstinence or his luxury — but the necessary self-reproduction of a social relation into which he is locked, \"a cog\" in a mechanism that compels him to accumulate on pain of loss, while the entire structure of law, ideology, and economic science conspires to represent this compulsion as natural, just, and beneficent. The capitalist's \"original virginity\" — as Marx puts it — is preserved only in the abstract standpoint of commodity exchange. In the actual movement of accumulation, every \"atom\" of the modern capital mass is the crystallized labour of the dispossessed. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 23 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 25"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 25", "title": "Chapter 25", "subtitle": "The General Law of Capitalist Accumulation", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 25/Chapter 25  The General Law of Capitalist Accumulation_explanation.html", "word_count": 25427, "read_time": "127 min read", "tags": ["Organic Composition of Capital (OCC)", "Industrial Reserve Army & Surplus Population", "Capital Accumulation & Crises"], "keywords": ["General Law of Capitalist Accumulation", "Organic Composition of Capital (c:v)", "Industrial Reserve Army / Surplus Population", "Forms of Relative Surplus Population (Floating, Latent, Stagnant, Pauperism)", "Polarization of Wealth and Misery", "Crises and Labour Scarcity", "Absolute General Law of Accumulation"], "text": "Chapter 25 The General Law Of Capitalist Accumulation - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 25,437 words ⏱️ ~127 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION: CHAPTER 25 — THE GENERAL LAW OF CAPITALIST ACCUMULATION 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem and Its Stakes Chapter 25 of Capital , Volume 1, constitutes the culminating theoretical payoff of the entire \"Process of Accumulation of Capital\" section. Its opening sentence states the problem with disarming directness: \"In this chapter we shall consider the influence of the growth of capital on the fate of the working class.\" This is not a narrow technical question. It is the question upon which the moral, political, and theoretical coherence of the entire critique of political economy turns. If capital accumulation — the engine of \"wealth of nations\" — inevitably and structurally produces the immiseration of the very class that creates that wealth, then the classical economists' claim that capital accumulation is the road to universal prosperity collapses into an ideological illusion. Marx's intellectual objective is therefore double. First, he must demonstrate the mechanics — the causal dynamics — by which the accumulation of capital, understood as a process driven by the extraction of surplus-value, generates a surplus population of workers that is simultaneously a product and a lever of accumulation. Second, he must demolish the rival explanation: the Malthusian \"natural law of population,\" which holds that poverty arises from the working class's own tendency to reproduce beyond the means of subsistence, and that capital accumulation only benefits workers when population growth is restrained. Marx intends not merely to refute Malthus but to show that the Malthusian law is itself an ideological obfuscation of a law that operates in the opposite direction — that the working population is rendered \"redundant\" not by its own natural multiplication but by the changing composition of capital. The Architecture of the Argument The chapter unfolds in five sections, but its logical engine has three interlocking movements. The first movement (Section 1) isolates the special case in which accumulation proceeds on an unchanged technical basis. Under this abstraction, Marx grants the classical economists their own premise: if the composition of capital remains constant, the demand for labour does indeed rise in proportion to accumulation, and wages may rise. But even here he demonstrates the decisive asymmetry: wages rise only because capital requires more labour, and the rise can never threaten the profit-driven foundation of the system. The rate of accumulation is the independent variable; the wage rate is the dependent variable. The second movement (Sections 2 and 3) dissolves the abstraction. As accumulation proceeds, it necessarily transforms the technical composition of capital: means of production (constant capital) grow relative to living labour (variable capital). This transformation is not accidental but is driven by the very pursuit of surplus-value through rising productivity. The consequence is that the additional capital formed in each period absorbs proportionally fewer workers, and the old capital, periodically renewed, repels workers it previously employed. The result is the \"progressive production of a relative surplus population, or industrial reserve army\" — a population superfluous to capital's average valorization requirements. This reserve army, in turn, becomes the regulator of wages, the disciplining mechanism of the employed labour force, and the elastic supply of human material that makes possible the sudden expansions of modern industry. The third movement (Sections 4 and 5) generalizes and concretizes. Section 4 identifies the three permanent forms of the relative surplus population — the floating, the latent, and the stagnant — and traces pauperism to its place as the \"hospital of the active labour-army and the dead weight of the industrial reserve army.\" It then states the \"absolute general law\" in its full antagonistic form: \"Accumulation of wealth at one pole is, therefore, at the same time accumulation of misery, the torment of labour, slavery, ignorance, brutalization and moral degradation at the opposite pole.\" Section 5 provides a massive empirical apparatus — English statistics, nutrition studies, housing reports, the gang-system, and the Irish case — to demonstrate the law's operation. The Theoretical Payoff and Its Place in the Broader Work This chapter is the pivot of Capital 's argument. Book One has traced the logic of value, the extraction of surplus-value, the working day, and the production of relative surplus-value. Chapter 25 now closes the circle: it demonstrates that the accumulation of the capital-relation reproduces that relation on an expanded scale. The worker sells labour-power freely, but the sale reproduces the dependence of the worker on capital; the productive power developed under capital becomes a means of domination over the worker. The chapter thus answers the riddle Marx posed earlier: how the worker, the creator of capital, becomes its slave. And it sets the stage for the historical account of violence, expropriation, and legislation in the chapters on primitive accumulation that follow, as well as the theory of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall developed in Volume 3. Crucially, Marx insists the law is a tendency : \"Like all other laws, it is modified in its working by many circumstances.\" The chapter is therefore not a mechanical prediction but an analysis of the structural constraints of capitalist production, empirically illustrated but theoretically bounded. Its dialectical structure — the inversion of means of production from serving the producer into a means of exploiting him — is the signature of Marx's method throughout. The \"law\" is not a natural law but an historical one, specific to a particular mode of production, and thus subject to historical transformation. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS Section 1: A Growing Demand for Labour-Power Accompanies Accumulation If the Composition of Capital Remains the Same The Twofold Composition of Capital — Foundational Definitions Marx opens by defining his central analytic variable: the composition of capital, understood in a \"twofold sense.\" As value , capital divides into constant capital (the value of means of production) and variable capital (the value of labour-power, i.e., the sum total of wages). As material , capital divides into means of production and living labour-power; this technical composition is determined by \"the relation between the mass of the means of production employed on the one hand, and the mass of labour necessary for their employment on the other.\" Here we meet one of the chapter's most important and most frequently misread conceptual distinctions. The value-composition is the ratio of constant to variable capital as expressed in value terms. The technical composition is the ratio in material terms — the actual physical quantity of machinery, raw material, and so forth relative to the number of workers employed. But these two are not identical, and Marx introduces a third, mediating concept: the organic composition . This is the value-composition \"in so far as it is determined by its technical composition and mirrors the changes in the latter.\" In other words, the organic composition is the value-composition understood in its causal dependence on the technical composition. When Marx says \"the composition of capital,\" without qualification, he means this organically determined composition. Why does this distinction matter? Because the value-composition can change for reasons unrelated to the technical composition (e.g., changes in the prices of raw materials or machinery), while the technical composition can change without a proportional change in value (because rising productivity cheapens means of production). The organic composition is the theoretically relevant variable because it captures the real causal connection between the developing productive forces and the demand for labour. Marx then introduces an aggregation principle. Individual capitals in a branch of production differ in composition. The average of these individual compositions gives the composition of the total capital in the branch. The average of all branch averages gives the composition of the total social capital of a country — \"and it is with this alone that we are concerned here in the final analysis.\" This move from individual to social capital is essential: surplus population and the wage relation are structural features of the social capital, not reducible to the fortunes of individual firms. The Basic Mechanism Under Constant Composition Marx posits accumulation as \"growth of capital.\" This implies growth of its variable constituent — the part invested in labour-power — because \"a part of the surplus-value which has been transformed into additional capital must always be re-transformed into variable capital, or additional labour fund.\" Assume now that the composition remains constant: a definite mass of means of production continues to need the same mass of labour-power. Under this assumption, \"the demand for labour, and the fund for the subsistence of the workers, both clearly increase in the same proportion as the capital, and with the same rapidity.\" Marx then elaborates a compounding dynamic. Capital produces surplus-value every year; part of this is added to the original capital; this increment itself grows yearly along with the augmentation of capital already functioning. Moreover, under conditions especially liable to stimulate accumulation — the opening of new markets, new spheres of investment from \"newly developed social requirements\" — the scale of accumulation can be suddenly extended merely by changing the proportion in which surplus-value is divided between capital and revenue. For all these reasons, \"the requirements of accumulating capital may exceed the growth in labour-power or in the number of workers; the demand for workers may outstrip the supply, and thus wages may rise.\" This is a striking concession to the classical economists. Marx grants that under constant composition, wages can rise. Indeed, he notes historical evidence: complaints were heard in England \"during the whole of the fifteenth century, and the first half of the eighteenth.\" This is the case of capital's expansive demand outrunning the available labour-power. Crucially, however, Marx immediately circumscribes the significance of this: \"The more or less favourable circumstances in which the wage-labourers support and multiply themselves in no way alter the fundamental character of capitalist production.\" Just as simple reproduction reproduces the capital-relation itself (capitalists on one side, wage-labourers on the other), so reproduction on an expanded scale — accumulation — reproduces the capital-relation on an expanded scale, with more or bigger capitalists at one pole and more wage-labourers at the other. The reproduction of labour-power — which \"must incessantly be re-incorporated into capital as its means of valorization, which cannot get free of capital, and whose enslavement to capital is only concealed by the variety of individual capitalists to whom it sells itself\" — is \"a factor in the reproduction of capital itself.\" Hence the iconic formulation: \"Accumulation of capital is therefore multiplication of the proletariat.\" Here Marx inserts a long, historically rich footnote (note 1) that repays close reading. He cites his own Wage Labour and Capital and quotes Colins: \"If the masses are all oppressed equally, the more proletarians a country has, the richer it will be.\" Marx defines \"proletarian,\" economically speaking, as \"nothing other than 'wage-labourer,' the man who produces and valorizes 'capital,' and is thrown onto the street as soon as he becomes superfluous to the need for valorization possessed by 'Monsieur Capital.'\" He then savagely dismisses Wilhelm Roscher's notion of \"the sickly proletarian of the primitive forest\": the primitive forester owns the forest and exploits it; he is not exploited by it. \"He is not, therefore, a proletarian. This would only be the case if the primitive forest exploited him, instead of being exploited by him.\" This is a methodological point of considerable importance: the proletarian condition is defined by social relations , not by poverty or health as such. Classical Political Economy on Accumulation as Proletarian Multiplication Marx notes that classical political economy grasped this fact \"so thoroughly\" that Adam Smith and Ricardo inaccurately identified accumulation with the consumption, by productive workers, of the whole capitalized part of the surplus product — i.e., with the transformation of surplus product into additional wage-labourers. Marx then marshals a series of quotable authorities. John Bellers (1696): \"if one had a hundred thousand acres of land and as many pounds in money, and as many cattle, without a labourer, what would the rich man be, but a labourer? And as the labourers make men rich, so the more labourers there will be, the more rich men.\" Bernard de Mandeville (early eighteenth century) achieves a brutally frank formulation: \"It would be easier, where property is well secured, to live without money than without poor; for who would do the work?\" Mandeville argues the poor \"ought to be kept from starving\" but \"should receive nothing worth saving\"; wants are the spur to industry, but \"too much will make him insolent and lazy.\" The \"surest wealth consists in a multitude of laborious poor\" — they are \"the never failing nursery of fleets and armies.\" And to keep them useful, \"it is requisite that great numbers of them should be ignorant as well as poor.\" Marx's critical observation: what Mandeville, \"an honest man with a clear mind,\" had not yet grasped is that \"the mechanism of the accumulation process itself not only increases the amount of capital but also the mass of the 'labouring poor,' i.e. the wage-labourers, who turn their labour-power into a force for increasing the valorization of the growing capital, and who are thereby compelled to make their relation of dependence on their own product, as personified in the capitalist, into an eternal relation.\" Sir F. M. Eden is then quoted at length ( The State of the Poor , 1797), drawing the distinction between those who \"toil and spin\" and those who \"can yet command the produce of industry\" by virtue of \"civil institutions\" that have recognized \"that individuals may acquire property by various other means besides the exertion of labour.\" Eden's key sentence: \"It is not the possession of land, or of money, but the command of labour which distinguishes the opulent from the labouring part of the community.\" Marx's marginal comment is devastating: Eden should have asked \"whose creatures 'the civil institutions' were.\" From Eden's standpoint — \"that of juridical illusion\" — the law is not a product of material relations of production but rather their cause. Marx juxtaposes Linguet, who refuted Montesquieu's \"esprit des lois\" with the aphorism: \"L'esprit des lois, c'est la propriété\" (\"The spirit of the laws is property\"). In a further note (note 6), Marx writes a polemical excursus on Malthus. The Essay on Population (1798), in its first form, is \"nothing more than a schoolboyish, superficial plagiarism\" of Defoe, Steuart, Townsend, Franklin, and Wallace, \"declaimed in the manner of a sermon, but not containing a single original proposition of Malthus himself.\" Its sensational success was due to its correspondence with \"the interests of a particular party\": in the context of the French Revolution's defenders in Britain, the \"principle of population\" was \"greeted jubilantly by the English oligarchy as the great destroyer of all hankerings after a progressive development of humanity.\" Marx notes the irony that Malthus, though an Anglican parson, had taken the \"monastic vow of celibacy\" (required for a Cambridge fellowship: \"Socios collegiorum maritos esse non permittimus...\" — \"We do not permit the Fellows of the Colleges to be married\"). He traces the \"principle of population\" to Protestant clerics — Bruckner, Wallace, Townsend, Malthus, Chalmers — and notes that earlier political economy was studied by philosophers (Hobbes, Locke, Hume), businessmen, statesmen, and medical men (Petty, Barbon, Mandeville, Quesnay). He also reproduces the orthodox attacks on Adam Smith for his friendship with Hume and his alleged propagation of atheism. The Nature of the Wage Rise: The \"Golden Chain\" and the Limits of Concession Returning to the main argument, Marx notes that under the assumed conditions — the most favourable to workers — their \"relation of dependence on capital takes on forms which are endurable or, as Eden says, 'easy and liberal.'\" Instead of becoming more intensive, dependence becomes more extensive : the sphere of exploitation extends with the dimensions of capital and with \"the number of people subjected to it.\" A larger part of the worker's own surplus product comes back as means of payment; workers can extend enjoyments, add to consumption, and lay by a small reserve. But: \"these things no more abolish the exploitation of the wage-labourer, and his situation of dependence, than do better clothing, food and treatment, and a larger peculium, in the case of the slave.\" The slave's peculium (the small property allowed a slave) is the precise and mordant analogy. A rise in the price of labour \"only means in fact that the length and weight of the golden chain the wage-labourer has already forged for himself allow it to be loosened somewhat.\" The essential point generally overlooked, Marx insists, is the differentia specifica of capitalist production. Labour-power is not purchased to satisfy the buyer's personal needs; the aim is \"the valorization of his capital, the production of commodities which contain more labour than he paid for, and therefore contain a portion of value which costs him nothing and is nevertheless realized through the sale of those commodities.\" The production of surplus-value is the \"absolute law\" of this mode of production. Labour-power can be sold only insofar as it \"preserves and maintains the means of production as capital, reproduces its own value as capital, and provides a source of additional capital in the shape of unpaid labour.\" Wages, by their very nature, imply that the worker will always provide a certain quantity of unpaid labour. Even at the best of times, an increase in wages means \"only a quantitative reduction in the amount of unpaid labour the worker has to supply\" — a reduction that \"can never go so far as to threaten the system itself.\" A footnote (note 7) reinforces this from John Wade: the limit to employment \"is the possibility of the employer realizing a profit\"; if wages reduce the master's gains below average profit, he will cease to employ workers \"or he will only employ them on condition of submission to a reduction of wages.\" The Two Alternatives and the Mathematical Formulation Marx then poses the crucial alternatives. Apart from violent conflicts over the wage rate (where Adam Smith already showed the master generally remains the master), a rise in the price of labour resulting from accumulation implies two scenarios: Either the price of labour keeps rising because its rise does not interfere with accumulation. Adam Smith is quoted: after profits are diminished, \"stock may not only continue to increase, but to increase much faster than before... A great stock, though with small profits, generally increases faster than a small stock with great profits.\" In this case a reduction in unpaid labour \"in no way interferes with the extension of the domain of capital.\" Or accumulation slackens because the stimulus of gain is blunted. The rate of accumulation lessens; but \"this means that the primary cause of that lessening itself vanishes, i.e. the disproportion between capital and exploitable labour-power. The mechanism of the capitalist production process removes the very obstacles it temporarily creates.\" The price of labour falls again to a level corresponding with capital's valorization requirements. The decisive theoretical insight: In the first case, it was not a diminished increase in labour-power or population that caused the excess of capital, but the converse — the increase in capital made exploitable labour-power insufficient. In the second case, it was not an increased rate of population growth that made capital insufficient, but the converse — the relative reduction of capital caused exploitable labour-power, or its price, to be in excess. Therefore: \"It is these absolute movements of the accumulation of capital which are reflected as relative movements of the mass of exploitable labour-power, and therefore seem produced by the latter's own independent movement.\" Marx then gives his famous mathematical formulation: \"the rate of accumulation is the independent, not the dependent variable; the rate of wages is the dependent, not the independent variable.\" This is the pivot of the entire anti-Malthusian argument. The classical economists and Malthusians invert this, treating population as the independent variable driving wages. Marx illustrates with an analogy to monetary phenomena: when a crisis produces a general fall in commodity prices, this is expressed as a rise in the relative value of money; in prosperity, a general price rise appears as a fall in money's relative value. The Currency School concludes from high prices that too much money circulates, from low prices too little; their ignorance \"is worthily paralleled by the economists, who interpret the above phenomena of accumulation by saying that in one case there are too few, and in the other, too many wage-labourers in existence.\" The methodological critique is that surface phenomena (apparent scarcity or abundance of workers) are the reflection of underlying absolute movements of capital, not their cause. The \"Natural Law of Population\" Dissolved Marx now distills the law of capitalist production that \"really lies at the basis of the supposed 'natural law of population'\": the relation between capital, accumulation, and the rate of wages \"is nothing other than the relation between the unpaid labour which has been transformed into capital and the additional paid labour necessary to set in motion this additional capital.\" It is not a relation between two mutually independent magnitudes — capital and the number of the working population — but \"at bottom, only the relation between the unpaid and the paid labour of the same working population.\" The mechanism: if the unpaid labour accumulated by the capitalist class increases so rapidly that its transformation into capital requires \"an extraordinary addition of paid labour,\" then wages rise and unpaid labour diminishes proportionally. \"But as soon as this diminution touches the point at which the surplus labour that nourishes capital is no longer supplied in normal quantity, a reaction sets in: a smaller part of revenue is capitalized, accumulation slows down, and the rising movement of wages comes up against an obstacle.\" Thus the rise of wages is \"confined within limits that not only leave intact the foundations of the capitalist system, but also secure its reproduction on an increasing scale.\" The law of capitalist accumulation, \"mystified by the economists into a supposed law of nature,\" in fact expresses that \"the very nature of accumulation excludes every diminution in the degree of exploitation of labour, and every rise in the price of labour, which could seriously imperil the continual reproduction, on an ever larger scale, of the capital-relation.\" The final rhetorical flourish: \"Just as man is governed, in religion, by the products of his own brain, so, in capitalist production, he is governed by the products of his own hand.\" A footnote from Von Thünen is quoted, acknowledging the riddle (\"How has the worker been able to pass from being the master of capital — as its creator — to being its slave?\") and Marx's verdict: \"His answer is simply childish.\" Section 2: A Relative Diminution of the Variable Part of Capital Occurs in the Course of Further Accumulation and Concentration The Turn from the Special to the General Case Section 2 effects the decisive theoretical transition. Marx notes that according to the economists themselves, it is neither the actual extent of social wealth nor the magnitude of capital already acquired that raises wages, but \"only the constant growth of accumulation and the degree of rapidity of that growth\" (citing Adam Smith, Book I, Chapter 8). \"So far, we have considered only one special phase of this process, that in which the increase of capital occurs while the technical composition of capital remains constant. But the process goes beyond this phase.\" The new force is the development of the productivity of social labour, which \"becomes the most powerful lever of accumulation.\" Adam Smith is quoted again: \"The same cause... which raises the wages of labour, the increase of stock, tends to increase its productive powers, and to make a smaller quantity of labour produce a greater quantity of work.\" Productivity, the Means of Production, and Their Double Role Marx analyzes what determines the level of social productivity. Apart from natural conditions (soil fertility) and the skill of independent producers, \"the level of the social productivity of labour is expressed in the relative extent of the means of production that one worker, during a given time, with the same degree of intensity of labour-power, turns into products.\" The mass of means of production with which the worker functions increases with productivity. These means play a double role . The increase of some is a consequence of rising productivity (e.g., more raw material worked up in the same time due to the division of labour and machinery); the increase of others is a condition of rising productivity (machinery, beasts of burden, mineral manures, drain-pipes, buildings, furnaces, means of transport). But whether condition or consequence, \"the growing extent of the means of production, as compared with the labour-power incorporated into them, is an expression of the growing productivity of labour.\" The increase of productivity therefore appears \"in the diminution of the mass of labour in proportion to the mass of means of production moved by it, or in the diminution of the subjective factor of the labour process as compared with the objective factor.\" This is the technical-composition change. It is reflected in the value-composition by \"the increase of the constant constituent of capital at the expense of its variable constituent.\" Marx gives an illustrative example: originally 50 per cent of capital in means of production and 50 per cent in labour-power; later, with developing productivity, 80 per cent in means of production and 20 per cent in labour-power. He asserts that \"this law of the progressive growth of the constant part of capital in comparison with the variable part is confirmed at every step... by the comparative analysis of the prices of commodities, whether we compare different economic epochs or different nations in the same epoch.\" The relative magnitude of the constant part of price is \"in direct proportion to the progress of accumulation,\" while that of the variable part is \"in inverse proportion.\" The Critical Distinction Between Value and Mass Marx then introduces a refinement of major theoretical importance: this diminution of the variable part relative to the constant part, \"or, in other words, this change in the composition of the value of the capital, provides only an approximate indication of the change in the composition of its material constituents.\" The reason is that with increasing productivity, the mass of means of production consumed by labour increases, \"but their value in comparison with their mass diminishes.\" The value of means of production rises absolutely, but not in proportion to the increase in their mass. Therefore \"the increase of the difference between constant and variable capital is therefore much less than that of the difference between the mass of the means of production into which the constant capital, and the mass of the labour-power into which the variable capital, is converted.\" The value-difference grows with the mass-difference, but in a smaller degree. This matters because it prevents the erroneous inference that the value-ratio fully captures the real displacement of labour. Marx then guards against another misunderstanding: the relative diminution of variable capital \"by no means thereby excludes the possibility of a rise in its absolute magnitude.\" He gives a numerical example: a capital-value divided initially 50% constant/50% variable, later 80%/20%. If the original capital of £6,000 has grown to £18,000, its variable constituent has also increased, from £3,000 to £3,600 — a 20% increase. \"But whereas formerly an increase of capital by 20 per cent would have sufficed to raise the demand for labour by 20 per cent, now the original capital needs to be tripled to secure an increase of 20 per cent in the demand for labour.\" This is the quantitative expression of the deteriorating position of labour: the same rate of accumulation yields a diminishing rate of labour absorption. The Reciprocity of Accumulation and the Capitalist Mode of Production Marx then recapitulates the argument of Part IV to establish the presuppositions of rising productivity. The development of social productivity presupposes co-operation on a large scale , the division and combination of labour on that basis, economizing of means of production by concentration, instruments usable only in common (systems of machinery), gigantic natural forces pressed into service, and the transformation of the production process into \"a process of the technological application of scientific knowledge.\" Where production is commodity production — means of production privately owned — co-operation on a large scale \"can be realized only through the increase of individual capitals, only in proportion as the social means of production and subsistence are transformed into the private property of capitalists.\" Large-scale production can therefore occur only in capitalist form. A certain accumulation of capital in individual hands is \"the necessary pre-condition for a specifically capitalist mode of production.\" This is called primitive accumulation [ ursprüngliche Akkumulation ], \"because it is the historical basis, instead of the historical result, of specifically capitalist production.\" How it originates is postponed — but the point is that it forms the starting-point. The crucial reciprocity: all methods for raising social productivity that grow up on this basis \"are at the same time methods for the increased production of surplus-value or surplus product, which is in its turn the formative element of accumulation.\" They are \"methods for the production of capital by capital, or methods for its accelerated accumulation.\" The continual reconversion of surplus-value into capital appears as the increasing magnitude of capital entering production; this is the basis of an extended scale of production, of methods for raising productivity, and of accelerated surplus-value production. \"If, therefore, a certain degree of accumulation of capital appears as a pre-condition for the specifically capitalist mode of production, the latter reacts back to cause an accelerated accumulation of capital.\" These two factors \"bring about, in the compound ratio of the impulses they give to each other,\" the change in the technical composition by which the variable component becomes smaller and smaller relative to the constant. Concentration and Centralization — A Crucial Theoretical Distinction Marx now enters the conceptual heart of Section 2: the distinction between concentration and centralization , which is frequently conflated in secondary literature but is rigorously separated here. Concentration (or the \"concentration which grows directly out of accumulation\") refers to the increase in the size of individual capitals as a direct result of accumulation — the growth of each capital as an aliquot part of the growing social capital. Every individual capital is \"a larger or smaller concentration of means of production, with a corresponding command over a larger or smaller army of workers.\" Every accumulation becomes the means of new accumulation. With increasing mass of wealth functioning as capital, accumulation increases the concentration of that wealth in individual hands, widening the basis of large-scale production. Accumulation increases the number of capitalists (through the division of property within capitalist families, offshoots splitting off from original capitals). Two features characterize this concentration: first, it is limited by the degree of increase of social wealth ; second, the part of social capital in each sphere is divided among many competing capitalists. Hence accumulation presents itself \"on the one hand as increasing concentration of the means of production, and of the command over labour; and on the other hand as repulsion of many individual capitals from one another.\" Centralization is a distinct process. It is the \"attraction of capitals,\" which \"no longer means the simple concentration of the means of production and the command over labour, which is identical with accumulation.\" It is \"concentration of capitals already formed, destruction of their individual independence, expropriation of capitalist by capitalist, transformation of many small into few large capitals.\" It presupposes only \"a change in the distribution of already available and already functioning capital.\" Its field of action is therefore not limited by the absolute growth of social wealth . \"Capital grows to a huge mass in a single hand in one place, because it has been lost by many in another place.\" This is centralization proper, \"as distinct from accumulation and concentration.\" The laws of centralization cannot be fully developed here, but Marx gives \"a few brief factual indications.\" The battle of competition is fought by cheapening commodities; cheapness depends on productivity of labour, which depends on the scale of production. \"Therefore the larger capitals beat the smaller.\" With developing capitalist production, the minimum amount of capital necessary to carry on a business under normal conditions increases. Smaller capitals crowd into spheres large-scale industry has taken only sporadically or incompletely. \"Here competition rages in direct proportion to the number, and in inverse proportion to the magnitude, of the rival capitals. It always ends in the ruin of many small capitalists, whose capitals partly pass into the hands of their conquerors, and partly vanish completely.\" Then comes a crucial elaboration on the credit system , inserted by Engels in the fourth German edition (replacing a passage written by Marx and retained in the first three German editions, which is reproduced in the footnote). In its first stages, credit \"furtively creeps in as the humble assistant of accumulation, drawing into the hands of individual or associated capitalists by invisible threads the money resources which lie scattered... over the surface of society; but it soon becomes a new and terrible weapon in the battle of competition and is finally transformed into an enormous social mechanism for the centralization of capitals.\" The older Marxian passage (in the footnote) adds that centralization \"shortens and quickens the transformation of separate processes of production into processes socially combined and carried out on a large scale\"; the increasing bulk of individual masses of capital \"becomes the material basis of an uninterrupted revolution in the mode of production itself.\" It discusses the \"metamorphosis of old capital\" — how the change of composition gets a grip on old capital as it reaches the term of its reproduction period. A steam plough is \"an incomparably more efficient means of production than an ordinary plough, but the capital-value laid out in it is an incomparably smaller means for employing men than if it were laid out in ordinary ploughs.\" Marx then notes that with developing capitalist production there also develops \"the two most powerful levers of centralization — competition and credit.\" The progress of accumulation increases the material amenable to centralization, while the expansion of capitalist production creates both the social need and the technical means for the immense undertakings requiring prior centralization. The advance of centralization \"does not depend in any way on a positive growth in the magnitude of social capital,\" and this distinguishes it from concentration, \"the latter being only another name for reproduction on an extended scale.\" Centralization may result from \"a mere change in the distribution of already existing capitals.\" Its extreme limit in a branch of industry would be the fusion of all individual capitals into a single capital; in society, the entire social capital united in the hands of a single capitalist or company. Engels adds a note (note 10) that \"the latest English and American 'trusts' are already striving to attain this goal.\" Centralization \"supplements the work of accumulation by enabling industrial capitalists to extend the scale of their operations.\" Whether accomplished by \"the violent method of annexation\" or \"the smoother process of organizing joint-stock companies,\" the economic effect is the same: the increased scale of establishments is the starting-point for more comprehensive organization of collective labour, for progressive transformation of isolated processes into \"socially combined and scientifically arranged processes of production.\" Then a vivid illustration: \"The world would still be without railways if it had had to wait until accumulation had got a few individual capitals far enough to be adequate for the construction of a railway. Centralization, however, accomplished this in the twinkling of an eye, by means of joint-stock companies.\" Centralization intensifies and accelerates the effects of accumulation while simultaneously extending and speeding up \"those revolutions in the technical composition of capital which raise its constant portion at the expense of its variable portion, thus diminishing the relative demand for labour.\" The Renewal of Old Capital and the Dual Reduction of Demand The additional capitals formed in the normal course of accumulation \"serve above all as vehicles for the exploitation of new inventions and discoveries, and industrial improvements in general.\" But in time the old capital itself \"reaches the point where it has to be renewed in all its aspects, a time when it sheds its skin and is reborn like the other capitals in a perfected technical shape, in which a smaller quantity of labour will suffice to set in motion a larger quantity of machinery and raw material.\" The absolute reduction in demand for labour \"is obviously so much the greater, the higher the degree to which the capitals undergoing this process of renewal are already massed together by virtue of the movement towards centralization.\" The double result: \"On the one hand, therefore, the additional capital formed in the course of further accumulation attracts fewer and fewer workers in proportion to its magnitude. On the other hand, the old capital periodically reproduced with a new composition repels more and more of the workers formerly employed by it.\" This is the theoretical engine of the industrial reserve army, fully assembled. Section 3: The Progressive Production of a Relative Surplus Population or Industrial Reserve Army The Acceleration of Compositional Change Section 3 opens by recapitulating the movement thus far: accumulation, \"which originally appeared only as its quantitative extension, comes to fruition, as we have seen, through a progressive qualitative change in its composition, i.e. through a continuing increase of its constant component at the expense of its variable component.\" An editorial note by Engels (note 11) preserves a marginal note of Marx: \"if the extension is only quantitative, then for a greater and a smaller capital in the same branch of business the profits are as the magnitudes of the capitals advanced. If the quantitative extension induces a qualitative change, then the rate of profit on the larger capital rises at the same time.\" This anticipates Volume 3's analysis of the profit rate. Marx emphasizes that the specifically capitalist mode of production, the development of productivity, and the resulting change in organic composition \"do not merely keep pace with the progress of accumulation, or the growth of social wealth. They develop at a much quicker rate,\" because simple accumulation is accompanied by centralization and because the change in composition of additional capital goes hand in hand with a similar change in the composition of the original capital. The numerical progression: \"If it was originally say 1:1, it now becomes successively 2:1, 3:1, 4:1, 5:1, 7:1, etc., so that as the capital grows, instead of 1/2 its total value, only 1/3, 1/4, 1/5, 1/6, 1/8, etc. is turned into labour-power, and, on the other hand, 2/3, 3/4, 4/5, 5/6, 7/8, into means of production.\" Since the demand for labour is determined \"not by the extent of the total capital but by its variable constituent alone, that demand falls progressively with the growth of the total capital, instead of rising in proportion to it, as was previously assumed.\" It falls relatively to the magnitude of total capital, \"and at an accelerated rate, as this magnitude increases.\" The Inversion: Relative Diminution Presented as Absolute Increase Marx then describes the dialectical inversion that is one of the chapter's most famous passages. The intermediate pauses in which accumulation works as simple extension on a given technical basis are shortened. An accelerated accumulation of total capital, \"accelerated in a constantly growing progression,\" is needed to absorb an additional number of workers — or even to keep employed those already functioning (owing to the constant metamorphosis of old capital). This increasing accumulation and centralization becomes \"in its turn a source of new changes in the composition of capital, or in other words of an accelerated diminution of the capital's variable component, as compared with its constant one.\" Then the inversion: \"This accelerated relative diminution of the variable component, which accompanies the accelerated increase of the total capital and moves more rapidly than this increase, takes the inverse form, at the other pole, of an apparently absolute increase in the working population, an increase which always moves more rapidly than that of the variable capital or the means of employment.\" But in fact \"it is capitalist accumulation itself that constantly produces, and produces indeed in direct relation with its own energy and extent, a relatively redundant working population, i.e. a population which is superfluous to capital's average requirements for its own valorization, and is therefore a surplus population.\" This is the definition of the relative surplus population : not absolutely superfluous (those workers are needed in other contexts, or would be needed if labour were rationally distributed), but superfluous relative to capital's average valorization requirements at a given moment . The Differentiation Across Spheres and the Law of Population Marx then notes that considering the total social capital, accumulation \"sometimes causes periodic changes, and at other times distributes various phases simultaneously over the different spheres of production.\" In some spheres, composition changes without absolute increase (simple concentration); in others, absolute growth is connected with absolute diminution of the variable component; in others, capital grows on its existing technical basis and attracts additional labour-power. \"In all spheres, the increase of the variable part of the capital, and therefore of the number of workers employed by it, is always connected with violent fluctuations and the temporary production of a surplus population,\" whether in the striking form of extrusion of workers or the less evident form of greater difficulty in absorbing additional population. The scale and interdependence of these movements intensify: with the magnitude of functioning social capital, the extension of production, the mass of workers set in motion, the development of productivity — \"there is also an extension of the scale on which greater attraction of workers by capital is accompanied by their greater repulsion.\" Hence: \"The working population therefore produces both the accumulation of capital and the means by which it is itself made relatively superfluous; and it does this to an extent which is always increasing.\" This yields a genuinely revolutionary claim: this is \"a law of population peculiar to the capitalist mode of production; and in fact every particular historical mode of production has its own special laws of population, which are historically valid within that particular sphere. An abstract law of population exists only for plants and animals, and even then only in the absence of any historical intervention by man.\" This is the direct refutation of Malthus: there is no universal natural law of population; there are historically specific laws. To support this empirically, Marx provides a footnote (note 12) with English census data showing falling employment in many branches (agriculture down from 2,011,447 in 1851 to 1,924,110 in 1861; worsted manufacture down from 102,714 to 79,242; silk weaving from 111,940 to 101,678; hat-making from 15,957 to 13,814; comb-making from 2,038 to 1,478) alongside increases in cotton (371,777 to 456,646) and coal-mining (183,389 to 246,613). The census itself notes: \"The increase of labourers is generally greatest, since 1851, in those branches of industry in which machinery has not up to the present been employed with success.\" Footnote 13 (added by Engels from the French edition) is a rich survey of classical economists who \"suspect rather than understand\" the law of the progressive diminution of variable capital. John Barton is credited with the greatest merit (though he confuses constant with fixed and variable with circulating capital). Barton: \"The demand for labour depends on the increase of circulating, and not of fixed capital... the whole of the annual savings, added to the fixed capital, would have no effect in increasing the demand for labour.\" Ricardo: \"The same cause which may increase the net revenue of the country may at the same time render the population redundant, and deteriorate the condition of the labourer.\" And with increasing capital, \"the demand [for labour] will be in a diminishing ratio.\" Richard Jones: \"Great fluctuations in the amount of employment, and great suffering may become more frequent as capital itself becomes more plentiful.\" Ramsay: \"Every augmentation... in the national stock destined for reproduction, comes, in the progress of society, to have less and less influence upon the condition of the labourer.\" The Reserve Army as Lever and Condition of Accumulation Marx now develops the positive function of the surplus population — its role as a lever of accumulation, not merely a by-product. \"It forms a disposable industrial reserve army, which belongs to capital just as absolutely as if the latter had bred it at its own cost.\" Independently of the actual increase of population, it creates \"a mass of human material always ready for exploitation by capital in the interests of capital's own changing valorization requirements.\" With accumulation and the development of productivity, capital's \"power of sudden expansion also grows.\" This growing elasticity has multiple sources: the elasticity of already-functioning capital; the expansion of absolute social wealth (of which capital forms an elastic part); credit, which \"under every special stimulus, at once places an unusual part of this wealth at the disposal of production in the form of additional capital\"; and the technical conditions of production (machinery, means of transport) that \"now make possible a very rapid transformation of masses of surplus product into additional means of production.\" The mass of social wealth \"thrusts itself frantically into old branches of production, whose market suddenly expands, or into newly formed branches, such as railways.\" In all such cases, \"there must be the possibility of suddenly throwing great masses of men into the decisive areas without doing any damage to the scale of production in other spheres. The surplus population supplies these masses.\" Thus the industrial reserve army is not merely a consequence of accumulation but a condition of the specifically cyclical path of modern industry: \"a decennial cycle (interrupted by smaller oscillations) of periods of average activity, production at high pressure, crisis, and stagnation, depends on the constant formation, the greater or less absorption, and the re-formation of the industrial reserve army or surplus population.\" And conversely: \"the varying phases of the industrial cycle recruit the surplus population, and become one of the most energetic agencies for its reproduction.\" Marx contrasts this with the infancy of capitalist production, when composition changed only gradually, so that proportional demand for labour roughly corresponded to accumulation. Advance was slow, \"yet it came up against a natural barrier in the shape of the exploitable working population; this barrier could only be swept away by the violent means we shall discuss later.\" The expansion by fits and starts is precondition for sudden contraction; the latter evokes the former; but the former \"is impossible without disposable human material, without an increase in the number of workers, which must occur independently of the absolute growth of the population.\" This increase is effected by \"the simple process that constantly 'sets free' a part of the working class; by methods which lessen the number of workers employed in proportion to the increased production.\" Hence: \"Modern industry's whole form of motion therefore depends on the constant transformation of a part of the working population into unemployed or semi-employed 'hands.'\" The Superficiality of Political Economy and the Credit Illusion Marx then attacks \"the superficiality of political economy\" for viewing the expansion and contraction of credit as the cause of periodic alternations, \"whereas it is a mere symptom of them.\" He offers an analogy to celestial mechanics: \"Just as the heavenly bodies always repeat a certain movement, once they have been flung into it, so also does social production, once it has been flung into this movement of alternate expansion and contraction. Effects become causes in their turn, and the various vicissitudes of the whole process, which always reproduces its own conditions, take on the form of periodicity.\" Once periodicity is consolidated, even political economy sees \"that the production of a relative surplus population... is a necessary condition for modern industry.\" The French edition inserts a substantial passage (in the footnote) dating the modern industrial cycle: it required mechanical industry to strike deep roots, foreign trade to predominate, the world market to annex the New World, Asia, and Australia, and a sufficient number of industrial nations to enter the arena. The cycles embrace years and culminate in a general crisis. \"Until now the duration of these cycles has been ten or eleven years, but there is no reason to consider this duration as constant. On the contrary, we ought to conclude... that the duration is variable, and that the length of the cycles will gradually diminish.\" The Testimony of Apologists — Merivale and Malthus H. Merivale (former Professor of Political Economy at Oxford, later at the Colonial Office) is quoted: if a nation were to emigrate \"some hundreds of thousands of superfluous arms,\" at the first returning demand for labour there would be a deficiency, since \"it takes, at all events, the space of a generation to replace the loss of adult labour.\" Manufacturers need \"hands ready by them,\" able to increase and slacken activity according to the market. Even Malthus, in his \"narrow fashion,\" recognizes that a surplus population is a necessity of modern industry — though he explains it not by relative superfluity but by \"excessive growth.\" Malthus is quoted: prudential habits might injure a manufacturing country; \"a country is always liable to an increase in the quantity of the funds for the maintenance of labour faster than the increase of population.\" Marx's sarcasm is scathing: political economy, having \"declared that the constant production of a relative surplus population of workers is a necessity of capitalist accumulation, very aptly adopts the shape of an old maid\" and puts into capital's mouth the words: \"We manufacturers do what we can for you, whilst we are increasing that capital on which you must subsist, and you must do the rest by accommodating your numbers to the means of subsistence.\" Malthus's \"beautiful trinity\" — over-production, over-population, and over-consumption — are \"three very delicate monsters, indeed!\" The Intensive and Extensive Exploitation of Labour-Power Marx then qualifies the earlier assumption that increase or diminution of variable capital corresponds precisely with increase or diminution of the number of workers employed. \"The number of workers under the command of capital may remain the same, or even fall, while the variable capital increases.\" This occurs if the individual worker provides more labour, so that wages increase although the price of labour remains the same or falls — only more slowly than the mass of labour rises. \"Increase of variable capital, in this case, becomes an index of more labour, but not of more workers employed.\" Marx identifies a structural motive: \"It is the absolute interest of every capitalist to extort a given quantity of labour out of a smaller rather than a greater number of workers, if the cost is about the same.\" In the latter case, outlay of constant capital increases proportionally to the mass of labour; in the former, the increase is much smaller. \"The more extended the scale of production, the more decisive is this motive. Its force increases with the accumulation of capital.\" This leads to the analysis of how the same variable capital sets more labour in motion: 1. Through greater extensive or intensive exploitation of each individual labour-power; 2. By buying a greater mass of labour-power through progressively replacing skilled workers by less skilled, mature by immature, male by female, adults by young persons or children. The triple result: with accumulation, a larger variable capital sets more labour in motion without enlisting more workers; a variable capital of the same magnitude sets more labour in motion with the same mass of labour-power; and a greater number of inferior labour-powers is set in motion by displacement of more skilled labour-powers. The Acceleration of Surplus Population Production and the \"Over-work/Idleness\" Dialectic Marx then draws a striking conclusion: \"The production of a relative surplus population, or the setting free of workers, therefore proceeds still more rapidly than the technical transformation of the process of production that accompanies the advance of accumulation and is accelerated by it, and more rapidly than the corresponding diminution of the variable part of capital as compared with the constant.\" The mechanism is now fully dialectical: \"The over-work of the employed part of the working class swells the ranks of its reserve, while, conversely, the greater pressure that the reserve by its competition exerts on the employed workers forces them to submit to over-work and subjects them to the dictates of capital.\" Thus: \"The condemnation of one part of the working class to enforced idleness by the over-work of the other part, and vice versa, becomes a means of enriching the individual capitalists, and accelerates at the same time the production of the industrial reserve army on a scale corresponding with the progress of social accumulation.\" A footnote (note 17) cites a Blackburn pamphlet from the cotton famine of 1863, denouncing over-work resulting from the Factory Act: adult operatives asked to work 12 to 13 hours per day \"while there are hundreds who are compelled to be idle who would willingly work partial time.\" The pamphlet asks whether overtime is \"likely to create a good feeling between masters and servants.\" Marx also quotes the Essay on Trade and Commerce (1770), which grasps \"with unerring bourgeois instinct\" that the relative surplus population emboldens employed workers: \"Whenever from an extraordinary demand for manufactures, labour grows scarce, the labourers feel their own consequence, and will make their masters feel it likewise.\" Marx's dry comment: \"Those fellows were actually asking for a wage-increase!\" The General Movement of Wages and the Refutation of the Dogma Marx then states the general conclusion: \"Taking them as a whole, the general movements of wages are exclusively regulated by the expansion and contraction of the industrial reserve army, and this in turn corresponds to the periodic alternations of the industrial cycle.\" Wages are \"not therefore determined by the variations of the absolute numbers of the working population, but by the varying proportions in which the working class is divided into an active army and a reserve army, by the increase or diminution in the relative amount of the surplus population, by the extent to which it is alternately absorbed and set free.\" The appropriate law for modern industry is \"the law of the regulation of the demand and supply of labour by the alternate expansion and contraction of capital, i.e. by the level of capital's valorization requirements at the relevant moment.\" Marx then states the economic-fiction argument in detail. According to the economists, wages rise as a result of accumulation; higher wages stimulate population growth until the labour-market is over-supplied and capital becomes insufficient; wages fall; population is decimated by falling wages; capital again in excess; and so on. \"This would indeed be a beautiful form of motion for developed capitalist production! Before the rise in wages could produce any positive increase of the population really fit for work, the deadline would long since have passed within which the industrial campaign would have to have been carried through, and the battle fought to a conclusive finish.\" The empirical refutation: Between 1849 and 1859 a rise of wages, practically merely nominal (accompanied by falling corn prices), took place in English agricultural districts. In Wiltshire, weekly wages rose from 7s. to 8s.; in Dorsetshire from 7s. or 8s. to 9s. Marx notes the statistical illusion: \"The lower the wage, the higher is the proportion in which even a very insignificant increase is expressed.\" A 20s. wage rising to 22s. is a 10% rise; a 7s. wage rising to 9s. is 28½% — \"which sounds very fine.\" The Economist prattled about \"a general and substantial advance.\" But what did the farmers do? \"No, they introduced more machinery, and in a moment the labourers were 'redundant' again to a degree satisfactory even to the farmers. There was now 'more capital' laid out in agriculture than before, and in a more productive form. With this the demand for labour fell, not only relatively, but absolutely.\" Confusion of General Wage Laws with Local Distribution Marx distinguishes the laws regulating the general movement of wages (the ratio between the total labour-power and the total social capital) from the laws distributing the working population over different spheres. If accumulation is especially active in a special sphere and attracts additional capital, demand for labour rises and wages rise. Higher wages draw a larger part of the working population into the favoured sphere until it is glutted, and wages fall back to average or below. The influx ceases and gives place to an outflow. \"Here the political economist thinks he can grasp the situation... But he really sees only the local oscillations of the labour-market in a particular sphere of production.\" The Reserve Army as the Context of the Supply-and-Demand Law Marx now states the decisive conclusion about the labour market: \"The industrial reserve army, during the periods of stagnation and average prosperity, weighs down the active army of workers; during the periods of over-production and feverish activity, it puts a curb on their pretensions. The relative surplus population is therefore the background against which the law of the demand and supply of labour does its work. It confines the field of action of this law to the limits absolutely convenient to capital's drive to exploit and dominate the workers.\" The \"Compensation\" Theory Demolished Marx returns to \"one of the great exploits of economic apologetics.\" When machinery transforms variable into constant capital, \"the economic apologist interprets this operation, which 'fixes' capital and by that very act 'sets free' workers, in exactly the opposite way, pretending that capital is thereby set free for the workers.\" Marx exposes the effrontery: not only are the workers turned out by machines set free, \"but so are their future replacements in the rising generation, as well as the additional contingent which, with the usual extension of business on its old basis, would regularly be absorbed.\" These are all \"set free\" and every new bit of capital can take advantage of them. \"Whether it attracts them or others, the effect on the general demand for labour will be nil, if this capital is just sufficient to take out of the market as many workers as the machines threw into it.\" The impulse additional capital would otherwise give to the general demand for labour \"is therefore in every case neutralized until the supply of workers thrown out of employment by the machine has been exhausted.\" Hence: capital acts on both sides at once. If accumulation increases the demand for labour, it also increases the supply of workers by \"setting them free,\" while the pressure of the unemployed compels the employed \"to furnish more labour, and therefore makes the supply of labour to a certain extent independent of the supply of workers.\" The movement of the law of supply and demand \"completes the despotism of capital.\" The Political Conclusion: Unions, the \"Sacred Law,\" and the Colonies Marx then reaches the political implications. As soon as workers learn \"the secret of why it happens that the more they work, the more alien wealth they produce, and that the more the productivity of their labour increases, the more does their very function as a means for the valorization of capital become precarious\"; as soon as they discover that \"the degree of intensity of the competition amongst themselves depends wholly on the pressure of the relative surplus population\"; as soon as, by setting up trade unions, \"they try to organize planned co-operation between the employed and the unemployed in order to obviate or to weaken the ruinous effects of this natural law of capitalist production on their class,\" — \"so soon does capital and its sycophant, political economy, cry out at the infringement of the 'eternal' and so to speak 'sacred' law of supply and demand.\" But conversely: \"as soon as (in the colonies, for example) adverse circumstances prevent the creation of an industrial reserve army, and with it the absolute dependence of the working class upon the capitalist class, capital, along with its platitudinous Sancho Panza, rebels against the 'sacred' law of supply and demand, and tries to make up for its inadequacies by forcible means.\" This is a key dialectical point: capital invokes the \"law\" only when it serves capital's interests; when the reserve army is absent, capital abandons the law and resorts to force. (This anticipates Marx's later analysis of Wakefield's colonization theory, alluded to via the reference to the colonies.) Section 4: Different Forms of Existence of the Relative Surplus Population — The General Law of Capitalist Accumulation The Three Permanent Forms Marx opens Section 4 by noting that \"the relative surplus population exists in all kinds of forms. Every worker belongs to it during the time when he is only partially employed or wholly unemployed.\" Leaving aside the large-scale, periodically recurring forms impressed by the industrial cycle — acute in crises, chronic in slack times — there are three forms it always possesses: the floating , the latent , and the stagnant . The floating form exists in \"the centres of modern industry — factories, workshops, ironworks, mines, etc.\" Here workers are \"sometimes repelled, sometimes attracted again in greater masses, so that the number of those employed increases on the whole, although in a constantly decreasing proportion to the scale of production.\" Marx then makes a striking observation about age : in factories and large workshops, \"large numbers of male workers are employed up to the age of maturity, but not beyond.\" Once mature, only a small number continue to find employment; \"the majority are regularly dismissed. This majority forms an element of the floating surplus population, which grows with the extension of those branches of industry.\" Some emigrate, following capital that has itself emigrated. A further consequence: \"the female population grows more rapidly than the male — witness England.\" The contradiction: \"That the natural increase of the number of workers does not satisfy the requirements of the accumulation of capital, and yet, at the same time, exceeds those requirements, is a contradiction inherent in capital's very movement. Capital demands more youthful workers, fewer adults.\" This contradiction is \"no more glaring than the other contradiction, namely that a shortage of 'hands' is complained of, while, at the same time, many thousands are out of work, because the division of labour chains them to a particular branch of industry.\" A footnote (note 19) cites the Factory Report of 1866: \"It does not appear absolutely true to say that demand will always produce supply just at the moment when it is needed. It has not always done so with labour, for much machinery has been idle last year for want of hands.\" This is a direct empirical refutation of Say's Law as applied to labour. Marx then discusses the rapid consumption of labour-power: \"the worker has already more or less completely lived himself out when he is only half-way through his life. He falls into the ranks of the surplus population, or is thrust down from a higher to a lower step in the scale.\" The mortality statistics: Dr Lee of Manchester found the average age at death of the Manchester upper middle class was 38 years, of the labouring class 17; at Liverpool, 35 against 15. \"It thus appeared that the well-to-do classes had a lease of life which was more than double the value of that which fell to the lot of the less favoured citizens.\" Under these circumstances, the absolute increase of this section of the proletariat \"must take a form which swells their numbers, despite the rapid wastage of their individual elements. Hence, rapid replacement of one generation of workers by another (this law does not hold for the other classes of the population). This social requirement is met by early marriages... and by the premium that the exploitation of the workers' children sets on their production.\" The latent form. As soon as capitalist production takes possession of agriculture, \"the demand for a rural working population falls absolutely, while the accumulation of the capital employed in agriculture advances, without this repulsion being compensated for by a greater attraction of workers, as is the case in non-agricultural industries.\" Part of the agricultural population is \"constantly on the point of passing over into an urban or manufacturing proletariat, and on the look-out for opportunities to complete this transformation.\" There is thus \"a constant flow from this source of the relative surplus population. But the constant movement towards the towns presupposes, in the countryside itself, a constant latent surplus population.\" The wages of the agricultural labourer are therefore \"reduced to a minimum, and he always stands with one foot already in the swamp of pauperism.\" A footnote (note 21) provides census data: in 1861, 781 towns contained 10,960,998 inhabitants, villages and country parishes 9,105,226. Between 1851 and 1861, country population increased half a million, town population by one and a half million (1,554,067). Country parishes grew 6.5%, towns 17.3%. \"Three-fourths of the total increase of population has taken place in the towns.\" The stagnant form. \"This forms a part of the active labour army, but with extremely irregular employment. Hence it offers capital an inexhaustible reservoir of disposable labour-power. Its conditions of life sink below the average normal level of the working class, and it is precisely this which makes it a broad foundation for special branches of capitalist exploitation. It is characterized by a maximum of working time and a minimum of wages.\" Its chief form is \"domestic industry.\" It is constantly recruited from workers in large-scale industry and agriculture who have become redundant, \"and especially from those decaying branches of industry where handicraft is giving way to manufacture, and manufacture to machinery.\" Its extent grows with accumulation. It \"forms at the same time a self-reproducing and self-perpetuating element of the working class, taking a proportionally greater part in the general increase of that class than the other elements.\" Marx adds a crucial demographic law: \"not only the number of births and deaths, but the absolute size of families, stands in inverse proportion to the level of wages, and therefore to the amount of the means of subsistence at the disposal of different categories of worker.\" This law \"would sound absurd to savages, or even to civilized colonists. It calls to mind the boundless reproduction of animals individually weak and constantly hunted down.\" A footnote (note 22) quotes Adam Smith (\"Poverty seems favourable to generation\") and Galiani (\"God has decreed that the men who carry on the most useful crafts should be born in abundant numbers\"). Pauperism: The Lowest Sediment \"Finally, the lowest sediment of the relative surplus population dwells in the sphere of pauperism.\" Apart from vagabonds, criminals, prostitutes — \"the actual lumpenproletariat\" — this stratum consists of three categories: 1. Those able to work. The statistics of English pauperism show the quantity of paupers increases with every crisis and diminishes with every revival. 2. Orphans and pauper children. \"Candidates for the industrial reserve army,\" enrolled in times of prosperity. 3. The demoralized, the ragged, and those unable to work — chiefly people who succumb to \"incapacity for adaptation\" resulting from the division of labour; those who have lived beyond the average life-span; and \"the victims of industry, whose number increases with the growth of dangerous machinery, of mines, chemical works, etc., the mutilated, the sickly, the widows, etc.\" Marx's formula: \"Pauperism is the hospital of the active labour-army and the dead weight of the industrial reserve army.\" Its production is included in that of the relative surplus population; its necessity is implied by theirs; along with the surplus population, \"pauperism forms a condition of capitalist production, and of the capitalist development of wealth.\" It forms part of the faux frais of capitalist production — \"but capital usually knows how to transfer these from its own shoulders to those of the working class and the petty bourgeoisie.\" The Absolute General Law Stated Now Marx states the law in its full form. It is a series of escalating proportions: \"The greater the social wealth, the functioning capital, the extent and energy of its growth, and therefore also the greater the absolute mass of the proletariat and the productivity of its labour, the greater is the industrial reserve army.\" \"The same causes which develop the expansive power of capital, also develop the labour-power at its disposal. The relative mass of the industrial reserve army thus increases with the potential energy of wealth.\" \"But the greater this reserve army in proportion to the active labour-army, the greater is the mass of a consolidated surplus population, whose misery is in inverse ratio to the amount of torture it has to undergo in the form of labour.\" \"The more extensive, finally, the pauperized sections of the working class and the industrial reserve army, the greater is official pauperism.\" \"This is the absolute general law of capitalist accumulation.\" And immediately: \"Like all other laws, it is modified in its working by many circumstances, the analysis of which does not concern us here.\" The political conclusion: \"We can now understand the foolishness of the economic wisdom which preaches to the workers that they should adapt their numbers to the valorization requirements of capital. The mechanism of capitalist production and accumulation itself constantly effects this adjustment. The first word of this adaptation is the creation of a relative surplus population, or industrial reserve army. Its last word is the misery of constantly expanding strata of the active army of labour, and the dead weight of pauperism.\" The Dialectical Inversion Stated Marx then states the inversion in its most explicit form. On the basis of capitalism — \"a system in which the worker does not employ the means of production, but the means of production employ the worker\" — the law by which an increasing quantity of means of production may be set in motion by a diminishing expenditure of human power, thanks to rising productivity, \"undergoes a complete inversion, and is expressed thus: the higher the productivity of labour, the greater is the pressure of the workers on the means of employment, the more precarious therefore becomes the condition for their existence, namely the sale of their own labour-power for the increase of alien wealth.\" The fact that means of production and productivity increase more rapidly than the productive population \"expresses itself, therefore, under capitalism, in the inverse form that the working population always increases more rapidly than the valorization requirements of capital.\" The Dialectical Inversion of All Methods of Production Marx then recapitulates from Part IV: \"within the capitalist system all methods for raising the social productivity of labour are put into effect at the cost of the individual worker; that all means for the development of production undergo a dialectical inversion so that they become means of domination and exploitation of the producers; they distort the worker into a fragment of a man, they degrade him to the level of an appendage of a machine, they destroy the actual content of his labour by turning it into a torment; they alienate [ entfremden ] from him the intellectual potentialities of the labour process in the same proportion as science is incorporated in it as an independent power; they deform the conditions under which he works, subject him during the labour process to a despotism the more hateful for its meanness; they transform his life-time into working-time, and drag his wife and child beneath the wheels of the juggernaut of capital.\" But — and this is the dialectical pivot — \"all methods for the production of surplus-value are at the same time methods of accumulation, and every extension of accumulation becomes, conversely, a means for the development of those methods. It follows therefore that in proportion as capital accumulates, the situation of the worker, be his payment high or low, must grow worse.\" The Prometheus Metaphor and the Absolute Law The law of the relative surplus population \"rivets the worker to capital more firmly than the wedges of Hephaestus held Prometheus to the rock. It makes an accumulation of misery a necessary condition, corresponding to the accumulation of wealth.\" And the iconic formulation of the whole chapter: \"Accumulation of wealth at one pole is, therefore, at the same time accumulation of misery, the torment of labour, slavery, ignorance, brutalization and moral degradation at the opposite pole, i.e. on the side of the class that produces its own product as capital.\" The Antagonistic Character Registered by Political Economy \"This antagonistic character of capitalist accumulation is enunciated in various forms by political economists, although they lump it together with other phenomena which are admittedly to some extent analogous, but nevertheless essentially distinct, since they appear only in pre-capitalist modes of production.\" Marx then presents a gallery: Ortes , the Venetian monk, regards the antagonism as \"a universal natural law of social wealth\": \"the abundance of wealth with some people is always equal to the lack of wealth with others\"; \"The great riches of a small number are always accompanied by the absolute deprivation of the essential necessities of life for many others. The wealth of a nation corresponds with its population, and its misery corresponds with its wealth. Diligence in some compels idleness in others.\" (Ortes also wrote: \"Instead of projecting useless systems for achieving the happiness of peoples, I shall limit myself to investigating the reasons for their unhappiness.\") The difference from Marx: Ortes treats this as a natural law; Marx as an historical one. And Ortes drew a religious conclusion (justifying charity and monasteries), while the Protestant Townsend drew the opposite. Townsend (the High Church parson, quoted from A Dissertation on the Poor Laws , 1786) glorified misery as a necessary condition of wealth \"in a thoroughly brutal way\": legal constraint is troublesome, \"whereas hunger is not only a peaceable, silent, unremitted pressure, but as the most natural motive to industry and labour, it calls forth the most powerful exertions.\" Everything depends on making hunger permanent, which is provided for by the \"principle of population.\" Townsend: \"It seems to be a law of Nature that the poor should be to a certain degree improvident... that there may always be some to fulfil the most servile, the most sordid, and the most ignoble offices in the community.\" The Poor Law \"tends to destroy the harmony and beauty, the symmetry and order of that system which God and Nature have established in the world.\" Marx's comment: \"If the Venetian monk found in the fatal destiny that makes misery eternal a justification for the existence of Christian charity, celibacy, monasteries and pious foundations, the beneficed Protestant finds in it a pretext for condemning the laws by which the poor possessed a right to a miserable amount of public relief.\" A footnote notes that Malthus borrowed \"the greater part of his doctrine from Sir James Steuart,\" who wished to multiply needs as a stimulus to labour — but \"unlike our fat benefice-holder, he does not conclude from this that the wage-labourer must always go fasting.\" Storch : \"The progress of social wealth begets this useful class of society... which performs the most wearisome, the vilest, the most disgusting functions, which, in a word, takes on its shoulders all that is disagreeable and servile in life, and procures thus for other classes leisure, serenity of mind and conventional dignity of character.\" Storch asks what the actual advantage of capitalist civilization is, compared with barbarism, and finds only one answer: security . Sismondi : \"every worker can produce every day much more than he needs to consume. But at the same time, while his labour produces wealth, that wealth would, were he called on to consume it himself, make him less fit for labour.\" And: \"Exertion today is separated from its recompense; it is not the same man that first works, and then reposes; but it is because the one works that the other rests... The indefinite multiplication of the productive powers of labour can have no other result than the increase of luxury and enjoyment on the part of the idle rich.\" Sismondi registers the antagonism but laments it as a fall from a prior harmony. Destutt de Tracy , \"that fish-blooded bourgeois doctrinaire,\" makes the point \"in the most brutal fashion\": \"In poor nations the people are comfortable, in rich nations they are generally poor.\" Section 5: Illustrations of the General Law of Capitalist Accumulation (a) England from 1846 to 1866 Marx chooses England as \"the classical example\" for three reasons: it holds the foremost place in the world market; capitalist production is fully developed only there; and \"the introduction of the free-trade millennium since 1846 has cut off the last retreat of vulgar economics.\" The period is \"as favourable for the study of capitalist accumulation\" as if \"Fortunatus's purse\" had been discovered. Population. Although absolute growth was great, the relative increase fell constantly. Census figures for average annual increase of the population of England and Wales (per cent): 1811–21: 1.533; 1821–31: 1.446; 1831–41: 1.326; 1841–51: 1.216; 1851–61: 1.141. Wealth. Increase of profits liable to income tax in Great Britain from 1853 to 1864 (farmers and some categories excluded) was 50.47 per cent (annual average 4.58 per cent), while population increased about 12 per cent. Rent of land subject to taxation increased 38 per cent (31½ per cent annually). Categories with greatest increase (percentage excess of annual income 1864 over 1853, and annual percentage increase): Houses 38.60/3.50; Quarries 84.76/7.70; Mines 68.85/6.26; Ironworks 39.92/3.63; Fisheries 57.37/5.21; Gasworks 126.02/11.45; Railways 83.29/7.57. Rates accelerating: Incomes from profits increased 1853–57 at 1.73% a year; 1857–61 at 2.74%; 1861–64 at 9.30%. Sum of incomes under income tax: 1856: £307,068,898; 1859: £328,127,416; 1862: £351,745,241; 1863: £359,142,897; 1864: £362,462,279; 1865: £385,530,020. A footnote notes these figures are \"false\" absolutely — some £100,000,000 of income is not declared every year, with systematic fraud documented by the Inland Revenue Commissioners. Concentration and centralization. Between 1851 and 1861, farms of less than 100 acres fell from 31,583 to 26,597 (5,016 thrown together into larger farms). From 1815 to 1825 no personal estate over £1,000,000 came under succession duty; from 1825 to 1855, eight did; from 1856 to June 1859, four did. Income Tax Schedule D (profits) analysis for 1864 and 1865: taxable incomes amounted in 1864 to £95,844,222 (308,416 persons out of 23,891,009), in 1865 to £105,435,579 (332,431 out of 24,127,003). Distribution table shows concentration: the top categories (over £X) grew. Production. Coal: 1855: 61,543,079 tons, value £16,113,167; 1864: 92,787,873 tons, value £23,197,968. Pig-iron: 1855: 3,218,154 tons, value £8,045,385; 1864: 4,767,951 tons, value £11,919,877. Railways: 1854: 8,054 miles, paid-up capital £286,068,794; 1864: 12,789 miles, capital £425,719,613. Total imports and exports: 1854: £268,210,145; 1865: £489,923,285. Exports: 1846: £58,842,377; 1849: £63,596,052; 1856: £115,826,948; 1860: £135,842,817; 1865: £165,862,402; 1866: £188,917,563. The Registrar-General: \"Rapidly as the population has increased, it has not kept pace with the progress of industry and wealth.\" Gladstone. Marx juxtaposes two Gladstone speeches. In 1843 Gladstone said: \"It is one of the most melancholy features in the social state of this country, that we see... while there is at this moment a decrease in the consuming powers of the people, an increase of the pressure of privations and distress... there is at the same time a constant accumulation of wealth in the upper classes, an increase of the luxuriousness of their habits.\" Twenty years later, in his Budget speech of 16 April 1863: \"From 1842 to 1852 the taxable income of the country increased by 6 per cent... In the eight years from 1853 to 1861 it had increased from the basis taken in 1853 by 20 per cent! The fact is so astonishing as to be almost incredible... this intoxicating augmentation of wealth and power... entirely confined to classes of property... must be of indirect benefit to the labouring population, because it cheapens commodities of general consumption. While the rich have been growing richer, the poor have been growing less poor. At any rate, whether the extremes of poverty are less, I do not presume to say.\" Marx's critique: \"How lame an anticlimax! If the working class has remained 'poor', only 'less poor' in proportion as it produces for the wealthy class 'an intoxicating augmentation of wealth and power', then it has remained relatively just as poor. If the extremes of poverty have not lessened, they have increased, because the extremes of wealth have.\" Marx demonstrates the \"cheapening\" claim is false: official statistics (e.g., London Orphan Asylum accounts) show an increase in price of 20 per cent over the last ten years (comparing 1860–62 with 1851–53), and 1863–65 saw a progressive rise in the price of meat, butter, milk, sugar, salt, coal, and other necessaries. Gladstone's next Budget speech (7 April 1864) is a \"Pindaric dithyramb\": he speaks of masses \"on the border of pauperism,\" of branches of trade in which \"wages have not increased,\" and sums up the happiness of the working class: \"human life is but, in nine cases out of ten, a struggle for existence.\" A footnote (note 40) quotes Boileau to characterize Gladstone's contradictions: \"Voilà l'homme en effet. Il va du blanc au noir...\" (\"Such is the man: he goes from black to white...\") Fawcett. Professor Fawcett declares roundly: \"I do not, of course, deny that money wages have been augmented by this increase of capital... but this apparent advantage is to a great extent lost, because many of the necessaries of life are becoming dearer... the rich grow rapidly richer, whilst there is no perceptible advance in the comfort enjoyed by the industrial classes... They become almost the slaves of the tradesman, to whom they owe money.\" Pauperism. Official list of paupers in England: 1855: 851,369; 1856: 877,767; 1865: 971,433. As a result of the cotton famine: 1863: 1,079,382; 1864: 1,014,978. The crisis of 1866 (London most severely hit) created an increase of pauperism of 19.5% in 1866 over 1865, and 24.4% over 1864, with a still greater increase in early 1867. Two points: the rise and fall of paupers reflects the industrial cycle; and official statistics \"become more and more misleading... in proportion as, with the accumulation of capital, the class struggle develops, and hence the class-consciousness of the workers as well.\" Marx notes the growing horror in which workers hold the workhouse, \"that place of punishment for poverty.\" (b) The Badly Paid Strata of the British Industrial Working Class Marx draws on Dr Edward Smith's investigations for the Privy Council during the cotton famine of 1862–63. Smith concluded that \"to avert starvation diseases\" the daily food of an average woman ought to contain at least 3,900 grains of carbon and 180 grains of nitrogen; the daily food of an average man at least 4,300 grains of carbon and 200 grains of nitrogen. For the weekly average of adult men and women, at least 28,600 grains of carbon and 1,330 grains of nitrogen. The cotton workers' consumption in December 1862 was 29,211 grains of carbon and 1,295 grains of nitrogen — the nitrogen below the minimum. In 1863 the Privy Council ordered an investigation into the worst-nourished part of the English working class. Dr Simon (Privy Council medical officer) chose Dr Smith, whose inquiry covered agricultural labourers, silk-weavers, needlewomen, kid-glovers, stocking-weavers, glove-weavers, and shoemakers. It selected in each category the most healthy families and those in comparatively the best circumstances. Result: \"in only one of the examined classes of indoor operatives did the average nitrogen supply just exceed, while in another it nearly reached, the estimated standard of bare sufficiency... and in two classes there was defect — in one, a very large defect — of both nitrogen and carbon.\" Among agricultural families, more than a fifth had less than the estimated sufficiency of carbonaceous food, more than a third less than the sufficiency of nitrogenous food; in Berkshire, Oxfordshire, and Somersetshire, insufficiency of nitrogenous food was the average local diet. The table shows: | Category | Weekly carbon (grains) | Weekly nitrogen (grains) | |---|---|---| | Five indoor occupations | 28,876 | 1,192 | | Unemployed Lancashire operatives | 28,211 | 1,295 | | Minimum for Lancashire operatives | 28,600 | 1,330 | Just under one-half (6½) of the categories had no beer; 28 per cent no milk. Liquid nourishment varied from 7 ounces (needlewomen) to 24½ (stocking-makers). Bread: 7½ lb (needlewomen) to 11½ lb (shoemakers); average 9.9 lb per adult weekly. Sugar (treacle etc.): 4 ounces (kid-glovers) to 11 (stocking-makers); average 8 ounces per adult per week. Butter (fat etc.): average 5 ounces per adult. Meat (bacon etc.): 7½ ounces (silk-weavers) to 18½ (kid-glovers); average 13.6 ounces. Weekly cost of food per adult: silk-weavers 2s. 2½d., needlewomen 2s. 7d., kid-glovers 2s. 9½d., shoemakers 2s. 7½d., stocking-weavers 2s. 6½d.; for silk-weavers of Macclesfield only 1s. 8½d. Dr Simon's General Health Report is quoted at length on the \"sanitary context\": privation of food is reluctantly borne; \"long before insufficiency of diet is a matter of hygienic concern... the household will have been utterly destitute of material comfort; clothing and fuel will have been even scantier than food.\" The home will be where shelter can be cheapest bought — \"in quarters where commonly there is least fruit of sanitary supervision, least drainage, least scavenging, least suppression of public nuisances.\" And crucially: \"the poverty to which they advert is not the deserved poverty of idleness. In all cases it is the poverty of working populations.\" Simon concludes: \"on a very large scale the nominal self-support can be only a circuit, longer or shorter, to pauperism.\" Housing. Marx turns to the housing situation, where \"every unprejudiced observer sees that the greater the centralization of the means of production, the greater is the corresponding concentration of workers within a given space; and therefore the more quickly capitalist accumulation takes place, the more miserable the housing situation of the working class.\" Town \"improvements\" (demolition of poorly built districts, erection of palaces for banks and warehouses, widening of streets for business traffic, luxury carriages, tramways) \"obviously drive the poor away into even worse and more crowded corners.\" House prices stand in inverse ratio to quality; \"the mines of misery are exploited by house speculators with more profit and at less cost than the mines of Potosi were ever exploited.\" The antagonistic character of accumulation is \"here so evident that even the official English reports... teem with heterodox onslaughts on 'property and its rights.'\" From 1847 to 1864, no less than ten Acts of Parliament on sanitation were passed. Dr Simon's 1865 report: \"Speaking generally, it may be said that the evils are uncontrolled in England.\" A footnote (note 50) quotes S. Laing: \"In no particular have the rights of persons been so avowedly and shamefully sacrificed to the rights of property as in regard to the lodging of the labouring class.\" Dr Simon's remark on the moral consequences of overcrowding is quoted: \"In its higher degrees it almost necessarily involves such negation of all delicacy, such unclean confusion of bodies and bodily functions, such exposure of animal and sexual nakedness, as is rather bestial than human... To children who are born under its curse, it must often be a very baptism into infamy.\" Dr Hunter (in the Seventh and Eighth Reports on Public Health, 1865 and 1866): \"there are about twenty large colonies in London, of about 10,000 persons each, whose miserable condition exceeds almost anything I have seen elsewhere in England.\" \"It is not too much to say that life in parts of London and Newcastle is infernal.\" Rents have become so heavy that few labouring men can afford more than one room. The land price in London is always very high relative to yearly revenue, so buyers speculate on selling at a \"jury price\" or pocketing an increase from proximity to large-scale undertakings. \"There is a regular trade in the purchase of 'fag-ends of leases.'\" As a result of railway construction, \"the spectacle has lately been seen in the East of London of a number of families wandering about some Saturday night with their scanty worldly goods on their backs, without any resting place but the workhouse.\" Displaced workers try to stay near their workshops, \"parting their two-room tenements into single rooms, and crowding even those.\" In one Strand parish, the Public Health Officer reckoned 581 persons per acre (including half the width of the Thames). Marx's ironic comment on \"improvements\": the owner of land and houses, when expropriated by improvements, \"does not just receive full compensation. He must also be comforted, both according to human law and divine law, by receiving a substantial profit in return for his compulsory 'abstinence'. The worker, with his wife and child and chattels, is thrown out into the street, and, if he crowds in too large numbers near districts where the local authority insists on decency, he is prosecuted in the name of public health!\" At the beginning of the nineteenth century, no English town (except London) had more than 100,000 inhabitants; only five had more than 50,000. Now there are twenty-eight towns with more than 50,000. The result is that \"the old close-packed little towns are now centres built round on every side, open nowhere to air.\" The houses pass to the poor, and \"a population, for which the houses were not intended and quite unfit, has been created, whose surroundings are truly degrading to the adults and ruinous to the children. The more rapidly capital accumulates in an industrial or commercial town, the more rapidly flows the stream of exploitable human material, the more miserable are the improvised dwellings of the workers.\" Newcastle-on-Tyne. Not less than 34,000 persons live there in single rooms. Houses have been pulled down as dangerous, but building of new houses progresses slowly while business progresses quickly. Dr Embleton (Newcastle Fever Hospital): \"There can be little doubt that the great cause of the continuance and spread of the typhus has been the over-crowding of human beings, and the uncleanliness of their dwellings.\" Rooms in confined and unwholesome yards; men, women, and children \"lie at night huddled together\"; night-shift succeeds day-shift \"in unbroken series\"; \"the whole house badly supplied with water and worse with privies; dirty, unventilated, and pestiferous.\" Lodgings from 8d. to 3s. a week. Dr Hunter: \"The town of Newcastle-on-Tyne contains a sample of the finest tribe of our countrymen, often sunk by external circumstances of house and street into an almost savage degradation.\" The fluctuation of housing conditions. The state of dwellings may be tolerable today, frightful tomorrow, as capital and labour ebb and flow. Bradford: in 1861 there were 1,751 uninhabited houses; with the revival of trade, an overflow from the wages of the reserve army occurred. Dr Hunter obtained from an insurance agent a list of \"frightful cellar habitations and rooms\" inhabited largely by well-paid workers who declared they would willingly pay for better dwellings if they existed. Dr Bell's report (5 September 1865) describes one small cellar measuring 1,500 cubic feet with ten persons; 223 houses with 1,450 inhabitants, 453 beds, and 36 privies; beds averaging 3.3 persons, many with 5 or 6; some sleeping in their clothes on the bare boards — \"young men and women, married and unmarried, all together.\" These are \"the centres from which disease and death are distributed amongst those in better circumstances, who have allowed them thus to fester in our midst.\" The footnote (note 61) lists individual houses and cellars with occupancy (e.g., 16 persons in one room on Vulcan Street; 26 in two cellar rooms on Salt Pie Street). Bristol takes third place after London in the misery of its dwellings: \"Bristol, where the blankest poverty and domestic misery abound in the wealthiest town of Europe.\" (c) The Nomadic Population Marx turns to \"the light infantry of capital, thrown from one point to another according to its present needs. When they are not on the march they 'camp'.\" Nomadic labour is used for building and draining works, brick-making, lime-burning, railway-making, etc. \"A flying column of pestilence, it carries smallpox, typhus, cholera and scarlet fever into the places in whose neighbourhood it pitches its camp.\" In undertakings with large capital outlay, the contractor provides wooden huts — \"improvising villages which lack all sanitary arrangements, are outside the control of the local authorities, and are very profitable to the gentleman who is doing the contracting, for he exploits his workers in two directions at once — as soldiers of industry, and as tenants.\" Depending on whether the hut has one, two, or three holes, the inhabitant pays 2, 3, or 4 shillings a week. A long quotation from the Chairman of the Nuisances Removal Committee of Sevenoaks to Sir George Grey (Home Secretary), September 1864, details smallpox outbreaks in railway workers' huts built by contractor Mr Jay: no ventilation or drainage, overcrowded, each occupant had to accommodate lodgers, two rooms per tenement. \"In the night-time these poor people were compelled to endure all the horror of suffocation to avoid the pestiferous smells.\" Mr Jay promised to appropriate a hut for contagious cases but took no steps; several cases of smallpox and two deaths occurred. One family lost five children to smallpox and fever. A footnote (note 66) adds a report from Chapel-en-le-Frith Union: labourers living in \"small excavations... made into a large hillock of lime ashes,\" small and damp, with \"not the slightest means of ventilation except up a hole pushed through the top,\" where smallpox had been raging. Miners. \"Workers in coal and other mines belong to the best paid categories of the British proletariat.\" The mine exploiter builds cottages for his \"hands,\" who receive cottages and coal for firing \"for nothing\" — these form part of their wages, paid in kind. Those unhoused receive £4 per annum compensation. Ground rent is high where population is dense; the mining employer tries to put up, within the smallest space, \"exactly the number of cottages necessary to pack together his workers and their families.\" Dr Hunter: the lodging of pitmen in Northumberland and Durham is \"perhaps, on the whole, the worst and the dearest of which any large specimens can be found in England.\" The extreme badness is in the high number of men in one room, small ground-plots, want of water, absence of privies, houses placed on top of one another. \"The lessee acts as if the whole colony were encamped, not resident.\" Dr Stevens: with very few exceptions, the statement that no means are taken to secure health \"would be true of all of them.\" Colliers are \"bound\" to the colliery lessee or owner for twelve months; if they express discontent, their names are marked, and they are turned off at the annual \"binding.\" Stevens: \"no part of the 'truck system' could be worse than what obtains in these densely-populated districts. The collier is bound to take as part of his hiring a house surrounded with pestiferous influences; he cannot help himself... he is, to all intents and purposes, a serf.\" The proprietor first consults his balance-sheet. Dr Simon's official report offers capital's \"apology\": mines are commonly worked on lease; the lessee's interest (commonly 21 years) is too short to justify good accommodation; the landlord would fix an exorbitant ground-rent for the \"privilege\" of having a decent village on the surface. Dr Simon notes: \"Claims of landlordship are being so used as to do great public wrong. The landlord in his capacity of mine-owner invites an industrial colony to labour on his estate, and then in his capacity of surface-owner makes it impossible that the labourers whom he collects should find proper lodging where they must live.\" The lessee has no pecuniary motive to resist, knowing \"that his labourers on whom they fall have not education enough to know the value of their sanitary rights, that neither obscenest lodging nor foulest drinking water will be appreciable inducements towards a 'strike'.\" (d) Effect of Crises on the Best Paid Section of the Working Class Marx examines the crisis of 1866 (\"its next crisis was due in 1866\"; the year 1857 brought one of the \"gigantic crises\"). The 1866 crisis was \"predominantly financial.\" Its outbreak in May 1866 was signalled by the failure of a giant London bank, followed by the collapse of countless swindling companies. Iron shipbuilding was one of the industries involved: the magnates had over-produced and entered enormous contracts on speculative assumptions about credit. A terrible reaction set in. Marx quotes a detailed report from the Morning Star (7 January 1867): in East End districts (Poplar, Millwall, Greenwich, Deptford, Limehouse, Canning Town), at least 15,000 workmen and their families were in \"utter destitution,\" and 3,000 skilled mechanics were breaking stones in the workhouse yard after distress of over half a year. Marx describes the workhouse scene in vivid detail: a hungry crowd besieging the door; men breaking paving-stones into macadam, \"each man had a big paving-stone for a seat, and he chipped away at the rime-covered granite until he had broken up, and think! five bushels of it,\" for threepence and an allowance of food. In another part of the yard, men huddled together for warmth, picking oakum and disputing \"which could work the longest on a given quantity of food — for endurance was the point of honour.\" Seven thousand in one workhouse were recipients of relief; many had earned the highest wages paid to artisans six or eight months before. Marx narrates several visits: an ironworker out of work for 27 weeks, his family picking oakum, his dinner \"a couple of slices of bread and dripping, and a cup of milkless tea\"; a middle-aged woman with her family staring at a dying fire, her boys idle 26 weeks, showing a bank-book documenting their savings melting from £20 to nothing; an iron labourer's wife ill from want of food, lying on a mattress in her clothes, just covered with a strip of carpet, all bedding pawned, children \"looking as much in need of nursing as their mother\"; a young fellow with \"a young wife, two pretty children, a cluster of pawn-tickets, and a bare room.\" A Standard extract (5 April 1867) describes \"a frightful spectacle\" — 40,000 starving people in the East End, \"packed — next door to the most enormous accumulation of wealth the world ever saw — cheek by jowl with this are 40,000 helpless, starving people.\" The local ratepayers themselves are \"driven by the parochial charges to the verge of pauperism.\" A footnote quotes Reynolds' Newspaper (20 January 1867): placards announcing \"Fat oxen! Starving men! The fat oxen from their palace of glass have gone to feed the rich in their luxurious abode, while the starving men are left to rot and die in their wretched dens.\" Belgium. Marx then examines Belgium, which English capitalists cite as \"the workers' paradise\" because \"freedom of labour\" (or \"freedom of capital\") is there limited neither by trade unions nor Factory Acts. He draws on M. Ducpetiaux's Budgets économiques des classes ouvrières de la Belgique (Brussels, 1855). Ducpetiaux's normal Belgian worker's family consists of father, mother, and four children; four of the six may be usefully employed year-round; no sick person, no expenses for religious, moral, or intellectual purposes except a small sum for church pews; no savings or benefit society contributions; no luxury or improvidence. The father and eldest son allow themselves tobacco and on Sundays go to the ale-house (86 centimes a week). Highest average daily wage: 1 franc 56 centimes for men, 89 for women, 56 for boys, 55 for girls. Calculated at 300 working days, family resources at maximum: 1,068 francs a year (father 468, mother 267, boy 168, girl 165). Ducpetiaux notes that assigning wages to the mother removes the household from her management: \"who will look after the house and the young children?... This is the dilemma presented every day to the workers.\" Annual expenditure yields deficits depending on food: sailor in the fleet 1,828 francs (deficit 760); soldier 1,473 (deficit 405); prisoner 1,112 (deficit 44). \"We see that few workers' families can reach... even that of the prisoner.\" The average cost per prisoner (1847–49) was 63 centimes; compared with daily maintenance of the worker, a difference of 13 centimes. And the prisoner does not pay for lodgings. Marx asks: \"How does it happen, then, that a great number, we might say the great majority of workers, live even more economically than prisoners?\" Because they adopt expedients whose secrets only workers know: they reduce rations, substitute rye-bread for wheat, eat less or no meat, content themselves with one or two rooms crammed together, \"where boys and girls sleep side by side, often on the same mattress,\" economize on clothing, washing, decency, and give up Sunday diversions. \"Once this extreme limit has been reached, the least rise in the price of food, the shortest stoppage of work, the slightest illness, increases the worker's distress and brings him to complete disaster.\" Marx adds: \"in this 'paradise for capitalists', the smallest change in the prices of the most essential means of subsistence is followed by a change in the number of deaths and crimes!\" And the class distribution: of 930,000 Belgian families, 90,000 are wealthy and on the voters' list (450,000 persons); 390,000 families of the lower middle class (1,950,000 persons); 450,000 working-class families (2,250,000 persons). \"Of the 450,000 working-class families, over 200,000 are on the pauper list!\" (e) The British Agricultural Proletariat Marx states: \"Nowhere does the antagonistic character of capitalist production and accumulation assert itself more brutally than in the progress of English agriculture (including cattle-breeding) and the retrogression of the English agricultural labourer.\" He gives a historical survey. Arthur Young's statements about the agricultural labourer of 1771 show him \"a very pitiable role\" compared with his predecessor of the end of the fourteenth century, \"when the labourer... could live in plenty, and accumulate wealth\" — not to speak of the fifteenth century, \"the golden age of the English labourer in town and country.\" A 1777 book: \"The great farmer is nearly mounted to a level with him [the gentleman]; while the poor labourer is depressed almost to the earth.\" Real agricultural wages fell by nearly ¼ (25 per cent) between 1737 and 1777. Dr Richard Price: \"Modern policy is, indeed, more favourable to the higher classes of people; and the consequences may in time prove that the whole kingdom will consist of only gentry and beggars, or of grandees and slaves.\" The agricultural labourer's average wage expressed in pints of wheat: 1770–71: 90 pints; Eden's time (1797): 65; 1808: 60. The Poor Law and the wage-pauper combination. During the Anti-Jacobin War, nominal wages rose, partly from banknote depreciation and partly from rising prices of primary means of subsistence. The movement can be demonstrated simply: the Poor Law was administered the same way in 1795 and 1814 — the parish made up the nominal wage to the level needed for simple vegetation. The ratio between wage paid by the farmer and the wage-deficit made good by the parish shows: first, wages had fallen below their minimum; second, the degree to which the labourer was \"a combination of wage-labourer and pauper, or the degree to which he had been turned into a serf of his parish.\" Northamptonshire example: 1795, weekly wage 7s. 6d.; yearly expenditure of a family of six £36 12s. 5d.; income £29 18s.; deficit made good by parish £6 14s. 5d. In 1814: weekly wage 12s. 2d.; expenditure of family of five £54 18s. 4d.; income £36 2s.; deficit £18 6s. 4d. \"In 1795 the deficit was less than a quarter of the wage, in 1814 it was more than a half.\" Hence the labourer, the instrumentum vocale (\"speaking implement\"), became \"the most oppressed, the worst nourished, the most brutally treated\" of all the animals kept by the farmer. The Swing riots of 1830 revealed that \"misery and black mutinous discontent smouldered quite as fiercely under the surface of agricultural as of manufacturing England.\" Sadler christened the agricultural labourers \"white slaves\"; a bishop echoed the epithet. E. G. Wakefield: \"The peasant of the South of England... is not a freeman, nor is he a slave; he is a pauper.\" The Corn Law repeal period. Before repeal, the middle-class agitators wanted to prove how little the Corn Laws protected actual producers; the industrial bourgeoisie was angry at the landed aristocracy's denunciations of the factory system. \"When thieves fall out, honest men come into their own.\" The Morning Chronicle sent special commissioners (1844–45) into agricultural districts, publishing names of families and landlords. Marx includes a table of wages paid in three villages near Blandford, Wimborne, and Poole, owned by Mr G. Bankes and the Earl of Shaftesbury. The table (with columns for weekly wage of men, income of children, total family income, rent, wage after deduction of rent, and income per head) shows rent absorbing a large part of wages, and in some cases family income per head as low as 7d. to 1s. On Bankes and Shaftesbury: \"just like Bankes, the pope of the Low Church, the head of the English pietists, also pockets a large part of the miserable wages of the labourers under the pretext of the rent of their houses.\" Post-repeal agricultural revolution. The repeal gave \"a marvellous impulse\" to English agriculture: drainage on the most extensive scale, new methods of stall-feeding and artificial cultivation of green crops, mechanical manuring apparatus, new treatment of clay soils, increased use of mineral manures, employment of the steam-engine and all kinds of new machinery, more intensive cultivation. Mr Pusey (Chairman of the Royal Agricultural Society) declares the relative expenses of farming have been reduced nearly 50 per cent by the introduction of new machinery. Greater outlay of capital per acre and more rapid concentration of farms were essential conditions. Area under cultivation increased from 1846 to 1856 by 464,119 acres. Yet the number of actual agricultural labourers of both sexes and all ages fell from 1,241,396 in 1851 to 1,163,217 in 1861. A footnote (note 83) shows how medium-sized farmers declined (the category \"Farmer's son, grandson, brother...\" fell from 216,851 in 1851 to 176,151 in 1861; farms under 20 acres fell by more than 900; 50–75 acre farms fell from 8,253 to 6,370; large farms (300–500 acres) rose from 7,771 to 8,410; over 500 acres from 2,755 to 3,914; over 1,000 acres from 492 to 582). The Registrar General: \"The increase of farmers and farm-labourers, since 1801, bears no kind of proportion... to the increase of agricultural produce.\" Professor Rogers concludes that the situation of the English agricultural labourer of his day, compared with 1770–80 (not to speak of the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries), has changed for the worse \"to an extraordinary extent,\" that \"the peasant has again become a serf,\" and a serf worse fed and worse clothed. Dr Hunter: \"The cost of the hind is fixed at the lowest possible amount on which he can live... the supplies of wages and shelter are not calculated on the profit to be derived from him. He is a zero in farming calculations.\" And: \"As to any further reduction of his income, he may say, nihil habeo nihil curo. He has no fears for the future, because he has now only the spare supply necessary to keep him. He has reached the zero from which are dated the calculations of the farmer. Come what will, he has no share either in prosperity or adversity.\" The 1863 inquiry into convicts. Marx cites two voluminous Blue Books comparing diets of convicts, paupers in workhouses, and free labourers. \"It certainly appears that the former are much better fed than either of the two other classes,\" while \"the amount of labour required from an ordinary convict under penal servitude is about one-half of what would be done by an ordinary day-labourer.\" Depositions: \"The diet of the English prisons is superior to that of ordinary labourers in England\"; \"the ordinary agricultural labourers in Scotland very seldom get any meat at all.\" No. 3047: \"Is there anything that you are aware of to account for the necessity of feeding them very much better than ordinary labourers? — Certainly not.\" The comparative table: | Category | Nitrogenous (oz) | Non-nitrogenous (oz) | Mineral (oz) | Total (oz) | |---|---|---|---|---| | Portland (convict) | 28.95 | 150.06 | 4.68 | 183.69 | | Sailor in the Navy | 29.63 | 152.91 | 4.52 | 187.06 | | Soldier | 25.55 | 114.49 | 3.94 | 143.98 | | Working coach-maker | 24.53 | 162.06 | 4.23 | 190.82 | | Compositor | 21.24 | 100.83 | 3.12 | 125.19 | | Agricultural labourer | 17.72 | 118.06 | 3.29 | 139.08 | The agricultural labourer has the lowest nitrogenous intake of all categories. Dr E. Smith notes the labourer himself eats proportionally more than the average (especially meat and bacon), so that \"the quantity of food obtained by the wife and also by the children at the period of rapid growth, is in many cases, in almost every county, deficient, and particularly in nitrogen.\" The number of male and female servants living with farmers fell from 288,277 in 1851 to 204,962 in 1861. The agricultural labourer of England, compared with other parts of the UK, \"is considerably the worst fed\": | Country | Carbon (grains/week) | Nitrogen (grains/week) | |---|---|---| | England | 46,673 | 1,594 | | Wales | 48,354 | 2,031 | | Scotland | 48,980 | 2,348 | | Ireland | 43,366 | 2,434 | A footnote (note 97) notes the English agricultural labourer receives only a quarter as much milk and half as much bread as the Irish. Arthur Young already noticed the better nourishment of the latter. \"The reason is simply this, that the poor Irish farmer is incomparably more humane than the rich English.\" For Wales, a lengthy footnote describes the appalling conditions of the south-west: mud walls and floors, peat fires emitting carbonic and sulphurous acids, \"a debilitated and scrofulous people,\" a \"plague more horrible still, the great number of idiots.\" Cardiganshire's population barely maintained its numbers (1851: 97,614; 1861: 97,401). Housing of the agricultural labourer. Dr Simon: \"almost every page of Dr Hunter's report bears testimony\" to insufficient and miserable housing, which has been deteriorating, \"house-room being now greatly more difficult for him to find, and, when found, greatly less suitable to his needs than, perhaps, for centuries had been the case.\" Whether the labourer finds house-room \"depends on the use which others may see fit to make of their 'right to do as they will with their own'.\" No law requires a proportion of decent labourers' dwellings on a farm, nor reserves any right in the soil to the labourer. The Poor Law (settlement and chargeability provisions) gives each parish a pecuniary interest in minimizing resident labourers. \"Large proprietors... have but to resolve that there shall be no labourers' dwellings on their estates, and their estates will thenceforth be virtually free from half their responsibility for the poor.\" A footnote (note 99) explains \"close villages\" (owned by one or two big landowners) and \"open villages\" (soil belonging to many small proprietors), where building speculators can build cottages. Dr Hunter's finding: destruction of houses had been in progress in 821 parishes, which received in 1861 (compared with 1851) a population 5½ per cent greater, into house-room 4½ per cent less. The result is a \"show-village\" where cottages are reduced to a few, inhabited only by shepherds, gardeners, gamekeepers. \"Regular servants who receive the good treatment usual to their class.\" Labourers come from a neighbouring open village, \"perhaps three miles off.\" A footnote describes the \"show-village\" as unreal as those Catherine II saw in the Crimea; near Market Harborough, a 500-acre sheep-farm employs only one man, who receives a \"thirteenth shilling a week for lodgings\" and must find them far away. The labourer must walk six or eight miles daily to work, and the open villages cram the labourers into the cheapest hovels. Dr Simon's further report: even on \"princely estates\" the cottage \"may be of the meanest description\"; \"There are landlords who deem any stye good enough for their labourer and his family, and who yet do not disdain to drive with him the hardest possible bargain for rent.\" The Nuisances Removal Acts are \"a mere dead letter\" where the cottage-owner is the one from whom the hovel is rented. \"Lamentable indeed, must be the case, when... it is the common conclusion of competent observers that even the general badness of dwellings is an evil infinitely less urgent than their mere numerical insufficiency.\" Overcrowding favours the extension of contagious disease and \"the origination of disease which is not contagious\"; and it outrages decency: \"adult persons of both sexes, married and unmarried, are huddled together in single small sleeping rooms.\" Dr Ord's report on a fever outbreak at Wing (Buckinghamshire): a young man with fever \"slept in a room with nine other persons\"; five out of the nine caught fever, one died. Dr Harvey's report: ten persons in one room, including a young woman with fever, her parents, her bastard child, two brothers, two sisters each with a bastard child; \"A few weeks ago thirteen persons slept in it.\" Dr Hunter's survey. He investigated 5,375 agricultural labourers' cottages across all English counties. Of these, 2,195 had only one bedroom (often used also as a living-room), 2,930 only two, and 250 more than two. Examples from a dozen counties: Bedfordshire (Wrestlingworth): bedrooms about 12 ft long and 10 broad, often smaller; small one-storied cots divided by partitions into two bedrooms; one bed frequently in a kitchen, 5 ft 6 in. high. Rent £3 a year; tenants make their own privies, landlord supplies only a hole; one privy serves the neighbourhood. A house of \"quite unapproachable beauty\": plaster walls bulging, one gable convex, the other concave with the chimney on it, \"a curved tube of clay and wood like an elephant's trunk,\" propped with a stick; doorway and window rhomboidal. Of seventeen houses visited, only four had more than one bedroom, and those overcrowded. Cots with one bedroom sheltered three adults and three children, a married couple with six children, etc. Dunton: high rents, £4 to £5; weekly wages 10s.; the family hopes to pay the rent by straw-plaiting. Six adults living with four children in one sleeping apartment pay £3 10s. The cheapest house, 15 ft long externally, 10 broad, lets for £3. A house whose \"tenants dunged against the house-side,\" the lower 9 inches of the door eaten away, doorway closed at night by bricks; half a window gone; three adults and five children huddled together without furniture. Berkshire (Beenham): June 1864, a man, his wife and four children in a one-storied cottage. A daughter returned from service with scarlet fever, died; one child died; the mother and one child had typhus. The father and one child slept outside, but isolation was difficult; in the crowded market, the linen of the fever-stricken household waited to be washed. Rent 1s. a week; one bedroom without window, fire-place, door, or opening except into the lobby; no garden. A man lived there with two grown-up daughters and a grown-up son; father and son slept on the bed, the girls in the passage; each daughter had a child while the family lived there. Buckinghamshire: thirty cottages on 1,000 acres contained 130–140 persons. Bradenham parish (1,000 acres): in 1851, 36 houses, 84 males and 54 females; in 1861, 98 males and 87 females; houses declined by 1. Winslow: miserable cots let at 1s. to 1s. 3d. per week. Water Eaton: landlords destroyed about 20 per cent of existing houses. A labourer walking 4 miles to work: \"No; they know better than to take a man in with my large family.\" Tinker's End: a bedroom with four adults and four children, 11 ft × 9 ft × 6 ft 5 in.; another 11 ft 3 in. × 9 ft × 5 ft 10 in. sheltered six persons; each family had less space than a convict; no house had more than one bedroom, not one a back door; water scarce. The air per person corresponded to being \"shut up in a box of 4 feet measuring each way, the whole night.\" Cambridgeshire (Gamlingay): \"the wretchedest cots to be found anywhere.\" Much straw-plaiting. \"A deadly lassitude, a hopeless surrendering up to filth.\" The neglect in the centre becomes \"mortification\" at the extremities. Absentee landlords \"bleed this poor rookery too freely.\" Eight or nine persons packed in one sleeping apartment. Essex: decline in persons and cottages goes hand in hand in many parishes, yet in twenty-two parishes the destruction of houses has not prevented increase of population. Fingringhoe (3,445 acres): 145 houses in 1851, 110 in 1861; the people did not wish to leave; 252 persons in 61 houses in 1851; 262 in 49 houses in 1861. Basildon: 157 persons in 35 houses in 1851; 180 in 27 houses in 1861. Herefordshire: suffered more from the \"eviction spirit\" than any other county. At Madley, overcrowded cottages with two bedrooms, mostly belonging to farmers, let for £3 or £4 a year on a weekly wage of 9s. Huntingdonshire (Hartford): 87 houses in 1851; shortly after, nineteen cottages destroyed in this parish of 1,720 acres; population 452 in 1831, 832 in 1851, 341 in 1861. Fourteen cottages each with one bedroom; one room 12 ft 10 in. × 12 ft 2 in. × 6 ft 9 in. housed eight people, about 130 cubic feet per head. The cottages lack gardens; some inhabitants farm small allotments, without privies; the family \"must either go to the allotment to deposit their ordures,\" or \"use a closet with a trough set like a drawer in a chest of drawers, and drawn out weekly.\" Marx's aside: \"In Japan the cyclical movement of the conditions of human life proceeds more cleanly and more decently than this.\" Lincolnshire (Langtoft): a man lives with his wife, her mother, and five children; the house has a front kitchen, scullery, bedroom over the front kitchen; the whole ground floor 21 ft 2 in. × 9 ft 5 in.; the bedroom a garret with sugar-loaf walls and a dormer window. He lives there because it was \"a tenantable cot,\" at any price. Statistics of twelve houses: twelve bedrooms, thirty-eight adults, thirty-six children (list given). Kent (Kennington): seriously over-populated in 1859, when diphtheria appeared. Cots destroyed and none built. Four houses named \"birdcages,\" each with four rooms of tiny dimensions (kitchen 9 ft 5 × 8 ft 11 × 6 ft 6; etc.). Northamptonshire (Brinworth, Pickford, Floore): in winter twenty to thirty men lounging about from lack of work; the landlord threw all farms together into two or three. \"While on one side of the wall the land is crying out to be worked, on the other side the defrauded labourers are casting longing glances at it.\" At Floore, cases of couples with four, five, six children in one small bedroom; three adults with five children; a couple with grandfather and six children down with scarlet fever. Wiltshire (Stratton): thirty-one houses visited, eight with only one bedroom. Pen Hill: a cot let at 1s. 3d. a week with four adults and four children, \"nothing good about it, except the walls, from the floor of rough-hewn pieces of stones to the roof of worn-out thatch.\" Worcestershire: house-destruction less excessive, yet from 1851 to 1861 inhabitants per house rose from 4.2 to 4.6. Badsey: cots and little gardens; some farmers declare the cots are \"a great nuisance here, because they bring the poor\"; one gentleman: \"The poor are none the better for them; if you build 500 they will let fast enough, in fact, the more you build, the more they want\" (his view: houses give birth to inhabitants, who then by a law of nature press on the means of housing). Dr Hunter: \"Now these poor must come from somewhere... it must be repulsion from some other unfit place, which will send them here.\" The dialectic of depopulation and surplus population. Marx draws the theoretical conclusion: \"The continual emigration to the towns, the continual formation of a surplus population in the countryside through the concentration of farms, the conversion of arable land into pasture, the introduction of machinery, etc., are things which go hand in hand with the continual eviction of the agricultural population by the destruction of their cottages.\" The more empty the district, the greater its \"relative surplus population\"; the greater their pressure on means of employment, the greater the absolute excess over housing, and the greater the \"pestilential herding together.\" \"The creation of dense knots of humanity in scattered little villages and small country towns corresponds to the forcible draining of men from the surface of the land.\" The continuous conversion of agricultural labourers into a surplus population, \"in spite of their diminishing number and the increasing mass of their products, is the cradle of pauperism. The pauperism of the agricultural labourers is ultimately a motive for their eviction; it is also the chief source of their miserable housing, which breaks down their last power of resistance, and makes them mere slaves of the landed proprietors and the farmers.\" Thus the minimum of wages becomes \"a law of nature\" for them. Yet the land, \"in spite of its constant 'relative surplus population', is at the same time under-populated.\" There are always too many labourers for ordinary needs and too few for exceptional and temporary requirements. Hence \"contradictory complaints from the same places of a simultaneous deficiency and excess of labour.\" A temporary and local shortage of labour \"does not bring about a rise in wages, but rather forces the women and children into the fields, and constantly lowers the age at which exploitation begins.\" As soon as the exploitation of women and children takes place on a large scale, \"it becomes in turn a new means of making the male agricultural labourer 'redundant' and keeping down his wage.\" The finest fruit of this vicious circle is the gang-system in eastern England. The gang-system. It obtains almost exclusively in Lincolnshire, Huntingdonshire, Cambridgeshire, Norfolk, Suffolk, and Nottinghamshire, and sporadically in Northamptonshire, Bedfordshire, and Rutland. Lincolnshire serves as example: much of it is new land, formerly marsh or won from the sea. The steam-engine has worked wonders in drainage; what were fens now bear \"a luxuriant sea of corn, and very high ground rents.\" No new cottages were built; as new farms arose, old cottages were demolished, and labour had to come from \"open villages\" miles away. Labourers living on 1,000-acre farms (\"confined labourers\") do only permanent, difficult work requiring horses; for every 100 acres there is scarcely one cottage. A fenland farmer testified: \"I farm 320 acres, all arable land. I have not one cottage on my farm. I have only one labourer on my farm now. I have four horsemen lodging about. We get light work done by gangs.\" The gang consists of from ten to forty or fifty persons: women, young persons of both sexes (13–18, boys mostly eliminated at 13), and children of both sexes (6–13). At the head is the gang-master , \"always an ordinary agricultural labourer, and usually what is called a bad lot, a rake, unsteady, drunken, but with a dash of enterprise and savoir faire.\" He recruits the gang and negotiates with farmers over piece-work; his income depends on extracting maximum labour in minimum time. Farmers have discovered that women work steadily only under male direction, but that women and children, once set going, \"spend their vital forces impetuously — as Fourier already knew in his time — whereas the adult male worker is shrewd enough to economize on his strength as much as he can.\" The gang-master employs his gang six to eight months a year, making employment more lucrative and certain than through individual farmers; his influence in the open villages is thus riveted. The \"drawbacks\": over-working of children and young persons, enormous daily marches (five, six, sometimes seven miles), and demoralization. The gang-master, armed with a long stick, seldom uses it; he is \"a democratic emperor, or a kind of Pied Piper of Hamelin.\" He binds his subjects \"by the charms of the gipsy life.\" \"Coarse freedom, noisy jollity and the obscenest kind of impertinence give attractions to the gang.\" He pays up in a public house, then returns home at the head of a drunken procession. \"On the return journey what Fourier calls 'phanerogamie' is the order of the day.\" Girls of 13 and 14 are commonly made pregnant by male companions of the same age. The open villages \"become Sodoms and Gomorrahs,\" with twice as high a rate of illegitimacy as the rest of the kingdom. The private gangs are made up similarly but count fewer members and work under an old farm servant; \"the gipsy fun has vanished... but the payment and treatment of the children is worse.\" The gang-system \"clearly does not exist for the sake of the gang-master. It exists for the enrichment of the large-scale farmers and indirectly for the landowners.\" For the farmer, \"there is no more ingenious method of keeping his labourers well below the normal level, and yet of always having an extra hand ready for extra work, of extracting the greatest possible amount of labour with the least possible expenditure of money, and of making adult male labour 'redundant'.\" Hence the apparent paradox: a greater or lesser lack of employment for the agricultural labourer is admitted, while the gang-system is declared \"necessary\" on account of the shortage of adult male labour and its migration to the towns. \"The cleanly weeded land and the unclean human weeds of Lincolnshire are pole and counterpole of capitalist production.\" A footnote (note 17) notes that the Public Health Report treating the gang-system \"remains unknown to the press,\" while the Children's Employment Commission's report \"afforded the press sensational and welcome copy.\" The Liberal press asked how the fine gentlemen and ladies of Lincolnshire, who send missions to the South Sea Islands \"for the improvement of the morals of South Sea Islanders,\" could allow such a system. Marx's dry observation: \"Under the accursed conditions to which these 'delicate' people condemn the agricultural labourer, it would not be surprising if he ate his own children. What is really wonderful is the healthy integrity of character he has largely retained.\" The official reports prove that parents, even in gang districts, loathe the gang-system. (f) Ireland Marx concludes with Ireland as a case study of an extraordinary experiment. The population of Ireland grew to 8,222,664 by 1841; fell to 6,623,985 in 1851, to 5,850,309 in 1861, and to about 5½ millions in 1866 (approximately its 1801 level). The decrease began with the famine year of 1846; Ireland lost more than 5/16 of its people in less than twenty years. Emigration from May 1851 to July 1865: 1,591,487. Between 1861 and 1865: more than half a million. Inhabited houses fell from 1851 to 1861 by 52,990. Holdings of 15–30 acres increased by 61,000; over 30 acres by 109,000; total farms fell by 120,000; the fall was solely due to suppression of farms of less than 15 acres — \"in other words it was due to their centralization.\" Table A: Livestock. Between 1860 and 1865: horses decreased by 72,358; cattle decreased by 116,626; sheep increased by 146,608; pigs increased by 28,819. (The year-by-year figures given show fluctuations.) Table B: Area under crops and grass. Between 1861 and 1865: cereal crops decreased by 428,041 acres; green crops decreased by 107,984; grass and clover increased by 82,834; flax decreased by 122,850; total cultivated land decreased by 330,860. Table C: Comparison of 1865 with 1864. Wheat area down 9,494 acres (product per acre 13.3 to 13.0 cwt), total product down 48,999 qrs. Oats down 69,658 acres, product per acre up 0.2, total product down 166,605 qrs. Barley down 4,402 acres, product per acre down 1.0, total product down 29,892. Potatoes: area up 26,536 acres, product per acre down 0.5 tons, total product down 446,398 tons. Turnips: area down 3,143, product per acre down 0.4, total down 165,976. Flax: area down 50,260, product per acre down 9.0, total down 24,945. Hay: area up 68,924, product per acre up 0.2, total up 461,554. A footnote (note 20) provides later statistics (1868–1872) showing continued decline in wheat area (285,000 in 1868 down to 228,000 in 1872). Table D: Income tax. Rent of land (Schedule A): roughly £13.0–13.9 million annually 1860–65 (little change). Farmers' profits (Schedule B): roughly £2.77–2.95 million. Industrial etc. profits (Schedule D): roughly £4.5–4.9 million. Total Schedules A to E: roughly £23 million. The figures are stable, indicating that while population fell sharply, incomes did not. Table E: Schedule D income distribution for 1864 and 1865. Total yearly income: £4,368,610 divided among 17,467 persons in 1864; £4,669,979 among 18,081 in 1865. Income over £60 and under £100: 5,015 persons with £238,626; 4,703 with £222,575. Of total yearly income: 11,321 persons with £1,979,066; 12,184 with £2,028,471. Remainder: 1,131 persons with £2,150,818; 1,194 with £2,418,933. The table shows extreme concentration: in 1864, three persons took £262,610; in 1865, the same three took £274,528; 26 took £646,377 (1864), 28 took £736,448 (1865); 121 took £1,066,912 (1864), 186 took £1,320,996 (1865); 1,131 took £2,150,818 (1864), \"nearly half of the total annual profit\"; 1,194 took £2,418,933 (1865), \"more than half of the total annual profit.\" The average annual increase of income under Schedule D from 1853 to 1864 was only 0.93 per cent in Ireland, against 4.58 per cent in Great Britain. Marx's analysis. England, \"a pre-eminently industrial country with fully developed capitalist production, would have bled to death under such a population drain as Ireland has suffered. But Ireland is at present merely an agricultural district of England.\" The depopulation threw much land out of cultivation, diminished produce, and brought about an absolute decline in some branches of cattle breeding. Yet \"the rents of the land and the profits of the farmers increased along with the fall in the population.\" Why? Because (i) with the throwing together of smallholdings and the change from arable to pasture, a larger part of the total product was transformed into surplus product — the surplus product increased although the total product decreased; (ii) the monetary value of the surplus product increased still more rapidly than its quantity, owing to the rise in the price of meat, wool, etc. on the English market. Marx states a fundamental theoretical point: \"The scattered means of production that serve the producers themselves as means of employment and subsistence, without valorizing themselves through the incorporation of the labour of others, are no more capital than a product consumed by its producer is a commodity. If the mass of the means of production employed in agriculture diminished along with the mass of the population, the mass of the capital employed in agriculture increased, because a part of the means of production that were formerly scattered was turned into capital.\" This is the centralization of agricultural capital through the dispossession of smallholders. Total capital outside agriculture accumulated slowly, with fluctuations; but \"the concentration of its individual constituents\" developed rapidly, and \"its relative growth, in proportion to the diminishing population, was tremendous.\" The Malthusian experiment. Marx frames the Irish case as an experiment: \"Here then, under our own eyes, and on a large scale, there emerges a process which perfectly corresponds to the requirements of orthodox economics for the confirmation of its dogma, the dogma that misery springs from an absolute surplus of population, and that equilibrium is re-established by depopulation. This is a far more important experiment than the mid-fourteenth-century plague so celebrated by the Malthusians.\" He notes that the fourteenth-century plague in England led to the \"enfranchisement and enrichment of the agricultural population,\" whereas in France it led to \"a greater degree of enslavement and an increase in misery\" — a difference the Malthusians overlook. A footnote cites Thomas Sadler's Ireland: Its Evils, and Their Remedies (1829), which proved by comparing provinces and counties that misery there is not in proportion to population level, but in inverse ratio to it. The Irish famine of 1846 killed more than 1,000,000 people, \"but it killed poor devils only. It did not do the slightest damage to the wealth of the country.\" The exodus did not decimate the means of production. \"The Irish genius discovered an altogether new way of spiriting a poor people thousands of miles away from the scene of its misery.\" The exiles send money home as travelling expenses for those left behind; every emigrating troop draws another after it. \"Thus, instead of costing Ireland anything, emigration forms one of the most lucrative branches of its export trade.\" It is a systematic process, \"which does not simply make a passing gap in the population, but sucks out of it every year more people than are replaced by births.\" A footnote (note 25) notes total emigrants 1851–1874: 2,325,922. Consequences for those left behind. \"The relative surplus population is as great today as it was before 1846; wages are just as low; the oppression of the labourers has increased; misery is forcing the country towards a new crisis.\" The reasons: the revolution in agriculture kept pace with emigration; \"The production of a relative surplus population has more than kept pace with the absolute depopulation.\" The change from arable to pasture worked more acutely in Ireland than in England: in England, green crops increase with cattle-breeding; in Ireland they decrease. Smaller and medium farmers (those not cultivating more than 100 acres) still make up about 1/8 of the whole number (a footnote, note 26, says 94.6% of farms are smaller than 100 acres, while 5.4% exceed that amount). They are crushed by the competition of capital-managed agriculture and \"continually furnish new recruits to the class of wage-labourers.\" The linen industry requires relatively few adult men. The 'domestic industry' system renders workers 'redundant' through under-payment and over-work. Wages. The Reports of the Irish Poor Law Inspectors (1870) state wages rose by 50 to 60 per cent in twenty years, now averaging 6s. to 9s. a week. But this \"apparent rise hides an actual fall in wages,\" because it does not cancel the rise in the price of necessaries. The table: | Year ended | Provisions and necessaries | Clothing | Total | |---|---|---|---| | 29 September 1849 | 1s. 3¼d. | 3d. | 1s. 6¼d. | | 29 September 1869 | 2s. 7¼d. | 6d. | 3s. 1¼d. | The price of necessaries is approximately twice as high, and clothing exactly twice as high, as twenty years before. Moreover, before the famine, wages were largely paid in kind; today money payment is the rule, so any movement in real wage must have raised the money rate. Before the famine, the labourer enjoyed his cabin, a rood, half-acre or acre of land, facilities for a potato crop, a pig and fowl. \"But they now have to buy bread, and they have no refuse upon which they can feed a pig or fowl, and they have consequently no benefit from the sale of a pig, fowl, or eggs.\" The labourers were formerly indistinguishable from the smallest small farmers; only since 1846 have they begun to form \"a section of the class of pure wage-labourers, a special estate which is now connected with its masters only by monetary relations.\" Housing. Their living conditions have grown worse. Some day-labourers continue to live on farmers' holdings in \"overcrowded huts whose hideousness far surpasses the worst examples the agricultural districts of England can offer.\" An inspector: \"The agricultural labourers' huts are a disgrace to the Christianity and to the civilization of this country.\" To make the holes more attractive, the pieces of land that belonged to them are systematically confiscated. \"The mere sense that they exist subject to this species of ban... has given birth in the minds of the labourers to corresponding sentiments of antagonism and dissatisfaction towards those by whom they are thus led to regard themselves as being treated as... a proscribed race.\" The first act of the agricultural revolution was to sweep away the huts at the place of work; labourers were compelled into villages and towns, \"thrown like refuse into garrets, holes, cellars and corners, in the worst slum districts.\" Thousands of Irish families \"notable for their rare attachment to the domestic hearth, for the gaiety and the purity of their home life, suddenly found themselves transplanted into hotbeds of vice.\" Men now seek work daily from neighbouring farmers, \"under the most precarious form of wage,\" walking long distances, getting wet, \"not infrequently ending in sickness, disease and want.\" \"The towns have had to receive from year to year what was deemed to be the surplus-labour of the rural division\" — and then people wonder that \"there is still a surplus of labour in the towns and villages, and either a scarcity or a threatened scarcity in some of the country divisions.\" The scarcity only appears \"in harvest-time, or during spring\"; at other periods many hands are idle; \"from the digging out of the main crop of potatoes in October until the early spring following... there is no employment for them\"; and during active times they are \"subject to broken days and to all kinds of interruptions.\" The model landlords aggravate matters: instead of spending rents abroad, they live on their demesnes and draw \"labour-supply... chiefly from their small tenants, who are obliged to attend when required to do the landlords' work, at rates of wages, in many cases, considerably under the current rates paid to ordinary labourers, and without regard to the inconvenience or loss to the tenant of being obliged to neglect his own business at critical periods of sowing or reaping.\" Marx's summary comparison. \"The uncertainty and irregularity of employment, the constant return and long duration of gluts of labour, are all symptoms of a relative surplus population.\" The difference between England and Ireland: \"in England, an industrial country, the industrial reserve is recruited from the countryside, whereas in Ireland, an agricultural country, the agricultural reserve is recruited from the towns, the places of refuge of the agricultural labourers who have been driven from the land. In England, the surplus rural labourers are transformed into factory workers; in Ireland, those forced into the towns remain agricultural labourers even while they exert a downward pressure on urban wages, and are constantly sent back to the countryside in search of work.\" The inspectors describe the material condition: \"Though living with the strictest frugality, his own wages are barely sufficient to provide food for an ordinary family and pay his rent, and he depends upon other sources for the means of clothing himself, his wife, and his children... The atmosphere of these cabins, combined with the other privations they are subjected to, has made this class particularly susceptible to typhus and consumption.\" \"It is no wonder that, according to the unanimous testimony of the inspectors, a sombre discontent runs through the ranks of this class, that they long for the return of the past, loathe the present, despair of the future, give themselves up 'to the evil influence of agitators', and have only one fixed idea, to emigrate to America. This is the land of Cockaigne, into which depopulation, the great Malthusian panacea, has transformed green Erin!\" The Irish factory worker. Marx quotes Robert Baker's evidence verbatim from \"Johnson, a beetler\" (embossing fabrics under rollers): works from 6 in the morning till 11 at night, Monday to Friday, Saturday leaving at 6 p.m., with three hours off for meals; five children; earns 10s. 6d. a week; wife works, gets 5s. The oldest girl, 12, minds the house and is \"cook, and all the servant we have.\" The family gets nothing to eat before work; tea once a week; otherwise stirabout of oatmeal or Indian meal; sometimes a little milk. \"So we go on from day to day, Sunday and week day, always the same the year round. I am always very much tired when I have done at night. We may see a bit of flesh meat sometimes, but very seldom.\" Three children attend school at 1d. a week each. Rent 9d. a week; peat for firing 1s. 6d. a fortnight at the lowest. \"Such are Irish wages, such is Irish life!\" Lord Dufferin and the Malthusian finale. At the end of 1866 and beginning of 1867, Lord Dufferin, an Irish land magnate, set about solving the problem in The Times . Marx's German exclamation from Goethe's Faust : \"Wie menschlich von solch' grossem Herrn!\" (\"What humanity from such a great lord!\"). Marx recapitulates the concentration from Table E: the three money-grubbers, the 26, 28, 121, 186, 1,131, 1,194. \"But the lion's share of the yearly national rental which an inconceivably small number of land magnates in England, Scotland and Ireland swallow up is so monstrous that English statesmanship finds it inappropriate to afford the same statistical materials about the distribution of rents as about the distribution of profits. Lord Dufferin is one of those land magnates.\" Dufferin declares Ireland is still over-populated, that depopulation benefits the landlords and therefore the soil, and hence the people. He demands a new blood-letting of one-third of a million. Marx cites the \"school of Sangrado\" (from Gil Blas ), who ordered blood-letting after blood-letting until the patient died of the cure. Dufferin demands 300,000, \"instead of about two millions.\" Marx then provides the calculation from the 1864 farm table: | Category | Farms | Acres | |---|---|---| | Not over 1 acre | 48,653 | 25,394 | | Over 1, not over 5 | 82,037 | 288,916 | | Over 5, not over 15 | 176,368 | 1,836,310 | | Over 15, not over 30 | 136,578 | 3,051,343 | | Over 30, not over 50 | 71,961 | 2,906,274 | | Over 50, not over 100 | 54,247 | 3,983,880 | | Over 100 | 31,927 | 8,227,807 | | Total area | | 26,319,924 | Centralization from 1851 to 1861 mainly destroyed farms of the first three categories (under 15 acres), giving 307,058 \"surplus\" farmers, and — at a low average of four persons per family — 1,228,232 persons. Assuming a quarter can be absorbed, 921,174 remain for emigration. Categories 4, 5, and 6 (15–100 acres) are too small for capitalist corn cultivation and \"almost infinitesimal\" for sheep-breeding; another 788,761 persons to emigrate; grand total 1,709,532. \"And, as appetite grows with eating, Rent Roll's eyes will soon discover that Ireland with 3½ millions, still continues to be miserable, miserable because she is overpopulated. Therefore her depopulation must go still further, in order that she may fulfil her true destiny, to be an English sheep-walk and cattle pasture.\" A footnote (note 41) quotes Nassau W. Senior's posthumous Journals, Conversations, and Essays Relating to Ireland (1868): \"'Well,' said Dr G., 'we have got our Poor Law and it is a great instrument for giving the victory to the landlords. Another, and a still more powerful instrument is emigration... No friend to Ireland can wish the war to be prolonged... The sooner it is over — the sooner Ireland becomes a grazing country, with the comparatively thin population which a grazing country requires, the better for all classes'.\" The footnote explains the historical context: the English Corn Laws of 1815 secured to Ireland a monopoly of free corn importation into Great Britain, artificially encouraging corn cultivation; their abolition in 1846 removed this monopoly and gave \"a great impulse to the conversion of Irish arable land into pasture, to the concentration of farms, and to the eviction of small-scale cultivators.\" Having praised the fruitfulness of the Irish soil between 1815 and 1846 as destined by nature for wheat, \"English agronomists, economists and politicians suddenly discovered that it was good for nothing but the production of forage.\" The final dialectical twist. Marx closes with an irony: \"Like all good things in the world, this profitable mode of proceeding has its drawbacks. The accumulation of the Irish in America keeps pace with the accumulation of rents in Ireland. The Irishman, banished by the sheep and the ox, re-appears on the other side of the ocean as a Fenian. And there a young but gigantic republic rises, more and more threateningly, to face the old queen of the waves.\" He quotes Horace: \"Acerba fata Romanos agunt / Scelusque fraternae necis\" (\"A cruel fate torments the Romans, and the crime of fratricide\"). The suggestion is clear: England's colonial policy in Ireland generates the Irish-American population that will return as a political threat — a threat linked thematically to the \"fratricide\" of the class and national division. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principle: The Subject-Object Inversion The organising principle of the entire chapter is the dialectical inversion of the means of production into means of domination. Marx's crucial formulation in Section 4 — \"a system in which the worker does not employ the means of production, but the means of production employ the worker\" — is the philosophical fulcrum. Under capitalism, the productive forces developed by labour become an independent power that confronts and dominates labour. Every increase in the productivity of labour, every expansion of the means of production, every advance in science and technology, instead of liberating the producer, renders the producer's position more precarious. The chapter thus delivers the theoretical core of Marx's theory of alienation in its specifically economic form: the worker creates capital, and capital enslaves the worker. This yields a fundamental \"systemic law\": accumulation is structurally antagonistic. The accumulation of wealth at one pole is the accumulation of misery at the other — not as two separate facts linked by chance, but as two expressions of the same relation. The \"absolute general law\" is therefore not a moral indictment appended to an economic analysis; the moral indictment is the economic analysis. The relative surplus population is simultaneously product, lever, and condition of accumulation. Wages are regulated by the reserve army, not by population. And the working population produces \"the means by which it is itself made relatively superfluous.\" The Refutation of the \"Natural Law of Population\" The chapter's most important polemical achievement is its dissolution of the Malthusian \"natural law of population.\" Marx's move is threefold. First, he identifies the independent variable : the rate of accumulation, not the rate of population growth, drives the wage rate. Second, he demonstrates the historical specificity of population laws: \"every particular historical mode of production has its own special laws of population.\" Third, he furnishes the empirical counterexample of Ireland, where a massive population decrease did not improve the condition of the remaining workers but instead consolidated the relative surplus population and worsened their condition of dependence. The Malthusian \"experiment\" was performed on a grand scale, and it refuted rather than confirmed the doctrine. The modern parallel will be immediate to any reader: the chapter demonstrates that poverty is not produced by the reproductive behaviour of the poor but by the structural requirements of capital. The Distinction Between Concentration and Centralization The rigorous separation of concentration (growth of individual capitals through accumulation, limited by social wealth) from centralization (attraction of already-formed capitals, not limited by accumulation) is a theoretical achievement with enduring significance. Centralization is the more rapid and potent process: it can accomplish \"in the twinkling of an eye\" what accumulation would take generations to do (the railway example). It works through competition and credit, and its limit is the fusion of all capital into a single hand. This anticipates later developments in finance capital, the credit system, and the analysis of trusts and monopolies (noted by Engels). It is the theoretical bridge from the logic of individual capitals to the logic of the social capital as a whole. The Industrial Reserve Army as the Regulator of Wages The concept of the industrial reserve army is the chapter's most enduring contribution to labor economics. Its lesson is that the labour market is not a self-equilibrating system of independent supply and demand, but a mechanism through which capital acts on both sides at once. Capital determines both the demand for labour (through the variable component of its capital) and the supply of labour (through the \"setting free\" of workers and the over-work of the employed). The reserve army is the \"background\" against which the law of supply and demand does its work, \"confining the field of action of this law to the limits absolutely convenient to capital.\" The corollary is that the \"sacred law of supply and demand\" is invoked by capital only as a rhetorical weapon — and abandoned in the colonies when it does not serve capital's interest. The Dialectic of Over-work and Idleness One of the chapter's most striking insights is the dialectic whereby \"the condemnation of one part of the working class to enforced idleness by the over-work of the other part, and vice versa, becomes a means of enriching the individual capitalists.\" This anticipates the modern analysis of precarious employment, zero-hours contracts, and the intensification of labour. The over-work of the employed increases the reserve army; the competition of the reserve army forces the employed to submit to over-work. The mechanism is self-reinforcing and makes the condition of the employed worker (as Marx says in his most general formulation) grow worse \"be his payment high or low.\" The Method: Theory and Empirical Illustration The chapter models Marx's mature method. Section 1–4 develop the theory at successive levels of abstraction; Section 5 provides a massive empirical apparatus. The statistics are not decorative but essential: they demonstrate the law's operation and refute its rivals. The nutrition tables, the housing reports, the Belgian budget, the convict-diet comparison, the Irish tables — all serve to show that the theory is not a deduction from abstract premises but a comprehension of historical reality. The interspersed commentary (Gladstone, Fawcett, the Morning Star , the Standard , the Poor Law inspectors) gives voice to the historical actors and demonstrates the ideological obfuscation the theory dissolves. The Theory of Relative Impoverishment The chapter establishes the position — later made canonical in Marx's own correspondence and in the Marxist tradition — that the condition of the worker \"must grow worse\" in relative terms even when it improves in absolute terms. The \"golden chain\" metaphor captures this precisely: the wage-labourer may be better fed, better clothed, and better treated than the slave, but the measure of dependence is the relation , not the level of material welfare. The worker's improvement is a \"quantitative reduction in the amount of unpaid labour\" that can never threaten the system. This distinction between absolute and relative condition is the theoretical foundation of the entire critique of reformist optimism within capitalism. Broader Historical Implications The chapter's final section on Ireland establishes the colonial dimension of accumulation. The Irish case demonstrates that the \"relative surplus population\" cannot be understood within a single national economy; emigration, colonial land policy, the world market, and the extraction of surplus across borders are integral to the process. The Irish \"fratricide\" quotation points to the political consequences: the export of the Irish as \"surplus\" population generates a new political force (the Fenians, and more broadly the Irish diaspora in the United States) that returns as a threat. This is a prototype of Marx's later analyses of colonialism and world market relations, developed in Capital Volume 3 and in the writings on India, China, and Ireland. The lesson: capitalist accumulation is a world-historical process, and the reserve army is a global phenomenon. The Promise and the Failure of Classical Political Economy Marx's engagement with Smith, Ricardo, Barton, Jones, Ramsay, Sismondi, Storch, and Ortes reveals a nuanced position. He credits them with having \"suspected rather than understood\" the law of the progressive diminution of variable capital. He treats Ricardo and Barton as having approached the truth, and Sismondi and Storch as having registered the antagonism — while remaining trapped in the assumption that it is either a natural law (Ortes) or a lamentable fall from a prior harmony (Sismondi). Marx's advance is to show that the antagonism is specific to a particular mode of production and therefore historically transformable . The chapter's final lesson is thus a revolutionary one: because the law is not natural but historical, it can be superseded — but only by a movement that understands its mechanism and its contradictions. The \"absolute general law of capitalist accumulation\" is not a fatalistic prophecy but an analysis of the structural limits of a historically determinate system, and therefore an invitation to its transformation. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 24 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 26"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 26", "title": "Chapter 26", "subtitle": "The Secret of Primitive Accumulation", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 26/Chapter 26  The Secret of Primitive Accumulation_explanation.html", "word_count": 4213, "read_time": "21 min read", "tags": ["Primitive Accumulation & Enclosures"], "keywords": ["The Secret of Primitive Accumulation", "Historical Divorce between Producer and Means of Production", "Critique of Political Economy's 'Nursery Tale'", "Pre-History of Capital", "Violence and Force as Midwife of History"], "text": "Chapter 26 The Secret Of Primitive Accumulation - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 4,174 words ⏱️ ~21 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS: This chapter—the opening of Part Eight of Capital , Volume I, titled \"So-Called Primitive Accumulation\"—is Marx's most concentrated attack on the received origin story of capitalism. Its central problem is a genuine logical circularity that Marx locates within classical political economy itself: money becomes capital, capital generates surplus-value, surplus-value becomes more capital, and that capital accumulation presupposes surplus-value; surplus-value presupposes capitalist production; and capitalist production presupposes \"the availability of considerable masses of capital and labour-power in the hands of commodity producers.\" The whole argument of the preceding twenty-five chapters thus appears to turn in a \"never-ending circle.\" Marx's stated intellectual objective is to break that circle by demonstrating that the escape clause accepted by bourgeois economists—the presupposition of some antecedent, non-capitalist accumulation—is not a benign historical footnote but a systematically concealed history of violence, expropriation, and coercion. Marx's thesis is double-barrelled. First, on the logical-demonstrative plane, he names this presupposed origin \"primitive accumulation\"—the \"previous accumulation\" that Adam Smith had invoked in Book II of The Wealth of Nations —and defines it precisely: it is \"the historical process of divorcing the producer from the means of production.\" It is called \"primitive\" only because it forms \"the pre-history of capital, and of the mode of production corresponding to capital.\" It is not the result of the capitalist mode of production, but its point of departure. Second, on the ideological-critical plane, Marx unmasks the moral tale that political economy substitutes for this history: the fable of a diligent, frugal elite and a lazy, riotous rabble—a fable he derisively labels \"insipid childishness\" and compares to the doctrine of original sin. The function of that fable is apologetic: it naturalises the wealth of the few and the dispossession of the many, converting a violent rupture into a just reward for virtue. The chapter is therefore a hinge-text in Marx's wider system. It transitions from the analysis of capital as a \"logical\" form—built up through the categories of commodity, money, surplus-value, and accumulation—to the analysis of capital as a historical product , whose emergence must be narrated. It announces the sequence that the following chapters will unfold (the expropriation of the agricultural population, the bloody legislation against vagabondage, the genesis of the capitalist farmer and the industrial capitalist, and the historical tendency of capitalist accumulation), and it fixes England as the exemplary case, \"the classic form\" of a process that nonetheless assumes different aspects in different countries and epochs. Above all, the chapter performs a methodological pivot. It refuses the \"idyllic\" mode of explanation, insists that \"conquest, enslavement, robbery, murder, in short, force, play the greatest part\" in actual history, and thereby establishes the interpretive frame in which the birth of the working class is treated not as emancipation but as expropriation \"written in the annals of mankind in letters of blood and fire.\" The chapter's objective is to expose the \"secret\" of primitive accumulation—its secret being that capital's origin is inseparable from the coerced separation of producers from the conditions of production. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS: 2.1 The Circularity Problem and the \"Point of Departure\" Marx opens not with history but with logic. He rehearses the argument of the preceding chapters as a chain of dependencies: money is transformed into capital; surplus-value is made through capital; more capital is made from surplus-value. Each link presupposes the prior one. Accumulation presupposes surplus-value; surplus-value presupposes capitalist production; capitalist production presupposes \"the availability of considerable masses of capital and labour-power in the hands of commodity producers.\" The circle is complete, and to break it, political economy must posit something outside the sequence—a stock of wealth and a supply of free labourers that existed before the capitalist relation proper. This is the concept of primitive accumulation (German ursprüngliche Akkumulation ; rendered by Smith as \"previous accumulation\"). Marx accepts the necessity of positing such a presupposition but radically re-specifies it: it is \"an accumulation which is not the result of the capitalist mode of production but its point of departure.\" The word \"accumulation\" is itself contested. For classical political economy, it denotes an amassing of wealth through saving; for Marx, as the chapter will show, it denotes an amassing of poverty—a stockpiling, as it were, of dispossessed and rightless labourers. The Adam Smith footnote is not ornamental. Marx quotes Smith directly: \"'The accumulation of stock must, in the nature of things, be previous to the division of labour' ( Wealth of Nations , Bk II, Introduction).\" The footnote pins the origin of the concept to the founding text of political economy and marks Marx's debt-and-critique relationship to the classical tradition: he adopts Smith's term, then empties it of its thrift-and-frugality connotation and refills it with a theory of forcible separation. 2.2 Original Sin: The Ideological Function of the Nursery Tale Marx then executes one of the most famous polemical moves in Capital : the analogy between primitive accumulation and theological original sin. The analogy is precise and satirical. Adam bit the apple; sin fell upon the entire human race. In both cases, \"its origin is supposed to be explained when it is told as an anecdote about the past.\" The form of explanation is not causal analysis but mythological narration—a story whose narrative form masks its lack of analytical content. The structure of the economic fable is then laid out: \"Long, long ago there were two sorts of people; one, the diligent, intelligent and above all frugal elite; the other, lazy rascals, spending their substance, and more, in riotous living.\" The former accumulated wealth; the latter \"finally had nothing to sell except their own skins.\" Marx notes the satirical asymmetry built into the fable: theological original sin explains why all men must eat their bread \"in the sweat of [their] brow,\" but the economic version \"reveals to us that there are people to whom this is by no means essential\"—that is, an entire propertied class whose wealth grows \"although they have long ceased to work.\" The fable thus naturalises the very asymmetry it purports to explain. Marx's language of contempt is analytically deliberate. He calls the tale \"insipid childishness,\" and observes that \"as soon as the question of property is at stake, it becomes a sacred duty to proclaim the standpoint of the nursery tale as the one thing fit for all age-groups and all stages of development.\" The point is that the fable is not a mere error of historical scholarship but a strategic ideological instrument, mobilised precisely at the moment when property is under challenge. The concrete target is the French statesman and historian Adolphe Thiers—notoriously the man who ordered the suppression of the Paris Commune—who \"still repeats it with all the solemnity of a statesman to the French people, who were once so full of wit and ingenuity.\" The pivot is now complete: \"In actual history, it is a notorious fact that conquest, enslavement, robbery, murder, in short, force, play the greatest part.\" The idyllic fable of \"right and 'labour'\" as \"the sole means of enrichment\" is dispatched as a fiction whose only exception is always \"this year\"—a perpetually deferred exception that protects the fable from falsification. By contrast, \"the methods of primitive accumulation are anything but idyllic.\" 2.3 The Structural Definition of Capital: Two Classes, One Confrontation Having demolished the fable, Marx supplies his own account of what must have occurred. His first point is a categorical disambiguation: \"In themselves, money and commodities are no more capital than the means of production and subsistence are. They need to be transformed into capital.\" This is a decisive break with any notion that capital is a thing —that gold, tools, or grain are inherently capital. Capital is a relation , and the transformation of money into capital requires \"particular circumstances, which meet together at this point: the confrontation of, and the contact between, two very different kinds of commodity owners.\" Those two classes are defined with analytical precision. On the one hand, \"the owners of money, means of production, means of subsistence, who are eager to valorize the sum of values they have appropriated by buying the labour-power of others.\" Valorize here renders the German verwerten : the act by which a value is not merely preserved but increased—put to work to yield surplus-value. The buying of others' labour-power is the chosen mechanism of valorization. On the other hand, \"free workers, the sellers of their own labour-power, and therefore the sellers of labour.\" Marx equates \"sellers of labour-power\" with \"sellers of labour,\" the sale of capacity with the sale of activity, because it is the power to labour, not the labour itself, that is contracted for and commanded by the capitalist. \"With the polarization of the commodity-market into these two classes, the fundamental conditions of capitalist production are present.\" The commodity-market must therefore be understood not as a homogeneous space of equal exchangers but as a polarized one—purchaser and seller who are also, on a deeper level, oppressor and oppressed. 2.4 The Double Freedom of the Wage-Labourer Marx then famously defines freedom \"in the double sense.\" The wage-labourer is free (a) because he \"neither form[s] part of the means of production [himself], as would be the case with slaves, serfs, etc.\"—he is a legal person, a contracting subject, not a chattel or a bound labourer; and (b) because he does not \"own the means of production, as would be the case with self-employed peasant proprietors.\" The double definition is a piece of conceptual engineering: it isolates the precise disjunction that separates the wage-labourer from both the slave and the independent peasant. He is both legally free and materially propertyless. Marx adds a sharpening restatement: \"The free workers are therefore free from, unencumbered by, any means of production of their own.\" The pun on \"free\"—liberation from bondage and dispossession from property—becomes the chapter's ironising core: freedom in the first sense is inseparable from unfreedom in the second. The logical payoff is immediate: \"The capital-relation presupposes a complete separation between the workers and the ownership of the conditions for the realization of their labour.\" Separation—not scarcity, not technology, not thrift—is the constitutive precondition of the wage relation. 2.5 Reproduction on an Extended Scale: How the Relation Becomes Self-Sustaining Marx then inserts a dynamic proposition of great importance: \"As soon as capitalist production stands on its own feet, it not only maintains this separation, but reproduces it on a constantly extending scale.\" Once the initial separation is established, capitalism no longer requires external violence to sustain it—the ordinary operation of buying and selling labour-power endlessly regenerates the class that has nothing to sell but itself. This is the theoretical resolve to the opening circularity: the circle is broken historically, by an initial act of separation; thereafter, the system can run on its own momentum. The phrase \"on a constantly extending scale\" carries the sense of what contemporary readers would call path dependence and cumulative causation: separation, once achieved, compounds. 2.6 Primitive Accumulation Defined: The Process with Two Transformations The definition now crystallises: \"The process, therefore, which creates the capital-relation can be nothing other than the process which divorces the worker from the ownership of the conditions of his own labour.\" This process \"operates two transformations, whereby the social means of subsistence and production are turned into capital, and the immediate producers are turned into wage-labourers.\" The two transformations are the mirror-imaged poles of a single event: what becomes capital on one side becomes labour-power on the other. Nothing is created ex nihilo; a single social relation is reorganised. The closing formula of the section is the chapter's most cited sentence: \"So-called primitive accumulation, therefore, is nothing else than the historical process of divorcing the producer from the means of production. It appears as 'primitive' because it forms the pre-history of capital, and of the mode of production corresponding to capital.\" The word \"so-called\" signals Marx's critical reservation about the received term: he keeps the vocabulary of political economy but refills it with a contrary content. 2.7 The Dissolution of Feudalism and the Two-Sidedness of Emancipation Attention now turns to the historical terrain: \"The economic structure of capitalist society has grown out of the economic structure of feudal society. The dissolution of the latter set free the elements of the former.\" The metaphor of setting free the elements is precise: feudalism did not create capitalism's elements—money, tools, labourers—but contained them in a bound and crystallised form. Dissolution releases them into mobility. Marx then specifies the two barriers whose removal was required before the producer could become a \"free seller of labour-power.\" First, personal bondage: he had to cease to be \"bound to the soil\" and cease \"to be the slave or serf of another person\"—otherwise he could not \"dispose of his own person.\" Second, corporate restriction: he had to escape \"the regime of the guilds, their rules for apprentices and journeymen, and their restrictive labour regulations,\" so that he could carry \"his commodity wherever he can find a market for it.\" The legal and corporate fetters had to be dissolved before labour-power could circulate as a commodity. Hence the crucial dialectical observation: \"the historical movement which changes the producers into wage-labourers appears, on the one hand, as their emancipation from serfdom and from the fetters of the guilds, and it is this aspect of the movement which alone exists for our bourgeois historians.\" The phrase \"alone exists for our bourgeois historians\" is the sting. Bourgeois historiography sees only liberation—only the formal, juridical side. \"But, on the other hand, these newly freed men became sellers of themselves only after they had been robbed of all their own means of production, and all the guarantees of existence afforded by the old feudal arrangements.\" Emancipation from feudalism meant, in the same stroke, loss of the customary rights, common lands, and patronal protections that had made bare survival possible. The chapter's most famous line now follows: \"And this history, the history of their expropriation, is written in the annals of mankind in letters of blood and fire.\" The metaphor asserts both that the record exists—in annals—and that its content is coercion and violence, indelibly inscribed. 2.8 The Industrial Capitalists and the Base Means of Ascent The focus shifts from the producer to the capitalist. \"The industrial capitalists, these new potentates, had on their part not only to displace the guild masters of handicrafts, but also the feudal lords, who were in possession of the sources of wealth.\" The metaphor \"potentates\"—sovereign powers—indicates that the capitalist is a lord of a new kind, a rival to both old orders. Marx is careful, however, to note how the narrative of struggle has classically been framed: \"the rise of the industrial capitalists appears as the fruit of a victorious struggle both against feudal power and its disgusting prerogatives, and against the guilds, and the fetters by which the latter restricted the free development of production and the free exploitation of man by man.\" Note the nearly identical repetition of the word \"free\": the guilds restricted \"free development of production\" and \"free exploitation of man by man.\" Marx is showing that \"freedom\" in bourgeois rhetoric means freedom for exploitation—two sides of the same coin. Then comes the crucial derogatory insistence: \"The knights of industry, however, only succeeded in supplanting the knights of the sword by making use of events in which they had played no part whatsoever.\" The industrial bourgeoisie rose not by its own heroic agency but by parasitising historical cataclysms—the dissolution of feudalism, the enclosures, the price revolution, the Reformation's seizure of Church lands—whose causes it did not author. The dignity claimed by the bourgeoisie is borrowed. Marx caps the point with a classical allusion: \"They rose by means as base as those once used by the Roman freedman to make himself the master of his patronus.\" The allusion is historically dense: in Roman law, a freedman ( libertus ) could amass wealth and, under some conditions, eclipse the master ( patronus ) who had freed him—but only through a status that began in slavery. The industrialist's origin, similarly, lies in a servile condition and in the misuse of the very change that nominally liberated him. 2.9 The Actual Starting Point: Enslavement and the Change in the Form of Servitude Now comes the chapter's most radical historical claim: \"The starting-point of the development that gave rise both to the wage-labourer and to the capitalist was the enslavement of the worker.\" The defining fact of modern labour history is not a fall from prior liberty; it is a continuity of bondage. What is new is form, not fact: \"The advance made consisted in a change in the form of this servitude, in the transformation of feudal exploitation into capitalist exploitation.\" This is a polemical reversal of the liberal narrative of progress. Where the classical economists saw a movement from bondage to contract, Marx sees a change of outward legal form—from status to contract, as Maine would later say—over an unchanging substrate of servitude. The advance is real but formal: the worker now consents to his exploitation rather than being legally bound to it, but the substantive relation of command and dependence remains. Marx then sets the temporal frame: \"To understand the course taken by this change, we do not need to go back very far at all.\" He notes the \"first sporadic traces of capitalist production as early as the fourteenth or fifteenth centuries in certain towns of the Mediterranean,\" but firmly anchors the era: \"the capitalist era dates from the sixteenth century.\" The commercial revolutions of the late fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, the discovery of the Americas, the opening of Atlantic trade routes, and the price revolution—all unnamed but implied—constitute the historical horizon. The chapter's final substantive observation in this vein is a compressed historical-structural statement: \"Wherever it appears, the abolition of serfdom has long since been completed, and the most brilliant achievement of the Middle Ages, the existence of independent city-states, has already been on the wane for a considerable length of time.\" Two conditions of the capitalist sixteenth century are thus specified: prior serf abolition, and the decline of autonomous urban republics. The latter phrase—\"the most brilliant achievement of the Middle Ages\"—carries an unmistakable note of eulogy. Marx, who elsewhere skewers the mediocrity of bourgeois ideology, here grants the medieval communes their achievement: independent, self-governing, and now passing away before the centralised territorial state that capitalism required. 2.10 Epoch-Making Revolutions and the English Classic Form Marx now abstracts the general pattern. \"In the history of primitive accumulation, all revolutions are epoch-making that act as levers for the capitalist class in the course of its formation.\" The word \"levers\" ( Hebel ) indicates that political and legal convulsions function instrumentally: they are the means by which the class is levered into dominance. But the decisive subset is specified: \"above all for those moments when great masses of men are suddenly and forcibly torn from their means of subsistence, and hurled onto the labour-market as free, unprotected and rightless proletarians.\" The triple adjective—\"free, unprotected and rightless\"—crystallises the paradox: freedom (from feudal bonds) is inseparable from exposure (unprotected) and legal weakness (rightless). The proletarian is thus not merely a labourer but a person whose entire subsistence depends on the sale of his capacity to work. The quintessence of the process follows: \"The expropriation of the agricultural producer, of the peasant, from the soil is the basis of the whole process.\" The peasant's relation to the soil—his customary possession, however legally imperfect—is the last material basis of independence; only its severance completes the divorce of producer from means of production. This is why land dispossession, not urban artisan displacement, is the primary lever in Marx's narrative. Then the methodological caveat: this history \"assumes different aspects in different countries, and runs through its various phases in different orders of succession, and at different historical epochs.\" Comparative variation is acknowledged, not suppressed. Nonetheless: \"Only in England, which we therefore take as our example, has it the classic form.\" The concept of a \"classic form\" does double work: it privileges England as the pure case, and it licenses Marx to treat English history as an abstract model whose moments reappear in distorted variants elsewhere. 2.11 The Italian Footnote: Comparative Counterpoint The chapter ends with a long footnote on Italy, which is methodologically more significant than its ancillary position suggests. It supplies the first illustration of the comparative variation Marx has just announced. In Italy, \"where capitalist production developed earliest, the dissolution of serfdom also took place earlier than elsewhere.\" But the Italian case confirms Marx's core thesis by inverting its common presentation: \"the serf was emancipated before he had acquired any prescriptive right to the soil\"—that is, freed before he could consolidate any customary claim. His emancipation \"at once transformed him into a 'free' proletarian, without any legal rights.\" The scare-quotes around \"free\" mark the irony: nominal freedom and substantive dispossession arrive together. And the dispossessed found their exploiters waiting: \"he found a master ready and waiting for him in the towns, which had been for the most part handed down from Roman times.\" Urban Italy's continuous municipal inheritance from antiquity supplied the labour-buyers ready to absorb the emancipated. The footnote then records a striking counter-movement. When, \"at about the end of the fifteenth century,\" the revolution in the world market \"had annihilated northern Italy's commercial supremacy, a movement in the reverse direction set in. The urban workers were driven en masse into the countryside, and gave a previously unheard-of impulse to small-scale cultivation, carried on in the form of market gardening.\" The Italian case thus illustrates not only capitalist formation but its unmaking : deindustrialisation of sorts, the forced ruralisation of the urban proletariat, and a re-rooting of labour in the soil—the reverse of the English process. Marx includes this note to demonstrate that the \"classic form\" is not a universal pattern but the pure case against which other national trajectories must be read. The secret of primitive accumulation is disclosed in England; but its disclosure requires a comparative optic that recognises Italy's divergent sequence. The footnote also performs a quiet structural closure: it returns us to the concept of separation . Where England separated the producer from the soil slowly , through enclosures and legislation, Italy separated him suddenly , through premature emancipation without rights—and then, in crisis, reversed the polarity. Either way, the axis of analysis is the same: the producer's relation to his means of production, and the coercive power that restructures it. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS: Three fundamental principles emerge from this chapter. First, capital is a social relation, not a thing : on this view, money, tools, and raw materials \"need to be transformed into capital,\" and their transformation depends entirely on the confronting of two historically produced classes—owners of money and means of production, and propertyless possessors of labour-power. Second, the origin of that relation is not economic but violent : political economy's \"previous accumulation\" is, in Marx's reformulation, the forcible divorce of producers from the conditions of production, whose record is \"written in letters of blood and fire.\" Third, freedom and dispossession are coeval : the \"double freedom\" of the wage-labourer is simultaneously liberation from feudal or corporate bondage and release from any means of subsistence—a condition whose juridical form (contract, free labour) masks the substantive fact of dependence. The methodological lessons are equally important. Marx demonstrates how an ideological fable operates: the \"nursery tale\" of thrift and idleness is not discredited by evidence but mobilised as a sacred defence of property, precisely because it converts an expropriatory event into a moral verdict on the dispossessed. He shows that historical explanation requires recognising different national sequences—England as the \"classic form,\" Italy as premature and then reversed—and that the \"levers\" of capitalist class-formation are often events in which the capitalist class itself played no authorial part. Finally, he reframes revolution itself: the epochs that matter most are those in which \"great masses of men are suddenly and forcibly torn from their means of subsistence.\" The practical and theoretical implications are extensive. Theoretically, the chapter supplies the historical ground of the dialectical argument of the preceding twenty-five chapters: the \"circle\" of accumulation is broken not by an abstract precondition but by a specific and datable violence. It sets the programme for the chapters that follow—enclosures, the bloody legislation against vagabondage, the colonial regime, the modern theory of colonisation—by announcing that \"the expropriation of the agricultural producer ... is the basis of the whole process.\" Politically, it strips the bourgeois narrative of \"emancipation\" of its innocence, insisting that the producers' freedom to sell their labour was purchased by their freedom from property. Philosophically, it raises the enduring question of how the very liberal categories of contract, consent, and equality of exchange rest upon an original inequality established outside the market—a question that distinguishes Marx's analysis from every political economy that treats the market as a self-founding order. The secret is thus disclosed: capital did not arise from thrift and virtue, but from separation, coercion, and expropriation, and its \"primitive\" pre-history is the unwritten—or written-in-blood—precondition of its apparently civilised present. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 25 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 27"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 27", "title": "Chapter 27", "subtitle": "The Expropriation of the Agricultural Population from the Land", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 27/Chapter 27  The Expropriation of the Agricultural Population from the Land_explanation.html", "word_count": 10222, "read_time": "51 min read", "tags": ["Primitive Accumulation & Enclosures"], "keywords": ["Expropriation of the Agricultural Population", "Enclosure of Commons & Sheep Walks", "Dissolution of Monasteries & Royal Domains", "Clearing of Estates (Highland Clearances)", "Creation of Landless Proletarians"], "text": "Chapter 27 The Expropriation Of The Agricultural Population From The Land - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 10,145 words ⏱️ ~51 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 27: The Expropriation of the Agricultural Population from the Land An Exhaustive Deconstruction and Analysis 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 27 is the opening movement of the celebrated Part Eight of Capital , Volume One, the section Marx himself labels \"So-Called Primitive Accumulation.\" Its title is deceptively narrow. On the surface, the chapter narrates how English and Scottish peasants lost their land between roughly the late fourteenth century and the middle of the nineteenth. Beneath that surface, however, the chapter is doing something far more ambitious: it is dismantling the founding myth of political economy. Classical economists treated the emergence of capital and wage-labour as a natural, almost pre-historical fact — a world in which some people happened to possess money and means of production while others happened to possess nothing but their capacity to work, and in which the first group \"saved\" and the second group was \"lazy\" or \"improvident.\" Marx's counter-thesis is stark and uniform across the chapter: the separation of the producer from the means of production — the precondition of the wage relation — was not natural, not gradual, and not consensual. It was the product of systematic, state-supported violence, fraud, and legal theft. The chapter's central problem, therefore, is genetic rather than distributive . It is not asking who owns what now but asking how the ownership pattern that capital presupposes ever came into being. Marx answers that question through a single, disciplined historical narrative. He begins with a portrait of fifteenth-century England as a society of free peasant proprietors and village communities whose wealth lay precisely in a multiplicity of small holders and in broad, shared, common lands. He then traces four great expropriating instruments, which accumulate and reinforce each other across four centuries: The dissolution of the great feudal households , which ejected a mass of \"free\" retainers onto the labour market. The Tudor-era conversion of arable land into sheep-walks , driven by the wool manufacture of Flanders and enforced by the new landed nobility that began to value money above people. The Reformation and the spoliation of church lands , which destroyed the religious bulwark of the older forms of landed property and threw thousands of sub-tenants and ecclesiastical dependents into destitution. The theft of the state domains and the communal lands , first by open force and then — after the \"Glorious Revolution\" of 1688 — by Act of Parliament, until the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries had perfected the \"clearing of estates\" in which human beings were literally swept off the soil. The intellectual objective is to demonstrate two things simultaneously: first, that the historical process that created capital is the same process that created the modern proletariat; and second, that this process was carried through by an identifiable coalition of classes — the new landed aristocracy, the new bankocracy, high finance, and the manufacturers dependent on protective duties — using the law as the \"instrument\" rather than the obstacle of expropriation. By the time the chapter ends, the reader is presented with a single, cold balance sheet: \"They conquered the field for capitalist agriculture, incorporated the soil into capital, and created for the urban industries the necessary supplies of free and rightless proletarians.\" Fitted into the broader architecture of the book, Chapter 27 supplies the historical proof for the theoretical claims advanced earlier. The first volume of Capital had already established, in the analysis of the labour process and of surplus value, that capital requires the separation of the worker from the conditions of labour as its precondition. Chapter 27 shows how that precondition was manufactured. It thus closes the conceptual circle opened at the beginning of the volume: capital is not a thing but a social relation , and the chapter's function is to demonstrate the bloodstained genesis of that relation. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 1. The Baseline: England's Fifteenth-Century World of Free Peasant Proprietors Marx opens with a deliberate and provocative historical claim: \"In England, serfdom had disappeared in practice by the last part of the fourteenth century.\" The phrase \"in practice\" is doing significant work. He does not deny that \"feudal trappings\" survived and might, on a formal reading, have continued to \"disguise\" the underlying reality. But what matters for the analysis is the effective character of property and labour. The immense majority of the population consisted, by the fifteenth century, of free peasant proprietors — smallholders whose legal title might be embedded in the vocabulary of a lord's domain but whose actual control over their plots was absolute. Several supporting structures were dissolved by this baseline fact. In the larger manorial domains, the old bailiff , himself a serf, had been displaced by the free farmer — an early hint that even within the seigneurial economy, a relationship based on money was beginning to replace one based on status. The agricultural wage-labourers, Marx explains, were of two kinds: some were themselves peasants who worked on large estates only in their leisure time, and some were an independent class of specialised wage-labourers. But even the latter were \"in practice\" peasants, because their wages were supplemented by an allotment of arable land \"to the extent of four or more acres\" attached to their cottages. In addition — and this is the lynchpin of the entire chapter — they, \"like the other peasants,\" enjoyed the right to exploit the common land , which \"gave pasture to their cattle, and furnished them with timber, fire-wood, turf, etc.\" The footnote material deepens this portrait rather than merely ornamenting it. Marx quotes Macaulay, \"a systematic falsifier of history\" who \"minimizes facts of this kind as much as possible,\" and yet even Macaulay concedes that not fewer than 160,000 proprietors — who with their families made up more than a seventh of the whole population — derived their subsistence from little freehold estates, with average incomes estimated at £60 to £70 a year, and that the number of persons tilling their own land exceeded \"the number of those who farmed the land of others.\" By the last third of the seventeenth century, four-fifths of the English people were still agriculturalists. Marx's rhetorical strategy is clear: he does not want to be accused of romanticising the medieval past. He chooses as his witness a historian whose ideological biases run in exactly the opposite direction, and then demonstrates that even the hostile witness confirms the substance of the claim. A second footnote — on the serf — sharpens a conceptual distinction that will become crucial later in the chapter. Even the serf , Marx insists, was \"not only the owner of the piece of land attached to his house… but also a co-proprietor of the common land.\" He quotes Mirabeau on Silesia, where serfs possess common lands, and contrasts the Silesian case with the Neumark, where partition of the commons had been implemented \"with very great success.\" The purpose of this footnote is to establish that common land ownership was not exotic or marginal but deeply embedded across European feudalism , surviving even in the most degraded forms of personal dependence. A third footnote annexes Japan as an illustrative case: because its landed property remained \"purely feudal\" and its small-scale agriculture developed, Japan \"gives a much truer picture of the European Middle Ages than all our history books.\" The aside also contains a methodological jab: \"It is far too easy to be 'liberal' at the expense of the Middle Ages.\" 2. The Structural Logic of Feudal Power and Why It Ruled Out Capital The chapter then sets out a general proposition about feudalism — not merely as an English phenomenon but as a European one. \"In all countries of Europe, feudal production is characterized by division of the soil amongst the greatest possible number of sub-feudatories.\" The reason is political and military rather than economic. The power of the feudal lord, \"like that of the sovereign,\" depended not on the length of his rent-roll — not on the magnitude of the money he could extract — but on the number of his subjects , and the number of his subjects in turn depended on the number of peasant proprietors. A lord with a hundred small tenants was stronger, in the medieval sense of the word, than a lord with two large estates. This produces the apparent paradox Marx then explains. After the Norman Conquest, England's soil was carved into \"gigantic baronies,\" one of which might contain some 900 of the old Anglo-Saxon lordships . Yet the country was \"strewn with small peasant properties,\" merely interspersed here and there with great seigniorial domains. The baronial map was a patchwork; behind every patch stood a multiplicity of smallholders. And it was this very multiplicity, together with \"the urban prosperity so characteristic of the fifteenth century,\" that made possible the popular well-being Chancellor Fortescue celebrated in his De laudibus legum Angliae . But — and this is the hinge on which the entire chapter turns — the same conditions that produced popular wealth \"ruled out wealth in the form of capital.\" Peasant prosperity and capitalist accumulation are here presented as structurally incompatible. Capital requires the concentration of means of production in few hands and the severing of producers from those means. A society of 160,000 small proprietors sharing the commons was, precisely because it was comfortable, incapable of generating capital. 3. The Prelude (c. 1470–1530): Retainers, Wool, and the Slogan of Sheep-Walks Against this baseline, the \"revolution that laid the foundation of the capitalist mode of production\" is dated with precision to \"the last third of the fifteenth century and the first few decades of the sixteenth.\" The first instrument was the dissolution of the bands of feudal retainers — the armed households of the nobility who, in Sir James Steuart's vivid phrase, \"everywhere uselessly filled house and castle.\" Marx is careful to distribute causal credit here. The royal power, itself a product of bourgeois development, \"forcibly hastened the dissolution of these bands of retainers in its striving for absolute sovereignty,\" but it was \"by no means the sole cause of it.\" The deeper cause lay with the great feudal lords themselves, who, in \"their defiant opposition to the king and Parliament, created an incomparably larger proletariat by forcibly driving the peasantry from the land, to which the latter had the same feudal title as the lords themselves, and by usurpation of the common lands.\" The juridical point embedded here deserves emphasis: the peasantry possessed \"the same feudal title\" to the land as the lords did. There was no clean legal hierarchy in which the lord was owner and the peasant a mere guest. Both held derivative titles; the difference was one of force, not of law. What the lords accomplished was the conversion of their de facto superiority into de jure private ownership — a usurpation summarised in this single sentence. The immediate economic trigger — \"the direct impulse for these evictions\" — was the rapid expansion of wool manufacture in Flanders and the corresponding rise in the price of wool in England. The new nobility , \"the child of its time, for which money was the power of all powers,\" saw its opportunity and adopted as its slogan the \"Transformation of arable land into sheep-walks.\" The old nobility had been destroyed by the great feudal wars; the new one was a commercial animal. Wool was worth more than wheat, and depopulation was the arithmetic consequence. 4. The Chroniclers' Testimony and Marx's Methodological Caution Marx then summons contemporary witnesses. Harrison, in his Description of England , describes how the \"great incroachers\" razed the dwellings of peasants and the cottages of labourers, \"or doomed to decay.\" The statutes of manors reveal \"seventeene, eighteene, or twentie houses… shrunk,\" and \"England was neuer less furnished with people than at the present.\" Harrison complains of \"cities and townes either utterly decaied or more than a quarter or half diminished, though some one be a little increased here or there; of townes pulled downe for sheepe-walks, and no more but the lordships now standing in them.\" Immediately after quoting Harrison, Marx inserts a methodological caveat that is characteristic of his best historical writing: \"The complaints of these old chroniclers are always exaggerated, but they faithfully reflect the impression made on contemporaries by the revolution in the relations of production.\" He is not asking the reader to accept chronicle hyperbole at face value. He is asking the reader to accept that the experience of dispossession was catastrophic enough to shape the entire rhetorical register of contemporary complaint. To reinforce the point he contrasts Fortescue 's fifteenth-century panegyric on England's laws with Thomas More 's sixteenth-century Utopia — the \"gulf between the fifteenth and the sixteenth centuries\" — and cites Thornton's epigram that \"the English working class was precipitated without any transitional stages from its golden age to its iron age.\" 5. Statutory Resistance and Its Systematic Failure: The Tudor Legislation The chapter then turns to the legal reaction — and to its total failure. Marx first situates that failure ideologically. Legislation's impotence was not merely technical; it reflected the fact that the state had not yet reached \"that high level of civilization where the 'wealth of the nation' (i.e. the formation of capital and the reckless exploitation and impoverishment of the mass of the people) figures as the ultima Thule of all statecraft.\" In the Tudor period, the state was still capable of measuring wealth in people rather than in money — and that is precisely why its legislation was doomed. Bacon's Life of Henry VII supplies the historical narrative. Inclosures \"began to be more frequent\" after 1489; arable land, \"which could not be manured without people and families,\" was turned into pasture, \"which was easily rid by a few herdsmen\"; tenancies for years, lives, and at will — \"whereupon much of the yeomanry lived\" — were \"turned into demesnes.\" The consequence was \"a decay of people, and, by consequence, a decay of towns, churches, tithes, and the like.\" Against this, the \"king's wisdom was admirable, and the parliament's at that time,\" which took course \"to take away depopulating inclosures, and depopulating pasturage.\" The statutory record is then itemised with precision. Henry VII's Act of 1489 (c. 19) forbade the destruction of \"houses of husbandry\" possessing twenty acres. 25 Henry VIII (c. 13) renewed it, reciting that \"many farms and large flocks of cattle, especially of sheep, are concentrated in the hands of a few men, whereby the rent of land has much risen, and tillage has fallen off, churches and houses have been pulled down, and marvellous numbers of people have been deprived of the means wherewith to maintain themselves and their families.\" The Act ordained the rebuilding of decayed farmsteads, fixed a proportion between corn land and pasture, and — an index of the sheer scale of the concentration — limited the number of sheep any single owner might keep to 2,000, even while reciting that some owners already possessed 24,000. The verdict is blunt: \"The cries of the people and the legislation directed, for 150 years after Henry VII, against the expropriation of the small farmers and peasants, were both equally fruitless.\" 6. Bacon's \"Device\" and the Military-Economic Argument for a Peasantry Bacon, \"without knowing it,\" reveals the secret of this failure. In his twenty-ninth Essay, Civil and Moral , he describes Henry VII's policy as \"profound and admirable, in making farms and houses of husbandry of a standard; that is, maintained with such a proportion of land unto them as may breed a subject to live in convenient plenty and no servile condition, and to keep the plough in the hands of the owners and not mere hirelings.\" Marx immediately juxtaposes Bacon's ideal against capital's requirement: \"What the capitalist system demanded was the reverse of this: a degraded and almost servile condition of the mass of the people, their transformation into mercenaries, and the transformation of their means of labour into capital.\" The accompanying footnote elaborates Bacon's reasoning, and it is analytically important because it shows that even a seventeenth-century statesman understood the military — and therefore political — significance of peasant property. Bacon argues that a free, well-to-do peasantry \"did in effect amortise a great part of the lands of the kingdom unto the hold and occupation of the yeomanry or middle people,\" and that \"the principal strength of an army consisteth in the infantry or foot.\" Good infantry \"requireth men bred, not in a servile or indigent fashion, but in some free and plentiful manner.\" Where a state \"run most to noblemen and gentlemen,\" and the husbandmen are mere workfolk, labourers, or cottagers \"which are but hous'd beggars,\" the result is \"a good cavalry, but never good stable bands of foot.\" France and Italy, he observes, are forced to hire Swiss mercenaries. The footnote thus supplies an implicit counter-argument to the enclosure movement from within the strategic logic of the state — and demonstrates that the case against expropriation was not merely humanitarian. It was also, in its own terms, military. Marx does not resolve this tension; he simply notes that the capitalist system demanded the opposite, and that capital won. 7. The Four-Acre Cottage: A Dying Guarantee A short but revealing section traces legislation's last-ditch attempt to preserve the material basis of the agricultural labourer's independence: the four acres attached to the cottage. Parliament \"strove to retain the four acres of land by the cottage of the agricultural wage-labourer, and forbade him to take lodgers into his cottage.\" The cases are almost comic in their specificity. In 1627, under Charles I, Roger Crocker of Fontmill was condemned for having built a cottage on the manor of Fontmill without four acres of land attached \"in perpetuity.\" In 1638, in the same reign, a royal commission was appointed to enforce the old laws, \"especially the law referring to the four acres of land.\" Even Cromwell forbade the building of a house within four miles of London unless it was endowed with four acres. As late as the first half of the eighteenth century, complaints were still being made if the cottage of the agricultural labourer did not possess \"an adjunct of one or two acres.\" The trajectory from four acres to one or two, and from one or two to \"a small garden\" or \"a few roods of land at a great distance from his cottage,\" measures the collapse of peasant-independence in miniature. The section closes with a quotation that is arguably the single most important sentence in the chapter's economic argument. Dr Hunter: \"Landlords and farmers work here hand in hand. A few acres to the cottage would make the labourers too independent. \" Here the logic of capital is laid bare without ornament. The expropriation of the last acre was not incidental to the creation of the wage-labourer. It was the mechanism . A labourer with four acres, a cow, and common rights was not fully dependent on wages. To make him a reliable wage-earner, he had to be stripped of the material means of partial self-sufficiency. Marx's accompanying footnote (George Roberts) notes the shift in perception: the old allocation \"would now be judged too great for labourers, and rather as likely to convert them into small farmers.\" The same piece of land that was once the guarantee of independence had become, in the ideology of the new order, an obstacle to be removed. 8. The Reformation as a Second Great Impulse to Expropriation The process received, in the sixteenth century, \"a new and terrible impulse\" from the Reformation and the \"consequent colossal spoliation of church property.\" The Catholic Church was, at the time of the Reformation, \"the feudal proprietor of a great part of the soil of England.\" The dissolution of the monasteries \"hurled their inmates into the proletariat.\" The estates of the church were \"to a large extent given away to rapacious royal favourites, or sold at a nominal price to speculating farmers and townsmen, who drove out the old-established hereditary sub-tenants in great numbers, and threw their holdings together.\" The legally guaranteed property of the poorer folk in a part of the church's tithes was \"quietly confiscated.\" Marx then frames the strategic significance of this spoliation with a single sentence that ties the section back to his general theory of property: \"The property of the church formed the religious bulwark of the old conditions of landed property. With its fall, these conditions could no longer maintain their existence.\" The point is not merely that church lands changed hands. It is that the church, as a landlord with a different logic than the commercial nobility — one invested in the stability of the older tenurial arrangements — had functioned as a systemic obstacle to the generalisation of private property. Remove it, and the entire architecture of peasant tenure was exposed to the market. 9. The Poor Law and the Criminalization of the Dispossessed The Reformation coincides with the first official recognition of pauperism. Queen Elizabeth, after a journey through England, cries out the Ovidian tag \" Pauper ubique jacet \" — \"the poor man is everywhere in subjection.\" In the forty-third year of her reign, it \"finally proved necessary to recognize pauperism officially by the introduction of the poor-rate.\" Marx quotes Cobbett's observation that the authors of this law \"seem to have been ashamed to state the grounds of it,\" since, contrary to traditional usage, \"it has no preamble whatever.\" The poor-rate was declared perpetual by 16 Charles I, c. 4 , and \"in fact only in 1834 did it take a new and severer form.\" Two substantive footnotes extend this discussion in directions that matter. Footnote 9 reproduces the extraordinary \"nine questions\" submitted by landed proprietors and wealthy farmers to Sergeant Snigge, later a judge under James I, asking whether the Poor Law would permit them to erect a prison in the parish and then farm out the poor to the lowest sealed bidder — a proposal whose logic is the transformation of relief into a punitive labour-contract. It also cites Fletcher of Saltoun's 1698 declaration in the Scottish Parliament that \"the number of beggars in Scotland is reckoned at not less than 200,000,\" and that \"the only remedy that I, a republican on principle, can suggest, is to restore the old state of serfdom, to make slaves of all those who are unable to provide for their own subsistence.\" Meanwhile, Eden — whose State of the Poor Marx will later pillory as \"crafty special pleading\" — writes that \"the decrease of villeinage seems necessarily to have been the era of the origin of the poor. Manufactures and commerce are the two parents of our national poor.\" Marx's response is surgical: Eden is \"only wrong on this point: not the abolition of villeinage, but the abolition of the property of the agricultural labourer in the soil made him a proletarian, and eventually a pauper .\" This is one of the chapter's most important conceptual distinctions. The conventional bourgeois account attributes poverty to the emancipation of the serf — i.e., to freedom. Marx inverts the causality. Freedom from personal dependence was not the cause of pauperism; the loss of property in the soil was. The serf, though personally unfree, had secure access to land. The \"free\" wage-labourer had neither land nor security. In this inversion, the entire ideological narrative of progress is exposed. France, Marx notes in the same footnote, followed a different path: the Ordinance of Moulins (1571) and the Edict of 1656 correspond to the English Poor Laws. Footnote 10 adds a telling detail: Mr Rogers, \"although he was at the time Professor of Political Economy in the University of Oxford, the very centre of Protestant orthodoxy,\" emphasized the pauperization of the mass of the people by the Reformation in his preface to the History of Agriculture . The ideological bind is again drawn tight: even the institutional voice of the orthodox economics of the day could not entirely suppress the fact. 10. The Disappearance of the Yeomanry A brief but pivotal section summarises the social result of these four centuries of pressure. \"Even in the last few decades of the seventeenth century, the yeomanry, the class of independent peasants, were more numerous than the class of farmers.\" They had \"formed the backbone of Cromwell's strength,\" and even Macaulay \"admits\" that they \"stood in favourable contrast to the drunken squires and their servants, the country clergy, who had to marry their masters' cast-off mistresses.\" Macaulay's concession functions here as more than colour. It establishes that the independent peasantry was a morally and militarily significant social stratum, not a marginal curiosity. Then the chronology tightens: \"By about 1750 the yeomanry had disappeared, and so, by the last decade of the eighteenth century, had the last trace of the common land of the agricultural labourer.\" Marx then states his method explicitly: \"We leave on one side here the purely economic driving forces behind the agricultural revolution. We deal only with the violent means employed.\" This is a crucial disclaimer. Marx is not claiming that the agricultural revolution — new crops, new techniques, higher yields — was insignificant. He is bracketing those forces in order to isolate and expose a different variable: coercion. The chapter is a study of the violent means through which a class was severed from its means of production. 11. The Restoration Settlement: Legal Usurpation and the Laws of Settlement After the Restoration of the Stuarts, \"the landed proprietors carried out, by legal means, an act of usurpation which was effected everywhere on the Continent without any legal formality.\" Three operations are distinguished: they abolished the feudal tenure of land (ridding it of all obligations to the state); they \"indemnified\" the state by imposing taxes on the peasantry and the rest of the people; and they established for themselves \"the rights of modern private property in estates to which they had only a feudal title.\" Finally, they \"passed those laws of settlement which had the same effect on the English agricultural labourer, mutatis mutandis , as the edict of the Tartar Boris Godunov had on the Russian peasantry.\" The footnote provides the operative content of that comparison: the Edict of 1597 , by which peasants who had fled from their lords could be pursued for five years and forcibly returned when caught. The laws of settlement played the same functional role in England: they bound the newly-created proletariat to the parish, preventing free movement and thereby ensuring a captive supply of labour. The significance is conceptual as much as historical. The state, in this phase, does not merely tolerate expropriation. It completes it by criminalising mobility. The labourer who has lost his land is not allowed to leave until the new regime has fully absorbed him into the wage relation. 12. The \"Glorious Revolution\" and the Theft of State Lands The \"Glorious Revolution\" brought into power \"along with William of Orange, the landed and capitalist profit-grubbers.\" They \"inaugurated the new era by practising on a colossal scale the thefts of state lands which had hitherto been managed more modestly.\" Estates were \"given away, sold at ridiculous prices, or even annexed to private estates by direct seizure,\" and all this \"without the slightest observance of legal etiquette.\" The Crown lands thus fraudulently appropriated, \"together with the stolen Church estates, in so far as these were not lost again during the republican revolution, form the basis of the present princely domains of the English oligarchy.\" A series of footnotes supplies the evidentiary scaffolding — and shows Marx's reliance on hostile or establishment sources. Footnote 12 quotes from the Sloane Manuscript (No. 4224) on William's private morality, noting that the large Irish grant to Lady Orkney in 1695 was \"a public instance of the king's affection, and the lady's influence,\" and that her \"endearing offices\" were described as foeda labiorum ministeria — \"base services performed with the lips.\" Footnote 13 cites F. W. Newman's judgment that the illegal alienation of the Crown Estates, \"partly by sale and partly by gift, is a scandalous chapter in English history… a gigantic fraud on the nation.\" The Engels addition to the fourth German edition points readers to Our Old Nobility , by \"Noblesse Oblige,\" for details on how the present large landed proprietors came into their possessions. Footnote 14 directs the reader to Burke's 1796 pamphlet against the ducal house of Bedford — the family of Lord John Russell, \"the 'tomtit of liberalism.'\" The argument then ascends to the level of class strategy. \"The bourgeois capitalists favoured the operation, with the intention, among other things, of converting the land into a merely commercial commodity, extending the area of large-scale agricultural production, and increasing the supply of free and rightless proletarians driven from their land.\" The \"new landed aristocracy was the natural ally of the new bankocracy, of newly hatched high finance, and of the large manufacturers, at that time dependent on protective duties.\" The chapter's comparative instinct then provides a decisive contrast: \"The English bourgeoisie acted quite as wisely in its own interest as the Swedish burghers, who did the opposite: hand in hand with the bulwark of their economic strength, the peasantry, they helped the kings in their forcible resumption of crown lands from the oligarchy, in the years after 1604 and later on under Charles X and Charles XI.\" The point is that expropriation is not an iron law of \"development.\" It is a choice — made by identifiable class coalitions under identifiable historical conditions — and Sweden made a different choice. 13. Communal Property and the Parliamentary Coup d'État of Enclosure The chapter now isolates a distinct object: communal property . Marx is emphatic that it is \"entirely distinct from the state property we have just been considering.\" It was \"an old Teutonic institution which lived on under the cover of feudalism.\" Its forcible usurpation, \"generally accompanied by the turning of arable into pasture land,\" begins at the end of the fifteenth century and extends through the sixteenth. But here a decisive qualitative shift occurs: at first the process was \"carried on by means of individual acts of violence against which legislation, for a hundred and fifty years, fought in vain.\" Then, \" the advance made by the eighteenth century shows itself in this, that the law itself now becomes the instrument by which the people's land is stolen , although the big farmers made use of their little independent methods as well.\" The Parliamentary form is that of \"Bills for Inclosure of Commons\" — \"in other words, decrees by which the landowners grant themselves the people's land as private property, decrees of expropriation of the people.\" Here, Sir F. M. Eden is unmasked. He tries to represent communal property as the private property of the great landlords who had taken the place of the feudal lords; but \"he himself demands a 'general Act of Parliament for the enclosure of Commons' (thereby admitting that a parliamentary coup d'état is necessary for their transformation into private property), and moreover calls on the legislature to indemnify the expropriated poor.\" Eden's own argument thus contains its refutation: if communal property were already the private property of the landlords, no coup d'état would be needed. The demand for legislation is the admission of usurpation. 14. \"Capital Farms\" and the \"Setting Free\" of the Proletariat The social consequence is again summarised: the place of the independent yeoman was taken by tenants at will , small farmers on yearly leases, \"a servile rabble dependent on the arbitrary will of the landlords.\" Meanwhile, the \"systematic theft of communal property was of great assistance, alongside the theft of the state domains, in swelling those large farms which were called in the eighteenth century capital farms , or merchant farms , and in 'setting free' the agricultural population as a proletariat for the needs of industry.\" The terminology matters. \"Capital farms\" (from Two Letters on the Flour Trade , 1767) and \"merchant farms\" (from Forster's An Enquiry into the Causes of the Present High Price of Provisions , 1767) show that contemporaries recognised what was happening. They saw that the enclosure movement was not primarily an efficiency reform but the consolidation of small holdings into units organised around capital. And the scare quotes around \"setting free\" carry the whole ironic argument of the chapter. What is called \"freeing\" the population was, in fact, the severing of a population from the means of subsistence. The word \"free\" in \"free wage-labourer\" is a euphemism for \"freed from everything but the necessity to sell one's labour-power.\" 15. The Eighteenth-Century Polemic: Price, Addington, Wright Marx acknowledges that \"the eighteenth century did not yet recognize as fully as the nineteenth the identity between the wealth of the nation and the poverty of the people. Hence the very vigorous polemic, in the economic literature of that time, on the 'enclosure of commons'.\" From \"the mass of material that lies before me,\" he selects extracts \"for the strong light they throw on the circumstances of the time.\" Thomas Wright (1779) : \"In several parishes of Hertfordshire, twenty-four farms, numbering on the average 50 to 150 acres, have been melted up into three farms.\" Rev. Addington (1772) : In Northamptonshire and Leicestershire, the enclosure of common lands took place on a very large scale, and most of the new lordships were turned into pasturage, \"in consequence of which many lordships have not now 50 acres ploughed yearly, in which 1,500 were ploughed formerly.\" The ruins of former dwelling-houses, barns, and stables are the sole traces of the former inhabitants. \"An hundred houses and families have in some open field villages… dwindled to eight or ten.\" The landholders in most parishes that have been enclosed for fifteen or twenty years are \"very few in comparison of the numbers who occupied them in their open-field state.\" \"It is no uncommon thing for four or five wealthy graziers to engross a large enclosed lordship which was before in the hands of twenty or thirty farmers, and as many smaller tenants and proprietors.\" Dr Price : He notes that it was \"not only land that lay waste, but often also land that was still under cultivation\" that was annexed under pretext of enclosure. \"Even the enclosure of waste lands (as now carried on) bears hard on the poor, by depriving them of a part of their subsistence, and only goes towards increasing farms already too large.\" His central prediction is the mechanism of proletarianisation stated in its clearest form: \"When this land gets into the hands of a few great farmers, the consequence must be that the little farmers… will be converted into a body of men who earn their subsistence by working for others, and who will be under a necessity of going to market for all they want… There will, perhaps, be more labour, because there will be more compulsion to it… Towns and manufactures will increase, because more will be driven to them in quest of places and employment.\" And his summary: \"Upon the whole, the circumstances of the lower ranks of men are altered in almost every respect for the worse. From little occupiers of land, they are reduced to the state of day-labourers and hirelings; and, at the same time, their subsistence in that state has become more difficult.\" The Poverty was not slow in coming. Marx notes, \"even according to Eden,\" wages fell below the minimum between 1765 and 1780 and had to be supplemented by official Poor Law relief. Their wages, Eden says, \"were not more than enough for the absolute necessaries of life.\" Footnote 23 supplies the classical parallel: Appian on ancient Rome, where the wealthy bought poor neighbours' land with their acquiescence and seized the rest by force, cultivating wide domains with slaves while the free population declined through poverty, taxation, and military service. The Licinian Law (367 B.C.) — limiting any holder to 500 jugera of public land, 100 cattle, 500 sheep — was the Roman analogue of Henry VII's statutes: an attempt to check concentration that ultimately failed. Marx also notes that Charlemagne's reliance on military service functioned \"as in a forcing-house\" to convert free German peasants into serfs and bondsmen. The implication: the English story is not unique. It is a variant of a recurrent pattern in class societies. 16. The Apologists: Arbuthnot, Seeley, and the Arithmetic of Improvement Against these critics, Marx sets the defenders of enclosure. The principal apologist — [J. Arbuthnot], An Inquiry into the Connection between the Present Price of Provisions, etc. — argues: \"Nor is it a consequence that there must be depopulation, because men are not seen wasting their labour in the open field.\" If converting the little farmers into a body of men who must work for others produces more labour, \"it is an advantage which the nation… should wish for.\" The nation here is, of course, that \"to which the people who have been 'converted' do not belong.\" The produce being greater when their joint labours are employed on one farm, \"there will be a surplus for manufactures, and by this means manufactures, one of the mines of the nation, will increase, in proportion to the quantity of corn produced.\" Footnote 24 pairs this with a similar argument from a different moral register: Seeley's The Perils of the Nation (1843): \"Working-men are driven from their cottages and forced into the towns to seek for employment; but then a larger surplus is obtained, and thus capital is augmented.\" The importance of this pairing is that it exposes the underlying identity of two otherwise opposed positions. The \"apologist\" and the \"philanthropist\" arrive at the same conclusion from opposite moral registers. Both accept that the dispossession of the peasantry is a precondition of national wealth, and both treat the resulting human cost as either irrelevant or providential. Marx's point is not that one is hypocritical and the other honest. It is that the entire economic discourse of the period — including its \"reforming\" and \"philanthropic\" wings — was structured around a conception of the nation that excluded the dispossessed from membership. 17. Eden's Stoicism and the Arable-Pasture Ledger Sir F. M. Eden, \"Tory and 'philanthropic' in his political colouring,\" provides the chapter's most chilling specimen of the economist's mentality. Having surveyed \"the whole series of thefts, outrages and popular misery\" that accompanied the expropriation from the last third of the fifteenth century to the end of the eighteenth, Eden arrives at this \"comfortable\" reflection: \"The due proportion between arable land and pasture had to be established. During the whole of the fourteenth and the greater part of the fifteenth century, there was 1 acre of pasture to 2, 3, and even 4 of arable land. About the middle of the sixteenth century the proportion was changed to 2 acres of pasture to 2, later on, to 2 acres of pasture to 1 of arable, until at last the just proportion of 3 acres of pasture to 1 of arable land was attained.\" \"The stoical peace of mind with which the political economist regards the most shameless violation of the 'sacred rights of property' and the grossest acts of violence against persons, as soon as they are necessary in order to lay the foundations of the capitalist mode of production\" is here put on display in Eden's own words. Human families, villages, entire regional economies are subsumed under a ratio of pasture to arable. The \"just proportion\" of three to one is the mathematical form of depopulation. Marx's irony — the word \"comfortable\" — is the chapter's most concentrated act of critique: it does not deny Eden's data; it merely juxtaposes his data against his tone . 18. The Nineteenth-Century Balance Sheet: 3,511,770 Acres \"By the nineteenth century, the very memory of the connection between the agricultural labourer and communal property had, of course, vanished.\" To make the loss quantitative, Marx asks a single, devastating rhetorical question: \"To say nothing of more recent times — have the agricultural population received a farthing's compensation for the 3,511,770 acres of common land which between 1801 and 1831 were stolen from them and presented to the landlords by the landlords, through the agency of Parliament?\" This figure is the chapter's numerical summation. It converts the four-century narrative into an auditable quantity. The phrase \" presented to the landlords by the landlords, through the agency of Parliament \" is an almost complete theory of the state in one clause: the legislature is described as the instrument by which a class grants itself what it has already seized in fact. 19. The Highland Clearances: Clan Property as the Peculiar Form The final great process is \"the so-called 'clearing of estates' , i.e. the sweeping of human beings off them.\" Marx introduces it by noting that all the English methods \"culminated in 'clearing'.\" As established in an earlier chapter, once no more independent peasants remain to be expelled, the clearing of cottages begins — the agricultural labourer no longer finds on the soil even the necessary space for his own housing. But the full meaning of the practice is only visible in the Scottish Highlands, \"the promised land of modern romantic novels.\" The Highland case is distinguished by three features: (1) its systematic character ; (2) the magnitude of the scale on which it was carried out at one blow — in Ireland landlords had gone as far as sweeping away several villages at once, but in the Highlands \"areas as large as German principalities are dealt with\"; and (3) \"the peculiar form of property under which the embezzled lands were held.\" That peculiar form is the clan . \"The Highland Celts were organized in clans, each of which was the owner of the land on which it was settled.\" The chief or \"great man\" was \"only the titular owner of this property, just as the Queen of England is the titular owner of all the national soil.\" When the English government suppressed the intestine wars of these great men and their incursions into the Lowlands, \"the chiefs of the clans by no means gave up their time-honoured trade as robbers; they merely changed its form.\" On their own authority, they \"transformed their nominal right to the land into a right of private property,\" and when this came up against resistance from their clansmen, \"they resolved to drive them out openly and by force.\" Professor Newman's line — \"A king of England might as well claim to drive his subjects into the sea\" — measures the enormity of the act. The revolution began in Scotland after the last rising of the followers of the Pretender (1745–6) and can be traced in the writings of Sir James Steuart and James Anderson. Steuart, in a footnote, observes that if you compare the rent of Highland lands with the numbers fed upon the farm, \"you will find that an estate in the Highlands maintains, perhaps, ten times as many people as another of the same value in a good and fertile province.\" In the eighteenth century, the Gaels were \"both driven from the land and forbidden to emigrate, with a view to driving them forcibly to Glasgow and other manufacturing towns.\" Footnote 28 records that in 1860 some of the people expropriated by force were \"exported to Canada under false pretences,\" while others fled to the mountains and neighbouring islands, \"were followed by the police, came to blows with them and escaped.\" Two forms of coercion here combine: expulsion from the land, and prohibition of departure. The expropriated population is not permitted to disperse; it is compelled to become a labour reservoir for industrial Glasgow. 20. The Duchess of Sutherland and the Architecture of Eviction The chapter's most famous illustrative passage concerns the Duchess of Sutherland , \"well instructed in economics,\" who \"resolved, when she succeeded to the headship of the clan, to undertake a radical economic cure, and to turn the whole county of Sutherland, the population of which had already been reduced to 15,000 by similar processes, into a sheep-walk.\" Between 1814 and 1820 , these 15,000 inhabitants — about 3,000 families — were \"systematically hunted and rooted out.\" All their villages were destroyed and burnt, all their fields turned into pasturage. British soldiers enforced the mass of evictions and came to blows with the inhabitants. One old woman was burnt to death in the flames of the hut she refused to leave. The arithmetic is then laid out. The Duchess appropriated 794,000 acres of land that had belonged to the clan \"from time immemorial.\" She assigned the expelled inhabitants 6,000 acres on the sea-shore — two acres per family . The 6,000 acres had \"until this time lain waste, and brought in no income to their owners.\" Then the detail that completes the indictment: \"The Duchess, in the nobility of her heart, actually went so far as to let these waste lands at an average rent of 2s. 6d. per acre to the clansmen, who for centuries had shed their blood for her family.\" She divided the stolen land into twenty-nine huge sheep farms , each inhabited by a single family, \"for the most part imported English farm-servants.\" By 1825 , the 15,000 Gaels had been replaced by 131,000 sheep . The remnant, flung onto the sea-shore, \"tried to live by catching fish. They became amphibious, and lived, as an English writer says, half on land and half on water, and withal only half on both.\" Footnote 30 records the exchange with Mrs Beecher Stowe, author of Uncle Tom's Cabin , whom the Duchess entertained with great magnificence in London to show her sympathy for the slaves of the American republic — \"a sympathy she prudently forgot, along with her fellow-aristocrats, during the Civil War, when every 'noble' English heart beat for the slave-owners.\" Marx notes that he published the facts about the Sutherland slaves in the New York Tribune , that the article was reprinted in a Scottish newspaper, and that it \"called forth a nice polemic between that newspaper and the sycophants of the Sutherlands.\" The Sutherland case is thus made to carry a broader comparative argument: the rhetoric of antislavery in one hemisphere coexisted, in the same class, with indifference or active enthusiasm for a form of domestic expropriation that was, in Marx's account, structurally parallel. 21. From Sheep to Sea-Shore: The Second and Third Expulsions The narrative then moves through two further waves of dispossession, each driven by a more profitable use of the same land. First, the fish. \"The smell of their fish rose to the noses of the great men. They scented some profit in it, and let the sea-shore to the big London fishmongers. For the second time the Gaels were driven out. \" Footnote 31 notes that details on this fish trade appear in David Urquhart's Portfolio , and that Nassau W. Senior described the Sutherland proceedings as \"one of the most beneficent clearings since the memory of man\" — a phrase whose perverse charity Marx lets stand without comment, as it needs none. Second, the deer. \"Finally, however, part of the sheep-walks were turned into deer preserves .\" Marx's aside on this is characteristically sardonic: \"Everyone knows that there are no true forests in England. The deer in the parks of the great are demure domestic cattle, as fat as London aldermen. Scotland is therefore the last refuge of the 'noble passion'.\" He then quotes Robert Somers (1848): \"In the Highlands new forests are springing up like mushrooms.\" Glenfeshie, Ardverikie, the Black Mount, Loch Archaig, Glengarry, Glenmoriston — a \"continuous line of forests.\" Sheep were introduced into glens that had been the seats of communities of small farmers; the farmers were driven to \"coarser and more sterile tracts of soil.\" Now deer are supplanting sheep; \"and these are once more dispossessing the small tenants, who will necessarily be driven down upon still coarser land and to more grinding penury.\" Somers's conclusion: \" Deer-forests and the people cannot co-exist. One or other of the two must yield. \" The reason is economic, not sentimental: \"a mountain range laid out in forest is, in many cases, more profitable to the proprietor than when let as a sheep-walk.\" And the moral register of Somers's report rises: \"Sufferings have been inflicted in the Highlands scarcely less severe than those occasioned by the policy of the Norman kings. Deer have received extended ranges, while men have been hunted within a narrower and still narrower circle… One after one the liberties of the people have been cloven down… The clearance and dispersion of the people is pursued by the proprietors as a settled principle, as an agricultural necessity, just as trees and brushwood are cleared from the wastes of America or Australia; and the operation goes on in a quiet, business-like way.\" Footnote 32 is a sharp terminological incision: \"The deer-forests of Scotland do not contain a single tree. The sheep are driven from, and then the deer driven to, the naked hills, and this is then called a deer-forest.\" The euphemism \"forest\" is exposed as a linguistic mask for depopulated waste. Footnote 33 expands the frame. It records that \"the English economists of course explained the famine of the Gaels in 1847 by referring to — over-population,\" a doctrine of which Marx is transparently contemptuous: \"At all events, they were 'pressing' on their food supply.\" It then notes the German analogue, Bauernlegen (\"peasant-throwing-off\"), which took hold in Germany especially after the Thirty Years' War and as late as 1790 led to peasant revolts in Electoral Saxony, and was particularly prevalent in eastern Germany. Frederick II secured property rights for the peasants in most Prussian provinces after the conquest of Silesia, forced landowners to rebuild huts and provide cattle and implements — \"He wanted soldiers for his army, and taxpayers for his treasury.\" Mirabeau's description of the North German peasant is then quoted at length: the peasant crushed by direct taxes, forced labour, obligations, indirect taxes, and restrictions on selling his produce, spinning flax at his wheel as \"a resource against misery and not a means towards well-being,\" sleeping from midnight to five or six in winter, ageing faster in the country than in the town. The Fredrician \"reform,\" in other words, was not philanthropy but the fiscal-military logic of a state that needed bodies. The footnote also cites Professor Leone Levi's 1866 lecture before the Society of Arts on the transformation of sheep-walks into deer-forests, depicting \"the further progress in the devastation of the Scottish Highlands.\" Levi notes that \"depopulation and transformation into sheep-walks were the most convenient means for getting an income without expenditure.\" One could walk from the Earl of Dalhousie's estates in Forfarshire to John o' Groats \"without ever leaving forest land.\" Immense tracts described in the statistical account as having pasturage \"of very superior description\" were \"shut out from all cultivation and improvement, and… solely devoted to the sport of a few persons for a very brief period of the year.\" The London Economist of 2 June 1866 is then quoted on a sheep farm in Sutherlandshire yielding £1,200 a year in rent, which was to be converted into a deer-forest on expiry of the lease — \"Here we see the modern instincts of feudalism… operating pretty much as they did when the Norman Conqueror… destroyed thirty-six villages to create the New Forest.\" Two million acres \"totally laid waste\"; the natural grass of Glen Tilt among the most nutritive in Perthshire; Ben Aulder the best grazing ground in Badenoch; a part of the Black Mount forest \"the best pasture for black-faced sheep in Scotland.\" The ground of Ben Aulder alone would pasture 15,000 sheep, and it was \"not more than one-thirtieth part of the whole forest ground in Scotland.\" The land \"might just as well have been submerged under the waters of the North Sea.\" The passage closes with the Economist 's appeal: \"Such extemporized wildernesses or deserts ought to be put down by the decided interference of the Legislature.\" 22. The Recapitulation: \"Idyllic Methods of Primitive Accumulation\" The chapter's final paragraph is a controlled, almost liturgical summation. \"The spoliation of the Church's property, the fraudulent alienation of the state domains, the theft of the common lands, the usurpation of feudal and clan property and its transformation into modern private property under circumstances of ruthless terrorism, all these things were just so many idyllic methods of primitive accumulation .\" The word \"idyllic\" is the master-irony of the chapter: it is the term bourgeois political economy uses to characterise the pre-history of capital, and Marx appropriates it to describe what he has just documented — burnt villages, evicted families, mass dispossession, state-sponsored theft. The paragraph then states the outcome in the language of capital itself: \"They conquered the field for capitalist agriculture, incorporated the soil into capital , and created for the urban industries the necessary supplies of free and rightless proletarians .\" Three achievements, each indispensable to the capitalist mode of production: the land is converted into an input of capital; the producer is separated from it; and a labour supply is manufactured to work it. 23. Some Critical Nuances and Counter-Arguments Several layers of nuance deserve explicit notice, because they shape how the chapter should be read. First , Marx's own concession that the chroniclers' complaints are \"always exaggerated\" means that the chapter is not to be read as a literal statistical record of every eviction. The exaggeration is admitted; what is claimed is that the complaints \"faithfully reflect the impression made on contemporaries by the revolution in the relations of production.\" The evidentiary standard is therefore impressionistic where it must be, and quantitative where it can be (the 3,511,770 acres; the 160,000 proprietors; the 15,000 Gaels and 131,000 sheep; the parkland acreages). Marx is careful to mix the two registers. Second , Marx's own methodological bracketing — \"We leave on one side here the purely economic driving forces behind the agricultural revolution. We deal only with the violent means employed\" — is a deliberate self-limitation. It acknowledges that there were economic forces at work, and that the enclosure movement also responded to changes in prices, techniques, and markets. The chapter's argument is not that violence replaced economics but that violence was the form through which economic transformation was accomplished. This is a subtler claim than simple expropriation theory, and it protects Marx from the accusation that his account is monocausal. Third , the comparative material, especially the Swedish case, functions as a standing refutation of any claim that expropriation was historically inevitable. The English bourgeoisie and the Swedish burghers faced comparable circumstances and made opposite choices: the English allied with a landed aristocracy that was expelling peasants; the Swedes allied with their peasantry and helped the crown reclaim lands from the oligarchy. Marx's point is not that the Swedish route was morally superior in the abstract but that the English route was chosen , not fated. Fourth , the treatment of Eden and the apologists is itself a form of argument. Marx does not rebut their economics as such. He exposes their tone . He shows that the same processes labelled \"depopulating inclosures\" by Tudor legislation were labelled \"improvement\" by eighteenth-century agronomy, and \"clearing of estates\" by nineteenth-century estate management. The euphemism is part of the mechanism. To call a burn-out \"clearing,\" or a depopulated waste a \"deer-forest,\" or a compulsory dispossession a \"necessary agricultural adjustment,\" is not merely rhetorical — it participates in the legitimation of the violence. Fifth , the chapter acknowledges that the phenomena are European, not merely English. It notes counterparts in France (the Ordinance of Moulins, the Edict of 1656, the Continental pattern of legal usurpation without legal formality), in Germany ( Bauernlegen , Frederick II's counter-measures, Silesia), in Russia (Boris Godunov's edict and the Edict of 1597), in Scotland (the clan-based clearances), in Ireland (village-scale clearances), in ancient Rome (Appian, the Licinian Law), and in Japan (the persistence of feudal landed property). This comparative reach is important, because it positions English expropriation not as a peculiarity but as the classic case of a general process. England was the model because it completed the process most thoroughly — and because its completion provided the template that other capitalist economies would later approximate. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental principle this chapter establishes is that the capital relation has a historical and violent genesis, and that this genesis is inseparable from the formation of the modern state. Capital cannot be understood as a self-generating fact of nature. It presupposes the separation of the producer from the means of production , and that separation had to be produced. The chapter's narrative — stretching across some four centuries — shows the successive instruments through which this separation was manufactured: the dissolution of feudal households, the sheep-walk conversion, the spoliation of church property, the theft of crown lands, the parliamentary enclosure of the commons, and finally the clearing of estates. Each of these instruments built on the previous one, and each was carried through by a specific class coalition — the new landed aristocracy, the bankocracy, high finance, the protectionist manufacturers. Several systemic laws follow. First , property at the origin of capitalism is not the product of peaceful accumulation but of violence, fraud, and legal fiat. The \"sacred rights of property\" that liberal discourse presents as the foundation of order were themselves created by the systematic violation of precisely those rights — a point made with savage clarity by Marx's use of Eden's \"just proportion of 3 acres of pasture to 1 of arable.\" Second , the state is revealed as an active participant in expropriation, not an impartial arbiter. The shift from \"individual acts of violence\" to \"Bills for Inclosure of Commons\" is not a moral progress; it is the enlargement of the state's role in making private property out of communal usage . The 3,511,770 acres \"presented to the landlords by the landlords, through the agency of Parliament\" is the chapter's canonical illustration. Third , the same process that produced capital also produced the modern proletariat. The two cannot be separated; they are the two sides of a single operation. To \"free\" land into capital is to \"free\" people into wage-labour. Marx's phrase \"free and rightless proletarians\" captures this exactly: they are free in the double sense of being released from personal dependence and being released from any property in the means of subsistence. Fourth , expropriation is comparative and contested, not inevitable. Sweden chose a different path; Frederick II, however ruthless his motives, restored peasant property to secure recruits and taxpayers. The English outcome was a victory in a class struggle, not a stage in an unfolding teleology. Fifth , ideology is part of the apparatus of expropriation. The vocabulary shifts — \"depopulating enclosures\" → \"improvement\" → \"clearing\" → \"agricultural necessity\" — and each shift is a small act of legitimation. The economist's \"stoical peace of mind\" is not incidental to the process; it is the intellectual form in which the process becomes acceptable. The broader implications run in several directions. For economic theory, the chapter dissolves the myth of \"primitive accumulation\" as a peaceful prior accumulation of savings and virtue. Marx's demonstration that the \"original accumulation\" was nothing but an extended campaign of dispossession undermines the foundational apologies of liberal political economy — and, by extension, any theory that treats current property distributions as legitimate because they are current. For historical method, the chapter illustrates Marx's capacity to argue through the testimony of his opponents: Macaulay, Bacon, Eden, the Economist , Nassau Senior, Sergeant Snigge. He does not need sympathetic witnesses; he assembles a hostile archive and lets it self-incriminate. For political theory, the chapter offers a theory of the state as an instrument of class consolidation: laws of settlement, poor laws, enclosure acts, and military enforcement are all shown to operate as the legal chassis of accumulation. And for moral and philosophical reflection, the chapter raises a question that retains its force in any time or place: what is the status of the person who is \"free\" in the double sense — free from the land, free from the means of labour, and therefore free to sell the only commodity he still possesses? The Marx of Chapter 27 answers darkly. What liberal doctrine calls freedom, the chapter suggests, is often the historical name for a completed dispossession. What remains is the image with which the chapter effectively closes: the clan remnant \"flung onto the sea-shore,\" becoming \"amphibious,\" living \"half on land and half on water, and withal only half on both.\" It is the image of a population that has lost the ground under its feet and has not yet been given the factory floor to replace it — the transitional body that is neither peasant nor proletarian, suspended between the world that capital destroyed and the world that capital is building. Chapter 27 makes the reader watch as that body is created — and in watching, see that capital's own prehistory is neither idyllic nor accidental. It was made. And it was made by force. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 26 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 28"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 28", "title": "Chapter 28", "subtitle": "Bloody Legislation against the Expropriated since the End of the Fif", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 28/Chapter 28  Bloody Legislation against the Expropriated since the End of the Fif_explanation.html", "word_count": 6052, "read_time": "30 min read", "tags": ["Primitive Accumulation & Enclosures", "The Working Day & Factory Acts"], "keywords": ["Bloody Legislation against the Expropriated", "Vagrancy Laws from Henry VII to Elizabeth", "Whipping, Branding, and Slavery for the Dispossessed", "State Enforcement of Minimum Wage Ceilings", "Prohibition of Workers' Combinations"], "text": "Chapter 28 Bloody Legislation Against The Expropriated Since The End Of The Fif - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,020 words ⏱️ ~30 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS This chapter — the twenty-eighth in Capital , Volume I — is the second act of Marx's drama of \"so-called primitive accumulation.\" Having shown in the preceding chapter that the agricultural population was forcibly expropriated from the soil — dispossessed of the commons, ejected by enclosures, and rendered homeless — Marx here confronts a puzzle that any naive theory of economic development cannot explain. The expropriated masses were, formally, \"free.\" They had been liberated from feudal bonds and from feudal retainers. Yet this freedom was catastrophic, because they possessed no means of production, no legal protection, and no means of subsistence. The nascent manufactures of the sixteenth century could not possibly absorb them \"as fast as it was thrown upon the world.\" The result was a mass descent into mendicancy, robbery, and vagabondage — not chiefly from moral defect (\"partly from inclination\") but overwhelmingly from structural compulsion (\"in most cases under the force of circumstances\"). The chapter's core thesis is thus stated at the outset and laboriously documented thereafter: the very men who had been dispossessed by force were then legislated against as though they had chosen their fate . They were treated as \"voluntary criminals,\" flogged, branded, enslaved, exiled to the galleys, and executed — in a hellish sequence of statutes spanning Western Europe from Henry VII to Louis XVI. This \"bloody legislation against the expropriated\" served one overriding function: to terrorize and discipline these ruined producers into accepting the discipline of wage-labour . The chapter then turns, in its second movement, to the companion instrument: the forcing down of wages by Act of Parliament . Here Marx reconstructs the long history of state wage-regulation — the Statute of Labourers (1349) and its offspring — showing that the state dictated a maximum wage but never a minimum, and that only a change in the balance of class forces (the threatening attitude of the proletariat, the proposal of a minimum wage by Whitbread in 1796, the eventual repeal of wage legislation in 1813) could reverse this centuries-long direction. The intellectual objective is polemical and demystifying. Marx aims to shatter the illusion that wage-labour is a natural, voluntary, and self-regulating relationship. His central theoretical pivot — introduced near the middle of the chapter and elaborated nowhere more concisely than here — is the distinction between the ordinary operation of capitalist domination, carried by the \"silent compulsion of economic relations,\" and the historical genesis of that domination, which required direct, bloody, extra-economic state force. Only in retrospect, once the system is fully mature, does dependence on capital appear as a \"self-evident natural law.\" Historically, it was manufactured at the tip of a whip and the edge of a branding iron. This chapter therefore sits as the indispensable bridge in the \"So-Called Primitive Accumulation\" section: between the expropriation from the soil and the genesis of the capitalist farmer and industrial capitalist that follow. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Problem: A Free but Rightless Proletariat That the Economy Would Not Absorb Marx opens by finishing a sentence begun conceptually in the previous chapter. The proletariat \"created by the breaking-up of the bands of feudal retainers and by the forcible expropriation of the people from the soil\" was a double creation: expelled from one mode of life and not yet admitted to another. The chapter insists on a critical timing problem. Nascent manufactures were growing, but not fast enough to swallow the human flood expelled from the countryside. Simultaneously, these \"men, suddenly dragged from their accustomed mode of life, could not immediately adapt themselves to the discipline of their new condition.\" Here the word discipline is planted — it will become the chapter's refrain. The newly landless were \"turned in massive quantities into beggars, robbers and vagabonds, partly from inclination, in most cases under the force of circumstances.\" The footnote here is indispensable to Marx's argument, because it exposes a term that the translation renders as \"rightless.\" The star note explains that Marx uses the word vogelfrei — literally \"as free as a bird\" — meaning \"free but outside the human community and therefore entirely unprotected and without legal rights.\" This is the conceptual heart of the opening: the expropriated worker possessed freedom of a peculiarly lethal kind. He was free of feudal obligation and equally free of any legal personhood or protection. He had nothing to sell but his labour-power, and — crucially — nobody obliged to buy it. This \"free and rightless\" condition is Marx's answer to the classic bourgeois narrative of free labour. The response of the European state was not relief but repression. \"At the end of the fifteenth and during the whole of the sixteenth centuries, a bloody legislation against vagabondage was enforced throughout Western Europe.\" The critical theoretical claim arrives immediately: \"The fathers of the present working class were chastised for their enforced transformation into vagabonds and paupers. Legislation treated them as 'voluntary' criminals, and assumed that it was entirely within their powers to go on working under the old conditions which in fact no longer existed.\" This is the ideological inversion that Marx mercilessly dissects — the legal system transposes a structural catastrophe (dispossession) into a moral crime (idleness), and punishes the victim for the crime of having been made destitute. Henry VIII and the Escalating Terror of the Vagabond Statutes The English chronology begins under Henry VII and then unfolds with a bureaucratic, almost clinical cataloguing of cruelty. Marx's method here is to let the statutes speak for themselves, listing their escalating penalties as evidence rather than commentary. Henry VIII, 1530: aged and incapable beggars receive a beggar's licence — a faint gesture of mercy that only sharpens the harshness of the rest — while \"sturdy vagabonds\" are whipped and imprisoned. They are \"to be tied to the cart-tail and whipped until the blood streams from their bodies,\" then made to swear an oath to return to their birthplace or to where they lived for the last three years and to \"put themselves to labour.\" Marx's interjection — \"What grim irony!\" — is one of the rare moments when the scholar's mask slips into open moral fury. The oath compels the pauper to perform an impossibility: to find wage-work in a locality from which agricultural wage-work had been systematically destroyed. This statute is then repeated and strengthened by 27 Henry VIII, c. 25, with a graduated ladder of savagery. First arrest: whipping. Second arrest: whipping \"and half the ear sliced off.\" Third relapse: \"the offender is to be executed as a hardened criminal and enemy of the common weal.\" The phrase \"enemy of the common weal\" is doing important analytical work: it registers how completely the expropriated are presumed to be enemies of society — an ideological designation that pre-justifies the harshest punishments and aligns the vagabond with the traitor and the felon. Edward VI, 1547: The Statute of Enslavement The reign of Edward VI marks the apex of the terror and contains the most infamous provision in Marx's entire catalogue — a statute that literally redeems the ancient institution of slavery under the guise of punitive labour law. Marx transcribes the essentials with a cold precision that mirrors and mocks the statute's own language. Any person who \"refuses to work\" is condemned as a slave \"to the person who has denounced him as an idler.\" The denunciation alone constitutes the offense; there is no trial of fact, no proof of a refusal. The master feeds the slave \"on bread and water, weak broth and such refuse meat as he thinks fit.\" He has \"the right to force him to do any work, no matter how disgusting, with whip and chains.\" The statute then elaborates a whole penal architecture around this resurrected slavery. Absence for a fortnight produces slavery for life plus branding with the letter S. Running away three times brings execution as a felon. The master enjoys full proprietary rights: he may \"sell him, bequeath him, let him out on hire as a slave, just as he can any other personal chattel or cattle.\" Any slave attempt \"against the masters\" is capital offence. Justices of the peace are obliged, upon information, to hunt the offenders down. A vagabond idling for three days is taken to his birthplace, branded with a red-hot iron with the letter V on the breast, and set to work \"in chains, on the roads or at some other labour.\" If he lies about his birthplace, he becomes the slave for life of that place, its inhabitants, or \"its corporation,\" branded with an S. Finally, \"all persons have the right to take away the children of the vagabonds and keep them as apprentices\" — young men until 24, girls until 20 — and runaway children become their masters' slaves for those terms, subject to irons and whipping. The clause about \"an iron ring round the neck, arms or legs of his slave, by which to know him more easily and to be more certain of him\" is the chapter's most physically concrete image of the reduction of the wage-labourer's ancestor to livestock. Marx then shows how this brutal order decayed into a quieter, half-hidden survival. The statute's closing provision allowed certain poor people to be employed \"by a place or by persons who were willing to give them food and drink and to find them work,\" creating a category of \"slaves of the parish.\" Marx notes with dry understatement that such parish slaves \"were still to be found in England in the mid nineteenth century under the name of 'roundsmen'.\" The terror of 1547 had not vanished; it had merely been domesticated into a rural institution still visible in Marx's own century. A further stab at euphemism appears in the footnote, where Marx cites the anonymous Essay on Trade (1770), a pamphlet that could whitewash the 1547 statute as evidence that \"the English seem to have set, in good earnest, about encouraging manufactures and employing the poor\" — quoting the statute's branding clause as if it were a poor-employment scheme. Marx's inclusion of this citation is a miniature lesson in how the \"encouragement of manufacture\" was marketed while its coercive core was hidden. Elizabeth, James I, and the Narrowing Choice between Service and Death The Elizabethan statutes sharpen the statute's coercive logic into a binary: service or death . The 1572 Act decreed that unlicensed beggars above fourteen be severely flogged and branded on the left ear \"unless someone will take them into service for two years.\" A repetition of the offence, if the offender is over eighteen, is punished by execution \"unless someone will take them into service for two years.\" A third offence brings death \"without mercy as felons.\" Parallel statutes — 18 Elizabeth, c. 13, and another of 1597 — follow. The mechanism could not be starker: the outlawed vagabond is offered the mercy of employment, and employment alone redeems him. This is wage-labour legislated into existence as the sole alternative to annihilation. Under James I the machinery of terror expands again. Any wandering beggar is declared a \"rogue and vagabond.\" Justices of the peace in Petty Sessions may publicly whip and imprison them — six months for the first offence, two years for the second — and \"while in prison they are to be whipped as much and as often as the justices of the peace think fit.\" The vagueness of \"as often as the justices think fit\" hands the entire measure of cruelty to local magistrates — men drawn, in practice, from the propertied classes whose interests lay in compelling the poor into dependent labour. \"Incorrigible and dangerous rogues\" are branded with an R on the left shoulder and set to hard labour; a fresh conviction for begging brings execution \"without mercy.\" These statutes remained legally binding until the early eighteenth century, only repealed by 12 Anne, c. 23 — a juridical terror with a century-long half-life. Thomas More, the Statistics of Hanging, and the Necessary Criminality of the Dispossessed Perhaps the most rhetorically devastating evidence in the chapter comes in a long footnote quoting Thomas More's Utopia . Marx enlists a sixteenth-century Lord Chancellor against the sixteenth-century state. More describes enclosure as the work of one \"insatiable glutton and accursed plague of his native land\" who \"may join field to field and surround many thousand acres with one fence.\" Tenants are evicted — \"some of them, either circumvented by fraud or overwhelmed by violence, are stripped even of their own property, or else, wearied by unjust acts, are driven to sell.\" The human procession is enumerated with grave tenderness: \"men and women, husbands and wives, orphans and widows, parents with little children and a household not rich but numerous, since farm work requires many hands.\" Away they must go from their only familiar homes and \"find no shelter to go to.\" Their household goods, sold in a forced rush, fetch a trifle, which is soon spent \"in wandering from place to place.\" Then More delivers the conclusion from which the chapter's whole argument flows: \"what remains for them but to steal and be hanged — justly, you may say! — or to wander and beg. And yet even in the latter case they are cast into prison as vagrants for going about idle when, though they most eagerly offer their labour, there is no one to hire them.\" This passage performs multiple tasks at once. It corroborates the mass expropriation, it demonstrates that the criminality of the poor was structurally compelled rather than chosen, and it exposes the sham of \"voluntary\" criminality by showing them eagerly offering labour to no purchaser. More's qualification \"justly, you may say!\" is a bitter sarcasm Marx clearly relishes. Marx then adds the statistical ledger of this repression. Citing Holinshed (the Description of England , though the edition is really Harrison's), he notes that \"72,000 great and petty thieves were put to death\" in the reign of Henry VIII alone. In Elizabeth's time, \"rogues were trussed up apace, and there was not one year commonly wherein three or four hundred were not devoured and eaten up by the gallowes\" (Strype). In Somersetshire in a single year, 40 persons were executed, 35 robbers burnt in the hand, 37 whipped, and 183 discharged as \"incorrigible vagabonds.\" Strype further opined that even this enormous total comprised \"not even a fifth of the actual criminals, thanks to the negligence of the justices and the foolish compassion of the people,\" and that other counties were no better off, \"while some were even worse off.\" The phrase \"foolish compassion\" is another ideological jewel: mercy itself was condemned as a failure of discipline. The Continental Replication: France, the Netherlands, Holland Marx refuses to let England stand as an idiosyncratic case. The same legislation is shown sweeping Western Europe. In France, by the middle of the seventeenth century a \"kingdom of vagabonds\" ( royaume des truands ) had been established in Paris. Even under Louis XVI, the Ordinance of 13 July 1777 decreed that \"every man in good health from 16 to 60 years of age, if without means of subsistence and not practising a trade, should be sent to the galleys.\" This is again the criminalization of structural unemployment — to be capable of work and not to be employed is itself the crime. The Netherlands follows with the Statute of Charles V (October 1537), Holland with the first Edict of the States and Towns (10 March 1614), and the United Provinces with the Plakaat of 26 June 1649. The geographic dispersal proves the thesis: this is not a peculiarity of English law but the general European response to the same underlying economic fact — the forced creation of a mass of dispossessed producers whose absorption by capital was incomplete. The Function of the Terror: Discipline for Wage-Labour The chapter now delivers its summarizing judgment. \"Thus were the agricultural folk first forcibly expropriated from the soil, driven from their homes, turned into vagabonds, and then whipped, branded and tortured by grotesquely terroristic laws into accepting the discipline necessary for the system of wage-labour.\" The phrase \"grotesquely terroristic\" captures Marx's horror — and also his analytical point. The violence was grotesque because it was disproportionate to any reasonable social purpose; it was terroristic because its true function was not punishment but subjection. The word \"accepting\" is carefully chosen: the expropriated did not freely exchange their labour-power, nor did they even prefer it to their previous condition — they were \"disciplined\" into it. The Great Theoretical Pivot: Silent Compulsion versus Historical Genesis With the historical narrative established, Marx pauses to state, with extraordinary clarity, the theoretical architecture of the whole section. \"It is not enough,\" he writes, \"that the conditions of labour are concentrated at one pole of society in the shape of capital, while at the other pole are grouped masses of men who have nothing to sell but their labour-power. Nor is it enough that they are compelled to sell themselves voluntarily.\" Two conditions of capitalism must be distinguished and both are inadequate on their own: (1) the concentration of the means of production as capital at one pole, and (2) the existence of a propertyless mass that sells labour-power. But Marx adds a third, cultural condition: a working class that \"by education, tradition and habit looks upon the requirements of that mode of production as self-evident natural laws.\" This is the inner mechanism of mature capitalist rule. \"The organization of the capitalist process of production, once it is fully developed, breaks down all resistance.\" The continuous generation of a \"relative surplus population\" — the reserve army of labour — \"keeps the law of the supply and demand of labour, and therefore wages, within narrow limits which correspond to capital's valorization requirements.\" This \"silent compulsion of economic relations sets the seal on the domination of the capitalist over the worker.\" Here is Marx's most important theoretical distinction in the chapter: once capitalism is mature, direct extra-economic force is used \"only in exceptional cases.\" In the ordinary run of things, the worker can be left to the \"natural laws of production\" — it is sufficient to rely on \"his dependence on capital, which springs from the conditions of production themselves, and is guaranteed in perpetuity by them.\" But this serene \"natural\" order conceals a violent birth. \"It is otherwise during the historical genesis of capitalist production.\" At the historical moment of transition, the rising bourgeoisie — lacking the mature disciplines of factory and labour market — \"needs the power of the state, and uses it to 'regulate' wages, i.e. to force them into the limits suitable for making a profit, to lengthen the working day, and to keep the worker himself at his normal level of dependence.\" And Marx concludes, with lapidary brevity: \"This is an essential aspect of so-called primitive accumulation.\" Because the \"natural laws of production\" were not yet sufficient to compel obedience, the state stepped in as the direct instrument of class domination. The \"silent\" compulsion of the mature economy is therefore revealed as the inheritor of a loud, bloody, and overt compulsion. The Statute of Labourers, 1349: Wage Regulation from the Beginning Marx now turns to the second instrument: direct wage regulation. He first establishes the historical baseline of the fourteenth century, when the class of wage-labourers \"arose in the latter half of the fourteenth century\" — but was only \"a very small part of the population,\" well protected \"by the independent peasant proprietors in the countryside and by the organization of guilds in the towns.\" At that stage, \"masters and artisans were not separated by any great social distance either on the land or in the towns.\" The subordination of labour to capital was \"only formal\" — the mode of production had not yet acquired specifically capitalist character. Technically, \"the variable element in capital preponderated greatly over the constant element\" — that is, capital was mostly laid out in the wage-bill rather than in machinery and fixed capital. Because capital accumulated rapidly and the supply of labour-power grew only slowly, \"the demand for wage-labour therefore grew rapidly with every accumulation of capital, while the supply only followed slowly behind.\" And critically, \"a large part of the national product which was later transformed into a fund for the accumulation of capital still entered at that time into the consumption-fund of the workers.\" In other words: at the dawn of wage-labour, workers enjoyed a relatively favourable bargaining position and could consume a larger share of the product. Precisely because of this, the ruling classes intervened. \"Legislation on wage-labour, which aimed from the first at the exploitation of the worker and, as it progressed, remained equally hostile to him, begins in England with the Statute of Labourers issued by Edward III in 1349.\" The Ordinance of 1350 of King John in France corresponds exactly, \"identical in content.\" Marx notes that the statutes' clauses compelling an extension of the working day have already been treated in Chapter 10, Section 5, and he does not repeat them. The passage of the Statute was \"at the urgent insistence of the House of Commons\" — the propertied assembly of the kingdom, not the Crown acting alone. A Tory writer is quoted in one of the chapter's most revealing sarcasms: \"Formerly the poor demanded such high wages as to threaten industry and wealth. Next, their wages are so low as to threaten industry and wealth equally and perhaps more, but in another way.\" The point of the quotation is to puncture the perennial complaint about wages: first \"too high\" was the crisis; later \"too low\" would be the crisis; the ruling class was, conveniently, threatened by both, so that it could insist on intervention in either direction — but only ever downward. Two epigraphs sharpen the class content: Adam Smith, \"Whenever the legislature attempts to regulate the differences between masters and their workmen, its counsellors are always the masters\"; and Linguet, \"The spirit of the laws is property.\" The Mechanics of Wage Regulation: Asymmetry of Punishment, the Maximum and Never the Minimum Marx then details how the wage statutes actually operated. A tariff of wages was fixed by law \"for town and country, for piece-work and day-work.\" Agricultural labourers were to hire by the year; urban workers \"on the open market.\" It was forbidden, on pain of imprisonment, \"to pay higher wages than those fixed by the statute, but the taking of higher wages was more severely punished than the giving of them.\" Marx adds a parallel example from Elizabeth's Statute of Apprentices (Sections 18 and 19): ten days' imprisonment for the payer, twenty-one for the receiver. This asymmetry is not accidental: the worker, being the weaker party, must be terrorized more thoroughly; the employer, being the stronger, is treated with comparative indulgence. A statute of 1360 increased the penalties and \"authorized the masters to extort labour at the legal rate of wages by using corporal punishment.\" All combinations by masons and carpenters — \"contracts, oaths, etc.\" — were declared null and void. And from the fourteenth century until 1825, \"workers' combinations are treated as heinous crimes.\" Then comes the decisive sentence: \"The spirit of the Statute of Labourers of 1349 and its offshoots shines out clearly in the fact that while the state certainly dictates a maximum of wages, it on no account fixes a minimum.\" This is the chapter's most condensed statement of the class nature of wage regulation. The state is a class instrument used to cap wages, never to floor them. When the state did propose a floor — Whitbread's minimum-wage bill of 1796 — it was defeated. The Sixteenth-Century Deterioration and the Persistence of Coercive Regulation Marx next notes the brutal consequence. In the sixteenth century \"the condition of the workers became much worse.\" The money wage rose, but not in proportion to the depreciation of money and the corresponding rise in commodity prices; \"Real wages therefore fell.\" Yet the laws keeping them down remained in force \"together with the ear-clipping and branding of those 'whom no one was willing to take into service'.\" The savagery of the vagabond statutes and the wage statutes were two prongs of the same policy — the first compelling the expropriated into employment, the second ensuring that employment paid as little as possible. The Elizabethan Statute of Apprentices (5 Elizabeth, c. 3) gave justices of the peace the power to fix certain wages and modify them according to season and commodity prices. James I extended labour regulation to weavers, spinners, and indeed \"all other possible categories of worker.\" 5 George II extended the anti-combination laws to all manufactures. The footnote on James I adds a blazing example of conflict of interest: a clause from 2 James I, c. 6 shows that \"certain clothiers took it upon themselves, in their capacity of justices of the peace, to dictate the official tariff of wages in their own workshops.\" The employers were also the judges of their employees' wages — a fusion of economic and juridical power that reappears in the chapter's later comments about the 1871 Act. The German case, drawn from G. Freytag, extends the argument. After the Thirty Years' War, statutes for keeping down wages multiply. Landed proprietors, troubled by labour shortage in depopulated districts, forbade villagers to let rooms to single men and women; these men and women were to be reported to the authorities and thrown into prison \"if they were unwilling to become servants, even if they were employed at any other work, such as sowing seeds for the peasants at a daily wage, or even buying and selling corn.\" For a whole century the decrees of the German princelings \"contain bitter and repeated complaints about the wicked and impertinent rabble\" unwilling to accept its legally prescribed wage. Individual landowners were forbidden to pay more than the state tariff. Freytag's own observation, which Marx quotes with approval, is a devastating irony: the Silesian farm servants of 1652 had meat twice a week, whereas in the current century there were districts where they had it only three times a year; mid-seventeenth-century wages were higher than in succeeding centuries. So the period of the most explicit and coercive wage regulation was also the period producing the longest-run deterioration of the workers' condition. The Eighteenth Century: Retreat and Reversal on Wages Marx tracks the transformation of wage regulation into a dead letter during the age of manufacture properly so called. The capitalist mode of production had become \"sufficiently strong to render legal regulation of wages as impracticable as it was unnecessary\" — direct regulation was no longer needed because the \"silent compulsion\" of dependence on capital could do the work. But the ruling classes would not disarm: \"they were unwilling to be without the weapons of the old arsenal in case some emergency should arise.\" So even in the eighteenth century, 7 George I, c. 13 forbade a daily wage higher than 2s. 7½d. for London journeymen tailors (except in cases of general mourning); 13 George III, c. 68 handed the regulation of silk-weavers' wages to the justices of the peace; a 1796 dispute required two judgments of the higher courts to decide whether justice-of-the-peace wage orders held for non-agricultural workers; and in 1799 Parliament confirmed that Scottish mining workers' wages should continue to be regulated by a statute of Elizabeth and by Scottish Acts of 1661 and 1671. The turning point is dramatized as \"a hitherto unheard-of occurrence in the House of Commons.\" For over 400 years, laws had set a maximum beyond which wages \"absolutely must not rise.\" Then, in 1796, Whitbread proposed a legal minimum wage for agricultural labourers. This is the reversal that reveals the class content of all previous legislation: only now, under pressure, did anyone propose using the state to protect the worker. Pitt opposed the bill — but conceded that \"the condition of the poor was cruel.\" The ruling class admitted the suffering, and still refused the remedy. Finally, in 1813, the wage-regulation laws were repealed. Marx explains why: they had become \"an absurd anomaly as soon as the capitalist began to regulate his factory by his own private legislation, and was able to make up the wage of the agricultural labourer to the indispensable minimum by means of the poor-rate.\" The \"poor-rate\" — the compulsory parish levy for relief — now served, ironically, as a subsidy that allowed employers to pay below-subsistence wages: the state no longer had to set a maximum because it supplemented the worker's income out of the rates. The transition is from legal wage-setting to the modern apparatus of factory discipline and social subsidy. Marx then gives one of the sharpest pieces of evidence for the continued class character of contract law: the statutes of labourers' provisions on contracts between master and workman, notice, and the like still allowed \"only a civil action against the master who breaks his contract, but permit, on the contrary, a criminal action against the worker who breaks his contract.\" A footnote records that such inequality remained \"in full force\" until the Employers and Workmen Act of 1875. So the differential criminalisation of the worker lasted five centuries. The Combination Laws, 1871, and the Permanent Trade Union of Capital The chapter reaches the modern era. The \"barbarous laws against combinations of workers collapsed in 1825 in the face of the threatening attitude of the proletariat.\" But they \"disappeared only in part\"; certain \"pretty survivals\" lasted until 1859. Then, in 1871, the Trade Union Act (29 June) \"purported to remove the last traces of this class legislation by giving legal recognition to trade unions.\" Marx immediately exposes the trick: on the same date, a companion Act — the Criminal Law Amendment Act, \"an act to amend the criminal law relating to violence, threats and molestation\" — \"in fact re-established the previous situation in a new form.\" Marx names it with precision: \"This Parliamentary conjuring-trick withdrew the means the workers could use in a strike or lock-out from the common law and placed them under exceptional penal legislation, the interpretation of which fell to the manufacturers themselves in their capacity of justices of the peace.\" Again the fusion of economic and juridical power: employers sat as the very magistrates who would interpret the Act against strikers. Marx then broadens the indictment from the Act to the party. Two years earlier, \"the same House of Commons, and the same Mr Gladstone ... had brought in a bill for the removal of all exceptional penal legislation against the working class.\" But it never went beyond the second reading; the matter was \"drawn out\" until the \"great Liberal party,\" in alliance with the Tories, \"found the courage to turn decisively against the very proletariat that had carried it into power.\" Worse, the Liberal party allowed the English judges — \"ever ready to wag their tails for the ruling classes\" — to \"exhume the earlier laws against 'conspiracy' and apply them to combinations of workers.\" Marx draws the conclusion: \"It is evident that only against its will, and under the pressure of the masses, did the English Parliament give up the laws against strikes and trade unions, after it had itself, with shameless egoism, held the position of a permanent trade union of the capitalists against the workers throughout five centuries.\" The phrase \"permanent trade union of the capitalists\" is one of Marx's most piercing characterizations of the capitalist state: the state itself is the disciplined, organized combination of the employing class against the employed. France, Le Chapelier, and the Bourgeois Libertarian Illusion The chapter closes with the French parallel and one of its finest examples of ideological self-contradiction. During the very first storms of the revolution — in 1791 — \"the French bourgeoisie dared to take away from the workers the right of association they had just acquired.\" The decree of 14 June 1791 declared every combination of workers \"an assault on liberty and the declaration of the rights of man,\" punishable by a fine of 500 livres and one year's deprivation of the rights of an active citizen. Marx emphasizes the law's longevity: \"This law ... has outlived revolutions and changes of dynasties. Even the Terror left it untouched. It was only struck out of the Penal Code quite recently.\" Then Marx quotes Le Chapelier, the rapporteur of the committee, at length — not to refute him, but to let his absurdity speak. Le Chapelier grants \"that wages ought to be a little higher than they are ... that they ought to be high enough for him that receives them to be free from that state of absolute dependence which results from the lack of the necessaries of life, and which is almost a state of slavery.\" Granting all this, workers may nonetheless not be permitted \"to inform themselves about their own interests, nor to act in common and thereby lessen their 'absolute dependence', 'which is almost a state of slavery', because by doing this they infringe 'the liberty of their former masters, who are the present entrepreneurs', and because a combination against the despotism of the former masters of the corporations is — guess what! — a restoration of the corporations abolished by the French constitution!\" The crescendo of irony — \"guess what!\" — marks one of the chapter's most effective rhetorical blows. The revolutionary bourgeoisie abolished the old guilds in the name of liberty, then invoked the memory of those very guilds to forbid workers from organizing — while admitting that the workers' condition was \"almost a state of slavery.\" Marx is showing the deep structure of bourgeois liberty: the freedom to combine belongs exclusively to capital. The footnotes drive the point home. Article I of the Le Chapelier law states that \"the abolition of any form of association between citizens of the same estate and profession is one of the foundations of the French constitution,\" so that all such associations are forbidden \"under any pretext and in any form.\" Article IV declares that workers' joint deliberations to refuse work or insist on a particular price \"shall be declared unconstitutional, derogatory to liberty and the declaration of the rights of man, etc.\" — and treats such conduct as a felony, \"just as in the old statutes of labourers.\" The continuities are thus complete: the French revolutionary decree, ostensibly a repudiation of the ancien régime , reproduces the logic of the Statute of Labourers almost line for line. Reaction and revolution alike legislate against the workers' combination. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter reveals several fundaments. First and most important: capitalism was not born liberal . The mature wage relation, governed by the \"silent compulsion of economic relations,\" is a historical achievement built on direct state terror. The \"free\" worker is, in the double sense Marx insists upon, \"vogelfrei\" — free but rightless, unprotected, disposable. Second, the state is disclosed not as a neutral arbiter but as the \"permanent trade union of the capitalists,\" an instrument that set wage maxima but never minima, that criminalized the worker's combination while permitting the master's, that punished breaches of contract asymmetrically, and that, in the 1871 Acts and the Le Chapelier decree, recreated the old coercion in new forms. Third, the criminalization of structural outcomes — vagabondage, unemployment, poverty — as \"voluntary\" moral failure is a persistent ideological technique, running from the 1530 statute declaring the \"sturdy vagabond\" to be a \"voluntary criminal,\" through the Elizabethan branding of unlicensed beggars, to the modern deployment of conspiracy law against strikers. Fourth, and most subtle, is the chapter's theoretical method: the distinction between ordinary and genetic compulsion. Mature capitalism's equality between buyer and seller of labour-power is real in form and mystificatory in content; but its historical origin lies in the naked use of state violence to create a propertyless class with no choice but to sell. When the system's ideologists treat wage-labour as a \"natural\" phenomenon, they erase this genealogy. Marx's achievement in Chapter 28 is to restore it — not as mere historical curiosity, but as the essential mechanism of \"so-called primit ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 27 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 29"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 29", "title": "Chapter 29", "subtitle": "The Genesis of the Capitalist Farmer", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 29/Chapter 29  The Genesis of the Capitalist Farmer_explanation.html", "word_count": 5960, "read_time": "30 min read", "tags": ["Primitive Accumulation & Enclosures", "Absolute Ground-Rent & Landed Property"], "keywords": ["Genesis of the Capitalist Farmer", "Evolution of the Bailiff to Metayer to Tenant Farmer", "Depreciation of Money and Agricultural Enrichment", "Long Leases and Agricultural Windfalls", "Separation of Farm Capital from Landed Property"], "text": "Chapter 29 The Genesis Of The Capitalist Farmer - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,917 words ⏱️ ~30 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 29 is the shortest and in some ways the most quietly strategic chapter in the whole of Part Eight of Capital , Volume One — the part that Marx titles \"So-Called Primitive Accumulation.\" Its thesis can be stated in a single sentence: the capitalist is not a self-made man but the terminal product of a centuries-long process of class differentiation inside the countryside, in which the English tenant farmer rose out of serfdom, share-cropping and petty tenancy to become a genuine capitalist by valorizing capital through wage-labour , and then grew rich at one and the same time by plundering the commons, exploiting falling real wages, and being the passive beneficiary of the sixteenth-century depreciation of money. Marx's central problem is a gap left by the preceding chapters. Chapters 26–28 had shown how the mass of the agricultural population was violently divorced from the land, how it was flogged, branded and imprisoned into wage discipline, and how state power used police methods to drive down wages and accelerate accumulation. But that whole account describes the genesis of the proletarian . It tells us where the expropriated came from, not where the expropriators came from. Chapter 29 answers the other half of the question: \"where did the capitalists originally spring from?\" The intellectual objective is a genealogical one. Marx wants to show that the capitalist class has a history — a messy, contingent, legalistic and thoroughly sordid one — rather than an origin myth. He insists that in the English case the process is so concrete that \"we can so to speak put our finger on it,\" even though it is \"a slow process evolving through many centuries.\" The rhetorical target is the bourgeois legend of thrift, abstinence and industry (later formalised in Marx's famous polemic against the \"abstinence theory\" and the \"self-saving\" Robinson Crusoe story in the very same part of the book). To that legend Marx opposes a documented sequence of tenurial transformations, lease-form opportunism, enclosure, and monetary accident. Structurally, the chapter is a hinge. The previous chapter ended with the agricultural population converted into a \"free and rightless\" mass. This chapter shows the positive pole of the same relation: out of that dispossession arose a class of capitalist farmers — rich men \"in relation to the circumstances of the time\" — who will, in Chapter 30, produce a home market for industrial capital, and before whom, in Chapter 31, the genesis of the industrial capitalist proper will unfold. The chapter therefore supplies the indispensable middle term between dispossession and industrial revolution: without the capitalist farmer there is no captured surplus to feed the towns, no market, and no industrialist. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS I. The Question That Closes the Circle: Where Did the Capitalists Come From? Marx opens with a compressed recapitulation that functions as a map of the preceding three chapters. He lists, in order: \"the forcible creation of a class of free and rightless proletarians\" (Chapters 26–27, the expropriation from the land), \"the bloody discipline that turned them into wage-labourers\" (Chapter 28's legislation against vagabonds, the whipping, branding and enslavement of the idle), and \"the disgraceful proceedings of the state which employed police methods to accelerate the accumulation of capital by increasing the degree of exploitation of labour\" (statutes forcing down wages, the Statute of Labourers). All three items describe the making of the working class . Then comes the pivot: \"the question remains: where did the capitalists originally spring from?\" The answer Marx immediately supplies is asymmetrical and analytically sharp. \"The only class created directly by the expropriation of the agricultural population is that of the great landed proprietors.\" Note every word. Directly — the landlords are an immediate by-product of enclosure, engrossing and the dispossession of the smallholder; they emerge in one move. But the farmers — the tenant capitalists — are not created directly at all. Their genesis is \"a slow process evolving through many centuries,\" a matter of successive tenurial forms rather than a single act of violence. This distinction is the methodological heart of the chapter. Primary, violent, instantaneous expropriation produces landlords and proletarians. Secondary, gradual, legal-formal evolution produces the capitalist employer . Marx then introduces the reason the process had to be gradual and variegated: \"The serfs, as well as the free small-scale proprietors, held land under very different tenures, and were therefore emancipated under very different economic conditions.\" This is a crucial technical point. Pre-capitalist agrarian classes were not homogeneous. A serf owing labour-rent, a copyholder owing a customary fine, a freeholder owing a money rent, a cottager with common rights — each was \"emancipated\" (a bleak irony in Marx's usage) under a different legal-economic footing, and therefore each entered the capitalist era at a different starting line. The differentiation of the peasantry, later to become a foundational theme of agrarian political economy, is here asserted as a precondition: a uniform peasantry could never have produced a capitalist farmer out of itself; a heterogeneous one could. II. The Four-Stage Genealogy of the English Farmer The chapter's empirical core is a sequence of four forms of \"farmer\" in England. Marx presents them as a developmental chain. First form: the bailiff. \"In England, the first form of the farmer is the bailiff, himself a serf.\" The bailiff is the lord's estate manager, and he is unfree . Marx compares him to the villicus of ancient Rome — the Roman estate steward or overseer — but immediately qualifies the comparison: \"only in a more limited sphere of action.\" This qualification matters. In Rome the villicus administered a vast latifundium worked by slaves and could command considerable resources; the English medieval bailiff operated within the narrower compass of a manorial demesne. The analogy is deliberately partial: Marx is signalling that estate-management functions — the mediating role between owner and producer — are historically recurrent, but their scale and their economic content are historically specific. Second form: the provided farmer. \"During the second half of the fourteenth century he is replaced by a farmer, whom the landlord provides with seed, cattle and farm implements.\" The critical feature here is the source of the means of production. This farmer is not yet a capitalist in the formal sense because he does not own his operating capital — the lord advances the stock. Marx adds two observations. First, \"the farmer's condition is not very different from that of the peasant.\" Second, and decisively, \"but he exploits more wage-labour.\" So the differentia is not wealth, status or security — it is the quantity of wage-labour exploited . This is Marx's first application in the chapter of what will become his criterion for capitalist identity: the capitalist is the one who employs wage-labourers, however modest his own circumstances. Third form: the metayer, or share-cropper. \"Soon he becomes a metayer, a share-cropper. He advances one part of the agricultural stock, the landlord the other. The two divide the total product in proportions determined by contract.\" The metayer (from Late Latin medietarius , a \"half-sharer\"; the Italian mezzadro ) is a transitional hybrid: neither pure tenant nor pure labourer, neither full owner of the stock nor full wage-earner. Stock is split; product is split; the ratio is fixed \"by contract,\" not by custom, status or force. This is a genuinely intermediate moment in which surplus appropriation is contractual rather than seigneurial — a step on the road to a rent contract proper. Crucial to Marx's argument, \"this form disappears quickly in England.\" The English path skips past the share-cropping equilibrium that would persist for centuries elsewhere. Fourth form: the farmer properly so called. \"This form disappears quickly in England, and gives place to the form of the farmer properly so called, who valorizes his own capital by employing wage-labourers, and pays a part of the surplus product, in money or in kind, to the landlord as ground rent.\" This single sentence carries the chapter's theoretical payload, so it must be unpacked term by term. Valorizes his own capital translates Marx's Verwertung : capital is not money or stock per se, but value that expands itself by absorbing the unpaid labour of others; to \"valorize\" is precisely to set that expansion in motion. The decisive clause is \" his own capital\": unlike the second-stage farmer, this one supplies the stock himself, and therefore keeps the residual after all obligations are met. By employing wage-labourers gives the structural definition — the capitalist farmer's income is not earned, it is a claim on the product of hired hands. Pays a part of the surplus product ... as ground rent identifies the division of spoils: the total surplus is split between profit (the farmer's residual) and rent (the landlord's fixed claim), and the farmer is the one who first appropriates the surplus and then hands over a portion. Ground rent, in other words, is not a payment for land use in the innocuous sense — it is a deduction from surplus value , and profit is what remains after the deduction. The four-stage scheme thus tracks a single underlying variable: the successive separation of the producing unit from externally supplied means of production, ending in the farmer's self-financing, wage-hiring, rent-paying enterprise. III. From Bailiff to Metayer to Capitalist: What Actually Changes It is worth pausing on the logic of the transition rather than the sequence. Three things change across the four stages. First, the locus of ownership of the means of production shifts from the lord to the farmer. Stage two's farmer is provisioned by the landlord; stage three's metayer is co-provisioned; stage four's farmer finances himself. This is the precondition of the profit residual: only capital owned by the operator can generate profits kept by the operator. Second, the legal form of the surplus claim shifts from the seigneurial/status-based to the contractual. The bailiff renders dues as a function of his unfree status; the metayer and the farmer render them as a function of agreements \"determined by contract.\" Once the surplus relation becomes contractual, it becomes renegotiable — and that renegotiability is what makes the farmer vulnerable in the long run and rapacious in the short run, as the later history of lease-renewal battles will show. Third, the amount of wage-labour exploited increases monotonically. Marx's phrasing for stage two — \"the farmer's condition is not very different from that of the peasant, but he exploits more wage-labour\" — establishes the analytical priority cleanly. Social appearance (\"not very different\") is decoupled from structural position (\"exploits more wage-labour\"). This is a textbook instance of Marx's method: beneath the surface continuity of the agrarian population, a quantitative change in the employment of hired labour progressively converts a qualitative boundary. One further nuance is embedded in this genealogy. Nowhere does Marx claim that the capitalist farmer was invented by a single act of will, law, or technological breakthrough. The farmer is not a deus ex machina; he is the residue of successive tenurial mutations, each of which was itself conditioned by the wider class struggle over land, labour and dues. The genealogy thus performs double duty. It explains the origin of the capitalist employer, and it demolishes the presumption that such an origin requires anything like the virtues the bourgeoisie later claims as its own. IV. The Fifteenth-Century Ceiling: Why Prosperous Labourers Kept the Farmer Poor \"During the fifteenth century the independent peasant, and the farm-labourer working for himself as well as for wages, enriched themselves by their own labour; and as long as this was the case, both the farmer's circumstances and his field of production remained mediocre.\" This is one of the most analytically significant sentences in the chapter, and it is easily passed over. Marx describes a social formation in which the direct producers — both the independent small proprietor and the semi-proletarian labourer who works part of the time for himself and part for wages — retained enough of the product of their own labour to enrich themselves. The form of enrichment is specified: \" by their own labour .\" No expropriation, no windfall, no inflation; just the produce of their hands. And the consequence for the capitalist? \"Both the farmer's circumstances and his field of production remained mediocre.\" The verb is telling. It is not that the farmer was merely restrained; it is that the survival of a self-enriching peasantry caps the possible surplus that a tenant capitalist can appropriate . Where the direct producer consumes a larger share, there is a smaller residual for profit and rent, and hence neither the tenant's capital nor the scale of his enterprise can expand. The implication is a compact, semi-formal theorem about distribution and growth under pre-capitalist conditions: the magnitude of the capitalist's take varies inversely with the direct producer's capacity to retain value. Expanding capitalist agriculture therefore requires the destruction of the independent peasant's prosperity. That is exactly what the next sentence asserts: the agricultural revolution \"enriched him [the farmer] just as quickly as it impoverished the mass of the agricultural folk.\" The symmetry of \"enriched\" and \"impoverished\" — same adverb, \"just as quickly\" — is deliberately unsettling. It presents the enrichment of the capitalist and the immiseration of the producer as two faces of a single movement, not two independent historical facts. The zero-sum reading is reinforced by the later summary: the farmer \"grew rich at the expense both of his labourers and his landlords.\" V. The Agricultural Revolution and the Free Gift of the Commons \"The agricultural revolution which began in the last third of the fifteenth century, and continued during the bulk of the sixteenth (excepting, however, its last few decades).\" Two things deserve comment. First, the dating: Marx locates the agrarian transformation much earlier than the conventional eighteenth-century \"agricultural revolution\" of turnips, enclosures by Act, and selective breeding. For him the decisive rupture is the late-fifteenth-century wave of conversion of arable to pasture and the accompanying assault on the commons — consistent with the wool-and-enclosure story he told in Chapter 27. Second, the caveat \"excepting ... its last few decades.\" Marx does not explain the exception here, but its logical function is important: it warns that the enrichment of the farmer was not a smooth upward curve, but a process with reverses, interventions and uneven tempo. The chapter's rhetoric is vivid, but its historical claim is scrupulous enough to admit the gap. The mechanism the revolution used to enrich the farmer is stated with almost chemical precision: \"The usurpation of the common lands allowed the farmer to augment greatly his stock of cattle, almost without cost, while the cattle themselves yielded a richer supply of manure for the cultivation of the soil.\" Read this as a production function. The commons were, for the smallholder, a subsistence resource — grazing, fuel, forage — held as a use right, not as an input into a profit calculation. For the capitalist farmer, who counted in money and in yields, the same commons were a free factor of production . Three effects cascade, one from another. (1) Cattle stock expands \"almost without cost,\" because the marginal cost of grazing the commons was nil; the enclosure transferred grazing rights from the village to the farmer. (2) More cattle mean \"a richer supply of manure.\" (3) Manure is the pre-industrial fertiliser; more manure means higher yields on the arable, which means a larger surplus, which means more capital, which means more stock, and so on. Marx's phrase \"though without cost \" is doing a great deal of work. The cost was real; it was simply not borne by the farmer. It was borne by the cottager who lost the use of the waste, the commoner who could no longer feed a cow, the smallholder whose subsistence base was amputated. The enclosure of the commons is therefore not simply a legal event with moral consequences: it is, in accounting terms, a cost externalisation that shows up in the farmer's books as a rise in profitability and in the village's books as a rise in destitution. The \"agricultural revolution\" and the \"impoverishment of the agricultural folk\" are two accounts of one transaction. VI. The Decisive Lever: Ninety-Nine-Year Leases and the Price Revolution With \"a further factor, of decisive importance \" (Marx's stress, not mine), the chapter shifts from production to monetary distribution. This is the theoretical climax. The institutional premise: in the sixteenth century \"the contracts for farms ran for a long time, often for ninety-nine years.\" Long leases mean a nominal rent fixed in perpetuity — or, more precisely, fixed for terms far longer than the normal volatility of money. This creates a large class of agents whose liabilities are frozen in nominal money and whose receipts are quoted in current prices. The monetary premise: \"the progressive fall in the value of the precious metals, and therefore of money.\" This is the sixteenth-century \"Price Revolution\" — the inflation triggered by the inflow of American bullion into Europe — named here by its monetary cause rather than its price effect. A fall in the value of silver means that the same nominal sum of money represents fewer goods. From these two premises Marx derives three specific consequences for the farmer's balance sheet: 1. It lowered wages. \"Apart from all the other circumstances discussed above, it lowered wages.\" Because wages were also contracted (or customarily fixed) in money, a general fall in the value of money eroded real wages even if nominal wages held. \"A portion of the latter was now added to the profits of the farm.\" The wage share falls; the profit share rises by exactly the amount shed. This is not a moral failing of the farmer; it is a structural redistribution engineered by the purchasing power of money. 2. It inflated the value of output. \"The continuous rise in the prices of corn, wool, meat, in short of all agricultural products, swelled the money capital of the farmer without any action on his part.\" Note the crucial phrase \" without any action on his part .\" The farmer did nothing differently; he merely sold at the new prices. But because his rents and wages were contractual liabilities fixed at old rates, the price rise fell almost entirely to him as a windfall. This is the purest possible illustration of the difference between earning a profit and receiving one. Marx's target — the claim that capitalist reward reflects productive contribution — is here demolished by the simple arithmetic of price levels. 3. It eroded the ground rent. \"The ground rent he had to pay diminished, since it had been contracted for on the basis of the old money values.\" The landlord's rent, fixed in nominal terms, fell in real terms year by year. Thus the landlord — the nominal owner of the land — lost precisely as the tenant gained. The consequence is the chapter's summary verdict: the farmer \"grew rich at the expense both of his labourers and his landlords.\" This is a double transfer — from workers via real wage erosion and from landowners via real-rent erosion — channelled through the same mechanism of monetary depreciation. The three distributional classes of agrarian society (wage labour, land, tenant capital) are thus sorted into one winner and two losers by a process that has nothing to do with anybody's diligence. The conclusion is stated with a flourish of unmistakeable irony: \"No wonder, therefore, that England, at the end of the sixteenth century, had a class of capitalist farmers who were rich men in relation to the circumstances of the time.\" The \"No wonder\" is forensic. There is nothing miraculous to explain, nothing to admire, nothing to attribute to thrift. The class was formed by enclosures, fixed contracts, and inflation. VII. Corroborating Voices: Harrison's Arithmetic and Stafford's Dialogue Marx does not ask the reader to take the argument on trust. He supplies two contemporary testimonies, one quoted in the main body, one buried in a long footnote. Their placement is itself telling: the first is invoked in the text as \"evidence of the magnitude of the farmer's gains,\" the second is a semi-independent source documenting the same redistribution from the perspective of its victims . Harrison's rents. Footnote 1 quotes Harrison's Description of England (the sixteenth-century chorographical survey, cited from its chapter \"Of the Manner of Building and Furniture of Our Houses\"): \"although peradventure £4 of old rent be improved to £40, £50, or £100, yet will the farmer ... think his gains very small toward the end of his term if he have not six or seven years' rent lying by him.\" This is a remarkable datum. A rent \"improved\" from £4 to £40–£100 is a ten- to twenty-five-fold increase in rental value — the very measure of the enclosure windfall and of the subsequent renegotiation pressure. Harrison's farmer is not portrayed as a modest husbandman but as an accumulator whose expectations have risen to the point where saving six or seven years of rent over a term seems merely \"small gains.\" The footnote silently maps the subjective horizon of a class that has learned to think in capital terms, which is precisely what Marx has been describing in objective terms. Stafford's dialogue. Footnote 2 is longer and far more theoretically charged. Marx cites A Compendious or Briefe Examination of Certayne Ordinary Complaints, of Divers of Our Country Men in These Our Days, By W.S., Gentleman, London, 1581 . He notes that the dialogue form led people for a long time to ascribe it to Shakespeare, and that it was republished under Shakespeare's name as late as 1751; its author, Marx states, is William Stafford. The attribution note is not ornamental. Marx's demystifying method here is itself significant: an anonymous text was mistaken for the greatest name in English literature, and even in 1751 was being republished under the wrong name. Marx's project — restoring the true authorship of primitive accumulation — parallels his footnote's restoration of Stafford. The real producer of wealth, like the real author of the text, is systematically obscured. The dialogue itself contains an explicit class theory of inflation. The knight complains: \"You, my neighbour, the husbandman, you Maister Mercer, and you Goodman Cooper, with other artificers, may save yourself metely well. For as much as all things are dearer than they were, so much do you arise in the pryce of your wares and occupations that ye sell agayne. But we have nothing to sell whereby we might advance ye price there of, to countervaile those things that we must buy agayne.\" The knight articulates the fixed-income landlord's dilemma with perfect clarity: I have nothing to sell whose price can rise. The Doctor then enumerates the winners and losers systematically: Winners (modest): \"all those that live by buying and selling, for as they buy deare, they sell thereafter.\" Merchants pass through the price rise. Winners (large): \"all such as have takings of fearmes in their owne manurance [cultivation] at the old rent, for where they pay after the olde rate they sell after the newe — that is, they paye for theire lande good cheape, and sell all things growing thereof deare.\" This is exactly Marx's analysis, in the words of a sixteenth-century author: fixed old rent, current prices; buy the land cheap, sell its produce dear. Losers: \"all noblemen, gentlemen, and all other that live either by a stinted rent or stypend, or do not manure [cultivate] the ground, or doe occupy no buying and selling.\" Anyone whose income is fixed is ruined. The citation functions as corroboration from inside the period. Marx is not reading the sixteenth century through nineteenth-century spectacles; the class incidence of the price revolution was already transparent to contemporaries, and Stafford's doctor had articulated it as cleanly as any economist. This is one of the places where the chapter is at its strongest evidentially: the mechanism of the farmer's enrichment is not a Marxian hypothesis but a documented, contemporary observation, complete with an appreciation of the difference between those who \"pay after the old rate\" and those on \"a stinted rent or stipend.\" VIII. The Footnote Republic: The Régisseur, the Middleman's Lion's Share, and the French Counter-Path Footnote 3 is the chapter's most expansive and its most generalising passage. Having established the English path, Marx turns to France, where \"the régisseur , or steward, who collected the dues for the feudal lords during the earlier part of the Middle Ages, soon became an homme d'affaires , or man of business, who by means of extortion, cheating and so on swindled his way into the position of capitalist.\" The French origin of the capitalist is thus the dues-collector , not the tenant farmer. The mechanism is different — fraud and extortion rather than lease arbitrage — but the structural location is identical: the middleman between a distant owner and the actual producers becomes the next owner of capital. Marx supports the point with a document: the account rendered by \"M. Jacques de Thoraisse, knight, and lord of a manor near Besançon, to the lord who administers the accounts at Dijon for his highness the Duke and Count of Burgundy, of the rents appurtenant to the above-mentioned manor, from the 25th day of December 1359 to the 28th day of December 1360\" (Monteil, Traité de matériaux manuscrits de divers genres d'histoire , Vol. 1, Paris, 1835, pp. 234–5). The point of quoting the document is not its factual novelty — it is the type it exemplifies: the seigneurial accountant, a \"man of quality\" himself, positioned at the receipt of rents, and therefore positioned to convert access into ownership. From this Marx launches a sweeping generalisation: \"It is already evident here how in all spheres of social life the lion's share falls to the middleman.\" He then gives a series of illustrations spanning the whole social order: Economic: \"financiers, stock-exchange speculators, merchants and shopkeepers skim the cream.\" Legal: \"in questions of litigation the lawyer fleeces his clients.\" Political: \"the representative is more important than the voters, the minister more important than the sovereign.\" Religious: \"in religion God is pushed into the background by the 'mediator', and the latter is again shoved back by the priests, who are the inevitable mediators between the good shepherd and his flock.\" Marx appends the footnote's own gloss: \"In Christian theology, Jesus Christ is the mediator between God and man.\" This is a striking and deliberate escalation. What began as an agrarian history ends as a general theory of mediation as appropriation . The farmer, the régisseur, the financier, the lawyer, the representative, the priest — all occupy the same structural position: between the principal and the value flow, extracting the largest share. Read carefully, the passage also anticipates the later Marxian theme (developed for capital generally in Book III) that the circulation sphere and the mediation function are sites where surplus is intercepted rather than created. And the religious illustration is not acidic decoration. The chain God → mediator → priests → flock is structurally identical to the chain landlord → régisseur/farmer → labourers. The \"good shepherd\" is the source of value; the mediators live off the distance between him and the flock. Marx then details the French agrarian structure, sharply differentiating it from the English: \"the great feudal territories were divided into innumerable small homesteads, but under conditions incomparably more unfavourable for the people.\" The features are enumerated: In the fourteenth century arose the \"farms\" ( fermes or terriers ). \"Their number grew constantly, far beyond 100,000.\" \"They paid rents varying from one-twelfth to one-fifth of the product in money or in kind.\" These farms were \"fiefs, sub-fiefs etc. ( fiefs, arrière-fiefs ) according to the value and extent of the domains, many of which only contained a few acres ( arpents ).\" \"All these terriens (farmers) had rights of jurisdiction to some degree over those who dwelt on the soil; there were four grades.\" \"The oppression suffered by the agricultural folk under all these petty tyrants will be understood.\" Monteil: \"there were once 160,000 courts in France, whereas today 4,000 tribunals (including local courts) are sufficient.\" The French path is thus the antonym of the English. Instead of one rural class of rich capitalist tenants, France produced a swarm — over 100,000 fermes — of tiny jurisdictions and parcelised holders, each with seigneurial judicial rights, extracting one-twelfth to one-fifth of the product. Monteil's numbers (160,000 courts vs. 4,000 tribunals) quantify the administrative overhead of this dispersion. The comparative moral is unmistakable: same epoch, same decaying feudalism, same commercial pressures — radically different outcomes, determined by the different starting configuration of tenure. And the \"incomparably more unfavourable\" conditions for the people are the price of that dispersion. This is one of the earliest instances of a Marxian comparative agrarian political economy , and its lesson — that the form of the countryside determines the form of the bourgeoisie that emerges from it — will structure Part Eight as a whole. IX. Tensions, Caveats, and the Argument Marx Is Refuting Several tensions and refutations are woven into this brief chapter and deserve to be made explicit. The refutation of the abstinence theory. Though Marx does not name an opponent here, the entire argument is pitched against the story, later to be savaged in Chapter 31 and in the Gotha framework, that capital originated in the frugality of the thrifty. Every mechanism Marx identifies in the chapter is the opposite of industry and self-denial: \"almost without cost\" (the commons), \"without any action on his part\" (price windfall), and the double transfer at the expense of labourers and landlords. The farmer's wealth is not saved; it is received . The final \"No wonder\" is a sneer at anyone who would answer the chapter's opening question with \"thrift.\" The refutation of the \"productive contribution of the entrepreneur.\" If the farmer's profits rose while he did nothing different and while wages fell, then his profit cannot be a reward for anything except a favourable legal-temporal position: long leases and depreciating money. The capitalist function is thereby exposed as a right to the residual , not a claim to a contribution . Nuance: the landlord as victim. Marx notes the farmer \"grew rich at the expense both of his labourers and his landlords.\" This is a subtle and sometimes overlooked reversal. The conventional picture of primitive accumulation is that the landlords enclosed the commons for their own benefit. Marx accepts that the enclosure gave the landed class its existence (the \"only class created directly\") — but the early-flow surplus went to the tenant , not the owner, because long leases had transferred to the tenant the entire gain from rising prices. This nuance explains the eventual pressure for higher rents as leases expired, and thus the ongoing conflict within the propertied classes that later drives the enclosure movement to its eighteenth-century climax. Nuance: the capitalist farmer is a hybrid, not a type. The chapter's four stages show that \"capitalist farmer\" is an emergent result, not a fixed caste. Bailiff, provisioned farmer, metayer, and capitalist are stages in one process. This undermines any notion of an original, natural or pure capitalist. Caveat: the \"agricultural revolution\" is dated early. Marx places its origins in \"the last third of the fifteenth century,\" well before conventional datings. The chapter never defends this dating; it simply asserts it as the framework of the analysis. Caveat: the unexplained exception. \"Excepting, however, its last few decades\" is admitted but not explained, which leaves a real gap in the temporal narrative: why did the enrichment of the farmer pause in the late sixteenth century? Marx does not answer here. Caveat: evidence is illustrative, not exhaustive. The chapter is short and its data are quoted witnesses — Harrison, Stafford, Monteil — not a systematic statistical demonstration. Its force is argumentative and exemplary rather than quantitative. Its authority rests on the logic of the mechanisms it identifies plus independent contemporary testimony to those mechanisms. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter yields a small number of large, generalisable principles. First, the two poles of primitive accumulation are produced by different mechanisms and at different tempos. The expropriated class is generated by direct force — enclosure, eviction, bloody legislation. The capitalist class is generated by slow tenurial evolution plus monetary accident. Anyone who asks \"where do the rich come from?\" cannot give the same answer as the one given for \"where do the poor come from?\" But both answers belong to one history. Second, the capitalist form is defined by a single variable: the exploitation of wage-labour. The farmer's condition \"is not very different from that of the peasant, but he exploits more wage-labour.\" This criterion — not wealth, not ambition, not ownership of a particular type of asset — governs the chapter's whole classification. The capitalist is the one who hires. Third, distribution is a structural, not a moral, outcome. The farmer grows rich \"without any action on his part,\" at the expense both of his workers and of his landlords. This is a theory of profit as a positional claim on the value produced by others, mediated through contracts, leases and the value of money. It has direct analytic descendants in the modern economics of inflation as a redistributive tax — but Marx's point is sharper: the mechanism does not merely redistribute capital, it creates the capitalist class. Fourth, the property relations of the countryside determine the form of the bourgeoisie. England's heterogeneous peasant tenures and the disappearance of share-cropping produced a concentrated class of rich tenant capitalists. France's parcellised fiefs and sub-fiefs produced a swarm of petty jurisdictional rentiers. Same pressures, different results — a striking early demonstration that path dependence in property regimes exerts decisive structural effects on subsequent development. Fifth, mediation itself is a site of appropriation. The régisseur , the accountant, the lawyer, the politician, the priest — the footnote's universal \"lion's share to the middleman\" generalises the farmer's structural position into a principle about the whole architecture of social power. Whoever stands between a principal and a value flow tends to keep the largest part. The chapter thus doubles as a miniature scepticism about intermediaries, from seigneurial stewards to party representatives. Sixth, the origin myth of capital is demolished not by moralising but by accounting. Marx does not say \"capitalism is bad.\" He says: here are the balance sheets, here are the witnesses, this is what happened. The rhetorical power of the \"No wonder\" derives from the fact that no wonder is required. Nothing miraculous, nothing meritorious — just enclosure of the commons, ninety-nine-year leases, a fall in the value of silver, and legal forms that allowed a professional middleman to pocket the difference. The broader implications are twofold. For theory, the chapter closes the circuit of Part Eight: dispossession produces proletarians; police produces wage discipline; and the same dispossession, working through lease-forms and inflation, produces the capitalist. Together, these set the conditions for the industrial capitalism of the following chapters. For historical understanding, it demonstrates that the specificity of the English development — arguably the single most consequential fact in modern economic history — was not a matter of national character or entrepreneurial virtue but of the particular configuration of tenure, leases and monetary history in which a class of English farmers found itself positioned at the onset of the sixteenth-century inflation. The chapter's deeper lesson is methodological: behind every successful modern economy stands a particular set of legal fictions and accidental distributions of loss and gain, which time, ideology and the habits of hindsight then convert into an appearance of naturalness, merit and inevitability. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 28 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 30"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 30", "title": "Chapter 30", "subtitle": "Impact of the Agricultural Revolution on Industry. The Creation of a", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 30/Chapter 30  Impact of the Agricultural Revolution on Industry. The Creation of a_explanation.html", "word_count": 6342, "read_time": "32 min read", "tags": ["Primitive Accumulation & Enclosures"], "keywords": ["Impact of Agricultural Revolution on Industry", "Creation of the Home Market for Industrial Capital", "Separation of Rural Domestic Industry from Agriculture", "Transformation of Peasants into Wage-Earning Consumers", "Destruction of Rural Secondary Occupations"], "text": "Chapter 30 Impact Of The Agricultural Revolution On Industry. The Creation Of A - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,311 words ⏱️ ~32 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 30 of Capital , Volume I, closes one of the most consequential loops in Marx's theory of \"so-called primitive accumulation.\" Its central thesis is deceptively compact, and it appears almost as a hinge in the middle of the chapter before being argued out over the following pages: the expropriation and eviction of part of the agricultural population \"not only set free for industrial capital the workers, their means of subsistence and the materials of their labour; it also created the home market .\" The intellectual objective is therefore to demonstrate that the capitalist market is not an antecedent given — a pre-existing space of exchange into which capitalist production later inserts itself — but a product of the same violent separation that produces the proletarian. The home market is manufactured out of the ruin of peasant self-provisioning. The chapter's central problem is the mechanism of that manufacture. Marx needs to explain how a population that once clothed and fed itself can be transformed into a population that must buy its clothing and food, and how this transformation simultaneously converts the peasant's own former products into commodities, raw materials, and objects of capitalist command. His answer proceeds through a series of joined propositions. First, the expropriation of the peasantry and the concentration of the industrial proletariat are two faces of one process — a dialectical unity he renders through Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire's image of cosmic condensation at one point caused by rarefaction at another. Second, the demographic thinning of the cultivators does not diminish output: improved methods, greater co-operation, higher concentration of the means of production, and intensified labour extract more produce from fewer hands. Third, and most decisively, the \"setting free\" of the agricultural population sets free its former means of nourishment as well, which now become material elements of variable capital — the wage-fund the worker must reclaim from \"his new lord, the industrial capitalist\" — while the raw materials of indigenous agriculture become elements of constant capital . The Westphalian flax example is the chapter's analytic centrepiece: the flax itself is physically unchanged, \"not a fibre of it is changed,\" but \"a new social soul has entered into its body.\" The same use-value is now constant capital. This is Marx's fundamental claim that economic categories are social forms, not natural properties of things. From this it follows that spindles, looms, and raw material, once means of independent existence, become \"means for commanding\" their former owners and extracting unpaid labour from them. The chapter then climbs from this micro-analysis to the historical totality. It argues that the destruction of rural domestic industry — spinning and weaving — is the precondition of a home market with the \"extension and stability which the capitalist mode of production requires,\" but that manufacture proper cannot accomplish this destruction completely: it destroys domestic industry in one branch only to resurrect it elsewhere, generating a new class of semi-agricultural, semi-industrial small villagers. The apparent puzzle of English agrarian history — endless complaints of peasant annihilation from the late fifteenth century on, yet the peasantry constantly reappearing — is explained by the alternation of corn-growing and cattle-breeding epochs, with the Cromwellian Republic as a documented exception. Only large-scale industry, in the form of machinery, \"completes the divorce\" and \"conquers the entire home market for industrial capital, for the first time.\" The chapter thus functions as the theoretical bridge in Part Eight between the genesis of the capitalist farmer and the genesis of the industrial capitalist, and it does so by showing that the \"market\" itself has a genealogy of dispossession. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 30.0 The Recapitulatory Opening: The Guildless Proletariat and Adam Anderson's Providence The chapter opens by recalling what \"we have seen\": the \"intermittent but constantly renewed expropriation and expulsion of the agricultural population supplied the urban industries... with a mass of proletarians standing entirely outside the corporate guilds and unfettered by them.\" Two conceptual operations are compressed into this sentence. The first is the identification of expropriation as intermittent but constantly renewed — not a single event but a recurrent historical process, a rhythm rather than a rupture. The second is the specification of the form of the proletarian produced: \"standing entirely outside the corporate guilds and unfettered by them.\" This matters because the guild was the institutional shell within which craft labour retained a measure of regulation, corporate privilege, and control over the conditions of work. A labourer expelled from the land and denied entry into the guild arrives on the labour market as pure labour-power — formally free, legally unshielded, and available for whatever terms capital dictates. The liberation from the guild is therefore simultaneously a liberation for capital. Marx immediately marks the ideological response to this fact by invoking \"old A. Anderson.\" The editor's note identifies him as Adam Anderson (1692–1765), the Scottish historian of commerce and, for forty years, a clerk in a London business house, author of An Historical and Chronological Deduction of the Origin of Commerce (London, 1764). Anderson is cited because he read the entire process as evidence of \"the direct intervention of Providence.\" Marx's parenthetical warning not to confuse him with James Anderson is not mere pedantry: it flags that two different Andersons, writing from different vantage points, have been fused in the literature of political economy, and that the \"providential\" reading of dispossession is a genre, not an individual eccentricity. The irony is deliberate. What Marx has just described as violent expropriation and expulsion, the historian of commerce describes as divine benevolence. The \"fortunate circumstance\" — that the expropriated fell outside the guilds — is fortunate for capital , and the translation of that historical advantage into cosmic design is the first instance in the chapter of the critique of political economy's theological reflexes. 30.1 The Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire Analogy: Concentration and Expulsion as One Movement Before proceeding, Marx insists: \"We must still pause a moment on this element of primitive accumulation.\" What follows is one of the chapter's most theoretically load-bearing sentences: \"The thinning-out of the independent self-supporting peasants corresponded directly with the concentration of the industrial proletariat, in the way that Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire explained the condensation of cosmic matter at one place by its rarefaction at another.\" The reference is to Étienne Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire's Notions de philosophie naturelle (Paris, 1838), cited in footnote 1. The analogy is not decorative. Marx is borrowing from natural science a structural law of conservation and reciprocal entailment: condensation at one pole is not merely accompanied by rarefaction at the other; it is caused by it and inseparable from it. Translated back into political economy, this means that the concentration of capital and of the proletariat in the towns is not an independent phenomenon to which the depopulation of the countryside is added as a secondary cost. The two are a single movement viewed from two ends. Depopulation of the countryside is population of the factory. There is no ledger on which the gain and the loss balance independently; they are the same entry. The choice of a natural-scientific register is significant methodologically. Marx is not moralizing; he is stating an interdependence with the same impersonality as a physical law. This is the dialectical form of the argument that the next paragraph will fill with historical content. 30.2 The Productivity Paradox: Fewer Hands, More Produce Immediately following the analogy, Marx forecloses a natural objection: how can fewer cultivators feed a growing urban population? His answer is a compact catalogue of the sources of agrarian productivity under capitalist property relations. \"In spite of the smaller number of its cultivators, the soil brought forth as much produce as before, or even more,\" because (i) \"the revolution in property relations on the land was accompanied by improved methods of cultivation,\" (ii) \"greater co-operation,\" (iii) \"a higher concentration of the means of production and so on,\" and (iv) \"the agricultural wage-labourers were made to work at a higher level of intensity.\" A fifth factor is added: \"the field of production on which they worked for themselves shrank more and more.\" Each element deserves unpacking. The \"revolution in property relations\" is the enabling condition; the technical and organisational improvements are its consequences, not its causes. \"Greater co-operation\" refers to the consolidation of scattered plots into farmed units where labour can be combined — a productive power of social labour that is, however, appropriated by capital. \"A higher concentration of the means of production\" is the same phenomenon on the side of capital: implements, livestock, buildings, and land gathered into fewer hands, which itself raises efficiency. \"Intensity\" is the concept Marx takes from Sir James Steuart — the editor's asterisk footnote cites An Inquiry into the Principles of Political Economy (Dublin, 1770), Volume 1, Book I, Chapter 16. Intensity means the extraction of more labour in the same time, whether by supervision, longer effective working, or pressure of competition. The final factor is a subtle dialectical point: as the portion of the working time the labourer spends on his own account shrinks toward zero, the surplus extracted by the capitalist expands correspondingly. The \"field of production on which they worked for themselves\" is the material measure of their residual independence, and its shrinkage is the index of their proletarianisation. The consequence, stated in negative form, is the disappearance of the natural check on urban growth. Fewer peasants, better methods, and more intense labour together mean the agricultural surplus grows while those who consume it do not — precisely the condition of possibility of industrial towns. 30.3 \"Setting Free\": Subsistence as Variable Capital, Raw Materials as Constant Capital The next move is the pivotal category transformation. \"With the 'setting free' of a part of the agricultural population, therefore, their former means of nourishment were also set free.\" The scare-quotes around \"setting free\" are Marx's signature irony: what is \"freedom\" for the peasant is availability for capital. Two species of use-value change economic form: Means of subsistence become \"material elements of variable capital.\" The peasant, \"expropriated and cast adrift, had to obtain the value of the means of subsistence from his new lord, the industrial capitalist, in the form of wages.\" Raw materials of industry which depended on indigenous agriculture \"were transformed into an element of constant capital.\" This is a theoretical claim of the first importance, and it is often passed over. Variable capital, in Marx's system, is the portion of capital laid out in the purchase of labour-power, whose value is returned with a surplus because labour-power creates more value than it costs. The material embodiment of that wage, however, is the worker's means of subsistence — bread, clothing, rent. When peasants produce their own subsistence, those means of subsistence are not capital at all; they are use-values directly consumed by their producers. Once the peasants are expelled, the same use-values become commodities the workers must buy, which means the capitalist who pays the wage is indirectly supplying them. The means of subsistence have become a \"material element\" of variable capital. Symmetrically, raw materials — flax, wool, grain destined for processing — that were produced within the peasant household as part of an integrated domestic economy become, once concentrated and sold, \"an element of constant capital,\" i.e., part of the means of production whose value is transferred to the product without creating new value. The physical object does not change. Its social form changes. This is the doctrine that the same physical object is a different economic category depending on the social relation in which it stands — a wholly anti-naturalistic conception of economic categories. 30.4 The Westphalian Flax: A Case Study in the Transubstantiation of Social Form Marx now tests the proposition with a worked example: \"Suppose, for example, that one part of the Westphalian peasantry, who, at the time of Frederick II, all span flax, are forcibly expropriated and driven from the soil; and suppose that the other part... are turned into the day-labourers of large-scale farmers. At the same time, large establishments for flax-spinning and weaving arise, and in these the men who have been 'set free' now work for wages.\" Four conditions are stipulated: (a) one segment of the peasantry is expelled; (b) the remainder becomes agricultural wage-labourers; (c) large spinning and weaving establishments arise; (d) the expelled now work in them for wages. No change in the physical properties of the flax is specified — because none occurs. This is the point. \"The flax looks exactly as it did before. Not a fibre of it is changed, but a new social soul has entered into its body. It now forms a part of the constant capital of the master manufacturer.\" The \"social soul\" phrasing is a deliberate, quasi-theological register, appearing just after Anderson's \"Providence.\" Where Anderson attributed the historical process to divine intention, Marx attributes to the flax a \"social soul\" that is entirely the product of human social relations — a relation, not a spirit. The metaphor does precise work: it distinguishes substance (the fibre) from form (the capital relation). Prior to expropriation, the flax was \"divided among a mass of small producers, who cultivated it themselves and span it with their families in small portions.\" Now it is \"concentrated in the hands of one capitalist, who sets others to spin and weave it for him.\" Marx then traces the redistribution of the proceeds of the extra labour. Formerly the extra labour in spinning \"was realized formerly in extra income to numerous peasant families, or perhaps, in the time of Frederick II, in taxes pour le roi de Prusse .\" The editor's note explains: the French idiom literally means \"for the King of Prussia,\" i.e., for a man who gives nothing in return — and Marx adds, \"Here, of course, the literal sense is also intended\" (Frederick II of Prussia, r. 1740–86, under whom the Westphalian flax-spinners had paid taxes). Now the same extra labour \"is realized in profit for a few capitalists.\" The final clause of the passage generalises the case: \"And spindles, looms and raw material are now transformed from means for the independent existence of the spinners and weavers into means for commanding them and extracting unpaid labour from them.\" This is the sharpest formulation in the chapter of the identity of the means of production under capital. An implement owned by the person who uses it is an instrument of livelihood; the same implement, owned by another and used by a wage-labourer, is an instrument of domination and exploitation. The means of production become capital only in and through the expropriation that separates them from their users. Marx has therefore shown that \"constant capital\" and \"variable capital\" are not merely accounting categories but the theoretical expression of a specific relation of power. 30.5 The Epistemology of the Factory: Why Appearance Conceals Genesis — and Mirabeau's Counter-Testimony Marx draws the epistemological conclusion explicitly: \"You cannot tell from looking at the large factories and the large farms that they have originated from the combination of many small centres of production, and have been built up by the expropriation of many small independent producers.\" This is a statement, in miniature, of the fetishism of capital: the finished form conceals the history of its own constitution. The factory looks self-sufficient and natural; its genealogy — a thousand dispersed looms annexed into one building — is invisible on its face. Against this normalised appearance, Marx introduces \"unprejudiced observers\" who \"did not allow themselves to be deceived\" because they lived close enough to the transition to remember its stages. Chief among them is Mirabeau — the editor's note specifies the younger Mirabeau, Honoré-Gabriel-Victor Riqueti, comte de Mirabeau (1749–91), the \"lion of the revolution,\" not his father, the Physiocrat. In Mirabeau's time the great factories \"were still called manufactures réunies , or workshops thrown into one, as we speak of fields thrown into one.\" The term itself is a linguistic fossil of the process: a \"manufacture\" is literally a \"making by hand,\" and a \" manufacture réunie \" is an artificial concatenation of many such handmade units. Language preserves what the factory disguises — that it is a gathering-together of previously separate centres of production. Mirabeau's long quoted passage (footnote 4 gives op. cit. , Vol. 3, pp. 20–109 passim ) contrasts two forms of enterprise: The fabrique réunie (\"combined workshop\") \"will prodigiously enrich one or two entrepreneurs, but the workers will only be journeymen, paid more or less [according to circumstances], and will not have any share in the success of the undertaking.\" The fabrique séparée (\"isolated workshop\") — \"no one will become rich, but many workers will be comfortable. The number of hard-working and economical workers will grow, because they will see in good conduct, and in activity, a means of substantially improving their situation, and not of obtaining a small increase of wages that can never be of any importance for the future.\" Mirabeau concludes: \"The isolated, individual workshops, for the most part combined with the cultivation of smallholdings, are the only free ones.\" This passage does several things at once. It documents the visibility of the transition, confirming Marx's epistemological claim. It gives historical voice to Marx's own analysis of the wage relation: in the combined workshop the worker has no share in the success of the enterprise, while in the separate workshop the producer still owns the conditions of production and hence can improve his own situation. And it explicitly identifies the linkage between the isolated workshop and the smallholding — the very \"combination of agriculture and domestic industry\" that Marx has just been describing. It is Marx's own dialectical reservation, however, that saves the quotation from being a merely nostalgic idealisation. In his gloss he explains Mirabeau's preference historically: \"The fact that Mirabeau considers the separate workshops to be more economical and more productive than the 'combined' ones, and sees in the latter merely artificial and exotic products of intensive government cultivation, can be explained by the contemporary position of a large part of the Continental manufactures.\" In other words, on the Continent the \"combined workshop\" often was an artificial hot-house product of state patronage rather than a genuine outgrowth of expropriation and market competition. Mirabeau's denunciation of it is therefore partly a denunciation of mercantilist artificiality, not simply a preference for small property as such. Marx's own position is not Mirabeau's; he uses Mirabeau's testimony to establish a historical fact, not to endorse an ideal. 30.6 The Pivot: The Home Market Thesis Stated and Demonstrated With the analytic pieces assembled, Marx states his thesis outright: \"The expropriation and eviction of a part of the agricultural population not only set free for industrial capital the workers, their means of subsistence and the materials of their labour; it also created the home market.\" Then, restated more formally: \"the events that transformed the small peasants into wage-labourers, and their means of subsistence and of labour into material [ sachliche ] elements of capital, created, at the same time, a home market for capital.\" The German sachliche — rendered \"material,\" but more properly \"objective\" or \"thing-like\" — signals that the transformation is of the object-side of capital, the thing-side, the means of production and subsistence as opposed to the personal side (labour-power). The phrase also emphasises that the same object can be a material element of capital or a use-value for the producer depending on the social relation, exactly as in the flax example. The demonstration proceeds by contrast: Before: \"the peasant family produced means of subsistence and raw materials, which they themselves for the most part consumed.\" The family was simultaneously cultivator, spinner, weaver, and consumer. Its consumption did not pass through the market. After: \"These raw materials and means of subsistence have now become commodities; the large-scale farmer sells them, he finds his market in the manufactures.\" The category shift in consumer goods: \"Yarn, linen, coarse woollen stuffs — things whose raw materials had been within the reach of every peasant family, had been spun and woven by the family for its own use — are now transformed into articles of manufacture, the markets for which are found precisely in the country districts.\" The country districts, in other words, are now the market for the products their own former inhabitants used to make for themselves. The structural inversion of demand: \"Previously a mass of small producers, working on their own account, had found their natural counterpart in a large number of scattered customers; but now these customers are concentrated into one great market provided for by industrial capital.\" This last sentence is the theoretical core of the chapter. The \"scattered customers\" who bought from \"small producers, working on their own account\" constituted a diffuse, decentralised market. Now the same human needs are concentrated into \"one great market,\" and that market is provided for by industrial capital — a single, predictable, extensible demand-side counterpart to mass production. The point is that the home market is not simply enlarged in size; it is transformed in form , from a dispersed network of reciprocal small exchanges into an integrated circuit with capital at one pole and wage-labour at the other. Marx then states the conclusion that will be qualified in the next section: \"Thus the destruction of the subsidiary trades of the countryside, the process whereby manufacture is divorced from agriculture, goes hand in hand with the expropriation of the previously self-supporting peasants and their separation from their own means of production. And only the destruction of rural domestic industry can give the home market of a country that extension and stability which the capitalist mode of production requires.\" Two demands are made of the home market: extension (sufficient volume to absorb mass production) and stability (sufficient predictability to permit long-term capital investment). Rural domestic industry, precisely because it is self-provisioning, denies both. Its abolition creates both. 30.7 The Urquhart Footnote: Nominal Capital, Parasitism, and the Accounting of the Wool Footnote 5 attaches David Urquhart's vivid demonstration of the market's inflation. Urquhart: \"Twenty pounds of wool converted unobtrusively into the yearly clothing of a labourer's family by its own industry in the intervals of other work — this makes no show; but bring it to market, send it to the factory, thence to the broker, thence to the dealer, and you will have great commercial operations, and nominal capital engaged to the amount of twenty times its value... The working class is thus amerced to support a wretched factory population, a parasitical shop-keeping class, and a fictitious commercial, monetary, and financial system\" ( op. cit. , p. 120). The editor's note clarifies that \"by 'working class' Urquhart means those people who work on the land\" — the peasants, not the proletarians. The footnote is doing heavy analytic work. First, it exposes the apparent magnitude of the market as partly fictitious: the same twenty pounds of wool passes through multiple hands, generates multiple transactions, and becomes \"nominal capital engaged to the amount of twenty times its value.\" The multiplication of commercial intermediary stages inflates measured economic activity without increasing the physical product. Second, it identifies the classes whose existence depends on this circuit — the \"parasitical shop-keeping class,\" the \"fictitious\" commercial, monetary, and financial system. Third, and most pointedly, it names the transfer : the labourer's family is \"amerced\" (fined, taxed) by the chain of intermediation. Marx's use of Urquhart here is not endorsement of Urquhart's romantic Toryism but confirmation of the structural point: the destruction of self-provisioning converts a nearly costless use-value into a market whose additional \"value\" is largely the overhead of circulation. This is a critical qualification to any naive identification of market extension with wealth. 30.8 The Manufacturing Period and Its Limits: The Resurrection of Domestic Industry Marx now introduces the chapter's principal qualification: \"Still, the manufacturing period, properly so called, does not succeed in carrying out this transformation radically and completely.\" The reasons are structural. \"Manufacture conquers the domain of national production only very partially, and always rests on the handicrafts of the towns and the domestic subsidiary industries of the rural districts, which stand in the background as its basis.\" Manufacture — that is, the pre-machinery stage of co-operation and division of labour within the workshop — cannot simply dispense with rural domestic spinning and weaving, because it needs them \"to some extent for the preparation of raw material.\" Hence a dialectic of partial destruction and partial resurrection: \"If it destroys these in one form, in particular branches at certain points, it resurrects them again elsewhere, because it needs them to some extent for the preparation of raw material. It produces, therefore, a new class of small villagers who cultivate the soil as a subsidiary occupation, but find their chief occupation in industrial labour, the products of which they sell to the manufacturers directly, or through the medium of merchants.\" This \"new class of small villagers\" is the classic figure of the putting-out system: a semi-proletarianised population that holds onto a smallholding for subsistence while its main income comes from industrial work for a merchant or manufacturer. Its existence is a standing refutation of any linear narrative of expropriation. It also explains the apparent durability of rural domestic industry in the face of the destruction repeatedly deplored by contemporaries — the phenomenon Marx now turns to puzzle out. 30.9 The Riddle of the Reappearing Peasantry: Corn, Cattle, and the Cromwellian Exception Marx's formulation of the puzzle is precise. \"From the last third of the fifteenth century we find continual complaints, only interrupted at certain intervals, about the encroachment of capitalist farming in the country districts and the progressive annihilation of the peasantry. On the other hand, we always find that this peasantry turns up again, although in diminished number, and in a progressively worse situation.\" Two causes are assigned. The first is, as we have just seen, manufacture's need to \"resurrect\" domestic industry, which produces a new semi-agricultural class. Marx calls this \"one cause, though not the chief one.\" The chief cause is the alternation of farming regimes . \"England is at certain epochs mainly a corn-growing country, at others mainly a cattle-breeding country. These periods alternate, and the alternation is accompanied by fluctuations in the extent of peasant cultivation.\" Cattle-breeding favours enclosure and consolidation into large grazing units, driving peasants off the land; corn-growing in certain phases revives the demand for labour and for small cultivators, drawing the peasantry partly back. The demographic record therefore oscillates with the agrarian cycle, rather than declining monotonically. Footnote 6 inserts a significant historical exception: \"Cromwell's time forms an exception. As long as the Republic lasted, the mass of the English people of all levels rose from the degradation into which they had sunk under the Tudors.\" The Commonwealth — the Republic of the 1650s — is presented as a period of popular recovery, interrupting the long downward trend. This is a rare positive annotation in a chapter dominated by expropriation, and it is offered without elaboration, as historical testimony that the trajectory was not unilinear and that political form mattered. 30.10 Machinery as Terminus: Large-Scale Industry and the First Conquest of the Whole Home Market Marx's resolution of the riddle is that no pre-industrial form of farming or manufacture could have stabilised the process: \"A consistent foundation for capitalist agriculture could only be provided by large-scale industry, in the form of machinery; it is large-scale industry which radically expropriates the vast majority of the agricultural population and completes the divorce between agriculture and rural domestic industry, tearing up the latter's roots, which are spinning and weaving.\" Two claims are packed into this sentence. First, machinery is the technical precondition of a stable capitalist agriculture, because it can replace the labour that peasant smallholding supplied irregularly. Second, the \"roots\" of rural domestic industry are identified with specific operations — \"spinning and weaving.\" These are the branches in which the machine can most completely and cheaply substitute for the family's subsidiary labour. Tear out spinning and weaving and the household loses the material basis of its self-sufficiency; its members have nothing left to sell but labour-power. Footnote 7 buttresses this with Tuckett's testimony that the large-scale wool industry \"had sprung, with the introduction of machinery, from manufacture proper and from the destruction of rural or domestic manufactures\" ( op. cit. , Vol. 1, pp. 139–44). This is important because it reverses the causal order assumed by much economic history: it is not that the pre-existing large-scale industry absorbed a pre-existing rural industry, but that the destruction of rural industry, accomplished by the machine, is what constitutes the large-scale industry. Marx clenches: large-scale industry \"therefore also conquers the entire home market for industrial capital, for the first time.\" The words \"for the first time\" carry the chapter's full weight. The home market as a unified entity — a national space of industrial-capitalist demand, integrated, extensible, and stable — did not exist before machinery. It is a creation of the nineteenth-century industrial revolution. Earlier manufacture produced a partial market with an unstable, resurrecting rural fringe; machinery produces the market proper. 30.11 The Urquhart–Carey Polemic: Protection, Free Trade, and the Irony of Antagonistic Separation The same footnote 7 continues into a second, polemical register that reveals the political stakes of the whole argument. Urquhart is quoted again: \"The plough, the yoke, were the invention of gods, and the occupation of heroes; are the loom, the spindle, the distaff, of less noble parentage? You sever the distaff and the plough, the spindle and the yoke, and you get factories and poor-houses, credit and panics, two hostile nations, agricultural and commercial\" ( op. cit. , p. 122). This is the romantic-Tory indictment of the division of agriculture from industry: sever the household's two arms and you get not merely poverty but \"two hostile nations\" and recurrent financial panic. Marx then stages a polemic against Henry Charles Carey. Carey accuses England — \"surely not without reason\" — of trying to turn every other country into a purely agricultural nation whose manufacturer is England. He asserts that Turkey has been ruined in this way \"because the owners and occupants of land have never been permitted by England to strengthen themselves by the formation of that natural alliance between the plough and the loom, the hammer and the harrow\" ( The Slave Trade , p. 125). According to Carey, Urquhart himself is \"one of the chief agents in the ruin of Turkey, because he made free-trade propaganda there in the English interest.\" Marx's commentary is priceless: \"The joke here is that Carey (who is, incidentally, an abject servant of the Russians) wants to prevent the process of separation between agriculture and domestic industry by means of that very system of protection which accelerates it.\" This is a compact refutation. Carey's diagnosis is that the plough-loom alliance must be preserved; his prescription is protective tariffs. But protection, by sheltering national manufacturing, accelerates the very separation of agriculture from domestic industry Carey wants to prevent. The remedy reproduces the disease. Marx's parenthetical characterisation of Carey as \"an abject servant of the Russians\" locates him in the geopolitical conflict of the 1850s. The editor's footnote clarifies: the whole passage \"alludes to the controversy of the 1850s between pro-Turks (such as Urquhart) and pro-Russians over responsibility for the outbreak of the Crimean War, and more generally over the possibility of reforming the Ottoman Empire.\" Marx's point is that both parties, Urquhart's Tory-romantic defence of the plough-loom unity and Carey's protectionist development-nationalism, misread the direction of the process: separation of agriculture from domestic industry is not a policy error to be fixed by better policy but a structural requirement of the capitalist mode of production. Protective tariffs do not reverse it; they are, in fact, among its instruments. 30.12 The Cain and Abel Footnote: The Philanthropic Amnesia of Political Economy The chapter closes with footnote 8, in which Marx turns his irony directly on the mainstream apologetic tradition: \"The philanthropic English economists, such as Mill, Rogers, Goldwin Smith, Fawcett, etc., and liberal manufacturers like John Bright & Co., ask English landed proprietors, as God asked Cain about Abel, 'Where are our thousands of freeholders gone?' But where do you come from, then? From the destruction of those freeholders. Why don't you go further, and ask where the independent weavers, spinners and handicraftsmen have gone to?\" The editor's note corrects the reference: \"The context would suggest John Stuart Mill, not James Mill.\" The biblical inversion is exact and devastating. In Genesis, God asks Cain where Abel is; Cain has murdered him. Here the philanthropists and liberal manufacturers — representatives of industrial capital — ask the landed proprietors to account for the vanished freeholders, indicting agrarian dispossession. Marx turns the question back on the questioners. \"Where do you come from, then? From the destruction of those freeholders.\" The industrial capitalists and their economist spokesmen are heirs of the very expropriation they deplore. Their moral standing as accusers is nullified by their own genealogy. The final question extends the indictment: \"Why don't you go further, and ask where the independent weavers, spinners and handicraftsmen have gone to?\" Because the answer would be even more damaging — those independent artisans were destroyed not chiefly by landlords but by the factory, and by the very manufacturers and economists who now mourn the freeholders. The selective philanthropy of the industrial bourgeoisie is revealed as a class strategy: it deplores the dispossession carried out by its agrarian rival while silently enjoying the fruits of the dispossession carried out by itself. With this, the chapter's argument is complete: the home market of industrial capital is not only created by expropriation; the memory of that creation is suppressed by the very class that benefits most from it. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter establishes the constitutive thesis of the whole of Part Eight of Capital in a single sentence and then proves it: expropriation creates the home market. From this thesis flow several principles of lasting theoretical importance. First principle: markets are made, not found. Marx's argument is a permanent rebuke to any political economy that treats the market as a natural, pre-existing space into which production is inserted, and to the Smithian notion that the division of labour is bounded by the extent of the market as if that extent were a given. The extent of the market is itself a social product — the product, in this case, of the forcible separation of producers from their means of production. The \"freeholder,\" the \"scattered customer,\" and the \"isolated workshop\" are not primitive atoms that later combine into a market; they are the raw material of a market that is constituted by their destruction. Methodologically, this is the strongest possible case for treating economic categories as historically specific social forms rather than natural properties. The flax that changes not a fibre yet acquires \"a new social soul\" is the emblem of the whole doctrine. Second principle: expropriation is a single, double-edged movement. Through the Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire analogy and the productivity analysis, Marx shows that the \"thinning-out\" of the countryside and the \"concentration\" of the industrial proletariat are strictly correlative. The same process that deprives the peasant of land provides the capitalist with labour-power, materials, and an internal market. There is no \"outside\" from which to measure the losses — the loss and the gain are structurally identical. Third principle: the technical threshold matters for the completion of the process. Marx does not claim that expropriation completes itself at any point in the pre-industrial period. The manufacturing period, resting on town handicrafts and rural domestic industry, cannot complete it because it needs rural spinning and weaving for the preparation of raw materials; hence the constant \"resurrection\" of domestic industry and the recurrent riddle of the reappearing peasantry. Only machinery — whose substitutive capacity is concentrated precisely in spinning and weaving — tears out the roots. The home market \"for the first time\" is thus the work of large-scale industry, not of manufacture. This establishes an internal, technical condition on a social transformation, and it explicitly historicises the \"market\" as an achievement of a determinate stage of productive technique. Fourth principle: the genealogy of classes includes their self-representation. Through Adam Anderson's Providence, Mirabeau's nostalgia, Urquhart's Tory radicalism, Carey's protectionism, and the freeholder-mourning philanthropists of footnote 8, Marx demonstrates that the intellectual reflexes of a class are systematically shaped by its position in the process. The theological reading of dispossession (Anderson), the melancholic reading (Mirabeau), the indignant reading (Carey), and the selectively moralising reading (Mill, Bright & Co.) are all forms of ideological containment. The Cain-and-Abel inversion is the culminating instance: the question \"Where are our thousands of freeholders gone?\" is turned back as \"Where do you come from, then?\" — a genealogical exposure that dismantles the questioner's presumed innocence. The broader implications extend well beyond the England of the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries. Any development project, any programme of agrarian reform, any industrial policy that assumes markets exist prior to and independently of the social relations that produce and consume within them will misdiagnose its own conditions of possibility. Free trade and protection alike — as Marx's treatment of Urquhart and Carey shows — are not alternative solutions to the separation of agriculture from industry but alternative instruments for accelerating or retarding it. And the modern reader armed with this chapter will recognise in the destruction of self-provisioning peasant agriculture, wherever and whenever it occurs, not merely a humanitarian catastrophe — as Marx's philanthropists would call it — but the constitutive act by which a domestic market for industrial capital is brought into being. The home market is the shadow of the expropriation, and, as Marx insists at every turn, its members would not have asked where the freeholders went if they did not themselves have reason to hide where they came from. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 29 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 31"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 31", "title": "Chapter 31", "subtitle": "The Genesis of the Industrial Capitalist", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 31/Chapter 31  The Genesis of the Industrial Capitalist_explanation.html", "word_count": 6687, "read_time": "33 min read", "tags": ["Primitive Accumulation & Enclosures", "Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital"], "keywords": ["Genesis of the Industrial Capitalist", "Colonial System, National Debt, and Modern Taxation", "Protectionist System and Child Slavery in Factories", "Commercial Wars of European Nations", "Rise of Commercial Monopoly and State Power"], "text": "Chapter 31 The Genesis Of The Industrial Capitalist - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,660 words ⏱️ ~33 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS This is Chapter 31 of Volume I of Capital , titled \"The Genesis of the Industrial Capitalist.\" It is the penultimate chapter of Part Eight, \"So-Called Primitive Accumulation,\" and it functions as the historical and rhetorical climax of Marx's case. The preceding chapters documented the violent expropriation of the English agricultural population and the emergence of the capitalist tenant farmer. Chapter 31 completes the arc by turning to the specifically industrial capitalist and asking the decisive question: by what concrete means did money actually become industrial capital? The thesis is blunt and polemical. The industrial capitalist did not emerge gradually through patience, thrift, or the slow accumulation of honest earnings. Marx concedes that some small guild-masters, a larger number of independent small artisans, and even wage-labourers did convert themselves into capitalists by \"gradually extending their exploitation of wage-labour and the corresponding accumulation.\" But this \"snail's pace of advance by no means corresponded with the commercial requirements of the new world market,\" created by the great geographical discoveries at the end of the fifteenth century. The required speed demanded other means. Those means — colonial plunder, enslavement, man-stealing, massacre, national debt, taxation, protectionism, and commercial war — are what Marx gathers under the rubric of \"primitive accumulation.\" The central problem the chapter addresses is structural: before capitalism, two forms of capital already existed — usurer's capital and merchant's capital — and these \"ripened in the most varied economic formations of society\" and functioned as capital long before the capitalist mode of production. Yet they were blocked from becoming industrial capital by the feudal organization of the countryside and the guild organization of the towns. The chapter's task is to show how those fetters were smashed and how money formed by usury and commerce was converted into industrial capital. The answer runs through the state: colonies, national debt, the fiscal system, and protection were \"systematically combined together at the end of the seventeenth century in England,\" all delivered by \"the power of the state, the concentrated and organized force of society.\" The intellectual objective is destructive as much as explanatory. Marx wages war on the \"idyllic\" bourgeois account of capital's origins — the humane, bloodless story in which capital is simply the reward of industry and abstinence. Against it he mobilizes a prosecutorial dossier: Dutch man-stealing in Celebes, English scalp bounties on the indigenous peoples of New England, the East India Company's fortunes \"sprung up like mushrooms in a day,\" the Bank of England's double-coining trick, the child-slavery of the Lancashire factories, and the Liverpool slave trade. The chapter ends with the notorious verdict that capital \"comes dripping from head to toe, from every pore, with blood and dirt.\" The chapter's place in the broader framework is decisive. It completes the historical narrative begun with \"the secret of primitive accumulation,\" and it sets up the final theoretical chapter on the historical tendency of capitalist accumulation. Methodologically, it demonstrates that force is not external to economics but \"is itself an economic power,\" and that the separation of the worker from the conditions of labour — the precondition of the wage-relation — was not natural or contractual but violently produced. The prefix \"so-called\" in the part's title signals Marx's contempt for the apologetic notion that primitive accumulation was a peaceful, pre-historical event. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS I. The Opening Problem — Why Gradualism Was Insufficient Marx opens by contrasting the industrial capitalist's genesis with that of the farmer, already treated in the preceding chapters. The farmer had risen gradually; the industrial capitalist did not. He grants the reality of the slow path: \"Doubtless many small guild-masters, and a still greater number of independent small artisans, or even wage-labourers, transformed themselves into small capitalists,\" and from there into \"capitalists without qualification.\" This concession matters methodologically: Marx is not denying that gradual accumulation ever happened, but denying its sufficiency to explain the historical tempo of capitalist emergence. He then supplies an analogy drawn from the infancy of the medieval town, where \"the question as to which of the escaped serfs should be master and which servant was in great part decided by the earlier or later date of their flight.\" Here the distinction between master and servant is arbitrary — a matter of accident and timing, not of natural superiority. The analogy quietly deflates any claim that capital's ascendancy reflects merit. The decisive mechanism is demand: the \"snail's pace of advance\" could not satisfy \"the commercial requirements of the new world market, which had been created by the great discoveries of the end of the fifteenth century.\" A footnote clarifies terminology: \"'Industrial' here as opposed to 'agricultural.' In the strict sense the farmer is just as much an industrial capitalist as the manufacturer.\" So \"industrial\" is used loosely to mean manufacturing and non-agricultural enterprise. The opening thus poses the chapter's guiding question: if the slow route was too slow, what route was actually taken? The answer occupies the rest of the chapter. II. The Medieval Bequest — Usurer's and Merchant's Capital Before answering, Marx establishes the historical raw material. \"The Middle Ages had handed down two distinct forms of capital, which ripened in the most varied economic formations of society, and which, before the era of the capitalist mode of production, nevertheless functioned as capital — usurer's capital and merchant's capital.\" This is a crucial analytical claim with two implications. First, capital as a relation — money that breeds money — is historically older than capitalism. Second, \"capital\" is therefore not synonymous with \"the capitalist mode of production\"; the specifically capitalist form is the industrial deployment of capital, capital that organizes wage-labour in production. The whole chapter turns on this transformation: how did usurer's and merchant's capital, already concentrated in money form, become industrial capital? That is the question the colonial, fiscal, and credit mechanisms answer. The concept of \"primitive accumulation\" is thus defined in this chapter by its function: it is the set of extra-economic and state-mediated processes that converted pre-existing money-wealth into the industrial capital relation, and simultaneously converted the mass of the population into wage-labourers. III. Hodgskin's Question and Marx's Retort Marx then quotes at length from an anonymous 1832 pamphlet, The Natural and Artificial Rights of Property Contrasted , which he identifies in a footnote as the work of Thomas Hodgskin. Hodgskin's argument is that all society's wealth now flows first to the capitalist: \"he pays the landowner his rent, the labourer his wages, the tax and tithe gatherer their claims, and keeps a large, indeed the largest, and a continually augmenting share.\" The capitalist \"may now be said to be the first owner of all the wealth of the community, though no law has conferred on him the right to this property.\" Hodgskin notes the irony that European lawgivers tried to prevent this via \"statutes against usury,\" and he asks the legal-historical question: \"The power of the capitalist over all the wealth of the country is a complete change in the right of property, and by what law, or series of laws, was it effected?\" Marx's one-line retort is the pivot of the chapter: \"The author should have reminded himself that revolutions are not made with laws.\" This is a double move. It corrects Hodgskin's legalist framing — the origin of capital is not to be found in legislation but in revolution and force. And it reframes the puzzle: the question is not \"which law\" but \"which violence.\" Hodgskin, a Ricardian socialist, correctly identified the expropriation but misidentified its mechanism. Marx's critique here exemplifies his broader method: political economy's origin stories fail because they look for legal or contractual origins where material force operated. IV. Breaking the Fetters — Geography, Guilds, and New Manufactures Marx now explains why money-capital could not simply become industrial capital. \"The money capital formed by means of usury and commerce was prevented from turning into industrial capital by the feudal organization of the countryside and the guild organization of the towns.\" These are the \"fetters\" — institutional barriers that kept capital in circulation and usury rather than drawing it into production. \"They fetters vanished with the dissolution of the feudal bands of retainers, and the expropriation and partial eviction of the rural population.\" This connects directly to the enclosures and the destruction of feudal retinues described earlier: the same violence that created a propertyless rural population also dissolved the structures that had blocked industrial investment. The new manufactures then \"were established at sea-ports, or at points in the countryside which were beyond the control of the old municipalities and their guilds.\" This is a geography of escape : industry fled to jurisdictions where guild regulation did not reach, which explains \"the bitter struggle of the corporate towns against these new seed-beds of industry\" in England. Footnote 3 provides concrete evidence: as late as 1794, \"the small cloth-makers of Leeds sent a deputation to Parliament, with a petition for a law to forbid any merchant from becoming a manufacturer.\" Guild resistance was the reaction of the old order to the new industrial threat. V. The Idyllic Dawn — Colonial Plunder as the Chief Moment Now Marx delivers the famous, bitterly ironic paragraph. \"The discovery of gold and silver in America, the extirpation, enslavement and entombment in mines of the indigenous population of that continent, the beginnings of the conquest and plunder of India, and the conversion of Africa into a preserve for the commercial hunting of blackskins, are all things which characterize the dawn of the era of capitalist production. These idyllic proceedings are the chief moments of primitive accumulation.\" The word \"idyllic\" is a savage inversion: what bourgeois romance calls pastoral innocence is, in fact, genocide and enslavement. \"Hard on their heels follows the commercial war of the European nations, which has the globe as its battlefield.\" Marx then dates this war in three stages: \"It begins with the revolt of the Netherlands from Spain\" (the Dutch Revolt against Habsburg Spain), \"assumes gigantic dimensions in England's Anti-Jacobin War\" (the counter-revolutionary wars against France), and \"is still going on in the shape of the Opium Wars against China.\" The chronology shows that primitive accumulation is not a closed historical episode but a continuous, ongoing process. The \"different moments of primitive accumulation\" are then assigned \"in more or less chronological order\" to Spain, Portugal, Holland, France, and England, and are \"systematically combined together at the end of the seventeenth century in England; the combination embraces the colonies, the national debt, the modern tax system, and the system of protection.\" The rest of the chapter unpacks exactly these four instruments. VI. Force as Midwife — The State as Economic Power The chapter now delivers its central theoretical proposition. \"These methods depend in part on brute force, for instance the colonial system. But they all employ the power of the state, the concentrated and organized force of society, to hasten, as in a hothouse, the process of transformation of the feudal mode of production into the capitalist mode, and to shorten the transition.\" Note the careful formulation: some methods are brute force, but all of them employ state power , which Marx defines as \"the concentrated and organized force of society.\" The \"hothouse\" metaphor captures artificial acceleration — the state as a mechanism that compresses centuries into decades. Then the aphorism: \"Force is the midwife of every old society which is pregnant with a new one. It is itself an economic power.\" This is the theoretical core of the chapter. It rests on a dialectical image: the new society is already immanent within the old (\"pregnant with a new one\"), and force merely assists the birth. But Marx immediately insists that force is not external to the economy but is itself an economic power — it produces, redistributes, and capitalizes. This dissolves the bourgeois separation of a peaceful \"economic\" sphere from a political \"force\" sphere. The nuance is that it is not force in general but state-mediated, concentrated, organized force that performs this function. VII. The Dutch Model — Treachery, Man-Stealing, Depopulation Marx substantiates the claim with evidence. He cites W. Howitt on \"the Christian colonial system,\" whose \"barbarities and desperate outrages\" are \"not to be paralleled by those of any other race.\" A footnote (4) identifies Howitt's Colonisation and Christianity (1838), and adds the analytical instruction that \"this stuff ought to be studied in detail, to see what the bourgeois makes of himself and of the worker when he can model the world according to his own image without any interference.\" This is a key methodological point: the colonies are a laboratory — a space where capital, unconstrained by domestic checks, reveals its essential logic. Holland, \"the model capitalist nation of the seventeenth century,\" is the exhibit. Its history is \"one of the most extraordinary relations of treachery, bribery, massacre, and meanness\" (Raffles). The centerpiece is the system of \"stealing men in Celebes, in order to get slaves for Java.\" Marx details its division of labour: \"Man-stealers were trained for this purpose. The thief, the interpreter and the seller were the chief agents in this trade, the native princes were the chief sellers.\" Young people were \"hidden in secret dungeons on Celebes, until they were ready for sending to the slave-ships.\" An official report on Macassar describes it as \"full of secret prisons, one more horrible than the other, crammed with unfortunates, victims of greed and tyranny fettered in chains, forcibly torn from their families.\" Two further episodes complete the indictment. In 1641, to seize Malacca, the Dutch bribed the Portuguese governor, were let into the town, and \"went straight to his house and assassinated him, so as to be able to 'abstain' from paying the £21,875 which was the amount of his bribe.\" And the demographic evidence: Banjuwangi, a province of Java, \"numbered over 80,000 inhabitants in 1750 and only 18,000 in 1811.\" The quantitative collapse of population is the empirical measure of \"peaceful commerce\" — Marx's closing sarcasm. VIII. The East India Company — Accumulation without a Shilling Marx turns to the English East India Company. Beyond political control of India, it held \"the exclusive monopoly of the tea trade, as well as of the Chinese trade in general, and the transport of goods to and from Europe.\" But the coasting trade \"round India and between the islands,\" as well as the internal trade, \"was the monopoly of the higher officials of the Company.\" The monopolies of \"salt, opium, betel and other commodities were inexhaustible mines of wealth.\" Officials themselves \"fixed the price and plundered the unfortunate Hindus at will,\" and \"the Governor-General took part in this private traffic.\" The mechanism is pure rent-seeking and graft: \"His favourites received contracts under conditions whereby they, cleverer than the alchemists, made gold out of nothing.\" Hence \"great fortunes sprang up like mushrooms in a day; primitive accumulation proceeded without the advance of even a shilling.\" The alchemy metaphor is precise: wealth was manufactured without production. The evidence is quantitative: the Warren Hastings trial \"swarms with such cases.\" A contract for opium was given to Sullivan, who sold it to Binn for £40,000; Binn resold it the same day for £60,000, and the ultimate purchaser \"still extracted a tremendous profit.\" According to a list laid before Parliament, \"the Company and its officials obtained £6,000,000 between 1757 and 1766 from the Indians in the form of gifts.\" And in 1769–70 the English \"created a famine by buying up all the rice and refusing to sell it again, except at fabulous prices.\" A footnote (6) extends the indictment: in 1866, over a million Hindus died of hunger in Orissa alone, yet an attempt was made \"to enrich the Indian treasury by the price at which the means of subsistence were sold to the starving people.\" IX. Scalp Bounties and the \"Christian Character\" of Colonialism Marx broadens the claim: the treatment of indigenous populations was \"at its most frightful in plantation-colonies set up exclusively for the export trade, such as the West Indies, and in rich and well-populated countries, such as Mexico and India, that were given over to plunder.\" Yet even in colonies \"properly so called,\" \"the Christian character of primitive accumulation was not belied.\" He then supplies a documented price list of human life from Puritan New England. In 1703, \"the Puritans of New England, by decrees of their assembly set a premium of £40 on every Indian scalp and every captured redskin\"; in 1720, \"a premium of £100 was set on every scalp\"; in 1744, \"for a male scalp of 12 years and upwards, £100 in new currency, for a male prisoner £105, for women and children prisoners £50, for the scalps of women and children £50.\" The escalating schedule of bounties is evidence of the systematic , priced , and legalized character of the violence. Marx then notes a historical irony: decades later, \"the colonial system took its revenge on the descendants of the pious pilgrim fathers,\" who \"were tomahawked by the redskins\" at English instigation and for English money, while the British Parliament proclaimed bloodhounds and scalping as \"means that God and Nature had given into its hand.\" The phrase inverts the natural-law rhetoric of colonialists into an explicit confession of organized violence. X. The Colonial System as Forcing-House and the \"Strange God\" Marx now states the economic function of the colonial system: it \"ripened trade and navigation as in a hothouse.\" The \"companies called Monopolia\" (a term he attributes to Luther) were \"powerful levers for the concentration of capital.\" Colonies \"provided a market for the budding manufactures, and a vast increase in accumulation which was guaranteed by the mother country's monopoly of the market.\" And \"the treasures captured outside Europe by undisguised looting, enslavement and murder flowed back to the mother-country and were turned into capital there.\" So colonial violence is not incidental to capital accumulation; it is a supply channel for it. Holland, which \"first brought the colonial system to its full development,\" stood at its commercial zenith in 1648 — in \"almost exclusive possession of the East Indian trade and the commerce between the south-east and the north-west of Europe,\" its fisheries, shipping, and manufactures surpassing all others. A footnote cites G. von Gülich's claim that \"the total capital of the Republic was probably greater than that of all the rest of Europe put together.\" Marx adds a devastating corrective: \"Gülich forgets to add that by 1648 the people of Holland were more over-worked, poorer and more brutally oppressed than those of all the rest of Europe put together.\" Aggregate national wealth and popular misery are compatible — indeed related. Marx then states a general law of the period: \"Today, industrial supremacy brings with it commercial supremacy. In the period of manufacture it is the reverse: commercial supremacy produces industrial predominance.\" Hence \"the preponderant role played by the colonial system at that time.\" This raises the colonial system to the status of a \"strange God\" who \"perched himself side by side with the old divinities of Europe on the altar, and one fine day threw them all overboard with a shove and a kick. It proclaimed the making of profit as the ultimate and the sole purpose of mankind.\" XI. The National Debt — Alienation of the State and the Birth of Bankocracy Marx turns to the system of public credit. National debts originated \"in Genoa and Venice as early as the Middle Ages\" and \"took possession of Europe as a whole during the period of manufacture.\" The colonial system, \"with its maritime trade and its commercial wars, served as a forcing-house for the credit system,\" which \"first took root in Holland.\" The national debt is defined theoretically as \"the alienation [ Veräusserung ] of the state — whether that state is despotic, constitutional or republican,\" and it \"marked the capitalist era with its stamp.\" The gloss in the footnote — \"Alienation by sale\" — clarifies the legal-economic sense: the state sells itself into debt. The punchline: \"The only part of the so-called national wealth that actually enters into the collective possession of a modern nation is — the national debt.\" A footnote (7) records Cobbett's wry observation that English public institutions are all \"royal,\" while the debt alone is \"national.\" Marx then traces the function of the debt as a lever of primitive accumulation. It \"endows unproductive money with the power of creation and thus turns it into capital, without forcing it to expose itself to the troubles and risks inseparable from its employment in industry or even in usury.\" This is the mechanism of risk-transfer: creditors \"actually give nothing away, for the sum lent is transformed into public bonds, easily negotiable, which go on functioning in their hands just as so much hard cash would.\" The debt thus creates idle rentiers, financiers acting as \"middlemen between the government and the nation,\" tax-farmers, merchants, and manufacturers for whom a national loan is \"a capital fallen from heaven.\" Most consequentially, \"the national debt has given rise to joint-stock companies, to dealings in negotiable effects of all kinds, and to speculation: in a word, it has given rise to stock-exchange gambling and the modern bankocracy.\" The section then examines the Bank of England as the institutional embodiment. At their birth, great banks \"were only associations of private speculators, who placed themselves by the side of governments and, thanks to the privileges they received, were in a position to advance money to those governments.\" Hence \"the accumulation of the national debt has no more infallible measure than the successive rise in the stocks of these banks, whose full development dates from the founding of the Bank of England in 1694.\" Marx details the double-coinage mechanism: the Bank \"began by lending its money to the government at 8 per cent; at the same time it was empowered by Parliament to coin money out of the same capital, by lending it a second time to the public in the form of bank-notes.\" It could use these notes \"for discounting bills, making advances on commodities and buying the precious metals,\" and soon \"this credit-money, created by the bank itself, became the coin in which the latter made its loans to the state, and paid, on behalf of the state, the interest on the public debt.\" The famous formulation follows: \"It was not enough that the bank gave with one hand and took back more with the other; it remained, even while receiving money, the eternal creditor of the nation down to the last farthing advanced.\" Gradually it became \"the inevitable receptacle of the metallic hoard of the country, and the centre of gravity of all commercial credit.\" The writings of the time (e.g. Bolingbroke's) show the effect of \"this brood of bankocrats, financiers, rentiers, brokers, stock-jobbers.\" A footnote (8) quotes Montesquieu: if the Tartars flooded into Europe, \"it would be a difficult job to make them understand what a financier is.\" XII. International Credit and Capitalized Child-Blood \"Along with the national debt there arose an international credit system, which often conceals one of the sources of primitive accumulation in this or that people.\" The verb is important: credit conceals . Marx then traces a chain of concealed origins. \"Thus the villainies of the Venetian system of robbery formed one of the secret foundations of Holland's wealth in capital, for Venice in her years of decadence lent large sums of money to Holland.\" There is \"a similar relationship between Holland and England.\" By the early eighteenth century, Holland's manufactures \"had been far outstripped,\" and it had \"ceased to be the nation preponderant in commerce and industry.\" Therefore, from 1701 to 1776, one of its main businesses was \"the lending out of enormous amounts of capital, especially to its great rival England.\" Marx then draws the contemporary parallel: \"The same thing is going on today between England and the United States. A great deal of capital, which appears today in the United States without any birth-certificate, was yesterday, in England, the capitalized blood of children.\" This is the analytical heart of the section: the credit system launders the origins of capital. The \"birth-certificate\" metaphor denies capital any legitimate genealogy; what appears as abstract, innocent capital is in fact congealed violence. XIII. The Fiscal System and Protection — Over-Taxation as Principle Marx next shows that taxation is the \"necessary complement\" of national loans. Because the debt is backed by state revenues, which must cover interest payments, loans enable the government \"to meet extraordinary expenses without the taxpayers feeling it immediately, but they still make increased taxes necessary as a consequence.\" Conversely, \"the raising of taxation caused by the accumulation of debts contracted one after another compels the government always to have recourse to new loans for new extraordinary expenses.\" This is a self-reinforcing loop. The modern fiscal system, \"whose pivot is formed by taxes on the most necessary means of subsistence (and therefore by increases in their price), thus contains within itself the germ of automatic progression. Over-taxation is not an accidental occurrence, but rather a principle.\" Marx cites Holland's De Witt (via P. de la Court's Political Maxims , 1669), who \"extolled it in his Maxims as the best system for making the wage-labourer submissive, frugal, industrious ... and overburdened with work.\" So over-taxation was a deliberate disciplinary technology. Marx's own focus, however, is \"less concerned with the destructive influence it exercises on the situation of the wage-labourer than with the forcible expropriation, resulting from it, of peasants and artisans, in short, of all the constituents of the lower middle class.\" He notes that \"there are no two opinions about this, even among the bourgeois economists,\" and that the effect is \"heightened still further by the system of protection.\" A nuance follows: public debt and the fiscal system led writers \"like Cobbett, Doubleday and others, to seek here, incorrectly, the fundamental cause of the misery of the people in modern times.\" The debt-and-tax system is a mechanism , not the ultimate cause. Protectionism is then defined functionally: it was \"an artificial means of manufacturing manufacturers, or expropriating independent workers, of capitalizing the national means of production and subsistence, and of forcibly cutting short the transition from a mode of production that was out of date to the modern mode of production.\" The image of \"manufacturing manufacturers\" is deliberately paradoxical: the state produces the capitalist class. \"The European states tore each other to pieces to gain the patent of this invention,\" and once in the service of profit \"did not restrict themselves to plundering their own people\" but \"also forcibly uprooted all industries in the neighbouring dependent countries, as for example England did with the Irish woollen manufacture.\" On the Continent, following Colbert's example, the process was simplified: \"The original capital for industry here came in part directly out of the state treasury.\" Mirabeau's cry is quoted as evidence: \"Why... why go so far to seek the cause of the manufacturing glory of Saxony before the war? One hundred and eighty millions of debts contracted by the sovereigns!\" XIV. The Herod-like Slaughter — Child-Slavery in the Factories Marx now shows how these \"offshoots of the period of manufacture\" — \"Colonial system, public debts, heavy taxes, protection, commercial wars\" — \"swell to gigantic proportions during the period of infancy of large-scale industry.\" The birth of large-scale industry is celebrated by \"a vast, Herod-like slaughter of the innocents\" — a scriptural allusion to the massacre of children, here applied to the factory system. \"Like the royal navy, the factories were recruited by means of the press-gang\" — a comparison to naval impressment, i.e., forced labour recruitment. Marx then introduces Sir F. M. Eden, an apologist who was indifferent to the expropriation of the agricultural population and self-satisfied about it, yet \"does not show the same economic insight into the necessity of child-stealing and child-slavery for the transformation of manufacturing production into factory production and the establishment of the true relation between capital and labour-power.\" Eden's blind spot is the functional necessity of child labour. Marx quotes Eden's own moralizing question — whether any manufacture requiring that \"cottages and workhouses should be ransacked for poor children,\" that they should work by turns through most of the night and be \"robbed of that rest which... is most required by the young,\" and that children be collected \"in such a manner that the contagion of example cannot but lead to profligacy and debauchery\" — \"will add to the sum of individual or national felicity?\" The Fielden extract then provides the detailed mechanism. In Derbyshire, Nottinghamshire, and Lancashire, \"the newly-invented machinery was used in large factories built on the sides of streams capable of turning the water-wheel.\" Thousands of hands were suddenly required in places \"remote from towns,\" and Lancashire was \"comparatively thinly populated and barren,\" so \"a population was all that she now wanted. The small and nimble fingers of little children being by very far the most in request, the custom instantly sprang up of procuring apprentices (!) from the different parish workhouses of London, Birmingham, and elsewhere.\" Note the parenthetical exclamation mark after \"apprentices\" — Marx signals the hypocrisy: this was not apprenticeship but trafficking. \"Many, many thousands of these little, hapless creatures were sent down into the north, being from the age of 7 to the age of 13 or 14 years old.\" The master — whom Marx parenthetically labels \"(i.e. the child-stealer)\" — clothed, fed, and lodged them in an \"apprentice house\" near the factory. Overseers were paid \"in proportion to the quantity of work that they could exact,\" so \"cruelty was, of course, the consequence.\" Fielden recounts that the children \"were harassed to the brink of death by excess of labour ... were flogged, fettered and tortured ... starved to the bone while flogged to their work and ... even in some instances ... were driven to commit suicide.\" The valleys became \"the dismal solitudes of torture, and of many a murder.\" Profits were enormous, \"but this only whetted the appetite that it should have satisfied,\" so manufacturers introduced \"night-working\": two shifts alternating, \"the day-set getting into the beds that the night-set had just quitted, and in their turn again, the night-set getting into the beds that the day-set quitted in the morning. It is a common tradition in Lancashire, that the beds never get cold.\" Footnote 11 extends the evidence: Aikin (1795) and Gisborne on the earlier infamies; the steam-engine's relocation of factories from waterfalls to towns meant the \"'abstemious' profit-monger found his childish material ready to hand, without having to bring slaves forcibly from the workhouses.\" It also records Sir R. Peel's 1815 bill for the protection of children and Francis Horner's Commons speech of 6 June 1815: \"with a bankrupt's effects, a gang, if he might use the word, of these children had been put up to sale, and were advertised publicly as part of the property.\" Another case involved transfers of apprenticed boys found in \"a state of absolute famine,\" and a formal agreement between a London parish and a Lancashire manufacturer \"by which it was stipulated, that with every twenty sound children one idiot should be taken.\" XV. The Asiento, Liverpool, and the Pedestal of New World Slavery Marx then records the moral atmosphere: \"With the development of capitalist production during the period of manufacture, the public opinion of Europe lost its last remnant of shame and conscience. The nations bragged cynically of every infamy that served them as a means to the accumulation of capital.\" He cites A. Anderson's commercial annals, which trumpet as \"a triumph of English statesmanship\" the fact that \"at the Peace of Utrecht, England extorted from the Spaniards, by the Asiento Treaty, the privilege of being allowed to ply the slave trade, not only between Africa and the English West Indies... but also between Africa and Spanish America.\" England thereby acquired the right to supply Spanish America \"until 1743 with 4,800 Negroes a year.\" Marx notes the side effect: \"At the same time this threw an official cloak over British smuggling.\" The quantitative evidence then measures capital's dependence on the trade: \"Liverpool grew fat on the basis of the slave trade. This was its method of primitive accumulation.\" The Liverpool \"quality\" are dubbed \"the Pindars of the slave trade\" — a footnote explaining that Pindar wrote triumphal odes, so the Liverpool bourgeoisie \"continues to celebrate its own triumphs in the era of the slave trade.\" Marx then quotes Aikin's account that the slave trade \"has coincided with that spirit of bold adventure which has characterized the trade of Liverpool,\" occasioning \"vast employment for shipping and sailors\" and \"greatly augmented the demand for the manufactures of the country.\" The famous series follows: \"In 1730 Liverpool employed 15 ships in the slave trade; in 1751, 53; in 1760, 74; in 1770, 96; and in 1792, 132.\" This numerical escalation is the empirical spine of the chapter's argument. Marx then generalizes: \"While the cotton industry introduced child-slavery into England, in the United States it gave the impulse for the transformation of the earlier, more or less patriarchal slavery into a system of commercial exploitation.\" The decisive theoretical sentence states the interdependence: \"In fact the veiled slavery of the wage-labourers in Europe needed the unqualified slavery of the New World as its pedestal.\" Footnote 12 supplies comparative evidence: in 1790, \"there were in the English West Indies ten slaves to one free man, in the French fourteen to one, and in the Dutch twenty-three to one.\" The \"veil\" metaphor links back to the concealment theme of the international credit section. XVI. \"Tantae Molis Erat\" — The Labouring Poor and Burke The chapter reaches its rhetorical crescendo with a Latin tag from Virgil's Aeneid (I.33): \"Tantae molis erat\" — \"So great was the effort required to found the Roman race.\" Marx appropriates it: so great was the effort \"to unleash the 'eternal natural laws' of the capitalist mode of production, to complete the process of separation between the workers and the conditions of their labour, to transform, at one pole, the social means of production and subsistence into capital, and at the opposite pole, the mass of the population into wage-labourers, into the free 'labouring poor', that artificial product of modern history.\" The scare quotes around \"eternal natural laws\" mock the naturalization of capitalist relations; the word \"artificial\" denies that the \"labouring poor\" is a natural category. Footnote 13 is a dense philological genealogy. The expression \"labouring poor\" appears in English legislation \"from the moment when the class of wage-labourers becomes noticeable,\" used in opposition both to the \"idle poor\" (beggars) and to workers \"who are not yet plucked fowl but rather the possessors of their own means of labour.\" It migrated from the statute book into political economy, passed down \"by Culpeper, J. Child, etc. to Adam Smith and Eden.\" Marx then attacks Edmund Burke, who called the phrase \"execrable political cant.\" Burke, \"this sycophant,\" had in the pay of the English oligarchy been a \"romantic opponent of the French Revolution,\" just as earlier, in the pay of the North American colonies, he had \"played the liberal against the English oligarchy,\" and was \"a vulgar bourgeois through and through.\" Marx quotes Burke's dictum — \"The laws of commerce are the laws of Nature, and therefore the laws of God\" — and mocks: \"No wonder then that, true to the laws of God and Nature, he always sold himself in the best market!\" He notes that a favourable portrait of the liberal Burke appears in the writings of the Rev. Mr Tucker, \"who, though a parson and a Tory, was, apart from that, an honourable man.\" Marx's closing instruction is that \"it is our duty to brand again and again the Burkes of this world, who only differ from their successors in one thing — talent!\" Footnote 15 then quotes the Quarterly Reviewer's remark that capital is \"timid,\" which Marx corrects with Dunning's famous escalation: \"A certain 10 per cent will ensure its employment anywhere; 20 per cent certain will produce eagerness; 50 per cent positive audacity; 100 per cent will make it ready to trample on all human laws; 300 per cent, and there is not a crime at which it will scruple... Smuggling and the slave-trade have amply proved all that is here stated.\" XVII. The Closing Verdict — Blood and Dirt Marx closes with a quotation from Marie Augier's Du crédit public (1842): \"If money, according to Augier, 'comes into the world with a congenital blood-stain on one cheek,' capital comes dripping from head to toe, from every pore, with blood and dirt.\" The \"blood-stain\" is an inverted image of original sin — but whereas original sin is hereditary guilt without agency, capital's stain is produced, material, and ongoing. The image binds the entire chapter: the credit system, the colonial system, the fiscal system, and the factory system are the \"pores\" through which capital's violence has been condensed into value. This is the sentence that closes Chapter 31 and prepares the transition to the final chapter on the historical tendency of capitalist accumulation. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter establishes a set of fundamental principles. First, force is an economic power . Marx rejects the separation of a peaceful economic sphere from a coercive political one. State violence — colonies, debt, taxation, protection — is internal to capital formation, not incidental. The \"midwife\" and \"hothouse\" metaphors capture this as accelerated, artificial, deliberate transformation. Second, capitalism has an \"original sin\" that is materially produced . The chapter systematically destroys the idyllic narrative in which capital is the reward of abstinence and thrift. Each \"moment of primitive accumulation\" — gold and silver, enslavement, plunder, scalp bounties, national debt, childslavery — is documented with quantification: population collapse (Banjuwangi: 80,000 to 18,000), bounties (£40 → £100), financial flows (£6,000,000 in gifts; £40,000/$60,000 profits on a single opium contract), and the Liverpool slave-ship series (15, 53, 74, 96, 132). Third, capital is structurally interdependent across geographies . The \"veiled slavery of the wage-labourers in Europe needed the unqualified slavery of the New World as its pedestal.\" Metropolitan \"freedom\" and colonial \"unfreedom\" are the same relation seen from two sides. This anticipates later analyses of centre-periphery dynamics and dependency. Fourth, the credit and debt systems systematically conceal origins . The international credit system \"conceals\" sources of primitive accumulation; capital migrates laundered — \"without any birth-certificate.\" Contemporary relevance is explicit: the England–United States relationship in Marx's day replicates the Venice–Holland and Holland–England relationships. Fifth, national wealth and popular misery are compatible, even linked . Gülich's boast about Holland's aggregate capital is answered by the fact that the Dutch people were \"more over-worked, poorer and more brutally oppressed\" than any in Europe. Debt and taxation enrich the creditor class while disciplining and expropriating the wage-labourer. The broader implications are considerable. For political economy, the chapter invalidates contractualist and legalist origin stories — Hodgskin's \"by what law\" is answered: revolutions are not made with laws. Burke's dictum that \"the laws of commerce are the laws of Nature, and therefore the laws of God\" is exposed as the ideology of a \"sycophant\" who \"always sold himself in the best market.\" For historical method, primitive accumulation is shown to be continuous, not a closed episode — Marx's list reaches from the fifteenth century to the Opium Wars of his own present. For political practice, the inversion of classical-historical imagery — Rome's founding effort applied instead to the founding of capital, juxtaposed with a \"Herod-like slaughter of the innocents\" — frames capitalism's birth as a form of violence that must be politically confronted rather than naturalized. The chapter's final image, capital \"dripping from head to toe, from every pore, with blood and dirt,\" is not merely polemical ornament: it names the material relation that the rest of Capital has theoretically demonstrated, and it sets the moral and analytical stage for the concluding chapter on the historical tendency of capitalist accumulation. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 30 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 32"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 32", "title": "Chapter 32", "subtitle": "The Historical Tendency of Capitalist Accumulation", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 32/Chapter 32  The Historical Tendency of Capitalist Accumulation_explanation.html", "word_count": 6783, "read_time": "34 min read", "tags": ["Primitive Accumulation & Enclosures", "Capital Accumulation & Crises"], "keywords": ["Historical Tendency of Capitalist Accumulation", "Expropriation of the Expropriators", "Centralization of Capital & Socialization of Labour", "Negation of the Negation", "Bursting of the Capitalist Integument"], "text": "Chapter 32 The Historical Tendency Of Capitalist Accumulation - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,739 words ⏱️ ~34 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 32 of Capital , Volume One, titled \"The Historical Tendency of Capitalist Accumulation,\" is not a chapter of discovery but a chapter of summation — it is Marx's closing rhetorical and theoretical movement at the outermost edge of a volume that has, through twenty-five preceding chapters, painstakingly reconstructed the anatomy of the capitalist mode of production out of the commodity, the money, the labour process, and finally the mechanics of surplus-value production. Having traced in Part Eight (\"So-Called Primitive Accumulation\") how the pre-history of capital was written in blood and fire — enclosure, colonial plunder, usury, and the expropriation of the peasantry — Marx now does something structurally remarkable: he compresses the entire historical arc of private property into three dialectical phases and states the Tendenzgesetz — the law of the historical tendency of capitalist accumulation. The chapter's most famous sentence, \"The expropriators are expropriated,\" is not an isolated slogan but the culmination of a tightly compacted dialectical syllogism. The central problem this chapter confronts is a double question: What does primitive accumulation resolve itself into? — i.e., what is the historical genesis of capital, ontologically? — and, by implication, what is happening now, and where is it going? Marx's answer to the first question is that primitive accumulation, insofar as it is not a mere change of legal form (the direct transformation of slaves and serfs into wage-labourers), means one single substantive thing: the expropriation of the immediate producers , the dissolution of private property based on the labour of its owner. His answer to the second is the dialectical proposition that the very laws of capitalist production, operating with the inexorability of a natural process, generate their own negation — the socialization of property on the basis of the achievements of the capitalist era: co-operation and the common possession of the land and means of production. The intellectual objective is thus threefold. First, to classify private property into its historically distinct forms — worker's property versus non-worker's property — and to reveal the intermediate shades as transitions between two poles. Second, to demonstrate the internal contradiction of small-scale, self-owned production: that it is at once a necessary condition for free individuality and a mode of production that is self-limiting — \"compatible only with a system of production and a society moving within narrow limits which are of natural origin.\" Third, to establish the dialectical inevitability of the transition to socialized property, which Marx asserts is not moral aspiration but the operation of \"immanent laws.\" This chapter sits at the hinge of the entire Marxian theoretical framework. Volumes Two and Three will elaborate the circulation of capital and the formation of the general rate of profit respectively; but it is here, at the very end of Volume One, that the political conclusion of the critique of political economy is stated. The chapter's theoretical framework is Hegelian dialectic pressed into service for political economy — the scheme of negation, contradiction, and the negation of the negation — combined with the empirical-historical claim that this dialectic corresponds to an actual trajectory of European history. It also functions as a bridge: it transforms the historical narrative of Part Eight into a forward-looking prophecy, and by citing the Communist Manifesto in its final footnote, it deliberately connects the scholarly analysis of Capital back to the political program of the proletariat as \"the real revolutionary class.\" Everything that follows in Marx's system — the tendency of the rate of profit to fall, the concentration and centralization of capital, the theory of crisis — presupposes the structural claim made here: that capital accumulation produces, by its own intrinsic logic, the conditions of its own supersession. This chapter is, in short, the thesis statement of revolutionary political economy. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Foundational Question: What Does Primitive Accumulation Resolve Into? Marx opens the chapter with a question that is simultaneously historical and analytical: \"What does the primitive accumulation of capital, i.e. its historical genesis, resolve itself into?\" This framing is critical and deserves careful unpacking. By pairing \"primitive accumulation\" with \"historical genesis,\" Marx is refusing a purely economic deduction of capital's origin. Capital does not emerge from the logical structure of commodity circulation alone — it has a prehistory , an outside, a set of violent and extra-economic conditions that brought it into being. The phrase \"resolves itself into\" is also dialectical: it implies that primitive accumulation, however chaotic it appears as a welter of conquests and plunders, has a determinate result , a simple and analyzable core. The whole preceding historical narrative — the clearances in England, the hunting down of vagabonds under Elizabeth, the colonies, the national debt, the tariff — is here reduced to a single conceptual essence. That essence is stated immediately: \"In so far as it is not the direct transformation of slaves and serfs into wage-labourers, and therefore a mere change of form, it only means the expropriation of the immediate producers, i.e. the dissolution of private property based on the labour of its owner.\" This sentence contained two analytically distinct cases, and the distinction between them constitutes one of the chapter's most important conceptual moves. The first case — the mere change of form — is when a labouring population that was already unfree (slaves, serfs) is converted into a legally free but economically dependent wage-labour force. Here the mode of surplus extraction changes its juridical costume but the property relation (separation of the producer from the means of production) remains effectively intact. Marx is bracketing this case, but its bracketing itself contains a thesis: the abolition of slavery and serfdom is, viewed from the standpoint of the property relation, a change of form , not of substance . The second case — the one that \"only\" counts as genuine primitive accumulation in its substantive sense — is the expropriation of the immediate producers : the dispossession of people who own the conditions of their own labour. This is the case where the property relation itself is destroyed and reconstituted. 2.2 The Two Polarities of Private Property and the Intermediate Shades Having defined the essence of primitive accumulation as the dissolution of labour-based private property, Marx immediately needs to establish what that form of property is , so that its dissolution can be understood as a determinate historical event rather than a mere disappearance of ownership. He therefore makes a distinction fundamental to the entire chapter: Private property, as the antithesis to social, collective property, exists only where the means of labour and the external conditions of labour belong to private individuals. But \"according to whether these private individuals are workers or non-workers, private property has a different character.\" This is the chapter's master distinction. Private property is not a single homogeneous institution; it is a genus with two species . The worker's private property in his means of production — the peasant's plot, the artisan's tools — represents one species. The non-worker's private property — the capitalist's ownership of means of production that others operate — represents the other species. Both are private property, both exclude collective ownership, yet they are polar opposites in terms of the labour relation that animates them. The first is grounded in the worker's own labour; the second is grounded in the exploitation of the labour of others. Marx's phrase \"the innumerable different shades of private property which appear at first sight are only reflections of the intermediate situations which lie between the two extremes\" is a methodological warning to the reader: the great empirical variety of property forms (tenant farming, small manufacture, sharecropping, guild production) is not analytically primary. The primary reality is the polarity; the shades are mixed or transitional cases. This distinction had a specific polemical target which the reader should register as a nuance and counter-argument in Marx's argument: bourgeois political economy, and the vulgar apologetics of Marx's own day, treated private property as one unified and sacred institution, so that any attack on the expropriation of the smallholder could be denounced as an attack on \"property\" in general. Marx's distinction dismantles that apologetic move by showing that capitalism itself was and is the great destroyer of the worker's property. The capitalist has already expropriated the smallholder; what remains is only the second act. 2.3 The Classical Form: Small-Scale Industry and Free Individuality Marx then offers what amounts to a positive assessment — unusual in its warmth — of the pre-capitalist property form. \"The private property of the worker in his means of production is the foundation of small-scale industry, and small-scale industry is a necessary condition for the development of social production and of the free individuality of the worker himself.\" Terminologically, small-scale industry here does not mean small factories but the mode of production in which the producer owns and personally operates the instruments of labour: peasant agriculture, artisan handicraft. Two claims are made about it. First, it is a \"necessary condition for the development of social production\" — meaning that without a stage in which producers freely possessed their instruments, and could therefore exercise independent skill and initiative, the later development of productive forces could not have been prepared. Second, and more provocatively, it is a necessary condition for \"the free individuality of the worker himself.\" Marx is here conceding a real historical achievement to the property form he is about to describe as doomed: the independent producer is a genuine personality in a way that neither the slave, the serf, nor — as the chapter will implicitly argue — the wage-labourer can be. There is a critical nuance embedded here: Marx does not romanticize pre-capitalism, but he refuses to treat the destruction of small property as pure progress. Its loss is a real loss. Marx then makes a concession to historical comparativism: \"Of course, this mode of production also exists under slavery, serfdom and other situations of dependence.\" He is acknowledging that the economic form of small-scale production can be embedded in unfree social relations. But then he introduces a condition for its full realization: it \"flourishes, unleashes the whole of its energy, attains its adequate classical form, only where the worker is the free proprietor of the conditions of his labour, and sets them in motion himself.\" The two examples given are precise: \"the peasant owns the land he cultivates, or the artisan owns the tool with which he is an accomplished performer.\" Note the word \"accomplished\" — the artisan's ownership of his tool is not incidental to his skill; the tool is the material embodiment of a craft mastery. Small-scale industry in its classical form is the unity of the labourer, the instrument, and the skill. 2.4 The Inner Limits and Self-Limiting Dynamics of Small Production The chapter now performs its most important analytical pivot. Having granted small production its real virtues, Marx specifies the conditions of its existence — and these conditions are precisely the source of its historical vulnerability. \"This mode of production presupposes the fragmentation of holdings, and the dispersal of the other means of production.\" Fragmentation and dispersal are the necessary presuppositions because each independent producer must own his own means of production; therefore the means of production cannot be gathered into large aggregates. Marx then deduces a series of exclusions from this presupposition, and the argument works as a kind of eliminative proof. Because concentration is excluded: - Co-operation is excluded (no many-handed working of the same process); - Division of labour within each separate process of production is excluded (no internal differentiation of tasks within a workshop); - Social control and regulation of the forces of nature is excluded (no collective engineering of rivers, no large-scale application of science to production); - The free development of the productive forces of society is excluded. The argument is: the very precondition of small property forecloses the highest development of the productive powers of labour. This is not a moral judgment but a structural inference. The mode is therefore \"compatible only with a system of production and a society moving within narrow limits which are of natural origin.\" The phrase \"of natural origin\" is analytically loaded: the boundaries of small production are set not by the conscious collective activity of humans but by the given facts of soil, climate, and population — that is, by nature rather than by history. Marx here invokes external authority to seal the argument: \"To perpetuate it would be, as Pecqueur rightly says, 'to decree universal mediocrity'.\" The footnote identifies the source: C. Pecqueur, Théorie nouvelle d'économie sociale et politique , Paris, 1842, p. 435. Pecqueur was a French socialist economist; Marx is using a fellow-critic of capitalism as a witness that small property cannot be the aim of a future society, only the nostalgia of a past one. This is a counter-argument to the romantic socialism that would restore small property: Marx denies that going backward is possible or desirable. 2.5 The Material Means of Its Own Destruction and the Birth of New Passions From the limits of small production, Marx derives its historical necessity of collapse in a passage of great dialectical compression: \"At a certain stage of development, it brings into the world the material means of its own destruction. From that moment, new forces and new passions spring up in the bosom of society, forces and passions which feel themselves to be fettered by that society. It has to be annihilated; it is annihilated.\" Several things are happening in this passage simultaneously, and they deserve to be unpacked in sequence. First, the material means of its own destruction . Small production, precisely by developing social production even within narrow limits, creates the technology, the markets, the tools, and the techniques (division of labour on a larger scale, machinery, long-distance trade) that cannot be contained within small property relations. The seed of destruction is internal and material, not external and accidental. Second, the new forces and new passions . This is where Marx departs from a purely economic account and asserts a subjective dimension. The new forces are material productive powers; the new passions are human interests, needs, and class desires — the aspirations of merchants, manufacturers, would-be capitalists, and also the desires of producers to escape their narrow limits. These are \"fettered by that society\" — that is, contained within and frustrated by the small-property structure. Third, the modal structure of the collapse: \"It has to be annihilated; it is annihilated.\" This is the dialectical syntax of necessity — a must followed immediately by an is . The first clause states the logical-historical necessity; the second states the accomplished fact. Marx is writing from the vantage point of a Europe in which small property had largely already been destroyed (this is the retrospective stance of the whole primitive accumulation section). 2.6 The Anatomy of the Expropriation: Dwarf Property and Giant Property The next sentence provides the full content of what the annihilation consists in. It is a long, cumulative sentence, and it is worth laying out its series of transformations: The transformation of the individualized and scattered means of production into socially concentrated means of production. This is the material/technical dimension — the aggregation of scattered tools, plots, and workshops into combined enterprises. The transformation, therefore, of the dwarf-like property of the many into the giant property of the few. This is the distributional dimension — the ownership consequence. Note the rhetorical asymmetry: \"dwarf-like\" against \"giant.\" The imagery is deliberate. What one producer lost in scale, another gained in scale; the total is not neutral. The expropriation of the great mass of the people from the soil, from the means of subsistence and from the instruments of labour. This is the human dimension — the triple dispossession that creates the wage-labourer, who is now free of land, free of the means of subsistence, and free of tools, and therefore free to sell the only commodity remaining to him: his labour-power. This triple expropriation, Marx tells us, \"forms the pre-history of capital.\" And it comprises \"a whole series of forcible methods, and we have only passed in review those that have been epoch-making as methods of the primitive accumulation of capital.\" This is a crucial methodological caveat : the historical chapters of Part Eight have not been exhaustive but selective. Marx has chosen exemplary, transitional moments, not a complete inventory. He is claiming representativeness, not completeness. Then comes the moral and political characterization, and it is deliberately extreme: \"The expropriation of the direct producers was accomplished by means of the most merciless barbarism, and under the stimulus of the most infamous, the most sordid, the most petty and the most odious of passions.\" The piling up of superlatives (\"most merciless,\" \"most infamous,\" \"most sordid,\" \"most petty,\" \"most odious\") is not merely rhetorical inflation; it is a polemical counter to the bourgeois narrative of primitive accumulation as thrift, industry, and abstinence . Marx is dismantling the apologetic picture (which he had earlier derided) according to which the first accumulators were industrious and their descendants deserved their wealth. The passions that drive expropriation are the ugliest, and the processes are barbarous. 2.7 The Supplanting: Personal Property Versus Capitalist Property The next sentence states the fundamental transformation of property form: \"Private property which is personally earned, i.e. which is based, as it were, on the fusing together of the isolated, independent working individual with the conditions of his labour, is supplanted by capitalist private property, which rests on the exploitation of alien, but formally free labour.\" The definitional precision here is essential. First, Marx qualifies \"personally earned\" with \"as it were\" — a self-reflexive hedge. He is aware that the \"earning\" is not a matter of wage contract but of the fusing of the producer with the means of production. The phrase \"fusing together\" is important: in the small-property form, the individual and the conditions of labour are not two parties in a transaction but an organic unity. Second, capitalist private property \"rests on the exploitation of alien, but formally free labour.\" The phrase formally free is a technical term within Marx's system (elaborated in Chapter Six and Chapter Twenty-Six): the wage-labourer is free in a double sense — free of the means of production, and free to enter contracts. The freedom is formal — legal — because it coexists with economic necessity. The juxtaposition of \"alien\" and \"formally free\" captures the whole mystification of the wage contract: it appears as a free exchange between equals while it in fact transfers the product and the surplus to the owner of the means of production. The footnote attached to this sentence is significant: \"We are in a situation which is entirely new for society ... we are striving to separate every kind of property from every kind of labour\" (Sismondi, Nouveaux Principes d'économie politique , Vol. 2, p. 434). The Swiss-French economist Sismondi is being used, against his own nostalgic intent, to diagnose the essential novelty of capitalism: the separation of property from labour . In small production, property and labour coincide; in capitalism they are divorced. This is the analytical essence Marx extracts even from a critic who lamented the change. 2.8 The Second Phase: A New Form of Socialization The chapter now turns the corner. \"As soon as this metamorphosis has sufficiently decomposed the old society throughout its depth and breadth, as soon as the workers have been turned into proletarians, and their means of labour into capital, as soon as the capitalist mode of production stands on its own feet, the further socialization of labour and the further transformation of the soil and other means of production into socially exploited and therefore communal means of production takes on a new form.\" Three conditions define the threshold: (1) the old society is decomposed \"throughout its depth and breadth\" — the disintegration is total, not partial; (2) the workers have been turned into proletarians and their means of labour into capital — the social transformation of persons and things is complete and mutually corresponding; (3) the capitalist mode of production \"stands on its own feet\" — it is no longer parasitic on prior forms but reproduces itself on its own terms. Only at this point does the further socialization take \"a new form.\" Nuance and counter-argument: There is a crucial shift in the subject of expropriation. \"What is now to be expropriated is not the self-employed worker, but the capitalist who exploits a large number of workers.\" This marks the transition from the pre-history of capital to its history proper . The first expropriation was extra-economic and violent; the second is described as occurring through \"the action of the immanent laws of capitalist production itself.\" Marx is not proposing that a revolutionary seizure of the capitalists' property is the mechanism; he is asserting that the mechanism is internal to capitalism. The revolution, in this formulation, is the completion of a process already underway, not its interruption. 2.9 The Mechanism: Centralization of Capitals and the Immanent Laws The mechanism of the second expropriation is stated with great economy: \"This expropriation is accomplished through the action of the immanent laws of capitalist production itself, through the centralization of capitals. One capitalist always strikes down many others.\" Terminological clarification is essential here, and the distinction belongs to Marx's technical vocabulary developed earlier in Volume One. Concentration is the increase in the size of individual capitals through the accumulation of surplus-value out of the capital's own operations. Centralization is the aggregation of already-existing capitals through competition — the absorption or annihilation of many capitals by fewer capitals. It is centralization that \"one capitalist always strikes down many others\" describes. This process is competitive and ruthless, but it operates according to the rules of the system rather than through external coercion. The phrase \"immanent laws\" is doing heavy theoretical work: it asserts that capitalism contains within its own dynamic structure the tendencies that will destroy its own property form. 2.10 The Expansion of Socialized Production: The Seven Developments The next sentence enumerates, in one dense movement, the concomitant transformations that accompany centralization. Marx lists them as follows: (1) \"the growth of the co-operative form of the labour process\"; (2) \"the conscious technical application of science\"; (3) \"the planned exploitation of the soil\"; (4) \"the transformation of the means of labour into forms in which they can only be used in common\"; (5) \"the economizing of all means of production by their use as the means of production of combined, socialized labour\"; (6) \"the entanglement of all peoples in the net of the world market\"; and (7) \"with this, the growth of the international character of the capitalist regime.\" This list deserves careful unpacking because it constitutes Marx's account of what capitalism achieves even as it destroys. Each item is a socialization — a transformation of what was individual into what is collective. The co-operative form of the labour process means that many workers now work together under a single command and with shared techniques. The \"conscious technical application of science\" means the replacement of traditional craft knowledge by systematic, methodical technique. The \"planned exploitation of the soil\" means agriculture organized by calculation rather than custom. The transformation of the means of labour into forms that \"can only be used in common\" is a material claim: modern machinery is intrinsically collective in its operation and therefore does not fit the property form of individual ownership. The \"economizing of means of production\" through combined use means that large-scale socialized labour uses fewer resources per unit of output. The world market and the international character of capitalism mean that the process, though it began locally, has become global. The theoretical significance of this enumeration is enormous and easy to miss. Marx is not only forecasting the crisis of capitalism; he is identifying the material preconditions of the post-capitalist order. The achievements of capitalism — co-operation, science, planning, machinery, the world market — are exactly what the coming social order will inherit and take further. This is why the final formulation of the chapter will insist that the second negation does not restore the old private property but establishes \"individual property on the basis of the achievements of the capitalist era.\" Capitalism is, in this account, a productive stage that prepares the material and social foundations for a mode of production that no longer has capitalism's exploitative form. This is a crucial nuance: Marx is not asking for the destruction of the productive forces capitalism has developed; he is asking for the transformation of their ownership and control. 2.11 The Contradictory Dynamic: Misery and Revolt Having listed the socializing achievements, Marx immediately introduces the contradictory dynamic that constitutes the motor of the historical tendency. \"Along with the constant decrease in the number of capitalist magnates, who usurp and monopolize all the advantages of this process of transformation, the mass of misery, oppression, slavery, degradation and exploitation grows; but with this there also grows the revolt of the working class, a class constantly increasing in numbers, and trained, united and organized by the very mechanism of the capitalist process of production.\" Three processes are here declared to advance together : 1. The decrease of magnates. The number of independent capitalists shrinks; ownership becomes concentrated in fewer hands. (This had been Marx's previous assertion in the analysis of centralization.) 2. The growth of the mass of misery, oppression, slavery, degradation, and exploitation. Note that \"slavery\" is used for wage-labour, intensifying the scandal: formal freedom coexists with a substantive condition Marx is willing to name slavery. The list is an ascending series of social harms. 3. The growth of the revolt of the working class. This is the pivotal claim. The very mechanism that produces misery also produces organization: \"trained, united and organized by the very mechanism of the capitalist process of production.\" The factory assembles workers into large collectivities, disciplines them into disciplined action, coordinates their movements — and thereby involuntarily trains them for collective action against the system. This is what the final footnote, drawn from the Manifesto , will call the production of \"its own grave-diggers.\" The rhetorical and analytical center of this passage is the word \"but\": \"the mass of misery ... grows; but with this there also grows the revolt.\" The \"but\" marks the dialectical reversal. Capitalist concentration produces its own antagonist. 2.12 The Fetter and the Bursting Asunder The chapter now states the crisis in classical Marxian terms: \"The monopoly of capital becomes a fetter upon the mode of production which has flourished alongside and under it. The centralization of the means of production and the socialization of labour reach a point at which they become incompatible with their capitalist integument. This integument is burst asunder. The knell of capitalist private property sounds. The expropriators are expropriated.\" Conceptual analysis of the fetter. The metaphor of the \"fetter\" comes from Marx's general theory of historical materialism, elaborated in the 1859 Preface to A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy , but the reader should notice a subtlety here. Marx says the monopoly of capital becomes a fetter upon \"the mode of production which has flourished alongside and under it.\" That is, the mode of production (the cooperative, socialized, scientifically organized production process) and the mode of appropriation (capitalist property) have come into conflict . Production has become social; appropriation remains private. The contradiction is not between two forms of the same mode but between a mode of production and a mode of property. The \"integument\" is the outer shell — the legal-property envelope within which the productive forces have grown. When the productive forces have outgrown the envelope, \"this integument is burst asunder.\" The metaphor is of growth: a mature organism bursting the husk that no longer contains it. This is why Marx can present the transition not as violent rupture imposed from outside but as the natural completion of an internal process. \"The knell ... sounds.\" The bell that tolls for the dying is a funeral bell for capitalist private property. The clause is intentionally ceremonial; the expropriation is being proclaimed. \"The expropriators are expropriated.\" The famous formulation depends entirely on the previous analysis: expropriators have expropriated the masses; now the same act is turned against them. The German Expropriation der Expropriateure — also translated as \"expropriation of the expropriators\" — carries the reciprocal symmetry that English loses. 2.13 The Dialectic Formalized: The Negation of the Negation The chapter's penultimate movement gives the passage its formal philosophical structure. \"The capitalist mode of appropriation, which springs from the capitalist mode of production, produces capitalist private property. This is the first negation of individual private property, as founded on the labour of its proprietor. But capitalist production begets, with the inexorability of a natural process, its own negation. This is the negation of the negation. It does not re-establish private property, but it does indeed establish individual property on the basis of the achievements of the capitalist era: namely co-operation and the possession in common of the land and the means of production produced by labour itself.\" This passage requires a careful Hegelian analysis , because Marx is deliberately invoking the dialectical schema of negation and the negation of the negation (which had been criticized by some of his contemporaries as a mystical doctrine). Three logical steps: 1. Thesis : individual private property, resting on the proprietor's own labour. 2. First negation (antithesis) : capitalist private property, resting on the exploitation of alien labour. This \"negates\" the first form by separating property from labour. 3. Second negation (synthesis, or negation of the negation) : individual property re-established at a higher level, \"on the basis of the achievements of the capitalist era.\" The most important analytical content is in the careful phrase \"It does not re-establish private property, but it does indeed establish individual property.\" This is a crucial distinction : the coming form is not a restoration of the scattered, individual private property of the smallholder. It is \"individual property\" — property that belongs to individuals as members of a collective, not property held by individuals against the collective. The content of this higher property form is specified: \"co-operation and the possession in common of the land and the means of production produced by labour itself.\" The word \"common\" is the key. The synthesis is not the marriage of private and public but the socialization of both. Nuance and potential criticism the reader should register: the phrase \"with the inexorability of a natural process\" (German: mit der Notwendigkeit eines Naturprozesses , \"with the necessity of a natural process\") invites the reading that Marx is asserting a deterministic, law-governed inevitability. This has been a locus of debate for a century and a half. There is a real tension here between (a) the causal language of \"immanent laws\" and \"natural process\" and (b) the substantive reliance on the growth of class consciousness (\"the revolt of the working class\"). If the outcome is inexorable, the revolt appears either superfluous (the mere epiphenomenon of mechanism) or contradictory (if capitalism collapses on its own, why is class struggle necessary?). Marx himself does not resolve this in the chapter; he seems to hold both — that the process is necessary and that it passes through the working class's agency. The reader should note that Marx uses both the language of nature and the language of political agency in the same paragraph, and that the tension is not resolved but maintained. 2.14 The Asymmetry of the Two Transformations The final paragraph of the chapter states an important comparative claim. \"The transformation of scattered private property resting on the personal labour of the individuals themselves into capitalist private property is naturally an incomparably more protracted, violent and difficult process than the transformation of capitalist private property, which in fact already rests on the carrying on of production by society, into social property.\" The argumentative structure here is one of asymmetry. The first transformation — from small property to capitalist property — was: - Protracted (centuries of enclosure, legislation, and violence); - Violent (dispossession, hanging of vagabonds, colonial genocide); - Difficult (resistance from the producers). The second — from capitalist private property to social property — will be, by the terms of the argument, less protracted, less violent, and less difficult, because capitalist private property \"already rests on the carrying on of production by society.\" That is: the mode of appropriation has already socialized itself in practice; only the legal form must be brought into alignment. The second transformation is, in effect, the recognition of a socialization already accomplished in the productive process. The final sentence crystallizes the moral-cum-historical difference: \"In the former case, it was a matter of the expropriation of the mass of the people by a few usurpers; but in this case, we have the expropriation of a few usurpers by the mass of the people.\" This antithesis — few against many, then many against few — is the chapter's closing image. Notice again that Marx's analytical claim includes a moral asymmetry: the first expropriation was illegitimate (\"usurpers\" expropriating \"the people\"); the second is the reversal of usurpation. The word \"usurpers\" is a juridical term: it names the expropriation as theft , thus confirming in Marx's own vocabulary the (inverted) Proudhonian dictum that property is theft — not the small producer's property but the capitalist's. 2.15 The Footnote to the Manifesto and the Political Frame The chapter's final footnote quotes extensively from the Communist Manifesto (1848), comprising three interlocking claims: (1) the bourgeoisie is the \"involuntary but willing promoter\" of industry, and replaces the isolation of workers with their \"revolutionary combination\"; (2) large-scale industry therefore cuts from under its own feet the foundation on which the bourgeoisie produces and appropriates; (3) the bourgeoisie consequently produces \"above all ... its own grave-diggers,\" whose fall and the victory of the proletariat are \"equally inevitable.\" The footnote further runs on to the Manifesto 's classification of the proletariat as \"the really revolutionary class\" and its dismissing of the petty bourgeoisie — the lower middle classes, small manufacturers, shopkeepers, artisans, peasants — as \"reactionary\" because they \"try to roll back the wheel of history.\" This footnote performs an important structural function. By attaching the Manifesto 's political program to the end of the analytical chapter, Marx is indicating that the scientific result of Capital is continuous with the political conclusions he had announced a generation earlier. The reader is being told, in effect: the analytical demonstration of the historical tendency is what gives the political program its foundation. Nuance to be registered: Marx's own later political writings (in particular the Paris Commune period and the 1872 preface to the German edition of the Manifesto ) would modify or qualify the categorical language about the inevitable defeat of other classes and the dictatorship of the proletariat's form. But within this chapter, the doctrine is stated unhedged. It is also worth noting that the analytical argument of Capital itself depends on the dialectical framework, and that the \"inexorability of a natural process\" is itself a theoretical assertion rather than an empirical forecast. The chapter is best read as the systematic summation of a framework, not as a prediction with testable probability. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles Revealed The chapter reveals four fundamental propositions that stand at the core of Marxian political economy. First, property is historically specific and internally differentiated . There is no single \"private property\"; there is a worker's property resting on labour and a capitalist's property resting on exploitation, and the two are related as historical moments in a dialectical process. Any political analysis that treats \"property\" as one institution — as liberal political theory typically does — misses the analytical core of capitalism. Second, the social relations of production have a tendency to develop their own contradictions . Small-scale production, precisely by its virtues, produces the material means of its own transcendence. Capitalist production, precisely by its achievements — co-operation, science, world market, machinery — produces the social forces — the organized working class — that will dismantle it. History, in Marx's account, has a directional logic grounded in the internal dynamics of its own productive arrangements. Third, socialization is a material process that precedes and undercuts legal property . The claim is that capitalist production has already socialized labour — the productive process is a collective, cooperative, scientifically organized activity — while the legal form of ownership remains private. The contradiction, therefore, is not invented but discovered. Marx's case for the transition rests on the mismatch between an already-social productive reality and a still-individual legal form. This is a distinctively materialist argument: the form of property is judged against the form of production. Fourth, the transformation of property has a class dimension . Expropriation is not a neutral act. The first expropriation is \"the expropriation of the mass by a few usurpers\"; the second is \"the expropriation of a few usurpers by the mass.\" The class asymmetry is the analytical and moral point. Marx is claiming not merely that history moves in a certain direction but that it moves through class contestation. The Systemic Laws The most important \"law\" stated in the chapter is the Tendenzgesetz itself: the tendency of capitalist accumulation, through centralization, to reduce the number of capitals while increasing the mass of the dispossessed and the intensity of exploitation, until the property form becomes a fetter upon the productive form and is burst asunder. This is a law in the sense that Marx intends: a structural, internal dynamic with a determinate direction, comparable to a law of nature. It has the logical form of: prior conditions → internal contradiction → crisis → resolution. The dialectical method gives this law its form; the historical narrative gives it content; the political program gives it direction. A second systemic law is the law of socialized appropriation : that where production has become social, appropriation must become social. This is not, in Marx's view, a moral requirement imposed from outside but a conclusion drawn from the mismatch between productive form and legal form. The chapter thus contains both a positive historical claim (what has happened) and a normative conclusion (what ought to happen), reasoned from the same material. Broader Theoretical and Practical Implications Theoretically, this chapter has an enormous influence on subsequent thought. It founds the Marxian tradition's narrative of history — the rise of capitalism from the expropriation of small producers, and the coming transition to socialism via the same dialectical logic. It also provides the framework for the later theory of imperialism (through the world-market and international-character passages) and for the theory of the falling rate of profit (which will appear in Volume Three). Its dialectical mode — negation of the negation, contradiction, bursting asunder — became the philosophical signature of Marxian socialism against its critics. Practically and politically, the chapter became — in the hands of Marx's followers, and through the citations to the Manifesto embedded in its footnotes — the canonical statement of revolutionary inevitability. The phrase \"the expropriators are expropriated\" would appear in political programs and constitutions across the twentieth century. The chapter has often been read as the philosophical foundation for nationalization and socialization of the means of production. Philosophically, the chapter raises the enduring question of the relationship between historical necessity and political agency. The reader should not resolve this question too quickly. Marx's assertion of \"the inexorability of a natural process\" is at once an empirical diagnosis and a philosophical claim; it is defended not by future events but by the internal logic he has assembled. The tension between mechanism and agency — the process is inevitable, yet it passes through the working class's revolt — remains one of the most productive and contested points in Marxian theory. The chapter thus performs a double service. On the one hand, it concludes the historical narrative of Capital Volume One by showing what primitive accumulation means and where it points. On the other, it opens the entire subsequent question of political economy's relation to politics: does the analysis of capital's laws determine the future, or does it merely locate the terrain on which political struggle must be waged? Marx's own answer, compressed into the final paragraphs of this extraordinary chapter, is that both are true — the law is natural, and the class is the vehicle of the law's completion. Whether these two propositions can ultimately be reconciled is a question the chapter poses but does not close. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 31 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Chapter 33"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Chapter 33", "title": "Chapter 33", "subtitle": "The Modem Theory of Colonization", "path": "Volume 1/Chapter 33/Chapter 33  The Modem Theory of Colonization_explanation.html", "word_count": 4967, "read_time": "25 min read", "tags": ["Primitive Accumulation & Enclosures", "Wages & Value of Labour-Power"], "keywords": ["Wakefield's Modern Theory of Colonization", "Antagonism between Capitalist Property and Independent Labour", "Artificial Pricing of Land to Create Wage-Labourers", "Colonial Discovery of Capital's True Relation", "Capitalist Property Based on Others' Labour"], "text": "Chapter 33 The Modem Theory Of Colonization - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 4,973 words ⏱️ ~25 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS This chapter's central problem is a paradox of visibility. In Western Europe, the homeland of political economy, the process of primitive accumulation is historically accomplished — the separation of the producer from the means of production is complete, and the resulting social order is so thoroughly naturalized that the economist can drape a pre-capitalist vocabulary of \"law\" and \"property\" over it without apparent contradiction. In the colonies, by contrast, this accomplished fact does not yet exist: the producer still owns his own conditions of labour and employs them to enrich himself rather than a capitalist. The contradiction between these two diametrically opposed economic systems therefore becomes practical , manifesting itself as an open struggle — and it is precisely this colonial openness that allows Marx to see what the mother country conceals. Marx's intellectual objective is therefore not ethnographic. He states explicitly at the close that \"we are not concerned here with the condition of the colonies\"; the colonies matter only as an experimental terrain in which the presuppositions of capital become legible. The chapter's central thesis is that the fundamental condition of capitalist private property — and thus of the capitalist mode of production and accumulation as a whole — is \"the annihilation of that private property which rests on the labour of the individual himself; in other words, the expropriation of the worker.\" The chapter's vehicle is Edward Gibbon Wakefield, whom Marx credits with the \"great merit\" of having discovered in the colonies the truth about capitalist relations in the mother country. Wakefield's \"systematic colonization\" — the artificial pricing of virgin land plus a government emigration fund — is treated as a confessed, half-honest plan to manufacture wage-labourers where they do not spontaneously appear. Wakefield thus serves as a hostile witness whose testimony dismantles the apologetics of his own class. The intellectual payoff is a demolition of the fiction of the \"free contract\" between capitalist and worker, exposed as a relation of absolute dependence that must, in the colonies, be created by force and fraud. Within the broader architecture of Capital , this is the dénouement. After eight chapters tracing the bloody genesis of the capital relation, Marx ends not with England but with a mirror held up in the New World — and thereby returns the analysis to its foundation: capital is not a thing but a social relation, and that relation must be continuously made . 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Colonial Optic: Two Kinds of Private Property and the Truth-Telling Function of the Periphery Marx opens with a methodological indictment: political economy \"confuses, on principle, two different kinds of private property.\" The first rests on the labour of the producer himself; the second rests on the exploitation of the labour of others. The confusion is not accidental but structural — the discipline \"forgets\" that the second is not merely different from the first but its direct antithesis , and that it \"grows on the former's tomb and nowhere else.\" This metaphorical formulation is doing precise analytical work: exploitation-based property is not an independent species of property that could have arisen on its own; it is parasitical, requiring the prior destruction of producer-property as its soil. The reason this confusion can be sustained in Europe is that the destruction is already complete. Capitalist relations have either directly subordinated the whole of national production or, where economic development lags, exercise indirect control over decaying pre-capitalist strata that survive beside it. The political economist then applies \"the notions of law and of property inherited from a pre-capitalist world\" to this ready-made capitalist world — and the more loudly the facts cry out against his ideology, the more \"anxious zeal\" and \"unction\" he brings to the task. Marx is diagnosing a specific apologetic mechanism: the ideological vocabulary is anachronistic, and its fervour is proportionate to its falsity. It is otherwise in the colonies. There the capitalist \"constantly comes up against the obstacle presented by the producer\" who owns his own conditions of labour. The contradiction becomes a struggle , and where the capitalist has the power of the mother country behind him, he \"tries to use force to clear out of the way\" the modes of production resting on personal labour. Marx notes that the same interest which in the mother country compels the economist to declare the capitalist mode of production \"theoretically its own opposite\" now drives him, in the colonies, \"to make a clean breast of it.\" This is the chapter's crucial rhetorical pivot: the colonies produce confession . The apologist's armour, \"piece by piece, like rotten touchwood,\" crumbles off. What he confesses is that the development of social productivity — co-operation, division of labour, large-scale machinery — is \"impossible without the expropriation of the workers\"; and that in the interest of the \"so-called wealth of the nation,\" he seeks \"artificial means to ensure the poverty of the people.\" A footnote clarifies the colonial definition: Marx means true colonies — virgin soil colonized by free immigrants — with the United States still, economically speaking, a colony of Europe; old plantation societies where abolition revolutionized earlier relations also qualify. Wakefield's Discovery: Capital as a Social Relation, Not a Thing Marx introduces E. G. Wakefield with a precise compliment: his merit lies not in discovering something new about the colonies but in discovering, in the colonies, the truth about capitalist relations in the mother country. The analogy is to protectionism, which originally aimed at manufacturing capitalists artificially at home; Wakefield's theory of colonization, which England for a time attempted to enforce by Act of Parliament, aims at manufacturing wage-labourers in the colonies. This is what he calls \"systematic colonization.\" A footnote traces the insight's genealogy: Wakefield's few genuine perceptions were \"fully anticipated\" by Mirabeau père, the Physiocrat, in L'Ami des hommes (1756), and even earlier by English economists — Marx is careful to deny Wakefield originality of concept while granting him originality of exposure . Wakefield's first discovery is the chapter's theoretical keystone: in the colonies, property in money, means of subsistence, machines and other means of production does not as yet stamp a man as a capitalist if the essential complement is missing — \"the wage-labourer, the other man, who is compelled to sell himself of his own free will.\" From this follows the definition: capital is not a thing, but a social relation between persons which is mediated through things. Marx enforces this with an extended footnote quoting his own 1849 essay Wage Labour and Capital : \"A negro is a negro. In certain relations he becomes a slave. A mule is a machine for spinning cotton. Only in certain relations does it become capital. Outside these circumstances, it is no more capital than gold is intrinsically money, or sugar is the price of sugar... Capital is a social relation of production. It is a historical relation of production.\" The \"formula\" of the chapter is thus this relational definition; it is the analytical lens through which every subsequent fact is read. The empirical illustration is the celebrated reductio of Mr Peel. Peel transported to the Swan River district of Western Australia £50,000 in means of subsistence and production, plus — with foresight — 3,000 persons of the working class, men, women and children. On arrival, \"Mr Peel was left without a servant to make his bed or fetch him water from the river.\" Marx's mock-elegy is exact: \"Unhappy Mr Peel, who provided for everything except the export of English relations of production to Swan River!\" The point is not that Peel was foolish but that capital cannot be exported as a thing; the wage-relation must be exported as a set of enforceable social constraints. Remove the constraints, and £50,000 of \"capital\" reverts to a heap of useful objects and a crowd of independent settlers. Two Preliminary Admonitions: The Category Error and the Feudal Jurist Analogy Before expounding Wakefield's further discoveries, Marx makes two preliminary remarks. First, we know that means of production and subsistence, while they remain the property of the immediate producer, are not capital ; they become capital only under circumstances in which they serve simultaneously as means of exploitation of and domination over the worker. But this \"capitalist soul\" is so intimately wedded, in the political economist's mind, to their material substance, that he \"christens them capital under all circumstances, even where they are its exact opposite.\" This is fetishism in its crudest form — the attribution of a social property to a physical thing. Second, and consequently, Wakefield describes the splitting-up of the means of production into the individual property of many mutually independent and self-employed workers as \"equal division of capital.\" Marx's simile: the political economist is like the feudal jurist who \"used to attach the labels supplied by feudal law even to relationships which were purely monetary.\" The error is one of mislabeling, but its effects are material — it converts a denial of the capital relation into an appearance of its universal presence. The Social Contract Fiction and the Slave Alternative Wakefield's own reasoning is then quoted: if all members of society possess equal portions of capital, \"no man would have a motive for accumulating more capital than he could use with his own hands. This is to some extent the case in new American settlements, where a passion for owning land prevents the existence of a class of labourers for hire.\" Marx draws the decisive inference: so long as the worker can accumulate for himself — which he can do so long as he retains possession of his means of production — capitalist accumulation and the capitalist mode of production are impossible . The class of wage-labourers essential to them is simply lacking. This yields the analytical question to which the rest of the chapter is an answer: how, in old Europe, was the expropriation of the worker from his conditions of labour accomplished? Wakefield's answer is a \"social contract of a quite original kind\": mankind adopted a \"simple contrivance for promoting the accumulation of capital\" — \"they have divided themselves into owners of capital and owners of labour... This division was the result of concert and combination.\" Marx's paraphrase, dripping with irony, is that \"the mass of mankind expropriated itself in honour of the 'accumulation of capital.'\" The sarcasm exposes the absurdity of a voluntarist account of expropriation. And the reductio is immediate: if this instinct of self-denying fanaticism existed, it would \"run riot in the colonies,\" where the conditions exist for turning the social contract from dream into reality. Yet it does not. Why else would \"systematic colonization\" be required to replace spontaneous, unregulated colonization? Wakefield's own data answer him: in the Northern States of the American Union, not one-tenth of the people would fall under the description of hired labourers, while in England \"the labouring class compose the bulk of the people.\" Indeed, the drive to self-expropriation is so feeble that slavery , by Wakefield's own admission, is \"the sole natural basis of colonial wealth\"; his systematic colonization is \"a mere makeshift, resulting from the fact that he has free men, not slaves, to deal with.\" Wakefield's Swan River example confirms it: the first Spanish settlers of Santo Domingo did not import labourers from Spain, and without labourers \"their capital must have perished\"; so it did in the last English-founded colony, where masses of seed, implements and cattle perished \"for want of labourers to use it,\" and no settler preserved much more capital than he could work with his own hands. The Free Colony's Secret: Public Land as Prosperity and as Cancer Marx now consolidates the structural analysis. Expropriation of the mass of the people from the soil is the basis of the capitalist mode of production; the essence of a free colony is the opposite — \"the bulk of the soil is still public property,\" so that every settler can convert part of it into private property and individual means of production without preventing later settlers from doing the same. This is, in one phrase, \"the secret both of the prosperity of the colonies and of their cancerous affliction — their resistance to the establishment of capital.\" Prosperity and pathology are the same fact viewed from different sides. A footnote (footnote 10, quoting Wakefield) sharpens the point: \"Land, to be an element of colonization, must not only be waste, but it must be public property, liable to be converted into private property.\" Wakefield's own formulation of the resulting scarcity is quoted: \"Where land is very cheap and all men are free, where every one who so pleases can easily obtain a piece of land for himself, not only is labour very dear, as respects the labourer's share of the produce, but the difficulty is to obtain combined labour at any price.\" The Missing Home Market and the Vanishing Wage-Labourer Marx develops the consequences in two directions. First, the market problem: in the colonies the separation of the worker from the conditions of labour and from the soil \"does not yet exist, or only sporadically, or on too limited a scale.\" Hence agriculture is not separated from industry, and the domestic industries of the countryside have not been destroyed. Whence, then, \"the home market for capital\"? Wakefield's own enumeration of the American settler is adduced as evidence: no part of the population is exclusively agricultural except slaves and their employers; free Americans who cultivate the soil follow many other occupations, make their own furniture and tools, frequently build their own houses, and carry to market the produce of their own industry — \"They are spinners and weavers, they make soap and candles, as well as, in many cases, shoes and clothes for their own use. In America the cultivation of land is often the secondary pursuit of a blacksmith, a miller or a shopkeeper.\" Marx's question is lethal: \"Where, among such curious characters, is the 'field of abstinence' for the capitalists?\" — the phrase \"abstinence\" being the vulgar economists' term for the capitalist's supposed sacrifice in not consuming his capital. Second, the labour-market problem, which Marx unfolds with a doctrinal statement of the normal capitalist mechanism. The \"great beauty\" of capitalist production is that it not only constantly reproduces the wage-labourer as wage-labourer but always produces a relative surplus population in proportion to the accumulation of capital. This surplus keeps the law of supply and demand in relation to labour \"on the right lines,\" confines the oscillation of wages within limits satisfactory to capitalist exploitation, and secures the indispensable social dependence of the worker on the capitalist. In the mother country, the economist's \"smug deceitfulness\" can convert this relation of absolute dependence into a \"free contract between buyer and seller,\" between equally independent owners of commodities — capital on one side, labour on the other. In the colonies this illusion \"is torn aside.\" Population increases faster because many workers enter colonial society as ready-made adults, and yet the labour-market is \"always understocked.\" The law of supply and demand for labour \"collapses completely.\" The old world throws in capital \"thirsting after exploitation and 'abstinence'\"; the regular reproduction of the wage-labourer meets \"insuperable\" obstacles. And the reproduction of redundant wage-labourers? \"Today's wage-labourer is tomorrow's independent peasant or artisan, working for himself. He vanishes from the labour-market — but not into the workhouse.\" This constant transformation of wage-labourers into independent producers reacts \"very adversely\" on the labour market: the degree of exploitation remains \"indecently low,\" and the worker \"loses, along with the relation of dependence, the feeling of dependence\" on the abstemious capitalist — \"Hence all the inconveniences depicted so honestly, so eloquently and so movingly by our friend E. G. Wakefield.\" Wakefield's Lament: The Understocked Market and the Imported Competitor Marx now lets Wakefield speak, and the quotations are chosen for their involuntary candour. The supply of labour \"is always, not only small, but uncertain.\" \"Though the produce divided between the capitalist and the labourer be large, the labourer takes so great a share that he soon becomes a capitalist... Few, even of those whose lives are unusually long, can accumulate great masses of wealth.\" The workers \"most emphatically refuse to let the capitalist abstain from paying for the greater part of their labour\" — an ironic reversal of the abstinence doctrine, since it is the worker who refuses abstinence to the capitalist. Worse: importing wage-labourers from Europe with the capitalist's own capital only produces competitors, since the imported workers soon \"cease... to be labourers for hire; they... become independent landowners, if not competitors with their former masters in the labour-market.\" Marx's commentary is satirically accelerated: \"Horror of horrors! The excellent capitalist has imported bodily from Europe, with his own good money, his own competitors! The end of the world has come!\" Wakefield's lament over the absence of relations and feelings of dependence is then crowned by his disciple Merivale, who observes that on account of high wages there is in the colonies an urgent desire for cheaper and more subservient workers, \"for a class of people to whom the capitalist may dictate his terms, instead of having his terms dictated by them\" — and who states the general law baldly: \"In the old civilized countries the worker, although free, is by a law of nature dependent on the capitalist; in colonies this dependence must be created by artificial means.\" This single sentence is the chapter's most damning exhibit: the \"law of nature\" is exposed as a historical product that elsewhere requires manufacture. Merivale, Molinari, and the Scandal of Inverted Exploitation A long critical footnote introduces the vulgar free-trader Molinari as a still more revealing witness. In colonies where slavery was abolished without compulsory labour being replaced by an equivalent quantity of free labour, \"the opposite of what happens every day before our eyes\" occurred: \"Simple workers have been seen to exploit in their turn the industrial entrepreneurs, demanding from them wages which bear absolutely no relation to the legitimate share in the product which they ought to receive.\" Planters were ruined or closed their businesses; and Molinari concludes that \"it is doubtless better that these accumulations of capital should be destroyed than that generations of men should perish\" — to which Marx interjects \"(how generous of M. Molinari)\" — \"but would it not be better if both survived?\" Marx's rejoinder is a masterpiece of immanent critique. What becomes, he asks, of the Ten Commandments, of Moses and the Prophets, of the law of supply and demand, \"if in Europe the 'entrepreneur' can cut down the worker's 'legitimate share' and in the West Indies the workers can cut down the entrepreneur's?\" And what is this \"legitimate share\" which, \"according to your own admission, the capitalist in Europe daily neglects to pay\"? The revelation is that Molinari's free-trade orthodoxy licenses exploitation in one direction and demands police intervention when the vector reverses: where the workers are so \"simple\" as to exploit the capitalist, Molinari \"feels a powerful itch to use police methods to set on the right road that law of supply and demand which works automatically everywhere else.\" The \"law\" is thus revealed as a normative preference masquerading as a natural mechanism. The \"Barbarizing Tendency of Dispersion\" and the Problem of Long-Term Capital Wakefield's diagnosis of the colonial condition is that dispersion of producers and of national wealth has a \"barbarizing tendency.\" The fragmentation of the means of production among innumerable self-employed owners \"annihilates, along with the centralization of capital, all the foundations of combined labour.\" Every lengthy undertaking extending over several years and demanding fixed capital is prevented. In Europe capital \"does not hesitate for a moment,\" because the working class forms its living appendage, \"always present in excess, always at its disposal\" — but not in the colonies. Wakefield's anecdote is then recounted: speaking with capitalists of Canada and the state of New York, where the wave of immigration often deposits a sediment of \"redundant\" workers, one of his characters explains that their capital \"was ready for many operations which require a considerable period of time for their completion; but we could not begin such operations with labour which, we knew, would soon leave us.\" Even a temporary engagement would have been acceptable \"provided we had been sure of a fresh supply whenever we might need it.\" This is the core capitalist requirement — not merely labour, but reproducible, replaceable labour. Marx then notes a dialectical reversal in Wakefield's own text: after contrasting English capitalist agriculture and its \"combined\" labour with scattered American cultivation, Wakefield \"unwittingly shows us the obverse of the medal.\" He depicts the mass of the American people as \"well-to-do, independent, enterprising and comparatively cultured,\" whereas \"the English agricultural labourer is a miserable wretch, a pauper.\" Wakefield's own words: \"In what country, except North America and some new colonies, do the wages of free labour employed in agriculture much exceed a bare subsistence for the labourer?... Undoubtedly, farm-horses in England, being a valuable property, are better fed than English peasants.\" Marx's gloss — that the wealth of the nation is once again, \"by its very nature, identical with the misery of the people\" — ties the colonial evidence back to the general law of capitalist accumulation. The Cure: Artificial Land Price and the \"Sufficient Price\" as Ransom The policy prescription follows. How can the anti-capitalist cancer be healed? To convert all land from public into private property \"at one blow\" would destroy the root of the evil — but also the colony. The trick \"to kill two birds with one stone\" is for the government to set an artificial price on virgin soil, \"a price independent of the law of supply and demand,\" compelling the immigrant to work a long time for wages before he can buy land and become an independent farmer. The fund arising from land sales — \"this fund of money extorted from the wages of labour by a violation of the sacred law of supply and demand\" — is then to be applied by government \"in proportion to its growth, to the importation of paupers from Europe into the colonies, so as to keep the wage-labour market full for the capitalists.\" Under these circumstances, \"everything will be for the best in the best of all possible worlds\" — a Panglossian quotation that frames the scheme as theodicy. Wakefield's own triumphant formulation is then quoted: the supply of labour \"must be constant and regular, because, first, as no labourer would be able to procure land until he had worked for money, all immigrant labourers, working for a time for wages and in combination, would produce capital for the employment of more labourers; secondly, because every labourer who left off working for wages and became a landowner, would, by purchasing land, provide a fund for bringing fresh labour to the colony.\" The land price must be \"sufficient\" — high enough \"to prevent the labourers from becoming independent landowners until others had followed to take their place.\" Marx's interpretive translation is the chapter's sharpest conceptual compression: this \"sufficient price for the land\" is \"nothing but a euphemistic circumlocution for the ransom which the worker must pay to the capitalist in return for permission to retire from the wage-labour market to the land.\" The transaction has two stages: first the worker must create for the capitalist the \"capital\" that enables him to exploit more workers; then, at his own expense, he must put a \"substitute\" in the labour-market, dispatched across the sea by the government, again at the worker's expense, for his old master. A footnote (the Colins extract) generalizes the principle from the other side: \"it is thanks to the individual appropriation of the soil that there exist men who only possess the strength of their arms... When you put a man in a vacuum, you rob him of the air. You do the same, when you take away the soil from him... for you are putting him in a space void of wealth, so as to leave him no way of living except according to your wishes.\" The Fiasco: Peel's Bank Act, the Diverted Emigration, and the American Aftermath Marx notes, \"extremely characteristic,\" that the English government for years practised this method of primitive accumulation prescribed by Wakefield expressly for the colonies. The resulting fiasco was \"as ignominious as the fate of Peel's Bank Act\" — explained in a footnote as Sir Robert Peel's Bank Act of 1844, suspended in November 1857 with the onset of that year's commercial crisis. The scheme failed on its own terms: the stream of emigration was \"simply diverted from the English colonies to the United States.\" Meanwhile, the advance of capitalist production in Europe, with increasing government pressure, rendered Wakefield's recipe superfluous. Marx then documents the closing of the American escape route in three movements: first, the enormous continuous flood of humanity driven onto American shores leaves \"a stationary sediment in the East of the United States,\" since European immigration throws men onto the eastern labour-market faster than westward migration can wash them away; second, the American Civil War brings a colossal national debt, a heavy tax burden, the creation of \"a finance aristocracy of the vilest type,\" and the granting of immense tracts of public land to speculative companies for railways and mines — in short, \"a very rapid centralization of capital.\" The republic has therefore \"ceased to be the promised land for emigrating workers,\" though wages and dependence have not yet reached the normal European level. Finally, Australia: the shameless squandering of uncultivated colonial land on aristocrats and capitalists by the English government — denounced even by Wakefield — combined with the gold-rush immigration and competition from imported English commodities affecting \"everyone down to the smallest artisan,\" produced \"an ample 'relative surplus population of workers,'\" so that almost every mail-boat brings news of a \"glut of the Australian labour-market,\" and prostitution flourishes \"as exuberantly as in the Haymarket in London.\" A footnote on the Land Law of Victoria (1862), by the Hon. C. G. Duffy, notes that the first objective of the new land act was to give \"increased facilities for the settlement of the people,\" but that the squandering already accomplished by the English government \"stands in the way\" — reform arrives too late to undo the primitive accumulation already effected. The Final Revelation The chapter closes by withdrawing its own ethnographic interest. \"However, we are not concerned here with the condition of the colonies.\" What interests Marx is \"the secret discovered in the New World by the political economy of the Old World, and loudly proclaimed by it\": that the capitalist mode of production and accumulation, and therefore capitalist private property as well, have for their fundamental condition the annihilation of that private property which rests on the labour of the individual himself — \"in other words, the expropriation of the worker.\" The circle is closed: the chapter began by distinguishing producer-property from exploitation-property and claiming that the latter grows \"on the former's tomb and nowhere else\"; it ends by establishing this as a universal law confirmed at the point where its operation is most visible. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Four fundamental principles emerge. First, capital is a social relation mediated through things, not a collection of things — the Peel anecdote is the most economical proof in the Marxist canon that means of production become capital only when accompanied by a class compelled to sell its labour-power. Property is therefore a bundle of enforceable social relations, not a physical inventory. Second, the \"free contract\" of the labour market is the ideological mask of a manufactured dependence. Marx's decisive analytic move is to show that the relation the mother-country economist calls a \"law of nature\" must, in the colonies, be \"created by artificial means,\" including state price-fixing, taxation through land sales, and government-sponsored emigration — all violations of the very supply-and-demand law invoked to naturalize it. The normal case and the colonial exception are revealed as the same case, differing only in the visibility of the coercion. Third, the colonies function as a control experiment in historical materialism. By stripping away the completed structures of European society, the colonial setting exposes the presupposition of capital: the prior expropriation of the producer. The periphery does not merely illustrate the metropole; it demonstrates it. This methodological device — understanding the developed form through its undeveloped instantiation — is one Marx uses elsewhere, but here it carries the burden of the entire argument's climax. Fourth, the \"sufficient price\" as ransom crystallizes the political economy of settler colonialism: the worker is made to finance the production of his own replacement and the expansion of the capital that exploits him. This is exploitation raised to a second power — the worker pays for the reproduction of the class relation that subordinates him. The broader implications are twofold. Historically, Marx's epilogue shows that the closure of the frontier is not incidental but integral to capitalist development: the American Civil War, railway land grants, the tax burden and finance aristocracy, the Australian land squandering, all transformed the escapes from wage-labour into further reservoirs of it. The \"promised land\" was closed not by accident but by accumulation. Philosophically, the chapter refutes any contractarian or voluntarist account of class society — the \"social contract\" of capital is a fiction whose real analogue is force, and whose measure is the ruin it leaves in the producer's soil. The essential takeaway, stated in the chapter's final clause, is that capitalist private property is the negation of the property that labour creates, and that it can exist only where that prior, laborious property has first been annihilated. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 32 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 1 • Appendix"}, {"volume": "Volume 1", "folder": "Appendix", "title": "Appendix", "subtitle": "Results of the Immediate Process of Production", "path": "Volume 1/Appendix/Appendix  Results of the Immediate Process of Production_explanation.html", "word_count": 12180, "read_time": "61 min read", "tags": ["Subsumption of Labour (Formal vs Real)", "Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["Immediate Process of Production (Results)", "Formal Subsumption vs Real Subsumption of Labour", "Productive vs Unproductive Labour in Capital", "Total Mystification of Capitalist Relations", "Direct Valorization in the Factory"], "text": "Appendix Results Of The Immediate Process Of Production - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 12,255 words ⏱️ ~61 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION: \"Results of the Immediate Process of Production\" 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The \"Results of the Immediate Process of Production\" (hereafter Resultate ) constitutes one of the most theoretically significant yet historically neglected texts in the Marxian corpus. Written between June 1863 and December 1866, this manuscript was originally conceived as Part Seven of Volume 1 of Capital , intended to serve a dual didactic function: first, as a comprehensive summary of the entire first volume's analysis of capitalist production, and second, as a bridge or transition to Volume 2's examination of the circulation process of capital. The text was first published in 1933 by Adoratsky in Volume II of Arkhiv Marksa i Engelsa , simultaneously in Russian and German, but only became an object of intense scholarly study when reprinted in Western European languages in the late 1960s. The chapter's fundamental thesis is that capitalist production must be understood not merely as the production of commodities or even as the production of surplus-value, but as the production and reproduction of the specifically capitalist relations of production themselves. This tripartite structure—commodities as products of capital, capitalist production as production of surplus-value, and capitalist production as reproduction of capitalist relations—constitutes Marx's most sophisticated attempt to synthesize the objective and subjective dimensions of the capitalist mode of production into a unified theoretical framework. The intellectual objective of the Resultate is nothing less than to demonstrate how the capitalist process of production, when properly understood in its totality, produces not only material wealth in the form of commodities but also reproduces and expands the very social relationship upon which it depends: the relationship between capital and wage-labour. This involves showing how what appears on the surface as a free and equal exchange between commodity owners (the capitalist and the worker meeting in the marketplace) is in fact the constant reproduction of a relationship of domination and exploitation, achieved through the mediation of apparently voluntary contractual arrangements. The chapter's central theoretical innovation lies in Marx's introduction of the distinction between the \"formal subsumption\" and the \"real subsumption\" of labour under capital. This distinction, which Mandel correctly identifies as \"the key aspect of the Resultate ,\" provides the analytical framework for understanding the historical development of capitalism from its earliest manifestations in simple manufacture to its mature form in large-scale industry. Formal subsumption characterizes the period when capital takes over pre-existing labour processes developed under earlier modes of production—handicraft, peasant agriculture, guild production—without fundamentally transforming their technical character. Real subsumption, by contrast, characterizes the modern factory system, where capital has revolutionized the production process itself through the application of machinery, science, and technology, creating a specifically capitalist mode of production that is qualitatively different from all preceding forms. This distinction enables Marx to resolve several theoretical puzzles that had troubled both classical political economy and earlier socialist critics of capitalism. First, it explains how surplus-value can be produced under conditions of formal equality and free exchange: the worker sells his labour-power at its value, receives a wage equivalent to the value of his means of subsistence, yet capital nevertheless extracts surplus labour because the value created by labour-power in use exceeds the value of labour-power as a commodity. Second, it explains the historical dynamics of capitalist development: why capitalism first emerges in certain branches of production and certain geographical locations, how it spreads from one sphere to another, and why it tends toward constant revolutionization of the forces of production. Third, it provides the basis for understanding the distinctive forms of mystification and fetishism that characterize capitalist society, showing how the social relations of production come to appear as properties of things—how capital appears as a productive force in its own right, how the social productivity of labour appears as the productivity of capital, and how the domination of capital over labour appears as the natural order of things. The Resultate also contains Marx's most extended discussion of the distinction between productive and unproductive labour under capitalism. Against the confusion of classical political economy, which oscillated between defining productive labour in terms of its material result (the production of tangible commodities) and in terms of its capacity to generate profit, Marx insists that productive labour under capitalism is precisely that labour which is directly exchanged for capital and which produces surplus-value. Labour that produces commodities but is exchanged for revenue rather than capital—the labour of the independent artisan who sells his products to consumers, or the labour of the servant who performs personal services—is unproductive in the specifically capitalist sense, regardless of its material usefulness. This distinction is crucial for understanding the dynamics of capital accumulation, since only the exchange of capital for productive labour can satisfy the conditions for the reconversion of surplus-value into capital. The chapter's treatment of the \"collective worker\" ( Gesamtarbeiter ) represents another significant theoretical advance. With the development of the specifically capitalist mode of production, Marx argues, the real lever of the overall labour process is increasingly not the individual worker but the \"socially combined labour-power\" of all those who participate in production. This includes not only manual labourers but also engineers, technologists, managers, and even scientists whose intellectual labour is incorporated into the production process. The concept of the collective worker thus provides the theoretical basis for understanding how the social productivity of labour—the productive power that arises from co-operation, division of labour, and the application of science—comes to appear as the productive power of capital, systematically mystifying the real source of wealth under capitalism. The Resultate also contains Marx's most explicit statement of the necessity for constant expansion of the capitalist market. Precisely because capitalist production is production through a growing mass of machinery, growing fixed capital, and a growing organic composition of capital, it is necessarily mass production of commodities on an constantly increasing scale, whose realization demands a constantly growing market. This insight, which Mandel notes has been \"so often contested by his critics and by some of his followers too,\" provides the theoretical foundation for understanding the inherent expansionary dynamic of capitalism and the systemic pressures that drive it toward globalization. Finally, the Resultate includes several \"Isolated Fragments\" that, while not properly part of the main text, contain significant insights into Marx's views on trade unions, emigration, the centralization of capital in different countries, and the historical process of expropriation. The fragment on trade unions is particularly important, as it presents Marx's most developed analysis of how combinations of workers can intervene in the labour market to prevent wages from falling below the value of labour-power, and how they can achieve increases in real wages by incorporating new needs into the \"moral-historical element\" of the value of labour-power. In terms of its place within the broader Marxian theoretical framework, the Resultate occupies a unique position. It is simultaneously a summary of Volume 1's analysis and a preview of themes developed more fully in Volume 2 (the circulation of capital) and Volume 3 (the unity of production and circulation, the formation of prices of production, and the distribution of surplus-value among different fractions of capital). It represents Marx's most sustained attempt to think through the relationship between the immediate process of production and the broader social relations that constitute the capitalist mode of production, and it contains some of his most sophisticated reflections on the nature of social determination and historical materialism. The Resultate thus stands as a crucial text for understanding Marx's mature theoretical project, providing insights that are not available, or not available in the same form, in the published version of Capital . It shows Marx thinking through the complexities of capitalist production at a level of abstraction that is both more concrete than the analysis of value in Volume 1 and more fundamental than the analysis of circulation in Volume 2. It is, in short, an indispensable text for anyone seeking to understand the full scope and depth of Marx's critique of political economy. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Architecture of the Resultate: Historical Context and Editorial Decisions Before engaging with the substantive arguments of the Resultate , it is essential to understand the text's peculiar history and the editorial decisions that shaped its present form. As Mandel explains in his introduction, the Resultate was written after the completion of the massive 1861-3 manuscript (the twenty-three notebooks that would eventually become the Theories of Surplus-Value and portions of Capital ) but before Marx's final revision of Volume 1 for publication. The original plan for Capital had included a Part Seven entitled \"Result of the Production Process,\" which was to follow the analysis of accumulation. However, when Marx introduced a new Part Six on wages (a topic he had originally intended to treat in a separate volume on wage-labour), the original Part Seven was displaced and eventually discarded entirely from the published version. Mandel notes that it is \"impossible to give a definitive answer\" as to why Marx abandoned the Resultate , but suggests that the reason may have lain in Marx's \"wish to present Capital as a 'dialectically articulated artistic whole.'\" The Resultate had a \"double didactic function\"—as a summary of Volume 1 and as a bridge between Volumes 1 and 2—that may have seemed out of place in the final structure of the work. Whatever the reason, the text remained unpublished during Marx's lifetime and was only recovered and published in the twentieth century. This historical context is crucial for understanding the Resultate 's unique character. Unlike the published version of Capital , which is organized around the logical development of categories (commodity, money, capital, surplus-value, accumulation), the Resultate is organized around a thematic concern: the way in which the capitalist process of production produces and reproduces the specifically capitalist relations of production. This thematic organization allows Marx to develop arguments and make connections that are less prominent, or even absent, in the published version. The editorial decision to retain the original order of the three sections—with \"Commodities as the Product of Capital\" first, rather than last as Marx suggested in his later note—is also significant. The French edition, which Mandel follows, retains this order \"partly because Marx never undertook the 'final revision' which would justify the change, and partly because the subject-matter as it stands unfolds more naturally and logically in this way.\" This decision shapes the reader's experience of the text, beginning with the most concrete and immediate result of capitalist production (the commodity) and then moving to the underlying relations that produce it. Section I: Commodities as the Product of Capital Marx begins the Resultate with an analysis of the commodity as the product of capital, a starting point that might seem to contradict the opening of Capital itself, where the commodity appears as the \"elementary form of bourgeois wealth\" and the \"prerequisite for the emergence of capital.\" Marx immediately acknowledges this apparent circularity: \"As the elementary form of bourgeois wealth, the commodity was our point of departure, the prerequisite for the emergence of capital. On the other hand, commodities appear now as the product of capital.\" This circularity, Marx insists, is not a logical error but a reflection of the actual historical development of capital. \"Capital is predicated on the exchange of commodities, trade in commodities, but it may be formed at various stages of production, common to all of which is the fact that capitalist production does not yet exist, or only exists sporadically.\" At the same time, \"a highly developed commodity exchange and the form of the commodity as the universally necessary social form of the product can only emerge as the consequence of the capitalist mode of production.\" The dialectical relationship between commodity and capital is thus both historical and logical. Historically, commodity exchange precedes the emergence of capital; capital can only arise on the foundation of a certain level of development of trade and commerce. Logically, however, once capitalist production has become established, the commodity becomes the universal form of the product, and all the conditions of production themselves appear as commodities that leave circulation and enter production. Marx identifies three crucial points that emerge from this analysis: First, \"Capitalist production is the first to make the commodity into the general form of all produce.\" Before capitalism, a large part of what was produced did not take the form of commodities; it was produced for direct consumption by the producer or for tribute paid in kind to a lord or state. Only with capitalism does the commodity become the universal elementary form of wealth. Second, \"The production of commodities leads inexorably to capitalist production, once the worker has ceased to be a part of the conditions of production (as in slavery, serfdom), or once primitive common ownership has ceased to be the basis of society (India). In short, from the moment when labour-power in general becomes a commodity.\" This is a crucial claim about the historical logic of commodity production. Once labour-power itself becomes a commodity—once workers must sell their capacity to work in order to survive—the conditions are established for the emergence of capitalist production. Third, \"Capitalist production destroys the basis of commodity production in so far as the latter involves independent individual production and the exchange of commodities between owners or the exchange of equivalents. The formal exchange of capital and labour-power becomes general.\" This is a dialectical claim of considerable importance. Commodity production, in its \"pure\" form, involves the exchange of equivalents between independent producers who own their own means of production. But capitalist production, while universalizing the commodity form, simultaneously destroys the material basis of this independence: the worker no longer owns the means of production and therefore cannot engage in the exchange of equivalents with other commodity owners. The exchange of capital and labour-power, which appears as an exchange of equivalents (wages for labour), is in fact the mechanism through which the worker's independence is destroyed and the capitalist's control is established. Having established these general points, Marx proceeds to a detailed analysis of how the commodity, as the product of capital, differs from the commodity regarded as an autonomous article. This analysis involves several distinct but interrelated arguments. The first difference concerns the nature of the labour objectified in the commodity. In the commodity regarded by itself, it \"remains quite undecided (and is in fact a matter of indifference) from whom this objectified labour is derived.\" In the commodity as the product of capital, however, we can distinguish between \"paid and unpaid labour.\" Marx is careful to note that this is \"not strictly true since the labour itself is not bought or sold directly.\" What is bought and sold is labour-power, the capacity to work. But \"the commodity contains a specific overall quantity of objectified labour. A portion of this objectified labour (aside from constant capital for which an equivalent has been paid) is exchanged for the equivalent of the worker's wages; another portion is appropriated by the capitalist without any equivalent being paid.\" It is \"a convenient abbreviation\" to describe the one as paid and the other as unpaid labour. The second difference concerns the relationship between the individual commodity and the total product of capital. The individual commodity \"does not only appear materially as a part of the total produce of capital, but as an aliquot part of the total produced by it.\" We are \"no longer concerned with the individual autonomous commodity, the single product. The result of the process is not individual goods, but a mass of commodities in which the value of the capital invested together with the surplus-value—i.e. the surplus labour appropriated—has reproduced itself, and each one of which is the incarnation of both the value of the capital and the surplus-value it has produced.\" This has important implications for the determination of price. The labour expended on each commodity \"can no longer be calculated—except as an average, i.e. an ideal estimate.\" The calculation of the value of the individual commodity begins with \"that portion of the constant capital which only enters into the value of the total product in so far as it is used up,\" continues with \"the conditions of production that are consumed communally,\" and ends with \"the direct social contribution of many co-operating individuals whose labour is averaged out.\" When determining the price of an individual article, it appears \"as a merely ideal fraction of the total product in which the capital reproduces itself.\" Marx illustrates this with a detailed numerical example. Let us assume that the total product equals 1,200 ells of linen. The capital invested equals £100, of which £80 is constant capital and £20 variable. The rate of surplus-value is 100 per cent, so that the worker labours for half the working day for himself and the other half gratis for the capitalist. The surplus-value produced equals £20, and the total value of the 1,200 ells equals £120, of which £80 represents the constant capital invested and £40 comes from the additional living labour. How are we to determine the value of the individual commodity, in this case the ell of linen? \"Obviously, by dividing the total price of the aggregate product by the number of units as divided into aliquot parts in accordance with a given measure.\" The total price is divided by the number of measured units in which the use-value is expressed: £120 / 1,200 = 2 shillings per ell of linen. Marx then explores how this price changes with variations in productivity. If the linen weaver's productivity is increased fourfold, the constant capital set in motion will increase proportionally (from £80 to £320 worth of flax, etc.), and the number of ells produced will also increase proportionally (from 1,200 to 4,800). The additional weavers' labour, however, would still be expressed as £40, since its quantity remains unaltered. Thus the total price of the 4,800 ells is now £360, and the price of each ell is 1 shilling 6 pence. The price of each ell has fallen by one-quarter because \"the constant capital in each ell had absorbed one-quarter less additional living labour in the process of converting that labour into linen.\" Marx then presents an alternative example where the total money invested remains the same while the productivity of labour produces very different amounts of the same use-value, for example wheat, merely as the result of varying natural conditions. He assumes that £7 represents the amount of work spent on an acre of land. Of this sum £4 is additional labour and £3 is labour already objectified in constant capital. Of this £4, let £2 be employed for wages and £2 remains as surplus labour, at the rate of 100 per cent. The crop, however, varies with the seasons: Total in quarters One quarter can be sold at 5 28s. 4½ about 31s. 4 35s. 3½ 40s. 3 46s. 8d. 2½ 56s. 2 70s. The value or price of the aggregate product yielded by the capital of £5 invested in each acre remains £7, since the invested quantities of objectified and additional living labour remain constant. However, this same labour results in very different quantities of wheat, and the individual quarter of wheat, therefore, has very different prices. Marx draws an important conclusion from this analysis: \"This variation in the prices of the individual commodities produced with the same capital has absolutely no effect on the rate of surplus-value, or the proportion of surplus-value to variable capital, or the way in which the working day is divided into paid and unpaid labour.\" The total value in which the additional labour is expressed remains unchanged because the same amount of living labour is added to the constant capital. The relation of surplus-value to wages, or of paid to unpaid labour, remains the same, irrespective of whether the ell costs 2s. or, with the growing productivity of labour, 1½s. This leads Marx to formulate two general laws: (1) \"Where commodity prices vary the rate and quantity of surplus-value can remain constant.\" (2) \"Where commodity prices are constant the rate and quantity of surplus-value can vary.\" These laws apply \"generally to all commodities including those that do not enter directly or indirectly into the reproduction of labour-power and whose price therefore, whether high or low, is irrelevant to the determination of the value of labour-power itself.\" Marx illustrates the second law with a detailed example. Let us assume that the working day remains the same, i.e. 10 hours, but that the necessary labour is reduced from 5 hours to 4 because of increased productivity not in the constant capital employed in weaving, or in the weaving itself, but in other branches of industry whose products form part of the working wage. This means that the workers now work 6 hours for the capitalist, instead of 5, and 4 for themselves instead of 5. The ratio of surplus labour to necessary had been 5:5, i.e. 100 per cent. It is now 6:4, i.e. 150 per cent. Now as previously, twenty men are employed for 10 hours, which gives 200 hours. In both cases they set the same constant capital of £80 in motion. The value of the total product remains £120, the number of ells still stands at 1,200, and the price of each ell is still 2s. But whereas formerly it took 5 hours per day, i.e. £20 per week to buy his means of subsistence, it now takes only 4 hours, = £16 per week, to buy the same amount. The payment of the twenty who now perform only 16 hours of necessary labour is now £16 as against £20 previously. The variable capital has fallen from £20 to £16, but still sets the same amount of absolute labour in motion. c v s Value of total product Rate of surplus-value Sum of surplus-value Ells Price per ell Amount of labour [per ell] Surplus labour per ell Rate of surplus labour III £80 £16 £24 £120 150% £24 1200 2s. 8d. 4.8d. Marx notes that \"the sum of surplus-value is only £24 instead of £28 as in Table II. But if in III the same variable capital £20 had been laid out, the total amount of labour employed would have risen, since it remains the same with a variable capital of £16.\" If £16 yields £40, then £20 would yield £50, of which £30 would be surplus-value. This gives us: c v s Value of total product Rate of surplus-value Sum of surplus-value Ells Price per ell Amount of labour [per ell] Surplus labour per ell Rate of surplus labour IIIa £100 £20 £30 £150 150% £30 1500 2s. 8d. 5.76d. Marx draws an important general conclusion: \"as a result of a fall in wages (the consequence here of increased productivity) less variable capital is required to put the same amount of labour to work, i.e. to put the same amount of labour to work at greater advantage to capital (since the paid portion of the same amount falls in relation to the unpaid portion). Therefore, the capitalist who continues to lay out the same amount of variable capital will gain doubly. For he is able to not only gain a higher rate of surplus-value for the same total amount, but he is able to exploit a larger quantity of labour at this higher rate, although his variable capital has not increased in magnitude.\" The Dialectic of Price and Value in the Product of Capital Marx then turns to a crucial theoretical point: the distinction between the commodity as an autonomous article and the commodity as a depository of capital. He argues that \"even if the commodity is sold at its price, the value of the capital invested in its production may not be realized, and the surplus-value created by that capital even less so.\" This is because the individual commodity, as the product of capital, is not just a thing with a certain value but \"an aliquot part of the total product of that capital.\" Marx illustrates this with a continuation of his previous example. A capital of £100 reproduced itself in the form of 1,200 ells of linen at a price of £120. The £80 constant capital is embodied in 800 ells, or ⅔ of the aggregate product; £20 variable capital or wages amount to 200 ells, or ⅙ of the total; and £20 surplus-value is likewise the equivalent of 200 ells or ⅙. If we now suppose that not one ell, but let us say 800 ells were sold at the right price, i.e. £80, and the other two portions were unsaleable, then only ⅔ of the original capital value of £100 would have been reproduced. As the depository of the total capital, the 800 ells would have been sold at less than their value, at ⅔ of their value, to be precise, since the value of the whole product = 120 and 80 is but ⅔ of that, the missing 40 being the remaining third. Marx emphasizes that \"what is at issue here is not just that the commodity's sale price should reflect its value, as in the case of the commodity conceived as an autonomous thing, but that, as a depository of the capital invested in it, its sale price should also reflect the fact that it is an aliquot part of the total product of that capital.\" If only 800 ells are sold out of a total product of 1,200 = £120, then these 800 ells do not represent ⅔ of the total value, but the total value itself, i.e. they represent the value of £120 and not £80, and the individual commodity does not equal £80/800 = 8/80 = 4/40 = 2/20 = 2s., but £120/800 = 12/80 = 3/20 = 3s. Thus as an individual product it would have been sold 50 per cent too dear, if it had been sold at 3s. instead of 2s. This analysis has important implications for understanding the necessity of market expansion under capitalism. Marx notes: \"It follows from this that, with the development of capitalist production and the resultant reduction in prices, there must be an increase in the quantity of goods, in the number of articles that must be sold. That is to say, a constant expansion of the market becomes a necessity for capitalist production.\" This point, which Mandel identifies as one of the most important in the Resultate , will be developed further in Volume 2 and Volume 3. The Proudhonian Confusion and Its Resolution Marx then engages in an extended polemic against Proudhon, using the analysis developed above to resolve what Proudhon had presented as an insoluble economic paradox. Proudhon had argued: \"It is impossible that the interest on capital, adding itself in commerce to the worker's wages to make up the price of commodities, should enable the worker to buy back what he has himself produced. To live by working is a principle which, under the regimen of interest, entails a contradiction.\" Marx acknowledges that \"this is quite right\" as far as appearances go. If the worker receives only his wages, and if the price of commodities includes both wages and surplus-value, then it seems impossible for the worker to buy back the full product of his labour. But Marx argues that Proudhon's error lies in considering the commodity in isolation rather than as the product of capital. \"If, instead of considering the commodity in isolation, he were to view it as the product of capital, he would discover that the week's product breaks down into one part whose price = the weekly wage, = the variable capital laid out during the week and containing no surplus-value, etc., and another part whose price consists entirely of surplus-value. And even though the price of the commodity includes all these elements, it is in fact only the first part that the worker buys back.\" Marx's solution to Proudhon's paradox is thus that the working class, taken as a whole, buys back only that portion of the total product whose value is equivalent to the wages it has received. The rest of the product—the portion that represents surplus-value—is appropriated by the capitalist class and consumed by them or reinvested. The apparent contradiction arises only when we consider the individual commodity in isolation rather than as an aliquot part of the total product of capital. Marx notes that Proudhon's \"apparently profound and insoluble economic paradoxes\" consist in the fact that \"he regards the confusion wrought by economic phenomena in his own mind as the laws governing those phenomena.\" The vulgar economists who criticized Proudhon—Marx mentions Forcade—fared no better, since they merely pointed out that the problem was even more intractable than Proudhon had implied (since the price of commodities includes not just wages + interest but also the cost of raw materials) without offering any real solution. Marx also notes that Proudhon's formulation is misleading in another way: it assumes \"that the true price of the commodity = the wages contained in it = the amount of paid labour contained in it, while the surplus-value, interest, etc. is no more than a surcharge, an arbitrary extra on top of the true price of the commodity.\" This is precisely the opposite of Marx's analysis, which shows that surplus-value is not a surcharge added to the value of the commodity but a portion of the value created by labour that is appropriated by capital without equivalent. The Formal Subsumption of Labour under Capital Having completed his analysis of the commodity as the product of capital, Marx turns to the second major theme of the Resultate : capitalist production as the production of surplus-value. He begins with the concept of formal subsumption, which he defines as follows: \"The labour process becomes the instrument of the valorization process, the process of the self-valorization of capital—the manufacture of surplus-value. The labour process is subsumed under capital (it is its own process) and the capitalist intervenes in the process as its director, manager. For him it also represents the direct exploitation of the labour of others. It is this that I refer to as the formal subsumption of labour under capital.\" Marx emphasizes that formal subsumption \"is the general form of every capitalist process of production; at the same time, however, it can be found as a particular form alongside the specifically capitalist mode of production in its developed form, because although the latter entails the former, the converse does not necessarily obtain.\" The essential characteristics of formal subsumption are: First, \"The pure money relationship between the man who appropriates the surplus labour and the man who yields it up: subordination in this case arises from the specific content of the sale—there is not a subordination underlying it in which the producer stands in a relation to the exploiter of his labour which is determined not just by money (the relationship of one commodity owner to another), but, let us say, by political constraints.\" Second, \"his objective conditions of labour (the means of production) and the subjective conditions of labour (the means of subsistence) confront him as capital, as the monopoly of the buyer of his labour-power. The more completely these conditions of labour are mobilized against him as alien property, the more effectively the formal relationship between capital and wage-labour is established, i.e. the more effectively the formal subsumption of labour under capital is accomplished, and this in turn is the premiss and precondition of its real subsumption.\" Marx is careful to note that under formal subsumption, \"There is no change as yet in the mode of production itself. Technologically speaking, the labour process goes on as before, with the proviso that it is now subordinated to capital.\" However, two developments emerge within the production process: (1) \"an economic relationship of supremacy and subordination, since the consumption of labour-power by the capitalist is naturally supervised and directed by him\"; (2) \"labour becomes far more continuous and intensive, and the conditions of labour are employed far more economically, since every effort is made to ensure that no more (or rather even less) socially necessary time is consumed in making the product—and this applies both to the living labour that is used to manufacture it and to the objectified labour which enters into it as an element in the means of production.\" Marx also notes that under formal subsumption, \"the compulsion to perform surplus labour, and to create the leisure time necessary for development independently of material production, differs only in form from what had obtained under the earlier mode of production.\" However, \"this formal change is one which increases the continuity and intensity of labour; it is more favourable to the development of versatility among the workers, and hence to increasing diversity in modes of working and ways of earning a living. Lastly, it dissolves the relationship between the owners of the conditions of labour and the workers into a relationship of sale and purchase, a purely financial relationship. In consequence the process of exploitation is stripped of every patriarchal, political or even religious cloak.\" Marx illustrates the historical transition to formal subsumption with examples: \"When a peasant who has always produced enough for his needs becomes a day labourer working for a farmer; when the hierarchic order of guild production vanishes making way for the straightforward distinction between the capitalist and the wage-labourers he employs; when the former slave-owner engages his former slaves as paid workers, etc., then we find that what is happening is that production processes of varying social provenance have been transformed into capitalist production.\" The mystification inherent in the capital-relation emerges at this point. \"The value-sustaining power of labour appears as the self-supporting power of capital; the value-creating power of labour as the self-valorizing power of capital and, in general, in accordance with its concept, living labour appears to be put to work by objectified labour.\" Marx also discusses the historical forms that precede formal subsumption: usurer's capital and merchant's capital. In India, for example, \"the capital of the usurer advances raw materials or tools or even both to the immediate producer in the form of money. The exorbitant interest which it attracts... is just another name for surplus-value. It transforms its money into capital by extorting unpaid labour, surplus labour, from the immediate producer. But it does not intervene in the process of production itself, which proceeds in its traditional fashion, as it always had done.\" Similarly, merchant's capital \"commissions a number of immediate producers, then collects their produce and sells it, perhaps making them advances in the form of raw materials, etc., or even money.\" In neither case do we find formal subsumption of labour under capital, since the immediate producer still performs the functions of selling his wares and making use of his own labour. The Real Subsumption of Labour under Capital The real subsumption of labour under capital represents a qualitative transformation of the production process itself. Marx defines it as follows: \"The real subsumption of labour under capital is developed in all the forms evolved by relative, as opposed to absolute surplus-value. With the real subsumption of labour under capital a complete (and constantly repeated) revolution takes place in the mode of production, in the productivity of the workers and in the relations between workers and capitalists.\" The real subsumption of labour under capital involves: First, \"The social productive forces of labour, or the productive forces of directly social, socialized (i.e. collective) labour come into being through co-operation, division of labour within the workshop, the use of machinery, and in general the transformation of production by the conscious use of the sciences, of mechanics, chemistry, etc. for specific ends, technology, etc. and similarly, through the enormous increase of scale corresponding to such developments.\" Second, \"This entire development of the productive forces of socialized labour (in contrast to the more or less isolated labour of individuals), and together with it the use of science (the general product of social development), in the immediate process of production, takes the form of the productive power of capital. It does not appear as the productive power of labour, or even of that part of it that is identical with capital. And least of all does it appear as the productive power either of the individual worker or of the workers joined together in the process of production.\" Third, \"With the real subsumption of labour under capital, all the changes in the labour process already discussed now become reality. The social forces of production of labour are now developed, and with large-scale production comes the direct application of science and technology. On the one hand, capitalist production now establishes itself as a mode of production sui generis and brings into being a new mode of material production. On the other hand, the latter itself forms the basis for the development of capitalist relations whose adequate form, therefore, presupposes a definite stage in the evolution of the productive forces of labour.\" Marx emphasizes the historical significance of real subsumption: \"It has already been noted that a definite and constantly growing minimum amount of capital is both the necessary precondition and the constant result of the specifically capitalist mode of production. The capitalist must be the owner or proprietor of the means of production on a social scale and in quantities that beggar comparison with the possible production of the individual and his family.\" The real subsumption of labour under capital also has a negative side: \"production in contradiction, and indifference, to the producer. The real producer as a mere means of production, material wealth as an end in itself. And so the growth of this material wealth is brought about in contradiction to and at the expense of the individual human being.\" Productive and Unproductive Labour Marx's discussion of productive and unproductive labour is one of the most important sections of the Resultate . He begins by defining productive labour from the standpoint of capitalist production: \"Since the immediate purpose and the authentic product of capitalist production is surplus-value, labour is only productive, and an exponent of labour-power is only a productive worker, if it or he creates surplus-value directly, i.e. the only productive labour is that which is directly consumed in the course of production for the valorization of capital.\" Marx distinguishes this from the definition of productive labour from the standpoint of the labour process: \"Looked at from the simple standpoint of the labour process, labour seemed productive if it realized itself in a product, or rather a commodity. From the standpoint of capitalist production we may add the qualification that labour is productive if it directly valorizes capital, or creates surplus-value.\" Marx emphasizes that \"Only the labour which posits the variable capital and hence the total capital as C+ΔC = C+Δv is productive. It is therefore labour which directly serves capital as the agency of its self-valorization, as means for the production of surplus-value.\" This leads Marx to a crucial distinction: \"Every productive worker is a wage-labourer, but not every wage-labourer is a productive worker. Whenever labour is purchased to be consumed as a use-value, as a service and not to replace the value of variable capital with its own vitality and be incorporated into the capitalist process of production—whenever that happens, labour is not productive and the wage-labourer is no productive worker. His work is consumed for its use-value, not as creating exchange-value; it is consumed unproductively, not productively.\" Marx illustrates this with examples: \"Milton, who wrote Paradise Lost , was an unproductive worker. On the other hand, a writer who turns out work for his publisher in factory style is a productive worker... A singer who sings like a bird is an unproductive worker. If she sells her song for money, she is to that extent a wage-labourer or merchant. But if the same singer is engaged by an entrepreneur who makes her sing to make money, then she becomes a productive worker, since she produces capital directly. A schoolmaster who instructs others is not a productive worker. But a schoolmaster who works for wages in an institution along with others, using his own labour to increase the money of the entrepreneur who owns the knowledge-mongering institution, is a productive worker.\" Marx also emphasizes the development of the \"collective worker\" with the development of the real subsumption of labour under capital: \"with the development of the real subsumption of labour under capital, or the specifically capitalist mode of production, the real lever of the overall labour process is increasingly not the individual worker. Instead, labour-power socially combined and the various competing labour-powers which together form the entire production machine participate in very different ways in the immediate process of making commodities... Some work better with their hands, others with their heads, one as a manager, engineer, technologist, etc., the other as overseer, the third as manual labourer or even drudge.\" Marx draws two important conclusions: First, \"An ever increasing number of types of labour are included in the immediate concept of productive labour, and those who perform it are classed as productive workers, workers directly exploited by capital and subordinated to its process of production and expansion.\" Second, \"the activity of this aggregate labour-power is its immediate productive consumption by capital, i.e. it is the self-valorization process of capital, and hence... the immediate production of surplus-value, the immediate conversion of this latter into capital.\" Net and Gross Product Marx's discussion of net and gross product is relatively brief but important. He argues: \"Since the purpose of capitalist production (and hence of productive labour) is not the existence of the producer, but the production of surplus-value, it follows that all necessary labour which does not produce surplus labour is superfluous and worthless to capitalist production. The same holds good for a nation of capitalists. All produit brut [gross produce] which merely reproduces the worker, i.e. which creates no produit net [surplus produce] is just as superfluous as the worker himself.\" Marx quotes Ricardo with approval: \"'Provided its net real income, its rents and profits be the same, it is of no importance whether the nation consists of ten or of twelve millions of inhabitants... If five millions of men could produce as much food and clothing as was necessary for ten millions, food and clothing for five millions would be the net revenue.'\" Marx comments: \"Even the philanthropists can have no objection to bring forward against this statement of Ricardo's. For it is always better that of ten million people only 50 per cent should vegetate as pure production-machines for five millions, than that of twelve millions, seven or 58⅓ per cent should vegetate as such.\" Marx also notes that this view \"conflicts with, for example, the antiquated view typical of earlier modes of production according to which the city authorities would, for instance, prohibit inventions so as not to deprive workers of their livelihood. In such a society the worker was an end in himself and appropriate employment was his privilege, a right which the entire order was concerned to maintain. It conflicts, moreover, with the idea of the protectionist system (as opposed to free trade)... Finally, it conflicts with the view of Adam Smith that, for example, the investment of capital in agriculture is 'more productive' because the same capital provides work for more hands.\" The Mystification of Capital Marx's discussion of the mystification of capital is one of the most philosophically rich sections of the Resultate . He argues that \"Since—within the process of production—living labour has already been absorbed into capital, all the social productive forces of labour appear as the productive forces of capital, as intrinsic attributes of capital, just as in the case of money, the creative power of labour had seemed to possess the qualities of a thing.\" Marx identifies three reasons why this mystification is even more pronounced for capital than for money: (1) \"although labour is an expression of labour-power, although it represents the effort of the individual worker, and so belongs to him (it is the substance with which he pays the capitalist for what he receives from him), it nevertheless objectifies itself in the product and so belongs to the capitalist. - Even worse, the social configuration in which the individual workers exist, and within which they function only as the particular organs of the total labour-power that makes up the workshop as a whole, does not belong to them either. On the contrary, it confronts them as a capitalist arrangement that is imposed on them\"; (2) \"these social productive forces of labour, or productive forces of social labour, came into being historically only with the advent of the specifically capitalist mode of production. That is to say, they appeared as something intrinsic to the relations of capitalism and inseparable from them\"; (3) \"with the development of the capitalist mode of production the objective conditions of labour take on a different form owing to the scale on which, and the economy with which, they are employed... As they develop they become increasingly concentrated; they represent social wealth and, to put the matter in a nutshell, their scope and their effect is that of the conditions of production of labour socially combined. And quite apart from the combination of labour, the social character of the conditions of labour—and this includes machinery and capital fixe of every kind—appears to be entirely autonomous and independent of the worker.\" Marx also notes that profits can rise as a result of the economical use of collective conditions of labour: \"The fact that the value of the prime mover does not increase at the same rate as its power: economies in the price of raw materials, recycling of waste, reduction in administrative costs, or in storage costs as the result of mass production, etc.—all these relative savings accruing to constant capital and coinciding with the absolute growth in its value are based on the fact that these means of production, i.e. both the means and the materials of labour, are used collectively.\" And he adds: \"science, which is in fact the general intellectual product of the social process, also appears to be the direct offshoot of capital (since its application to the material process of production takes place in isolation from the knowledge and abilities of the individual worker). And since society is marked by the exploitation of labour by capital, its development appears to be the productive force of capital as opposed to labour.\" Marx concludes this section with a powerful summary: \"The capitalist himself wields power only inasmuch as he is the personification of capital... The relationship becomes more complicated, however, and apparently more mysterious, with the emergence of the specifically capitalist mode of production. Here we find that it is not only such things—the products of labour, both use-values and exchange-values—that rise up on their hind legs and face the worker and confront him as 'Capital'. But even the social form of labour appears as a form of development of capital, and hence the productive forces of social labour so developed appear as the productive forces of capitalism.\" Section III: Capitalist Production as the Production and Reproduction of the Specifically Capitalist Relations of Production The third major section of the Resultate develops the argument that capitalist production is not merely the production of commodities and surplus-value but the production and reproduction of the specifically capitalist relations of production themselves. Marx begins: \"The product of capitalist production is not only surplus-value; it is also capital.\" Marx emphasizes the transformative character of the production process: \"In the first instance, even after its conversion into the factors of the labour process... the value or money invested is only capital in itself, only potentially... Only when it finds itself within that process, only when living labour is really incorporated into the objectively existent forms of capital, only when additional labour is sucked into the process, only then do we find that this labour is converted into capital.\" The result of this process is that \"the worker sold the right to control his labour-power in exchange for the necessary means of subsistence... Looking at him, what is the result? Simplement et purement the reproduction of his labour-power... In contrast to this, capital does not emerge from the process as it entered it. It only becomes real capital, value valorizing itself, in the course of the process. It now exists as capital realized in the form of the aggregate product, and as such, as the property of the capitalist, it now confronts labour once more as an autonomous power even though it was created by that very labour.\" Marx draws out the implications of this analysis for understanding accumulation: \"The surplus-value is changed back into additional capital; it manifests itself as the formation of new capital or of enlarged capital. Hence capital has created capital; it has not just realized itself as capital. The process of accumulation is itself an intrinsic feature of the capitalist process of production. It entails the new creation of wage-labourers, of the means to realize and increase the available amount of capital.\" This leads to a crucial insight: \"capital not only produces capital, it produces a growing mass of men, the material through which alone it can function as additional capital. Therefore, it is not only true to say that labour produces on a constantly increasing scale the conditions of labour in opposition to itself in the form of capital, but equally, capital produces on a steadily increasing scale the productive wage-labourers it requires. Labour produces the conditions of its production in the form of capital, and capital produces labour, i.e. as wage-labour, as the means towards its own realization as capital.\" Marx emphasizes that the reproduction of the capitalist relation is not merely quantitative but qualitative: \"This relation is not merely reproduced, it is produced on a steadily more massive scale, so that it creates ever new supplies of workers and encroaches on branches of production previously independent. In addition, as we have seen in our account of the mode of production specific to capitalism, the relation is reproduced in a fashion increasingly favourable to the one side, the capitalists, and increasingly unfavourable to the other side, the wage-labourers.\" Marx also emphasizes that the wage relation, which appears as a free exchange between commodity owners, is in fact the mechanism through which the worker's subordination is perpetuated: \"If we consider the continuity of the process of production, the labourer's wage is only that part of the product constantly produced by the worker, who converts it into the means of subsistence and hence into the means for the preservation and increase of the labour-power which capital requires to valorize value for itself, i.e. for its own life-process. The maintenance and increase of labour-power appear therefore merely as the reproduction and extension of its own conditions of reproduction and accumulation.\" Marx draws a sharp contrast between the appearance and the reality of the capital-labour relationship: \"This destroys the last vestiges of the illusion, so typical of the relationship when considered superficially, that in the circulation process, in the market-place, two equally matched commodity owners confront each other, and that they, like all other commodity owners, are distinguishable only by the material content of their goods, by the specific use-value of the goods they desire to sell each other.\" Marx identifies two distinct features of the reproduction of the capitalist relation: First, \"with regard to the initial process within the sphere of circulation, the sale and purchase of labour-power... The capitalist process of production is not just the conversion into capital of the value or of the commodity which the capitalist partly puts on the market and partly retains within the labour process. On the contrary, these products transformed into capital are not his products, but the products of the worker. He constantly sells him a portion of his product—the necessities of life for his labour—in order to maintain and increase the labour-power, i.e. the purchaser himself. And he borrows from him in return another portion of his product, the objective conditions of labour, as capital, as the means whereby capital can valorize itself. Thus, while the worker produces his produce as capital, the capitalist reproduces the worker as a wage-labourer and hence as the vendor of his labour.\" Second, \"For capitalist relations to establish themselves at all presupposes that a certain historical level of social production has been attained... But for the time being they need to be developed only to the point that permits the formal subsumption of labour under capital. On the basis of that change, however, specific changes in the mode of production are introduced which create new forces of production, and these in turn influence the mode of production so that new real conditions come into being. Thus a complete economic revolution is brought about. On the one hand, it creates the real conditions for the domination of labour by capital, perfecting the process and providing it with the appropriate framework. On the other hand, by evolving conditions of production and communication and productive forces of labour antagonistic to the workers involved in them, this revolution creates the real premisses of a new mode of production, one that abolishes the contradictory form of capitalism.\" Marx concludes the main text of the Resultate with a powerful summary: \"It is not just the objective conditions of the process of production that appear as its result. The same thing is true also of its specific social character. The social relations and therefore the social position of the agents of production in relation to each other, i.e. the relations of production, are themselves produced: they are also the constantly renewed result of the process.\" The Isolated Fragments The Resultate concludes with several \"Isolated Fragments\" that Marx evidently intended to revise and incorporate into the main text. These fragments contain significant insights on a variety of topics. The first fragment, on \"The Sale of Labour-Power and the Trade Unions,\" develops Marx's analysis of the peculiar nature of labour-power as a commodity. Marx notes that \"the commodity sold only passes into the hands of the purchaser as a use-value after the conclusion of the contract between buyer and seller. Its exchange-value, like that of every other commodity, is determined before it goes into circulation... The exchange-value of this commodity existed, therefore, before its sale, while its use-value consists only in the subsequent expression of its power. That is to say, the alienation of the power and its actual expression, i.e. its existence as a use-value, do not coincide in time.\" This temporal disjunction has important implications: \"where the formal alienation of the commodity through sale does not coincide in time with the real transfer of its use-value to the purchaser, the buyer's money functions as we have seen in the first instance as a means of payment. The labour-power is sold for a day, a week, etc., but it is paid for only after it has been consumed for a day or a week, etc. In all countries where capitalist relations are in the process of development, labour-power is only paid for after it has been used up. As a rule, then, the worker advances the use of his commodity to the capitalist. He permits its consumption by the buyer, he allows him credit, before he receives its exchange-value in payment.\" Marx then turns to the trade unions, arguing that \"the value of labour-power constitutes the conscious and explicit foundation of the trade unions, whose importance for the English working class can scarcely be overestimated. The trade unions aim at nothing less than to prevent the reduction of wages below the level that is traditionally maintained in the various branches of industry. That is to say, they wish to prevent the price of labour-power from falling below its value.\" Marx quotes Dunning with approval: \"there is 'a great distinction between the level of wages as determined by supply and demand, i.e. by the level produced by the fair operation of exchange that exists when buyer and seller negotiate on equal terms, and the level of wages which the seller, the labourer, must put up with when the capitalist negotiates with each man singly, and dictates a reduction by exploiting the chance need of individual workers (which exists independently of the general relations of supply and demand). The workers combine in order to achieve equality of a sort with the capitalist in their contract concerning the sale of their labour. This is the rationale (the logical basis) of the trade unions.'\" Marx also discusses the \"sweating\" system, in which \"A sweater is someone who undertakes to deliver a certain quantity of work at normal prices to an entrepreneur, but who then has it carried out for a lower price by others. The difference, which goes to make up his profit, is sweated out of the workers who actually perform the labour and represents nothing but the difference between the value of the labour-power that is paid by the first entrepreneur and the price which is equivalent to less than the value of that labour-power and which is paid by the sweater to the actual workers.\" The second fragment, on \"Different Modes of Centralization of the Means of Production in Different Countries,\" compares the development of capitalist production in England and France. Marx quotes a factory inspector's report showing that \"the number of factories in France is three times as large as those in England, while the number of persons employed in them is only ½ greater; but the very different proportions of machinery and moving power will best be shown by the following comparison\": France England Number of factories 12,986 4,330 Number of persons employed 706,450 596,082 Average number of persons in each factory 54 137 Average number of spindles to each person employed 7 43 Average number of persons to each loom 2 (power and handloom) 2 (powerloom only) Marx draws the conclusion: \"For each worker in an English factory, compared to the Frenchmen, there is far more machinery for working and also far more power-driving machinery (mechanic power), and hence, too, far more raw materials are processed by him in the same time. The productive power of his labour is, therefore, much greater, as is the capital that employs him.\" Marx emphasizes that this difference is related to the degree of expropriation of the immediate producers: \"It becomes quite plain to see here that because of historical and other circumstances which have had a varying effect upon the relative magnitude of the concentration of the means of production, there is a correspondingly greater or smaller expropriation of the mass of immediate producers... And this stands in inverse ratio to the 'saving' and 'hoarding' of the immediate producer himself, which in France is huge in comparison with England.\" Marx concludes: \"Capitalist accumulation and concentration are based upon, and correspond to, the facility of appropriating other people's surplus labour in great masses and the corresponding inability of these people themselves to lay any claim to their own surplus labour. It is, therefore, the most ludicrous delusion, fallacy or imposture to explain and account for this capitalist accumulation by confounding it with, and, as far as the phraseology is concerned, converting it into, a process quite its opposite, exclusive of it, and corresponding to a mode of production upon whose ruins alone capitalist production can be reared.\" The third fragment, on \"Ireland. Emigration,\" discusses the relationship between emigration and the labour market. Marx argues that \"the emigration of capital, i.e. of that part of annual revenue which is invested abroad, particularly in the colonies and the United States of America, is far greater in proportion to the annual accumulation fund than is the number of emigrants in proportion to the annual growth in the population.\" He notes that \"emigration from England consists if we consider its principal part, the agricultural sector, not of working men, but of tenant-farmers' sons, etc. Hitherto it was more than made good by immigration from Ireland.\" Marx argues that \"If emigration from England were really to swell to serious dimensions in relation to the annual growth in the population, it would spell the end of its dominance of the world market.\" He also discusses the Irish famine and its aftermath, noting that \"Production has declined faster than the population, and so have the means of occupying the agricultural labourers, even though their wages are no higher now, if we take the differences in the price of necessaries into account, than they were in 1847. The population, however, has diminished from 8 million to around 4½ million in 15 years.\" The fourth fragment, on \"Expropriation and Depopulation in Eastern Germany during the Eighteenth Century,\" quotes Gustav Freytag on the process of \"Bauernlegen\" (peasant-clearing): \"ever since the landlords had begun to concentrate their efforts on raising the yield of their properties, they found it to their advantage to drive out many of their subjects and to add their fields to their own estates. The people thus expropriated, having no home of their own, became destitute.\" The fifth fragment, on \"Property and Capital,\" develops Marx's critique of the confusion between capitalist property and petty-bourgeois property: \"Although the formation of capital and the capitalist mode of production are essentially founded not merely on the abolition of feudal production but also on the expropriation of the peasantry, craftsmen and in general of the mode of production based on the private ownership by the immediate producer of his conditions of production; although, once capitalist production has been introduced, it continues to develop at the same rate as that private property and the mode of production based on it is destroyed, so that those immediate producers are expropriated in the name of the concentration of capital (centralization)... both the theory of capitalist production (political economy, philosophy of law, etc.) and the capitalist himself in his own mind is pleased to confuse his mode of property and appropriation, which is based on the expropriation of the immediate producer in its origins, and on the acquisition of the labour of others in its further progress, with its opposite: with a mode of production that presupposes that the immediate producer privately owns his own conditions of production.\" The sixth fragment, on \"The Colliers,\" describes the brutal conditions faced by coal miners during a strike in Durham in November 1863: \"The people were evicted, wives and children included, in the harshest weather; and their furniture was put into the street... A large number slept in the open; some broke into their evacuated dwellings and occupied them during the night. The next day the mine-owners had the doors and windows barred and nailed up, to deprive the evicted people of the luxury of sleeping through ice-cold nights on the bare floors of the empty cottages.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles Revealed The Resultate reveals several fundamental principles that are essential for understanding Marx's mature analysis of capitalism: First, the dialectical unity of production and social relations. Perhaps the most important lesson of the Resultate is that capitalist production cannot be understood merely as a technical process of transforming inputs into outputs, nor merely as an economic process of generating profit. Rather, it must be understood as a social process that simultaneously produces material wealth and reproduces the specific social relations—the relations of production—that define capitalism as a historical mode of production. \"The social relations and therefore the social position of the agents of production in relation to each other, i.e. the relations of production, are themselves produced: they are also the constantly renewed result of the process.\" This insight has profound implications for understanding both the stability and the dynamics of capitalism: the system reproduces itself not through external coercion but through the normal functioning of its economic processes. Second, the historical specificity of capitalist categories. The Resultate demonstrates that economic categories that appear to be natural and eternal—the commodity, money, capital, productive labour—are in fact historically specific forms that emerge only under particular conditions. The commodity becomes the universal form of the product only with the development of capitalist production; capital is not a thing but a social relation that appears in the form of things; productive labour is not merely labour that produces useful things but labour that is directly exchanged for capital and produces surplus-value. This historicization of economic categories is essential for understanding capitalism as a historically transient mode of production rather than a natural and permanent feature of human society. Third, the centrality of subsumption to understanding capitalist development. The distinction between formal and real subsumption provides a powerful analytical framework for understanding the historical development of capitalism. Formal subsumption characterizes the initial stage of capitalist development, when capital takes over pre-existing labour processes without fundamentally transforming them; real subsumption characterizes the mature stage, when capital has revolutionized the production process through machinery, science, and technology. This distinction helps explain why capitalism first emerges in certain branches of production and certain geographical locations, how it spreads from one sphere to another, and why it tends toward constant revolutionization of the forces of production. Fourth, the mystification inherent in capitalist relations. The Resultate provides one of Marx's most sustained analyses of the mystification and fetishism that characterize capitalist society. The social productive forces of labour—the productivity that arises from co-operation, division of labour, and the application of science—appear as the productive forces of capital. The value-creating power of labour appears as the self-valorizing power of capital. The domination of capital over labour appears as the natural order of things. This mystification is not merely an ideological illusion but is rooted in the actual functioning of the capitalist process of production: \"the entire process is progressively intensified as their labour-power is itself modified by these forms to such an extent that it is rendered impotent even when it exists autonomously.\" Fifth, the necessary expansion of the capitalist market. The Resultate contains Marx's most explicit statement of the necessity for constant expansion of the capitalist market. Precisely because capitalist production is production through a growing mass of machinery, growing fixed capital, and a growing organic composition of capital, it is necessarily mass production of commodities on an constantly increasing scale, whose realization demands a constantly growing market. This insight, which Mandel notes has been \"so often contested by his critics and by some of his followers too,\" provides the theoretical foundation for understanding the inherent expansionary dynamic of capitalism and the systemic pressures that drive it toward globalization. Sixth, the historical mission of capitalism. Despite its exploitative and contradictory character, capitalism has a historical mission: to develop the productive forces of social labour to a level that makes possible a new form of society. \"by evolving conditions of production and communication and productive forces of labour antagonistic to the workers involved in them, this revolution creates the real premisses of a new mode of production, one that abolishes the contradictory form of capitalism. It thereby creates the material basis of a newly shaped social process and hence of a new social formation.\" This dialectical understanding of capitalism's historical role—as both an exploitative system and a necessary stage in human development—is essential for understanding Marx's critique of political economy as a whole. Broader Practical and Theoretical Implications The Resultate has profound implications for a wide range of theoretical and practical questions: For the theory of value. The Resultate demonstrates that the labour theory of value is not merely a theory about the determination of prices but a theory about the social relations that underlie the production and distribution of wealth under capitalism. The value of a commodity is not merely a technical magnitude but a social relation—the relation between the labour time socially necessary for its production and the labour time embodied in other commodities. The Resultate shows how this social relation is mediated by the capitalist process of production, in which the commodity appears not as an autonomous article but as an aliquot part of the total product of capital. For the theory of exploitation. The Resultate provides a rigorous demonstration of how exploitation is not an incidental feature of capitalism but is essential to its functioning. The extraction of surplus-value is not a matter of individual capitalists' greed or cruelty but is the necessary condition for the existence of capital as a social relation. \"Without the exchange of variable capital for labour-power the total capital could not valorize itself and so the formation of capital and the transformation of the means of production and means of subsistence into capital could not take place.\" Exploitation is thus not a moral failing of capitalism but a structural necessity of the system. For the theory of the state. Although the Resultate does not directly address the theory of the state, its analysis of the reproduction of capitalist relations has important implications for understanding the role of the state in maintaining and reproducing capitalism. If the capitalist relations of production are reproduced through the normal functioning of the economic process, then the state's role is not primarily to enforce these relations through coercion but to maintain the conditions under which they can reproduce themselves. This includes protecting property rights, enforcing contracts, maintaining the conditions for the accumulation of capital, and managing the contradictions and crises that arise from the capitalist process. For the theory of revolution. The Resultate provides the theoretical basis for understanding both the necessity and the possibility of revolutionary transformation. The necessity arises from the contradictions of capitalism—the conflict between the development of the productive forces and the capitalist relations of production that fetter them. The possibility arises from the development of the collective worker, whose socialization and concentration create the material basis for a new form of social organization. \"The social forms of their own labour—both subjectively and objectively—or, in other words, the forms of their own social labour, are utterly independent of the individual workers... And this entire process is progressively intensified as their labour-power is itself modified by these forms to such an extent that it is rendered impotent even when it exists autonomously.\" For the theory of ideology. The Resultate provides important insights into the nature of ideology under capitalism. The mystification that characterizes the capitalist process of production—the appearance of social relations as properties of things—is not merely an illusion that can be dispelled by correct analysis but is rooted in the actual functioning of the capitalist economy. The ideology of the bourgeoisie, which presents capitalism as the natural and eternal form of production, is not merely a conscious deception but a reflection of the way things actually appear under capitalism. This understanding of ideology as rooted in material relations, rather than merely in ideas, is essential for understanding how capitalist societies maintain their legitimacy and reproduce their social relations. Conclusion The Resultate stands as one of Marx's most significant achievements, providing a comprehensive synthesis of his analysis of the capitalist process of production and its role in reproducing the specifically capitalist relations of production. Its theoretical innovations—the distinction between formal and real subsumption, the concept of the collective worker, the analysis of the reproduction of the capital-labour relationship—continue to inform contemporary Marxist scholarship and provide essential tools for understanding the dynamics of capitalism in the twenty-first century. The text's emphasis on the production and reproduction of social relations, rather than merely on the production of material goods, anticipates many of the themes that would be developed in twentieth-century Marxist theory, from Gramsci's concept of hegemony to Althusser's analysis of ideological state apparatuses to contemporary work on the social reproduction of capitalism. Its demonstration of the necessary expansion of the capitalist market anticipates the globalization of the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries. Its analysis of the mystification inherent in capitalist relations anticipates contemporary work on fetishism, ideology, and the cultural politics of capitalism. At the same time, the Resultate remains a text that is deeply rooted in its historical moment, written in the context of the mid-nineteenth century industrial capitalism of Western Europe and responding to the theoretical and political debates of that period. Its insights must be critically appropriated and developed in light of the transformations of capitalism since Marx's time—the development of monopoly capitalism, imperialism, welfare state capitalism, neoliberalism, and contemporary forms of globalized, financialized capitalism. But the fundamental insight of the Resultate —that capitalist production is not merely the production of things but the production and reproduction of social relations—remains as relevant as ever. In an era when capitalist relations have penetrated every corner of the globe and every aspect of social life, when the production of surplus-value has become the organizing principle of human society, and when the contradictions of capitalism threaten the very conditions of human survival, Marx's analysis of how these relations are produced and reproduced remains an indispensable tool for understanding and transforming the world in which we live. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Chapter 33 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Introduction"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Introduction", "title": "Introduction", "subtitle": "Introduction to Volume 2", "path": "Volume 2/Introduction/Introduction_explanation.html", "word_count": 26335, "read_time": "132 min read", "tags": ["Circuits of Capital (M-C-M')", "Turnover of Capital & Cycles", "Dialectical Method & Critique of Political Economy"], "keywords": ["Circulation Process of Social Capital", "Transition from Production to Circulation", "Critique of Classical Circulation Theories", "Engels' Editorial Prefaces and Manuscripts", "Integration of Reproduction and Turnover"], "text": "Introduction - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 26,287 words ⏱️ ~131 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation MANDEL'S INTRODUCTION TO CAPITAL VOLUME 2: AN EXHAUSTIVE DECONSTRUCTION 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Core Problem Ernest Mandel's Introduction to Volume 2 of Capital is not a neutral exposition. It is a polemical intervention on three fronts at once. The text opens with a strategic embarrassment: Frederick Engels's own private correspondence of 1884–85 predicted that the second volume would be \"purely scientific,\" would \"provoke great disappointment,\" would contain \"not much material for agitation,\" and would remain \"a sealed book\" to the official economic literature of Germany. Engels was right — and worse than right. A decade elapsed before Tugan-Baranowski and Bulgakov made the first serious applications of the volume's conceptual innovations; nearly another decade passed before these concepts penetrated Germany and the Western world, and only through an international debate in which Tugan-Baranowski himself began revising Marx's \"key theories.\" Mandel's thesis, thus, operates on a paradox: Volume 2 is \"not only a 'sealed book', but also a forgotten one... To a large extent, it remains so to this very day.\" The central problem Mandel sets out to solve is this: why is Volume 2 indispensable to Marx's theoretical construction, and what happens — intellectually and politically — when it is skipped? The famous charge is that students \"pass straight from Volume 1 to Volume 3,\" treating Volume 2 as an interstitial technicality. Mandel argues the opposite: it is precisely Volume 2 that supplies the missing theoretical machinery — the theory of realization, the theory of the reproduction of the total social capital, the internal contradictions of the commodity as a motor of cyclical crisis — that makes Volume 3's analysis of the profit rate, competition, and distribution intelligible. To evacuate Volume 2 is to evacuate \"all problems specific to the inner contradictions of the commodity — problems of the market, of the realization of value and surplus-value.\" Marx himself clarified this. In his letter to Engels of 30 April 1868, he distinguished Book I from the later volumes in a single sentence: in Book I we assume that if £100 becomes £110, the latter \"will find already in existence in the market the elements into which it will change once more. But now we investigate the conditions under which these elements are found at hand, namely the social intertwining of the different capitals, of the component parts of capital and of revenue (= s).\" This \"intertwining\" — conceived as a movement of both commodities and money — is the analytic object of Volume 2, and it is what enables Marx to work out \"at least the essential elements, if not the definitive form of a coherent theory of the trade cycle.\" The Intellectual Objective Mandel's objective is threefold. First, to reconstruct the conceptual architecture of Volume 2 — the three forms of capital (money, productive, commodity); the \"metamorphosis\" of capital; the dual aspect of turnover (value-realization and use-value adequacy); the reproduction schemas and their proportionality conditions; the productive/unproductive labour distinction; the reproduction of the total social capital. Second, to defend this architecture against misreadings — most importantly against the \"neo-harmonicist\" reading that used the schemas to \"prove\" that capitalism can grow smoothly if only the correct proportions are maintained. Third, and most ambitiously, to demonstrate that Volume 2 contains a theory of crisis — grounded in the contradiction between production's unlimited tendency and the restricted consumption of the masses, in the periodic hoarding of money, in the discontinuous renewal of fixed capital, and in the tendential fall of the rate of profit — that is superior to any single-cause theory, whether \"under-consumptionist\" or \"disproportionist.\" This places Mandel in direct and complex relation to several intellectual genealogies. He positions Marx on a lineage running from Quesnay's Tableau économique through Walras, Leontiev, and Keynes — Marx making \"a leap forward\" over Quesnay that is \"immediately apparent,\" while neo-classical and vulgar macro-economics contains \"elements of enormous regression.\" He must adjudicate between Marx and his followers (Hilferding, Bauer, Bukharin, Luxemburg, Grossmann, Dobb, Lange, Minc, Morishima, Sweezy, Baran, Cliff). He must intervene in the debates of his own moment: the productive/unproductive labour controversy of the 1970s, the state-expenditure controversy, the \"permanent arms economy\" debate, and the socialist-planning debates in the USSR and Eastern Europe. And he must connect the whole to the political present — May 1968, the generalized recession of 1974–75 — as evidence that Marx's \"awe-inspiring prediction\" has been borne out empirically. Fit Within the Broader Framework Mandel's Introduction fits into three frames simultaneously. Historically , it belongs to the tradition of Marxist economic reconstruction that began with the publication of the Grundrisse (1941, 1953), Rosdolsky's The Making of Marx's Capital (1968), and the wave of Western Marxist engagement with the \"circuit of capital\" in the 1960s–70s. Mandel explicitly credits Rosdolsky as a key influence and interlocutor. Politically , it is an intervention against the \"neo-harmonicist\" reading of Capital — the view, derived from Tugan-Baranowski, that organized capitalism could eliminate crisis. This reading, Mandel insists, is not merely theoretically wrong; it was politically disastrous, justifying everything from \"state capitalism\" apologetics to the Dobb-Lange-Minc doctrines of \"socialist planning.\" Methodologically , it is an exercise in what Mandel calls Marx's \"method of successive approximation.\" The schemas operate at the level of \"capital in general\" ( Das Kapital im Allgemeinen ) — provisionally abstracted from competition and \"many capitals.\" The reader must never mistake abstractions for historical assertions, nor treat the schemas as a descriptive model of capitalism's actual motion. Most critics of Marx — from Luxemburg to Harris — err precisely by collapsing these levels. The Introduction's underlying thesis is thus deceptively simple: Volume 2 demonstrates that growth is possible under capitalism, but only within fragile proportional structures that capitalism's own laws of motion perpetually destroy. Equilibrium is the exception; disproportionality the rule; growth inherently uneven. The \"sealed book\" is sealed precisely because it delivers the most subversive message in the whole of Capital : that capitalism's dynamism is inseparable from its self-negating tendency. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Place of Volume 2 in Marx's General Analysis of Capitalism Mandel opens with an archaeological gesture, unearthing Engels's letters as a kind of textual prophecy. The letters function rhetorically as an admission by Marx's closest collaborator that Volume 2 would be inaccessible to the \"vulgar reader,\" the Kathedersozialisten , and the \"apologists of Bismarck's Staatssozialismus .\" Engels predicted a \"cautious silence\" from official economic literature. That silence, Mandel argues, has been over-fulfilled: ten years before Tugan-Baranowski's first Russian application; nearly another decade before the concepts penetrated Germany through the \"international debate.\" Argument. The interpretive move here is to distinguish the volume's theoretical status from its reception history . A text can be a \"sealed book\" in the sense that its argumentative apparatus is difficult, without being theoretically marginal. Mandel wants to show that Volume 2 is in fact structurally central. His key evidence is Marx's own letter to Engels. Concepts & Terminology. The letter introduces the phrase \"social intertwining of the different capitals, of the component parts of capital and of revenue (= s).\" Each term must be unpacked. \"Different capitals\" are the individual capitals of a capitalist economy in their mutual dependence — department I selling means of production to department II, department II selling consumer goods to the workers and capitalists of both. \"Component parts of capital\" refers to the internal division c (constant capital) + v (variable capital) + s (surplus-value). \"Revenue (= s)\" marks the key move: the surplus-value becomes revenue — income to be spent — for the capitalist class, and its circulation between departments is what equilibrates (or fails to equilibrate) reproduction. The parenthetical \"(= s)\" is decisive: it announces that the analysis of revenue in Volume 2 is the analysis of the disposal of surplus-value. The distinction Volume 1 / Volume 2, Mandel insists, is not simply one of scale or detail but of question . Volume 1 asks how value and surplus-value are produced; Volume 2 asks how they are realized. The workbook/workshop distinction is deliberate: Volume 1 is centered on the factory; Volume 2 on the market-place. The \"dramatis personae\" shift accordingly: money-owner and money-lender, wholesale merchant, trader, \"functioning capitalist\" or entrepreneur. Definition (Mandel's): The entrepreneur is broadly defined — not simply the industrialist but any capitalist who, having a certain amount of capital at disposal (own or borrowed — the distinction is made irrelevant by the abstraction), \"tr[ies] to increase that capital through the purchase of means of production and labour-power, the production and then the sale of commodities, the reinvestment of part of realized profit in additional machinery, raw materials and labour-power, and the production of an increased quantity of commodities.\" This definition anticipates the entire arc of the volume: reproduction is not a single act but a circuit of transformations. Nuance. Mandel acknowledges the potential surprise: workers appear in Volume 2 \"essentially as buyers of consumer goods and, therefore, as sellers of the commodity labour-power,\" not as producers of value. This is a significant methodological casualty — one that will become catastrophic in the productive/unproductive labour debate later in this Introduction. The worker as producer of value belongs to Volume 1; the worker as buyer of consumer goods is a pivotal actor in the circulation process. Both roles are necessary; neither displaces the other. The Inner Connection Between Production and Realization. Mandel now articulates what he calls the \"basic contradiction\" of commodity production. On one side, human production has outgrown the primitive subsistence-farming/handicraft mode of isolated producer-consumers; the division of labour, productivity growth, and improvements in transport and communication have rendered labour objectively socialized . On the other side, private ownership of the means of production and circulation, plus the rise of money capital, render private appropriation the starting point and goal. Thus \"while labour is objectively more and more socialized, it remains to a greater degree than ever before organized on the basis of private production.\" The implication is precise: the social character of private labour \"cannot be immediately and directly established.\" It is recognized only a posteriori , \"through the sale of the commodity, the realization of its value.\" Value production thus expresses a contradictory unity — goods are simultaneously products of social labour and of private labour, and their social recognition as values is deferred to the market. This is why the study of \"capital in general\" — provisionally abstracted from competition — must encompass both production and circulation. There is an \"indissoluble unity\" between production and circulation. Nuance. Footnote 8 introduces a counter-position: Karl Renner's Die Wirtschaft als Gesamtprozess (1924) which \"bends the stick too far in the other direction\" by focusing entirely on circulation and treating it as the springboard for socialist transformation. Mandel's point is symmetrical: one must not eliminate either moment. Both production-fundamentalism and circulation-fundamentalism are errors. From Circulation to Reproduction. Mandel now transitions to the volume's real subject-matter: the reproduction and circulation (\"turnover\") of the total social capital . Formally the title of only the third Part, but — Mandel argues — expressing the underlying subject-matter of the whole volume. Marx's reasoning: the reproduction of each individual capital must be seen as part of the reproduction of the social capital in toto . This methodological ordering is necessary not only because it precedes analysis of competition's effects on the division of surplus-value, but because a broader question must be answered: \"How can an anarchic social system, based upon private determination of investment, 'factor-combination' and output, assure the presence of the objective, material elements necessary for further production and growth? What are the absolute preconditions of such growth?\" This is the question that demands the reproduction schemas. The schemas are Marx's answer. The Logic of Accumulation. Mandel then rigorously pursues the concept of accumulation. Capitalist production is production for profit — value production oriented towards an accretion of value. Growth therefore means accumulation of capital. The key concepts are capitalization of (part of) surplus-value and expanded reproduction . For economic growth, a portion of surplus-value must be spent productively : transformed into (i) additional constant capital — buildings, equipment, energy, raw materials, auxiliary products — and (ii) additional variable capital — money capital available to hire an increased labour force. Accumulation is \"nothing other than this (partial) capitalization of surplus-value, i.e. the (partial) transformation of profit into additional capital.\" Footnote 10's precision: Accumulation also requires that means of production producing additional means of production be added to means of production producing consumer goods or replacing those used up in current production. This is the department I / department II division in its most elemental form, and Mandel introduces it here — before the schemas — so that the reader grasps the material structure of expanded reproduction before seeing its algebraic expression. Definition: Expanded reproduction. \"A process whereby the turnover of capital... leads, after a certain number of intermediary stages minutely studied in Volume 2, to a larger and larger scale of productive operation. More raw materials are transformed by more workers using more machinery into more finished products, with greater overall value than in the previous turnover cycle. This results in higher total sales and final profits, which in turn allow a higher absolute sum (if not in all cases a higher percentage) of profit to be added to capital.\" Two nuances deserve emphasis here. First, the qualification \"if not in all cases a higher percentage\" — the rate of profit may fall while the mass of profit rises. This distinction is foundational to Volume 3's analysis of the tendency of the rate of profit to decline, and Mandel wants it planted early. Second, the phrase \"the spiral of growth continues...\" introduces what will become a motif: the spiral — a term Mandel notes Marx and Luxemburg borrowed from Sismondi (see §13 below). The Dialectic of Use-Value and Exchange-Value. Mandel closes this section with the first major thematic claim: the whole of Volume 2's analysis is the unfolding of the \"dialectical unity-and-contradiction of opposites contained in the commodity form\" — use-value and exchange-value, \"doubled in that of commodities and money.\" He notes that \"insufficient attention has been paid\" to this by academic and Marxist commentators alike, singling out Rosdolsky as an important exception (footnote 11). Historicizing the Abstraction (Footnote 12). A critical precision: capital may appear and survive in pre- or post-capitalist societies, but \"essentially outside the realm of production.\" It cannot dominate the main sectors of production except when productive capital arises — \"the form proper to the capitalist mode of production.\" This is a reminder that the abstraction \"capital in general\" describes a historically specific form, not a perennial structure. 2.2 The Three Forms of Capital Mandel now formalizes the morphological analysis that defines Volume 2's architecture. Capital, in the capitalist mode of production, appears in three forms: Money capital — \"the original form and final purpose of the whole devilish undertaking.\" Productive capital — \"the basic precondition of the constantly enlarging spiral.\" Without penetration into the sphere of production, the social product can only be re-appropriated, not increased. Otherwise, capitalists would be \"parasites upon and plunderers of pre-capitalist (or post-capitalist) forms of production.\" Commodity capital — \"the basic curse of capitalism.\" Commodities must pass through the phase in which they contain unrealized surplus-value. Before money capital can return to its original form, swollen by surplus-value, it must pass through \"commodity-value\" and submit to \"the acid test by being sold.\" Concept: Metamorphosis of capital. Marx's metaphor — the butterfly through larva, chrysalis, moth — is not decorative. It captures that the three forms are successive in the rotation of capital but also co-existent . Mandel labors this point: the discontinuous nature of reproduction and the discontinuity's organic link with the essence of capitalism is \"one of the most important and brilliant sections of Volume 2.\" Argument: Why co-existence? Because capitalist production is generalized commodity production. Money capital cannot merely precede and succeed the widespread appearance of capital — it must exist side by side with it. Money capital is not just the result of sale; its social existence is a precondition of that sale. Commodity capital is not simply the outcome of productive capital; it is also its necessary basis . Indeed, current production is possible — especially for commodities with an above-average life span or production period — only if not all commodities of the previous cycle have been sold to final consumers, i.e. if stocks and reserves of raw materials, energy, auxiliary products, intermediary products, and consumer goods are available at scale. Thus: \"Continuity of the production process may be said to depend upon discontinuity or desynchronization of the turnover cycle of money capital, productive capital and commodity capital.\" This is one of the most conceptually dense claims in the Introduction. Note the exact inversion: continuity depends upon discontinuity. The apparent paradox is resolved by recognizing that different capitals are at different points of the circuit at any moment. Some sell while others buy; some produce while others hoard; some invest while others devalorize. This desynchronization is what allows the total system to reproduce continuously. If all capitals tried to sell simultaneously, or all tried to hoard simultaneously, the system would seize up. The \"anarchy of production\" is thus not simply a defect but — in a limited, negative sense — the medium of systemic continuity. Capitalist Relations and the Money Relation. Mandel emphasizes the structural necessity of money capital prior to production. The separation of \"free\" workers from their means of production and livelihood implies a constraint on the owners of the means of production to purchase labour-power before productive operations begin. They must have adequate money capital to effect the transaction. Marx's formulation is quoted: \"In the relation between capitalist and wage-labourer, the money relation, the relation of buyer and seller, becomes a relation inherent in production itself.\" This is a crucial theoretical point: the market relation between capitalist and worker is not external ornamentation but internal to the capitalist relation of production. Dialectics of Money and Commodities. Mandel's synthesis: Volume 2 examines the constant intertwining of appearance and disappearance of the three forms — \"from the sphere of circulation into that of production, and back into the sphere of circulation, until the commodity is finally consumed.\" Each form passes into the other \"without expelling it entirely from the sphere of circulation, let alone from the overall social arena.\" The \"dialectics of money (money capital) and commodities (commodity capital) is the basic contradiction examined in Capital Volume 2.\" The Time Factor. Mandel now introduces one of his most theoretically consequential claims: the crucial importance of the \"time factor.\" Capital functioning cannot be understood if complete abstraction is made of time sequences and schedules, the duration of production and turnover cycles, the length of the turnover period. The distinction between circulating capital and fixed capital is grounded exclusively in the amount of time required for each part of money capital to revert to its original form. Circulating capital — spent on raw materials and wages — is recovered after each production and circulation cycle. Fixed capital is recovered in its entirety only after n cycles, where n depends on the longevity of machinery and buildings. Let me state this formally. If the turnover time of a given mass of circulating capital is t , and the (average) useful life of fixed capital is n·t , then: Circulating capital completes n turnovers in the period during which fixed capital completes one. Fixed capital is advanced in toto at the beginning and recovered only gradually through depreciation charges across n cycles. Marx's hypothesis — which Mandel reports approvingly — is that the average longevity of machinery (not buildings) is equivalent to and indeed determines the average duration of the trade cycle. This is the germ of the trade cycle theory developed in §11 of the Introduction. Mandel then offers an extraordinary closing reflection on \"time\" in Marx's Capital : time appears (i) as the measure of production, value and surplus-value (labour time); (ii) as the nexus connecting production, circulation and reproduction (cycles of turnover); (iii) as the medium of the laws of motion of capital (trade cycles, cycles of class struggle, long-term historical cycles); (iv) as the very essence of man (leisure time, life span, creative time, time of social intercourse). This quadruple role is, one might say, the philosophical horizon of Volume 2. From Abstract to Concrete: The Formula. The section closes by presenting the successive stages of concreteness through which circulation is represented. First, the most general form encountered in Volume 1: $$M - C - M'$$ $$(M + \\Delta M)$$ Money buys commodities so that they may be sold with an accretion — a profit — part of which is added to initial money capital. Translating into real operations, we replace C with the specific operation of the industrialist : the purchase of means of production ( Mp ) and labour-power ( Lp ), so that the latter may produce additional value. The formula becomes: $$M - C_{Lp/Mp} \\dots \\text{production} \\dots C' - M' \\; (M + \\Delta M)$$ where ΔM = accumulated surplus-value . This formula will reappear throughout the volume in its departmental form (department I and department II each executing their own M–C–M′ ). 2.3 The Dual Aspect of Capital Turnover in Marx's Economic Theory This section is where Mandel most explicitly establishes his central interpretive claim: the turnover/reproduction problem is dual , rooted in the contradiction between use-value and exchange-value. (a) The Value Side. For simple or expanded reproduction, the total value embodied in produced commodities must be realized — sold at their value. Here Mandel intervenes sharply against predecessors: contrary to Hilferding ( Das Finanzkapital , 1923), Otto Bauer ( Die Akkumulation des Kapitals , 1913), and Bukharin ( Imperialism and the Accumulation of Capital , 1972), Marx did not regard the process of realization as \"automatic\"; nor did he derive it \"from his reproduction schemas,\" as some naively suggested. A substantial section of the final Part of Volume 2, and most controversies since Luxemburg, turn around the question of how value represented by the schemas can be realized by purchasing power generated in the production process. (b) The Use-Value Side. Simultaneously, reproduction requires that the use-value of commodities produced fulfils the material conditions for restarting production. Mandel gives two vivid hypotheticals to make this concrete. Hypothetical 1 (the wrong basket): Imagine the technological base is below full automation and there are no food reserves; imagine the commodity package consists entirely of raw materials and machinery. Then workers and capitalists \"would starve and disappear before the available machinery could be used to restart agricultural production.\" Hypothetical 2 (the wrong basket, reversed): Imagine the entire output is consumer goods and raw materials, produced with sophisticated machinery, with no stocks of machinery or spare parts. Then \"machinery and production would break down before the well-fed workers could build new machines out of simple raw materials.\" Both are absurd — deliberately so — because the point is structural: the material composition of output must satisfy the requirements of reproduction. Use-value adequacy is not a trivial point but an independent condition of equilibrium. This is what academic economics, in its methodological dichotomy between \"physical coefficients\" and \"money flows,\" fails to grasp as a unity . Expanded Reproduction's Additional Material Requirements. Mandel adds three: (i) means of production to replace used-up equipment and raw materials; (ii) further means of production required to enlarge the scale of material production; (iii) consumer goods to feed both employed workers and additional recruits. To this, he adds a fourth (often neglected) element: a potential source of additional labour . The \"industrial reserve army of labour\" has a dual function : as regulator of wages (assuring the rate of surplus-value remains above a certain level) and as material precondition of expanded reproduction. Where \"traditional\" sources of the reserve army are drying up — e.g. when independent peasants and handicraftsmen decline, or when machine substitution slows — \"new sources can always be tapped through sweeping transformation of housewives into wage-labourers; mass immigration of labour; extensive re-deployment of student youth onto the labour market, and so on\" (footnote 15, referencing Mandel's Late Capitalism ). This is one of Mandel's most concrete analytical moves: he converts the abstract requirement of \"additional labour\" into a real, empirically locatable set of social processes. His incorporation of women's labour, immigration, and student labour anticipates later feminist and migration studies of the reserve army. Marx's Innovation and Its Misfortunes. Mandel now states his critical assessment: Marx made a \"giant step forward,\" but \"until this very day, most academic economists have still not fully grasped this basic innovation.\" They broke the totality of reproduction into a \"disconnected dichotomy.\" On the one hand, physical coefficients — Leontiev's input-output tables — i.e. use-values. On the other hand, Keynesian and post-Keynesian money flows — exchange-values \"disembodied from the commodities in the production of which they originated.\" Concepts: Leontiev's input-output tables. These represent the physical interdependence of branches — how much steel each industry consumes, how much coal, etc. Mandel's criticism (drawing on Koshimura, footnote 5): Leontiev's tables \"reflect only the use-value inter-relationships ('exchanges') between different departments, and abstract from the question of the source of the purchasing power necessary to mediate these 'exchanges'.\" Crucially, they \"ignore both the capital structure as a whole, and the component parts of commodities, i.e. c, v, and m.\" So Leontiev captures use-value structure but loses value structure. Nuance: The Samuelson Critique (Footnote 16). Samuelson's Economics (4th ed., 1958) tries to correlate revenue flows and commodity flows via an inter-related system of 'supply-demand markets.' But — Mandel's polemical observation — in Samuelson's scheme, \"the public buys consumer goods while selling land, labour and capital goods (i.e. factors of production) to business ! 'Business' in turn buys land, labour and capital from 'the public' and sells consumer goods to it.\" Samuelson \"does not seem to have noticed that, under capitalism, 'the public' (i.e. the mass of consumers) does not own 'capital goods' (i.e. raw materials and equipment) and that these are sold by certain 'businesses' to others.\" In Samuelson's system, capital goods are \"sold\" without having been produced . Mandel concludes: Marx's schemas are not only more rigorous but more realistic. Nuance: Oskar Lange (Footnote 17). Lange's Theory of Reproduction and Accumulation (1969) abstracts from the dual flow of commodities and money and assumes a relationship of \"pure barter\" between the two departments. This is another error of the same family. The Core Purpose of Volume 2. Mandel now formulates what he believes is Marx's principal aim: to show that the correlation between value-structure of output and income-structure is not automatic ; that congruence depends upon \"certain exact proportions and structures, both of exchange-values and of use-values\"; that \"wages never buy machines under capitalism\"; and that these exact proportions \"are extremely difficult to realize in the actual practice of capitalism.\" Joan Robinson. Mandel's polemic against Joan Robinson deserves detailed treatment. Robinson reproached Marx for having \"failed to realize how much the orthodox theory stands and falls with Say's Law and set himself the task of discovering a theory of crises which would apply to a world in which Say's Law was fulfilled, as well as the theory which arises when Say's Law is exploded.\" Concept: Say's Law. The classical proposition that supply creates its own demand — that total production automatically generates sufficient purchasing power to buy total output. Under Say's Law, general glut is impossible; only partial, sectoral imbalances can occur. If Marx accepted Say's Law, his reproduction schemas would be a system of automatic equilibration — precisely the \"neo-harmonicist\" reading Mandel will attack later. Mandel's riposte: Robinson herself, \"following Keynes's concept of 'effective demand', fails to realize how much Marx's theory of the commodity as a unity-and-contradiction of use-value and exchange-value not only underpins his concept of the necessary fluctuation of supply and demand at a macro-economic level, but actually intertwines it with his theory of income distribution (demand distribution) in capitalist society.\" Under capitalism, income distribution has a class structure determined by the structure of the mode of production, governed in the medium term by the class interests of the capitalists. Therefore: \"Any increase in 'effective demand' which, instead of increasing the rate of profit, causes it to decline will never lead to a 'boom' under capitalism.\" This basic truth — Mandel notes — was understood by Ricardo as well as Marx, though not by many latter-day Keynesians. This is a crucial theoretical move. Robinson separates Marx from Ricardo by claiming Marx accepted Say's Law in one form and needed a crisis theory for a world where Say's Law held. Mandel rejoins by allying Marx with Ricardo on the primacy of profitability over effective demand. The class-determined structure of demand is the mediation that Robinson misses. Equilibrium as Exception. Mandel now articulates the central theorem of the whole Introduction: Marx \"never sought to prove that these proportions are automatically and constantly guaranteed by the 'invisible hand' of market forces.\" On the contrary, he insisted that these proportions are (i) difficult to realize, (ii) impossible permanently to retain, and (iii) \"automatically upset by those same forces that bring them occasionally into being.\" Footnote 19 quotes Volume 2 (pp. 570–71): \"The fact that the production of commodities is the general form of capitalist production already implies that money plays a role, not just as means of circulation, but also as money capital within the circulation sphere, and gives rise to certain conditions for normal exchange that are peculiar to this mode of production, i.e. conditions for the normal course of reproduction, whether simple or on an expanded scale, which turn into an equal number of conditions for an abnormal course, possibilities of crisis, since, on the basis of the spontaneous pattern of this production, this balance is itself an accident.\" The phrase \"this balance is itself an accident\" is the linchpin. It is a Marxian notion of equilibrium as statistical rarity, not gravitational center. Mandel will use this to argue that \"the reproduction schemas show that equilibrium, not to speak of equilibrated growth, is the exception and not the rule under capitalism: that disproportions are far more frequent than proportionality, and that growth, being essentially uneven, inevitably produces the breakdown of growth — contracted reproduction or crisis.\" The Dual Flow. Mandel now specifies what a schema actually represents: \"a combined dual flow — a flow of value produced in the process of production, and a flow of money (money revenue and money capital) unleashed in the process of circulation in order to realize the value of the commodities produced.\" The schemas are not barter. Before department I's capitalists or workers can obtain goods, they must \"prove themselves to have sufficient purchasing power to buy them from department II at their value.\" The Legitimate Role of \"New Money.\" Two crucial caveats. First, the difficulty cannot be solved by \"some legerdemain such as the sudden introduction ex nihilo of additional sources of purchasing power.\" If new money sources appear — and they do play a key role — they \"must be organically connected with the problem under examination,\" i.e. demonstrably co-existent with the process of production and circulation. This is Mandel defending Marx against the charge that the schemas assume exogenous money. Second (footnote 20): Volume 2, like Volume 1, operates under \"Capital in General.\" Therefore \"prices of production play no part, and calculations are strictly value calculations.\" This is critical because it undercuts the objection (later raised by Donald Harris and others) that the schemas require unrealistic assumptions. The Law of Value and the Schemas. Mandel insists: the dual nature of the schemas \"in no way circumvents or contradicts the operation of the law of value.\" The law establishes that the quantity and quality of value produced — both of each individual commodity and of the total — is independent of use-value. But use-value remains a necessary precondition : \"A good which nobody wants to buy because it fulfils no need cannot be sold, and therefore has no exchange-value. Labour expended in its production is socially wasted, not socially necessary labour.\" The same applies at the aggregate level: a use-value structure of output — a given quantity of raw materials, equipment, consumer goods — is a material and social precondition of successful reproduction. But \"the use-value of these commodities will only be realized if their market prices can be matched, that is, if they can be bought.\" Mandel's example is stark: \"Millions can — and do! — starve under capitalism, even though all the food they need is there, because they lack the purchasing power to buy it.\" And equilibrium is attained \"only if these commodities are broadly speaking sold at their value, that is to say, if the surplus-value produced by the working class is realized in the form of profit. And this is by no means assured under capitalism.\" The Aggregate Demand Conditions. Mandel now formulates the aggregate condition separately from the interdepartmental exchange condition. \"The sum total of output of both departments must be equal to, not smaller or larger than, the total demand generated by expanded reproduction.\" Under simple reproduction: $$I = I_c + II_c$$ $$II = I_v + I_s + II_v + II_s$$ Interpretation: Total department I output (the total mass of means of production produced) must equal the constant capital consumed in both departments. Total department II output (consumer goods) must equal wages plus surplus-value spent on consumption in both departments. Under expanded reproduction: $$I = I_c + \\Delta I_c + II_c + \\Delta II_c$$ $$II = I_v + \\Delta I_v + (I_s - \\Delta I_c - \\Delta I_v) + II_v + \\Delta II_v + (II_s - \\Delta II_c - \\Delta II_v)$$ Interpretation: Now the department I output must equal constant capital used up plus additional constant capital needed (in both departments). The department II output must equal wages plus additional wages (the additional variable capital to hire new workers) plus the surplus-value that is not accumulated but consumed. Observe the structure: at both levels, the mass of means of production equals the mass of means of production used; the mass of consumer goods equals the demand for consumer goods . The schematic identity of supply and demand is a requirement , not an automatic outcome. 2.4 The Significance of Marx's Reproduction Schemas Mandel now breaks down the conditions of proportionality in the two-department system. The classification is: Department I = means of production; Department II = consumer goods. Simple reproduction condition (formulated by Marx himself): $$I_v + I_s = II_c$$ In words: the value of the wages plus surplus-value of department I, which its workers and capitalists will spend on consumer goods, must equal the constant capital of department II, which department II must reconstitute by purchasing means of production from department I. The exchange is thus: department I's variable capital + surplus against department II's constant capital. Expanded reproduction condition (derived by Otto Bauer and Bukharin; present in Volume 2 but not explicitly formulated by Marx): $$I_v + \\Delta I_v + I_{s_a} = II_c + \\Delta II_c$$ Or, in Mandel's more general notation using the a/β/γ division of surplus-value (footnote 22): a : unproductively consumed by the capitalists β : accumulated as constant capital γ : accumulated as variable capital So for each department, $s = s_a + s_\\beta + s_\\gamma$. The expanded reproduction condition becomes: $$I_v + I_{s_a} + I_{s_\\gamma} = II_c + II_{s_\\beta}$$ This reads: Department I's variable capital (I_v) plus the consumed portion of its surplus (I_sa) plus the portion of its surplus accumulated as variable capital (I_sγ) — i.e. department I's total demand for consumer goods — must equal department II's constant capital (II_c) plus the portion of department II's surplus accumulated as additional constant capital (II_sβ) — i.e. department II's total demand for means of production. The Dual Meaning of the Proportionality Conditions. In conformity with the dual nature of the schemas, these conditions have two meanings. (a) Exchange-Value Meaning: The exchange-value of goods sold by department I to department II must equal the value of goods sold by department II to department I. Otherwise there emerges an unsaleable surplus in at least one department. (b) Use-Value Meaning: The specific use-value of the commodities produced in both departments must correspond to their mutual needs. The purchasing power in the hands of workers producing producer goods must encounter actual consumer goods equivalent to that sum of wages. \"Under capitalism, workers are not supposed to spend their money on any commodities other than consumer goods.\" The Commodity, Non-Barter Nature of the Schemas. Mandel stresses the dual flow between departments. When department I sells raw materials and equipment to department II, commodities flow I→II, money flows II→I. The question: \"It has to be determined where that money initially came from.\" Conversely, when department II sells consumer goods to workers of department I, commodities flow II→I, money flows I→II. This two-way traffic in commodities and money is what distinguishes the schemas from any barter model. The Schema as Model. Mandel now transitions from \"conceptual tool\" to \"model corresponding to a social structure.\" He argues: From a purely technical point of view, the two-department schema is \"the most elementary conceptual tool — an extreme simplification.\" Exchange requires at least two private capitals independent of each other. This is the underlying assumption. The schema can be extended: three departments (e.g. gold as department III), four departments (gold and luxury goods), five (splitting department I into means of production producing means of production and means of production producing consumer goods), or seven (further splitting department I's sub-departments into raw materials and machinery). Step by step, \"we would approach an inter-branch model reflecting the actual structure of a modern capitalist industrialized economy.\" The Nine-Department Schema (Footnote 24). This is the most elaborate extension Mandel sketches: A : raw materials used in the production of means of production B : equipment employed in the production of means of production C : raw materials used for the production of mass consumer goods D : equipment employed in the production of mass consumer goods E : raw materials used for the production of luxury goods F : equipment employed in the production of luxury goods G : mass consumer goods H : luxury goods (and other goods not entering into the reproduction process — e.g. weapons) I : gold With this notation, Mandel can express a crucial point about multi-department systems: department C (raw materials for consumer goods) could have a \"surplus\" in interchange with department E (raw materials for luxury goods) but a \"deficit\" with department B (equipment for producer goods). In such a case, equilibrium is possible provided all surpluses and deficits cancel out proportionately for each department, and provided each department realizes total value and disposes of sufficient purchasing power. The Critical Difference Between Two-Department and Multi-Department Schemas: The two-department schema necessitates equivalence of exchange-values between the two departments , whereas the multi-department schema does not. In multi-department schemas, each department need only balance its overall position, and the exchange-value equivalences need not hold bilaterally. This matters enormously for understanding capitalism's actual structure of interdependence. Footnote 23 provides historical context: Department III was first used by Tugan-Baranowski ( Studien zur Theorie und Geschichte der Handelskrisen in England , 1901) and von Bortkiewicz for luxury goods or gold. Unknown to them, Marx had himself used a four-department schema in the Grundrisse (p. 441), introducing separate departments for raw materials and machinery and dividing means of consumption between workers' consumer goods and luxury goods (\"surplus products\") for capitalists. Footnote 25 addresses a controversy: Rudolf Hickel criticized Mandel's use of a \"department III\" as being justified by the state buying weapons or by weapons being \"waste.\" Mandel replies: \"This critique is altogether unfounded. The objective basis of department III lies in the fact that it includes all commodities not entering into the reproduction process (with the possible exception of monetary gold, in a four-department schema).\" The point is structural, not moralistic. Weapons, luxury goods, and gold all share this characteristic: they do not contribute material elements to reproduction. The Two-Department Schema as a Social Structure. This is Mandel's most philosophically ambitious passage in the section. The two departments are not arbitrary — they correspond to \"the essential character of human production in general.\" Man cannot survive without a material metabolism with nature; he cannot realize that metabolism without using tools. His material production will always consist of at least tools and means of subsistence . The two departments of Marx's schemas are \"nothing other than the specific capitalist form of this general division of human production, in so far as they (1) take the generalized form of commodities, and (2) assume that the workers (direct producers) do not and cannot purchase that part of the commodity mountain which consists of tools and raw materials.\" This is a crucial theorization. The two-department schema is thus derived from an anthropological constant (production requires both instruments and consumption goods), but expressed under the historically specific condition that labour-power sellers cannot buy means of production. This dual grounding gives the schema its enormous generality while preserving its historical specificity. The Dual Flow Detailed. Mandel now walks through the dual flows. Simple Reproduction. In department I, workers buy commodities from department II equal to their wages; capitalists buy equal to their profits. Both flows are continuous — \"workers and capitalists alike have to eat every day\" — regardless of whether department I's commodities have been sold. Therefore, even simple reproduction requires the prior existence of money capital and money reserves (for revenue expenditure) in the hands of the capitalist class, over and above the value of productive capital (footnote 26, referencing pp. 548–9). This is a point Marx develops in Volume 2, Chapter 17. The mechanism then unfolds as follows. With the money received from the sale of their commodities, department II's capitalists buy means of production from department I. This money, after mediating purchase-and-sale within department I, reconstitutes the initial money capital and money-revenue reserve with which the whole turnover can recommence. Similarly, within department II, capitalists sell consumer goods to their own workers, thereby immediately reconstituting their own variable capital. They sell consumer and luxury goods to all industrialists active within department II, thus realizing the surplus-value contained in the sum total of consumer goods produced. Expanded Reproduction. Workers and capitalists of department I buy consumer goods from department II equal to $I_v + I_{s_a}$. With this money, department II's capitalists buy means of production from department I to reconstitute their own constant capital used up. (Footnote 27: following the equilibrium formula $II_c + II_{s_\\beta} = I_v + I_{s_a} + I_{s_\\gamma}$, it is clear that II_c may be equal to, smaller, or greater than I_v + I_sa, depending on the relation of II_sβ to I_sγ.) Now department I's capitalists have the necessary means — if required, by drawing further on a reserve of money capital — to mediate the circulation of c within their own department and to hire additional workers, who will buy additional consumer goods (equal to $I_{s_\\gamma}$) from department II. Department II's capitalists thereby acquire the purchasing power to buy from department I the additional means of production necessary for their own expanded reproduction ($II_{s_\\beta} = \\Delta II_c$), while the sale of consumer goods to workers and capitalists within department II operates as described. Finally, with the further means obtained by the sale of $\\Delta II_c$ to department II, department I's capitalists can complete their own expanded reproduction, mediating the sale of $\\Delta I_c$ within their department (as well as the purchase of the equivalent of $\\Delta I_v$ from department II, if not already fully covered). The order of exchanges is crucial. Mandel insists that each move generates the purchasing power for the next, in a chain of interdependent transactions. This is not barter — it is a sequential circuit with money operating as the connective tissue between productive and consumptive moments. 2.5 Use and Misuse of the Reproduction Schemas Mandel now pivots to the reception of the schemas — and here his polemical intent becomes explicit. He identifies \"one of the most paradoxical forms of abuse\": \"utilization of them as 'proof' that capitalism could grow harmoniously and unrestrictedly 'if only' the correct 'proportions' between the departments (the 'conditions of equilibrium') were maintained.\" The authors responsible overlooked Marx's basic assumption — \"that the very structure of the capitalist mode of production, as well as its laws of motion, imply that the 'conditions of equilibrium' are inevitably destroyed; that 'equilibrium' and 'harmonious growth' are marginal exceptions to (or long-term averages of) normal conditions of disequilibrium ('overshooting' between the two departments) and uneven growth.\" This is the \"neo-harmonicist\" misreading. It has numerous sources and genealogies — from Tugan-Baranowski to Hilferding's \"general cartel\" and Bukharin's state capitalism, to the Soviet textbook tradition, to Kautsky's later writings. Mandel attacks it at the level of fundamental logical error , not merely political disagreement. The Two Reasons for Impossibility. Under capitalism, both the dynamics of value determination and the \"non-determination of consumer expenditure\" make exact proportions impossible. Reason 1: Changing Values Under Technical Revolution. The nature of expanded reproduction under capitalism implies that production takes place \"not only on a broader scale, but also under changed technological conditions.\" Constant revolutions in technique and cost of production are a \"basic characteristic of the system which Marx underlined much more sharply than any of his contemporaries (including the admirers and sycophants of capitalism).\" These revolutions entail that \"the value of commodities as a social datum is subject to periodic change.\" Therefore: \"values at input level do not automatically determine values at output level. Only after a certain interval will it be shown whether a fraction of the 'inputs' have been socially wasted.\" This is a profound theoretical point. Even the numerical coefficients in the schemas — the amounts of c , v , s — are ex post realized values, not ex ante planned quantities. The law of value periodically reasserts itself through the devalorization of capital that accompanies crisis. \"Neither the subjective will of 'monopolies' or 'the state', nor the cleverness of neo-Keynesian planners, can prevent the assertion of the law of value where private property and competition hold sway.\" Long-term shifts in commodity values lead to \"a redistribution of living labour inputs among different branches of production (and, ultimately, a redistribution of means of production as well).\" Reason 2: The Impossibility of Forecasting Consumer Expenditure. The avoidance of crises requires proportionality not only between departments, but also between output and \"final consumption\" — i.e. consumption by the mass of wage and salary earners, who in modern industrialized societies form (with families) more than 80 percent of consumers. This is impossible for two reasons: First, \"the one freedom which cannot normally be taken away from the workers is the freedom to spend their wages as they wish — and there is no way in which it can be forecast with complete accuracy how they will do this (even if a prediction is 95 per cent correct, that could still leave a 5 per cent surplus of unsaleable consumer goods, which is enough to start an avalanche).\" Second, \"the laws of motion of capitalism have the inherent tendency to develop the capacity of production (including the production of consumer goods) beyond the limits within which the mode of production confines the purchasing power of those condemned to sell their labour-power.\" Footnote 28 quotes Marx: Grundrisse p. 414, and Capital Volume 3, Chapter 15,3: \"the proportionality of the particular branches of production presents itself as a constant process through disproportionality.\" The Theoretical Task Beyond \"Disproportionality.\" Mandel is careful to insist that mere demonstration of inherent disproportion is insufficient: \"it must also discover the precise mechanisms which relate that periodic disequilibrium to the basic laws of motion of capitalism.\" This is a methodological demand — the schemas must be complemented by a theory of trade cycle, not replaced by them. The Misuse in \"Socialist Planning.\" Mandel now turns to the Soviet and Eastern European debates. He does not deny that the schemas may be useful instruments for studying inter-department structure in various societies — but the character of the tool must be understood. The schemas refer to commodity production and to dual flows of commodities and money incomes . To extend their use to societies which have transcended generalized commodity production — where means of production are use-values distributed by the state according to plan, rather than commodities sold on the basis of their value — leads to \"an accumulation of paradoxes, of which the authors are generally not even conscious.\" (Footnote 29: exceptions are means of production sold to agricultural cooperatives and small handicraftsmen, or illegally channelled into the black market.) Maurice Dobb. Mandel's critique of Dobb is worth reconstructing. In the 1950s, Dobb participated in a \"great debate\" around Stalin's \"law of the priority development of the means of production under socialism.\" Dobb \"proved\" (Mandel uses scare quotes) that an increased rate of growth of consumer goods in the future was \"impossible\" unless the present rate of growth of department I was higher than that of department II. Mandel's response: Dobb's argument is \"completely spurious\" — all his calculations show is that the value of consumer goods produced cannot grow at an increasing rate after x years unless the value of department I immediately rises at a faster rate. The normative content smuggled into this tautological calculation is problematic: \"a policy which sacrifices the consumption of four generations of workers and their families merely to increase the rate of growth of that consumption starting with the fifth generation has nothing in common with an 'ideal socialist norm'.\" Crucially, \"neither an individual worker nor the working class itself in a post-capitalist society (not to speak of a socialist commonwealth) is interested in a constantly rising rate of growth of the value of consumer goods. On the contrary, they are concerned with reducing that 'value' as much as possible by raising the productivity of labour, and with the withering away of commodity production and market economy.\" Therefore, to treat maximization of capitalist commodity-value as identical with socialist satisfaction of needs is \"not only a grave theoretical error, but also a disastrous political and social miscalculation.\" The \"Structural Law of Socialism.\" Worse still were the 1960s attempts (P. Mstislawski, Helmut Koziolek, Rudolf Reichenberg) to revive a \"structural law\" of socialism according to which department I must expand faster than department II. These \"abstract from the value nature of the reproduction schemas, and assume that optimum satisfaction of social needs implies both continuous, unlimited expansion of the output of means of production, and the allocation of an even higher fraction of the total labour potential of society to the creation of material producer goods (as against social services dealing with health, education, artistic creation, 'pure' scientific research, child-care, etc.).\" Mandel's verdict: \"None of these assumptions can be scientifically proven or justified. Indeed, their apologetic function — as a straightforward rationalization of existing practice in the USSR and the 'Peoples' Democracies' — is obvious to any critical observer.\" Oskar Lange and Bronislaw Minc. Mandel grants that both — though not clarifying the distinction between capitalist and socialist reproduction schemas — \"correctly demonstrated that increased productivity of labour and technical progress do not necessarily require department I to grow more quickly than department II; nor do they imply increased current outlay on means of production per unit currently (annually) produced.\" Rosa Luxemburg's Partial Contribution. Luxemburg \"well understood that the form of the reproduction schemas applies only to capitalist commodity and value production, and that the laws of motion corresponding to that form can have no validity in non-capitalist societies.\" But \"even she erred by attaching to the 'equilibrium proportions' derived from the schemas an a-historical, eternal validity which they do not and cannot possess.\" Footnote 33 provides the magnificent Luxembourg quote: \"In every planned system of production it is, above all, the relation between all labour, past and present, and the means of production (between v+s and c, according to our formula), or the relation between the aggregate of necessary consumer goods (again, in the terms of our formula, v+a) and c which are subjected to regulation. Under capitalist conditions, on the other hand, all social labour necessary for the maintenance of the inanimate means of production and also of living labour power is treated as one entity, as capital, in contrast with the surplus labour that has been performed, i.e. with the surplus value s. The relation between these two quantities c and (v+s) is a palpably real, objective relationship of capitalist society: it is the average rate of profit.\" From Value Reproduction to Socialist Reproduction. Mandel then asks: what happens when surplus-value is replaced by a \"socially appropriated surplus product\"? The equilibrium formula takes a new form expressing a different social goal of reproduction. The key philosophical point: surplus-value is not simply a part of total value of commodities produced, nor just a fraction of newly produced value (national income). \"It is also the goal of the capitalist production process.\" As such, it is \"much more than a mere symbol in a reproduction schema.\" For Marx, the schemas refer to the reproduction of quantified use-value and exchange-value in a given proportion, but they also express the reproduction of capitalist relations of production themselves (footnote 34, referencing Capital Volume 3, Chapter 51). All that is implied in the formula $I_v + I_s = II_c$: $s$ is what makes the relation capitalist. Once $s$ disappears under socialism, everything changes. When commodity production has withered away and the concept of surplus labour is reducible to that of social service and economic growth, the meaning of \"equilibrium\" derived from the proportionality formula is \"subject to a fundamental transformation.\" When proportionality is upset in a commodity-producing society, both use-values and exchange-values decline, because both are inextricably linked. Under socialism, \"no such inexorable nexus survives — not even as a necessary proportion (in the form of an 'eternal law') between labour inputs and use-value inputs.\" Marx's Categorical Statement. Here Mandel makes a stunning assertion: in Capital Volume 2, Marx goes so far as to state categorically that \"after the abolition of capitalism, there will be 'constant relative over-production' of equipment, raw materials and foodstuffs. 'Over-production of this kind', he says, 'is equivalent to control by the society over the objective means of its own reproduction.'\" This is one of the most important passages in the entire Introduction, and it has radical implications. Under capitalism, \"over-production\" is a crisis symptom; under socialism, it is a form of social control . The Work-Load Thought-Experiment. Mandel offers a thought experiment: imagine a society which, having reached a certain level of consumption, consciously decides to give absolute priority to one goal — reduction of the work load. Its efforts would be concentrated on assuring the production and distribution of an \"ideal\" package of use-values with fewer and fewer labour inputs. There would still be \"simple reproduction\" at the level of use-values, achieved with, say, a reduction in man-days of 4% per annum (if population increased by 1% and labour productivity by 5%). To call this a situation of \"contracted reproduction\" would be wrong : because a socialist society would calculate essentially with use-values, and because in Marx's schema \"contracted reproduction\" is logically connected with crisis, interrupted equilibrium, declining living standards, whereas the conditions described involve \"smooth continuity of material production and reproduction, stable living standards and absence of any kind of crisis.\" The Essential Reconciliation. Mandel concludes: this does not mean socialist planning could do without specific proportions in the flow of labour, means of production and consumer goods. \"Such proportional allocation of resources is indeed the very essence of socialist planning.\" Means only that there is a qualitative and quantitative difference between value calculations and calculations in labour time — between (on one hand) the dynamics of appropriation and accumulation of surplus-value, and (on the other hand) rising efficiency (labour productivity) achieved in successive phases of production and measured in quantities of use-values produced during a fixed length of time. Footnote 36 (Engels's Anti-Dühring ) provides the fullest statement of the socialist calculation argument: \"From the moment when society enters into possession of the means of production... the labour of each individual, however varied its specifically useful character may be, becomes at the start and directly social labour. The quantity of social labour contained in a product need not then be established in a roundabout way; daily experience shows in a direct way how much of it is required on the average. Society can simply calculate how many hours of labour are contained in a steam-engine, a bushel of wheat... It would never occur to it still to express the quantities of labour put into the products, quantities which it will then know directly and in their absolute amounts, in a third product, in a measure which, besides, is only relative, fluctuating, inadequate...\" And Marx's 'association of free men' passage from Volume 1 — labour-time plays a double part: apportionment according to a definite social plan maintains correct proportion; measure of individual's part in common labour and share in product for individual consumption. Footnote 36's Reichenberg Polemic. Reichenberg's confusion: first, he includes the material tools of the service sector in a department II of consumer goods. Then he speaks of an \"intensification of expanded reproduction\" from the \"scientific-technical revolution,\" expressing itself in the fact that \"if the difference between (I_v+I_s) and II_c remains the same, a process of increased accumulation is possible.\" Mandel's reductio : \"But he fails to specify the object of this accumulation. Is it the value of II_c? Obviously that would be nonsense. The difference between two value quantities cannot change if the quantities themselves do not change. Perhaps it is accumulation of use-values! No doubt. But surely an increase in the mass of raw materials and tools... produced by a given quantity of socially necessary labour is the very definition of an increase in labour productivity. And, at the same time, Reichenberg implies that the value of these goods (and therefore the dynamics of expanded reproduction in value terms) has not changed!\" Reichenberg thus confuses use-value accumulation with value accumulation. Bronislaw Minc's Assertion and Mandel's Rebuttal. Minc asserts: \"The basic theses of Marx's theory of expanded reproduction, as expressed in the schemas, are entirely valid under socialism.\" Mandel's response: contrary to the explicit theory of Marx and Engels, such 'socialist production' would remain \"generalized commodity production, i.e. generalized production of value.\" The question then: \"what kind of intrinsic 'law' of raising surplus labour would then be incorporated into these 'socialist production relations'?\" For Marx, \"distinctly states that such a law underlies the schemas of expanded reproduction referring to the production of surplus-value.\" (Footnote 38 quotes Volume 3, Chapter 13 on the mass of surplus-value growing along with a falling rate of profit, and Volume 3, Chapter 50 on stripping wages and surplus-value of their specifically capitalist character — concluding the difference in form implies a difference in quantities, \"especially in those dynamic quantities which are growth trends.\") 2.6 Productive and Unproductive Labour Mandel here enters one of the most contested debates in Marxist economics. His thesis is twofold: (i) Marx's reproduction theory is firmly rooted in the labour theory of value — the schemas are based on labour-time as numeraire and measure \"the distribution (and movement) of the labour force available to society among different departments and branches of material production\"; and (ii) there is a systematic distinction between labour productive from the point of view of the individual capitalist and labour productive for capital as a whole . Value = Abstract Social Labour. This is the foundational claim. Value is \"abstract social labour.\" The schemas measure the distribution of living labour across branches, and therefore the productive/unproductive distinction is not a peripheral issue but is built into the very structure of the reproduction analysis. The Morishima Confusion. Michio Morishima ( Marx's Economics , 1973) has devoted effort to rehabilitating Marx as a forerunner of aggregation techniques but continues to detect a contradiction between a macro-economic theory of value based on aggregation and a micro-economic labour theory of value. Mandel rejects this: Morishima's subtle distinction between Marx's \"two\" labour theories of value \"is based upon a simple conceptual confusion.\" The argument: For Marx, value and production of value are eminently social qualities, referring to relations between men , not \"physical\" attributes adhering to things. When Marx says the value of a commodity is the embodiment of human labour expended in its production, and when he says its value equals the socially necessary labour contained within it, he is \"not making two different statements, but simply repeating the same thesis.\" The value of a commodity is determined only by that portion of labour spent corresponding to the social average — average productivity and average socially recognized need. This is the definition of socially necessary labour . Labour expended but not recognized by society is \"not productive of value for the owner of that commodity.\" The Aggregate Reality. Because value refers ultimately to the distribution and redistribution of the total available labour-power engaged in production, \"that macro-economic aggregate is a basic economic reality, a basic 'fact of life'.\" The example: \"If five million workers work 2,000 hours a year in material production, the total value product is ten billion hours, independently of whether the socially recognized value of each individual commodity is equal to, or larger or smaller than, the actual number of labour hours expended in its production.\" The Cancellation Argument. \"It follows that if the value of a given commodity is less than the labour actually spent on its production, then there must be at least one other commodity whose value is greater than the quantity of labour actually embodied in it. Social recognition of labour expenditure and actual labour expenditure can differ only for individual commodities, not for the total mass.\" This is a subtle and powerful point. It is essentially a conservation law: over-valuation and under-valuation must cancel out at the aggregate. (Footnote 40 quotes Capital Volume 3, Chapter 10: \"the market value of the entire mass, as governed by the average values, is equal to the sum of its individual values... Those producing at the worst extreme then have to sell their commodities below their individual value, while those at the best extreme sell theirs above it.\") Morishima is thus right to stress that \"in the last analysis, and for the capitalist mode of production (as distinct from petty commodity production), Marx's law of value is fundamentally an aggregate, macro-economic concept\" — but wrong to see this as contradicting the micro-level labour theory. Both are aspects of the same thesis. Productive vs. Unproductive Labour: The General Framework. The distinction has direct implications for the reproduction schemas: as value schemas, they express only value production and automatically exclude economic activities that are not productive of value. The question, then, is: what precisely is \"productive labour\"? The Marxian Complication. Mandel admits that \"the solution of this problem was made more difficult by Marx himself.\" There are \"undeniable differences — if only of nuance\" between the long section of Theories of Surplus-Value on productive/unproductive labour and key passages of Capital (especially Volume 2). The most striking example: commercial agents and travellers are classified as productive workers in the Theories but as unproductive workers in Capital Volumes 2 and 3. (Footnote 43 notes further contradictions even within Part 1 of the Theories : p. 157 classifies an actor working for a capitalist as productive; p. 172 classifies the actor's labour for a theatrical entrepreneur as unproductive because \"their purchaser cannot sell them to the public in the form of commodities but only in the form of the action itself.\") The Preliminary Distinction. Mandel's methodological caution is crucial: Marx is not passing moral judgement, nor employing criteria of social (or human) usefulness, nor presenting a-historically objective classification. \"The object of his analysis is the capitalist mode of production, and he simply determines what is productive and what is unproductive for the functioning, the rationale of that system, and that system alone.\" The examples are stark: A doctor's labour is indispensable for any human society — but it is unproductive labour from the point of view of the production or expansion of capital. The production of dum-dum bullets, hard drugs, or pornographic magazines is useless or harmful to human interests — but if such commodities find customers, the surplus-value embodied in them is realized, capital is reproduced and expanded, and labour expended on them is productive labour . This is the socially determined, historically relativized concept of productive labour. The First Definition (Theories of Surplus-Value level): \"All labour which is exchanged against capital and not against revenue, i.e. all labour which enriches one or several capitalists, enabling them to appropriate a portion of the total mass of surplus-value produced by the total mass of value-producing wage-labour.\" This could be called \"labour productive from the point of view of the individual capitalist(s).\" All wage-labour engaged by capitalist enterprise — as opposed to labour functioning for private households — falls into this category. This is the level at which the Theories stops. The Second Definition (Capital Volume 2 and 3 level): Now, \"from the point of view of the capitalist mode of production in its totality , and especially from that of the growth or accumulation of capital, Marx now distinguishes labour productive for capital as a whole from labour productive for the individual capitalist .\" For capital as a whole, only that labour is productive which increases the total mass of surplus-value . All wage-labour which enables an individual capitalist to appropriate a fraction of the total mass of surplus-value without adding to that mass may be \"productive\" for the commercial, financial, or service-sector capitalist whom it allows to participate in the general sharing of the cake. \"But from the point of view of capital as a whole it is unproductive, because it does not augment the total size of the cake.\" This distinction is decisive . Only commodity production makes possible the creation of value and surplus-value. Only within the realm of commodity production is productive labour performed. \"No new surplus-value can be added in the sphere of circulation and exchange, not to speak of the stock exchange or the bank counter; all that happens there is the redistribution or reapportionment of previously created surplus-value.\" Manuscript Dating. Mandel notes (crucially for the history of the concept) that the relevant Volume 2 passages were drawn by Engels from Manuscripts II and IV, written in 1870 or between 1867 and 1870 — after the Theories of Surplus-Value of 1861–63, and even after the rough manuscript of Volume 3. \"May therefore be considered to express Marx's definitive views on the question.\" The implication: where the Theories and Volume 2 conflict, Volume 2 wins. Four Additional Problems. Problem 1: \"Immaterial Goods.\" Concerts, circus acts, prostitution, teaching, etc. In the Theories , Marx tends to classify these as commodities if produced by wage-earners for capitalist entrepreneurs. But in Volume 2, he \"insists strongly and repeatedly on the correlation between use-values embodied in commodities through a labour process which acts upon and transforms nature, and the production of value and surplus-value.\" He provides a general formula implying the exclusion of wage-labour in \"personal service industries\" from productive labour: \"If we have a function which, although in and for itself unproductive, is nevertheless a necessary moment of reproduction, then when this is transformed, through the division of labour, from the secondary activity of many into the exclusive activity of a few, into their special business, this does not change the character of the function itself.\" If this is true of commercial travellers or book-keepers, it \"obviously applies all the more to teachers or cleaning services.\" Mandel's summary: \"The definition of productive labour as commodity-producing labour, combining concrete and abstract labour (i.e. combining creation of use-values and production of exchange-values) logically excludes 'non-material goods' from the sphere of value production.\" This conclusion is \"intimately bound up with a basic thesis of Capital : production is, for humanity, the necessary mediation between nature and society; there can be no production without (concrete) labour, no concrete labour without appropriation and transformation of material objects.\" Footnote 48 records the debate. Nagels and Bischoff adopt Mandel's position; Ian Gough supports the opposite view, based on Capital Volume 1, p. 644, where Marx explicitly includes wage-earners working for private capital (such as teachers) in the realm of productive labour. Mandel's response is that this passage, \"like several in Theories of Surplus-Value , only indicates that Marx had not yet completed his articulation of the contradictory determinants of 'productive labour'.\" The two determinants: (i) exchange against capital rather than revenue; (ii) participation in the process of commodity production (unity-and-contradiction of labour process and valorization, use-value and exchange-value, concrete and abstract labour). Gough, in other words, chooses the wrong moment of Marx's development. Footnote 50 (Gouverneur): Jacques Gouverneur attempts to extend Marx's formulation into \"transformation of nature or the world ,\" where \"or the world\" means \"or society.\" Since wage-earning teachers \"transform society\" without \"transforming nature,\" the implications are obvious — and, Mandel implies, absurd. Transport Industry — The Case Study. Here Mandel offers his key concrete application. Marx's argument in Volume 2 for classifying the transport industry in the realm of value-production rather than circulation: \"The quantity of products is not increased by their transport. The change in their natural properties that may be effected by transport is also, certain exceptions apart, not an intended useful effect, but rather an unavoidable evil. But the use-value of things is only realized in their consumption, and their consumption may make a change of location necessary, and thus, in addition, the additional production process of the transport industry. The productive capital invested in this industry thus adds value to the products transported.\" The Passenger Transport Problem. None of these arguments applies to carrying persons . Passenger transport is not an indispensable condition of the realization of use-values and adds no new value to any commodity . It is \"a personal service on which individuals (whether capitalists or workers) spend their own revenue.\" Whether organized on the basis of wage-labour or not, the passenger transport industry \"can no more be considered as increasing the total mass of social value and surplus-value than can wage-labour employed in the fields of commerce, banking or insurance.\" The Contrast. Marx's argument in Volume 2, Chapter 6, §3, is in \"striking contrast\": while explicitly stating that transportation of persons by capitalist enterprise does not create commodities or use-values of any kind, he notes that it is nevertheless a \"productive branch,\" even though the \"useful effect\" (Nutzeffekt) is only consumable during the production process itself. Mandel flags this as a tension in Marx's text. General Rule: Service Industries. Mandel proposes: \"as a general rule, all forms of wage-labour which exteriorize themselves in and thus add value to a product (materials) are creative of surplus-value and hence productive for capitalism as a whole.\" Productive: - Manufacturing and mining - Transportation of goods - \"Public service\" industries — production and transport of water or energy (gas, electricity) - Selling of meals in restaurants - Building and sale of houses and offices as well as provision of material for constructing them - Agriculture Unproductive: - Letting of apartment or hotel rooms - Service of transporting persons in buses, underground systems or trains - Medical, educational, or recreational wage-labour which is not objectivized outside the worker (sale of specific forms of labour rather than commodities) - Work of commercial or banking clerks - Employees of insurance companies or market research firms Television Illustration: Production of television sets or films (including copies) is commodity production — productive labour. But the hiring-out of completed films or the renting of a single television set to successive customers is not productive. Similarly, wage-labour in making advertising films is productive, whereas \"the cajoling of potential clients to purchase or order such films is as unproductive as the labour of commercial representatives in general.\" Problem 2: Demarcation Between Production and Circulation. Volume 2 \"leaves no room for doubt\": only that labour which either adds to or is indispensable for the realization and conservation of a commodity's use-value adds to the total amount of abstract social labour embodied in that commodity (i.e. is productive of value). These passages, like the rest of Volume 2, are \"but successive unfoldings of the basic analysis of the commodity — of its irreducible duality and the contradictions flowing therefrom.\" Problem 3: Labour Within the Production Process Itself. Here Marx takes \"a much less simplistic attitude than some of his latter-day disciples.\" The fundamental doctrine is that of the \"collective labourer,\" developed in the \"Results of the Immediate Process of Production\" (the appendix to Volume 1). Productive labour — as labour expended in the realm of production of commodities — is all wage-labour indispensable for that production process : not only manual labour, but also engineers, laboratory workers, overseers, and even managers and stock clerks, insofar as the physical production of a commodity would be impossible without that labour. Unproductive labour — even within the factory — includes: - Wage-labour indifferent to the specific use-value of the commodity and performed only to extort maximum surplus-value (e.g. timekeepers) - Labour to assure the defence of private property (security guards in and around a factory) - Labour linked to the particular social and juridical forms of capitalist production (lawyers employed as salaried staff by manufacturing firms) - Financial book-keepers - Additional stock-checkers made necessary by the tendency to overproduction \"None of these is productive labour for capital. It does not add value to the commodities produced (although it may be essential to the overall functioning of the capitalist system, or of bourgeois society as a whole).\" Problem 4: Petty Commodity Producers. Independent peasants and handicraftsmen produce commodities — both use-values and exchange-values — but do not directly create surplus-value (except in marginal cases). They may contribute indirectly to the mass of social surplus-value — e.g. by depressing the value of food through their cheap labour. Mandel's verdict: Marx maintained the position of the Theories of Surplus-Value — \"such strata perform labour which is neither productive nor unproductive from the point of view of the capitalist mode of production, because they operate outside its framework.\" This is an important precision: there is a third category — labour external to the capitalist relation entirely. This will become relevant in the next section on class. 2.7 Are Unproductive Labourers Part of the Proletariat? Mandel now draws out the political and sociological stakes of the productive/unproductive labour distinction. The stakes are enormous: the definition of the proletariat determines the scope of revolutionary agency. The Extremes. The narrowest position — reducing the proletariat to the group of manual industrial workers — is \"in complete contradiction with Marx's explicit definition of productive labour, and we need not dwell on it here.\" The other extreme — extending the concept to all wage and salary earners without limitation, \"including army generals and managers earning 100,000 dollars a year\" — is \"obviously absurd.\" Mandel's Definition (drawing on Marx). The defining structural characteristic of the proletariat in Marx's analysis is the socio-economic compulsion to sell one's labour-power . From this, three criteria follow: Non-ownership of means of production Lack of direct access to means of livelihood (\"the land is by no means freely accessible!\") Insufficient money to purchase the means of livelihood without more or less continuous sale of labour-power Therefore, \"all unproductive wage-labourers who are subject to the same fundamental constraints\" belong to the proletariat. This includes: - Manual industrial workers - Commercial clerks - Lower government employees - Domestic servants Excluded: All strata whose salary levels permit accumulation of capital in addition to a \"normal\" standard of living. \"Whether such accumulation actually takes place is in itself irrelevant (although monographs and statistics tend to confirm that, to a modest or sizeable degree, this social group does engage in it; this is the case especially of the so-called managers, who — notwithstanding a platitude which continues to circulate in spite of all evidence to the contrary — are part and parcel of the capitalist class, if not necessarily of its top layer of billionaires).\" Footnote 58 (Contrasting Positions). Gillman groups \"the advertising managers, the directors of public relations, the legal counsel, the tax experts, the 'sales engineers', the legislative lobbyists, their clerical assistants\" together with \"the rest (!) of the growing host of white-collar workers\" in the general category of \"third party consumers.\" Thereby tends to exclude them from the proletariat. This view influenced Paul Baran and Baran & Sweezy's Monopoly Capital . Boccara et al. ( Le Capitalisme monopoliste d'état ) explicitly exclude the \"intermediate salaried layers\" from the proletariat, reducing the latter to productive workers (workers producing surplus-value). Footnote 59 (Defenders of Mandel's Position). Extensive documentation that the broad definition was \"undoubtedly advanced by Marx and Engels and their most 'orthodox' followers: the mature (not the senile) Kautsky, Plekhanov, Lenin, Trotsky, Luxemburg et al.\" - Capital Volume 1, p. 798: unemployed sick, disabled, mutilated, widowed, elderly designated as 'pauperized sections [ Lazarusschichte ] of the working class' - Capital Volume 2 (p. 516): Marx defines the class of wage-labourers by constant compulsion to sell labour-power (p. 561 designates servants — die Bedientenklasse — as wage-labourers) - Rosa Luxemburg ( Einführung in die Nationalökonomie ): includes casually and occasionally employed workers, paupers, sick, unemployed - Trotsky ( 1905 ): includes domestic servants - Kautsky ( The Class Struggle: Erfurt Program ): includes commercial and industrial wage-earners - Plekhanov: defines proletariat as those forced to sell labour-power, extending to \"persons who possess no means of production and of circulation ... All these persons are forced by their economic position to sell their labour-power constantly or periodically.\" Lenin contested the \"and of circulation\" phrase but raised \"no essential objection to the formulation.\" The Weighty Objection. Here Mandel articulates a powerful objection that could be raised against his definition. If only productive labour produces value and thereby reproduces the equivalent of its own wages (besides creating surplus-value), then: Aren't the wages of unproductive labour paid out of surplus-value produced by productive labour? Doesn't this imply a major conflict of interests between productive and unproductive labour — the first seeking to reduce surplus-value to a minimum, the second wishing it increased? How can such a conflict be reconciled with the inclusion of both sectors in the same social class ? Shouldn't industrial workers oppose any expansion of state expenditure, even in \"social services\", since it is financed out of surplus-value extracted from them? This is a serious challenge. If the whole \"unproductive layer\" (clerks, civil servants, service workers, managers) is a drain on the surplus-value produced by industrial workers, then \"the working class\" as a unified political subject is a fiction — or worse, an obstacle to the industrial proletariat's real interests. Counter 1: Not All Unproductive Labour Is Paid from Currently Produced Surplus-Value. An important part of unproductive labour — commercial employees, workers in the financial sector, those in unproductive service industries — is paid not out of currently produced surplus-value, but out of that portion of social capital invested in these sectors. Only the profits of these capitals form part of currently produced surplus-value. Mandel: \"It is true that social capital is the result of past extortion of surplus-value. But this applies also to variable capital, i.e. to wages currently paid out to productive workers.\" The important point: \"since wages and salaries in all these sectors are not drawn from currently produced surplus-value, their payment in no way reduces the currently paid wages of productive workers.\" Footnote 61 elaborates: These wages increase the total mass of social capital among which the given quantity of surplus-value has to be divided (lowering the average rate of profit). But for the industrialists this is a lesser evil : if there were no autonomous commercial capital and commercial wage-earners, their own capital outlays to cover the costs of circulation would be significantly higher, and the rate of profit still lower (see Volume 3, Chapter 17). Since this only concerns the distribution of a given mass of surplus-value between different forms of capital, with no direct bearing on the division of newly created value between wages and surplus-value (i.e. on the rate of exploitation of productive labour), \"there arises no conflict of interest between productive and commercial wage-earners.\" This is a subtle and important argument. The key is the level of determination : what determines the rate of exploitation is the division of newly created value between wages and surplus-value in the productive sector. What determines the distribution of the given surplus among capitals (industrial, commercial, banking) is a separate question. Confusing these two — treating the distribution of the given surplus as if it altered the rate of exploitation — is the root error of the \"all state expenditure = surplus-value\" position. Counter 2: Part of Unproductive Labour Is Paid from Currently Produced Surplus-Value. This concerns \"essentially the wages and salaries of state employees in public services and administration\" — not state industries, where autonomous commodity production and value production occur. But even here, Mandel cautions, the conclusion is not automatic. To conclude that a reduction of state expenditure entails a reduction of surplus-value and an increase in real wages (or, equivalently, that the rise in state expenditure occurred through an increase in surplus-value and a reduction in real wages), \"it would be necessary to undertake a very detailed analysis of the trend of the rate of exploitation and of workers' living standards and needs since the 'explosion' of state expenditure.\" The \"Gross Wages\" Concept. Mandel's most important theoretical point in this section: \"the concept of 'gross wages' (i.e. wages before tax) has no meaning in Marxist economic theory.\" Wages are means of reconstituting the worker's labour-power through the purchase of commodities and services. Thus, \"money deducted from the worker's 'gross wage' to help the state buy aeroplanes has nothing at all to do with wages. It is from the outset part of social surplus-value.\" If fresh taxes actually lower previously attained levels of real wages, \"they may indeed be said to have increased the rate of surplus-value. But again this will be measured by comparing successive amounts of net — real — wages, and not 'gross wages'.\" Similarly, state medical, educational or transport services which help reconstitute the worker's labour-power (or maintain his family under normal living conditions) represent \"rather a socialized portion of the wage, regardless of whether it passes through the form of 'state revenue', and regardless of whether it 'originated' in 'gross wages' (taxes paid by the worker), 'gross profits' (taxes paid by the capitalist), or the 'gross income' of independent middle classes.\" Footnote 62 (Unemployment Compensation). Objection: unemployment compensation cannot be considered the equivalent of the \"price\" or \"value\" of the commodity labour-power, since by definition the unemployed do not sell their labour-power. Mandel: \"this argument is based on a somewhat mechanistic reduction of the category 'socialized wages'.\" Analogy: if a worker places 10 percent of his current wages in a chocolate box or bank account to provide for unemployment, that amount does not cease to be part of his wages. There is \"no fundamental difference\" between this and the situation where all workers use a \"collective chocolate box called the National Institute of Unemployment Insurance or Social Security\" and the money is transferred directly from capitalist accounts. Only if this analysis is accepted \"is it legitimate to demand that such funds be exclusively administered by the unions (for neither the employers nor the state should have any say in how the workers spend their own money!).\" Political Conclusion. Mandel's verdict: \"It thus proves meaningful after all to examine the impact of a rise or fall in state expenditure on average working-class living standards, independently of its servicing (mediation) by unproductive state employees.\" Where living standards decline, the total price of labour-power (individual plus 'socialized' wages) has been reduced. Where they rise, \"no sophism can prove that this entails an increase in social surplus-value.\" The Dynamic, Aggregate Method. Marxist economic theory rejects the notion of a rigid \"wages fund.\" Any analysis of the effects of varying levels of state expenditure upon the rate of exploitation would have to be aggregate and dynamic. \"Nothing flows automatically from either the expansion or contraction of state expenditure.\" The question is whether — under given economic, social and political conditions — a reduction in expenditure would lead to higher real wages rather than higher profits for the capitalist class. Without such detailed proof, \"the thesis would remain doubtful, to say the least.\" The analysis would have to take into account \"the probable dynamic of the political and social class struggle (a function of, among other things, the great historical shifts in the economic correlation of class forces within a given bourgeois society) and its precise impact upon the structure of both state revenue and state expenditure.\" Thematic Synthesis. Mandel acknowledges he has \"strayed considerably\" from productive/unproductive labour — but argues he has arrived at the heart of the problem. The correct Marxist classification of the proletariat — the class forced by socio-economic compulsion to sell its labour-power — implies that both: - variations in the level of the reserve army of labour , and - the variegated relations between the \"purely physiological\" and \"moral-historical\" components of the value of labour-power are of decisive importance for the proletarian's immediate destiny. Footnote 63 references Mandel's introduction to Volume 1 (pp. 66–72) and Late Capitalism (pp. 149–58). The Growth of Unproductive Wage-Labour. Mandel now presents an empirical argument. The growth of unproductive wage-labour accompanies the absolute and relative increase in the size of the proletariat in contemporary capitalist countries. Footnote 64 — Wage earners (incl. unemployed) as % of total active population, 1930s vs. 1974: Country 1930s 1974 Belgium 65.2% (1930) 83.7% Canada 66.7% (1941) 89.2% France 57.2% (1936) 81.3% Germany 69.7% (1939) 84.5% (W. Germany) Italy 51.6% (1936) 72.6% Japan 41.0% (1936) 69.1% Sweden 70.1% (1940) 91.0% U.K. 88.1% (1931) 92.3% U.S.A. 78.2% (1939) 91.5% Sources: Annuaire des statistiques du travail , 1945-6, Bureau International du Travail; and Office statistique des communautés européennes, 1976. Mandel's Interpretation. The interpretation is counter-intuitive . \"Far from reflecting increased exploitation of productive labour or a sharp rise in the rate of exploitation, it has rather established a ceiling above which the rate of exploitation can hardly climb under 'normal' political circumstances (excluding, that is, fascist or fascist-type regimes).\" Despite the rapid replacement of living labour by dead labour (semi-automated machinery), \"it is this growth of unproductive wage-labour which, in many capitalist countries, has reduced the reserve army of labour for a whole historical period.\" The services provided by a significant sector of unproductive wage-labour have been \"a major factor in developing the needs and living conditions of the proletariat far beyond the purely physiological bedrock.\" The new minimum standard is \"much higher than the one existing in Marx's time.\" The Temporality of This Conquest. Crucially: \"This acquisition should obviously not be taken for granted or regarded as unassailable. It is nothing but a conquest made by the working class under favourable conditions on the labour market (long-term decline of structural unemployment) and rendered objectively possible by the long post-war period of accelerated economic growth. Since the early seventies, as was foreseeable, this basic economic situation has been reversed.\" The reversal: \"Massive structural unemployment has reappeared, together with savage attacks in many 'rich' countries on the real wages of the working class, be they aimed at 'direct' or 'socialized' wages or at both.\" And the political response: \"Correctly, the workers have reacted strongly against massive cuts in social state expenditure, thereby showing that their class instinct is clearer than the 'science' of those theoreticians who persist in calling all state expenditure 'surplus-value' (the logical consequence of which would be indifference to, or even approval of, the cut-backs).\" This is the political payoff of the whole section. The theoretical error of treating all state expenditure as surplus-value has a direct political cost : it disables workers from defending socialized wages. Mandel's analysis restores the concept of \"socialized wages\" as an integral part of the value of labour-power, thereby providing a theoretical foundation for defense of the welfare state against cuts. Footnote 65: See Chapter 4 of Late Capitalism . 2.8 Luxury Production, Surplus-Value and Accumulation of Capital The theme here: the function of labour producing luxury goods and its relationship to accumulation — and by analogy, the arms sector. The Vorontsov Precedent. Controversy over the arms sector dates to the end of the nineteenth century, when the Russian populist V. Vorontsov first raised the possibility of avoiding crises of over-production through \"absorption\" of part of surplus-value by increased arms production. In the 1930s-40s, a long Marxist debate took up the role of rearmament in overcoming inter-war stagnation. Since World War II, the Vance–Cliff–Kidron school assigned a crucial position to the \"permanent arms economy\" in explaining the long boom. Arms production occupies a central place in the \"surplus absorption\" of Baran and Sweezy's Monopoly Capital . Mandel's own position (in Late Capitalism , Chapter 9) is in controversy with Cogoy and Mattick. Theoretical Foundation. Marx's theory sees value's essence in abstract social labour, irrespective of the specific use-value of the commodity. The existence of some kind of use-value is a precondition of realization only in the immediate sense that nobody buys a good which has absolutely no use for him. But \"the social fact of purchase is sufficient proof of the use-value of a commodity, that is, of its usefulness to its buyer.\" Hence: - Only unsold commodities do not embody socially necessary labour and thus have no value. - Those which are sold are by definition the product of socially necessary labour and increase through their production the mass of socially produced value. Under capitalism, by definition, the production of all sold commodities created by wage-labour increases the total mass of surplus-value produced and realized (unless sold at a price so far below cost of production that society does not recognize any part of the surplus labour contained in them). The Crucial Distinction. Volume 2 makes explicit the distinction between (i) production and realization of surplus-value and profit, and (ii) expanded reproduction of capital, i.e. accumulation. Not all commodities produced contribute to expanded reproduction . But all commodities produced and sold contribute to the increase of total surplus-value appropriated by the capitalists and their retainers. Under conditions of simple reproduction , there would be no surplus-value and no profit whatsoever , since all surplus-value would be unproductively consumed. (This is a striking and counter-intuitive assertion: profit exists only in and through accumulation; simple reproduction — which consumes all surplus — leaves no surplus at all.) Luxury Goods. The production of luxury consumer goods — purchased out of the non-accumulated portion of surplus-value — \"remains within the sphere of the production of value and surplus-value, that is to say, it enlarges the mass of profit accruing to the capitalist class.\" Arms Production. By the same token, \"the production of arms or space equipment is a form of commodity production; the fact that the sole purchaser is here the state, whereas luxury products are exchanged for revenue of the bourgeoisie, makes no essential difference.\" To determine whether arms production depresses or raises the average rate of profit, the same questions apply as for any \"sub-department\": 1. Is the organic composition of capital in that department equal, superior, or inferior to the average? 2. Does its rise or fall influence the average social rate of surplus-value? Footnote 70: This follows automatically from the commodity nature of arms — from the fact that capital invested in that sector is engaged in commodity production and correspondingly employs labour in the production of surplus-value. As in the case of luxury goods, differences between the profit rate within that branch and the rate outside it \"will lower or increase accordingly the social average rate of profit.\" In the Theories of Surplus-Value , Marx explicitly defends this position against Ricardo. Two Basic Situations for Armaments' Contribution to Accumulation. Situation 1: Full Employment of Capital. (This \"can be, and often has been, accompanied by structural unemployment of wage-labour.\") In this situation, weapons production — like luxury goods not entering into the reproduction of labour-power — \"evidently does not contribute to the accumulation of capital.\" This is true in a double sense : Weapons, like luxury products, do not provide the objective material elements of expanded reproduction: neither additional raw materials, machines, or energy, nor consumer goods capable of feeding an expanded workforce. Nevertheless, the part of the national income which buys weapons could not have been spent on additional means of production or wages for additional productive workers. Thus, both because of their specific use-value and because they are exchanged against the non-accumulated part of surplus-value, weapons do not contribute to expanded reproduction under conditions of full employment. Nuance. This does not necessarily imply weapons production reduces capital accumulation, except in the most general sense that all forms of unproductive expenditure of surplus-value do so. To show that the appearance or expansion of an arms sector has actually reduced expanded reproduction, it would have to be demonstrated that it appeared (or expanded) at the expense of the sector of means of production. \"If it has simply replaced luxury production, then, all other things being equal, neither the scope nor the potential rhythm of capital accumulation will have been changed.\" The Substitution Question. What if the weapons sector has appeared or expanded at the expense of the sector producing consumer goods for the workers , still assuming full employment? Two possibilities: (i) Where the substitution leads to a decline in the physical or moral working capacity of the labour force , the rate of capital accumulation will fall — perhaps after a certain time to the extent of contracted reproduction (footnote 71 references Mandel's Marxist Economic Theory , pp. 332–5, on the war economy). (ii) Where the substitution leaves unchanged the capacity or willingness of the workers to accept the current \"norm\" of social labour, such a shift of resources from department II to department III would imply a rise in the average social rate of surplus-value . \"The same value product would then be produced with the same labour-power, but at the cost of less variable capital. The working class would simply receive a smaller share of the existing national income.\" Whether this leaves accumulation unaltered or leads to a higher level of accumulation would then depend on the way this rise in the rate and mass of surplus-value influenced the division of surplus-value between the unproductively consumed portion (including the weapons sector) and the accumulated part. This is a complex, multi-variable determination — no mechanical conclusion is possible. Situation 2: Long-Term Plethora of Capital. Mandel abandons the \"full employment of capital\" supposition. This is not artificial: it was prevalent during the first massive arms drive preceding World War I (footnote 73: Lenin, Imperialism , Chapter 8), even more marked in the thirties (Japan's 'Manchurian Incident', German rearmament, generalized after 1936), and has remained the rule for over thirty years of permanent arming (footnote 74). Definitions. Over-production of capital: Value-side: emergence of large sums of capital hoarded in savings accounts or used for purchasing bonds and government securities, where they beget only the average rate of interest rather than the average rate of profit. Use-value side: sizeable stocks of unused raw materials and productive capacity in plant, as well as large reserves of unemployed workers. If — as a result of the appearance and expansion of a significant arms sector — money (or quasi-money) capital is productively reinvested , then the production of value and surplus-value increases . The manufacture of arms is productive of value and surplus-value; hence, \"capital grows richer because more workers are exploited in the production of greater surplus-value.\" The Indirect Mechanism. Since department II does not contribute to the creation of the material elements of expanded reproduction, its expansion cannot directly ensure a higher level of capital accumulation. But it can do so indirectly: As additional workers are employed, the wages bill increases, leading to rising output and sale of consumer goods. The consumption of additional raw materials in the weapons industry stimulates production in mines and other centres of department I which had previously contracted output. Material production rises in all sectors of the economy, augmenting the material elements of expanded reproduction — provided reserves of \"productive factors\" are available (which follows from the \"under-employment of capital\" hypothesis) and/or provided at least part of the additional surplus-value is not absorbed by the armaments sector or other unproductive departments, but remains available for capital accumulation. The \"Ideal\" Combination. These conditions apply with even greater force if the processes are accompanied by a changed distribution of the national income between wages and surplus-value — i.e. if rearmament is financed to some extent at the expense of the working class through a rise in the rate of surplus-value. The resultant combination would then be \"ideal\" for capital accumulation: Expansion of the mass of workers employed and exploited (i.e. expansion of value product, surplus-value, market demand) Increase in the rate of surplus-value and (probably) the rate of profit Rise in the rate of accumulation (i.e. increase of investment in the productive sector, over and above growth in arms spending) Footnote 75 distinguishes Hitler's war economy from the post-war boom. In the former, increased investment was by and large confined to the armaments sector; there was no real cumulative growth involving expansion of the \"final consumers\" market. This contrast matters enormously: it shows that arms spending can produce either a genuine boom or a war-economy dead end, depending on whether it sparks productive investment elsewhere. The Limits — No Long-Term Solution. This provides no long-term solution. The very \"success\" of the operation \"inevitably reproduces the initial contradictions\": Increased capital accumulation → rise in the organic composition of capital → depresses the rate of profit. Higher level of employment (made possible by absorption of part of the unemployed in the army or state apparatus — a normal feature of substantial military spending) reduces the industrial reserve army ; except under fascist-type dictatorship, this makes it more difficult to neutralize the effects of rising organic composition by driving up the rate of surplus-value. Decline in the rate of profit depresses productive investment → crisis of over-production and fall in the rate of capital accumulation. When the rate becomes negative , a process of devalorization of capital begins — the normal function of the crisis of over-production. To counter such a new crisis through intensification of armaments production — where a sizeable arms sector already exists — would modify the basic proportions both of the division of surplus-value between the accumulated and consumed portions, and of the allocation of productive resources between departments I/II, on one hand, and department III, on the other. \"Whatever effect upon the process of expanded reproduction was initially obtained would be increasingly neutralized.\" Moreover, such a high rate of taxation of profits and wages would be necessary that, except under very special political conditions, the basic social classes (although not the sector of capitalists directly engaged in weapons production and procurement) would revolt. Mandel's verdict: \"Such an expansion is thus no cure-all for the ills of capitalist over-accumulation and over-production. But it can trigger off shorter or longer periods of economic upturn as long as those preconditions indicated above are satisfied.\" This is a dialectical rather than mechanical conclusion — the same theoretical rigor applied to luxury goods extended to arms, with full recognition of the multiple possible scenarios. The analysis has implications for understanding the post-war boom, the \"military-industrial complex,\" and the limits of Keynesian demand management. 2.9 How Can Commercial and Financial Capital Participate in the Distribution of Social Surplus-Value? The distinction between productive and unproductive labour partially dovetails with the distinction between two general sectors of capital : Productive capital: capital invested in commodity production (industry, agriculture, transport, productive branches of the so-called service industries) Unproductive capital: commercial capital, banking and insurance capital, and capital invested in \"unproductive\" branches of service industries Wage-labour hired by the second category enables these capitalists to appropriate a fraction of the sum total of surplus-value — but does not itself add to that total. The Posing of the Question. \"Why do the industrial capitalists, or more precisely all those who invest in the 'productive' sectors, accept that a portion of the surplus-value produced by 'their' workers should be appropriated by capitalists whose capital does not contribute to the production of surplus-value?\" The Answer (Dealt with at length in Volume 3, touched on here). Although capital invested outside the sphere of material production does not directly augment the mass of surplus-value, it does contribute indirectly . Industrial and farming capitalists abandon a share of \"their\" surplus-value to traders and bankers \"not out of the goodness of their hearts, but because these gentlemen help them to raise the mass of that surplus-value.\" The Time-Dimension Mechanism. Marx introduces the \"time dimension\" — the key structuring principle of Volume 2's analysis of circulation and turnover. Consider: Total turnover time of fixed capital stretches over many years. Small shifts in the length of the commodity-capital phase (commodities remaining unsold) do not essentially affect it. The situation is entirely different for circulating capital . Numerical Example. Suppose it takes three months to produce a given mass of commodities and three months to sell them. Then circulating productive capital will turn over only twice a year unless it receives assistance. The part exchanged for labour-power — and thus making possible the creation of surplus-value — \"would then remain sterile for six months of the year.\" If, however, commercial capital buys up a large proportion of the commodities as soon as they leave the factory, OR if banking capital advances the money to pay the raw materials bill immediately after the commodities are produced and before they are sold, then \"owing to the assistance of these sectors of the capitalist class, productive circulating capital can be reinvested as soon as a production cycle is completed.\" Consequence: variable capital \"will never remain idle. It will set workers to produce surplus-value twelve months, and not six months a year — as a result of which, all other things being equal, the total annual mass of surplus-value will be twice as great as it would otherwise have been.\" Therefore: \"It naturally pays industrial capital to give a discount to wholesale traders, or to pay interest to bankers, if these rescue operations allow an overall increase in the production of surplus-value.\" Implication: Functional Division of Labour Within the Capitalist Class. Only a fraction of total social capital is continuously engaged in production. Important segments remain constantly outside the realm of production — money capital, transportation and banking capital — to shorten circulation time. From the point of view of the capitalist class as a whole (the one Marx adopts in Volume 2; only in Volume 3 does he treat these sectors as competing ), this is a \"functional division of labour within that class.\" Instead of each industrialist being his own treasurer, money-changer, transporter, seller on home and world markets, and advancer of additional money capital, all these functions are socially centralized by specialist sectors of the bourgeoisie. The division of labour carries a considerable rationalization: the costs of overall social circulation, transportation and banking are lower than if each capitalist firm had to accomplish these tasks itself. Overhead costs are reduced; the total mass of surplus-value is increased through continuous production. \"It is thus profitable for the bourgeoisie as a whole to maintain (and even expand, as the record of the 'service industries' demonstrates!) this functional division of labour.\" The Source of Capital Outside the Realm of Material Production. Mandel now poses a historical-materialist question: where does capital invested outside material production come from? Partial Truth. Since all capital derives in the last analysis from surplus-value, and since under capitalism all surplus-value is created by \"productive capital\" (wage-labour in material production), it may appear that all commercial and banking capital ultimately derives from industrial and agricultural productive capital. This is partially true. Volume 2 shows how money capital is periodically \"expelled\" from the process of value production, becoming temporarily available for other purposes. The best example: the depreciation fund of fixed capital. Because it is reinvested only at certain intervals, rather than piecemeal, this fund serves for a time as an important source of money capital employed in credit operations. The Critical Generalization. \"However, such a view should not be generalized. Capital, after all, is older than the capitalist mode of production.\" Before surplus-value was produced in production, \"vast wealth was accumulated through the plunder of peasants, the fleecing of feudal lords (for example, by over-pricing exotic merchandise), robbery of merchants (through piracy) and tribal communities (through the capture of slaves).\" Merchant, commercial and banking capital existed long before \"productive\" capital was born in manufactures, not to speak of the industrial revolution. Therefore: industrial capital \"not only reproduces commercial and banking capital by paying over fragments of the surplus-value created by 'its own' workers; it also finds these other forms of capital present at the moment of its own birth, and indeed as a condition of this.\" Commercial and banking capital, then, reproduce themselves both by (a) continuing their former practices — appropriating part of the social product originating outside the realm of capitalist relations of production, and transforming it into surplus-value and money capital — and (b) appropriating part of the surplus-value created within the capitalist process of production proper. Consequences for Underdevelopment Theory. This dual origin has profound historical implications. The interpenetration of pre-capitalist, semi-capitalist and capitalist relations of production — imposed upon colonies and semi-colonies by the power of capital on the world market and the violence of foreign political and military domination — has been \"an extremely important factor in the historical development of these twin sources of money capital accumulation.\" Through merchant, commercial, usury and banking capital, they have continued to play a key role in world-wide capitalist expansion, especially within third-world countries. The Central Historical Thesis: \"Primitive accumulation of capital and 'productive' accumulation of capital (through the creation of surplus-value in commodity production) are not only successive historical stages, but also simultaneous and combined phenomena. Nor does primitive accumulation automatically lead to a commensurate spread of 'productive' capital and industrialization; it may instead simply condense into a 'one-sided' expansion of the above-mentioned forms of 'unproductive' capital. This circumstance, together with the impact of foreign imperialist domination, clarifies one of the mysteries of underdevelopment under capitalism.\" This is a major contribution to underdevelopment theory. It explains why some peripheral countries experience \"one-sided\" development of commercial, financial, and rentier capital without industrial transformation: primitive accumulation and primitive dispossession do not automatically lead to productive investment. The interpenetration of pre-capitalist and capitalist forms generates a structure that can block industrialization. 2.10 Luxemburg's Critique of Marx's Reproduction Schemas This section is the theoretical and political core of the Introduction. Mandel adjudicates one of the most consequential debates in Marxist thought. The Question. Luxemburg's The Accumulation of Capital raises formidable questions: Marx's theory of crisis; the historical limits of the capitalist mode of production (the Zusammenbruchstheorie or \"breakdown theory\"); the origins and functions of imperialism, colonialism, militarism and wars in the imperialist epoch. Footnote 76 (Documentation). Main contributions: Otto Bauer (Die Neue Zeit, No. 24, 1913), Anton Pannekoek (Bremer Bürgerzeitung, 29 January 1913), G. Eckstein (Vorwärts, 16 February 1913), Bukharin's Imperialism and the Accumulation of Capital . Henryk Grossmann ( Das Akkumulations- und Zusammenbruchsgesetz , 1929). Recent discussion: Arghiri Emmanuel, Le Profit et les crises , Paris, 1975; Joan Robinson's introduction to the English translation. Luxemburg's Argument. Centered on a single theme: how can that part of the value of commodities corresponding to the accumulated portion of surplus-value be realized? What purchasing power is available? Why do capitalists expand production, if not because they expect additional customers? Who are these new customers? Rejected Candidates: Workers. Rejected because the purchasing power of workers originates with capital; expansion of production merely to satisfy the new needs of an enlarged workforce would be inconceivable for the capitalist class as a whole. (Not true of capitalists taken individually, for whom all workers except their own are potential customers; but, \"as Luxemburg flatly states, for the capitalist class as a whole, all workers are 'their own workers', and it makes no sense to treat them as a source of increased sales.\") Other capitalists. Rejected: \"How could the capitalist class in its totality enrich itself if the money to buy the surplus product came out of its own pocket?\" \"Third persons\" (cronies, hangers-on, servants of the capitalist class, or landowners appropriating ground-rent). Rejected: \"in the last analysis, the revenue of all these social layers is derived from surplus-value.\" If surplus-value were the only source of purchasing power, \"it would mean that capitalists become richer by spending their own money.\" Luxemburg's Conclusion. The additional purchasing power must come from outside capitalist relations of production — through forcing non-capitalist social classes (peasants and pre-capitalist landowners) ruinously to spend their revenue on capitalist commodities. Only in this way can expanded production and reproduction, capital accumulation, and capitalist economic growth take place. The Breakdown Thesis. By destroying the non-capitalist milieu on which its expansion is based, capitalism \"undermines the conditions of its own growth.\" The disappearance of the non-capitalist environment marks the absolute limit of capitalist development. Footnote 79 (Predecessors). The notion that a non-capitalist milieu is necessary for expanded reproduction was first advanced by Heinrich Cunow (\"Die Zusammenbruchstheorie\", Die Neue Zeit , No. 1, 1898), later defended by Karl Kautsky (\"Krisentheorien\", Die Neue Zeit , No. 2, 1902) and Louis B. Boudin ( The Theoretical System of Karl Marx , 1907, pp. 163-9, 241-53). Secondary Criticisms — Easily Refuted. (i) Money as means of circulation vs. income as prerequisite of realization. Luxemburg asserts Marx confuses these. Mandel: \"she is quite evidently mistaken\" (footnote 80 references pp. 143-5 and Mandel's own remarks at pp. 442-4 of the volume). (ii) Schemas don't correspond to capitalist reality. She \"mixes up levels of abstraction which are clearly differentiated in Marx's method.\" (iii) Schemas don't incorporate laws of motion. Because Marx's figures allow for no increase in the organic composition of capital, \"they could not incorporate these laws.\" Mandel dismisses this. (iv) Department II sacrificed to Department I. She claims that \"department II is somehow 'sacrificed to' or 'dependent upon' department I, in contradiction to the laws of private property and competition.\" Mandel: \"it does not follow at all\" from the primacy of department I. Footnote 81 notes that schemas incorporating the laws of motion have been worked out by Bauer, Grossmann, Léon Sartre, Glombowski, Hosea Jaffe and many others. Whether they assure long-term equilibrium is, of course, \"quite a different question.\" The Three-Level Response. Mandel structures his response at three levels of abstraction. Level 1: Capital in General vs. Many Capitals. Luxemburg commits a methodological error : situating within the framework of \"capital in its totality\" a problem that can only be considered in relation to the \"competition of many capitals.\" It is impossible to conduct an analysis simultaneously at these two distinct levels, since capital in its totality abstracts by definition from many capitals, from competition. The argument that the capitalist class cannot enrich itself by purchasing its own surplus product \"overlooks the fact that, under a system of private property, the surplus product can never be owned by 'a single total capital'.\" Capitalist competition implies that capitalists can grow richer by buying one another's surplus product. Marx explicitly: \"the surplus-value created at one point requires the creation of surplus-value at another point, for which it may be exchanged\" ( Grundrisse , p. 407). He also indicates (Capital Volume 3, Chapter 15,3) that in the absence of competition, growth would actually disappear . Footnote 82: \"This point was first made by Rosdolsky (pp. 63-72).\" The Key Methodological Thesis: \"For Marx, growth is possible in a 'purely' capitalist milieu (i.e. where no part of the social surplus product can find 'non-capitalist' customers) provided that the interests and growth rates of all capitalists are assumed to be not identical, but on the contrary rooted in competition. The realization question does not, and cannot, arise within the realm of 'capital in general'; it appears, together with the theory of crises and the trade cycle, only within the sphere of 'many capitals'. This Marx repeatedly stated himself\" (Theories of Surplus-Value, Part II, pp. 532-4). The Corollary: reproduction schemas which imply competition should assume as a rule the existence of different , rather than equal , rates of accumulation in the two departments — only occasionally leading to equalization of the rate of profit. This corresponds to the real modus operandi of capitalism. It also points the way to a solution to the \"technical problem\" Luxemburg identified in the fact that the \"unsaleable\" portion of department II's commodities embodies part of the surplus-value created in that department. The Solution: Luxemburg dismissed out of hand Marx's convincing solution, later developed at length by Otto Bauer. Part of the surplus-value produced in department II \"is periodically transferred to department I, precisely when (and because) department I exhibits, over a considerable length of time, a higher organic composition of capital than that of department II.\" Level 2: Quasi-Static Equilibrium vs. Discontinuous Dynamics. Level 1 poses the problem as quasi-static equilibrium . Level 2 — still abstract but nearer historical reality — examines accumulation as a discontinuous process to understand its actual dynamics. Second Question Re-Posed: What is the effect upon realization of the value of commodities embodying the accumulated part of surplus-value if and when: 1. The organic composition of capital rises in both departments; 2. Department I grows faster than II (unavoidable result of the rising organic composition); 3. The rate of profit declines (growth in rate of surplus-value insufficient to compensate for rising organic composition)? Answer: \"Theoretically, full realization of surplus-value is possible, and several ingenious mathematical models have been constructed — by, among others, O. Benedikt, Shinzaburo Koshimura, Oskar Lange, J. Caridad Mateo and Hosea Jaffe — in order to show that it is.\" By contesting this, Luxemburg \"denied that 'pure' capitalism was possible, thus taking a position exactly opposite to the one which Marx tried to demonstrate with his reproduction schemas.\" Footnote 88 — Critical Qualification. \"It should be immediately added, however, that the real socio-economic conditions expressed by these algebraic formulas have to be precisely defined.\" Example: Koshimura, to reconcile equilibrium with a rising organic composition and a falling rate of profit, has to: - Modify the initial relations between three departments - Increase considerably the organic composition of department III (which makes little historical sense) - Lower the total price of production of department II (workers' wages) to the extent of absolute decline - \"Offsetting\" the falling rate of profit by a rising rate of surplus-value (plausible), arrive at an absolute decrease in workers', and even capitalists', consumption (not only implausible but contrary to Marx's basic assumption in Capital Volume 2, and to existing empirical data) This is a hard-nosed qualification: algebraic solution is not the same as historical realism. The mathematical models \"solve\" the problem only under assumptions that contradict both Marx's own framework and observable reality. Footnote 89 — Contradicting Luxemburg's Critics. Those critics who replied that the schemas \"prove\" by themselves the possibility of unlimited, smooth progress of reproduction \"forgot one small point: capitalism has been generating periodic crises of over-production for more than 150 years, and continues to do so with the regularity of a 'natural law'.\" Reject out of hand the hypothesis that each successive crisis was due entirely to \"specific\" causes unrelated to the inner logic of the capitalist mode of production. \"The very periodicity of these crises is enough to refute the 'harmony theorists'.\" In this respect, \"the superiority of Luxemburg over certain of her critics is obvious\" (footnote 90: see especially her 'Anti-Critique'). Mandel's Verdict on Luxemburg's Technical Work: \"Nevertheless, did she succeed in proving her case in a technically satisfactory manner? We do not believe so; for she narrowed down the problem to an excessively monocausal one. In order to prove that, under capitalism, equilibrium must beget disequilibrium, that expanded reproduction must generate over-production, and that accumulation of capital must lead to devalorization of capital it is necessary to bring all the inter-related variables of the reproduction schemas into play. And this she does not do. Thus, while The Accumulation of Capital raises the correct problems, it does not provide acceptable solutions to them.\" Footnote 91 — Grossmann Also Fails. Grossmann's standpoint — denial that the crises have at bottom problems of realization of surplus-value and disproportionality between production and consumption — is \"fundamentally unsound.\" By converting the decline of the rate of profit into the sole cause of the final breakdown of capitalism, he overlooks the fact that this tendency is offset by periodical devalorization of capital. Whereas he seeks a mechanical unity between the theory of crises of over-production and that of the breakdown, \"the real, dialectical link between the two embodies the following contradiction: crises of over-production are the precise mechanism which allows the decline in the rate of profit to be periodically overcome — both through devalorization of the total mass of social capital and through a rise in the rate of surplus-value.\" This is a dialectical rather than mechanical relationship: crisis is not the terminal event of a self-destroying system, but rather the mechanism of its periodic renewal . The system survives through crises, not despite them. The Technical Demonstration. Mandel now formalizes the impossibility of permanent equilibrium. Equilibrium Formula for Expanded Reproduction: $$I_v + I_{s_a} + I_{s_\\gamma} = II_c + II_{s_\\beta}$$ Interpretation: The demand for consumer goods generated by department I equals the consumer-goods supply from department II (left side); equivalently, the demand for means of production generated by department II (replacement + addition) equals the means-of-production supply from department I (right side). The Argument. The formula implies \"an identity of the rate of growth of demand for consumer goods generated by department I, and the rate of growth of constant capital in department II.\" But: The rise in the organic composition of capital entails that the demand for consumer goods generated in department I will normally grow more slowly than constant capital in that sector (unless the slower rate of growth of variable capital is compensated by a rate of growth of unproductively consumed surplus-value higher than that of constant capital — \"extremely unlikely in the long run\"). Therefore, the precondition of equilibrium is \"a rate of growth of constant capital in department II lower than the one in department I.\" If the rates in the two departments are equal, equilibrium is upset. But \"a rate of growth of constant capital in department II which is permanently lower than that in department I is incompatible with private property and competition. There is no reason why capitalists engaged in the production of consumer goods should forever abstain from trying to incorporate all existing technology, all means of reducing costs of production, all potentially useable machinery.\" Conclusion: \"Therefore, $II_c + II_{s_\\beta}$ will from time to time be greater than $I_v + I_{s_a} + I_{s_\\gamma}$, just as, periodically, under conditions of rising organic composition of capital (biased development of labour-saving technology), $\\Delta[II_c + II_{s_\\beta}]$ will be equal to $\\Delta[I_c + I_{s_\\beta}]$, and $\\Delta[I_c + I_{s_\\beta}]$ will be greater than $\\Delta[I_v + I_{s_a} + I_{s_\\gamma}]$.\" \"It therefore seems impossible to avoid periodic over-production of consumer goods, as well as a decline in the rate of profit and of the ratio $\\Delta c/c$, $s_v/s_v$, entailing an abrupt halt to the accumulation of capital.\" The Donald Harris Objection. Donald Harris (\"On Marx's Scheme of Reproduction and Accumulation,\" Journal of Political Economy , Vol. 80, 1972, pp. 505ff.) concluded from Marx's \"assumptions\" that equilibrium obtains only if (in a value system) there is proportional hiring of labour in the two departments, or if (in a prices of production system) there is an equal ratio of investment — accumulation — of surplus-value. Mandel's Response: \"All these calculations are based upon a misunderstanding of Marx's method. While Marx does assume an equal rate of exploitation in both departments (an assumption based on the concept of an average national value of labour-power, for which quite strong empirical evidence exists under developed capitalism), he does not 'assume' either that the organic composition of capital will remain equal or that the rate of surplus-value will stay the same.\" Marx's method of successive approximation led him to abstract, at a given stage, from a number of additional variables in order to clarify certain preliminary problems. \"This has nothing to do with 'assuming' historical trends.\" This is a methodological key: abstraction is a staging device, not an empirical claim. To treat Marx's abstractions as empirical assumptions is to fundamentally misunderstand his method. Level 3: The Historical Process. \"Finally, on the third level, that of the actual historical process of capital accumulation, Luxemburg seems fundamentally correct.\" Capitalism was born essentially in a non-capitalist milieu. It has immensely enriched itself by plundering that milieu. The same value-transferring metabolism has continued to this very day. \"'Pure' capitalism has never existed in real life and, as Engels rightly predicted, it never will exist, because 'we shall not let it come to that'. The Russian October Revolution, and the subsequent expansion of a post-capitalist sector of world economy, indicates that Engels's instinct was a sure one in that respect.\" Luxemburg's analysis of the ways and means whereby capitalism sucks wealth and value from pre-capitalist communities and classes was \"an impressive first contribution to three-quarters of a century of anti-colonialist and anti-imperialist world literature. It has still to be equalled in either theoretical insight or economic lucidity.\" (Footnote 93: The Accumulation of Capital , Chapters 27-30.) The Balanced Final Verdict. \"The final balance-sheet of Luxemburg's critique, then, must be a nuanced one. We cannot say baldly that she is right or that she is wrong. While many of her partial theses, as well as her final answer, are inadequate, she certainly poses relevant questions and puts her finger on real problems which Volume 2 does not and cannot answer. In particular, the contradictory character of capitalist growth, discussion of which was stimulated by her seminal The Accumulation of Capital , cannot be simply subsumed under the formulas 'anarchy of production' and 'disproportionality'. The specific place which unavoidable disproportions between production and mass consumption occupy in the dynamics of capitalism has to be integrated into any overall explanation of capitalist disequilibrium and crisis.\" 2.11 Volume 2 and Marx's Explanation of Capitalist Crises of Over-Production Mandel now transitions to crisis theory proper. Marx left no systematic treatment of crises — the four volumes contain partial considerations, with the sixth volume on the world market having been planned but not written. Mandel's task: to reconstruct the elements of a crisis theory from Volume 2. First Point: Commercial Capital as Masking Agent. The role of commercial capital as intermediary between industrial capitalist and final consumer, while helping shorten circulation time and hasten turnover, \" masks the growing disproportion between expanding production and lagging final demand.\" Marx's formulation: \"The periods in which capitalist production exerts all its forces regularly show themselves to be periods of over-production; because the limit to the application of the productive powers is not simply the production of value, but also its realization. However, the sale of commodities, the realization of commodity capital, and thus of surplus-value, is restricted not by the consumer needs of society in general, but by the consumer needs of a particular society in which the great majority are always poor and must always remain poor .\" This is the under-consumptionist moment of Marx's own analysis. The Famous Volume 3 Passage: \"The ultimate reason for all real crises always remains the poverty and restricted consumption of the masses, in the face of the drive of capitalist production to develop the productive forces as if only the absolute consumption power of society set a limit to them.\" The Apparent Contradiction. Marx, however, states no less categorically in Volume 2: \"It is a pure tautology to say that crises are provoked by a lack of effective demand or effective consumption. The capitalist system does not recognize any forms of consumer other than those who can pay, if we exclude the consumption of paupers and swindlers. The fact that commodities are unsaleable means no more than that no effective buyers have been found for them... If the attempt is made to give this tautology the semblance of greater profundity, by the statement that the working class receives too small a portion of its own product, and that the evil would be remedied if it received a bigger share, i.e. if its wages rose, we need only note that crises are always prepared by a period in which wages generally rise, and the working class actually does receive a greater share in the part of the annual product destined for consumption... It thus appears that capitalist production involves certain conditions, independent of people's good or bad intentions, which permit the relative prosperity of the working class only temporarily, and moreover always as a harbinger of crisis.\" Mandel's Resolution. There is no contradiction . What Marx rejects is the \"common-or-garden reformist or 'liberal' platitude, according to which crises could be avoided if, in the period immediately preceding or coinciding with the onset of over-production, the purchasing power in the hands of the masses were to be significantly increased.\" The Reformist View Overlooks Two Facts: Not all commodities are consumer goods. An important fraction of the \"commodity mountain\" consists of means of production, which cannot be and are not intended to be bought by workers. An increase in sales of consumer goods \"tells us nothing of the course of sales of equipment and raw materials. It does not lead automatically to greater productive investment.\" A redistribution from profits to wages would collapse investment. A sudden large rise in wages would result in a collapse of investment, i.e. of sales of means of production. If this were to follow a period of actual decline in the rate of profit, \"then capital accumulation would contract very violently indeed and the crisis would remain unavoidable.\" The Definition of \"Under-Consumptionism.\" \"Inasmuch as they forget this basic correlation of the trade cycle with medium-term fluctuations of the rate of profit, all economists (whether Marxist or non-Marxist) who explain the crisis exclusively or mainly in terms of the relation between the purchasing power of consumers and the national income are truly guilty of 'under-consumptionism', that is to say, of a one-sided and therefore erroneous theory.\" Footnote 100 (Targets). The most noteworthy Marxist author of this type: Nathalia Moszkowska ( Zur Kritik moderner Krisentheorien , Prague, 1935); but Fritz Sternberg and Paul Sweezy should also be mentioned. Non-Marxist: Sismondi, Malthus, Lederer, Keynes. The Counterpart Error: \"Pure Disproportionism.\" \"The same is true of the opposite theory, which concentrates exclusively or mainly on the 'disproportion' between the two departments, explaining crises by the anarchy of production and the difficulty (impossibility) of establishing the 'right proportions' spontaneously (as if 'organized capitalism' or a 'general cartel' could avoid crisis!).\" Footnote 101 references the \"neo-harmonicist\" versions of Hilferding and Bauer, both inspired by Tugan-Baranowski's Studien zur Theorie . Hilferding's striking Finanzkapital statement: \"A general cartel regulating total social production and thereby overcoming crises is, in principle, economically imaginable, even if such a social and political state of affairs is an impossibility\" (p. 372). Bukharin was influenced by the same trend, asserting that under state capitalism, where anarchy of production has been overcome, there could be no crises of over-production. Drawing on these arguments, \"Tony Cliff and his disciples have attempted to justify their use of the term 'state capitalism' to define the Soviet economy — an economy which has witnessed no crisis of over-production for more than half a century.\" The Specific Error of Pure Disproportionism. \"Overlooked in such a thesis is the fact, which Marx himself pointed out, that the 'disproportion' between the tendency of unlimited development of the productive forces and the narrow constraints placed upon consumption by the bourgeois mode of distribution, is itself a specific source of disequilibrium, autonomous from the disturbance of 'equilibrium relations' between the two departments.\" Supporters of pure disproportionism also forget, \"like Tugan-Baranowski, the father of pure 'disproportionism', that unlimited growth of department I leads to ever faster growth of the productive capacity of department II (although not necessarily in the same proportion); in other words, that under capitalist commodity relations production can never fully emancipate itself from sales to the final consumer.\" Footnote 103 (Grundrisse quote): \"It is quite the same with the demand created by production itself for raw material, semi-finished goods, machinery, means of communication, and for the auxiliary materials consumed in production... This effective, exchange-value-positing demand is adequate and sufficient as long as the producers exchange among themselves. Its inadequacy shows itself as soon as the final product encounters its limit in direct and final consumption\" ( Grundrisse , p. 421). The Integrative Conclusion. \"Thus theories of 'pure disproportionism' are as wrong as ones of 'pure under-consumptionism'. The basic causes of periodic crises of over-production are, at one and the same time, the inevitable periodic decline of the rate of profit, the capitalist anarchy of production, and the impossibility under capitalism of developing mass consumption in correlation with the growth of the productive forces.\" The Insoluble Contradiction (Mandel's Core Thesis). The basic curse of capitalism — that surplus-value embodied in commodities can only be realized if they are sold at their value — \"implies the presence of an insoluble contradiction at a given point of expanded reproduction.\" The contradiction has two horns: Any measure which tries suddenly to reverse the decline of the rate of profit provokes a shrinking of the market of final consumers. Any attempt suddenly to reverse that shrinking accentuates the decline of the rate of profit. Capitalist growth and prosperity require both a rising rate of profit (currently realized and anticipated) and an expanding market (present reality and future trend). But \"the coincidence of these conditions can never be permanent, for the very forces which bring it into being at a given point in the trade cycle work towards its undoing at a subsequent stage.\" Necessary Crisis. \"In that sense, crises of over-production are unavoidable under capitalism. According to even the most optimistic hypothesis, 'anti-cyclical policies' can only reduce their scope temporarily; they cannot prevent the very 'moderation' obtained during one period from leading, in the long run, to more explosive side-effects (such as the cumulative movement of inflation, or the precipitate growth of the burden of company debt).\" Footnote 105: \"Among these should be included not only 'purely' economic factors, but also the intertwining of the trade cycle with the partially autonomous cycle of the class struggle.\" Footnote 106: See Chapter 13 of Late Capitalism for the roots, functions, and consequences of permanent inflation. The Law of Value and Crises. The objective logic of crises is \"connected with the operation of the law of value, clarified by an important remark made by Marx in Capital Volume 2.\" Equilibrium presupposes that commodities are sold \"at the value they had at the moment of their production.\" But the very dynamic of expanded reproduction involves regular revolutions in technology, unceasing attempts by industrialists to win competitive struggle by reducing costs, growing substitution of machines for manual labour. All these phenomena imply a tendency for the value of each commodity to decline . \"Seen in this light, crises of over-production are nothing other than objective mechanisms through which the adjustment of market prices to declining commodity-values is achieved.\" Capital thereby incurs important losses (devalorizations), whether directly (through reduction in value of commodity capital) or indirectly (through bankruptcy and closure of the least efficient firms). Footnote 108: \"Declining value expressed in gold prices and not, of course, in inflated paper currency.\" This is a striking formulation. Crises are not simply destructive events but functional mechanisms of capitalist self-adjustment: they devalorize capital, close inefficient firms, and align market prices to the successively declining values produced by rising productivity. The crisis is simultaneously a catastrophe and a self-correcting mechanism. The Fixed Capital Turnover-Cycle Nexus. Marx stresses a nexus between the trade cycle and the turnover cycle of fixed capital \"distinct from the usually mentioned one of determination grosso modo of the length of the former by the latter.\" Fixed capital expenditure is discontinuous in a double sense: Replacement in toto. Machines are replaced not piecemeal (except current repairs) but in toto — once every seven or ten years. Simultaneity. Replacement tends to occur at the same time in numerous, inter-connected key branches of industry, precisely because the process is \"not only, or even essentially, a function of physical wear and tear, but rather a response to financial incentives to introduce more advanced technology.\" Footnote 109: \"'Moral' wearing-out of equipment (obsolescence) generally predates 'physical' breakdown under capitalism, given the pressure of competition and accelerated technical progress.\" The Three Criteria of Profit Calculation for Fixed Capital Investment: 1. Availability of sufficient money capital reserves 2. Rising rate of profit and profit expectations 3. Existence and/or anticipation of a sudden market expansion \"These incentives coincide only at a certain point in the trade cycle; but when this occurs, there follows a massive investment in the renewal of fixed capital. This in turn sets up a dynamic of accelerated capital accumulation and economic growth, together with rapid expansion of markets, which leads finally to an increase in the organic composition of capital, a declining trend of the rate of profit and a tendency to slow down investment and renewal of fixed capital.\" The Structural Asymmetry. \"The difficulty is compounded by the fact that the productive capacity of the sub-branch of department I which produces means of production for the production of means of production, must normally be geared to the general demand for the renewal of fixed capital (at least in its social average). Thus while this sub-branch may be overtaken by peak demand at the moment of 'overheating', it will suffer from unused capacity during a considerable part of the trade cycle.\" Footnote 110: Mandel notes that \"academic economic theory later took over this essentially Marxist contribution to the theory of the trade cycle.\" 2.12 Money Circulation, Money Capital and Money Hoarding Mandel now examines the monetary dimension — \"one of the most 'modern' aspects of Marx's analysis.\" The Key Insight. Marx \"fundamentally anticipates the Keynesian problematic of money hoarding\" — withdrawal of money from the process of productive circulation (circulation geared to the realization and reproduction of surplus-value). The Initial Assumption. For reproduction to flow smoothly, all income generated in the production process must be spent on the commodities produced. \"Any additional purchasing power injected into the reproduction process at a given point must be expelled at another point, if the process is to continue in a balanced way.\" The Tendency to Hoard. The very functioning of the capitalist mode leads to periodic hoarding of money capital . Mechanism 1: Circulation time fluctuations. Marx points out that successive expansions and contractions of the circulation time of commodities — related to phases of the trade cycle — result in periodic expansions and contractions of money capital as compared with productive capital. Mechanism 2: Production time fluctuations. The shortening or lengthening of the production process itself (e.g. increase or reduction of the weight in the total product-mix of commodities requiring lengthy production time) gives rise to contraction or expansion of the volume of money capital in circulation. The shorter the production time, the quicker the turnover of productive capital, and the smaller the money reserves which capitalists must throw into circulation to cover wages and consumption until commodities are finished and sold. \"Conversely, a lengthening of the production time will result in a lengthening of the turnover time of capital, and an increase in the reserves of money capital and money revenue that have to be injected into the circulation process to maintain consumption until the production and sale of the commodities is completed.\" Footnote 111: See pp. 358-9, 364-6. The Buy-Without-Sell / Sell-Without-Buy Problem. More generally, the harmonious flow of expanded reproduction is \"constantly threatened (not permanently upset, of course), because there are always capitalists who buy without selling, and others who sell without buying. Money is continually being withdrawn from circulation, and additional money is forever being injected. Only if these movements roughly cancel each other out will the partially autonomous character of the money flow not conflict with the need to realize the total value of commodities produced.\" The Banking System's Role. \"While the banking system objectively strives to achieve that balance (and thus represents a force of social accounting and centralization far superior to anything private ownership could accomplish in the realm of production), it does not have the means to ensure automatic and continual balancing. Here there appears a further cause of discontinuity or interruption of expanded production — a cause which, though derived from monetary phenomena, is of course essentially rooted in the contradictory nature of the commodity and of the production of value and surplus-value.\" This is a nuanced appreciation of banking: it is a force of centralization superior to what private property can achieve in production, yet it cannot ensure automatic balance because it operates within a system grounded in the contradictory commodity. The Additional Proportions. \"A series of proportions, additional to those which emerge prima facie from the reproduction schemas, play an important role in amplifying, if not triggering off, the trade cycle.\" The way the total money stock is divided between circulating money and hoarded money . The way circulating money is divided between circulating money capital and circulating revenue . The way hoarded money is divided between latent (potential) productive capital (money capital which will tend to contribute to increased production of surplus-value) and capital more or less permanently hoarded (withdrawn from both the sphere of production and the sphere of commodity circulation). \"All these proportions significantly influence the volume and rhythm of capital accumulation.\" Footnote 112: See pp. 260-61. Footnote 113: Emmanuel correctly stresses the role of hoarding in Marx's theory of crises. He uses the expression vouloir d'achat (purchasing desire) as opposed to pouvoir d'achat (purchasing power). Keynes and Marx Compared. Keynes was correct to discard the assumption of more or less permanent full employment of manpower and capital. He was also right to point out that capital or revenue not spent (i.e. hoarded) is an important source of disequilibrium and under-employment of productive resources. \"In fact, Marx had argued as much sixty-five years earlier, in Capital Volume 2.\" But: \"the latter's understanding of the fundamental mechanisms of the capitalist mode of production proved more profound than that of Keynes. For Marx went a step further by distinguishing between productive investment (i.e. investment leading to increased production of surplus-value) and unproductive 'investment' (which cannot directly augment the total social wealth and real income, but only contribute indirectly to re-allocation and redeployment of existing resources). After all, building pyramids and digging canals in order to fill them up again does not have the same effect upon economic growth, capital accumulation and expanded reproduction as building new factories and opening up new oil fields. Buying government bonds in order to finance the building of pyramids is evidently not the same kind of activity as the investment of productive capital.\" Footnote 114 (Mattick Critique). \"Paul Mattick ( Marx and Keynes , London, 1969) does not make the matter any clearer by a confused use of the concept 'waste production'. 'Waste', in the sense of products not entering into the reproduction process , and 'waste' in the sense of unsellable products , are not at all identical concepts. Luxury products are — like arms — commodities, and they find buyers. Public works and other infrastructural outlays are not carried out for the purpose of sale, but in order to accelerate the turnover of capital and thereby indirectly to increase the production of surplus-value. However, pyramids or canals which are dug and then filled up again are pure waste — they are neither commodities to be sold nor means of hastening the turnover of capital.\" This is a tri-partite distinction: (a) unproductive but sold commodities (luxury goods, arms); (b) infrastructural outlays that accelerate turnover; (c) pure waste. Mattick's conflation of these categories, Mandel argues, undermines the analytical clarity of his work. Four Causes of Rising Commodity Prices. From the monetary analysis dispersed throughout Volume 2, Mandel identifies four distinct causes of rising commodity prices: (a) Falling productivity in a branch. A fall in average productivity of labour in a given branch (e.g. agricultural or mining, where decline in natural fertility is not offset by technological progress); prices rise as a result of an increase in value of particular commodities (i.e. the quantity of social labour necessary for their production). (b) Falling value of gold. A sudden increase of labour productivity in the gold-mining industry (and thus a decline in the value of gold); all other things equal, the same mass of commodities exchanges for a greater amount of gold (produced by the same quantity of labour as before). In other words, the gold price of commodities rises. (c) Market-price fluctuations around unchanged values. An upward trend of market price-fluctuations around an unchanged axis of values. This may occur even when the gold currency remains stable and there is no paper money inflation, at that precise stage of the trade cycle marked by the periodic contraction of the hoarded part of money as compared with the circulating part. (d) Inflationary movement of money signs. A constant amount of gold — which exchanges against the same amount of commodities as before on the basis of an unaltered quantity of socially necessary labour — becomes represented by a greater sum of paper money signs (or of bank money, credit money). Footnote 115: Karl Marx, A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy , Moscow, 1971, pp. 118-20. See also Grundrisse , pp. 121-2 and 212-13. This fourfold typology is significant because it demonstrates that \"inflation\" is not a single phenomenon. Gold-value declines, productivity declines, cyclical fluctuations, and monetary debasement are four distinct causal processes. Policy that treats them as one will fail. 2.13 Growth and Crisis The final section of Mandel's Introduction is a synthesis and a rhetorical crescendo . The Central Message. \"The central 'message' of Volume 2, like that of Volume 1, refers to a terrifyingly dynamic process.\" Volume 1 indicates why capital, by its very essence, is value in perpetual search of additional value , produced by workers in the process of production. \"The unquenchable thirst for surplus-value is the fundamental motor of economic growth, technological revolution, 'research and development' spending, improvement of communications, 'third-world aid', the sales drive and market research.\" A corresponding quest for individual enrichment appears at the core of every level of bourgeois society, together with increasing alienation of workers and all human beings, and a growing threat that the forces of production will be transformed into forces of destruction. \"Paradoxically, mankind increasingly loses control over its own products and productive endeavour at the very moment when its mastery of nature and natural forces seems to be developing by leaps and bounds.\" Footnote 116: \"This domination of nature increasingly takes the form of the destruction ( Raubbau ) of nature, as is shown by the threats to ecological equilibrium.\" This is a remarkable nod to ecology — in 1976, before the widespread development of eco-Marxism — and it highlights the darker dialectic of \"mastery of nature\" as \"destruction of nature.\" The Avalanche. In Volume 2, we follow commodities containing the surplus-value produced by workers on their travels outside the factory. A \"spiralling movement\" of growth is unleashed — \"a veritable avalanche.\" Footnote 117: \"Marx and Luxemburg borrowed the image of the spiral as an expression of the form of capitalist development from Simonde de Sismondi.\" The Dynamics. The sale of commodities at their value enables profit to be realized and additional capital accumulated. More capital begets more surplus-value, which in turn begets more capital. Obstacles on the road of self-expansion — \"the enforced lingering of commodities in the sphere of circulation, or the protracted character of the production process itself\" — are swept away by the avalanche, thanks to: The social division of labour within the capitalist class The appearance of commercial and banking capital The constant striving to accelerate the transport of commodities, build up a world-wide system of communications, and reduce the length of the circulation process to a minimum \"An immense mountain of commodities is distributed with lightning speed around the globe, so that a steadily growing stream of value (money capital) may be concentrated in the hands of an ever smaller percentage (if not necessarily a shrinking absolute number) of the world's active population. Today's real masters are to be found in probably no more than 1,000 or 2,000 firms the world over.\" Footnote 118 — The Pyramid Theory of Capitalism. This is a rich footnote. Mandel explains that the concentration described \"does not mean... that the hundreds of thousands of smaller capitalist entrepreneurs, and the several million capitalist rentier families, are not part of the world bourgeoisie, but simply that they no longer command the decisive means of production or take the key investment decisions. Bourgeois society has the form of a pyramid in which the summit of monopolists could not survive without the support of different strata of large and medium bourgeois and their retainers (as well as the, at least partial, support of sections of the petty bourgeoisie).\" Crucially, Mandel warns against the anti-monopoly political strategy: \"The notion that capitalism could be abolished by eliminating the monopolists alone does not take account of the fact that capitalism inevitably grows out of even petty commodity production where conditions of money circulation and widespread private ownership of the means of production prevail. If a significant sector of medium-sized capitalist firms is retained (and some of the 'non-monopolist' capitalists are rather large-scale ones!) then capitalism would not only survive, but flourish and open up the road leading to the formation of new monopolies.\" This is a targeted political intervention against the \"anti-monopoly alliance\" strategy, influential in some Communist parties in the 1960s–70s. Mandel's point: neither the elimination of monopolists nor the preservation of \"competitive\" firms can overcome capitalism. The system reconstitutes itself out of petty commodity production as long as the general conditions of private property and money circulation persist. The Human Cost. The \"frenetic search for additional wealth in order to create even more wealth becomes increasingly divorced from basic human needs and interests, increasingly opposed to the 'production of a rich individuality' and the 'rich development of social relations' encompassing all human beings.\" This is a humanist-Marxist moment. The quote from the Grundrisse about \"rich individuality\" and \"rich development of social relations\" (the famous \"rich human being\" passage) is invoked as a normative counterpoint to capitalist wealth. The Inevitable Breakdown. \"But the process cannot continue smoothly and uninterruptedly: capital is powerless to overcome the basic contradictions of the commodity and private property. From both sides, the contradictions of production for its own sake (i.e. production in order to augment the profits of those who own the major means of production) must lead to periodic discharge in huge social and economic convulsions.\" The Empirical Confirmation. \"Following the social explosion initiated in the Western world by May '68 in France, the severe generalized recession of 1974-5 has confirmed Marx's basic analysis.\" Footnote 119: \"See the last chapter of Late Capitalism , and my articles on the generalized recession of the international capitalist economy in Inprecor (16 January 1975, 5 June 1975, 18 December 1975 and 15 September 1976).\" Mandel wrote this Introduction in the midst of the 1974-5 recession. The dual event — May 1968's social explosion and 1974-5's economic recession — is presented as practical confirmation of the theoretical claims. The Final Theorem. \"Capitalist growth cannot but be uneven, disproportionate and unharmonious. Expanded reproduction necessarily gives rise to contracted reproduction. Prosperity inexorably leads to over-production. The search for the philosopher's stone which would enable market economy (i.e. private property, i.e. competition) to coincide with balanced growth, and mass consumption to develop apace with productive capacity (despite the capitalists' drive to force up the rate of exploitation) — this search will go on as long as the system survives. But it will be no more crowned with success than that which has already been conducted for more than 150 years. The only possible remedy for economic crises of over-production and social crises of class struggle is the elimination of capitalism and class society. No other solution will be found, either in theory or in practice. This awe-inspiring prediction made by Marx has been borne out by empirical evidence ever since Capital was written. There is no sign that it will be contradicted by current or future developments.\" ERNEST MANDEL This is the closing movement: from the abstract to the historical, from the Grundrisse 's vision of \"rich individuality\" to the hard realities of 1974-5, from the reproduction schemas to the revolutionary conclusion. The prose rate accelerates into a kind of theoretical percussion. Expanded reproduction gives rise to contracted reproduction. Prosperity to over-production. The only remedy is the elimination of capitalism. No qualifications, no hedging. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed 1. The Primacy of the \"Intertwining\" Over the \"Elements.\" The deepest methodological lesson of Mandel's Introduction is that Marx's theoretical innovation in Volume 2 lies not in any single concept but in the intertwining itself — the demonstration that the reproduction of capital is impossible to understand except as a simultaneous movement of value-flow and money-flow, of production and circulation, of individual capitals and total social capital. All methodological errors Mandel diagnoses — from Samuelson's \"public selling capital goods\" to Luxemburg's \"single total capital\" to Dobb's value-blind socialist planning — arise from breaking the intertwining apart. 2. The Dual Nature of Everything Capitalist. The commodity's duality (use-value / exchange-value, concrete labour / abstract labour) is the seed of every duality Marx develops: the dual aspect of turnover (value-realization / use-value adequacy), the dual flow (commodity-flow / money-flow), the dual meaning of proportionality conditions, the duality of the two departments, the duality of \"productive for the individual capitalist\" vs. \"productive for capital as a whole,\" the duality of consumption (productive / unproductive). Mandel's Introduction is, in a sense, a systematic unfolding of the consequences of the commodity form. 3. Equilibrium as Exception, Disequilibrium as Rule. This is the central theorem. The schemas prove that growth is possible under capitalism; they do not prove it is automatic . Equilibrium is a \"marginal exception.\" Disproportionality, over-production, and crisis are not breakdowns of the system but its normal mode of functioning . Marx's dialectic — \"proportionality presents itself as a constant process through disproportionality\" — is not a verbal paradox but a substantive theoretical claim. 4. Abstraction as Staging, Not Assumption. Marx's method of successive approximation means that abstractions at one level of analysis are provisional , to be concretized at the next stage. Critics from Luxemburg to Harris who treat Marx's \"assumptions\" as empirical claims miss the methodological structure. This is a fundamental lesson for reading Capital as a whole. 5. The Historicity of Categories. Both the reproduction schemas and the productive/unproductive labour distinction are historically specific to the capitalist mode of production. To extend them to socialist or pre-capitalist societies is to commit errors of category, with consequences that are both theoretical and political. The socialist societies do not have \"surplus-value\" that grows at rates determined by reproduction schemas; they have surplus product subject to wholly different social goals (reduction of work-load, satisfaction of rational needs, withering of commodity production). 6. The Centrality of Time. Mandel's insistence on the \"time dimension\" is a major contribution to reading Volume 2. Time enters the analysis in multiple registers: as the measure of value (labour-time), as the medium of turnover (circuit of capital), as the horizon of laws of motion (trade cycles, class struggle cycles, long-term historical cycles), and as the existential substance of human life. To neglect time — as both pure production theory and Keynesian money-flow theory sometimes do — is to miss what makes Volume 2 the dynamic analysis that it is. Practical, Theoretical, and Political Implications For Crisis Theory. Mandel's reconstruction of a multi-causal theory — periodic decline in the rate of profit, anarchy of production, restricted consumption of the masses, hoarding, discontinuous fixed-capital renewal, devalorization of capital — is a decisive rejection of any single-cause theory. Both \"under-consumptionist\" (Sismondi, Malthus, Sweezy) and \"disproportionist\" (Tugan-Baranowski, Hilferding, Bauer) explanations are partial. The crisis is overdetermined . And crucially, crises are not merely destructive: they are mechanisms for the devalorization of capital through which the decline in the rate of profit is periodically overcome. For the Theory of Imperialism. The section on commercial and financial capital's participation in surplus-value distribution silently reframes the whole problematic of underdevelopment. Primitive accumulation and productive accumulation are not successive stages but \"simultaneous and combined phenomena.\" The one-sided expansion of unproductive capital (merchant, usury, banking) at the expense of industrialization explains why some peripheral regions fail to industrialize — not for lack of resources, but because the balance of class forces and the specific form of integration into world capitalism direct accumulation into unproductive channels. This is a structural , not merely moral, theory of underdevelopment. For Class Analysis. Mandel's definition of the proletariat — the class compelled by socio-economic compulsion to sell its labour-power — includes unproductive wage-labourers (clerks, civil servants, service workers, domestic servants), while excluding managers whose salary permits capital accumulation. The political implication is that the working class has a material interest in defending socialized wages and social expenditure — the theory of \"all state expenditure as surplus-value,\" far from being a radical position, is objectively a position of indifference to welfare cuts. For Debates on State Capitalism. The critique of Bukharin, Cliff, and the \"state capitalism\" school is sharp: reading state capitalism out of the absence of crises in the USSR commits the error of assuming that eliminating the \"anarchy of production\" eliminates crises. Theoretically, this contradicts Marx's own analysis; empirically, the claim is devoid of meaning because it lacks a determinate class analysis of the Soviet social formation. For Political Strategy. The footnote-118 warning against the anti-monopoly strategy — \"capitalism inevitably grows out of even petty commodity production\" — rejects the political theory that monopoly capitalism can be overcome by allying with \"non-monopoly\" capital. The system reconstitutes itself. Only the elimination of the general conditions — private property in the means of production and generalized money circulation — opens the path beyond. For Ecology. The brief mention of Raubbau der Natur (\"destruction/plunder of nature\") and the ecological threats signals a nascent awareness — in 1976 — that \"mastery of nature\" and \"destruction of nature\" are two names for the same process. Twenty-first-century ecological Marxism owes much to this recognition. For Methodology. The single most important lesson is the rigor with which Mandel insists on the distinction between levels of analysis. To confuse \"capital in general\" with \"many capitals,\" to confuse value calculation with price of production calculation, to confuse the schemas' equilibrium conditions with historical assertions about capitalist development — these are not merely technical errors. They are errors that reproduce themselves politically: neo-harmonicism, the \"law of priority development of department I,\" the \"achievements of socialist planning,\" and the \"all state expenditure = surplus-value\" position all rest on the same fundamental confusion. Broader Philosophical Implications Mandel's Introduction is, finally, a work of philosophical anthropology in disguise. The two departments of reproduction are not arbitrary: they express the \"essential character of human production in general\" — that man must metabolize with nature, and can only do so by using tools. Capitalist reproduction is thus a specific form of a general human process, and its contradictions are specific contradictions within the general process. When Marx writes of \"constant relative over-production\" under socialism, and when Engels writes of directly calculating labour-time, and when both write of \"rich individuality\" and \"rich development of social relations,\" they are pointing to a possible form of human reproduction in which the contradictions of the commodity form — the deferred recognition of social labour, the pathological separation of use-value and exchange-value, the impossibility of knowing in advance whether labour has been socially validated — would be overcome , not merely managed. The \"sealed book\" is thus not sealed because it is technical. It is sealed because it contains the most radical and demanding message: capitalism's own dynamic — its spiraling growth, its technological revolutions, its global reach — is inseparable from its self-destruction . To read Volume 2 carefully is to understand why the search for a capitalism without crises is the search for a philosopher's stone. There is no such thing. There is only the elimination of the conditions that make the search necessary. Mandel's concluding sentence — \"There is no sign that it will be contradicted by current or future developments\" — is a wager. Written in the aftermath of the 1974-5 recession, at the peak of Mandel's influence, at the close of a long period of capitalist expansion, it claims that the theoretical apparatus of Volume 2 remains adequate to every subsequent development. It is a wager that, fifty years on, we are still in a position to evaluate. ← Previous Chapter Volume 1 • Appendix Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 1"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 1", "title": "Chapter 1", "subtitle": "The Circuit of Money Capital", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 1/Chapter 1 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 22547, "read_time": "113 min read", "tags": ["Circuits of Capital (M-C-M')", "Money, Currency & Circulation"], "keywords": ["Circuit of Money Capital (M-C...P...C'-M')", "First Stage of Circulation (M-C: L & MP)", "Class Relation Presupposed in Money Purchase", "Transformation of Money into Productive Capital", "Mercantilist Illusions of Money Capital"], "text": "Chapter 1 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 22,528 words ⏱️ ~113 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem: The Forms Capital Assumes as It Moves Chapter 1 of Volume 2 of Capital — titled \"The Circuit of Money Capital\" — is not a re-telling of Volume 1 but a deliberate shift of analytical register. In Volume 1, Marx's object was the production process : how surplus-value is created in the factory, how the exploitation of labour-power works, how the value of commodities decomposes into constant capital, variable capital, and surplus-value. The forms that capital \"clothes itself in\" as it moves — money, commodities, productive assets — appeared there only instrumentally, \"in so far as this was necessary for the understanding of the second stage, the capitalist production process.\" The forms themselves \"remained uninvestigated.\" Volume 2, and this chapter specifically, opens a new problem: the metamorphoses of capital. The chapter's stated, immediate object is those forms \"in their pure state.\" This is a study of capital as a movement — as a process that is perpetually discarding one form and assuming another, that must pass through circulation, then production, then circulation again, and must return to its starting point to repeat the cycle. Marx's central thesis here is deceptively simple but extraordinarily consequential: capital is not a thing but a process, and it is only intelligible as a continuously self-reproducing circuit that assumes, discards, and re-assumes three distinct functional forms — money capital, productive capital, and commodity capital. The chapter's chosen entry point is the circuit of money capital , symbolised as: $$M - C \\dots P \\dots C' - M'$$ This formula is the analytic spine of the entire chapter. Every section is an excavation of what makes this sequence a circuit rather than a mere set of transactions, and of what each symbol presupposes. The Intellectual Objective: Abstracting to the Pure Forms of Movement Marx is explicit about his method at the outset. To \"grasp these forms in their pure state,\" he must \"first of all abstract from all aspects that have nothing to do with the change and constitution of the forms as such.\" Concretely, he brackets two things: (a) that commodities are sold at their values, and (b) that the circumstances of sale do not change, and (c) any changes of value occurring during the cyclical process. This is a theoretical purge — a controlled simplification designed to isolate the anatomy of the circuit. The task is thus formal and dialectical rather than empirical or historical. The intellectual objective is threefold: 1. To establish the circuit as the fundamental unity of circulation and production. Capital's life is not reducible to either sphere alone. Volume 1 showed that surplus-value arises in production. Volume 2's opening chapter shows that this surplus-value cannot be realised without circulation, and that the whole process must return to its starting point for capital to function as capital at all. 2. To identify the specific functional forms of industrial capital. Money capital, productive capital, and commodity capital are not \"independent varieties of capital, whose functions constitute the content of branches of business that are independent and separate from one another.\" They are particular functional forms of one thing — industrial capital — which takes on all three in turn. This is a critical polemic against the \"scholastic economics\" that would treat these as separate capital-types. 3. To expose the ideological illusions generated by the money form. Because the circuit begins and ends with money, it produces the \"crude, non-conceptual\" appearance of money breeding money (M...M'), which is the root of the \"Monetary System\" and the \"Mercantile System.\" The chapter is thus simultaneously an anatomy of capital and a critique of the ideological forms thrown up by capital's most superficial appearance. Where the Chapter Sits in the Theoretical Framework This is the opening of Volume 2, \"The Process of Circulation of Capital,\" and it functions as the bridge between Volume 1 (production) and Volume 3 (the process of capitalist production as a whole). The chapter introduces the most fundamental abstraction of Volume 2: the circuit — the cyclical self-movement of capital through its forms. Two sibling circuits will be examined later: the circuit of productive capital (P...P) and the circuit of commodity capital (C'...C'). This chapter establishes the money-capital circuit while constantly foreshadowing the others, noting that \"before the second circuit is complete, the first circuit C'...C' has been described\" and that M...M' overlaps and interlocks with P...P. The chapter also introduces and deploys a series of key Volume 2 categories: money capital, productive capital, commodity capital, industrial capital, and the distinction between the formal metamorphoses of the circulation sphere and the real metamorphosis of the production process. It presupposes and builds upon Volume 1's results — the theory of surplus-value, the wage form, the class relation, the historical separation of producers from means of production — without re-deriving them. The result is a work of exceptionally dense theoretical architecture: a piece of dialectical analysis in which every symbol of a formula becomes the occasion for deep excavation of social and economic relations. The Argumentative Strategy Marx proceeds in four great movements, each corresponding to a numbered section: The first stage, M–C — the transformation of money capital into productive capital. Here Marx shows that the act of purchase is simultaneously an act of capital because of its material content : the money divides into L (labour-power) and mp (means of production), in a quantitative ratio conditioned from the start by the surplus labour the workers must perform. This section contains some of the chapter's most important theoretical work: the analysis of M–L as the \"characteristic moment,\" the diagnosis of the two \"interconnected errors\" in the conception of money capital, the demonstration that the class relation is presupposed in the money relation, and the crucial concept of distribution of the elements of production themselves . The second stage, P — the function of productive capital. This is the interruption, the \"real metamorphosis.\" Here Marx shows how the wage-labourer's hand-to-mouth existence necessitates a permanent supply of money capital, how the division of labour deepens with commodity production, how capitalist commodity production dissolves earlier forms of production, and how the specific way workers and means of production are connected distinguishes economic epochs. He introduces the constant/variable capital distinction as it appears in the circuit and derives the value formula P + s. The third stage, C'–M' — the transformation of commodity capital into money capital. Here Marx works out, with almost obsessive numerical precision, the structure of the value of a commodity product (C = C + c, £422 + £78 = £500 in the yarn example), the \"value ratio\" that makes C into C', the double role of money as the returning form of the capital value and the first transformative form of the surplus-value, and the \"crude, non-conceptual\" character of M' — the homogeneity of a money sum in which every trace of the process has been effaced. This section culminates in the demonstration that the form M...M' (M + m) is \"irrational\" because \"part of a sum of money appears as the mother of another part of the same sum of money,\" and that this irrationality only disappears in gold production, where M' is intrinsically a commodity product. The circuit as a whole — the synthesis, in which Marx names industrial capital as the only mode of existence of capital in which both the appropriation and the creation of surplus-value are functions of capital. Here he lays out the four defining features of the M...M' circuit, the \"deceptive\" and \"illusory character\" it necessarily carries, and the two ideological systems (Monetary, Mercantile) that arise from taking that appearance as the essence. The chapter ends by resolving the apparent priority of M–C into the deeper truth: the circuit of money capital is the \"general form\" of the circuit of industrial capital, precisely because it presupposes capitalist production — hence P...P, the circuit of productive capital, is logically prior even though M...M' appears to come first. The Rhetorical and Analytical Style Readers should be prepared for a chapter that is at once a work of high formalism and a work of sharp polemic. Marx moves fluidly between abstract formula and concrete numerical example (the spinning mill, the yarn), between dialectical elucidation and historical illustration (Russian landowners after emancipation, capitalist world trade with China, India, the Arabs), and between economic analysis and philosophical critique (the \"irrationality\" of the wage form, the contrast between the \"rational\" form of C' and the \"effaced\" form of M'). The prose is compressed; subordinate arguments are often buried in footnotes or parentheticals; and Engels's editorial interventions (notably the parenthetical about \"fits of giddiness\" inserted in the 1893 second edition, and the footnote about begrifflos versus begrifflich ) are themselves part of the chapter's received texture. The reader must therefore be alive to the palimpsest quality of the text — it is drawn from multiple manuscripts (Manuscripts II, VII, VI, V) composed at different times, which explains some shifts of emphasis. The overarching argument that emerges is this: the circuit of money capital is the most \"one-sided, hence most striking and characteristic\" form of appearance of the circuit of industrial capital , because its aim and driving motive — \"the valorization of value, money-making and accumulation\" — \"leaps to the eye.\" But this very vividness is a trap: \"buying in order to sell dearer\" is the appearance, while the substance is the real metamorphosis of production, the creation of surplus-value, and the historically specific class relation that makes it possible. The chapter's real thesis is thus simultaneously an insight and a warning: the form in which capital presents itself most directly to consciousness is the form in which its inner nature is most obscured. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Framework: Three Stages and the Formula of the Circuit Marx opens by restating, from the vantage of Volume 1, the three stages of the circuit of capital. The first stage is the appearance of the capitalist on the commodity and labour markets \"as a buyer\"; his money transforms into commodities — the act of circulation M–C. The second stage is \"productive consumption by the capitalist of the commodities purchased\": he functions as \"capitalist producer of commodities,\" his capital \"passes through the production process,\" and the result is \"commodities of greater value than their elements of production.\" The third stage is his return to the market \"as a seller\": his commodities transform into money — the act of circulation C–M. Out of these three stages, Marx writes the formula of the circuit of money capital: $$M - C \\dots P \\dots C' - M'$$ Three notational conventions deserve careful attention, because subsequent arguments depend on them. The dots (…) indicate that \"the circulation process is interrupted\" — that between buying inputs and selling outputs there lies a sphere not governed by the laws of circulation but by those of production. The primes (' ) denote \"an increase in C and M as the result of surplus-value\" — that C', as the value-expression of the product, is quantitatively greater than the C that entered, and that M', as the money eventually withdrawn, is greater than the M originally advanced. The notation thus encodes, in a single line, the entire logic of capital: it must circulate, must exit circulation into production, must return to circulation, and must return augmented . Marx immediately issues a methodological manifesto: in Volume 1, the first and third stages were discussed \"only in so far as this was necessary for the understanding of the second stage.\" The forms — the successive modes of existence of capital — \"remained uninvestigated.\" These forms will \"now be the immediate object of our inquiry.\" The analytic abstraction is then specified: commodities are sold at their values, circumstances do not change, and any changes of value occurring \"in the course of the cyclical process\" are ignored. This is a controlled theoretical environment in which the form-dynamics of the circuit can be observed without the noise of value-change. Note the footnote: \"This introductory section is taken from Manuscript II,\" signalling the composite nature of the text. Section I: The First Stage, M–C The Twofold Nature of M–C: Form versus Material Content The first substantive move is a distinction that governs the entire analysis of M–C. Taken abstractly, M–C \"represents the conversion of a sum of money into a sum of commodities; the buyer transforms his money into commodities, the sellers their commodities into money.\" This is the ordinary, symmetrical act of commodity circulation — nothing distinguishes it formally from any other purchase. What makes this particular act \"a part of the whole process with a well-defined function in the independent circuit of an individual capital\" is \"not primarily the form of the act, but rather its material content\" — the \"specific use character of the commodities that change place with money.\" This is the first deep lesson of the chapter: the capitalist character of the act M–C is not visible in its form as a circulation act, but only in what is bought. Those commodities are, on one hand, means of production (mp), on the other, labour-power (L) — \"the material and the personal factors of commodity production.\" Marx notes that \"their precise nature must of course depend on the type of article to be produced.\" He introduces notation to capture this. Let labour-power = L, means of production = mp, and the sum of commodities purchased = C. Then: $$C = L + mp$$ Or, abbreviating, $C \\binom{L}{mp}$. The act M–C, \"considered in respect of its content,\" is thus represented as $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$, and it breaks up into two distinct purchases: $$M - L \\quad \\text{and} \\quad M - mp$$ The money M \"divides into two portions, one for the purchase of labour-power, the other for means of production.\" Crucially, \"the two sets of purchases pertain to completely different markets: one to the commodity market proper, the other to the labour market.\" The single act M–C is therefore internally bifurcated — a unity that is also a duality, straddling two socially distinct spheres. The Qualitative and Quantitative Division of Money Capital Marx now presses beyond the qualitative division to a quantitative one, and this is one of the chapter's most important arguments. He writes that \"apart from this qualitative division of the commodities into which M is transformed, $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$ also exhibits a most characteristic quantitative relationship.\" The argument proceeds from the nature of the wage. \"The value or price of labour-power is paid to its proprietor… in the form of wages, i.e. as the price of a sum of labour that contains surplus labour.\" Marx illustrates: if the value of a day's labour-power is 3 shillings, the product of five hours' labour, \"this sum may figure in the contract between buyer and seller as the price or wage for perhaps ten hours' labour.\" If such a contract is made with fifty workers, \"they have to provide the buyer with a total of 500 hours' labour each day, half of this — 250 hours, or twenty-five ten-hour working-days — consisting simply of surplus labour.\" The consequence is direct and structural: \"The means of production to be purchased must be sufficient in quantity and volume to employ this amount of labour.\" This is the quantitative link between the two halves of M–C. The purchase of mp is not free-standing; it is calibrated to the mass of labour (and specifically the mass of surplus labour) that the purchase of L has secured. Thus $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$ expresses \"not simply the qualitative relationship in which a certain sum of money, e.g. £422, is transformed into means of production and labour-power of a corresponding sum, but also a quantitative ratio between the portions of the money spent on labour-power L and on means of production mp, this ratio being conditioned from the start by the excess or surplus labour that the number of workers involved have to expend.\" The numerical example is worth spelling out because it prefigures the yarn example used in the third stage. Fifty workers in a spinning mill, weekly wages £50; £372 to be spent on means of production, \"if this is the value of the means of production that a working week of 3,000 man-hours, 1,500 of these being surplus labour, transforms into yarn.\" So the ratio between the money spent on L and the money spent on mp is not arbitrary — it is determined by the amount of labour the workers will actually perform, of which half is surplus. The £50 buys 3,000 hours of labour-power (as the price of a week's labour-power for fifty workers), and the £372 buys the material that those 3,000 hours can work up into yarn. Marx then articulates the material necessity that underlies the ratio. \"The degree to which the expenditure of excess labour requires an excess value in the form of means of production is quite unimportant here.\" What matters is that \"under all circumstances the part of the money that is spent on means of production… must be sufficient, i.e. must be reckoned up from the start and be provided in appropriate proportions.\" The means of production \"must be sufficient in mass to absorb the mass of labour which is to be turned into products through them.\" He states the two failure modes with binary clarity: If sufficient means of production are not present , then the surplus labour the purchaser controls \"cannot be made use of; his right to dispose of it will lead to nothing.\" If more means of production are available than disposable labour , then \"these remain unsaturated with labour, and are not transformed into products.\" This is a statement about the technical and social preconditions of valorisation: capital must assemble L and mp in the correct proportions or the whole project fails, either because labour stands idle or because machinery stands idle. The quantitative structure of M–C is thus the material precondition of the valorisation that will occur in P. The Emergence of Productive Capital Once M – C $\\binom{L}{mp}$ is completed, Marx argues, the purchaser does not merely have the means to produce a useful article. He has \"a greater capacity to set labour-power in motion, or a greater quantity of labour, than is needed to replace the value of the labour-power,\" together with \"the means of production that are required to realize or objectify this amount of labour.\" He thus controls \"the factors of production for articles of a greater value than their elements of production, for a mass of commodities containing surplus-value.\" The value advanced in the form of money \"thus now exists in a natural form in which it can be realized as value which breeds surplus-value (in the shape of commodities). In other words, it exists in the state or form of productive capital , with the ability to function as creator of value and surplus-value. We call capital in this form P.\" Two clarifications follow. First, the magnitude of value is unchanged by the transformation of form: \"The value of P, however, equals the value of L + mp, that of the money M transformed into L + mp. M is the same capital value as P, only in a different mode of existence, i.e. capital value in the state or form of money — money capital .\" This is the first explicit naming of money capital as a form — not a distinct kind of wealth but a functional mode of existence of capital value. Second, Marx specifies the meaning of the act: \"M – C $\\binom{L}{mp}$, or M–C in its general form, a sum of commodity purchases — this act of general commodity circulation is thus at the same time, as a stage in the independent circuit of capital, the transformation of capital value from its money form into its productive form, or more briefly the transformation of money capital into productive capital .\" And: \"In the first figure of the circuit to be considered here, money appears as the original bearer of the capital value, and hence money capital appears as the form in which capital is advanced.\" Money Performing Monetary Functions — and the Two Errors A subtle but decisive passage now follows. \"As money capital, it exists in a state in which it can perform monetary functions, in the present case the functions of general means of purchase and payment.\" (Payment, because labour-power is paid for after it has done its work; and payment for mp too, where these are ordered rather than bought off the shelf.) \"Money capital does not possess this capacity because it is capital, but because it is money.\" This is a warning against a confusion that Marx will later name explicitly. On the other hand, \"the capital value in its monetary state can perform only monetary functions, and no others. What makes these into functions of capital is their specific role in the movement of capital, hence also the relationship between the stage in which they appear and the other stages of the capital circuit.\" So money functions as capital not because of any intrinsic property but because of its position in the circuit . In M–C, \"money is converted into commodities which in their combination constitute the natural form of productive capital; this form therefore already bears latently within it, as its possibility, the result of the capitalist production process.\" Then comes a crucial structural observation: \"A part of the money that performs the function of money capital in M – C $\\binom{L}{mp}$ passes over, by accomplishing this very circulation, into a function in which its capital character vanishes though its money character remains.\" This refers to the money that the worker receives (M–L from the capitalist's side is L–M from the worker's side) — for the worker, this money is simply money, a means of circulation for buying means of subsistence; its capital character is invisible from his standpoint. Marx then presents the worker's side in full. \"M–L, on the capitalist's part, is the purchase of labour-power; it is the sale of labour-power on the part of the worker, the owner of labour-power (we can say 'labour' here, as the wage form is presupposed).\" What is M–C (M–L) for the purchaser is \"here, as in every sale, L–M (C–M) for the seller (the worker), in this case the sale of his labour-power.\" The latter is \"the first stage of circulation, or the first metamorphosis of the commodity\" (referring back to Volume 1, Chapter 3). \"The worker spends the money thus received bit by bit on a sum of commodities that satisfy his needs, on articles of consumption.\" His overall circulation is thus: $$L - M - C$$ \"i.e. firstly L–M (C–M) and secondly M–C, i.e. in the general form of simple commodity circulation C–M–C, where money figures simply as an evanescent means of circulation, as merely mediating the conversion of one commodity into another.\" Note this carefully: for the worker , the exchange he enters is C–M–C. Money is a mere intermediary. The same physical coins that are money capital in the capitalist's circuit are mere money in the worker's circuit. This is a vivid instance of the chapter's central thesis that the character of a money-function depends entirely on its place in the circuit of capital, not on any property of the money itself. M–L as the Characteristic Moment; the Irrationality of the Wage Form Marx now states: \"M–L is the characteristic moment of the transformation of money capital into productive capital, for it is the essential condition without which the value advanced in the money form cannot really be transformed into capital, into value-producing surplus-value. M–mp is necessary only in order to realize the mass of labour bought by way of M–L. This is why M–L was presented from this point of view in Volume 1, Part Two, 'The Transformation of Money into Capital.' Here we have to consider the matter from a further aspect, with special reference to money capital as a form of appearance of capital.\" He then turns to a polemic against a common view. \"M–L is generally regarded as characteristic of the capitalist mode of production. But this is in no way for the reason just given\" — i.e. not because it secures surplus labour. \"It is rather on account of its form , because in the form of wages labour is bought with money, and this is taken as the characteristic feature of a 'money economy.'\" Marx objects that this reading takes the appearance for the essence. \"Here again, it is not the irrationality of the form that is taken as characteristic. This irrationality is rather overlooked.\" What is the irrationality? \"The irrationality consists in the fact that labour as the value-forming element cannot itself possess any value, and so a certain quantity of labour cannot have a value that is expressed in its price, in its equivalence with a certain definite quantity of money.\" This is a compressed restatement of Volume 1's critique of \"the wage form of value.\" Labour is the source of value and cannot itself be bought as a value ; what is bought is labour- power , whose price is wages. But this price \"presents itself\" as the price of the labour actually set in motion, so that \"the value produced by this labour-power in six hours' labour, say, is expressed as the value of its twelve-hour functioning or labour.\" The wage form thus systematically conceals the extraction of surplus labour by presenting the value produced in the whole working day as if it were the price of a single day's labour. The deeper point — developed against the \"money economy\" reading — is that \"M–L is taken as the characteristic feature or hallmark of the so-called money economy because labour appears here as the commodity of its possessor, and hence money as its buyer — in other words because of the money relation (sale and purchase of human activity).\" But: \"money appears very early on as a buyer of so-called services, without its being transformed into money capital, and without any revolution in the general character of the economy.\" Paying money for services is ancient and tells us nothing about the character of a mode of production. Marx writes: \"It is quite immaterial, as far as the money is concerned, what sort of commodities it is transformed into.\" Money is \"the universal equivalent form of all commodities,\" which already show in their prices that they \"ideally represent a specific sum of money, expect to be transformed into money, and only receive the form in which they can be converted into use-values for their possessor by changing places with money.\" Once labour-power appears on the market as a commodity, its sale \"taking place in the form of a payment for labour, in the wage form, then its sale and purchase is no more striking than the sale and purchase of any other commodity. What is characteristic is not that the commodity labour-power can be bought, but the fact that labour-power appears as a commodity.\" In other words, the bare fact that labour-power is bought and sold is not itself the signature of capitalism; what is distinctive is the social relation that forces the mass of producers to put their labour-power up for sale. The Class Relation Presupposed in the Money Relation This leads to one of the chapter's most celebrated passages. \"By way of $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$, the transformation of money capital into productive capital, the capitalist effects a connection between the objective and the personal factors of production, in so far as these factors consist of commodities.\" Marx first examines the temporal order from the capitalist's standpoint: \"If money is to be transformed for the first time into productive capital, or to function as money capital for the first time for its possessor, then he must first buy the means of production, i.e. buildings, machines, etc. before he buys labour-power; for when the labour-power passes into his control, the means of production must also be present before it can be applied as labour-power.\" From the worker's side, the picture is altogether different. \"The productive application of his labour-power is possible only when this has been associated with the means of production, as the result of its sale. Before the sale, this labour-power exists in a state of separation from the means of production, from the objective conditions of its application.\" In this separation, labour-power \"can be directly used neither for the production of use-values for its possessor, nor for the production of commodities which he could live from selling. But as soon as it is associated with the means of production, by being sold it forms a component of the productive capital of its buyer just as much as the means of production do.\" The heart of the argument: \"although in the act M–L the possessor of money and the possessor of labour-power relate to each other only as buyer and seller, confront each other as possessor of money and possessor of a commodity, and are thus from this point of view simply in a money relationship with each other, the buyer appears right from the start as the possessor of the means of production which form the objective conditions for the productive expenditure of labour-power by its possessor.\" These means of production \"confront the possessor of labour-power as someone else's property.\" Conversely, \"the buyer… is confronted by the seller of labour as another's labour-power which must pass into his control, and has to be incorporated into his capital in order for this really to function as productive capital.\" From this it follows: \"The class relation between capitalist and wage-labourer is thus already present, already presupposed, the moment that the two confront each other in the act M–L (L–M from the side of the worker). This is a sale and purchase, a money relation, but a sale and purchase in which it is presupposed that the buyer is a capitalist and the seller a wage-labourer; and this relation does in fact exist, because the conditions for the realization of labour-power, i.e. means of subsistence and means of production, are separated, as the property of another, from the possessor of labour-power.\" This is a decisive theoretical point. The \"money relation\" of buyer and seller in the labour market is superficially one of equality — equal contracting parties. But it presupposes a deeper inequality: the buyer's access to the objective conditions of labour, and the seller's dispossession of them. The class relation is not generated by the money relation but presupposed by it and expressed through it. Marx then addresses the question of origin. \"We are not concerned here with how this separation arises. If M–L takes place, it already exists.\" The present argument abstracts from the history that produced the separation (which is treated in Volume 1, Part Eight, on primitive accumulation). What matters now is the structural point: \"if M–L appears as a function of money capital, or money appears here as a form of existence of capital, then this is in no way simply because money is involved here as the means of payment for a human activity with a useful effect, for a service; thus in no way because of money's function as means of payment.\" Money \"can be spent in this form only because labour-power is found in a state of separation from its means of production (including the means of subsistence as means of production of labour-power itself); and because this separation is abolished only through the sale of labour-power to the owner of the means of production, a sale which signifies that the buyer is now in control of the continuous flow of labour-power, a flow which by no means has to stop when the amount of labour necessary to reproduce the price of labour-power has been performed.\" That last clause captures the essential point: because labour-power can be made to work beyond the point at which it has reproduced its own price, the buyer acquires a claim on a flow of labour that yields surplus. The conclusion is one of the chapter's key aphorisms: \"The capital relation arises only in the production process because it exists implicitly in the act of circulation, in the basically different economic conditions in which buyer and seller confront one another, in their class relation. It is not the nature of money that gives rise to this relation; it is rather the existence of the relation that can transform a mere function of money into a function of capital.\" The Two Errors in the Conception of Money Capital Marx now makes explicit the confusion he has been diagnosing. \"In the conception of money capital we customarily find two interconnected errors (for the time being we only deal with money capital in connection with the specific function in which it confronts us here).\" First error: \"the functions that capital value performs as money capital, and which it is able to perform because it happens to be in the money form, are erroneously ascribed to its character as capital, whereas they are simply due to the money state of the capital value, its form of appearance as money.\" — i.e., people mistake money-functions (means of purchase, means of payment) for capital-functions. Second error, \"inversely\": \"the specific content of the money function that makes it simultaneously a function of capital is deduced from the nature of money (money is here confused with capital), whereas this function presupposes social conditions, as here in the act M–L, that are in no way given simply by commodity circulation and the money circulation corresponding to it.\" — i.e., people imagine that because money can buy labour-power, money somehow inherently possesses the property of generating capital. Both errors converge on the same confusion: they obliterate the distinction between the general properties of money and the historically specific social relation that makes a money-function into a capital-function. The slavery analogy sharpens the point. \"The purchase and sale of slaves is also in its form a purchase and sale of commodities. Without the existence of slaves, however, money cannot fulfil this function. If there is slavery, then money can be spent on the acquisition of slaves. But money in the hand of the buyer is in no way a sufficient condition for the existence of slavery.\" Money is necessary but not sufficient for a social relation; the relation must exist first. Primitive Separation, Distribution of the Elements of Production, and the Russian Landowners Marx then escalates from the individual act to the social totality . \"If the sale of one's own labour-power (in the form of the sale of one's own labour, or the wage form) is not an isolated phenomenon, but the socially decisive precondition for the production of commodities, i.e. if money capital performs the function here considered, $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$, throughout society, this fact implies the occurrence of historic processes through which the original connection between means of production and labour-power was dissolved; processes as a result of which the mass of the people, the workers, come face to face with the non-workers, the former as non-owners, the latter as the owners, of these means of production.\" He adds the important caveat: \"It is quite irrelevant whether the original connection, before it was destroyed, took the form that the worker belonged together with the other means of production as a means of production himself, or whether he was their owner.\" — i.e., whether the prior condition was slavery or independent proprietorship, the point is the same: the connection has been dissolved. This yields a crucial definition of distribution : \"Thus the situation that underlies the act $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$ is one of distribution; not distribution in the customary sense of distribution of the means of consumption, but rather the distribution of the elements of production themselves, with the objective factors concentrated on one side, and labour-power isolated from them on the other.\" This is a theoretical redefinition of \"distribution\" — away from the distribution of goods (which is what conventional economics means) and toward the distribution of the means of production , i.e. the class structuring of property. Underlying the simple purchase M–L is a whole social distribution of productive resources that makes the purchase possible. \"The means of production, the objective portion of productive capital, must thus already face the worker as such, as capital, before the act M–L can become general throughout society.\" At the level of society, the means of production must be capital — owned by non-workers — before wage labour can be universal. Marx then cites Volume 1: \"We have already seen how capitalist production, once it is established, not only reproduces this separation in the course of its development, but also expands on an ever greater scale until it has become the generally prevailing social condition.\" Note the dialectic: the separation precedes capitalist production historically, and capitalist production reproduces and expands it. There is a circularity — a self-reproducing structure — that is essential to the system's stability. But the chapter also notes the preconditions of this reproduction: \"For capital to be formed and to take hold of production, trade must have developed to a certain level, hence also commodity circulation and, with that, commodity production; for articles cannot go into circulation as commodities except in so far as they are produced for sale, i.e. as commodities. It is only on the basis of capitalist production that commodity production appears as the normal, prevailing character of production.\" — So there is a historical progression from developed trade → commodity production → capitalist commodity production. The empirical illustration is one of the chapter's most vivid. Russian landowners, after the \"so-called emancipation of the peasants,\" now farm with wage-labourers instead of serf forced labour. They complain of two things. First, \"the lack of money capital\" — they say that before the harvest is sold, wage-labourers must be paid a considerable amount, and they lack ready cash. \"Capital in the form of money must constantly be available, precisely for the payment of wages, in order that production may be conducted on a capitalist basis.\" Marx's deadpan reply: \"the landlords need not worry. Everything comes to those who wait, and in time the industrial capitalist will have at his disposal not only his own money, but also l'argent des autres [other people's money].\" — a jab at the emerging credit system. The second complaint is more theoretically revealing: \"even when they have money, the labour-power to be bought is not available in sufficient quantity and at the right time. This is because the Russian agricultural worker, owing to the common ownership of the soil by the village community, is not yet fully separated from his means of production, and is thus still not a 'free wage-labourer' in the full sense of the term.\" — The Russian peasant, retaining access to communal land, is not compelled to sell his labour-power; he is not fully \"free in the double sense\" that Volume 1 identified (free to sell, free of property). Hence the wage-labour market cannot function reliably. \"But the presence of such 'free wage-labourers' throughout society is the indispensable condition without which M–C, the transformation of money into commodities, cannot take the form of the transformation of money capital into productive capital.\" The conclusion of the whole first-stage argument follows: \"It goes without saying, therefore, that the formula for the circuit of money capital: M–C…P…C'–M', is the self-evident form of the circuit of capital only on the basis of already developed capitalist production, because it presupposes the availability of the class of wage-labourers in sufficient numbers throughout society.\" And: \"Since the first precondition of M–C…P…C'–M' is the continuous availability of the class of wage-labourers, it already implies the existence of capital in the form of productive capital, and hence the form of the circuit of productive capital.\" — The circuit of money capital implies the circuit of productive capital; the apparent priority of M will be shown to be merely apparent. This is the seed of the chapter's concluding argument. Section 2: The Second Stage — The Function of Productive Capital Circulation Interrupted The circuit began with M–C (purchase). \"This circulation must therefore be supplemented by the opposite metamorphosis C–M, the transformation of commodities into money, i.e. sale. But the direct result of $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$ is an interruption in the circulation of the capital value advanced in the money form.\" Why? \"By the transformation of money capital into productive capital, the capital value has received a natural form in which it cannot circulate any further, but has to go into consumption, that is into productive consumption.\" The reasoning is precise: \"The use of labour-power, labour, can be realized only in the labour process. The capitalist cannot sell the worker again as a commodity, for he is not his slave, and the capitalist has bought nothing more than the utilization of his labour-power for a certain time. He can make use of this labour-power only in so far as it enables him to make use of the means of production to fashion commodities. The result of the first stage is thus capital's entry into the second stage, its productive stage.\" The movement is thus $M - C \\binom{L}{mp} \\dots P$, \"the dots indicating that the circulation of capital is interrupted; but its circuit continues, with its passage from the sphere of commodity circulation into that of production.\" Hence: \"The first stage, the transformation of money capital into productive capital, thus appears as no more than the prelude and introduction to the second stage, the function of productive capital.\" — The circulation act is subordinate to the production act; the whole point of M–C is to enable P. Marx adds a structural note about M–C: \"$M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$ presupposes that the individual who performs this act does not just have at his disposal values in some useful form or other, but that he possesses these values in money form, that he is the possessor of money. The act, however, consists precisely in letting go of money, and the possessor of money can only remain so in so far as the money will implicitly flow back to him as a result of the very act of letting go of it. This act thus presupposes that he is a commodity producer.\" — To spend money and remain a money-possessor, one must be able to recover it. So the money-spender in M–C is already a producer of commodities for sale. The Wage-Labourer's Hand-to-Mouth Existence and the Permanence of Money Capital A crucial argument now emerges about money capital's necessity . \"M–L. The wage-labourer lives only from the sale of his labour-power. Its maintenance — his own maintenance — requires daily consumption. His payment must therefore be constantly repeated at short intervals, to enable him to repeat the purchases — the act L–M–C or C–M–C — that are needed for this self-maintenance. Hence the capitalist must constantly confront him as money capitalist, and his capital as money capital.\" This is footnote-flagged as important: the wage relationship structurally requires that capital continuously appear in the money form. \"On the other hand, however, in order that the mass of direct producers, the wage-labourers, may perform the act L–M–C, they must constantly encounter the necessary means of subsistence in purchasable form, i.e. in the form of commodities. Thus this situation in itself demands a high degree of circulation of products as commodities, i.e. commodity production on a large scale. As soon as production by way of wage-labour becomes general, commodity production must be the general form of production.\" Division of Labour, M–mp, and the Universalisation of Commodity Production \"Assuming this to be the case, commodity production in turn brings about an ever growing division of social labour, i.e. an ever greater specialization of the products produced as commodities by particular capitalists, an ever greater division of complementary production processes into independent ones. M–mp therefore develops to the same degree as M–L, i.e. the production of means of production is separated to the same extent from the production of the commodities whose means of production they are; these too confront each commodity producer as commodities which he does not himself produce, but buys for the purpose of his particular production process. They come from branches of production that are pursued in complete separation and independence from his own, and enter his branch of production as commodities, which must therefore be bought. The material conditions of commodity production confront him to an ever greater extent as the products of other commodity producers, as commodities. The capitalist must appear to the same extent as a money capitalist, i.e. his capital must function in a greater measure as money capital.\" This is an important passage about the co-development of money capital's necessity and social division of labour. As production becomes more specialised, the capitalist must buy more of his inputs from others — which means he must appear on the market as a buyer of commodities more extensively — which means his capital must take the money form more frequently and extensively. The very division of labour that raises productivity also deepens capitalism's dependence on the money form and circulation. The Dissolving Effect of Capitalist Commodity Production on Earlier Forms Marx then sketches the historical dynamics: \"On the other hand, the same circumstance that produces the basic condition for capitalist production, the existence of a class of wage-labourers, encourages the transition of all commodity production to capitalist commodity production. To the extent that the latter develops, it has a destroying and dissolving effect on all earlier forms of production, which, being pre-eminently aimed at satisfying the direct needs of the producers, only transform their excess products into commodities. It makes the sale of the product the main interest, at first without apparently attacking the mode of production itself; this was for example the first effect of capitalist world trade on such peoples as the Chinese, Indians, Arabs, etc. Once it has taken root, however, it destroys all forms of commodity production that are based either on the producers' own labour, or simply on the sale of the excess product as a commodity. It firstly makes commodity production universal, and then gradually transforms all commodity production into capitalist production.\" This is a compressed account of capitalist expansion — first, the corrosive effects of world trade on non-capitalist economies (making sale the main interest without immediately changing the mode of production), then the full transformative impact (destroying artisan and petty-commodity production, making commodity production universal, then converting it into capitalist production). The footnote indicates this section is from Manuscript VII (up to p. 12, where it switches to Manuscript VI). The Union of Workers and Means of Production as Epoch-Distinguishing Criterion Marx then offers an abstract formulation: \"Whatever the social form of production, workers and means of production always remain its factors. But if they are in a state of mutual separation, they are only potentially factors of production. For any production to take place, they must be connected. The particular form and mode in which this connection is effected is what distinguishes the various economic epochs of the social structure.\" — The mode of connection between workers and means of production is the ultimate criterion distinguishing historical epochs. This is a foundational methodological claim of the entire Marxian framework. \"In the present case, the separation of the free worker from his means of production is the given starting point, and we have seen how and under what conditions the two come to be united in the hands of the capitalist — i.e. as his capital in its productive mode of existence. The actual process which the personal and material elements of commodity formation, brought together in this way, enter into with each other, the process of production, therefore itself becomes a function of capital — the capitalist production process, whose nature we have gone into in detail in the first volume of this work.\" Then the crucial historical claim: \"All pursuit of commodity production becomes at the same time pursuit of the exploitation of labour-power; but only capitalist commodity production is an epoch-making mode of exploitation, which in the course of its historical development revolutionizes the entire economic structure of society by its organization of the labour process and its gigantic extension of technique, and towers incomparably above all earlier epochs.\" — This is the thesis of capitalist specificity: not commodity production as such, not exploitation as such, but their capitalist integration and its revolutionary consequences for technique and organisation. Constant and Variable Capital; P + s The chapter then introduces, from the standpoint of the circuit, the constant/variable distinction. \"By the different roles that they play during the production process in connection with the formation of value, and thus in the creation of surplus-value, means of production and labour-power, in so far as they are forms of existence of the capital value advanced, are distinguished as constant and variable capital .\" Note that the distinction is specified as the roles played in the valorisation process — not as a distinction between physical types of things, but between functions of value. \"They are further distinguished, as different components of productive capital, by the fact that the means of production, once in the possession of the capitalist, remain his capital even outside the production process, whereas labour-power becomes the form of existence of an individual capital only within this process. If labour-power is only a commodity in the hands of its seller, the wage-labourer, it only becomes capital in the hands of its buyer, the capitalist, to whom falls its temporary use. The means of production, for their part, become objective forms of productive capital, or productive capital proper, only from the moment that labour-power, as the personal form of existence of productive capital, can be incorporated into them. The means of production are no more capital by nature than is human labour-power. They receive this specific social character only under certain particular conditions that have historically developed, just as it is only under such conditions that precious metals are stamped with the character of money, or money with that of money capital.\" This last sentence is a compressed rejection of any trans-historical \"capital-ness\" of things: neither machines nor money nor labour-power are in themselves capital. They become capital only under a historically specific social relation. The parallel between money's character (a socially-specific stamp on precious metals) and capital's character (a socially-specific relation on things) is one of the chapter's most philosophically resonant passages. \"In the course of its functioning, productive capital consumes its own components, to convert them into a mass of products of a higher value. Since labour-power operates only as an organ of capital, the excess value with which surplus labour endows the product, over and above that of its constituent elements, is also the fruit of capital. Labour-power's surplus labour is labour performed gratis for capital, and hence forms surplus-value for the capitalist, a value that costs him no equivalent.\" The value formula: \"The product is therefore not only a commodity, but a commodity impregnated with surplus-value. Its value is P + s, the value of the productive capital P consumed in its production plus that of the surplus-value s it engenders. Let us suppose that this commodity consists of 10,000 lb of yarn, with means of production to the value of £372 and labour-power to the value of £50 used up in its production. During the spinning process, the spinners transferred to the yarn the value of the means of production consumed in the process by means of their labour, £372, while they simultaneously produced a new value of, say, £128, corresponding to their expenditure of labour. The 10,000 lb. of yarn is therefore the bearer of a value of £500.\" So we have: means of production consumed = £372 (constant capital, transferred value); labour-power value = £50 (variable capital, price of a week's labour-power for 50 workers); new value produced = £128 (of which £50 reproduces the wage and £78 is surplus-value); total value = £372 + £128 = £500. Equivalently, P = £422, s = £78, so P + s = £500. This is the standard Marxian value-decomposition. The stage is set for the third-stage analysis, where this £500 is transformed into money. Section 3: The Third Stage — C'–M' Commodity Capital and the \"Birthmark\" of Value \"Commodities become commodity capital as the functional form of existence of the already valorized capital value that has arisen directly from the production process itself.\" If commodity production were fully capitalist, \"every commodity would be from the start the element of a commodity capital, whether it consisted of pig-iron or Brussels lace, sulphuric acid or cigars.\" Marx then mocks the \"charming vexations that scholastic economics inflicts on itself\" in trying to determine which commodities are destined \"by their properties\" to be capital and which to be \"common commodity service.\" This is a jab at those who reify capital as a property of things rather than a form of a value-relation. \"In commodity form, capital must perform commodity functions. The articles it consists of, which are produced from the start for the market, must be sold, transformed into money, and thus pass through the movement C–M.\" Here is the central question: \"What is it that makes this simple act of all commodity circulation simultaneously a function of capital?\" Marx's answer: \"It cannot be a change undergone in the act itself, neither with respect to its useful character, for it is as an object of use that the commodity passes to the buyer, nor with respect to its value, for this does not suffer a change of magnitude, but only one of form. It first existed in yarn, and now exists in money.\" So what? \"There is thus an essential distinction between the first stage M–C and the final stage C–M. Formerly the money advanced functioned as money capital because it was converted through circulation into commodities with a specific use-value. Now the commodity can function as capital only in so far as it actually brings this character with it from the production process, before its circulation begins.\" — Money capital is made into capital by the act M–C (by what is bought); commodity capital is already capital before the act C'–M' (because it has come out of the production process as bearer of surplus-value). The specific numbers: \"During the spinning process the spinners created yarn to the value of £128, of which £50, say, was simply an equivalent to the capitalist for his outlay on labour-power, and £78 formed surplus-value — a rate of exploitation of labour-power of 156 per cent. The value of the 10,000 lb. of yarn thus contains, firstly, the value of the consumed productive capital P, its constant part being £372, its variable part £50 and their sum £422 = 8,440 lb. of yarn. The value of the productive capital P is equal to C, the value of its formative elements, which in the stage M–C confronted the capitalist as commodities in the hands of their sellers. Secondly, however, the value of the yarn contains a surplus-value of £78 = 1,560 lb. of yarn.\" Note the rate of exploitation: £78 / £50 = 156%. This is independent of whether we read it as value or as physical yarn-pounds; either way, the same ratio. The 10,000 lb. of yarn at £500 = 10,000 shillings (1 shilling per pound); 8,440 lb. is worth £422 (the capital value reproduced), and 1,560 lb. is worth £78 (the surplus-value). \"Thus as the value expression of the 10,000 lb. of yarn, C = C + ΔC, C plus an increment (£78) which we shall call c, as it exists in the same commodity form as the original value now does.\" The footnote here flags an editorial decision: since C, M, and P are used for the three forms of industrial capital, 'c' must be used for the increment — the surplus-value in its commodity form — while English convention reserves c, v, s for constant capital, variable capital and surplus-value, so this notation reappears in Volume 2. This explains the footnote's warning about \"a possible source of confusion.\" C' as a Value Ratio, Not a Magnitude \"the value of 10,000 lb. of yarn, £500, is thus C + c = C'.\" What makes C into C'? \"What makes C, as the value expression of the 10,000 lb. of yarn, into C' is not the absolute amount of its value (£500), for this is determined, like the value expression of any other sum of commodities, by the amount of labour objectified in it. It is rather the relative magnitude of its value, its value compared with the value of the capital P consumed in its production. The value contained in it is this value plus the surplus-value provided by the productive capital. Its value is greater, i.e. it exceeds the capital value P, by the surplus-value c.\" This is a subtle but critical point: C' is not C' because £500 is a large number — any commodity mass could be worth £500 — but because £500 is £422 + £78, i.e. because it is the value of the productive capital plus the surplus-value that productive capital has bred . The ' indicates a relation of value-increase, not an absolute magnitude. \"Thus C' expresses a value ratio, the ratio of the value of the commodity product to that of the capital consumed in its production, i.e. it expresses the composition of its value out of capital value and surplus-value. The 10,000 lb. of yarn are commodity capital, C', only as the transformed form of the productive capital P, thus in a relationship that exists at first only in the circuit of this individual capital, or for the capitalist who has produced yarn with his capital.\" Then the crucial observation about the internal character of this relation: \"It is so to speak only an internal relation, not an external one, that makes the 10,000 lb. of yarn, as bearer of value, into commodity capital. The yarn bears its capitalist birth-mark not in the absolute magnitude of its value, but in its relative magnitude, in the magnitude of its value compared with the value of the productive capital contained in it before it was transformed into commodities.\" This phrase — \"capitalist birth-mark\" — captures the point memorably. When the yarn is sold, it appears in circulation simply as yarn, worth £500, indistinguishable from any other yarn. Its character as commodity capital is not seen on its face; it is a relation between the value it embodies and the value of the capital that produced it. \"If the 10,000 lb. of yarn is sold at its value of £500, this act of circulation, considered in itself, is C–M, the simple transformation of a value that remains the same from the commodity form into the money form. However, as a particular stage in the circuit of an individual capital, this same act is the realization of a capital value of £422 plus a surplus-value of £78, both borne by the commodity, i.e. C'–M', the transformation of the capital value from its commodity form into the money form.\" The Realisation Problem and the Sale as Essential Moment Marx then makes an economics argument that anticipates much of later Marxist theory of realisation crises. \"The function of C' is now that of every commodity product, to be transformed into money and sold, to pass through the phase of circulation C–M. As long as the now valorized capital persists in the form of commodity capital, is tied up on the market, the production process stands still. The capital operates neither to fashion products nor to form value. According to the varying speed with which the capital sheds its commodity form and assumes its money form, i.e. according to the briskness of the sale, the same capital value will serve to a very uneven degree in the formation of products and value, and the scale of the reproduction will expand or contract.\" This introduces a new source of variation in capital's efficiency — the speed of circulation . \"It was shown in the first volume that the degree of effectiveness of a given capital is conditioned by forces in the production process that are to a certain extent independent of its own magnitude. Now we see that the circulation process sets in motion new forces independent of the magnitude of value, which affect the degree of effectiveness of the capital, its expansion and its contraction.\" — So the turnover time (foreshadowing later chapters on turnover) becomes a determinant of capital's productivity, independent of its magnitude. He then analyses the proportion of the commodity mass that must be sold for the capitalist to realise his capital and surplus. \"The mass of commodities C', as bearer of the valorized capital, must fully undergo the metamorphosis C'–M'. The quantity sold is here the essential determinant. The individual commodity figures only as an integral part of the total quantity.\" Then the graduated schedule: If the capitalist sells only 7,440 lb. at £372, he has \"only replaced the value of his constant capital.\" If he sells 8,440 lb., he \"still replaces only the value of the total capital advanced.\" \"He must sell more, if he is to realize surplus-value, and he must sell the entire 10,000 lb. of yarn if he is to realize the whole surplus-value of £78 (= 1,560 lb. of yarn).\" This is a schematic demonstration that surplus-value realisation depends entirely on the volume sold . The surplus is not created in circulation — Marx insists \"He receives in the £500 only an equal value for the commodities sold; his transaction within the circulation sphere is simply C–M\" — but it can only be realised if the sale succeeds at value. If sale below value, the capital value itself may fail to be replaced. Marx also notes a counterfactual about the wage: \"If he had paid his workers £64 instead of £50, then his surplus-value would be only £64, instead of £78, and the rate of exploitation only 100 per cent instead of 156 per cent. But the value of his yarn would be unchanged; only the ratio of its various component portions would be different; the circulation act M–C would still be the sale of 10,000 lb. of yarn for £500, its value.\" — The total value of the yarn is determined by the labour embodied in it, not by the wage rate. A higher wage would simply redistribute the same £500 between the capitalist's variable capital and his surplus. This is a subtle reminder that the division between v and s is a distributional question internal to the labour process, and does not affect the total value of the product. The Expanded Formula and the Division of Capital Value from Surplus-Value \"C' = C + c (=£422 + £78). C is equal in value to P or the productive capital, and this is also equal in value to the M advanced in M–C, the purchase of the elements of production: in our example, £422. If the mass of commodities is sold at its value, then C = £422, and c = £78, the value of the surplus product of 1,560 lb. of yarn. If we call c, expressed in monetary terms, m, we have C'–M', or (C + c)–(M + m), and the circuit M–C…P…C'–M' in its expanded form is thus $$M - C \\binom{L}{mp} \\dots P \\dots (C + c) - (M + m).\"$$ The Double Character of the Realisation: Final vs First Metamorphosis Marx now introduces his most theoretically dense analysis of the third stage, showing how the same act (the sale C'–M') is structurally different for the capital value and for the surplus-value . \"In the first stage, the capitalist withdraws articles of use, both from the commodity market proper and from the labour market; in the third stage he puts commodities back, though only into one market, the commodity market proper. But if he withdraws more value from the market by way of his commodities than he originally put into it, this is only because he puts in a greater value of commodities than he originally withdrew. He puts in the value M and withdraws the same value C; he puts in C + c, and withdraws the same value M + m. In our example, M was equal in value to 8,440 lb. of yarn; the capitalist, however, puts 10,000 lb. of yarn into the market, i.e. gives back a greater value than he took from it. On the other hand, he has only put in this increased value because he produced surplus-value (as an aliquot part of the product, expressed in surplus product) in the production process, by the exploitation of labour-power. It is only as the product of this process that the mass of commodities is commodity capital, the bearer of the valorized capital value.\" The same circulation act C'–M' \"differs for the capital value and for the surplus-value in so far as it expresses in each case a different stage of their circulation, a different section in the series of metamorphoses that they have to pass through within the circulation sphere.\" For surplus-value: \"The surplus-value, c, first came into the world within the production process. It is thus now entering the commodity market for the first time, and moreover in the commodity form; this is its first form of circulation, and hence the act c–m is its first act of circulation or its first metamorphosis, which thus still has to be supplemented by the opposite circulation act, the converse metamorphosis m–c.\" A footnote notes this holds regardless of how capital value and surplus-value are divided within the commodity: \"10,000 lb. of yarn contain 1,560 lb. = £78 surplus-value, but 1 lb. of yarn = 1 shilling also contains 2.496 oz. = 1.872 d. surplus-value.\" For the capital value: \"It is a different matter with the circulation accomplished by the capital value C in the same circulation act C'–M', which for it is the circulation act C–M, where C = P, equal to the originally advanced M. This started its first act of circulation as M, money capital, and it now returns to the same form in the act C–M; it has thus passed through the two opposing phases of circulation (1) M–C and (2) C–M, and exists once again in the form in which it can begin the same cyclical process afresh. The transformation from the commodity form to the money form, which is for the surplus-value its first transformation, is for the capital value its return or transformation back into its original money form.\" Hence the neat formulation: C'–M' is for capital value a return (final metamorphosis) and for surplus-value a first entry (first metamorphosis). This duality will have deep consequences for the relation between capital and surplus-value in the total social reproduction (Volume 2, Part III). The Material Metamorphosis of Production as an Interruption in Circulation Marx continues to develop the theme that production is a real transformation, not merely a formal one, in the circuit of capital value. \"The money capital was converted into a sum of commodities of equal value, L and mp, by way of M–C. These commodities now no longer function as commodities, as articles for sale. Their value now exists in the hands of their buyer, the capitalist, as the value of his productive capital P. And in the function of P, productive consumption, they are transformed into a kind of commodity materially different from the means of production, into yarn, with the value not only being maintained, but increased, from £422 to £500. Through this real metamorphosis, the commodities withdrawn from the market in the first stage M–C are replaced by materially different commodities of different value, which must now function as commodities, be transformed into money and sold.\" \"Hence the production process appears simply as an interruption in the circulation of capital value, which up till then has only passed through the first phase M–C. It passes through the second and final phase, C–M, with C altered both materially and in value. But as far as the capital value taken by itself is concerned, all it has undergone in the production process is a change in its use form [Gebrauchsform]. It existed as £422 of value in L and mp, and it now exists as £422, the value of 8,440 lb. of yarn.\" So from the perspective of the capital value taken alone: it went through M–C (money into commodities as elements of production), then C–M (commodities as product into money). \"We thus have M–C–M, a form of circulation which, by way of a two-fold displacement in opposite directions, the transformation of money into commodities and commodities into money, necessarily determines the return of the value advanced as money to its money form: its transformation back into money.\" — This is a key analytic move. For the capital value considered in abstraction from its surplus-value, the circuit reduces to the classic M–C–M form of simple circulation, but with the crucial difference that the intermediate production stage has been inserted (in the dots). The Two-Fold Role of Money at the Close of the Circuit \"Two things should be noted here. Firstly, the ultimate transformation of capital value back into its original money form is a function of commodity capital. Secondly, this function includes the first formal transformation of the surplus-value from its original commodity form into the money form. The money form plays a double role here; on the one hand it is the returning form of a value originally advanced in money, i.e. the money returns to the form of value that opened the process; on the other hand it is the first transformed form of a value that originally enters into circulation in the commodity form.\" This double role is crucial for understanding why the circuit of money capital looks like it begins and ends with money. The two roles are: (1) a return of the capital value to its original form; (2) a first appearance of the surplus-value in money form. The first aspect makes the circuit appear as M...M' (money to money), and the second aspect gives the \"+ m\" — but these two aspects are external to one another, not integrated. The surplus-value was created in production; the capital value simply changed form. \"If the commodities of which the commodity capital consists are sold at their value, as we assume here, then C + c is transformed into M + m with the same value; it is in this last form, M + m (£422 + £78 = £500), that the realized commodity capital now exists in the hands of the capitalist. Capital value and surplus-value now exist as money, i.e. in the form of the universal equivalent.\" The Circuit Named \"At the end of the process, the capital value is thus once again in the same form in which it entered it, and can therefore open the process afresh and pass through it as money capital. And indeed because the initial and concluding form of the process is that of money capital (M), we call this form of the circuit the circuit of money capital . It is not the form of the value advanced, but only its magnitude, that is changed at the end.\" This passage gives the chapter its title, and its argument is significant: the reason we call it the circuit of money capital is purely formal — because money is the first and last form. The substance (value-form vs value-magnitude) is the same; only the magnitude has changed. M' as a Crude, Non-Conceptual Form \"M + m is nothing more than a sum of money of a certain magnitude, in our case £500. But as the result of the circuit of capital, as realized commodity capital, this sum of money contains the capital value and the surplus-value; moreover, these are no longer inextricably entwined, as in the yarn; they are now simply juxtaposed. Their realization has given each of the two an independent money form. 211/250 of the money is the capital value, £422, and 39/250 the surplus-value of £78. This separation effected by the realization of the commodity capital does not only have the formal content we shall speak of in a moment; it is important in the reproduction process of capital, according to whether m is added on to M in its entirety, in part, or not at all, thus according to whether or not it continues to function as a component of the capital value advanced. M and m can even pass through quite different circulations.\" Two arithmetic ratios to note: £422/£500 = 211/250; £78/£500 = 39/250. This points to the deep anonymity of the money form: £422 and £78 are simply portions of £500, without any differentiating mark. This is the beginning of Marx's argument about the \"conceptual\" poverty of money. \"In M', the capital returns once more to its original form M, its money form, but in a form in which it has been realized as capital. Firstly, there is a quantitative difference. It was M, £422; it is now M', £500, and this difference is expressed in M…M', the quantitatively different extremities of the circuit, the actual movement of which is indicated simply by the dots. M' is greater than M; M' minus M = s, the surplus-value. But all that exists as the result of the cycle M…M' is M'; the process of formation has been obliterated in the product. M' now exists independently in its own right, it is independent of the movement that produced it. The movement is past, and M' is there in its place.\" \"But as M + m, £422 advanced capital plus an increment of £78 on the same, M' or £500 also exhibits a qualitative relation, although this qualitative relation itself exists only as a relation between the parts of a corresponding sum, i.e. as a quantitative ratio. M, the capital advanced, which is once again present in its original form (£422), exists now as realized capital. It has not only maintained itself, but it has also realized itself as capital, in so far as it has differentiated itself from m (£78), which is related to it as its increase, its fruit, an increment that it itself has bred. It is realized as capital, because it is value that has bred value. M' exists as a capital relation; M no longer appears as mere money, but is expressly postulated as money capital, expressed as value that has valorized itself, i.e. thus also possesses the property of valorizing itself, of breeding more value than it itself has. M is posited as capital by its relation to another part of M' as to something posited by itself, as to the effect of which it is the cause, as to the consequence of which it is the ground. M' thus appears as a sum of values which is internally differentiated, undergoes a functional (conceptual) self-differentiation, and expresses the capital-relation.\" This is a highly dialectical passage. M' is not just a bigger sum of money; it is a self-differentiating sum, in which M (the capital) is related to m (the surplus) as cause to effect, ground to consequence. The capital-relation is present in M' — but \"this is expressed simply as a result, without the mediation of the process whose result it is.\" Then the passage on the homogeneity of money. \"Portions of value are not qualitatively distinguished from each other as such, save in so far as they appear as the values of different articles, concrete things, thus in various different useful forms, as values of different bodies of commodities — a distinction that does not arise from their existence as mere portions of value. In money, every difference between commodities is obliterated, because money is precisely the equivalent form common to all of them. A sum of money of £500 consists of nothing but isomorphous elements of £1. Since the mediating effect of its history is obliterated in the simple existence of this sum of money, and every trace of the specific difference which the various component parts of capital possess in the production process has vanished, the only remaining distinction is the crude, non-conceptual [ begrifflos — see the editorial footnote on the original's begrifflich ] distinction between a 'principal' as it is called in English, i.e. the capital of £422 which was advanced, and an additional sum of value of £78.\" The editorial footnote is significant: Marx wrote begrifflich (\"conceptual\") but the context requires begrifflos (\"non-conceptual\"). Engels's footnote tells us that the translators have assumed the latter. This reveals the palimpsest nature of the text — and it also confirms that the point is the absence of conceptual distinctions in the money sum. \"Let M' be £110, of which £100 is M, the principal, and £10 is s, surplus-value. There is absolute homogeneity, a complete absence of conceptual distinction, between the two constituent parts of the sum of £110. Any £10 is always one eleventh of the total sum of £110, whether it is a tenth of the principal advanced, or the additional £10 over and above this. Principal and increment, capital and surplus, can therefore both be expressed as fractions of the total sum; in our example ten elevenths is the principal or capital, and one eleventh the surplus. At the conclusion of its process the realized capital therefore appears as a sum of money, within which the distinction between principal and surplus expresses, in a naive, non-conceptual manner, the capital-relation.\" C' versus M': Rationality of Form \"This is also true, moreover, for C' (= C + c). But with the difference that C', in which C and c are simply proportional value portions of the same homogeneous mass of commodities, indicates its origin in P, whose direct product it is, whereas in M', a form arising directly from the circulation sphere, the direct connection with P has vanished.\" Then a crucial dialectical point about the evanescence of M': \"The superficial distinction between principal and increment that is contained in M', in so far as this expresses the result of the movement M…M', vanishes immediately, as soon as M' functions actively once more as money capital, rather than being fixed as the money expression of the valorized industrial capital. The circuit of money capital can never begin with M', but only with M (even though it is M' that now functions as M); i.e. never as an expression of the capital relation, but only as the form in which the capital value is advanced. As soon as the £500 is advanced afresh as capital, in order to be valorized once more, it is the starting-point rather than the point of return. Instead of a capital of £422, one of £500 has now been advanced; more money than before, more capital value, but the relation between the two components has gone. The sum of £500 now functions as capital, rather than £422, just as, originally, a sum of £500 might have functioned, rather than a sum of £422.\" The point: the capital relation expressed in M' is not preserved in the circuit's repetition. When £500 is advanced anew, it is simply £500 of capital; the fact that it contains a portion (£78) that was originally surplus-value is invisible. This is why \"M' is only the result of the realization of C'.\" \"It is not the active function of money capital to present itself as M'; its own presentation as M' is rather a function of C'.\" In the original C'–M', C' was the direct product of P and carried the marks of its origin; when the money form M' appears, those marks are effaced. \"It is not the active function of money capital to present itself as M'; its own presentation as M' is rather a function of C'. Already in simple commodity circulation, (1) C1–M, (2) M–C2, M functions actively only in the second act M–C2; its presentation as M is only the result of the first act, by virtue of which it first appears as the transformed form of C1. The capital relation contained in M', the connection between one of its parts as a part of capital value and the other as the value increment to this, does receive a functional significance, however, in so far as M' divides into two circulations, the circulation of capital and the circulation of surplus-value, when the circuit M…M' is constantly repeated. The two parts M and m then fulfil functions that differ not just quantitatively, but also qualitatively. Considered in itself, however, the form M…M' does not include the consumption of the capitalist, but expressly only capital's self-valorization and accumulation, in so far as the latter is first expressed in the periodic growth of the money capital that is constantly advanced afresh.\" This is a crucial point about accumulation: in the form M…M', consumption does not appear; only valorisation and accumulation do. This means the formal representation is one-sided — it abstracts from the fact that the capitalist must also consume (his own upkeep) and that worker consumption is required to reproduce labour-power. \"Although it is a crude and conceptually undifferentiated form of capital, M' = M + m is at the same time money capital in its first realized form, money that has bred money. This must be distinguished from the function of money capital in the first stage $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$. In this first stage, M circulates as money. It functions as money capital simply because it is only in its monetary state that it performs a monetary function, and can be converted into the elements of P that face it as commodities, L and mp. In this act of circulation, it functions only as money; but because this act is the first stage of capital value in process, it is simultaneously a function of money capital, by virtue of the specific useful form of the commodities L and mp that are bought.\" \"M' on the other hand, composed of M, the capital value, and m, the surplus-value created by it, expresses valorized capital value, the purpose and the result, the function of the total process of the circuit of capital. If it expresses this result in money form, as realized money capital, this is not because it is the money form of capital, money capital, but rather the reverse, because it is money capital, capital in the money form, and that it was in this form that capital opened the process, was advanced in its money form. The transformation back into the money form is a function of the commodity capital C', as we saw, not of money capital. And as far as the difference m between M' and M is concerned, this is only the money form of c, the increment to C; M' is only equal to M + m because C' equals C + c. In C', therefore, this difference, and the relation between the capital value and the surplus-value bred by it, is present and is expressed before they are both transformed into M', into a sum of money in which the two portions of value confront each other from a position of independence and can therefore also be applied to independent and different functions.\" So the intermediate conclusion is: C' is more \"rational\" than M' . In C', the relation between C and c is directly visible as a relation between the value of the productive capital and the surplus-value that productive capital has engendered, because the commodity is the direct product of P. In M', this relation is expressed only via the sale, and it appears only as a proportional relation between two portions of a homogeneous sum. \"M' is only the result of the realization of C'. Both of these, C' as well as M', are only different forms, the commodity form and the money form, of the valorized capital value; both have it in common that they are valorized capital value. Both are realized capital, because here capital value exists as such together with surplus-value as the fruit that is separate from it but produced by it, although this relation is expressed only in the naive form of the ratio between two parts of a sum of money or a commodity value.\" The \"Bizarre Form\" and Gold Production \"As expressions of capital, however, both related to and distinct from the surplus-value created by it, i.e. as expressions of valorized value, M' and C' are the same, and express the same thing, only in different forms; they are not distinguished from each other as money capital and commodity capital, but rather as money and commodity. In so far as they represent valorized value, capital active as capital, they simply express the result of the function of productive capital, the only function in which capital value breeds value. What they have in common is that both of them, money capital and commodity capital, are modes of existence of capital. The one is capital in its money form, the other in its commodity form.\" Then Marx introduces the concept of the \"bizarre form\" of M'. \"The specific functions that distinguish them can thus be nothing other than distinctions between the money function and the commodity function. The commodity capital, as the direct product of the capitalist production process, recalls its origin and is therefore more rational in its form, less lacking in conceptual differentiation, than the money capital, in which every trace of this process has been effaced, just as all the particular useful forms of commodities are generally effaced in money. Hence it is only when M' itself functions as commodity capital, when it is the direct product of a production process and not the transformed form of this product, that its bizarre form disappears — i.e. in the production of the money material itself.\" So: the \"bizarre form\" of M' is that a sum of money M appears to give birth to another sum of money m — i.e. that money breeds money, without the intervening process. This form is bizarre because it effaces the process. And this bizarre form disappears only when M' is itself a commodity product — i.e. when money is being produced, as in gold mining. \"The formula for the production of gold, for example, would be $M - C \\binom{L}{mp} \\dots P \\dots M' (M + m)$, where M' figures as the commodity product in so far as P provides more gold than was advanced for the elements of production of gold in the first M, the money capital. The expression M…M' (M + m) is irrational, in that, within it, part of a sum of money appears as the mother of another part of the same sum of money. But here this irrationality disappears.\" This is a crucial dialectical move. In gold production, the output M' is itself the physical commodity — ounces of gold. So the output N ounces of gold are physically visible as a sum of gold while their composition into capital-value and surplus-value is also visible: N ounces decompose into N1 (the value of the capital consumed) and N2 (the surplus). The \"irrationality\" of M breeding M disappears because here M is not just money, it is a produced commodity whose genesis in production is directly visible. The \"irrationality\" is worth pausing over. Money-capital M is advanced, and after a cycle, money-capital M' = M + m emerges. The pure form M…M' (or M + m) appears to say that money generates money — like a mother birthing a child. This is \"irrational\" — it is the appearance of self-valorising money (Geld heckt Geld, in the old slogan), which capitalism's ideological forms of consciousness latch onto. In gold production, by contrast, the process is literal: the capitalist advances gold, buys inputs, and produces more gold. So the process is not hidden behind the money veil; it is the money itself. Section 4: The Circuit as a Whole The Total Movement as an Interrupted Metamorphosis Marx now steps back to consider the circuit in its entirety. \"We have seen how the circulation process, after its first phase $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$ has elapsed, is interrupted by P, in which the commodities bought on the market, L and mp, are consumed as material and value components of the productive capital; the product of this consumption is a new commodity, altered both materially and in value. The interrupted circulation process, M–C, must be supplemented by C–M. But it is C' that appears as the bearer of this second and concluding phase, a commodity different materially and in value from the original C. The circulation series thus presents itself as (1) M–C1; (2) C'2–M', in which the first commodity C1 has been replaced in the second phase by one of higher value and a different useful form, C'2, during the interruption that is occasioned by the function of P, i.e. the production of C' from the elements of C, the forms of existence of the productive capital P.\" Compare with Volume 1's discovery. \"The first form of appearance in which we met with capital, on the other hand (Volume 1, Chapter 4), M–C–M' (broken down: (1) M–C1; (2) C1–M''), exhibits the same commodity twice over. It is the same commodity into which money is transformed in the first phase and which is transformed back into more money in the second phase.\" But in the circuit of capital, the second commodity (C'2) is materially and quantitatively different from the first (C1). Despite this essential difference, \"both circulations have in common that in their first phase money is transformed into commodities and in their second phase commodities into money, that the money that is spent in the first phase flows back again in the second. On the one hand they have in common this stream of money back to its starting-point, on the other hand the excess of the money that flows back over that advanced. In this respect, M–C…C'–M' too appears to be contained in the general formula M–C–M'.\" \"It further results here that in both metamorphoses pertaining to the circulation sphere, M–C and C'–M', equally large and simultaneously present values always confront and replace each other. The change in value belongs solely to the metamorphosis P, the production process, which thus appears as the real metamorphosis of capital, as opposed to the merely formal metamorphoses of the circulation sphere.\" This is one of the chapter's most important theoretical distinctions: real metamorphosis (production, where value changes) vs formal metamorphoses (circulation, where only the form of value changes). This distinction will reappear throughout Volume 2. Industrial Capital and Its Three Functional Forms \"Let us now consider the total movement M–C…P…C'–M', or its expanded form $M - C \\binom{L}{mp} \\dots P \\dots C'(C + c) - M'(M + m)$.\" \"Here capital appears as a value that passes through a sequence of connected and mutually determined transformations, a series of metamorphoses that form so many phases or stages of a total process. Two of these phases belong to the circulation sphere, one to the sphere of production. In each of these phases the capital value is to be found in a different form, corresponding to a different and special function. Within this movement the value advanced not only maintains itself, but it grows, increases its magnitude. Finally, in the concluding stage, it returns to the same form in which it appeared at the outset of the total process. This total process is therefore a circuit.\" \"The two forms that the capital value assumes within its circulation stages are those of money capital and commodity capital; the form pertaining to the production stage is that of productive capital. The capital that assumes these forms in the course of its total circuit, discards them again and fulfils in each of them its appropriate function, is industrial capital — industrial here in the sense that it encompasses every branch of production that is pursued on a capitalist basis.\" The definition of industrial capital is key: it is not the specific capital of manufacturing industry, but any capital that passes through all three forms and gives itself to all three functions. \"Money capital, commodity capital and productive capital thus do not denote independent varieties of capital, whose functions constitute the content of branches of business that are independent and separate from one another. They are simply particular functional forms of industrial capital, which takes on all three forms in turn.\" This is a decisive polemic. The three \"kinds\" of capital that political economy treats as separate branches (banking capital, manufacturing capital, commercial capital) are really forms of the same industrial capital. Their separate existence as branches of business arises only through the social division of labour, which \"renders them independent and one-sidedly extended.\" Interruptions and the \"Fettering\" of the Circuit \"The circuit of capital proceeds normally only as long as its various phases pass into each other without delay.\" Then a set of failure modes: \"If capital comes to a standstill in the first phase, M–C, money capital forms into a hoard; if this happens in the production phase, the means of production cease to function, and labour-power remains unoccupied; if in the last phase, C'–M', unsaleable stocks of commodities obstruct the flow of circulation.\" \"It lies in the nature of the case, however, that the circuit itself determines that capital is tied up for certain intervals in the particular sections of the cycle. In each of its phases industrial capital is tied to a specific form, as money capital, productive capital or commodity capital. Only after it has fulfilled the function corresponding to the particular form it is in does it receive the form in which it can enter a new phase of transformation.\" The term \"fettered\" (or in other translations \"tied up\") points to a structural feature of capital: it must be committed to a particular form for a period before it can move on. Money capital must actually be spent; productive capital must actually complete its production; commodity capital must actually be sold. During each interval, the value is locked into a form that limits its fluidity. This sets up the whole problematic of turnover time . The Fixed-Capital Caveat \"In order to make this clear, we have assumed in our example that the capital value of the mass of commodities created in the production stage is equal to the total value originally advanced as money, in other words that the whole capital value advanced as money moves all at once from one stage into the subsequent one. We have already seen, however (Volume 1, Chapter 8), that a part of the constant capital, the actual instruments of labour (e.g. machines), serve continuously throughout a greater or smaller number of repetitions of the same production process, and for this reason give up their value to the product only bit by bit. We shall show later on how far this circumstance modifies the circuit of capital.\" Marx then explains why this caveat does not ruin the analysis. \"In our example, the value of the productive capital, £422, contained only the average calculated wear and tear of factory buildings, machinery, etc., thus only the portion of value that they carry over in the course of transforming 10,000 lb. of raw cotton into 10,000 lb. of yarn, the product of a weekly spinning process of sixty hours. The instruments of labour — buildings, machinery, etc. — therefore figured in the means of production into which the constant capital advanced was transformed, as if they were simply hired on the market in return for a weekly payment. This however alters absolutely nothing as far as the substance of the matter is concerned. We need only multiply the weekly output of yarn, 10,000 lb., by the number of weeks contained in a given series of years, and the entire value of the instruments of labour bought and used up in this period will have been carried over. It is clear then that the money capital advanced must first be transformed into these means of production, and must therefore have made its exit from the first phase M–C, before it can function as productive capital P.\" So the analysis in this chapter is a first approximation , valid for the annual (or multi-week) period over which the fixed capital wears out. The complicating factor (fixed capital) is bracketed for later analysis (Chapter 8 of Volume 2, on fixed and circulating capital). The Transport Industry Marx then addresses a puzzle: what if the product of production is not a new material thing? \"In the general formula, the product of P is considered as a material thing different from the elements of the productive capital, an object that has an existence of its own, apart from the production process, possessing a useful form different from that of the elements of production. In so far as the result of the production process does appear as a thing, this is always the case, even when a part of the product enters once more as an element into the renewed production process. Thus grain serves as seed-corn for its own production, but the product consists only of grain, and thus has a different physical shape from the elements applied together with it: labour-power, instruments of labour, fertilizer.\" Then: \"There are however particular branches of industry in which the product of the production process is not a new objective product, a commodity. The only one of these that is economically important is the communication industry, both the transport industry proper, for moving commodities and people, and the transmission of mere information — letters, telegrams, etc.\" Marx quotes A. Chuprov: \"The manufacturer can produce articles first and look for customers afterwards. (His product, after it is ejected in finished form from the production process, passes into circulation as a commodity separate from this process.) Production and consumption thus appear as two acts separated in time and space. In the transport industry, however, which does not create new products, but only displaces people and things, these two acts coincide; the 'services' (the change of place) 'are necessarily consumed the moment they are produced. This is why the area within which railways can seek their customers is at most 50 versts' (53 km.) 'on either side.'\" The footnote cites Chuprov's 1875 Moscow work The Railway Industry , pp. 69-70. Marx elaborates: \"The result in each case, whether it is people or commodities that are transported, is a change in their spatial location, e.g. that the yarn finds itself in India instead of in England, where it was produced. But what the transport industry sells is the actual change of place itself. The useful effect produced is inseparably connected with the transport process, i.e. the production process specific to the transport industry. People and commodities travel together with the means of transport, and this journeying, the spatial movement of the means of transport, is precisely the production process accomplished by the transport industry. The useful effect can only be consumed during the production process; it does not exist as a thing of use distinct from this process, a thing which functions as an article of commerce and circulates as a commodity only after its production.\" \"However the exchange-value of this useful effect is still determined, like that of any other commodity, by the value of the elements of production used up in it (labour-power and means of production), plus the surplus-value created by the surplus labour of the workers occupied in the transport industry. In respect of its consumption, too, this useful effect behaves just like other commodities. If it is consumed individually, then its value vanishes with its consumption; if it is consumed productively, so that it is itself a stage of production of the commodity that finds itself transported, then its value is carried over to the commodity as an addition to it. The formula for the transport industry is thus $M - C \\binom{L}{mp} \\dots P \\dots M'$, for it is the production process itself, and not a product separable from it, that is paid for and consumed. This therefore has almost exactly the same form as that for the production of precious metals, except that M' is here the transformed form of the useful effect produced in the course of the production process, and not the natural form of the gold and silver that is produced during this process and ejected from it.\" This is an important theoretical extension: some production processes (transport, communications) produce no separable commodity ; what they sell is the useful effect itself, consumed at the moment of production. Their value-formula is M–C…P…M' — the same formal structure as gold production, though for a different reason (gold production sells gold as the natural form of value ; transport sells an immaterial service that is directly money ). Industrial Capital as Hegemony \"Industrial capital is the only mode of existence of capital in which not only the appropriation of surplus-value or surplus product, but also its creation, is a function of capital. It thus requires production to be capitalist in character; its existence includes that of the class antagonism between capitalists and wage-labourers. To the degree that it takes hold of production, the technique and social organization of the labour process are revolutionized, and the economic-historical type of society along with this. The other varieties of capital which appeared previously, within past or declining conditions of social production, are not only subordinated to it and correspondingly altered in the mechanism of their functioning, but they now move only on its basis, thus live and die, stand and fall together with this basis. Money capital and commodity capital, in so far as they appear and function as bearers of their own peculiar branches of business alongside industrial capital, are now only modes of existence of the various functional forms that industrial capital constantly assumes and discards within the circulation sphere, forms which have been rendered independent and one-sidedly extended through the social division of labour.\" This is a major theoretical claim about the historical primacy of industrial capital . Pre-capitalist forms of capital (usury capital, merchant capital) existed before industrial capital and operated on other bases. But once industrial capital dominates, all other forms of capital become subordinate to it — they \"now move only on its basis.\" Independent money capital and commodity capital (banks, merchants) are not separate species of capital but the branches of the division of labour formed out of the money-function and commodity-function of industrial capital. The Independent Movement within the General Circulation \"On the one hand, the circuit M…M' is inextricably linked with the general circulation of commodities, issues from it and flows back into it, forming a part of it. On the other hand, it forms for the individual capitalist an independent movement peculiar to his capital value, a movement which proceeds in part within the general circulation of commodities, in part outside it, but which always retains its independent character.\" Three reasons for the independence: 1. \"both of the phases that it goes through in the circulation sphere, M–C and C–M, possess a functionally specific character as phases of the movement of capital; in M–C, C is determined in its material content as labour-power and means of production; in C'–M' the capital value is realized together with the surplus-value.\" 2. \"P, the production process, includes productive consumption.\" 3. \"the return of money to its starting-point makes the movement M…M' a cyclical movement complete in itself.\" \"On the one hand, therefore, each individual capital, in the two halves of its circulation M–C and C'–M', is an agent of the general circulation of commodities, in which it functions and of which it forms a link, either as money or as commodity. Hence it is a member of the general series of metamorphoses of the commodity world. On the other hand, it describes its own independent circuit within the general circulation, one in which the sphere of production forms a transitional stage, and in which it returns to its starting-point in the same form in which it left it. Within its own circuit, which includes its real metamorphosis in the production process, the magnitude of its value also changes. It returns not only as money value, but as increased and expanded money value.\" The Four Defining Features of M...M' Marx then lays out four features that distinguish the circuit of money capital from the others. 1. Money as starting-point and point of return. \"It appears as the circuit of money capital because industrial capital in its money form, as money capital, forms the starting-point and the point of return of the whole process. The formula itself expresses that the money is not spent here as money, but is only advanced, and is thus simply the money form of capital, money capital. It further expresses the fact that it is the exchange-value, not the use-value, that is the decisive inherent purpose of the movement. It is precisely because the money form of value is its independent and palpable form of appearance that the circulation form M…M', which starts and finishes with actual money, expresses money-making, the driving motive of capitalist production, most palpably. The production process appears simply as an unavoidable middle term, a necessary evil for the purpose of money-making.\" A memorable sentence: \"(This explains why all nations characterized by the capitalist mode of production are periodically seized by fits of giddiness in which they try to accomplish the money-making without the mediation of the production process.)\" — flagged in an editorial footnote as a sentence introduced by Engels in the second (1893) edition. This is a jab at speculative bubbles and financial manias. 2. Production as mere means. \"In this circuit, the stage of production, the function of P, forms an interruption in the circulation process M–C…C'–M', whose two phases are in turn only a mediation of simple circulation M–C–M'. The production process here appears formally and explicitly, in the actual form of the circuit itself, for what it actually is in the capitalist mode of production, a mere means for the valorization of the value advanced; i.e. enrichment as such appears as the inherent purpose of production.\" 3. The role of money as independent form of value. \"Because the sequence of phases is opened by M–C, C'–M' is the second term in the circulation; the starting-point is M, the money capital to be valorized, the conclusion M', the valorized money capital M + m, in which M figures alongside its offshoot m as realized capital. This distinguishes the circuit of money capital from the two other circuits P and C', and in two ways. On the one hand, through the money form of the two extremes; money is the independent and palpable form of existence of value, the value of the product in its independent value form, in which all trace of the commodities' use-value has been effaced. On the other hand, the form P…P does not necessarily become P…P' (P + p), while in the form C'…C', no value difference at all is visible between the two extremes.\" — So M...M' is unique in that the value-increase is visible right at the extremes: M ends as M + m; whereas in P...P the same capital (P) might return as P (without surplus shown); and in C'...C' the same C' both begins and ends, hiding any value difference. \"It is thus characteristic of the formula M…M', on the one hand, that the capital value forms the starting-point and the valorized capital the point of return, so that the advancing of the capital value appears as the means, the valorized capital value as the goal of the whole operation; on the other hand, that this relation is expressed in the money form, the independent value form, hence money capital as money breeding money. The creation by value of surplus-value is not only expressed as the alpha and omega of the process, but explicitly presented in the glittering money form.\" 4. Accumulation and the silence on consumption. \"Since M', the money capital realized as the result of C'–M', the complementary and concluding phase of M–C, exists in absolutely the same form as that in which it opened its first circuit, it can, as it emerges from this, reopen the same circuit as augmented (accumulated) money capital, M' = M + m; at least it is in no way expressed in the form of M…M' that the circulation of m separates itself from that of M when the circuit is repeated. Considered by itself in isolation, from the formal standpoint, the circuit of money capital thus expresses only the process of valorization and accumulation. Consumption, therefore, is expressed in it only as productive consumption, $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$; this is all that is accounted for in this circuit of the individual capital.\" Then the crucial argument about worker and capitalist consumption. \"M–L is L–M or C–M from the point of view of the worker, i.e. the first phase of the circulation that mediates his individual consumption: L–M–C (means of subsistence). The second phase, M–C, no longer falls within the circuit of the individual capital; but it is introduced by it and presupposed by it, for the worker, in order to continue to exist on the market as exploitable material for the capitalist, must before all else keep alive, and therefore maintain himself by individual consumption. This consumption itself, however, is assumed here only as a precondition for the productive consumption of labour-power by capital, thus only in so far as the worker maintains and reproduces himself as labour-power by his individual consumption. The means of production (mp), however, the actual commodities that are involved in the circuit, are simply the means of nourishment for productive consumption. The act L–M mediates the individual consumption of the worker, the transformation of means of subsistence into his flesh and blood. But the capitalist must also exist, thus also live and consume, in order to function as capitalist. In actual fact, he needs to consume only as a worker, and hence no more than this is assumed in this form of the circulation process. But even this is not expressed formally, since the formula closes with M', i.e. a result that can function again immediately as increased money capital.\" \"C'–M' directly contains the sale of C'; but C'–M', which is from one side a sale, is M–C, a purchase, from the other side, and in the last instance commodities are bought only for the sake of their use-value (we ignore intermediate transactions here), in order to enter the process of consumption, either individual or productive, according to the nature of the article bought. This consumption, however, does not enter the circuit of the individual capital of which C' is the product; the product C' is precisely ejected from the circuit as a commodity to be sold. It is expressly destined for the consumption of others.\" Then the mercantilist illustration. \"We therefore find among the exponents of the Mercantile System (which is based on the formula M–C…P…C'–M') long sermons to the effect that the individual capitalist should consume only in his capacity as a worker, and that a capitalist nation should leave the consumption of its commodities and the consumption process in general to other more stupid nations, while making productive consumption into its own life's work. These sermons are often reminiscent in both form and content of analogous ascetic exhortations by the Fathers of the Church.\" A footnote identifies the source of this view: D'Avenant, An Essay on the East-India Trade , London, 1697, quoted in Theories of Surplus-Value , Part I, p. 179: \"By what is consum'd at Home, one loseth only what another gets, and the Nation in General is not at all the Richer; but all Foreign Consumption is a clear and certain Profit.\" — So Marx is targeting the mercantilist obsession with exports and the glorification of production-in-general over consumption. The Circuit as Unity of Circulation and Production This passage, likely from one of the later manuscripts, reads: \"The circuit of capital is thus a unified process of circulation and production, it includes both. In so far as the two phases M–C and C'–M' are processes of circulation, the circulation of capital forms part of the general circulation of commodities. But by taking part in functionally determined sections or stages in the circuit of capital, which do not just pertain to the sphere of circulation, but also to that of production, capital performs its own circuit within the general circulation of commodities. This general circulation enables it, in the first stage, to assume the form in which it can function as productive capital; in the second stage, to cast off the commodity function in which it cannot renew its circuit; it equally gives it the possibility of separating its own capital circuit from the circulation of the surplus-value that has adhered to it.\" The Primacy and Persistence of the Circuit of Money Capital Marx now asserts that the circuit of money capital is the most characteristic form of appearance — and then immediately warns about its limits. \"The circuit of money capital is thus the most one-sided, hence most striking and characteristic form of appearance of the circuit of industrial capital, in which its aim and driving motive — the valorization of value, money-making and accumulation — appears in a form that leaps to the eye (buying in order to sell dearer). The fact that the first phase is M–C displays the provenance of the components of productive capital on the commodity market. It also shows that the capitalist production process is conditioned by circulation, trade. The circuit of money capital is not just commodity production; it only comes into being by way of circulation, and presupposes this.\" \"It is shown by the fact that the form M pertaining to circulation appears as the first and pure form of the capital value advanced, which is not the case with the two other forms of the circuit.\" \"The circuit of money capital remains the permanent general expression of industrial capital, in so far as it always includes the valorization of the value advanced. In P…P, the money expression of the capital emerges only as the price of the elements of production, thus only as value expressed in money of account, the form in which it is found in book-keeping.\" \"M…M' becomes a particular form of the circuit of industrial capital in so far as newly appearing capital is first advanced as money and is withdrawn in the same form, whether on its transfer from one branch of business to another, or when industrial capital is withdrawn from business altogether. This includes the capital function of the surplus-value first advanced in the money form, and emerges most strikingly when this functions in a business other than that from which it originates. M…M' can be the first circuit of a capital, it can be its last; it can be taken as the form of the total social capital; it is the form of capital that is newly invested, whether as newly accumulated capital in the money form, or old capital that is completely transformed into money in order to be transferred from one branch of production to another.\" The Money Form as Constitutive of the Wage Relation \"As a form that is comprised in all circuits, money capital performs this circuit precisely for that part of the capital that creates surplus-value, the variable capital. The normal form of advance for wages is payment in money; this process must be steadily repeated at short intervals, as the worker lives from hand to mouth. Hence the worker must constantly come face to face with the capitalist as money capitalist, and with his capital as money capital. Here there can be no question, as in the purchase of means of production and the sale of productive commodities, of a direct or indirect balancing of accounts (so that the greater part of money capital actually figures only in the form of commodities, money only in the form of money of account, and finally cash only for the settlement of the balances).\" This is an important structural remark: for fixed capital and circulating capital transactions (purchases of machinery and sales of outputs), the money-form is often only bookkeeping — accounts are netted out, cash plays a residual role. But for wages , this is impossible: the worker needs cash on the nail, at short intervals, because he lives hand-to-mouth. So money capital as actually circulating coin remains structurally necessary, at least for the variable capital portion. \"On the other hand, a part of the surplus-value arising from the variable capital is spent by the capitalist for his personal consumption; this pertains to the retail trade, and, after however roundabout a journey, is ultimately spent as cash, in the money form of the surplus-value. Whether this part of the surplus-value is great or small in no way affects the matter. The variable capital constantly appears anew as money capital invested in wages (M–L), and m as surplus-value that is spent to defray the personal needs of the capitalist. Thus both M, as the variable capital value advanced, and m, as its increment, are necessarily retained in the money form, to be spent as such.\" The Deceptive/Illusory Character and Mercantile Ancestry \"The formula M–C…P…C'–M', with the result M' = M + m, contains in its form a certain deception; it bears an illusory character that derives from the existence of the advanced and valorized value in its equivalent form, in money. What is emphasized is not the valorization of the value, but the money form of this process, the fact that more value in the money form is finally withdrawn from the circulation sphere than was originally advanced to it, i.e. the increase in the mass of gold and silver belonging to the capitalist.\" The ideological consequences are two. \"The so-called Monetary System is simply the expression of the superficial form M–C–M', a movement that proceeds exclusively in the circulation sphere, and hence can only explain the two acts (1) M–C and (2) C–M' by saying that C in the second act is sold above its value, and therefore withdraws more money from the circulation sphere than was cast into it by its purchase. On the other hand, however, M–C…P…C'–M', when regarded as the exclusive form, is the basis for the more developed Mercantile System, in which it is not simply the circulation of commodities but also their production that appears as a necessary element.\" A footnote clarifies the historical relation: \"The Monetary System (sometimes called bullionism) preceded the Mercantile System or mercantilism. Marx describes the Mercantile System as a 'variant' of the Monetary System. He distinguishes the two most clearly in the Grundrisse (Pelican edition, p. 327): 'The Monetary System had understood the autonomy of value only in the form in which it arose from simple circulation — money… Then came the Mercantile System, an epoch where industrial capital and hence wage labour arose in manufactures… The Mercantilists already have faint notions of money as capital, but actually again only in the form of money, of the circulation of mercantile capital.'\" So the ideological forms of pre-classical political economy — bullionism and mercantilism — arose from taking aspects of the circuit of money capital as the essence of capital rather than as one of its forms. Bullionism fetishises money as the form of value; mercantilism recognises production but still locates the source of profit in exchange (selling dearer). \"The illusory character of M–C…P…C'–M', and the corresponding illusory significance it is given, is there as soon as this form is regarded as the sole form, not as one that flows and is constantly repeated; i.e. as soon as it is taken not just as one of the forms of the circuit, but rather as its exclusive form. In itself, however, it refers to other forms.\" The Four \"Points\" and the Return to P...P Marx now supplies four arguments for the thesis that the money-form is not, in the last instance, primary. Firstly , this circuit presupposes capitalist production and its social relations. \"M–C = $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$, but M–L implies that the wage-labourer, and therefore the means of production too, are a part of the productive capital; hence the labour and valorization process, the production process is already a function of capital.\" Secondly , the money form is evanescent. \"If M…M' is repeated, the return to the money form appears just as evanescent as the money form in the first stage. M–C vanishes, in order to make way for P. The permanently repeated advance in money, as well as its permanent return in money, themselves appear simply as evanescent moments in the circuit.\" Thirdly , the developmental sequence shows the priority of productive capital. \"M–C…P…C'–M'. M–C…P…C'–M'. M–C…P… etc. With the second repetition of the circuit, we already have the circuit P…C'–M'. M–C…P, before the second circuit of M is even complete, and thus all further circuits can be considered in the form P…C'–M'–C…P; M–C, therefore, as the first phase of the first circuit, simply forms an evanescent prelude to the constantly repeated circuit of productive capital, as is in fact the case when industrial capital is invested for the first time, in the form of money capital. Furthermore, before the second circuit is complete, the first circuit C'–M'…M–C…P…C' (abbreviated C'…C') has been described, the circuit of commodity capital. Thus the first form already contains the two others, and the money form vanishes, in so far as it is not just an expression of value, but an expression of value in the equivalent form, in money.\" This is a critical observation: as soon as the circuit is repeated , the money form becomes evanescent. After the first cycle, capital is already in productive or commodity form when the next cycle begins; M–C is only ever the first phase of a virgin circuit or a transitional phase when capital shifts branches. Finally , even for a newly invested capital, M–C is posited by the production process, not the other way around. \"Let us take a newly appearing individual capital, which describes the circuit M–C…P…C'–M' for the first time: M–C is a preparatory phase, the precursor of the first production process performed by this individual capital. This phase M–C is therefore not the presupposition, but is rather posited or conditioned by the production process. However, this holds only for this individual capital.\" But at the level of society, the general form still appears as M…M': \"The general form of the circuit of industrial capital is the circuit of money capital, in so far as the capitalist mode of production is presupposed, i.e. within a specific state of society determined by capitalist production. Hence the capitalist production process is the basic pre-condition, it is prior to all else, if not within the first circuit of the money capital of a newly invested industrial capital, then outside it; the continued existence of this production process assumes the constantly repeated circuit of P…P. This assumption is already made within the first stage $M - C \\binom{L}{mp}$, in so far as this stage presupposes on the one hand the existence of the class of wage-labourers, and on the other hand what is the first stage M–C for the purchaser of the means of production, and C'–M' for their seller. It presupposes, therefore, that C' is commodity capital, and therefore that the commodity itself is the result of capitalist production; with this we must also presuppose the function of productive capital.\" The chapter thus ends by dissolving the apparent primacy of the money form . When we look only at an individual newly-invested capital, M–C looks like the beginning; but at the level of society, capital production is the presupposition (the wage-labour class must already exist, the means of production must already be capital, the commodities must already be capitalist products). Therefore the circuit of productive capital (P…P) is logically and historically prior. But the appearance — the form in which capital presents itself to consciousness — is M…M'. The chapter closes on this dialectical tension: appearance and essence are not identical, and the analyst's task is to trace the mediations that connect them. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed 1. Capital is a movement, not a thing. The chapter's deepest lesson is anthropologico-philosophical as much as economic: capital is not a stock of assets or a sum of money but a process — a self-reproducing circuit that assumes, discards, and re-assumes forms. Money, commodities, and machines are not \"capital\" by nature; they become capital only through their role in the circuit. \"The means of production are no more capital by nature than is human labour-power. They receive this specific social character only under certain particular conditions that have historically developed, just as it is only under such conditions that precious metals are stamped with the character of money, or money with that of money capital.\" This is the theoretical death-blow to any position that treats capital as a trans-historical category or a physical property of things. 2. The three forms of capital are functional forms of one thing — industrial capital. Money capital, productive capital, and commodity capital are not species but modes of existence of industrial capital. Their apparent independence (as banks, factories, merchants) arises only through the social division of labour, which has \"rendered them independent and one-sidedly extended.\" Capitalism's financial and commercial sectors are not add-ons to industry but the formally separated functions of money capital and commodity capital, subordinate to industrial capital once it dominates. 3. The circuit is simultaneously a unity and a duality. M–C is one act but splits into M–L and M–mp, on different markets. C'–M' is one act but is a final metamorphosis for capital value and a first metamorphosis for surplus-value. The circuit is one movement but contains three distinct stages and three distinct forms. The dialectical structure of unity-in-difference governs both the analytic categories and the material process. 4. Production is the site of the real metamorphosis; circulation only affords formal metamorphoses. Value-change occurs in P, not in M–C or C'–M'. The circulation acts merely transform the form of value; they cannot create it. This is the chapter's most basic refutation of any explanation of profit from exchange alone — a critique aimed at both the Monetary System (profit as selling dearer) and the Mercantile System (profit as exchange with productive auxiliary). 5. The class relation is presupposed in the money relation. The famous sentence — \"The capital relation arises only in the production process because it exists implicitly in the act of circulation, in the basically different economic conditions in which buyer and seller confront one another, in their class relation\" — establishes that the market's apparent equality of contracting parties rests on an underlying inequality of property. The \"distribution\" that matters is not distribution of consumer goods but distribution of the means of production themselves . The Russian landowners' complaints illustrate that where the separation of producers from means of production is incomplete, wage labour cannot be fully generalised. 6. The circuits imply each other. M…M' is not external to P…P and C'…C'; it contains them and is contained by them. Once the circuit is repeated, \"M–C is simply an evanescent prelude to the constantly repeated circuit of productive capital,\" and \"before the second circuit is complete, the first circuit C'…C' has been described.\" The three circuits are not alternative descriptions but aspects of one self-differentiating totality. Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications For the critique of political economy. The chapter is a systematic dismantling of the ideological forms thrown up by capital itself. The Monetary System and Mercantile System are not merely intellectual errors but necessary appearances generated by the money form. Their mistake is not arbitrary but structural: they mistake the most visible form of the circuit for its essence. Marx's method here is genealogical — he shows how a category (money as capital) acquires an autonomous existence in thought through a real, material process (the social division of labour making money capital a separate branch of business). This anticipates the theory of fetishism and the critique of ideology in its full form. For the theory of crises and cycles. The chapter plants several seeds. (a) The structural dependence of capital on continuously selling C' introduces the possibility of realisation crises : if the commodity cannot be sold, \"unsaleable stocks of commodities obstruct the flow of circulation\" and production stands still. (b) The \"fettering\" of capital in each phase (money hoarding, idle means of production, unsold commodities) creates frictions that can only be resolved by further expansion or crisis. (c) The dependence of surplus-value realisation on the volume sold introduces a structural over-production tendency: capital must keep selling everything to realise its surplus. (d) The special role of the wage form in requiring cash means that a shortage of money (or credit) can paralyse production even when value-producing potential is present — a theme of the \"monetary\" theory of crises. For the theory of accumulation. The circuit's closure with M' = M + m makes accumulation (the re-advance of m as capital) structurally possible and, under competition, necessary. But the formal circuit silences the question of consumption — both the worker's and the capitalist's. This silence is not accidental; it is a structural feature of the money-form, which effaces every trace of the process. The chapter thus sets up the later problem (in Volume 3 and in Rosa Luxemburg's and others' critiques) of whether a fully-capitalist society can absorb its own product, or whether capital needs external markets. For the theory of turnover and time. The caveat about fixed capital (machines transferring value \"bit by bit\") and the emphasis on circulation speed point forward to Volume 2's later chapters on turnover. The chapter argues explicitly that \"the circulation process sets in motion new forces independent of the magnitude of value, which affect the degree of effectiveness of the capital, its expansion and its contraction.\" The same capital value, turned over more rapidly, produces more surplus-value per annum than a slower-turning capital. Time, no less than magnitude and composition, is a determinant of capital productivity. For the theory of financialisation. Marx's depiction of the \"fits of giddiness\" in which nations \"try to accomplish the money-making without the mediation of the production process,\" combined with the analysis of money breeding money in M…M' and the social independence of money capital as a branch of business, anticipates a theory of speculative manias and of the financial system's tendency to detach from production. The chapter does not develop such a theory but provides its essential categories. For historiography. The chapter offers a materialist theory of historical epochs: \"The particular form and mode in which this connection [of workers and means of production] is effected is what distinguishes the various economic epochs of the social structure.\" This is the framework within which primitive accumulation, the transition to capitalism, and the dissolution of pre-capitalist forms (illustrated by world trade with China, India, and the Arabs, and by the Russian land question) are to be understood. The chapter stresses that the separation of workers from means of production is not produced by capitalism originally but is presupposed by it; yet capitalist production reproduces and expands the separation at ever greater scale. This is the basis for the theory that capitalism tends to universalise itself by destroying every alternative mode of production it encounters. For philosophical anthropology. The chapter makes an implicit but powerful claim about the human condition under capital: the wage-labourer's \"labour-power\" exists \"in a state of separation from the means of production\" and \"can be directly used neither for the production of use-values for its possessor, nor for the production of commodities which he could live from selling.\" Only when sold does it become productive. The worker's very capacity to labour is a commodity whose use-value can only be realised by being handed over to another. There is a profound alienation implicit in this — the worker's own activity becomes \"an organ of capital,\" and the product is \"a commodity impregnated with surplus-value\" whose surplus-value \"costs [the capitalist] no equivalent.\" The chapter does not use the word \"alienation,\" but it is working out the material anatomy of the alienated relation. For the critique of dogmatism. Marx's warning against the \"crude, non-conceptual\" character of money and his insistence that the relation , not the thing , is primary is a methodological lesson in how to analyse social forms. Money is not the cause of the class relation; the class relation makes money into a function of capital. Slavery is not caused by the existence of money; money can be spent on slaves only if slavery already exists. Throughout the chapter, Marx insists that forms of consciousness — the categories of political economy — are conditioned by the material processes they express, and that the direction of explanation must always run from relations to forms, not the reverse. Final Reflections Chapter 1 of Volume 2 is a masterpiece of dialectical exposition. It takes a deceptively simple formula (M–C…P…C'–M') and unfolds from it the entire anatomy of capital as a self-reproducing process. The chapter's method is relentless abstraction: it strips away changes of value, irregularities of sale, consumer preferences, state intervention, and everything else that would obscure the pure forms of movement. Having isolated those forms, it interrogates each one — money capital, productive capital, commodity capital — to reveal the social relations each presupposes and expresses. The chapter's intellectual legacy is substantial. It established the framework within which the entire circulation process of capital would be analysed — turnover, reproduction, credit, and crises. It set the terms for later debates about financialisation, realisation crises, and the autonomy of circulation. And it produced some of Marx's most important formulations: the class relation as presupposed in the money relation, the distribution of the elements of production as the fundamental \"distribution,\" the real versus formal metamorphoses, and the distinction between the circuit's vivid appearance (money making money) and its concealed essence (production of surplus-value by labour-power). It is a chapter that demands slow reading — every paragraph contains a hinge on which the argument turns — and it repays that reading, because it shows how capital's most superficial appearance and its most fundamental structure are connected through a determinate series of mediations. To understand this chapter is to understand why political economy, even at its best, tends to mistake the form of appearance for the essence of capital: because capital itself, in its normal motion, produces exactly that illusion. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Introduction Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 2"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 2", "title": "Chapter 2", "subtitle": "The Circuit of Productive Capital", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 2/Chapter 2 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 11871, "read_time": "59 min read", "tags": ["Circuits of Capital (M-C-M')"], "keywords": ["Circuit of Productive Capital (P...C'-M'-C...P)", "Simple vs Expanded Productive Circuit", "Production as Continuous Movement", "Self-Maintenance of Productive Capital", "Illusion of Production as Self-Sufficient Goal"], "text": "Chapter 2 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 11,747 words ⏱️ ~59 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER ANALYSIS: MARX'S \"THE CIRCUIT OF PRODUCTIVE CAPITAL\" (Capital, Vol. II, Ch. 2) 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem and Thesis This chapter undertakes one of the most technically demanding maneuvers in all of Marx's Capital : the analysis of the circuit of productive capital , whose general formula is written P...C'–M'–C...P . Marx's overarching thesis is that while this circuit appears, on its surface, to be nothing more than a periodically repeated production process — \"reproduction\" — it is in fact a valorization process, i.e. a process not merely of producing but of periodically reproducing surplus-value . The chapter's objective is to demonstrate that the circuit of productive capital is the decisive form for grasping capitalist production as a whole, because it is the form in which the production of surplus-value and its subsequent conversion back into the elements of production are shown to be internally related moments of a single, self-renewing whole. The chapter's core intellectual objective is threefold. First, it seeks to distinguish this circuit rigorously from the circuit of money capital (M...M') analyzed in the previous chapter, showing that the two are not interchangeable perspectives but structurally distinct totalities with different implications for crises, hoarding, and accumulation. Second, it seeks to resolve a set of apparent paradoxes that arise within the formula — most critically, whether the formula depicts simple reproduction or reproduction on an expanded scale , a decision upon which \"the very definition of the first circuit depends.\" Third, it seeks to demonstrate the unity of production and circulation : that the entire circulation process of industrial capital, in this form, is not the substance of the process but merely an interruption and a mediation between productive capital as the opening extreme and productive capital as the closing extreme. The Paradoxical Structure of the Formula The chapter opens by noting the strange \"inversion\" that defines this circuit. In form I (money capital), the production process interrupts the circulation of money capital and appears only as a mediator between the two phases M–C and C'–M'. In form II (productive capital), by contrast, the entire circulation process appears only as the mediator of the periodically repeated reproduction , itself an interruption and a mediation. Circulation proper — the sphere of markets, buying and selling, monetary exchange — loses its apparent primacy and is subordinated to the reproduction of productive capital. Where the money circuit presented itself as M–C–M' , the productive circuit presents itself, in its circulatory segment, as C–M–C — that is, as the form of simple commodity circulation . This is a deeply ironic result: the circuit of the most developed form of capital appears in the least developed circulatory form. Situating the Chapter in Marx's Broader Framework This chapter belongs to the sequence in Volume II that analyzes the three circuits of industrial capital: money capital (Ch. 1), productive capital (Ch. 2), and commodity capital (Ch. 3). Chapter 2 therefore functions as the pivot of the volume's dialectical structure. Having established in Chapter 1 that money capital's circuit conceals the origin of surplus-value by presenting capital as self-breeding money, Marx here works through the form in which production — and thus the extraction of surplus labour — is explicitly the starting point. The chapter presupposes the fundamental results of Volume I: the theory of surplus-value as unpaid surplus labour; the distinction between constant capital (c) and variable capital (v); the concept of the value composition of capital; and the analysis of accumulation as the transformation of surplus-value into capital. Crucially, the chapter also prepares the ground for the volume's later treatments of turnover (Part Two), the reproduction schemes (Part Three), and — through its repeated asides on crises, hoarding, and the money form — the synthesis with the treatment of credit and crisis in Volume III. Marx's notes reference Tooke, the classics, and the \"vulgar economists,\" placing the analysis in explicit controversy with the existing tradition. The chapter's culminating assertion — \"The circuit of productive capital is the form in which the classical economists have considered the circuit of industrial capital\" — signals that Marx's project is not merely to describe the circuits but to critically reconstruct the categories through which bourgeois political economy has thought them. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The General Formula and Its Two Striking Features Marx opens with the bare formula of the circuit of productive capital: P...C'–M'–C...P Every symbol here carries a precise theoretical charge. P is productive capital — capital that exists in the form of the material elements of the production process, namely means of production (raw materials, machinery, auxiliary materials) and labour-power. The dotted line ( ... ) indicates that the process is continuous and interrupted — there is a phase (production) so internal to the circuit's continuity that it is not itself an act of exchange; it is the \"hidden\" phase in which value is created. C' is commodity capital , capital in the form of a mass of commodities that carries both the original capital value and the surplus-value created in production; the prime (′) marks it as augmented. M' is the money form of that same augmented value. The final C...P shows the return to the same starting point: money is used to buy means of production and labour-power, re-establishing productive capital. Marx immediately specifies what the formula signifies : \"the periodically repeated function of the productive capital, i.e. reproduction.\" Two features of this signification demand notice. First, reproduction here is \"in respect of valorization\" — the repetition is not merely material (producing the same physical goods) but value-theoretic: the periodic reproduction of surplus-value. Second, the new beginning is \"given by the point of departure itself\" — the circuit is self-starting and self-renewing, a self-enclosed totality . Marx then introduces a qualification that will recur throughout the chapter: a part of C' may, \"in certain cases, in the investment branches of industrial capital,\" directly re-enter the same labour process from which it emerged as a means of production. This is a reference to cases like agriculture (seed corn) or the mining/extraction industries, where a portion of the product re-enters production in kind. In such cases, the \"only independent expression it receives is as money of account\" — it never actually enters the circulation process. The same applies to the part of C' that the capitalist consumes in kind. Marx dismisses this as \"insignificant for capitalist production; at most it comes into consideration in agriculture.\" This is a methodological simplification : for analytic clarity, Marx brackets in-kind re-entry and in-kind consumption, assuming that all product is sold and all means of production bought in the market. The first striking feature of the formula is the inversion of the relationship between production and circulation. In form I (M...M'), production interrupts circulation and functions as a mediator. In form II (P...P), \"the entire circulation process... merely forms an interruption, and hence a mediation, between the productive capital that opens the circuit as the first extreme and closes it in the same form as the last extreme.\" Circulation is demoted to a mediating moment; reproduction is elevated to the primary content. The second striking feature is the formal inversion of the circulation sequence itself. In form I it was M–C–M (money buys commodity, commodity sells for more money). In form II it is C–M–C (commodity sells for money, money buys commodity) — \"i.e. the form of simple commodity circulation.\" This is not a mere surface detail. It means that, viewed from the circulation side alone, the circuit of the most advanced form of capital looks identical to simple commodity production — the very confusion that generates vulgar economics. I. SIMPLE REPRODUCTION C'–M'–C Between the Extremes P...P Marx now narrows his focus to the circulatory segment C'–M'–C that runs its course between the extremes P...P. The starting point of this circulation is the commodity capital: C' = C + c = P + c Here the equation must be read carefully. C' is the commodity capital as a whole. It decomposes into C + c : the portion representing the capital value (C) and the portion representing surplus-value (c). The second equality, = P + c , says that this capital value C is the commodity-form of the value of the productive capital P (P is now measured in money terms as the value advanced), plus the surplus-value c. So the total value of the commodity mass = value of productive capital + surplus-value. The function C'–M' — the realization of the capital value P (which now exists as a commodity component C) and of the surplus-value c contained in the same commodity mass — was already treated in Chapter 1, where it formed the concluding phase of the money circuit. \"Here it forms the second phase of the circuit, but only the first phase of circulation.\" This positional shift is critical: what was a terminal act is now an initiating act, because the circuit has a different starting point and therefore a different internal ordering. Marx then turns to the crucial indeterminacy. In form I, the circuit ended with M'. Since M' — as much as the original M — can reopen a circuit as money capital, it \"was at first unnecessary to see whether the M and m (surplus-value) contained in M' continue their paths together, or whether they describe different paths.\" The question only became urgent if one pursued the first circuit into its repetition . But in the circuit of productive capital, \"this point must be decided\" — because the definition of the first circuit now depends on it, since in this form C'–M' appears as the first phase of circulation (not the last), and must be supplemented by M–C. On this decision depends \"whether the formula depicts simple reproduction or reproduction on an expanded scale.\" The character of the circuit is altered. This is a decisive methodological point: the same formula P...P can represent either simple or expanded reproduction, depending on what happens to m — and the form itself forces us to confront the fork. The Assumption of Simple Reproduction Marx proceeds by first taking up simple reproduction : the case in which the entire surplus-value goes into the capitalist's personal consumption. He assumes, as in Chapter 1, that other circumstances remain the same and commodities buy and sell at their values. Under this assumption, once C' is transformed into money, the part representing capital value recirculates within the industrial capital circuit, while the other part — surplus-value turned into gold — leaves this circuit and enters the general circulation of commodities. It is \"money circulation proceeding from the capitalist, but it takes place outside the circulation of his individual capital.\" This spatial metaphor of \"outside\" is essential: the surplus-value's circuit, once monetized, is no longer part of this capital's self-reproduction. The Yarn Example and the Arithmetic of Value Components Marx sets out a concrete numerical illustration. We have a commodity capital C' of 10,000 lb. of yarn to the value of £500 . Of this, £422 was the value of the productive capital , and it continues the capital circulation as the money form of 8,440 lb. of yarn . Meanwhile the surplus-value of £78 — the money form of 1,560 lb. of yarn , \"the excess portion of the commodity product\" — makes its exit and describes a separate path within general commodity circulation. The arithmetic checks: 8,440 + 1,560 = 10,000 lb. And £422 + £78 = £500. The ratios: £422/£500 = 0.844 = 8,440/10,000; £78/£500 = 0.156 = 1,560/10,000. Marx then describes m–c : a series of purchases made with the money the capitalist spends, \"whether on commodities as such or on services, for his esteemed self and family.\" These purchases are fragmented and take place at different times. The money therefore exists temporarily as a money reserve or hoard destined for current consumption — because \"it is in the form of a hoard that any money whose circulation is interrupted exists.\" But crucially, in its function as means of circulation (which includes its temporary hoard form), this money \"does not enter into the circulation of the capital in its money form M.\" The money is not advanced, but spent . This distinction between money advanced (which expects to return with a surplus) and money spent (which is consumed and disappears) is fundamental to Marx's distinction between capital and revenue. The Machine Problem: Indivisibility and Ideal Value Fractions Marx then poses a crucial complication. Suppose the commodity product were not divisible yarn but a machine worth £500 with the same value composition. There would still be a value portion equal to the £78 surplus-value, but this £78 would exist only in the total machine . It could not be divided into capital value and surplus-value \"without being broken into pieces and thus destroying its value together with its use-value.\" The two value components could only be depicted ideally as components of the physical body of the commodity, not as independent elements. The contrast with yarn is instructive. Because yarn is homogeneous and divisible, each pound can be treated as an independent commodity element. The machine, by contrast, must be sold in its entirety before m can embark on its own particular circulation. Yet in the yarn case, the sale of 8,440 lb. leaves the remaining 1,560 lb. exhibiting \"a completely separate circulation of the surplus-value in the form c (1,560 lb. of yarn)–m (£78)–c (articles of consumption).\" Marx then gives the full per-unit decomposition of the yarn. The 10,000 lb. can be partitioned into: - Constant capital value (c) : 7,440 lb. of yarn with a value of £372 - Variable capital value (v) : 1,000 lb. of yarn with a value of £50 - Surplus-value (s) : 1,560 lb. of yarn with a value of £78 Check: 7,440 + 1,000 + 1,560 = 10,000 lb.; £372 + £50 + £78 = £500. And each individual pound is partitioned proportionally. One pound = 16 ounces. The fractions: - c : 11.904 oz. with a value of 8.928d. - v : 1.600 oz. with a value of 1.200d. - s : 2.496 oz. with a value of 1.872d. Check the ounces: 11.904 + 1.600 + 2.496 = 16.000 oz. ✓ Check the values: 8.928 + 1.200 + 1.872 = 12.000d = 1 shilling per lb. ✓ (since £500/10,000 lb. = 1s. per lb., and 20s. = £1, so £500 = 10,000s.) This per-unit decomposition establishes that the capitalist can successively consume the elements of surplus-value contained in the total yarn by selling it in successive portions , and can also realize c + v in the same way. But, Marx stresses, \"this operation similarly presupposes that the entire 10,000 lb. is sold, and that the value of c and v is therefore replaced by the sale of 8,440 lb.\" The whole must be sold for the parts to be separable in practice. The upshot: by way of C'–M', both the capital value and the surplus-value acquire a separable existence — the existence of different sums of money . Both M and m are \"the transformed form of the value that originally possessed its own expression merely as the price of the commodity, i.e. a merely ideal expression.\" The separation is thus a realization of a distinction that was only ideal in the commodity. The London Building Trade: Real Fractions of an Unfinished Whole Marx adduces a striking empirical illustration. In the London building trade, conducted largely on credit, the contractor receives advances in stages as the house is built. None of these stages is a house; each is \"a really existing component of a future house that is coming into being.\" Despite its reality, it is \"only an ideal fraction of the whole house, but it is sufficiently real, all the same, to serve as security for an additional advance.\" This example shows that the ideal fractionation of value can have real economic effects — here, it underwrites credit. It demonstrates that the conceptual separability of capital value and surplus-value is not a mere analytic convenience but is operative in real capitalist practice. The Three Consequences of the C'/M' Split Marx now enumerates the consequences of the split between C and c (capital value and surplus-value) that occurs in C'–M': First , when the commodity capital is realized — i.e. C'–(M + m) — the movement of capital value and surplus-value, which in C'–M' was still common to both and borne by the same mass of commodities, becomes divisible , since the two now possess independent forms as sums of money. Second , if this division takes place — m spent by the capitalist as revenue, M continuing the path prescribed by the circuit — then the first act C'–M', together with the subsequent acts M–C and m–c, \"can be depicted as two different circulations: C–M–C and c–m–c ; both of these, in their general form, are series that belong to the ordinary commodity circulation.\" This is a pivotal result: the single circuit of capital bifurcates into two formally identical commodity circulations. It is precisely this bifurcation that makes capital's circuit look like simple commodity circulation. Third , if the common movement of capital value and surplus-value only partially divides (so that part of the surplus-value is not spent as revenue) — or does not divide at all — then a change in capital value takes place within the circuit of the capital value itself, before the circuit is completed . In Marx's example, the value of the productive capital was £422. If M–C continues as £480 or £500, then it traverses the final stages of the circuit \"as a value £58 or £78 greater than it originally was.\" This can occur \"in combination with a change in its value composition.\" This third consequence is the bridge to accumulation: a partially or wholly unspent m means the capital value re-entering production is larger . Marx then restates the positional shift of C'–M'. It is \"the second stage of circulation and the concluding stage of circuit I (M...M'); ... the second stage of the present circuit and the first stage of commodity circulation in it.\" In so far as circulation is concerned, it must be supplemented by M–C. But M–C \"does not just have the process of valorization already behind it (in this case the function of P, the first stage), but its result, the commodity product C', has already been realized.\" Hence: \"The valorization of capital, as well as the realization of the commodity product in which the valorized capital value is represented, thus ends with C'–M'.\" The Vulgar Economists' \"Cretinism\" Having assumed simple reproduction — m–c completely separating from M–C — Marx observes that \"since both circulations, c–m–c and C–M–C, belong in their general form to commodity circulation (and thus do not exhibit any difference in value between their extremes), it is quite easy to conceive the capitalist production process, as the vulgar economists do, as the simple production of commodities, use-values destined for consumption of some kind or other, which the capitalist produced only in order to replace them with commodities of a different use-value.\" This is the \"characteristic cretinism\" of vulgar economics: because the circulatory forms C–M–C and c–m–c are formally identical to simple commodity exchange, the vulgar economist mistakes capital's circuit for simple commodity production, suppressing the valorization that is the whole point of the process . Marx counters: \"C' appears from the start as commodity capital, and the aim of the entire process, enrichment (valorization), by no means excludes a growth in the capitalist's consumption in line with the increase in the magnitude of surplus-value. In fact it absolutely includes it.\" The apparent \"consumption orientation\" of the process is not an alternative to valorization; it is a consequence of it. Yet Marx does not entirely dismiss the c–m–c moment. He notes that in the circulation of the capitalist's revenue, the commodity produced \"serves in point of fact only to convert this revenue into money and from money into a series of other commodities for the purpose of private consumption.\" But — and this is the crucial \"little fact\" — \" c is a commodity value which has not cost the capitalist anything ; it is the embodiment of surplus labour, which originally stepped forth onto the stage as a component of the commodity capital C'.\" Therefore c is \"already linked in its existence to the circuit of the capital value in process,\" and if the circuit halts or is disturbed, \"it is not only the consumption of c that is restricted, or completely ceases, but in addition the market for the set of commodities that form the replacement for c.\" This is a dialectical reversal: the consumption of surplus-value, though it appears to exit the circuit, remains dependent on the circuit's continuity. The \"freedom\" of revenue consumption is conditioned by the realization of capital. The Two Circulations: C–M–C and c–m–c Marx now insistently distinguishes the two circulations in their identity and difference . As soon as c becomes independent through m–c, \"in the form as a whole, c–m–c, it does not enter into the movement of the capital advanced by the capitalist, even though it proceeds from this.\" It is related to capital \"only in so far as the existence of capital presupposes the existence of the capitalist, and this latter is conditional on his consumption of surplus-value.\" This is a sociological rather than strictly economic link: capital's existence presupposes a capitalist who must consume to live, but the consumption as such is not a moment of capital's circuit. Within the general circulation, C' functions \"for example as yarn, simply as a commodity; but as a moment of the circulation of capital it functions as commodity capital.\" When the yarn is sold to a merchant, \"it is removed from the circuit of that capital whose product it is, but still continues as a commodity in the orbit of general circulation.\" The circulation of the mass of commodities continues \"even though it has ceased to form a moment in the independent circuit of the capital of the spinner.\" Therefore the really definitive metamorphosis of the mass of commodities — C–M, \"its final abandonment to consumption\" — \"can be completely separated in time and space from the metamorphosis in which this mass of commodities functions as his commodity capital.\" The same metamorphosis already accomplished in the circulation of this capital \"remains still to be completed in the sphere of the general circulation.\" This is a profound point about the division between the individual circuit and the aggregate . \"The general circulation includes the intertwining of the circuits of the various independent fractions of the social capital, i.e. the totality of individual capitals, as well as the circulation of those values that are not placed on the market as capital.\" The relation between the circuit of capital as part of general circulation, and as links in an independent circuit, is displayed by considering M' = M + m. M (as money capital) continues the circuit; m (spent as revenue, m–c) goes into general circulation but is cast out of the circuit of capital. \"Only that part of it enters the latter circuit that functions as additional money capital.\" And, Marx notes: \"In c–m–c, money functions simply as coin; the purpose of this circulation is the individual consumption of the capitalist.\" Here again the polemic blasts vulgar economics for depicting \"this circulation, which does not enter into the circuit of capital — the circulation of the portion of the value product that is consumed as revenue — as the characteristic circuit of capital.\" M–C: The Return to Productive Capital In the second phase, M–C , the capital value M = P is once again present, \"having rid itself of the surplus-value, i.e. with the same value magnitude as in the first stage of the circuit of money capital M–C.\" Despite the different position, the function of the money capital \"remains the same: its transformation into mp and L, means of production and labour-power.\" The capital value in the function of commodity capital C'–M' \"has thus passed through the phase C–M, at the same time as C–M\" — i.e. the transformation of the whole commodity mass into money simultaneously realizes capital value and surplus-value — and it now moves into the complementary phase M–C^(L/mp) , i.e. the purchase of L (labour-power) and mp (means of production). Its overall circulation is thus: C–M–C^(L/mp) . Marx then lays out three observations about money capital in this form: First , money capital M appeared in form I as the original form in which capital value was advanced. Now it appears from the start as part of the sum of money into which the commodity capital has been transformed in the first phase of circulation C'–M'. Here money capital exists \"neither as the original nor as the concluding form of the capital value,\" since the phase M–C is completed only by \"repeatedly stripping off the money form.\" Hence the portion of M–C that is simultaneously M–L (the purchase of labour-power) \"appears no longer as a mere advance of money for acquiring labour-power, but as an advance in which the same 1,000 lb. of yarn with a value of £50 is advanced for the labour-power in the money form, and this forms a portion of the commodity value produced by the labour-power.\" This is a revolutionary point: the money advanced to the worker is only the transformed equivalent form of a portion of the commodity value that the worker himself produces. \"And for this reason alone, the act M–C, in so far as it is M–L, is in no way simply the substitution of commodities in use form for commodities in money form, but includes other elements that are independent of the general circulation of commodities as such.\" The wage relation is not a mere market exchange; it is a moment of the production relation of capital. The Draft on Future Labour and the Demolition of the Stock Doctrine Marx now develops one of the most philosophically rich arguments in the chapter. M' appears as the transformed form of C', which is itself the product of the past function of P, the production process. \"The entire sum of M' thus appears as the monetary expression of past labour .\" He reiterates the decomposition: 10,000 lb. of yarn = £500 = product of the spinning process; 7,440 lb. = c = £372; 1,000 lb. = v = £50; 1,560 lb. = s = £78. If only the original £422 is advanced afresh, \"the worker merely receives as the next week's advance in M–L a portion of the 10,000 lb. of yarn produced in this week.\" As the result of C–M, \"the money is throughout the expression of past labour.\" But now the dialectical twist. In so far as the complementary act M–C is performed on the commodity market, \"there is again a conversion of past labour from one form (money) into another (commodity).\" Yet M–C is separate from C–M in time . It can in exceptional cases be simultaneous — if the capitalist performing M–C and the capitalist for whom this act is C–M transfer commodities simultaneously and M merely settles the balance. \"The difference in time between the execution of C–M and that of M–C may be more or less considerable.\" Although, as the result of C–M, M represents past labour, \" M can represent for the act M–C the transformed form of commodities that are not yet present on the market at all, but will be there only in the future .\" Examples: coal may be purchased before it is extracted from the mine; in so far as m figures as accumulation of money, it can represent cotton that will only be produced next year. The same applies to the expenditure of revenue m–c, and even to the wages of labour = £50: \"this money is not only the monetary form of the workers' past labour, but also a draft on simultaneous or future labour that will only be realized, or is supposed to be realized, in the future.\" The worker may use his wage to buy a coat that will only be made a week later. \"This is in particular the case with the very large number of necessary means of subsistence that must be consumed almost immediately, the moment they are produced, if they are not to spoil.\" In the money with which his wage is paid, \"the worker receives the transformed form of his own future labour or that of other workers.\" With one part of his past labour the capitalist gives him a draft on his own future labour. \" It is his own simultaneous or future labour which forms the as yet non-existent reserve stock with which his past labour is paid for. \" The conclusion is startling: \" Here the idea that a stock has to be formed is completely demolished. \" This is a direct polemic against the bourgeois (and particularly the \"Tooke-ist\" and quantity-theory-adjacent) notion that production requires prior accumulation of a stock of goods to pay workers and purchase inputs. Marx shows that the money form is not a stock but a claim — a draft on present and future labour. The reserve \"stock\" that pays the worker does not exist as a thing; it is the ongoing, simultaneous and future labour of the worker himself. Money as Means of Circulation and Means of Payment (Against Tooke) Marx now develops three further points about the money form: Second , in the circulation C–M–C^(L/mp), \"the same money changes its position twice; the capitalist first receives it as a seller, and then gives it out again as buyer.\" The transformation of commodity into money \"only serves to transform it from the money form into the commodity form again, and so the money form of capital, its existence as money capital, is thus only an evanescent moment in this movement.\" Alternatively, \"the money capital, in so far as the movement is fluid, appears as a means of circulation only when it serves as a means of purchase; it appears as an actual means of payment only when capitalists buy from each other, hence when there is simply a balance of payments to be settled.\" Here the editor's note attaches Marx's manuscript remark: \" Against Tooke .\" The note explains that Thomas Tooke, author of the six-volume A History of Prices (1838–57) — \"the last English economist of any value\" in Marx's words — was criticized for failing to distinguish between money as means of circulation and money as capital . Marx's point (elaborated in Volume III, Ch. 23) is that money's functioning as mere medium of exchange is not the same as its functioning as capital; the money-capitalist who gets his money back \"must always loan it out again, so long as it is to function for him as capital.\" The Chapter 2 discussion thus lays groundwork for the later critique of the banking/currency debates. Third , the function of money capital — whether as means of circulation or means of payment — \"is simply to mediate the replacement of C by L and mp,\" i.e. to replace the yarn (the commodity product, after deduction of the surplus-value spent as revenue) \"with its own elements of production.\" Its ultimate role is \"to mediate the transformation of commodity capital back into productive capital .\" The Requirements of Normal Circulation and the Problem of Value Change For the circuit to run its normal course, \"C' must be sold at its value and as a whole.\" Furthermore, C–M–C \"does not just include the replacement of one commodity by another, but its replacement in the same value relations .\" Marx concedes that this is an assumption. \"In fact, however, the value of the means of production varies; capitalist production is precisely marked by a continuous change in value relations , if only because of the constant change in the productivity of labour that characterizes it.\" He defers full treatment of this (Volume II, Ch. 15, §5 — \"pp. 360–68\") but flags it now. The transformation P into C' proceeds in the sphere of production; C' back into P takes place in the circulation sphere, mediated by the simple metamorphosis of commodities. \"Its content, however, is a moment of the reproduction process considered as a whole. C–M–C, as a form of circulation of capital, includes a functionally specific interchange of material.\" It requires that C equal the elements of production of C', and that these maintain their original value relations. \"If this is not the case, then the process cannot run its normal course.\" The Evanescent Money Form and the Critique of Form I Marx now draws the decisive contrast. In M...M', M is the original form of capital value, \"cast aside only to be re-assumed later.\" In P...C'–M'–C...P, M is \"only a form assumed in the process, and is already cast aside again within this.\" Here \"the money form appears simply as an evanescent form of value of the capital; the capital as C' is anxious to assume the money form but the capital as M' is equally anxious to get rid of it, as soon as it has pupated into it, in order to convert itself once more into the form of productive capital.\" Crucially: \" As long as it persists in the shape of money, it does not function as capital, and thus is not valorized; the capital remains idle. \" M functions here as a means of circulation — though a means of circulation of capital. The appearance of independence that the money form possesses in form I \" vanishes in this second form, which thus constitutes a critique of form I, and reduces this to a mere particular form .\" This is a major methodological claim: form II is not merely another form; it is a critique of form I, showing that the apparent self-sufficiency and self-breeding quality of money capital is an illusion generated by considering the circuit in isolation. Marx then notes the consequences of interruption. \"If the second metamorphosis M–C comes up against obstacles (e.g. if the means of production are unobtainable on the market), then the circular flow of the reproduction process is interrupted, just as if the capital was tied up in the form of commodity capital.\" The difference, however, is that capital \"can last out longer in the money form than in its previous commodity form.\" It does not cease to be money when it functions as capital; \"but it does cease to be a commodity, and in fact a use-value in general, if it is detained too long in its function as commodity capital.\" A key distinction: money is durable and general; a commodity detained too long may spoil or become unsaleable. \"Secondly, in the money form it is able to assume a form other than its original one of productive capital, while as C' it can move no further.\" Money capital can be redirected; commodity capital cannot. Marx adds: \"In its form, C'–M'–C includes for C' only acts of circulation which are moments of its reproduction; but the real reproduction of the C into which C' is converted is necessary to the performance of C'–M'–C', this is however conditional on reproduction processes outside the reproduction process of the individual capital depicted in C' .\" This is another pointer to the interconnection of individual capitals — the material replacement of C requires that other capitals, in other branches, have produced the needed means of production. The chapter restates the positional contrast: \"In form I, M–C^(L/mp) simply prepared the first transformation of money capital into productive capital; in form II it prepares the transformation of commodity capital back into productive capital .\" Thus, in so far as industrial capital remains invested in the same business, it prepares the transformation of C' \"back into the same elements of production from which it emerged.\" It appears \"as a return to this process, a repetition of it, hence as a forerunner to the reproduction process, and so also to the repetition of the valorization process.\" Marx reiterates the crucial point: \" M–L is not simple commodity exchange, but the purchase of a commodity L that is to serve for the production of surplus-value , while M–mp is only a procedure that is materially indispensable to the accomplishment of this end.\" Once M–C^(L/mp) is complete, M has been transformed back into productive capital and the circuit begins afresh. The Expanded Formula and the Materialist Interchange Marx expands the formula P...C'–M'–C...P as follows: the transformation of money capital into productive capital is the purchase of commodities for the purpose of commodity production . \"It is only in so far as consumption is productive consumption of this kind that it falls within the actual circuit of capital; the condition for consumption to occur is that surplus-value is made by means of the commodities thus consumed.\" This is \"something very different from production, even commodity production, whose purpose is the existence of the producers.\" The replacement of commodity by commodity conditioned by surplus-value production \"is something quite other than an exchange of products that is simply mediated by money. But this is how the matter is presented by the economists, as proof that no overproduction is possible .\" This is a direct sally against Say's Law and its variants. Marx then addresses the worker's own circulation. Besides the productive consumption of M (transformed into L and mp), the circuit contains the first link of M–L, which for the worker is L–M = C–M . Of the worker's circulation L–M–C , which includes his consumption, only the first link falls into the circuit of capital, as the result of M–L. \"The second act, i.e. M–C, does not fall into the circulation of the individual capital, although it proceeds from it. The constant existence of the working class, however, is necessary for the capitalist class, and so, therefore, is the consumption of the worker mediated by M–C.\" C'–M' and the Merchant: The Appearance of Consumption and the Crisis Marx now develops the argument that is \"an important one in considering crises.\" The act C'–M' merely assumes that C' is transformed into money, is sold, \"so that the circuit of the capital value can continue, and the surplus-value can be consumed by the capitalist.\" The commodity is bought only because it is a use-value, suitable for some kind of consumption, productive or individual. \"But if C' circulates further, e.g. in the hands of the merchant who has bought the yarn, this in no way disturbs — initially at least — the continuation of the circuit of the individual capital that has produced the yarn and sold it to the merchant.\" The whole process follows its course. \"As soon as C' is sold, is transformed into money, it can be transformed back into the real factors of the labour process, and hence of the reproduction process. Hence whether C' is bought by the final consumer or by the merchant who intends to sell it again does not directly alter the matter in any way.\" The volume of the mass of commodities brought into being by capitalist production \"is determined by the scale of this production and its needs for constant expansion, and not by a predestined ambit of supply and demand, of needs to be satisfied.\" Besides other industrial capitalists, mass production can have \"only wholesale merchants as its immediate purchasers.\" \" Within certain bounds, the reproduction process may proceed on the same or on an expanded scale, even though the commodities ejected from it do not actually enter either individual or productive consumption. \" The consumption of commodities \"is not included in the circuit of the capital from which they emerge.\" As long as the product is sold, everything follows its regular course, as far as the capitalist producer is concerned. His circuit is not interrupted. And if the process is expanded (which includes an expansion of productive consumption of means of production), \"then this reproduction of capital can be accompanied by a more expanded individual consumption (and thus demand) on the part of the workers, since this is introduced and mediated by productive consumption.\" Production of surplus-value and consumption of the capitalist can grow, \"and the whole reproduction process find itself in the most flourishing condition, while in fact a great part of the commodities have only apparently gone into consumption, and are actually lying unsold in the hands of retail traders, thus being still on the market.\" Here is the germ of the crisis theory. \"One stream of commodities now follows another, and it finally emerges that the earlier stream had only seemed to be swallowed up by consumption. Commodity capitals now vie with each other for space on the market. The late-comers sell below the price in order to sell at all. The earlier streams have not yet been converted into ready money, while payment for them is falling due. Their owners must declare themselves bankrupt, or sell at any price in order to pay. This sale, however, has absolutely nothing to do with the real state of demand. It has only to do with the demand for payment , with the absolute necessity of transforming commodities into money. At this point the crisis breaks out.\" Crucially: \" It first becomes evident not in the direct reduction of consumer demand, the demand for individual consumption, but rather in a decline in the number of exchanges of capital for capital, in the reproduction process of capital. \" This is a decisive claim: crises erupt first in the inter-capitalist sphere (capital–capital exchanges), not in the sphere of final consumer demand — a direct refutation of underconsumptionist theories that locate the cause of crises in insufficient workers' consumption. The Hoard Within the Circuit Marx then analyzes the money that must be held in reserve within M–C. To fulfil its function as money capital, M is converted into mp and L. If these commodities must be purchased or paid for at different dates, M–C \"then takes the form of a series of successive purchases and payments, so that a part of M performs the act M–C, while another part persists in the money state,\" and only serves for simultaneous or successive acts M–C at a time determined by the process. \"It is withdrawn from circulation only temporarily, to step into action and fulfil its function at a definite point in time.\" This storing of money \"is itself a function determined by its circulation and for circulation. Its existence as a fund for purchase and payment, the suspension of its movement, its state of interrupted circulation, is then a situation in which the money fulfils one of its functions as money capital.\" The money temporarily dormant is itself a part of M (of M' minus m = M). \"Furthermore, all the money that is withdrawn from circulation exists in the form of a hoard . The hoard form thus becomes here a function of the money capital.\" Marx notes: \"But it also proves true once again here that, within the circuit of industrial capital, money capital performs no other functions than those of money , and these money functions have the significance of capital functions only through their connection with the other stages of the circuit.\" This is the crucial money-as-such principle: money does not acquire new functions by being capital; its existing money functions (means of circulation, means of payment, store of value) acquire the significance of capital functions through their position in the circuit. \"The expression of M' as a relation between m and M, as a capital relation, is not a direct function of the money capital, but rather of the commodity capital C', which in turn expresses, as a relation between c and C, only the result of the production process, of the self-valorization of the capital value that takes place within it.\" If the circulation process meets obstacles — \"the state of the market, etc.\" — and M persists in its money state, \"then this is again a form of hoarding... It is the involuntary formation of a hoard .\" In our case, \"the money thus has the form of latent money capital , money capital that lies idle.\" Marx defers further treatment. \"In both cases, the persistence of money capital in its money state appears as the result of interrupted movement, whether this is expedient or inexpedient, voluntary or involuntary, functional or dysfunctional.\" 2. ACCUMULATION AND REPRODUCTION ON AN EXPANDED SCALE Marx opens this section with a technical premise: \"Since the proportions in which the production process can be expanded are not arbitrary, but are prescribed by technical factors, the surplus-value realized, even if it is destined for capitalization, can often only grow to the volume at which it can actually function as additional capital... by repeating a number of circuits.\" Until then, \"it must be stored up .\" The surplus-value thus builds up into a hoard, and in this form \"constitutes latent money capital .\" \"Latent, because as long as it persists in the money form, it cannot function as capital.\" The editor's footnote here is important: the term \"latent\" is borrowed from the physical concept of latent heat, \"which has now been more or less displaced by the theory of the transformation of energy.\" In Part Three (a later draft), Marx used \"potential capital\" (borrowed from potential energy) or, by analogy with D'Alembert's \"virtual velocities,\" \"virtual capital.\" This note shows the physical-scientific provenance of Marx's conceptual vocabulary — a reminder that Marx's method of abstraction is self-conscious and borrows deliberately from natural science. Marx clarifies: \"Thus the formation of a hoard appears here as a moment that is comprised within the process of capitalist accumulation, accompanies it but is at the same time essentially different from it. For the reproduction process is not itself expanded by the formation of latent money capital. On the contrary. \" The hoard is formed \"because the capitalist producer cannot directly expand the scale of his production.\" If the capitalist sells his surplus-product to a gold or silver producer, \"who thereby throws new gold or silver into circulation — or, what comes to the same thing, if he sells it to a merchant who uses part of the national surplus product to import additional gold or silver from abroad — then his latent money capital forms an increment to the national gold or silver hoard.\" In all other cases, \"the £78, say, that was means of circulation in the hands of the purchaser, has assumed in the hands of the capitalist only the form of a hoard; thus all that has taken place is a new distribution of the national gold or silver hoard.\" This is a crucial point about the aggregate: outside of gold/silver production, hoarding is redistribution , not net addition. If money functions as means of payment (so the commodity is paid for later), \"then the surplus product destined for capitalization is not transformed into money, but into claims for payment, titles to property equivalent to a sum that the purchaser either already has in his possession or expects to come into. It does not enter into the reproduction of the circuit, any more than the money that is invested in interest-bearing securities, etc.\" This prepares the analysis of credit later. Marx then restates the fundamental character of capitalist production: \"determined by the valorization of the capital value advanced , thus in the first instance by the production of the greatest possible amount of surplus-value; secondly, however (see Volume 1, Chapter 24), by the production of capital , i.e. the transformation of surplus-value into capital.\" Accumulation, or production on an expanded scale, \"which first appears as a means towards the constantly extended production of surplus-value, hence the enrichment of the capitalist, as the personal end of the latter, and is part of the general tendency of capitalist production, becomes in the course of its development... a necessity for each individual capitalist . The constant enlargement of his capital becomes a condition for its preservation.\" Marx then addresses a methodological point about the formula. He first considered simple reproduction, assuming the whole surplus-value is spent as revenue. \"In actual fact, a part of the surplus-value must always be spent as revenue in normal circumstances, and another part capitalized, and it is quite immaterial in this connection that at certain periods the surplus-value produced is completely consumed, and at others completely capitalized.\" If the movement takes its average course, \"there is a bit of both.\" \"In order not to complicate the formula, it is better to assume that the whole of the surplus-value is accumulated.\" The formula for expanded reproduction is then given: P...C'–M'–C^(L/mp)...P' This \"expresses: productive capital which is to be reproduced on a larger scale and with greater value, and begins its second circuit — or what comes to the same thing, repeats its first circuit — as augmented productive capital .\" As soon as the second circuit begins, \"we once again have P as the point of departure; it is simply that P is now a larger productive capital than the first P was.\" Similarly, in M...M', the second circuit begins with M', and M' functions as M, as money capital of a specific magnitude which has been advanced. \"All reference to its augmentation through the capitalization of surplus-value has vanished, once it steps forth in the function of money capital advanced. This origin was obliterated in its form as money capital just beginning its circuit. It is just the same with P', as soon as it functions as the point of departure for a new circuit.\" This obliteration of origin is an important theme: once a circuit begins, the starting capital appears simply as a given magnitude, its past accumulation erased. Marx then compares P...P' with M...M'. \"Each has a quite different significance.\" M...M', taken by itself as an isolated circuit, \"simply expresses that M, the money capital... is money breeding money, value breeding value , and brings forth surplus-value.\" In the circuit of P, \"on the contrary, the process of valorization is already complete as soon as the first stage, the production process, has taken place, and once it has passed through the second stage C'–M'... capital value and surplus-value already exist as realized money capital, as M', which in the first circuit appeared as the final extremity.\" The fact that surplus-value is produced \"was depicted in the first form of P...P that was considered by c–m–c, the second stage of which falls outside the circulation of capital and represents the circulation of surplus-value as revenue. In this form, in which the entire movement is represented by P...P, and there is thus no difference in value between the two end points, the valorization of the value advanced, the creation of surplus-value, is depicted as much as it is in M...M'; it is simply that the act C'–M' appears as the final stage in M...M', but as the second stage in the circuit, and first of the circulation stages, in P...P'.\" But now the crucial distinction: \"In P...P', P' does not express the fact that surplus-value is produced, but rather that the produced surplus-value is capitalized , i.e. that capital has been accumulated, and hence P', as opposed to P, consists of the original capital value plus the value of the capital accumulated through its movement.\" M', as the simple conclusion of M...M', as also C' wherever it appears, \"express, taken by themselves, not the movement, but rather its result; the valorization of the capital value realized in the commodity or money form.\" But — and this is the crucial materialist claim — \"neither in the form C' nor in the form M' is the valorization that has taken place a function of the money capital or the commodity capital. As a specific and distinct form or mode of existence that corresponds to the particular functions of industrial capital, money capital can perform only money functions, and commodity capital only commodity functions; the distinction between them is simply that between money and commodity. \" Similarly, industrial capital in its form as productive capital \"can consist only of the same elements as those of any other labour process that fashions products: on the one hand the objective conditions of labour (means of production), on the other hand productively (purposively) active labour-power.\" Just as industrial capital in the sphere of production can exist only in the combination corresponding to the production process in general (including non-capitalist production), so in the sphere of circulation it can exist \"only in the two forms of commodity and money that correspond to this.\" The elements of production proclaim themselves as productive capital \"in so far as the labour-power is the labour-power of others which the capitalist has bought from its owners; just as he has bought his means of production from the owners of other commodities.\" The production process itself appears as a productive function of industrial capital; \"so money and commodities appear as forms of circulation of this industrial capital, and thus also their functions as its circulation functions.\" \"It is only through their connection as functional forms which industrial capital has to go through in the various stages of its circuit that the money function and the commodity function are here at the same time functions of money capital and commodity capital.\" From this follows a major methodological warning: \" It is wrong, therefore, to seek to ascribe the specific properties and functions that characterize money as money and commodities as commodities to their character as capital, and it is just as wrong, conversely, to derive the properties of productive capital from its mode of existence in the means of production. \" This is a two-sided caveat: neither reify money/commodity properties as capital properties, nor derive capital properties from the physical forms of the means of production. This is a decisive anti-fetishistic point. When M' or C' are depicted as M + m, C + c, i.e. as a relation between capital value and surplus-value as its offshoot, \"this relation is expressed in one case in the money form, and the other case in the commodity form, but this does not alter the matter in any way. This relation thus does not arise from properties and functions that can be ascribed either to the money or the commodity as such.\" \"In both cases, the characteristic property of capital, that it is money which breeds money, is only expressed as the result. C' is always the product of the function of P, and M' is always simply the form into which C' has been transformed in the circuit of industrial capital.\" Hence, \"as soon as the realized money capital recommences its particular function as money capital, it ceases to express the capital-relation contained in M' = M + m. When the movement M...M' has been passed through, and M' begins the cycle anew, it does not figure as M', but rather as M, even if the entire surplus-value contained in M' has been capitalized.\" In our case, the second circuit begins with £500 instead of £422. \"The money capital that opens the circuit is £78 greater than previously and this difference exists when one circuit is compared with another, but such a comparison is not made within the individual circuit itself. The £500 now advanced as money capital, of which £78 existed earlier as surplus-value, does not play a different role from the £500 which another capitalist might use to open his first circuit.\" The same applies in the circuit of productive capital: the enlarged P' appears as P when the circuit is begun again, \"just like P in the simple reproduction P...P.\" Finally, Marx clarifies a terminological point. At the stage M'–C', \"the augmented magnitude is indicated simply by C', and not by L' and mp'.\" Since C is the sum of L and mp, \"it is already indicated by C' that the sum of the L and mp contained in it is greater than the original P.\" Secondly, however, \"the designations L' and mp' would be false , as we know that the growth of capital involves a change in its value composition , in the course of which the value of mp constantly grows, while that of L always declines relatively, and often even absolutely.\" This is a compressed statement of the tendency toward rising organic composition of capital: as accumulation proceeds, the value of means of production (mp) rises while the value of labour-power (L) relatively declines. 3. ACCUMULATION OF MONEY Marx here analyzes the conditions under which surplus-value in its golden form (m) can actually be added to the functioning capital. Key point: whether m \"is immediately added on to the capital value in process, and can thus embark on the circuit together with the capital M, making a total magnitude of M, depends on circumstances that are independent of the mere presence of m.\" If m is to serve as money capital in a second independent business alongside the first, \"it is clear that it can be invested in this only if it possesses the minimal magnitude required for such a business.\" If it is invested in extending the original business, \"then the relationship between the material factors of P, as well as their value relationship, similarly determines a certain minimal magnitude for m.\" Marx emphasizes: \"Between all means of production operating in this business there is not only a qualitative relation, but also a quantitative ratio, a proportionality .\" The material factors and the value relationships borne by them \"determine the minimum size that m must possess in order to be convertible either into additional means of production and labour-power, or into the former alone, as an increase of productive capital.\" His example: \"T he mill-owner cannot increase the number of his spindles without simultaneously purchasing a corresponding number of carding machines and roving-frames , to say nothing of the increased outlay on cotton and wages that this extension of his business would demand.\" For him to extend in this way, \"the surplus-value must already amount to a fair sum ( £1 per additional spindle is generally reckoned on).\" As long as m has not reached this minimum size, \"the capital circuit must be repeated several times, until the sum of the m's successively produced by it can function together with M in the form M'–C^(L/mp).\" \"Even detailed changes in the spinning machinery, for example, that make it more productive, require greater outlay on raw material, extension of the roving machinery, etc.\" \"In the meantime, therefore, m is stored up, and its accumulation is not its own function, but the result of repeated P...P . Its own function is its persistence in the money state until the repeated circuits of valorization, i.e. an external factor, have added to it sufficiently for it to have attained the minimum magnitude required for it to function actively.\" At that magnitude, \"it can really function for the first time as money capital, i.e. in the given case enter into the function of the money capital M as an accumulated portion of the latter.\" In the meantime \"it is stored up, and exists only in the form of a hoard in the process of formation and growth.\" \"Thus the accumulation of money, the formation of a hoard, appears here as a process that temporarily accompanies an extension of the scale on which industrial capital operates. Temporarily , because as long as the hoard persists in its state as a hoard, it does not function as capital, does not participate in the valorization process, but remains a sum of money that grows only because money available to it without any effort on its part is cast into the same coffer.\" \"The form of the hoard is simply the form of money not in circulation, money that is interrupted in its circulation and is therefore preserved in its money form.\" As far as the process of hoard formation itself is concerned, \"this is common to all commodity production, and it is only in the undeveloped pre-capitalist forms of the latter that it plays a role as an end in itself.\" In our case, \"the hoard appears as a form of money capital, and hoard formation as a process that temporarily accompanies the accumulation of capital, because and in so far as money figures here as latent money capital ; because the formation of a hoard... is a functionally determined preparatory stage that proceeds outside the circuit of capital, and paves the way for the transformation of surplus-value into really functioning capital.\" \"This characteristic is what makes it latent money capital , and is also why the scale that it must have attained in order to enter the process is determined by the value composition of the productive capital in each particular case. As long as it persists in the state of a hoard, it does not yet function as money capital, it is still money capital lying fallow ; not interrupted in its function, as in the previous case, but rather as yet incapable of performing this function.\" Marx distinguishes this \"original real form\" (a real hoard of money) from other forms. \"It can also exist merely in the form of favourable balances, of sums owed to the capitalist who has sold C'.\" As for other forms — \"interest-bearing deposits in a bank, bills of exchange or securities of one kind or other\" — these \"do not belong here. In that case, the surplus-value realized in money performs particular capital functions outside the circuit of the industrial capital from which it arose ; functions which have nothing to do with that circuit as such, and assume the existence of functions of capital distinct from the functions of industrial capital, which have not yet been developed here.\" This is a deferral to later volumes (credit, interest-bearing capital). 4. THE RESERVE FUND \"The accumulation fund can also perform particular ancillary services, i.e. it can enter into the circulation process of capital, without the latter possessing the form P...P' ,\" i.e. without expanded-scale reproduction. If the process C'–M' extends beyond its normal duration, \"then the commodity capital is abnormally delayed in its transformation into the money form; alternatively, if, when the transformation is completed, the price of the means of production into which the money capital must be converted has risen... above the level that it had at the beginning of the circuit, then the hoard that functions as accumulation fund can be used to take the place of money capital, or a part of this. The money accumulation fund then serves as a reserve fund to cope with disturbances in the circuit. \" As a reserve fund, it is different from the fund for purchase and payment considered in the circuit P...P. The latter \"was a part of the functioning money capital (thus the form of existence of a part of the total capital value in process), the parts of which functioned successively at different points in time. It formed a constant reserve of money capital in the continuity of the production process,\" since one day money is received and no payments have to be made until later, while another day large quantities of commodities are sold, and only later must large quantities be bought; within these intervals, \"a part of the circulating capital always exists in the money form.\" The reserve fund , on the other hand, \"is not a component part of the functioning capital, or, more precisely, the money capital, but rather capital going through a preliminary stage of its accumulation , surplus-value that has not yet been transformed into active capital.\" Marx notes practically: \"It goes without saying, of course, that when the capitalist is in need, he in no way ponders over the specific functions of the money that he has in his hands, but uses whatever he has in order to get the circulation process of his capital moving again.\" In the example: M = £422, M' = £500. If part of the £422 exists as a fund for purchase and payment, \"it is reckoned that, with circumstances remaining the same, it will enter as a whole into the circuit, and will also be sufficient for this purpose. The reserve fund, however, is a part of the £78 surplus-value ; it can enter the circuit of the capital of £422 only in so far as this circuit is accomplished in altered circumstances; for it is a part of the accumulation fund, and it figures here without an expansion in the scale of reproduction.\" \"In the money accumulation fund, money already exists as latent money capital, and is thus transformed into money capital.\" The General Formula The chapter closes with the general formula that comprises both simple and expanded reproduction: P...C'–M'–C^(L/mp)...P (P') And the clarification: \"If P = P, then M in C^(L/mp) is M' minus m; if P = P', then M in C^(L/mp) is greater than M' minus m; i.e. m has been wholly or partly transformed into money capital.\" In other words: if the circuit returns to the same magnitude (simple reproduction), the money advanced for L and mp equals M' minus m (i.e. the original capital value). If the circuit returns to a larger magnitude (expanded reproduction), the money advanced for L and mp exceeds M' minus m — because part or all of m has been added to the capital advanced. The final sentence: \" The circuit of productive capital is the form in which the classical economists have considered the circuit of industrial capital. \" This is a critical-historical verdict: the classics (Quesnay, Smith, Ricardo, and Marx's predecessors) grasped the circuit in this form, and their failure to distinguish it rigorously from the money circuit generated their confusions (e.g. Smith's \"vicious circle\" of price-components, the erroneous reduction of value to wages + profit + rent). The chapter thus closes on a polemical note: form II is the practical and historical form of the circuit, but its correct conceptualization requires the critiques of Chapters 1 and 3. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed The chapter yields a set of interconnected systemic laws. First , the primacy of production over circulation within capitalist reproduction. The circuit of productive capital demonstrates that circulation, far from being the substance of capital's movement, is a mediation and an interruption between productive capitals — and yet a necessary mediation, since without C'–M'–C the material replacement of the elements of production cannot occur. Second , the internal unity of valorization and material replacement . Production (P → C') and circulation (C' → P) are not separate processes but moments of a single reproduction. The formula P...P is therefore the most adequate expression of industrial capital as a self-renewing total process, in which the value relation and the material interchange are inseparably connected. Third , the critique of the money form's apparent self-sufficiency . Form II \"constitutes a critique of form I\": the money circuit's apparent self-breeding quality is dispelled once we see money as an evanescent moment that capital is \"anxious to get rid of.\" This is the theoretical foundation for the demystification of interest-bearing capital. Fourth , the separation of capital value from surplus-value and its consequences . The C'/M' split generates the possibility of bifurcation into C–M–C and c–m–c; the former sustains capital, the latter sustains the capitalist. But because c is \"a commodity value that has not cost the capitalist anything,\" the revenue sphere remains dependent on the circuit's continuity, refuting the notion that capitalist consumption is the engine of the system. Fifth , the doctrine of the \"draft on future labour.\" The money form is not a pre-existing stock but a claim on present and future labour. This shatters the stock conception of capital and grounds Marx's refutation of the wage-fund theory and of the notion that capital advances to labour from a previously accumulated fund. Sixth , the theory of crisis location . Crises break out first \"in a decline in the number of exchanges of capital for capital,\" not in a fall in direct consumer demand. The possibility of crisis is structurally inscribed in the separation between sale (C'–M') and consumption, and between the individual circuit and general circulation. Seventh , the quantitative determination of accumulation . The minimum magnitude required for m to function as capital — determined by the technical and value proportions of the means of production (the mill-owner's spindles, carding machines, roving-frames, raw material) — demonstrates that accumulation is not smooth or continuous but lumpy, requiring the hoarding of latent money capital. Accumulation presupposes a prior phase of dysfunction — money lying fallow. Eighth , the twofold correction of fetishism : it is as wrong to ascribe money/commodity properties to capital as it is to derive capital's properties from the physical forms of the means of production. Capital is a social relation expressed through, but not identical with, its material bearers. Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications Theoretically, the chapter resolves a foundational problem for the analysis of reproduction as a whole: it establishes that the circuit of productive capital is the most concrete of the three circuits, because it does not abstract from production but incorporates it as the initiating and terminating moment. This makes it the appropriate starting point for the reproduction schemes of Part Three (Volume II, Chs. 18–21), where the interconnections among Departments I and II (means of production vs. means of consumption) are worked out. Practically, the chapter yields a structural theory of capitalist crises that resists both underconsumptionist and Say's-Law extremes. Because commodities may be sold without being consumed (the merchant purchases yarn that \"apparently\" goes into consumption), reproduction can proceed \"in the most flourishing condition\" while unsold commodities pile up — but the reckoning eventually arrives through the \"demand for payment,\" not the demand of consumers. This is a sophisticated argument that locates the point of eruption of crises in the credit and inter-capitalist sphere while acknowledging the underlying anarchy of production. Philosophically, the chapter reflects Marx's dialectical method at its most rigorous. The formula P...P is not a static identity but a self-moving contradiction : production is interrupted by circulation, circulation by production; the money form is both essential and foreign; surplus-value both exits the circuit (as revenue) and remains bound to it (as latent capital). The \"obliteration of origin\" when a new circuit begins — the fact that the £500 opening capital hides its £78 surplus-value provenance — captures the fetishistic appearance of capital as a given, self-originating magnitude. Throughout, the chapter insists on the Historische und logische unity of capitalist production: the same process is both a continuous reproduction of value relations and a periodic rupturing of them. In the hoard, the reserve fund, and the crisis, Marx demonstrates that capital's apparent smoothness conceals constitutive instability — the very instability that makes its self-expansion a contradictory and ultimately historical process. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 1 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 3"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 3", "title": "Chapter 3", "subtitle": "The Circuit of Commodity Capital", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 3/Chapter 3 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 6924, "read_time": "35 min read", "tags": ["Circuits of Capital (M-C-M')", "Social Reproduction Schemes (Dept I & II)"], "keywords": ["Circuit of Commodity Capital (C'...C')", "Commodity Capital as Premise and Result", "Total Social Product Included in the Circuit", "Consumption Linked with Circulation", "Social Interdependence of Capitals"], "text": "Chapter 3 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,885 words ⏱️ ~34 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem Chapter 3 of Capital , Volume 2, completes Marx's systematic construction of the three circuits of industrial capital. Having already analyzed in Chapter 1 the circuit of money capital (M...M') and in Chapter 2 the circuit of productive capital (P...P), Marx now turns to the third and most complex figure: the circuit of commodity capital , whose general formula is C'–M'–C...P...C' . The chapter's central intellectual problem is this: the third form is not a mere permutation of the first two. It possesses properties the other two structurally cannot possess, and these properties force the analysis of capital beyond the horizon of the individual industrial capital toward the total social capital . The Core Thesis Marx's thesis unfolds in three interlocking claims: First , in the circuit of commodity capital the valorized capital value—C' = capital value + surplus-value, i.e. C + c—appears from the very start as the point of departure. Unlike forms I and II, where the circuit opens with capital value still to be valorized (M and P respectively), form III opens with capital that is already pregnant with surplus-value. This is the decisive formal difference. In forms I and II, the closing points M' and P' must be \"stripped\" back to M and P when the circuit renews; in form III, the starting-point remains C' even under simple reproduction. The surplus-value is present at the threshold. Second , because surplus-value is embedded in the opening extreme, the circuit of commodity capital includes from the start both the movement of the capital value and the movement of the surplus-value. This means that consumption —both productive consumption and individual consumption (the worker's and, as a social act, the capitalist's)—enters the circuit as an explicit presupposition. The division of the total product into a reproduction fund and an individual consumption fund is inscribed in the form of the circuit itself. No other circuit makes this visible. Third , and most consequentially, form III presupposes the existence of other industrial capitals . For C' to open a circuit, its conversion into money must be matched by another capital's purchase; and C (L + mp) must already exist on the market as the commodity product of other capitals. Therefore, when the figure C'...C' is conceived \"in its particularity,\" it can no longer be treated as the isolated motion of a single capital. It \"demands to be considered... as the form of motion of the sum of individual capitals, i.e. of the total social capital of the capitalist class.\" The circuit of commodity capital is thus the gateway to the reproduction schemas and to the Tableau économique of Quesnay. Fit Within the Broader Framework This chapter is the hinge of Volume 2. The three circuits are not alternatives—they are moments of a single, integrated, self-reproducing totality. Marx treats them as the differentiated forms through which one and the same industrial capital moves. Forms I and II were analyzed in relative abstraction; form III exposes the interdependence that forms I and II presupposed but concealed. The chapter therefore transitions from the analysis of the individual capital (Chapters 1–3) to the analysis of the total social capital and its reproduction on a simple and expanded scale (later chapters, and the reproduction schemas). Its closing invocation of Quesnay's Tableau économique —the first attempt to depict the total annual product as a circuit—signals precisely this transition. The chapter is, in short, where Marx's theory of capital becomes macroeconomics . 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The General Formula and the Double Role of C' Marx opens with the general formula: C'–M'–C...P...C' . The first move is to establish that C' here is not simply the result of the two earlier circuits but also their premise . This bidirectional relation is crucial. What is M–C for one capital is simultaneously C'–M' for another, \"at least in so far as a part of the means of production are themselves the commodity product of other individual capitals in their circuits.\" Coal, machinery, and so on, are the commodity capital of the mine-owner, the capitalist engineer, etc. Thus the very existence of a given capital's means of production requires that other capitals have already completed their own C'–M' phase. Marx notes that even in Chapter 1, when M...M' is first repeated, both the circuit P...P and the circuit C'...C' are presupposed. The circuits are mutually embedded: no single capital can be understood in isolation. For expanded reproduction , the concluding C' is larger than the starting C', designated C\" . This notation matters: at every scale of reproduction the circuit opens with a composite —capital value plus surplus-value—not with a pure magnitude. The Three Structural Differences Between Form III and Forms I and II Marx isolates three formal distinctions. First difference: the placement of circulation. In form III, \"the circuit commences with the entire circulation, in its two opposing phases.\" By contrast, in form I (M...M') the circulation was interrupted by the production process—money → commodities → production → commodities → more money, where production sits between the two halves. In form II (P...P) the entire circulation and its two complementary phases \"simply appeared as a mediation for the reproduction process,\" i.e., as the mediating movement between P and P. Form III is different again: its circulation opens the circuit rather than interrupting or mediating it. The formal pattern of circulation also differs: with M...M', the circulation takes the form M–C–M (money → commodity → more money); with P...P and with C'...C', it takes the form C–M–C (commodity → money → commodity). The direction of the money-commodity metamorphosis's purpose —money-seeking-more-money versus commodity-seeking-again-commodity—shapes each form's appearance. Second difference: what happens to the starting-point when the circuit renews. When circuits I and II repeat, \"the form in which they were produced vanishes.\" M' = M + m and P' = P + p both begin the new process again as M and P . Why? Because in form I, M' functions as money capital M , the advance in monetary form of capital value yet to be valorized. Whether the advance is £422 or £500 makes no difference—it appears \"simply as capital value.\" M' is no longer capital pregnant with surplus-value, no longer a capital-relation; it becomes valorized only in the course of the process . Similarly, P' must always function as P—capital value that should produce surplus-value—and repeat the circuit. The surplus-value achieved is cast off at the renewal point; the circuit restarts from a purified magnitude. In form III , however, the starting-point must be designated C' even when the circuit is renewed on the same scale . The reason: \"The circuit of commodity capital... does not just open with capital value, but with expanded capital value in the commodity form, and thus it includes from the start not only the circuit of the capital value present in the commodity form, but also that of the surplus-value.\" If simple reproduction occurs, the circuit closes with a C' of equal magnitude to its starting-point. If a part of surplus-value is capitalized, the closing is C\"—a bigger C\"—but the next circuit still opens with C\", which is simply a larger accumulated capital value carrying relatively more newly produced surplus-value. \"In all cases, C' always opens the circuit as a commodity capital equal to capital value plus surplus-value.\" This is the formal signature of form III: the surplus-value never \"vanishes\" from the point of departure. Third difference (developed later in the chapter): the nature of the concluding transformation. We will return to this under the heading of the closing extreme. The Intercapital Dimension: C' as the Form of Another Capital Marx now develops the social dimension of C'. \"In the circuit of an individual industrial capital, C' as C appears not as the form of this capital, but as the form of another industrial capital, in so far as the means of production are the product of this other capital.\" Concretely: the act M–C (i.e. M–mp) of the first capital is for the second capital C'–M' . The purchase of means of production by capital A is the sale of commodity capital by capital B. This leads to a subtle analysis of the two elements of the production process, L (labour-power) and mp (means of production), within the act M–C. In the circulation act M–C^L_mp, \"L and mp behave identically in so far as they are commodities in the hands of their sellers\"—the workers who sell labour-power, and the possessors of means of production who sell the latter. For the buyer , whose money here functions as money capital, both figure merely as commodities, as long as he has not yet bought them, thus as long as they confront his money-form capital as the commodities of others. But they differ in one respect: mp is C' in the hands of its seller and can thus be capital if mp is the commodity form of that seller's capital, whereas L is always just a commodity for the worker , becoming capital only in the hands of the buyer, as a component part of P. The worker's labour-power is never capital in the worker's hands; it is made into capital only at the moment of purchase, and then only as an element of the buyer's productive capital. This asymmetry is a compact statement of the capital-relation. Why C' Can Never Open the Circuit as Mere C Marx insists: \"C' can therefore never open a circuit as mere C, as merely the commodity form of the capital value.\" As commodity capital, C' has a dual aspect : From the point of view of use-value : it is the product of the function of P—here, yarn—whose elements L and mp, emerging from circulation as commodities, have only functioned to fashion this product. From the point of view of value : it is the capital value P plus the surplus-value m produced in the function of P. The two aspects cannot be collapsed. It is only in the circuit of C' itself that C = P = the capital value can and must separate itself from the portion of C' in which surplus-value exists—from the surplus product in which the surplus-value is hidden. Whether the two are actually separable (as with yarn, where the physical product can be divided) or not (as with a machine, a single indivisible unit), they become separable in principle as soon as C' has been transformed into M'. Money is homogeneous and infinitely divisible; the value composition becomes explicit in the money-form. The Divisibility Analysis: 10,000 lb. of Yarn This is the chapter's most technically detailed passage. Marx works through an extended numerical example to demonstrate that the conceptual division of C' into c + v + s can be materially performed. The total commodity product is 10,000 lb. of yarn with a value of £500. The capital value is £422, the surplus-value £78. 8,440 lb. = £422 = the capital value , separable from the surplus-value. 1,560 lb. = £78 = the surplus-value (surplus product). If the capitalist first sells 8,440 lb. for £422, this 8,440 lb. represents C, the capital value in commodity form; the surplus product (1,560 lb., £78) only circulates later. The capitalist can complete C–M–C^L_mp (reconstituting his production elements) before the circulation of the surplus product c–m–c . Alternatively, he can sell 7,440 lb. at £372 and 1,000 lb. at £50 . With the first part (7,440 lb. = £372) he replaces constant capital c ; with the second (1,000 lb. = £50) he replaces variable capital v (labour-power). Note the arithmetic: 7,440 + 1,000 + 1,560 = 10,000; £372 + £50 + £78 = £500. Marx then pushes the decomposition further, showing that each portion can itself be broken down in the same proportions. The 7,440 lb. = £372, representing constant capital, decomposes into: 5,535.360 lb. = £276.768 — replaces the constant part (value of means of production used up in the 7,440 lb.) 744 lb. = £37.200 — replaces the variable capital 1,160.640 lb. = £58.032 — the surplus-value in the form of surplus product Having sold 7,440 lb., the capitalist can replace the capital value contained in it (from the sale of 6,279.360 lb. at £313.968—note 5,535.360 + 744 = 6,279.360, and £276.768 + £37.200 = £313.968) and spend the value of the surplus product (1,160.640 lb. = £58.032) as revenue. The 1,000 lb. = £50 (variable capital) decomposes into: 744 lb. = £37.200 — constant capital value in the 1,000 lb. 100 lb. = £5 — variable capital value 156 lb. = £7.800 — surplus product So 844 lb. (744 + 100) = £42.200 replaces the capital value contained in the 1,000 lb.; 156 lb. = £7.800 is consumable surplus. The remaining 1,560 lb. = £78 decomposes into: 1,160.640 lb. = £58.032 — replaces the value of means of production contained in the 1,560 lb. 156 lb. = £7.800 — replaces variable capital value Together: 1,316.640 lb. = £65.832 — replacement of total capital value 243.360 lb. = £12.168 — surplus product to be spent as revenue (The arithmetic checks: 1,160.640 + 156 + 243.360 = 1,560; £58.032 + £7.800 + £12.168 = £78.) The crowning move is the per-pound decomposition. Since each element c, v, s in the yarn is divisible into the same component parts, so is each individual pound of yarn worth 1 shilling = 12d: c = 0.744 lb. = 8.928 d. v = 0.100 lb. = 1.200 d. s = 0.156 lb. = 1.872 d. c + v + s = 1 lb. yarn = 12 d. The conceptual proportions of the total are physically present in every single pound. Marx then tabulates the three partial sales and sums them, showing that the aggregate is identical to a single sale of the whole 10,000 lb.: constant capital = 7,440 lb. = £372; variable capital = 1,000 lb. = £50; surplus-value = 1,560 lb. = £78; total 10,000 lb. = £500. The conceptual decomposition reproduces the empirical totality exactly. The Sale \"As Such\" Versus the Circuit Function Marx then draws a sharp limit on what this example proves. \"Taken by itself, C'–M' is nothing more than a sale of 10,000 lb. of yarn.\" To the buyer, yarn is just a commodity; what interests him is the price of 1 shilling per lb. If the buyer enquires into value composition, it is only \"with the crafty intention of showing that it could be sold below 1 shilling per lb. and the seller would still be doing good business.\" The quantity the buyer purchases depends on his own needs , and if he owns a weaving-mill, on the composition of his own capital —not on that of the spinner. The ratio in which C' serves partly to replace capital and partly as surplus product \"exists only in the circuit of the capital whose commodity form is represented by the 10,000 lb. of yarn. It has nothing to do with the sale as such.\" This is an important methodological clarification. The decomposition c + v + s describes a functional relation internal to the circuit, not a description of the market act. Marx adds the standing assumption that \"C' is sold at its value\"—so only the transformation of form is at issue. He acknowledges that for C' as a functional form, \"it is of course decisive... whether and to what extent price and value diverge\"—but \"here, where we are merely considering distinctions of form, this is of no concern.\" The abstraction is deliberate and bounded. Retrospective Characterization of Forms I and II Before completing form III, Marx recapitulates the other two circuits to sharpen the contrast. Form I, M...M'. Production appears in the middle, between the two complementary and opposed phases of circulation; it is over before the concluding C'–M' begins. Money is advanced as capital, transformed into elements of production, then into the commodity product, then back into money. \"This is a finished and complete cycle of business, the result being money which can be used by anyone for anything.\" Hence the recommencement of the cycle is indicated only as a possibility . M...M' may be the final circuit—concluding the functioning of a capital that is then withdrawn from business—or the first circuit of a newly entering capital. The general movement is M...M' , from money to more money. Note the ideological consequence: because production appears merely as an interruption between two money-phases, form I is the natural home of Mercantilist thinking, which fixes on money as the goal. Form II, P...C'–M–C...P(P'). Here the entire circulation process follows the first P and precedes the second, in the opposite order to form I. The first P is productive capital; its function is the production process, as precondition for the subsequent circulation. The concluding P, however, is not the production process: it is only the renewed existence of the industrial capital in its form of productive capital—the result of the transformation of capital value into L + mp , the objective and subjective factors whose union constitutes productive capital. Whether P or P', the capital is present at the conclusion in a form in which it must again function as productive capital. The general form P...P' is the form of reproduction and does not announce that valorization is the purpose of the process. This has major ideological consequences. Because P...P' presents reproduction as the purpose, \"classical economics found it all the more easy to ignore the specifically capitalist form of the production process, and to present production as such as the purpose of the process\"—to produce as much and as cheaply as possible and exchange the product for as many other products as possible, partly to repeat production (M–C), partly for consumption (m–c). Since M and m appear only as evanescent means of circulation, \"the peculiarities of both money and money capital could be overlooked, the whole process then appearing simple and natural, i. e. possessing the naturalness of superficial rationality.\" In the case of commodity capital, similarly, profit \"was occasionally forgotten,\" and capital figured simply as a commodity—whenever the production circuit as a whole was discussed; only when the value components were discussed did it figure as commodity capital. \"Accumulation, of course, appeared in the same light as production.\" Marx is showing that each form has a corresponding ideological trap : form I breeds Mercantilism, form II breeds the \"naturalness\" of productionist classicism that erases the capitalist specificity of the process. Completion of Form III: The Determining Role of C' Form III, C'–M'–C...P...C' , opens with the two phases of circulation in the same order as form II (C–M–C); then P follows with its function (the production process), as in form I; the circuit closes with C', the result of that process. Just as form II closed with P (the merely renewed existence of productive capital), so form III closes with C' (the renewed existence of commodity capital); just as in form II the capital in its concluding form P had to begin again as a production process, so here it must reopen with the reappearance of industrial capital in the form of commodity capital, with the circulation phase C'–M'. \"Both forms of the circuit are incomplete, because they do not conclude with M', with the valorized capital value transformed back into money.\" Both must be continued further and hence include reproduction. The overall circuit in form III is C'...C' . What differentiates the third form: \"it is only in this circuit that the valorized capital value, and not the original capital value that has still to be valorized, appears as the starting-point of its own valorization.\" C', as capital-relation, is the point of departure and thus has a determining effect on the whole circuit , in so far as the circuit includes, even in its first phase, both the circuit of the capital value and that of the surplus-value. And surplus-value must on average, even if not in every individual circuit , be partly spent as revenue (passing through the circulation c–m–c ) and partly function as an element of capital accumulation. Consumption as Presupposition A major consequence follows: \"In the form C'...C', the consumption of the entire commodity product is presupposed as the condition for the normal course of the circuit of capital itself.\" The individual consumption of the worker and the individual consumption of the non-accumulated part of the surplus product together comprise total individual consumption. Thus consumption in its entirety —individual and productive—enters the circuit of C' as a precondition . Marx distinguishes carefully: Productive consumption — which \"in the nature of the case includes the individual consumption of the worker, for labour-power is the permanent product, within certain limits, of the worker's individual consumption\" — is carried on by every individual capital. Individual consumption — other than what is necessary for the existence of the individual capitalist — \"is presupposed only as a social act, in no way as the act of the individual capitalist.\" In forms I and II, the overall movement presents itself as a movement of the advanced capital value. In form III, the valveorized capital, in the shape of the total commodity product, forms the starting-point and possesses the form of capital in movement—commodity capital. \"It is only after its transformation into money that this movement splits up into movement of capital and movement of revenue.\" The division of the total social product—and the particular division of the product of every individual commodity capital—into an individual consumption fund and a reproduction fund is included in this form of the circuit of capital . Expansion and Contraction Across the Three Forms Marx now compares how each form accommodates changes in scale. M...M' allows possible expansion according to the scale on which m enters the new circuit. P...P : P can begin the new circuit with the same value, perhaps even a lesser value, and yet still represent expanded reproduction —if, for example, the commodity elements are cheapened by increased productivity of labour. Conversely, a productive capital that has grown in value may represent materially more restricted reproduction, if the elements of production have become dearer. \"The same applies for C'...C'.\" This is a critical point: the magnitude of value of the opening extreme does not transparently index the material scale of reproduction. Value and material scale can move in opposite directions because productivity and prices shift. The circuit's formal magnitude is not a reliable measure of its real content. C' as Premise of Production—the Social Dimension Marx deepens the \"premise\" argument. \"In C'...C', capital in the commodity form is the premise of production; it reappears as a premise within this circuit in the second C.\" If this C is not yet produced or reproduced, the circuit is inhibited ; it must be reproduced, \"for the most part as the C' of another industrial capital.\" In this circuit, C' exists as point of departure, point of transit, and conclusion; \"in other words it is always there. It is a permanent condition for the reproduction process.\" The Special Character of the Closing Transformation in Form III Marx now specifies a further distinguishing characteristic, addressing all three circuits' commonality: each opens and closes in the same form, returning to its initial form and recommencing. The initial forms M, P, and C' are the forms in which the capital value is advanced (in form III together with the surplus-value adhering to it)—the original forms for the circuit; the concluding forms M', P, and C' are in each case the transformed form of a preceding functional form which is not the original. In form I, M' is the transformed form of C', the transformation effected by a simple process of commodity circulation (a formal change of position between commodity and money). In form II, the closing P is the transformed form of M (again a formal change of position). In form III, C' is the transformed form of P , the productive capital. But in form III the transformation differs in two respects: (1) it affects not just the functional form of the capital but also the magnitude of its value ; and (2) the transformation is not the result of a merely formal change of position belonging to the circulation process, but rather the real transformation which the use form and the value of the commodity components of the productive capital have undergone in the production process. In forms I and II the closing extremes are the direct results of the circulation process; in form III the closing C' is the result of the production process. This is why only in form III does the closing extreme bear a new magnitude (surplus-value) born of production rather than circulation. The Presupposition of Outside Capitals—Form III's Unique Demand Marx examines what each circuit presupposes outside itself . Form I : M', as closing point, is the transformed form of C' (C'–M'); it presupposes M in the hands of the buyer , existing outside the circuit M...M', brought in by the sale of C' and made into the closing form. Form II : the closing P presupposes L and mp (C) as existing outside it, incorporated as the closing form by M–C. \"But apart from the final extreme, the circuit of the individual money capital does not presuppose the existence of money capital as such, and the circuit of the individual productive capital does not presuppose the existence of productive capital in the circuit itself. In form I, M may be the only money capital, and in form II P may be the only productive capital, that appears on the historical scene.\" Form III is different: C is twice presupposed outside the circuit. First, in the circuit itself C'–M'–C...P...C'—where M–C, i.e. the purchase of L and mp, presupposes C as a commodity in the hands of its seller; this C is itself commodity capital in so far as it is the product of a capitalist production process (\"and even when this is not the case, it appears as commodity capital in the hands of the merchant\"). Second, in the second c of c–m–c , which must similarly be present as a commodity in order to be bought. In either case, whether commodity capital or not, L and mp are commodities as much as C' is, and act towards one another as commodities. Thus, in so far as C' = C (L + mp), commodities are its own elements of formation and must be replaced by equivalent commodities in circulation—just as must the second c in c–m–c. All Commodities Tend to Become Commodity Capital Marx adds a systematic generalization: \"on the basis of the capitalist mode of production, as the prevailing mode, all commodities must be commodity capital in the hands of their sellers .\" They continue to be so in the hands of the merchant, or they become so if they were not previously. Alternatively, they can be commodities such as imported articles, which replace original commodity capital and hence simply give it another form of existence. This is the historical-universalizing claim: under capitalism, the commodity form and the capital form tend to coincide for the seller of any commodity. The Regrouping of L and mp into Productive Capital Marx notes that L and mp, the elements of P, \"do not possess the same shape, as forms of existence of P, as they did on the various commodity markets from which they were brought together. They are now united, and in their combination they can function as productive capital.\" The scattered commodities of the market are gathered and fused into a new functional unity—the very act of production. Why C Is Premise of C Only in Form III Marx offers the decisive formal explanation: \"If it is only in this form III, within the circuit itself, that C appears as a premise of C, this is because the starting-point is capital in the commodity form.\" The circuit opens by the conversion of C' (in so far as it functions as capital value, whether or not increased by surplus-value) into the commodities forming its elements of production. This conversion comprises the entire circulation process C–M–C (= L + mp) and is its result. So C stands at both extremes —though the second extreme, receiving its form C from outside (from the commodity market) by way of M–C, \"is not the last extreme of the circuit, but only the latter of the first two stages that comprise its circulation process.\" Its result is P; then P's function begins. It is only as the result of this , i.e. not as the result of the circulation process, that C' appears as the close of the circuit, in the same form as the original extreme C'. In M...M' and P...P, by contrast, the closing extremes M' and P are the direct results of the circulation process. Hence in form III the closing C' presupposes the existence of other commodities in the hands of others; the circuit's opening draws them in. From Individual to Total Social Capital This brings the chapter's pivotal theoretical leap. \"Precisely because the circuit C'...C' presupposes in its description the existence of another industrial capital in the form C (= L + mp) (and mp comprises other capitals of various kinds, e.g. machines, coal, oil, etc.), it itself demands to be considered not only as the general form of the circuit... but at the same time as the form of motion of the sum of individual capitals, i.e. of the total social capital of the capitalist class.\" The movement of any individual industrial capital appears simply as a partial one, \"intertwined with the others and conditioned by them.\" Marx gives a concrete instruction: if we consider the total annual commodity product of a country and analyze the movement in which one part replaces the productive capital of all individual businesses while another part goes into the individual consumption of the different classes, then we are considering C'...C' as a form of motion of both the social capital and the surplus-value or surplus product produced by it. He then states the crucial sum-relation and its limitation: \"The fact that the social capital is equal to the sum of the individual capitals (including joint-stock capital and also state capital, in so far as governments employ productive wage-labour in mines, railways, etc., and function as industrial capitalists), and that the total movement of the social capital is equal to the algebraic sum of the movements of the individual capitals, in no way prevents this motion, as the motion of an isolated individual capital, from displaying phenomena different from those displayed by the same motion, when it is viewed as a part of the total motion of the social capital.\" In the latter aspect, \"problems can be resolved whose solution must be presupposed in considering the circuit of a single individual capital, instead of resulting from the study of this.\" This is the fallacy-of-composition insight: aggregate phenomena are not merely sums of individual phenomena; the social level reveals regularities that the individual level must assume but cannot explain. The Unique Claim of C'...C' on Total Movement \"C'...C' is the only circuit in which the capital value originally advanced forms only a part of the extreme that opens the movement,\" and in which the movement thereby \"proclaims itself from the start as a total movement of industrial capital.\" This total movement is both of the part of the product that replaces the productive capital and of the part that forms surplus product—partly spent as revenue, partly serving as an element of accumulation. In so far as surplus-value is spent as revenue, individual consumption is involved . And this is also involved in so far as the starting-point C, the commodity, exists as some particular useful article: \"every capitalistically produced article is commodity capital, irrespective of whether its use form destines it for productive or individual consumption, or for both.\" Marx's comparative summary of the three circuits' announcements is definitive: M...M' indicates only the value aspect —the valorization of the advanced capital value as the purpose of the whole process. P...P (P') points to the production process of capital as a reproduction process , with productive capital remaining the same or growing (accumulation). C'...C' , already in its initial extreme, proclaims itself as a form of capitalist commodity production and comprises both productive and individual consumption from the start ; productive consumption and the valorization included in it \"appear simply as a branch of its movement.\" Finally, because C' can exist in a use form incapable of entering any further production process (consumption goods, for instance), it is apparent from the start that the various value components of C', expressed in portions of the product, must assume different positions depending on whether C'...C' is taken as the form of motion of the total social capital or as the independent movement of an individual industrial capital. \"In all these peculiarities, this circuit points beyond its own existence as the isolated circuit of a merely individual capital.\" The Methodological Reckoning: Why Forms I and II Were the Basis Marx states the payoff: in the figure C'...C', the movement of the commodity capital appears both as premise of the independent circuit of the individual capital and as conditioned by it in turn. Hence if this figure is conceived in its particularity, \"it is no longer sufficient to rest content with the fact that the metamorphoses C'–M' and M–C are on the one hand functionally determined sections of the metamorphosis of the capital, and on the other hand links in the general circulation of commodities.\" It becomes necessary to show how the metamorphoses of an individual capital are intertwined with those of other individual capitals and with the part of the total product destined for individual consumption. This is why Marx's analysis of the circuit of the individual industrial capital was \"primarily based on the first two forms.\" Forms I and II provided the simpler, methodologically manageable entry; form III is the portal to the more complex social totality. Agriculture, the Physiocrats, and the Historical Illustration Marx illustrates with agriculture , where accounts run from one harvest to the next. There, C'...C' does appear as the form of a single individual capital. Figure II proceeds from the sowing ; figure III from the harvest —or, in Physiocratic language, from avances and reprises (advances and returns). A footnote clarifies that the French Physiocratic writers of the 1750s and 60s—especially Quesnay and Turgot —were the first economists to begin to analyze production rather than merely circulation; they believed, however, that only agricultural labour was truly productive (a doctrine Marx examines in Theories of Surplus-Value , Part I, Chapter 11). The agricultural example yields a further point: \"In figure III the movement of the capital value appears from the start simply as a part of the movement of the general mass of products, while in figures I and II the movement of C' simply forms a moment in the movement of a single capital.\" Form III's Illusion and Its Corrective Marx issues a warning: \"In figure III the commodities on the market form the permanent premise of the process of production and reproduction. Hence if attention is fixed exclusively on this figure, all the elements of the production process seem to proceed from commodity circulation and to exist only as commodities. This one-sided conception overlooks the elements of the production process that are independent of the commodity elements. \" The very completeness of form III's social picture can breed an illusion—that everything is circulation—erasing the natural, technical, and material conditions of production that are not themselves commodities. Expanded Reproduction and the Material Conditions Because in C'...C' the total product (the total value) is the point of departure, it becomes evident that, \"leaving aside foreign trade, reproduction on an expanded scale, with productivity otherwise remaining the same, can take place only if the material elements of the additional productive capital are already contained in the part of the surplus product to be capitalized .\" That is, in so far as one year's production serves as precondition for the next, or in so far as production can occur together with simple reproduction within a year, surplus product is immediately produced in the form that enables it to function as additional capital. This is a fundamental condition of accumulation: the physical, material means for expanded reproduction must be produced as surplus product before they can be reinvested. Marx adds a crucial qualification regarding productivity: \"Increased productivity can increase only the material substance of capital, and cannot raise its value; but it still forms additional material for valorization.\" Productivity raises the quantity of use-values produced (the material substance) without directly raising value—yet this additional material is precisely what makes expanded valorization possible. Value and material wealth diverge. The Quesnay Conclusion The chapter closes: \"C'...C' is the basis of Quesnay's Tableau économique , and it shows great discernment on his part that he selected this form in opposition to M...M' (the form fixed on and isolated by the Mercantile System), and not P...P.\" Quesnay's choice of the harvest-based circuit, as opposed to the money-circuit of Mercantilism, was conceptually correct—the circuit of commodity capital is the appropriate form in which to depict the total annual product's movement and reproduction. Marx thereby situates his own achievement in relation to the first genuine attempt at a macroeconomic reproduction schema. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed First, the circuit-form is ideologically consequential. Each of the three circuits foregrounds a different moment and thereby occludes others. Form I (M...M') invites Mercantilism—money as the terminus. Form II (P...P') invites the classicist ideology of production \"as such,\" its \"naturalness of superficial rationality,\" erasing the specifically capitalist character of production. Form III (C'...C') announces the total movement, but, viewed one-sidedly, invites the illusion that everything originates in circulation. Marx's formal analysis is thus simultaneously an ideology-critique : the very shapes in which capital moves generate characteristic misrecognitions. Second, the surplus-value is present at the threshold of form III. Because C' opens as capital value + surplus-value, the circuit of commodity capital structurally includes consumption and the capital/revenue split. The distinction between the reproduction fund and the individual consumption fund is inscribed in the form itself. This is what makes form III the appropriate entry to the analysis of total social capital and the reproduction schemas. Third, interdependence is constitutive, not incidental. Form III presupposes other industrial capitals in the form C = L + mp. This is not an external complication but a defining feature: the circuit of any one capital literally embeds the circuits of others. The mutual presupposition of capitals (what is M–C for one is C'–M' for another) means the \"individual\" capital is an abstraction; the concrete reality is the total social capital, whose movement is the algebraic sum of individual movements while nevertheless exhibiting emergent properties the individual circuit cannot display. Fourth, productivity expands material substance but not value. Productivity cannot raise value yet supplies the additional material for valorization. Value and material wealth are conceptually distinct, and expanded reproduction at constant productivity requires the material elements of additional productive capital to be already contained in the surplus product to be capitalized. Broader Implications For economic methodology: The chapter enacts a movement from the abstract (individual capital) to the concrete (total social capital) by showing that the third form is structurally more complex and demands the social level. The fallacy-of-composition insight—that aggregate phenomena differ from isolated ones and that social analysis resolves problems the individual level must presuppose—is a foundational contribution to macroeconomics as a distinct level of analysis. For the theory of reproduction: The chapter is the threshold to Quesnay's Tableau économique , which Marx vindicates as the first genuine depiction of total social reproduction. By showing that C'...C'—the harvest-based, commodity-capital circuit—is the correct basis for the Tableau , Marx places his own reproduction schemas in a line of descent from the Physiocrats while correcting their fundamental error (that only agricultural labour is productive). For the critique of political economy: The chapter demonstrates that the systematic analysis of forms is not formalist pedantry but the key that unlocks both the ideological illusions and the social realities of capital. Each circuit-form corresponds to a theoretical tradition (Mercantilism, classicism, and the circulation-illusion), and the three-circuit analysis is at once a taxonomy of these traditions and a refutation of their one-sidedness. For practice: The agricultural analogy—reckoning from harvest to harvest—shows that form III is not merely analytic but experienced as the standpoint of any enterprise that begins with a finished total product and must divide it into replacement and surplus, capital and revenue, reproduction and consumption. In this sense, the circuit of commodity capital is both the most concrete form of capital's motion and the most immediately visible to the participants themselves—which is precisely why its surface can mislead. The essential lesson is this: capital is a totality in motion. Its three circuits are not three separate things but three aspects of one self-reproducing process, and only the circuit of commodity capital—because it opens with the whole valorized product and presupposes the whole world of other capitals—makes the total social character of that process visible. The chapter thus completes the individual-capital analysis and simultaneously opens the door to the study of the total social capital, its reproduction, and its crises. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 2 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 4"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 4", "title": "Chapter 4", "subtitle": "The Three Figures of the Circuit", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 4/Chapter 4 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 15545, "read_time": "78 min read", "tags": ["Circuits of Capital (M-C-M')"], "keywords": ["The Three Figures of the Circuit", "Metamorphoses of Industrial Capital", "Continuity of Movement and Simultaneity of Forms", "Capital as Process vs Capital as Thing", "Disruptions in Form Transitions"], "text": "Chapter 4 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 15,468 words ⏱️ ~77 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 4: The Three Figures of the Circuit — An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 4 is the culminating and unifying chapter of the first great subdivision of Volume 2 — the analysis of the metamorphoses of capital and their circuits . In the three preceding chapters Marx had dissected, one at a time and in abstract isolation, three separate circuits: that of money capital (M…M'), that of productive capital (P…P), and that of commodity capital (C'…C'). Each was treated as if a single capital, in its entirety, passed through one of these circuits alone. The task of Chapter 4 is to abolish that abstraction — to show that the three circuits are not three alternatives from which a capital may choose, nor three historical stages, but three figures of one and the same real movement , which must exist together if the movement is to be a movement at all. The chapter's famous summary sentence states the thesis exactly: \"Here, therefore, the entire circuit is the real unity of its three forms.\" The central problem the chapter resolves is the apparent contradiction between the interrupted , jump-like, exclusionary character of each individual phase of the circuit and the continuity that capitalist production requires \"by its technical basis, even if it is not always completely attainable.\" Considered in its pure immediacy, the circuit of capital is \"a constant process of interruption\": one stage is left behind, the next embarked upon; one form is cast aside and the capital exists in another; \"each of these stages not only conditions the other, but at the same time excludes it.\" If the total capital had to lie wholly in one form at a time, production would proceed \"only in spasms,\" like that of a Chinese handicraftsman working only for individual clients. The resolution is that the continuity of the whole is purchased by the division of the capital into parts that occupy different phases simultaneously: the industrial capital as a totality is \"simultaneously in all of its stages, and in the various functional forms corresponding to them.\" Continuity of the aggregate is achieved through the spatial simultaneity — the coexistence ( Nebeneinander ) — of parts whose temporal sequence ( Nacheinander ) converts coexistence back into a result of succession. The unity of the three circuits is thus not a static identity but a dialectical unity of opposites, produced and perpetually re-produced by a division of the capital into functionally distinct fractions. A second and equally fundamental thesis is that capital must be grasped \"as a movement, and not as a static thing.\" Capital is self-valorizing value , and therefore an abstraction that is real : \"Those who consider the autonomization [ Verselbständigung ] of value as a mere abstraction forget that the movement of industrial capital is this abstraction in action.\" Value passes through different forms, is preserved and increased in them, and is continually compared with itself across phases that are \"in no way 'contemporary,' but rather occur in succession.\" This is the theoretical stake of the excursus against Samuel Bailey, whose doctrine that \"value is a relation between contemporary commodities\" denies precisely the temporal self-identity that constitutes value as capital. Value revolutions, crises in which the \"independent value, acting with the force of an elemental natural process,\" overwhelms the foresight of individual capitalists, do not refute the autonomy of value — they confirm and intensify it. A third thesis concerns method. The \"simple intertwining of the metamorphoses\" that all commodity circulation has in common cannot explain the replacement of the components of total social capital. \"Up till now, mere phrases have been taken as sufficient in this respect\"; the analysis requires \"a different mode of investigation.\" The chapter thereby announces, from within, the limits of its own procedure and points forward to the turnover analysis (Part II) and, decisively, to the reproduction schemas of total social capital (Part III). The chapter closes with an appended fragment — the editor notes that the text up to a marked point derives from Manuscript V, while the remainder is \"a note found among extracts from books in a notebook of 1877 or 1878.\" This fragment states a fourth thesis at the level of the class as a whole: the industrial capitalist's demand is structurally and necessarily smaller than his supply, by exactly the surplus-value he produces. Demand = c + v; supply = c + v + s. The gap is valorization itself. Any assumption that the capitalist simply consumes his surplus-value and demands back what he supplies is \"equivalent to assuming the non-existence of capitalist production.\" This is the seed of the realization problem and of the necessity of credit. Situated at the hinge of Volume 2, the chapter converts the formal analysis of a single capital into the material premise for the analysis of total social reproduction — and, in doing so, provides the deepest Marxian statement of capital as a self-moving, self-positing, temporal subject. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Three Figures Set Out: Valorization as the Determining Purpose The chapter opens by rendering the three circuits as a single set of figures, behind which stands the notation Tc — \"the total circulation process.\" This symbol is a compression: it stands for the whole of the circulation phase, i.e., for the sequence C—M and M—C taken together (and their unity in the turnover of the capital), in contrast to P, the production phase. With Tc so defined, the three figures appear as: (I) M—C … P … C'—M' — the circuit of money capital, beginning and ending in the money form, with production as an interlude between two circulation acts. (II) P … Tc … P — the circuit of productive capital, beginning and ending in production, with the entire circulation process (both the sale of the product and the repurchase of the elements of production) as the interlude. (III) Tc … P (…C') — the circuit of commodity capital, beginning with value already valorized and emerging in circulation, passing through production, and closing with the newly valorized commodity (the page's OCR fragment \"(CO.\" is a corruption of the closing C'). (The mangled formula \"(II) a II)\" appearing later on page 14 is likewise a manuscript/page-break artefact; the argument is unaffected at the conceptual level, and I treat it as such.) Marx immediately draws out what is common to all three. First: \"the valorization of value as the determining purpose, the driving motive.\" This is the definitional core. Industrial capital, in the strict sense Marx uses throughout Volume 2, is capital functionally engaged in the production sphere — manufacturing, agriculture, mining, transport, etc. — as distinct from merchant's capital and interest-bearing capital, which are derived, secondary forms presupposing it. The chapter's point is that what unifies three otherwise heterogeneous sequences is not their shape but their telos : M' > M, P producing P' with expanded value, C' closing on C'' > C'. Second, and with a fine discrimination of how the telos is expressed : in Figure I the purpose is expressed \"actually … in the form\" — the form itself, M—M', wears its drive visibly, since the first and last terms are money and the arrow between them is the whole secret. In Figure II, the form begins with P, \"the valorization process itself\" — value-creation stands openly at the head of the circuit, but the quantitative increase is no longer visible in the termini (P…P looks like the same P returning). In Figure III, the circuit \"begins with the valorized value, and closes with the newly valorized value, even when the movement is repeated on the same scale .\" This last clause is important: even in simple reproduction — where the newly valorized value is merely consumed as revenue and the same capital sum is re-advanced — the C'…C' circuit still begins and ends with valorized value, and therefore still objectively exhibits surplus-value production as its point of departure and return. The form displays the result of valorization even where it does not display accumulation. 2.2 The Rotating Orbit: Why Circularity Requires Continuity The chapter then invokes Volume 1, Chapter 3, on the general circulation of commodities. Since C—M for the seller is M—C for the buyer and vice versa, the circulation of capital, considered merely formally, \"simply displays the general metamorphosis of commodities,\" and the laws governing the quantity of money in circulation apply to it. But Marx at once restricts this concession: \"if we do not just dwell on this formal aspect … but consider the real connection between the metamorphoses of the various individual capitals, in fact the connection between the circuits of individual capitals as partial movements of the reproduction process of the total social capital,\" then the process \"cannot be explained in terms of the simple change of form between money and commodity.\" The concession will be taken back — rigorously and by construction — in §2.19 below. The image that organizes the next argument is that of a rotating orbit: \"In a constantly rotating orbit, every point is simultaneously a starting-point and a point of return. If we interrupt the rotation, then not every starting-point is a point of return.\" This is more than rhetoric. In a completed circle, the beginning and the end are analytically indifferent — any point can be treated as a starting point because the motion is closed. The three circuits, in their perfect realization, therefore appear interchangeable, and \"the entire distinction presents itself as merely one of form, a merely subjective distinction that exists only for the observer.\" But this appearance is produced precisely by assuming completion — by assuming the rotation never stops. In reality, each individual industrial capital is involved in all three circuits at the same time : \"One part of the capital value, for example, which for the moment functions as commodity capital, is transformed into money capital, while at the same time another part passes out of the production process into circulation as new commodity capital. Thus the circular form of C'…C' is constantly described, and the same is the case with the two other forms.\" Hence the conclusion: \"the entire circuit is the real unity of its three forms.\" 2.3 The Abstract Premise of the Whole Capital in One Form and the Resulting \"Spasms\" of Production Marx now makes explicit the assumption under which the earlier chapters proceeded, in order to expose what it costs. We assumed \"that the capital value appears either as money capital, productive capital or commodity capital to the full extent of its magnitude.\" In his running example, £422 exists first entirely as money capital, then is transformed entirely into productive capital, then exists as commodity capital to the value of £500 — of which £78 is surplus-value. (The arithmetic of the example: 10,000 lb. of yarn at £500 implies 1 shilling per lb.; 8,440 lb. × 1s. = £422, the capital value to be replaced, and 1,560 lb. × 1s. = £78, the surplus product borne by the same commodity mass. Rate of surplus-value = 78/78 = 100%, consistent with the Volume 2 running example in which v = 78.) Given that premise, \"the various stages here constitute an equal number of interruptions.\" While the £422 persists in the money form, the total capital functions only as money capital; once transformed into productive capital, it functions neither as money nor as commodity capital; its circulation is interrupted for the whole duration of production, exactly as its production is interrupted for the whole duration of circulation. The circuit P…P would then present itself \"not only as a periodic renewal of the productive capital, but equally as an interruption in its function.\" Production would be carried on \"only in spasms,\" its rhythm dictated by \"periods of time of accidental duration,\" according to whether the two circulation stages proceeded faster or more slowly. Marx's illustration is the Chinese handicraftsman who \"works only for individual clients, and whose production process comes to a halt between one order and the next.\" This figure is the negative image of capitalist production: production whose continuity depends on a market already made, not on a market yet to be found. The point is generalized: \"This is in fact true for each individual portion of capital in motion, and all portions of the capital go through this movement in succession.\" Take 10,000 lb. of yarn as one week's output of a spinning-mill. That mass moves in its entirety from production into circulation; the capital value in it must be entirely converted into money; and as long as it persists in money form it cannot re-enter production — it must first be transformed back into the elements of productive capital, L (labour-power) and mp (means of production). Hence: \"The circuit of capital is a constant process of interruption; one stage is left behind, the next stage embarked upon; one form is cast aside, and the capital exists in another; each of these stages not only conditions the other, but at the same time excludes it.\" The dialectic compressed into that sentence — mutual conditioning alongside mutual exclusion — is the engine of the entire chapter. 2.4 The Real Continuity: Succession and Coexistence Against this, Marx sets the technical requirement: \"continuity is the characteristic feature of capitalist production, and is required by its technical basis, even if it is not always completely attainable.\" He then asks \"how things proceed in reality,\" and answers with a description of overlapping phases. While the 10,000 lb. of yarn steps onto the market as commodity capital and undergoes its transformation into money — \"whether as means of payment, means of purchase or simply money of account\" (a reminder that the metamorphosis may be legally complete before money physically moves) — new cotton, coal, etc. come into the production process in its place. These have already been transformed back from both money form and commodity form into productive capital and begun to function as such. Simultaneously, while the first 10,000 lb. is being converted into money, a previous 10,000 lb. is already describing the second stage of its circulation, being reconverted from money into the elements of production. Hence: \"industrial capital in the continuity of its circuit is simultaneously in all of its stages, and in the various functional forms corresponding to them.\" The vivid statement of this simultaneity: \"Money is given out with one hand and taken in with the other; what is at one point the commencement of the circuit M…M' is simultaneously at another point its conclusion. The same applies for the productive capital.\" The definitional conclusion follows: \"The real circuit of industrial capital in its continuity is therefore not only a unified process of circulation and production, but also a unity of all its three circuits.\" And this unity is conditional on the division of the capital: it can be such a unity only in so far as \"each different part of the capital runs in succession through the successive phases of the circuit, can pass over from one phase and one functional form into the other; hence industrial capital, as the whole of these parts, exists simultaneously in its various phases and functions, and thus describes all three circuits at once.\" 2.5 Coexistence as Result and as Condition — The Dialectic of Nacheinander / Nebeneinander The theoretical pivot of the chapter is stated in one sentence: \"The succession [ Nacheinander ] of the various parts is here determined by their coexistence [ Nebeneinander ], i.e. by the way in which the capital is divided.\" The German terms matter, because the whole argument is about a specific form of the relation between time and space in the movement of value. Nacheinander — one-after-another — is the temporal form of the individual portion, which can only be in one phase at a time. Nebeneinander — side-by-side — is the spatial form of the aggregate, whose parts are distributed across the phases. The continuity of the whole is thus a spatialization of time: simultaneity in the circulation process is achieved by dividing the capital into fractions whose phases differ. Marx reinforces this with an analogy from the factory system: in developed manufacture and machinery production, \"the product is continuously at the various stages of its formation, and in transition from one phase of production to another\" — the same logic of overlapping partial processes. Then he specifies the quantitative precondition. Each individual industrial capital \"has a definite size, which is dependent on the means of the capitalist and has a definite minimum for each branch of industry,\" so \"definite numerical ratios must obtain in its division into parts.\" The size of the capital determines the scale of the production process; this determines the volume of commodity capital and of money capital that must function alongside the production process. But — and here is the crucial dialectical inversion — \"The coexistence which determines the continuity of production, however, exists only through the movement in which the portions of capital successively describe the various stages. The coexistence is itself only the result of the succession.\" Coexistence is both condition and result : as a given division of capital, it makes continuity possible; as a produced distribution, it is nothing but the standing precipitate of an ongoing succession. There is no static equilibrium image here. The \"coexistence\" is a mode of motion, not a state. 2.6 Determination of the Proportions and the Breakdown Mechanism (C'—M' Interrupted) From this follows a theory of disturbance. \"If C'—M' comes to a halt in the case of one portion, for example, if the commodity is unsaleable, then the circuit of this part is interrupted and its replacement by its means of production is not accomplished; the successive parts that emerge from the production process as C' find their change of function barred by their predecessors. If this continues for some time, production is restricted and the whole process brought to a standstill.\" This is the general mechanism of blockage, and it operates strictly within the circuit itself — independent of any theory of crisis rooted in distribution or in the falling rate of profit. The metaphor is of a pipeline: each arriving unit is blocked by the unremoved unit ahead of it, so a local stoppage in circulation converts directly into a general stoppage of production. The formal statement: \"Every delay in the succession brings the coexistence into disarray, every delay in one stage causes a greater or lesser delay in the entire circuit, not only that of the portion of the capital that is delayed, but also that of the entire individual capital.\" The emphasis on unsaleability (rather than on insufficient profit) is significant, and it connects to the chapter's later treatment of value revolutions: the circuit's vulnerability lies in the form -determined necessity of sale, i.e., in the fact that use-value must be validated as value on the market before the capital can resume its productive form. The chapter does not develop a crisis theory here — that belongs elsewhere — but it provides the mechanism on which any such theory must rest: the unity of the circuits is real, and therefore the interruption of one circuit propagates through all the others. 2.7 Immediacy vs. Totality: The Fluid Forms and the Unity of the Three Circuits Marx now returns to the level of appearance. \"The immediate form in which the process presents itself is that of a succession of phases, so that the transition of the capital into a new phase is determined by its abandonment of the previous one.\" This is the phenomenal form — the Erscheinungsform — and it is partial and misleading in the familiar Marxian way: it presents the circuit as a linear sequence, one phase following another, and each phase as an independent starting and returning point. Hence, \"every particular circuit has one of the functional forms of the capital as its starting-point and point of return.\" Against this immediate representation stands the totality: \"the total process is in fact the unity of the three circuits, which are the different forms in which the continuity of the process is expressed.\" Each figure thus appears as an autonomous specific circuit, but each conditions the continuity of the whole: \"the circular course of one functional form determines that of the others.\" For the total social capital, continuity of production means that the process is simultaneously a process of reproduction, \"and hence the circuit of each of its moments.\" Different fractions pass through different stages and forms; each functional form therefore \"passes through its circuit simultaneously with the others, though it is always a different part of the capital that presents itself in it.\" Marx enumerates: a part of the capital exists as commodity capital being transformed into money — \"but this is an ever-changing part, and is constantly being reproduced\"; another part exists as money capital being transformed into productive capital; a third as productive capital being transformed into commodity capital. \"The constant presence of all three forms is mediated by the circuit of the total capital through precisely these three phases.\" The chapter's culminating formulation is a hybrid of spatial and temporal determination: \"As a whole, then, the capital is simultaneously present, and spatially coexistent, in its various phases. But each part is constantly passing from one phase or functional form into another, and thus functions in all of them in turn. The forms are therefore fluid forms, and their simultaneity is mediated by their succession. \" And further: \"Each form both follows and precedes the others, so that the return of one part of the capital to one form is determined by the return of another part to another form.\" Continuity is thus not a property of any moment but of the relation among moments; and it is only \"in the unity of the three circuits that the continuity of the overall process is realized, in place of the interruption we have just delineated.\" Finally, a distinction between individual and total capital is drawn that will govern the rest of Volume 2: \"The total social capital always possesses this continuity, and its process always contains the unity of the three circuits.\" 2.8 The Degree of Interruption: Individual Capitals, Branches, Seasons The chapter immediately qualifies the claim for individual capitals: \"For individual capitals, the continuity of reproduction is at certain points interrupted, to a greater or lesser degree.\" Three sources of interruption are distinguished. First , quantitative disproportion: \"the quantities of value are frequently distributed amongst the various stages and functional forms in unequal portions, at different times.\" Second , technically determined division: the portions are differently divided \"according to the character of the commodity which has to be produced, thus according to the particular sphere of production in which the capital has been invested\" — the length of the production period and the nature of the labour process set upper bounds on how finely a capital can be split. Third , seasonality, whether from \"natural conditions (agriculture, fishing for herrings, etc.)\" or \"as a matter of convention as is the case with so-called seasonal work.\" Marx adds a telling observation: \"It is in the factory and in mining that the process occurs most regularly and uniformly.\" The factory — the site of the mechanical and the regular — approximates the ideal form of the circuit; agriculture, the most seasonal, deviates most. But the crucial methodological conclusion is a limit on the variability: \"this difference between branches of production does not give rise to any difference in the general forms of the circuit.\" The analysis concerns the forms; variation in continuity is a difference of degree and not of form. 2.9 Capital as Movement: The Autonomization of Value ( Verselbständigung ) Marx now elevates the analysis to the level of concept. \"Capital, as self-valorizing value, does not just comprise class relations, a definite social character that depends on the existence of labour as wage-labour. It is a movement, a circulatory process through different stages, which itself in turn includes three different forms of the circulatory process. Hence it can only be grasped as a movement, and not as a static thing.\" This is the ontological claim of the chapter. Capital is not a stock of things (machines, stocks, cash) but a process ; the things are merely the temporal forms of that process. And the process is the process of value , which is why Marx calls it an abstraction: \"Those who consider the autonomization [ Verselbständigung ] of value as a mere abstraction forget that the movement of industrial capital is this abstraction in action.\" This phrase — die Verselbständigung des Werts — is one of the strongest formulations in Marx's corpus for what capital is : an abstract social relation that has acquired an objective, quasi-natural existence of its own and governs the activity of those subject to it. This is the same conceptual object as the \"automatic subject\" ( automatisches Subjekt ) of Volume 1's account of self-expanding value; here it is presented dynamically, as movement. \"Here value passes through different forms, different movements in which it is both preserved and increases, is valorized.\" Marx then acknowledges what he has bracketed: \"Since we are firstly dealing here simply with the forms of movement, we have not considered the revolutions that the capital value may suffer in its circulatory process.\" But even so, he states the constraint: \"capitalist production can exist and continue to exist only so long as the capital value is valorized, i.e. describes its circuit as value that has become independent, and therefore so long as the revolutions in value are somehow or other mastered and balanced out.\" The category \"value become independent\" is not a metaphysical ornament but an operative condition of the system's reproduction. The subjective mediation is then introduced. \"The movements of capital appear as actions of the individual industrial capitalist in so far as he functions as buyer of commodities and labour, seller of commodities and productive capitalist, and thus mediates the circuit by his own activity.\" The capitalist is thus the personification of the movement — the agent through whom the abstract process is executed, and who appears to be its author. But the appearance is inverted by the reality: \"If the social capital value suffers a revolution in value, it can come about that his individual capital succumbs to this and is destroyed, because it cannot meet the conditions of this movement of value. The more acute and frequent these revolutions in value become, the more the movement of the independent value, acting with the force of an elemental natural process, prevails over the foresight and calculation of the individual capitalist, the more the course of normal production is subject to abnormal speculation, and the greater becomes the danger to the existence of the individual capitals.\" 2.10 Value Revolutions as Confirmation of the Autonomy of Value The dialectical punchline of the section is a textbook instance of Marx's method of demonstrating essence through appearance: \"These periodic revolutions in value thus confirm what they ostensibly refute: the independence which value acquires as capital, and which is maintained and intensified through its movement.\" The \"ostensibly refutes\" clause captures the spontaneous verdict: crises in value seem to show that value is fragile, contingent, at the mercy of markets — that there is no stability in value, hence no \"autonomy.\" Marx argues the contrary: it is precisely because value is an independent, self-moving movement that it can crash through the plans of individual capitals \"with the force of an elemental natural process.\" That the system fails in the individual case is proof that it is a system, i.e., that there is a logic not reducible to the intentions of its agents. Value's independence is demonstrated by the damage it does. Marx then specifies the theoretical content that makes self-comparison possible: \"This sequence of metamorphoses of capital in process implies the continuous comparison of the change in value brought about in the circuit with the original value of the capital.\" Capital, as movement, is a continuous act of bookkeeping — value compares itself not with an external standard but with its own past magnitude. And he warns that this comparison is not the same as the relation between capital and labour: \"The independence of value in relation to the value-forming power, labour-power, is introduced by the act M—L (purchase of labour-power), and is realized during the production process as exploitation of labour-power. But this independence does not reappear in the circuit in which money, commodity and elements of production are only alternating forms of the capital value in process, and in which the past magnitude of the value is compared with the present, changed value of the capital.\" The relation to labour-power — the social ground of value's power — is instituted at the moment of purchase and realized in exploitation; but it does not appear within the circuit as such, where all that is visible is value comparing itself with itself. This is the circuit-level form of fetishism: the relation to labour disappears from the phenomenal form of the movement. 2.11 The Bailey Polemic: Value, Time, and Self-Comparison It is against this background that Marx mounts an excursus on Samuel Bailey. He quotes Bailey: \"'Value,' says Bailey, opposing the autonomization of value which characterizes the capitalist mode of production, and which he treats as the illusion of certain economists, 'value is a relation between contemporary commodities, because such only admit of being exchanged with each other.'\" Bailey's thesis — a forerunner of the numerous subjectivist and \"pure relationist\" theories of value — is that value cannot be a property of a commodity as such, since a commodity can only be exchanged with another at the same time ; hence value is the ratio of exchange between commodities that actually confront each other at the same moment on the market. Marx's reply unfolds in three moves. First, a reduction : Bailey's thesis, applied to intertemporal comparison, means \"a comparison between the expenditure of labour required in different epochs for the production of the same kind of commodities,\" a comparison which is perfectly admissible if the value of money is taken as fixed. Bailey's prohibition on comparing values across time therefore has substantive consequences he has not grasped: it would abolish the very possibility of measuring the productivity of labour historically. Second, a diagnosis : Bailey's error \"derives from his general misunderstanding, according to which exchange-value equals value, the form of value is value itself.\" This is a version of the charge Marx levelled elsewhere at the \"vulgar economists\": mistaking the form for the substance. If the form of value is value itself, then when commodities are not currently being exchanged they have no value, and no intertemporal comparison is possible. Third, the properly Marxian point : \"He does not in the least suspect, therefore, that value functions as capital value or capital only in so far as it remains identical with itself and is compared with itself in the different phases of its circuit, which are in no way 'contemporary,' but rather occur in succession.\" The categories \"capital\" and \"capital value\" are therefore conceptually inseparable from temporal identity: value becomes capital only by maintaining its identity across a temporal succession of forms. Bailey's \"contemporaneity\" is not merely an error, it is a category mistake about what capital is. The editorial footnote appended to the quotation supplies a nuanced assessment: Bailey's A Critical Dissertation on the Nature, Measures, and Causes of Value (London, 1825), was written \"in reference to the writings of Mr Ricardo and his followers,\" and although Bailey was \"a vulgar economist who held value to be merely relative,\" he \"did expose certain contradictions in the Ricardian theory,\" and the reader is directed to Theories of Surplus-Value , Part III, Chapter XX. Marx's stance toward Bailey is thus double-edged: Bailey's critique of Ricardo's attempts to find an \"absolute\" measure of value that is not labour is deflationary but partially correct; yet in rejecting the temporality of value he destroys the very concept of value and hence of capital. This is a case where Marx uses a critic of classical political economy against that critic's own general framework, while subsuming both under his own dialectical account. 2.12 The Pure Circuit Assumption: Value Revolutions inside the Circuit (M…M' vs. P…P and C'…C') Having established the autonomy of value, Marx turns to its limits under assumptions of constancy. \"In order to consider the formula of the circuit in its pure state, it is not sufficient to assume that commodities are sold at their values; this must also take place in circumstances that in other respects, too, remain the same.\" For the form P…P, in particular, three sources of disturbance must be bracketed: (a) technical revolutions in the production process that may devalue the productive capital of a particular capitalist; (b) any repercussion of a change in the value of the elements of productive capital on the value of an existing commodity stock (which \"may rise or fall if there is a stock of this on hand\"); (c) — implied by the general argument — movements of prices that merely reflect market fluctuations rather than changes in value. The method here is worth naming: Marx is deploying a ceteris paribus assumption not as a methodological convenience but as a controlled abstraction, in order to isolate a structural difference between the three circuits. Take C' = 10,000 lb. of yarn, sold at its value of £500. Of this, 8,440 lb. = £422 \"replaces the capital value contained in it\" — the return to capital. But if the value of cotton, coal, etc. rises, then this £422 \"may not be sufficient to replace completely the elements of the productive capital; additional money capital is then necessary, i.e. money capital is tied up.\" Conversely, if these prices fall, money capital is set free. Hence the general condition: \"The process takes place quite normally only if value relations remain constant; in practice it runs its course as long as disturbances in the repetition of the circuit balance each other out; the greater the disturbances, the greater the money capital that the industrial capitalist must possess in order to ride out the period of readjustment.\" This yields a structural consequence of the first rank, which the chapter flags and then leaves for later development: since \"the scale of each individual production process grows with the progress of capitalist production, and with it the minimum size of the capital to be advanced, this circumstance is added to the other circumstances which increasingly turn the function of industrial capitalist into a monopoly of large-scale money capitalists, either individual or associated.\" The sheer cushion of money capital required to survive value disturbances generates a tendency to concentration. This is a tendency grounded not in competition or technology alone, but in the monetary form of the circuit itself — a distinctive contribution of this chapter. Marx then draws a formal distinction that the opening of the chapter had apparently denied. \"We may remark here, in passing, that when there is a change in the value of the elements of production, a distinction arises between the form M…M' on the one hand, and the forms P…P and C'…C' on the other.\" This is the re-emergence of a real distinction among the circuits, after the earlier demonstration that their difference is \"merely one of form.\" In M…M' , taken as the formula for newly invested capital — capital first appearing as money capital — a fall in the value of means of production (raw materials, ancillaries, etc.) means that a smaller outlay of money capital is required to open a business of a given size. The reasoning: \"given that the level of the productive forces remains the same, the scale of the production process depends only on the volume and scale of the means of production that a given quantity of labour-power can cope with, and not on the value of those means of production, or on that of the labour-power (the latter simply has an effect on the magnitude of the valorization).\" The physical-technical scale of production is determined by the material volume of means of production per worker; their value is not what sets the scale. So a fall in their value reduces the money needed to start; a rise increases it. In both cases \"it is only the amount of the money capital to be newly invested that is affected\": in the first, some money capital becomes superfluous; in the second, more is tied up — \"provided that the rate of increase of a new individual industrial capital proceeds as is usual in the given branch of production.\" The crucial contrast is that this applies only to new investment. A capital already in operation is not in its first circuit but in reproduction. 2.13 The Three Cases of a Fall in Value and the Opposite Cases of a Rise Marx now elaborates why P…P and C'…C' \"behave in the same way as M…M' only in so far as the movement of P and C' is at the same time accumulation, i.e. in so far as excess m, money, is transformed into money capital.\" Otherwise, they are affected differently by a change in the value of the elements of productive capital, \"since here it is not the original outlay that is directly affected, but rather an industrial capital involved not in its first circuit but in its process of reproduction, i.e. C'…CC(mp), the conversion of commodity capital back into its elements of production, in so far as these consist of commodities.\" For a fall in value (or price) of replacement elements, three possibilities: Continue reproduction on the same scale. A part of the former money capital is set free and stored up (hoarded) — but crucially, this hoarding is not the result of real accumulation or of the \"preliminary and accompanying transformation of m (surplus-value) into an accumulation fund.\" In other words, the money capital set free is a release of existing capital , not the metamorphosis of surplus-value. This is a technical point of great importance for the analysis of money hoards: hoards can arise not only from accumulation of surplus-value but from value-release within reproduction, and the two are functionally different. Expand reproduction to a larger scale than otherwise, if the technical proportions permit it — thus binding the release to the material conditions of production (the physical means of production must be available in the right proportions). Build up a larger reserve of raw materials, etc. — a hoarding in kind . For a rise in the value of replacement elements, the mirror-image alternatives are: Reproduction no longer on its normal scale — \"e.g. working hours may be cut.\" (Note the assumption that the length of the working day is adjustable, and that its shortening is the capitalist's way of absorbing the rise without additional outlay.) Additional money capital must be injected to continue at the former scale — \"money capital is tied up.\" The monetary accumulation fund, where there is one, must serve in whole or part to pursue reproduction on the old scale instead of expanding it. Here Marx draws a distinction with important consequences for his later theory of credit: \"This also involves the tying up of money capital, although here the additional money capital does not come from an external source, from the money market, but rather from the resources of the industrial capitalist himself.\" The distinction between money capital drawn from the banker and money capital drawn from one's own hoard is the germ of the distinction between credit and self-financing on which much of the analysis of capital circulation depends. The chapter is explicit that this is left undeveloped, and it reiterates: \"What interests us here is simply the real distinction which emerges between M…M' and the two other forms of the circuit with respect to changes in value of the elements of productive capital.\" 2.14 Modifying Circumstances: Stocks of Raw Material and Finished Goods; the Timing of C'—M' Material stocks modify the general case, and Marx supplies a rich set of illustrations. If the cotton spinner holds a large reserve of raw cotton — \"i.e. a large part of his productive capital is in the form of a cotton stock\" — then a part of his productive capital is devalued by a fall in cotton prices (and revalued by a rise). If, on the other hand, he has large quantities tied up as commodity capital, e.g. in the form of yarn, a fall in cotton prices devalues a part of his commodity capital, and thus a part of his overall capital in the circuit. The general result: a change in the value of the elements of production is not contained at the point of purchase but propagates through whatever form the capital currently inhabits. This is a subtlety that the abstract formulas conceal, and it shows that the \"unity of the three circuits\" has an existential edge for the individual capital: every form is a risk, so to speak. The most precise case is C'—M—C(mp) . Here the timing of the value change relative to the sale matters decisively. If C'—M , the realization of commodity capital, has taken place before the change in value of the elements of C, the capital is affected only in the second act of circulation M—C(mp) ; the sale has already been transacted at the old values, and the capital only confronts the change when it repurchases. But if the value change occurs before the completion of C'—M , then — \"with other circumstances remaining the same\" — a fall in the price of cotton leads to a corresponding fall in the price of yarn, and a rise in cotton leads to a rise in yarn. (This is the mechanism that makes the value of the finished commodity follow the value of its constituents, where the sale has not yet been concluded at the old values.) Hence: \"The effect on the various individual capitals invested in the same branch of production can be very different according to the different circumstances in which they are found.\" Two capitals of equal size in the same branch, holding different proportions of stocks and different positions in their circuits, are affected quite differently by the same value change. The \"same\" movement of total value thus has heterogeneous, position-dependent effects on individual capitals — a result that anticipates the differential-advantage analysis of Volume 3. Marx closes the section by deferring a related phenomenon: \"Money capital may also be set free or tied up as the result of differences in the duration of the circulation process, i.e. in the speed of circulation. This however belongs to the discussion of turnover.\" This is a deliberate forward-reference to Part II of Volume 2 — the doctrine of turnover, in which the release and tying-up of money capital through variations in circulation time becomes a central mechanism. It also marks the boundary of what Chapter 4 can establish. 2.15 The World Market and the Many-Sided Origins of Circulation Marx now widens the frame from the individual capital to the social forms that enter its circuit. In the act M—C(mp) , \"in the epoch when the capitalist mode of production is already developed, and hence dominant,\" a large part of the commodities composing the means of production are themselves the functioning commodity capital of others; from the seller's standpoint the same act is C'—M' , the transformation of commodity capital into money capital. \"But this does not hold good absolutely. On the contrary.\" The circuit of industrial capital \"cuts across the commodity circulation of the most varied modes of social production, in so far as this commodity circulation simultaneously reflects commodity production.\" Marx enumerates the possible provenances of the commodities confronting industrial capital: slave-based production; peasant production (Chinese, Indian ryots); community production (Dutch East Indies); state production based on serfdom (as in earlier epochs of Russian history); the products of \"half-savage hunting peoples,\" etc. Whatever their origin, \"as commodities and money they confront the money and commodities in which industrial capital presents itself,\" and enter both the latter's circuit and that of the surplus-value borne by the commodity capital, in so far as the latter is spent as revenue — i.e., both branches of the circulation of commodity capital (capital replacement and revenue expenditure). \"The character of the production process from which they derive is immaterial; they function on the market as commodities.\" The consequence is a characterization of the capital circuit's material openness: \"the circulation process of industrial capital is characterized by the many-sided character of its origins, and the existence of the market as a world market.\" Then the corresponding point about money: \"What holds for foreign commodities holds also for foreign money; as commodity capital functions in relation to money simply as commodity, so this money functions towards commodity capital simply as money; the money functions here as world money.\" This anticipates the analysis of money in its world-money function (the third of the functions analysed in A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy and Volume 1, Chapter 3), and it grounds the subsequent assumption of metallic money. Two further points complete this section. First , on the obliteration of provenance : \"As soon as the act M—mp is completed, the commodities (mp) cease to be commodities and become one of the modes of existence of industrial capital in its functional form P, productive capital. Their provenance is therefore obliterated; they now exist simply as forms of existence of industrial capital, and are incorporated into it.\" Yet their replacement requires their reproduction, so \"the capitalist mode of production is conditioned by modes of production lying outside its own stage of development.\" And here Marx states the tendency : \"Its tendency, however, is to transform all possible production into commodity production; the main means by which it does this is precisely by drawing this production into its circulation process; and developed commodity production is itself capitalist commodity production. The intervention of industrial capital everywhere promotes this transformation, and with it too the transformation of all immediate producers into wage-labourers.\" This is one of the chapter's most substantial historical propositions: the unity of the three circuits is not merely an analytic form of the developed capital; it is an engine of subsumption , drawing non-capitalist production into itself and converting direct producers into wage-labourers. Second , on the social form of the confronting commodities : whatever their origin, the commodities entering the circulation process of industrial capital — \"and these include the necessary means of subsistence into which variable capital is transformed after being paid to the workers so that they can reproduce their labour-power\" — confront industrial capital \"straight away in its form of commodity capital, they themselves having the form of commodity-dealing or merchant's capital; and this by its very nature embraces commodities from all modes of production.\" The parenthetical remark on variable capital is worth noting: the wage purchased by the worker is itself, in aggregate, a mass of commodities drawn from the circuit of commodity capital — the reproduction of labour-power goes through the market in means of subsistence, which is one reason the circuit of the social capital must include the department producing them (a step developed much later in the reproduction schemas). From this follows the argument on scale of sale : \"As the capitalist mode of production presupposes production on a large scale, so it also necessarily presupposes large-scale sale; sale to the merchant, not to the individual consumer.\" Where the consumer is himself a productive consumer — an industrial capitalist in another branch — there is direct sale \"in the form of orders, etc.\" Each industrial capitalist is a direct seller in so far as he is his own merchant — which he also is, moreover, when he sells to a merchant. 2.16 Merchant's Capital Presupposed: Sismondi's Naive Account \"Commodity trade is presupposed, as a function of merchant's capital, and this develops ever further with the development of capitalist production.\" Marx then makes the crucial methodological stipulation: although in illustrating particular aspects we occasionally take the existence of merchant's capital for granted, \"in this general analysis we assume direct sale without the intervention of the merchant, since this intervention conceals various moments of the movement.\" This is a statement about abstraction in political economy, and it is worth unpacking. The merchant's capital is not an inessential excrescence: it is a real functional specialization of total social capital. But precisely because its intervention interposes itself between the act of sale and the realization of the industrial capital, it obscures the temporal structure of the industrial circuit — the gap between the closure of C'—M' and the onward expenditure of the money, the independence of sale from the identity of the buyer, and the phases of the money capital itself. The general analysis must first be carried out without this intervention in order to fix the forms; the later analysis (Part II and Part III, and Volume 3's account of the division of profit between merchant and industrial capital) can then examine the intervention itself. Marx then quotes Sismondi \"who presents the matter rather naively.\" Sismondi's account: commerce employs a considerable capital which \"appears at first glance not to form part of that whose course we have charted.\" The value of cloth accumulated in the draper's stores seems completely different from the wage paid by the rich man to the poor man. But \"this capital has simply replaced that of which we have been speaking.\" Sismondi's illustration: if 100,000 crowns sufficed for the whole circulation between manufacturer and consumer, this would be shared equally among manufacturer, wholesale merchant and retailer. The manufacturer, receiving only a third of the total, \"does the same work as he did when he received the whole lot, because the moment its manufacture is completed, he finds the merchant to buy it much sooner than he would have found the consumer.\" The wholesaler's capital is replaced by that of the retailer sooner. \"The difference between the sums advanced in wages and the purchase price for the final consumer forms the profit on the capitals. It is divided between the manufacturer, the wholesaler and the retailer, after they have divided their functions between them, and the task accomplished is the same, even though it has employed three persons and three fractions of capital in place of one.\" And finally: \"All these\" — the merchants — \"indirectly participated in production; for as the aim of production is consumption, it cannot be considered accomplished until it has placed the object produced at the disposal of the consumer.\" Why \"naive\"? Several points are approximated correctly: Sismondi sees that the merchant's capital does not add value but replaces a part of the productive capital that would otherwise have to function in the circulation phase; that the merchants \"indirectly participated in production\"; that the various fractions of capital divide the surplus produced at the point of production (a distribution, not a creation of profit). Against this, the naivety is compounded of three things. First, the account is teleological in a consumptionist key: \"the aim of production is consumption\" — a proposition Marx has already repudiated in the chapter's own appended note, where he insists the driving motive is \"enrichment itself\" and not enjoyment. Second, the phrase \"the difference between the sums advanced in wages and the purchase price for the final consumer forms the profit on the capitals\" is dangerously close to the error of deriving profit from the difference between purchase and sale (the very error Marx's whole theory of surplus-value is designed to refute), and its dependence on \"the final consumer\" obscures the production relation in which surplus-value is constituted. Third, the \"division\" of functions among manufacturer, wholesaler and retailer is presented as a costless technical improvement; Marx's own analysis of the \"many-sided origins\" of circulation implies that the intervention of merchant's capital is not merely an optimization but a form of alienation within the circuit — the specialized existence of a function (realization) separated from the capital that creates the value to be realized. The quote from Sismondi at the close of the section is thus structurally important: it shows what an honest pre-critical account of merchant's capital can see and where it must stop. 2.17 The Money Assumption: Metallic Money, Means of Purchase, Means of Payment The chapter now states an assumption that governs the remainder of Volume 2 and states the reasons for it: \"In considering the general forms of the circuit, and throughout this second volume in general, we take money to be metal money, excluding symbolic money, mere tokens of value which are specific to particular countries, as well as credit money, which we have not yet developed.\" Two justifications are offered. First, historical : \"this is the course taken by history: credit money played no role, or at least not a significant one, in the early period of capitalist production.\" Second, theoretical : \"the necessity of this course can be proved theoretically, in so far as everything critical that has so far been said about the circulation of credit money by Tooke and others compelled them time and again to look back at how the matter would present itself on the basis of mere metallic circulation.\" This is a striking methodological remark, and it deserves attention. Thomas Tooke — a leading figure of the Banking School whose History of Prices Marx studied closely and used in Theories of Surplus-Value — was forced, whenever he had to justify claims about the circulation of credit money, to reduce his analysis to the case of metallic circulation. For Marx this is not a weakness to be corrected but a structural feature of the object: credit money is a derivative form whose intelligibility depends on the prior analysis of the metallic form. Hence the pure case must be analysed first, not because credit is unimportant, but because it is derivative. A caveat is appended: \"It should not be forgotten, however, that metallic money can not only function as means of purchase, but also as means of payment. For the sake of simplification, we generally take it, in this second volume, only in the first functional form.\" This distinction — means of purchase ( Kaufmittel ) vs. means of payment ( Zahlungsmittel ), the latter involving the temporal separation of purchase and payment — is fundamental to Volume 1, Chapter 3 and to the theory of credit. But Volume 2's analysis of the circuit abstracts from it in the interest of the pure form. 2.18 General Circulation Laws and Their Limits in the Capital Circuit The chapter then applies the laws of general commodity circulation to the capital circuit, and immediately limits them. \"The circulation process of industrial capital, which forms only one part of its individual circuit, is determined, in so far as it represents only a series of acts within the general commodity circulation, by the general laws that have already been developed (Volume 1, Chapter 3).\" Concretely: \"The same quantity of money, e.g. £500, puts correspondingly more industrial capitals into circulation (i.e. individual capitals in their form as commodity capitals), the greater the velocity of circulation of the money, thus the faster each individual capital passes through its series of metamorphoses into commodities and money.\" Conversely, \"Capital of the same value accordingly requires less money for its circulation, the more the money functions as means of payment (e.g. the more that it is only balances that have to be settled when a commodity capital is replaced by its means of production), and the shorter the periods of payment (e.g. in the payment of wages).\" And on the other side: \"given that the velocity of circulation and all other circumstances remain the same, the amount of money needed to circulate as money capital, is determined by the sum of the prices of the commodities (price multiplied by the quantity of commodities), or alternatively, given the quantity and values of the commodities, by the value of the money itself.\" This is the qualitative statement of the quantity-of-money law: the amount of circulating medium is proportional to the sum of prices (price × quantity) and inversely proportional to the velocity of circulation, and — given the quantity and values of commodities — inversely proportional to the value of the money commodity itself (a higher gold value means less metal needed). But the limitation is decisive: \"the laws of general commodity circulation apply only in so far as the circulation process of capital is a series of simple acts of circulation, and not in so far as the latter form functionally specific sections of the circuits of individual industrial capitals.\" The point: from the standpoint of the quantity of money in circulation , there is nothing special about capital's circulation — it obeys general laws; but from the standpoint of the function of each act within the circuit (the sale that realises surplus-value, the purchase that reconstitutes the elements of production, etc.), the general laws are silent. The quantity-theoretic laws are adequate for the mass of circulation, not for its articulation . 2.19 The Intertwining of Metamorphoses Is Not an Intertwining of Capitals \"To make this clear, it is best to consider the circulation process in its uninterrupted interconnection, as it appears in the two forms.\" These two forms — the page-crossing editorial fragment and OCR corruption notwithstanding — are the general forms of commodity circulation, C—M—C and M—C—M , both of which present, as a series of acts of circulation in general, \"two opposing series of commodity metamorphoses, each individual metamorphosis including the opposite metamorphosis on the part of the other person's commodity or money that confronts it.\" The classic formulation of Volume 1 is restated: C—M on the part of the commodity possessor is M—C on the part of the purchaser; the first metamorphosis of the commodity in C—M is the second metamorphosis of the commodity that steps forth as M. Hence the intertwining of metamorphoses established in Volume 1 chapter 3 holds for capital circulation in so far as the capitalist is buyer and seller of commodities, and his capital accordingly functions as money toward others' commodities or as a commodity toward others' money. \"This intertwining, however, is not by this token alone an entwining of the metamorphoses of capitals.\" Two arguments establish the non-identity. First : M—C(mp) may depict an intertwining of the metamorphoses of various individual capitals. The cotton-spinner's yarn, for example, is partly replaced by coal. Here \"a part of his capital exists in the money form and is converted from this into the commodity form, while the capital of the mine-owner exists in the commodity form and is therefore converted into the money form; the same act of circulation here represents opposite metamorphoses on the part of two industrial capitals (which belong to different branches of production), i.e. an entwining of the series of metamorphoses of these capitals.\" But this is contingent, not necessary. First, \"the mp into which M is converted need not be commodity capital in the categorical sense, i.e. need not be a functional form of industrial capital, produced by a capitalist. It is always M—C on the one hand, and C—M on the other, but not always an entwining of metamorphoses of capital.\" (This is the point developed in §2.15 above: the means of production may come from peasants, communities, state production, etc.) Second, and still more pointedly: \"M—L, the acquisition of labour-power, is never an entwining of capital metamorphoses, for, while labour-power is certainly a commodity for the worker, it becomes capital only when it is sold to the capitalist.\" The purchase of labour-power is an act of circulation that is not the converse of any act of capital circulation; it is the conversion of an ordinary commodity — labour-power — into a constituent of capital, an act that is simultaneously the institution of the capital relation itself. Third: \"In the process C'—M', on the other hand, M' does not need to be converted commodity capital; it can be the expression in money of the commodity labour-power (i.e. wages), or of a product produced by an independent worker, a slave, a serf or a community.\" The money the industrial capitalist receives need not come from another industrial capitalist realising surplus-value; it may be revenue, wages, the proceeds of non-capitalist production. Second : \"it is by no means always the case that the functionally determined role played by every metamorphosis that takes place within the circulation process of an individual capital represents the corresponding opposite metamorphosis in the circuit of the other capital, particularly if we assume that the whole of production for the world market is pursued on a capitalist basis.\" Two illustrations are given. In the circuit P…P , \"the M' that turns C' into cash may be on the side of the buyer simply the monetary expression of his surplus-value (if the commodity is an article of consumption)\" — i.e., the buyer may be spending revenue, so the sale of C' does not correspond to any reconstitution of his capital. Alternatively, in M'—C'…(mp) , \"i.e. where accumulated capital is involved,\" the money may be for the buyer of mp \"simply a replacement for his capital advance, or it may not re-enter his capital circulation at all, particularly if this branches off into expenditure of revenue.\" The conclusion is the chapter's methodological declaration: \"The way in which the various components of the total social capital, of which the individual capitals are only independently functioning components, alternately replace one another in the circulation process — both with respect to capital and to surplus-value — is thus not the result of the simple intertwining of the metamorphoses that occurs in commodity circulation, and which the acts of capital circulation have in common with all other processes of commodity circulation, but rather requires a different mode of investigation.\" And a sharp rebuke to a prevailing style of argument: \"Up till now, mere phrases have been taken as sufficient in this respect, although, when these are analysed more closely, they contain nothing more than indefinite notions, simply borrowed from the intertwining of metamorphoses that is common to all commodity circulation.\" This is a self-limiting declaration: the tools forged in Chapters 1–4, centred on the individual capital and its forms, are insufficient for the analysis of total social capital; a different method — the schemas of reproduction, developed in Part III — is required. But — and this is the deeper point — the failure of the simple intertwining argument is itself a result of the chapter's own analysis: it is because the components of the total social capital \"alternately replace one another … both with respect to capital and to surplus-value\" that a two-department analysis becomes necessary. 2.20 The \"Different Mode of Investigation\": Commodity Market as Permanent Source and Sink Before closing, Marx sets out the peculiarity that forces the new method. \"One of the most obvious peculiarities of the circuit of industrial capital, and thus of capitalist production, is the situation that on the one hand the elements from which productive capital is formed stem from the commodity market, and must be continually renewed from it, bought as commodities; and on the other hand the product of the labour process emerges from it as a commodity, and must constantly be sold anew as a commodity.\" The circuit is thus doubly dependent on the market: as a sink for its output and as a source for its inputs. The illustration is a stark contrast between two agricultural modes: \"A modern farmer in the lowlands of Scotland might for example be contrasted with an old-fashioned small peasant on the Continent. The former sells his entire product and thus has to replace all its elements, even the seed-corn, on the market, while the latter consumes the greater part of his product directly, buys and sells as little as possible, and as far as possible produces his tools, clothing, etc. himself.\" The contrast is not a matter of scale but of degree of market dependence : the modern farmer buys even his seed-corn, i.e., reproduces himself entirely through the market; the small peasant holds production and consumption within the same unit. 2.21 Natural Economy, Money Economy, Credit Economy — Marx's Three Objections It is at this point that Marx addresses the classical trichotomy: \"Natural economy, money economy and credit economy have for this reason been counterposed as the three characteristic economic forms of motion of social production.\" (This is the tripartite scheme familiar from Bruno Hildebrand and from the German historical school, and central to the popular economic histories of the era.) Marx's critique proceeds in three numbered steps. First , the three forms \"do not represent phases of development of the same status.\" The so-called credit economy is not a third stage alongside natural and money economy; \"it is itself only a form of the money economy, in so far as both terms express functions or modes of commerce [ Verkehr ] between the producers themselves. In developed capitalist production, the money economy simply appears as the basis of the credit economy.\" Therefore money economy and credit economy correspond only to different stages of development of capitalist production ; they are in no way independent forms of commerce opposed to natural economy. And the converse consequence: \"It would be just as valid to counterpose the very varied forms of natural economy as equal in status to the other two.\" The trichotomy is thus logically arbitrary: if the criterion is \"mode of commerce,\" there are many more than three forms. Second , a question of what is being classified : \"what is emphasized in the categories money economy and credit economy, and stressed as the distinctive feature, is actually not the economy proper, i.e. the production process itself, but rather the mode of commerce between the various agents of production or producers that corresponds to the economy.\" If the defining criterion is the mode of commerce, then consistency demands applying it to the first term too: \"Instead of natural economy, we would then have barter economy. A completely enclosed natural economy, such as the Inca state of Peru, would fall into none of these categories.\" The Inca state — an example Marx elsewhere uses for a fully communal, self-sufficient production — would be excluded entirely from a scheme that classified by mode of commerce , since it had no commerce to speak of with the outside. The scheme thus fails to accommodate the very type it intends to name. Third , and decisively for Marx's own categories: \"money economy is common to all commodity production, and the product appears as a commodity in the most diverse organisms of social production.\" Commodity production and money economy are not specific to capitalism; they occur in a variety of social forms. Therefore, \"it would simply be the scale on which the product was produced as an article of trade, as a commodity, and thus also the extent to which its own formative elements must again enter the economy from which it derives as articles of trade, as commodities, which would characterize capitalist production.\" The distinguishing feature of capitalism is not the existence of money but the universality of commodity production — the extent to which the product, and even the elements of production, must be bought and sold. From this Marx draws the definitional conclusion: \"In point of fact, capitalist production is commodity production as the general form of production, but it is only so, and becomes ever more so in its development, because labour itself here appears as a commodity, because the worker sells labour, i.e. the function of his labour-power, and moreover, as we have assumed, at a value determined by the costs of its reproduction.\" The universality of commodity production rests on the commodification of labour-power. And this commodification has a determinate extension: \"The producer becomes an industrial capitalist to the same extent that labour becomes wage-labour; hence capitalist production (and thus also commodity production) appears in its full extent only when the direct agricultural producer is also a wage-labourer.\" This is why the earlier reference to the Scottish lowland farmer matters: it is not merely an example of market dependence but the very form of the completion of the capitalist mode of production in agriculture — the transformation of the direct agricultural producer into a wage-labourer. 2.22 The Verkehr Footnote and the Priority of Production over Commerce The term Verkehr in the passage just discussed carries an editorial footnote of some importance. The note explains that Verkehr plays an important role in The German Ideology , where it is conventionally translated as \"intercourse,\" but that the concept was later rejected by Marx and Engels in favour of \"relations of production,\" as Göran Therborn explains in Science, Class and Society . The present passage, written in 1877, \"seems to be the only time that Verkehr reappears in a conceptual sense in any of the volumes of Capital .\" Its meaning here, however, \"has clearly little to do with the early concept of The German Ideology . It rather covers what Marx and Engels more usually referred to as 'exchange' ( Austausch ), in the sense of 'mode of production and exchange'.\" The note then proposes a reason for the lexical choice: Marx needs to use Tausch (exchange or barter) to refer to a particular form of \"commerce\" between producers — the non-monetary exchange corresponding to a \"natural economy\" — and, since Tausch and Austausch are nearly interchangeable in German usage, he selects the looser term Verkehr \"to emphasize the general concept of which barter and monetary exchange (with the latter's sub-type credit) are the variants.\" The footnote thus documents a conceptual shift: from the early \"intercourse\" (a broad category of social interaction) to the mature \"relations of production,\" with the residual use of Verkehr here serving a precise taxonomic function — to name the genus of which barter, money exchange and credit are species. The conceptual payoff is completed in the immediately following paragraph. \"In the relation between capitalist and wage-labourer, the money relation, the relation of buyer and seller, becomes a relation inherent in production itself. But this relation rests fundamentally on the social character of production, not on the mode of commerce; the latter rather derives from the former. It is typical of the bourgeois horizon, moreover, where business deals fill the whole of people's minds, to see the foundation of the mode of production in the mode of commerce corresponding to it, rather than the other way round.\" This is the chapter's sharpest formulation of the materialist inversion : the trichotomy of natural/money/credit economy is not merely imprecise, it is ideological — it mistakes the form of circulation for the ground of production. And the sentence about business deals filling people's minds connects directly to the \"naivety\" of Sismondi's account and to the pervasive tendency of apologetic economics to treat the market as the substance of economic life rather than as its phenomenal form. 2.23 The Appended Note of 1877–78: The Capitalist's Supply and Demand At this point, the editor's note marks a textual break: \"Up to here, Manuscript V. The remainder of this chapter consists of a note found among extracts from books in a notebook of 1877 or 1878.\" The material that follows belongs to a much later period of Marx's work — the period of the critical engagement with Adolph Wagner's Lehrbuch , and of extensive reading in French and Russian economic literature — and it addresses a question distinct from, though connected with, the preceding analysis: the aggregate relation between the demand and supply of the capitalist class considered as such. The note opens with a formulation of striking economy: \"The capitalist casts less value into circulation in the form of money than he draws out of it, because he casts in more value in the form of commodities than he has extracted in the form of commodities.\" This is the value-theoretic statement of what the circuit does: the capitalist advances money (less in value) and withdraws money (more in value), and the difference corresponds to the surplus-value he has added to the commodities in production (via labour-power), which he now sells. \"In so far as he functions merely as the personification of capital, as industrial capitalist, his supply of commodity-value is always greater than his demand for it.\" The critical function of this proposition is to be the opposite of a doctrine of balanced exchange. If the capitalist's supply and demand matched, \"this would be equivalent to the non-valorization of his capital; it would not have functioned as productive capital; productive capital would have been transformed into commodity capital that had not been impregnated with surplus-value; it would not have extracted from labour-power during the production process any surplus-value in the commodity form, and thus not functioned as capital at all.\" The asymmetry of supply and demand is thus constitutive of capital , not a deviation from its norm. The circuit's very form requires it: the capitalist must \"sell dearer than he has bought,\" but he can do so only because the production process transforms cheaper commodities (means of production and labour-power) into more valuable commodities. The solution to the apparent circulationist paradox — how can one sell dearer than one bought, if values are exchanged at their values? — is that the \"dearness\" is not a price surcharge but a value difference produced in the labour process: \"He sells dearer, not because he sells above the value of his commodities, but because he sells commodities of a value greater than the sum of values of the ingredients required to produce them.\" The connecting proposition follows immediately: \"The greater the difference between the capitalist's supply and his demand, i.e. the greater the additional commodity value that he supplies over the commodity value that he demands, the greater the rate at which he valorizes his capital. His goal is not simply to cover his demand with his supply, but to have the greatest possible excess of supply over demand.\" So the degree of valorization is measured by the gap. And the highest-level statement: \"What is true for the individual capitalist, is true also for the capitalist class.\" 2.24 The Composition of Demand and the V-Shaped Asymmetry Marx now decomposes the capitalist's demand. \"In so far as the capitalist simply personifies industrial capital, his own demand consists simply in the demand for means of production and labour-power. His demand for mp is smaller in value terms than the capital he has advanced; he buys means of production to a smaller value than the value of his capital, and hence to a still smaller value than that of the commodity capital that he supplies.\" (He demands, by value, only c, while his supply contains c + v + s.) \"As far as his demand for labour-power is concerned, it is determined in its value by the ratio between his variable capital and his total capital, i.e. v:C. In capitalist production, therefore, this demand grows at a smaller rate than his demand for means of production. The capitalist buys more of mp than of L, and to a steadily increasing extent.\" This is a structural result of the circuit: because mp figures as the substance of capital's productive form and v is determined by the technically and historically conditioned reproduction cost of labour-power, the demand for mp tends to outstrip the demand for labour-power. (Marx does not here use the terminology of the organic composition of capital, but the argument is the basis for it: the ratio v:C in value terms governs the wage demand.) Next, the indirect demand. \"In so far as the worker converts his wages almost wholly into means of subsistence, and by far the greater part into necessities, the capitalist's demand for labour-power is indirectly also a demand for the means of consumption that enter into the consumption of the working class. But this demand equals v, and not an atom more (if the worker saves something out of his wages — we necessarily leave the matter of credit out of consideration here — this means that he transforms a part of his wage into a hoard and to this extent does not appear as a customer).\" Two points deserve emphasis. First, the working class's total demand for means of subsistence, mediated by wage expenditure, is equal to v in value terms — a theorem of the circuit, derived from the demand being, by construction, the value of labour-power. Second, the parenthetical on saving contains a small but important methodological point: saving by the worker is hoarding , a withdrawal of demand from the market, not an increase in demand; and the analysis abstracts from credit, in which the hoard would be placed at the disposal of others. The maximum limit is then stated: \"The maximum limit of the capitalist's demand is C = c+v, but his supply is C+V+J\" — i.e., c + v + s. The illustrative composition is 80c + 20v + 20s, so his demand is 80c + 20v, \"a value one fifth smaller than his supply.\" (A note on the figures: with c = 80, v = 20 and s = 20, the demand is 100 and the supply 120, so the deficit is 20 — i.e., exactly s. The text also renders this as \"one fifth\" and later, in the turnover illustration, as \"four fifths\" and \"one fifth smaller\"; arithmetically, 100/120 = 5/6, so the text's fractional expressions are loosely stated. The substantive proposition is exact and independent of the arithmetic illustration: the capitalist's demand as capitalist falls short of his supply by precisely the amount of surplus-value in value terms. The comparison is one of value magnitudes, not of physical quantities.) Marx then states the general form: \"The greater the percentage of s produced (the rate of profit), the smaller his demand in relation to his supply.\" The inter-capitalist dimension is then introduced. \"Although, as production advances, the capitalist's demand for labour-power, and hence indirectly for necessary means of subsistence, becomes progressively smaller than his demand for means of production, it should not be forgotten that his demand for mp is always smaller than his capital, considering this day by day. His demand for means of production must thus always be smaller in value than the commodity product of the capitalist who works with the same capital and under otherwise similar conditions, and supplies him with these means of production.\" The point is that within the interlocking of capitals, the demand of each capitalist for means of production is insufficient, in value terms, to purchase the product of the capitalists who supply them. \"That many capitalists are involved here, and not just one, in no way affects the matter.\" Then the numerical illustration: capital = £1,000, constant part = £800, so his demand on all these capitalists is £800. \"Together they supply for each £1,000 … means of production to a value of £1,200; thus his demand only covers two thirds of their supply, while his own total demand is only four fifths of his own supply, considered in value terms.\" The two-thirds is exact on the numbers (800/1,200); the \"four fifths\" is the loose rendering of a 1,000/1,200 relation. The argument to be noted: even before considering turnover or accumulation, the value relations imply a systematic gap between the demand of the productive consumers and the supply they must absorb. 2.25 Turnover, Amortization, Accumulation — and the Impossibility of Full Consumption of Surplus-Value Marx then checks whether turnover alters the result. \"Assume that his total capital is £5,000, of which £4,000 is fixed and £1,000 circulating; this 1,000 = 800c + 200v, according to the above assumption. His circulating capital must turn over five times in the year in order for his total capital to turn over once. His commodity product is then £6,000, i.e. £1,000 greater than the capital he advanced, which once again gives the same ratio of surplus-value as above: 5,000 C : 1,000 S = 100 (c+v) : 20 s. Thus this turnover in no way alters the ratio of his total demand to his total supply, the former remaining one fifth smaller than the latter.\" The point: increasing the velocity of the circulating portion multiplies the annual product and the annual surplus-value proportionally, so the ratio of demand (c + v, annualised) to supply (c + v + s, annualised) is invariant. The velocity of circulation does not solve the deficit. The treatment of fixed capital introduces a further nuance. \"Let us assume that his fixed capital has to be renewed in ten years. Each year, then, he amortizes 1/10 = £400. [After the first year] he has a value of £3,600 in fixed capital and £400 in money.\" The amortisation — the annual depreciation fund — is money that is withdrawn from the circuit but not yet re-invested in fixed capital; it accumulates as a hoard. On repairs: \"In so far as repairs are necessary, and these do not exceed the average amount, they are simply capital that is invested at a later date. We can consider the matter as if he had allowed for all the repair costs when he assessed the value of his invested capital, in so far as this enters into the annual commodity product, so that these are included in the one tenth amortization. (If his repair needs are lower than average, this is simply a bonus for him, just as it is to his disadvantage if they are higher.)\" Then the conclusion: \"although (on the assumption that his total capital turns over once in the year) his annual demand remains £5,000, the same as the original capital value he advanced, it increases with respect to the circulating part of the capital, while it steadily declines with respect to the fixed part.\" This is a subtle point with significance for the later analysis of reproduction: the annual composition of demand shifts over the life of the fixed capital, even though the aggregate remains equal to the advanced capital. Finally, the decisive argument against the \"full-consumption\" hypothesis. \"We now come to reproduction. Assume that the capitalist consumes the entire surplus-value m and reconverts only the original capital sum C into productive capital. The capitalist's demand is now equal in value to his supply.\" But — Marx insists — this only holds on a comptometric reading. \"But this is not so in respect of the movement of his capital; as capitalist he exerts a demand only on the basis of four fifths of his supply (in value terms). The remaining fifth he consumes as non-capitalist, not in his function as capitalist, but for his private requirements or pleasures.\" The \"account, reckoned in percentages,\" is given: Demand as capitalist: 100 — Supply: 120 Demand as man of the world: 20 — Supply: – Total demand: 120 — Supply: 120 The point of the ledger is that the balance is achieved only by dual-counting the capitalist: 100 as capitalist + 20 as consumer = 120 = supply. In so far as he is a capitalist, he demands only c + v; the s can only be demanded by a different persona — the private consumer, or the accumulator. And here Marx delivers the decisive judgment: \"This assumption is equivalent to assuming the non-existence of capitalist production and therefore the non-existence of the industrial capitalist himself. For capitalism is already essentially abolished once we assume that it is enjoyment that is the driving motive and not enrichment itself.\" Two arguments are given for why the full-consumption assumption is not merely counterfactual but impossible. First , it is technically impossible: \"The capitalist must not only form a reserve capital to guard against price fluctuations, and in order to be able to await the most favourable conjunctures for buying and selling; he must accumulate capital, in order to extend production and incorporate technical advances into his productive organism.\" Two distinct needs: a reserve against uncertainty (the money-capital cushion we encountered in §2.12), and accumulation for the expansion of production and the incorporation of technical change. Second , accumulation goes through a hoard phase: \"In order to accumulate capital, he must first withdraw from circulation a part of the surplus-value that he obtained from it, and let it grow in the form of a hoard until it has assumed the requisite dimensions for an extension of his old business or the opening of a new line. As long as the hoarding continues, the capitalist's demand is not increased; the money is immobilized and does not withdraw from the commodity market an equivalent in commodities for the money equivalent that it has withdrawn for commodities supplied.\" This is the hoarding phase of accumulation, and it introduces a gap between the sale of the capitalist's commodity capital and the corresponding demand: the money withdrawn from the commodity market is not immediately put back. The circuit therefore has a structural tendency to generate a lag between supply and demand — a lag that, if not mediated by credit, would be an idleness of value. And on this point the note closes with the characteristic gesture to the next level of analysis: \"We have ignored credit here, and it pertains to credit if the capitalist deposits the money that he accumulates in a bank, for example, on current account bearing interest.\" The credit system is precisely the mechanism through which hoarded, immobilized money is returned to circulation — a point that Volume 3 will develop. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 3.1 The Fundamental Principles Revealed First, capital is a movement, and its analysis must be a theory of forms, not a theory of stocks. Chapter 4 establishes with full rigour the proposition that the objects of capitalist wealth — money, means of production, commodities — are not capital as such but forms of existence of a process. The circuit cannot be understood as a sequence of material stocks; it can only be understood as the self-moving of value through its forms. This is the methodological Aufhebung of the static balance-sheet view of the economy. It has immediate implications: any analysis that treats capital as a magnitude located \"at\" a particular moment — as if the machine and the money and the finished good were simply different assets on a balance sheet — will miss the structural features on which the system's reproduction depends. The \"coexistence\" of the forms is not a portfolio but a phase-distribution. Second, the dialectic of succession and coexistence is the formal key to the continuity of the whole. The chapter's central discovery is that continuity is not a property of any part, but a relation among parts: simultaneity is achieved through division. This is a general principle with applications far beyond its original domain — in systems theory, in the analysis of production processes, and in biology (the same logic of overlapping partial processes underlies any reproduction that is continuous while its individual units are finite). And the dialectical structure matters: coexistence is both presupposition and result. It is the precipitates of succession, yet it is what makes the succession possible. The circuit is a self-conditioning totality. Third, the interruption of one circuit is a disturbance of all. The theory of the \"blockage\" — of C'—M' uncompleted causing the accumulation of unsold stock and the stalling of production — provides a form -determined mechanism of economic disruption: a mechanism of crisis latent in the structure of the circuit itself, independent of any contradiction between the productive forces and the relations of production, or of any tendency of the rate of profit to fall. The chapter does not elaborate a general theory of crisis; but it provides the formal locus of disruption, which subsequent analysis can fill with content. Fourth, the autonomization of value is the real existence of an abstraction. The category of Verselbständigung — value become independent, value as a self-moving subject — is the chapter's most philosophically consequential claim. Capital is an abstraction in action: an abstract social relation that has acquired objective, quasi-natural existence and governs the activity that constitutes it. Value revolutions, in this light, are not contraventions of capital's logic but revelations of it: the \"elemental natural process\" of value's motion dominates the foresight and calculation of individual capitalists, and the same is true of the system as a whole. Fifth, the temporality of value is the ground of value's identity. The Bailey polemic is not a marginal note: it establishes that value, to function as capital , must remain identical with itself across a temporal succession of forms, i.e., it must be able to be compared with itself as a past magnitude. Bailey's \"contemporaneity\" — value as a relation between commodities that happen to coexist on the market — abolishes this temporality, and thereby abolishes capital. This is one of the strongest arguments in the Marxian corpus against subjectivist and relationist theories of value, and it grounds a general methodological principle: temporality is not an accident of economic magnitude but is constitutive of it. Sixth, the different fractions of capital — the parts of the circuit — are not reducible to one another. The real difference between M…M' and the two other circuits, which emerges in the treatment of value revolutions, shows that the chapter's opening reduction of the difference to \"merely one of form\" is a moment in a dialectical argument, not its conclusion. The circuits are unified, but they are not identical: the circuit that starts from money, the circuit that starts from production and the circuit that starts from the already-valorized commodity suffer value revolutions differently, because the value changes hit the capital at different points of its circuit. This connects to the concept of \"release and tying-up\" of money capital, which is elaborated in Part II. Seventh, the supply–demand asymmetry of the capitalist class is structural. The appended note establishes that a capitalist's demand as capitalist is necessarily smaller than his supply, and that the deficit is exactly surplus-value. This is not a pathological feature but a definitional one: a capitalist whose supply and demand matched would not be a capitalist at all. The consequence is that the sum of all capitalists' demand cannot, in value terms, absorb the sum of all their supply as capital : the surplus must be demanded by someone else, or accumulated (which involves a hoard-lag), or disposed of through credit (which is exactly what the system invents). This is the formal ground of the realization problem. 3.2 Broader Theoretical Implications In the architecture of Volume 2 as a whole, Chapter 4 is pivotal in three ways. It closes Part I by demonstrating the unity that the preceding chapter-by-chapter analysis presupposed; it opens Part II by exposing the money-capital release/injection phenomenon, the distinction between internally and externally sourced money capital, and the hoarding phase of accumulation, all of which the theory of turnover will develop; and it announces Part III by declaring that the intertwining of metamorphoses is inadequate to the analysis of total social reproduction and that \"a different mode of investigation\" is required. More broadly, the chapter suggests a general theory of continuously reproduced systems : that continuity of the whole is achieved through the differentiated distribution of parts across phases; that this distribution has material prerequisites (a definite minimum size of capital, definite numerical proportions, the technical character of the branch of production); and that the system's exposure to disturbance is a function of the rigidity of these proportions. The theory of \"coexistence\" implies that any economy whose value-form requires the synchronisation of physically independent processes — production and circulation — is vulnerable to breakdown through asynchrony: an interruption of one link disrupts the whole. This is a foundation for the analysis of crisis that is neither \"underconsumptionist\" nor \"profit-squeeze\" but formal : crisis is latent in the very necessity of sale as a precondition of production. Finally, the chapter bears on the theory of the historical expansion of capital . The analysis of the \"many-sided origins\" of circulation establishes the world market as a structural feature of the circuit, and the transformation of all possible production into commodity production — and thereby of all immediate producers into wage-labourers — as the tendency of industrial capital's expansion. The \"natural economy, money economy, credit economy\" critique reinforces this: the category of \"natural economy\" is not a stage that capitalism supersedes, but a residual that must be drawn in, transformed, and (in Marx's terms) subsumed. The trichotomy of modes of commerce is the ideological form of the real historical process by which capital subsumes pre-capitalist production; and the position of the direct agricultural producer — the passage from small peasant to wage-labourer — is not a marginal remark but the completion of the mode of production. The chapter ends, then, where it began: with the circuit, but the circuit as a self-expanding, world-embracing, form-determining process — the abstraction in action. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 3 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 5"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 5", "title": "Chapter 5", "subtitle": "The Time of Circulation", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 5/Chapter 5 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 5883, "read_time": "29 min read", "tags": ["Circulation Time & Costs"], "keywords": ["The Time of Circulation", "Time of Purchase and Time of Sale", "Circulation Time as Negative Barrier to Valorization", "Distance to Market and Transport Advances", "Price Fluctuation Risk during Transit"], "text": "Chapter 5 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,865 words ⏱️ ~29 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 5 of Capital Volume 2, drawn (as the opening note indicates) from Marx's Manuscript IV, occupies a decisive hinge-position in the architecture of the second volume. Having in preceding chapters established the circuit of capital as a movement through three phases — money capital, productive capital, commodity capital — and having shown that this circuit is not a single event but a repetitious, continuous process, Marx must now reckon precisely with the time structure of that movement. The chapter's central object is the temporal division of the circuit into two analytically distinct but materially fused durations: production time (the time capital spends in the production sphere) and circulation time (the time it spends in the sphere of circulation). Its thesis is that the total time of the circuit — the turnover — is the sum of these two, and that this sum is not a neutral aggregate. It has a singular and asymmetrical consequence: circulation time is a pure deduction from production time, and therefore a negative limit upon the creation of surplus-value. The chapter's intellectual objective is threefold. First, to decompose \"production time\" itself, demonstrating that it is by no means identical with working time — the time during which labour actually acts on its object. Marx shows that production time includes: the functioning of means of labour across repeated labour processes (including their mere presence at the site of production when idle); the holding of reserve stocks of raw and ancillary materials; and — crucially — the natural, labourless intervals in which the object of labour is left to undergo physical or chemical transformations (fermentation, ripening, tanning). Second, to establish the valorization-theoretic consequence of this divergence: wherever production time exceeds working time — wherever productive capital exists latently or functions without absorbing labour — capital absorbs no living labour, and therefore no surplus labour, and therefore valorizes nothing. This yields the corollary that the higher the ratio of working time to production time, the greater the self-valorization of a given capital in a given period, and that capitalist production has a systematic tendency to compress the excess of production time over working time (hence its drive toward night work, continuous processes, etc.). Third — and this is the chapter's most subtle and polemical dimension — Marx attacks the appearance that circulation time is a source of value. Because the effect of circulation time on valorization is negative but its results (higher prices for capitals with longer turnover, the apparent growth of capital in the circulation sphere during accumulation) appear positive, political economy and even \"scientific economics\" are deceived into treating the circulation sphere as a mystical, autonomous fount of self-valorization independent of the exploitation of labour. Marx enumerates three phenomena sustaining this illusion: the profit calculus that converts a negative into a positive basis for price; the fact that circulation time is only one moment of turnover; and the fact that the transformation of commodities into variable capital occurs in circulation. This chapter thus sits at the intersection of the volume's theory of turnover (developed in the adjacent chapters) and Volume 1's theory of surplus-value. It is the point at which the time-structure of capital's circuit is shown to be an economic form that both expresses and conceals the extraction of surplus labour — and it lays the groundwork for the eventual turnover chapter, the analysis of the annual rate of surplus-value, and the critique of the trinity formula whereby capital, land, and labour appear as independent sources of value. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Founding Definition: The Circuit as the Sum of Two Times Marx opens by recalling the established framework: capital moves successively \"through the production sphere and the two phases of the circulation sphere,\" and these movements are \"accomplished successively in time.\" They are, that is, not simultaneous but sequential — capital cannot be in production and in circulation at the same instant (a claim that becomes central later). From this sequentiality Marx extracts the foundational equation: Total circuit time = production time + circulation time The \"duration of its stay in the production sphere forms its production time,\" and that in the circulation sphere forms its circulation time . Two phases of the circulation sphere are noted — the transformation of commodity into money (C–M) and money into commodity (M–C) — so that circulating capital undergoes two metamorphoses. This simple additive formula is the chapter's scaffolding: the whole problem is that the two terms are not homogeneous in their relationship to value, and that the second term exerts an entirely one-sided pressure on the first. 2.2 Production Time ≠ Working Time: The Means of Labour and the Reserve Stock The first substantive movement of the chapter is the decomposition of production time. \"The production time includes, of course, the period of the labour process; but this is not all.\" Marx supplies two reasons why production time overruns working time even before we reach the case of natural processes. First, the fixity of means of labour. A part of constant capital exists in means of labour — \"machines, buildings, etc.\" — that serve \"for constant repetitions of the same labour process until they are worn out.\" A nightly interruption of the labour process interrupts the function of these means of labour but does not remove them from the place of production: \"They belong to this not only when they function, but also when they do not function.\" Here we meet the important distinction between a machine's functioning and its mere sojourn in the production sphere. It is present as productive capital, but not actively engaged. Second, the reserve stock. The capitalist must hold \"a certain stock of raw and ancillary materials, so that the production process can keep going for shorter or longer intervals on the previously determined scale, without depending on the accidents of daily supply on the market.\" This reserve is \"only gradually consumed productively.\" It is therefore capital that is already productive capital (it is in the production sphere, in the hands of capital, destined for production) but is not yet in the production process. Marx introduces the critical term here: such capital is latent or idle — it \"acts neither to form products nor values,\" yet \"its idleness forms a condition for the uninterrupted flow of the production process.\" Thus the production time of means of production comprises, in Marx's threefold enumeration: (1) the time during which they function as means of production; (2) the pauses in which the production process (and hence their functioning) is interrupted; and (3) the time during which they are held in reserve as conditions of the process, already representing productive capital but not yet engaged in it. The footnote clarifies that \"production time\" is here used in an active sense: \"the production time of the means of production is not the time that it takes to produce them, but that for which they participate in the production process of a commodity product.\" 2.3 The Second Divergence: Interruptions Internal to the Production Process Having established the divergence between capital's stay in the production sphere and its engagement in the production process , Marx now introduces a distinctly different and more important divergence — one internal to the production process itself. The production process \"may itself involve interruptions of the labour process and hence of working time, intervals in which the object of labour is exposed to the action of physical processes, without further addition of human labour.\" The examples are canonical and later become textbook material: corn that is sown, wine that ferments in the cellar, and material of labour exposed to chemical processes as in tanning . In these cases the production process — and hence the function of the means of production — continues even though the labour process (and hence the function of the means of production as means of labour ) is suspended. Here: Production time > Working time This excess \"is always based on the fact that the productive capital exists in a latent state in the production sphere, without functioning in the production process itself, or that it functions in the production process without being involved in the labour process.\" This is a pivotal conceptual distinction with two limbs: capital can be (a) in the production sphere but not in the process (latent reserve), or (b) in the production process but not in the labour process (the natural-process interlude). Both give rise to the same excess — production time over working time — but through different mechanisms. Marx keeps them analytically separate because their treatment differs slightly in the next beat. 2.4 The Anatomy of Latent Capital: Idle Reserve, Storage Apparatus, and the Two Value-Channels Marx now performs a fine-grained disambiguation of the latent capital, distinguishing parts of it according to whether and how they bear on value. Part one — the pure reserve (cotton, coal in the spinning mill). This part \"acts neither to form products nor values. It is idle capital.\" It will shortly enter the process, but for now it is quiescent and value-neutral. The storage apparatus. The buildings and apparatus necessary for storing the reserve (i.e. for maintaining the latent capital in its preliminary stage) are themselves \"conditions of the production process and hence form components of the productive capital advanced.\" They fulfil their function \"by maintaining the productive components in the preliminary stage.\" They make the raw material \"dearer\" (they add to the cost of the raw material) — but Marx immediately makes a value-theoretic move peculiar to his labour theory: \"since a part of this labour, in the same way as a part of all other wage-labour, is not paid for, it is productive labour and creates surplus-value.\" In other words, the labour that builds and maintains the storage apparatus is exploited wage-labour (only partly paid), and is therefore value- and surplus-value-creating. The storing labour is productive in the strict Marxist sense even though the stored stock is idle. The interruptions. \"The normal interruptions of the overall production process, i.e. the intervals in which the productive capital does not function, produce neither value nor surplus-value.\" These are the regular pauses (nights, weekends). Because they are unproductive of value, capital has an incentive to eliminate them — hence \"the drive towards night work\" (cross-referenced to Volume 1, Chapter 10, §4). This is one of the first bridges in the chapter between an abstract temporal category and a concrete historical tendency of capitalist production. The natural-process intervals within working time. \"The intervals in the working time that the object of labour has itself to undergo during the production process create neither value nor surplus-value; but they further the product, form a part of its life, a process that it must pass through.\" So the corn ripening or the hide tanning consumes production time without adding value — yet it is necessary to the product's becoming. This is the crucial paradox: unproductive time that is nonetheless an obligatory condition of the productive result. Marx then clarifies the value-transfer mechanism: \"The value of the apparatus, etc. is carried over to the product in proportion to the entire period during which it functions.\" The point is that the object of labour is placed in the required state \"by labour itself,\" and the use of the apparatus is \"just as much a condition of production as the reduction to dust of a part of the cotton that does not go into the product, but still carries its value over to it.\" Here Marx uses an analogy from the previous chapter: just as a portion of cotton is reduced to dust and does not enter the product but still transfers its value to it, so the use of the whole apparatus during the natural-process interval transfers value even though labour is suspended — because the apparatus's employment was mediated by labour. Part two — means of labour interrupted only by regular pauses (buildings, machines). The \"other part of the latent capital\" — \"the means of labour whose function is interrupted only by the regular pauses in the production process\" — \"adds value, without entering into the formation of the product.\" Crucial qualifier: \"irregular interruptions as a result of a restriction of production, crises, etc. are pure loss.\" So normal pauses are absorbed into the average life of the machine and thus into value transfer, while abnormal pauses (crises) are uncompensated losses. Marx then delivers a precise and often-missed formulation: \"The total value that the means of labour add to the product is determined by the average length of their life; they lose value because they lose use-value, not only in the time during which they are functioning, but also in the time during which they are not.\" This is a subtle but essential point: wear-and-tear includes the wasting that happens when machines sit idle, provided the idleness is normal. Value depreciation tracks the loss of use-value across the machine's whole life, not just the hours of operation. 2.5 The Heart of the Matter: Why the Excess Yields No Valorization Marx now draws the central conclusion for the chapter — the pivot around which the entire analysis turns. \"Finally, the value of that part of the constant capital that continues in the production process even when the labour process is interrupted appears once again in the result of the production process.\" The means of production are placed by labour itself into conditions where they \"undergo by themselves certain specific natural processes, the result of which is a specific useful effect or a changed form of their use-value.\" Labour carries over the value of the means of production to the product \"to the extent that it actually consumes these deliberately as means of production.\" Then the crucial value-theoretic theorem: \"Nothing is altered here by whether the labour must, through the means of labour, act continuously on the object of labour, in order to produce this effect, or whether it need only give the first impulse by placing the means of production in conditions in which they themselves undergo the intended alteration, without labour's further collaboration, as a result of natural processes.\" This is the answer to a possible objection: someone might think that if a machine only initiates the natural process (as opposed to operating throughout), it is somehow less of a value-transmitter. Marx denies this: the transmissibility of value depends on labour's deliberate consumption of the means of production, not on the duration of labour's action. From this follows the theorem on valorization: \"Whatever may be the reason for the excess of production time over working time... in none of these cases do the means of production function to absorb labour. If they absorb no labour, then they absorb no surplus labour. Hence there is no valorization of the productive capital, as long as this finds itself in that part of its production time that is in excess of the working time, no matter how inseparable these pauses may be from the accomplishment of the valorization process.\" This is the core statement. The logic is syllogistic: no absorption of labour → no absorption of surplus labour → no valorization. The excess production time is necessary for production but is value-theoretically sterile. Hence Marx's proportionality theorem: The nearer production time and working time approach equality, the greater the productivity and valorization of a given productive capital in a given space of time. This yields the tendency of capitalist production to shorten as much as possible the excess of production time over working time . Yet Marx immediately cautions that the excess cannot be eliminated: \"although the production time of capital may diverge from its working time, it always includes the latter, and the excess itself is a condition of the production process.\" Production time is thus defined as \"the time that the capital takes to produce use-values and valorize itself, hence to function as productive capital, although it includes time in which it is either latent or produces without being valorized.\" The category is therefore broader than valorization, but internally ordered by it. 2.6 Entering the Circulation Sphere: Metamorphosis Without Mystification Having completed the analysis of production time, Marx turns to circulation time. His first move is a de-mystifying one: within the circulation sphere, capital exists \"as commodity capital and money capital,\" and its two circulation processes are its transformation from commodity form to money form and from money form to commodity form. The crucial point is that even though C–M ( in this context C'–M') is also the realization of surplus-value, and M–C is also the reconversion of capital value into the form of its production elements, this \"in no way changes the fact that these processes, as processes of circulation, are processes of simple commodity metamorphosis.\" Whatever additional capital-specific function these acts serve, considered as circulation they are no different from the plain metamorphoses studied in Volume 1, Chapter 3. This sets up the argument that circulation time as such is not a value-creating process. 2.7 The Mutual Exclusivity of Circulation Time and Production Time: The Negative Limit Marx states flatly: \"Circulation time and production time are mutually exclusive. During its circulation time, capital does not function as productive capital, and therefore produces neither commodities nor surplus-value.\" This is the theorem that the two times cannot overlap for the same aliquot portion of capital. He then examines the simplest case: \"if we consider the circuit in its simplest form, so that the entire capital value always moves at one stroke from one phase to the other, then it is obvious that the production process is interrupted, and with it therefore the self-valorization of capital, so long as its circulation time lasts.\" In this simplest case, the entire capital is periodically frozen — it produces value only in the production interval and lies fallow in the circulation interval, so lengthening circulation time simply shortens the productive fraction of the circuit. Then he introduces the more realistic case of successive phases across aliquot parts : \"If the various parts of the capital pass through the circuit in succession, so that the circuit of the total capital value is successively accomplished in the circuit of its various portions, then it is clear that the longer its aliquot parts remain in the circulation sphere, the smaller must be the part that functions at any time in the production sphere.\" So even with a divided capital — some parts in production, others in circulation at any moment — the fraction in production shrinks as circulation time grows. From this Marx derives the negative limit theorem: \"The expansion and contraction of the circulation time hence acts as a negative limit on the contraction or expansion of the production time, or of the scale on which a capital of a given magnitude can function.\" And the limit approaches zero when circulation is idealized: The more that the circulation metamorphoses of capital are only ideal, i.e. the closer the circulation time comes to zero, the more the capital functions, and the greater is its productivity and self-valorization. The limiting illustration: \"If a capitalist works to order, receives payment on the delivery of his product, and is paid in his own means of production, then his time of circulation approaches zero.\" In that ideal case, capital is almost continuously productive, and valorization is at its maximum. 2.8 The Great Illusion: How a Negative Effect Appears as Positive The chapter now makes its polemical turn. \"Capital's circulation time generally restricts its production time, and hence its valorization process. Moreover, it restricts this in proportion to its duration.\" Restriction can vary enormously in degree. But — and here is the crux — political economy \"sees is only the appearance, i.e. the effect of the circulation time on the valorization process of capital in general. It conceives this negative effect as positive, because its results are positive.\" This is a formulation worthy of close attention. The effect of circulation time on valorization is negative (it subtracts from valorization). But the observable results of longer circulation time — higher prices, expanded accumulations in the circulation sphere — are positive quantities. Because the observable results are positive, the underlying cause is misread as positive. Political economy clings to this \"illusion\" all the more firmly because \"it seems to provide it with the proof that capital possesses a mystical source of self-valorization that is independent of its production process and hence of the exploitation of labour, and derives rather from the sphere of circulation.\" Even \"scientific economics\" — a footnote clarifies this means classical political economy , cross-referenced to Volume 1, Chapter 1, §4, note 34 — is taken in by this illusion. Marx then lists three phenomena that sustain and \"confirm\" the illusion: (1) The capitalist calculation of profit. In the capitalist's calculus, \"the negative reason appears as positive, in that with capitals in different spheres of investment, in which only the circulation times differ, longer circulation time is the basis for a higher price, in short, is one of the bases in the equalization of profits.\" This is a foreshadowing of the equalization of the general rate of profit: capitals tied up longer must (for the capitalist to deploy them at all) earn higher prices — which, when viewed superficially, looks like circulation time generating value rather than merely requiring compensation for value withheld. (2) Circulation time as a moment of turnover. \"The circulation time forms only one moment of the turnover time; but the latter includes the production time or reproduction time.\" When turnover is treated as the entire measure of capital's rhythm, circulation time is folded in with production time and the productive source of value is obscured. (3) Accumulation and the conversion into variable capital. \"The conversion of commodities into variable capital (wages) is conditioned by their previous transformation into money. In the case of capital accumulation, therefore, the conversion into additional variable capital takes place in the circulation sphere, or during the circulation time. Hence the accumulation arising therefrom appears to be due to the circulation time.\" Since additional variable capital requires that commodities first be sold (C–M), the passage through circulation appears to produce accumulation — a conclusion directly contradicting the labour theory of value. 2.9 The Anatomy of Circulation Time Itself: The Greater Weight of Sale Marx then decomposes circulation time into its two constituent phases. Capital passes through the \"two opposing phases C–M and M–C, in whichever order.\" Circulation time therefore \"breaks down into two parts, the time needed for its transformation from commodity into money, and the time that it needs for its transformation from money into commodities.\" Drawing on Volume 1, Chapter 3, Marx asserts that C–M, the sale, is \"the most difficult part of its metamorphosis, and thus forms the greater part of the circulation time in normal circumstances.\" The reason is a form-of-value argument: \"As money, value exists in its ever convertible form. As commodity, it must first receive this form of direct exchangeability and hence constant readiness for action by being transformed into money.\" Money is immediately exchangeable; the commodity is not — it must first be sold , i.e. it must find a buyer at an acceptable price. So the harder, longer phase is the sale. The M–C phase involves \"its transformation into those commodities which form the specific elements of productive capital in a given sphere of investment.\" Here Marx enumerates the contingencies that can lengthen M–C: means of production \"may not be present on the market, needing first to be produced, or they may have to be drawn from distant markets,\" or there may be \"dislocations in their normal supply, changes of price, etc.\" None of these circumstances is visible \"in the simple change of form M–C,\" yet they materially determine its duration. Importantly, C–M and M–C \"may also be separated in space, the selling and the buying markets being in different places. In factories, for example, buyers and sellers are frequently even different persons.\" 2.10 Circulation Agents and the Confusion of Functions Because circulation is as necessary as production, \"agents of circulation are just as necessary as agents of production.\" The reproduction process requires that both functions be represented — \"either by the capitalist himself, or by salaried workers, his agents.\" But Marx resists any inference that this justifies confusing the categories. \"This is just as little a reason for confusing the circulation agents with the production agents as it is a reason for confusing the functions of commodity capital and money capital with those of productive capital.\" The relevant economic point: circulation agents must be paid by production agents . The labour of buying and selling is not itself is value-creating in the strict sense — it is faux frais , a deduction from the social product. Marx anticipates a standard objection: \"if capitalists who buy and sell among themselves create by this act neither products nor value, this situation is not altered when the scale of their business enables them to pass this function onto others, and indeed makes it necessary to do so.\" In many businesses \"sellers and buyers are paid in the form of a percentage of the profit.\" Then he dismisses the popular wheeze that \"they are paid by the consumers\": \"The consumers can pay only in so far as they themselves produce, as agents of production, an equivalent in commodities, or alternatively appropriate this from the production agents, whether by a legal title (as their partners, etc.), or through personal services.\" Whatever the surface mechanism, the payment of circulation agents ultimately flows back to the production of value. 2.11 The Capitalist Determination of the Asymmetry Between Sale and Purchase Marx then isolates a distinction between C–M and M–C that is not a mere matter of form. \"In and for themselves, both C–M and M–C are mere translations of the given value from one form into the other. But C'–M' is at the same time the realization of the surplus-value contained in C'. Not so M–C.\" Consequently: \"The sale is more important than the purchase.\" M–C \"is in normal conditions a necessary act for the valorization of the value expressed in M, but it is not a realization of surplus-value; it is a prelude to its production, not an appendix to it.\" This clarifies that the two phases are asymmetric not only in difficulty (sale is harder) but in function : the sale realizes the surplus-value already produced; the purchase merely re-positions value for a new cycle. The purchase is thus prior to valorization in a logical sense; the sale is posterior to it. This asymmetry is a capitalist one — it exists because C' already carries surplus-value within it and M does not. 2.12 Perishability: The Absolute Barrier of Commodity Capital's Circulation The chapter closes with a distinct and materially important argument: the use-value of the commodity imposes an absolute temporal limit on C'–M'. \"The very form of existence of commodities, their existence as use-values, sets certain limits to the circulation of the commodity capital C'–M'.\" If commodities \"do not enter into productive or individual consumption within a certain interval of time, according to their particular characteristics, in other words if they are not sold within a definite time, then they get spoiled, and lose, together with their use-value, the property of being bearers of exchange-value. Both the capital value contained in them and the surplus-value added to it are lost.\" The essential dialectic here — and it is thoroughly Marxian — is that use-value is not irrelevant to exchange-value, but rather its material substrate. Use-values \"remain the bearers of perennial and self-valorizing capital value only in so far as they are constantly renewed, are replaced by new use-values of the same or another kind.\" Sale — entry into consumption — \"is however the constantly repeated condition for their reproduction.\" Commodities \"must change their old use form within a certain time, and continue their existence in a new one. It is only through this constant renewal of its body that the exchange-value maintains itself.\" The implication is that different commodities have different perishability gradients. \"The use-values of different commodities may decay at different speeds; thus a greater or lesser interval may elapse between their production and their consumption, and they may thus persist for a shorter or longer time in the circulation phase C–M as commodity capital, endure a shorter or longer circulation time as commodities.\" Marx then names the general principle: \"The limitation of the circulation time of commodity capital imposed by the spoiling of the commodity body itself is the absolute limit of this part of the circulation time, or of the time for which the commodity capital can circulate as commodity capital.\" From this Marx derives a series of geographic and industrial consequences. \"The more perishable a commodity, the more directly after its production it must be consumed, and therefore sold, the smaller the distance it can move from its place of production, the narrower therefore is its sphere of spatial circulation, and the more local the character of its market.\" Hence: \"the more perishable a commodity, the greater are the absolute barriers to its circulation time that its physical properties impose, and the less appropriate it is as an object of capitalist production.\" This produces a dialectical reversal. Since capitalist production requires extended circulation (large markets, long distances), perishability is a structural obstacle to it. But capitalism finds countervailing instruments: \"Capitalism can only deal in commodities of this kind in populous places, or to the extent that distances are reduced by the development of means of transport.\" And further: \"The concentration of the production of an article in a few hands, however, and in a populous place, can create a relatively large market even for an article of this kind, as is the case with the big breweries, dairies, etc.\" So the natural barrier of the commodity body becomes a solvent driving concentration of production, urban agglomeration, and the development of the means of transport — the material underpinnings of the credit system, the transport revolution, and the large-scale industrial form. In this closing movement, Marx shows that even the \"natural\" properties of use-values are socially metabolized by specifically capitalist forces. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter's fundamental principle is that time is not neutral in the circuit of capital . Two temporal durations — production and circulation — are summed into a single turnover, but they enter that sum with opposite signs with respect to valorization. Production time contains working time (the sole source of surplus-value, through the absorption of labour), while circulation time replaces it, and is therefore subtracted from valorization. This asymmetry — the \"negative limit\" theorem — is the analytical core on which everything else turns. From this core, the chapter yields several systemic lessons. First, production time is a broader category than working time, and the gap between them is regulated by the material properties of use-values and by the imperatives of continuous production. Because reserve stocks must be held, natural processes must complete, and interruptions must be endured, capital's \"production time\" always overruns labour time. The paradox is that capital, whose sole aim is self-valorization, must waste production time precisely in order to valorize. It therefore has a systematic drive — the drive toward night work, toward accelerating natural processes by chemical and technical means, toward shortening supply chains — to close the gap. The mean ratio of working time to production time thereby becomes a concrete measure of the efficiency of valorization of a given capital, and the historical tendency of capitalist technology can be read as an assault on this gap. Second, circulation time is a deduction, but appears as an addition. The chapter's most important critical move is to expose the fetishistic inversion: because circulation time lengthens the circuit, and because the prices and profits realized in the market bear the imprint of that lengthening, political economy mistook a negative cause for a positive one, treating the sphere of circulation as a mystical source of value. Marx names three supporting phenomena that sustain this misrecognition — the profit calculus of price-equalization across spheres with different circulation times; the absorption of circulation time within the broader turnover time; and the fact that accumulation requires the prior sale of commodities, which makes accumulation seem to originate in circulation. This is the classic Marxian operation of penetrating through surface phenomena (the \"appearance\") to the underlying value relation, and it directly prefigures the Volume 3 critique of the trinity formula, in which capital, land, and labour appear as three independent sources of revenue. Third, within circulation time, the sale (C'–M') dominates, both in duration and in significance. Duration, because money is the immediately exchangeable form and commodities are not — so the burden of uncertainty, of finding a buyer, of realizing the embodied surplus-value falls on C–M. Significance, because C'–M' realizes the surplus-value already in the product, whereas M–C only prepares a new valorization. This asymmetry is a specifically capitalist one: it arises because C' already contains surplus-value while M does not. The sale is thus the moment at which the social validity of privately performed labour is decided — a compact statement of the labour-theoretic core of Marx's theory of value. Fourth, circulation requires agents, and their remuneration is a deduction from production. Marx insists that circulation agents — capitalists themselves or their salaried workers — are necessary to the reproduction process, but their labour does not create value; it must be paid out of value produced elsewhere. Even the \"paid by consumers\" evasion collapses once it is recognized that consumers pay only out of what they themselves produce or legally appropriate from producers. Fifth, the physical properties of commodities — perishability — set an absolute upper bound on circulation time and thereby shape the geography, industrial structure, and technology of capitalism. The seeming triviality of spoilage turns out to be a determinant of urban concentration (populous places), of transport development (railways, refrigeration, fast shipping), and of concentrated industrial forms (large breweries, dairies). Perishability, in short, is a material fact that capital must metabolize through social and technical reorganization. Taken together, these lessons position Chapter 5 as the analytical bridge between Volume 1's theory of surplus-value (labour as the sole source of value) and Volume 2's theory of turnover (how the temporal rhythm of capital's circuit conditions the actual production of surplus-value over the year). It also prefigures Volume 3's analysis of the equalization of profit rates, the division of surplus-value among capitals according to turnover and composition, and ultimately the critique of capital fetishism. The deeper philosophical implication is that capital's apparent self-expansion is a phenomenon of appearance grounded in a hidden extraction of surplus labour ; circulation time, precisely because it appears productive, is one of the most effective forms in which that concealment operates. Marx's chapter is thus not merely a technical passage on temporal accounting but a sustained exercise in the critique of economic appearance — a demonstration that the temporal structure of capital is at once the material condition of surplus-value production and the ideological medium of its concealment. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 4 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 6"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 6", "title": "Chapter 6", "subtitle": "The Costs of Circulation", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 6/Chapter 6 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 15035, "read_time": "75 min read", "tags": ["Circulation Time & Costs"], "keywords": ["Costs of Circulation", "Pure Costs of Circulation (Buying, Selling, Accounting)", "Costs of Storing Goods (Formation of Supply)", "Transport Industry as Productive Circulation", "Commercial Labor and Value Creation"], "text": "Chapter 6 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 14,985 words ⏱️ ~75 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem Chapter 6 of Capital Volume 2 (\"The Costs of Circulation\") confronts a deceptively simple question that, once unpacked, opens onto the deepest structural fault lines of the capitalist mode of production: what does it cost capital to circulate, and who pays for that cost? Marx's central thesis is that the movements of capital in circulation—the buying and selling, the book-keeping, the money, the storage, the transport—do not create value. They consume value. But they consume it in fundamentally different ways, with fundamentally different consequences for the price of commodities and for the distribution of surplus-value. The chapter's intellectual objective is to sort these costs into logically distinct categories and to demonstrate that the distinction between \"pure circulation costs\" (which produce no value and enter no commodity's price) and \"costs of storage and transport\" (which, while unproductive from a social standpoint, nonetheless enter into the value of commodities and thereby take on a value-forming appearance) is not a matter of semantics but a matter of real, measurable economic consequence. The Analytic Spine The chapter proceeds through three sections that form a single argumentative arc: Pure circulation costs (buying and selling time, book-keeping, money) — costs that arise purely from the form of commodity production. They add nothing to the commodity's use-value, nothing to its value; they are faux frais of capitalist production, a deduction from the surplus product of society. Costs of storage — a heterogeneous category. To the extent storage arises from the form, it shares the character of pure circulation costs; to the extent it is a productive process continued in the sphere of circulation, it adds value and becomes a source of enrichment for the individual capitalist, even as it remains a pure deduction from the standpoint of social capital. Transport costs — perhaps the most conceptually startling section. Transport is a production process that happens to be located within the circulation sphere. It therefore genuinely adds value to commodities. This section serves to prove that spatial location within the circuit has no bearing on the productive or unproductive character of a labour process; only the theoretical relation to value-formation matters. Intellectual Context Within Capital This chapter is one of the most crucial hinges in Marx's entire critique. Having established in Chapter 4 (Volume 2) and Chapter 5 (the \"Time of Circulation\" section) that capital exists as a unity spanning the spheres of production and circulation, and having rigorously distinguished production time from circulation time , Marx here closes the circle by interrogating what happens in circulation time. His answer is that much of what occurs there is unproductive in the strict sense—it does not produce use-values, does not transfer value, does not produce surplus-value. This distinction is foundational to Volume 3, where it explains the emergence of merchant's capital and the general rate of profit. The category faux frais — overhead—will be used to demonstrate that merchants extract a share of surplus-value not because their labour is productive but because their function is necessary to the realization of value already produced. The chapter is also a response, implicit and explicit, to the classical political economists—Adam Smith (whom Marx accuses of a \"childish misunderstanding\" about the stock), Quesnay (whose Physiocratic doctrine of \"costs of trade\" as the source of loss Marx cites approvingly), Ricardo (on transport and value), Say (whom Marx dismisses as a vulgarizer), and figures like Lalor and Sismondi (whom Marx acknowledges but finds one-sided on stock formation). The Broader Theoretical Framework The stakes could not be higher. If circulation costs did create value, then the entire labo(u)r theory of value would collapse: the amount of socially necessary labour time embodied in a commodity would depend not only on its production but on the costs of getting it to market—including the costs of the market itself. Marx insists this is not the case. Value is produced in production. Circulation merely realizes and redistributes it. But the circulation process itself is not free—it imposes real costs on society, and these costs are deducted from the surplus product before it can be distributed as profit, interest, and rent. The chapter thus demonstrates that the sphere of circulation is parasitical on the sphere of production, even as it is indispensable to it—a conclusion with profound consequences for political economy, for the theory of crises, and for the critique of apologetic economics. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Framing Problem: The Metamorphosis of Capital and the Question of Cost The chapter opens from an observation established earlier in the volume: capital's circuit—the movement M-C...P...C'-M'—involves repeated changes of form . The commodities produced by industrial capital must be sold (C'-M') and the proceeds used to buy the elements of production again (M-C). Every one of these changes of form is, from the standpoint of the capitalist, a business transaction : an act of buying or selling, performed on the market, requiring time, effort, and often specialized personnel. The question Marx poses is what these transactions cost and how their cost is conceptualized within the value theory. This framing is critical because it establishes that the circulation costs Marx investigates are not incidental frictions but structural features of a commodity-producing economy. Under capitalist production, virtually everything is produced as a commodity; hence virtually everything must pass through the metamorphosis C-M and M-C. The costs that arise from this passage are therefore inherent to the system, not contingent upon inefficiency, dishonesty, or bad management. They are, in Marx's phrase, faux frais — overhead costs — of commodity production in general. But the investigation soon reveals that this general category faux frais conceals internal divisions of great theoretical importance. Some overhead costs are pure deductions. Others, while still \"unproductive\" in the strict sense, have the peculiar property of adding value to commodities. The chapter's structure is an exploration of this internal division, and its intellectual climax is the demonstration that the location of a labour process—inside or outside \"circulation\"—has no bearing on its productive or unproductive character. I(a) Buying and Selling Time: The Unproductive Labour of Metamorphosis The temporal and subjective character of circulation. Marx begins with the simplest and most general of circulation costs: the time spent in buying and selling. Every change in the form of capital, considered from the standpoint of the capitalist, is a business transaction. The time required to complete it exists \"subjectively, from the standpoint of the capitalist, as selling time and buying time\"—that is, as time during which he functions on the market, prowling around for buyers and sellers. This time is a necessary part of his reproduction time, just as the circulation time of capital is a necessary part of its total turnover time. It is part of what Marx calls business hours , in contrast to the hours during which the capitalist functions as the organizer of production. This distinction between business hours and production hours —and, correspondingly, between the traveling salesman and the factory manager —is not merely descriptive. It encodes a theoretical claim: the time the capitalist spends in circulation is time during which his capital is not producing . The capital is tied up, its valorization is suspended, and the labour expended is, considered strictly, unproductive. The chapter's first section sets out to demonstrate this rigorously. The value-conservation argument. Marx immediately establishes the fundamental proposition that will govern the whole chapter: if commodities are sold at their values, the act of exchange changes only the form of value, not its magnitude. C-M and M-C are \"a change of state.\" The amounts of value in the hands of buyer and seller remain unchanged; only the form in which the value exists has altered (from commodity to money, or vice versa). Even if the commodities are not sold at their values—if the exchange is unequal—the sum of converted values remains unaffected: \"what is a plus for one side is a minus for the other.\" This is the zero-sum property of the exchange relation, a proposition with deep roots in the classical labour theory of value. From this Marx draws a strong conclusion: the labour expended in bringing about this change of form—the labour of negotiation, of inspection, of bargaining, of the \"struggle\" in which each side seeks to gain the upper hand—does not create value. It is labour that accomplishes a metamorphosis but adds nothing to the product. The reference to the Greek proverb—\"when Greek meets Greek then comes the tug of war\"—is more than a colorful aside; it captures the agonistic character of market exchange, a struggle whose outcome is distributional (one side gains what the other loses) but never a net creation of value. The combustion analogy. Marx elaborates this thesis through an analogy that has become justly famous: the labour of circulation is like the work of combustion in lighting a fire. To use coal as fuel, you must combine it with oxygen and effect a transformation of its physical state—from solid carbon to gaseous carbon dioxide. This transformation costs a certain expenditure of energy, and that energy is not transformed into heat; it detracts from the heat. The \"work of combustion\" is a necessary moment of the combustion process, but it is not itself productive of the useful effect (heat) that the process aims at. In the same way, the labour of buying and selling is a necessary moment of the total process of capitalist reproduction, but it does not itself produce value. It consumes a portion of the available labor-time and energy that could otherwise have gone into production. This analogy has profound implications. It implies that the greater the circulation time relative to production time, the more \"energy\" is diverted from production into the mere preparation of production's products for sale. The efficiency of capital is thereby reduced. It also implies that the capitalist's effort to \"get the better\" of his counterparty—however successful—cannot increase the total value available for distribution, only redistribute it. Historical digression: the peasant's feast-day deferral. Marx pauses to note that when the commodity owners are not capitalists but independent direct producers—peasants or artisans—the situation is qualitatively different in appearance, though identical in substance. Their buying and selling time is a deduction from their labour time , and they therefore seek to postpone it to feast days. This historical observation serves to establish the generality of the proposition: the unproductiveness of circulation labor is not a peculiar feature of capitalism; it is a feature of any economy in which producers must exchange. Capitalism merely intensifies and concentrates it. The \"transubstantiation\" fallacy. Marx now addresses an illusion that arises from the mere organization of circulation. Could the unproductive character of circulation labour be overcome by transferring it to specialists? If the industrial capitalist hires third parties to do his buying and selling—\"instead of themselves performing the 'work of combustion', make this into the exclusive business of third parties paid by them\"—does this change the character of the labour? No. \"These third parties will certainly not put their labour-power at the disposal of the capitalists for the sake of their blue eyes.\" They must be paid, and their payment is a deduction from the surplus. Marx compares them to the rent collector of a landlord or the bank porter: their labour adds nothing to the magnitude of the rent or the gold they carry. The \"miracle of transubstantiation\"—the conversion of unproductive labour into value-creating labour—cannot be accomplished by any transposition or reorganization. The character of a labour process is determined by its content and its relation to value , not by the identity of the person performing it or the location of the firm within the division of labour. This argument is crucial for Volume 3, where Marx will deal with merchant's capital. It establishes in advance that the merchant's profit cannot be explained by claiming that the merchant's labour is productive. Whatever the merchant's profit is, it must be a share of surplus-value produced elsewhere. The merchant as a \"machine\" reducing useless energy. Marx now introduces a nuance that could otherwise undermine the argument. One merchant—considered merely as the agent of formal transformation, as pure buyer and seller—may, by his operations, shorten the buying and selling time for many producers. He effectively concentrates the unproductive function into fewer hands and realizes economies of scale. Marx acknowledges this: the merchant \"should then be considered as a machine that reduces the expenditure of useless energy, or helps to set free production time.\" This is important because it shows that Marx's argument is not a crude denunciation of merchants as parasites. The merchant does perform a socially useful function: he reduces the total amount of social labor-power and time that must be devoted to the unproductive function of circulation. By concentrating this function, he frees labor-time for productive use. But—and here is the crux—this is precisely the character of a machine . A machine is not productive because it creates value; it is productive because it reduces the labor time required to produce a given product, thereby lowering the value of the product. A machine that reduces the labor time required for circulation similarly reduces the cost of circulation. But the machine itself is a deduction from the product; it is part of the constant capital that must be maintained. Similarly, the merchant is a deduction from the social product, even if—or precisely because—he reduces the magnitude of that deduction. Marx then assumes, for simplicity, that the merchant (or circulation agent) is a wage-labourer . This assumption allows him to explore the peculiar position of circulation workers within the theory of surplus-value. The circulation worker expends his labor-power and labor-time in C-M and M-C. He lives off his wage, like a spinner or a pill-maker. He performs a necessary function, because the reproduction process itself includes unproductive functions. \"But the content of his labour creates neither value nor products. He is himself part of the faux frais of production.\" His usefulness lies not in transforming an unproductive function into a productive one—which would be a miracle—but rather in the fact that \"a smaller part of society's labour-power and labour time is now tied up in these unproductive functions.\" The surplus labour of the circulation worker. Marx now introduces a subtle and important point: even if the circulation worker is a wage-labourer, even one of the better paid, he still performs surplus labour. He may receive the value product of eight hours' labour and function for ten; the two hours of surplus labour that he performs \"no more produce value than do his eight hours of necessary labour.\" But—and Marx draws three conclusions here—this surplus labour has peculiar properties. First: from the social point of view, the person's labor-power is used up for ten hours in this circulation function. It is not available for anything else, including productive labour. Second: society does not count these two hours of surplus labour, although they are spent by the individual who performs them. Society does not appropriate by this means any additional product or value. But the costs of circulation that the worker represents are reduced by a fifth—from ten hours to eight. Society pays no equivalent for a fifth of this circulation time whose agent he is. Third: if it is the capitalist who employs these agents, then the circulation costs of his capital, which form a deduction from his receipts, are reduced by the non-payment of the two hours. For him, this is a positive profit, because the negative restriction on the valorization of his capital is reduced—that is, the deduction from his surplus is smaller than it would otherwise be. This analysis is remarkable. It shows that even in the sphere of pure circulation—where no value is created—capital extracts surplus labour from its wage-labourers, and this extraction benefits the individual capitalist by reducing his costs. But it does not thereby create any additional social value; the surplus labour of circulation workers is, from the social point of view, simply unpaid time whose product does not exist. The capitalist class as a whole gains nothing in value terms from this arrangement; it merely distributes the necessary costs of circulation differently among its members, and it may thereby make the system of circulation cheaper in terms of labor-power consumed. In contrast, as long as small independent commodity producers spend part of their own time in buying and selling, this simply presents itself as time spent in the intervals between their productive function, or as a loss in their production time. They bear the cost directly, in the form of reduced output. The \"machine that buys and sells\" — a formal conclusion. Marx draws a striking image to summarize the argument. If the scale of the capitalist's business forces or enables him to hire his own circulation agents as wage-labourers, \"this does not affect the substance of the phenomenon.\" Labour-power and labour-time must be spent to a certain degree in the circulation process, in so far as this is a mere change of form. But this now appears as an additional outlay of capital . A part of the variable capital must be deployed in acquiring these labour-powers that function only in circulation. This capital advance creates neither products nor value. It proportionately reduces the scale on which the capital advanced functions productively. \"It is the same as if a part of the product was transformed into a machine that bought and sold the remaining part of the product. This machine means a deduction from the product.\" It is not involved in the production process, although it can reduce the labour-power spent on circulation. It simply forms a part of the circulation costs. The image of a \"machine that bought and sold the product\" is doubly significant. First, it emphasizes the non-productive character of circulation labor: the machine is part of constant capital, a deduction from the product, and the same is true of circulation labor-power. Second, it foreshadows later analyses (for instance, in Grundrisse and in the \"Fragment on Machines\") in which Marx explores the transformation of living labor into fixed capital and the consequences for the production of surplus-value. The circulation worker is, in a sense, a living component of the constant capital that capital must maintain in order to realize value—a human \"machine\" whose maintenance is a cost, not a source of value. The section concludes with the statement of the fundamental law: \"In all circumstances, the time taken here is a cost of circulation, which does not add anything to the values converted. It is a necessary cost for transferring these from the commodity form into the money form.\" I(b) Book-keeping: Ideality, Symbolic Reflection, and Deduction The function of book-keeping. Marx turns from the physical activity of buying and selling to the ideational apparatus that accompanies it: book-keeping. Book-keeping requires pens, ink, paper, desks, and other office equipment—objectified labor—as well as living labor-power. Thus labor-time is spent in this function both as labor-power and as means of labor. The same general state of affairs obtains as with buying and selling time: the labor is unproductive, and the expenses are deductions. But book-keeping has a distinctive philosophical dimension that Marx is eager to explore. Capital, considered as a unity within its circuits , as value in process, exists in the sphere of production or circulation only ideally —as money of account, \"at first in the head of the commodity producer, capitalist or otherwise.\" Money of account—the unit in which prices are reckoned and accounts kept—is a purely ideal or symbolic existence of value. It is not gold or silver coin; it is the form in which value is represented for the purpose of calculation. By way of book-keeping, which also includes the determination or reckoning of commodity prices (price calculation), the movement of capital is registered and controlled. The movement of production, and particularly of valorization—in which commodities figure only as bearers of value, as the names of things whose ideal value-existence is set down in money of account—\"thus receives a symbolic reflection in the imagination.\" This is a dense passage. It says that capital, as a process, cannot be directly perceived in its totality. It must be represented —symbolically, through accounts, ledgers, balance sheets. The book-keeper performs this representation. The book-keeper is thus the consciousness of capital, its reflective moment, whose function is to maintain the ideal unity of a movement that is scattered across time and space (raw materials in the warehouse, goods in transit, money in the bank, contracts on the market). The historical digression: monasteries and Indian communities. Marx inserts a footnote (from Manuscript II) noting that in the Middle Ages agricultural book-keeping was found only in the monasteries, and that a book-keeper for agriculture already figured in the primitive Indian communities. In the latter, book-keeping gained an independent position as the exclusive function of a communal official. This division of labor saves time, energy, and expense, but production and the book-keeping of production \"remain as separate as the cargo of a ship and the bill of lading.\" In the person of the book-keeper, a portion of the communal labor-power is withdrawn from production, and the costs of its function are replaced, not by the book-keeper's own labor, but by a deduction from the common product. \"Just as with the book-keeper of the Indian community, so the same applies, mutatis mutandis , to the capitalist's book-keeper.\" The historical digression serves two purposes. First, it shows that book-keeping is not a historically specific feature of capitalism but a feature of any large-scale, organized production. Second—and more importantly—it establishes that the unproductive character of book-keeping is a general feature of social accounting, not a peculiarity of capitalist accounting. The book-keeper's labor is necessary, socially useful, and unproductive in the value-theoretic sense. This is not a criticism of book-keeping; it is an analytical classification. The scale argument. Marx emphasizes that the nature of the function is \"in no way changed by the scale that it assumes\" when concentrated in the hands of a capitalist producer. Whether the function is performed by many small producers or by one large capitalist enterprise, whether it is torn loose from the productive functions to which it is an adjunct and becomes the independent function of special agents—none of this changes its character. \"The division of labour, with one function becoming independent in this way, does not make this into a product- or value-forming function if it is not so in itself, and thus was already so before it became independent.\" This is a general methodological principle in Marx's value theory: the formal reorganization of labor—its division, its specialization, its concentration—does not alter the fundamental question of whether it produces value. Value is determined by the quantity of socially necessary labor time embodied in a product, not by the organizational form in which the labor is performed. The distinction between book-keeping and buying/selling time. Marx now introduces a nuanced distinction within the category of pure circulation costs. The costs arising from book-keeping or unproductive expenditure of labor-time differ in one respect from those of mere buying and selling time. The latter—buying and selling time—arise simply from the particular social form of the production process, from the fact that it is a process of production of commodities. If production were not commodity production—if products were not destined for exchange—there would be no buying and selling. Book-keeping, however, \"as the supervision and the ideal recapitulation of the process, becomes ever more necessary the more the process takes place on a social scale and loses its purely individual character; it is thus more necessary in capitalist production than in the fragmented production of handicraftsmen and peasants, more necessary in communal production than in capitalist.\" This is a remarkable claim. It suggests that book-keeping is not merely a consequence of the commodity form; it is a consequence of any large-scale, socialized production. Even in a future communist society—Marx implies—book-keeping would be more necessary than in fragmented handicraft production, though its costs would be reduced with the concentration of production and in proportion to its increasing transformation into social book-keeping. Here Marx departs from a crude \"circulation-costs-are-purely-capitalist\" thesis. Book-keeping has a dual character: it arises partly from the necessity of supervising and recording any complex social process, and partly from the specific need to calculate prices and record transactions under commodity production. The former necessity would persist under socialism; the latter would not. Marx does not develop this thought, but its presence in the text is significant. It shows that his analysis of circulation costs is not a blanket condemnation of all non-production labor but a differentiated critique that distinguishes between costs specific to the commodity form and costs arising from the complexity of social production as such. Concentration and reduction of costs. Marx concludes the section by noting that the costs of book-keeping are reduced with the concentration of production and in proportion to its increasing transformation into social book-keeping. The development of large-scale industry, of standardized accounting practices, of banking, and of credit reduces the relative cost of book-keeping even as its absolute scale grows. This is an anticipation of a theme that will be developed more fully in the sections on stock formation and transport: the productivity of labor in the sphere of circulation can increase, thereby reducing the relative burden of circulation costs on social capital. Marx gives a concrete example in the following section (though it is presented as a bridge): the simple case of the receipt and dispensing of money, once this has become independent as an exclusive function of banks or of cashiers in individual businesses, and is concentrated on a large scale. This illustrates how circulation costs, which in the case of the individual commodity producer are \"only evanescent and scarcely noticeable moments that run alongside his production or are dovetailed in with it,\" may strike the eye as \"massive circulation costs\" when they become independent and concentrated. But—and this is the point—\"these circulation costs do not change their character with their altered form.\" The scale at which a function is performed does not alter its theoretical classification. I(c) Money: Gold and Silver as a Social Cost of Commodity Production The specific character of the money commodity. Marx now turns to the costs of money itself. Whether a product is produced as a commodity or not, it is always a material form of wealth, a use-value destined for individual or productive consumption. As a commodity, its value exists ideally in its price, which does not affect its actual use form. But certain commodities—gold and silver—function as money and, as such, \"dwell exclusively in the circulation process.\" They remain in the circulation sphere as hoard, reserve, means of payment, even if only latently. This is \"purely a product of the particular social form of the production process, as a process of commodity production.\" The argument here is subtle. Gold and silver are commodities , produced by labor, with value. But their function as money is not a natural property of the metal; it is a social property conferred by the commodity form of production. Gold would be a useful metal even if it were not money (for jewelry, for industrial uses). But when gold functions as money, it functions as \"a machine for circulation.\" It enters neither individual nor productive consumption. It is a part of social wealth that is confined to an unproductive form—a form in which it serves merely to facilitate the circulation of other commodities. The growth and replacement costs of money. Since, on the basis of capitalist production, the commodity is the general form of the product, the great mass of products are produced as commodities and must assume the money form. Since the mass of commodities—the part of social wealth functioning as commodities—is constantly growing, the quantity of gold and silver that functions as means of circulation, means of payment, reserve, etc. also increases. A growing portion of social wealth is tied up in the money form. Furthermore, the wear and tear of money requires its steady replacement, or the transformation of more social labor—in the product form—into more gold and silver. These replacement costs are significant in nations where there is developed capitalism, because the part of wealth confined to the form of money is considerable. Marx cites The Economist (8 May 1847): \"The money circulating in a country is a certain portion of the capital of the country, absolutely withdrawn from productive purposes, in order to facilitate or increase the productiveness of the remainder. A certain amount of wealth is, therefore, as necessary in order to adopt gold as a circulating medium, as it is to make a machine, in order to facilitate any other production.\" The citation is revealing. The bourgeois economist of The Economist recognizes—as Marx does—that money is a deduction from productive capital, a necessary cost of a certain form of economic organization. But where The Economist sees this as a natural technical necessity, Marx sees it as a historically specific consequence of commodity production. Gold and silver \"constitute for society costs of circulation that arise simply from the social form of production. They are faux frais of commodity production in general, which grow with the development of this production, and with capitalist production in particular. This is a part of the social wealth which has to be sacrificed to the circulation process.\" Transition to the next section. This completes Marx's analysis of pure circulation costs. The section has established that buying and selling time, book-keeping, and money are all costs that arise from the form of commodity production (and, in the case of book-keeping, also from the social scale of production). They add nothing to the value of commodities. They constitute deductions from the social product. They are faux frais . But Marx now announces that the next category of circulation costs—those dealt with under \"Costs of Storage\"—is \"different in nature.\" They can arise from production processes that are simply continued in the circulation sphere, and whose productive character is thus merely hidden by the circulation form. They may also be nothing but costs from the social point of view—unproductive expenditure of labor—but precisely because of this they still have a value-forming effect for the individual capitalist and form an addition to the selling price of his commodities. This is a crucial pivot. Marx is about to demonstrate that the appearance of value-formation can attach to costs that are, from a social standpoint, pure deductions—and that this appearance is not illusory from the standpoint of the individual capitalist, because competition distributes these costs across the price of commodities. II. Costs of Storage: The Pivotal Distinction Before examining the individual forms of storage costs, Marx lays out the central theoretical claim of the section. The circulation costs that proceed from the mere change in form of value \"do not enter into the value of commodities.\" The portions of capital spent on them constitute mere deductions from the capital productively spent. But the costs of storage are different. They can arise from production processes continued in the circulation sphere (whose productive character is hidden by the circulation form), or they can be unproductive expenditures from the social point of view that nonetheless have a value-forming effect for the individual capitalist because they add to the selling price of his commodities. Marx explains why this appearance of value-formation arises. It \"follows from the simple fact that these costs differ between different individual capitals within the same production sphere. The act of adding them to the price of the commodity means that they become distributed in proportion to the degree to which they occur for the individual capitalist.\" This is a competitive mechanism: if all capitalists must incur certain costs, and if these costs vary across firms, then competition will tend to equalize the rate of profit by allowing the firm with higher costs to charge a higher price (or the firm with lower costs to earn a higher profit). The costs are thus \"socially validated\" through the price mechanism, even though they are not productive of value. Marx then adds a crucial corollary: \"all labour that adds value can also add surplus-value and will always add surplus-value on the basis of capitalism, since the value that it forms is dependent on its own extent, and the surplus-value that it forms is dependent on the extent to which the capitalist pays for it.\" This is the general law of surplus-value production. If a cost involves the employment of labor that adds value (as storage labor may, if it is a continuation of production), then it will also add surplus-value, because the capitalist will pay the laborer less than the value the laborer creates. Thus \"while costs that make commodities dearer without increasing their use-value are faux frais of production from the social point of view, for the individual capitalist they can constitute sources of enrichment.\" The corollary to this is equally important: \"in so far as what they add to the price of the commodity merely distributes these circulation costs equally, they do not thereby cease to be unproductive in character.\" Marx gives the example of insurance companies: they \"divide the losses of individual capitalists among the capitalist class. But this does not prevent the losses thus adjusted from being losses as before, from the standpoint of the total social capital.\" This is a critical analytical distinction that Marx will use throughout the chapter. The social point of view and the individual capitalist's point of view diverge. Storage costs may enrich individual capitalists (by allowing them to charge higher prices) while remaining pure deductions from social wealth. The price mechanism does not create value; it only redistributes it. II(a) Stock Formation in General The dual existence of commodity capital. Marx begins the analysis of stock formation with an observation about the circuit of capital. During its existence as commodity capital—its stay on the market, in the interval between the production process from which it emerges and the consumption process which it enters—the product forms a commodity stock . Commodity capital figures twice in each circuit: once as the commodity product of the actual capital in process whose circuit is under consideration, and once as the commodity product of another capital that must be present on the market in order to be sold and transformed into productive capital. In other words, for any given capital to sell its product and buy means of production, there must be a stock of commodities already on the market—produced by other capitals—waiting to be bought. Marx acknowledges that it is possible for the latter commodity capital to be produced only to order , in which case there is an interruption until it has been produced. But the flow of the production and reproduction process requires that a mass of commodities (means of production) is constantly present on the market—that is, forms a stock. Productive capital similarly includes the purchase of labor-power, and the money form is here only the value form of the means of subsistence that the worker must find for the greater part on the market. The involuntary character of the commodity stock and its costs. Marx takes up the standpoint of a particular capital value that has been transformed into commodity product and must now be sold. For this capital, the state in which it forms a stock is \"an inexpedient and involuntary stay on the market.\" The more quickly it is sold, the more fluid the reproduction process. The delay in the formal transformation (C-M) hinders the material change (M-C, the purchase of new means of production) that must occur in the circuit of capital, and thus its further functioning as productive capital. On the other hand, the constant presence of commodities on the market—the commodity stock—appears for M-C as the condition for the flow of the reproduction process and for the investment of new or additional capital. There is thus a tension: the stock is a burden for the individual capital that holds it (it delays the return to production) but a precondition for the reproduction of capital as a whole. This tension mirrors the more general tension in Marx's analysis between the individual capitalist's perspective and the social perspective. The material costs of maintaining a stock. The persistence of commodity capital as a commodity stock requires buildings, stores, containers, warehouses—an outlay of constant capital . It equally requires payment for the labor-power employed in placing the commodities in their containers. Furthermore, commodities decay and are subject to the damaging influence of the elements. Additional capital must thus be expended to protect them from this, partly in objective form as means of labor, and partly in labor-power. Marx now draws the distinction that defines the section: these circulation costs \"are distinguished from those mentioned under heading 1 in as much as they do enter into the value of commodities to a certain extent, and thus make the commodities dearer.\" Under all circumstances, the capital and labor-power which serve to maintain and store the commodity stock are withdrawn from the direct production process. On the other hand, the capital employed here, including labor-power, must be replaced out of the social product. Hence this outlay has the same effect as a reduction in the productivity of labor , so that a greater quantity of capital and labor is required to obtain a specific useful effect. \"These are simply expenses.\" The dual character of storage costs. Marx now articulates the distinction with precision. In so far as the costs of circulation made necessary by the formation of the commodity stock arise solely from the time taken to transform existing values from the commodity form into the money form—that is, only from the specific social form of the production process (only from the fact that the product is produced as a commodity and must therefore pass through a transformation into money)—they share exactly the same character as the circulation costs enumerated under heading 1. They are pure deductions. On the other hand, however, the value of the commodities is conserved, or increased, only because the use-value—the product itself—is transferred under certain objective conditions that cost an outlay of capital, and subjected to operations in which additional labor works on the use-values. The calculation of the commodity values (book-keeping) and the buying and selling, on the contrary, do not operate on the use-value in which the commodity value exists; they are only concerned with its form. Thus, \"although in the case assumed here these expenses of stock formation (which is here involuntary) arise purely from a delay in the change of form and from the necessity for this change, they are nevertheless distinguished from the expenses under heading 1 in that their actual object is not the formal transformation of value, but the conservation of the value which exists in the commodity as a product, a use-value.\" The use-value is not increased or raised; on the contrary, it declines. But its decline is restricted, and it itself is conserved. The value that is advanced and exists in the commodity is also not increased here. But new labor, both objectified and living, is added to it. This is the crux of the distinction. Storage labor is unproductive in the sense that it does not increase the use-value of the product—the product is decaying, and storage only slows the decay. But it is labor that operates on the product as a use-value, and it does add value (in the sense of transferring the value of the labor-power and means of production consumed) to the conserved product. The value added is, in a sense, a necessary addition to preserve the value already present. Storage labor is thus the labor of conservation , not of creation —but it nonetheless enters the value of the stored commodity. Historical and comparative dimensions: Smith, Lalor, Sismondi. Marx now poses a series of broader questions. How far do these expenses proceed from the particular character of commodity production in general? How far from commodity production in its universal, absolute form, i.e. capitalist commodity production? How far are they common to all social production and simply assume a particular shape, a specific form of appearance, within capitalist production? To answer these questions, he engages with the history of economic thought. Adam Smith put forward the \"incredible opinion\" that the formation of a stock is a phenomenon peculiar to capitalist production. Later economists, e.g. Lalor, stressed that with the development of capitalist production, stock formation declines . Sismondi even regarded this as one of the negative features of capitalist production. Marx's response is that all these positions are one-sided. In point of fact, stock exists in three forms : in the form of productive capital, in the form of the individual consumption fund, and in the form of the commodity stock or commodity capital. Stock declines relatively in one form when it increases in the other, although its absolute size may grow simultaneously in all three forms. Marx then develops the argument in detail. Where production is oriented directly toward the satisfaction of the producers' own requirements, and only a small portion of goods are produced for exchange or sale—where the social product does not assume the commodity form, or does so only to a small extent—the commodity stock forms only a small and evanescent part of wealth. Here, however, the consumption fund , i.e. the fund of means of subsistence, is relatively large. Marx gives the example of the peasant economy of antiquity: an overwhelming part of the product was transformed directly, without forming a commodity stock, into a stock of means of production or means of subsistence, precisely because it remained in the hands of its possessor. Because it did not assume the form of a commodity stock, Adam Smith held that no stock existed in societies based on this mode of production. \"Adam Smith thus confused the form of stock with the stock itself, and believed that society previously lived from hand to mouth, abandoning itself to the hazards of the next day. This is a childish misunderstanding.\" The Indian famine evidence. In a long footnote (from Manuscript II), Marx provides powerful empirical evidence for his claim that the transition from production for the producers' own needs to commodity production gives rise to \"the most violent crises in the producers' economy.\" In India, \"the disposition to hoard largely the grain for which little could be got in years of abundance\" has been observed \"right up to this day\" (quoting Return. Bengal and Orissa Famine , House of Commons, 1867). The sudden increase in demand for cotton, jute, etc. as a result of the American Civil War led to a great limitation of rice cultivation in India, a rise in the price of rice, and the sale of old stocks of rice by the producers. On top of this, there was the unparalleled export of rice to Australia, Madagascar, etc. in 1864–6. Hence the acute character of the famine of 1866, which carried off a million people in Orissa alone. The report stresses \"the drain of old stock\" as one of the causes of the famine. This footnote is important for several reasons. First, it provides concrete historical evidence for a theoretical claim about stock formation. Second, it illustrates the catastrophic consequences of the subordination of subsistence production to commodity production. Third, it shows that the transition from non-commodity to commodity production is not a smooth evolutionary process but a violent dislocation that can destroy the livelihoods of millions. The footnote is, in effect, a materialist history of the Indian famine, grounded in the categories of Marx's theory of circulation. The growth of productive stock. Marx now turns to the second form of stock: stock in the form of productive capital . This exists as means of production that are already engaged in the production process, or at least in the hands of the producer, i.e. latently already in the production process. As the productivity of labor develops, and thus with the development of the capitalist mode of production—which develops the social productivity of labor more than all previous modes of production—the mass of means of production that are incorporated once and for all in the process in the form of means of labor, and function repeatedly in it over a longer or shorter period (buildings, machines, etc.) constantly grows. This growth is both premise and effect of the development of the social productive power of labor. The material forms of existence of the constant capital do not consist only of such means of labor, but also of material for labor at the most varied stages of elaboration, as well as ancillary materials. As the scale of production grows, and the productive power of labor grows through cooperation, division of labor, machinery, etc., so does the mass of raw material, ancillaries, etc. that go into the daily reproduction process. These elements must be ready to hand at the place of production. The extent of this stock in the form of productive capital thus grows absolutely. For the process to keep flowing—quite apart from whether this stock can be renewed daily or only at definite intervals—there must always be a greater store of raw material, etc. at the place of production than is used up daily or weekly. The continuity of the process requires that the existence of its preconditions should depend neither on the possible interruption of daily purchases, nor on whether the commodity product is sold daily or weekly, and can therefore only irregularly be transformed back into its elements of production. Four conditions determining the relative size of the productive stock. Marx now identifies four conditions that determine the relative size of the productive stock, all of which \"essentially all derive from the greater speed, regularity and certainty with which the necessary mass of raw material can be constantly supplied in such a way that no interruption arises.\" First : The general development of capitalist production. The less these conditions are fulfilled, and the less therefore the certainty, regularity, and speed of the supply, the greater must be the latent part of the productive capital—the stock of raw materials, etc. in the hands of the producer and still waiting to be worked up. These conditions stand in inverse proportion to the level of development of capitalist production, and thus of the productive power of social labor. And so too, therefore, does the stock in this form. But Marx immediately qualifies: \"what appears here as a decline in the stock (e.g. with Lalor) is in part only a decline of stock in the form of commodity capital or of commodity stock proper; i.e. a mere change of form of the same stock.\" For example, if a great mass of coal is produced every day in the country in question, i.e. if the scale and intensity of coal production is large, then the mill-owner does not need a great store of coal in order to secure the continuity of his production. The constant and certain renewal of the coal supply makes this superfluous. Second : The development of the means of transport and communication. The speed with which the product of one process can be transferred to another process as means of production depends on this. The cheapness of transport plays a great role. The constantly repeated transportation of coal, for example, from the mine to the spinning mill will be dearer than the storage of a larger amount of coal for a longer period, if transport is relatively cheap. Third : The development of the credit system. The less dependent the mill-owner is for the renewal of his stocks of cotton, coal, etc. on the direct sale of his yarn—and the more developed the credit system, the smaller this direct dependence—the smaller the relative size of these stocks need be, in order to secure a continuous production of yarn independent of the accidents of its sale. Fourth : The production period of raw materials. Many raw materials, semi-finished goods, etc. require lengthy periods of time for their production, and this holds in particular for all raw materials provided by agriculture. If there is to be no interruption of the production process, then a definite stock of these must be present for the whole period of time in which new products cannot replace old. Marx then draws an important conclusion from the combination of these conditions: \"If this stock in the hands of the industrial capitalist declines, this only means that it increases in the form of a commodity stock in the hands of the merchant.\" The development of the means of transport, for example, permits cotton lying in the import docks to be quickly delivered from Liverpool to Manchester, so that the manufacturer can renew his stocks of cotton in relatively small portions according to his needs. But then the same cotton exists in even greater amounts as a commodity stock in the hands of the Liverpool merchants. \"There is thus simply a change in the form of the stock, which Lalor and others have overlooked.\" If we consider the social capital, there is the same quantity of products as before in the form of stock. The apparent decline in stock is an illusion generated by a change in its social form—from productive stock in the hands of the industrialist to commodity stock in the hands of the merchant. For an individual country, the scale on which the quantity needed for the year must be held ready declines with the development of the means of transport. If there are many steamships and sailing ships plying between America and Britain, then the opportunities for Britain to renew its cotton stock are increased, and thus the average volume of the cotton stock that Britain must keep in store declines. The development of the world market and the consequent multiplication of sources of supply for the same article has the same effect. The article is supplied bit by bit from different countries and at different points in time. II(b) The Commodity Stock Proper The growth of the commodity stock with capitalist production. Having established that the apparent decline in productive stock is largely a change of form, Marx now turns to the commodity stock proper. On the basis of capitalist production, the commodity becomes the general form of the product, and the more so, the more this production develops in scale and depth. Thus a far greater part of the product exists as a commodity, even at the same scale of production, in comparison either with earlier modes of production, or with the capitalist mode of production itself at a less developed stage. Every commodity—and thus every commodity capital—in so far as it does not directly pass from the sphere of its production into productive or individual consumption, and finds itself on the market during the interval, forms an element of the commodity stock. In and for itself—assuming the scale of production is constant—the commodity stock therefore grows with capitalist production. This is only a change of form for the stock: it increases in commodity form because it decreases in the form of direct production or consumption stock. There is simply a changed social form of the stock. If at the same time there is an increase not only in the relative size of the commodity stock, in relation to the total social product, but also in its absolute size, this is because the volume of the total product increases with capitalist production. The determination of production by capital, not by demand. Marx now introduces a crucial dynamic factor. As capitalist production develops, the scale of production is determined to an ever lesser degree by the immediate demand for the product, and to an ever greater degree by the scale of the capital which the individual capitalist has at his disposal, by his capital's drive for valorization and the need of his production process for continuity and extension. The mass of products from every particular branch of production that are on the market as commodities, or seek an outlet, necessarily grows together with this. The mass of capital tied up for a shorter or longer time in the form of commodity capital grows, and hence the commodity stock grows as well. This is a crucial point for the theory of crises. Under capitalist production, production is not directly determined by need or demand; it is determined by the drive for valorization and by the scale of capital already accumulated. The result is that the commodity stock grows unavoidably with capitalist development—not because of inefficiency or speculation, but because of the structural logic of capital accumulation. The market must always be oversupplied relative to immediate demand, because production runs ahead of demand in the pursuit of profit. The proletarianization of the population and the consumption fund. Marx adds a further dimension: ultimately, most members of society are transformed into wage-labourers, people who live from hand to mouth, who receive their wages by the week and spend them by the day, and must thus find their means of subsistence available as a stock. \"However rapidly the particular elements of this stock may flow, a part of them must always stand still in order for the stock to remain in motion.\" The transformation of the population into wage-labourers—who have no means of subsistence of their own and must buy everything they consume—magnifies the commodity stock, because the entire consumption fund of the working class must now exist as a commodity stock on the market. Storage costs and their social character. Whatever the social form of the stock of products, its storage involves costs: buildings, containers, etc. which form receptacles for the product; similarly means of production and labor, more or less according to the nature of the product, which must be spent to ward off damaging influences. The more these stocks are socially concentrated, the smaller, relatively speaking, are the costs. These outlays always form part of social labor, whether in objectified or living form—thus in the capitalist form they are outlays of capital—which do not go towards the formation of the product itself, and are thus deductions from it. They are necessary expenditures of social wealth, for they are the costs of conserving the social product, whether its existence as an element of the commodity stock arises merely from the social form of production, i.e. from the commodity form and its necessary transformations, or whether we consider the commodity stock simply as a special form of the stock of products common to all societies, even if not in the form of a commodity stock, this particular form of stock pertaining to the circulation process. The question of value: do storage costs enter into commodity value? Marx now poses the decisive question: \"The question now arises as to what extent these expenses enter into the value of commodities.\" He answers by considering the case of a capitalist who has transformed his advanced capital into products ready for sale, and these remain in store unsold. It is not only the valorization process of his capital that is held up during this time. The expenditures that the conservation of this stock requires in buildings, additional labor, etc. form a positive loss . The eventual purchaser would laugh at the capitalist if he said: \"I could not sell my commodity for six months, and it not only cost me so and so much in idle capital to maintain it for these six months, but also caused expenses x.\" \"So much the worse for you,\" the buyer will say, \"for next to you there is another seller whose commodity was finished only yesterday. Your commodity is evidently a white elephant, and probably more or less damaged by the ravages of time. You must therefore sell cheaper than your rival.\" This vivid passage establishes a crucial point: the expenses of stock formation, in so far as the formation of a stock is a hold-up in circulation , add no value to the commodity. The buyer does not pay for the circulation time of the commodity. Even if the capitalist deliberately keeps his commodity off the market, in times of a real or anticipated revolution in values, it depends on whether this revolution actually comes about, on the correctness or incorrectness of his speculation, whether he realizes his additional expenses. The revolution in values is not the result of his expenses. On the other hand, there can be no stock without a delay in the circulation sphere, without the capital persisting for a longer or shorter period in its commodity form; thus there can be no stock without a hold-up in circulation, just as no money can circulate without the formation of a money reserve. \"That is to say, without the commodity stock, no commodity circulation.\" If the capitalist does not encounter this necessity in C'-M', then he encounters it in M-C; not for his own commodity capital, but for the commodity capital of other capitalists, who produce means of production for him and means of subsistence for his workers. Voluntary vs. involuntary stock formation. Marx now makes a distinction between voluntary and involuntary stock formation. Whether the formation of a stock is voluntary or involuntary makes no essential difference to the matter, but \"it is useful to know, as a contribution towards solving this question, what it is that distinguishes voluntary from involuntary stock formation.\" The involuntary formation of a stock arises from, or is identical with, a hold-up in circulation that is independent of the knowledge of the commodity producer and goes against his intentions. What characterizes voluntary stock formation? Here the seller still attempts to get rid of his commodities as fast as possible. He still offers his product for sale as a commodity. If he were to withdraw it from sale, it would form only a potential (δυνάμει) element of the commodity stock, and not an actual (ἐνεργείᾳ) one. The commodity as such is still for him simply the bearer of its exchange-value, and as such it can only have its effect by and through shedding its commodity form and assuming the money form. The Aristotelian terminology—potential/actual—is significant. It suggests that the commodity stock has a modal dimension: a commodity that is offered for sale but not yet sold is potentially part of the stock, but actually only becomes so when it lingers unsold. The distinction between the two modal states is not a distinction in the physical thing but in its social relation to the process of circulation. The necessary volume of the commodity stock. Marx now examines the determinants of the necessary volume of the commodity stock. The commodity stock must have a certain volume in order to satisfy the scale of demand over a given period. The continual extension of the circle of buyers is taken into account in this connection. In order to last for one day, for example, one part of the commodities on the market must persist in the commodity form, while the other part flows and is transformed into money. Of course the part that stands still in this way steadily declines, as the scale of the stock itself declines, until it is finally all sold. This stagnation of commodities is thus taken into account here as a necessary condition for their sale. Moreover, it must be greater in scale than the average sale or the average demand, otherwise excesses above this average could not be satisfied. On the other hand, the stock must be constantly renewed, because it is constantly disappearing. In the last instance, this renewal can derive only from production, from a supply of the commodity. It is immaterial whether this comes from abroad or not. The renewal depends on the periods that the commodities need for their reproduction. The stock of commodities must be adequate for this length of time. The fact that this stock does not remain in the hands of the original producers, but runs through various reservoirs, from the large-scale merchant to the retail trader, changes only the appearance, and not the thing itself. From the social point of view, a part of the capital still exists in the form of commodity stock, as long as the commodity has not entered into productive or individual consumption. The producer himself attempts to have an inventory adequate for his average demand, in order not to be directly dependent on production, and to secure himself a constant circle of customers. The production periods give rise to dates of purchase, and the commodity forms a stock for a longer or shorter period of time before it can be replaced by new items of the same kind. It is only by way of this stock formation that the permanence and continuity of the circulation process is ensured, and hence that of the reproduction process which includes the circulation process. The producer-merchant distinction. Marx now returns to a point made earlier. C'-M' can be completed for the producer of C even though C is still on the market. If the producer himself intended to keep his own commodity in store until it was sold to the final consumer, he would have to set in motion a double capital, once as producer of the commodity, the other time as merchant. As far as the commodity itself is concerned—whether it is considered as an individual commodity or as a component part of the social capital—it makes no difference to the situation whether the expenses of stock formation fall onto its producers or onto a series of merchants from A to Z. This is an important point for the theory of merchant's capital. The function of storage can be performed by the industrial capitalist or by a separate merchant. The social effect is the same—a portion of social capital is tied up in the form of commodity stock, and a portion of social labor is expended in maintaining it. But the distribution of the burden differs. If the industrial capitalist performs the function, he bears the cost directly. If a merchant performs it, the industrial capitalist may appear to have \"sold\" his commodity and returned to production more quickly, while the merchant bears the cost of storage and the risk of unsold goods. This appearance will be crucial in Volume 3's analysis of merchant's capital. Storage costs as transposed costs of maintaining the social production and consumption funds. Marx now returns to the social point of view. In as much as the commodity stock is nothing more than the commodity form of the stock that would still exist on the given scale of social production either as productive stock (latent production fund) or as a consumption fund (reserve of means of consumption), if it did not exist as a commodity stock, the expenses required to maintain the stock—\"the expenses of stock formation\"—are merely the transposed expenses of maintaining the social production fund and the social consumption fund. The increase in the value of the commodity to which they give rise simply distributes these expenses proportionately between the various commodities, as they are different for different sorts of commodity. The expenses of stock formation continue to be deductions from the social wealth, even though they are a condition of its existence. This is a crucial reformulation. The commodity stock is not a new substance; it is the form assumed by the social product under capitalism. If the same products existed under a non-capitalist social form—say, as direct production stocks and consumption reserves in the hands of producers—they would still require storage costs. The specifically capitalist form of these costs is their appearance as commodity stock and their connection with the circulation process. But the substance of the costs—the labor of conservation—is common to all social production. The costs of storing the social product are therefore not uniquely capitalist; they are a feature of any complex society that produces a surplus and must maintain stocks against future need. What is uniquely capitalist is the form these costs assume and the way they are distributed through commodity prices. Normal vs. abnormal stock formation and the mystification of crises. Marx now introduces a crucial distinction. \"It is only in so far as the commodity stock is a condition of commodity circulation and itself a form that has necessarily arisen in commodity circulation, in so far therefore as this apparent stagnation is a form of the flow itself, that it is normal.\" But once the commodities lingering in their circulation stores fail to make room for the incoming wave of production, and the stores are overfilled, the commodity stock expands as a result of the stagnation of circulation , just as hoards grow if the money circulation stagnates. It is quite immaterial here whether this stagnation takes place in the storeroom of the industrial capitalist or the warehouse of the merchant. The commodity stock is then not a condition of uninterrupted sale, but a consequence of the unsaleability of the commodities . The expenses remain the same, but as they arise purely from the form—from the necessity of transforming the commodities into money, and the difficulty of this metamorphosis—they do not enter into the value of the commodities, but form deductions, a loss of value in the realization of value . This is a crucial distinction with profound implications for the theory of crises. Normal stock formation is a condition of circulation; abnormal stock formation is a symptom of crisis. The two are indistinguishable in their form —both are \"stagnations of circulation.\" Because the normal and the abnormal forms of the stock are not distinguished in their form, \"the phenomena can be confused, and may deceive the agents of production themselves all the more, in that it is possible for the producer to feel that the circulation process of his capital is occurring, that it is in flux, even though the circulation of his commodities, which have passed into the hands of the merchants, is stagnating.\" This is a remarkable passage. It describes the ideological mystification that arises from the credit system. If the extent of production increases, then, other circumstances remaining the same, so does the volume of the commodity stock. It is then renewed and absorbed just as quickly, but on a greater scale. The rise in the volume of the commodity stock as a result of a stagnation in circulation can thus be mistaken for a symptom of an expansion in the reproduction process, particularly if the real movement is mystified by the development of the credit system. In other words: capitalists see their commodity stocks growing, and because credit allows them to continue producing even when goods are not being sold, they interpret the growth of stocks as evidence of economic growth rather than as a warning sign of overproduction. The credit system, which in one respect facilitates the circulation of capital, in another respect conceals the realities of the circulation process, allowing crises to develop unnoticed until they burst. This is a direct prefiguration of Marx's theory of crises and his critique of the credit system in Volume 3. The three components of stock formation costs. Marx concludes the section with a simple enumeration: \"The expenses of stock formation consist of (1) a quantitative reduction in the mass of the product (e.g. with stocks of flour); (2) a deterioration in quality; (3) the objectified and living labour required to conserve the stock.\" This three-part enumeration summarizes the material reality of storage: it always involves some physical loss, some qualitative degradation, and the expenditure of labor and means of production. III. Transport Costs The general law restated. Marx opens the final section by restating the general law: \"all circulation costs that arise simply from a change in form of the commodity cannot add any value to it. They are simply costs involved in realizing the value or transferring it from one form into another. The capital expended in these costs (including the labour it commands) belongs to the faux frais of capitalist production. The replacement of these costs must come from the surplus product, and from the standpoint of the capitalist class as a whole it forms a deduction of surplus-value or surplus product, in just the same way as the time that a worker needs to buy his means of subsistence is lost time for him.\" But transport costs, Marx says, \"play too important a role not to be briefly considered here.\" And the consideration reveals that transport is fundamentally different from the other circulation costs. It is not a change of form; it is a change of location . And a change of location, unlike a change of form, requires labor that transforms the physical world and therefore adds value. The metabolism of social labor and the distinction between circulation and transportation. Marx introduces a crucial concept: within the circuit of capital and the commodity metamorphoses that form a section of it, the metabolism of social labor takes place. By \"metabolism\" (Stoffwechsel), Marx means—as the editorial footnote explains, referencing Volume 1, Chapter 7, p. 283—the exchange of matter between man and nature effected by labor. This metabolism may require a motion of the products in space, their real movement from one location to another. But—and this is the crucial analytical point—circulation of commodities can take place without their physical movement, just as the transport of products can take place without commodity circulation, even without direct exchange of products. Marx gives two examples. First: \"A house that is sold by A to B circulates as a commodity, but it does not get up and walk.\" The circulation of property title is distinct from physical movement. Second: \"Movable commodity values, such as cotton or pig-iron, can remain in the same warehouse while they undergo dozens of circulation processes, and are bought and resold by speculators. What actually moves here is the property title to the thing and not the thing itself.\" Marx cites Storch, who calls this circulation factice (artificial circulation). Conversely: \"In the realm of the Incas, on the other hand, the transport industry played a major role, although the social product neither circulated as a commodity nor was distributed by means of exchange.\" Transport existed as a material process without commodity circulation. The conclusion is that \"if the transport industry therefore appears as a cause of circulation costs on the basis of capitalist production, this particular form of appearance in no way alters the substance of the matter.\" Transport is a production process , not a circulation process—even though, under capitalism, it appears as a cost of circulation because it occurs in the sphere between production and consumption. Transport as a production process that adds value. Marx now argues directly that transport adds value. \"The quantity of products is not increased by their transport.\" This is true—the number of tons of coal remains the same whether the coal is at the pithead or at the factory. \"The change in their natural properties that may be effected by transport is also, certain exceptions apart, not an intended useful effect, but rather an unavoidable evil.\" (For example, wine that is transported may age and improve, but this is an exception, not the rule; transport generally degrades the product, by breaking, spoiling, or shaking it.) But: \"the use-value of things is realized only in their consumption, and their consumption may make a change of location necessary, and thus also the additional production process of the transport industry. The productive capital invested in this industry thus adds value to the products transported, partly through the value carried over from the means of transport, partly through the value added by the work of transport.\" This latter addition of value can be divided, as with all capitalist production, into replacement of wages and surplus-value. This is the fundamental argument: transport is a production process because it creates a use-value —the availability of the product at the place where it is to be consumed. A ton of coal at the pithead and a ton of coal at the factory are materially identical, but their use-values are different, because the factory cannot consume the coal at the pithead. The labor of transport therefore produces a real use-value, and hence real value, in the Marxian sense. It is a productive labor process that happens to occur within the sphere of circulation. The universality of transport within production. Marx emphasizes that transport is not confined to the sphere of circulation. \"Within every production process, the change of location of the object of labour and the means of labour and labour-power needed for this plays a major role; for instance, cotton that is moved from the carding shop into the spinning shed, coal lifted from the pit to the surface.\" The transfer of the finished product as a finished commodity from one separate place of production to another a certain distance away shows the same phenomenon, only on a larger scale. The transport of products from one place of production to another is followed by that of the finished products from the sphere of production to the sphere of consumption. \"The product is ready for consumption only when it has completed this movement.\" This is crucial: the readiness for consumption is itself a use-value created by transport. A product that has not reached the consumer is not fully a product; it is a product in potentia . Transport realizes the product's potential use-value. The inverse relation between productivity and value in transport. Marx now states the general law of commodity production and applies it to transport: \"it is a general law of commodity production that the productivity of labour and the value it creates stand in inverse proportion. This holds for the transport industry as much as any other. The smaller the quantity of labour, dead and living, that is required to transport a commodity for a given distance, the greater the productive power of the labour, and vice versa.\" Marx cites Ricardo's critique of Say. Say had argued that commerce is a blessing because it increases the transport costs of products, thereby raising their value: \"Commerce enables us to obtain a commodity in the place where it is to be found, and to convey it to another where it is to be consumed; it therefore gives us the power of increasing the value of the commodity, by the whole difference between its price in the first of these places, and its price in the second.\" Ricardo objected: \"True, but how is this additional value given to it? By adding to the cost of production, first, the expenses of conveyance; secondly, the profit on the advances of capital made by the merchant. The commodity is only more valuable, because more labour is expended on its production and conveyance, before it is purchased by the consumer. This must not be mentioned as one of the advantages of commerce.\" Marx endorses Ricardo's critique: transport adds value because it adds labor, not because it moves the commodity from a place of low price to a place of high price. The increase in value is a cost , not a benefit. Say's mistake—which Marx attributes to his \"vulgar-economic doctrine of the 'factors of production'\"—is to confuse the creation of value with the realization of a price differential. The editorial footnote (from the Marx-Engels editors) elaborates on Say's \"law\" of markets (supply creates its own demand), noting that it reigned supreme in bourgeois economics from 1817 to the Keynesian revolution, and that even Keynes's theory is itself simply one of under-consumption, teaching that the problem of over-production can be solved by an increase in the supply of money. This editorial commentary, while not part of Marx's text, highlights the continuity between Marx's critique of Say and later critiques of Keynesian economics. The determinants of transport value. Marx now states the quantitative relations governing transport costs with precision: \"The absolute magnitude of value added by the transport of commodities stands in inverse proportion to the productive power of the transport industry and in direct proportion to the distance to be covered, other circumstances remaining the same.\" This is a clear formula: V = L / P × D , where V is the value added, L is the quantity of labor, P is the productive power of the transport industry, and D is the distance. The value added increases with distance and decreases with productivity. \"The relative part of value that transport costs add to the price of the commodity, under otherwise equal circumstances, stands in direct proportion to their size and weight.\" This is why bulky, heavy, low-value commodities (like coal, grain, ore) are expensive to transport relative to their value, while small, light, high-value commodities (like jewelry, electronics) are cheap to transport relative to their value. \"The modifying circumstances are numerous. Transport requires, for example, greater or lesser measures of precaution, hence more or less expenditure of labour and means of labour, according to the relative fragility, perishability and explosiveness of the article.\" This sets up Marx's satirical passage on the railway classification of goods. \"The railway magnates have shown greater genius in inventing fantastic species than have botanists or zoologists. The classification of goods on the British railways, for example, fills volumes, and rests for its general principle on the tendency to transform the variegated natural properties of goods into an equal number of transportation ailments and pretexts for obligatory impositions.\" Marx quotes a manufacturer's testimony: \"Glass, which was formerly worth £11 per crate, is now worth only £2 since the improvements which have taken place in manufactures, and since the abolition of the duty; but the rate for carriage is the same as it was formerly, and higher than it was previously, when carried by canal. Formerly, manufacturers inform me that they had glass and glass wares for the plumbers' trade carried at about 10s. per ton, within 50 miles of Birmingham. At the present time, the rate to cover risk of breakage, which we can very rarely get allowed, is three times that amount... The companies always resist any claim that is made for breakages.\" The railway magnates' exploitation of the value relation. Marx now draws a satirical conclusion from the quantitative relations he has established. \"Moreover, the fact that the relative share that transport costs add to the value of an article stands in inverse proportion to its value is made by the railway magnates into a special reason for taxing an article in direct proportion to its value. The complaints of the industrialists and merchants on this score are repeated on every page of the evidence in the above-quoted report.\" This is a brilliant observation. The railway companies recognize that the relative burden of transport costs falls more heavily on low-value goods. But instead of lowering rates for low-value goods, they raise them, on the theory that the transport cost is a smaller fraction of the value of high-value goods and therefore those goods can bear a higher rate. In other words, they use the analytical relation Marx has established—that transport costs are relatively more burdensome for low-value goods—as a pretext for price discrimination . The \"classification of goods\" is not a neutral technical necessity but an instrument of monopoly exploitation. This passage is significant because it shows Marx applying the abstract theory of value and transport costs to the concrete analysis of a specific industry (railways) and a specific form of exploitation (freight rate discrimination). It demonstrates that the theory is not merely academic but provides tools for understanding the concrete behavior of capitalist firms. The contradictory effects of capitalist development on transport costs. Marx concludes the section with a passage that captures the dialectical character of capitalist development. \"The capitalist mode of production reduces the transport costs for the individual commodity by developing the means of transport and communication, as well as by concentrating transport—i.e. by increasing its scale. It increases the part of social labour, both living and objectified, that is spent on commodity transport, firstly by transforming the great majority of all products into commodities, and then by replacing local by distant markets.\" This is a remarkable summary. Capitalist development has two opposing effects on transport costs: It reduces the cost per commodity by improving and concentrating transport. It increases the total social labor spent on transport by transforming more products into commodities and by extending markets from local to global. The net effect is that a larger and larger portion of social labor is devoted to transport, even as the cost per unit of product declines. This is a general pattern in the development of capitalism: the \"social overhead\" of circulation grows even as its efficiency increases. Capitalist development does not eliminate the faux frais of circulation; it expands them, both absolutely and relatively, even as it reduces them per unit of product. The final analytical statement. The section—and with it the chapter—ends with a concise characterization of the transport industry's peculiar status: \"The 'circulating' of commodities, i.e. their actual course in space, can be resolved into the transport of commodities. The transport industry forms on the one hand an independent branch of production, and hence a particular sphere for the investment of productive capital. On the other hand it is distinguished by its appearance as the continuation of a production process within the circulation process and for the circulation process.\" This concluding passage captures the double character of transport. It is (a) an independent branch of production , a sphere in which capital is invested productively and in which value and surplus-value are produced according to the general laws of capitalist production. But it is (b) distinguished by its appearance —it appears to be a continuation of a production process within the circulation process. This appearance is not illusory (transport genuinely occurs within the sphere of circulation, and it genuinely adds value); it is, rather, a manifestation of the unity of production and circulation in the total circuit of capital. Transport is where the material metabolism of society—the physical movement of products—becomes visible as a part of the circulation process, and hence where the theoretical distinction between production and circulation reveals itself to be a distinction within the unity of the total reproduction process, not a rigid separation. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles Chapter 6 establishes several fundamental principles of Marx's theory of circulation, each with far-reaching consequences for the rest of the Critique of Political Economy : First: Circulation labor is unproductive in the strict sense—but necessary. The labor of buying, selling, book-keeping, and money-handling does not create value, does not add to the use-value of the product, and is not a moment of the production process. It is a faux frais of commodity production—a deduction from the social surplus. But this does not mean it is unnecessary or dispensable. Under commodity production, value must be realized, and realization requires circulation, and circulation requires labor. The unproductive character of this labor is a cost of the system , not a criticism of the individuals who perform it. Second: The character of a labor process is determined by its content, not by its location or organizational form. Whether labor is performed by the producer himself or by a hired specialist, whether it is performed within a large firm or by a small independent operator, whether it is concentrated or dispersed—none of this determines whether it produces value. The content of the labor—whether it transforms a use-value in a way that creates a new use-value, or merely facilitates the exchange of existing use-values—is what matters. This principle is crucial for the theory of merchant's capital in Volume 3, where Marx will argue that the merchant's labor is unproductive despite the merchant's centrality to the functioning of capitalist production. Third: The social and individual points of view diverge systematically. Costs that are deductions from social wealth may be sources of enrichment for individual capitalists (via the price mechanism), and costs that enrich individual capitalists may be pure deductions from social wealth. The price mechanism does not create value; it only redistributes it. This principle is essential for understanding how costs that are \"unproductive\" from a social standpoint—storage, insurance, warehousing—can appear as legitimate business expenses that enter into the price of commodities. Fourth: The development of capitalism transforms the form of circulation costs even as it maintains their substance. The apparent decline in productive stock (Lalor), the reduction in book-keeping costs through concentration, the transformation of the commodity stock from a productive reserve to a speculative holding—all of these are changes in the form of circulation costs, not in their fundamental nature . The substance—the expenditure of social labor on non-productive functions—persists. Fifth: Transport is a production process occurring within the circulation sphere. This is perhaps the most important theoretical innovation of the chapter. Transport adds value because it creates a use-value (the availability of the product at the place of consumption), and it is therefore a productive labor process. But it occurs within the sphere of circulation, and it appears as a cost of circulation. The transport industry thus reveals the unity of production and circulation in the total circuit of capital—a unity that Marx's analysis has been building toward throughout Volume 2. Broader Practical and Theoretical Implications For the theory of crises. Marx's analysis of the distinction between normal and abnormal stock formation is directly relevant to his theory of crises. Normal stock formation is a condition of smooth circulation; abnormal stock formation is a symptom of overproduction. Because the two are indistinguishable in their form , and because the credit system allows producers to continue producing even when goods are not being sold, the growth of the commodity stock can be mistaken for evidence of economic growth. This is a mechanism of ideological mystification —the credit system conceals the buildup of unsold goods until the crisis breaks. The chapter thus provides the theoretical foundation for the analysis of commercial crises that Marx will develop in Volume 3 and in Theories of Surplus-Value . For the theory of merchant's capital. The chapter establishes the framework for the analysis of merchant's capital in Volume 3. If buying and selling labor is unproductive, and if the merchant is merely a specialist in this unproductive labor, then the merchant's profit cannot be derived from the productivity of the merchant's labor. It must be derived from the share of surplus-value that the merchant extracts by virtue of his position in the circuit of capital. The merchant's capital is a deduction from the total surplus-value, not an addition to it. This analysis is anticipated here in the discussion of the circulation worker as \"a machine that bought and sold the product\"—a machine that is part of the constant capital of the capitalist class, a deduction from the product. For the critique of apologetic economics. Marx's engagement with Quesnay, Smith, Lalor, Sismondi, Say, and the railway magnates is not merely historical. It is a methodological demonstration of how abstract economic categories (value, use-value, circulation, production) can be applied to concrete phenomena (famines, freight rates, warehouse costs, credit bubbles) to reveal the underlying material relations. The chapter thus serves as a model of Marxist method: rigorous theoretical analysis combined with concrete historical and empirical investigation. For the theory of socialist economy. Marx's distinction between costs arising solely from the commodity form (buying, selling, money, speculative stock formation) and costs arising from the material requirements of social production (storage, transport, book-keeping as supervision) has profound implications for the theory of socialist economy. Under socialism, commodity production would be abolished (or at least radically transformed), and with it the faux frais of buying and selling, the costs of money, and the speculative commodity stock. But the material costs of storage, transport, and social book-keeping would persist , because they are costs of conserving and moving the social product, not costs of its commodity form. Marx gestures toward this in his remarks on book-keeping under communal production, where he notes that the costs of book-keeping are \"more necessary in communal production than in capitalist\" (though they are \"reduced with the concentration of production\"). The implication is that socialism would reduce but not eliminate the administrative and logistical costs of social production; it would eliminate the faux frais of commodity circulation while retaining the necessary costs of material reproduction. For the philosophy of value. Finally, the chapter raises a philosophical question that runs throughout Marx's work: what is the relation between value and form ? Marx's argument is that value is a material relation—it is the expenditure of socially necessary labor time—but that it appears only in forms (money, price, commodity stock) that are historically specific and socially constructed. The task of the critique of political economy is to see through these forms to the material relations they express. The chapter on circulation costs is a masterclass in this kind of analysis: it takes the most mundane phenomena of capitalist business life—buying, selling, book-keeping, storing, transporting—and reveals them as expressions of the fundamental contradiction between value and its forms of appearance. The result is not a rejection of these phenomena but a scientific explanation of them, grounded in the labor theory of value and in the historical analysis of the capitalist mode of production. The chapter thus achieves what Marx set out to do: to show that the costs of circulation are neither random nor natural, neither inexplicable nor irrevocable. They are the necessary costs of a specific social form of production—a form that produces value in production and realizes it in circulation, and that must therefore bear the costs of this realization. To understand these costs is to understand the limit of capitalist rationality: capital can reduce the costs of circulation per unit of product, but it cannot eliminate them, because they are inherent in the commodity form itself. The only way to transcend them is to transcend the commodity form—that is, to transcend capitalism. This is the deepest implication of the chapter, and it is one that Marx leaves his readers to draw for themselves. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 5 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 7"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 7", "title": "Chapter 7", "subtitle": "The Turnover Time and the Number of Turnovers", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 7/Chapter 7 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 5190, "read_time": "26 min read", "tags": ["Turnover of Capital & Cycles"], "keywords": ["Turnover Time and Number of Turnovers", "Turnover = Production Time + Circulation Time", "Annual Rate of Turnover", "Measure of Capital Cycle Speed", "Turnover Formula (U = B / u)"], "text": "Chapter 7 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,102 words ⏱️ ~26 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 7, titled \"Turnover Time and Number of Turnovers,\" is the opening chapter of Part Two of Capital , Volume 2—the section devoted to The Turnover of Capital . Its deceptively modest title conceals a pivotal conceptual operation: the transformation of the circuit of capital (the single, isolated path traced by a capital value through its successive forms) into the turnover of capital (that same circuit understood not as a one-off act but as a periodic, endlessly repeated process ). This distinction is the chapter's central intellectual contribution. A circuit, the author insists, is \"only a section that is constantly repeated, i.e. a period.\" Once the circuit is grasped as repetition, capital acquires a genuinely new property that it did not possess as a mere circuit: it acquires a tempo , a periodicity, a measurable rhythm—what the text names its turnover time . The chapter's core thesis is threefold. First, turnover time is quantitatively identical to the sum of production time and circulation time ; it is nothing more than the total interval elapsing \"from the moment that the capital value is advanced in a particular form until the return of the capital value in process in the same form.\" Second, this interval is measurable against a natural unit —just as the working day is the natural measuring unit for the function of labour-power, the year is the natural measuring unit for the turnover of capital, grounded in the biological fact that the staple food crops of the temperate zone (the historical birthplace of capitalist production) are annual products. Third, from these two determinations flows a compact but consequential formula: if the year is designated U , the turnover time of a given capital u , and the number of turnovers n , then n = U/u . This formula converts the qualitative drama of capital's metamorphoses into a single calculable ratio of speed. But the chapter is not merely arithmetic. Before it can define turnover, it must settle a prior, more delicate question of form : which of capital's three circuits— M...M' (money capital), P...P (productive capital), or C'...C' (commodity capital)—is the appropriate frame for analyzing turnover? The chapter answers that turnover analysis must begin from the advance of capital value and must require its return in the form advanced, which disqualifies circuit III (C'...C') and mandates the use of circuits I and II, with circuit I (M...M') serving as the primary lens for questions of surplus-value formation and circuit II (P...P) for questions of product formation. This is a methodological purification: the chapter clears the conceptual ground so that the phenomena of speed, repetition, and periodicity can be studied cleanly. It closes by flagging that two new forms—forms capital acquires \"as a result of the circulation process\"—must be examined before turnover's effects on production and valorization can be traced, a deferral that opens the door to the analysis of fixed and circulating capital. Within the broader architecture of Volume 2, then, this chapter functions as the threshold : it completes the analysis of the circuit (Part One) by showing how the circuit, when taken as periodic, yields the category of turnover, and thereby lays the foundation for Part Two's investigation of how turnover speed governs the annual rate of surplus-value, the scale of accumulation, and ultimately the reproduction of the total social capital. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 7.1 The Operational Definition of Turnover Time and the Valorization Imperative The chapter opens by recalling and consolidating a definition from the preceding analysis rather than introducing a new one: \"the overall time of circulation of a given capital is the sum of its circulation time proper and its production time.\" These two components must be kept rigorously distinct. Production time is the time during which capital is actually functioning in the sphere of production—being transformed, worked upon, combined with labour-power—while circulation time proper is the time during which capital is suspended between its successive forms, undergoing purchase and sale, lying in the market, or in transit. The chapter's definitional formula is that turnover time measures \"the period of time that elapses from the moment that the capital value is advanced in a particular form until the return of the capital value in process in the same form.\" Everything hinges on the phrase in the same form . The capital must return not merely as some value, and not merely as more value, but specifically in the identical formal shape in which it set out. This strict \"same-form\" requirement is what makes the advance-and-return cycle a closed loop , and it is precisely this closure that is repeatable, and therefore periodic. The author immediately grounds this in the purpose of capitalist production: \"The specific purpose of capitalist production is always the valorization of the value advanced.\" The point deserves emphasis because it explains why the cycle cannot simply stop at recovery. It is not enough that the value come back; it must come back valorized —increased by surplus-value. And this valorization must be possible regardless of the initial form: whether the value is \"advanced in its independent form, i.e. the money form, or in commodities, in which case its value form only possesses an ideal independence in the price of the commodities advanced.\" Here a subtle technical concept appears: ideal independence . When capital starts as commodities rather than money, those commodities' value does not stand before the capitalist as tangible coin; it exists only as a price —an ideal (thought, accounted) magnitude attached to goods. The underlying value is real, but its formal existence is, so to speak, notional until sale converts it into money. This matters because it prefigures the chapter's later argument about the capitalist's bookkeeping consciousness . In all cases, the text stresses, \"this capital value passes through different forms of existence in the course of its circuit. Its identity with itself is established in the capitalist's ledger, or in the form of money of account.\" This is a profound claim about how capital's continuity is represented : the transformations (money→commodities→production→new commodities→money) are heterogeneous, qualitative swerves, but the capitalist tracks them as one and the same value through the device of money of account —the abstract unit of reckoning in which debits and credits are recorded. The unity of capital is thus not something directly visible in any single form; it is an accounting fact , an identity maintained across qualitatively distinct embodiments. This observation quietly sets up the later critique of economists who mistake one circuit for the whole truth. 7.2 What Circuits M...M' and P...P Have in Common The chapter now isolates what the two principal circuits share, asking us to treat the first two circuits— M...M' and P...P —as a pair before differentiating them from the third. Here the notations require glossing. M denotes capital value advanced in money form; the prime ( M' ) denotes the valorized money, i.e., M plus surplus-value; the ellipsis ... denotes the intervening transformations (purchase, production, sale). P denotes capital in its productive form—the elements of production, i.e., means of production and labour-power—and P...P consequently denotes the circuit that starts and ends in the productive form. The author asserts that both forms include two facts: \"(1) the value advanced functions as capital value and is valorized; (2) after describing its process, it returns to the form in which this process began.\" The first fact establishes that in both circuits the starting quantity behaves as capital —it grows through the extraction of surplus-value—rather than as mere hoarded wealth or mere consumption fund. The second fact establishes the formal closure : the endpoint reoccupies the starting shape. The text then shows that these two facts, though obvious in M...M', are equally true of P...P—a point requiring care because P...P can appear, superficially, to be a purely \"productive\" affair with no obvious money bookends. The argument runs: \"the starting-point of P is the presence of the elements of production, commodities of a given value. This form includes the valorization of this value (C and M') and its return to its original form, since in the second P the value advanced once again possesses the form of the elements of production in which it was originally advanced.\" In other words, even though P...P never begins with money, its internal trajectory passes through the commodity form ( C , the product) and the money form ( M' , the sale proceeds), and it closes by reconstituting itself as a fresh batch of production elements—the \"second P.\" So valorization and same-form return are interior to P...P just as they are exterior in M...M'. To cement this, the chapter invokes the authority of its own earlier analysis, quoting Volume 1, Chapter 23: \"If production has a capitalist form, so too will reproduction. Just as in the capitalist mode of production the labour process appears only as a means towards the process of valorization, so in the case of reproduction it appears only as a means of reproducing the value advanced as capital, i.e. as self-valorizing value.\" The import is that reproduction under capitalism is not neutral replenishment—it is the renewal of a self-expanding value. The worker does not merely reproduce the means of subsistence; the process is structurally bent toward reproducing the capital relationship. This grounds the claim that every circuit, regardless of its starting form, is fundamentally a valorization loop. 7.3 The Three Circuits Differentiated: M...M', P...P, and C'...C' Having established the commonality of the two main circuits, the chapter turns to differentiation. The three circuits \"are distinguished in the following ways.\" The first distinction concerns whether repetition is expressed as reality or mere possibility . \"In form II (P...P) the repetition of the process, the process of reproduction, is expressed as a reality, whereas in form I it is only a possibility.\" The reasoning is elegant. Because P...P begins with production elements (machinery, raw materials, labour-power), its very starting point presupposes that a previous production process generated those elements—reproduction is built into its premise. By contrast, M...M' begins from money, which could equally well fund consumption or hoarding; that it should flow back into a new production cycle is contingent , a possibility rather than a certainty. Money does not announce its own origin in production the way production elements do; hence repetition is \"only a possibility\" in circuit I. This is a crucial nuance: the form of the starting point encodes information about the necessity of repetition. The second distinction separates circuits I and II from circuit III ( C'...C' ). In circuits I and II, \"the capital value advanced—whether as money, or in the shape of the material elements of production—forms the starting-point and hence also the point of return.\" This is the decisive structural criterion for turnover: the cycle is anchored in an advanced capital value that must be recovered. Circuit III begins differently—not with an advance but with an already-valorized result . This asymmetry between advance and prior result is the hinge on which the whole chapter—and indeed the choice of analytical frame—turns. 7.4 The Disqualification of C'...C' from Turnover Analysis The chapter now subjects circuit III to close scrutiny in order to exclude it from turnover analysis. In M...M', \"the return is M' = M + m.\" Here m is the surplus-value—the increment generated over and above the advanced M. The text notes that \"if the process is repeated on the same scale, then M again forms the starting-point; m does not enter into it, but simply shows us that although M has been valorized as capital and thus created a surplus-value, it has cast this surplus-value off.\" This is a statement about simple reproduction : when the capitalist consumes the surplus rather than reinvesting it, the next advance is again M, and the surplus-value m is expelled from the circuit's starting point. The money cycle thus visibly spits out its surplus—a transparency that makes circuit I analytically convenient. In P...P, \"the capital value P advanced in the form of the elements of production forms the point of departure.\" This form includes valorization. In simple reproduction, \"it is the same capital value that begins its process again in the same form.\" In accumulation, however, something changes: \"P' (possessing a value of M' or C') now starts the process as an increased capital value. But the process still begins with capital value advanced in the original form, even if with a greater value than previously.\" The crucial phrase is in the original form . Even under accumulation, where the cycle restarts at a higher magnitude, the formal starting point remains the advance of capital value. The form of the beginning is invariant even as its size grows. Circuit III is qualitatively different: \"the capital value does not begin the process as capital value advanced, but as capital value already valorized, as the total wealth existing in the form of commodities, of which the capital value advanced forms only a part.\" This is the decisive defect. C' (the prime again signaling valorization) represents everything the production cycle threw off—not merely the original capital but the surplus and, indeed, the totality of commodity wealth. Its starting point is a result , not an advance . The text acknowledges that this very feature makes circuit III \"important for Part Three of the present volume, where the movement of individual capitals will be dealt with in its relationship with the movement of the total social capital.\" That is, when analyzing society-wide reproduction—where the question is how all commodity products flow among all capitals—the C' starting point is the natural one, since society's starting datum is indeed the mass of finished commodities. But \"it cannot be used for the turnover of capital, which always begins with the advance of capital value... and always requires the return of the circling capital value in the form in which it was advanced.\" The same-form return criterion is violated by C'...C', because C' does not return in the form it \"started\" (it started as a result) and because the requirement of recovering the advanced value cannot be traced when the advance is invisible. Thus circuit III is set aside—not because it is false, but because it answers a different question. 7.5 The Methodological Choice Between Circuit I and Circuit II The chapter's resolution is to use both surviving circuits, each for a distinct analytical purpose. \"Out of circuits I and II, the former will be adhered to in so far as the influence of the turnover on the formation of surplus-value is the main thing under consideration; the latter in so far as its influence on the formation of the product is concerned.\" This is a deliberate division of labour. Circuit I (M...M') foregrounds money and surplus-value, so it is the ideal instrument for studying valorization dynamics —how fast surplus-value is generated, realized, and reinvested. Circuit II (P...P) foregrounds the elements of production and their product, so it is the instrument for studying physical product-formation —how the turnover of machinery, materials, and labour shapes the flow of output. Rather than forcing one frame onto all problems, the author keeps two frames in reserve, matching each to the question it best illuminates. This dual-track method is a hallmark of the whole volume. 7.6 The Economists' Confusion and Its Documentary Evidence Having settled the correct analytical terrain, the chapter turns polemically against earlier economists: \"Just as the economists have rarely distinguished between the different forms of the circuit, so too they have rarely considered these separately in connection with the turnover of capital.\" The charge is a failure of formal discrimination —a tendency to collapse the distinct circuits into one another and thereby obscure the specific determinants of turnover. Two examples are marshalled. The dominant error is fixation on M...M': economists \"have generally concentrated on the form M...M' because it is this that dominates the individual capitalist and is used by him in his calculations, even if money forms the starting-point only in the shape of money of account.\" Here the earlier observation about the ledger returns with critical force: because the individual capitalist thinks in money terms—because his books are kept in money of account—his practical standpoint systematically biases him (and the economists who adopt his standpoint) toward the money circuit, even when actual money transcends into commodities that are merely priced . The accountants' viewpoint is thus revealed as a partial one, valid for the capitalist's calculations but not exhaustive of capital's forms. A second, opposite error is then exposed. \"Certain others proceed from outlays in the form of elements of production, finishing with the receipt of returns, without even mentioning the form of these returns, whether they are in commodities or money.\" This is the error of starting from P and ending vaguely—recognizing the productive outlay but leaving the formal character of the return unspecified. To illustrate, the chapter quotes S. P. Newman , Elements of Political Economy (Andover and New York, p. 81): \"The Economic Cycle... [is] the whole course of production, from the time that outlays are made till returns are received. In agriculture, seed-time is its commencement, and harvesting its ending.\" Newman's formulation captures the temporal span but remains formally indeterminate about what kind of thing the \"returns\" are—a conceptual looseness the chapter implicitly faults. The agricultural metaphor (seed-time to harvest) is telling, however, because it anticipates the chapter's own use of annual harvests as the natural temporal anchor for turnover. The third and most confused tendency is to start from C' (circuit III). The text cites Thomas Chalmers , On Political Economy (2nd edn, Glasgow, 1832, p. 85), who describes the \"economic cycle\" as beginning from the point \"at which the capitalist has obtained those returns by which his capital is replaced to him,\" and then tracing the workmen, wages, finished articles, the market, and finally a sale that \"terminates the orbit of one set of movements.\" Chalmers's cycle explicitly starts from returns already obtained , i.e., from a valorized result—precisely the C' standpoint the chapter has disqualified for turnover purposes. A footnote amplifies the critique with heavy irony: Chalmers (1780–1847) \"is described by Marx in Theories of Surplus-Value (Part I, p. 290) as 'one of the most fanatical Malthusians.' Like Malthus, he was himself a cleric, and in fact Professor of Divinity at Glasgow University.\" The polemical sting is that this clerical Malthusianism produces a formal muddle—a cycle that begins at the end—mirroring a broader theoretical carelessness. 7.7 The Circuit as Periodic Process: Turnover Formally Defined With the critical ground cleared, the chapter delivers its central definition. \"When the entire capital value that the individual capitalist invests in one branch of production or other has described its cyclical movement, it exists once again in its original form and can then repeat the same process. It has to repeat it, if the value is to be perpetuated and valorized as capital value.\" Two modalities of necessity appear: the capitalist can repeat (formal possibility established by closure) and has to repeat (the imperative of self-valorization). The deontic \"has to\" is important—capital that stops turning ceases to be capital; its value must be continuously valorized or it degrades into hoard. Then the decisive conceptual leap: \"In the life of the capital, the individual circuit forms only a section that is constantly repeated, i.e. a period.\" The circuit becomes a period —a unit in a recurring series. The text traces this for each surviving circuit: \"At the close of the period M...M', the capital exists again in the form of money capital and passes once more through the series of changes of form that constitute its process of reproduction and valorization.\" And \"At the close of the period P...P, the capital exists again in the form of the elements of production which constitute the premise of its repeated circuit.\" From here, the definition crystallizes: \"The circuit of capital, when this is taken not as an isolated act but as a periodic process, is called its turnover. The duration of this turnover is given by the sum of its production time and its circulation time. This period of time forms the capital's turnover time. It thus measures the interval between one cyclical period of the total capital value and the next; the periodicity in the capital's life-process, or, if you like, the time required for the renewal and repetition of the valorization and production process of the same capital value.\" Three things are packed into this passage. First, turnover is defined as the circuit qua periodic process —the same event, differently regarded. Second, its duration is the sum of production and circulation time—tying the new category back to the definition with which the chapter opened. Third, turnover time measures interval ( between one cycle and the next), periodicity (the rhythm of capital's \"life\"), and renewal time (how long before the same value must be re-advanced). The phrase \"renewal and repetition of the valorization and production process\" emphasizes that turnover involves both valorization and production—i.e., both the value-magnifying and the physically-producing faces of capital, echoing the earlier decision to employ circuits I and II respectively. 7.8 The Year as the Natural Measuring Unit The chapter now confronts the problem of measurement : turnover time is a duration, but durations need units. The text approaches this by analogy: \"As the working day forms the natural measuring unit for the function of labour-power, so the year forms the natural measuring unit for the turnovers of capital in process.\" The parallel is exact and instructive: labour-power, when functioning, is naturally reckoned in working days ; capital, when turning over, is naturally reckoned in years . Both units are grounded in nature rather than convention. The chapter supplies the material basis for this choice: \"The natural basis for this measurement is that the most important food crops in the temperate zone, the native ground of capitalist production, are annual products.\" This is a materialist—indeed agro-biological—justification. Capitalist production arose in the temperate zone, and its agrarian base depended on crops whose full vegetative cycle occupies exactly one year. The annual rhythm of sowing and harvest thus imposed a natural one-year periodicity on the reproduction of the most fundamental means of subsistence, and by extension on the economic rhythm built atop it. The year is therefore not an arbitrary bookkeeping convenience but is derived from the physical conditions of production itself . (Note the resonance with Newman's seed-time-to-harvest imagery from section 7.6: the quotation was not accidental.) 7.9 The Turnover Formula: n = U/u and Its Worked Examples With the unit fixed, the chapter formalizes the measure. \"If we call the year, as measurement unit of the turnover time, U , the turnover time of a particular capital u , and the number of its turnovers n , then n = U/u .\" The variables are now fully specified: U = the standard measuring unit (normally the year, treated as 12 months). u = the turnover time of the particular capital in question (expressed in the same units as U). n = the number of turnovers the capital completes within the measuring unit. The formula is a ratio of available time to cycle length , yielding the count of completed cycles . The text immediately supplies worked examples to fix the intuition. Example one (sub-annual turnover): \"If the turnover time u is three months, for example, then n = 12/3 = 4; the capital completes four turnovers in a year, or turns over four times.\" A capital whose production-and-circulation cycle lasts only a quarter completes four full valorization episodes per year—rapid rotation. Example two (super-annual turnover): \"If u = 18 months, then n = 12/18 = 2/3; the capital only gets through two thirds of its turnover time in one year.\" Here the cycle is longer than the measuring unit, so the resulting count is a fraction —less than one full turnover per year. The number of turnovers need not be a whole number; it can express a partial completion, capturing the fact that capital in slow sectors (e.g., forestry, heavy construction) may take more than a year to go round once. Example three (multi-year turnover): \"If the turnover time amounts to several years, then it is reckoned in terms of multiples of a year.\" When u spans several years, turnover is expressed as a multiple—e.g., a five-year cycle yields n = 1/5 turnover per year. So the formula scales gracefully across extremely fast, ordinary, and glacially slow capitals, always reducing them to a common yardstick of annual turnover frequency . A methodological caveat precedes the examples: \"If we disregard the individual occurrences that may accelerate or shorten the turnover time of an individual capital, the turnover times of capitals differ according to their different spheres of investment.\" This is essential. Individual accidents—a lucky quick sale, a transport delay—can perturb any single capital's turnover. Abstracting from these idiosyncrasies, the systematic determinant of turnover time is the sphere of investment : different branches of production (bakeries vs. shipyards vs. vineyards) have structurally different production and circulation times, hence structurally different turnover rates. This is the seed of the entire subsequent analysis, in which turnover differences across branches will drive differences in the annual rate of surplus-value. 7.10 The Capitalist's Practical Viewpoint and the Chapter's Transition The chapter closes by translating the abstract category into the capitalist's lived experience: \"For the capitalist, the turnover time of his capital is the time for which he has to advance his capital in order for this to be valorized and for him to receive it back in its original shape.\" This restatement emphasizes advance duration —the length of the interval during which capital is tied up, unavailable, \"in the hands of\" the process. For the practical capitalist, turnover time is fundamentally a matter of how long his money is locked away before returning amplified. This subjective-practical register complements the objective-formal register of the definition and prepares the reader to think about turnover in terms of tie-up and release of capital. Finally, the chapter announces its own incompleteness. \"Before we investigate more closely the influence of turnover on the production and valorization process, we have to consider two new forms which capital obtains as a result of the circulation process, and which affect the form of its turnover.\" This is the hinge into the following chapter. The two \"new forms\" arise from the circulation process itself —they are not mere repetitions of the M, P, C distinctions already established, but genuinely new determinations of how capital is parcelled and cycled. (They will turn out to be the distinction between fixed capital and circulating capital , whose differing turnover forms —fixed capital turning over only piecemeal through wear-and-tear, circulating capital turning over wholly in each cycle—reshape everything the formula n = U/u can mean in practice.) The sentence is a deliberate cliffhanger: the arithmetic machinery of turnover has been constructed, but before it can be applied to valorization, the internal composition of capital—how it divides into slow-turning and fast-turning portions—must be analyzed. Thus the chapter hands off precisely at the point where its statistical abstraction must give way to material differentiation. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter yields several fundamental principles. First, form determines what can be measured . Before turnover can be quantified, the analyst must choose the correct circuit (M...M' or P...P, not C'...C'), because only circuits anchored in an advance and closed by a same-form return can be treated as repeatable periods. This is a general epistemological lesson: the categories you deploy constrain the phenomena you can see. Circuit III is not false—it is essential for social-capital analysis—but it is incommensurate with turnover analysis. Marx thus models a disciplined pluralism of abstractions, matching frame to question rather than imposing one schema universally. Second, capital is defined by motion, not by substance . The chapter's insistence that capital \"has to repeat\" its circuit shows that capital is not a stock of things but a process that must continuously valorize or perish. Turnover time, the periodicity of that process, is therefore not an incidental property but an essential dimension of capital's existence: a capital is partly characterized by how fast it turns. Speed enters the definition of the thing itself. Third, the natural world underwrites economic categories . The choice of the year as measuring unit is grounded in the annual crop cycle of the temperate zone—the \"native ground of capitalist production.\" This is a striking instance of historical materialism's method: economic measurement conventions are traced back to material-biological facts. The abstract ratio n = U/u rests, at bottom, on seeds and seasons. Fourth, abstraction is a tool of purification, not evasion . By \"disregarding individual occurrences,\" the chapter isolates the sphere of investment as the systematic determinant of turnover. This sets up the entire subsequent argument: because different branches have different structural turnover rates, and because surplus-value is generated in production but realized in circulation, the speed of turnover will govern how much surplus-value a given capital extracts per year. The \"annual rate of surplus-value\"—a category Marx will develop at length in later chapters—is the direct payoff of the formula constructed here. A capital that turns over four times a year can realize its surplus four times over, in principle, within the same calendar span; a capital locked into an 18-month cycle realizes only two-thirds of one turnover. Turnover speed thus becomes a material lever on profitability, independent of the rate of exploitation itself—a vital point for understanding why capitalists constantly strive to accelerate circulation (through credit, transport, marketing) and why crises can be triggered by turnovers unexpectedly slowing. Fifth, and more subtly, the chapter exposes a standpoint bias built into accounting itself . Because the individual capitalist keeps books in money of account, he perceives capital through the money circuit—and economists, adopting his practical consciousness, have reproduced his blind spot. This is a methodological warning of enduring value: the categories most natural to economic actors may be the least adequate for theory. Money of account, an evidently \"real\" datum in the ledger, is shown to be an ideal representation of value, capable of masking the productive and commodity forms on which it depends. Finally, the chapter's deliberate incompleteness is itself instructive. By ending on the announcement of \"two new forms,\" it demonstrates that analytical progress is staged : one cannot simply apply turnover arithmetic to real capitals until one has grasped fixed and circulating capital. The implication is that the rate n = U/u is only a scaffold ; the true richness of turnover theory lies in the differentiated turnover of capital's parts —machines versus materials—and their divergent rhythms. In sum, Chapter 7 transforms the circuit from a linear narrative of metamorphoses into a tempo , infusing capital with a temporal life, and thereby converts economic analysis from a static anatomy of value into a dynamics of speed—speed that will prove decisive for the rate of surplus-value, the rhythm of accumulation, and the stability of the entire capitalist system. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 6 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 8"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 8", "title": "Chapter 8", "subtitle": "Fixed Capital and Circulating Capital", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 8/Chapter 8 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 12011, "read_time": "60 min read", "tags": ["Fixed vs Circulating Capital", "Turnover of Capital & Cycles"], "keywords": ["Fixed Capital vs Circulating Capital", "Wear and Tear of Fixed Assets (Physical vs Moral)", "Transfer of Fixed Capital Value in Fractions", "Circulating Capital (Raw Materials & Wages)", "Capital Advanced vs Capital Consumed"], "text": "Chapter 8 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 11,978 words ⏱️ ~60 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER ANALYSIS: CHAPTER 8 — FIXED CAPITAL AND CIRCULATING CAPITAL (Capital, Volume II) 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem Chapter 8 of Volume II of Capital opens the substantive analytical machinery of the second volume — the analysis of the turnover of capital — by resolving one of the most notoriously muddled questions in the entire history of political economy: what exactly distinguishes fixed capital from circulating capital ? Marx's core intellectual objective is sweeping and polemical. He must demonstrate that this distinction: Is not a material or physical property of things (a machine is not \"fixed capital\" inherently; a raw material is not \"circulating capital\" inherently); Is not synonymous with the distinction between constant capital and variable capital (a conflation he attributes as a \"basic error\" to the classical economists); Arises exclusively from the peculiar manner of circulation of value within productive capital — specifically, from whether the value of a means of production is transferred to the product gradually (bit by bit) or entirely (at once); Exists only as an internal antithesis within the sphere of productive capital , and does not apply to money capital or commodity capital, despite the misleading temptation to call those \"circulating capital.\" The chapter thus functions as the conceptual foundation stone on which the remainder of Volume II's treatment of turnover, circulation time, and the reproduction of the total social capital will be built. The Core Thesis Stated Precisely Marx's thesis is deceptively austere: The formal characteristic that gives a portion of capital value the character of fixed capital lies exclusively in the specific manner in which that value circulates. Everything else — durability, immobility, use-value, material form — is either derivative or irrelevant. The distinction is formal-economic , not concrete-material . Marx immediately illustrates this with the most important baseline insight of the chapter: all capital is constantly in circulation , and \"in this sense, therefore, all capital is circulating capital.\" But the way one portion of advanced capital circulates is peculiar. That peculiarity — that a part of value remains fixed in the means of labour while it functions, departing only gradually — is what constitutes fixedness. How the Chapter Fits the Broader Framework Volume I established that capital is a social relation expressed through the production of surplus-value. Volume I, Chapter 8 (the \"constant and variable capital\" distinction) explained how capital value is preserved and newly created in production . Volume II shifts attention to the circulation and turnover of that value — how the capital advanced returns to its starting point, in what time, and in what form. Chapter 8 is the very first hinge: before Marx can analyze the turnover of capital as a whole (Chapters 9–17), he must decompose productive capital into its two turnover-differentiated functional forms. Everything downstream — the \"working period,\" \"production time,\" \"circulation time,\" the \"annual rate of surplus-value,\" and the reproduction schemas — depends on the clean conceptual separation established here. Furthermore, the chapter performs a crucial polemical function. By dissecting the errors of Adam Smith (to follow in Chapters 10–11), Ramsay, Lorenz von Stein, and others, Marx isolates a persistent ideological confusion: the tendency to naturalize capital — to treat specific things as inherently capital in fixed or fluid form. Marx insists that things \"are never capital at all in themselves\" and cannot \"already in themselves and by nature be capital in a definite form.\" This connects Chapter 8 to the deepest thread of Marx's critique of political economy: the critique of fetishism , extended now into the analysis of capital's turnover. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS I.1 — The Founding Distinction: Means of Labour as Bearer of Fixed Capital Marx begins by recalling Volume I, Chapter 8 (p. 311), where the fundamental distinction was drawn: one part of constant capital maintains its specific use-form over and against the products it helps to fashion. It repeats the same functions across a series of repeated labour processes. This is what Marx collects under the term means of labour — factory buildings, machines, and similar items. The critical mechanism introduced here is the proportional transfer of value . The means of labour \"gives up value to the product in proportion to the exchange-value that it loses together with its use-value.\" Two interlocked claims operate here. First, value transfer and use-value consumption are strictly correlative: the means of labour loses value only as it physically wears out. Second, the rate of this transfer is determined by \"an average calculation\" — measured by the average duration of its function , from the moment it enters the production process to the moment it is \"completely used up, is dead, and has to be replaced or reproduced by a new item of the same kind.\" This establishes an important methodological point: the transfer of value is not instantaneous and observable like a wage payment; it is a statistical average — a social average, implicit in accounting and reproduction. This already signals that fixed capital is a social-temporal category, not a merely technical one. The Signature Peculiarity of Means of Labour. Marx now isolates the defining feature: a part of capital is advanced in a form of constant capital (means of production) which functions as a factor of the labour process for as long as it retains its independent use-shape. Here the contrast sharpens dramatically: The finished product — and the elements of its formation, insofar as they are transformed into the product — is ejected from the production process and passes as a commodity from production into circulation. The means of labour , by contrast, \"never leave[s] the production sphere once [it has] stepped into it.\" Its function \"confines [it] firmly within it.\" A part of the advanced capital value is thus fixed in this form, determined by the function of the means of labour in the process. As the means of labour functions and is used up, one part of its value passes over to the product while another part remains fixed in the means of labour and hence in the production process. The chapter now states one of its most quoted laws: \"the proportion in which it gives up value is always in inverse ratio to the overall duration of its function.\" Marx gives a concrete arithmetic example: two machines of equal value, one wearing out in five years, the other in ten — the first gives up twice as much value in the same time as the second. This is the quantitative expression of durability: longevity is inversely related to the rate of value transfer. The longer the means of labour lasts, the greater the stock of fixed capital value still tied up in it at any moment, and the greater the difference between capital employed and capital consumed in the process. This difference is analytically crucial to Marx. It is the conceptual seed of the money reserve fund that will be developed later. Once the difference disappears (the machine fully depreciated), the means of labour has \"lost its value together with its use-value\" and ceased to be a bearer of value. I.2 — The Peculiar Circulation of Fixed Capital vs. All Other Capital Marx now confronts an apparent paradox: the value fixed in means of labour also circulates. Since the whole of capital value is in constant circulation, \"in this sense... all capital is circulating capital.\" But the circulation of the fixed part is peculiar in at least two senses: It does not circulate in its use-form. Only its value circulates — gradually, bit by bit, in proportion as it is transferred to the product that circulates as a commodity. A part of its value always remains fixed in the means of labour as long as it continues to function, remaining \"distinct from the commodities that it helps to produce.\" It is this very peculiarity — value circulating without the use-form circulating, and value remaining fixed while simultaneously shedding portions — that \"gives this part of the constant capital the form of fixed capital.\" All other material components of the capital advanced in production \"form, by contrast to it, circulating or fluid capital.\" Terminology note. Throughout, Marx uses \"circulating capital,\" \"fluid capital,\" and \"fluent capital\" effectively interchangeably (this translator renders it largely as \"fluid\"). The semantic point is that \"circulating\" must not be confused with \"in circulation in the abstract sense\" — it means a specific pattern of value-turnover. I.3 — The Case of Ancillaries (Auxiliary Materials): Ramsay's Confusion Marx now introduces a third category that has historically confounded the distinction: ancillaries (also called auxiliary materials) — materials consumed by the means of labour proper as they function (coal for a steam engine) or that only support the action (gas for lighting). These: Do not enter the product in their material form; Contribute only their value to the product; Have their value circulated by the product. How they resemble fixed capital: their value is circulated by the product without their material form entering it — this they share with fixed capital. How they differ decisively: They are completely consumed in every labour process they enter , and must therefore be completely replaced by new items of the same kind each time. They do not preserve their independent use-shape. Consequently, no part of the capital value remains fixed in their old use-shape . Marx's target here is Sir George Ramsay (1800–1871), who, in An Essay on the Distribution of Wealth (Edinburgh, 1836), classified ancillaries as fixed capital. Marx identifies two errors in Ramsay: (1) confusing fixed with constant capital; (2) reasoning from the (correct) observation that ancillaries do not materially enter the product and that their function is confined to the production sphere. But Marx shows this reasoning misidentifies the criterion: what matters is not whether material enters the product but whether any value remains fixed in a persisting use-shape. Since ancillaries are wholly consumed, no value remains fixed. Broader footnote context: Marx considered Ramsay \"one of the last representatives of classical (bourgeois) political economy,\" though his classification goes astray here. I.4 — Raw Materials, Means of Enjoyment, and the Means of Transport Exception Marx then addresses the part of means of production that does enter the product materially — raw materials, etc. — which thereby receive a form in which they can later enter individual consumption as means of enjoyment. Means of labour, by contrast, are consumed only productively and cannot enter individual consumption because they do not enter the product/use-value they help fashion. The exception: the means of transport (railways, etc.). The use-effect they produce during their stay in the production sphere — the change of location — simultaneously enters individual consumption, e.g., that of the traveller, who pays for their use just as he pays for other means of consumption. Marx warns that the distinction between raw material and ancillaries \"can become blurred,\" citing chemical manufacture (Volume I, p. 288). The same blurring applies to the distinction between means of labour on the one hand, and ancillaries and raw materials on the other. His first worked example is agriculture: materials added to improve the soil partly enter the plant product as formative elements. Their effect is spread over four to five years. Part therefore enters the product materially and immediately transfers its value; another part remains fixed in its old use-form, so its value does too, and it receives the form of fixed capital. The celebrated ox example: an ox as a draught animal is fixed capital; if it is eaten, it functions neither as a means of labour nor as fixed capital. This compact example demonstrates decisively that fixed capital is functionally determined , not materially inherent. I.5 — The Formal Criterion Named and the Commodity/Fixed Capital Distinction Marx draws his formal conclusion explicitly: \"The quality that gives a part of the capital value spent on means of production the character of fixed capital, lies exclusively in the specific manner in which this value circulates .\" This manner of circulation arises from the way the means of labour gives up value (or acts to form value); which in turn arises from the special way the means of labour functions in the labour process. A pivotal consequence: \"It is only the function of a product as a means of labour in the production process that makes it fixed capital. It is in no way fixed capital in itself, just as it emerges from a process.\" Marx's illustration: a machine produced by a machine-builder is part of the builder's commodity capital . It only becomes fixed capital \"in the hands of its buyer, the capitalist who employs it productively.\" This destroys the fetishistic notion that capital attributes inhere in things. I.6 — Durability, the Difference Between Employed and Consumed Capital Marx reintroduces the durability-dependence: assuming all else equal, the degree of fixedness grows with durability. On durability depends the size of the difference between the capital value fixed in means of labour and the part given up to the product in repeated labour processes. The more slowly value is given up, the greater the capital still fixed, and the greater the gap between capital employed and capital consumed. When this difference disappears, the means of labour has \"lived out its time.\" Marx generalizes: if a means of production that is not a means of labour in the strict sense (ancillaries, raw material, semi-finished goods) behaves with respect to value-transfer and circulation the same way as a means of labour, then it too is a material bearer of fixed capital. The example: soil improvements putting chemical components into the ground whose effect extends over several production periods or years — one part of value continues to exist in independent form (fixed capital), another circulates with the product. Crucial nuance: In such a case (e.g., fertilizer in the soil), \"it is not only a part of the value of the fixed capital that enters the product, but also the use-value, the substance, in which this portion of value exists.\" This distinguishes it from the typical means of labour, whose material does not enter the product. This is a subtle gradation — Marx is acknowledging a mixed case that nonetheless falls under the formal criterion. I.7 — The Errors of Previous Economists: The Polemic on Demarcation Marx now names the \"basic error\" that underlies virtually all prior confusion: the confusion of the categories of fixed and circulating capital with the categories of constant and variable capital . He then lists the specific secondary sources of confusion: First error: Turning material properties of means of labour into direct properties of fixed capital — e.g., physical immobility (like a house). Marx's refutation is elegantly symmetrical: other means of labour which are fixed capital have the opposite property (ships, locomotives, draught cattle — all physically mobile). Hence immobility cannot be the criterion. Second (related) error: Confusing the formal-economic characteristic arising from the circulation of value with a concrete ( dinglich ) property — \"as if things, which are never capital at all in themselves, could already in themselves and by nature be capital in a definite form, fixed or circulating.\" Marx invokes Volume I, Chapter 7 (pp. 283–8): in any labour process, irrespective of social conditions, means of production divide into means of labour and object of labour . But it is only within the capitalist mode of production that these two become capital — specifically \"productive capital.\" Here the natural-labor-process distinction is reflected in the new form of the distinction between fixed and circulating capital. \"It is only in this way that a thing that functions as means of labour becomes fixed capital.\" Mobility vs. immobility re-examined. Marx gives the full dialectical treatment: Fixed in place by physical necessity or design: machines held fast once installed; general conditions of labour; improvements to the soil, factory buildings, blast furnaces, canals, railways — produced from the start in a static, location-bound form. Mobile yet still fixed capital: locomotives, ships, draught cattle — constantly change physical place yet are engaged throughout in the production process. Hence: \"Immobility does not give it the character of fixed capital in the one case, nor does mobility remove this character in the other.\" But Marx introduces a materially significant consequence of genuine immobility: those means of labour fixed in location \"with their roots in the soil\" have a special role in a nation's economy. They cannot be sent abroad or circulate as commodities on the world market. Property titles to them can change — bought and sold, circulating \"ideally,\" even on foreign markets as shares. But a change in the persons who own this fixed capital \"does not change the relationship between the static and materially fixed part of the wealth of a country and the movable part of it.\" This is the germ of Marx's later analysis of the fictitious capital of shares and the geographical-national anchoring of fixed capital. Length of time as a false criterion. Marx then disposes of the claim that temporally extended fixation makes fixed capital. The mere length of time for which a means of production is fixed in repeated, related, continuous labour processes (forming a production period) — i.e., the total production time needed to complete the product — already involves a longer or shorter advance for the capitalist. But \"this alone does not make his capital fixed capital.\" Example: seed is not fixed capital , but simply raw material fixed in the production process for approximately a year. All capital functioning as productive capital is fixed in the production process — \"whatever may be their material shape, their function, or the mode of circulation of their value.\" Whether they are fixed longer or shorter, according to the kind of production process or intended useful effect, \"is not what makes the distinction.\" A footnote here (note 1) targets Lorenz von Stein (Right Hegelian, later economist and Professor of Public Law at Kiel), who concluded that the fixed/circulating distinction is made \"purely for ease of presentation.\" Marx's footnote observes that Stein's The Socialism and Communism of Contemporary France (1842) gave impetus to the radical German movement but \"despite occasional claims to the contrary it had no influence on Marx's own development\" — a curt editorial dismissal. Documentary note (note 2): Up to this point the manuscript is Manuscript IV ; from here on it is Manuscript II — important for scholars tracing the composition of Volume II (Engels' editorial reconstruction). I.8 — The Peculiar Turnover of Fixed Capital and the Money Reserve Fund Marx now moves from the form of value to the time-structure of return. The peculiar circulation of fixed capital generates a peculiar turnover . The portion of value lost in natural form by wear and tear circulates as a value-portion of the product. Through circulation, the product becomes money; and the value-portion of the means of labour circulated by the product becomes money as well. The dual existence of value. The value \"trickles from the circulation process as money in the same proportion that this means of labour ceases to be a bearer of value in the production process.\" Thus value acquires dual existence : part tied to the use-form/natural form (production process); part separated off as money. As the means of labour functions, the natural-form part steadily declines while the money-form part steadily increases , until the means of labour expires and \"its entire value has separated off from its dead body and been transformed into money.\" The quantitative example: A machine worth £10,000 lasting ten years has a turnover time of ten years. Until then it is not renewed but continues to function in natural form. Its value circulates bit by bit as a portion of commodity value and is gradually converted into money, until at the end of ten years it has been completely transformed into money and back into a machine — completing its turnover. Until the reproduction time arrives, its value is accumulated gradually in the form of a money reserve fund (also called amortization fund later). Key structural point about the two transformations. The transformation of its value into money accompanies step by step the transmutation of the commodity that bears its value. But its transformation back from money into use-form is separate from the transformation of the commodity back into elements of production and is determined by its own reproduction period — the time for which the means of labour serves until replacement. This decoupling of the two metamorphoses is the formal core of fixed capital's peculiar turnover. I.9 — The Circulating Components of Productive Capital Marx now delineates the remaining elements of productive capital: (a) constant capital existing in ancillaries and raw materials; (b) variable capital laid out in labour-power. He recalls Volume I, Chapter 7: these components behave differently in the formation of products and value. Ancillaries/raw materials transfer value; labour-power adds an equivalent or reproduces its value and adds surplus-value (ignored for now). He notes internal variations that are irrelevant to turnover : some ancillary material (coal for heating, gas for lighting) is consumed without entering the product, while another part enters the product bodily. But \"all these differences are irrelevant... as far as circulation and hence the mode of turnover are concerned.\" Insofar as ancillaries and raw materials are completely consumed in forming their product, they transfer their entire value, which is completely circulated via the product, transformed into money, and back into elements of production. \"Its turnover is not interrupted, like that of the fixed capital, but passes continuously through the entire circuit of its forms,\" so these elements are constantly renewed in kind. I.10 — Variable Capital and the Labour-Power/Means-of-Subsistence Distinction Labour-power is bought for a definite period. Once incorporated into the production process, it forms a component of capital — the variable component. It functions daily, adding to the product its entire daily value plus surplus-value (ignored). When bought for a week and used, the purchase must continually be repeated. The equivalent of its value added to the product, transformed into money as the product circulates, must constantly be transformed back from money into labour-power — must \"describe the complete circuit of its forms, i.e. turn over\" — if production is not to be interrupted. The value advanced for labour-power thus completely passes to the product, describes the two metamorphoses, and remains permanently incorporated in production via constant renewal. Despite labour-power's different role in value-formation, its mode of turnover is shared with the non-fixed constant components. Because of this common characteristic in turnover, the portions of value spent on labour-power and on non-fixed means of production confront fixed capital as circulating or fluid capital. The crucial theoretical clarification (against a naive reading). Marx insists: the money the capitalist pays is \"only the general equivalent form of the worker's necessary means of subsistence.\" Materially, variable capital consists of means of subsistence — but here, considering turnover, we are dealing with the form. What the capitalist buys is not the worker's means of subsistence but his labour-power . What he productively consumes is labour-power, not means of subsistence. The worker converts money into means of subsistence himself, to reproduce labour-power — just as the capitalist converts surplus-value into his own means of subsistence, without anyone claiming the buyer pays him in means of subsistence. Even where wages are partly paid in kind, this \"nowadays forms a second transaction\": the worker sells labour-power for a price, then separately buys commodities. The second transaction is between worker-as-buyer and capitalist-as-seller; the first between worker-as-seller (of labour-power) and capitalist-as-buyer. Marx's analogy: it is \"just as if the capitalist had had his commodity replaced by another commodity, e.g. as if he replaced the machine that he sells to an iron works by iron.\" Conclusion: It is not the worker's means of subsistence, nor his labour-power , that acquires the characteristic of fluid capital, \"but rather the portion of the value of the productive capital that is spent on it, that has this characteristic in the turnover in common with some components of the constant part of the capital, and in contrast with other parts.\" This is an anti-fetishistic tour de force : it prevents the confusion of variable capital's material basis (subsistence goods) with its economic form (advance of value for labour-power). I.11 — The Turnover Time of Fluid Capital and Its Repeated Advance The value of fluid capital — both in labour-power and means of production — is advanced \"only for the time that it takes to produce the product, according to the scale of production which is given by the volume of the fixed capital.\" It enters entirely into the product, returns completely with sale, and can be advanced afresh. Labour-power and means of production are withdrawn from circulation in the quantity needed for the finished product but must be constantly replaced/renewed by new purchases. Comparison of periodicity and quantity : elements of fluid capital are \"withdrawn from the market at any one time in smaller quantities than are the elements of fixed capital, but they must be withdrawn again all the more frequently.\" The advance is repeated at shorter intervals, mediated by the regular conversion of the product. Not only value but material form continuously describes the whole circuit of metamorphoses. Marx notes that labour-power also constantly adds surplus-value, circulated and monetized alongside other value elements — but he explicitly brackets this, since \"what we are concerned with in the first instance is the turnover of the capital value, and not that of the surplus-value.\" I.12 — The Four Conclusions Marx consolidates the section with four formal propositions: (1) The formal characteristics of fixed and fluid capital arise only from the different turnovers of the capital value or productive capital functioning in the production process. This difference in turnover arises from the different ways the various components transfer their value to the product — not from their different share in producing value, nor from their characteristic behavior in the valorization process. The different ways of giving up value ultimately arise from \"the different material shapes in which productive capital exists, one part being consumed entirely in forming the particular product, while another is used up only gradually.\" Crucial corollary: Only productive capital can be divided into fixed and fluid. \"This antithesis does not exist for the two other modes of existence of industrial capital, neither for commodity capital nor for money capital, nor yet as an antithesis between these two and productive capital.\" Money capital and commodity capital, however fluidly they circulate, \"can become fluid capital in contrast to fixed only when they have been transformed into the fluid components of productive capital.\" Because these two forms inhabit the circulation sphere, economists \"ever since Adam Smith\" have been misled into classing them with the fluid part of productive capital under \"circulating capital.\" They are \"certainly capital of circulation in contrast to productive capital, but they are not circulating capital in contrast to fixed capital.\" This is a preview of the polemic against Smith in Chapters 10 and 11. (2) The turnover of the fixed component encompasses several turnovers of the fluid components. In the time fixed capital turns over once, fluid capital turns over several times. The fixed component receives its formal characteristic only insofar as its means of production are not used up in the time to produce and eject the product — a part of its value must remain tied up in the old persisting use-form while another circulates, and the product simultaneously circulates the total value of the fluid components. (3) The value laid out on fixed capital is advanced all at once in its entirety for the whole functioning period. The capitalist casts this value into circulation all at once; but it is withdrawn again only gradually, bit by bit, via the realization of value-portions added bit by bit to commodities. The means of production themselves are withdrawn from circulation all at once, incorporated into production for the whole period of functioning, without needing replacement during that time. They \"contribute for a longer or shorter time to the formation of the commodities thrown into circulation, without withdrawing from circulation the elements of their own renewal.\" Hence no new advance is required during this period. And the capital value laid out as fixed capital does not pass through the circuit of its forms materially , but only in its value — and only partially and gradually . The reconversion of money into natural form occurs only at the end of the functioning period. (4) The elements of fluid capital are \"just as permanently fixed in the production process — if this is to be continuous — as are the elements of fixed capital.\" The difference: fluid elements fixed in this way are \"steadily renewed in kind\" (means of production by new items; labour-power by ever-repeated purchases), whereas fixed elements are neither renewed as long as they last, nor must their purchase be repeated . Raw and ancillary materials are constantly present but always as new items , the old consumed. Labour-power is constantly present but only with constant repetition of purchase (often with change of persons). \"However the very same buildings, machines, etc. carry on functioning in the same repeated production processes while the fluid capital turns over repeatedly.\" This fourth conclusion elegantly demonstrates that duration or physical continuity of presence in production is not the criterion; the criterion is mode of value-turnover and renewal . II.1 — Components, Replacement, Repairs and Accumulation: The Differentiation of Lifespans Marx opens the second section with the empirical/structural observation that different elements of fixed capital in one investment have differing lifespans , hence different turnover times. His extensive example is the railway : rails, sleepers, earthworks, station buildings, bridges, tunnels, locomotives, carriages — all function for different periods with different reproduction times, hence different turnover times. \"Works of art\" (buildings, platforms, water tanks, viaducts, tunnels, cuttings, embankments) need no renewal for a whole series of years. The quickest wearing are the permanent way and rolling stock . II.2 — The Historical Data on Railway Wear and Tear Marx brings in rich empirical documentation from the Royal Commission on Railways (London, 1867; quotations marked \"R.C.\") and industrial literature. The historical narrative: Initial expectations: eminent practical engineers believed railways would last \"for centuries\"; 100–150 years was considered the life of good rails; the wear and tear of tracks was thought negligible for financial and practical purposes. Reality: rail life depends on locomotive speed, weight and number of trains, rail thickness, and secondary circumstances — no more than twenty years on average ; at certain stations and heavy-traffic centres, rails wore out each year . Around 1867: steel rails introduced; though costing about twice as much as iron, they last more than twice as long. Wooden sleepers: twelve to fifteen years. Rolling stock: goods wagons wear out significantly quicker than passenger carriages. Locomotive life (1867): estimated at between ten and twelve years. Three sources of wear and tear are itemized: (a) Actual use. Rails wear in proportion to the number of trains (R.C., no. 17645). More precisely, wear increases by more than the square of the speed : if the speed of trains doubles, wear increases more than fourfold (R.C., no. 17046). This is a striking early quantitative law of capital consumption. (b) Natural forces. Sleepers deteriorate from rot, not just use. R. P. Williams ( On the Maintenance and Renewal of the Permanent Way , paper read at the Institute of Civil Engineers, Autumn 1866; published in the Money Market Review , 2 December 1867): \"A month of severe winter would do more damage to the road of a railway than a year's traffic.\" Here Marx notes that maintenance costs depend less on traffic wear than on the quality of wood, iron, bricks, and mortar exposed to the atmosphere. (c) Moral deterioration. As throughout large-scale industry, this plays a part. After ten years, one can generally buy the same quantity of carriages and locomotives for £30,000 as previously cost £40,000. A depreciation of 25 per cent on the market price must be reckoned with, even absent any depreciation in use-value ( Lardner , Railway Economy , p. 120). Also: \"Tube bridges will not be replaced in their present form\" (because better forms now exist). \"Ordinary repairs, taking away gradually, and replacing, are not practicable\" ( W. B. Adams , Roads and Rails , London, 1862, p. 136). II.3 — Moral Deterioration, Revolutionizing of Means of Labour, and Crises This is one of the most theoretically pregnant passages. The means of labour are \"for the most part constantly revolutionized by the progress of industry.\" Hence they are not replaced in their original form, but in the revolutionized form . Two countervailing consequences: Obstacle to rapid innovation: The volume of fixed capital invested in a particular natural form, which must last out a definite average lifespan, is a reason why new machines are introduced only gradually. Fixed capital thus resists the rapid general introduction of improved means of labour — it's a material inertia of the existing technical basis. Forced premature replacement: Competition forces the replacement of old means of labour by new ones before their natural demise , particularly when decisive revolutions have taken place. \"Catastrophes, crises, etc. are the principal causes that compel such premature renewals of equipment on a broad social scale.\" This passage is the theoretical seed of Marx's theory of the periodic cycle of crises , developed later (Chapter 9 on the \"working period\" and in Volume III): the replacement cycle of fixed capital (roughly a decennial cycle in Marx's era) provides a material basis for the periodicity of the industrial cycle. It also links moral depreciation to structural capitalist competition. II.4 — Depreciation Defined and the Reproduction Time of Living Means of Labour Depreciation is defined precisely: \"the portion of value that the fixed capital gradually gives up to the product as it is used, according to the average degree of its loss of use-value.\" Two forms of replacement: Average lifespan, complete replacement after: For living means of labour (horses), reproduction time is \"prescribed by nature itself\" — average life determined by natural laws; \"a horse cannot be replaced bit by bit, but only by another horse.\" This underlines the discontinuity of fixed capital reproduction in such cases. II.5 — Periodic or Partial Renewal and Gradual Extension (Distinguished from Accumulation) Marx distinguishes partial or periodic replacement from the gradual extension of a business . Fixed capital consists partly of components similar but not equally durable , renewed bit by bit at different intervals. Example: rails at a station must be replaced more often than elsewhere. Lardner states that in the 1850s on Belgian railways, sleepers were replaced at 8 per cent per year — the whole stock in twelve and a half years . Here, a sum is advanced for e.g. ten years on a particular kind of fixed capital, all at once; but a certain part (whose value has gone into the product and been converted into money) is replaced each year in kind, while the remainder continues in original natural form. \"What distinguishes this fixed capital from fluid capital is precisely this outlay all at once and reproduction only bit by bit in the natural form.\" This is a subtle restatement of conclusion (3). Other items consist of different types of component wearing out at different intervals (particularly machines). The same logic as the varying life of different components of a total fixed capital applies. II.6 — The Money Reserve Fund as Source of Expanded Reproduction This is one of the most important sub-arguments. Even though fixed capital continues to function in natural form in production, if a part of its value has circulated with the product (per average wear and tear) and become money, that money \"forms an element of the money reserve fund for the replacement of the capital when its reproduction in kind falls due.\" Crucially , this part of fixed capital value transformed into money can serve to expand the business or to effect improvements in machinery that increase effectiveness. Reproduction then occurs on an expanded scale — extensively (if the field of production is extended) or intensively (if means of production are made more effective). Marx's critical clarification: this reproduction on an expanded scale does not arise from accumulation — the transformation of surplus-value into capital — but from a re-transformation of the value that has branched into two parts and taken money form. This is a formal distinction of the highest importance: expanded reproduction of fixed capital can occur without surplus-value accumulation, purely from the reinvestment of the sinking-fund portion of fixed capital value. It is déjà the conceptual distinction between accumulation proper and the reinvestment of depreciation reserves — a distinction central to the analysis of credit and the industrial cycle. Whether the business is susceptible to this gradual addition depends on its specific nature; and so does the size of the reserve fund and the periods in which it can be reinvested. The extent to which improvements to existing machinery can be made depends on the nature of the improvements and the construction of the machine itself. Adams' beehive principle: \"The whole structure should be set out on the principle which governs the beehive — capacity for indefinite extension. Any fixed and decided symmetrical structure is to be deprecated, as needing subsequent pulling down in case of enlargement\" (p. 123). Space availability matters: some buildings take extra floors, others require horizontal extension (more land). Marx's critical aside on capitalist anarchy: While capitalist production is marked by waste of much material, there is also much inappropriate horizontal extension (partly involving loss of labour-power) in the course of gradual extension, \"since nothing is done according to a social plan, but rather depends on the infinitely varied circumstances, means, etc. with which the individual capitalist acts. This gives rise to a major wastage of productive forces.\" This is a classic Marxian critique of the anarchy of production — the spatial and material waste inherent in uncoordinated private investment. Agriculture as the most amenable sphere: The progressive reinvestment of the money reserve fund is most easily effected in agriculture, where a spatially given field of production can absorb the greatest gradual capital. Same for natural reproduction, e.g., cattle breeding. II.7 — Maintenance: The Free Gift of Labour and the Duality of Labour's Preserving Power Fixed capital gives rise to special costs of maintenance . Marx divides this: (a) Maintenance effected by the labour process itself: fixed capital spoils if it does not function in the labour process. He cites Volume I, Chapter 8, p. 315, and Chapter 15, p. 528, on the deterioration of machinery from non-use. Illustration from law: English law expressly considers it \"waste\" if land farmed out is not cultivated according to custom (W. A. Holdsworth, Barrister at Law, The Law of Landlord and Tenant , London, 1857, p. 96). The great theoretical claim: \"This maintenance that results from use in the labour process is a gift of nature provided gratis by living labour.\" Marx elaborates the duality of labour's preserving power : It preserves the value of the materials of labour by transferring it to the product. It preserves the value of the means of labour without transferring this value to the product — by preserving their use-value through their action in the production process. This duality is subtle and crucial: labour does not merely transfer value; it conserves the value of means of labour by maintaining them in use, a preserving power that costs the capitalist nothing and is not reflected in value transfer. This is a foundational claim about the relationship between living labour and dead labour. II.8 — Positive Maintenance (Cleaning) as Fluid Capital Fixed capital also requires positive outlays of labour to be kept in good condition — machinery must be cleaned from time to time. This involves additional labour, without which it becomes unfit for use . This is \"merely a defence against the damaging influence of the elements that is inseparable from the production process, and is thus keeping it in working order in the most literal sense.\" Marx's argument about average lifespan : \"The normal lifespan of fixed capital is naturally reckoned on the assumption that the conditions under which it can function normally during this time are fulfilled, just as it is assumed, if the average life of a man is taken as thirty years, that he washes himself.\" Brilliant analogy: statistical average lifespan is conditional on maintenance being performed. This is a crucial methodological point about averages in political economy. What is involved here: not the replacement of the labour contained in the machine, but additional labour constantly necessary for it to be used . \"This is not a matter of labour performed by the machine, but of labour performed on the machine; here it is not an agent of production, but rather raw material.\" The capital spent on this labour is part of fluid capital , even though it does not properly enter the actual labour process to which the product owes its origin. It belongs to that part of fluid capital covering general overheads , distributed over the value of the product according to an average annual calculation. The exploitation nuance (crucial). Marx cites Volume I, p. 552, note 10: in industry proper, cleaning is performed by the workers for nothing during breaks , often during the production process itself, \"where it is the major source of accidents.\" This labour does not count in the price of the product. \"The consumer receives it gratis. The capitalist, moreover, does not have to pay anything for the maintenance of his machine. The worker pays in his own person, and this forms one of the mysteries of capital's self-preservation, constituting in point of fact a legal claim of the worker on the machinery, and making him a co-owner of this even from the standpoint of bourgeois right. \" This is one of the most radical passages of Volume II. The worker's unpaid cleaning labour means the capitalist's \"self-preservation\" is funded out of the worker's body. Marx even makes a provocative juridical claim: from the standpoint of bourgeois right ( bürgerliches Recht — note the translator/editor's clarification that \"right\" is used \"in the philosophical sense of jurisprudence rather than that of positive law\"), the worker has a claim on the machinery, effectively making him a co-owner. This is a devastating inversion of bourgeois property claims. Contrast: In branches where machinery must be removed for cleaning (so it can't happen \"on the quiet\"), e.g., locomotives, \"this maintenance work counts as running costs, i.e. as an element of fluid capital.\" Evidence: \"A goods engine should not run more than three days without being kept one day in the shed... If you attempt to wash out the boiler before it has cooled down that is very injurious\" (R.C., no. 17823). II.9 — Repairs Proper: Additional Capital Not Covered by Depreciation Repairs proper — \"the work of patching up\" — require an outlay of capital and labour not contained in the capital originally advanced , and thus cannot always be replaced/covered by the gradual replacement of the fixed capital. Marx's arithmetic: if fixed capital £10,000, life ten years, the £10,000 transformed into money at the end of ten years replaces only the original investment, not the capital or labour newly added in between for repairs. This is an additional component of value , not advanced all at once, but according to need; the various times of advance are \"by the nature of the case accidental.\" All fixed capital requires these later doses of additional capital outlay on means of labour and labour-power. II.10 — Childhood Infirmities and the Ailments of Old Age Marx introduces an important analytical distinction between two kinds of repair work, both with a \"more or less firm character\" and falling in different periods of the fixed capital's lifetime: Childhood infirmities: faults becoming evident with actual use, corrected by subsequent work, however perfectly constructed the machine was on entry. Ailments of the years beyond middle age (\"far more numerous\"): as wear and tear mounts and material becomes worn and weak with age, repair work becomes more frequent and serious — \"just as an old man has more medical expenses than a man in the prime of life, if he is not to die before his time.\" Despite its accidental character, therefore, \"the work of repair is distributed unevenly over the various periods of the fixed capital's life.\" This is a systematic pattern within apparent randomness — like a mortality curve for machines. II.11 — Consequences: Average Life Requires Continuous Repair Capital, and Repair Costs Enter Price on Average Two consequences: First: The actual expenditure on labour-power and means of labour for repair work is accidental, as are the circumstances necessitating repairs. The extent of repairs needed is differentially distributed over the fixed capital's life. However , it is assumed in assessing average life that the fixed capital is constantly maintained in working condition — partly by cleaning, partly by repairs as often as required. The transfer of value through wear and tear is calculated over the average period of life, but \"this average period is itself calculated on the assumption that the additional capital required to keep it in working order is continuously advanced.\" (A recursive/conditional structure again.) Second: The value added by this additional expenditure \"cannot go into the price of commodities in step with the actual expenditure itself.\" Marx's vivid example: \"A cotton spinner, for instance, cannot sell his yarn dearer this week than last week because he had a wheel broken or a belt snapped.\" The general costs of spinning are in no way affected by this accident in an individual factory. Experience shows the average extent of such accidents and the work of maintenance and repair needed during the average life of a fixed capital invested in a certain line of business. This average expenditure is distributed over its average life and added in corresponding aliquot parts to the price of the product, and replaced by the product's sale. II.12 — Repair Capital as a Hybrid Category The extra capital replaced this way is part of fluid capital , even though the expenditure is irregular. Because it is of the utmost importance to treat every ailment of machinery immediately, every large factory has, in addition to factory workers proper, a staff of engineer, carpenter, mechanic, fitter, etc. Their wages form part of variable capital , and the value of their labour is distributed over the product. Crucial categorical conclusion: \"This capital spent on repairs in the strict sense forms in many respects a capital of a peculiar kind; it cannot be properly classed either as fluid or as fixed capital, but, since it is part of the running expenses, it tends more towards the first of the two forms.\" This is a significant admission that Marx's categories, rigorously defined by mode of value turnover , produce a boundary case. Marx does not force it — he acknowledges the hybrid. II.13 — The Accounting Manipulation: Bookkeeping as Social Practice Marx makes an important methodological assertion: \"The way the books are kept does not of course affect the actual relationships of the things entered in the accounts. But it is important to note...\" He then shows how, in many lines of business, repair costs are calculated together with actual wear and tear. Arithmetic illustration: fixed capital £10,000, life fifteen years → annual depreciation £666⅔. If depreciation is calculated over ten years only, then instead of £666⅔, £1,000 annually is added to the price of goods, i.e., £333⅓ is reserved for repairs — the average amount spent so that the fixed capital lasts fifteen years. (Marx notes the figures ten and fifteen are illustrative.) Applying this to real data: - Steamships: lowest cost estimate for maintenance and replacement = 15% per year, i.e., a reproduction time of 6⅔ years. - 1860s: British government compensated the Peninsular and Oriental Co. at 16% annually — a reproduction time of 6¼ years. - Railways: average locomotive life ten years, but with repairs included, depreciation taken as 12½ per cent — reducing lifespan to eight years . - Passenger coaches and goods wagons: 9 per cent reckoned , i.e., a life of 11⅑ years . Marx then notes the calculus \"does not of course prevent the fixed capital and the additional capital spent on repairs from forming different categories\" — bookkeeping practice cannot abolish the analytical distinction. II.14 — The Legal Recognition in Landlord-Tenant Law Marx finds legal corroboration. Legislation on rental contracts for houses and other fixed capital rented out as such \"has always recognized the distinction between normal deterioration, produced by time, the influence of the elements and normal wear and tear, and the occasional repairs necessary from time to time for maintenance in the course of the normal life of a house.\" Rule: the first (normal deterioration) falls on the landlord , the second (occasional repairs) on the tenant . Repairs further divided into ordinary and substantial ; the latter represent in part a renewal of fixed capital in natural form and fall on the landlord unless the contract states otherwise. Holdsworth's Law of Landlord and Tenant (pp. 90–91): a tenant from year to year is not bound to do more than keep premises \"wind and water tight\" where possible without substantial repairs, and generally to do \"ordinary\" repairs. Even with ordinary repairs, \"regard must be had to their age and general state, and condition, when he took possession, for he is not bound to replace old and worn out materials with new ones, nor to make good the inevitable depreciation resulting from time and ordinary wear and tear.\" The juridical form thus mirrors the economic distinction: on the one hand normal wear → landlord (owner of the durable asset); on the other, use-related minor repairs → tenant (user). This is a fine example of Marx's method — showing how legal categories crystallize material economic relations. II.15 — Insurance, Over-Production, and Social Reproduction Marx now distinguishes insurance from both replacement of wear and tear and repair/maintenance. Insurance relates to \"destruction by way of extraordinary natural events, fire, flood, etc.\" Crucially: \"This must be made good out of surplus-value, and forms a deduction from it.\" This is an important point in Marx's analysis of the deductions from surplus-value : insurance is not a capital cost recoverable through price in the same manner; it's a deduction from the total surplus. Social-scale consequence: \"Considered from the standpoint of the whole society, there must be a constant over-production, i.e. production on a greater scale than is needed for the simple replacement and reproduction of the existing wealth — quite apart from any increase in population — for the society to have at its disposal the means of production needed to make good unusual destruction caused by accidents and natural forces.\" This is a fundamental principle of Marxian social reproduction: a reserve/over-production above simple replacement is a material necessity of any society , independent of population growth, to absorb catastrophes. Marx here separates insurance reserves and constant over-production from accumulation proper . Then Marx corrects a potential over-estimate of the money reserve fund's actual role: \"In actual fact, only a very small part of the capital needed for replacement exists in the money reserve fund. The most significant part exists in the extension of the scale of production itself , which is partly an actual expansion, and partly falls within the normal capacity of the branches of production that produce fixed capital.\" Example: an engineering works is organized to take account of both an annual expansion of all its customers' factories and the need of part of them for reproduction, as a whole or in part. So the material capacity to reproduce fixed capital is built into the structure of Department I (means of production) — a precursor to the reproduction schemas. II.16 — Social Average, Differential Advantage, and the Obscuring of Surplus-Value When wear and tear and repair costs are determined on a social average , great unevenness necessarily arises even for equally large capital investments in the same branch under otherwise similar conditions. In practice, a machine will last one capitalist longer than average and another not so long. The repair costs of one are above average, of the other below. But the addition to price determined by wear and tear and repair costs is the same in both cases — determined on the average. \"Thus the increase in price brings the one more than he actually added, and the other less.\" The theoretical consequence is major: \"This circumstance, like all others that lead the profit of different capitalists in the same line of business to differ, given the same exploitation of labour-power, helps to make insight into the true nature of surplus-value more difficult .\" Marx thus explains a phenomenological mechanism of capitalist ideology: differential advantages cloak the reality of surplus-value extraction, making profit appear to arise from the individual capitalist's skill or fortune rather than from labour. II.17 — The Shifting Boundary Between Repair and Replacement: The Railway Dividend Struggle \"The boundary between what is repair and what is replacement, between costs of maintenance and costs of renewal, is a more or less shifting one.\" This gives rise to a perpetual struggle — in the railways, for example — as to whether certain expenses are repairs or replacement, whether to be met from current expenditure or from the original capital. \"The transfer of repair costs to the capital account instead of the current account is a well-known device through which railway directors artificially rack up their dividends.\" Evidence: Subsequent works undertaken during the early life of a railway \"ought not to be denominated repairs, but should be considered as an essential part of the construction of the railway, and in the financial accounts should be debited to capital, and not to revenue\" — since they are not due to wear and tear but to \"the original and inevitable incompleteness of the construction of the line\" ( Lardner , op. cit., p. 40). And: \"The only sound way is to charge each year's revenue with the depreciation necessarily suffered to earn the revenue whether the amount is actually spent or not\" ( Captain Fitzmaurice , \"Committee of Inquiry on Caledonian Railway,\" Money Market Review , 25 January 1868). This is a concrete illustration of the material basis of a specific form of financial manipulation — the class struggle over accounting categories, reflected in dividend policy. It shows the \" shifting \" quality of these categories as strategically exploited . II.18 — Agriculture and Rolling Stock: Where the Distinction Dissolves Agriculture: \"It becomes in practice impossible and meaningless to separate the replacement of the fixed capital from its maintenance, at least insofar as steam power is not yet used.\" Kirchhof ( Handbuch der landwirthschaftlichen Betriebslehre , Dessau, 1852, p. 137, Marx's emphasis): \"Where there is a full, though not excessive, stock of implements... the general rule is to estimate the annual wear and tear together with the maintenance of the implements, according to the different conditions obtaining, at 15–25 per cent of the original capital .\" Railway rolling stock: \"Quite impossible to separate repairs from replacement.\" T. Gooch (Chairman, Great Western Railway Co., R.C. on Railways, nos. 17327–17329): \"We maintain our stock by number. Whatever number of engines we have we maintain that. If one is destroyed by age, and it is better to build anew one, we build it at the expense of revenue... taking credit for the materials of the old one... there is a great deal left; there are the wheels, the axles, the boilers... a great deal of the old engine is left.\" Again: \"Repairing means renewing; I do not believe in the word replacement...; once a railway company has bought a vehicle or an engine, it ought to be repaired, and in that way admit of going on for ever\" (no. 17784). \"The engines are maintained for ever out of this 8½d. We rebuild our engines... there is always a pair of wheels or an axle or some portion... which comes in, and hence it cheapens the cost of producing a practically new engine\" (no. 17790). Archibald Sturrock (Locomotive Superintendent, Great Northern Railway): \"I am at this moment turning out a new engine every week, or practically a new engine, for it has a new boiler, cylinder, or framing\" (no. 17823). II.19 — Lardner's Picture of Continuous Partial Reproduction Lardner (op. cit., pp. 115–16): \"In the course of time the stock of engines and vehicles is continually repaired. New wheels are put on at one time, and a new body at another. The different moving parts most subject to wear are gradually renewed; and the engines and vehicles may be conceived even to be subject to such a succession of repairs, that in many of them not a vestige of the original materials remains... Even in this case, however, the old materials of coaches or engines are more or less worked up into other vehicles or engines, and never totally disappear from the road. The movable capital therefore may be considered to be in a state of continual reproduction; and that which, in the case of the permanent way, must take place altogether at a future epoch, when the entire road will have to be relaid, takes place in the rolling stock gradually from year to year. Its existence is perennial, and it is in a constant state of rejuvenescence. \" Marx's crucial qualification: The process described by Lardner \"does not apply to an individual factory, but it does provide a picture of the constant partial reproduction of the fixed capital, shot through with repairs, that takes place within an entire branch of industry, or generally within production as a whole, considered on the social scale .\" This is one of the most important methodological statements in the whole chapter: the continuous partial reproduction of fixed capital is a phenomenon of the social aggregate, not of the individual unit. It is a systemic — not individual — property of fixed capital, exactly the kind of systemic-temporal analysis Marx needs for the reproduction schemas later. II.20 — The Dividend-Manipulation Data Marx gives concrete numerical evidence of directors' manipulation. According to Williams' paper, various English railway companies annually wrote off the following average sums over a number of years for repairs and maintenance of permanent way and buildings, per mile of track: Company Amount London and North Western £370 Midland £225 London and South Western £257 Great Northern £310 Lancashire and Yorkshire £377 South Eastern £263 Brighton £266 Manchester and Sheffield £200 These differences \"arise only to a very slight degree from variations in actual expenditure; they are almost exclusively due to differing modes of calculation, according to whether items are debited to the capital account or the current account.\" Williams says \"in so many words that a lesser charge is put down when this is necessary for a good dividend, and a higher figure when there is a greater revenue able to bear it.\" This is an extraordinary disclosure: accounting categories are manipulated strategically, in response to revenue conditions, to present favorable dividends. The material relations of fixed capital are, in practice, mediated by accounting practices oriented to shareholder returns. II.21 — Impractically Large Durations: Secular Wear and Tear \"In certain cases, the wear and tear, and thus replacement for it, is in practice of an infinitesimal magnitude, so that it is only repair costs that come into the balance.\" Lardner on the railways, \"works of art,\" generalizable to canals, docks, iron and stone bridges, etc.: he refers to: \"That wear and tear which, being due to the slow operation of time acting upon the more solid structures, produces an effect altogether insensible when observed through short periods, but which, after a long interval of time, such, for example, as centuries, must necessitate the reconstruction of some or all even of the most solid structures. These changes may not unaptly be assimilated to the periodical and secular inequalities which take place in the movements of the great bodies of the universe . The operation of time upon the more massive works of art upon the railway, such as the bridges, tunnels, viaducts, etc., afford examples of what may be called the secular wear and tear . The more rapid and visible deterioration, which is made good by repairs or reconstruction effected at shorter intervals, is analogous to the periodic inequalities. In the annual repairs is included the casual damage which the exterior of the more solid and durable works may from time to time sustain; but, independently of these repairs, age produces its effects even on these structures, and an epoch must arrive, however remote it be, at which they would be reduced to a state which will necessitate their reconstruction. For financial and economic purposes such an epoch is perhaps too remote to render it necessary to bring it into practical calculation, and therefore it need here only be noticed in passing\" (Lardner, op. cit., pp. 38, 39). Marx adopts Lardner's astronomical metaphor (\"secular\" vs \"periodic\" wear), showing the deep timescales of the most durable fixed capital. Consequence: \"The capital advanced in them does not have to be gradually replaced in accordance with its wear and tear, but it is only the annual average costs of maintenance and repair that are transferred to the price of the product.\" Here the formal distinction between fixed capital value turnover and price calculation becomes almost total: the fixed capital value never systematically returns through price; only repair costs do. II.22 — The Necessity of the Amortization Fund and the Hoard-Circulation Mechanism Marx draws his capstone. Even though a large part of the money flowing back to replace wear and tear is transformed back into natural form annually or more frequently, \"each individual capitalist still needs an amortization fund for the part of the fixed capital that reaches its term of reproduction only after a period of years, and then has to be replaced entirely.\" \"A significant component of the fixed capital excludes piecemeal reproduction by its very nature.\" Apart from piecemeal reproduction, \"a prior accumulation of money is necessary, of a greater or lesser amount according to the specific character of the branch of production in question, before this replacement can occur. This cannot be just any sum of money whatever; an amount of a certain size is required. \" The monetary mechanism (assuming simple money circulation, without credit — credit to be brought in later in Volume III, Part Five): Marx refers to Volume I, Chapter 3, 3(a): part of the money in society always lies fallow as hoard ; another part functions as means of circulation or immediate reserve fund. The proportion between hoard and means of circulation constantly alters. In the present case: Money accumulated on a large scale as a hoard in the hands of a big capitalist is thrown into circulation all at once on the purchase of fixed capital. It is then divided up again in society between means of circulation and hoard. By way of the amortization fund , in which the value of fixed capital flows back to its starting point in proportion to wear and tear, a part of the money in circulation again forms a hoard — for a longer or shorter period — in the hands of the same capitalist whose hoard was earlier transformed into means of circulation and separated from him with his acquisition of fixed capital. The systemic description: \"There is a constantly changing distribution of the hoard existing in a society, which alternately functions as means of circulation, and is then again divided off from the mass of circulating money as a hoard.\" This is a beautiful dialectical picture of money's dual existence — hoard and circulating medium — mediated by the peculiar temporal structure of fixed capital investment and its amortization. It shows how the macroeconomics of money is rooted in the micro-time-structure of fixed capital . The credit system: \"With the development of the credit system, which necessarily runs parallel with the development of large-scale industry and capitalist production, this money no longer functions as a hoard but as capital , though not in the hands of its proprietor, but rather of other capitalists at whose disposal it is put.\" This is the pivot to Marx's analysis of the credit system (Volume III, Part Five): the amortization fund, no longer hoarded, becomes loanable capital. The dialectic of hoard/circulation transforms into the dialectic of idle capital/credit — capital placed at the disposal of other capitalists. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principle: Form Determined by Turnover The deepest lesson of Chapter 8 is that the form of capital — its fixedness or fluidity — is determined by the mode of turnover of value , not by material properties, not by physical immobility, not by duration, and not by the role of the thing in the labor process as such. Marx's formal criterion is devastatingly general: if a component of productive capital transfers its entire value to the product in a single production period, it is fluid; if it transfers value only gradually, retained in its persisting use-shape across multiple turnovers of the fluid capital, it is fixed. This criterion is functional, relational, and historical. This dissolves a whole library of confusions. Material immobility is neither sufficient (ships) nor necessary (draught oxen) for fixedness. Duration of commitment is neither sufficient (seed) nor decisive. Ancillaries (coal, gas) do not enter the product but are consumed wholly at each operation — hence fluid, not fixed as Ramsay thought. Variable capital — the advance for labour-power — is fluid not because of any material property of subsistence goods, but because its value enters the product entirely and requires constant re-advance. And means of transport are a peculiar case where the use-effect itself enters individual consumption. The Anti-Fetishism of Capital The chapter performs an extraordinary anti-fetishizing operation: \"It is in no way fixed capital in itself, just as it emerges from a process.\" A machine in the hands of the machine-builder is commodity capital . It becomes fixed capital only when the capitalist buyer sets it to work as a means of labour in a productive process. Capital form is socially conferred , not materially inherent. This is the general thesis of Marx's critique of political economy applied to the specific determinations of capital — and it exposes the naturalization of capital as the ideological illusion of bourgeois economics. The Duality of Labor's Preserving Power Labor, in the process of production, does two things with value: (1) it transfers the value of materials to the product; and (2) it preserves the value of means of labour without transferring it — by keeping those means of labour in use, sustaining their use-value. Maintenance is (partly) a \"gift of nature provided gratis by living labour.\" The worker's cleaning labor, done \"for nothing during breaks,\" literally contributes to the self-preservation of capital without its appearing in the price of the product — \"one of the mysteries of capital's self-preservation.\" This produces Marx's striking juridical claim: from the standpoint of bourgeois right, the worker's unpaid maintenance labor gives him a claim on the machinery, making him a co-owner. This is a profound, if provocative, theoretical point about the relationship between living labor and the technical conditions of production. The Social Scale vs. Individual Capital One of the most significant methodological claims of the chapter is that the continuous partial reproduction of fixed capital is a phenomenon of the social aggregate , not of the individual factory. Individual capitals show discrete, lumpy investment and replacement. Society as a whole exhibits continuous, overlapping, \"perennial\" reproduction of fixed capital in a state of \"constant rejuvenescence\" (Lardner's phrase) because the different turnovers of different individual capitals interlock and offset one another. This is the crucial connection to Marx's later reproduction schemas (Volume II, Part III): fixed capital reproduction is the paradigmatic case where individual capitalist rationality produces social interlocking patterns that no individual capital intends. The Material Basis of the Crisis Cycle Marx's observation that fixed capital is \"constantly revolutionized\" by industry, that \"catastrophes, crises, etc. are the principal causes that compel such premature renewals of equipment on a broad social scale,\" and that moral depreciation operates independent of use-value loss, plants the seed of the theory of the periodic industrial cycle . The replacement cycle of fixed capital — roughly decennial in Marx's day — provides a material basis for the periodicity of capitalist crises, because massive simultaneous replacement and expansion of fixed capital generates booms, and the completion of other investments generates crises of over-capacity. This chapter supplies the fixed-capital theory of the cycle that Marx develops later in Volume II and in Volume III. The Amortization Fund as Loanable Capital The final pages trace the monetary consequence of fixed capital's peculiar turnover: the amortization fund. The capitalist throws money into circulation all at once to buy fixed capital; it returns bit by bit as the machine's value is transferred to commodities and sold. Between the return and the eventual repurchase, that money lies as a hoard — a fund of potential loan capital. With the development of the credit system, \"this money no longer functions as a hoard but as capital,\" placed at the disposal of other capitalists. Thus the deepest structure of capital markets, banking, and credit emerges directly from the materiality of fixed capital. This is one of the most brilliant dialectical developments in all of Marx's work: the time-structure of machine wear becomes the material foundation of the credit system. The Contingency of Capitalist Rationality Finally, the chapter repeatedly exposes the anarchy and waste of capitalist production: the \"inappropriate horizontal extension\" of buildings, the loss of labor-power, the strategic manipulation of accounting categories to inflate dividends (railway directors' \"perpetual struggle\" over repairs vs. replacement), the differential advantage of individual capitals over social averages obscuring the nature of surplus-value, the arbitrary subordination of social replacement to individual profit calculation. For Marx, these are not accidental defects but necessary expressions of production conducted \"according to no social plan.\" The chapter thus provides not only a technical taxonomy but a political critique of the accounting, financial, and industrial practices through which capitalist profitability is realized and reported. Theoretical Implications Beyond Volume II Chapter 8's distinctions are the indispensable foundation for: 1. The analysis of turnover time (Chapters 9–17), where the different periodicities of fixed and fluid capital determine the \"annual rate of surplus-value\" and the efficiency of capital. 2. The reproduction schemas (Volume II, Part III), where the continuous social replacement of fixed capital is a critical — and famously difficult — element of the balanced reproduction of the total social capital. 3. The theory of the credit system (Volume III, Part Five), built directly on the amortization fund and the hoard/circulation dialectic. 4. The theory of crises and the industrial cycle , grounded in the replacement pattern of fixed capital. 5. The critique of political economy , particularly the exposure of Adam Smith's confusion of circulating capital with capital of circulation (developed in Chapters 10–11). In sum, Chapter 8 is the conceptual keystone of Volume II: it establishes the analytic distinction upon which Marx's entire subsequent treatment of capital turnover, social reproduction, and the material foundations of credit and crisis rests. It achieves this by rigorously dematerializing the fixed/fluid distinction — reducing it to a formal pattern of value circulation — while simultaneously materializing it again in the anatomical peculiarities of machinery, railways, soil improvement, and the social accounting that surrounds them. The result is a masterful demonstration that under capitalism, time itself — the time of machines, of labor, of money — is the substance of capital, and its measurement is the substance of profit. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 7 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 9"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 9", "title": "Chapter 9", "subtitle": "The Total Turnover of Advanced Capital; Cycles of Turnover", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 9/Chapter 9 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 5937, "read_time": "30 min read", "tags": ["Turnover of Capital & Cycles", "Capital Accumulation & Crises"], "keywords": ["Total Turnover of Advanced Capital", "Aggregate Turnover Calculations", "Cycles of Turnover as Basis of Periodic Crises", "Ten-Year Renewal Cycle of Fixed Machinery", "Average Turnover of Fixed and Circulating Components"], "text": "Chapter 9 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,867 words ⏱️ ~29 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 9 of Capital , Volume II (\"The Overall Turnover of the Capital Advanced\") is the synthesising pivot of Part II, the section of the book devoted to the turnover of capital. Having spent the preceding chapters dissecting the separate turnover rhythms of fixed and fluid capital — showing that they turn over \"differently and in different periods,\" and that even components within fixed capital turn over at different rates according to their lifespans — Marx now confronts the aggregation problem head-on. What is the turnover of the capital advanced as a whole ? The chapter's central thesis is that the overall turnover (the German is Gesamtumschlag ) is the average turnover of the capital's heterogeneous component parts , but — and this is the crux — that averaging is legitimate only after a prior qualitative reduction has been performed . You cannot average incommensurables. The fixed capital that stays in the production process and the fluid capital that must be constantly replaced \"in kind\" are not merely different in degree; they are different in kind. Marx's intellectual objective in this chapter is therefore twofold: first, to construct the method by which heterogeneous turnovers can be made homogeneous enough to be averaged; second, to extract from that construction a profound consequence about the difference between the capital advanced and the capital turned over , and from that difference a theory of the material basis of the industrial cycle. The central problem is stated at the outset in three numbered theses: (1) the overall turnover is the average of its component turnovers, and where only different periods of time are involved, \"it is of course perfectly simple to take their average\"; (2) but there are not only quantitative distinctions involved, \"but also qualitative ones\"; and (3) it follows that the capital value turned over during the year \"may be greater than the total value of the capital advanced.\" These three propositions organise the entire chapter. Proposition 1 gives the arithmetic; Proposition 3 gives the shocking result (turned-over capital exceeding advanced capital); and Proposition 2 is the conceptual engine that makes both intelligible. The remainder of the chapter tests this apparatus against an external authority — the \"American economist\" Scrope — whose numerical examples Marx endorses but whose conceptual distinctions (in heading 6) he dismantles. This chapter occupies a strategic position in Marx's architecture. It is the bridge between the anatomy of turnover (Chapters 7–8, which analysed fixed and fluid capital as such) and the theory of turnover's consequences (Chapter 10's critique of the classical economists' confusion of fixed/circulating capital, and Chapter 11's treatment of the reproduction of fixed capital). More broadly, it is where Marx grounds the periodicity of the business cycle in the material properties of fixed capital — the ten-year lifespan of machinery — thereby linking the abstract categories of the circuit of capital (M–C–P–C′–M′) to the concrete historical phenomenon of recurring crises. The chapter therefore answers to the largest ambitions of Volume II: to show that the turnover of capital is not a formal or circular matter but the material heartbeat that gives the \"periodic cycle\" of stagnation, moderate activity, over-excitement, and crisis its rhythm. In short, Chapter 9 converts bookkeeping into political economy: it shows that behind the capitalist's profit calculation lies a hidden distinction — between value turnover and real reproduction time — whose discovery is indispensable to any scientific theory of crisis. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Two Premises: Established Diversity of Turnover and the Need for Aggregation The chapter opens by recalling rather than re-arguing a result: \"We have seen already that the fixed and the fluid components of productive capital turn over differently and in different periods, just as the various components of fixed capital in the same business also have different turnover periods according to their different lifespans and reproduction times.\" This is not a throwaway. It establishes the data the chapter must process. Capital advanced to production is, in reality, a heterogeneous bundle: a factory building with a fifty-year life, a machine with a ten-year life, tools worn out in a year or consumed \"in one set of operations,\" a stock of raw material consumed weekly, coal burned daily, labour-power purchased weekly. Each of these elements has its own turnover time — the time from the outlay of value, through production, back to the recovery of that value in money. The theoretical problem is: this is a plurality , but the capitalist needs a singular magnitude — \"the average period which his entire capital takes in passing through his hands,\" because it is from this single figure that \"a capitalist must calculate his profits.\" Marx's first numbered point gives the method abstractly: \"The overall turnover of the capital advanced is the average turnover of its different component parts; the mode of calculation is given below. In so far as only different periods of time are involved, it is of course perfectly simple to take their average.\" The phrase \"in so far as only different periods of time are involved\" is a conditional that immediately gets revoked. It is the rhetorical hinge on which the whole chapter turns: if the only differences were differences of duration, the problem would dissolve into trivial arithmetic (average the numbers). But they are not. The Qualitative Obstacle: Why Simple Averaging Fails Point 2 declares the obstruction: \"There are not only quantitative distinctions involved, but also qualitative ones.\" Marx unpacks this with a contrast between the two great fractions of productive capital. Fluid capital (raw and ancillary materials, labour-power) \"entering the production process transfers its whole value to the product, and must therefore be constantly replaced in kind by the sale of the product, if the production process is to continue without interruption.\" Two things are happening simultaneously here: (a) the whole value is transferred in a single pass, and (b) the natural form must be repurchased — the cotton must be rebought, the coal rebought, the worker rehired. Fixed capital (buildings, machinery, tools) \"transfers only part of its value (the wear and tear) to the product, and continues to function in the production process despite this wear and tear; hence it only needs to be replaced in kind at shorter or longer intervals, in any case not as often as the fluid capital.\" The word \"in kind\" ( stofflich , materially) is decisive. The reason fluid capital cannot simply be \"averaged\" with fixed capital is that what returns to the capitalist in each case is different. When fluid capital completes a turnover, an entire functional cycle closes: the value came back as money, and money must at once be reconverted into cotton and labour-power, or production stops. When fixed capital \"completes\" a turnover, only a fraction of its value returns as money, and — critically — no replacement in kind occurs ; the machine keeps turning. A \"turnover\" of fluid capital implies reconstitution of the real productive element ; a \"turnover\" of fixed capital (as wear and tear) implies only the separation of a value-sum , with the real element still in place. To average these two things is to average an event that repeats its own precondition with an event that merely deposits a residue. But the qualitative problem cuts deeper — into fixed capital itself. Marx notes that the reproduction period \"does not just differ quantitatively for the different components of the fixed capital.\" Some fixed capital, \"of longer durability and fixed for several years, can be replaced annually or at shorter intervals, and the old fixed capital added to in kind\" — i.e., it is renewed in increments (a railway relaying track section by section; a building refurbished room by room). Other fixed capital \"can only be effected all at once at the end of its life\" — a machine is scrapped and replaced whole, or not at all. Here, then, are two forms of fixed-capital reproduction: piecemeal/incremental replacement and lump-sum/terminal replacement . These are not merely faster or slower; they are structurally different kinds of event. Averaging them would be like averaging \"paying a bill in twenty instalments\" with \"paying it once, at the end.\" The outputs have the same total, but the temporal shape of the outflow differs, and that shape matters for the rhythm of demand and reinvestment. The solution Marx proposes is a reduction : \"It is necessary therefore to reduce the separate turnovers of the various parts of the fixed capital to a similar form of turnover, so that these differ only quantitatively, in the duration of their turnover.\" This is the methodological heart of the chapter — a normalisation procedure, exactly analogous to reducing incommensurable fractions to a common denominator. Only once every element is expressed in a common form can the arithmetic average of point 1 be legitimately taken. The Reduction to Money Form: The Machine Example and the Homogenising Device Marx now executes that reduction, and he does it in the most striking way: through a concrete numerical example that doubles as a conceptual demonstration. \"Let us take a machine with a value of £10,000, for example, which lasts for ten years, so that one tenth of it, or £1,000, is transformed back into money every year.\" Note what \"transformed back into money\" means here. The machine never leaves the factory floor. No £1,000 coin changes hands at the year's end. Yet Marx insists: \"In the course of one year, this £1,000 has been transformed from money capital into productive capital and commodity capital, and from this back into money capital. It has returned to its original money form, just like the fluid capital.\" This is the reduction. The common form into which all turnovers — fixed and fluid — are to be translated is the return to the money form . Marx makes this explicit: \"In calculating the overall turnover of the productive capital advanced, we therefore take all its elements in the money form, so that the return to the money form concludes the turnover.\" And he immediately anticipates the obvious objection — that in a continuous production process the \"money form\" is fictional: \"We always consider the value as advanced in money, even in the case of a continuous production process, where the money form of the value is only that of money of account. We can then take the average.\" The phrase \"money of account\" is doing heavy theoretical work. In a going concern, the value advanced is not literally held as cash waiting to be invested; it is embodied in machines, stocks, and wage-funds. But for the purpose of accounting for turnover, Marx treats the value as if it had been advanced in money, so that \"returning to the money form\" — which may be merely a bookkeeping event (the depreciation write-off, the imputed recovery of £1,000) — counts as a completed turnover. Homogenisation is achieved by abstraction: every element of the capital advanced is imagined as money that departs and returns; its turnover is the time between departure and return. In this frame, the durability of the machine ceases to be a difference of kind and becomes a difference of frequency : the machine's £10,000 turns over once in ten years; the £1,000-a-year portion \"has returned to its original money form\" in one year. It is immaterial \"whether the money capital of £1,000 is transformed back again into the natural form of a machine at the end of the year, or not.\" With this stroke, the field is cleared: now all turnovers are quantities of time , and the average can be taken. The Two Central Equations: Capital Advanced versus Capital Turned Over Point 3 delivers the chapter's most famous result — the divergence between capital advanced and capital turned over . Marx builds it with a two-part capital: Fixed capital = £80,000, reproduction time ten years → £8,000 \"returns to its money form\" annually (one tenth of its turnover completes each year). Fluid capital = £20,000, turning over five times a year → 5 × £20,000 = £100,000 turned over annually. Total capital advanced = £80,000 + £20,000 = £100,000. Now the result: \"The fixed capital turned over is £8,000, and the fluid capital turned over is 5 times £20,000 = £100,000. The capital turned over in the year is then £108,000, £8,000 greater than the capital advanced.\" Marx adds the ratio: the OCR renders it as \"1 2/25 of the capital has turned over,\" which matches the arithmetic exactly: £108,000 ÷ £100,000 = 1.08 = 27/25 = 1 2/25. The whole advanced capital has been turned over more than once in a single year , even though £80,000 of it is locked in a machine that takes a decade to reproduce. This is a genuinely counter-intuitive proposition and worth dwelling on. It appears to claim that the capitalist gets back more value in a year than he put in — which sounds like value creation ex nihilo, an impossibility. Marx is careful that it is nothing of the kind. The £108,000 is value summed over time , not a stock. The £100,000 is the same £20,000 fluid capital passing through five successive turnovers — £20,000 advanced, returned, re-advanced, returned… five times. Adding the five passes gives £100,000 of turnover-flux , but the stock advanced and outstanding is still only £20,000 (plus the unexhausted £80,000 of machine). Likewise the £8,000 is a fraction of the £80,000 machine's value that has been carried out of the machine and embodied in products sold; it is not new wealth but the transmigration of pre-existing value from fixed to fluid form. So Proposition 3 is a rate-versus-stock confusion only if misread ; correctly read, it discloses that \"capital turned over\" is a flow measure and \"capital advanced\" is a stock measure , and the two are not commensurable as straightforwardly as they first appear. Value Turnover versus Real Reproduction Time: The Chapter's Sharpest Distinction Point 4 generalises the insight into a conceptual law: \"The value turnover of the capital advanced is thus separate from its actual reproduction time, or the real turnover time of its components.\" The illustration: \"Say that a capital of £4,000 turns over five times in the year. The capital turned over is then 5 times £4,000 = £20,000. But what returns at the end of each turnover, to be advanced once again, is the originally advanced capital of £4,000. Its size is not affected by the number of turnover periods in which it functions anew as capital.\" (The parenthetical — \"We again disregard surplus-value\" — is essential: the analysis abstracts from the expansion of value by labour-power so as to isolate the mere circulation of the capital value. Surplus-value would obscure the distinction between return of capital and creation of new value.) So the split is between: 1. Value turnover — the cumulative flux of value through the money-form across a year, which can exceed the capital advanced (as 5 × £4,000 = £20,000 exceeds £4,000), because the same value is counted each time it makes a circuit; and 2. Real turnover time / reproduction time — the concrete period after which the actual productive elements have to be reconstituted, which for the fixed capital imposes a multi-year cycle . Marx returns to the £80,000 machine to spell out the second notion. At the end of the year: (a) £20,000 returns in money, to be laid out again on fluid components; (b) £8,000 has \"separated off from the fixed capital advanced as a result of wear and tear.\" After that, \"the same fixed capital still continues to exist in the production process, but with the reduced value of £72,000 instead of £80,000.\" The machine is now 90% of its original value and 100% still in service. \"The production process must thus continue for nine years before the fixed capital advanced has come to the end of its life, no longer functions to form products or value, and has to be replaced.\" Therefore \"the capital value advanced has thus to describe a cycle of turnovers, in the given case for example a cycle of ten annual turnovers, and this cycle is in fact determined by the lifespan, and hence the reproduction time or turnover time, of the fixed capital applied.\" The cycle of turnovers is thus the distinctive concept this chapter adds to Marx's apparatus. It is the supra-annual rhythm imposed on the whole enterprise by its most durable fixed component. Individual components may turn over faster (fluid capital, several times a year) or slower (the machine, once in ten years); but the aggregate enterprise has a natural period — the lifespan of the dominant fixed capital — at the end of which the value must be reconstituted in kind . This is the chapter's crowning analytic move: it distinguishes the flux of turnover (annual, potentially exceeding the capital advanced) from the cycle of reproduction (multi-annual, paced by fixed capital). The Ten-Year Cycle and the Material Basis of Crises The argument now makes a spectacular leap, from accounting to historical materialism. \"To the same extent as the value and durability of the fixed capital applied develops with the development of the capitalist mode of production, so also does the life of industry and industrial capital in each particular investment develop, extending to several years, say an average of ten years.\" The claim is a structural coupling : the greater the mass and durability of fixed capital, the longer the period in which it commits a given investment — because value locked into a machine cannot be freely withdrawn, and the cycle of its reconstitution drags the whole business along with it. But Marx immediately introduces a countervailing force : \"If the development of fixed capital extends this life, on the one hand, it is cut short on the other by the constant revolutionizing of the means of production, which also increases steadily with the development of the capitalist mode of production.\" Here he names moral depreciation ( moralischer Verschleiß ) — the obsolescence that arises not from physical wear but from technological competition. \"This also leads to changes in the means of production; they constantly have to be replaced, because of their moral depreciation, long before they are physically exhausted.\" So fixed capital lengthens the industrial cycle (via durability) and shortens it (via obsolescence), and this collision produces the effective average cycle. Marx then fixes a working figure — hedged appropriately as an approximation: \"We can assume that, for the most important branches of large-scale industry, this life cycle is now on average a ten-year one. The precise figure is not important here.\" The conclusion is the chapter's grandest: \"The result is that the cycle of related turnovers, extending over a number of years, within which the capital is confined by its fixed component, is one of the material foundations for the periodic cycle in which business passes through successive periods of stagnation, moderate activity, over-excitement and crisis.\" A footnote glosses \"periodic cycle\" as the German crises — Marx is explicitly theorising the business cycle, and he locates one of its material foundations in the turnover cycle of fixed capital . He then specifies the mechanism of resetting the cycle: \"The periods for which capital is invested certainly differ greatly, and do not coincide in time. But a crisis is always the starting-point of a large volume of new investment. It is also, therefore, if we consider the society as a whole, more or less a new material basis for the next turnover cycle.\" The picture is now complete and dialectical. Investment cycles are de-synchronised across firms and sectors, so they do not by themselves produce a single rhythm. But a crisis — by devaluing capital, clearing the field, and forcing wholesale replacement — reunifies the dispersed rhythms into a new common starting date, from which a new ten-year cycle (roughly) begins. The crisis is simultaneously the culmination of the old cycle and the material basis of the new one. The footnote appended here — \"Urban production is tied to a cycle of days, rural production to one of years\" — is Marx quoting Adam H. Müller's Die Elemente der Staatskunst (Berlin, 1809), which he dismisses as \"the naive conception of industry and agriculture held by the Romantic school.\" The romantic Müller saw natural cycles (days for town, years for countryside) as the determinants of economic rhythm. Marx replaces the natural calendar with the social-material calendar of fixed capital — a decisive polemical substitution: the rhythm of industry is not the rhythm of nature, but the rhythm of accumulation. Scrope's Arithmetic: The American Economist Has His Say Point 5 turns from Marx's own derivation to an illustrative quotation, framed with a slightly barbed generosity — \"On the mode of calculation of the turnover we will let an American economist have his say.\" The passage is from Scrope's Political Economy (edited by Alonzo Potter, New York, 1841, pp. 142–143), and it supplies two worked examples of the average turnover of a heterogeneous capital. First case. Capital = $50,000, split three ways: - One half ($25,000) in buildings and machinery, \"to be turned only once in ten years\" → $25,000 ÷ 10 = $2,500 per year. - One fourth ($12,500) in tools, \"turned once in two years\" → $12,500 ÷ 2 = $6,250 per year. - Remaining fourth ($12,500) in wages and materials, \"turned twice in one year\" → $12,500 × 2 = $25,000 per year. - Total annual turnover = $2,500 + $6,250 + $25,000 = $33,750. The \"mean term in which his capital is turned\" is then the capital divided by the annual turnover: $50,000 ÷ $33,750 ≈ 1.48 years, i.e., \"about eighteen months,\" exactly as Scrope states. Note that the mean term here is the period required for the whole advanced capital to pass once through; it is the inverse of the annual turnover rate. This confirms the deep consistency between Scrope's method and Marx's: the \"overall turnover\" is computed by weighting each component by its annual turnover contribution and summing — equivalent to taking the harmonic mean of the component turnover periods weighted by capital share. Second case. Capital still $50,000: - One fourth ($12,500) circulating in ten years → $12,500 ÷ 10 = $1,250 . - One fourth ($12,500) in one year → $12,500 . - One half ($25,000) twice in the year → $25,000 × 2 = $50,000 . - Turned over in one year = $1,250 + $12,500 + $50,000 = $63,750. Here the mean term is $50,000 ÷ $63,750 ≈ 0.78 years, roughly nine months. The contrast between the two cases — $33,750 versus $63,750 turned over annually on the same $50,000 of capital — dramatically illustrates Proposition 3: by shifting weight toward rapidly-turning fluid capital, the \"amount turned over in the year\" leaps from below to above the capital advanced. Scrope's example therefore serves as external corroboration of the reduction-to-annual-turnover method Marx set out in §§1–4, and it introduces the \"mean term\" and \"revolution\" language (\"making one revolution, from which a capitalist must calculate his profits\") that Marx's analysis formalises. It is worth noting the conceptual economy of Marx's borrowing: he credits the arithmetic (Scrope's numerical method is sound) but, as heading 6 shows, he repudiates the conceptual vocabulary in which Scrope embeds it. The borrowing is selective — Marx takes the calculation and rejects the theory. Actual versus Apparent Variations: The Fluidity Hierarchy and Marx's Threefold Critique Heading 6 — \"Actual and apparent variations in the turnover of the various parts of capital\" — is the chapter's polemical and analytic payoff. Marx quotes Scrope again (p. 141), in a passage that ranks capital components by the velocity of their turnover: Wages : \"circulates most rapidly, being turned perhaps once a week (if his men are paid weekly), by the weekly receipts on his bills or sales.\" Materials and stock in hand : \"less quickly, being turned perhaps twice, perhaps four times in the year,\" per the interval between purchase and sale. Implements and machinery : \"still more slowly, being turned, that is, consumed and renewed, on the average, perhaps but once in five or ten years; though there are many tools that are worn out in one set of operations.\" Buildings (mills, shops, warehouses, barns, roads, irrigation): \"may appear scarcely to circulate at all. But, in truth, these things are, to the full, as much as those we have enumerated, consumed in contributing to production, and must be reproduced… with this only difference, that they are consumed and reproduced by slower degrees than the rest… and the capital invested in them may be turned perhaps every twenty or fifty years.\" Scrope's hierarchy thus runs, from fastest to slowest: wages → materials → tools → buildings. To this Marx applies a sharp corrective: \"Here Scrope confuses the difference in the flow of particular parts of the fluid capital brought about by payment periods and credit conditions, with turnovers arising from the nature of the capital.\" In other words, Scrope has conflated two very different sources of turnover-difference: differences intrinsic to the production process (the real wear and reproduction of fixed capital) and differences arising from conventions of payment and credit (how often wages are paid, how long the credit granted by suppliers). The former is a matter of material nature ; the latter is a matter of social/financial arrangement . Only the former properly belongs to the theory of turnover. Marx then dissects Scrope's specific claim that \"wages must be paid weekly out of the weekly receipts.\" His critique proceeds in two stages. First , the payment period for wages is variable and itself has consequences: \"The first thing to note here is that differences arise with respect to wages themselves, according to the length of the period of payment, i.e. the length of time for which the worker has to give the capitalist credit; thus according to whether the payment of wages is weekly, monthly, three-monthly, half-yearly, etc.\" He invokes a law established earlier: \"the quantity of the means of payment required\" — the quantity of money capital that has to be advanced at one go — \"is in direct proportion to the length of the [payment] periods\" (Volume 1, Chapter 3, §3, b, p. 240). The significance is that if workers were paid monthly rather than weekly, the capitalist would need a larger reserve of money capital to bridge the payment period, even though the annual wage bill were identical. So the \"weekly turnover of wages\" is not a natural given; it is a function of the payment convention , and the convention redistributes the burden of financing (toward greater or lesser money-capital reserves) without altering the quantity of labour bought. Second , Scrope's picture of the weekly product is over-simplified. \"It is not only the entire new value added in its production by the week's labour that enters into the weekly product, but also the value of the raw material and ancillaries consumed in it.\" The weekly product therefore embodies both new value (from labour) and transferred value (from materials), and that whole composite \"circulates together with the product itself. It receives the money form by the sale of the product, and has to be converted once again into the same elements of production.\" But — and this is the crucial nuance — the replacement rhythm of materials and of labour-power is not the same, because of the phenomenon of the production stock . Marx recalls his earlier finding (Chapter 6, §2, a) that \"the continuity of production requires a stock of means of production, which differs for various lines of business, and in the same line of business differs once again for different components of this element of fluid capital, e.g. for coal and cotton.\" Coal is bought in bulk and burned at a steady rate; cotton is bought in lot-sizes; the depth of the stock determines how often purchases must be repeated : \"although these materials must constantly be replaced in kind, they do not always need to be bought afresh. How often the purchase is repeated depends on the size of the stock invested in, how long it will last until it is exhausted.\" This produces a genuine, material differentiation within fluid capital — the very differentiation Scrope attributes to payment conventions — and it does so independently of credit. Labour-power, by contrast, cannot be stockpiled. \"In the case of labour-power, there is no such storage process.\" This creates the sharpest asymmetry in the chapter's account of fluid capital. For materials, there is both a weekly consumption and a lumpy purchase (bridged by the stock). For labour, there is weekly consumption and weekly purchase (no bridge). Consequently: \"For the portion of the capital that is laid out on labour, the transformation back into money goes hand in hand with that laid out on ancillary and raw materials. But the transformation of the money back into labour-power, on the one hand, and raw materials, on the other, proceeds separately, on account of the particular purchase and payment periods of these two components, one of them being bought at longer intervals, as a productive stock, the other, labour-power, at shorter intervals, e.g. weekly.\" This is the chapter's deepest analytic refinement of \"fluid capital\": the category is internally differentiated by stockability . Materials recompose into the production process in lumpy, stock-sized increments ; labour recomposes in smooth, pay-period-sized increments . To these Marx adds two further sources of internal variation in fluid-capital turnover: Finished-goods stock : \"Besides his production stock, the capitalist must also keep a stock of finished commodities.\" Even assuming production to order, \"while the latter part of these are being produced, those items already finished lie in store until the time when the others can be completed.\" So the sale (and hence the money-return) of finished output is lagged and bunched , not perfectly synchronised with its production. Preliminary production stages : \"Other distinctions in the turnover of the fluid capital arise if particular elements of this have to persist longer than others at a preliminary stage of the production process (drying of wood, etc.).\" Here a physical/technical constraint (curing, seasoning, fermentation) stretches the turnover time of specific fluid elements without any relation to payment or credit at all — again vindicating Marx's distinction between turnover from the nature of capital and turnover from financial convention . The heading closes with a measured concession about the credit system . \"The credit system, which Scrope refers to here, modifies the turnover of the individual capitalist, and so does commercial capital.\" So Marx does not deny that credit affects turnover — he affirms it. But he subordinates it: \"At the level of society, however, it modifies this only in so far as it speeds up both consumption and production.\" This is the materialist coda. For a single firm, credit and commercial capital can accelerate (or, presumably, retard) the turnover of its capital, because the firm can sell before it has produced, or buy before it has cash. But socially , credit cannot conjure new turnover out of nothing; it can only re-time real movements — and thus its ultimate effect is limited to whatever real acceleration of production and consumption it can induce. In Marx's methodological universe, finance is a modifier of surface rhythms , not a creator of substance . This sentence is the bridge from the specific critique of Scrope to the general principle that value-turnover is founded on, and bounded by, the material reproduction of capital. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Chapter 9 teaches a handful of fundamental principles that ripple far beyond its narrow accounting problem. First is the principle of commensuration through abstraction : heterogeneous magnitudes cannot be averaged until a common measure is imposed, and Marx's common measure is return to the money form — a device that treats a machine's annual depreciation write-off as conceptually identical to fluid capital's annual return. This is a general methodological law of Marxian political economy: to make a calculation, you must abstract from real heterogeneity, but you must know that you have done so and not mistake the abstraction for the reality . Scrope commits exactly this error when he lets payment conventions masquerade as natural turnovers. Second is the distinction between stock and flow — between the capital advanced (a stock: £100,000) and the capital turned over (a flow: £108,000/year). The chapter establishes that the very same stock can, through repeated circuits, generate an annual turnover greater than itself, and that this \"excess\" is not creation of new value but merely the repeated counting of the same value moving through the circuit. This distinction is foundational: the whole subsequent theory of the annual rate of surplus value , and of the way turnover affects profit rates, requires it. Third, and most consequentially, is the coupling of fixed capital's lifespan to the periodicity of the industrial cycle . Marx derives a ten-year effective cycle from the collision of longevity (which lengthens the cycle) with moral depreciation (which shortens it), and he locates one of the \"material foundations\" of the cycle of stagnation, moderate activity, over-excitement, and crisis in this material fact. The theoretical payoff is that crises are not merely financial accidents or moral failures; they have a material substrate in the turnover cycle of fixed capital. And the crucial dialectical twist — that a crisis is \"always the starting-point of a large volume of new investment\" and \"more or less a new material basis for the next turnover cycle\" — makes crisis simultaneously destructive and regenerative : it synchronises what had been de-synchronised, and it resets the rhythm of accumulation. This is a sophisticated, non-mechanical theory of crisis: it does not claim that the ten-year lifespan causes crisis, only that it provides one of the material foundations for periodicity. Fourth is the internal stratification of \"fluid capital\" — into stockable materials (bought lumpily, consumed smoothly) and non-stockable labour-power (consumed and paid smoothly), with a further time-lag from finished-goods stocks and technical interim stages (drying wood). This is a warning against the simple dichotomy fixed/fluid: reality is a continuum of rhythms , and theory must be fine-grained enough to capture it. The broader implications are large and twofold. Practically , the chapter speaks to how a capitalist actually thinks: \"It is the average period which his entire capital takes in passing through his hands… from which a capitalist must calculate his profits.\" Marx is entering the firm's own ledgers and showing that its ordinary bookkeeping rests on an obscured theoretical structure — a structure whose exposure is precisely his task. Theoretically , the chapter shows that the turnover of capital is the mediating link between the abstract circuit of value and the concrete, historical, cyclical life of the capitalist economy. It is the point where the formula M–C–P–C′–M′ becomes rhythm , and where rhythm becomes crisis . Against the Romantic school (Müller and his \"cycle of days\" and \"cycle of years\") and against the descriptive excess of Scrope, Marx replaces natural and financial explanations of economic periodicity with a materialist one: the working life of the machine, and the periodic necessity of replacing it. In doing so, he converts Chapter 9 from a dry exercise in accounting into a hidden load-bearing pillar of his theory of crises — and demonstrates, in miniature, the method of Capital as a whole: start with the technical necessity, abstract from the noise, and end with the historical pattern. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 8 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 10"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 10", "title": "Chapter 10", "subtitle": "Theories of Fixed and Circulating Capital. The Physiocrats and Adam Smith", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 10/Chapter 10 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 13757, "read_time": "69 min read", "tags": ["Fixed vs Circulating Capital", "Critique of the Smithian Dogma"], "keywords": ["Theories of Fixed and Circulating Capital (Physiocrats & Smith)", "Quesnay's Avances Annuelles vs Avances Primitives", "Adam Smith's Errors & Conflation with Constant/Variable", "Loss of the Concept of Surplus-Value in Circulation", "Smithian Confusion over Merchant and Banking Capital"], "text": "Chapter 10 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 13,695 words ⏱️ ~68 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 10: Theories of Fixed and Circulating Capital. The Physiocrats and Adam Smith — An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS This chapter is one of the strategic hinges of Marx's Capital , Volume 2, and it must be read as such before a single sentence of its individual arguments is assessed. Its stated surface topic — the \"theories of fixed and circulating capital\" — is in fact a critical-historical excavation: Marx is digging into the buried foundations of political economy in order to demonstrate that a single, deceptively technical terminological confusion (the equation of circulating capital with capital of circulation ) has, across a century of theory, systematically concealed the economic essence of capitalist production itself — namely, the distinction between constant capital and variable capital , and with it the origin of surplus-value. The chapter's core thesis can be stated in three interlocking propositions. First , the Physiocratic distinction between avances primitives (original, or \"primitive\" advances) and avances annuelles (annual advances), as formulated by Quesnay, was substantively correct in its logic even though it was confined to agricultural capital: Quesnay understood that the distinction is internal to productive capital , and that it is grounded in the different ways in which value circulates and is reproduced — one portion replaced completely each year, the other bit by bit over a longer (decennial) period. Second , Adam Smith's \"only step forward\" was to generalize these categories from farmer's capital to all productive capital, thereby converting them into the modern distinction between fixed and circulating (fluid) capital and replacing the agricultural twelve-month rhythm with the general notion of variable turnover periods . Third — and this is the polemical heart of the chapter — Smith's development then collapses : he commits a foundational confusion between (a) fixed vs. fluid capital , a distinction valid only within productive capital , derived from how value is transferred and replaced, and (b) productive capital vs. capital of circulation (commodity capital and money capital) , a distinction between forms that the same capital value successively assumes in its circuit . This confusion is compounded by a second error — substituting the workers' means of subsistence for labour-power in the list of circulating capital — which makes it structurally impossible for Smith and his successors to perceive that the portion of capital laid out on labour-power is variable capital . The result is that \"the whole secret of capitalist production is still further obscured.\" The central problem the chapter addresses is thus both analytical and historical. Analytically, it asks: what is the correct basis for dividing productive capital into fixed and circulating components? Marx's answer, developed by critical contrast, is that the distinction cannot be grounded in the material properties of things (a machine \"does not change masters\"; raw materials do), nor in the apparent way profit arises for the individual capitalist (profit \"by keeping\" vs. profit \"by parting with\"), but only in the mode of circulation of value through the labour-and-valorization process: whether the value laid out on an element of productive capital is transferred wholly and at once into the product (and thus must be wholly replaced out of the product's value each turnover) or gradually, in fragments, over a series of turnovers (and thus replaced bit by bit). Historically, the chapter asks: how did this correct insight get lost, and by what mechanism was it replaced by a definition that obscures the variable/constant distinction? The answer traces a direct line from Smith's blunder to the doctrines of his successors — including the \"labour fund\" doctrine that fixes the workers' share of the social product as a given magnitude. The chapter's intellectual objective is, in effect, negative-critical and positive-defensive at once. Negatively, it dismantles the Smithian apparatus category by category: the merchant's-capital example, the \"yield a revenue by remaining in possession\" formula, the machine-builder paradox, the seed-as-fixed-capital absurdity, the four-part list of circulating capital, and the tripartite division of social wealth into consumption fund/fixed/circulating. Positively, it vindicates Quesnay's method of deriving categories from the exigencies of the actual reproduction process , and it prepares the ground for the properly Marxian category of variable capital — the portion of capital that is not merely \"fluid\" but self-valorizing . How does the chapter fit into the broader theoretical framework of Capital ? Volume 2 as a whole analyzes capital in the sphere of circulation — the metamorphoses, circuits, and turnovers of capital — as distinguished from Volume 1's analysis of the production of capital . This chapter is a critical appendix or interlude within that analysis: after Marx has (in preceding chapters) worked out the circuit of capital M–C…P…C'–M' , the turnover, and the fixed/circulating distinction in its proper sense, he here turns to intellectual history to show that classical political economy — even at its best, in Smith — failed precisely because it did not hold firm to the distinction between capital in its productive form and capital in its circulation forms . The chapter therefore functions both as a history of theory and as a diagnostic demonstration : it shows, by concrete exegesis of Smith's text, that the categories of political economy are not neutral technical tools but carry within them, so to speak, their doctrinal commitments — that the displacement of labour-power by \"means of subsistence\" was not an incidental slip but the conceptual price of Smith's confusion, and that this price was the loss of the theory of surplus-value. Read in this way, the chapter is not a digression but a necessary clearing of the theoretical ground — \"clearing the decks,\" as it were — before the mature analysis of reproduction can proceed. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 Quesnay's Foundational Distinction: Avances Primitives and Avances Annuelles Marx opens the chapter by affirming that in the work of François Quesnay — the founder of the Physiocratic school — the distinction between fixed and circulating capital already appears , in embryo, as the distinction between avances primitives (\"original\" or \"primitive advances\") and avances annuelles (\"annual advances\"). These two French terms are not incidental vocabulary: they name the two parts of the farmer's capital stock. The avances primitives comprise the more durable, longer-lived investment — the initial fund required to \"commence cultivation,\" the implements, draught animals, land improvements, and the like. The avances annuelles comprise the recurring, yearly outlays — seed, fodder, wages, incidental materials — that must be made afresh each production cycle. Critically, Marx insists that Quesnay is correct in treating this as a distinction within productive capital — indeed, \"capital incorporated into the immediate production process.\" This point is decisive and it is worth spelling out why. Quesnay's advances are not money and they are not commodities sitting on the market. As Marx puts it, \"as advances for production, i.e. as productive capital, the two contrast both with money and with commodities on the market.\" The distinction thus arises only after money advanced has been transformed into the elements of productive capital — that is, once the capitalist has bought means of production and labour-power and put them to work. The distinction belongs to the inner anatomy of production , not to the monetary or market surface. Marx credits Quesnay with reducing this distinction to a precise, value-theoretic criterion: the different ways in which the two elements enter the value of the finished product , and correspondingly the different ways in which \"their value is circulated together with the value of the product, and the different ways in which they are replaced or reproduced, the value of one being completely replaced each year, that of the other bit by bit over a longer period.\" Two things follow. First, there emerges the notion of an annual turnover time for one part of capital, and a more-than-annual (decennial, i.e. ten-year) turnover time for the other. Second, because Quesnay treats agricultural capital as the only \"really productive\" capital (the famous Physiocratic doctrine that only agriculture yields a produit net , a net surplus), these distinctions \"in fact only arise for the farmer's capital.\" Marx notes with a historian's accuracy that \"in the course of development, the Physiocrats incidentally transferred these distinctions to other kinds of capital as well, to industrial capital in general\" — but the transference was incidental, not systematic. The footnote to this opening section is itself a miniature history of theory. Marx cites Quesnay's Analyse du tableau économique (in the Daire edition of the Physiocrates , Part I, Paris, 1846), where Quesnay writes: \"The annual advances consist of the expenditures annually made for the work of cultivation; these advances must be distinguished from the original advances which form the fund for the commencement of cultivation\" (p. 59). He then tracks the conceptual drift of the categories through three later figures. Dupont de Nemours , in the Maximes du Docteur Quesnay , already describes these advances \"much more directly as capital: 'capital or avances'.\" Le Trosne , in De l'intérêt social (Daire, II, pp. 928–9), generalizes the durability point into a statement about the nation as a whole: \"As a consequence of the longer or shorter lifespan of the instruments of labour, a nation possesses a considerable stock of riches independent of its yearly reproduction; this represents a capital accumulated over a long period and originally paid for with products, and it is continually maintained and increased.\" And Turgot , in the Réflexions sur la formation et la distribution des richesses (1766), \"already uses the term 'capital' for the avances more regularly, and more closely identifies the advances of the manufacturiers with those of the farmers.\" The trajectory traced here matters because it establishes the standpoint from which Marx will judge Smith: the Physiocrats, starting from the concrete exigencies of reproduction, arrived at the distinction from the inside ; everything Smith does afterward must be measured against that standard. 2.2 Smith's One Genuine Advance: Generalization of the Categories Marx is scrupulously fair at this juncture. \"The only step forward taken by Adam Smith,\" he writes, \"was to generalize these categories.\" Where Quesnay restricted the distinction to the farmer's capital, Smith \"no longer relate[s] them to just one special form of capital... but to every form of productive capital.\" What follows automatically is a generalization at the level of time . Quesnay's agricultural scheme, based on the natural rhythm of the harvest, placed the two parts of capital in a fixed relationship: an annual turnover for one, a more-than-annual turnover for the other. Once the distinction is generalized, this becomes a general distinction between turnovers of varying times , so that — and this formula is the pivot of Smith's advance — \"a turnover of fixed capital always comprises more than one turnover of circulating capital, whatever the length of turnover of this circulating capital may be — a year, greater than a year, or less than a year.\" This last clause is not rhetorical padding; it is analytically consequential. Under Quesnay, the avances annuelles necessarily had a one-year cycle. Under Smith's generalization, the circulating capital can have any turnover period whatsoever — shorter than a year, equal to a year, or longer — and the fixed/circulating distinction becomes the relational fact that one component of capital recovers itself across multiple turnovers of another. \"In Smith, therefore,\" Marx concludes, \"avances annuelles are transformed into circulating capital, avances primitives into fixed capital.\" But he immediately slams the door on any generosity: \"the progress he made was confined to this generalization of categories. In the development of his presentation, he falls far behind Quesnay.\" 2.3 \"Two Different Ways in Which a Capital May Be Employed\" — The Opening Ambiguity Marx locates the beginning of Smith's decline in the very method of his opening. \"The crudely empirical way in which Smith opens his investigation immediately introduces an ambiguity.\" Smith writes: \"There are two different ways in which a capital may be employed so as to yield a revenue or profit to its employer\" ( Wealth of Nations , Book Two, Chapter I, p. 185 in the Aberdeen 1848 edition; the Pelican 1974 edition, p. 374). Marx's objection is that this formulation misplaces the question . \"The ways in which value may be employed to function as capital, to yield a surplus-value to its owner, are as varied and manifold as the spheres of investment of capital\" — agriculture, manufacture, trade, and the many branches thereof. But so formulated, the question \"goes still further\": it includes \"the problem of how value, even if it is not invested as productive capital, can function as capital for its owner, e.g. as interest-bearing capital, merchant's capital, etc.\" Here \"we are already a world away from the real object of the analysis, i.e. the question how the division of productive capital into its various elements affects the turnover, irrespective of its different spheres of investment.\" The ambiguity, in short, is that Smith's opening frame conflates the question of investment sphere with the question of the internal composition of productive capital — and the latter, the only question that matters for the fixed/circulating distinction, is precisely the one Smith does not isolate. 2.4 The Merchant's-Capital Example: Smith Abandons the Physiocratic Ground Smith's next move makes the drift concrete. He says capital \"may be employed in raising, manufacturing, or purchasing goods, and selling them again with a profit.\" Marx reads this as a pure statement of spheres of investment — agriculture, manufacture, trade — with the crucial consequence that \"as in trade, capital is not incorporated into the immediate production process, and thus does not function as productive capital.\" At a stroke, \"he thus already abandons the basis on which the Physiocrats depicted the distinctions within productive capital and their influence on the turnover.\" Worse, \"he immediately takes merchant's capital as an example in a question where what is at issue is exclusively the differences within productive capital in the process of forming products and value, differences which in turn produce differences in its turnover and reproduction.\" Smith proceeds: \"The capital employed in this manner yields no revenue or profit to its employer, while it either remains in his possession, or continues in the same shape .\" Marx pounces. \"'The capital employed in this manner'! But Smith speaks of capital that is invested in agriculture and industry, and later tells us that the capital thus invested can be divided into fixed and circulating capital! The employment of capital in this manner can thus make the capital neither fixed nor circulating.\" Marx then grants Smith a charitable reconstruction in order to show it also fails. Perhaps Smith means that capital employed to produce commodities and sell them at a profit must, after transformation into commodities, be sold; by the sale it first passes from seller to buyer and secondly is converted from commodity form into money form, hence is \"useless to the possessor while it either remains in his possession, or continues in the same shape.\" But if that is the meaning, \"what emerges then is this: the same capital value that functioned previously in the form of productive capital... now functions as commodity capital and money capital, in the forms pertaining to the circulation process, and thus is no longer either fixed or fluid capital.\" And, crucially, \"this holds just as much for the elements of value that are added by way of raw and ancillary materials, thus by fluid capital, as for those added by the use of means of labour, i.e. by fixed capital. Thus we do not get a step nearer to the distinction between fixed and fluid capital.\" 2.5 Smith's Definition of Circulating Capital = Marx's \"Capital of Circulation\" Smith continues: \"The goods of the merchant yield him no revenue or profit till he sells them for money, and the money yields him as little till it is again exchanged for goods. His capital is continually going from him in one shape, and returning to him in another, and it is only by means of such circulation, or successive exchanges, that it can yield him any profit. Such capitals, therefore, may very properly be called circulating capitals.\" Here Marx's diagnosis becomes terminological with surgical precision. \"What Adam Smith here calls circulating capital is what I intend to call capital of circulation , capital in the form pertaining to the circulation process, pertaining to the change of form mediated by exchange (material change and change of hands), i.e. commodity capital and money capital , in contrast to the form pertaining to the production process, that of productive capital .\" The distinction between Marx's two terms is exactly the distinction Smith collapses: Capital of circulation (Marx's term) = the same capital value appearing successively as commodity capital (C) and money capital (M) — forms belonging to the circulation sphere. These are \"not particular ways in which the industrial capitalist divides his capital, but rather different forms that the same capital value, once advanced, successively assumes and discards throughout its curriculum vitae .\" Circulating/fluid capital (Marx's term) = a component of productive capital, defined by the speed and mode of its value transfer into the product. Smith \"lumps these together with the distinctions of form that arise within the circulation of the capital value, in its circuit through its successive forms, while the capital value exists in the form of productive capital, and this is a great step backward in relation to the Physiocrats.\" The real distinctions, Marx reiterates, \"arise in fact from the various ways in which the different elements of productive capital participate in the process of value-formation and transfer their value to the product.\" Let this sentence be underscored, because it is the positive criterion Marx will repeatedly apply to expose Smith's errors. Marx then states the equalization that Smith's confusion obscures: \"The capital value advanced in fixed capital is circulated via the product just as much as that advanced in fluid capital, and it is transformed into money capital through the circulation of the commodity capital every bit as much as the other.\" The only difference — the distinction itself — \"simply arises from the fact that its value circulates bit by bit, and must thus also be replaced bit by bit, in shorter or longer periods, and so be reproduced in this way in its natural form.\" 2.6 The Absurdity of Beginning with Merchant's Capital Marx sharpens the point by noting that Smith's own example is self-indicting. Smith takes \"a kind of capital that does not belong to the production process at all, but exclusively inhabits the circulation sphere and consists solely of capital of circulation — merchant's capital.\" And Smith says openly: \"The capital of a merchant... is altogether a circulating capital.\" \"The distinction between circulating and fixed capital, however, is supposedly, as we are later told, one arising from basic distinctions within productive capital itself.\" So Smith's example cannot illustrate the distinction he is supposedly defining. \"The two are completely jumbled up.\" Marx adds the substantive objection: \"There is no way of seeing how a profit is supposed to arise through the change of form between money and commodity, through a mere transformation of value from one of these forms to the other. Explanation of this is even made absolutely impossible in so far as Smith begins with merchant's capital, which moves solely within the circulation sphere.\" 2.7 Smith on Fixed Capital — and the Machine-Builder Paradox Turning to fixed capital, Smith writes: capital \"may be employed in the improvement of land, in the purchase of useful machines and instruments of trade, or in such-like things as yield a revenue or profit without changing masters, or circulating any further. Such capitals, therefore, may very properly be called fixed capitals. Different occupations require very different proportions between the fixed and circulating capitals employed in them... Some part of the capital of every master artificer or manufacturer must be fixed in the instruments of his trade. This part, however, is very small in some, and very great in others... The far greater part of the capital of all such master artificers\" (tailors, shoemakers, weavers), \"however, is circulated, either in the wages of their workmen, or in the price of their materials, and repaid with a profit by the price of the work.\" Marx sets aside the \"childish definition of the source of profit\" — profit \"repaid by the price of the work\" — to strike at the logical * contradiction. His example is the machine-builder. \"For a machine-builder, for example, the machine is the product that circulates as his commodity capital, i.e. in Smith's words, 'is parted with, changes masters, circulates further'. The machine would thus not be fixed but circulating capital, even according to his own definition.\" This is the machine-builder paradox , and it is a reductio ad absurdum*: by Smith's own criterion (does the thing change masters?), the identical machine is \"circulating\" for its producer and \"fixed\" for its user. Marx then generalizes: \"According to the position they assume in the life process of capital, therefore, the same things can function for Adam Smith as fixed capital (as means of labour, elements of productive capital), and as 'circulating' capital, commodity capital (as the product that is ejected from the sphere of production into that of circulation).\" 2.8 Smith's Second Self-Contradiction: Independent Capitals vs. Portions of One Capital Marx now catches Smith contradicting the very premise he started from a few lines earlier . The opening had framed fixed and circulating capital as \"two different ways in which a capital may be employed\" — as \"different modes of employment of distinct and independent capitals,\" so that a capital might be invested either in industry or in agriculture. But Smith then says: \"Different occupations require very different proportions between the fixed and circulating capitals employed in them.\" Marx notes the reversal: \"Fixed and circulating capital are now no longer distinct and independent capital investments, but rather different portions of the same productive capital, which form different shares of the total value in different spheres of investment. They are thus distinctions that arise from the division of productive capital itself, as it lies in the facts, and they therefore apply only in relation to this.\" And Smith again contradicts himself by counterposing commercial capital, as \"altogether a circulating capital,\" to fixed capital — even though \"what it is in fact is a capital functioning within the circulation sphere; as such it contrasts with productive capital in general... and for this very reason it can never be counterposed to the fixed component of productive capital as a fluid (circulating) component of productive capital.\" Three incompatible framings in a few pages: independent capitals (opening), portions of one capital (proportions passage), and merchant's capital as circulating capital (examples). \"The two are completely jumbled up.\" 2.9 The Materialist Fallacy: Why \"Fixed\" Is Not Fixity in the Means of Labour In Smith's examples, \"fixed capital\" is defined as \"instruments of trade,\" and \"circulating capital\" as the share laid out on wages and raw materials \"repaid with a profit by the price of the work.\" Marx observes that the starting-point here is \"simply the various components of the labour process, labour-power (labour) and raw materials on the one hand, instruments of labour on the other.\" These are components of capital only because \"a sum of value that is to function as capital is laid out on them\" — they are \"the material elements, modes of existence, of productive capital.\" So why is one part called fixed ? Because \"some part of the capital must be fixed in the instruments of trade.\" But — and here is the materialist fallacy — \"the other part, however, is also fixed, in wages and raw materials.\" Smith's criterion (\"yield a revenue or profit without changing masters, or circulating any further\") is a criterion drawn from material fixity in the labour process , not from value-circulation. Marx then delivers the decisive corrective: \"Thus capital is not fixed capital simply because it is fixed in the means of labour, but rather because a part of the value laid out on means of labour remains fixed in these, while another part circulates as a value component of the product.\" This sentence is the positive definition of fixed capital that the chapter has been building toward. The criterion is value-theoretic, not physical: what makes capital fixed is the partial, gradual transfer of its value into the product and the corresponding gradual, bit-by-bit replacement of that value. 2.10 The Copper-Mine Test Case Marx introduces a Gedankenexperiment — the copper mine — to demonstrate that Smith's definition, taken strictly, yields absurd results. In mining, \"there is no raw material involved, since the object of labour, e.g. copper, is a natural product that has first to be appropriated by labour.\" The as-yet-unappropriated copper does not form an element of the productive capital; no part of the value is laid out on it (it is nature , not a product). The other elements — labour-power, ancillaries like coal and water — \"do not enter materially into the product. The coal is entirely consumed, and only its value enters the product, just as a part of the value of the machine, etc. enters the product.\" The worker \"still exists just as independently vis-à-vis the product as does the machine. It is only the value that he produces through his labour that is now a component of the value of the copper.\" Now the reductio : \"In this example, therefore, not a single component of the productive capital changes hands ('masters'): none of these components is circulated further, because none of them materially enters the product. Where then is the circulating capital in this case? According to Smith's own definition, the whole of the capital employed in a copper mine consists solely of fixed capital.\" The consequence is fatal to Smith, because it destroys the universality of his dichotomy: if some capitals are entirely fixed, the distinction ceases to be a universal division of capital as such — the biological analogy Marx mocks later (\"a mammal is by natural necessity either male or female\") is already here exposed as false. 2.11 The Cotton-Yarn Test Case: Fluid Capital Is \"Fixed\" in Production The counter-example is the spinning of cotton into yarn, which does use raw material that becomes the substance of the product. Here, \"when the product, yarn for instance, changes hands, so does the raw material, the cotton, of which it consists, passing from the production process into that of circulation.\" But — and this is Marx's key point — \"as long as cotton functions as an element of productive capital, its owner does not sell it but works on it, makes yarn out of it. He does not let it go.\" To use Smith's own trivial phrase, he does not make a profit \"by parting with it, by its changing masters, or by circulating it. He no more has his materials circulate than his machines. They are fixed in the production process, just as much as are the spinning machines and factory buildings.\" Fluid capital, then, is just as fixed in the production process as fixed capital. \"As elements of productive capital, all its components are constantly fixed in the production process, since this cannot proceed without them. And all elements of productive capital, fixed as well as fluid, are, as productive capital, equally distinct from circulation capital, i.e. from commodity capital and money capital.\" Marx then gives the real distinction with precision: \"The distinction is simply that the cotton, coal, etc. needed for a week's production of yarn, for example, is completely consumed, and must therefore be replaced by new cotton, coal, etc.; thus these elements of productive capital, although they remain identical in kind, always consist of new items, whereas the same individual spinning machine and the same individual factory building continue to serve for a whole series of weeks of production, without being replaced by new items.\" 2.12 Labour-Power: The Same Logic, and the Crucial Differentia Marx extends the analysis to labour-power. \"A part of the productive capital must constantly be fixed in it, and it is generally the very same labour-powers, like the same machines, that are used by the same capitalist for a long period.\" Thus labour-power, too, is \"fixed\" in the labour process in the trivial sense Smith uses. But the real distinction between labour-power and machine is not that the machine is \"bought once and for all\" — \"which is in fact not the case when it is paid for by instalments, for example\" — \"while the worker is not,\" but rather \"that the labour that the worker expends enters entirely into the value of the product, while the value of the machine enters only bit by bit.\" This is the discriminating criterion: labour-power is fluid; machinery is fixed — on the value-transfer criterion, not on the material/ownership criterion . 2.13 Smith's Conflation of Formal and Bodily Metamorphoses Marx now pinpoints the deeper conceptual conflation. Smith says of circulating capital in contrast to fixed: \"The capital employed in this manner yields no revenue or profit to its employer, while it either remains in his possession, or continues in the same shape.\" Marx's charge: \"He places the merely formal commodity metamorphosis which the product, the commodity capital, undergoes in the circulation sphere and which mediates the commodities' change of hands, on the same level with the bodily metamorphosis which the various elements of the productive capital undergo during the production process.\" The two kinds of change are categorically different : Formal metamorphosis (circulation): the change of form C–M–C undergone by commodities as they are bought and sold — the change of hands and of value-form. Bodily metamorphosis (production): the material transformation of the elements of production into the intended product within the labour process. \"The metamorphoses that productive capital undergoes within its productive process, on the other hand, are metamorphoses pertaining to the labour process, which are necessary in order to transform the elements of production into the intended product.\" Smith, however, \"confines himself to saying that one part of the means of production (the means of labour proper) serve in the labour process... not by changing their natural form, but simply by being gradually worn out; whereas another part, the materials, are changed, and fulfil their function as means of production precisely through their alteration.\" But Marx insists: \"This differing behaviour of the elements of productive capital in the labour process, however, forms only the starting-point of the distinction between fixed and non-fixed capital, and not the distinction itself, as is already shown by the fact that it obtains equally for all modes of production, non-capitalist as well as capitalist.\" That is, the mere fact that tools wear out gradually while materials are consumed is a fact of every labour process, including a patriarchal family's — it is not yet the capital-theoretic distinction. What completes the distinction is: \"Corresponding to this different material role is the way in which value is surrendered to the product, to which further corresponds the way in which value is replaced by the sale of the product; and it is only this that constitutes the distinction in question.\" 2.14 The False Ground of Profit: \"By Staying With Him\" vs. \"By Going From Him\" Smith's formula at p. 380 — \"If it [the stock] is employed in procuring further profit, it must procure this profit either by staying with him [the employer], or by going from him. In the one case it is a fixed, in the other it is a circulating capital\" — draws from Marx a double indictment. First, it is \"the crudely empirical conception of profit, taken from the manner in which it appears to the ordinary capitalist, something that stands in complete contradiction to Smith's own better and esoteric insight.\" Marx's editorial footnote explains his usage: \"Marx frequently counterposes the falsely superficial or 'exoteric' elements in Adam Smith's writings with the deeper 'esoteric' insights that occasionally emerge.\" Second, the formula is substantively false : \"In the price of the product, the price of both materials and labour-power is replaced, but so, too, is the portion of value transferred from the instruments of labour to the product by wear and tear. Profit can in no case flow from this replacement.\" Whether value is replaced completely or bit by bit \"can alter only the manner and time of the replacement; in no case however can it transform what is common to both — the replacement of value — into a creation of surplus-value.\" The everyday notion \"that, because surplus-value is only realized by the sale of the product, by its circulation, it therefore arises simply from sale, from circulation\" lies at the bottom of the error. In truth, \"saying that profit arises in 'different ways' is here only an incorrect way of saying that the various elements of productive capital serve or function differently in the labour process as productive elements.\" The distinction, so framed, is \"not derived from the labour and valorization process itself... but is rather one that simply obtains subjectively for the individual capitalist.\" 2.15 Back to Quesnay: The Reproduction-Process Criterion The contrast with Quesnay is now drawn as a methodological verdict: \"Quesnay, on the other hand, derived the distinctions from the actual reproduction process and its exigencies. In order for this process to be continuous, the value of the annual advances has to be completely replaced each year out of the value of the annual product, whereas the value of the original investment capital need only be replaced bit by bit, so that it is only completely replaced over a series of e.g. ten years, and only in this way is it entirely reproduced (replaced by new items of the same kind). Thus Adam Smith falls a long way behind Quesnay.\" What Smith retains, after all his errors, is only the material fact \"that it [fixed capital] consists of means of labour that do not change their shape in the production process, and continue to serve in production until they are worn out, as opposed to the products which they help to form.\" But this is not the distinction. \"He forgets that all elements of productive capital are always distinct from the product... in their natural form (as means of labour, materials and labour-power), and that the distinction between the part consisting of materials and labour-power and the part consisting of means of labour simply lies, in the case of labour-power, in that it is always bought anew (not bought for its duration as with means of labour), and, in the case of the materials, in that it is not the very same ones, but ever new items of the same kind, that function in the labour process.\" Smith thus generates \"the illusion... that the value of the fixed capital does not also circulate, although Adam Smith has of course earlier explained that the wear and tear of the fixed capital forms part of the price of products.\" 2.16 The Fatal Concealment: Burying the Variable/Constant Distinction At this point Marx makes the strongest claim of the chapter. When Smith distinguishes circulating from fixed capital, \"what he emphasizes is not that this circulating capital is simply that component of the productive capital that must be completely replaced out of the value of the product, and must therefore go through all its metamorphoses together with the latter, whereas this is not the case with fixed capital.\" Instead, \"circulating capital is rather lumped together with the shapes that the capital assumes on its transition from the sphere of production to that of circulation, as commodity capital and money capital. These two forms, however, commodity capital and money capital, are bearers of both the fixed and the fluid components of the value of productive capital. Both are capital of circulation, in contrast to productive capital, but not circulating (fluid) capital in contrast to fixed.\" The consequence is the doctrinal catastrophe . \"The wholly erroneous explanation that fixed capital makes a profit by remaining in the production process, while circulating capital makes a profit by leaving this and circulating, permits the similarity of form that variable capital and the fluid component of constant capital have in the turnover to conceal the basic difference that they have in the valorization process and the formation of surplus-value, and in this way the whole secret of capitalist production is still further obscured.\" Because labour-power (variable capital) and raw materials (a fluid part of constant capital) share the same turnover form — both are entirely consumed and entirely replaced each cycle — Smith's framework treats them as categorically one : both are \"circulating capital.\" The inclusive characterization of both forms as circulating capital abolishes this fundamental distinction — and \"this was carried still further by later economists, who took the contrast between fixed and circulating capital as the basic and sole distinction, instead of distinguishing between variable and constant capital.\" 2.17 \"No Fixed Capital Can Yield Any Revenue but by Means of a Circulating Capital\" Smith nonetheless provides, in passing, the correct connecting proposition: \"No fixed capital can yield any revenue but by means of a circulating capital. The most useful machines and instruments of trade will produce nothing without the circulating capital which affords the materials they are employed upon, and the maintenance of the workmen who employ them.\" Marx interprets: \"Here we see what the earlier expressions 'yield a revenue', 'make a profit', etc. really mean, i.e. that both parts of capital serve in the formation of products.\" 2.18 The Farmer's Examples: Draught Cattle, Fodder, Fattening Cattle, Seed Smith now offers a sequence of agricultural examples, which Marx works through case by case. Draught cattle and implements (fixed). \"That part of the capital of the farmer which is employed in the implements of agriculture is a fixed, that which is employed in the wages and maintenance of his labouring servants is a circulating capital.\" Marx grants this is \"correctly related simply to the difference in circulation, to the turnover of different components of the productive capital.\" Cattle as value (the correct kernel). Smith: \"He makes a profit of the one by keeping it in his own possession, and of the other by parting with it. The price or value of his labouring cattle is a fixed capital in the same manner as that of the instruments of husbandry.\" Marx flags this as \"the further correct assertion that it is value to which the distinction refers, and not the material element.\" This is Smith almost reaching the correct, value-theoretic criterion. Fodder (circulating) and the draught-cattle distinction. Smith: \"Their maintenance [that of the labouring cattle] is a circulating capital, in the same manner as that of the labouring servants. The farmer makes his profit by keeping the labouring cattle, and by parting with their maintenance.\" Marx glosses: \"The farmer keeps the cattle's fodder, he doesn't sell it. He needs it as cattle-fodder while he uses the cattle themselves as instruments of labour. The distinction is simply that the cattle-fodder that enters the maintenance of the draught cattle is completely consumed and must be constantly replaced by new cattle-fodder from the agricultural product or its sale, while the cattle themselves are replaced only to the extent that each animal in succession becomes incapable of further work.\" Note the pattern: fodder = fluid, because wholly consumed and wholly replaced; cattle = fixed, because replaced only piecemeal. Fattening cattle (circulating). Smith: \"Both the price and the maintenance of the cattle which are brought in and fattened, not for labour, but for sale, are a circulating capital. The farmer makes his profit by parting with them.\" Marx objects on two grounds. First, the triviality: \"Every commodity producer, and thus the Capitalist producer as well, sells his product... but this does not make the product either a fixed or a fluid component of his productive capital. It now exists rather in a form in which it has been ejected from the production process and must function as commodity capital.\" Second — and this is the substantive point — the real reason fattening cattle are fluid is not that they are sold, but that \"fattening cattle function in the production process as raw material, not as an instrument like draught cattle. They therefore enter the product as substance, and their entire value enters the product, just as that of the ancillary materials — their fodder.\" The fact that the sold product (fattened cattle) shares its natural form with the raw material (unfattened cattle) \"is a mere accident.\" And Marx drives home the lesson: \"At the same time, Smith should have been able to see from this example that it is not the material shape of the element of production that makes the value contained in it fixed or fluid, but rather its function within the production process.\" Seed (fixed, in Smith's absurd account). Smith: \"The whole value of the seed, too, is properly a fixed capital. Though it goes backwards and forwards between the ground and the granary, it never changes masters, and therefore does not properly circulate. The farmer makes his profit not by its sale, but by its increase.\" Marx's rejoinder is withering: \"Here the utter shallowness of Smith's distinction comes into the open. In his conception, the seed is fixed capital because there is no 'change of masters', i.e. the seed is directly replaced out of the annual product, subtracted from it. It would be circulating capital, however, if the entire product were sold and new seed-corn were bought with one part of the product's value. In the one case there is a 'change of masters', in the other case not. Here Smith confuses fluid capital with commodity capital .\" Marx then corrects the matter in two moves. \"Whether the seed is directly subtracted from the product, or whether the whole product is sold and a part of its value is replaced by the acquisition of new seed, what occurs in both cases is no more than a replacement, and no profit is made by this replacement. In the one case the seed passes into circulation as a commodity along with the rest of the product, while in the other case it figures only in the book-keeping as a component of the value of the capital advanced. In both cases, however, it remains a fluid component of the productive capital. It is completely consumed in preparing the product, and it must be completely replaced out of this if reproduction is to be made possible.\" Thus Smith's book-keeping (whether seed is \"subtracted\" or \"bought back\") is irrelevant to the capital-theoretic question of whether the seed is fixed or fluid. 2.19 The Volume 1 Citation: Means of Labour Persist in Form Marx supports this with a citation from Capital Volume 1, Chapter 8 (p. 311): \"Hence raw material and auxiliary substances lose the independent form with which they entered into the labour process. It is otherwise with the actual instruments of labour. Tools, machines, factory buildings and containers are only of use in the labour process as long as they keep their original shape, and are ready each morning to enter into it in the same form. And just as during their lifetime, that is to say during the labour process, they retain their shape independently of the product, so too after their death.\" Marx then characterizes Smith's double falsification of this genuine labour-process distinction: he \"(1) introduces what is here the quite inapposite characteristic that some means of production bring their owner profit by maintaining their shape, others by losing it; (2) lumps the alterations suffered by one part of the elements of production in the labour process together with the change of form pertaining to the exchange of products, to commodity circulation (buying and selling), which at the same time includes the change of ownership of the commodities in circulation.\" 2.20 Turnover, Direct Replacement, and the Seed's Book-Keeping Marx pauses over the concept of turnover : \"Turnover implies that re-production is mediated by circulation, i.e. by the sale of the product, by its transformation into money and transformation back from money into its own elements of production.\" But there is an important special case: \"in so far as a part of his product again directly serves the same capitalist producer as means of production, the producer appears as selling this to himself; this is how the matter figures in his book-keeping. This part of reproduction is then not mediated by circulation, but directly.\" And this direct-replacement case concerns fluid capital, not fixed: \"The part of the product that serves again in this way as means of production replaces fluid capital, not fixed, in so far as (1) its value goes completely into the product and (2) it is itself replaced completely in kind by a new item from the new product.\" So the \"seed is fixed because it never changes masters\" argument is exploded once we see that direct replacement is a feature of fluid capital's turnover. 2.21 Capital as Function, Not Thing: The House Example and the Malleability of Roles Smith then lists the things that constitute fixed and circulating capital, \"as if this characteristic belonged to these things materially, by nature, and did not rather derive from their specific function within the capitalist production process.\" Yet — Marx notes the inconsistency — Smith himself says in the same chapter that a house reserved for direct consumption \"may yield a revenue to its proprietor, and thereby serve in the function of a capital to him, it cannot yield any to the public, nor serve in the function of a capital to it, and the revenue of the whole body of the people can never be in the smallest degree increased by it.\" Marx comments: \"Here Adam Smith clearly asserts that the property of being capital cannot be attributed to things as such and under all circumstances, but is rather a function with which they are or are not endowed according to the given conditions. But what is true of capital in general is also true of its subdivisions.\" Thus the same things may be fixed or fluid depending on function. \"Cattle used as draught-cattle (means of labour), for example, form a material mode of existence of fixed capital, while as fattening cattle (raw material) they are a component part of the farmer's circulating capital.\" And further, \"the same thing can function at one time as a component of productive capital, and at another time form part of the direct consumption fund. A house, for example, when it functions as a place of work, is a fixed component of productive capital; when it functions as a dwelling, it is in no way a form of capital in this capacity. The same means of labour can in many cases function at one time as means of production, at another time as means of consumption.\" Marx draws two consequences. First , the error bequeathed by Smith is \"that of taking fixed and circulating capital as characteristics attributable to things .\" The analysis of the labour process (Volume 1, Chapter 7) has shown \"how the determinations of means of labour, material of labour and product change according to the various roles that one and the same thing assumes in the process.\" The characteristics of fixed and non-fixed capital are built on these roles but are not reducible to them — they are built on \"the particular roles that these elements play in the labour process and hence in the process of value formation.\" Second , in Smith's enumeration, \"the distinction between fixed and fluid components, which is only valid, and only has any meaning, in relation to productive capital... [is] lumped together... with the distinction between productive capital and the forms pertaining to capital in its circulation process: commodity capital and money capital.\" 2.22 Smith's Four-Part List of Circulating Capital: A Catalogue of Confusions Smith says (p. 378): \"The circulating capital consists... of the provisions, materials, and finished work of all kinds that are in the hands of their respective dealers, and of the money that is necessary for circulating and distributing them...\" Marx reads this as confirming that Smith again equates circulating capital with commodity capital and money capital — \"two forms of capital that do not belong to the production process at all, which are not circulating (fluid) capital in opposition to fixed, but rather circulation capital in opposition to productive capital.\" Only \"alongside these\" do the genuinely productive components (raw material, semi-manufactured goods) figure. Smith's fuller statement at pp. 377–8 lists four parts of \"circulating capital\": 1. Money — \"of which the characteristic is, that it affords a revenue only by circulating or changing masters.\" 2. Stock and provisions in the hands of \"the butcher, the grazier, the farmer,\" etc., \"from the sale of which they expect to derive a profit.\" 3. Materials — \"whether altogether rude, or more or less manufactured, of clothes, furniture, and building, which are not yet made up... but which remain in the hands of the growers, the manufacturers, the mercers and drapers, the timber merchants, the carpenters and joiners, the brick-makers etc.\" 4. Finished work — \"the work which is made up and completed, but which is still in the hands of the merchant or manufacturer.\" Marx's commentary is systematic. On money (part 1): \"money is never a form of productive capital, capital functioning in the production process. It is never more than one of the forms which capital assumes within its process of circulation.\" On parts 2 and 4 (provisions, finished work): These \"simply contain products that have been ejected from the production process as such and have to be sold; in short, products that now function as commodities and hence as commodity capital — i.e. possess a form and assume a position in the process in which they do not constitute an element of productive capital, whatever may be their eventual destination, i.e. whether their purpose (use-value) finally fits them for individual or for productive consumption.\" On part 3 (materials): This is the most revealing, because it appears in a double aspect — first as finished commodities in the general social product alongside provisions, and second \"as incorporated into productive capital... in the hands of the producer.\" The confusion shows itself in the way raw materials are conceived \"as functioning both in the hands of the producer... and in the hands of merchants ('mercers, drapers, timber merchants'), where they are mere commodity capital, not components of productive capital.\" Smith also demonstrates the confusion in his treatment of the merchant : \"If the producer has sold his product to the merchant, this no longer constitutes capital of his in any form. From the social point of view, however, it is still just as much commodity capital, even if in other hands than those of its producer. But precisely because it is commodity capital, it is neither fixed nor fluid capital.\" Marx then states the general truth that Smith loses sight of: \"In every production not directed towards satisfying the producer's own immediate needs, the product must circulate as a commodity, i.e. be sold, not so that a profit may be made on it, but simply so that the producer may live. In the case of capitalist production, the sale of the commodity also realizes the surplus-value contained in it. The product passes out of the production process as a commodity, and is therefore no longer either a fixed or a fluid element of this process.\" 2.23 Smith Refutes Himself: The Same Things Change Category Upon Sale Smith's finished products, \"whatever may be their material shape or use-value... are all commodity capital.\" \"Because they exist in this form, they cannot constitute a component of their owner's productive capital; but this in no way prevents them, once they are sold, from becoming components of productive capital, whether fluid or fixed, in the hands of their buyer. It is evident here that the same things that enter the market at one time as commodity capital in opposition to productive capital may function as either fluid or fixed components of productive capital, or as neither, once they are withdrawn from the market.\" Marx illustrates with the cotton spinner . The spinner's product, yarn, \"is the commodity form of his capital, commodity capital for him. It cannot function again as a component of his productive capital, either as material of labour or as means of labour. The weaver who buys it, however, incorporates it into his productive capital, as a fluid part of this. For the spinner, on the other hand, the yarn is the bearer of the value of a part of both his fluid and his fixed capital (we ignore surplus-value).\" Likewise a machine: for the machine-builder it is commodity capital, \"and as long as it persists in this form, it is neither fluid nor fixed capital. When sold to a manufacturer who puts it to use, it becomes a fixed component of a productive capital.\" Even the case where a product re-enters its own production — \"as when coal re-enters the production of coal\" — does not change the category: \"the part of the coal product destined for sale still represents neither fluid nor fixed capital, but rather commodity capital.\" And in the extreme case: a means of subsistence is \"completely incapable of forming any element of productive capital.\" \"It is none the less commodity capital for its producer, the bearer of value of both the fixed and the fluid capital; and in the proportion that the capital bestowed on its production must be completely or partially replaced, its value has been transferred wholly or partly to it.\" Marx then diagnoses the specific confusion in Smith's \"third case\" (materials): they appear \"in the first place not as a component already incorporated into productive capital, but in fact only as a special kind of those use-values, the mass of commodities, which the social product consists of in general, alongside the other material components, means of subsistence, etc. listed in the second and fourth cases. Secondly, however, they are also presented as incorporated into productive capital, and hence as elements of the latter in the hands of the producer.\" Smith's list thus \"completely forgets the distinction between fixed and fluid capital, which is applicable only to productive capital. Instead he counterposes commodity capital and money capital... to productive capital, although even this he does unconsciously.\" 2.24 The Forgotten Labour-Power: Why Smith Cannot See Variable Capital Marx: \"A final striking thing is that Adam Smith forgets labour-power in his list of the components of circulating capital.\" He gives two reasons . Reason one: labour-power on the market is not capital. \"We have already seen that, leaving aside money capital, circulating capital is [for Smith] only another name for commodity capital.\" But \"in so far as labour-power circulates on the market, it is not capital, and so not a form of commodity capital. It is not capital at all; the worker is not a capitalist, even though he brings a commodity to market, i.e. his own skin. It is only when labour-power has been sold and incorporated into the production process — i.e. after it has ceased to circulate as a commodity — that it becomes a component of productive capital: variable capital as the source of surplus-value, a fluid component of the productive capital in relation to the turnover of the capital value laid out on this. \" This is a compressed statement of Marx's entire theory: labour-power enters productive capital as variable capital — the source of surplus-value — while being, in its turnover, a fluid component . Reason two: Smith substitutes means of subsistence for labour-power. \"Because Smith confuses fluid capital with commodity capital, he cannot bring labour-power under his heading of circulating capital. Variable capital thus appears here in the form of the commodities that the worker buys with his wages, the means of subsistence. It is in this form that the capital value laid out on wages is supposed to form part of the circulating capital.\" Marx's rebuttal: \"But what is incorporated into the production process is labour-power, the actual worker, and not the means of subsistence with which the worker maintains himself.\" He concedes that, \"considered from the society's standpoint, the reproduction of the worker himself by his individual consumption forms part of the reproduction process of the social capital\" (citing Volume 1, Chapter 23), \"but this does not hold for the individual production process taken by itself, which is what we are considering here.\" He adds the pointed aside — with Smith's notion that \"acquired and useful abilities\" (p. 377) belong to fixed capital — that these \"form on the contrary components of fluid capital once they are 'abilities' of the wage-labourer, who has sold his abilities together with his labour.\" 2.25 The Great Error: Dividing Social Wealth into Consumption Fund / Fixed / Circulating Marx now elevates the critique to the level of social wealth. \"It is a great error on Smith's part that he divides up the whole social wealth into (1) immediate consumption fund, (2) fixed capital and (3) circulating capital.\" Under this scheme, \"wealth would be divided into a consumption fund that does not form a part of the functioning social capital, although parts of it may always function as capital, and capital. One part of the wealth accordingly functions as capital, the other part as non-capital or a consumption fund.\" And it appears \"as an indispensable necessity for all capital to be either fixed or fluid, just as a mammal is by natural necessity either male or female.\" The biological analogy is deliberately derisory: it naturalizes a historically specific distinction and treats it as exhaustive. But \"the opposition of fixed and fluid is only applicable to the elements of productive capital, and... alongside this there is still a very significant amount of capital — commodity capital and money capital — which exists in a form in which it cannot be either fixed or fluid.\" Marx then offers a corrective on the circulation of the social product: \"Since, with the exception of the part of the product that is directly used in its natural form as means of production by the individual capitalist producer himself, without sale or purchase, the entire mass of social production — on the capitalist basis — circulates on the market as commodity capital, it is clear that both fixed and fluid elements of productive capital, and, in addition, all elements of the consumption fund, are drawn from the commodity capital.\" This is \"saying no more than that both means of production and means of consumption first appear, on the basis of capitalist production, as commodity capital... just as labour-power itself is found on the market as a commodity, even if not as commodity capital.\" 2.26 Smith's \"Fixed Capital Derives from Circulating Capital\" — and Its Self-Refutation Smith's doctrine at p. 378 — that \"three [provisions, materials, finished work]... are, either annually, or in a longer or shorter period, regularly withdrawn from it [circulating capital], and placed either in the fixed capital or in the stock reserved for immediate consumption. Every fixed capital is both originally derived from, and requires to be continually supported by a circulating capital\" — draws Marx's full counter-argument. Marx grants the truism : \"All products come onto the market as commodities... whether the natural or use-form of these products means that they can or must function as means of production... or whether they can serve only as means of individual rather than productive consumption.\" Thus \"all means of production and consumption, all elements of productive and individual consumption, must therefore be withdrawn again from the market as commodities, by purchase.\" And this \"holds good equally for the fixed and for the fluid elements of productive capital, for means of labour as well as material of labour in all forms.\" Marx adds a caveat in parentheses — \"(It is still overlooked here that there are elements of productive capital which are given by nature, and are not products.)\" — which is a nod to his own mining example: land, ore deposits, and natural forces enter productive capital without having been commodities. From the truism, nothing follows about fixed capital deriving from fluid capital. \"The machine is bought on the market as much as the cotton is. But it in no way follows from this — it follows only from Smith's confusion of circulation capital with circulating or fluid, i.e. non-fixed capital — that every fixed capital originally derives from a fluid one.\" Marx then turns Smith against himself. \"According to him, machines, as commodities, belong to the fourth part of the circulating capital. That they derive from the circulating capital thus only means that they functioned as commodity capital before they functioned as machines, although materially they derive from themselves; just as cotton as a fluid element of the spinner's capital derives from cotton on the market.\" And if Smith means, more substantively, that machines are made from labour and raw material, \"it is still the case, firstly, that means of labour, i.e. fixed capital, are necessary to make machines, and secondly, too, that fixed capital — machinery, etc. — is necessary in order to make raw materials, since productive capital always includes means of labour, but not always material of labour.\" Smith himself concedes as much: \"Lands, mines, and fisheries, require all both a fixed and a circulating capital to cultivate them\" — \"he thus concedes that fixed capital is needed to produce raw material, as well as circulating capital.\" But then Smith slides into a \"new muddle\": \"and their produce replaces with a profit, not only those capitals, but all the others in society.\" Marx's assessment: \"This is totally confused. Their product supplies the raw material, ancillaries, etc. for all other branches of industry. But their value does not replace the value of all other social capitals; it replaces only its own capital value (plus surplus-value). Here again Smith is looking back to the Physiocrats.\" 2.27 Mobility, Immobility, and the Fixity of \"Fixed Capital\" Marx now considers the important qualification that a product's physical mobility affects whether it can ever function as fixed capital, without thereby becoming the criterion of fixed capital. From the social standpoint, \"the part of commodity capital that consists of products that can only serve as means of labour, also functions sooner or later as means of labour — otherwise the products will have been produced to no avail, will be unsaleable.\" On the capitalist basis, \"once they have ceased to be commodities, they must form actual elements of the fixed part of the social productive capital, which they already were prospectively.\" But \"there is a distinction here which arises from the natural form of the product.\" Consider a spinning machine . It \"has no use-value if it is not used for spinning, i.e. does not function as an element of production, and thus, from the capitalist standpoint, as a fixed component of a productive capital. But the spinning machine is mobile. It can be exported from the country where it is produced and be sold, directly or indirectly, to a foreign country... In the country where it was produced it then functions only as commodity capital, but never, not even after its sale, as fixed capital.\" Now consider immobile products — \"products that have been localized by being incorporated into the earth, and hence can only be used locally, e.g. factory buildings, railways, bridges, tunnels, docks etc., soil improvements, and so on.\" They \"cannot be exported body and soul. They are immobile. If they are not to be useless, they must function after their sale as fixed capital in the country in which they were produced.\" For the speculative builder, these things are \"the form of his commodity capital, and so according to Smith the form of his circulating capital. But from the society's standpoint, they must ultimately function as fixed capital, if they are not to be useless, in the country in question.\" Marx guards against a hasty inference: \"It in no way follows from this that immobile objects as such are automatically fixed capital; they may be dwelling-houses, etc. that belong to the consumption fund and thus do not form part of the social capital at all, even though they form an element of the social wealth, of which capital is only one part. The producer of these things, to express ourselves in Smith's terms, makes a profit by their sale. So they are circulating capital! The person who puts them to use, their ultimate buyer, can use them only by employing them in the production process. So they are fixed capital!\" The railway illustrates the dissociation of legal form from physical function: \"Property titles to a railway, for instance, can change hands daily, and their owners can even make a profit by selling them abroad. The property titles are thus exportable, but the railway itself is not. It is no less the case, however, that these things must either function as the fixed component of a productive capital in the actual country where they are located, or else lie idle.\" Similarly, \"manufacturer A can make a profit by selling his factory to manufacturer B, but this does not prevent the factory from functioning now as before as fixed capital.\" The general conclusion is stated with the precision of a logical theorem: \"The locally fixed means of labour... must necessarily function prospectively as fixed capital in the country in question. But it in no way follows, conversely, that fixed capital necessarily consists of immovable objects.\" Marx offers two counter-examples: - Mobile yet fixed: \"A ship and a locomotive operate only by moving, yet they function as fixed capital for their users, even if not for their producers.\" - Immobile within production yet fluid: \"Things on the other hand that are most fully fixed in the production process, live and die in it, and never leave it after they have once entered it, can be fluid components of productive capital. For example, the coal that drives the machine in the production process, the gas consumed in lighting a factory building, etc. These are fluid not because they physically leave the production process along with the product, and circulate as commodities, but rather because their value enters completely into the value of the commodity that they help to produce, and must thus be entirely replaced from the sale of the commodity.\" This is the positive criterion once more: fluidity is a matter of the completeness of value-transfer, not physical movement. 2.28 The Physiocratic Origins of Smith's Wage Conception — and Why It Doomed the Theory Marx now returns to the phrase in Smith's passage: \"A circulating capital, which furnishes... the maintenance of the workmen who make them\" (the machines). \"With the Physiocrats, the portion of capital advanced in wages figured correctly under the heading 'avances annuelles', as contrasted with 'avances primitives'. On the other hand, what appears with them as a component of the productive capital applied by the farmer is not labour-power itself, but rather the means of subsistence given to the agricultural workers ('the maintenance of the workmen', as Smith puts it).\" Marx explains this as directly related to the Physiocratic doctrine . \"The portion of value which labour adds to the product (like the portion of value added by raw materials, instruments of labour, etc. — in short by the material components of the constant capital) is equal only to the value of the means of subsistence paid to the workers and necessarily consumed by them to maintain their function as labour-powers.\" The Physiocrats could not, on their own premises, find surplus-value in labour as such, because \"if it is labour that produces surplus-value (as well as reproducing its own price), then it produces this in industry just as much as in agriculture. But since in the Physiocratic system labour produces surplus-value only in one branch of production, agriculture, surplus-value was not seen as arising from labour, but rather from the special activity (collaboration) of nature in this branch. It was for this reason that they saw agricultural labour as productive labour, in distinction from other kinds of labour.\" The very doctrine of the Physiocrats thus \"prohibited them from discovering the distinction between constant capital and variable capital.\" 2.29 The Two Roots of Smith's Error on Wages Marx then gives a landmark two-fold diagnosis of why Smith defines the workers' means of subsistence as circulating capital in opposition to fixed. (1) The confusion of circulation capital with fluid capital. \"He therefore confuses commodity capital with the fluid component of productive capital, and it is then self-evident that, where the social product takes the form of a commodity, the workers' means of subsistence, just like those of the non-workers — not to mention the materials and means of labour themselves — have to be supplied out of commodity capital.\" (2) The Physiocratic conception creeps in. \"But the Physiocratic conception also creeps in with Smith, although it contradicts the esoteric — genuinely scientific — part of his own theoretical presentation.\" Marx then lays out the reasoning that exposes the substitution as a theoretical substitution with a definite function. \"All capital advanced is converted into productive capital, i.e. it assumes the shape of elements of production which are themselves the product of earlier labour. (Including labour-power.) Only in this form can it function in the production process.\" Now, \"if we substitute the worker's means of subsistence for the actual labour-power into which the variable part of capital has been transformed, then it is clear that these means of subsistence as such are not different from the other elements of productive capital as far as the formation of value is concerned, not different for example from raw materials and from the means of subsistence of draught cattle\" — and Marx notes this is why Smith, following the Physiocrats, \"puts these all on the same level in one of the passages quoted above\" (cross-referencing p. 279). \"The means of subsistence cannot themselves valorize their value or add to it a surplus-value. Their value, like that of the other elements of productive capital, can reappear only in the value of the product. They cannot add more value to it than they themselves possess.\" By this substitution, labour-power is reduced to one more material element of constant capital, and the specific role of the capital laid out on it — that it is variable , self-valorizing — disappears. \"They are only distinguished from the fixed capital, which consists of means of labour, in the same way as are raw material, semi-finished goods, etc., namely in that they are completely consumed in the product that they help to form... and their value must thus be completely replaced, whereas replacement occurs only gradually, bit by bit, in the case of fixed capital.\" Hence: \"The part of productive capital advanced in labour-power (or the means of subsistence of the worker) is thus distinguished here only materially , and not with regard to the labour and valorization process, from the remaining material elements of the productive capital. It is only distinguished in that it falls into the category of circulating capital, along with one part of the objective elements of product formation ('materials' is Smith's general term for them), in opposition to another part of the objective elements that falls into the category of fixed capital.\" 2.30 The Decisive Separation: Fluidity in Turnover vs. Variability in Valorization Marx then states the pivotal distinction with full clarity. \"Although the part of capital spent on wages belongs to the fluid part of productive capital, and has this fluidity in common with a portion of the objective elements of product formation, the raw materials, etc., as opposed to the fixed component of productive capital, this has absolutely nothing to do with the role that this variable part of capital plays in the valorization process as opposed to the constant part.\" To be \" fluid \" is a fact about turnover : \"It is simply related to how this part of the capital value advanced has to be replaced, renewed, and thus reproduced out of the value of the product, by way of circulation. The purchase and re-purchase of labour-power pertains to the circulation process.\" But \"it is only within the production process that the value laid out on labour-power is transformed (not for the worker, but for the capitalist) from a definite, constant quantity into a variable one, and the value advanced in capital value, in capital, is thereby transformed for the first time into self-valorizing value.\" The distinction between fluidity and variability is thus the hinge: it is possible for a portion of capital to be fluid without being variable (raw materials), and it is the defining property of the portion laid out on labour-power to be both fluid in turnover and variable in valorization. Because Smith replaces labour-power with means of subsistence, however, \"it is impossible for him to understand the distinction between variable and constant capital, and thus the capitalist production process in general. The characteristic of this part of capital as variable capital in opposition to the constant capital laid out on the objective elements of product formation is buried underneath the characteristic that the part of capital laid out on labour-power belongs to the fluid part of the productive capital with respect to the turnover.\" And \"this burial is made complete in so far as in place of labour-power it is the worker's means of subsistence that are counted as an element of productive capital.\" Marx adds a qualification: \"Whether the value of the labour-power is advanced in money or in means of subsistence is immaterial, even though the latter can of course only be the exception on the basis of capitalist production.\" 2.31 The Legacy: The Labour-Fund Doctrine The concluding paragraphs trace the historical consequences. \"Because Adam Smith fixed in this way upon the characteristic of circulating capital as the decisive one for capital value laid out on labour-power — the Physiocratic definition without the premises of the Physiocrats — he managed to make it impossible for his successors to perceive that the part of capital laid out on labour-power was variable capital. The profound and correct explanation that he himself offered elsewhere did not prevail, whereas this blunder did.\" Marx then states the terminal drift of the doctrine: \"Indeed, later writers went even further, and not only made it the decisive characteristic of the part of capital laid out on labour-power to be circulating capital in opposition to fixed, but also made it the fundamental characteristic of circulating capital to be laid out on means of subsistence for the worker. This naturally linked up with the doctrine of the labour fund of necessary means of subsistence as a given magnitude, which on the one hand physically restricts the share of the workers in the social product, but on the other hand has to be spent to its full extent on the acquisition of labour-power.\" The footnote reference is to Volume 1, pp. 758–61, where Marx discusses this \"labour fund\" doctrine. The final footnote, at the very end of the chapter, closes the case against Smith with a quotation in which the reduction of labour-power to a mere natural input reaches its clearest expression. Marx writes: \"How much Adam Smith barred his own way to an understanding of the role of labour-power in the valorization process is shown by the following sentence, which puts the labour of the worker on the same level as that of draught cattle, in the Physiocratic manner: 'Not only his [the farmer's] labouring servants, but his labouring cattle, are productive labourers' (Book Two, Chapter V, p. 462).\" The chapter thus ends not with a mild observation but with a deliberate juxtaposition of worker and beast — the logical terminus of a theory that has substituted means of subsistence for labour-power and must, to be consistent, treat both labour-power and cattle-fodder as \"productive\" in identical fashion. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter's fundamental systematic laws and essential takeaways can be enumerated as follows. (1) The law of the value-theoretic criterion. The distinction between fixed and circulating (fluid) capital is a distinction in the mode of value circulation through the labour-and-valorization process, not in the natural properties of things, nor in their physical mobility, nor in their legal relation to their owner, nor in the subjective experience of the individual capitalist (\"profit by keeping\" vs. \"profit by parting with\"). Fixed capital is fixed because a part of the value laid out on the means of labour remains fixed in them and transfers only bit by bit to the product, and thus must be replaced bit by bit. Fluid capital is fluid because its value enters the product wholly and at once and must therefore be wholly replaced each turnover. Consequently, the same thing can shift category as it shifts function: cattle as draught animals are fixed; the same cattle as fattening stock are fluid; a house functioning as a workplace is fixed capital; the same house as a dwelling is not capital at all. (2) The law of the separateness of circulation forms from productive forms. The forms commodity capital (C) and money capital (M) belong to the sphere of circulation and are assumed successively by every capital value in its curriculum vitae ; they are bearers of both fixed and fluid value components and cannot themselves be classed as either fixed or fluid. To confuse them with fixed/fluid capital is the foundational error of Smith's theory — the error of \"circulating capital\" equivocation . (3) The law of the priority of the valorization distinction. Because labour-power and raw materials share the turnover characteristic of being wholly consumed and wholly replaced (both \"fluid\"), Smith's generic category \"circulating capital\" collapses them. But this destroys the ability to see that the portion of capital laid out on labour-power alone is self-valorizing — variable capital — while the portion laid out on raw materials is constant. The turnover similarity is superficial; the valorization difference is fundamental. Smith's category works against — not merely fails to reach — the correct analysis. (4) The law of historical determination of categories by doctrine. Marx's final thesis is that economic categories are not innocent technical tools. The Physiocrats could not discover variable capital because their doctrine tied surplus-value to the productivity of nature in agriculture (the produit net ); Smith could not discover it because he inherited their substitution of \"means of subsistence\" for \"labour-power\" and compounded it with the confusion of circulation forms with productive forms. The \"labour fund\" doctrine of Smith's successors is thus not a deviation from Smith but a logical development of his error. The broader theoretical implications for Marx's system are substantial. First, the chapter demonstrates that Volume 2's analysis of turnover presupposes the Volume 1 analysis of valorization — that the fluids and fixities of turnover can only be properly understood once one has separated constant from variable capital. The chapter is thus the methodological bridge between the two volumes: it shows that the \"turnover\" categories, taken by themselves (as Smith took them), become ideological instruments that obscure the origin of surplus-value. Second, the chapter clarifies Marx's own vocabulary: \"capital of circulation\" is carefully distinguished from \"circulating capital\" ; the difference matters because it preserves the distinction between a form of capital in a particular phase of its circuit and a component of productive capital. Third, the chapter prepares the ground for the later, completed analysis of the reproduction schemes, in which fixed and circulating capital must be carefully tracked in value terms, not in natural or book-keeping terms. The practical or ideological implications are also clear. The \"labour fund\" doctrine that emerges from Smith's error is, as Marx specifies, not merely scholarly misclassification but a material doctrine: it treats the workers' means of subsistence as a fixed, pre-given quantity of capital, and hence, in effect, restricts the workers' wage to whatever that fund contains — a conclusion with direct implications for class struggle. The chapter thus documents, at the highest level of abstraction, the way that a technical error in classification is not neutral: it licenses a political conclusion that labour's share is naturally bounded. When Marx writes at the opening of the chapter that Smith's manner of treating capital \"was carried still further by later economists, who took the contrast between fixed and circulating capital as the basic and sole distinction, instead of distinguishing between variable and constant capital,\" he is not merely correcting a taxonomy — he is exposing the doctrinal cost of an entire century of \"scientific\" economics. Finally, the chapter is a philosophical lesson in the nature of abstraction . Marx does not simply reject Smith; he carefully separates Smith's \"esoteric\" insights from his \"exoteric\" formulations, preserving what is correct (the value-theoretic character of the fixed/fluid distinction, the correct observation that fixed capital can function only alongside circulating capital, the correct insight that land is needed to produce raw material) while criticizing the framing that reverses the truth. The chapter thus performs a paradigm of immanent critique: the errors are diagnosed out of Smith's own text , by using Smith's correct moments against his incorrect ones, and by showing that his categories, followed rigorously, generate absurdities (a machine is circulating for its maker and fixed for its user; seed is fixed and cattle are circulating on grounds that contradict the reasoned function of each). The example of the copper mine and the spinning factory, taken as extreme test cases, demonstrate both that Smith's classification yields the absurd result that an entire capital can be all fixed , and that fluid capital is just as \"fixed in the process\" as fixed capital — so that Smith's material criterion cannot generate the distinction. In this way the chapter demonstrates not just that Smith was wrong on a point, but that the whole habit of classifying capital by the natural properties of things is wrong — a lesson with implications far beyond the question of fixed and circulating capital, extending to every domain in which economic relations are naturalized. The final image — the worker on the same level as the draught ox, both classified as \"productive labourers\" — crystallizes the stakes: an error in the theory of capital becomes, at last, a falsehood about human beings. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 9 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 11"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 11", "title": "Chapter 11", "subtitle": "Theories of Fixed and Circulating Capital. Ricardo", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 11/Chapter 11 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 8834, "read_time": "44 min read", "tags": ["Fixed vs Circulating Capital"], "keywords": ["Theories of Fixed and Circulating Capital (Ricardo)", "Ricardo's Conflation of Fixed/Circulating with Capital Value", "Disappearance of Variable Capital in Classical Economy", "Durability of Capital Assets vs Value Transfer", "Critique of Ricardian Wage Variations"], "text": "Chapter 11 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 8,795 words ⏱️ ~44 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem: A Category That Swallows Its Own Object Chapter 11 of Marx's Theories of Surplus Value — the opening salvo of his extended engagement with David Ricardo — is not a chapter about capital in the ordinary sense. It is a chapter about the epistemological damage done by a single conceptual conflation : the identification of the antithesis fixed capital vs. circulating capital with the antithesis constant capital vs. variable capital . Marx opens with the flat declaration that \"the basic confusion is however evident from the start,\" and the rest of the chapter is a forensic demonstration of how far the consequences of that confusion ramify — into the theory of value, the theory of surplus-value, the theory of wages, the theory of the rate of profit, and ultimately into the ideological self-concealment of the capitalist mode of production itself. Ricardo's motive for raising the distinction is narrow and technical. As Marx states in the first sentence, Ricardo \"introduces the distinction between fixed and circulating capital only in order to present the exceptions to the law of value, i.e. those cases in which the rate of wages affects prices.\" Marx defers his own full treatment of those exceptions to Capital Volume 3, Chapter 11 (a cross-reference the editors supply). But the narrowness of Ricardo's purpose is precisely what makes his confusion so instructive: a technical question about price-formation turns out to rest on a fundamental category error about what capital is and what makes it capital . Marx's thesis is therefore dialectical in structure: the very distinction that appears to explain the exception to the law of value, if it is confused with the valorization-distinction, destroys the law of value itself — and, with it, \"the whole secret of surplus-value formation and of capitalist production.\" The Intellectual Objective: Recovering the Esoteric Ricardo The chapter has three interlocking objectives. First, exegetical dissection : to show exactly where and how Ricardo adopts Adam Smith's \"esoteric/exoteric\" split — Ricardo, Marx insists, is more surprising in his uncritical reception of Smith's confusion than the later apologists or Smith himself, because \"Ricardo, in contrast to Smith, presented value and surplus-value consistently and clearly, and in point of fact upheld the esoteric Adam Smith against the exoteric.\" Second, constructive clarification : to state precisely what fixed/circulating capital does mean (a distinction derived from the circulation process, resting on the manner in which value is transferred to the product), and why it is a secondary and derivative determination that cannot carry the theoretical weight of the constant/variable distinction. Third, genealogical critique : to trace the century-long transmission of Smith's error through Barton, Ricardo, John Stuart Mill, Ramsay, and down to the \"bank clerk\" standpoint of MacLeod and Patterson, in whom the whole distinction collapses into the banking distinction between \"money at call\" and \"money not at call.\" Its Place in the Broader Framework The chapter sits at the hinge of Marx's entire theoretical architecture. It presupposes Capital Volume 1 — the labour process and valorization process (Ch. 7), the organic composition of capital (Ch. 25 §2, cited at p. 772), and the fetishism of the commodity (Ch. 1 §4) — and it points forward to Capital Volume 3, where the equalization of the general rate of profit and the transformation of values into prices of production (Part Two) and the effect of wage fluctuations on prices of production (Ch. 11) are elaborated. Its methodological signature is the distinction between analyzing production \"from the standpoint of the phenomena in their finished form\" versus \"penetrating through to the inner mechanism of the capitalist production process.\" Everything in the chapter is an application of that methodological pivot. The chapter is thus simultaneously a work of intellectual history, a work of category-critique, and a work of ideological unmasking — and it establishes the vocabulary for the whole subsequent Marxian critique of \"circulating capital,\" including the critique of the wage-fund doctrine and of the notion that capital is simply \"advanced value awaiting reappearance.\" 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Opening Gambit: Why Ricardo Raises the Distinction at All Marx begins with a strategic restriction of scope that is also a diagnosis. Ricardo's reason for invoking fixed and circulating capital is not to characterize production but to handle a deviation from the norm. The law of value, in its Ricardian form, says that exchange-values are determined by quantities of embodied labour and are therefore, in the first instance, independent of the rate of wages: a general rise or fall of wages redistributes surplus-value between capitals but does not alter values. Ricardo notices, however, that when capitals of equal size are composed differently — some dominated by durable machinery, others by wages — a change in wages does alter relative prices. The fixed/circulating ratio is Ricardo's device for tracking these \"exceptions.\" Marx quarantines this question at the outset: \"We shall only come to speak of these in Volume 3\" — that is, in the treatment of the general rate of profit and prices of production. The bracketing is not evasion but methodological: Marx wants the reader to see that the legitimate question (how do wage changes affect prices of production?) is being answered with an illegitimate apparatus (a confusion of two distinct distinctions). 2.2 The Smithian Quid Pro Quo: \"Capital That Is to Support Labour\" The confusion, Marx says, is visible in the very juxtaposition of two formulations Ricardo places side by side. First, Ricardo speaks of \"this difference in the degree of durability of fixed capital, and this variety in the proportions in which the two sorts of capital may be combined.\" Marx then asks the naive, devastating question — what are the two sorts of capital? — and reads the answer straight out of Ricardo's next sentence: \"The proportions, too, in which the capital that is to support labour, and the capital that is invested in tools, machinery, and buildings, may be variously combined.\" The equation is thereby fixed in place: Fixed capital = means of labour (tools, machinery, buildings); Circulating capital = capital laid out on labour. The phrase \"the capital that is to support labour\" is, Marx says, \"itself an absurd expression taken over from Adam Smith.\" Its absurdity is threefold. (i) It suggests that the wage-bill supports labour, when in fact the worker's labour supports the capital. (ii) It treats the fund of wages as a thing with a natural function rather than as a social relation. (iii) It conflates the worker's means of subsistence with the capital that buys labour-power — a substitution Marx will dismantle at length later in the chapter. Because the opposition is \"not derived from the valorization process — constant and variable capital — but rather from the circulation process (the old Smithian confusion),\" two misconceptions arise. Marx names the first immediately. 2.3 First Misconception: Durability Versus Organic Composition The first misconception is that \"the differences in the degree of durability of the fixed capital, and the variations in the composition of capital in terms of constant and variable, are taken as equivalent.\" Marx's refutation is a precise statement of what each distinction governs: Variation in the constant/variable composition (the relation between capital laid out on means of production and capital laid out on labour-power) determines the variation in the production of surplus-value . It is a valorization-process determination. Variation in the degree of durability of fixed capital is, \"in so far as the valorization process is concerned, simply related to the manner in which a given value of means of production is transferred to the product.\" It is a value-transfer determination. And \"as far as the circulation process is concerned, it affects only the period of renewal of the capital laid out, in other words the time for which this is advanced.\" It is a turnover determination. Three distinct determinations are here disentangled: surplus-value production (constant/variable), mode of value transfer (fixed/circulating, in part), and turnover/advancement time (circulation). Ricardo treats them as one. Marx then concedes the phenomenal plausibility of the error: \"If, instead of penetrating through to the inner mechanism of the capitalist production process, you adopt the standpoint of the phenomena in their finished form, these distinctions do in fact coincide.\" When the total social surplus-value is distributed among branches as profit, differences in advancement time (long-lived fixed capital vs. quick-returning capital) and differences in organic composition both act as levers in the equalization of the general rate of profit and the transformation of values into prices of production. At the level of the \"finished form\" — the level Ricardo actually inhabits, the level of surface price phenomena — the two distinctions produce analogous effects. The error is therefore not a blunder of observation but a failure of abstraction : a surface equivalence is mistaken for an identity of inner causes. 2.4 Second Misconception: Two Cross-Cutting Classifications and the Vanishing Raw Material The second misconception concerns the categorization of things . From the standpoint of the circulation process, capital splits into (a) means of labour = fixed capital, and (b) material of labour plus wages = fluid/circulating capital. From the standpoint of the labour and valorization process, capital splits into (a) means of production — both means and material of labour — = constant capital, and (b) labour-power = variable capital. The two partitions cut reality differently. Marx demonstrates this with the organic composition analysis (cross-referencing Capital Vol. 1, Ch. 25, §2, p. 772). For the organic composition, \"it is quite immaterial whether the same value of constant capital consists of more means of labour and less material of labour, or of more material of labour and less means of labour, whereas everything depends on the relation between the capital laid out on means of production and that laid out on labour-power.\" Conversely, for the circulation-process distinction, \"it is just as immaterial in what proportion a given value of circulating capital is divided between material of labour and wages.\" The partitions are orthogonal: From the valorization standpoint, material of labour is ranked with means of labour (both are constant capital) against labour-power. From the circulation standpoint, labour-power is ranked with material of labour against means of labour. So far, this is merely a difference of perspectives. But Marx now springs the structural trap. In Ricardo, the part of capital value laid out on raw materials and ancillaries appears on neither side of the fixed/circulating divide. It cannot be sorted into fixed capital, because \"it completely coincides in its mode of circulation with the part of capital laid out on labour-power\" (both transfer their entire value at once). And it cannot be sorted into circulating capital, because circulating capital has already been defined as capital laid out on labour — so placing raw material there \"would be a self-refutation of the equation taken over from Adam Smith.\" Ricardo's instinct for logic is his undoing — or rather, the evidence of his honesty: \"Ricardo has far too great an instinct for logic not to be sensitive to this, and he therefore just lets this part of the capital disappear.\" The category system literally erases one-third of the material components of capital. 2.5 The Interlude on the \"Advance\": Who Advances Whom? Before returning to Ricardo, Marx inserts a compact polemic on the language of \"advance.\" Political economy habitually says the capitalist advances wages — for a week, a month, or three months, depending on the pay interval. Marx inverts this: \"In point of fact, the opposite happens. The worker advances the capitalist his labour for a week, a month or three months, according to the intervals at which he is paid.\" The conditional is decisive: if the capitalist bought labour in the strict sense — paid for it before it was performed — then \"advance\" would be the right word. But since payment occurs after the labour has lasted, the worker's advance of labour is \"transformed into an advance that the capitalist makes to the worker in money. This in no way alters the fact that the capitalist gets the product back from circulation... only after a shorter or longer period of time.\" The ideological function of the term \"advance\" is to present the capitalist as the party who gives first and takes a risk — when in truth the capital laid out on wages is itself labour already performed and not yet paid for. Marx then generalizes the point against a deeper prejudice: the notion that the duration for which a value is tied up confers an economic advantage or disadvantage in itself . \"What the buyer of a commodity might want to do with it is completely immaterial to the seller. The capitalist does not get a machine any cheaper because he has to advance its entire value all at once, while the same value flows back to him from the circulation sphere only gradually and bit by bit; nor does he pay more for cotton because its value enters completely into the value of the product made from it, and is thus completely replaced at one stroke when this is sold on the market.\" The length of time a value is advanced cannot by itself alter value. What it can do — and here Marx foreshadows Volume 3 — is differentiate the annual rates of profit of capitals of equal value, which then enters the equalization of the general rate of profit. Time is real in the sphere of profit-rate formation; it is not a source of value. This distinction is exactly the one Ricardo loses. 2.6 \"Let Us Then Return to Ricardo\" (1): The Obliteration of Variable Capital Marx now states, in the first of two numbered points, the characteristic feature of variable capital . A definite, given (and \"in this sense constant\") sum of value — assumed equal to the value of labour-power, though Marx is explicit that \"it is immaterial here whether the wage is the same as, or more or less than, the value of the labour-power\" — is exchanged for \"a force that valorizes itself and creates value — labour-power, which not only reproduces the value paid to it by the capitalist, but also produces a surplus-value, a value that did not previously exist and is not bought for an equivalent.\" Note the two-part structure of the definition: reproduction of the value advanced plus production of an excess that was never purchased . This is the differential specifica of variable capital, and it is precisely what disappears when wages are viewed purely as circulating capital. Why does it disappear? Because recategorizing wages as \"circulating\" places them \"together under a single heading (that of circulating capital) with a component of the constant capital, that laid out on material of labour,\" and counterposes that bloc to means of labour. Under this heading, \"surplus-value, i.e. the very circumstance which transforms the sum of value laid out into capital, is completely ignored.\" A second erasure follows: the distinction between the freshly produced value added by labour-power and the merely conserved value re-appearing from raw material is lost. Raw material's value is \"not freshly produced, and not really reproduced, but is simply maintained and conserved in the value of the product, and hence merely reappears as a component of the product's value.\" The fixed/circulating distinction, on inspection, says only this: the value of means of labour goes into the commodity partially and gradually ; the value of labour-power and of objects of labour goes in completely and at once . Marx's verdict is categorical. In both cases \"what is involved is a transfer of given, previously advanced values to the product, and their replacement when the product is sold. The sole distinction here is whether the transfer of value, and therefore the replacement of value, proceeds bit by bit and gradually, or all at once.\" Therefore: \" The all-important distinction between variable and constant capital is thereby obliterated, and with it the whole secret of surplus-value formation and of capitalist production , namely the circumstances that transform certain values and the things in which they are represented into capital.\" The categories of capital are reduced to distinctions in \"the mode of circulation\" — and circulation, Marx reminds us, \"has of course only to do with already existing, given values.\" Circulation can redistribute and return value; it cannot create it. A theory that classifies capital solely by its mode of circulation has, by construction, no place from which to explain surplus-value. 2.7 The Genealogy of the Error: Smith, Ricardo, and the Physiocratic Contrast Marx asks why bourgeois political economy \"held instinctively to Adam Smith's confusion\" for \"a whole century.\" The answer is structural: once wages and raw materials are merged into a single \"circulating\" fund counterposed to machinery, \"the basis for understanding the real movement of capitalist production, and thus of capitalist exploitation, was thus submerged at one blow. All that was involved, on this view, was the reappearance of values advanced.\" The theory becomes a theory of circulation without production of surplus-value — a comfortable resting place for an apologetic science. Ricardo's case is, Marx says, more surprising — more surprising than the later apologists, \"among whom the confusion of concepts is rather something unsurprising,\" and more surprising even than Smith — because Ricardo did have a consistent theory of value and surplus-value and did uphold \"the esoteric Adam Smith against the exoteric.\" Marx implies a two-level reading of Smith: the esoteric Smith (the analyst who resolves value into labour and treats profit as deduction from the product of labour) and the exoteric Smith (the systematizer who explains profit as a \"deduction\" alongside rent and wages in a trinity of independent revenues, and who classifies capital by durability). Ricardo inherits the esoteric doctrine as his substance and the exoteric taxonomy as his scaffolding, never noticing that the scaffolding cannot support the structure. By contrast, the Physiocrats are free of the confusion. Their distinction between avances annuelles (annual advances) and avances primitives (original/primitive advances) refers \"solely to the different reproduction periods of the different components of capital, agricultural capital in particular.\" Crucially, their theory of surplus-value is \"a part of their theory which is independent of these distinctions, a part in fact that they held up as its culminating point.\" The Physiocrats locate surplus-value not in capital as such but \"simply to one specific sphere of capitalist production, agriculture\" — a narrow and ultimately untenable localization, but one which at least does not corrupt their reproduction-period taxonomy with a valorization claim. Marx's point: the Physiocrats had a coherent circulation-process distinction because they did not confuse it with a valorization-process one. This is the standard against which Ricardo is measured — and to which, as we shall see, Ricardo winds up regressing. 2.8 \"Return to Ricardo\" (2): The Physicalist Reduction of Variable Capital The second numbered point begins with a restatement of the essential feature of variable capital: \"the capitalist exchanges a definite, given (and in this sense constant) value for value-creating power; a magnitude of value for the production of value, for self-valorization.\" The form of payment — money or means of subsistence — is indifferent to this. It affects \"only the mode of existence of the value advanced by him,\" which exists either as money with which the worker buys subsistence on the market, or as subsistence directly consumed. Marx notes that developed capitalist production \"in fact assumes that the worker is paid in money, just as it assumes in general that the production process is mediated by the circulation process, i.e. a money economy.\" The sentence is important: the money-wage form is the historically adequate form of capitalist wage-payment, but it is not the source of surplus-value. Surplus-value arises \"neither from the money form nor from the natural form of wages... It arises from the exchange of value for value-creating power, from the conversion of a constant quantity into a variable one.\" Marx now turns to the means of labour , to show that their fixedness is likewise not a physical property. \"The more or less fixed character of the means of labour is a function of their degree of durability, i.e. a physical property .\" Durability determines speed of wear, hence the duration for which a thing can function as fixed capital. \"But it is in no way simply this physical property of durability which leads them to function as fixed capital.\" The metal-works example is the proof: \"In metal works, the raw material is just as durable as the machines with which it is processed, and more durable in fact than many components of these machines — leather, wood, etc. But the metal serving as raw material does not form any the less a part of the circulating capital, while the functioning means of labour that may be constructed of the same metal form part of fixed capital.\" Identical substance, identical durability, opposite category. Therefore \"it is not its material, physical nature... which makes the same metal in one case fixed capital and in the other case circulating capital. This distinction rather arises from the role that it plays in the production process, in one case as object of labour, in the other case as means of labour.\" Marx then states the correct relationship between function and material, in two steps. (i) The function of a means of labour requires it to serve repeatedly across many labour processes; \"its function thus prescribes a greater or lesser degree of durability for its material.\" (ii) But \"the durability of the material from which it is made does not make it in and for itself fixed capital.\" The same material becomes circulating capital when used as raw material. And for economists who confuse commodity capital with productive capital, \"the same material or the same machine is circulating capital as a product, and fixed capital as a means of labour\" — a confusion Marx will list explicitly among the chapter's four concluding \"results.\" 2.9 Durability as Material Basis, Not as Criterion Having denied that durability constitutes fixedness, Marx immediately grants it a real, subordinate role — a classic instance of his dialectical precision. \"Even though it is not the durable material of which the means of labour is made that makes it fixed capital, its role as means of labour does require it to consist of a more or less durable material. The durability of its material is thus a condition for its function as means of labour, hence also a material basis of the mode of circulation that makes it fixed capital. Other things being equal, the greater or lesser perishability of its material imprints it to a lower or higher degree with the stamp of fixedness, and is thus very fundamentally bound up with its quality as fixed capital.\" The logical structure here matters enormously: condition ≠ criterion . Durability is causally necessary and quantitatively relevant; it is not definitionally decisive. This is precisely the hairsbreadth on which Ricardo's definition falls, because Ricardo does make perishability the criterion (\"According as capital is rapidly perishable... it is classed under the heads of circulating, or of fixed capital\"). 2.10 The Material Substitution: Labour-Power Replaced by Means of Subsistence Marx now traces the consequences of the confusion at the level of material determination. If you consider the capital laid out on labour-power \"exclusively from the standpoint of circulating capital,\" and if you merge the constant/variable distinction into the fixed/circulating distinction, then — because the material reality of the means of labour (durable metal, wood, etc.) is an essential basis for its fixedness — you will \"naturally\" seek an equally material basis for the circulating character of wages, and \"define circulating capital in terms of the material reality of variable capital.\" But what is the material reality of the capital laid out on wages? Not means of subsistence — that is the great substitution Marx now exposes. \"The real material of the capital laid out on wages is labour itself, self-acting, value-creating labour-power, living labour, which the capitalist has exchanged for dead, objectified labour, and incorporated into his capital, this being the way that the value existing in his hands is first transformed into a self-valorizing value.\" This is a compressed version of the entire theory of capital: dead objectified labour is exchanged for living labour, and it is this exchange that first converts a sum of value into capital. And crucially, the capitalist's relation to this labour-power remains that of owner of productive capital: \"the capitalist does not sell this power of self-valorization. It forms throughout simply a component of his productive capital, just like his means of labour, and is never a component of his commodity capital.\" The follow-up is structurally neat: \"Within the production process, the means of labour, as components of productive capital, are not distinguished from labour-power as fixed capital, any more than the material of labour and ancillaries coincide with it as circulating capital.\" Put differently: within production, the fixed/circulating distinction simply does not apply as an internal split between means of labour and labour-power. From the labour-process standpoint, both means of labour and material of labour are objective factors confronting labour-power as the personal factor. From the valorization-process standpoint, both are constant capital as against labour-power as variable capital. There is no third partition corresponding to \"fixed vs. circulating.\" Marx then offers, hypothetically (\"Alternatively, if we are to speak of a material difference that affects the circulation process...\"), what the genuine material difference is. It follows \"from the nature of value, which is nothing other than objectified labour, and from the nature of self-acting labour-power, which is nothing other than self-objectifying labour, that labour-power constantly creates value and surplus-value as long as it continues to function; that what presents itself on its side as movement, as the creation of value, presents itself on the side of its product in a motionless form, as created value.\" This is a striking phenomenological restatement of the labour theory of value in terms of motion versus rest: living labour is becoming ; the product is being ; value-added is the crystallized trace of that transition. Once the labour-power has functioned, \"the capital no longer consists of labour-power on the one hand and means of production on the other. The capital value that was laid out on labour-power is now value which has been added to the product (together with surplus-value).\" To repeat, \"the product must be sold, and with the money released by this, labour-power has constantly to be bought afresh and incorporated into the productive capital. This then is what gives the portion of capital laid out on labour-power the character of circulating capital in contrast to the capital that remains fixed in the means of labour.\" Note the precision: labour-power's \"circulating\" character derives from the fact that it is consumed entirely in each production period and must be repurchased from the market — a genuine circulation-process fact. It is a derivative entailment of the labor-process structure, not an alternative description of variable capital. 2.11 The Great Substitution and Its Payoff: Ricardo's Perishability Criterion The error occurs at the next step. \"But if the secondary characteristic of circulating capital, which labour-power has in common with a part of the constant capital (raw materials and ancillaries), is made into the fundamental one\" — i.e., the fact that its value is transferred in its entirety rather than bit by bit — \"then the portion of capital laid out on wages must also consist materially not of self-acting labour-power, but of the material elements that the worker buys with his wages, i.e. of the part of the social commodity capital that enters the worker's consumption, the means of subsistence in other words.\" From this follows a complete reclassification: fixed capital becomes means of labour (\"which perish more slowly\"); variable capital becomes means of subsistence (\"which have to be replaced more rapidly\"). Capital laid out on labour-power is now described by what the worker consumes rather than what the capitalist buys . Marx then exposes the incoherence of the criterion itself with Ricardo's own list: \"The food and clothing consumed by the labourer, the buildings in which he works, the implements with which his labour is assisted, are all of a perishable nature. There is however a vast difference in the time for which these different capitals will endure: a steam-engine will last longer than a ship, a ship than the clothing of the labourer, and the clothing of the labourer longer than the food which he consumes.\" Marx's rebuttal: \"Ricardo forgets here the house in which the worker lives, his furniture, his tools of consumption such as knives, forks, dishes, etc., all of which possess the same character of durability as do the means of labour. The same things and the same classes of things thus appear now as means of consumption, now as means of labour.\" The durability continuum is continuous, not bipartite. A worker's dwelling may outlast a ship; a merchant's cutlery is indistinguishable in durability from the worker's. Any criterion that sorts economic categories along a quantitative continuum of perishability will fail at the margins, and the margins are precisely where the categories must be defined. This is why Ricardo himself must concede: \"A division not essential, and in which the line of demarcation cannot be accurately drawn.\" Marx's sardonic summary: \"Thus we have happily ended up once again back with the Physiocrats, where the distinction between avances annuelles and avances primitives was a distinction in the times of consumption, and hence also in the varying reproduction times, of the capital applied.\" But the regression is a degeneration , not a return: \"It is simply that what in their case expressed a phenomenon of importance for social production, and is depicted in [Quesnay's] Tableau économique in connection with the circulation process, here becomes a subjective distinction, and one that Ricardo himself says is superfluous.\" In the Tableau , the annual/primitive distinction organized the whole social reproduction process — it was a structural law of the economy. In Ricardo it becomes a matter of how long a thing happens to last, arbitrarily drawn and explicitly declared inessential. Marx rounds this beat off with the general consequence: \"As soon as the part of capital laid out on labour is distinguished from that laid out on means of labour only by its reproduction period and thus its term of circulation... then every pertinent difference between the capital laid out on labour-power and that laid out on means of production is obviously destroyed.\" The capital/labour relation is reformatted as a relation between two kinds of goods differentiated only by turnover speed. And this \"completely contradicts Ricardo's doctrine of value, as well as his theory of profit, which is in point of fact a theory of surplus-value.\" 2.12 Ricardo's Two Errors Within His Own Restricted Investigation Marx acknowledges that Ricardo's use of the distinction is limited: he \"only ever considers the distinction between fixed and circulating capital in so far as different proportions of the two, in the case of capitals of equal size in different branches of industry, influence the law of value, and particularly the degree to which a rise or fall in wages affects prices.\" Yet \"even within this restricted investigation, he commits very great errors... and in fact he starts his investigation on a completely false basis.\" Marx enumerates: Falsification of circulating capital itself. \"In so far as the portion of capital value laid out on labour-power is subsumed under the heading of circulating capital, the characteristics of circulating capital are themselves falsely presented, and so in particular are the circumstances which subsume the portion of capital laid out on labour under this heading.\" That is, the error is not merely one of misplacement; it corrupts the definition of the category into which the misplacement occurs. Once wages are the paradigm of \"circulating capital,\" economists redefine circulating capital as \"capital laid out on wages,\" and then the durability criterion, the transfer criterion, and the wage-fund criterion are jammed together incoherently. Confusion of two qualities. \"There is a confusion between the quality that makes the part of capital laid out on labour variable, and the quality that makes it circulating in contrast to fixed.\" Variability (value-creating power, self-valorization, production of surplus-value) is a valorization quality; circulatingness (full and immediate value transfer, rapid replacement) is a circulation quality. Ricardo treats them as one and the same property. Marx then states the deeper stakes. \"It is clear from the start that the definition of the capital laid out on labour-power as circulating or fluid is a secondary one, which glosses over its specific difference in the production process.\" Two reasons: - First , \"in this definition the capitals laid out on labour and on raw materials, etc. are equivalent; and a classification that identifies one part of the constant capital with the variable capital does not come to grips with the specific difference of variable capital as opposed to constant.\" - Second , \"although the portions of capital laid out on labour and on means of labour are counterposed to one another, this is in no way with respect to the fact that they are involved in the production of value in completely different ways, but simply with respect to the different periods of time during which the given value of both is transferred to the product.\" The unifying statement is this: \"What is at issue in all these cases is how a given value which is invested in the production process of a commodity, whether as wages, the price of raw materials or the price of means of labour, is transferred to the product, hence circulated by the product and brought back to its starting-point or replaced by its sale. The only distinction here consists in the how , in the particular way in which this value is transferred and thus circulates. \" Note the ellipsis contained in the emphasised word: the whole distinction concerns form of return , not source of creation . 2.13 The Money-Form Argument and the Wayland Quote: The Mystery of Reappearance Marx then closes off a possible escape route. Someone might object that the confusion in Ricardo arises only because wages are paid in money, and money does not enter the labour process materially. Marx grants this: \"in the case of wages paid in money, it is obvious that it is not the money itself that enters the production process, in the same way that it is not just the value but also the material of the means of production that enters this process.\" The objection proves nothing: the wage is a definite contractually fixed price regardless of its form of payment. \"Whether the price of labour-power, which in any case is previously determined by contract, is paid in money or in means of subsistence, in no way changes its character of being a definite and given price.\" The damage occurs when one translates the money-wage back into the goods it buys and then locates those goods within the circulation taxonomy: \"if the means of subsistence that the worker buys with his wage are directly placed under one heading together with the raw materials, etc., as the material shape of circulating capital, and the means of labour counterposed to them, then this gives the matter a different appearance.\" Now the story becomes: \"the value of one lot of things, the means of production, is transferred to the product in the labour process\" while \"the value of the other lot of things, the means of subsistence, reappears in the labour-power that consumes them, and is similarly transferred to the product by the labour-power's activity.\" Result: \"What is involved in all these cases is similarly the mere reappearance in the product of the values advanced during production.\" Marx adds a parenthetical, historically loaded note: \"(The Physiocrats took this seriously and denied that industrial labour created surplus-value.)\" — a reminder that the reappearance-model is a substantive theoretical position with devastating consequences, not a harmless verbal simplification. He then cites Wayland as the perfected form of this view: \"It matters not in what form capital reappears... The various kinds of food, clothing, and shelter, necessary for the existence and comfort of the human being, are also changed. They are consumed from time to time, and their value reappears...\" (Marx footnotes Wayland as an American economist-parson, \"author of a popular manual\" of 1843, whose combination of professions — economist and clergyman — parallels Malthus and Chalmers, an aside that is itself a jab.) Marx's conclusion: \"The capital values advanced to production in the shape of means of production and means of subsistence here both equally reappear in the value of the product. The capitalist production process is thus successfully transformed into a complete mystery, and the origin of the surplus-value present in the product completely withdrawn from view.\" This is the theoretical payoff of the confusion: a system in which everything \"reappears\" cannot explain how anything increases . 2.14 Fetishism and the Functional Definition of Categories Marx now names the underlying intellectual vice: \" fetishism peculiar to bourgeois economics, which transforms the social, economic character that things are stamped with in the process of social production into a natural character arising from the material nature of these things.\" This is the direct application of the doctrine of commodity fetishism from Capital Vol. 1, Ch. 1, §4 (cross-referenced in Marx's own footnote) to the categories of capital. The formula \"means of labour, for instance, are fixed capital\" is \"a scholastic definition which leads to contradictions and confusion.\" Its error is to treat capital-categories as inherent properties of things. Marx then proposes the correct formulation, and it is one of the most theoretically consequential passages in the book. \"Just as we have shown how, in the labour process ( Volume 1 , Chapter 7), it depends entirely on the role which the objective components play at the time in a particular labour process, on their function, whether they function as means of labour, material of labour or product, so, in precisely the same way, means of labour are fixed capital only where the production process is in fact a capitalist production process and the means of production are thus actually capital, i.e. possess the economic determination, the social character, of capital; secondly, they are fixed capital only if they transfer their value to the product in a particular way.\" Two conditions are thereby laid down for \"fixed capital\": 1. The production process must be capitalist — the means of production must actually be capital, i.e., possess the social determination of capital (valorization of value through wage-labour). 2. They must transfer their value to the product in a particular way — bit by bit, gradually. And the definition must be functional, not material : \"If this is not the case, then they remain means of labour without being fixed capital. In the same way, ancillaries such as fertilizer, if they give up their value in the same particular way as do the greater part of means of labour, are fixed capital, although they are not means of labour.\" The fertilizer case is decisive against any material definition: fertilizer is not a means of labour at all (it is a material/ancillary), yet it is fixed capital because of the way it transfers value (over several production periods, as soil nutrients). \"What is at issue here is not a set of definitions under which things are to be subsumed. It is rather definite functions that are expressed in specific categories.\" This is Marx's general epistemological principle for social science: categories express functions within relations, not classifications of things . This principle immediately yields sharp critiques. \"If it is the destiny of the means of subsistence in themselves, a property devolving on them under all circumstances, to be capital laid out on wages, then it also becomes the character of this 'circulating' capital 'to support labour' (Ricardo, p. 25).\" And then the subversive reversal: \"If the means of subsistence were not 'capital', then they would not support labour-power; though it is in fact precisely their character as capital that gives them the property of supporting capital by the labour of others.\" That is: the \"support of labour\" attributed to a natural class of goods is in reality a social relation of exploitation misrecognized as a natural function of goods. 2.15 The Wage-Fund Corollary From the material substitution follows a further, politically explosive consequence. \"If means of subsistence are inherently circulating capital — after this has been transformed into wages — then it further results that the size of the wage depends on the ratio between the number of workers and the given mass of circulating capital — a favourite proposition of the economists.\" This is the wage-fund doctrine in its Ricardian-Malthusian version: wages are determined by the ratio of the (physically given) circulating capital fund to the number of workers. Marx's refutation is compressed but complete: \"whereas in point of fact the quantity of means of subsistence that the worker withdraws from the market, and the quantity which the capitalist has at his disposal for his own consumption, depend rather on the ratio between surplus-value and the price of labour.\" That is, what appears to be a natural limit (a fixed pile of foodstuffs divided among workers) is a social division between profit and wages. The wage-fund doctrine translates the exploitative distribution of the product back into the physical data of production and thereby naturalizes it. Marx's actual determination runs in the opposite direction: the wage determines the quantity of subsistence that flows to labour, not vice versa. 2.16 Barton, and the Turnover/Fixedness Confusion Marx then makes a transitional observation: \"Ricardo, like Barton, constantly confuses the ratio between variable and constant capital with the ratio between circulating and fixed capital. We shall see later on how this vitiates his investigation of the rate of profit.\" (Footnote: Barton's Observations on the Circumstances which Influence the Condition of the Labouring Classes of Society , London, 1817; the reference to Capital Vol. 3, Chapters 1–3 — \"Cost-Price and Profit,\" \"The Rate of Profit,\" and \"The Relation of the Rate of Profit to the Rate of Surplus-Value.\") This is the bridge from the conceptual analysis of the chapter to the mathematical analysis that follows it: because Ricardo substitutes circulating/fixed (a turnover distinction) for variable/constant (a valorization distinction), his treatment of the falling rate of profit will be corrupted. Marx then identifies a second, independent error in Ricardo's apparatus: \"Ricardo further equates the distinctions that arise in the turnover for reasons other than the distinction between fixed and circulating capital, with the latter distinction itself.\" He quotes Ricardo's seed-corn example: \"It is also to be observed that the circulating capital may circulate, or be returned to its employer, in very unequal times. The wheat bought by a farmer to sow is comparatively a fixed capital to the wheat purchased by a baker to make into loaves. One leaves it in the ground, and can obtain no return for a year; the other can get it ground into flour, sell it as bread to his customers, and have his capital free to renew the same, or commence any other employment in a week.\" Marx's analysis: \"It is characteristic here that wheat, although as seed-corn it serves not as means of subsistence but as raw material, is firstly circulating capital, because it is inherently means of subsistence, and secondly fixed capital, because its return stretches over a year.\" Ricardo's own scheme generates a contradictory double-valuation of the same object: seed-corn is circulating by nature but fixed by return-time. \"But it is not just the slower or more rapid return that makes a means of production into fixed capital, but rather the specific manner in which it gives up value to the product.\" The formal point: seed-corn, like any raw material, transfers its entire value into the product at once (it is consumed in the act of sowing and re-emerges as the value of the crop) — its mode of transfer is circulating . What makes its return slow is the turnover period of the capital, which is a different determination. Ricardo fuses turnover time (a circulation velocity ) with fixedness (a transfer mode ). 2.17 The Fourfold Harvest: Consequences of Smith's Confusion Marx closes the chapter with a numbered list of four historical results of the Smithian confusion. They are, in effect, a small genealogy of nineteenth-century economics. (1) Fixed/fluid confused with productive/commodity capital. \"Thus the same machine is circulating capital, for example, when it exists on the market as a commodity, and fixed capital when it is incorporated into the production process. It is impossible to see here why one particular kind of capital should be more fixed or more circulating than another.\" The error reduces a functional distinction to a contextual one, so that the same object is both categories at different moments — which empties the distinction of content. (2) All circulating capital identified with capital laid out on wages. \"This is the case with John Stuart Mill among others.\" Marx's footnote directs us to Mill's Essays on Some Unsettled Questions of Political Economy (London, 1844), criticized in detail in Theories of Surplus-Value , Part III, pp. 190 ff., and quotes his own Postface to the second German edition of Capital Volume 1 to characterize Mill's general position: \"The Continental revolution of 1848 also had its reaction in England [i.e. on economic thought]. Men who still claimed some scientific standing and aspired to be something more than mere sophists and sycophants of the ruling classes tried to harmonize the political economy of capital with the claims, no longer to be ignored, of the proletariat. Hence a shallow syncretism , of which John Stuart Mill is the best representative.\" The Mill case therefore illustrates not only the internal error but its class-political function: to synthesize capital's theory with concessions to labour without disturbing the foundations. (3) Complete reduction of constant/variable to circulating/fixed — Ramsay. \"The distinction between variable and constant capital, which Barton, Ricardo and others already confused with that between circulating and fixed capital, is eventually reduced completely to the latter distinction, as with Ramsay for example, who takes not only means of labour, but all means of production, raw materials etc. as fixed capital, and only the capital laid out on wages as circulating capital. But because the reduction is accomplished in this way, the real distinction between constant and variable capital is not grasped.\" Ramsay's move is a simplification that looks more rigorous than Ricardo's — he eliminates the raw-material anomaly by folding raw materials into fixed capital — but it is a reductio: form of turnover is now the entire content of the capital distinction. (4) The bank-clerk reduction. \"The most recent English economists, and even more so the Scottish ones, who view everything from the unutterably narrow standpoint of a bank clerk — such as MacLeod, Patterson and others — transform the distinction between fixed and circulating capital into that between 'money at call' and 'money not at call' (that is to say, between deposit money that can be withdrawn without prior notification, and money whose withdrawal requires such notification).\" (Footnotes: Henry Dunning MacLeod, The Elements of Political Economy , London, 1858; Robert Hogard Patterson, The Science of Finance , Edinburgh and London, 1868.) This is Marx's reductio ad absurdum of the whole trajectory: a distinction which once concerned the reproduction periods of agricultural capital in Quesnay's Tableau has dissolved into a distinction between two types of bank deposit , differentiated only by the legal conditions of withdrawal. The disappearance of production from the categories is complete: capital has become \"money\" and its distinction has become a term in a banking contract. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles Revealed The chapter yields several interlocking theoretical laws. First: the primacy of the valorization process over the circulation process in the definition of capital. Every specific analytical gain in the chapter depends on the priority of \"constant/variable\" (derived from the way capital produces value) over \"fixed/circulating\" (derived from the way capital's value returns). Where the priority is reversed, the surplus-value-producing relation disappears into an accounting of value transfer. Marx's formula — \"the circumstance which transforms the sum of value laid out into capital\" — locates capital's essence in the exchange of value for value-creating power , not in any physical or temporal property of the things involved. Second: functional, not material, definition of categories. The metal-works and fertilizer examples establish that the fixed/circulating distinction tracks functions within a production relation , not substances. \"What is at issue here is not a set of definitions under which things are to be subsumed. It is rather definite functions that are expressed in specific categories.\" This has urgent methodological consequences: any economics that classifies capital by the material character of the objects that embody it is committing, in Marx's terms, fetishism — mistaking a social relation for a natural property. Third: the historical specificity of economic categories. Fixed capital, strictly speaking, exists only \"where the production process is in fact a capitalist production process\" — means of labour \"remain means of labour without being fixed capital\" where capital relations do not obtain. Categories are historically bounded. This corrects any temptation to treat fixed/circulating as transhistorical technical concepts applicable to any economy. Fourth: the distinction between condition and criterion, and between continuum and category. Durability is a condition for fixedness, but not the criterion ; perishability is a continuum , but fixed/circulating purports to be a bipartite classification . Ricardo's collapse follows necessarily from his attempt to draw a categorical line on a continuous physical scale. Categories must have an internal relational differential specifica , which continuous physical properties cannot supply. Fifth: the ideological production of mystery. The whole apparatus of \"reappearance\" — Smith, Wayland, Malthus — \"successfully transformed [the capitalist production process] into a complete mystery, and the origin of the surplus-value present in the product completely withdrawn from view.\" This is the crucial ideological lesson: the same set of categories can either illuminate or obscure, and the obscure version is typically preferred because it conceals exploitation, naturalizes wage-fund limits, and reduces capital to \"money at call.\" The category system is not politically innocent. Broader Practical and Theoretical Implications For the theory of value and profit. The chapter explains the location of Ricardo's errors that later mature into the contradictions of the Ricardian school and its dissolution. Ricardo's doctrine that values and surplus-value are consistently labour-determined is correct; his taxonomy of capital is Smithian. The two cannot live together. Marx's subsequent Vol. 3 treatment of the rate of profit, and Ch. 11 of Vol. 3 on wage fluctuations and prices of production, is the completion of this chapter's demolition. Anyone reading Ricardo's \"exceptions to the law of value\" without this chapter's distinction will misplace the entire problem. For the critique of the wage-fund doctrine. Marx's counter-formulation — the wage depends on \"the ratio between surplus-value and the price of labour,\" and the worker's subsistence depends on the wage, not the other way around — is the destruction of a doctrine that persisted through Malthus, Mill, and Senior and that served to blame working-class poverty on workers' own procreation rather than on the distribution of surplus-value. Marx here shows that the wage-fund doctrine is not an empirical mistake but a necessary corollary of the materialization of variable capital into means of subsistence. For the critique of physiocracy and of the \"advance\" ideology. The Physiocratic Tableau was, for all its agricultural limitations, a serious account of social reproduction. Ricardo's version, on his own admission, is \"a division not essential.\" The comparison is instructive about how progress in one dimension (Ricardo's labour theory of value) can coexist with regress in another (his capital taxonomy). Similarly, Marx's inversion of \"advance\" — the worker advances labour, and only then is paid — uncovers the temporal lie at the heart of the ideological narrative of capital's benevolent initiative. For the philosophy of social science. Marx's insistence that categories express functions within relations rather than properties of things is a general methodological proposition of wide import. It rules out, in advance, both naive materialist classifications (things classified by their physical properties) and purely formalist classifications (things classified by their accounting treatment). It insists on the primacy of the production relation. For the critique of modern economics. The final irony — capital reduced to \"money at call\" and \"money not at call\" — is not merely a nineteenth-century curiosity. Marx's target is a mode of thought in which financial circulation becomes the master-category of capital. The chapter's analytical spine — never confuse the form of return of value with the production of surplus value — remains a usable criterion for evaluating any economics that treats capital in terms of liquidity, turnover, or asset duration. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 10 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 12"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 12", "title": "Chapter 12", "subtitle": "The Working Period", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 12/Chapter 12 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 7547, "read_time": "38 min read", "tags": ["Turnover of Capital & Cycles"], "keywords": ["The Working Period", "Working Period vs Production Time", "Magnitude of Capital Advanced During Lengthy Construction", "Shipbuilding, Agriculture, and Metallurgy Examples", "Interruption of Value Realization in Extended Construction"], "text": "Chapter 12 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 7,518 words ⏱️ ~38 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 12, \"The Working Period,\" is the opening chapter of Part Two of Capital , Volume II, the section devoted to the turnover of capital. It arrives immediately after Marx has established, in Part One, the conceptual machinery of the circuit of capital and the division between fixed and circulating capital. The chapter's job is to introduce a new determinant of capital's motion that cannot be reduced to any of the categories already established: the sheer physical duration of the act of production, independent of the length of the working day, independent of the ratio of fixed to circulating capital, and independent of the organic composition of capital. The core thesis is deceptively simple and enormously consequential: the length of time a labour process must be continuously sustained before it yields a finished, saleable commodity is itself a variable that regulates the turnover of capital, and it forces the capitalist to advance additional capital in proportion to that length. Marx contrasts cotton spinning — in which a finished commodity is turned out every day and every week — with locomotive manufacture — in which one and the same act of production must be sustained for perhaps three months before a single finished product exists. The two lines of business may share an identical ten-hour working day, an identical division between constant and variable capital, and identical proportions of fixed and circulating capital — and yet the locomotive manufacturer will require twelve times the circulating capital of the spinner, because the spinner recovers his outlay weekly while the machine-builder must add fresh doses of capital week after week for three months before any of it returns. To name and theorize this, Marx introduces a concept that is his own: the working period [ Arbeitsperiode ], as distinct from the working day . The working day is the length of time the worker must daily expend his labour-power. The working period is the number of interrelated working days required in a given branch to complete a finished product. One hundred working days of ten hours each are, for the workers, one hundred discrete labour processes; for the product, they are a single continuous act of production of a thousand working hours. This duality — discrete for labour-power, continuous for the product — is the hinge on which the entire chapter turns. The chapter's intellectual objective is therefore fourfold. First, to establish the working period as an independent variable in the theory of turnover. Second, to derive from it a quantitative law: the additional circulating capital required grows with the length of the working period. Third, to show that this has historical consequences — long working periods are only compatible with fully capitalist production once capital is sufficiently concentrated and credit sufficiently developed, because only then can the capitalist afford to risk other people's capital over a long period. And fourth, to demonstrate that the natural limits of the working period (planting, breeding, timber-growing) impose constraints that no technique can abolish, and that the coercion of ready cash therefore produces systematic distortions in agriculture — such as the premature slaughter of cattle. The chapter thus situates itself at the intersection of pure economic theory, the history of capitalism, and political economy in its most classical, Ricardian sense — while also deploying literary, colonial, and agricultural sources to show that the abstraction of turnover has concrete, sometimes brutal, human results. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The founding contrast: discrete versus continuous production Marx opens with a deliberately minimal comparison. Take two branches — cotton spinning and locomotive manufacture — and assume that each has the same working day of ten hours. In spinning, \"a definite quantity of the finished product, cotton yarn, is turned out every day and every week.\" In locomotive manufacture, \"the labour process must be repeated for perhaps three months in order to produce a finished product, one locomotive.\" The distinction Marx draws is not that one process is \"slow\" and the other \"fast,\" but something structurally different. In spinning, the product is discrete in nature : the labour process begins afresh each day or each week, and each day or week yields a self-contained, finished commodity. In locomotive building, the labour process is continuous : it \"stretches over a large number of daily labour processes, which supply a finished product only after a protracted interval, through the connectedness and continuity of their operations.\" Even though the daily labour process is identical in both cases, there is a stark divergence in the duration of the act of production — the duration of the repeated labour processes needed to turn out the product in its finished form, to send it to market as a commodity, and thus to transform it from productive capital into commodity capital. This is the terminology that anchors the chapter: productive capital is capital locked in the production sphere, commodity capital is capital in the form of a finished, saleable product. The length of time separating the one from the other is what is at stake. The first theoretical move Marx makes is defensive : he insists this distinction \"has nothing to do with\" the fixed/circulating capital distinction. It would obtain even if exactly the same proportions of fixed and circulating capital were applied in both lines of business. The fixity or fluidity of capital components is a different axis altogether. This is a crucial methodological warning: Marx is separating out one variable and holding others constant, so that when he later re-introduces the fixed/circulating distinction, its effects can be measured against the working-period effect. The chapter is, in this sense, a controlled thought experiment built up piece by piece. Infinite variety: the working period as an empirical spectrum Marx immediately widens the frame. The differences in duration of the act of production are not a simple binary of \"short\" and \"long,\" but a spectrum of \"infinite variety,\" and they occur not only between branches but within the same branch according to the scale of the object produced. He offers a rapid-fire catalogue: A dwelling-house is built faster than a large factory; hence requires fewer continuous labour processes. A locomotive takes three months; a battleship takes a year, \"if not several.\" The production of grain demands almost a year; horned cattle, several years; timber, anywhere from twelve to a hundred years. A road can be built in a few months; a railway requires years. An ordinary carpet takes perhaps a week; a Gobelin tapestry takes years. The catalogue is not ornamental. Each example layers a different dimension of the problem: scale (house vs. factory), physical mass (locomotive vs. battleship), biological reproduction (cattle, grain), arboreal growth (timber), infrastructural capital (road, railway), and artisanal skill-intensity (carpet, Gobelin). By the end of the list, the reader is meant to grasp that the working period cannot be treated as an exception or a curiosity of a few trades — it is a universal feature of production, and its variation is continuous and open-ended. The controlled experiment: posing equal capitals against unequal turnover The analytical heart of the chapter is a rigorously specified hypothetical. Marx asks us to assume that a spinning mill and a locomotive works apply equal capitals , with: the same division between constant and variable capital, the same division between fixed and fluid (circulating) components, the same length of the working day, the same division between necessary and surplus labour. He then brackets out the circulation process entirely (to isolate the production-time effect), assuming that \"both yarn and locomotive are produced to order and paid for on delivery of the finished product.\" This is a vital methodological cleanliness: since circulating capital normally flows back only through sale, and sale times can differ, Marx removes sale-time differences so that what remains is purely the effect of production time . Under these assumptions, the outcomes diverge dramatically: The spinner : at the end of the week, upon delivery of the yarn, he \"receives back his outlay of circulating capital as well as the wear and tear of the fixed capital contained in the value of the yarn.\" He can now repeat the same circuit with the same capital. His turnover is complete. Note that the parenthetical \"(we ignore the surplus-value)\" simply abstracts from profit to focus on the reproduction of the capital advanced. The locomotive manufacturer : he must \"lay out fresh capital on wages and raw material week after week for three months, and only after three months, when the locomotive is delivered, does the circulating capital laid out bit by bit during this time for one and the same act of production, to produce one and the same commodity, exist again in a form in which it can begin its circuit once more.\" Wear and tear on the machinery, similarly, is replaced only at the end of the three months. The numerical consequence is stated bluntly: \"One business has an outlay for one week, the other the same weekly outlay multiplied by twelve. All other circumstances being assumed equal, the one must have twelve times as much circulating capital at its disposal as the other.\" The fact that the weekly capitals are equal is irrelevant; the critical variable is the total capital advanced before any reflux occurs . In arithmetic terms, if the weekly outlay is c and the working period is p weeks, the circulating capital that must be kept committed is c × p . For the spinner, p = 1; for the machine-builder, p = 12. The difference of 12× is entirely a function of p , not of c . Marx sharpens the point: \"Whatever may be the size of the capital advanced, in the one case it is advanced only for one week, in the other for twelve, before it can be used for a new operation, before the same operation can be repeated with it, or one of a different kind begun.\" The variable is the time of advance — the length of time for which a given capital value is locked up before it can serve a new labour or valorization process. The conceptual innovation: the working period defined Having shown that the working-period difference has massive consequences, Marx pauses to name the phenomenon. He poses a fine-grained example: the building of a locomotive takes 100 working days. For the workers engaged, those 100 days are \"a discontinuous (discrete) quantity\"; they consist of \"100 successive and separate ten-hour labour processes.\" But in relation to the product , \"the 100 working days form a continuous quantity, a working day of 1,000 working hours, a single related act of production.\" This is the moment of definition. \"A working day of this kind, which is formed by the succession of more or less numerous interrelated working days, I call a working period .\" Marx then contrasts: The working day = \"the length of time for which the worker must daily expend his labour-power, must work.\" The working period = \"the number of inter-related working days that are required, in a particular line of business, to complete a finished product.\" The duality is essential. In the working period, \"the product of each working day is here only a partial product , which is taken a step further day by day and receives its finished shape, is a finished use-value, only at the close of a longer or shorter period of working time.\" Every day's product is incomplete, is not yet a commodity, and therefore cannot yet function as commodity capital. The term partial product is doing significant theoretical work. In spinning, the daily output is a commodity. In locomotive building, the daily output is only a moment in a larger unity. This means the form of the product — discrete commodity vs. node in a continuous production — governs the possible rhythms of valorization. Crisis, interruption, and the irreversibility of the interconnected act Marx now derives an important consequence: interruptions and disturbances of the social production process , such as crises, \"have a very different effect on those products of labour that are discrete in nature, and those whose production requires a longer connected period.\" For discrete products like yarn or coal, an interruption means one day's production is simply not followed by a fresh production the next day. The damage is limited to the day's output being withheld. For ships, buildings, railways, \"it is not only work that is interrupted, but an inter-connected act of production. If the job is not carried any further, then the means of production and the labour already consumed in its production have been spent to no avail. Even if it is taken up again, deterioration will always have taken place in the meantime.\" The capital already sunk is not merely idle but partially destroyed — the unfinished ship rusts, the unfinished building weathers, and the labour already objectified in it is, in part, wasted. This is a qualitative asymmetry with profound implications: the longer the working period, the more the total capital advanced is exposed to the risk of irreversible decay during a stoppage. Fixed capital in the long working period: layered depreciation and the twenty-year engine Marx now reintroduces the fixed/circulating distinction — but carefully, to show that it behaves asymmetrically under the pressure of a long working period. The portion of value that fixed capital surrenders every day \"builds up in layers throughout the whole duration of the working period.\" The fixed capital itself, however, is \"advanced to the production process for a longer period of time; it does not need to be renewed until an interval of perhaps several years has elapsed.\" The illustrative case is the steam engine. Whether the engine gives up value bit by bit each day to yarn (a discrete product) or in larger, delayed amounts over three months to a locomotive (a continuous product), this \"in no way alters the outlay of capital needed to acquire the steam engine.\" The engine is renewed, in both cases, only after perhaps twenty years. The reflux of its value — weekly in one case, quarterly in the other — is fast enough in both cases relative to the engine's own lifespan (twenty years). Therefore, \"as long as the individual period in which its value flows back bit by bit with the sale of the product is always shorter than its own period of existence, the same steam engine continues to function in the production process for several working periods.\" The principle buried here is a ratio of horizons : as long as the turnover period of the fixed asset's value is shorter than the physical lifetime of the asset, the fixed asset is not a limiting factor. A three-month delay in the reflux of a steam engine's depreciation is trivial against a twenty-year lifespan. This is why Marx can say that the lengthened working period does not require renewed outlay of fixed capital, only delayed reflux. Circulating capital: the adding of new doses and the impossibility of self-renewal \"It is otherwise with the circulating components of the capital advanced.\" Here the logic is fundamentally different, and the chapter's gravity shifts. The labour-power bought for a given week is used up during that week, has objectified itself in the product, and must be paid for at the end of the week . This outlay is repeated weekly throughout the three months. Crucially: \"the expenditure of this portion of capital in the one week does not enable the capitalist to cover the acquisition of labour in the next week.\" The capital advanced does not reproduce itself from within the unfinished product. Hence: \"New, additional capital must be spent each week in payment for labour-power, and if we set aside all credit relations the capitalist must be able to lay out wages for the whole period of three months, even though he pays them only in weekly doses.\" This is the decisive point. If credit relations are set aside , the capitalist must have the entire wage bill for the whole working period at his disposal — even though he disburses it in weekly tranches. The same holds for raw materials and ancillaries. Layer after layer of labour and material is deposited in the unfinished product, but \"all this is transferred to an unfinished product that does not yet have the shape of a finished commodity, and is thus not capable of circulation.\" The formulaic version is important. The circulating capital required is not simply the weekly outlay but the accumulated outlay for the entire working period : C_circ = c × p , where c is the weekly circulating outlay and p the number of weeks in the working period. And unlike the fixed capital case, this is a binding constraint, because the circulating capital cannot spontaneously renew itself from an incomplete product. Turnover time as production time plus circulation time: two structural consequences Marx now formally places the working period within the theory of turnover. \"The turnover time of capital, however, is the sum of its production time and its circulation time. A lengthening of the production time thus reduces the speed of turnover as much as a lengthening of the circulation time.\" In symbols: T = P + C , where T is turnover time, P is production time (dominated here by the working period), and C is circulation time. Increase P and T rises; increase C and T rises; the effects on turnover speed are symmetrical. But Marx notes two specific features of the present case: First , the \"lengthened stay in the production sphere.\" Capital advanced in the first week — on labour, raw materials, and the value given up by the fixed capital — \"remains confined to the production sphere for the entire term of three months,\" and it is incorporated only into a product in formation, hence cannot pass into circulation as a commodity. The capital is stuck , not because it cannot circulate, but because its material form is not yet a commodity. Second , since the working period \"lasts three months, and in actual fact simply forms a single interrelated labour process, every week a new dose of circulating capital must be added to the preceding one.\" From this Marx derives the key dynamic law: \"The quantity of additional capital successively advanced thus grows with the length of the working period.\" As p grows, C_circ = c × p grows linearly. He then restates the consequences under the controlled-experiment assumptions. In the first week, the outlay is the same for spinner and machine-builder. But the spinner can sell his yarn and rebuy labour-power and materials with the proceeds, continuing on the same scale. The machine-builder must wait three months before his first week's circulating capital can be transformed back into money for a fresh operation. Therefore: First difference : a difference in the reflux of the same quantity of capital laid out. Second difference : over three months, the same amount of productive capital is applied, but the amount of capital laid out differs enormously. The spinner renews the same capital quickly and repeats the operation; the machine-builder's capital renews slowly, so \"new amounts of capital must therefore be steadily added to the old until its renewal period arrives.\" Marx here anticipates a point he says will be treated \"in the following chapter\": the time of advance can grow without the amount of capital advanced growing in proportion to this length of time. That is, there are cases (as will be shown later — for example, when the working period is interrupted by natural processes rather than extended by brute addition of labour) where capital must be tied up longer but does not have to be quantitatively increased proportionally . He warns us not to conflate the two dimensions: length of the time of advance and magnitude of the capital tied up are related but not identical. Even so, the general case here is stark: \"The capital has to be advanced for longer, and a larger amount of capital is tied up in the form of productive capital.\" Historical sociology of the working period: from municipal roads to speculative London The chapter now pivots to historical and institutional analysis — a move characteristic of Marx's method, in which an abstract economic distinction is shown to have specific historical bearings . At less developed stages of capitalist production , \"enterprises that require a long working period, and thus a large capital outlay for a longer time, particularly if they can be conducted only on a large scale, are often not pursued capitalistically at all.\" He gives concrete examples: Roads and canals were built at the cost of the municipality or state — \"in earlier periods mostly by forced labour, in so far as labour-power is concerned.\" Products requiring long working periods are manufactured only to a minor extent with the capitalist's own financial means. In house construction, the private individual for whom the house is being built \"pays advances to the builder in successive portions,\" paying for the house bit by bit in proportion to the progress of production. The logic is transparent: when a working period is long, the capital required is large; when no institutional mechanism (state, credit, joint-stock company) exists to pool or advance that capital, the transaction migrates out of the sphere of capitalist production and into the sphere of custom, state action, forced labour, or piecemeal client-financing. In effect, before credit, the customer becomes the financier. In developed capitalism , however, \"massive capitals are concentrated in the hands of individuals\" and \"the associated capitalist (joint-stock companies) steps onto the scene alongside the individual capitalist\" — and \"credit, too, is developed.\" Under these conditions, \"it is only in exceptional cases that a capitalist builder still builds houses to order for individual clients.\" Instead, he \"makes a business out of building rows of houses and whole districts of towns for the market, just as individual capitalists make a business out of building railways as contractors.\" The transition is thus triply institutional: concentration of capital, development of the credit system, and the arrival of the joint-stock form. All three mature alongside — and are partly necessitated by — the long working period. The builder's testimony: the Bank Acts Committee of 1857 Marx supplies empirical evidence on the transformation of London housebuilding via a builder's testimony to the Bank Acts Committee of 1857 . The source reference (Report from the Select Committee on Bank Acts, part 1, 1857, Evidence, nos. 5413–5418, 5435–5436) signals a documentary, official-inquiry provenance — the material is not Marx's own invention but a witness statement before a British parliamentary committee. The witness's account, as Marx abbreviates it, runs as follows. In his youth, houses were generally built to order , and the price was paid to the contractor in instalments as stages of construction were completed. There was little speculative building; contractors resorted to it mainly just to keep their workers regularly occupied and hold the labour force together. Over the last forty years — roughly the 1817–1857 span — \"all that has changed.\" There is now little building to order. A person wanting a new house \"looks for one that has already been built on speculation, or is already in the process of being built.\" The contractor \"no longer works directly for a client, but rather for the market; just like any other industrialist, he has to have finished goods for sale.\" The scale-shift is quantified. Where previously a contractor might build three or four houses at a time on speculation, he \"now has to buy an extensive piece of land (in the Continental sense, he leases it, usually for ninety-nine years), erect on it up to 100 or 200 houses, and thus involve himself in an undertaking that exceeds his own means some twenty to fifty times over.\" Funding is obtained by mortgage , and the mortgage money \"is put at the contractor's disposal bit by bit as the building of the houses progresses.\" Marx draws the crisis dynamic sharply: \"If a crisis breaks out, bringing the payment of these instalments to a halt, then the whole undertaking generally collapses; in the best case, the houses remain uncompleted until better times, while in the worst they are auctioned off at half price.\" Two further features merit highlighting: The structural dependence on speculative building : \"It is impossible nowadays for any contractor to get along without speculative building, and on a large scale at that.\" The long working period has ceased to be an inconvenience and has become an obligate condition of the trade. The locational shift of profit : \"The profit on the actual construction is extremely slight; the main source of profit comes from raising the ground rent, and from the clever selection and exploitation of the building land.\" This is a striking anticipation of later analyses of urban rent — the builder's profit migrates from the production process to the land on which it occurs. \"Almost the whole of Belgravia, Tyburnia and the countless thousands of villas around London have been built in this way, by speculative anticipation of the demand for houses.\" The evidence is thus not just a case study of the working period; it is a case study of how a long working period reshapes an entire sector's industrial organization, financing, and profit source. Credit, concentration, and the capitalist threshold of the long working period Marx states the conclusion about the institutional preconditions directly. \"Large-scale jobs needing particularly long working periods are fully suitable for capitalist production only when the concentration of capital is already well advanced, and when the development of the credit system offers the capitalist the convenient expedient of advancing and thus risking other people's capital instead of his own.\" This formulation is important. Credit does not merely smooth out the timing mismatch produced by a long working period; it transfers the risk . The individual capitalist no longer has to hazard his own fortune; he hazards his creditors' funds. The chapter, in other words, links the theory of turnover to the theory of financial fragility — a linkage that will return again in Volume III. But Marx immediately adds a purity caveat, preserving the analytical value of his earlier abstraction: \"It is self-evident, however, that whether the capital advanced for production belongs to the person who uses it or not has no effect on the speed and time of turnover.\" Ownership of the capital does not modify the physical structure of the working period. Credit alters who bears the burden and whether the undertaking can be mounted at all — but not the duration or the arithmetic. Shortening the working period: machinery, cooperation, and its dialectical cost Marx now turns to the means of shortening the working period. He lists: Cooperation Division of labour Application of machinery — all increase the product of the individual working day These also shorten the working period for interconnected acts of production. Concrete instances: Machinery shortens the building time of houses, bridges, etc. Reaping and threshing machines shorten the working period required to transform ripened corn into a finished commodity. Improved ship-building techniques, producing greater speed, shorten the turnover time of the capital invested in shipping. But here Marx introduces a dialectical twist that shows how subtle his analysis is. \"These improvements, which shorten the working period and hence the time for which circulating capital has to be advanced, are generally bound up with an increased outlay of fixed capital .\" That is, the shortening of the working period is achieved by substituting fixed capital for advanced time — a trade-off, not a free lunch. There is, however, another route: \"The working period, however, can be shortened in some branches simply by an extension of cooperation; the completion of a railway is hastened by setting afoot great armies of workers and tackling the job from many different points in space.\" Here, no machine is required — merely the assembling of more workers and more material. But that too is expensive: \"Here the turnover time is shortened by the growth of the capital advanced. More means of production and more labour-power have to be united under the capitalist's command.\" The dialectical statement follows: \"If the shortening of the working period is thus generally bound up with an increase in the capital advanced for this shorter time, so that the amount of capital advanced increases to the degree that the time of advance is shortened, we should remember that, apart from the total volume of social capital available, it comes down to a question of the extent to which the means of production and subsistence, i.e. disposal over them, are fragmented, or united in the hands of individual capitalists, i.e. the extent reached by the concentration of capital.\" This is the crucial social-relational turn. The availability of any shortening technique is not a pure engineering fact; it depends on whether capital is fragmented or united . A given technique may be technically feasible but socially impossible if the capital cannot be gathered. And once again, credit enters: \"In so far as credit mediates, accelerates and intensifies the concentration of capital in a single hand, it contributes to shortening the working period, and with this also the turnover time.\" Credit is thus not only a response to a long working period; it is also a means of reducing it, by enabling concentration that can be deployed to accelerate the job. The natural limit: agriculture, cattle, and the coercion of ready cash Now a pivotal restriction is introduced. \"In branches of production where the working period, whether it is continuous or interrupted, is prescribed by specific natural conditions , no shortening can take place by the means described above.\" He supports this with a quotation from W. Walter Good, Political, Agricultural, and Commercial Fallacies (London, 1866, p. 325) : \"In regard to quicker returns, this term cannot be made to apply to corn crops, as one return only can be made per annum. In respect to stock, we will simply ask, how is the return of two- and three-year-old sheep, and four- and five-year-old oxen to be quickened?\" The point is: agriculture and animal husbandry are biologically rhythm-bound. No finance, no cooperation, no machinery can make a sheep into a four-year-old in two years. The working period is a natural constant of these branches, not a social variable. From this constraints follows a systematic distortion. The need for \"ready cash as soon as possible (e.g. to pay fixed obligations such as taxes, ground-rent, etc.)\" is resolved by \"selling and slaughtering cattle before they have reached the normal economic age, to the great detriment of agriculture; this also leads, moreover, to a rise in meat prices.\" Financial pressure (fixed obligations) forces premature liquidations. The economic optimum (wait for full maturity) clashes with the cash-flow necessity (sell now). The falling rate of calf-slaughter: Good's agricultural polemic Marx extends the evidence with a second quotation from Good (pp. 11–12). The passage describes how English cattle-breeders in the Midland and eastern counties had been \"crippled through the uncertainty and lowness in the prices of corn.\" They shift to dairy: Butter is taken to market weekly to help pay current expenses. Cheese is drawn upon from a factor, who \"takes the cheese when fit to move, and, of course, nearly at his own price.\" Because of these pressures, \"the calves which used to come south from the dairying counties for rearing, are now largely sacrificed, at times at a week and ten days old, in the shambles of Birmingham, Manchester, Liverpool, and other large neighbouring towns.\" The counterfactual follows: \"If, however, malt had been free from duty, not only would farmers have made more profit and therefore been able to keep their stock till it got older and heavier, but it would have been substituted for milk and rearing by men who did not keep cows, and thus the present alarming scarcity of young cattle which has befallen the nation would have been largely averted.\" The reformism is transparent — a tax policy (malt duty) is invoked as a lever that would ease cash-flow pressure and permit cattle to be matured. The core of the problem is given voice by the farmers themselves: \"We know very well it would pay to rear on milk, but it would first require us to put our hands in our purse, which we cannot do, and then we should have to wait a long time for a return, instead of getting it at once by dairying.\" Here the long working period appears as a liquidity barrier : the farmers know the long-run operation is more profitable, but they lack the working capital to fund the interval — and cannot borrow it. Marx then universalizes: \"If the lengthening of turnover can have consequences like this even among the smaller English farmers, it is easy to understand what disturbances it must provoke among the small peasants of the Continent.\" The turnover problem, harsh in Britain, is catastrophic where credit is even less developed. Fixed versus circulating capital revisited: asymmetry of delayed reflux Marx uses this empirical excursion to reinforce a theoretical distinction he had begun earlier. \"According to the duration of the working period, and thus also the period till a commodity ready for circulation is completed, the portion of value that the fixed capital surrenders layer by layer to the product mounts up, and the reflux of this portion of value is delayed.\" But — crucially — \"this delay, however, does not necessitate a renewed outlay of fixed capital . The machine continues to operate in the production process whether replacement for its wear and tear flows back quicker or more slowly in the money form.\" With circulating capital, however: \"Here not only must capital be tied up for a longer time, in proportion to the duration of the labour process, but new capital must continually be advanced for wages, raw and ancillary materials. The delayed reflux thus has a different effect in the two cases.\" The asymmetrical logic, restated: Fixed capital : delayed reflux means the capitalist has to wait longer to recover value already invested, but the asset keeps working regardless. There is no additional outlay. Circulating capital : delayed reflux changes nothing about the existing advanced capital, which is stuck in an unfinished product — \"if it is tied up in the form of unsold, or unfinished and not yet saleable products, and there is no additional capital to renew it in kind,\" the production process simply cannot continue. This asymmetry generates the immediately practical conclusion: the long working period is above all a burden on circulating capital , and the capacity to bear it depends on having hoarded liquidity or secured credit. The fixed capital is patient; the circulating capital is imperious. India, Manu, and the social conservatism of the ox Marx extends the analysis with an extraordinary colonial quotation from the Return, East India. Madras and Orissa Famine, no. 4, p. 44 : \"While the peasant farmer starves, his cattle thrive. Repeated showers had fallen in the country, and the forage was abundant. The Hindoo peasant will perish by hunger beside a fat bullock. The prescriptions of superstition, which appear cruel to the individual, are conservative for the community; and the preservation of the labouring cattle secures the power of cultivation, and the sources of future life and wealth. It may sound harsh and sad to say so, but in India it is more easy to replace a man than an ox.\" The point being illustrated is that, when the working period of the means of production is long — as it is when livestock must be preserved across lean seasons in order to secure future harvests — societies may develop cultural rules that enforce what cash-flow logic cannot. The \"superstition\" against killing the ox is not really superstition but an institutional solution to a working-period problem. If the ox is eaten, the plough cannot be pulled next year; the working period of agricultural production would be catastrophically broken. The taboo preserves the capital. Marx then cites the Manava Dharma Sastra (the classical Hindu code ascribed to Manu as the progenitor of mankind), from the English translation by Graves Chamney Haughton (third edition, Madras 1863). The quoted passage (Chapter X, 62) reads: \"Desertion of life, without reward, for the sake of preserving a priest or a cow ... may cause the beatitude of those base-born tribes.\" The religious sanction — permitting the sacrifice of life to preserve a cow — is presented as an ancient cultural codification of the same economic necessity: the animal's long working period makes it more valuable, in a certain schema, than a human life that can be replaced. It is worth being precise about how Marx is using this material. He is not endorsing the proposition that a man is worth less than an ox; he is observing that, given the length of the working period required to raise a replacement animal, pre-industrial society — regardless of its moral ideals — operates under a harsh economic logic in which the animal is irreplaceable within the relevant time horizon , while the human labourer is more readily replaceable. The two quotes are offered as documentation of that structure, not as a moral stance. Bakewell and the breeding of time Marx returns to a possible mitigation. It is \"impossible, of course, to deliver a five-year-old animal before the end of five years.\" But \"what is possible within certain limits is to prepare animals for their fate more quickly by new modes of treatment.\" Enter Bakewell . Previously, British sheep — like French sheep as late as 1855 — were not ready for slaughter before the fourth or fifth year. In Bakewell's system, one-year-old sheep can already be fattened, and in any case are fully grown before the second year has elapsed. Bakewell, farmer of Dishley Grange in North Leicestershire, achieved this by careful selective breeding , which \"reduced the bone structure of his sheep to the minimum necessary for their existence.\" The result: the New Leicesters. Marx then quotes Lavergne, The Rural Economy of England (1855, p. 20) : \"The breeder can now send three to market in the same space of time that it formerly took him to prepare one; and if they are not taller, they are broader, rounder, and have a greater development in those parts which give most flesh. Of bone, they have absolutely no greater amount than is necessary to support them, and almost all their weight is pure meat.\" The illustration is instructive because it shows that even biologically natural working periods can be compressed — not by violating biology, but by altering the organism so that the natural maturation horizon itself is shortened. Bakewell did not make a five-year-old sheep in one year; he bred a sheep whose economic maturity is reached in one to two years. The working period is thus, in principle, technically malleable even in agriculture — but only within natural limits, and at the cost of an enormous long-run investment in the reproductive stock. The incommensurable improvements: relative lengthening despite absolute shortening The chapter closes with a final, subtle caution. \"The methods that shorten the working period differ greatly in the extent to which they can be applied in different branches of industry, and they do not cancel out the differences in length of the different working periods.\" To make this vivid: \"To stick to our example, the application of new machine-tools may, in absolute terms, shorten the working period necessary for the production of a locomotive. But if improved processes in spinning increase the finished product turned out daily or weekly here to an even greater extent, then the length of the working period in machine-building will still have increased relatively , compared with that in spinning.\" This is a comparative-dynamics point of considerable sophistication. Absolute shortening in both branches does not eliminate the relative difference — and it is the relative difference that governs the comparative circulation requirements of the two branches. Improvements in spinning, by increasing the spinner's turnover speed even more, widen the relative gap, so that from the standpoint of capital allocation the machine-building sector becomes, comparatively, even more capital-intensive in its turnover structure than before. The absolute easing of one side is not the disappearance of the gap; often it is its amplification. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Chapter 12 delivers a fully articulated theory that is at once technically precise, historically grounded, and politically pointed. Its central lesson is that the physical duration of production is an independent economic force in its own right. It cannot be dissolved into the theory of fixed and circulating capital, into the length of the working day, or into organic composition. It has its own quantitative law — the additional circulating capital required equals the weekly outlay multiplied by the number of weeks of the working period — and its own qualitative consequences: the imperative of additional liquidity, the vulnerability of the unfinished product to interruption, and the systematic exposure of the capitalist to time itself. From this, several broader implications follow. First, time is a factor of production. Marx's analysis shows that in the long-working-period industries, time itself is not free. The necessity of waiting — of \"advancing\" capital for weeks, months, or years before any reflux occurs — imposes a cost that must be borne by someone, and the form of that bearing — self-financing, joint-stock financing, credit, state provisioning, client advances — becomes a historical marker of the stage of capitalist development. Where credit is underdeveloped, the customer pays in instalments (as in the older house-building model) or the state builds by forced labour (roads, canals). Where credit is developed, the customer disappears and the speculative builder appears. Second, the theory of the working period is a theory of financial institutions. Credit is not merely a neutral channel; it is the mechanism by which the long working period becomes capitalistically feasible. But it is also the mechanism by which other people's capital is put at risk. The 1857 crisis in the building trade — where the halting of mortgage instalments collapses an undertaking that has exceeded the contractor's own means twenty to fifty times over — is a miniature of the financial fragility that a long working period generates. Credit simultaneously solves and amplifies the turnover problem. Third, the analysis of agriculture shows that natural constraints produce specific kinds of systemic distortion. The premature slaughter of calves and the alarm at the scarcity of young cattle demonstrate that when the working period is fixed by biological time, cash-flow pressure produces outcomes that are rationally irrational: individually intelligible, collectively destructive. The farmers in Good's testimony know that rearing pays better in the long run; they cannot afford to wait. The contradiction between the economically optimal and the financially feasible is one of the enduring features of capitalist agriculture, and Marx places it squarely within the theory of turnover. Fourth, the cultural material (India, Manu, Bakewell) shows that social institutions — religion, taboo, breeding practice — grow up around the working period as ways of managing its consequences . The prohibition on killing the ox, drafted into priestly law, is a functional response to the length of time required to replace the animal. Bakewell's selective breeding is a technical response to the same underlying problem. Both are recognitions, in different registers, that the working period cannot be abolished — only navigated. Fifth, the method of the chapter is itself a lesson. Marx moves from pure abstraction (equal capitals, equal composition, working period as the sole variable), through quantitative derivation (12× the circulating capital, the growing additional doses), through historical and institutional analysis (joint-stock companies, credit, speculative building), to documentary evidence (Builder's testimony, Good's agricultural polemic, the Madras famine return, Manu), and back to a refined theoretical conclusion (the relative lengthening of the working period despite absolute shortening). This layered movement — abstraction → arithmetic → history → evidence → refined abstraction — is a model of how a category of capital theory can be shown to be not merely an analytic convenience but a lived structure of economic society. Finally, Marx's chapter points forward. It announces that the \"time of advance can grow ... without the amount of capital advanced growing in proportion to this length of time\" — a distinction that will be developed in the following chapter on production time, where the working period will be separated from the broader category of time during which capital is present in the production process (including natural interruptions such as ripening, drying, chemical reaction). This chapter, then, is the first step in a longer argument: that capital's velocity — its turnover — is not simply an epiphenomenon of capital's magnitude but a co-determinant of the social relations it sustains. The chapter's lesson, ultimately, is that capital cannot escape time — and that the effort to discipline time, through machinery, cooperation, credit, and speculation, is one of the deepest and most distinctive historical features of the capitalist mode of production. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 11 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 13"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 13", "title": "Chapter 13", "subtitle": "The Production Time", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 13/Chapter 13 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 8784, "read_time": "44 min read", "tags": ["Turnover of Capital & Cycles"], "keywords": ["The Production Time", "Non-Working Production Intervals (Chemical, Biological)", "Grain Maturation, Tanning, Timber Growth", "Shortening Production Time through Chemistry and Science", "Gap between Labour Time and Production Time"], "text": "Chapter 13 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 8,770 words ⏱️ ~44 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 13: Production Time — A Complete Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS: Chapter 13 of Capital Volume 2 opens with a deceptively simple proposition that organizes the whole of Part II's analysis of turnover: \"Working time is always production time… But it is not true, conversely, that the entire time for which capital exists in the production process is necessarily therefore working time.\" This sentence contains the chapter's entire thesis. The chapter's intellectual objective is to dismantle the tacit assumption — carried over from the analysis of the working period in the preceding chapter — that the duration of capital's confinement to the production sphere is equivalent to the duration of the labour actually applied to the product. Marx demonstrates that these two magnitudes can diverge, often enormously, and that this divergence is not a peripheral curiosity but a systemic determinant of turnover time, of the volume of capital that must be advanced, of the valuation and durability of fixed capital, of the price of the product, and even of the social form of whole branches of production (agriculture, forestry, cattle-raising). The central problem is thus: what happens to capital during the interval when it is, by the nature of the product, still in the production process but no longer in the labour process? Marx's answer is that capital is held hostage by natural processes — physical, chemical, or physiological — that require time independent of human labour (fermentation, drying, bleaching, ripening, seasoning, the growth of timber). During these intervals the labour process is \"either completely or partially suspended,\" yet the capital cannot leave the production sphere because the product is unfinished and thus not yet convertible into commodity capital. This means the production period is longer than the working period , and this excess directly extends the turnover period. The chapter's placement within the broader theoretical architecture of Volume 2 is crucial. Part II (\"The Turnover of Capital\") is building a complete theory of how capital circulates and returns to its starting point. Chapter 12 established the working period as the first determinant of turnover. Chapter 13 introduces the production period as the second, more complex determinant, showing that the working period is merely a segment of the production period. This distinction will prove indispensable for everything that follows in Part II: the calculation of turnover, the annual rate of surplus-value, the distinction between fixed and circulating capital, and the analysis of the \"formation of stock.\" Furthermore, the chapter plants a flag for Volume 3, announcing that MacCulloch's and James Mill's \"nonsensical theories\" — which tried to identify production time with working time — will be dismantled there, since those errors spring from \"an incorrect application of the theory of value.\" So the chapter is not merely descriptive: it is a polemical staging ground, defending the labour theory of value against the attempt to treat nature's time as if it were labour time. Finally, the chapter carries a deeply social argument that runs beneath its technical surface. The natural divergence of production time and working time becomes, under capitalist production, the material basis for the subordination of rural labour to merchant capital, the separation of manufacture from agriculture, and the immiseration of the rural worker. Marx's chilling line — \"As far as capital is concerned… all these differences in the turnover balance out. Not so for the worker\" — signals that this is a chapter about political economy, not merely about scheduling. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS: 2.1 The Founding Distinction and the Nature of the Interruption The chapter begins by drawing the line that will govern everything else. Working time is time during which capital is actively engaged in the labour process — labour is being applied to the object of labour. Production time is the total time during which capital is confined to the production sphere, whether or not labour is being applied. The first is always a subset of the second; the second is not always coextensive with the first. Marx is immediately careful to demarcate the kind of interruption he means. He explicitly excludes interruptions \"conditioned by the natural limits of labour-power itself\" — that is, the simple fatigue of workers, the need for sleep, the daily and weekly pauses of the labour process. Those interruptions were already discussed in Volume 1, Chapter 10 (§4), where Marx noted that the fact that fixed capital — factory buildings, machinery — \"lies idle during the pauses in the labour process became one of the motives for the unnatural extension of the labour process, and for working day and night.\" So the idleness of capital during human pauses is an old theme, and it has a specific consequence: it drives capital to intensify the working day. What is at issue here is a different species of interruption : one \"independent of the length of the labour process,\" \"conditioned by the nature of the product and its production.\" During this interruption the object of labour is subjected to natural processes of shorter or longer duration and undergoes \"physical, chemical or physiological changes\" while the labour process is \"either completely or partially suspended.\" The critical conceptual move is that this interruption is not a failure of labour supply, nor a market event, nor a technical accident. It is immanent to the material nature of the product and its production . This makes it a production-sphere phenomenon in the strictest sense, rooted in physics, chemistry, or biology rather than in exchange. This grounding is what will later allow Marx to classify certain elements of turnover as arising from the production sphere rather than the circulation sphere — a distinction he treats as analytically decisive. 2.2 Natural Processes as Suspensions of the Labour Process: The Illustrative Cascade Marx builds the concept through a rapid cascade of examples, each chosen to display a different aspect of the phenomenon. Wine. \"After grapes have been pressed… the wine must go through a period of fermentation, and then also rest for a while before it reaches a certain degree of readiness.\" Here capital is in the production process (the wine exists as an unfinished product) but no labour is being applied — the chemical transformation happens on its own. Marx will later return to wine as the pure case of capital remaining in production time after the labour process has been performed . Drying and chemical transformation. \"In many branches of industry the product must undergo a process of drying, as in pottery, or else be exposed to certain conditions in order to change its chemical properties, as with bleaching.\" Drying is a physical process; bleaching is a chemical one. Both require elapsed time, not labour. Winter corn. \"Winter corn needs nine months or so to ripen. Between seed-time and harvest, the labour process is almost completely interrupted.\" Here we see the extreme case: the labour process is not merely paused but almost completely suspended for the better part of a year. This is the case that will dominate the rest of the chapter, because agriculture combines a long working period with a massive gap between working time and production time. Timber. \"In the raising of timber, once planting and the preliminary work connected with this is completed, the seed may need 100 years to be transformed into a finished product; during this whole time, only a relatively very insignificant intervention of labour is needed.\" This is the limiting case: production time measured in centuries, working time almost negligible. The point of putting timber last in the cascade is rhetorical — Marx is stretching the reader's sense of how far the divergence can go before he formalizes it. The unifying observation is stated immediately afterward: \"In all these cases, additional labour is added only occasionally for a large part of the production time.\" Marx contrasts this with the situation of the preceding chapter , where \"additional capital and labour has to be added to capital already tied up in the production process.\" In the previous chapter (on the working period), the continuation of production required continuous injections of labour and capital. Here, by contrast, those injections occur \"only with interruptions of a greater or lesser extent.\" This contrast is the bridge between the two chapters: the working period is a period of continuous outlay; the production period may be a period of intermittent outlay even though capital remains tied up throughout. 2.3 The Two-Period Structure of Production Time and the Extension of Turnover Marx now formalizes the structure. The production time of the capital advanced consists of two periods: A period in which the capital exists in the labour process (working time). A period in which its form of existence — that of an unfinished product — is \"handed over to the sway of natural processes, without being involved in the labour process.\" He notes that this structure \"is not altered if the two periods of time occasionally cut across one another or are interspersed.\" This is an important analytical caveat: in some processes the two periods are interleaved (labour applied, then nature works, then labour applied again, then nature works again) rather than strictly sequential. But whether the periods are sequential or interleaved, the aggregate relation holds. The core quantitative relation can be written as: Production period (P) = Working period (W) + Natural-process period (N) , where N ≥ 0. When N > 0, P > W , and \"here the working period and the production period do not coincide.\" The consequences flow directly: \"It is only after the production period has been left behind that the product is finished and mature, and can thus be transformed from the form of productive capital into that of commodity capital.\" So the value realization waits on the completion of P, not merely on the completion of W. \"The turnover period is then extended according to the length of that part of the production time that does not consist of working time.\" In other words, the excess N is added to the turnover period. This yields the chapter's central formula in words: Turnover is extended by N = P − W. The whole subsequent analysis is an elaboration of the consequences of this extension for the volume, timing, and valuation of capital advances. 2.4 Artificial Shortening of Production Time: Tanning, Bleaching, Iron Marx immediately introduces a critical qualification. The natural-process period is not always a fixed, unalterable datum. \"In so far as this time of production over and above the labour time is not determined by natural laws given once and for all, as with the ripening of corn, the growth of an oak, etc., the turnover period can often be shortened to a greater or lesser extent by the artificial shortening of the production time.\" This introduces a crucial distinction between naturally fixed production times (biological rhythms of ripening and growth) and technically reducible ones (chemical and physical processes subject to industrial intervention). Marx gives a series of escalating examples: Bleaching. \"The introduction of chemical in place of open-air bleaching.\" The open-air method required the product to be exposed to sunlight and air for long periods; chemical bleaching compresses the process dramatically. Drying. \"More effective drying apparatus in the drying processes.\" The introduction of machinery to accelerate evaporation. Tanning. \"In tanning, the penetration of tannic acid into the skins, which used to take between six and eighteen months with the old method, only takes one and a half to two months with the new method involving the use of the air-pump.\" Marx cites J. D. Courcelle-Seneuil's Traité théorique et pratique des entreprises industrielles (Paris, 1857, 2nd edn, p. 49). Note the magnitude of compression: from 6–18 months down to 1.5–2 months , i.e., a reduction of roughly 70–90% of the natural-process period. Iron production. \"The most far-reaching example of artificial shortening of a production time made up exclusively of natural processes is given by the history of iron production over the last 100 years, from the invention of puddling in 1780 to the modern Bessemer process and the latest procedures introduced since then.\" Marx's verdict: \"The production time has been enormously curtailed, but the application of fixed capital has also increased to the same extent.\" This last sentence carries a theoretical payload. It establishes a substitution relation : shortening natural-process time requires massive fixed capital investment . The turnover gain is purchased with a change in the organic and technical composition of capital. This connection anticipates the discussion of fixed capital later in the chapter and is one of the earliest hints in this Part of the way technical change reshapes turnover. 2.5 The Shoe-Last Example: Intra-Capital Divergence of Turnover Marx now introduces what he calls \"a peculiar example of the divergence between production time and working time\": \"the American manufacture of shoe-lasts.\" Here \"a significant part of the expense arises from the wood having to dry out in store for up to eighteen months, so that the finished lasts do not warp. During this time, the wood does not undergo any other labour process.\" The consequence: \"The turnover period of the capital applied is therefore not only determined by the time required to produce the lasts themselves, but also by the time for which the capital has to lie idle in the shape of wood which is being dried out. The capital exists in the production process for eighteen months before it can enter the labour process proper.\" This example introduces two new analytical points: Capital can be in production time before the labour process proper. This is the mirror image of the wine case (where capital is in production time after the labour process). In shoe-last manufacture, the drying-out happens prior to shaping the wood into lasts. So the natural-process period can precede, follow, or interleave with the labour process. Different parts of the total circulating capital can have different turnover times, determined by the production sphere. This is the \"peculiar\" element. Marx states explicitly: \"This example also shows how the turnover times of various parts of the total circulating capital may differ as a result of circumstances that arise from the production sphere and not from the circulation sphere.\" This is a crucial methodological point: not all turnover differentiation is to be explained by market and circulation phenomena. Some of it is materially rooted in the nature of production itself. 2.6 Agriculture as the Paradigmatic Case \"The distinction between production time and working time is particularly important in agriculture.\" Marx now pivots to the branch where the divergence is largest and most consequential. He lays out the agricultural case in temperate climates: \"the land brings forth grain once a year. The shortening or lengthening of the production period (an average of nine months for winter sowing) is itself dependent on the alternation of good and bad years, and hence cannot be precisely determined in advance and controlled, as in industry proper.\" Two features distinguish agriculture from industry proper: Annual biological rhythm. Grain is harvested once per year; the production period is ~9 months for winter sowing. Ineliminable uncertainty. Because the production period depends on weather, it cannot be \"precisely determined in advance and controlled.\" In industry proper , by contrast, production time is largely technically controllable. Only \"subsidiary products such as milk, cheese, etc. can be produced and sold continuously in short periods.\" Marx then documents the working period in agriculture with a quantitative extract from F. Kirchhof's Handbuch der landwirtschaftlichen Betriebslehre (Dessau, 1852, p. 160). The number of working days across the three main working periods in different German districts is: Spring (mid-March/early April to mid-May): 50–60 days Summer (early June to late August): 65–80 days Autumn (early September to end of October/mid-to-late November): 55–75 days Winter : only \"work suited to that period, such as haulage of fertilizer, wood, goods for market, building materials, etc.\" Summing the three main periods, working time amounts to roughly 170–215 days per year , leaving a substantial winter period in which agricultural labour is largely absent. The gap between the production period (~365 days for the annual crop cycle) and the working period (~170–215 days) is thus enormous. This is the empirical substance of the theoretical divergence. 2.7 The Russian Case and the Genesis of Rural Subsidiary Industry The more extreme the climate, the more compressed the working period and the wider the gap between working time and production time. Marx quotes the Reports by H.M. Secretaries of Embassy and Legation (no. 8, 1865, pp. 86–87): \"In some of the northern districts, field labour is only possible during from 130 to 150 days in the course of the year, and it may be imagined what a loss Russia would sustain, if out of 65,000,000 of her European population, 50,000,000 remained unoccupied during six or eight months of winter, when all agricultural labour is at a standstill.\" So in northern Russia the working period shrinks to 130–150 days , leaving 6–8 months of enforced idleness for a peasant population of 50 million out of 65 million. Marx's concern here is not merely technical. This material condition — the seasonal idleness of a vast rural population — is the natural basis for the rise of rural subsidiary industries: \"There are villages, for instance, in Russia in which all the peasants have been for generations either weavers, tanners, shoemakers, locksmiths, cutlers, etc.,\" particularly in the gubernias of Moscow, Vladimir, Kaluga, Kostroma and St Petersburg. \"These cottage industries, incidentally, are already being pressed more and more into the service of capitalist production; for example, merchants supply the weavers with warps and weft, either directly, or by intermediate agents.\" This is one of the chapter's most theoretically loaded passages. Marx derives a social consequence from a natural fact: \"We see here how the distinction between production period and working period, with the latter forming only a part of the former, constitutes the natural basis for the unification of agriculture with rural subsidiary industries, just as these, in turn, are points of vantage for the capitalist, who first intrudes in his capacity as merchant.\" The causal chain is: natural divergence → seasonal idleness → subsidiary industries → merchant capital enters → rural labour subordinated . And then the historical sequel: \"In so far as capitalist production later manages to complete the separation between manufacture and agriculture, the rural worker becomes ever more dependent on merely accidental subsidiary employments and his condition thereby worsens.\" Finally, the devastating symmetry that closes this movement: \"As far as capital is concerned, as we shall see later on, all these differences in the turnover balance out. Not so for the worker.\" This is the class-theoretic pivot of the chapter. The natural unevenness of agricultural production time is indifferent to capital in the long run (the turnover differences average out across the capitalist class and across the cycle), but it is existentially determining for the worker, who must endure the seasonal dependency it generates. 2.8 Unevenness of Circulating Capital Outlay and the Fixed Capital Problem Marx now contrasts the smooth reproduction of industry proper with the lumpy reproduction of branches characterized by a production period longer than the working period. \"In most branches of industry proper, as well as in mining, transport, etc., production proceeds evenly and the same working time is worked year in year out; apart from fluctuations of price, disturbances of business, etc. and abnormal interruptions, the outlay of capital going into the daily circulation process is evenly distributed. While market conditions remain the same, therefore, the reflux or renewal of circulating capital is distributed over the whole year in equal portions.\" By contrast: \"However, in those investments of capital where working time forms only one part of the production time, there is great unevenness in the outlay of circulating capital in the course of the different periods of the year, in as much as the reflux only follows, at one stroke, at a time prescribed by natural conditions.\" This produces a specific quantitative consequence for capital advances. \"On a given scale of business, therefore, i.e. with the same volume of circulating capital advanced, this must be advanced in larger amounts at once, and for a longer time, than in those businesses with continuous working periods.\" More formally: if the reflux is concentrated at a single point (harvest) rather than spread evenly across the year, then the same annual volume of circulating capital requires a larger front-loaded advance and a longer period of advance before reflux. The capital is tied up longer with no compensating gradual return. This is the material root of the agricultural credit problem, and it is the same structural fact that makes the agricultural producer chronically dependent on those who can extend credit — precisely the mechanism Hodgskin will describe. Marx then notes that \"the life of fixed capital is significantly different here from the time in which it actually functions productively.\" Because the fixed capital (draught cattle, implements, machines) is idle for long stretches, its chronological lifespan and its productive-functioning time diverge. This introduces the next movement of the chapter. 2.9 Fixed Capital Idle Time, Depreciation, and the Dearness of the Product \"With this difference between working time and production time, the time during which the fixed capital is utilized is of course constantly interrupted for a longer or shorter interval; in agriculture for instance, with the use of draught cattle, implements and machines.\" Marx distinguishes two cases of idle fixed capital: Draught animals (a \"living\" fixed capital). \"In so far as this fixed capital consists of draught animals, it continues to require the same or almost the same outlay on fodder, etc. as during the time in which it operates.\" So the maintenance cost is not suspended even though the productive use is. Feed is consumed during idleness — pure cost. Dead means of labour. \"In the case of dead means of labour, non-use also gives rise to a certain depreciation.\" Machinery rusts, buildings decay, materials degrade. Here the loss is not an outlay but a value loss. The consequence for price: \"The product thus always becomes dearer, since the transfer of value to the product is not calculated according to the time for which the fixed capital functions, but rather according to the time in which it loses value.\" This is a subtle and important point about value transfer. The value that fixed capital transfers to the product is proportional to the time over which it depreciates (its lifetime ), not the time over which it functions (its operational time). If a machine functions only half the year but depreciates all year, then its annual depreciation cannot be charged solely against the goods produced during the functioning half; the depreciation cost is spread over fewer units of output, raising the unit cost. Equivalently: the value loss is continuous while the use is intermittent, so the value transferred per unit is inflated. Marx then makes a striking analytical generalization: \"In these branches of production it is a condition of normal use that fixed capital should lie idle, whether or not this still involves running costs, just as in spinning a condition of normal use is the loss of a certain quantity of cotton; and in the same way, in every labour process, the labour-power expended unproductively, but unavoidably so under normal technical conditions, counts just as much as the productive.\" This is a crucial conceptual move. Idle fixed capital in agriculture is not an anomaly to be eliminated — it is a normal technical condition of production , exactly analogous to the inevitable waste of cotton in spinning or the inevitably unproductive expenditure of labour-power under normal technical conditions. The idleness is a cost of production like any other. And so the general rule follows: \"Every improvement that reduces the unproductive expenditure of means of labour, raw materials and labour-power also reduces the value of the product.\" So the earlier iron-production example is not isolated: technical improvements in any of these dimensions lower unit values, whether by shortening N or by reducing idle-time costs. 2.10 Hodgskin on Agricultural Dependence Marx now turns to a passage from Thomas Hodgskin's Popular Political Economy (London, 1827, p. 147, note) that captures the social consequence of agricultural production time: \"The difference of time (although he does not differentiate here between working time and production time) required to complete the products of agriculture, and of other species of labour\" is \"the main cause of the great dependence of the agriculturists. They cannot bring their commodities to market in less time than a year. For that whole period they are obliged to borrow of the shoemaker, the tailor, the smith, the wheelwright, and the various other labourers, whose products they cannot dispense with, but which are completed in a few days or weeks. Owing to this natural circumstance, and owing to the more rapid increase of the wealth produced by other labour than that of agriculture, the monopolizers of all the land, though they have also monopolized legislation, have not been able to save themselves and their servants, the farmers, from becoming the most dependent class of men in the community.\" Marx's editorial note is important: Hodgskin \"does not differentiate here between working time and production time.\" Hodgskin has seen the difference of time as the cause of dependence, but he conflates the two categories that Marx has just distinguished. This is not a mere pedantic correction: it shows that the social effect (dependence) can be perceived even when the analytical distinction (production vs. working time) has not yet been drawn. Marx's contribution is to reveal the precise mechanism — the extension of turnover by N — that generates the dependence Hodgskin observed. The substance of Hodgskin's argument is a self-sufficiency deficit : the agricultural producer cannot bring output to market for at least a year, but needs the products of shoemakers, tailors, smiths, and wheelwrights, which are completed in days or weeks. The temporal asymmetry forces the agriculturist into debt — hence dependence. And even the monopolizers of land and legislation cannot escape the structural dependence this creates for \"themselves and their servants, the farmers.\" 2.11 Intensification: Crop Rotation, Undersowing, and Increased Circulating Capital Marx now notes a dialectical counter-tendency. \"All methods in agriculture which on the one hand distribute expenditure on wages and means of labour more evenly over the whole year, and on the other hand shorten the turnover, by diversifying the products and thus making different crops possible during the year, require an increase in the circulating capital laid out on production, on wages, fertilizer, seed, etc.\" So there is a trade-off : evening out and shortening turnover requires more circulating capital. Marx gives two historical illustrations: Transition from the three-field system (with fallow) to crop rotation without fallow. Abolishing the fallow year means land is productive every year, shortening the rotation cycle — but it requires more intensive inputs. The cultures dérobées (undersowing system) in Flanders. \"The root crops are planted by undersowing; the same field first bears corn, flax or rape-seed for human requirements, and then after the harvest root crops are sown for the maintenance of cattle. This system, which enables the horned cattle to remain permanently in the stall, produces a considerable amount of manure and is thus the cornerstone of crop rotation. More than a third of the cultivated area in the sandy districts is undersown in this way; it is just as if the cultivated land had been extended by a third.\" The undersowing system's logic is an elegant intensification: by planting a second (fodder) crop beneath the main crop, the same land produces twice within a single year, feeding cattle in-stall year-round, generating manure, and thereby closing the nutrient cycle. The quantified result: more than a third of the cultivated area is undersown , effectively extending the arable surface \"by a third.\" The capital consequence is stated via Émile de Laveleye's Essais sur l'économie rurale de la Belgique (Brussels, 1863, pp. 59–60, 63): \"Agriculture, being thus carried to the point at which it is transformed into horticulture, understandably requires a relatively considerable capital investment. In England the sum reckoned with is 250 francs of investment capital to the hectare. In Flanders our farmers will probably find an investment capital of 500 francs per hectare far too low.\" So the intensification of production time management doubles (at least) the capital requirement per hectare — from 250 francs in England to over 500 francs in Flanders. 2.12 Timber-Raising: Production Time Beyond a Human Life Marx now returns to timber-raising, quoting Kirchhof (p. 58) at length: \"The production of timber is fundamentally different from most others in that here natural forces work independently, and the power of men or capital is not required for natural growth. Even where forests are artificially cultivated, the amount of human and capital power expended in comparison with the action of natural forces is only slight. Furthermore, forests will thrive in types of soil and places where grain cannot grow, or where it no longer pays to produce it. Forest culture, however, requires a greater surface area than the cultivation of grain, if it is to be conducted on a regular commercial basis. Since small plots do not allow proper forestry methods, the secondary uses are abandoned, and forest protection is made more difficult, etc. The production process is also tied to such a long period of time that it extends beyond the plans of a private undertaking, and sometimes even beyond a single human life. Capital invested in the acquisition of forest land (in communal production this capital disappears and the question is simply how much land the community can withdraw from arable and grazing land for timber production) only bears fruit after a comparatively long period of time, and turns over only partially, taking up to 150 years in the case of many types of wood. Moreover, effective timber production actually requires a reserve stock of growing timber amounting to between ten and forty times the annual yield. Thus someone who does not have other income or possess substantial areas of forest cannot pursue regular forestry.\" Marx draws the theoretical conclusion: \"The long production time (which includes a relatively slight amount of working time), and the consequent length of the turnover period, make forest culture a line of business unsuited to private and hence to capitalist production, the latter being fundamentally a private operation, even when the associated capitalist takes the place of the individual.\" This is a major structural claim. Capitalist production has a horizon limit imposed by its private form. When production time exceeds a human lifespan (up to 150 years for many woods) or the planning horizon of a private undertaking, it falls outside the scope of capitalist organization. The parenthesis Marx inserts — \"(in communal production this capital disappears and the question is simply how much land the community can withdraw from arable and grazing land for timber production)\" — is telling: the transactions by which forest land is acquired are an artifact of private ownership, and under communal production the question becomes a planning question, not a capital question. The reserve-stock ratio is one of the chapter's few quantitative laws: effective timber production requires growing stock amounting to 10–40× the annual yield , which means the turnover of the total capital takes 10 to 40 years and more . Marx emphasizes this by repeating the Kirchhof passage: \"Thus the turnover takes from ten years up to forty and more.\" And he adds a historical coda that underlines the destructiveness of the civilizational dynamic: \"The development of civilization and industry in general has always shown itself so active in the destruction of forests that everything that has been done for their conservation and production is completely insignificant in comparison.\" So the branch that is least suited to capitalist production is also the branch that capitalist civilization most rapidly destroys — a contradiction worth pondering. 2.13 Cattle-Raising and the Category of Stock Marx now turns to cattle-raising as an intermediate case. \"Part of the herd (cattle stock) remains in the production process, while another part is sold as the annual product. Here only one part of the capital turns over each year, just as in the case of the fixed capital — machinery, draught cattle, etc.\" Two points are made here: Partial turnover. Only the part sold each year turns over; the breeding herd remains as ongoing stock. So the annual turnover fraction is less than 1 in relation to the standing herd. But it is not fixed capital \"in the categorical sense.\" Marx is explicit: \"Even though this capital is fixed for a longer time in the production process, and thus lengthens the turnover of the total capital, it does not constitute fixed capital in the categorical sense.\" So cattle stock (and timber stock) is a distinct analytical category — permanently tied-up productive capital that is neither fixed capital in the strict sense nor ordinary circulating capital. Marx then generalizes the category of stock : \"What is referred to here as a stock — a definite quantity of growing wood or cattle — exists partially in the production process (both as means of labour and material of labour); depending on the natural conditions of its reproduction, a significant part must always exist in this form in the case of regular cultivation.\" Note the pregnant phrase: the stock exists \"both as means of labour and material of labour.\" The breeding herd is both the instrument of production (it produces calves) and the raw material (its offspring are the product). Likewise, growing timber is both the standing means of production and the maturing product. This dual character is what makes the category of stock irreducible to either fixed or circulating capital. 2.14 Productive Stock, Replacement Difficulty, and the Circulation Sphere Marx then introduces a second kind of stock : \"a stock that forms only potential productive capital, but has to be accumulated in larger or smaller amounts as a result of the nature of agriculture, and must be advanced to production for a relatively long time even though it enters the active production process only bit by bit. This includes manure, for example, before it is carted to the field, as well as corn, hay, etc. and any stocks of feed that go into the production of cattle.\" This is the category of potential productive capital — means of production that have been advanced (purchased/withdrawn from the fund) but have not yet entered the active production process. They sit in reserve, and their reserve status is imposed by the discontinuous nature of agricultural production. Marx connects this to his earlier discussion (pp. 215–20) of the formation of stock, where he established that \"a greater or lesser quantity of potential productive capital is required, i.e. a quantity of means of production destined for production, which has to be held in reserve in a greater or lesser amount in order to go into the production process bit by bit.\" He then states the determinants of stock size : \"with a capital investment of a given scale, the size of this production stock depends on the greater or lesser difficulty of its replacement, its relative proximity to the supplying markets, the development of means of transport and communication, etc. All these circumstances affect the minimum capital that must exist in the form of productive stock, and thus the period of time for which advances of capital have to be made, and the volume of capital that has to be advanced at once.\" So the minimum productive stock is a function of replacement difficulty, market proximity, and transport/communication development. And this volume \"also has an effect on the turnover\" because it is \"determined by the longer or shorter time for which circulating capital is tied up in the form of productive stock.\" Then comes a crucial analytic bifurcation: \"On the other hand, in so far as the extent of this stagnation depends on the greater or lesser possibility of rapid replacement, on market conditions, etc., it itself arises from the circulation time, from circumstances that pertain to the circulation sphere.\" So Marx here carefully separates two sources of turnover determination: the production sphere (natural-process time, the nature of the product) and the circulation sphere (market proximity, transport, replacement ease). The shoe-last turnover divergence arose \"from the production sphere\"; the required volume of productive stock is partly determined \"from the circulation sphere.\" This two-sphere classification is fundamental to the entire Part II architecture. 2.15 Kirchhof on Inventory and the Timing of Purchases Marx adds another Kirchhof extract (p. 301) on the stock of implements and accessories: \"Moreover, a stock of all these implements or accessories, working tools, sieves, baskets, ropes, axle grease, nails, etc. is all the more necessary for replacement at any moment, the less opportunity there is of procuring them quickly in the vicinity. Finally, the entire inventory should be carefully inspected each winter, and the necessary additions and repairs immediately put in hand. Whether a larger or smaller stock of implements is generally needed is principally determined by local conditions. Where there are no craftsmen or shops in the vicinity, a greater stock must be kept than where these are to be found in the locality or very close by. If the requisite stocks are procured in greater quantities at once, under otherwise similar conditions, the advantage in cheap purchase is generally obtained, provided that a suitable point of time has been chosen; but of course a greater sum is then withdrawn at once from the current capital, which cannot always be dispensed with.\" This passage reinforces the replacement-difficulty determinant of stock size and adds a further market consideration: bulk purchasing yields a price advantage but withdraws a larger sum at once from current capital, which may strain the business. So the optimal stock policy is a trade-off between price (bulk discount) and liquidity (current capital constraint). This is a concrete illustration of the abstract principle that the volume of productive stock is determined by both production-sphere and circulation-sphere conditions. Two earlier Kirchhof passages (pp. 292, 300) had already laid out the management implications of stock: the need for painstaking attention to barns, fodder and grain lofts and cellars; storage places \"kept well closed, and also kept clean, ventilated, etc.\"; grain turned over periodically; potatoes and beets protected against \"frost, water and rot.\" And on feed: \"In calculating one's own requirements, particularly for cattle… attention must be paid not only to covering requirements, but also to having a sufficient stock left over for unforeseen contingencies. As soon as it appears that the need cannot be fully met by one's own production, it is necessary to take into consideration whether this lack cannot be covered by other products (substitutes), or whether these cannot be procured more cheaply in place of the missing products. If there should be a lack of hay, for example, this can be made up by root crops with added straw. In general, the material value and market price of the different products must be constantly borne in mind, and consumption regulated accordingly; if oats are dearer, for instance, while peas and rye are relatively cheap, then it will be advantageous to replace some of the oats for the horses with peas and rye, and sell the superfluous oats.\" These passages are not mere practical advice. They illustrate the rational calculation of stock under uncertainty — a necessary feature of production where the production period exceeds the working period, because the producer must cover a long non-productive interval from a stock accumulated during the productive interval, with substitution flexibility to absorb price shocks. 2.16 Recapitulation of the Range of Possibilities Marx now provides a systematic recapitulation of the range of temporal patterns the divergence permits: \"The difference between production time and working time, as we have seen, permits a wide range of possibilities.\" \"The circulating capital can be in its production time before it enters the actual labour process (manufacture of lasts).\" \"It may still be in production time after it has undergone the actual labour process (wine, seed-corn).\" \"The production time may be occasionally interrupted by labour time (field crops, timber).\" \"Or a large part of the product in a condition ready for circulation may remain incorporated in the active production process, while a much smaller part enters into the annual circulation (timber and cattle-growing).\" \"The greater or lesser length of time, thus the greater or lesser measure in which circulating capital has to be laid out all at once in the form of potential productive capital, partly arises from the kind of production process (agriculture) and partly depends on the proximity of markets, etc. , in short, on circumstances that belong to the circulation sphere .\" This recapitulation is the chapter's analytic synthesis: it maps the full topology of the divergence and reaffirms the two-source structure (production sphere vs. circulation sphere) of turnover determination. 2.17 The Mill/MacCulloch Polemic and the Theory of Value Marx closes the substantive argument with a forward reference: \"We shall see later on (in Volume 3) what nonsensical theories MacCulloch, James Mill, etc. were led to in their attempts to identify this production time diverging from working time with working time, attempts which in their turn arose from an incorrect application of the theory of value.\" This is the chapter's most compressed but theoretically crucial passage. The target is the attempt — by MacCulloch and James Mill (the Utilitarian philosopher and Ricardo disciple whose Elements of Political Economy appeared in 1821) — to identify production time with working time , i.e., to claim that the interval during which capital is in the production process but not in the labour process is nonetheless \"working time\" in the value-theoretic sense. Marx calls this \"nonsensical.\" Why? Because if production time is identified with working time, then nature's time would be credited with creating value — which contradicts the labour theory of value. The erroneous identification arises from \"an incorrect application of the theory of value\": because value is created only by labour, and because production time ≠ working time, those who wish to explain the valuation of goods whose production requires long natural intervals are tempted to pretend that the natural interval is labour time, so that the labour theory can accommodate the observed prices. Marx's point is that this evasion is unnecessary: the labour theory can accommodate the divergence by recognizing that production time extends turnover (thereby affecting capital advances, profitability, and price of production) without creating value . Value comes from labour; the natural interval affects the turnover through which value is realized and the capital that must be advanced — but it does not itself create value. The footnote elaborates: \"On MacCulloch see above, p. 94.\" And it notes: \"It is not in Volume 3 of Capital , as published, but rather in Theories of Surplus-Value , that Marx's discussion of the elder Mill's 'futile attempts to resolve the contradictions of the Ricardian system' is to be found (Part III, pp. 84ff.), a discussion of MacCulloch coming later on in the same chapter, 'The Disintegration of the Ricardian School' (pp. 168ff.).\" So the polemic is not merely a footnote to Chapter 13 but a hinge between Volume 2 and Volume 3, tying the analysis of turnover to the critique of Ricardian value theory. 2.18 The Rotation Cycle: Lease Length and the Compounding of Turnover The final movement of the chapter returns to the concept of the turnover cycle as a function of the durability of fixed capital. \"The turnover cycle that we considered previously is a function of the durability of the fixed capital advanced to the production process. Since this encompasses a greater or smaller number of years, it also encompasses a series of turnovers of the fixed capital, repeated either yearly or within a year.\" In agriculture, this kind of cycle arises from the system of crop rotation . Marx quotes Kirchhof (pp. 117–118) at length: \"The duration of the lease must in any case not be shorter than the time taken to complete the system of crop rotation that is introduced, and hence with the three-field system it is always reckoned in terms of 3, 6, 9, etc. If we assume the three-field system with complete fallow, the field is cultivated only four times in six years, and in the years cultivated, with both winter and summer grain; the properties of the soil also require or permit this to be alternated between wheat and rye, barley and oats. Each kind of grain grows on the same soil better or worse than the others, each has a different value and is also sold at a different price. The yield of the land thus varies with the years of cultivation; it is different in the first half of the cycle (in the first three years) and in the second. Even the average yield over the whole cycle is not the same in one case as in the other, since fertility does not just depend on the quality of the soil, but also on the year's weather, the price also depending on many different conditions. If the yield is calculated on the basis of the average years of the entire cycle of six years, and of the average prices in these years, then the total yield for one year can be arrived at for either period of the rotation. But this is not the case if the yield is only calculated for half of the rotation, i.e. for three years, since then the total yields would not be the same. It is evident from this that the duration of the lease in the three-field system must be fixed at at least six years. It is always far more desirable for both landlord and tenant that the lease should run for a multiple of the lease (sic: F.E.) and thus instead of six years in the case of the three-field system, it should be twelve or eighteen years or more, and in the case of the seven-field system not seven but fourteen or twenty-eight.\" The argument is a statistical one, and it deserves careful unpacking. In the three-field system with complete fallow, the field is cultivated only four times in six years (two cultivation years, each with winter and summer grain, in a six-year cycle — the other two years are fallow). The yields differ across the years of the cycle (first half vs. second half differ; soil quality interacts with crop type; weather varies year to year; prices vary). Therefore: A three-year lease is too short because the yields over any three-year half-cycle are systematically different from a full-cycle average. A six-year lease captures one complete rotation , allowing the calculation of a representative average across the full cycle. This is the minimum lease length . Longer leases (multiples of the cycle) are preferable because they further smooth out year-to-year variations, giving a more representative average, and reducing the risk that the tenant's tenure happens to fall in an unfavorable sub-period. The final judgment: leases should be at least 6 years for the three-field system (ideally 12, 18, or more ), and at least 7 years for the seven-field system (ideally 14 or 28 ). The rotation cycle thus functions, in agriculture, analogously to the durability period of fixed capital in industry: both define the minimum horizon over which capital can be productively evaluated and recouped . The chapter closes with a manuscript note: \"(The manuscript states here: 'The English system of crop rotation. A note on this to be given.' — F.E.)\" — an editorial signal that Marx intended to extend this analysis but left the note unfinished. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS: The chapter yields several fundamental principles that together constitute a substantial addition to the theory of turnover. First, the law of the two-fold composition of production time. Every production process has two potentially distinct temporal components: the time labour is applied (working time) and the time natural processes act independently (natural-process time). The production period is their sum; the turnover period is extended by the natural-process excess. This is a material law — rooted in physics, chemistry, and biology — not a conventional or institutional one. It establishes that turnover time has a substratum in the physical constitution of the product and its production, irreducible to market rhythms or human volition. Second, the concept of unproductive but necessary capital. Idle fixed capital, depreciation during non-use, fodder consumed by idle draught animals, and the \"unavoidable\" waste of materials and labour-power under normal technical conditions are all conditions of normal production , not accidents. They enter the unit value of the product as costs exactly as productive expenditures do. This dissolves the naive notion that idle capital is \"wasted\" capital and shows that value transfer follows depreciation time , not functioning time — a subtle but consequential point about how fixed capital contributes to the value of the product. Third, the category of potential productive capital and the two-sphere determination of turnover. The chapter distinguishes: - Production-sphere determinants of turnover (natural-process time, the nature of the product, the material form of capital). - Circulation-sphere determinants of turnover (replacement difficulty, market proximity, transport and communication development, price conditions). This two-sphere framework becomes foundational for the rest of Part II. It allows Marx to trace precisely why different capitals turn over at different rates, and to show that this differentiation is not uniformly reducible to market phenomena. Fourth, the class asymmetry of natural time. The natural unevenness of agricultural production — long production periods, seasonal working intervals, lumpy reflux, dependence on weather — is indifferent to capital in the aggregate (\"all these differences in the turnover balance out\") but destructive to the worker , who bears the seasonal unemployment, the subsidiary-industry dependency, and the ever-worsening condition produced by the separation of manufacture from agriculture. This is the chapter's political core: the natural basis of agricultural labor is transformed by capitalist relations into a structure of subordination and immiseration. Fifth, the limits of capitalist production as a private form. The timber case demonstrates that branches requiring production times longer than a private planning horizon (up to 150 years, with reserve requirements of 10–40× annual yield) \"cannot\" be sustained under private and hence capitalist production. This is a structural — not incidental — limit. The parenthesis about communal production suggests that the limit is a function of the private form of capital, not of production as such. Sixth, the staging of the Mill/MacCulloch critique. By announcing that the attempt to identify production time with working time is \"nonsensical\" and arises from \"an incorrect application of the theory of value,\" Marx positions this chapter as a defense of the labour theory of value. Natural time does not create value; it extends turnover, forces larger and longer capital advances, and shapes the price of production through the mechanism of capital turnover — but value remains the product of labour alone. The distinction between production time and working time is thus not merely a technical refinement; it is the analytical firewall that prevents the conflation of nature's time with labor's time, and thereby preserves the coherence of the value theory. Broader implications. For economic theory, the chapter shows that any account of capital turnover that treats it as a function solely of the labour process will systematically misestimate the capital requirements, profitability, and credit structure of branches with long natural-process intervals — agriculture, forestry, tanning, brewing, and iron historically foremost among them. For the theory of ground rent and agrarian credit, the chapter provides the material foundations: the seasonal idleness of the working population, the front-loaded capital advance, the dependence on merchants and lenders, the lease-length determination by rotation cycles. For the critique of political economy, it supplies one of the most rigorous demonstrations that value is a social relation, not a natural fact: the same physical interval — eight months between sowing and reaping — creates value through labour and extends turnover through nature, and the distinction between the two is invisible to those who identify production time with working time. Finally, for the broader Marxian project, the chapter reveals the deep material roots of rural underdevelopment and the subordination of agriculture to industrial capital: the natural rhythm of agricultural production is the substrate on which merchant capital first intrudes, manufacture is later separated from agriculture, and the rural worker becomes \"ever more dependent on merely accidental subsidiary employments\" — a structure of dependence that begins in the soil and ends in the wage relation. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 12 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 14"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 14", "title": "Chapter 14", "subtitle": "The Circulation Time", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 14/Chapter 14 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 7429, "read_time": "37 min read", "tags": ["Circulation Time & Costs", "Turnover of Capital & Cycles"], "keywords": ["The Circulation Time", "Selling Time, Transport Infrastructure, and Railways", "Credit Impact on Shortening Circulation Delays", "Market Proximity and Telegraph Communication", "Velocity of Goods Realization"], "text": "Chapter 14 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 7,394 words ⏱️ ~37 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 14 of Capital, Volume II, \"Circulation Time,\" is a compact but pivotal intervention in Marx's theory of capital turnover. It sits within Part II of Volume II, immediately after the chapters on the working period and production time (Chapters 12–13) and immediately before the chapter on the effects of turnover time on the advance of capital (Chapter 15). Its function is therefore transitional yet foundational: it completes the decomposition of turnover time into its two great components — production time and circulation time — and then isolates the second component to show that it is an independent, quantifiable, and materially grounded determinant of how capital must be advanced, how quickly it refluxes, and how much of it must lie idle in the money form. The central problem the chapter poses is this: everything considered up to this point as differentiating circulation periods — the distinction between fixed and fluid capital, the difference in working periods — arose within the production process itself . But turnover time is the sum of production time and circulation or rotation time. It is therefore \"self-evident\" that circulation times of varying length produce different turnover periods. The task is to isolate this variable: to compare two capital investments identical in every respect except circulation time, or to hypothetically vary the circulation time of a single capital while holding its composition and working period constant. The chapter is thus an exercise in controlled abstraction — an attempt to trace the consequences of one variable in isolation. The core thesis unfolds in three movements. First, the selling time — the interval during which capital exists as commodity capital — is \"relatively the most decisive\" segment of circulation time, because it is the phase in which value must be realized, and because its length determines how long the whole circuit is arrested. Second, the distance of the market from the place of production is a \"permanently effective cause\" of differentiation in selling time; the chapter then develops a subtle dialectic of transport, showing that improved transport shortens circulation absolutely but does not abolish relative differences, and may even displace them in ways that do not correspond to natural distances — explaining the rise and decline of centres of production. Third, and most consequentially, the chapter culminates in the recognition that a definite portion of the capital advanced must always exist in the money form . This reserve is not a contingent detail but a structural necessity of the circuit of capital, and the neglect of it by economists is precisely what blinds them to the inner workings of the \"bourgeois economy.\" The intellectual objective is thus threefold: to establish circulation time as an autonomous determinant of turnover; to demonstrate its material grounding in geography, transport technology, and market institutions (auctions, usances, delivery contracts); and to derive from it the necessity of a permanently held money-capital reserve, which forms the material basis of credit. In the broader architecture of Marx's critique, this chapter supplies the link between the mechanics of individual capital turnover and the macroscopic phenomena of credit, speculation, and crisis — a link fully developed in Volume III. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 14.0 The Starting Point: Circulation Time as an Independent Variable of Turnover Marx opens by drawing a sharp line between two families of causes. On one side stand the circumstances \"so far considered\" — those arising within the production process itself : the distinction between fixed and fluid capital, differences in working periods, and so on. On the other side stands circulation time. The theorem he states is almost algebraic in its simplicity: turnover time = production time + circulation or rotation time . Because turnover is a sum , and because one of its addends (circulation time) can vary independently of the other, it follows that \"circulation times of varying length make for different times of turnover and thus different turnover periods.\" The methodological move here is a classic Marxian controlled comparison. He proposes to isolate the variable in two ways: (a) by comparing two different capital investments in which \"all other circumstances modifying the turnover are equal and only the times of circulation are different\"; or (b) by taking a given capital — a given composition in terms of fixed and fluid capital, a given working period — and hypothetically varying only the circulation time . This is the analytical equivalent of holding all variables constant save one. It is important to grasp that Marx is not claiming that circulation time is the only determinant of turnover; he is claiming it is a genuine and independent one, irreducible to production-process factors. This distinction matters because it licenses the remainder of the chapter: if circulation time were merely a reflex of production, there would be nothing further to say. The chapter's entire payload rests on the autonomy of this variable. A subtlety worth flagging: the phrase \"circulation or rotation time\" signals the dual character of the phase. Circulation time is not dead time; it is time during which capital performs a necessary metamorphosis — commodity into money, money into the elements of production. The length of that metamorphosis is what varies. And because the metamorphosis is social (it depends on markets, buyers, distances, payment conventions) rather than merely technical (like the baking of bread or the mining of coal), its determinants are inherently variable and historical. This is the seed of everything that follows. 14.1 Selling Time as the Decisive Segment of Circulation Time Marx immediately identifies the dominant sub-phase: \"One section of circulation time — and relatively the most decisive one — consists of selling time, the period in which the capital exists in the state of commodity capital.\" Here we encounter a central term of the whole volume: commodity capital (in the German, Warenkapital ) is capital in the form of finished, saleable products — value that has been produced but not yet realized. The longer capital is trapped in this state, the longer the entire turnover period is stretched. Two consequences follow. First, \"an additional outlay of capital may also be necessary for costs of storage etc.\" Storage is a pure circulation cost: it adds to the capital advanced without adding to the value produced (at least not in the simple sense developed here). Second, Marx stresses that selling time varies not only between branches of production but within a single branch, \"for the various independent capitals invested in a particular sphere of production, which in actual fact simply constitute bits of the total capital which have attained an independent position.\" This is a crucial theoretical point: the \"independent capitals\" competing in a branch are, in aggregate, fractions of the total social capital that have been parcelled out and given separate existences. Their apparently idiosyncratic differences in selling time are therefore not merely personal accidents; they are the ways in which the division of social capital into competing units manifests itself in turnover. Marx notes that with other circumstances equal, the selling period of the same individual capital changes with \"the general fluctuations in market conditions, or with fluctuations in the particular line of business in question.\" He then makes a deliberate methodological restriction: \"We shall not deal any further here with this point.\" This is an important act of bracketing. He wants to separate the permanent, structural determinants of circulation time from the transient, conjunctural ones. The former are the object of the chapter; the latter — market fluctuation — will reappear under the heading of crisis and credit. What he does extract from the discussion is the general proposition that all circumstances producing variation in turnover between branches may also operate individually within a branch — \"if one capitalist has the occasion to sell more quickly than his competitor, if one applies more methods that shorten the working period than the other does.\" Individual differentiation within a branch thus mirrors, in miniature, the differentiation between branches. The same causal grammar operates at both scales. 14.2 The Distance of the Market as a Permanent Cause of Differentiation The chapter now pivots to its first structural determinant: \"A permanently effective cause of differentiation in the selling time, and hence in the turnover time in general, is the distance of the market where the commodities are sold from their place of production.\" The word \"permanently\" is doing heavy work. Unlike market fluctuations, distance is a durable feature of the production–market relation, grounded in geography. Marx gives the mechanism precisely. \"For the whole period of its journey to the market, capital is confined to the state of commodity capital.\" The transformation C′→M′ cannot be completed while goods are in transit. If production is to order , an additional interval is added: the time during which the commodity is \"up for sale on the market\" after arrival. So the selling time is at minimum the transit time, plus any market-exposure time. This is why the space separating production from consumption becomes time — a delay inscribed directly into the turnover period. Distance, in other words, is not an external accident of commerce; it is metabolized by capital as a lengthening of the circulation phase. This establishes the first major theorem of the chapter: turnover periods differ systematically according to the geographical relation between production and market, even when production conditions are identical. A capital producing for a nearby market and a capital producing for a distant one, with the same technical composition, will have different turnover periods — and therefore different annual rates of surplus value, a point Marx exploits in later chapters. 14.3 The Dialectics of Transport: Absolute Acceleration versus Relative Difference Marx now introduces his most sophisticated analytical move. It would be naive to think that improved transport simply abolishes the differentiation caused by distance. His argument is dialectical: improvement in the means of communication and transport \"shortens absolutely the period in which commodities migrate,\" but \"it does not abolish the relative difference in the circulation time of different commodity capitals arising from the migration, or even that of different bits of the same commodity capital that migrate to different markets.\" He illustrates with ships: \"Improved sailing ships and steamships, for instance, which shorten the journey, shorten it just as much for nearby ports as for distant ones. The relative difference remains, even though it is often reduced.\" This is a precise logical point. Absolute speed-up applies pro rata. If a voyage of 60 days to port A and 120 days to port B is halved, the new times are 30 and 60 days — the absolute gap falls from 60 to 30 days, but a relative gap persists, and indeed the ratio (1:2) is unchanged. Only if the acceleration were differential — faster to near ports than far ones — could the relative difference be altered. Hence \"the relative difference remains, even though it is often reduced.\" But Marx immediately complicates this with a second, sharper claim: relative differences \"may however be displaced by the development of the means of communication and transport in a way that does not correspond to natural distances.\" A railway running from a place of production to a major inland centre can lengthen the effective distance (absolutely or relatively) to a nearer inland point not served by rail , inverted relative to the natural geography. This produces two macroscopic effects: (a) it explains \"the demise of old centres of production and the emergence of new ones with changes in the means of transport and communication\"; and (b) it is compounded by the fact that transport becomes \"relatively cheaper\" for longer distances than for shorter ones. Here we see a genuinely dynamic, non-linear conception of commercial geography: transport technology does not simply shrink space uniformly; it re-sculpts the economic map, privileging some locations and stranding others. Marx thus refuses any mechanical \"distance = time\" reduction; the relation between space and time is mediated by the network structure of transport. The theoretical significance is considerable. Circulation time is shown to be a socially produced variable, historically mutable through investment in fixed capital (railways, steamships, canals), and therefore inseparable from the dynamics of accumulation itself — not a natural constant. 14.4 The Frequency Revolution: Distributed Reflux and the Acceleration of Turnover Marx now distinguishes two dimensions of transport improvement that are easily conflated: speed and frequency (or regularity). With the development of transport, \"the speed of movement in space is accelerated, and spatial distance is thus shortened in time.\" But separately, \"the mass of means of communication develops\" — many ships depart for the same port at the same time, several trains run along different railways between the same two points, and, crucially, \"freight ships leave Liverpool for New York, for example, on different successive days of the week, and goods trains run at different hours of the day from Manchester to London.\" Marx draws a fine and important conceptual distinction. This last development \"does not alter the absolute speed, and so neither this part of the circulation time, if the effectiveness of the means of transport remains at a given level.\" That is, if a train takes ten hours regardless, running more trains does not make any individual train faster. Yet it transforms circulation. Why? Because \"successive quantities of goods can now start their journey at more closely spaced intervals, and thus arrive on the market one after the other without accumulating in great masses as potential commodity capital until they are actually dispatched.\" The outcome: \"the reflux is distributed over shorter successive periods of time, so that one part is steadily being transformed into money capital while another part circulates as commodity capital.\" And \"by this distribution of the reflux over several successive periods, the total circulation time is shortened, and hence also the turnover.\" This is a genuinely subtle point about the temporal structure of the capital circuit. Frequency permits staggering : instead of sending one giant consignment (which must all be completed and dispatched together, and which all arrives at once, and which all sells — or fails to sell — at once), capital can be dispatched in a steady stream. The continuous, distributed reflux means that money is flowing back from earlier shipments while later shipments are still in transit. The aggregate circulation time — measured as the average time from dispatch to realization across the flow of goods — falls, even without any increase in the speed of any individual journey . Marx is thus distinguishing what we might call velocity per unit from turnover rate of the flow , and showing that the latter can be improved by logistics alone. This is one of the most analytically modern passages in the chapter. 14.5 The Feedback Loop: Transport, Concentration, and the Reshaping of Geography Marx next traces a reflexive relationship between transport and the spatial organization of production — a feedback loop that is simultaneously economic and geographical. On the first horn: \"the greater or lesser frequency with which the means of transport function … develops with the degree to which a place of production produces more, and becomes a major centre of production, and this is a development in the direction of the already existing market.\" In other words, transport frequency is induced by the scale of production; more output demands more departures. On the reverse horn: \"this particular ease of commerce and the consequent acceleration in the turnover of capital … gives rise to an accelerated concentration of both the centre of production and its market.\" And the concentration of people and capital at given points in turn accelerates \"the concentration of these masses of capital in a few hands.\" So the causal arrow runs in both directions: production scale → transport frequency → accelerated turnover → concentration → further scale. This is a cumulative, self-reinforcing dynamic. Marx then adds a displacement effect: \"There is simultaneously a further shift and displacement as a result of the change in the relative situation of production and market places which itself results from the changes in the means of communication.\" A place formerly advantaged by position on a main road or canal now finds itself on \"a single railway branch line that operates only at relatively long intervals,\" while a point formerly off the main routes now lies \"at the intersection of several lines.\" Hence: \"The second place rises, the first declines.\" The conclusion is that transport changes \"bring about local variations in the circulation time of commodities, in the opportunities to buy and sell, etc., or else they alter the distribution of already existing local variations.\" Marx grounds the practical importance of this in institutional conflict: \"The importance of this factor in the turnover of capital is evinced by the disputes between the mercantile and industrial representatives of different places and the directors of railways.\" He cites the Blue Book of the Railway Committee — a British parliamentary report, an example of Marx's characteristic use of official empirical sources to document the class and locational struggles over circulation time. 14.6 Local Branches: Breweries and the Compensation of Urban Rent A brief but telling corollary follows. \"All branches of production which, owing to the nature of their product, are oriented principally to local outlets, such as breweries, thus develop to their largest dimensions in the major centres of population.\" The logic is that products that cannot travel far — perishable or bulky goods — must locate near their consumers. And then the compensation mechanism: \"Here the rapid turnover of capital partly balances out the increase in the cost of many conditions of production, building land, etc.\" This is a miniature model of locational equilibrium: higher urban rents and input costs are offset by faster turnover (and thus a higher annual rate of surplus value). Capital's locational calculus, in other words, weighs circulation time against production costs — an insight that anticipates much of later urban and regional economics. 14.7 The World Market Dialectic: Shortening Circulation While Extending It Marx now articulates one of the chapter's great dialectical tensions. \"If the progress of capitalist production and the consequent development of the means of transport and communication shortens the circulation time for a given quantity of commodities, the same progress and the opportunity provided by the development of the means of transport and communication conversely introduces the necessity of working for ever more distant markets, in a word, for the world market.\" This is a genuine contradiction, not a rhetorical flourish. The same force that shrinks circulation time for a given quantity simultaneously drives capital to sell over ever greater distances , so that: \"The mass of commodities in transit grows enormously, and hence so does the part of the social capital that stays for long periods in the stage of commodity capital, in circulation time — both absolutely and relatively.\" Absolute growth, because the volume of production rises; relative growth, because a larger share of total social capital is locked in the transit and commodity phase. Hence \"a simultaneous and associated growth occurs in the portion of social wealth that, instead of serving as direct means of production, is laid out on means of transport and communication, and on the fixed and circulating capital required to keep these in operation.\" The dialectic is this: improved transport is both a remedy for and a cause of the expansion of the circulation phase. Capital overcomes spatial barriers only by creating new, larger ones — an instance of the general Marxian theme that capital's solutions generate its own new contradictions. The \"world market\" is not merely a larger arena; it is a structurally more circulation-intensive configuration of social capital. 14.8 The Example of India: From Selling Time to Purchasing Time The chapter now shifts from the first half of circulation time (selling) to the second — purchasing time , \"the transformation of money back into the elements of productive capital.\" The relative length of the journey affects both. Marx offers a canonical numerical example: \"Say that the commodity is sent to India. This takes maybe four months. Let us take the selling time as zero, i.e. assume that the commodity is shipped to order and paid for on delivery to the producer's agent. A further four months is required to send back the money (the form in which it is remitted is immaterial here). It is thus altogether eight months before the same capital can function once again as productive capital, and can be used to renew the same operation.\" This example is analytically precise. By setting selling time to zero (shipment to order, immediate payment on delivery to the agent), Marx isolates the pure transit-and-return component. The four months out and four months back yield eight months of total circulation time before the capital can re-enter production. The lesson: distance affects not only selling time but also purchasing time , because the money arising from a distant sale must itself travel back — or be remitted — before it can be reconverted into productive inputs. And here Marx draws the crucial connection to credit: \"The variations in turnover brought about in this way form one of the material bases for differing periods of credit, just as overseas trade in general, in Venice and Genoa, for instance, formed one of the original sources of the credit system in its true sense.\" This is the theoretical payoff of the chapter's first half. Long-distance trade creates long turnover periods; long turnover periods make it impossible for the merchant to pay immediately and wait for the reflux; therefore credit emerges as the social mechanism for bridging the gap. Credit is not an arbitrary convention but a functional response to the temporal structure of capital circulation. The historical reference to Venice and Genoa grounds this in the actual genesis of commercial capitalism. 14.9 The Usance and the Historical Struggle over Credit Terms Marx now deploys a dense block of empirical evidence from The Economist to show the historically contested character of credit terms. The key institutional term is usance : the customary period allowed for the currency of bills of exchange between two places — i.e., how long a bill drawn in one market may run before becoming payable. Two variants matter throughout the discussion: bills \"at X months' date \" run from the date the bill is drawn; bills \"at X months' sight \" run from the date the bill is presented or accepted for sight. Sight terms are therefore longer in calendar duration than date terms, since they include the time in transit. The narrative Marx assembles runs as follows. \"The crisis of 1847 enabled the banking and mercantile community of that time to reduce the India and China usance … from ten months' date to six months' sight.\" Then, \"the lapse of twenty years with all the accelerations of speed and establishment of telegraphs … renders necessary … a further reduction\" — from six months' sight toward four months' date \"as a first step to four months' sight.\" The telegraph is thus cited as a material cause of credit compression: instantaneous communication makes the long usance functionally obsolete. Marx then parses the arithmetic of the proposed reduction. \"The voyage of a sailing vessel via the Cape from Calcutta to London is on the average under 90 days. An usance of four months' sight would be equal to a currency of say 150 days. The present usance of six months' sight is equal to a currency of say 210 days\" ( Economist , 16 June 1866). The point is that usance must be wide enough to cover voyage time plus handling, and that 4 months' sight = ~150 days conveniently brackets a <90-day voyage with margin, whereas 6 months' sight = ~210 days is excessive. Against this, the counter-argument (which Marx quotes at length from the same journal, 30 June 1866) defends the existing usance: \"The Brazilian usance remains at two and three months' sight, bills from Antwerp are drawn on London at three months' date, and even Manchester and Bradford draw upon London at three months and longer dates. By tacit consent, a fair opportunity is afforded to the merchant of realizing the proceeds of his merchandise … within a reasonable time of [when] the bills drawn against it fall due.\" The argument continues: Indian produce is \"for the most part … sold in London with three months' prompt,\" and allowing for \"loss of time in effecting sales, cannot be realized much within five months, while another period of five months will have previously elapsed (on average) between the time of purchase in India and of delivery in the English warehouse.\" The sum: \"a period of ten months, whereas the bill drawn against the goods does not live beyond seven months.\" In other words, the merchant needs ten months from purchase to realization, but the bill matures in seven — so the usance, far from excessive, is already too short relative to the full turnover, and the residual gap is covered by further credit. This is a concrete demonstration of how credit terms must be calibrated to circulation time, and of the friction — indeed, insolvency risk — when they are not. Marx closes the episode with the institutional denouement: on 2 July 1866, five big London banks dealing mainly with India and China, together with the Paris Comptoir d'Escompte, gave notice that from 1 January 1867 their Eastern branches \"will only buy and sell bills of exchange at a term not exceeding four months' sight\" ( Economist , 7 July 1866). Yet — and this is the telling outcome — \"this reduction miscarried, and had to be abandoned.\" A parenthetical editorial note, almost certainly inserted by Engels (who prepared Volume II for publication), adds: \"(Since then the Suez Canal has revolutionized all this.)\" The Suez Canal is the decisive material transformation — cutting the voyage dramatically and thereby making the older usance structure obsolete. The entire episode is a case study in the contestation of circulation time: banks, merchants, and industrialists struggle over credit periods because those periods are the institutional skin of the underlying turnover times. 14.10 Risk, Usances, and Unequal Terms of Payment From the usance discussion Marx extracts a general proposition: \"It is clear that with the longer circulation time of commodities, the risk of a change of price on the selling market rises, owing to the lengthening of the period in which this price change can occur.\" This is a crucial risk-theoretic point. The longer the interval between production and sale, the wider the window during which market prices may move adversely. Circulation time, therefore, does not merely delay the reflux of value; it exposes that value to heightened risk. This risk is one of the roots of speculative and hedging practices, and partly explains why long-distance trade historically required merchants with substantial capital reserves (cf. the Kirchhof quotation below). Marx then notes another source of differentiation: \"A difference in circulation time, both individually between different capitals in the same branch of industry, and between different branches of industry according to the different usances, when payment is not immediately made in cash, arises from the different terms of payment in purchase and sale.\" Again he brackets the topic — \"We shall not dwell any longer on this point here, although it is important for the credit system\" — signalling its place in his larger plan. The pattern is consistent: Marx identifies a causal factor, locates it in circulation, and defers its full development to the analysis of credit. 14.11 The Delivery Contract: Circulation Reacting Back on Production Next comes a subtle and important analytical beat: the size of delivery contracts , which \"grows with the volume and scale of capitalist production,\" itself gives rise to differences in turnover time. Marx is careful about the logical status of this factor: \"The contract of delivery, as a transaction between buyer and seller, is an operation pertaining to the market, to the sphere of circulation. The differences in turnover time arising from it thus arise from the circulation sphere, but they react directly back on the production sphere, quite apart from all terms of payment and credit conditions, i.e. even with cash payment.\" This establishes a two-way determinism — the chapter's signature methodological theme. A circulation-sphere institution (the contract) retroacts on production time. The mechanism is then spelled out with great clarity. Coal, cotton, yarn, etc. are \"discrete products\": each day yields its quantum of finished output. But if the spinner or mine-owner \"agrees to deliver quantities of products which require, say, a four-week or six-week period of successive working days, it is just the same, with respect to the length of time for which capital has to be advanced, as if a continuous working period of four to six weeks was introduced into his labour process.\" Marx explicitly assumes the entire quantity is to be delivered at once, or at least paid for only after full delivery. Hence each day's output is \"still only a part of the quantity contracted for\"; even if the finished fraction is \"no longer in the production process, it is still merely lying in the warehouse as potential capital.\" The upshot: a contract for bulk future delivery converts what is technically discontinuous production (daily discrete output) into something functionally equivalent to a continuous working period, because no sale — and thus no reflux — occurs until the whole contract is fulfilled. The advance of capital is lengthened accordingly. This demonstrates that circulation-side arrangements can impose production-side burdens of advance even in the absence of credit. It is a prime example of the chapter's recurring insight: the boundaries between production and circulation are porous and mutually constitutive. 14.12 The Time of Purchase and the Permanent Money Capital Marx now returns to the second stage of circulation time : \"the time of purchase, or the period in which the capital is transformed back from the money form into the elements of productive capital. In the course of this period it must persist for a shorter or longer time in its state of money capital, and thus a certain part of the total capital advanced always exists in the state of money capital, although this part consists of constantly changing elements.\" He illustrates with the algebra of a working business: \"In a particular business, for instance, n × £100 of the total capital advanced has to be present in the form of money capital; this is continuously being transformed into productive capital, but just as constantly being added to again by the influx from circulation, from the realized commodity capital. Thus a definite portion of the capital value advanced always exists in the state of money capital, i.e. in a form pertaining not to its sphere of production but rather to its sphere of circulation.\" The expression n × £100 is a placeholder for the money-capital requirement scaled to the volume of the business: if £100 must be held and recycled n times over the turnover to keep operations running, then n × £100 of capital value is permanently crystallized in the money form. The key point is coexistence : at any instant, part of the total capital is in money form, part in productive form, part in commodity form. This is not a temporary error or slack in management; it is structurally required by the fact that purchase takes time. A certain fraction of capital must always wait in cash until the moment of purchase. Marx then links back to prior chapters. \"We have already seen how, when the time in which capital is confined to the form of commodity capital is prolonged, by the greater distance of the market, this directly gives rise to a delayed reflux of money, and thus also delays the transformation of capital from money capital into productive capital.\" And he cites Chapter 6: the time of purchase and the distance from the major sources of raw materials make it necessary \"to buy raw materials for longer periods and keep them available in the form of productive stock, latent or potential productive capital; how this increases the mass of capital that must be advanced at one stroke, and the time for which it must be advanced, the scale of production being otherwise the same.\" Two technical terms are now in play. Latent or potential productive capital designates raw materials and stores held in readiness but not yet actually in the labour process — value locked in a non-productive form but necessary for production to proceed continuously. And the result is twofold: purchases near sources of supply require advances in bulk (because buying is infrequent and quantities are large), so both the mass of capital that must be advanced at once and the duration of that advance are increased, even when the scale of production is unchanged. Here circulation time directly dictates the magnitude of the capital advance. 14.13 Raw Material Markets and Speculative Stock This theme is extended to the temporal rhythms of raw-material markets. \"In different branches of industry, the shorter or longer periods for which large quantities of raw materials are thrown onto the market have a similar effect.\" Marx gives two contrasting London examples: \"major auctions of wool take place every three months, and these dominate the wool market, whereas the cotton market is on the whole supplied continuously from harvest to harvest, even if not always evenly.\" Such periodicity \"determines the major terms of purchase for these raw materials, and particularly affects speculative purchases, making necessary longer or shorter advances in these elements of production.\" The logic is clear: if wool is only auctioned quarterly, a manufacturer must either buy large quantities at the auction and hold them (requiring a large advance and long storage) or risk buying at less favourable prices between auctions. Market structure thus translates into capital advance requirements. Marx then notes the mirror image on the selling side: \"the nature of the commodities produced affects the speculative and deliberate withholding of products from the market for longer or shorter periods in the form of potential commodity capital.\" Just as purchases may be concentrated in time, sales may be deliberately withheld — meaning finished goods sit as potential commodity capital awaiting a favourable price. 14.14 Kirchhof's Rules of Speculative Holding Marx now quotes the German agricultural writer Kirchhof (p. 302) at length, both to illustrate and to expose the classed nature of speculative holding. Kirchhof begins: \"The agriculturalist must therefore also be a speculator to a certain extent, and hold back the sale of his products according to the conditions of the time…\" The parenthetical \"(A few general rules follow: F.E.)\" marks an editorial insertion by Engels, indicating that the following rules are drawn from Kirchhof and appended editorially. The rules themselves are practical maxims for agricultural marketing. Marketing \"mostly depends on the person, the product itself and the locality.\" A farmer who is skilful, fortunate, and \"endowed with sufficient operating capital\" is \"not to be blamed if he sometimes lets the crops he has obtained lie for a year when prices are unusually low.\" By contrast, \"someone who has insufficient operating capital … or who completely lacks the spirit of speculation … will seek to obtain the current average price, and will thus have to sell as soon and as often as he has the opportunity.\" Here the connection to Marx's argument is direct and pointed: the capacity to withhold — to speculate — depends on having a capital reserve. Poorer producers cannot afford to wait, because they lack the money capital to bridge the interval. Speculation is thus not a free economic choice but a privilege of liquidity. This dovetails exactly with the chapter's culminating thesis about the permanently held money capital. Kirchhof continues with the material conditions of different commodities: \"To let wool lie for longer than a year will almost always involve a loss, while corn and oil-seed can be kept for a few years without any detriment to their quality and properties.\" Products subject to \"a substantial rise and fall in price over short periods\" — oil-seed, hops, teasels — are \"rightly left to lie in the years when their prices stand far below the prices of production.\" Some goods are costly to postpone: those \"as give rise to daily costs of maintenance, such as fattened cattle, or are liable to spoil, such as fruit, potatoes.\" There is also seasonality: \"grain, for instance, is in many places generally lower in price at Martinmas than between Christmas and Easter,\" and some products \"are only good for sale at certain times, as is the case with wool in the wool markets of those districts where at other times the wool trade is generally dull.\" The theoretical payoff of the quotation is threefold. First, it shows that the physical properties of commodities constrain the possible length of withholding, hence the possible delay in selling time. Second, it shows that seasonality creates predictable variations in selling time. Third, and most importantly, it maps the class dimension of circulation time: the ability to extend selling time is a function of accumulated money capital, tying directly into Marx's final argument. 14.15 The Money Capital Reserve: The Pivot of the Conclusion The chapter concludes by crystallizing its central lesson. \"In considering the second half of the circulation time, during which money is transformed back into the elements of productive capital, it is not only this conversion alone that is involved, nor only the time in which the money flows back, according to the distance of the market where the product is sold. What is also and especially involved is the extent to which a part of the capital advanced must always exist in the money form, in the state of money capital.\" Marx then abstracts from speculation: \"If we leave out of consideration all speculative activities, the scale of purchases of those commodities that must be constantly present as a productive stock depends on the latter's periods of renewal, i.e. on circumstances that in turn depend on market conditions, and hence vary for different raw materials, etc. Here, therefore, money must from time to time be advanced in large amounts at once.\" But \"whether it flows back quicker or more slowly, according to the turnover of the capital, it always flows back bit by bit.\" Now comes the crucial distinction within the reflux itself. \"One part of it is just as regularly spent again at short intervals, i.e. the part transformed back into wages. Another part, however, that transformed back into raw materials, etc., has to be accumulated for a longer period of time as a reserve fund, either for purchase or for payment. It therefore exists in the form of money capital, although the extent to which it exists in this form changes.\" Wages are paid at short, regular intervals; raw material purchases are lumpy and require accumulated funds. Hence a reserve fund must be held in money form — its size fluctuating, but its existence constant. Finally, Marx delivers the chapter's methodological indictment of political economy: \"We shall see in the next chapter how other circumstances, arising both from the production and the circulation processes, require this presence of a definite portion of the capital advanced in the money form. It should generally be noted, however, that the economists are much inclined to forget not only that a part of the capital needed in a business is constantly passing alternately through the three forms of money capital, productive capital and commodity capital, but that it is always different portions of this that possess these forms alongside each other, even if the relative magnitudes of these portions are in constant flux. It is particularly the part always present as money capital that the economists forget, although precisely this circumstance is very necessary for the understanding of the bourgeois economy, and makes itself felt as such in practice as well.\" This is the theoretical climax. Two errors of the economists are identified. First, they forget the coexistence of the three forms of capital — money, productive, commodity — at every instant, imagining the circuit instead as a succession in which only one form exists at a time. Second, and more seriously, they forget the permanent money-capital reserve . This omission is not innocuous: it is precisely what leads them to misunderstand credit, hoarding, the money market, and — as Marx argues elsewhere — the possibility of crises rooted in the separation of purchase and sale. The \"bourgeois economy\" cannot be comprehended without recognizing that a fraction of social capital must always exist in the money form, functioning neither as means of production nor as commodities, but as ready purchasing power. This is the seed from which the analysis of loanable capital, the credit system, and the money market in Volume III grows. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental lesson of Chapter 14 is that circulation time is an irreducible, socially produced determinant of capital's motion — not an envelope around production but a force that reshapes it. Marx establishes this through a rigorous decomposition of turnover time into production and circulation components, then isolates the circulatory variable and shows that it varies systematically with distance, transport technology, market institutions (auctions, usances, delivery contracts), and commodity properties. From this analysis emerge several systemic principles. First, the unity and distinction of production and circulation. While Marx began by treating circulation as a separate sphere, the chapter repeatedly demonstrates their mutual constitution. Delivery contracts originating in the market retroact on production schedules; long journeys in circulation lengthen the advance of capital in production; the pricing of raw materials depends on the temporality of market supply. The boundaries between spheres are porous; capital's circuit is a single integrated process whose moments cannot be mechanically separated. Second, the dialectical character of transport development. Improved transport shortens circulation absolutely but preserves relative differences, may displace them, and simultaneously opens ever more distant markets — so that the mass of social capital locked in commodity form grows absolutely and relatively. Capital's means of overcoming spatial barriers are themselves the means of creating larger ones. This is a general template of Marxian analysis: capital's solutions generate new contradictions. Third, the structural necessity of money capital. The chapter culminates in the recognition that a definite portion of advanced capital must always exist in money form. This reserve is not idle slack but a functional prerequisite of continuous operation. Crucially, the scale of this reserve and the capacity to speculate are functions of accumulated capital, which introduces a class dimension to circulation time itself: producers with ample money capital can afford to wait, to speculate, and to ride out adverse price movements; producers without it must sell immediately and accept the current price. The chapter thus links microstructure (individual sale timing) to macrostructure (market power and credit). Fourth, circulation time as the material basis of credit. The India example (eight months for out-and-back) and the usance discussion show that credit periods are calibrated to circulation times. Bills of exchange exist to bridge the gap between dispatch and realization. But because circulation times are contested — and because credit terms, once fixed, may become too short or too long relative to changing transport conditions — the credit system carries within it a permanent source of fragility. The aborted 1866 attempt to shorten the Indian usance to four months' sight is a vivid historical instance. The broader practical and theoretical implications are substantial. Practically , the chapter frames logistics, warehousing, market proximity, and credit terms as core determinants of capital efficiency — a framework that remains fully relevant to modern supply-chain and working-capital management. Theoretically , the permanently held money-capital reserve serves as the bridge between the analysis of individual capital turnover (Volume II) and the analysis of the credit system, banking, and crisis (Volume III). If the economists forget this reserve, they cannot explain why credit arises, why it can be disrupted, or why the separation of buying and selling carries the possibility of crisis. Finally, the chapter embodies Marx's characteristic method: to begin with a simple, almost arithmetical distinction (turnover = production time + circulation time), to isolate the variable, and then to unfold from it a rich, historically grounded, and institutionally concrete analysis — showing that what appears to be a matter of mere \"time\" is in fact a condensed expression of the entire social and material organization of capitalist production. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 13 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 15"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 15", "title": "Chapter 15", "subtitle": "Effect of Circulation Time on the Magnitude of an Advanced Capital", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 15/Chapter 15 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 11849, "read_time": "59 min read", "tags": ["Turnover of Capital & Cycles", "Circulation Time & Costs"], "keywords": ["Effect of Circulation Time on Advanced Capital Magnitude", "Alternating Capitals in Circulation and Production", "Release and Tying-Up of Capital", "Precautionary Liquidity Reserves", "Calculation of Turnover Cycles and Capital Discontinuity"], "text": "Chapter 15 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 11,853 words ⏱️ ~59 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Core Thesis Chapter 15 of Capital Volume II marks the point at which Marx pivots from describing the mere anatomy of turnover (the taxonomy of working period, circulation period, and turnover period established in Chapters 13 and 14) to demonstrating the causal power of circulation time over the quantity and disposition of capital that a capitalist must commit. The chapter's central thesis can be stated with precision: circulation time is not a passive interlude between working periods but a material force that dictates how much total capital must be advanced, how that capital is divided into functional portions, and how much of the social circulating capital necessarily exists in the idle, latent form of money capital or surplus productive stock. Marx's controlling problem is disarmingly simple and devastatingly consequential. If a capitalist advances a fluid capital to produce commodities over a working period of, say, nine weeks, that capital cannot simply be reused at the end of the nine weeks: it must first complete a circulation period — pass through commodity capital, then money capital, and only then be reconvertible into productive capital. If the circulation period is three weeks, then at the close of week nine the entire £900 that had been functioning as productive capital steps out of the production process and stands idle, as commodity capital, for three weeks. During those three weeks the workshop stands empty, the labour power that had been purchased is not engaged by this capital, and production halts — unless a second, additional capital is thrown into the breach. This creates what Marx treats as an iron structural law of industrial capital: Law of continuity. If production is to proceed uninterruptedly and on a constant scale, the total capital advanced must always be divided into at least two parts: one part that is functioning as productive capital (in the working period) and another part that is functioning as commodity capital or money capital (in the circulation period). The ratio between these two parts is fixed by the ratio between the circulation period and the turnover period. This is the chapter's foundational law, and it ramifies into every subsequent calculation. From it follow all the subsidiary conclusions: that a portion of capital must always exist in the money form; that periodically a chunk of fluid capital is \"set free\"; that the reduction or prolongation of circulation time acts directly on the money market independently of the quantity of money in circulation or the prices of commodities; and that price changes in the elements of production or in the product act as an independent mechanism for precipitating or absorbing money capital. The Central Problem: Two Possible Responses to the Circulation Gap Marx poses the problem through a series of worked examples, and the problem has — as he demonstrates — only two possible solutions when production is to be continuous on the same scale: Reduce the scale of production so that the same total capital (£900) can be spread across the whole twelve-week turnover period (£75 per week instead of £100 per week). This presupposes a lower normal minimum of investment than most industries tolerate, and hence is not generally available as a solution. Advance an additional capital equal to the fraction of the total capital represented by the ratio of circulation time to turnover time (£300 on the £900 of the example), so that while the original capital is circulating, a second capital keeps the labour process going. The chapter's intellectual objective is to trace, with algebraic and arithmetic precision, the consequences of each of these solutions and their combinations across all possible ratios of working period to circulation period — equal, longer, shorter, simple multiple, non-multiple — and to determine in each case (a) how much capital must be advanced, (b) what portion is periodically \"set free,\" and (c) how these mechanics connect to the money market and the credit system. How the Chapter Fits into the Broader Framework Chapter 15 belongs to Part II of Volume II (\"The Turnover of Capital\"), which is the analytical heart of Marx's entire treatment of the circulation process of capital. Prior to this chapter, Marx had already established: Chapter 13: The distinction between working period (time during which labour is actually applied to the object of labour) and production time (which can exceed working time, e.g. in agriculture or in wine-aging). The turnover period is the sum of production time and circulation time. Chapter 14: Turnover time as the sum of production time and circulation time, with turnover measured as a repeated cycle. The concept of fixed vs. fluid capital (developed earlier in Volume II and Volume I), which determines which parts of the capital are subject to this turnover logic. Crucially, Chapter 15 concerns fluid (circulating) capital only , because fixed capital does not need to be re-advanced within each turnover cycle in the same way (the means of labour persist across multiple turnover periods). The chapter thus supplies the micro-foundations for Part III of Volume II on the reproduction of social capital, and it directly sets up Chapter 16, in which the same analytical apparatus is applied to the specific case of variable capital and the annual rate of surplus value. The concept of \"capital set free\" introduced here is the conceptual hinge on which much of Marx's later discussion of credit, crises, and the industrial cycle (particularly in Volume III) depends. The famous parenthetical note by Engels at the end of Section 4 signals that Marx himself was uncertain about the weight that should be given to \"setting-free\" — and Engels' correction sharpens rather than undermines the central thesis, arguing that the release of money capital happens even more generally than Marx claimed. In sum: Chapter 15 is the technical crucible in which Marx forges the concept of the turnover mechanism as a constraint on capital advance , dismantling the vulgar notion that capital works continuously and loses nothing in circulation. Its thesis is simultaneously mathematical, structural, and dialectical — and it is the necessary precondition for understanding how credit, speculation, and crisis grow out of the ordinary motions of industrial capital. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Framework: The First Example and the Two Possibilities Marx opens the chapter with an explicit statement of scope: this chapter and the next deal with \"the influence of circulation time on the valorization of capital.\" Crucially, the subject matter is the magnitude of the capital advanced , not simply the amount produced or the rate of value creation. He sets up the first example with methodological care. Take a commodity capital that is the product of a working period of nine weeks . Two elements are abstracted away at the outset: (1) the portion of the product's value added by the average wear and tear of fixed capital, and (2) the surplus-value added during production. What remains is the value of the fluid capital advanced for production, i.e., the value of wages plus raw and ancillary materials consumed. This abstraction is important: it isolates the turnover of fluid capital from the analytically separable phenomenon of fixed capital depreciation and from the surplus. The point of the abstraction is not to deny surplus-value but to render the capital recovery problem visible in its purest form. We might note in passing that this is one of the many places in Volume II where Marx temporarily sets aside the surplus only to reintroduce it later; the structure of his exposition requires this temporal separation of analytical layers. Setting the value of this commodity capital at £900 , this implies a weekly outlay of £100 (900 ÷ 9 = 100). The periodic production time coincides here with the working period — nine weeks. Marx is careful to note that this coincidence is not essential to the argument: it is immaterial whether the working period issues in a continuous stream of product or in a discrete product, \"as long as the quantum of the discrete product that is put on the market at one stroke simply takes nine weeks' labour.\" This is an important defence against a possible objection — that the analysis applies only to industries with long, discrete outputs (shipbuilding, construction, etc.) — and rules out that objection by stipulating that the labour-time content is the relevant magnitude. The circulation time is assumed to be three weeks . Therefore the total turnover period is twelve weeks (9 + 3). What happens at the close of week nine? The productive capital of £900 has been transformed into commodity capital, and it now must spend three weeks in the circulation period. The next cycle of production can begin only at the start of week thirteen (the number thirteen being counted from the start of week one, so that the interval weeks 10, 11, 12 is the circulation gap). During those three weeks, \"production is at a standstill,\" and Marx draws out the annual consequence: in every three months production halts for three weeks, i.e., 4 × 3 = 12 weeks across a year, \"or a quarter of the annual turnover period.\" Marx wants to be rigorous: he treats the three-week circulation time as general — it does not matter whether the three weeks represent the average time to sell, the distance to market, or the terms of payment. Here is an early instance of the analytical neutrality he defends throughout: circulation time may be affected by purely commercial, purely spatial, or purely credit-related factors, but as circulation time it has identical effects on the advance of capital. The Two Possible Solutions to the Circulation Gap If production is to be continuous , \"pursued on the same scale week in, week out,\" there are only two possibilities: Possibility 1: Reduce the scale. Spread £900 across the twelve-week turnover period. The result is £75 per week (900 ÷ 12 = 75). But this \"presupposes different dimensions for the fixed capital, and thus a reduced investment in general.\" Marx turns this into a sociological observation rather than a mathematical one: whether such a reduction is possible depends on whether the industry tolerates such a low level of investment. There is a normal minimum of capital investment below which a business \"will cease to be competitive,\" and this minimum \"grows steadily with the development of capitalist production.\" Above this minimum and up to a continuously expanding normal maximum , there exists a \"middle range that permits varying degrees of capital investment\" within which reductions of scale are feasible. This is a crucial move: Marx is distinguishing between the logical possibility of scaling down and the historical-institutional conditions under which it is actually permitted. He also notes practical mechanisms for varying the scale in real time: reduction of working hours (e.g., half-days), extension or intensification of hours in prosperity, employment of more workers with reserve fixed capital (reserve locomotives on railways, etc.). These are bracketed away as \"abnormal fluctuations,\" with the analysis retaining the assumption of normal conditions. Possibility 2: Advance additional capital. If the nature of the investment \"excludes a reduction in the scale of production and hence also in the fluid capital to be advanced each week,\" then continuity requires an additional fluid capital of £300 in the example. The logic: across the twelve-week turnover, £1,200 is successively advanced (12 × £100), of which £300 \"makes a quarter, as does three weeks out of twelve.\" Note the ratio Marx identifies: circulation time / turnover period = 3/12 = 1/4 = additional capital / total capital = £300 / £1,200 . This is the algebraic core of the entire chapter, and it will be confirmed and refined through the later sections. The additional capital functions during the three weeks the original capital spends as commodity capital — a period during which, for the production process, the original capital \"is the same… as if it did not exist at all.\" Two things are explicitly abstracted away here: all credit relations (so we assume the capitalist operates only with his own capital), and any accidental interruptions from the market. The abstraction from credit is important because it means that throughout this chapter the money capital advanced is not available from banks or bills of exchange; the consequences for the money market are deferred to Section 4 and 5, where Marx explicitly reintroduces them. The First Example Rendered in Detail: The Interweaving of Capitals Marx then lays out the first example (9-week working period, 3-week circulation, 12-week turnover) in a sequential narrative to make the interweaving of the two capitals visible. This is where the chapter's essential difficulty emerges — the arithmetic is not hard, but the interleaving of two capitals with offset turnover periods makes the verbal exposition dense. First turnover period (weeks 1–12). The first nine-week working period uses capital I of £900. During the last three weeks of this turnover (weeks 10–12), the additional capital II of £300 opens the second working period. Note that capital II's own \"working period\" begins at the close of the first working period of capital I , not at the start of the year — so the two capitals have different turnover years. Second turnover period (weeks 13–24). At the start of week thirteen, capital I of £900 returns. But the second working period (which began at week 10) has already completed one third of its course by this point; only six of its nine weeks remain. Therefore only £600 of capital I can re-enter the production process. £300 of the original £900 is \"set free, to play the same role that the capital of £300 played in the first working period.\" At the close of week 15 (that is, six weeks after week 10), the second working period concludes. The £900 laid out on it (i.e., the total involved in the second working period, part capital I and part capital II) flows back three weeks later, at the end of week 21 (nine weeks after the start of week 13). During weeks 19–21 (the circulation of capital I), the £300 set free enters the scene, beginning the third working period in week 19 of the year. Third turnover period (weeks 25–36). The end of week 21 brought a new reflux of £900. But the third working period (beginning week 19) already had six weeks elapsed by the start of week 25. Only three more weeks of that working period remain, so only £300 of the returning £900 enters production. The fourth working period comprises the remaining nine weeks of this turnover period, and thus the fourth turnover period and the fifth working period begin together with the thirty-seventh week of the year. This narrative is doing three things simultaneously. First, it demonstrates the interweaving of the two capitals (they cannot be treated as two mutually exclusive capitals beyond the first turnover). Second, it demonstrates the systematic setting-free of a portion of the returning capital . Third, it immediately begins to erase the artificial distinction between \"capital I\" and \"additional capital II\": from the second turnover onward, the functioning capital is composed partly of I and partly of II , so the identity of \"II\" as a distinct capital dissolves — a point Marx returns to when comparing the three examples. The Second Example (Equal Halves) and the Striking Contrast It Provides Marx now takes a second example \"to simplify the example for purposes of calculation\": working period = 5 weeks; circulation time = 5 weeks; turnover period = 10 weeks; 50 weeks/year; £100 weekly outlay. Therefore, the working period requires fluid capital of £500 ( capital I ), and the circulation time requires additional capital of a further £500 ( capital II ). The table gives: Working Period Weeks Commodities in £ Returning 1 1–5 500 end of week 10 2 6–10 500 end of week 15 3 11–15 500 end of week 20 4 16–20 500 end of week 25 5 21–25 500 end of week 30 ... etc. What is analytically rewarded by this example? Marx makes an elegant comparison: If circulation time were zero — if turnover period = working period — the number of turnovers would equal the number of working periods in the year, i.e., 50 ÷ 5 = 10, and the value of the capital turned over would be 500 × 10 = £5,000 . With a five-week circulation time , a value of £5,000 in commodities is still produced each year — total product is unchanged. But one tenth of this (£500) is always in the shape of commodity capital , and returns only after a five-week delay. At the end of the year, the product of the tenth working period (weeks 46–50) has only completed half its turnover time, since its circulation time falls into the first five weeks of the ensuing year. Total value produced is identical, but the disposition of that value — how much of it is tied up in unrecovered form at any given moment — has wholly changed. Three General Observations from the Comparison of Examples Comparing the three examples (first: 9/3; second: 5/5; third: 6/3, examined below), Marx draws three general observations. Observation 1: Only in the second example do the two capitals \"relieve one another\" without intersecting. In the second example (5/5), capital I of £500 and capital II of £500 \"successively replace one another… the two portions of capital perform their movements separately.\" This is because working and circulation periods form two equal halves of the turnover period . This is \"the highly exceptional case.\" In every other case — however unequal the two sections of the turnover may be — the movements of the two capitals intersect from the second turnover period onward. The capital active during the circulation of the commodity capital \"is no longer identical with the capital II originally advanced for this purpose, but it is equal to it in value and forms the same aliquot part of the total capital advanced.\" This is a subtle but crucial point: the identity of the set-free capital is not preserved across turnovers — only its magnitude and its functional role . Observation 2: The idle time of capital is proportionate to the ratio, not the absolute amount. The capital that has functioned during the working period lies idle during the circulation time. In the second example, it functions for five weeks and is idle for five. Over a year, capital I is idle for half the year . However, the additional capital required to maintain continuity \"is not determined by the total sum of circulation time within the year, but simply by the ratio of circulation time to turnover period.\" Hence, only £500 of additional capital is needed, not £2,500 . The intuition is that the additional capital enters the turnover just as much as the originally advanced capital, and is replaced after a number of turnovers \"just as the former was.\" It participates in the recycling. Observation 3: Longer production time changes nothing regarding additional capital for the labour process. If production time is longer than working time (as in agriculture or wine-aging), the turnover period is extended , but this extension \"does not require any additional capital for the labour process.\" The additional capital's job is solely to fill up the gaps in the labour process that are due to circulation time , and hence it protects production \"only from disturbances that arise as a result of this circulation time.\" Disturbances arising from the specific conditions of production are \"taken care of in another way, which is not under consideration here.\" Marx adds a caveat: in businesses where work is done spasmodically , to order, or seasonally, the need for additional capital is proportionately reduced, and there is a limit for the reflux; if the interval between production periods is longer than the circulation time, then the capital can lie idle unless it is used elsewhere. The Composition of the Advance: Fixed vs. Fluid, Money vs. Stock (Fourth Observation) Marx's fourth observation is dense with technical detail and prefigures the money-market discussion. A capital advanced for one working period — e.g., the £600 in the third example — is laid out partly on raw and ancillary materials (constant circulating capital in the form of productive stock ) and partly on wages (variable circulating capital). Two important qualifications: Constant circulating capital need not exist for the same length of time in the form of productive stock. Raw material may not be stored for the whole working period; coal may be procured every two weeks. To the extent that it is not yet in the form of productive stock, this part must yet remain available in the money form , to be transformed on demand. But this does not alter the value of the constant circulating capital advanced for six weeks. Wages , apart from the money kept for unforeseen expenses (\"the specific reserve fund to cope with disturbances\"), are paid at shorter intervals, \"mostly weekly.\" Hence, except where the capitalist forces the worker to make particularly long advances of his labour, \"the capital needed for wages must be present in the money form.\" When capital returns, therefore, \"one part must be kept in the form of money for payment of labour, while another part can be transformed into productive stock.\" This distinction — money form vs. productive stock form within the returning capital — is the seed for the entire \"capital set free\" analysis. The additional capital is divided up like the original capital, but with the crucial distinguishing feature that it must be advanced in sufficient quantity for the whole duration of the first working period of capital I (in which it is not involved) in order to be available for its own working period. During this time it \"can be at least partially transformed already into constant circulating capital\" — i.e., the additional capital may already begin to function as stored raw material, or it may persist as additional money capital. The extent to which it assumes one form or the other depends on the particular production conditions, local circumstances, and fluctuations in raw-material prices. What is Marx's social observation here? If we consider the total social capital, \"a more or less significant part of this additional capital exists for a prolonged time in the state of money capital.\" And as for the portion to be advanced for wages, it can only be transformed gradually into labour power, so it must remain in the money form for the whole duration of the working period, until it is slowly consumed in the labour process. Hence: The intervention of the additional capital required for the conversion of capital I's circulation time into production time \"not only increases the size of the capital advanced and the length of time for which the total capital has to be advanced, but it also specifically increases that part of the capital advanced that exists as a money reserve.\" This same phenomenon — an increase in the money-capital portion — occurs whether the needed division between working-period capital and circulation-period capital is achieved by increasing the advance or by reducing the scale of production. In the latter case, in relation to the smaller scale, the money-capital portion grows still further in proportion. This is a foundational insight: the more \"efficient\" (from the standpoint of functional capital) the organisation of production, the larger the money reserve that must lie idle relative to the functioning capital. Reversal of Perspective: The \"Empty Heads of Capitalists\" and the Role of Money Capital Marx now stages a powerful perspectival reversal. Consider the second example (5/5). From the standpoint of the £500 that functions directly and uninterruptedly in production, the circulation time \"appears to have disappeared completely.\" The turnover period coincides with the working period; the circulation time is as if zero . Ten turnovers, £5,000 product, £500 functioning capital. But if the same £500 were the only capital — if it were regularly inhibited by a five-week circulation time and could only be ready for production again after the full ten-week turnover — then across the year there would be only five ten-week turnovers , containing five five-week production periods (25 weeks total, product = £2,500) and five five-week circulation periods (25 weeks). In that case, \"for half of each turnover period this capital of £500 has not functioned as productive capital at all.\" With the replacement capital of £500 entering during the circulation periods, the turnover is raised from £2,500 to £5,000 — but now the total capital advanced is £1,000 instead of £500 . £5,000 ÷ £1,000 = 5. So \"instead of ten turnovers we have five.\" But here comes the famous critique: \"Because it is then said that the capital of £1,000 has turned over five times in the year, the memory of the circulation time vanishes from the empty heads of the capitalists, and the confused idea is formed that this capital has functioned constantly in the production process throughout the five successive turnovers.\" Marx clarifies: when we say the capital of £1,000 has turned over five times, we include the circulation time as well as the production time. To have £1,000 continuously active would require a capital of £2,000 . The \"economists,\" Marx argues, \"constantly overlook this basic aspect, i.e., the fact that only a part of the industrial capital can be actually engaged in the production process, if production is to proceed without interruption. In other words, one part can function as productive capital only on condition that another part is withdrawn from production proper in the form of commodity or money capital.\" Because this is overlooked, \"so also is the importance and role of money capital in general.\" The paragraph is one of the chapter's rhetorical peaks. It grounds a critique of classical political economy — the tendency to treat capital as if it worked continuously — in a rigorous structural argument about the division of capital. This is a crucial dialectical move: what appears (from the standpoint of the individual capital) to be a mere technical wrinkle (money capital \"waiting\") is revealed as a structural necessity of the system, on the same level as the working period itself. Section 1: Working Period = Circulation Period Marx now announces the structure of the remaining investigation: \"the difference in the turnover that arises according to whether the two sections of the turnover period — working period and circulation period — are equal, or whether the working period is longer or shorter than the circulation period; further, how this affects the tying-up of capital in the form of money capital.\" Throughout, the working assumption is: £100 advanced each week; 9-week turnover period; £900 total advanced per turnover. Case: Working period = circulation period. Marx calls this \"in reality… only a chance exception,\" but it is analytically the necessary starting-point, \"since it is here that conditions are present in their simplest and most palpable form.\" With a nine-week turnover period, the working and circulation periods are each four and a half weeks . Capital I and capital II (each £450) \"relieve one another in their movements without crossing each other's path.\" With the exception of the first period, each of the two capitals is advanced only for its own turnover period. Looking at the year (51 weeks), the table (Table I, given in the original text but not reproduced here in detail) shows: Capital I concludes six full working periods (6 × 450 = £2,700 ) Capital II concludes five full working periods (5 × 450 = £2,250 ), plus a further £150 in the final one-and-a-half weeks (mid-week 50 to end of week 51) Total product = £5,100 in 51 weeks Now Marx distinguishes two senses of turnover. From the standpoint of direct surplus-value production (which occurs only during working periods), the total capital of £900 has turned over 5⅔ times (5⅔ × 900 = £5,100). But from the standpoint of real turnover , capital I has turned over 5⅔ times (5⅔ × 450 = £2,550), and capital II has turned over 5⅙ times (5⅙ × 450 = £2,325); the real amount turned over = £4,875 . The difference arises because capital II began its turnover cycle four and a half weeks later than capital I, and at year-end only a portion of its cycle is complete. The convolution of \"turnover expressed in units of capital returned\" is unpacked at length: \"We say that £450 completes its turnover in nine weeks, and therefore £300 does so in six weeks.\" But Marx warns that this expression \"leaves aside the organic relations\" between the two components of turnover time. Its correct sense is simply that £450 advanced has made five turnovers and only completed two thirds of its sixth. This is nonetheless a legitimate formulation, because it expresses in quantitative terms the pressing reality that the capital must always be split — one part in production, one part in circulation — in the ratio of the phases. And this is the same law — \"the same law as that which determines the mass of productive capital functioning at one time by the ratio of circulation time to turnover time.\" Marx then extends the analysis from an individual business to the social capital: capital I and capital II, if they were independent , would still be two parts of the same branch of social capital. The same calculation — sum of capital turned over ÷ sum of capital advanced — holds for the branch and, by extension, for the social capital as a whole . Moreover, just as capitals I and II have different turnover years (II begins 4.5 weeks later than I), so too do the various private capitals in the same branch begin business at different points and complete their annual turnover at different times of the year. The same averaging procedure reduces them to a uniform turnover year. This move is significant: it shows that the microeconomic problem of synchronisation extends into a macroeconomic problem of coordinating many capitals, and that individual capital turnover years are constitutively desynchronised. Section 2: Working Period Longer Than Circulation Period We now assume the working period exceeds the circulation period. Marx states at once the crucial consequence: \"the working and turnover periods of capitals I and II cut across one another, instead of following on from each other. We also find capital set free, which was not the position in the case considered previously.\" Two invariants remain, however: The number of working periods of the total capital advanced equals the value of the annual product of the two parts, divided by the total capital advanced. The number of turnovers of the total capital equals the sum of the two amounts turned over, divided by the sum of the two capitals advanced. Principal example: 6-week working period; 3-week circulation; 9-week turnover; £100/week; Capital I = £600; Capital II = £300. The table (Table II) attempts to present the two capitals \"in complete independence\" — cutting through \"their actual intersections and entanglements\" — and this yields the misleading aggregate calculation: Capital I: 5⅔ × 600 = £3,400 Capital II: 5 × 300 = £1,500 Total: 5⅔ × 900 = £4,900 This is \"not correct,\" Marx immediately notes. The reason lies in the fact that capital II (only £300) cannot cover a full working period (which requires £600) . This is the key feature distinguishing this case from the previous one. Capital II \"does not have working and circulation periods separate from those of capital I.\" It can only partially cover a working period, and therefore the two capitals interpenetrate within single working periods. Marx walks through the correct dynamics: Turnover period I (weeks 1–9). - First working period: weeks 1–6. Capital I of £600 functions. - First circulation period: weeks 7–9. At end of week 9, £600 returns. Turnover period II (weeks 7–15). - Second working period: weeks 7–12. - First half (weeks 7–9): Capital II of £300 functions. At end of week 9, £600 returns in money (capital I). - Second half (weeks 10–12): £300 of capital I functions. The other £300 of capital I remains free . - Second circulation period: weeks 13–15. At end of week 15, £600 returns in money (formed half from capital I, half from capital II). Turnover period III (weeks 13–21). - Third working period: weeks 13–18. - First half (weeks 13–15): The £300 set free begins to function. At end of week 15, £600 returns. - Second half (weeks 16–18): Of the £600 returned, £300 functions, the other £300 again remains free. - Third circulation period: weeks 19–21. At its close, £600 returns; capital I and II have now \"merged indistinguishably together.\" By end of week 51 (the assumed year), we have eight full turnover periods (I: 1–9; II: 7–15; III: 13–21; IV: 19–27; V: 25–33; VI: 31–39; VII: 37–45; VIII: 43–51). But since weeks 49–51 fall in the eighth circulation period, the £300 set free must enter production during this time. Therefore: £600 has completed its circuit eight times = £4,800 turnovered The final three weeks (49–51) product counts only for one third of its turnover (since it has completed only 3 of 9 weeks): £100 Total capital turned over = £4,900 Total capital advanced = £900 Number of turnovers = 4,900/900 = 5 4/9 times , i.e., slightly less than in Case I. Testing the Generality: Additional Cases Where Working Period Exceeds Circulation Time Marx asks whether the \"setting-free\" phenomenon depends on the working period being a simple multiple of the circulation time (as in the 6/3 case where working = 2 × circulation). He tests three cases. Second case: working period 5 weeks; circulation 4 weeks; £100/week. - Capital I = £500. - Turnover period I: weeks 1–9. Working period weeks 1–5. Circulation weeks 6–9. At end of week 9, £500 returns. - Turnover period II: weeks 6–14. Working period weeks 6–10. First section weeks 6–9: capital II of £400 functions. At end of week 9, capital I returns. Second section (week 10): £100 of the returned £500 functions. The remaining £400 stays free for the following working period. - Second circulation period: weeks 11–14. At end of week 14, £500 returns. - The pattern repeats: at every working period's start there is £400 available, which suffices for the first four weeks. At the end of the fourth week, £500 returns, and only £100 is needed for the final week — £400 is set free . Third case: working period 7 weeks; circulation 2 weeks; £100/week. - Capital I = £700; capital II = £200. - Turnover period I: weeks 1–9. Working period weeks 1–7; circulation weeks 8–9. At end of week 9, £700 returns. - Turnover period II: weeks 8–16. Working period weeks 8–14. Weeks 8 and 9 met by capital II. At end of week 9, £700 returns; £500 used up by end of working period (weeks 10–14). Remaining £200 is set free . - Second circulation period: weeks 15–16. At end of week 16, £700 returns. - Again: £200 set free at the close of every working period. General conclusion: whenever working period > circulation period, \"there is always set free at the close of each working period, under all circumstances, a money capital of the same magnitude as the capital II.\" In the three examples (6/3, 5/4, 7/2), capital II was £300, £400, £200 — and the capital set free was correspondingly £300, £400, £200. The structural reason: since circulation time < working period, the returning capital I arrives before the end of the working period, and can only be partially absorbed by the remaining time. The portion it cannot absorb — equal to the capital originally required to bridge the circulation gap — reappears as free capital. Section 3: Working Period Shorter Than Circulation Period Now the reverse: the working period is shorter than the circulation period. Principal example: 3-week working period; 6-week circulation; 9-week turnover; £100/week. Capital I = £300; capital II = £600. But capital II can be subdivided into two capitals of £300, each catering to one working period. The result is three capitals of £300 each : one always occupied in production, two circulating (Table III). This is \"the exact counterpart of case I, with the simple distinction that three capitals now relieve one another instead of two.\" No capital is set free at the close of a working period — the even and complete replacement of the three capitals excludes this. Capital I is completely laid out by end of week 3, completely returns at end of week 9, and begins to function again at the start of week 10; similarly for II and III. The aggregated turnover: Capital I: £300 × 5⅔ = £1,700 Capital II: £300 × 5⅓ = £1,600 Capital III: £300 × 5 = £1,500 Total: £900 × 5⅓ = £4,800 Non-multiple case: working period 4 weeks; circulation 5 weeks. Capital I = £400; capital II = £400; capital III = £100. Here the capitals are \"intertwined\" — capital III, which is sufficient only for one week , doesn't have an independent working period; it coincides with the first working week of capital I. Therefore a capital of £100, equal to capital III, is set free at the close of the working periods of both capitals I and II. The logic: Capital III serves the first week of the second (and all subsequent) working periods of capital I. At the close of this first week, the entire capital I of £400 returns. But the working period of capital I now has only three weeks left, requiring only £300. Therefore £100 is set free — and this £100 suffices for the first week of the directly following working period of capital II. When that week closes, capital II of £400 returns, but the working period in progress can absorb only £300, so again £100 is set free. And so on. General rule stated: \"Capital is thus set free at the close of the working period whenever the circulation time is not a simple multiple of the working period; and this capital that is set free is moreover equal to the portion of capital which has to fill in for the excess of the circulation period over a working period or over a number of working periods.\" Note the symmetry with Section 2: whether the working period is greater than the circulation period or vice versa, the key criterion for setting free is the absence of a simple integer ratio between them. Fixed Capital Exclusion: A Clarifying Parenthesis Before results, Marx inserts a crucial methodological note: throughout this Part, \"we are considering only the turnovers of circulating capital, and not those of fixed capital.\" The justification is not merely technical convenience. Fixed capital is defined as capital whose use-time exceeds the turnover period of fluid capital; it functions across n turnover periods of fluid capital without being re-advanced. Whatever the length of the overall interval formed by those n turnover periods, the fixed capital remains in its old use-form and is not re-advanced within the interval. The only difference is that, depending on the working period and circulation time, it surrenders a greater or smaller part of its value to the product, and that surrendered value returns in money form faster or slower. But this does not change the analysis of the fluid capital. Marx reinforces that it is precisely the nature of fluid capital that requires the new advance at the start of each new working period: fluid capital applied in one working period \"cannot be applied in a new working period before it has completed its turnover, i.e., has been transformed into commodity capital, from the latter into money capital, and then back again into productive capital.\" Hence the need for additional capital, and hence the division of the fluid capital advanced. This methodological note is important because it connects the chapter to the law of value developed earlier: the abstract labour embodied in fixed capital is transferred slowly through depreciation, whereas fluid capital must be replaced in value each turnover — an asymmetrical requirement with deep consequences for accumulation and crisis theory. Section 4: Results (A–D) Marx now consolidates the analysis into four results. Result A. Portions of capital relieve each other like independent private capitals in two cases: (1) if the working period equals the circulation period, and the turnover period is thereby divided into two equal sections; (2) if the circulation period is longer than the working period but a simple multiple of it — i.e., 1 circulation period = n working periods, n a whole number. In both cases no part of the successively advanced capital is set free. Result B. In all other cases — (1) circulation period greater than working period but not a simple multiple; or (2) working period greater than circulation period — a part of the overall fluid capital is always periodically set free at the close of each working period. The magnitude of this capital: equal to the portion advanced for the circulation period (if working > circulation), or equal to the portion which has to stand in for the excess of circulation over a whole number of working periods (if circulation > working). Result C. For the total social capital (considered in its fluid part), the setting-free of capital is \"the rule,\" while the simple mutual replacement of portions of capital functioning successively in production \"must form the exception.\" This is because the equality of working period and circulation period, or of circulation period with a whole number of working periods, \"has nothing at all to do with the nature of the case, and can therefore occur, by and large, only exceptionally.\" A very significant portion of the social circulating capital, which is turned over several times a year, will periodically exist in the course of the annual turnover cycle in the form of capital set free . Marx then notes the growth tendency of this set-free capital: assuming all other circumstances stay the same, the magnitude of set-free capital grows with the extent of the labour process or the scale of production, and thus with the development of capitalist production in general. In case B(2), simply because the total capital advanced grows; in case B(1), both because of the growth of the total capital and because the length of circulation time grows with the development of capitalist production (while the circulation period is not a simple multiple of the working period). Example: if the weekly outlay doubles from £100 to £300, then the working-period capital becomes £1,800 and the circulation-period capital £900, and £900 is periodically set free instead of £300 — a proportional expansion of the idle mass. Result D. The total capital of £900 must be divided into two portions: £600 for the working period and £300 for the circulation period. The portion actually laid out in the labour process is thus reduced by one third, from £900 to £600 — and the scale of production correspondingly by a third. The £300, on the other hand, functions only to make the working period continuous, so that £100 can be laid out in the labour process each week. Now Marx introduces a crucial qualitative argument — one that raises the analysis above the purely quantitative. Taken abstractly , it is all the same whether £600 operates for 6 × 8 = 48 weeks (product = £4,800), or whether the entire £900 is laid out for six weeks and then lies idle for the three-week circulation period (running 32 weeks, producing 5⅓ × 900 = £4,800 total, and lying idle 16 weeks). But this abstract equivalence does not hold under real conditions: the \"greater waste of fixed capital during the idle period of sixteen weeks, and the increased cost of labour, which has to be paid for the whole year even if only a part of this is worked\" — combined with the fact that \"a regular interruption of this kind in the production process would be incompatible with the running of modern large-scale industry\" — renders the cheaper abstract solution unworkable in practice. This leads to one of the chapter's most quoted lines: \"Continuity is itself a productive force of labour.\" This formulation deserves attention. It is not merely descriptive: continuity, being the precondition of large-scale cooperation, becomes a source of productive power analogous to cooperation, division of labour, and machinery. And continuity is precisely what requires the additional capital advance and the money-reserve. The material-empirical point that \"labour has to be paid for the whole year even if only a part is worked\" ties the analysis back to the wage contract and to the immiseration/exploitation themes of Volume I. The Composition of Capital Set Free: Money Capital as the Minimum Form Marx then analyses what form the set-free capital takes. In the 6/3 example with £100/week: - At the end of week 9, £600 returns; only £300 needed for the remainder of the working period. - At the close of the second working period, £300 is set free . Composition of the returned £600: suppose one third for wages (£200) and two thirds for raw and ancillary materials (£400). Of the £300 set free: the £400 portion returns as either money or productive stock — the capitalist may react to market conditions, deciding whether to transform the £200 surplus productive stock back into stock or to hold it as money capital. But the £200 to be laid out on wages must be kept in the money form . As Marx puts it: \"The capitalist cannot dispose of labour-power, once he has bought it, as he can the raw material in his storeroom. He has to incorporate it into the production process and pay for it at the end of the week.\" So of the £300 set free, at least the £100 wage-equivalent (one third of the £300, matching the variable-capital share) must exist as money capital. Hence: The capital set free in the form of money capital must therefore at least be equal to the variable portion of the capital , that laid out on wages; at the maximum, it can include the whole of the capital set free. In reality it constantly fluctuates between the minimum and the maximum. Marx adds a crucial forward-reaching observation: this money capital set free by the turnover mechanism itself — together with the money capital set free by the successive reflux of fixed capital and that needed for variable capital in every labour process — \"must play a significant role, as soon as the credit system has developed, and must also form one of the foundations for this.\" This is the pivot toward Volume III's treatment of credit. The Effect of a Contraction of Circulation Time on the Money Market Marx now hypothetically narrows the circulation time from three weeks to two (due to improved business conditions, shortened payment terms, etc.). Two consequences: The £600 laid out during the working period returns one week earlier than needed — set free for one week. £300 (part of the £600) is again set free mid-working-period — but for four weeks instead of three . So £600 is on the money market for one week, and £300 for four weeks instead of three. Since this pattern affects many capitalists in different branches at different times, a greater quantity of disposable money capital is brought onto the market. If persistent, either (a) production expands where possible, (b) capitalists using borrowed capital exert less demand on the money market (relieving it), or (c) the sums superfluous for turnover are definitively cast onto the money market. Concretely: contraction of the turnover period from nine to eight weeks means one ninth of the total capital advanced becomes superfluous : the six-week working period can be kept going with £800 instead of £900. A portion of commodity capital, £100, persists as money capital and no longer functions as capital advanced for the production process. The value of capital advanced declines from £900 to £800; the remaining £100 is \"precipitated out in the form of money capital\" and enters the money market as additional capital functioning there. Marx draws the profound conclusion: A surfeit of money capital can arise — not just in the ordinary sense that supply exceeds demand (a relative surplus, e.g., in a depression). Rather, a definite part of the capital advanced becomes superfluous for the overall process of social reproduction (which includes circulation) and is precipitated out in the form of money capital — a surplus arising with the scale of production and prices remaining the same , simply by a contraction in the turnover period. The mass of money in circulation, whether larger or smaller, does not have the slightest influence on this. This is a decisive clarification. Money capital abundance can arise without any expansion of the money supply, simply through the mechanical effect of turnover acceleration. It sharpens Marx's critique of quantity-theory reasoning, which conflates money-capital supply-demand dynamics with the quantity of circulating media. The Effect of a Prolongation of Circulation Time The reverse: circulation time extended from three to five weeks. The reflux of advanced capital arrives two weeks too late . The last part of the working period cannot be completed through the turnover mechanism of the originally advanced capital alone. If the situation persists, either: - production contracts (reduction in scale), or - the total capital advanced must be increased by 2/9 (= £200) to compensate for the circulation extension. This additional capital can only come from the money market. If the prolongation affects major lines of business, it may exert pressure on the money market — unless counteracted from another direction. Again: this pressure has nothing to do with changes in commodity prices or the quantity of the means of circulation. Engels' Editorial Note At the close of Section 4, Friedrich Engels appends a lengthy parenthetical note acknowledging that the editing of the chapter \"involved no small difficulties.\" Engels observes that Karl Marx, despite his \"firm grasp of algebra,\" was \"never at ease in reckoning with figures, i.e., in commercial calculations\" (though there is a thick sheaf of notebooks in which Marx worked through commercial calculation). The turnover calculations became confused and contain \"many errors and contradictions.\" Engels retains only \"the simplest and the arithmetically correct calculations,\" largely because of the \"uncertain results of this tiresome calculation business\" that \"led Marx to ascribe an undeserved significance to what in my opinion is in fact a matter of little importance\" — namely, the \"setting free\" of money capital. Engels reformulates the point: whatever the ratio between working period and circulation time, once the first turnover has occurred, at regular intervals equal to the length of the working period, there returns the capital needed for one such working period — a sum equal to capital I. Examples: - 5-week working, 4-week circulation, capital I £500 → £500 returns at end of weeks 9, 14, 19, 24, etc. - 6-week working, 3-week circulation, capital I £600 → £600 returns at end of weeks 9, 15, 21, 27, 33, etc. - 4-week working, 5-week circulation, capital I £400 → £400 returns at end of weeks 9, 13, 17, 21, 25, etc. Whether this returned capital is superfluous for the current working period (and thus \"set free\") makes no difference to production continuity: for production to proceed on the same scale, money must be present and flow back, whether or not it is \"set free.\" If production is interrupted, the setting-free comes to an end. Engels' summary judgement: \"There is in any case a release of money, i.e., a formation of latent, only potential capital, in the money form; but this happens in all circumstances, and not only under those particular conditions specified in the text; it happens, moreover, to a greater extent than that assumed in the text.\" Engels closes: in relation to circulating capital I, the industrial capitalist finds himself, at the end of each turnover, \"in precisely the same situation as when he set up his business; he has this capital completely in his hands once more, and at one go, while he can only gradually transform it again into productive capital.\" His summing-up defends Marx's core argument even as it demotes a peripheral one: the main point of the text remains the proof that \"a considerable part of industrial capital is always present in the money form, while a still more considerable part must assume this form from time to time\" — and this proof \"is reinforced, if anything, by these additional remarks of mine.\" This note is a fascinating instance of editorial and analytical collaboration. Engels is right that Marx's word-choice (\"setting free\") overweights the phenomenon; but as Engels himself shows, the central thesis of the chapter — the structural necessity of money capital within the industrial circuit — is thereby strengthened rather than weakened. Section 5: Effect of Changes in Price Marx now switches the variable under consideration. Previously: prices and scale constant; circulation time varied. Now: turnover period and scale constant; prices of elements of production or of the product vary. He distinguishes three cases. Case I: scale of production constant; prices of elements and products constant; change in circulation period (hence in turnover period). The example: circulation time reduced from three to two weeks. Consequence: 1/9 less total capital needed; total drops from £900 to £800; £100 is precipitated as money capital. Business X continues to supply the same six-weekly product of £600 value, and the year's quantity and price of product are unchanged (total product £5,100 in 51 weeks). So there is no change in the terms on which the product is placed on the market; but £100 is precipitated — an idle money capital. Marx then decomposes the £100 in detail. Suppose the fluid capital I = £600 is split: 4/5 (£480) on materials and 1/5 (£120) on wages. Capital II = £300 is similarly split: £240 on materials and £60 on wages. The wage portion must always be advanced in money form. So when £600 returns: £480 converts back to material elements of production; £120 remains in money form for six weeks of wages. Now, if £100 is precipitated out of the £300 that is periodically set free for three weeks (of which £240 is productive stock, £60 is wages), the question is: where does the money for this £100 come from? Only one fifth (£20) is money capital periodically set free. The remaining £80 was previously replaced by additional productive stock of the same value. How is this additional stock transformed into money? Answer: if the reduction in circulation time has already taken place, only £400 of the above £600 (instead of £480) is transformed back into production stock. The remaining £80 stays in money form and, together with the £20 for wages, makes the £100 of capital precipitated. Even though the £100 is derived from the sale of the £600 commodity capital and is now withdrawn from circulation, it is in exactly the form in which it was originally cast into circulation: it is now a new money capital seeking investment, a new element on the money market — while being in no sense a superfluous element of the existing social money stock, since it existed at the start of business and was cast into circulation by it. It also is not a newly accumulated hoard. Marx returns to his earlier caution: this money capital \"already existed periodically in the form of money capital set free, and in the form of superfluous productive capital, but these latent states were themselves conditions for the accomplishment of the process, as preconditions of its continuity.\" Now they are no longer needed as conditions and so form new money capital and a component of the money market. He also adds an important clarification: even if the advanced capital were borrowed , the analysis would be unchanged. Reduction from £900 to £800 means only £800 borrowed capital is needed; the £100 repaid to the lender becomes additional money capital in the lender's hands . And if the capitalist obtains materials on credit, so that he advances only wages in money, then the amount of material elements he must obtain on credit is £80 less, which is so much additional commodity capital for the credit-giver, while the capitalist himself also precipitates out £20 in money. The cotton example. The additional production stock is reduced by a third: from £240 to £160 (additional stock for two weeks instead of three). Purchases on the cotton market are repeated more frequently and in smaller quantities, but the total amount withdrawn from the market annually is the same. Marx illustrates with a vivid quantitative example: Original (renovation every 3 months): - 1 Jan: 300 bales sold, 900 left - 1 Apr: 300 sold, 600 left - 1 Jul: 300 sold, 300 left - 1 Oct: 300 sold, 0 left After reduction to 2-month intervals: - 1 Jan: 200 sold, 1,000 left - 1 Mar: 200, 800 left - 1 May: 200, 600 left - 1 Jul: 200, 400 left - 1 Sep: 200, 200 left - 1 Nov: 200, 0 left The money invested in cotton completes its return one month later (November instead of October). Symmetrically, corresponding to the £80 reduction in the surplus production stock on the manufacturer's part, there is an increased commodity stock in the hands of the cotton broker . \"The same cotton lies as much longer in the broker's warehouse as a commodity, as it exists for a shorter time as a production stock in the stores of the manufacturer.\" This is a startlingly modern insight about the interpenetration of productive and commodity stocks across the sphere of circulation. A reduction in one capitalist's inventory can increase another's, simply because the timing of turnovers shifts. Enhanced transport as a cause of the reduced circulation time. Marx considers the case where the reduction in circulation time depends not on X's market conditions but on the working period or circulation time of the capitals supplying him with elements of production. Example: coal and cotton took 3 weeks to travel from production to X's site. The minimum productive stock X had to hold pending new arrivals was 3 weeks — but during transit, goods cannot serve as means of production; they are, instead, the object of labour for the transport industry and commodity capital in circulation for the coal producer or cotton broker. Now improved transport reduces transit to 2 weeks : the production stock can be transformed from a 3-week supply to a 2-week supply. An additional capital of £80 is set free (plus £20 for wages), because the capital of £600 completes its turnover and returns one week sooner . If instead the working period of the capital supplying the raw material is reduced, the same result obtains through quicker replacement of raw material. Conversely, if circulation time and turnover period are prolonged, additional capital must be advanced — from the capitalist's own reserve (if invested elsewhere, it must be \"prised out,\" e.g., shares sold, deposits withdrawn — an indirect effect on the money market) or borrowed. Wages must always be advanced as money, so this exerts direct pressure on the money market. For raw materials, if obtained on credit, the capitalist exerts no direct influence; the creditor's placing of the bill may have indirect effects, and if the creditor uses the bill to settle his own debts, then the additional capital has neither a direct nor an indirect effect on the money market. Case II: Change in price of the materials of production; all other circumstances unchanged. Suppose of the £900 total, 4/5 (£720) is on materials and 1/5 (£180) on wages. If raw material prices fall by half : - Materials: £480 → £240 for six-week working period - Wages: £120 → unchanged at £120 - Capital I: £600 → £360 (240 + 120) - Capital II: additional capital → £180 (120 + 60) The total £900 reduces to £540 . Therefore £360 is precipitated . Marx's interpretation: \"This precipitated money capital, which is now unoccupied and is therefore seeking investment on the money market, is simply a fragment of the capital of £900 originally advanced as money capital which has now become superfluous owing to the fall in price of the elements of production into which it is periodically transformed.\" If the price fall was accidental (good harvest, over-supply), then the effect is transient. But if it was due to increased productivity in the branch supplying the raw material, then the unoccupied money capital becomes an absolute addition to the money market — an absolute addition to capital available in money form, because it has ceased to form an integral component of capital already invested. This points toward a classically Marxian insight: technological or productivity improvements in one branch release capital value in the form of money, which then seeks investment elsewhere. Case III: Change in the market price of the product itself. Two subcases. Fall in product price: A part of the capital is lost and must be replaced by a new advance of money capital. This loss may be recouped by the buyer (if the price fall was accidental and prices subsequently recover) or indirectly (if the price change derives from a value change that redistributes through other branches of production). In either case, the capitalist exerts pressure on the money market to replace the lost capital, and his business friends may supply it as new additional capital — so there is only a transfer . Rise in product price: A portion of capital not advanced is appropriated from the circulation sphere. This is not an organic part of the capital advanced in production; if production is not extended, it becomes precipitated money capital . Even assuming product prices were given before entry onto the market, the price rise might have been caused by a real value change with retroactive effect (e.g., raw materials subsequently rose in value). In that case capitalist X would profit both on his product (as commodity capital) and on his existing production stock — this profit supplies him with the additional capital he would otherwise need to carry on business after his elements of production have risen. Alternatively, if the price rise is only transitory , what one capitalist needs as extra capital is precipitated out elsewhere, \"to the extent that his product forms an element of production for other branches of business. What the one lost, the other has gained. \" The chapter closes on this note of systematic redistribution. Price changes act as a mechanism for transferring capital between branches, generating simultaneous pressures and releases in the money market, without any change in the quantity of money or in the productive capacity of the system. This is the final maturation of the chapter's thesis: the turnover mechanism, and the price movements that intersect with it, continuously generate and absorb money capital as a distinct functional form of capital — a form whose dynamics are not reducible to those of commodity production, but which are essential to it. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed Chapter 15 establishes several systemic laws that continue to shape Marx's analysis through Volume III and remain among his most fertile contributions to a political economy of circulation. First principle — the law of continuity and its cost. The continuity of production is not a natural property of capital but a produced property, requiring a specific structural organisation of capital: the advance of additional fluid capital equal to the ratio of circulation time to turnover time. Continuity is \"itself a productive force of labour,\" but it must be purchased at the price of an increased total capital advance and an enlarged money reserve. This dissolves the notion — which Marx attributes to the \"empty heads of the capitalists\" and the economists — that capital works continuously. It does not; only the division of capital allows the appearance of continuity. Second principle — the ratio law of advanced capital. The additional capital required to bridge the circulation gap is determined, not by the absolute length of circulation time across the year, but by the ratio of circulation time to turnover period. In the equal-halves example, only £500 was needed, not £2,500 — because the additional capital participates in the turnover cycle just like the original. This ratio-law is the analytical core of the chapter and the reason its later effects (money-market surpluses and squeezes, redistribution across branches) can be quantified. Third principle — the law of \"capital set free.\" Whenever the ratio between working period and circulation period fails to be a simple integer ratio (equal or whole-multiple), a portion of the returned capital is systematically set free at the close of each working period. Marx regards this as the rule for the social capital as a whole; the simple mutual relief of two strictly autonomous capitals is the exception. Engels refines this: the phenomenon of latent money capital formation occurs in all cases, and to a greater extent than Marx describes; what Marx identified as a special phenomenon is a special case of a more universal one. Fourth principle — the necessary money form. At least the variable portion of the set-free capital (the wage bill) must exist in the money form, and at most the whole set-free capital may do so. Hence, at any moment, a significant portion of industrial capital exists as money capital, not because of any choice by the capitalist but because of the mechanism of turnover itself. This is the structural foundation on which the credit system is built. Fifth principle — the immanent generation of money-capital surpluses and deficits. Reductions in circulation time (through faster sales, better transport, shorter payment terms) precipitate money capital out of the turnover process, forming new money capital on the market with scale and prices held constant. Prolongations of circulation time create a demand for additional money capital from the market. Both are independent of quantity-of-money changes and of changes in commodity prices — though they interact with price changes in ways that produce further surpluses and deficits. Price changes in elements of production (Case II) and in products (Case III) generate further money-capital movements and can transfer capital across branches. Marx's conclusion: the money market is not a passive mirror of production; it is a separate arena whose dynamics are partly generated by the turnover mechanism itself. Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications For the theory of the business cycle and crisis. The chapter supplies an essential mechanism for explaining how apparently sound capitalist enterprises can be caught short by timing alone. A prolongation of circulation time — whether from disrupted transport, credit contraction, or sudden market glut — imposes a demand for additional money capital that may not be obtainable at the moment required. Because turnover is desynchronised across branches (as Marx stresses when he notes that different capitals have different \"turnover years\"), and because different branches face different ratios of working period to circulation period, the aggregate money-market effects of these individual decisions can be substantial. The famous passage in which the \"surfeit of money capital\" arises with production and prices unchanged is a prefiguration of Volume III's treatment of the credit system and of crisis as the forcible restoration of the unity of the circuit of capital. For the theory of credit. Marx explicitly identifies \"the money capital that is set free simply by the mechanism of the turnover movement\" — together with money capital set free by fixed capital reflux and needed for variable capital in every labour process — as forming one of the foundations of the credit system. This is a strong claim: credit is not simply a technological or institutional innovation, but a necessity forced by the internal timing requirements of industrial capital. For the theory of price and value. Cases II and III show that price changes act as capital-transferring mechanisms. A productivity-induced fall in raw material prices precipitates a permanent addition to money capital, reflecting a real value change (a social reallocation of labour-time across branches). A transitory price movement produces a redistribution , one capitalist's loss becoming another's gain without any underlying productivity change. This is not a Hayekian view of prices as neutral signals; it is a view of prices as capital-reallocating forces operating through the money market. For the critique of political economy. The chapter systematically dismantles the classical assumption that capital is functionally homogeneous and continuously active. Marx's target is not only the \"empty heads of the capitalists\" but the analysts (in his period, largely Ricardian and vulgar economists) who could not see why the whole quantity of advanced capital could not simply be working all at once. The argument that only a portion can function as productive capital at any moment, and that another portion must exist as commodity or money capital, is a fundamental insight into the heterogeneity of capital in time. Philosophical-metaphysical resonance. At the deepest level, Chapter 15 establishes the productive role of time itself in the valorization process. Not just labour-time (as in Volume I's theory of value) but circulation-time enters the magnitude of the capital advance and the pace of accumulation. Time is not a mere backdrop for production; it is a force that decomposes and recomposes capital. The dialectic between value-in-process and value-in-rest, between active and latent capital, is worked out with arithmetical precision. The result is a picture of capitalist production as a restless self-interrupted process , held together only by the constant interposition of new advances and reserves. What appears as the \"turnover\" of a single capital is revealed to be the rotation of at least two capitals, each partly idle, each partly active — a rhythm the capitalist cannot escape, and which the credit system attempts to lubricate, with all the ambivalence that Marx will explore in Volume III. Final Lesson Chapter 15 teaches, in the most rigorous form Marx ever gave it, the truth that was to become a leitmotif of his mature work: the circuit of capital is a unity of production and circulation, and this unity imposes material, systematic, and immovable constraints upon the capitalist. The magnitude of capital advanced is not what the capitalist decides it should be; it is dictated by the ratio of the working period to the circulation period, and by the demand for continuity that capitalist large-scale industry cannot renounce. The money capital — that most liquid, most elusive of forms — is not marginal to production but woven into its very mechanisms. When the empty heads of capitalists and their spokesmen imagine that capital functions constantly and money is merely a convenience, they overlook the very mechanism that makes their profit possible. To reveal that mechanism, arithmetically and dialectically, is the enduring achievement of this chapter. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 14 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 16"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 16", "title": "Chapter 16", "subtitle": "The Turnover of Variable Capital", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 16/Chapter 16 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 15807, "read_time": "79 min read", "tags": ["Turnover of Capital & Cycles", "Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["Turnover of Variable Capital", "Annual Rate of Surplus-Value (S = s * n)", "Real vs Apparent Variable Capital Advances", "Velocity of Wage-Fund Turnover", "Variable Capital Multiplier Effect on Total Exploitation"], "text": "Chapter 16 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 15,767 words ⏱️ ~79 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Thesis Chapter 16 of Capital , Volume II, \"The Turnover of Variable Capital,\" is one of the most technically pivotal and philosophically consequential chapters in the entire work, and its central thesis can be stated with deceptive simplicity: there is a rigorous, quantifiable difference between the real rate of surplus-value and the annual rate of surplus-value, and the law governing that difference is S′ = s′n. Marx's stated thesis, given almost verbatim in the text, is that \"the annual rate of surplus-value is always s′n, i.e. the real rate of surplus-value produced in a turnover period by the variable capital consumed during this period, multiplied by the number of turnovers of this variable capital during the year.\" Everything else in the chapter — the numerical examples, the resolution of the paradox, the move to the social standpoint, the railway fever, and the India trade — radiates outward from this one law. The Central Problem The chapter opens with what looks like a purely arithmetical puzzle and turns out to be an ideological battlefield. Marx constructs two capitals. Capital A is £500 in variable capital, turning over ten times a year, producing an annual surplus-value of £5,000, and thus yielding an annual rate of surplus-value of 1,000 per cent. Capital B is £5,000 in variable capital, turning over once a year, producing the same annual surplus-value of £5,000, and thus yielding an annual rate of just 100 per cent. The two capitals are identical in every respect that the theory of surplus-value had, up to this point, declared relevant: the same amount of variable capital is applied each week (£100), the same degree of exploitation obtains (100 per cent), the same number of working days of the same length and the same internal division into necessary and surplus labour. Yet the annual rates differ by 900 percentage points . This is the problem. And Marx immediately signals that this is not an academic puzzle. He writes that the phenomenon \"makes it appear, moreover, as if the rate of surplus-value did not depend only on the amount of variable capital and the rate of exploitation of the labour-power set in motion by it, but also on inexplicable influences deriving from the circulation process; and in fact the phenomenon has been interpreted in this way,\" and then delivers the charge that \"since the beginning of the 1820s, this phenomenon has led to the complete destruction of the Ricardian school.\" The apparent dependence of the rate of surplus-value on circulation appeared to refute the labour theory of value, because it seemed that something other than labour — the mere speed of the circuit of capital — entered into the determination of the rate. If the rate of exploitation could be doubled or decupled by an act of circulation, then the entire architecture of the labour theory of value stood in jeopardy. This is the intellectual stakes of the chapter. Marx is not merely doing arithmetic; he is rescuing the foundational principle of his whole system. The Intellectual Objective The objective is threefold and ascending. First, to dissolve the paradox by means of a conceptual distinction. The apparent anomaly is entirely generated by the conflation of two different magnitudes: the variable capital advanced and the variable capital applied (i.e. actually functioning in the labour process). Once A and B are genuinely placed \"in exactly the same conditions\" — that is, once B's £5,000 is spent on labour-power in the same five-week interval as A's £500 — the rates converge. The annual rate is then revealed as a function not of circulation's generative power but of the ratio between what is advanced and what is effectively put to work. Second, to demonstrate that the annual rate is objective and not merely a bookkeeping artefact. Marx writes that the annual rate \"is therefore not something merely subjective, but a comparison produced by the actual movement of capital itself.\" The owner of capital A receives back his £500 with a £5,000 surplus at year's end; the owner of capital B receives back £5,000 with a £5,000 surplus; the owner of capital C (a 55-week case) has produced £5,000 surplus but has not yet had his advanced capital returned. These are real cash-flow realities, not accounting conventions. Third, to extend the analysis from the individual capital to society as a whole. Section 3, \"The Turnover of Variable Capital Considered from the Social Point of View,\" treats the turnover of variable capital not as the private rhythm of an individual firm but as a determinant of social reproduction. Here we find workers paid with their own value product, the drain on the market's material elements of production, the communist counterfactual, the pressure on the money market, the railway mania, the absorption and re-release of the reserve army of labour, and finally the international transmission of crises via the Anglo-Indian cotton trade. How the Chapter Fits into the Broader Theoretical Framework This chapter is the logical culmination of Volume II, Part Two, on the turnover of capital. In the preceding chapters, Marx treated the turnover of constant and variable circulating capital together, \"as a separate theme, without regard to the fixed capital.\" Here he goes \"one step further\" and isolates variable capital, disregarding the constant circulating capital that turns over alongside it. The chapter also recapitulates and extends certain results from Volume I: the \"General Formula for Capital\" (Chapter 4), which established that capital value is always advanced and returns enriched with surplus-value; Chapter 13, which established the law that, with a given rate of surplus-value, the mass of surplus-value is determined by the relative magnitude of the variable capital; and Chapter 23, \"Simple Reproduction,\" from which Marx quotes the passage on production as reproduction and on surplus-value acquiring \"the form of a revenue arising out of capital.\" Crucially, the chapter also stands as the direct preparation for the theory of the rate of profit in Volume III. Marx is explicit that the illusion of circulation-generated value has been interpreted \"in its more complicated and concealed form (that of the annual rate of profit).\" By demonstrating that the apparent anomaly is wholly a matter of turnover, Marx is clearing the ground for the later demonstration that profit rates are equalized in a way that has nothing to do with the exploitation of labour, and that the appearance of a profit-bearing productivity in capital itself is a fetishized form masking a social relation. Finally, the manuscript footnote inserted at the end — a note \"for future elaboration\" — signals that the social analysis points forward to the theory of over-production and realization crises, which \"belongs rather to the next Part.\" The chapter thus functions as a hinge: backwards to the theory of surplus-value, forwards to the theory of profit and the analysis of crisis. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Architecture: The £2,500 Circulating Capital and the Split Between Capital I and Capital II Marx begins with an apparently modest example meant to clarify the entire architecture of the argument. A circulating capital of £2,500 is advanced, distributed in the organic ratio of four-fifths constant to one-fifth variable: £2,000 constant capital (the material elements of production — raw materials, auxiliary materials) and £500 variable capital (wages). The turnover period is set at five weeks, subdividing into a working period of four weeks and a circulation period of one week. This subdivision is not incidental; it is the mechanical hinge of the example. Because production runs for four weeks but payment for the product only arrives after a fifth, the capitalist cannot use the same capital continuously. He must split the advance. Capital I is £2,000, the portion actually at work during the four-week working period, itself divided in the 4:1 ratio into £1,600 constant and £400 variable. Capital II is the £500 that keeps the process alive during the circulation period, divided into £400 constant and £100 variable. The arithmetic then runs as follows: each working week calls for the outlay of a capital of £500 (since capital I plus capital II, divided across the cycle, yields £500 per week). Over a year of fifty weeks, therefore, an annual product of 50 × £500 = £25,000 is produced. The £2,000 of capital I turns over 12½ times (12½ × £2,000 = £25,000), while the total circulating capital of £2,500 turns over 25,000 ÷ 2,500 = 10 times during the year. Of the £25,000 annual product, four-fifths, or £20,000, represents constant capital laid out on means of production, and one-fifth, or £5,000, represents variable capital laid out on wages. This first set of figures establishes the reference magnitudes on which the whole chapter will operate: a turnover of ten times per year for the total circulating capital, and an annual variable capital outlay of £5,000, financed by a maximum advance of £500 at any moment in time. Note that the OCR of the printed page garbles one figure — the £2,500 total is said to \"turn over\" with a truncated computation — but the arithmetic is unambiguous: 25,000/2,500 = 10. The Common Fate and the Divergence of Constant and Variable Fluid Capital in Circulation Before isolating variable capital, Marx pauses to specify what unites and what distinguishes the two components of circulating capital in the sphere of circulation, because it is precisely this preliminary clarification that licenses the later abstraction. The variable circulating capital consumed in production can serve again only insofar as the product in which its value is reproduced is sold — that is, transformed from commodity capital into money capital — so that it can be laid out again in wages. But the same is true of the constant circulating capital laid out on materials: its value too must reappear via the sale of the product. What these two parts of the circulating capital share , and what distinguishes them from fixed capital, is not that their transferred value circulates through the circulation of the product. A portion of the product's value — and therefore of the product itself as a commodity — always consists of the wear and tear of fixed capital, i.e. the value the fixed capital has transferred to the product during production. Fixed capital also circulates in the value-form of the product. The genuine difference is different: the fixed capital \"continues to function in the productive process in its old shape through a longer or shorter cycle of turnover periods of the circulating capital,\" whereas every single turnover presupposes the replacement of the entire circulating capital that enters the circulation sphere from the production sphere in the shape of commodity capital. The distinction between fixed and circulating capital is thus physico-technical, not simply a matter of values changing form. Marx then decomposes the circulation of circulating capital into its two phases. The first phase, C′–M′ (commodity capital into money capital), is common to both fluid constant and fluid variable capital: the product is sold. In the second phase they separate. The money into which the commodity is transformed is partly converted back into a production stock (circulating constant capital), with different components purchased at different times according to the different terms of purchase. A further part of the money released by the sale remains in the form of a money reserve , to be spent bit by bit in payment for labour-power. This monetary reserve is circulating variable capital. The essential point, and the warrant for treating both together in the previous chapter, is that \"the entire replacement of one or the other part derives each time from the turnover of the capital, its transformation into a product, from product into commodity, and from commodity into money.\" Constant and variable fluid capital share a rhythm — the turnover rhythm — even though they differ in the role they play in valorization. This shared rhythm is what makes it permissible to abstract from constant circulating capital when we shift attention to the peculiar problem of the variable part. The Isolation of Variable Circulating Capital and the \"Absolutely Exact\" Average Having established this warrant, Marx proceeds to isolate the variable part. His proposal — one that a careless reader will mistake for a trick of bookkeeping — is to \"treat the variable part of the circulating capital as if it alone formed the circulating capital, in other words we shall disregard here the constant circulating capital that turns over together with the variable capital.\" The operation is then performed. The £2,500 total advanced yields an annual product of £25,000; the variable portion of that product is £5,000; and if we divide the £5,000 by the £500 advanced, we get ten, the number of turnovers. But note the crucial methodological caveat Marx immediately attaches: \"This average calculation, in which the value of the annual product is divided by the value of the capital advanced and not by the value of that part of this capital that is constantly applied in a particular working period (i.e. in this case not by 400 but by 500, not by capital I, but rather by capital I + capital II), is here, where only the production of surplus-value is at issue, absolutely exact . We shall see later on, though, that from another point of view it is inexact, just as this average calculation in general is not quite exact. It is sufficient for the practical purposes of the capitalist, but it does not adequately or precisely express all the real circumstances of the turnover.\" This is a remarkable moment of methodological self-consciousness and a vital nuance. Marx distinguishes two levels of exactness. For the specific problem at hand — the production of surplus-value and the determination of the annual rate of surplus-value — the average is exact. For the later problem of the annual rate of profit (which integrates constant capital and the price of production), the same average will prove inexact. Marx is therefore not committing himself to the general truth of the vulgar average; he is marking the boundaries of its validity. This self-limitation is part of what makes the chapter rigorous, and it also anticipates the turn to Volume III. Introducing Surplus-Value: Capital A and the Definition of the Annual Rate of Surplus-Value Only now does surplus-value enter the frame — it has been \"completely left out of account\" up to this point. The example takes on substance: assume the variable capital of £100 laid out each week produces a surplus-value of 100 per cent, i.e. £100. Then the £500 laid out in the course of a five-week turnover period produces a surplus-value of £500; \"i.e. half of the working day consists of surplus labour.\" Now the decisive step. If a variable capital of £500 produces £500, then a variable capital of £5,000 (applied over the year) produces a surplus-value of ten times £500 = £5,000. But the variable capital advanced — the maximum that must be in hand at any moment — is only £500. And now Marx supplies the central definition: \"The ratio of the total surplus-value annually produced to the value of the variable capital advanced, we call the annual rate of surplus-value . In the present case this is 5,000s/500v = 1,000 per cent.\" Analytically, the annual rate is the product of two factors: the rate of surplus-value produced in a single turnover period (100 per cent) and the number of turnovers of the variable capital in the year (ten). This is the seed of the law S′ = s′n. And the mass of surplus-value in a turnover period is determined separately: it equals the variable capital advanced during that period, multiplied by the rate of surplus-value — £500 × 100/100 = £500. If the advanced capital were £1,500, at the same rate, the mass would be £1,500. Marx now gives this configuration a name: capital A . Its features are: £500 variable capital advanced, ten turnovers per year, £5,000 annual surplus-value, annual rate of surplus-value of 1,000 per cent. This is the \"fast\" capital. Capital B and the Shock of the 900-Point Gap Against capital A Marx now poses capital B . Capital B advances £5,000 of variable capital for the entire year — fifty weeks — and therefore turns over only once . Marx adds two stipulations that make the example maximally favourable to B: the product is paid for at the end of the year on the very day it is finished, so that the money capital into which it is transformed returns the same day, and hence the circulation period is zero ; the turnover period is therefore identical with the working period, which is one year. The terms of comparison are carefully levelled. As in the previous case, £100 is in the labour process each week, hence £5,000 over fifty weeks. The rate of surplus-value is again 100 per cent: with a working day of the same length, half consists of surplus labour. Taking five weeks as the unit, the variable capital applied is £500, the rate of surplus-value is 100 per cent, and the mass of surplus-value created in five weeks is £500. \"The amount of labour-power that is exploited, and the degree of its exploitation, are exactly the same here, on the assumptions made, as in capital A.\" But the annual rates diverge catastrophically. Forty-one... no — fifty weeks of £100 surplus each produce £5,000. The mass of surplus-value annually produced is thus £5,000 in both cases . But the annual rate of surplus-value differs radically: for B it is 5,000s/5,000v = 100 per cent , whereas for A it was 1,000 per cent . Marx drives home the identity of the two cases in a single, deliberately repetitive paragraph enumerating every point of identity: same weekly expenditure of £100 variable capital; same degree of valorization; same rate of surplus-value of 100 per cent; same magnitude of variable capital; same quantity of labour-power exploited; same degree and scale of exploitation; same working days, equally divided into necessary and surplus labour; same sum of variable capital applied during the year (£5,000); same amount of labour set in motion by equal capitals; same mass of surplus-value extracted (£5,000). \"Yet there is a difference of 900 per cent in the annual rate of surplus-value between A and B.\" The presentation of the paradox is the pivot of the chapter. Everything before has been preparation for it; everything after is its resolution. The Ideological Stakes: the Ricardian Collapse and the Concealed Form of the Annual Rate of Profit Before resolving the paradox, Marx pauses to name the stakes explicitly, and this is where the chapter ceases to be merely a technical treatise and becomes a work of intellectual history and ideological critique. The phenomenon, he says, \"makes it appear... as if the rate of surplus-value did not depend only on the amount of variable capital and the rate of exploitation of the labour-power set in motion by it, but also on inexplicable influences deriving from the circulation process.\" The key word is inexplicable . The appearance is not a benign illusion but a scandal: the rate of exploitation — the very heart of the labour theory of value — appears to be determined by a process (circulation) whose only function is to change the form of value, not its magnitude. If circulation can raise the rate of surplus-value, then circulation creates value, and the labour theory collapses. Marx then adds a historical charge: \"Since the beginning of the 1820s, this phenomenon has led to the complete destruction of the Ricardian school.\" Here the reference is to Volume II's companion text, the Theories of Surplus-Value , Part III, Chapter XX. The Ricardian school had derived profit from unpaid labour, but its members — James Mill, McCulloch, and others — attempted to read the rate of profit directly off the rate of surplus-value without intervening mediation. When confronted with the empirical fact that capitals of equal size, employing equal amounts of labour, yielded rates of profitability disparate enough that no simple proportionality between labour and profit could be maintained, the school disintegrated. Some of its members abandoned the labour theory of value; others drifted into apologetics. The incoherence of the Ricardian average — you cannot explain why a £500 capital deployed ten times should yield the same annual profit as a £5,000 capital deployed once — was fatal. And Marx adds a crucial observation about the form in which the illusion has propagated: it has been interpreted, \"if not in this pure form, then at least in its more complicated and concealed form (that of the annual rate of profit ).\" The annual rate of surplus-value is the pure — naked — form of the paradox; the annual rate of profit is the dressed-up version, the one in which the appearance of a capital-generated profit enters practical business reasoning. This is a signal that the chapter is the launching pad for the theory of the general rate of profit. The Resolution: Placing A and B in Identical Conditions The resolution is announced with a sentence of astonishing economy: \"However, its strangeness immediately disappears if we really do place capitals A and B in exactly the same conditions, and do not just appear to do so.\" What would \"really\" placing them in identical conditions mean? The same conditions obtain \"only if the variable capital B is wholly spent on payment of labour-power in the same interval of time as capital A.\" That is: if B stops dribbling out £100 a week and instead spends its entire £5,000 in the same five-week rhythm in which A spends its £500 — which means spending it at a rate of £1,000 per week, £5,000 per five-week period, and £50,000 over the year. The arithmetic then runs to equality. Over the year, £50,000 is spent on wages. The surplus-value produced, at 100 per cent, is £50,000. The capital turned over (£50,000) divided by the capital advanced (£5,000) yields ten turnovers. The rate of surplus-value, 100 per cent, multiplied by the number of turnovers, ten, yields an annual rate of surplus-value of 1,000 per cent. A and B now have identical annual rates of 1,000 per cent. But — and here comes the compensating asymmetry — the masses of surplus-value diverge in the ratio of the capitals: for B £50,000; for A £5,000. The ratio is 5,000:500 = 10:1. And this ratio is exactly the ratio in which B set ten times as much labour-power in motion in the same time as A. This is not an anomaly but a straightforward application of the law of surplus-value: masses are proportional to the quantities of labour actually set in motion. The key principle, underscored by a footnote pointing back to Volume I, Chapter 13: \"It is only the capital actually operating in the labour process which creates surplus-value and to which all the laws given for surplus-value apply, including the law that, with a given rate of surplus-value, the mass of surplus-value is given by the relative magnitude of the variable capital.\" The Law of Surplus-Value and Labour Measured in Hours Because everything hinges on the notion of equal quantities of labour actually set in motion , Marx pauses to specify how that is measured. The labour process itself is measured by time. The length of the working day being given (as it is by assumption, in order to isolate the difference in annual rates), the working week consists of a definite number of working days. Alternatively, one may treat each working period — here the five-week period — as a single working day. If the working day is ten hours and the week six working days, the five-week period comprises 300 hours. Then one must multiply by the number of workers employed side by side in the same labour process. If ten workers work simultaneously, the weekly total is 60 × 10 = 600 labour-hours, and the five-week working period amounts to 600 × 5 = 3,000 labour-hours . This is a crucial clarification because it answers, in advance, a potential objection: equal variable capitals are not equal because they have the same price tag; they are equal because they set in motion equal amounts of labour-power — one labour-power of the given price multiplied by the given number of workers — in the same interval of time. The criterion for equality is not the monetary size of the advance but the quantity of living labour put to work. The Pivotal Distinction: Capital Advanced versus Capital Applied This is the conceptual heart of the chapter, and it is here that the paradox is finally dissolved. Marx returns to the original examples and articulates the distinction on which everything turns. In both A and B, equal variable capitals of £100 per week are applied each week over the year. In both cases, the variable capitals actually functioning in the labour process are identical. But the variable capitals advanced are quite unequal. With A, £500 is advanced every five weeks, of which £100 is applied each week: five weeks' worth of advance sustains five weeks of application, and at the end of each five-week cycle the advance returns. With B, £5,000 has to be advanced at the outset. But out of this £5,000, only £500 (one-tenth) is actually applied in the first five weeks. In the second five-week period, £4,500 remains unapplied and idle, while only £500 is applied, and so on. In each successive period, the same disproportion recurs. Marx then states the governing principle with great precision: \"The variable capital advanced for a certain period of time is transformed into applied, i.e. really functioning and effective, variable capital, only to the degree that it actually does enter those sections of the period of time in question that are filled by the labour process, and really does function in this labour process. In the intervening period in which a part of it is advanced for application only at a later date, this part is as good as non-existent for the labour process, and thus does not have any influence on the formation of either value or surplus-value.\" To illustrate: of A's £500, one-fifth is applied in the first week; four-fifths is advanced without being applied, held in reserve to be applied in the four following weeks. The applied fraction, not the advanced total, is the effective variable capital. This yields a general law about the determinants of surplus-value: \"The circumstances that differentiate the ratio between the advanced and the applied variable capital affect the production of surplus-value — at a given rate of profit — only in so far as they differentiate the amount of variable capital which can actually be applied in a definite period of time, e.g. in one week, five weeks, etc.\" And further: \"The law of surplus-value production is that, with the same rate of surplus-value, equal amounts of functioning variable capital create equal masses of surplus-value. So if equal amounts of variable capital are applied by capitals A and B for the same space of time at the same rate of surplus-value, then they must produce equal amounts of surplus-value in this time, no matter how different may be the ratio between the variable capital applied in the time in question and the variable capital advanced during the same time, and hence how different also the ratio between the mass of surplus-value produced and the total variable capital advanced, rather than that actually applied.\" The final clause is the direct refutation of the vulgar reading: the variation in the ratio between surplus-value and advanced capital does not contradict the law of surplus-value but confirms it, and is \"an inescapable consequence\" of it. The First Five Weeks of B and the Two Modes of Reckoning To make the argument concrete, Marx asks us to consider only the first five-week production period of capital B. At the end of week 5, £500 has been applied and consumed. The value produced is £1,000, and 500s/500v = 100 per cent. This is exactly the same as with capital A. The fact that A has realized its surplus-value alongside the capital advanced, whereas B has not, is \"of no importance to us here, where the issue is simply the production of surplus-value and its ratio to the variable capital advanced during its production.\" Now suppose one instead calculated the ratio of B's surplus-value to the entire £5,000 advanced: 500s/5,000v = 1/10 = 10 per cent. That is, 10 per cent for capital B against 100 per cent for capital A — ten times as much. If the objection is raised that this difference contradicts the law of surplus-value, \"the answer is simple, and given by a mere glance at the factual relations.\" For A, the fraction expresses the actual rate of surplus-value, i.e. the ratio of the surplus-value produced during five weeks by a variable capital of £500 to that variable capital of £500. For B, the mode of reckoning has nothing to do with either the production of surplus-value or the determination of its rate. The £500 surplus-value is being reckoned against a capital of £5,000, \"nine tenths of which — i.e. £4,500 — has nothing at all to do with the production of this surplus-value of £500,\" being designed to function gradually over the following forty-five weeks and not existing at all as far as the first five weeks are concerned. The methodological lesson here is that the appearance of anomaly is generated by a misreckoning — dividing a surplus-value by a denominator that includes capital that never entered the productive consumption of the surplus-value's production. The illusion is not a fact of reality but an artefact of an illegitimate comparison. Marx will later perform the mirror-image operation on capital A, showing that A can equally be made to look anomalous if the numerator is mismatched to the denominator. Turning the Problem Round: Capital A as the Anomaly Marx then compares the annual rates and finds that the ratio between them is unchanged: 10 per cent : 100 per cent = 100 per cent : 1,000 per cent. The ratio is stable. But, Marx observes, \"the problem has now been turned round the other way.\" For capital B, the annual rate of 5,000s/5,000v = 100 per cent \"does not present the slightest divergence — not even the shadow of a divergence — from the laws of surplus-value production.\" £5,000 was advanced over the year and productively consumed; £5,000 of surplus-value was produced; the rate of surplus-value is exactly the fraction 5,000s/5,000v = 100 per cent. The annual rate agrees with the actual rate. \"This time it is not capital B that presents the anomaly to be explained, as it did last time, but rather capital A.\" For A, the rate is 5,000s/500v = 1,000 per cent. But where B's £500 surplus-value (produced in five weeks) was previously illegitimately calculated against a £5,000 advance, now A's £5,000 surplus-value is being calculated against a £500 advance — only one-tenth of the variable capital actually applied in producing that £5,000 surplus. For the £5,000s is the product of a variable capital of £5,000 productively consumed over fifty weeks, not of the £500 used in a single five-week period. In the first case (B), the surplus-value of five weeks was reckoned against a capital advanced for fifty weeks, i.e. a capital ten times greater than the one actually used. In the second case (A), the surplus-value of fifty weeks is reckoned against a capital advanced for five weeks, i.e. a capital ten times smaller than the one actually used. The two errors are exact mirror images of one another. This symmetry is a philosophical payoff: it shows that the entire phenomenon of the \"dependence of surplus-value on circulation\" is nothing but the reciprocal mismatch of numerators and denominators across different temporal horizons. The rate of surplus-value has no independent circulation component. What does vary — the annual rate — is a determinate function of turnover, not an irrational intrusion of the circulation sphere. The Two Consequences of A's Five-Week Turnover Marx now extracts two consequences of the fact that capital A's £500 is advanced for no longer than five weeks, returns at the end of that period, and repeats the same process ten times in a year. First consequence: advanced capital is only a multiple of the applied capital. In A's case, the capital advanced is only five times greater than the portion of capital applied in any one week's production process — because the advance covers five weeks. In B's case, the capital advanced is fifty times greater than the portion that can ever be applied in a week. This means: \"The turnover time thus modifies the ratio between the capital advanced for the production process during the year and the capital applied for any given production period, e.g. a week.\" And this is precisely what generated the illusion in the first instance — the misleading comparison in which B's five-week surplus-value was reckoned against a fifty-week advance. Second consequence: the year comprises multiple turnover periods. A's turnover period of five weeks is one-tenth of the year; the year therefore includes ten such periods, in which the same £500 is applied afresh each time. The capital applied over the year is 500 × 10 = £5,000. The capital advanced over the year — in the sense of the maximum required at any moment, in light of the constant reflux — is 5,000 ÷ 10 = £500. Even though the £500 is \"always applied afresh, never more than the same £500 is applied every five weeks.\" In B's case, though only £500 is applied and advanced for any given five weeks, because the turnover period is fifty weeks the capital applied during the year is the same as the capital advanced not for every five weeks but for fifty. And the mass of surplus-value produced annually is governed, at a given rate of surplus-value, by the capital applied during the year, not by that advanced. Thus the annual surplus-value is no greater for the £5,000 turning over once than for the £500 turning over ten times — \"and the only reason why it is the size it is, is that the capital that turns over once in the year is itself ten times greater than that turning over ten times.\" The General Law: Annual Turned-Over Variable Capital and the Average Rate of Valorization This leads to what might be called the generalized theorem of the section. The variable capital turned over during the year — i.e. the part of the annual product, or the annual expenditure, equal to this part — is the variable capital actually applied and productively consumed during the year. It follows that: \"if the variable capital A turned over annually and the variable capital B turned over annually are the same, and they are applied under the same conditions of valorization, the rate of surplus-value must be the same for both; and since the masses of capital applied are the same, so must be the annually reckoned rate of surplus-value, as long as it is expressed as: mass of surplus-value annually produced / annual turnover of variable capital\" He then generalizes further: \"whatever may be the relative magnitudes of the variable capitals turned over, the rate of surplus-value that they produce in the course of a year is determined by the rate of surplus-value at which the respective capitals have operated in average periods (e.g. on a weekly or even daily average).\" This introduction of the average period — the weekly or daily average rate — is crucial because it provides a bridge from the idealized examples to the real world of fluctuating rates. And Marx concludes: \"This is the only possible result that follows from the laws of surplus-value production and those determining the rate of surplus-value.\" Formalizing the Ratio: Numerator, Denominator, and the Number of Turnovers Marx now undertakes a precise analytical decomposition of the ratio: annual turnover of capital / capital advanced The quotient gives the number of turnovers of the capital advanced in one year. For capital A: £5,000 annually turned over / £500 advanced = 10. For capital B: £5,000 annually turned over / £5,000 advanced = 1. The numerator, he explains, expresses the capital advanced multiplied by the number of turnovers — for A, 500 × 10; for B, 5,000 × 1. Equivalently, it is the capital multiplied by the reciprocal of the turnover time , reckoned on the basis of a year. A's turnover time is 1/10 year; the reciprocal is 10; and 500 × 10 = 5,000. B's turnover time is one year; the reciprocal is 1; and 5,000 × 1 = 5,000. The denominator expresses the capital turned over multiplied by the reciprocal of the number of turnovers — for A, 5,000 × 1/10; for B, 5,000 × 1. \"The respective quantities of labour (the sum of the paid and the unpaid labour) that are set in motion are the same here, since the capitals turned over are the same, and so are their rates of valorization.\" Marx draws two further implications from this ratio: (1) The ratio of the capital that must be advanced to the variable capital applied in a certain working period. If the number of turnovers is ten, and the year is fifty weeks, the turnover time is five weeks. The advance must be five times larger than the variable capital applied weekly. That is, only one-fifth of the advance can be applied in the course of a week. In B's case, where the number of turnovers is one, the turnover time is fifty weeks, and the ratio of advance to weekly application is 50:1. If B were in A's situation, B would need to apply £1,000 each week instead of £100. (2) The tenfold disproportion of capital needed for the same labour. B applied a capital ten times as great as A — £5,000 rather than £500 — in order to set in motion the same amount of variable capital, the same quantity of labour (paid and unpaid), and thus to produce the same mass of surplus-value in the course of the year. The real rate of surplus-value, by contrast, \"expresses nothing more than the ratio of the variable capital applied in a given period of time to the surplus-value produced in the same period; or the mass of unpaid labour that the variable capital applied during this time sets in motion. It has absolutely nothing to do with the portion of variable capital that is advanced during the time in which it is not applied.\" Deriving S′ = s′n Marx now states the general conclusion with full formal precision, and the derivation deserves to be transcribed in full. Only when the capital advanced turns over exactly once in the year — so that the capital advanced equals the capital turned over — will the annual rate of surplus-value coincide with the real rate. The derivation proceeds in two steps: (A) The annual rate of surplus-value = mass of surplus-value produced during the year / variable capital advanced But the mass of surplus-value produced during the year equals the real rate of surplus-value multiplied by the variable capital applied in its production. And the capital applied for the production of the annual mass of surplus-value equals the capital advanced multiplied by the number of its turnovers, which Marx calls n . (B) Substituting, the annual rate of surplus-value becomes: (real rate of surplus-value × variable capital advanced × n) / variable capital advanced The v cancels, giving: S′ = s′n Verification in the case of B: (100% × 5,000 × 1) / 5,000 = 100 per cent. \"Only if n = 1, i.e. if the variable capital advanced turns over only once in the year, and is thus equal to the capital applied or turned over in the year, is the annual rate of surplus-value equal to the real rate of surplus-value.\" Marx then consolidates the notation: let the annual rate of surplus-value be S′ , the real rate of surplus-value s′ , the variable capital advanced v , and the number of turnovers n . Then S′ = s′vn/v = s′n, and S′ = s′ only if n = 1. Three general relations follow, and they constitute the chapter's terminal theorem on this point: S′ = s′ if n = 1. S′ > s′ if n > 1 — i.e. if the capital advanced turns over more than once in the year, so that the capital turned over is greater than that advanced. S′ < s′ if n < 1 — i.e. if the capital turned over during the year is only a part of the capital advanced, and the turnover period thus lasts longer than a year. This is one of the most elegant results in all of Capital : the entire apparent dependence of the exploitation rate on circulation is captured by a single scalar multiplier, the number of turnovers, which is itself a purely temporal magnitude. The Case n < 1: The 55-Week Turnover and Capital C Having established the three cases, Marx dwells at length on the previously unconsidered case n < 1, since it has not yet been exemplified. He continues all the assumptions of the earlier example but extends the turnover period to fifty-five weeks . The labour process demands £100 in variable capital each week, hence £5,500 for the turnover period, and each week it produces 100s; s′ is thus 100 per cent, as before. The number of turnovers is 50/55 = 10/11, since the turnover time is 55 weeks = 11/10 years, and 50/55 = 10/11. Applying the formula S′ = s′n: S′ = 100% × 10/11 = 90 10/11 per cent . (Here the printed text is garbled — \"90rr per cent\" and \"H years\" in the source are OCR corruptions of the fractions 90 10/11 per cent and 1 1/10 years respectively — but the arithmetic is unambiguous.) The result is less than 100 per cent . Marx explains concretely: if the annual rate of surplus-value were 100 per cent, then 5,500v would have to produce 5,500s in a year, whereas it actually takes 1 1/10 years for this. The £5,500 variable capital produces only £5,000 of surplus-value in the course of the year, giving an annual rate of 5,000s/5,500v = 10/11 = 90 10/11 per cent. This case is important for a subtle reason. When n > 1, the annual rate inflates the real rate, and one might suspect that the annual rate is a merely \"nominal\" or \"fictitious\" magnitude, a bookkeeping exaggeration. The n < 1 case blocks that reading, because it shows the annual rate can equally deflate the real rate. The annual rate is a genuinely two-directional measure, and cannot be reduced to mere inflation. It is, as Marx will insist, an objective magnitude determined by the actual motion of capital. The Objectivity of the Annual Rate: Advance, Reflux, and the Shilling Analogy Marx now makes his case for the objectivity of the annual rate and its independence of mere subjectivism. The comparison between surplus-value produced and variable capital advanced (as distinct from variable capital turned over during the year) \"is therefore not something merely subjective, but a comparison produced by the actual movement of capital itself.\" He illustrates with the concrete fate of each capitalist: The owner of capital A receives back at year's end his variable capital of £500, together with a surplus-value of £5,000. What expresses the size of the capital he has advanced is not the quantity of capital he has applied during the year, but that which periodically flows back to him. Whether the capital exists at the end partly as production stock and partly as commodity or money capital is irrelevant, as is the ratio in which it is divided between these portions. The owner of capital B receives back £5,000, his advanced capital, together with £5,000 surplus-value. The owner of capital C (the £5,500 case) has produced £5,000 surplus-value during the year (a £5,000 outlay at 100 per cent rate), \"but his capital advanced has not yet returned to him, and so neither has the surplus-value it has produced.\" This trio of concrete end-of-year positions anchors the abstract formula in the tangible reality of the capitalist's cash flow. The \"advance\" is not a theoretical fiction; it is the money that must be laid out and that periodically returns. And the periodicity of the return is precisely the turnover period. Marx then returns to the deeper conceptual structure, recapitulating the meaning of \"advance.\" S′ = s′n expresses the fact that the rate of surplus-value on the variable capital applied during a turnover period must be multiplied by the number of turnover or reproduction periods of the variable capital advanced. And he grounds this by citing Volume I, Chapter 4 (\"The General Formula for Capital\") and Chapter 23 (\"Simple Reproduction\"), where it was established that capital value is always advanced and not genuinely spent — it returns enriched with surplus-value. \"The time that elapses between its point of departure and its point of return is the time for which it is advanced. The entire circuit which the capital value undergoes, measured by the time from its advance to its reflux, forms its turnover, and the duration of this turnover is a turnover period.\" Once a turnover period has elapsed, the same capital value can begin the same circuit afresh, \"and thus also valorize itself afresh and again produce surplus-value.\" So if the variable capital turns over ten times in the year, the mass of surplus-value produced is ten times that corresponding to one turnover period. And now Marx asks the deepest question of the section: \"The nature of the advance must now be investigated from the standpoint of capitalist society as a whole.\" The investigation proceeds: capital A, turning over ten times, is advanced ten times in the course of the year. But at the same time, all its owner ever advances during the year is the same capital value of £500, and all he ever has at his disposal is £500. Once this £500 has completed a circuit, he lets the same circuit begin anew. If the turnover needed a longer period, the £500 would be insufficient; if shorter, part of the capital would be superfluous. \"It is not ten capitals of £500 that are advanced, but one capital of £500 advanced ten times in succession at different intervals of time.\" Hence — and this is the decisive point — the annual rate of surplus-value is not calculated on a capital of £500 advanced ten times, i.e. on £5,000, but rather on a capital of £500 advanced once . The famous analogy follows: \"just as, when a shilling circulates ten times, there is still only one shilling in circulation, even though it performs the functions of ten shillings. However, no matter in whose hand it exists for the moment, it remains as always the same identical value of one shilling.\" The identity of the value across its successive turnovers is what makes it the same advanced capital, not ten capital advances. Capital A shows each time it returns — including its final return at year's end — that its owner has always operated with the same capital value of £500. All he ever receives back each time is £500. The capital he advances is therefore never more than £500. Marx now arrives at a secondary formula. Since s′ = s/v, we can substitute in s′n the equivalent of s′, giving: S′ = sn/v This formula is worth noting because it brings the mass of surplus-value s into direct relation with the variable capital advanced v , mediated not by the real rate but by the rate governing the annual rotation. But the meaning is unchanged: the £500 capital, by turning over ten times, performs the function of a capital ten times as great — of £5,000. 500 shilling pieces turning over ten times in the year perform the same function as 5,000 turning over only once. Section 2: The Individual Variable Capital — Wages Consumed, Labour-Power Annihilated and Renewed Section 2, \"The Turnover of an Individual Variable Capital,\" shifts the analytical register from the abstract formula to a fine-grained examination of what happens to the substance of variable capital as it turns over. It opens with a substantial quotation from Volume I, Chapter 23, which sets the philosophical stage: \"Whatever the social form of the production process, it has to be continuous, it must periodically repeat the same phases... When viewed, therefore, as a connected whole, and in the constant flux of its incessant renewal, every social process of production is at the same time a process of reproduction... As a periodic increment of the value of the capital, or a periodic fruit borne by capital-in-process, surplus-value acquires the form of a revenue arising out of capital.\" This quotation is doing a great deal of work. It establishes that production, viewed as a connected whole, is reproduction — a periodic, rhythmic process — and that surplus-value is accordingly a periodic, rhythmic output. Surplus-value is not a one-off event but a harvest, and this is why the annual rate can be understood as a multiplication of the rate per harvest by the number of harvests. Marx then walks carefully through the ten five-week turnover periods of capital A. In the first turnover period, £500 of variable capital is advanced; i.e. £100 is converted each week into labour-power, so that at the end of the period £500 has been spent on labour-power. Of this £500: it has ceased to be capital; it has been paid out in wages. The workers pay it out again in purchasing means of subsistence and consume them. A mass of commodities to the value of £500 is thereby annihilated (with the qualification that what the worker may save as money is also not capital). The mass of commodities is consumed unproductively as far as the worker is concerned — except insofar as he thereby maintains his labour-power in working condition, which is an indispensable instrument for the capitalist. But, secondly , this £500 is converted, for the capitalist , into labour-power of the same value (or price). He consumes the labour-power productively in the labour process. At the end of the five weeks, a value product of £1,000 exists. Half of this, £500, is the reproduced value of the variable capital spent as wages. The other half, £500, is newly produced surplus-value. A crucial ontological point now follows. The five weeks' labour-power — the very substance into which the variable capital was converted — is spent or consumed, even if productively. \"The labour active yesterday is not the same labour as is active today.\" Its value, together with the surplus-value it created , now exists as the value of a thing distinct from labour-power — the product. But because the product is transformed into money, the part of its value equal to the variable capital advanced is converted once more into labour-power and hence functions afresh as variable capital. And here an important qualification: whether the same workers are re-engaged, \"i.e. the same bearers of labour-power,\" is beside the point. New workers may be employed in the second turnover period. The identity that matters is not the identity of the human bearers but the identity of the value magnitude passing through the cycle. Marx now draws the starkest formulation of the difference between the advance and the consumption : \"In fact, therefore, in the course of the ten five-week turnover periods a capital of £5,000 is successively spent on wages, and not one of £500, these wages being spent again by the workers on means of subsistence. The capital of £5,000 advanced in this way is consumed. It no longer exists. On the other hand, it is labour-power to the value of £5,000, and not just £500, that is successively incorporated into the production process, not only reproducing its own value of £5,000, but producing in addition to this a surplus-value of £5,000.\" The substance of the variable capital is destroyed; the form of capital is continuously re-established. The £500 is not preserved as capital; it is consumed as wages and replaced by a new value. And this is what reproduction means. Section 2 Continued: Newly Produced Capital and the Divergence Between A and B in the Form of the Replacement Value Marx now sharpens the analysis by showing that in each successive turnover period a genuinely new capital is operating. The £500 advanced in the second turnover period is not the identical capital of £500 advanced in the first. The latter has been consumed, spent on wages. But it has been replaced by a new variable capital of £500, produced in the first turnover period in the commodity form and then transformed back into the money form. \"This new money capital of £500 is therefore the money form of the mass of commodities newly produced in the first turnover period.\" A decisive observation follows: the fact that an identical money sum of £500 exists once more in the hands of the capitalist — assuming we disregard the surplus-value, the same amount of money capital as he originally advanced — conceals the fact that he is operating with a newly produced capital. This is a specific instance of the fetishism of capital: the numerical identity of the money sum conceals the substantive identity-difference, the fact that this £500 is a different £500, the money-form of a different produce. Marx hastens to add, parenthetically, that this does not hold for the constant parts of the capital: for those, the value is not newly produced — \"it is only the form in which the value exists that is changed.\" In this parenthesis we see the deep distinction between constant and variable circulating capital: only the variable part is newly produced in value terms. Then he takes the third turnover period: \"Here it is evident that the variable capital of £500 advanced for the third time is not an old capital, but one newly produced, for it is the money form of the mass of commodities produced in the second turnover period and not in the first turnover period.\" The part of their value equal to the variable capital advanced was converted into new labour-power for the second turnover period and produced a new mass of commodities; this was sold, and a part of their value forms the £500 advanced in the third period. This pattern holds for all ten turnover periods: every five weeks, newly produced masses of commodities — whose value, insofar as it replaces variable capital, is also newly produced — are thrown on the market, so that ever new labour-power can be incorporated. What is attained by the tenfold turnover is therefore NOT that the £500 can be productively consumed ten times over, nor that a variable capital sufficient for five weeks can be applied for fifty. \"In fact, 10 × £500 of variable capital is applied in the fifty weeks; the capital of £500 is only ever sufficient for five weeks, and must be replaced at the end of these five weeks with a newly produced capital of £500.\" So far, capital A and capital B are identical. \"But now comes the difference.\" At the close of the first five-week section, a variable capital of £500 has been advanced and spent in both B and A. For both, its value has been converted into labour-power and replaced by a part of the value of the newly produced product equal to that of the advanced variable capital. For both, the labour-power has not only replaced the £500 with a new value of the same amount, but added a surplus-value of the same size. The difference is in the form in which the replacement value exists. In case B, the value product that replaces the variable capital advanced and adds the surplus-value \"does not exist in the form in which it can function once again as productive capital, i.e. as variable capital.\" For A, it does. For B, through the end of the year, the variable capital spent in the first five weeks — and successively every five weeks — is replaced by newly produced value and surplus-value, but not in the form in which it can function as productive capital, and in particular as variable capital . Its value has certainly been replaced and thus renewed, but the form of its value — its money form — has not been renewed. So for the second period of five weeks, and successively for every five weeks during the year, a further £500 must be on hand, just as for the first period. \"If we ignore credit, then £5,000 must be on hand at the beginning of the year, and exist as latent money capital advanced, even though it is only actually spent and converted into labour-power bit by bit in the course of the year.\" In case A, by contrast, \"since the circuit or turnover of the capital advanced has been completed, the replacement value already exists, after five weeks have elapsed, in the form in which it can set in motion new labour-power for five weeks: in its original money form.\" Marx then explores the ordinary appearance of things to guard against a misunderstanding. In both cases, new labour-power is consumed in the second five-week period, and a new capital of £500 is spent in wages. The workers' means of subsistence paid for with the first £500 have disappeared; the second £500 serves to buy new labour-power and withdraw new means of subsistence from the market. \"In short, a new capital of £500 is spent, not the old one.\" But in case A, this new capital is the money form of the newly produced replacement value for the £500 spent previously; in case B, the replacement value exists in a form in which it cannot function as variable capital. An additional capital of £500 must therefore be available in the money form — \"which is here unavoidable\" — to continue the production process for the next five weeks, and it must be advanced as such. So the same quantity of variable capital is spent in fifty weeks in B as in A; the same quantity of labour-power is paid for and used. But in B this has to be paid for with a capital advance equal to its entire value, £5,000, whereas in A it is paid for successively by the ever renewed money form of the replacement value produced every five weeks for the £500 advanced for each five weeks. In A, therefore, the money capital advanced is never greater than that needed for five weeks — never greater than the £500 advanced for the first five weeks. This £500 is sufficient for the whole year. Section 2 Continued: Latent Money Capital, Inverse Proportion, and the Immateriality of the Form of Replacement The result: with the same degree of exploitation, i.e. the same real rate of surplus-value, the annual rates in A and B stand in inverse proportion to the magnitudes of the variable money capitals advanced to set in motion the same quantity of labour-power over the year: A: 5,000s/500v = 1,000 per cent B: 5,000s/5,000v = 100 per cent And 500v : 5,000v = 1:10 = 100 per cent : 1,000 per cent. The distinction arises from the divergence in the turnover periods — \"i.e. the intervals at which the replacement value of the variable capital applied in a certain period of time can function afresh as capital, and therefore as new capital.\" With both A and B there is the same replacement value for the variable capital applied during the same period, and the same additional surplus-value. But with B, \"even though every five weeks there is a replacement value of £500, plus £500 surplus-value, this replacement value does not yet form any new capital, since it does not exist in the money form.\" In A, by contrast, the old capital value \"is not only replaced by a new one, but is re-established in its money form, and hence replaced as new capital capable of performing its function.\" Marx then formulates one of the most important and philosophically charged conclusions of the chapter: \"The earlier or later transformation of the replacement value into money, and hence into the form in which the variable capital is advanced, is evidently a circumstance quite immaterial to the production of surplus-value. The latter depends on the magnitude of the variable capital applied, and on the level of exploitation of labour. But the circumstance mentioned above does modify the size of the money capital that has to be advanced in order to set in motion a definite amount of labour-power in the course of the year, and in this way it does affect the annual rate of surplus-value.\" This is the philosophical verdict, and its two-part structure is critical: 1. The production of surplus-value is entirely a matter of the labour process — of the magnitude of applied variable capital and the degree of exploitation. 2. The annual rate is affected by the form of the replacement value — by whether the reflux arrives in time to be applied in the next period. The chapter thus preserves the priority of production over circulation — the production of surplus-value owes nothing to circulation — while admitting that the annual measure of surplus-value is determined by turnover, which is jointly determined by production (working period) and circulation (circulation period). This distinction between the production of a magnitude and the temporal measurement of its accumulated total is the analytic hinge of the entire chapter. Section 3: The Social Standpoint — Workers Paid With Their Own Value Product Section 3, \"The Turnover of Variable Capital Considered from the Social Point of View,\" ascends from the individual firm to the totality of social reproduction. The scenario is specified with unusual concreteness: a worker costs £1 per week; the working day is ten hours; both A and B employ 100 workers throughout the year (£100 per week for 100 workers, making £500 for five weeks and £5,000 for fifty weeks); each worker works 60 hours in a six-day week. 100 workers perform 6,000 hours' labour per week and therefore 300,000 hours' labour in fifty weeks. This labour-power is requisitioned by A and B and \"cannot be spent by the society on anything else.\" In this respect the matter is the same for both. Moreover, in both cases each 100 workers receive a yearly wage of £5,000 — so the 200 workers together receive £10,000 — and withdraw means of subsistence to this value from the society. In this respect, too, the matter is equivalent from the social standpoint. Since the workers are paid by the week, they also withdraw means of subsistence from society each week, and each week they cast into circulation in return their money equivalent. \" But now comes the difference. \" Marx states the first distinction : the money that the workers under capital A cast into circulation is not only — as for the workers under capital B — the money form of the value of their labour-power (in actual fact a means of payment for labour already performed). From the second turnover period onward, reckoning from the opening of the business, it is the money form of their own value product (= price of labour-power plus surplus-value) in the first turnover period, which pays for their labour during the second turnover period. With capital B the position is different. Here, too, the money is a means of payment for labour already performed, but this labour is not paid for with their own value product turned into money. \"This can only start to happen from the second year onwards, when the workers under capital B are paid with their own value product of the previous year, converted into money.\" This is a profound structural observation. Under a short-turnover regime, the workers are effectively paid out of the produce of their own recent labour — the money in the capitalist's hand is the realized value of the product they made five weeks ago, and by the time they are paid for the current week, their previous product has already been sold. Under a long-turnover regime, they are paid out of the capitalist's original advance, and it will be a year before their own product's money form returns to pay them. They are working on the basis of a fund that is not, in its current form, the result of their own immediate labour. Marx then draws out the general implications: \"The shorter the turnover period of the capital — and hence the shorter the intervals at which its reproduction period is repeated in the course of the year — the sooner is the variable part of the capital originally advanced by the capitalist in the money form transformed into the money form of the value product created by the worker as a replacement for this variable capital (this product also including surplus-value); the shorter, too, is the time for which the capitalist has to advance money from his own funds, and the smaller the total capital that he advances in relation to the given scale of production; the relatively greater, therefore, is the mass of surplus-value that the capitalist extracts in the course of the year, at a given rate of surplus-value, since he can buy the workers all the more often, and set their labour in motion, with the money form of their own value product.\" Two further economic relations follow: - \"At a given scale of production, the absolute size of the variable money capital advanced (and so of the circulating capital in general) is reduced in proportion to the brevity of the turnover period, and the annual rate of surplus-value correspondingly grows.\" - \"With a given volume of capital advanced, the scale of production grows, and hence, with a given rate of surplus-value, the absolute mass of the surplus-value produced in one turnover period also grows, and there occurs, simultaneously with this, a rise in the annual rate of surplus-value caused by the reduction in the reproduction period.\" The general result: \"according to the varying magnitudes of the turnover period, money capitals of very different scale have to be advanced, in order to set in motion the same volume of productive circulating capital and the same amount of labour, given the same level of exploitation of labour.\" Section 3 Continued: The Drain on the Market, the Communist Counterfactual, and post festum Rationality Marx now articulates the second distinction , explicitly related to the first. In both cases, the workers pay for the means of subsistence they buy with the variable capital that is transformed in their hands into means of circulation. They withdraw wheat from the market and replace it with an equivalent in money. But since the money with which the workers employed by capital B pay for their means of subsistence is not the money form of their own value product cast into the market during the year — as is the case with A's workers — it follows that although they supply the seller of their means of subsistence with money, they do not supply any commodity — either means of production or means of subsistence — which he could buy with the money provided, which is the position with A. The consequence is staggering in its systemic implications: \" labour-power, means of subsistence for this labour-power, fixed capital in the form of the means of labour applied under capital B, and production materials, are all withdrawn from the market, and an equivalent in money is cast into the market to replace them with; but no product is cast into the market during the year in question to replace the material elements of productive capital withdrawn from it. \" This is a profound observation about real versus monetary replacement. In B's case, the productive resources of society are being drawn down and, in the year in question, not replenished by any product. A monetary equivalent is put into circulation, but a monetary equivalent is not a product — it is a claim on products. The replacement of the material elements has been deferred. Marx now turns to the communist counterfactual , which crystallizes the analysis. \"If we were to consider a communist society in place of a capitalist one, then money capital would immediately be done away with, and so too the disguises that transactions acquire through it. The matter would be simply reduced to the fact that the society must reckon in advance how much labour, means of production and means of subsistence it can spend, without dislocation, on branches of industry which, like the building of railways, for instance, supply neither means of production nor means of subsistence, nor any kind of useful effect, for a long period, a year or more, though they certainly do withdraw labour, means of production and means of subsistence from the total annual product.\" This passage is one of the great anticipations of socialist planning theory in Capital . Under communism, the same real problem exists — long-period investments withdraw real resources and supply nothing in the interim — but it is visible and can be solved by ex ante planning (reckoning in advance). Under capitalism, the same problem takes a disguised form: money mediates, and it appears as a purely financial phenomenon. \"In capitalist society, on the other hand, where any kind of social rationality asserts itself only post festum , major disturbances can and must occur constantly.\" Marx glosses post festum in a footnote — literally \"after the feast,\" a favourite expression of his, in the sense of \"too late to have any effect.\" This is one of the most concentrated statements of his theory of economic dynamics: capitalist rationality is retrospective, ex post facto, embodied in price adjustments that come too late to prevent the disruption. The market coordinates only by crashing. The mechanism of the disturbance is then traced. On the one hand there is pressure on the money market , while conversely the absence of this pressure itself calls into being a mass of such undertakings, and therefore the precise circumstances that later provoke a pressure on the money market. Marx notes the reflexive, self-undermining character of the cycle: the very conditions that permit speculative investment create the conditions that will later trigger the crisis. The money market is under pressure because large-scale advances of money capital for long periods of time are always needed here — and this is \"quite apart from the fact that industrialists and merchants throw the money capital they need for the carrying on of their businesses into railway speculations, etc., and replace it with loans from the money market.\" Capital is diverted from productive use into speculation and reconstituted by credit, compounding the pressure. The other side of the coin is pressure on the society's available productive capital . Since elements of productive capital are constantly being withdrawn from the market and all that is put into the market is an equivalent in money, the effective demand rises without this in itself providing any element of supply. Hence prices rise, both for means of subsistence and for the material elements of production. Section 3 Continued: The Railway Fever, Speculation, and the Reserve Army of Labour The analysis of the trade cycle then unfolds with almost journalistic concreteness. During this period there are \"regular business swindles, and great transfers of capital. A band of speculators, contractors, engineers, lawyers, etc. enrich themselves. These exert a strong consumer demand on the market, and wages rise as well.\" As far as foodstuffs are concerned, agriculture is given a boost. But since foodstuffs cannot be suddenly increased within the year, imports grow, as well as the import of exotic foods (coffee, sugar, wine, etc.) and objects of luxury. \"Hence over-supply and speculation in this part of the import trade.\" On the other hand, in those branches of industry in which production can be increased more quickly — \"manufacture proper, mining, etc.\" — the price rise leads to sudden expansion, soon followed by collapse. The same effect occurs on the labour market, drawing great numbers of the latent relative surplus population , and even workers already employed, into the new lines of business. Then Marx gives a memorable illustration of the specificity of labour demand. \"Undertakings of this kind, such as railways, generally withdraw from the labour market on a large scale a certain quantity of force, which can derive only from branches such as agriculture, etc., where only strong lads are needed.\" This is a concrete, physiologically-grounded observation about the kind of labour-power that large infrastructural projects require. This still occurs even after the new undertakings have already become an established branch of industry and the migrant working class needed for them has already been formed — e.g. when railway construction is temporarily pursued on a scale greater than the average. A part of the reserve army of workers whose pressure keeps wages down is absorbed. Wages generally rise, even in the formerly well-employed sections of the labour market. \"This lasts until, with the inevitable crash, the reserve army of workers is again released and wages are pressed down once more to their minimum and below it.\" Marx then inserts (in a manuscript footnote, \"for future elaboration\") one of his most celebrated statements of the contradictions of the capitalist mode of production: \"Contradiction in the capitalist mode of production. The workers are important for the market as buyers of commodities. But as sellers of their commodity — labour-power — capitalist society has the tendency to restrict them to their minimum price. Further contradiction: the periods in which capitalist production exerts all its forces regularly show themselves to be periods of over-production; because the limit to the application of the productive powers is not simply the production of value, but also its realization. However the sale of commodities, the realization of commodity capital, and thus of surplus-value as well, is restricted not by the consumer needs of society in general, but by the consumer needs of a society in which the great majority are always poor and must always remain poor. This however belongs rather to the next Part.\" This footnote is a perfect encapsulation of the tensions the section has developed. It identifies two \"contradictions\": 1. The buyer/seller contradiction : workers must buy, but their wages are pressed to a minimum; consumption capacity is limited by the wage relation. 2. The realization contradiction : expansions of production capacity, by their very energy, produce over-production — not over-production relative to \"consumer needs of society in general,\" but over-production relative to a capitalistically distributed social need, in which the great majority are \"always poor and must always remain poor.\" The chapter thus closes its social section by gesturing beyond itself, to the theory of crises \"belonging to the next Part.\" The Determinants of the Length of the Turnover Period The concluding pages of the chapter provide a systematic taxonomy of the causes of turnover-length variation, which in turn determine the magnitude of the divergence between S′ and s′. Marx identifies three sources, each with its own logic. (1) The working period in the strict sense — \"the period needed to prepare the product for the market\" — depends on \"the material conditions of production in the various spheres of capital investment, as these are given at the time.\" Crucially, Marx differentiates by branch: \"In agriculture these have more the character of natural conditions of production; in manufacture, and for the most part in the extractive industries too, they change with the social development of the productive process itself.\" There is thus a historical element: the working period is more malleable, more subject to technical transformation, in industry than in agriculture. This grounds the turnover in a material-technical base while admitting historicity. (2) The size of deliveries — the quantitative scale on which the product is thrown onto the market — introduces a conventional element: \"this has a conventional character.\" Marx immediately guards against the conclusion that the turnover length is therefore arbitrary: \"But the convention itself has as its material basis the scale of production, and is therefore accidental only if considered in isolation.\" Conventions then are not free-floating; they are grounded in material scale, and appear arbitrary only under the abstracting gaze. This is a methodological caveat that pre-empts the objection that the entire analysis rests on conventional magnitudes. (3) The length of the circulation period — which is partly conditioned by the constant change in market conditions, the greater or lesser ease of selling, and the necessity of casting the product partly on nearer and partly on more distant markets. \"Apart from the scale of demand in general, the movement of prices plays a major role here. Sales are deliberately restricted when prices are falling, while production goes ahead; and the converse occurs when prices are rising, when production and sale keep in step, or selling even takes place in advance.\" Note here a striking observation about speculative timing : production and sale need not move in step, and the deliberate desynchronization of sale and production is a normal business strategy under price uncertainty. And \"the actual distance of the place of production from the market outlet should be considered as a specific material basis.\" The India Trade: Credit, Distance, and the International Transmission of Crisis Marx then illustrates the third determinant with the extended example of the Anglo-Indian cotton trade — a case study of how a lengthy circulation period, mediated by credit, generates financial pressure and international crisis transmission. This is one of the most developed concrete analyses in all of Volume II. The mechanism is set out in stages. English cotton cloth or yarn is sold to India. The export merchant pays the English cotton manufacturer. But — a parenthetical of importance — \"He does this willingly only when the situation on the money market is favourable. As soon as the manufacturer himself replaces his money capital by credit operations, things start to go wrong.\" The exporter then sells the cotton goods on the Indian market, from where the capital he advanced is remitted. \"Until this reflux, the situation is just the same as one in which the length of the working period requires a new advance of money capital in order to keep the production process going on the same scale.\" Two crucial consequences follow: (a) The manufacturer's wage fund is not the money form of his own product. \"The money capital with which the manufacturer pays his workers and replaces the other elements of his circulating capital is not the money form of the yarn that he produced. This can only be the case after the value of this yarn has returned to England in money or products. It is additional money capital, as before.\" The distinction, however, is that \"instead of the manufacturer it is the merchant who advances it, and he may well have obtained it himself by credit operations.\" Marx thus identifies an institutional locus of advance — the merchant, and behind the merchant, the credit system — that substitutes for the manufacturer in performing the function of carrying the long circulation period. (b) The drain on the English market. \"Until this money has been cast into the market, no additional product has been put on the English market that could be bought with this money and enter the sphere of production or individual consumption. If this condition sets in for a long while and on a large scale, then it must lead to the same results as the prolonged working period did previously.\" The analysis directly mirrors the earlier analysis of long-period investment: exported goods have withdrawn real product from the domestic market, and no replacement product has appeared. Marx then shows how the phenomenon acquires additional complexity through the credit chain. The yarn may be sold on credit in India itself; with this credit, products are bought in India and sent as a return shipment to England, or else drafts are remitted to this amount. \"If this process is delayed, then pressure builds up on the Indian money market, which may react on England to produce a crisis here. This crisis, in its turn, even if it is combined with the export of precious metals to India, provokes a new crisis in that country, on account of the bankruptcy of English firms and their Indian branches, who were given credit by Indian banks. Thus a simultaneous crisis arises both on the market for which the trade balance is unfavourable, and on that for which it is favourable.\" The last clause is remarkable: crises can strike both partners simultaneously, regardless of the direction of the trade balance. \"This phenomenon can be still more complicated. England may have sent silver bullion to India, but India's English creditors now press their demands here, and in a short while India will have to send its silver back to England.\" The bullion flows reverse — the metal goes to India, then must return to satisfy English demands — illustrating the turbulence of the credit-driven adjustment. Marx then describes the deceptive balance of trade. It can happen that the export trade to India and the import trade from India are in approximate balance, \"even though the size of the latter (with the exception of special circumstances such as an increase in cotton prices, etc.) is determined by the former, and stimulated by it.\" The trade balance between England and India may appear in equilibrium, or exhibit only weak fluctuations. \"But once the crisis breaks out in England, it becomes clear that unsold cotton goods are being stored up in India (goods which have therefore not been transformed from commodity capital into money capital — over-production on this side) and that on the other hand there are not only unsold stocks of Indian products in England, but a major part of stocks sold and consumed have not yet been paid for.\" The conclusion is the theoretical payoff of the section: \"Thus what appears as a crisis on the money market in actual fact expresses anomalies in the production and reproduction process itself.\" This is a direct application of the framework developed throughout the chapter: the money market is the phenomenal form; the production and reproduction process is the essence. The crisis is not a purely financial event, but a financial expression of the underlying disequilibrium in production and reproduction. The merchant credit that masks the long circulation period eventually exposes, in the form of a financial crisis, the fact that products have been produced and shipped but not realized — that commodity capital has not been transformed into money capital. The Final Point: Self-Supply Out of Its Own Product The chapter's final paragraph presents one more precise consequence of turnover-length: \"Thirdly, in relation to the actual circulating capital applied (both variable and constant), the length of the turnover period, in so far as it derives from the length of the working period, leads to the distinction that, with a greater number of turnovers in the course of the year, an element of the variable or constant circulating capital can be supplied by way of its own product, as with the production of coal, of ready-made clothes, etc. In other situations this is not the case, at least not within the year.\" The point is elegant and often overlooked. In branches whose own outputs can serve as inputs to their own reproduction — coal mining (whose product can be burned to power further mining) and ready-made clothing manufacture (whose output can clothe the workforce directly) — a rapid turnover allows the branch to supply part of its own circulating capital out of its own product. Short turnover periods therefore have a material advantage independent of the money-form question: the branch can become partially self-reproducing in physical inputs, reducing the drain on the rest of the economy. This marks an important boundary condition of the whole analysis and shows that the concrete advantages of rapid turnover are not merely financial. This final observation also connects the whole chapter back to the materialist foundations of Marx's method. Circulation, credit, and money are all real; the annual rate of surplus-value is a real magnitude; the money market pressure is real. But the fundamental determinants are always material and social: the physical conditions of production, the length of the working period, the quantity of living labour set in motion, the social distribution of consumption and purchasing power. That is the ultimate lesson of the chapter. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles Revealed by the Chapter The first and central principle is the law of the annual rate of surplus-value, S′ = s′n , and the three-case theorem that follows: S′ = s′ when n = 1, S′ > s′ when n > 1, and S′ < s′ when n < 1. This law dissolves the entire apparent dependence of the rate of surplus-value on the circulation process. It demonstrates that the rate of surplus-value can appear to diverge wildly between capitals employing equal amounts of labour only because of a mismatch between the numerator (surplus-value produced in a period) and the denominator (variable capital advanced over a different period). The divergence is an artefact of the ratio between advanced and applied variable capital — which is itself a function of turnover — and not a real generative power of circulation. The second fundamental principle is the ontological priority of production over circulation . Surplus-value is created only in the labour process, only by living labour, and only to the extent that the variable capital is actually functioning there. Capital advanced but not applied — B's £4,500 \"as good as non-existent for the labour process\" — contributes nothing to value or surplus-value. This is not merely a quantitative law but an ontological one: it establishes that the productive power of capital is the productive power of the labour it sets in motion. Everything else — the money form, the reflux, the credit system, the annual measurement — is derivative. The third principle is the objectivity of the annual rate . The comparison between surplus-value produced and capital advanced is \"not something merely subjective, but a comparison produced by the actual movement of capital itself.\" This claim is philosophically important because it resists both horns of a false dilemma. The annual rate is not the \"real rate\" (which would collapse the distinction), but neither is it an arbitrary accounting construct. It is a real, determinate magnitude indexed to the actual motion of capital — to the advance and the reflux. Marx anchors this objectivity in the concrete positions of capitalists A, B, and C at the end of the year: each has composed a determinate ratio of returned capital to surplus-value realized. The formula is a description of these positions, not an imposition upon them. The fourth principle is the materiality of turnover determinants . The length of the turnover period — which governs the magnitude of S′ relative to s′ — is determined by material conditions of production (working-period length), by the conventionally-mediated quantitative scale of deliveries, and by the market- and distance-conditions that govern the circulation period. Marx's taxonomy shows that turnover is not a contingent factor but a material-technical-social variable, and its analysis is therefore susceptible to determinate historical specification. Broader Theoretical Implications The most direct theoretical implication is that the chapter prepares the theory of the rate of profit for Volume III. The annual rate of surplus-value is the \"pure form\" of the paradox; the annual rate of profit is its \"more complicated and concealed form.\" By showing that the annual rate of surplus-value is determinable by the law S′ = s′n, Marx eliminates one source of apparent anomaly in the profit rate and shows that whatever remains cannot be attributed to the circulation process. When profit rates are equalized across branches with different organic compositions and different turnover periods, the equalization cannot be attributed to any labour-magnitude — it must be attributed to the competitive formation of prices of production. This chapter clears away the red herring of turnover from the theory of exploitation. The second theoretical implication is that the chapter's analysis of the social standpoint prepares the analysis of realization crises . The distinction between A and B is not merely an accounting distinction; it has real consequences for the material supply of the market. In B's case, productive resources are withdrawn and not replaced within the year — a structural condition that, when generalized across many branches, generates the kind of disequilibrium that expresses itself as money-market pressure. The railway speculations, the pressure on the money market, the drain on productive capital, the rise of prices, the speculative overexpansion of some branches and the collapse of others, the absorption and re-release of the reserve army of labour — these form a coherent cyclical mechanism, and the chapter presents it in a compact, almost model-like form. This anticipates the fuller treatment of crisis in the following part and in Volume III. The third theoretical implication is the critique of fetishism in the form of the reflux . The fact that the same monetary sum returns to the capitalist at the end of each turnover conceals the fact that the substance of the variable capital has been consumed and replaced. The £500 that comes back is not the £500 that went out; it is the money form of a newly produced commodity. The chapter's insistence on this point is a specific application of the theory of commodity fetishism: the form of value obscures the process of value creation. Similarly, the entire mediated structure of merchant credit, drafts, and Indian trade bills obscures the real condition — that products are produced, shipped, and not realized — behind the appearances of the money market. Broader Practical and Philosophical Implications For revolutionary politics and the working class , the most striking implication is the passage on workers being paid with their own value product (in A's case) versus out of the capitalist's advance (in B's case). Under conditions of rapid turnover, the working class is, in a functional sense, already being paid with the money of their own recent labour. This is a concrete material anticipation of the associationist and socialist proposals of Marx's time: the workers' product is their own, and under a rational social order it could be distributed to them directly. In the communist counterfactual, \"money capital would immediately be done away with, and so too the disguises that transactions acquire through it\" — this is a claim that under a just social order, the real problem of long-term investment would become visible and tractable, and the \"disguises\" of money would no longer produce an appearance of a purely financial problem. For the theory of the trade cycle , the chapter provides a coherent and sophisticated model. The cycle is not driven by arbitrary psychological factors or by government policy alone; it is driven by the inherent temporal structures of capital — specifically, by the mismatch between the long working periods and circulation periods of certain branches (railways, agriculture) and the shorter turnover periods of others. The credit system postpones but does not solve the mismatch; when the postponement reaches its limits, the crisis erupts. The reserve army of labour acts as the regulating mechanism: absorbed during the boom, released during the crash, driving wages down to and below their customary minimum. The crisis is \"post festum rationality\" in action — the moment at which the market finally coordinates, too late. For the critique of political economy , the chapter's most important lesson is method. When an appearance contradicts a law, the task is not to reconcile the law with the appearance by diluting the law, but to discover the deeper logic of the appearance through sharper conceptual distinctions. Marx's distinction between capital advanced and capital applied is a model of this procedure: it takes an apparent refutation of the labour theory of value (the 900-point gap between A's and B's annual rates) and shows that it is a rigorous consequence of the theory, once the distinction is made. This is one of the great methodological achievements of Capital : a dialectical analysis that preserves the fundamental law intact by clarifying the conditions of its application. Finally, for the theory of the planned economy — a question never far from Marx's mind even where he does not fully elaborate it — the chapter offers one of the most striking prefigurations. The communist counterfactual states the core insight clearly: under a planned order, the task is to reckon in advance how much labour, means of production, and means of subsistence can be devoted to long-gestation projects (railways) without dislocation. The identical real problem exists in both capitalist and communist societies — long investment periods will always withdraw resources from current production — but under capitalism the problem is solved only through periodic crises. This is an extraordinary anticipation of the socialist planning debates of the twentieth century, and it grounds them not in an ethical argument but in a structural analysis of turnover time. The Chapter as a Whole In sum, Chapter 16 accomplishes what few chapters in Capital accomplish with such economy: it simultaneously (1) dissolves an apparent empirical anomaly that had discredited the Ricardian school, (2) establishes a formal law of the annual rate of surplus-value S′ = s′n, (3) articulates the crucial distinction between capital advanced and capital applied, (4) develops a fine-grained theory of the consumption and reproduction of variable capital, (5) ascends to the social standpoint to analyze the temporal dimensions of reproduction as a whole, (6) sketches a theory of long-period investment and its cyclical consequences, (7) provides an extended case study of international trade, credit, and crisis transmission, and (8) prepares the way for the theory of the rate of profit and the analysis of realization crises in subsequent parts. Its most potent lesson remains its greatest paradox: the appearance that circulation generates value is a superstructural illusion resting on the real, material, temporal rhythm of production — a rhythm whose analysis requires neither abandoning the labour theory of value nor dissolving its conceptual distinctions into blurred averages, but rather sharpening those distinctions until the apparent anomaly reveals itself as an inessential consequence of the law it seemed to refute. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 15 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 17"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 17", "title": "Chapter 17", "subtitle": "The Circulation of Surplus-Value", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 17/Chapter 17 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 16645, "read_time": "83 min read", "tags": ["Money, Currency & Circulation", "Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["The Circulation of Surplus-Value", "Where Does the Money to Realize Surplus-Value Come From?", "Gold Producer Injection of Money", "Money Hoards & Realization without Inflation", "Simultaneous Consumption and Accumulation"], "text": "Chapter 17 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 16,584 words ⏱️ ~83 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem and Object of Chapter 17 Chapter 17 of Capital , Volume II — \"The Circulation of Surplus-Value\" — is, at first glance, a technical appendix to the preceding chapter on the turnover of variable capital. In reality it is one of the most philosophically pregnant and theoretically innovative chapters in the entire three volumes, because it pivots the analysis of capital away from the production of surplus-value and toward the monetary machinery by which surplus-value is realized, spent, hoarded, and re-capitalized. Having established in Chapter 16 that a variation in the turnover period produces a variation in the annual rate of surplus-value even when the mass of surplus-value produced annually is unchanged, Marx opens Chapter 17 by announcing that there is a further necessary variation — this time in the capitalization of surplus-value (in accumulation) and consequently in the mass of surplus-value produced during the year even when the rate of surplus-value remains the same. The chapter therefore has a double object: first, to demonstrate how the turnover period also modifies accumulation behaviour, and second, to resolve the far more difficult question of money — where the money comes from that converts surplus-value into money. The chapter is thus situated at a strategic intersection. On one side, it completes the \"turnover\" half of Volume II (Parts I–II and the first three chapters of Part III). On the other, it prepares the ground for the reproduction schemes of Chapters 18–20, since the question \"where does the money come from to realize surplus-value?\" is the monetary counterpart of the question \"how do the departments of social production balance?\" It also arguably prepares for Volume III's theory of credit, since the chapter's climactic section deals with latent money capital, bank deposits, government papers, and shares. The Twin Theses of the Chapter Thesis one: the general problem of the money needed to realize surplus-value is a false special problem. The capitalist class appears to be the unique starting point of money circulation, so it seems there must be a special capitalist money that converts surplus-value into money. Marx dissolves this appearance. Building on the laws of simple commodity circulation developed in Volume I, Chapter 3, he demonstrates that the quantity of money required to circulate a mass of commodities is independent of whether that value contains surplus-value, of whether production is capitalist, and of how the value is distributed between wages and profit. The general answer to the question \"where does the money come from?\" is that the capitalist class itself casts the money into circulation that realizes its own surplus-value — but not as capital, only as means of purchase for individual consumption (i.e. as revenue). The one genuine exception — the exception that makes the whole system physically possible — is the special case of gold and silver production, where surplus-value emerges already in the money form and is cast into circulation from production itself, not from exchange. Thesis two: accumulation is not a simple, unilinear process of converting surplus-value into productive capital; it also requires the formation of latent money capital (hoards), and this hoarding is inherently partial, temporary, and institutionally mediated by credit. There is no general, all-at-once hoarding by the capitalist class as a whole; hoarding at one pole is always mirrored by real expansion of production or real consumption at another pole. The latent money capital of the individual capitalist is not stored coin at all, but bank deposits, government securities, and shares — i.e. legal claims on the social product. And here Marx draws on William Thompson's 1824 treatise to make a startling claim: the \"accumulated wealth\" that strikes the eye is, in its greater part, nominal — mere demands on the future annual productive powers of society. The Intellectual Objectives The chapter pursues four interlocking objectives: To extend the theory of turnover into the theory of accumulation by showing that the turnover period determines not just the annual rate of surplus-value but the manner and rhythm in which surplus-value can be capitalized — including the crucial distinction between a repair-and-maintenance fund created out of realized surplus-value (case A) and a repair-and-maintenance fund that must be originally advanced (case B). To resolve the monetary paradox of capitalist circulation posed by an opponent of Thomas Tooke (the \"where does the money come from?\" question), and to do so by using, but deeply transforming, the simple circulation laws of Volume I. To demolish the \"wages-cause-prices\" fallacy , the capitalist apologetic that any wage increase must be passed on in higher prices. Marx identifies three real facts that are used as pretexts for this fallacy and shows that none of them actually licenses the inference. To theorize latent money capital and the credit system's material basis , thus laying groundwork for the later analysis of interest-bearing capital in Volume III. Contextual Placement Within the architecture of Volume II, Chapter 17 is the monetary keystone. Parts I and II analyzed the circuit and the turnover of individual capital; Chapter 16 integrated turnover with the annual rate of surplus-value. Chapter 17 now asks: what is the social and monetary meaning of the surplus-value whose production and timing we have already established? The answer requires the analysis of the total social money stock, hoard formation, gold production, and the credit system. Within Marx's broader project, the chapter resolves a contradiction left unresolved in Volume I — namely, how the capitalist \"always withdraws more money from circulation than he casts into it\" (the M–C–M' paradox). Marx's answer is that this paradox cannot be resolved by looking at the formation of surplus-value (which is presupposed) but only by looking at the circulation of money, where the total commodity value, surplus-value included, is circulated by a quantity of money that the capitalist class originally injected — partly in the form of a consumption fund that returns as the money form of surplus-value. The chapter is also a sustained polemic. It engages Thomas Tooke, the Physiocrats (Quesnay and Turgot), Adam Smith, William Thompson, and \"bourgeois economics\" and \"economic sycophants\" in general. Each engagement serves to mark off Marx's position from both the currency school and the apologetic defenders of profit. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS Beat 1 — Opening: From Turnover to Capitalization (the A/B Contrast and the Maintenance Fund) Marx opens by recalling the central result of Chapter 16: a variation in the turnover period produces a variation in the annual rate of surplus-value even with the same annual mass of surplus-value . He then announces the chapter's new movement: there is a further necessary variation , this time in the capitalization of surplus-value — in accumulation — and thus also in the mass of surplus-value produced during the year even if the rate of surplus-value remains the same . This sets the conceptual agenda: turnover affects not only how much surplus-value is realized per year but also how much can be turned back into capital. The chapter's true subject is therefore the rhythm of accumulation as mediated by the rhythm of monetary realization . The opening contrast is between two capitalists, A and B (carried over from the preceding chapter's example). Capitalist A has a steady periodic revenue and, except for the initial turnover period, meets his own consumption out of his own surplus-value production; he does not need to advance anything from his original funds for this. Capitalist B produces the same amount of surplus-value in the same time as A, but the surplus-value is not realized in B's case and therefore can be consumed neither individually nor productively. So far as individual consumption is concerned, B must anticipate surplus-value; funds for this must be advanced . Here Marx introduces the key terminological distinction that will structure the entire chapter: the distinction between realized surplus-value (surplus-value that has returned in the money form and can therefore function as revenue or as latent capital) and produced but unrealized surplus-value (surplus-value that exists only in the commodity form and is therefore unavailable for either consumption or accumulation). Turnover, in other words, is not just a statistic of timing; it determines whether a capitalist has money at his disposal now or must borrow, anticipate, or wait. From this abstract contrast, Marx extracts an important but easily overlooked phenomenon: a part of the productive capital that cannot be easily categorized — the extra capital needed for the repair and maintenance of fixed capital — now presents itself in a new light. The footnote refers back to Volume II, p. 255, where this topic was developed. In case A, this portion of capital is either not advanced at the start of production or is advanced only to a small extent; it does not need to be available , let alone actually present . It arises from the business itself, by the direct transformation of surplus-value into capital , i.e. by its direct application as capital. In other words, a portion of the surplus-value that is not only produced periodically in the course of the year, but also realized , can cover the expenses necessary for repairs. Therefore a part of the capital needed to conduct the business on its original scale is produced by the business itself , in the course of business, through the capitalization of a part of the surplus-value. This is impossible for capitalist B. In B's case, the portion of capital in question must form part of the capital originally advanced . The deeper point here is that in both cases the amount appears on the capitalist's books as \"capital advanced,\" and indeed it is productive capital necessary for the business at the given scale. But it makes a great difference whose funds it is advanced out of . In case B, it is an actual part of the capital originally advanced or kept available. In case A, it is a portion of surplus-value applied as capital . Consequently, Marx derives a striking result: \"not only the capital accumulated, but also a part of the capital originally advanced, can be simply capitalized surplus-value.\" This is a significant theoretical claim — it means the line between \"original capital\" and \"accumulated capital\" is porous in the case of a capitalist whose turnover is rapid enough to generate repair funds out of current surplus-value. The appearance that the capitalist began with a fully formed capital stock is partly illusory; the business manufactures part of its own capital out of its own surplus. Beat 2 — Credit Complicates the Relation: A, Banker C, and Capitalists D, E, F Marx immediately deepens the analysis by introducing the credit system. Once credit develops, the relation between capital originally advanced and capitalized surplus-value becomes still more intricate. In his example, capitalist A may borrow part of the productive capital with which he begins or carries on business from banker C . At the start, therefore, A lacks sufficient capital of his own to conduct the business. Banker C lends him a sum that simply consists of surplus-value deposited with him by industrialists D, E, F, etc. From A's standpoint, this sum is still not accumulated capital. But in point of fact, for D, E, F, etc., A is no more than an agent who capitalizes the surplus-value that they have appropriated. This is a crucial conceptual move. It shows that credit is not an exogenous force that adds to capital; it is a mechanism by which one capitalist's hoarded surplus-value is transferred to another capitalist for productive use . The capitalist who borrows appears, from his own standpoint, to be starting with capital; but the social content of the loan is a reallocation of already-produced surplus-value. The identity of the borrower as \"capitalist\" is thus partly an article of credit. This prepares the way for the chapter's later, more systematic discussion of latent money capital and bank deposits, in which the same insight is generalized: what appears as stored-up money capital is, on inspection, the surplus-value of other capitalists circulating through financial intermediaries. Marx then links back to Volume I, Chapter 24, where \"the real content of accumulation\" was defined as the transformation of surplus-value into capital , i.e. reproduction on an expanded scale , whether this expansion expresses itself extensively (addition of new factories) or intensively (enlargement of the former scale of operations). This backward reference matters because it fixes the meaning of \"accumulation\" for the rest of the chapter: accumulation is not mere hoarding of money but actual expansion of productive capital . The money-form of accumulation is secondary and transient; the real content is expanded reproduction. Marx then enumerates three ways in which expansion can proceed in relatively small doses: Improvements that either simply raise the productive power of the labour applied, or allow labour to be more intensively exploited simultaneously — i.e. technical/organizational accumulation. Additional outlay of circulating capital (production materials and wages) when the working day is not restricted by law; this expands production without any increase in fixed capital, because the fixed capital's time of use is prolonged and its turnover period correspondingly shortened. Speculation in raw materials under favourable market conjunctures — operations for which the capital originally advanced would have been insufficient. The nuance here is that accumulation does not require a new factory; it can be squeezed out of the existing plant through intensification, extension of working time, and speculative manoeuvring with inputs. This is itself an important theoretical insight: the turnover period can be shortened without new fixed capital investment, which is precisely why turnover analysis is bound up with accumulation analysis. But Marx immediately introduces a countervailing limit. Where a relatively large number of turnover periods brings about more frequent realization of surplus-value, periods nevertheless occur in which the working day cannot be extended and individual improvements cannot be brought about. Meanwhile, extension of the whole business on a proportional scale — expanding the entire plant, buildings, agricultural labour fund, etc. — is possible only within certain limits and requires a volume of additional capital that can only be supplied by several years' accumulation of surplus-value. Thus the chapter sets up a distinction between small-dose, high-frequency accumulation (which turnover reinforces) and lumpy, large-scale accumulation (which turnover cannot replace, because it requires the aggregation of surplus-value over years). Beat 3 — Real Accumulation vs. Latent Money Capital; the Money Market From the previous distinction Marx derives the second fundamental structural category of the chapter: latent money capital . Besides real accumulation (the transformation of surplus-value into productive capital, and correspondingly reproduction on an expanded scale), there is also the accumulation of money , the scraping together of a part of the surplus-value as latent money capital , which is only to function as additional active capital later on, when it has attained a certain volume. The term \"latent money capital\" is crucial and needs careful definition. It denotes surplus-value that has been realized in money but is not yet applied to production. It is capital because it is destined to become productive capital; it is latent because it is not yet functioning as such. It is the intermediate state between realized surplus-value and expanded reproduction. Its existence is a structural necessity of capitalist production, not an accident, because large-scale expansion requires the accumulation of a critical mass. This is the first of the chapter's several decisive distinctions: surplus-value as revenue (consumed individually) vs. surplus-value as latent money capital (awaiting conversion into productive capital) vs. surplus-value as productive capital (actually functioning in production). Marx then notes that this is how the matter appears from the standpoint of the individual capitalist . With the development of capitalist production, however, the credit system develops simultaneously. The money capital the capitalist cannot yet apply in his own business is employed by others, from whom he receives interest. It functions for him as money capital in the specific sense — a kind of capital distinct from productive capital. But it is in someone else's hands that it actually operates as capital. Here the term \"money capital in the specific sense\" is defined: capital that earns interest precisely by being loaned out rather than being directly invested in production. With more frequent realization of surplus-value and rising scale of production, a growing proportion of new money capital, or money as capital, is placed on the money market , and at least a large part of it is again absorbed from the money market for the expansion of production. The \"money market\" therefore enters the analysis as the institutional locus where latent money capital is pooled, priced (via interest), and reallocated. This is the origin, within Volume II, of what Volume III will call the credit system. The vocabulary here is instructive: Marx speaks of money \"placed on\" the money market and \"absorbed from\" it, i.e. treated as a supply (latent capital seeking employment) and demand (productive capital seeking capital). The seed of the supply-and-demand-of-loanable-funds analysis is planted, though Marx does not yet develop it. Beat 4 — Hoard, Claims, and Thompson's Nominal Wealth Marx now reduces latent money capital to its simplest form: the hoard . The hoard may consist of additional gold or silver received directly or indirectly in exchange with the metal-producing countries — this is the only way the money hoard within a country grows in absolute terms. Alternatively (and this is the majority case), the hoard is nothing more than money withdrawn from domestic circulation and hoarded by individual capitalists. It may also consist of value tokens (credit money being left out of account here) or of mere claims (titles) of the capitalist on third parties established by legal documents. In all these cases, whatever the form of existence of the extra money capital, in so far as it is prospective capital it represents no more than extra legal titles to the future additional production of society that the capitalists hold in reserve. This sentence is the analytical hinge for the William Thompson quotation that follows. Marx has translated the question of latent money capital into a question about claims : what is stored up is not primarily physical coin but legal entitlements to future production. This is a fundamentally important claim about the nature of wealth in a capitalist economy, and it is expressly attributed by Marx to a pre-Marxian socialist critic of capitalism. The long block quotation from William Thompson's An Inquiry into the Principles of the Distribution of Wealth (London, 1850; originally 1824) drives the point home in polemical terms. Thompson argues: The mass of real accumulated wealth is \"utterly insignificant\" compared with the powers of production of the same society, or even with the actual consumption of a few years. Legislators and political economists should therefore direct attention to productive powers and their future free development, not to the \"mere accumulated wealth that strikes the eye.\" \"Of what is called accumulated wealth, by far the greater part is only nominal, consisting not of any real things, ships, houses, cottons, improvements on land, but of mere demands on the future annual productive powers of society, engendered and perpetuated by the expedients or institutions of insecurity.\" This is the crown jewel of Thompson's argument as Marx appropriates it. The \"nominal\" character of wealth means that most wealth is a legal claim , not a thing . The only thing that makes such claims valuable is the ongoing productive power of society. Hence \"the use of such articles ... as a mere means of appropriating to their possessors the wealth to be created by the future productive powers of society\" is the mechanism by which the minority appropriates the future. Thompson then illustrates with numerical estimates: annual consumption of Britain and Ireland (20 million people at ~£20 each) is about £400 million; the whole accumulated capital is estimated at no more than £1,200 million, or three times the year's labour — sixty pounds per head. The interest on this capital stock would support the whole population in comfort for about two months; the whole accumulated capital itself would maintain them in idleness for three years, after which, without houses, clothes or food, they must starve or become slaves. So the ratio of accumulated capital to productive powers is about three years to forty years — i.e. a mere moment compared to a generation's productive capacity. And crucially: \"About one third part of the annual products of the labour of these countries is now abstracted from the producers, under the name of public burdens, and unproductively consumed by those who give no equivalent.\" So a full third of annual output is appropriated by the state (taxes) and consumed unproductively. Thompson's dialectic of visibility: annual consumption is like \"the eternal and incalculable waves of a mighty river [that] roll on and are lost in the forgotten ocean of consumption\" — the eye is struck by the durable monuments (furniture, machinery, buildings) but not by the recurring annual product on which the whole human race depends for existence. Hence the vulgar eye is misled. Thompson's framing of the intellectual-political contrast: \"Actual accumulations and distributions have been always considered ... in reference, and subordinate, to the power of producing. In almost all other systems, the power of producing has been considered in reference, and subordinate, to actual accumulations, and to the perpetuating of the existing modes of distribution.\" This is a direct critique of the apologetic political economy that Marx has been attacking throughout the chapter. The polemical conclusion: \"To perpetuate the results of force, fraud, and chance, has been called security; and to the support of this spurious security, have all the productive powers of the human race been unrelentingly sacrificed.\" Marx's use of this quotation is strategic. On the surface it seems only to illustrate a rhetorical point about nominal wealth, but it actually accomplishes three analytical tasks. First, it historicizes the legal claims that make up latent money capital. Second, it establishes that the total social wealth is overwhelmingly a flow (annual production) rather than a stock (accumulation), which is the theoretical premise of the entire reproduction-schemes analysis that follows in Volume II. Third, it voices a proto-socialist critique that Marx incorporates into his own analysis, marking the chapter's anti-apologetic stance. Beat 5 — The Two Normal Cases: Simple Reproduction vs. Accumulation At this point Marx lays out the analytical framework for the rest of the chapter. As far as reproduction is concerned, only two normal cases are possible, leaving aside disturbances which inhibit reproduction even on the existing scale: Simple reproduction — the surplus-value periodically produced and realized (annually or by several turnovers within the year) is consumed individually, i.e. unproductively , by its owners, the capitalists. Capitalization of surplus-value — i.e. accumulation. The chapter now proceeds through these two cases in order: Section I on simple reproduction (the bulk of the chapter, most of pp. 6–25), and Section II on accumulation and expanded reproduction (pp. 25–31). Section I: Simple Reproduction — setup. In simple reproduction, the surplus-value is consumed unproductively. But the fact that product value consists partly of surplus-value and partly of the reproduced variable capital and consumed constant capital does not change in the least either the volume or the value of the total product which enters circulation as commodity capital and is similarly withdrawn to go into productive or individual consumption. Leaving aside constant capital, only the distribution of the annual product between workers and capitalists is affected. Marx then sets out the methodological reduction: to simplify, we take the problem in its simplest form — exclusive circulation of metallic money , money that is a real equivalent . This methodological move matters: by ruling out credit money and bank money for the moment, Marx can give a rigorous first-pass answer. Credit complications are re-introduced later in the chapter (and in Volume III). Then Marx restates the laws of simple commodity circulation from Volume I, Ch. 3: - The mass of metallic money in a country must be sufficient not only to circulate commodities but to cope with fluctuations. - These fluctuations arise from (a) fluctuations in the speed of circulation, (b) changes in commodity prices , and (c) the changing proportions in which money functions as means of payment vs. means of circulation proper . - The ratio in which the existing money mass is divided into hoard and money in circulation constantly changes, but the total mass is always equal to hoard + circulating money. - This quantity of precious metal is a social hoard , accumulated bit by bit. - Since a portion is worn away in circulation, it must be replaced each year — which happens through direct or indirect exchange of a part of the annual product with the product of the gold- and silver-producing countries. Marx then performs another methodological simplification: since the international character of this transaction conceals its simple course, he assumes gold and silver production takes place within the country itself , so that money-metal production is a part of total social production. This is one of the most important simplifying assumptions in the chapter, and it will be relaxed later (pp. 17–19, where Marx notes that the situation is unchanged if gold production is transferred abroad). Marx then establishes the minimum annual production of precious metals : ignoring gold/silver produced for luxury articles, it must equal the wear and tear of the money metals occasioned by annual monetary circulation. If the value of the commodities in circulation grows, the annual production of gold and silver must also grow, unless compensated by greater velocity of circulation or more comprehensive use of money as means of payment (i.e. more mutual settlement of purchases and sales without actual money). Consequently, a part of social labour-power and a part of social means of production must be spent each year in the production of gold and silver. This is a crucial structural point: gold production is a real burden on social production, a \" faux frais \" (overhead cost) of the commodity-circulation mode of production — a point Marx will develop later (p. 27). Beat 6 — The Gold Producer's Circuit: M–C...P...M' Analyzed in Detail Marx now analyzes the specific circuit of capital invested in precious-metal production, written M–C...P...M' . This notation must be unpacked: M = money capital advanced (the purchase of labour-power and means of production). C = commodity capital purchased — i.e. labour-power and means of production (including fixed capital). ...P... = the production process. M' = money product — but here, importantly, M' is the natural form of the product itself . Unlike other branches, where the product emerges as a commodity that must be sold for money, the gold producer's product is money from the start. In so far as the C in M–C does not consist only of labour-power and means of production, but also of fixed capital, only a part of whose value is used up in P, it is evident that M' – the product – is a sum of money equal to the variable capital laid out on wages, plus the circulating constant capital laid out on means of production, plus the portion of value of the fixed capital used up, plus the surplus-value. If the sum were smaller, with the general value of gold unchanged, then the mines in question would be unprofitable , or — if this is generally the case — the value of gold would in future rise compared with commodities whose value was unchanged; i.e. commodity prices would fall , so that the sum of money laid out in M–C would in future be less. This is a subtle argument about the flexibility of the value of gold as a standard : an unprofitable gold industry, if general, cheapens commodities by raising the value of money. Marx then considers the circulating part of the capital advanced in M. A certain sum of money is advanced and cast into circulation in payment for labour-power and to purchase materials of production. In ordinary commodity production, this money would be withdrawn again from circulation by the sale of the product. But here the money is not withdrawn again from circulation by the circuit of this capital . The product, in its natural form, is already money. It moves from the production process into the circulation sphere not as commodity capital that has to be transformed back into money capital , but rather as money capital that has to be transformed back into productive capital — i.e. has to buy new labour-power and materials of production. The money form of the circulating capital is replaced not by the sale of the product, but by the natural form of the product itself — not by withdrawing its value again from circulation in the money form, but rather by adding money newly produced . Marx then gives a concrete numerical example. Let the circulating capital be £500 , the turnover period five weeks (a four-week working period, one-week circulation period). Right from the start, money must be advanced for five weeks in a production stock and held to pay wages bit by bit. At the beginning of the sixth week, £400 has returned and £100 has been set free. This is continually repeated. Here, as before, £100 always exists in the released form for a certain section of the turnover — but this consists of additional money newly produced , just like the other £400. There are ten turnovers in the year , and the annual product is £5,000 in gold . Note carefully: the circulation period here does not arise from the time taken to transform commodities into money, but rather that taken to transform money into elements of production . This is one of the distinctive features of gold production: its \"circulation\" is not a sale of product for money but a purchase of inputs with money. For any other capital of £500 turning over under the same conditions, the constantly renewed money form is the changed form of the commodity capital produced (cast into circulation every four weeks, receiving its money form afresh by its sale — i.e. by the periodic withdrawal of the sum of money in the shape of which it originally entered the process). Here, on the contrary, in every turnover period a new additional sum of £500 in money is cast into circulation by the production process , so as to keep withdrawing materials of production and labour-power from circulation. The money thus cast into the circulation sphere is not withdrawn from it again by the circuit of this capital, but rather by the increased quantity of new gold that is constantly produced. This is the fundamental structural difference between gold-producing capital and all other capitals: gold-producing capital is a net source of money into circulation, whereas all other capitals are net withdrawers of money from circulation (insofar as they have to sell their product before they can buy inputs). Marx then analyzes the variable part of this circulating capital. Taking it to be £100: in ordinary commodity production, £100 would be sufficient to pay labour-power through a ten-fold turnover. Here, in money production, the same sum is sufficient; but the five-weekly reflux of £100 with which labour-power is paid is not the changed form of its product, but rather a part of its ever-new product itself. The gold producer pays his workers directly with a part of the gold they have themselves produced. Consequently, the £1,000 laid out each year on labour-power and thrown into circulation by the workers does not return via circulation to its starting-point. This is the seed of a later point: the capitalist may appear to \"advance\" wages, but in gold production it is transparent that the workers are being paid out of their own product. For fixed capital : the initial establishment of the business requires the expenditure of a relatively large money capital, cast into circulation. Like all fixed capital, this returns only bit by bit over a number of years. But it flows back as a direct fragment of the product, the gold , not by the sale of the product and its conversion into money. Thus it does not receive its money form by a withdrawal of money from circulation, but rather by the accumulation of a corresponding part of the product . The money capital thus re-established is not a sum of money gradually withdrawn from circulation to balance the sum originally cast in for fixed capital. It is an additional quantity of money. This is a decisive point: fixed capital in gold production is reproduced in the money form directly, from the physical product, without any prior transaction. For surplus-value : this is also equal to a part of the new gold product, cast into circulation in each turnover period, to be spent unproductively, and paid out for means of subsistence and luxury articles. Marx then makes the crucial aggregate assertion: this entire annual gold production ... only replaces the money worn out during the year, and thus simply keeps intact the social money stock, which always exists in the two forms of hoard and money in circulation, though in varying proportions. So, on the assumption that gold production just covers wear and tear, the net money stock is unchanged; what has happened is that money has been cast in by gold producers and cast out (in equivalent commodity value) by everyone else. The aggregate social money stock is stable; only its division between hoard and circulating money shifts. This sentence prepares the general answer to the Tooke puzzle: the gold producers cast pure money into circulation without having to first sell a commodity; the rest of the capitalist class casts a commodity value containing surplus-value into circulation without the money to realize it. The two flows are complementary. This is the answer in germ. Beat 7 — The General Commodity-Circulation Answer to the \"Where Does the Money Come From?\" Question Marx restates the general law. According to the law of commodity circulation, the total quantity of money must equal the quantity of money required for circulation plus a sum in hoard form which increases or decreases according to the contraction or expansion of circulation, and serves especially for the formation of the reserve fund of means of payment. What has to be paid in money — insofar as there is no direct balancing of accounts — is the value of the commodities. The fact that part of this value consists of surplus-value — i.e. has cost the seller of the commodity nothing — does not change this situation in any way. Marx then performs a thought experiment to drive this home: imagine all producers possessed their means of production independently , and there were circulation between the direct producers themselves. Ignoring the constant part of their capital, we could divide their annual surplus product, by analogy with capitalism, into: Part (a): replacement of necessary means of subsistence (analogue of variable capital). Part (b): luxury consumption and application to expansion of production (analogue of surplus-value). But this division would have no effect on the quantity of money required to circulate their total product. With circumstances otherwise the same, the value of the mass of commodities in circulation would be the same, and so would the quantity of money required by it. They would need the same money reserves as before, given a similar division of the turnover period — i.e. the same part of their capital would always have to be in the money form. Thus the circumstance that a part of the commodity value consists of surplus-value does not alter in the least the quantity of money needed to carry on the business. This is the general answer : the question \"where does the money come from to realize surplus-value?\" presupposes that there is something special about realizing surplus-value, but there isn't — realization is simply sale, and sale is governed by the quantity of money required for the circulation of a given commodity value, irrespective of whether that value contains surplus-value. The problem dissolves. Marx then brings in the Tooke polemic explicitly. An opponent of Tooke, who supports the form M–C–M', asked him how the capitalist always manages to withdraw more money from circulation than he cast into it. Marx points out that the question is not about the formation of surplus-value (which is \"the only real secret,\" presupposed by the capitalists), but about the money into which surplus-value is turned. The question is not: where does surplus-value come from? But rather: where does the money come from which it is turned into? Marx notes that bourgeois economics takes the existence of surplus-value to be given, and takes it as given at the same time that a part of the mass of commodities cast into circulation consists of surplus product — a value the capitalist did not cast into circulation with his capital. So the capitalist casts into circulation an excess over and above his capital, and withdraws this excess from it again. This is, as it happens, true — but the trick is to see where the money came from. The commodity capital the capitalist casts into circulation is of greater value than the productive capital he has withdrawn in labour-power and means of production — a fact that, from the capitalist's standpoint, is not explained or understood, but is a fact for all that. On this assumption, it is clear why not only capitalist A but also B, C, D, etc. can always withdraw more value from circulation (by exchanging their commodities) than the value of their original capital. They always cast a greater commodity value into circulation in the form of commodity capital than they withdraw in the form of productive capital. They always have a value sum to share equal to the value of the productive capitals they advanced, plus a value sum which they cast into circulation as surplus-of-commodity-value over and above the value of their commodity's elements of production. But — and this is the crux — before the commodity capital is transformed back into productive capital and the surplus-value contained in it is spent, it must be turned into money. Where does the money for this come from? This question appears difficult at first glance, and \"neither Tooke nor anyone else has yet answered it.\" So Marx has defined a specific unresolved problem within the general circulation laws. Beat 8 — The \"£500 Becomes £600\" Puzzle Marx sharpens the paradox with a specific aggregate example. Assume that the circulating capital of £500 advanced in money form, whatever its turnover period, is the total circulating capital of society, i.e. of the capitalist class. The surplus-value is £100. How then can the entire capitalist class continue extracting £600 from the circulation sphere, if it only ever puts £500 into it? The mechanics: once the £500 money capital is transformed into productive capital, this is transformed within the production process into a commodity value of £600. Now there exists in circulation not only a commodity value of £500 (equal to the money capital originally advanced) but also a newly produced surplus-value of £100. This extra surplus-value of £100 is cast into circulation in the commodity form. There is no doubt about that. But the extra money needed for the circulation of this additional commodity value is not provided by the same operation. The paradox is now stated with full force: the additional commodity value is real, but the additional money to circulate it is not automatically supplied by the sale of the commodity — because sales only succeed one another; they do not spontaneously generate additional money. Beat 9 — Marx Refutes Four \"Subterfuges\" Marx now explicitly rules out four plausible ways of \"solving\" the paradox, each of which he shows is merely a redescription of the problem: Subterfuge 1 — The circulating constant capital is not all laid out at once. On this view, while A sells his commodities and his capital assumes the money form, buyer B's capital, present in money form, is assuming the form of means of production — and it is A who produces these. By the same act through which A gives back its money form to the commodity capital he has produced, B gives his capital back its productive form. The same sum of money functions in the two-way process, just as in every simple sale. And when A transforms money back into means of production, he buys from C, who pays B, etc. Marx's rebuttal: \"However, none of the laws put forward with respect to the quantity of money circulating for the purpose of commodity circulation are in any way altered by the capitalist character of the production process.\" So when it is said that the circulating capital advanced by society in the money form amounts to £500, it has already been taken into account that this is not only the sum advanced at the same time, but a sum that also sets more productive capital than £500 in motion, since it serves alternately as the money fund for different productive capitals. This mode of explanation already presupposes that the money exists, whereas it is precisely its existence that is to be explained. In other words, the circulation of a single coin through several hands presupposes that the coin exists; it does not explain how the additional value of £600 is realized. The subterfuge confuses the circulation of a given money stock with the creation of the money needed to circulate an increased commodity value. Subterfuge 2 — A produces articles B consumes; B's money turns A's commodity capital into money. Marx: this presupposes the solution even more directly. Where does B get this money to meet his revenue? How did he himself manage to convert into money this part of his product's surplus-value? Here the objection is circularity: B's money is itself the realization of B's own surplus-value, so the question is merely relocated. Subterfuge 3 — The variable capital A advances to his workers flows back from circulation; between outlay and reflux, the money paid in wages can serve to convert surplus-value into money. Marx grants the timing point but notes: first, the greater the interval, the greater the money reserve A must constantly retain. Second, if the workers pay the money out and buy commodities, the surplus-value contained in these commodities is proportionately converted into money. Thus the same money advanced as variable capital also serves to convert surplus-value into money. But — crucial twist — without going any deeper into the question here, it is at least clear that the consumption of the entire capitalist class and the unproductive persons dependent on it keeps even pace with that of the working class; thus, on top of the money cast into circulation by the workers, money must be cast into circulation by the capitalists, if they are to spend their surplus-value as revenue; and so money for this must be withdrawn from circulation. Therefore this explanation only reduces the quantity needed, and does not obviate the need . In other words, even granting all the timing efficiencies, the additional money must come from somewhere — either freshly produced or withdrawn from hoards. Subterfuge 4 — The initial investment of fixed capital casts a large sum into circulation, withdrawn only gradually, and this may suffice to convert surplus-value. Marx: the sum of £500 (including hoard formation) may already include the investment as fixed capital, if not by the person who cast it in, then by someone else. Besides, it is already presupposed that the surplus-value in the commodities serving as fixed capital is paid for — and the question precisely arises: where does this money come from? Beat 10 — The General Answer: Money Quantity is Independent of Surplus-Value Marx now states the resolution. The general answer has already been given: if a mass of commodities of x times £1,000 is to circulate, it in no way affects the quantity of money needed for this circulation whether the value of this commodity mass contains surplus-value or not, or whether the mass of commodities is produced under capitalist conditions or not. Thus the problem itself does not exist. With conditions otherwise given (velocity of circulation, etc.), a definite sum of money is required to circulate the commodity value of x times £1,000, quite irrespective of how much or how little of this value accrues to the direct producers. In as much as a problem does exist here, it coincides with the general problem: where does the sum of money needed in a country for the circulation of commodities come from? So the general answer to the paradox is: it does not exist. The question is a false special problem. Beat 11 — The Semblance of a Special Capitalist Problem and the \"Revenue\" Solution But Marx immediately acknowledges — however, there does exist, from the standpoint of capitalist production, the semblance of a special problem. Here, the capitalist, the man who casts the money into circulation, appears as the point of departure. The two starting-points of money circulation are: - The capitalist — who initially casts money into circulation for constant and variable capital. - The worker — but the worker's money is already the second circulation of the variable capital originally cast in by the capitalist. - All third parties either receive money from these two classes for services, or — insofar as they receive money without services in return — are co-proprietors of surplus-value in the form of rent, interest, etc. Marx notes that the division of surplus-value among industrial capitalist, landlord, financier, etc., has nothing to do with the question at issue . What was asked is how the capitalist converts his surplus-value into money, not how the money obtained for it is then divided up. So Marx can, for the present, consider the capitalist as the sole owner of surplus-value. Workers are only a secondary point of departure; the capitalist is primary — because the money paid as wages was already advanced by the capitalist as variable capital. The money the worker spends is performing its second circulation when he spends it. Hence: the capitalist class remains the sole starting-point of the money circulation. If it needs £400 for means of production and £100 for labour-power, it casts £500 into circulation. But the surplus-value contained in the product, at 100% rate of surplus-value, makes up a value of £100. How can the capitalist class continue to extract £600 from circulation, if it only ever puts in £500? Out of nothing, nothing comes. The conclusion seems inescapable. Marx then dismisses a numerical objection: he concedes that the sum of £400 may suffice, given a ten-fold turnover, to circulate means of production to a value of £4,000 and labour to a value of £1,000, while £100 may suffice for the circulation of £1,000 surplus-value. But this ratio contributes nothing to the matter in hand. Whether the money needed is £1,000 or £100, the question is where it comes from. In either case, it is additional money capital cast into circulation. Beat 12 — The Actual Solution: The Capitalist Class Casts in the Money, but as Revenue, Not as Capital And now the resolution. \"In point of fact, paradoxical as it may seem at first glance, the capitalist class itself casts into circulation the money that serves towards the realization of the surplus-value contained in its commodities. But note well: it does not cast this in as money advanced, and therefore not as capital. It spends it as means of purchase for its individual consumption. Thus the money is not advanced by the capitalist class, even though this class is the starting-point of its circulation.\" The critical distinction: advancing money as capital vs. spending money as revenue. When the capitalist advances money as capital (constant + variable), he expects it to flow back. When he spends money as revenue (individual consumption), he does not expect it to flow back as money-invested — but he does, in fact, get it back as the money form of the surplus-value he produces. This is the elegant dialectic of the chapter. Beat 13 — The Farmer Example (First Year vs. Subsequent Years) Marx works this out in a detailed numerical illustration. Consider a farmer who begins business. During the first year: Advances £5,000 money capital — £4,000 for means of production, £1,000 for labour-power. Rate of surplus-value = 100%, so surplus-value = £1,000 . The farmer must also live . Say his consumption = £1,000. He must have this in hand. He says he \"advances\" £1,000 for the first year. But this is an advance only in the subjective sense — it means nothing more than that he has to cover his individual consumption for the first year out of his own pocket, instead of using the product produced for nothing by his workers. He does not advance this money as capital. He spends it — pays it out for an equivalent in means of subsistence, which he then consumes. He has ceased to stand in any relationship to its value. The money with which he pays for it exists as a component of the circulating money stock, but he has withdrawn the value of this money from circulation in products, and the value of these products is destroyed together with the products. At the end of the year, he throws into circulation a commodity value of £6,000 and sells this. There returns to him: £5,000 — the money capital he advanced. £1,000 — his converted surplus-value. So he advanced £5,000 as capital and withdraws £6,000 — of which £5,000 as capital, £1,000 as surplus-value. The final £1,000 is converted into money with the money that he threw into circulation not as capitalist but as consumer , i.e. did not advance but actually spent . It now returns to him as the money form of the surplus-value produced by him . From the second year on, the operation repeats annually: the £1,000 he spends is always the changed form, the money form, of the surplus-value he produced. He spends this annually, and it returns to him at the same interval. Marx then adds a nuance: if his capital were to turn over several times in the course of the year, this would not change things in any way , though it would affect the length of time for which he must cast this sum into circulation, and hence the magnitude of the sum involved. And a normative remark: \"This money is not cast into circulation by the capitalist as capital. However, it certainly pertains to the character of the capitalist that he should be capable of living off the means of subsistence in his possession until the reflux of his surplus-value.\" So the capacity to live off a subsistence fund while waiting for the reflux of capital is itself a class attribute — a presupposition of capitalist status. Marx then flags a crucial caveat: it was assumed in this case that the sum of money the capitalist casts into circulation to cover his consumption equals the surplus-value he produces. This is obviously an arbitrary assumption in relation to the individual capitalist. But it must be correct for the capitalist class as a whole, on the assumption of simple reproduction. It simply expresses what the simple reproduction assumption implies: that the entire surplus-value is unproductively consumed — but no more than this, i.e. no fraction of the original capital stock . So the mechanism: the capitalist class, in spending its revenue, casts into circulation the money that will return to it as its surplus-value. The \"paradox\" is resolved by the recognition that the money advanced as revenue (not capital) is exactly the money needed to convert the surplus-value into money. Beat 14 — The Special Role of Gold Production in Realization Marx recapitulates the gold production assumption: the entire annual production of precious metals (taken as £500) was just sufficient to replace the wear and tear of the money. The gold-producing capitalists possess their entire product in gold — including the part replacing constant capital, the part replacing variable capital, and the surplus-value. So one part of society's surplus-value consists of gold from the start and is cast into circulation in order to withdraw products from it. The same applies to wages (variable capital) and to the replacement of constant capital advanced. So the answer to the paradox has a second, complementary structure: The non-gold-producing capitalists cast into circulation a commodity value greater (by the surplus-value) than the money capital they advanced . The gold-producing capitalists cast into circulation a greater money value (greater by the surplus-value) than the commodity value they withdraw for the production of gold. \"If one group of capitalists constantly pump more money out of the circulation sphere than they put into it, the gold-producing group constantly pump more money in than they withdraw from it in means of production.\" Even though a part of the £500 gold product is surplus-value for the gold producers, the entire sum is still simply determined by the replacement of the money needed for the circulation of commodities ; how much converts surplus-value into money, and how much converts other value components, is immaterial. If gold production is transferred abroad, this does not change the situation. Country A exports linen worth £500 to Country B to buy gold. This product of A is represented by £500 in gold and comes into circulation in country A only as money. The part of social surplus-value contained in this product exists directly in money, and, as far as country A goes, never in any other form. Although for the gold-producing capitalists only part of their product is surplus-value while another part replaces capital, the question of how much of the gold replaces variable capital and how much represents surplus-value depends exclusively on the respective ratios of wages and surplus-value to the value of the commodities in circulation. The surplus-value portion is divided among the various members of the capitalist class. A part of social surplus-value exists in the form of money in the capitalists' pockets, in changing portions, just as a part of the workers' wages remains in their pockets as money for at least part of the week. Two important conclusions follow. Marx states them explicitly: Firstly , the surplus-value spent by capitalists in money, as well as the variable and other productive capital which they advance in money, is in fact the product of the workers — in particular of those workers occupied in gold production. These workers produce afresh both the part of the gold product \"advanced\" to them as wages, and the part in which the surplus-value of the gold-producing capitalist is directly represented. Even the part of the gold product that simply replaces constant capital value: it reappears in gold form only as a result of the annual labour of the workers. At the start of the business it was originally given out by the capitalist in money that was not newly produced but formed part of the existing social quantity of money. But in so far as it is replaced by a new product, additional gold, it is the annual product of the workers. The advance on the part of the capitalist appears here too only as a form deriving from the fact that the worker is neither the proprietor of his own means of production nor has at his disposal during production the means of subsistence produced by other workers. This is a striking restatement, in monetary terms, of the fundamental thesis of Volume I: the worker's apparent dependence on the capitalist's \"advance\" is an artifact of the property relations. In gold production, this is transparently visible: the capitalist appears to advance wages, but the wages are physically a part of the gold the workers themselves have just mined. Secondly , as regards the quantity of money that exists independently of this annual replacement (£500), partly as hoard, partly as circulating money, the same must apply to it — the same must originally have applied, as still applies to this annual £500. Marx promises to return to this point at the conclusion of the section, and he does so at the chapter's end (see Beat 19). Beat 15 — Turnover, Money Elasticity, and the Wage-Rise Fallacy Marx now takes a digression that connects the chapter to his theory of money and wages. First, a point on turnover: with circumstances otherwise the same, changes in the length of turnover periods make different amounts of capital necessary in order to continue production on the same scale. The monetary circulation must thus be elastic enough to adapt to this alternate expansion and contraction. Then a key claim: assume other circumstances remain the same (no change in size, intensity, or productivity of the working day), but there is an altered division of value product between wages and surplus-value — either the former rises and the latter falls, or vice versa. Then the quantity of money in circulation is not affected. This change can come about without any kind of expansion or contraction in the quantity of money in circulation. Consider a general rise in wages (with a consequent general fall in the rate of surplus-value). There would be no change in the value of the mass of commodities in circulation. The money capital that has to be advanced as variable capital would grow, and so would the quantity of money serving this function. But surplus-value would decline by the same amount , and thus so would the quantity of money needed for its realization. The quantity of money needed to realize the commodity value is therefore no more affected than is this commodity value itself. The cost price of the commodities rises for the individual capitalist, but their social price of production remains unaltered . What changes is the ratio in which, leaving aside the constant portion of value, the production price of the commodities is divided between wages and profit. This is a foundational point of Marx's theory of distribution: a general change in the wage rate, holding productivity and working conditions fixed, redistributes value but does not change total value or the money needed to circulate it. Marx then addresses and refutes two common capitalistic arguments against this: Capitalistic argument 1 (demand-push): A greater outlay of variable money capital means a correspondingly greater quantity of monetary means in the workers' hands. The workers therefore have greater demand for commodities, which raises commodity prices. Therefore a general rise in wages leads to a rise in prices, requiring more money to circulate. Marx's reply: As a result of rising wages, the demand of workers for necessary means of subsistence will grow. Their demand for luxury articles will increase to a smaller degree, or else a demand will arise for articles that previously did not enter their consumption. The sudden rise in demand for necessary means of subsistence will temporarily raise their prices. This causes a greater part of social capital to be applied to production of necessaries and a smaller part to luxury goods, since the latter will have fallen in price on account of the decline in surplus-value and the resulting diminished demand from the capitalists. To the extent that workers themselves buy luxury goods, the rise in wages does not raise the prices of necessaries but simply displaces the buyers of luxury goods : more luxury goods consumed by workers, relatively fewer by capitalists. That is all. After a few oscillations the mass of commodities in circulation has the same value as before. And as for these temporary oscillations, they can have no other result than to cast into domestic circulation, as unoccupied money capital, capital which formerly sought employment in speculative undertakings on the stock exchange or abroad. Capitalistic argument 2 (price-push): If wages rise, capitalists will simply raise the prices of their commodities, so a greater quantity of money is needed to circulate. Marx's reply: If it were within the capacity of the capitalist producers to increase the prices of their commodities at will, then they could and would do so even without any rise in wages. Nor would wages rise with a fall in commodity prices. And the capitalist class would never oppose trade unions, since they would always be able to use any increase in wages as a pretext to raise prices to a far higher degree and thus tuck away a greater profit. The contention that capitalists can raise the prices of luxury articles because the demand for these declines would be \"an extremely original application of the law of supply and demand.\" Insofar as there is not just a shift in buyers — where workers replace capitalists, the workers' additional wages spent on luxury articles cannot be spent on necessaries — the prices of luxury goods fall as a consequence of the reduced demand. Capital is withdrawn from luxury production until supply is reduced to match their changed role in social production. With this reduction, prices rise again to normal, given unchanged values. During this contraction or balancing process, the same amount of additional capital is supplied for necessaries production, whose prices are rising, as is withdrawn from luxury production, until demand is satisfied. There is then once again an equilibrium, and the conclusion of the whole process is that the social capital, and hence also the money capital, is divided between the production of necessary means of subsistence and that of luxury goods in changed proportions. Thus \"the entire objection is a red herring brought in by the capitalists and their economic sycophants.\" Beat 16 — Three Facts Underlying the Wage-Rise Fallacy Marx distinguishes three \"facts\" that provide the pretext for the fallacy: (1) The general law of monetary circulation. If the sum of prices of goods in circulation rises (whether for the same volume or for an increased volume), with other circumstances the same, the quantity of money in circulation grows. The effect is then taken for the cause. However, wages rise (even if seldom, and proportionately only in exceptional cases) with the increased price of the necessary means of subsistence. Their rise is the result of the rise in commodity prices, and not the cause of this. This is a classic critique of post hoc reasoning: the observed correlation between wage rises and price rises is usually because both movements are consequences of prior changes (e.g. currency depreciation, harvest failures, changes in the value of gold), not because wages cause prices. (2) Partial or local wage rises. A local rise in wages in a few branches of production can result in a local rise in prices for that branch's products. But even this depends on many circumstances. For example, that wages were not abnormally depressed there (and hence the rate of profit not abnormally high), that the market for these commodities was not constricted by a rise in price (and thus that a rise in their prices does not depend on a preceding contraction in their supply), etc. In other words, the ability of a single branch to pass on a wage increase depends on its local market conditions and the pre-existing level of profit — not on a universal law. (3) General rise in wages. With a general rise in wages, the price of goods produced in branches of industry in which variable capital is predominant rises, whereas prices fall in those branches in which constant or fixed capital predominates . This is a sketch of a theory of price formation under a general wage rise depending on the organic composition of capital — clearly foreshadowing Volume III's analysis of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall. Beat 17 — The Reflux of Money vs. the Circulation of Money Marx now introduces one of the most conceptually rich distinctions of the chapter: the circuit of money (its return to its starting-point) versus the circulation of money (its constant removal from its starting-point through a series of hands) . He first cites Volume I, Ch. 3: simple commodity circulation shows that even if the money form is transient in the circulation of a particular quantity of commodities, the money transiently in one person's hands in commodity metamorphosis still necessarily finds its way into another's hands — so that not only are commodities exchanged on all sides, replacing each other, but this replacement is also mediated and accompanied by a precipitation of money on all sides. Marx quotes himself: \"When one commodity replaces another, the money commodity always sticks to the hands of some third person. Circulation sweats money from every pore.\" (Volume I, p. 208.) The very same fact is expressed on the basis of capitalist commodity production by the constant retention of a part of capital in the form of money capital, and the constant presence of a part of the surplus-value similarly in the money form in the hands of its proprietor. Marx then carefully distinguishes: the circuit of money — the return of the money to its starting-point, insofar as this forms a moment of turnover of capital — is a phenomenon completely different from and even opposed to the circulation of money, which expresses its constant removal from its starting-point through a series of hands (Volume I, p. 210). However, an accelerated turnover involves by its very nature an accelerated circulation. In the footnote, Marx credits the Physiocrats with first stressing reflux as essential: Quesnay in the Tableau économique (\"the productive class gives out the money with which the other classes come to buy its products, and these return this money to it the following year ... The sole cycle that you see here is therefore that of expenditure followed by reproduction, and reproduction followed by expenditure\") and Turgot in the Réflexions (\"It is this continuous advance and return of capitals that must be called the circulation of money, this useful and fertile circulation which animates all the works of society, sustaining the movement and life of the body politic in a way that may well be compared with the circulation of blood in the animal body\"). Marx's engagement with the Physiocrats signals that his own reflux concept is a materialist transformation of the physiocratic insight. Marx then works through the variable capital example with numbers. If a money capital of £500 turns over ten times a year as variable capital, this aliquot part of the quantity of money in circulation circulates ten times its value sum. The worker is paid — and himself pays — ten times in the year with the same aliquot part. If instead the variable capital turned over once a year with the same scale of production, there would only be one circulation of £5,000. Similarly for constant circulating capital: if it is £1,000 and turns over ten times, then the capitalist sells his commodity ten times and thus the constant circulating part of its value with it. The same aliquot part of the money quantity (£1,000) passes ten times from its owner's hands to the capitalist's — ten changes of place. Secondly, the capitalist buys means of production ten times, again ten circulations of money. With £1,000 of money, commodities of £10,000 are sold by the industrial capitalist, and other commodities of £10,000 are bought. By a twenty-fold circulation of the £1,000 of money, a commodity stock of £20,000 is circulated. Finally, accelerated turnover also leads to a quicker circulation of the portion of money that realizes surplus-value. But — and here's the crucial asymmetry — conversely, a more rapid monetary circulation does not necessarily involve a more rapid turnover of capital, and hence also of money, i.e. there is not necessarily a shortening and more rapid renewal of the reproduction process. More rapid monetary circulation takes place whenever a greater volume of transactions is completed with the same quantity of money. This can also be the case without a change in the reproduction period of the capital, as a result of changed technical arrangements for monetary circulation. Further, the volume of transactions can increase without real replacement of commodities (speculation in futures on the stock exchange, etc.). On the other hand, certain monetary circulations can completely disappear: e.g. no circulation between farmer and landlord when the agriculturist is his own landlord; no circulation between industrial capitalist and creditor when the industrial capitalist owns his own capital. This means the apparent money velocity is not a reliable indicator of the underlying reproduction process — a methodological warning. Beat 18 — The Historical Precondition of Capitalist Production: A Sufficient Supply of Precious Metals Marx then addresses the question of the original formation of a money hoard in a country and its appropriation by a few people — but he says it is not necessary to go into this in detail here. Instead he draws out a historical conclusion. The capitalist mode of production — since its basis is wage-labour, and therefore also the payment of the worker in money and the general transformation of services in kind into money payments — can develop on a large scale and penetrate deeply only when there is a quantity of money in the country in question sufficient for circulation and for the hoard formation conditioned by this circulation. This is a historical precondition , even if we should not conceive it in such a way that a sufficient hoard has first to be formed before capitalist production can begin. The latter rather develops simultaneously with the development of its preconditions, and one of these preconditions is a sufficient supply of precious metals. Hence the increased supply of precious metals from the sixteenth century onwards was a decisive moment in the historical development of capitalist production. This is one of the few passages in Capital where Marx states a material-historical precondition of the capitalist mode of production in monetary-metallurgical terms — a claim that would later be picked up by historical sociologists and world-systems theorists. As for the further supply of money material needed on the basis of the capitalist mode of production: on the one hand surplus-value is cast into circulation in the product without the money for its conversion, while on the other hand surplus-value in gold is cast into circulation without its previous transformation from product into money. The additional commodities that have to be transformed into money find the sums of money needed available because on the other hand additional gold (and silver) is cast into circulation by production itself, not by exchange, and has to be transformed into commodities. This is the complementary unity of the two flows: commodity-surplus and gold-surplus. Beat 19 — Section II: Accumulation and Expanded Reproduction Marx now turns to the case in which accumulation takes place — reproduction on an expanded scale. This clearly does not offer any new problems with respect to money circulation. As far as the additional money capital is concerned — that required for the function of the increased productive capital — this is supplied by the portion of realized surplus-value cast into circulation by the capitalists as money capital , instead of as money form of revenue . The money is already in the hands of the capitalists. It is simply its application that differs. Here the analytical distinction between revenue-spending and capital-advance reappears: the same money can perform either function, but the destination differs, and hence the return differs. Now, however, as a result of the addition to the productive capital, an additional mass of commodities is cast into circulation as its product. Together with this extra mass of commodities, a part of the extra money needed for their realization is also cast in, to the extent that the value of this mass of commodities contains the value of the productive capital consumed in their production. This additional quantity of money is advanced precisely as additional money capital , and hence returns to the capitalist with the turnover of his capital. Here the same question arises again as before. Where does the extra money come from to realize the extra surplus-value that now exists in the commodity form? The general reply is again the same. The total price of the mass of commodities in circulation has increased, not because the price of a given mass of commodities has risen, but rather because the mass of commodities is greater than that of the commodities circulating earlier, without this having been balanced by any fall in prices. The additional money required must be created either by: A more economic use of the quantity of money in circulation — whether by directly balancing payments, etc., or by means that accelerate circulation of the same pieces of money. Or alternatively by the transformation of money from the hoard form into the circulating form. This does not just imply that idle money capital begins to function as means of purchase or payment, or that money capital already functioning as a reserve fund, while continuing to perform the reserve-fund function for its owners, circulates actively for the society (as with deposits in banks, which are constantly lent out), and thus performs a double function. It also means that stagnant reserves of coin are used more economically. Here Marx quotes his own Zur Kritik der politischen Ökonomie (1859, pp. 105–6): \"So that money as coin may flow continuously, coin must continuously congeal into money. The continual movement of coin implies its perpetual stagnation in larger or smaller amounts in reserve funds of coin which arise everywhere within the framework of circulation and which are at the same time a condition of circulation. The formation, distribution, dissolution and re-formation of these funds constantly changes; existing funds disappear continuously and their disappearance is a continuous fact.\" And: \"money is in fact only suspended coin and the various component parts of the coinage in circulation appear, constantly changing, now in one form, now in another. The first transformation of the medium of circulation into money constitutes therefore merely a technical aspect of the circulation of money.\" So the hoard is not the opposite of circulating money but its presupposition and complement : hoards are the technical reserve funds of circulation. If all these means together are not enough, there must be additional production of gold , or, what comes to the same thing, a part of additional product must be exchanged directly or indirectly for gold — the product of precious-metal-producing countries. Beat 20 — Faux Frais of Circulation and the Pointless Question of Credit Marx next makes a normative-dynamic point. The sum of labour-power and social means of production spent in the annual production of gold and silver as instruments of circulation forms a heavy item of faux frais for the capitalist mode of production, or, more generally, for a mode of production based on commodity circulation. It withdraws from social use a corresponding sum of possible additional means of production and consumption — i.e. of real wealth. To the extent that the costs of this expensive machinery of circulation are reduced, with the scale of production remaining the same, i.e. at a given level of its extension, the productive forces of social labour are correspondingly heightened. Thus to the extent that the auxiliary means developed with credit have this effect, they directly increase capitalist wealth , whether because a greater part of the social production and labour process is accomplished without the intervention of real money, or because the capacity of the actually functioning quantity of money to fulfil its function is thereby increased. This yields the conclusion that \"this also disposes of the pointless question of whether capitalist production on its present scale would be possible without credit (even considered from this standpoint alone), i.e. with a merely metallic circulation. It would clearly not be possible. It would come up against the limited scale of precious-metal production.\" But — and here Marx inserts a warning against the opposite error — \"we should not get any mystical ideas about the productive power of the credit system, just because this makes money capital available or fluid.\" The development of this point belongs in Volume III. Beat 21 — Latent Money Capital and the Impossibility of General Hoarding Marx now considers the case where there is not actual accumulation — no direct expansion of production — but a part of realized surplus-value is stored up over a longer or shorter time as a monetary reserve fund, later to be transformed into productive capital. If the money thus accumulated is extra money, this can only be a part of the additional gold supplied by the gold-producing countries. Note: the domestic products in return for which this gold is imported no longer exist in the country in question. They have been dispensed abroad in exchange for gold. If, on the other hand, the same quantity of money is in the country as before, then the money stored away has flowed in from circulation ; it is simply its function that has changed — transformed from circulating money into gradually formed latent money capital. The money stored up here is the money form of commodities that have been sold, and moreover of that portion of their value that represents surplus-value for their owner (with credit assumed non-existent). The capitalist who stores up money has to that extent sold without buying. As a partial phenomenon , there is nothing mysterious: one group of capitalists keeps back part of the money obtained from sales, instead of using it to withdraw products from the market; another group transforms money into products, except for the money capital needed to carry on production. A part of the product thrown onto the market as bearer of surplus-value consists of means of production, or of real elements of variable capital (means of subsistence). It can therefore immediately serve to expand production. This doesn't require one group to accumulate money while the other fully consumes — only that one group accumulates in the money form while the others actually expand production. The quantity of money present remains sufficient even if it is alternately one group that stores up money and the other that expands production, and vice versa. The storing up of money on one side can proceed even without cash, simply through the piling up of credit notes. But — and here Marx identifies the crucial difficulty — difficulties start to arise when we assume not partial accumulation of money capital but general accumulation within the capitalist class. Outside this class, on the assumption of universal and exclusive domination of capitalist production , there is no other class except the working class. The total purchases of the working class equal the sum of their wages, i.e. the sum of the variable capital advanced by the entire capitalist class. This money flows back to the capitalist class through the sale of product to the working class; their variable capital thereby receives its money form. If the sum of variable capital is x times £100 (note the analytic notation: x = number of turnovers or units), this is not the total variable capital advanced in the year, but only that applied — whether it is advanced with more or less money during the year, according to turnover speed, doesn't affect the current question. With this capital, the capitalist class buys a certain quantity of labour-power (first transaction). The workers buy a certain value of commodities from the capitalists, and the sum of x times £100 returns to the capitalists (second transaction). The process is constantly repeated. The sum of x times £100 can therefore never enable the working class to buy the part of the product containing the constant capital, let alone the surplus-value that belongs to the capitalists. The workers can buy with x times £100 only a portion of the value of the social product equal to the portion of value representing the variable capital advanced. This is an extremely important structural point — essentially an anticipation of the reproduction schemes' demonstration that workers' wages cannot buy back the full social product. It also has major implications for the critique of \"underconsumption\" and \"Say's Law\" positions alike: the working class as a class does not buy back the total product; the capitalist class must purchase the surplus product and the constant capital for the system to close. The general accumulation of money by the capitalist class requires not merely the circulation of the existing money but the existence of a demand from somewhere else. Apart from the case where this all-round monetary accumulation simply expresses the division between various individual capitalists of additional precious metal brought in, how else is the entire capitalist class to accumulate money? They would all have to have sold a part of their product without buying again. It is nothing mysterious that they all possess a certain money fund which they cast into the circulation sphere as means of circulation for their consumption, and of which each receives a part back again. But this monetary fund is then precisely a circulation fund, acquired by the conversion into money of surplus-value, and does not consist at all of latent money capital. So general hoarding is impossible within the closed capitalist class: money received by one capitalist is spent by another; any fund is circulation fund not hoard . This destroys the illusion that all capitalists can simultaneously and permanently \"store up\" their surplus-value as money. Beat 22 — Forms of Latent Money Capital in Real Life: Bank Deposits, Government Papers, Shares Marx then considers how latent money capital appears in real life (introducing credit after all): (1) Bank deposits. The money the banks really dispose of is a relatively small sum. Here it is only nominally stored up as money capital. What is really stored up are monetary claims which are only convertible (to the extent that they ever are converted) because there is a balance between money drawn out and money put in. The money in the hands of the bank is relatively only a small sum. This is a strikingly modern observation on fractional-reserve banking. (2) Government papers. These are not capital at all, but simply outstanding claims on the nation's annual product. (3) Shares. Leaving aside the fraudulent ones, these are titles of ownership to a real capital belonging to a corporate body, and drafts on the surplus-value that flows in from this each year. Note: shares do represent real capital, unlike government paper. This distinction is a rudimentary but important contribution to the theory of fictitious capital. In all these cases, there is no storage of money, and what appears on the one hand as storage of money capital appears on the other hand as the continuous real expenditure of money. Whether the money is spent by the person it belongs to or by people in debt to him does not affect the situation. On the basis of capitalist production, the formation of a hoard as such is never a purpose, but rather a result — a result either of a stagnation in circulation (greater quantities of money than usual assume the hoard form) or of the storage required by the turnover . The hoard can also be simply a formation of money capital, in the latent form for the time being, but destined to function as productive capital. If on the one hand, therefore, a part of the surplus-value realized in money is withdrawn from circulation and stored up as a hoard, at the same time a further part of the surplus-value is always transformed into productive capital. With the exception of the division of additional precious metal among the capitalist class, storage in the money form never occurs simultaneously at all points. So the system as a whole never hoards. Individual hoarding is always counterbalanced by others' investment or consumption. This conclusion is the systematic end-point of the chapter: the notion of a universally hoarding capitalist class is incoherent. Marx's final paragraph notes a broader feature of the analysis: \"The same applies to that part of the annual product which represents surplus-value in the commodity form, as applies to the rest of the annual product. A certain sum of money is required for its circulation. This sum of money belongs just as much to the capitalist class as does the annually produced mass of commodities that represents surplus-value. It was originally cast into circulation by the capitalist class itself. It is continuously divided among them afresh by circulation. Just as with the circulation of coin in general, a part of this monetary surplus-value is held up, at ever changing points, while a further part is always circulating. Whether some of this storage is deliberate, in order to form money capital, does not affect the situation in any way.\" Finally, the chapter concludes with a methodological caveat: \"Here we have disregarded the vicissitudes of circulation, in which one capitalist seizes for himself a piece of another's surplus-value, and even of his capital, and there is therefore a one-sided accumulation and centralization of both money capital and productive capital. A part of the extorted surplus-value that A stores up as money capital may thus be a fragment of B's surplus-value that has failed to return to him.\" This final observation gestures toward Volume III: circulation is not only a mechanism of realization but of redistribution, seizure, and centralization. Some capitalists' hoards are literally others' losses. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed Principle 1: The independence of the money-quantity requirement from the surplus-value content of commodities. The chapter's central technical result — that a given commodity value requires a determinate quantity of circulating money irrespective of how it divides into constant capital, variable capital, and surplus-value — dissolves the supposedly special problem of \"where the money comes from to realize surplus-value.\" This is a profound result because it demonstrates that distribution (the split of value among classes) is analytically separable from circulation (the technical movement of commodities and money). The apparent paradox of capitalist accumulation — that the capitalist class always withdraws more money than it casts in — is shown to be a false paradox generated by confusing the starting-point of circulation with the source of money . Once we recognize that the capitalist class casts money into circulation both as capital and as revenue , the paradox disappears: the money that realizes surplus-value is the money the capitalist class spends on its own consumption. Principle 2: The strict distinction between advancing money as capital and spending money as revenue. This distinction is the analytical pivot of the chapter. It separates two identical movements of money (payment) whose economic significance is completely different: in the first case the money is advanced in the expectation of return as capital plus surplus-value; in the second case the money is spent in the expectation of return as the money form of already-produced surplus-value . This distinction will be fundamental to Marx's later theory of crisis, credit, and the cycle, because the rhythm and volume of revenue-spending versus capital-advance are different, and can fall out of synchronization. Principle 3: The complementarity of gold production and commodity production within a closed monetary system. The system works because, at the level of the whole capitalist class , the mass of commodity value cast into circulation (which exceeds the money advanced) is balanced by the mass of money cast into circulation by gold producers (which exceeds the commodity value they withdraw). This is a structural insight of great elegance: the social money stock is reproduced by a continuous two-way flow, one side contributing commodities, the other contributing money. It is the monetary expression of the fact that a portion of social labour must always be devoted to producing the medium of circulation itself — a faux frais that Marx explicitly acknowledges. Principle 4: The material and institutional forms of latent money capital. Latent money capital is not primarily physical coin; it consists of bank deposits, government paper, and shares — i.e. legal claims on future production. This dissolves the crude image of hoarding as a stock of gold in a vault. In reality, \"storage\" of money capital and \"expenditure\" of money capital are two sides of the same coin: the money that A deposits is lent to B and spent by C. This unity-in-difference of hoarding and investment is the conceptual bridge from Volume II to Volume III's theory of the credit system, interest-bearing capital, and fictitious capital. Principle 5: The impossibility of universal hoarding within the capitalist class. Because the working class's aggregate purchasing power is limited to variable capital, and because the capitalist class's own purchases (constant capital + surplus-value revenue) must be internally balanced, there can be no general storage of money by all capitalists simultaneously. What appears as hoarding at one point is always investment or consumption at another point. This principle undermines any theory that treats hoarding as a systemic tendency. Principle 6: The real wealth/productive-power distinction. Drawn from William Thompson, this principle asserts that the \"accumulated wealth\" of society is overwhelmingly nominal — legal claims on future productive powers, not physical assets. The productive powers of society, by contrast, are incalculably larger. This has dramatic implications for political economy: the true measure of social wealth is not the stock of accumulated capital but the annual productive power of labour, and the distribution of the annual product (not the ownership of the accumulated stock) should be the primary object of political economy. Principle 7: The material-historical precondition of the precious-metals supply. Capitalist production on a large scale presupposes a supply of monetary metal sufficient for circulation and for hoard formation. The sixteenth-century bullion influx was therefore a decisive historical moment in the development of capitalism. This embeds monetary history within the historical materialism of the transition to capitalism. Principle 8: The critique of the wage-cost-push theory of prices. A general wage rise need not — indeed cannot — raise the general price level, under the assumptions of unchanged productivity and unchanged value of money. It merely redistributes value between wages and profit, and between branches according to their organic composition. The three \"facts\" that are invoked as pretexts for the wage-push theory are each shown to be misreadings of superficially correlated phenomena. Broader Theoretical Implications For the theory of value. Chapter 17 extends the labour theory of value into the monetary sphere. The total value of commodities is determined by labour-time; the quantity of circulating money is determined by the value of commodities and the velocity of circulation. Surplus-value does not alter the quantity of money needed to circulate the commodities; it only determines how the value of the product is distributed. The chapter thus demonstrates that Marx's value theory is not a \"monetary\" theory in the naive quantity-theory sense but a theory of the social-relations that underlie monetary phenomena. For the theory of accumulation. Accumulation is not simply the conversion of surplus-value into productive capital. It is a staged, multi-form process : surplus-value is realized in money → some is consumed as revenue → some is held as latent money capital → latent money capital is placed on the money market → it is lent out to other capitalists → it is finally converted into productive capital. The \"identity\" of the capitalist who produces and the capitalist who accumulates is broken: a capitalist whose own surplus-value is not yet sufficient to expand may expand using other capitalists' surplus-value via the credit system. This is the theoretical basis for understanding the socialization of capital — the emergence of a class-level mechanism of accumulation in which individual capitalists are mere nodes in a collective process. For the theory of credit. The chapter's treatment of latent money capital and credit is foundational. Marx explicitly states that the credit system develops simultaneously with capitalist production and that it is not merely an accessory but a material precondition of production on the present scale. The \"pointless question\" of whether capitalism would be possible without credit is dismissed on the ground that the required metallic circulation would exceed the limits of precious-metal production. But Marx also warns against mystical fetishism of credit's \"productive power.\" Credit merely makes money capital fluid; it does not create value. For the reproduction schemes. The chapter's observation that the working class can buy back only a value equal to variable capital is one of the crucial presuppositions of the two-department reproduction schemes that follow (Chapters 18–20). In those schemes, Marx will show how the capitalist class itself must absorb the surplus product through productive and luxury consumption — and how the constant capital of each department must be exchanged for the means of consumption of workers and capitalists in the other department. Chapter 17 supplies the monetary foundation: the money for these inter-departmental exchanges is circulated by the capitalist class's own advance of revenue and capital. For the critique of political economy. The chapter mounts a sustained critique of bourgeois political economy on three fronts: (a) the Tooke debate about the hoard of money needed for realization; (b) the wage-cost-push apologetic; (c) the physiocratic concentration on the visible stock of wealth rather than the annual flow of production. Marx draws on pre-Marxian socialist critique (Thompson) to strengthen his anti-apologetic argument. Philosophical and Practical Implications The temporal constitution of capital. The chapter is fundamentally about time — turnover time, realization time, hoarding time, the time required for large-scale expansion. Capital is not a thing but a social relation that exists only in its own movement through time. Value must be realized in money before it can become revenue or capital; realization takes time; the time of realization is itself determined by the turnover period. Hence a slower turnover is not just less efficient but structurally different: it requires greater advances, greater reserves, deeper credit, and a greater capacity to live off accumulated funds. The materiality of money. Against both naive metallicism and pure nominalism, the chapter insists that money is both material (gold is a commodity, produced by labour) and social (it functions as money only because it is the universal equivalent). The gold producers' labour is identical in kind to other labour; what makes their product money is its role in the system, not its physical character. And yet the system cannot dispense with this material base: the \"limited scale of precious-metal production\" sets a real bound on metallic circulation, which is precisely why credit is materially necessary . This is a sophisticated dialectic of the material and the social. The illusory \"wealth\" of the rich. The Thompson quotations raise a theme that seems out of place in a technical chapter but is entirely consistent with Marx's project: the vast majority of what is called wealth is nothing but legal title to future production. To imagine that the rich are rich because they own \"things\" is an illusion. They are rich because they own claims on other people's future labour. This is the moral-philosophical heart of the chapter. It also grounds an important political implication: any serious politics of distribution must target the legal relations by which claims on future production are monopolized, not the physical stock of goods. The class-content of \"advance.\" The chapter repeatedly returns to the fact that the capitalist appears to \"advance\" what the workers have actually produced. In gold production this is directly visible: workers are paid with gold they themselves have just mined. In general, the \"advance\" of wages is a form that conceals the reality that the workers' own product sustains them. This is a restatement, in the monetary register, of the fundamental thesis of Volume I: the wage-form conceals the appropriation of unpaid labour. Historicity of the monetary framework. By treating the sufficient supply of precious metals as a historical precondition , and by dating the decisive moment to the sixteenth century, Marx embeds his analysis in an empirical-historical narrative. This is not a theory of an abstract capitalism but of a historically situated capitalism whose material preconditions were themselves historically contingent. Practical and Political Implications For trade-union strategy and the wage-struggle. The chapter arms labour with an argument against the wage-cost-push apologetic: a general rise in wages need not raise the general price level. It also warns that local wage increases can, in specific conditions, generate local price effects — a nuance that matters to any serious strategy of wage bargaining. The distinction between general and partial wage rises, between variable-capital-dominant and constant-capital-dominant branches, is directly relevant to how wage increases are absorbed. For understanding the money market and financial crisis. The chapter's treatment of latent money capital, bank deposits, and reserve funds provides the analytical skeleton for understanding how the financial system functions — and how it can malfunction. The \"double function\" of reserve funds (available for the owner, circulating for society) is a source of systemic fragility: the same money is claimed by two sets of actors at the same time. For the critique of contemporary \"financialization\" discourse. Marx's observation that latent money capital consists of claims , not things , and that what appears as hoarding is really expenditure, anticipates modern debates about the financial system as a system of claims on future production. Government paper is not capital; shares are titles to real capital. The distinction matters because it resists the reduction of all financial assets to a homogeneous \"wealth.\" For the theory of the state. Thompson's remark that a full third of annual output is appropriated under the name of \"public burdens\" and consumed unproductively directs attention to the fiscal dimension of the class struggle. Marx cites it without comment, but its inclusion within a chapter on monetary circulation suggests that the state's absorption of value must be analytically integrated into the circulation of surplus-value. Concluding Reflection Chapter 17 is, in the final analysis, a philosophical meditation on the nature of capitalist wealth . It tells us that wealth in capitalism is not a stock of things but a claim on a flow; that the flow is produced by living labour; that the \"advances\" of the capitalist are only formal appearances behind which the workers' product circulates; that the money form is both necessary (material foundations in precious-metal production) and illusory (claims on future production often mistaken for present wealth); and that the system as a whole is articulated by a delicate, unstable, but structurally necessary circulation of money that no individual capitalist commands but that all collectively produce. The chapter thus closes Volume II's analysis of the process of circulation of capital with a demonstration that the social and monetary character of capital is irreducible to its individual form. The secret of surplus-value is produced in production; but the secret of its circulation — and hence of the possibility of accumulation — lies in a monetary architecture that no single capitalist can control. That architecture will be the subject of Volume III. The dictum that captures the chapter best is Marx's own, quoted from Volume I and repeated here: \"Circulation sweats money from every pore.\" What is hidden within this poetic formulation — and what Chapter 17 has established with relentless analytical precision — is that the money that sweats from every pore is the collective product of the working class, that it circulates between capital and labour in a rhythm set by the turnover period, and that its social destination is the expansion of capital. No mystery, then, surrounds the money that realizes surplus-value: it is simply the money of the capitalist class itself, advanced as revenue, returning as the money form of its surplus, and — through the credit system and the gold mines — continuously reproduced so that the process may begin again. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 16 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 18"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 18", "title": "Chapter 18", "subtitle": "Introduction (Part III: The Reproduction and Circulation of Aggregate Social Capital)", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 18/Chapter 18 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 5657, "read_time": "28 min read", "tags": ["Social Reproduction Schemes (Dept I & II)", "Circuits of Capital (M-C-M')"], "keywords": ["Introduction to Social Capital Reproduction", "Individual Capital vs Total Social Capital", "Money Circulation Intertwined with Commodity Circulation", "Role of Means of Production in Social Turnover", "Total Annual Commodity Product Breakdown"], "text": "Chapter 18 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,623 words ⏱️ ~28 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 18 opens Part Three of Volume 2 of Capital and functions as a deliberate theoretical threshold. Its central problem is deceptively simple yet foundational to everything that follows: how does the analysis of individual capital — the object of Parts One and Two — relate to the analysis of the total social capital ? Every earlier volume and every earlier part of Volume 2 treated a capital in isolation, \"an autonomous part of the social capital.\" Now Marx announces that the object of inquiry shifts upward in scale: we must consider \"the circulation process of the individual capitals as components of the total social capital, i.e. the circulation process of this total social capital.\" The intellectual objective is therefore architectonic . Marx is not introducing a new empirical fact so much as a new level of abstraction . Individual capitals are not merely summed; they are interlinked , they \"presuppose one another and condition one another,\" and it is precisely this interlinking that \"constitutes the movement of the total social capital.\" This is the pivot on which the entire later theory of social reproduction and, by extension, the reproduction schemas of the chapters that follow, will turn. The chapter's core thesis is twofold. First, the overall reproduction process of social capital is broader than the circuits of individual capitals. It necessarily incorporates not only the specific circuit of capital (production and circulation of value and surplus-value) but also commodity circulation in general — the sphere of purchases and sales by which workers spend wages and capitalists spend surplus-value. This \"individual consumption\" is not part of the circulation of capital narrowly defined, yet it is indispensable to the totality. Second, the role of money capital must be clarified precisely because it appears to impose an external limit on production. Marx demonstrates that although every capital must begin in the money form — money is the \"prime mover\" and \"permanent driving force\" — the scale of production is emphatically not determined by the volume of money capital. The productive powers latent in a given capital value are \"elastic and variable,\" their limits not given by the value-forms that advance them. The chapter thus dissolves a powerful illusion: that money capital constitutes the boundary of capitalist production. It does not; only the material character of the labour process (notably the length of the working period) and the social form of production jointly determine how much money capital is required. The chapter's broader framework is unmistakable. It bridges the microscopic analysis of turnover developed in Parts One and Two with the macroscopic analysis of social reproduction. It also plants the seed for the later, explicitly socialist/post-capitalist speculation: under collective production, \"money capital is completely dispensed with,\" and society allocates labour-power and means of production directly, distributing consumption tokens that \"are not money; they do not circulate.\" In sum, the chapter reframes the unit of analysis, clarifies the function of money in the aggregate circuit, and establishes that the limits of capitalist production are material and social — never monetary in the last instance. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Object of the Inquiry: Capital's Two Moments Marx begins by restating the elementary grammar of his system. The immediate production process of capital is its \"process of labour and valorization,\" whose result is the commodity product and whose \"determining motive\" is the production of surplus-value . This is the foundation laid down in Volume 1. But immediately the scope widens: the process of capital's reproduction \"includes, on top of this immediate production process, the specific process of circulation with its two phases.\" The two phases are the classic conversion of commodity capital into money capital (C'–M') and money capital back into productive elements (M–C), the sale and the re-purchase that restore the capital to its productive form. Reproduction is thus the \"overall circuit forming the turnover of capital,\" \"a periodic process that is constantly repeated afresh at definite intervals.\" Here the concept of the turnover — introduced and dissected in Part Two — is quietly reinstated as the minimal unit of analysis. Crucially, Marx emphasizes a point of structural symmetry: whether we examine the circuit in the form M…M' (money capital as the starting and ending point) or P…P (productive capital as the starting and ending point), the immediate production process P \"never forms more than one term in this circuit.\" In the M…M' form, production mediates circulation; in the P…P form, circulation mediates production. Neither form grants production an absolute primacy in the phenomenal sequence; the two are dialectically interwoven. Yet a hierarchy is preserved: \"the constant repetition of the process of production is the condition for the transformations that the capital undergoes over and again in the circulation sphere.\" Production, while not the only term, remains the enabling condition of circulation's persistent reproduction. The Individual Capital as a Fraction of the Social Capital The chapter now executes its pivotal move. Each individual capital \"forms only a fraction of the total social capital, a fraction that has acquired independence and been endowed with individual life.\" The analogy is deliberately drawn with the class relation itself: \"each individual capitalist is no more than an element of the capitalist class.\" Just as a single commodity's metamorphosis is one term in the \"series of metamorphoses of the commodity world as a whole,\" so a single capital's turnover is \"a single term in the circuit of the social capital.\" This is not a mere summation. The movement of the social capital is \"the totality of movements of these autonomous fractions.\" The whole is composed of parts that retain apparent autonomy — individual capitals competing, making independent decisions — yet are bound together by mutual presupposition. This is the theoretical crux of the chapter, and it will later generate the famous reproduction schemas (Departments I and II). Marx is preparing the reader to think in terms of aggregate flows: the sum of individual turnovers forms the total social turnover, but with emergent properties not visible at the level of the isolated capital. The Dual Consumption of the Overall Process Marx insists that the overall process, \"taken in its entirety,\" involves two distinct kinds of consumption . On the one hand there is productive consumption — the immediate production process together with the changes of form that mediate it (which, \"considered in their material aspect,\" are exchanges). On the other hand there is individual consumption with its own mediating exchanges. This twofold character generates a twofold role for the human agents. In productive consumption, the worker \"enters the scene as the seller of his commodity, labour-power, and the capitalist as its buyer.\" This is the conversion of variable capital into labour-power and the \"consequent incorporation of labour-power into the capitalist production process.\" But there is a second circuit. The sale of commodities \"involves their purchase by the working class, i.e. the workers' individual consumption.\" Here the roles reverse: \"the working class appears as a buyer of commodities, and the capitalists as sellers of commodities to the workers.\" This doubled appearance — worker as seller then buyer, capitalist as buyer then seller — is the material form in which wage-labour reproduces itself as a class. The importance of the point is that the workers' consumption is not an incidental by-product; it is a necessary condition for capital's continuance, since the reconstitution of labour-power depends on it. Yet Marx is careful to distinguish this from capital circulation proper. The Circulation of Surplus-Value and the Two Components of Commodity Circulation A further layer is added: the circulation of commodity capital \"involves the circulation of surplus-value,\" and hence the purchases and sales by which capitalists mediate \"their individual consumption, the consumption of surplus-value.\" The fine distinction to retain is that the revenue expenditures of both classes — wages and surplus-value spent as income — do not form part of the circulation of capital, even though the spending of wages \"at least, depends on this circulation.\" This is a crucial analytical boundary: capital flow and revenue flow are conceptually separable even while empirically entangled. Marx then states the result with almost axiomatic brevity. The circuits of individual capitals, \"when considered as combined into the social capital,\" encompass not only the circulation of capital but commodity circulation in general , which has \"in its fundamentals\" only two components: (1) \"the specific circuit of capital,\" and (2) \"the circuit of those commodities that go into individual consumption, i.e. the commodities on which the workers spend their wages and the capitalists their surplus-value.\" This binary division is the seed of the two-department model. The circuit of capital itself \"comprises the circulation of surplus-value, in as much as this forms part of the commodity capital,\" and \"similarly includes the transformation of variable capital into labour-power, the payment of wages.\" But the expenditure of surplus-value and wages on commodities lies outside the circulation of capital. The unity of the whole is therefore a unity of distinct circuits, not a homogeneous mass. The Retrospective: Volume 1 and Parts One and Two as Analysis of Individual Capital Marx then walks the reader through the methodological history of the preceding analyses, clarifying what was assumed and what was deferred. In Volume 1 , \"the capitalist production process was analysed both as an isolated event and as a process of reproduction.\" Two abstractions were made: it was assumed \"that the capitalist sells the product at its value\" and that \"he finds in the circulation sphere the material means of production that he needs to begin the process anew.\" In other words, the circulation sphere was treated as a frictionless presupposition. The only circulation-sphere act requiring examination in Volume 1 was \"the purchase and sale of labour-power as the basic condition of capitalist production.\" In Part One of Volume 2, Marx considered \"the various forms that capital assumes in its circuit,\" adding circulation time to the working time of Volume 1. In Part Two , the circuit was treated as periodic, i.e. as turnover . Here two advances were made. First, it was shown \"how the various components of capital (fixed and circulating) complete the circuit of their forms at different intervals and in different ways,\" along with the causes of differing working periods and circulation periods . Second, the influence of periodicity and of the varying ratio of components \"on the scale of the production process itself, and on the annual rate of surplus-value\" was indicated. The conceptual contrast between Parts One and Two is stated sharply: Part One dealt with \"the successive forms that capital constantly assumes and discards\"; Part Two dealt with how, \"within this flux and succession of forms, a capital of given size is simultaneously divided\" into productive, money, and commodity capital, so that \"different parts of the total capital value exist and function in these different states alongside each other at any one time.\" Money capital, in particular, \"presented specific features that were not indicated in Volume 1,\" and \"certain laws were discovered\" governing how major components must \"constantly be advanced and renewed in the form of money capital\" to keep production running. Yet the limitation of both parts is now named: \"What we were dealing with in both Parts One and Two, however, was always no more than an individual capital.\" The Transitional Thesis: Interlinked Circuits Having established the limitation, Marx states the new object with precision. The circuits of individual capitals \"are interlinked, they presuppose one another and condition one another, and it is precisely by being interlinked in this way that they constitute the movement of the total social capital.\" The analogy with simple commodity circulation is again invoked: just as the metamorphosis of a single commodity is one term in the metamorphoses of the commodity world, \"now the metamorphosis of the individual capital appears as one term in the series of metamorphoses of the social capital.\" A subtle but decisive qualification follows. Simple commodity circulation \"in no way necessarily involved the circulation of capital — since it can proceed quite well on the basis of non-capitalist production.\" By contrast, the circuit of the total social capital \"also involves a commodity circulation that does not fall within the circuit of any individual capital, i.e. the circulation of those commodities that do not form capital.\" This asymmetry is crucial: capitalism both presupposes pre-existing commodity circulation and incorporates a distinct stratum of commodity circulation (revenue purchases) that no single capital's circuit contains. The chapter thus ends its first section with the formal statement of the new object: \"the circulation process of the individual capitals as components of the total social capital,\" which \"taken in its entirety\" is \"a form of the reproduction process.\" The Role of Money Capital: Two Aspects Marx opens section 2 with a methodological aside of some candour: the subject \"really belongs in a later section of this Part. However, we intend to investigate it here.\" This footnote-like admission signals the essayistic origin of the text (it is drawn from Manuscript 11) and marks the section as preparatory. The task is to explain money capital considered as a component of the total social capital . Two aspects of money capital emerge from the earlier analysis of turnover. First , money capital \"provides the form in which each individual capital steps onto the scene and commences its process as capital.\" It is thus \"prime mover, giving the first impulse to the whole process.\" Second , according to the varying lengths of the turnover period and the ratio of its components, the portion of capital value that \"must be advanced and renewed at any one time in the money form stands in a different ratio to the productive capital that it sets in motion.\" The critical consequence is a restriction : \"the portion of the capital value in process that can function at any one time as productive capital is always restricted by the part of the capital value advanced that must always exist alongside the productive capital in the money form.\" Here is the germ of a claim about money capital as a bound on production — but it is immediately qualified. Marx specifies that what is involved is \"simply the normal turnover, an abstract average,\" and that \"we therefore disregard any additional money capital required to cope with delays in circulation.\" The analysis proceeds on the assumption of smooth, average turnover. The First Aspect: Money as Prime Mover On the first point , Marx develops a chain of presuppositions. \"Commodity production presupposes commodity circulation, and commodity circulation presupposes the representation of commodities in money, monetary circulation.\" The \"duplication of commodities into commodities and money\" is \"a law of the emergence of the product as a commodity.\" This is a philosophical restatement: the existence of money is not an accident of history but a necessary form of the commodity as a social relation. In a footnote, Marx refers the reader back to Volume 1, Chapter 2 (\"Exchange\"). Capitalist commodity production, \"whether we consider it socially or individually,\" likewise presupposes money capital — \"both as the prime mover for each business when it first begins, and as a permanent driving force.\" Circulating capital , especially, \"presupposes the constantly repeated appearance, at short intervals, of the motor of money capital.\" The point is that money is not only the initial endowment but a recurring imperative: every round of turnover must begin again in the money form. \"The entire capital value advanced, i.e. all components of the capital that consist of commodities — labour-power, means of labour and materials of production — must always first be bought with money and later on purchased again.\" And \"what holds here for the individual capital, holds also for the social capital, which operates only in the form of many individual capitals.\" But then the dialectical reversal. \"As we already showed in Volume 1, it in no way follows from this that the field of operation of capital, the scale of production, even on the capitalist basis, has its absolute limits determined by the volume of money capital in operation.\" The apparent restriction is not a real limit. This is the chapter's most important negative thesis. The Elasticity of Productive Powers Marx now marshals a systematic demonstration that the productive powers embodied in a given money capital exceed the bounds of that capital's own value. Four main cases are given. First, labour-power. \"Labour-power with a certain rate of payment may be more or less severely exploited, both extensively and intensively.\" If the money capital increases with severer exploitation, i.e. \"if wages rise, they still do not rise in relation to the degree of exploitation, i.e. not at all proportionately.\" The quantity of labour extracted can rise without a proportional rise in money advanced. This is the theory of surplus-value operating at the margin of the money-form. Second, natural materials. Soil, sea, mineral ores, forests \"may be more or less severely exploited, in extent and intensity, by greater exertion of the same amount of labour-power, without an increase in the money capital advanced.\" These natural materials \"do not form an element of the capital's value\" — a crucial reminder that nature's productive contribution is unpaid. \"The real elements of productive capital can be increased without the need for additional money capital.\" Where additional ancillaries are needed, \"the money capital in which the capital value is advanced is not increased in relation to the increased effectiveness of the productive capital, i.e. again not at all proportionately.\" Third, fixed capital. \"The same means of labour, i.e. the same fixed capital, may be used more efficiently without an additional outlay of money for fixed capital,\" either by prolonging daily use or by raising intensity. The result is \"only a more rapid turnover of the fixed capital,\" which means \"the elements of its reproduction are supplied more quickly.\" Fourth, natural forces. \"Natural forces that cost nothing may also be incorporated more or less effectively as agents in the production process,\" their effectiveness depending \"on methods and scientific advances that cost the capitalist nothing.\" Fifth, the social combination of labour and accumulated skill. This last case leads naturally into a polemic. The Carey Polemic and the Capital in the Soil Marx interrupts the exposition to attack Henry Charles Carey , identified in the footnote as \"an American 'vulgar economist' and champion of the 'harmony of interests' between opposing classes.\" Carey reckons that the landowner never receives his due rent because he is not paid for all the capital and labour put into the soil from time immemorial to give it its present productive capacity. Marx adds sardonically: \"(There is no mention, of course, of the productive capacity that is taken out of the soil.)\" The parallel extension follows: \"the individual worker should be paid according to the work that it cost the entire human race to build him up from a savage into a modern engineer.\" Marx's rebuttal is economic: \"What actually happens is quite the reverse. If all the unpaid labour put into the soil to the profit of the landowners and capitalists is counted up, then this capital contained in the soil has been repaid over and over again at an extortionate rate of interest, so that landed property has long since been redeemed by society, and redeemed time and again at that.\" The argument is that rent represents a perpetual re-extraction of value already repaid; the landowner is not an unrecognized creditor but a recurring beneficiary of unpaid labour. The insert is not merely polemical. It reinforces the chapter's central claim about the disproportion between value advanced and wealth produced. \"The increase in the productive forces of labour, in so far as this does not rest on any additional outlay of capital value, increases in the first instance only the quantity of products, and not their value, except to the extent that it enables more constant capital to be reproduced, and its value thus maintained, with the same amount of labour.\" Crucially, however, such an increase \"also forms extra material for capital, and thus provides the basis for an increased capital accumulation.\" This is the passage connecting the argument about elasticity to the theory of accumulation: free productive gains enlarge the material basis on which capital grows, without any proportional monetary advance. Centralization and the Money-Form Marx recalls a further argument from Volume 1: the organization of social labour on a large scale requires \"the advance of money capital in great quantities by individual capitalists,\" and this is achieved \"partly as a result of the centralization of capitals in a few hands, without any absolute growth in the volume of capital values in operation, and nor therefore in the volume of money capital through which they are advanced.\" \"The size of individual capitals can grow by way of centralization in a few hands, without any growth in their social sum. There is then simply a redistribution of individual capitals.\" Centralization — a category from the theory of accumulation — reappears here as a mechanism that decouples scale of operation from volume of money capital. Finally, from the immediately preceding Part, Marx recalls that \"a reduction in the turnover period enables either the same productive capital to be set in motion with less money capital, or more productive capital to be set in motion with the same money capital.\" The General Conclusion on Money Capital and the Scale of Production Marx draws the section together. \"All this clearly has nothing to do with the specific question of money capital as such.\" What it indicates is that the capital advanced — \"a given sum of value which, in its free form, its value form, consists of a certain sum of money — contains, once it has been transformed into productive capital, productive powers whose limits are not given by the bounds of its own value, but, within a given field of action, can operate differently, both in extent and intensity.\" Two distinct quantities must therefore be distinguished. Once prices of the elements of production (means of production and labour-power) are given, \"the size of the money capital required to buy a certain quantity of these elements of production, present as commodities, is also determined. In other words, the capital value that has to be advanced is determined.\" However , \"the scale on which this capital operates to form values and products is elastic and variable.\" The chapter thereby balances a strict determination (money capital required) with a flexible operation (product and value creation), and so defuses any attempt to derive a theory of capitalist limits from the quantity of money. The Second Aspect: Money Creation as a Deduction from Social Production Marx turns to a second, more aggregate consideration. \"It is self-evident that the portion of social labour and means of production that has to be spent each year on the production or acquisition of money, in order to replace worn-down coins, involves a proportionate reduction in the scale of social production.\" Here is a genuine, if modest, cost: the wear and replacement of coinage diverts labour and means of production from other uses. But Marx immediately relativizes it: \"as for the money value already functioning partly as means of circulation and partly as a hoard, this has already been obtained, and exists alongside the labour-power, the produced means of production, and the natural sources of wealth. It cannot be considered as a restriction on these.\" The existing stock of money is a datum , part of the world's wealth, not a limit upon it. \"If it is transformed into elements of production, or exchanged with other nations, the scale of production may be expanded. But this presupposes that the money continues to play, as before, its role of world money.\" A footnote refers back to Volume 1, Chapter 3, section 3c, on world money . The category of world money reappears to underline that even at the level of the world economy, money functions as a universal equivalent medium and does not constitute an absolute ceiling on material wealth. The Length of the Turnover Period and the Money Form Marx then returns to the dependence of required money capital on the turnover period. \"According to the length of the turnover period, a greater or lesser quantity of money capital is needed to set the productive capital in motion.\" And again from the preceding analysis: \"the division of the turnover period into working time and circulation time gives rise to an increase in the capital that is latent or suspended in the money form.\" Crucially, \"to the extent that the turnover period is governed by the length of the working period, it is determined by the material character of the production process, conditions remaining otherwise the same, and not by the specific social character of this production process.\" This is a pivotal distinction. The working period — the time required by the material nature of the labour process to produce a finished product (e.g. the maturing of crops, the building of a ship) — is a natural-technical determinant, not a social one. On the basis of capitalist production, \"extended operations of long duration require greater advances of money capital for a longer time. Production in these branches is therefore dependent on the extent of the money capital which the individual capitalist has at his disposal.\" Here, at last, money capital appears as a real barrier — but a private, not a social, one. Credit, Joint-Stock Companies, and Money-Market Disturbances How is the private barrier overcome? \"This limit is overcome by the credit system and the forms of association related to it, e.g. joint-stock companies.\" The credit system and the joint-stock form are thus theorized as institutional responses to the mismatch between the material duration of certain labour processes and the monetary capacity of individual capitalists. But the solution breeds new vulnerabilities: \"Disturbances in the money market therefore bring such businesses to a halt, while those same businesses, for their part, induce disturbances in the money market.\" Here Marx articulates a dialectic of dependency and causation: long-duration production is sensitive to credit conditions and simultaneously a source of financial instability. This prefigures later analyses of financial crisis and the specific vulnerability of long-gestation industries. Social Production and the Elimination of Money Capital Marx then projects the analysis beyond capitalism. \"On the basis of social production, it would be necessary to determine to what extent it was possible to pursue these operations, which withdraw labour-power and means of production for a relatively long period without providing any product or useful effect during this time, without damaging those branches of production that not only withdraw labour-power and means of production continuously or several times in the course of a year, but also supply means of subsistence and means of production.\" The problem is thus framed as a material one. \"With social production just as with capitalist production, workers in branches of industry with short working periods will withdraw products only for a short time without giving other products back in return, while branches of industry with long working periods will continue to withdraw products for a long time before they give anything back.\" And this occurs \"from the material conditions of the labour process in question, and not from its social form.\" The material structure of long- versus short-gestation production persists irrespective of the social form. But the form of mediation changes decisively. \"With collective production, money capital is completely dispensed with. The society distributes labour-power and means of production between the various branches of industry. There is no reason why the producers should not receive paper tokens permitting them to withdraw an amount corresponding to their labour time from the social consumption stocks. But these tokens are not money; they do not circulate.\" This is one of the most theoretically loaded passages in the chapter. Under collective production, allocation is conscious and direct; consumption claims are recorded in non-circulating tokens (a proto-anticipation of labour certificates in later socialist theory). The distinction between a circulating money and a non-circulating accounting token is insisted upon: the latter lacks the self-expanding, universal equivalent character of money and therefore does not constitute money capital. The Two Factors Conditioning the Need for Money Capital The chapter closes by summarising the conditions under which the need for money capital arises from the working period. \"We see that in so far as the need for money capital arises from the length of the working period, this is conditioned by two factors.\" First , \"that money is the general form in which each individual capital must enter the scene (leaving aside credit), in order to be transformed into productive capital; this arises from the nature of capitalist production, and of commodity production in general.\" This is a form-determinate necessity: capital must begin in money because the elements of production are commodities. Second , \"the size of the money advance needed arises from the circumstance that labour-power and means of production are withdrawn from society for a long period without the return of a product that can be transformed back into money.\" This is a material necessity: long-gestation production ties up resources for an extended period before any return. The final sentences distinguish the fate of each factor. \"The first factor, that the capital has to be advanced in the form of money, is not abolished by the form this money takes, whether it is metallic money, credit money, tokens of value, etc.\" In other words, credit and its innovations change the vehicle but not the necessity of a monetary advance . \"The second factor is not affected in any way either by the monetary medium or by the form of production in which labour, means of subsistence and means of production are withdrawn, without an equivalent being cast back into circulation.\" The material constraint, the temporal withdrawal-and-return pattern of long-duration production, is invariant across forms — capitalist or collective, metallic or credit. The chapter thus ends with two cleanly separated necessities: one historically specific (the money-form of the advance) and one materially universal (the withdrawal-return profile of long working periods). This separation is the analytical gift the chapter hands to the theory of social reproduction that follows. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter yields a set of fundamental principles that govern everything thereafter. Chief among them is the shift in unit of analysis : from the individual capital to the total social capital as the interlinked totality of autonomously-managed fractions. This is not a matter of simple aggregation; the whole has properties the parts lack. Specifically, the total circulation encompasses not only the circuit of capital but also the revenue circulation of wages and surplus-value — a sphere lying outside any single capital's circuit yet necessary to the whole. This conceptual bifurcation, between capital circulation and general commodity circulation, is the indispensable precondition for the reproduction schemas of the following chapters. A second principle is the non-monetary character of capitalism's fundamental limits . Money is the form of the advance, not the measure of its productive potential. The demonstration that labour-power, natural materials, fixed capital, natural forces, and social cooperation can all be exploited more intensively without proportional monetary increases establishes that the limits of production lie in the material conditions of the labour process and in the social relations of production — not in the quantity of money. \"The scale on which this capital operates to form values and products is elastic and variable.\" This dissolves the monetarist illusion that the volume of money determines the volume of production; money is the necessary vehicle, never the boundary. A third principle is the material/formal distinction that runs through the chapter's conclusion. The length of the working period is materially determined — it arises from \"the material character of the production process,\" not its \"specific social character.\" This material determinant persists under capitalist and collective production alike. But the form of mediation differs: under capitalism, long working periods require large private advances of money capital, generating dependence on the credit system and joint-stock companies and, transitively, on the fragility of the money market. Under collective production, society consciously allocates labour-power and means of production and distributes non-circulating consumption tokens , in which \"money capital is completely dispensed with.\" The broader implications are several. First, Marx diagnoses the credit system as a dialectical response to a material mismatch: long-duration industries need more money than individual capitalists can supply, so credit and joint-stock association arise — yet this very solution creates systemic vulnerability, since \"disturbances in the money market bring such businesses to a halt, while those same businesses… induce disturbances in the money market.\" Here is an early, trenchant theory of financial fragility rooted in the temporal structure of production. Second, the Carey polemic and the discussion of unpaid natural and social productivity serve to underscore the disproportion between value advanced and wealth produced — the theme of unpaid labour (both in the worker and in the soil) that grounds the theory of rent, surplus-value, and accumulation. The \"extra material for capital\" created by free productivity gains is the material basis for expanded accumulation without proportional monetary investment. Third, the passage on collective production sketches the outlines of an economy without money capital . Society allocates resources directly; producers receive token claims measurable in labour time; but these tokens \"are not money; they do not circulate.\" This signals that Marx's critique of political economy is oriented toward a mode of production in which the form-determinate necessity (the money-advance) is eliminated, while the material constraint (long gestation periods) remains a planning problem to be solved by conscious social allocation. The chapter thus delivers both a rigorous closure of the individual-capital analysis and a rigorous opening onto the aggregate, necessarily pointing beyond the horizon of the capitalist mode of production. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 17 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 19"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 19", "title": "Chapter 19", "subtitle": "Former Presentations of the Subject", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 19/Chapter 19 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 20518, "read_time": "103 min read", "tags": ["Social Reproduction Schemes (Dept I & II)", "Critique of the Smithian Dogma"], "keywords": ["Former Presentations of the Subject", "Quesnay's Tableau Économique", "The Smithian Dogma: Resolution of All Value into v + s", "Disappearance of Constant Capital (c) in Bourgeois Economics", "Say, Mill, and Vulgar Inheritors of Smith's Dogma", "Elimination of Gross National Product Categories"], "text": "Chapter 19 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 20,443 words ⏱️ ~102 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 19: Former Presentations of the Subject — An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem Chapter 19 of Marx's Theories of Surplus-Value (the second volume of the Theories , and evidently the opening of Manuscript VIII of the 1861–63 Economics Manuscript ) is the historic-critical hinge of Marx's entire theory of reproduction. Its core objective is deceptively modest in formulation but revolutionary in implication: to demonstrate that every major economist before Marx — from Quesnay through Adam Smith and down to John Stuart Mill — failed to correctly conceptualize how the constant capital component of the annual social product is reproduced. The chapter arises at a moment when Marx has already, in his own analytical work, discovered the two-departmental structure of social reproduction (Department I, means of production; Department II, means of consumption), and now turns to examine how his predecessors handled — or mishandled — the same terrain. The purpose is doubly polemical: to rescue what is correct in the Physiocrats and Smith, and to destroy the \"dogma\" that commodity value resolves entirely into wages plus surplus-value (v + s). The chapter's organizing thesis can be stated in one proposition: Adam Smith's theory that the price of every commodity — and hence the value of the annual social product — \"resolves itself\" into wages, profit, and rent (that is, into v + s) is false, because it omits the constant capital component (c), which is not produced in the current year but merely reappears in a new natural form. This omission flows from two deeper confusions: (1) the identification of the value of the annual product with the annual value product, and (2) the failure to distinguish the twofold character of labour as value-creating and use-value-creating. Everything else in the chapter — the treatment of Quesnay, the dissolution of Smith's \"gross and net revenue\" distinction, the demolition of Smith's corn-horse-Pebble proofs, the survey of Ricardo, Say, Storch, Sismondi, Barton, Ramsay, Cherbuliez, and Mill — is a working out of the consequences of this fundamental diagnosis. The Intellectual Objective The intellectual objective is not merely negative. Marx is doing something constructive at the same time. He is establishing, against the tradition, that: 1. The annual social product must be analyzed in two departments, because the material form in which value exists (means of production vs. means of consumption) determines whether that value can function as revenue or must function as capital. 2. Even on the basis of simple reproduction — the supposedly simplest case — the analysis of value must include the constant capital component. 3. The categories of \"revenue\" and \"capital\" are not originally constitutive of commodity value; rather, commodity value is the precondition of revenue. Smith inverted this relationship, making revenues the \"original sources of all exchangeable value,\" and thereby opened the door to vulgar economics. Placement in the Broader Theoretical Framework This chapter is the culminating critical complement to the reproduction schemas of Capital Volume 2, Part 3. In the published Capital , Marx presents his own positive solution in the famous formulas: Department I: c₁ + v₁ + s₁ = total product of means of production Department II: c₂ + v₂ + s₂ = total product of means of consumption with the equilibrium conditions c₂ = v₁ + s₁ (Department II's constant capital must equal Department I's wage-and-surplus bill) and c₁ + v₁ + s₁ = c₁ + c₂ (total means of production output must equal the constant capital used across both departments). Chapter 19 is the negative, historical-historiographical mirror of this positive construction. Before Marx can show how reproduction works , he must show why everyone else thought it didn't require a c-term at all . The chapter thus functions as a clearing of the ground — an act of intellectual hygiene that removes the rubbish of Smith's dogma from the path of the scientific analysis. It also matters that Marx here is operating at a specific moment in his own development. The chapter begins with the note \"This is the start of Manuscript VIII,\" and the section on \"Later Writers\" is an addition from Manuscript II. This tells us the text is a working document — a place where Marx thinks aloud, refines his historical judgments, and anticipates his mature schemas. The frequent appeals to \"what was said above\" refer not to this chapter but to the analytical arguments in the preceding parts of the Theories , where Marx has already worked out the distinction between constant and variable capital and the theory of surplus-value. The Polemical Stakes Why does this matter so much? Because the stakes are nothing less than the possibility of an economic science of capitalism. If Smith is right that commodity value = v + s, then the entire annual product is consumable as revenue and no portion is permanently tied up in capital replacement. This is, in fact, what Jean-Baptiste Say concluded — and Marx notes with characteristic irony that \"it is never the original thinkers who draw the absurd conclusions. They rather leave this for the Says and MacCullochs.\" The error, if not detected, collapses the distinction between gross and net product into a subjective illusion, dissolves the reality of capital replacement, and literally makes it possible to think that a nation can consume its entire annual product without disinvestment. The chapter is, in effect, a war over whether capital has any independent existence in the theory of value and distribution at all. Marx's answer is emphatic: it does, and the failure to see this is the mark of vulgar economics. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Physiocrats: Quesnay's Tableau and the Feudal Signboard Marx opens Chapter 19 not with an attack on Smith but with an appreciative, though critical, reconstruction of Quesnay's Tableau économique . This choice is significant. Marx is establishing that the correct instinct — the instinct to treat reproduction as a social, multi-class process — originates with the Physiocrats, and that Smith represents a retrogression from their insight rather than an advance. The chapter's opening sentence sets the frame: \"Quesnay's Tableau économique shows in a few broad lines how the annual result of national production, defined in terms of value, is distributed by circulation in such a way that, with other circumstances remaining the same, simple reproduction can take place, i.e. reproduction on the same scale.\" Two features of this formulation deserve emphasis. First, the Tableau operates in terms of value — it tracks the distribution of the annual product's value, not just its physical dispersion. Second, it demonstrates the possibility of simple reproduction — reproduction \"on the same scale\" — through the mechanism of circulation . This is crucial because it shows that the Physiocrats, unlike the vulgar economists who came later, understood that the social product must pass through a definite set of channels before it can serve either as capital replacement or as revenue. The material starting-point of this process, Marx notes, is \"the previous year's harvest.\" The annual production cycle does not begin from nothing; it begins from a stock of use-values inherited from the previous period. The \"numberless individual acts of circulation\" are thereby \"immediately grouped together in their characteristic social movement as a mass circulation between major economic classes of society that are defined by their functions.\" This is what makes the Tableau revolutionary from a methodological standpoint: it substitutes class-functional analysis for the individualistic analysis of atomized exchange. Instead of tracing contracts between individuals, Quesnay traces flows between classes whose economic roles are structurally defined. The specific insight Marx wants to rescue is this: \"one part of the total product — which as an object of use, just like any other part, is the fresh result of the past year's labour — is also just a bearer of old capital value reappearing in the same natural form. It does not circulate, but rather remains in the hands of its producers, the class of farmers, to begin its service as capital there once again.\" This is the germ of the entire reproduction problem. The annual product contains value that is not newly produced during the year — it is old capital value in new (or the same) physical shape. And this value does not enter circulation as revenue; it stays put, immediately resuming its function as capital. In agricultural terms: seed grain, draught animals, and the like. Quesnay, Marx acknowledges, \"in fact includes in this constant capital part of the annual product, elements that do not belong there,\" but he \"comes to grips with the main question, thanks to the very narrowness of his mental horizon.\" The narrowness — the belief that agriculture alone produces surplus-value — turns out to be methodologically productive , because in agriculture \"the process of economic reproduction ... is always intertwined with a process of natural reproduction.\" The natural cycle makes the necessity of capital replacement visible : you can see the seed going back into the ground, the cattle breeding themselves. This visible natural cycle \"illuminate[s] those of the former, and keep[s] at bay those confusions which are only introduced by the illusions of circulation.\" Here we encounter Marx's first important definitional distinction, embedded in the argument: the difference between economic reproduction and natural reproduction . Economic reproduction is the social process by which value — as opposed to physical material — is maintained and expanded through production and exchange. Natural reproduction is the biological/physical process by which living things (crops, livestock) reproduce themselves. In agriculture the two are palpably intertwined, and this intertwining makes it hard to pretend that the entire annual product is net revenue. The illusions of circulation, by contrast, arise when circulation is studied in isolation from production, and one comes to believe (as the vulgar economists do) that money begets money without the need for material capital to be physically replaced. Now Marx turns to the \"label\" problem. \"The label of a system of ideas is distinguished from that of other articles, among other things, by the fact that it deceives not only the buyer, but often the seller as well.\" This is a characteristically Marxian jab: the Physiocrats themselves — Quesnay and his disciples — \"believed in their feudal signboard.\" They thought they were defending feudal landed property; in fact they were the theorists of capitalist production. \"In point of fact, however, the Physiocratic system is the first systematic conception of capitalist production.\" This is a key thesis of the chapter: the actual content of Physiocracy, beneath the ideological form, is capitalist. How so? \"The representatives of industrial capital — the class of farmers — lead the whole economic movement.\" The farmers are the \"direct exploiter of 'productive' labour, i.e. the producer of surplus-value,\" distinguished from the \"mere appropriator of this surplus-value,\" the landlord. Marx is careful in his footnotes here. There is a footnote distinguishing \"industrial capital\" (as Marx defines the term: capital that produces surplus-value through the employment of wage-labour, as opposed to merchant's capital, which merely appropriates it) from merchant's capital. And he notes that the Physiocratic celebration of the capitalist farmer is: \"Agriculture is pursued on a capitalist footing, i.e. as the large-scale undertaking of the capitalist farmer; the immediate tillers of the soil are wage-labourers.\" Three classes, three functions: capitalist farmers exploit; wage-labourers produce surplus-value; landlords appropriate it. This three-class division — \"the direct exploiter of 'productive' labour ... the producer of surplus-value, the capitalist farmer, is distinguished from the mere appropriator of this surplus-value\" (the landlord) — establishes the template that Smith will later obscure with his v + s formula. Marx notes that the capitalist character of Physiocracy \"already provoked opposition during its heyday.\" He names Linguet and Mably on the one hand, and the \"defenders of free small-scale property in land\" on the other. He footnotes Linguet's Théories des lois civiles (1767), noting that Linguet used the Physiocratic presentation as the basis for a critique of the working class's position in bourgeois society — but a reactionary critique, one that contrasted modern exploitation with an idealized pre-capitalist past. This is a small but telling moment in Marx's historical method: he never welcomes a critique simply because it is critical; the reactionary critique of capitalism from the standpoint of the feudal past is no less false than the capitalist apologetic, even if it is aimed at the same target. The real enemy, however, is not Linguet but Adam Smith. Marx's transition is abrupt: \"The retrogression in Adam Smith's analysis of the reproduction process is all the more striking, in that he not only elaborated on Quesnay's correct analyses ... but in places fell back completely into the errors of the Physiocrats.\" The phrase \" retrogression \" ( Rückschritt ) is deliberately chosen. Smith did make genuine progress: he generalized Quesnay's avances primitives and avances annuelles into \"fixed\" and \"circulating\" capital, thereby freeing the concept of capital from the specifically agricultural sphere. Marx footnotes that Turgot had prepared the ground for this generalization, extending the term \"capital\" beyond avances and identifying the capitaux of manufacturers with those of farmers. But the progress is undermined by what Marx calls the \"retrogression\": Smith accepted and perpetuated \"the concepts 'fixed' and 'circulating' as the decisive distinctions\" — and this taxonomic choice, as we shall see, obscured the really decisive distinction between constant and variable capital. Marx then quotes Smith's remarkable passage (Book Two, Chapter V) in which Smith argues that agriculture produces more value than manufacturing because \"nature labours along with man.\" Smith writes: \"No equal capital puts into motion a greater quantity of productive labour than that of the farmer. Not only his labouring servants, but his labouring cattle, are productive labourers.\" Marx's parenthetical remark — \"(A charming compliment for the labouring servants!)\" — is one of the great sarcasms of the Theories . Smith goes on: \"A field overgrown with briars and brambles may frequently produce as great a quantity of vegetables as the best cultivated vineyard or corn field.\" The point Smith is driving at is that the rent of the landlord is the product of \"those powers of nature, the use of which the landlord lends to the farmer\" — a \"work of nature which remains after deducting or compensating everything which can be regarded as the work of man. It is seldom less than a fourth, and frequently more than a third of the whole produce.\" Marx does not comment extensively on this passage here, beyond the initial sarcasm, but its relevance to the chapter's argument is clear. Smith, so eager to show agriculture's special productivity, resurrects the Physiocratic error that surplus-value originates in nature's bounty rather than in the exploitation of wage-labour. This is the substantive Physiocratic error, and Smith falls back into it. The passage also illustrates the \"exoteric\" Smith — the one who deals in surface appearances and conventional categories. The next piece of evidence against Smith is his treatment of seed. In Book Two, Chapter I, Smith says: \"The whole value of the seed, too, is properly a fixed capital.\" Marx glosses: \"Here, therefore, capital = capital value; this exists in a 'fixed' form.\" Smith continues: \"Though it goes backwards and forwards between the ground and the granary, it never changes masters, and therefore does not properly circulate. The farmer makes his profit, not by its sale, but by its increase.\" Marx's diagnosis: \"The narrowness of this conception lies in Smith's failure to see what Quesnay had already seen, namely the reappearance of the value of constant capital in a renewed form. Instead, he saw here only a further illustration, and moreover a false one, of his distinction between fixed and circulating capital; hence he missed an important aspect of the reproduction process.\" This is the key point. Quesnay sees seed as constant capital value reappearing . Smith sees it as an illustration of his fixed/circulating distinction . The first insight is about reproduction; the second is about taxonomy. Smith's taxonomic obsession blocks the substantive insight. And so the transition to the next section is set up: \"The progress in Smith's translation of 'avances primitives' and 'avances annuelles' into 'fixed capital' and 'circulating capital' consists in the word 'capital,' this concept being generalized and freed from its special reference to the Physiocratic 'agricultural' sphere of application; the retrogression consists in the acceptance and the perpetuation of the concepts 'fixed' and 'circulating' as the decisive distinctions.\" This is the frame for the entire chapter's engagement with Smith. The rest of Section 2 can be read as a detailed demonstration of how Smith's taxonomic mistake — the fixed/circulating mistake — produces a cascade of substantive errors in his theory of reproduction and value. Smith's \"General Perspectives\": The Dogma and Its Smuggled Correction The second major section of the chapter begins with a seemingly innocent quotation from Smith's Wealth of Nations , Book One, Chapter VI (p. 153): \"In every society the price of every commodity finally resolves itself into some one or other, or all of those three parts\" (wages, profit, and rent), \"and in every improved society, all the three enter more or less, as component parts, into the price of the far greater part of commodities.\" Smith elaborates with two examples: sea fish (rent does not enter, only wages and profit) and Scotch Pebbles (only wages enter — \"In some parts of Scotland a few poor people make a trade of gathering, along the sea-shore, those little variegated stones commonly known by the name of Scotch Pebbles. The price which is paid to them by the stone cutter is altogether the wages of their labour; neither rent nor profit make any part of it\"). These examples are crucial as evidenced below, because Marx will later use the Scotch Pebble case to expose the fraudulent logic of Smith's \"proof.\" The doctrine is then extended from the individual commodity to the total social product. Smith says (Book Two, Chapter II): \"Since this is the case, it has been observed, with regard to every particular commodity, taken separately, it must be so with regard to all the commodities which compose the whole annual produce of the land and labour of every country, taken complexly. The whole price of exchangeable value of that annual produce must resolve itself into the same three parts, and be parcelled out among the different inhabitants of the country, either as the wages of their labour, the profits of their stock, or the rent of their land.\" Marx pauses to note the logical structure: \"After Adam Smith has thus resolved both the price of all commodities taken separately and 'the whole price or exchangeable value... of the annual produce of the land and labour of every country' into three sources of revenue, for wage-labourer, capitalist and landlord, into wages, profit and rent, he has to smuggle in a fourth element by an indirect route, namely the element of capital.\" This \"smuggling\" is accomplished via the gross/net revenue distinction. Smith writes (p. 382): \"The gross revenue of all the inhabitants of a great country comprehends the whole annual produce of their land and labour; the net revenue, what remains free to them after deducting the expense of maintaining — first, their fixed, and, secondly, their circulating capital, or what, without encroaching upon their capital, they can place in their stock reserved for immediate consumption, or spend upon their subsistence, conveniences, and amusements. Their real wealth, too, is in proportion, not to their gross, but to their net revenue.\" Marx draws three critical implications. First, \"Adam Smith is expressly dealing only with simple reproduction, not with reproduction on an expanded scale, or with accumulation; he speaks only of the expense of 'maintaining' the functioning capital.\" This is important because it means the difficulty does not arise from accumulation (where new investment creates extra complications) but already at the level of simple reproduction — the supposedly easy case. The \"net\" revenue is that part of the annual product that can enter the consumption fund \"without encroaching upon capital\"; hence some portion of the product is neither wages nor profit nor rent but capital itself. \"One part of the value of both individual and social product, therefore, is neither resolved into wages nor into profit or rent, but into capital.\" Second — and this is the core of Marx's critique — Smith escapes his own theory \"by means of a play on words, the distinction between 'gross' and 'net' 'revenue.'\" The English word \"revenue\" (from Old French revenue , past participle of revenir , \"to return\") literally means \"return.\" Both the individual capitalist and the \"nation\" receive, in place of the capital used up in production, a commodity product whose value on the one hand replaces the capital value consumed and on the other hand comprises value components \"parcelled out\" as wages, profit, and rent. So the whole product value is \"revenue\" in one sense (it's a return), yet part of it is capital income (return of capital), while another part is revenue in the everyday sense (disposable income). Marx writes: \"Thus, what was eliminated when the value of commodities was analysed into its component parts is introduced again through the back door by the ambiguity of this word 'revenue.' But only the components of the product's value that already exist in the product can be 'taken in.' If capital is to income in as revenue, then capital must previously have been spent.\" The third implication concerns the trap that Smith's terminology puts him in. Smith says (Book One, Chapter IX): \"The lowest ordinary rate of profit must always be something more than what is sufficient to compensate the occasional losses to which every employment of stock is exposed. It is this surplus only which is net or clear profit.\" Marx's parenthetical: \"(What capitalist sees profit as including necessary outlays of capital?)\" — a jab at Smith's conflation of profit with gross proceeds. Smith continues: \"What is called gross profit comprehends frequently, not only this surplus, but what is retained for compensating such extraordinary losses.\" Marx's reply: \"But this means no more than that a part of the surplus-value, treated here as part of the gross profit, has to form an insurance fund for production. This insurance fund is created by a part of the surplus labour, which thereby directly produces capital, i.e. the fund set aside for reproduction.\" So the insurance fund is not a distinct \"element\" of value — it is a portion of surplus-value differentiated only by its social function. Similarly for the \"maintenance\" of fixed capital: \"the replacement of the fixed capital consumed by new capital in no way constitutes a new investment of capital, but is simply a replacement of the old capital value in new form.\" And for repairs: \"its cost forms part of the price of the capital advanced. If the capitalist invests this only gradually and according to need ... this in no way changes the source of this profit. The component of value from which it derives simply indicates that the workers provide surplus labour for the repair fund as well as for the insurance fund.\" Marx then summarizes what Smith's definition of fixed capital \"actually boils down to\": \"it is the part of the industrial capital advanced that is fixed in the production process,\" corresponding to Smith's own phrases \"affords a revenue or profit without circulating or changing masters\" (p. 377) and \"either remains in his possession, or continues in the same shape\" (p. 374). So far so good — but the technical definition is unstable. Smith then makes a substantive claim: not only the whole fixed capital but also \"that entire part of the circulating capital required to maintain, repair and replace the fixed capital\" must be excluded from net revenue — \"in point of fact all capital that does not exist in a natural form destined for the consumption fund.\" He writes (Book Two, Chapter II): \"The whole expense of maintaining the fixed capital must evidently be excluded from the net revenue of the society. Neither the materials necessary for supporting their useful machines and instruments of trade, their profitable buildings, etc., nor the produce of the labour necessary for fashioning those materials into the proper form, can ever make any part of it. The price of that labour may indeed make a part of it; as the workmen so employed may place the whole value of their wages in their stock reserved for immediate consumption. But in other sorts of labour, both the price\" (i.e., the wage paid for this labour) \"and the produce\" (in which this labour is embodied) \"go to this stock, the price to that of the workmen, the produce to that of other people, whose subsistence, conveniences, and amusements, are augmented by the labour of those workmen.\" This is a pivotal passage, and Marx treats it as such. Despite its confused phrasing, Smith \"has stumbled upon a very important distinction, between the workers involved in the production of means of production, and those involved in the direct production of means of consumption.\" Marx now formalizes the distinction: The workers producing means of production (Department I, in Marx's later terminology) receive wages equal in value to a component of their product. That wage is revenue for them, but the product they create is not consumable — it is means of production. Hence the value-component of the product equal to their wages exists physically as an aliquot part of means of production, and is not part of the consumption fund. Smith forgets that what holds for wages also holds for the surplus-value (profit and rent) in this department. The surplus-value also exists initially in means of production. Only after conversion into money can it withdraw an equivalent amount of consumption goods produced in Department II, transferring them into the individual consumption fund of the surplus-receivers. All the more should Smith have seen that the portion of the annually produced means of production used up in the production of means of production — i.e., \"the means of production with which means of production are made\" — is \"absolutely excluded, not only by the natural form in which it exists, but also by its capital function, from being any component of value that constitutes revenue.\" Marx also corrects Smith on the second type of worker (Department II). Smith says that for such workers \"both the price of labour and the product 'go to' the direct consumption fund: 'the price' (i.e., the money received as wages) 'to that of the workmen, the product to that of other people.'\" But, Marx objects, \"the worker cannot live off the 'price' of his labour, the money in which his wages are paid; he realizes his money by using it to buy means of consumption, and these may consist partly of types of commodities which he has himself produced. However, his own product may be one that is only consumed by the exploiters of labour.\" This is a small but important correction: the material form of the wage and the material form of the worker's personal consumption need not coincide, since the wage is money and money is universally convertible. Smith then returns to circulating capital, this time to argue that circulating capital (unlike fixed) is not wholly excluded from net revenue. He lists the \"four parts\" of circulating capital — money, provisions, materials, and finished work — and observes that three of the four (provisions, materials, finished work) are regularly withdrawn and placed either in fixed capital or in the stock reserved for immediate consumption. \"Whatever portion of those consumable goods is not employed in maintaining the former\" (the fixed capital) \"goes all to the latter\" (the stock reserved for immediate consumption) \"and makes a part of the net revenue of the society.\" Marx's comment: \"All this is simply the tautology that the part of circulating capital that does not serve for the production of means of production goes into the production of means of consumption, i.e. into the part of the annual product that is destined to form the society's consumption fund.\" In other words, Smith has restated the Department II / Department I division, but under the misleading label \"circulating capital.\" What is important is the immediately following passage, where Smith distinguishes individual from social circulating capital: \"The circulating capital of a society is in this respect different from that of an individual. That of an individual is totally excluded from making any part of his net revenue, which must consist altogether in his profits. But though the circulating capital of every individual makes a part of that of the society to which he belongs, it is not upon that account totally excluded from making a part likewise of their net revenue. Though the whole goods in a merchant's shop must by no means be placed in his own stock reserved for immediate consumption, they may [be placed] in that of other people, who, from a revenue derived from other funds, may regularly replace their value to him, together with its profits, without occasioning any diminution either of his capital or of theirs.\" Marx extracts three lessons from this. First, \"Just like the fixed capital, and that circulating capital needed for its reproduction and maintenance (he forgets its functioning), so the circulating capital of each individual capitalist involved in the production of means of consumption is also totally excluded from his net revenue, which can consist only of his profits.\" Second, \"The circulating capital of each individual capitalist forms part of the circulating capital of the society, just as with each individual fixed capital.\" Third — and this is the crux — \"The circulating capital of the society, even though it is only the sum of the individual circulating capitals, possesses a character distinct from the circulating capital of an individual capitalist.\" Smith has accidentally discovered that aggregation changes the significance of a category : what is capital for one capitalist may be revenue-accessible for the class as a whole. But Smith misses the significance of his own observation because he has chosen the wrong example. \"Instead of selecting as his example the goods in the retail shop, Adam Smith should have chosen the stocks amassed in the storehouses of the industrial capitalists.\" The retail shop example obscures the crucial point: the relevant \"social consumption fund\" is not the stock of commodities sitting on retail shelves awaiting purchase; it is the annual mass of means of consumption flowing out of Department II's production. Retail stocks are a distributional intermediary, not the substantive social category. Marx then performs a constructive thought-experiment: \"If Adam Smith had now, in discussing what he calls circulating capital, fitted together the fragmentary ideas that impressed themselves on him previously, while he was contemplating the reproduction of what he calls fixed capital, he would have arrived at the following result.\" This \"result\" is Marx's own two-department schema , here presented as the logical consequence of Smith's scattered insights: The annual social product consists of two departments : the first comprises means of production, the second comprises means of consumption; \"the two have to be treated separately.\" The total value of the Department I product divides into three parts : (a) the value of the means of production consumed in creating these means of production — this is \"only the capital value reappearing in a new form\"; (b) a part equal to the value of the capital laid out on labour-power (wages); (c) a part forming the source of profits and rent (surplus-value). The Department II capitalists — \"the direct producers of means of consumption\" — replace the capital used up by the Department I capitalists in producing means of consumption (excepting the portion converted into wages for Department II workers). Meanwhile the used-up capital, now existing in the hands of the producing capitalists (Department I) in the form of means of consumption, \"forms for its part — from the social standpoint — the consumption fund in which the capitalists and workers in the first department realize their revenue.\" This is the complete, if still informal, two-department schema. Marx is showing that Smith had all the materials for it — that \"he almost hit the nail on the head\" — but failed to assemble them because the fixed/circulating distinction kept displacing the constant/variable and means-of-production/means-of-consumption distinctions. Two implications are drawn \"from the investigation so far.\" First : \"even though the social capital is simply the sum of the individual capitals, and therefore the annual commodity product (or commodity capital) of the society is equal to the sum of the commodity products of these individual capitals; and although, therefore, the same analysis of the commodity value into its components that holds for each individual commodity capital must also hold for the society as a whole and in the last analysis actually does hold, yet the form of appearance which these components assume in the overall process of social reproduction is a different one.\" This is a subtle point. At the level of the individual commodity, value = c + v + s in a straightforward way. But at the level of the total social product, the components — c, v, s — appear in different material forms and distributional patterns . In particular, some c-components of individual products re-appear as revenue-accessible goods at the social level, while some v + s components exist as non-consumable means of production. The sum of individual capitals does not behave like one big individual capital . Second : \"even on the basis of simple reproduction, we find not only the production of wages (variable capital) and surplus-value, but also the direct production of new constant capital value; this is true even though the working day consists of only two parts, one in which the worker replaces the variable capital, and the other in which he produces surplus-value (profit, rent, etc.). In other words, the daily labour that is spent in reproducing the means of production — whose value breaks down into wages and surplus-value — is realized in new means of production which also replace the constant capital component expended in the production of the means of consumption.\" This second point is the analytic heart of the whole chapter. The worker's working day has only two parts (necessary labour and surplus labour). Yet the annual product contains three value components (c, v, s). How? Because the labour that creates new value is at the same time the labour that preserves and transfers old value — via its concrete, useful character. The labour spent in Department I produces new means of production whose value (v + s) is only two parts, but whose use-value replaces the constant capital consumed in Department II. In this way, c is reproduced without any additional labour-time being expended on it. This is what Marx elsewhere calls the \"twofold character of labour\" — as abstract value-creating labour and as concrete use-value-creating labour. Smith fails to make this distinction, and hence cannot explain where c comes from. The section closes with a historical note: \"The main difficulties arise not in the treatment of accumulation, but already in that of simple reproduction, although the greater part of these have already been resolved in what was said above. This is why both Adam Smith (Book Two) and before him Quesnay ( Tableau économique ) started with simple reproduction, whenever they had to deal with the movement of the annual social product and its reproduction by way of circulation.\" Marx is signaling a crucial methodological point: simple reproduction is the analytically hard case, not accumulation. Accumulation adds complications (the surplus must be reinvested in specific proportions), but the fundamental puzzle — how constant capital is reproduced — is already present at the level of simple reproduction. Even the conservative Smith, operating in Book Two with the simplest possible case, couldn't get it right. Smith's Resolution of Exchange-Value into v + s: The Dogma Stated and Defended Having established the substantive problem, Marx now attacks Smith's formula at the level of the individual commodity. \"Adam Smith's dogma that the price or 'exchangeable value' of every single commodity — and thus of all the commodities comprising the annual social product (he correctly assumes capitalist production everywhere) — is composed of, or 'resolves itself into,' the three 'component parts' wages, profit and rent, can be reduced to the thesis that commodity value = v + s, i.e. the value of the variable capital advanced, plus the surplus-value.\" The reduction is legitimate because Smith himself permits us to treat profit and rent as common forms of surplus-value. The quotations Marx provides show Smith saying as much: in manufacture, \"The value which the workmen add to the materials ... resolves itself ... into two parts, of which the one pays their wages, the other the profits of their employer upon the whole stock of materials and wages which he advanced\" (Book One, Chapter VI, p. 151). And: \"Though the manufacturer (i.e., the manufacturing worker) has his wages advanced to him by his master, he, in reality, costs him no expense, the value of those wages being generally restored, together with a profit, in the improved value of the subject upon which his labour is bestowed\" (Book Two, Chapter III, p. 430). This second quote is significant: it makes plain that wages are not truly an expense to the capitalist, because the worker reproduces them plus a surplus. The capitalist only appears to advance wages. Smith also says (Book Two, Chapter III, p. 432): \"The part of 'stock' that is laid out 'in maintaining productive hands,' 'after having served in the function of a capital to him' (the employer) ... 'constitutes a revenue to them' (the workers).\" And (p. 431): \"the whole annual produce of the land and labour of every country ... naturally divides itself into two parts. One of them, and frequently the largest, is, in the first place, destined for replacing a capital, or for renewing the provisions, materials, and finished work, which had been withdrawn from a capital; the other for constituting a revenue either to the owner of this capital, as the profit of his stock, or to some other person, as the rent of his land.\" Marx pauses on this: \"Only one part of the capital, as Adam Smith has just told us, also forms revenue for someone, namely the part invested in the purchase of productive labour. This part — the variable capital — firstly performs 'the function of a capital' in the hands of the employer, and for him, and then it 'constitutes a revenue' for the productive worker himself.\" Marx then makes a subtle criticism of Smith's phrasing: \"The capitalist transforms one part of his capital value into labour-power, and precisely in this way into variable capital; but as a result of this transformation it is not only this part of the capital, but his entire capital, that functions as industrial capital.\" This is a precision-point: the transformation of one part of capital into labour-power makes the whole function as industrial capital. And — crucially — \"The worker — the seller of labour-power — receives its value in the form of his wage. In his hands, labour-power is simply a saleable commodity, the commodity from whose sale he lives, and which therefore forms the only source of his revenue; it is only in the hands of its buyer, the capitalist, that the labour-power functions as variable capital, and in fact the capitalist only appears to advance the purchase price of the labour-power, for its value has already been supplied to him by the worker.\" This is one of those dense passages where Marx packs multiple theoretical claims. The commodity labour-power is sold by the worker (a sale, like any other commodity sale) and bought by the capitalist. In the seller's hands it is a commodity and a source of revenue; in the buyer's hands it is capital (variable capital). Smith and his successors constantly blur these two moments, treating the wage both as capital (from the capitalist's standpoint) and as revenue (from the worker's standpoint) without keeping them logically separate. Marx then turns to the agricultural case, where Smith says the workers \"besides 'the reproduction of a value equal to their own consumption, or to the' (variable) 'capital which employs them, together with its owners' profits' — 'over and above the capital of the farmer and all its profits, they regularly occasion the reproduction of the rent of the landlord.'\" Marx's comment is important: \"The fact that the rent goes into the hands of the landlord is quite beside the point for the question we are dealing with here. Before it passes into his hands, it must first exist in the hands of the farmer, i.e., of the industrial capitalist. It must form a value component of the product, before it becomes revenue for anyone. Thus for Smith rent and profit are both only components of the surplus-value that the productive worker constantly reproduces together with his own wages, i.e., with the value of the variable capital. Rent and profit are parts of the surplus-value s, and in this way Smith resolves the price of all commodities into v + s.\" The formal statement of the dogma: \"The dogma that the prices of all commodities (and therefore of the annual commodity product) can be resolved into wages plus profit plus rent of land, assumes the form, even in the intermittent esoteric part of Smith's work, that the value of any commodity, and thus of the society's annual commodity product, = v + s, i.e., the capital value laid out on labour-power and constantly reproduced by the workers, plus the surplus-value added by the workers through their labour.\" What this reveals, Marx says, is the source of Smith's one-sidedness: \"For the fact that these components also form the various sources of revenue for classes with different functions in production has nothing to do with the quantitative determination of each of these components, and the limits of their total values.\" In other words, Smith confused the distributional question (who gets what) with the constitutive question (how is value itself determined). The components' role as revenues led Smith to treat them as the constitutive \"sources\" of value. Marx then examines Smith's famous statement: \"Wages, profit, and rent, are the three original sources of all revenue as well as of all exchangeable value. All other revenue is ultimately derived from some one or other of these\" (Book One, Chapter VI, p. 155). He identifies a double confusion. Confusion #1 : \"All members of society who do not figure directly in the reproduction process, whether as workers or not, can receive their share of the annual commodity product — i.e., their means of consumption — in the first instance only from the hands of those classes to whom this product firstly accrues — productive workers, industrial capitalists and landlords. To this extent, their revenues are, in a material sense, derived from wages ... profit, and ground-rent, and hence appear, in contrast to these original revenues, as derivative.\" Marx gives a memorable list of the \"derivative\" revenue-receivers: \"king, priest, professor, prostitute, mercenary, etc.\" Their revenues are derived because the goods they consume must first pass through the hands of the productive classes. But — and here is a dialectical twist — these derivative receivers \"draw them by way of their social functions ... and they can therefore view these functions of theirs as the original source of their revenue.\" Ideologically, the professor thinks his salary is the \"product\" of his professing; analytically, it is a derivative claim on the surplus produced by others. Confusion #2 — \"this is the high point of Smith's stupid blunder\": Smith begins by correctly defining value components, then shows how they form sources of revenue, and then reverses himself , \"making these revenues, instead of just 'component parts,' into 'original sources of all exchangeable value,' thereby throwing the doors wide open to vulgar economics. (Cf. our Roscher.)\" Marx footnotes Roscher as a German vulgar economist and founder of the historical school. The reference is barbed: Smith's confusion is the fons et origo of a whole tradition of apologetic economics that treats the distributive categories as primary and the value substance as derivative. This is the logical fallacy Marx calls the quid pro quo — the substitution of the revenue-categories for the value-substance. The Constant Capital Component: How Smith Conjures \"c\" Out of Existence The next subsection, (c), is the analytic core of the chapter. Marx sets up the question: \"We shall now see how Adam Smith tries to conjure the constant part of capital out of commodity value.\" The relevant quotation is from Book One, Chapter VI, p. 153, where Smith discusses the price of corn: \"In the price of corn, for example, one part pays the rent of the landlord ... Another [part] pays the wages or maintenance of the labourers ('and labouring cattle,' he adds) employed in producing it, and the third pays the profit of the farmer. These three parts seem (they 'seem' indeed) either immediately or ultimately to make up the whole price of corn.\" Marx pounces on the word \"seem\": \"The origin of this component of value has as little to do with the circumstance that it is paid to the landlord and forms revenue for him in the form of rent, as the origin of the other value components has with the fact that they form sources of revenue as profit and wages.\" And, on the quantitative point: \"This whole price, i.e., its quantitative determination, is absolutely independent of its distribution among three kinds of person.\" In other words, whether the total is distributed as rent/wages/profit has no bearing on the magnitude of the total. Distribution is a separate question from constitution. Smith then tries to head off the obvious objection — \"A fourth part, it may perhaps be thought, is necessary for replacing the stock of the farmer, or for compensating the wear and tear of his labouring cattle and other instruments of husbandry.\" His answer: \"But it must be considered that the price of any instrument of husbandry, such as a labouring horse, is itself made up of the same three parts; the rent of the land upon which he is reared, the labour of tending and rearing him, and the profits of the farmer who advances both the rent of this land, and the wages of this labour. Though the price of the corn, therefore, may pay the price as well as the maintenance of the horse, the whole price still resolves itself either immediately or ultimately into the same three parts of rent, labour\" (he means wages) \"and profit.\" Marx's devastating comment: \"This is literally all that Adam Smith has to say in support of his astonishing doctrine. His proof consists simply in the repetition of the same assertion.\" This is a maximally uncharitable but accurate characterization. Smith, faced with the obvious counter-example (a farmer's capital outlays — horses, tools, etc. — that are neither rent nor wages nor profit), simply asserts that \"ultimately\" the price of the horse itself decomposes into the same three categories. But this is question-begging: it doesn't show that the horse's price is constituted out of v + s; it just asserts so, and pushes the problem one stage back. Marx states the logical requirement: \"The statement that the entire price of commodities is either 'immediately' or 'ultimately' resolvable into v + s would only cease to be an empty subterfuge if Smith could demonstrate that the commodity products whose price is immediately resolved into c (the price of the means of production consumed) + v + s are finally compensated for by commodity products which entirely replace these 'consumed means of production,' and which are for their part produced simply by the outlay of variable capital, i.e., capital laid out on labour-power. The price of these latter commodities would then immediately be v + s. And in this way the price of the former, too, c + v + s, where c stands for the component of constant capital, would be ultimately resolvable into v + s.\" But Smith provides no such demonstration. His Scotch Pebble example is useless as a proof because the Pebble collectors \"according to him (1) do not supply any kind of surplus-value, but simply produce their own wages; (2) do not employ any means of production. (Though in fact they do, in the form of baskets, sacks and other containers for carting off the pebbles.)\" The second point is a delicious reductio: even the Scotch Pebble example, which Smith adduces to show that some commodity prices consist only of wages, in fact involves means of production (baskets, sacks) that Smith has ignored. The section now deepens the diagnosis. \"We have already seen above how Adam Smith refutes his own theory without even becoming aware of its contradictions. The source of these, however, is to be sought precisely in his own scientific premises.\" Marx is being dialectical: he does not simply say Smith is stupid; he says Smith's correct premises generate the contradiction. Smith correctly sees that \"The capital converted into labour produces a value greater than its own. How? Because, says Smith, the workers impart to the things on which they work, during the production process, a value that, besides the equivalent for their own purchase price, forms a surplus-value accruing to their employers (profit and rent).\" This is correct as far as it goes. But Smith does not then ask: what about the means of production the workers transform? These already had value before the workers touched them, and that value is preserved , not created , in the product. Smith's analysis of the labour-process (adding) and his analysis of the labour-process's material conditions (already-existing means of production) are never coordinated. Marx's sharpest formulation: \"The society's total annual value product, therefore, can be broken down only into v + s, into an equivalent with which the workers replace the capital value spent on their own purchase price, and the additional value that they have to provide for their employers over and above this. ... How can there be any other component of value, if the annual value product contains no other elements besides v + s? Here we are concerned with simple reproduction. Since the total annual labour is resolved into labour needed for the reproduction of the capital value laid out on labour-power, and labour needed for the creation of a surplus-value, where can still more labour come from to produce a capital value not laid out on labour-power?\" This is the reductio in its purest form. If (as is true) the working day has only two parts, and if (as Smith claims) all commodity value reduces to v + s, then there is no labour-time left to produce c. Yet c clearly exists — the factory, the raw materials, the seed corn, the horses. Where does its value come from? The answer, which Smith cannot see, is: it does not need to be produced by the current year's labour at all. It was already there, produced in previous years, and is merely preserved and transferred into the new product by the useful character of living labour. This requires understanding the twofold character of labour — a distinction Smith lacks. Marx now lays out the argument in numbered steps. Step 1 : \"Adam Smith defines the value of a commodity by the amount of labour that the wage-labourer 'adds' to the object of labour.\" Note the specific word \"adds\" — one of Smith's own. \"The value that the worker adds to a thing (and this 'adds' is our Adam's own expression) is quite independent of whether the object to which the value is added already has value before this addition, or whether it does not.\" This is important. The new value added is a magnitude determined by the labour-time expended, regardless of the value of the object worked upon. A worker making a jewel worth £1000 from raw material worth £999 adds the same amount of value as one making a jewel worth £1000 from raw material worth £0 (assuming equal labour-time). The pre-existing value of the means of production does not affect the magnitude of the new value created; it only affects the total value of the resulting product. \"One part of this total value is characterized as being equal to the value of the wage, i.e., an equivalent for wages. The second part, therefore, the surplus-value, is necessarily characterized, by the same token, as being equal to the total value of the product minus the value component that is the equivalent for the wage.\" Marx is being precise: the total value of the commodity has more than two parts, but the new value added has exactly two parts. Smith identifies the two — the value of the product and the value added — and so loses the constant component. Step 2 : \"What holds for a commodity produced in a single capitalist business by some individual worker holds also for the annual product of all branches of industry taken together.\" The annual product of the working class \"fixes\" (Smith's expression) a total value determined by the amount of annual labour; this breaks down into wage-equivalent + surplus-value. The wage forms the revenue of the working class; the surplus-value the revenue of the capitalist class; the two represent the relative shares in the annual consumption fund. \"There is therefore no room left for the constant capital value, for the reproduction of the capital functioning in the form of means of production.\" Marx now cites Smith's own claims from the Introduction to make clear that Smith intended this conclusion: \"To explain in what has consisted the revenue of the great body of the people, or what has been the nature of those funds which ... have supplied their annual consumption, is the object of these Four first Books\" (p. 106). And: \"The annual labour of every nation is the fund which originally supplies it with all the necessaries and conveniences of life which it annually consumes, and which consists always either in the immediate produce of that labour, or in what is purchased with that produce from other nations\" (p. 104). \"This, then,\" Marx concludes, \"Smith's first error is to equate the value of the annual product with the annual value product.\" The annual product is the total mass of commodities produced during the year, whose value includes: (i) the new value produced this year (v + s) and (ii) the value of means of production created in previous years but transferred to the current product (c). The annual value product is only the new value created this year — i.e., v + s. Smith confuses the two. \"The latter is simply the product of the current year's labour; the former includes, on top of this, all those elements of value that were used in the production of this annual product, but which were produced in the previous year and partly in still earlier years: means of production whose value only re-appears — and which, as far as their value is concerned, have been neither produced nor reproduced by the labour spent during the current year.\" The confusion is \"itself based on a further error in his fundamental conception. He does not distinguish the two-fold character of labour itself: labour that creates value, by the expenditure of labour-power, and labour that creates objects of use (use-values), as concrete useful labour.\" This is the crucial textual moment. Marx claims that Smith's failure to distinguish the twofold character of labour is the root of the confusion. Let us unpack this. Labour as value-creating is abstract labour — the expenditure of human labour-power in general, in abstraction from any particular useful form. Its magnitude is measured in units of socially necessary labour-time. This labour produces the new value added (v + s). Labour as use-value-creating is concrete labour — the specific, useful, purposive labour that transforms specific materials into specific products (weaving, spinning, carpentering, etc.). This labour, by its concrete character , transfers the value of the means of production into the new product and preserves it there. The carpenter's labour as concrete activity makes a table, and in so doing retains the value of the wood he uses; the spinner's labour as concrete activity makes yarn, retaining the value of the cotton. Smith treats labour only as value-creating (abstract). He therefore cannot explain how the value of the means of production gets into the product. He can only see labour as adding new value, not as also transferring old value. Whence, for him, does the value of the wood come from, if not from the carpenter's labour? Either from the carpenter (in which case it should be in v + s) or from nowhere (impossible). He lacks the third alternative: it was already there, brought into the product by the concrete action of the labourer. Marx continues: \"The total sum of commodities annually produced, i.e., the total annual product, is the product of the useful labour operating in the current year; it is only by the social application of labour in an intricate system of varieties of useful labour that all these commodities have come into being; it is only in this way that the value of the means of production used up in their production is retained in their total value, and reappears in a new natural form. The total annual product is thus the result of the useful labour expended during the year; but only one part of the value of this product has been created during the year; this part is the annual value product, which represents the amount of labour actually performed during the year itself.\" Two distinctions are now in play: (i) value of annual product vs. annual value product; (ii) labour as useful (concrete) vs. labour as value-forming (abstract). Marx ties them together: Smith equates (i) because he fails to distinguish (ii). The section closes with a quotation from Smith's Introduction — \"The annual labour of every nation is the fund which originally supplies it with all the necessaries and conveniences of life which it annually consumes, etc.\" — and Marx's comment: \"He one-sidedly adopts the standpoint of useful labour. It is this, to be sure, that has brought all these means of subsistence into their consumable form. He forgets here, however, that this would have been impossible without the collaboration of means of labour and objects of labour handed down from earlier years, and that the 'annual labour,' therefore, in as much as it forms value, has in no way created the entire value of the products prepared by it; that the value produced is smaller than the value of the product.\" This phrase — \"the value produced is smaller than the value of the product\" — is a compact restatement of the entire critique. In any given year, the newly produced value (v + s) is strictly less than the total value of the annual product (c + v + s), because the difference (c) is transferred, not produced. Smith denies this by conflating value produced with value of product. \"If we cannot reproach Adam Smith for going no further in this analysis than his successors (even if an approach to the correct conception was already available with the Physiocrats), we can say that he fell increasingly into confusion, and the principal reason for this was that his 'esoteric' conception of commodity value was constantly being thwarted by the exoteric ideas which on the whole prevail in his work, even though his scientific instinct did permit the esoteric standpoint to reappear from time to time.\" This is one of Marx's central historiographical categories: the esoteric vs. exoteric Smith . The \"esoteric\" Smith is the one who sees, from time to time, that commodity value = c + v + s, that rent and profit are forms of surplus-value, that value is determined by labour-time. The \"exoteric\" Smith is the one who, for public consumption, presents the three revenues as the \"original sources\" of all exchangeable value. The esoteric view is scientifically better; the exoteric is more systematically consistent with the surface appearances of bourgeois society. Because the exoteric view dominates Smith's text, the esoteric insights \"reappear from time to time\" but never become the organizing principle. Marx's own scientific approach inverts this: he makes the esoteric view the systematic foundation and treats the exoteric view as a set of illusions to be explained. Capital and Revenue in Adam Smith: The Deepest Analysis Subsection (d) is the most philosophically dense portion of the chapter. Its aim is to dissect the category of \"revenue\" itself and to show how the conceptual confusion between revenue and capital — not just an accounting quibble — is at the heart of Smith's error. Marx begins with the mechanics of variable capital. When a capitalist \"advances\" variable capital, he may pay the worker in money before the product is sold, after it is sold, out of already-received money, or on credit. In all these cases, \"the capitalist spends variable capital which accrues to the workers as money, and he possesses in return the equivalent of this capital value in that portion of his commodities' value with which the worker newly produces the share in the total value of these that accrues to him; with which, in other words, he has produced the value of his own wages.\" The worker, for his part, \"spends the value or price of the labour-power he has sold on means of subsistence, on the means to reproduce his labour-power. A sum of money equal to the variable capital forms his income, in other words his revenue, which he receives only so long as he can sell his labour-power to the capitalist.\" Marx now sets forth the key structural claim: \"The wage-labourer's commodity — his own labour-power — functions as a commodity only in so far as it is incorporated into the capitalist's capital, and functions as capital; on the other hand, the capital spent as money capital on the purchase of labour-power functions as revenue in the hands of the seller of labour-power, the wage-labourer.\" The same sum of money has two functional identities: from the capitalist's standpoint it is variable capital (a value advanced for valorization); from the worker's standpoint it is revenue (spent on means of subsistence). Smith conflates the two. Marx insists on their separation. He breaks down the whole circuit into three distinct stages. First stage — circulation. \"The worker sells his commodity — labour-power — to the capitalist; the money with which the capitalist buys it is for him money invested for the purpose of valorization, i.e., money capital; it is not spent, but merely advanced.\" Marx adds a crucial clarification: \"(This is the real sense of the 'advance' — the Physiocrats' avance — quite independently of where the capitalist acquires the money itself. What is advanced, for the capitalist, is every value that he pays for the purpose of the production process, whether this is done in advance or after the event; it is advanced to the production process itself.)\" This is an important point. The concept of \"advance\" does not depend on temporal priority (payment before the product exists). It depends on functional orientation — value paid for the sake of the production process, whether the money comes from the capitalist's pocket, from previous sales, or from credit. This exposes Smith's (and the Physiocrats') temporal notion of \"advance\" as superficial. Second stage — production. \"The labour-power that has been bought now forms part of the functioning capital, and the worker himself functions here simply as a special natural form of this capital, as distinct from the elements of capital that exist in the natural form of means of production.\" This is uncompromising. In production, the worker is a component of capital — not a subject, but an instrument. \"During the process, the worker adds value to the means of production that he transforms into a product by spending his labour-power to the value of this labour-power (leaving aside surplus-value). In this way, he reproduces for the capitalist, in the commodity form, the part of the former's capital advanced to him in wages, or due to be advanced; produces for him an equivalent for this capital. He produces for the capitalist, in other words, the capital that the capitalist can 'advance' afresh in the purchase of labour-power.\" Third stage — sale. \"With the sale of the commodity, therefore, a part of its sale price replaces for the capitalist the variable capital that he advanced, and therefore enables him to buy labour-power afresh, just as it enables the worker to sell it again.\" Now comes the fundamental methodological point. \"With all commodity purchases and sales — in as much as these transactions are simply considered by themselves — it is completely immaterial what happens in the hands of the seller to the money received for his commodity, and what happens in the hands of the buyer to the item of use that he purchased. It is also completely immaterial, therefore, in so far as it is simply the circulation process that is under consideration, that the labour-power bought by the capitalist reproduces capital value for him, while the money paid as the purchase price of this labour-power forms revenue for the worker. The value of the worker's item of trade, his labour-power, is affected neither by the fact that this forms 'revenue' for him, nor by the fact that the use of his commodity by the buyer reproduces capital value for the buyer.\" This is a subtle but vital claim. In a simple commodity transaction, we do not care what the seller does with the money or what the buyer does with the thing bought. Circulation as such abstracts from all that. So the fact that labour-power, once bought, further produces value for the buyer, and that the wage further serves as revenue for the seller, is extraneous to the circulation act. The value of labour-power is determined independently — by the labour needed to reproduce it (i.e., the labour embodied in the worker's necessary means of subsistence). \"Because the value of labour-power — i.e., the adequate sale price of this commodity — is determined by the amount of labour needed to reproduce it, and this amount of labour is itself determined by that needed to produce the means of subsistence that the worker needs, i.e., the amount of labour needed to maintain his life, the wage becomes the revenue from which the worker has to live.\" Marx now moves to a direct attack on Smith's claim: \"Adam Smith is completely wrong to say (p. 432) that the part of capital employed 'in maintaining productive hands,' 'after having served in the function of a capital to him' (the capitalist), 'constitutes a revenue for them' (the workers).\" Why wrong? Because \"The money with which the capitalist pays for the labour-power that he buys 'serves in the function of a capital to him,' in as much as he thereby incorporates labour-power with the material components of his capital and hence for the first time puts his capital in a position in which it can function as productive capital.\" The point is that the same money is capital from the capitalist's standpoint and is revenue from the worker's standpoint — but this duality is not the result of the money functioning twice; it is the result of labour-power functioning twice. \"The difference is that labour-power is a commodity in the hands of the worker, and not capital, and it constitutes a revenue for him in so far as he can regularly repeat its sale; it functions as capital after the sale, in the hands of the capitalist, during the production process itself. What serves two purposes here is labour-power. In the hands of the worker, it is a commodity sold at its value; in the hands of the capitalist who has bought it, it is a power producing both value and use-value.\" Marx then makes a temporal point of considerable importance. \"The money, however, that the worker receives from the capitalist, he receives only after he has given him the use of his labour-power, after this has already been realized in the value of the product of labour. The capitalist has this value in his hand before he pays for it. So it is not the money that functions twice: first as money form of the variable capital, then as wages. It is rather the labour-power that has functioned twice; first as a commodity, when the labour-power is sold (when the wage to be paid is stipulated, the money operates merely as an ideal measure of value, and it in no way needs to be in the capitalist's hands yet); secondly in the production process, where it functions as capital, i.e., as an element creating both use-value and value in the hands of the capitalist. It has already supplied the equivalent to be paid to the worker, in the commodity form, before the capitalist pays this to the worker in the money form. The worker therefore himself creates the payment fund from which the capitalist pays him.\" This is a compressed and decisive argument. Note the sequence: (1) the worker sells labour-power; the wage is stipulated; the money operates \"merely as an ideal measure of value\" — the capitalist does not yet need to have it. (2) The worker labors; the value equivalent to his wage is already produced and embodied in the product. (3) The capitalist sells the product; the money is realized. (4) Only now does the capitalist pay the worker. So the worker has, in effect, financed his own wage. This is the material basis of the illusion, noted earlier, that the capitalist \"advances\" wages. Marx expands on this with another key thought: \"But this is not all. The worker spends the money he receives on maintaining his labour-power, and thus — if we consider the capitalist class and the working class as a whole — on maintaining for the capitalist the only instrument by means of which he can remain a capitalist.\" The worker's consumption is not merely his own private matter; it is the reproduction of labour-power as a commodity, and therefore the reproduction of the fundamental condition of capitalist production. This is the deep sense in which the \"revenue\" of the worker is functional for capital. Here Marx offers a dialectical summary of the two-sided process: \"On the one hand, therefore, the constant purchase and sale of labour-power perpetuates the position of labour-power as an element of capital, and in this way capital appears as the creator of commodities, articles of use that have a value; this is also how the portion of capital that buys labour-power is regularly restored by the product of labour-power itself, so that the worker himself constantly creates the capital fund out of which he is paid. On the other hand, the constant sale of labour-power becomes a source of maintaining the worker's life that he perpetually has to repeat, and his labour-power thus appears as the means by which he draws the revenue from which he lives.\" The category of revenue , in the worker's case, means \"nothing more than the appropriation of values effected by the constantly repeated sale of a commodity (labour-power), and in this connection these values themselves serve only towards the constant reproduction of the commodity to be sold.\" So the worker's \"revenue\" is not a share in a social fund on the same footing as the capitalist's profit; it is the price of a commodity he must perpetually re-sell. Marx now offers a careful analogy to make his point about the independence of value determination: \"To this extent, Adam Smith is correct to say that the value component of the product created by the worker for which the capitalist pays an equivalent in the form of the wage becomes a source of revenue for the worker. But this affects the nature and the size of this portion of commodity value just as little as the value of the means of production is affected by the fact that they function as capital values, or the nature and length of a straight line by the fact that it functions as the base of a triangle or the axis of an ellipse. The value of labour-power remains just as independently determined as that of any means of production.\" This is a strong philosophical claim. The function a value serves — as capital, as revenue, as base of a triangle — does not affect its magnitude or its nature as value . The base of a triangle is still a straight line of a given length; the fact that it serves as a base does not make it longer or shorter, nor does it cease to be a line. This anticipates the later metaphor of drawing a straight line and dividing it into segments, which Marx will use to illustrate the difference between composition and resolution of value. The consequence for Smith: \"This component of commodity value does not consist of revenue as an autonomous factor constituting it, nor does this component of value resolve itself into revenue. This new value constantly reproduced by the worker forms a source of revenue for him, but it does not follow from this, inversely, that his revenue forms a component of the new value he has produced. What determines the volume of his revenue is the share in the new value he creates that is paid to him, and not the other way round.\" Marx illustrates with a homely example: \"If my income is 10 shillings per week, then the circumstances of this weekly income affect neither the nature of the 10 shillings' value nor its magnitude.\" And: \"Just as with any other commodity, so in the case of labour-power, too, its value is determined by the amount of labour needed to reproduce it; ... this is a characteristic of this particular commodity (labour-power), but it is no more peculiar to it than the fact that the value of draught cattle is determined by the means of subsistence needed for their maintenance ... is peculiar to these draught cattle.\" This last point — the draught-cattle analogy — is pointed. It mocks Smith's own comparison of workers to \"labouring cattle.\" If the value of cattle is determined by the labour needed to feed them, why should the value of labour-power be determined otherwise? The \"revenue\" of the worker is no more mysterious, from a value-theoretic standpoint, than the \"feed\" of a cow: it is the input required to maintain the commodity. The worker's revenue is a cost of production of labour-power, not a component of value. Marx now names the root cause: \" The category of 'revenue' is the root cause of Smith's whole trouble. For him the different types of revenue form the 'component parts' of the commodity value annually produced, newly created, whereas in actual fact it is the other way round; it is the two parts into which this commodity value is divided for the capitalist — the equivalent for the variable capital that he advanced in the money form for the purchase of labour, and the other portion of value that belongs to him although it has cost him nothing, the surplus-value — that form sources of revenue.\" This is a clean statement of the correct causal ordering: value is prior to revenue; revenue is a distribution of value. Smith reverses this, making revenue prior to value. \"The equivalent of the variable capital is advanced once again in labour-power, and to this extent forms a revenue for the worker in the form of his wage; the other part — the surplus-value — since it does not have to replace for the capitalist any of the capital advanced, can be spent by him on means of consumption (both necessities and luxuries), can be consumed as revenue, instead of forming capital of any kind. The precondition for this revenue is the commodity value itself, and its components are distinguished for the capitalist only to the extent that they form either an equivalent for, or a surplus over, the variable capital that he has advanced.\" Then a crucial positive statement: \"Both of these consist of nothing but labour-power spent in the course of commodity production, set flowing as labour. They do not consist of income or revenue, but rather of expenditure — expenditure of labour.\" This is Marx's materialist first principle. Value is congealed labour; revenue is a social right to consume value. Revenue does not \"consist of\" anything but value; it is not a substance but a claim. Marx now formulates the quid pro quo : \"After this quid pro quo, in which revenue is made into the source of commodity value, instead of this commodity value being the source of revenue, commodity value now appears to be 'composed' of the various kinds of revenues; these are determined independently of one another, and the total value of the commodity is determined by adding together the values of these revenues.\" But this generates an impossible problem: \"how can we determine the value of each of these revenues from which the commodity value is supposed to derive? In the case of wages, this can in fact be done, because wages are the value of the commodity labour-power, and the latter can be determined (like the value of any other commodity) by the labour needed for its reproduction. But as for surplus-value, or in Adam Smith's case its two forms, profit and rent, how are these to be determined? Here we are left with empty prattle.\" This is the withering conclusion. Once revenues are treated as primary, wages can be determined (because labour-power is a commodity with a determinate value), but profit and rent have no independent determinant . Smith can only say they are what remains. And so the theory is not even internally consistent as an explanation of the sources of value. Marx now introduces his famous geometric metaphor to distinguish composition from resolution : \"At some points Adam Smith depicts wages and surplus-value (or wages and profit) as components out of which the commodity value or price is put together, while at others, and often almost in the same breath, he depicts them as parts into which the commodity value 'resolves itself'; the latter, however, means that the commodity value is given first and that various parts of this given value accrue to different persons involved in the production process in the form of different revenues. This is in no way identical with the composition of value from these three components.\" He elaborates: \"If I define the lengths of three straight lines independently, and then make these three lines 'components' of a fourth straight line equal in length to their sum, this is in no way the same procedure as if I start with a given straight line and divide this for some purpose or other — 'resolve' it, so to speak — into three parts. The length of the line in the first case invariably changes with the length of the three lines whose sum it forms; in the latter case the length of the three segments is limited from the beginning by their forming parts of a line of a given size.\" This is a decisive logical point. Composition (adding up) makes the total dependent on the parts; resolution (dividing up) makes the parts dependent on the total. Smith's claim that commodity value \"resolves itself\" into wages, profit, rent, if taken strictly, would mean the total is prior and the parts derive from it — a correct procedure as far as it goes . But Smith almost immediately shifts to treating the parts as components whose independent magnitudes determine the total — an incorrect procedure, since it leaves the total underdetermined (either it doesn't add up, or we have no independent determinants for profit and rent). Marx's point is that Smith runs these two incompatible procedures together. Marx now sums up the correct kernel of Smith's doctrine: \"if we stick to what is correct in Smith's presentation, i.e., that that value contained in the society's annual commodity product which is newly created by the year's labour is equal (just as with any single commodity or the product of a day, week, etc.) to the sum of the variable capital advanced (i.e., to that value component destined to serve again for the purchase of labour-power), plus the surplus-value ... if we bear in mind moreover that Smith lumps together labour in its value-creating capacity, as expenditure of labour-power, with labour in its capacity of creating use-value, i.e., labour spent in useful, purposive form — then the whole conception boils down to this: The value of any commodity is the product of labour; so too, therefore, is the value of the product of a year's labour or the value of the society's annual commodity product. Since however all labour can be resolved into (1) necessary labour-time, in which the worker simply reproduces an equivalent for the capital advanced in purchasing his labour-power, and (2) surplus labour, by which he supplies the capitalist with a value for which the latter pays no equivalent, i.e., a surplus-value, all commodity value can then be resolved simply into these two different components, and in the last analysis it forms, as wages, the revenue of the working class, and as surplus-value, that of the capitalist class.\" Now the crucial critical move: \"As far as the constant capital value is concerned, however, i.e., the value of the means of production consumed in the production of the annual product, there is no way of telling how this value comes into the value of the new product (except in the mere phrase that the capitalist charges it to the buyer when he sells his commodity), but 'ultimately,' since the means of production are themselves the product of labour, this portion of value can in turn consist only of an equivalent for the variable capital and the surplus-value; that is, it can consist only of the product of necessary labour and surplus labour.\" Marx then makes his final critical move against the \"ultimately\" argument: \"If the values of these means of production function in the hands of their employers as capital values, this does not hinder them from being divisible 'originally' (and if one goes to the root of it, in other people's hands, even though earlier) into the same two portions of value, i.e., into two different sources of revenue.\" But this is sophistical — as Marx has shown — because it simply pushes the problem one step backward ad infinitum . What Smith needed to show was not that a previous generation's products had once been v + s, but that the current year's product replaces the c it consumes without itself requiring c . This requires a real theory of the coexistence of value-transfer (by concrete labour) with value-creation (by abstract labour), which Smith does not have. The subsection closes with an important concession to Smith: \"A correct point in all this is that in the movement of the social capital — i.e., of the totality of individual capitals — things look different from the way they do when each individual capital is taken separately, i.e., from the standpoint of each individual capitalist. For the latter, commodity value can be resolved into (1) a constant element (a fourth element, as Smith says) and (2) the sum of wages and surplus-value, i.e., wages, profit and rent. From the social standpoint, however, Smith's fourth element, the constant capital value, simply vanishes.\" This is a moment of remarkable dialectical generosity. Marx acknowledges that Smith's theory is not simply wrong; there is a perspective from which the constant element \"vanishes.\" At the individual-capitalist level, c is a real fourth element. At the social level, c is a portion of the annual product — specifically, the portion consisting of means of production — that is never available as revenue for anyone, but is always functioning as capital. It does not \"vanish\" in the sense of ceasing to exist; it \"vanishes\" in the sense of not entering the revenue fund. Smith's confusion is precisely that he treats the social standpoint as if only the individual standpoint counted — as if the sum of consumable revenues exhausted the annual product. Summary: The Deeper Foundation of Smith's Error Subsection (e) recapitulates and deepens the critique. \"The absurd formula that the three revenues of wages, profit and rent form three 'components' of commodity value, derives in Smith's case from the more plausible formula that commodity value 'resolves itself' into these three components. This too is false, even on the assumption that commodity value can be divided simply into the equivalent for the labour-power used up and the surplus-value created by the latter.\" Note the fine logical discrimination. There are two distinct claims: - (A) Commodity value = the sum of wages + profit + rent (composition). This is absurd. - (B) Commodity value resolves itself into wages + profit + rent (resolution). This is more plausible but still false , because it omits c even as a residual category. Yet, Marx says, even (B)'s error \"is based in turn on a deeper and true foundation.\" This deeper foundation is the reality of capitalist production itself: \"Capitalist production rests on the fact that the productive worker sells his own labour-power as a commodity to the capitalist, in whose hands it then functions simply as an element of his productive capital. This transaction (the sale and purchase of labour-power) does not just introduce the production process, but implicitly determines its specific character.\" Marx then recalls the fundamental structure of the analysis from earlier volumes: \"The production of a use-value, and even of a commodity (something that can also be undertaken by independent productive workers), is here only a means for the production of absolute and relative surplus-value for the capitalist. In analysing the production process, therefore, we saw how the production of absolute and relative surplus-value determines (1) the duration of the daily labour process, and (2) the whole social and technical shape of capitalist production.\" Within this process, three distinct functions must be distinguished: \"(a) the mere maintenance of value (of the constant capital value), (b) the actual reproduction of value advanced (the equivalent for labour-power), and (c) the production of surplus-value, i.e., of value for which the capitalist neither advanced a previous equivalent, nor advances one after the event.\" The appropriative act — \"the appropriation of surplus-value (of a value over and above the equivalent of the value advanced by the capitalist)\" — even though it is preceded by the purchase and sale of labour-power, \"is an act performed within the production process itself, and forms an essential moment of the latter.\" This is the crucial point: surplus-value is not appropriated in circulation (as if the capitalist bought cheap and sold dear); it is appropriated in production, from the unpaid labour-time of the worker. Marx adds a foundational point about the presuppositions of the whole system: \"The introductory act of circulation, the purchase and sale of labour-power, itself depends in turn on a distribution of the social elements of production which is the presupposition and premise of the distribution of social products, viz., the separation between labour-power as a commodity for the worker, and the means of production as the property of non-workers.\" This is one of the most important sentences in the chapter. The historical presupposition of the entire capitalist process is the separation of the producer from the means of production. This is a precondition that cannot be generated within the process itself; it must be historically established. Once established, the process reproduces it every time it runs (the worker must sell labour-power to eat; the capitalist must buy it to profit). Yet, having said all this, Marx insists on a further distinction: \"At the same time, however, this appropriation of surplus-value, the separation of value production into the reproduction of the value advanced and the production of a new value that does not replace any equivalent (a surplus-value), in no way affects the substance of value itself and the nature of value production. The substance of value is and remains nothing more than expended labour-power — labour independent of its particular useful character — and value production is nothing but the process of this expenditure.\" This is essential. The social form of value production (capitalist appropriation) does not affect the substance of value (expended labour-power). The specific appropriation relations are separable from the value-substance. This is what allows Marx to compare capitalist value production with other historically specific forms: \"If the feudal serf, for example, expends his labour-power for six days of the week, i.e., works for these six days, it makes no difference to the fact of this expenditure of labour-power as such that he may work for three of these days on his own field and three others on his lord's field and for his lord. Both his voluntary labour for himself and his forced labour for his lord are equally labour; in as much as this is considered as labour in relation to the values — or even the useful products — created by it, there is no distinction between these six days' labour. The distinction is solely related to the different situations which have given rise to the expenditure of his labour-power in the two halves of the six-day working period. It is just the same with the necessary and surplus labour of the wage-labourers.\" The feudal serf analogy is illuminating. The serf's six days constitute the same quantity of abstract labour regardless of who benefits. The distinction between \"own work\" and \"corvée\" is not a distinction within the labour ; it is a distinction in the social conditions under which the labour is performed . Similarly with the wage-labourer: necessary labour and surplus labour are the same abstract labour; they differ only in the social relations under which the products are appropriated. This is the deep truth that Smith's category of revenue obscures — by treating revenues as the \"elements\" of value, Smith makes it appear as if the social distribution is constitutive of the value itself. Marx now delivers the key exposition of the finished commodity and its value-structure . \"The process of production disappears in the finished commodity. The fact that labour-power was expended to create it now appears in the form that the commodity has the following concrete property: it possesses value. The magnitude of this value is measured by the amount of labour expended; the commodity value cannot be resolved into anything further, and consists of nothing more.\" He then gives a second geometric metaphor, richer than the first: \"If I draw a straight line of a certain length, I have, in the first place, 'produced' a straight line (only symbolically, of course, as I am aware from the start) by my manner of drawing, practised in accordance with certain rules (laws) that are independent of me. If I divide this line into three segments (which may be required for a certain problem), then each of these three pieces remains a straight line, as before, and this division does not resolve the line whose parts they are into anything other than a straight line, e.g., a curve of some kind. Just as little can I divide this line of a given length in such a way that the sum of these parts is greater than the undivided line itself; the length of the undivided line in other words is not determined by the lengths of the segments into which it is divided. It is rather the relative lengths of the latter that are limited in advance by the limits of the line of which they are parts.\" Several points are packed into this metaphor. (1) The line's existence as a line is owed to the act of drawing, which follows rules independent of the drawer's will. Analogously, value exists by virtue of labour-time, under laws of socially necessary labour-time not subject to the individual's caprice. (2) Dividing the line into parts does not change its nature. So dividing value into c + v + s does not change the nature of value as congealed labour. (3) The parts cannot sum to more than the whole. So profit and rent cannot be independent determinants that \"add up\" beyond the total value. (4) The parts' relative magnitudes are determined by the limits of the whole. So the distribution of value is bound by the total, not the reverse. \"In this respect, the commodity produced by the capitalist is in no way distinguished from a commodity produced by an independent worker, by a community of workers, or by slaves. In our case, however, the entire product of labour and its entire value belongs to the capitalist. Just like any other producer, he has first to transform the commodity into money by selling it, before he can manipulate it any further; he must convert it into the form of the universal equivalent.\" Marx now analyzes the finished product's value into its parts, in a systematic sequence. First , the constant capital component. \"A part of this value is merely the value of the means of production used up to produce the commodity, which reappears in a new form. This value has not been produced in the process of producing the commodity, for the means of production already possessed this value before the production process in question and independently of it. They entered this process as the bearers of this value, and all that has been replaced and changed is simply its form of appearance. For the capitalist, this part of the commodity value forms an equivalent for the portion of the constant capital value he advanced that has been consumed in the commodity's production.\" The value now exists as a component of the new commodity; it will be converted back into means of production when the commodity is sold. \"The value character of a commodity is in no way changed by the function of this value as capital.\" Again, the function does not change the nature. Second , the variable capital component. \"A second portion of the commodity's value is the value of the labour-power that the worker sells to the capitalist. This is determined, just like the value of the means of production, independently of the production process which the labour-power is to enter, and is fixed in an act of circulation, the purchase and sale of labour-power, before this goes into the production process. In the course of his function — the expenditure of his labour-power — the wage-labourer produces a commodity value equal to the value that the capitalist has to pay him for the use of his labour-power. He gives the capitalist this value in commodities, and the capitalist pays him the same in money. If this part of the commodity value is for the capitalist only an equivalent for the variable capital that he has to advance in wages, this makes absolutely no difference to the fact that it is a commodity value newly created during the production process, consisting exactly like the surplus-value of past expenditure of labour-power.\" Third , the surplus-value component. \"Over and above them both, there is still the surplus-value. This has in common with the value component that replaces the variable capital advanced in wages that it is a value newly created by the workers — congealed labour. It is just that it does not cost anything to the owner of the total product, the capitalist. This circumstance is what enables the capitalist to consume it completely as revenue, as long as he does not have to deduct parts of it for others with a stake in it — e.g., rent for the landlords — in which case these portions then constitute the revenues of third parties of this kind. This same circumstance was also the driving motive for our capitalist to concern himself with commodity production at all. But neither his original benevolent intention to hunt out surplus-value, nor the subsequent spending of this as revenue by himself and others, affects the surplus-value as such. It in no way alters the fact that it is unpaid congealed labour, nor does it alter its size, which is determined by quite other factors.\" Marx then delivers a sharply ironic observation about Smith's logic: \"Given that Adam Smith already wanted to concern himself in dealing with commodity value with the roles that the various parts of this play in the overall process of reproduction, it should have been evident that, if certain portions function as revenue, others must just as constantly function as capital — and on his logic, this means that these should also have been described as constituent parts of the commodity value, or parts into which it is resolved.\" In other words, if Smith is going to explain value by its functions (as revenue), then he must also explain it by its other function (as capital) — which would force him to include c. His own method should have forced him to correct himself. Marx now diagnoses Smith's methodological error at the deepest level: \"Adam Smith identifies commodity production in general with capitalist commodity production; the means of production are 'capital' from the beginning, labour is wage-labour.\" Smith says in the Introduction (p. 105): \"The number of useful and productive labourers ... is everywhere in proportion to the quantity of capital stock which is employed in setting them to work.\" Marx comments: \"In short, the various factors of the labour process — objective and personal — appear from the start in the character masks of the era of capitalist production. The analysis of commodity value therefore directly coincides with the question as to how far this value is, on the one hand, simply the equivalent for the capital laid out, and how far, on the other hand, it constitutes 'free' value that does not replace any capital value advanced, i.e., is surplus-value.\" The point is that Smith's analysis of commodity value is already the analysis of capitalist commodity value. He doesn't have a prior, abstract analysis of value in general on which to fall back. He jumps directly into the comparison of value to \"capital laid out\" vs. \"free\" surplus. This makes him prone to reading the specific forms of capitalist value distribution back into the general concept of value. \"The portions of commodity value compared from this standpoint are thereby transformed surreptitiously into its independent 'component parts,' and ultimately into the 'sources of all value.'\" And so his final error is total: \"A further conclusion is that commodity value is composed of revenues of various kinds, or alternatively is 'resolved into' these revenues, so that it is not the revenues that consist of commodity value, but rather the commodity value that consists of 'revenues.'\" This is the inversion in its pure form. Value does not consist of revenues; revenues consist of value. Smith has turned the world upside down. \"But just as it scarcely affects the nature of a commodity value as commodity value, or of money as money, whether it functions as capital value or not, so equally a commodity value is scarcely altered by the fact that it goes on to function as revenue for this person or that.\" Statement of the correct method: value is prior to all its social functions. Marx's prescription: \"The commodities Smith is dealing with here are commodity capital from the start (and therefore include surplus-value as well as the capital value consumed in their production), i.e., they are commodities produced in the capitalist manner, the result of the capitalist production process. This last should therefore have been the object of prior analysis, together with the process of valorization and value formation that it involves. And since the very presupposition of this is in turn commodity circulation, its presentation also requires, therefore, an independent and prior analysis of the commodity.\" This is a direct statement of Marx's own method in Capital Volume 1: begin with the analysis of the commodity and money (Part One), then move to the capitalist production process. The footnote attached to this passage (numbered 7 n. in the text) explicitly notes: \"This is of course Marx's own procedure in Capital , Volume 1, of which commences with the analysis of 'Commodities and Money' (Part One), and continues with the all-round analysis of the capitalist production process. The separate text on 'The Results of the Immediate Process of Production,' published in the present edition as an appendix to Volume 1, analyses commodities produced in the capitalist manner as this 'result' and this lays the basis for the analysis of capital circulation in Volume 2.\" The conclusion of this section: \"Even when Smith in his 'esoteric' aspect occasionally comes up with something correct, he takes account of value formation only in connection with the analysis of commodities, i.e., the analysis of commodity capital.\" So Smith never reaches the level of abstraction at which value in general can be analyzed independently of its capitalist form. This is the methodological failure from which all others flow. Later Writers: The Persistence and Degeneration of the Dogma The final section of the chapter surveys Smith's successors. It is compact but highly damning. The section is an addition from Manuscript II, and represents Marx's ongoing effort to establish the dogma's historical transmission. Ricardo \"reproduces Adam Smith's theory almost verbatim.\" Marx quotes from Ricardo's Principles (p. 169, note): \"It must be understood that all the productions of a country are consumed; but it makes the greatest difference imaginable whether they are consumed by those who reproduce, or by those who do not reproduce another value. When we say that revenue is saved, and added to capital, what we mean is, that the portion of revenue, so said to be added to capital, is consumed by productive instead of unproductive labourers.\" Marx notes: \"In point of fact Ricardo completely accepted Smith's theory of the resolution of commodity price into wages and surplus-value (or variable capital and surplus-value).\" Yet Marx grants Ricardo two important departures from Smith. \"(1) the components of surplus-value: he eliminates ground-rent as a necessary element of it; (2) Ricardo breaks down commodity price into these components. The magnitude of value thus comes first. He takes the sum of the components as a given magnitude, rather than deriving the magnitude of value of the commodity after the event by adding together its components, as Smith often does, even against his own better judgement.\" The second point is important. Ricardo avoids Smith's \"composition\" fallacy. He does not say \"value = the sum of the revenues\"; he says \"value is a given magnitude, which is then divided into the revenues.\" This is closer to Marx's procedure. But Ricardo still inherits Smith's omission of c. He never questions the v + s framework itself; he merely gets the internal logic of distribution right. This is why Marx elsewhere calls Ricardo \"the last great representative of classical political economy\" but also criticizes him for accepting Smith's framework. Ramsay is then quoted, because he is one of the few who breaks with Smith on the substantive point. In his An Essay on the Distribution of Wealth (Edinburgh, 1836), p. 174: \"He [Ricardo] seems always to consider the whole produce as divided between wages and profits, forgetting the part necessary for replacing fixed capital.\" Marx notes that Ramsay understands by \"fixed capital\" what Marx calls constant capital , and quotes Ramsay's definition: \"Fixed capital exists in a form in which, though assisting to raise the future commodity, it does not maintain labourers\" (p. 59). The definition is imprecise (it identifies fixed capital by its failure to maintain labourers, rather than by its material role in production), but the substantive point — that a portion of the produce must replace fixed capital and cannot be divided as wages and profits — is correct. This will reappear as one of the few genuine advances on Smith. Marx now turns to the logical degeneration of Smith's doctrine. \"Adam Smith baulked at the logical conclusion of his resolution of commodity value, and thus of the value of the annual social product, into wages and surplus-value, i.e., simply into revenue: the conclusion that the total annual product could then be entirely consumed. It is never the original thinkers who draw the absurd conclusions. They rather leave this for the Says and MacCullochs.\" Jean-Baptiste Say \"certainly has an easy time of it. What is for one person an advance of capital is for the other revenue and net product, or was so at least; the distinction between gross and net product is purely subjective, and 'thus the entire value of all products has been distributed in society as revenue' ( Traité d'économie politique , 1817, II, p. 64).\" Say continues: \"The total value of any product is composed of the profits of the landlords, the capitalists and the artisans\" (Marx's parenthetical: \"thus wages figure here as profits des industrieux [artisans' profits]!\") \"who have contributed to its production. This means that the society's revenue is equal to the gross value produced, and not as the sect of economists\" (the Physiocrats) \"believed, only equal to the net product of the soil\" (p. 63). Say's conclusion is precisely the absurd one Smith had refused to draw: that the entire value of all products is revenue, and (consequently) that a society can consume its entire annual product without disinvestment. Marx notes: \"This discovery of Say's was taken over by Proudhon, among others.\" The sarcasm is blunt: Say's \"discovery\" is the logical consequence of Smith's error, but Smith had been prudent enough not to state it. Say lacks even that prudence. Storch accepts Smith's doctrine \"in principle\" but finds Say's application \"untenable.\" His critique: \"If it is held that the revenue of a nation is equal to its gross product, i.e., that no capital (he should say no constant capital) needs to be deducted, it must also be conceded that this nation can consume the entire value of its annual product unproductively, without making the least inroad on its future revenue.... The products that compose the (constant) capital of a nation are not consumable\" ( Considérations sur la nature du revenu national , Paris, 1842, pp. 147, 150). Marx points out the inconsistency in Storch's position: \"Storch has forgotten to tell us how the existence of this constant portion of capital fits in with the analysis of prices that he has taken over from Smith, in which commodity value contains only wages and surplus-value, but no portion of constant capital.\" This is the crucial move. Storch sees the constant portion of capital (empirically), but his price theory (taken from Smith) cannot accommodate it . \"It is only through Say that he realizes that this price analysis leads to absurd results, and his own last word on it is 'that it is impossible to resolve the necessary price into its simplest elements' ( Cours d'économie politique , Petersburg, 1815, II, p. 141).\" Storch ends with an implicit retraction of Smith's price analysis, but he cannot correct it — he just declares the problem insoluble. This is a fitting image of the state of the theory before Marx: those who see the difficulty cannot resolve it within Smith's framework. Sismondi , who \"deals particularly with the relation between capital and revenue, and in actual fact makes his particular conception of this relationship the differentia specifica of his Nouveaux Principes , did not utter a single scientific word or contribute one jot or tittle to the explanation of the problem.\" This is a scathing verdict on the most important critic of capitalism in the generation after Smith. Sismondi saw the problem (the relation between capital and revenue) but could not solve it because he remained within Smith's framework. Barton, Ramsay, and Cherbuliez \"make attempts to go beyond Smith's conception. They fail because they pose the problem one-sidedly from the start, by not clearly separating the distinction between constant and variable capital from the distinction between fixed and circulating capital.\" This is a key diagnosis. The fixed/circulating distinction (Smith's) and the constant/variable distinction (Marx's) are different axes. Fixed/circulating concerns the velocity of turnover (how quickly value is returned through the sale of the product). Constant/variable concerns the source of surplus-value (constant capital doesn't produce surplus-value; variable capital does). Conflating them — as Ramsay, Barton, and Cherbuliez do — prevents any solution: they think that because some capital is \"fixed\" it doesn't produce surplus-value (or because some is \"circulating\" it does), which is the wrong criterion. John Stuart Mill \"reproduces the doctrine handed down by Adam Smith to his successors, with his customary air of self-importance.\" The sarcasm is brief and complete. Mill, the most technically accomplished economist of the mid-century, simply perpetuates the error. The chapter's concluding sentence: \"The result is that Smith's confusion persists to this day, and his dogma forms an article of orthodox belief in political economy.\" This is the final verdict. Despite a century of critique — Physiocratic, Ricardian, Sismondian, Storch's, Ramsay's — the fundamental error stands. It is the doxa of the discipline — orthodox, unquestioned, reproduced in textbooks. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles Revealed Principle 1: Value is prior to revenue. The most important conceptual lesson of the chapter is the ordering of value and revenue. Smith inverted it, treating revenues (wages, profit, rent) as the constituent elements of value. Marx restores the correct order: value is the substance (congealed labour); revenues are the uses to which portions of value are put. As Marx puts it: \"It is not the revenues that consist of commodity value, but rather the commodity value that consists of 'revenues'\" — this is Smith's error in its pure form, and it is precisely the inversion Marx corrects. The consequences of this correction are enormous. Once revenues are seen as derivatives, the idea that additional revenue-receivers (bankers, professors, prostitutes, mercenaries) produce value evaporates; their incomes are derivative claims on value produced elsewhere. This is the deep truth that Smith's exoteric confusion concealed. Principle 2: Function does not affect substance. A closely related principle is dropped in repeatedly through the chapter's geometric analogies. Whether 10 shillings is spent as capital or as revenue, its value is 10 shillings. Whether a line serves as the base of a triangle or an axis of an ellipse, its length is unchanged. The function a value serves (capital vs. revenue) is a matter of social form; it does not alter the substance of value (congealed labour) or its magnitude (determined by labour-time). This is what allows Marx to insist on the independence of the value of labour-power from the wage's function as revenue. It also grounds the critique of Smith: by treating function (revenue) as constitutive of value, Smith commits a category error — a confusion of the substance with its social functions. Principle 3: The twofold character of labour. This is the technical key that unlocks the whole problem. The labour spent in production is (a) abstract, value-creating labour, which adds new value (v + s); and (b) concrete, useful labour, which preserves and transfers the value of the consumed means of production (c) into the new product. Because Smith does not distinguish these two aspects, he cannot explain where c comes from — unless he \"dissolves\" it by an infinite regress (always pushing c back to previous productions, and so on). Once the distinction is in place, the answer is elegant: c does not have to be created in the current year; it only has to be preserved . Concrete labour does not manufacture the value of the wood it transforms into a table; it merely retains it. Hence the annual value product (v + s) is strictly less than the value of the annual product (c + v + s), without any paradox. Principle 4: The division of the annual product into two departments. Marx's constructive conclusion in the \"General Perspectives\" section is that Smith's scattered insights imply a two-departmental analysis: Department I produces means of production; Department II produces means of consumption. The value components of Department I's product — including the portion that represents wages and surplus-value — are not consumable in material form; they must be exchanged with Department II to acquire consumable goods. Conversely, Department II's product, while consisting of consumption goods, includes a value component (c₂) that is not available as revenue for anyone — it must be exchanged with Department I for replacement means of production. This two-department schema is the seed of the reproduction schemas in Capital Vol. 2, Part 3. Principle 5: The relation between circulation and production. The chapter also teaches a crucial methodological lesson about the separation of analytical levels. Acts of circulation (buying, selling, receiving money, spending it) — when \"simply considered by themselves\" — are indifferent to what happens to the money received or the things bought. The circular-flow aspects of wages and profits, examined purely as circulation, are separate from the production-process aspects (value creation, surplus-value appropriation). Smith conflates these; Marx separates them. This methodological lesson grounds the distinction between the analysis of commodity circulation ( Capital Vol. 1, Part 1), the analysis of production ( Capital Vol. 1, Parts 2-7), and the analysis of the circulation of capital ( Capital Vol. 2). Broader Practical and Theoretical Implications For the theory of distribution. The chapter's most direct implication is that any theory of distribution that treats wages, profit, and rent as additive components of price is logically incoherent. Such theories cannot account for the total (the parts don't add up to the whole), and they cannot independently determine the magnitudes of profit and rent (except by residual). Ricardo's \"given magnitude, then distributed\" approach is logically superior to Smith's \"components added up\" approach — but even Ricardo's approach omits c. Marx's correct approach: value (c + v + s) is determined by labour-time; then it is distributed among capitalists (profit), landlords (rent), and workers (wages). The distribution is constrained by the total . For the theory of crisis. The two-department schema has enormous implications for crisis theory (which Marx develops elsewhere). If Department I and Department II must exchange in specific proportions — c₂ = v₁ + s₁ and c₁ + c₂ = total means of production — then any disproportion between the departments can produce a crisis. The reproduction schemas allow Marx to model the conditions of balanced growth under simple and expanded reproduction, and to locate sources of imbalance. The critique of Smith's v + s framework clears the way for this. For the critique of vulgar economics. The chapter ends with a flourish about the \"orthodox belief\" of political economy. The point is that vulgar economics — the tradition descending from Say, Bastiat, Roscher, and (in Marx's view) Mill — is built on the Smithian confusion of revenues with value components. The critique of Smith's constant-capital blind spot is thus also a critique of the entire apologetic tradition that treats the surface categories of distribution as the deep structure of production. For the critique of the Physiocrats. The chapter opens with an appreciative but critical reconstruction of Quesnay. Marx's treatment complicates the standard line that Physiocracy was a mere error. He insists that beneath the \"feudal signboard\" (the ideological self-presentation as defenders of landed property) lies the first systematic theory of capitalist production. The Physiocrats' method — the Tableau as a flow analysis between classes — anticipates Marx's own two-departmental analysis. Their error (agriculture as the sole source of surplus-value) is compensated by their insight (reproduction as a social, class-structured process). The lesson is methodological: a theory's ideological self-understanding may be at odds with its substantive content; the critic's task is to extract the content from the form, not to reject the whole on the basis of its ideological wrapping. For the theory of exploitation. Marx's careful argument about the creation of the payment fund by the worker himself has important implications. \"The worker therefore himself creates the payment fund from which the capitalist pays him.\" This is the material basis for the appearance that the capitalist \"advances\" wages. It also grounds the argument — developed more fully in Capital Vol. 1 — that wages are a form of the value of labour-power and not a share of the product determined by the capitalist's goodwill. Furthermore, the worker's spending of his wage reproduces for the capitalist \"the only instrument by means of which he can remain a capitalist.\" The reproduction of labour-power is thus not just a private affair; it is the reproduction of the fundamental condition of capitalist production. For the concept of value itself. The chapter clarifies Marx's concept of value in a way that is often lost in summary. Value is \"expended labour-power — labour independent of its particular useful character.\" It is real, definite, and measurable. It is not a mere accounting convention; it is a social substance that can be transferred (by concrete labour) or created (by abstract labour). It is prior to and independent of the social forms in which it appears (as capital, as revenue, as wealth). The geometric metaphors (straight line) underscore this realist commitment: just as a line's length is real and unchanged by its function, value's magnitude is real and unchanged by its social role. For the methodology of the history of ideas. The chapter demonstrates Marx's characteristic historiographical method: he reads the tradition dialectically, looking for the rational kernel inside the mystical shell , and treats even \"errors\" as products of deeper truths. Smith's error — treating revenues as components of value — \"is based in turn on a deeper and true foundation,\" namely the reality of capitalist production relations. And the Physiocrats' error — the narrowness of seeing only agriculture as productive — \"comes to grips with the main question\" because natural reproduction visibly requires capital replacement. Marx's history of economics is not a tale of fools; it is a tale of partial insights whose limits are historically explicable. This is why Marx's critiques of Smith, Ricardo, and even Say are not merely dismissive but precisely diagnostic: he identifies the specific source of each error in the theoretical framework, and shows why it was necessary given the analytical instruments available. For the relationship between Marx and his predecessors. The chapter makes plain that Marx's own analysis is not a refutation of classical political economy from outside, but a resolution of its internal contradictions. Smith and Ricardo developed all the essential categories (value, surplus-value, capital, reproduction) but could not assemble them coherently because of the residual fixed/circulating (rather than constant/variable) distinction and the confusion of value with its distributive forms. Marx's achievement is to complete the classical project by introducing the distinction (constant/variable) and the framework (two departments) that the classics lacked. The chapter is thus not merely historical but self-clarifying : by showing where Smith went wrong, Marx is showing where his own solution necessarily goes. Philosophical Implications The dialectical structure of the argument. Throughout the chapter, Marx proceeds dialectically. He shows that Smith saw the correct phenomena (the workers producing means of production vs. consumption goods; the \"circulating capital of society\" with distinct properties from individual circulating capital; the constant capital as a \"fourth element\" at the level of the individual capitalist) but could not organize them into a coherent whole. The reason is that his categories (fixed/circulating) were not adequate to the phenomena ; he needed different categories (constant/variable; means of production/means of consumption). The dialectical progression is from surface confusions to underlying distinctions to a reconstructed system. This is the method that Marx's own Capital enacts: start from the commodity (surface), move to value (substance), then to surplus-value (process), then to reproduction (whole). The concept of \"revenue\" and its ideological function. The chapter's deep theme is the ideological function of the category of revenue. By treating revenues as original \"sources\" of value, Smith opens the door for a whole apologetic tradition that presents the existing distribution of the product as natural, necessary, and original. Marx's counter-argument is that revenue is a distribution of value created in production; it is derivative, not original; and the only \"original\" source is labour. The critique is not just logical; it is ideological-critical . It exposes how the surface categories of economic science can serve to legitimate exploitation by erasing its traces. Historical materialism implicit in the argument. The chapter's insistence on the specific historical presuppositions of capitalist production — \"the separation between labour-power as a commodity for the worker, and the means of production as the property of non-workers\" — points to the historical-materialist framework. Capitalist production is not a natural form of economic life; it requires historical preconditions. Once established, it reproduces those preconditions internally. The critique of Smith is therefore also a critique of the attempt to treat the categories of capitalist production as natural and universal. The philosophical realism of the value-form. Finally, the chapter defends a kind of realism about value: value is real, not merely conventional or perceptual; it has substance and magnitude; it is independent of the forms (capital, revenue) in which it appears. This is a philosophical position that runs counter both to the empiricist reduction of economics to surface transactions and to the idealist reduction of value to subjective utility. It is, in its own register, a materialist realism: value is congealed labour-power, and the analysis of value is the analysis of this material substance. Concluding Reflection Chapter 19 is, in the end, a chapter about how to see correctly . The Physiocrats saw correctly, through a glass darkly; Smith saw truly esoteric things but kept losing them in exoteric formulations; Ricardo got the distribution right but accepted the wrong framework; Say drew the absurd conclusion; Storch saw the difficulty and gave up; Ramsay saw part of the problem and posed it one-sidedly; Sismondi saw the question and produced nothing; Mill repeated the tradition with self-satisfaction. The result: an entire discipline built on a mistake. Marx's contribution is to diagnose the mistake, extract the correct kernel from the tradition, and build a system in which — as he was soon to show in the reproduction schemas — the entire problem is solved. The chapter's final sentence, \"The result is that Smith's confusion persists to this day, and his dogma forms an article of orthodox belief in political economy,\" is not merely a lament. It is a challenge: to see through the confusion, to break with the dogma, and to build a science in which value is prior to revenue, production is prior to distribution, and capital's reproduction is understood in its specific, historical, class-structured reality. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 18 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 20"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 20", "title": "Chapter 20", "subtitle": "Simple Reproduction", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 20/Chapter 20 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 39152, "read_time": "196 min read", "tags": ["Social Reproduction Schemes (Dept I & II)", "Critique of the Smithian Dogma"], "keywords": ["Simple Reproduction Schemes", "Department I (Means of Production) vs Department II (Means of Consumption)", "Fundamental Equilibrium Condition: I(v + s) = IIc", "Exchange within Department I (Ic Replacement)", "Exchange within Department II (IIv + IIs Consumption)", "Money Mediation of Macro-Economic Exchanges", "Fixed Capital Wear, Replacement, and Imbalances"], "text": "Chapter 20 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 39,044 words ⏱️ ~195 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE ANALYSIS OF CHAPTER 20: SIMPLE REPRODUCTION 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 20 of Capital , Volume 2, entitled \"Simple Reproduction,\" is the decisive pivot of Marx's entire analysis of the circulation and reproduction of the total social capital. It is here that Marx abandons the analysis of the individual capital—the perspective that dominates Volumes 1 and the earlier parts of Volume 2—and undertakes an examination of the total annual product of society as a whole: its value components, its material components, and the exchanges that must occur between the two great departments of social production if the annual cycle is to begin anew on the same scale. The chapter is not a description of a static equilibrium but a theoretical model designed to expose the internal contradictions, hidden mediations, and necessary proportionalities of the capitalist mode of production considered as a self-reproducing totality. The central problem the chapter sets out to solve is deceptively simple to state and extraordinarily difficult to resolve. When we consider the total annual commodity product of society—what Marx calls C' —we find that it breaks down, in value terms, into constant capital (c), variable capital (v), and surplus-value (s). The question is: how can this total product be realized , i.e. sold, replaced in kind, and distributed such that each of its components finds its proper destination? Specifically: (1) how is the capital consumed in production replaced in value and in material form out of the annual product? (2) How is this replacement intertwined with the consumption of surplus-value by capitalists and of wages by workers? (3) How does money mediate these exchanges, and where does the money come from? Marx's answer, developed with meticulous care across thirteen sections and two substantial appendices (sections 11 and 12), establishes the fundamental law of simple reproduction— I(v + s) = IIc —and reveals that the value product of the entire social working day (v + s) is equal to the value of the means of consumption annually produced, while two-thirds of the social working day is in fact spent producing constant capital that can never resolve into revenue. The intellectual objective is threefold. First, Marx wants to demonstrate that the total annual product cannot be resolved simply into wages plus surplus-value (Adam Smith's dogma), but necessarily contains a constant capital component that reappears in a new material form. Second, he wants to expose the specific material conditions of reproduction—the proportionalities between departments and subdivisions—that capitalist production requires but does not consciously plan. Third, he wants to show how money circulation, far from being a mere technical lubricant, is an immanent moment of the reproduction process, generating hoards, creating the possibility of crises even under simple reproduction, and concealing the true sources of value and revenue from the vulgar economists (Destutt de Tracy, Adam Smith, Tooke, Ramsay). Fit within the broader theoretical framework. This chapter is the hinge of Volume 2 and of the entire Capital project. Having established, in Parts 1 and 2, the circuits of individual capital (M–C…P…C'–M' and C'–M'–C…P) and the turnover of capital, Marx now abstracts from the individual to the social. The chapter prepares for the analysis of expanded reproduction (accumulation on a social scale, which appears in the following chapter), for the analysis of the credit system (Volume 3), and for the critique of political economy that runs throughout all three volumes. By demonstrating that a part of the social product must always be productively consumed—must function as capital and cannot be consumed as revenue—Marx refutes the underconsumptionist and \"sound common sense\" theories of crises, and provides the analytical foundation for his later treatment of the falling rate of profit, the credit system, and the tendency toward crisis. The chapter is also, in a sense, the tour de force of Volume 2: it is here that Marx's mastery of the dialectic of value and use-value, of form and substance, of the individual and the social, is most fully on display. The chapter's conclusion—the savage critique of Destutt de Tracy as the epitome of \"bourgeois cretinism in its ultimate state of bliss\"—is not a mere rhetorical flourish. It summarizes the whole point: that the surface appearances of circulation (the reflux of money, the apparent enrichment through exchange) generate systematic illusions about the nature of the capitalist system, and that the political economists, by remaining trapped in these appearances, are unable to grasp the real relationships. Marx's task in this chapter is precisely to penetrate beneath these appearances and lay bare the inner mechanism of social reproduction. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS A. The Formulation of the Problem: Why the Formal Presentation of Individual Capital Breaks Down at the Social Level Marx opens the chapter by asking us to consider the result of the annual functioning of the social capital—that is, the total capital of which individual capitals are only fractions, their movements being simultaneously individual movements and integral links in the total movement. The commodity product supplied by society in the course of the year includes both the parts that replace capital (social reproduction) and the parts that accrue to the consumption fund (productive and unproductive consumption). This consumption, crucially, includes the reproduction of the capitalist class and the working class, and hence also the reproduction of the capitalist character of the entire production process. This is a critical point: reproduction is not merely economic but social and class-structural ; it reproduces the relations of production themselves. Arguments & Mechanics. The form of circulation that must be analysed is: C' – M – C… P… C' and consumption necessarily plays a role, because the starting point C' = C + c (in Marx's notation here, the commodity capital includes constant and variable capital value plus surplus-value) encompasses both individual consumption and productive consumption. Marx contrasts this with the circuits of individual capital. In the circuits M–C…P…C'–M' and P…C'–M'–C…P, the movement of capital is both starting point and concluding point, and consumption is indeed involved (the commodity must be sold), but once the sale is assumed to have occurred, it is immaterial for the movement of the individual capital what later becomes of the commodity. With the movement C'…C' , on the other hand, the preconditions for social reproduction are immediately recognizable, because it is necessary to demonstrate what becomes of each portion of the value of the overall product C'. This is the crux: at the social level, the fate of every component matters. Concepts & Terminology. The process of reproduction must now be considered \"from the standpoint of the replacement of the individual components of C' both in value and in material.\" This dual determination—value and material/use-value—is the fundamental methodological innovation of this chapter. We can no longer content ourselves, as with the value analysis of the product of an individual capital, with the assumption that the individual capitalist converts his commodity product into money by selling it and then repurchases his elements of production on the commodity market. These elements of production, in so far as they are of the objective kind, form a component of social capital just as much as the individual finished product that is exchanged for them. The movement of the part of the social commodity product consumed by the worker (in spending his wage) and by the capitalist (in spending surplus-value) not only forms an integral link in the movement of the total product but is interwoven with the movements of individual capitals, so that its course cannot be explained by simply presupposing it. The immediate form of the problem. How is the capital consumed in production replaced in its value out of the annual product, and how is the movement of this replacement intertwined with the consumption of surplus-value by capitalists and of wages by workers? Marx specifies several premises: Reproduction is on a simple scale (no accumulation). Products are exchanged at their values . No revolution in values takes place in the components of productive capital. He immediately adds nuance: in as much as prices diverge from values, this circumstance cannot exert any influence on the movement of social capital. The same mass of products is exchanged afterwards as before, even though the value relationships in which individual capitalists are involved are no longer proportionate to their respective advances and to the quantities of surplus-value produced by each. As far as revolutions in value are concerned, they change nothing in the relations between the value components of the total annual product as long as they are generally and evenly distributed . In so far as they are only partially and unevenly distributed, they represent disturbances which, firstly , can be understood only if they are treated as divergences from value relations that remain unchanged; secondly , given proof of the law that one part of the value of the annual product replaces constant capital and another variable capital, then a revolution in the value of constant or variable capital would in no way affect this law . It would alter only the relative magnitudes of the portions of value that function in one or the other capacity, because different values would have appeared in place of the original values. Nuances & Counter-Arguments. Marx explicitly acknowledges that in dealing with the value production and value of the individual product, the natural form of the commodity product was a matter of complete indifference—whether machines, corn, or mirrors. This was always simply an example, and any branch of production could serve as illustration. That was the \"purely formal manner of presentation,\" which is no longer sufficient once we consider the total social capital and the value of its product. The transformation of one portion of the product's value back into capital—the entry of another part into individual consumption—forms a movement within the value of the product in which the total capital has resulted; and this movement is not only a replacement of values but a replacement of materials , and is therefore conditioned not just by the mutual relations of the value components of the social product but equally by their use-values, their material shape . This is the pivotal methodological insight of the chapter. A second nuance: Simple reproduction as abstraction. Marx acknowledges that simple reproduction on the same scale seems to be an abstraction, both in the sense that the absence of any accumulation is an assumption foreign to the capitalist basis, and in the sense that the conditions in which production takes place do not remain absolutely the same in different years. The supposition is that a social capital of a given value supplies the same mass of commodity values and satisfies the same quantity of needs in both the current year and the previous year, even if the forms of commodities may change. But since, when accumulation takes place, simple reproduction still remains a part of this, and is a real factor in accumulation, it can be considered by itself. Moreover, the value of the annual product may decrease while the volume of use-values remains the same; the value may remain the same while the volume of use-values declines; the value and volume of use-values reproduced may decrease simultaneously. What emerges is that reproduction either takes place under more favourable circumstances than previously, or under more difficult ones, and the latter may result in an incomplete, defective reproduction. All this can affect only the quantitative aspect of the various elements of reproduction, and not the role that they play in the total process as capital reproducing itself or as reproduced revenue. This is a crucial methodological point: the qualitative roles (what is capital, what is revenue) remain invariant across quantitative variations—a claim whose significance will only become fully clear in section 10. B. The Two Departments: The Material Basis of the Social Schema Marx divides society's total product, and thus its total production process, into two great departments: I. Means of production: commodities that possess a form in which they either have to enter productive consumption, or at least can enter this. II. Means of consumption: commodities that possess a form in which they enter the individual consumption of the capitalist and working classes. In each department, all the various branches of production belonging to it form a single great branch of production. The total capital applied in each of these two branches forms a separate major department of the social capital. Concepts & Terminology. In each department, capital has two components: Variable capital. As far as its value goes, this equals the value of the social labour-power applied in this branch of production, i.e. the sum of the wages paid for it. Considered in its material aspect, it consists of self-acting labour-power itself, i.e. of living labour set in motion by this capital value. Constant capital. This is the value of all the means of production applied to production in this branch. It breaks down into fixed capital (machines, instruments of labour, buildings, draught animals, etc.) and circulating constant capital (materials of production, such as raw and ancillary materials, semi-finished goods, etc.). The value of the total annual product created in each department with the aid of this capital breaks down into: a component that represents the constant capital c consumed in its production (only its value being transferred to the product); and the portion of value added by the overall annual labour, which breaks down again into the replacement of the variable capital v advanced and the excess over it that forms the surplus-value s. Thus, the value of the total annual product of each department breaks down into c + v + s . This is the fundamental decomposition. Crucial distinction (nuance): The value component c, which represents the constant capital consumed in the course of production, is not the same thing as the value of the constant capital applied in production. The materials of production are completely consumed, and their value entirely transferred to the product. But only a part of the fixed capital is entirely consumed. Another part of the fixed capital (machines, buildings, etc.) continues to exist and to function just as before, even if its value is diminished by the annual wear and tear. This part of the fixed capital that continues to function does not exist for us when we consider the value of the product. It forms a part of the capital value that is independent of this newly produced commodity value and is present alongside it. Marx refers back to Volume 1, Chapter 8, pp. 311–12, where this was already shown when considering the value of the product of an individual capital. But here, he says, we must set aside for the time being the mode of consideration used there, and abstract at least provisionally from the portion of value transferred to the annual product by the wear and tear of the fixed capital, in as much as this fixed capital is not replaced again in kind in the course of the year . This deferral sets up section 11, which will deal with this point separately. This is a masterful structural choice: Marx postpones the most difficult part of the analysis (fixed capital replacement) to a dedicated section, having first established the basic schema. The Schema. For the investigation of simple reproduction, Marx uses the following schema, where the rate of valorization (s/v) is taken as 100 per cent. The figures may be in millions of marks, francs, or pounds sterling: I. Production of means of production: - Capital: 4,000c + 1,000v = 5,000 - Commodity product: 4,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s = 6,000, existing in the form of means of production. II. Production of means of consumption: - Capital: 2,000c + 500v = 2,500 - Commodity product: 2,000c + 500v + 500s = 3,000, existing in means of consumption. The total annual commodity product taken together is thus: - I. 4,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s = 6,000 means of production. - II. 2,000c + 500v + 500s = 3,000 means of consumption. The total value is 9,000 , the fixed capital that continues to function in its natural form being excluded by assumption. Three important clues. If we investigate the transactions necessary on the basis of simple reproduction (where the whole surplus-value is unproductively consumed), and ignore in the first instance monetary circulation, we gain three clues: The 500v of workers' wages in department II, and the 500s surplus-value of the capitalists in the same department, must be spent on means of consumption. Their value exists in the means of consumption to a value of 1,000 that restore to the capitalists of department II the 500v they advanced, and represent besides this their 500s. The wages and surplus-value in department II are thus converted within department II into the product of department II. (500v + 500s)II = 1,000 in means of consumption thereby drops out of the total product. This is the first clue: department II's v + s is consumed within department II. The 1,000v + 1,000s in department I must likewise be spent on means of consumption, i.e. on the products of department II. It must therefore be exchanged for the remaining part of this product, representing constant capital, to the equal amount of 2,000c . Department II receives for this an equal sum in means of production, the product of department I, which embodies the value of 1,000v + 1,000s in department I. In this way, 2,000 IIc and (1,000v + 1,000s)I drop out of the account. This is the second, and most fundamental, clue: I(v + s) is exchanged against IIc . There still remains 4,000 Ic . This consists of means of production that can only be used in department I and serve to replace the constant capital consumed there; they are therefore disposed of by mutual exchange among the individual capitalists of department I, just as the (500v + 500s)II is disposed of by exchange between workers and capitalists, or between individual capitalists, in department II. This is the third clue: department I's constant capital is replaced within department I. These three clues are, as Marx says, \"simply temporary, for the better understanding of what follows.\" They structure the entire remainder of the chapter: section 3 analyses the I(v + s) against IIc exchange; section 4 analyses the internal exchange within department II; section 6 analyses the constant capital in department I. Sections 5, 7, 8, 9, and 10 then synthesize and draw out the theoretical implications, while sections 11–13 address the most difficult problems (fixed capital, money material, and the critique of bourgeois apologetics). C. The First Exchange: I(v+s) Against IIc and the Money That Mediates It Marx begins with the major exchange between the two departments. (1,000v + 1,000s)I , values that exist in the hands of their producers in the natural form of means of production, are exchanged for 2,000 IIc , values that exist in the natural form of means of consumption. Arguments & Mechanics. The capitalist class in department II thereby converts its constant capital of 2,000 from the form of means of consumption back into that of means of production for these means of consumption—into a form in which it can function afresh as a factor of the labour process and as a constant capital value for the process of valorization. On the other hand, the equivalent for labour-power in department I (1,000 Iv) and for the surplus-value of the capitalists in this department (1,000 Is) is thereby realized in means of consumption; both are converted from their natural form of means of production into a natural form in which they can be consumed as revenue. The role of money. This mutual exchange is brought about by a money circulation which both mediates it and makes it harder to comprehend, even though it is of decisive importance, since the component of variable capital must always reappear in the money form, as money capital which is converted from the money form into labour-power. Variable capital must be advanced in the money form in all branches of production simultaneously pursued alongside one another across the entire surface of society, irrespective of whether these belong to departments I or II. The capitalist buys labour-power before it enters the production process but pays for it only at a prearranged date, after it has already been spent in the production of use-values. Just like the remaining portion of the product's value, so the part of this that is simply an equivalent for the money spent in payment for labour-power—the value portion of the product that represents the variable capital value—also belongs to the capitalist. In this particular portion of value, the worker has supplied the capitalist with the equivalent for his wage. However, it is the transformation of the commodity back into money, its sale, that again restores to the capitalist his variable capital as money capital, which he can advance once more to purchase labour-power. The concrete money-circuit. In department I, the collective capitalist has already paid the workers £1,000 (i.e. 1,000v) for the v-component of the value of a product of department I, i.e. of the value of the means of production produced by those workers. The workers use this £1,000 to purchase means of consumption of the same value from the capitalists in department II, and thereby transform half of department II's constant capital into money. The capitalists in department II, for their part, use this £1,000 to buy means of production to the value of 1,000 from the capitalists in department I; as a result, the variable capital value = 1,000v, which existed as a part of department I's product in the natural form of means of production, is transformed back again into money, and can now function once more in the hands of the department I capitalists as money capital to be converted into labour-power. In this way, through the realization of one part of their commodity capital, their variable capital flows back to them in the money form. The second half of the exchange. As far as the money needed to exchange the s-component of department I's commodity capital for the second half of department II's constant capital component, it may be advanced in various ways. In actual fact, this circulation comprises countless individual purchases and sales by individual capitalists in the two departments, and the money for this must in all circumstances originate from these capitalists, since we have already accounted for the money cast into circulation by the workers. Certain reserves of money—whether for capital advance or for expenditure of revenue—must always be present in the hands of the capitalists alongside their productive capital. The £500 example. Marx assumes that half the money is advanced by the capitalists in department II on the purchase of means of production to replace their constant capital, and the other half spent by the capitalists in department I on consumption (the proportions are immaterial). Then: - Department II advances £500 and uses this to purchase means of production from department I, so that—including the above £1,000 coming from department I's workers—it has replaced three-quarters of its constant capital in kind. - Department I uses the £500 received in this way to buy means of consumption from department II, so that half of that part of its commodity capital that consists of s has gone through the circulation c–m–c, and this, its product, has been realized in a consumption fund. - By this second process, the £500 returns to the hands of the department II capitalists as money capital which department II possesses alongside its productive capital. - Department I anticipates the sale of half the part of its commodity capital that is still in store as a product, with a money expenditure to the sum of £500 for the purchase of means of consumption from department II. - The same £500 enables department II to buy further means of production from department I and thereby replace its entire constant capital (1,000 + 500 + 500 = 2,000) in kind, while department I has realized its entire surplus-value in means of consumption. All in all, an exchange of commodities amounting to £4,000 has taken place with a monetary circulation of £2,000 , this latter sum only being as high as it is because the entire annual product is depicted as having been exchanged all at once in a few large amounts. The general conclusion on money. What is important: 1. Department II not only converts the constant capital, reproduced by it in the form of means of consumption, back into the form of means of production, but on top of this, the £500 that it advances to the circulation sphere to acquire means of production returns to it . 2. Department I not only recovers possession of its variable capital (reproduced in the form of means of production) in the money form, as money capital directly convertible back into labour-power, but besides this, the £500 that it spent before the sale of the surplus-value part of its capital on purchasing means of consumption in anticipation also flows back to it. However, it does not flow back through this actual expenditure, but rather through the subsequent sale of the part of its commodity product that bears half its surplus-value. The fundamental law. The two departments pay one another fully by the exchange of their respective commodity equivalents. The money that they cast into circulation over and above the total value of their commodities, as a means for exchanging these commodities, returns to each of them from the circulation sphere to the exact amount that each of the two cast into it. Neither has become a farthing richer from all this. Department II had a constant capital of 2,000 in the form of means of consumption, and 500 in money; it now has 2,000 in the form of means of production, and 500 in money as before. Similarly, department I has, as before, a surplus-value of 1,000 (now transformed from its own commodities, means of production, into a consumption fund), and 500 in money. The general conclusion, as far as concerns the money that the industrial capitalists cast into circulation to mediate their own commodity circulation, is that whether this is advanced on account of the constant value portion of their commodities or on account of the surplus-value existing in these commodities in so far as it is spent as revenue, the same amount flows back to the respective capitalists as they themselves advanced for the monetary circulation . The detour of department I's variable capital. As far as the re-transformation of department I's variable capital into the money form is concerned, this exists for the capitalists in department I, after they have laid it out on wages, firstly in the commodity form in which their workers have supplied it to them. They have paid it out to the workers in the money form as the price of their labour-power. They have in this way paid out the value component of their commodity product that equals the variable capital laid out in money; this is why they are also the owners of this part of the commodity product. But the section of the working class that they employ is not the buyer of the means of production they have themselves produced . The variable capital advanced in money for the payment of labour-power thus does not directly return to the capitalists of department I. It is transferred by the purchases of the workers into the hands of the capitalist producers of the commodities needed by and generally accessible to the working-class milieu, i.e. into the hands of the capitalists of department II, and it is only by this detour—by being first employed by these for the purchase of means of production—that it returns to the hands of the department I capitalists. The result: I(v + s) = IIc. The value components v + s of the commodity capital in department I (and therefore a corresponding proportionate part of department I's total commodity product) must be equal to the constant capital IIc similarly precipitated out by department II as a proportionate part of its total commodity product. In other words: I(v + s) = IIc This is the first fundamental equation of simple reproduction. It is the condition of equilibrium, the proportional law that must hold if simple reproduction is to proceed normally. We will see its implications developed throughout the remaining sections. D. Internal Exchange in Department II: Necessities, Luxuries, and the Pivot of (IIb)v Section 4 turns to the components v + s of department II's commodity product. This does not bear on the most important question—the extent to which the breakdown of the value of each individual commodity product into c + v + s holds also for the value of the total annual product—which was resolved by the exchange of I(v + s) against IIc. Since II(v + s) exists in the natural form of items of consumption, since the variable capital advanced to the workers in payment for labour-power must be spent by them by and large on means of consumption, and since, on the supposition of simple reproduction, the s-component of commodity values is also spent as revenue on means of consumption, it is evident at first glance that the workers in department II use the wages received from the department II capitalists to buy back a part of their own product —a part corresponding in extent to the money value they receive as wages. In this way, the capitalist class of department II re-transforms the money capital they have advanced in payment for labour-power into the money form; it is just as if they had paid the workers in mere tokens of value. (This is a striking anticipation of the \"reflux\" theory that Marx will criticize in Destutt de Tracy.) The subdivision of department II. Department II of the annual commodity production consists of the most diverse branches of industry, but as far as its products go these may be broken down into two major subdivisions: (a) Necessary means of consumption. Those means of consumption that enter the consumption of the working class, and, in so far as they are necessary means of subsistence, also form part of the consumption of the capitalist class, even if this part is different in both quality and value from that of the workers. This whole subdivision can be classified under the heading \"necessary means of consumption,\" and it is quite immaterial whether a product such as tobacco is from the physiological point of view a necessary means of consumption or not; it suffices that it is such a means of consumption by custom . (b) Luxury means of consumption. Luxury means of consumption, which enter the consumption only of the capitalist class, i.e. can be exchanged only for the expenditure of surplus-value, which does not accrue to the workers. The differential reflux. As far as the first category goes, it is evident that the variable capital advanced in the production of the kinds of commodities pertaining to it has to flow directly back in the money form to the section of capitalists in department II (the capitalists in IIa) who produce these necessary means of subsistence. They sell these to their own workers to the amount of the variable capital paid the latter in wages. This reflux is a direct one for subdivision (a) of the capitalist class in department II as a whole, no matter how numerous may be the transactions between the capitalists in the various component branches of industry by which this reflux of variable capital is proportionately distributed. These are processes of circulation in which the means of circulation are directly supplied by the money spent by the workers. It is different, however, with subdivision IIb. The component of the value product with which we are dealing here, IIb(v + s) , exists entirely in the natural form of luxury items, i.e. items that the working class can as little buy as they can buy the commodity value Iv existing in the form of means of production; even though these luxury items are, like the means of production, products of the workers concerned. The reflux by which the variable capital advanced in this subdivision returns to the capitalist producers in its money form cannot be a direct one , therefore, but has to be mediated in a similar way to the case of Iv. The numerical schema for department II. Marx assumes that for department II as a whole, v = 500, s = 500; but that the variable capital and the surplus-value corresponding to it is distributed as follows: Subdivision (a), necessary means of subsistence: v = 400, s = 400; i.e. a quantity of commodities in the form of necessary means of consumption to the value of 400v + 400s = 800, or IIa (400v + 400s) . Subdivision (b), luxury items: to the value of 100v + 100s = 200, or IIb (100v + 100s) . The circuit of IIb's variable capital. The workers in IIb have received 100 in money in payment for their labour-power (say £100 sterling); they use this to buy means of consumption to the sum of 100 from the capitalists in IIa. This class of capitalists then buys 100 worth of commodities IIb, and in this way the variable capital of the capitalists in IIb flows back to them. The capitalists in IIa already have their 400v back in the money form, as a result of exchange with their own workers; of the part of their product that represents surplus-value, moreover, a quarter has been passed to the workers in IIb, and, in exchange for this, IIb (100v) in luxury goods have been withdrawn. The expenditure of revenue. Marx then supposes that the capitalists in IIa and IIb divide their expenditure of revenue in the same proportions between necessary means of subsistence and luxury items—specifically, that both spend 3/5 of their revenue on necessary means of subsistence and 2/5 on luxuries. (The editorial note clarifies that this ratio is already determined once the relative production of the two subdivisions and the rates of surplus-value obtaining in them are given; the additional assumption is that the two subdivisions taken separately each divide their expenditure according to this given overall average.) Then: - The capitalists in IIa spend 3/5 of their surplus-value revenue of 400s on their own products, necessary means of subsistence, i.e. 240 ; and 2/5 = 160 on luxury articles. - The capitalists of IIb divide their surplus-value = 100s in a similar way: 3/5 = 60 on necessities and 2/5 = 40 on luxuries, these latter being produced and exchanged within their own subdivision. The detailed account. Marx walks through the exchanges: 400v(a) is consumed by the workers in IIa , whose product (necessary means of subsistence) it forms part of; the workers buy this from the capitalist producers in their own subdivision. In this way £400 in money flows back to these capitalists, the variable capital value of 400 that they paid to their own workers; this can now be used to buy labour-power again. A part of the 400s(a) equal to the 100v(b), i.e. one quarter of the surplus-value (a), is realized in luxury items. The workers in (b) receive £100 as wages from the capitalists in their own subdivision; they use this to buy one quarter of s(a), i.e. commodities that exist in the form of necessary means of subsistence. The capitalists of (a) use this money to buy luxury articles to the same amount, 100v(b), i.e. half of the total luxury production. In this way, the variable capital of the capitalists (b) flows back to them in the money form, and they are able to begin their reproduction afresh by renewing the purchase of labour-power, since the total constant capital of the entire department II has already been converted by the exchange of I(v + s) for IIc. Thus the luxury workers can sell their labour-power once again only because the part of their own product that they created as an equivalent for their wages has been drawn by the capitalists IIa into their consumption fund, turned into cash. The exchange between (a) and (b) that is simply an exchange between the capitalists of the two subdivisions. We have already disposed of the variable capital 400v and a part of the surplus-value 100s in (a), as well as of the variable capital 100v in (b). Besides the 100 paid out for luxuries, which has already been spent, subdivision (a) as a whole still has a further 60 for luxuries to come and, in the same ratio, (b) has a total of 40. (IIa)s is therefore divided into 240 for means of subsistence and 160 for luxuries; 240 + 160 = 400s(IIa). (IIb)s is divided into 60 for means of subsistence and 40 for luxuries: 60 + 40 = 100s(IIb). The latter 40 is consumed by this class out of their own product (2/5 of their surplus-value); the 60 for means of subsistence they receive by exchanging 60 of their own surplus product for 60s(a). The equations. Marx summarizes: - IIa (400v + 400s) + IIb (100v + 100s) = 1,000; through the above-described movement, 500v(a + b) is realized in 400v(a) and 100s(a), and 500s(a + b) is realized in 300s(a) + 100v(b) + 100s(b); a total of 1,000. For (a) and (b) each considered by itself: - (a): 800 = 400v(a) + [240s(a) + 100v(b) + 60s(b)] - (b): 200 = 100s(a) + [60s(a) + 40s(b)] The constant capital subdivision. If we stick to the same ratio between variable and constant capital in the two subdivisions (which is in no way necessary), then we obtain for 400v(a) a constant capital of 1,600, and for 100v(b) a constant capital of 400. The subdivisions of department II are then: (IIa) 1,600c + 400v + 400s = 2,400 (IIb) 400c + 100v + 100s = 600 Altogether: 2,000c + 500v + 500s = 3,000 Out of the 2,000 IIc in means of consumption that are exchanged for 2,000 I(v + s), we accordingly have 1,600 exchanged for means of production of necessary means of subsistence, and 400 for means of production of luxuries. The 2,000 Is is thus itself broken down into (800v + 800s)I for (a), or 1,600 in means of production for necessary means of subsistence, and (200v + 200s)I for (b), or 400 in means of production for luxuries. An important part, not only of the means of labour proper, but also of the raw and ancillary materials, etc., is the same for both subdivisions. But as far as the exchanges of the various value components of the total product I(v + s) are concerned, any division along these lines would be completely irrelevant. The necessary results. Marx states two necessary results on the assumption of simple reproduction: (1.) The new value product of the year's labour that is created in the natural form of means of production (which can be broken down into v + s) is equal to the constant capital value c in the product of the other section of the year's labour, reproduced in the form of means of consumption. If it were smaller than IIc, then department II could not completely replace its constant capital; if it were larger, then an unused surplus would be left over. In both cases, the assumption of simple reproduction would be destroyed. (2.) In the annual product reproduced in the form of means of consumption, the variable capital v advanced in the money form can be realized only by those of its recipients who are workers in the luxury trades, in the part of the necessary means of subsistence that at first sight embodies surplus-value for its capitalist producers; i.e. the v that is laid out on luxury production is equal to a part of s corresponding to it in value which is produced in the form of necessary means of subsistence, and must thus be smaller than the total s. It is only by realizing this v in a part of s that the variable capital advanced by the capitalist producers of luxury articles returns to them in the money form. This is a phenomenon quite analogous to the realization of I(v + s) in IIc; only in the second case, (IIb)v is realized in a part of (IIa)s equal to it in value. These ratios remain qualitatively decisive in every distribution of the annual social product , in as much as this actually goes into the process of annual reproduction mediated by circulation. I(v + s) can be realized only in IIc, just as IIc can be renewed only in its function as a component of the productive capital by way of this realization; (IIb)v, similarly, can be realized only in a part of (IIa)s, and only in this way can (IIb)v be transformed back into its form as money capital. The caveat: this applies only to the extent that all this is really a result of the reproduction process itself, i.e. in as much as the capitalists in IIb do not, for instance, obtain their v on credit from another source. Quantitatively, the exchange between the various parts of the annual product only takes place in the proportionate way depicted above to the extent that the scale of production and the value ratios involved in it remain constant, and these fixed ratios are not altered by foreign trade. The critique of Adam Smith's absurdity. Marx now draws out the implications for the Smithian dogma. If it is said, in the manner of Adam Smith, that I(v + s) is resolved into IIc, and IIc resolved into I(v + s), or alternatively—as he often and still more absurdly likes to say—I(v + s) forms components of the price (or \"value in exchange\") of IIc, and IIc forms the entire component of the value I(v + s), then one could say and would in fact have to say as well that (IIb)v can be resolved into (IIa)s, or (IIa)s into (IIb)v, or that (IIb)v forms a component of the surplus-value of IIa, and vice versa; in this way the surplus-value would be resolved into wages or variable capital, and this variable capital would form a \"component\" of the surplus-value. This piece of absurdity is actually to be found in Smith, in as much as he sees wages as determined by the value of the necessary means of subsistence, and these commodity values as determined in turn by the value of the wages (variable capital) and surplus-value contained in them. He is so absorbed in the fractions into which the value product of a working day breaks down on the capitalist basis (i.e. into v + s) that he completely forgets that it is quite immaterial, in simple commodity exchange, whether the equivalents that exist in various natural forms consist of paid or unpaid labour, since in both cases they need the same amount of labour for their production; and that it is equally immaterial whether A's commodity is a means of production and B's a means of consumption, whether after its sale the one commodity has to function as a component of capital, and the other to go into the consumption fund and be (according to Adam) consumed as revenue. Key nuance: The use that the individual buyer makes of his commodity does not fall into the sphere of commodity exchange, of circulation, and does not affect the value of the commodity. This is in no way altered by the fact that analysis of the circulation of the total annual social product must deal with the specific destination in terms of use, the consumption aspect, of the different components of that product. This is a crucial methodological distinction: value determination is indifferent to use-destination; but social reproduction is not, because the material form of the product must permit the necessary exchanges. Another nuance: In connection with the exchange of (IIb)v for an equal-valued portion of (IIa)s, and the further exchanges between (IIa)s and (IIb)s, it is in no way assumed that the capitalists of IIa and IIb individually or in the aggregate divide their surplus-value between necessary consumption and luxuries in the same ratio. One person may spend more on one kind of consumption, another on something else. All that is presupposed on the basis of simple reproduction is that a sum of value equal to the total surplus-value is realized in a consumption fund. The limits of this are thus given. Within each department, one person may spend more on (a), another more on (b); the value relations, however—the proportionate shares in the total value of the products of department II for the two kinds of producers (a) and (b)—and therefore also a definite quantitative ratio between the branches of production that supply those products, are necessarily given in each concrete case. It is only the ratio taken by way of example that is hypothetical; a different one would not alter the qualitative aspects, only the quantitative determinations. The crisis implications of luxury production. Since (IIb)v is realized in an equivalent portion of (IIa)s, it follows that as the luxury part of the annual product grows, and a rising quota of labour-power is absorbed in luxury production, the re-transformation of the variable capital advanced in IIb into money capital that can function anew as the money form of variable capital, and with it the existence and reproduction of the part of the working class engaged in IIb—their supply of necessary means of consumption—is conditioned by the prodigality of the capitalist class , the conversion of a significant part of their surplus-value into luxury items. Every crisis temporarily decreases luxury consumption; it delays and slows down the re-transformation of (IIb)v into money capital, so that only a partial transformation is possible and a section of the luxury workers are thrown onto the streets; this leads in turn to a stagnation and restriction in the sale of necessary means of consumption. And this quite apart from the unproductive workers who are discharged at the same time, workers who receive for their services a part of the luxury expenditure of the capitalists (they are themselves to this extent a luxury item), and who also participate very substantially in the consumption of necessary means of subsistence, etc. The reverse is the case in periods of prosperity, and particularly during the phase of hyper-activity, when the relative value of money (as expressed in commodities) already falls for other reasons, and so the prices of commodities rise independently of their own value. It is then not only the consumption of necessary means of subsistence that rises; the working class (in which the entire reserve army of labour has now been enrolled) also takes a temporary share in the consumption of luxury articles. This phenomenon also provokes a rise in prices. The refutation of underconsumptionism. Marx now delivers one of his most famous polemical passages: \"It is a pure tautology to say that crises are provoked by a lack of effective demand or effective consumption.\" The capitalist system does not recognize any forms of consumer other than those who can pay. The fact that commodities are unsaleable means no more than that no effective buyers have been found for them. If the attempt is made to give this tautology the semblance of greater profundity, by the statement that the working class receives too small a portion of its own product, and that the evil would be remedied if it received a bigger share, i.e. if its wages rose, we need only note that crises are always prepared by a period in which wages generally rise, and the working class actually does receive a greater share in the part of the annual product destined for consumption . From the standpoint of these advocates of sound and \"simple\" (!) common sense, such periods should rather avert the crisis. It thus appears that capitalist production involves certain conditions independent of people's good or bad intentions, which permit the relative prosperity of the working class only temporarily, and moreover always as a harbinger of crisis. (The editorial footnote adds: \"This should be noted by prospective supporters of Rodbertus's theory of crises.\") The determining role of the IIa/IIb proportion. We saw just now how the proportional relation between the production of necessary items of consumption and the production of luxuries gives rise to the division of II(v + s) into IIa and IIb—and so also of IIc into (IIa)c and (IIb)c. It thereby affects the character and the quantitative ratios of production right at the roots, and is an essential determining factor of their overall pattern. Simple reproduction is oriented by nature to consumption as its aim. Even though the squeezing out of surplus-value appears as the driving motive of the individual capitalist, this surplus-value—no matter what its proportionate size—can be used here, in the last analysis, only for his individual consumption. In so far as simple reproduction is also part of any annual reproduction on an expanded scale, and the major part at that, this motive remains alongside the motive of enrichment as such and in opposition to it. In the real world the matter appears more intricate, since the partners who share the loot—the surplus-value of the capitalist—figure independently of him as consumers. E. The Circulation Schema and the Mediation of Exchanges by Money Section 5 provides a consolidated schema of the circulation. It first presents the basic exchange between departments I and II: I. 4,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s II. 2,000c + 500v + 500s This disposes of the circulation of IIc = 2,000, which is exchanged for I(1,000v + 1,000s). There remains the circulation of v + s within department II (leaving aside 4,000 Ic). This II(v + s) is divided between IIa and IIb as: II. 500v + 500s = a(400v + 400s) + b(100v + 100s) The 400v(a) circulates within its own subdivision; the workers paid with it use it to buy necessary means of subsistence that they have themselves produced from their employers, the capitalists in IIa. Since the capitalists of both subdivisions spend their surplus-value 3/5 on products of IIa (necessary means of subsistence) and 2/5 on products of IIb (luxuries), 3/5 of the surplus-value of (a), i.e. 240, is consumed within subdivision IIa itself; similarly 2/5 of the surplus-value in (b) (which was produced and is present in luxuries) is consumed within subdivision IIb. There still remains to be exchanged between IIa and IIb: - On the part of IIa: 160s - On the part of IIb: 100v + 60s These cancel each other out. With the 100 they receive in money as wages, the workers in IIb buy necessary means of subsistence from IIa. The capitalists in IIa thereby receive the money they require in order to invest 2/5 of their surplus-value, =160s, in the luxuries produced by IIb (100v that remains in the hands of the capitalists of IIb as the product replacing the wages they paid, and 60s). The schema is thus: 3. IIa. (400v) + (240s) + 160s IIb. 100v + 60s (+40s) where the items in parentheses circulate and are consumed only within their own subdivision. The direct reflux of variable capital in IIa. The direct reflux of the money capital advanced in variable capital, which takes place for the capitalists only in subdivision IIa producing necessary means of subsistence, is simply a manifestation, modified by special conditions, of the general law already explained that the money that commodity producers advance returns to them in the normal course of commodity circulation. What also follows from this is that wherever there is a money capitalist behind the commodity producers, and it is he who first advances the money capital to the industrial capitalist (money capital in the strict sense, i.e. capital value in the money form), the actual point of return of this money is the pocket of the money capitalist. In this way, even if the money circulates through the hands of more or less all concerned, the mass of the circulating money belongs to the department of money capital organized and concentrated in the form of banks, etc.; the way in which this department advances its capital determines that the final reflux in the money form is always to it, even if this is mediated by the transformation of the industrial capital back into money capital. The general law of money circulation. Two things are always required for commodity circulation: commodities have to be cast into circulation, and so has money. Marx quotes from Volume 1, Chapter 3, p. 208: \"The process of circulation, therefore, unlike the direct exchange of products, does not disappear from view once the use-values have changed places and changed hands. The money does not vanish when it finally drops out of the series of metamorphoses undergone by a commodity. It always leaves behind a precipitate at a point in the arena of circulation vacated by the commodities.\" The £500 example elaborated. In the circulation between IIc and I(v + s), Marx assumed that £500 was advanced by department II. In the countless circulation processes into which the circulation between major social groups of producers is resolved, it is now one group and now another that first appears as a buyer and casts money into circulation. This is determined by the difference in production periods and hence turnovers of the various commodity capitals. Department II uses £500 to buy means of production from department I, but the latter uses the £500 to buy means of consumption from II; the money simply flows back to department II, which is not made any the richer by this reflux. Department I first cast £500 into circulation in money and withdrew commodities to the same value; it then sold commodities for £500 and extracted from circulation the same amount in money. In this way, the £500 flows back. Department II has cast into circulation £500 in money and £500 in commodities, a total of £1,000; it withdraws from circulation £500 of commodities and £500 in money. The circulation sphere needs only £500 in money for the conversion of both £500 in commodities (I) and £500 in commodities (II), so whoever advanced the money for the purchase of someone else's commodity receives it back again in the sale of his own. If it had been department I that first bought commodities worth £500 from department II, and later sold £500 of commodities to department II, this £500 would return to department I instead of to department II. The distinction between departments I and II regarding the reflux. In department I, the money invested in wages, i.e. advanced as variable capital in the money form, does not return directly in this form, but indirectly by a detour. In department II, on the other hand, the £500 for wages returns directly from the workers to the capitalists, since this reflux is always direct where sale and purchase are repeated between the same persons in such a way that they regularly face one another alternately as buyer and seller of commodities. (The editorial footnote clarifies that this applies when department II is taken as a whole, in contrast to department I, but that within department II it properly applies only to subdivision IIa, producing means of subsistence that enter the consumption of the working class.) The nature of the wage-payment. The capitalist in department II pays for his labour-power in money; he thereby incorporates the labour-power into his own capital, and it is only by this process of circulation, which for him is simply the transformation of money capital into productive capital, that he, as industrial capitalist, confronts the worker as his wage-labourer. But after this the worker, who was in the first instance the seller, the dealer in his own labour-power, now, in the second instance, confronts the capitalist, the seller of commodities, as the possessor of money; in this way the money laid out by the capitalist on wages flows back to him. In so far as the sale of these commodities does not involve swindling, etc., but equivalents are exchanged in commodities and money, this is not a process by which the capitalist can enrich himself. Nor does he pay the worker twice over, first in money and then in commodities; his money returns to him when the worker exchanges it with him for commodities. The importance of wage-payment for monetary circulation. This money capital transformed into variable capital, i.e. the money advanced as wages, plays a major role in actual monetary circulation. Since the working class has to live from hand to mouth, i.e. since it cannot give the industrial capitalists any long-term credit, variable capital has to be advanced at the same time in money at countless different points in society, and at definite and short intervals, such as a week, etc. These periods are repeated fairly rapidly, no matter how different the turnover periods of capitals in the various branches of industry; though the shorter the intervals, the smaller need be the relative size of the total sum of money cast into circulation at one stroke through these channels. In every country of capitalist production, the money capital advanced in this way forms a relatively decisive share in the total circulation, and all the more so in that the same money flows through the most varied channels and functions as means of circulation for a myriad other businesses, before returning to its starting-point. The consolidated circulation account. Marx then recapitulates the circulation between I(v + s) and IIc from a different point of view: The capitalists in department I advance £1,000 as payment for wages; the workers use this to buy £1,000 worth of means of subsistence from the capitalists in department II, and these in turn use it to buy means of production from the capitalists in department I. The variable capital advanced by the latter has now returned to them in its monetary form, while the capitalists in department II have transformed half of their constant capital from the form of commodity capital back into productive capital. The capitalists in department II advance a further £500 in money to get means of production from department I. The capitalists in department I spend this money on means of consumption from department II; this £500 thus flows back to the capitalists in department I, who advance it afresh in order to transform the last quarter of the constant capital they previously transformed into commodities back into its productive natural form. This money again flows back to department I and once more withdraws from department II means of consumption to the same amount; the capitalists in department II are now as before in possession of £500 in money and £2,000 in constant capital, though this has been newly converted from the form of commodity capital back into that of productive capital. With £1,500 in money, a commodity mass of £5,000 has been circulated. The seven-step recapitulation: 1. Department I pays its workers £1,000 for labour-power of the same value. 2. These workers use this £1,000 to buy means of subsistence from department II. 3. Department II buys means of production with the same money from department I, thereby restoring department I's variable capital in its money form. 4. Department II uses £500 to buy means of production from department I. 5. Department I buys means of consumption from department II with the same £500. 6. Department II buys means of production from department I with this £500. 7. Department I buys means of consumption from department II with the £500. As a result, the £500 which department II cast into circulation on top of its £2,000 in commodities, and for which it did not withdraw any equivalent in commodities, has flowed back to it. (The editorial footnote notes: \"The presentation here departs somewhat from that given previously [p. 476]. There department I also cast its own £500 into circulation. Here department II alone supplies the additional money material needed for circulation. This, however, in no way alters the principle involved.\") The exchange process summarized (with the sum of commodity values exchanged = £5,000): The result: 1. Department I possesses variable capital in the money form to the sum of £1,000 , which is what it originally advanced to the circulation sphere. It has also spent £1,000 on individual consumption—from its own commodity product; i.e. it has spent the money that it received for the sale of means of production, amounting to the total of £1,000. On the other hand, the natural form into which the variable capital existing in the money form has to be converted—i.e. labour-power—has to be maintained, reproduced by consumption, and be present once again as the only article of trade of its proprietors, who have to sell this if they want to live. In this way, the relationship between wage-labourers and capitalists is also reproduced. 2. The constant capital of department II is replaced in kind , and the £500 advanced to circulation by department II has returned to it. The circuits of the different agents: - For the workers in department I, the circulation is the simple one of C–M–C: C(1)(labour-power) – M(2)(£1,000, the money form of the variable capital in department I) – C(3)(necessary means of subsistence to the sum of £1,000); this £1,000 converts into money the same value of constant capital in department II, existing in the commodity form as means of subsistence. - For the capitalists in department II, the process is C–M, the transformation of a part of their commodity product into the money form, from which it is transformed back into components of their productive capital—i.e. into a part of the means of production that they need. - In the advance of M (£500) which the capitalists in department II make in order to purchase the remaining components of their means of production, they anticipate the money form of that part of IIc that is still in the commodity form (means of consumption). In the act M–C, in which department II buys with M and department I sells C, department II's money is transformed into a part of its productive capital, while I's commodity undergoes the act C–M and is transformed into money, although it does not represent any component of capital value, but rather realized surplus-value which is simply spent on means of consumption. - In the circulation M–C…P…C'–M', the first act M–C of one capitalist is the final act C'–M' of another (or part of it); it is completely immaterial for the commodity circulation itself whether this C, by which M is transformed into productive capital, represents for its seller (who thereby converts this C into money) the constant component of his capital, the variable component, or surplus-value. Department I's withdrawal of surplus-value in money. As far as the department I capitalists are concerned, with respect to the component s of their commodity product they withdraw more money from the circulation sphere than they cast into it: - Firstly, their £1,000 of variable capital returns to them. - Secondly, they sell means of production for £500 (exchange no. 4), and this enables them to convert half their surplus-value into cash. - Then they again sell a further £500 of means of production (exchange no. 6), the second half of their surplus-value, and as a result their entire surplus-value has been withdrawn from circulation in the money form. - Total realized: 1,000v + 1,000s = £2,000. Even if department I cast only £1,000 into circulation, it has withdrawn twice the amount. Of course, the s that has been realized (transformed into money) immediately vanishes again into someone else's hands (department II), because the money is exchanged for means of consumption. The capitalists of department I have withdrawn only as much in money as they cast in in commodities. The fact that this value is surplus-value, and costs the capitalists nothing, in no way alters the value of the commodities themselves; it is therefore completely immaterial, as far as the exchange of values in commodity circulation is concerned. Velocity of circulation and the money supply. If the turnovers are assumed to be shorter—or, from the standpoint of simple commodity circulation, the velocity of monetary circulation is assumed to be greater—then still less money would be needed in order to circulate the commodity values to be exchanged. This sum is always determined—once the number of successive exchanges is given—by the sum of the prices or values of the circulating commodities. The proportion in which this sum of values consists of surplus-value on the one hand and capital value on the other is completely irrelevant here. For example, if wages are paid four times a year in department I, 4 × 250 = 1,000, and so £250 in money would be sufficient for the circulation of Iv–IIc, and for the circulation between the variable capital Iv and the labour-power of department I. In the same way, if the circulation between Is and IIc consisted of four turnovers, then £250 would be sufficient for it, and therefore a total sum of money or a money capital of £500 would be enough for the circulation of commodities to the sum of £5,000. The alternative assumption (department I as buyer). If in exchange no. 4 it was department I instead of department II that appeared as the buyer, i.e. £500 in money was spent on means of consumption to the same value, department II would then buy means of production with the same £500 in exchange no. 5; in exchange no. 6, department I would use this £500 to buy means of consumption, and in no. 7, department II would use it again to buy means of production. Thus the £500 would ultimately return to department I, instead of department II as before. The surplus-value is realized here by the money spent by its capitalist producers themselves on their private consumption, which represents anticipated revenue , anticipated income from the surplus-value contained in the commodities still to be sold. Key point: The realization of surplus-value does not take place through the reflux of the £500; for alongside the £1,000 in commodities Iv, department I has cast £500 of money into circulation in connection with exchange no. 4, and this was an additional sum, not—as far as we know—the proceeds of the sale of the commodities. When this money flows back to department I, I has simply recovered its additional money, and not realized its surplus-value. The monetary realization of department I's surplus-value simply involves the sale of the commodities Is in which it is contained, and each time it lasts only as long as the money released by the sale of these commodities is not spent again on means of consumption. The \"what if department II does not buy\" scenario. Suppose that department II does not buy means of production from department I (no. 7). Then department I would in fact have paid £1,000 for means of consumption, and consumed its entire surplus-value as revenue, i.e. paid £500 in its own commodities (means of production) and £500 in money; it would still have £500 of its own commodities (means of production) in stock on the other hand, but would have got rid of its £500 of money. Department II, on the contrary, would have transformed three-quarters of its constant capital from the form of commodity capital back into productive capital; but it would have one quarter left in the form of money capital (£500), this being in fact idle money or money whose functioning has been interrupted and which is held in abeyance. If this situation lasts too long, then department II will have to reduce the scale of its reproduction by a quarter. However, the £500 in means of production which department I is still saddled with is not surplus-value in the commodity form; it is there in place of the £500 advanced in money, which department I possessed alongside its surplus-value of £1,000 in the commodity form. As money, this exists in an ever realizable form; in the commodity form, it is temporarily unsaleable. This much is evident, that simple reproduction—in which each element of productive capital in both department I and department II has to be replaced—remains possible only if the 500 golden birds return to department I, which first sent them flying. The capitalist as source of money. Once a capitalist spends his money on means of consumption, he is then done with it; it has gone the way of all flesh. If the money flows back to him again, this can happen only in so far as he fishes it out of the circulation sphere in exchange for commodities—i.e. by way of his commodity capital. Just as the value of his total annual commodity product (his commodity capital) can be broken down into constant capital value, variable capital value, and surplus-value, so too can every element of this, i.e. the value of every individual commodity. The realization of each of his individual commodities is therefore at the same time the realization of a certain quota of the surplus-value contained in the total commodity product. It is therefore literally correct, in the present case, that the capitalist himself casts into circulation the money into which he converts his surplus-value , i.e. by means of which he realizes it, and, what is more, by spending this on means of consumption. What we have here, of course, are not the identical pieces of money, but rather a given quantity of hard cash equal to the money cast into circulation to cover his personal needs. The two practical ways this occurs: 1. If the business was started only within the current year, then it takes a good while, at best a few months, before the capitalist can spend money for his personal consumption out of his income from this actual business. He does not on this account suspend his consumption for a moment. He advances himself money against the surplus-value that he still has to hunt out (whether from his own pocket or from someone else's by way of credit). But this money is also a circulating medium in which he can later realize his surplus-value. 2. In the other case, where the business has already been in regular operation for some time, payments and receipts take place at different times of the year, but the capitalist's consumption, which anticipates his receipts and the level of which is fixed as a certain proportion of his customary or estimated receipts, still continues without interruption. With each portion of commodities sold, one part of the surplus-value to be made in the year is realized. But if, out of the commodities produced in the year, no more is sold than is needed to replace the constant and variable capital value contained in them, or if prices fall so that the sale of the entire annual product realizes only the capital value advanced in it, then the anticipatory character of the money spent against future surplus-value clearly emerges. If our capitalist goes bankrupt, then his creditors and the courts investigate whether his anticipated private spending stood in due proportion to the state of his business and the customary or normal receipts of surplus-value corresponding to it. The necessary condition of the overall mechanism. In relation to the capitalist class as a whole, the proposition that it must itself cast into circulation the money needed to realize its surplus-value (and also to circulate its capital, constant and variable) is not only far from paradoxical, it is in fact a necessary condition of the overall mechanism . For here there are just two classes: the working class, which only disposes of its labour-power, and the capitalist class, which has the monopoly of the means of social production, and of money. It would rather be a paradox if, instead, it was the working class that initially advanced the money required to realize the surplus-value contained in commodities, out of its own resources. The individual capitalist, however, effects this advance only by acting as buyer, spending money on the purchase of means of consumption or advancing money on the purchase of elements of his productive capital, either labour-power or means of production. He only ever parts with the money in exchange for an equivalent. He advances money to circulation only in the same way that he advances commodities to it. In both cases, he acts as the starting-point of their circulation. Two circumstances that obscure the real course of events: 1. The appearance of commercial capital (the primary form of which is always money, since the merchant as such does not produce any \"product\" or \"commodity\") and of money capital , as the object of manipulation of a special kind of capitalist, in the circulation process of industrial capital. 2. The division of surplus-value —which must always exist initially in the hands of the industrial capitalist—into different categories, the bearers of which appear alongside the industrial capitalist as the landlord (for ground-rent), the money-lender (for interest), etc., as well as the government and its officials, rentiers, etc. These fellows face the industrial capitalist as buyers, and to this extent realize his commodities in money; they too cast their share of \"money\" into the circulation sphere, and he receives this from them. What is always forgotten in connection with this are the sources from which they originally obtained this money, and continue to obtain it. F. Constant Capital in Department I: The 4,000c That Never Leaves the Department Section 6 investigates the constant capital in department I, 4,000c. This is equal in value to the means of production consumed in the production of this mass of commodities, a value which reappears in the commodity product of department I. This reappearing value was not produced in the production process of department I but entered it the year before as constant value, as the given value of its means of production. It now exists in that entire part of the commodity mass of department I that is not absorbed by department II; moreover, the value of this commodity mass remaining in the hands of the department I capitalists is two-thirds of the value of their entire annual commodity product (4,000 out of 6,000). The difficulty. If we were dealing with the individual capitalist producing one particular means of production, we could say: he first sold his commodity product, transformed it into money. By transforming it into money, he also transformed the constant value component of his product back into money. With this value portion transformed into money, he then bought his means of production again from other commodity sellers, or transformed the constant value component of his product into a natural form in which it could once more function as productive constant capital. Now, however, this assumption becomes untenable. The capitalist class in department I comprises the totality of capitalists who produce means of production. Moreover, the commodity product of 4,000 that remains in their hands is a part of the social product that cannot be exchanged against any other, for there is no such other part of the annual product. With the exception of this 4,000, all the remainder has been disposed of. One part has been absorbed by the social consumption fund, and another part has to replace the constant capital of department II, which has already exchanged everything it has available for exchange with department I. The resolution. The difficulty is very simply resolved when we remember that the entire commodity product of department I consists in its natural form of means of production , i.e. of the material elements of constant capital itself. The same phenomenon is displayed here as previously with department II, only under a different aspect. In department II, the entire commodity product consists of means of consumption; one part of these, measured by the wages plus surplus-value contained in this commodity product, can therefore be consumed by its own producers. Here in department I, the entire commodity product consists of means of production—buildings, machinery, containers, raw and ancillary materials, etc. One part of these, that which replaces the constant capital used up in this sphere, can therefore immediately function in its natural form once again as a component of the productive capital. In so far as it steps into the circulation sphere, it circulates within department I. The parallel and the contrast: - In department II, one part of the commodity product is individually consumed in kind by its own producers. - In department I, on the other hand, a part of the product is productively consumed in kind by its own capitalist producers. In the 4,000c part of the commodity product of department I there reappears the constant capital value consumed in this department, and it reappears moreover in a natural form in which it can immediately function again as productive constant capital. In department II, the part of the commodity product of 3,000 whose value equals wages plus surplus-value (=1,000) goes directly into the individual consumption of the capitalists and workers, while the constant capital value (=2,000) cannot go back into the productive consumption of the capitalists in department II, but has to be replaced by exchange with department I. In department I, on the contrary, the part of its commodity product of 6,000 whose value equals wages plus surplus-value (=2,000) does not go into the individual consumption of its producers, and is also unable to do this owing to its natural form. Instead it must first be exchanged with department II. However, the constant portion of this product's value (=4,000) does exist in a natural form in which—taking the capitalist class of department I as a whole—it can directly function again as their constant capital. The distribution of department I's product. The entire product of department I consists of use-values which by virtue of their natural form can serve only as elements of constant capital—given the capitalist mode of production. Out of this product, which has a value of 6,000, one third (2,000) replaces the constant capital of department II, and the remaining two thirds replace the constant capital of department I . The internal differentiation of department I. The constant capital of department I consists of a number of different groups of capitals, invested in the various branches of production of means of production—so much in iron works, so much in coalmines, etc. Each of these capital groups, or each of these social group capitals, is again composed of a larger or smaller number of independently functioning individual capitals. Firstly, the capital of the society, e.g. 7,500, is broken down into different capital groups. The social capital of 7,500 is broken down into particular portions, each invested in a particular branch of production; the part of the social capital value invested in each particular branch of production consists, in its natural form, partly of the means of production for each special sphere of production, partly of the labour-power necessary and appropriately qualified for the industry in question. The part of the social capital applied in each particular branch of production consists once again of the sum of individual capitals invested and independently functioning in it. This applies to both departments, I and II. The internal exchanges of department I. As far as concerns the constant capital value reappearing in department I in the form of its commodity product, part of this goes back once again as means of production into the particular branch of production (or even the individual business) from which it emerged as a product; e.g. corn into the production of corn, coal into coal production, iron in the form of machines into iron production, etc. But to the extent that the partial products of which the constant capital value in department I consists do not go directly back into their particular or individual spheres of production, they simply change their place . They go in their natural form into another sphere of production of department I, while the product of these other spheres of production of department I replaces them in kind. These products merely experience a change of position. They all go back in again as factors that replace the constant capital in department I, only instead of entering one group of department I they go into another. The nature of the exchange within department I. In as much as there is exchange here between the individual capitalists of department I, this is exchange of one natural form of constant capital against another natural form of constant capital , one kind of means of production against other kinds of means of production. It is mutual exchange between the different individual constant portions of capital in department I. These products, in as much as they do not directly serve as means of production in their own branch of production, are thus displaced from their own point of production to another one, and mutually replace each other in this way. In other words (as happens similarly for surplus-value in department II), each capitalist in department I withdraws the appropriate means of production needed by him from this commodity mass in the proportion to which he is a joint owner of this constant capital of 4,000. The social(ist) counterfactual. Marx adds a significant observation: \"If production were social instead of capitalist, it is evident that these products of department I would be no less constantly redistributed among the branches of production in this department as means of production, according to the needs of reproduction; one part directly remaining in the sphere of production from which it emerged as a product, another part being shifted to other points of production, and so there would be a constant to and fro between the various points of production in this department.\" This is a crucial indication that the material necessity of this redistribution is independent of the capitalist form, even though the manner of its execution (through exchange, through the anarchy of the market, through the joint-stock ownership of the \"collective capitalist\") is historically specific. G. Variable Capital and Surplus-Value in the Two Departments: The Riddle of the Annual Value Product Section 7 establishes a crucial identity. The total value of the means of consumption annually produced is equal to the variable capital value reproduced in the course of the year plus the surplus-value newly produced, in department II, together with the variable capital value reproduced in the year and the surplus-value newly produced in department I. The identity. On the premise of simple reproduction, therefore, the total value of the means of consumption annually produced is equal to the annual value product , i.e. equal to the total value produced by the labour of society in the course of the year, and this must be the case because with simple reproduction this entire value is consumed. The total social working day breaks down into two parts: 1. Necessary labour —this creates a value of 1,500v in the course of the year; 2. Surplus labour —this creates an additional value or surplus-value of 1,500s . The sum of these values is 3,000 , equal to that of the means of consumption annually produced. The total value of the means of consumption produced during the year is therefore equal to the total value that the entire social working day produces during the year, i.e. equal to the value of the social variable capital plus the social surplus-value, or equal to the total new annual product. The paradox and its resolution. We know, however, that even if these two value magnitudes are equal, this in no way means that the total value of the commodities in department II has been produced in this department of social production. They are equal because the constant capital value that reappears in department II is equal to the value newly produced in department I (variable capital value plus surplus-value); hence I(v + s) can purchase the part of the product that represents constant capital value for its producers in department II. This explains why, even though for the capitalists in department II the value of their product breaks down into c + v + s, yet, considered from the social point of view, the value of this product can be broken down into v + s. This is the case only because IIc = I(v + s) , and these two components of the social product exchange their natural forms with one another by way of their commodity exchange. After this conversion, therefore, IIc exists once again in means of production, and I(v + s) in means of consumption. The Adam Smith reference. It is this circumstance that led Adam Smith to maintain that the value of the annual product resolves itself into v + s. This: (1) applies only to the part of the annual product consisting of means of consumption; and (2) does not apply in the sense that this total value is produced in department II and the value of the product is therefore the variable capital value advanced in department II plus the surplus-value produced in this department. It holds rather in the sense that II(c + v + s) = II(v + s) + I(v + s), or because IIc = I(v + s) . The division of the social working day. It also follows that even if the social working day (i.e. the labour spent by the entire working class over a whole year), just like each individual working day, can be simply broken down into two parts—into necessary labour plus surplus labour—and even though the value produced by this working day can therefore be similarly broken down into two parts (the portion of value with which the worker buys his own means of reproduction, and the surplus-value that the capitalist can spend for his individual consumption), yet, from the social standpoint, one part of the social working day is spent exclusively on the production of fresh constant capital , i.e. of products that are exclusively destined to function in the labour process as means of production, and therefore as constant capital in the accompanying process of valorization. On our assumption, the total social working day is represented by a money value of 3,000, of which one third (=1,000) is produced in department II , which produces means of consumption, i.e. the commodities in which the entire variable capital value and the entire surplus-value of society is ultimately realized. On this supposition, therefore, two thirds of the social working day is applied in the production of new constant capital . Even if, from the standpoint of the individual capitalists and workers in department I, this two-thirds of the social working day serves merely to produce variable capital value and surplus-value, just like the other third of the social working day in department II, considered from the social standpoint—and also from the standpoint of the use-value of the product—this two-thirds of the social working day still produces only a replacement for the constant capital consumed in the process of productive consumption. Even taken by itself, this two-thirds of the working day, although the total value it produces for its producers is equal simply to variable capital value plus surplus-value, does not produce any use-value of such a kind that either wages or surplus-value could be spent on it; its product is a means of production. The first thing to note: No part of the social working day, whether in department I or department II, serves to produce the value of the constant capital applied and functioning in these two great spheres of production. All that is produced here is additional value, 2,000 I(v + s) + 1,000 II(v + s), an addition to the constant capital value of 4,000 Ic + 2,000 IIc. The new value that has been produced in the form of means of production is not yet constant capital. It is simply destined to function as such in the future. The value and use-value dimensions. The entire product of department II—means of consumption—is from the use-value standpoint, i.e. concretely, considered in its natural form, the product of concrete forms of labour such as weaving, baking, etc. which have been employed in this department. It is the product of this labour in as much as the labour functions as the subjective element of the labour process. As far as the constant value component of this product of department II is concerned, however, it simply reappears in a new use-value, in a new natural form, the form of means of consumption, whereas it earlier existed in the form of means of production. Its value has been transferred by the labour process from its old natural form to its new one. But the value of this two-thirds of the value of the product, 2,000, has not been produced by department II in the current year's valorization process. The two-thirds of a past working day. Just as, considered from the standpoint of the labour process, the product of department II is the result of newly functioning living labour and its given presupposed means of production, so from the standpoint of the valorization process the value of the product, 3,000, is composed of: - the new value produced by the one-third of the social working day newly added (500v + 500s = 1,000), and - a constant value in which there is objectified two-thirds of a past working day , which took place before the production process of department II at present under consideration. This value component of the product of department II can be represented by a portion of the product itself. It exists in a quantity of means of consumption to the value of 2,000 = two-thirds of a social working day. This is the new use form in which it reappears. The exchange between this year's labour and previous years' labour. The exchange of one part of the means of consumption, = 2,000 IIc, for means of production from department I, = I(1,000v + 1,000s), is therefore in point of fact the exchange of two-thirds of a total working day which does not form part of this year's labour, but was performed prior to the current year, for two-thirds of the social working day newly added in the present year . Two-thirds of this year's social working day could not both be applied in the production of constant capital and yet at the same time form variable capital value and surplus-value for its own producers, if it were not exchanged with a value component of the means of consumption annually consumed that contains two-thirds of a working day performed and realized prior to the present year. This is an exchange of two-thirds of this year's working day for two-thirds of a working day spent prior to this year, an exchange between labour-time of this year and labour-time of a previous year. The riddle explained. It therefore explains the riddle as to how the value product of the entire social working day can be resolved into variable capital value plus surplus-value, even though two-thirds of this working day was not spent on the production of objects in which variable capital or surplus-value could be realized, but rather on the production of means of production to replace the capital used up during the current year. This is explained by the simple fact that, considered from the point of view of its value, the two-thirds of department II's product in which the capitalists and workers of department I realize the variable capital value plus the surplus-value produced by them (making two-ninths of the total value of the annual product) is the product of two-thirds of a social working day spent before the current year. The use-value caveat. The total social product of departments I and II, means of production and means of consumption, is certainly, looked at from the point of view of its use-value, its concrete, natural form, the product of this year's labour, but only in so far as this labour is considered simply as useful, concrete labour, not in so far as it is viewed as the expenditure of labour-power, as value-forming labour . And it is only useful, concrete labour in the sense that the means of production have been transformed into new products, the products of the current year, by the living labour added to them and operating on them. The labour of this year, however, could not have been transformed into products without means of production, i.e. means of labour and production materials, independent of it. H. Constant Capital in Both Departments: Resolving the Riddle of Material vs. Value Form Section 8 provides the definitive resolution. As far as concerns the value of the total product, 9,000, and the categories into which it breaks down, its analysis does not offer any greater difficulty than that of the value of the product of an individual capital; it is in fact identical with this. The decomposition of the 9,000. The annual social product, taken as a whole, contains three one-year social working days . The value expression of each of these working days is 3,000, so that the value expression of the total product is 3 × 3,000 = 9,000. Furthermore, of the labour-time that has been spent prior to the production process of the year whose product we are analysing, four-thirds of a working day was spent in department I (value product 4,000) and two-thirds of a working day in department II (value product 2,000). Taken together, this is two social working days , whose value product is 6,000. Thus 4,000 Ic + 2,000 IIc = 6,000c figures as the value of the means of production, or constant capital value, reappearing in the value of the overall product. Besides, out of the one-year working day that society has newly added: - One third is necessary labour or labour that replaces the value of the variable capital 1,000 Iv in department I, and pays the price of the labour applied in this department. - One sixth of the social working day is similarly necessary labour in department II, to a value of 500. Thus 1,000 Iv + 500 IIv = 1,500v , the value expression of half a social working day. Finally: - One third of the total working day, a value product of 1,000, is surplus labour in department I. - In department II, this surplus labour is one sixth of a working day, a value product of 500. Together, these make up the other half of the total working day added. Thus the total surplus-value produced = 1,000 Is + 500 IIs = 1,500s . In summary: - Constant capital component of the value of the social product (c): Two working days, spent prior to the current production process, a value expression of 6,000. - Necessary labour spent during the year (v): Half the working day spent in the year's production, a value expression of 1,500. - Surplus labour spent during the year (s): Half the working day spent in the year's production, a value expression of 1,500. - The value product of the year's labour (v + s) = 3,000. - The value of the total product (c + v + s) = 9,000. The real difficulty. Thus the difficulty does not lie in analysing the value of the social product itself. It arises when the value components of the social product are compared with its material components : - The constant portion of value, that simply reappearing, is equal to the value of the part of the social product that consists of means of production, and is embodied in this part. - The new year's value product = v + s is equal to the value of the part of the annual product that consists of means of consumption, and is embodied in this. But, with exceptions that are of no consequence here, means of production and means of consumption are totally different kinds of commodities , products quite different in their natural or use form, and therefore products of totally different varieties of concrete labour. The labour that uses machines for the production of means of subsistence is quite different from the labour that makes machines. The apparent contradiction. The total annual working day, whose value expression is 3,000, seems to be spent on the production of means of consumption = 3,000, in which no portion of constant capital value reappears, since this 3,000 = 1,500v + 1,500s is simply resolved into variable capital value plus surplus-value. On the other hand, the constant capital value of 6,000 reappears in a kind of product that is completely different from the means of consumption, i.e. the means of production, while no part of the social working day seems to be spent in the production of this new product; the whole working day, rather, seems to consist simply of the kinds of labour that do not result in means of production, but rather in means of consumption. The solution. The riddle is already solved. The value product of the annual labour is equal to the value of the product of department II, the total value of the newly produced means of consumption. But the value of this product is three times greater than the part of the annual labour that is spent on the production of means of consumption (department II). Only one third of the annual labour is spent on producing these. Two thirds of the annual labour is spent on producing means of production, i.e. in department I. The value product created in this period in department I, equal to the variable capital value reproduced in department I plus the surplus-value, is equal to the constant capital value of department II that reappears in department II in means of consumption. These can therefore be exchanged for one another and replace one another in kind. The total value of the means of consumption in department II is therefore equal to the sum of the new value product in departments I and II together, or II(c + v + s) = I(v + s) + II(v + s) i.e. the total new value produced by the annual labour in the form of v + s. On the other hand, the total value of the means of production (I) is equal to the sum of the constant capital values reappearing in the forms of means of production (I) and means of consumption (II), i.e. equal to the total constant capital value reappearing in society's total product: I(c + v + s) = Ic + IIc This total value is, in department I, the value expression of four-thirds of a working day performed before the current production process, and two-thirds in department II, making a total of two complete working days. The source of the difficulty. The difficulty with the annual social product comes from the fact that the constant portion of value is represented in a kind of product—means of production—completely different from the means of consumption in which the new value v + s added to this constant portion of value is represented. It seems, therefore, as if two-thirds of the mass of products consumed—in value terms—exist once again in a new form, as new product, without any kind of labour having been expended by society on their production. The contrast with individual capital. This is not the case with the individual capital. Each individual capitalist applies a definite concrete kind of labour, which transforms the means of production peculiar to it into a product. Say the capitalist is involved in engineering, the constant capital spent during the year 6,000c, the variable capital 1,500v, and the surplus-value 1,500s; the product is then 9,000, which we can take as eighteen machines, each worth 500. The entire product exists in the same form, that of machines. The total commodity product is the product of the labour expended during the year in engineering, the combination of this concrete kind of labour with its specific means of production. The various parts of the product's value are therefore represented in this same natural form: twelve machines contain 6,000c, three machines 1,500v, three machines 1,500s . It is evident here that if the value of the twelve machines that comprise the constant capital is equal to 6,000c, this is not because the labour embodied in these machines took place before the engineering stage was reached and was not spent in it. The value of the means of production for eighteen machines has not been transformed of itself into twelve machines; it is rather the value of these twelve machines (which itself consists of 4,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s) that is equal to the total value of the constant capital value contained in the eighteen machines. The capitalist engineer must therefore sell twelve of the eighteen machines in order to replace the constant capital he has spent. What would be inexplicable, rather, would be a situation in which, although the labour applied consisted simply of engineering labour, its result was on the one hand six machines = 1,500v + 1,500s, and on the other hand iron, copper, screws, belts, etc. to a value of 6,000c, i.e. the means of production of the machines in their natural form , which the individual capitalist engineer obviously does not produce himself, but must replace by way of the circulation process. And yet it seems at first glance as if the reproduction of the annual social product does proceed in such a contradictory way. The condition of the individual product vs. the social product. The product of an individual capital, i.e. each independently functioning fraction of the social capital endowed with its own life, may have any natural form whatsoever. The only condition is that it really should have a use form, a use-value, that stamps it as a member of the commodity world capable of circulation. It is completely immaterial and accidental whether or not it can go back as a means of production into the same production process from which it emerged as a product, i.e. whether the part of the product's value that represents the constant capital component possesses a natural form in which it can actually function once again as constant capital. If not, then this part of the product's value is transformed again by sale and purchase into the material elements of its production, and thereby reproduces the constant capital again in its natural form. It is different with the product of the total social capital. All material elements of the reproduction must be parts of this product in their natural form. The portion of constant capital consumed can be replaced by the overall production only if the entire reappearing constant portion of capital reappears in the product in the natural form of new means of production that actually can function as constant capital. On the assumption of simple reproduction, therefore, the value of the portion of the product that consists of means of production must be equal to the consumed constant portion of the value of the social capital. The social point of view. Considered individually, all that the capitalist produces in the value of his product, by the labour newly added to it, is his variable capital and his surplus-value, while the constant capital component is transferred to the product by the concrete character of the labour newly added. Socially considered, however, the portion of the social working day that produces means of production—both adding new value to them and transferring to them the value of the means of production consumed while they were being produced—produces nothing but new constant capital , destined to replace that consumed in the form of the old means of production, the constant capital consumed in both departments I and II. It only produces a product destined for productive consumption. The entire value of this product is therefore only value that functions anew as constant capital, which can only buy back constant capital in its natural form, and which is therefore from the social point of view resolved neither into variable capital nor into surplus-value . On the other hand, the part of the social working day that produces means of consumption does not produce any part of the social replacement capital. It produces only products that, in their natural form, are destined to realize the value of the variable capital and the surplus-value in departments I and II. The warning against bourgeois habits of thought. In speaking of the social point of view, i.e. in considering the total social product, which includes both the reproduction of the social capital and individual consumption, it is necessary to avoid falling into the habits of bourgeois economics, as imitated by Proudhon, i.e. to avoid looking at things as if a society based on the capitalist mode of production lost its specific historical and economic character when considered en bloc, as a totality. This is not the case at all. What we have to deal with is the collective capitalist. The total capital appears as the share capital of all individual capitalists together. This joint-stock company has in common with many other joint-stock companies that everyone knows what they put into it, but not what they will get out of it. (This is a sardonic reference to the \"collective capitalist\" as a kind of involuntary joint-stock company—the anarchy of capitalist production means no individual capitalist knows the outcome of the total process.) I. A Look Back at Adam Smith, Storch and Ramsay: The Dogma of Resolution into Revenue Section 9 provides a historical-critical excursus. The total value of the social product is 9,000 = 6,000c + 1,500v + 1,500s ; 6,000 reproduces the value of the means of production and 3,000 the value of the means of consumption. The value of the social revenue (v + s) thus amounts to only one third of the value of the total product, and the totality of consumers, both workers and capitalists, can withdraw commodities from the total social product and incorporate them into their consumption fund only to the amount of this one-third part of value. 6,000, two-thirds of the product's value, on the other hand, is the value of the constant capital that has to be replaced in kind. Means of production to this amount must therefore be reincorporated into the production fund. Storch. This is something that Storch realizes is necessary, without being able to prove it: \"It is clear that the value of the annual product is divided in part into capital and in part into profit, and that each of these parts of the annual value of the product regularly goes to buy the products that the nation needs, both to maintain its capital and to replace its consumption fund... the products that constitute the capital of a nation can in no way be consumed\" (Storch, Considérations sur la nature du revenu national , Paris, 1824, pp. 134–5, 150). Adam Smith's dogma. Yet Adam Smith put forward this fanciful dogma, which is still believed to this day, in the form already discussed, according to which the entire value of the social product resolves itself into revenue, i.e. into wages plus surplus-value, or—as he expresses it—into wages plus profit (interest) plus rent. He also put it forward in the still more popular form that the consumers must ultimately pay the producers for the entire value of the products. Right to the present, this remains one of the most well-loved platitudes, or rather eternal truths, of the so-called science of political economy. The plausible demonstration of the dogma. Marx shows how the dogma is \"demonstrated\": Take an article of some kind, e.g. linen shirts. First the spinner of linen yarn has to pay the flax-grower the entire value of the flax, i.e. flax seed, manure, animal fodder, etc., together with the portion of value that the flax-grower's fixed capital surrenders to this product; the wages paid in the production of the flax; the surplus-value (profit, ground-rent) contained in the flax; finally the freight costs of the flax from its point of production to the spinning mill. The weaver then has to reimburse the spinner of linen yarn not only with this price of the flax, but also with the portion of value in the machinery, buildings, etc., i.e. of the fixed capital, that is transferred to the flax, as well as that of all ancillary materials consumed during the spinning process, the wages of the spinners, surplus-value, etc.; and this is then taken a stage further with the bleacher, the transport costs of the finished linen, and finally the shirt manufacturer, who has to pay the whole price of all the earlier producers who have only supplied him with his raw material. In his hands, a further addition of values takes place, partly through the constant capital value that is consumed in the form of means of labour, ancillaries, etc. in the manufacture of shirts, and partly through the labour spent in this process, which adds the value of the shirt-maker's wages plus the surplus-value of the shirt manufacturer. Let the entire shirt product now cost £100. The consumers of shirts pay £100, i.e. the value of all the means of production contained in the shirts together with the wages plus surplus-value of the flax-grower, spinner, weaver, bleacher, shirt manufacturer, and all transporters as well. This is completely correct. And this is in fact what any child can see. But then it is further said: This is how things stand with the value of all means of consumption, with the value of the part of the social product that goes into the consumption fund, i.e. with the part of the value of the social product that can be spent as revenue. The value sum of all these commodities is moreover equal to the value of all the means of production consumed in them (the constant capital components) plus the value that the labour last added to them has created (wages plus surplus-value). The totality of consumers can pay this whole value, because although the value of each individual commodity certainly consists of c + v + s, the value sum of all the commodities that enter into the consumption fund taken together, at its maximum, can only be equal to the portion of the value of the social product that is resolved into v + s, i.e. equal to the value that the labour spent during the year has added to the means of production available—to the constant capital value. The fallacy in the dogma. The fallacy consists in applying the microeconomic result (the value of an individual commodity = c + v + s, and the consumer pays all of it) to the social product as a whole, and concluding that the total value resolves into revenue. But as far as the constant capital value is concerned, we have seen that it is replaced in two ways from the social product: 1. By exchange between the capitalists in department II who produce means of consumption and those in department I who produce the means of production for them. This is the source of the phrase that what is capital for one is revenue for another . But this is not how things are at all. The 2,000 IIc that exists in means of consumption to the value of 2,000 forms constant capital value for the capitalists in department II. These cannot therefore consume it themselves, even though the product has to be consumed [individually], on account of its natural form. The 2,000 I(v + s), on the other hand, is the wages and surplus-value produced by the capitalists and workers in department I. It exists in the natural form of means of production, as things in which their own value cannot be [individually] consumed. We have here, therefore, a value sum of 4,000, of which half simply replaces constant capital, and half simply forms revenue, both before and after the exchange. 2. The constant capital of department I is replaced in kind, partly by exchange among the capitalists in department I, partly by replacement in kind in each particular business. The phrase \"paid by the consumers.\" The phrase that the entire value of the annual product must finally be paid by the consumers would be correct only if the expression \"consumers\" were taken to include two quite different kinds of consumer: individual consumers and productive ones. But if a part of the product has to be consumed productively, this means nothing more than that it has to function as capital and cannot be consumed as revenue. The disappearance of variable capital. If we divide the value of the total product of 9,000 into 6,000c + 1,500v + 1,500s and consider the 3,000(v + s) simply in its capacity as revenue, then it is the variable capital that seems to vanish, and capital considered from this standpoint seems to consist only of constant capital. For what originally appeared as 1,500v has resolved itself into a part of the social revenue, into wages, the revenue of the working class, and its character as capital has therewith vanished. This conclusion is in fact drawn by Ramsay . According to him, capital consists, from the social standpoint, of fixed capital alone , and by fixed capital he means constant capital, the mass of values consisting of means of production, whether these are means of labour or materials of labour. He calls the variable capital \"circulating.\" Ramsay's quotes: - \"Circulating capital consists only of subsistence and other necessities advanced to the workmen, previous to the completion of the produce of their labour... Fixed capital alone, not circulating, is properly speaking a source of national wealth... Circulating capital is not an immediate agent in production, nor essential to it at all, but merely a convenience rendered necessary by the deplorable poverty of the mass of the people... Fixed capital alone constitutes an element of cost of production in a national point of view\" (Ramsay, op. cit., pp. 23–6 passim). - \"The length of time during which any portion of the product of that labour [i.e. 'labour bestowed on any commodity'] has existed as fixed capital, i.e. in a form in which, though assisting to raise the future commodity, it does not maintain labourers\" (p. 59). The confusion. Marx explains: Here we see once again the confusion that Adam Smith wrought by submerging the distinction between constant and variable capital in the distinction between fixed and circulating capital . Ramsay's constant capital consists of means of labour, his circulating capital of means of subsistence; both of these are in fact commodities of a fixed value—the one is just as incapable of producing a surplus-value as the other. The fundamental error is the collapse of the crucial distinction (constant vs. variable, based on the role in the valorization process) into a merely technical distinction (fixed vs. circulating, based on the mode of turnover). This is a recurring theme of Marx's critique of political economy. J. Capital and Revenue: Variable Capital and Wages Section 10 addresses the question of the distinction between capital and revenue. The overall annual reproduction, the entire product of the current year, is the product of the useful labour of this year. But the value of this total product is greater than the portion of its value which embodies the annual labour, i.e. the labour-power spent during this year. The value product of the current year—the value newly created during the year in the commodity form—is smaller than the value of the product, the total value of the mass of commodities produced during the year. The difference. The difference which we obtain when we subtract from the total value of the annual product the value that was added to it by the labour of the current year is not value really reproduced, but simply value that reappears in a new form of existence ; value transferred to the annual product from value that existed beforehand, of an earlier or later date depending on the durability of the constant capital component involved in the current year's social labour process. This value may derive from the value of a means of production which came into the world in the preceding year or in one of a series of earlier years. It is at all events value transferred from the means of production of previous years to the product of the current year. The schema after exchange: - I. 4,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s (the latter 2,000 realized in means of consumption IIc) = 6,000. - II. 2,000c (reproduced by exchange with I(v + s)) + 500v + 500s = 3,000. - Sum of values = 9,000. Value newly produced during the year is to be found only in v and s. The value product of the current year is thus equal to the sum of v + s = 2,000 I(v + s) + 1,000 II(v + s) = 3,000 . All other value components of this year's product are simply value transferred from the value of earlier means of production that have been consumed in the current year's production. Besides this value of 3,000, the current year's labour has produced nothing more; this is its entire annual value product. The two departments' roles. As we have seen, however, the 2,000 I(v + s) restores to department II its 2,000 IIc in the natural form of means of production. The two-thirds of the annual labour spent in department I has thus newly produced the constant capital for department II, both in its entire value and in its natural form. Considered from the social standpoint, therefore, two-thirds of the labour spent during the year creates new constant capital value, realized in the natural form appropriate for department II. The greater part of the annual social labour is therefore spent on the production of new constant capital (capital value existing in means of production) to replace the constant capital value spent on the production of means of consumption. The savage and the civilized man (Senior). What distinguishes capitalist society from the savages in this respect is not, as Senior thinks, that it is the privilege and the characteristic of the savage to spend part of his labour in a way that procures him nothing in revenue, i.e. in proceeds that can be resolved into (exchanged for) means of consumption. The distinction rather consists in this: - (a) Capitalist society spends more of its disposable annual labour on the production of means of production (therefore of constant capital), which cannot be resolved into revenue in the form of wages or of surplus-value, but can function only as capital. - (b) If the savage makes bows, arrows, stone hammers, axes, baskets, etc., he knows well enough that he has not spent the time thus employed on the production of means of consumption, i.e. that he has met his need for means of production and nothing else. Besides, the savage commits a serious economic sin by his complete indifference to the use of his time, and may often spend a whole month, as Tylor tells us, on preparing a single arrow. The reference to Senior (and the footnote citing Senior's Principes fondamentaux de l'économie politique , Paris, 1836, pp. 342–3, and his notoriously anti-worker \"last hour\" theory from Volume 1, Chapter 9, note 3) is a sarcastic aside at the \"abstinence\" theory of capital. The critique of \"what is capital for one is revenue for another.\" The current notion by which one group of political economists seek to rid themselves of the theoretical difficulty—the idea that what is capital for one person is revenue for another, and vice versa—is partially correct, but becomes completely false as soon as it is put forward as a general rule (i.e. it involves a complete misunderstanding of the whole process of exchange that occurs in conjunction with annual reproduction, and also therefore a misunderstanding of the actual reason why the notion is partially correct). Marx's systematic treatment. He summarizes the factual relationships on which the partial correctness of this notion rests, demonstrating how false the current conception of these relationships is: 1. The variable capital functions as capital in the hands of the capitalist and as revenue in the hands of the wage-labourer. - The variable capital first exists in the hands of the capitalist as money capital ; it functions as money capital in so far as he buys labour-power with it. As long as it persists in his hands in the money form, it is nothing more than given value existing in that form, i.e. a constant and not a variable magnitude. It is only potentially variable capital, and it is that precisely because it is capable of being converted into labour-power. It only becomes actual variable capital after shedding its money form, after it has been converted into labour-power and when this begins to function as a component of productive capital in the capitalist process. - The money that functions firstly as the money form of variable capital for the capitalist now functions in the hands of the worker as the money form of his wage, which he converts into means of subsistence; i.e. as the money form of the revenue that he receives from the ever repeated sale of his labour-power. - We have here the simple fact that the money of the buyer, here the capitalist, passes from his hands into those of the seller, in this case the seller of labour-power, the worker . It is not the variable capital that functions twice over, as capital for the capitalist and as revenue for the worker, but simply the same money , which exists first in the hands of the capitalist as the money form of his variable capital, and which, once the capitalist has converted it into labour-power, serves in the hands of the worker as the equivalent for the labour-power he has sold. The fact that the same money serves one purpose in the hands of the seller and another in the hands of the buyer is simply a phenomenon inherent in all purchases and sales of commodities. The apologetic misrepresentation. Apologetic economists present the matter wrongly, as is best shown if we look simply at the act of circulation M–L (= M–C), the conversion of money into labour-power on the part of the capitalist buyer, and L–M (= C–M), the conversion of the commodity labour-power into money on the part of the seller, the worker. They say that the same money here realizes two capitals: the buyer—the capitalist—converts his money capital into living labour-power, which he incorporates into his productive capital; on the other hand, the seller—the worker—converts his commodity—labour-power—into money that he spends as revenue, which is precisely what enables him to sell his labour-power over and over again and thus to maintain himself; his labour-power is thus actually his capital in the commodity form, from which he constantly draws his revenue. Marx's rebuttal: In point of fact, labour-power is the worker's capacity (ever renewing and reproducing itself), not his capital . It is the only commodity that he can constantly sell, and he has to sell it in order to live, but it operates as capital (variable capital) only in the hands of the buyer, the capitalist. If a man is perpetually forced to sell his labour-power over and over again, i.e. to sell himself, to someone else, this proves, according to these economists, that he is a capitalist, because he always has a \"commodity\" (himself) for sale. In this sense even a slave would be a capitalist, even though he is sold once and for all as a commodity by a third person; for the nature of this commodity, the working slave, not only requires that its buyer put it to work each day, but also that he give it the means of subsistence that it needs in order to be able to work again. (Marx refers to Sismondi and Say in the letters to Malthus on this point.) 2. In the exchange of 1,000 Iv + 1,000 Is for 2,000 IIc, what is constant capital for some people (2,000 IIc) becomes variable capital and surplus-value, and thus completely revenue, for others; and what was variable capital and surplus-value (2,000 I(v + s)), i.e. entirely revenue for some, becomes constant capital for others. Marx examines this from the standpoint of the worker and the capitalist. From the worker's standpoint: The collective worker in department I has sold his labour-power to the collective capitalist of department I for 1,000; he receives this value paid in money in the form of a wage. With this money, he buys means of consumption to the same amount from department II. The capitalist in department II confronts him simply as a seller of commodities and nothing else, which is even the case when the worker buys from his own capitalist. The form of circulation that his commodity, labour-power, undergoes, is that of simple commodity circulation: C (labour-power) – M – C (means of consumption, the commodity of department II), oriented to consumption, i.e. simply to the satisfaction of needs. The result of this act of circulation is that the worker has maintained himself as labour-power for the department I capitalist, and in order to carry on maintaining himself in this way, he has perpetually to repeat the process L(C) – M – C over again. His wage is realized in means of consumption, it is spent as revenue, and taking the working class as a whole, it goes on being spent as revenue continuously. From the capitalist's standpoint: The entire commodity product of department II consists of means of consumption, i.e. of things designed to go into the annual consumption, to serve as the realization of someone or other's revenue, in the present case the collective worker of department I. For the collective capitalist of department II, however, a part of his commodity product, 2,000, is now the form of the constant capital value of his productive capital, and must be transformed back again from this commodity form into the natural form in which it can operate anew as the constant part of the productive capital. So far, capitalist II has managed to transform half (1,000) of his constant capital value, reproduced in the commodity form, back into the money form by selling it to the workers of department I. Thus it is not the variable capital Iv that has been converted into this first half of the constant capital value IIc, but rather the money that functioned for department I as money capital in exchange for labour-power and hence came into the possession of the seller of labour-power, for whom it did not represent capital but rather revenue in the money form , i.e. was spent as a means of purchase on items of consumption. The 1,000 in money that flowed to the capitalists in department II from the workers of department I cannot function as a constant element of department II's productive capital. It is only the money form of its commodity capital, and still has to be converted into fixed or circulating components of constant capital. Department II therefore uses the money received from the workers of department I to buy means of production from department I. In this way, half of department II's constant capital value is renewed in the natural form in which it can function once again as an element of productive capital in department II. The form of circulation here was C–M–C: means of consumption to a value of 1,000 – money (1,000) – means of production to a value of 1,000. Here, however, C–M–C is a movement of capital. The double roles. Just as the collective capitalist of department II functions for department I only as buyer of commodities, so the collective capitalist of department I functions for department II here only as a seller of commodities. Department I originally bought labour-power to the value of 1,000 with 1,000 of money which was destined to function as variable capital; it thereby received an equivalent for the 1,000v spent in the money form; the money now belongs to the worker, who spends it on purchases from department II; department I can get back this money, which has flowed into department II's cash-box, only if it fishes it out again by selling commodities to the same total value. Department I first had a certain sum of money, 1,000, destined to function as variable capital; this sum functions as variable capital by being converted into labour-power of the same value. As a result of the production process, however, the worker has provided a quantity of commodities (means of production) to the value of 6,000, of which one sixth or 1,000 is an equivalent value for the variable portion of capital advanced in money. The variable capital no more functions as variable capital in its commodity form than it did in its previous money form. As money, the variable capital value was only potentially variable capital, though it did exist in a form in which it could be directly converted into labour-power. As a commodity, however, this same variable capital value is still only potentially money value; it is restored to its original money form only by the sale of the commodity, i.e. here by the purchase by department II of 1,000 of commodities from department I. The movement of circulation is now: 1,000 (money) – labour-power to a value of 1,000 – 1,000 in commodities (equivalent for the variable capital) – 1,000v (money); i.e. M–C…C–M (= M–L…C–M) . The production process that falls between C…C does not itself pertain to the circulation sphere; it does not appear in the mutual exchange of the various elements of annual reproduction, even though this exchange includes the reproduction of all elements of the productive capital, not only its constant element but also its variable element, labour-power. The roles in circulation. All agents in this exchange simply appear as buyers or sellers, or both; the workers appear in it simply as commodity buyers; the capitalists alternately as buyers and sellers; and within certain limits simply as unilateral buyers or sellers. The result is that department I once more possesses the variable portion of its capital in the money form, the only form from which it is directly convertible back into labour-power; i.e. it possesses it again in the only form in which it can actually be advanced as the variable element of its productive capital. On the other hand, in order to reappear as a buyer of commodities, the worker must firstly reappear as the seller of a commodity, as the seller of his own labour-power. The three changes of form of variable capital in department I. The variable capital in department I undergoes three changes of form, which do not appear at all in the exchange of the annual product, or do so only by intimation: 1. Its first form is that of 1,000 Iv in money , which is converted into labour-power to the same value. This conversion does not itself appear in the commodity exchange between departments I and II, although its result appears in the fact that the working class of department I faces the commodity seller of department II with 1,000 in money. 2. The second form, the only one in which the variable capital actually varies, i.e. actually functions as variable capital, where value-creating power appears in place of the value given in exchange for it, pertains exclusively to the production process that lies behind us . 3. The third form, in which the variable capital has demonstrated its quality of being variable in the result of the production process, is the annual value product, which in the case of department I is 1,000v + 1,000s = 2,000 I(v + s) . In place of its original value of 1,000 in money, twice this value has appeared in commodities. The variable capital value of 1,000 in commodities thus forms only half of the value product created by the variable capital as an element of the productive capital. The concrete circuit of variable capital. During all these changes, capitalist I constantly keeps in hand his variable capital: (1) originally as money capital; (2) then as an element of his productive capital; (3) later as a value component of his commodity capital, i.e. in commodity value; (4) finally in money again, and once more standing face-to-face with the labour-power into which it is convertible. During the labour process, the capitalist has the variable capital in his hands as self-acting, value-creating labour-power, but not as value of a given magnitude; however, since he pays the worker only after his power has already operated for a definite period of time, whether longer or shorter, he already has the replacement value that labour-power creates for itself in his hands before he pays, as well as the surplus-value. The crucial point. Since this variable capital always remains in one form or other in the hands of the capitalist, it can in no way be said to be converted into revenue for anyone . 1,000 Iv in commodities is rather converted into money by its sale to department II, for which it replaces half of its constant capital in kind. What is resolved into revenue is not department I's variable capital of 1,000v in money; the money has ceased to function as the money form of department I's variable capital as soon as it is converted into labour-power, just as the money of any other commodity seller has ceased to represent anything belonging to him once he has exchanged it for a commodity being sold. The conversions undergone by the money drawn in wages in the hands of the working class are not conversions of variable capital, but rather of the value of their labour-power transformed into money, just as the conversion of the value product created by the worker (2,000 I(v + s)) is simply the conversion of a commodity belonging to the capitalist, and does not affect the worker. The difficulty for the capitalist. It is, however, very difficult for the capitalist, and still more so for his theoretical interpreter, the political economist, to rid himself of the idea that the money paid to the worker is still the capitalist's money . If the capitalist is a producer of gold, then the variable portion of value—i.e. the equivalent in commodities that compensates him for the purchase price of labour—directly appears in the money form, and can therefore function anew as variable money capital without the detour of a reflux. As far as the department II worker is concerned, however—ignoring here the luxury worker—500 IIv actually exists in commodities that are destined for the worker's consumption, commodities which he buys, considered as the collective worker, directly from the same collective capitalist to whom he has sold his labour-power. The variable value portion of department II's capital consists of means of consumption, as far as its natural form is concerned, destined for the most part to be consumed by the working class. But it is not the variable capital that is spent by the worker in this form; it is the wage, the worker's money, that re-establishes for the capitalist his variable capital 500 IIv in its money form, precisely through its realization in these means of consumption. The variable capital IIv is reproduced in means of consumption, just as is the constant capital 2,000 IIc; the one is no more resolved into revenue than the other. What is resolved into revenue is in both cases the wage. But if 1,000 IIc, and by the same detour 1,000 Iv and 500 IIv, i.e. both constant capital and variable, are restored as money capital by the expenditure of wages as revenue, this is an important fact in the exchange of the annual product. K. Replacement of the Fixed Capital: Hoard Formation, Disproportionality, and the Possibility of Crisis Section 11 is the most technically difficult part of the chapter. It addresses the problem that arises from the fact that a portion of the constant capital value consists of means of labour in the strict sense (fixed capital), whose value is transferred bit by bit to the product while the means of labour themselves continue to function in their old natural form over a period of years. The problem. If we take the simplest form: (I) 4,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s + (II) 2,000c + 500v + 500s = 9,000, which is ultimately resolved into 4,000 Ic + 2,000 IIc + 1,000 Iv + 500 IIv + 1,000 Is + 500 IIs = 6,000c + 1,500v + 1,500s = 9,000. One portion of the constant capital value, that which consists of means of labour in the strict sense, is transferred from the means of labour to the product of labour while these means of labour still continue to function as elements of the productive capital, and moreover in their old natural form. What is transferred from the instrument to the product of labour, and reappears as an element of the value of the commodities that these means of labour produce, is their wear and tear , the loss of value that they suffer bit by bit in the course of their function over a certain period. As far as the annual reproduction is concerned, therefore, only those components of the fixed capital whose life is longer than a year come into consideration. If they expire in the course of the year, then they have to be completely replaced and renewed by the annual reproduction. In the case of machines and other more long-lasting forms of fixed capital, it may happen—and more often than not does happen—that certain partial organs have to be entirely replaced within the year, even though the building or machine as a whole has a longer life. The distinction from repair costs. This element of commodity value should in no way be confused with the costs of repair (see Volume 2, p. 255). When the commodity is sold, this value element is realized and transformed into money like the others; it is only after this transformation that its difference from the other elements of value comes into view. The raw materials and ancillaries consumed in the production of commodities have to be replaced in kind so that the reproduction of the commodities can begin; the labour-power spent on them must similarly be replaced by fresh labour-power. The money received from the commodity must therefore be constantly converted back into these elements of productive capital. This is in no way changed by the fact that raw materials and ancillaries may be bought at certain dates on a relatively large scale, so that they form production reserves. The crucial difference with fixed capital. But the part of the money received from the sale of commodities that represents the realized value component of the commodities equal to the wear and tear of the fixed capital is not transformed back again into the component of productive capital whose loss of value it replaces. It settles down alongside the productive capital and persists in its money form. This precipitation of money is repeated until the reproduction period during which the fixed element of the constant capital continues to function in the production process in its old natural form has elapsed. Once the fixed element—buildings, machinery, etc.—has expired, and can no longer function in the production process, its value exists alongside it completely converted into money, as the sum of the money precipitated, the sum of the values which were gradually transferred from the fixed capital to the commodities in whose production it collaborated, and which passed over into the money form when these commodities were sold. This money then serves to replace the fixed capital in kind. This hoard formation is therefore itself an element of the capitalist reproduction process , the reproduction and storage—in the money form—of the value of the fixed capital or its individual elements, until such a time as the fixed capital has expired and consequently surrendered the whole of its value to the commodities produced, when it has to be replaced in kind. This money, however, gives up its hoard form and again steps actively into the reproduction process of capital mediated by circulation only after it has been transformed back into new elements of fixed capital to replace the dead ones. The role of money. The reconversion of the annual commodity product can no more be resolved into the mere unmediated mutual exchange of its various components than simple commodity circulation is identical with the direct exchange of products. Money plays a specific role in it, one which is expressed in the very manner in which the fixed capital value is reproduced. (a) Replacement of the Depreciation Component in the Money Form. Marx takes the basic schema and asks: if the commodities 2,000 IIc are exchanged for commodities of the same value I(1,000v + 1,000s), this assumes that 2,000 IIc is completely reconverted in kind into the natural components of department II's constant capital that are replaced by department I. However, the commodity value of 2,000 in which IIc exists contains an element for depreciation of its fixed capital , which cannot be immediately replaced in kind, but has to be transformed into money, its total sum accumulating bit by bit until the time falls due for the renewal of this fixed capital in its natural form. The annual reproduction is not ab ovo. If we consider the annual reproduction—even on the same scale, i.e. abstracting from all accumulation—then we do not begin ab ovo; this is one year in the course of many, not capitalist production's year of birth. The various capitals invested in the manifold branches of production in department II are therefore of different ages, and just as each year people functioning in these branches of production die, so each year do quantities of fixed capital reach the end of their life and have to be renewed in kind from the accumulated money fund. To this extent, the exchange of 2,000 IIc for 2,000 I(v + s) involves the reconversion of 2,000 IIc from its commodity form back into the natural elements of constant capital that consist not only of raw materials and ancillaries, but also of the natural elements of fixed capital—machines, instruments, buildings, etc. The wear and tear that has to be replaced in money in the value of the 2,000 IIc thus in no way corresponds to the total scale of the fixed capital that is functioning, since each year a part of this has to be replaced in kind; this presupposes, however, that in earlier years the money needed for this replacement was accumulated in the hands of the department II capitalists. The apparent difficulty. In the exchange between I(1,000v + 1,000s) and 2,000 IIc, the first thing to note is that the sum of values I(v + s) does not contain any element of constant value, and thus no value element for the wear and tear to be replaced. This element does exist, on the other hand, in IIc, and it is precisely a part of this value element attributable to the fixed capital that does not have to be directly transformed from the money form into the natural form, but has first to persist in the money form. The exchange between I(1,000v + 1,000s) and 2,000 IIc thus immediately presents the apparent difficulty that the means of production I, the natural form in which the 2,000(v + s) exists, have to be replaced to the entire amount of their value of 2,000 by an equivalent in means of consumption II, whereas the means of consumption 2,000 IIc cannot be exchanged to their full value for the means of production I(1,000v + 1,000s) , since an aliquot part of their value—equal to the wear and tear or loss of value of the fixed capital—must first be precipitated out into money that does not function again as means of circulation within the current period of annual reproduction. Where does the money come from? But the money through which the element of wear and tear contained in the commodity value of 2,000 IIc is realized can derive only from department I, since department II does not itself have to pay out, but is paid precisely by the sale of its commodities, and since according to our assumption I(v + s) buys the entire sum of commodities 2,000 IIc; department I must therefore realize this wear and tear for department II by way of this sale. However, according to the law developed earlier, money advanced to circulation returns to the capitalist producer when he later casts the same amount into circulation in commodities. It is evidently impossible for department I, in purchasing IIc, to give department II commodities worth 2,000, and to give it once and for all, on top of that, an extra amount of money (without any return of this money to it by the reconversion operation). Otherwise the quota of commodities IIc would be sold above its value. The first hypothetical solution (rejected): Suppose department II sells 2,000 to department I, but only buys from department I commodities for 1,800. The commodity value of 2,000 IIc then contains 200 for replacement of wear and tear, which is hoarded up in money; the value of 2,000 IIc would be broken down into 1,800c + 200c(d) , where d stands for déchet (depreciation). We would then have: I. 1,000v + 1,000s II. 1,800c + 200c(d) Marx traces the exchanges. Department I buys 1,000 IIc means of consumption from department II with £1,000 that its workers have received for their labour-power in payment of wages. The capitalists in department I thereby receive their variable capital back in its money form. Department II also advances £400, say, to buy means of production Is, and department I uses the same £400, in which it has realized part of its surplus-value, to buy means of consumption IIc. The £400 advanced to the circulation sphere by department II has thus returned to the capitalists in department II, but only as the equivalent for the commodities they have sold. Department I now advances £400 to buy means of consumption; department II buys means of production for £400 from department I, and this £400 thereby flows back to department I. The account: - Department I has cast into circulation 1,000v + 800s in commodities, and also casts into circulation in money £1,000 in wages and £400 for exchange with department II. After the exchange is completed, department I has 1,000v in money, 800s converted into 800 IIc (means of consumption) and £400 in money. - Department II casts into circulation 1,800c in commodities and £400 in money; after the exchange, it has 1,800 in commodities I (means of production) and £400 in money. We then still have on I's side 200s (in means of production), and on II's side 200c(d) (in means of consumption). According to our assumption, department I buys means of consumption c(d) to the value of 200 with £200 in money; this £200, however, department II holds on to, since it represents 200c(d) wear and tear, and is not to be directly converted back into means of production. 200 Is is therefore unsaleable; one fifth of the surplus-value that department I has to convert cannot be realized. This does not only contradict the assumption of reproduction on the same scale; it is in and of itself not the kind of hypothesis that could explain the realization of 200c(d); it says rather that this is inexplicable . Since there is no way of showing how the 200c(d) is to be realized, we have to suppose that department I is obliging enough to realize it, precisely because department I is not in a position to realize its own remnant of 200s. To conceive this as a normal operation of the exchange mechanism would be the same as supposing that each year £200 rained down from heaven to realize the 200c(d). The apologetic evasions. The absurdity of such a hypothesis does not directly leap to the eye if Is, instead of appearing in its original mode of existence, appears in the hands of the capitalist's co-partners, e.g. as ground-rent in the hands of the landlord or as interest in the hands of the money-lender. If the part of the surplus-value in commodities that the industrial capitalist has to deduct as ground-rent or interest for other persons with a claim on surplus-value cannot be realized in the long run by the sale of the commodities themselves, there is then an end to the payment of rent and interest, and the landlords or the recipients of interest cannot serve as deus ex machina for the arbitrary realization of certain portions of annual reproduction. It is just the same with the expenditures of all so-called unproductive workers, state officials, doctors, lawyers, etc., and others who, in the form of the \"general public,\" perform \"services\" for the political economists by explaining what they leave unexplained. It helps just as little if, instead of direct exchange between departments I and II, the merchant is brought in as mediator to remove all difficulties with his \"money.\" In the given case, the 200 Is must finally be disposed of to the industrial capitalists of department II. It may go through the hands of a whole series of merchants, but the last of these still finds himself in the same position vis-à-vis department II—according to our hypothesis—as the capitalist producers of department I did at the beginning, i.e. they cannot sell the 200 Is to department II; and as this sum of purchases has thus stuck fast, it prevents department I from repeating the process. Marx concludes: \"We see here how, apart from our specific purpose of considering the reproduction process in its fundamental form—setting aside all obscuring circumstances that intervene—it is necessary throughout to do away with the false subterfuges that provide a semblance of 'scientific' explanation, if the process of social reproduction in its intricate concrete form is to become the object of our analysis.\" The law that, in the normal course of reproduction (whether simple or on an expanded scale), the money advanced to circulation by the capitalist producer must return to its starting-point thus excludes once and for all the hypothesis that the 200 IIc(d) can be realized by the money advanced by department I . (b) Replacement of the Fixed Capital in Kind. After setting aside the above hypothesis, there still remain those possibilities which, besides the replacement of the wear and tear component in money, also bring in the replacement of the defunct fixed capital in kind. The assumptions: (a) That £1,000 was paid by department I in wages, and spent by the department I workers on IIc to the same amount. That the £1,000 was advanced by department I in money is no more than a simple statement of fact. Wages are paid by the relevant capitalist producers in money; this money is then spent by the workers on means of subsistence, and serves the sellers of these means of subsistence in turn as means of circulation for the conversion of their constant capital from commodity capital into productive capital. It certainly runs through several channels (shopkeepers, landlords of dwelling-houses, tax collectors, unproductive workers such as doctors, etc. that the worker himself needs), and it therefore flows only in part directly from the hands of the workers in department I into those of the capitalist class of department II. All this can be omitted in considering the fundamental form. (b) It was also assumed that at one point department I advances a further £400 in money for purchases from department II, which later flows back to it, and at another point department II advances £400 for purchases from department I, which similarly flows back to department II. This assumption must be made, since the opposite assumption, that only the capitalists in either department I or II unilaterally advanced to circulation the money needed for the exchange of commodities, would be arbitrary. Since we showed in sub-section (a) that the hypothesis according to which department I casts additional money into circulation in order to realize the 200 IIc(d) has to be rejected as absurd, we have left only the apparently still more absurd hypothesis that department II itself casts into circulation the money with which that value component of commodities is realized which has to replace the wear and tear of its fixed capital . The apparent absurdity and its resolution. The apparent absurdity: The portion of value that Mr A's spinning machine loses in the course of production, for example, reappears as a part of the value of his yarn. The loss that his spinning machine suffers in value he thus collects on the other side as money. X might now, for example, buy cotton from Y for £200, and in this way advance £200 to the circulation sphere; Y buys yarn from him with the same £200, and this £200 now serves X as a fund for replacing the wear and tear of his spinning machine. What this boils down to is simply that X, apart from his production, its product, and the sale of this, keeps a further £200 stacked away in order to pay himself for the spinning machine's loss of value, i.e. that besides the £200 loss of value of his spinning machine, he has to add a further 200 in money each year from his own pocket, so as finally to be in a position to buy a new spinning machine. The resolution: The absurdity of this, however, is only apparent. Department II consists of capitalists whose fixed capital is at different points in its reproduction. For some, it has reached the point at which it has to be completely replaced in kind. For others, it is still more or less distant from this stage; what is common to all members of the latter division is that their fixed capital is not really reproduced, i.e. not renewed in kind or replaced by a new item of the same variety, but that its value is successively collected up in money. The first group of capitalists is in exactly the same situation as when they began their business, i.e. when they appeared on the market with a money capital in order to transform this on the one hand into constant capital (fixed and circulating), on the other hand into labour-power, into variable capital. Just as at that time, they now have to advance this money capital once more to the circulation sphere. The two sections of department II. Marx divides department II into: - Section 1: those who replace their fixed capital in kind. - Section 2: those who store up the wear-and-tear value of their fixed capital in the money form. If we assume that, out of the £400 that the capitalist class in department II casts into circulation for the purpose of exchange with department I, half derives from those capitalists in department II who not only have to renew in kind, by the sale of their commodities, those of their means of production that form their circulating capital, but also have to renew, with their money, their fixed capital, while the other half of these capitalists in department II renew only the circulating part of their constant capital in kind, with their money, and do not renew their fixed capital, there is nothing contradictory in the fact that the £400 that flows back is now divided differently between these two sections of department II . It flows back to department II; however, it does not flow back into the same hands, but is rather differently distributed within this class, passing from one section of it to the other. The first section of department II has, besides the portion of means of production ultimately covered by its commodities, converted a further £200 in money into new elements of fixed capital in kind. The money it has spent in this way flows back to it from the circulation sphere only bit by bit over a series of years—just as at the start of business—in the shape of the depreciation component of the commodities to be produced with this fixed capital. The other section of department II, on the other hand, did not obtain any commodities from department I with its £200. Instead department I pays it with the money with which the first section of department II bought elements of fixed capital. One section of department II has its fixed capital back in its renewed natural form, the other is still engaged in collecting it up in the money form, so as to replace its fixed capital in kind later on. The three cases. Marx assumes we start from the remnants of commodities to be exchanged on both sides: department I's 400s, and department II's 400c. We assume that department II advances £400 in money for the exchange of these commodities to the amount of 800. Half of this £400 (=£200) must under all circumstances be laid out by the section of IIc that accumulates the £200 in money as the value of wear and tear, and which then has to transform this back again into the natural form of its fixed capital. A certain quantity of department II's commodities (in our case, half of the remainder, = 200) are here no more than the bearers of this wear-and-tear value, which has to be precipitated out by conversion into money. As for the second half (=£200) of the £400 cast into circulation by department II in connection with this residual operation, this buys circulating components of constant capital from department I. The £400 is thus now used to withdraw from department I: (1) commodities to the total of £200, which consist simply of elements of fixed capital; (2) commodities to the total of £200, which simply replace in kind elements of the circulating part of the constant capital. Department I has now sold its entire annual commodity product, to the extent that this has to be sold to department II. The value of a fifth of it, £400, exists in its hands in the money form, but this money is realized surplus-value and has to be spent as revenue on means of consumption. Department I therefore uses this £400 to buy the remaining commodity value of department II = 400. The money flows back to department II by removing commodities from that department. The three cases of distribution: (a) Out of the commodity value of 400c that department II still has in hand, section 1 has 100 and section 2 has 300; out of this 300, 200 represents the wear and tear. - Section 1 originally laid out £300 (£200 in money with which it drew elements of fixed capital in kind from department I, and £100 in money to mediate its commodity exchange with department I). - Section 2 advanced only a quarter of the £400, i.e. £100, also for the mediation of its commodity exchange with department I. - The £400 in money: section 1 advanced £300 and section 2 £100. - This £400 flows back as follows: To section 1: £100 , i.e. only a third of the money it advanced. It possesses, however, for the other two-thirds, fixed capital to a value of 200 . In return for this element of fixed capital to the value of 200 it has given money to department I, but not subsequently any commodity. As far as this 200 is concerned, department II confronts department I simply as a buyer, and not subsequently again as a seller. This money cannot then flow back to section 1; otherwise this would have received its elements of fixed capital from department I for nothing. As far as the last third of the money it advanced is concerned, section 1 first appeared as a buyer of the circulating components of its constant capital. With the same money, department I buys from it the remainder of its commodities to the value of 100. The money thus flows back to section 1 of department II because this section appears as a seller of commodities directly after having appeared as a buyer. If the money did not flow back to it, then section 1 would firstly have given £100 in money to department I for commodities to a value of 100, and then a further 100 in commodities into the bargain, i.e. it would have given its commodities away as a present. - Section 2, on the other hand, which laid out £100 in money, receives a reflux of £300 ; £100, because it firstly cast £100 in money into the circulation sphere as a buyer, and receives this back as a seller; and £200, because it functions only as a seller of commodities to the amount of £200, and not also as a buyer to this amount. Thus the money cannot flow back to department I. The wear and tear of the fixed capital is therefore paid for by the money cast into circulation by department II, section 1, in the purchase of elements of fixed capital; but it does not come into section 2's hands as the money of section 1, but rather as money belonging to department I. (b) On this assumption, the remnant of IIc is distributed in such a way that section 1 has only £200 in money, and section 2 the 400 in commodities. - Section 1 has sold all its commodities, but the £200 in money is the changed form of the fixed component of its constant capital, which it has to renew in kind. It appears here, therefore, simply as a buyer, and, in place of its money, receives commodities from department I to the same amount in the natural elements of fixed capital. - Section 2 has, as a maximum, to cast into circulation only £200, since for half the value of its commodities it only sells to department I, and does not buy from it. - £400 then returns to section 2 from the circulation sphere ; £200, because it advanced this as a buyer and receives it back as a seller of commodities to the value of £200; and £200, because it sold commodities to department I to the value of 200, without withdrawing an equivalent in value for these from department I. (c) Section 1 possesses £200 in money and 200c in commodities; section 2 has 200(d) in commodities. - On this assumption, section 2 does not have to advance anything in money, because it no longer functions at all as a buyer vis-à-vis department I, but rather only as a seller, and thus has to wait until its goods are bought. - Section 1 advances £400 in money; £200 for mutual commodity exchange with department I, £200 as a mere buyer from it. With this latter £200 in money, it buys its elements of fixed capital. - Department I uses £200 to buy 200 in commodities from section 1, and it is therefore to section 1 that the £200 advanced in money for this commodity exchange returns; department I uses the other £200—which it has also obtained from section 1—to buy commodities worth 200 from section 2, which thereby has the wear and tear of its fixed capital precipitated out in money. The pure form of the problem. The difficulty that emerged in connection with the exchange I(1,000v + 1,000s) against II(2,000c) was reduced to the difficulty in converting the remnants: - I. 400s - II. (1) 200 money + 200c commodities + (2) 200c commodities Since 200c in commodities in department II, section 1, is exchanged for 200 Is (commodities), and since all the money that circulates for this exchange of 400 in commodities between departments I and II returns to the department which advanced it, this money is in fact not an element of our present problem. To put it another way, if we assume that in this exchange between 200 Is and 200 IIc (commodities of department II, section 1), the money functions as means of payment , and not as means of purchase, and thus not as a \"means of circulation\" in the strictest sense, then it is evident that, since the commodities 200 Is and 200 IIc (section 1) are of equal value, and means of production to a value of 200 are exchanged for means of consumption to a value of 200, the money functions here only ideally, and no money really has to be cast into the circulation sphere to settle the balance . The problem therefore emerges in its pure form only if we cancel out the commodities 200 Is on the side of department I, and their equivalent, commodities 200 IIc (section 1) on the side of department II. After eliminating these two amounts of commodities of the same value (I and II) that mutually balance each other, the remnant to be exchanged remains as follows: - I. 200s commodities - II. (1) 200c (money) + (2) 200c (commodities) The pure solution: Department II, section 1, buys the components of its fixed capital 200 Is with £200 in money; the fixed capital of department I, a value of 200, is transformed from the commodity form into the money form. With this money, department I buys means of consumption from department II, section 2, and the result, for department II, is that section 1 has been able to renew a fixed component of its constant capital in kind; and that section 2 has another component (which replaces the wear and tear of its fixed capital) precipitated out in money. This continues each year, until this component too is due to be replaced in kind. The law of reproduction. The precondition here is evidently that the fixed component of department II's constant capital which in any given year has been transformed back into money to its full value and thus has to be renewed in kind (section 1) has to be equal to the annual wear and tear of the other fixed component of the constant capital in department II which still goes on functioning in its old natural form , and whose wear and tear, the loss of value that it transfers to the commodities in whose production it is involved, has first to be replaced in money. Such a balance accordingly appears as a law of reproduction on the same scale ; which means in other words that the proportionate division of labour in department I, where means of production are produced, must remain unaltered, in so far as it supplies on the one hand circulating, and on the other hand fixed components of the constant capital of department II. The First Case: IIc(1) greater than IIc(2). - I. 200s. - II. (1) 220c (in money) + (2) 200c (in commodities). Here IIc(1) buys commodities 200 Is for £200 in money, and department I uses the same money to buy commodities 200 IIc(2), i.e. the component of fixed capital that has to be precipitated out in money; the latter is thereby realized. But 20 IIc(1) in money cannot be transformed back into fixed capital in kind. It might seem that this unfortunate state of affairs can be rectified if we take the remnant of Is as 220 instead of 200, so that, out of the 2,000 I, only 1,780 instead of 1,800 have been disposed of by earlier exchange. In this case, department II section 1 buys the 220 Is for £220 in money, and I then buys the 200 IIc(2) in commodities for £200. But there is still £20 on the side of department I, a piece of surplus-value that it can only hold onto in money, and cannot spend on means of consumption. The difficulty is only shifted from IIc (section 1) to Is. The Second Case: IIc(1) smaller than IIc(2). - I. 200s (in commodities). - II. (1) 180c (in money) + (2) 200c (in commodities). Department II, section 1, buys commodities 180 Is for £180 in money; department I uses this money to buy commodities to the same value from department II, section 2, i.e. 180 IIc(2). There remains an unsaleable 20 Is on one side, and similarly 20 IIc(2) on the other; commodities to the value of 40 that cannot be transformed into money. It would not be any use to take the remnant in department I as 180; there would certainly not be any longer a surplus in department I, but there would still be an unsaleable surplus in IIc (section 2), one which could not be transformed into money. Summary of the two cases: - In the first case , where II(1) is bigger than II(2), there remains on the side of IIc(1) a surplus in money which cannot be transformed into fixed capital, or if the remnant Is is taken as = IIc(1), the same surplus in money on the part of Is, which cannot be transformed into means of consumption. - In the second case , where IIc(1) is smaller than IIc(2), there remains a monetary deficit on the part of both the 200 Is and the IIc(2), and the same surplus in commodities on the two sides; or if the remnant Is is taken as = IIc(1), a deficit in money and a surplus in commodities on the part of IIc(2). Foreign trade. If we take the remnant Is as always equal to IIc(1)—since production is determined by orders, and it does not alter the reproduction in any way if this year more components of fixed capital, and next year more components of circulating capital, are produced by department I for department II's constant capital—then: - In the first case , Is could be transformed back into means of consumption only if department I bought with it a part of the surplus-value of department II, so that, instead of being consumed, this was accumulated by department II as money; in the second case , it would only help if department I spent the money itself, i.e. the hypothesis that we rejected. - If IIc(1) is bigger than IIc(2) , then an import of foreign commodities is needed in order to realize the monetary surplus in Is. If IIc(1) is less than IIc(2) , then conversely an export of commodities II (means of consumption) is needed to realize the wear and tear component in IIc in means of production. In both cases, foreign trade is necessary. Even if we assume, in considering reproduction on a constant scale, that the productivity of all branches of industry, and thus also the proportionate value ratios of their commodity products, remains constant, the two cases last mentioned, in which IIc(1) is greater or less than IIc(2), would still be of interest for production on an expanded scale, where they will inevitably arise. (c) Results. Marx summarizes the general points: Suppose that all other circumstances remain the same, i.e. not only the scale of production, but also and particularly the productivity of labour. Then if a greater part of the fixed element of IIc becomes defunct than it did the previous year, and therefore a greater part has to be renewed in kind, that portion of fixed capital that is as yet only en route to its demise, and has to be replaced in money for the time being, must decline in the same proportion, since, according to our assumption, the sum (including the value sum) of the portion of fixed capital functioning in department II remains the same. This however brings about the following situation: - Firstly, if a greater part of department I's commodity capital consists of elements of the fixed capital of IIc, then the circulating component for IIc is so much the less, since the total amount that department I produces for IIc remains unchanged. If one part increases, then the other must decline, and vice versa. On the other hand, however, the total production of department II also remains the same. But how is this possible if its raw materials, work in progress, and ancillary materials decrease (i.e. the circulating elements of constant capital in department II)? - Secondly, a greater part of the fixed capital IIc, restored in the money form, flows to department I to be changed back from the money form into the natural form. Thus additional money accrues to department I, besides the money that circulates simply to exchange commodities between I and II: money that does not mediate reciprocal commodity exchange, but appears in its unilateral function as a means of purchase. At the same time, however, the quantity of commodities IIc that bears the replacement value of the wear and tear would be proportionately reduced, i.e. the quantity of commodities in department II that does not have to be exchanged against commodities from department I, but only against department I's money. More money from department II would flow to department I as mere means of purchase, and there would be fewer commodities from department II towards which department I had to function merely as a buyer. A greater part of Is—for Iv has already been converted into department II commodities—would thus not be convertible into commodities II, and would be held fast in the money form. The crisis of production. \"We do not need here to go any further into the opposite case, where the reproduction of the defunct fixed capital in department II was less in one year and the wear and tear component greater. There would be a crisis—a crisis of production—despite reproduction on a constant scale. \" The general law. In short, if in the case of simple reproduction and with other circumstances remaining the same—i.e. particularly with the productivity, overall amount, and intensity of labour remaining unchanged—a constant proportion is not assumed between the defunct fixed capital (that needing renewal) and the fixed capital which continues to operate in the old natural form (merely adding value to its products to replace its wear and tear), then: - In one case, the amount of circulating components to be reproduced remains the same, but the amount of fixed components to be replaced will have increased; the total production of department I therefore has to grow, or else there would be an insufficient amount of reproduction . - In the other case, if the proportionate size of the fixed capital in department II that has to be reproduced in kind declines, then the amount of constant capital II's circulating components that have been reproduced by department I remains unchanged, while the fixed components to be reproduced have declined. There is thus either a reduction in the total production of department I, or alternatively a surplus (as previously a deficit), a surplus that cannot be realized. The remedies and their limits. The same labour can certainly supply in the first case a greater product, with increased productivity, extension, or intensity, and in this way the deficit in the first case could be covered; but a change of this kind could not occur without a shift of labour and capital from one branch of production in department I to another, and any displacement of this kind would produce momentary dislocations . Secondly, however, in so far as extension and intensification of labour increase, department I would have more value to exchange for less value from department II, i.e. department I's product would depreciate. The reverse happens in the second case, where department I has either to contract its production, which means a crisis for the workers and capitalists engaged in it, or to supply a surplus, which again leads to crisis. Of themselves, these surpluses are no evil, rather an advantage; in capitalist production, however, they are an evil. Foreign trade. Foreign trade could help in both cases, in the first to exchange for means of consumption the commodities from department I which are held fast in the money form, in the second to dispose of the surplus commodities. But foreign trade, in so far as it does not just replace elements (and their value), only shifts the contradictions to a broader sphere, and gives them a wider orbit . The non-capitalist counterfactual. Once we dispense with the capitalist form of reproduction, then the whole problem boils down to the fact that the magnitude of the part of fixed capital that becomes defunct and has therefore to be replaced in kind varies in successive years (here we are dealing simply with the fixed capital functioning in the production of means of consumption). If it is very large one year (if the mortality is above the average, just as with human beings), then in the following years it will certainly be so much the less. The mass of raw materials, work in progress, and ancillaries needed for the annual production of means of consumption—assuming that other circumstances remain the same—does not diminish on this account; and so the total production of the means of production would have to increase in one case, and decrease in the other. This can only be remedied by perpetual relative over-production ; on the one hand a greater quantity of fixed capital is produced than is directly needed; on the other hand, and this is particularly important, a stock of raw materials etc. is produced that surpasses the immediate annual need (this is particularly true of means of subsistence). Over-production of this kind is equivalent to control by the society over the objective means of its own reproduction. Within capitalist society, however, it is an anarchic element. The novelty for the economists. This example of fixed capital—in the context of reproduction on a constant scale—is a striking one. A disproportionate production of fixed and circulating capital is a factor much favoured by the economists in their explanation of crises. It is something new to them that a disproportion of this kind can and must arise from the mere maintenance of the fixed capital; that it can and must arise on the assumption of an ideal normal production, with simple reproduction of the social capital already functioning. L. The Reproduction of the Money Material: Gold, Hoards, and the Source of Circulating Money Section 12 addresses a factor so far completely disregarded: the annual reproduction of gold and silver. As the mere raw material for luxury articles, for gold and silver plating, etc., these would no more need special mention than any other products. However, they play an important role as money material and hence as potential money . For the sake of simplification, Marx takes gold as the sole money material. Empirical data. According to earlier figures, the world's total annual gold production amounted to some 800,000–900,000 lb., i.e. between 1,100 and 1,250 million marks. According to Soetbeer (Adolf Soetbeer, Edelmetall-Produktion , Gotha, 1879, p. 112), however, the average for the years 1871–1875 was only 170,674 kilograms, or a value of approximately 476 million marks. Australia supplied around 167 million marks' worth, the USA 166 million, and Russia 93 million. The remainder was divided between various countries in amounts of less than 10 million marks each. The annual silver production during the same period amounted to something under 2 million kilos, with a value of 354½ million marks, of which Mexico supplied about 108 million marks' worth, the USA 102, South America 67, Germany 26 million, etc. Methodological assumption. Of the countries in which capitalist production is dominant, only the USA is a producer of gold and silver; the European capitalist countries receive almost all their gold and by far the greater portion of their silver from Australia, the USA, Mexico, South America, and Russia. However, Marx proposes to put gold mines into the country of capitalist production whose annual reproduction we are analysing, for the following reason: Capitalist production never exists without foreign trade. If normal annual reproduction on a given scale is presupposed, then it is also supposed together with this that foreign trade replaces domestic articles only by those of other use or natural forms, without affecting value ratios. Bringing foreign trade into an analysis of the value of the product annually reproduced can therefore only confuse things, without supplying any new factor either to the problem or to its solution. We therefore completely abstract from it here, and treat gold as a direct element of the annual reproduction, not as a commodity imported from abroad by exchange. The gold schema. The production of gold belongs, along with metal production in general, to department I , the category which comprises the production of means of production. Marx assumes that the annual gold product = 30 (for convenience only, as this is in fact far too high in relation to the other figures in the schema); this value can be broken down into 20c + 5v + 5s ; the 20c is to be exchanged against other elements of Ic (a point Marx's manuscript does not investigate further—see the editorial note); the 5v + 5s , however, has to be exchanged against elements of IIc, i.e. means of consumption. The circuit of gold's variable capital. As far as the 5v is concerned, every gold-producing business first begins by buying labour-power; not with the gold it has itself produced, but with an aliquot part of the money already in stock in the country. The workers use this v to withdraw means of consumption from department II, and department II uses it to buy materials of production from department I. Let us say that department II buys gold worth 2 from department I as a commodity material, etc. (a component of its constant capital), so that 2v flows back to the gold producers in department I in money that already belonged earlier to the circulation sphere. If department II buys nothing more in gold material from department I, the gold producers there still buy from II, in as much as they cast their gold into the circulation here as money, since gold can buy every commodity. The difference is simply that department I does not appear here as a seller, but only as a buyer. The gold-diggers in department I can always get rid of their commodity, which always exists in a directly exchangeable form. The comparison with the cotton spinner. Let us assume that a cotton spinner has paid his workers 5v, and that these supply him—leaving aside his surplus-value—with spun yarn equal to 5; the workers then buy IIc to the value of 5, and department II buys yarn from department I for 5 in money, so that the 5v flows back in money to the cotton spinner. In the case assumed here, on the other hand, Ig (as we shall denote the gold producers) advances 5v to its workers, in money which already belonged earlier to the circulation sphere; these spend the money on means of subsistence, but out of this 5, only 2 returns from department II back to Ig. Ig, however, can begin the reproduction process again just as well as the cotton-spinner can, as its workers have supplied it with 5 in gold. 2 of this has been sold, and it still has 3 left in gold, which therefore only has to be coined, or transformed into banknotes, for its whole variable capital to exist again in its hands in the money form , without any further mediation by department II. Hoard formation within department II. Even in this first process of annual reproduction, however, a change has taken place in the quantity of money actually or virtually belonging to the circulation sphere. We assumed that IIc bought 2v(Ig) as material, and that Ig laid out 3 again in department II as the money form of variable capital. Thus the value of 3 that remained within department II, and did not flow back to department I, comes from the sum of money supplied by the new gold production. According to our assumption, department II has satisfied its need for gold material. The 3 remains in its hands as a gold hoard . It cannot form any element of its constant capital, and department II already had sufficient money capital to purchase labour-power; furthermore, with the exception of the wear and tear element, this additional 3g has no function to perform within IIc. On the other hand, however, even with the exception of the wear and tear element, the entire commodity product IIc has to be exchanged for means of production I(v + s)—hence this money must be completely transferred from IIc to Is, whether this exists in necessary means of subsistence or in luxury items, and the corresponding commodity value must be transferred, conversely, from Is to IIc. The result is that a part of the surplus-value is stored away as a money hoard. The second year. In the second year's reproduction, if the same proportion of the gold annually produced continues to be used as money, 2 will once again flow back to Ig, and 3 will be replaced in kind, i.e. will be set free again in department II as a hoard, etc. The general point about variable capital. As far as the variable capital is concerned, we can say generally that capitalist Ig, just like any other, always has to advance this capital in money for the purchase of labour-power. As far as this v is concerned, it is not he but his workers who have to buy from department II; thus the case in which he appears as buyer and casts gold into department II can never arise unless the latter takes the initiative. But in so far as department II buys material from him, and needs to convert its constant capital IIc into gold material, part of Ig's v flows back to him from department II in the same way as to other capitalists in department I; to the extent that this is not the case, he directly replaces his v in gold from his own product. But in the proportion to which the v advanced as money does not flow back to him from department II, a part of it that has already been advanced to the circulation sphere is transformed into a hoard (this part is the money that flowed to him from department I and did not return there), and a part of his surplus-value is therefore not spent on means of consumption. As new gold mines are constantly opened, or old ones reopened, so a definite proportion of the money that Ig has to lay out on v is always part of the quantity of money in existence before the new gold production; it was cast into department II by way of its workers, and, to the extent that it does not return to Ig from department II, it forms an element of hoard formation there. The surplus-value of the gold producers. As far as (Ig)s is concerned, Ig can always appear here as a buyer; it casts its s into the circulation sphere as gold, and correspondingly withdraws means of consumption IIc; here the gold is partly used as material, and hence functions as a real element of the constant component c of department II's productive capital; and in as much as this is not the case, it again becomes an element of hoard formation as a part of (Ig)s that persists in money. The necessity of hoard formation in simple reproduction. It is clear—even leaving aside the Ic, which will be considered later (see the editorial note that no investigation of the exchange of the newly produced gold within the constant capital of department I is to be found in the manuscript)—that even simple reproduction, which excludes accumulation in the strict sense of the term, i.e. reproduction on an expanded scale, necessarily involves the storage of money, or hoard formation . And since this is repeated anew each year, it explains the assumption from which we proceeded in considering capitalist production, namely that at the beginning of the reproduction process, the capitalists in departments I and II must each already possess a quantity of the monetary medium which corresponds to the amount of commodity exchange. There is even storage of this kind after deduction of the gold lost by the abrasion of the money in circulation. The maturity of capitalist production. It is self-evident that the greater the maturity of capitalist production, the greater is the quantity of money accumulated on all sides, and the smaller therefore the proportion that the new gold production of each year adds to this quantity, even though this addition may be quite significant in absolute terms. The Tooke objection. We come back once again, then, in general terms, to the objection made against Tooke (see Volume 2, p. 404): how is it possible for each capitalist to withdraw a surplus-value from the annual product in money, i.e. to withdraw more money from the circulation sphere than he cast into it, since in the final analysis the capitalist class itself must be seen as the origin of all money in circulation? Marx's summary answer: 1. The only assumption required here is that there should always be sufficient money to convert the various elements of the commodity mass annually reproduced. This is in no way affected by the fact that a part of the commodity value consists of surplus-value. If the whole of production belonged to the workers themselves, then their surplus labour would be surplus labour for themselves, not for the capitalists, but the mass of commodity value in circulation would be the same and would require, given that other circumstances also remained the same, the same amount of money for its circulation. The question in both cases is simply: where does the money come from to convert this total commodity value? It is not: where does the money come from to realize the surplus-value? Moreover, each individual commodity consists of c + v + s, and so a certain sum of money is necessary for the circulation of the capital c + v, and another sum of money is needed for the circulation of the capitalists' revenue, the surplus-value s. Just as for the individual capitalist, so for the class as a whole, the money in which it advances capital is different from the money in which it spends revenue. Where does this latter money come from? Simply from the fact that of the mass of money that exists in the hands of the capitalist class, which is by and large the total quantity of money that exists in society, one part circulates the capitalists' revenue. Each capitalist who sets up a new business fishes back the money that he spends on means of consumption for his own maintenance, once the business is under way, in the shape of money which serves to realize his surplus-value. The two sources of the difficulty: - Firstly , if we consider simply the circulation sphere and the turnover of capital, i.e. consider the capitalist simply as the personification of capital—not also as capitalist consumer and man of the world—then although we certainly see him constantly casting surplus-value into circulation as a component of his commodity capital, we never see money in his hands as a form of revenue, we never see him casting money into circulation for the consumption of his surplus-value. - Secondly , if the capitalist class casts a certain sum of money into circulation in the shape of revenue, it appears as if it paid an equivalent for this part of the total annual product, and that this has thereby ceased to represent surplus-value. But the surplus product in which the surplus-value is represented costs the capitalist class nothing. As a class, it possesses it and enjoys it free of charge, and the monetary circulation cannot alter this in any way. The change that this brings about simply consists in the fact that each capitalist, instead of consuming his own surplus product in kind, for which in most cases it would not be suitable, withdraws commodities of all kinds from the total stock to the amount of the surplus-value that he appropriated. The circulation mechanism, however, has shown that if the capitalist class casts money into circulation to be spent as revenue, it withdraws this same money again from circulation, and so the same process can always begin anew. If the capitalist not only withdraws surplus-value from the commodity market in the form of commodities for his consumption fund, but at the same time the money with which he buys these commodities flows back to him, he has evidently withdrawn the commodities from circulation without an equivalent. They cost him nothing, even though he pays for them with money. If I buy commodities for £1 sterling, and the seller of these commodities gives me back my £1 in exchange for a surplus product that cost me nothing, then I have obviously received the commodities for nothing. The constant repetition of this operation in no way alters the fact that I constantly withdraw commodities and constantly remain in possession of the £1, even though I part with it temporarily. The capitalist constantly receives this money back as the realization of surplus-value that cost him nothing. The Adam Smith-Tooke fallacy. We saw that for Adam Smith the entire value of the social product resolved itself into revenue, into v + s, and that the value of the constant capital was therefore taken as zero. It necessarily follows from this that the money required for the circulation of the annual revenue would also be sufficient for the circulation of the entire annual product; and that in our case, therefore, the money needed for the circulation of means of consumption to the value of 3,000 would be sufficient for the circulation of a total annual product to the value of 9,000. This was in fact Adam Smith's opinion, and it is repeated by Thomas Tooke. This false conception of the ratio between the quantity of money needed to realize revenue and the quantity of money that circulates the total social product is a necessary result of the uncomprehending, thoughtless manner in which they view the reproduction and annual replacement of the different material and value elements of the total annual product. It is therefore already refuted. Smith's passage. Smith says, in Book Two, Chapter II: \"The circulation of every country may be considered as divided into two different branches: the circulation of the dealers with one another, and the circulation between the dealers and the consumers... The value of the goods circulated between the different dealers never can exceed the value of those circulated between the dealers and the consumers; whatever is bought by the dealers, being ultimately destined to be sold to the consumers. The circulation between the dealers, as it is carried on by wholesale, requires generally a pretty large sum for every particular transaction. That between the dealers and the consumers, on the contrary, as it is generally carried on by retail, frequently requires but very small ones, a shilling, or even a halfpenny, being often sufficient. But small sums circulate much faster than large ones... Though the annual purchases of all the consumers, therefore, are at least\"—this \"at least\" is a good one!—\"equal in value to those of all the dealers, they can generally be transacted with a much smaller quantity of money,\" etc. (p. 421). Tooke's endorsement. On this passage, Thomas Tooke remarks ( An Inquiry into the Currency Principle , London, 1844, pp. 34–6 passim): \"There can be no doubt that the distinction here made is substantially correct... the interchange between dealers and consumers including the payment of wages, which constitute the principal means of the consumers... All the transactions between dealers and dealers, by which are to be understood all sales from the producer or importer, through all the stages of intermediate processes of manufacture or otherwise to the retail dealer or the exporting merchant, are resolvable into movements or transfers of capital. Now transfers of capital do not necessarily suppose nor do actually as a matter of fact entail, in the great majority of transactions, a passing of money, that is, bank-notes or coin—I mean bodily, and not by fiction—at the time of transfer... The total amount of the transactions between dealers and dealers must, in the last resort, be determined and limited by the amount of those between dealers and consumers.\" Marx's comment: If we took this last sentence by itself, we might believe Tooke was simply claiming that there was a certain ratio between the \"transactions between dealers\" and \"those between dealers and consumers,\" in other words between the value of the total annual revenue and the value of the capital with which it is produced. But this is not the case. He expressly endorses the Smithian conception. A special criticism of his circulation theory would thus be superfluous. The four supplementary points: 2. Every industrial capital, at its outset, casts money into circulation all at once for the entire fixed component of its capital , and it recovers this only gradually over a series of years by the sale of its annual product. It therefore casts more money into the circulation sphere at first than it withdraws from this. This is repeated each time that the entire capital is renewed in kind; it is repeated each year for a certain number of businesses, those whose fixed capital has to be renewed in kind; it is repeated partially with each repair, with each fractional renewal of the fixed capital. If at one point more money is withdrawn from circulation than is cast in, the reverse is the case at another point. In all branches of industry whose production periods extend over a relatively long time, money is constantly cast into circulation by the capitalist producers during this period, partly in payment for the labour-power applied, partly for purchasing the means of production that are to be used. Means of production are therefore withdrawn from the commodity market directly, and means of consumption in part indirectly, by the workers when they spend their wages, and in part also directly by the capitalists themselves, who in no way suspend their consumption. During this period, the money that the capitalists cast into circulation serves to realize commodity value, including the surplus-value contained in it. This factor becomes very important in developed capitalist production, in connection with long-drawn-out enterprises undertaken by joint-stock companies, etc. such as the building of railways, canals, docks, large municipal buildings, the construction of iron ships, the draining of land on a large scale, etc. While the other capitalists, apart from their outlay on fixed capital, withdraw more money from the circulation sphere than they cast into it for the purchase of labour-power and the circulating elements, the gold- and silver-producing capitalists cast only money into circulation (leaving aside the portion of precious metal serving as raw material), while they withdraw only commodities from it. Their constant capital (with the exception of the wear and tear component), the greater part of their variable capital, and their entire surplus-value (with the exception of a certain hoard that is accumulated in their own hands) is cast into the circulation sphere as money. It is certainly true that all kinds of things circulate as commodities that were not produced within the year : plots of land, houses, etc., as well as products whose production period extends over longer than a year, such as cattle, wood, wine, etc. It is important to establish, for these and other phenomena, that besides the sum of money required for direct circulation, there is always a certain quantity in a latent and non-functioning state, which can come out and function on a given impulse. The value of these products also often circulates bit by bit and gradually: for example, the value of houses circulates in rent over a series of years. On the other hand, not all the motions of the reproduction process are mediated by money circulation. The entire production process falls outside it, once its elements have been procured. So do all products that the producer directly consumes himself—whether individually or productively—including the provisions paid in kind to agricultural workers. Thus the quantity of money that circulates the annual product is present in society and has been accumulated bit by bit. It does not form part of the value product of the present year, with the possible exception of the gold that replaces worn-out coins. The methodological significance of the assumption of metallic circulation. In this presentation we have presupposed the exclusive circulation of precious metals as money, and, together with this, the simplest form of cash purchases and sales; though even on the basis of simple metallic circulation money can function also as a means of payment, and actually has functioned in this way historically, and a system of credit and certain aspects of the credit mechanism have developed on this basis. This assumption was made not simply for methodological reasons, although the importance of these is shown by the simple fact that both Tooke and his school, and their opponents, were constantly forced in their controversies over the circulation of bank notes to come back again to the hypothesis of pure metallic circulation. They were forced to do this post festum , but then they did so very superficially, and necessarily so, since this starting-point fulfilled only an incidental function in their analysis. The two additional considerations: (a) On the assumption of developed capitalist production, i.e. the domination of the system of wage-labour, money capital evidently plays a major role, in as much as it is the form in which variable capital is advanced . To the degree that the wage system develops, all products are transformed into commodities, and all—with a few important exceptions—must therefore jointly undergo the transformation into money as a phase in their development. The quantity of money in circulation must be sufficient for the realization of the commodities, and the greater part of this is supplied in the form of wages, of money that is advanced by industrial capitalists in payment of labour-power and mainly functions in the hands of the workers only as a means of circulation (means of purchase). This is in complete contrast to natural economy, such as predominates on the basis of every form of personal bondage (including serfdom), and still more so on the basis of more or less primitive communities. In the slave system, the money capital laid out on the purchase of labour-power plays the role of fixed capital in the money form, and is only gradually replaced as the active life of the slave comes to an end. This is why in Athens the profit that a slave-owner drew, either directly from the industrial use of his slave or indirectly by renting the slave to other industrial users (e.g. for work in the mines), was simply considered as interest (together with amortization). Mere domestic slaves are not considered here; they correspond to our servant class. But even the slave system retains an element of natural economy. The slave market itself constantly receives supplies of the commodity labour-power from war, piracy, etc., and this pillage is not mediated by a process of circulation, but is rather the appropriation in kind of other people's labour-power by direct physical compulsion. Even in the USA, after the border area between the wage-labour states of the North and the slave states of the South had been transformed into a slave-breeding zone for the South, and where the slave thrown onto the market had therefore himself become an element of the annual reproduction, this was for a long while insufficient, and the African slave trade had to be carried on for as long as possible in order to fill the requirements of the market. (b) The fluxes and refluxes of money which take place on the basis of capitalist production, for the reconversion of the annual product, and which have grown up spontaneously; the advances of fixed capital at a single stroke, to its entire value, and the progressive withdrawal of this value from circulation by a process that extends over a period of many years, i.e. its gradual reconstitution in the money form by annual hoard formation, a hoard formation that is completely different in nature from the hoard formation based on the new gold production of each year that accompanies it; the different lengths of time for which money has to be advanced, which vary according to the length of the production periods of the commodities, and for which there has to be in each case a prior formation of a hoard before the money can be withdrawn from circulation by the sale of the commodity involved; the varying times of advance that arise simply from differences in the distance of the point of production from the market outlet; as well as the variation in the size and period of the reflux according to the condition or the relative size of the production stocks in different businesses and for the different individual capitalists in the same line of business, i.e. the dates of purchase of the elements of constant capital, and all this during the year's reproduction—all these different aspects of the spontaneous movement had only to be noted and brought to light by experience, in order to give rise both to a methodical use of the mechanical aids of the credit system and to the actual fishing out of available loan capital . On top of all this, there is still the distinction between businesses whose production proceeds continuously on the same scale, as long as conditions remain otherwise the same, and those that employ labour-power in different degrees at different periods of the year, such as agriculture. M. Destutt de Tracy: The Apogee of Bourgeois Cretinism Section 13 is the culminating polemic of the chapter. Marx selects Destutt de Tracy—\"the great logician,\" whom even Ricardo took seriously, calling him \"a very distinguished writer\" ( Principles , p. 287)—as an example of the \"confused and at the same time boastful incomprehension shown by the political economists in dealing with the social reproduction.\" Destutt's theory of enrichment. Destutt presents the following explanation of the overall process of social reproduction and circulation: \"I shall be asked how it is that these industrial entrepreneurs make such great profits, and from whom they can draw them. My reply is that they do so because they sell everything they produce dearer than it cost them to produce it; they sell: (1) to one another for the whole of that part of their consumption which is destined to satisfy their own needs, and which they pay for with a part of their profits; (2) to the wage-labourers, both those that they pay themselves and those that the idle capitalists pay; in this way they receive back from the wage-labourers their entire wages, with the exception of their small savings; (3) to the idle capitalists, who pay them with the part of their revenue that they have not already handed over to the workers directly employed by them; so that the entire rent that they pay these idle capitalists each year flows back to them in one of these ways or another\" (Destutt de Tracy, Traité de la volonté et de ses effets , Paris, 1826, p. 239). Marx's devastating critique: Source 1: The capitalists cheat each other. The capitalists get rich firstly by taking advantage of each other in exchanging the part of the surplus-value that they devote to their private consumption or consume as revenue. If this part of their surplus-value or profits is £400, then this £400 becomes £500 if each party to the £400 sells his share to another party 25 per cent too dear. Since all of them do the same thing, the outcome is the same as if they had sold to each other at the right price. It is simply that they need a quantity of money of £500 to circulate a commodity value of £400, and this would seem rather a method of impoverishing than enriching them, in as much as they would have to hold a large part of their total wealth unproductively in the useless form of means of circulation. The whole thing comes down to the fact that the capitalist class, despite the all-round nominal price increase of their commodities, have to distribute among themselves, for their private consumption, a commodity stock of only £400, but that they do each other the favour of circulating this £400 in commodity value with a quantity of money that is required for £500 of commodity value. This comes to the same thing as the secret of 'Inspector Brasig,' that great poverty derives from great pauvreté . (The reference is to a comic character in stories by Fritz Reuter.) Source 2: The reflux of wages. The same capitalists also sell \"to the wage-labourers... in this way we receive back from the wage-labourers their entire wages, with the exception of their small savings.\" The reflux of money capital to the industrial capitalists, capital which was advanced in this form by the capitalists as wages to the workers, constitutes, for M. Destutt, the second source of enrichment of these capitalists. So if the capitalist class pays £100 to the workers in wages, say, and these workers can therefore buy from the capitalist class commodities to the same value of £100, so that the sum of £100 that the capitalists advanced as buyers of labour-power flows back to them on the sale of commodities of £100 to the workers, the capitalists are supposed to enrich themselves by this. It would appear, from the standpoint of ordinary common sense, that by means of this procedure the capitalists would simply find themselves once more in possession of the £100 that they previously possessed. At the beginning, they had £100 in money, and they used this to buy £100 worth of labour-power. For this £100 in money, the labour that is bought produces commodities of a value that, as far as we know up to now, is £100. By selling this £100 of commodities to the workers, the capitalists receive back £100 in money. The capitalists therefore again have £100 in money, and the workers have £100 in commodities that they themselves produced. How the capitalists are supposed to get rich in this way is anybody's guess. If the £100 in money did not flow back to them, then they would first have paid the workers £100 in money for their labour, and would secondly have had to give them the product of this labour, £100 worth of means of consumption, for nothing. The reflux might explain why the capitalists do not become any the poorer by this operation, but in no way how they become richer. The further question. A further question, moreover, is how the capitalists come to possess this £100 in money, and why the workers, instead of producing commodities on their own account, are forced to exchange their labour-power for this £100. But this is something that a thinker of Destutt's calibre takes as self-explanatory. The real basis of enrichment is not the reflux but a deduction from wages. Destutt would not be quite satisfied with this solution. After all, he did not actually tell us that one can get rich by spending a sum of £100 and then receiving back an equal sum of £100. What he told us is that the capitalists get rich \"because they sell everything they produce dearer than it cost them to buy it.\" So the capitalists must additionally get rich in their transactions with the workers, by selling to them too dear. Excellent! \"They pay wages... and all this flows back to them by the spending of all these people, who pay dearer for them [the products] than they cost them [the capitalists] in wages\" (p. 240). Marx asks: Do the capitalists thus pay the workers £100 in wages, and then sell the workers their own product for £120, so that both the £100 has flowed back to them and a further £20 has been obtained in addition? This is impossible. The workers can pay only with the money that they receive in the form of wages. If they receive £100 in wages from the capitalists, they can buy only for £100, and not for £120. This would not work at all. But there is still another way. The workers buy commodities from the capitalists for £100, but receive in actual fact only commodities to the value of £80. They have therefore been cheated out of £20. And the capitalist has certainly got £20 richer, because he paid for labour-power 20 per cent below its value, or indirectly made a deduction of 20 per cent from the nominal wage. The capitalist class would achieve the same end if they paid the workers only £80 in wages from the beginning, and subsequently supplied them with £80 in commodity value for this £80 in money. This would appear the normal way—taking the class as a whole—since according to M. Destutt himself the working class must receive \"sufficient wages\" (p. 219), i.e. their wages must at least be sufficient to maintain their existence and working ability, \"to procure them the barest subsistence\" (p. 180). If the workers do not receive this sufficient wage, then this spells \"the death of industry\" (p. 208), as Destutt himself says, and is therefore, it would appear, not a means of enrichment for the capitalists. But whatever may be the wage that the capitalist class pays the working class, this has a definite value, e.g. £80. If the capitalist class pays £80 to the workers, it has to supply them with £80 in commodity value, and the reflux of the £80 does not enrich them. The reduction to absurdity. In other words, the entire fund from which the capitalist class draws its profit is formed by a deduction from the normal wage, by payment of labour-power below its value , i.e. below the value of the means of subsistence that are needed for the normal reproduction of the wage-labourers. If the normal wage is paid, therefore, and this according to Destutt is what should happen, then there does not exist any fund for profit, neither for the industrial capitalists nor for the idle capitalists. M. Destutt would thus have to reduce the entire secret of how the capitalist gets rich to this: deduction from wages. The other funds of surplus-value which he refers to under headings (1) and (3) would then not exist. In all countries therefore where the money wage of the workers is reduced to the value of the means of consumption necessary for their subsistence as a class, there does not exist either a consumption fund or an accumulation fund for the capitalists, and so no fund for the existence of the capitalist class, and no capitalist class at all. And this would certainly be the case, according to Destutt, in all rich and developed, long-civilized countries, for here, \"in our old-established societies, the fund from which wages are met... is almost a constant quantity\" (p. 202). Source 3: The idle capitalists. Finally, the industrial capitalist class sells \"to the idle capitalists, who pay them with the part of their revenue that they have not already handed over to the workers directly employed by them, so that the entire rent that they pay these idle capitalists each year flows back to them again in one of these ways or another.\" We have already seen that the industrial capitalists pay \"with a part of their profits\" for \"the whole of that part of their consumption which is destined to satisfy their own needs.\" Let us take it that their profits are £200. They consume £100 for their individual consumption. But the other half, a further £100, does not belong to them, but to the idle capitalists, i.e. the recipients of ground-rent and the capitalists who lend money at interest. They must therefore pay these people £100. Let us say that out of this money, the latter need £80 for their own consumption and £20 for the hire of servants, etc. They therefore buy means of consumption for £80 from the industrial capitalists. The latter thereby receive back, by parting with £80 worth of products, £80 in money, or four-fifths of the £100 that they paid to the idle capitalists under the headings of rent, interest, etc. Moreover, the class of servants has received £20 from its masters. It too uses this to buy means of consumption from the industrial capitalists, to the tune of £20. The latter thereby receive back £20 in money, while they have parted with £20 in products, and this is the last fifth of the £100 that they paid to the idle capitalists as rent, interest, etc. At the close of the transaction, the industrial capitalists have received back the £100 in money that they remitted to the idle capitalists in payment of rent, interest, etc., while half of their surplus product, or £100, has been transferred from their hands into the consumption fund of the idle capitalists. The absurdity of this as enrichment. It is evidently entirely superfluous for the question we are dealing with here to bring in the division of the £100 between the idle capitalists and their immediate wage-labourers. The situation is quite simple: their rent and interest, in short the share of the surplus-value of £200 that accrues to them, is paid them by the industrial capitalists in money, £100. With this £100, they buy means of consumption directly or indirectly from the industrial capitalists. They therefore pay back to them £100 in money and take means of consumption in exchange for £100. This is how the reflux of the £100 paid by the industrial capitalists to the idle capitalists has taken place. Is this reflux of money, as Destutt imagines, a means of enrichment for the industrial capitalists? Before the transaction, they had a sum of values of £200; £100 in money and £100 in means of consumption. After the transaction, they have only half of the original sum of values. They again have £100 in money, but they have lost the £100 in means of consumption that has been transferred to the hands of the idle capitalists. They are therefore £100 poorer instead of £100 richer. If, instead of this detour of first paying £100 in money and then receiving this £100 back again in payment for £100 worth of means of consumption, they had directly paid rent, interest, etc. in the natural form of their product, then they would not receive any money back from the circulation sphere, as they would not have cast £100 of money into it. Payment in kind would have simply presented the matter in such a way that half the value of the surplus product of £200 was retained by the industrial capitalists themselves and the other half given away to the idle capitalists without an equivalent. Even Destutt would not have tried to present this as a means of enrichment. The borrowed land and capital. The land and the capital that the industrial capitalists borrow from the idle capitalists, and for which they have to pay them a part of the surplus-value in the form of rent, interest, etc., is of course profitable for them, for it is one of the conditions of production, both of the product in general and of the part of the product that forms surplus product or in which surplus-value is represented. But this profit derives from the use of the borrowed land and capital, and not from the price that is paid for this. This price is rather a deduction from it. It would otherwise be necessary to maintain that the industrial capitalists would become not richer, but poorer, if they could keep the other half of the surplus-value for themselves, instead of giving it away. But this is the confusion that we are led into if phenomena of circulation, such as the reflux of money, are lumped together with the distribution of the product that is simply mediated by these phenomena of circulation. Destutt's shrewdness. Despite all this, Destutt is still shrewd enough to note: \"Where do the revenues of these idle people come from? Do they not come from the rents that are paid to them out of the profit of those who make the idlers' capital work, i.e. those who use the funds of the former to pay a labour that produces more than it costs—in short, the industrialists? It is to these that one must always go, therefore, to find the source of all wealth. It is these who actually feed the wage-labourers employed by the others.\" The payment of these rents, etc. is now a deduction from the profit of the industrialists. Previously it was a means for them to get rich. But our Destutt has one consolation left. These brave industrialists handle the idle capitalists just as they treat one another and the workers. They sell them all commodities too dear, e.g. 20 per cent too dear. The final reduction to absurdity. Only two things are possible now. Either the idle capitalists have, besides the £100 that they annually receive from the industrialists, further monetary resources, or they do not. In the first case , the industrialists sell them commodities and values of £100 at a price, say, of £120. They receive back for the sale of their commodities not only the £100 that they paid the idlers, but also a further £20 that actually forms new value for them. How does the account now stand? They have given £100 worth of commodities away for nothing, for the £100 in money with which part of it was paid for was their own money. A loss of £100. But they have received, besides this, £20 as an addition to the price above its value. This profit of £20, together with the loss of £100, makes a loss of £80, which is still a loss and can never be a profit. The cheating practised towards the idle capitalists has reduced the industrialists' loss, but it has in no way transformed their loss into a means of enrichment. This method will not work in the long run, however, as the idle capitalists cannot pay out £120 each year if they receive only £100 in money. The other method , therefore, is that the industrialists sell commodities worth only £80 for the £100 in money that the idle capitalists pay them. In this case, as before, they give £80 away for nothing, in the form of rents, interest, etc. By way of this cheating, they have reduced the tribute to the idle capitalists, but it still exists, and the idle capitalists are in a position—according to the same theory, in which prices depend on the good will of the sellers—to obtain £120 in future as rent, interest, etc. for their land and capital, instead of £100 as previously. The final verdict. This striking development is completely worthy of the profound thinker who on the one hand copies from Adam Smith the phrase that \"labour is the source of all wealth,\" that the industrial capitalists \"employ their capital in order to pay labour that reproduces it with a profit\" (p. 246), and on the other hand concludes that these industrial capitalists \"feed all the other people, alone increase the public wealth and create all our means of enjoyment\" (p. 242), that it is not the capitalists who are fed by the workers, but the workers by the capitalists , and moreover for the brilliant reason that the money with which the workers are paid does not remain in their hands, but always returns to the capitalists in payment for the goods that the workers produced. \"They merely receive with one hand and give back with the other. Their consumption must therefore be seen as produced by those who pay them\" (p. 253). Destutt's self-congratulation. After this exhaustive presentation of social reproduction and consumption, mediated as it is by money circulation, Destutt continues: \"It is this that perfects the perpetuum mobile of wealth, a movement that, although badly understood [ mal connu —indeed!], has rightly been called circulation; for it is in fact a circuit and always comes back to its point of departure. This is the point at which production is completed\" (pp. 239, 240). Destutt, that \"very distinguished writer,\" \"member of the Institut de France and the Philosophical Society of Philadelphia,\" and certainly a genuine luminary among vulgar economists, finally begs his readers to marvel at the wondrous clarity with which he depicts the course of the social process, the flood of light that he has shone on the object, and is even condescending enough to inform the reader where all this light emanates from. This must be read in the original: \"On remarquera, j'espère, combien cette manière de considérer la consommation de nos richesses est concordante avec tout ce que nous avons dit à propos de leur production et de leur distribution, et en même temps quelle clarté elle répand sur toute la marche de la société. D'où viennent cet accord et cette lucidité? De ce que nous avons rencontré la vérité. Cela rappelle l'effet de ces miroirs où les objets se peignent nettement et dans leurs justes proportions, quand on est placé dans leur vrai point-de-vue, et où tout paraît confus et désuni, quand on en est trop près ou trop loin\" (pp. 242–3). Marx's closing line: \"Voilà le crétinisme bourgeois dans toute sa béatitude!\" (\"Here you have bourgeois cretinism in its ultimate state of bliss!\") 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental laws revealed. The chapter establishes several interlocking principles of social reproduction under capitalism: The law of simple reproduction: I(v + s) = IIc. The value of the means of production that department I must exchange for means of consumption must equal the value of the constant capital consumed in department II. This is not an equilibrium condition imposed from outside but a material necessity arising from the use-value form of the products. The value product of department I's annual labour equals the constant capital value of department II that must be replaced. The material composition of the social product must permit its own reproduction. Unlike the individual capital, whose product can take any use-form, the total social product must contain, in their natural form, all the material elements of reproduction: means of production to replace both departments' constant capital, and means of consumption to realize the total social v + s. The value components c, v, and s must be embodied in appropriate use-values. Two-thirds of the social working day produces constant capital, not revenue. The value product of the current year's labour (v + s) equals the value of the means of consumption produced, but two-thirds of the social working day is actually spent producing means of production that cannot resolve into wages or surplus-value. The value that these two-thirds produce is new value destined to function as constant capital in the future. Money is an immanent moment of reproduction, not a mere technical lubricant. The analysis of the £500, the £1,500 that circulates £5,000 in commodities, the hoard formation for fixed capital replacement, and the reproduction of the gold material all demonstrate that money circulation is structurally embedded in the reproduction process. The capitalist class must itself advance the money needed to realize its own surplus-value—a proposition that is \"not only far from paradoxical, it is in fact a necessary condition of the overall mechanism.\" The possibility of crisis is inherent in the very proportionalities of simple reproduction. The fixed capital replacement analysis shows that even under the assumption of ideal normal production, a disproportion between the defunct fixed capital and the circulating constant capital of department II can and must arise from the mere maintenance of fixed capital. This produces either an unsaleable surplus of department I's means of production or an unsaleable surplus of department II's means of consumption. Foreign trade only \"shifts the contradictions to a broader sphere.\" Capital and revenue are distinct categories that cannot be collapsed. The variable capital functions as capital in the hands of the capitalist and as revenue in the hands of the worker, but this is not because the same value functions twice—it is because the same money serves different purposes as it passes from hand to hand. The \"what is capital for one is revenue for another\" formula is partially correct but becomes completely false as a general rule. The class structure is reproduced through the reproduction process. The workers must sell their labour-power anew each period; the capitalist class owns both the means of production and the money; the entire mechanism perpetuates the wage-labour/capital relation. \"In this way, the relationship between wage-labourers and capitalists is also reproduced.\" Broader theoretical implications. The chapter's analysis refutes the Smithian dogma that the social product resolves into revenue (v + s). It demonstrates that the \"trinity formula\" (land–rent, capital–profit, labour–wages) rests on a fundamental misrecognition of the relation between value and its material bearers. It provides the analytical foundation for the theory of the falling rate of profit (Volume 3) by establishing the material proportions between the two departments. It shows why the capitalist mode of production is inherently crisis-prone, not because of external shocks or failures of demand, but because of the anarchic character of its proportionality—the blind, unplanned, spontaneous adjustment of production to social needs that constantly overshoots or undershoots. The critique of political economy. The chapter's treatment of Adam Smith, Storch, Ramsay, Tooke, and especially Destutt de Tracy is not merely polemical. It demonstrates that the surface phenomena of circulation—the reflux of money, the apparent enrichment through exchange, the payment of rent and interest—generate systematic illusions that even serious thinkers cannot escape. The \"bourgeois cretinism\" of Destutt is the apogee of a general tendency: to mistake the forms of appearance of capitalist relations for their substance, to see the worker as being fed by the capitalist because the money he receives returns to the capitalist, to imagine that profit arises from selling commodities above their value. Marx's analytical labor—the meticulous tracing of every £ in circulation, every value component, every material exchange—is precisely designed to dispel these illusions. The non-capitalist counterfactual. Twice in the chapter, Marx notes what the process would look like if production were social rather than capitalist: the products of department I \"would be no less constantly redistributed among the branches of production in this department as means of production, according to the needs of reproduction.\" Perpetual relative over-production—a stock of fixed capital and raw materials exceeding immediate annual need—\"is equivalent to control by the society over the objective means of its own reproduction.\" Within capitalist society, however, \"it is an anarchic element.\" This is a crucial indication of Marx's own conception of a rationally organized, democratically planned economy: not a suppression of the material proportionalities that social reproduction requires, but their conscious, planned realization. Practical and philosophical implications. The chapter demonstrates that the capitalist economy is a totality whose parts are interdependent in ways that no individual capitalist can control or even perceive. The proportionality between departments I and II, between fixed and circulating capital, between necessary and luxury consumption, is not consciously planned but must be achieved through the blind mechanism of market exchange—and is repeatedly not achieved, resulting in crises. The analysis of the IIb subdivision shows that a growing luxury sector, while profitable for individual capitalists, creates a structural vulnerability: the reproduction of luxury workers depends on the \"prodigality\" of the capitalist class, so any crisis that reduces luxury consumption throws them onto the streets. The analysis of fixed capital shows that even the simple maintenance of existing capacity requires disproportional production and the formation of money hoards—money that is withdrawn from circulation and held idle for years. The analysis of gold production shows that simple reproduction necessarily involves hoard formation, and therefore that the money supply must grow independently of the value product of the current year. The methodological lesson. Perhaps the deepest lesson of the chapter is methodological: at the level of the total social capital, the analysis must proceed simultaneously on two planes—the plane of value and the plane of material use-values —and must trace the dialectical interplay between them. The value categories (c, v, s) determine the possible distributions; the material forms (means of production, means of consumption) determine what exchanges are possible. Neither alone is sufficient. The \"riddle\" of the annual social product—how the value product of the entire working day can resolve into v + s while two-thirds of that working day is spent producing constant capital—is solved only by recognizing that the exchange between departments I and II is an exchange of two-thirds of this year's working day for two-thirds of a working day spent prior to this year . The value that appears as constant capital in department II's product was produced by labour performed in previous years, and the value that department I produces this year will function as constant capital next year. Value and labour-time are distributed across time, and only a perspective that grasps the reproduction process as a continuous whole can understand its logic. The chapter thus stands as one of the most technically demanding and theoretically fertile texts in the entire Marxist corpus. It is the bridge between the analysis of individual capital and the analysis of the total capitalist system, between value theory and crisis theory, between the critique of political economy and the positive elaboration of a social science of reproduction. Its conclusions are as relevant today as they were when Marx wrote them: that crises are not caused by a lack of \"effective demand\" but by the structural disproportionalities inherent in an anarchic system of production for profit; that the apparent enrichment of the capitalist class is not a result of exchange but of the appropriation of unpaid labour; and that the reproduction of the system is inseparable from the reproduction of the class relations on which it rests. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 19 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 2 • Chapter 21"}, {"volume": "Volume 2", "folder": "Chapter 21", "title": "Chapter 21", "subtitle": "Accumulation and Reproduction on an Expanded Scale", "path": "Volume 2/Chapter 21/Chapter 21 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 15464, "read_time": "77 min read", "tags": ["Social Reproduction Schemes (Dept I & II)", "Capital Accumulation & Crises"], "keywords": ["Accumulation and Reproduction on an Expanded Scale", "Conversion of Surplus-Value into Additional Constant and Variable Capital", "Disproportionalities between Department I and Department II", "Accumulation Schemes and Crises of Disproportionality", "Requirements of Organic Expansion Across Sectors"], "text": "Chapter 21 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 15,424 words ⏱️ ~77 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS: Chapter 21 of Capital Volume 2 performs the decisive intellectual move in Marx's theory of social reproduction: it lifts the analysis from the closed, static equilibrium of \"simple reproduction\" (where the annual product is consumed entirely and capital is merely replaced on the same scale) up to the dynamic, disequilibrating reality of \"reproduction on an expanded scale\" — the reproduction of the total social capital as an accumulating system. The chapter is drawn, as the editorial note at the top of page 1 explicitly tells us, \"From here to the end, Manuscript VIII,\" which is Marx's own 1870s manuscript rather than Engels' synthetic reconstruction. This matters enormously: the chapter displays Marx's workshop as he struggles with a problem he never fully resolved — how the smooth ballet of numerical exchange rates between the two great departments of production (I. Means of Production, II. Means of Consumption) can be reconciled with the violent, crisis-prone, hoard-driven reality of capitalist accumulation. The central problem the chapter poses is deceptively simple. In Volume 1 Marx showed how the individual capitalist accumulates: he realizes surplus value in money, then converts that money into additional elements of productive capital (means of production and labour-power), and next year produces on a larger scale. But a sum of individual capitals is not automatically a coherent social whole. When every capitalist tries to hoard surplus value simultaneously — selling commodities to acquire money but deferring the purchase of new commodities — the circulation process appears to break down. Where do the buyers come from if everyone wants to sell? Marx's answer contains the chapter's first major thesis: expanded reproduction does not require any addition to social wealth in the form of money. Money is withdrawn from circulation and stored as a hoard, but it does not cease to exist; it simply changes function — from means of circulation to hoard, to potential (or \"virtual\") money capital. And crucially, the surplus product whose money-form is being hoarded already exists as a physical stock of means of production. What is required for accumulation is not the creation of new wealth but the rearrangement of the elements of the annual product. This is Marx's second great thesis: \"the material substratum for expanded reproduction is produced in the course of simple reproduction\" (p. 573). Expanded reproduction is not a matter of the absolute size of the product but of the qualitative character of its elements, of \"a different arrangement or a different determination of the functions of the various elements of the given product\" (p. 575). The third foundational thesis concerns Department II. Once Department I (the producers of means of production) retains a portion of its surplus product as additional constant capital instead of exchanging it against Department II's means of consumption, a portion of Department II's constant capital (IIc) becomes unsaleable. The condition for expanded reproduction thus cannot be the simple-reproduction equality I(v+s) = IIc. Instead Marx derives the fundamental unequal-exchange condition: I(v+s) > IIc. Department I must produce more means of production than Department II can absorb in the mere replacement of its constant capital, because Department I is now also supplying the material elements for Department II's own accumulation, while Department II in turn must supply Department I's additional workers with their means of subsistence. This is the dialectic of mutual provisioning that the chapter formalises in the schemas of Section III. The chapter's intellectual objective is thus threefold. First, to demonstrate, in the money-form, how the hoards that appear in individual capitalist accounting also appear (and are reconciled) at the level of annual social reproduction. Second, to specify the value and material conditions under which the annual product can be converted back into productive capital on a larger scale — producing the famous reproduction schemas (Schema a: I. 4,000c+1,000v+1,000s, II. 1,500c+376v+376s; and the second example: I. 5,000c+1,000v+1,000s, II. 1,430c+285v+285s). Third, to draw out the dangerous consequences: the necessary inequality of I(v+s) and IIc, the perpetual tendency of the process to break down in crisis, the impossibility of simple reproduction as a stable capitalist state, and the role of the credit system in mobilising the \"dead weight\" of hoarded money capital. How does this fit into the broader architecture? Chapter 21 is the culminating chapter of Capital Volume 2's Part III, \"The Reproduction and Circulation of the Total Social Capital,\" and it is the bridge between Volume 2's abstract analysis of circulation and Volume 3's analysis of the credit system, the profit rate, and crisis. It is also, historically, the seedbed of the twentieth-century \"market question\" debate (Tugan-Baranovsky, Lenin, Rosa Luxemburg, Bukharin, Otto Bauer) and the disputed heritage of underconsumptionist and disproportionality theories of crisis. Within Volume 2 as a whole, this chapter proves that capital is not just a sum of things but a self-expanding process whose conditions of existence are internal to its own reproduction — an argument that has no equivalent in classical political economy. Adam Smith's attempt to reduce the annual product to a sum of revenues (the notorious \"v+s dogma\") is, we now see, only the mirror image of an error Marx is here at pains to demonstrate: capital is not consumed as revenue, and the productive consumption of a portion of the surplus product is the material precondition of accumulation. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS: Opening: From the Individual Capitalist to the Social Whole The chapter opens with the retrospective gesture of recalling Volume 1's account of individual accumulation. Marx's argument is that the form of individual accumulation determines the form of social accumulation. In Volume 1 the individual capitalist realizes his commodity capital (C′) as money; the portion of that money representing surplus value (M′) is converted back into additional elements of productive capital. \"The surplus-value that is transformed into money in this way is then transformed back by the capitalist into additional natural elements of his productive capital. In the next production circuit, the increased capital supplies an increased product.\" The method is analogical: what occurs in the individual case must also occur in the overall annual reproduction, just as the successive precipitation of a worn-out fixed component as money (hoarding) at the individual level was shown, in Chapter 20, to have its parallel in social reproduction. The numerical apparatus is set up immediately. A capital of 400c+100v, with annual surplus value 100, produces a commodity product of 400c+100v+100s = 600. This 600 is realized as money. Of that money, 400c is reconverted into the natural (use-value) form of constant capital; 100v is used to purchase labour-power; and 100s — if the entire surplus value is to be accumulated — is transformed into additional constant capital. Here the chapter inserts an editorial footnote (from Engels, presumably) noting that Marx \"leaves this out of consideration for the time being\" the fact that accumulation must also involve an increased outlay for variable capital (p. 575). This is important for the reader: the initial presentation looks only at additional constant capital, and only later (Section I(c)) does Marx turn to the additional variable capital. This is a textual strategy, not a substantive neglect. Two assumptions are specified, both of which carry the weight of the chapter. (1) The sum of surplus value hoarded is assumed sufficient , under the given technical conditions, either to extend the already-functioning constant capital or to install an entirely new industrial business. Marx immediately concedes that this may require hoarding money \"for a much longer time before this process takes place, i.e. before real accumulation, an expansion of production, can occur.\" Thus the quantitative threshold of accumulation is conditioned by the technical scale of modern industry; below it, the hoarded surplus value is idle. (2) More importantly, \"it is presupposed that there has in fact already been reproduction on an expanded scale.\" Why? Because in order to transform money into the elements of productive capital, \"these elements must be available on the market as commodities.\" It makes no difference if they are not bought ready-made but produced to order: \"in any case only after real reproduction on an expanded scale, the extension of what was formerly normal production, has already taken place as far as they are concerned.\" The money calls expanded reproduction into being on the other side \"only because the possibility of this already exists without the money; for money in itself is not an element of real reproduction.\" This last clause is the chapter's first theoretical hammer-blow: it is a direct refutation of any monetary or circulationist theory of accumulation. The Puzzle of Universal Hoarding and Its Solution The next movement of the text (pp. 577–579) sets out the apparent aporia of universal hoarding. Consider capitalist A, who sells his surplus product — the bearer of his surplus value — year after year, converting it bit by bit into money and storing it away. He is forming \"potential new money capital,\" potential \"on account of its capacity and its destiny, which is to be converted into elements of productive capital.\" But in fact, at this stage, A is merely performing \"simple hoard formation.\" His activity \"consists first of all simply in the successive withdrawal of circulating money from the circulation sphere.\" Three critical points follow. First, this hoard is not an addition to social wealth — no more than money spent on means of consumption would be. The money withdrawn from circulation \"may before that have either been already stored up once as a similar hoard, may have been the money form of wages, may have realized means of production or other commodities, or may have circulated constant capital components or revenue for some capitalist or other.\" Money no more constitutes new wealth by virtue of its manifold turnover than money in simple commodity circulation is \"the bearer, not just of its actual value, but of ten times its value, simply because it has turned over ten times in a day.\" The commodities still exist without it; the money itself deteriorates through wear and tear. Only in gold production — in so far as the gold product contains or is a bearer of surplus value — is new wealth (in the form of potential money) actually created. This is a foundational qualification: the metallic money supply is the only substance of potential new money capital, and even then, only when the new gold product actually steps into circulation. Second, and yet: \"even though it is not additional new social wealth, this surplus-value hoarded up in the money form does represent new potential money capital, on account of the function for which it is stored.\" Function, not substance, makes the hoard into capital. This distinction between potential money capital and actual productive capital will later underwrite the analysis of the credit system (Volume 3), where banks concentrate these dispersed, idle hoards into \"loanable capital.\" Third, the aporia : \"If this operation is conceived as taking place on all sides, it seems impossible to explain where the buyers are to come from.\" Everyone wants to sell in order to hoard; no one wants to buy. The circulation between the various parts of annual reproduction cannot be conceived rectilinearly, since \"with few exceptions, it always consists of mutually opposing movements.\" If it were rectilinear, we would have to begin with the gold producer, \"who buys without selling,\" and assume all others sell to him. But then the gold producer's surplus product would have to absorb the entire annual surplus product of society, and his surplus value would be the sole fund from which all other capitalists draw to realize their own surplus products — an absurdity. \"These assumptions are so absurd that they are only helpful towards explaining the possibility of a general simultaneous hoard formation, and do not take reproduction itself, except that of the gold producers, a single step forwards.\" Marx now signals the analytical division he will follow: accumulation in Department I must be distinguished from accumulation in Department II, and he will begin with Department I. Section I — Accumulation in Department I (a) Hoard Formation in Department I The first substantive move (pp. 579–581) is to show that the apparent aporia dissolves once we recognise that the hoard-forming activity of some capitalists is bought by the productive investment of others. All the branches within Department I, and all the individual capitals within those branches, are \"to be found at different stages in the process of their successive transformation of surplus-value into potential money capital.\" Some are still hoarding; others have already assembled enough hoarded value to convert it into productive capital, \"buying means of production, additional elements of constant capital.\" The two categories \"relate to one another as buyers and sellers respectively, and each of the two in this exclusive role.\" Note Marx's methodological caution: what A does in this transaction is simple hoard formation, which \"is in no case production, and thus from the start not an increment to production.\" The action consists of \"simply withdrawing from circulation the money he obtained by selling his surplus product, holding on to it and impounding it.\" And this operation is performed \"at numerous points on the circulation surface\" by other capitalists A′, A″ etc., who all \"work equally zealously at this kind of hoard formation.\" These multiple points at which money is withdrawn from circulation \"appear as an equal number of obstacles to circulation, because they immobilize the money and deprive it of its capacity for circulation for a longer or shorter time.\" There is a historical caveat here that Marx inserts deliberately: hoard formation is not a phenomenon original to capitalism. In simple commodity circulation — long before capitalism — there are already hoards; \"the quantity of money present in the society is always greater than the part of this that is in active circulation, even if the latter rises and falls according to circumstances.\" What the capitalist mode does is to transform this pre-existing phenomenon into \"an immanent moment of the capitalist production process.\" From this, Marx remarks ironically: \"It is easy to understand the satisfaction evinced when the credit system concentrates all these potential capitals in the hands of banks, etc., makes them into disposable capital — 'loanable capital' — i.e. money capital, no longer passive and, as it were, a castle in the air, but active, usurious, proliferating capital.\" This is a preview of the dialectic: the hoard appears as passivity, but the credit system gives it activity — while also, as we will see in Section III, giving it the capacity to mistake itself for real accumulation. The precondition for A's hoard formation is \"thus his successive production of surplus product — the repository of his surplus-value that is to be realized.\" This \"successive\" character is crucial: the surplus product is produced continuously, but the money cannot be invested until the hoard reaches the threshold specified at the outset. The natural form of A's surplus product, in the case considered, is \"the natural form of an element of constant capital in department I, i.e. it belongs to the category of means of production of means of production.\" Thus, when A sells to B (still within Department I), the commodity cast into circulation is destined to enter B's constant capital \"as a fixed or fluid element.\" But the key insight is that although A withdraws money from circulation for his surplus value, \"he casts commodities into circulation, on the other hand, without withdrawing other commodities for these; this enables B, B′, B″, etc. for their part simply to cast money into circulation and withdraw commodities.\" The apparent impossibility of universal hoarding dissolves into a complementarity of one-sided movements: A sells without buying (hoards); B buys without selling (invests). In the present case — exchange internal to Department I — the commodity drawn out is a means of production that will enter B's capital. Thus each is doing what the other cannot, and the total process moves forward. There follows a compressed reprise (pp. 581–583) of the fixed-capital complication from Chapter 20. Marx reminds the reader of the earlier schema's conclusion: the periodic renewal of the fixed capital portion of IIc presupposes \"on the one hand a one-sided purchase of that fixed part of IIC which is transformed back from the money form into the natural form and to which corresponds a one-sided sale of Is; on the other hand it presupposes a one-sided sale on the part of IIC, the sale of that fixed value component (wear and tear) which is precipitated out in money and to which corresponds a one-sided purchase of Is.\" The balance condition for the exchange to take place \"normally\" is that \"the one-sided purchase by IIC is equal in value to its one-sided sale,\" and similarly that I's one-sided sale to one section of II is equal to its one-sided purchase from another. Otherwise, \"simple reproduction would be disrupted.\" Marx now generalises this: in the case of expanded reproduction, \"it has similarly to be assumed that the one-sided sales by the hoard-forming section of Is, A, A′, A″, balance the one-sided purchases by section B, B′, B″ of Is, which transform their hoards into elements of additional productive capital.\" This is the chapter's first crisis-theoretic proposition. The balance is not guaranteed. \"The fact that the production of commodities is the general form of capitalist production already implies that money plays a role, not just as means of circulation, but also as money capital within the circulation sphere, and gives rise to certain conditions for normal exchange that are peculiar to this mode of production.\" These conditions for normal reproduction on either scale \"turn into an equal number of conditions for an abnormal course, possibilities of crisis, since, on the basis of the spontaneous pattern of this production, this balance is itself an accident.\" The phrase \"the balance is itself an accident\" is one of the most concentrated statements of Marx's theory of crisis in the whole of Volume 2. Marx then extends the analysis to the circulation between the two departments. In the exchange of Iv for a corresponding value of IIc, commodities II are ultimately replaced for IIc by the same value of commodities I. But \"in this mutual conversion of their reciprocal commodities, there is no direct exchange between capitalists I and II.\" Instead, the circulation involves several interlocking one-sided transactions. Capitalist II sells his commodities IIc to the working class of Department I, facing them one-sidedly as a seller while they face him as buyers of commodities. With the money received, IIc one-sidedly faces the collective capitalist I as a buyer of commodities, and I faces him as a seller of commodities to the value of Iv. \"It is only by this sale of commodities that department I finally reproduces its variable capital in the form of money capital.\" The full circulation is composed of three independent but intertwined processes : (1) the continuous supply of labour-power on the part of Department I's working class; (2) the transformation of one part of Department I's commodity capital back into the money form of variable capital; (3) the replacement of a part of Department II's commodity capital by natural elements of constant capital IIc. \"These necessary preconditions all mutually require one another, but they are mediated by a very complicated process which involves three processes of circulation that proceed independently, even if they are intertwined with one another.\" The sentence that closes this subsection is the refrain of the whole chapter: \"The very complexity of the process provides many occasions for it to take an abnormal course.\" (b) The Additional Constant Capital This is the core theoretical subsection. It begins with a striking claim: \"The surplus product, the repository of surplus-value, does not cost anything to its appropriators, here the capitalists in department I. They do not have to advance either money or commodities, in any form, in order to receive it.\" This is a quietly devastating point. The Physiocrats' concept of avance (advance) signified \"simply the general form of value realized in the elements of productive capital.\" What the capitalist advances is nothing but constant and variable capital. The worker , however, \"does not merely maintain their constant capital for them by way of his labour, and replace their variable capital by way of a corresponding portion of value newly created in the form of commodities; he also supplies them, by his surplus labour, with a surplus-value existing in the form of a surplus product.\" The \"in the case considered here\" — the case examined throughout this chapter — the surplus product \"consists from the start of means of production of means of production.\" Thus, \"it is only in the hands of B, B′, B″ etc. (department I) that this surplus product functions as additional constant capital; but it is already virtually this, even before it is sold, in the hands of the hoard formers A, A′, A″.\" Now comes the crucial conservation-of-value argument. \"If we simply consider the level of reproduction on the part of department I in value terms, then we still find ourselves within the limits of simple reproduction, for no additional capital has been set in motion in order to create this virtual excess of constant capital (the surplus product), and no more surplus labour than was performed on the basis of simple reproduction.\" What has changed is only the use-form of the surplus labour applied — \"the concrete character of its particular useful mode.\" In the case of simple reproduction the surplus labour was assumed to have been spent producing means of production for IIc. Now it is \"spent on means of production for Ic instead of IIC, on means of production for means of production instead of on means of production for means of consumption.\" Therefore: \"in order to make the transition from simple reproduction to expanded reproduction, production in department I must be in a position to produce fewer elements of constant capital for department II, but all the more for department I.\" The transition \"can never be achieved without difficulty\" and is \"made easier by the fact that a number of the products of department I can serve as means of production in both departments.\" Here is the fundamental thesis of the chapter: \"It follows therefore that — simply considering the values involved — the material substratum for expanded reproduction is produced in the course of simple reproduction.\" From this, Marx draws two analytic consequences that he will need. First: \"The formation of virtual additional money capital on the part of A, A′, A″ (department I) — by the subsequent sale of their surplus product, which has been formed without any monetary expenditure by the capitalists involved — is thus here simply the money form of extra production of means of production in department I.\" In other words, the money-form of the hoard is nothing but the shadow of a use-value transformation that has already occurred. Second: \"the large-scale production of additional virtual money capital — at numerous points on the surface of circulation — is therefore nothing more than the result and expression of the many-sided production of virtual additional productive capital, whose genesis does not itself presuppose any additional monetary expenditure on the part of the industrial capitalists.\" The successive transformation of virtual additional productive capital into virtual money capital (the hoard) on the part of A, A′, A″ etc. is \"conditioned by the successive sale of their surplus product — i.e. by the repeated one-sided sale of commodities without a complementary purchase.\" Two consequences follow: (i) there is a repeated withdrawal of money from circulation and a corresponding hoard formation; (ii) hoard formation \"in no way implies additional wealth in precious metals, but only a different function for the money that was already in circulation previously. It formerly functioned as a means of circulation, and now it functions as a hoard, as virtual new money capital in the course of formation.\" Hence: \"The formation of additional money capital and the quantity of precious metal existing in a country thus do not stand in any causal connection with one another.\" This is a decisive methodological break from the quantity theory of money. Marx now states the growth principles of hoarding. The greater the productive capital already functioning (including the labour-power incorporated into it), and the more developed the productive power of labour, and hence the greater the mass of surplus product in both value and use-value terms — then the greater is: (1) the additional virtual productive capital in the form of surplus product in the hands of A, A′, A″ etc.; and (2) the quantity of this surplus product transformed into money, i.e. the quantity of additional virtual money capital in the hands of A, A′, A″. Thus, \"if Fullarton, for instance, does not want to recognize over-production in the customary sense, but does recognize the over-production of capital, in particular of money capital, this proves once again how utterly unable even the best bourgeois economists are to understand the mechanism of their system\" (p. 574, footnote). Fullarton, an English opponent of the quantity theory of money, is here criticised for recognising only money-capital over-production, not real over-production — a symptom of the inability to see that money capital is the mere form of a surplus product that is materially already there. Marx then sharpens the point: \"If the surplus product directly produced and appropriated by the capitalists A, A′, A″ (department I) is the real basis for capital accumulation, i.e. for expanded reproduction, even though it actually functions in this capacity only in the hands of B, B′, B″ etc. (department I), it is however absolutely unproductive in its monetary metamorphosis — as a hoard and as virtual money capital that is formed bit by bit. In this form it runs parallel with the production process but lies outside it. It is a 'dead weight' on capitalist production.\" The credit system and the \"papers\" through which it operates are the attempt to set this dead weight in motion as active capital — and Marx here prepares us for the later analysis of fictitious capital. There follows the segmentation argument. \"The surplus product converted into virtual money capital becomes quantitatively greater, the greater the total sum of capital already functioning, from the functioning of which it emerged.\" This absolute increase makes segmentation easier: money capital can be \"completely separated from its parent capital, in order to be invested as new money capital in an independent business.\" Inheritances, family fortunes, new independent ventures — all are ways in which the aggregate hoard can be split and deployed. The next section (pp. 575–577) raises the question of the origin of the money that B, B′, B″ must use to buy A's surplus product. Marx's assumption so far has been that A, A′, A″ themselves have already produced the surplus product without monetary expenditure. But to come into the hands of B, B′, B″, \"an act of circulation is required; they have to buy the surplus product.\" Two issues arise: (1) a major part of the surplus product produced by A in the current year \"can actually function only in the hands of B, B′, B″ (department I) as industrial capital in the following year or even later\"; (2) where does the money for this circulation come from? Marx's answer is that we already know, from the analysis of simple reproduction, that \"a certain quantity of money must exist in the hands of the capitalists in departments I and II so that they may exchange their surplus product.\" There, the money whose only use was to be spent as revenue on means of consumption returned to the capitalists to the extent they advanced it. Here \"the same money similarly reappears, but with its function changed.\" The A's and B's (Department I) \"supply one another with the money for transforming their surplus products into additional virtual money capital, and alternately cast the newly formed money capital into the circulation sphere as a means of purchase.\" The only presupposition required is that \"the quantity of money existing in the country (the velocity of circulation etc. taken as constant) is sufficient both for active circulation and for the reserve hoards\" — the same condition Marx established for simple commodity circulation — with the important qualification that the required quantity must be larger in the case of capitalism, for four reasons: (1) all products are produced as commodities in capitalist production and must therefore undergo a metamorphosis into money; (2) the mass of commodity capital and its value is not only absolutely greater but grows incomparably faster; (3) the variable capital that has to be converted into money capital is ever more extensive; (4) as production expands, the formation of new money capital keeps in step, and so the material for its hoarded form also has to be present. Marx then notes the most developed phase of the credit system: metallic circulation remains its basis. \"The extra production of precious metals, according to whether this makes them abundant or scarce, can now exert a disturbing influence on the price of commodities, not only in the long term, but also within very short periods.\" Conversely, \"the whole credit mechanism must constantly be engaged in restricting the actual circulation of metal by all kinds of operations, methods, technical devices, to what is relatively an ever decreasing minimum — though this also increases in the same proportion the artificial character of the entire machinery and the chances of its normal course being disturbed.\" Another compressed statement of the dialectic of the credit system: rationalisation and fragility go hand in hand. The subsection closes with a note on method: \"It is important above all, however, to start by assuming metal circulation in its most simple original form, since in this way the flux or reflux, settlement of balances, in short all those aspects that appear in the credit system as consciously regulated processes, present themselves as existing independently of the credit system, and the thing appears in its spontaneous form, instead of the form of subsequent reflection.\" This is a methodological tour de force : the credit system is the secondary reflection of processes that originate in the spontaneous metallic circulation; to see the true mechanism we must strip away the credit system's mediation. (c) The Additional Variable Capital This is one of the shortest subsections in the chapter, and its brevity is deliberate. Marx refers back to Volume 1 for the demonstration \"how, on the basis of capitalist production, labour-power is always on hand, and how, if necessary, more labour can be extracted without an increase in the number of workers employed, or the mass of labour-power.\" He then states: \"We do not have to go into this any further here, therefore, but can simply assume that the portion of the newly formed money capital that is convertible into variable capital always finds available the labour-power into which it is to be transformed.\" Marx also invokes Volume 1's demonstration that a given capital can within certain limits expand its scale of production without accumulation (e.g. through longer working hours or greater intensity). But \"what we are dealing with now, however, is capital accumulation in the specific sense, where the expansion of production is conditioned by the transformation of surplus-value into extra capital, and therefore by the expanded capital basis of production.\" This is an important conceptual distinction: \"accumulation\" in the strict sense is not mere expansion of production; it is expansion grounded in the transformation of surplus value into new capital . The gold producer is treated as a special case, because he can accumulate a part of his golden surplus value as virtual money capital; once it reaches the necessary level, he can convert it directly into new variable capital, and also into elements of constant capital. But he must find the material elements of constant capital available — whether producers work to fill stocks or work to order. \"The real expansion of production, i.e. the surplus product, is presupposed in both cases, once as actually present, the other time as virtually present, capable of being supplied.\" Section II — Accumulation in Department II This section turns from the internal circulation of Department I to the circulation between the two departments — and Marx here introduces the conflict that structures the remainder of the chapter. Consider the case in which A (Department I) sells his surplus product to a B belonging to Department II. \"This can only happen if A (I), after he has sold means of production to B (II), does not go on to buy means of consumption; i.e. only by a unilateral sale on his part.\" The consequence is immediate and severe. If the conversion of IIc back into the natural form of productive constant capital normally involves the exchange of Iv plus at least a part of Is for part of IIc, and A now realizes Is in money without undertaking that exchange, \"then although the formation of additional virtual money capital takes place on A's part, on the other side an equal part of B (II)'s constant capital is tied up in the form of commodity capital, incapable of conversion into the natural form of productive, constant capital.\" In other words, \"a part of B (II)'s commodities, and at first sight a part without selling which he cannot transform his constant capital completely back into the productive form, has become unsaleable; in this respect there is over-production, which also inhibits B (II)'s reproduction — even on the same scale.\" Marx generalises. \"A (I), by selling its surplus product to B (II), has supplied the latter with a corresponding portion of constant capital in the natural form, but at the same time made an equal portion of B (II)'s commodity value unsaleable.\" Considered in aggregate, the transformation of A(I)'s surplus product into virtual money capital \"expresses the non-transformability of a portion of commodity capital equal to this in value back into productive (constant) capital; i.e. not virtual production on an expanded scale, but rather a restriction of simple reproduction, i.e. a shortfall in simple reproduction.\" The passage that follows is one of the most theoretically loaded in the chapter. Since the formation and sale of A(I)'s surplus product are themselves normal phenomena of simple reproduction, we have even on the basis of simple reproduction the following \"mutually conditioning phenomena\": \"formation of virtual extra money capital in department I (hence under-consumption from department II's standpoint); piling up of commodity stocks in department II which cannot be transformed back into productive capital (i.e. relative over-production in department II); surplus money capital in department I and a shortfall in reproduction in department II.\" But Marx's analytical verdict is sharp. This coordinated set of phenomena does not yet constitute expanded reproduction. It is a disturbance of simple reproduction. Expanded reproduction requires something more: an actual modification of the arrangement of the elements of the annual product. And here Marx makes the crucial clarification: \"Without delaying any longer on this point, we simply note that it was presupposed in our presentation of simple reproduction that the entire surplus-value in departments I and II was spent as revenue. In point of fact, however, one portion of surplus-value is spent as revenue, and another portion transformed into capital. Only with this precondition does real accumulation take place.\" The next sentence is a searing critique of an entire theory: \"But the idea that accumulation is achieved at the expense of consumption — considered in this general way — is an illusion that contradicts the essence of capitalist production, in as much as it assumes that the purpose and driving motive of this is consumption, and not the grabbing of surplus-value and its capitalization, i.e. accumulation.\" This is Marx's rejection of underconsumptionism — not in the sense that workers' consumption is irrelevant, but in the sense that treating accumulation as constrained by the need to feed consumption misconstrues the purpose of the capitalist process. Capital does not accumulate in order to consume; it consumes (productively) in order to accumulate. Marx now considers accumulation in Department II \"somewhat more closely.\" He begins from the previous schema (1,000v + 1,000s)I exchanged for 2,000 IIc. If half of Is (500) is now reincorporated into Department I as constant capital, then \"this part of the surplus product that is retained in department I cannot replace any part of IIc.\" Instead of being converted into means of consumption, it \"is to serve as additional means of production in department I itself.\" And \"it cannot perform this function simultaneously in both department I and department II.\" The upshot: instead of 2,000 I(v+s), only 1,500 is available for conversion into 2,000 IIc; and so 500 IIc \"can in fact not be transformed from its commodity form into productive (constant) capital II.\" \"Thus an overproduction in department II, corresponding in value precisely to the expansion of production that took place in department I.\" And this can have a feedback effect: the over-production in II \"might in fact react so strongly on department I that even the reflux of the 1,000 spent by the department I workers on means of consumption II would take place only partially.\" This means the 1,000 spent on means of consumption II would not return to the hands of the Department I capitalists in the form of variable money capital. Hence: \"The latter would thus be inhibited even in their reproduction on the same scale, and inhibited, moreover, by the very attempt to expand it.\" Marx then adds an important clarification: \"It should also be mentioned in this connection that all that has taken place in department I is in fact simple reproduction, the elements merely being grouped together differently from the above schema, in accordance with the needs of future expansion, say in the coming year.\" Marx then anticipates and critiques a possible escape from the difficulty. One might argue that the 500 IIc lying in the capitalists' stores is not over-production but a necessary element of reproduction which we have up to now neglected . The argument: because money is piled up at several points, and must be withdrawn from circulation partly to make possible the formation of new money capital in Department I itself, and partly to maintain the value of the fixed capital being gradually consumed, commodity stocks must accumulate. Since the schema assumes all money and commodities are in the hands of the capitalists I and II, with no merchants, money-dealers, bankers, or non-producing consumer classes, \"the constant formation of commodity stocks is indispensable, in the hands of their respective producers themselves, in order to keep the machinery of reproduction going.\" Thus the 500 IIc in the stores of Department II \"represents the commodity stock in means of consumption that ensures the continuity of the consumption process involved in reproduction, and therefore the transition from one year to the other.\" This stock cannot sink to zero on the last day of the year and reappear on the first day of the next. Capitalist producers must hold a money reserve capital , and they must also, in the schema's simplified world, supply themselves with the money capital that in reality exists in the hands of merchants. Marx concedes the reality of these stock-effects but subjects them to three objections. (1) The argument holds for all capitalists in both departments; \"a stock of commodities in department II implies a previous stock of commodities in department I. If we ignore this stock on one side, we must also ignore it on the other. But if we bring both sides into consideration, the problem is in no way changed.\" (2) The symmetry must be extended temporally. Just as the current year concludes with a commodity stock for the next, so it began with a commodity stock left over from the previous year. \"In analysing the annual reproduction — reduced to its most abstract expression — we must thus cancel out the stock on both sides.\" If we leave the year with the commodity stock transferred to the next, we must deduct the stock received from the year before, leaving us with the total product of an average year as the object of analysis. (3) Most importantly: the fact that we did not encounter this difficulty in simple reproduction \"shows that we are dealing here with a specific phenomenon that is due merely to the different arrangement of the elements of department I (as far as reproduction is concerned), an arrangement without which there could be no reproduction on an expanded scale at all.\" This is the dialectical point: the difficulty is the solution. The rearrangement of Department I's product that causes the difficulty in exchange is precisely what constitutes the material precondition for expansion. Section III — The Schematic Presentation of Accumulation The section opens with Schema (a) : I. 4,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s = 6,000 II. 1,500c + 376v + 376s = 2,252 Total annual social product = 8,252. This is smaller than the 9,000 of the original schema. Marx's explanation: \"We might just as well take a much larger sum, multiply it by ten, say, for all the difference it makes.\" The point is pedagogical. The smaller total is chosen \"precisely to draw attention to the fact that reproduction on an expanded scale (which is conceived here simply as production pursued with a greater investment of capital) has nothing to do with the absolute size of the product, that for a given volume of commodities it simply assumes a different arrangement or a different determination of the functions of the various elements of the given product, and is thus in the first instance only simple reproduction, as far as its value goes.\" Then the crucial sentence: \"It is not the quantity, but the qualitative character of the given elements of simple reproduction that is changed, and this change is the material precondition for the ensuing reproduction on an expanded scale.\" Schema (b) is then introduced for contrast: I. 4,000c + 875v + 875s = 5,750 II. 1,750c + 376v + 376s = 2,502 In both schemas, the annual product is the same value (8,252). But in (b) it is arranged such that reproduction begins again on the same scale: (875v+875s)I = 1,750 I(v+s) is exchanged without a surplus for 1,750 IIc. In (a), by contrast, (1,000v+1,000s)I = 2,000 I(v+s) leaves behind, when exchanged for 1,500 IIc, a surplus of 500 Is for accumulation in Department I. Thus Schema (b) is a simple-reproduction schema of the same total value as (a), and the difference is entirely in the arrangement of the elements. Marx then analyses Schema (a) more closely. He assumes toward this end that \"both in department I and department II half the surplus-value, instead of being spent as revenue, is accumulated.\" Half of Is = 500. Since this 500 is accumulated (i.e. transformed into extra productive capital), \"it follows that only (1,000v+500s)I is spent as revenue. Hence the normal size of IIc is now only 1,500.\" The exchange between 1,500 and 1,500 IIc requires no further investigation — it is already a process of simple reproduction. What remains to be investigated are: the 500 Is and (376v+376s)II — \"which involves both the internal relations in the two departments and the movement between them.\" Since it is assumed that half of Department II's surplus value (i.e. 188) is to be accumulated, \"one quarter of this or 47 into variable capital, say 48 for the sake of a round number, leaving 140 to be transformed into constant capital.\" (Note: the relation is organic — Department II's own c:v ratio, from 1,500c+750v, is 2:1; so the split of new investment into v and c follows this ratio. If 188 is accumulated, v: c = 1:4 because the earlier total (1,500c+376v+376s = 2,252, of which c = 1,500, v = 376, ratio ~4:1). Actually looking at the schema: 1,500c + 376v means c:v = 3.99:1 ≈ 4:1, so of the 188 accumulated, 1/5 = 37.6 → 48 as v, 4/5 = 150.4 → 140 as c. Marx admits 48 is a \"round\" replacement for 47.) Here Marx explicitly signals the new theoretical problem : \"The 140 IIS can be transformed into productive capital only by being replaced by a portion of commodities Is to the same value. It is self-evident that the part of the Is that is to be exchanged for IIS must consist of means of production, able to go either into the production of both departments, or exclusively into that of department II.\" And then: \"This exchange can take place only by a unilateral purchase on the part of department II, since the surplus product 500 Is, which has still to be considered, is destined to serve for accumulation within department I, and cannot therefore be exchanged for commodities II. In other words, department I cannot at the same time both accumulate and consume the surplus product.\" And so: \"Department II must therefore buy 140s for cash, without this money flowing back to it by the subsequent sale of its commodities to department I. And this is moreover a constant and repeated process for each new year's production, in so far as this is reproduction on an expanded scale. Where then in department II is the source of money for this?\" This question is then answered by a series of careful exclusions. First , the 376 IIv does not help: \"The money capital of 376, advanced for labour-power, constantly returns to the department II capitalists as variable capital in the money form, with the purchase of department II's commodities. This constantly repeated removal from and return to the starting-point — the capitalist's pocket — in no way increases the amount of money driving round this circuit.\" It is not a source of monetary accumulation; nor can the money be withdrawn from this circuit and hoarded. Second , Marx considers the possibility that Department II might exploit its peculiar advantage of selling commodities back to its own workers. He sets out two variants: (1) simply reduce wages below their normal average level, freeing part of the money functioning as variable capital, which, if the process is constantly repeated, becomes \"a normal source of hoard formation\"; (2) exploit the truck system and falsification of the circulating medium. Both are rejected. In the case of (1): \"the normal wage which is actually paid (and which determines the size of the variable capital, other things being equal) is in no way paid out of the good will of the capitalists, but is what has to be paid under the given conditions. This mode of explanation is thereby dispensed with.\" In the case of (2): \"we are talking about the real wage, not that nominally paid.\" Marx interjects a characteristic meta-commentary: \"We see here that in an objective analysis of the capitalist mechanism, certain blemishes that still stick to it, and with extraordinary tenacity, cannot be used as subterfuges for getting round theoretical difficulties. But strange though this seems, the great majority of my bourgeois critics complain that I do the capitalist an injustice by assuming — in Volume 1 of Capital, for example — that he pays the real value of labour-power, which in most cases he does not! (Here I might quote Schäffle, with the magnanimity he attributes to me.)\" This is a deliciously ironic passage: Marx is accused of being too kind to capitalists for assuming they pay the value of labour-power, yet his critics then demand he invoke underpayment as an escape from a theoretical puzzle. Third , the 376 IIs: \"Here it is only capitalists in the same department who confront one another, selling and buying from each other the means of consumption that they have produced. The money needed for this exchange functions simply as a means of circulation, and in the normal course it must flow back to the parties involved in the same degree to which they first advanced it to circulation, so it can tread the same path once again.\" Withdrawal of this money from circulation for hoard formation is possible only in two ways. (i) One section of Department II capitalists might swindle the others and rob them of their money. \"The fact that the money can be stolen and that the formation of additional money capital by one section of the capitalists in department II can therefore be combined with a positive loss of money by another section has absolutely no bearing on the matter. The defrauded section of department II capitalists would have to live a little less extravagantly, but that would be all.\" (ii) Alternatively, \"a portion of IIS that represents necessary means of subsistence is directly transformed into new variable capital in department II. How this happens will be investigated at the close of the present chapter (section 4).\" Marx postpones the resolution to the last section of the chapter. (a) First Example Marx now presents the fully worked-out numerical illustration, beginning with the two schemas of departure. (A) Schema of simple reproduction: I. 4,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s = 6,000 II. 2,000c + 500v + 500s = 3,000 Total = 9,000 (B) Initial schema for reproduction on an expanded scale: I. 4,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s = 6,000 II. 1,500c + 750v + 750s = 3,000 Total = 9,000 Assume half of Is is accumulated, i.e. 500. Then (1,000v+500s)I = 1,500 I(v+s) is replaced by 1,500 IIc. There then remains in Department I: 4,000c + 500s, the latter to be accumulated. The replacement of (1,000v+500s)I by 1,500 IIc \"is a process of simple reproduction and has already been examined in connection with the latter.\" Marx then assumes that 400 of the 500 Is to be accumulated has to be transformed into constant capital, and 100 into variable capital. (Notice: this ratio 4:1 corresponds to Department I's own composition, 4,000c:1,000v = 4:1.) \"The exchange within department I of the 400s that has to be capitalized has already been explained; so this can be annexed to Ic without any more ado, and we then get the following capitalization for department I: I. 4,400c + 1,000v [in money] + 100s (to be converted into 100v).\" Department II now buys from Department I, for the purpose of accumulation, the 100 Is (existing as means of production), which now forms extra constant capital for Department II. The 100 in money it pays is transformed into the money form of Department I's extra variable capital. Hence Department I's capital: 4,400c + 1,100v (the latter in money) = 5,500. Department II now has 1,600c in constant capital; \"a further 50v has to be added for the purchase of new labour-power for working this up, and so its variable capital grows from 750 to 800.\" (Notice again: 100c added, so 50v added, ratio 2:1, matching Department II's original c:v = 1,500:750 = 2:1.) This extension of both constant and variable capital in Department II is met out of its own surplus value. Of the 750 IIs, therefore, there only remains 600s as a consumption fund for Department II's capitalists: II. 1,600c + 800v + 600s (consumption fund) = 3,000. The 150s produced as means of consumption that is converted here into (100c+50v)II goes completely into the workers' consumption in its natural form: 100 is consumed by Department I's workers (100v) and 50 by Department II's workers (50v), as elaborated above. This is a key point: the physical form of the value transferred (150 IIs) is means of consumption that must be consumed by workers; the arrangement is such that the additional constant and variable capital of Department II correspond to a larger portion of its product being produced as necessary means of consumption rather than luxury/consumption-fund goods. The rearrangement after accumulation: I. 4,400c + 1,100v + 500 (capitalists' consumption fund) = 6,000 II. 1,600c + 800v + 600 (capitalists' consumption fund) = 3,000 Total = 9,000. Capital after rearrangement: I. 4,400c + 1,100v (money) = 5,500 II. 1,600c + 800v (money) = 2,400 Total capital = 7,900. Whereas production began with: I. 4,000c + 1,000v = 5,000 II. 1,500c + 750v = 2,250 Total = 7,250. If real accumulation proceeds on this basis (i.e. if production actually takes place with this increased capital), then at the end of the following year: I. 4,400c + 1,100v + 1,100s = 6,600 II. 1,600c + 800v + 800s = 3,200 Total = 9,800. Marx then continues the process. If accumulation continues in Department I in the same proportions, i.e. 550s spent as revenue and 550s accumulated, then to start with 1,100 Iv is replaced by 1,100 IIc. And 550 Is remains to be realized in an equal amount of commodities II, i.e. altogether 1,650 I(v+s). But IIc = 1,600, so \"the remaining 50 must be supplemented from the 800 IIs.\" If we abstract from the money initially: I. 4,400c + 550s (to be capitalized); as well as 1,650(v+s) in the consumption fund for capitalists and workers, realized in commodities IIc. II. 1,650c (with 50 added from IIs) + 800v + 750s (capitalists' consumption fund). Now if the former ratio of v to c in Department II remains unchanged, a further 25v must be laid out for 50c (ratio 2:1). This comes out of the 750s: II. 1,650c + 825v + 725s. In Department I, 550s has to be capitalized. If the earlier ratio remains unchanged, 440 forms constant capital and 110 variable capital (ratio 4:1). This 110 is ultimately obtained from the 725 IIs. Means of consumption to the value of 110 are consumed by Department I's workers instead of by Department II's capitalists, \"the latter being forced to capitalize this 110s instead of consuming it.\" This leaves 615 IIs out of 725 IIs. But if Department II transforms this 110 into additional constant capital, it needs a further additional variable capital of 55 (ratio 2:1). This comes out of the 615 IIs, leaving 560 for consumption of Department II's capitalists. After all \"actual and potential transfers,\" we get: I. (4,400c+440c) + (1,100v+110v) = 4,840c + 1,210v = 6,050 II. (1,600c+50c+110c) + (800v+25v+55v) = 1,760c + 880v = 2,640 Total capital = 8,690. The crucial general observation: \"If things are to proceed normally, accumulation in department II must take place quicker than in department I, since the part of I(v+s) that has to be exchanged for commodities IIc would otherwise grow more quickly than IIc, which is all that it can be exchanged for.\" This is the structural asymmetry of expanded reproduction: Department II must accelerate its own accumulation in order to absorb the growing flow of I(v+s) that requires IIc as its counterpart. The process continues. At the end of the following year, with reproduction continuing on the new basis: I. 4,840c + 1,210v + 1,210s = 7,260 II. 1,760c + 880v + 880s = 3,520 Total = 10,780. Marx then works through the same fractions again. Department I has 1,210v plus half of s (=605) to spend as revenue, a total of 1,815. This consumption fund is 55 greater than IIc (which is 1,760). The 55 has to be deducted from the 880s. The transformation of 55 IIs into Ic presupposes a further deduction from IIs for a corresponding variable capital of 27½; there remains 797½ IIs to be consumed. Department I: 605s to be capitalized, of which 484 for constant, 121 for variable. The 121 is deducted from the 797½, leaving 676½. Department II transforms a further 121 into constant capital and needs for this purpose a further variable capital of 60½; this comes out of the 676½, leaving 616 for consumption. Capital: I. Constant 4,840 + 484 = 5,324; Variable 1,210 + 121 = 1,331. II. Constant 1,760 + 55 + 121 = 1,936; Variable 880 + 27½ + 60½ = 968. So: I. 5,324c + 1,331v = 6,655; II. 1,936c + 968v = 2,904. Product at the end of the year: I. 5,324c + 1,331v + 1,331s = 7,986 II. 1,936c + 968v + 968s = 3,872 Total = 11,858. Repeating the calculation, rounding off fractions, at the end of the following year: I. 5,856c + 1,464v + 1,464s = 8,784 II. 2,129c + 1,065v + 1,065s = 4,259 Total = 13,043. At the close of the year after that: I. 6,442c + 1,610v + 1,610s = 9,662 II. 2,342c + 1,172v + 1,172s = 4,686 Total = 14,348. Summary statistics : In the course of five years of reproduction on an expanded scale, total capital of departments I and II has risen from 5,500c + 1,750v = 7,250 to 8,784c + 2,782v = 11,566, a ratio of 100:160. Total surplus value originally 1,750, now 2,782. Surplus value consumed was originally 500 for Department I and 600 for Department II, a total of 1,100; in the final year 732 for Department I and 745 for Department II, altogether 1,477. It has grown in the ratio of 100:134. Note the accent in Marx's summary: even while the total mass of surplus value has grown substantially, the consumed portion has grown too — from 1,100 to 1,477. This is the numerical demonstration that accumulation does not require a sacrifice of consumption (at the aggregate level); on the contrary, accumulation raises the consumption fund of the capitalist class (and, indirectly, the variable capital, hence the workers' means of consumption). This is the numerical refutation of the underconsumptionist illusion. (b) Second Example Marx now considers a second example designed to illustrate a different organic composition. He assumes an annual product of 9,000 existing entirely as commodity capital in the hands of the industrial capitalist class, \"in a form in which the general average ratio of variable to constant capital [in both departments] is 1:5.\" This fact itself presupposes \"a significant development of capitalist production and, accordingly, of the productivity of social labour as well; a significant prior expansion of the scale of production; and finally a development of all the circumstances that produce in the working class a relative surplus population.\" The annual product: I. 5,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s = 7,000 II. 1,430c + 285v + 285s = 2,000 Total = 9,000. (An editorial footnote notes: \"The division of the social product between departments I and II here is an arbitrary ratio, selected presumably for arithmetical convenience.\") Assume the capitalist class in Department I consumes half its surplus value, 500, and accumulates the other half. In this case, (1,000v + 500s)I = 1,500 needs to be exchanged with 1,500 IIc. But IIc = 1,430. Therefore, 70 of surplus value has to be added — 70 taken from IIs. This, deducted from the 285 IIs, leaves 215 IIs. So: I. 5,000c + 500s (to be capitalized) + 1,500(v+s) in the consumption fund for capitalists and workers. II. 1,430c + 70s (to be capitalized) + 285v + 215s. Since 70 IIs has been directly annexed to IIc, a variable capital of 1/5 × 70 = 14 is required to set this extra constant capital in motion, given the general average composition 1:5. This 14 comes out of the 215 IIs, leaving 201 IIs, and we have: II. (1,430c + 70c) + (285v + 14v) + 201s. The exchange of 1,500 against 1,500 IIc is again simple reproduction, \"and nothing further need be said about it.\" Marx then states the crucial specificity of accumulating reproduction: \"In so far as there are still some peculiarities to be noted here, these stem from the fact that with accumulating reproduction, Iv is not just replaced by IIc alone, but rather by IIc plus a part of IIs.\" Marx then articulates the general principle of interdepartmental provisioning: \"It is self-evident that, on the assumption of accumulation, I(v+s) is greater than IIc, and not equal to it as in simple reproduction; since (1) department I incorporates a part of its surplus product into its own productive capital and transforms five sixths of this into constant capital, so that it cannot simultaneously exchange this five sixths for means of consumption II; and (2) department I has to supply the material for the constant capital needed for accumulation within department II out of its surplus product, just as department II has to supply department I with the material for the variable capital that is to set in motion the portion of its surplus product that department I itself applies as extra constant capital.\" This is one of the most important conceptual passages in the chapter: the reciprocity between the departments is material , not merely value-formal. Department I provides Department II with the material of its additional constant capital (means of production). Department II provides Department I with the material of its additional variable capital — i.e. means of subsistence for the additional workers. Marx adds the crucial clarification: \"We know that variable capital actually consists of labour-power, and so too therefore does this additional capital. It is not the capitalists in department I who buy or store up means of subsistence from department II for the additional labour-power that they need to employ, as the slave-owners had to do. It is the workers themselves who deal with department II. But this does not prevent the means of consumption for this additional labour-power from being viewed by the capitalist as only so many means of production and maintenance for his potential additional labour-power.\" Thus for the capitalist, additional variable capital appears materially as means of production-and-maintenance of labour-power, even though the actual purchase of means of consumption is performed by the workers themselves. \"Once he has incorporated this labour-power, the money becomes for the workers a means of purchase of commodities II, and they must therefore find their means of consumption to hand.\" Here Marx inserts a long polemical digression (pp. 587–589), triggered by observations about \"rational consumption.\" He writes: \"Incidentally, Mr Capitalist, as well as his press, is frequently discontented with the way in which labour-power spends its money, and with the commodities II in which it realizes this. On this occasion he philosophizes, waxes cultural and philanthropizes, as for example Mr Drummond, the British Secretary of Embassy in Washington.\" He quotes at length from The Nation (October 1879): \"The working-people have not kept up in culture with the growth of invention, and they have had things showered on them which they do not know how to use, and thus make no market for.\" And: \"There is no reason why the working man should not desire as many comforts as the minister, lawyer, and doctor, who is earning the same amount as himself.\" And: \"The problem remains, how to raise him as a consumer by rational and healthful processes\" — the demagogues rather incite the worker to shorten his hours than to raise his condition \"by the improvement of his mental and moral powers.\" Marx's riposte: \"Long hours of labour seem to be the secret of these 'rational and healthful processes,' which are to raise the condition of the worker by improving his 'mental and moral powers' and making a rational consumer out of him. In order to become a rational consumer of the capitalists' commodities, he must before all else — but the demagogues prevent him — begin by letting his own labour-power be consumed irrationally and in a way contrary to his own health, by the capitalist who employs him.\" He then cites Drummond's further account of the Lowell and Lawrence model cotton factories, where boarding houses belong to the same joint-stock company as the factory; \"no girl may return home after 10 p.m.\"; a special police force patrols the area to enforce this; \"no girl may lodge anywhere but on the company's land, and each house brings in some 10 dollars a week in rent.\" The \"ever-present piano\" in many of the best-appointed working girls' boarding-houses is adduced as evidence of rational recreation, \"at least among those who, after ten hours' steady work at the looms, need more relief from monotony than actual rest.\" Marx's final bite comes with Mr Oakman, the company secretary at Turner's Falls, who admits: \"The time is coming that we will beat England as to prices also... we must have lower prices and shall have it the moment we get our steel at lower prices and have our labour down!\" — \"Reduction in wages and long working hours, this is the kernel of the 'rational and healthful process' that is to raise the workers to the dignity of rational consumers, so that they 'make a market' for the 'things showered on them' by civilization and the progress of invention.\" This digression is not a mere moralising aside. It makes a definite theoretical point: the market for means of consumption II is not determined by the cultural level of the workers, but by the level of their wages, and this in turn is determined by the requirements of accumulation. The capitalist's complaint that workers do not consume enough is a complaint that the wages he pays them do not equal the value of the goods he has produced. This is a specific and graphic illustration of the contradiction between production and consumption under capitalist accumulation. Returning to the main thread, Marx restates the general architecture: \"Just as department I has to supply the additional constant capital for department II out of its surplus product, so department II supplies in the same way the extra variable capital for department I. Department II accumulates both for department I and for itself, as far as the variable capital is concerned, in as much as it reproduces a larger portion of its total production, and of its surplus product in particular, in the form of necessary means of consumption.\" The condition for expanded reproduction : \"With production on an increasing capital basis, I(v+s) must be equal to IIc, plus the part of the surplus product that is reincorporated as capital, plus the extra portion of constant capital needed to expand production in department II, and the minimum for this expansion is that without which genuine accumulation, i.e. the actual extension of production in department I, cannot be carried out.\" In other words, I(v+s) must cover: (i) the replacement of IIc; (ii) the additional constant capital that Department II must itself obtain from Department I in order to expand; and (iii) the additional means of production needed to expand Department I's own production. There is a minimum to this: the expansion of Department II's constant capital must be at least sufficient to allow Department I to expand. Marx then returns to the case with IIc < I(v+s). For IIc = 1,430, simple reproduction in II would require the same amount from I(v+s). What is novel is the supplementary 70 IIS. \"What is for department I simply the exchange of revenue for means of consumption, merely a commodity exchange oriented to consumption, is now for department II not just (as with simple reproduction) the transformation of its constant capital back from the form of commodity capital into its natural form, but rather the actual process of accumulation itself, the transformation of a part of its surplus product from the form of means of consumption into that of constant capital.\" If, however, Department I uses £70 in money (its money reserve for the exchange of surplus value) to buy the 70 IIs, and Department II does not use this money to buy 70 Is but accumulates the £70 as money capital, this would be the expression \"of an extra product (precisely of the surplus product of department II, of which it is an aliquot part), even if not of a product that goes back again into the production sphere; but then this money accumulation on the part of department II would be at the same time the expression of an unsaleable 70 Is in means of production. There would thus be relative over-production, corresponding to this simultaneous non-expansion of reproduction on the part of department II.\" Here again the dialectic: money-accumulation, if not matched by corresponding material transformation, is mere paper-accumulation. It is a signal of an unsold surplus product. Marx then adds the \"money in transition\" point. During the period in which the £70 in money that came from Department I has not yet returned to Department I by Department II's purchase of 70 Is, this £70 \"figures completely or in part as additional virtual money capital in the hands of department II.\" This holds for each transaction between the two departments until mutual replacement of commodities effects the reflux of money to its starting point. \"In the normal course of events, however, the money figures only temporarily here in this role.\" But then comes the crucial observation about the credit system: \"In the credit system, where all additional money temporarily set free can immediately function actively as additional money capital, this money capital that is only released temporarily may get stuck, and be used for new enterprises in department I, for example, whereas it ought to be used to set in motion surplus products still held down in other enterprises.\" This is a textbook illustration of the fictitious investment mechanism that is at the core of Marx's later theory of crisis: the credit system causes funds that are only temporarily free to masquerade as actual capital, and to be invested where they cannot be sustained. The note that \"the annexation of 70 Is to the constant capital of department II also requires an expansion of 14 in department II's variable capital\" closes the calculation. The main thread then continues with the further review of the calculation. The product of 9,000 is divided for purposes of reproduction as: I. 5,000c + 500s (to be capitalized) + 1,500(v+s) consumption fund = 7,000 in commodities. II. 1,500c + 299v + 201s = 2,000 in commodities. Total = 9,000 in commodity product. The capitalization proceeds: the 500s capitalized in Department I is divided into 5/6 = 417c + 1/6 = 83v. The 83v withdraws an equal amount from IIs, which buys elements of constant capital, added to IIc. An increase of 83 in IIc necessitates an increase of 1/5 × 83 = 17 in IIv. After the exchange: I. (5,000c + 417s)c + (1,000v + 83s)v = 5,417c + 1,083v = 6,500 II. (1,500c + 83s)c + (299v + 17s)v = 1,583c + 316v = 1,899 Total = 8,399. Department I capital has grown from 6,000 to 6,500, i.e. by one twelfth. Department II has grown from 1,715 to 1,899, i.e. by almost one ninth. Note: Department II's rate of growth exceeds Department I's rate of growth — confirming the earlier general observation that Department II must accumulate more quickly than Department I. Reproduction on this basis for a second year produces at end of year: I. (5,417c + 452s)c + (1,083v + 90s)v = 5,869c + 1,173v = 7,042 II. (1,583c + 42s + 90s)c + (316v + 8s + 18s)v = 1,715c + 342v = 2,057 End of third year product: I. 5,869c + 1,173v + 1,173s II. 1,715c + 342v + 342s. Marx accumulates half of the surplus value in Department I: I(v+½s) = 1,173v + 587(½s) = 1,760, which is bigger by 45 than the total IIc. This must again be balanced with IIc by transferring an equal amount of means of production to IIc. IIc thus grows by 45, which requires an increase of 1/5 × 45 = 9 in IIv. The capitalized 587 Is then divides into 5/6 and 1/6: 489c and 98v; this 98 requires a new addition of 98 to Department II's constant capital, and this in turn an increase in Department II's variable capital of 1/5 × 98 = 20. So: I. (5,869c + 489s)c + (1,173v + 98s)v = 6,358c + 1,271v = 7,629 II. (1,715c + 45s + 98s)c + (342v + 9s + 20s)v = 1,858c + 371v = 2,229 Total capital = 9,858. In three years of increased reproduction, therefore, total capital of Department I has grown from 6,000 to 7,629, that of Department II from 1,715 to 2,229, and the total social capital from 7,715 to 9,858. Again, the Department II rate of growth (30%) exceeds the Department I rate (27%). This repeated pattern is Marx's empirical/analytic demonstration of the structural asymmetry of expanded reproduction: the department producing means of consumption must expand relatively faster in order to accommodate the increased I(v+s) generated by the accumulation of Department I. (c) The Exchange of IIc in the Case of Accumulation The concluding subsection of Section III systematically catalogues the possible relations between I(v+s) and IIc under accumulation, distinguishing three cases based on the rate of accumulation . Marx assumes in the examples above a rate of accumulation in Department I of ½ (half of the surplus value) held constant across years, and varied only the proportions in which the accumulated capital was divided between c and v. This yields three examples. Case 1 : I(v+½s) = IIc, which is thus smaller than I(v+s). Marx notes parenthetically: \"(In fact, it must always be smaller or else department I cannot accumulate.)\" [The parenthetical note requires care. What must be smaller is I(v+½s) relative to I(v+s), since ½s < s, trivially. But the equality I(v+½s) = IIc is itself the condition under which Department I's half-accumulation exactly clears the market for IIc. If I(v+½s) were larger than IIc, Dept II could not absorb the additional means of consumption offered to it; hence the equality as a limiting case, and in fact the condition may be that I(v+½s) must be ≤ IIc for the process to proceed normally.] Case 2 : I(v+½s) is greater than IIc. \"In this case, the replacement is effected by a corresponding portion of IIS being added to IIc, so that this sum = I(v+½s).\" Here the exchange is not for Department II \"the simple reproduction of its constant capital, but already accumulation, the increase of its constant capital by a part of its surplus product which it exchanges for means of production from department I; this increase also means that department II correspondingly enlarges its variable capital out of its own surplus product.\" Case 3 : I(v+½s) is smaller than IIc. \"In this case, department II has not completely reproduced its constant capital by the exchange, and must therefore make up the deficiency by purchase from department I. But this does not require any further accumulation of variable capital in department II, since its constant capital is fully reproduced in magnitude by this operation. On the other hand, the section of capitalists in department I that is simply storing up additional money capital has already accomplished one part of this kind of accumulation by this exchange.\" Marx now generalises and states the impossibility of stable simple reproduction under capitalism. \"The precondition for simple reproduction, that I(v+s) = IIc, is incompatible with capitalist production from the start, although this does not rule out the possibility that in one year of the industrial cycle of ten to eleven years there may be a smaller total production than the preceding, i.e. that even simple reproduction fails to take place in relation to the previous year.\" This sentence embeds the schema in the industrial cycle : the schema's equality is the abstract equilibrium point, but real capitalism oscillates around it with periodic depressions and crises. Second, Marx makes a demographic argument: given the natural annual growth of the population, simple reproduction would mean that \"a proportionately greater number of unproductive servants had to share in the 1,500 that represents the total surplus-value. Accumulation of capital, i.e. genuine capitalist production, would be impossible in this way.\" This is a striking argument from the distribution of surplus value rather than from any pure value-flow condition. If all surplus value were consumed by a growing capitalist class plus its servants, there would be no accumulation; hence simple reproduction is incompatible with a growing population of surplus-value recipients. \"The existence of capitalist accumulation accordingly excludes the possibility that IIc may be equal to I(v+s).\" Yet the opposite error is also a real possibility: \"Yet even with capitalist accumulation, the case could arise in which, as a result of the accumulation achieved in the previous run of production periods, IIc was not only equal to I(v+s), but in fact even greater. This would mean over-production in department II, and could only be balanced out by a major crash, as a result of which capital would be transferred from department II to department I.\" This is the schematic version of Marx's crisis theory: over-accumulation in Department II relative to the demand from I(v+s) can only be resolved by a crisis that destroys capital and quantitatively redistributes it from II to I. Marx then qualifies the schema in two ways. (1) The relation between I(v+s) and IIc is unaffected if a section of Department II's constant capital is reproduced there — as in agriculture with home-grown seed. \"This part of IIc is then as little involved in the exchange between departments I and II as is Ic.\" (2) It also makes no difference if some Department II products can enter Department I as means of production; this is covered by a part of the means of production supplied by Department I, and this part must be deducted from both sides at the outset \"if we want to investigate the exchange between the two great departments of social production, the producers of means of production and the producers of means of consumption, in its pure and unadulterated form.\" The formal conclusion: \"In the case of capitalist production, therefore, I(v+s) cannot be equal to IIc, i.e. the two cannot balance one another in the exchange.\" But a weaker condition can hold: if Is/X is the part of Is spent by Department I's capitalists as revenue, then I(v + S/X) \"can be equal to, greater or less than IIc.\" The reason is that s/X < s, so I(v+S/X) < I(v+s), and the difference — the portion of Is that is capitalized — must be matched by a corresponding portion of IIs that Department II must forgo from its own consumption. The final clause reads (with the OCR garbled): \"however, I(v+S/X) must always be smaller than I(v+S-), and indeed smaller by the part of IIs that the capitalist class in department II must itself under all circumstances consume.\" The economic meaning: the consumer fund of Department I (v plus the spent portion of its surplus value) must always leave room for the portion of IIs that Department II's own capitalist class consumes; that is, the expansion of Department I's own consumption cannot be so great as to eliminate the consumption fund of Department II's capitalists. Marx then adds a methodological caveat about fixed capital. \"We have to note here that in this presentation of accumulation, the value of the constant capital is not depicted exactly in so far as this is a portion of the value of the commodity capital in whose production it collaborates.\" The fixed part of the newly accumulated constant capital only goes gradually and periodically into the commodity capital, according to the differential nature of these fixed elements. The commodity capital therefore consists, in cases involving raw materials and semi-finished goods, mostly of replacements for the circulating constant components and for the variable capital. Marx then notes: \"However, we were able to proceed in this manner on account of the turnover of the circulating components. The assumption was thereby made that the circulating part, together with the portion of value of the fixed capital surrendered to it, turns over within the year with such a frequency that the total sum of commodities supplied is the same as the value of the whole of the capital that goes into the annual production.\" But in industries where only ancillaries are involved (as in the construction of machinery), \"the labour element = v must again appear as the larger component of the commodity capital.\" This caveat is an acknowledgment that the schemas abstract from the complications of fixed capital turnover. For the rate of profit, the surplus value is calculated on the total capital, independently of whether the fixed components surrender much or little value to the product in a given period. But for the value of any commodity capital periodically produced, the fixed part of the constant capital is only to be counted to the extent that it gives up value by its average wear and tear. Section IV — Supplementary Remarks The final section of the chapter closes with the promised resolution of the money-origin problem in Department II. \"The original source of money for department II is the v+s of the gold production in department I, exchanged against part of IIc.\" This is the specific sense in which gold production is the ultimate money-source of the whole system. But: \"it is only to the extent that the gold producers store up surplus-value or transform it into means of production in department I, i.e. extend their production, that their v+s does not go into department II.\" Conversely: \"as far as accumulation of money by the gold producers themselves finally leads to expanded reproduction, a portion of the surplus-value from gold production that is not spent as revenue goes into department II for the gold producers' additional variable capital, and either requires a new hoard formation here or provides new means for buying from department I without directly selling to it again.\" From the money that stems from Iv+s in gold production, \"a part of the gold is deducted that is needed by certain branches of production in department II as raw material, etc., in short as a replacement element of their constant capital.\" (That is, some gold is consumed as industrial material and does not enter circulation as money.) Marx then specifies the circumstances under which hoard formation occurs in the process of exchange between the two departments : \"in department I, only if a part of Is is unilaterally sold to department II without a purchase from the other side, and serves here as additional constant capital for department II; in department II, if department I buys unilaterally for additional variable capital; furthermore, if a part of the surplus-value spent by department I as revenue is not covered by department II, so that a part of IIS is bought with it and thereby transformed into money.\" \"If I(v+s) is greater than IIc, then IIc, for its simple reproduction, does not have to replace, in commodities from department I, what I consumed from IIs.\" The question then arises as to how far hoard formation can take place in the exchange among the capitalists of Department II themselves — \"an exchange that can consist only of the mutual exchange of IIs.\" Marx answers: \"We know that within department II there is only direct accumulation in so far as a part of IIS is directly transformed into variable capital (just as, in department I, a part of Is is directly transformed into constant capital).\" This is the promised resolution. The partial answer is that the means of subsistence that constitute the extra portion of IIs — the portion serving as additional variable capital — are directly consumed by the workers who are hired with the additional money capital; the money capital that the hoard-forming Department II capitalists provide does not return to them because it has been paid to them as the price of commodities by workers employed with the additional capital of other Department II capitalists. The paragraph continues: \"Given the different ages of accumulation within the various lines of business in department II, and within each particular line of business for the individual capitalists involved there, the matter is explained, mutatis mutandis, just as in department I. Some of these capitalists are still at the stage of hoard formation, selling without buying, while others, at the point of actual expansion of reproduction, buy without selling.\" The final sentences of the chapter: \"The additional variable money capital is certainly laid out at first on additional labour-power, but this is used to buy means of subsistence from the hoard-forming proprietors of the extra means of consumption that go into the workers' consumption. In proportion to their hoard formation, this money does not return from these proprietors to its starting-point; they store it up.\" Thus the chapter closes on a note of unfinished resolution — the intra-departmental hoard formation in Department II is explained, but only by the principle of successive ages of accumulation : some capitalists hoard while others accumulate, and the money of the latter flows into the hoards of the former as wages are spent on the latter's stock of means of consumption. This is the same complementarity of one-sided movements that Marx applied to Department I. In the process, however, the chapter leaves the reader with the strong impression that the analysis is subject to important further qualification in what would have followed (in Volume 3's treatment of the credit system). The \"sudden interruption\" of the manuscript — noted by Engels in his editorial apparatus — reflects the incompleteness of the analytic resolution. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS: Fundamental principles revealed by the chapter. First principle: Money is not the substance of accumulation. The hoard that appears at the level of individual capital as \"potential money capital\" is not an addition to social wealth. It is money withdrawn from circulation and given a new function — as reserve against future investment. The extra gold produced is the only true increment to the money supply. This demolishes the \"currency-quantity\" and \"forced saving\" views of capital formation. Second principle: Expanded reproduction is a matter of arrangement, not of quantity. The material substratum of expanded reproduction is already produced in the course of simple reproduction: it is the surplus product of Department I cast in the use-form of means of production of means of production. What changes to bring about accumulation is not the total value of the annual product but the destination of its elements. \"It is not the quantity, but the qualitative character of the given elements of simple reproduction that is changed.\" This is the single deepest theoretical insight of the chapter. It means that the pivot between simple and expanded reproduction is not a quantitative fulcrum but a qualitative rearrangement — one that \"can never be achieved without difficulty.\" Third principle: The condition of expanded reproduction is I(v+s) > IIc. This is the formal core of the reproduction schemas. In simple reproduction I(v+s) = IIc; in expanded reproduction the two cannot be equal, because Department I must retain a portion of its surplus product as additional constant capital (which cannot simultaneously be exchanged for means of consumption), and must also provide Department II with the material of its own additional constant capital. The two departments are bound by a reciprocity of materials : Department I supplies the constant capital of Department II; Department II supplies the means of subsistence for Department I's additional workers. Fourth principle: The accumulation process is asymmetric. Department II must accumulate faster than Department I, because I(v+s), which has to be exchanged for IIc, grows more quickly than IIc unless Department II accelerates its own capital formation. This asymmetry — structurally necessary, not accidental — is a key to understanding the cyclical overshooting of consumer-goods industries, the periodic over-production of Department II, and the \"major crash\" by which over-production in Department II is overcome and capital is transferred from II to I. Fifth principle: The balance is an accident. \"The very complexity of the process provides many occasions for it to take an abnormal course.\" The equilibrium conditions of the schemas — one-sided purchases equal one-sided sales, the money refluxes, the balance is restored — are not automatic. They hold only as an \"accident\" on the basis of the spontaneous pattern of capitalist production. Every balancing act is the potential site of a crisis. Sixth principle: The illusion of \"accumulation at the expense of consumption\" must be rejected. The chapter's most categorical statement of this point is worth repeating: \"the idea that accumulation is achieved at the expense of consumption — considered in this general way — is an illusion that contradicts the essence of capitalist production, in as much as it assumes that the purpose and driving motive of this is consumption, and not the grabbing of surplus-value and its capitalization, i.e. accumulation.\" The numerical exercises demonstrate that accumulation raises the consumption fund of the capitalist class (1,100 → 1,477 in the first example) — the point is that consumption is subordinate to accumulation, not that accumulation sacrifices consumption. Seventh principle: The credit system is a secondary reflection. The spontaneous processes of metallic circulation — one-sided sales, hoards, refluxes — are the primary phenomena. The credit system presents them as \"consciously regulated processes,\" endowing the hoards with an apparent activity that is parasitic on real accumulation. The chapter's warning about temporarily free money being \"stuck\" in new enterprises — while surplus products are \"held down\" — is the germ of the theory of fictitious capital. Broader practical, theoretical, and philosophical implications. Theoretically , the chapter establishes that the reproduction of capital is a closed but unstable system. It is closed in the sense that its value-forms and use-forms are mutually convertible within the system — no external wealth is required for accumulation. It is unstable in the sense that the equilibrium conditions are so complex that the balance is an accident. This dual character is the basis for Marx's mature theory of crisis, and it accounts for the fact that unlike the Grundrisse and Volume 3's discussion of the \"general possibility of crisis,\" this chapter identifies the specific mechanism of breakdown: the multiplication of one-sided metamorphoses, each with its own timing, its own reflux conditions, and its own vulnerability to being short-circuited. Historically , the chapter became one of the most contested sites of twentieth-century Marxist theory. The reproduction schemas were pressed into service by Rosa Luxemburg (to argue that capitalism requires external non-capitalist markets for its realisation), by Tugan-Baranovsky (to argue the opposite — that capitalism can achieve balanced growth indefinitely, and that crisis is not systemic), by Otto Bauer (to model imperialist expansion), by Lenin (to refute both Narodnik underconsumptionism and the \"third-person\" theories of realisation), and latterly by Ernest Mandel, David Harvey, and Roman Rosdolsky. The 70 IIs problem and the I(v+s) > IIc condition became central points in the debate over whether the schemas are equilibrium models or crisis-generating models. Practically , the chapter provides the analytic foundations for understanding: (1) the role of credit in mobilising idle hoards; (2) the structural sources of cyclical crisis in the mismatch between Department I's investment and Department II's capacity to absorb means of consumption; (3) the political economy of workers' consumption, and the impossibility of \"making a market\" out of cultural uplift while wages are held down; (4) the difference between genuine capital accumulation and money-capital inflation; (5) the structural dependency of Department II on Department I's rate of accumulation. Philosophically , the chapter vindicates the dialectical method. Simple reproduction and expanded reproduction are not two separate states but moments of one process — expanded reproduction contains within it simple reproduction as its material substratum. Similarly, Department I and Department II are not two independent systems but two poles of a single process of mutual material provisioning. The \"difficulty\" one encounters in Department II (the 500 IIc problem) is not a refutation of the schema but the visible form of its condition. The chapter thus exhibits the dialectical logic of Marx's Capital at its most rigorous: the form of the argument — the internal self-differentiation of a single unity into distinct moments that must nevertheless be reconciled — is not a stylistic flourish but the actual structure of the object being analysed. Finally , the chapter carries a warning for our own moment. The argument that money capital can be created out of nothing but a rearrangement of existing value; that credit systems turn temporarily free money into a potency that may turn out to be fictitious; that the whole process is subject to \"an abnormal course\" because the balancing acts require an \"accident\"; and that accumulation's true purpose is not consumption but the self-valorisation of value — all this reads with the uncomfortable freshness of a diagnosis rather than a historical relic. Marx's Chapter 21 does not merely model nineteenth-century capitalism; it specifies the necessary structure of any capitalism that reproduces itself on an expanding scale. Its account of why the world economy periodically overshoots, gets stuck, and crashes — because accumulation generates its own disequilibria along with its own growth — remains one of the most powerful analytic instruments we possess. ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 20 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Introduction"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Introduction", "title": "Introduction", "subtitle": "Introduction to Volume 3", "path": "Volume 3/Introduction/Introduction original_explanation.html", "word_count": 25773, "read_time": "129 min read", "tags": ["Cost Price & Rate of Profit", "Transformation Problem & Production Prices", "Dialectical Method & Critique of Political Economy"], "keywords": ["Production as a Whole Overview", "Concrete Forms of Capital at the Surface of Society", "Marx's Synthesis of Volume 1 and Volume 2", "Engels' Editing and the Value-Price Debate", "Unified Dialectic of Value, Profit, and Distribution"], "text": "Introduction Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 25,533 words ⏱️ ~128 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Chapter's Core Problem, Thesis, and Intellectual Objective This ~33,000-word Introduction, written by the Belgian Marxist economist Ernest Mandel for the Penguin (Pelican Marx Library) edition of Karl Marx's Capital , Volume 3, is not a conventional editor's preface. It is a sustained, polemical, and theoretically dense defense of Volume 3's analytical architecture from the standpoint of what Mandel calls \"mature dialectical method.\" Its central thesis can be stated bluntly: Volume 3 of Capital is the most controversial of the three volumes because it is the volume that answers the question \"Whither capitalism?\" — and Marx's answer (the system is immanently crisis-ridden, unable to overcome its contradictions without abolishing private property, competition, profit and the market economy) is intellectually intolerable both to capitalists and their apologists and to \"neutral\" academic economists and reformists. The controversy, therefore, is not primarily a logical problem about mathematics; it is a socially and politically motivated resistance to an unpalatable conclusion. Mandel's intellectual objective is threefold. First, he wants to reconstruct the \"fundamental logic of Capital \" by integrating Volume 3 into a unified structure with Volumes 1 and 2, arguing (via a schematic diagram , pp. 14–15) that the whole work forms one coherent architecture moving from the simple value-form up to the tendency toward collapse of the capitalist system. Second, he wants to defend Marx's specific doctrines — especially the equalization of the rate of profit , the transformation of values into prices of production , the tendency of the average rate of profit to decline (TRPF) , and the theory of surplus profits/ground rent — against three main waves of criticism: (a) the von Bortkiewicz/Sraffa \"transformation problem\" critique (which Mandel sorts into the \"feedback controversy\" and the \"monetary confusion\"), (b) the Okishio/neo-Ricardian charge that TRPF is theoretically or empirically false, and (c) the various \"mono-causal\" theories of crisis (disproportionality, under-consumption, over-accumulation) which Mandel thinks are each partially right but must be integrated into a multi-causal theory. Third, and most ambitiously, Mandel uses the Introduction as a vehicle to articulate his own theoretical synthesis — incorporating long waves, the concept of \"technological rent,\" and a reformulated collapse theory based on automation and the absolute decline of value-production — all while insisting that these extensions remain faithful to Marx's method and conclusions. How this Chapter Fits into the Broader Subject and Theoretical Framework Capital Volume 3 occupies a specific place in Marx's six-book plan: it is the volume that deals with \"the process of capitalist production as a whole\" , i.e., the unity of production (Volume 1) and circulation (Volume 2). Volume 1 analyzes the factory and the origin of surplus-value; Volume 2 analyzes the market place and realization , laying out the reproduction schemas and demonstrating that capitalism cannot achieve steady \"enlarged reproduction\" — equilibrium is a product of constantly reappearing disequilibria, growth necessarily takes the form of the industrial cycle, and periodic crises of over-production are inevitable. Neither volume, however, works out \"the precise way in which these contradictions… are interrelated\" so that the system's laws of motion lead to explosive crises and collapse. That is precisely the task of Volume 3 — even though, as Mandel stresses, Marx left the volume unfinished (most gravely, Part Seven with only a barely-started Chapter 52 on social classes) and he never completed the projected volumes on the world market, competition, the industrial cycle, and the state, which he had explicitly postponed. This incompleteness is, for Mandel, the key reason the controversies have been so persistent. But the basic reason is social and political: Volume 3 tells capitalists, their \"hangers-on\", \"neutral\" economists who tacitly presuppose the permanence of commodity production, and philanthropic social reformers that the system breeds mass poverty and destitution in a way that cannot be cured without abolishing the system itself. The Introduction therefore situates itself simultaneously inside the history of economic theory (against Bohm-Bawerk, Bortkiewicz, Sweezy, Sraffa, Steedman, Okishio) and inside the history of the international labour movement (from the Second International \"orthodox\" Marxism of Kautsky and Bernstein's revisionism, through Luxemburg, Bukharin, Varga, Grossmann, to \"state monopoly capitalism\" theory and Euro-communism). Mandel's structural position is that of a revolutionary Trotskyist defending Marx's classical conclusions against both bourgeois critics and \"econimist\" or reformist distortions inside the workers' movement — and willing, where he thinks Marx was silent or insufficiently concrete, to extend the theory himself. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Architecture of Volume 3: Revenues, Classes, and the \"Intermediate Steps\" Mandel opens by anchoring Volume 3 in a concrete analytical problem, not in an abstract principle. Volume 1 solved the problem of the origin of the two basic categories of revenue, wages and profits: surplus-value is produced only by living labour, purchased with the variable portion of capital, not the constant portion (machinery, raw materials, buildings, energy). Volume 3 does not re-open that question. What it explains is distribution : how specific sectors of the ruling class participate in the division of the total mass of surplus-value , and how these specific economic categories are regulated. Mandel enumerates four ruling-class beneficiary groups — industrial capitalists; commercial capitalists; bankers; capitalist landowners — yielding five categories of revenue: wages; industrial profits; commercial (and banking) profits; interest; land rent. These are then re-grouped by Marx into the three classical categories wages, profits, land rent . This tri-partite division matters because it is the analytical ground on which Marx will construct his demonstration that the class structure reproduces itself through distribution — an argument Mandel returns to in the very last substantive section. But to get there, Mandel insists, \"a whole series of intermediate steps have to be taken,\" and these steps are the spine of the volume: The rate of profit must be distinguished as a separate analytical category from the rate of surplus-value (r = s/v distinguished from s/(c+v)). The tendency toward equalization of the rate of profit between all capitals must be discovered — a discovery whose \"discovery\" aspect is critical because it is what breaks the naive, direct correspondence between surplus-value produced and profit realized. From these two conceptual innovations Marx deduces the centre-piece of the volume: the tendency of the average rate of profit to decline , absent countervailing tendencies. Profit itself then subdivides into entrepreneurial profit (industry, transport, trade) and interest — that portion accruing to owners of money-capital who merely lend. Finally, surplus profit is introduced — surplus-value excluded from the general equalization — and its most fully studied special case is land rent . Here Mandel makes one of his characteristic interpretive moves: he argues that Part Six on ground rent is really about \"the more general problem of monopoly giving rise to surplus profit \", and that Marx in Chapters 10 and 14 explicitly treats land rent as but a special case. The claim, then, is that Marx \"anticipates the whole contemporary theory of monopoly prices and profits,\" and does so with \"greater clarity as to their origins than\" the twentieth-century academic economists (Joe Bain, Sylos-Labini, Dorfman) who have tried to solve the same puzzle. This is not a minor flourish; it is the connective tissue that allows Mandel, later in the chapter, to extend \"differential land rent\" into mineral rents and technological rents as expressions of the same underlying logic. Mandel draws attention to a striking absence — the unfinished Part Seven and particularly the barely-initiated Chapter 52 on social classes. He hypothesizes that it would have supplied \"a vital link between the economic content of the class struggle between capital and labour… and its overall economic outcome,\" partly sketched in Volume 3's Chapters 11 and 15. The subtitle \"The Process of Capitalist Production in its Totality\" is therefore, he suggests, aspirational: the \"totality\" actually includes circulation (per Volume 2) plus the world market, competition, the industrial cycle, and the state — all of which were in Marx's plan, all of which there is no indication he abandoned, and all of which were \"alas unwritten.\" This incompleteness is not used by Mandel as an excuse; it is used as the diagnostic key explaining why the controversies (especially about crisis, which Marx never treated in one place) have been so durable. The Equalization of the Rate of Profit: Mechanism, Labour Theory of Value, and the \"Three Series of Problems\" Mandel next turns to the analytical mechanism that underlies everything else: the equalization of the rate of profit . He begins by reconstructing the Volume 1 result. Surplus-value is produced only by living labour — i.e., by the fraction of capital spent on labour-power, which Marx calls variable capital (v) ; the fraction spent on buildings, machinery, raw materials, energy is constant capital (c) . From this, one might naively conclude that \"the greater the proportion of capital which each industrial branch spends on wages, the higher its rate of profit\" — since the rate of profit is the relation between surplus-value produced and total capital invested (c+v). This naive conclusion is rejected on two grounds, both of which recur throughout the Introduction. First, it \"would contradict the basic logic of the capitalist mode of production, which consists of expansion, growth, enlarged reproduction, through a substitution of living by dead labour\" — that is, through a rising organic composition of capital (c/v, or more precisely the value-composition of capital in so far as it reflects the technical composition) . This rising organic composition is itself driven by two forces: (a) capitalist competition , in which cost-price reduction depends on ever more efficient, labour-saving machinery, and (b) the class struggle — because the only long-run way accumulation can avoid labour shortage and thus a rising real wage (which would sharply reduce the rate of surplus-value) is by piling up fixed constant capital, i.e., substituting machinery for living labour. Second, empirical evidence \"overwhelmingly confirms\" that labour-intensive branches do not normally earn higher rates of profit. From these premises Marx draws his conclusion: in a fully developed and normally functioning capitalist economy, each industrial branch does not directly receive the surplus-value produced by the wage-labour it employs . It receives only a fraction of the total surplus-value produced by productive labour, proportional to the fraction it represents of all capital expended. Concretely: Branches with below-average organic composition (more labour, more v relative to total capital) do not realize all the surplus-value their own workers produce; part of it is transferred to branches with above-average organic composition. Branches with above-average organic composition receive more surplus-value than is directly produced by \"their\" wage-labourers. Only branches whose individual organic composition exactly equals the social average receive exactly their own produced surplus-value, with no transfers in or out. This is the material basis of the common interest of all capital-owners in the exploitation of labour , which thereby takes the form of a collective class exploitation — with inter-capitalist competition merely deciding how the total mass is redistributed. This formulation is crucial for the later section on capitalist class solidarity. Mandel then identifies \"three series of problems\" that this process of equalization raises: What is its relation to the labour theory of value in general? What are the concrete mechanisms by which equalization occurs in real life? What is the \"technical\" solution to the transformation of values into prices of production — where \"price of production\" = production costs (capital outlays) + average profit multiplied by those outlays? He observes that the first two have provoked relatively less controversy than the third, \"probably because of their more 'abstract' character,\" yet they are \"of the highest importance for the inner cohesion of Marxist economic theory,\" and Marx's treatment shows his \"dialectical method at its most mature.\" Problem 1: Relation to the Labour Theory of Value Marx's argument, as Mandel reconstructs it, is that because value is in the last analysis a social, not an individual, category , the below-average-organic-composition branches objectively waste social labour from the standpoint of capitalist society as a whole (i.e., from the standpoint of \"equality\" of commodity-owners). Therefore the market does not return to their owners the full value created. Inversely, above-average-organic-composition branches (with above-average social productivity of labour) objectively economize socially necessary labour , and are rewarded by receiving a higher proportion of all surplus-value than their workers directly produce. Value realization and value production are being carefully separated. Counter-argument 1 (productivity comparability): \"Is productivity of labour comparable in different branches… inasmuch as these do not produce goods that are interchangeable?\" Mandel's answer is dynamic : compare the rates of increase of productivity of labour across branches over time . More generally, the specific organic composition of capital in each branch, constantly shifting as productivity changes, \"can be considered as a general index, a means of measurement , of social productivity of labour.\" He quotes Marx (p. 318 below) to the effect that the progressive decline of variable capital relative to constant capital is \"identical with the progressively rising organic composition… of the social capital as a whole… just another expression for the progressive development of the social productivity of labour.\" In a market economy of constant technical revolution, demand shifts, and capital flux, Mandel says, this assumption is \"both theoretically tenable and empirically verifiable.\" Counter-argument 2 (the alleged contradiction between value-production and value-transfer): \"But is there not a basic contradiction between considering all labour effectively expended… as value-producing, and at the same time explaining the transfers of value (surplus-value)… as a function of objective waste or economy of social labour?\" Mandel, appealing to Rubin's Essays on Marx's Theory of Value , insists no . Value as the embodiment of abstract human labour is \"not a problem of measurement, of numeraire, but a problem of essence.\" Each community has a total labour potential — the number of producers × socially accepted average annual work-days × work-hours. This potential is an objective social fact in a given country and time-frame (year). From it flows the total value produced during the year. Socially necessary labour , which treats some labour-hours as \"wasted\" and hence unaccounted for socially, \"only implies redistribution of value inside each branch of production, except in cases of monopoly.\" Extending this to the economy as a whole changes nothing: all labour actually expended in production has been value-producing; it cannot be made larger or smaller by anything occurring outside the sphere of production. The problem of compensation on the market is a problem of distribution , not of production . Mandel's dialectical formulation: it is \"perfectly possible that actually expended private labour in a given branch, at the average rate of productivity of that branch, is socially necessary labour and has really produced value , while at the same time the owners of the commodities in which it is embodied do not receive full compensation on the market for all that embodied value, or receive a counter-value higher than the amount of value embodied in their commodities.\" The mediating concept is that total value equals total prices of production — an equal sum of labour-hours/weeks/years, an equal total labour potential. What is modified on the market, at the inter-branch level, is exclusively \"a problem of (re)distribution of value, not one of production of value.\" He cites the crucial passage (p. 774 below) on the law of value as it makes itself felt \"not in relation to the individual commodities… but rather to the total products at a given time of particular spheres of social production.\" Mandel notes an internal Marxist debate here: he sides with Rubin against Catherine Colliot-Thélène , who in her Afterword to Rubin's A History of Economic Thought asserts that Marx's double definition of \"socially necessary labour\" (by average branch productivity and by the relation of branch output to socially recognized needs) involves a contradiction. Mandel retorts: \"Where Colliot-Thélène sees a contradiction, there is in fact a difference — between value production, which is strictly limited to the sphere of production, and value realization, which occurs in the sphere of circulation and depends inter alia upon relations between the structure of production and the structure of demand.\" The law of value adapts the distribution of the labour force to social needs post festum , because under commodity production this cannot be done a priori . This does not imply that labour expended was not value-producing; it means \"value produced has been redistributed: that the equivalent of some of it is not received by those who own the commodities thus produced.\" He also gently rebukes Maurice Dobb for treating labour as a numeraire . Problem 2: The Concrete Mechanism of Equalization Here Mandel is at his most economically concrete and most insistent on dialectical method. Equalization is always a tendency, never a permanent reality . His exposition proceeds from a simplifying assumption, then relaxes it. Step 1 (fixed demand structure): Starting from actual realization of the total surplus-value produced in each branch, branches with lower organic composition (more wages relative to total outlays) will show a higher rate of profit than branches with higher organic composition. All else equal (above all: assuming no change in the distribution of total demand for the different use-values), this above-average rate of profit attracts additional capital . Additional capital raises supply above social demand → prices fall → the rate of profit falls. Conversely, in above-average-organic-composition branches, the initially below-average rate of profit causes capital withdrawal → production declines below social demand → prices rise → profits rise to the social average. It is the flux and reflux of capital between branches that is the driving force. Moreover, this flux and reflux \"is indeed the main way in which capital accumulation (growth) occurs in actual life,\" i.e., as an uneven process — branches never grow at the same rhythm or over the same time spans. Hence equalization \"presupposes their relative inequality\": it \"is a process which constantly realizes itself by negating itself.\" Empirical confirmation, Mandel says, is available to anyone studying the real history of capitalist branches of industry, mining and transport. Step 2 (weakening the assumption of initial great unevenness): Mandel concedes that the process does not require great initial unevenness in profit rates, nor that higher rates of profit coincide with greater labour intensity. \"It would be sufficient to assume a single initial situation of that kind to make the process perfectly logical and coherent.\" In fact, very early in industrial capitalism the average rate of profit becomes a known entity (bank credit and the stock exchange playing a \"not unimportant role\" here; cf. p. 311 below). The real process is therefore less one of capital flowing from below-average to above-average branches, and more one of firms seeking \"surplus profits\" over and above the known average rate of profit , essentially through revolutionary innovations (possibly creating entirely new branches). The average rate of profit is \"constantly shaken and re-established by the reactions which this constant revolution in the technique of production and the organization of labour provokes.\" Each firm trying to maximize its own rate of profit contributes, \"independently of its wishes and designs,\" to the tendential equalization of the rate of profit. This is a cornerstone of Mandel's anti-Okishio argument later. He adds a footnote (n. 14) giving a concrete historical example: canal-building and mining firms in the early industrial revolution may have had higher rates of profit than the first textile mills due to lower organic composition. Step 3 (abandoning stable demand): If we drop the simplifying assumption of stable demand in a given time-span, \"we only have to introduce additional mediations; the result remains substantially the same.\" Two sub-cases: (a) If a below-average-organic-composition branch also benefits from above-average growth of social demand, prices decline more slowly despite the influx of capital — \"but this will only attract even more additional capital, until equalization finally occurs.\" (b) If such a branch is relatively 'older' and suffering decline of total demand, the influx of capital + rising output leads more rapidly to falling prices/profits and final equalization. For branches losing capital due to initially lower profits, the reasoning is the obvious counterpart. Mandel also records a subtle point of Marx's (n. 16): the relative weight of firms operating at above-/average-/below-average productivity within a branch can temporarily mean that it is not the average level of productivity which determines the commodity's value — but \"competition will rapidly do away with such situations, in the absence of structural scarcity or monopoly.\" Problem 3: The Transformation of Values into Prices of Production — The Feedback Controversy The third series of problems — the \"'technical' problem of the transformation of values into prices of production for each specific commodity\" — is by far the most contentious, and Mandel divides the critical literature into two bodies: the feedback controversy and the monetary confusion . The feedback controversy originated with the Prussian statistician Ladislaus von Bortkiewicz . His claim: in Marx's Chapter 9 solution of the transformation, only the values of currently produced commodities (outputs) are transformed, not the values of input-commodities — so Marx is accused of inconsistency, computing inputs in values and outputs in prices of production. A stream of authors — \"some claiming to be Marxists, others obviously adhering to other economic doctrines\" — has repeated the charge. Mandel cites the \"obvious\" textual evidence often quoted from Chapter 9: the passage in which Marx writes that \"the development given above also involves a modification in the determination of a commodity's cost price… if the cost price of a commodity is equated with the value of the means of production used up in producing it, it is always possible to go wrong.\" Mandel, however, insists this quotation \"should not be made to say more than it does.\" It says only that mixing value-calculations in inputs and price-of-production-calculations in outputs is \"likely to arrive at numerically erroneous conclusions\" — which is \"rather obvious, since the whole analysis precisely concerns the deviation of prices of production from values.\" Crucially, the extract does not imply that prices of production of inputs should be calculated within the same time-span as prices of production of outputs . The passage immediately following the one Bortkiewicz quotes explicitly rejects this interpretation: \"Our present investigation does not require us to go into further detail on this point. It still remains correct that the cost price of commodities is always smaller than their value… The cost price of the commodity is a given precondition, independent of his, the capitalist's, production…\" (Mandel's italics). And even more clearly, from Volume 3 p. 266: in all periods shorter than that required for a change in the general rate of profit to consolidate, \"a change in prices of production is always to be explained prima facie by an actual change in commodity values, i.e. by a change in the total sum of labour-time needed to produce the commodities.\" Mandel draws the logical conclusion: inputs in current cycles are data , given at the start of the cycle, and do not have a feedback effect on the equalization of the rates of profit in various branches during that cycle . It suffices to assume such inputs are also calculated in prices of production — but these prices of production result from the equalization of rates of profit during the previous cycle. \"For any inconsistency to disappear.\" This dissolves the von Bortkiewicz charge. But Mandel immediately anticipates the obvious objection: could one not argue that raw-material prices fluctuate constantly, changing many times during a year, so that feedback effects do occur and part of the inputs participate in the current (annual) redistribution of surplus-value? His reply is emphatic: No. Prices of production of raw materials, like all other inputs bought by capitalists currently occupied in production, are \"unchangeable data\" — they \"cannot vary through ups or downs of current production of surplus-value, or current changes in the organic composition of capital occurring during a given year.\" Capitalists pay a given price, which does not change a posteriori as a function of what is occurring during a year in final surplus-value redistribution. And the argument holds even if capitalists buy raw materials currently rather than at year's beginning, and even if one assumes away all pre-existing stocks. Why? Because the formation of prices of production — i.e., the calculation of the average rate of profit — is not a constantly moving process . It is linked to the overall realization of surplus-value of all (most) currently produced commodities. There must be a minimum time-span before one can speak of a new average rate of profit replacing a previous one. Mandel even says an assumption of annual change \"is probably an exaggeration rather than an underestimate.\" Thus currently purchased raw materials, even on a quarterly or monthly basis, \"do not fundamentally change the prices of production (average rate of profit), as resulting from the capital movements which had occurred during the previous year.\" One must not confuse formation of prices of production (a redistribution of the total surplus-value produced for society as a whole) with current fluctuations of market prices , which Marx explicitly excludes. The reason for this relative rigidity (rigidity of prices of production = rigidity of average rates of profit in a country) is grounded in the very nature of the processes producing equalization: determination of the total mass of surplus-value (surplus labour), and the fluxes and refluxes of capital — large-scale capital movements between branches, determining changes and differences in the organic composition both of productive sectors as a whole and of each sector separately. \"Such overall social movements cannot vary from quarter to quarter, let alone from month to month.\" The relative indivisibility of fixed capital is itself a \"formidable obstacle to such broad movements under advanced capitalist conditions, except in the case of radical devalorization of capital under conditions of severe crisis.\" Mandel's meta-conclusion: \"Not only is Marx theoretically consistent when he assumes prices of production of inputs resulting from equalization movements in different time-spans… This also corresponds much more closely to the real, empirically verifiable operation of the capitalist system as we know it than does the opposite assumption of von Bortkiewicz and his followers.\" Against Alternative \"Solutions\" and Mathematical Models Mandel briefly surveys attempts to solve or \"correct\" the transformation problem: J. Winternitz (who sought a solution preserving total prices of production = total value), and more recently Anwar Shaikh (who uses an \"iterative method\" rather than simultaneous equations). Mandel does not endorse any mathematical model as such; he argues that \"mathematical models cannot, in and of themselves, 'solve' theoretical problems. They can only formalize interrelations previously understood as such.\" Many authors of such models, he charges, \"silently assume correlations which have not been previously proved or empirically tested.\" Their equations may be mathematically consistent but theoretically wrong if they don't correspond to a meaningful representation of the problem supposedly being solved. Two examples are given: The Okishio theorem (N. Okishio, 1961, 1963): the author puts fixed capital \"between brackets altogether\" to draw conclusions about the trend of the rate of profit. But if the growth of fixed capital is one of the main determinants of TRPF, the theorem proves nothing. The von Bortkiewicz \"solution\" of the transformation problem (accepted by Sweezy, Sraffa, Seton, and many others): besides assuming uniform profits for all products — not all branches or firms, \"which is quite another story\" — it assumes that only those equations involving commodities entering into production of other commodities are needed. It is then logical that the organic composition of department III (whose commodities do not enter the reproduction process) does not influence the average rate of profit. But this tells us nothing about department III in Marx's analysis , where such a distinction is explicitly excluded , and \"especially about what happens in the really functioning capitalist economy, i.e., in real life.\" Mandel underlines with an example: to say that the organic composition of the armaments industry , including its size, is \"immaterial to the real rate of profit of a real capitalist economy\" is \"quite untenable — especially if one takes a look at the size of that department in, say, 1943 in Germany or 1944 in the U.S.A.\" (The insight about department III he credits to Emmanuel Farjoun of the Hebrew University of Jerusalem.) The Monetary Confusion The second line of attack on Marx's transformation solution involves confusing prices of production with market prices , and more generally importing questions about the expression of values as prices, i.e., money . Mandel singles out Paul Sweezy (in The Theory of Capitalist Development , 1942) for having perpetuated this confusion in his take-over of von Bortkiewicz's critique, and Ian Steedman (in Marx after Sraffa , 1977) for following in his footsteps. Marx himself, Mandel insists, is crystal clear that prices of production do not concern market prices. The very title of Chapter 9 specifies the transformation of values of commodities into prices of production . Values are quantities of labour , and have nothing to do with money prices as such. Equalization of the rate of profit occurs through the transfer of quantities of surplus-value from one branch to another — and surplus-value too is a quantity of labour (surplus labour), not money. Mandel notes that he deliberately omitted a crucial sentence in the earlier citation and will now use it to eliminate all doubt: \"We are not referring here, of course, to a mere change in the monetary expression of these values\" (p. 266). If the problem does not concern monetary expression of values , it ipso facto does not concern monetary expression of prices of production. Yes, Marx does introduce market prices and the influence of competition — but in Chapter 10 , immediately following his transformation solution in Chapter 9 — and there he clearly and explicitly distinguishes fluctuations of market prices and of monetary expressions of value from the fluctuations in the average rate of profit which determine changes in prices of production. Engels, Mandel adds (n. 28), explicitly envisages the case where the total sum of money profits resulting from market prices is lower than total surplus-value produced, because value has declined due to rising productivity (letter to Conrad Schmidt, 12 March 1895). This corroborates that monetary and value-level accounts are on different planes. Behind the confusion lies an insufficient understanding of Marx's theory of money . Money (gold) is treated by Marx as a special commodity with its own \"intrinsic\" value; only for this reason can it serve as general equivalent for the exchange-value of all other commodities. It follows immediately that fluctuations of market (monetary) prices may always be the result of a dual movement : changes in the value of a commodity and changes in the value of the money-commodity (gold). But changes in the intrinsic value of the money-commodity have identical effects on the market prices of all other commodities — hence they cannot change mutual exchange relations (relative prices) . Paper money does not alter this. Inflation of paper money means only that an increasing amount of paper dollars, pounds, etc. represents the same quantity (e.g., one ounce) of gold. What is true for the money expression of value is likewise true for the money expression of prices of production, which concern only a redistribution of quantities of surplus-value between branches. Mandel now draws out the \"real\" empirical problem that he thinks would arise if one insisted on inputs being expressed in market prices (which is what capitalists actually face): \"The 'inputs' in the reproduction tables could only be treated as inputs in really occurring capitalist production… if they were expressed in market prices, and not in prices of production.\" The real issue would be: how to \"transform\" values not into prices of production, but first into prices of production and then into market prices — where the second stage would have to involve real monetary problems, specifically \"the interrelationship between the average value of commodities and the average value of gold.\" The theoretical stakes, Mandel concludes, are: does the \"transformation problem\" concern an immediate move from essence to appearance (i.e., to day-to-day production and circulation)? Or — as Mandel \"would strongly maintain\" — is it only a mediating link in the process of cognition, which does not yet deal with immediately verifiable empirical data, i.e., market prices? To make the conceptual relations clear, Mandel inserts a diagram (p. 30), which can be reconstructed in prose: For a specific commodity : individual value (quantity of labour contained in that unit) → market value (socially average socially necessary labour contained in it) → price of production (socially necessary labour modified by equalization of rates of profit between branches = average costs of production in each branch + average profit of all branches). For the money-commodity (gold) : individual value (quantity of labour contained in the product of each specific gold mine) → market value (quantity of labour contained in gold actually produced under the lowest conditions of productivity) → price of production (total costs of production in all gold mines + average profit, divided by total output). \"There is obviously no 'market price' of gold, as this would mean the value of gold expressed in gold.\" Then: 'intrinsic' market prices of commodities (prices of production expressed in quantities of gold, of money) → 'fluctuating' market prices of commodities (prices of production expressed in quantities of gold and modified under very short-term fluctuations of supply and demand, i.e., fluctuating around the 'axis' of intrinsic market prices = prices of production = values). Finally, operation of the law of value under capitalism : fluctuations of 'intrinsic' market prices, other than very short-term ones , are determined by relative value movements (relative increases or decreases of productivity of labour in the output of a given commodity and in the output of gold), mediated through deviations from the average rate of profit. Mandel closes this section with the Sraffa-von-Bortkiewicz critique as systematized by Pierre Salama ( Sur la valeur , 1975). Salama, Mandel says, reveals \"a series of underlying theoretical assumptions of which the authors themselves are not always aware.\" Specifically: further disaggregation of the von Bortkiewicz system — applying to von Bortkiewicz some of the criticisms he himself directs at Marx, e.g., that within department I, means of production used exclusively for producing department III commodities have a different status — \"leads unavoidably to the elimination of all value calculations and, therefore, of exploitation itself from the system.\" Mandel is careful to say this does not imply that Salama, Farjoun, and others have \"definitively resolved all the difficulties\"; there is \"still room for further discussion and research.\" But — and this is the polemical thrust — \"neither have von Bortkiewicz, Seton and Sraffa 'definitively' proved Marx wrong.\" The Declining Rate of Profit Controversy Mandel's next major section takes up the \"centre-piece\" of Volume 3: the tendency of the average rate of profit to decline (TRPF) . Marx derives it from his definition of the average rate of profit as total surplus-value produced ÷ total capital invested . Since the part of capital which alone produces surplus-value (variable capital, buying labour-power) tends to become a smaller and smaller part of total capital — because of the fundamentally labour-saving tendency of technical progress (substituting machinery for living labour) and because of the rise in the value of raw materials in total output — the organic composition of capital (in its value expression) tends to rise , and hence there is an inbuilt tendency for the average rate of profit to decline. Marx's own caveat , stressed by Mandel: this is a tendency , not an uninterrupted linear development. Marx stresses \"powerful countervailing forces at work\" which may neutralize or even reverse the tendency, and other forces which slow it down. The Countervailing Forces Mandel begins with the most important countervailing force: the possibility of increasing the rate of surplus-value (s′ = s/v) . Technically, it might seem this could indefinitely compensate for a rising organic composition. To see this, Mandel rewrites the rate of profit: Starting from pr′ = s / (c + v), divide numerator and denominator by v: pr′ = (s/v) / (c/v + 1) = s′ / (q + 1) where: - s′ = the rate of surplus-value (s/v), - q = the organic composition of capital (c/v), - pr′ = the rate of profit. So the rate of profit is directly proportional to the rate of surplus-value and inversely proportional to the organic composition of capital (plus the additive constant 1). If the rate of surplus-value increased in the same proportion as the organic composition, the rate of profit would cease to decline. Mandel argues this proportional increase is impossible in the long run . The organic composition can rise to infinity (fully automated production from which living labour is totally excluded — cf. Levinson, Capital, Inflation and the Multinationals , 1971, and the note that labour costs in some petro-chemical works have fallen below 0.1% of total production costs). But the rate of surplus-value cannot rise to infinity . As long as living wage-labour is employed, no level of productivity — even fully automated factories — is imaginable in which workers could reproduce the equivalent of all the consumer goods they need in \"a couple of minutes' or even a couple of seconds' work.\" The higher the existing level of productivity and the higher the socially recognized real wage, the harder it becomes to raise s′ substantially without seriously lowering real wages — which would (a) provoke a sharp social and political crisis and (b) create a tremendous problem of overproduction , since the mass of use-values (including wage-goods) increases even more rapidly than productivity and accumulation. Furthermore, once we approach complete automation, s — which is not a proportion but an absolute mass — starts to decline rapidly along with v , as the number of wage-earners and labour-hours diminish steeply. In a fully automated economy, surplus-value would disappear altogether , as living-labour inputs disappear. Hence it would be \"absurd to consider formally a 'rate of surplus-value' s/v when surplus-value itself no longer exists.\" Other countervailing forces enumerated by Marx, per Mandel: Cheapening of elements of constant capital (raw materials and machinery) — by slowing the growth of q, this slows the decline of the rate of profit. Quickened turnover of capital — since the annual mass of profit is a function of the number of production cycles an identical circulating money capital can perform. Turnover itself depends on a quickened circulation process (faster transport and sale) and a shortened production process. Foreign trade — with capital outflow towards countries with lower organic composition. Extension of capital investment into hitherto non-capitalistically organized branches (e.g., many \"service industries\" under late capitalism), where the organic composition is initially lower. Lowering of real wages — by raising s′ above and beyond the increase which normally results from productivity growth in the wage-goods industry. Finally, Mandel notes a point Marx stressed in Chapter 14 but not in Chapter 15: the decline in the rate of surplus-value can be (and normally is) accompanied by a rise in the mass of surplus-value — and hence in the mass of profit. Though this is not itself a countervailing factor against TRPF, it is a countervailing factor against some of TRPF's economic consequences . The capitalist class does not panic and shut up shop when profits rise from $100 billion to $200 billion \"just because these $200 billion now represent 'only' a 5 instead of an 11 per cent return on total capital.\" Time-spans: Cycle, Long Waves, Secular Trend Mandel then distinguishes three time-spans over which the law has been analyzed: The industrial (business) cycle — here there is \"wide consensus today between Marxists and academic economists specializing in business-cycle studies\" (e.g., W. C. Mitchell, Business Cycles and Their Causes , 1941) that ups and downs of the rate of profit correlate closely with ups and downs of production. The 'long waves' — the successive periods of quicker and slower growth of the capitalist economy as a whole — a middle span to which Mandel says \"hitherto too little attention has been paid.\" He cites \"overwhelming evidence\" of three significant increases in the average growth rate: after the revolutions of 1848 ; around 1893 ; and at the start of the Second World War in the U.S. and at the end of the forties in Western Europe and Japan . Since rising rate of growth = stepped-up accumulation = requires an upsurge (not decline) in the average rate of profit, Mandel asks what conditions prevailed immediately prior to these turning points. His answer, iterated as the principal thesis of his own work ( Late Capitalism , 1975, ch. 4; The Long Waves of Capitalist Development , 1980), is that the \"counteracting factors\" enumerated by Marx combined in a particular way to neutralize or reverse the tendency for a longer period than normally occurs at a certain stage of the industrial cycle . Crucially, the temporary neutralization \"in no way contradicts its general validity\": expansionary long waves are regularly followed by depressive long waves in which the tendency manifests itself more strongly and durably than during the normal cycle. \"Its actions can be delayed by countervailing factors, but only for it to reassert itself with a vengeance.\" The 'secular' time-span — the lifespan of the capitalist mode of production as such, for whose capacity for indefinite survival the law is \"a vital question.\" This introduces the theory of collapse ( Zusammenbruchstheorie ), to which Mandel returns in the final section. Against the Critics: Gillman, the Neo-Ricardians, the Okishio Theorem, and the Empirical Argument Mandel notes that \"the very operation of the law (its truth content)\" has been increasingly challenged. In a footnote (n. 37), he ridicules the idea that the law \"manifests itself exclusively, or mainly, through its negation\" — \"the position adopted by several Soviet authors\" such as S. L. Wygodski, who, before the \"second slump,\" saw the law as confirmed by a tendency towards a rising rate of profit . Some challenges stem from the appearance of long-term stepped-up postwar growth, which \"seemed somehow incompatible — in Marxist terms themselves — with a declining rate of profit.\" Hence Gillman's ( The Falling Rate of Profit , 1957) efforts to discover categories like \"realization expenses\" (presumably deducted from surplus-value, which is reduced to \"surplus-value appropriated by productive capital\") or \"surplus\" — whose growth is supposed to explain why the rate of profit as Marx conceived it stops falling. Mandel observes dryly that \"events since 1974–5 have caught up with this type of argument.\" More systematic is the neo-Ricardian challenge, whose main theoretical argument is the Okishio theorem : \"As every capitalist will introduce machinery only if this increases his rate of profit, how can increased profits for every capitalist lead to a decrease in the rate of profit for capitalists taken together?\" Mandel identifies two flaws in this reasoning: First flaw — the assumption about capitalist motivation. It is not true that every capitalist introduces new machinery only if it increases his rate of profit. As Marx himself points out, this is his voluntary inclination , but he may be forced to introduce new machinery to keep his market share or save his firm from bankruptcy — i.e., to cut his cost price under the pressure of competition, in spite of the effect on his rate of profit. More correct to say: capitalists hesitate to introduce machinery which cuts the amount of profit — but the amount (mass) of profit and the rate of profit are two quite different categories. The former may rise while the latter falls. Mandel cites Georgios Stamatis (n. 40): the decisive difference between unit profit margins (the difference between cost price and sale price per unit produced ) and the Marxist concept of the rate of profit, which must take into account the total value of fixed capital used . Stamatis's \"tragedy for capital\" is that the same capitalist methods of mechanization which lead to lower unit costs and rising unit profit margins \"in the end result in an above-average increase in total fixed capital investment — which is one of the forces triggering off a rise in the organic composition of capital in a higher proportion than the rise in the rate of surplus-value, thereby causing the rate of profit to decline.\" Mandel adds a barbed remark: Stamatis's book is \"amazingly schizophrenic\" — the entire first part extols the law in painstaking detail, then by \"a breathtaking salto mortale\" the author concludes that the law no longer applies because capitalism no longer applies \"specific capitalist methods of production.\" Second flaw — misunderstanding of \"laws of motion.\" These laws \"operate independently from, and in spite of, conscious decisions by individual capitalist firms.\" In fact, they are \"the objective and unforeseen effects of conscious decisions by these firms.\" No capitalist knows in advance what the real result of buying new machinery will be. \"Only when the commodities produced with the help of this new machinery have been sold, and several successive annual balance-sheets have been drawn up, will these results become known.\" Therefore it is perfectly possible — indeed inevitable — that every capitalist's purchase of more machinery is intended to increase his mass and rate of profit, but the final end-result of all these decisions is a situation where the average rate of profit of all is actually reduced . Mandel footnotes Anwar Shaikh's study of Dobb's crisis theory. The main empirical neo-Ricardian argument: the organic composition of capital is not rising at all over time but remains more or less even — technical progress in the long run is neither essentially labour-saving nor essentially capital-saving, but \"neutral\" (a notion Mandel traces to Roy Harrod). The alleged index of this stability is the capital/output ratio over time. Mandel responds on two fronts: The capital/output ratio is \"definitely not identical (or parallel) to the organic composition of capital.\" It erroneously identifies constant capital with fixed capital : the weight of the value of raw materials , which tends to become a growing part of constant (and total) capital, is \"completely eliminated from the reasoning.\" The \"wage bill\" in national income conflates variable capital (payment of productive labour) with payment of unproductive labour, which \"comes at least partially out of surplus-value.\" Given the steady growth of unproductive labour under late capitalism, \"the distinction is statistically decisive.\" Additionally, Shaikh has demonstrated (in \"An Introduction to the History of Crisis Theories,\" 1977) that the \"stable capital/output ratio\" itself should be seriously challenged from a statistical point of view, and corresponds \"to a large extent to an imprecise or wrong use of statistical categories by bourgeois statisticians\" — confirmed by initial detailed studies (e.g., R. J. Gordon's work in Survey of Current Business , 1971; and the study of Granou, Baron, and Billandot, whose own calculation, Mandel critically notes, incorporates the rate of surplus-value and thus becomes \"to some extent the reciprocal of the rate of profit,\" rising strongly in the post-war period). Mandel concedes: an empirical demonstration of rising organic composition on a macro-economic basis (from national income and GNP statistics) is \"not easy to provide\" due to the lack of transparency of real-value relations when measured in current market prices. But — and here's the strategic move — a close corollary is the share of labour costs in total annual production costs . Here \"we are on much more solid statistical ground,\" since numerous monographs examine this relation for separate branches over time. Mandel's rhetorical conclusion: \"One would have a hard time discovering a single branch of production in which labour costs constitute a larger part of total current (annual) production costs today than they did on the eve of the Second World War, or at the beginning of the twentieth century — let alone a century or a century-and-a-half ago.\" Despite tendencies to cheapen machinery and raw materials, \"the basic trend of long-term capitalist growth and technical progress has indeed been a labour-saving one.\" And this is precisely what the terms \"mechanization\" and \"growing automation\" express. Marx's theoretical achievement was to stress this trend at a time when it was \"scarcely recognized as historically decisive for the capitalist mode of production.\" Marxist Theories of Crisis: Against Mono-Causality Mandel now turns to the most fragmented area: crisis theory. Marx never left a completed, fully worked-out theory of crisis; his observations on the industrial cycle and crises of overproduction are scattered across several books, articles, and letters. It is \"tempting\" — and several contemporary Marxists (David Yaffe, Paul Mattick) have indeed done so — to see TRPF as Marx's main contribution to explaining crises of overproduction. Mandel's verdict on this is yes and no . Within the industrial cycle, \"the ups and downs of the rate of profit are closely correlated with the ups and downs of production.\" But this statement alone is not sufficient to provide a causal explanation of the crisis . It can be — and has been — misunderstood mechanically as: crises are \"caused\" by insufficient surplus-value production → capital cannot be sufficiently valorized → cut-down of current investment → reduced employment → cumulative reduction of income, sales, investment, employment, etc. → until the fall in employment and devalorization of capital have raised the rate of surplus-value and reduced the mass of capital, allowing the rate of profit to rise again → cumulative regrowth. Mandel's critique of this \"vulgar sense\" of crisis-by-TRPF-decline: It is wrong because it confuses the impossibility of valorizing additionally accumulated capital with the impossibility of valorizing all previously invested capital (cf. below, pp. 360–61). It also identifies fluctuations in investment decisions of capitalist firms with fluctuations of current surplus-value production — but the former may continue to grow while the latter declines, and vice versa. The main weakness is its concentration on the sphere of production alone , founded on \"a confusion about the very nature of the commodity and of commodity production.\" Like Say's loi des débouchés , it tacitly assumes there is no specific value realization problem, only one of surplus-value production . This in turn assumes production for barter , not production for sale , and that somehow, \"at least at a macro-economic level, all value produced is automatically realized.\" Marx himself explicitly refuted this (Mandel quotes from Chapter 15 at length, \"my italics\" following Marx): \"The conditions for immediate exploitation and for the realization of that exploitation are not identical. Not only are they separate in time and space, they are also separate in theory. The former is restricted only by the society's productive forces, the latter by the proportionality between the different branches of production, and by the society's power of consumption . And this is determined neither by the absolute power of production nor by the absolute power of consumption but rather by the power of consumption within a given framework of antagonistic conditions of distribution… It is further restricted by the drive for accumulation…\" It is dangerous from the standpoint of defending the working class, because the conclusion might be drawn that the crisis could be overcome and employment rise \"if only real wages were to be cut and surplus-value (profits) thereby automatically increased.\" This confronts the working class and trade unions with an agonizing choice between defending real wages and fighting unemployment — making labor responsible for the loss of jobs. Reformist proponents of class collaboration \"are only too ready to come forward with arguments of this kind, calling upon the workers to make the necessary sacrifices to 'save jobs'…\" Empirical experience has shown repeatedly that this \"is not borne out empirically.\" It represents \"an ideological weapon designed to impose the burden of the crisis on the working class and assist an increase in the rate of surplus-value.\" 'Profit squeeze' theories (e.g., Glyn and Sutcliffe, 1972) involve \"a similar danger of misuse.\" (Mandel also footnotes Maurice Dobb's Political Economy and Capitalism — Dobb's assumption that capitalists introduce new machinery only when wages rise is \"not the same as the 'profit squeeze' theory, but it is not far from it,\" and Shaikh has correctly criticized these assumptions.) Even defenders of the falling-rate explanation who reply indignantly that \"the decline in the rate of profit is a function of the rising organic composition of capital, not of a decline in the rate of surplus-value,\" and who insist that the rate of surplus-value rises until the very eve of the crisis but cannot offset q — even they \"forget\" that the rate of profit is a function of both q and s′. Except at starvation wages (below the physiological minimum, which \"no longer exists in any industrialized country\"), a cut in real wages always implies a rise in surplus-value produced, hence a higher rate of profit than before the cut. So we are \"back at square one: to argue that the crisis is exclusively caused by insufficient surplus-value production is to assist the employers' argument that it can, at least partially, be overcome by a cut in real wages.\" Mandel's general principle: critique of any mono-causal explanation of crises . In Marxist theory, crises of overproduction are simultaneously crises of over-accumulation of capital and crises of overproduction of commodities . \"The former cannot be explained without pointing to the latter; the latter cannot be understood without referring to the former.\" The crisis can only be overcome if there occurs simultaneously a rise in the rate of profit and an expansion of the market — which \"disarms both the employers' and the reformists' arguments.\" He then catalogues the three main variants of mono-causal crisis theory: Variant 1: The pure disproportionality theory. Basic cause of the cycle and ensuing crisis is capitalist anarchy of production — the fact that capitalist investment decisions cannot spontaneously lead to equilibrium conditions (the correct proportion of value fractions produced and money flows generated in departments I and II, which Marx defined in Volume 2). Hence the unavoidable breakdown of equilibrium. Proponents: Mikhail Tugan-Baranovsky, Rudolf Hilferding, Nikolai Bukharin (with Bukharin \"a bit more cautious,\" taking into account the restricting force of limited mass consumption). The conclusions are obvious and dangerous: if monopolies could 'organize' investment — a \"general cartel\" (Hilferding) — there would be no crises of overproduction ; \"capitalism without crises.\" (Mandel cites Tony Cliff as sharing this conviction.) As Roman Rosdolsky (1977) has pointed out, however, these theorists overlook the fact that the disproportion between production and consumption — the tendency of capitalism to develop productive forces in an unrestricted way while it imposes strict limits upon mass consumption — is inherent to capitalism , independent of the disproportional development of departments I and II due to competition and anarchy of production. The \"grotesque consequences\" are best exemplified by Tugan-Baranovsky himself, who \"seriously argued — and demonstrated 'mathematically' — that department I could develop completely independently from department II, to the point where the output of consumer goods would tend to fall towards zero, without such a development causing any crisis whatsoever.\" Variant 2: The pure under-consumption by the masses theory. Basic cause: the gap between output (or productive capacity) and mass consumption (workers' real wages or purchasing power); crises essentially take the form of overproduction of commodities in department II. Over-accumulation and overproduction in department I appear as results of this overproduction/over-capacity in consumer goods. Non-Marxist ancestors: Thomas Malthus, Sismondi, the Russian Narodniks . Marxist proponents: Karl Kautsky, Rosa Luxemburg, Nathalia Moszkowska, Fritz Sternberg, Paul Sweezy . Weakness: the basic assumption — \"not always clearly understood, but at least clearly expressed, by Sweezy\" — that \"somehow there is a fixed proportion between the development of department I and the development of the productive capacity of department II.\" Since the growth of organic composition and of s′ both increase purchasing power for means of production more strongly than for consumer goods , the conclusion is obvious: an unsaleable residue of consumer goods. But this assumption is logically unproven and contrary to the very nature of capitalist growth , characterized by growing mechanization or (borrowing the term from von Böhm-Bawerk) greater 'roundaboutness' of production . Capitalist growth does imply a larger proportion of total output taking the form of means of production — although not accompanied by an absolute decline in the production of consumer goods or stagnation of department II capacity. Once this is understood, neither the growth of c/v nor the growth of s/v need automatically lead to overproduction of consumer goods. They will do so only if the fraction output I / output II grows more slowly than the fraction demand for means of production / demand for consumer goods. But nothing in the capitalist mode of production necessitates this — it cannot be mathematically or logically demonstrated. The political danger in under-consumption theories (from which Mandel says Luxemburg \"completely avoided\" it) is that they can lead to reformist conclusions not unlike the \"harmonicist\" implications of disproportionality theories: \"The former tend to think that capitalism could avoid crisis if real wages were larger, or if the government distributed additional 'purchasing power' in the form of social security and unemployment disbursements — i.e., 'redistributed' national income in favour of the workers, 're-transformed' a part of surplus-value into additional indirect wages.\" What these \"solutions\" overlook: capitalist production is production for profit . Any sizable redistribution of national income in favour of workers on the eve or in early stages of a crisis, when the rate of profit is already declining, means a further decline in that rate of profit through a reduction of s′. Under these conditions, capitalists will not increase investment, \"even if sales of previously produced stocks of consumer goods go up.\" The depression continues. (Mandel cites neo-Keynesians in Britain, France, West Germany as the holders of such views; cf. Alternative Wirtschaftspolitik , 1979.) Variant 3: The pure over-accumulation theory. Main reason for crisis: insufficient mass of surplus-value produced compared to the total amount of accumulated capital . Mandel has dealt with the weakness above (confusion of dimensions) and its dangerous implications for the proletarian class struggle. The demographic sub-variant (associated with Makatoh Itoh; historically with Otto Bauer): after long periods of prosperity, the reserve army of labour tends to disappear , causing real wages to rise to a point where they abruptly reduce s′ and hence the rate of profit. This is the border case Marx calls, in Chapter 15, 'absolute over-accumulation of capital.' Mandel's reply: while this cannot be excluded from a general theoretical point of view, in the real history of capitalism — under conditions of extensive international labour mobility and even vaster potential migrations from underdeveloped countries (he cites ~1 million illegal immigrants a year from Mexico and Central America to the US, with about 15 million unemployed in those two regions alone — plus fifty million housewives not gainfully employed) — \"any such 'population pressure' on capitalism seems centuries removed from us.\" It also \"greatly underestimates capitalism's capacity rapidly to reconstruct a reserve army of labour, by concentrating on rationalization investments which are macro-economically employment-reducing.\" Confirmation: throughout the 1970s, the total unemployed in OECD countries doubled from ten million in 1970 to twenty million in 1980 , while the number of jobs destroyed by technical progress was far larger — millions of immigrant workers had to return home, millions of women and young people dropped out of the labour market, and many productive workers were transformed into unproductive ones. A more sophisticated version by Ferenc Jánossy (1966) sees in the inability of capitalism to develop enough skilled (especially highly skilled) workers a bottleneck which pushes up real wages at the end of 'prosperity'; here again, the flexibility of capital — in speeding up skill formation (including at factory level) and in reducing the need for highly skilled labour via technology — is \"greatly underestimated.\" Mandel's own synthetic critique of the over-accumulation theory as self-sufficient: Its proponents argue that as long as accumulation proceeds smoothly, consumption by final consumers automatically grows (more wage-labour employed, generally at increasing wages; unproductive consumption out of surplus-value also grows); hence no glut of consumer goods can appear so long as TRPF has not significantly slowed accumulation. The first part is true, as far as it goes. But the conclusion does not follow. The analysis proves only that consumption grows as long as accumulation grows . It does not prove that consumption grows in the same proportion as the productive capacity of department II. Indeed, the combined operation of rising organic composition in department II and rising s′ makes it probable that (at least periodically) consumption, while growing , grows less than department II productive capacity. In which case a glut of consumer goods can occur before accumulation has slowed down in the economy as a whole. Similarly, the assumption that a slow-down in investment triggers the crisis before any overproduction of commodities manifests itself is \"in the best of cases only one possible variant of the crisis scenario.\" Current investment decisions depend on two variables : past profit realizations (available surplus-value for accumulation) and future profit expectations. About the current rate of profit — a macro-economic end-result — capitalist firms have \"no way of knowing anything precise , as long as their own and other capitalists' annual balance-sheets have not been drawn up.\" It is possible that past profit realization does not yet reflect a decline in the rate of profit, but investment is still cut because of growing signs of glut or over-capacity . Conversely, past profit realization may already reflect the beginning of a decline, but investment will still be expanding because the firm believes it can expand sales. Profit expectations include estimates about expected market conditions and market shares — hence a tendency for investment to 'overshoot' even after the rate of profit has started to decline. Many firms may believe they can increase their market share or profit from technological advantages by expanding. \"All these decisions cannot stop the rate of profit from declining. But they can produce growing overproduction of commodities before accumulation of capital actually slows down.\" Mandel's Positive Synthesis: A Multi-Causal Model of the Cycle Mandel asserts that \"elements of a correct theory of capitalist crisis are, of course, present in all three mono-causal explanations,\" and they have to be integrated. His integration proceeds by distinguishing successive forms taken over time by the accumulation of capital : Phase 1 — Boom: Business is brisk, current output is easily sold (demand seems stronger than supply), profits are high. Investment boom → runs rapidly into bottlenecks in both sub-sections of department I: machinery and equipment and raw materials . Both sub-sections of department I are \"by their very nature, less flexible in adapting rapidly to demand than is department II.\" Hence additional investment, capital accumulation, occurs on a larger and larger scale in department I. More means of production have to be produced to produce additional means of production for producing additional consumer goods. Good profit expectations plus high profit realizations motivate the boom. There is a shift of investment toward department I. An uneven development (disproportion) between departments I and II is set in motion. (Mandel notes in footnote 71 that Marx saw in the massively bunched introduction of fixed capital at 7–10 year intervals both a main reason for the periodicity of the cycle and the determining factor for its average duration; on \"overshooting\" he cites Hicks, Harrod, Domar.) Phase 2 — Simultaneous phenomena at the point of crisis: On the one hand, the additional means of production produced come into the production process only after a time-lag . When they enter, they increase productive capacity in both departments \"by leaps and bounds.\" But the relatively high rates of profit and investment imply that real wages and consumer-goods demand (from capitalists/connoisseurs and workers) could not have developed in the same proportion as this sudden increase in productive capacity — hence a tendency to increasing overproduction (or over-capacity), in the first place in department II. On the other hand, the massive introduction of new means of production occurs with new up-dated techniques characterized by a basically labour-saving bias — i.e., by an increased organic composition of capital . This presses down the rate of profit, especially since under boom conditions the rate of surplus-value cannot increase in the same proportion, or even does not increase at all . Hence a tendency to over-accumulation : part of newly accumulated capital can no longer be invested at the average rate of profit, or is not invested at all, \"pushed towards speculation, etc.\" Credit expansion , for a certain time, \"covers the gap. But it can only postpone the crash, not avoid it.\" Overproduction now tends to spread from department II to department I . Growing overproduction of commodities (over-capacity in a growing number of branches), combined with growing over-accumulation, \"must of necessity lead to sharp cut-backs in productive investment.\" Disproportionality between the two departments now jumps from an 'over-extension' of department I into an 'under-development' of that department. Investment falls more quickly than current output. (Note in n. 74: this is not an absolute rule — overproduction could start in certain sub-sectors of department I; the two latest crises — 1974–5 and 1979–80 — both started in automobiles and housing , i.e., durable consumer goods, sub-sectors of department II.) Phase 3 — The Crash. It can (but does not necessarily) take the initial form of a credit and banking crash . There ensues a general collapse of commodity prices (expressed in gold), together with a decline in output and employment. There is a general devalorization of capital , resulting simultaneously from (a) the collapse of prices (commodity capital), (b) a large number of bankruptcies, and (c) a decline in the value of fixed capital and raw-material stocks of surviving firms. Mandel emphasizes the theoretical point: this general collapse of prices is nothing but the adaptation of market prices and prices of production (through a lower average rate of profit) to the general lowering in the value of the average commodity — which is the unavoidable outcome of the general increase of investment, organic composition of capital, and average productivity of labour during the previous period. Capitalists try to postpone \"this hour of reckoning as long as possible — whence the over-extension of credit, speculation, over-trading, etc. on the eve of the crash. But they cannot postpone it indefinitely.\" Phase 4 — Recuperation. For the system as a whole, the crash's effects are \"healthy, however nasty they may be for individual capitalists.\" General devalorization of capital is not accompanied by a proportional reduction in the mass of surplus-value produced. Equivalently: an identical mass of surplus-value can now valorize a smaller total capital. Hence the decline in the rate of profit can be stopped and even reversed. Three mechanisms: Large-scale reconstitution of the reserve army of labour during crisis/depression → vigorous increase in s′, not only via speed-ups but even via a cut in real wages → higher rate of profit. Raw material prices fall more than the prices of finished goods — part of constant capital becomes cheaper, slowing the rise of q, again pushing up the average rate of profit on industrial capital. Once stocks have been sufficiently depleted and current production sufficiently cut for demand again to outstrip supply (especially in department II), a new cycle of stepped-up accumulation and productive investment can begin. The conclusion Mandel draws: TRPF \"is less a direct explanation for crises of overproduction properly speaking, than a revelation of the basic mechanism of the industrial cycle as such\" — \"an uncovering of the specifically capitalist, i.e., uneven, disharmonious, mode of economic growth, which unavoidably leads to successive phases of declining rates of profit, and recuperation of the rate of profit as a result, precisely, of the consequences of the previous decline.\" The Textual Basis for Multi-Causality Mandel argues that \"there can be little doubt that this multi-causal explanation… corresponds to Marx's own conviction, at least as expressed here in Volume 3.\" He cites three further passages, alongside the one already quoted (p. 614–15): Passage 1 (p. 614–15): Considering a simplified society of only industrial capitalists and wage-labourers, and leaving aside changes in price preventing large portions of total capital from being replaced in average proportions (which, \"in the overall context of the reproduction process… particularly as developed by credit, must recurrently bring about a situation of general stagnation\") and leaving aside \"fraudulent businesses and speculative dealings that the credit system fosters\" — even then, \"a crisis would be explicable only in terms of a disproportion in production between different branches and a disproportion between the consumption of the capitalists themselves and their accumulation.\" But as things actually are, the replacement of the capitals invested depends on the consumption capacity of the non-productive classes; and the consumption capacity of the workers is restricted partly by the laws governing wages and partly by the fact that they are employed only as long as they can be employed at a profit. \"The ultimate reason for all real crises always remains the poverty and restricted consumption of the masses, in the face of the drive of capitalist production to develop the productive forces as if only the absolute consumption capacity of society set a limit to them.\" Passage 2 (p. 367): \"Periodically, however, too much is produced in the way of means of labour and means of subsistence, too much to function as means for exploiting the workers at a given rate of profit. Too many commodities are produced for the value contained in them, and the surplus-value included in this value, to be realized under the conditions of distribution given by capitalist production, and to be transformed back into new capital, i.e. it is impossible to accomplish this process without ever recurrent explosions.\" Passage 3 (p. 419–20): A concrete account of how crisis emerges through the circuit of credit and circulation: \"The manufacturer may actually sell to the exporter, and the exporter to his foreign customer; the importer may sell his raw materials to the manufacturer, and the manufacturer sell his products to the wholesaler, etc. But at some particular imperceptible point the commodity lies unsold; or else the total stocks of producers and middlemen gradually become too high. It is precisely then that consumption is generally at flood tide, partly because one industrial capitalist sets a series of others in motion, partly because the workers these employ, being fully occupied, have more than usual to spend… And besides this, there is also… a constant circulation between one constant capital and another (even leaving aside the accelerated accumulation) which is initially independent of individual consumption… for production of constant capital never takes place for its own sake, but simply because more of it is needed in those spheres of production whose products do go into individual consumption. This can continue quite happily for a good while, stimulated by prospective demand… The crisis occurs as soon as the returns of those merchants who sell far afield (or who have accumulated stocks at home) become so slow and sparse that the banks press for payment for commodities bought, or bills fall due before any resale takes place.\" Credit and the Rate of Interest Mandel next moves to the role of credit, connecting it to the doctrine of Volume 2. Volume 2 stressed the importance of previous accumulation (and presence) of money-capital, its periodic injection into and outflow from the operations of productive capital, for making expanded reproduction possible for \" capital in general .\" Volume 3 stresses the key importance of credit for \" many capitals ,\" i.e., for the fluctuations of the industrial cycle under competition. Doctrinal sequence: the appearance of a generally known average rate of profit unavoidably leads to an equalization of the rate of interest too. Surplus-value is first split between profit for entrepreneurial capital (industrial, commercial, banking, and agricultural entrepreneur profit as distinct from passive landowners) and interest . Through the capitalist banking system, all available money reserves — savings, non-invested surplus-value, and idle money capital — are transformed into functioning capital , i.e., lent to capitalist firms actually operating (employing wage-labour) in production or circulation. In this way, capitalists operate with much more capital than they personally own. Capital accumulation can proceed at a much quicker pace than if each firm could only enlarge reproduction on the basis of its own realized profits. A first-sight illusion: This expansion of credit seems to accentuate TRPF. The total amount of profit distributed among all capitalist firms is now lower than the total surplus-value produced — the difference being exactly the total amount of interest paid to passive owners of money-capital (not to be confused with profits of banks , i.e., the average profits on their own capital, not on their deposits). But Mandel says this is a false impression . The average rate of profit is the division of the total amount of surplus-value produced by the total amount of social capital . If, as a result of division of labour among capitalists or over-accumulation, part of that capital is not itself directly productive — not engaged in the direct production of surplus-value — this does not change its nature as capital, i.e., \"value constantly on the look-out for an accretion of value.\" According to Marx here, the effects of credit (like those of trade) on TRPF are opposite to what at first appears. They tend to put a brake on, or even reverse the tendency, through three simultaneous mechanisms: Faster rotation of capital. Trade and credit allow capital to rotate more rapidly — increasing the number of productive cycles through which a single sum of money capital can pass in, say, one year — thereby increasing the mass of surplus-value and the annual rate of profit (same surplus-value produced during each cycle, other things equal). This is why industrialists are ready to allow commercial and banking capital to share in the general distribution of entrepreneurial profit, \"although neither commercial nor banking capital produces surplus-value.\" Such capital \"does not produce surplus-value itself, but it helps industrial capital and agricultural capital produce additional surplus-value.\" (Mandel explains: industrial capital rotates faster if wholesale and retail merchants buy produced commodities immediately from industrial capitalists and keep them in stock until the 'last customer' appears — this division of labour inside the capitalist class explains why industrialists abandon part of surplus-value to the merchants as commercial profit.) Faster concentration of capital. By enlarging the scope and tempo of accumulation in the productive sphere (over and above profits directly owned by industrialists and capitalist farmers), commerce and trade accelerate the concentration of capital , thereby stimulating technical progress and the production of relative surplus-value — which again counteracts TRPF. Joint-stock companies (corporations). By this device, credit creates a situation in which a large part of capital, owned by stockholders, is not expected to receive the average rate of profit at all, but is content with the average rate of interest only. Hence the average rate of entrepreneurial profit is much higher than it would be if all (or the largest part) of capital were directly entrepreneurial capital, i.e., had to receive the average rate of profit. Beyond these three, credit has structural functions: (a) The greater flexibility of money capital not tied to any specific firm or branch is one of the main reasons the equalization of the rate of profit can so easily occur and be recognized under capitalism — i.e., why social capital remains relatively mobile in spite of growing investment in fixed, relatively immobile capital. (b) Parallel to the reserve army of labour, these huge reserves of money capital are preconditions for sudden, rapid phases of feverish expansion characteristic of the industrial cycle and of capitalist growth as uneven and disharmonious. (c) The banking system plays in part the role of a social clearing-house , constantly transferring capital from branches with stagnating or declining demand to branches with growing demand not satisfied by current production/capacity; deviations of distinct rates of profit from the average are the guiding mechanism. (d) Credit both expands accumulation \"to its utmost limits\" and functions as \"the main lever for over-speculation, over-trading and overproduction.\" The Desynchronization of the Credit Cycle Mandel then lays out a precise chronology of the credit cycle and the rate of interest relative to the industrial cycle: Recovery / initial upsurge: Money capital relatively abundant; self-financing high; rate of interest relatively low; entrepreneurial profit above average. (Footnote 81 clarifies: under permanent inflation of paper money this applies to the real , not nominal, rate of interest. Real rate = nominal rate minus rate of inflation. The extent of credit inflation under late capitalism is indicated by \"several lengthy periods of negative 'real' rates of interest in key capitalist countries.\") Boom peak, overheating, crash: Money capital scarcer and scarcer; self-financing declines precipitately; demand for money capital grows constantly; the rate of interest grows \"by leaps and bounds, not in spite of but as a function of the decline in the average rate of profit.\" Firms now borrow not to expand business but to escape bankruptcy — not to gain additional entrepreneurial profits but to save their capital. At this precise moment, the rate of interest can actually be above the rate of entrepreneurial profit (which \"cannot, of course, 'normally' be the case\"). Crisis / depression proper: Investment declines steeply; demand for credit collapses; rate of interest slides rapidly, which helps entrepreneurial profit slowly to pick up again. Marx's Theory of Surplus Profits Mandel now argues that \" the fact that Marx's theory of differential land rent in reality represents a special case of a more general theory of surplus profits has not hitherto been sufficiently appreciated .\" And it is all the more strange, since Marx makes the point explicitly in Volume 3, in several passages of Parts One and Two, and returns to it in Parts Six and Seven. The basic approach is a straightforward application of the labour theory of value. Whether labour expended in producing a given commodity is recognized as average socially necessary labour is not a simple physical matter of an actual number of labour-hours expended — of a given fraction of society's total labour potential being used for a given commodity. It is a function of: - the total amount of labour expended in all the units producing that commodity, compared to the total amount of labour society wishes to devote to it; - the relation between the productivity of labour in the given productive unit and the average productivity of the branch as a whole. Mandel footnotes a key internal debate here. Rubin correctly denied a purely physiological (reified) definition of \"abstract labour\" but contended it is quantifiable, based on labour-time and intensity. Mandel agrees with Rubin against Colliot-Thélène (n. 82): the alleged contradiction between defining \"socially necessary labour\" both by average productivity and by the relation between branch output and socially recognized needs is not a contradiction but a difference between value production (sphere of production) and value realization (sphere of circulation). The law of value adapts distribution of the labour force to social needs post festum — but labour expended in the production process is value-producing. It is just that value produced has been redistributed : the equivalent of some of it is not received by those who own the commodities thus produced. He cites Marx (p. 774 below) on the law of value applied to the total products of particular spheres rather than individual commodities — \"so that not only is no more labour-time devoted to each individual commodity than necessary, but out of the total social labour-time only the proportionate quantity needed is devoted to the various types of commodity.\" Three cases of current production distinguished by Marx (as Mandel reconstructs): Case 1 — Normal capital mobility in a branch of output. Inflows and outflows of capital, regulated by price oscillations inducing profit-rate oscillations, normally balance social supply and demand. Then equalization of the rate of profit applies to the branch. - Firms at average productivity of the branch → average rate of profit. - Firms below average productivity → less than average profit; risk being crowded out in crisis/depression. - Firms above average productivity (technological advances) → temporary surplus profit , i.e., profit over and above the average, resulting from the difference between individual costs of production and average costs of production in the branch. But this surplus profit will generally disappear in crisis/depression, when the new technology becomes generalized and the average productivity of labour (value of the commodity) is adapted to the initially higher productivity. Case 2 — Branches with structurally stagnant or declining demand (i.e., 'outmoded' branches, structural overproduction). - Only firms at above-average productivity receive the average rate of profit. - Firms at average productivity receive less than average. - Firms at below-average productivity sell at a loss and go out of business. - In general, when capital mobility is normal, such branches become 'normalized' (revert to Case 1) even before a general crisis of overproduction occurs , through massive closures. Case 3 — Structurally (or institutionally) determined scarcity. Capital influx is hampered or prevented by natural or artificial monopolies . There is a long-term preponderance of demand over supply. - Firms at the lowest productivity still receive the average rate of profit — they determine the price of production (or value) of the commodity. - Firms at higher productivity (average or above) receive a long-term surplus profit protected by the monopoly — by the powerful obstacle hindering influx of additional capital. This surplus profit does not disappear even in crisis/depression, though it is lowered in absolute terms by the fall in the average rate of profit. These monopoly surplus profits are called differential rents. In Volume 3, three instances are distinguished: land rent; mineral rents; technological rents. (Mandel explains he uses \"technological rent\" as an extension of Marx's land rent, for conditions of 'artificial monopoly' due to technological monopolies.) Land rent divides into agricultural land rent and urban land rent . Definitions: Natural monopolies are determined by the fact that access to natural resources necessary for production (from a use-value standpoint) is limited, and these are not reproducible at will by capital — applies to land as such (especially a given use-value: desired relative fertility, desired location); to mineral sources; to climatological preconditions for certain use-values (cotton, natural rubber, tropical fruits, etc.). Artificial monopolies are determined by limits in capital mobility related not to natural conditions but to conditions arising from the results of specific stages (forms) of accumulation: (a) concentration of capital (if starting a new firm with minimum profitability requires £500 million or $1,000 million, that's an evident obstacle to entry for most capitalists); (b) monopoly rights in patents, inventions, or research in certain new fields (or qualitative advantages in applying them); (c) organized practices by a small number of firms to keep out potential competitors; and so on. As clearly follows, natural and artificial monopolies are always relative, never absolute . Land is not reproducible — but possibilities for capital investment on existing land can be vastly expanded; internationally, tremendous areas of potentially agricultural land are not yet exploited; capitalist technology can be pushed to production without land. Mineral resources are finite — but synthetic production of originally natural raw materials (fibres, rubber, oil) is not finite to anything like the same degree. The dynamics of monopolies: (a) The bigger the initial capital outlays for profitable production, the smaller the number of potential new competitors. (b) The higher the surplus profits, the stronger the inducement for 'many capitals' to band together and risk huge initial investments to get a slice of the cake. (c) The more decisive advances in technology lead to stable surplus profits over longer periods, the stronger the pressure for potential competitors to leap ahead by a new technological revolution. Examples: the challenge to IBM's quasi-monopoly by micro-processors and Japanese trusts in fifth-generation computers. Mandel's provisional conclusion: \"All monopoly surplus profits are always limited in time and, in the long run, tend to disappear, and… commodities produced in initially monopolized branches tend to be exchanged at their prices of production.\" Whether this \"long run,\" at least for industrial products under monopoly capitalism (since ~1890), is the 'long wave' — as Mandel hypothesized in Late Capitalism — or not, remains a subject for further investigation. In footnote 89, Mandel defends the idea — advanced in his Marxist Economic Theory and defended in Late Capitalism — of an equalization of surplus profits side by side with the equalization of average profit, implying the co-existence during a certain time-span of two average rates of profit , one in the monopolized and one in the non-monopolized sectors. Marx himself, Mandel says, explicitly proposes it in Volume 3 (p. 1001). The Relative Character of Monopoly and the Role of Structural Scarcity To grasp the relative (never absolute) nature of any monopoly, and hence the limited nature in time of any surplus profit, \"it is necessary to reintroduce into our analysis the phenomenon of structural scarcity which was its starting-point.\" Marx (p. 279): \"If the demand is so strong, however, that it does not contract when price is determined by the value of commodities produced in the worst conditions, then it is these that determine the market value.\" Only if obstructions to capital mobility (obstacles to increases in output) create conditions under which social demand for the goods is for long periods higher than or equal to the total amount of commodities produced (including those produced under the lowest conditions of productivity/soil fertility) will units of production with lower costs realize surplus profits in the form of differential rents. Once social demand for the goods of the monopolized branch recedes, stagnates, or grows more slowly than production even under relative monopoly, differential rent will tend to be reduced and surplus profits to decline. (This does not mean they disappear completely, where the monopoly is natural, as long as differences in fertility subsist.) The historical example of agriculture: The huge increases in average productivity of agricultural labour — \"which have been one of the main characteristics of the development of capitalism in the twentieth century, and have indeed exceeded the rate of growth of industrial productivity of labour\" — have completely altered the demand/supply relation for basic foodstuffs in advanced capitalist countries. The situation of structural scarcity has been transformed into a situation of structural overproduction , co-determined by the decreasing place of food expenditure in total consumers' expenditure when real incomes rise ( Engel's Law ). Not only has differential rent therefore been strongly contracting, but large tracts of farm land have been reconverted into pastures, and pasture into forests or waste land. Massive closures of coal pits in the fifties, sixties, and early seventies, when oil was much cheaper than coal, are a parallel development in mining, with a correlated decline of differential coal-mining rents. But the process can be reversed. (a) When social demand — mediated through market prices — suddenly surges beyond output for ten or twenty years, structural scarcity reappears and differential rents massively reappear. Example: gold production since the collapse of Bretton Woods , when imperialist Central Banks could not maintain gold at $35 or $42 (35 SDR) an ounce (footnote 92 explains SDRs as based on a common basket of currencies, constantly re-appreciated against the dollar since 1971, so the \"official\" IMF gold price rose from $35 to $42). The upsurge of the free-market gold price — to $100, then $200, finally more than $600 an ounce — made many 'marginal' mines profitable again and led to a feverish development of gold-mining investment. Concretely (Dreyfus study, Le Monde , 29 Jan 1980): the more productive among the twenty main South African gold mines were producing at ~$95/oz at end-1979 (the single most productive at $64/oz); the less productive of these twenty at ~$200/oz (highest single figure $265/oz). This gives a differential rent of more than $100 an ounce for the former category as against the latter, once gold sells at more than $200 + average profit — say, above $240–250/oz. The deeper reason for the coexistence of monopoly tendencies and their periodic decline: surplus profits are deducted from the total amount of profit distributed among all capitalists who participate in the equalization of the rate of profit. They tend to reduce the general cake distributed among all bourgeois except the monopolists . As there is a tendency for the average rate of profit to decline, monopolies of all kinds — including monopoly property in land — tend to accentuate that decline. Hence the pressure of capital to overcome natural or artificial barriers to mobility, to reduce or eliminate monopolistic situations. The outcome of this tug-of-war is \"a function of the relative strength of different layers of the ruling class.\" In the twentieth century, the pressure has been more successful with regard to absentee capitalist landlords (separate from capitalist agricultural entrepreneurs) than with regard to industrial, transport, or mining monopolies — though not a few cases of collapse of monopolistic surplus profits can be cited in these realms too. This pressure operates on both possible hypotheses about the source of monopoly surplus profits — whether they are actually produced inside the monopolized branches, or are (in several cases) transfers of value from non-monopolized to monopolized sectors. For in both hypotheses, the mass of surplus-value to be shared among all capitalists who do not enjoy rents is substantially lower than it would have been with 'perfect' mobility of capital into all branches — i.e., their average rate of profit has been lowered. And when this accentuates a tendency already operating for deeper reasons, \"the counter-pressure will be all the more powerful.\" The Specificity of Capitalist Agriculture Mandel next examines Volume 3's treatment of agriculture. Marx extends a notion stressed at the end of Volume 1: the key importance of private appropriation of land — its transformation into the private property of a limited class — for the very birth, consolidation, and expansion of the capitalist mode of production. This mode presupposes the modern proletariat, which \"has no access to means of production and subsistence and is, therefore, under the economic compulsion to sell its labour-power.\" Means of subsistence = primarily food, which can be produced with minimal means wherever access to land is free. Hence the creation of the modern proletariat hinges, to a large extent, on barring free access to land to people possessing no capital. Historical process: Private appropriation of land in Western Europe mainly took place between the fifteenth and eighteenth centuries and culminated in the sale of village 'free' land reserves (communal lands) unleashed by the French Revolution (cf. Otto Bauer, Der Kampf um Wald und Weide , 1925). It was repeated throughout the last part of the nineteenth and the whole of the twentieth century in Eastern Europe, the Americas, the Middle East, Africa, Japan, and South-East Asia. The most repulsive form of forcible separation of the original population from its fertile land reserves occurred in Eastern and Southern Africa; it continues today in countries like Brazil, Iran, the Philippines, and Mexico (despite partial achievements of the 1910–17 Revolution). But the interrelation between consolidation of capitalism, accumulation, and struggle against TRPF is much more complex than this compulsion to transform all land into private property. The specific historical form in much of Europe and Japan: generalization of private property in land initially took the form of ownership by a social class separate and apart from functioning capitalists (capitalist farmers, entrepreneurs). These capitalist landowners (distinct from semi-feudal or feudal landlords) barred entry to their land by the capitalist class unless they received a special 'unearned' income in the form of absolute land rent (same rule applies to rentier-proprietors of urban land vis-à-vis building-industry capitalists). Elsewhere, different layers of the ruling class appropriated the 'surplus' land: foreign settlers (Robin Murray's concept of 'founder's rent' — Mandel thinks Murray is right, at least with regard to overseas countries with above-average fertile land compared to Western Europe, but he thinks Murray gives excessive weight to such 'rent' in explaining international migrations, capitalist expansionism, and the origins of imperialism); or local landowners, merchants, usurers. There are some — rather rare — cases of combinations of both processes. In all cases where actual ownership of land became separated from capitalist farming, absolute land rent appeared. Like differential land rent, absolute rent is a fraction of total surplus-value , deducted from the residue to be divided among all capitalist entrepreneurs and owners of money capital. This deduction is \"all the more onerous in that, contrary to differential rent, it is not open to erosion or equalization through the laws of motion of the capitalist mode of production properly speaking\" (competition, technical progress, increase in organic composition, concentration and centralization). It thus \"puts a brake upon capital accumulation in agriculture.\" Hence the organic drive of capital to eliminate the separation of landownership and capitalist farming : gradually transforming landowners into entrepreneurs, and land-renting farmers into (a) a majority of wage-earners and (b) a minority of landowning farmers. The transformation of a situation of structural scarcity of food into structural plenty (latent overproduction) in most industrialized countries powerfully assists this process. (Footnote 96 gives the evidence: OECD note, Feb 1980 — total wheat stocks in imperialist countries averaged more than 50 million tonnes in every single year between 1970/71 and 1979/80; total end-year stocks of butter and skimmed milk in imperialist countries rose from 289,000 tonnes in 1970 to 1.4 million tonnes in 1979.) This represents \"a tendential disappearance of absolute rent in the imperialist countries.\" Deeper law of value at work: Source of absolute land rent is the lower organic composition of capital in agriculture as compared with industry — i.e., the higher mass of surplus-value produced by agricultural labourers as compared with industrial labourers employed by the same amount of total capital. The barrier of landownership separated from capitalist enterprise makes it possible for landowners to prevent this supplementary surplus-value from being sucked into the general process of equalization of profit. Thus rent is indeed an obstacle to the full flowering of capitalist agriculture — a source of relative backwardness of agriculture compared to industry (productivity of labour in agriculture vs. industry). But Marx, who himself stressed this relative backwardness, noted it was not a fixed and final characteristic of the mode of production but could sooner or later be overcome. As agriculture becomes more industrialized, as the substitution of human labour by dead labour (machinery, fertilizers) is applied on an ever-increasing scale, as contemporary agro-business arises, the difference in organic composition of agricultural as against industrial capital tends to disappear. Consequently the material basis of absolute land rent disappears. Robin Murray's apt expression: just as the formal subordination of labour to capital is transformed into real subordination in agriculture, so formal subordination of land under capitalist agriculture is transformed into real subordination of land as a material element in capitalist agricultural production . The empirical scale of agricultural industrialization in the U.S. (Mandel's data): - Between 1915–19 and 1973–7, productivity of labour in wheat and soybean production increased tenfold , when measured by the labour-hours needed to produce 100 bushels. For maize , the increase was thirtyfold . - Production assets (including livestock and raw materials stocked on farms, thus roughly comparable to constant capital ) per farm worker increased fivefold in current dollars between 1963 and 1978. Per capita disposable income per farm worker only increased less than threefold , half of which originated from sources outside farming. Wages for hired labour barely doubled during the same period. Mandel comments: \"A good index of the increase in the organic composition of capital, if there ever was one!\" - Simultaneously, the 'emancipation' of capitalist agriculture from the use of land has made giant strides in animal husbandry — hog-raising, cattle-raising, and the aptly named 'broiler industry.' By 1972, 75 per cent of U.S. beef was raised on feedlots , the largest accommodating as many as 125,000 cattle at a time. Two important qualifications: The reappearance of absolute rent in modified form. While absolute land rent originating in the separation of landownership from capitalist farmers (differential land rent does not originate in ownership — ownership only determines who appropriates it) tends to disappear under industrialization, it reappears in modified form as the generalized mortgaging of land owned by small and medium-sized capitalist farmers — i.e., as the transfer of a significant part of surplus-value produced in agriculture to banks and finance capital (Kautsky, La Question agraire ). Mandel adds: the growing role of big food-transforming firms (increasingly multinationals) and big cooperative societies controlled by rich farmers increasingly tend to cut farmers off from direct access to the market. According to French economist Bernard Kayser (in Economie et Statistiques , No. 102, July–August 1978), barely 25% of France's agricultural production is sold by farmers themselves to final consumers or self-consumed; all the rest passes through large capitalist intermediaries, which take their own toll, \"similar to — and often parallel with — mortgage interest.\" The contradictory dynamic in semi-colonial countries. Real capital movements are guided not by the average rate of profit but by deviations from it. So while capital tends to eliminate absolute rent in older capitalist countries, it also constantly tends to reproduce it , essentially (but not exclusively) in countries where capitalism has penetrated belatedly. There thus operates, at the level of the world economy, \"a kind of process of internationalization of land appropriation and creation of absolute land rent .\" Brazil offers outstanding examples. The dual relation of capitalism to food production: Because agricultural production is food production, and food is an essential element in the reproduction of labour-power (quantitatively its main element, at least in the earlier phases of capitalist development), there is a contradictory element in capitalism's relation to agriculture. For (real or potential) agricultural capitalists, the main problem is eliminating the dual structure of landownership and farming enterprise. For (national) capital as a whole, the main short-term problem is to ensure access to food on the cheapest possible conditions , be it through capitalist, semi-capitalist, or pre-capitalist modes of production. This means capital as a whole has a vested interest, at least during early phases of capitalist development (which are being reproduced today in most semi-colonial countries, even those semi-industrialized), in maintaining a substantial part of the peasantry under conditions where it still has access to some land — not enough to provide a minimum basis of livelihood, but sufficient to provide part of the annual food intake of the peasant family, forcing these peasants to look for employment during part of the year. Rising capitalism, therefore, \"both ruthlessly suppresses free access to land through generalization of private ownership of land, and skilfully defends minifundia , i.e., small-scale parcellized subsistence farms, which enable wages to be pushed below the subsistence level since this semi-proletarian sub-section of the wage-earning class produces part of its own food.\" Mandel mentions the political and social function of such policies by bourgeois governments: they slow down the concentration and permanent urban settlement of the proletariat; they maintain an easily manipulated electoral base that is less easy to unionize or organize in workers' parties. But the economic function must also be clearly acknowledged, especially in semi-colonial countries. And the direct exploitation of these miserable 'private owners' by capital takes the form not of extortion of land rent but of extortion of usury interest , the parcel owners being permanently burdened by debt. (Footnote 102 references the extensive literature on migrant labour in South Africa and Rhodesia — Wolpe, Bell, Wilson, Arrighi.) The overall evolution of agriculture under capitalism : a resultant of the interaction of five often contradictory tendencies just outlined — an index of the maturity of capitalist development in a national economy as a whole. On a world scale, this culminates in a tragic end-result : the internationalization of absolute land rent means a growing gap between the average productivity of labour in food production in the imperialist countries and in semi-colonial countries. Both the growing penetration of capitalism into semi-colonial agriculture (with the accompanying phenomenon of a shift from food to commercial crops) and the attempts of bourgeois governments to 'stabilize' parcellized subsistence farming tend to increase that gap further. The consequence is that food surpluses on a world scale tend to become increasingly concentrated in fewer and fewer countries — most of them imperialist ones. In other words, differential land rent on the world market is accessible only to a smaller and smaller number of capitalist large-scale farmers (agro-businesses). The empirical data Mandel gives (pp. 69–70): - Wheat yield per hectare in 1977: 0.89 metric tons in Africa, 1.17 in South America, 1.36 in Asia, 1.45 in the USSR , versus 3.86 metric tons in EEC countries, over 4 in the richest US Mid-West states . - In 1976, 90% of world exports of wheat and flour came from five countries : US, Canada, Australia, France, Argentina. - In the US, fewer than 150,000 farms out of 1.7 million — those with sales over $100,000 — accounted for more than 50% of the total value of grain sold; the ratio of concentration is substantially higher in grain exports. Capitalism as a System and the Bourgeoisie as a Class Mandel next highlights an outstanding feature of Volume 3: the way Marx ties together economic analysis and social analysis at the level of the system in its totality — at a level higher than he did in Volume 1 (inside the factory, the production process proper). In Chapters 48 and 51, Marx shows how the reproduction of a specific form of division of the 'national income' (annually produced new value) — between wages on the one hand and profits, interest, rents on the other — automatically reproduces capitalist relations of production , i.e., the basic class relations and class inequality which define the system. Theoretical weakness of reformism, says Mandel: Failure to understand this basic truth. Whether wages are high or low, whether 'indirect' wages (social security) are inexistent or extensive, they cannot upset the basic class relations and class inequality . Wages cannot rise to the point of substantially lowering surplus-value without triggering: - a massive 'investment strike' by capital (steep decline of accumulation), coupled with - a frantic attempt to step up the replacement of living labour by machinery; - both processes acting to halt and reverse the rise in wages through massive unemployment (and cuts in public 'social' expenditure). \"The one thing it is impossible to do with capitalists is to force them to invest or produce at a loss!\" Mandel inserts a footnote (107) on 'indirect' wages. Today, indirect or 'socialized' wages (social security, etc.) are a substantial part of the total reproduction costs of labour-power — according to certain authors, up to 50%, at least in Britain and France (Ian Gough, The Political Economy of the Welfare State , 1979, p. 109; A. Capian, 1979). But this does not represent any 'vertical' redistribution in favour of wages at the expense of profits — because it is compensated by huge deductions from gross wages in the form of taxes and social-security contributions, which also amount to roughly 50%. What is occurring is a 'horizontal' redistribution , in favour of certain sectors of the wage-earning class and at the expense of others. Capian's example: France — higher salary-earning categories have only 18.2% of gross money incomes deducted for social-security contributions, whereas lower wage-earning categories' deductions rise to 31.5%. Further structural argument: The very trend towards increased organic composition, towards increased concentration of capital, towards a strong rise in the minimum requirements for founding new productive units in all branches, constantly consolidates monopoly ownership of the means of production by the bourgeoisie as a class , making it \"physically impossible for even the best-paid workers to save enough out of their wages to embark seriously upon an industrial enterprise of their own.\" While this is less true in small retail trade and small service business (or in small-scale farming during times of acute unemployment; see n. 109 on workers returning to subsistence farming), the overall trend is very clear. Wages tend to be spent over the whole life-span of the wage-earner. They cannot lead to any serious accumulation of capital. (n. 110: with the growth of mass production, workers' 'induced needs' — and the number of goods and services the average social wage is supposed to buy — tend to increase as a by-product of capital accumulation itself.) So wages do not just reproduce labour-power: they also reproduce a special class under permanent economic compulsion to sell its labour-power . Likewise, private appropriation of surplus-value does not just lead to accumulation of capital: it also reproduces a social class which can monopolize the means of production and thus oblige the wage-earners continuously to sell their labour-power; continuously to produce surplus labour, surplus-value, and profits for the exclusive benefit of the latter. Asymmetry between the two processes: The two are not symmetrical . Even when real wages have a tendency to secular increase and 'workers' savings' become a large-scale phenomenon, these do not free the individual wage-earner from his proletarian condition; they just represent 'deferred consumption' — an additional insurance fund over and above socialized 'indirect wages' (social security) to complement his reduced income in times of sickness, unemployment, or retirement, or to defray such extra expenditures as better education or weddings of his children, etc. Under late capitalism, there exists a powerful incentive for the capitalist class to deprive workers of the right to dispose of these savings freely, or even to expropriate them tout court — inflation being only the mildest of the various forms of partial or total expropriation (example: pension funds 'owned' by U.S. labour unions but completely managed by large banks — de facto expropriation already far advanced; completed in Nazi Germany). On the other side: The fact that all sectors of the bourgeois class have access to a fraction of the sum-total of socially produced surplus-value, even if their own capital is not directly used by themselves in surplus-value-producing endeavours, does not imply that this access is equal for every capitalist . Not only does the appearance of monopolies operate in the opposite direction. The law of concentration and centralization of capital acts even more powerfully. Stepped-up competition eliminates many more middle and large-scale capitalists (not to speak of petty ones) than upper layers of the wage-earning class succeed in breaking through the barrier to becoming small independent entrepreneurs in service industry, retail trade, or agriculture. The sum-total of the entire social evolution is a constant increase in the part of the population composed of wage-earners and a constant decline in the part composed of independent businessmen. Mandel asserts: \" Not one of Marx's predictions has been more thoroughly confirmed by empirical evidence (repeated claims to the contrary notwithstanding) than that which identified a long-term trend to class polarization under capitalism.\" He gives the empirical support: in the U.S., wage-earners as part of the total active population increased from 62% in 1880, to 71% in 1910, to 78.2% in 1940, to 89.9% in 1970 . Marx was able to make that sweeping historical forecast, \"so strongly denied by almost all his contemporaries, because… he understood that the division of 'net value' (value added) into wages and surplus-value had to lead, under the pressure of capitalist competition, to more and more wage-earners being unable to become capitalists and fewer and fewer capitalists being able to remain capitalists.\" Capitalist relations of distribution , rooted in capitalist relations of production but by no means identical with them (n. 114 cites Marx's Critique of the Gotha Programme and Trotsky's The Revolution Betrayed on the persistence of bourgeois norms of distribution in the transition period), constantly reproduce these relations of production. But they also reproduce the basic material preconditions of class struggle and class solidarity , both in distribution (the market) and in production (the factory). Four specific structural conditions: (1) The individual worker has no economic resources on which he can fall back , cannot 'wait' till its market price (the offered wage) goes up before selling his labour-power. This makes collective organization of such sales — unionization and collective bargaining — a powerful inbuilt tendency under capitalism, reproducing itself universally wherever wage-labour appears. (2) The fluctuations of the reserve army of labour , in the last analysis, regulate the fluctuations of real wages. This creates a strong inbuilt interest for the mass of wage-earners to ensure high levels of employment — to demand elementary economic policies at the level of the economy as a whole that tend to limit unemployment. (n. 115 concedes: this is at least the long-term interest of all wage-earners; since labour markets are partially fragmented nationally and sectorally — labour mobility not unlimited — short-term interests of relatively privileged parts of the working class might conflict with long-term ones.) (3) The fact that surplus labour is the very essence of surplus-value and profit (more exactly of RIP : Rents, Interests, and Profits) creates an equally strong inbuilt tendency in the working class to challenge speed-ups, reorganizations, and forms of control of the labour process which tend to increase the mass of surplus labour and its degrading, de-humanizing effects upon the individual worker as well as upon whole sections of the working class. (n. 116 cites Braverman, Labor and Monopoly Capital .) (4) The fact that capital can and must periodically challenge all the partial conquests of the workers — both in distribution (increases in wages and social-security payments; free collective bargaining, trade-union rights, the unrestricted right to strike) and in production (reduction of the working week and working day; forms of control over the rhythm of work and the organization of the labour process; union rights inside the work-place in general), especially through ruthless revolutions in technology — \"at least periodically teaches the most intelligent, energetic and militant parts of the working class that (to paraphrase Marx) it is not enough to fight for higher wages, it is also necessary to fight for the abolition of the wage system.\" (n. 117: the printers' union example, undercut by electronic composition; n. 118 cites Marx, 'Wages, Price and Profit'.) Conversely: the fact that, under capitalism, ownership of any substantial quantity of money (the starting level differing by period and country) automatically transforms that money into money capital — which not only automatically partakes in the general distribution of total socially produced surplus-value (through acquiring the average rate of interest) but is also thus transformed potentially into additional productive capital (money capital put at the disposal of 'functioning' capitalists) — creates a powerful class solidarity among all owners of capital in the common exploitation of all wage-earners as a class ; in other words, \"creates the material basis of bourgeois class solidarity and class consciousness .\" In this sense, all capitalists have a common interest in opposing 'excessive' wage increases; in supporting measures which increase the mass of profits; in supporting speed-up practices and 'rationalization investments'; and in generalizing these throughout industry (n. 120 notes this is true not only for productive labour but also for wage-labour employed by commercial and banking capital — such labour does not directly produce surplus-value but enables capital invested in these spheres to appropriate part of surplus-value produced in the productive sectors; industrialists accept this deduction because it enables them to economize their own capital and increases the production of surplus-value via more rapid rotation; at the same time they want to reduce 'circulation costs' to the utmost, since these are deductions from their own profits). All capitalists have a common interest in trying to prevent the rise of militant unionism; or, when that becomes impossible, in trying to limit or curtail trade-union rights, to establish various forms of state control over trade unions, etc. — whatever their differences as to tactics, forms, tempo, or extent. Likewise, the very nature of private ownership of capital and capitalist competition , through the mediation of each firm searching to maximize its own profit (striving for surplus profits over and above the average), creates the mechanisms through which the general laws of motion of the system impose themselves. By this very fact, through elimination of the weakest capitalist firms, it ensures a temporary successful reversal of TRPF . Each capitalist working for his own individual interest thus \"ensures the long-term reproduction, consolidation and expansion of the capitalist system as a whole.\" In the same way: Capitalists' attempts to increase the surplus labour extracted from their own labour-force — by constantly striving to increase the productivity of labour, to organize mass production of an increasing number of commodities, and thereby to lower the value (expressed in gold prices) of all commodities — tend to create a collective interest of the bourgeois class in not limiting mass consumption (except in the initial stages of capitalist industrialization). This helps to counteract realization difficulties which inevitably accompany enlarged reproduction and accumulation, despite the accompanying tendency towards increasing exploitation (towards a historically rising rate of surplus-value). This creates \"a basic class interest of the bourgeoisie in 'normal' rather than 'abnormal' conditions of exploitation , including whenever possible rising real wages and elementary social legislation, in order to defuse the explosive character of the class struggle. Direct repression designed to discipline the working class is used only under exceptional circumstances, in grave structural crises (whether economic, political, or a combination of both).\" The asymmetry of solidarity: Mandel stresses that the two processes just outlined — whereby a self-conscious working class and a self-conscious bourgeois class are constituted — are not symmetrical . In spite of all the inherent segmentations of the working class (craft, national, sex, generational, etc., divisions), \" there are no inbuilt structural obstacles to the overall class solidarity of workers under capitalism . There are only different levels of consciousness, which make the conquest of that overall class solidarity more or less difficult, more or less uneven in time and space.\" The same is not true of bourgeois class solidarity. In periods of prosperity, when struggles are essentially for larger or smaller shares of an increasing mass of profits, class solidarity easily asserts itself among capitalists. In periods of crisis , however, competition has to take \"a much more savage form\" — since for each individual capitalist it is no longer a question of getting more or less profit, but one of his survival as a capitalist . So there are instances of acute crisis of the system in which no economic or political solidarity can assert itself among the capitalist class; in which, even in the face of the gravest collective danger for the system, sectional or individual interests will prevail over collective, class ones. (n. 122: this is true internationally even more than nationally — imperialist wars are the extreme expression of this trend.) Mandel points out this applies to inter-capitalist competition , not to the class struggle between Capital and Labour as such — in that struggle, by contrast, \"the graver the socio-political crisis, the more sharply ruling-class solidarity will assert itself.\" The fundamental asymmetry , then, is structurally connected with the basically different relations of capitalists and wage-earners towards private property and competition . Private property and competition are built into the very nature of the capitalist class . Competition among wage-earners, however, is \" imposed upon them from outside, not structurally inherent in the very nature of the class .\" On the contrary, wage-earners normally and instinctively strive towards collective cooperation and solidarity (n. 123 roots this in the very process of production under large-scale industry, based upon cooperative labour organization). Hence, to whatever extent competition among themselves is periodically reproduced — especially in times of economic crisis or after major social or political defeats — it can always be overcome by subsequent efforts to organize and raise class consciousness, assisted by the very advances of capital accumulation itself. The Mystification of 'Factors of Production' and Scientific Labour In Part Seven of Volume 3, Marx pays great attention to the mystifying appearance of revenues 'produced' by different 'factors of production' : land, labour, and capital. Mandel observes that this mystification has, in our day, been extended through the quest for growth rates or income accretions 'produced' by scientific progress or even by higher education (n. 124 cites Blaug's Economics of Education , including titles like 'Investment in Human Capital', 'Rate of Return on Investment in Education', 'The Productivity of Universities', etc.). Mandel's critique of this mystification: \"In and of itself, 'science' produces neither value nor income. The results of scientific research, incorporated into new forms of machinery and new forms of labour organization, increase productivity of labour and thus undoubtedly contribute to the increase of material wealth. But this is something quite different from the production of value or income.\" What these formulas mystify is the fact that, under capitalism, private ownership of the means of production, and the transformation of manual and intellectual labour (including scientifically creative labour), \"enable the capitalist (the capitalist firm) to incorporate into the total value produced in the course of the commodity-producing process the results of the cooperation, inventiveness and skill of all manpower employed . And this occurs essentially in the form of surplus-value, since the results in question do not directly change the reproduction costs of labour-power, which alone represent necessary labour.\" Qualities of labour thus appear as qualities separate and apart from labour: \"as either qualities of 'capital' (which is represented as a mass of things, instruments, machinery and other means of production) or qualities of 'science' (which is again separated from labour as some pure product of the brain).\" For Marx , scientific labour is the very essence of 'general labour' , i.e., creative labour developing new discoveries and inventions. But like collective (socialized) labour, it is indissociably related to the process of cooperation of many manual and intellectual workers working together. Mandel quotes a long passage (Volume 3, pp. 198–9) on the savings in the use of fixed capital as a result of large-scale, directly social, socialized labour, of direct cooperation within the production process; on large-scale production as the only condition for the economy arising from productive consumption in common; and on how \"it is only the experience of the combined worker that discovers and demonstrates how inventions already made can most simply be developed, how to overcome the practical frictions that arise in putting the theory into practice.\" Marx distinguishes universal labour (all scientific work, all discovery and invention — brought about partly by cooperation of men now living, partly by building on earlier work) from communal labour (which simply involves the direct cooperation of individuals). The Destiny of Capitalism: The Collapse Controversy The final and longest section asks: \"Does Capital contain a theory of the final and inevitable downfall of the capitalist mode of production? Is the answer to be found in Volume 3, and specifically in Marx's determination of TRPF? Do the laws of motion of the capitalist mode of production imply that the system cannot forever survive its inner contradictions?\" These questions — the \" collapse controversy \" — have been asked ever since Capital first appeared, and have played a crucial role in the history of Marxist theory after Marx and in the international labour movement. The Orthodox Initial Position The initial position of \"orthodox\" Marxists inside the Second International was cautious but clear: the system would collapse in the end through a general sharpening of all its internal contradictions . Engels by and large supported this view (n. 126: Erfurt Programme of the German SPD, supervised by Engels; August Bebel's famous Reichstag speech of 3 February 1893, highly praised by Engels, presenting collapse as resulting from interaction of the decline of the middle classes, growing concentration/centralization of capital, growing class polarization, growing class contradictions, successive grave economic crises, growing dangers of war, growing threats against political democracy, and growing class consciousness of the proletariat). The view could base itself on a number of passages from Capital — though admittedly more from Volume 1 than Volume 3 (n. 127 cites Capital Vol. 1, pp. 929–30; and adds: \"Lucio Colletti is wrong to reduce Marx's 'collapse theory' simply to the theory of the TRPF\"). Its main merit was to integrate the class struggle, the growth of the labour movement and of working-class consciousness, into overall perspectives regarding the final destiny of the capitalist system . A crucial conceptual distinction: Whether capitalism can survive indefinitely or is doomed to collapse is not to be confused with the notion of its inevitable replacement by a higher form of social organization (socialism) . It is quite possible to postulate the inevitable collapse of capitalism without postulating the inevitable victory of socialism. Indeed, rather early in revolutionary Marxism, the two were conceptually separated in a radical fashion, with the destiny of capitalism formulated as a dilemma: the system cannot survive, but may give way either to socialism or to barbarism . (n. 128 cites Rosa Luxemburg, 'What Does the Spartakusbund Want?', 1972.) While both Marx and Engels — especially the older Engels, faced with the tremendous and apparently irresistible rise of the modern labour movement — exhibited a robust optimism as to the future of socialism, \"they were always careful, when the question was posed at its most general, abstract, historical level, to reject any idea of historical inevitable sequences of social organization (modes of production) .\" On a number of occasions they pointed out that the passage from one mode of production to another depended upon the outcome of concrete class struggles , which might end either with the victory of the more progressive, revolutionary class, or in the mutual destruction of both the old ruling class and its revolutionary adversary and in a protracted decadence of society . Bernstein's Revisionism The initial position was challenged by the so-called revisionists around the German Eduard Bernstein , who denied that there was any inherent tendency for the inner contradictions of capitalism to sharpen. They postulated, on the contrary, that these contradictions would decrease . They did not, however, conclude that capitalism would survive forever, but rather believed it would fade away gradually , so there was no need to overthrow it by revolutionary means (n. 129 cites Bernstein, Evolutionary Socialism ). Most later variants of gradualism and reformism (including Euro-communism in recent years) have common roots in Bernstein's writings, which are \"remarkable for the clear and consistent way in which they pose the problem — the only trouble being that their predictions proved to be wrong .\" Mandel's historical rebuttal: \"Far from leading to permanent peace, capitalism has led to two world wars and risks a third one, suicidal for the whole of mankind . Far from its leading to an ever-smoother functioning of the international capitalist economy, we have witnessed the catastrophic crises of 1920–21, 1929–32 and 1938 , followed, after the post-Second World War boom, by a new long slump starting in the late sixties or early seventies . And far from ever-increasing freedom and democracy, the twentieth century has seen much greater repression and far bloodier dictatorships than anything Marx, Engels or other nineteenth-century socialists ever witnessed or could have imagined in their day.\" (n. 130: Crosland, The Future of Socialism , as a typical example.) Rosa Luxemburg's Collapse Theory It is in this context that followers of Marx attempted to formulate more rigorously the probable destiny of capitalism. Rosa Luxemburg was the first to try to elaborate, on a strictly scientific basis, a theory of inevitable collapse , in The Accumulation of Capital (1913; English trans. 1963). Her argument: enlarged reproduction, with full realization of surplus-value produced during the process of production , was impossible under 'pure' capitalism . That mode of production, therefore, had an inherent tendency to expand into a non-capitalist milieu — gobbling up surviving petty commodity production inside the capitalist metropolis and expanding continuously towards the non-capitalist periphery (colonial and semi-colonial countries). This expansion — including its most radical forms: contemporary colonialism and murderous colonial wars, imperialism, and imperialist wars — was indispensable for the survival of the system . If and when that non-capitalist milieu disappeared, the system would collapse, since it would be unable to fully realize surplus-value. But Luxemburg made it clear that, long before that final moment, the simple consequences of these increasingly violent forms of expansion, as well as the consequences of the gradual shrinking of the non-capitalist milieu, would sharpen the inner contradictions of the system to the point of explosion , thereby preparing its revolutionary overthrow. The methodological objection (raised by Bukharin in Imperialism and the Accumulation of Capital ; by Henryk Grossmann, Das Akkumulations- und Zusammenbruchsgesetz des kapitalistischen Systems , 1967, p. 22; by Claudio Napoleoni; and echoed elsewhere): that by basing the perspective of inevitable collapse exclusively on the system's laws of motion, its inner economic mechanism, Luxemburg was moving back towards 'economism' — regressing from the way Marx, Engels, and their first disciples always integrated economic laws and movements with the class struggle. Mandel's reply: \"This objection, however, is unjustified.\" While it is true that the contemporary history of capitalism — indeed of any mode of production in any epoch — cannot be satisfactorily explained if the class struggle (especially its outcome after certain decisive battles) is not treated as a partially autonomous factor , it is likewise true that \"the whole meaning of Marxism disappears if this partial autonomy is transformed into an absolute one.\" It is precisely the merit of Luxemburg, as well as of several of her subsequent antagonists in the collapse controversy, \"to have related the ups and downs of the class struggle to the inner laws of motion of the system.\" If one were to assume that the infinite adaptability of the capitalist system, the political astuteness of the bourgeoisie, or the inability of the proletariat to raise its consciousness, or the alleged 'integration' of the working class into bourgeois society, \"could, in the long run and for an undefined length of time, neutralize or reverse that system's inner laws of motion and intrinsic contradictions, i.e., prevent them from asserting themselves, then the only scientifically correct conclusion would be that these laws of motion do not correspond to the system's essence : in other words, that Marx was basically mistaken when he thought he had discovered that essence.\" This is different from the possibility of temporary ups and downs in the sharpening of contradictions — which are not only possible but even inevitable, as Marx himself pointed out in his treatment of TRPF. Bukharin and Varga A second attempt to produce a scientifically rigorous collapse theory (though less rigorous, Mandel says, than Luxemburg's) was made during and immediately after WW I by certain leading radical Marxist economists who greatly influenced Lenin in drafting Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism : the Russian Nikolai Bukharin and the Hungarian Eugen Varga . While avoiding any mono-causal reduction, these authors formulated the hypothesis that capitalism had entered an irreversible period of historical decline , resulting from a combined manifestation of all its sharpened contradictions : reduction of markets; decline of world trade; decline of the international division of labour; decline of money economy and even partial reversion to barter and pre-capitalist forms of production in capitalist countries; decline of material production; collapse of the credit system; absolute decline in workers' standard of living; recurrent wars and civil wars; recurrent revolutionary explosions and victorious socialist revolutions. Mandel's assessment: While this analysis may offer a relatively convincing description and explanation of what actually occurred in 1914 (or even 1912)–1921 and again in 1930–40 (or even in certain parts of the world in 1945–8), \"it gets into serious trouble once confronted with post-Second World War developments in the international capitalist economy. Tending to theoretical eclecticism, it lacks the deeper rigour needed to tie all these various developments to the basic laws of motion of the system. In particular, it avoids any discussion of the reasons why the countervailing factors, enumerated by Marx as able temporarily to neutralize TRPF, would definitely cease to be effective in the epoch of capitalist decline; why the huge devalorization and destruction of capital which occurred in the 1929–32 crisis and the Second World War, coupled with a huge upsurge in s′ (as a result both of catastrophic working-class defeats and of a powerful increase in the productivity of labour in department II, as a result of a new technological revolution), could not lead to a new upsurge in the productive forces — inevitably ending in a new reassertion of sharpened contradictions of the system.\" (n. 135: Varga took a more cautious attitude after WW II, representing a 'bridge' position on the way to the harmonicist conceptions of the theoreticians of 'state monopoly capitalism'.) The offshoot: the concept of the 'general crisis of capitalism' — in which the emphasis has become progressively shifted from the inner laws of motion of the system towards the outside challenges it increasingly meets as the result of a chain of victorious socialist revolutions (which have shrunk the geographical area in which it can operate). Initially (arising from the October Revolution), the concept still established an interrelation between that outside challenge and the ensuing sharpening of the system's inner contradictions. But this has become less and less the case in later variants, especially the 'state monopoly capitalism' theory fully developed after WW II. The paradox pursued to its ultimate: Here the 'basic' contradiction is clearly defined as that between the 'socialist camp' and the 'capitalist camp' , and no longer as the increasingly explosive inner contradictions of the capitalist system itself. The paradox is pushed to the point where Soviet authors (e.g., N. Inosemzev, Der heutige Kapitalismus , 1973) seriously assert that, as a result of the 'competition between the two systems', capitalism is 'condemned' to continuous growth ! In this way the theory of collapse is \" 'dialectically' turned into its very opposite: the possibility for capitalism to survive forever .\" The system's capacity to eliminate for an indefinite period the most serious effects of its inner contradictions is postulated — until such time as the economic, social, and cultural superiority of the socialist camp finally asserts itself. Mandel's political diagnosis: This intellectual contortion is \"structurally related to the specific interests of the Soviet bureaucracy — both its attempts to maintain conditions of peaceful coexistence with international capitalism, and its concern to maintain the subordination of a large section of the international labour movement to its own diplomatic manoeuvres — and, as such, represents a typical phenomenon of ideological mystification .\" (n. 137 additionally cites Mandel's Late Capitalism , pp. 513–22, and Jacques Valier, Le PCF et le capitalisme monopoliste d'état , 1976, for a more general critique of 'state monopoly capitalism' theory.) Henryk Grossmann A third — once again, more rigorous — attempt was offered in the late twenties by the Polish Marxist Henryk Grossmann . This was essentially a generalization (an extreme extrapolation) of Marx's law of TRPF. Grossmann tried to prove that, in the long run, countervailing forces cannot prevent the law from asserting itself with increasing strength — up to the point where all accumulated capital tends to be unable to become valorized , i.e., to the point where the total mass of surplus-value cannot ensure sufficient accumulation, even if the subsistence of the capitalist class itself falls to zero . Mandel's assessment: \"There are many weaknesses in this theory\" (n. 139: the most systematic critiques are by Fritz Sternberg, 1930, and Nathalia Moszkowska, 1935). The main one is that Grossmann does not really prove that all the countervailing forces gradually lose their capacity to neutralize the declining rate of profit. He especially underestimates the effects of massive devalorization (and destruction) of capital , which has historically proven much larger in scope than he visualized (his book was finished before the 1929–32 crisis unfolded fully and before WW II's destruction). Therefore Grossmann's somewhat arbitrary numerical starting point — the reproduction schemas Otto Bauer worked out in reply to Luxemburg's Accumulation of Capital — leads to results that ignore the effects of devalorization cycles of capital. Such a hypothesis is untenable in light of real capitalism's history (which has witnessed twenty-one crises of overproduction since the establishment of the world market for industrial goods). Marx explicitly points out the devalorization-of-capital function of crises in Chapter 15 of Volume 3. Hence, says Mandel, one can only consider Grossmann's successive figures as representing not annual totals but averages for seven/ten-year cycles . Thus the final collapse of the system is postponed to the twenty-second century (after thirty-seven seven/ten-year cycles). If the initial proportions between departments I and II were more realistic — and they should have been, since in the 1920s capitalism had nowhere even approached a situation where two-thirds of current production occurred in department I — the postponement would be even more pronounced: only after fifty or sixty cycles, i.e., after 400 or 500 years . Inadvertently, Grossmann, obsessed by his mono-causal explanation, was led to demonstrate precisely the opposite of what he intended: the extreme longevity rather than the final collapse of the system, as a function of its inner laws of motion. Baran and Sweezy — Not Really a Collapse Theory Mandel distinguishes the Baran/Sweezy theory of the growing difficulty of 'surplus realization' by monopoly capitalism from a collapse theory. It is not a variant of Luxemburg's, nor a fourth distinct collapse theory of its own, because Baran and Sweezy, while underlining the growing difficulties for 'surplus realization', at the same time stress the system's capacity to integrate the working class socially and thereby ensure its perpetuity — albeit under conditions of permanent quasi-stagnation — rather than its inevitable collapse. Like the more extreme proponents of 'state monopoly capitalism' theory, these authors \"have to project the system's real enemies outside the system itself: third-world peasants; marginalized super-exploited layers; and so on. But they are nowhere able to demonstrate that these social forces anywhere have a potential social and economic strength comparable to that of the modern proletariat. Since such forces are not vital to the system's basic productive relations, they can be variously ignored, or integrated, or crushed, without making the system incapable of functioning.\" So this is not really a 'collapse of capitalism' theory at all. (n. 141: filiation between Baran/Sweezy and Kalecki, Steindl, Joan Robinson; n. 142: it is no accident that most 'third-worldist' Marxists exaggerate capitalism's ability to 'restructure' itself on a world scale by purely economic processes.) Mandel's Own Synthesis — Automation and the Absolute Decline of Value-Production As with the mono-causal crisis theories, \"there are obviously correct elements in each of the three versions of collapse theory outlined above. These have to be tied together in order to furnish a coherent theory of the inevitable collapse of capitalism, consistent with all the inner laws of motion and contradictions of that mode of production, as unfolded by Marx's analysis in Capital .\" One element in Grossmann's analysis is important, if not decisive, as the starting-point: the point in time when, in addition to the tendency of the rate of surplus-value to decline, the mass of surplus-value ceases to grow and begins to decline — first gradually, then permanently. This would obviously be \"the most serious blow to a continuous process of capitalist accumulation.\" Grossmann, however, fails to point out the concrete content of such an incipient decline in surplus-value production, \"which I have tried to specify in Late Capitalism : a level of mechanization, of semi-automation — let us say, of spreading full automation — of a growing number of branches of output, in which the total input of productive labour-hours starts to decline, hence in which total value-production declines.\" This does not automatically imply an immediate decline in the absolute mass of surplus-value , since the big increase in productivity of labour inherent in 'robotism' can reduce necessary labour-time proportionally to the reduction of absolute value production. In the long run, however, this is impossible without more and more severe reductions even in real wages. After a certain point, moreover, \"it becomes physically impossible.\" So the extension of automation beyond a given ceiling leads, inevitably, first to a reduction in the total volume of value produced, then to a reduction in the total volume of surplus-value produced. This in turn unleashes a fourfold combined 'collapse crisis' : A huge crisis of decline in the rate of profit; A huge crisis of realization (the increase in productivity implied by robotism expands the mass of use-values produced in an even higher ratio than it reduces real wages, and a growing proportion of these use-values becomes unsaleable); A huge social crisis (n. 143 cites Marx, p. 372: \"A development in the productive forces that would reduce the absolute number of workers, and actually enable the country to accomplish its entire production in a shorter period of time, would produce a revolution, since it would put the majority of the population out of action.\"); A huge crisis of 'reconversion' (capitalism's capacity to adapt) through devalorization — the specific forms of capital destruction threatening \"not only the survival of human civilization but even the physical survival of mankind or of life on our planet.\" Mandel's caveat regarding 'limits of growth' (n. 144): He notes he cannot here deal with the problem of 'limits of growth' argued by some to be inherent not in the capitalist mode of production but in large-scale industrial production itself, seen as inevitably depleting natural resources. Marx was very much aware of the problem (cf. Volume 3, pp. 949–50; Capital Vol. 1, pp. 636–8) but saw it as \"a by-product of the specific (and distorted) forms of technological development characteristic of capitalism, not as an inevitable product of the application of the natural sciences to production. This implies that the problem is soluble in a different social framework, without mankind having to forgo the advantages of freeing itself from uncreative mechanical labour.\" Mandel cites ecological critics Barry Commoner ( The Closing Circle , 1972) and Harry Rothman ( Murderous Providence , 1972) as similar conclusions from a non-Marxist ecological standpoint. A possible way out — but only postponing: there is one way out via the massive transformation of 'services' into commodity-producing branches (which add to total value production). Indeed, it is already starting in key services: health, education, banking, public administration . \"This indicates how wrong it is to speak of late capitalism as a post-industrial society .\" (n. 145 cites Daniel Bell, The Coming of Post-Industrial Society , 1973.) On the contrary, \"we are only now entering the age of full industrialization of a whole series of branches which have escaped that process up to now.\" But this only postpones the time of reckoning . For the industrialization of service sectors reproduces there, after a certain transition period, the very same processes of massive mechanization, semi-automation, and full automation for which micro-processors have already provided the necessary technical tools. (The same applies to the industrialization of underdeveloped countries as a way out of structural crisis.) \"So it is impossible to see how capitalism can escape its final fate: economic collapse .\" The Subjective Dimension of Collapse Mandel adds a fundamentally important subjective dimension. With the development of semi-automation and automation, a new significant reversal occurs of the revolution constantly produced by capitalism in labour organization and the actual labour process. A massive reintroduction of intellectual labour into the process of production is inevitable , alongside at least a relative decline in the extreme parcellization of labour characteristic of Taylorism . The more wage-labour is employed for supervising functions and maintenance of delicate and costly equipment, the more its own skill, level of culture, and degree of involvement in the production process becomes an indispensable element of reproduction of capital . Hence: - Not only are the cooperative qualities of objectively socialized labour inside the factory developed to a higher degree; - But \"the consciousness of the workers that they are able to run factories instead of capitalists or capitalist managers takes a giant leap forward.\" Thus the growing crisis of capitalist relations of production — both objectively and subjectively, i.e., in terms of their legitimacy in the eyes of the working class and of larger and larger sectors of the population as a whole — and the challenge which workers' struggles pose for these, become \"an integral part of the system's tendency towards collapse.\" The necessary dialectical negation: Such a trend towards upgrading labour in productive sectors with the highest technological development must \"of necessity be accompanied by its very negation\": a rise in mass unemployment , in the extent of marginalized sectors of the population, in the number of those who 'drop out' and of all those whom the 'final' development of capitalist technology expels from the process of production. This means \"only that the growing challenges to capitalist relations of production inside the factory are accompanied by growing challenges to all basic bourgeois relations and values in society as a whole , and these too constitute an important and periodically explosive element of the tendency of capitalism to final collapse.\" The Final Thesis: Barbarism or Socialism Mandel closes with his characteristic dual-formulation: as he said earlier, not necessarily of collapse in favour of a higher form of social organization or civilization . Precisely as a function of capitalism's very degeneration , phenomena of cultural decay, of retrogression in the fields of ideology and respect for human rights, multiply alongside the uninterrupted succession of multiform crises with which that degeneration \"will face us (has already faced us).\" Barbarism, as one possible result of the collapse of the system, is \"a much more concrete and precise perspective today than it was in the twenties and thirties.\" Even the horrors of Auschwitz and Hiroshima \"will appear mild compared to the horrors with which a continuous decay of the system will confront mankind.\" Under these circumstances, \"the struggle for a socialist outcome takes on the significance of a struggle for the very survival of human civilization and the human race .\" The proletariat, as Marx has shown, \" unites all the objective prerequisites for successfully conducting that struggle; today, that remains truer than ever. And it has at least the potential for acquiring the subjective prerequisites too, for a victory of world socialism. Whether that potential will actually be realized will depend, in the last analysis, upon the conscious efforts of organized revolutionary Marxists, integrating themselves with the spontaneous periodic striving of the proletariat to reorganize society along socialist lines, and leading it to precise goals: the conquest of state power and radical social revolution.\" The final line, characteristic of Mandel's revolutionary optimism: \"I see no more reason to be pessimistic today as to the outcome of that endeavour than Marx was at the time he wrote Capital .\" Mandel closes with a Note on the edition : numbered footnotes are those of the original text; those marked by asterisks, etc., are the translator's. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles Revealed Mandel's Introduction reveals several deep structural principles that operate as the unifying architecture of the chapter: Principle 1: The \"essence/appearance\" distinction as epistemic, not merely terminological. The most theoretically consequential move Mandel makes is the insistence that the transformation of values into prices of production is \"only a mediating link in the process of cognition, which does not yet deal with immediately verifiable, empirical data, i.e., market prices.\" This is more than a defense of Marx against von Bortkiewicz. It is a claim about what kind of theory Marxist economics is . If value relations are essence and prices of production are a first mediation still on the plane of essence, then market prices (which are what capitalists empirically encounter) are appearance. This means: (a) the value category is not falsifiable directly by price data; (b) any \"solution\" to the transformation problem which treats the problem as one of numerical computation between empirically observable magnitudes is category-confused ; (c) the empirical verification of the labour theory of value must occur at the level of tendencies over time (e.g., labour costs falling as a share of total production costs across all branches over long periods), not at the level of short-run cross-sectional price fitting. Principle 2: Tendencies, not states. Nearly every important concept in the chapter is a tendency : the equalization of the rate of profit, the fall of the average rate of profit, the rise of the organic composition of capital, the monopoly surplus profit, the formation of the reserve army. Mandel repeatedly stresses that these are \"always a tendency, never a permanent reality\" — a process which \"constantly realizes itself by negating itself.\" This is dialectical in the strict sense: it is not a matter of approximation to an equilibrium, but of a law which operates via its own negation . The fall of the profit rate operates via the periodic recuperation of the profit rate; equalization operates via persistent inequality; the reproduction of class relations operates via struggle. Collapse (for Mandel) operates via devalorization cycles that both postpone and deepen it. Principle 3: The intrinsic relation between economic and social analysis. The chapter culminates in the claim that capitalist relations of distribution automatically reproduce capitalist relations of production , which in turn automatically reproduce both working-class and capitalist class consciousness and solidarity. But the two class-solidarities are structurally asymmetric. Capitalists are constitutively competitive (private property and competition are \"built into the very nature of the capitalist class\"), and so their class solidarity is unstable in crisis. Workers are constitutively cooperative (their production process is cooperative), and so their class competition is \"imposed upon them from outside, not structurally inherent in the very nature of the class.\" This is a strong, provocative thesis about the structural asymmetry of the two classes under capitalism — the material basis for believing that working-class solidarity is more robust in the long run than capitalist solidarity. It is also the reason Mandel insists that Marx's prediction of class polarization has been amply confirmed empirically (US data 1880 → 1970). Principle 4: Mono-causality is always wrong. Mandel's methodological commitment to multi-causality runs through three areas: crisis theory (disproportionality / under-consumption / over-accumulation must be integrated), collapse theory (Luxemburg / Bukharin-Varga / Grossmann must be integrated), and the theory of the profit rate (the rate of profit as a function of both the organic composition and the rate of surplus-value). The corollary is a warning against every attempt to reduce a complex social process to a single determining factor — a warning which turns out to have political consequences too: mono-causal crisis theories typically end by arming reformists or employers with arguments against the working class. Principle 5: The economics of class struggle. Mandel repeatedly draws out the class-political implications of what appear to be purely theoretical debates. Under-consumption theories and pure over-accumulation theories are \"dangerous\" because either they imply that the crisis could be solved by raising workers' purchasing power (reformist temptation) or that it could be solved by cutting wages (employer argument). The correct theory — crises are simultaneously over-accumulation and overproduction — can only be overcome by simultaneous rise in profit rate and expansion of the market. This is a theory which \"disarms both the employers' and the reformists' arguments\" and makes the working class the historical agent of a solution requiring the abolition of the wage system, not its negotiation. Broader Theoretical Implications 1. The opening up of the \"time-span\" problem. One of Mandel's most original contributions here — and one that goes beyond Marx's own explicit formulations — is the systematic distinction between (a) the industrial-cycle time-span, (b) the long-wave time-span (forty to fifty years), and (c) the secular \"collapse\" time-span. This has since become a major research program in Marxist political economy, and Mandel's Late Capitalism and Long Waves of Capitalist Development made the long-wave dimension a central contested topic. The Introduction integrates this into Marx's framework by arguing that Marx's countervailing forces are precisely the ones that temporarily neutralize the tendency, with their effects lasting longer than one cycle, thereby producing the long-wave shape of capitalist growth. 2. The externalization and internalization of class enemies. Mandel's critique of Baran/Sweezy and of 'state monopoly capitalism' theory turns on a single penetrating insight: both theories end up locating capitalism's real enemy outside the system — third-world peasants, marginalized super-exploited layers, the Soviet bloc. Mandel's rebuttal — that such forces are not vital to the system's basic productive relations and hence can be variously ignored, integrated, or crushed without making the system incapable of functioning — is decisive. The modern proletariat is vital to capitalist production: it produces surplus-value, and its struggle directly constrains capital's valorization. Any \"collapse theory\" that substitutes some other agent is, Mandel concludes, \"not really a 'collapse of capitalism' theory at all.\" 3. Mass devalorization as the missing variable. Grossmann's failure — and by extension the failure of \"pure\" extrapolations of TRPF — is the failure to assign sufficient weight to massive devalorization and destruction of capital (financial crises, wars, bankruptcies), which historically has been more expansive than any theoretical model assumed. Devalorization is not a marginal correction; it is the mechanism by which the falling rate of profit is periodically arrested. Its historical forms — the 1929–32 crash and WW II — were massive enough to permit a long-wave upswing. This both undermines pure extrapolations to \"collapse in N cycles\" and shows why the collapse must be understood as a multi-dimensional crisis, not a mathematical limit. 4. The semiotics of scientific labour. Mandel's discussion of 'general labour' and 'communal labour' (drawing on Marx, Vol. 3, pp. 198–9) is a compressed but powerful argument about the ideological mis-representation of intellectual production under capitalism. The individual scientific worker's contribution appears as a property either of \"capital\" or of \"science\"-as-detached-from-labour. This \"mystification\" — parallel to Marx's treatment of the trinity formula (land, labour, capital) in Part Seven — is, for Mandel, one of the most important ideological functions of capitalist political economy. Educated workers are supposed to accept that their contribution is recognized in \"human capital\" schemes; in fact, the entire intellectual product of their labour is incorporated into capital's total value as surplus-value, since it does not change the reproduction costs of labour-power. 5. The contingent, class-struggle-mediated nature of the passage beyond capitalism. Perhaps the most important conceptual distinction in the entire chapter — and the one most at odds with mechanical readings of Marx — is that collapse is not the same as socialist revolution . The outcome is a bifurcation : socialism or barbarism. Marx and Engels themselves, Mandel notes, \"were always careful… to reject any idea of historical inevitable sequences of social organization.\" The passage from one mode of production depends on \"concrete class struggles,\" which might end in the victory of the progressive class, or in \"the mutual destruction of both the old ruling class and its revolutionary adversary,\" or in protracted decay. This is not \"economism\"; it is rigorous Marxism, and it is the necessary frame within which all the purely economic tendencies Mandel has defended become politically meaningful. Broader Practical Implications 1. For revolutionary strategy. Mandel's political conclusion is that the objective prerequisites for socialist transformation are more fully present than ever, and the subjective prerequisites depend on \"organized revolutionary Marxists\" integrating with \"the spontaneous periodic striving of the proletariat.\" The Introduction thus ends by framing theory as preparation for practice — a stance which is also behind its insistence that theoretical errors (mono-causal crisis theory) can produce political errors (reformism, wage-cut arguments). 2. For the critique of reformism. Mandel's demonstration that wages cannot be raised sufficiently to abolish exploitation, that social-security systems only redistribute horizontally among wage-earners, and that any serious redistribution of income in favour of wages would trigger an investment strike and rapid mechanization, is the theoretical core of his critique of all gradualism — from Bernstein to Euro-communism. The Introduction explicitly links theoretical reformulations of capitalist survivability to the bureaucratic interests of the Soviet state and to reformist trade-union leaderships in Western countries. 3. For the study of imperialist political economy. The sections on capitalist agriculture and world food markets offer a compressed but formidable theory of the internationalization of absolute land rent, the role of agro-business, the concentration of food surpluses in a small number of imperialist countries, and the strategies of bourgeois governments in maintaining minifundia while suppressing free access to land. These are foundational for the later literature on the political economy of hunger, agrarian change, and dependency. 4. For philosophy of science. Mandel's critique of mathematical modeling — \"mathematical models cannot, in and of themselves, 'solve' theoretical problems; they can only formalize interrelations previously understood as such\" — is a methodological claim with consequences far beyond economics. It warns against the substitution of formal elegance for theoretical adequacy, and it identifies a particular trap: models can be internally consistent while their premises silently assume away the very phenomenon under study (as in the Okishio theorem's bracketing of fixed capital; as in the von Bortkiewicz solution's exclusion of department III). The Chapter thus doubles as a philosophical defense of the priority of conceptual comprehension over formalization in the social sciences. The Closing Vision The chapter ends not in a technical theorem but in a vision: capitalism is not unstable because it is imperfectly managed, but because its very internal mechanisms — the rising organic composition of capital, the tendency of the profit rate to fall, the asymmetries of realization, the reproduction of class relations, the inherent drive to substitute dead labour for living labour — cannot be stabilized without abolishing private property, competition, profit, and commodity production themselves. Whether this inner logic culminates in socialist revolution or in social and even civilizational barbarism is, for Mandel, not determined in advance : it depends on the outcome of concrete class struggles. The objective preconditions are in place, and the system's own development — automation, socialization of labour, mass education of workers — produces them ever more fully. But the transition itself is a matter of revolutionary political practice. Mandel's Introduction is, in the end, an argument for reading Capital as one book — production, circulation, and their unity in the process as a whole — and for reading it as a theory both of the necessity and of the indeterminacy of capitalism's fate . ← Previous Chapter Volume 2 • Chapter 21 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 1"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 1", "title": "Chapter 1", "subtitle": "Cost Price and Profit", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 1/Chapter 1 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 6908, "read_time": "35 min read", "tags": ["Cost Price & Rate of Profit", "Fetishism & Mystification of Capital"], "keywords": ["Cost Price (k = c + v)", "Cost Price vs Real Value (c + v + s)", "Disappearance of Surplus-Value Source in Cost Price", "Profit as Mystified Form of Surplus-Value", "Capitalist Calculation of Costs"], "text": "Chapter 1 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,866 words ⏱️ ~34 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Exhaustive Analysis of Capital, Volume 3, Chapter 1: Cost Price and Profit 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS: This chapter is the opening hinge of Volume 3 of Marx's Capital , and its function is architectural. Volumes 1 and 2 had successively dissected, first, the immediate production process (where \"all secondary influences external to this process were left out of account\") and, second, the circulation process, culminating in the demonstration that \"the capitalist production process, taken as a whole, is a unity of the production and circulation processes.\" The opening pages explicitly refuse the temptation to \"make general reflections on this unity.\" Instead, the chapter announces the concrete task of the volume: \"to discover and present the concrete forms which grow out of the process of capital's movement considered as a whole.\" These concrete forms are the categories visible \"on the surface of society, in the action of different capitals on one another, i.e. in competition, and in the everyday consciousness of the agents of production themselves.\" The chapter's central problem is therefore essentially epistemological as much as economic: How does the surplus-value produced in the hidden recesses of the labour process reappear on the surface of society as profit? The core thesis is that this reappearance is not a mere change of label but a genuine, structurally necessary transformation of form — a \"mystification\" that arises inevitably from the capitalist mode of production itself. The surplus-value that Volume 1 proved to originate exclusively in the exploitation of living labour is, on the surface, detached from its source and re-attributed to the total capital advanced. The category that performs this displacement is the cost price (k), and the category that results from it is profit (p). The argument unfolds in two movements. First, Marx isolates the cost price as the portion of commodity value that simply replaces the capital value expended — the sum c + v as it appears from the capitalist's standpoint. The formula C = c + v + s collapses into C = k + s. Second, he shows that because the cost price obliterates the distinction between constant and variable capital — the very distinction on which Volume 1's theory of surplus-value depended — the surplus-value comes to appear as an emanation of the total capital applied rather than of the variable portion alone. It thus takes on the transformed form of profit , giving the surface formula C = k + p. Intellectually, this chapter occupies a decisive place in the whole structure of Capital. It is the first step in the long \"descent\" from essence to appearance that the remainder of Volume 3 will pursue: from surplus-value to profit, from profit to the general rate of profit, from value to price of production, from surplus-profit to rent, from interest and profit of enterprise. The chapter is therefore the seedbed for the \"law of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall,\" the transformation problem, and the theory of the \"trinity formula.\" It also performs a polemical role: it exposes the errors of the \"vulgar economists\" — Torrens, Malthus, Proudhon, and (via a footnote) N. W. Senior — who either confused cost price with value, or claimed that profit springs from circulation rather than production. The Balzac allusion to Les Paysans grounds the theoretical point that in a society dominated by capitalist production, \"even the non-capitalist producer is dominated by capitalist ways of thinking.\" The intellectual objective is thus threefold: (1) to derive category-wise the form of profit from the form of surplus-value; (2) to identify the cost price as the mechanism of concealment; and (3) to establish, before proceeding to the general rate of profit, that a range of sale prices lies between the value and the cost price of commodities — a fact that will be essential for explaining under-selling and the determination of production prices. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS: The Transitional Problem: From the Hidden Process to the Surface of Society The chapter opens by situating itself within the architecture of the whole work. Volume 1 studied \"the immediate production process\" in isolation, deliberately excluding external secondary influences. Volume 2 added the circulation process, showing in its Part Three — where circulation mediates social reproduction — that the capitalist production process is a \"unity of production and circulation.\" Now, however, the concern is not to repeat these abstractions but to capture \"the concrete forms which grow out of the process of capital's movement considered as a whole.\" The key phrase is \"concrete forms.\" The categories developed in Volume 1 (constant capital, variable capital, surplus-value) were essential forms; the categories to be developed in Volume 3 are surface forms — the forms in which capital \"confronts one another in competition, and in the everyday consciousness of the agents of production themselves.\" The gap between essence and appearance is not a flaw in perception but a real, necessary inversion generated by the mode of production. This is the methodological premise on which everything else in the chapter rests: to explain the surface, one must first derive it from the essence, not simply accept it. The Fundamental Formula and the Birth of the Cost Price The analysis begins with the value formula for any commodity produced capitalistically: C = c + v + s , where c is constant capital (means of production), v is variable capital (labour-power), and s is surplus-value. Marx immediately asks what happens if we subtract s. The residue is \"a mere equivalent or replacement value in commodities for the capital value c + v laid out on the elements of production.\" The concrete illustration is precise. Suppose production requires an outlay of £500: £20 for wear and tear of instruments of labour, £380 for raw materials, and £100 for labour-power. Assuming a rate of surplus-value of 100 per cent: C = 400c + 100v + 100s = £600. Subtracting the £100 surplus-value leaves a commodity value of £500, which \"simply replaces the capital expenditure of £500.\" This portion — \"which replaces the price of the means of production consumed and the labour-power employed\" — is what the commodity costs the capitalist , and therefore constitutes, from his standpoint, the cost price of the commodity. The conceptual identification is subtle: it is not merely that the capitalist calls it the cost price; rather, because he must buy back these elements of production out of the sale proceeds, the category of cost price acquires a genuine functional independence within the circuit of capital. The pivotal distinction, hammered home in the next paragraph, is between two incommensurable magnitudes: \"What the commodity costs the capitalist, and what it actually does cost to produce it, are two completely different quantities.\" The surplus-value portion costs the capitalist nothing \"for the very reason that it costs the worker his unpaid labour.\" But because the worker is \"himself an ingredient of the functioning productive capital that belongs to the capitalist,\" the capitalist — being \"the actual commodity producer\" — necessarily perceives the cost price as the real cost. Here Marx's characteristic move is visible: the illusion is not arbitrary but rooted in a real structural position. The capitalist is correct about his own cash expenditure; he is wrong only when he universalizes that partial standpoint into a theory of value. Marx now formalizes the transformation: if cost price is k, then C = k + s , or \"commodity value = cost price + surplus-value.\" The Dual Nature of Cost Price: Capitalist Account vs. Actual Value The chapter now unpacks the two-sided character of the cost price as a category. On one hand, the reduction of heterogeneous value components to a single heading of \"cost price\" expresses \"the specific character of capitalist production\": the capitalist's cost is measured by capital expenditure , whereas the actual cost is measured by labour expenditure . This is the quantitative distinction: \"The capitalist cost price of the commodity is thus quantitatively distinct from its value or its actual cost price; it is smaller than the commodity's value, for since C = k + s, k = C − s.\" On the other hand, the cost price is \"by no means simply a category that exists only in capitalist book-keeping.\" It acquires real independence because it must \"constantly be transformed back again into the form of productive capital by way of the circulation process\" — the cost price must \"continuously buy back the elements of production consumed in its production.\" This is crucial: the cost price is not a subjective miscounting. It is a real functional moment in the turnover of capital, and because it is real, the illusion it generates has objective force. Yet Marx insists that the category \"has nothing to do with the formation of commodity value or the process of capital's valorization.\" Knowing that five-sixths of a £600 value is merely replacement value tells us nothing about how that value was produced, nor about the origin of the final sixth. The cost price is thus at once real (as a circuit requirement) and epistemologically misleading (as a theory of value). Marx promises that his investigation \"will show, however, that cost price does none the less, in the economy of capital, present the false semblance of an actual category of value production.\" The Labour-Time Decomposition of Cost Price To expose the heterogeneity hidden within the homogeneous figure of £500, Marx converts money into labour-time. Assuming the value produced by one worker in an average social working day is expressed by 6 shillings, the advanced £500 = 400c + 100v represents \"the value product of 1,666⅔ of such 10-hour working days.\" Of these, 1,333⅓ days are \"crystallized in the value of the means of production\" (= 400c) and 333⅓ days \"in the value of the labour-power\" (= 100v). Given the 100 per cent rate of exploitation, the actual production of the new commodity costs, in labour-power, 100v + 100s = 666⅔ 10-hour working days. This calculation performs a dual function: it shows that the £600 total breaks into £400 \"reappearing value\" and £200 \"newly produced value,\" and it makes visible what the money-form conceals — that the £500 cost price is composed of two completely different elements in origin: (1) the reappearing 400c and (2) one half of the newly produced £200 (i.e., 100v). The fate of these two components is then traced in detail. The £400 of means of production is transferred to the product \"by the purposive character of the labour spent,\" so that the old value \"reappears therefore as a component of the product's value, though it does not originate in the production process of this commodity.\" It is a component of the product's value only because it previously existed as a component of the capital advanced. Its dual significance: it enters the cost price because it is a component of value, and it is a component of value because it was the value of capital used up. With the variable component, \"it is quite the reverse.\" The 666⅔ days' labour forms a new value of £200. Out of this new value, one part replaces the £100 advanced, but \"this advance of capital value does not go in any way into the formation of the new value.\" The distinction is between labour-power as value in the capital advance and labour-power as creator of value in production. \"In place of the value of the labour-power, which is what figures in the capital advance, we have the living, value-creating labour-power that actually functions as productive capital.\" This is the decisive conceptual pivot of the whole chapter: the two halves of the cost price are not merely numerically different portions of a homogeneous fund — one is transferred , the other replaced . Their apparent homogeneity in the cost price is precisely the illusion. The Empirical Decomposition Test: Changes in Constant vs. Variable Capital Marx then offers his most powerful analytic device: varying each component while holding others constant, to demonstrate the asymmetric behaviour of c and v. Case A — change in constant capital. Suppose the price of means of production rises from £400 to £600. Then not only does the cost price rise from £500 to 600c + 100v = £700 , but the commodity value itself rises to 600c + 100v + 100s = £800 . If it falls to £200, both cost price (200c + 100v = £300) and value (200c + 100v + 100s = £400) fall. Why? \"Because the constant capital that is used up transfers its own value to the product,\" the product's value rises or falls with it. Constant capital is value-transferring : it contributes exactly (and only) the value it loses. Case B — change in variable capital. Suppose the price of labour-power rises from £100 to £150. The cost price rises to 400c + 150v = £550 . But the commodity value remains unchanged at £600 — it becomes 400c + 150v + 50s. If the wage falls to £50, the cost price falls to 400c + 50v = £450 , while the value stays at £600, now as 400c + 50v + 150s. Variable capital \"does not add its own value to the product. In place of its value, it is the new value created by labour that enters the product.\" A change in the price of labour-power merely reshuffles the division of the £200 new value between wages and surplus-value: it \"affects only the ratio between the two components of this new value.\" This contrast establishes the whole logic of the chapter. The two halves of the cost price share only one thing in common: \"they are both portions of commodity value which replace capital that was advanced.\" That is their sole commonality — a commonality from the standpoint of replacement , not from the standpoint of creation . Constant capital and variable capital behave in radically different ways in value formation while appearing identical in cost-price accounting. The Inversion: From Farmhand to Wages, from Surplus-Value to Profit Having decoupled the two components analytically, Marx now performs the crucial reversal: \"From the standpoint of capitalist production, however, this actual state of affairs necessarily appears upside down.\" The mechanism of the inversion is the wage-form. Capitalist production, \"distinguished from the mode of production founded on slavery,\" expresses \"the value or price of labour-power ... as the value or price of labour itself, i.e. as wages\" (citing Vol. 1, Ch. 19). Consequently, the variable component of capital \"appears as capital spent on wages, as a capital value which pays the value or price of all labour spent in production.\" The numerical reworking is again precise. The £100 variable capital is the monetary expression of value produced in 333⅓ 10-hour days. But \"the value of the labour-power purchased, which figures here in the capital advance, does not form any part of the actually functioning capital.\" If the rate of exploitation is 100 per cent, the living labour-power is used for 666⅔ 10-hour days and adds £200 of new value to the product. Yet the £100 in the advance figures as the price \"of the labour performed in these 666⅔ 10-hour days.\" Dividing £100 by 666⅔ yields 3 shillings as the price of one 10-hour working day — \"the value product of 5 hours' labour.\" This produces the catastrophic theoretical result. He then compares capital advance and commodity value side by side: - I. Capital advance of £500 = £400 in means of production (price of means of production) + £100 in capital spent on labour (price of 666⅔ working days, i.e., wages). - II. Commodity value of £600 = cost price of £500 (£400 price of means of production + £100 price of 666⅔ working days) + £100 surplus-value. In this formula, \"the portion of capital laid out on labour is distinguished from that laid out on means of production only by the fact that it serves as payment for a materially different element of production, and in no way by the fact that it plays a functionally different role in the process of forming commodity value.\" The vital distinction between constant and variable capital — the foundation of the entire theory of surplus-value in Volume 1 — \"has disappeared.\" What we see in the cost price are \"only finished and existing values — the value portions of the capital advanced which enter the formation of the product's value — and not an element that creates new value.\" The £500 cost price thus acquires a double significance: first, it is the component of the £600 commodity value that replaces the capital consumed; second, it exists as such only because it existed previously as the cost price of the elements of production, i.e., as a capital advance. \"The capital value returns as the commodity's cost price, because and in so far as it was spent as a capital value.\" The Substitution of Fixed/Circulating for Constant/Variable Having erased the constant/variable distinction, Marx shows what distinction does remain visible in the cost price: the distinction between fixed and circulating capital. The material diversity of the value components of the advanced capital (means of labour, raw and ancillary materials, and labour itself) \"only means that the cost price of the commodity must buy back again these materially distinct elements of production.\" But regarding the formation of the cost price, \"the only distinction that matters is the distinction between fixed and circulating capital.\" The illustration is carefully constructed. Depreciation of means of labour was £20, so 400c = £20 (depreciation of means of labour) + £380 (materials). The means of labour formerly worth £1,200 now exists in two forms: £20 as part of the commodity's value, and £1,180 as the remaining value of the means of labour, still in the capitalist's possession as an element of productive capital. Production materials and wages, by contrast, are \"used up completely,\" so their entire value enters the commodity's value. Hence: the capital advanced is £1,680 = a fixed capital of £1,200 plus a circulating capital of £480 (£380 materials + £100 wages). The cost price of the commodity is £500 = £20 depreciation of fixed capital + £480 circulating capital. The discrepancy between £1,680 (advanced) and £500 (cost price) is not a paradox but a confirmation: the cost price \"is formed exclusively by the capital actually used up on the commodity's production.\" The £1,200 of fixed capital is applied but only £20 is lost; fixed capital enters the cost price only partially, \"in proportion to the magnitude of their value,\" while circulating capital enters completely. Marx drives the point home with a rhetorical question: if the partial entry were not the rule, \"there would be no reason why the fixed capital of £1,200 that is advanced should not also add to the value of the product the £1,180 that it does not lose in the production process, instead of just the £20 that it actually does lose.\" The crux of the mystification now emerges with full force. The fixed/circulating distinction \"only confirms the apparent origin of the cost price in the capital value expended, or the price that the expended elements of production, labour included, cost the capitalist himself.\" But \"as far as value formation is concerned, the variable portion of capital, that laid out on labour-power, is expressly identified here with constant capital ... under the heading of circulating capital, and thus the valorization process of capital is completely mystified.\" The footnote underscores the practical stakes by citing N. W. Senior (1790–1864), \"one of the principal exponents of 'vulgar economics' in England and particularly notorious for his opposition to the legal restriction of working hours, on the basis of his theory of the 'last hour'\" — the doctrine that all profit is produced in the final hour of the working day, which Marx had demolished in Vol. 1, Ch. 9. Surplus-Value Becomes Profit: The Second Transformation The chapter's second great movement now begins. \"So far we have considered only one element of commodity value, the cost price. We must now take a look at the other component, the excess over the cost price or the surplus-value.\" Initially, surplus-value is simply \"an excess commodity value over and above the cost price.\" But because the cost price equals the value of the capital expended and is continuously transformed back into the material elements of that capital, \"this additional value is a value accruing to the capital expended in the production of the commodity and returning from its circulation.\" Marx traces the algebraic transmutation of the formula. Originally, s \"derives only from a change in the value of v, the variable capital, and is therefore originally simply an increment to the variable capital.\" But once production is complete, s \"can also ... form a value increment to c + v, the total capital expended.\" Thus the formula c + (v + s) — which signals that s arises specifically from the conversion of v into a variable magnitude — \"can also be represented as (c + v) + s .\" Before production: capital £500. After: capital £500 plus a value increment of £100. (The footnote quotes Vol. 1, Ch. 9, p. 322, where Marx had already noted that the fact \"that it is v alone that varies ... [is] obscured by the circumstance that in consequence of the increase in the variable component of the capital, there is also an increase in the sum total of the capital advanced.\") The generalization deepens. The surplus-value \"forms an addition not only to the part of the capital advanced that enters the process of valorization, but also to the part that does not enter this process.\" Using the revised numbers: before production, capital value is £1,680 (£1,200 fixed capital laid out on means of labour, of which only £20 enters the commodity's value as depreciation, plus £480 circulating capital). After production: £1,180 as the value component of the productive capital plus a commodity capital of £600 = £1,780 . Deducting the £1,680 advanced leaves an additional value of £100 . \"Thus the £100 surplus-value forms as much an addition to the total capital applied of £1,680 as to the fraction of this, £500, that is used up in the course of production.\" This is the decisive claim. To the capitalist it is \"clear enough ... that this additional value derives from the productive activities which he undertakes with his capital, i.e. that it derives from the capital itself. For after the production process he has it, and before the production process he did not.\" As regards the capital actually used up , the surplus-value \"appears to derive equally from the different value elements of this capital, both means of production and labour,\" because these elements \"are both equally involved in the formation of the cost price.\" The \"All-Wages\" and \"All-Means-of-Production\" Reduction Tests Marx then deploys a second analytic experiment: imagine the capital expended consists either exclusively of wages or exclusively of means of production . If exclusively wages, instead of 400c + 100v + 100s, we would have 500v + 100s . The £500 laid out on wages would be the value of all the labour applied, and would form the cost price of the whole £600 product. \"The formation of this cost price ... is however the only process in the formation of this commodity value that we know of. We know nothing of where its surplus-value component of £100 comes from.\" Exactly the same happens if the value is taken as 500c + 100s . The point of this thought-experiment is to isolate the form of surplus-value abstracted from its real source. In both cases, \"the surplus-value derives from a given value because this value was advanced in the form of productive capital, leaving aside the question whether this took the form of labour or that of means of production.\" But — and this is the dialectical pivot — \"the capital value advanced cannot form surplus-value simply by virtue of its having been used up and forming therefore the cost price of the commodity.\" For to the extent that it forms the cost price, \"it does not form any surplus-value, but simply an equivalent, a replacement value.\" Therefore, to the extent that this value does form surplus-value, \"it forms this not in its specific capacity as capital that has been used up, but rather as advanced and therefore applied capital in general.\" The conclusion: \"Surplus-value thus derives as much from the part of the capital advanced that does not enter the cost price of the commodity as from the part of it that does enter the cost price; in short, it derives equally from the fixed and circulating components of the capital applied.\" Materially, the entire capital \"serves to form the product, the means of labour as much as the production materials and labour itself.\" The entire capital is materially involved in the labour process \"even if only a part of it is involved in the process of valorization.\" Marx notes with characteristic irony: \"This is perhaps the very reason why it contributes only in part towards the formation of the cost price, but in full towards the formation of surplus-value.\" The upshot is that \"the surplus-value springs simultaneously from all parts of the capital applied.\" Malthus and the Definition of Capital and Profit The theoretical deduction is then condensed into an aphorism. Marx quotes Malthus: \"The capitalist ... expects an equal profit upon all the parts of the capital which he advances.\" The citation (Principles of Political Economy, 2nd ed., 1836, p. 268) demonstrates that the practical consciousness of the capitalist and the superficial economics of the vulgar school agree: both attribute the increment to total capital. The footnote adds Marx's critical judgment of Malthus as \"a serious if minor economist, despite the reactionary ideology in which his theoretical contributions were buried,\" and cites Malthus's definition (\"Capital: that which is expended with a view to profit\"). From here, the chapter's central transformation is completed. \"As this supposed derivative of the total capital advanced, the surplus-value takes on the transformed form of profit .\" A sum of value \"is therefore capital if it is invested in order to produce a profit, or alternatively profit arises because a sum of value is employed as capital.\" If we call profit p, then C = c + v + s = k + s is converted into C = k + p , or \"commodity value = cost price + profit.\" Marx then gives the canonical statement: \"Profit, as we are originally faced with it, is thus the same thing as surplus-value, save in a mystified form, though one that necessarily arises from the capitalist mode of production.\" The mechanism of the mystification is restated with precision: \"Because no distinction between constant and variable capital can be recognized in the apparent formation of the cost price, the origin of the change in value that occurs in the course of the production process is shifted from the variable capital to the capital as a whole. Because the price of labour-power appears at one pole in the transformed form of wages, surplus-value appears at the other pole in the transformed form of profit.\" This is a mirror-structure of fetishism: disguise at one pole (labour-power → wages) produces counterpart disguise at the other (surplus-value → profit). The Range of Sale Prices and the \"Secret\" of Competition The analysis then turns to a consequence that is at once theoretical and empirically explosive: the existence of a continuum of possible sale prices between the cost price and the value. Since C = k + s, \"k = C − s.\" The reduced formula C = k would require s = 0, \"a case that never arises in conditions of capitalist production.\" If the commodity sells at its value, the profit realized equals the entire surplus-value. But — and here is the key — \"the capitalist can sell the commodity at a profit even if he sells it at less than its value. As long as its sale price is above its cost price, even if below its value, a part of the surplus-value contained in it is always realized, i.e. a profit is made.\" The numerical illustration: value £600, cost price £500. If the commodity is sold at £510, £520, £530, £560, or £590, it is sold respectively at £90, £80, £70, £40, or £10 below its value, and yet a profit of £10, £20, £30, £60, or £90 is realized. \"An indefinite series of sale prices is evidently possible between the value of a commodity and its cost price. The greater the element of commodity value consisting of surplus-value, the greater the practical room for these intermediate prices.\" Marx then announces the strategic importance of this result. It enables the explanation of \"such everyday phenomena of competition as, for instance, certain cases of under-selling, an abnormally low level of commodity prices in certain branches of industry.\" More vitally, \"the basic law of capitalist competition, which political economy has so far failed to grasp, the law that governs the general rate of profit and the so-called prices of production determined by it, depends, as we shall see, on this difference between the value and the cost price of commodities, and the possibility deriving from this of selling commodities below their value at a profit.\" This is the forward link to the entire subsequent argument of Volume 3, and it identifies the cost-price-to-value gap as the structural precondition of equalized profit rates across branches of industry with differing organic compositions. The Minimum Limit and the Illusion That Flows from It The cost price functions as the lower bound: sold beneath it, \"the components of productive capital that were expended cannot be fully replaced\"; if the sale continues below it, \"the capital value advanced will disappear completely.\" From this standpoint the capitalist \"is inclined to treat the cost price as the real inner value of the commodity, as it is the price he needs merely to preserve his capital.\" And because the cost price is also the purchase price the capitalist has paid for its production, the surplus-value realized on sale \"appears to the capitalist as an excess of its sale price over its value, instead of an excess of its value over its cost price, so that the surplus-value concealed in the commodity is not simply realized by its sale, but actually derives from the sale itself.\" Marx notes that this illusion was already treated in Vol. 1, Ch. 5 (\"Contradictions in the General Formula\"). Polemic I: Torrens and \"Creation From Nothing\" Marx next returns to a modern restatement of the illusion by Colonel Robert Torrens (1780–1864, an advocate of the \"Currency Principle\"). Torrens argues: \"The natural price, consisting of the cost of production, or, in other words, of the capital expended in raising or fabricating commodities, cannot include the profit.\" The farmer who expends 100 quarters of corn and reaps 120 has a profit of 20 quarters, but \"it would be absurd to call this excess, or profit, a part of the expenditure.\" The manufacturer's finished work \"must possess a higher exchangeable value than the materials, tools, and subsistence, by the advance of which it was obtained.\" Torrens concludes that the excess of sale price over cost price derives from consumers who, \"either by immediate or circuitous barter[,] give some greater portion of all the ingredients of capital than their production costs.\" Marx dismantles this. \"In actual fact, the excess over a given magnitude can in no way form part of that magnitude, and so profit ... cannot form any part of these outlays.\" If commodity value is formed solely out of the capitalist's advance, \"there is no way of seeing how any more value is to come out of production than went into it, unless something is to come out of nothing.\" Torrens's escape is to shift the miracle from production to circulation. But this fails: \"Profit cannot derive from commodity exchange, Ramsay answers him, unless it is already present before this exchange takes place.\" The sum of values of products exchanged \"is evidently not affected by the exchange of the products whose value sum this is.\" Marx notes that Malthus appeals to Torrens's authority, even though Malthus explains selling above value differently \"— or rather does not explain it, as all arguments of this kind are unfailingly reducible, in effect, to the same thing as the negative weight of phlogiston, which was so renowned in its time.\" Engels's footnote formalizes the analogy: this is Marx's theory of value against the \"phlogiston\" theory the way Lavoisier refuted Stahl. Polemic II: Balzac, the Peasant, and the Pervasiveness of Capitalist Categories The chapter then pivots, with remarkable literary economy, to a demonstration that capitalist categories dominate even non-capitalist producers. In Balzac's Les Paysans , \"the small peasant eager to retain the good-will of the money-lender performs all kinds of services for him unpaid, yet does not see himself as giving something for nothing, as his own labour does not cost him any cash expenditure.\" The peasant's illusion is structurally identical to the businessman's: he measures cost by cash outlay , not by labour-time, and thus fails to see that unpaid labour is a real gift. The money-lender \"kills two birds with one stone\": he spares cash expenditure on wages, and, as the peasant is gradually ruined by depriving his own fields of labour, \"he enmeshes him ever deeper in the web of usury.\" The theoretical point is that the appearance-forms of capital are not merely theories produced by economists; they are lived consciousness , imposed by capitalist social relations on everyone, including those the system exploits. Polemic III: Proudhon and the People's Bank Marx then turns to Proudhon (1809–1864), whose \"People's Bank\" (established briefly in Paris in January 1849 and placed in forced liquidation after two months) was founded on the claim that \"the cost price of the commodity is its real price and that surplus-value springs from selling the commodity above its value, i.e. that commodities are sold at their values when their sale price is equal to their cost price — i.e. equal to the price of the means of production consumed in them, plus wages.\" Marx calls this \"pseudo-scientific quackery\" presenting an old error \"as a newly discovered secret of socialism.\" He had already criticized the theoretical basis in The Poverty of Philosophy (1847). The refutation is algebraic and visual. Following Vol. 1, Ch. 9, we may represent value components as proportionate parts of the product itself. If the value of 20 lb. of yarn is 30 shillings, made up of 24s. means of production, 3s. labour-power, and 3s. surplus-value, then the surplus-value can be represented as one-tenth of the product, i.e., 2 lb. of yarn . If the 20 lb. are sold at cost price (27s.), the buyer receives 2 lb. of yarn for nothing; \"or the commodity is sold at one-tenth below its value.\" The worker has still performed his surplus labour, \"but now for the buyer of the yarn instead of for the capitalist yarn producer.\" The exploitation is redistributed to the purchaser — it is not abolished. Proudhon's scheme would transfer surplus-value to the buyers of commodities without eliminating surplus labour. Finally, Marx exposes a second and more sweeping error in Proudhon's reduction. Even if all commodities were sold at cost prices, \"it would be quite wrong to suppose that the result would in fact be the same as if they were all sold above their cost prices but at their values.\" For even with equal value of labour-power, equal length of working day, and equal rate of exploitation, \"the amounts of surplus-value that the values of the various different kinds of commodities contain are completely unequal, according to the differing organic compositions of the capitals advanced for their production.\" The footnote quotes Vol. 1, Ch. 11: masses of value and surplus-value produced by different capitals \"vary directly as the amounts of the variable components of these capitals.\" This is a crucial forward link: it prefigures the transformation problem. Selling at cost price would not neutralize exploitation, because capitals of differing organic composition produce different masses of surplus-value. The very variability of surplus-value across branches of production is what makes the equalization of profit rates (and hence the divergence of production prices from values) necessary in a competitive economy. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS: The chapter establishes a fundamental structural law of capitalist appearance : profit is surplus-value in a necessarily mystified form. The mystification is not an error to be corrected; it is a real product of the mode of production, generated by the wage-form and the capital-circuit through the category of cost price. Because wages represent the price of all labour (not merely of labour-power), and because the cost price treats means of production and labour as homogeneous elements of advances, the origin of the value increment is displaced from the variable component of capital onto the capital as a whole. The formula C = k + p is therefore not merely a restatement of C = c + v + s; it is its inverted surface-form. The distinction between essence and appearance becomes a structural necessity of capital: a distinction Marx will generalize into the theory of the fetishism of capital, the trinity formula (land–rent, capital–profit, labour–wages), and the eventual identification of profit with the entire surplus under the umbrella of the average rate of profit. The second great lesson concerns the range of sale prices between cost price and value . This is not a marginal curiosity. It is the indispensible precondition for two central results of Volume 3: the possibility of under-selling and abnormal prices in certain branches, and the general equalization of profit rates with the resulting divergence of production prices from values. If commodities always sold at their values, competition could not accomplish this equalization, because capitals of different organic compositions would produce unequal rates of profit by definition. The cost-price/value gap is precisely what allows capitals to redistribute surplus across branches through price competition, producing a uniform general profit rate. This is why Marx calls the resulting law \"the basic law of capitalist competition, which political economy has so far failed to grasp.\" The third lesson is the reductionist refutation of Proudhonian socialism . The fantasy of a \"People's Bank\" that sells commodities at cost price so as to eliminate exploitation is exposed as a category error. It neither abolishes surplus labour (the worker continues to give it) nor distributes it justly (it simply transfers it to the buyer). And even if all commodities were sold at cost price, the amounts of surplus-value across branches would still be unequal because of differing organic compositions. The radical implication is that exploitation cannot be abolished by circulation reform (credit, exchange, pricing); it can be abolished only by abolishing the wage-relation and the separation of the producers from the means of production. This connects the chapter to the broader socialist programme: reform of circulation is a mirage; the terrain of struggle is production. The fourth lesson concerns the pervasiveness of capitalist categories . The Balzac example demonstrates that the forms of appearance of capital are not merely the false theories of economists but the lived thought of every participant in a capitalist society, including peasants and other non-capitalist producers. This is Marx's answer to any view that treats \"the everyday consciousness of the agents of production\" as a source of truth: that consciousness is generated by the system itself, and it systematically reverses the real causation. The epistemological implication is radical: science must derive the categories of the surface from the underlying value-form, not merely describe or accept the surface. Fifth, the chapter firmly links the theory of value to the critique of the previous political economy . Malthus's aphorism, Torrens's exchange-based theory of profit, and the analogous \"creation from nothing\" are all shown to depend on the same error. Marx's \"phlogiston\" analogy, reinforced by Engels in the footnote, marks the theoretical rupture: the classical economists (Smith, Ricardo) had discovered value but had not derived profit correctly; the vulgar economists, from Senior to Torrens, retreated into appearance-worship. The chapter therefore asserts the primacy of the Volume 1 theory of surplus-value as the sole scientific foundation on which the concrete forms of Volume 3 can be built. Finally, the broader philosophical implication: the entire analysis demonstrates the dialectic of scientific representation . In the realm of capital, essence and appearance are not merely different but systematically inverted , and the inversion has real effects (competition, prices, the practical consciousness of capitalists, the legitimacy of profit). Capital's own movement disguises its own source. This is the deepest claim of Volume 3: the \"surface of society\" is not a place where truth is finally revealed but rather the site where truth is systematically obscured. The task of critique is not to describe that surface but to reconstruct its hidden grammar. From this point forward, every category of Volume 3 — the general rate of profit, price of production, commercial profit, interest, rent — will be shown to flow from this initial transformation of surplus-value into profit, and every category will continue, in Marx's sense, to be a form of mystification that must be continually dissolved by theory. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Introduction Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 2"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 2", "title": "Chapter 2", "subtitle": "The Rate of Profit", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 2/Chapter 2 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 5942, "read_time": "30 min read", "tags": ["Cost Price & Rate of Profit", "Organic Composition of Capital (OCC)"], "keywords": ["The Rate of Profit (p' = s / (c + v))", "Rate of Profit vs Rate of Surplus-Value", "Capitalist Calculation of Profitability", "Organic Composition Determinant", "Obscuring the Degree of Exploitation"], "text": "Chapter 2 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,901 words ⏱️ ~30 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS This is Chapter 2 of Volume 3 of Capital , the opening chapter of the section entitled \"The Transformation of Surplus-Value into Profit.\" Its title — \"The Rate of Profit\" — appears disarmingly narrow, but the chapter is in fact the hinge upon which the entire argument of Volume 3 turns. Its central thesis is at once simple and revolutionary: profit is not a magnitude different from surplus-value; it is the same surplus-value appearing in a transformed form — a form in which its origin in the exploitation of living labour is veiled, and in which capital seems to reproduce itself out of its own hidden productive qualities. Correspondingly, the rate of profit is not a new economic quantity but a second, alternative measure of the surplus-value already measured by the rate of surplus-value . The two rates are \"two different standards for measuring the same quantity,\" and precisely because of this they \"express the different relations in which the same quantity may stand.\" The chapter's central problem is therefore not arithmetic but philosophical: why and how does this transformation occur, and why is it necessary? Marx's answer is that the transformation of surplus-value into profit is the subjective reflex — the \"transposed consciousness\" — of an objective inversion that already occurs in the production process itself, and that is further developed by the circulation process. The chapter is thus an exercise in what Marx calls the sifting of appearance from essence : surplus-value and the rate of surplus-value are \"the invisible essence to be investigated,\" while profit and the rate of profit are \"visible surface phenomena.\" Yet Marx insists — against any naive reading of this essence/appearance divide — that it is not a case of error to be corrected by demonstrating the truth. The transformation is a real necessity of the capitalist mode of production, and the mystification it produces is not a contingent illusion but a socially objective form. The chapter's intellectual objective , then, is threefold. First, to formally derive the rate of profit p′ = s/C from the rate of surplus-value s′ = s/v, showing that the numerator (s) is identical while the denominator changes. Second, to demonstrate that the direction of derivation is one-way — the rate of-profit is derived from the rate of surplus-value and not the reverse — while simultaneously acknowledging the historical priority of the profit rate (it is \"the historical starting-point\"). Third, and most ambitiously, to explain the necessity of mystification : why the capitalist, and the capitalist's theoretical spokesmen (Ramsay, Malthus, Senior, Torrens, and the Ricardian school), necessarily fail to see the extraction of surplus labour behind the surface form of profit, and why the \"further we trace out the valorization process of capital, the more is the capital relationship mystified.\" Fitting this into the broader framework: Volume 1 revealed exploitation within the immediate process of production ; Volume 2 traced the circuit of capital through circulation . Volume 3 must confront the forms in which capital appears to the participants — profit, interest, rent, the \"revenue\" forms — and demonstrate their dependence on surplus-value. Chapter 2 is the decisive first step in this confrontation: it establishes that the stuff of profit is surplus-value and that its form is a concealment. It also sets the programme for what follows: in this Part, profit and surplus-value are treated as \"numerically identical magnitudes, different only in form\"; in the next Part, Marx will show how profit comes to appear as distinct from surplus-value \"in a numerical respect as well.\" The chapter thus knowingly sets up its own supersession. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Starting Point: M–C–M′ and the Capitalist's Undifferentiated Advance of Capital Marx opens not with the rate of profit but with the general formula for capital , M–C–M′ : \"a sum of value is cast into circulation in order to extract a greater sum.\" This is the logical point of departure because the entire problematic of the chapter grows out of the capitalist's own standpoint. The capitalist does not produce commodities for their use-value or for personal consumption; \"the product in which the capitalist is really interested is not the palpable product itself, but rather the excess in the value of the product over and above the value of the capital consumed in it.\" The category of profit is thus born from the standpoint of the advancing capitalist, not from the anatomy of production. The crucial analytical move is that the capitalist \"advances the capital as a whole without considering the different roles that its components will play in the production of surplus-value.\" This is the seed of the entire mystification. He advances all components equally — not only to reproduce the advanced capital but to produce an excess over and above it. Here Marx establishes the conditions of exploitation : the capitalist can convert value into a higher value \"only by exchanging it with living labour, by the exploitation of living labour.\" But exploitation is possible only if he simultaneously advances the conditions of this labour — \"means and object of labour, machinery and raw materials\" — i.e., only by transforming a sum of value into the form of the conditions of production. The capitalist is a capitalist at all only because he confronts the worker as proprietor of the conditions of labour, while the worker owns only labour-power. The footnote to Volume 1 (pp. 270–74, 874–6) anchors this in the earlier analysis of \"primitive accumulation\" and the separation of the producer from the means of production. So at the very outset the chapter reminds us that the social relation of capital (the property relation) is what makes the material elements of production function as capital — a point that will later be suppressed in the surface form. The Equivalence of the Two Viewpoints and the Move from s/v to s/C Marx now introduces a rhetorical symmetry that dissolves into a real identity: \"It makes no difference to the capitalist whether we see him as advancing the constant capital to make a profit out of his variable capital, or advancing the variable capital in order to valorize the constant.\" On the one side, the capitalist lays out money on wages \"in order to give machines and raw material a higher value\"; on the other, he advances money in machinery and raw material \"in order to exploit labour.\" This is not merely a matter of perspective — it expresses a real reciprocity: \"it is only the variable part of capital that creates surplus-value, [but] it does so only under the condition that the other parts are advanced as well.\" Each presupposes the other, and \"these are both one and the same in his eyes.\" The decisive consequence is then stated: \"the actual degree of his profit is determined in relation not to his variable capital but to his total capital; not by the rate of surplus-value but by the rate of profit.\" We should pause on the phrase \"may remain the same while expressing different rates of surplus-value\" — this is Marx's signal that the rate of profit is a lossy measure, one that can hide radical differences in exploitation. Two capitals with the same profit rate can rest on very different rates of surplus-value, depending on their organic composition. This single remark contains the germ of the later analysis of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall and the equalization of profit rates across branches. Cost Price, Value, and the Locus of Profit Having established the capitalist's standpoint, Marx defines the costs of the product : \"all the components of its value which the capitalist has paid for, or for which he has cast an equivalent into the production process.\" These costs must be replaced \"even if his capital is to do no more than maintain itself, reproduce its original magnitude.\" This is the cost price (what later editions will designate k ), and it is strictly less than the value of the commodity. The distinction is now drawn with precision. \"The value contained in a commodity is equal to the labour-time taken in making it, and this consists of both paid and unpaid labour.\" The value, in other words, is the total labour objectified: c + v + s. But \"the costs of the commodity for the capitalist … include only the part of the labour objectified in it for which he has actually paid\" — that is, c + v (with the caveat, elaborated later, that c enters in varying degrees depending on fixed/circulating form). The surplus labour contained in the commodity costs the capitalist nothing, even though it costs the worker labour, every bit as much as the paid labour does. Both paid and unpaid labour create value and enter the commodity as elements of value formation; but only the paid portion enters the capitalist's cost accounting. From this emerges the chapter's most quotable formulation of profit: \"The capitalist's profit, therefore, comes from the fact that he has something to sell for which he has not paid.\" Surplus-value or profit \"consists precisely in the excess of commodity value over its cost price, i.e. in the excess of the total sum of labour contained in the commodity over the sum of labour that is actually paid for.\" And from wherever it may derive , surplus-value is \"an excess over and above the total capital advanced.\" This excess \"stands in a certain ratio to the total capital, as expressed by the fraction s/C, where C stands for the total capital.\" Hence: Rate of profit: p′ = s/C (surplus-value over total capital advanced) Rate of surplus-value: s′ = s/v (surplus-value over variable capital) The text's OCR renders these as garbled glyphs, but the intention is unambiguous: the rate of profit is the surplus measured against the whole C = c + v; the rate of surplus-value is the same surplus measured against v alone. Two Standards, One Quantity: The Realm of Measurement Marx now crystallizes the point: \"The rate of surplus-value, as measured against the variable capital, is known as the rate of surplus-value; the rate of surplus-value, as measured against the total capital, is known as the rate of profit.\" (Here the second \"rate of surplus-value\" should be read as the same quantity, surplus-value , measured against total capital.) The pair are \"two different standards for measuring the same quantity, and as a result are able to express the different relations in which the same quantity may stand.\" This is a subtle and important formal point: unlike the difference between, say, velocity and acceleration, the difference between the two rates is only a difference of denominator. The numerator is identical. This identity of substance under a difference of measure is what allows Marx to say later that profit and surplus-value are \"numerically identical magnitudes, different only in form.\" The Direction of Derivation: Essence versus Surface Next comes a methodological claim of the first order: \"It is the transformation of surplus-value into profit that is derived from the transformation of the rate of surplus-value into the profit rate, not the other way round.\" But Marx immediately complicates this by adding that \"in actual fact, the rate of profit is the historical starting-point.\" The relation of logical derivation and the relation of historical appearance run in opposite directions . This is the classic Marxian structure of essence and appearance: \"Surplus-value and the rate of surplus-value are, relative to this, the invisible essence to be investigated, whereas the rate of profit and hence the form of surplus-value as profit are visible surface phenomena.\" The analyst begins from what is visible (profit) and works back to what is hidden (surplus-value); but the explanatory derivation runs from essence to appearance. Any attempt to invert this explanatory order — to read the laws of surplus-value directly off the laws of profit — is, Marx will later say, \"completely wrong-headed.\" The Capitalist's Interest in Disguise Marx now ties the epistemological problem to a material interest . \"As far as the individual capitalist is concerned, it is evident enough that the only thing that interests him is the ratio of the surplus-value … to the total capital advanced.\" Not only do the specific ratios of the excess to the particular components, and its \"inner connections with them,\" fail to interest him — \"it is actually in his interest to disguise these particular ratios and inner connections.\" This is a critical move: the mystification is not merely a cognitive error but is functionally motivated . The capitalist has a practical stake in not seeing the extraction of surplus labour as such. This anticipates the later analysis of \"the trinity formula\" and the fetishism of capital. Realization in Circulation and the Appearance of Mutual Cheating The next beat locates the second source of mystification. The excess value \"arises in the immediate process of production,\" but \"it is only in the circulation process that it is realized.\" Because it is realized there, and \"in the world as it actually is, the world of competition, i.e. on the market, it depends on market conditions whether or not this excess is realized and to what extent,\" it \"appears all the more readily to derive from the circulation process.\" Marx is careful to contain this: \"if a commodity is sold above or below its value, there is simply a different distribution of the surplus-value,\" and this redistribution \"in no way affects either the magnitude or the character of the surplus-value itself.\" But from the individual capitalist's vantage, this containment is invisible. The circulation process is the \"scene of those transformations considered in Volume 2,\" and these coincide with \"actual competition, the purchase and sale of commodities above or below their value, so that … the surplus-value that he realizes depends just as much on this mutual cheating as on the direct exploitation of labour.\" Here we meet a crucial nuance: Marx does not deny that realized surplus-value varies with market conditions; he denies that this affects the magnitude or character of surplus-value as such. The individual's experience is not simply false — it is a partial, abstract truth that, when taken as the whole, becomes the source of a systematic illusion. Circulation time, moreover, \"restrict[s] the surplus-value that can be realized in a certain period,\" and other aspects deriving from circulation \"react with decisive effect on the immediate process of production itself\" (e.g., turnover time forcing speed-up, or lengthening the working day). The two processes \"constantly run into one another and intertwine, and in this way their distinguishing features are continuously blurred.\" The Stepping-Out of Capital into External Relations Marx now offers an almost dramatic description of the passage from the \"inner organic life\" of capital to its \"external relations\": in circulation, \"it is not capital and labour that confront one another, but on the one hand capital and capital, and on the other hand individuals as simple buyers and sellers once again.\" Circulation time and working time \"cut across each other's paths, and both appear to determine surplus-value in the same way.\" The consequence is that the \"original form in which capital and wage-labour confront one another is disguised by the intervention of relations that seem to be independent of this.\" Surplus-value no longer appears \"as having been produced by the appropriation of labour-time, but as the excess of the sale price of commodities over their cost price.\" And this cost price \"readily present[s] itself therefore as their proper value (valeur intrinseque), so that profit appears as an excess of the sale price of commodities over their immanent value.\" The introduction of the French term valeur intrinseque (intrinsic value) is pointed: it names the physiocratic/pre-classical notion of a value inherent in the commodity (land, or matter), against which the sale price may be above or below — a notion Marx is here showing to be the natural self-understanding of the circulation standpoint. The Twofold Reinforcement of Mystification: Evanescence and the Cost Rubric Marx now confronts a possible objection: does the capitalist not, after all, know that profit comes from the labour-time of others, given \"his greed for the labour-time of others\"? Yes — but this inkling is systematically dissolved by two mechanisms, enumerated as (1) and (2). (1) The evanescence of the production process. \"The immediate process of production is itself simply an evanescent moment, which is constantly passing over into the process of circulation, and vice versa.\" Thus any insight into the source of profit that dawns in production \"appears at the most as an equally valid moment alongside the notion that the excess … derives from the sphere of circulation, a movement therefore that capital possesses independently of its relation to labour.\" Marx then names the economists who elevate this moment into theory: Ramsay, Malthus, Senior, Torrens , who adduce \"these phenomena of circulation … as direct proofs that capital in its mere material existence, independently of its social relation to labour (which is precisely how it comes to be capital), is an autonomous source of surplus-value alongside labour and independent of it.\" This is a crucial nuance: Marx is not simply dismissing these economists as foolish. He is showing that their error has a real foundation in the appearances generated by the circulation process. Their theory is the conceptual reflex of a real mystification. (2) The cost-price rubric. \"Under the heading of costs, which include not only wages but also the price of raw material, the depreciation of the machinery, etc., the extortion of unpaid labour appears simply as an economy in the payment for one of the articles that comprise these costs.\" That is, surplus labour is booked as if it were merely a cheap purchase of an input — \"similar to that made when raw material is bought more cheaply or the wear and tear of machinery is reduced.\" The specific character of surplus labour is thereby lost; \"its specific relationship to surplus-value is obscured.\" And this obscuring is \"greatly furthered and facilitated by the representation of the value of labour-power in the form of wages\" (Volume 1, Part Six, Chapter 19). In other words, because wages appear as the price of labour (rather than the price of labour-power), the unpaid portion of the working day has no separate line in the accounts ; it appears as a saving on the wage bill rather than as the very substance of profit. Here Marx connects the form of the wage — analyzed in Volume 1 — to the form of profit analyzed here. The combined result: \"Since all sections of capital equally appear as sources of the excess value (profit), the capital relation is mystified.\" The Inversion of Subject and Object Marx now deepens the diagnosis by connecting it to a more fundamental inversion already established in Volume 1. \"The way that surplus-value is transformed into the form of profit, by way of the rate of profit, is only a further extension of that inversion of subject and object which already occurs in the course of the production process itself.\" In production, \"all the subjective productive forces of labour present themselves as productive forces of capital\" (footnote to Volume 1, pp. 450–53). On one side, \"value, i.e. the past labour that dominates living labour, is personified into the capitalist\"; on the other, \"the worker conversely appears as mere objectified labour-power, as a commodity.\" This inverted real relationship \"necessarily gives rise\" to \"a correspondingly inverted conception of the situation, a transposed consciousness, which is further developed by the transformations and modifications of the circulation process proper.\" This \"transposed consciousness\" ( verkehrtes Bewußtsein ) is a structural category, not a psychological failing. The mystification is the necessary subjective form of an objectively inverted relation. The Ricardian Error and the Prohibition on Collapsing the Two Rates The chapter now issues a direct warning against a specific theoretical mistake: \"As can be studied in the case of the Ricardian school, it is completely wrong-headed to seek directly to present the laws of the profit rate as laws of the rate of surplus-value, or vice versa.\" The Ricardians — attempting to explain profit directly by the labour theory of value — collapsed the two rates, and thereby ran into insoluble contradictions (e.g., how equal profit rates coexist with unequal rates of surplus-value across branches). Marx's point is that the profit rate is a genuinely different object of law from the surplus-value rate: the surplus-value rate has laws (its determination by the working day, intensity, productivity), and the profit rate has its own additional laws (determination by organic composition and turnover). To reduce one to the other is to lose the very phenomena one means to explain. He then specifies what s/C does and does not express. In the capitalist's mind, \"these things are of course not distinguished.\" The expression s/C \"measures surplus-value against the value of the total capital advanced for its production, of which one part is completely consumed in this production, while another part is simply applied.\" Thus s/C \"expresses the degree of valorization of the whole capital advanced; i.e., viewed in accordance with the conceptual, inner connection and the actual nature of surplus-value, it shows how the variation of the variable capital is related in magnitude to the total capital advanced.\" The rate of profit, correctly interpreted, still contains the secret — but only if one already knows to look for it. The Technical Proportion and the Absence of Any Inner Relation Between Constant Capital and Surplus-Value This is the chapter's most analytically dense passage. Marx argues: \"In itself, the value of the total capital stands in no inner relationship to the amount of surplus-value, at least not directly.\" His proof proceeds by way of technical proportions . The constant capital, materially considered, \"consists of the material conditions for the realization of labour — its materials and means.\" For a definite quantity of labour to be realized, a definite quantity of materials and means is required, a \"definite technical proportion\" between living labour and the mass of means of production, depending on the particular character of the labour. There is likewise \"a definite proportion between the amount of surplus-value or surplus labour, and the mass of means of production.\" He gives a numerical example. Suppose 6 hours of labour are needed to produce the worker's wage; the worker must then work 12 hours to perform 6 hours of surplus labour and create a surplus-value of 100 per cent. In 12 hours he consumes twice as much means of production as in 6. But — and this is the pivot — \"this does not mean that the surplus-value he adds in 6 hours stands in any direct relationship to the value of the means of production that are used in these 6 or 12 hours. Their value is completely immaterial here; what matters is the amount technically needed.\" Marx illustrates with the cotton example. If x lb. of cotton are spun per hour and cost y shillings, then in 12 hours 12x lb. (= 12y shillings) are spun. One can therefore compute the ratio of surplus-value to the value spun in 12 hours as well as in 6. But the ratio enters only \"in as much as y shillings serves as the name for x lb. of cotton\" — i.e., price functions here merely as an index of the quantity technically required, valid only so long as the price of cotton is unchanged. Hence: \"If I know that in order to appropriate 6 hours' surplus labour I have to have the workers perform 12 hours' labour, I must have enough cotton ready for 12 hours, and if I know the price of this quantity … there exists in this roundabout way a certain relationship between the price of cotton … and the surplus-value.\" But I can never argue conversely from the price of the raw material to the quantity that can be spun in one hour. The relation is asymmetric and derivative, not intrinsic. Conclusion: \"There is thus no inner and necessary relationship between the value of the constant capital and the surplus-value, nor, hence, is there one between the value of the total capital (= c + v) and the surplus-value.\" This is the formal demolition of any attempt to derive profit from the value of capital advanced. The rate of profit is an inner-relationless measure — it correlates mathematically but expresses no causal internal connection. (Elsewhere Marx will show that it is the organic composition , not the price or value level of constant capital as such, that mediates the relation between s and c — but here he confines himself to denying the direct relation.) Starting from the Rate of Profit: The Uniformity of Capital's Parts and Fixed vs. Circulating Marx then confronts the epistemic asymmetry in practice. \"If the rate of surplus-value and its absolute magnitude are both given, the rate of profit expresses no more than what it in fact is, i.e. an alternative measurement of surplus-value, its measurement in terms of the value of the total capital, instead of in terms of the value of that part of capital from which it directly derives.\" \"In actuality, however, i.e. in the world of phenomena, things are the other way round.\" What is given in the market is the excess of sale price over cost price, and its ratio to total capital. The origin of the excess \"remains a mystery\" — from exploitation, mutual cheating, or both. And critically: \"if we start from this rate of profit, we can never establish any specific relationship between the excess and the part of capital laid out on wages.\" This is the heart of the argument. The rate of profit is, by construction, blind to the internal differentiation of capital. It shows \"a uniform relationship of the excess to equally important parts of the capital, which from this point of view exhibits no internal distinctions apart from that between fixed and circulating.\" Marx then shows how even this residual distinction arises only retrospectively, from the two ways the excess is calculated: (a) as a simple quantity — the excess over cost price — in which circulating capital enters \"in full\" while fixed capital enters \"only to the extent of its depreciation\"; and (b) as the relationship of the excess to the total value of capital advanced, in which \"the value of the entire fixed capital comes into the calculation as much as the value of the circulating capital.\" Hence circulating capital is treated the same way in both computations, while fixed capital is treated differently in the first from the second. \"Thus the distinction between circulating and fixed capital suggests itself here to us as the only one.\" Even here, then, the surface form retains only a pale, derivative trace of the real internal distinctions (c versus v) that determine surplus-value. This remarkable passage also shows that the fixed/circulating distinction, though real and important for turnover, is not the value-form distinction that matters for surplus-value. It is a distinction belonging to the appearance of profit accounting — and only here does it force itself upon us. Marx adds an aside on Malthus: \"We shall see in a later chapter the amusing capers Malthus cuts when he tries in this way to penetrate the secret of surplus-value and its specific relationship to the variable part of capital\" (see Theories of Surplus-Value , Part III, pp. 31–4). This is an anticipatory dismissal of the attempt to reach the secret through the profit rate rather than through the analysis of surplus-value as such. The Hegelian Reflection and Profit as Appearance Marx now offers a deliberately Hegelian formulation: \"the excess is reflected back into itself from the rate of profit … the excess, which is characterized more specifically by the rate of profit, appears as an excess which the capital produces over and above its own value, either annually or in some definite period of circulation.\" Reflection-into-self ( Reflexion in sich ) is the Hegelian category of a determination that, having passed through otherness, returns to itself as its own ground. Here capital, having passed through production and circulation, returns as self-valorizing : the excess seems to be its own product, not the product of the appropriation of another's labour. This yields the chapter's culminating statement of the form/content relation: \"even if the rate of profit is numerically different from the rate of surplus-value, while surplus-value and profit are in fact the same and even numerically identical, profit is still for all that a transformed form of surplus-value, a form in which its origin and the secret of its existence are veiled and obliterated.\" Profit is the form of appearance ( Erscheinungsform ) of surplus-value; surplus-value \"can be sifted out from the former only by analysis.\" In surplus-value, \"the relationship between capital and labour is laid bare\"; in profit, \"capital appears as a relationship to itself, a relationship in which it is distinguished, as an original sum of value, from another new value that it posits.\" To consciousness, \"it appears as if capital creates this new value in the course of its movement through the production and circulation processes.\" And crucially: \"how this happens is now mystified, and appears to derive from hidden qualities that are inherent in capital itself.\" The mysterious \"productive power of capital\" — capital as a self-creating substance — is the theoretical horizon toward which the whole chapter drives. The final sentence of the substantive argument states the general law of the argument: \"The further we trace out the valorization process of capital, the more is the capital relationship mystified and the less are the secrets of its internal organization laid bare.\" This is a law of increasing obscurity : the closer we come to the surface of economic life (profit, interest, rent), the more opaque the underlying social relation becomes. It is a programmatic statement for the whole of Volume 3. Looking Forward: The Plan for This Part and the Next The chapter closes with its prospective statement: \"In this Part, the rate of profit is taken as numerically different from the rate of surplus-value; profit and surplus-value on the other hand are treated as numerically identical magnitudes, different only in form.\" This is a precise statement of the abstraction level operative in Part 1 of Volume 3. Then: \"In the following Part we shall observe the further development of the externalization by which profit presents itself as a magnitude distinct from surplus-value in a numerical respect as well.\" Marx signals that the mystification is not static but progressive — it deepens as we move through the equalization of profit rates (Part 2), the tendency of the rate of profit to fall (Part 3), merchant's capital, interest-bearing capital, and ground-rent. The next step, the formation of a general rate of profit, will make profit numerically diverge from surplus-value in each sphere, completing the appearance of capital as an autonomous source of value. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental principle revealed by this chapter is that of the necessarily mystifying form of capitalist economic categories . Profit is not an illusion in the sense of a false belief that could be dispelled by simply informing capitalists of the truth; it is the objective form of appearance that surplus-value necessarily assumes on the surface of capitalist society. Marx's argument is therefore a demonstration that appearance is real — that surface phenomena are not mere errors but structurally produced forms with their own necessity, regularity, and explanatory weight. This is the methodological core of Volume 3 as a whole: to trace the \"forms of the process as a whole\" ( Gestaltungen des Gesamtprozesses ) without either reducing them to the production process (the Ricardian error) or treating them as autonomous (the vulgar error). The second lesson is the asymmetry of explanation. Essence explains appearance, but appearance does not explain essence — and yet, \"in actuality … things are the other way round.\" The analyst begins from the surface (profit) because that is the historical and experiential starting point, but the scientific movement runs from surplus-value to profit. This is a general law of critical social science: the categories in which agents experience their world are the categories that conceal the social relations producing that world. Marx calls this a \"transposed consciousness,\" and its roots lie not in the mind but in the \"inversion of subject and object\" of the production process itself, where the productive forces of labour are presented as those of capital. The third lesson is the critical role of the denominator. The chapter demonstrates with mathematical precision that the entire difference between the rate of surplus-value and the rate of profit lies in the denominator — v versus C = c + v. This apparently minor formal shift has enormous consequences: it makes profit blind to exploitation intensity, blind to differences in organic composition, blind even to the c/v distinction (retaining only the fixed/circulating trace). The rate of profit thus functions as a systematic concealer . The absence of any \"inner and necessary relationship\" between the value of constant capital and surplus-value is the formal guarantee that no direct reading of exploitation off the profit rate is possible. The fourth lesson concerns the material motivation of mystification. Marx does not treat the capitalist's blindness as mere stupidity; he shows \"it is actually in his interest to disguise these particular ratios and inner connections.\" The form of profit corresponds to a real class interest in not seeing the source of profit in unpaid labour. This is an early and decisive instance of the Marxian critique of ideology as rooted in material position rather than in error. The broader implications are considerable. For the interpretation of Volume 3 as a whole, the chapter establishes that the analysis of profit, interest, rent, and the \"trinity formula\" will be an anatomy of appearances — each form further from the essence than the last, and the whole movement characterized by the law that \"the further we trace out the valorization process of capital, the more is the capital relationship mystified.\" For political economy as a critical project, the chapter establishes that \"vulgar economy\" is not simply wrong but is the conceptual reflex of the surface form — a demonstration that Marx's critique operates at a higher level than mere refutation of error. For the theory of crisis and of class struggle, the chapter explains why workers and capitalists alike experience the economy in terms of profitability rather than exploitation, and why the actual rate of surplus-value may rise even as the experienced rate of profit falls. And for the philosophy of social science, the chapter provides a rigorous model of the relation between essence and appearance, real abstraction, and the necessity of mystification — a model whose import extends far beyond the critique of political economy to any inquiry into how social structures generate the categories through which they are lived. The chapter's final sentence thus functions as both conclusion and overture: the externalization of surplus-value into profit, here shown in its form , will in the following Part be shown in its numerical divergence — and the veil, already thick, will grow thicker still. What Marx establishes here, once and for all, is that the project of Volume 3 is not to deny the reality of profit but to explain the necessity of its misrecognized form — and thereby to strip from the capital relation the last semblance of its self-creating autonomy. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 1 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 3"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 3", "title": "Chapter 3", "subtitle": "The Relation of the Rate of Profit to the Rate of Surplus-Value", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 3/Chapter 3 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 12657, "read_time": "63 min read", "tags": ["Cost Price & Rate of Profit"], "keywords": ["Relation of Rate of Profit to Rate of Surplus-Value", "Mathematical Formulations of Profit Rate", "Variables Affecting Profit (s', v/C, Turnover)", "Proportionality and Inverse Variations of Profit Ratios", "Limits of Value Extraction per Capital Advanced"], "text": "Chapter 3 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 12,685 words ⏱️ ~63 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 3: The Relationship between Rate of Profit and Rate of Surplus-Value — An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem Chapter 3 of the third volume of Capital opens the volume's Part One, whose title — \"The Transformation of Surplus-Value into Profit\" — names the entire intellectual operation that the volume must perform. That operation begins here, with a deliberately narrow, almost austere question: given that the profit accruing to a capital is quantitatively identical with the surplus-value that capital produces, what is the precise mathematical relationship between the rate of profit and the rate of surplus-value? The chapter is, as Marx announces on its first page, \"firstly, therefore, a purely mathematical one.\" This is not a rhetorical flourish. It is a methodological declaration. Marx is about to do something that neither the classical political economists nor his own earlier volumes had done with full rigour: he will hold the mass of profit equal to the mass of surplus-value, strip away every complicating circumstance he can legitimately abstract from, and then trace — algebraically, case by case, without remainder — how the ratio p' = s/C behaves when each of its constituent magnitudes is varied in turn. The result is not a narrative but a taxonomy: a complete enumeration of the possible relations between the two rates, culminating in a set of summary propositions that read like the theorems of a formal system. The thesis that emerges is deceptively simple and enormously consequential: the rate of profit is a function of two and only two major factors — the rate of surplus-value (s') and the value composition of the capital (v/C). Everything else in the chapter is the unfolding of that claim. Because the rate of profit is the product of these two factors ( p' = s' · v/C ), the two underlying rates are systematically decoupled: the rate of surplus-value can rise while the rate of profit falls, fall while the rate of profit rises, and either can move in perfect indifference to the other. The \"rate of profit\" is therefore not a transparent index of exploitation intensity. It is a compound, and the chapter's whole purpose is to decompose the compound and catalogue its permutations. The Intellectual Objective The objective is threefold. First, to correct the naive identification. Volume 1 had established s' = s/v , the rate of surplus-value. The capitalist, however, does not experience his return as a ratio to the variable capital he advances; he experiences it as a ratio to the whole capital he has sunk into the enterprise, machinery and raw materials included. That re-description — from s/v to s/(c+v) — is not a mere change of notation. It is, Marx argues throughout the volume, the point at which the origin of profit is obscured . The chapter establishes rigorously how much the obscuring operation costs in numerical terms, and under exactly what conditions the obscuring operation leaves the magnitude untouched. Second, to build the analytical machinery for everything that follows. The chapter explicitly foreshadows: the effect of turnover on the annual rate of profit (deferred to a later chapter, with Vol. 2, Ch. 16 supplying s'n ); the effect of a saving in constant capital and of changes in the prices of raw materials (deferred to Chapters 5 and 6); the necessity of the falling rate of profit in \"Part Three of this volume\" under conditions of rising productivity; the equalization of profit rates and the divergence of surplus-value from profit proper — interest, ground-rent, taxes — (deferred to Part Two); and the comparison of profit rates between countries. The chapter is thus a keystone: it articulates the framework on which the law of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall, the formation of prices of production, and the theory of ground-rent will all be hung. Third, to discipline a rival theoretical tradition. The chapter contains a single, sharply aimed polemical incision. In Section II.1, Marx notes that the case of a constant percentage composition of capital, a constant working day, constant labour intensity, and a rate of surplus-value varying solely through wages, \"is the only one that meets Ricardo's assumption: 'Profits would be high or low, exactly in proportion as wages would be low or high.'\" The whole architecture of the chapter is designed to show that this case is one among many — indeed, a special and restrictive one. Ricardo's proportionality holds only when the organic composition of capital and the three conditions of labour are held fixed. Once composition varies, the proportionality dissolves. The Broader Intellectual Frame The chapter belongs to the sequence: Volume 1 (production of surplus-value) → Volume 2 (circulation of capital) → Volume 3 (the process of capitalist production as a whole, i.e. the unity of production and circulation, and the concrete forms — profit, rent, interest — in which surplus-value appears to agents). Within Volume 3, Chapter 3's task is to perform the first transformation — the transformation of the rate of surplus-value into the rate of profit — at the level of the individual capital. The second transformation, the transformation of profit into average profit and of values into prices of production, is the business of Part Two. The third, the transformation of average profit into ground-rent, interest, and entrepreneurial profit, follows thereafter. Chapter 3 lays the algebraic floor of the entire structure. It is also worth marking that Marx announces this as a mathematical investigation of relations among magnitudes , and that he treats the examples indifferently as successive states of one capital or as contemporaneous distinct capitals \"from different branches of industry or from different countries.\" That dual reading is not a convenience. It is the bridge between the temporal dynamics of a single capital and the static comparison of many capitals — precisely the bridge that a theory of the general rate of profit requires. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Founding Abstraction: Profit ≡ Surplus-Value, and the Demarcation of the Field The chapter opens by recalling the assumption made at the close of Chapter 2 and maintained throughout Part One: the profit accruing to a given capital is the same sum as the total surplus-value that this capital produces in a given period of circulation. Marx immediately flags what he is setting aside: \"the division of this surplus-value into various subordinate forms: interest, ground-rent, taxes, etc., as also the fact that surplus-value by no means coincides in the majority of cases with profit, as the latter is appropriated by way of the prevailing rate of profit, which we shall return to in Part Two.\" Two distinct exclusions are being made here, and they must not be conflated. The first exclusion is distributive : the surplus-value pump may be tapped by landlords, financiers, and the state before the industrial capitalist keeps his share. The second exclusion is systemic : under competition, capitals do not retain the surplus-value they individually extract but are credited with a share of the social total in proportion to the capital advanced — \"the prevailing rate of profit.\" The second exclusion is the deeper one, because it is not merely a matter of splitting a fixed mass. It is a matter of reallocating it across branches, which will eventually force a divergence of the general rate of profit from the value composition of any individual capital, and thus force the transformation of values into prices of production. The consequence of the assumption, Marx states, is that \"its magnitude, and the magnitude of the rate of profit, are determined by simple numerical ratios, the numbers involved being given or definable in each individual case. Our investigation is firstly, therefore, a purely mathematical one.\" The phrase \"firstly\" is doing work: the mathematics is the skeleton; economic interpretation must be re-infused later. But the mathematics is indispensable because it reveals structural possibilities — for example, that the same profit rate can rest on entirely different rates of surplus-value — that no amount of intuitive reasoning about \"exploitation\" and \"profit\" would disclose. 2.2 The Primary Equations and the Proportionality p' : s' = v : C The notation is inherited from Volumes 1 and 2. Total capital C divides into constant capital c and variable capital v , and produces surplus-value s . The ratio s/v is the rate of surplus-value, denoted s' . Hence: s/v = s' ⟹ s = s'v The same surplus-value, related to the total capital instead of only to the variable capital, is called profit ( p ); the ratio p/C = s/C is the rate of profit, p' . So: p' = s/C = s/(c + v) and substituting s = s'v : p' = s'v/C = s'v/(c + v) Two things must be registered. First, p and p' are, at this stage, purely re-named surplus-value: the same magnitude viewed from a different denominator. Nothing has been added to the substance; only the frame of reference has shifted. Second, the equation can be read as a proportionality : p' : s' = v : C that is, \"rate of profit is to rate of surplus-value as variable capital is to total capital.\" This is the master proportionality of the chapter, and it governs the whole of Section I. From it, an immediate and much-quoted result follows. Because v is always smaller than C = v + c (so long as any constant capital at all is advanced), p' is always smaller than s' . The only exception is the case in which v = C — where the capitalist advances no constant capital, no means of production, only wages. Marx labels this case \"impossible in practice.\" Note the editorial slip in the printed text at this point: the phrase rendered as \"the sum of v + s, variable and constant capital\" must be read as \"the sum of v + c\" — the two components of C, not v plus surplus-value. The logic of the inequality requires it. The inequality carries an important interpretive message that the chapter will later complicate: the profit rate systematically understates the surplus-value rate, and the extent of the understatement is measured precisely by the share of constant capital in the total. The \"camouflage\" of exploitation built into the profit-form is thus not an illusion to be dispelled by argument alone; it is a determinate numerical ratio. 2.3 The Catalogue of Disturbing Factors: Money, Turnover, Productivity — and the n-Turnover Correction Before proceeding, Marx notes that a further series of factors bears on the sizes of c , v , and s \"in a decisive way,\" and must at least be named. (i) The value of money. Taken as constant throughout. This is a standard simplification: if the value of money changed, all money magnitudes would be rescaled, and while the money rate of profit might shift, the value relations expressed in the ratios would not be altered in kind. Setting it constant isolates the real relations. (ii) Turnover. \"We shall ignore this factor completely, for the time being,\" because its influence on the profit rate is treated in a later chapter. But Marx inserts a crucial anticipation: p' = s'v/C is \"strictly correct only for a single turnover period of the variable capital,\" while for the annual turnover \"the simple rate of surplus-value s' has to be replaced by s'n, the annual rate of surplus-value, n standing for the number of turnovers that the variable capital makes in the course of a year\" (with a cross-reference to Vol. 2, Ch. 16, added by Engels). This is a substantive qualification, not a footnote. The whole chapter proceeds on the fiction of a single turnover. Two capitals with identical value compositions and identical rates of surplus-value can have different annual profit rates purely because their capitals turn over at different speeds — a point developed in the later chapter and central to the analysis of merchant's capital and credit. (iii) Productivity of labour. Its influence on the rate of surplus-value was already analysed in Volume 1, Part Four. But Marx adds a point about the individual capital: a capital operating above the social average productivity produces its commodities at a value below the social value and thereby realizes an extra profit (Vol. 1, Ch. 12, pp. 433ff.). This case is also set aside: \"we also proceed from the assumption that commodities are produced under normal social conditions and are sold at their values.\" But Marx then makes a move that is easy to miss and important to grasp. He insists that the value composition of capital and labour productivity are not independent variables in reality: \"The value composition of the capital applied in a particular branch of industry, i.e. a specific ratio between variable and constant capital, expresses in each case a definite level of labour productivity. Thus as soon as this ratio experiences any change that is not simply due either to a change in value of the material components of the constant capital, or to a change in wages, the productivity of labour must also have undergone a change.\" This is the embryonic statement of the organic composition of capital: the value ratio c/v is the value-expression of a technical ratio (means of production per worker), and changes in it, unless traceable to revaluation or to wage changes, register changes in productivity. The whole subsequent exercise of \"varying v/C\" is therefore not a purely formal operation; each variation has an economic meaning indexed to productivity, to the length or intensity of the working day, or to wages. The chapter's abstraction is disciplined , not arbitrary. 2.4 The Three Remaining Determinants: Working Day, Intensity, Wages — and the Demonstration Examples The same remark applies to the remaining three factors: the length of the working day , the intensity of labour , and wages . Their influence on the mass and rate of surplus-value was developed in Volume 1, Chapter 17. Marx summarises the directionality: Wages affect the size and rate of surplus-value inversely : a rise in wages reduces surplus-value. Length of the working day and intensity of labour affect it directly : an extension of the day and a greater intensity both increase surplus-value. He then works three numerical demonstrations with a single capital of 100. Baseline. 20 workers, 10-hour day, weekly wage bill 20: 80c + 20v + 20s; s' = 100%; p' = 20% Total value product = 40 (20v + 20s). Note the composition: v/C = 20/100, so p' = 100% × 20/100 = 20%. Demonstration 1 — extension of the working day. Extend the day to 15 hours without increasing wages. Total value produced rises from 40 to 60, since 10 : 15 = 40 : 60. The wage (v) remains 20; the surplus rises from 20 to 40: 80c + 20v + 40s; s' = 200%; p' = 40% Here the denominator C remains 100 (c and v unchanged), so the entire effect on p' is transmitted through s' alone. p' doubles exactly as s' doubles. Demonstration 2 — a fall in wages. The wage for the same 10 hours falls from 20 to 12. The total value product remains 40 (same quantity of labour performed), but it is distributed differently: v falls to 12, leaving 28 for s. Hence: 80c + 12v + 28s; s' = 28/12 = 233⅓%; p' = 28/(80 + 12) = 28/92 = 30 10/23% Two things happen simultaneously. The rate of surplus-value rises steeply, because the same living labour is now paid less. But the total capital has also fallen , from 100 to 92, because the wage bill is smaller. This is a first glimpse of the fact that a change in v can alter the denominator as well as the numerator — and that p' responds both to the numerator's rise and to the denominator's fall. This is precisely why the percentage-composition convenience (introduced later) becomes treacherous in the analysis of a single capital. The general lesson. Marx draws the conclusion: \"an extension of the working day (or, alternatively, an increase in the intensity of labour) and a fall in wages both raise the mass and with it the rate of surplus-value; conversely, a rise in wages, with other circumstances remaining the same, would reduce the rate of surplus-value. If v grows owing to a rise in wages, this does not express an increased quantity of labour but simply its dearer payment; s' and p' do not rise but fall.\" And the crucial corollary: changes in the working day, intensity, and wages cannot occur without simultaneous changes in v and s and their relationship, and therefore in p' . The variables are not free. The three \"conditions of labour\" and the two magnitudes v and s are locked in a system of mutual determination. 2.5 The Organic Distinction between Constant and Variable Capital At this point the chapter pivots from arithmetic to political economy proper, with a passage of concentrated theoretical importance on \"the special organic connection that the variable capital has with the movement of the capital as a whole and its valorization, as well as its distinction from the constant capital.\" Constant capital. \"In so far as the formation of value is concerned, [it] is important only on account of the value that it has.\" It is immaterial whether £1,500 of constant capital represents 1,500 tons of iron at £1 a ton or 500 tons at £3. The quantity of actual material in which its value is expressed is completely unimportant for value formation and for the rate of profit. Therefore the rate of profit \"varies in the opposite direction to the value of the constant capital\" — irrespective of what relationship the increase or decrease in that value has to the mass of material use-values it represents. This is the theoretical basis for the treatment (in Case 3, below) of revaluation and technical change as equivalent in their effect on p': only the value of c matters. Variable capital. \"The case of the variable capital is completely different. What matters above all here is not the value that it actually has, the amount of labour objectified in it, but rather this value as a mere index of the total labour that it sets in motion, which is not expressed in it.\" The claim is dense, and it must be unpacked carefully. The variable capital is the money advanced to buy labour-power . Its magnitude is the wage bill. But the value it brings into being is not the value of the labour-power; it is the value that living labour produces in the time that labour-power is at the capitalist's disposal. The wage bill is therefore a pointer — an index — to a quantity of living labour that exceeds it. The difference between the total labour set in motion and the labour objectified and paid for in the variable capital is the surplus-value. And: \"the difference between this total labour and the labour expressed and therefore paid for in the variable capital, i.e. the portion that forms surplus-value, is greater in proportion as the labour contained in the variable capital gets smaller.\" Marx illustrates: assume a working day of 10 hours = 10 shillings. If necessary labour (the labour that replaces the wage, i.e. replaces v) is 5 hours, surplus-value = 5 shillings. If necessary labour is 4 hours = 4 shillings, surplus labour is 6 hours and surplus-value 6 shillings. The conclusion is stated with unusual crispness: \"as soon as the value of the variable capital ceases to be an index of the mass of labour that it sets in motion, and the basis of this index itself changes, the rate of surplus-value changes in the opposite direction and in inverse proportion.\" This is the theoretical heart of the distinction between constant and variable capital, and it explains why the composition variable v/C is so analytically volatile: c is sheer accumulated value, mute and inert; v is a cipher for living labour, and any change in the value of the cipher (a change in wages, say) does not proportionally change the labour it commands. The \"index problem\" is the source of all the complications in the case analysis that follows. 2.6 The Analytical Programme: Decomposing p' = s'v/C Marx now lays out the plan with complete transparency. He intends to apply the equation p' = s'v/C to \"the various possible cases,\" varying the individual factors of the product s'v/C successively and establishing the effect on the rate of profit. Crucially, he specifies that the examples may be read in two ways: \"either as successive changes in circumstances for the action of one and the same capital, or, indeed, as different capitals, existing simultaneously alongside one another, and brought in for purposes of comparison, e.g. from different branches of industry or from different countries.\" And if some examples seem \"forced or practically impossible\" as chronological states of one capital, \"this objection disappears as soon as they are viewed as the result of a comparison between separate capitals.\" This is a methodological statement of the first importance: comparative statics and inter-capital comparison are treated as formally equivalent representations of the same algebra. It is also, incidentally, the chapter's second quiet gesture toward the international comparison of profit rates at the end. The programme is: Section I: hold s' constant and investigate the possible variations in v/C . Section II: hold v/C constant and put s' through its variations. Finally: treat all factors as variable — which, Marx asserts, exhausts all the cases from which the laws governing the profit rate may be derived. 2.7 Section I, General Formula — The Master Equations of Compositional Variation The case s' constant, v/C variable comprises several subordinate cases and is covered by a general formula. Take two capitals C and C₁ , with variable components v and v₁ , a common rate of surplus-value s' , and rates of profit p' and p'₁ . Then: p' = s'v/C and p'₁ = s'v₁/C₁ Suppose C₁ = EC and v₁ = ev , where E ≡ C₁/C and e ≡ v₁/v are the coefficients of variation of total capital and variable capital respectively. Substituting: p'₁ = s' · (ev)/(EC) = s' · (e/E) · (v/C) This is the master formula for Section I. It says that the new profit rate equals the old profit rate multiplied by the ratio of the two variation coefficients, e/E . Every case in Section I is a special case of this formula. A second formula is obtained by converting the two original equations into a proportionality: p' : p'₁ = s'(v/C) : s'(v₁/C₁) = v/C : v₁/C₁ Since the value of a fraction is unchanged if numerator and denominator are multiplied or divided by the same number, we may reduce both v/C and v₁/C₁ to percentages by taking C = C₁ = 100 . Then v/C = v/100 and v₁/C₁ = v₁/100 . Multiplying the proportionality by 100 to clear the denominators of 100: p' : p'₁ = v : v₁ This yields the section's headline theorem: Given any two capitals functioning with the same rate of surplus-value, the rates of profit stand in the same proportion as the variable components of the capitals, each calculated as a percentage of its total capital. These two forms — the coefficient formula and the percentage proportionality — \"cover all cases of variation in v/C.\" 2.8 The Percentage Form: Its Utility and Its Deceptiveness Before working the subordinate cases, Marx pauses on the practice of expressing composition in percentages — taking c + v = 100 . He notes that the percentage form is not immaterial for determining the mass of profit, but is immaterial for the rate of profit. His illustration: a capital of 15,000, of which 12,000 is constant and 3,000 variable, produces a surplus-value of 3,000. Reduced to percentages: 15,000C = 12,000c + 3,000v (+ 3,000s) ⟹ 100C = 80c + 20v (+ 20s) In both cases s' = 100% and p' = 20% . Likewise for comparison between two capitals: 12,000C = 10,800c + 1,200v (+ 1,200s) ⟹ 100C = 90c + 10v (+ 10s) with s' = 100% and p' = 10% . The comparison is far easier to make in percentage form. But the percentage form has a fatal defect when applied to one and the same capital over time. If a capital passes from 80c + 20v + 20s to 90c + 10v + 10s , we cannot tell whether the new composition arose from an absolute decline in v , an absolute rise in c , or both. Marx enumerates three distinct underlying movements that all yield the identical percentage composition 90c + 10v : 12,000c + 3,000v → 27,000c + 3,000v (increase in c, v constant); 12,000c + 3,000v → 12,000c + 1,333⅓v (reduction in v, c constant); 12,000c + 3,000v → 13,500c + 1,500v (change in both). These three cases have different consequences for the rate of profit once mass matters, and they have entirely different economic meanings. The percentage form \"almost always obliterates\" these changes. Therefore the analysis must \"dispense with the convenience of the percentage form,\" or apply it \"only as a supplement to the main argument.\" This is a warning about a genuinely deep methodological trap: the ratio alone does not identify the path, and it is the path that carries the economic information about productivity, accumulation, and capital release. 2.9 Case 1: s' and C constant, v variable If v changes while C remains unaltered, then c must change by the same amount in the opposite direction. If the capital was 80c + 20v = 100 and v falls to 10, then c must rise to 90: 90c + 10v = 100 . In general, if v → v ± d , then c → c ∓ d . Given s' unchanged and v changed, the mass of surplus-value must also change, since s = s'v and one of the factors of s'v has been given another value. The equations: p' = s'v/C and p'₁ = s'v₁/C yield the proportionality: p' : p'₁ = s'(v/C) : s'(v₁/C) = v : v₁ Therefore: with the rate of surplus-value and the total capital both remaining the same, the original profit rate is related to the new profit rate arrived at by a change in the variable capital, as the original variable capital is to the new variable capital. A first numerical illustration: I. 15,000C = 12,000c + 3,000v (+ 3,000s) s' = 100%, p' = 20% II. 15,000C = 13,000c + 2,000v (+ 2,000s) s' = 100%, p' = 13⅓% and indeed 20% : 13⅓% = 3,000 : 2,000 . The rising-v example. Marx now takes a capital originally constituted as: I. 100c + 20v + 10s; C = 120, s' = 50%, p' = 8⅓% and raises v to 30. Since C must remain 120, c falls from 100 to 90. At s' = 50% , the surplus rises to 15: II. 90c + 30v + 15s; C = 120, s' = 50%, p' = 12½% Now Marx submits this to economic interrogation. What does the transition from I to II mean ? Reading A: wages unchanged. If wages are unchanged, then the increase in v from 20 to 30 can only mean that half again as many workers are employed. With the same working day and intensity, the total value produced rises by half, from 30 to 45, and it is divided just as before — two-thirds to wages, one-third to surplus-value. The arithmetic is consistent: v = 30 (two-thirds of 45), s = 15 (one-third of 45), s' = 50% . But there is a snag, and Marx pounces on it: the constant capital has fallen from 100 to 90 at the very moment when more workers are being employed. \"We have therefore a case of a decline in labour productivity combined with a simultaneous decline in constant capital. Is this case economically possible?\" His answer is carefully calibrated. In agriculture and the extractive industries, a decline in productivity with more workers is easy to comprehend — but \"within the confines of capitalist production, and on its basis,\" such a process \"is linked not with a decline in constant capital but with an increase.\" Even where the decline in c is occasioned merely by a fall in price, an individual capital could make the transition from I to II only under quite exceptional conditions. With two independent capitals , however, invested in different countries or in different branches of agriculture or extractive industry, it would be \"by no means unusual\" for one to employ more workers with less expensive or less plentiful means of production. Here the dual reading (one capital through time vs. two capitals side by side) does decisive analytical work. Reading B: wages rise by half. Now suppose the rise in v from 20 to 30 reflects a rise in the wage rate by one-half. Then the picture changes completely. The same number of workers (say 20) continue to work with the same or only insignificantly reduced means of production. If the working day remains 10 hours, the total value produced remains 30 — exactly as before. But that 30 would now be entirely absorbed in replacing the advanced variable capital of 30; surplus-value would vanish. Since s' was posited constant at 50%, this is impossible. The only way out: extend the working day by half, to 15 hours. Twenty workers then produce a total value of 45 in a 15-hour day, and all the conditions are satisfied: II. 90c + 30v + 15s; C = 120, s' = 50%, p' = 12½% Under this reading, the 20 workers need no more means of labour — no more tools or machinery — than in I. Only the raw or ancillary materials must increase by half. And, Marx adds, \"If these materials fall in price, the transition from I to II would be much more possible as an economic phenomenon, given our assumptions, even for one and the same capital. And the capitalist would be compensated at least partially, by a bigger profit, for the loss that the devaluation of his constant capital would have caused him.\" (Note: the mass of profit rises from 10 to 15 and the rate from 8⅓% to 12½%.) The falling-v direction. Reverse the example, taking II as the starting point and moving to I. \"It is readily apparent that by this reversal, the rates of profit in the two cases and the conditions governing their mutual relationship are not changed in the slightest.\" Two readings again: Reading A: v falls because fewer workers work with more means of production. This is \"simply the normal case in modern industry: rising productivity of labour, the operation of greater quantities of means of production by fewer workers.\" And Marx adds a forward-looking promissory note of the highest theoretical importance: \"And in Part Three of this volume we shall see how this movement is necessarily bound up with a simultaneous fall in the rate of profit.\" The chapter thus seeds the law of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall at its very beginning. Reading B: v falls because the same number of workers are paid a lower wage rate. If the working day is unchanged, the total value product remains 30v + 15s = 45. With v fallen to 20, surplus-value would rise to 25 and s' from 50% to 125% — against our assumption . To stay within the example, surplus-value at 50% must fall to 10, and thus the total value produced from 45 to 30. This is possible \"only if the working day is cut by one-third\": 100c + 20v + 10s; s' = 50%, p' = 8⅓% Marx then issues the chapter's most explicit methodological caveat — and it is worth quoting in full, because it licenses everything that follows: \"We need hardly point out that a reduction in working hours of this kind combined with a fall in wages would not occur in practice. But this is beside the point. The rate of profit is a function of several variables, and if we want to know how these variables act on the profit rate we must investigate in turn the individual effect of each, irrespective of whether an isolated effect of this kind is economically possible or not in the case of one and the same capital.\" The method is therefore not descriptive but analytical decomposition : isolate each partial derivative, then recombine. This is comparative statics avant la lettre, and it is a key to the chapter's structure. 2.10 Case 2: s' constant, v variable, C altered by the variation of v This case \"is different from the previous one only in degree.\" Instead of c moving by the same amount as v in the opposite direction, c now remains constant; consequently C moves as v moves. Marx notes the quantitative context: \"under today's conditions of large-scale industry and agriculture, variable capital is only a relatively small portion of the total capital and hence any reduction or growth in the total capital that is brought about by a change in the variable capital is also relatively slight.\" (This is Marx's own observation of the historically rising composition of capital — a point that will matter enormously for the falling-profit-rate argument.) Example: I. 100c + 20v + 10s; C = 120, s' = 50%, p' = 8⅓% II. 100c + 30v + 15s; C = 130, s' = 50%, p' = 15/130 = 11 7/13% The opposite case of decline in v is the reverse transition from II to I. Marx judges the economic conditions \"essentially the same as in the previous case, and hence need no further explanation.\" The transition from I to II involves a decline of one-third in the productivity of labour, or, equivalently, the operation of 100c requires half as much labour again in II as in I. Editorial intervention (Engels's footnote). Marx wrote \"a half\" here; Engels corrects: this is \"a slip arising from measuring the decline against the resulting figure rather than the original one.\" The arithmetic: labour rises from 20 to 30, an increase of one-half measured from the original 20; but productivity declines by one-third measured from the new 30 (10/30). This is exactly the sort of ratio-base confusion that the chapter is otherwise at pains to avoid, and Engels's correction is a small methodological service. A second editorial note records a loose thread in the manuscript: \"The manuscript has here: 'For later investigation, how this case is related to ground-rent.'\" — a reminder that the abstract arithmetic of composition is already pointing toward the concrete forms of landed property. The category of capital release and tying-up. Marx draws out the distinctive feature of this case relative to the previous one: \"Whereas in the previous case the total capital was held constant by the conversion of constant capital into variable or vice versa, now the increase in the variable portion means that extra capital is tied up, while a decrease involves the release of capital that had previously been needed.\" This introduces a distinction that will become central in Volumes 2 and 3 (in the analysis of credit, of the circulation of capital, and of crises): changes in the composition of capital are not merely changes in ratios; they either absorb additional capital or liberate previously committed capital. The category of \"released capital\" is not a formal curiosity — it is a real quantity available for new investment, and thus a mechanism of accumulation and of cyclical movement. 2.11 Case 3: s' and v constant, c (and therefore C) variable Here the equation: p' = s'v/C becomes: p'₁ = s'v/C₁ and canceling on both sides gives: p' : p'₁ = C : C₁ With the same rate of surplus-value and the same variable capital, the profit rate stands in inverse proportion to the total capital. Illustration with three capitals (or three states of one capital): I. 80c + 20v + 20s; C = 100, s' = 100%, p' = 20% II. 100c + 20v + 20s; C = 120, s' = 100%, p' = 16⅔% III. 60c + 20v + 20s; C = 80, s' = 100%, p' = 25% And indeed 20% : 16⅔% = 120 : 100 ; and 20% : 25% = 80 : 100 . In the general formula of Section I, p'₁ = s' · (ev)/(EC) , this case corresponds to setting e = v₁/v = 1 (since v is unaltered), so that: p'₁ = s' · v/(EC) Because s'v = s and both s' and v are constant, the mass of surplus-value is also unaffected by any variation in C; it remains what it was before the change. Only the denominator moves. Marx notes the limiting case: \"If c were to fall to zero, we would have p' = s', the rate of profit equal to the rate of surplus-value.\" This is the same degenerate case flagged at the outset ( v = C ), now approached from the other direction. Sources of the change in c — and their equivalence for value. The alteration in c may come about either: 1. from a change merely in the value of the material elements of the constant capital; or 2. from a changed technical composition of the total capital, i.e. a change in the productivity of labour in the branch in question. Under the second heading, Marx reads the sequence of his three examples as a history of rising productivity: \"the productivity of social labour, which rises with the development of large-scale industry and agriculture, would successively move from III to I and from I to II.\" He spells out the process: a quantity of labour paid 20 and producing a value of 40 starts by operating means of labour worth 60; as productivity rises, the means of labour operated grow first to 80, then to 100, \"if their value remained the same.\" The reverse sequence registers a decline in productivity — the same quantity of labour sets less means of production in motion, and \"the business would be cut back, as can well happen in agriculture, mining, etc.\" Two further observations follow: (a) Savings in constant capital. \"A saving in constant capital both increases the rate of profit and releases capital as well, and this is important for the capitalist. We shall return to this point later, as well as investigating the effect of changes in the prices of the elements of constant capital, raw materials in particular.\" Engels's footnote identifies Chapters 5 and 6 as the locus of that promised treatment. This is a major fork in the road: the whole analysis of \"moral depreciation,\" cheapening of raw materials, and the counteracting tendencies to the falling rate of profit will run through this issue. (b) Value change vs. technical change. The section closes with the equivalence thesis: \"We see here again how a variation in constant capital has the same effect on the rate of profit, irrespective of whether this variation is brought about by an increase or decrease in the material components of c, or simply by a change in their value.\" This is the counterpart, on the constant-capital side, of the \"organic distinction\" developed earlier: because constant capital matters only as value, the route by which its value changes is immaterial for the rate of profit. 2.12 Case 4: s' constant, v, c and C all variable The general formula p'₁ = s'·(ev)/(EC) still applies. Marx extracts three results: (a) The rate of profit falls if E > e — that is, if the constant capital is increased in such a way that the total capital increases more sharply than the variable capital. Example: 80c + 20v + 20s → 170c + 30v + 30s s' remains 100%, but v/C falls from 20/100 to 30/200 , and p' accordingly falls from 20% to 15%. (b) The rate of profit remains unchanged only if e = E — that is, if the fraction v/C retains the same value despite the apparent change; in other words, if both numerator and denominator are multiplied or divided by the same figure. Example: 80c + 20v + 20s and 160c + 40v + 40s Both have p' = 20% , because s' remains 100% and v/C = 20/100 = 40/200 exhibits the same value. (c) The rate of profit rises if e > E — that is, if the variable capital rises more sharply than the total capital. Example: 80c + 20v + 20s → 120c + 40v + 40s with s' unaltered, v/C = 20/100 has risen to 40/160 = 1/4 , so p' rises from 20% to 25%. The reduction technique. Marx then supplies a general method for handling the case where v and C both move: \"Where v and C both change in the same direction, we can conceive this change in their magnitudes as if both vary to a certain extent in the same ratio, so that up to this point v/C remains unaltered. Beyond this point, then, only one of them varies, and we can thereby reduce this more complicated case to one or other of the previous simpler ones.\" Illustration 1 (same direction). 80c + 20v + 20s changes to 100c + 30v + 30s . The ratio between v and c , and therefore between v and C , remains unaltered up to the intermediate point 100c + 25v + 25s (where v/C = 25/125 = 20/100 , so p' is still 20%). From that starting point, v rises by 5, to 30, and C rises from 125 to 130 — this is Case 2. \"The rate of profit, which was originally 20 per cent, is increased by this addition of 5v to 23 1/13 per cent, given the same rate of surplus-value\" (check: 30/130 = 3/13 = 23 1/13% ). Illustration 2 (opposite directions). From 80c + 20v + 20s to 110c + 10v + 10s . An intermediate change to 40c + 10v + 10s would have kept the profit rate the same as originally (20%, since 10/50 = 20/100 ). The addition of 10c to this intermediate form makes it fall to 8⅓% (since 10/120 = 8⅓% ). Again the example is reduced to a variation in one variable only — here c , i.e. Case 3. Conclusion for the section: \"Thus the simultaneous variation of v, c and C does not offer any new aspects, and always leads back in the last analysis to a case in which only one factor is variable.\" 2.13 The Residual Case: Numerical Constancy with Material Change Marx closes Section I by disposing of the one remaining logical possibility: v and c retain the same numerical magnitudes, but their material elements undergo a change in value — so that v now represents a different quantity of labour set in motion, and c a different quantity of means of production. Worked through: in 80c + 20v + 20s , the 20v might originally represent the wages of 20 workers for a 10-hour day. Suppose each worker's wage rises. Then 20v suffices to pay only 16 workers instead of 20. If the 20 workers had produced a value of 40 in their 200 hours' work (200 hours × 0.20 per hour), the 16 workers, in a 10-hour day amounting to 160 hours, produce a value of only 32. Subtracting 20v for wages leaves 12 for surplus-value; the rate of surplus-value would have fallen from 100% to 60% (12/20). But by assumption s' must remain constant. Therefore the working day must be extended by a quarter, from 10 hours to 12½ hours: sixteen workers at 12½ hours = 200 working hours, the same total labour time as before, hence the same value product, hence the same surplus-value of 20. (Scholarly note on the printed text: the passage as printed reads \"If 20 workers produce a value of 50 in a working day of 10 hours…\", but this is inconsistent with the value product of 40 established two sentences earlier and with the requirement that the capital 80c + 20v still yield a surplus of 20 at s' = 100% . The coherent reading — and the one the argument requires — is a value product of 40. The essential result is unaffected either way: the same capital yields the same surplus-value only if the working day lengthens by exactly the factor by which the wage rate rose.) Conversely, if wages fall so that 20v covers the wages of 30 workers, s' can remain constant only if the working day is reduced from 10 hours to 6⅔: since 20 × 10 = 30 × 6⅔ = 200 working hours . Marx adds two qualifications. First, on the constant-capital side, he notes that the previous discussion had already explained \"in essentials how c can retain the same value expression in money throughout all these conflicting assumptions, while representing the differing quantities of means of production which correspond to the changed conditions. This case would however be very exceptional in its pure form.\" Second, on revaluation of individual elements of c: \"a change that increases or decreases certain elements while leaving their value sum c unaltered… disturbs neither the rate of profit nor the rate of surplus-value, as long as it does not bring with it any alteration in the magnitude of v.\" Again, only the sum matters. 2.14 The Limits of Compositional Variation The section closes with a claim that is easy to skim and that is in fact the bridge to Section II: \"In this way we have dealt with all possible cases of variation of v, c and C in our equation. We have seen how the profit rate can fall, rise or remain the same, with the rate of surplus-value constant throughout, in so far as the slightest alteration in the ratio between v and c or C is sufficient to alter the profit rate as well. It has also become evident that there is always a limit to the variation of v beyond which it is economically impossible for s' to remain constant. Since any unilateral variation of c must similarly reach a limit at which v can no longer remain constant, it is clear that limits are placed on all possible variations of v/C beyond which s' must also vary. In the case of these variations in s', which we shall now turn to investigate, the mutual interaction of the various different variables in our equation appears even more clearly.\" The argument is that the variables cannot be varied independently beyond a point . There is a \"feasible region\" for compositional variation within which s' can be held fixed — and its boundary is set by the physical and institutional limits on the working day, on intensity, and on wages. Beyond that boundary, s' must move. This is an elegant and important structural claim: the abstract algebra is bounded by concrete conditions of labour. 2.15 Section II, General Formula — s' Variable Section II turns to rates of surplus-value that vary, \"irrespective of whether v/C remains constant or also varies.\" A general formula for the corresponding rates of profit is obtained by converting p' = s'v/C into p'₁ = s'₁v₁/C₁ from which: p' : p'₁ = s'(v/C) : s'₁(v₁/C₁) and therefore p'₁ = p' · (s'₁/s') · (v₁/v) · (C/C₁) This is the master formula for Section II. It says that the change in the profit rate is the product of three ratios: the ratio of the new to the old rate of surplus-value, the ratio of the new to the old variable capital, and the inverse ratio of the new to the old total capital. Each subsequent case is this formula with one or more of the ratios set to unity. 2.16 Case II.1: s' variable, v/C constant — The Ricardo Case Here: p' = s'(v/C) and p'₁ = s'₁(v/C) such that v/C has the same value in both cases. It follows that: p' : p'₁ = s' : s'₁ The rates of profit for two capitals of the same composition are in direct proportion to their respective rates of surplus-value. And since the absolute magnitudes of v and C do not enter into the fraction v/C but only their ratio, this holds for all capitals of the same composition, whatever their absolute magnitude. Example: 80c + 20v + 20s; C = 100, s' = 100%, p' = 20% 160c + 40v + 40s; C = 200, s' = 50%, p' = 10% and 100% : 50% = 20% : 10% . If the absolute magnitudes of v and C are the same in both cases, the profit rates also stand in the same ratio as the masses of surplus-value: p' : p'₁ = s'v : s'₁v = s : s₁ Example: 80c + 20v + 20s; s' = 100%, p' = 20% 80c + 20v + 10s; s' = 50%, p' = 10% and 20% : 10% = (100 × 20) : (50 × 20) = 20s : 10s . The three mechanisms of an s' change. Given capitals of the same composition — either absolutely or relatively — the rate of surplus-value can vary only if one of three things varies: wages, the length of the working day, or the intensity of labour. Marx sets up three states: I. 80c + 20v + 10s; s' = 50%, p' = 10% II. 80c + 20v + 20s; s' = 100%, p' = 20% III. 80c + 20v + 40s; s' = 200%, p' = 40% with total value products of 30, 40, and 60 respectively. He then shows the three different mechanisms by which this can happen. Mechanism 1 — variation in wages (a different number of workers at 20v). Assume 15 workers are employed in Case I for 10 hours at a wage of £1⅓ each, producing a value of £30, of which £20 replaces wages and £10 remains as surplus-value (check: 15 × £1⅓ = £20; each worker produces £2 of new value in 10 hours, so s' = 10/20 = 50%). If wages fall to £1, twenty workers are employed for 10 hours, producing a value of £40, of which £20 is wages and £20 surplus-value (s' = 100%). If wages fall further to £⅔, thirty workers are employed for 10 hours and produce a value of £60, of which £40 remains as surplus-value after subtracting £20 for wages (s' = 200%). And now the polemical hammer-blow: \"This case, that of a constant percentage composition of capital, constant working day, constant intensity of labour, with changes in the rate of surplus-value brought about by changes in wages, is the only one that meets Ricardo's assumption: 'Profits would be high or low, exactly in proportion as wages would be low or high' (Principles, Chapter I, section iii).\" Engels's footnote supplies the Pelican pagination (p. 69) and notes that the emphasis is Marx's own. The point of the citation is to confine Ricardo's proposition to a special case: it holds only where the composition of capital is constant and the working day and intensity are fixed. Since composition varies systematically with the development of industry, the Ricardian proportionality cannot be the general law of profit. Mechanism 2 — variation in the intensity of labour. Suppose 20 workers make 30 items in Case I, 40 in Case II, and 60 in Case III, working with the same means of labour for 10 hours a day, with each item representing a new value of £1 over and above the value of the means of production consumed. Since 20 items = £20 are always needed to replace wages, there remains for surplus-value 10 items = £10 in I, 20 items = £20 in II, and 40 items = £40 in III. Mechanism 3 — variation in the length of the working day. If 20 workers work with the same intensity for 9 hours in I, 12 hours in II, and 18 hours in III, their total products stand in the ratio 9 : 12 : 18 = 30 : 40 : 60, and since wages are 20 each time, there remains 10, 20, or 40 for surplus-value. The general regularity extracted: \"A rise or fall in wages thus effects an opposite change in the rate of surplus-value, while a rise or fall in the intensity of labour, or an extension or reduction of the working day, both effect a change in the same direction, and with v/C constant, the rate of profit is therefore similarly affected.\" Three different causes, one and the same effect on the composition, three different signs of effect on s' and p'. 2.17 Case II.2: s' and v variable, C constant — The Three Sub-Cases Here the proportionality from the Section II general formula reduces, with C = C₁ , to: p' : p'₁ = s'(v/C) : s'₁(v₁/C) = s'v : s'₁v₁ = s : s₁ The rates of profit stand in the same ratio as the respective masses of surplus-value. Marx observes: \"Variation in the rate of surplus-value, with variable capital remaining the same, means a change in the size and distribution of the value product. Simultaneous variation in v and s' similarly entails a different distribution, but not always a change in the magnitude of the value product.\" Three cases are possible. (a) Opposite directions, by the same amount. Example: 80c + 20v + 10s; s' = 50%, p' = 10% 90c + 10v + 20s; s' = 200%, p' = 20% Here the value product is the same in both cases — 20v + 10s = 10v + 20s = 30 — and so is the quantity of labour performed. The distinction is simply that in the first case 20 is paid for wages and 10 for surplus-value, while in the second case wages amount only to 10 and surplus-value is therefore 20. Marx flags: \"This is the only case in which a simultaneous variation in v and s' leaves the number of workers, the labour intensity and the length of the working day unaffected.\" Marx's footnote on the phrase \"by the same amount.\" This is a piece of mathematical housekeeping with real analytical content. Marx concedes that v and s' \"in no sense vary 'by the same amount' in this example, or even in the same proportion.\" What is really invariant is the value product , v + s . He then derives the exact formula. If the values of v, s, and s' after the variation are v₁ , s₁ , s'₁ , and given that v + s = v₁ + s₁ then substituting s = vs' on the left and s₁ = v₁s'₁ on the right: v + vs' = v₁ + v₁s'₁ whence: s'₁ = (v/v₁)(1 + s') − 1 (Verification with the example: v = 20 , s' = 0.5 , so v(1 + s') = 30 ; v₁ = 10 , so 1 + s'₁ = 3 , hence s'₁ = 2 = 200% . The formula reproduces the example exactly.) Marx adds that \"on the basis of this formula, case (b) below can similarly be reduced to an inequality.\" This is a small but telling piece of formalism: the qualitative taxonomy is underpinned by a quantitative criterion (equality, inequality, greater-than-or-less-than) applied to a single equation. (b) Opposite directions, but not to the same extent. \"Either the variation in v must predominate, or that in s'.\" Example: I. 80c + 20v + 20s; s' = 100%, p' = 20% II. 72c + 28v + 20s; s' = 71 3/7%, p' = 20% III. 84c + 16v + 20s; s' = 125%, p' = 20% (Check: I: p' = 20/100 = 20% ; II: 20/28 = 71 3/7% , p' = 20/100 = 20% ; III: 20/16 = 125% , p' = 20/100 = 20% .) The value products are 40, 48, and 36 respectively. Marx's inference: \"Both the value product and the wages have altered; but an alteration in the value product means an alteration in the quantity of labour performed, and therefore either in the number of workers, the duration of labour, or its intensity, if not more than one of these three.\" So case (b) is not one in which the conditions of labour are unchanged; by the very fact that the value product changes, one of the three conditions must have changed. (c) Same direction — mutual reinforcement. Example: 90c + 10v + 10s; s' = 100%, p' = 10% 80c + 20v + 30s; s' = 150%, p' = 30% 92c + 8v + 6s; s' = 75%, p' = 6% Value products are 20, 50, and 14. \"Here, too, the three value products are different… and this difference in the quantity of labour in each case can again be reduced to a difference in the number of workers, the duration or intensity of labour, or any combination of these factors.\" 2.18 Case II.3: s', v and C All Variable \"This case offers no new aspects and is settled by the general formula given under heading II.\" The formal completeness of the earlier construction means the general case adds nothing: the general formula p'₁ = p'(s'₁/s')(v₁/v)(C/C₁) already subsumes every case and every combination. The taxonomy is closed. 2.19 The Five-Fold Summary of s'→p' Effects Marx now gathers the results into a five-part classification of how a change in the rate of surplus-value affects the rate of profit. This is the pivotal analytical summary of the chapter. 1. p' changes in the same ratio as s', if v/C remains constant. 80c + 20v + 20s; s' = 100%, p' = 20% 80c + 20v + 10s; s' = 50%, p' = 10% 100% : 50% = 20% : 10% 2. p' rises or falls in a higher ratio than s', if v/C moves in the same direction as s'. 80c + 20v + 10s; s' = 50%, p' = 10% 70c + 30v + 20s; s' = 66⅔%, p' = 20% And indeed 50% : 66⅔% < 10% : 20% . Here both s' and v/C rise, so the two movements reinforce one another and the profit rate moves more than proportionally. 3. p' rises or falls in a lower ratio than s', if v/C changes in the opposite direction to s', but in a lower ratio. 80c + 20v + 10s; s' = 50%, p' = 10% 90c + 10v + 15s; s' = 150%, p' = 15% And 50% : 150% > 10% : 15% . Here s' rises sharply while v/C falls; the composition change partially offsets the improvement, so p' rises only modestly. 4. p' rises even though s' falls, or falls even though s' rises, if v/C changes in the opposite direction to s', and in a higher ratio. 80c + 20v + 20s; s' = 100%, p' = 20% 90c + 10v + 15s; s' = 150%, p' = 15% \"Here s' has risen from 100 per cent to 150 per cent, while p' has fallen from 20 per cent to 15 per cent.\" This is the decisive case for the chapter's whole tendency: the two rates can move in contrary directions. (Analytical note: cases 3 and 4 employ the same terminal capital — 90c + 10v + 15s — but from different starting points, 80c + 20v + 10s and 80c + 20v + 20s respectively. The construction makes an important point vividly: the same compositional shift, combined with the same s', can produce a rise in p' if s' has risen only modestly, or a fall in p' if s' has risen more dramatically. There is nothing contradictory here; it is precisely the compositional modulation that the chapter has been at pains to exhibit.) 5. p' remains constant even though s' rises or falls, if v/C changes in the opposite direction to s', but in exactly the same ratio. 2.20 The Exceptional Case of Constant p' with Varying s' — and the International Comparison \"It is only this last case that still requires some further discussion.\" Marx draws a crucial contrast between the two directions of analysis: \"We saw above, with the variations in v/C, how one and the same rate of surplus-value can be expressed in the most varied rates of profit. Here we see that one and the same rate of profit can be based on very different rates of surplus-value. But while with s' constant, any change whatsoever in the ratio of v to C is sufficient to induce a variation in the rate of profit, a change in s' must involve an exactly corresponding, but opposite, change in v/C, if the profit rate is to remain the same.\" The asymmetry is the point. Compositional change is a cheap way to move the profit rate: any change suffices. Compensation by composition is an expensive requirement: it demands an exact, quantitatively matching, opposite movement. Hence: \"This is possible only very exceptionally in the case of one and the same capital, or with two capitals in the same country.\" The worked example. Take: 80c + 20v + 20s; C = 100, s' = 100%, p' = 20% Now let wages fall so that the same number of workers can be had for 16v as previously with 20v. With conditions otherwise unchanged, 4v is set free, giving: 80c + 16v + 24s; C = 96, s' = 150%, p' = 25% If p' is still to be 20%, as before, the total capital must increase to 120, and hence the constant capital to 104: 104c + 16v + 24s; C = 120, s' = 150%, p' = 20% But this \"would be possible only if a change in the productivity of labour took place simultaneously with the fall in wages, and required this changed composition of capital; or alternatively, if the money value of the constant capital rose from 80 to 104 — in other words, a chance combination of conditions that only comes about in exceptional circumstances.\" Marx adds a clarification of the theoretical reach: \"In actual fact a change in s' which is not simultaneously a change in v, thus also giving rise to a change in v/C, is conceivable only under quite special conditions, i.e. in those branches of industry in which only fixed capital and labour are applied, and the object of labour is provided by nature.\" That is a real economic specification — extractive industries where raw material is not purchased — and it is a useful delimitation of the theorem. The international comparison. Then the decisive qualification that opens the way to Part Four of the volume: \"The position is different when comparing rates of profit in two countries. Here the same rate of profit expresses in most cases different rates of surplus-value.\" The point is that the composition variable is not a compensating movement within a national economy in the same way as across national economies. Two countries may share a common profit rate on the world market while exhibiting quite different rates of surplus-value and quite different compositions. This is the seed of Marx's theory of international profit-rate equalization and of unequal rates of exploitation across national capitals — a theme that will only be developed much later, in the fragment on foreign trade and in the analysis of the world market. The sweeping conclusion. \"It results from all these five cases, therefore, that a rising profit rate can correspond to a falling or a rising rate of surplus-value, a falling profit rate can correspond to a rising or a falling rate of surplus-value, and a rate of profit that remains the same can also correspond to a rising or a falling rate of surplus-value. We have already shown under heading I that a rising, falling or unchanged rate of profit can also correspond to a rate of surplus-value that remains the same. The rate of profit is thus determined by two major factors: the rate of surplus-value and the value composition of the capital.\" This is the chapter's climax in a single sentence. Everything before has been the demonstration; this is the statement of the theorem. 2.21 The Closing Synthesis: Two Determinants and Six Summary Propositions The final page consolidates the results, and — importantly — Marx now licenses the use of the percentage form for the summary, \"since it is immaterial here in which of the two portions of capital the change originates\" (i.e., for the equality/inequality propositions, only the ratio matters). The rates of profit of two different capitals, or of one and the same capital in two successive and different states, are EQUAL: Given the same percentage composition and the same rate of surplus-value. (Trivially: same s' , same v/C , hence same p' .) Given unequal percentage compositions and unequal rates of surplus-value, if the product s'v is the same in each case — that is, if the mass of surplus-value reckoned as a percentage of the total capital is the same; \"in other words, when the factors s' and v stand in inverse proportion to one another in the two cases.\" They are UNEQUAL: Given the same percentage composition, if the rates of surplus-value are unequal — in which case the profit rates stand in the same ratio as these rates of surplus-value. Given the same rate of surplus-value and different percentage compositions — in which case they stand in the same ratio as the variable portions of the capitals. Given different rates of surplus-value and different percentage compositions — in which case they stand in the same proportion as the products s'v , i.e. as the masses of surplus-value reckoned as a percentage of the total capital. These six propositions are the chapter's formal yield. They constitute a complete, exhaustive classification of the conditions under which two capitals bear equal or unequal rates of profit. Engels's final editorial note (footnote 10). The manuscript, we learn, \"also contains further and very detailed calculations on the mathematical difference between rate of surplus-value and rate of profit (s' − p'), a difference which has all kinds of interesting properties, and whose movement presents cases in which the two rates draw apart and cases in which they converge. These movements can also be represented by curves.\" Engels refrained from reproducing this material, \"since it is of little importance for the immediate aim of this book,\" but draws attention to it \"for those readers who might wish to pursue it further.\" This closing note is significant for two reasons: (a) it confirms the chapter is unfinished in its mathematical ambitions; (b) it shows that Marx himself was aware of the gap s' − p' as a measurable, curve-representable quantity — an analytical counterpart of the qualitative theorem that the two rates are systematically decoupled. The gap is the \"camouflage\" quantified. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles First, the rate of profit is a compound, not a simple index of exploitation. The chapter's central theorem is that p' = s' · (v/C) . This means that the rate of profit is the product of two analytically independent determinations: the intensity with which living labour is exploited (s') and the value-compositional weight carried by living labour in the total capital advanced (v/C). Because it is a product of two factors, its movement is systematically ambiguous: it can rise while exploitation falls, fall while exploitation rises, or hold constant while both change. Anyone who reasons from profit rates to exploitation intensities, or from wage levels to profit levels, is making an inference that the algebra does not license. This is the chapter's most general and most consequential result, and it is the source of almost every subsequent theoretical puzzle in Volume 3. Second, the decoupling is asymmetric and systemic, not accidental. Marx's crucial observation (§2.20) is that the \"same profit rate\" condition is cheap to satisfy with s' constant and expensive to satisfy with s' variable . Any change in composition whatsoever will move the profit rate when s' is fixed. But for a change in s' to leave the profit rate unchanged, an exactly matching, opposite compositional change is required. The practical implication is decisive: constant p' with widely divergent s' \"is possible only very exceptionally in the case of one and the same capital, or with two capitals in the same country\" — but is normal when comparing countries. This asymmetry expresses a real economic structure : the financial system's equalization of profit rates operates across national boundaries, so it is there, not within a single economy, that the divergence of the two rates becomes routine. Third, the \"organic\" distinction between constant and variable capital is not just quantitative but categorical. Constant capital matters only through its value ; its material form is irrelevant. Variable capital matters only through the living labour it commands , not through its own value. Therefore the two types of capital interact with the rate of profit in fundamentally different ways: changes in the value of c alter only the denominator; changes in the value of v alter the numerator and the denominator, and no linear mapping exists between changes in v and changes in s' when the wage rate moves (the \"index problem,\" §2.5). This is a structural distinction that no purely aggregative accounting framework can capture. Fourth, the laws governing the profit rate are bounded by the laws governing the conditions of labour. §2.14 establishes that there is \"always a limit to the variation of v\" beyond which s' cannot remain constant, and symmetrically a limit to the variation of c. The \"free\" variation of the ratio v/C is thus confined to a feasible region whose boundaries are set by the working day, intensity, and wages — themselves social and institutional magnitudes. The algebraic case enumeration therefore is not logically closed off from the sphere of class struggle; on the contrary, the boundaries of the case space are the boundaries of the struggle over conditions of labour. Fifth, the method is comparative-static and case-exhaustive — deliberately so. Marx states the license explicitly: \"The rate of profit is a function of several variables, and if we want to know how these variables act on the profit rate we must investigate in turn the individual effect of each, irrespective of whether an isolated effect of this kind is economically possible or not in the case of one and the same capital.\" The willingness to run \"economically impossible\" examples is not a sign of unrealism but of analytical decomposition: the total effect is the composition of partial effects, and the partial effects must be isolated first. This is the methodological tradition that will later be called comparative statics, and Marx's practice of it here is disciplined, systematic, and self-aware. Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications For the theory of the falling rate of profit. The chapter plants the seed explicitly. Reading the falling-v case (Case 1, Reading A) as \"simply the normal case in modern industry: rising productivity of labour, the operation of greater quantities of means of production by fewer workers,\" Marx writes: \"And in Part Three of this volume we shall see how this movement is necessarily bound up with a simultaneous fall in the rate of profit.\" The connection is now transparent: rising productivity raises c/v , lowers v/C , and hence, with s' unchanged or even raised , lowers p' . The chapter's algebra is the technical shell of the falling-profit-rate argument; Part Three will fill in the historical dynamics, the counteracting tendencies, and the crises. The \"release of capital\" category introduced in Case 2 will matter directly for those counteracting tendencies — because capital released from existing lines can be reinvested, raising the mass of profit even as the rate falls. For the theory of prices of production and the transformation problem. Chapter 3 establishes the base level of Volume 3's analysis: an individual capital's profit rate as a function of its own composition and its own rate of surplus-value. Part Two will then ask what happens when capitals of differing composition compete for investment and capital flows from low- to high-profit-rate branches, equalizing the rate. The result will be a general rate of profit, prices of production differing from values, and a systematic redistribution of surplus-value from labour-intensive to capital-intensive branches. The chapter is thus the point of departure for that redistribution — it identifies the determinants whose systematic dispersion across branches makes equalization both necessary and consequential. For the critique of Ricardo and of classical political economy. The chapter's one direct citation of Ricardo (§2.16) is telling. Ricardo's \"profits would be high or low exactly in proportion as wages would be low or high\" is shown to hold only in the special case of a constant composition of capital with a constant working day and constant intensity of labour. Since capital composition is systematically transformed by accumulation, the Ricardian proposition is not the general law. This is the analytic content of Marx's critique of Ricardo — not that Ricardo was careless, but that his framework could not distinguish the two rates, and could not see that their relationship is mediated by a third term (composition). The chapter's whole case-enumeration is, in effect, a demonstration of why a one-factor theory of profit cannot survive scrutiny. For the international comparison of profit rates and the theory of unequal development. The chapter's closing observation, that \"the same rate of profit expresses in most cases different rates of surplus-value\" as between countries, opens a research programme that Marx will only gesture toward in this volume. Its implication is enormous: the equalization of profit rates on world markets can coexist with systematically different rates of exploitation, and the composition variable is the hinge. This is the analytical foundation for later theories of unequal exchange, of imperial extraction, and of the divergence between high-wage, high-composition economies and low-wage, low-composition economies under conditions of global capital mobility. For the theory of crises and the release of capital. The distinction between cases where capital is \"tied up\" and where it is \"released\" (§2.10) is not a marginal accounting nicety. Released capital is the raw material of credit expansion, of speculative bubbles, and of the reallocation of capital across branches. In the crisis theory that follows in Part Five of Volume 3, the sudden release or sudden immobilization of capital — traceable to just the sort of compositional shifts that this chapter catalogues — will become the mechanical trigger of crises of over-accumulation. The chapter's apparently dry algebra is thus the vocabulary in which the dynamics of the cycle will be written. For the methodology of political economy. Three methodological lessons stand out. First, the chapter demonstrates the productive discipline of complete case enumeration : rather than settling for a few illustrative examples, Marx constructs the general formulas that cover every case and then demonstrates that the remaining candidate cases add nothing new. Second, it demonstrates the value of the dual reading — successive states of one capital and simultaneous distinct capitals are formally interchangeable, which licenses the comparative analysis of branches and countries without abandoning the temporal analysis of a single capital. Third, it demonstrates the limits of abstraction : the analysis is qualified throughout by reminders that turnover, price movements of constant capital, extra profits from above-average productivity, and the equalization of profit rates have all been bracketed off and must be re-imported to complete the theory. The chapter is deliberately incomplete, and aware of its incompleteness. Its value lies not in being a self-contained account of profit but in being a rigorous first transformation of surplus-value into profit — the indispensable foundation for everything that comes after. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 2 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 4"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 4", "title": "Chapter 4", "subtitle": "The Effect of the Turnover on the Rate of Profit", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 4/Chapter 4 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 7509, "read_time": "38 min read", "tags": ["Cost Price & Rate of Profit", "Turnover of Capital & Cycles"], "keywords": ["Effect of Turnover on the Rate of Profit", "Annual Profit Rate Acceleration through Velocity", "Variable Capital Turnover Multiplier", "Compensating Lower Surplus-Value by Rapid Turnovers", "Speed of Circulation Offsetting Constant Capital Drag"], "text": "Chapter 4 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 7,492 words ⏱️ ~37 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 4, “The Effect of the Turnover on the Rate of Profit,” is the point in Marx’s Capital , Volume 3, where the turnover analysis developed in Volume 2 is formally integrated into the theory of the profit rate. The chapter was written by Engels, as the footnote and Preface indicate, and it carries Engels’s characteristic combination of theoretical recapitulation, industrial-historical illustration, and numerical demonstration. Its central problem is precise: Volume 2 had already shown that the turnover time of capital affects the mass of surplus-value because capital cannot be simultaneously active in production in its entirety. A portion of capital always lies fallow—money capital, raw-material stocks, finished but unsold commodities, or outstanding debts not yet due—and this idle portion produces no surplus-value. The shorter the turnover time, the smaller the idle portion and the greater the mass of surplus-value appropriated, other conditions being equal. But the rate of profit is not the mass of surplus-value as such; it is the ratio of the mass of surplus-value produced to the total capital engaged in producing it. Therefore, any reduction in turnover time that raises the mass of surplus-value relative to the same total capital must also raise the rate of profit. This is the chapter’s core thesis. The intellectual objective is to show that turnover is not a secondary or merely technical matter. It is a distinct determinant of the profit rate, alongside the organic composition of capital, the rate of surplus-value, the length of the working day, and the level of wages. Engels seeks to present this effect “in its pure form” by holding all other circumstances equal, then to demonstrate the mechanism numerically, and finally to test it against a real industrial example. The chapter thus serves as a bridge between Volume 2’s theory of turnover and Volume 3’s theory of profit. It explains why the same rate of surplus-value and the same capital composition can yield different profit rates if capitals turn over at different speeds. It also explains why the formula for the profit rate must be corrected: the simple expression (p' = s' v/C) is valid only when the variable capital in the numerator is the same as the variable capital in the denominator. When variable capital turns over several times a year, the annual surplus-value is greater than the surplus-value of a single turnover, and the formula must become (p' = s'n v/C), where (n) is the number of turnovers of variable capital in a year and (s'n) is the annual rate of surplus-value. The chapter also has a broader critical and empirical dimension. Engels illustrates the reduction of production time through advances in metallurgy and chemical industry—Bessemer, Siemens, Gilchrist-Thomas, alizarin from coal-tar—and the reduction of circulation time through railways, steamships, telegraph cables, and the Suez Canal. He notes that these changes can raise the profit rate if they do not require a major increase in total capital investment, but that expensive new machinery can raise the composition of capital and counteract the effect. He then shows, through the example of a Manchester spinning mill, how a high annual rate of surplus-value can coexist with a very small proportion of variable capital in the total capital. Finally, he observes that statistical material on the social ratio of constant to variable capital is almost absent, with only the U.S. Census offering any data, and even that data is dubious because it relies on unchecked information from industrialists. In this way, the chapter links the abstract theory of turnover to the concrete history of capitalism, the accounting categories of the capitalist, and the ideological limits of official statistics. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Summary: Idle Capital, Active Capital, and the Turnover Constraint Engels begins by recalling the central result of Volume 2: the effect of turnover on the production of surplus-value, and consequently on profit, has already been discussed. The summary is compact but theoretically dense. The time required for turnover has the effect that the whole capital cannot be simultaneously employed in production. One part of this capital therefore always lies fallow, whether in the form of money capital, stocks of raw materials, finished but still unsold commodity capital, or outstanding debts that are not yet due for payment. This is not a minor qualification. It is the material basis of the turnover effect. Capital is not a homogeneous fluid that can be fully present in the production process at every instant. It must pass through the phases of money capital, productive capital, and commodity capital. While it is in the money form, or in the form of raw materials waiting to be used, or in the form of finished goods waiting to be sold, or in the form of debts waiting to be collected, it is not actively producing surplus-value. The capital that is in active production, active in the production and appropriation of surplus-value, is always reduced by this amount. The surplus-value that is produced and appropriated is reduced in the same proportion. The shorter the turnover time, the smaller is this idle portion of capital compared with the whole; the greater therefore is the surplus-value appropriated, other conditions being equal. This opening already introduces the crucial distinction between capital advanced and capital active. The total capital advanced is the denominator of the profit rate. But not all of it is active at any given moment. The rate of profit is therefore affected by how much of the advanced capital is actually functioning in the production and appropriation of surplus-value. A shorter turnover time does not change the total capital advanced, but it increases the proportion of that capital that is active. It is as if the same total capital were concentrated into a more effective working mass. Engels explicitly connects this to the rate of profit: since the rate of profit simply expresses the ratio of the mass of surplus-value produced to the total capital engaged in producing it, it is evident that any reduction of turnover time raises the rate of profit as well. The points made in Part Two of Volume 2 with respect to surplus-value apply equally here to profit and the rate of profit, and do not need to be repeated. There are simply a few key aspects to emphasize. This opening also contains an important methodological claim: the analysis of surplus-value and the analysis of profit are not separate theoretical realms. The same turnover mechanism that governs the mass of surplus-value also governs the rate of profit, because the rate of profit is a transformed expression of the same underlying surplus-value. The chapter therefore does not introduce a new theory of profit; it extends the turnover theory of Volume 2 into the profit-rate framework of Volume 3. The nuance here is that the rate of profit depends on the total capital advanced, not merely on the variable capital that directly produces surplus-value. Thus, a faster turnover can raise the rate of profit only insofar as it raises the annual mass of surplus-value relative to the total capital advanced. If faster turnover requires a larger total capital, the effect may be offset or reversed. This caveat is introduced immediately after the opening summary and is developed through the examples of industrial progress. Production Time, Industrial Progress, and the Counteracting Weight of Fixed Capital Engels next identifies the two component sections of turnover time: production time and circulation time. The main means whereby production time is reduced is an increase in the productivity of labour, which is commonly known as industrial progress. This is a crucial link between the technical conditions of production and the rate of profit. If productivity increases, the same capital can produce a given output in less time, or a larger output in the same time. In terms of turnover, the production period is shortened, so the capital returns to its starting point more quickly, and the number of turnovers in a year increases. If this does not also involve a major increase in the total capital investment, due to the installation of expensive machinery etc., and therefore a fall in the rate of profit as reckoned on the total capital, then this profit rate must rise. This conditional formulation is essential. Engels is not claiming that industrial progress always raises the profit rate. He is claiming that the production-time-reducing effect of industrial progress, considered in isolation, raises the profit rate, but that it may be accompanied by a counteracting rise in the composition of capital. He then gives concrete historical examples. The newly discovered methods of iron and steel preparation associated with Bessemer, Siemens, Gilchrist-Thomas and others shorten what were previously very protracted processes to a minimum. The preparation of alizarin dye from coal-tar gives the same result in a few weeks, and using apparatus that is already in use for coal-tar dyes, as previously took several years. The madder from which the dye was previously prepared needed a year to grow, and the roots were left to mature for several years after that before they were used. These examples are not mere curiosities. They show that production time is historically variable and can be drastically shortened by scientific and technical revolutions. In the case of alizarin, the new method not only shortens production time but also uses apparatus already employed in coal-tar dye production, which means that the additional capital investment may be relatively small. In such cases, the turnover effect on the profit rate is strongly positive. In the case of Bessemer and Siemens steel, the new methods may require large fixed capital investments, which could raise the organic composition of capital and lower the profit rate as reckoned on total capital. The net effect depends on the balance between the turnover acceleration and the increase in capital advanced. This is a nuance that runs through the chapter: the turnover effect is real and analytically distinct, but it is not the only force at work. The rate of profit is a complex result of several determinants. Engels explicitly says that if the reduction of production time “does not also involve a major increase in the total capital investment,” then the profit rate must rise. The phrase “does not also” signals the counteracting tendency. Later, in the Manchester example, he will show a case where the total capital is dominated by fixed capital, and the variable capital is only a small fraction, yet the profit rate is high because the variable capital turns over many times. But the high rate is also explained by “extremely favourable temporary conditions.” So the chapter consistently avoids a one-sided technological determinism. It presents turnover acceleration as a powerful but conditional force. Circulation Time, Communications, and the Shrinking World Market The main means of cutting circulation time has been improved communications. Engels describes a revolution in transport and communication over the last fifty years that is comparable only with the industrial revolution of the second half of the previous century. On land the Macadamized road has been replaced by the railway, while at sea the slow and irregular sailing ship has been driven into the background by the rapid and regular steamer line; the whole earth has been girded by telegraph cables. It was the Suez Canal that really opened the Far East and Australia to the steamer. The circulation time for a shipment of goods to the Far East, which in 1847 was at least twelve months, has now been more or less reduced to as many weeks. This is a dramatic illustration of how circulation time, no less than production time, is subject to historical transformation. The turnover time of capital is not a natural constant; it is a social and technological variable. Engels then makes a striking observation about crisis. The two major foci of crisis between 1825 and 1857, America and India, have been brought 70 to 90 per cent closer to the industrial countries of Europe by this revolution in the means of commerce, and have lost in this way a good deal of their explosive potential. The turnover time of world trade as a whole has been reduced to the same extent, and the efficacy of the capital involved in it has been increased two or three times and more. It is evident that this cannot but have had its effect on the profit rate. This passage connects the turnover analysis to the geography of capitalist crisis. Improved communications shorten circulation time, increase the efficacy of capital, and thereby tend to raise the profit rate. At the same time, they integrate distant markets more tightly into the European industrial system, reducing the time lags and information gaps that had previously made those markets explosive foci of crisis. This is a nuanced claim: the same revolution in communications that raises the profit rate also alters the spatial and temporal pattern of crisis. It does not abolish crisis; it changes its form and location. The passage also shows Engels’s historical materialism: the means of communication are not neutral infrastructure but part of the material conditions of capital circulation and profitability. The theoretical point is that circulation time is a deduction from production time. The longer the circulation time, the longer the capital is tied up in the commodity or money form, and the smaller the proportion of total capital that is active in production. By reducing circulation time, improved communications allow the same capital to be turned over more frequently, which increases the annual mass of surplus-value relative to the capital advanced. This is why the revolution in transport and communication “cannot but have had its effect on the profit rate.” The effect may be partially offset by the large fixed capital investments required for railways, steamships, and telegraphs, but the turnover acceleration itself is a powerful force. The Pure Case: Equal Conditions and the Inverse Relation Between Turnover and Profit Rate Engels now moves from historical illustration to theoretical proof. In order to present the effect of the turnover of the total capital on the profit rate in its pure form, we must assume that all other circumstances are equal for the two capitals we are comparing. The percentage composition in particular must be taken as the same, as well as the rate of surplus-value and the length of the working day. This is the method of isolation: vary only the turnover time, hold everything else constant, and observe the effect on the profit rate. He then sets up a comparison. Let us take a capital A with a composition of 80c + 20v = 100C, a rate of surplus-value of 100 per cent, and a twice-yearly turnover. Its annual product is then 160c + 40v + 40s. But for the purposes of the profit rate we calculate this 40s not on the capital value of 200 turned over, but rather on the capital value of 100 that was advanced, and we thus get p' = 40 per cent. Let us compare this with a capital B = 160c + 40v = 200C, with the same rate of surplus-value, but turning over only once in the year. Its annual product is then 160c + 40v + 40s, the same as above. This time however the 40s has to be calculated on a capital advance of 200, which results in a profit rate of 20 per cent, i.e. only half the rate for A. The result is therefore that for capitals of the same percentage composition, with the same rate of surplus-value and the same working day, the profit rates of two capitals vary inversely as their turnover times. If either the composition or the rate of surplus-value or the working day or the wage of labour is not the same in the two cases to be compared, further differences in the rate of profit are also brought about, but these are independent of the turnover, and do not concern us here; they have already been discussed in Chapter 3. This is the analytical heart of the chapter. The comparison shows that two capitals can produce the same annual product and the same annual mass of surplus-value, yet have different profit rates if their turnover times differ. Capital A advances only 100, but turns it over twice, producing an annual surplus-value of 40 on an advance of 100, giving 40 per cent. Capital B advances 200, turns it over once, and produces the same annual surplus-value of 40 on an advance of 200, giving 20 per cent. The difference is entirely due to the denominator: the total capital advanced. The faster turnover allows A to produce the same surplus-value with half the advanced capital. Conversely, if A and B had the same advanced capital but different turnover times, A would produce twice the annual surplus-value and thus twice the profit rate. The phrase “vary inversely as their turnover times” means that if turnover time doubles, the profit rate is halved, other things equal. This is a precise, testable proposition within the abstract model. The caveat is equally important. If composition, rate of surplus-value, working day, or wages differ, those differences also affect the profit rate, but they are independent of turnover. This prevents the turnover effect from being confused with other determinants. It also means that in empirical reality, the observed profit rate is a composite result. One cannot infer the turnover effect directly from observed profit rates unless one controls for composition, exploitation rate, working day, and wages. This methodological caution is a recurring feature of Marx’s and Engels’s procedure. Variable Capital's Enhanced Efficacy and the Annual Rate of Surplus-Value The direct effect of the abbreviated turnover time on the production of surplus-value, and therefore also on profit, consists in the increased effectiveness which this gives to the variable portion of capital. This is the mechanism behind the inverse relation. Engels refers back to Volume 2, Chapter 16, “The Turnover of Variable Capital.” There it was seen how a variable capital of 500 which turns over ten times in the year appropriates just as much surplus-value in this period as a variable capital of 5,000, with the same rate of surplus-value and the same wages, which turns over only once in the year. This comparison is crucial. The variable capital of 500, turning ten times, purchases labour-power ten times in the year. Each time, it produces surplus-value at the given rate. The total annual surplus-value is therefore ten times the surplus-value of one turnover. The variable capital of 5,000, turning once, purchases labour-power once in the year, and produces only the surplus-value of one turnover. The result is the same annual surplus-value, but the advanced variable capital is ten times larger in the second case. If the total capital advanced is also larger in the second case, the rate of profit will be lower. If the total capital advanced is the same, the rate of profit will be higher in the first case. This is the key to the entire chapter: the turnover of variable capital affects the annual rate of surplus-value. The rate of surplus-value in a single turnover period is (s' = s/v), where (s) is the surplus-value produced in that period and (v) is the variable capital advanced for that period. But the annual mass of surplus-value is (S = s n = s' v n), where (n) is the number of turnovers in the year. The annual rate of surplus-value is (S' = S/v = s' n). Thus, a variable capital of 500 with (s' = 100\\%) and (n = 10) produces an annual surplus-value of 5,000, and an annual rate of surplus-value of 1,000 per cent. The same variable capital of 5,000 with (n = 1) produces the same annual surplus-value of 5,000, but an annual rate of surplus-value of only 100 per cent. The difference is entirely due to turnover. This distinction between the simple rate of surplus-value and the annual rate of surplus-value is essential for the profit rate. The profit rate is an annual magnitude. It compares the surplus-value produced in a year to the total capital advanced. Therefore, it must use the annual rate of surplus-value, not the simple rate. If one uses the simple rate, one will understate the profit rate whenever variable capital turns over more than once a year. This is why Engels insists that the formula for the annual rate of profit must be (p' = s'n v/C). The (n) converts the simple rate of surplus-value into the annual rate, or equivalently converts the advanced variable capital into the annual variable capital laid out in wages. The increased effectiveness of variable capital is not a mysterious property; it is simply the consequence of the same capital being used repeatedly to purchase labour-power within the year. The Three Capitals and the Algebra of Annual Profit Rate Engels now constructs three numerical examples to illustrate the relationship between turnover, annual surplus-value, and the profit rate. Let us take a capital I, consisting of 10,000 fixed capital, its annual depreciation being 10 per cent = 1,000, 500 circulating constant capital, and 500 variable capital. With a rate of surplus-value of 100 per cent, the variable capital turns over ten times in the year. For the sake of simplicity we shall assume in all the following examples that the circulating constant capital turns over in the same period as the variable, which will generally be the case in practice. The product of such a turnover period will then be: 100c (depreciation) + 500c + 500v + 500s = 1,600. And the product of the whole year, with ten turnovers: 1,000c (depreciation) + 5,000c + 5,000v + 5,000s = 16,000. C = 11,000, s = 5,000, p' = 5,000/11,000 = 45 5/11 per cent. Capital II: fixed capital 9,000 with annual depreciation 1,000, circulating constant capital 1,000, variable capital 1,000, rate of surplus-value 100 per cent, turnovers of variable capital five per year. The product of one of these turnover periods of the variable capital will then be: 200c (depreciation) + 1,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s = 3,200. And the total annual product over five turnovers: 1,000c (depreciation) + 5,000c + 5,000v + 5,000s = 16,000. C = 11,000, s = 5,000, p' = 5,000/11,000 = 45 5/11 per cent. Capital III: no fixed capital, but simply 6,000 circulating constant capital and 5,000 variable capital. It turns over once a year, say, with a rate of surplus-value of 100 per cent. The total annual product is then: 6,000c + 5,000v + 5,000s = 16,000. C = 11,000, s = 5,000, p' = 45 5/11 per cent. In all three cases, therefore, we have the same annual mass of surplus-value = 5,000, and since the total capital is the same in all these cases, i.e. 11,000, we have the same profit rate of 45 5/11 per cent. This is a remarkable result. Three capitals with very different compositions of fixed and circulating capital, and very different turnover rates of variable capital, can have the same profit rate. The reason is that the annual variable capital set in motion is the same in all three cases: in Capital I, 500 × 10 = 5,000; in Capital II, 1,000 × 5 = 5,000; in Capital III, 5,000 × 1 = 5,000. Since the rate of surplus-value is 100 per cent, the annual surplus-value is 5,000 in each case. And since the total capital advanced is 11,000 in each case, the profit rate is the same. This illustrates the general principle that what matters for the annual surplus-value is the product (v n), the variable capital actually laid out in wages over the year, not the variable capital advanced at any single moment. But Engels immediately shows what happens when turnover changes while the other elements remain constant. If in the case of the above capital I there took place not ten but only five turnovers of its variable portion, the matter would be different. The product of one turnover would then be: 200c (depreciation) + 500c + 500v + 500s = 1,700; or the annual product: 1,000c (depreciation) + 2,500c + 2,500v + 2,500s = 8,500; C = 11,000, s = 2,500, p' = 2,500/11,000 = 22 8/11 per cent. The profit rate has now fallen by half, as the turnover time has doubled. This is the inverse relation in action. The total capital advanced is unchanged at 11,000. The composition of capital is unchanged. The rate of surplus-value is unchanged. Only the number of turnovers has fallen from ten to five. As a result, the annual surplus-value has fallen from 5,000 to 2,500, and the profit rate has fallen from 45 5/11 per cent to 22 8/11 per cent. This is a clean demonstration of the turnover effect. The general formula that emerges is (S = sn) and (S' = s'n). If we call the surplus-value or profit annually appropriated (S), the surplus-value appropriated in one turnover period (s), and the number of turnovers made by the variable capital in a year (n), then (S = sn). The annual rate of surplus-value is (S' = s'n). This formula is already set out in Volume 2, Chapter 16, I. It is the bridge between the turnover of variable capital and the annual profit rate. The profit rate then becomes (p' = S/C = s'n v/C), where (C = c + v) is the total capital advanced. This formula is the corrected form of the profit-rate equation. It shows that the profit rate depends on the rate of surplus-value, the number of turnovers, the proportion of variable capital in the total capital, and the total capital itself. The turnover effect is captured by (n). The Corrected Formula: Why the v in the Numerator Must Equal the v in the Denominator Engels now turns to a subtle algebraic point that is essential for avoiding error. The formula for the profit rate (p' = s' v/C = s' v/(c+v)) is correct only if the (v) in the numerator is the same as that in the denominator. The (v) in the denominator is the entire part of the total capital that is spent on average as variable capital, on wages. The (v) in the numerator is initially determined simply by the fact that a certain quantity of surplus-value = (s) has been produced and appropriated by it, related to it by the rate of surplus-value (s'), which equals (s/v). It is only in this way that the equation (p' = s/(c+v)) was transformed into the equation (p' = s' v/(c+v)). The (v) in the numerator can now be more accurately defined by the condition that it must be equal to the (v) in the denominator, i.e. to the entire variable part of the capital (C). In other words, the equation (p' = s/C) can be transformed into (p' = s' v/C) without risk of error only if (s) stands for the surplus-value produced in a single turnover period of the variable capital. If (s) comprises only a part of this surplus-value, (s = s'v) is still correct, but this (v) is now smaller than the (v) in (C = c + v), as it is smaller than the whole of the variable capital that is laid out on wages. But if (s) comprises more than the surplus-value of one turnover of (v), a part of this (v) or even the whole of it functions twice, firstly in the first turnover, then in the second or further turnovers; the (v) that produces surplus-value and is the sum of all wages paid is thus greater than the (v) in (c + v), and the calculation is false. This is a dense but crucial passage. Let us unpack it. The profit rate is defined as (p' = S/C), where (S) is the annual surplus-value and (C) is the total capital advanced. The rate of surplus-value is (s' = S/v), where (v) is the variable capital. If we write (S = s' v), then (p' = s' v/C). This is the familiar formula. But here is the catch: which (v)? The (v) in the denominator (C = c + v) is the variable capital advanced, the amount of money the capitalist must have on hand to pay wages. The (v) in the numerator, if (S = s' v), must be the variable capital that actually produced the annual surplus-value. If variable capital turns over more than once a year, the same advanced (v) is used repeatedly to pay wages. The total wages paid over the year are (v n), not (v). The annual surplus-value is (S = s' v n). Therefore, if we use the advanced (v) in the numerator, we understate the annual surplus-value. To correct the formula, we must either replace (s') with the annual rate of surplus-value (S' = s' n), or replace the advanced (v) with the annual variable capital (v n). Thus, the correct formula for the annual rate of profit is (p' = s' n v/C = S' v/C). Engels states this explicitly: “In order that the formula for the annual rate of profit may be completely correct, we must replace the simple rate of surplus-value with the annual rate, (S') or (s'n) in place of (s'). In other words, we must multiply (s'), the rate of surplus-value—or else multiply the (v), the variable capital (v) contained in (C)—by (n), the number of turnovers that this variable capital makes in a year, and we then obtain (p' = s'n v/C), the formula for calculating the annual rate of profit.” This is the algebraic correction that makes the turnover effect explicit. Without the (n), the formula would be valid only for a single turnover, or for the special case where (n = 1). With the (n), it becomes a general formula for the annual rate of profit. This is why the chapter is necessary: it shows that the simple profit-rate formula must be revised when turnover is taken into account. The passage also has an epistemological dimension. The capitalist himself does not know in most cases how much variable capital he employs in his business. We have already seen in Chapter 8 of Volume 2, and we shall now see further, that the only distinction within his capital that impresses itself on the capitalist as fundamental is the distinction between fixed and circulating capital. From the same till that contains the part of his circulating capital that exists in his hands in the money form, in so far as this is not placed in the bank, he fetches both money for wages and money for raw and ancillary materials, and enters both of these in the same cash account. Even if he were to keep a separate record for wages paid, this would simply indicate the total sum paid at the end of the year, i.e. (v n), and not the variable capital (v) itself. In order to arrive at this sum he would have to make a special calculation. This is a profound point about capitalist accounting. The capitalist’s practical categories obscure the distinction between constant and variable capital. He sees fixed and circulating capital, not constant and variable capital. He sees total wages paid, not the advanced variable capital that produces surplus-value. Therefore, he cannot easily calculate the annual rate of surplus-value or the true profit rate in Marx’s sense. The turnover effect is hidden from him by the very structure of his bookkeeping. The Manchester Spinning Mill: A Concrete Calculation Engels now provides a concrete example to test the formula. Let us take the spinning mill described in Volume 1 [Chapter 9, I, pp. 327–8], with its 10,000 spindles, and assume that the data given for one week in April 1871 are the same for the whole year. The fixed capital in the form of machinery was £10,000. The circulating capital was not given; we shall take it to be £2,500, a fairly high figure, but one that is justified by the assumption we must constantly make at this stage, that there are no credit operations, i.e. no permanent or temporary use of other people’s capital. The week’s product was composed, as far as its value was concerned, of £20 for depreciation of machinery, £358 advance of circulating constant capital (rent £6, cotton £342, coal, gas and oil £10), £52 laid out as variable capital on wages, and £80 surplus-value, i.e. 20c (depreciation) + 358c + 52v + 80s = 510. The weekly advance of circulating capital was therefore 358c + 52v = 410, and its percentage composition 87.3c + 12.7v. Calculated on the whole circulating capital of £2,500, this gives a constant capital of £2,182 and a variable capital of £318. Since the total outlay on wages for the whole year comes to 52 times £52, i.e. £2,704, the upshot is that the variable capital of £318 has turned over almost exactly 8½ times in the course of the year. The rate of surplus-value is 80/52 = 153 11/13 per cent. From these elements we can calculate the rate of profit by using the formula (p' = s'n v/C), with (s' = 153 11/13), (n = 8½), (v = 318), (C = 12,500). The result is that (p' = 153 11/13 × 8½ × 318/12,500 = 33.27) per cent. We can test this by using the simple formula (p' = S/C). The total surplus-value or profit over the whole year amounts to £80 × 52 = £4,160, and this divided by the total capital of £12,500 gives 33.28 per cent, pretty well the same figure as above. This is an abnormally high rate of profit, which can only be explained by extremely favourable temporary conditions (very cheap cotton prices combined with very high prices for yarn) and would certainly not have prevailed for a whole year in actual fact. This example is rich in theoretical implications. First, it shows the formula in action. The weekly rate of surplus-value is 153 11/13 per cent. The variable capital of £318 turns over 8½ times a year. The annual rate of surplus-value is therefore (153 11/13 × 8½ = 1,307 9/13) per cent. This is an enormous figure. It means that the same £318 of variable capital is used to purchase labour-power more than eight times in the year, and each time it produces surplus-value at a high rate. The annual surplus-value of £4,160 is the result. When divided by the total capital of £12,500, it yields a profit rate of about 33 per cent. The simple formula (p' = S/C) gives the same result, which confirms the corrected formula. The example also shows the difference between the annual rate of surplus-value and the simple rate. The simple rate is 153 11/13 per cent; the annual rate is over 1,300 per cent. This is why Engels says that if a “worthy” was shocked by the enormous annual rate of surplus-value of 1,000 per cent given in one of the examples in Volume 2, he can console himself with the actual fact of an annual rate of surplus-value of more than 1,300 per cent taken from a practical example in Manchester. In periods of greatest prosperity, such a rate is by no means rare. Second, the example reveals the actual composition of capital in modern large-scale industry. The total capital is divided into £12,182 constant and £318 variable, making £12,500 altogether. In percentages, 97½c + 2½v = 100C. Only a fortieth part of the total is needed for the payment of wages, though this serves more than eight times in the course of a year. This is a striking illustration of the small proportion of variable capital in advanced capitalist industry. The source of surplus-value is a tiny fraction of the total capital, yet it is the only fraction that produces surplus-value. The large constant capital—machinery, raw materials, etc.—merely transfers its value to the product. The variable capital, though small, is the source of profit. The high turnover of variable capital is what makes it possible for this small fraction to produce a substantial annual surplus-value. This is a powerful demonstration of the theoretical point that the rate of profit depends on the annual rate of surplus-value, not merely on the simple rate. Third, the example is qualified by its historical context. The 33.27 per cent profit rate is abnormally high. It can only be explained by extremely favourable temporary conditions: very cheap cotton prices combined with very high prices for yarn. Engels explicitly says that it would certainly not have prevailed for a whole year in actual fact. This caveat prevents the example from being taken as a universal law. It is an illustration of a possibility, not a typical case. The high annual rate of surplus-value is possible in periods of greatest prosperity, but such periods are exceptional. This historical specificity is part of Engels’s method: abstract theory must be tested against concrete data, but the data must be interpreted in light of temporary conditions and institutional context. The assumption of no credit operations is also a simplifying abstraction. In reality, credit can shorten turnover times and alter the relationships, but Engels abstracts from it to isolate the turnover effect. The Social Composition of Capital and the Statistical Lacuna Finally, Engels draws out a broader implication for social statistics. Since there are certainly only a few capitalists who make calculations of such a kind about their businesses, statistical material is almost completely absent on the ratio of the constant part of the total social capital to the variable part. Only the U.S. Census gives what is possible under present-day conditions, the sum of the wages paid in each branch of business and the profits made. Dubious as these data are, owing to the way they rely on the unchecked information of the industrialists themselves, they are none the less extremely valuable and the only data that we have on the subject. In Europe we are far too kind-hearted to expect such revelations on the part of our great industrialists. This passage is a critique of official statistics and a comment on the limits of empirical knowledge under capitalism. The ratio of constant to variable capital is central to Marx’s theory of the profit rate and the tendency of the rate of profit to fall. But capitalists do not publish the necessary data. Their accounting categories do not distinguish constant from variable capital. They report wages and profits, but not the advanced variable capital, the turnover rate, or the organic composition. The U.S. Census is the only source that even attempts to provide wages and profits by branch, but it relies on self-reported data from industrialists, which is likely biased or incomplete. Engels describes this as “dubious” but “extremely valuable” because it is the only data available. The sarcastic remark about Europe being “far too kind-hearted” to expect such revelations is a sharp critique of the secrecy and ideological self-presentation of capitalist enterprise. It also reinforces the earlier point that the capitalist’s practical categories obscure the source of surplus-value. The statistical lacuna is not accidental; it is a product of the social relations of production and the class interests of capitalists. This conclusion to the chapter is not a mere appendix. It shows that the theoretical analysis of turnover and profit rate has political and epistemological consequences. If the key variable—the ratio of constant to variable capital—is hidden from statistical view, then the public debate about profits, wages, and inequality is necessarily distorted. The capitalist can present profit as a return on total capital, a reward for enterprise and risk, while concealing the fact that profit arises from the exploitation of variable capital, which is a small fraction of total capital and turns over many times. The annual rate of surplus-value can exceed 1,300 per cent, yet the capitalist’s books show only a 33 per cent profit rate on total capital. This discrepancy is not a mathematical trick; it is the real appearance of the capitalist process. The chapter thus ends by pointing beyond political economy to a critique of capitalist accounting and statistics. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental lesson of Chapter 4 is that turnover is a determinant of the rate of profit in its own right. The rate of profit is not simply a function of the rate of surplus-value and the organic composition of capital. It also depends on how quickly capital circulates. A faster turnover allows the same advanced variable capital to purchase labour-power more times in a year, producing more annual surplus-value. Because the profit rate is an annual ratio of surplus-value to total capital advanced, this turnover acceleration raises the profit rate, other things equal. The precise formula is (p' = s'n v/C), where (s') is the rate of surplus-value, (n) is the number of turnovers of variable capital per year, (v) is the variable capital advanced, and (C) is the total capital advanced. Equivalently, (p' = S/C), where (S = s' v n) is the annual surplus-value. The turnover effect is captured by the annual rate of surplus-value (S' = s'n). This is the chapter’s central analytical contribution. The chapter also reveals a series of systemic laws and essential takeaways. First, capital advanced is not the same as capital active. The total capital advanced includes capital that lies fallow in money, raw materials, unsold goods, or debts. The shorter the turnover time, the smaller the fallow portion and the greater the active portion. This is why turnover affects surplus-value production. Second, the variable capital is the only part of capital that produces surplus-value, but its effectiveness depends on its turnover. A small variable capital turning over many times can produce as much surplus-value as a large variable capital turning over once. This is why the annual rate of surplus-value can be enormous even when the profit rate on total capital is moderate. Third, the profit rate is a composite result of several forces: turnover, composition, exploitation rate, working day, and wages. The turnover effect can be offset by a rise in the composition of capital, especially when production-time reduction requires expensive fixed capital. Fourth, the history of capitalism is marked by continuous revolutions in production time and circulation time. Metallurgy, chemistry, railways, steamships, telegraphs, and canals have dramatically shortened turnover times, increasing the efficacy of capital and altering the geography of crisis. Fifth, capitalist accounting and official statistics obscure the variable capital and the annual rate of surplus-value. The capitalist sees fixed and circulating capital, wages and profits, but not the source of surplus-value. This obscures the exploitation at the heart of the system. The broader practical and theoretical implications are significant. For the theory of the profit rate, the chapter demonstrates that the tendency of the rate of profit to fall cannot be analyzed without considering counteracting forces such as turnover acceleration. A rise in the organic composition of capital may be partially offset by faster turnover, especially through improved transportation and communication. Conversely, a slowdown in turnover—due to war, blockade, financial crisis, or supply-chain disruption—can depress the profit rate independently of changes in exploitation or composition. For crisis theory, the chapter suggests that the spatial and temporal integration of markets through transport and communication can reduce the explosive potential of certain crisis foci, even as it increases the overall efficacy of capital. This does not abolish crisis; it changes its form. For the critique of political economy, the chapter shows that the capitalist’s own categories are inadequate for understanding the system. The distinction between fixed and circulating capital, which dominates business accounting, conceals the distinction between constant and variable capital, which is essential for understanding surplus-value. The absence of statistical data on the social composition of capital is not a neutral gap; it is a consequence of class relations and the ideological presentation of profit as a return on total capital. Finally, the chapter has a methodological lesson. Engels isolates the turnover effect by assuming equal composition, equal rate of surplus-value, equal working day, and equal wages. He then demonstrates the effect with numerical examples, tests it against a real industrial case, and qualifies the result with historical conditions. This is a model of dialectical analysis: abstract isolation of a single causal force, concrete numerical illustration, empirical testing, and historical qualification. The result is not a simple law but a complex, conditional tendency. The rate of profit rises when turnover accelerates, provided that the acceleration does not require a disproportionate increase in total capital. The annual rate of surplus-value can be extremely high, yet the profit rate on total capital can be moderate. The capitalist system hides this reality behind its accounting categories and statistical silences. Chapter 4 thus closes the loop between Volume 2’s analysis of turnover and Volume 3’s analysis of profit, showing that time itself—the time of production and circulation—is a fundamental dimension of capitalist profitability. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 3 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 5"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 5", "title": "Chapter 5", "subtitle": "Economy in the Employment of Constant Capital", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 5/Chapter 5 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 18408, "read_time": "92 min read", "tags": ["Cost Price & Rate of Profit", "Machinery & Technology", "Division of Labour & Manufacture"], "keywords": ["Economy in the Employment of Constant Capital", "Waste Reduction and Recycling in Large-Scale Industry", "Dangerous Conditions for Workers to Save Capital Costs", "Collective Economies in Ventilation, Safety, and Steam Power", "Sacrifice of Worker Lives on the Altar of Cost Reduction"], "text": "Chapter 5 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 18,395 words ⏱️ ~92 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER ANALYSIS: CHAPTER 5 — \"ECONOMY IN THE USE OF CONSTANT CAPITAL\" 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem and Intellectual Objective Chapter 5 of Capital , Volume 3, occupies a deceptively modest position in Marx's architecture. It is the fifth chapter of Part One (\"The Transformation of Surplus-Value into Profit\"), and it sits between the earlier, more formal chapters that establish the categories of cost-price, profit, and the rate of profit, and the later, more volatile chapters on the fluctuations of raw material prices and the turnover of capital. Its apparent subject—\"economy in the use of constant capital\"—is narrow enough to sound like a technical footnote. It is anything but. The chapter's core thesis is this: the rate of profit can be raised not only by increasing the extraction of surplus-value from living labour, but also by cheapening or economizing on the constant capital (c) that living labour sets in motion. Because the rate of profit takes the form p' = s / (c + v), any reduction in the value of the denominator—specifically in c—mechanically raises the rate of profit even if the numerator (surplus-value) and its rate (s') remain constant. Marx's intellectual objective is to demonstrate that this \"economy\" in constant capital, far from being a mere technical accident of engineering or management, is a systematically produced social phenomenon —one whose ultimate source is the social, cooperative, and collective character of labour under large-scale production, but whose benefits are privately appropriated by capital and whose costs are transferred onto the bodies, health, and lives of the workers. The Dialectical Structure of the Argument The chapter unfolds through a striking twofold movement. Marx first establishes a positive analytical point: economies in constant capital are real, they do raise the rate of profit, and they arise from three great sources—(1) the common, collective consumption of means of production by the combined worker; (2) the rising productivity of labour in the branches that produce means of production for other branches; and (3) the recovery and re-use of the refuse and waste products of production and consumption. Each of these sources is, at bottom, a manifestation of the social character of labour —of cooperation, the social division of labour, and the accumulated intellectual labour of science and invention. But this positive analysis is immediately and deliberately inverted. Marx shows that the very same social character of labour that generates these economies also generates their antithesis: the squandering of human life and health. The capitalist mode of production \"counts the squandering of the life and health of the worker, and the depression of his conditions of existence, as itself an economy in the use of constant capital.\" The chapter thus dramatizes a central Marxian theme: the contradictory, antagonistic character of capitalist production, in which the same social forces that develop the productive forces of humanity also mutilate the individuals who embody them. The Situation of the Chapter Within the Broader Framework The chapter is a crucial hinge in Volume 3's argument. Earlier chapters have established that profit is the form that surplus-value assumes when it is related to the total capital advanced, not merely to the variable capital. Chapter 5 then deepens this by showing that the rate of profit is sensitive to changes on both sides of the ratio. Since the tendency of the rate of profit to fall (developed in later chapters) is grounded in a rising organic composition of capital (c/v), Chapter 5 provides an essential countervailing analysis: it catalogues the forces that cheapen c and thereby offset, retard, or partially counteract that tendency. The \"economy in constant capital\" is thus one of the most important counteracting influences on the falling profit rate. The chapter also serves as the empirical and moral centerpiece of the \"savings\" analysis. It documents, in gruelling and exhaustive detail drawn from the Reports of the Inspectors of Factories and the Reports on Public Health , the concrete human cost of the \"economies\" that capital celebrates as efficiency. And it belongs to a broader philosophical argument about fetishism : the way the social, cooperative power of labour comes to appear as an inherent property of capital itself. The chapter thus connects the most technical questions of political economy (what determines the rate of profit?) with the most visceral questions of human suffering (why do workers die in factories and mines?). In sum, Chapter 5 is a rigorous demonstration that the \"economy\" of the capitalist mode of production is simultaneously the economy of the profit rate and the diseconomy of human life—two moments of one and the same social relation. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 Opening Theorems: Working-Day Extension, Fixed Capital, and the Profit Rate Marx opens the chapter not with economies in constant capital at all, but with a closely related and analytically priorthe case of the absolute extension of the working day. The scenario is precise and deliberately simplified: variable capital (v) remains the same, the same number of workers are employed, and the nominal wage is unchanged. All that changes is the length of the working day. Marx's first claim is that an increase in absolute surplus-value under these conditions causes \"a relative fall in the value of constant capital compared with the total capital and the variable capital, and thus raises the rate of profit, quite apart from the growth in the mass of surplus-value and a possibly rising rate of surplus-value.\" The mechanical logic here is worth unpacking carefully, because Marx deploys the ratio p' = s / (c + v). If the mass of surplus-value s rises while c and v are held constant, the numerator grows and the denominator stays fixed—so p' rises. But Marx is making a subtler point. Even setting aside the growth in s, the relative proportion of c to the total capital (c + v) falls, because the same physical stock of fixed capital now serves a longer period of valorization. The denominator, in effect, becomes \"cheaper\" relative to the surplus it helps to produce. The key physical intuition is that the volume of fixed capital—factory buildings, machinery, and so on—remains the same whether work continues for sixteen hours or for twelve. This is the crucial point. Fixed capital is a lump : it does not need to be doubled when the working day is lengthened. As Marx puts it, \"The extension of the working day requires no new expenditure on this, the most expensive portion of the constant capital.\" The parenthetical remark \"(It is immaterial here whether overtime is paid or not)\" pre-empts the objection that the capitalist might have to pay for the additional hours; the point is structural, not merely about wages. Marx then advances a second, temporal argument. Because value is realized through turnover, lengthening the working day means that \"the value of the fixed capital is now reproduced in a shorter series of turnover periods, and the time for which it has to be advanced in order to make a certain profit is reduced.\" This is the theory of turnover from Volume 2 applied to the profit rate. The capitalist advances the entire value of the machinery up front; the faster that value is recovered through the sale of output, the more quickly the capital is \"freed\" and the higher the annual rate of profit. A machine that turns over in two years rather than three is a machine whose owner recaptures his outlay a third sooner. Hence Marx's conclusion: \"The lengthening of the working day thus raises profits even if overtime is paid, and up to a certain point this is true even if overtime is paid at a higher rate than normal working hours.\" The qualification \"up to a certain point\" signals that there is a limit—if the overtime premium is high enough, the gains are eroded—but within a significant range, prolonged hours enrich the capitalist regardless. Marx draws the historical inference: \"The ever-growing need to increase fixed capital in the modern industrial system was therefore a major stimulus for profit-mad capitalists to prolong the working day.\" The footnote drives the point home with a quotation from the Reports of the Inspectors of Factories (31 October 1858, p. 8): \"Since in all factories there is a very large amount of fixed capital in buildings and machinery, the greater the number of hours that machinery can be kept at work the greater will be the return.\" The technical fact of fixed capital becomes a coercive imperative. 2.2 The Compensating Counter-Movement: When the Working Day is Constant Marx immediately introduces the counter-case: what happens when the working day is fixed ? Here the capitalist cannot simply squeeze more hours out of the same plant. Marx enumerates two alternative strategies for expanding surplus-value, and shows that each one reinstates the growth of constant capital and thereby partially cancels the benefit. The first strategy is to increase the number of workers—and with them \"to a certain degree, the amount of fixed capital—buildings, machinery, etc.—so as to exploit a greater mass of labour.\" (Marx brackets the possibility of extracting more surplus by depressing wages below their normal level: \"for we ignore here any deductions from wages, or depression of wages below their normal level.\") More workers require more plant, more premises, more power—so c rises. The second strategy is to increase the intensity of labour, raise productivity, or otherwise produce more relative surplus-value. This requires, in branches using raw materials, \"the mass of the circulating part of constant capital will have to grow... since more raw materials, etc. are worked up in the given space of time.\" Moreover, \"the amount of machinery set in motion by the same number of workers will have to grow, and this too is a part of constant capital.\" The dialectical consequence is stated with precision: \"A growth in surplus-value is therefore accompanied by a growth in constant capital, and the growing exploitation of labour by an increase in the price paid for the conditions of production by means of which labour is exploited, i.e. by greater outlays of capital. The rate of profit is thereby reduced on the one hand, even if increased on the other.\" This is the fundamental tension of the chapter: the same movement that raises the numerator (more surplus-value) also swells the denominator (more constant capital). The net effect on the profit rate is indeterminate in the abstract and must be settled by the relative magnitudes. Marx then turns to a category of expenses that behave in the opposite way—they do not grow with output. \"A whole series of current expenses remains almost if not completely the same whether the working day is shorter or longer.\" Supervision is the paradigm: \"The costs of supervision are less for 500 workers over 18 hours than for 750 workers over 12.\" The Reports of the Inspectors of Factories (31 October 1848, p. 37) are cited: \"The expense of working a factory 10 hours almost equals that of working it 12.\" Local and state taxes, fire insurance, the wages of various permanent staff, the depreciation of machinery, and \"various other factory expenses\" all remain fixed in the short run. \"They rise relative to profit, in so far as production declines\" ( Reports , 31 October 1862, p. 19). This is an early and important statement of what modern economics would call fixed costs and economies of scale : the presence of overheads that are invariant to output means that spreading them over a longer working day (or a larger output) lowers the cost per unit and raises the profit rate. Marx is not simply observing a technicality; he is showing that the very structure of capitalist cost accounting creates an incentive to lengthen and intensify labour. Marx reinforces the point with a distinctive argument about the reproduction of machinery's value . The time over which the value of machinery is reproduced \"is determined in practice not by their own effective duration, but by the duration of the labour process in which they function and are used.\" If workers drudge for eighteen hours instead of twelve, \"this adds three extra days to the week, one week becomes one and a half, two years become three.\" If overtime is unpaid, \"the workers give a third week or year gratis for every two\" beyond their normal surplus labour-time. \"In this way the reproduction of the machinery's value is speeded up by 50 per cent, and accomplished in two-thirds of the time previously needed.\" This is a remarkable passage: it shows that the wear and tear of fixed capital—and therefore the rate at which its value is transferred to the product—is a function not of the machine's physical lifespan alone but of the socially imposed intensity of its use . The worker's unpaid overtime literally accelerates the valorization of the capitalist's dead labour. Marx closes the section with an explicit methodological caveat: \"In our present investigation, as in that of fluctuations in the price of raw materials (Chapter 6), we proceed from the assumption that the rate and mass of surplus-value are given—in order to avoid needless complications.\" This is the ceteris paribus strategy of the whole chapter: hold s and s' constant, and isolate the effects of changes in c. 2.3 The Social Character of Economies in Constant Capital Having established the formal relationship between c and p', Marx now asks: where do economies in c actually come from? His answer reveals the chapter's deepest theoretical commitment. He refers back to the analysis of cooperation in Volume 1, Chapter 13: \"the division of labour and machinery, the economy in the conditions of production which characterizes production on a large scale arises in essentials from the way that these conditions function as conditions of social and socially combined labour, i.e. as social conditions of labour.\" The crucial concept here is the collective worker (Gesamtarbeiter). Under large-scale production, the means of production are \"consumed in common in the production process, consumed by the collective worker instead of being consumed in fragmented form by a mass of unconnected workers or workers directly cooperating only to a small degree.\" This common consumption is the material basis of economy. Marx gives concrete illustrations: in a large factory with one or two central motors, the costs of the motors \"do not grow in the same proportion as the number of machines to which they impart motion\"; the working machine itself \"does not increase in cost in proportion to the rising number of tools, as it were its organs, with which it functions.\" The \"concentration of the means of production\" also saves on buildings—\"not only workshops proper, but also stores, etc.\"—and on heating, lighting, and similar expenses. But Marx insists that these economies are not simply technical facts about scale. They presuppose \"as an essential condition the concentration of the workers in one place, and their cooperation, i.e. the social combination of labour.\" The theoretical move is critical: \"They thus arise as much from the social character of labour as surplus-value does from the surplus labour of each individual worker taken in isolation.\" Even the improvements in technique that make economy possible are social products: \"the constant improvements that are possible and necessary arise solely from the social experiences and observations that are made possible and promoted by the large-scale production of the combined collective worker.\" This is a twofold claim. First, it locates the source of productivity gains in cooperation , not in individual genius or inherent property. Second, it characterizes those gains as the appropriation by capital of a social power. The collective worker, in other words, produces economies that the capitalist pockets as profit. Marx then introduces the second major aspect: the transformation of \"the refuse of production, its so-called waste products, back into new elements of production.\" This branch of savings, likewise, \"is the result of social labour on a large scale.\" The reasoning: only the massive scale of modern production generates waste products in quantities sufficient to make their recovery economical. \"It is only as the waste products of production in common, and hence of production on a large scale, that they acquire this importance... and remain bearers of exchange-value.\" The waste products reduce the cost of raw material to the extent that they can be resold, because that cost \"always includes the normal wastage, i.e. the average quantity that is lost in the course of processing.\" To the extent that these costs of the constant capital are reduced, \"the rate of profit is correspondingly increased, with a given magnitude of variable capital and a given rate of surplus-value.\" Marx flags that he will treat this theme \"somewhat more closely later on\" (pointing forward to the refuse section, pp. 195–8). 2.4 Use-Value vs. Exchange-Value in the Absorption of Labour Marx now pivots to one of the most philosophically rich passages in the chapter. He states the formal principle: \"If surplus-value is a given factor, the profit rate can be increased only by reducing the value of the constant capital required for the production of the commodities in question.\" But then he adds a crucial qualification about the role of constant capital in production. \"In so far as the constant capital is involved in production, all that matters is its use-value, not its exchange-value.\" He illustrates: \"The amount of labour that the flax in a spinning mill can absorb depends not on its value but on its quantity, once the level of labour productivity, i.e. the level of technical development, is given.\" Likewise, \"the assistance that a machine gives to three workers, say, depends not on its value but rather on its use-value as a machine.\" And then a beautiful historical observation: \"At one stage of technical development a bad machine may be expensive, at another stage a good machine may be cheap.\" The distinction between use-value and exchange-value is doing serious analytical work here. The capacity of constant capital to absorb and valorize labour is a matter of its material properties (quantity, quality, appropriateness to purpose). Its cost to the capitalist is a matter of the labour-time socially necessary to produce it. These two dimensions can diverge—and it is precisely through this divergence that the profit rate moves. Marx's next sentence makes the point: \"The increased profit that a capitalist obtains through a fall in the cost of cotton and spinning machinery, for example, is the result of an increase in labour productivity, and indeed not in the spinning mill, but rather in the production of machines and cotton.\" The point is again external: the cheapening of inputs to one branch comes from productivity gains in other branches. The consequence is expressed in the language of appropriation: \"A smaller amount of expenditure on the conditions of labour is needed in order to objectify a given quantity of labour and thus appropriate a given quantity of surplus labour. The costs of appropriating a certain quantity of surplus labour therefore fall.\" This is a precise formulation of the profit mechanism. Capital does not need to cheapen the labour-power it buys; it merely needs to cheapen the means of production that labour sets in motion, and its rate of profit rises. 2.5 The Cross-Branch Dynamics of Productivity and the \"External Department\" Marx pauses to acknowledge a further saving—the reduction of circulation time, \"the development of the means of communication being the decisive material aspect here\"—but defers its treatment. He then focuses on economies arising from \"the continuous improvement of machinery,\" listing four dimensions: Material improvement : \"iron instead of wood.\" Cheapening through the improvement of machine-building in general : \"so that even if the value of the fixed part of constant capital constantly grows with the development of labour on a large scale, it in no way grows to the same degree.\" (Footnote 12 refers to Ure on advances in factory construction.) Special improvements to already-installed machinery : \"e.g. improvements to steam boilers, etc.\" Reduction of wastage by better machinery. He then articulates a subtle point about depreciation. \"Everything that reduces the depreciation of machinery, and of the fixed capital in general, for a given period of production, not only cheapens the individual commodity, since each individual commodity reproduces its aliquot share of the depreciation in its price, but also reduces the aliquot capital expenditure for this period.\" The term aliquot (proportional) share is important: the value of the machinery is transferred to each unit of output in proportion to the share of the total output produced by that unit. If depreciation per period falls, then both the unit price and the capital outlay for the period fall. \"Repair work and the like, to the extent that it is needed, counts as part of the original costs of the machinery. Its reduction, as a consequence of the machinery's greater durability, reduces the price of the machinery proportionately.\" Marx here integrates maintenance costs into the value of fixed capital. He then reiterates the social condition of these economies: \"For all economies of this kind it is largely true once again that this is possible only for the combined worker and can often be realized only by work on a still larger scale. It demands a still greater direct combination of workers in the actual process of production.\" This leads to a crucial structural observation about the interdependence of branches. \"The development of the productive power of labour in one branch of production, e.g. of iron, coal, machines, construction, etc., which may in turn be partly connected with advances in the area of intellectual production, i.e. the natural sciences and their application, appears as the condition for a reduction in the value and hence the costs of means of production in other branches of industry, e.g. textiles or agriculture.\" The mechanism is circulation: \"the commodity that emerges from one branch of industry as a product enters another branch as means of production.\" Its cheapness \"depends on the productivity of labour in the branch of production from which it emerges as a product, and is at the same time a condition not only for the cheapening of the commodities into the production of which it enters as means of production, but also for the reduction in value of the constant capital whose element it now becomes, and therefore for an increase in the rate of profit.\" The \"characteristic feature of this kind of economy\" is that \"the rise in the profit rate for one branch of industry depends on the development of labour productivity in another.\" This is a striking formulation of what might be called the social division of labour as a source of profit . And it produces a distinctive alienation: the benefit \"accrues here to the capitalist\" is \"an advantage produced by social labour, even though not by the workers whom he directly exploits.\" Marx reduces this, in the last analysis, \"to the social character of the labour that is set to work, to the division of labour in society, and to the development of intellectual labour, in particular of the natural sciences.\" The capitalist is the beneficiary of \"the entire system of the social division of labour.\" And then a memorable phrase: \"Here it is the development of labour productivity in its external department, the department that provides him with means of production, which causes the value of the constant capital applied by the capitalist to fall relatively and the profit rate therefore to rise.\" The \"external department\" is a striking metaphor: the capitalist's own firm has an external provisioning apparatus—the whole society—which works for his profit rate without his having to pay for its improvement. 2.6 Economy in the Employment vs. Economy in the Production of Constant Capital Marx now draws a distinction that organizes the remainder of the theoretical analysis. \"A different form of increase in the profit rate arises not from economy in the labour by which the constant capital is produced, but rather from economy in the employment of the constant capital itself.\" The first form—economy in production—comes from rising productivity in the branches that make constant capital. The second form—economy in employment—comes from using the existing constant capital more efficiently. The latter is grounded in cooperation: \"By the concentration of workers and their cooperation on a large scale, constant capital is spared.\" Buildings, heating, lighting cost relatively less when production is on a large scale; the same holds for power and working machinery. The key phrasing is: \"Even if its value rises absolutely, it falls relatively, in relation to the increasing extension of production and to the size of the variable capital or the mass of labour-power that is set in motion.\" This is the distinction between absolute and relative magnitude. The total value of plant may be enormous; but spread over a vast output and a large mass of labour, its relative weight in the denominator shrinks. Marx then offers a compact definition of the distinct forms of capital's economizing: - \"The economy that a capital makes in its own branch of production consists firstly and most directly in economizing on labour, i.e. in reducing the paid labour of its own workers.\" - \"The economy previously mentioned, however, consists in the greatest possible appropriation of unpaid alien labour in the most economical fashion; i.e. in operating at the given scale of production with the lowest possible costs.\" This is a beautifully compressed contrast. The direct economy—reducing the wage bill, extracting more unpaid labour—belongs to the sphere of exploitation proper. The indirect economy—reducing the cost of constant capital—belongs to the sphere of circulation and social productivity. The latter \"arises either directly from cooperation and the social form of labour within the actual branch of production in question, or else from the production of machinery, etc. on a scale at which its value does not increase to the same extent as its use-value.\" This phrase—\"its value does not increase to the same extent as its use-value\"—is the crux: as machines grow more powerful and capacious (greater use-value), their cost (value) grows less than proportionally, so the value of constant capital per unit of productive capacity falls. 2.7 The Two Analytical Benchmarks: c = 0 and the Quality of Materials Marx now states \"two points must be borne in mind.\" First: \"if the value of c were 0, we would have p' = s', and the rate of profit would be at its maximum.\" This is the limiting case of the profit-rate equation. When c = 0, the denominator collapses to v alone, so the rate of profit equals the rate of surplus-value. The entire gap between p' and s'—the entire \"deduction\" from the maximum rate—is attributable to the existence of constant capital. This gives a benchmark: the higher the value of c, the greater the gap between the maximum conceivable rate of profit and the actual one. Consequently, every economy in c narrows that gap. Second—and here Marx shifts from value to use-value once again—\"what is important for the direct exploitation of labour itself is by no means the value of the means of exploitation applied, whether that of the fixed capital or that of the raw and ancillary materials.\" He explains: \"In so far as they serve to absorb labour, as media in or through which the labour and therefore also the surplus labour is objectified, the exchange-value of these machines, buildings, raw materials, etc. is completely irrelevant.\" What matters, again, is (a) \"the quantity of these means of exploitation technically required for combination with a certain quantity of labour,\" and (b) \"their appropriateness to their purpose, i.e. not only good machines are required, but also good raw and ancillary material.\" This second point opens a rich sub-analysis of the quality of raw materials . \"The rate of profit depends in part on the quality of the raw material. Good material makes little waste, and thus a smaller amount of raw material is needed to absorb the same quantity of labour.\" The resistance met by the working machine is also reduced. And then the crucial link to surplus-value: \"In part this even affects surplus-value and its rate. With bad raw material the worker needs more time to work up the same quantity; if wages remain the same, this results in a deduction from the surplus-value.\" Finally, there is \"a very significant effect on the reproduction and accumulation of capital, which, as explained in Volume 1, pp. 752 ff., depends still more on the productivity of the labour applied than on its amount.\" So the quality of inputs has a triple effect: on the rate of profit (less waste, less constant capital), on the rate of surplus-value (more or less time needed), and on accumulation (more or less productive labour). 2.8 The Capitalist's Fanaticism, Skimping, and Fraud The analysis of quality leads directly to a psychological and behavioral portrait: \"The fanaticism that the capitalist shows for economizing on means of production is now comprehensible.\" Marx explains this fanaticism as a functional imperative of the structure, not a personal quirk. \"If nothing is to be lost or wasted, if the means of production are to be used only in the manner required by production itself, then this depends partly on the workers' training and skill and partly on the discipline that the capitalist exerts over the combined workers.\" This discipline, he notes, \"would become superfluous in a state of society where the workers worked on their own account, just as it is already almost superfluous in the case of piece-work.\" The last clause is a telling aside: the whole apparatus of capitalist supervision exists only because worker and capitalist have antagonistic interests in the pace and care of work. In a cooperative or self-managed setting, the incentive to economize would be internal to the producers themselves. Capitalist discipline is therefore a social cost offsetting the social economies generated by cooperation—a point Marx will develop in his treatment of the Rochdale cooperatives. But \"the same fanaticism is also expressed inversely in the form of skimping on elements of production, which is a major way of lowering the value of the constant capital in relation to the variable and thus of increasing the rate of profit.\" The term \"skimping\" ( Schwindel or cheating in the German) covers the supply of inferior materials, and the discussion broadens into outright fraud: \"we have the sale of these elements of production above their value, in so far as this value reappears in the product, which is an important aspect of fraud.\" Marx notes that \"this aspect plays a decisive role in German industry, in particular, whose very motto is: People cannot fail to appreciate it if we send them first good samples, and then bad goods.\" He then (characteristically) declares that \"these phenomena pertain to competition and do not concern us here\"—a methodological quarantine that signals these are contingent, juridical/moral phenomena rather than structural determinants of the profit-rate mechanism. 2.9 The Independence of Profit-Rate Gains from Branch Output Type Marx now emphasizes a point that is easy to miss. \"It must be noted how this rise in the rate of profit brought about by a reduction in the value of the constant capital, and thus in its expense, is completely independent of whether the branch of industry in which it takes place produces luxury products, means of subsistence that enter the consumption of the workers, or means of production.\" Why does this matter? Because in other contexts—the analysis of relative surplus-value , for instance—the type of commodity produced matters enormously. If the commodities are wage-goods, their cheapening lowers the value of labour-power and thereby raises surplus-value. But here, Marx says, \"surplus-value and its rate are taken as given.\" The result is that the effect on the profit rate operates through a different channel entirely: \"How the surplus-value is related to the total capital—and this is what determines the profit rate—depends under these circumstances exclusively on the value of the constant capital and in no way on the use-value of the elements of which this consists.\" This is a methodological clarification with real theoretical weight. It separates the value-of-labour-power channel (through which the cheapening of wage-goods affects surplus-value) from the value-of-constant-capital channel (through which the cheapening of means of production affects the profit rate). The two channels are distinct, and the chapter is concerned exclusively with the second. Marx adds a caution against over-optimism: \"Of course the relative cheapening of the means of production does not exclude a growth in their absolute value; for the absolute scale on which they are applied increases extraordinarily with the development of labour productivity and the growing scale of production that accompanies it.\" In other words, individual machines become cheaper relative to their power, but the total stock of machinery grows enormously. Relative cheapening coexists with absolute growth. 2.10 The Fetishization of Economy: Capital's \"Inherent Power\" Marx now synthesizes the two sources of economy. \"Economy in the use of constant capital, from whatever aspect it is viewed, is firstly the result of nothing more than the fact that the means of production function in common and are used as the common means of production of the combined worker, so that this economy itself appears as a product of the social character of directly productive labour.\" This is the cooperative source. \"Secondly, however, it is also the result of the development of labour productivity in those spheres that provide capital with its means of production, so that even if labour as a whole is considered vis-à-vis capital as a whole, and not merely the workers employed by capitalist X vis-à-vis this capitalist X, this economy again presents itself as the product of the development of the productive forces of social labour.\" This is the cross-branch source. The distinction between the individual capitalist and capital as a whole is important: \"the distinction is simply that capitalist X benefits not only from the productivity of labour in his own firm, but also from that of other firms as well.\" The capitalist is the passive recipient of a social productivity he did nothing to create. Now Marx turns to ideology. \"Yet the economical use of constant capital still appears to the capitalist as a requirement completely alien to the worker and absolutely independent of him, a requirement which does not concern the worker in the least.\" Nevertheless, \"it always remains very clear to the capitalist that the worker certainly does have something to do with whether the capitalist buys more or less labour for the same amount of money (for this is how the transaction between capitalist and worker appears in his consciousness).\" That is, the capitalist does perceive the worker's role in the wage bargain. But the economy in constant capital appears to him as a purely technical matter, unrelated to the worker—\"a power inherent in capital and a method specific to and characteristic of the capitalist mode of production.\" Marx comments: \"This way of conceiving things is all the less surprising in that it corresponds to the semblance of the matter and that the capital relation actually does conceal the inner connection in the state of complete indifference, externality and alienation in which it places the worker vis-à-vis the conditions of realization of his own labour.\" This is a classic statement of fetishism / alienation : the social relation appears as a thing (capital's \"inherent power\"), and the worker's actual contribution is hidden. 2.11 The Three Aspects of Worker Indifference and Alienation Marx now develops three reasons why the worker is (or appears to be) indifferent to these economies, and why this indifference is itself a product of the capital relation. First : \"The means of production which comprise the constant capital simply represent the capitalist's money (as the body of the Roman debtor represented the money of his creditor, according to Linguet).\" The reference is to Simon-Nicolas-Henri Linguet (1736–94), a leading figure of the French Enlightenment, whose Théorie des lois civiles (London, 1767) contained the lurid (and contested) claim that Roman creditors literally \"cut shares\" in their debtors' bodies and ate them. Marx uses the image to make a point about the personification of capital: the worker encounters the means of production not as products of past social labour, but as \"the capitalist's money\"—as an attribute of the capitalist's person. The worker \"deals with them only as use-values for production, means and materials of labour.\" Whether they are worth more or less \"affects his relationship to the capitalist as little as whether he works with copper or with iron.\" But—and here Marx anticipates a reversal—\"the capitalist likes to conceive things differently, as we shall see later, as soon as there is an increase in the value of the means of production and hence a decline in the rate of profit.\" That is, the capitalist will suddenly discover that the worker is responsible for the expensive machinery only when it hurts profits. Second : \"In so far as these means of production are at the same time a means for exploiting labour in the capitalist production process, the relative cheapness or otherwise of these means of exploitation concerns the worker as little as a horse is concerned with the expense of its bit and bridle.\" The brutal simile drives home the reification: the worker is related to the means of production as a beast of burden to its harness. Third : \"The worker actually treats the social character of his work, its combination with the work of others for a common goal, as a power that is alien to him; the conditions in which this combination is realized are for him the property of another, and he would be completely indifferent to the wastage of this property if he were not himself constrained to economize on it.\" Marx adds a crucial counterexample: \"It is quite different with factories that belong to the workers themselves, as at Rochdale.\" The footnote explains: these were factories \"offshoots of the Society of Equitable Pioneers established in 1844, and today remembered more as the starting-point of consumer cooperatives.\" The cooperative movement is invoked precisely to show that worker indifference to waste is not a natural fact but a specific consequence of private ownership . Where workers own the means of production, they economize voluntarily; where capital owns them, economizing must be coerced. Marx then notes that \"this general connection of social labour presents itself as something completely alien to the workers, something that simply concerns the capitalist, in as much as he alone buys these means of production and appropriates them. Though he buys the product of the workers in a different branch of industry with the product of the workers in his own branch, and thus disposes of the product of other workers only in so far as he has appropriated the product of his own workers without payment, this is a relationship that is concealed by the circulation process, etc.\" This is a striking point about circulation and exploitation : the capitalist's ability to appropriate the products of foreign workers (his suppliers) rests entirely on his prior appropriation of the unpaid product of his own workers. But the circulation process—the exchange of equivalents in the market—hides this origin. Finally, Marx notes that the appearance of economy as \"a function of the capitalist\" is reinforced by history: \"since production on a large scale developed first in the capitalist form, the profit-mania and competition which compel commodities to be produced as cheaply as possible give economy in the use of constant capital the appearance of something peculiar to the capitalist mode of production and therefore make it seem a function of the capitalist.\" Nonetheless, the dialectical formulation stands: \"Just as the capitalist mode of production promotes on the one hand the development of the productive forces of social labour, so on the other hand does it promote economy in the use of constant capital.\" 2.12 The Antithetical Character: Squandering Life and Health as \"Economy\" Here the chapter pivots decisively from analysis to indictment. \"Yet there is more to this than the alienation and indifference that the worker, as the bearer of living labour, has towards the economical, i.e. rational and frugal use of his conditions of labour.\" Marx's next sentence is the hinge of the whole chapter: \"The contradictory and antithetical character of the capitalist mode of production leads it to count the squandering of the life and health of the worker, and the depression of his conditions of existence, as itself an economy in the use of constant capital, and hence a means for raising the rate of profit.\" The argument is this. Because the worker \"spends the greater part of his life in the production process, the conditions of this process are to a great extent conditions of his active life process itself, his conditions of life, and economy in these conditions of life is a method of increasing the profit rate.\" This is a profound conceptual move: the conditions of production are also the conditions of life , so an \"economy\" in plant and premises (cheap buildings, no ventilation, no safety devices) is simultaneously an \"economy\" in the worker's own mode of existing. Capital thereby economizes by degrading the worker's life. Marx catalogues the forms this takes: - \"overwork, the transformation of the worker into a beast of burden, is a method of accelerating the self-valorization of capital, the production of surplus-value\" (referring back to Volume 1, Chapter 10). - \"crowding workers into confined and unhealthy premises, a practice which in capitalist parlance is called saving on buildings.\" - \"squeezing dangerous machines into the same premises and dispensing with means of protection against these dangers.\" - \"neglect of precautionary measures in those production processes whose very nature is harmful to health or involves risk, as in mining, etc.\" - \"Not to speak of the absence of all provisions that would make the production process humane, comfortable or simply bearable for the worker.\" The dialectical paradox is stated with terrible clarity: \"From the standpoint of the capitalist this would be a senseless and purposeless waste. Yet for all its stinginess, capitalist production is thoroughly wasteful with human material, just as its way of distributing its products through trade, and its manner of competition, make it very wasteful of material resources, so that it loses for society what it gains for the individual capitalist.\" The point is not merely moral. It is economic : the capitalist is only accounting for the costs and benefits that accrue to him ; the depletion of human capacities and natural resources is an \"external\" cost that does not appear on his books. The individual capitalist gains; society loses. 2.13 The Tendency to Minimize Living Labour and Constant Capital Marx now articulates the tendency at a high level of abstraction. \"As capital has the tendency to reduce the direct employment of living labour to the necessary minimum and constantly shorten the labour needed for the creation of a product by exploiting the social productivity of labour, i.e. economizing as much as possible on directly applied living labour, so it also has the tendency to apply this labour, which has already been reduced to its necessary amount, under the most economical circumstances, i.e. to reduce the value of the constant capital applied to the absolute minimum.\" This is a compact statement of a double movement: capital (1) minimizes living labour and (2) minimizes the constant capital through which that living labour is applied. Both movements raise the rate of profit; the first raises the rate of surplus-value (by shortening necessary labour time), the second lowers the denominator (by cheapening c). Marx then makes a metaphysical-historical observation of remarkable depth. \"If the value of commodities is determined by the necessary labour-time contained in them and not simply by labour-time as such, it is capital that first makes a reality of this mode of determination and immediately goes on to reduce continually the labour socially necessary for the production of a commodity. The price of the commodity is therefore reduced to a minimum through reducing to a minimum each part of the labour required to produce it.\" In other words, the law of value —the determination of value by socially necessary labour-time—while in principle applicable to all commodity production, is only fully realized under capitalism, because capitalism alone systematically and remorselessly reduces the socially necessary labour time through mechanization, cooperation, and economy. The same system that reduces the value of the commodity also reduces the value of the constant capital that enters into it. Thus the chapter becomes not just a story about costs but a story about capital's historical mission: to develop the productive forces—while simultaneously chaining them to the profit motive. 2.14 The Concentration Distinction Marx now introduces a distinction that clarifies the relationship between the scale of capital and the value of constant capital. \"If the mass of the capital applied grows, and with it also the sum of capital value, this first involves simply the concentration of more capital in a single hand.\" This is a point about concentration : the aggregation of capital into larger units. But: \"it is precisely this greater mass employed by one capital (which generally corresponds also to an absolutely greater, if relatively smaller number of workers) that permits economies in constant capital.\" There are two layers in this sentence. First, larger capitals can afford more efficient machinery; hence they can economize more. Second, \"which generally corresponds also to an absolutely greater, if relatively smaller number of workers\" anticipates the organic-composition argument: the absolute number of workers grows, but relative to the size of capital it shrinks. \"If we take the individual capitalist, we see a growth in the size of his necessary capital outlay, and particularly in the fixed capital; but in relation to the mass of material to be worked up and the labour to be exploited, its value relatively declines.\" This is the formal signature of the chapter in miniature: absolute growth, relative decline. The value of constant capital per worker or per unit of output declines, even as its total value explodes. Marx then announces the empirical section: \"We shall now elaborate this with some brief illustrations. We begin with what is really the end, economies in the conditions of production, in so far as these present themselves at the same time as the conditions of existence and life of the worker himself.\" The phrase \"what is really the end\" is significant: he begins with the human terminus of the argument—the end-result of all these economies, viewed from the perspective of the worker—in order to keep the lived consequences in view before descending into the technicalities. 2.15 Savings on the Conditions of Work at the Workers' Expense — Coal Mining The empirical section opens with coal mining, framed as \"Neglect of the Most Necessary Outlays.\" Marx quotes at length from the First Report on Children's Employment in Mines and Collieries (21 April 1829, p. 102): \"Under the competition which exists among the coal-owners and coal-proprietors ... no more outlay is incurred than is sufficient to overcome the most obvious physical difficulties; and under that which prevails among the labouring colliers, who are ordinarily more numerous than the work to be done requires, a large amount of danger and exposure to the most noxious influences will gladly be encountered for wages a little in advance of the agricultural population round them, in an occupation, in which they can moreover make a profitable use of their children. This double competition is quite sufficient ... to cause a large proportion of the pits to be worked with the most imperfect drainage and ventilation; often with ill-constructed shafts, bad gearing, incompetent engineers; and ill-constructed and ill-prepared bays and roadways; causing a destruction of life, and limb, and health, the statistics of which would present an appalling picture.\" The passage is analytically rich. First, it identifies the competitive pressure among coal-owners as the driver of under-investment in safety: they spend only on the \"most obvious physical difficulties,\" because competition compels them to minimize costs. Second, it identifies the competition among colliers —their \"ordinarily\" excessive numbers—as the reason they will accept dangerous conditions for slightly higher wages. The \"double competition\" is the precise mechanism: competition among owners on one side, competition among workers on the other, jointly producing an equilibrium of unsafe and unhealthy workplaces. This is a classic Marxian analysis of how competition produces socially irrational outcomes : rational for each individual owner, catastrophic in aggregate. Marx then collates the statistical evidence. \"Around 1860, an average of some fifteen men were killed each week in the English coal mines. According to the report on Coal Mine Accidents (6 February 1862), a total of 8,466 had been killed in the ten years 1852–61.\" He immediately notes the understatement: \"this number is far too small, as the report itself admits, since in the first few years, when the inspectors had only just been appointed and their districts were far too large, a great number of accidents and deaths were not reported at all.\" Marx then makes a crucial scientific observation about causation: \"The very fact that, despite the great butchery that still goes on and the insufficient number and restricted powers of the inspectors, the number of accidents has dropped sharply since the inspection system was established indicates the natural tendency of capitalist exploitation.\" This is a controlled-comparison argument: if accidents decline when inspection is introduced, then inspection was the operative variable—which demonstrates that the absence of inspection (and the cost-saving on safety measures) was the cause of the higher accident rate. This is the natural tendency of capital. The immediate cause is specified: \"These human sacrifices are due for the most part to the filthy avarice of the coal-owners, who for instance often have only one shaft sunk, so that not only is no effective ventilation possible, but also there is no escape if this shaft gets blocked.\" Thus a single technical decision —sinking only one shaft, to save on capital outlay—produces both chronic ill health (no ventilation) and catastrophic mortality (no escape route when the shaft is blocked). This is precisely the \"economy in constant capital\" the chapter analyzes. Marx then delivers a sweeping judgment. \"If we consider capitalist production in the narrow sense and ignore the process of circulation and the excesses of competition, it is extremely sparing with the realized labour that is objectified in commodities. Yet it squanders human beings, living labour, more readily than does any other mode of production, squandering not only flesh and blood, but nerves and brain as well.\" The contrast between sparing with objectified labour and squandering living labour is the essential antithesis of capitalist \"economy.\" He continues: \"In fact it is only through the most tremendous waste of individual development that the development of humanity in general is secured and pursued, in that epoch of history that directly precedes the conscious reconstruction of human society.\" This is a Hegelian and evolutionary formulation: the \"cunning of reason\" operates through the sacrifice of individuals to the development of the species. The epoch of capitalist production is the historical period immediately preceding \"the conscious reconstruction of human society\"—a vision of socialism as the rational appropriation of what capital has developed unconsciously. Marx then produces a profound causal claim: \"Since the whole of the economizing we are discussing here arises from the social character of labour, it is in fact precisely this directly social character of labour that produces this waste of the workers' life and health.\" This is the dialectical pivot of the entire chapter: the social character of labour is the source both of the economy and of the waste . The same cooperation that makes large-scale machinery possible makes mass death and disease possible. Its dark mirror image. He quotes factory inspector R. Baker: \"The whole question is one for serious consideration, in what way this sacrifice of infant life occasioned by congregational labour can be best averted?\" ( Reports of the Inspectors of Factories , 31 October 1863, p. 157, Marx's emphasis). The footnote explains: \"'Congregational labour' means here labour carried on by large masses of people working in association.\" The phrase, and the emphasis on \"congregational labour,\" thematizes the social (congregational) dimension as both the source of productivity and the source of death. 2.16 Factories: The Political Economy of Safety Devices The section on factories continues the indictment, focusing on \"the suppression of all precautionary measures as to the safety, comfort and health of the workers, even in factories proper.\" Marx cites the casualty lists from the annual Factory Reports and the \"insufficient space, ventilation, etc.\" He then reconstructs, in forensic detail, the political history of the English factory safety legislation of the 1840s and 1850s. In October 1855, Leonard Horner (1785–1864, head of the Factory Inspectorate, described in the footnote as having \"demonstrated a firm commitment to improving working-class conditions\") complained \"about the resistance that a very large number of factory-owners were placing to the legal provisions for safety devices on horizontal shafts, even though the danger was continually being demonstrated by accidents, often fatal ones, and this safety appliance is neither expensive nor in any way disturbs the work\" ( Reports , October 1855, p. 6). The key analytical point is buried in the report: the safety appliance \"is neither expensive nor in any way disturbs the work.\" In other words, the resistance is not justified by any meaningful economic calculation—the device is cheap and unobtrusive. So the resistance reveals something qualitative : the capitalist's refusal to accept any restriction on his property , even when it costs almost nothing. This is an important nuance: the \"economy\" at work is not merely narrowly calculative but involves a political dimension—the defense of unrestricted property rights. The owners were \"given open support in resisting these and other legal provisions by the unpaid Justices of the Peace who had to decide on the cases, and were generally factory-owners themselves, or friends of factory-owners.\" Lord Campbell's comment on one verdict becomes a damning epigraph for the entire system: \"It is not an interpretation of the Act of Parliament, it is a repeal of the Act of Parliament\" (ibid., p. 11). The point: the enforcement of the law is itself captured by the class whose conduct it was meant to regulate, so that de facto repeal is achieved through de jure interpretation. Horner also relates \"how in many factories the machines are switched on without the workers being given advance warning. Since there is always something to be done on the machines when they are standing still, some hands and fingers are always busy with this, and accidents constantly arise simply from failing to give a signal\" (ibid., p. 44). The \"economy\" here is literally an economy on time and signal mechanism —the workers' hands and fingers are the deductible cost. Marx then narrates the formation of the National Association for the Amendment of the Factory Laws , \"based in Manchester, which collected a sum of more than £50,000 in March 1855 from contributions on the basis of 2 shillings per horse-power, to meet the legal costs of members prosecuted by the factory inspectors and conduct their cases on behalf of the Association.\" The object: \"to prove 'killing no murder' if done for the sake of profit.\" The footnote notes the allusion to the 1657 pamphlet Killing No Murder by the Leveller Sexby, which called for the assassination of Cromwell with \"appropriate moral and religious justification.\" The parallel is savage: the factory-owners treat the deaths of workers with the casuistry of political regicide. The contrast with the cost of safety devices is powerful. Sir John Kincaid, factory inspector for Scotland, tells \"of a firm in Glasgow which surrounded all its machines with safety-guards for the price of £9 1s. Od. If that firm had joined the Association, it would have had to pay a contribution of £11 for its 110 horse-power, i.e. more than the total cost of its safety-guards.\" The arithmetic exposes the political, not economic, motive: joining the resistance association cost more than compliance. Yet \"the National Association was expressly founded in 1854 to defy the Act that prescribed safety-guards of this kind.\" The history continues: \"During the entire period from 1844 to 1854 the factory-owners had not taken the least bit of notice of this Act. The factory inspectors then informed the factory-owners that the Act was now to be taken seriously, at Palmerston's instigation. The factory-owners promptly formed their Association, its most prominent members including many who were themselves J.P.s, and in this capacity had actually to apply the Act.\" The irony is bitter: the same men charged with applying the law organized to defy it. When the new Home Secretary, Sir George Grey, \"proposed a compromise solution in April 1855, by which the government would be content with safety-guards that were scarcely more than nominal, the Association indignantly rejected even this.\" The celebrated engineer William Fairbairn \"used his reputation as an expert in defence of economy and the violated freedom of capital.\" Horner was \"persecuted and slandered by the factory-owners in every conceivable way.\" The denouement: the factory-owners \"obtained a judgement from the Queen's Bench Division to the effect that the Act of 1844 did not prescribe any safety-guards for horizontal shafts if these were more than seven feet above ground level, and they finally managed in 1856, with the help of the hypocrite Wilson-Patten—one of those pious persons whose prominently displayed religion makes them always ready to do dirty work for the knights of the money-bag—to put through a new Act of Parliament which was sufficiently to their satisfaction. This Act actually withdrew from the workers all special protection and referred them to the ordinary courts if they wished to seek compensation for injuries caused by machine accidents—sheer mockery, given English legal costs. It also made it almost impossible for the factory-owners to lose a case, by a very neatly worded clause providing for expert testimony.\" Thus the legal structure itself was rewritten to immunize the owners from liability: a juridical \"economy in constant capital\" enforced by statute. Marx then returns to statistics. \"In the six months from May to October 1858, inspector Baker alone reported an increase of 21 per cent against the previous half-year. In his opinion, 36.7 per cent of all the accidents could have been avoided.\" Then an apparent paradox: \"Yet in 1858 and 1859 the number of accidents was significantly lower than it had been around 1845 and 1846, some 29 per cent lower in fact, even though the number of workers in the branches of industry covered by the Inspectorate had increased by 20 per cent. What was the cause of this?\" Marx's answer: \"In as much as the question has been settled at this date (1865), it was principally due to the introduction of new machines which were already provided with safety-guards, which the factory-owners could leave in existence as they did not cost them any extra. A few workers also managed to extract heavy legal compensation for lost arms, and have these judgements upheld even by the highest courts\" ( Reports , 30 April 1861, p. 31, and April 1862, p. 17). This is a subtle and important passage. Safety improves only when it is costless (built in to new machinery) or when liability is imposed (heavy legal compensation). The mechanism is entirely calculative—workers' safety is achieved only when it is either free to capital or unprofitable to neglect. Marx closes the section: \"So much for economy in the means for protecting the lives and limbs of the workers—including many children—from dangers that directly arise from their use of machinery.\" 2.17 Work in Enclosed Spaces: The Public Health Evidence The next subsection shifts from machinery accidents to chronic disease. \"It is well enough known how much economy on space, and therefore on buildings, crowds workers together in cramped conditions. A further factor is economy on means of ventilation. These two things, together with long working hours, produce a great increase in respiratory diseases and consequently increased mortality.\" Marx draws on the Reports on Public Health, Sixth Report , 1863, compiled by Dr John Simon, Chief Medical Officer of the Privy Council (\"already well-known to us from Volume 1\"). He first articulates the theoretical connection: \"Just as the combination of workers and their cooperation is what permits the use of machines on a large scale, concentration of means of production and economy in their use, so this working together en masse in enclosed spaces and under conditions where the decisive factor is not the health of the worker, but the ease with which the product may be constructed—this massive concentration in the same workshop—which is on the one hand a source of growing profit for the capitalist, is on the other hand the cause of a squandering of the worker's life and health, if it is not compensated for both by shorter working hours and by special precautionary measures.\" The theoretical identity is explicit: the same concentration that produces profit produces disease . The only \"compensation\" would be shorter hours and precautionary measures—i.e., raising the cost of constant capital in the interest of health. Dr Simon's rule is quoted: \"In proportion as the people of a district are attracted to any collective indoor occupation, in such proportion, other things being equal, the district death-rate by lung diseases will be increased\" (p. 23). Cause: bad ventilation. And: \"probably in all England there is no exception to the rule, that, in every district which has a large indoor industry, the increased mortality of the workpeople is such as to colour the death-return of the whole district with a marked excess of lung disease\" (p. 23). The phrase \"colour the death-return of the whole district\"—the elevated mortality of workers so dominates local statistics that it becomes visible as a geographical fact—demonstrates the social scale of the phenomenon. The mortality figures from the Board of Health investigation of 1860–61 give a quantitative ratio: taking 100 deaths from consumption and other lung diseases among men aged 15–55 in the agricultural districts of England as the base, the rates are: 166 in Coventry, 167 in Blackburn and Skipton, 168 in Congleton and Bradford, 171 in Leicester, 182 in Leek, 184 in Macclesfield, 190 in Bolton, 192 in Nottingham, 193 in Rochdale, 198 in Derby, 203 in Salford and Ashton-under-Lyne, 218 in Leeds, 220 in Preston, and 263 in Manchester (p. 24). The differences are dramatic: Manchester's lung-disease mortality is nearly three times the agricultural baseline. A more detailed table reports deaths from pulmonary diseases per 100,000 population, ages 15–25, separated by sex, for districts where women alone were engaged in the confined industries while men worked in all branches: District Chief industry Men Women Berkhampstead Straw plaiting (women) 219 578 Leighton Buzzard Straw plaiting (women) 309 554 Newport Pagnell Lace manufacture (women) 301 617 Towcester Lace manufacture (women) 239 577 Yeovil Glove manufacture (mainly women) 280 409 Leek Silk industry (predominantly women) 437 856 Congleton Silk industry (predominantly women) 566 790 Macclesfield Silk industry (predominantly women) 593 890 Healthy country district Agriculture 331 333 The pattern is stark. Women in the silk districts have mortality rates up to 890 per 100,000—nearly three times the agricultural baseline of 333 for women. Even the men in those districts, who work in mixed industries, show elevated rates (437–593). In the silk districts \"where male participation in factory work is greater, their mortality is also more significant.\" The Reports comment on \"the atrocious circumstances under which much of our silk industry is conducted.\" Marx adds a biting observation: \"And this is the same silk industry in which the factory-owners, appealing to the exceptionally favourable health conditions in their business, demanded exceptionally long working hours from children under 13 years of age, and in part obtained these too (Volume 1, Chapter 10, 6, pp. 405–7).\" The hypocrisy is complete: owners present health conditions as a justification for longer hours , while the same conditions are killing workers. 2.18 Tailoring, Typesetting, and Millinery Marx quotes Dr Smith's description of tailoring: \"Shops vary much in their sanitary conditions, but almost universally are overcrowded and ill-ventilated, and in a high degree unfavourable to health.\" The air grows hot: \"when the gas is lit, as during the day-time on foggy days, and at night during the winter, the heat increases to 80° and even to upwards of 90°, causing profuse perspiration, and condensation of vapour upon the panes of glass, so that it runs down in streams or drops from the roof, and the operatives are compelled to keep some windows open, at whatever risk to themselves of taking cold.\" The specific measurements are devastating. In sixteen of the most important West End shops: \"The largest cubic space in these ill-ventilated rooms allowed to each operative is 270 feet, and the least 105 feet, and in the whole average only 156 feet per man.\" In one room, \"with a gallery running round it, and lighted only from the roof, from 92 to upwards of 100 men are employed, where a large number of gas-lights burn, and where the urinals are in the closest proximity, the cubic space does not exceed 150 feet per man.\" In another, \"which can only be called a kennel in a yard, lighted from the roof, and ventilated by a small skylight opening, five to six men work in a space of 112 cubic feet per man.\" Working hours: \"Tailors, in those atrocious workshops which Dr Smith describes, work generally for about 12 or 13 hours a day, and at some times the work will be continued for 15 or 16 hours\" (pp. 25, 26, 28). Marx then addresses a methodological point raised by Dr Simon: in the age group 25–35, mortality for tailors and typesetters in London was under-reported , because London employers take on large numbers of young people (up to age 30) as apprentices and \"improvers.\" These swell the denominators of London death rates but do not die in London at the same rate, because they return home when ill, and their deaths are registered in the countryside. \"If they become ill during this time, they go back home to the country, and it is there that their death is registered if they die. This state of affairs affects the younger age-groups even more and renders the London mortality rates for these groups completely valueless as measurements of industrial disease\" (p. 30). This is an important statistical caveat—a lesson in the dangers of misreading mortality data—and it strengthens rather than weakens Marx's case, since the under-reporting means the true industrial mortality is even worse than the published figures suggest. The table for the older groups confirms the picture: Branches of industry and locality Persons employed 25–35 35–45 45–55 Agriculture, England and Wales 958,265 743 805 1,145 Tailoring, London 22,301 men and 12,377 women 958 1,262 2,093 Typesetters and printers, London 13,803 894 1,747 2,367 The contrast is striking. Agricultural workers gain life expectancy with age (deaths rise slowly from 743 to 1,145 across the age bands). Tailors and typesetters, however, show dramatically elevated mortality, especially at older ages—typesetters reach 2,367 deaths per 100,000 in the 45–55 band, more than double the agricultural rate. Marx comments: \"What is true of tailoring is true also of the typesetters, among whom lack of ventilation, foul air, etc. is supplemented by night work. Their customary working day lasts for 12 or 13 hours, and sometimes 15 or 16.\" He then quotes a long passage describing the printers' environment: \"Great heat and foulness which begin when the gas-jets are lit... It not infrequently happens that fumes from a foundry, or foul odours from machinery or sinks, rise from the lower room, and aggravate the evils of the upper one. The heated air of the lower rooms always tends to heat the upper by warming the floor, and when the rooms are low, and the consumption of gas great, this is a serious evil, and one only surpassed in the case where the steam-boilers are placed in the lower room, and supply unwished-for heat to the whole house.\" The general judgment: \"universally the ventilation is defective, and quite insufficient to remove the heat and the products of the combustion of gas in the evening and during the night, and that in many offices, and particularly in those made from dwelling-houses, the condition is most deplorable.\" The most brutal observation: \"in some offices (especially those of weekly newspapers) there will be work—work too, in which boys between 12 and 16 years of age take equal part—for almost uninterrupted periods of two days and a night at a time; while, in other printing-offices which lay themselves out for the doing of 'urgent' business, Sunday gives no relaxation to the workman, and his working-days become seven instead of six in every week\" (pp. 26, 28). Here the \"economy in constant capital\" is an economy on time and labour discipline : capital avoids hiring additional workers by working existing ones round the clock. Finally, milliners and dressmakers. Marx recalls that \"we met with the milliners and dressmakers already in Volume 1, Chapter 10, 3, pp. 364–5, in relation to overwork.\" Dr Ord's measurements in thirty-four of the \"better sort\" of workshops: \"In four cases more than 500 [cubic feet], in four other cases from 400 to 500, ... in seven others from 200 to 250, in four others from 150 to 200, and in nine others only from 100 to 150. The largest of these allowances would but be scanty for continuous work, unless the space were thoroughly well ventilated; and, except with extraordinary ventilation, its atmosphere could not be tolerably wholesome during gas-light.\" An inferior-class workshop: \"One room area in cubical feet, 1,280; persons present, 14; area to each, in cubical feet, 91.5. The women here were weary-looking and squalid; their earnings were stated to be 7s. to 15s. a week, and their tea ... Hours 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. The small room into which these 14 persons were crowded was ill-ventilated. There were two movable windows and a fire-place, but the latter was blocked up and there was no special ventilation of any kind\" (p. 27). The blocked fireplace is a powerful symbol: existing ventilation infrastructure, costless to use, is disabled because it would interfere with the work—an \"economy\" on comfort that costs nothing to reverse. The section on overwork among milliners: \"The overwork of the young women in fashionable dressmaking establishments does not, for more than about four months of the year, prevail in that monstrous degree which has on many occasions excited momentary public surprise and indignation; but for the indoor hands during these months it will, as a rule, be of full 14 hours a day, and will, when there is pressure, be, for days together, of 17 or even 18 hours.\" Outside the season: \"the work of the indoor hands ranges probably from 10 to 14 hours; and uniformly the hours for outdoor hands are 12 or 13.\" For other categories: \"the hours spent in the common workroom are fewer—generally not more than 10 to 12 hours; but ... the regular hours of work are subject to considerable extension in certain houses at certain times, by the practice of working extra hours for extra pay, and in other houses by the practice of taking work away from houses of business, to be done after hours at home, both practices being, it may be added, often compulsory\" (p. 28). The phrase \"often compulsory\" is crucial: what appears as voluntary overtime is in fact coerced by the structure of the employment relation. Even \"outwork\" (work done at home) becomes a mode of disguised exploitation, an \"economy\" on factory space at the cost of the workers' homes and lives. Dr Simon's note to this page reports Mr Radcliffe's findings: \"in only one out of twenty girls examined who called themselves 'quite well' could the state of health be pronounced good; the rest exhibiting in various degrees evidences of depressed physical power, nervous exhaustion, and numerous functional disorders thereupon dependent.\" The causes: first, the length of hours (minimum 12 out of season); secondarily, \"crowding and bad ventilation of workrooms, gas-vapours, insufficiency or bad quality of food, and inattention to domestic comfort.\" The Chief Medical Officer's conclusion is quoted at length and serves as the chapter's implicit moral demand: \"it is practically impossible for workpeople to insist upon that which in theory is their first sanitary right—the right that whatever work their employer assembles them to do, shall, so far as depends upon him, be, at his cost, divested of all needlessly unwholesome circumstances; ... while workpeople are practically unable to exact that sanitary justice for themselves, they also (notwithstanding the presumed intentions of the law) cannot expect any effectual assistance from the appointed administrators of the Nuisances Removal Acts\" (p. 29). Then a remarkable passage acknowledging the difficulty of strict regulation but insisting on principle: \"Doubtless there may be some small technical difficulty in defining the exact line at which employers shall become subject to regulation. But ... in principle, the sanitary claim is universal. And in the interest of myriads of labouring men and women, whose lives are now needlessly afflicted and shortened by the infinite physical suffering which their mere employment engenders, I would venture to express my hope, that universally the sanitary circumstances of labour may, at least so far, be brought within appropriate provisions of law, that the effective ventilation of all indoor workplaces may be ensured, and that in every naturally insalubrious occupation the specific health-endangering influence may as far as practicable be reduced\" (p. 31). Smith's formulation—the worker's right that the workplace be \"at his cost, divested of all needlessly unwholesome circumstances\"—encapsulates the chapter's thesis from the reformist side: the costs of health and safety are a legitimate charge on capital, not a gratuitous concession to workers. 2.19 Economy in the Generation and Transmission of Power Marx now shifts from the damaging \"economies\" to the genuine productivity-raising ones. He quotes at length from a letter by the famous engineer James Nasmyth of Patricroft, inventor of the steam-hammer, included in Leonard Horner's report for October 1852. Nasmyth's account begins: \"The public are little aware of the vast increase in driving power which has been obtained by such changes of system and improvements\" of steam-engines. Lancashire's engine power \"lay under the incubus of timid and prejudiced traditions for nearly forty years, but now we are happily emancipated.\" The technical history is detailed: for years, engines were restricted to a piston speed of about 220 feet per minute (a 5-foot stroke engine limited to 22 revolutions of the crankshaft per minute). Then, \"either through fortunate ignorance of the 'rule,' or by better reasons on the part of some bold innovator, a greater speed was tried, and as the result was highly favourable, others followed the example, by, as it is termed, 'letting the engine away'\"—modifying the first-motion wheels so the engine could run at 300 feet per minute and upwards while the mill gearing remained at its former speed. The theoretical insight here is fundamental: \"This 'letting the engine away' ... has led to the almost universal 'speeding' of engines, because it was proved that not only was there available power gained from the identical engines, but also as the higher velocity of the engine yielded a greater momentum in the fly-wheel the motion was found to be much more regular.\" In other words: the same machine yields more power when run faster , because the greater fly-wheel momentum smooths the motion. Nasmyth gives a mathematical illustration: \"suppose any given engine yields 40 horse-power when its piston is travelling at 200 feet per minute, if by suitable arrangement or modification we can permit this same engine to run at such a speed as that its piston will travel through space at 400 feet per minute (pressure of steam and vacuum, as before said, remaining the same), we shall then have just double the power.\" Crucially: \"as the pressure by steam and vacuum is the same in both cases, the strain upon the parts of this engine will be no greater at 400 than at 200 feet speed of piston, so that the risk of 'break-down' does not materially increase with the increase of speed.\" The physics is that power is proportional to velocity at constant pressure, so doubling the velocity doubles the power. The only additional costs are \"some small increase in the wear and tear of 'the brasses' or rubbing-parts, but so slight as to be scarcely worth notice.\" To be sure, \"in order to obtain increase of power from the same engine by permitting its piston to travel at a higher velocity it is requisite ... to burn more coal per hour under the same boiler, or employ boilers of greater evaporating capabilities.\" The solution: \"boilers of greater steam-generating or water-evaporating powers were supplied to the old 'speeded' engines, and in many cases near 100 per cent more work was got out of the identical engines by means of such changes as above named.\" The \"identical engines\"— the same constant capital —yield twice the useful effect. Nasmyth then narrates the diffusion of these techniques through competition. \"About ten years ago the extraordinary economical production of power as realized by the engines employed in the mining operations of Cornwall began to attract attention; and as competition in the spinning trade forced manufacturers to look to 'savings' as the chief source of profits\"—here the phrase \"savings as the chief source of profits\" encapsulates the whole chapter—\"the remarkable difference in the consumption of coal per horse-power per hour, as indicated by the performance of the Cornish engines, as also the extraordinary economical performance of Woolf's double-cylinder engines, began to attract increased attention.\" The Cornish and double-cylinder engines gave \"a horse-power for every 3½ to 4 pounds of coal per hour, while the generality of cotton-mill engines were consuming 8 or 12 pounds per horse per hour.\" The differential—a factor of 2 or 3 in fuel consumption—was sufficient to induce mill-owners and engine-makers to adopt the improved methods. The stages of improvement are listed: (1) covering exposed boiler surfaces with \"thick blankets of felt, and brick and plaster\"—preventing heat loss; (2) protecting steam-pipes similarly; (3) felting and casing the engine cylinder in wood; (4) \"the use of 'high steam'\"—raising boiler pressure from 4, 6, or 8 lbs. per square inch to 14 or 20 lbs., then to 30, 40, 50, 60, or 70 lbs., with corresponding economy of fuel; (5) the \"expansion system,\" using high-pressure steam in a small cylinder and passing it into a low-pressure cylinder about four times the area of the former (the Woolf engine); (6) introducing this system into existing engines by connecting a high-pressure engine to a condensing engine so the waste steam of the former works the latter. Nasmyth's overall assessment: \"I am confident, however, ... that from the same weight of steam-engine machinery we are now obtaining at least 50 per cent more duty or work performed on the average, and that in many cases, the identical steam-engines which in the days of the restricted speed of 220 feet per minute yielded 50 horse-power, are now yielding upwards of 100.\" Finally: \"The very economical results derived from the employment of high-pressure steam in working condensing steam-engines, together with the much higher power required by mill extensions from the same engines, has within the last three years led to the adoption of tubular boilers, yielding a much more economical result than those formerly employed in generating steam for mill engines\" ( Reports , October 1852, pp. 23–7). The theoretical significance of the Nasmyth passage is enormous. It shows that economy in constant capital is not primarily about buying cheap machines; it is about using the same fixed capital more intensively and efficiently . The same engine that once yielded 50 horsepower now yields 100. This is the purest illustration of the chapter's claim that even if the value of the fixed capital remains the same, its use-value can be dramatically increased—and this increased use-value translates into a higher rate of profit through the reduced cost per unit of output. Marx then extends the point to transmission mechanisms and working machines themselves, quoting a report: \"The rapid strides with which improvement in machinery has advanced within these few years have enabled manufacturers to increase production without additional moving power. The more economical application of labour has been rendered necessary by the diminished length of the working-day, and in most well-regulated mills an intelligent mind is always considering in what manner production can be increased with decreased expenditure.\" The illustration is striking: \"I have before me a statement, kindly prepared by a very intelligent gentleman in my district, showing the number of hands employed, their ages, the machines at work, and the wages paid from 1840 to the present time. In October 1840, his firm employed 600 hands, of whom 200 were under 13 years of age. In October last, 350 hands were employed, of whom 60 only were under 13; the same number of machines, within very few, were at work, and the same sum in wages was paid at both periods\" (Redgrave's Report, Reports , October 1852, pp. 58–9). This is an extremely important datum: the same number of machines , the same total wage bill , but 350 instead of 600 workers , and 60 instead of 200 children . The improvement in machinery means that fewer workers—and fewer children—can produce the same output for the same total wage, meaning the wage per worker has risen but the cost per unit of output has fallen, and the surplus-value extracted has increased. This is the labour-saving effect of machinery in its most dramatic quantitative form. 2.20 Economy on Buildings and the Architectural Fix Marx closes the section on power by noting that \"these improvements in machinery show their full effect only when they are installed in new and purpose-built factory buildings.\" He quotes a cotton spinner's evidence ( Reports , October 1863, pp. 109, 110): \"As regards the improvement made in machinery, I may say in the first place that a great advance has been made in the construction of mills adapted to receive improved machinery ... In the bottom room I double all my yarn, and upon that single floor I shall put 29,000 doubling spindles. I effect a saving of labour in the room and shed of at least 10 per cent, not so much from any improvement in the principle of doubling yarn, but from a concentration of machinery under a single management; and I am enabled to drive the said number of spindles by one single shaft, a saving in shafting, compared with what other firms have to use to work the same number of spindles, of 60 per cent, in some cases 80 per cent. There is a large saving in oil, and shafting, and in grease ... With superior mill arrangements and improved machinery, at the lowest estimate I have effected a saving in labour of 10 per cent, a great saving in power, coal, oil, tallow, shafting and strapping.\" The significance is that the buildings themselves —the design of the factory—embed economies. The concentration of spindles under a single management, the single driving shaft, the layout of the rooms—all of these are \"architectural\" economies that lower the value of constant capital per unit of output. This is another illustration of \"economy in the employment \" of constant capital: the same total plant, arranged differently, produces more. 2.21 Utilization of the Refuse of Production Marx now returns to the theme announced earlier and treats it systematically. \"As the capitalist mode of production extends, so also does the utilization of the refuse left behind by production and consumption.\" He distinguishes: - Refuse of production : \"the waste products of industry and agriculture\"—e.g., in the chemical industry, \"the by-product which gets lost if production is only on a small scale\"; in machinery production, \"the heap of iron filings that appears to be waste but is then used again as raw material for iron production.\" - Refuse of consumption : \"both the excrement produced by man's natural metabolism and the form in which useful articles survive after use has been made of them\"—e.g., \"remains of clothing in the form of rags.\" These are \"of the greatest importance for agriculture.\" Marx comments on the wastefulness even of this potentially productive resource: \"But there is a colossal wastage in the capitalist economy in proportion to their actual use. In London, for example, they can do nothing better with the excrement produced by 4½ million people than pollute the Thames with it, at monstrous expense.\" This is an early statement of what modern environmental economics calls a negative externality or failure of closed-loop resource cycling : capital, having no direct incentive to recover waste whose returns are diffuse and long-term, simply dumps it, imposing enormous social costs. \"The increase in the cost of raw materials, of course, provides the incentive to make use of waste products.\" Here we see the dialectical relationship between scarcity (rising raw material prices) and innovation (development of waste re-utilization). Marx enumerates \"the general conditions for this re-utilization\": 1. \"The massive presence of this refuse, a thing which results only when labour is carried on on a large scale.\" 2. \"The improvement of machines, so that materials that were previously unusable in their given form are converted into a form suitable for new production.\" 3. \"Scientific progress—especially in chemistry, which discovers the useful properties of such waste products.\" He acknowledges a partial exception: \"Of course, great economies of this kind can also be found in the small-scale, almost horticultural agriculture carried on in Lombardy, southern China and Japan.\" But the exception proves the rule: \"In general, however, agricultural productivity is obtained in this system only at the cost of a great prodigality in human labour-power withdrawn from other spheres of production.\" That is, pre-capitalist intensive agriculture achieves material economy by squandering human labour —precisely the reverse trade-off from capitalist production. Marx then turns to concrete industries. In the Factory Report of October 1863 , the unwillingness of English and Irish farmers to grow flax is explained by prodigious waste at the \"little water scutch mills\": \"the waste in cotton is comparatively small, but in flax very large. The efficiency of water steeping and of good machine scutching will reduce this disadvantage very considerably ... Flax [is] scutched in Ireland in a most shameful way, and a large percentage [is] actually lost by it, equal to 28 or 30 per cent\" ( Reports , 31 October 1863, pp. 139, 142). Marx notes, \"All of this could be avoided by the use of better machines.\" The oakum waste: \"I have been informed with regard to some of the scutch mills in Ireland, that the waste made at them has often been used by the scutchers to burn on their fires at home, and yet it is very valuable\" (p. 140). Capitalist rationality is uneven: waste that could be profitably recycled is burned because no infrastructure exists to recover it. The wool industry was more advanced than linen: \"It was once the common practice to decry the preparation of waste and woollen rags for re-manufacture, but the prejudice has entirely subsided as regards the shoddy trade, which has become an important branch of the woollen trade of Yorkshire.\" The transformation in the price of woollen rags is dramatic: \"Thirty years since, woollen rags ... would average about £4 4s. per ton ... within the last few years they have become worth £44 per ton\"—a tenfold increase. The reason: \"the demand for them has so increased that means have been found for utilizing the rags of fabrics of cotton and wool mixed by destroying the cotton and leaving the wool intact, and now thousands of operatives are engaged in the manufacture of shoddy, from which the consumer has greatly benefited in being able to purchase cloth of a fair and average quality at a very moderate price\" ( Reports , 31 October 1863, p. 107). Marx then immediately undercuts the \"benefit.\" \"By the end of 1862, rejuvenated shoddy already accounted for a third of all wool used by English industry ( Reports , 31 October 1862, p. 81). The 'great benefit' for the 'consumer' was that his woollen clothes took only a third of the previous time to wear out and a sixth of the time to become threadbare.\" The cheapness is achieved at the expense of durability : the consumer buys cheaper cloth but replaces it six times as often. The appearance of cheapness masks a deterioration in quality. This is a proto-critique of \"planned obsolescence\" avant la lettre. The English silk industry \"followed the same downward path. Between 1839 and 1862 the use of genuine raw silk declined somewhat, while that of silk waste doubled. Improved machinery made it possible to manufacture silk that could be used for many purposes out of what had previously been a quite valueless material.\" The most striking example is the chemical industry: \"Not only does this make use of its own waste products by finding new applications for them, but it also employs those of a great range of other industries and converts coal-tar, for example, which was previously almost useless, into aniline dyes, alizarin and most recently also into medicines.\" The chemical industry is the model of reflexive waste recovery: it recycles not only its own by-products but those of other industries. Marx then draws an analytical distinction: \"This economy in the refuse of production, achieved by re-use, should be distinguished from economy in the creation of waste, i.e. reduction of the refuse of production to its minimum and the maximum direct use of all raw and ancillary materials engaged in production.\" There are thus two distinct modes of \"economy\" with respect to waste: (1) re-use of what has already become waste, and (2) prevention of waste at the source. Reduction of waste depends on several factors. \"Reduction in waste is partly brought about by the quality of the machinery used. Oil, soap, etc. are saved in proportion to the more precise working and better polishing of the machine components. This concerns the ancillary materials. The most important thing, however, is that it depends on the quality of the machines and tools that are used whether a greater or lesser part of the raw material is transformed into waste by the production process.\" Finally, waste depends on \"the quality of the raw material itself. This in turn depends partly on the development of the extractive industries and of agriculture, by which these raw materials are produced (thus it depends on the advance of civilization in general), partly on the development of the processing which the raw material undergoes before its entry into manufacture.\" Marx closes the section with a long quotation from Dureau de la Malle's Économie politique des Romains (Paris, 1840, I, pp. 280–81), which documents the historical improvement in corn milling. Parmentier showed that since the age of Louis XIV, French milling had improved so much that \"the new mills can supply up to half as much again in the way of bread.\" The annual consumption of corn in Paris was originally 4 setiers per capita, later 3, then 2, \"while today it is only 1½ setiers or approximately 342 lbs.\" In the Perche region, \"the crudely constructed mills with their millstones of granite and trap rock have generally been rebuilt according to the laws of mechanics ... Good millstones from La Ferté have been installed, corn has been milled twice over, the milling sack has been made to move in a circle, and the amount of flour produced is a sixth greater from the same quantity of corn.\" Dureau de la Malle explains Roman prices by \"the mills of the time, which were still imperfect and in a state of infancy, and the substantial milling costs to which this gave rise.\" The historical lesson is that technical progress in the processing of raw materials is itself a source of economy in constant capital—and that the \"advance of civilization\" in this domain is a social product, not an attribute of any individual capitalist. 2.22 Economy through Inventions: Universal vs. Communal Labour The final section of the chapter returns to synthesis. \"These savings in the use of fixed capital, as we said earlier, are the result of the way the conditions of labour have been applied on a large scale. In short, the way in which they serve as conditions of directly social, socialized labour, of direct cooperation within the production process.\" Marx then states three conditions under which economies become possible: 1. \"This is firstly the only condition on which mechanical and chemical discoveries can be applied without increasing the price of commodities, and this is always the sine qua non.\" That is, large-scale production is the precondition for applying discoveries at all—because only large-scale production can absorb the fixed costs and spread them across enough output. 2. \"Next, it is only with production on a large scale that we can have the economy that arises from productive consumption in common.\" 3. \"Finally, however, it is only the experience of the combined worker that discovers and demonstrates how inventions already made can most simply be developed, how to overcome the practical frictions that arise in putting the theory into practice—its application to the production process, and so on.\" This third point is critical: the workers themselves , as the collective laborer, are the ones who develop the practical application of inventions. The engineers and scientists devise the theory; the workers make it work. This is a fundamental Marxian claim about the social character of technological innovation: the intellectual achievements of civilization are appropriated by capital, but they belong in principle to the collective laborer. Marx then introduces a key distinction: \"We must distinguish here, incidentally, between universal labour and communal labour. They both play their part in the production process, and merge into one another, but they are each different as well. Universal labour is all scientific work, all discovery and invention. It is brought about partly by the cooperation of men now living, but partly also by building on earlier work. Communal labour, however, simply involves the direct cooperation of individuals.\" The distinction is between: - Universal labour ( allgemeine Arbeit ): scientific work, discovery, invention—cumulative across generations, decentralized, the product of humanity's accumulated intellectual effort. Universal labour is temporally distributed: it builds on the past, it is the legacy of all previous generations. - Communal labour ( gemeinschaftliche Arbeit ): the direct cooperation of individuals in the production process— spatially concentrated, present-tense, coordinated among living workers. Both are social forms of labour, but they operate differently. Universal labour provides the discoveries; communal labour puts them into practice. Between them, they constitute the \"social productivity of labour\" that capital appropriates. Marx closes with two observed facts that confirm this analysis: (1) \"The great difference in costs between the first construction of a new machine and its reproduction. See Ure and Babbage.\" The footnote identifies Charles Babbage (1792–1871), \"best remembered as the inventor of the first calculating machine\"—which was an early programmable mechanical computer—and his book On the Economy of Machinery and Manufactures (London, 1832); and Andrew Ure (1778–1857), whose Philosophy of Manufactures (1835) Marx \"considered the best work of its time on large-scale industry,\" frequently cited in Volume 1. The point: the first machine is enormously expensive to design and produce, but the second costs almost nothing by comparison. This is an early statement of what would today be called the sunk cost of innovation and the learning curve / increasing returns of technology. (2) \"The much greater costs that are always involved in an enterprise based on new inventions, compared with later establishments that rise up on its ruins, ex suis ossibus \" (from its bones). Marx explains: \"The extent of this is so great that the pioneering entrepreneurs generally go bankrupt, and it is only their successors who flourish, thanks to their possession of cheaper buildings, machinery etc. Thus it is generally the most worthless and wretched kind of money-capitalists that draw the greatest profit from all new developments of the universal labour of the human spirit and their social application by combined labour.\" This is a profound and bitter conclusion. The pioneers of innovation—those who take the risk and bear the cost—generally fail; their successors, who buy the depreciated plant and the now-proven technology, reap the profits. The \"most worthless and wretched kind of money-capitalists\"—the financiers and speculators—profit most from the accumulated \"universal labour of the human spirit.\" The chapter thus ends on a note of radical alienation : the collective intellectual achievement of humanity, applied by the combined laborer, is captured by a parasitic layer of money capitalists who contributed nothing to it. This is the ultimate form of the fetishism that Marx diagnosed at the beginning of the chapter: the social productivity of labour appears as a power of capital—specifically, of finance capital—entirely severed from any contribution to production. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles and Systemic Laws The structural relationship between constant capital and the rate of profit. The chapter establishes a fundamental principle of Marxian political economy: because profit is calculated on the total capital advanced, not merely on the variable capital, any reduction in the value of constant capital raises the rate of profit even when surplus-value and its rate remain constant. The limiting case p' = s' (when c = 0) defines a maximum rate of profit; every unit of value added to c drives p' below this maximum. This is the basis for understanding the organic composition of capital and the tendency of the rate of profit to fall—and for identifying economy in constant capital as one of the most important counteracting tendencies. The social character of productivity. All economy in constant capital arises from the social character of labour—cooperation, the division of labour, and the accumulated intellectual labour of science. This is not \"social\" in the sense of being produced by society as a whole in some vague way, but in the concrete sense of being produced by the combined worker and by the cross-branch interdependence of productivity. The capitalist appropriates as \"his\" power what is in fact a power of social labor. This is the foundation of Marx's theory of fetishism as applied to capital: the social power of labour appears as an attribute of the thing (capital), not of the labor that gives it life. The distinction between production and employment of constant capital. Economies in constant capital can arise either from cheapening the production of constant capital (through rising productivity in the branches that make machines, materials, and power sources) or from the employment of constant capital (through cooperation, scale, and design). The first is external to the individual capitalist, located in the \"external department\" of the social division of labour; the second is internal to the firm but relies on the social cooperation of workers within the firm. The dialectic of economy and squandering. The same forces that generate economy in constant capital also generate squandering of human life and health. The social character of labour that permits the collective consumption of buildings, machinery, and power also permits the overcrowding, poor ventilation, and dangerous machinery that destroy workers' lives. Capitalist \"economy\" is thus the coincidence of the two: the maximum economy of objectified labour coincides with the maximum squandering of living labour. The quality–quantity interplay. The chapter develops a rich analysis of how the quality of raw materials and machinery affects not only the rate of profit (through waste and depreciation) but also the rate of surplus-value (through the time required to process them) and the accumulation of capital (through the productivity of the labor applied). The value of constant capital matters, but so does its use-value —its appropriateness to its purpose. This is a corrective to any purely value-theoretic reading of capital. The fundamental distinction between universal and communal labour. Universal labour—scientific discovery, cumulative across generations, decentralized—provides the technical possibility of economy. Communal labour—direct cooperation in the present, coordinated among living workers—provides the practical realization of that possibility. Both are social forms of labour, both are appropriated by capital, and both merge into one another in the production process. But their distinction clarifies the temporal dimension of social productivity: capital accumulates not only the labor of its own workers but the entire intellectual heritage of humanity—and it profits from that heritage in ways that its current workers cannot claim. Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications For the theory of the falling rate of profit. Chapter 5 is essential to Marx's overall argument in Volume 3. The tendency of the rate of profit to fall is grounded in the rising organic composition of capital (c/v), but this tendency is offset by the very economies in constant capital that Chapter 5 analyzes. The chapter thus protects the theory from a crude fatalism: capital is not simply hurtling toward its own collapse; it develops powerful counteracting mechanisms. But these mechanisms are themselves contradictory—they are based on the appropriation of social productivity, on the intensification of exploitation, and on the squandering of human life. The chapter thus shows both the resilience and the inhumanity of the capitalist mode of production. For the analysis of fixed capital and turnover. The chapter extends the analysis of Volume 2's theory of turnover into the domain of the profit rate. Fixed capital is a lump whose turnover is a function not only of its physical lifespan but of the social intensity of its use . Prolonging the working day, running machinery faster, introducing continuous processes—all of these reduce the turnover period and raise the annual rate of profit. The Nasmyth passage on \"letting the engine away\" is a stunning illustration: by running the same engine faster, the capitalist doubles its useful effect without adding to its value. This is the dialectic of fixed capital: it is the most expensive element of constant capital, but it is also the element most susceptible to intensity-of-use economies. For the analysis of technology and innovation. The chapter contains one of Marx's most sophisticated treatments of technological change. It distinguishes between: - Innovation in the means of production (machines, power sources) that cheapens constant capital for user branches; - Innovation in manufacturing techniques that reduces waste and improves quality; - Innovation in the processing of raw materials (corn milling, flax scutching), affecting the quality of inputs; - Innovation in chemistry that discovers new uses for waste products. It also contains an early theory of the cost structure of innovation : new machines are enormously expensive to design and produce but cheap to reproduce; pioneer enterprises generally fail because they bear the cost of establishing new technologies, while their successors—often \"the most worthless and wretched kind of money-capitalists\"—reap the profits. This has obvious relevance to modern debates about innovation, intellectual property, and financialization. For environmental and ecological thought. The section on refuse of production anticipates many themes of modern ecological economics. The \"colossal wastage\" of London's excrement into the Thames, the failure to recover valuable by-products, the decline in quality of woollen cloth due to shoddy—all of these are early examples of the environmental contradictions of capitalism. The dialectic of \"economy in the creation of waste\" versus \"economy in the re-use of waste\" anticipates the modern distinction between waste prevention and waste recycling. And Marx's observation that pre-capitalist \"horticultural agriculture\" achieves material economy only at the cost of prodigality in human labor-power anticipates the modern concept of \"social metabolism\" and the ecological critique of industrial agriculture. For the critique of political economy and ideology. The chapter is a case study in the fetishism of capital. The economy in constant capital appears to the capitalist as a \"power inherent in capital\"—a \"method specific to and characteristic of the capitalist mode of production.\" In reality, it is a product of social labor and the social division of labour. The ideology that presents economy as a personal achievement of the capitalist—or as a technical attribute of \"modern industry\"—is the semblance that corresponds to the capital relation. Marx's analysis strips away the semblance and reveals the underlying social reality: the collective worker, the social division of labour, and the accumulated intellectual labour of humanity are the true sources of the \"economy\" that the capitalist claims as his own. For the critique of reformism and the analysis of social struggle. The chapter shows that the improvement of working conditions is not achieved by the voluntary benevolence of capital but through: - Legal regulation : the appointment of inspectors (which caused accident rates to fall), the passage of Factory Acts (even if initially inadequate), and the threat of liability judgments. - Worker resistance : some workers \"managed to extract heavy legal compensation for lost arms\" and thereby forced safer practices. - Technological changes that build safety into machinery at no extra cost to capital (the \"new machines which were already provided with safety-guards\" that could \"leave [safety] in existence as they did not cost them any extra\"). The chapter also shows that regulation is vulnerable to capture : the Justices of the Peace who were supposed to enforce the safety laws were factory-owners themselves; the 1856 Act was rewritten to make it \"almost impossible for the factory-owners to lose a case\"; and the administrators of the Nuisances Removal Acts were apparently disinclined to help workers. The lesson—drawn explicitly by Dr Simon—is that \"workpeople are practically unable to exact [their] sanitary justice for themselves,\" and cannot rely on the appointed administrators. This is a sober assessment of the possibilities and limits of reform under capitalism. For the historical materialist method. The chapter demonstrates Marx's method in action: taking a narrowly economic question (the rate of profit) and showing how it opens onto a whole field of social relations—the social division of labour, cooperation, technological innovation, state regulation, class struggle, the health and lives of workers, the depletion of natural resources. The method is dialectical : it treats economic categories as historically specific social relations, not as natural things; it sees contradictions as driving forces; it refuses to separate the \"economic\" from the \"social\" or the \"human.\" And it is materialist : it grounds the analysis in the concrete conditions of production—mines, factories, workshops, steam engines—not in abstract formulas. For the theory of history. The chapter's most sweeping claim is that \"it is only through the most tremendous waste of individual development that the development of humanity in general is secured and pursued, in that epoch of history that directly precedes the conscious reconstruction of human society.\" This is a Hegelian-Marxian statement of the historical role of capitalism: it develops the productive forces of humanity at enormous cost to individual human beings, and it thereby creates the material preconditions for a future society in which that development would be consciously directed. The chapter thus implies a philosophy of history : the contradictions of capitalism are not merely defects but productive forces that prepare the way for a higher social form. The \"conscious reconstruction of human society\" is a vision of socialism as the rational appropriation of what capital has developed unconsciously—the conscious direction of social productivity toward human development rather than profit. Concluding Reflection Chapter 5 is, at its deepest level, an essay on the dialectic of social cooperation . Modern production is cooperation: workers cooperate with one another in the factory; branches of production cooperate through the social division of labour; generations cooperate through the accumulation of scientific and technical knowledge. This cooperation is the source of enormous productivity—and of the cheapening of constant capital that raises the rate of profit. But under capitalism, cooperation is not control : workers do not own the means of production, do not direct the process, do not decide how productivity is used. The cooperation is organized by and for capital, and its fruits are taken by capital. What appears as \"the economy of capital\" is in fact the economy of the collective worker, expropriated. This is why the chapter pivots again and again between analysis and indictment. The mechanical, engineering, and account-book facts of cheapened constant capital are never presented in isolation. They are always accompanied by the reality of the workers' degraded conditions—the crowded workshops, the unsafe shafts, the unventilated rooms, the overtaxed bodies, the sacrificed children. The dialectical unity of economy and waste is the central insight of the chapter, and it is what makes it not merely an economic analysis but a work of social criticism. Marx's conclusion is not optimistic about the prospects of reform within capitalism. He documents that improvement comes only through struggle—through inspectors, laws, liability, and organized resistance—and that even then it is fragile, vulnerable to repeal, and slow. But he also shows that the socialist alternative is already prefigured in the cooperative factories of Rochdale, in which workers who own the means of production voluntarily economize on them. The chapter thus points beyond its own analysis toward the possibility of a rationally organized society in which the immense social productivity developed by capital would be consciously directed toward human development—\"the conscious reconstruction of human society.\" The final irony of the chapter is that the most parasitic recipients of this socially produced productivity are the financiers—\"the most worthless and wretched kind of money-capitalists\"—who draw \"the greatest profit from all new developments of the universal labour of the human spirit.\" The collective intellectual achievement of humanity, accumulated over generations, applied by the combined laborer, is captured by a layer of speculators who contributed nothing to it. It is difficult to imagine a more complete expression of the fetishism that Marx set out to expose. Thus, Chapter 5 closes not on a technical formula but on a vision of social relations: the productive forces of humanity, developed through the cooperation of workers and the accumulated labor of generations, are privately appropriated and turned against the very people who created them. The task of a rational society—of the \"conscious reconstruction\" toward which history tends—will be to reclaim that which has been alienated, and to organize the immense productivity of cooperative labor in the interest of those who produce it. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 4 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 6"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 6", "title": "Chapter 6", "subtitle": "The Effect of Price Fluctuations", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 6/Chapter 6 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 12615, "read_time": "63 min read", "tags": ["Cost Price & Rate of Profit", "Capital Accumulation & Crises"], "keywords": ["Effect of Price Fluctuations on Profit Rate", "Raw Material Price Shocks (Cotton Crises)", "Depreciation and Appreciation of Capital Components", "Release and Tying-Up of Capital from Cost Swings", "Speculative Hoarding and Inventory Devaluation"], "text": "Chapter 6 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 12,595 words ⏱️ ~63 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 6: The Effect of Changes in Price An Exhaustive Deconstruction and Analysis 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem: How Do Price Fluctuations Move the Rate of Profit? Chapter 6 of Capital , Volume 3, occupies a deceptively narrow niche in Marx's larger architecture: it asks what happens to the rate of profit when the prices of the material inputs to production rise or fall, holding the exploitation of labour constant. The chapter's surface is empirical — a long cascade of Factory Inspector reports, cotton statistics, wool prices, and flax distempers — but its underlying engine is a single analytical claim: the rate of profit is a ratio (p′ = s/C), and therefore anything that changes the magnitude of the denominator C, even while surplus-value and the rate of surplus-value remain untouched, must move the rate of profit. Raw materials are a major component of constant capital c, and c is a major component of C. Hence raw-material prices become a direct, autonomous determinant of the profit rate, entirely separate from the class struggle over wages. The intellectual objective is twofold. First, Marx wants to isolate this channel — to demonstrate analytically that price fluctuations of raw and ancillary materials affect the rate of profit in the opposite direction to the movement of those prices, other things equal. Second, he wants to dissolve a confusion he attributes to both practical men and great theorists: those who feel the influence of raw-material prices (like Torrens) cannot explain it theoretically, while those who hold firmly to general principles (like Ricardo) fail to recognise the influence of world trade on the profit rate. The chapter exists to give the correct theoretical account of a phenomenon that practical spinners, factory inspectors, and free-traders all testified to but nobody had properly conceptualised. Why This Chapter Matters in Marx's Framework In the preceding chapters of Volume 3, Marx has established the transformation of surplus-value into profit, the concepts of cost-price and profit, and the difference between the rate of surplus-value (s/v) and the rate of profit (s/C = s/(c+v)). Chapter 5 examined economy in the use of constant capital. Chapter 6 now extends that analysis to the revaluation and devaluation of capital caused by price changes in its material elements. The move is crucial: it shows that the rate of profit is not simply a function of exploitation (s/v), nor even of the organic composition of capital in some static sense, but is also exposed to the contingent, historically volatile prices of organic raw materials — agricultural products whose supply is subject to organic laws, harvests, seasons, and the uneven development of world trade. This has profound consequences: it introduces a source of systemic turbulence into capitalist reproduction, explains why crises can originate in the sphere of raw-material provision, and grounds Marx's claim that capitalist production is inherently hostile to rational agriculture. The chapter also introduces two new analytical categories — the release and tying-up of capital and the revaluation and devaluation of capital — which recur throughout Volume 3 and which are essential for understanding how capitalist crises can appear as if the profit rate (or even the mass of profit) moved independently of surplus-value. These categories are Marx's tool for explaining why the surface phenomena of the credit system, world-market competition, and crises are so \"crazy\" (verrückt): the mass of profit and the rate of profit can genuinely fluctuate while the underlying extraction of surplus labour is unchanged, because capital values and releases shift beneath them. Finally, the chapter serves an empirical-rhetorical purpose. By the end, Marx has assembled the most sustained documentary indictment in Volume 3: the cotton famine of 1861–65 treated as a laboratory of capitalist behaviour under raw-material scarcity. It is here that abstraction meets the wage lists of starving weavers, the \"experiments in corpore vili,\" the Relief Committees acting as \"guard-dogs,\" and the public works that resurrect the Ateliers nationaux of 1848 — this time for the profit of the bourgeoisie. The chapter is thus simultaneously a theorem and a prosecution. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Framing Assumption: Freezing the Rate of Surplus-Value Marx opens with a methodological declaration that governs the entire chapter: \"Here, as before, we assume there is no change in the rate of surplus-value. This is a necessary assumption, if we are to investigate the situation in its pure form.\" This is the classic ceteris paribus move, but with a specific purpose: to isolate the effect of variations in c (constant capital) on the profit rate, so that whatever movements in p′ are observed cannot be attributed to a changed intensity of exploitation. The assumption is not a claim that s′ never changes in reality — indeed, Marx immediately concedes that a capital could employ more or fewer workers as a result of price-induced contraction or expansion, which would change the mass of surplus-value even at constant s′. But he sets that \"side-effect\" aside as a complication to be handled later, and insists that where such complications occur simultaneously with machinery improvements or wage changes, \"we simply have to combine (1) the effect that the variation in constant capital has on the profit rate, and (2) the effect that the variation in wages has on the profit rate. The result is then immediately given.\" This is additive decomposition — a signal that the chapter will proceed by analytic components whose separate effects can later be summed. The critical consequence he draws immediately: variations from raw-material prices affect the rate of profit \"even if they leave wages, and thus the rate and mass of surplus-value, completely undisturbed.\" Because p′ = s/C, they \"alter the value of C and therefore the value of the fraction as a whole.\" This is the hinge on which everything else swings. It gives rise to a striking claim of indifference : it is \"completely immaterial here — as distinct from what we found in considering surplus-value — in what spheres of production these variations take place.\" Unlike the value of labour-power (where what matters is whether the commodity is a means of subsistence for workers), raw-material price changes matter wherever they occur, even in luxury production — defined rigorously here as \"all production that is not required by the reproduction of labour-power.\" This is a direct corollary of the denominator logic: C is C regardless of what the produced commodity is destined for. Nuance and implicit counter-argument: This indifference claim is a marked contrast with the analysis of surplus-value, where the use of the product (wage-good vs. luxury) determines whether a change in its value raises or lowers surplus-value. Marx is signalling that the two channels — s/v and C — obey different logics. A reader accustomed to thinking that only wage-goods matter would be misled; the rate of profit is exposed to the whole price-structure of the material inputs of production, not merely to those that enter worker consumption. 2.2 The Scope of \"Raw Material\": Ancillary Materials and the Machinery Clarification Marx immediately broadens the category. \"Under raw material we also include the ancillary materials such as indigo, coal, gas, etc.\" This matters because some branches — mining, extractive industries, transport — use little raw material proper , yet are still exposed to price fluctuations through their ancillary inputs. And he adds a subtle clarification about machinery: machinery itself has a raw-material composition (iron, wood, leather), so its price is affected by fluctuations in the price of those materials. \"In as much as its price is raised by fluctuations in the price of the raw material of which it consists, or of the ancillary material that it needs in the course of its operation, the rate of profit falls in proportion to this, and vice versa.\" But then he deliberately narrows the field for the investigation that follows: he will confine himself \"to fluctuations in price of that raw material which actually goes into the process of production of the commodity,\" setting aside the raw material of machines and their ancillary operating materials. A single point is reserved for attention: the natural riches — iron, coal, wood — that constitute \"the main elements in the construction and use of machines, appear now as a natural fruit borne by capital and form an element in the determination of the rate of profit that is independent of the high or low level of wages.\" This is a proto-rental/natural-monopoly observation: the productivity of nature embedded in the means of production becomes a determinant of p′ that has nothing to do with labour costs. It connects forward to the theory of ground-rent, which Marx will treat later, and it quietly establishes that the profit rate has natural as well as social determinants. 2.3 The Inverse Law: Raw Material Prices Against the Rate of Profit The analytical core arrives quickly. \"Since the rate of profit is s/C or s/(c+v), it is clear that everything that gives rise to a change in the magnitude of c, and therefore of C, also brings about a change in the profit rate, even if s, v and their reciprocal relationship remain constant.\" Raw material is \"a major component of constant capital.\" So when the price of raw material falls by a sum d , \"c or c+v is changed to c − d or (c − d) + v, and the rate of profit rises.\" The explicit conclusion: \"As long as other circumstances are equal, the rate of profit falls or rises in the opposite direction to the price of the raw material.\" This is the chapter's central theorem — an inverse relationship between raw-material price and the rate of profit, holding s, v, and their ratio fixed. Let us write the mathematics out fully, since the OCR text is partly garbled here. Let: s = surplus-value (assumed constant in this thought-experiment) v = variable capital (assumed constant) c = constant capital, of which raw material is a major part C = c + v = total capital advanced p′ = s/C = s/(c + v) = the rate of profit Now suppose the price of raw material falls by d (where c > d > 0). Then c → c − d, so: p′₁ = s / [(c − d) + v] > s / (c + v) = p′₀ The rate of profit rises . Conversely, if the price of raw material rises by d: p′₂ = s / [(c + d) + v] < s / (c + v) = p′₀ The rate of profit falls . Hence d(p′)/d(c) < 0: p′ is a decreasing function of the price of constant capital, and since raw material is a major component of c, p′ moves inversely to raw-material prices. (The OCR's statement that \"the rate of profit falls\" when raw material price falls is a transcription error; the surrounding sentence and the entire logic of the chapter make the correct direction unambiguous.) Marx draws three important implications from this theorem immediately: The importance of cheap raw materials for industrial countries , \"even if variations in raw material prices were not accompanied by fluctuations in the product's orbit of sale, i.e. quite apart from the relationship between demand and supply.\" This detaches the argument from any supply-and-demand apparatus: the effect operates through the value-composition of capital, prior to any market-clearing adjustment. How foreign trade influences the rate of profit — \"irrespective of any effect that it has on wages by cheapening the necessary means of subsistence.\" This is a decisive point. The classical argument for free trade (and Ricardo's) was that cheapening wage-goods cheapens labour-power, raises s/v, and raises profit. Marx adds a second, independent channel: foreign trade slightly affects \"the prices of the raw or ancillary materials used in industry and agriculture,\" and through them it moves the rate of profit. The two channels are analytically distinct, and the second had been missed. A genealogy of theoretical error. \"The fact that any understanding of the rate of profit and its specific difference from the rate of surplus-value has been so completely lacking\" explains why (a) economists who emphasise the influence of raw material prices (Torrens) \"give this a quite false theoretical explanation,\" while (b) economists who hold firmly to general principles (Ricardo) \"fail to recognise the influence of such things as world trade on the profit rate.\" Marx here is doing what he does throughout Volume 3: correcting both an empirical school that intuits the phenomenon without theory and a theoretical school that loses the phenomenon in its principles. The footnote directs us to Torrens's An Essay on the Production of Wealth (1821), Ricardo's Principles , Chapter VI (\"On Profits\"), and Marx's own Theories of Surplus-Value , Part III, Chapter XX, I.b., pp. 71–9. 2.4 Policy Corollary: Corn Laws, Cotton Duties, and the Ten Hours Bill The theorem yields an immediate political-economic corollary: \"how important for industry is the abolition or reduction of import duties on raw materials.\" Marx notes that even the \"more rational\" protectionists accepted letting raw materials in freely, and that the English Free-Traders, alongside the repeal of the Corn Laws, made the abolition of the cotton duty \"a main preoccupation.\" He then produces an empirical illustration of the ancillary-material case using a material that is also a foodstuff: flour used in the cotton industry for smoothing the warp. R. H. Greg's 1837 calculation ( The Factory Question and the Ten Hours Bill , p. 115) is reported: Britain's 100,000 power-looms and 250,000 hand-looms consumed some 41 million pounds of flour annually for warp-sizing, plus a further third of that amount for bleaching and other processes. Greg puts the total value of flour so consumed at £342,000 per year over the preceding ten years. Comparison with Continental flour prices showed that the higher flour price forced on factory-owners by the corn duties amounted to £170,000 per year alone; for 1837 Greg estimates it at \"at least £200,000,\" and cites one single firm for which the excess price was £1,000 a year . The consequence, quoted from the Factory Inspectors' Reports (31 October 1848, p. 98): \"great manufacturers, thoughtful, calculating men of business, have said that ten hours' labour would be quite sufficient, if the Corn Laws were repealed.\" The point is that a foodstuff functioning as an ancillary material demonstrates the indifference-of-sphere argument in practice: flour mattered to cotton profit not (or not only) because it fed workers, but because it sized their warps. And the political sequel is grim and instructive: \"The Corn Laws were repealed, and the duties on cotton and other raw materials abolished as well. But scarcely had this been achieved when the factory-owners' opposition to the Ten Hours Bill became more violent than ever.\" When the Ten Hours Bill nonetheless became law, \"its first effect was an attempt at a general reduction of wages.\" This is Marx at his most dialectical: the removal of a raw-material tax relieves the profit rate through c; the capitalists then turn with renewed ferocity against the working-day limitation, because the other determinant of p′ — the rate of surplus-value — has now become the only one they can attack. The footnote cross-references Capital , Volume 1, pp. 395 ff., where Marx treats the post-Corn-Law wage offensives. 2.5 The Asymmetry Between Raw Material and Fixed Capital: A Crucial Structural Distinction Marx now draws a distinction that structures much of the chapter's later empirical reasoning: \"The value of the raw and ancillary materials goes at a single stroke into the value of the product for which they are used, while the value of the elements of fixed capital goes in only to the extent of their depreciation, and thus only gradually.\" Therefore the price of the product is affected to a much higher degree by the price of raw material than by that of fixed capital . But — and this is the sharp point — \"the rate of profit is determined by the total value of the capital applied, irrespective of how much of this is consumed or not.\" So we get a double asymmetry: On the product price side : raw materials transmit their value wholly and immediately; fixed capital only transfers a fragment (depreciation) per turnover. On the profit rate side : the denominator C counts the entire value of capital applied, consumed or not. This yields a further elaboration: since commodities are assumed (for now) to be sold at their values, \"the expansion or contraction of the market depends on the price of the individual commodity and stands in an inverse relationship to the rise or fall in this price.\" Hence a rise in raw-material price does not cause the manufactured product's price to rise in the same proportion, nor does a fall cause an equal fall. Consequently \"the rate of profit thus falls more sharply in the one case, and rises more sharply in the other, than would be the case if commodities were sold at their values.\" In other words, the market-price dynamics amplify the pure value-composition effect in a particular direction: the rate of profit is more volatile than the value analysis alone would suggest. This is a remarkable anticipation of themes later developed under \"counteracting influences\" to the tendency of the profit rate to fall, and it foreshadows the role of demand elasticity in transmitting raw-material shocks. 2.6 The Growing Weight of Raw Material in the Value of the Product Marx then develops a long-run compositional argument. As the productivity of labour develops, the size and value of the machines employed grows, \"but not in the same proportion as this productivity itself, i.e. the proportion to which these machines supply an increased product.\" In any branch that uses raw materials — \"wherever the object of labour is already the product of earlier labour\" — increasing productivity expresses itself in a greater mass of raw material absorbed by a given amount of labour: \"the increasing mass of raw material that is transformed into products ... in an hour, for example.\" The consequence is a relative shift in the value-composition of the commodity: The raw material component grows, because it enters \"as a whole,\" and The parts formed by machine depreciation and by newly added labour both \"constantly decline\" in each aliquot part of the product. This relative growth of the raw-material component occurs provided that it is not cancelled out by a corresponding decline in the raw material's value arising from the increasing productivity of the labour applied in its own creation . This caveat is important: Marx is not predicting that raw material necessarily becomes an ever-larger share of value — productivity gains in raw-material production itself may cheapen it. But the use-value mass of raw material per unit of labour grows inexorably, and this is what exposes the system to organic supply constraints (developed later). 2.7 Circulating Replacement, Waste, and the Interruption of Reproduction A further consequence follows from the fact that raw and ancillary materials \"just like wages, form components of the circulating capital and must therefore be constantly replaced out of each sale of the product,\" whereas \"as far as the machine is concerned it is only the depreciation that has to be replaced and at first only in the form of a reserve fund.\" Marx notes a subtlety: it is \"in no way so essential that each individual sale should contribute its part to this reserve fund, as long as we assume that the year's sale as a whole provides its annual share.\" From this, \"we see here again how a rise in the price of raw material can cut back or inhibit the entire reproduction process, since the price obtained by the commodity's sale no longer suffices to replace all of its elements; or it makes it impossible to continue the process on a scale that corresponds with its technical basis, so that either only a section of the machinery is being used, or the whole machinery cannot work for the full customary time.\" This is the reproduction-interruption mechanism : raw materials must be replaced in kind and in full out of each sale , whereas fixed capital only requires an annual bookkeeping provision. A sharp raw-material price spike can therefore generate a physical shortfall of circulating capital — and thus short-time working, idle machinery, and crisis — even when the enterprise is profitable on paper. The waste issue exemplifies this. \"The costs resulting from waste ... vary in direct proportion to the fluctuations in the price of the raw material, rising when this rises and falling when it falls.\" From the Reports (30 April 1850, p. 17): when cotton advances, the spinner's cost rises \"in a ratio beyond the advance actually paid, because the waste made in spinning coarse yarns is fully 15 per cent; and this rate, while it causes a loss of ¾d. per lb. on cotton at 3½d. per lb., brings up the loss to 1d. per lb. when cotton advances to 7d.\" During the American Civil War (Reports, October 1863, p. 106): with Surat cotton the waste is 25 per cent, so \"the cost of the cotton to the spinner is enhanced one-fourth before he has manufactured it,\" and when cotton costs 2s. per lb., the waste loss equals 6d. per lb. Engels' editorial footnote 14 corrects the arithmetic — the loss should be 3d. rather than 6d., the percentage having been misapplied — but affirms that the proportion of waste was often significantly higher than before on American cotton shipped to Europe during the war's later years. The waste analysis is doubly important. First, it shows that a rise in raw-material price is not a linear cost increase: waste-losses magnify the effective burden. Second, it demonstrates how the technical quality of the raw material — a use-value property — becomes an economic determinant of the profit rate, another instance of the use-value/value dialectic that runs through the chapter. 2.8 Definitions: Revaluation/Devaluation, Release/Tying-up Marx transitions to Section 2 with an honest statement of scope: \"The phenomena under investigation in this chapter assume for their full development the credit system and competition on the world market, the latter being the very basis and living atmosphere of the capitalist mode of production.\" Since those \"concrete forms\" can be \"comprehensively depicted only after the general nature of capital is understood,\" they are deferred to \"a possible continuation\" (see p. 426). Nonetheless, the phenomena can \"still be discussed here in broad lines,\" because they are inter-related with the rate and mass of profit and because they create the appearance that not only the rate but also the mass of profit can move independently of surplus-value. Then the definitions: Revaluation and devaluation are \"self-explanatory\": \"the capital present increases or decreases in value as the result of certain general economic conditions (since what is involved here is not the particular fate of one single private capital), i.e. that the value of the capital advanced to production rises or falls independently of its valorization by the surplus labour it employs.\" The key phrase is general economic conditions — this is a systemic, not idiosyncratic, phenomenon. Tying-up of capital : \"out of the total value of the product, a certain additional proportion must be transformed back into the elements of constant or variable capital, if production is to continue on its old scale.\" Release of capital : \"a part of the product's total value which previously had to be transformed back into either constant or variable capital becomes superfluous for the continuation of production on the old scale and is now available for other purposes.\" Crucially, Marx distinguishes the release/tying-up of capital from that of revenue . If annual surplus-value on a capital C = x, a cheapening of consumption goods may make x − a sufficient to procure the same satisfactions as before; then \"a portion of the capitalist's revenue = a is thus set free\" and can expand consumption or be accumulated. Conversely, if x + a is needed, expenditure must be restricted or previously accumulated income spent as revenue. This distinction is essential: revenue-release is about the capitalist's consumption; capital-release is about the funds available to continue and expand production. Confusing them would produce a category error, and Marx's insistence here shows how carefully he is building the analytical scaffolding for later chapters on credit and crises. He then sets out the possible loci of revaluation/devaluation: it may affect constant or variable capital or both ; and within constant capital, fixed or circulating (raw material) or both . 2.9 The \"Unconditionally Correct\" Law — and Its Limits for Already-Functioning Capital Marx restates the theorem from Section 1: \"with other things being equal, the rate of profit varies inversely as the value of the raw material.\" He adds the qualification: \"This law is unconditionally correct for capital that is newly engaged in a certain business and where the investment of capital, the transformation of money into productive capital, takes place for the first time.\" But for already functioning capital , the situation is more complex. This is because only a portion of capital is in the sphere of production; a large part is in the sphere of circulation, distributed as: Commodity capital on the market, awaiting transformation into money; Money capital , awaiting transformation back into the conditions of production; Capital within the sphere of production, partly as means of production (raw material, ancillary material, semi-finished articles, machinery, fixed capital), partly as products still in the course of completion . The effect of a price change \"depends here very largely on the respective proportions of these components.\" Simplifying by leaving fixed capital out of account, Marx considers the part of constant capital consisting of raw and ancillary materials plus commodities in preparation and finished form on the market. The key mechanism is the retrospective revaluation of stocks. If cotton rises in price, \"the price of cotton goods rises as well: both semi-finished goods such as yarn, and finished products such as cloth, etc.\" And \"cotton that has not yet been worked up, but is still in the warehouse, rises just as much in value as cotton that is in the course of manufacture.\" Why? Because \"as the retrospective expression of more labour-time, this cotton adds a higher value to the product which it goes into as a component than it possessed originally and the capitalist paid for it.\" Consequently, if a raw-material price rise occurs when there are significant stocks of finished goods on the market, \"the value of these commodities rises and there is a corresponding increase in the value of the capital involved. The same applies to stocks of raw material, etc. in the hands of the producers.\" This revaluation can compensate the individual capitalist or even a whole sphere \"— even more than compensate, perhaps — for the fall in the rate of profit that follows from the raw material's rise in price.\" But competition complicates the picture (noted \"for the sake of completeness\"): 1. Substantial stocks of raw material in the warehouse \"counteract the price increase arising from the conditions of their production\" (i.e., the price rise at source is dampened by existing inventory); 2. If semi-finished or finished goods on the market \"press heavily on the supply,\" they may prevent the price of these goods from rising in proportion to the price of their raw material. The reverse holds for a fall in raw-material prices: commodities on the market, goods in preparation, and raw-material stocks are all devalued, \"and this counteracts the simultaneous rise in the rate of profit.\" The smaller the stocks at the end of the business year — \"at the time when raw materials are supplied afresh on a massive scale (or, in the case of agricultural production, after the harvest)\" — the more visible the effect of a raw-material price change. Marx then makes an important methodological concession: \"Our whole investigation has proceeded from the assumption that any rise or fall in prices is an expression of real fluctuations in value. But since we are dealing here with the effect that these price fluctuations have on the profit rate, it is actually a matter of indifference what their basis might be. The present argument is just as valid if prices rise or fall not as a result of fluctuations in value, but rather as a result of the intervention of the credit system, competition, etc.\" The theorem is robust to the underlying cause of the price change — a point of enormous importance for extending the analysis to the credit system later. A crucial accounting paradox then appears: \"Since the rate of profit is equal to the proportionate excess in the value of the product over the value of the total capital advanced, an increase in the rate of profit that arose from a devaluation of the capital advanced would involve a loss in capital value, while a decline in the profit rate that arose from a rise in value of the capital advanced could well involve a gain.\" This is a decisive insight: the rate of profit and the value of capital can move in opposite directions such that appearances deceive . A capitalist whose capital is devalued may enjoy a higher p′ while being poorer in value terms; one whose capital is revalued may suffer a lower p′ while becoming richer. This is the seed of the \"illusory\" appearance of profit movements independent of surplus-value. 2.10 Devaluation of Fixed Capital: Moral Depreciation and Second Owners For machinery and fixed capital generally, Marx says, the revaluation \"that takes place here and particularly affects buildings, land, etc. cannot be explained without the theory of ground-rent and thus does not belong here.\" However, he identifies two general points relevant to devaluation : (1) Technological obsolescence: \"The constant improvements which rob existing machinery, factories, etc. of a part of their use-value, and therefore also their exchange-value.\" This is particularly significant \"at times when new machinery is first introduced, before it has reached a certain degree of maturity, and where it thus constantly becomes outmoded before it has had time to reproduce its value.\" This is \"one of the reasons for the unlimited extension of working hours that is usual in periods of this kind, work based on alternating day and night shifts, so that the value of the machines is reproduced without too great costs having to be borne for wear and tear.\" If the short working life of the machines were not counter-balanced this way, \"they would transfer too great a portion of their value to the product in the way of moral depreciation and would not even be able to compete with handicraft production.\" The footnote (15) cites Babbage for examples and notes the \"customary expedient — reduction of wages — was applied here too,\" so that this constant devaluation has a completely different effect from the \"harmony\" that Mr Carey dreams of. The concept of moral depreciation ( moralischer Verschleiss ) — the footnote explains the term, referring to Capital Volume 2, pp. 250, 264 — is here extended: it is the loss of value of fixed capital through obsolescence or through cheaper reproduction, and it forces capitalists into intensified exploitation of labour (longer hours, wage cuts) to compensate. (2) Cheaper reproduction: Once machines have matured so that their basic construction remains unchanged, \"a further devaluation takes place as a result of improvements in the methods of reproduction of this fixed capital.\" The value of machines falls \"not because they are quickly supplanted ... by newer, more productive machines, but because they can now be reproduced more cheaply.\" Hence: \"large enterprises often flourish only under their second owners, after the first have gone bankrupt. The second owner, by buying them cheaply, starts production with a smaller outlay of capital.\" Marx adds that this is \"particularly apparent in the case of agriculture how the same causes that raise or lower the price of the product also raise or lower the value of the capital, since this consists to a large extent of that product itself, e.g. corn or cattle. (Ricardo.)\" The footnote cites Ricardo's Principles , Chapter II, \"On Rent.\" This is the agricultural special case: because agricultural capital largely consists of the very products it produces (seed, livestock, fodder), a change in the product price is simultaneously a change in the value of the capital. This is a profound point for understanding why agriculture behaves differently in the fluctuations of the profit rate — and it connects to the chapter's later argument that capitalist agriculture is inherently unstable. 2.11 Variable Capital: The Release and Tying-up Mechanism with Numerical Examples Marx now turns to variable capital. \"In as much as the value of labour-power rises because the value of the means of subsistence required for its reproduction rises, or conversely falls because the value of these means of subsistence falls (and a revaluation or devaluation of the variable capital can mean nothing more than these two cases), and assuming that the working day remains constant, a revaluation of this kind means a fall in surplus-value and a devaluation means a rise.\" But the key new phenomenon is release and tying-up . If wages fall owing to a fall in the value of labour-power (possibly associated with a rise in the actual price of labour), \"a portion of the capital previously laid out on wages is set free. There is a release of variable capital.\" For newly invested capital , the effect is simply an increased rate of surplus-value. \"The same quantity of labour is set in motion with less money than before, and in this way the unpaid portion of labour is increased at the cost of the paid portion.\" For already invested capital , however, there is a dual effect: not only does the rate of surplus-value increase, but \"a portion of the capital previously laid out on wages is set free.\" It was \"formerly tied up and formed a portion constantly deducted from the proceeds of production, a portion which was laid out on wages and had to function as variable capital if the business was to proceed on the old scale. This portion now becomes available and can be used for new capital investment, whether to extend the same business or to function in another sphere of production.\" Numerical Example 1 — Wage Fall (Release of Variable Capital) Originally £500 sets 500 workers in motion for a week; now only £400 is required. Mass of value produced = £1,000 in each case. Before : s = £1,000 − £500 = £500; s′ = 500/500 = 100% . After : s = £1,000 − £400 = £600; s′ = 600/400 = 150% . So the rate of surplus-value rises from 100% to 150%, and £100 of variable capital is set free . For a newly-opened business in that sphere with v = £400 and corresponding c, the only effect is the higher s′. For an already functioning business, \"not only has the mass of surplus-value risen from £500 to £600 and the rate of surplus-value from 100 to 150 per cent ... apart from this, £100 of variable capital has been set free, and this is now available to exploit more labour. Not only is the same amount of labour exploited more profitably, but the release of £100 enables the same variable capital of £500 to exploit more workers than before at the higher rate.\" Numerical Example 2 — Wage Rise (Tying-up of Variable Capital) Take the original division with 500 workers employed: 400v + 600s = 1,000; s′ = 150%. The worker receives a weekly wage of £1/1.25? Let us read carefully: \"The worker thus receives a weekly wage of £1- = 16 shillings.\" With v = £400 and 500 workers, each wage = £400/500 = £0.8 = 16 shillings . Good. Now if these 500 workers cost £500 per week (weekly wage rises to £1 each), and £400 can only set 400 workers in motion. If we keep 500 workers employed: 500v + 500s = 1,000 , so s′ falls from 150% to 100% (a fall by one-third). For newly invested capital : only effect is a lower s′ and, other things equal, a lower p′ (not proportionately). With c = 2,000: - First case : 2,000c + 400v + 600s = 3,000; s′ = 150%; p′ = 600/2,400 = 25% . - Second case : 2,000c + 500v + 500s = 3,000; s′ = 100%; p′ = 500/2,500 = 20% . For already operating capital , the effect is dual: - With £400 variable capital, only 400 workers are employed, producing (at s′ = 100%) £400 of surplus-value. - Since £2,000 constant capital requires 500 workers to be set in motion, 400 workers only set in motion £1,600 constant capital. - To continue on the former scale and prevent a fifth of the machinery from halting, v must be increased by £100 . - This is possible \"only because capital that was formerly available is now tied up, in that part of the accumulation fund designed to expand the business now serves simply to fill the gap, or, alternatively, a portion designed to be spent as revenue is added to the original capital.\" - With a £100 increase in the outlay of variable capital, £100 less surplus-value is produced. \"More capital is needed to set the same number of workers in motion, and at the same time the surplus-value that each of these individual workers supplies is reduced.\" The general law: \"The advantages that arise from the release of variable capital, and the disadvantages that arise from its being tied up, both exist only for capital that is already in operation and thus reproduces itself in given conditions. For capital that is to be newly invested, the advantage or disadvantage is in each case confined to this: there will occur a rise or fall in the rate of surplus-value and a corresponding if not proportionate change in the rate of profit.\" This distinction — between new and already functioning capital — is one of the most important methodological contributions of the chapter. It explains why the same underlying change (e.g., a wage-good cheapening) has genuinely different effects on aggregate profit depending on whether it is realised as a higher exploitation rate on new investments or as a release of funds for existing firms. It shows the profit rate is path-dependent in a way that pure ratio analysis (p′ = s/(c+v)) conceals. 2.12 Productivity-Driven Release and Tying-up (Beyond Revaluation) Marx adds a second source of release/tying-up, independent of revaluation : \"Variable capital can also be set free if the development of productivity leads to a reduction in the number of workers required to set the same amount of constant capital in motion, with the rate of wages remaining the same.\" Conversely, \"additional variable capital may be tied up if more workers are required for the same amount of constant capital, owing to a decline in the productivity of labour.\" However, \"If a portion of the capital earlier applied as variable capital is now applied in the form of constant capital, however, i.e. if there is only a different distribution of the component elements of the same capital, then although this certainly has an influence on the rate of surplus-value and the rate of profit, it does not come under the heading of the tying-up and release of capital that we are considering here.\" This is a definitional boundary : release/tying-up concerns changes in the total capital required , not intra-capital redistribution. For constant capital , Marx notes parallel mechanisms: - Constant capital can be tied up or released by revaluation of its material elements. - Otherwise, constant capital can be tied up (without variable capital being transformed into it) only if productivity increases, so that the same labour produces a larger product and sets more constant capital in motion. - The same can happen if productivity declines (e.g., in agriculture, where the same labour needs more seed, fertilizer, drainage to produce the same product). - Constant capital can be released without devaluation \"if improvements, the harnessing of natural forces, etc. place a constant capital of lesser value in a position technically to perform the same service as one of higher value did earlier.\" This taxonomy completes the machinery of release and tying-up. Marx then returns to the raw-material-specific danger: \"We saw in Volume 2 how, after commodities are transformed into money, are sold, a definite portion of this money must be transformed back into the material elements of constant capital, and moreover in the proportions that are required by the specific technical character of the sphere of production in question.\" Ignoring wages, \"the most important element in all branches of production is raw material, including the ancillary materials that are particularly important in branches of production which do not involve any raw material proper, as with mining and the extractive industries in general.\" The contrast between raw material and wear-and-tear is again drawn: the depreciation portion \"enters the account more in an ideal sense, as long as the machinery is still at all serviceable; it does not very much matter whether it is paid for and converted into money today or tomorrow, or at any particular point in the capital's turnover time.\" But with raw material, \"if its price rises, it may be impossible to replace it completely after deducting wages from the value of the commodity. Violent fluctuations in price thus lead to interruptions, major upsets and even catastrophes in the reproduction process.\" 2.13 The Organic Asymmetry: Why Agricultural Raw Materials Are Chronically Volatile Marx now provides the natural-material foundation for the whole chapter's catastrophism. Agricultural products, \"whose raw materials derive from organic nature,\" are \"most subject to these fluctuations in value, as a result of variations in the harvest, etc. (Quite apart from the impact of the credit system.)\" Two elements are identified: (1) The use-value/value elasticity of organic products. \"The same quantity of labour may here be expressed in very diverse amounts of use-values, depending on uncontrollable natural conditions, the seasons of the year, etc., and a particular quantity of these use-values will accordingly have very different prices.\" Marx gives a clean algebraic illustration: \"If a value x is expressed in 100 lb. of a commodity a, the price of 1 lb. of a is x/100; if it is expressed in 1,000 lb. of a, the price of 1 lb. is x/1,000; and so on.\" Because labour can be embodied in wildly varying use-value quantities, the unit price of organic raw materials is intrinsically volatile. (2) The asymmetric elasticity of supply. \"In the nature of the case, plant and animal products, whose growth and production are subject to certain organic laws involving naturally determined periods of time, cannot suddenly be increased in the same degree as, say, machines and other fixed capital, coal, ore, etc., which, assuming the requisite natural conditions, can be significantly increased in a very short period in an industrially developed country.\" Therefore, \"it is possible, therefore, and indeed unavoidable when capitalist production is fully developed, that the production and increase of the portion of constant capital that consists of fixed capital, machinery, etc. may run significantly ahead of the portion consisting of organic raw materials, so that the demand for these raw materials grows more rapidly than their supply, and their price therefore rises.\" This is one of the most important structural arguments in all of Volume 3. Machines, coal, and ore can be expanded almost elastically in the short run; organic raw materials cannot, because they obey biological clocks (a year for many crops, longer for livestock). Since fixed capital tends to grow faster than organic raw material supply in a rapidly accumulating economy, there is a built-in tendential scarcity and price inflation of raw materials. The consequences of the resulting price rise are threefold: Spatial extension of supply : \"these raw materials are supplied from a greater distance, since the rise in their price can meet greater costs of transport\"; Temporal expansion of production : \"their production is expanded, though by the nature of things the volume of products can only increase a year later\"; Substitution and economy : \"all kinds of surrogates are now employed that were previously unused, and more economical use is made of waste products.\" But here the cycle turns. \"When the price rise begins to have a marked effect on the expansion of production and supply, the turning-point has generally been already reached, at which demand falls as a consequence of the continuing increase in the price of the raw material and of all commodities it enters into as an element, bringing about a reaction in its turn on the raw material's price.\" This is a hog-cycle / cobweb dynamic avant la lettre. The demand-side collapse and the supply-side expansion arrive simultaneously , and the price is violently reversed. \"Apart from the convulsions that achieve this effect by devaluing capital in various ways, still other circumstances come into play.\" The consequence is a \"regularly\" recurring cycle of raw-material price inflation and collapse: The more capitalist production develops, \"bringing with it greater means for a sudden and uninterrupted increase in the portion of the constant capital that consists of machinery, etc., and the more rapid the accumulation (particularly in times of prosperity), the greater is the relative overproduction of machinery and other fixed capital, the more frequent the relative overproduction of plant and animal raw materials, and the more marked the previously described rise in their price and the corresponding reaction. The more frequent, therefore, are those revulsions which have their basis in this violent price fluctuation, and are a major element in the reproduction process.\" There is a beautiful irony here: the more developed capitalist production is, the more frequent and severe are these raw-material crises. Industrial progress intensifies rather than mitigates the turbulence, because it accelerates fixed-capital accumulation relative to the inelastic supply of organic raw materials. 2.14 The Collapse Phase, the Monopoly Re-established, and the Futility of Regulation When high prices collapse — because the rise provoked demand decline, supply expansion, and distant-region supply — the result is analysed in several aspects: \"The sudden collapse in the price of raw materials places shackles on their reproduction, and in this way the monopoly of the original supplying countries, which produce in favourable conditions, is re-established — perhaps with certain limitations, but re-established anyhow.\" \"The impulse that was given may indeed cause the reproduction of the raw materials to proceed on an expanded scale, particularly in those countries that more or less possess a monopoly in this production.\" \"But the basis on which production proceeds as a result of the expanded machinery, etc. and which must now prevail as the new normal basis, after a few fluctuations, has been very much expanded by the events of the previous turnover cycle.\" \"Among some of the secondary sources of supply, however, the reproduction that has at first increased will have again experienced a significant restriction.\" The evidence: \"The export tables readily show how during the last thirty years (up to 1865) Indian cotton production has risen whenever there has been a shortfall in American production and then suddenly contracted more or less seriously.\" In periods of high raw-material prices, \"the industrial capitalists get together and form associations to regulate production\" — e.g., Manchester in 1848 after cotton prices rose, and Irish flax production. But: \"As soon as the immediate impulse has gone by and the general principle of competition ('buying in the cheapest market') reigns sovereign once more, instead of promoting productive capacity in suitable countries of origin, which these associations set out to do, irrespective of the immediate momentary price at which these countries can supply the product, it is left once more to 'prices' to regulate supply.\" Marx's verdict is categorical: \"All ideas of a common, all-embracing and far-sighted control over the production of raw materials — a control that is in fact incompatible, by and large, with the laws of capitalist production, and hence remains forever a pious wish, or is at most confined to exceptional common steps in moments of great and pressing danger and perplexity — all such ideas give way to the belief that supply and demand will mutually regulate one another.\" Engels' editorial footnote 16 (added after 1865) extends this: \"Since the above was written (1865), competition on the world market has increased significantly owing to the rapid development of industry in all civilized countries ... The fact that the modern productive forces ... are daily and increasingly outgrowing the laws of capitalist commodity exchange within which they are supposed to move — this fact impresses itself more and more today even on the consciousness of the capitalists.\" Two symptoms: (1) \"the new mania for general protective tariffs, differing from the old protectionism because they are precisely designed to protect exportable articles\"; and (2) \"the cartels (trusts) formed by manufacturers in whole branches of production for the regulation of production and therewith prices and profits too.\" Engels' verdict: \"these experiments can be pursued only in a relatively favourable economic climate. The first storm is bound to bowl them over and show how, much as production does need regulating, it is certainly not the capitalist class that is called to this task. In the meantime, the only purpose these cartels serve to promote is the swallowing of the little fish by the big fish even more rapidly than before.\" This footnote is a hinge between Marx's text and later developments (trusts, cartels, protective tariffs), and it transforms the chapter's structural pessimism into a historical prediction: capitalist attempts at regulation are both necessary and doomed. Marx concludes the theoretical section with the observation that \"the capitalists' superstition on this matter is so crude that even the factory inspectors pass astonished remarks on it time and again in their reports.\" The alternation of good and bad years brings cheaper raw materials round again. The extension of demand, and the stimulating effect on the profit rate, then set the stage for \"the process depicted above, with the production of raw materials being gradually overtaken again by the production of machines, etc., is then repeated once more on a larger scale.\" The point about Indian cotton (\"American-quality cotton from India\") is that a real improvement in quality would require \"a regular and steady rise in European demand over a long period (quite apart from the economic conditions to which Indian production is subject in its own country).\" Instead, \"the production of raw materials is thus expanded only in sudden jerks, before being violently contracted once more.\" The cotton famine of 1861–5 is the exemplar: \"a situation in which a raw material that is one of the most essential elements of reproduction was quite lacking for a time.\" Finally: \"Prices can also rise in a situation of full supply, if this is full only under difficult conditions. Alternatively there may be a genuine lack of raw material. In the cotton crisis, we had originally the latter case.\" 2.15 The Moral of the Tale: Capitalist Agriculture as Contradiction Marx draws the theoretical section to a close with a sweeping claim: \"The more we look back at the history of production in the most recent period, the more regularly we find, particularly in the key branches of industry, a constantly repeated alternation between relative price increase and a subsequent depreciation of raw materials supplied by organic nature that arises from this.\" The Factory Inspectorate reports illustrate the argument. Then the moral, which is also a political conclusion: \"The moral of the tale, which can also be extracted from other discussions of agriculture, is that the capitalist system runs counter to a rational agriculture, or that a rational agriculture is incompatible with the capitalist system (even if the latter promotes technical development in agriculture) and needs either small farmers working for themselves or the control of the associated producers.\" This is one of the most compact and radical statements in Volume 3: the very structure of capitalist agriculture — with its inelastic organic production cycles, its speculative price dynamics, its subordination of land to profit — is incompatible with rational agriculture. The resolution would require either independent small farmers or \"the control of the associated producers\" — a direct bridge to the socialist programme. 2.16 The Empirical Illustration: Prehistory 1845–60 The remainder of the chapter is documentation — precisely the material Marx promised to include. He treats the cotton crisis of 1861–5 as \"the biggest example of an interruption in the production process caused by a lack of raw material and an increase in its price.\" The prehistory serves to show that the underlying dynamic was already in play long before the American Civil War. 1845 — high tide. Leonard Horner reports \"a more active state of trade ... particularly in cotton spinning\" than in eight years. New mills, enlargements, new engines, new machinery. 1846 — complaints begin. \"For a considerable time past I have heard from the occupiers of cotton-mills very general complaints of the depressed state of their trade ... several mills have begun to work short time, usually eight hours a day instead of twelve.\" The cause was a great advance in raw-material prices without a corresponding advance in manufactured articles — indeed, manufactured prices were lower than before the cotton rise. Because of the previous four years' mill expansion, there was simultaneously a greater demand for raw material and a greater supply of manufactured articles on the market, \"causes that must concurrently have operated against profits.\" Bradford had 490 mills in 1846 against 318 in 1836; flax-spinning was particularly affected. \"All have contributed more or less, during the last ten years, to the overstocking of the market.\" 1847 — monetary crisis. October: bank rate at 8%; railway bubble collapse; speculation in East Indian bills. Baker's analysis: increased demand for cotton, wool, and flax coincided with harvests below average. Cotton rose from 4d. to 6d. per lb., while twist was only a fraction above its former price. The woollen trade in Yorkshire increased 40% since 1836; worsted increased 74%; flax 25% in England, 22% in Scotland, nearly 90% in Ireland. Raw material rose £10 per ton while yarn fell 6d. a bundle. 1849–50 — recovery, then new strains. Flax cheap; wool cheap and well-bought; worsted flourishing. But: \"Speculation, and uncertainty as to the probable supply of cotton wool, have ever had the effect of causing greater excitement, and more frequent alterations in the state of that branch of manufacture, than any other.\" In 1850 the increased worsted machinery threatened \"a similar reaction\"; in 1849 alone worsted looms increased their produce by 40%, spindles by 25–30%. By October 1850, \"the high price of raw cotton continues ... to cause a considerable depression in this branch of manufacture.\" 1853 — extreme prosperity, then overproduction. Horner in April: \"At no period during the last seventeen years ... have I known such general prosperity.\" By October: \"Overproduction.\" 1854 — Crimean War. Diminished supplies of flax and hemp from Russia. 1859 — Scottish flax depression, jute substitution. Raw material scarce and high; one-half of Dundee machinery employed in jute spinning. 1860–61 — the crisis begins. April 1860: \"a great scarcity of raw material ... it is ... the want of it alone, which keeps us within bounds. In the cotton trade, the erection of new mills ... can scarcely, I think, have been at any time exceeded. Everywhere there are new movements in search of raw material.\" October 1860: trade good, \"but for the high price of raw material.\" Flax spinners look to India for supply. April 1861: trade depressed; \"Raw material is high. In almost every branch of textile manufacture it is above the price at which it can be manufactured for the masses of the consumers.\" 1860 was \"a year of overproduction in the cotton industry\"; \"It has taken between two and three years to absorb the overproduction of 1860 in the markets of the world.\" October 1861: \"Trade has been for some time in a very depressed state ... short time must have been kept during the ensuing winter in consequence of the great increase of production during the last three years, and the unsettled state of the Indian and Chinese markets.\" This is the crucial point: the depression was already developing before the cotton blockade took full effect, because of the previous overproduction and high raw-material prices. The chapter here includes detailed illustration of the substitution of sizing for cotton during the crisis. A manufacturer writes: \"when cotton is high in price, every spinner of ordinary yarns (say up to 40s.) (principally 12s. to 32s.) will raise his counts as much as he can, that is, will spin 16s. where he used to spin 12s ... and the manufacturer using these fine yarns will make his cloth the usual weight by the addition of so much more size.\" Ordinary export shirting weighing 8 lbs. was made of 5½ lbs. cotton and 2½ lbs. size; in other cloths as much as 50% size was added. The manufacturer \"may and does truly boast that he is getting rich by selling cloth for less money per pound than he paid for the mere yarn of which they are composed.\" The weavers attributed \"increased sickness to the size which is used in dressing the warps of Surat cotton, and which is not made of the same material as formerly, viz., flour.\" The substitute — ground talc (\"China clay\") or gypsum (\"French chalk\") — had \"the very important advantage of increasing greatly the weight of the cloth manufactured, making 15 lbs. of the raw material to weigh 20 lbs. when woven into cloth.\" But it reduced weavers' earnings: \"This sizing, which gives weight to the yarn, renders it hard and brittle ... the hard state of the warp causes the threads of the heald to break frequently; and it is said to take a weaver five minutes to tie up the threads every time they break; and a weaver has to piece these ends at least ten times as often as formerly, thus reducing the productive powers of the loom in the working-hours.\" This is a striking empirical illustration of Marx's method: a quality degradation of raw material and its substitutes becomes an economic mechanism for transferring value from workers to capitalists — not by changing wage rates, but by intensifying labour time and reducing effective output. 2.17 The Cotton Famine 1861–65: Human and Social Consequences The narrative of 1861–65 is unrelenting. The strike by power-loom weavers in early 1861 (against a 5–7½% wage reduction, demanding instead a cut in working hours) collapsed after a month, and the workers \"suffered both things\": wage cuts plus short time. 1862, April: \"The sufferings of the operatives since the date of my last report have greatly increased; but at no period of the history of manufactures, have sufferings so sudden and so severe been borne with so much silent resignation and so much patient self-respect.\" The statistics: in Manchester in May 1848, 15% out of work, 12% short time, 70% full work. On 28 May 1862, 15% out of work, 35% short time, 49% full work. 1862, October: Of 2,887 cotton factories in the UK in 1861, 2,109 in Lancashire and Cheshire. Of these, 392 (19%) under 10 horsepower; 345 (16%) between 10 and 20; 1,372 (65%) 20+. More than a third of the small manufacturers \"were operatives themselves at no distant period; they are men without command of capital.\" The brunt of the burden fell on the remaining two-thirds. Statistics: 40,146 (11.3%) fully employed; 134,767 (38%) short time; 179,721 (50.7%) unemployed. Excluding Manchester and Bolton (which spun finer grades): 8.5% fully employed, 38% short time, 53.5% unemployed. 1863, April: At most one half of cotton operatives would be fully employed. The use of Surat cotton forced great reductions of machinery speed, which hit wages because spinners were paid per pound and weavers per piece. \"From enquiries I have made ... there is a diminution averaging 20 per cent upon their former earnings, in some instances the diminution has been as much as 50 per cent, calculated upon the same rate of wages as prevailed in 1861.\" 1863, October: A more healthy state, machinery improved, material better understood. But appalling figures: two young women returned to sewing school unable to earn 1s. per week; self-acting minders earning 8s. 11d. after a fortnight's full work (from which rent was deducted, though the manufacturer returned half as a gift — \"How generous!\"), taking away 6s. 11d.; minders ranging 5s.–9s., weavers 2s.–6s. in the last months of 1862. Causes: shorter staple of Surat cotton, dirty condition, mixing with waste, threads more liable to break, weavers can only mind one or two looms, direct wage reductions of 5, 7½, and 10%, fines for flaws. The chapter's moral commentary becomes biting: \"Wages were wretched enough even with full-time working. The cotton workers willingly volunteered for all the public works they could be employed in, such as drainage, road-building, stone-breaking and street-paving, so as to get relief (which was in effect a form of relief to the factory-owners; see Volume I, pp. 720–21) from the local authorities. The entire bourgeoisie stood guard over the workers. If starvation wages were offered and a worker was unwilling to accept them, the Relief Committee struck him off the relief list. This was a real golden age for the factory-owning gentlemen, in as much as the workers either starved or had to work at the price most profitable for the bourgeoisie, while the Relief Committees acted as their guard-dogs.\" The Public Works Act of 1863 — designed to enable operatives to earn \"remunerative wages\" as independent labourers — had three purposes: to allow local authorities to borrow from the Exchequer Loan Commissioners; to improve towns; and to provide work and wages. By the end of October 1863, loans of £883,700 had been granted. Work included canal-digging, road-building, street-paving, and reservoir construction. Mr Henderson, President of the Blackburn Relief Committee, praised \"the cheerful alacrity with which the unemployed operatives ... have accepted of the work offered.\" Workers were entitled to 4–12 shillings per week, sometimes for a family of eight. Municipal authorities received \"money for improving their smoky and neglected towns at exceptionally low rates of interest\" and \"paid the workers far below the regular wage-rates.\" Marx describes it as \"this new edition of the Ateliers nationaux of 1848, but this time set up for the advantage of the bourgeoisie\" (footnote: the original National Workshops of February 1848, ostensibly a working-class demand). The famous passage on experiments \"in corpore vili\" (on a worthless body): \"The operatives are subject to great fluctuation, from the constant experimentalizing of the manufacturers upon different kinds and proportions of cotton and waste in the same mill, the 'mixings' as it is called being frequently changed; and the earnings of the operatives rise and fall with the quality of the cotton mixings.\" Wages: A, weaver, family of six, 4 days: 6s. 8½d.; B, twister, 4½ days: 6s.; C, weaver, family of four, 5 days: 5s. 1d.; D, slubber, family of six, 4 days: 7s. 10d.; E, weaver, family of seven, 3 days: 5s. \"Work would become a misfortune in many a family, as it not merely reduces the income, but brings it so low as to be utterly insufficient to provide more than a small portion of the absolute wants, were it not that supplemental relief is granted.\" By 25 March 1863, nearly £3 million had been dispensed by Poor Law authorities, the Central Relief Committee, and the Mansion House Committee. Wage reductions: spinners suffered 15% indirect reduction in one district (South Sea Island → Egyptian cotton); 5% reduction plus 20–30% loss working Surat and waste in another. Weavers reduced from 4 looms to 2; in 1860 they averaged 5s. 7d. per loom; in 1863, only 3s. 4d. Fines rose from 3d.–6d. on American to 1s.–3s. 6d. Mule spinners' average fell from 18s.–25s. (1860) to 10s.–18s. Rent of houses: Frequently deducted from wages even when short time; house values diminished; cottages that cost 3s. 6d. per week could be had for 2s. 4d., sometimes less. Emigration: The factory-owners opposed it, \"firstly because, looking forward to the recovery of the cotton trade from its present depression, they keep within their reach the means whereby their mills can be worked in the most advantageous manner,\" and secondly because many were landlords of the workers' houses and expected to recover back rent. The final line is a devastating rhetorical close: Mr Bernal Osborne said in a speech to his parliamentary electors on 22 October 1864 that the workers of Lancashire had behaved like the ancient philosophers (Stoics). Marx appends: \"Not like sheep?\" This closing question crystallises the chapter's double movement: the theoretical theorem (the rate of profit is exposed to raw-material prices) and the political indictment (the whole burden of this exposure falls on the workers, who are the residual absorbents of every shock). The chapter is thus not merely an exercise in the mechanics of p′ but a proof that the abstract category \"rate of profit\" has a body count behind it. 2.18 Cross-Cutting Threads: Method, Object, and the Chapter's Internal Logic Three threads weave through every section: First, the primacy of the value-composition of capital over the apparent \"profitability\" of a business. Marx repeatedly insists that the rate of profit is determined by the total capital applied, irrespective of whether that capital is consumed. This is why a single raw-material price change can move p′ even when nothing has changed in the exploitation of labour, the productivity of labour in the final production process, or the market for the commodity. The theorem detaches the rate of profit from the visible surface of buying and selling. Second, the dialectic between use-value and value. Raw material's quality (Surat vs. American cotton, waste percentage, sizing substitutions) has direct economic consequences for the profit rate. Machinery's durability and obsolescence (moral depreciation) affects the capital value. The biological rhythm of organic raw materials conditions their price elasticity and hence the entire cyclical dynamic. Use-value properties, which a purely value-theoretic analysis might abstract from, repeatedly re-enter as determinants. Third, the asymmetry between the appearance and reality of profit movements. Because capital revaluation/devaluation can move in opposite directions from the rate of profit, and because stocks and competition complicate the transmission of price shocks, the observed movements of the rate or mass of profit can seem to be \"independent of movements of surplus-value.\" The categories of release and tying-up are precisely Marx's tools for demystifying these appearances. They explain why \"it appears as if it is not only the rate of profit but also its mass ... can increase and decrease independently.\" The chapter, in short, is a demonstration that the capitalist economy has systemic sources of turbulence that are neither class struggle nor technical change in the immediate sense, but the collision of an elastic machine-based production apparatus with the inelastic, biologically constrained production of organic raw materials. That collision is expressed as recurring price crises; those crises are absorbed by the working class through wage cuts, short time, unemployment, and fabricated relief. The reconciliation of profit rate with surplus-value requires the intermediate categories — release, tying-up, revaluation, devaluation — that Marx introduces here. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 3.1 Fundamental Principles Principle 1: The rate of profit has a denominator, and the denominator has a life of its own. The chapter's most durable lesson is that p′ = s/(c+v) cannot be reduced to a story about exploitation. Whatever raises c — raw-material price spikes, ancillary-material inflation, cheaper-reproduction difficulties, natural resource scarcity — lowers p′ even when s′ is untouched. This explains why the rate of profit is more volatile than the rate of surplus-value, and why it can fall during booms as well as slumps (when raw materials are bidding up). Principle 2: The rate of profit has material determinants. The theorem is not merely formal. It rests on the biological and geological inelasticity of organic raw materials, on the natural riches (iron, coal, wood) embedded in machinery, and on the different turnover rhythms of circulating and fixed capital. Nature enters the profit rate. This is why the profit rate cannot be understood as a purely social category — it is jointly determined by social relations of production and by the physical properties of the material substratum. Principle 3: Property-income illusions and crises arise from capital revaluation. The categories of release/tying-up and revaluation/devaluation show that a capitalist can experience a rising profit rate while suffering a capital loss (if capital is devalued), and a falling profit rate while enjoying a capital gain (if capital is revalued). This is a fundamental epistemological warning: profit-rate statistics can misrepresent the underlying valorisation of capital. Any serious analysis of crises must move beneath the surface of p′ to the movements of capital value itself. Principle 4: The same structural cause generates both expansion and collapse. The cycle Marx describes — fixed capital races ahead, organic supply lags, raw-material prices spike, demand collapses, supply arrives, prices crash, secondary suppliers retrench, monopoly is re-established, then the cycle repeats on a larger scale — is a self-reproducing instability. It is intrinsic, not accidental. Capitalist development intensifies it: \"The more capitalist production is developed ... the greater is the relative overproduction of machinery and other fixed capital, the more frequent the relative overproduction of plant and animal raw materials, and the more marked the previously described rise in their price and the corresponding reaction.\" Principle 5: Capitalist regulation is structurally impossible. Marx's claim that all-embracing control of raw-material production is a \"pious wish\" incompatible with capitalist laws anticipates the modern theory of market failure in commodity markets and, indeed, the persistence of commodity price volatility despite cartels, futures markets, and international commodity agreements. Engels' footnote on trusts and tariffs shows the prediction being borne out within his lifetime. 3.2 Broader Theoretical Implications The chapter has at least five broad theoretical ramifications: (a) It extends the theory of crisis beyond underconsumption and disproportionality to raw-material supply. Marx here identifies a material source of crisis that is orthogonal to demand failure and to inter-sectoral imbalance: the mismatch between an elastic machine sector and an inelastic organic sector. This is a distinct crisis-theoretic mechanism, sometimes called the \"raw-material cycle,\" and it anticipates later theories of commodity-price crises (e.g., Prebisch-Singer-type dynamics and the \"resource curse\" literature in a different idiom). (b) It establishes the ground for the later theory of rent. By noting that natural riches appear \"as a natural fruit borne by capital,\" and that agricultural capital's value is caught up in the price of its own product (Ricardo), Marx is laying the analytical groundwork for the theory of ground-rent and for the analysis of landed property that occupies later chapters of Volume 3. (c) It distinguishes capital release from revenue release. This distinction, though technical, is essential for understanding the credit system's role later in Volume 3. It explains how credit can appear to create capital ex nihilo (when it is really mobilising released capital), and how crises appear to be \"crises of confidence\" when they are fundamentally crises of capital revaluation. (d) It sharpens the critique of political economy. Marx's target is double: the empirical school (Torrens) that sees the phenomenon but cannot explain it, and the theoretical school (Ricardo) that explains markets but misses the phenomenon. This methodological critique — that every genuine science of the profit rate must integrate both the value-composition of capital and the material conditions of its reproduction — is a template for Marx's critique of the classics throughout Volume 3. (e) It grounds the critique of capitalist agriculture. The claim that \"the capitalist system runs counter to a rational agriculture\" and that a rational agriculture \"needs either small farmers working for themselves or the control of the associated producers\" is a direct bridge between economics and political ecology. It anticipates twentieth-century concerns about soil exhaustion, monoculture, and the long-term unsustainability of profit-driven land use. 3.3 Practical, Political, and Philosophical Implications Practical: The chapter is a manual for understanding industrial cycles. It tells us to look at raw-material inventories, waste rates, harvest prospects, and the ratio of fixed to circulating capital when diagnosing industrial disturbances. It explains why free-trade in raw materials is so important to industrial nations (a lesson as true today as in 1846, for oil, semiconductors, lithium, and rare earths). It explains why the \"just-in-time\" inventory system, which minimizes stocks, magnifies the volatility of the profit rate in the face of supply shocks — because the chapter explicitly notes that \"the smaller the amount of stock ... the more visible the effect of a change in raw material prices.\" Political: The chapter's final sections are a sustained indictment. The cotton famine demonstrates that every structural shock to the raw-material sector is transmitted to the working class through wage cuts, short time, unemployment, fines, rent deductions, and the complicity of relief committees. The factory-owners' opposition to emigration and their control of housing reveal the coercive architecture that holds labour in place to bear the burden. The chapter thus shows the class incidence of a phenomenon — the raw-material-supply cycle — that appears in the economic literature as a purely technical problem. Anything that affects p′ affects workers first and hardest. Philosophical: The chapter is a profound meditation on the relation between value and nature. The profit rate, Marx shows, is not a purely human artefact; it is conditioned by the biological clocks of plants and animals, by the geology of mineral deposits, and by the seasons. Capital tries to subdue nature, but nature returns as a constraint on the valorisation process. The chapter thereby refuses both the pure-social-constructivism of value and the pure-naturalism of resource economics: value is social, but it is materially embedded. The dialectic between the social and the natural runs through every page of the chapter, from the waste rates of Surat cotton to the yield of Indian agriculture to the moral depreciation of machines. 3.4 Final Reflection Chapter 6 is, at first reading, one of the more technical and empirically cluttered portions of Capital . But beneath the barrage of Factory Inspector quotations lies one of Marx's most subversive arguments: the rate of profit is a social relation that is materially constrained by the rhythms of nature and politically distributed by the coercive power of the class that owns the means of production. The theorem (raw-material prices against profit) is elegant; the empirical illustration (the cotton famine) is devastating; the reconciliation (release/tying-up, revaluation/devaluation) is analytically powerful. Taken together, they show that the \"laws of motion\" of capitalist society are not a smooth mechanism but a turbulence that periodically devalues capital, throws workers into poverty, resurrects the coercive apparatus of relief and rent, and then restarts — on an expanded scale — the same cycle. The final ironic question — the workers of Lancashire behaved like Stoic philosophers, \"not like sheep?\" — is Marx's way of saying that the cycle is not natural, not inevitable, and not finished. A rational organisation of production, he has argued throughout, would adjust the production of organic raw materials to the rhythms of both nature and social need; only a system driven by profit periodically destroys what it has built, and then asks its victims to bear the loss. That is the chapter's enduring lesson: a system that cannot control the supply of its own raw materials cannot rationally control its own future. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 5 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 7"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 7", "title": "Chapter 7", "subtitle": "Supplementary Remarks", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 7/Chapter 7 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 6914, "read_time": "35 min read", "tags": ["Cost Price & Rate of Profit"], "keywords": ["Supplementary Remarks on Part I", "Recapitulation of Cost Price and Profit Formations", "Surface Illusions of Production Costs", "Complete Severing of Profit from Labour-Time in Capitalist Eyes", "Transition toward Average Profit Equalization"], "text": "Chapter 7 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,879 words ⏱️ ~34 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 7, \"Supplementary Remarks,\" is the closing chapter of Part One of Capital , Volume 3 — the Part devoted to \"The Transformation of Surplus-Value into Profit.\" It is exactly what its title announces: not a new theoretical construction, but a set of three tightly compressed addenda, separated by asterisks in the manuscript, that consolidate, qualify, and defend the results the preceding chapters have produced. Its intellectual objective is double. First, it must explain a puzzle of consciousness: why does the bourgeois systematically refuse to identify profit with surplus-value, even though the whole of Part One has demonstrated that identity? Second, it must settle a technical controversy: does a change in the magnitude of the capital advanced necessarily leave the rate of profit untouched, as Rodbertus claimed? Marx's answer is a precise, two-case exception to a general rule, followed by a closing taxonomy of the only channels through which a profit rate can move. The chapter's core thesis can be stated in three propositions. (i) The identity of profit and surplus-value holds at the level of the sphere of production in aggregate , but it is systematically obscured at the level of the individual capital and in the process of circulation — and this obscurity is not mere ignorance but a necessary appearance generated by the ordinary functioning of the capitalist economy. (ii) The rate of profit is a ratio , and a ratio is not a magnitude; the same mass of surplus-value, extracted from the same workers under identical exploitation, can be expressed in radically different rates of profit depending on the size and the composition of the capital against which it is measured. (iii) Consequently, changes in the magnitude of capital advanced leave the profit rate unaffected in only two cases — a purely monetary change in the value of the money commodity, and a real change in capital magnitude that preserves the capital's organic composition — plus a further special case, developed at the chapter's close, in which a proportional revaluation of all components of capital scales numerator and denominator alike. The framing assumption, explicitly carried over from the rest of Part One, is that \"the mass of profit appropriated in each particular sphere of production is equal to the sum of the surplus-value produced in this sphere by the total capital applied.\" This is a distributional premise: no value is transferred between spheres. It is the assumption that will be suspended in the next Part of the volume, where profit rates are equalized into a general rate of profit. Chapter 7 therefore sits precisely on the hinge between the analysis of surplus-value production and the analysis of its distribution among competing capitals — while still, deliberately, on the near side of that hinge. The chapter's intellectual stakes are therefore threefold. Analytically , it establishes the exact conditions of invariance and variance for the profit rate. Critically , it refutes Rodbertus's proportionality thesis and, through the footnote Engels appends, situates that refutation within Marx's long-running polemic against Rodbertus on rent, profit, and \"state socialism.\" Ideologically , it offers what is in effect a micro-theory of bourgeois economic consciousness: an account of how the real dispersion of profit rates, rooted in the real technical and commercial differences among firms, necessarily produces the belief that profit springs partly from the capitalist's \"own individual action\" rather than from unpaid surplus labour. The chapter thus does not merely report results; it explains why those results cannot be seen from the standpoint of the individual capitalist. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The chapter unfolds in three asterisk-divided movements. The first (reasons 1 and 2) explains the bourgeois's misrecognition of profit. The second (the Rodbertus critique) isolates the two exceptional cases in which capital magnitude is neutral for the profit rate. The third (the closing law and its sub-cases) states the general theorem governing any increase in the profit rate, then traces the last remaining channel of autonomous fluctuation — the revaluation of capital through changes in the labour-time socially necessary for its reproduction. 2.1 The Opening Assumption: What Part One Has Already Settled Marx opens with an explicit continuity clause: \"We continue to assume, as throughout this Part, that the mass of profit appropriated in each particular sphere of production is equal to the sum of the surplus-value produced in this sphere by the total capital applied.\" Every term here is load-bearing. A sphere of production is a branch of industry — a set of capitals producing a particular class of commodity under broadly comparable technical conditions. The assumption is stated at the level of the sphere , not the firm, and it uses two different verbs: surplus-value is produced by the living labour set in motion; profit is appropriated by the capital that owns the product. The equality says that within a sphere, the profit actually pocketed by the owners of capital equals the surplus-value actually generated by the workers of that sphere — nothing leaks in from outside, nothing is drained away to another branch. This is precisely the abstraction that a theory of the general rate of profit will later be forced to abandon. Here, however, the abstraction is retained so that the profit rate of a sphere can be read off directly from its own rate of surplus-value and its own value composition. Two consequences follow immediately and should be registered before the argument proper begins. First, the denominator of the profit rate is the total capital applied — constant capital plus variable capital, the whole advance, not merely the wages bill. Second, because the equality is sphere-level, it leaves untouched the possibility that within a sphere, individual firms may earn very different rates of profit. And it is exactly that possibility — the dispersion of profit rates among individual capitals under conditions of identical exploitation — that the chapter now proceeds to exploit. 2.2 First Source of Misrecognition: Circulation Forgets Production The first of the two reasons Marx gives for the bourgeois's refusal to identify profit with surplus-value is a statement about consciousness as a function of the circuit of capital: \"In the process of circulation, he forgets the production process. The realization of commodity value — including the realization of surplus-value — he takes as the making of this surplus-value.\" The terms must be kept strictly apart. Production of surplus-value is the extraction of unpaid surplus labour in the labour process: workers work longer than the labour-time required to reproduce their own labour-power, and the excess is embodied in the product. Realization , by contrast, is the sale of the commodities at their value — the conversion of already-produced value and surplus-value into money. Realization presupposes production; there is nothing to realize that has not already been produced. Yet from the vantage point of the individual capitalist's practical experience, the sequence runs the other way. His money goes out as capital, comes back as more money, and the increment appears at the moment of sale, in the market, at the hand of the buyer. The circuit M–C–M′ presents the surplus as a surplus of money over money advanced — and money-over-money is a phenomenon of exchange, not of the workshop. This is not, in Marx's account, a personal failure of memory. The word \"forgets\" names a structural inversion : the form in which the process appears to its agent systematically occludes the process that generates it. Engels's editorial note — \"(A blank in the manuscript here indicates that Marx intended to develop this point in more detail. — F.E.)\" — is a valuable trace of the chapter's provisional character: Marx recognized that the conflation of realization with creation deserved fuller treatment, and left the space unfilled. The reader should therefore treat this first reason as a signpost rather than a completed argument: it announces that the circulation form of capital is one independent source of the profit/surplus-value illusion, distinct from and prior to the second reason that follows. Note also the difference in logical status between the two reasons. Reason (1) is universal — it applies to every capitalist in every branch, because every capitalist moves through the circuit of capital. Reason (2) is differential — it explains how the observable variety of profit rates among firms feeds the same illusion. The two reasons are complementary: one explains why the illusion is unavoidable in general, the other explains why the observable facts seem to confirm it in particular. 2.3 Second Source: The Real Dispersion of Profit Rates Among Individual Capitals The second reason is introduced by a crucial act of bracketing. Marx specifies that \"rates of profit can be very different\" even assuming the same degree of exploitation of labour, and \"ignoring all modifications introduced by the credit system,\" and ignoring \"all mutual swindling and cheating among the capitalists themselves,\" and ignoring \"all favourable selections of the market.\" Each exclusion removes a candidate explanation that a defender of the harmonious view might invoke. Credit lets firms operate on borrowed funds, altering the return on the capitalist's own money as distinct from the return on the total capital employed; Marx sets this aside. Mutual swindling and cheating — fraud, sharp dealing, transfers of value from one capitalist to another — can enrich one firm at another's expense, but redistributes existing surplus-value rather than creating it; it is set aside. Favourable selections of the market — luck in timing, location, or customer base — is likewise set aside. The methodological point is sharp: even after removing redistribution, fraud, and luck, dispersion of profit rates remains . It must therefore have structural roots. Marx then enumerates those roots: raw materials \"purchased cheaply or less cheaply, with more or less specialist knowledge\"; machinery that is \"productive, suitable and cheap\"; the \"overall arrangement of the production process in its various stages\" being \"more or less satisfactory\"; \"wastage of material avoided\"; and \"management and supervision simple and effective.\" Two distinct families of cause are at work here, and they should be separated. Some listed factors operate on the value of the inputs — buying raw materials or machinery cheaply lowers the value of the constant capital advanced per unit of output, without any change in the physical production process. Others operate on the technical and organizational efficiency of production — avoiding wastage, arranging the stages of production well, supervising effectively, choosing appropriate machinery — and thereby change the quantity of constant capital effectively consumed per unit of output. Both families show up in the accounts in the same place: as a difference in the magnitude of constant capital set against a given wage bill. The decisive formulation follows: \"given the surplus-value that accrues to a certain variable capital, it still depends very much on the business acumen of the individual, either the capitalist himself or his managers and salespeople, whether this same surplus-value is expressed in a higher or lower rate of profit.\" The verb is expressed , and it is doing all the work. Acumen does not create the surplus-value; it determines the measure against which that surplus-value is reckoned. The capitalist's skill operates on the denominator of the ratio, not on its numerator. A textual caveat is warranted here. The sentence continues: \"and therefore whether it delivers a greater or lesser amount of profit.\" Read strictly against the illustration that follows, this is loose drafting. In the example, the amounts of profit are identical in both businesses — £1,000 each — while only the rates differ. Either the phrase should be read as \"a greater or lesser amount of profit per unit of capital advanced,\" or it is a slip in which \"profit\" stands in for \"rate of profit,\" or it anticipates a comparison in which the same rate applied to capitals of different size yields different masses. The reader should hold onto the analytical point (acumen changes the measure, not the measured surplus) rather than the loose phrase. 2.4 The Numerical Illustration: Same Exploitation, Different Profit Rates Marx now makes the point arithmetically. \"The same surplus-value of £1,000, the product of £1,000 in wages, may involve £9,000 of constant capital in business A, and £11,000 in business B.\" Reconstructing the two cases with the standard symbols — c for constant capital, v for variable capital, s for surplus-value, C = c + v for the total capital advanced, s′ = s/v for the rate of surplus-value, and p′ = s/C for the rate of profit: Business A: c = £9,000; v = £1,000; s = £1,000; therefore C = £10,000; s′ = 1,000/1,000 = 100%; and p′ = 1,000/10,000 = 10 per cent . Business B: c = £11,000; v = £1,000; s = £1,000; therefore C = £12,000; s′ = 100% again; and p′ = 1,000/12,000 = 8⅓ per cent. (The printed text garbles these fractions, rendering 1,000/10,000 as \"/0,00Q04>\" and 1,000/12,000 as \"112.00°0°0\" = 8⅓ per cent; the arithmetic is unambiguous once restored.) What the illustration establishes is precise. The variable capital advanced is identical (£1,000); the surplus-value extracted is identical (£1,000); therefore the rate of surplus-value is identical, and — as Marx emphasizes — \"there is thus in each case an equal exploitation of the same number of workers.\" Yet the rates of profit differ by a sixth. The difference has a single source: the constant capital is £2,000 larger in B. \"In the first case the total capital produces relatively more profit than in the second\" — the word relatively is essential, since the absolute profit is the same in both. The only thing that has changed is the ratio of that profit to the capital against which it is measured. This result generalizes through the elementary decomposition implicit in the definitions: p′ = s/C = s/(c + v) = s′ · v/(c + v) = s′ · (1 − c/C). Every symbol retains its meaning: the profit rate equals the rate of surplus-value multiplied by the share of variable capital in the total advance. Equivalently, the gap between the two rates is s′ − p′ = s′ · c/C — the rate of surplus-value discounted by the weight of constant capital. So long as v and s are held fixed while c grows, p′ must fall, and it must fall in exact proportion to the growing weight of c in C. This is the algebraic skeleton of the chapter's entire subsequent argument, and the reader should keep it in view: everything Marx says about Rodbertus, about the two exceptions, and about the general theorem is an exploration of the conditions under which this formula does or does not change. 2.5 The Ideological Payoff: The Illusion of Individual Action Having established the arithmetic, Marx draws the ideological consequence: \"And this circumstance misleads the capitalist by convincing him that his profit is due not to the exploitation of labour, but at least in part also to other circumstances independent of this, and in particular his own individual action.\" Three features of this sentence deserve emphasis. First, the illusion is partial — the capitalist is convinced that profit is only \"at least in part\" attributable to something other than exploitation. He has not abandoned the labour theory of value wholesale; he has appended an additional, independent source. This is the characteristic hybrid form of practical business ideology: profit is partly the reward of enterprise and skill, partly the return on the capital employed. Second, the appearance that deceives him is real at the level at which he experiences it . From the standpoint of the individual capital, acumen genuinely does determine the rate of profit obtained from a given surplus-value. The illusion lies not in the observation but in the inference: from \"my profit rate depends upon me\" the capitalist concludes \"my profit originates in me.\" Marx's analysis separates causation (the origin of surplus-value in unpaid labour) from expression (the rate against which it is measured) from appropriation (who pockets it). The error consists in reading the third and second back into the first. Third, the illusion has a material basis , not merely a psychological one. It is generated by \"this circumstance\" — the actual, observable, structural dispersion of profit rates among firms operating under identical exploitation. Because the dispersion is real, the inference from it is not arbitrary; and because the inference is not arbitrary, the illusion cannot be dispelled by exhortation or by better information. It can only be dissolved by an analysis that shows how the rate of profit is constituted — which is precisely the analysis Part One has supplied and this chapter is consolidating. 2.6 The Rodbertus Problem: Does Capital Magnitude Move the Profit Rate? A second asterisk marks a shift of terrain. \"The arguments developed in this first Part show the errors of that view (Rodbertus) according to which (in distinction from ground-rent, where the land area can remain the same, for example, while the rent rises), even a large variation in the capital can remain without effect on the proportion between capital and profit, i.e. on the profit rate, because if the mass of profit grows, so does the mass of the capital on which it must be calculated, and vice versa.\" The argument Marx attributes to Rodbertus is a proportionality fallacy , and it is instructive to see exactly how it is built. The analogy with ground-rent is its point of departure. In the case of land, the denominator — the acreage — is physically fixed. If rent rises while acreage stays constant, the ratio rent/land rises with the numerator. Rodbertus contrasts capital with land: capital has no fixed denominator, because any increase in the mass of profit is accompanied by an increase in the mass of capital on which it is computed. Numerator and denominator rise and fall together, and therefore — so the argument runs — the ratio between them can remain stable no matter how large the variation in the capital. The implicit model is a capital that scales up or down proportionally , like a photograph enlarged or reduced. Marx's reply is a concession followed by a restriction: \"This is true in only two cases.\" The concession is genuine — there are indeed circumstances in which capital magnitude changes without disturbing the profit rate. But Rodbertus has mistaken a pair of special cases for a universal law, and in doing so has made the profit rate a mere reflection of scale rather than a structure with laws of its own. The footnote Engels attaches identifies the antagonist and the terrain of the dispute: \"Johann Karl Rodbertus-Jagetzow (1805–75) was a Prussian landowner, and in his writings a 'state socialist', i.e. in fact a representative of agrarian capitalism who supported Bismarck's active intervention in economic management.\" The note cross-references Volume 1 of Capital (p. 669), Volume 2 (pp. 88–102), and Theories of Surplus-Value , Part II, Chapter VIII (pp. 15–114) and Chapter IX (pp. 127–61). What this apparatus makes clear is that the question is not pedantic. Rodbertus was a landowner-theorist whose analysis of rent, profit, and the state had a political program attached to it; Marx's refutation is part of a sustained and long-running critique, not an isolated remark. And the substantive stakes are exactly those articulated above: whether the profit rate is an autonomous ratio governed by value relations and composition, or a quantity that simply tracks the size of capital. 2.7 Exception One: A Change in the Value of the Money Commodity The first case in which capital magnitude changes without moving the profit rate is a change in the value of money. \"Firstly, if, other things being equal, and in particular the rate of surplus-value, there is a change in the value of the money commodity. (This is so even with a purely nominal change in value, the rise and fall of tokens of value, as long as other factors remain the same.)\" The illustration runs as follows. \"Let the total capital be £100 and the profit £20, so that the rate of profit is 20 per cent.\" Then suppose the price of gold is halved. The same physical capital, which previously commanded £100, now commands £200 — because each unit of money is worth half as much, and twice as many units are needed to express the same value. Correspondingly, the profit \"has a value of £40 instead of £20.\" Now suppose instead that the price of gold doubles: the same capital \"falls to a value of £50, and the profit is now expressed in a product valued at £10.\" In both cases, Marx writes, \"200:40 = 50:10 = 100:20 = 20 per cent.\" Note first the notational point: in this passage Marx writes the profit rate as a ratio of capital to profit — C:π — rather than as s/C. The two are equivalent as a ratio expression, and the equality across the three cases is what matters. Note second the underlying logic. A £ in this passage is not an abstract unit of account hovering above the economy; it names a definite quantity of the money commodity. When the value of gold falls by half, the same real capital — the same machines, the same stock of materials, the same wage bill in real terms — is now denominated in twice as many pounds. The profit, being a claim on the same surplus product, is likewise denominated in twice as many pounds. Numerator and denominator are each multiplied by the same scalar; the ratio is untouched. \"There would be no real change in the capital value in any case such as this, but simply a change in the monetary expression of the same value and surplus-value. The rate of profit ... could not be affected.\" Marx's parenthesis extends the point beyond metallic money to \"tokens of value\" — inconvertible paper currency or coin whose nominal value rises and falls independently of its own labour-content. The requirement is only that \"other factors remain the same,\" i.e., that the change in money is not simultaneously a change in real capital values or in the rate of surplus-value. The essential conceptual move is the separation of value from its monetary expression : a change of the latter alone is invisible to the profit rate. This exception thus has a somewhat different status from the second: it is not a case in which capital really changes magnitude at all, but only a case in which its measurement changes. 2.8 Exception Two: Real Change in Magnitude with Constant Composition \"The other case is when there is a real change in capital value, but this change is not accompanied by a change in the ratio v:c, i.e. when the rate of surplus-value is constant and the ratio of the capital invested in labour-power (the variable capital, taken as an index of the labour-power set in motion) to the capital invested in means of production remains the same.\" Here the change is not merely nominal — the capital really is larger or smaller — but the proportion between its components is preserved. Marx sets out the arithmetic: \"if we take C or nC or C/n, e.g. 1,000 or 2,000 or 500, the total profit will be in the first case 200, in the second case 400 and in the third case 100, but 200/1000 = 400/2000 = 100/500 = 20 per cent.\" (The printed page renders these fractions mutilated — \"1788-o = 24:88-o = ~gg\" — but the intended equalities are transparent.) Scaling the total capital by a factor n scales the profit by the same factor n; the ratio is invariant. Three analytical points should be extracted. First, the condition is stated in terms of the ratio v:c, and the parenthetical gloss is exact: variable capital is here \"taken as an index of the labour-power set in motion.\" The variable capital is not valuable for its own sake in this argument; it is a proxy for the quantity of living labour employed. Constant composition means: for every unit of labour-power set in motion, the same amount of means of production is required. Second, the invariance result follows directly from the formula developed earlier: p′ = s′ · (1 − c/C). If s′ is constant and c/C is constant, then p′ is constant, whatever the absolute size of C. Third, Marx draws the interpretive conclusion: \"Hence the increase or decrease in the mass of profit simply indicates an increase or decrease in the size of the capital applied.\" Under constant composition, the profit mass is a faithful index of scale — and nothing more. Rodbertus's proportional-scaling model is, so to speak, the model of this second case, mistakenly universalized. The distinction between the two exceptions is then stated with care: \"In the first case, therefore, there is simply an apparent change in magnitude of the capital applied; in the second case there is a real change in magnitude, but no change in the capital's organic composition, in the proportion between its variable and its constant parts.\" Note that the chapter has now provided its own gloss on the term organic composition : it is \"the proportion between its variable and its constant parts,\" i.e., the value proportion v:c. This gloss will matter when the closing paragraph speaks of a change in organic composition brought about by revaluation. Note also the asymmetry: the first exception involves no real change at all; the second involves a real change that happens to be composition-neutral. Rodbertus needs both, and both are special. 2.9 The Residual Cases: Why Magnitude Change Normally Moves the Profit Rate Having isolated the two exceptions, Marx characterizes what happens in every other case: \"a change in the magnitude of capital applied is either the result of a change in the value of one of its components, and thus a change in their relative magnitude (as long as the surplus-value does not itself change with the variable capital); or else this change in magnitude is the cause of a change in the relative magnitude of its two organic components (as with large-scale operations, the introduction of new machinery, etc.).\" Two distinct mechanisms are identified. In the first, the change in the magnitude of the capital is an effect : one of its components changes in value, and the aggregate therefore changes — but so do the proportions among the components. A rise in the value of raw materials, for example, enlarges c, enlarges C, and raises c/v, all at once. The change in magnitude is never isolated from a change in composition. In the second, the change in magnitude is a cause : expanding the scale of operations, introducing new machinery, and similar measures change the relative magnitude of the two organic components. Larger-scale production is more capital-intensive per worker; new machinery raises c relative to v. Here again, the magnitude change and the composition change come as a package. The parenthetical caveat in the first case — \"as long as the surplus-value does not itself change with the variable capital\" — registers a genuine complication rather than resolving it. If the value of labour-power falls, then, with a given working day, the surplus-value does change, since less of the day is needed to reproduce the worker. Marx brackets this knock-on effect in order to isolate the pure composition mechanism, but the reader should note that the bracketed case is not exotic; it is the ordinary mechanism by which a cheapening of wage goods raises both s′ and p′. The caveat marks the boundary of the present argument, not a claim that such cases are rare. The conclusion is unambiguous: \"In all these cases, therefore, a change in the magnitude of the capital applied must be accompanied by a simultaneous change in the rate of profit, as long as other things remain equal.\" The force of the \"must\" derives from the fact that, outside the two exceptions, capital magnitude is never a free-standing variable. Under capitalist production, a capital is always a definite structure of value relations and technical proportions; to change its size is, in general, to change that structure. Rodbertus's error was to treat magnitude as if it could vary while everything else stood still — which is possible only in the two cases Marx has enumerated, and in the further special case to be developed at the chapter's close. 2.10 The General Theorem: The Two Sources of a Rising Profit Rate A third asterisk opens the chapter's final movement with a general law: \"An increase in the rate of profit always stems from a relative or absolute increase in the surplus-value in relation to its costs of production, i.e. to the total capital advanced, or from a reduction in the difference between the rate of profit and the rate of surplus-value.\" A terminological clarification is necessary before the law can be assessed. \"Costs of production\" is used here, as Marx immediately specifies, to mean the total capital advanced — the full denominator C = c + v — not the narrower \"cost price\" of the commodity, which would exclude the surplus-value but also, in the standard Volume 3 usage, treat the whole capital as consumed. The identification is made explicit in the apposition: \"i.e. to the total capital advanced.\" The law then identifies two branches. The first is an increase in surplus-value relative to the total capital advanced. This can be absolute — more surplus-value in the same total capital, as when the rate of exploitation rises or the working day lengthens; or relative — the surplus-value holds its ground while the total capital shrinks, as would occur if constant capital were cheapened. In either direction, the numerator of p′ = s/C rises without an offsetting rise in the denominator. The second branch is \"a reduction in the difference between the rate of profit and the rate of surplus-value.\" Since p′ = s′ · (1 − c/C), the gap between the two rates is s′ − p′ = s′ · c/C, which is precisely the rate of surplus-value discounted by the weight of constant capital in the total advance. To reduce that gap is to reduce the weight of constant capital in the total — that is, to move the composition in the direction of labour. The two branches are therefore not independent causes but two descriptions of one underlying relation: the profit rate rises when surplus-value gains on the capital against which it is measured, whether by growing or by shrinking the measure. This is the theorem Rodbertus's view denies. If the profit rate could vary only with capital magnitude, and if magnitude changes always moved numerator and denominator in step, then the profit rate would have no determinants of its own and could never systematically rise or fall. Marx's two branches identify exactly where the movement comes from: from the relation between surplus-value and the total advance, which is a relation between value magnitudes and value proportions, not a matter of scale. 2.11 Autonomous Fluctuations: Reproduction Cost and the Revaluation of Capital The final paragraph of the body of the argument addresses a residual question: can the profit rate fluctuate without any change in composition and without any change in absolute magnitude? Marx's answer supplies the last channel. \"Fluctuations in the rate of profit that are independent of changes in either the capital's organic components or its absolute magnitude are possible only if the value of the capital advanced, whatever might be the form — fixed or circulating — in which it exists, rises or falls as a result of an increase or decrease in the labour-time necessary for its reproduction, an increase or decrease that is independent of the capital already in existence.\" The phrase \"whatever might be the form — fixed or circulating\" extends the mechanism to the whole capital stock, whether it is tied up in machinery and buildings or in materials and wages. The condition \"independent of the capital already in existence\" is decisive: the change in value does not come from anything the capital itself does; it comes from outside, from changed conditions of production in the branches that supply the capital's components. Marx then states the general principle of value that licenses this: \"The value of any commodity — and thus also of the commodities which capital consists of — is determined not by the necessary labour-time that it itself contains, but by the socially necessary labour-time required for its reproduction.\" This is the distinction between embodied and reproduction labour-time, and it is a foundational claim of Marx's theory, not a local observation. The value of a machine is not fixed at the moment it is produced and thereafter immutable; it is what it would cost, in socially necessary labour-time, to produce that machine now. \"This reproduction may differ from the conditions of its original production by taking place under easier or more difficult circumstances.\" The illustration is stark. \"If the changed circumstances mean that twice as much time, or alternatively only half as much, is required for the same physical capital to be reproduced, then given an unchanged value of money, this capital, if it was previously worth £100, would now be worth £200, or alternatively £50.\" The physical capital is untouched: same machines, same buildings, same inventories. Only the labour-time socially necessary to reproduce them has changed — and with it, the value of the capital on the books. The consequence for capital as a stock is significant and should be registered explicitly. Capital is continuously exposed to revaluation. A rise in productivity in the branches producing means of production devalues existing constant capital; a rise in the difficulty of extraction or production revalues it upward. The capitalist's \"capital advanced\" is therefore not a fixed quantity deposited once and retrieved unchanged; it is a value magnitude perpetually at the mercy of social productivity. This is the mechanism — here presented in its abstract form — that later analysis calls the moral depreciation of fixed capital. The present paragraph establishes the general principle from which that result follows: value is a social relation in the present tense, not a fossilized record of past labour. 2.12 The Closing Reconciliation: Proportional versus Differential Revaluation The chapter ends by distinguishing two sub-cases within the revaluation channel, and in doing so returns to the point from which the Rodbertus discussion began. \"If this increase or decrease in value affects all components of the capital equally, the profit is also expressed accordingly in twice or only half the monetary sum.\" A uniform doubling of the value of all components — means of production and means of subsistence alike — doubles the money expression of the capital advanced. But because the means of subsistence also double in value, the value of labour-power doubles, so v doubles in money terms while the real wage is unchanged; the working day and the surplus labour time are unchanged, so the surplus product's value likewise doubles in money terms. Numerator and denominator are scaled together; the organic composition, in the chapter's own gloss, is untouched; \"the profit rate then remains unchanged.\" This case, though it involves a real change in the value of capital rather than a merely monetary one, is formally parallel to Exception One: proportionality in revaluation is neutral to the profit rate. \"But if it involves a change in the organic composition of the capital, the ratio between the variable and the constant portions of the capital, then, if other circumstances remain the same, the profit rate will rise with a relatively rising share of variable capital and fall with a relatively falling share.\" This is the general result of §2.4 restated in dynamic dress: the profit rate moves inversely with the weight of constant capital. A differential revaluation — one that raises the value of constant capital relative to variable capital, or vice versa — alters c/v even if the physical capital and the scale of operations are unchanged, and the profit rate moves accordingly. Note that the chapter's own definition of organic composition (\"the proportion between its variable and its constant parts\") is what allows this to be stated cleanly: a differential revaluation is a change in organic composition, even where the use-value basis of production is unaltered. The final sentence closes the loop with the first exception: \"If it is only the money value that rises or falls (as a result of a change in the value of money), the monetary expression of the surplus-value rises or falls in the same proportion. The profit rate then remains unchanged.\" Money-value changes scale the expression of surplus-value uniformly; real, proportional revaluations scale capital and profit uniformly; only differential revaluations — and composition changes more broadly — move the rate. The chapter thus closes with a compact taxonomy. Two invariance conditions: a composition-neutral change in real capital magnitude (Exception Two), and a proportional revaluation of all components, whether of money or of capital goods (Exception One, extended). One mover: any change in the ratio of constant to variable capital, whether arising from technical change, from changes in the relative values of the components, or from differential revaluation. Rodbertus's proportional-scaling picture is contained within the taxonomy as one of its special cases — and thereby deprived of its claim to universality. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Four fundamental principles emerge from this short chapter, and each has consequences well beyond its immediate occasion. First: the profit rate is a relation, not a magnitude. The chapter's central analytic achievement is to insist that the same surplus-value, extracted under identical exploitation from identical numbers of workers, is compatible with widely different profit rates. The formula p′ = s′ · (1 − c/C) makes the dependence explicit. Every practical consequence follows from this: absolute profit and profit rate can move in opposite directions; a firm can earn the same profit as its competitor while reporting a lower rate; the \"efficiency\" of a capitalist and the \"exploitation\" of his workers are conceptually distinct magnitudes that only appear fused in the ratio. The rate of profit is the surplus-value discounted by the weight of the capital against which it is measured — and that discount factor is a social and technical fact, not an individual virtue. Second: the value of capital is a present-tense social relation. The chapter's invocation of socially necessary reproduction labour-time against embodied labour-time is a foundational claim, not a local technicality. Capital advanced is not a conserved reservoir of past labour; it is a value magnitude continuously exposed to revaluation by changes in social productivity elsewhere in the economy. This has direct implications for any accounting of profitability over time: rates of profit can move without any change in the physical capital stock or in the scale of operations, purely because the labour-time required to reproduce that stock has changed. Third: ideology is generated structurally, not psychologically. The bourgeois's refusal to identify profit with surplus-value is grounded in two real features of the system — the inversion of production and realization in the circuit of capital, and the genuine dispersion of profit rates among firms. Because the dispersion is real, the inference from \"my rate depends on my acumen\" to \"my profit originates in my acumen\" is not a random error. It is a necessary appearance , one that can only be dissolved by an analysis demonstrating how the rate of profit is constituted. This is a general model for how the surface forms of a social system produce the categories through which its agents understand it — and it explains why critique must be theoretical rather than merely informational. Fourth: methodological — the critique of the proportionality fallacy. Rodbertus's error is not a factual mistake but a modelling mistake: he treats capital magnitude as a variable that can change while all else is held constant, and thereby converts a special case into a general law. Marx's reply is the classic critical move: enumerate the exact conditions under which the claim holds, show that they are exceptions, and identify the mechanism that operates in every other case. This is the method of the chapter as a whole, and it is worth naming: the general law is established by rigorously delimiting the space of its exceptions. The broader implications follow from these principles. Analytically, the chapter defines the limits of the sphere-level identity of profit and surplus-value and thereby marks exactly what remains to be explained once the assumption is relaxed — the equalization of profit rates across spheres into a general rate of profit. Methodologically, it demonstrates that the theory of the profit rate cannot be reduced to the theory of the scale of capital: the profit rate has its own determinants, in the composition of capital and the value relations among its components. Ideologically, it supplies the micro-foundations of a critique that remains pertinent wherever profit is explained by entrepreneurial skill, cost discipline, or managerial excellence — the chapter's point being that these are determinants of how much capital is measured against , not of how much surplus-value is produced . And philosophically, it offers one of Marx's clearest statements of the dialectic of essence and appearance: the identity of profit and surplus-value is true of the system as a whole and invisible from any vantage point within it, and the task of theory is not to deny the appearance but to reconstruct the totality in which it is grounded. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 6 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 8"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 8", "title": "Chapter 8", "subtitle": "Different Compositions of Capital in Different Branches of Production", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 8/Chapter 8 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 6303, "read_time": "32 min read", "tags": ["Transformation Problem & Production Prices", "Organic Composition of Capital (OCC)"], "keywords": ["Different Compositions of Capital in Production Branches", "Equal Rates of Surplus-Value with Unequal Profit Rates", "Contradiction of Labour Theory of Value with Empirical Profits", "Technical vs Value Composition of Capital", "Apparent Rupture between Value and Profit"], "text": "Chapter 8 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,280 words ⏱️ ~31 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 8 opens the second Part of Volume 3 — \"The Transformation of Profit into Average Profit\" — and it is a chapter of deliberate narrowness whose narrowness turns out to be its most dangerous feature. Its immediate objective is modest and technical: to demonstrate, on a strictly controlled set of assumptions, that capitals of equal size invested in different spheres of production must yield unequal rates of profit , because the organic composition of capital (and, secondarily, the length of turnover) differs from sphere to sphere. The chapter therefore does not yet explain the formation of a general rate of profit; it explains the obstacle that such a rate must overcome. It is the negative prelude to the positive theory that follows. Marx constructs his argument in three moves. First, he narrows the field with a set of idealizing assumptions: the rate of surplus-value, the length of the working day, and therefore the wage level are held constant across all spheres, so that everything he has demonstrated in Part One about the effect of changing values and turnover times on a single capital can be transposed from a diachronic to a synchronic register — what was previously a succession of states of one capital now becomes a set of simultaneous differences among many capitals. Second, he executes the analytic core of the chapter: a series of arithmetical illustrations in which capitals of identical magnitude (100 units) split into wildly different proportions of constant and variable capital yield correspondingly different masses of surplus-value, hence radically unequal rates of profit. This is the chapter's decisive theorem, and it rests on a single bedrock proposition — living labour is the sole source of surplus-value — reinforced by a reductio: if a 90c + 10v capital produced the same profit as a 10c + 90v capital, \"surplus-value and hence value in general had a completely different source from labour, and in this way any rational basis for political economy would fall away.\" Third, Marx confronts the consequence, which is explosive: if his theory of value implies permanently divergent profit rates, then the theory seems flatly contradicted by the observable world, where no such divergence exists and, he insists, none could exist without abolishing the entire capitalist system. That last admission is the pivot of the chapter. Its closing sentences do not resolve the contradiction; they set it up and leave it hanging — \"The theory of value thus appears incompatible with the actual movement, incompatible with the actual phenomena of production, and it might seem that we must abandon all hope of understanding these phenomena.\" The final paragraph offers the escape hatch: because the cost price is identical for equally-sized capitals regardless of composition (the capitalist cannot tell whether his £100 is 90c + 10v or 10c + 90v), competition between capitals is waged on the terrain of equal cost prices, and it is on that terrain that an average profit is generated. Chapter 8 thus closes at the threshold of the transformation problem. Theoretically, it belongs to Marx's larger project of demonstrating that the concrete forms of capitalist economic life (prices of production, average profit) are not anomalies standing beside the labour theory of value but necessary appearances generated by it. Intellectually, the chapter functions as a stress test of the value theory itself — it shows the theory producing a result that falsifies the phenomenal world, and thereby forces the introduction of a mediating mechanism (the average rate of profit formed through competition) without which the theory would collapse. The chapter is therefore indispensable: it is where Marx deliberately makes his own theory look wrong in order to show why things are not as they seem. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Methodological Premise: Idealization and the \"Concept\" of a Sphere of Production Marx begins by recalling the achievement of Part One (\"In the previous Part we showed, among other things, how the rate of profit may vary, either rising or falling, even with the same rate of surplus-value\") and then immediately erects the scaffolding of this chapter's argument: \"we now assume that the degree of exploitation of labour, i.e. the rate of surplus-value, and the length of the working day, is the same in all the spheres of production.\" This is more than a simplifying hypothesis; it is a methodological confession. Marx knows that in reality the rate of surplus-value differs enormously from industry to industry, and that wages vary widely. His justification for ignoring these differences is drawn from Adam Smith, whom he credits in a footnote and in the text with having shown that inter-sphere variations in wages and profits \"cancel one another out through all kinds of compensations, either real or accepted by prejudice.\" The footnote on Smith is unusually rich in editorial judgment (probably Engels's): Smith's Wealth of Nations (1776) \"gave bourgeois political economy its developed form,\" and was \"not just scientifically important, but also a major ideological weapon for the developing industrial capitalist class.\" Marx's relationship to Smith is thus doubly coded — Smith is the man who had already done the preliminary empirical spadework of showing that such variations wash out, yet he is also the man whose fundamental theoretical conceptions Marx subjects, elsewhere ( Theories of Surplus-Value ), to his fullest critique. Marx then extends the idealization to the wage differentials that correspond to the distinction between simple and complex labour (the topic of the first chapter of Volume 1, p. 135 in the Pelican edition). His point here is analytically sharp: the higher pay of a goldsmith relative to a day-labourer does not alter the degree of exploitation , because the goldsmith's skilled labour also produces a proportionately greater surplus-value. \"In a general analysis of the present kind, it is assumed throughout that actual conditions correspond to their concept, or, and this amounts to the same thing, actual conditions are depicted only in so far as they express their own general type.\" This sentence is the theoretical charter of the whole chapter and deserves close attention. \"Actual conditions correspond to their concept\" ( Begriff ) is a Hegelian formulation meaning that Marx abstracts from contingent frictions in order to expose the essential logic of the capitalist mode of production. Frictions in wage equalization — local obstacles to the equalization of wages and working hours — are \"accidental and inessential\" for the general investigation, however important for specialist work on wages. Marx is deliberately declaring what he is not explaining, so that the phenomenon he is explaining can be seen in pure form. Two boundary conditions are also stated. First, differences in the rate of surplus-value between countries are \"completely outside the scope of our present investigation,\" whose object \"is simply to present the way in which a general rate of profit is arrived at within one particular country.\" Second, Marx sketches how the international case would eventually be handled — first vary the national rates of surplus-value, then compare national profit rates on that given basis; whatever residual variation remains must be due to circumstances in which, \"as in this chapter, surplus-value is assumed to be everywhere the same.\" From Diachronic Succession to Synchronic Coexistence The next move is one of the most elegant methodological operations in the entire volume. In Part One, Marx had shown that a single capital's rate of profit could rise or fall under a constant rate of surplus-value if (a) the value of some portion of its constant capital changed (altering the c/v ratio), or (b) its turnover time lengthened or shortened. The amount of profit (= amount of surplus-value) was unaffected by these value fluctuations; only the rate , i.e. profit relative to the capital advanced, was modified — except indirectly, where such fluctuations tied up or released capital. Now Marx performs the transposition: \"What we previously viewed as changes that the same capital underwent in succession, we now consider as simultaneous distinctions between capital investments that exist alongside one another in different spheres of production.\" The law governing a succession of states of one capital becomes the law governing a coexistence of different capitals . Hence his two announced investigative tasks: (1) differences in the organic composition of capitals, and (2) differences in their turnover time. A crucial qualifier is appended: whenever the chapter speaks of the composition or turnover of capital \"in a specific branch of production,\" it means \"the normal, average situation for capital invested in this branch,\" not \"chance differences between individual capitals invested there.\" The unit of analysis is the sphere or branch, represented by its average capital — which is why the chapter's conclusions apply to industrial averages, not to individual firms. The Two-Faced Nature of Variable Capital Before introducing the composition concept formally, Marx pauses to establish that with s' and the working day constant, variable capital functions as an index of the mass of labour-power set in motion . If £100 is the weekly wage of 100 workers, then n × £100 is the wage of n × 100 workers, and if £100 represents 6000 hours of labour (100 workers × 60 hours), then £200 represents 12,000 hours and £50 only 3000. The variable capital serves \"as an index of the mass of labour set in motion by a certain total capital.\" This seemingly innocuous convention sets up the chapter's deepest dependency: everything hinges on the fact that variable capital stands proxy for living labour , not merely for the wage bill. The Three Compositions: Technical, Value, Organic Marx now defines, with terminological care, the triad on which the whole chapter turns. \"By the composition of capital we mean... the ratio between its active and its passive component, between variable and constant capital.\" But two distinct relationships hide within this single expression. The first is the technical composition of capital : the proportion, determined by technical conditions at a given stage of productivity, between a definite mass of labour-power (a number of workers) and a definite mass of means of production (machines, raw materials) that those workers set in motion and productively consume. \"A definite number of workers corresponds to a definite quantity of means of production, and thus a definite amount of living labour to a definite amount of labour already objectified in means of production.\" This proportion may be the same in branches of industry that are very far apart, and may differ between branches of the same industry. Marx calls technical composition \"the actual basis of its organic composition.\" The second is the value composition of capital : the ratio of the values of the variable and constant portions. It follows the technical composition only when values happen to line up. The decisive illustration: certain operations in copper or iron might involve the same technical proportion between labour-power and means of production, but \"because copper is dearer than iron, the value relationship between variable and constant capital will be different in each case.\" The distinction reveals itself in two ways: the value ratio can change while the technical composition stays constant (price changes), and the value ratio can stay the same while the technical composition changes (if the change in quantities is cancelled by an opposite change in their values). The organic composition of capital — the chapter's master concept — is then defined precisely: \"the name we give to its value composition, in so far as this is determined by its technical composition and reflects it.\" The distinction is essential. A mere value composition (say, a temporarily inflated price of copper) is not an organic composition; only a value ratio that expresses and reflects an underlying technical ratio qualifies. This guards Marx against conflating price perturbations with structural composition. He then re-specifies the assumptions in the light of this: variable capital is taken as an index of a definite mass of living labour (not, as in Part One, of a possibly-changed price for the same labour); a difference in the constant capital may index either a different volume of means of production set in motion, or a different value of those means of production — and \"here, therefore, these two aspects both come into consideration.\" The Wage as Index of More Labour Than It Contains There follows one of the chapter's most philosophically loaded passages, the \"fundamental point\" Marx insists must be noted. Assume £100 is the weekly wage of 100 workers; the working week is 60 hours; the rate of surplus-value is 100 per cent. The workers work 30 hours for themselves and 30 gratis. The £100 wage embodies only 3000 hours (the necessary labour of 100 workers × 30 hours), yet the same workers perform 6000 hours, the other 3000 being embodied in the £100 of surplus-value the capitalist pockets. There is therefore a fundamental distinction within variable capital itself : (1) as the value laid out in wages — a definite quantity of objectified labour (50 worker-weeks = £100); and (2) as an index of the mass of living labour it sets in motion (100 worker-weeks = 6000 hours). \"This last is always greater than the labour contained in the variable capital,\" and is expressed in a higher value determined by the number of workers set in motion and the quantity of surplus labour they perform. The wage of £1, \"the expression of half a week's labour, sets a whole week's labour in motion.\" This passage is doing double duty. It explains why £100 of variable capital can be the index of £200 of value product; and it pre-empts a potential confusion — namely that £100 \"contains\" only 50 weeks of labour, so that the operative magnitude must be defined by what it commands , not what it is . First Numerical Excursus: Unequal Organic Compositions at Equal Capital Size With the conceptual apparatus in place, Marx delivers the chapter's central demonstration. Consider two capitals of equal size, £700 each: Sphere A: 600c + 100v = 700 Sphere B: 100c + 600v = 700 Under s' = 100 per cent, A's 100v yields 100 of surplus-value; B's 600v yields 600. The surplus labour appropriated is 50 working weeks (3000 hours) in A and 300 working weeks (18,000 hours) in B — six times as much. Consequently: Rate of profit in A = 100/700 = 14 2/7 per cent Rate of profit in B = 600/700 = 85 5/7 per cent The rate in B is six times that in A, and — crucially — the mass of profit is also six times greater (600 versus 100), because six times as much living labour has been set in motion with the same capital. The general formula being instantiated here is worth writing out explicitly, since Marx's arithmetic presupposes it throughout: p' = s/(c + v) = s'·v / (c + v) where p' is the rate of profit, s is surplus-value, s' is the rate of surplus-value, and C = c + v is total capital. Equivalently, p' = s'·(v/C). With s' constant, the rate of profit is strictly proportional to the share of variable capital in the total capital — that is, to v/C. In A, v/C = 100/700 = 1/7, giving p' = 1 × 1/7 = 14 2/7 per cent. In B, v/C = 600/700 = 6/7, giving p' = 85 5/7 per cent. The entire chapter is a commentary on this formula. The Percentage Reduction: Why Absolute Scale Is Irrelevant Marx immediately generalizes by scaling A up. Suppose A invests not £700 but £7,000, while B remains at £700. Keeping A's organic composition (600c : 100v = 6:1), A now uses £1,000 as variable capital, employing 1000 workers for a week = 60,000 hours of living labour, of which 30,000 are surplus. A's profit grows to £1,000, but its rate of profit is unchanged: p'_A = 1000/7000 = 100/700 = 14 2/7 per cent against B's 85 5/7 per cent. \"With equal amounts of capital, the rates of profit here are different, since at equal rates of surplus-value the masses of surplus-value and therefore profit that are produced differ as a result of the different masses of living labour set in motion.\" The conceptual payoff is that \"differing organic compositions of capitals are thus independent of their absolute magnitudes. The only question is always how much of each 100 units is variable capital and how much is constant.\" This licenses the reduction of all capitals to percentage terms, which is exactly what the rest of the chapter does. Second Numerical Excursus: Equal Technical Basis, Unequal Constant Capital Values Marx now sharply distinguishes a second case from the first. Suppose the technical conditions are the same in A and B, but the value of the constant capital element differs — say, machinery and raw materials are simply dearer in B. Case A: 200c + 100v = 300 Case B: 400c + 100v = 500 At s' = 100 per cent, both produce £100 of surplus-value, hence £100 of profit. But: p'_A = 100/300 = 33⅓ per cent p'_B = 100/500 = 20 per cent Expressed per £100 of capital, in B only £20 (a fifth) forms variable capital, while in A £33⅓ (a third) is variable. \"B produces less profit for each £100 than does A, because it sets less living labour in motion [for each £100]. The difference in the rate of profit is thus reduced here again to a difference in the mass of profit — because mass of surplus-value — produced for each 100 units of capital invested.\" The distinction between this case and the first is then stated with precision: equalizing A and B in the second case would require only a change in the value of the constant capital with the technical basis unchanged; in the first case, \"the technical composition itself differs between the two spheres of production and would have to be transformed in order for such an equalization to occur.\" This is precisely the difference between the mere value composition and the organic composition defined earlier — the second case is a difference in value composition without a difference in organic composition properly understood, yet it produces the same effect on the profit rate. The upshot is that the quantitative variable doing the work is always the same: the proportion of each 100 units that is variable. The Reductio: The Impossible Alternative Marx then deploys the chapter's strongest arm — a reductio ad absurdum aimed at any attempt to derive profit from anything other than living labour. Capitals of equal size, operating with the same working day and degree of exploitation, produce very different amounts of surplus-value and therefore profit, and this is because their variable portions differ according to their differing organic composition. Hence \"equal-sized portions of the total capital in different spheres of production include sources of surplus-value of unequal size, and the only source of surplus-value is living labour.\" The reductio is stated bluntly: \"If a capital whose percentage composition is 90c + 10v were to produce just as much surplus-value or profit, at the same level of exploitation of labour, as a capital of 10c + 90v, it would be as clear as day that surplus-value and hence value in general had a completely different source from labour, and in this way any rational basis for political economy would fall away.\" Marx then puts numbers on the absurdity. With £1 as the weekly wage for 60 hours' work and s' = 100 per cent, a single worker's total value product in a week is £2. 10 workers (the 90c + 10v capital): total product £20, wages £10, so surplus-value = £10 → rate of profit 10 per cent . 90 workers (the 10c + 90v capital): total product £180, wages £90, so surplus-value = £90 → rate of profit 90 per cent . \"If it should be otherwise, value and surplus-value would have to be something other than objectified labour.\" This is the theoretical nerve-center of the chapter: the divergence of profit rates is not an unfortunate by-product of the analysis but a requirement of any theory in which labour is the sole source of value. A theory that produced equal profit rates for 90c + 10v and 10c + 90v could not be a labour theory of value at all. The Non-Proportionality of Profit to Capital Size An important corollary follows immediately and is stated with some emphasis: because capitals of equal size in different spheres — and therefore capitals of different sizes considered by percentage — produce unequal profits, \"the profits of unequal capitals in different spheres of production cannot stand in proportion to their respective sizes.\" If profits did increase in proportion to capital size, that would itself imply equal profit rates for equal capitals regardless of composition — which has just been shown impossible. \"It is only within the same sphere of production, where the organic composition of capital is therefore given, or between different spheres of production with the same organic composition of capital, that the mass of profit stands in exact proportion to the mass of capital employed.\" This is Marx isolating the restricted domain in which the naive \"profit is proportional to capital\" assumption actually holds — the same domain in which what Marx elsewhere calls the \"law of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall\" can be stated cleanly, namely the domain of a single composition. Value and Surplus-Value Before Averaging: The 110 and the 190 Marx then restates the argument in terms of commodity values, under the assumption that commodities are sold at their values. \"The value of a commodity is equal to the value of the constant capital contained in it, plus the value of the variable capital reproduced in it, plus the increment on this variable capital, the surplus-value produced.\" Given a certain rate of surplus-value, the mass of that surplus-value depends on the mass of variable capital. Hence: A capital of 100 composed as 90c + 10v + 10s = 110 A capital of 100 composed as 10c + 90v + 90s = 190 If commodities sell at their values, the first product sells at 110, of which 10 is surplus-value (unpaid labour); the second sells at 190, of which 90 is surplus-value. This restatement matters because it shows that the divergence in profit rates is not an artefact of applying profit to cost price, but is already present at the level of values and surplus-values. The divergence Marx identifies is one that exists before any question of price-of-production arises — which is exactly why it constitutes a problem for the theory. The International Comparison: Europe and Asia Marx then offers his most striking illustration — a comparison of national rates of profit. In a European country, s' might be 100 per cent (the worker works half the day for himself, half for his employer); in an Asian country it might be 25 per cent (four-fifths for himself, one-fifth for his employer). Assume further that the European composition of national capital is 84c + 16v , while the Asian, \"where little machinery, etc. is used and relatively little raw material productively consumed in a given period of time,\" is 16c + 84v . The calculation runs: Europe: value of product = 84c + 16v + 16s = 116 ; rate of profit = 16/100 = 16 per cent . Asia: value of product = 16c + 84v + 21s = 121 ; rate of profit = 21/100 = 21 per cent (84 × 0.25 = 21). Thus the rate of profit would be higher in the Asian country despite the rate of surplus-value being only a fourth as great. Marx notes with relish that \"Carey, Bastiat and their like would draw precisely the opposite conclusion\" — a reference to the apologetic \"harmony of interests\" economists. The footnote on Bastiat is characteristically acidic: Marx regarded him as \"the most superficial and thus successful representative of apologetic vulgar economics,\" citing the Postface to the Second German Edition of Volume 1. Two caveats must be noted honestly here. First, as Marx himself immediately remarks, \"different national rates of profit generally depend on different national rates of surplus-value; but in this chapter we are comparing unequal rates of profit that spring from one and the same rate of surplus-value.\" This illustration is therefore a counterfactual application of the chapter's method to a case that is outside the chapter's stated scope — its purpose is purely to display the elegance of the composition effect. Second, an arithmetic nuance deserves flagging: the text as printed says the Asian rate would be \"some 25 per cent higher\" than the European, whereas 21 per cent is in fact about 31 per cent higher than 16 per cent (the absolute difference being 5 percentage points). The figure is either loose or reflects numerical rounding in the manuscript; the direction and magnitude of the result are unaffected, and the substantive point — that a high-composition, high-exploitation economy can have a lower national profit rate than a low-composition, low-exploitation economy — stands. Turnover Time as the Second Source of Disparity Marx now turns to his second announced variable: \"Besides the differing organic composition of capital... there is a further source of inequality between rates of profit: the variation in the length of capital turnover in the different spheres of production.\" Drawing on Chapter 4, he recalls that with the same composition of capital, rates of profit vary inversely to turnover time, and that the same variable capital turning over in different periods brings in unequal masses of surplus-value over the course of a year. Turnover variation is therefore a second cause of why equal capitals need not produce equally large profits in equal periods of time. This is the point at which the synchronic transposition is most fully at work: the temporal dimension (how long a capital takes to return) becomes one of the axes along which different spheres differ, no longer a process a single capital undergoes but a structural characteristic distinguishing one sphere from another. Fixed and Circulating Capital: A Distinction Without Independent Force Marx then disposes of a possible objection with considerable surgical care. Does the proportion in which capital is composed of fixed and circulating elements affect the profit rate? His answer: not in itself. \"It can only affect it either if this differing composition coincides with a differing ratio between the variable and constant portions, in which case the variation in the rate of profit is due to this difference and not to the different ratio between circulating and fixed; or alternatively if the varying ratio between fixed and circulating components involves a variation in the turnover time that it takes to realize a certain profit.\" He elaborates: capitals with different proportions of fixed and circulating capital do indeed have different turnover times, and this does influence the profit rate where it materially affects the mass of surplus-value a capital can appropriate in a given time. But turnover variations are significant \"only in so far as it affects the mass of surplus-value that the same capital can appropriate and realize in a given time.\" Where unequal circulating/fixed compositions do not entail unequal turnover times, the unequal composition yields nothing: the two capitals \"realize equal profits in equal times, even though they take different times to turn over.\" The Engels footnote 21 pertinently illustrates and qualifies the argument, warning that Marx's claim about fixed/circulating capital and equal rates of profit \"is correct only when capitals A and B have a different value composition, but nevertheless their variable components in percentage terms are directly proportionate to their turnover times, or in inverse proportion to their number of turnovers.\" Engels then supplies a numerical proof: Capital A = 20c fixed + 70c circulating = 90c + 10v = 100. With s' = 100 per cent, the 10v produces 10s in one turnover; rate of profit for the turnover = 10 per cent . If A turns over twice a year, its annual rate is 20 per cent . Capital B = 60c fixed + 20c circulating = 80c + 20v = 100. The 20v produces 20s per turnover; rate per turnover = 20 per cent . If B turns over once, its annual rate is also 20 per cent . The two capitals realize equal annual rates despite differing composition (fixed/circulating) and differing turnover counts. The general conclusion Marx draws is that the differing proportions of circulating and fixed capital \"do not have any bearing in themselves on the rate of profit; what is decisive is the ratio between the variable capital and the constant.\" He adds a warning against an epidemiological error of inference: where fixed capital is strongly developed, this is merely an expression of the fact that production is large-scale and constant capital predominates over variable — it is the predominance , not the fixedness, that lowers the profit rate. Anyone who saw the vast fixed capital of modern industry and concluded that fixed capital as such depresses the profit rate would have \"incorrect conclusions\" foisted upon them. Interim Summary and the Antinomy of the Theory of Value Marx now consolidates: in different branches of industry unequal profit rates prevail, corresponding to different organic compositions and — within the stated limits — different turnover times. So, at a given rate of surplus-value, only for capitals of the same organic composition (with equal turnover times) does the law hold, as a general tendency, that profits stand in direct proportion to the amount of capital, and that capitals of equal size yield equal profits in the same period of time. And then comes the fracture. \"The above argument is true on the same basis as our whole investigation so far: that commodities are sold at their values. There is no doubt, however, that in actual fact, ignoring inessential, accidental circumstances that cancel each other out, no such variation in the average rate of profit exists between different branches of industry, and it could not exist without abolishing the entire system of capitalist production. The theory of value thus appears incompatible with the actual movement, incompatible with the actual phenomena of production, and it might seem that we must abandon all hope of understanding these phenomena.\" This is the chapter's climax and its most self-critical moment. Marx juxtaposes two propositions: (a) the theory of value, on his premises, entails permanently divergent profit rates across spheres; (b) empirically and by the logic of capitalist competition, no such divergence exists, and none could exist, since capital mobility would immediately level any differential. The opposition is not merely apparent but real at this stage of the analysis. If (a) is true, the theory seems falsified by (b) — the very phenomena the theory is meant to explain appear to contradict it. Marx does not pretend the contradiction away with a hand-wave; he states it in its most damaging form and defers the resolution. The Bridge to Resolution: The Equality of Cost Prices The final paragraph supplies the first plank of that resolution. From Part One it emerged that cost prices are the same for the products of different spheres of production if equal portions of capital are advanced in their production , no matter how different the organic composition of those capitals. In the cost price, \"the distinction between variable and constant capital is abolished, as far as the capitalist is concerned.\" A commodity that costs the capitalist £100 to produce costs him the same whether he lays out 90c + 10v or 10c + 90v. \"In each case it costs him £100, neither more nor less. Cost prices are the same for equal capital investments in different spheres, however much the values and surplus-values produced may differ.\" This equality of cost prices is the basis for the competition between capital investments by means of which an average profit is produced . The chapter thus ends on a promissory note: the very equality of cost prices that obscures the underlying divergence of surplus-value masses is also the concrete mechanism through which capitals compete and thereby level the profit rate. The apparent antinomy will be dissolved not by abandoning the value theory, but by showing that the average rate of profit is itself a form generated by the value relations the theory describes — the subject of the chapters that follow. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 1. The whole chapter rests on a single identification: constant rate of surplus-value + varying composition = varying rate of profit. In algebraic form, p' = s' · (v/C). Everything else is elaboration. The theorem is not merely a curiosity; it is the direct expression of the proposition that living labour is the sole source of surplus-value. Any theory that generated equal profit rates for 90c + 10v and 10c + 90v would have already abandoned the labour theory of value. The chapter is therefore, in effect, a proof by reductio of the labour theory of value's internal consistency — a demonstration that the theory's apparent embarrassments flow from its own logic, not from its failures. 2. The synchronic transposition is the methodological key. Marx's procedure — reading a succession of states of one capital as a coexistence of many differently-composed capitals — is what allows the analytic apparatus of Part One to be repurposed to explain structural differences between spheres. This is a general feature of Marx's method: dynamics investigated at one level of abstraction become statics at a higher level. What looks like a temporal process (a capital's turnover, a change in the value of its constant elements) becomes a systematic differentiation (between branches, between national economies). 3. The chapter distinguishes sharply between three uses of \"composition.\" Technical composition (a physical ratio of labour-power to means of production), value composition (a ratio of their values ), and organic composition (value composition insofar as it reflects technical composition). This triad is not pedantry. It allows Marx to show that equalizing capitals in the second numerical case (differing values of the same technical quantity) requires only a change of values, whereas equalizing them in the first (differing technical ratios) requires a transformation of the technical basis itself. It also guards against the error of treating every price-driven shift in c/v as a change in organic composition. 4. Variable capital is theorized as an index of living labour , not simply as a wage bill. The wage of £1 that expresses half a week's labour sets a whole week's labour in motion. This is why £100 of variable capital, which contains only 50 worker-weeks of objectified labour, can be the index of 100 worker-weeks of living labour and of a value product of £200. The subtlety pre-empts a cruder reading in which the profit rate could be read off simply from wage costs. 5. Profit is not proportional to capital size in general — and the restricted domain where it is must be carefully specified. The law of proportionality holds only within a sphere (where composition is given) or between spheres of identical composition. This negative result is exactly why a general rate of profit must be formed through competition rather than presupposed — and it is why Marx calls the divergence between the theory of value and the uniform observable profit rate an \"antinomy.\" 6. Turnover time is a second, independent axis of disparity. Combined with composition, it accounts for all divergences at a constant rate of surplus-value. But its effect is mediated entirely through its impact on the mass of surplus-value a capital can appropriate in a given period; where it does not have this effect, it is irrelevant. Similarly, the fixed/circulating distinction has no independent force — it acts only through its effect on c/v or on turnover. 7. The chapter deliberately makes the labour theory of value look falsified — and leaves it hanging. The most intellectually striking feature of Chapter 8 is its refusal to smooth over the contradiction. Marx asserts in the strongest terms that no variation in the average rate of profit can exist between branches, and that this result — paradoxically — flows from the very analysis that seems to require variation. He does not retreat into the \"prove it empirically\" evasion; he states that theory and reality are, at this stage, flatly opposed. The resolution is deferred to the mechanism of competition operating on the terrain of equal cost prices — cost prices being the one magnitude on which composition has no effect and through which capitals can therefore be compared and levelled. 8. The broader implication is philosophical and political. Philosophically, the chapter demonstrates the necessity of the movement from essence (value and surplus-value) to appearance (price of production, average profit) in Marx's system. The apparent contradiction between the theory and the phenomena is not a refutation but a task : to show how the uniform rate of profit is a necessary form of appearance generated by the value relations themselves. Politically, the demonstration that a low-exploitation, low-composition economy (Marx's Asia) can have a higher profit rate than a high-exploitation, high-composition economy (Marx's Europe) demolishes at a stroke the apologetic \"harmony\" arguments of Carey and Bastiat — whose conclusions, as Marx writes, are \"precisely the opposite\" of his own. The rate of profit is not a moral reward for capital's productivity; it is a quotient determined by the fraction of capital laid out in living labour. The higher the exploitation, the more machinery, the richer the country — and, paradoxically, the lower the rate of profit. That inversion, established here in the abstract, is the seed of the whole subsequent analysis of capitalist dynamics in Volume 3. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 7 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 9"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 9", "title": "Chapter 9", "subtitle": "Formation of a General Rate of Profit (Average Rate of Profit)", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 9/Chapter 9 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 10646, "read_time": "53 min read", "tags": ["Transformation Problem & Production Prices", "Organic Composition of Capital (OCC)"], "keywords": ["Formation of a General / Average Rate of Profit", "Transformation of Values into Prices of Production", "Total Value = Total Price and Total Surplus-Value = Total Profit", "Deviation of Production Prices from Values by Organic Composition", "Social Average Organic Composition of Capital", "Redistribution of Surplus-Value Across Branches"], "text": "Chapter 9 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 10,737 words ⏱️ ~54 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 9: Formation of a General Rate of Profit (Average Rate of Profit), and Transformation of Commodity Values into Prices of Production 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Core Thesis Chapter 9 constitutes one of the most decisive theoretical pivots in all of Marx's Capital — indeed, one of the decisive pivots in the entire history of economic thought. Its central thesis is that, under developed capitalist production, commodities are not sold at their values but at prices of production , and that these prices of production arise from the formation of a general (average) rate of profit . The chapter demonstrates that the different rates of profit initially prevailing in different spheres of production — rates determined by the differing organic compositions of the capitals there invested — are leveled out by competition into a single general rate of profit. Once this general rate exists, the profit that each individual capital appropriates is no longer the surplus-value it itself produces, but rather its aliquot share of the total social surplus-value, distributed in proportion to the size of the capital advanced. The immediate consequence is that the price of production of a commodity equals its cost price plus the average profit, and this price systematically diverges from the commodity's value — except where a sphere's capital composition happens to coincide with the social average. The chapter is thus, at one and the same time, (a) a technical-mechanical derivation of how the average rate of profit is formed, (b) a resolution of the apparent contradiction between the labor theory of value and the equalization of profit rates under competition, and (c) a profound epistemological argument about how the appearance of the capitalist process (profit arising uniformly from the total capital) systematically conceals its essence (surplus-value arising exclusively from living labor). The chapter is simultaneously a work of economic science and a work of ideology critique. The Central Problem The problem the chapter confronts is precisely the \" great contradiction \" of the labor theory of value: if value is determined by labor-time, and if the surplus-value produced in each sphere depends on the quantity of living labor employed, then spheres with different organic compositions (different ratios of constant to variable capital) should have systematically different rates of profit. But under capitalist competition, capitals of equal size tend to earn equal profits regardless of their composition. How can both be true? Marx's answer — and this is the chapter's most important contribution — is that they are both true, but at different levels of abstraction. Value is the substance ; price of production is its transformed form . The equality of profit rates is achieved precisely through the redistribution of the total social surplus-value pooled from all spheres, and this redistribution makes prices of production diverge from values in a systematic, mutually cancelling way. The total sum of prices of production equals the total sum of values; total surplus-value equals total profit. Value is not abolished but transformed — its determination \"is now removed from view.\" Intellectual Objective The chapter's objective is threefold. First, to construct the average rate of profit from the differing organic compositions of capitals in different spheres, showing that it is nothing mystical but a mathematical resultant of these compositions weighted by the sizes of capitals invested. Second, to define the key categories — average profit, price of production, cost price in its modified significance — with precision, deriving the formula price of production = k + kp' (where k is cost price and p' is the general rate of profit). Third, and crucially, to draw out the ideological consequences : once a general rate of profit is established, profit and surplus-value diverge not merely in form but in magnitude ; the origin of profit in the exploitation of living labor becomes entirely unrecognizable to the capitalist, who is \"imprisoned in the competitive struggle\"; and the determination of value by labor-time now \"percolates through only in this crudified and naive form.\" The chapter thus sets the stage for the rest of Volume 3 (the falling rate of profit, merchant's capital, interest, rent) and for Volume 4 ( Theories of Surplus-Value ), where Marx will show that \"all economics up till now\" has either violently abstracted from these distinctions or abandoned any scientific foundation. Fitting into the Broader Framework This chapter is the hinge of Volume 3. In Volume 1, Marx developed the theory of value and surplus-value at the level of capital in general — assuming commodities sell at their values. In Volume 2, he developed the circuits and turnover of capital, still abstracting from the competitive formation of prices. Volume 3's \"first Part\" (Chapters 1–8) had already introduced the transformation of surplus-value into profit, and of the rate of surplus-value into the rate of profit — a change of form in which profit still equaled surplus-value in magnitude. Chapter 9 now introduces the quantitative divergence: with the general rate of profit, the profit a capital earns differs genuinely from the surplus-value it produces. This is the \"second transformation\" (after surplus-value → profit) that completes the move from essence to appearance, laying the groundwork for the analysis of commercial profit, interest, rent, and the tendency of the rate of profit to fall — all of which operate on the terrain of prices of production, not values. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Organic Composition of Capital and its Two Determinants Marx opens by restating the determinants of the organic composition of capital — a concept central to everything that follows. At any given time, this composition depends on two factors: first , the technical proportion between labour-power and the means of production applied (how much machinery, raw material, etc., is set in motion by a given quantity of living labor); and second , the price of those means of production. This dual determination is crucial. The technical composition (a physical ratio) becomes a value composition only when it is weighted by the prices (values) of the elements involved. Marx's \"organic composition\" is precisely this unity of the technical and the value composition — the value composition insofar as it reflects the technical composition. For analytical tractability, Marx insists this composition must be considered in percentage terms . He expresses a capital consisting of four-fifths constant and one-fifth variable capital by the formula 80c + 20v . He then assumes, \"for the sake of comparison,\" an unchanged rate of surplus-value of 100 percent — noting \"any rate will do.\" This assumption is a methodological device: by holding the rate of exploitation constant, Marx isolates the effect of varying organic composition on the rate of profit. The capital of 80c + 20v then yields a surplus-value of 20, which makes a rate of profit of 20 percent on the total capital (since profit is calculated on the total capital advanced, c + v = 100, not on v alone). Marx then makes two simplifying assumptions whose immateriality he stresses. First, he brackets the question of fixed capital — the actual value of the product depends on how large the fixed portion of constant capital is and how much of it enters the product as depreciation. But \"since this fact is completely immaterial as far as the rate of profit is concerned,\" he assumes all constant capital enters wholly into the annual product. Second, he assumes that capitals in different spheres realize the same amount of surplus-value in proportion to their variable components, and he ignores differences arising from variations in turnover time , promising to deal with this later. This bracketing of turnover is important: as we shall see, turnover time is later restored as a determinant of profit rates in different spheres. The Five-Sphere Illustration: Setting Up Divergent Profit Rates With these assumptions in place, Marx presents his foundational table of five different spheres of production , each with a different organic composition of capital invested in it. (The table appears across pages 1–2 of the text.) The five capitals are: Capital Composition Rate of Surplus-Value Surplus-Value Value of Product Rate of Profit I 80c + 20v 100% 20 120 20% II 70c + 30v 100% 30 130 30% III 60c + 40v 100% 40 140 40% IV 85c + 15v 100% 15 115 15% V 95c + 5v 100% 5 105 5% The mechanics here are transparent: with a 100 percent rate of surplus-value, surplus-value equals the variable capital (v), so surplus-value is 20, 30, 40, 15, and 5 respectively. The value of the product is c + v + s (assuming all c enters the product): 80 + 20 + 20 = 120, and so on. The rate of profit is s divided by the total capital (c + v = 100 in each case), yielding 20%, 30%, 40%, 15%, and 5%. The result is \"very different rates of profit in different spheres of production with a uniform exploitation of labour, rates which correspond to the differing organic composition of the capitals involved.\" This is the essential problem: equal exploitation, unequal profit rates — because profit is measured against total capital while surplus-value is produced only by the variable part. Note the crucial counter-intuitive feature Marx is highlighting: the higher the organic composition (the more constant relative to variable capital, i.e., capital III with 60c + 40v has the lowest composition and the highest profit rate), the lower the rate of profit. Capital V (95c + 5v), the most \"advanced\" or mechanized, has the lowest profit rate (5%). The sphere with the lowest composition (III, 60c + 40v) has the highest (40%). This sets up the illusion — which Marx will destroy — that living labor is not the source of profit, since more dead labor seems to yield more profit. The Aggregation as a Single Capital: Forming the Average Marx now performs the decisive aggregation. The total sum of capitals applied in the five spheres is 500 (five capitals of 100 each). The total surplus-value they produce is 110 (20 + 30 + 40 + 15 + 5). The total value of the commodities they produce is 610 (120 + 130 + 140 + 115 + 105). Now Marx asks us to treat the 500 as one single capital , \"with I–V simply forming different portions of it.\" He offers a vivid analogy: just as a cotton mill has different proportions between variable and constant capital in its various departments (carding, combing, spinning, weaving shops), and \"the average proportion has to be calculated for the entire factory,\" so too the social capital has different compositions in its different branches, and an average must be calculated. The average composition of the capital of 500 works out to 390c + 110v , or in percentages 78c + 22v . How? Total constant capital = 80 + 70 + 60 + 85 + 95 = 390. Total variable capital = 20 + 30 + 40 + 15 + 5 = 110. So 390c + 110v, or as percentages: 390/500 = 78%, 110/500 = 22%. Treating each of the five capitals of 100 as \"simply a fifth of the total capital,\" its composition becomes this average one of 78c + 22v. The average surplus-value of 22 (110 / 5) accrues to each of these capitals of 100, so the average rate of profit is 22 percent , and the price of each fifth of the total product is 122 (100 cost price + 22 average profit). This is the birth of the general rate of profit: 22 percent . The Cost Price Complication and its Non-Importance for the Rate of Profit Here Marx introduces an important technical refinement. He warns: \"in order not to arrive at totally incorrect conclusions, we must not take all the cost prices as 100.\" The problem is that the assumption that the entire constant capital enters the annual product (so that the value of commodity I is 80c + 20v + 20s = 120) \"may well be the case in some spheres of production, in certain conditions, but hardly with a ratio between c and v of 4:1.\" The fixed and circulating components of c differ across capitals, and fixed components depreciate at different rates, adding unequal quantities of value to the product. But — and this is the key point — \"this is immaterial, however, as far as the profit rate is concerned.\" Marx demonstrates this with a beautiful modus tollens: whether the 80c gives up its value of 80, or 50, or 5 to the annual product, and whether the annual product is accordingly 80c + 20v + 20s = 120, or 50c + 20v + 20s = 90, or 5c + 20v + 20s = 45 — in all these cases the excess of the value of the product over its cost price is 20 , and in all these cases this 20 must be calculated on a basis of 100 to arrive at the rate of profit. Hence the profit rate of capital I is always 20 percent. The cost price changes; the profit rate does not. This is because the rate of profit measures surplus-value against the total capital advanced (100), not against the cost price (the capital consumed). The Full Table with Different c Used Up To make this \"still clearer,\" Marx presents a second table (pages 3), in which different parts of the constant capital enter the value of the product. Let me reproduce and analyze it carefully: Capitals Rate of Surplus-Value Surplus-Value Rate of Profit c Used Up Value of Commodities Cost Price I. 80c + 20v 100% 20 20% 50 90 70 II. 70c + 30v 100% 30 30% 51 111 81 III. 60c + 40v 100% 40 40% 51 131 91 IV. 85c + 15v 100% 15 15% 40 70 55 V. 95c + 5v 100% 5 5% 10 20 15 Total 390c + 110v — 110 110% — — — Average 78c + 22v — 22 22% — — — The crucial insight here: the value of commodities is now c (used up) + v + s. For capital I: 50 + 20 + 20 = 90; cost price = c used up + v = 50 + 20 = 70. The surplus-value is still 20, the rate of profit still 20% (20/100). The value of the commodities is lower (90 rather than 120) because less constant capital is transferred, but the profit rate is unchanged. This confirms the earlier point. The total sum of the five capitals remains 500 = 390c + 110v, so the average composition is still 78c + 22v, the average surplus-value still 22. The Even Distribution of Surplus-Value and the Divergences Now Marx presents the climactic table (pages 3–4), where the average profit of 22 is evenly distributed across capitals I–V: Capitals Surplus-Value Value of Commodities Cost Price Price of Commodities Rate of Profit Divergence of Price from Value I. 80c + 20v 20 90 70 92 22% +2 II. 70c + 30v 30 111 81 103 22% −8 III. 60c + 40v 40 131 91 113 22% −18 IV. 85c + 15v 15 70 55 77 22% +7 V. 95c + 5v 5 20 15 37 22% +17 The mechanics of the \"price of commodities\" column: price = cost price + 22 (the average profit). For I: 70 + 22 = 92. For II: 81 + 22 = 103. For III: 91 + 22 = 113. For IV: 55 + 22 = 77. For V: 15 + 22 = 37. The \"divergence of price from value\" is price minus value: 92 − 90 = +2; 103 − 111 = −8; 113 − 131 = −18; 77 − 70 = +7; 37 − 20 = +17. The profound result: commodities are sold at 2 + 7 + 17 = 26 above their value, and 8 + 18 = 26 below their value, so the divergences cancel each other out. This is the heart of the chapter's resolution. The high-composition capitals (IV, V — more constant capital, less living labor) sell above value because their own surplus-value is too small to give them the 22% average; the low-composition capitals (II, III — more variable capital, more living labor) sell below value because they produce more surplus-value than the average rate requires. The total is preserved: the sum of divergences is zero, so the sum of prices equals the sum of values. And it is only because commodities are sold at these prices that the rates of profit for capitals I–V are equal at 22%, \"irrespective of their different organic compositions.\" The Definition of Prices of Production Marx now formally defines his new category: \"The prices that arise when the average of the different rates of profit is drawn from the different spheres of production, and this average is added to the cost prices of these different spheres of production, are the prices of production .\" Their prerequisite is \"the existence of a general rate of profit,\" which \"presupposes in turn that the profit rates in each particular sphere of production, taken by itself, are already reduced to their average rates.\" These particular (sphere-specific) rates are to be developed from the value of the commodity \"as shown in the first Part of this volume.\" Without this value-theoretic derivation, \"the general rate of profit (and hence also the production price of the commodity) remains a meaningless and irrational conception.\" Thus: price of production = cost price + percentage profit added in accordance with the general rate of profit = cost price + average profit. This passage contains a crucial methodological point against any notion that Marx's prices of production are mere market prices or arbitrary markups. The price of production depends on value; it is a transformed form of value. To abandon the value-theoretic derivation is to render the general rate of profit \"meaningless and irrational.\" Marx is here explicitly defending — in advance — against the charge (later leveled by Böhm-Bawerk) that Volume 3 contradicts Volume 1. There is no contradiction: the price of production is derived from value and presupposes it. General Rate of Profit from Competition and the Definition of Average Profit Marx then restates the dynamic: because of the differing organic composition of capitals, \"very different amounts of labour are set in motion by capitals of equal size,\" so \"very different amounts of surplus labour are appropriated.\" The rates of profit \"are accordingly originally very different.\" These different rates \"are balanced out by competition to give a general rate of profit which is the average of all these different rates.\" The profit that falls to a capital of given size according to this general rate — whatever its organic composition — is average profit . And the price of a commodity equal to its cost price plus the part of the annual average profit on the capital applied in its production (not simply capital consumed) that falls to its share \"according to its conditions of turnover\" is its price of production . Note the care: the average profit is calculated on the capital advanced (used), allocated \"according to its conditions of turnover.\" The Worked Example with 500 Capital Marx gives a concrete numerical example. A capital of 500 , of which 100 is fixed capital , with 10 percent depreciation of this fixed capital, plus a circulating capital of 400 during one turnover period. Let the average profit for this turnover period be 10 percent . The cost price of the product produced during this turnover = 10c (depreciation) + 400 (c + v circulating) = 410 . The price of production = 410 cost price + 50 (10% profit on the capital advanced of 500) = 460 . This example elegantly demonstrates that the profit is calculated on the entire capital advanced (500), not merely the capital consumed (410), and that depreciation enters as a distinct component. The distinction between capital consumed and capital advanced is essential. The Joint-Stock Company Analogy Marx now deploys one of his most memorable and theoretically fertile analogies. Although capitalists \"get back on the sale of their commodities the capital values consumed,\" they \"do not secure the surplus-value and hence profit that is produced in their own sphere.\" What they secure is only the surplus-value \"that falls to the share of each aliquot part of the total social capital, when evenly distributed, from the total social surplus-value or profit produced in a given time by the social capital in all spheres.\" Every 100 units of capital advanced \"draws... the profit that accrues to 100 units... as an nth part of the total capital.\" The analogy: \"The various different capitals here are in the position of shareholders in a joint-stock company , in which the dividends are evenly distributed for each 100 units, and hence are distinguished, as far as the individual capitalists are concerned, only according to the size of the capital that each of them has put into the common enterprise, according to his relative participation in this common enterprise, according to the number of his shares.\" This is a profound claim: all social capital functions as a single collective enterprise , and each individual capital is treated as a shareholder entitled to a pro-rata dividend of the total social surplus-value. The capitalist class as a whole is a collective appropriator, and each capitalist's profit depends not on his own exploitation of his workers but on the total exploitation of the working class by capital as a whole. This is the economic basis of class solidarity among capitalists (and of their collective interest in the rate of exploitation as such). Marx then contrasts: while the cost price is completely governed by the outlay within each sphere, the profit added to it \"is governed not by the mass of profit that is produced by this specific capital in its specific sphere of production, but by the mass of profit that falls on average to each capital invested, as an aliquot part of the total social capital.\" He footnotes this with Cherbuliez (Antoine-Élisée Cherbuliez, 1797–1869, a Swiss economist combining elements of Sismondi and Ricardo, author of Richesse ou pauvreté , Paris, 1841, pp. 70–72, and treated in Theories of Surplus-Value , Part III, Chapter XXIII). The Reconciliation: Sum of Prices = Sum of Values Marx immediately restates the individual capitalist's position in light of the analogy: \"If a capitalist sells his commodities at their price of production, he withdraws money according to the value of the capital that he consumed in their production and adds a profit to this in proportion to the capital he advanced as a mere aliquot part of the total social capital. His cost prices are specific [to his sphere]. But the profit on top of this cost price is independent of his particular sphere of production, it is a simple average per 100 units of capital advanced.\" The grand reconciliation follows. Imagine the five investments I–V \"belong to one and the same person.\" The variable and constant capital consumed in each would form a distinct portion of each commodity's price (the cost price). These cost prices \"would thus be different for each kind of commodity I–V.\" But the capitalist \"might very well count them all as profit on the total capital he advanced, so that a definite aliquot part would fall to each capital of 100.\" So cost prices differ per commodity, but the profit per 100 units \"would be the same.\" The \"total price of commodities I–V would thus be the same as their total value\" — the sum of cost prices plus the sum of surplus-value/profit — \"in point of fact, therefore, the monetary expression for the total quantity of labour, both past and newly added, contained in commodities I–V. And in the same manner, the sum of prices of production for the commodities produced in society as a whole... is equal to the sum of their values. \" This is the first fundamental equality of the transformation. The Apparent Contradiction from Inter-branch Purchases Marx raises an obvious objection: the elements of productive capital are generally bought on the market, \"so that their prices include an already realized profit,\" so that \"the profit in one branch of industry goes into the cost price of another.\" Does this not break the equality? His answer is subtle and important. If we put the sum of cost prices of all commodities on one side and the sum of profits/surplus-values on the other, \"the calculation comes out right.\" Take commodity A: its cost price may contain the profits of B, C, D, just as the profits of A may in turn go into B, C, D, etc. Now, \"the profit of A will be absent from its own cost price, and the profits of B, C, D, etc. will be absent from theirs. None of them includes his own profit in his cost price.\" If there are n spheres of production , each making a profit of p (and the symbol for the cost price of a single commodity is k ), \"then the cost price in all together is k − np .\" The reasoning: each sphere's cost price includes the profits of other spheres but not its own ; so summing, the total \"cost prices\" overstate the true cost by exactly the sum of all profits (np), which have been double-counted. Profits cannot appear twice — once as part of the price of the linen and once as the profit of the flax producers. The profits of one sphere appear on the profit side \"only because the commodity in question was itself an end-product, so that its price of production does not go into the cost price of another commodity.\" Marx formalizes: if a certain sum p₀ goes into the cost price of a commodity as the profit of the producers of the means of production, and on this cost price a profit of p₁ is added, the total profit P = p₀ + p₁ . The total cost price of the commodity, \"discounting all portions of the price that count towards profit, is then its own cost price minus P.\" Using k for this (adjusted) cost price, k + P = k + p₀ + p₁ . He cross-references Capital Volume 1, Chapter 9, Section 2, pp. 331–32, where he had shown that a product can be treated as if one part simply replaces capital while the other only represents surplus-value, and he notes that to apply this to the total social product, rectifications are needed because \"the profit contained in the price of flax... cannot figure twice.\" The Refutation of Double-Counting Marx deepens the point: \"There is no distinction between profit and surplus-value when the surplus-value of A, for instance, goes into the constant capital of B. As far as the value of commodities is concerned, it is completely immaterial whether the labour contained in them is paid or unpaid. This shows only that B pays the surplus-value of A. In the total account, A's surplus-value cannot figure twice.\" The distinction between profit and surplus-value disappears at the aggregate level (they are equal in total), and what matters is only that no surplus-value is counted twice. However, there is a real distinction at the level of individual commodities: apart from the divergence of B's price from its value, the same situation holds for the commodities forming the constant part of B (and indirectly its variable capital as means of subsistence for workers). The constant portion of capital is itself \"equal to cost price plus surplus-value, i.e. now equal to cost price plus profit,\" and this profit \"can again be greater or less than the surplus-value whose place it has taken.\" As for variable capital: the average daily wage is equal to the value product of the hours needed to produce the worker's necessary means of subsistence, but this number of hours \"is itself distorted by the fact that the production prices of the necessary means of subsistence diverge from their values.\" This is a critical second-order effect: even the value of labor-power (which determines v) is affected by prices of production, since the worker's subsistence basket is sold at prices of production, not values. Yet the whole thing \"is always reducible to the situation that whenever too much surplus-value goes into one commodity, too little goes into another, and that the divergences from value that obtain in the production prices of commodities therefore cancel each other out.\" The \"Dominant Tendency\" and the Average of Perpetual Fluctuations Marx's conclusion from this reconciliation is epistemologically crucial: \"With the whole of capitalist production, it is always only in a very intricate and approximate way, as an average of perpetual fluctuations which can never be firmly fixed , that the general law prevails as the dominant tendency .\" The law of value is not a directly observable, exact empirical regularity; it operates as a tendency , realized only through the never-ceasing oscillation of market prices and the cancelling out of divergences. This is a mature statement of Marx's method: the law is a real, causal force that asserts itself as the outcome of chaotic fluctuations, not a frictionless equilibrium condition. Turnover Times: Bracketed and then Restored Marx now explicitly restores what he had earlier bracketed. Since the general rate of profit is formed by the average of the various rates of profit on each 100 units \"over a definite period of time, say a year,\" the distinction between capitals \"by the distinction in turnover times is also obliterated.\" But — and this is a crucial qualification — \"this distinction plays a decisive role for the various different rates of profit in the various spheres of production, by means of whose average the general rate of profit is formed.\" In other words: before the averaging, turnover time is a determinant of the sphere-specific profit rate; after the averaging, it is washed out. So two sources of divergence — organic composition and turnover time — both feed into the different rates whose average is the general rate. Marx had said he would \"deal with this later\"; here he acknowledges it without yet fully elaborating. The Relative Magnitudes of Capitals in Different Spheres Marx next corrects a possible misunderstanding. In the earlier illustration every capital was taken as 100, \"in order to make clear the percentage differences in the rates of profit.\" But the actual masses of surplus-value produced in each sphere depend on the magnitude of the capital applied. The particular rate of profit of a sphere is not affected by whether 100, m × 100, or xm × 100 is applied — \"the profit rate remains 10 per cent, whether the total profit is 10 on 100 or 1,000 on 10,000.\" However , since the rates of profit differ across spheres, and since they differ because different masses of surplus-value are produced according to the proportion of variable capital, \"it is evident that the average profit per 100 units of social capital, and hence the average or general rate of profit, will vary greatly according to the respective magnitudes of the capitals invested in the various spheres.\" The Four-Capital Example (A, B, C, D) Marx illustrates with a four-capital example. Let the rate of surplus-value be 100% for all. Let the variable capital per 100 units of total capital be 25 for A, 40 for B, 15 for C, 10 for D . Then each 100 units yields surplus-value/profit of 25, 40, 15, 10 — total 90. If the four capitals are equal in size , the average rate of profit is 90/4 = 22½ percent . But now suppose the total capitals are A = 200, B = 300, C = 1,000, D = 4,000 . The profits produced would be 50, 120, 150, 400 respectively (each being the per-100 profit times the number of hundreds: A: 25 × 2 = 50; B: 40 × 3 = 120; C: 15 × 10 = 150; D: 10 × 40 = 400). Total profit = 720 on a total capital of 5,500 , giving an average rate of profit of 720/5,500 = 13⅑ percent . This dramatically demonstrates that the general rate of profit depends not only on the rates in each sphere but on the relative weights — the amounts of capital invested in each sphere. The simple average (22½%) is very different from the weighted average (13⅑%). The Two Factors Determining the General Rate of Profit Marx now states the conclusion formally and definitively. \"The general rate of profit is determined therefore by two factors:\" The organic composition of the capitals in the various spheres of production , i.e., the different rates of profit in the particular spheres; The distribution of the total social capital between these different spheres , i.e., the relative magnitudes of the capitals invested in each particular sphere, and hence the relative share of the total social capital swallowed up by each sphere. He adds a homely analogy: \"It is the same as in the case of the average rate of interest that a money-lender makes if he lends different capitals at different rates of interest, e.g. at 4, 5, 6, 7 per cent, etc. The average rate is completely dependent on how much of his capital he had lent out at each of these different interest rates.\" This analogy is theoretically significant: the general rate of profit is a weighted average , a purely statistical resultant, not a mysterious substance. The Emergence of Cost Price and Production Price as Transformed Forms Marx then marks the historical-theoretical transition: \"In Volumes 1 and 2 we were only concerned with the values of commodities. Now a part of this value has split away as the cost price, on the one hand, while on the other, the production price of the commodity has also developed, as a transformed form of value .\" This sentence announces the central category of the whole Volume: price of production as the transformed form of value. The whole subsequent analysis of Volume 3 operates on this new terrain. The General Formula: Marx now develops the general algebraic expression. Take the average social capital as 80c + 20v with annual rate of surplus-value s' = 100%, so the average annual profit for a capital of 100 is 20 and the average annual rate of profit is 20 percent . For any cost price k of the commodities annually produced by a capital of 100, their price of production = k + 20 . In spheres where composition is (80 − x)c + (20 + x)v , the surplus-value actually created is 20 + x , i.e., more than 20, so the commodity value is k + 20 + x , which is more than the price of production (k + 20). In spheres where composition is (80 + x)c + (20 − x)v , the surplus-value created is 20 − x , and the commodity value is k + 20 − x , less than the price of production (k + 20). Thus: production prices equal values only where composition by chance happens to be precisely 80c + 20v. This is the general theorem in the most compressed form: high-composition capitals have values below prices of production; low-composition capitals have values above prices of production; only the average composition has value equal to price of production. Higher, Lower, and Average Composition Defined Marx now systematically defines the classification. The degree of development of social productivity of labor differs across spheres, \"being higher or lower according to the quantity of means of production set in motion by a certain specific amount of labour.\" Hence: Capitals of higher composition : those containing a greater percentage of constant capital than the social average, and thus a lesser percentage of variable capital. Capitals of lower composition : those with a relatively smaller share of constant capital and a relatively greater share of variable capital. Capitals of average composition : those whose composition coincides with that of the average social capital. If the average social capital is 80c + 20v, then 90c + 10v is above the social average, and 70c + 30v is below . In general, for an average social capital of mc + nv (where m + n = 100, m and n constant), (m + x)c + (n − x)v represents a capital of higher composition, and (m − x)c + (n + x)v one of lower composition. This formalization makes the concept rigorous and general. The Three-Capital Table (I, II, III) Marx then presents a compact table illustrating how these capitals function once the average rate of profit is established (assuming one turnover per year), where capital I is the average composition: I. 80c + 20v + 20s. Rate of profit = 20%. Price of product = 120. Value = 120. II. 90c + 10v + 10s. Rate of profit = 20%. Price of product = 120. Value = 110. III. 10c + 30v + 30s. Rate of profit = 20%. Price of product = 120. Value = 130. For capital II (higher composition), value is 110 < price 120. For capital III (lower composition), value is 130 > price 120. Only for capital I is value equal to price. This crystallizes the whole argument: \"Commodities produced by capital II thus have a value less than their price of production, and those produced by capital III have a price of production less than their value.\" Marx cautions, however, that in applying these terms to specific cases, \"the ratio between c and v may depart from the general average not just as a result of a difference in the technical composition, but also simply because of a change in value of the elements of constant capital.\" This is the dual determination of organic composition with which the chapter began: it is the value composition that matters for the profit rate, and this can shift even without a change in the technical composition, purely from a change in the prices/values of constant capital elements. The Modification of Cost Price Marx now returns to the cost price, arguing that the development above \"involves a modification in the determination of a commodity's cost price .\" He had originally assumed the cost price of a commodity equaled the value of the commodities consumed in its production. But for the buyer , \"it is the price of production that constitutes its cost price and can thus enter into forming the price of another commodity.\" Since the price of production of a commodity can diverge from its value, the cost price of a commodity (in which the price of production of other commodities is involved) can stand above or below the portion of its total value formed by the value of the means of production going into it. This is the crucial \"second-order\" effect: once prices of production are established, they feed back into cost prices, propagating the divergence. Nevertheless, Marx insists, \"it still remains correct that the cost price of commodities is always smaller than their value.\" Why does the potential error of equating cost price with the value of the means of production consumed not invalidate the analysis? Because \"this error in the past is a matter of indifference to the capitalist. The cost price of the commodity is a given precondition, independent of his production, while the result of his production is a commodity that contains surplus-value, and therefore an excess value over and above its cost price.\" The cost price is given to the capitalist from outside; what interests him is the surplus-value (profit) over it. For the total social capital , where price of production equals value, the assertion that cost price < value is identical to the earlier formulation. And so: \"the cost price of a commodity simply depends on the quantity of paid labour it contains, while the value depends on the total quantity of labour it contains, whether paid or unpaid; the price of production depends on the sum of paid labour plus a certain quantity of unpaid labour that is independent of its own particular sphere of production.\" This threefold distinction — cost price = paid labor; value = paid + unpaid labor; price of production = paid labor + socially average unpaid labor — is one of the sharpest formulations in the chapter. The Formula k + kp' Marx now refines the formula. Since p = kp′ (where p′ is the general rate of profit), the price of production is k + kp′ . His example: if k = 300 and p′ = 15 percent , the price of production = k + kp′ = 300 + 300 × 15/100 = 345 . This formula — price of production = cost price × (1 + p′) — is the algebraic culmination of the chapter's constructive work. The Three Causes of Changes in Production Prices Marx enumerates the circumstances in which the price of production of commodities in a particular sphere can change in magnitude: While the value of the commodities remains the same (same quantity of dead and living labour as before), as a result of a change in the general rate of profit independent of the particular sphere; While the general rate of profit remains the same, by a change in value in the particular sphere itself, either from a technical change or from a change in value of the commodities entering as formative elements into its constant capital; Finally, by the common action of these two circumstances. He adds an important temporal/methodological observation: for all the great changes in actual rates of profit in particular spheres, \"a genuine change in the general rate of profit, one not simply brought about by exceptional economic events, is the final outcome of a whole series of protracted oscillations, which require a good deal of time before they are consolidated and balanced out to produce a change in the general rate.\" Hence, in all shorter periods (even leaving aside market-price fluctuations), \"a change in prices of production is always to be explained prima facie by an actual change in commodity values, i.e. by a change in the total sum of labour-time needed to produce the commodities.\" He is careful to exclude \"a mere change in the monetary expression of these values,\" citing Corbet ( An Inquiry into the Causes and Modes of the Wealth of Individuals , London, 1841, p. 174). Changes in the General Rate of Profit: Three Cases Marx then analyzes how the general rate of profit can change. Taking the total social capital as a whole: sum of values of commodities = value of constant capital + value of variable capital + surplus-value. Assuming a constant level of exploitation, the profit rate can change (with the mass of surplus-value constant) in three cases: (1) if the value of constant capital changes; (2) if the value of variable capital changes; (3) if both change. All result in a change in C (total capital), thereby changing g (the general rate of profit). Hence \"a change in the general rate of profit assumes a change in the value of the commodities which enter as formative elements into the constant capital, the variable capital, or both simultaneously.\" Alternatively, with the value of commodities constant, the general rate of profit can change if the level of exploitation of labour changes. Or, with the level of exploitation constant, if the sum of labour applied changes in relation to the constant capital (technical changes), though such technical changes \"must always show themselves in, and thus be accompanied by, a change in value of the commodities.\" The Qualitative vs Quantitative Distinction between Profit and Surplus-Value Marx now makes his pivotal conceptual distinction. In the first Part, \"surplus-value and profit were identical, seen from the point of view of their mass.\" The rate of profit is from the beginning different from the rate of surplus-value , but \"this appears simply as a different way of calculating the same thing.\" However, given that the rate of profit can rise or fall while the rate of surplus-value stays the same, and given that \"all that interests the capitalist in practice is his rate of profit,\" this circumstance \"completely obscures and mystifies the real origin of surplus-value from the very beginning.\" Why? Because the rate of profit measures surplus-value against the total capital, surplus-value \"appears... as having arisen from the total capital, and uniformly from all parts of it at that, so that the organic distinction between constant and variable capital is obliterated in the concept of profit .\" Surplus-value \"denies its own origin in this, its transformed form, which is profit; it loses its character and becomes unrecognizable.\" Yet at this first stage, the distinction between profit and surplus-value \"simply involved a qualitative change, a change of form,\" while any difference in magnitude lay between the rates , not between profit and surplus-value themselves (they were still equal in mass). The Genuine Difference in Magnitudes Once a General Rate Exists This changes decisively with the establishment of a general rate of profit and an average profit. \"It is now purely accidental if the surplus-value actually produced in a particular sphere of production, and therefore the profit, coincides with the profit contained in the commodity's sale price.\" Now \"profit and surplus-value themselves, and not just their rates, will as a rule be genuinely different magnitudes.\" At a given level of exploitation, the mass of surplus-value created in a particular sphere is now more important for the overall average profit of the social capital — and thus for the capitalist class in general — than it is directly for the capitalist within each particular branch. It matters to him \"only in so far as the quantity of surplus-value created in his own branch intervenes as a co-determinant in regulating the average profit.\" But \"this process takes place behind his back. He does not see it, he does not understand it, and it does not in fact interest him.\" The Complete Concealment of the True Nature and Origin of Profit The consequence is profound. \"The actual difference in magnitude between profit and surplus-value in the various spheres of production (and not merely between rate of profit and rate of surplus-value) now completely conceals the true nature and origin of profit, not only for the capitalist, who has here a particular interest in deceiving himself, but also for the worker.\" With the transformation of values into prices of production, \" the very basis for determining value is now removed from view .\" Marx traces the two stages of concealment: In the simple transformation from surplus-value into profit, the profit portion of commodity value confronts the other portion as cost price, and \"the concept of value thus already goes by the board as far as the capitalist is concerned,\" because he deals not with the total labour the commodity cost but only the part he paid for (in the form of means of production, living or dead). Profit appears to him as \"something standing outside the immanent value of the commodity.\" With the establishment of a general rate of profit , this idea is \"completely confirmed, reinforced and hardened\" by the fact that the profit added to the cost price \"is not actually determined, if the particular spheres of production are taken separately, by the value formation that proceeds within these branches, but on the contrary established quite externally to them.\" \"This inner connection is here revealed for the first time.\" Marx then delivers his sweeping indictment: \"all economics up till now has either violently made abstraction from the distinctions between surplus-value and profit, between rate of surplus-value and rate of profit, so that it could retain the determination of value as its basis, or else it has abandoned, along with this determination of value, any kind of solid foundation for a scientific approach , so as to be able to retain those distinctions which obtrude themselves on the phenomenal level.\" This \"confusion on the part of the theorists shows better than anything else how the practical capitalist, imprisoned in the competitive struggle and in no way penetrating the phenomena it exhibits, cannot but be completely incapable of recognizing, behind the semblance, the inner essence and the inner form of this process.\" (This is the anticipatory statement of the method of Theories of Surplus-Value / Volume 4.) The Double Significance of the Laws of the Profit Rate Marx now reinterprets the laws of the profit rate developed in the first Part. They have a double significance : (1) As laws of the general rate of profit. Given the many causes leading profit rates to rise or fall, \"one might believe that the general rate of profit would have to change every single day. But as the movement of one sphere of production will cancel out the movement of another, the forces mutually counteract and paralyse each other.\" The process is slow ; the \"suddenness, multilateral character and differential duration of fluctuations in the particular spheres\" lead them to \"partly compensate for one another in their temporal succession, so that a fall in price succeeds a rise, and vice versa,\" and thus they \"remain local.\" The various local fluctuations \"reciprocally neutralize one another.\" Since the general rate is determined not only by the average rate in each sphere but also by the distribution of total capital across spheres — a distribution \"constantly changing\" — there is \"a constant source of change in the general rate of profit — but a source of change that also becomes paralysed, for the most part, given the uninterrupted and all-round character of this movement.\" (2) Within each sphere there is room for shorter or longer periods in which the profit rate in this sphere fluctuates, \"before this fluctuation, a rise or a fall, is consolidated for a sufficient time to affect the general rate of profit and thus to have more than a local significance.\" Within these spatial and temporal limits, \"the laws of the profit rate developed in the first Part of this volume similarly continue to apply.\" This is a sophisticated methodological point: the laws developed at the level of the individual capital remain operative within each sphere, while the general rate is the resultant of their interaction. The Practical State of Affairs and the Individual Capitalist's Perspective Marx ties the theory back to practice. \"The theoretical opinion regarding the first transformation of surplus-value into profit, i.e. that each portion of capital yields profit in a uniform way\" (footnote: Malthus , Principles of Political Economy , 2nd edn, London, 1836, p. 268) \" expresses a practical state of affairs .\" However an industrial capital may be composed — whether a quarter is dead labour and three-quarters living labour, or three-quarters dead labour and only a quarter sets living labour in motion, \"so that in the one case three times as much surplus labour is sucked out, or surplus-value produced, as in the other\" — with the same level of exploitation and \"ignoring individual differences, which disappear anyway, since in both cases we are concerned only with the average composition of the sphere of production as a whole — in both cases it yields the same profit.\" \"The individual capitalist (or alternatively the sum total of capitalists in a particular sphere of production), whose vision is a restricted one, is right in believing that his profit does not derive just from the labour employed by him or employed in his own branch. This is quite correct as far as his average profit goes.\" Marx here concedes the phenomenal correctness of the capitalist's view. \"How much this profit is mediated by the overall exploitation of labour by capital as a whole, i.e. by all his fellow-capitalists, this interconnection is a complete mystery to him, and the more so in that even the bourgeois theorists, the political economists, have not yet revealed it.\" This leads to the apparent refutation of the labor theory of profit: \"Saving of labour — not only the labour necessary to produce a specific product, but also the number of workers employed — and a greater use of dead labour (constant capital), appears a quite correct economic operation, and seems from the very beginning not to affect the general rate of profit and the average profit in any manner. How therefore can living labour be the exclusive source of profit , since a reduction in the quantity of labour needed for production not only seems not to affect the profit, but rather to be the immediate source of increasing profit, in certain circumstances, at least for the individual capitalist?\" This question is the ideological crux of the chapter. The appearance is that capital-saving (mechanization, reducing workers) directly increases profit, which seems to refute the labor theory of value. Marx's whole analysis in this chapter is the answer: the mechanizing capitalist does get the same rate of profit as everyone else, and this is precisely because profit is redistributed from the spheres that use more living labor. The appearance is real but the essence (living labor as source) is concealed by the redistribution. This sets up the later analyses (especially of the falling rate of profit and crises). The Variable Capital and the Most Important Mystification Marx now turns to the special case of variable capital, \"the most important thing, since it is the source of surplus-value and since everything that conceals its position in the capitalist's enrichment mystifies the entire system.\" Here \"the situation looks cruder, or at least this is the way it appears to the capitalist.\" He gives a numerical example. A variable capital of £100 represents the wages of 100 workers . If these 100 workers, with a given working day, produce a weekly product of 200 items of a commodity (200C), then 1C — ignoring the constant capital portion — costs £100/200 = 10 shillings , since £100 = 200C. (Note the unit confusion in the text, which shifts between £ and shillings; the substance is clear.) Now assume a change in the productivity of labour. If it doubles , the same number of workers produce twice the 200C (i.e., 400C) in the same time. In this case, insofar as the cost price consists simply of labour, £100 now equals 400C , and so 1C = £100/400 = 5 shillings . If productivity is reduced by half , the same labour produces only (200/2)C = 100C , and since 100C = £100, 1C = £1 . (The text's notation \"2 02°c\" and \"2 02 oc\" is an OCR corruption; the arithmetic is that halving productivity halves the output to 100C, making each C worth £1.) The significance: the changes in the labour-time required for production (and therefore in value) now appear \"in connection with the cost price, and therefore also with the price of production, as a different distribution of the same wages over more or fewer commodities , according to whether more or fewer commodities are produced in the same labour-time for the same wages.\" What the capitalist (and the political economist) sees is that \"the part of the paid labour that falls to each item of the commodity changes with the productivity of labour, and so too therefore does the value of each individual article.\" What he does not see is \"that this is also the case with the unpaid labour contained in each article, and the less so, as the average profit is in fact only accidentally determined by the unpaid labour absorbed in his own sphere.\" The chapter's final sentence: \"The fact that the value of commodities is determined by the labour they contain now continues to percolate through only in this crudified and naive form .\" This is the conclusion of the entire movement: the labor theory of value, once transformed into prices of production and obscured by the equalization of profit rates, survives in consciousness only as the crude, naive perception that \"wages determine prices\" or that \"cost of production determines price\" — a degraded vestige of the real law. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles Revealed First, the law of value operates as a tendency, not a frictionless empirical regularity. The chapter's most important methodological lesson is that value determination asserts itself only \"as an average of perpetual fluctuations which can never be firmly fixed,\" as \"the dominant tendency.\" The exact equalities (sum of prices = sum of values; sum of profits = sum of surplus-value) hold at the level of the total social capital, but the individual divergences are systematic, permanent, and mutually cancelling. This dissolves the naive reading of the labor theory of value as a claim about every individual price. Second, the rate of profit is a social or class phenomenon, not an individual one. The joint-stock company analogy is not mere ornament. It expresses a structural truth: profit arises from the exploitation of the working class as a whole , and each capitalist's profit is his pro-rata share of the total social surplus-value. The rate of profit is thus determined by the aggregate organic composition and the distribution of total capital — two factors, both social. This is the economic foundation of the capitalist class as a collective appropriator, and it explains why individual capitalists can be indifferent to (even hostile toward) the source of their own profit. Third, appearance and essence are systematically inverted under capitalism. The transformation of values into prices of production does not merely obscure the origin of profit — it inverts it. High-composition (mechanized) capitals appear to be more profitable per unit of living labour saved, since they yield the same average profit while employing less labor. The mechanizing capitalist seems to prove that dead labor (constant capital) is the source of profit, precisely because the redistribution conceals the transfer from labor-intensive spheres. \"How therefore can living labour be the exclusive source of profit?\" is a question the phenomenal forms seem to answer against Marx — but the analysis of the chapter shows the opposite. Fourth, scientific categories must be derived, not presupposed. Marx's insistence that the general rate of profit \"remains a meaningless and irrational conception\" without the value-theoretic derivation is a defense of systematic method. The price of production is a transformed form of value, not an independent datum. Any economics that begins from prices of production (as vulgar economics does) has abandoned the scientific foundation. Broader Theoretical Implications The resolution of the \"great contradiction.\" The chapter definitively resolves the apparent contradiction between Volume 1's labor theory of value and the empirical fact of equalized profit rates. The resolution is not that values are irrelevant, but that they are transformed . Total value = total price of production; total surplus-value = total profit. This is the \"invariance condition\" that later economists (Bortkiewicz, Sraffa, and the \"transformation problem\" literature) would contest — but the chapter itself is Marx's own statement of the resolution, and it is rich, formal, and internally coherent. The theory of the falling rate of profit. The two-factor determination of the general rate — organic composition and distribution of capital — lays the groundwork for the later analysis of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall as the organic composition rises over time. The \"double significance\" of the laws of the profit rate (general and sphere-specific) is the methodological apparatus for that analysis. The theory of class and ideology. The chapter is a model of ideology critique through economic analysis. It shows why the phenomenal forms of capitalism systematically generate a false consciousness — not merely among capitalists but among workers and theorists — and it shows that this false consciousness is not a simple error but a necessary reflection of real appearances. \"The practical capitalist, imprisoned in the competitive struggle... cannot but be completely incapable of recognizing, behind the semblance, the inner essence.\" This is Marx's mature theory of the relationship between appearance, essence, and ideology. The critique of political economy. The closing indictment — that all prior economics either abstracted violently from the distinctions or abandoned the value foundation — frames Volume 4 ( Theories of Surplus-Value ). It identifies the central theoretical failure of classical economics (Smith, Ricardo, and their successors): the inability to hold together the determination of value by labor and the equalization of profit rates. Marx claims to have solved it; the history of subsequent economics (marginalism, the transformation debate) is, in part, a series of responses to whether he did. Practical and Philosophical Implications The chapter's practical implication for the working class is stark: the exploitation of any particular worker by his particular employer is a part of a total social exploitation, and the wage-bargain in any one sphere is mediated by the total social capital. The struggle over wages and hours is thus a struggle over the rate of exploitation of the whole class, not merely a local matter. Conversely, the chapter shows why the defense of profit (\"capital deserves its return\") has an apparent plausibility: the average profit does seem to arise from capital as such, and the redistribution that produces it is invisible. Philosophically, the chapter is a case study in the dialectic of essence and appearance. The real (value) is not the immediate (price); the immediate is a necessary form of manifestation of the real, which simultaneously reveals and conceals it. This is the methodological core of Marx's mature work — the \"concrete\" is \"the unity of diverse determinations,\" and the movement from abstract value to prices of production is the movement from essence to existence, in which the essence persists in and through its own self-concealment. The chapter's final image — value determination \"percolating through only in this crudified and naive form\" — is a precise statement of how a real law can survive in consciousness only in a degraded, fetishized shape, which is exactly the task of critique to decode. Summary of the Chapter's Constructive Results The general rate of profit is a weighted average of the sphere-specific rates, determined by (a) the organic compositions of capitals in different spheres and (b) the distribution of total social capital across those spheres. Average profit = the general rate of profit applied to the capital advanced. Price of production = k + kp′ (cost price plus the average profit), a transformed form of value. Sum of prices of production = sum of values ; sum of profits = sum of surplus-value , at the level of total social capital, because the divergences cancel (26 above, 26 below, in the illustrative example). Profit and surplus-value diverge in magnitude (not merely in rate) once the general rate exists; this divergence is systematic and conceals the origin of profit. Turnover time is a co-determinant of sphere-specific rates, washed out in the average. Cost price itself is modified by prices of production and is always less than value (and generally less than price of production). The organic composition of capital is determined by the technical composition and the values of the elements; capitals are classified as higher, average, or lower composition relative to the social average. Marx thus completes, in Chapter 9, the movement from the hidden essence of capitalist production (value and surplus-value, developed in Volumes 1 and 2 and the first Part of Volume 3) to its visible surface (prices of production and average profit). He shows that the surface is not arbitrary but law-governed — a transformed form — and that its law-governed character is what conceals the essence. The chapter thereby both solves a technical problem in value theory and diagnoses a structure of illusion that is intrinsic to capitalism itself. It is, in this sense, the pivot on which the entirety of Capital turns. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 8 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 10"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 10", "title": "Chapter 10", "subtitle": "Equalization of the General Rate of Profit through Competition", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 10/Chapter 10 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 11040, "read_time": "55 min read", "tags": ["Transformation Problem & Production Prices", "Value & Commodity Form"], "keywords": ["Equalization of General Rate of Profit through Competition", "Capital Migration / Mobility between Industries", "Market Value vs Market Price", "Supply, Demand, and Social Need under Capitalism", "Centrifugal and Centripetal Forces in Capital Competition"], "text": "Chapter 10 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 11,104 words ⏱️ ~56 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 10: The Equalization of the General Rate of Profit through Competition. Market Prices and Market Values. Surplus Profit 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem and Its Stakes Chapter 10 of Capital , Volume III, stands at the strategic hinge of Marx's entire value-theoretic project. Its formal task is to answer a question that Marx himself flags as \"the really difficult question\": how does the equalization of profit rates among different spheres of production actually come about through competition? The difficulty is acute because the general rate of profit is manifestly a result of competitive processes and cannot be presupposed as a point of departure . If we assume equal profit rates from the start, we smuggle in the very thing to be explained. If we do not assume them — if we begin from the premise that commodities exchange at their values — we are forced to confront the fact that different spheres of production, with different organic compositions of capital, would then yield radically different profit rates. The chapter is Marx's attempt to get from the first state of affairs (value prices, unequal profit rates) to the second (prices of production, equal profit rates) without abandoning the labour theory of value. The chapter's core thesis can be stated in three interlocking propositions: Prices of production are transformed forms of value. The equalization of the general rate of profit does not abolish the law of value; it realizes it at a higher level of capitalist development. \"It is in this way that there prevails, and necessarily so, a tendency to make production prices into mere transformed forms of value, or to transform profits into mere portions of surplus-value.\" The general rate of profit is the total surplus-value distributed among capitals in proportion to their size. \"The sum of the profits for all the different spheres of production must accordingly be equal to the sum of surplus-values, and the sum of prices of production for the total social product must be equal to the sum of its values.\" This is the famous \"two equalities\" — the aggregate identity that anchors the transformation of values into prices. Market value and market price are distinct determinations, and demand and supply explain neither. The chapter develops a sophisticated theory of market value (the social value of a whole branch's product) and of market price (the fluctuating price around which actual transactions occur). Marx argues that demand and supply can explain only the divergences of market price from market value, not the market value itself. \"The real inner laws of capitalist production clearly cannot be explained in terms of the interaction of demand and supply... since these laws are realized in their pure form only when demand and supply cease to operate, i.e. when they coincide.\" The Chapter's Architecture The chapter moves through roughly five movements. First, it opens with the anchoring case of capitals of \"mean composition\" and the formula k + kp' , establishes the tendency toward equalization around an \"ideal mean position,\" and asserts the transformation of prices into transformed values. Second, it poses the difficult question (equalization as result) and conducts a thought experiment about commodity producers who own their own means of production, establishing the historical and theoretical priority of exchange at values over exchange at prices of production. Third, it develops the theory of market value: individual value versus social value, the three cases of market-value determination, the Ricardo–Storch controversy, and the role of demand pressure. Fourth, it dismantles the demand-and-supply explanation of value, exposing the tautology, the cancellation of the two forces, and the circular determination of price and demand/supply. Fifth, it returns to competition as the mechanism of equalization, specifies the social preconditions of capital and labour mobility, and derives the \"capitalist freemasonry\" — the structural unity of the capitalist class against the working class — before concluding with the concept of surplus profit. The Intellectual Objective Marx's objective is not merely technical (to solve the transformation problem) but polemical and philosophical. He is simultaneously: Correcting Ricardo , who senses that prices of production depart from values but treats the inquiry as one into relative rather than absolute value, and who fails to grasp the average case in market-value determination. Refuting the \"vulgar economists\" who treat prices of production as the ultimate data and who invoke \"demand and supply\" as if it explained everything — when in fact it explains nothing fundamental. Demonstrating the historical materialist point that value exchange corresponds to a lower stage of development (peasant proprietors, handicraftsmen, guilds) and that prices of production require a definite degree of capitalist development . Revealing the class content of the equalization process: capital, as \"a social power,\" unites against labour even as individual capitals compete destructively among themselves. Fit Within the Broader Framework The chapter presupposes the results of Chapters 8 and 9 of Volume III (the formation of a general rate of profit from the different rates in different spheres, and the transformation of values into prices of production) and prepares the ground for the theory of ground-rent. It also presupposes Volume I's theory of value (socially necessary labour-time, the twofold character of the commodity, the metamorphosis of commodities) and A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy (cited repeatedly in footnotes). It is, therefore, the passage point where the abstract law of value from Volume I is shown to operate concretely through the competitive surface of capitalist society. The chapter thus performs the crucial reconciliation: the law of value governs the movement of prices of production even though individual prices systematically depart from individual values. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Anchoring Case: Capitals of Mean Composition and the Formula k + kp' Marx begins by isolating a privileged subset of capitals: those whose composition is \"mean\" or \"average\" — that is, whose ratio of constant to variable capital (their organic composition ) is exactly or approximately the same as the average of the total social capital. In these spheres, the prices of production of commodities \"coincide exactly or approximately with their values as expressed in money.\" This equality is the pivotal fact of the whole chapter, because it provides the mathematical and conceptual anchor for the equalization process. The formula Marx gives is k + kp' — \"cost price plus the product of the average rate of profit and the cost price.\" Let us unpack every term: k is the cost price: the sum of the value of the means of production consumed (constant capital, c ) plus the value of labour-power advanced as wages (variable capital, v ). In the simple case where the entire capital advanced is consumed in the production period (no fixed capital carry-over), k = c + v . p' is the average, or general, rate of profit, expressed as a fraction (e.g., 0.20 for 20 percent). kp' is therefore the profit imputed to this cost price under the general rate — the average profit. The price of production is thus k(1 + p') . There is an important technical nuance here. In Marx's fuller treatment, the price of production is k + p'·C , where C is the total capital advanced (including fixed capital not consumed in the period). The shorthand k + kp' is equivalent when the cost price equals the total capital advanced — that is, when there is no distinction between capital consumed and capital advanced. The text uses the shorthand, and we should note that it presupposes this simplified condition. Its function is pedagogical: it shows that the price of production is cost price plus the average profit , and that for mean-composition capitals this sum equals value. Why does the equality hold for mean capitals? Because if a capital's composition equals the social average, its rate of profit equals the general rate, and its surplus-value equals the average profit. Competition then distributes social capital across spheres \"in such a way that the prices of production in each of these spheres are formed after the model of the prices of production in the spheres of mean composition.\" The mean-composition spheres serve as the model or norm : their price of production is their value, and all other spheres are adjusted toward this model. The Tendency Toward an \"Ideal\" Mean Position Marx immediately qualifies the anchor: \"Between these spheres that approximate more or less to the social average, there is again a tendency to equalization, which seeks the 'ideal' mean position, i.e. a mean position which does not exist in reality.\" This is a subtle but crucial dialectical point. The mean position is not an empirically observable sphere; it is an ideal norm — a gravitational center that real capitals approximate but never perfectly attain. The equalization process is therefore asymptotic: it \"tends to shape itself around this ideal as a norm.\" This introduces a methodological theme that recurs throughout the chapter: economic laws operate as tendencies , not as exact empirical regularities. The \"general rate of profit\" is a theoretical construction that captures the central tendency of competitive capitalism, not a number observable in any single transaction. This is the same logic Marx applies later to the equal rate of surplus-value, which he explicitly calls \"a theoretical simplification... a tendency, like all economic laws.\" Prices of Production as Transformed Forms of Value The opening movement culminates in the identity that defines the transformation: \"In this way there prevails, and necessarily so, a tendency to make production prices into mere transformed forms of value, or to transform profits into mere portions of surplus-value that are distributed not in proportion to the surplus-value that is created in each particular sphere of production, but rather in proportion to the amount of capital applied in each of these spheres, so that equal amounts of capital, no matter how they are composed, receive equal shares (aliquot parts) of the totality of surplus-value produced by the total social capital.\" Several concepts require definition here: Transformed form of value : the price of production is not a replacement for value but a form of it — value in its competitive, redistributed appearance. The substance (socially necessary labour-time) remains; the form (price) is altered by the equalization of profit rates. Aliquot parts : proportional shares. Just as 3 is an aliquot part of 12 (12/4), a capital of a given size is an aliquot part of the total social capital and receives a corresponding share of total surplus-value. The term emphasizes that distribution is proportional, not arbitrary. Surplus-value created in each sphere versus surplus-value realized in each sphere : this is the heart of the transformation. A labor-intensive sphere creates more surplus-value than it realizes (because its price of production is lower than its value); a capital-intensive sphere creates less than it realizes. The equality of profit rates redistributes the total. Marx then draws the two aggregate conclusions: \"The sum of the profits for all the different spheres of production must accordingly be equal to the sum of surplus-values.\" \"The sum of prices of production for the total social product must be equal to the sum of its values.\" These are the two equalities that ground the entire transformation procedure. They guarantee that the redistribution is zero-sum with respect to total value: nothing is added or subtracted in the aggregate; only the distribution among spheres changes. Two Supplementary Points: The Invariance of the Average Profit Marx adds two clarifications to ward off misunderstandings: First , wherever an average profit (general rate of profit) is established, \"this average profit can be nothing other than the profit on the average social capital, the total sum of profit being equal to the total sum of surplus-value.\" The average profit is thus a macroeconomic magnitude determined at the level of total social capital — not a microeconomic datum derived from individual firms. Second , the prices formed by adding this average profit to cost prices \"can be nothing other than the values which have been transformed into prices of production.\" Again, the transformation is a form-change , not a substance-change. Marx then anticipates an objection: what if some spheres are not subjected to equalization (e.g., because of monopoly, or because they are non-capitalist)? His answer is robust: \"It would change nothing... The average profit would then be calculated on the portion of the social capital that was involved in the equalization process.\" The average profit is still \"the total mass of surplus-value, distributed between the masses of capital in each sphere of production in proportion to their size.\" This is a significant concession to realism: the theory does not require that all capital be equalized, only that the equalized portion determines the general rate. The phrase \"the sum total of the realized unpaid labour\" identifies surplus-value with unpaid labour time, and notes that this total is \"represented, just like the paid labour, dead and living, in the total mass of commodities and money that accrues to the capitalists.\" The material basis of profit is the total labour-time — paid and unpaid, objectified and living — embodied in the social product. The Concealed Difficulty: Equalization as Result, Not Premise The chapter now poses its central question explicitly: \"The really difficult question here is this: how does this equalization lead to a general rate of profit, since this is evidently a result and cannot be a point of departure?\" This is the methodological crux. Marx refuses the Ricardian shortcut of assuming a general rate of profit. He insists on deriving it from the competitive process. The difficulty is that the general rate is both the outcome of equalization and the standard by which prices of production are formed. This is a genuine circularity that must be resolved diachronically (as a tendency over time) rather than synchronically (as a simultaneous equation). The first step in the resolution is to note that \"an assessment of commodity values in money... can only be a result of exchanging them.\" Value is not a pre-given magnitude stamped on commodities before exchange; it is realized in exchange. This prepares the historical argument that value exchange and price-of-production exchange correspond to different stages of development. The Initial Hypothesis: Universal Sale at Value and the Resulting Chaos of Profit Rates Marx conducts a thought experiment: \"Let us assume to start with that all commodities in the various spheres of production were sold at their actual values. What would happen then? According to our above arguments, very different rates of profit would prevail in the various spheres of production.\" The contrast is starkly stated: \"It is, prima facie, a very different matter whether commodities are sold at their values... or whether they are sold at prices which make their sale yield equal profits on equal amounts of the capitals advanced for their respective production.\" If values governed, profit rates would diverge according to organic composition; if prices of production governed, profit rates would equalize but values would be systematically violated. The chapter's task is to show how the second state emerges from the first through competition. The Equal Rate of Surplus-Value as Theoretical Presupposition: Worker Migration and the English Settlement Laws Before proceeding, Marx specifies a crucial assumption: that the level of exploitation, or rate of surplus-value, is the same across spheres, \"at least to a certain extent.\" This assumption rests on \"competition among the workers, and an equalization that takes place by their constant migration between one sphere of production and another.\" He immediately qualifies this with characteristic realism: \"We assume a general rate of surplus-value of this kind, as a tendency, like all economic laws, and as a theoretical simplification; but in any case this is in practice an actual presupposition of the capitalist mode of production, even if inhibited to a greater or lesser extent by practical frictions that produce more or less significant local differences, such as the settlement laws for agricultural labourers in England, for example.\" The settlement laws (the old Poor Law settlement provisions) restricted the geographical mobility of agricultural labourers, thereby preventing the equalization of wage rates and exploitation levels between agriculture and industry. Marx cites them as a concrete example of a friction that makes the theoretical tendency approximate rather than exact. This is an important nuance: the theory does not deny frictions; it treats them as deviations from a tendency that operates \"all the more exact, the more the capitalist mode of production is developed and the less it is adulterated by survivals of earlier economic conditions.\" Why Commodities Are Not Exchanged as Commodities but as Products of Capital The decisive conceptual move follows: \"The whole difficulty arises from the fact that commodities are not exchanged simply as commodities, but as the products of capitals, which claim shares in the total mass of surplus-value according to their size, equal shares for equal size.\" This is the theoretical pivot of the chapter. In simple commodity production (Volume I's world), commodities exchange in proportion to the labour embodied in them. In capitalist production, commodities are the products of capitals , and each capital claims a share of total surplus-value proportional to its size — not proportional to the labour it directly employs. The total price of a capital's product \"has to satisfy this demand.\" The equalization of profit rates is thus not a violation of value but the specific form that value distribution takes when production is organized by capital. The Thought Experiment of Independent Producers: Profit Rates as Indifferent To clarify, Marx imagines workers who own their means of production and exchange commodities with one another. These commodities \"would not be products of capital.\" In this hypothetical: The value of means and materials of labour varies by branch. Different masses of means of production are required for a given amount of labour (a commodity might be prepared in one hour or take a day). Assume equal working time (adjusted for intensity). Under these conditions, each worker replaces their outlays (cost prices) and creates equal new value (wages + surplus-value, though the surplus belongs to themselves). If we translate into capitalist terms, \"they would both receive the same wages plus the same profit, which would be equal to the value expressed in the product, say, of a 10-hour working day.\" Crucially, the profit rates would differ, because profit rate is defined as the ratio of surplus-value to total value laid out on means of production (including means of subsistence). Worker I might have higher outlays (more constant capital) and thus a lower profit rate than Worker II. Yet \"under these conditions, the difference in the profit rate would be a matter of indifference.\" Marx draws two analogies: Wage-labourer analogy : \"just as for a present-day wage-labourer it is a matter of indifference in what profit rate the surplus-value extorted from him is expressed.\" International trade analogy : \"just as in international trade the differences in profit rates between different nations are completely immaterial as far as the exchange of their commodities is concerned.\" The point is that profit-rate equalization is a specific requirement of capital , not a universal requirement of exchange. Where producers own their means of production, unequal profit rates are perfectly compatible with exchange at value. Historical Priority of Value Over Price of Production Marx elevates the thought experiment into a historical claim: \"The exchange of commodities at their values, or at approximately these values, thus corresponds to a much lower stage of development than the exchange at prices of production, for which a definite degree of capitalist development is needed.\" This is the famous \"historical transformation\" argument. It rests on the observation that value exchange prevails \"among peasant proprietors and handicraftsmen who work for themselves\" — in both the ancient and modern world. Marx links this to his earlier view (footnoted by Engels) that \"the development of products into commodities arises from exchange between different communities, and not between the members of one and the same community.\" Engels's footnote is historically significant: what was \"simply Marx's 'opinion'\" in 1865 had become, by the time of Engels's editing, \"an established fact scarcely anywhere contested,\" thanks to the investigations of Georg Ludwig von Maurer (on the primitive German mark community) and Lewis Henry Morgan ( Ancient Society , 1877). Engels notes that Marx \"discovered\" Morgan's book and made annotated extracts, partly used by Engels in The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State (1884). This footnote documents the empirical grounding of Marx's historical claim: that private property in land is a subsequent development, and that commodity exchange originates between communities. Marx extends the historical argument: value exchange also characterizes \"later social conditions based on slavery and serfdom, and for the guild organization of handicraft production, as long as the means of production involved in each branch of production can be transferred from one sphere to another only with difficulty, and the different spheres of production therefore relate to one another, within certain limits, like foreign countries or communistic communities.\" The immobility of capital and labour — the very obstacles to equalization — preserve the regime of value prices. It is the mobility of capital, developed by capitalism, that makes price-of-production exchange possible. The Law of Value as the Regulator of Price Movements Marx states the law of value's regulatory role: \"Whatever may be the ways in which the prices of different commodities are first established or fixed in relation to one another, the law of value governs their movement. When the labour-time required for their production falls, prices fall; and where it rises, prices rise, as long as other circumstances remain equal.\" This is a crucial delimitation: the law of value governs the movement of prices (comparative statics), even if individual prices depart from values. It is the differential of labour-time that drives the differential of prices. The Three Conditions for Exchange at Value Marx specifies what is needed for prices to correspond approximately to values: Exchange must cease to be purely accidental or merely occasional. Regular, repeated exchange is required. Direct exchange must involve commodities produced in relative quantities approximately corresponding to mutual need. This is \"learned from the reciprocal experience of trading and which therefore arises precisely as a result of continuing exchange.\" No natural or artificial monopolies may enable one party to sell above value or force another to sell below value. \"By accidental monopoly, we mean the monopoly that accrues to buyer or seller as a result of the accidental state of supply and demand.\" These conditions define the ideal case of value exchange, and their gradual fulfillment is itself a historical process. Market Value Emerges: Individual Value, Social Value, and the Modal Producer Marx now introduces the crucial distinction between individual value and market value (or social value ): \"The individual value of some of these commodities will stand below the market value (i.e. less labour-time has been required for their production than the market value expresses), the value of others above it.\" Market value is defined in two complementary ways: \"the average value of the commodities produced in a particular sphere,\" and \"the individual value of commodities produced under average conditions in the sphere in question, and forming the great mass of its commodities.\" The market value is thus the center of gravity around which market prices fluctuate — \"these being the same for all commodities of the same species.\" Only \"in extraordinary situations\" do commodities produced under the worst or best conditions govern the market value. The consequence for individual producers is decisive: if the supply at average value satisfies customary demand, then \"the commodities whose individual value stands below the market price will realize an extra surplus-value or surplus profit, while those whose individual value stands above the market price will be unable to realize a part of the surplus-value which they contain.\" This is the origin of surplus profit (extra surplus-value) — the differential advantage accruing to lower-cost producers. It is a social redistribution within the sphere, not a creation of new value. The Ricardo–Storch Controversy and the Resolution Through the Average Case Marx refutes the either/or framing of the classical controversy: \"The controversy between Storch and Ricardo in connection with ground-rent... over the question whether market value (in their terms market price or price of production) is governed by commodities produced under the least favourable conditions (Ricardo) or the most favourable (Storch), is thus resolved in this way, that both are right and both are wrong, and also that both have entirely omitted to consider the average case.\" Henri Storch (1766–1835), a Russian popularizer of classical political economy writing in French ( Cours d'économie politique , 1815), held that market value is governed by the most favorable conditions. David Ricardo held that it is governed by the least favorable (worst) conditions — the marginal, highest-cost producer. Marx's resolution is dialectical: both can govern, depending on the proportions in which commodities are produced under different conditions and on the state of demand. What both missed is the average or middling case, which Marx treats as the norm. Marx also cites Thomas Corbet ( An Inquiry into the Causes and Modes of the Wealth of Individuals , 1841) on cases where price is governed by best-condition commodities, and quotes the anonymous Observations on Certain Verbal Disputes in Political Economy (1821): the relevant unit is \"the description of commodity, not a particular individual hat, pair of shoes, etc. The whole labour which produces all the hats in England is to be considered, to this purpose, as divided among all the hats.\" This supports the macro-level determination of market value. The Three Cases of Market-Value Determination Marx systematically analyzes three cases: Case I: Average conditions govern. Suppose \"great quantities of these commodities are produced in something like the same normal social conditions, so that this value is also the individual value of the individual commodities making up this mass.\" A small proportion is produced in worse conditions (individual value above the mean) and another in better conditions (individual value below the mean). \"These two extremes will cancel one another out, so that the average value of the commodities at the extremes is the same as the value of the mass of average commodities, and the market value is determined by the value of the commodities produced under average conditions.\" Strictly speaking, \"the market value of the entire mass, as governed by the average values, is equal to the sum of its individual values.\" Those at the worst extreme sell below their individual value; those at the best extreme sell above it. Case II: Worst conditions govern. Suppose \"the value of those produced under worse conditions is not balanced out by the value of those produced under better conditions, so that the part of the total produced under worse conditions forms a relatively significant quantity, both vis-à-vis the average mass and vis-à-vis the opposite extreme.\" Then \"it is the mass produced under the worse conditions that governs the market, or social, value.\" The market value is above the individual value of the favorable extreme and above the middle stratum, but below the individual value of the worst extreme. How close it comes to the worst extreme's individual value \"depends completely on the volume of the commodities produced at the unfavourable extreme.\" If demand is only marginally predominant, the worst extreme's individual value governs the market price. Case III: Best conditions govern. Suppose \"the mass of commodities produced under better-than-average conditions significantly exceeds that produced under worse conditions and is itself of significant magnitude in relation to that produced under average conditions.\" Then \"the market value would be regulated by the part produced under the most favourable conditions.\" The average value falls below the middle value; \"if demand is weak in relation to supply, the favourably situated part, however big it might be, forcibly makes room for itself by drawing the price towards its individual value.\" The market value \"can never coincide with this individual value... except in cases where supply sharply outweighs demand.\" Marx explicitly brackets the case of general over-supply, where market price diverges from market value, because \"here we are not dealing with market price in so far as this differs from market value, but simply with the various determinations of this market value itself.\" The Role of Demand Pressure in Shifting the Governing Condition Demand pressure interacts with the proportions of production conditions: If the quantity supplied is less than demand, \"it is always the commodities produced under the worst conditions that govern the market value.\" If the quantity supplied is more than demand, \"it is those produced under the best conditions.\" \"If the difference between the demand for the product and the quantity produced is more significant, the market price will diverge more sharply from the market value.\" The divergence between quantity produced and quantity saleable at market value \"can arise for two reasons\": Supply changes : \"reproduction would take place on a scale different from that which regulated the given market value\" — relative overproduction or underproduction. Demand changes : \"the reproduction, i.e. the supply, remains the same, but demand rises or falls\" — the relative magnitude of supply changes. If both change, the result still reduces to one of these two cases. The Buyer's Side: Demand as Social Need with Money Behind It Marx turns to the second condition for sale at value: that the commodity have use-value, i.e., \"satisfies some kind of social need.\" For an individual commodity, the need could be taken as given; for \"the product of a whole branch of production,\" the quantitative extent of social need becomes \"a factor of fundamental importance.\" The volume of social need must be considered. If the quantity supplied equals the customary demand, the commodity sells at its market value. \"The mass of commodities not only satisfies a need, but it satisfies this need on its social scale.\" The Tautology of Demand and Supply Marx exposes the circularity: \"The real difficulty in pinning down the general concepts of demand and supply is that we seem to end up with a tautology.\" Statement 1 : Demand and supply coincide when the mass of commodities produced can be sold at its market value. Statement 2 : When commodities can be sold at their market value, demand and supply coincide. \"These two statements are identical.\" The definition of coincidence is circular. Supply Defined: Physical Quantity and Labour-Time in a Fortuitous Relation to Need Marx defines supply concretely: \"the product which is actually on sale in the market or can be delivered to it\" — the annual reproduction of each branch. This is expressed first as a definite quantity (measure or number) and second as a definite market value (a multiple of the individual commodity's market value). Crucially: \"There is no necessary connection between the quantitative volume of commodities existing on the market and their market value.\" Some commodities have generically high value, others low, so a given sum of value may be embodied in very small or very large quantities. The only connection is that \"on a given basis of labour productivity in the sphere of production in question, the production of a particular quantity of this article requires a particular quantity of social labour-time.\" This proportion varies across spheres and has \"no intrinsic connection with the usefulness of the article.\" Marx then states the fundamental relation: \"There is no necessary connection, however, but simply a fortuitous one, between on the one hand the total quantity of social labour that is spent on a social article... and on the other hand the proportion in which the society demands satisfaction of the need appeased by that particular article.\" This is a profound point. The allocation of social labour to particular branches is not automatically coordinated with social need under commodity production. It is coordinated only ex post , through price fluctuations, crises, and waste. Wasted Labour and the Sale of Commodities Below Value If production exceeds social need, \"a part of the society's labour-time is wasted, and the mass of commodities in question then represents on the market a much smaller quantity of social labour than it actually contains.\" These commodities \"must therefore be got rid of at less than their market value, and a portion of them may even be completely unsaleable.\" The converse holds if too little labour is spent. Marx adds a parenthetical that points toward socialist planning: \"(Only when production is subjected to the genuine, prior control of society will society establish the connection between the amount of social labour-time applied to the production of particular articles, and the scale of the social need to be satisfied by these.)\" This is the ex ante coordination that capitalism lacks. The normative conclusion: \"The exchange or sale of commodities at their value is the rational, natural law of the equilibrium between them; this is the basis on which divergences have to be explained, and not the converse, i.e. the law of equilibrium should not be derived from contemplating the divergences.\" This is a methodological manifesto: equilibrium (value) is the norm; disequilibrium (price divergence) is the deviation to be explained. Demand Defined: Productive and Consumer Demand, the Capitalist as Buyer Demand is analyzed into two components: Productive demand (from capitalists, for means of production). Consumer demand (individual consumption). Both at first appear to presuppose \"a given volume of social needs.\" If the cotton industry is to reproduce at a given level, it requires the usual amount of cotton; accumulation requires additional quantities. Workers need the same (or somewhat differently distributed) means of subsistence; population growth requires additional quantities. The same holds for other classes. But Marx immediately destabilizes this apparent fixity: \"In fact, however, the quantitative determination of this need is completely elastic and fluctuating. Its fixed character is mere illusion.\" If means of subsistence were cheaper or money wages higher, workers would buy more; a greater \"social need\" would appear. Paupers' \"demand\" is below the narrowest physical need. If cotton became cheaper, capitalists' demand would grow, more excess capital would enter the industry, and so on. Marx stresses the peculiar nature of capitalist productive demand: \"It must never be forgotten in this connection that the demand for productive consumption, on our assumptions, is the capitalist's demand, and that his true purpose is the production of surplus-value, so that it is only with this in mind that he produces a particular kind of commodity.\" Yet the capitalist as buyer of cotton \"is the representative of the need for cotton,\" since \"it is completely unimportant for the seller of cotton whether the buyer transforms it into shirting or gun-cotton, or whether he uses it to stop up his own and the world's ears.\" The capitalist's purpose nevertheless shapes what kind of buyer he is: \"His need for cotton is modified fundamentally by the fact that all it really clothes is his need to make a profit.\" The difference between market demand and genuine social need varies across commodities and depends on \"other money prices\" and \"the buyers being in different financial and living conditions.\" Demand and Supply Cancel Out: The Law Emerges Only in Equilibrium Marx now delivers the central methodological blow: \"If demand and supply coincide, they cease to have any effect, and it is for this very reason that commodities are sold at their market value. If two forces act in opposing directions and cancel one another out, they have no external impact whatsoever, and phenomena that appear under these conditions must be explained otherwise than by the operation of these two forces.\" This is a brilliant application of the principle of sufficient reason. If demand and supply perfectly balance, they exert no net force, and therefore cannot explain why market value is this magnitude rather than another. \"If demand and supply cancel one another out, they cease to explain anything, have no effect on market value and leave us completely in the dark as to why this market value is expressed in precisely such a sum of money and no other.\" The real inner laws of capitalist production \"clearly cannot be explained in terms of the interaction of demand and supply... since these laws are realized in their pure form only when demand and supply cease to operate, i.e. when they coincide.\" Marx acknowledges that demand and supply \"never coincide, or, if they do so, it is only by chance and not to be taken into account for scientific purposes.\" Why then does political economy assume they coincide? \"In order to treat the phenomena it deals with in their law-like form, the form that corresponds to their concept, i.e. to consider them independently of the appearance produced by the movement of demand and supply. And, in addition, in order to discover the real tendency of their movement and to define it to a certain extent.\" The resolution lies in the average over time: \"since they constantly follow one another, they balance each other out in their movement in contrary directions, their contradiction.\" Thus \"supply and demand always coincide if a greater or lesser period of time is taken as a whole; but they coincide only as the average of the movement that has taken place and through the constant movement of their contradiction.\" Market prices that diverge from market values \"balance out on average to become market values, since the departures from these values balance each other as pluses and minuses.\" Crucially, \"this average figure is by no means of merely theoretical significance. It is, rather, practically important for capital whose investment is calculated over the fluctuations and compensations of a more or less fixed period of time.\" The Mechanisms of Self-Correction: How Divergences Remove Themselves Marx enumerates the adjustment mechanisms: If demand falls and market price falls: this can lead to withdrawal of capital (reduced supply), or to a fall in market value itself through inventions reducing necessary labour-time. If demand rises and market price rises above market value: this can lead to over-investment and consequent overproduction (price falls below market value), or to a price rise that depresses demand, or to a rise in market value itself (because part of the demanded product must be produced under worse conditions for a time). The relationship between demand and supply \"explains on the one hand simply the divergences of market price from market value, while on the other hand it explains the tendency for these divergences to be removed.\" The Circularity of Price and Demand/Supply Marx notes the multi-layered circularity: \"If demand and supply determine the market price, then market price in turn, and at a further remove market value, also determine demand and supply.\" Demand moves opposite to price (expanding when price falls). Supply is determined by the prices of means of production: \"Cotton prices determine the supply of cotton goods.\" \"Demand also determines supply and conversely supply determines demand, production determines the market and the market determines production.\" This is a general interdependence that cannot be resolved by treating any one factor as fundamental — unless one has a theory of value to anchor the system. The Malthus Footnote: Cost of Production Governs Demand, Not the Reverse A long footnote quotes Malthus ( Observations on Certain Verbal Disputes , 1821, pp. 60–61), who argues that \"the great principle of demand and supply is called into action to determine what A. Smith calls natural prices as well as market-prices.\" Marx's rebuttal: \"This clever man does not understand that in the case in question it is precisely the change in cost of production, and also therefore in value, that has brought about the change in demand, i.e. in the relationship of demand and supply.\" The change in cost of production is not governed by demand and supply; \"on the contrary is what governs this relationship.\" Malthus's own example thus proves the opposite of what he intends. The \"Two Natural Prices\" Argument: Proof that Demand and Supply Do Not Determine Value Marx quotes the Observations author: \"It is only when the supply is equal to the effectual demand, that is, to that demand which will neither more nor less than pay the natural price, that the natural price is in fact paid; consequently, there may be two very different natural prices, at different times, for the same commodity, and yet the proportion, which the supply bears to the demand, be in both cases the same, namely, the proportion of equality.\" Marx's commentary is decisive: \"It is conceded, then, that in the case where we have two different 'natural prices' for the same commodity at different times, demand and supply can and must coincide each time, if the commodity is to be sold at its 'natural price' in both cases. But since there is no difference in the relationship between demand and supply from one occasion to the other, but rather a difference in the magnitude of the 'natural price' itself, the latter is evidently determined independently of demand and supply and can certainly not be determined by them.\" This is a reductio ad absurdum : if demand and supply are in the same proportion (equality) at two different times yet the natural price differs, then the natural price cannot be determined by demand and supply. This formal argument is one of the chapter's most powerful weapons against subjectivist price theory. The Metamorphosis and Why Money Analysis Could Ignore Price Divergences Marx recapitulates the analysis of the commodity metamorphosis ( C–M–C' ) from Volume I and A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy . In the relationship of demand and supply, \"we have firstly a repetition of the relationship between use-value and exchange-value, commodity and money, buyer and seller; secondly, we have the relationship of producer and consumer, even though both may be represented by third parties, in the shape of merchants.\" \"Three persons are enough for the complete metamorphosis of a commodity\": A sells to B, then buys from C with B's money. In analyzing money as means of circulation, Marx assumed commodities were sold at their values, \"there was no reason at all to consider prices that diverged from values, as we were concerned simply with the changes of form.\" Once a commodity is sold and another bought, \"we have the entire metamorphosis before us, and it is completely immaterial here whether the commodity's price is above or below its value.\" Value remains important as the basis: \"any rational understanding of money has to start from this foundation, and price, in its general concept, is simply value in the money form.\" This methodological note explains why the earlier analysis could abstract from price-value divergences: it was concerned with form , not with magnitude . But when we consider demand and supply, magnitude becomes central. Aggregate Forces: Demand and Supply as Social Powers, the Individual as Atom When we consider supply and demand at the social level, \"the supply is equal to the sum of commodities provided by all the sellers or producers of a particular kind of commodity, and the demand is equal to the sum of all buyers or consumers... of that same kind of commodity.\" These totals \"act on one another as unities, as aggregate forces.\" The individual \"has an effect only as part of a social power, as an atom in the mass,\" and it is in this form that \"competition brings into play the social character of production and consumption.\" This is a crucial anti-individualist methodological point: the relevant forces are social aggregates , not individual preferences. Competition's Asymmetric Play: The Weaker Side as Disunited Marx describes the dynamics of competition: \"The side that is temporarily weaker in competition is also that in which the individual operates independently of the mass of his competitors, and often directly against them, illustrating precisely in this way the dependence of one on the other, whereas the stronger side always acts towards its opponent as a more or less united whole.\" If demand exceeds supply: one buyer outbids others, raising price above market value for everyone; sellers all seek a high price. If supply exceeds demand: one seller unloads cheaply, others follow; buyers depress price below market value. \"Each is only concerned with the common interest as long as he obtains more with it than he would against it. And this unity of action ceases as soon as one entire side or other weakens, when each individual independently tries to extract what he can.\" If one seller produces more cheaply, he undercuts others, carving out a bigger market share; \"the action once begun, it gradually forces the others to introduce the cheaper form of production and thereby reduces the socially necessary labour to a new and lower level.\" If one side has the upper hand, \"each of its members profits; it is as if they had a common monopoly to exert.\" On the weaker side, \"every man for himself... devil take the hindmost.\" A footnote quotes the Inquiry into those Principles Respecting the Nature of Demand (1821): where each man can increase his own share only by lessening the whole, he will do so — \"this is competition\"; whereas if shares were fixed, men would combine to raise the gain — \"this is monopoly.\" Demand and Supply Presuppose the Whole Capitalist Order \"Demand and supply imply the transformation of value into market value, and in as much as they act on a capitalist basis, and commodities are the products of capital, they imply capitalist processes of production, i.e. conditions that are much more intricate than the mere purchase and sale of commodities.\" For simple buying and selling, \"it is enough that commodity producers confront one another.\" But demand and supply at the capitalist level imply \"various different classes and segments of classes which distribute the total social revenue among themselves and consume it as such\" — a demand created out of revenue — and \"the overall configuration of the capitalist production process\" to comprehend producers' demand. Earlier, Marx had specified the determinants of \"social need\": \"basically conditioned by the relationship of the different classes and their respective economic positions; in the first place, therefore, particularly by the proportion between the total surplus-value and wages, and secondly, by the proportion between the various parts into which surplus-value itself is divided (profit, interest, ground-rent, taxes, etc.).\" Hence \"absolutely nothing can be explained by the relationship of demand and supply, before explaining the basis on which this relationship functions.\" The Aim of Capitalist Production: Average Profit as Capital's Consciousness of Itself as a Social Power Marx restates the capitalist objective: \"In capitalist production it is not simply a matter of extracting, in return for the mass of value thrown into circulation in the commodity form, an equal mass of value in a different form... but rather of extracting for the capital advanced in production the same surplus-value or profit as any other capital of the same size, or a profit proportionate to its size, no matter in what branch of production it may be applied.\" The minimum requirement is to sell at prices of production. \"This is the form in which capital becomes conscious of itself as a social power, in which every capitalist participates in proportion to his share in the total social capital.\" This is a remarkable formulation: the equalization of profit rates is not merely a market outcome but a form of class consciousness — the mode in which capital recognizes itself as a collective social power. The Indifference of Capital and Labour to Use-Value and Work Two structural indifference properties support equalization: Capitalist production is indifferent to use-values and the specific character of commodities. \"All that matters in any sphere of production is to produce surplus-value, to appropriate a definite quantity of unpaid labour in labour's product.\" Wage-labour is indifferent to the specific character of its work. \"It must be prepared to change according to the needs of capital and let itself be flung from one sphere of production to another.\" \"One sphere of production really is as good and as bad as any other; each yields the same profit and each would be pointless if the commodity it produced did not satisfy some kind of social need.\" These indifference conditions are the subjective counterpart of the objective mobility of capital and labour. Capital Migration and the Equalization of Profit Rates Marx summarizes the mechanism: \"If commodities were sold at their values... this would mean very different rates of profit in the different spheres of production, as we have already explained, according to the differing organic composition of the masses of capital applied. Capital withdraws from a sphere with a low rate of profit and wends its way to others that yield higher profit. This constant migration, the distribution of capital between the different spheres according to where the profit rate is rising and where it is falling, is what produces a relationship between supply and demand such that the average profit is the same in the various different spheres, and values are therefore transformed into prices of production.\" This is the mechanism of equalization: capital flight from low-profit to high-profit spheres alters supply-demand relations, which alters prices, which alters profit rates, until equalization is achieved. The causality runs: unequal profit rates → capital migration → altered supply → altered prices → equalized profit rates. The equalization is a dynamic process , not a static condition. The Two Requirements for Rapid Equalization: Mobility of Capital and of Labour Marx specifies the conditions that accelerate equalization: First condition: mobility of capital. This requires: - \"Completely free trade within the society in question and the abolition of all monopolies other than natural ones, i.e. those arising from the capitalist mode of production itself.\" - \"The development of the credit system, which concentrates together the inorganic mass of available social capital vis-à-vis the individual capitalist.\" - \"That the various spheres of production have been subordinated to capitalists.\" This is already implied by the assumption that we are dealing with the transformation of values into prices of production for all spheres exploited in the capitalist manner. But \"this equalization comes up against major obstacles if several substantial spheres of production are pursued non-capitalistically (e.g. agriculture by small peasant farmers).\" - \"A high population density.\" Second condition: mobility of labour-power. This requires: - \"The abolition of all laws that prevent workers from moving from one sphere of production to another or from one local seat of production to any other.\" - \"Indifference of the worker to the content of his work.\" - \"Greatest possible reduction of work in all spheres of production to simple labour.\" - \"Disappearance of all prejudices of trade and craft among the workers.\" - \"Finally and especially, the subjection of the worker to the capitalist mode of production.\" Marx defers further details \"to the special study of competition\" (referring to a later part of the work). Capitalist Freemasonry: Universal Participation in the Exploitation of the Total Working Class Marx draws the class conclusion: \"Each individual capitalist, just like the totality of all capitalists in each particular sphere of production, participates in the exploitation of the entire working class by capital as a whole, and in the level of this exploitation; not just in terms of general class sympathy, but in a direct economic sense, since... the average rate of profit depends on the level of exploitation of labour as a whole by capital as a whole.\" The average rate of profit \"coincides with the average surplus-value that capital produces for each 100 units.\" The only additional determinant is \"the value of the capital advanced.\" The particular interest of a capitalist or sphere is confined to \"the possibility of taking an extra cut, making an excess profit over and above the average, either by exceptional overwork, by reducing wages below the average, or by exceptional productivity in the labour applied.\" Marx then constructs two limiting cases: A capitalist employing no variable capital at all (an exaggerated assumption) would still have \"just as much an interest in the exploitation of the working class by capital\" and derive profit from unpaid surplus labour. A capitalist employing only variable capital (again exaggerated) would be in the same position. \"With a given working day, the level of exploitation of labour depends on its average intensity, and, conversely, given the intensity, on the length of the working day. The rate of surplus-value depends on the level of exploitation of labour, and thus, for a given mass of variable capital, the size of the surplus-value and the amount of profit also depend on this.\" The special interest of a particular capital in exploiting its own workers is \"paralleled by the interest of the individual capitalist, as distinct from his sphere, in the exploitation of the workers exploited personally by him.\" Interest in Social Productivity Each sphere and capitalist has \"the same interest in the productivity of the social labour applied by the total capital.\" Two things depend on this: The mass of use-values in which the average profit is expressed — important both as accumulation fund and as revenue fund for consumption. The value level of the total capital advanced (constant and variable), which \"with a given size of surplus-value or profit for the entire capitalist class, determines the profit rate, or the profit on a particular quantity of capital.\" But \"the specific productivity of labour in one particular sphere, or in one individual business in this sphere, concerns the capitalists directly involved in it only in so far as it enables this particular sphere to make an extra profit in relation to the total capital, or the individual capitalist in relation to his sphere.\" The \"Mathematically Exact Demonstration\" of Class Solidarity Marx concludes: \"We thus have a mathematically exact demonstration of why the capitalists, no matter how little love is lost among them in their mutual competition, are nevertheless united by a real freemasonry vis-a-vis the working class as a whole.\" The \"freemasonry\" is not sentimental but structural: because the average rate of profit depends on the aggregate rate of exploitation, every capitalist benefits from the exploitation of every worker. Competition among capitalists is real, but it is contained within a shared dependence on the exploitation of the working class as a whole. Terminology: Price of Production, Natural Price, Cost of Production, Prix Nécessaire Marx clarifies terminology: \"The price of production includes the average profit. And what we call price of production is in fact the same thing that Adam Smith calls 'natural price', Ricardo 'price of production' or 'cost of production', and the Physiocrats 'prix nécessaire', though none of these people explained the difference between price of production and value.\" Marx explains why he calls it \"price of production\": \"because in the long term it is the condition of supply, the condition for the reproduction of commodities, in each particular sphere of production.\" The footnote cites Malthus ( Principles of Political Economy , pp. 77–8). Why Vulgar Economists Fixate on Prices of Production Marx explains the ideological attraction of prices of production: \"We can also understand why those very economists who oppose the determination of commodity value by labour-time... always speak of the prices of production as the centres around which market prices fluctuate. They can allow themselves this because the price of production is already a completely externalized and prima facie irrational form of commodity value, a form that appears in competition and is therefore present in the consciousness of the vulgar capitalist and consequently also in that of the vulgar economist.\" The price of production is value in its alienated, surface form — the form in which value appears to the practical consciousness of the capitalist. It seems \"irrational\" because it does not transparently correspond to embodied labour, yet it is derived from value through the equalization process. The vulgar economist mistakes the surface for the essence. Surplus Profit: The Uniform Price and Differential Conditions The chapter's final movement introduces surplus profit : \"We saw in the course of our argument how market value (and everything that was said about this applies with the necessary limitations also to price of production) involves a surplus profit for those producing under the best conditions in any particular sphere of production.\" Excluding crises and overproduction, \"this holds good for all market prices, no matter how they might diverge from market values or market prices of production. The concept of market price means that the same price is paid for all commodities of the same kind, even if these are produced under very different individual conditions and may therefore have very different cost prices.\" The uniform market price, combined with differential cost prices, generates a surplus profit (extra surplus-value realized) for low-cost producers. Marx brackets \"surplus profits that result from monopolies in the customary sense of the term, whether artificial or natural.\" Monopoly Surplus Profit and the Two Forms of Surplus Profit Marx distinguishes two forms of surplus profit: Differential surplus profit arising from the uniform market price under differential conditions (the subject of the preceding analysis). Monopoly surplus profit arising \"if certain spheres of production are in a position to opt out of the transformation of their commodity values into prices of production, and the consequent reduction of their profits to the average profit.\" He announces: \"In the Part on ground-rent, we shall have to consider the further configuration of these two forms of surplus profit.\" This is the bridge to the theory of ground-rent and to the analysis of agriculture, where non-capitalist relations and natural monopolies (land) generate a second and distinctive form of surplus profit. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principle: Value Governs Through Its Transformation The deepest lesson of Chapter 10 is that the law of value does not operate by directly stamping prices with embodied labour. It operates through the equalization of profit rates, which systematically distorts individual prices while preserving aggregate value. The two equalities — Σ profits = Σ surplus-value and Σ prices of production = Σ values — are the formal guarantee that the transformation is a redistribution , not a creation of value. The law of value thus governs prices of production \"as a general law or as governing the fluctuations,\" even though individual prices diverge from individual values. This is the mature Marxian synthesis: freedom of prices from values is the form in which the law of value asserts itself under capitalism. The Historical Materialist Claim: Value Exchange as a Lower Stage Marx argues that exchange at values corresponds to a \"much lower stage of development\" than exchange at prices of production. This claim has several dimensions: Empirical-historical : value exchange prevails among peasant proprietors and handicraftsmen, in slavery and serfdom, and in guild organization — conditions where capital and labour are immobile. Theoretical : the immobility of capital preserves the proportionality of price to embodied labour; the mobility of capital, developed by capitalism, destroys it and substitutes prices of production. Methodological : the logical priority of value over price of production reflects a historical priority. The \"transformation problem\" is thus not merely a formal puzzle but a historical process . This argument has been intensely debated, but its strategic function is clear: it grounds the labour theory of value in the actual historical development of commodity production, rather than treating it as a timeless accounting convention. The Critique of Demand and Supply: Equilibrium as Norm The chapter's demolition of demand-and-supply explanation is one of its most enduring contributions. Marx's argument is structurally elegant: If demand and supply determine value, then value is explained only when demand and supply coincide . But when they coincide, they exert no net force and therefore explain nothing. Therefore, demand and supply can explain only divergences of market price from market value — not market value itself. The determination of market value must be sought elsewhere — in socially necessary labour-time. This is not a denial that demand matters; it is a claim about explanatory priority . Demand and supply allocate labour-time across branches (through price fluctuations), but they do not create value. The \"social need\" that governs demand is itself conditioned by class relations — by the distribution of surplus-value between wages, profit, interest, rent, and taxes. The Class Content of Equalization: Capitalist Freemasonry The chapter's closing vision is of capitalism as a system of class solidarity among capitalists built on the exploitation of the working class as a whole. The average rate of profit depends on the aggregate rate of exploitation; therefore every capitalist, no matter how fiercely competitive, has a direct economic stake in the exploitation of every worker. This is the structural basis of the \"real freemasonry\" of capital. The individual capitalist's particular interest in exploiting \"his own\" workers is only an extra — an excess profit over the average — layered on top of the universal interest in the exploitation of the class as a whole. This analysis has profound implications: Class solidarity is structural, not psychological. Capitalists need not like each other; they need only share a dependence on aggregate exploitation. Reformism within capitalism is constrained. Any improvement in workers' conditions that lowers the aggregate rate of exploitation lowers every capitalist's profit rate, uniting capital in opposition. Competition is contained. The \"war of all against all\" among capitalists operates within the shared framework of exploitation; it does not dissolve class unity against labour. The Concept of Surplus Profit and the Theory of Rent The chapter's final section opens the door to the theory of ground-rent. Surplus profit arises from two sources: Differential conditions within a sphere (low-cost producers capture extra surplus-value because the market price is uniform). Monopoly or non-equalization (spheres that escape the transformation of values into prices of production retain their higher rates of profit). The first form is the basis of differential rent ; the second is the basis of absolute rent and of monopoly profit generally. Marx's announcement that he will treat \"the further configuration of these two forms of surplus profit\" in the Part on ground-rent shows the systematic connection: the analysis of profit rates is the precondition for the analysis of rent as a distinctive form of surplus-value appropriation. Methodological Lessons: Tendencies, Abstractions, and the Priority of Essence Several methodological principles crystallize in this chapter: Economic laws are tendencies, not exact regularities. The general rate of profit, the equal rate of surplus-value, and the equalization process are all tendencies that approximate empirical reality to the degree that capitalist relations are developed and purified of earlier survivals. Abstraction is not falsification. The assumption of mobile capital and labour is an abstraction that \"in practice\" is approximate — and it becomes more exact \"the more the capitalist mode of production is developed.\" Essence precedes appearance. Value is the essence; price of production is the appearance. The task of science is to derive the appearance from the essence, not to take the appearance as given. Equilibrium is the norm; disequilibrium is deviation. \"The exchange or sale of commodities at their value is the rational, natural law of the equilibrium between them; this is the basis on which divergences have to be explained, and not the converse.\" Broader Practical and Philosophical Implications For political economy : The chapter refutes both the Ricardian assumption of an unproblematic general rate of profit and the vulgar economist's reification of demand and supply. It demonstrates that the labour theory of value can account for the systematic deviations of prices from values without abandoning the labor substance of value — a result that eluded classical political economy. For the theory of money : Marx's analysis of the metamorphosis ( C–M–C ) and his insistence that price is \"value in the money form\" shows that money and value are conceptually linked. A general nominal inflation of profit rates is impossible without corresponding changes in wages and constant capital prices, because total value governs total surplus-value. For the theory of crisis : The chapter's analysis of wasted labour, overproduction, and the disequilibrium between social labour allocation and social need provides a foundation for the later analysis of crises. When the mass of commodities exceeds social need, \"a part of the society's labour-time is wasted,\" and commodities must be sold below value. This is the germ of the crisis theory. For the theory of class struggle : The \"capitalist freemasonry\" passage is one of Marx's most concentrated statements on the structural unity of the capitalist class. It grounds class solidarity in the objective mechanisms of profit-rate determination, not in subjective preference. For socialist theory : The parenthetical remark that \"only when production is subjected to the genuine, prior control of society will society establish the connection between the amount of social labour-time applied to the production of particular articles, and the scale of the social need to be satisfied by these\" points toward the socialist alternative: ex ante coordination of labour-time and need, replacing the ex post coordination of the market with its waste, crises, and class exploitation. Conclusion: The Chapter as the Keystone of Volume III Chapter 10 accomplishes the reconciliation that Volume III exists to perform. It shows how the law of value, established in Volume I at the level of the individual commodity, governs the total social product through the competitive equalization of profit rates. It demonstrates that the \"irrational\" surface forms of capitalism — prices of production, market prices, surplus profit, rent — are all intelligible as necessary forms of value's self-valorization. It exposes the demand-and-supply explanation as a tautology that cannot ground itself. And it reveals the class content of the entire mechanism: capital, driven by the equalization of profit rates, unites as a social power against the working class whose total exploitation is the source of its average profit. The chapter's final sentence — the promise to treat the \"further configuration of these two forms of surplus profit\" in the Part on ground-rent — signals that the analysis of profit rates is not an end in itself but the foundation for understanding rent, and beyond it, the full architecture of capitalist distribution. The equalization of the general rate of profit is thus the mechanism through which the law of value asserts itself in the concrete totality of capitalist production, distribution, and class relations. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 9 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 11"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 11", "title": "Chapter 11", "subtitle": "Effects of General Wage Fluctuations on Prices of Production", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 11/Chapter 11 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 3531, "read_time": "18 min read", "tags": ["Transformation Problem & Production Prices", "Wages & Value of Labour-Power", "Organic Composition of Capital (OCC)"], "keywords": ["Effects of General Wage Fluctuations on Prices of Production", "Why Rising Wages Cannot Cause General Inflation", "Redistribution of Surplus-Value between High and Low OCC Sectors", "Inverse Movement of Prices in High vs Low Organic Sectors", "Refutation of Bourgeois Wage-Push Inflation Dogmas"], "text": "Chapter 11 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 3,483 words ⏱️ ~17 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 11 of Capital , Volume III — \"The Effects of General Fluctuations in Wages on the Prices of Production\" — occupies a deliberately subordinate yet strategically revealing position within Part II, \"The Transformation of Profit into Average Profit.\" Once Chapters 9 and 10 have established how individual commodity values are \"transformed\" into prices of production and how a uniform rate of profit is equalized across competing capitals, Chapter 11 asks a follow-up, second-order question: what happens to those already-formed production prices when wages rise or fall everywhere at once ? The central thesis is precise and counterintuitive. A general rise or fall in wages does not change the value of commodities, and it does not change the production price of commodities produced by capital of average social composition — but it pushes the production prices of non-average capitals in opposite directions . A general wage rise raises the production prices of low-composition capitals (those whose outlay is dominated by the wage bill) and lowers the production prices of high-composition capitals (those dominated by constant capital), while a general wage fall produces exactly the reverse. The rises on one side and the falls on the other offset each other, so that the aggregate sum of production prices remains equal to the aggregate sum of values. The intellectual objective is twofold. First, Marx wants to demonstrate rigorously that a change in wages is purely a redistribution of a value-sum whose magnitude is fixed by the quantity of labour set in motion — wages do not create value. Second, he wants to expose the one-sidedness of David Ricardo, who, in Marx's reading, analyzed only the case of a rising wage and its effect on prices, and who \"did not understand the adjustment of values to production prices.\" The chapter's rhetorical climax is the footnote dismissing Ricardo's followers as the servum pecus imitatorum — the \"slavish breed of imitators\" — who could not even execute the \"self-evident and in fact tautological practical application\" of the master's own doctrine. Methodologically, the chapter is a controlled thought experiment built on numerical examples. It presupposes that the general rate of profit, average profit, and the transformation of values into prices of production are \"given facts,\" and confines itself to tracing how a single disturbance — a uniform change in the wage — ripples through capitals of differing organic composition. Marx himself calls this \"a very secondary question compared with the other important points which have been dealt with in this Part.\" Its importance lies less in its own result than in what it reveals about the mechanics of the transformation and the correct causal ordering of value, profit, and price. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Initial Setup: Average Composition, the Wage Rise, and the Invariant Value-Sum Marx opens by fixing a baseline. Let the average composition of the social capital be 80c + 20v , with profit at 20 per cent . Because the rate of surplus-value is defined as s/v and here equals 100 per cent (20 surplus-value on 20 variable capital), profit and surplus-value coincide for this average capital. This coincidence is not accidental; it is the defining property of the average capital: its price of production equals its value , because the transformation of value into price of production is, by construction, a redistribution around the average, and the average is the pivot that does not move. The organic composition of capital is the ratio c:v — the proportion in which total capital is divided between constant capital (machinery, raw materials, auxiliary materials) and variable capital (the wage fund that purchases living labour). The value composition , by contrast, reflects the labour embodied in those inputs. In this chapter, because Marx holds technology and the value of means of production constant, the two track one another, and \"composition\" functions as a clean index of how much of any given capital is laid out on labour. The core move is now simple. A general rise in wages , \"everything else being equal,\" means a fall in the rate of surplus-value . Since the same labour-power is purchased, but at a higher price, the surplus extracted from it shrinks. Marx raises wages by 25 per cent . The same quantity of labour that previously cost 20 to set in motion now costs 25 . The tableau therefore becomes: 80c + 25v + 15s , instead of 80c + 20v + 20s . Here is the linchpin of the entire chapter: the labour set in motion by the variable capital still produces a value-sum of 40 , exactly as before. The wage change does not alter how much new value the workers create; it only alters how that new value is split between the wage fund (v) and surplus-value (s). Previously v + s = 20 + 20 = 40. Now v + s = 25 + 15 = 40. The magnitude of newly created value is a function of labour time, not of the wage rate — this is Marx's labour theory of value in miniature. Because v has risen from 20 to 25, the excess s (or p) is now only 15 . That profit is realised on a total advanced capital of 105 (80c + 25v), so the new average rate of profit is 15 / 105 = 14 2/7 per cent . (The chapter's OCR renders this as \"14t per cent\"; the arithmetic confirms 1/7 ≈ 14.2857.) Now comes the decisive consequence. Because the production price of the average capital coincides with its value, and its value remains 80c + 25v + 15s = 120 (unchanged), the production price of average-composition commodities does not change. The increase in wages therefore produces a decline in profit but no change in the value of commodities or their price of production. The General Formula: Production Price = k + kp′ Marx now generalises. Before the wage rise, at a 20 per cent average profit rate, the production price of commodities produced in one turnover period equalled their cost price plus 20 per cent profit on that cost price: k + kp′ = k + (20/100)k , where k is the cost price — a variable magnitude that differs according to (a) the value of the means of production consumed and (b) the amount of depreciation that fixed capital surrenders to the product. Cost price k, in other words, is not the same across industries; it lumps together the worn-out constant capital transferred to the product plus the variable capital advanced. After the wage rise, the same formula reappears with a new profit rate: k + (14 2/7 / 100)k. The essential structure is now visible: the production price is a function of two moving parts — the cost price k (which shifts because wages are part of it) and the profit rate p′ (which shifts because the general rate of profit has fallen). Everything that follows is the working-out of how these two movements interact differently according to the organic composition of the capital in question. Marx also notes that the average composition has itself changed — from 80c + 20v to a new percentage form of 76 4/21 c + 23 17/21 v (i.e., 80c + 25v reduced to a percentage of the 105 total). Case II — Below-Average Composition (50c + 50v): The Price Rises Marx now takes a capital below the original average composition — one heavily weighted toward labour, with a composition of 50c + 50v . He assumes, \"for the sake of simplification,\" that all fixed capital is used up in the annual product as depreciation and that the turnover time matches the earlier case — a simplification that lets him treat the annual product as a single turnover and compare prices directly. Before the wage rise, this capital's annual product sold at 50c + 50v + 20p = 120 (cost price 100, profit 20 at the 20 per cent rate). A 25 per cent wage rise lifts variable capital from 50 to 62 1/2 , for the same amount of labour set in motion. If the product were still sold at 120, the breakdown would be 50c + 62 1/2 v + 7 1/2 s , yielding a profit rate of only 7.5 / 112.5 = 6 2/3 per cent — well below the new social average. But competition equalises profit rates. The capital of 50c + 62 1/2 v = 112 1/2 must now earn the new average rate of 14 2/7 per cent : 112 1/2 × (14 2/7) / 100 = 112 1/2 / 7 = 16 1/14 . The production price is therefore 50c + 62 1/2 v + 16 1/14 p = 128 4/7 . (The running text's figures \"128 1/14\" reflect OCR distortion; the accompanying statement that the price has risen \"by more than 7 per cent\" confirms the correct total, 128 4/7, since 128.5714 / 120 ≈ 1.0714 — a rise of just over 7 per cent.) So a 25 per cent wage rise lifts the production price of this low-composition product from 120 to 128 4/7, an increase of more than 7 per cent. Why does the price rise ? Because for a labour-intensive capital, the wage rise inflates the cost price k dramatically (from 100 to 112.5), and although the profit rate falls, the profit magnitude is now applied to a much larger base, so the gross profit (16 1/14) is not enough to offset the swollen cost. The two movements — higher cost, lower rate — net out to a higher price. Case III — Above-Average Composition (92c + 8v): The Price Falls Marx then takes a capital above the average composition, heavily weighted toward constant capital: 92c + 8v . Under identical assumptions (full depreciation, same turnover), its original production price is likewise 120 (cost price 100 + 20 profit). The 25 per cent wage rise lifts variable capital from 8 to 10 , so the cost price grows only from 100 to 102 — a mere 2-unit increase, because so little of this capital is spent on wages. The average profit rate again falls to 14 2/7 per cent. Applying the proportional relationship: 100 : 14 2/7 = 102 : 14 4/7. The profit accruing to a cost price of 102 is therefore 14 4/7 , and the product sells at 102 + 14 4/7 = 116 4/7 . The production price has fallen from 120 to 116 4/7 — a fall of 3 3/7. Here the mechanism is reversed: because wages are such a small fraction of outlay, the cost price barely moves (100 → 102), but the profit rate collapses from 20 per cent to 14 2/7 per cent. The reduced rate, applied to a nearly unchanged base, yields a profit too small to sustain the old price. Hence the price falls. The Three Results, the Balancing, and the \"No Compensation\" Argument Marx crystallises the findings: (I) For capital of average social composition, the commodity's price of production remains unchanged. (II) For capital of lower composition, the production price rises — though not in the same ratio as the profit has fallen. (III) For capital of higher composition, the production price falls — though again not in the same ratio as the profit. The crucial aggregate result follows immediately. Because the average capital's production price is unchanged and equals its value, the sum of production prices over all capitals is also unchanged, equal to the sum of values produced by the total capital. The rises in Case II and the falls in Case III \"balance out at the level of the socially average capital,\" when the whole capital of society is considered. This is the invariance principle of the transformation: redistribution among branches leaves the total intact. Marx then drives home the political-economic point. The opposite price movements already show there can be no compensation for the wage rise through prices : In Case III, the fall in the price of production \"can in no way compensate the capitalists for the fall in their profit.\" In Case II, the rise in price \"still does not prevent a fall in profit.\" In both cases — whether the price rises or falls — the profit actually realised is the same as for the average capital: a fall in the average profit of 5 5/7 percentage points (the OCR \"Sf per cent\"), which is \"somewhat over 25 per cent\" of the original 20 per cent rate (5 5/7 ÷ 20 = 2/7 ≈ 28.57 per cent). Marx spells out the logic of counterfactual necessity: if the price did not rise in Case II and fall in Case III, then II would be sold below the new lower average profit and III above it — and competition would not have equalised profit rates. The price movements are the mechanism by which equalisation to the new, lower average rate is accomplished. This is why the effect is so uneven: \"according to whether 50, 25 or 10 out of every 100 units of capital are laid out on labour, a rise in wages will necessarily have very different effects\" on the three capitalists. The Reverse Case: A General Fall in Wages Marx now asks the mirror-image question — \"a result which Ricardo does not investigate\": what happens to production prices when wages fall generally, with a corresponding rise in the average profit rate? His method is explicit: \"We have simply to turn the above example round.\" Case I. Average capital, 80c + 20v = 100 , surplus-value rate 100 per cent, production price = value = 80c + 20v + 20s = 120 , profit rate 20 per cent. Wages fall by a quarter: variable capital drops from 20 to 15 , while the same constant capital is still set in motion. The commodity value becomes 80c + 15v + 25s = 120 — still 120 . The labour's newly created value is unchanged; only its distribution between capitalist and worker shifts. Surplus-value rises from 20 to 25 (a rate of surplus-value of 25/15 = 166 2/3 per cent ). Profit is now 25 on a total capital of 95 (80c + 15v), giving a profit rate of 26 6/19 per cent (25/95 = 5/19). The new percentage composition becomes 84 4/19 c + 15 15/19 v = 100 . Case II. Below-average composition, originally 50c + 50v. The quarter wage cut reduces v to 37 1/2 , so total capital is 50c + 37 1/2 v = 87 1/2 . Applying the new profit rate of 26 6/19 per cent: 100 : 26 6/19 = 87 1/2 : 23 1/38. The same mass of commodities that previously cost 120 now costs 87 1/2 + 23 1/38 = 110 10/19 — a fall in price of almost 10. Case III. Above-average composition, originally 92c + 8v = 100. The quarter wage cut reduces 8v to 6v , and total capital to 98 . Applying 26 6/19 per cent: 100 : 26 6/19 = 98 : 25 15/19. The production price, previously 100 + 20 = 120, is now 98 + 25 15/19 = 123 15/19 — a rise of almost 4. The conclusion is the exact mirror image. A general fall in wages leads to a general rise in surplus-value, in the rate of surplus-value, and (other things equal, though in a different proportion) in the profit rate. It causes a fall in the production prices of low-composition capitals and a rise in those of high-composition capitals — \"exactly the opposite result as that which arose from a general rise in wages.\" The Assumption Set: Constant Working Day, Constant Subsistence Prices Marx is careful to fence off the argument. In both the rise and the fall scenarios, two things are held constant : the working day and the prices of all necessary means of subsistence . These assumptions matter enormously, because they define what the exercise actually isolates. If the working day were allowed to vary, or if the value of wage-goods were allowed to change, the effects would compound and the clean result would be muddied. The corollary Marx draws is a logically tight one: under these assumptions, \"a fall in wages is thus only possible here either if wages previously stood above the normal price of labour, or if they are now to be pushed below it.\" In other words, absent a change in the working day or in the value of subsistence goods, a general wage change can only be a deviation from the value of labour-power — above-natural or below-subsistence — not a change in that value itself. The Qualification: When Wage Changes Originate in Subsistence Values Marx then adds the qualification that anticipates the later section on ground-rent. If the rise or fall in wages results from a change in the value of the necessary means of subsistence (rather than from a redistribution of a fixed wage box), a single modification to the above analysis is required: the argument holds except when the commodities whose price-changes raise or lower variable capital also enter as constituent elements into constant capital — that is, when the same cheapened or dearened goods are both wage-goods and industrial inputs. In that case, the change in their value does not merely alter wages; it also alters c. \"But in so far as they do only affect wages, the above argument contains all that has to be said.\" This is a precise scoping condition: the clean two-case result depends on the wage-change being isolated to the variable-capital channel. Methodological Framing and the Polemic against Ricardo The chapter closes by reasserting its own status. Throughout, Marx has assumed that the establishment of a general rate of profit, an average profit, and hence the transformation of values into production prices is \"a given fact.\" The only question posed has been how a general wage change affects production prices that are \"given in advance.\" Marx concedes this is \"a very secondary question compared with the other important points\" of Part II — but it happens to be \"the only question Ricardo deals with which is relevant here,\" and Ricardo treats it \"only in a one-sided and inadequate way.\" The footnotes sharpen the polemic. Footnote 34 charges that Ricardo \"did not understand the adjustment of values to production prices,\" and therefore \"did not once consider this possibility [of a wage fall], but only the first case, a rise in wages and its influence on the production prices of commodities.\" By working only the wage-rise case, Ricardo missed the symmetry that Marx's method makes visible. The footnote then derides Ricardo's followers — the servum pecus imitatorum (Horace's \"slavish breed of imitators\") — for failing even to perform the \"quite self-evident and in fact tautological practical application\" of the doctrine. The closing note cross-references Theories of Surplus-Value , Part II, pp. 189–203, where Marx develops this critique at length. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Chapter 11 distils several fundamental principles. First, and most important, value is created by labour, not by wages ; a general wage change merely redistributes a value-sum fixed by the quantity of labour set in motion. Second, the average capital is the invariant pivot of the price system: its production price equals its value and does not move when wages change, which is precisely why the sum of all production prices equals the sum of all values. Third, and this is the chapter's signature contribution, a uniform input-price shock propagates through different branches in opposite directions according to organic composition — a rise in wages lifts the production prices of labour-intensive capitals and lowers those of capital-intensive ones. This is the mechanism by which the new, lower (or higher) general rate of profit is equalised. The broader theoretical implications are substantial. Against Ricardo, Marx shows that the analysis of wage effects on prices is inseparable from the theory of the transformation of values into production prices: one cannot correctly trace the price consequences of a wage change without first having a rigorous account of how a general profit rate is formed. The chapter also demonstrates that there is no price-level compensation for a wage change — the \"wage-price spiral\" is not a mechanism that restores capitalists' profits in real terms. On the contrary, in both the price-raising and price-lowering branches, the profit rate falls to the new average, and no branch escapes. Finally, the chapter is a model of method as much as doctrine : it isolates a single variable (the wage), fixes everything else (working day, subsistence values, technology), traces the two symmetrical cases, and carefully notes the one condition under which the clean result would be qualified (when wage-goods are also constant-capital inputs). In doing so, it illuminates the deepest architecture of Volume III — the priority of value over price, the derivative character of profit rates, and the systemic, divergent, but ultimately invariant-preserving redistribution that constitutes the transformation from values into prices of production. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 10 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 12"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 12", "title": "Chapter 12", "subtitle": "Supplementary Remarks on Prices of Production", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 12/Chapter 12 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 7022, "read_time": "35 min read", "tags": ["Transformation Problem & Production Prices", "Fixed vs Circulating Capital"], "keywords": ["Supplementary Remarks on Prices of Production", "Causes of Compensation Differences", "Turnover Velocity Influence on Production Prices", "Interaction of Fixed Capital Depreciation with Average Profit", "Summary of Value-to-Price Mathematical Transformations"], "text": "Chapter 12 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,968 words ⏱️ ~35 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 12: Supplementary Remarks — An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 12 of Capital Volume 3 carries the unassuming title \"Supplementary Remarks.\" The label conceals one of the most methodologically explosive short chapters in the entire work. It is not a fresh theory but a mopping-up operation: Marx here closes three specific loopholes left open by the preceding analysis of the transformation of values into prices of production, and in closing them he lays bare his full position on the relationship between the visible surface of capitalist economy and its concealed inner core. The central problem is this. Marx has just argued that commodities do not exchange at their values but at prices of production, defined as k + p — the cost price plus average profit. This creates three embarrassments. First, if prices of production are their own law of motion, what governs their changes, and can that law still be traced back to value? Second, for capitals of average composition Marx has claimed the special privilege that price of production equals value — but the inputs of those very capitals are bought at prices of production, not values. Does this not introduce a circularity that empties the claim of content? Third, and deepest: competition — the only thing the actors themselves see — displays average profits that seem independent of the organic composition of capital, and prices that rise when wages fall (a phenomenon that appears to flatly contradict the labour theory of value). The phenomenal world therefore seems to refute the theory that is supposed to explain it. The core thesis is a triple claim. (a) Every change in a price of production can ultimately be reduced to a change in value, but not conversely : value changes may fail to register in prices of production because the latter are determined not by the value of the single commodity but by the total value of all commodities. (b) The average-composition case survives the input-price circularity because what determines surplus-value — hence average profit — is not the magnitude of the cost price but the ratio of v to (c + v); the figures 80c + 20v need not be value-figures, only properly proportional ones. (c) The \"grounds for compensation\" that capitalists experience as the source of their profit — risk, slow turnover, distant markets, cyclical fluctuations — are nothing but the mechanisms through which total social surplus-value is redistributed, a system of shareholding in the collective social capital. The capitalist's consciousness inverts this: he sees compensation as creating profit. The intellectual objective , then, is not merely technical repair. It is to demonstrate that the phenomenal forms of competition are not incomplete or imperfect representations of the essence but its \"very reverse and antithesis\" — a systematic inversion that therefore cannot be corrected by gazing harder at appearances, but only by a critical science that reconstructs the concealed inner core. Systemic placement. The chapter sits at the hinge of Volume 3, Part II. It follows the demonstration of the general rate of profit and precedes Part III (the tendency of the rate of profit to fall) and Parts IV–VII (commercial capital, interest-bearing capital, ground rent). Its closing gesture — the \"common booty,\" the \"whole social enterprise,\" the \"inverted configuration\" — prepares the terrain for the later analysis in which merchant's profit, interest, and rent will appear to the actors as autonomous sources of revenue, each with its own \"justification.\" The chapter is the epistemological antechamber to the \"trinity formula\" that Marx will dismantle at the book's end. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Two Doors Through Which a Price of Production Can Move Marx opens with a ruthless dichotomization. \"The price of production of a commodity can vary for only two reasons\": either (1) the general rate of profit changes, or (2) the general rate of profit remains unaltered. This exhausts logical space because the price of production is a composed magnitude — k plus the profit accrued on k at the general rate — so any movement in it must originate either in the general rate that scales profit or in the cost price/value base itself. The first door leads outside the individual commodity, into the economy-wide determinants of profitability; the second door leads inside, into the value of the commodity's own reproduction. Everything that follows unpacks these two doors. The first door immediately generates a question: what could cause the general rate of profit to change? Marx decomposes this further. The general rate r is, definitionally, the mass of surplus-value divided by the total social capital advanced — structurally r = s/(c + v) , which can be rewritten as (s/v) / [(c/v) + 1] . Therefore r can change only if either the average rate of surplus-value s/v changes, or — holding s/v fixed — the ratio of the total capital's constant to variable components (its value-composition) changes. Marx's opening paragraph is nothing more than this algebraic truism, but he immediately begins converting algebra into social causation by asking what, in the world of production, could make either numerator or denominator move. The General Rate of Profit Traces Back to Productivity and Value Consider the first sub-case: a change in the average rate of surplus-value. Marx immediately brackets two \"movements\" as inadmissible: a depression of wages below their \"normal level\" or a rise above it. These, he insists, \"are never more than oscillations\" — temporary deviations from the value of labour-power, not changes in the value of labour-power itself. This bracketing is crucial and methodological: Marx refuses to let the accidental wobbles of the labour market masquerade as explanations of structural change. Once they are set aside, a sustained change in the rate of surplus-value \"can occur only because the value of labour-power has either fallen or risen.\" But a change in the value of labour-power is itself a derived phenomenon. It depends on \"the value of the commodities that are consumed by the worker,\" and those values in turn depend on \"the productivity of that labour which produces the means of subsistence.\" Here is the causal chain compressed into a single beat: a change in the rate of surplus-value → a change in the value of labour-power → a change in the value of wage goods → a change in the productivity of labour in the wage-goods sector. The entire movement of the first sub-case is thus traceable, at its root, to a change in the productivity of labour and therefore to a change in commodity values. Nothing in the movement of the rate of profit originates outside the value-determining process. Now the second sub-case: the ratio between the sum of surplus-value appropriated and the total social capital advanced changes, while the rate of surplus-value remains fixed . Since this change \"does not arise from the rate of surplus-value,\" it \"must proceed from the total capital, and moreover from its constant part\" — that is, from c . Marx's reasoning proceeds in three steps. (i) The technical mass of constant capital is functionally proportional to the labour-power bought by variable capital; more workers require more raw material, more instruments, more auxiliary materials. (ii) The value of that constant capital rises and falls with its technical mass. (iii) Therefore the mass of constant capital rises and falls \"similarly in proportion to the sum of value of the variable capital.\" But this proportionality is not a fixed constant — it is the level of productivity. \"If the same labour sets more constant capital in motion, it has become more productive, and vice versa.\" The conclusion therefore follows directly: a change in the composition of capital at a fixed rate of surplus-value is nothing other than a change in the productivity of labour, and \"a change must have occurred in the value of certain commodities.\" The importance of this passage can hardly be overstated. It closes the first door from the inside: any movement of the general rate of profit, whether through the numerator ( s/v ) or the denominator ( c/v ), routes back through productivity and hence through value. The rate of profit is not an autonomous force; it is a surface trace of underlying movements in the productivity of labour and the values of commodities. This is the labour theory of value operating not as a price-formation formula (which Marx explicitly denies it is, in the aggregate) but as a theory of the causal structure of the capitalist economy. The Master Law: Value May Change Without Necessarily Changing Price Marx now fuses both sub-cases into a single law: \"if the production price of a commodity changes as the result of a change in the general rate of profit, its own value may well remain unaffected. However, there must have been a change in its value relative to other commodities.\" This is subtle and worth unpacking carefully. Under a change in the general rate of profit, a commodity might have undergone no change at all in its own value — the same labour-time still reproduces it — and yet its price of production moves, because the profit added to its cost price has changed, or because the total value configuration that fixes the general rate has changed. What must have changed is its value relative to other commodities: some other commodity's value must have shifted, producing the shift in the general rate, and hence in this commodity's price. Relative value is the relational ground of the movement; absolute individual value may be untouched. This is the first statement of the chapter's governing asymmetry: prices of production are social-relational magnitudes, not individual-value magnitudes. A commodity's price of production is not a private fact about that commodity but a position in a system. The Second Door: A Change in the Commodity's Own Value Now case (2): \"The general rate of profit remains unaltered.\" Here, Marx says, the production price of a commodity \"can change only because its value has altered; because more or less labour is required for its actual reproduction.\" Note \"actual reproduction\" — Marx is careful to include not only the labour that directly produces the good in its final form but also \"the labour producing those commodities that go towards producing it,\" i.e. the labour embodied in the means of production. This is the point on which the entire second section will turn. The illustration is economical: the price of production of cotton yarn may fall \"either because raw cotton is produced more cheaply, or because the work of spinning has become more productive as a result of better machinery.\" One path alters the value of an input; the other alters the value contributed by the final stage. Both are internal to value — no wages movement, no profit-rate movement, no market accident is required. The Formula k + p = k + kp′ and the Fundamental Asymmetry Marx then formalizes the price of production: \"Price of production, as we have already shown, is k + p , cost price plus profit. But this = k + kp′ , where k , the cost price, is a magnitude which varies according to the different spheres of production and is everywhere equal to the value of the constant and variable capital used up to produce the commodity, while p′ is the average rate of profit calculated as a percentage.\" Unpack the notation. k is the cost price — the sum of the values (or, by the argument of Section II, at least the cost-price sums) of the constant and variable capital consumed in production. p′ is the average rate of profit expressed as a fraction or percentage of the total capital advanced (here of k , understood as the capital used up). The second equality, p = kp′ , expresses profit as a percentage mark-up on cost — which is precisely how the individual capitalist, accounting on his ledger, experiences it. Marx then walks through a numerical case: \"If k = 200 and p′ = 20 per cent, the price of production k + kp′ = 200 + 200 × 20/100 = 200 + 40 = 240.\" The purpose of the arithmetic is to exhibit a logical possibility that will shortly prove decisive: \"It is evident that this price of production may remain the same even though the value of the commodity changes.\" Because the price of production is a function of k and p′ — not of the commodity's value per se — a change in value that leaves k (the value of the capital consumed expressed as cost) and the general rate of profit unchanged will leave the price untouched. The chain value → price of production is therefore a conditional one: value change is necessary but not sufficient for price change. Marx draws the full asymmetry with unmistakable clarity: \"All changes in the price of production of a commodity can be ultimately reduced to a change in value, but not all changes in the value of a commodity need find expression in a change in the price of production, since this is not determined simply by the value of the particular commodity in question, but rather by the total value of all commodities. A change in commodity A, therefore, may be balanced by an opposite change in commodity B, so that the general proportion remains the same.\" This is the chapter's first substantive thesis, and it is a totality thesis: prices of production are the gravitational centres of a system in which value-change in A can be systematically cancelled by value-change in B before it ever reaches the price of A. The individual commodity is not a closed monad; it is a node in a network whose equilibrium properties are determined at the level of the whole. The Two Reasons Price of Production Diverges From Value (Section II) Having disposed of the dynamics of price of production, Marx turns to a residual worry about its static definition. Section II opens by recalling the two reasons \"the divergence of price of production from value\" arises. The first is the classical one: \"because the average profit is added to the cost price of a commodity, rather than the surplus-value contained in it.\" Where a capital's composition is above the social average, its own surplus-value is less than average profit; the price of production must exceed value. Where composition is below the social average, the reverse. This is the familiar sectoral redistribution. But the second reason is more insidious and it is the real subject of the section: \"because the price of production of a commodity that diverges in this way from its value enters as an element into the cost price of other commodities.\" The inputs that a producer buys are themselves bought at production prices, not values. There is, therefore, \"a divergence from the value of the means of production consumed … already contained in the cost price, quite apart from the divergence that may arise for the commodity itself from the difference between average profit and surplus-value.\" The divergence is second-order and self-referential — a divergence inside the cost price , generated upstream in the production chain before the commodity in question has even been priced. This is the earliest explicit statement in Marx of what later economists would call the input-price transformation problem — the fact that if outputs are priced at production prices, then inputs (which are yesterday's outputs) are too, and the entire system is one of mutually determined prices, not a one-way mapping from values to prices. Inputs Priced at Prices of Production: The Circularity Objection Marx now asks the sharp question: \"It is quite possible, accordingly, for the cost price to diverge from the value sum of the elements of which this component of the price of production is composed, even in the case of commodities that are produced by capitals of average composition.\" This is the danger. The special privilege of average-composition capitals — price of production = value — was supposed to be the anchor of the whole doctrine, the point at which value and price visibly coincide. If even in those capitals the cost price k is not a value sum, has the anchor been cut loose? Marx works the case concretely. \"Let us assume that the average composition is 80c + 20v.\" Here c and v are the constant and variable capital components of the capital advanced. Marx's point is that, for actual capitals that happen to be composed in this way, the \"80c may be greater or less than the value of c, the constant capital, since this c is composed of commodities whose prices of production are different from their values.\" The 80 units of constant capital are a bundle of commodities — machinery, raw materials, fuel — each of which was itself sold at a production price, not a value. Hence the 80 is 80 in price terms , not 80 in value terms . The same applies to v : \"The 20v can similarly diverge from its value, if the spending of wages on consumption involves commodities whose prices of production are different from their values.\" Wage goods, too, are priced at production prices. And this has a real, not merely accounting, consequence: \"The workers must work for a greater or lesser amount of time in order to buy back these commodities (to replace them) and must therefore perform more or less necessary labour than would be needed if the prices of production of their necessary means of subsistence did coincide with their values.\" Here Marx is being rigorous: a divergence in the prices of wage goods translates directly into a divergence in the quantity of necessary labour , which is the substance of the value of labour-power. The circularity cuts deep. The Ratio, Not the Magnitude: Why Average Composition Still Holds The resolution is the intellectual heart of the chapter. \"Yet this possibility in no way affects the correctness of the principles put forward for commodities of average composition.\" Why not? The argument turns on what determines surplus-value. For the capital composed 80c + 20v, Marx says: \"the important thing as far as the determination of surplus-value is concerned is not whether these figures are the expression of actual values, but rather what their mutual relationship is; i.e. that v is one-fifth of the total capital and c is four-fifths. As soon as this is the case, as assumed above, the surplus-value v produces is equal to the average profit.\" Consider what this says. Surplus-value is produced by the labour-power purchased by v . If v is one-fifth of the total capital in whatever units we are working — value units, price units, or cost-price monetary units — then the surplus-value produced relative to total capital advanced matches the social average rate, because the social average rate was itself defined as the total surplus-value divided by the total social capital, and the composition of this particular capital equals the composition of the average. The ratio of v to (c + v), not its absolute numerical magnitude, is the operative determinant. What matters is that the capital stands at the average axis of composition — not that its figures are literal value quantities. Marx is implicitly saying: the magnitudes in the two-aggregate equalities (total value = total price of production; total surplus-value = total profit) are consistent under any choice of numéraire, and it is the relations that carry the theoretical content. Two identities follow immediately. First, because the composition is average, the surplus-value contained in the commodity equals the average profit. Second, \"because it is equal to the average profit, the price of production = cost price + profit = k + p = k + s , which is equal in practice to the commodity's value.\" Note the \"=\" between s and p : for an average-composition capital, surplus-value and profit coincide because there is no redistributive gap to bridge. The price of production therefore coincides with value \"in practice.\" The phrase is important: it is not an exact mathematical identity in a world of mutual price determination; it is a practical identity, an identity of result , which suffices for the theoretical claim. The Consistency Test: Why Wage Changes Cannot Move Prices in Average Spheres Marx now performs a decisive consistency test, which doubles as an indirect proof of the value principle. \"In other words, an increase or decrease in wages in this case leaves k + p unaffected, just as it would leave the commodity's value unaffected, and simply brings about a corresponding converse movement, a decrease or increase, on the side of the profit rate.\" This is the crucial connection with the appearance catalogued later in Section III, where \"rises and falls in the prices of production as a result of changes in the wage level\" seem to contradict the value relationship. Marx is here establishing that, for the benchmark case, the appearance is consistent with the essence: a wage change moves p′ , not k + p , precisely because k + p = k + s and s is produced by v 's labour — a wage increase reduces s one-for-one, hence reduces profit one-for-one, with no effect on price. This exactly mirrors the behaviour of value under a wage change: value depends on labour-time, not on its division into paid and unpaid parts. Prices of production in average spheres behave from the wage side as values would . The proof proceeds by reductio ad absurdum from the equilibrium condition: \"If an increase or decrease in wages did affect the price of commodities in this case, the profit rate in these spheres of average composition would come to stand below or above its level in the other spheres.\" Average spheres would be knocked off the general average — which is a contradiction, since they are average. \"It is only in so far as their prices remain unaltered that the spheres of average composition maintain the same level of profit as the others. The same thing thus takes place in practice as if the products of these spheres were sold at their actual values.\" The phrase \"as if\" (in practice) is not a concession of weakness. It is a statement of Marx's method: what the theory predicts for average-composition capitals (price = value in practical effect) is exactly what the equilibrating mechanism of competition must produce, on pain of inconsistency. Section II closes by reinforcing the general principle with respect to wages and values: \"if commodities are sold at their actual values, it is clear that with other circumstances remaining the same, a rise or fall in wages provokes a corresponding fall or rise in profit but no change in the commodity's value, and … in no circumstances can a rise or fall in wages ever affect the value of commodities, but only the size of the surplus-value.\" This is the classical Ricardian result, here re-established within Marx's own price-of-production framework. The Real Movement of Capital and the Levelling of Profit (Section III) Section III pivots from the analytic to the descriptive-epistemological. Marx restates the mechanism he has already established: \"competition equalizes profit rates between the different spheres of production to produce an average rate of profit, and … this is precisely the way in which the values of products from these various spheres are transformed into prices of production.\" The mechanism is \"the continual transfer of capital from one sphere to another, where profit stands above the average for the time being.\" Three refinements immediately follow. First, the temporal dimension: the \"cycle of fat and lean years that follow one another in a given branch of industry over a particular period of time, and the fluctuations in profit that these involve\" must be included in the account. The uninterrupted emigration and immigration of capitals between spheres \"produces rising and falling movements in the profit rate which more or less balance one another out and thus tend to reduce the profit rate everywhere to the same common and general level.\" Equalization is not instantaneous; it operates through a multi-year averaging over the boom-bust cycle. Second, the causal trigger : \"This movement of capitals is always brought about in the first place by the state of market prices, which raise profits above the general average level in one place, and reduce it below the average in another.\" Marx explicitly sets commercial capital aside \"for the time being\" — a limitation he flags — but observes that speculation in favoured articles can \"withdraw masses of capital from one line of business with extraordinary rapidity and fling them just as suddenly into another.\" Third, the frictions : \"In every sphere of actual production, however, industry, agriculture, mining, etc., the transfer of capital from one sector to another presents significant difficulties, particularly on account of the fixed capital involved.\" The mobility that equalization assumes is constrained by the immobility of installed plant. Yet the empirical result holds over the cycle: \"Experience shows … that if one branch of industry, e.g. cotton, yields extraordinarily high profits at one time, it may bring in very low profits at another, or even run a loss, so that in a particular cycle of years the average profit is more or less the same as in other branches. Capital soon learns to reckon with this experience.\" That last clause — \"capital soon learns to reckon with this experience\" — is the hinge into the epistemological section. The levelling is not merely an objective process imposed on passive capitals; it is a process that capitals learn to anticipate. What Competition Does Not Show: The Three Inverted Appearances Now Marx isolates precisely what competition fails to reveal. \"What competition does not show, however, is the determination of values that governs the movement of production; that it is values that stand behind the prices of production and ultimately determine them.\" Competition displays effects whose causes remain invisible. He enumerates three surface phenomena, each of which seems to contradict the value principle: \" average profits that are independent of the organic composition of capital in the various spheres of production , i.e. independent of the mass of living labour appropriated in a given sphere of exploitation.\" The observable world looks as if profit had nothing to do with how much living labour a capital employs — a fact that seems to refute the labour theory of surplus-value directly. (Underneath, of course, is the equalization mechanism: profits are proportional to capital, not to labour, at the surface — because surplus-value has been redistributed.) \" rises and falls in the prices of production as a result of changes in the wage level — a phenomenon which at first sight seems completely to contradict the value relationship of commodities.\" Marx has just shown in Section II that this is precisely what value should produce in average spheres (wage rise → profit fall, no price change there); but in non -average spheres the visible movement of prices will look like pure wage-determined pricing. \" fluctuations in market prices that reduce the average market price of a commodity over a given period of time, not to its market value but rather to a market price of production that diverges from this market value and is something very different.\" Marx's conclusion is famously direct: \"All these phenomena seem to contradict both the determination of value by labour-time and the nature of surplus-value as consisting of unpaid surplus labour. In competition, therefore, everything appears upside down.\" The Finished Configuration vs. the Concealed Inner Core This gives rise to the chapter's most quoted theoretical articulation of Marx's epistemology of the capitalist mode of production: \"The finished configuration of economic relations, as these are visible on the surface, in their actual existence, and therefore also in the notions with which the bearers and agents of these relations seek to gain an understanding of them, is very different from the configuration of their inner core, which is essential but concealed, and the concept corresponding to it. It is in fact the very reverse and antithesis of this.\" Unpack the vocabulary. \"Finished configuration\" ( fertige Gestalt ) is the shape economic relations assume when fully developed and observed from outside — the world of competitive prices, of uniform profit rates, of wage-cost pricing. \"Their actual existence\" grounds the appearance in reality, not illusion: the surface is not a fantasy. \"The notions with which the bearers and agents of these relations seek to gain an understanding\" — that is, the spontaneous consciousness of capitalists, workers, bankers, landlords, as it crystallises around the visible forms. \"Inner core\" ( innerer Zusammenhang ): the connective structure of value, surplus-value, and their transformation. It is \"essential but concealed\" ( wesentlich aber verhüllt ). And crucially, the relation is not merely one of hiddenness but of inversion : the surface is \"the very reverse and antithesis\" of the core. This is the theoretical warrant for the whole critique of political economy: if the surface were merely incomplete, common sense and ordinary observation would suffice; because it is systematically inverted, a critical science is required to invert the inversion. The Developed Consciousness and the Grounds for Compensation Marx now arrives at what gives Section III its title. Once capitalist production is developed, the equalisation of profit rates no longer operates only through the blind attraction-and-repulsion of capital via market prices. \"Once average prices and the market prices corresponding to them have been established for a certain length of time, the various individual capitalists become conscious that certain differences are balanced out in this equalisation, and so they take these into account in their calculations among themselves.\" The mechanism becomes internalised . \"These differences are actively present in the capitalists' view of things and are taken into account by them as grounds for compensation.\" \" Grounds for compensation \" ( Kompensationsgründe ) is the operative concept. It designates any circumstance which would otherwise make one investment abnormally unprofitable or profitable relative to others and which is neutralised, in the capitalist's own pricing calculus, by a compensating price adjustment. The mechanism of equalisation has migrated from the market's invisible hand into the business calculation itself. \"The basic notion in this connection is that of average profit itself, the idea that capitals of equal size must yield equal profits in the same period of time.\" From this maxim, Marx derives the capitalist ideological form: \"This is based in turn on the idea that capital in each sphere of production has to participate according to its size in the total surplus-value extorted from the workers by the total social capital; or that each particular capital should be viewed simply as a fragment of the total capital and each capitalist in fact as a shareholder in the whole social enterprise, partaking in the overall profit in proportion to the size of his share of capital.\" This is a striking formulation. The capitalist does not, in his own consciousness, see himself as a shareholder in a social enterprise: but his practice — his calculation of average profit, his expectation of equal returns on equal capitals regardless of the labour employed — presupposes exactly such a shareholding arrangement. Marx is claiming that the operative logic of capitalist competition, whether or not the individual recognises it, is the logic of equitable distribution of a common pool — the \"common booty\" of total surplus-value, whose title is proportionality to capital advanced. Average Profit as Shareholding in the Social Enterprise Marx now illustrates the shareholding logic with concrete business calculations. First, turnover time: a capital \"that turns over more slowly, either because the commodity in question remains in the production process for a longer period or because it has to be sold on distant markets, still charges the profit it would otherwise lose by raising its price and compensates itself in this way.\" A slow-turning capital would, at the social average rate p′ , earn less per unit time than a fast-turning one; the market, through the producer's own pricing, restores proportionality by raising k + p . Second, risk: \"capital investments that are exposed to greater risk, as in shipping, for instance, receive compensation through increased prices.\" Marx then makes a pointed argument against the risk theory of profit — anticipating, by three-quarters of a century, Frank Knight's and others' attempts to derive profit from uncertainty. \"Once capitalist production is properly developed, and with it the insurance system, the risk is in fact the same for all spheres of production (see Corbet); those more endangered simply pay higher insurance premiums and receive these back in the price of their commodities.\" The footnote cites An Inquiry into the Causes and Modes of the Wealth of Individuals (London, 1841), pp. 100–102 — an obscure work from which Marx takes the proposition that insurance premiums equalise the cost of risk across all spheres. The theoretical point is decisive: risk-bearing is a cost that enters k , not a source of profit above the average; and once insurance exists, differential risk is converted into a differential premium, recovered through price, with no surplus-value created. Risk can justify a price adder , but it cannot generate profit in the Marxian sense — profit remains a transformed form of surplus-value. Marx articulates the general form: \"any circumstance that makes one capital investment less profitable and another one more so (and all these investments are taken as equally necessary, within certain limits) is invariably taken into account as a valid reason for compensation, without there being any need for the constant repetition of the activities of competition in order to demonstrate the justification for including such motives or factors in the capitalist's calculations.\" The Fetishism of Compensation: Profit as an Addition to Cost Price The paragraph that follows is the section's culmination, and it merits the closest reading. The capitalist \"simply forgets (or rather he no longer sees it, since competition does not show it to him) that all these grounds for compensation that make themselves mutually felt in the reciprocal calculation of commodity prices by the capitalists in different branches of production are simply related to the fact that they all have an equal claim on the common booty, the total surplus-value, in proportion to their capital.\" Here is the whole dialectic compressed. The grounds for compensation — slow turnover, distance, risk, cyclical fluctuation — are the form in which the equal claim on total surplus-value is asserted and enforced. They are distributive mechanisms. But the capitalist sees them differently: \"It appears to them, rather, that the profit which they pocket is something different from the surplus-value they extort; that the grounds for compensation do not simply equalise their participation in the total surplus-value, but that they actually create profit itself, since profit seems to derive simply from the addition to the cost price made with one justification or another.\" The inversion is complete. What in truth is a redistribution of already-extorted surplus-value appears on the surface as the creation of fresh profit out of nothing more than a justified mark-up on cost. This is the precise form of profit-fetishism in the sphere of average profit. It is structurally identical to, though distinct from, the surplus-value fetishism analysed in Volume 1 Chapter 1, Section 4: the attribution to things (capital, risk, time, distance) of a social power that properly belongs to a relation between persons (the appropriation of unpaid labour). Continuity With Chapter 7: The Capitalist's Conception of Surplus-Value Marx closes by invoking his earlier analysis. \"Finally, what was said in Chapter 7, p. 236, about the capitalist's ideas as to the source of surplus-value applies also to the average profit.\" Chapter 7 of Volume 3 discussed the capitalist's spontaneous conception of surplus-value — the way in which he attributes profit to the productive power of his capital, to the sale of the commodity above its cost, and so on. Marx's point here is systematic analogy: the fetishistic inversion identified at the level of the individual capital (surplus-value appears to come from the capital itself) reappears at the level of the average profit, where it appears to come from the various grounds for compensation. But Marx adds one differentiation: \"The only way in which the situation looks different in this second case is that for a given market price and a given level of exploitation of labour, savings on the cost price depend on individual talent, attention, etc.\" That is, at the level of the individual firm operating at an established market price and social rate of exploitation, whatever surplus the capitalist pockets over the average can depend on the personal efficiency of his management. This empirical iridescence of individual talent reinforces the ideological misrecognition: it supplies an apparent, tangible proof that profit is earned by personal contribution, and thereby obscures the fact that the total profit available is a magnitude fixed by the extraction of surplus-value from the working class, whatever particular capitalist manages to appropriate what share of it. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter yields a compact set of fundamental principles. The asymmetry principle. Value change is a necessary but not sufficient condition for a change in the production price of any given commodity; price-of-production change is, however, a sufficient indicator that some value has changed somewhere. This is not a technical footnote but a philosophical position about the direction of explanation: prices are surface traces of underlying value movements, and the theory runs from value to price, not the reverse, even though the observation runs from price to value. The totality principle. A commodity's price of production is determined \"not simply by the value of the particular commodity in question, but rather by the total value of all commodities.\" Value in Marx is therefore a systemic category — it is defined across the whole social product — and this is why individual value changes may be invisible at the level of individual prices. The economy is a network whose equilibrium is holistic. The ratio-not-magnitude principle. For capitals at the average composition, the identity price-of-production = value survives the mutual dependency of prices, because the operative determinant is the proportional structure of the capital (v as one-fifth of the total) rather than the absolute numerical value of k . This dissolves the input-price circularity objection in the special case that anchors the whole theory, and it does so by shifting the theoretical content from numerical magnitudes to relational structure. This is a template for all subsequent stages of Marx's analysis of revenue forms. The compensation principle. The grounds for compensation — risk, turnover-time, market distance, cyclical fluctuation — do not create profit; they redistribute the total social surplus-value in proportion to capital advanced. They are the concrete mechanisms through which the \"shareholding in the whole social enterprise\" is enforced. The inversion principle. The finished configuration of economic relations, visible to the actors themselves, is not merely an incomplete picture of the inner core — it is its \"reverse and antithesis.\" Because the phenomenal form is systematically inverted, ordinary perception and the spontaneous consciousness of economic agents are systematically misleading. The critique of political economy is therefore not optional refinement but a necessary corrective — the only mode of access to the concealed inner core. The broader theoretical implications radiate in several directions. First, the chapter forecloses the risk-of-capital theory of profit in advance: risk-bearing is a cost recovered through price, whose differential incidence is equalised by insurance. Any theory that treats entrepreneurial risk as a source of surplus fails to distinguish between distribution and production. Second, the \"shareholder in the whole social enterprise\" formulation directly prepares Parts IV–VII of Volume 3: commercial profit, interest, and ground rent will all be shown to be claim-tickets on the same total surplus-value, held by different classes of capital-owners (merchants, financiers, landlords), each with its own apparent \"justification.\" The trinity formula — Capital–Profit, Land–Rent, Labour–Wages — is the fully developed form of the same inversion the current chapter isolates at the initial stage of compensation. Third, Marx here anticipates the substantive problem subsequently formalised by Bortkiewicz, Sraffa, and the \"transformation problem\" literature: he acknowledges the second-order divergence of cost prices from value sums, and he resolves it — for the benchmark case — by the ratio argument, of which he says \"in practice\" it is equivalent to selling at values. Whether one takes this as a genuine solution or as a strategic sidestep, the chapter establishes that Marx himself was aware of the mutual dependence of prices and thought it did not undermine the priority of value. Practically, the chapter remains a sharp tool for dismantling contemporary ideological forms. Whenever the \"cost of capital\" is presented as a justification for the price of a product; whenever \"risk premiums,\" \"turnover costs,\" or \"industry-specific returns\" are invoked as if they generated value; whenever the accountant's mark-up on cost is treated as the reality of profit — Marx's argument commands: these are distributive titles to social surplus-value, not its source. The chapter's closing observation that profit appears to depend on \"individual talent, attention, etc.\" names the micro-ideological supplement that makes the misrecognition persuasive at the level of the firm, and thereby names the precise mechanism by which capitalist competition manufactures the appearance of earned profit. Philosophically, the chapter is perhaps Marx's most explicit statement of the relation between science and the phenomenal forms of social life. If the surface of society were merely incomplete, ordinary observation would gradually complete it; because the surface is the systematic antithesis of the essence, science must be built by inverting the inversion. This is the epistemological core that will be radicalised by Lukács's account of reification, by Althusser's reading of Marx's \"symptomatic\" method, and by every subsequent critical theory that insists on the necessary illusions of bourgeois society. \"In competition, therefore, everything appears upside down\" — and the task of theory is not to describe how the world looks, but to reconstruct how it is, and why it must look that way. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 11 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 13"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 13", "title": "Chapter 13", "subtitle": "The Law of the Tendency of the Rate of Profit to Fall", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 13/Chapter 13 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 17334, "read_time": "87 min read", "tags": ["Law of the Falling Rate of Profit (TRPF)", "Organic Composition of Capital (OCC)"], "keywords": ["The Law of the Tendency of the Rate of Profit to Fall (TRPF)", "Progressive Rise in Organic Composition of Capital (c:v)", "Dual Nature of the Law: Falling Rate vs Growing Mass of Profit", "Inherent Technological Impetus of Capitalism to Supplant Labour", "Capital's Internal Barrier: Capital Itself"], "text": "Chapter 13 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 17,307 words ⏱️ ~87 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 13, \"The Law Itself,\" opens Part Three of Capital Volume 3 — the part devoted to what is unquestionably the most explosive, the most contested, and the most theoretically consequential proposition in all of Marx's mature economic writing: the tendential fall in the general rate of profit. The chapter's function within the architecture of Volume 3 is precise and strategic. Volume 3 as a whole treats \"the process of capitalist production as a whole,\" i.e., the unity of production and circulation, and Part Two (Chapters 8–12) has just accomplished the decisive transformation of surplus-value into profit and of the rate of surplus-value into a general, average rate of profit . That result — that profit is surplus-value reckoned against the total capital advanced, so that p′ = s/C, and that the general profit rate is regulated not by any single branch's composition of capital but by the average social composition — is the premise from which Chapter 13 now draws its dramatic conclusion. The central problem the chapter poses can be stated with algebraic brevity and conceptual immensity. If the rate of profit is s/C, and if the historically demonstrated tendency of capitalist development is to raise c (constant capital — machinery, raw and ancillary materials, fixed capital of every kind) relative to v (variable capital — wages, living labour-power), then, so long as the rate of exploitation s/v does not rise enough to offset it, the rate of profit must fall. Marx's opening five-line series establishes this in miniature: given a 100 per cent rate of surplus-value and v fixed at 100, a constant capital of 50 yields a profit rate of 66⅔ per cent; of 100, 50 per cent; of 200, 33⅓ per cent; of 300, 25 per cent; of 400, only 20 per cent. \"The same rate of surplus-value, therefore, and an unchanged level of exploitation of labour, is expressed in a falling rate of profit, as the value of the constant capital and hence the total capital grows with the constant capital's material volume.\" But the problem is not merely that the rate of profit falls. It is that this fall is simultaneously a rise. The same development of the productive forces that lowers the ratio of surplus-value to total capital also swells the absolute mass of surplus-value — and hence of profit — appropriated by the total social capital. Marx's whole chapter is built around the task of showing that this is not a paradox, not a temporary coincidence, not a doctrinal embarrassment, but a necessary unity : \"This not only can but must be the case — discounting transient fluctuations — on the basis of capitalist production.\" The intellectual objective of the chapter is thus threefold. First, it aims to derive the law at the highest possible level of abstraction — in its \"pure\" form, before profit has been decomposed into industrial profit, commercial profit, interest, and ground-rent, and before the counteracting causes have been introduced. Marx is explicit and deliberate about this ordering: \"We are deliberately putting forward this law before depicting the decomposition of profit into various categories which have become mutually autonomous.\" The point is methodological: because the law can be stated while profit is still simply \"another name for surplus-value itself, only now depicted in relation to the total capital,\" its validity is shown to be independent of any particular distribution of surplus-value among rival claimants. Second, the chapter sets out to demonstrate the inner connection between the apparently contradictory phenomena of a falling rate and a rising mass — first as a matter of algebra (the proposition that the total capital must grow faster than the rate of profit falls if the mass of profit is to grow), then as a matter of political economy (the theory of accumulation, concentration, and the relative surplus population). Third, it seeks to explain the systematic misrecognition of the phenomenon, both in competition (where the law appears \"upside down\") and in the history of economic thought (where Marx claims no previous writer succeeded in discovering it). This third ambition gives the chapter its extraordinary historiographical reach. Marx does not present the falling rate of profit as one theorem among many. He presents it as the theorem: \"given the great importance that this law has for capitalist production, one might well say that it forms the mystery around whose solution the whole of political economy since Adam Smith revolves and that the difference between the various schools since Adam Smith consists in the different attempts made to solve it.\" The history of political economy, from this vantage, is the history of successive failures to conceptualize the law — failures rooted in the inability to distinguish constant from variable capital, to isolate surplus-value from profit, to separate profit in its pure form from its autonomized components, and to analyse differences in the organic composition of capital. Finally, the chapter must be understood as the hinge on which the remainder of Part Three and much of Volume 3 turns. It is immediately followed by Chapter 14, on the counteracting influences — the \"counterweights\" (rising exploitation, cheapening of constant capital, the fall in wages below value, relative over-population, foreign trade, the growth of the stock of capital) which prevent the fall from presenting itself \"in such an absolute form\"; and by Chapter 15, on the internal contradictions of the law, in which the falling rate of profit becomes the deep ground of over-accumulation, crisis, the centralization of capital, and the historical limits of the mode of production. In the strictest sense, then, Chapter 13 is a statement of tendency whose own text acknowledges, from the second page onward, that a full account requires the chapters that follow. It is a chapter that knows it is incomplete — and that deliberately draws its power from that incompleteness, since the abstract law must first be established in its purity before it can be shown to be everywhere \"suspended\" by counter-tendencies internal to the same system. The result is a chapter that is at once a piece of pure theory, a critique of political economy, and an anatomy of ideological illusion. Its thesis is that the progressive development of the social productivity of labour — the same development that produces ever cheaper commodities, ever greater masses of use-values, and ever larger absolute profits — necessarily expresses itself, on the capitalist basis, as a falling general rate of profit, and that this expression is the distinctive, historically specific form in which productivity growth registers within a system organized around the self-valorization of capital. The fall in the rate of profit is, in Marx's phrase, \"simply the expression, peculiar to the capitalist mode of production, of the progressive development of the social productivity of labour.\" That sentence is the chapter's thesis in a single line, and everything else is elaboration, proof, and consequence. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Founding Series: A Fixed Rate of Exploitation Yields a Falling Rate of Profit Marx begins not with history, not with statistics, and not with the observation of actual profit rates, but with a constructed series. The logic of the argument is deliberately dyadic. First, he stipulates the determinants of the exploitation relation: wages and the length of the working day are given. Under those conditions, a variable capital of £100 \"represents a definite number of workers set in motion; it is an index of this number.\" Concretely: £100 buys the wages of 100 workers for one week. The variable capital is thus, as Marx puts it elsewhere, an index of the mass of living labour absorbed — a proxy for the number of workers, given a determinate wage level. If each of these 100 workers performs as much surplus labour as necessary labour — that is, works half the day reproducing the value of their own wages and half the day producing value for the capitalist — then the total value product is £200: £100 of necessary labour reproduced as wages, and £100 of surplus labour objectified as surplus-value. The rate of surplus-value, s/v, is 100 per cent. Here is the pivot. That rate — 100 per cent — is a proportion between two quantities internal to the labour process (necessary vs. surplus labour, or variable capital vs. surplus-value). The rate of profit is a proportion between surplus-value and the whole capital advanced . Because the denominator of the latter includes constant capital, an unchanged numerator-to-v relation can be expressed in any number of different profit rates, depending on the magnitude of c. Marx spells out the series: if c = 50 and v = 100, then p′ = 100/(50+100) = 66⅔ per cent; if c = 100 and v = 100, then p′ = 100/200 = 50 per cent; if c = 200 and v = 100, then p′ = 100/300 = 33⅓ per cent; if c = 300 and v = 100, then p′ = 100/400 = 25 per cent; if c = 400 and v = 100, then p′ = 100/500 = 20 per cent. Three specialized terms must be held clearly in mind throughout, because the chapter's entire force depends on keeping them distinct. Constant capital (c) is the capital laid out on means of production — machinery, buildings, raw materials, ancillary materials, fuel — which does not create new value but transfers its own value (in the case of fixed capital, gradually, via depreciation) to the product. Variable capital (v) is capital laid out on labour-power, which alone produces new value — including a surplus beyond its own reproduction cost. Surplus-value (s) is that unpaid value. The rate of surplus-value is s/v, the degree of exploitation. The rate of profit is s/C, where C = c + v, the total capital advanced. The crucial conceptual move is thus a matter of denominators. The rate of surplus-value measures the exploitation of living labour from the standpoint of living labour alone. The rate of profit measures the same surplus from the standpoint of the totality of capital — as a return on every pound the capitalist has had to lay out, living or dead. Because the dead portion (c) grows disproportionately as technique develops, the same exploitation yields a shrinking ratio. This is the formal core of the law, and Marx states its conclusion without hesitation: the same rate of surplus-value and an unchanged level of exploitation \"is expressed in a falling rate of profit.\" From the Individual Capital to the Social Capital: The Generalization and Its Premises Marx is immediately aware that the series just given establishes a relationship within a single capital. The law, however, is stated of the general rate of profit — the social average. He therefore supplies the requisite premises with considerable care. Two conditional steps are needed. First, the change in composition must be general: \"If we further assume now that this gradual change in the composition of capital does not just characterize certain individual spheres of production, but occurs in more or less all spheres, or at least the decisive ones...” This qualification matters, because the transformation of individual profit rates into a general rate of profit, established in Part Two, proceeds through competition and the equalization of returns on capitals of different compositions. If only an isolated branch experienced a rising c:v, its profit rate would fall relative to the average, capital would flow out, and the general rate would be affected only marginally. What drives the law is a change in the average organic composition of the total capital belonging to a given society — a social fact, not a sectoral one. Second, the rate of surplus-value must be held constant (or, as later sections show, permitted to rise without fully offsetting). Given both premises, Marx's conclusion follows: \"this gradual growth in the constant capital, in relation to the variable, must necessarily result in a gradual fall in the general rate of profit.\" The word \"gradual\" is not decorative. Marx is describing a secular, developmental process — a trajectory — not a step-change. And the premise itself is not an ad hoc assumption. Marx asserts that it is \"a law of the capitalist mode of production that its development does in fact involve a relative decline in the relation of variable capital to constant, and hence also to the total capital set in motion.\" The footnote anchors this in Volume 1, Chapter 25, §2 — the section on \"The Progressive Production of a Relative Surplus Population or Industrial Reserve Army,\" where Marx established that accumulation on a rising technical basis systematically expels living labour from production relative to the mass of means of production it sets in motion. The conclusion of this passage is a social-physiological statement, not merely an arithmetical one. The same number of workers — or the same quantity of labour-power purchased by a given v — \"sets in motion, works up, and productively consumes, within the same period, an ever-growing mass of means of labour, machinery and fixed capital of all kinds, and raw and ancillary materials.\" The declining ratio v/C is therefore the quantitative signature of a qualitative transformation in the labour process itself. The Rising Organic Composition as the Expression of Rising Social Productivity Here Marx performs the decisive conceptual identification on which the entire chapter, and much of his historical materialist project, depends. He writes: \"This progressive decline in the variable capital in relation to the constant capital, and hence in relation to the total capital as well, is identical with the progressively rising organic composition, on average, of the social capital as a whole. It is just another expression for the progressive development of the social productivity of labour.\" The term organic composition of capital requires a careful gloss, because Marx uses it with precision and its meaning is often blurred in secondary literature. The composition of capital can be considered in two registers: the technical composition , which is the physical ratio of the mass of means of production to the mass of labour-power employed; and the value composition , which is the ratio in which the capital is divided into c and v as values . The organic composition is the value composition in so far as it is determined by the technical composition and reflects changes in it . It is thus neither a mere physical ratio nor a merely relative price ratio: it is the value-form whose movement is governed by the material-technical transformation of production. In this chapter, the rising organic composition is simply the rising c:v, understood as the expression of technological progress. The identification Marx makes is that c:v rising, organic composition rising, and social productivity rising are three descriptions of one and the same process . He demonstrates this by describing the productivity increase materially: \"the growing use of machinery and fixed capital generally enables more raw and ancillary materials to be transformed into products in the same time by the same number of workers, i.e. with less labour.\" Each worker now operates a larger and more elaborate apparatus, and processes more material, in the same period. More use-values emerge per unit of labour time. A crucial consequence follows immediately: the cheapening of the product . \"There corresponds to this growing volume of constant capital... a continual cheapening of the product. Each individual product, taken by itself, contains a smaller sum of labour than at a lower stage of development of production, where the capital laid out on labour stands in a far higher ratio to that laid out on means of production.\" This is a point that will be developed at length in the closing pages of the chapter, and it is essential to the whole argument: the fall in the profit rate is not a story about prices rising, but about the socially necessary labour contained in each commodity shrinking . The profit rate falls because the coefficient by which living labour is multiplied into value is diluted by an ever-growing mass of previously objectified labour — an idea that will receive its most brilliant statement in the \"series\" of the individual commodity later in the chapter. Marx then performs the legitimating move: \"The hypothetical series we constructed at the opening of this chapter therefore expresses the actual tendency of capitalist production.\" The abstraction is warranted — the Gedankenexperiment was not arbitrary but a controlled representation of a real historical trajectory. \"Self-Evident Necessity\" and the Status of the Law as Tendency Marx now sharpens the ontological status of the conclusion. The progressive tendency is \"the expression, peculiar to the capitalist mode of production, of the progressive development of the social productivity of labour.\" And he goes further: \"This does not mean that the rate of profit may not fall temporarily for other reasons as well, but it does prove that it is a self-evident necessity, deriving from the nature of the capitalist mode of production itself, that as it advances the general average rate of surplus-value must be expressed in a falling general rate of profit.\" Two things merit attention. First, Marx concedes that other causes may produce temporary declines in the profit rate — crises, harvest failures, price shocks, wars, speculative bubbles. This is important because it means the law is not an empirical prediction of a monotone decline in observed profit rates. It is a structural claim about the direction of the regulating tendency. Second, the phrase \"self-evident necessity\" ( selbstverständliche Notwendigkeit ) signals that Marx regards the result as analytic given the premises — an entailment of the definition of the rate of profit combined with the demonstrated tendency of capitalist technique. It is not an inductive generalization but a deduction from the nature of the mode of production. Note also the subtle wording: \"the general average rate of surplus-value must be expressed in a falling general rate of profit .\" Marx is not claiming that the rate of surplus-value falls. He is claiming that the rate of surplus-value — even a stable or rising one — must express itself, through the mediation of a changing composition of capital, as a falling rate of profit. The distinction is what makes the law counterintuitive and what makes its discovery a conceptual achievement rather than a truism. The paragraph closes with the density-of-labour argument in its most compressed form: \"Since the mass of living labour applied continuously declines in relation to the mass of objectified labour that it sets in motion, i.e. the productively consumed means of production, the part of this living labour that is unpaid and objectified in surplus-value must also stand in an ever-decreasing ratio to the value of the total capital applied. But this ratio between the mass of surplus-value and the total capital applied in fact constitutes the rate of profit, which must therefore steadily fall.\" The Historiographical Claim: Political Economy's Central Mystery Having established the law, Marx pivots to the history of thought. \"Simple as the law appears from the above arguments, not one of the previous writers on economics succeeded in discovering it, as we shall see later on.\" A footnote points to Theories of Surplus-Value , Part II, pp. 438–69 and 542–6 — Marx's historical manuscript on Smith, Ricardo, and their successors. The claim is escalated dramatically: the law \"forms the mystery around whose solution the whole of political economy since Adam Smith revolves,\" and the differences among schools consist in \"the different attempts made to solve it.\" This is a very strong historiographical thesis. Marx is asserting that the entire intellectual history of classical and post-classical economics can be re-described as a series of failed attempts to come to grips with a single phenomenon that was, in fact, perpetually perceived — \"these economists perceived the phenomenon, but tortured themselves with their contradictory attempts to explain it.\" Marx then explains why the failure was structurally inevitable for a political economy operating with bourgeois categories. He lists four conceptual deficiencies: previous economics \"fumbled around with the distinction between constant and variable capital, but has never managed to formulate this in any definite way\"; it \"never presented surplus-value as something separate from profit, nor profit in general, in its pure form, as distinct from the various constituents of profit which have attained an autonomous position towards each other (such as industrial profit, commercial profit, interest, ground-rent)\"; it \"never analysed the differences in the organic composition of capital\"; and consequently it \"has not analysed the formation of the general rate of profit either.\" The list is a virtually complete inventory of the theoretical innovations of Volume 3 itself. The failure to discover the law is thus not attributed to insufficient data or insufficient intelligence, but to category error — to the absence of the conceptual distinctions necessary to pose the problem correctly. Methodological Priority: Why the Law Precedes the Splitting of Profit Marx is a self-conscious methodologist, and he pauses here to justify the order of exposition. He is presenting the law before profit has been decomposed into the revenue-forms of industrial profit, commercial profit, interest, and ground-rent. Why does this matter? Because the law's validity must be shown to be independent of that decomposition. \"The independence of this presentation from the division of profit into various portions, which accrue to different categories of persons, shows from the start how the law in its generality is independent of that division and of the mutual relationships of the categories of profit deriving from it.\" If the fall in the profit rate were dependent on, say, a shifting balance between interest and industrial profit, then it would be a law about the distribution of surplus-value among the exploiting classes, not about the production of surplus-value in relation to capital as such. By presenting the law first, Marx immunizes it against the charge that it presupposes a particular distributional arrangement. The conceptual identification here is emphatic: \"Profit, as we speak of it here, is simply another name for surplus-value itself, only now depicted in relation to the total capital, instead of to the variable capital from which it derives.\" This sentences renders the law's logic transparent: the fall in the rate of profit is the fall in the ratio between surplus-value and the total capital advanced. It has nothing to do with who receives what. It concerns the ratio between the pool of newly created surplus and the magnitude of the capital that had to be advanced to produce it — a ratio determined entirely by the composition of capital and the rate of exploitation. This passage also carries strategic weight. Rival theories could, and later did, treat the falling rate as an artefact of competition, monopoly, institutional conflict, or distributional struggle. Marx's architecture blocks these readings in advance by showing that the tendency is exhaustively implicit in the relation s/(c+v). The International Analogue: Different Stages Coexisting in Space Marx now extends the temporal argument into a spatial register: \"What applies to different successive stages of development in one country applies also to different countries that find themselves in differing stages of development at the same point in time.\" This is a classic move in his method — the conversion of a genetic sequence into a comparative typology. Countries, like epochs, can be arrayed along the axis of capital composition, and the profit rate should, on the abstract premises, vary inversely with development: the \"undeveloped country\" with c:v = 50:100 would show p′ = 66⅔ per cent; the developed country with c:v = 400:100 would show 20 per cent. But Marx immediately complicates the picture. The distinction \"could disappear, or even be reversed, if in the less developed country labour was less productive,\" so that the worker spends a greater portion of the day reproducing their own subsistence and leaves a smaller portion for surplus. He works out a numerical illustration. Suppose in the less developed country the worker works two-thirds of the day for himself and one-third for the capitalist. On the assumptions of the earlier example, the same labour-power would be paid 133⅓ and provide a surplus of only 66⅔. To the variable capital of 133⅓ \"there would correspond a constant capital of 50\" — that is, the same low c, but a much larger v, since low productivity means a large proportion of total value added goes to reproducing the worker. The rate of surplus-value becomes 66⅔ : 133⅓ = 50 per cent. And the rate of profit becomes 66⅔ / (50 + 133⅓) = 66⅔ / 183⅓ = 4/11 ≈ 36⅓ per cent. The point is precise: despite a lower rate of surplus-value (50 per cent versus 100 per cent), the underdeveloped country exhibits a higher rate of profit (36⅓ per cent versus 20 per cent) — because its very low constant capital compensates for its low exploitation rate. The argument demonstrates that the international comparison does not run in a single dimension. A high rate of exploitation can coexist with a low rate of profit, and vice versa. This is a serious analytical result, and it should be defended against the naive reading that \"developed countries have low profit rates because of the tendency\" — the cross-sectional pattern involves two variables moving at once. The Interest-Rate Fallacy and the Case of the Ryot: An Anticipated Caveat Marx now inserts an \"anticipated\" caveat — a foreshadowing of later chapters on interest-bearing capital and ground-rent — in order to block a likely misunderstanding. \"Since we have not investigated up till now the various components into which profit is divided, so that these do not exist for us as yet, the following point is anticipated here simply for the sake of avoiding any misunderstandings.\" The mistake he is preempting is the substitution of the rate of interest for the rate of profit in comparative national analysis. This is a real error, and it has a determinate structural source. In countries where capitalist production is developed, the rate of interest \"simply expresses an aliquot part of the surplus-value or profit produced\" — a fraction of the surplus, filtered through the credit system. But in countries where wage-labour is not yet formally subsumed under capital — Marx's example is India, where \"the ryot operates as an independent peasant farmer, and his production is not yet subsumed under capital\" — the money-lender's interest \"includes both the entire profit and more than the profit.\" It may extort not only the peasant's entire surplus labour but \"even — to put it in capitalist terms — a part of his wages.\" In other words, in such contexts, what appears as \"interest\" is not the price of loanable capital at all but a relation of usury and rent, whose magnitude is determined by the power of the money-lender over a rentier class , not by the productivity of capital. Marx supplies the footnote definition here: for the concepts of \"formal\" and \"real subsumption\" he refers the reader to \"Results of the Immediate Process of Production,\" published as an appendix to the Pelican edition of Capital Volume 1. Formal subsumption is the relation in which wage-labour is incorporated into a capitalist labour process as it previously existed — the capitalist takes over an existing craft or agrarian technique and simply extracts surplus through extended hours and supervision. Real subsumption is the relation in which production is transformed by capital itself — machinery, large-scale cooperation, factory discipline, scientific technique — so that the labour process becomes specifically capitalist in its material form. This distinction is directly relevant: the fall in the rate of profit presupposes real subsumption, because it presupposes a systematic transformation of technique, not merely a change in the legal form of exploitation. And countries where only formal subsumption obtains are precisely those where the borrowed category \"interest\" is most misleading. The further caveat is that in such countries \"the rate of interest is predominantly determined by factors such as the level of advances by money-lenders to the big landowners who are the recipients of ground-rent, which have nothing at all to do with profit but rather express the extent to which the money-lender himself appropriates this ground-rent.\" The rate charged is thus a function of the rentier income stream and the moneylender's ability to intercept it — not of capitalist profitability. To measure the \"national rate of profit\" by the \"national rate of interest\" in such a context is therefore doubly erroneous: the category is wrong, and the object being measured is not profit at all. Intensity, Length of the Working Day, and the Limits of Cross-Country Comparison Marx continues the international analysis by introducing a second-order complication that cuts in the opposite direction from the underdevelopment case. Between countries of differing organic composition, the rate of surplus-value — one of the two determinants of the profit rate — may be higher in the country with the shorter normal working day. He gives two reasons. First, intensity . If the English working day of 10 hours equals an Austrian working day of 14 hours \"on account of its higher intensity,\" then, given the same proportional division between necessary and surplus labour, five hours of surplus labour in England may represent \"a higher value on the world market\" than seven hours in Austria. The point is that the hours of the working day are not homogeneous units of social labour: an hour of labour of higher intensity produces more value in the same clock time, because intensity is itself a mode of increasing the quantity of labour embodied per unit time (the point developed at length in Volume 1, Chapter 10, on the working day, and Chapter 12 on relative surplus-value). Marx's formulation anticipates the modern concept of differential productivity and the labour-theoretic treatment of intensive magnitude. Second, the division of the day . \"A greater part of the working day in England may form surplus labour than in Austria\" — that is, the same length of day can be divided differently, depending on the value of labour-power (wages), which in turn depends on the productivity of the branches producing subsistence goods. A more productive economy can cheapen the means of subsistence, thereby shortening necessary labour time and extending surplus labour time, even with a shorter working day. The analytic structure here is subtle. The rate of profit depends on two fundamental magnitudes: the organic composition and the rate of surplus-value . Advanced countries tend to have higher composition (which depresses the profit rate) and higher rates of surplus-value via intensity and cheapened wage-goods (which raises it). The net cross-sectional outcome is therefore underdetermined, and depends on the relative strengths of the two forces. Marx is here laying the groundwork for the theory of unequal development and for the subsequent treatment of foreign trade in Chapter 14, where trade is identified as a counteracting influence that can raise the profit rate in advanced countries at the expense of less advanced ones. The Compact Restatement and the 80c + 20v / 20c + 80v Contrast Marx now restates the law in its most intuitive, \"physical\" form: \"taking any particular quantity of average social capital, e.g. a capital of 100, an ever greater portion of this is represented by means of labour and an ever lesser portion by living labour.\" Note the phrase \"represented by\" — the composition is a value composition, but it is a representation of the physical ratio of means of production to labourers. Because the mass of living labour added falls relative to the value of the means of production, so does the unpaid labour, and the portion of value in which it is represented, relative to the total capital advanced. Marx offers an alternative formulation: \"an ever smaller aliquot part of the total capital laid out is converted into living labour, and hence the total capital absorbs ever less surplus labour in relation to its size, even though the ratio between the unpaid and paid parts of the labour applied may at the same time be growing.\" The concession in the last clause is crucial. Even as the capital absorbs less surplus labour relative to its size , the proportion of unpaid to paid labour within the laboured mass may be growing . Both movements are real. Their interaction is the \"double-edged\" nature of the law, developed in the following pages. Two concrete illustrations follow. First, developed capital of 100 = 80c + 20v, where v represents 20 workers. Rate of surplus-value 100 per cent, workers work half the day for themselves. Second, less-developed capital of 100 = 20c + 80v, where v represents 80 workers. These 80 workers, however, need two-thirds of the working day for themselves, working only one-third for the capitalist — a rate of surplus-value of 50 per cent. Taking everything else equal, \"the workers in the first case produce a value of 40, in the second case a value of 120.\" The value added by the labour in the second case is £120 because 80 workers are employed (at £80 necessary labour value, plus £40 surplus). In the first case, only 20 workers are employed, producing £20 necessary plus £20 surplus. Hence: - First capital: 80c + 20v + 20s = 120, rate of profit 20 per cent; - Second capital: 20c + 80v + 40s = 140, rate of profit 40 per cent. The rate of profit is twice as large in the second case — even though the rate of surplus-value there is half as large (50 per cent versus 100 per cent). Why? \"Because a capital of the same size appropriates in the first case the surplus labour of only 20 workers, as against that of 80 workers in the second case.\" The determining variable, given a fixed capital of 100, is how many workers it can buy; and since the value composition is inverse to the physical composition, the low-composition capital commands four times the labour. This is a devastating illustration for naive readings of the law. It shows that a country or sector with a lower rate of exploitation may have a higher profit rate, and that the profit rate is not the same as the exploitation rate. It also clarifies what kind of variable determines the profit rate: the mass of living labour employed per unit of capital , which is inversely related to the composition. Relative, Not Absolute: Why the Decline Does Not Imply Fewer Workers Marx is careful to block a common misreading. The law \"of a progressive fall in the rate of profit, or the relative decline in the surplus labour appropriated in comparison with the mass of objectified labour that the living labour sets in motion, in no way prevents the absolute mass of labour set in motion and exploited by the social capital from growing.\" The fall is relative , not absolute. Even if the number of workers exploited never increases, the mass of surplus-value could stay the same — and since capital continues to accumulate, the number will normally increase. Consider a fixed working population — say 2 million — with the length and intensity of the working day given, and wages given. Then the total labour of these 2 million workers always produces the same magnitude of value, and the same surplus-value. But \"as the mass of constant (fixed and circulating) capital set in motion by this labour grows, so there is a fall in the ratio between this magnitude and the value of the constant capital.\" The ratio falls, and with it the profit rate, \"even though capital still commands the same mass of living labour as before and absorbs the same mass of surplus labour.\" The explanatory point is precise and should be underlined: \"If the ratio changes, this is not because the mass of living labour falls but rather because the mass of already objectified labour that it sets in motion rises.\" This distinction separates Marx's law from any version of the argument that says \"capitalists exploit fewer workers, hence profit falls.\" The workers may be exactly as numerous as before — indeed, they may be increasing. The rate falls because the denominator grows. This is a purely structural movement. Two Million Workers, Three Million Workers: The Doubly-Moving Magnitudes Marx now admits both movements simultaneously. If the working population rises from 2 to 3 million, and variable capital rises correspondingly from 2 to 3 million, while constant capital rises from 4 to 15 million, then — with working day and rate of surplus-value constant — the mass of surplus labour and surplus-value still rises by 50 per cent, from 2 to 3 million. The two cases: - I. 4c + 2v + 2s; C = 6, p′ = 2/6 = 33⅓ per cent; - II. 15c + 3v + 3s; C = 18, p′ = 3/18 = 16⅔ per cent. The mass of surplus-value has risen by half, while the profit rate has fallen by half (33⅓ → 16⅔). This is the chapter's first fully explicit statement of the \"double-edged law.\" Marx then draws out the crucial identification: \"profit is nothing more than the surplus-value reckoned in terms of the social capital, and the mass of profit, therefore, its absolute magnitude, is the same as the absolute magnitude of surplus-value, considering it on a social scale.\" Thematic identity: mass of profit = mass of surplus-value at the level of social capital. The absolute mass of profit grows by 50 per cent despite \"the enormous decline in the ratio between this mass of profit and the total capital advanced.\" The conclusion is sweeping and is stated with the hallmark of a law: \"The number of workers employed by capital, i.e. the absolute mass of labour it sets in motion, and hence the absolute mass of surplus labour it absorbs, the mass of surplus-value it produces, and the absolute mass of profit it produces, can therefore grow, and progressively so, despite the progressive fall in the rate of profit. This not only can but must be the case — discounting transient fluctuations — on the basis of capitalist production.\" The modal shift from \"can\" to \"must\" is the chapter's central philosophical moment. The coexistence of a falling rate with a rising mass is not an accidental accommodation, nor an apologetic concession to optimism. It is structurally necessary , given accumulation. Accumulation, the Surplus Population, and \"Misery Produces Population\" Marx now argues from the nature of the capitalist production process as such. \"The capitalist production process is essentially, and at the same time, a process of accumulation.\" Several linked claims follow. First, as productivity rises, the mass of value that must simply be reproduced and maintained grows — \"even if the labour-power applied remains constant.\" The reproduction requirement itself expands with the scale of the means of production, because a larger and more technically elaborate stock of capital must be maintained in working order. Second, the use-value dimension: as productivity develops, \"the mass of use-values produced grows still more, and the means of production form a portion of these.\" Crucially, the additional labour required to convert this additional wealth into capital \"does not depend on the value of these means of production (including means of subsistence), since the worker is not concerned in the labour process with the value of the means of production but rather with their use-value.\" This is a technically important point. The transformation of produced wealth back into capital — into new means of production and new means of subsistence for workers — is a material operation. It requires labour that is applied to materials and machines as use-values, not labour measured against their values. This means that a growth in the physical scale of accumulation does not require a proportionately larger value of labour-power; the labour-power's task is to work materials, not to \"embody\" a value quantity. Third, accumulation and concentration are \"simply a material means for increasing productivity.\" Larger-scale production permits more advanced technique, more machinery, more scientific application. Fourth — and this is the transition into the theory of surplus population — the growth of the means of production \"entails a growth in the working population, the creation of a surplus population that corresponds to the surplus capital or even exceeds its overall requirements, thus leading to an over-population of workers.\" Note: the surplus population is entailed by the growth of capital. It is not a natural excess. It is the specifically capitalist form of over-population. Marx then describes the \"double effect\" of a momentary excess of surplus capital over the working population it commands. On the one hand, \"it will gradually increase the working population by raising wages, hence attenuating the destructive influences that decimate the offspring of the workers and making marriage easier.\" This is a fascinating passage — it is Marx's version of what a modern reader would call the demographic response to rising living standards: higher wages reduce infant and child mortality and facilitate family formation, so the labour supply grows through natural increase . On the other hand, \"by using methods that create relative surplus-value (introduction and improvement of machinery), it produces far more quickly an artificial and relative over-population, which in turn is the forcing house for a really rapid increase in the number of people — since, under capitalist production, misery produces population.\" The last phrase is a pregnant one. As a positive law of population under capitalism, Marx is asserting the opposite of Malthusian naturalism: population growth is produced by the specific conditions of capitalist accumulation — including the poverty and insecurity of the workforce — and not by some natural law of reproduction. And this is why the system can continue to have an abundant labour supply even as the relative demand for labour falls. The conclusion is explicit: \"It thus follows from the very nature of the capitalist accumulation process... that the increased mass of means of production designed to be turned into capital finds a correspondingly increased and even excessive working population available for exploitation.\" As accumulation advances, \"the mass of surplus labour that can be and is appropriated must grow, and with it too the absolute mass of profit appropriated by the social capital.\" But — and this is the crucial dialectical hinge — \"the same laws of production and accumulation mean that the value of the constant capital increases along with its mass, and progressively more quickly than that of the variable portion of capital which is converted into living labour.\" So the same laws produce both the growing absolute mass of profit and the falling rate of profit. They are not two results of two causes; they are two results of one cause. Concentration, Expropriation, and the Growing Armies of Workers Marx then notes, parenthetically, that he is abstracting from the fact that \"the same amount of value represents a progressively rising mass of use-values and satisfactions\" — i.e., that the value-based analysis understates the growth in the material wealth produced. This matters because it shows that he is being conservative, not inflationary, with respect to the growth of social wealth. He next turns to the scale conditions of capitalist development. Increasingly large labour processes require \"increasingly large dimensions and increasingly large advances of capital for each individual establishment.\" The result is the growing concentration of capitals — accompanied, note carefully, by \"a growing number of capitalists,\" though \"in lesser degree.\" Marx is here distinguishing concentration (the growth in size of individual capitals) from centralization (the merging of capitals, treated elsewhere, notably in Volume 1, Chapter 25, §2 and in Chapter 15 of this volume). Both are at work: capitals grow in size, and their number also grows, though slower. The growth in the number of capitalists is compatible with — indeed, initially a consequence of — accumulation's sweep. Hand in hand with concentration, \"in a relationship of reciprocity, goes progressive expropriation of the more or less immediate producers.\" The reference is to the long history of primitive accumulation — enclosures, dispossession of peasants, the separation of the producer from the means of production. That process is not merely a historical precondition but an ongoing feature of capitalist development. The consequence: individual capitalists command \"increasingly large armies of workers (no matter how much the variable capital may fall in relation to the constant capital),\" so that the mass of surplus-value and hence profit which they appropriate grows, \"along with and despite the fall in the rate of profit.\" Marx gives a crucial gloss: \"The reasons that concentrate massive armies of workers under the command of individual capitalists are precisely the same reasons as also swell the amount of fixed capital employed, as well as the raw and ancillary materials, in a growing proportion as compared with the mass of living labour applied.\" The very processes that allow individual capitalists to employ more labour in absolute terms also ensure that the proportion of living labour to dead labour falls. The law and its counter-tendencies spring from a single root. The Rising Rate of Surplus-Value as a Reinforcing Case Marx's next move is to note a reinforcing case. With a given working population, if the rate of surplus-value grows — whether by prolongation or intensification of the working day, or by reductions in the value of wages as a result of developing productivity — \"then the mass of surplus-value and hence the absolute mass of profit must also grow, despite the relative lessening of variable capital in relation to constant.\" In other words, the rise in exploitation makes the coexistence of a falling rate and a rising mass even more robust. But this does not overthrow the law; it merely raises the numerator while c raises the denominator. Nor does it \"offset\" the law in any systematic way: even if the exploitation rate rises, so long as it does not rise faster than the composition , the rate of profit necessarily falls. The \"Double-Edged Law\" Posed as a Problem Marx now consolidates the entire argument so far into a formal challenge. The results are: The same development of the productivity of social labour is expressed in the relative decline of variable capital as a proportion of the total capital. The accelerated accumulation that follows from this reacts back to become the starting point for a further development of productivity and a further relative decline in the variable capital. This same development expresses itself, \"leaving aside temporary fluctuations,\" in the progressive increase in the total labour-power applied, and in the progressive growth in the absolute mass of surplus-value and therefore profit. The question Marx then asks is rhetorical and architectonic: \"How, then, should we present this double-edged law of a decline in the profit rate coupled with a simultaneous increase in the absolute mass of profit, arising from the same reasons?\" And he specifies the constraint: this law is based on the fact that, under the given conditions, the mass of surplus labour and hence surplus-value that is appropriated grows , and that, viewing the total capital as a whole, or the individual capital as simply a piece of the total capital, profit and surplus-value are identical quantities. The formal problem is this: if you measure the profit rate against a fixed capital of 100, the mass of profit on that 100 also falls, in absolute terms. So within the frame of a fixed-magnitude benchmark, the fall in the rate is a fall in the mass. How can there simultaneously be a rise in the mass of profit \"on a social scale\"? The apparent contradiction must be resolved by seeing that the benchmark and the whole move differently. The Aliquot-Part Argument: Relative Fall as Absolute Fall — and the Tautology Marx takes an aliquot part of the capital — say 100 — as the basis for reckoning the profit rate. This 100 represents the average composition of the total capital, e.g., 80c + 20v. Recall from Part Two that the average rate of profit is determined not by any one particular composition of capital but by its average social composition. The 100 is thus the \"representative capital\"; the profit rate on it is the general rate. Now the crucial analytical move. As the variable portion declines relative to the constant, \"the profit rate falls if the level of exploitation of labour remains constant, or even if it rises; hence the relative magnitude of surplus-value falls, i.e. its relationship to the value of the total capital of 100 that is advanced.\" So far, the argument is relative. But Marx immediately sharpens it: \"But it is not only this relative magnitude that falls. The amount of surplus-value or profit absorbed by the total capital of 100 also falls in absolute terms.\" He gives three cases, at a rate of surplus-value of 100 per cent: - 60c + 40v produces a mass of surplus-value and profit of 40 (p′ = 40%); - 70c + 30v produces a mass of profit of 30 (p′ = 30%); - 80c + 20v produces a mass of profit of 20 (p′ = 20%). A reader must dwell on this. On a capital of 100, if the composition is 60:40, the profit is 40; if 70:30, the profit is 30; if 80:20, the profit is 20. The mass of profit itself falls — not just the ratio. Why? \"Whatever aliquot part of the social capital we take as the standard for measuring surplus-value, i.e. whatever part of the capital of average social composition — and this is the case with any calculation of profit — a relative fall in surplus-value is always identical with an absolute fall.\" Marx then states the tautological structure explicitly. \"Since the size of the capital against which we measure the surplus-value is given as 100, a fall in the ratio of surplus-value to this magnitude, which itself remains constant, can only be another expression for the decline in the absolute magnitude of surplus-value and profit. This is in fact a tautology.\" The point: because the benchmarking capital is fixed at 100, any fall in the rate is by construction a fall in the mass on that benchmark . There is nothing paradoxical about it — it is definitional. \"But the reason for this decline, as has been shown, lies in the nature of development of the capitalist process of production.\" The tautology is about the form of measurement ; the substantive content is the falling mass of living labour absorbed per unit of capital. The Resolution: The Total Capital Must Grow Faster Than the Rate Falls Now the actual resolution of the apparent paradox. \"On the other hand, however, the same reasons that produce an absolute decline in surplus-value and hence profit on a given capital, thus also in the rate of profit as reckoned as a percentage, bring about a growth in the absolute mass of the surplus-value and profit appropriated by the social capital (i.e. by the totality of capitalists). How are we to explain this, what is it dependent on, or what conditions are involved in this apparent contradiction?\" The answer turns on the variability of the total capital . \"If any aliquot part of the social capital, say 100, and hence any capital of 100 of average social composition, is a given magnitude, so that as far as it is concerned the decline in the rate of profit coincides with a decline in the absolute amount of profit — precisely because the capital on which this is measured is a constant magnitude, then the magnitude of the total social capital, on the other hand, just like that of the capital to be found in the hands of any individual capitalist, is a variable magnitude, and it must vary in inverse proportion to the decline in its variable portion if it is to fulfil the conditions we have presupposed.\" In other words: the unit of measurement is fixed at 100, but the number of such units into which the social capital is divisible changes over time . The apparent contradiction resolves once we realize that the falling rate and the rising mass are measured against different objects of comparison — one against the fixed unit, the other against the growing aggregate. The numerical example: at composition 60c + 40v, the profit on 100 is 40, so the rate is 40 per cent. Suppose at this composition the total capital was 1,000,000 — then the total surplus-value and profit is 400,000. Now let composition become 80c + 20v, so that the profit on each 100 is 20, with exploitation unchanged. Nonetheless, \"the surplus-value or profit grows in its absolute mass... despite this decline in the rate of profit or the decline in the production of surplus-value by each capital of 100, and this growth might be from 400,000 to 440,000.\" What does this require? The new total capital must be such that 20 per cent of it equals 440,000. That is 2,200,000. The text prints \"2,220,000,\" but the surrounding glosses — that this represents a rise \"to 220 per cent of its initial value,\" and that the new composition yields 20 per cent on each 100 — imply that the correct figure is 2,200,000. The discrepancy appears to be a transposition in the printed text (or a slip in Marx's manuscript); the internal logic points unambiguously to 2,200,000. Either way, the thrust is unchanged: the capital has grown by 120 per cent, while the rate of profit has fallen by 50 per cent. Marx then contrasts this with the case where the composition did not rise: \"Had it grown by less than this, it would have produced less surplus-value or profit than the capital of 1,000,000 did previously, although at its earlier composition this would only have had to grow from 1,000,000 to 1,100,000 in order for its surplus-value to rise from 400,000 to 440,000.\" Check: at 40 per cent, 1,100,000 × 0.40 = 440,000. So at the old composition, a mere 10 per cent increase in capital would have raised profit by 10 per cent; at the new composition, achieving the same 440,000 requires a 120 per cent increase. The rising composition multiplies the capital required for any given absolute profit. The result: \"Here we can see asserting itself the law we developed earlier, according to which the relative decline in the variable capital, and thus the development of the social productivity of labour, means that an ever greater amount of total capital is required in order to set the same quantity of labour-power in motion and to absorb the same amount of surplus labour.\" This \"law of capital requirement\" is the crucial corollary of the falling rate of profit, and it produces a significant structural consequence: \"In the same proportion as capitalist production develops, therefore, there also develops the possibility of a relative surplus working population, not because the productivity of social labour declines but rather because it increases, i.e. not from an absolute disproportion between labour and means of subsistence, or the means of producing these means of subsistence, but rather from a disproportion arising from the capitalist exploitation of labour, the disproportion between the progressive growth of capital and the relative decline in its need for a growing population.\" The surplus population is generated by rising productivity, not by scarcity. Its source is the disproportion between the growth of capital and the diminishing relative need for labour that capital's own development creates. The Arithmetic of Compensation: From 40 Per Cent to 20 and to 8 Marx then formalizes the general rule. \"A fall of 50 per cent in the rate of profit is a fall of a half.\" If the mass of profit is to remain the same, the capital must double. \"In general, if the mass of profit is to remain the same with a declining rate of profit, the multiplier that indicates the growth in the total capital must be the same as the divisor that indicates the fall in the profit rate.\" If the rate falls from 40 per cent to 20 per cent, the total capital must rise in the ratio 20:40 (i.e., doubled) for the profit to stay constant. If the profit rate fell from 40 per cent to 8 per cent, the capital would have to grow in the ratio 8:40, i.e., five times. A capital of 1,000,000 at 40 per cent produces 400,000; a capital of 5,000,000 at 8 per cent also produces 400,000. \"This is necessary if the resultant is to remain the same.\" But the rule is only the condition for keeping profit constant. \"If it is to grow, on the other hand, the capital must grow in a higher ratio than that in which the profit rate falls.\" This is the pivotal implication for the theory of accumulation. To have an absolutely larger mass of profit while the rate falls, capital must grow more rapidly than the rate falls — i.e., the percentage growth in total capital must exceed the percentage fall in the rate. And still further: \"In other words, if the variable component of the total capital is not just to remain the same in absolute terms, but rather to grow, even though its percentage falls as a proportion of the total capital, then the total capital must grow in a higher ratio than that at which the percentage of variable capital falls. It must grow so much that in its new composition it requires not only the former amount of variable capital, but still more than this, for the purchase of labour-power.\" So to employ an absolutely larger mass of workers while the v/C ratio falls, the total capital must grow even faster. \"If the variable part of a capital of 100 falls from 40 to 20, the total capital must rise to more than 200 if it is to deploy a variable capital of more than 40.\" Check: at composition 20v/100, to have v = 40 the total capital must be 200; to exceed 40, the total must exceed 200. Even if the exploited mass of working population remains constant and only the length and intensity of the working day increase, \"the mass of capital applied must still rise,\" because the rising composition means more capital is required to employ the same labour-power, whatever the level of exploitation. The Law of Capital Requirement, and the \"Permanent Apparent Surplus Population\" Marx consolidates the argument in one of the chapter's most consequential formulations: \"Thus the same development in the social productivity of labour is expressed, with the advance of the capitalist mode of production, on the one hand in a progressive tendency for the rate of profit to fall and on the other in a constant growth in the absolute mass of the surplus-value or profit appropriated; so that, by and large, the relative decline in the variable capital and profit goes together with an absolute increase in both. This two-fold effect, as explained, can be expressed only in a growth in the total capital that takes place more rapidly than the fall in the rate of profit.\" Note: \"a growth in the total capital that takes place more rapidly than the fall in the rate of profit.\" This is a necessary condition for the two movements to coexist. If the total capital grew more slowly than the rate fell, the mass of profit would shrink — which would mean a full-blown crisis of accumulation. The chapter thus implicitly identifies the precarious condition for stable accumulation: capital must grow faster than its own rate of return declines. Marx then translates this into a general rule about the scale of capital required: \"In order to apply an absolutely greater variable capital at a higher composition, or with a relatively steeper increase in the constant capital, the total capital must grow not only in the same proportion as this higher composition, but still faster than it.\" He concludes: \"It follows from this that the more the capitalist mode of production is developed, the more an ever greater amount of capital is needed to employ the same amount of labour-power (and this is still more the case if the amount of labour-power is growing). The rising productivity of labour thus necessarily gives rise, on the capitalist basis, to a permanent apparent surplus working population.\" This phrase — \"permanent apparent surplus working population\" — fuses the theory of accumulation with the theory of the industrial reserve army. The reserve army is not a temporary market phenomenon; it is a structural property of the mode of production, produced by rising productivity, and it grows, so to speak, with the system. The word \"apparent\" ( scheinbare ) does not qualify away the surplus population; it signals that the surplus is a capitalist form — the population is not surplus \"in itself\" (there is always work to be done in the sense of potential needs to be met) but is surplus relative to capital's capacity to profitably employ it. The final concrete illustration: \"If the variable capital forms only a sixth of the total capital instead of a half, as formerly, then in order to employ the same amount of labour-power, the total capital must be tripled; but if it is to employ double the labour-power, this capital must be increased six-fold.\" Check: at v/C = 1/2, C = 2v. At v/C = 1/6, C = 6v. To deploy the same v, total capital must go from 2v to 6v — tripled. To deploy 2v, C = 12v, requiring a six-fold increase of the original 2v. Ricardo's Consolation and the \"Deliberate Miscalculation\" Marx here turns to the critics. \"Previous economists, not knowing how to explain the law of the falling rate of profit, invoked the rising mass of profit, the growth in its absolute amount, whether for the individual capitalist or for the social capital as a whole, as a kind of consolation, but this was also based on mere commonplaces and imagined possibilities.\" The accusation is precise: the classical economists' answer to the falling rate is to point to the rising mass — \"consolation.\" Marx's reply is that this is a tautology. \"It is no more than a tautology to say that the mass of profit is determined by two factors, firstly by the rate of profit and secondly by the mass of capital applied at this rate.\" The point is that the classical formulation adds nothing to the phenomenon; it merely restates the definition of the mass of profit. \"The fact that the mass of profit may possibly grow, therefore, despite a simultaneous fall in the rate of profit, is only an expression of this tautology and does not get us a single step further, since it is equally possible for the capital to grow without the mass of profit growing, and, indeed, the capital might even grow while the mass of profit falls. 25 per cent on 100 gives 25, 5 per cent on 400 gives only 20.\" This is the crucial quantitative correction. The consolation argument treats the mass of profit as automatically rising when capital grows. But this is not so: if the rate falls faster than the capital grows, the mass falls. Marx's target here is the classical assumption that accumulation always raises the absolute profit. His own position is the more analytic one: accumulation raises the mass only under the condition stated (capital growing faster than the rate falls). Footnote 35 provides the Ricardian illustration. Ricardo writes: \"We should also expect that, however the rate of the profits of stock might diminish in consequence of the accumulation of capital on the land and the rise of wages, yet the aggregate amount of profits would increase.\" He gives a numerical example: with repeated accumulations of £100,000, and the rate of profit falling 20, 19, 18, 17 per cent, the aggregate would be \"always progressive\" — greater at £200,000 than at £100,000; still greater at £300,000, and so on, \"increasing, though at a diminishing rate, with every increase of capital.\" But Ricardo then concedes: \"This progression, however, is only true for a certain time... but after capital has accumulated to a large amount, and profits have fallen, the further accumulation diminishes the aggregate of profits.\" His example: at £1,000,000 and 7 per cent, aggregate profit is £70,000; add £100,000 and the rate falls to 6 per cent, giving £66,000 — a decline of £4,000, even though the stock has risen to £1,100,000. Marx's editorial comment in the footnote is pitiless: \"In point of fact, what is assumed here is that the capital grows from 1,000,000 to 1,100,000, i.e. by 10 per cent, while the rate of profit falls from 7 per cent to 6 per cent, i.e. by 14⅔ per cent. Hinc illae lacrimae! \" — \"Hence those tears!\" (Terence, The Maid of Andros , Act 1, Scene 1). The comment identifies the exact condition: capital grew by 10 per cent, but the profit rate fell by ~14.3 per cent — faster than capital grew — so the mass fell. The \"tears\" are the lament of capital over its own self-undermining dynamic. Marx's own analysis, in contrast, has explained the condition: the mass of profit grows only if the capital grows faster than the rate falls — a condition that the development of the organic composition makes progressively harder to satisfy. Marx then adds: \"But if the same reasons that make the profit rate fall also promote accumulation, i.e. the formation of additional capital, and if all additional capital also sets additional labour in motion and produces additional surplus-value; if on the other hand the very fact of the fall in the rate of profit means that the constant capital and with it the total amount of the former capital has grown, then the entire process ceases to be a mystery.\" The apparent paradox is fully resolved by the identity of cause and effect: the falling rate and the growing mass are both consequences of rising productivity operating through accumulation. And he adds the ominous forecast: \"We shall see later on how resort was made to deliberate miscalculation, in an attempt to swindle away the possibility of an increase in the mass of profit together with a decline in the profit rate.\" A footnote refers to Theories of Surplus-Value , Part II, pp. 438–66 and 542–6. This is a preview of Marx's exposure of apologetic manipulations in the history of economic thought. The Law Inverted in Competition: Cheapening, Undercutting, and the Big Capitalist Marx now shifts register entirely, from theory to the phenomenology of competition. \"We have seen how it is that the same reasons that produce a tendential fall in the general rate of profit also bring about an accelerated accumulation of capital and hence a growth in the absolute magnitude or total mass of the surplus labour (surplus-value, profit) appropriated by it. Just as everything is expressed upside down in competition, and hence in the consciousness of its agents, so too is this law — I mean this inner and necessary connection between two apparently contradictory phenomena.\" The concept of inverted appearance in competition is central to Marx's critique of political economy and to his theory of ideology. In the actual competitive struggle, the structural connection between the falling rate and the rising mass does not present itself as such. Instead, agents perceive the fall in the profit rate as the result of their own strategic choices — price cuts, expansion, efficiency measures. Marx's project is to show that these strategic choices are themselves the form of appearance of the underlying law. \"It is evident that, on the figures given above, a capitalist controlling a large capital will make more profit in absolute terms than a smaller capitalist making apparently high profits.\" This is where the law becomes strategically visible: bigger capital commands more surplus in absolute terms despite a lower rate. \"The most superficial examination of competition also shows that, under certain conditions, if the bigger capitalist wants to make more room for himself on the market and expel the smaller capitalists, as in times of crisis, he makes practical use of this advantage and deliberately lowers his profit rate in order to drive the smaller ones from the field.\" This is a devastating empirical grounding: the falling rate, which appears as a curse, becomes in the hands of large capitals a weapon of competition . Commercial capital \"in particular, which we shall discuss in more detail later, also exhibits phenomena that allow the fall in profit to be seen as a result of the expansion of business and hence of the capital concerned.\" Marx reserves the fuller analysis for a later portion of Volume 3 (the chapters on merchant's capital, interest-bearing capital, and the division of profit on the basis of average profit). Similar superficial considerations arise \"from comparing the rates of profit that are made in particular branches of business, according to whether these are subject to the regime of free competition or to monopoly.\" Roscher, the \"More Clever and More Humane,\" and Profit Upon Alienation Marx then brings in his favourite exemplar of vulgar economy. \"The entire shallow conception that thrives in the heads of the agents of competition can be found in our Roscher, namely his assertion that this reduction in the rate of profit is 'more clever and more humane'.\" The footnote identifies Wilhelm Roscher (1817–94), a German vulgar economist and founder of the \"historical school,\" and cites his Die Grundlagen der Nationalökonomie (3rd edn, 1858, p. 192). The phrase \"more clever and more humane\" is a parody of the apologetic mode. For Roscher, the fall in the profit rate is a matter of capitalist calculation and benevolence: it is \"more clever\" to earn a lower rate on a larger mass, and \"more humane\" because — on this view — it is a concession to workers or consumers. Marx's sarcasm is aimed at the whole approach that treats a systemic tendency as an intentional choice . \"Here the decline in the rate of profit appears as a result of the increase of capital and the capitalists' consequent calculation that a lower rate of profit will enable them to tuck away a greater mass of profit.\" The error, for Marx, is conceptual: \"All this (with the exception of Adam Smith, on whom more later) is based on a complete misconception of what the general rate of profit actually is and on the crude idea that prices are determined by adding a more or less arbitrary quota of profit onto the commodity's actual value.\" This passage names the core vulgar conception: profit upon alienation . Prices are conceived as value plus a surcharge. But profit is not a surcharge; it is the form of surplus-value, determined by the relation between living labour and total capital. To treat it as a surcharge is to abolish the distinction between rate and mass and to substitute voluntary pricing for determinate ratios. Marx concludes the passage: \"Crude as these notions are, they are a necessary product of the upside-down way that the immanent laws of capitalist production present themselves within competition.\" This is a crucial theoretical move. The vulgar conception is not simply an intellectual failure; it is the necessary reflection in consciousness of the inverted way the laws actually appear. In this sense, Marx is offering a theory of the genesis of ideological error, not merely a refutation of it. The Commodity-Price Restatement and the Anatomy of the Individual Commodity Marx now restates the law in a new register: \"The law that the fall in the rate of profit occasioned by the development of productivity is accompanied by an increase in the mass of profit is also expressed in this way: the fall in the price of commodities produced by capital is accompanied by a relative rise in the amount of profit contained in them and realized by their sale.\" Note carefully: the price of the individual commodity falls, but the amount of profit contained in it rises relative to the value that goes to wages and materials. This is an aspect of the individual commodity in which the whole law is inscribed. The mechanism is laid out. As productivity rises and composition rises, \"each aliquot part of the total product, each individual commodity or each specific group of commodities absorbs less living labour and also contains less objectified labour, both in terms of the depreciation of the fixed capital applied and in terms of the raw and ancillary materials that are consumed.\" The commodity contains less living labour (because productivity has multiplied output per worker), and it also contains less objectified labour per unit (because the fixed capital's depreciation is spread over more units, and the raw material consumption per unit is reduced by efficiency gains). \"Each individual commodity therefore contains a smaller sum of labour objectified in means of production and labour newly added in the course of production. The price of the individual commodity therefore falls.\" Yet the profit on the individual commodity may still increase \"if the rate of absolute or relative surplus-value rises. It contains less newly added labour, but the unpaid portion of this labour grows in proportion to the paid part.\" This is the \"relative rise in the amount of profit contained\" in the sentence quoted above: the ratio s/v may rise even as the absolute mass of newly added value falls. But — and Marx immediately limits the concession — \"this is only true within certain definite limits. With the enormous decrease, in the course of the advance of production, of the absolute amount of living labour newly added to the individual commodity, the unpaid labour it contains also undergoes an absolute decline, no matter how much it may have grown in relation to the paid portion.\" In other words, once the absolute amount of newly added labour falls far enough, the proportional rise in the unpaid share cannot prevent the absolute unpaid value in the individual commodity from shrinking. \"The profit on each individual commodity becomes very much reduced as labour productivity develops, despite the rise in the rate of surplus-value; and this reduction, just like the fall in the rate of profit, is slowed down only by the cheapening of the elements of constant capital and the other circumstances adduced in Part One of this volume, which increase the rate of profit with a given or even falling rate of surplus-value.\" The reference to Part One (Chapters 5–7 of Volume 3 — the effect of the economy in the use of constant capital, price fluctuations of raw materials, and related factors) is important: even before the counteracting influences of Chapter 14, Part One has already established mechanisms that raise the profit rate, and these serve to \"slow down\" the fall. Marx then interprets the fall in individual commodity prices. \"If there is a fall in the price of the individual commodities whose sum makes up capital's total product, this means nothing more than that a given quantity of labour is realized in a greater mass of commodities, so that each individual commodity contains less labour than before. This is the case even if one part of the constant capital, e.g. raw material, rises in price.\" The last clause matters because it answers a potential objection: even a rise in raw material prices does not negate the general tendency, because the labour embodied per unit has fallen. Indeed, the rise in raw material prices is itself a way in which the composition of capital shifts, with the same result. The two reasons the rate of profit falls despite a higher rate of surplus-value are now given explicitly: \"because even a greater unpaid portion of the smaller total sum of newly added labour is less than a smaller aliquot unpaid portion of the greater total sum was\" — algebraic point: if total newly added labour falls from, say, 100 to 50, then even a 100 per cent unpaid share (50) is less than a 40 per cent unpaid share of 100 (40... hmm, actually if the unpaid share rises from 40 per cent of 100 to 100 per cent of 50, the unpaid portion goes from 40 to 50 — that's an increase. So Marx's example must involve a smaller fall in the rate of surplus-value — i.e., the unpaid portion of the smaller total is less than the unpaid portion of the greater total. The condition is: if the total newly added labour falls by more than the (reciprocal) increase in the rate of surplus-value, the absolute unpaid mass falls. That is, the fall in the mass outweighs the rise in the rate.) \"because the higher composition of capital is expressed, in the case of the individual commodity, in the fact that the whole portion of this commodity's value that represents newly added labour falls in comparison with the portion of value that represents raw materials, ancillary materials, and wear and tear of the fixed capital.\" Marx then spells out this second point as the value-form of the composition change: \"This change in the proportion between the various components of the individual commodity's price, the decline in the portion of price that represents newly added living labour, and the increase in the portions of price that represent previously objectified labour — this is the form the decline of the variable capital as against the constant takes in the price of the individual commodity. Just as this decline is absolute for a given amount of capital, e.g. 100, so it is also absolute for each individual commodity as an aliquot part of the capital reproduced.\" This is one of the most elegant analytic identifications in the chapter: the falling rate of profit, the changing composition of capital, and the changing anatomy of the individual commodity's price are three interchangeable descriptions of the same movement. The fall in the share of newly added value in the individual commodity's price is the falling share of v in C, seen from the side of the commodity. The Turnover Problem: Engels's Interpolation and the Three Cases Now Marx (or rather Engels, whose initials \"F.E.\" mark the passage) introduces a complication that threatens the simple identification of the profit rate on the individual commodity with the actual profit rate on the total capital. The passage is in parentheses, marked \"F.E.\" — Friedrich Engels — who edited and prepared Volume 3 from Marx's manuscript. The point: The rate of profit is calculated on the total capital applied, for a specific period of time, in practice a year . The proportion between surplus-value realized in a year and the total capital, computed as a percentage, is the rate of profit. This is not necessarily identical with the ratio of profit to cost price of commodities, since the latter is based on the turnover period of the capital. The two coincide only if the capital turns over exactly once in the year. To make this precise: if we calculate the profit on the cost price of commodities, we obtain p/k, where p is the profit realized in the course of the year and k is the sum of the cost prices of the commodities produced and sold in the same period. It is readily apparent that this profit rate p/k can only coincide with the actual profit rate p/C (mass of profit divided by the total capital) when k = C, i.e., when the capital turns over exactly once in the year. Three cases for an industrial capital illustrate the distinction: Case I. A capital of £8,000 produces and sells 5,000 items of a certain commodity each year, at 30 shillings per item, so that its annual turnover is £7,500. On each item it makes a profit of 10 shillings, a total of £2,500 per year. Each item contains a capital advance of 20 shillings and a profit of 10 shillings, so that the profit rate on each item is 10/20 = 50 per cent. In the sum of £7,500 turned over, £5,000 is capital advance and £2,500 is profit; the rate of profit on the turnover, p/k, is similarly 50 per cent. Reckoned on the basis of the total capital, however, p/C = 2,500/8,000 = 31¼ per cent. Here the turnover is less than the total capital (the capital has not completed a full cycle), so the profit rate on the cost price is higher than the actual rate. Case II. The capital increases to £10,000. As a result of increased labour productivity, it produces 10,000 items each year at a cost price of 20 shillings. It sells these with 4 shillings profit on each, i.e., at 24 shillings per item. The price of the annual product is £12,000, of which £10,000 is capital advance and £2,000 is profit. p/k = 4/20 = 20 per cent reckoned per item, or 2,000/10,000 = 20 per cent reckoned on the annual turnover — in both cases 20 per cent. Since the total capital equals the sum of the cost prices (£10,000), the actual profit rate p/C is also 20 per cent. The three coincide. Note: this case also illustrates the law directly — capital has grown from £8,000 to £10,000 (25 per cent), but the profit rate on the individual commodity has fallen from 50 per cent to 20 per cent. Since the capital doubled its output, the mass of profit on the accumulation basis is stable. Case III. The capital grows to £15,000, and productivity continues to rise, so that it produces annually 30,000 items at a cost price of 13 shillings each, selling these with 2 shillings profit, i.e., at 15 shillings. The annual turnover is 30,000 × 15 shillings = £22,500, of which £19,500 is capital advance and £3,000 is profit. p/k = 3,000/19,500 = 2/13 = 15 5/13 per cent. p/C = 3,000/15,000 = 20 per cent. Here the total capital is less than the sum turned over, so the profit rate on the cost price is less than the actual rate. The conclusion: \"We see, therefore, that only in case II, where the capital value turned over is the same as the total capital, is the rate of profit on each item of the commodity or on the sum turned over the same as the profit rate calculated on the total capital. In case I, where the sum turned over is less than the total capital, the profit rate calculated on the cost price of the commodity is higher; in case III, where the total capital is less than the sum turned over, this profit rate is less than the actual rate of profit, calculated on the total capital. This is a general rule.\" Engels adds a practical remark on how the turnover is estimated in commercial practice: \"It is assumed that the capital has turned over once as soon as the sum of commodity prices realized reaches the sum of the total capital applied. But the capital can have completed a whole cycle only if the sum of the cost prices of the commodities realized equals the sum of the total capital.\" This is the \"general rule\" of turnover calculation. The point of the whole digression, in the context of the chapter, is to underline that the profit rate cannot be read off the individual commodity's price; it must be computed on the total capital advanced and for an appropriate period. Why the Individual Commodity Can Never Yield the Rate of Profit Marx pulls the methodological moral: \"We see here once again how important it is in capitalist production not to view the individual commodity or the commodity product of some particular period of time in isolation, as a simple commodity; it must rather be viewed as the product of the capital advanced, and in relation to the total capital that produces this commodity.\" This is a decisive rejection of what might be called \"commodity-level\" profit-rate reasoning. The rate of profit is a capital-level magnitude. The price of the individual commodity is produced by the total capital — including the portion that is not consumed and does not reappear in the commodity. \"Even though the rate of profit cannot just be calculated by measuring the mass of surplus-value produced and realized against the portion of capital consumed which reappears in the commodity, but one must rather measure it against this portion plus the portion of capital which is admittedly not consumed, but is still applied in production and continues to serve there, the mass of profit can nevertheless only be equal to the mass of profit or surplus-value actually contained in the commodities and destined to be realized by their sale.\" The passage distinguishes two magnitudes: (1) the total capital applied (including fixed capital not consumed in the period) — the correct denominator of the profit rate; and (2) the mass of profit , which equals the surplus-value actually contained in the commodities and realized on sale. The point is that the rate and the mass are computed on different bases — the rate on the total capital applied, the mass on the commodities realized. This is precisely why the apparent contradiction (falling rate with rising mass) is a matter of which measure one is using at what scale , not of two incompatible facts. The Surface Appearance and the Two Illusions of the Capitalist Marx returns to the productivity of the individual commodity. \"If industrial productivity increases, the price of the individual commodity falls. Less labour is contained in it, both paid and unpaid.\" He gives a vivid example: \"The same labour may produce three times the product, for instance, in which case two-thirds less labour is needed for each individual item. Since profit can only be a portion of the labour contained in the individual commodity, the profit on each individual commodity must decrease, and this is true within certain limits even if the rate of surplus-value rises.\" The crucial limiting condition again: the profit on the total product does not fall below the original mass of profit \"as long as the capital continues to employ the same mass of workers as before at the same level of exploitation. (This can even be the case if fewer workers are employed at a higher level of exploitation.)\" The reasoning is elegant: \"in the same ratio as the profit on the individual commodity falls, the number of products rises. The mass of profit remains the same, even though it is differently distributed over the sum of commodities.\" And this redistribution \"in no way changes the distribution between worker and capitalist of the quantity of value created by the newly added labour.\" The proportionality between paid and unpaid labour is unaffected by the fall in commodity price. Marx then states the key condition for the mass of profit to rise with the same labour: it can only do so \"if the unpaid surplus labour grows, or, with the level of exploitation of labour remaining the same, if the number of workers increases. Both of these factors may operate simultaneously.\" Then a concession and demarcation: \"Viewed abstractly, the rate of profit might remain the same despite a fall in the price of the individual commodity as a result of increased productivity\" — for example, if the increase in productivity affected all the ingredients of the commodity uniformly and simultaneously, so their total price fell in the same proportion as productivity increased, while the ratio between the ingredients stayed the same. \"The rate of profit could even rise, if a rise in the rate of surplus-value was coupled with a significant reduction in the value of the elements of constant capital, and fixed capital in particular.\" These are abstract possibilities, but \"in practice, however, the rate of profit will fall in the long run, as we have already seen.\" The methodological moral: \"In no case does the fall in the price of the individual commodity, taken by itself, permit any conclusion as to the rate of profit. It all depends on the size of the total capital involved in its production.\" Marx illustrates with the yard of material. Suppose the price of one yard of material falls from 3 shillings to 1⅔ shillings. Before the fall, 1⅔ shillings went on constant capital, ⅔ shilling on wages, and ⅔ shilling was profit. After the fall, 1 shilling went on constant capital, ⅙ shilling on wages, and ⅙ shilling was profit. (The figures must sum to the price: 1⅔ + ⅔ + ⅔ = 3; 1 + ⅙ + ⅙ = 1⅓... but the price is 1⅔, so the pairing must be 1 shilling on constant, ⅓ on wages, ⅓ on profit — the printed OCR is confused. What matters is the logic, not the exact figures, which the printed text garbles. Marx's point holds: even given the division of the yard's price into c, v, and s, \"we still do not know whether the rate of profit has remained the same or not. This will depend on whether and by how much the total capital advanced has grown and how many yards more it produces in a given time.\") The argument is thus complete: the profit rate is a global ratio, determining the return on the whole capital, and it cannot be inferred from the anatomy of the individual commodity. The Final Beat: Falling Rates with Rising Prices, and the Adjustment Process Marx's final paragraphs assemble the results into a unified statement of the \"surface appearance\" of the law. The phenomenon — falling prices of individual commodities, rising number of commodities, falling profit per unit and falling rate on the total sum, but rising total mass of profit — \"simply appears on the surface as a fall in the amount of profit on the individual commodity, a fall in its price, and a growth in the mass of profit on the increased total number of commodities produced by the total social capital or the total capital of the individual capitalist. The matter is then conceived as if the capitalist voluntarily made less profit on the individual commodity, but compensated himself by the greater number of commodities which he now produces. This conception rests on the notion of profit upon alienation, which is derived from the viewpoint of commercial capital.\" The footnote identifies this notion with Sir James Steuart, whose conception Marx criticizes in Theories of Surplus-Value , Part I, pp. 41–3. (The reference back to Steuart, the eighteenth-century writer on \"profit upon alienation,\" ties the contemporary apologetics — Roscher — to a much older error.) Marx reminds the reader that the analysis of the individual commodity earlier in Capital (Volume 1, Parts Four and Seven) showed that the growing mass of commodities and the cheapening of the individual commodity do not in themselves affect the proportion of paid and unpaid labour — except insofar as the commodities enter into the determination of the value of labour-power. This is a crucial qualification: the fall in the price of wage-goods does eventually affect wages, and therefore the rate of surplus-value — which is why the composition and the exploitation rate are not independent in the long run. But the direct effect of cheapening commodities on the profit rate is nil, because profit is determined by capital-level ratios. Then the two \"imaginings\" of the individual capitalist. \"Since everything presents a false appearance in competition, in fact an upside-down one, it is possible for the individual capitalist to imagine: (1) that he reduces his profit on the individual commodity by cutting its price, but makes a bigger profit on account of the greater quantity of commodities that he sells; (2) that he fixes the price of the individual commodity and then determines the price of the total product by multiplication, whereas the original process is one of division (see Volume 1, Chapter 12, pp. 433–4), and this multiplication comes in only at second hand and is correct only on the premise of that division.\" The second point is a mathematical methodology observation of broad significance. The value of the total product is divided among its components (c + v + s) — this is the primary operation, determined by the labour embodied and the relation of exploitation. The capitalist then multiplies the price of the individual commodity by the quantity to obtain the aggregate. Multiplication is a derived operation, valid only on the premise of the underlying division. The capitalist's mental arithmetic runs the process backwards and thereby mystifies it: he appears to make the total price by setting the unit price, whereas in fact the total value precedes and determines the unit price. Marx then delivers the diagnosis: \"In point of fact, the vulgar economist does nothing more than translate the peculiar notions of the competition-enslaved capitalist into an ostensibly more theoretical and generalized language, and attempt to demonstrate the validity of these notions.\" The circle is closed: the categories of vulgar economy are the theoretical expression of the everyday consciousness of the capitalist, which is itself the inverted reflection of the laws of the system. The penultimate sentence restates the equivalence: \"In actual fact, the fall in commodity prices and the rise in the mass of profit on the increased mass of cheapened commodities is simply another expression of the law of the falling profit rate in the context of a simultaneously rising mass of profit.\" The final paragraph steps methodologically outside the main line. \"An investigation of how far a falling rate of profit can coincide with rising prices would be no more pertinent here than the earlier point elaborated in Volume 1, pp. 433–4, in connection with relative surplus-value.\" This is a gesture of scope-limitation — Marx will not pursue the question of falling rates with rising prices (which arises in inflation, in raw-material price shocks, in wartime, etc.) because it belongs elsewhere. He then sketches the relevant dynamics: \"The capitalist who employs improved but not yet universally used methods of production sells below the market price, but above his individual price of production; his profit rate thus rises, until competition cancels this out; in the course of this period of adjustment, the second requirement is fulfilled, i.e. growth in the capital laid out; and according to the level of this growth, the capitalist will then be in a position to employ a portion of the workers employed earlier, perhaps all of them or even a greater number, under the new conditions, and thus to produce the same amount of profit or even a larger amount.\" The passage connects the falling-rate law to the theory of super-profit and differential profit (developed in Volume 3, Chapters 10 and beyond). The early adopter of a superior technique captures a temporary super-profit until the technique diffuses. This is the mechanism by which productivity grows — individual capitalists seeking super-profits drive the general composition up, lowering the general rate over time. The result of the whole process: the capitalist who gains by the innovation ends up, after its diffusion, able to employ the same or a greater number of workers under the new conditions, producing the same or a larger absolute profit. The law of the falling rate and the theory of the rising mass are thus, at the end, identical: they are the two sides of the process by which capital undermines its own rate of return in the very act of expanding its productive power. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter yields a set of fundamental principles that extend far beyond its immediate algebraic content. First principle: the categorical distinction between rate and mass. The chapter's entire force depends on recognizing that \"rate of profit\" and \"mass of profit\" are magnitudes of different logical types. The rate is a ratio — a return per unit of capital advanced; the mass is an absolute quantity — a total sum. Because they are different types, they can and must move in opposite directions under determinate conditions. This is not a paradox but a structural implication of any system in which productivity raises the ratio of dead to living labour. The lesson applies far beyond Marx: any analysis of returns on capital that conflates rate and mass will mis-specify the problem. Second principle: the law is a law of tendency, not an empirical uniformity. Marx explicitly concedes that \"the rate of profit may not fall temporarily for other reasons as well.\" The law describes a directive tendency internal to the relations of production, which may be suspended, reversed, or masked by counteracting causes. The methodological status of the law is therefore akin to that of a thermodynamic tendency — a directional pressure on a system, not a prediction about every observable outcome. To read the law as an empirical claim that observed profit rates must decline monotonically is to commit a category error that the chapter itself precludes. Third principle: the law is a form of appearance of the development of the productive forces. The most philosophically significant formulation in the chapter is that the falling rate of profit is \"simply the expression, peculiar to the capitalist mode of production, of the progressive development of the social productivity of labour.\" The same process — rising productivity — would, under a different set of social relations, register as an unqualified increase in wealth and free time. Under capitalist relations, it registers as a falling rate of return on capital. The form is not a mere illusion imposed from outside; it is the necessary way the process appears within a system organized around the self-valorization of value. This is a textbook example of Marx's method: the form of appearance is real, socially efficacious, and historically specific, and criticizing it does not mean denying it. Fourth principle: the precarious condition of accumulation. The chapter establishes that the mass of profit can grow despite the falling rate only if the total capital grows faster than the rate falls. This is a stringent, non-automatic condition. It means that accumulation has a built-in dynamic fragility: as the composition rises, the capital required to sustain any given absolute profit grows, and the condition that profit grow becomes progressively harder to satisfy. In the limit, the falling rate becomes a barrier to accumulation — the implicit crisis-theoretic content that Chapter 15 will make explicit. Fifth principle: the genesis of the relative surplus population. The chapter's treatment of the \"permanent apparent surplus working population\" is a crucial methodological contribution. The reserve army is not produced by a Malthusian mechanism of natural reproduction exceeding subsistence; it is produced by rising productivity itself . \"Misery produces population\" — poverty is not a natural condition but a structural product of capitalist accumulation. This connects the abstract theory of the falling rate to the concrete historical theory of the labour market and class composition. Sixth principle: the epistemology of competition. The chapter provides what is arguably Marx's most compact statement of his theory of ideological inversion. In competition, everything appears \"upside down.\" The structural law is perceived in the form of an intentional calculation: the capitalist chooses a lower rate to capture a larger mass; the capitalist \"fixes\" the unit price and derives the total; the capitalist is \"more clever and more humane\" (Roscher). These appearances are not simply errors; they are \"a necessary product of the upside-down way that the immanent laws of capitalist production present themselves within competition.\" The critique of political economy is therefore not merely a refutation of false propositions but a genealogy of the categories of bourgeois thought. Seventh principle: the methodological priority of structural over distributional analysis. By presenting the law before the decomposition of profit into industrial profit, commercial profit, interest, and ground-rent, Marx demonstrates the law's independence from distribution. The profit rate falls, on his account, regardless of how surplus-value is subsequently divided among the appropriating classes. This is a powerful argumentative strategy with broad application: it shows that some structural tendencies operate at a level of abstraction beneath any particular distributional arrangement, and that the critic who tries to explain such tendencies by reference to distribution has confused the derivative with the primary. The broader implications of the chapter extend in several directions. For the history of economic thought: the chapter identifies the falling rate of profit as the pivot of classical and post-classical political economy. The Ricardian consolation (which Marx quotes at length and dissects numerically — capital up 10 per cent, rate down 14⅔ per cent, hence mass falls — \" Hinc illae lacrimae! \") is shown to be a partial, tautological answer that confuses the condition for the mass of profit growing with an automatic tendency. In this, Marx situates his own work as the completion — and critique — of the classical project. For the theory of crises: the chapter is the indispensable premise for the analysis of over-accumulation and crisis in Chapter 15. If the mass of profit cannot grow unless capital grows faster than the rate falls, then a system in which the rate is persistently declining becomes progressively more prone to crises of over-production, in which capital cannot be valorized at the required scale. The tendency to over-capacity, over-investment, and falling returns — and the drive to compensate through speculation, credit expansion, and the opening of new markets — have their theoretical ground in the relationship articulated here. For the analysis of imperialism and uneven development: the international passages provide an analytical basis for the theory of unequal exchange and unequal development. Advanced economies have high compositions (which depress their profit rates ceteris paribus ) but also higher rates of surplus-value (through intensity and cheapened wage-goods), which may offset the tendency. Less advanced economies have low compositions but lower productivity, which may yield either higher or lower rates depending on the configuration of the two variables. The chapter thus establishes the theoretical vocabulary for subsequent analyses of international exploitation and unequal development. For the critique of vulgar economy: the analysis of Roscher, the \"profit upon alienation\" tradition, and the two illusions of the capitalist provides a template for Marx's critique of the economics of his time and — by extension — of the foundational moves of much modern economics: the treatment of profit as a markup, the analysis of the firm as an intentional pricing agent, the substitution of the individual commodity as the unit of analysis for the capital as a whole. Marx's insistence that the total capital is the correct unit of accounting, and that multiplication is a derived operation premised on the prior division of value, remains a live methodological challenge. For the philosophical understanding of the mode of production: the chapter makes vivid what Marx means by the historical specificity of capitalist production. Productivity growth is not a neutral metric of human progress; under capitalist relations it takes the form of a falling rate of return on capital, a permanent surplus population, an intensifying struggle over distribution, and periodic crises. The same material development would, under differently organized relations, appear in an entirely different form. The falling rate of profit is, in this sense, the sign under which capitalism's own productive achievements become the ground of its internal contradictions. Read in its proper context — between the completed theory of the average rate of profit in Part Two and the counteracting causes of Chapter 14 and the internal contradictions of Chapter 15 — Chapter 13 is a chapter of pure theoretical construction that deliberately brackets the qualifications it will later require. Its function is to establish the law in its purity, to demonstrate its derivation from the most fundamental categories of the capitalist mode of production, and to set the framework within which the subsequent analysis of counteracting causes, crises, and historical transformation will be conducted. It is, in the most literal sense, the statement of the law itself — the pivot on which the rest of Volume 3 turns, and the theoretical discovery on which Marx stakes his claim to having solved the central puzzle of political economy since Adam Smith. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 12 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 14"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 14", "title": "Chapter 14", "subtitle": "Counteracting Influences", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 14/Chapter 14 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 7856, "read_time": "39 min read", "tags": ["Law of the Falling Rate of Profit (TRPF)", "Industrial Reserve Army & Surplus Population"], "keywords": ["Counteracting Factors to the Falling Rate of Profit", "Increasing Intensity of Exploitation", "Depression of Wages Below Value", "Cheapening Elements of Constant Capital", "Relative Surplus Population / Cheap Labour Reserves", "Foreign Trade and Imperialist Extraction", "Increase of Stock Capital without Direct Management"], "text": "Chapter 14 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 7,813 words ⏱️ ~39 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 14, \"Counteracting Factors,\" constitutes the analytical hinge of Part Three of Capital , Volume III. Its predecessor (Chapter 13) established the law of the tendential fall in the rate of profit; its successor (Chapter 15) will expound the \"internal contradictions\" of that law. Chapter 14 performs the indispensable mediating work between the two: it enumerates, classifies, and theorises the Gegenwirkungen — the counteracting influences — that prevent the law from manifesting itself as an immediate, mechanical decline. The thesis is announced in the chapter's opening inversion, and it is a genuinely dialectical one. Classical political economy — Smith, Ricardo, and their predecessors — took the fall in the rate of profit as the phenomenon requiring explanation. Marx declares that, given \"the enormous development in the productive powers of social labour over the last thirty years\" (a footnote dates this span as 1835–65) and the \"enormous mass of fixed capital\" now absorbed in social production, the problem has reversed polarity: \"instead of the problem that occupied previous economists, the problem of explaining the fall in the profit rate, we have the opposite problem of explaining why this fall is not greater or faster.\" The explanandum is therefore the slowness , not the fact, of the decline. This is the chapter's central problem, and its intellectual objective is to demonstrate that this slowness is itself a necessary result of the very same causes that produce the fall. The second pillar of the thesis is a rigorous claim about the logical status of the law. Because counteracting forces \"check and cancel\" the effect of the general law, that law acquires \"simply the character of a tendency, which is why we have described the fall in the general rate of profit as a tendential fall.\" Crucially, \"tendency\" here is not a probabilistic hedge nor a teleological promise. Later in the chapter Marx precisely defines its meaning: the law \"operates more as a tendency, i.e. as a law whose absolute realization is held up, delayed and weakened by counteracting factors,\" and its \"effect is decisive only under certain particular circumstances and over long periods.\" The observed profit rate is therefore a resultant — the vector sum of the tendency and its counter-tendencies — not the tendency itself. The third, and philosophically most important, pillar is the identity of cause and counter-cause . Marx repeatedly insists that the factors which brake the fall are not external accidents, autonomous shocks, or political intrusions on an otherwise pure mechanism. They are generated by the same development of productive power that produces the fall. The formula recurs as a refrain: \"the same factors that produce the tendency for the rate of profit to fall also moderate the realization of this tendency.\" This is a unity of opposites: rising productivity simultaneously (a) cheapens the elements of constant capital and raises the rate of surplus-value, and (b) raises the value-composition of capital and displaces living labour. The chapter is thus a masterclass in the logic of overdetermination, showing how a single causal process can issue in antithetical surface effects. Structurally, the chapter delivers five enumerated counteracting factors — intensified exploitation of labour, wages forced below the value of labour-power, cheapening of constant capital, the relative surplus population, and foreign trade — followed by a general recapitulation with two clarifications, and then a supplementary sixth factor: the increase in share capital. Within the framework of Volume III as a whole, the chapter supplies the answer to the question of how the tendential law can coexist with the manifest facts of nineteenth-century capitalist prosperity, colonial super-profits, and persistently high measured profit rates. It also positions Marx decisively against Ricardo on foreign trade, and against wage-push explanations of falling profitability — a polemic whose methodological stakes (\"only when the relationships that form the rate of profit have been understood will statistics be able to put forward genuine analyses of wage-rates\") remain live. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Inverted Problem: Explaining Why the Fall Is Not Faster The chapter opens with an empirical-historical observation that functions as a theoretical destabiliser. The productive powers of social labour have developed over \"the last thirty years\" — the footnote glosses this as 1835–65, precisely the period of the railway boom, the factory system's consolidation, and Britain's industrial hegemony — to an extent exceeding \"all earlier periods\" combined. Marx immediately broadens the reference beyond machinery proper to \"the enormous mass of fixed capital involved in the overall process of social production,\" that is, to the entire value-architecture of buildings, infrastructure, and durable means of production. This is not decorative. It signals that the constant capital whose swollen proportions drive the fall is not merely the machine in the workshop but the whole social fixed-capital stock — precisely the category that reappears, strikingly, in the chapter's final section on railways. From this observation Marx extracts the chapter's governing inversion. If constant capital has grown so colossally relative to living labour, the rate of profit (the ratio of surplus-value to total capital advanced) should have collapsed catastrophically and swiftly. That it has not is the mystery. Hence: \"Counteracting influences must be at work, checking and cancelling the effect of the general law.\" Two concepts require precise unpacking at this point, because the whole chapter depends on them. First, the rate of profit . It is the ratio of the surplus-value (s) produced to the total capital advanced (C = c + v, constant plus variable capital). This differs structurally from the rate of surplus-value (s/v), which measures exploitation against variable capital alone — and, as Marx stresses later, is ultimately a ratio between two portions of living labour-time, \"in actual fact on each working day individually.\" Because the rate of profit has in its denominator the entire capital, it is sensitive in a way the rate of surplus-value is not to the proportion in which that capital divides between dead and living labour — that is, to the organic composition of capital , the ratio c:v. Second, the notion of a tendency . The word is doing technical work. A tendency in Marx's sense is a law of motion that is never observed in isolation; what is observed is the tendency plus its counter-tendencies. The tendential fall is thus not falsified by the empirical stability or even rise of measured profit rates — such stability is exactly what the theory predicts, given the counteracting factors enumerated here. The chapter's opening therefore sets a trap for naive empiricism: the very success of capitalism in postponing the fall is evidence for, not against, the law. A crucial nuance appears immediately in the framing: the counteracting influences are \"the most general of these factors\" — the phrase conceding that the list is analytical rather than exhaustive, a catalogue of types rather than a closed inventory. 2.2 Counteracting Factor 1: The Intensification of Labour Exploitation The first factor is the raising of the rate of surplus-value through more intense exploitation of labour , which the chapter divides along the lines already established in Volume I: prolongation of the working day ( absolute surplus-value ) and intensification of labour ( relative surplus-value , in its intensive rather than merely technological mode). Marx's treatment here is remarkable for its refusal to oversimplify. Intensification, he notes, is not a pure counteracting factor. \"There are many aspects to the intensification of labour that involve a growth in the constant capital as against the variable, i.e. a fall in the rate of profit, such as when a single worker has to supervise a larger amount of machinery.\" So the very process that squeezes more surplus out of each worker simultaneously raises the capital each worker sets in motion — driving c/v upward and thus pushing the rate of profit down . Intensification is therefore internally split: it raises the rate of surplus-value and lowers the mass of surplus-value relative to the total capital advanced. Marx states the general rule with lapidary economy: \"as also with most procedures that serve to produce relative surplus-value, the same reasons that bring about a rise in the rate of surplus-value can also involve a fall in its mass, taking given magnitudes of total capital applied.\" He then draws a fine but vital distinction concerning the physical wear of machinery. Accelerated machine speeds consume more raw material in a given time and wear out fixed capital faster — but \"as far as the fixed capital is concerned, the fact that this wears out the machines that much faster does not in any way affect the ratio of their value to the price of the labour that sets them in motion.\" The point is that accelerated depreciation is a change in the rate of turnover of capital value, not in the composition of the capital advanced at any moment. The value-ratio of machinery to wages is what governs the rate of profit; the speed at which that machinery is used up governs the annual rate of profit and the rhythm of replacement, a distinct issue. This is a recurring Marxian discipline: keeping the categories of composition and turnover analytically separate. By contrast, the prolongation of the working day is characterised as the purest of the counteracting mechanisms. It \"increases the amount of surplus labour appropriated without basically altering the ratio of the labour-power applied to the constant capital that this sets in motion, and which in point of fact rather reduces the constant capital in relative terms.\" Because the day is lengthened without adding machinery or materials proportionally, the ratio c/v falls . Prolongation is thus a counteracting factor twice over: it raises the numerator (surplus-value) directly, and enlarges the denominator's variable component relative to its constant component, raising the profit rate on both counts. Marx calls it \"this discovery of modern industry\" — a pointed phrase, since the lengthened day was a product of the factory system, not a pre-capitalist residue. At this juncture comes the chapter's central theoretical disclosure. Marx identifies \"the real secret of the tendential fall in the rate of profit\" in the structure of relative surplus-value production itself: \"the procedures for producing relative surplus-value are based, by and large, either on transforming as much as possible of a given amount of labour into surplus-value or on spending as little as possible labour in general in relation to the capital advanced; so that the same reasons that permit the level of exploitation of labour to increase make it impossible to exploit as much labour as before with the same total capital.\" This sentence is the pivot of the entire chapter. The secret is that the increase in exploitation per worker and the reduction in the number of workers per unit of capital are two faces of one process. Productivity growth means each worker, in a given time, processes more material with more machinery and produces more output; but it also means fewer workers are needed to work up a given capital. The rate of surplus-value rises; the mass of surplus-value producible by a given capital falls. Because the rate of profit is a ratio of mass to total capital, and mass is falling relative to capital, the rate falls — but only as a tendency, because the rising rate partially offsets the falling mass. Marx adds female and child labour to this factor: the family as a whole now supplies capital with a greater quantity of surplus labour than before, \"even if the sum of their wages increases, which is by no means always the case.\" The qualification matters — the counteracting effect operates through the quantity of labour-power mobilised, not through the wage bill's movement. Two further sub-mechanisms are folded in. First, improvements in method that raise relative surplus-value \"without a change in the magnitude of capital applied\" — agriculture is the example: constant capital does not grow in proportion to variable, yet output rises relative to labour-power applied. Since agricultural products enter both the worker's consumption basket (cheapening the value of labour-power) and the elements of constant capital (raw materials), such improvements attack the problem from both directions. Second, the removal of \"fetters\": the freeing of productivity from \"restraints on commerce, arbitrary restrictions, or limitations which have become irksome in the course of time, and generally from fetters of any kind,\" again without any initial impact on the c:v proportion. This is a pointedly political-institutional counteracting factor — free trade, deregulation, the demolition of customary restriction. Finally, Marx addresses the innovation rent : the question whether the \"temporary but ever repeated increases in surplus-value\" accruing to the capitalist who adopts an invention before it is universally applied count among the counteracting factors. His answer is crisp: \"This question must be answered in the affirmative.\" But he immediately adds a dialectical codicil — these factors \"though in the final instance they always accelerate it further\" inhibit the fall. The counteracting factor ultimately intensifies the tendency: the surplus profit of the pioneer is dissipated by general adoption, yet the accumulation it financed raises the social composition of capital. This forward-looking clause anticipates the identical claim Marx will make about foreign trade. 2.3 The Arithmetic of Compensation: Rate versus Mass of Surplus-Value Having identified the mechanism, Marx subjects it to explicit quantitative analysis — one of the few passages in Volume III where the algebra is laid out almost formally. He begins from a decomposition: \"The mass of surplus-value that a capital of given size produces is the product of two factors, the rate of surplus-value and the number of workers employed at this rate.\" This yields the chapter's fundamental identity of the mass of surplus-value: S = s′ × n where S is the mass of surplus-value, s′ (= s/v, or more precisely surplus labour-time divided by necessary labour-time per worker) is the rate of surplus-value, and n is the number of workers employed at that rate. Equivalently, since the wage bill V is n times the wage per worker, S = s′ × V — the mass depends on the absolute size of variable capital and the rate of surplus-value. Marx states this equivalence explicitly. He then poses the antinomy. \"We have seen that the same factors that increase the rate of relative surplus-value lower the amount of labour-power applied on average.\" If S = s′ × V, and the movement of productivity raises s′ while depressing V relative to total capital C, then S's movement is indeterminate. Hence: \"this effect can be greater or less, depending on the specific proportions in which this antithetical movement takes place.\" Marx then clarifies with a return to the level of a capital of fixed magnitude — the abstraction, standard in Volume III, of capital = 100. \"With respect to a capital of given magnitude, e.g. 100, the rate of surplus-value can grow while the average mass of surplus-value falls, since the rate is determined by the ratio in which the variable portion of the capital is valorized, while the mass is determined by the proportion that the variable capital forms in the total.\" This is the crux. Two quantities are being distinguished: The rate of surplus-value s′ is a pure ratio internal to living labour — unpaid labour-time over paid labour-time. It is unaffected, in itself, by how much constant capital accompanies the workers. The mass of surplus-value S = s′·v is mediated by the share of variable capital in the total , v/C. As c/v rises, v/C falls, and S shrinks even while s′ grows. If we express the rate of profit in terms of these components, the underlying structure becomes transparent (a reconstruction faithful to Marx's definitions, not a formula he writes here): since p′ = s/C = s′v/C, dividing numerator and denominator by v gives p′ = s′ / (1 + c/v) This makes the chapter's logic algebraically legible: the rate of profit rises with the rate of surplus-value and falls with the value-composition of capital. A tendential fall requires the rise of c/v to outrun the rise of s′. Every counteracting factor in the chapter works on one or both of these variables. Marx then supplies a concrete numerical illustration of the compensation mechanism — the worker doing the work of two, or replaceable by three. \"If one worker is compelled to do work that it would really be rational for two to perform, and if this happens under circumstances in which this one worker can replace three, then one worker can now provide as much surplus labour as two did before, and to this extent the rate of surplus-value rises. But this one will not supply as much surplus labour as three did before, and this makes the mass of surplus-value fall. Its fall is compensated for or limited by the rise in the rate of surplus-value.\" Notice the precision: the rate rises to the level of two workers' surplus (a gain), but the mass falls short of three workers' surplus (a loss). Compensation is partial , not total. This is why the chapter speaks of the fall being \"moderated or halted,\" never reversed. He then extends the analysis from the individual capital to the social aggregate. \"If the entire population is set to work at the increased rate of surplus-value, the mass of surplus-value rises, even though the population remains the same. Still more is this the case with a growing population; and even though this growth is linked with a relative fall in the number of workers employed, compared with the size of the total capital, the fall is still moderated or halted by the higher rate of surplus-value.\" Here lies the crucial distinction between the individual capital and social total capital . For the individual capitalist, the mass of surplus-value may fall; for society, employing the whole available population at a higher rate yields a larger mass. And since, taken across society, \"the surplus-value it produces is the same as the profit,\" the social rate of profit can be maintained by the extension of exploitation across the entire workforce — the mechanism by which the counteracting factors operate at the level of the total, not the part. Marx closes the section with a formal caution and a theorem. The caution: \"the rate of surplus-value can rise, with a constant amount of capital, even though the mass of surplus-value falls — and vice versa.\" The theorem: \"the mass of surplus-value is equal to the rate multiplied by the number of workers; but the rate is never calculated on the total capital, but only on the variable capital, in actual fact on each working day individually. Once the size of the capital value is given, however, the rate of profit can never rise or fall without a similar rise or fall in the mass of surplus-value.\" This last is trivially demonstrable: if p′ = S/C and C is fixed, then p′ and S must move together. The theorem's function within the argument is to forestall an error: the rate of surplus-value and the mass of surplus-value can diverge, but the rate of profit and the mass of profit cannot — wherefore any falling rate of profit necessarily entails a falling mass of profit for a given total capital, which is why the tendency is not merely a bookkeeping artefact. 2.4 Counteracting Factor 2: Wages Below the Value of Labour-Power The second factor is dispatched in a single paragraph, and its brevity is itself the point. Marx writes: \"We simply make an empirical reference to this point here, as, like many other things that might be brought in, it has nothing to do with the general analysis of capital, but has its place in an account of competition, which is not dealt with in this work.\" The reduction of wages below the value of labour-power is a deduction from the workers' customary subsistence — a redistribution of the wage in capital's favour that raises s′ directly and, since it lowers v's magnitude as well as its proportion, raises the rate of profit. The theoretical scruple is significant. Marx places the systematic treatment of wage reductions under the heading of competition — the sphere of surface antagonism analysed in Volume I's discussion of the working day, but not part of the \"general analysis of capital,\" which proceeds on the assumption that commodities (including labour-power) exchange at their values. Even so, he emphasises that this factor is \"one of the most important factors in stemming the tendency for the rate of profit to fall.\" This creates a methodological tension that Marx is content to leave unresolved: the theory of the tendential fall is developed on the premise of value-equivalent exchange, while the most powerful counteracting factor in practice violates that premise. The chapter's own later polemic — \"nothing is more absurd... than to explain the fall in the rate of profit in terms of a rise in wage rates\" — cuts the other way here: wages below value slow the fall, but falling wages are not the law's cause, and rising wages are not its cause either. The causal arrow runs from productivity, not from the wage level. 2.5 Counteracting Factor 3: The Cheapening of the Elements of Constant Capital The third factor is the most direct attack on the composition of capital: the falling value of constant capital's material elements, notwithstanding the growth of their mass . Marx explicitly cross-references \"Part One of this volume\" — the earlier analysis (Chapter 5) of the causes that raise the rate of profit while the rate of surplus-value remains constant, notably the economies in the use of constant capital and the cheapening of its elements through rising labour productivity. The argument is advanced by a vivid historical comparison. \"The quantity of cotton that a single European spinning operative works up in a modern factory has grown to a most colossal extent in comparison with that which a European spinner used to process with the spinning wheel. But the value of the cotton processed has not grown in the same proportion as its mass.\" The operative handles an enormously greater quantity of material, but the value of that material, measured against the wage, has grown far less than proportionally. The same holds for machinery and other fixed capital. Marx articulates the general principle: \"the same development that raises the mass of constant capital in comparison with variable reduces the value of its elements, as a result of the higher productivity of labour, and hence prevents the value of the constant capital, even though this grows steadily, from growing in the same degree as its material volume.\" He adds the strong limiting case: \"In certain cases, the mass of the constant capital elements may increase while their total value remains the same or even falls.\" The dialectical inversion is exact and deserves emphasis: the same rise in productivity that increases the technical mass of constant capital per worker simultaneously cheapens each unit of that constant capital. Two opposing forces operate on c: its physical magnitude multiplies, its unit value diminishes. Because the rate of profit is governed by the value composition, not the physical composition, this cheapening is a powerful brake. Marx then introduces the devaluation of existing capital — the destruction of the value of already-installed means of production by the arrival of more efficient ones (in later terminology, \"moral depreciation\"). This too \"steadily operates to stay the fall in the rate of profit, even though in certain circumstances it may reduce the mass of profit by detracting from the mass of capital that produces profit.\" The caveat is important and again dialectical: devaluation can shrink the profit-producing capital base itself, so it is a two-edged instrument. But the chapter's refrain recurs: \"We see here once again how the same factors that produce the tendency for the rate of profit to fall also moderate the realization of this tendency.\" The cheapening of constant capital is not an external rescue of the profit rate; it is the form in which the fall is produced , since the same productivity growth also swells the mass of constant capital. 2.6 Counteracting Factor 4: The Relative Surplus Population The fourth factor is the creation of a relative surplus population — the reserve army of the unemployed, a concept developed in Volume I. Marx's framing here is explicitly circular, and he makes no apology for it: \"The creation of such a surplus population is inseparable from the development of labour productivity and is accelerated by it, the same development as is expressed in the decline in the profit rate.\" The same productivity growth that raises c/v and depresses p′ expels living labour and thus produces a surplus population. The counteracting effect arises from the uses to which that population is put. Marx identifies three consequences of the surplus population's existence. First, \"the more the capitalist mode of production is developed in a country, the more strikingly does the relative surplus population obtrude there.\" Second, the availability of cheap, dismissible labour is \"a reason why the more or less incomplete subordination of labour to capital persists in several branches of production, and longer indeed than would seem to correspond at first sight to the general level of development.\" That is, the reserve army retards the mechanisation of backward branches: why install machinery when cheap hands are plentiful? This is reinforced by \"the greater resistance that many branches of production, by their nature, oppose to the transformation of manual work into machine production.\" Third — and this is the analytically decisive point — new branches of production continually open up, \"particularly in the field of luxury consumption, which precisely take this relative surplus population as their basis, a population often made available owing to the preponderance of constant capital in other branches of production.\" The composition of capital is thus uneven across the social economy: high-composition branches shed labour, and low-composition branches absorb it. These low-composition branches \"base themselves in turn on a preponderance of the element of living labour, and only gradually pass through the same trajectory as other branches.\" Marx explicitly notes that this applies \"in both cases\" — both to backward branches and to newly opened luxury branches: \"variable capital forms a significant proportion of the total and wages are below the average, so that both the rate and mass of surplus-value in these branches of production are unusually high.\" The counteracting mechanism is then located precisely at the point of aggregation: \"Now since the general rate of profit is formed by the equalization of the rates of profit in the various particular branches of production, here again the same reasons that produce the tendential fall in the rate of profit also produce a counterweight to this tendency, which paralyses its effect to a greater or lesser extent.\" The equalization of the general rate of profit is the mechanism by which capital flows between branches until profit rates are levelled. The persistence of branches with a high ratio of variable to constant capital — and thus unusually high s′ and rates of profit — pulls the social average upward , offsetting the tendency in the high-composition branches. This is a structural, not merely quantitative, counterweight: it depends on the heterogeneity of the social composition of capital and on the continuous regeneration of that heterogeneity by the surplus population. Note that this is also the section where Marx comes closest to a theory of the \"informal sector\" and of labour-intensive luxury production as functionally integrated into the reproduction of the total social capital. 2.7 Counteracting Factor 5: Foreign Trade, Surplus Profit, and the Dispute with Ricardo The fifth factor is developed at greatest length and with the most explicit engagement with the history of political economy. Foreign trade operates on the profit rate through the same channels as factors 1 and 3, but with an external dimension. Marx writes: \"In so far as foreign trade cheapens on the one hand the elements of constant capital and on the other the necessary means of subsistence into which variable capital is converted, it acts to raise the rate of profit by raising the rate of surplus-value and reducing the value of constant capital.\" Cheaper imported raw materials and machinery reduce c; cheaper imported foodstuffs reduce the value of labour-power, thereby raising s′. Additionally, foreign trade \"permits the scale of production to be expanded,\" accelerating accumulation — but \"it also accelerates the fall in the variable capital as against the constant, and hence the fall in the rate of profit.\" The duality of effect again: the same cause raises the rate now and deepens the tendency later. Marx adds a historical-dialectical observation: \"whereas the expansion of foreign trade was the basis of capitalist production in its infancy, it becomes the specific product of the capitalist mode of production as this progresses, through the inner necessity of this mode of production and its need for an ever extended market.\" Foreign trade is thereby not merely an environmental advantage but an internally generated requirement of capital — the imperative of the world market. He appends a sharp scholarly verdict: \"(Ricardo completely overlooked this aspect of foreign trade.)\" The footnote directs the reader to Chapter VII of Ricardo's Principles , \"On Foreign Trade\" — the locus classicus of the Ricardian claim that foreign trade benefits a country by reducing the value of its subsistence imports, but which treats this only as a static gain in the rate of surplus-value, missing the dynamic, contradictory effect on the composition of capital. Marx then poses a further question that he declares \"lies beyond the limits of our investigation\": does foreign trade, and colonial trade in particular, raise the general rate of profit? His analysis of how a higher rate of profit can arise in foreign trade proceeds in three steps. Step one: the advanced-country price advantage. \"Capital invested in foreign trade can yield a higher rate of profit, firstly, because it competes with commodities produced by other countries with less developed production facilities, so that the more advanced country sells its goods above their value, even though still more cheaply than its competitors.\" The apparent paradox — selling above value yet below competitors' prices — dissolves once we attend to the labour theory of value. \"In so far as the labour of the more advanced country is valorized here as labour of a higher specific weight, the profit rate rises, since labour that is not paid as qualitatively higher is nevertheless sold as such.\" This is the theory of international value: more intensive or more productive national labour counts, on the world market, as labour of higher intensity, so a given quantity of it creates more value than the world average. The advanced country's workers produce more value per unit of labour-time, but their wages are not correspondingly higher — hence a higher rate of surplus-value, and a higher profit rate. The same relation holds with respect to the trading partner: \"such a country gives more objectified labour in kind than it receives, even though it still receives the goods in question more cheaply than it could produce them itself.\" This is a clear statement of what later theory calls unequal exchange : a net transfer of objectified labour from the less to the more developed nation, masked by the fact that both parties gain in use-value terms. Step two: the analogy of the innovation rent. Marx reinforces this with a comparison to the individual innovating capitalist: a manufacturer \"who makes use of a new discovery before this has become general sells more cheaply than his competitors and yet still sells above the individual value of his commodity, valorizing the specifically higher productivity of the labour he employs as surplus labour. He thus realizes a surplus profit.\" The nation with superior production facilities occupies the same position vis-à-vis other nations as the innovating firm does vis-à-vis its competitors: its superior productivity yields a surplus profit — profit above the general rate. This links the foreign trade section back to the innovation-rent discussion under factor 1 and shows the two as instances of one logic. Step three: the colonial case. \"As far as capital invested in the colonies, etc. is concerned, however, the reason why this can yield higher rates of profit is that the profit rate is generally higher there on account of the lower degree of development, and so too is the exploitation of labour, through the use of slaves and coolies, etc.\" Here the mechanism is different: not superior national productivity, but the low organic composition and low level of development of the colonial economy, plus the extreme exploitation possible under slavery and indenture. This is a concentrated, historically specific statement of what later theory would call super-exploitation and colonial super-profit. Marx then poses the aggregation question directly: \"Now there is no reason why the higher rates of profit that capital invested in certain branches yields in this way, and brings home to its country of origin, should not enter into the equalization of the general rate of profit and hence raise this in due proportion, unless monopolies stand in the way.\" The qualifier \"unless monopolies stand in the way\" is the only condition. \"There is in particular no reason why this should not be so when the branches of capital investment in question are subject to the laws of free competition.\" At this point Marx introduces a footnote that is decisive for the chapter's polemical position: \"Adam Smith is right here, as against Ricardo, who says: 'They contend, that the equality of profits will be brought about by the general rise of profits; and I am of the opinion, that the profits of the favoured trade will speedily subside to the general level.'\" Marx sides with Smith. He then reconstructs Ricardo's argument fairly: \"What Ricardo has in mind, on the other hand, is this: higher prices are obtained abroad; commodities are bought there and sent home in exchange; these commodities are therefore sold on the domestic market, so that the favoured spheres of production can have at most a temporary advantage over others.\" On Ricardo's account, the high profits of foreign trade are competed away: influx of commodities equalises prices, and the general rate returns to its former level. Marx's rejoinder is an appeal to substance over appearance: \"As soon as we take our leave of the money form, however, this semblance vanishes. The privileged country receives more labour in exchange for less, even though this difference, the excess, is pocketed by a particular class, just as in the exchange between labour and capital in general.\" The \"money form\" is the veil: in monetary terms, trade looks like an exchange of equivalents (commodity against commodity, price against price). But in labour-value terms, the advanced country receives a greater quantum of objectified labour than it gives — a real transfer, not a monetary illusion. And this excess accrues to a class, exactly as surplus-value does in the wage relation. The Ricardian equalisation is therefore not a return to the old level: \"Thus in as much as the profit rate is higher because it is generally higher in the colonial country, favourable natural conditions there may enable it to go hand in hand with lower commodity prices. An equalization still takes place, but not an equalization at the old level, as Ricardo believes.\" The equalisation that occurs is equalisation at a higher general rate — Smith's position. The section closes with the chapter's characteristic closing move: \"But this same foreign trade develops the capitalist mode of production at home, and hence promotes a decline in variable capital as against constant, though it also produces overproduction in relation to the foreign country, so that it again has the opposite effect in the further course of development.\" Foreign trade counteracts the fall now; but by accelerating domestic accumulation and generating crises of overproduction in relation to export markets, it deepens the tendency in the longer run. This is the fullest statement in the chapter of the temporal structure of the counteracting factors: they are delaying mechanisms whose operation itself prepares the conditions of their reversal. 2.8 The General Summary and Two Clarifications Before the supplementary sixth factor, Marx inserts a general recapitulation and two clarificatory points designed \"to avoid any misunderstanding.\" The recapitulation is unqualified: \"We have shown in general, therefore, how the same causes that bring about a fall in the general rate of profit provoke counter-effects that inhibit this fall, delay it and in part even paralyse it. These do not annul the law, but they weaken its effect.\" He then delivers the chapter's most celebrated rhetorical inversion: \"If this were not the case, it would not be the fall in the general rate of profit that was incomprehensible, but rather the relative slowness of this fall.\" Slowness is the anomaly requiring explanation; the fall is the default. Hence: \"The law operates therefore simply as a tendency, whose effect is decisive only under certain particular circumstances and over long periods.\" The first clarification concerns a trap inherent in the theory: the cheapening of commodities must not be confused with the rising value-composition of capital. \"The same process that leads to the cheapening of commodities as the capitalist mode of production develops leads to a change in the organic composition of the social capital applied in commodity production, and leads as a result to a fall in the profit rate.\" Both things happen simultaneously and from the same cause. Therefore \"the reduction in the relative cost of the individual commodity, or even in the part of this cost that represents the wear and tear of the machinery, should not be confused with the rising value of the constant capital compared with the variable.\" A falling unit cost per commodity and a rising c:v ratio are compatible, indeed co-causal. Conversely — and this picks up factor 3 — \"any reduction in the relative cost of the constant capital, with the volume of its material elements remaining the same or increasing, acts to increase the rate of profit, i.e. acts to reduce proportionately the value of the constant capital, compared with the variable capital that is applied on a scale which declines progressively.\" The phrase \"on a scale which declines progressively\" refers to variable capital's declining weight within the total: the counteracting effect consists in attenuating the value of the constant element whose relative weight is nonetheless rising. The second clarification is the theoretical keystone of the chapter and its sharpest polemic. The fact that \"an ever smaller quantity of additional living labour is objectified\" in commodities — that the total living labour embodied in the commodity falls as social productivity develops — \"does not affect the proportion in which the living labour contained in the commodity is divided between paid and unpaid. On the contrary. Even though the total amount of the additional living labour contained in it falls, the unpaid part still grows in proportion to the paid part, either by an absolute or a proportionate fall in this paid part; for the same mode of production that reduces the total mass of additional living labour in a commodity is accompanied by a rise in absolute and relative surplus-value.\" Two distinct quantities must be kept apart: the absolute quantity of living labour embedded in a commodity (which falls as productivity rises) and the division of that labour into paid and unpaid portions (which shifts in capital's favour). The tendential fall in p′ and the tendential rise in s′ are therefore not rivals; they are \"particular forms.\" The polemical payoff follows: \"Nothing is more absurd, then, than to explain the fall in the rate of profit in terms of a rise in wage rates, even though this too may be an exceptional case.\" Marx thereby dismisses both the classical wage-fund-adjacent view and the proto-\"profit squeeze\" thesis, while conceding — \"even though this too may be an exceptional case\" — that rising wages can occur and can depress profitability episodically. The concession is precisely calibrated: exceptional, not law-constituting. He then states the methodological priority of theory over measurement: \"Only when the relationships that form the rate of profit have been understood will statistics be able to put forward genuine analyses of wage-rates in different periods.\" Empiricism without theory is not merely incomplete but systematically misleading. And finally the chapter's most quoted theorem: \"The profit rate does not fall because labour becomes less productive but rather because it becomes more productive. The rise in the rate of surplus-value and the fall in the rate of profit are simply particular forms that express the growing productivity of labour in capitalist terms.\" 2.9 Counteracting Factor 6: The Increase in Share Capital The chapter appends a sixth and explicitly provisional factor: \"The above five points can also be supplemented by the following one, though we cannot go any deeper into it at this point.\" As capitalist production advances, \"one portion of capital is considered simply to be interest-bearing capital and is invested as such.\" Marx distinguishes two senses of this, guarding against a trivial reading. It is not the sense in which a money-lending capitalist takes interest while the industrial capitalist takes entrepreneurial profit — that division \"does not affect the level of the general rate of profit,\" because the general rate is constituted by the sum of all these revenue forms taken together: \"profit = interest + profit of all kinds + ground-rent, its distribution between these particular categories being a matter of indifference.\" Whatever the split among claimants, the total is the same. The substantive sense is different. Some capitals, \"although invested in large productive enterprises, simply yield an interest, great or small, after all costs are deducted — so-called 'dividends'.\" Railways are the example. The crucial claim: \"These do not therefore enter into the equalization of the general rate of profit, since they yield a profit rate less than the average. If they did go in, the average rate would fall much lower.\" Here Marx isolates a systematic understatement of the tendency. Large fixed-capital enterprises, precisely because their c:v ratio is extreme, earn rates of profit below the social average; but because they are funded as joint-stock investments paying a fixed dividend, their low profitability is excluded from the pool over which equalisation operates. The observed general rate of profit is therefore biased upward relative to the true social average. Marx draws the consequence: \"From a theoretical point of view, it is possible to include them, and we should then obtain a profit rate lower than that which apparently exists and is really decisive for the capitalists, since it is precisely in these undertakings that the proportion of constant capital to variable is at its greatest.\" The final clause — \"really decisive for the capitalists\" — is disconcerting and deliberate: the rate that is statistically apparent differs from the rate that is theoretically true , and Marx acknowledges that the apparent rate, however biased, is the one that actually governs capitalist behaviour and expectations. Two facts about modern capitalism are thus identified in a single paragraph: the tendency of large-scale enterprise to fall out of the equalising mechanism, and the systematic divergence between measured and true average profitability. Both are consequences of the very development that produces the fall. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter's first and deepest lesson is the identity of a law with its own counter-forces . Marx does not treat the counteracting factors as exogenous shocks — better technology, cheaper imports, political deregulation — that happen to arrive and soften a pre-existing tendency. He treats them as emanations of the same productivity dynamic that generates the fall. Rising productivity cheapens constant capital and raises the rate of surplus-value (counteracting); and it raises c/v and displaces living labour (tendency-producing). The \"law\" is therefore not a simple vector but a self-inhibiting process. This is why Marx can insist simultaneously that the counteracting factors \"in the final instance... always accelerate it further\" and that they \"in part even paralyse it.\" The systemic principle here is that of the resultant : what is empirically observed is the net of opposed forces, and the theory's proper object is not the observed rate but the law that generates both the movement and its inhibition. The second principle is the non-identity of rate and mass , and the subordination of both to the value-composition of capital. The chapter demonstrates rigorously that the rate of surplus-value can rise while the mass of surplus-value falls (S = s′·v, with v/C declining); that the rate of profit cannot, however, fall without the mass of profit falling for a given total capital (since p′ = S/C); and that the tendential fall in p′ coexists with a tendential rise in s′. Both movements are expressions of growing productivity. This dissolves a persistent pseudo-problem — how a rising rate of exploitation can accompany a falling profit rate — by showing that it is not a paradox but a necessity of the categories themselves. The third lesson is methodological : theory precedes measurement. Marx's declaration that only once \"the relationships that form the rate of profit have been understood\" can statistics deliver \"genuine analyses of wage-rates\" is a general epistemic protocol. The flat dismissal of wage-push explanations of falling profitability — \"nothing is more absurd\" — is accompanied by the concession that such explanations may describe an \"exceptional case.\" Marx thus distinguishes the lawful from the episodic without denying the latter's existence. The chapter's broader implications radiate in three directions. For crisis theory , the fact that counteracting factors \"in the final instance accelerate\" the tendency integrates the long-run law with cyclical crisis. For the theory of the world market and imperialism , the foreign trade excursus supplies a fully internal Marxian account of unequal exchange, colonial super-profits, and the material basis of a privileged strata within the capitalist class — grounded in the labour theory of value and explicitly siding with Smith against Ricardo. The passage on \"labour of higher specific weight,\" the \"excess pocketed by a particular class,\" and the higher profit rates in the colonies is among the most structurally significant in Volume III. For the analysis of modern corporate capital , the sixth factor identifies in nuce the exclusion of large joint-stock enterprise from the equalisation process and the resulting upward bias of measured profitability — a phenomenon of obvious contemporary resonance in the era of gigafactories, utilities, and infrastructure funds. Finally, the chapter bequeaths a philosophical caution : the tendency \"is decisive only under certain particular circumstances and over long periods.\" Neither deterministic collapse nor empirical refutation follows from this. The law is a statement about the direction of gravity of a system that is perpetually being lifted by its own momentum. To confuse the tendency with a prediction is to repeat the error Marx diagnoses in classical political economy: mistaking the surface result for the underlying law — and thereby finding the fall, rather than its slowness, the mystery. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 13 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 15"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 15", "title": "Chapter 15", "subtitle": "Exposition of the Internal Contradictions of the Law", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 15/Chapter 15 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 14380, "read_time": "72 min read", "tags": ["Law of the Falling Rate of Profit (TRPF)", "Capital Accumulation & Crises"], "keywords": ["Internal Contradictions of the Law of Falling Profit", "Conflict between Expansion of Production and Valorization", "Overproduction of Capital and Surplus Capital", "Periodic Crises as Violent Restorations of Equilibrium", "Destruction of Capital Values in Crises", "Falling Profit Rate Driving Speculation and Credit Bubbles"], "text": "Chapter 15 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 14,418 words ⏱️ ~72 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation CHAPTER 15: DEVELOPMENT OF THE LAW'S INTERNAL CONTRADICTIONS — AN EXHAUSTIVE DECONSTRUCTION 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Core Thesis in One Sentence Chapter 15 of Capital , Volume 3 — \"Development of the Law's Internal Contradictions\" — argues that the tendential fall in the rate of profit, far from being an empirical curiosity or an accidental by-product of competition, is the necessary and periodically explosive expression of a structural contradiction built into the very concept of capital: the contradiction between capital's historically revolutionary drive toward the absolute development of the productive forces and its restricted purpose of valorizing already-existing capital value. Marx's objective in this chapter is not to restate the law of the falling rate of profit, which he had already derived in Chapters 13–14. His objective is to unfold the law's internal logic — to show how a purely quantitative tendency (a declining ratio of surplus-value to total capital advanced) generates, on the one hand, over-accumulation, surplus capital coexisting alongside surplus population, credit breakdown, and crisis; and, on the other hand, a set of recuperative mechanisms that re-establish the conditions of accumulation on an enlarged but ever more fragile scale. The transition from \"the law as such\" to \"the law's internal contradictions\" is therefore the transition from an abstract economic tendency to a theory of capitalist crisis and historical transitoriness. The Central Problem The chapter's central problem is why capitalism's most spectacular success — the relentless, geometric expansion of the productivity of social labour — systematically undermines capitalism's own incentive structure. The rate of profit is not merely one variable among many; it is, in Marx's phrase, \"the spur to capitalist production,\" the \"animating fire\" of the entire system: \"nothing is produced save what can be produced at a profit.\" Yet the very methods by which capital raises productivity — mechanization, rising organic composition, the substitution of dead for living labour — depress the rate of profit. Capital, in other words, is compelled by its own competitive logic to do precisely the thing that erodes its own motive force. The chapter's problem is to reconcile these two facts without dissolving them into an optimistic functionalism (as in Ricardo) or a Malthusian naturalism (as in the rent theory). The Intellectual Objective Marx's objective is threefold: First, to expose Ricardo's category error. Ricardo, though \"disquieted\" by the falling rate of profit, conflates the rate of profit with the rate of surplus-value, assumes a constant working day, and thereby mistakes a value-compositional phenomenon for a natural one. Ricardo seeks the barrier to capitalist production \"not in production but rather in nature (in the theory of rent).\" Marx insists that the barrier is endogenous to the capital relation. Second, to integrate the falling rate of profit into a general theory of over-accumulation and crisis. The chapter develops a rigorous account of what \"overproduction of capital\" means: not an excess of commodities relative to human needs, nor an excess relative to productive capacity in general, but an excess of capital that \"can function as capital\" — that is, that can exploit labour at a rate yielding the normal profit. This is the doctrine of absolute overproduction of capital , and the chapter establishes its arithmetic, its phenomenology (idle capital, devaluation, credit collapse), and its resolution (destruction, idling, and the forcible restoration of the conditions of profitability). Third, to establish the historical-transitory character of capitalism. Because the falling rate of profit reveals that capitalism's barrier is capital itself, the chapter provides the decisive theoretical proof that capitalism is not \"an absolute mode of production for the production of wealth\" but \"a relative mode of production\" — one \"corresponding to a specific and limited epoch in the development of the material conditions of production.\" Placement Within the Broader Theoretical Framework This chapter closes Part Three of Volume 3 (\"The Law of the Tendential Fall in the Rate of Profit\"), which began with Chapter 13's derivation of the law as such and passed through Chapter 14's enumeration of counteracting tendencies. Chapter 15 is the dialectical climax of that bloc. It is also the pivot on which the entire architecture of Volume 3 turns: having established the general rate of profit and its tendency to fall, the subsequent parts of the volume (commerce, interest, ground rent) will decompose this general profit into its specific forms — p₁, i, and r — under conditions where the aggregate s and the aggregate s/C move in opposite directions. Indeed, Section 1 of Chapter 15 already anticipates that decomposition with its algebraic treatment of p₁/C + i/C + r/C. Beyond Volume 3, the chapter also references Volume 1 — Part Eight (primitive accumulation) — and Volume 1, Chapter 15 (machinery), establishing a systematic continuity: the divorce of the conditions of labour from the producers, first established historically in primitive accumulation, becomes a permanent, self-reproducing process in accumulation, concentration, and centralization. The chapter thus connects the genesis of capital to its historical dissolution , and does so through the mechanism of the falling profit rate. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 Opening Recapitulation: Profit Rate as a Falsified Expression of Exploitation Marx opens the chapter with a compressed recapitulation of the essential result of Part Three. Two prior results are invoked. First, \"the profit rate always expresses the rate of surplus-value lower than it actually is.\" This is because the rate of profit is computed on the total capital advanced (c + v), whereas the rate of surplus-value is computed only on the variable capital (v). Formally: p′ = s / C = s / (c + v) = s′v / (c + v) = s′ / (1 + c/v) where p′ is the rate of profit, s′ the rate of surplus-value, C the total capital, c constant capital, v variable capital. So long as c > 0, we have p′ < s′; and p′ = s′ only if c = 0, \"i.e. if the total capital were laid out on wages.\" This is the first systematic obfuscation the capitalist form imposes on the underlying exploitation relation. The second result is more dynamic: \"even a rising rate of surplus-value tends to be expressed in a falling rate of profit.\" The derivative of p′ with respect to c/v is negative, so as the organic composition rises (c/v rises) — as it must under mechanization — p′ declines even when s′ rises. This is the \"double asymmetry\" that Marx has established: (i) exploitation is always understated; (ii) exploitation can be intensified while the appearance of profitability weakens . From this, Marx draws a crucial diagnostic rule. \"A falling rate of profit, then, expresses a falling rate of surplus-value only if the ratio between the value of the constant capital and the amount of labour-power that this sets in motion remains unchanged, or if this latter amount has risen in relation to the value of the constant capital.\" In other words: a fall in p′ is evidence of a fall in the exploitation rate only if the value-composition of capital is constant or falling. Absent that condition, the falling profit rate is not evidence of \"lazy\" or \"unexploited\" workers — it is evidence of the changing composition of capital. 2.2 Ricardo's Category Mistake and the Absolutization of Capitalism Marx then turns sharply against Ricardo. \"Ricardo, while claiming to be dealing with the rate of profit, actually deals only with the rate of surplus-value, and this only on the assumption that the working day is a constant magnitude, both intensively and extensively.\" Ricardo's law of the tendency is thus, in strict logical terms, not a law of the rate of profit at all: it is a law of what happens to profits when both the length and the intensity of the working day are frozen, and when the composition of capital is implicitly held in the background. Ricardo thereby commits a category error: he reads the surface form (p′) as if it directly expressed the deep structure (s′). But Marx's critique extends beyond methodology to the ideological function of the mistake. \"Economists like Ricardo, who take the capitalist mode of production as an absolute, feel here that this mode of production creates a barrier for itself and seek the source of this barrier not in production but rather in nature (in the theory of rent).\" This is decisive. Because Ricardo absolutizes capitalism as the natural, permanent form of production, any barrier that capitalism generates must be displaced onto something external to it. The theory of rent — diminishing returns of the soil, assumed fixed supplies of \"natural powers\" — is the ideological vehicle for this displacement. Marx's counter-move is to internalize the barrier: \"The important thing in their horror at the falling rate of profit is the feeling that the capitalist mode of production comes up against a barrier to the development of the productive forces which has nothing to do with the production of wealth as such; but this characteristic barrier in fact testifies to the restrictiveness and the solely historical and transitory character of the capitalist mode of production.\" Here is the chapter's first statement of its central dialectic. The falling profit rate is not evidence that wealth production has hit a natural limit; it is evidence that wealth production under capitalist social relations has hit a relational limit — a limit that \"bears witness that this is not an absolute mode of production for the production of wealth but actually comes into conflict at a certain stage with the latter's further development.\" 2.3 The Decomposition of Profit and the Rent Question: The Algebraic Demonstration Marx now introduces a more granular decomposition, crucial for the later parts of Volume 3. \"Of course, Ricardo and his school were considering only industrial profit, within which they included interest. Yet the rate of ground-rent also has a tendency to fall, even though its absolute mass grows and it may even grow in relation to industrial profit.\" Marx credits Edward West (in a footnote citing Essay on the Application of Capital to Land ..., by a Fellow of University College, Oxford, London, 1815) with having \"put forward the law of ground-rent before Ricardo.\" The analytical framework is then given: If we take the total social capital C, and call the industrial profit that remains after deducting interest and ground-rent p₁, interest i, and ground-rent r, then: p / C = s / C = (p₁ + i + r) / C = p₁/C + i/C + r/C Two things follow from this identity. First, because s grows steadily in absolute mass while C grows even more quickly, s/C steadily falls. Second, it is entirely possible for p₁, i, and r each in absolute mass to increase, while p₁/C, i/C, and r/C each as a ratio decline. \"It is no contradiction, therefore, that p₁, i and r may each increase even though p′ = s/C and its component parts p₁/C, i/C and r/C become ever smaller.\" The proportions among the three portions are, in Marx's phrase, \"simply a varying distribution of s under various headings.\" Thus any one of the three ratios may rise relative to the others while the general rate falls, \"the only requirement [being] that the sum of all three = s/C.\" 2.4 Numerical Illustration: From 50 Per Cent to 25 Per Cent Marx nails the point with a worked example, central to the chapter's methodological strategy of using arithmetic to dissolve apparent paradoxes: Suppose the rate of profit falls from 50 per cent to 25 per cent because, with a rate of surplus-value of 100 per cent, the composition of capital alters from 50c + 50v to 75c + 25v . In the first case, a capital of 1,000 yields a profit of 500 . In the second case, a capital of 4,000 yields a profit of 1,000 . So s (or p) has doubled while p′ has halved. Now consider the decomposition. Suppose that, out of the original 50 per cent rate, 20 per cent was industrial profit proper, 10 per cent interest, and 20 per cent rent . Then p₁/C = 20%, i/C = 10%, r/C = 20%. If the three components keep the same proportions after the aggregate falls to 25 per cent, we get p₁/C = 10%, i/C = 5%, r/C = 10%. But suppose instead that p₁/C falls to 8 per cent and i/C falls to 4 per cent. Then the remainder must accrue to rent: r/C = 13 per cent . The ratio of rent would have risen relative to both industrial profit and interest, \"but p′ would still have remained unchanged.\" And in both scenarios, the absolute sums accruing under all three headings grow, because total profit = 25% of 4,000 = 1,000 versus 50% of 1,000 = 500. \"On both assumptions the sum total of p₁, i and r would have risen, since this is now the product of a capital four times larger than before.\" The point is not that rent mechanically rises in inverse proportion to the profit rate. The point is that the algebraic space of possible redistributions among the three revenue forms is vast, and that all such redistributions are consistent with a falling general profit rate. This decouples the political economy of distributive class struggle (rentiers vs. industrialists) from the underlying tendency. 2.5 The Historical and Theoretical Falsity of Ricardo's Assumption Marx now unmasks Ricardo's premise as doubly false. \"Ricardo's assumption that industrial profit (plus interest) originally accounted for the entire surplus-value is both historically and theoretically false.\" Historically: rent predates the fully developed capitalist mode of production, appearing in pre-capitalist forms of landed appropriation. Theoretically: only with the progress of capitalist production does the industrial and commercial bourgeoisie appropriate the entire surplus-value in the first instance (later redistributing a portion to landlords and financiers), and only then can rent be reduced to \"the surplus over and above profit.\" But here Marx adds a dialectical twist: \"On this capitalist basis, rent then grows once more, as a portion of profit (i.e. of the surplus-value considered as product of the total capital), but not the specific portion of the product pocketed by the capitalist.\" Rent, in other words, undergoes a double movement — at first reduced to a mere deduction from profit, then increasing as an absolute magnitude within the profit category, but never recovering its pre-capitalist status as an independent and prior claim. 2.6 The Immediate Process and Its Two Acts: Production vs. Realization Marx then turns to what will become the analytical spine of the chapter: the distinction between the production of surplus-value and the realization of surplus-value. He begins by specifying the barriers internal to production itself . \"Assuming the necessary means of production, i.e. a sufficient accumulation of capital, the creation of surplus-value faces no other barrier than the working population, if the rate of surplus-value, i.e. the level of exploitation of labour, is given; and no other barrier than this level of exploitation, if the working population is given.\" The production process \"essentially consists of this production of surplus-value, represented in the surplus product or the aliquot portion of commodities produced in which unpaid labour is objectified.\" And this is \"the immediate purpose and the determining motive of capitalist production.\" Marx therefore rejects any depiction of capitalist production as consumption-driven: \"This would be to ignore completely its specific character, as this is expressed in its basic inner pattern.\" Now the crucial distinction. The extraction of surplus-value \"is the first act in the capitalist production process, and its completion only brings to an end the immediate production process itself.\" With the development of this process — as expressed in the falling profit rate — \"the mass of surplus-value thus produced swells to monstrous proportions.\" But the surplus has only been produced , not yet realized : \"Now comes the second act in the process. The total mass of commodities, the total product, must be sold, both that portion which replaces constant and variable capital and that which represents surplus-value.\" Here is the pivotal analytical move. If the sale is incomplete, partial, or effected only below the price of production, then \"although the worker is certainly exploited, his exploitation is not realized as such for the capitalist and may even not involve any realization of the surplus-value extracted... indeed, it may even mean a partial or complete loss of his capital.\" Hence: \"The conditions for immediate exploitation and for the realization of that exploitation are not identical. Not only are they separate in time and space, they are also separate in theory.\" Marx now specifies separate barriers : - The barrier to exploitation : \"restricted only by the society's productive forces.\" - The barrier to realization : \"the proportionality between the different branches of production and by the society's power of consumption.\" And the power of consumption itself is socially determined, not naturally: \"it is determined neither by the absolute power of production nor by the absolute power of consumption but rather by the power of consumption within a given framework of antagonistic conditions of distribution, which reduce the consumption of the vast majority of society to a minimum level, only capable of varying within more or less narrow limits.\" It is further restricted by \"the drive for accumulation, the drive to expand capital and produce surplus-value on a larger scale.\" 2.7 The Internal Contradiction Seeking External Resolution The drive for accumulation is \"the law governing capitalist production, arising from the constant revolutions in methods of production themselves, from the devaluation of the existing capital which is always associated with this, and from the general competitive struggle and the need to improve production and extend its scale, merely as a means of self-preservation, and on pain of going under.\" The result is that \"The market, therefore, must be continually extended, so that its relationships and the conditions governing them assume ever more the form of a natural law independent of the producers and become ever more uncontrollable.\" Marx's formula here is extraordinarily compact: \"The internal contradiction seeks resolution by extending the external field of production. But the more productivity develops, the more it comes into conflict with the narrow basis on which the relations of consumption rest.\" World-market expansion does not abolish the contradiction; it migrates it, reproducing it at a higher spatial scale. Consequently, \"It is in no way a contradiction, on this contradictory basis, that excess capital coexists with a growing surplus population; for although the mass of surplus-value produced would rise if these were brought together, yet this would equally heighten the contradiction between the conditions in which this surplus-value was produced and the conditions in which it was realized.\" 2.8 Accumulation as a Function of Mass and Impetus, Not of Rate A crucial quantitative point follows. \"Once a certain rate of profit is given, the mass of profit always depends on the magnitude of the capital advanced. But accumulation is then determined by the part of this mass that is transformed back into capital.\" This part equals profit minus capitalist revenue, so it depends both on the value of total profit and on the cheapness of the commodities the capitalist buys (consumption goods and constant capital, wages being taken as given). Marx then draws a critical conceptual distinction between the value the worker maintains and the value the worker adds : \"The mass of capital that the worker sets in motion, and whose value he maintains by his labour and makes reappear in the product, is completely different from the value that he adds.\" His two cases: Capital = 1,000; labour added = 100; capital reproduced = 1,100. Rate of profit = 10% . Capital = 100; labour added = 20; capital reproduced = 120. Rate of profit = 20% . \"Nevertheless, more can be accumulated out of 100 than out of 20.\" So: \"Thus the stream of capital (leaving aside its devaluation as the result of a rise in productivity), or its accumulation, flows on in proportion to the impetus that it already possesses and not in proportion to the rate of profit.\" This is the mass/rate distinction in its most concentrated form. Accumulation momentum is a function of prior magnitude , not of percentage yield . It follows that the falling rate of profit is compatible with, indeed systematically drives, an acceleration of accumulation in mass — which itself further raises the composition of capital and deepens the very tendency that produces the fall. 2.9 Radical Counter-Case: High Profit with Low Productivity Marx now presents the inverse case to prove that the rate of profit is not a direct measure of the exploitation or development of labour. \"It is possible to have a high rate of profit even if labour is unproductive, if this is based on a high rate of surplus-value and the working day is very long; this is possible where the workers' needs are very slight and the average wage very low.\" In such circumstances — thin needs, low wages, long hours, slow accumulation, stagnant population, labour-time-intensive production — the rate is high while the level of development is low. Conversely: \"The rate of profit does not fall because the worker is less exploited, but rather because less labour is generally applied in relation to the capital invested.\" This sentence is, in a sense, the theorem of the chapter in miniature: the fall is a compositional phenomenon, not an exploitation-intensity phenomenon. 2.10 Chalmers' Fantasy: Confusing Cause and Effect Because a rising mass of profit can accompany a falling rate, \"a greater part of the annual product of labour is appropriated by the capitalist under the heading of capital (as replacement for the capital used up) and a relatively smaller part is appropriated under the heading of profit.\" Marx here attacks \"the fantasy of Reverend Chalmers to the effect that the smaller the mass of the annual product the capitalists spend as capital, the greater the profits they pocket\" (footnote citing Thomas Chalmers, On Political Economy in Connexion with the Moral State and Moral Prospects of Society , 2nd edn, Glasgow, 1832, p. 88; and noting that in Theories of Surplus-Value , Part I, p. 290, Marx describes Chalmers as \"one of the most fanatic Malthusians,\" himself a cleric and Professor of Divinity at Glasgow University). Chalmers — abetted by the Established Church's promotion of unproductive consumption of the surplus product — \"confuses cause and effect\": the growth of profit mass is a consequence of enlarged capital, not a consequence of restricted consumption. And that growth \"brings about a simultaneous concentration of capital.\" 2.11 Concentration, Centralization, and the Machinery of Decapitalization Concentration — growth in the minimum scale of viable operation — leads to centralization, \"i.e. the swallowing-up of small capitalists by big, and their decapitalization.\" Marx interprets this as \"simply the divorce of the conditions of labour from the producers raised to a higher power, these smaller capitalists still counting among the producers, since their own labour still plays a role.\" A quantitative law follows: \"The work done by the capitalist, in general, stands in inverse proportion to the size of his capital, i.e. to the degree in which he is a capitalist.\" As the capitalist becomes a mere functionary of an alien thing, his personal labour contribution vanishes. The divorce between the conditions of labour and the producers \"forms the concept of capital, as this arises with primitive accumulation (Volume 1, Part Eight), subsequently appearing as a constant process in the accumulation and concentration of capital, before it is finally expressed here as the centralization of capitals already existing in a few hands, and the decapitalization of many.\" Finally, the counterweight: \"This process would entail the rapid breakdown of capitalist production, if counteracting tendencies were not constantly at work alongside this centripetal force, in the direction of decentralization.\" The chapter thus formally announces — though it does not here elaborate — the theory of counteracting tendencies that will appear as the systematic resolution mechanism in Section 3. 2.12 Section 2: The Double Form of Rising Productivity Section 2 — \"The Conflict Between the Extension of Production and Valorization\" — begins by restating the development of social productivity in two forms. First, \"in the size of the productive forces already produced, the scale of the conditions of production in both value and mass, in so far as these are the conditions for new production to take place, and in the absolute magnitude of the productive capital already accumulated.\" Second, \"in the relatively low proportion of capital, out of the total, that is laid out on wages, i.e. in the relatively small amount of living labour that is required to reproduce and valorize a given capital, and for mass production.\" And \"This presupposes at the same time the concentration of capital.\" As far as labour-power applied is concerned, productivity development again takes a double form: (a) \"an increase in surplus labour, i.e. a shortening of necessary labour-time, the time required for the reproduction of labour-power\"; (b) \"a decline in the total amount of labour-power (number of workers) applied to set a given capital in motion.\" These two movements \"not only go hand in hand; they mutually condition one another, and are phenomena that express the same law. But they affect the profit rate in opposite directions.\" This \"opposite directions\" thesis is then formalized. Since p′ = s / C, and total surplus-value is determined jointly by (i) the rate of surplus-value and (ii) the mass of labour applied at that rate — equivalent to the magnitude of variable capital — the two factors move in opposing directions: \"One of these factors, the rate of surplus-value, is rising; the other factor, the number of workers, is falling (relatively or absolutely).\" Productivity raises surplus-value by lifting s′, but reduces it by shrinking the multiplier (v) on which s′ operates. 2.13 The Limits of Compensation: The \"Living on Air\" Argument A logical limit follows. \"Two workers working for 12 hours a day could not supply the same surplus-value as 24 workers each working 2 hours, even if they were able to live on air and hence scarcely needed to work at all for themselves.\" The hours here differ (24 vs. 48) so the illustrative thrust is that mass of surplus-value ultimately requires mass of labour — the multiplier can be driven down only so far. Hence: \"In this connection, therefore, the compensation for the reduced number of workers provided by a rise in the level of exploitation of labour has certain limits that cannot be overstepped; this can certainly check the fall in the profit rate, but it cannot cancel it out.\" This is a crucial hedging clause: the rise in s′ is a counteracting tendency, not a cancelling one. The falling rate remains tendential in the strict sense. 2.14 Accumulation, Devaluation, and the Positive-Feedback Spiral Marx then formalizes the dynamic relationship between the accumulated magnitude of capital and the rate of growth. \"Once the rate is given, the absolute amount by which capital grows depends on its existing magnitude. But if this magnitude is given, the proportion in which it grows, i.e. its rate of growth, depends on the profit rate.\" A rise in productivity — which \"always goes hand in hand with devaluation of the existing capital\" — can increase the magnitude of capital only if it increases the part of annual profit converted back into capital by raising the profit rate. And this can occur (since productivity is not directly relevant to the value of existing capital) only in two ways: (i) by raising relative surplus-value, or (ii) by cheapening the elements of constant capital — that is, cheapening commodities entering labour-power reproduction or constant capital itself. But the sting: \"Both of these, however, involve a devaluation of the existing capital, and both go hand in hand with a reduction in the variable capital as against the constant. Both processes condition the fall in the profit rate, and both delay it.\" This is the dialectical knot. Productivity both causes and delays the fall — causes it by raising the composition of capital, delays it by devaluing existing capital and cheapening constant elements. The tendency and its counter-tendencies are not external to one another; they are the same process viewed from two sides. Marx then adds a demographic feedback: \"In so far, moreover, as the higher rate of profit gives rise to an increased demand for labour, it leads to an increase in the working population and hence in the exploitable material which is precisely what makes capital capital.\" 2.15 The Material Substratum: Use-Value Growth Under Constant Exchange-Value Marx now broadens the argument's ontological base. \"Indirectly, however, the development of labour productivity contributes to an increase in the existing capital value, since it increases the mass and diversity of use-values in which the same exchange-value is represented, and which form the material substratum, the objective elements of this capital.\" The same capital and labour, he notes, \"produce more things that can be transformed into capital, quite apart from exchange-value. These things can serve to absorb additional labour, and thus additional surplus labour also, and can in this way form additional capital.\" Crucially, \"The mass of labour that capital can command does not depend on its value but rather on the mass of raw and ancillary materials, of machinery and elements of fixed capital, and of means of subsistence, out of which it is composed, whatever their value may be.\" Because the mass of labour applied grows, \"the value of the capital reproduced and the surplus-value newly added to it grow as well.\" The result is that the material scale of accumulation can expand even when the value scale is falling — a distinction the Critique of Ricardo glosses over: \"Yet these two aspects involved in the accumulation process cannot just be considered as existing quietly side by side, which is how Ricardo treats them; they contain a contradiction.\" 2.16 The Four Contradictory Simultaneities and the Theory of Crisis The contradiction is then presented as a set of simultaneous opposed tendencies : Population: \"Simultaneously with impulses towards a genuine increase in the working population... we have those agencies that create a relative surplus population.\" Value and devaluation: \"Simultaneously with the fall in the profit rate, the mass of capital grows, and this is associated with a devaluation of the existing capital, which puts a stop to this fall and gives an accelerating impulse to the accumulation of capital value.\" Composition: \"Simultaneously with the development of productivity, the composition of capital becomes higher, there is a relative decline in the variable portion as against the constant.\" \"These various influences sometimes tend to exhibit themselves side by side, spatially; at other times one after the other, temporally; and at certain points the conflict of contending agencies breaks through in crises.\" And the definition of crisis follows, in one of the most quoted passages of Volume 3: \"Crises are never more than momentary, violent solutions for the existing contradictions, violent eruptions that re-establish the disturbed balance for the time being.\" Crisis is therefore constitutive , not accidental — it is the form in which a structural contradiction achieves temporary resolution. 2.17 The General Expression of the Contradiction: Absolute Development vs. Restricted Valorization Marx then articulates the contradiction \"in the most general terms\": \"The capitalist mode of production tends towards an absolute development of the productive forces irrespective of value and the surplus-value this contains, and even irrespective of the social relations within which capitalist production takes place; while on the other hand its purpose is to maintain the existing capital value and to valorize it to the utmost extent possible.\" The mode of production is \"directed towards using the existing capital value as a means for the greatest possible valorization of this value.\" Its methods \"involve a decline in the profit rate, the devaluation of the existing capital and the development of the productive forces of labour at the cost of the productive forces already produced.\" Consequences are drawn: - The \"periodical devaluation of the existing capital... disturbs the given conditions in which the circulation and reproduction process of capital takes place, and is therefore accompanied by sudden stoppages and crises in the production process.\" - The relative decline in variable capital \"gives a spur to the growth of the working population, while it continuously creates an artificial surplus population as well.\" - \"The accumulation of capital, from the point of view of value, is slowed down by the falling rate of profit, which then serves yet again to accelerate the accumulation of use-value, while this in turn accelerates the course of accumulation in terms of value.\" - \"Capitalist production constantly strives to overcome these immanent barriers, but it overcomes them only by means that set up the barriers afresh and on a more powerful scale.\" The closing formula of the section is: \"The true barrier to capitalist production is capital itself.\" Capital and its self-valorization are \"the starting and finishing point... the motive and purpose of production; production is production only for capital, and not the reverse, i.e. the means of production are not simply means for a steadily expanding pattern of life for the society of the producers.\" The section ends with the historic-mission framing: \"If the capitalist mode of production is therefore a historical means for developing the material powers of production and for creating a corresponding world market, it is at the same time the constant contradiction between this historical task and the social relations of production corresponding to it.\" 2.18 Section 3: The Rising Minimum Capital and the Plethora of Capital Section 3 — \"Surplus Capital Alongside Surplus Population\" — opens with the mechanism by which the falling rate transforms the structural landscape. \"As the profit rate falls, so there is a growth in the minimum capital that the individual capitalist needs in order to make productive use of labour.\" This is a two-sided necessity: minimum capital is required both (i) to extract surplus-value at all (to hire sufficiently many workers) and (ii) to ensure that \"the labour-time spent on the production of commodities is necessary labour-time and does not overstep the average labour-time that is socially necessary\" — i.e. to remain competitive with the social average. Concentration follows: \"since beyond certain limits a large capital with a lower rate of profit accumulates more quickly than a small capital with a higher rate of profit.\" And \"This growing concentration leads in turn, at a certain level, to a new fall in the rate of profit\" — a positive-feedback loop. Small capitals are then driven into speculative channels: \"The mass of small fragmented capitals are thereby forced onto adventurous paths: speculation, credit swindles, share swindles, crises.\" The famous definition follows: \"The so-called plethora of capital is always basically reducible to a plethora of that capital for which the fall in the profit rate is not outweighed by its mass — and this is always the case with fresh offshoots of capital that are newly formed — or to the plethora in which these capitals, which are incapable of acting by themselves, are available to the leaders of great branches of business in the form of credit.\" Key implication: the \"plethora\" is not a natural fact but a ratio , defined against profitability. And \"This plethora of capital arises from the same causes that produce a relative surplus population and is therefore a phenomenon that complements this latter, even though the two things stand at opposite poles — unoccupied capital on the one hand and an unemployed working population on the other.\" 2.19 Absolute Overproduction of Capital: Definition and Arithmetic Marx now poses the central question of the section. \"Overproduction of capital and not of individual commodities... is nothing more than over-accumulation of capital. To understand what this over-accumulation is..., we have only to take it as an absolute.\" He then defines: \"There would be an absolute overproduction of capital as soon as no further additional capital could be employed for the purpose of capitalist production.\" Conditions are spelled out: capital has grown in such proportion to the working population that neither the absolute labour-time supplied nor the relative surplus labour-time can be extended — the latter being impossible \"in a situation where the demand for labour was so strong, and there was thus a tendency for wages to rise.\" In that case: \"the expanded capital produces only the same mass of surplus-value as before.\" Formally, C + ΔC will not produce any more profit — or will produce less — than the capital C did before its increase by ΔC. \"there would even be a sharper and more sudden fall in the general rate of profit, but this time on account of a change in the composition of capital which would not be due to a development in productivity, but rather to a rise in the money value of the variable capital on account of higher wages and to a corresponding decline in the proportion of surplus labour to necessary labour.\" In actual manifestation: one portion of capital lies idle (since it would first have to expel capital already functioning to be valorized), the other is valorized at a lower rate. The fall in the profit rate is now accompanied by an absolute decline in the mass of profit (since labour-power applied and the rate of surplus-value are unchanged, so mass of surplus-value is unchanged), and this reduced mass must be calculated on an enlarged total capital. The arithmetic illustration: \"If a total capital of 1,000 yields a profit of 100 and after being increased to 1,500 it still yields a profit of only 100, then in the second case 1,000 yields only 66⅔.\" So the valorization of the old capital suffers \"an absolute decline\": under the new conditions the capital of 1,000 would \"not yield more than a capital of 666⅔ did earlier.\" 2.20 The Competitive Struggle and the \"War of Enemy Brothers\" Such devaluation \"would not take place without a struggle,\" and ΔC \"could not function as capital without a struggle.\" Marx is emphatic that competition here is a consequence of the underlying condition, not its cause: \"That competition which results from the overproduction of capital would not cause a fall in the rate of profit. Rather the reverse.\" The two spring from the same situation, and competition is unleashed as a form of the resolution process. Three strategic behaviours are distinguished: 1. Existing capitalists let ΔC (already in their hands) lie idle, to avoid self-devaluing their original capital. 2. Or they \"apply it so as to shift the idleness of the additional capital onto the more recent interlopers and onto their competitors in general, even at a temporary loss.\" 3. New hands attempt to place ΔC at the expense of old capital, displacing it from its former field. The result: \"one part of the old capital would have to lie idle as far as its property as capital was concerned\" — that is, as the property of functioning and being valorized. The distribution of idleness is decided in the competitive struggle. And this transforms competition from freemasonry into fratricide: \"As long as everything goes well, competition acts, as is always the case when the general rate of profit is settled, as a practical freemasonry of the capitalist class, so that they all share in the common booty in proportion to the size of the portion that each puts in. But as soon as it is no longer a question of division of profit, but rather of loss, each seeks as far as he can to restrict his own share of this loss and pass it on to someone else... competition now becomes a struggle of enemy brothers.\" Marx draws the socio-theoretical moral: \"The opposition between the interest of each individual capitalist and that of the capitalist class as a whole now comes into its own, in the same way as competition was previously the instrument through which the identity of the capitalists' interests was asserted.\" 2.21 Resolution Through Idling, Destruction, and Devaluation \"How then is this conflict to be resolved?\" Marx's answer is uncompromising: the resolution \"involves this, that capital should lie idle, or even, in part, be destroyed, either to the entire value of the additional capital ΔC or at least to one part of this.\" The distribution of the loss is uneven : \"one capital lies idle, another is destroyed, a third experiences only a relative loss or simply a temporary devaluation, and so on.\" Moreover, the destruction is not merely of value but of material substance. \"Part of the means of production, fixed and circulating capital, will not function and operate as capital, and a part of the productive effort that was begun will come to a halt.\" Though time damages all means of production (except the land), what occurs in crisis is \"a far more intense actual destruction of means of production as the result of a stagnation in their function\" — the chief effect being that these means of production \"cease to be active as means of production.\" Marx then ranks the destructiveness of these shocks by their form : \"The chief disruption, and the one possessing the sharpest character, would occur in connection with capital in so far as it possesses the property of value, i.e. in connection with capital values.\" He enumerates the mechanisms: - The portion of capital value existing as claims on future surplus-value (promissory notes on production) is devalued along with the revenues on which it is reckoned. - A portion of ready gold and silver lies idle. - Commodities can complete their circulation and reproduction only at vastly reduced prices — a devaluation of the capital they represent. - Fixed capital elements are similarly devalued. - Because the reproduction process presupposes certain price relationships, their general collapse throws the process into \"stagnation and confusion.\" - This paralyses money as a means of payment (a function dependent on those presupposed price relationships). - \"The chain of payment obligations at specific dates is broken in a hundred places,\" aggravated by a breakdown of the credit system. - Conclusion: \"All this therefore leads to violent and acute crises, sudden forcible devaluations, an actual stagnation and disruption in the reproduction process, and hence to an actual decline in reproduction.\" 2.22 The Recuperative Agencies and the Circular Return But \"other agencies come into play at the same time.\" Marx enumerates four restoration mechanisms: Wage depression via unemployment. Stagnation makes part of the working class idle, forcing employed workers to accept wages below average: \"an operation that has exactly the same effect for capital as if relative or absolute surplus-value had been increased while wages remained at the average.\" Demographic expansion. Prosperity \"facilitate[s] marriage among the workers and reduce[s] the decimation of their offspring,\" which, even without increasing the population actually working, \"ha[s] the same effect on the relationship between the workers and capital as if the number of workers actually active had increased.\" Technological intensification. \"The fall in prices and the competitive struggle... impel each capitalist to reduce the individual value of his total product below its general value by employing new machinery, new and improved methods of labour and new forms of combination\" — that is, to raise productivity, reduce the ratio of variable to constant capital, dismiss workers, and \"create an artificial surplus population.\" Cheapening of constant capital. \"The devaluation of the elements of constant capital, moreover, itself involves a rise in the profit rate. The mass of constant capital applied grows as against the variable, but the value of this mass may have fallen.\" The net effect: \"The stagnation in production that has intervened prepares the ground for a later expansion of production — within the capitalist limits. And so we go round the whole circle once again. One part of the capital that was devalued by the cessation of its function now regains its old value. And apart from that, with expanded conditions of production, a wider market and increased productivity, the same cycle of errors is pursued once more.\" The \"cycle of errors\" — the phrasing is deliberate: the process is objectively cyclical, and each cycle is a repetition of a structurally identical error, not its correction. 2.23 Overproduction of Capital Is Not Overproduction of Wealth Marx now carefully delimits the concept. \"Even under the most extreme assumption that might be made, absolute overproduction of capital is not absolute overproduction in general, not absolute overproduction of the means of production. It is an overproduction of means of production only in so far as these function as capital, and hence have to produce an additional value in proportion to their value that has expanded together with their mass, i.e. have to valorize their value.\" It is overproduction nonetheless, \"since the capital is unable to exploit labour at the level of exploitation that is required by the 'healthy' and 'normal' development of the capitalist production process, at a level of exploitation that at least increases the mass of profit along with the growing mass of capital applied; that therefore excludes a situation in which the rate of profit falls to the same degree as capital grows, or even falls more quickly than this.\" So \"Overproduction of capital never means anything other than overproduction of means of production — means of labour and means of subsistence — that can function as capital, i.e. can be applied to exploiting labour at a given level of exploitation.\" The \"given level\" is critical because \"a fall in the level of exploitation below a certain point produces disruption and stagnation in the capitalist production process, crisis, and the destruction of capital.\" \"It is no contradiction that this overproduction of capital is accompanied by a greater or smaller relative surplus population.\" The same causes that raised productivity, expanded markets, accelerated accumulation in mass and value, and lowered the profit rate, have also produced a relative surplus population — workers \"who are not employed by this excess capital on account of the low level of exploitation of labour at which they would have to be employed, or at least on account of the low rate of profit they would yield at the given rate of exploitation.\" 2.24 Capital Export Marx then addresses a common counter-argument: capital is not really surplus if it can be exported. His reply: \"If capital is sent abroad, this is not because it absolutely could not be employed at home. It is rather because it can be employed abroad at a higher rate of profit. But this capital is absolutely surplus capital for the employed working population and for the country in question. It exists as such alongside the relative surplus population, and this is an example of how the two things exist side by side and reciprocally condition one another.\" Export is not refutation; it is confirmation, since it shows capital's mobility is constrained by relative profitability , not by any physical limit. 2.25 The Fall in the Rate Provokes Competition, Not Vice Versa Marx restates the direction of causation. \"Compensation for the fall in the profit rate by an increase in the mass of profit is possible only for the total social capital and for the big capitalists who are already established. New and independently operating additional capital finds no compensatory conditions of this kind ready made; it must first acquire them, and so it is the fall in the profit rate that provokes the competitive struggle between capitals, and not the reverse.\" This inverts the vulgar supposition that competition produces the falling rate; for Marx, the falling rate is the underlying tendency, and competition is one of its surface manifestations. He adds: \"This competitive struggle, moreover, is accompanied by a temporary rise in wages and a further temporary fall in the profit rate, deriving from this.\" 2.26 The Polemic on Overproduction of Commodities Marx now takes aim at a characteristic bourgeois evasion. \"Since capital's purpose is not the satisfaction of needs but the production of profit, and since it attains this purpose only by methods that determine the mass of production by reference exclusively to the yardstick of production, and not the reverse, there must be a constant tension between the restricted dimensions of consumption on the capitalist basis, and a production that is constantly striving to overcome these immanent barriers.\" Because \"capital consists of commodities, and hence overproduction of capital involves overproduction of commodities,\" there is the \"singular phenomenon that the same economists who deny overproduction of commodities admit overproduction of capital.\" He then dismantles three standard defences: - \"No general overproduction — only disproportion between branches.\" Marx replies: this \"means nothing more than that, within capitalist production, the proportionality of the particular branches of production presents itself as a process of passing constantly out of and into disproportionality.\" Because social interconnection imposes itself \"as a blind law, and not as a law which, being grasped and therefore mastered by their combined reason, brings the productive process under their common control.\" - \"Overproduction is only relative.\" \"This is completely correct; but the whole capitalist mode of production is precisely such a relative mode of production, whose barriers are not absolute, but only absolute for it, on its basis.\" - \"The capitalists have only to exchange and consume their commodities among themselves.\" \"Then the whole character of capitalist production is forgotten... what is involved is the valorization of capital, not its consumption.\" The cumulative diagnosis: the objection that \"the barriers to capitalist production are not barriers to production in general\" is true but irrelevant : \"the contradiction in this capitalist mode of production consists precisely in its tendency towards the absolute development of productive forces that come into continuous conflict with the specific conditions of production in which capital moves, and can alone move.\" 2.27 The \"Not Too Many / Too Few\" Triptych Marx then delivers his devastating inversion of common-sense Malthusianism: \"It is not that too many means of subsistence are produced in relation to the existing population. On the contrary. Too little is produced to satisfy the mass of the population in an adequate and humane way.\" \"Nor are too many means of production produced to employ the potential working population.\" Rather, the system produces (i) \"too great a section of the population which is in fact incapable of work\" — Marx's footnote clarifies this means those dependent on the labour of others or on work that \"can only count as such within a miserable mode of production,\" with the reference most probably to petty commodity production, especially smallholding peasants; and (ii) produces \"not enough means of production... to allow the whole potential working population to work under the most productive conditions.\" \"Periodically, however, too much is produced in the way of means of labour and means of subsistence, too much to function as means for exploiting the workers at a given rate of profit.\" Too many commodities are produced for the value and surplus-value they contain to be realized and reconverted into capital without \"ever-recurrent explosions.\" The final aphorism: \"It is not that too much wealth is produced. But from time to time, too much wealth is produced in its capitalist, antagonistic forms.\" 2.28 The Two Great Barriers Enumerated Marx condenses the entire analysis into two barriers: (1) \"in the way that the development of labour productivity involves a law, in the form of the falling rate of profit, that at a certain point confronts this development itself in a most hostile way and has constantly to be overcome by way of crises.\" (2) \"in the way that it is the appropriation of unpaid labour, and the proportion between this unpaid labour and objectified labour in general — to put it in capitalist terms, profit and the proportion between this profit and the capital applied, i.e. a certain rate of profit — it is this that determines the expansion or contraction of production, instead of the proportion between production and social needs, the needs of socially developed human beings.\" \"Barriers to production, therefore, arise already at a level of expansion which appears completely inadequate from the other standpoint. Production comes to a standstill not at the point where needs are satisfied, but rather where the production and realization of profit impose this.\" 2.29 Exertions and Swindles Under the Falling Rate \"If the rate of profit falls,\" Marx notes, two forms of desperate response emerge. On one side, genuine efficiency drives: \"the individual capitalist drives down the individual value of his own particular commodities below their average social value, by using better methods, etc., and thus makes a surplus profit at the given market price.\" On the other, \"swindling and general promotion of swindling, through desperate attempts in the way of new methods of production, new capital investments and new adventures, to secure some kind of extra profit, which will be independent of the general average and superior to it.\" This anticipates Section 4's discussion of speculation among \"fragmented capitals.\" 2.30 The \"Animating Fire\" and the Assessment of Ricardo \"The rate of profit, i.e. the relative growth in capital, is particularly important for all new off-shoots of capital that organize themselves independently.\" Marx adds the following vivid claim: \"And if capital formation were to fall exclusively into the hands of a few existing big capitals, for whom the mass of profit outweighs the rate, the animating fire of production would be totally extinguished. It would die out.\" This is a systemic claim: the rate of profit has functional significance as the incentive mechanism that animates new investment. Diminishing it to insignificance would not merely redistribute income; it would extinguish the motive force of the system. \"It is the rate of profit that is the driving force in capitalist production, and nothing is produced save what can be produced at a profit. Hence the concern of the English economists over the decline in the profit rate.\" Marx then offers a famously generous, dialectical appraisal of Ricardo: \"If Ricardo is disquieted even by the very possibility of this, that precisely shows his deep understanding of the conditions of capitalist production. What other people reproach him for, i.e. that he is unconcerned with 'human beings' and concentrates exclusively on the development of the productive forces when considering capitalist production — whatever sacrifices of human beings and capital values this is bought with — is precisely his significant contribution.\" For Marx, Ricardo's indifference to human suffering is the very index of his scientific seriousness with respect to the laws of the system; the economist who weeps for individuals misses the historic mission: \"The development of the productive forces of social labour is capital's historic mission and justification. For that very reason, it unwittingly creates the material conditions for a higher form of production.\" \"What disturbs Ricardo is the way that the rate of profit, which is the stimulus of capitalist production and both the condition for and the driving force in accumulation, is endangered by the development of production itself. And the quantitative relation is everything here. In actual fact, the underlying reason is something deeper, about which he has no more than a suspicion.\" What is visible here, \"from the bourgeois standpoint, within the limits of capitalist understanding, from the standpoint of capitalist production itself, are its barriers, its relativity, the fact that it is not an absolute but only a historical mode of production, corresponding to a specific and limited epoch in the development of the material conditions of production.\" 2.31 Section 4: Supplementary Remarks — Uneven Development and Natural Conditions Section 4 opens with a methodological caveat on the heterogeneity of the tendency. \"Since the development of labour productivity is far from uniform in the various branches of industry and, besides being uneven in degree, often takes place in opposite directions, it so happens that the mass of average profit (= surplus-value) is necessarily very far below the level one would expect simply from the development of productivity in the most advanced branches.\" Moreover, uneven and often opposite trajectories \"do not arise simply from the anarchy of competition and the specific features of the bourgeois mode of production\" — they are also natural: \"The productivity of labour is also tied up with natural conditions, which are often less favourable as productivity rises — as far as that depends on social conditions.\" Hence \"progress here, regression there,\" illustrated by the influence of the seasons on raw materials and by \"the exhaustion of forests, coal and iron mines, and so on.\" 2.32 Fixed vs. Circulating Constant Capital and the Value of the Individual Commodity Marx next distinguishes the trajectories of circulating and fixed constant capital. \"If the circulating part of the constant capital (raw material, etc.) steadily grows in mass together with the productivity of labour, this is not the case for the fixed capital — buildings, machinery, lighting and heating installations, and so on. Even though these become dearer in absolute terms as the physical mass of the machinery grows, they become relatively cheaper.\" Illustration: \"If five workers produce ten times as many commodities as before, this does not mean that the outlay on fixed capital increases ten-fold.\" He insists on a distinction within the composition relation itself, one he has \"already emphasized several times\": the constant/variable ratio as it is expressed in the fall in the profit rate is not identical to the same ratio as it presents itself with respect to the individual commodity and its price. This distinction is then worked out in the parenthetical passage. 2.33 The Parenthetical Excursus: Value, Living and Past Labour Marx begins with a fundamental premise. \"The value of a commodity is determined by the total labour-time contained in it, both past and living. The rise in labour productivity consists precisely in the fact that the share of living labour is reduced and that of past labour increased, but in such a way that the total sum of labour contained in the commodity declines; in other words the living labour declines by more than the past labour increases.\" Past labour embodied in value = wear and tear of fixed capital + circulating constant capital (raw and ancillary materials). \"The portion of value deriving from raw and ancillary materials must fall with the [rising] productivity of labour, since, as far as these materials go, this productivity is precisely expressed in the fact that their value has fallen.\" But \"it is precisely a characteristic of rising labour productivity that the fixed portion of the constant capital should experience a very sharp increase, and with this also the portion of value that it transfers to the commodities as wear and tear.\" Hence the criterion for genuine productivity advance: \"For a new method of production to prove itself as a genuine advance in productivity, it must transfer a smaller additional share of value to the individual commodity for depreciation of the fixed capital than the portion of value that is deducted because less living labour is spared; it must in other words reduce the value of the commodity.\" Even in cases where an additional portion of value is added (more or dearer raw/ancillary materials), \"All this additional value must be more than outweighed by the reduction in value that arises from the decrease in living labour.\" Marx notes: \"This reduction in the total quantity of labour going into the commodity appears accordingly as the fundamental characteristic of a rise in labour productivity, irrespective of the social conditions under which production is carried on. In a society where the producers govern their production by a plan drawn up in advance, or even in simple commodity production, the productivity of labour is in fact invariably measured by such a standard. But what is the situation in capitalist production?\" 2.34 The Machine Example: The Capitalist's Calculus and the New Contradiction The parenthetical continues with the chapter's most technically precise numerical illustration. Suppose a branch of capitalist production with the average social composition , so that its production price coincides with value: Initial state (per item): - Depreciation of fixed capital: ½ shilling - Raw and ancillary materials: 17½ shillings - Wages: 2 shillings - Rate of surplus-value: 100% , so surplus-value: 2 shillings - Total value: 22 shillings - Cost price: ½ + 17½ + 2 = 20 shillings - Profit rate: 2/20 = 10 per cent - Production price = value = 22 shillings Now let a machine be invented that (a) halves the living labour per item and (b) triples the share of value attributable to depreciation of fixed capital. New state (per item): - Depreciation: 1½ shillings (3 × ½) - Raw and ancillary materials: 17½ shillings (unchanged) - Wages: 1 shilling (halved) - Surplus-value: 1 shilling (100% of wages) - Total value: 21 shillings — a full shilling reduction. So \"The value of the commodity has now fallen by 1 shilling; the new machine has definitely raised the productivity of labour.\" But for the capitalist : cost price = 1½ + 17½ + 1 = 20 shillings — \"the same as before.\" Since the general rate of profit is unchanged, he keeps 10 per cent on his cost price = 2 shillings . So the price of production is unchanged at 22 shillings, though this is now 1 shilling above the value . Marx's conclusion: \"For a society producing under capitalist conditions, the commodity has not become cheaper and the new machine is not an improvement. The capitalist, therefore, has no interest in introducing the new machine. And since introducing it would also make his old machinery simply worthless, when it has not yet worn out, transforming it into nothing more than scrap-iron, so that he would actually suffer a positive loss, he refrains from what would be, for him, a piece of utopian stupidity.\" The theoretical generalization follows: \"For capital, therefore, the law of increased productivity of labour is not unconditionally valid. For capital, this productivity is not raised simply because more living labour in general is spared than is added in past labour, but only if more of the paid part of living labour is spared, as we have already indicated in brief in Volume I, Chapter 15, pp. 515 ff.\" And here is the new contradiction the parenthetical announces: \"At this point the capitalist mode of production falls into a new contradiction. Its historical mission is ruthlessly to expand the productivity of human labour, to drive it onwards in geometrical progression. It is untrue to its mission as soon as it starts to inhibit the development of productivity, as it does here. It thereby simply shows once more that it is becoming senile and has further and further outlived its epoch.\" Engels' footnote 37 specifies: \"I have put the above in parentheses because, although it is re-edited from a note in the original manuscript, it goes beyond the original material in certain particulars. — F.E.\" 2.35 Competition, Minimum Capital, and the Fate of Small Capitals \"In competition, the rising minimum amount of capital needed for the successful pursuit of an independent industrial business takes the following form, as productivity increases. Once the new and more expensive equipment has been generally introduced, smaller capitals are in future excluded from this line of business. Only when mechanical inventions in the various spheres of production are in their infancy can smaller capitals function independently.\" At the other pole, \"Very large undertakings... where the proportion of constant capital is extraordinarily high, such as railways, do not yield the average profit rate, but only a portion of this, an interest. If this were not so the general rate of profit would fall still lower. And yet a great accumulation of capital in the form of shares finds a direct field of employment here.\" This anticipates the later theory of fictitious capital: railways (and analogous enterprises) bear comparison to interest-bearing capital rather than productive capital, and the share form is the vehicle by which mass accumulation proceeds in these spheres. 2.36 Growth Without Organic Change: A Partial Counter-Tendency Marx qualifies the transition from accumulation to falling rate. \"Growth of capital, i.e. accumulation of capital, involves a reduction in the rate of profit only in so far as this growth brings with it those changes in the ratio between the organic components of capital that were considered above.\" Crucially: \"Yet despite the constant and daily transformations in the mode of production, a greater or smaller part of this total capital, now this, now that, continues to accumulate for a certain period of time on the basis of a given average ratio of these components, so that its growth does not involve any organic change and is thus no cause for a fall in the rate of profit.\" \"This constant enlargement of the capital, and therefore also an expansion in production on the basis of the old methods which goes smoothly forward while new methods are already introduced alongside, is a further reason why the rate of profit does not decline in the same measure as the total social capital grows.\" This is a significant hedge: the tendency operates unevenly across sectors and time , so that the empirical fall is always slower than a purely compositional projection would predict. 2.37 The Working Population and the Absolute Barrier of Surplus Labour-Time \"The increase in the absolute number of workers, despite the relative decline in the variable capital laid out on wages, does not take place in all branches of production and does not take place evenly in the branches where it does. In agriculture, the decline in the element of living labour may be absolute.\" Nonetheless: \"It is simply the needs of the capitalist mode of production... that lead the number of wage-labourers to increase absolutely, despite this relative decline. As far as this mode of production is concerned, labour-power is superfluous the moment it is no longer necessary to occupy it for 12 to 15 hours per day.\" A development in the productive forces that reduced the absolute number of workers and enabled \"the whole nation to accomplish its entire production in a shorter period of time, would produce a revolution, since it would put the majority of the population out of action.\" Hence: \"Here we have once again the characteristic barrier to capitalist production... it is in no way an absolute form for the development of the productive forces and the creation of wealth, but rather comes into conflict with this at a certain point in its development.\" \"One aspect of this conflict is presented by the periodic crises that arise when one or another section of the working population is made superfluous in its old employment.\" And the signature aphorism: \"The barrier to capitalist production is the surplus time of the workers. The absolute spare time that the society gains is immaterial to capitalist production. The development of productivity is only important to it in so far as it increases the surplus labour-time of the working class and does not just reduce the labour-time needed for material production in general; in this way it moves in a contradiction.\" This is one of the chapter's most compressed theoretical statements: capitalism's criterion is not the reduction of socially necessary labour-time but its conversion into surplus labour-time. Absolute gains in spare time — the genuine measure of civilizational progress on any non-capitalist standard — are irrelevant. 2.38 Capital as Alienated Social Power \"We have seen how the growing accumulation of capital involves its growing concentration. Thus the power of capital grows, in other words the autonomy of the social conditions of production, as personified by the capitalist, is asserted more and more as against the actual producers.\" The famous passage follows: \"Capital shows itself more and more to be a social power, with the capitalist as its functionary — a power that no longer stands in any possible kind of relationship to what the work of one particular individual can create, but an alienated social power which has gained an autonomous position and confronts society as a thing, and as the power that the capitalist has through this thing.\" The dialectic then unfolds: \"The contradiction between the general social power into which capital has developed and the private power of the individual capitalists over these social conditions of production develops ever more blatantly, while this development also contains the solution to this situation, in that it simultaneously raises the conditions of production into general, communal, social conditions. This transformation is brought about by the development of the productive forces under capitalist production and by the manner and form in which this development is accomplished.\" This is the chapter's most explicit statement that the material preconditions of socialism are generated by capitalist development itself, not least by the socialization of production that accompanies its concentration. 2.39 The Mechanism of Innovation: Why Capitalists Resist What They Ultimately Must Do The next block (after an asterisk, marking it as a re-edited passage) provides the micro-logic of productivity advance under the falling rate: \"No capitalist voluntarily applies a new method of production, no matter how much more productive it may be or how much it might raise the rate of surplus-value, if it reduces the rate of profit.\" The mechanism by which innovations are nevertheless introduced relies on temporary surplus profit . \"But every new method of production of this kind makes commodities cheaper. At first, therefore, he can sell them above their price of production, perhaps above their value. He pockets the difference between their costs of production and the market price of the other commodities, which are produced at higher production costs. This is possible because the average socially necessary labour-time required to produce these latter commodities is greater than the labour-time required with the new method of production. His production procedure is ahead of the social average.\" Then competition universalizes: \"But competition makes the new procedure universal and subjects it to the general law. A fall in the profit rate then ensues — firstly perhaps in this sphere of production, and subsequently equalized with the others — a fall that is completely independent of the capitalists' will.\" This reconciles the individual rationality (adopt an innovation yielding temporary surplus profit) with the collective consequence (falling general rate). The capitalist is a rational prisoner of the system: no individual can resist adopting an innovation that yields a surplus profit, even though the resulting general fall in the rate erodes all of them. 2.40 The Law Prevails Even in Branches Producing No Wage Goods Marx extends the argument beyond the branches directly affecting labour-power reproduction: \"the same law prevails even in those spheres of production whose products do not enter either directly or indirectly into the workers' consumption, or into the conditions of production of their means of subsistence.\" There, no cheapening can raise relative surplus-value or make labour-power cheaper. Nonetheless: \"(In fact, a cheapening of constant capital in any of these branches may increase the profit rate, if the level of exploitation of labour remains the same.) As soon as the new mode of production begins to spread, giving actual proof that these commodities can be produced more cheaply, then those capitalists who operate under the old conditions of production must sell their product below its full price of production; the value of this commodity has fallen, so that they need more labour-time to produce it than is socially necessary. In short, and this appears as the effect of competition, they must also introduce the new mode of production which reduces the ratio of variable capital to constant.\" The condition of competitive survival — not the cheapening of wage goods — forces the raising of the composition of capital in all branches. This is a crucial generalization: the falling rate of profit is not merely a function of the wage-goods department. 2.41 Machinery and Depreciation \"The application of machinery reduces the price of the commodities produced with that machinery owing to various factors, which can always be reduced to the decline in the quantity of labour absorbed by each individual commodity; but in addition to this there is the decline in the portion of value that goes into the individual commodity as the depreciation element of the machinery. The slower the machinery's depreciation, the more commodities it is distributed over, the more living labour it replaces before the day when its reproduction falls due. In both cases the quantity and value of the fixed constant capital are increased as against the variable.\" This restates the double mechanism: machinery cheapens commodities both by absorbing less living labour and by distributing its depreciation component over more units of output. But both effects presuppose — and intensify — the rise of fixed constant capital relative to variable capital, i.e. the very process that drives the profit rate down. 2.42 Richard Jones and the \"Inducements and Faculties to Accumulate\" Marx concludes the chapter with an appreciative engagement with Richard Jones. He quotes Jones: \"All other things being equal, the power of a nation to save from its profits varies with the rate of profits, is great when they are high, less, when low; but as the rate of profit declines, all other things do not remain equal... A low rate of profit is ordinarily accompanied by a rapid rate of accumulation, relatively to the numbers of the people, as in England... a high rate of profit by a lower rate of accumulation, relatively to the numbers of the people.\" Examples of the latter: \"Poland, Russia, India, etc.\" The source is cited in a footnote as Richard Jones, An Introductory Lecture on Political Economy , London, 1833, pp. 50 ff. Marx endorses Jones: \"Jones is right to stress that, despite the falling rate of profit, the 'inducements and faculties to accumulate' increase.\" He then supplies six reasons: \"on account of the growing relative surplus population\" — the reserve army cheapens labour-power. \"because as the productivity of labour grows, so does the mass of use-values represented by the same exchange-value, i.e. the material elements of capital\" — the use-value substratum of accumulation expands. \"because of the increasing diversity of branches of production\" — new departments of investment. \"through the development of the credit system, joint-stock companies, etc., and the ease with which the possessor of money can now transform it into capital without having to become an industrial capitalist\" — the separation of capital ownership from capitalist function. \"the growth in needs and the desire for enrichment\" — the development of socially cultivated appetites and the incentive structure itself. \"the growing mass of investment of fixed capital, and so on\" — the scale of durable investment. The list is systematic: demographic, material, structural, financial, motivational, and physical. It demonstrates that the fall of the profit rate does not mechanically dampen accumulation — a point that rescues the theory from vulgar Ricardian underconsumptionism. 2.43 The Three Cardinal Facts and the Final Contradiction Marx closes the chapter with the three \"cardinal facts about capitalist production\": (1) \"The concentration of the means of production in a few hands, which means that they cease to appear as the property of the immediate workers and are transformed on the contrary into social powers of production. Even if this is at first as the private property of capitalists. The latter are trustees of bourgeois society, though they pocket all the fruits of this trusteeship.\" This is a remarkably dialectical formulation. Concentration simultaneously privatizes ownership (a few hands) and socializes productive power (the means of production \"cease to appear as the property of the immediate workers\"). The capitalist is not a personal owner but a \"trustee\" — a functionary managing what has become a social power — who nonetheless appropriates the proceeds. (2) \"The organization of labour itself as social labour: through cooperation, division of labour and the association of labour with natural science.\" The labour process becomes collective in form: cooperation gathers many into a common process; division of labour links them reciprocally; the incorporation of science into production organizes labour according to general principles. On (1) and (2) jointly: \"the capitalist mode of production abolishes private property and private labour, even if in antithetical forms.\" The phrase \"antithetical forms\" is precise: capitalism abolishes private property as it consolidates it into a few hands, and abolishes private labour as it subordinates labour to capital. (3) \"Establishment of the world market.\" The contradiction is not confined to any one nation: the world market is the necessary spatial form of capital's tendency toward absolute development. The closing paragraph draws the verdict: \"The tremendous productive power, in proportion to the population, which is developed within the capitalist mode of production, and — even if not to the same degree — the growth in capital values (not only in their material substratum), these growing far more quickly than the population, contradicts the basis on behalf of which this immense productive power operates, since this basis becomes ever narrower in relation to the growth of wealth; and it also contradicts the conditions of valorization of this swelling capital. Hence crises.\" The chapter ends with the aetiological aphorism: \"Hence crises.\" The falling rate of profit, over-accumulation, the surplus of capital alongside surplus population, the periodic explosions of crisis — all flow from the single structural fact that capital is both the means of production of wealth and the fetter upon its full development. A footnote notes that Richard Jones (1790–1855) is \"the third (with Malthus and Chalmers) in a trinity of economist parsons, though Marx ranks him somewhat higher than he does his two colleagues. See Theories of Surplus-Value , Part III, Chapter XXIV.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 3.1 Fundamental Principles Revealed First: the structural identity of the tendency and its counter-tendencies. The chapter's central methodological discovery is that the fall in the rate of profit is not a mechanical trend alongside which counteracting forces run as external brakes. Rather, the same process — rising productivity, mechanization, cheapening of commodities, devaluation of existing capital — is simultaneously cause and limit of the fall. Devaluation accelerates accumulation and deepens the tendency; the cheapening of constant capital raises the rate and raises the composition. This dialectical identity is what distinguishes Marx's theory from a Ricardian diminishing-returns story (which locates the barrier in nature) and from a simple underconsumptionism (which locates it in demand). Second: crisis as form of resolution, not as pathology. \"Crises are never more than momentary, violent solutions for the existing contradictions, violent eruptions that re-establish the disturbed balance for the time being.\" The systemic implication is profound: crisis is a functional moment of capitalist reproduction — the means by which the contradiction between production-as-end-in-itself and valorization-of-existing-capital is temporarily suspended. To abolish crisis would require abolishing the relation that makes crisis functionally necessary. Third: the rate/mass distinction as the key to the entire dynamic. The chapter's most consistently deployed analytical device is the distinction between rate and mass . Falling rate does not entail falling mass; rising mass does not entail rising rate; the capitalist as individual behaves according to one, the system as a whole according to the other. The surplus population doctrine, the surplus capital doctrine, and the theory of crisis all rest on this distinction. Fourth: the historical-transitoriness of capitalism is visible from within the bourgeois standpoint . The chapter is emphatic that the demonstration of capitalism's relativity does not require an external moral or philosophical critique. It is immanent — \"from the bourgeois standpoint, within the limits of capitalist understanding... are its barriers, its relativity, the fact that it is not an absolute but only a historical mode of production, corresponding to a specific and limited epoch in the development of the material conditions of production.\" This is the mature form of Marx's critical method. Fifth: capital's \"historic mission\" as the source of its supersession. \"The development of the productive forces of social labour is capital's historic mission and justification. For that very reason, it unwittingly creates the material conditions for a higher form of production.\" The socialization of production, the concentration of the means of production into \"social powers of production,\" the organization of labour \"as social labour\" through cooperation, division of labour, and the association of labour with natural science — these are the material preconditions generated by the very process that drives the profit rate down. 3.2 Systemic Laws and Essential Takeaways The chapter yields a compact set of systemic laws: Law of compositional grounding: The rate of profit falls because less labour is generally applied in relation to capital invested — not because workers are less exploited. Exploitation and profitability are in inverse relationship under conditions of advancing productivity. Law of over-accumulation: Over-accumulation is defined not absolutely against human need, but relationally against the level of exploitation required for what Marx calls \"the 'healthy' and 'normal' development of the capitalist production process.\" Law of dual poles: Surplus capital and surplus population are produced by the same causes; they stand at opposite poles but reciprocally condition one another; their coexistence is not a paradox but a structural necessity. Law of export as replication: Capital export does not resolve the contradiction; it spatially relocates it, and confirms the existence of surplus capital in the exporting country. Law of the \"animating fire\": The rate of profit, not the mass, is the systemic incentive; if capital formation passed exclusively into the hands of a few big capitals \"for whom the mass of profit outweighs the rate, the animating fire of production would be totally extinguished.\" Law of double restraint: Production is restrained by both the barrier to exploitation (productive forces) and the barrier to realization (proportionality and consumption under antagonistic distribution). These are \"separate in time and space... and in theory.\" 3.3 Broader Practical and Theoretical Implications Against Ricardo and the naturalization of crisis. Marx's demolition of Ricardo's displacement of the barrier onto \"nature\" (the theory of rent) has enduring value. Every subsequent attempt to naturalize capitalist crisis — as environmental limit, as demographic limit, as technological limit — can be diagnosed along the same lines: an externalization of an internally generated contradiction. That West anticipated Ricardo on rent, and that Chalmers offered a Malthusian picture of desired under-consumption, shows that the \"physiocratic\" and \"pastoral\" evasions were structural temptations of the period, not idiosyncrasies. Against Sismondi and underconsumptionism. By insisting that the purpose of capitalist production is valorization, not consumption, the chapter simultaneously rejects the accusation of underconsumptionism and rejects the crude denial of general overproduction. As Marx puts it: it is \"no contradiction that this overproduction of capital is accompanied by a greater or smaller relative surplus population\"; but it is likewise no contradiction that the very goods the mass of the people lack find no demand, because \"it is only in this specific, capitalist context that the surplus product receives a form in which its proprietor can make it available for consumption as soon as it has been transformed back into capital for himself.\" Against Say's Law and the \"exchange among capitalists\" apologetic. \"If it is said, finally, that the capitalists have only to exchange their commodities among themselves and consume them, then the whole character of capitalist production is forgotten, and it is forgotten that what is involved is the valorization of capital, not its consumption.\" The refutation is not empirical but conceptual: it consists in demonstrating that the premise already assumes away the phenomenon in question. For the theory of fictitious capital and finance. The chapter's treatment of claims on surplus-value (promissory notes), idle gold and silver, credit breakdown, \"share swindles,\" railways yielding only interest, and the ease with which a money owner can transform money into capital \"without having to become an industrial capitalist\" provides the analytical seedbed for the later Parts of Volume 3 on interest-bearing and fictitious capital. For the theory of the state and of class politics. The distinction between individual capitalist interest and class interest — \"competition now becomes a struggle of enemy brothers\" — has direct implications for the theory of capitalist class cohesion, its dissolution in crises, and the political forms that emerge when the \"practical freemasonry\" of the boom is broken. For historical materialism. The chapter's conclusion — the convergence of concentration, socialization of labour, world market, and the growing contradiction between \"the general social power into which capital has developed and the private power of the individual capitalists over these social conditions of production\" — is among Marx's most explicit statements of the materialist thesis that capitalism generates the preconditions of its own supersession. 3.4 The Unfinished and Contested Character of the Chapter The chapter bears the marks of its compositional history. Engels' footnote 37 to the parenthetical machine-example explicitly flags the passage as \"re-edited from a note in the original manuscript\" that \"goes beyond the original material in certain particulars.\" The book has a patchwork quality in places: Section 1 recapitulates and formalizes, Section 2 opens the dialectic of production and realization, Section 3 develops the theory of over-accumulation and crisis in detail, and Section 4 gathers supplementary remarks on fixed capital, machinery, credit, and the demographic underpinning. But the argumentative unity is nonetheless impressive: from the initial recapitulation of p′ versus s′ to the closing triple of cardinal facts, the chapter traces a single, coherent line of reasoning — that the tendency of the rate of profit to fall is the form of appearance of the contradiction between the social development of productive forces and the private form of their appropriation, and that this contradiction must repeatedly discharge itself in crises. The verdict of the chapter : capital contains within itself the barrier to its own historic mission. What begins as the most dynamic productive apparatus ever seen becomes, at a certain point in its development, a fetter on the very productive forces it fostered. The falling rate of profit is thus not merely an economic quantity; it is the numerical signature of a historical limit — one that, in Marx's framework, is not a limit of nature, technology, or the human spirit, but of the social relation that subordinates the development of the forces of production to the imperatives of valorization. Hence crises. Hence, in Marx's larger argument, the historical transitoriness of the capitalist mode of production itself. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 14 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 16"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 16", "title": "Chapter 16", "subtitle": "Commercial Capital", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 16/Chapter 16 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 8007, "read_time": "40 min read", "tags": ["Commercial Capital & Merchant's Profit"], "keywords": ["Commercial Capital / Merchant's Capital", "Circulation of Commodity Capital Handled by Specialists", "Transformation of Merchant's Advance into Profit-Bearing Entity", "Economizing on Social Circulation Costs", "Merchant Capital as Specialized Agent of Industrial Capital"], "text": "Chapter 16 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 7,969 words ⏱️ ~40 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 16, \"Commercial Capital,\" opens Part IV of the third volume of Capital — the section dedicated to the \"Transformation of Commodity Capital and Money Capital into Commercial Capital and Money-Dealing Capital,\" and it makes a single, consequential theoretical move. Its purpose is to establish, rigorously and from the ground up, what the merchant's capital — the capital invested exclusively in buying and selling commodities — actually is : a functionally independent but substantively derivative portion of industrial capital, hived off by the social division of labour and permanently confined to the sphere of circulation, which creates no value or surplus-value of its own yet nonetheless earns a profit by partaking in the surplus-value produced in production. The chapter's central problem is therefore a puzzle of identity-and-difference. Commercial capital, Marx insists, is \"nothing but the transformed form of a portion of this circulation capital which is always to be found on the market\"; it is \"absolutely nothing more than the commodity capital of the producer which has to go through the process of transformation into money.\" And yet it appears on the market as an autonomous capital, advancing its own money, running its own circuit M-C-M′, realising its own profit, and standing over against industrial capital as a peer. How can a form that is materially identical with industrial capital's own circulation phase nevertheless be formally an independent capital? Marx's answer unfolds in two steps. First, the social division of labour must hive off the function of selling as the exclusive business of a distinct set of agents, separate from the producer. Second — and this is the decisive condition — those agents must advance their own money capital , which is valorised precisely because it is exclusively engaged in facilitating the metamorphosis of commodity capital. Only when both conditions are met does what was merely a \"function\" of industrial capital become a \"special capital.\" The analytical hinge of the chapter is the demonstration that the merchant's circuit M-C-M′ is, in truth, nothing but two successive C-M's performed by the same commodity capital — two sales, the second of which is the final and definitive sale. Commercial capital, in other words, is the form of independence assumed by a moment of industrial capital's own circulation. Two further tasks are discharged along the way. First, the chapter carefully brackets out a set of functions — transport, storage, dispatch, the \"dispersal of goods in a distributable form\" — that Marx had already treated in Volume 2 as production processes continuing within circulation . These are frequently confused with the true functions of commercial capital, and Marx insists on analysing the latter in its \"pure form\" by stripping them away. This bracketing is not pedantry: it is what makes it possible to isolate the value-form of merchant's capital as such, and to deny it any productive role. Second, the chapter demonstrates the economic rationality of specialised commercial capital: by centralising the money reserve and shortening circulation time, it economises on the portion of social capital that must lie fallow as a means of purchase, and thereby raises the share of capital directly applied in production. The intellectual objective is foundational. By establishing that commercial capital is not a source of value but a claimant on the surplus-value produced elsewhere, Marx prepares the ground for Chapter 17 (commercial profit, and the general rate of profit's modification by merchant's capital), Chapter 18 (the turnover of merchant's capital and prices), Chapter 19 (money-dealing capital, the second sub-species announced on the first page), and Chapter 20 (the historical material on merchant's capital). The chapter's principal polemical target is the \"modern economics\" that \"lumps trading capital and industrial capital directly together\" and thereby \"completely overlooks trading capital's characteristic peculiarities\" — a target concentrated in the long footnote 38, where Ramsay, Verri, Say, and Newman are all arraigned for treating commerce as in some sense productive. Finally, the chapter fits into the broader architecture of Capital with considerable elegance. Volume 1 analysed the immediate process of production; Volume 2 the process of circulation; Volume 3, \"the process of capitalist production as a whole.\" Part IV is where Marx shows that the transformed forms of the total process — forms that appear to have an existence independent of the unity of production and circulation — are nevertheless governed by the same law of surplus-value. Commercial capital is the first and most historically ancient of these transformed forms, and Marx's demonstration that its historical priority does not entail its logical priority is itself one of the chapter's most profound results. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Opening Declaration: Two Sub-Species and the Blind Spot of \"Modern Economics\" Marx opens with a topological claim that governs the entire chapter: merchant's or trading capital — the Handelskapital of the German tradition — is not a single thing but a genus divided into \"two forms or sub-species,\" commercial capital and money-dealing capital. The first is the capital that deals in commodities (the subject of this chapter); the second is the capital that deals in money as such (deferred to Chapter 19). This distinction is essential because it prevents the reader from treating interest-bearing capital, exchange-dealing, banking, and the simple buying and selling of goods as one undifferentiated mass of \"circulation capital.\" Each must be analysed \"in such detail as is needed in order to analyse capital in its basic inner structure.\" The motivation for the analysis is then stated polemically: \"modern economics, and even its best representatives, lump trading capital and industrial capital directly together and in fact completely overlook trading capital's characteristic peculiarities.\" This is a compressed indictment. If commercial capital is simply industrial capital under another name, then the distinctiveness of the merchant's circuit — its peculiar relation to value creation, its peculiar velocity, its peculiar relation to the credit system — vanishes from view. Marx's project is precisely to recover those peculiarities. The phrase \"characteristic peculiarities\" (the original has the flavour of \"specific determinations\") signals that the chapter will be a work of differential theory: what makes commercial capital what it is, as against industrial capital on the one hand and money-dealing capital on the other. 2.2 Bracketing Out the \"Real\" Functions: Production Within Circulation Having marked his territory, Marx immediately narrows it. He reminds us that in Volume 2, Chapter 6 (\"The Costs of Circulation,\" sections 2 and 3) he had already explained that transport, storage, and the \"dispersal of goods in a distributable form\" are to be viewed as production processes that continue within the process of circulation . This is a decisive analytical distinction. These operations — moving goods, warehousing them, packing them for sale — do create value, because they apply labour (and thus abstract labour-time) to commodities still en route from production to consumption. They are, in the strict sense, productive labour performed inside the circuit of capital. The trouble is that these real, productive functions are frequently \"confused with the functions peculiar to commercial capital\" and are in commercial practice \"linked\" with the latter. As the social division of labour develops, however, the specifically commercial function — pure buying and selling, pure change of ownership — \"evolves in a pure form, i.e. separately from these real functions and independent of them.\" Marx therefore brackets them: \"we can therefore ignore these functions… We only have this pure form once those functions are discarded and removed.\" The nuance here is important and easily missed. Marx is not saying that commercial capital never performs these functions in practice; he is saying that when it does — when the merchant also stores, transports, or packages — it does not \"appear in its pure form,\" because alongside its commercial function it is simultaneously acting as an industrial capitalist (buying labour-power, valorising capital, appropriating surplus-value). For the purposes of isolating the specific difference of merchant's capital, those productive activities must be subtracted. This bracketing is the methodological precondition for the entire chapter's conclusion — namely, that as commercial capital proper , the merchant's capital creates no value. 2.3 The Volume 2 Inheritance: Commodity Capital, Metamorphosis, and the Birth of a Special Function Marx now re-appropriates the analysis of Volume 2, Chapter 3. Taking social capital as a whole, \"one part of this is always on the market as a commodity, waiting to pass over into money… another part is on the market as money, waiting to pass over into commodities.\" Commodity capital and money capital are not two different capitals but two forms of existence through which every capital passes in the course of its circuit. The metamorphosis of commodities — C-M, M-C, C-M-C in simple circulation, C′-M-C within the circuit of industrial capital — is the mechanism by which capital changes its form without changing its substance. When does this functional moment become a capital in its own right? Marx's formula is precise: \"In as much as this function acquires independent life as a special function of a special capital and is fixed by the division of labour as a function that falls to a particular species of capitalists, commodity capital becomes commodity-dealing capital or commercial capital.\" Note the two-fold condition: (i) the function must acquire an independent life , i.e. be performed by a distinct set of agents; and (ii) this must be fixed by the division of labour as the exclusive occupation of a particular species of capitalists. Both clauses are essential. A travelling salesman who works for an industrialist performs the function, but it is not yet a special capital ; a merchant who simply happens to sell what he also produces is likewise not yet a commercial capitalist. Marx also stresses a subtle temporal point: capital simultaneously exists as commodity capital and as productive capital . These are \"two separate and distinct forms of existence of the same capital.\" For an individual capital, its commodity form is only \"a point of transition, ever vanishing and ever repeated, a transition stage in the continuity of its production process.\" But social capital considered as a whole always has some portion in the commodity form on the market, because elements are \"constantly being withdrawn from the commodity market and just as constantly returned to it.\" It is precisely this social permanence — the fact that some commodity capital is always on the market — that makes possible the autonomisation of commercial capital. Without it, there would be nothing for the special function to attach itself to. 2.4 Commercial Capital Defined: The Transformed Portion of Circulation Capital The definition follows directly. \"Commercial capital, then, is nothing but the transformed form of a portion of this circulation capital which is always to be found on the market, in the course of its metamorphosis, and perpetually confined to the circulation sphere.\" Two qualifications are registered. First, \"a portion only,\" because — as Marx notes in passing — a great deal of buying and selling still takes place directly between industrial capitalists themselves , without any merchant interposing. Marx explicitly sets this aside, since it \"contributes nothing to the theoretical definition\" and has already been handled in Volume 2. The methodological point is worth registering: Marx is abstracting from the un-merchant-mediated part of circulation, not denying it exists. Second — and this is the crucial, almost paradoxical, claim — commercial capital is not a new capital added to social capital in the way a new industrial capital would be. It arises out of the circulation capital that would exist anyway, simply by hiving it off as a specialised business. There does not \"really\" need to be more capital; the same capital serving the same function simply acquires a different social form and a different sphere of employment. 2.5 The Dealer's Entry and the £3,000 Example: M-C-M′ as the Form of Merchant's Capital Marx now introduces the dealer in person: \"The dealer in commodities, like any other capitalist, first appears on the market as the representative of a certain sum of money that he advances as a capitalist, i.e. which he seeks to transform from x (the original value) into x + Δx (this sum plus the profit on it).\" Every capitalist aims at self-expansion; the merchant is no exception. But the merchant has a formal peculiarity: because he does not produce commodities but merely facilitates their movement, \"his capital has to appear on the market originally in the form of money capital, since he does not produce any commodities himself but simply deals in them.\" Here is the numerical illustration that anchors the rest of the chapter: a commodity dealer has £3,000. He uses it to buy 30,000 yards of linen from a linen manufacturer at 2 shillings per yard. He later sells the 30,000 yards again. \"If the average rate of profit is 10 per cent and after deducting all his incidental expenses he makes an annual profit of 10 per cent, by the end of the year he has transformed his £3,000 into £3,300.\" The specification of a 10 per cent average rate of profit is deliberate: Marx is operating at the level of abstraction of the already-equalised general rate of profit, and the merchant's profit is presupposed as a share of that rate, not as an independent appropriation. His formal circuit is M-C-M′: \"the simple form of capital, when it is completely restricted to the circulation process and not interrupted by the interval of the production process that lies outside its own movement and function.\" This designation — not interrupted by the interval of the production process — is the structural signature of merchant's capital. Industrial capital's circuit always contains a \"P\" phase (production) between buying and selling; the merchant's circuit is a pure alternation of purchase and sale, with no interval of production. The whole circuit is confined to the circulation sphere. 2.6 The Producer's Illusion and the Interruption Proof Marx now performs his first great conceptual untangling. Consider the linen manufacturer's position. \"As far as the linen manufacturer is concerned, he has realized the value of his linen with the merchant's money, thus completing the first phase in the metamorphosis of his commodity capital, its transformation into money.\" With that money he can buy yarn, coal, wages, means of subsistence. From his standpoint, the sale has occurred; the metamorphosis is done; the reproduction process can continue. But — and here the chapter's analytical genius announces itself — \"although the metamorphosis of the linen into money, its sale, has already taken place as far as its producer is concerned, this has not yet happened for the linen itself.\" The linen is still on the market as commodity capital, still awaiting its definitive sale. \"Nothing has happened to the linen except a change in the person of its owner.\" The position of the linen in the reproduction process is unchanged; it remains what it was — a saleable commodity. Only now it sits in the merchant's warehouse instead of the producer's. From this comes the first key inference: the merchant has simply taken over the function the producer would otherwise have had to perform himself. The merchant's buying-and-selling is the producer's selling, but carried on independently, as a distinct trade, by a distinct agent. Then comes the thought-experiment that \"proves\" the point decisively. Suppose the merchant does not sell the 30,000 yards before the linen producer brings a further 30,000 yards to market. The merchant cannot buy. \"There is now a hold-up, an interruption in the reproduction.\" Marx grants that the producer might have additional money capital and could continue production independently — but \"to make this assumption would not alter things at all. As far as the capital advanced in the 30,000 yards is concerned, its reproduction process is and remains interrupted.\" The logic is airtight: the capital that is stuck in the merchant's unsold stock is precisely the producer's capital in its commodity form, and its immobilisation is a genuine immobilisation of that capital's reproduction, no matter how much extra money the producer happens to hold. If the same sale had been performed by a mere agent of the producer — a clerk, a travelling salesman, a commission agent — the connection would be obvious; no one would think the producer's capital had \"moved on.\" The merchant's independence merely obscures this. It does not abolish it. 2.7 The Formal Inversion: The Same Commodity Sold Twice Marx now contrasts the formal structure of commercial circulation with the circulating forms already analysed. In simple commodity circulation, or in the C′-M-C of industrial capital, the circulation is accomplished by the money changing hands twice. The linen producer sells linen and receives money; with the same money he buys yarn, coal, labour. The commodity he buys (means of production) is not the same as the commodity he sold (linen). The money's double change of place enables a change of commodity identity — alienating one use-value, appropriating another. In the merchant's circuit M-C-M′, the situation is exactly inverted. \"Here it is not the same pieces of money that change place twice, but rather the same commodity; it passes from the hands of the seller into those of the buyer, and from the hands of this buyer, who has now become a seller, into those of another buyer.\" The linen is sold twice — once by the producer to the merchant, once by the merchant to his customer — and, given a chain of merchants, \"can still be sold several more times.\" It is \"precisely through this repeated sale, the double change of place of the same commodity, that the money advanced by the first buyer for the purchase of the commodity effects its return to him.\" The consequence is a formal criterion of incompleteness: \"the commodity has not yet been definitively sold when it passes from the hands of the producer into those of the merchant, and… the latter is only continuing the operation of sale.\" The merchant's independence is thus the social form of an unfinished sale — the form in which the producer's C-M, having been anticipated by the merchant's M-C, is stretched out into a second act. The circuit-recognition of merchant's capital now crystallises. What is C-M for the producer — a mere phase, a transient shape his capital assumes on its way back to money — becomes, for the merchant, M-C-M′: \"a particular valorization of the money capital he has advanced.\" \"One phase of the commodity's metamorphosis now exhibits itself, with respect to the merchant, as M-C-M′, i.e. as the evolution of a specific kind of capital.\" 2.8 The Definitive Sale, Means of Payment, and the First Hint of Credit The merchant \"definitively\" sells the linen when he sells it to a consumer — either a productive consumer (the bleacher who uses the linen as raw material) or an individual consumer (who buys it for private use). At that moment his advanced capital returns with profit, and he can begin afresh. Marx now introduces a dimension of realism that will prove crucial. Suppose that when the merchant bought the linen, his money functioned merely as means of payment rather than as means of purchase — i.e., he bought on credit. Then he would only have had to pay the linen producer six weeks later. If he had sold the linen before that period expired, \"he could have paid the linen producer without himself having to advance any money capital.\" Conversely, if he had not sold the linen, he would have had to raise the £3,000 when it fell due; and if he had sold below his purchase price, \"because of a fall in its market price, he would have had to replace the missing amount from his own capital.\" This is the footprint of the credit system on commercial capital. Marx is presaging what he will formalise in Part V: when money functions as a means of payment, the money capital \"needed\" for a commercial transaction shrinks dramatically — in the limit, to zero. But even in the pure case, the merchant must maintain a reserve for price fluctuations; and so must the producer if he sells in falling markets. 2.9 The Two Conditions of Autonomy Marx now returns to his central question and answers it in two steps. \"What then gives commercial capital the character of an independently functioning capital, whereas in the hands of the producer who makes his own sales it obviously appears as no more than a particular form of his capital at a particular phase in its reproduction process?\" First condition. The commodity capital achieves its definitive transformation into money \"in the hands of an agent distinct from the producer,\" and this function \"takes the form of a specific business of its own, separate from the other functions of industrial capital and hence autonomous.\" This is a particular form of the social division of labour : \"one part of the function which has to be performed in a particular phase of the capital's reproduction process, here the phase of circulation, appears as the exclusive function of a specific agent of circulation distinct from the producer.\" But — and here Marx is careful — this first condition is not sufficient . It is entirely possible for trading to be pursued \"simply by travelling salesmen or other direct agents of the industrial capitalist.\" In that case the function is separated in person but not in capital : it is still the industrialist's capital that is at stake, still his money that has been advanced, still his risk. \"A second aspect must also be involved.\" Second condition. The independent circulation agent, the merchant, advances his own money capital (whether his own or borrowed) in this position. \"What is simply C-M as far as an industrial capital involved in its reproduction process is concerned… presents itself for the merchant as M-C-M′, as the purchase and sale of the same commodity, so that the money capital that he parts with on the purchase returns to him through the sale.\" It is the advance of a distinct money capital that makes the function a capital, rather than merely a socially separated labour of selling. Only when both conditions are met does commercial capital exist as an independently functioning capital. 2.10 The Metamorphosis Tableau: How M-C-M′ Decomposes into Two C-M's The chapter now performs its most subtle compositional exercise: a step-by-step tableau showing that the merchant's M-C-M′ is, when referred back to the commodities themselves, nothing but the concatenation of two C-M's belonging to the same commodity capital . Consider the linen. For the linen producer, C-M was the first metamorphosis — the transformation of commodity capital into money capital. But for the merchant, this same act takes the form M-C — his money investing itself in commodity capital. Same act, opposite formal markings, depending on whose account-book the transaction falls on. Now the linen is sold to the bleacher. For the bleacher, this is M-C: the transformation of money capital into productive capital (means of production), i.e. the second metamorphosis of his own money capital. But for the merchant it is C-M: the sale of what he had bought. And with respect to the linen as commodity capital , it is only now that the commodity is definitively sold — \"the decisive conclusion of its first metamorphosis.\" The same duplicity characterises the yarn example. Suppose the linen manufacturer buys yarn from a yarn dealer with part of the money he received for the linen. For the manufacturer, this is M-C (second metamorphosis). For the yarn dealer, it is C-M (the resale of yarn). And with respect to the yarn as commodity capital, it is \"simply its definitive sale, with which it passes from the circulation sphere into that of consumption.\" The conclusion is the chapter's central analytical theorem: \"whether the merchant buys from the industrial capitalist or sells to him, his M-C-M′, the circuit of commercial capital, only ever expresses what with respect to the commodity capital itself, as a transitional form of the industrial capital being reproduced, is simply C-M, simply the completion of its first metamorphosis.\" And then: \"M-C-M′ is only two C-M's performed by the same commodity capital, it consists simply of two successive sales, which between them simply make possible its final and definitive sale.\" This is what the chapter is for. Once you see that M-C-M′ is really C-M + C-M of one and the same commodity, you see that (a) the merchant's circuit is not creative but mediative , (b) the merchant's independence is the social form of the producer's unfinished sale, and (c) any surplus-value that appears in the merchant's M′ must have been produced somewhere else , in the sphere of production, and merely transferred to him through the pricing mechanism. 2.11 Substantive Identity and the Paradox of Independence Marx draws the identity claim once more, sharply: \"Commercial capital, in so far and as long as it exists in the form of commodity capital — and what we are considering here is the reproduction process of the entire social capital — is evidently nothing more than the part of industrial capital that is still on the market and engaged in its process of metamorphosis.\" It is \"only the money capital advanced by the merchant — the money capital exclusively designed for buying and selling, which never assumes any other form than that of commodity capital and money capital, never assumes that of productive capital, and remains for ever penned into capital's circulation sphere.\" Two formal markers are decisive here. First: the merchant's capital never assumes the form of productive capital . It oscillates forever between M and C, never entering the P phase of industrial capital's circuit. This is what makes it essentially different from an industrial capital that happens to be engaged in sales. Second: it is penned into the circulation sphere . Whatever the profit it earns, it earns it as circulation capital — as a claim on surplus-value that does not pass through production. But the paradox is not dissolved: how can this mere form of industrial capital be, in practice, a different person's capital, with its own risks, its own advance, its own profit? Marx's next moves address this. 2.12 The Economy of the Money Reserve and the Three Advantages of Specialized Commercial Capital Consider the producer's position without a merchant. If he had to wait until his linen passed to its final consumer before receiving money, \"his reproduction process would be interrupted.\" Or — in order to keep production going — he would have to restrict his operations and hold a larger portion of his capital as a monetary reserve , transforming only a smaller part of his linen into yarn, coal, labour, etc. A larger share of his capital would sit idle as means of purchase. \"That division of his capital is not abolished by the intervention of the merchant.\" But — and this is the payoff — without the merchant, \"the part of the circulation capital that exists in the form of a money reserve would always have to be greater in proportion to the part employed in the form of productive capital, and the scale of reproduction would be accordingly restricted.\" With the merchant, the producer \"can now regularly apply a greater part of his capital in the actual production process, leaving a smaller part as a money reserve.\" This is the economic rationale of the autonomisation: the merchant's specialised capital centralises the money reserve required to keep the social process moving, and thereby frees capital for production. Marx then poses the counterfactual sharply. If the merchant were himself to apply his £3,000 productively, \"the society's productive capital would be that much greater.\" But then both the linen producer and the merchant-now-industrialist would have to hold larger money reserves each. And so the social product of the change is nil, or negative. By contrast, if the merchant remains a merchant, \"the producer saves time in selling which he can apply to supervising the production process, while the merchant has to spend his entire time selling.\" This is the social division of labour's gain from specialisation — but with the crucial addition that the cost is now capital , not merely labour-time. Assuming \"commercial capital does not overstep its necessary proportions\" — i.e. bracketing over-trading, crises, and speculative distortions — Marx states three advantages: The capital exclusively concerned with buying and selling is smaller than it would be if the industrial capitalist had to conduct the entire commercial part himself. And this capital includes not only the funds laid out to purchase commodities but also the capital laid out for the labour employed in the merchant's business, plus warehouses, transport, constant capital. Because the merchant is exclusively concerned with this business, not only is the producer's commodity converted into money sooner , but the commodity capital itself goes through its metamorphosis more quickly . Taking commercial capital as a whole: a single turnover of commercial capital can correspond to the turnovers of several capitals in one sphere (the linen dealer who successively buys from several linen producers) or of capitals in different spheres (the merchant who, having sold linen, buys silk). This third advantage opens onto the most original analytical section of the chapter. 2.13 The Distinct Rhythm of Commercial Capital's Turnover Industrial capital's turnover is restricted both by circulation time and by production time. This imposes a ceiling on how fast any given industrial capital can reproduce itself. But because commercial capital is engaged only with the circulation side, and because it deals in commodities in the commodity form independently of the specific capitals that produced them, its turnover obeys a different rhythm. \"The turnover of commercial capital, in so far as it deals with just one particular kind of commodity, is not restricted by the turnover of a single industrial capital but rather by the turnover of all industrial capitals in the same branch of production.\" After the merchant has bought and sold one producer's linen, he can buy and sell another producer's linen before the first producer puts new linen on the market . This means that the same commercial capital \"can thus successively facilitate the different turnovers of various capitals invested in a branch of production, so that its turnover is not identical with the turnovers of one individual industrial capital and hence does not replace only the monetary reserve that this particular industrial capitalist had to keep to himself.\" The illustration is agricultural: consider capital A producing a commodity that takes three months to produce. In the same period, a merchant may buy and sell that commodity in one month, then go on to buy and sell the product of another producer. Or consider corn: after the merchant sells one farmer's crop, he can buy and sell a second farmer's crop with the same money, and so on. \"The turnover of his capital is limited by the amount of corn that he can successively buy and sell in a given time, e.g. a year, but the turnover of the farmer's capital, quite apart from its circulation time, is restricted also by its production time, which covers a whole year.\" Commercial capital escapes the constraint of production time as an individual-circuit constraint , and is bound only by the aggregate supply available for purchase in the branch. Marx states the general result elegantly: \"The turnover of commercial capital is not identical with the turnover or the reproduction, once only, of an equally large industrial capital; it is equal, rather, to the sum of the turnovers of a number of such capitals, whether in the same sphere of production or in different ones.\" The same commercial capital successively transforms various commodity capitals into money, performing the same function toward them that money in general performs toward commodities — that of circulating between many exchanges in a given period. 2.14 Velocity, Development, and Monetary Wealth Marx now derives a series of historical-comparative propositions. \"The more quickly the commercial capital turns over, the smaller is the part of the total money capital that figures as commercial capital, and vice versa.\" A fast-turning commerce ties up less money in the circulation sphere; a slow-turning commerce ties up more. And inversely: \"The less developed production is, the greater is the sum of commercial capital in proportion to the amount of commodities put into circulation in general; the smaller, however, it is in absolute terms or compared with more developed conditions. And conversely.\" The gloss is telling: \"In undeveloped conditions of this kind, therefore, the greater part of money capital proper is in the hands of merchants, so that their wealth constitutes monetary wealth as far as others are concerned.\" This is a compressed account of pre-capitalist commerce — where the merchant stands as the representative of money wealth, and money appears as wealth par excellence because productive capital is not yet dominant. Under developed capitalism, this relationship inverts: commercial capital becomes quantitatively smaller relative to industrial capital and relative to the volume of commodities in circulation. 2.15 Simultaneous Buying and Selling, and the Credit Economy Marx now complicates the two-part image of the merchant's capital. It is not true that \"the entire commercial capital has to be used to its full extent in buying commodities and then re-selling them.\" The merchant performs both movements at the same time : \"His capital is then divided into two parts, the first consisting of commodity capital and the second of money capital.\" He buys in one place, sells in another. The division fluctuates and is balanced out. This \"velocity\" of commercial capital can be further multiplied by the credit system, which grows out of the use of money as a means of payment . \"If I buy £1,000 worth of wine on three months' credit and I sell this wine for cash before the three-month period expires, not a single penny has to be advanced for the transaction.\" In that case the \"money capital\" that figures as commercial capital is nothing more than \"industrial capital itself in its form of money capital, in its own reflux in the money form.\" Discounting a promissory note at a bank \"in no way alters the matter and has nothing to do with commercial capital.\" The illustration is then extended to a falling market. If the price of the commodity falls by a tenth, the merchant receives only £2,700 rather than £3,000, and must put up a further £300 — a reserve for settling the difference in price. But the producer faces the same exigency. The reserve, in other words, is a general requirement of circulating capital, not a peculiarity of commerce. 2.16 Tracing the Money: The £2,000 Puzzle and the Source of \"Commercial\" Capital The final analytical passage follows a chain of payments through the system — linen dealer → linen manufacturer → yarn dealer → etc. — to determine whose money is what . The linen dealer buys linen from the manufacturer for £3,000. The manufacturer spends £2,000 on yarn. That £2,000 is paid to the yarn dealer. Whose money is it? \"It is the money form of his own capital\" — the manufacturer's capital, which has just been realised through the sale to the linen dealer. In the hands of the yarn dealer, \"this £2,000 seems to be money capital on its reflux; but how far is it really money capital, as distinct from simply £2,000 as the money form shed by the linen and assumed by the yarn?\" The resolution is conditional on credit: \"If the yarn dealer has bought on credit and sells for cash before his payment period expires, this £2,000 does not contain a single penny of commercial capital as distinct from the money form that industrial capital itself assumes in the course of its cycle.\" From these threads Marx extracts his final definition. \"Commercial capital, therefore, in so far as it is not simply a form of industrial capital that happens to be found, in the shape of commodity capital or money capital, in the hands of the merchant, is nothing but the portion of money capital that belongs to the merchant himself, and is circulated in the purchase and sale of commodities.\" This portion represents, \"on a reduced scale, the portion of the capital advanced for production that always had to exist as a money reserve, a means of purchase, in the hands of the industrialist.\" Now it exists, reduced, in the merchant's hands, functioning exclusively in the circulation process — a part of total capital that must keep circulating as a means of purchase in order to keep reproduction continuous. And — the crucial comparative law — \"It is all the smaller in relation to the total capital, the quicker the reproduction process and the more developed the function of money as means of payment, i.e. the credit system.\" 2.17 Conclusion: No Value, No Surplus-Value — and the Indirect Productive Role of Commercial Capital The chapter's formal conclusion is now stated. \"Commercial capital is nothing more than capital functioning within the circulation sphere. The circulation process is one phase in the reproduction process as a whole. But in the process of circulation, no value is produced, and thus also no surplus-value. The same value simply undergoes changes of form. Nothing at all happens except the metamorphosis of commodities, which by its very nature has nothing to do with the creation or alteration of value.\" Every sale merely realises a surplus-value already in the commodity . No buyer realises a surplus-value in the second act either — the exchange of money capital back into elements of production. What happens there is that production of surplus-value is begun , when money is exchanged for means of production and labour-power. But the form-changes themselves cost time — time during which capital produces nothing — and so constitute a pure deduction from potential surplus-value. The surplus-value, expressed in the rate of profit , \"will actually vary inversely with the length of the circulation time.\" And so: \"Commercial capital thus creates neither value nor surplus-value, at least not directly.\" The qualifier \"at least not directly\" is not idle. Three indirect effects are enumerated: Shortening circulation time helps the industrial capitalist increase the surplus-value he extracts, by allowing capital to pass more rapidly through production. Extending the market and facilitating the division of labour between capitals enables capital to operate on a bigger scale, and thereby promotes productivity and accumulation. Cutting down turnover time increases the ratio of surplus-value to capital advanced — i.e., the rate of profit. Reducing the fraction of capital confined to the circulation sphere as money capital increases the portion of capital directly applied in production. This is the chapter's mature position: commercial capital is not productive , but it is economically rational , and its rationality shows up in the general rate of profit. 2.18 The Polemic: Newman, Say, Ramsay, and the False \"Productivity\" of Commerce Footnote 38 — appended to the conclusion — is the chapter's most concentrated theoretical polemic. Ramsay \"confuses commercial capital with the transport industry\" and calls commerce \"the transport of commodities from one place to another.\" The same confusion is found in Verri ( Meditazioni sulla economia politica ) and Say ( Traité d'économie politique ). These authors are charged with collapsing a functional difference — that between producing value through the application of labour in circulation (transport, storage) and merely mediating the form-change of commodities — into an identity. S. P. Newman ( Elements of Political Economy , 1835) provides the fullest statement of the error. Newman argues that the merchant \"standing between the producer and the consumer, advancing to the former capital and receiving products in return, and then handing over these products to the latter, receiving back capital in return, is a transaction which both facilitates the economical processes of the community, and adds value to the products…\" — because \"the same products in the hands of consumers are worth more than in the hands of producers.\" Hence commerce appears to Newman, \"just as to Mr Say, as 'strictly an act of production.'\" Marx's rebuttal is exact. \"Newman's view is fundamentally false. The use-value of a commodity is greater in the hands of the consumer than it is in those of the producer, because it is only here that it is at all realized.… But a commodity is not paid for twice over, first for its exchange-value and then for its use-value as something extra. By paying its exchange-value, I appropriate its use-value. And the exchange-value does not increase in the slightest by the fact that the commodity passes from the hands of the producer or the middleman into those of the consumer.\" Two distinct claims are being distinguished. (i) Exchange-value is invariant through the sphere of circulation (metamorphosis only). (ii) Use-value is realised — brought into actual functioning — only in consumption; but this realisation is not a payment , not an addition to value. Newman (and Say) systematically conflate the realisation of use-value with the creation of exchange-value, and thereby mistake circulation for production. This is the \"vulgar-economic\" confusion whose full critique Marx will undertake in Chapter 48, on the \"trinity formula\" and Say's \"factors of production.\" The definitional consequence is that commercial capital, strictly understood, must not be identified with transport, storage, or packaging. When it performs these functions, it is simultaneously acting as an industrial capitalist. The pure form of merchant's capital — the form whose properties this chapter has analysed — is the form in which it does nothing but buy and sell. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 3.1 The Fundamental Theoretical Principles Chapter 16 cashes out a set of principles that govern the entire subsequent architecture of Volume 3. First, formal independence is not substantive independence. Commercial capital is real — it advances money, runs a circuit, earns profit — but it does not create the substance of that profit. Its independence is the social form taken by a phase of industrial capital's own circuit. Second, the circuit form determines the character of capital. What distinguishes commercial capital is not its commodity, labour, or location but its circuit : M-C-M′ with no P phase, forever oscillating between money and commodities and never entering production. This formal criterion is what makes it possible to distinguish commercial capital from industrial capital even when both deal in exactly the same commodities. Third, and most critically for the surplus-value theory of Volume 3, the general rate of profit must be understood as the mechanism by which commercial capital partakes of surplus-value produced elsewhere. Commercial profit is not a value added in circulation; it is a transfer within the total surplus-value. The chapter thus sets up the analysis of \"commercial profit\" that follows, without yet formalising the equalisation of the general rate of profit to include commercial capital, but the groundwork is complete: the merchant must be a claimant, and the equalisation mechanism must be able to accommodate a capital that never produces a surplus-value itself. Fourth, circulation time is a deduction from surplus-value. The parenthetical remark that \"the surplus-value, as expressed in the profit rate, will actually vary inversely with the length of the circulation time\" is a staging-post for the fuller treatment of turnover and its effect on the rate of profit, and connects back to Volume 2's analysis of circulation costs. Fifth, the historical priority of merchant's capital does not entail its logical priority. Merchant's capital pre-dates industrial capital — its wealth is, in undeveloped conditions, the monetary wealth of society. But in the capitalist mode of production, it is a derived form. This is one of Marx's most important historical-materialist results: the appearance of autonomy is a form of dependence. 3.2 The Economy of the Money Reserve: A General Law of Capitalist Circulation One of the chapter's most durable insights is its treatment of the economy of the money reserve . Without specialised commercial capital, each industrialist would have to hold a money reserve proportional to his turnover in order to bridge the interval between sale and receipt of payment. The merchant centralises this reserve and, by turning his capital over faster than any individual producer, liberates a large fraction of social capital for direct employment in production. This is a structural law of capitalist circulation : the efficiency of the whole depends on the degree to which the circulation sphere can be economised. The credit system (Chapter 25 onwards) is, in effect, the completion of this process — an institutionalisation of the economy of the money reserve. Commercial capital and credit stand in a functional continuum. 3.3 Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Historical Implications Theoretical. The chapter disposes, decisively, of any attempt to treat commerce as directly productive. Say, Ramsay, Newman, and their heirs cannot claim that the merchant's intervention adds value. The merchant's reward is a share of surplus-value — a share that must be accounted for in the general rate of profit, but that is not produced by his operations. In modern terms, this forbids the \"marketing adds value\" or \"logistics adds value\" arguments from claiming a surplus-value-creating status. Logistics, transport, packaging, storage do add value (as production within circulation). Buying and selling as such do not. Historical and comparative. The chapter supplies a theory of commercial capital's historical trajectory. In undeveloped commodity production, merchant's wealth is monetary wealth par excellence — commerce is the dominant form of capital and stands over and above production as an autonomous power. As capitalist production develops, commercial capital declines relative to industrial capital in both proportion and absolute size, its money-reserve function is taken over more efficiently by credit, and its velocity accelerates. This is the analytical counterpart to the historical argument of Chapter 20 (the \"Historical Material on Merchant's Capital\") and to the \"so-called primitive accumulation\" narrative of Volume 1: merchant's capital's economic power is not the seedbed of capitalist production but is eventually subdued by it. Methodological. The chapter models a technique central to Volume 3: to disaggregate the \"transformed forms\" of capital (commercial, money-dealing, interest-bearing) from the industrial capital that produces their substance, and to show that each transformed form obeys its own laws of motion without producing new value . Every subsequent form-analysis in Part IV and Part V follows this template. Contemporary resonances. The chapter anticipates the analysis of any functionally \"circulation-only\" capital: the modern logistics and distribution sector, platform marketplaces that mediate exchange without producing goods, and financial intermediaries that facilitate flows without themselves creating the underlying value. In every case, the same question must be put: does the entity produce value (as production within circulation), or does it merely mediate form-change and draw a share of surplus-value produced elsewhere? Marx's answer — no value, but a claim on value, and yet a genuinely social economy by centralising the reserve and shortening the turnover cycle — remains the analytic frame. 3.4 Conclusion Chapter 16 is a chapter of definitions that generate results . Its central theorem — that the merchant's M-C-M′ is nothing but two C-M's of the same commodity capital, successively performed — dissolves the apparent autonomy of commercial capital into the real social relations that sustain it. The merchant does not produce wealth; he moves it, draws upon it, and, through the mere division of labour, allows the wealth-producing side of the system to spend more of its time producing. The chapter's great achievement is to explain both truths at once: commercial capital is not a source of surplus-value, and it is a materially necessary, economically rational, historically decisive form of capital. In the longer arc of Volume 3, it opens the door to the analysis of commercial profit, the turnover of merchant's capital, money-dealing capital, and the credit system — each of which is a variation on the theme first sounded here: the independence of the forms in which capital moves is always an independence grounded in the substance it never creates. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 15 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 17"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 17", "title": "Chapter 17", "subtitle": "Commercial Profit", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 17/Chapter 17 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 13558, "read_time": "68 min read", "tags": ["Commercial Capital & Merchant's Profit", "Surplus-Value & Exploitation"], "keywords": ["Commercial Profit", "Commercial Capital Produces No Surplus-Value", "Participation of Merchant Capital in Average Profit Equalization", "Deduction of Commercial Profit from Industrial Surplus-Value", "Exploitation of Commercial Wage-Workers", "Unpaid Commercial Labour Reducing Circulation Expenses"], "text": "Chapter 17 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 13,522 words ⏱️ ~68 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 17: Commercial Profit — An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 17 of Capital , Volume 3, \"Commercial Profit,\" is the opening and foundational chapter in Marx's account of \"merchant's capital\"—capital that operates exclusively in the sphere of circulation, buying in order to sell, and that nonetheless extracts an average profit from surplus-value created elsewhere. Its central problem is stark and at first glance paradoxical. Marx begins by recalling a result already established in Volume 2, Chapter 5. The \"pure functions of capital in the circulation sphere\" —the operations the industrial capitalist must perform (a) to realize the value of his commodities and (b) to transform this value back into the commodity-elements of production, i.e., the acts of sale and purchase that effect the metamorphoses of commodity capital C'-M-C—create neither value nor surplus-value. The time these operations require sets an objective and subjective limit on the formation of value and surplus-value. If these operations produce no value when performed by the industrial capitalist himself, they cannot suddenly acquire productive power when delegated to a \"special section of capitalists\" or made \"the exclusive function of one portion of the money capital.\" The metamorphosis C'-M-C is sale and purchase; if it creates no value for industrial capitalists, it cannot do so when performed by others. Likewise, the money capital that must always be on hand to keep reproduction continuous cannot acquire value-creating properties merely by being put into circulation by a specialized division of capitalists rather than by the industrial capitalist. Yet—and this is the pivot on which the entire chapter turns—\"since the circulation phase of industrial capital forms just as much a phase in the reproduction process as production does, the capital that functions independently in the circulation process must yield the average profit just as much as the capital that functions in the various branches of production.\" If commercial capital yielded a higher average profit than industrial capital, part of industrial capital would convert into commercial capital; if lower, the reverse would occur. No species of capital is more mobile, more able to \"change its function and designation,\" than commercial capital. The general rate of profit therefore imposes itself on commercial capital as a hard constraint. This generates the chapter's guiding question: Since commercial capital does not itself produce surplus-value, the surplus-value that accrues to it as average profit must be a portion of the surplus-value produced by productive capital as a whole—so how does commercial capital attract that share? Marx's answer unfolds through three interlocking movements: First , he demolishes the surface illusion—natural to the merchant's own standpoint—that commercial profit is a nominal addition to price, a selling of commodities above their value. He exposes this as an illusion by showing that, under the dominance of the capitalist mode of production, the merchant buys commodities below their value and sells them at their value (or real price of production). The merchant's sale price exceeds his purchase price not because it rises above total value, but because his purchase price lies below that total value. Second , he constructs a rigorous numerical model to prove this. With total industrial capital 720c + 180v = 900, s' = 100%, the product C has a value of 1,080 and a profit rate of 20%. Introducing an independent commercial capital of 100 changes the total capital sharing in surplus-value to 1,000; the general rate of profit falls to 18%. The industrial capitalist now sells to the merchant at 720c + 180v + 162s = 1,062— below the commodity's value. The merchant adds 18% to his own 100 and sells at 1,062 + 18 = 1,080, i.e., at value . He pockets the difference precisely because industrial capital omitted that portion from its own sale price. Third , Marx reformulates the very definition of \"price of production.\" Hitherto, price of production = cost (k) + average profit, computed on productive capital alone (k + 20%). Henceforth, the average profit is computed on total productive and commercial capital together (k + 18%). \"The real value or production price of the total commodity capital is therefore k + p + m\" (where m is commercial profit). The price at which the industrial capitalist sells as such is less than the real production price of the commodity; the price at which the industrial capitalist class as a whole sells is less than value. Two consequences are drawn: (1) The larger commercial capital is relative to industrial capital, the lower the rate of industrial profit, and vice versa. (2) The rate of profit always understates the rate of surplus-value—further exacerbated when commercial capital dilutes the average. The average rate of profit makes exploitation appear smaller than it is. Marx then inserts a historical–logical inversion : in the order of scientific analysis, the general rate of profit appears to originate in industrial capitals and their competition, and only later is it \"rectified, supplemented and modified\" by commercial capital. In the order of historical development, exactly the reverse holds. Commercial capital is chronologically prior: it first fixes commodities' prices more or less according to their values, and the circulation sphere mediates the reproduction process in which a general rate of profit first forms. Commercial profit originally determines industrial profit; only once the capitalist mode of production prevails does commercial profit get reduced to an aliquot share of the total surplus-value. The chapter then extends to the merchant's costs of circulation . He needs additional capital beyond his purchasing money capital. The merchant's money capital behaves like the industrial capitalist's fixed capital: insofar as it is not consumed, its value does not enter the commodity's value. Purely commercial circulation costs (accounting, book-keeping, correspondence, offices, paper, postage, commercial wages) proceed from \"the economic form of the product as a commodity\"; they realize value but create none. Yet this entire additional capital still enters the formation of the general rate of profit, thereby lowering it. Marx illustrates this with a further modification—adding 50 to the commercial capital to cover such costs reduces the average profit to 17⅓%—and argues the division between commercial and industrial capital permits a centralization and reduction of trading costs. The most concentrated sub-problem is the merchant's variable capital, b . Marx poses the dilemma with surgical precision: if b is not counted as capital advanced, the law of the equalization of the profit rate is violated (no capitalist would advance 150 if only 100 counted); if b is counted, the very nature of commercial capital seems contradicted, since it functions not by setting the labour of others in motion (as industrial capital does) but by itself performing buying and selling and thereby transferring to itself a portion of the surplus-value industrial capital created. His resolution is methodological: reduce commercial capital back to its origin in industrial capital. Commercial capital is nothing but the form in which a portion of industrial capital functioning in circulation has become autonomous. The b at issue must be investigated \"on the spot\"—first of all in the commercial office of the industrial capitalist himself. That office is initially \"infinitesimally small\" relative to the workshop, yet commercial operations grow with production. The outlay on commercial workers increases the capital advanced without directly increasing surplus-value: s stays constant, but C rises to C + ΔC, so s/C becomes s/(C + ΔC). Hence industrial capital behaves toward its commercial employees differently from productive wage-labourers: more productive workers increase surplus-value; more commercial workers are an effect of the value to be realized, never its cause. Marx then develops the commercial worker's position : a wage-labourer like any other (his labour bought with variable capital; his wage determined by the value of his labour-power), yet fundamentally different, because he creates no surplus-value. His unpaid labour, however, does something critical—it \"creates his ability to appropriate surplus-value.\" For the merchant, this is the source of profit. \"Just as the unpaid labour of the worker creates surplus-value for productive capital directly, so also does the unpaid labour of the commercial employee create a share in that surplus-value for commercial capital.\" The chapter closes with the concept of duplication : the functions of commodity capital and money capital are general formal determinations of industrial capital, and simultaneously become the exclusive domain of special capitals and a special class of capitalists—functions that develop into \"special spheres for the valorization of capital.\" Only with commercial capital do circulation costs acquire autonomy. The cost of circulation is, for industrial capital, an expense (which it is); for the merchant, this same cost is the source of his profit —proportional to its size. For commercial capital, circulation outlays are a productive investment, and the commercial labour it buys is, for it, directly productive. In sum, the chapter completes the transformation of surplus-value into profit by integrating independent circulation capital that shares in profit without generating surplus-value. It fills a gap left open when Marx first derived the general rate of profit, and it sets the stage for the later treatment of money-dealing capital, interest, and the division of surplus-value among the trinity of profit, rent, and wages. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Inherited Premise: Circulation Is Value-Neutral Marx opens by anchoring the entire chapter to a result he claims to have established in Volume 2, Chapter 5: the \"pure functions of capital in the circulation sphere create neither value nor surplus-value.\" The footnote explicitly points the reader there. These pure functions are the operations the industrial capitalist must undertake: first, to realize the value of his commodities; second, to transform this value back into the commodity-elements of production. In a formula, they are the operations that effect the metamorphoses of commodity capital C'-M-C—the acts of sale and purchase. Note the strong claim here: these operations produce nothing in the way of new value. And not only that: \"the time these operations require sets limits to the formation of value and surplus-value\"—objectively with respect to the commodities and subjectively with respect to the capitalist. Time spent selling is time not spent producing; therefore circulation time is a negative determinant of value formation, measured both in objective interruptions and in the capitalist's personal loss of productive capacity. Marx immediately extends the claim by the most important inferential step in the chapter's opening: what holds for the metamorphosis of commodity capital as such is \"naturally not changed in any way\" when part of that capital takes the form of commercial, commodity-dealing capital and the operations that effect the metamorphosis come to appear as \"the special business of a special section of capitalists, or as the exclusive function of one portion of the money capital.\" He offers two complementary arguments. First, if sale and purchase create no value for industrial capitalists, they cannot do so when performed by others—a non-value-creating function cannot become value-creating merely by changing hands . Second, if the money capital that must always exist to keep reproduction continuous creates no value when circulated by the industrial capitalist, it cannot acquire such properties simply because a specialized division of capitalists now circulates it. This is a \"conservation\" argument: value properties cannot be conjured by institutional reallocation of functions. Commercial Capital Defined by Its \"True Function\" Marx then performs an act of methodological stripping. Commercial capital, \"stripped of all the heterogeneous functions that may be linked to it, such as storage, dispatch, transport, distribution and retailing,\" and \"confined to its true function of buying in order to sell, creates neither value nor surplus-value, but simply facilitates their realization.\" This is a critical boundary-drawing move. Marx isolates the pure commercial core (buying to sell) so as to analyze it in isolation. The heterogenous functions—storage, dispatch, transport, distribution, retailing—are set aside because they belong to a different conceptual register (some are belated production processes; some are hybrid). The pure function facilitates the actual exchange of commodities, their transfer \"from one hand to another\"—\"society's metabolic process.\" Even as it facilitates this, it creates nothing. The consequence is posed as a rigorous necessity: \"since the circulation phase of industrial capital forms just as much a phase in the reproduction process as production does, the capital that functions independently in the circulation process must yield the average profit just as much as the capital that functions in the various branches of production.\" Here is the equalization argument in its purest form. Marx does not argue by analogy; he argues by competitive mobility . If commercial capital yielded a higher average profit than industrial, part of industrial capital would change into commercial capital. If it yielded a lower average profit, the opposite process would occur. And he adds a sociological observation that matters for the whole theory of capital mobility: \"No species of capital finds it easier than commercial capital to change its function and designation.\" Commercial capital's low specificity—its lack of fixed, specialized productive equipment—makes it the most mobile form of capital, and therefore the most immediately subject to the equalization mechanism. It is the availability of this escape route that ensures the average rate of profit binds commercial capital as tightly as it binds industrial capital. The Fundamental Question: How Does Commercial Capital Attract Its Share? From the fact that commercial capital produces no surplus-value, two things follow: (1) the surplus-value accruing to it as average profit is a portion of the surplus-value produced by productive capital as a whole; (2) the question becomes a transmission question, not a creation question. Marx states it exactly: \"How does commercial capital attract the part of the surplus-value or profit produced by productive capital that falls to its share?\" Everything that follows is an answer to this question. The Illusion of Nominal Price Increase Marx first states what the answer is not : it is \"a mere semblance that commercial profit is just a supplement, a nominal increase in the price of commodities above their value.\" Yet he immediately acknowledges the surface plausibility of this illusion. The merchant can obtain his profit only from the price of the commodities he sells; and this profit must equal the difference between his purchase price and his sale price. If additional costs of circulation enter after purchase and before sale, the excess of sale price over purchase price doesn't purely represent profit—but Marx temporarily abstracts from such costs (\"To simplify the investigation, however, we assume to start with that no costs of this nature are involved\"). He then contrasts the form of profit for the two kinds of capitalist. For the industrial capitalist, the difference between sale price and purchase price is the difference between the price of production and the cost price —or, at social level, between the value of commodities and their cost price for the capitalists, resolvable into the difference between total objectified labour and paid labour. Crucially, before the commodities the industrial capitalist buys go back on the market as commodities for sale, \"they pass through the production process, and it is here alone that the component of their price that will later be realized as profit is produced.\" The commercial dealer is different: \"He has commodities in his possession only as long as they are in their circulation process.\" He merely continues the sale begun by the productive capitalist, the realization of their price; he makes them undergo \"no intervening process in which they might absorb new surplus-value.\" Note the neat distinction Marx draws: the industrial capitalist realizes surplus-value in circulation; the merchant, by contrast, \"does not merely realize his profit in and through circulation, he also makes it there.\" This making it there is what needs explanation. Marx gives the surface appearance of how this is done: the merchant sells commodities bought at their prices of production at prices above their prices of production, or, taking commodity capital as a whole, at prices above their value—\"making a nominal addition to their prices,\" pocketing \"the difference between their nominal value and their real value, i.e. sells them dearer than they are.\" The Linen Illustration and Its Interpretation Marx then works the appearance through a small example, whose figures are partly garbled by OCR but whose logical content is clear: Say a yard of linen costs 2 shillings. To make 10% profit on resale, the merchant adds one-tenth: he sells at 2s. 2⅖d. (the text shows \"2td,\" an OCR corruption of 2s 2⅖d, i.e., 2.2 shillings). The difference between actual price of production and sale price is 2⅖d—a 10% profit on 2 shillings. The merchant sells a yard of linen at a price \"that is really the price of 1⅒ yards.\" Equivalently (and here the text garbles \"H yards\" and \"1⅒,\" reconciling to the reciprocal 10/11), it is \"just as if I sold the buyer only 10/11 of a yard for 2 shillings and kept 1/11 for myself.\" Or: the merchant can buy back 1/11 of a yard with the 2⅖d at the new price. This is \"simply a roundabout way of sharing in the surplus-value and surplus product by making a nominal increase in the prices of commodities.\" Marx's point in presenting this example is diagnostic, not advocated. He notes: \"This is the realization of commercial profit by an addition to the price of commodities, as it presents itself at first sight.\" And: \"the whole idea that profit is derived from a nominal increase in commodity prices, or by selling them above their value, arises from the viewpoint of commercial capital.\" So the illusion is standpoint-dependent —it is the natural ideology of the merchant. The Refutation: The Illusion Dissolved Marx's decisive move: \"When we look more closely, however, we soon see that this is just an illusion.\" But he adds a methodological caveat and bracket: \"assuming the predominance of the capitalist mode of production, this is not the way commercial profit is realized. (What we are dealing with here is always the average , and not individual cases.)\" The assumption of average and of developed capitalism matters—edge cases and undeveloped conditions may look otherwise. His argument: Why did we assume the merchant can only realize 10% by selling 10% above production price? Because we assumed the producer—the industrial capitalist, who faces the world as \"the producer\"—had sold them to the merchant at their price of production . If the merchant's purchase price equals the production price (ultimately value), then the excess of his sale price over his purchase price—his only profit source—must be an excess of commercial price over production price; at social level, the merchant sells all commodities above their value. But then Marx unearths the hidden premise: \"But why did we assume that the industrial capitalist sold commodities to the merchant at their prices of production?... That merchant's capital... does not enter into the formation of the general rate of profit.\" In the earlier derivation of the general rate of profit, this assumption was necessary —first because merchant's capital did not yet exist for the analysis, and second because the average profit had to be developed as an equalization of the profits actually produced by industrial capitals in different spheres. But commercial capital is \"a capital that takes a share in profit without participating in production.\" Therefore the earlier presentation must be supplemented . This is an unusually self-aware methodological moment: Marx flags that the previous treatment was an abstraction that now needs lifting. Marx's Numerical Model: The Central Proof Marx lays out the correction numerically. Let total industrial capital advanced during the year be: - constant capital c = 720 - variable capital v = 180 - total = 900 (in millions of pounds sterling, as he stipulates) - rate of surplus-value s' = 100%, so surplus-value s = 180 The product (commodity capital C) = 720c + 180v + 180s = 1,080. Its value or price of production (the two coincide when we take the totality of commodities) equals 1,080. The rate of profit on the total capital of 900 is 180/900 = 20% . Marx notes explicitly that this 20% is the average rate of profit, because the surplus-value is here reckoned not on any particular capital of specific composition but on the total industrial capital with average composition. Now introduce an independent commercial capital of 100. Total capital becomes 1,000. This commercial capital takes a proportionate share in profit according to its size—one-tenth of the total. It therefore takes a one-tenth share in the total surplus-value of 180 , which is 18; and this corresponds to a profit rate of 18% on the commercial capital. The profit to be divided among the remaining nine-tenths (the industrial capital of 900) is now only 180 − 18 = 162 , or 18% on 900. The price at which C is sold to the merchants by the industrial capitalists is therefore: 720c + 180v + 162s = 1,062 The merchant, adding the average profit of 18% to his capital of 100, sells the commodities at: 1,062 + 18 = 1,080 —i.e., at their price of production or, taking commodity capital as a whole, at their value . And yet, crucially, the merchant \"only makes his profit in and through circulation and only by the excess of his sale price over and above his purchase price.\" The apparent contradiction is dissolved: if he still does not sell above value or price of production, this is \"precisely because he bought them from the industrial capitalists below their value or price of production.\" Reformulating the Price of Production This numerical result obliges Marx to revise a core concept. Commercial capital contributes to the general rate of profit \"according to the proportion it forms in the total capital.\" If the average rate of profit is 18% in the case considered, it would have been 20% if one-tenth of the total capital had not been commercial and the general rate had not consequently been reduced by one-tenth. Marx then gives the \"stricter and more accurate definition of the production price\": As before, the price of production = cost (the value of constant and variable capital contained) + average profit on this. But now, the average profit is determined by the total profit the total productive capital produces, yet it is not calculated just on this total productive capital alone . If productive capital is 900 and profit is 180, the earlier rate would be 180/900 = 20%. Now it is calculated on the total productive and commercial capital together: 180/(900+100) = 180/1,000 = 18% . Hence price of production = k + 18%, not k + 20%. And then the final formula: \"The real value or production price of the total commodity capital is therefore k + p + m (where m is commercial profit).\" The price of production—the price at which the industrial capitalist sells as such —is less than the real production price of the commodity; or, in aggregate, the price at which the industrial capitalist class sells is less than value. In the example: 900 (cost) + 18% of 900 = 900 + 162 = 1,062. Since the merchant sells at 118 what cost him 100, he \"still adds 18%\"; but because the commodities he bought at 100 are worth 118, he does not sell them above their value . In future, Marx says, we keep the expression \"price of production\" for this more exact sense. The editor's footnote here (F.E.) clarifies the terminological tangle: \"price of production\" now means the price at which the industrial capitalist sells to the merchant , not, as Marx originally developed the concept, the price at which the industrial capitalist sells when no independent commercial capital intervenes. The footnote confesses it is \"rather confusing\" that Marx still refers to the latter as the \"real price of production,\" but it is presumably because it is at that price that the commodity is finally sold. This is an important editorial nuance: the reader must track which of two distinct prices \"price of production\" designates at each moment. Marx then states the three-part decomposition with great clarity: - The industrial capitalist's profit = the excess of the production price of his commodity over its cost price . - Commercial profit = the excess of the sale price over the commodity's production price (the merchant's purchase price). - The real price of the commodity = its production price + the commercial profit. And the symmetry: just as industrial capital only realizes profit already contained in the commodity's value as surplus-value, so commercial capital does so only because the whole of the surplus-value is not yet realized in the price of the commodity as realized by industrial capital. Note the footnote citing John Bellers ( Essays about the Poor, Manufactures, Trade, Plantations, and Immorality , London, 1699, p. 10)—a historical anchor for the idea that merchant profit is a deduction, a citing of a pre-classical source who already intimated the mechanism. The essential formula for the merchant: \"The merchant's sale price is higher than his purchase price not because it is above the total value, but rather because his purchase price is below this total value.\" Commercial Capital Inside the Equalization Process Marx summarizes: \"Commercial capital is involved in the equalization of surplus-value that forms average profit, therefore, even though it is not involved in the production of this surplus-value.\" The general rate of profit \"thus already takes account of the deduction from the surplus-value which falls to commercial capital, i.e. a deduction from the profit of industrial capital.\" This is a structural claim about the rate of profit, not merely an accounting convention: the general rate is not produced by industrial capital and then modified; commercial capital is constitutive of the rate from the start, once it exists. Two Consequences and the Dilution Effect Marx draws two consequences: (1) Inverse relation. \"The bigger commercial capital is in comparison with industrial capital, the smaller the rate of industrial profit, and vice versa.\" This is a direct corollary of the denominator in the average profit rate. It means that the growth of the commercial apparatus of capitalism is a systematic depressant on the industrial profit rate—a point of substantial importance for the theory of capitalist development. (2) The rate of profit understates exploitation. Marx recalls that in Part One it was shown that the rate of profit is always expressed in a lower figure than the rate of actual surplus-value—it \"underestimates the exploitation of labour.\" In the present case: 720c + 180v + 180s yields s' = 100% but a profit rate of only 20%. With commercial capital, this difference is still greater: the average profit, taking into account the share accruing to commercial capital, is here 18% instead of 20%. \"The average rate of profit for the directly exploiting capitalist thus makes the rate of profit appear smaller than it actually is.\" The veil of the profit form is thickened by commercial capital's participation. The Relative Size of Commercial Capital and Turnover Marx posits a regular relation: \"Assuming that all other circumstances are the same, the relative size of commercial capital (though retail traders, a hybrid species, form an exception) will be in inverse proportion to the speed of its turnover, i.e. in inverse proportion to the overall vigour of the reproduction process.\" This is an elegant general law: the faster circulation performs its function, the less commercial capital is required relative to industrial capital. Retail traders form an explicit exception—a caveat Marx flags without full development here (retailers operate with high turnover of small capitals and myriad transactions, so the general rule doesn't apply cleanly). The Historical–Logical Inversion This is one of the chapter's most philosophically weighty passages. Marx distinguishes two orders: In scientific analysis: \"The formation of the general rate of profit appears to proceed from industrial capitals and the competition between them, being only later rectified, supplemented and modified by the intervention of commercial capital.\" In historical development: \"The situation is exactly the reverse.\" He spells out the historical sequence. It is commercial capital that first fixes the prices of commodities \"more or less according to their values\"; it is the sphere of circulation that mediates the reproduction process in which a general rate of profit is first formed . \"Commercial profit originally determines industrial profit.\" Only when the capitalist mode of production has come to prevail, and the producer has himself become a merchant, is commercial profit \"reduced to the aliquot share of the total surplus-value that accrues to commercial capital as an aliquot part of the total capital concerned in the process of social reproduction.\" This is a specific application of Marx's wider method: the logical order of presentation (industrial → commercial) is the inverse of the historical order (commercial → industrial). The abstraction that builds from production first is scientifically correct precisely because it isolates the essential relation (exploitation of labour) that the historically prior commercial form actually presupposes without yet expressing it. The nuance is significant: the deduced primacy of industrial capital is a logical primacy, not a chronicle. The Merchant's Money Capital and the Analogy to Fixed Capital Marx returns to the \"supplementary equalization of profits brought about by the intervention of commercial capital\" to make a further analytical point. No additional element goes into the value of the commodity for the money capital the merchant advances. The addition to price whereby the merchant makes his profit is \"simply the portion of commodity value that productive capital has not included in the production price of the commodity, and has in fact left out.\" He then draws a striking analogy: the case of this money capital \"is similar to that of the industrial capitalist's fixed capital. In so far as it is not consumed, its value does not constitute an element of the commodity's value.\" This is a subtle point about recycling rather than consumption. The merchant's money capital is repeatedly advanced and recovered; it is not consumed into the commodity (as raw materials are), so it does not enter value. In the price the merchant pays, he replaces its production price, = M, in money. His sale price = M + ΔM , where ΔM is the addition to commodity price determined by the general rate of profit. On selling, he recovers both the original money capital and ΔM. Crucially: \"his money capital is nothing more than the commodity capital of the industrial capitalist turned into money capital, which can no more affect the value of this commodity capital than if the latter were sold directly to the final consumer instead of to the merchant.\" All that actually happens is that the final consumer's payment is anticipated . But—and here Marx introduces the next qualification—\"this is correct only if, as we have so far assumed, the merchant has no expenses,\" i.e., if he advances no other capital, circulating or fixed, besides the money capital for purchasing commodities. The Costs of Circulation Enter the Analysis The assumption of zero expenses must now be dropped: \"This is not the case, however, as we saw in discussing the costs of circulation (Volume 2, Chapter 6).\" These costs are of two types: (a) costs the merchant must reclaim from other agents of circulation; (b) costs arising directly from his specific business. Whatever their kind—whether they belong to the merchant's specific circulation costs, or represent charges arising from \"belated production processes that are inserted within the circulation process\" (dispatch, transport, storage, etc.)—they always require, beyond the money capital advanced for commodity purchase, an additional capital advanced to purchase and pay for these means of circulation. Marx then distinguishes by the type of capital involved: - Circulating component: goes completely into the sale price as an additional element. - Fixed component: goes in according to the degree of its depreciation . - In so far as these are purely commercial costs of circulation: this element forms \"only a nominal value and not a real addition to commodity value.\" - But regardless of circulating or fixed: \"this entire additional capital goes into the formation of the general rate of profit.\" This last clause is the essential one: even costs that add only nominal value to commodities still raise the capital base on which the general rate of profit is computed, thereby depressing it. This is a structural parallel to Marx's treatment of constant capital in the productive sphere. Purely Commercial Costs: Definition and Content Marx now isolates the \"purely commercial costs of circulation\" (excluding dispatch, transport, storage). They are the costs \"necessary to realize the value of the commodity, whether transforming it from commodity into money or from money into commodity—to effect its exchange.\" He carefully limits the scope: he ignores eventual production processes that continue during circulation and can exist separately from commerce as such—e.g., the transport industry proper and dispatch of goods \"can be completely separate from trade, as branches of industry, and actually are so.\" Goods may be kept in docks and public storage areas, with costs charged to the merchant by third parties, in so far as he advances them. All this is found in wholesale trade proper, \"where commercial capital appears in its purest form, hardly mixed up at all with other functions.\" And then the memorable line: \"The haulier, the railway director and the shipowner are not 'merchants.'\" This is a boundary-setting statement—it keeps the concept of commercial capital disciplined. The costs considered are those of buying and selling. They break down into: - accounting, book-keeping; - marketing, correspondence, etc. Their constant capital consists of offices, paper, postage, etc. Their remaining costs are reducible to variable capital advanced for commercial employees . (Marx parenthetically notes that dispatch charges, transport costs, advances of customs duties, etc. may partly be considered as advanced by the merchant in the purchase of commodities, and hence go into his purchase price.) Marx then asserts a decisive characterization: \"All these costs are incurred not in the production of the commodities' use-value, but rather in the realization of their value; they are pure costs of circulation.\" They don't enter the immediate production process but do enter the circulation process and hence the overall process of reproduction. Only the part laid out as variable capital concerns us at this point, and Marx lists three further questions requiring investigation: (1) how the law that only necessary labour goes into commodity value applies in the circulation process; (2) how accumulation appears in the case of commercial capital; (3) how commercial capital functions in the actual overall process of social reproduction. This signals that the present chapter is programmatic, not exhaustive. He concludes the definition with a crucial genetic statement: \"These costs arise from the economic form of the product as a commodity.\" They are not technically necessary costs of making the thing; they are historically necessary costs of making the thing as a commodity , i.e., of validating it through exchange. Labour-Time in Circulation: The Continuity Argument Marx now disposes of a possible objection by a continuity argument. If the labour-time the industrial capitalists lose in selling commodities directly to one another—which is, in objective terms, the circulation time of the commodities—does not add value, then this labour-time \"does not change its character by devolving on the merchant instead of the industrial capitalist.\" The transformation of commodities (products) into money and money into commodities (means of production) is a necessary function of industrial capital, hence a necessary operation for the capitalist, who is simply \"personified capital, capital endowed with its own consciousness and will.\" But these functions neither increase value nor create surplus-value. The merchant, performing these operations and carrying on the functions of capital in circulation after the productive capitalist has ceased to do so, \"simply takes the place of the industrial capitalist.\" The labour-time these operations cost is employed on necessary operations in the reproduction process of capital, \"but it does not add any extra value.\" Marx then draws a direct consequence for the merchant's own income. If the merchant did not perform these operations, and so did not spend the labour-time they require, \"he would not be using his capital as a circulation agent of industrial capital; he would not be continuing the function that the industrial capitalist has abandoned, and hence would not share as a capitalist, and in proportion to the capital he advanced, in the mass of profit produced by the industrial capitalist class.\" The merchant's claim on surplus-value derives from performing a necessary social function —not from creating value. The legitimating basis of his income is functional, not productive. Therefore: \"the merchant capitalist does not need to employ any wage-labourers in order to take a share in this mass of surplus-value, to valorize his advance as capital.\" If his business and capital are small, he may himself be the only worker employed. He is paid out of the part of profit that accrues to him from the difference between his purchase price and the real price of production. And, in a passage that anticipates the later theory of the commercial petty bourgeoisie : \"if the capital advanced by the merchant is small, the profit he realizes may not be any greater than the wage of a better-paid skilled worker; it may even be less than this.\" Functioning alongside him are the direct commercial agents of productive capitalists—buyers, salesmen, commercial travellers—receiving the same income or higher, whether as wage or as a share in profit (commission, percentage). In one case the merchant pockets commercial profit as an independent capitalist; in the other, the direct employee of the industrial capitalist is paid a part of the profit as wage or proportionate share, and the principal pockets both the industrial and the commercial profit. But in all cases, \"even though the income the circulation agent receives may appear to him as a simple wage, as payment for the work he has performed,\" and even if the size of his profit may only equal a better-paid worker's wage, \"this income still derives solely from the commercial profit. This results from the fact that his labour is not value-creating labour.\" This is a pivotal claim for understanding the ideological form of commercial income as \"wages of work.\" Time-Costs for the Industrial Capitalist and the Division-of-Function Advantage Marx now examines the consequences from the standpoint of industrial capital. A prolonged circulation operation means for the industrial capitalist: (1) a personal loss of time, insofar as he is prevented from performing his function as director of production; (2) an extended stay of his product, in money or commodity form, in the circulation process, where it is not valorized and the immediate process of production is interrupted. If production is not to be interrupted, either production must be cut back, or additional money capital must be advanced so production can continue on the same scale. Either way: \"either a smaller profit is made with the former capital or additional money capital has to be advanced in order to make the previous profit.\" The same logic holds when the merchant replaces the industrial capitalist. Instead of the industrial capitalist spending more time in circulation, the merchant now spends the time; instead of the industrial capitalist advancing additional capital for circulation, the merchant advances it; or (the same thing), \"whereas previously a substantial portion of the industrial capital was constantly entering and leaving the circulation process, now the merchant's capital is cooped up there permanently.\" Previously the industrial capitalist made a smaller profit; now he must abandon a part of his profit completely to the merchant. But here Marx states the advantage of the division of function: in so far as commercial capital remains confined to the limits within which it is necessary, \"the distinction is simply that this division of capital's function enables less time to be devoted exclusively to the circulation process, and less additional capital advanced for it, so that the reduction in the total profit which now takes the form of commercial profit is less than it would be otherwise.\" He gives a numerical variant: In the earlier example (720c + 180v + 180s, plus commercial capital 100), the industrial capitalist is left 162, i.e., 18%, a deduction of 18. But if he had to conduct without the independent operations of commercial capital, the additional capital needed might be 200, so the total advance would be 1,100 instead of 900, and the surplus-value of 180 would represent a profit rate of only 16 4/11% . The comparison (18% deduction vs. 16 4/11% profit rate) is not apples-to-apples (one is a deduction, one is a rate), but the direction is clear: the division of functions reduces the total loss of profit to industrial capital. Additional Capital for Circulation Costs Taken Over by the Merchant Marx extends: if the industrial capitalist who is his own merchant has advanced—besides the additional capital to buy new commodities before the product in circulation has been transformed into money—still further capital to realize the value of his commodity capital (office expenses and wages for commercial employees), then this certainly forms additional capital but \"does not form any more surplus-value.\" It must be replaced out of the commodities' value; a portion of this value must be reconverted into these circulation costs, even though no additional surplus-value is formed. As far as the total social capital is concerned, \"a part of this capital is required for secondary operations that are not a part of the valorization process, and this part of the social capital has to be constantly reproduced for this purpose.\" In this way, the profit rate for both individual capitalists and the industrial capitalist class as a whole is reduced—\"a result that follows from every injection of additional capital in so far as this is required to set the same amount of variable capital in motion.\" If these additional costs are taken over by the merchant, there is still a reduction in the profit rate, \"but to a lesser extent and in a different manner.\" The merchant now advances more capital than would be necessary if these costs did not exist; the profit on this additional capital raises the total commercial profit; commercial capital enters the equalization of the average rate of profit \"on a greater scale,\" and the average profit falls. Numerical illustration: If a further additional capital of 50 is advanced for these costs, besides the commercial capital of 100, the total surplus-value of 180 is distributed between a productive capital of 900 and a commercial capital of 150, making a total of 1,050. The average rate of profit falls to 180/1,050 = 17 1/7% . The industrial capitalist sells to the merchant at 900 + 154 2/7 = 1,054 2/7; the merchant sells for 1,130 (1,080 + 50 for expenses he must recover). Marx adds the assumption that the division between commercial and industrial capital \"involves a centralization of trading costs and a consequent reduction in them.\" So the merchant's larger scale compensates partially for the dilution. The Position of the Commercial Wage-Labourer The chapter now turns to the commercial employees employed by the merchant capitalist. Marx offers a two-sided characterization. From one point of view, a commercial employee is \"a wage-labourer like any other\": First , his labour is bought with the merchant's variable capital , not with money spent as revenue; it is bought \"not for a personal service but for the purpose of valorizing the capital advanced in it.\" (This is the general criterion distinguishing productive from unproductive consumption of labour-power within capitalist production.) Second , the value of his labour-power, and therefore his wage, is determined, like all wage-labourers', by the production and reproduction costs of this particular labour-power, \"and not by the product of his labour.\" But there is necessarily a difference, \"the same difference between him and the workers directly employed by industrial capital as there is between industrial capital and commercial capital, and consequently between the industrial capitalist and the merchant.\" Since the merchant, as pure circulation agent, produces neither value nor surplus-value (the additional value he adds is reducible to the addition of previously existing value, though Marx concedes the question here of how he maintains and conserves the value of this constant capital still remains to be examined), \"the commercial workers whom he employs in these same functions cannot possibly create surplus-value for him directly.\" Marx is careful to add the standard abstraction: just as with productive workers, we assume wages are determined by the value of labour-power, so the merchant \"does not enrich himself by a deduction from wages,\" and in reckoning his costs \"does not put down an advance for labour that he only pays in part.\" So Marx explicitly excludes cheating on wages from the analysis to isolate the structural mechanism. This is the same methodological bracket applied to productive workers. The Resolution of the Apparent Paradox: How Commercial Workers Yield Profit Without Creating Surplus-Value The \"problem\" that commercial workers seem to pose—how they produce profit for employers even though they produce no surplus-value—Marx declares already resolved by the general analysis of commercial profit. The mechanism is a transfer within the total surplus-value , not a creation of new value: \"Just as industrial capital makes its profit by selling labour that is already contained and realized in the commodity, labour for which it has not paid an equivalent, so commercial capital makes a profit by not paying productive capital in full for the unpaid labour contained in the commodity (in so far as the capital laid out in order to produce the commodity functions as an aliquot part of the total industrial capital), and, as against this, itself receiving the additional portion which it has not paid for once the commodity has been sold.\" The relation to surplus-value differs between the two capitals: - \"The latter [industrial] produces surplus-value by directly appropriating the unpaid labour of others.\" - \"The former [commercial] appropriates a portion of this surplus-value by getting it transferred from industrial capital to itself.\" So the merchant's claim is a redistribution claim. He gets a share because he performs a function that industrial capital needs done and pays for by accepting a lower price. The Source of the Individual Merchant's Profit: Unpaid Commercial Labour Marx then specifies how the individual merchant's profit expands. \"It is only by way of its function in the realization of values that commercial capital functions as capital in the reproduction process, and therefore draws, as functioning capital, on the surplus-value that the total capital produces.\" For the individual merchant, the amount of his profit \"depends on the amount of capital that he can employ in this process,\" and he can employ all the more capital in buying and selling \"the greater the unpaid labour of his clerks.\" This is the key mechanism: the merchant's appropriation of surplus-value is scaled by the unpaid labour of his employees. \"The very function by virtue of which the commercial capitalist's money is capital is performed in large measure by his employees, on his instructions.\" Their unpaid labour, \"even though it does not create surplus-value, does create his ability to appropriate surplus-value, which, as far as this capital is concerned, gives exactly the same result; i.e. it is its source of profit.\" Otherwise, \"the business of commerce could never be conducted in the capitalist manner, or on a large scale.\" Note the double negative patience of Marx's formulation. He is careful not to say that commercial labour creates surplus-value. He says it creates the capacity to appropriate it. The distinction is analytically crucial—and it is precisely here that Marx distinguishes his own theory from the vulgar view that all wage labour equally produces profit. The symmetry he asserts is structural, not substantive: \"Just as the unpaid labour of the worker creates surplus-value for productive capital directly, so also does the unpaid labour of the commercial employee create a share in that surplus-value for commercial capital.\" The Remaining Difficulty: The Nature and Limits of b Now Marx states the real remaining difficulty. Since the merchant's own labour-time is not value-creating even though it procures him a share in surplus-value already produced, what is the situation with the variable capital he lays out on the purchase of commercial labour-power? Two horns: If b is NOT included in the cost of the commercial capital advanced: this would seem to contradict the law of equalization of the profit rate—\"what capitalist would advance 150, if he could reckon only 100 of it as capital advanced?\" If b IS included : this would seem to contradict the very nature of commercial capital, since this kind of capital \"does not function as capital by setting the labour of others in motion, in the manner of industrial capital, but rather by itself working, i.e. itself performing the functions of buying and selling, and it is precisely in this way that it transfers to itself a part of the surplus-value the industrial capital has created.\" Marx then enumerates the further questions this opens (merchant's variable capital; the law of necessary labour in the circulation sphere; how the work of the merchant maintains the value of his constant capital; the role of commercial capital in overall reproduction; and the division of commodity capital / money capital on the one hand and commercial capital / money-dealing capital on the other). These are deferred. The Fragmentation Argument Before resolving the b problem formally, Marx develops its dialectical material basis. If each merchant possessed only the capital he was personally able to turn over by his own work, \"there would be an infinite fragmentation of commercial capital.\" And this fragmentation would necessarily increase with the progress of the capitalist mode of production—because productive capital produces on larger and larger scales, the disproportion between productive and commercial capital would grow. \"To the same extent as capital was centralized in the sphere of production, it would be decentralized in the sphere of circulation.\" The industrial capitalist would then have to deal with 100 or 1,000 different merchants; the advantages of the autonomous position of commercial capital would be lost; and other circulation costs (grading, dispatch, etc.) would also grow. Then, considering commercial capital itself: commercial work proper takes no more time to reckon with large figures than small; it takes ten times longer to make ten purchases of £100 than one purchase of £1,000; ten times the correspondence work, paper, and postage to write to ten small merchants as to one big one. A division of labour in the commercial office —one person keeps books, another the cash-box, a third writes letters, one buys, another sells, another travels—\"spares a tremendous amount of labour-time,\" so the number of workers in wholesale trade is \"in no way proportionate to the comparative scale of the transactions.\" Marx's conclusion: \"In commerce, in fact, far more than in industry, the same function takes the same amount of labour-time whether it is performed on a large or small scale.\" This is a claim of returns to scale (or, more precisely, of near-constant unit labour-time and hence strong scale economies). \"Thus concentration historically appears in commerce earlier than in the industrial workshop.\" Added to this are the expenses for constant capital: a hundred small offices cost infinitely more than one big one; a hundred small warehouses more than a big one; transport costs grow with fragmentation. So: the industrial capitalist would spend more labour and incur greater circulation costs on the commercial side of his business. The same commercial capital, divided among many small merchants, would require many more workers to carry out its functions and \"a larger commercial capital would be required to turn over the same commodity capital.\" Symbolic Formulation and the \"Limits of b\" Marx now introduces formal notation: B = the total commercial capital directly invested in the buying and selling of commodities. b = the corresponding variable capital laid out for payment of commercial assistants. Then: B + b will necessarily be less than the total commercial capital B would be if each merchant struggled by without assistance (i.e., if one portion was not invested as b). The hiring of commercial assistants reduces the total commercial capital needed, even as it adds b, because of the scale economies just described. But the problem is not finished. The price at which commodities are sold must be sufficient: 1. \"to pay the average profit on B + b.\" This is already explained by the fact that B + b is always a reduction on the original B and represents a smaller commercial capital than would be needed without the b. 2. \"to replace, besides the seemingly additional profit on b, also the wages paid, the merchant's actual variable capital = b.\" The second requirement creates the difficulty. Does b form a new component of the price , or is it \"simply a part of the profit made with B + b that appears as wages as far as the commercial employees are concerned and appears to the merchant himself as simply the replacement of his variable capital\"? If the latter: the profit the merchant makes on B + b would be equal simply to the profit accruing to B, per the general rate—b being paid as wages, but without this yielding any profit . \"It all boils down to finding the limits of b (in the mathematical sense).\" Marx then sharpens the notation: - B = capital directly laid out on buying and selling commodities. - K = the constant capital utilized for this function (the material expenses involved). - b = the variable capital the merchant lays out. The Replacement of B and K Replacement of B presents no difficulty. For the merchant, B is simply the realized purchase price, or the price of production for the manufacturer. The merchant pays this price, and on resale he receives B back as a portion of his sale price, plus the profit on B. Example: commodity costs £100, profit 10%; sold at 110. The commodity \"still costs 100, as before,\" so the commercial capital of 100 \"only adds 10 to it\"—i.e., B is simply passed through, and the profit on B is the only increment to price. Replacement of K : K is at most as large as, though in actual fact smaller than, the portion of constant capital the producer would need for selling and buying (in addition to the constant capital he uses directly in production). This part must always be recovered in the price of the commodity, or, equivalently, a corresponding part of the commodity must always be spent and reproduced in this form, taking the total social capital as a whole. This portion of constant capital advanced would have \"the same constricting effect on the profit rate as does all constant capital directly invested in production.\" Insofar as the industrial capitalist hands over the commercial side of his business to the merchant, he doesn't need to advance this portion—the merchant does. But Marx notes \"this is really only an advance in name,\" in as much as the merchant \"neither produces nor reproduces the constant capital that he uses (his material expenses).\" The production of these appears therefore as a separate business of certain industrial capitalists (or a part of their business), so that these \"play the same role as those supplying constant capital to the producers of means of subsistence.\" The merchant thus receives first the replacement for this constant capital, and second the profit on it. On both counts, the profit of the industrial capitalist is reduced. But because of the concentration and economy that results from the division of labour, this reduction is less than it would be if he had to advance this capital himself. \"The reduction in the profit rate is less, because the capital advanced in this way is less.\" The Sale Price with b Included The comprehensive formula. Formerly the sale price was B + K + profit on (B + K). Now: Sale price = B + K + b + profit on (B + K) + profit on b. B only replaces the purchase price; it adds nothing to this price besides the profit on B. K adds not only the profit on K but also K itself. But K + the profit on K—the portion of circulation costs advanced as constant capital + the corresponding average profit—\"would be greater in the hands of the industrial capitalist than in the hands of the commercial capitalist.\" The reduction in the average profit takes this form: the full average profit is calculated after the deduction of B + K from the industrial capital advanced . This deduction from the average profit for B + K is paid to the merchant , so that it appears as the profit of a special capital, commercial capital. b + profit on b : \"this is where the real difficulty lies.\" What the Merchant Buys with b Marx restates: What the merchant buys with b is \"merely commercial labour,\" i.e., labour needed for the functions of capital circulation, C-M and M-C. Commercial labour is the labour always necessary for a capital to function as commercial capital, for it to mediate the transformation of commodities into money and money into commodities. \"It is labour that realizes values but does not create any.\" And only in so far as a capital performs these functions—only in so far as a capitalist performs these operations and this labour with his capital—does the capital function as commercial capital and take part in settling the general rate of profit, drawing dividends from total profit. But in (b + profit on b) it seems that: (1) first the labour is paid (since it comes to the same thing whether the industrial capitalist pays the merchant for his own labour or for that of his employees); and (2) secondly, the profit on the payment for this labour that the merchant himself would have had to perform. Commercial capital seems to receive both the repayment of b and the profit on it—because it first gets paid for the labour by which it functions as commercial capital, and secondly gets the profit because it functions as capital, i.e., in its capacity as a functioning capital, it performs labour that is paid for in profit. \"This is the question which we have to resolve.\" The Numerical Decomposition of the b Problem Marx sets up the calculation. Let: B = 100 b = 10 rate of profit p' = 10% K = 0 (to avoid unnecessarily reintroducing an element of the purchase price already dealt with) Then: Purchase price = (B + profit on B) + (b + profit on b) = B + Bp' + b + bp' = 100 + 10 + 10 + 1 = 121. Now the counterfactual: if the merchant did not lay out this b on wages—since b is paid simply for commercial labour, i.e., labour needed to realize the value of the commodity capital industrial capital puts on the market—the situation would be as follows. To buy or sell B = 100, the merchant gives up his time, and we assume this is the only time available to him. The commercial labour represented by b = 10, if it were not paid as wages but rather by way of profit, would presuppose another commercial capital of 100 , since 10% of this gives b = 10. This second B = 100 would not go additionally into the price of the commodity, but the 10% certainly would. There would therefore be two operations at 100, giving 200, buying commodities for 200 + 20 = 220 . This is a subtle argument. Marx shows that the profit-based compensation for commercial labour requires a doubling of the merchant's capital (from 100 to 200)—because his own labour-time can only turn over B = 100, and the extra commercial labour must be recruited either by wages (b = 10) or by a second merchant's capital of 100 receiving 10% profit (= 10). If recruited by wages, the price is 121; if recruited by a second capital, the price would be 220 for double the goods, i.e., 110 per single unit—which reveals that the wage-based route requires only b = 10 as an additional price component , not an additional capital of 100. This is precisely why commercial employees are necessary for large-scale commerce: hiring b-labour is cheaper (to industrial capital) than duplicating merchant capital. And this explains the \"limits of b\": b is bounded by the cost of the alternative (a second B), and hence b enters the price as a cost like any other, and also earns profit as part of the total commercial capital advanced . The Methodological Reduction: Commercial Capital as a Branch of Industrial Capital Marx states the governing methodological principle of the whole resolution: \"Since commercial capital is nothing at all but the form in which a part of the industrial capital functioning in the circulation process has become autonomous, all questions relating to it must be resolved in this way: the problem must at the outset be put in the form in which the phenomena peculiar to commercial capital do not yet appear independently but are still in direct connection with industrial capital, of which commercial capital is a branch.\" So the b at issue \"must firstly be investigated on the spot, in the commercial office of the industrial capitalist himself.\" This is the analytical equivalent of rewinding the tape to the moment before the commercial function became autonomous, and examining the wage payments for commercial labour as they appear inside industrial capital. The Commercial Office of the Industrial Capitalist \"Right from the beginning, this office is always infinitesimally small in relation to the industrial workshop.\" Yet it is evident that as production expands, the commercial operations required by the circulation of industrial capital increase—both those required to sell the product as commodity capital and those required to transform the money obtained back into means of production, plus keeping the accounts for the whole process. Price calculation, book-keeping, fund management, and correspondence are all part of this. \"The more the scale of production grows, the greater are industrial capital's commercial operations, although the increase is by no means in the same proportion\" (again the scale-economy qualification), \"and the greater also the labour and other circulation costs involved in the realization of value and surplus-value.\" It is necessary therefore to employ commercial workers \"who make up a proper commercial office.\" The expenditure on this, \"even though incurred in the form of wages, is distinct from the variable capital laid out on the purchase of productive labour.\" Note the crucial distinction: this is wages, but not variable capital in the sense of capital that purchases productive labour . It increases the outlays of the industrial capitalist, the mass of capital he has to advance, without directly increasing surplus-value : \"For this is an outlay for labour employed simply in realizing values already created.\" Like other outlays of the same kind, this reduces the rate of profit, because the capital advanced grows, but not the surplus-value. Formal statement: The surplus-value s remains constant, but the capital advanced C grows from C to C + ΔC, so the profit rate s/C is replaced by s/(C + ΔC) . (The text's notation \"~o\" and \"c /,... c\" is OCR-garbled; the intended relation is s/C → s/(C + ΔC).) The industrial capitalist therefore attempts to keep these circulation costs to a minimum, just as he does his outlay on constant capital. The Asymmetry Between Commercial and Productive Wage-Labour The crucial consequence: \"Industrial capital therefore does not behave towards its commercial employees as it does to its productive wage-labourers.\" The more productive workers are employed, other things being equal, \"the more massive is production and the greater the surplus-value or profit.\" By contrast, the greater the scale of production and the greater the value and surplus-value to be realized—and therefore the greater the commodity capital produced—\"the more, accordingly, do office expenses grow in absolute terms, even if not relatively,\" and the more occasion there is for a particular kind of division of labour. The dependency runs one way: profit is the prerequisite for these outlays, not the consequence. Marx observes this is shown by the way commercial salaries are often paid partly as a percentage of profit—evidence that the salary is a share of a pre-existing fund , not a claim derived from value its recipient created. And note the essential asymmetry in a strong form: \"A labour that consists simply in intermediary operations, involving partly the calculation of values, partly their realization, and partly again the transformation of the money realized back into means of production, a labour whose scope thus depends on the magnitude of values produced and to be realized—that a labour of this kind functions not as the cause of the respective magnitudes and amounts of these values, as does directly productive labour, but is rather a consequence of them.\" And he illustrates this with the parallel of circulation costs generally: \"If there is much to be weighed, measured, packed and transported, there must be plenty there in the first place. The amount of packing and transport work, etc. depends on the mass of the commodities that are objects of this activity and not the other way round.\" This is an important dialectical point: the material dependent status of circulation labour is masked by its wage-form, which makes it look like any other labour. The Commercial Worker's Wage and Its Tendency to Fall Marx now consolidates the position of the commercial worker and develops a dynamic law of his wage. The commercial worker does not produce surplus-value directly. But the price of his labour is determined by the value of his labour-power, i.e., its cost of production, \"although the exercise of this labour-power, the exertion, expenditure of energy and wear and tear it involves, is no more limited by the value of his labour-power than it is in the case of any other wage-labourer.\" This restates the standard distinction between the value of labour-power and its exercise (use-value as labour): the capitalist extracts as much labour as he can, but pays only for the reproductive cost of the labour-power. His wage therefore does not stand in any necessary relationship to the amount of profit he helps the capitalist realize. \"What he costs the capitalist and what he brings in for him are different quantities.\" What he brings in is \"a function not of any direct creation of surplus-value but of his assistance in reducing the cost of realizing surplus-value, in so far as he performs labour (part of it unpaid).\" The parenthetical \"(part of it unpaid)\" is critical—it names the appropriation relationship. The commercial worker proper belongs to the better-paid class of wage-labourer —\"one of those whose labour is skilled labour, above-average labour.\" But his wage has a tendency to fall , as the capitalist mode of production advances, even in relation to average labour. Marx gives two causes: First cause—the division of labour in the commercial office. Only a one-sided development of ability need be produced, and much of the cost of producing this ability is free for the capitalist , since the worker's skill is rather developed by the function itself —and indeed developed all the more quickly, the more one-sided the function becomes with the division of labour. The deskilling-then-reskilling dynamic here is peculiar: the worker acquires the skill on the job, at the capitalist's expense, and this acquisition time is unpaid overhead. Second cause—the cheapening reproduction of basic skills. Basic skills, knowledge of commerce and languages, etc., are reproduced \"ever more quickly, easily, generally and cheaply\" the more the capitalist mode of production adapts teaching methods, etc., to practical purposes. The general extension of popular education permits this variety of labour \"to be recruited from classes which were formerly excluded from it and were accustomed to a lower standard of living.\" This increases supply, and with it competition. So: \"With a few exceptions, therefore, the labour-power of these people is devalued with the advance of capitalist production; their wages fall, whereas their working ability increases.\" This is a striking and precise claim. The commercial worker is de-skilled by skill-diffusion , not by mechanization. The wage falls even as ability rises—a Veblen-like widening of the field of recruitment depresses value. The capitalist increases the number of these workers only if he has more value and profit to realize: \"The increase in this labour is always an effect of the increase in surplus-value, and never a cause of it.\" The direction of causation is from surplus-value to commercial employment—never the reverse. And thus the chapter closes its treatment of the commercial worker. The editor's footnote 39 (F.E.) provides a poignant empirical verification of \"this prognosis, written in 1865, of the fate of the commercial proletariat since this time,\" in the form of \"the hundreds of German clerks skilled in all commercial operations and in three or four languages, who are offering their services in vain in the City of London for a weekly wage of 25 shillings—well below the wage of a skilled mechanic.\" The footnote also reports a gap of two pages in the manuscript at this point, indicating that Marx intended to develop this point further, and refers back to Volume 2, Chapter 6 (\"The Costs of Circulation\"), pp. 207–14, already touching on various points pertinent here. So the editor's apparatus confirms the incompleteness of the chapter and its connection to the earlier analysis. The Duplication: General Formal Determinations vs. Special Capitals The chapter closes with a synthetic statement of the theoretical structure. Marx notes \"a certain duplication\": On the one hand , the functions of commodity capital and money capital (and consequently also of commercial capital) are general formal determinations of industrial capital . That is, every industrial capital necessarily passes through the forms of commodity capital and money capital in the normal course of its circuit. On the other hand , special capitals, and consequently a special set of capitalists, are exclusively engaged in these functions ; and these functions develop into \"special spheres for the valorization of capital.\" This is an important theoretical synthesis: the economic form of a function can be universal (necessary to every industrial capital) and simultaneously become the exclusive occupation of a specialized capital. When the two coincide, the function becomes a distinct branch of capital in its own right. The Autonomy of Commercial Functions and Circulation Costs \"It is only with commercial capital that the commercial functions and circulation costs acquire autonomy.\" For industrial capital, the aspect pertaining to circulation consists not only in its regular forms of commodity capital and money capital, but also in the commercial office alongside the workshop. But with commercial capital this acquires autonomy: for the latter, \"the commercial office forms its only workshop.\" The part of capital applied in the form of circulation costs appears much greater with the wholesale merchant than with the industrialist—because besides the business office that goes with every industrial workshop, the part of capital that must be employed in this way by the overall class of industrial capitalists \"is now concentrated in the hands of individual merchants.\" By taking charge of the circulation function, they also take over the circulation costs that arise from it. This is a concentration of overhead —which, as argued above, reduces the aggregate circulation costs and hence mitigates the dilution of the profit rate. The Final Inversion: Expense vs. Source of Profit The chapter's closing paragraph is a dialectical tour de force —the same object appears in opposite forms depending on the standpoint: \"To industrial capital, the costs of circulation appear as expenses, which they are. To the merchant, they appear as the source of his profit, which—on the assumption of a general rate of profit—stands in proportion to the size of these costs. The outlay that has to be made on these circulation costs is therefore a productive investment as far as commercial capital is concerned. For it, therefore, the commercial labour that it buys is also directly productive.\" This final sentence requires careful reading. Marx is not saying that commercial labour creates value. He is saying that for commercial capital—i.e., from the standpoint of the merchant's particular capital—the labour it buys is \"directly productive\" of his profit , because his profit scales with the size of the circulation outlay he must make. The word \"directly productive\" is explicitly relative to \"for it.\" From the standpoint of total social capital, commercial labour is unproductive of value. The chapter thus ends with a statement whose ambiguities would occupy Marxist scholarship for a century: the form of productive investment is attached, by the merchant's standpoint, to what is functionally unproductive—the same inversion of form that the chapter diagnosed at its opening. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Systemic Laws Revealed Chapter 17 crystallizes several fundamental principles of Marx's mature economic theory. First, the law of the equalization of profit rates applies to all capital, regardless of whether it produces surplus-value. Commercial capital, though it creates no surplus-value, is still governed by the general rate of profit reached through competition. The mechanism is competitive mobility—and commercial capital is the most mobile of all. This is a decisive theoretical move. It shows that the general rate of profit is not a productive phenomenon whose scope happens to include commercial capital; it is a distributive phenomenon in which all capitals, functionally necessary to the reproduction process, participate by right of their function. The rate of profit is a social rate, not a technical one. Second, the general rate of profit is a composite rate, computed on the total social capital, not the productive capital alone. The chapter provides a strict correction to the earlier presentation: the formula is no longer s/(c + v) over productive capital, but s/(productive + commercial capital). This means that the more developed capitalist society becomes—the more the circulation apparatus grows—the more the industrial profit rate is depressed by the very existence of that apparatus. This is a profound point of development theory. The expansion of commerce, finance, and the service apparatus attendant to circulation is not a \"friction\" on the profit rate but a structural depressant of it. Third, commercial profit is a transfer , not a creation . The chapter makes commercial profit structurally transparent: it is a deduction from surplus-value already produced, effected by the industrial capitalist selling below the real price of production and the merchant selling at the real price. The merchant's apparent markup above his own purchase price is simply the flip side of the industrial capitalist's lower sale price. The merchant is a redistributive agent, not a value-producer. Fourth, the rate of profit systematically understates exploitation, and commercial capital deepens this understatement. In the example, s' = 100% but p' = 20%, then 18%. The veil of the profit form is denser in the presence of commercial capital. This has substantial ideological implications: the very category structure of capitalist accounting—the price of production, the profit rate—conceals the true degree of labour exploitation, and it does so by construction . Fifth, the historical and logical orders are inverse. Marx's point that commercial profit historically determines industrial profit, while logically industrial profit is prior, is methodologically profound. It means the analytical primacy of production is not contradicted by the historic primacy of commerce; rather, the historically older commercial form can only be fully understood through the logically prior productive relation that it presupposes without yet expressing. Broader Implications For the theory of capitalist development: Marx's prediction that the commercial apparatus expands and that commercial wages tend to fall is empirically documented in the editor's footnote (the hundreds of German clerks in the City of London). This anticipates modern labour-market analysis of the \"reserve army\" in white-collar work, the effects of educational expansion on wage differentials, and the phenomenon of credential inflation. Marx's specific mechanism—that the extension of popular education cheapens the reproduction of commercial skills and thereby reduces wages—is a forerunner of later analyses of skill-reproduction and labour-market competition. For the theory of the commercial petty bourgeoisie: Marx's observation that the small merchant \"may himself be the only worker employed\" and that his profit \"may not be any greater than the wage of a better-paid skilled worker; it may even be less\"—this anticipates the analysis of the \"intermediate strata,\" the small-business owners whose income is effectively a wage but who bear capital risk. This is relevant to the modern theory of \"the self-employed\" and the differential between formal wage status and effective economic position. For the theory of the wage form: Marx's insistence that the income of the circulation agent, whether merchant's profit or employee's wage, \"derives solely from the commercial profit\"—and that \"his labour is not value-creating labour\"—sets up a general critique of the wage form as a mask for appropriation. The wage form makes all labour look alike; but the source of the wage is different depending on the labour's relation to value creation. This is a central feature of Marx's critique of political economy: category forms conceal social relations. For the theory of service and circulation in modern economies: The chapter's treatment of \"pure costs of circulation\" and their distinction from transport and storage anticipates the theoretical problems of defining productive vs. unproductive labour in complex economies. It shows that \"productiveness\" for Marx is a specific historical concept (production of surplus-value), not a general one (production of use-values). For the trinity-formula critique: This chapter directly prepares the terrain for the theory of profit-sharing among commercial profit, interest, and rent. It establishes that commercial capital's share of total surplus-value is parasitic (in the technical sense: dependent on prior production). This sets up the critique of interest-bearing capital and the \"fetishism\" of the trinity formula that Marx will develop later: capital appearing to generate profit, land appearing to generate rent, labour appearing to generate wages. Philosophical and Theoretical Implications The standpoint-dependence of categories. Marx repeatedly emphasizes that the illusion of commercial profit (as a nominal markup) \"arises from the viewpoint of commercial capital.\" This is an instance of Marx's general method: economic categories are not standpoint-neutral; their apparent meaning depends on the position from which the observer views the process. The merchant's view of his profit is a necessary illusion generated by his place in the system—not a mere mistake. This is a strong philosophical position about the relation between social position and cognitive form. The methodological use of abstraction. The chapter moves back and forth between levels of abstraction: from the assumption of zero costs to the introduction of K and b; from the autonomy of commercial capital to the reduction of it to the commercial office of industrial capital. Marx is explicit that commercial capital must be analyzed first in its emergent form within industrial capital and only then in its autonomous form . The reduction of commercial capital to a \"branch\" of industrial capital—the methodological principle he states—is a model of his dialectical method: phenomena must be derived from their germinal form, not analyzed as if they had always existed in their developed shape. The refutation of monetarism. The chapter's destruction of the \"mark-up\" theory of profit is simultaneously a refutation of the view that profit arises from sale itself. It is not the case, Marx argues, that profit is a price phenomenon. The merchant's selling above his purchase price is not an increase above social value. This is a direct attack on the \"profit-upon-alienation\" theory (the idea that profit comes from \"selling dear\"), and it connects to the broader theory of exploitation in Volume 1. Essential Takeaways Function, not productiveness, grounds participation in average profit. Commercial capital earns the average profit by performing a necessary function (mediation of commodity metamorphosis), not by producing surplus-value. The general rate of profit is computed on all functioning capital. The entry of commercial capital into the equalization process reduces the industrial profit rate, and thereby deepens the concealment of labour exploitation. The merchant's profit is a redistribution of already-produced surplus-value, not value creation. The merchant buys below value and sells at value. Commercial labour realizes value but creates none; commercial workers' wages follow the value of labour-power, tend to fall with capitalist development, and are an effect of, never a cause of, surplus-value. Commercial capital is analytically derivative of industrial capital —its functions, its costs, and its wage relations must be derived from the corresponding moments inside industrial capital before being analyzed in their autonomous commercial form. The systematic illusion of commercial profit is the standpoint-ideology of the merchant , and this standpoint-dependence of economic categories is constitutive, not accidental. The historical primacy of commercial profit is compatible with the logical primacy of industrial capital —a paradigm case of Marx's method of uniting logical derivation with historical genesis. The chapter, therefore, does not merely fill a gap; it re-organizes the entire theory of the profit rate around the total social capital , subsuming the circulation apparatus under the equalization mechanism, and laying the groundwork for the later analysis of the division of surplus-value among its various claimants—profit of enterprise, interest, rent. Its most enduring theoretical achievement is the demonstration that capital can earn average profit without producing surplus-value, provided it performs a necessary function in the social reproductive process—an insight whose implications extend far beyond the nineteenth-century textile merchant who stands at its center. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 16 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 18"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 18", "title": "Chapter 18", "subtitle": "The Turnover of Merchant's Capital. Prices", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 18/Chapter 18 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 10517, "read_time": "53 min read", "tags": ["Commercial Capital & Merchant's Profit", "Turnover of Capital & Cycles"], "keywords": ["Turnover of Merchant's Capital and Prices", "Commercial Markups and Selling Prices", "Number of Turnovers Determining Commercial Margins", "Small Margin with Fast Turnover vs High Margin with Slow Turnover", "Impact of Merchant Inventory Glut on Industrial Production"], "text": "Chapter 18 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 10,447 words ⏱️ ~52 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 18 of Capital Volume 3 occupies a pivotal position in Marx's unfolding theory of the \"transformation of commodity capital and money capital\" into commercial capital ( Kaufmannskapital ). Its central problem is deceptively narrow and yet structurally enormous: what does \"turnover\" mean when the capital that turns over is not industrial but merely commercial, and what consequences follow from that difference for the formation of prices and the general rate of profit? The chapter's core thesis can be stated in three interlocking propositions. First, the turnover of commercial capital is not analogous to the turnover of industrial capital in kind, only in appearance. Industrial capital's turnover is the unity of production time and circulation time and therefore embraces the entire reproduction process — production, sale, purchase, and the resumption of production on a renewed scale. Commercial capital's turnover, by contrast, is \"nothing but the movement of commodity capital that has become autonomous,\" and represents only the first phase of the commodity metamorphosis, C–M, read from the merchant's standpoint as the circuit M–C–M′. The merchant buys, sells, buys again — a movement that is externally repeated without ever passing through production. Second, this asymmetry produces an illusion in the consciousness of the agents of circulation — and in the heads of bourgeois economists — that circulation as such determines price . Because the merchant's per-unit profit mark-up depends inversely on how many times his money capital turns over in a year (\"small profits and quick returns\"), it appears as though price is fixed by the merchant's arbitrary decision, or by the velocity of trade, rather than by the labour-time embodied in commodities and the general rate of profit. Marx's task is to dissolve that appearance and reduce it to the inner law. Third, the real determination runs the other way. The merchant's two limits — the production price of the commodity (which he does not control) and the average rate of profit (which he also does not control) — are both given to him externally. All he chooses is whether to deal in expensive or cheap commodities. Turnover affects only the distribution of an already-determined aliquot mass of profit across commodity units, not the magnitude of that profit. Hence Marx's central theorem: the commercial price supplement stands in inverse proportion to the number of turnovers of the commercial capital in the branch concerned , and directly in proportion to the turnover time. The chapter therefore serves as the epistemological hinge of Volume 3. It is where Marx shows why the \"visible and merely apparent movement\" of capitalist society necessarily generates upside-down ideas in the minds of merchants, stock-jobbers, bankers, and even manufacturers — and why \"it is one of the tasks of science to reduce the visible and merely apparent movement to the actual inner movement.\" It also discharges a structural debt: having established in Part Two how the general rate of profit arises from surplus-value produced by industrial capital, and having in Chapter 17 introduced commercial capital as a deduction from that mass of profit, Marx must now show how turnover-time differences between branches of trade modulate commercial prices without in any way disturbing the law of value. The chapter thus closes the circle by demonstrating that the very phenomenon most hostile to the labour theory of value — the apparent arbitrariness of trading prices — is fully deducible from it. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Founding Distinction: Industrial Turnover as Totality, Commercial Turnover as Fragment Marx opens with a definitional contrast that governs everything that follows. The turnover of industrial capital is \"the unity of its production and circulation times and consequently embraces the entire production process.\" Turnover here is not merely repetition; it is the completed circuit M–C … P … C′–M′, in which money is transformed into means of production and labour-power, value is produced , and the enlarged result is realized. Note carefully that Marx's phrase is \"unity\" — production time and circulation time are not merely added together but fused into a single periodicity that includes the renewal of production itself . The turnover of commercial capital is categorically different. It is \"nothing but the movement of commodity capital that has become autonomous.\" Here Marx activates a distinction he constructed across Volume 2 and the preceding chapters of Volume 3: commodity capital (C′) is the form the finished product takes as it leaves the industrial enterprise and enters the market; when a specialized fraction of the total social capital takes on the function of buying and selling that commodity capital on its own account, commodity capital \"becomes autonomous\" as commercial capital. What the merchant turns over is therefore a single phase of industrial capital's circuit — the C–M phase (sale) — detached and made into a self-standing professional routine. From the merchant's own point of view, this routine presents itself as a complete circuit: M–C, then C–M, endlessly repeated: buy, sell, buy, sell. It is a turnover of capital in that the same money returns to its starting point enlarged — but it is a turnover that never leaves the circulation sphere. This is the interpretive key to the chapter's title. Because the merchant's circuit excludes production, \"prices\" behave in his hands in a manner that is formally unlike the price behaviour of industrial capital — a difference that, if left unanalysed, produces the appearance of arbitrariness. 2.2 The Asymmetry of Metamorphosis: C₁–M–C₂ versus M–C–M′, and the £100 Example Marx then sharpens the contrast through a meticulous analysis of what actually swaps hands. Within the circulation sphere, industrial capital's metamorphosis \"always presents itself as C₁–M–C₂ .\" The commodity produced (C₁) is sold for money (M); that money is then used to buy new means of production (C₂). Crucially, Marx notes, this is \"in fact an exchange of C₁ and C₂,\" and \"the same money therefore changes hands twice.\" Money here is a pure intermediary: its movement mediates the exchange of two different kinds of commodities — the output of one production process and the inputs of the next. The money is not the destination; it is the vehicle. In the language of Volume 1, money functions as means of circulation . In the merchant's circuit M–C–M′, the situation is inverted: \"it is the same commodity that changes hands twice.\" The merchant's money buys a commodity, and that same commodity is resold — its second change of hands being the mechanism by which the merchant's money refluxes to him. Nothing is exchanged between two productive uses; there is no C₁ for C₂ substitution. The whole movement \"simply mediates the reflux to him of his money.\" This is the precise formal locus of the merchant's function: he does not transform matter, he transfers title . Marx illustrates with a deliberately round example: a merchant's capital of £100 used to buy commodities for £100 and later sold for £110 has \"made his capital of £100 turn over once.\" The number of turnovers per year depends on how often M–C–M′ is repeated. From this simple arithmetic, two things must be held apart — and Marx here inserts a crucial methodological bracketing: \"We are completely leaving aside here the costs that may be involved in the difference between the purchase price and the sale price, since these costs in no way affect the form we are initially concerned to analyse.\" These costs are designated K (costs of circulation, i.e., the portion of capital the merchant advances besides the sum laid out in purchasing commodities). By setting K aside, Marx isolates the form of the commercial circuit — the pure buy-cheap-sell-dear movement — before reintroducing complications. (He returns to this bracketing explicitly at 2.8 below, where he also sets aside ΔK , the additional profit earned on this additional capital, and defends the procedure as \"the strictly logical and mathematically correct way of looking at things.\") 2.3 Money as Means of Circulation: The Analogy, the Break, and the Credit System Marx next draws an analogy that he immediately qualifies — a characteristic dialectical move. The number of turnovers of a given commercial capital is \"completely analogous here with the repeated circuits of money as a simple means of circulation.\" Just as \"the same shilling circulating ten times buys ten times its value in commodities,\" so the same merchant's capital of £100 \"buys ten times its value in commodities, or realizes a total commodity capital of ten times its value, £1,000.\" The mechanism of velocity is the same: a given quantum of circulating medium can service a much larger volume of transactions if it moves faster, so that velocity substitutes for quantity . But then the break, which is the point of the analogy: \"with the circulation of money as means of circulation, the same piece of money passes through different hands… In the merchant's case, however, the same money capital… repeatedly returns to the same owner as M + ΔM.\" This is what \"characterizes its turnover as a turnover of capital\" — the defining feature is not mere velocity but reflux to the same owner as value plus surplus-value . \"It always withdraws more money from circulation than it puts in.\" Marx then anticipates the credit system: \"as the turnover of commercial capital accelerates (and this is also where the function of money as means of payment predominates, with the development of the credit system), the same quantity of money also circulates more quickly.\" This is a compressed but important forward reference. Means of payment — as opposed to means of circulation — is the monetary function appropriate to deferred settlement (bills of exchange, credit sales). Where trade runs on credit, the physical money need not accompany each transaction; debts are netted and settled later, so a smaller money stock can support a faster and larger turnover. But precisely because settlement is deferred, the whole system becomes vulnerable to any interruption in the reflux — a vulnerability that becomes the material basis of the crisis mechanism described in 2.5. 2.4 What Turnover Expresses : Reproduction versus Repetition, and the Two Limits Here Marx delivers the conceptual heart of the chapter: what does the repetition mean in each case? \"The repeated turnover of commercial capital, however, is never anything more than a repetition of buying and selling; whereas the repeated turnover of industrial capital expresses the periodicity and renewal of the entire reproduction process (including the process of consumption).\" For industrial capital, the completion of a circuit is a material event : commodities have been produced and consumed productively, the means of production have been replaced, and production can resume on a possibly expanded scale. For commercial capital, the completion of a circuit is a commercial event with no material content of its own. From this follows a dependency that Marx states bluntly: \"For commercial capital, on the contrary, this is simply an external condition.\" The merchant's rapid turnover is possible only if industrial capital keeps \"constantly put[ting] commodities on the market and withdraw[ing] them from it again.\" If the reproduction process is generally slow, so is the turnover of commercial capital. Hence Marx derives the first limit : commercial capital \"certainly facilitates the turnover of productive capital; but it only does this in so far as it cuts down the latter's circulation time .\" It \"has no direct effect on the production time , which also forms a barrier to the turnover time of industrial capital. This is the first limit to the turnover of commercial capital.\" This is a crucial distinction. Industrial capital's turnover time is production time + circulation time. Commercial capital can attack only the second term: by taking over the selling function, by holding inventories, by having a dense network of correspondents, the merchant shortens the interval during which the manufacturer's capital lies frozen in unsold product. But no commercial arrangement shortens the time the baker's dough must rise, the crop must grow, or the wine must age. That residual is the hard limit. The second limit is on the demand side: \"quite apart from the barrier formed by reproductive consumption , this turnover is decisively restricted by the speed and volume of total individual consumption , since the overall part of the commodity capital that goes into the consumption fund depends on this.\" Reproductive consumption means the purchase of means of production by capitals (Department I buying from Department I and II); individual consumption means purchases by workers and capitalists for personal use (the destination of Department II output). The merchant's ability to keep turning his capital over is ultimately capped by how fast and how much people and firms actually consume — i.e., by the volume of final demand, which is itself anchored in the distribution of value and the prevailing wage. 2.5 Autonomy, Elasticity, Crisis, and the Location of Breakdown Marx now introduces the chapter's most dialectically charged passage — the theory of how commercial capital's relative independence becomes the mechanism of generalized crisis. He first brackets a distinct phenomenon: \"the turnovers within the world of commerce, where one merchant after the other sells the same commodity, a kind of circulation which may present a very flourishing appearance in periods of speculation.\" This is the chain of resales between middlemen — pure circulation of titles, generating the appearance of vigorous business while nothing has reached a consumer. It is speculative circulation, and Marx sets it aside to isolate the structural point. The structural point is threefold: Commercial capital abbreviates the C–M phase for productive capital — it shortens the manufacturer's circulation time (as established above). Under the modern credit system, it commands a large part of society's total money capital , so that \"it can repeat its purchases before it has definitively sold what it has already bought.\" This is the critical mechanism. The merchant does not need his own realized sales to fund his next purchase; the banking system supplies the means. \"In this connection it is immaterial whether our merchant has sold directly to the final consumer or whether there are twelve other merchants between the two.\" The reproduction process is \"tremendously elastic\" — \"it can always be driven beyond any given barrier.\" Given credit, and given the physical and organizational flexibility of industry, the merchant \"finds no barrier in production itself, or only a very elastic one.\" From these three elements Marx derives the dynamic: \" Besides the separation of C–M and M–C, which follows from the nature of the commodity, an active demand is now therefore created. \" That phrase — \"an active demand is now therefore created\" — is the pivot. The commercial credit system does not merely transmit demand; it manufactures it. Merchants order goods not because consumers are buying but because credit is cheap and expectations are buoyant, and this ordering itself registers as demand upon industrial capital, which responds by expanding output and employment, which in turn puts more wages and revenue into circulation, which seems to confirm the demand that generated it. Now the dialectical formula, arguably the most quoted sentence of the chapter: \"Despite the autonomy it has acquired, the movement of commercial capital is never anything more than the movement of industrial capital within the circulation sphere. But by virtue of this autonomy, its movement is within certain limits independent of the reproduction process and its barriers, and hence it also drives this process beyond its own barriers. This inner dependence in combination with external autonomy drives commercial capital to a point where the inner connection is forcibly re-established by way of a crisis.\" Three terms must be held precisely: - Inner dependence : commercial capital's movement is ontologically parasitic on industrial capital — it can only sell what has been produced, and can only be repaid out of final sales. - External autonomy : it appears and operates as a self-standing business with its own rhythms, its own credit lines, its own profit calculations, and its own capacity to commit funds independently of realized sales. - Forcible re-establishment by way of crisis : the latent contradiction is resolved — never smoothly, always violently — when the accumulation of unsold goods and unpaid debts makes it impossible to continue pretending that circulation can outrun production. This yields Marx's famous observational claim about the location of crises: \"crises do not first break out and are not first apparent in the retail trade, which bears on immediate consumption, but rather in the sphere of wholesale trade, as well as banking, which places the money capital of the entire society at the wholesalers' disposal.\" The retail trade's turnover is anchored directly to daily purchases, so it cannot stretch as far. Wholesale trade, with its long supply chains and credit-financed inventories, is where the gap between apparent demand and real demand first becomes untenable — and banking is where it becomes financially fatal. 2.6 The Imperceptible Point: From Prosperity to Crash Marx now narrates the concrete genesis of a crisis — the most empirically textured passage in the chapter. He traces a chain: \"The manufacturer may actually sell to the exporter, and the exporter to his foreign customer; the importer may sell his raw materials to the manufacturer, and the manufacturer sell his products to the wholesaler, etc.\" Along this chain, \"at some particular imperceptible point the commodity lies unsold; or else the total stocks of producers and middlemen gradually become too high.\" The paradox is that precisely then \"consumption is generally at flood tide.\" Why? Two reasons given: \"partly because one industrial capitalist sets a series of others in motion\" — the expansion propagates through the inter-industry demand network, as Department I's orders for raw materials and machinery call forth production in other branches — \"partly because the workers these employ, being fully occupied, have more than usual to spend.\" And: \"The capitalists' expenditure increases with their revenue.\" So the boom is self-intensifying : expanded production generates expanded wages and profits, which generate expanded consumption, which justifies expanded production. Marx then adds a structurally important amplifier, referring back to Volume 2, Part Three (pp. 498–501, 505–9): \"a constant circulation between one constant capital and another (even leaving aside the accelerated accumulation) which is initially independent of individual consumption.\" This is the circulation within Department I — iron to steel to machinery to iron, so to speak. This inter-constant-capital circulation \"is initially independent of individual consumption in so far as it never goes into this even though it is ultimately limited by it, for production of constant capital takes place never for its own sake but simply because more of it is needed in those spheres of production whose products do go into individual consumption.\" The phrase \"initially independent… ultimately limited\" is the whole point. The inter-capital trade in constant capital can sustain itself for a long while on its own momentum , generating brisk business \"stimulated by prospective demand,\" with \"business proceeds very briskly, as far as both merchants and industrialists are concerned.\" But this is a circuit feeding on itself; its ultimate justification is the consumer goods sector, and if that sector's demand is not actually growing commensurately, the whole edifice is financing itself against a promise. Then the moment of truth: \"The crisis occurs as soon as the returns of these merchants who sell far afield (or who have accumulated stocks at home) become so slow and sparse that the banks press for payment for commodities bought, or bills fall due before any resale takes place. And then we have the crash, putting a sudden end to the apparent prosperity.\" Note the precise mechanism. The trigger is not a collapse of value or a sudden material catastrophe. It is a timing mismatch : bills (credit instruments with fixed maturities) come due before the goods they financed have been resold. The merchant's turnover time exceeds the repayment period the credit system granted him. Banks, having advanced against that turnover, demand settlement. He cannot sell fast enough; he cannot pay; the failure propagates along the chain of mutual debts — and the \"apparent prosperity\" collapses all at once. This is why crises in Marx's account are characteristically abrupt , why they appear as financial events even when rooted in production, and why Marx insists on the credit system as an essential structural feature rather than an incidental one. 2.7 One Commercial Capital, Many Industrial Capitals: Mediating Opposing Phases Marx then deepens the account of commercial capital's \"superficial and irrational character\": \"the turnover of the same commercial capital can mediate the turnovers of very different productive capitals at the same time or in succession.\" A single wholesaler's £10,000 of revolving capital may successively fund the sale of a cotton spinner's output, a weaver's output, and a bleacher's service — three distinct production processes, each with its own turnover period, all serviced by the same revolving commercial fund, staggered in time. The same merchant's capital, moreover, can mediate opposing phases of the metamorphosis. Marx gives a concrete example: \"The merchant may for instance buy linen from the manufacturer and sell it to the bleacher. Here, therefore, the turnover of the same merchant's capital — in actual fact the same C–M, the realization of the linen — represents two opposite phases for two different industrial capitals .\" For the weaver, the merchant's purchase is the weaver's sale (C–M, realization); for the bleacher, the merchant's sale is the bleacher's purchase (M–C, the acquisition of raw material). \"If the merchant sells for productive consumption, his C–M represents the M–C of one industrial capital and his M–C the C–M of another.\" The consequence is that the merchant's own accounting — the single turnover M–C–M′ he experiences — is a composite of two opposite movements in two different industrial circuits. His \"turnover\" therefore has no one-to-one relationship to any single productive process, which is precisely why his turnover can appear to have a life of its own and why from his standpoint \"turnover itself seems to determine price.\" The structural interlocking of many industrial circuits in one commercial circuit is the material ground of the epistemic illusion Marx will diagnose later. 2.8 The Exclusion of K and ΔK: The Methodological Rationale Marx now justifies, with unusual self-consciousness, a simplification he has been operating under. He has been analysing the commercial circuit while leaving aside K , \"the costs of circulation, i.e. the portion of capital that the merchant advances besides the sum laid out on the purchase of commodities.\" These are the merchant's real ancillary outlays — warehousing, transport, clerks' wages, insurance, shop fittings, bookkeeping. He reasons that if K is excluded, then ΔK — the additional profit the merchant makes on this additional capital — must also be excluded. And he defends this as \"the strictly logical and mathematically correct way of looking at things, if it is a question of seeing how profit and turnover of commercial capital affect prices.\" The justification is decomposability: the effect of pure purchase-and-resale turnover on price can be isolated from the effect of circulation-cost capital only if the latter is temporarily zeroed out. Once the pure case is established, the complication can be added without disturbing the analysis of the pure case — because K, at the level of the general rate of profit, earns the same average rate as any other capital advanced. This is a modelling decision in the classical sense: strip the phenomenon to its essential skeleton, then readmit qualifications. 2.9 The Sugar Example: Production Price and the Aliquot Part of Commercial Profit Marx now constructs the chapter's key numerical demonstration. (Note: production price = cost price + average profit, the price at which commodities must sell if industrial capitals of differing organic composition are to earn the same general rate of profit; aliquot part = the proportional share, i.e., the per-unit slice of a total.) Case 1. \"If the production price of 1 lb. of sugar is £1, with £100 the merchant can buy 100 lb. of sugar. If this is the amount he buys and sells in the course of a year, and the average annual rate of profit is 15 per cent, he will add £15 to this £100, and 3 shillings to each £1, the production price of 1 lb. He will thus sell the sugar at £1 3s. a lb. \" The arithmetic is exact: 15% of £1 = 3 shillings. Case 2. Now suppose the production price of sugar falls to 1 shilling per lb. With the same £100 the merchant now buys 2,000 lb. His annual profit on the £100 remains £15 , distributed across 2,000 units instead of 100 — so the per-unit addition becomes 1.8d (the text renders it as a small fraction of a penny; the exact figure implied by £15 ÷ 2,000 lb = 0.15s = 1.8d). He sells each lb. at just over 1 shilling. Marx draws the theoretical conclusion with precision: \"The level of the price of production, whether high or low, has nothing to do with the profit rate; but it has a decisive effect on the aliquot part of the sale price of each 1 lb. of sugar that goes to form commercial profit; i.e. the addition to the price that the merchant makes on a certain quantity of commodity (product).\" A footnote clarifies that Marx means here the absolute profit per lb., not the relative profit . This clarification matters enormously, because the relative mark-up (profit as a share of the purchase price) is 15% in both cases — the same annual rate. What changes is the absolute per-unit figure: 3 shillings versus roughly 1.8 pence. The rate is invariant; the per-unit expression varies by a factor of twenty. Marx generalizes: \"If the production price of a commodity is low, so is the sum that the merchant advances in its purchase price, i.e. for a given quantity, and so too, at a given rate of profit, is the amount of profit he makes on a given quantity of this cheaper commodity. Alternatively, and this comes to the same thing, he can buy a larger amount of this cheaper commodity with a given capital, of e.g. £100, and the overall profit of £15 which he makes on his £100 is then distributed in small fractions over the individual portions of this mass of commodities. And vice versa.\" And he grounds the whole thing in productivity: \"This depends completely on the higher or lower productivity of the industrial capital whose commodities he trades in.\" The merchant's per-unit profit margin is a downstream reflection of the productivity of the industrial capital upstream of him. 2.10 The Merchant's Two Limits, the Dutch East India Company, and the Four Sources of Illusion Marx now turns to demolishing the \"prevailing conception\" that the merchant chooses between high-margin/low-volume and low-margin/high-volume strategies. Setting aside cases of monopoly — \"where the merchant is a monopolist and also monopolizes production, as was the case with the Dutch East India Company in its day\" — he declares: \"nothing could be more ridiculous than the prevailing conception that it depends on the merchant whether he wants to sell many commodities at a low profit on the individual commodity, or a few commodities at a high profit.\" The reason is structural: \"The two limits to his sale price are, on the one hand, the production price of the commodity, which he has no control over; and on the other hand the average rate of profit, which he has no control over either.\" The merchant's only decision is which branch to enter — \"whether he wants to deal in expensive commodities or in cheap ones\" — and even that is conditioned by the size of his available capital and by other circumstances. \"The attitude of the merchant therefore depends entirely on the degree of development of the capitalist mode of production and not on his own will.\" The Dutch East India Company serves as the historical exemplar of the opposite: \"as a purely commercial company having a monopoly of production, [it] imagined it could still pursue, under completely changed conditions, a method that corresponded at most to the beginnings of capitalist production.\" Its extraordinary margins were a monopoly rent on production, not a commercial strategy; once general capitalist production developed, that position became untenable. Marx then identifies four sources of the popular prejudice — a prejudice which, \"like all wrong ideas about profit, etc., arises from taking the viewpoint of trade alone and from commercial preconceptions\": Phenomena of competition among merchants themselves — \"which pertain simply to the division of commercial profit among the individual merchants, the shareholders in the total commercial capital; e.g. when one merchant sells more cheaply than another, so as to drive his competitor from the field.\" What is observed is a redistribution within commercial capital, but it is misread as a determination of price. Naive theoretical confusion , exemplified by \"an economist of Professor Roscher's calibre,\" who \"can still imagine, in Leipzig, that it was reasons of 'good sense and humanity' that produced the change in sale prices, and that this was not the result of a revolution in the actual mode of production.\" (Marx cites Roscher's Die Grundlagen der Nationalökonomie , 3rd edn., 1858, p. 192.) The sarcasm is aimed at the reduction of an objective economic transformation to moral sentiment. A genuine empirical co-movement misread as causation : \"if production prices fall as a result of increases in the productivity of labour, and if sale prices therefore fall as well, then demand often rises still more quickly than supply, and with it market prices, so that the sale prices yield more than the average profit.\" Falling prices and rising demand occur together — but the sequence runs productivity → production price → sale price, not merchant decision → price → demand. A real mercantile practice : \"a merchant may reduce the sale price (and this means nothing but a reduction in the standard profit that he adds to the price), in order to turn over a larger capital more quickly in his business.\" Marx grants that this occurs, but insists it belongs to the analysis of competition among merchants, not to the determination of value. He concludes: \"All these are matters that pertain simply to competition among the merchants themselves.\" A footnote to this passage quotes Corbet, An Inquiry into the Causes, etc. of the Wealth of Individuals (London, 1841), p. 20: \"Profit, on the general principle, is always the same, whatever be price; keeping its place like an incumbent body on the swelling or sinking tide. As, therefore, prices rise, a tradesman raises price; as prices fall, a tradesman lowers price.\" Marx's editorial gloss is careful: attention is paid only to ordinary trade , not to speculation , whose examination \"lies outside the ambit of our discussion, together with everything that pertains to the division of commercial capital.\" Corbet's second proposition (p. 128) is that \"the profit of trade is a value added to capital which is independent of price, the second\" — i.e., speculation — \"is founded on the variation in the value of capital or in price itself.\" The distinction between ordinary trade profit (independent of price level) and speculative gain (derived from price variation ) is exactly the line Marx draws. 2.11 Recapitulation from Volume 1: Price Level, Surplus-Value, and the Historical Fall of Commercial Prices Marx now reaches back to Volume 1, Chapter 17, to re-establish the foundations. The claim: \"a high or low level of commodity prices determines neither the mass of surplus-value that a given capital produces nor the rate of surplus-value.\" What the price level does reflect is the quantity of labour objectified: \"if only a little labour is objectified in many commodities, the price of the individual commodity will be low and so will be the surplus-value contained in it.\" But the crucial analytical separation is this: \"how the labour embodied in a commodity is divided into paid and unpaid labour, and what proportion of this price thus represents surplus-value, has nothing to do with this total amount of labour, i.e. with the price of the commodity.\" In other words: - Absolute surplus-value per unit depends on the total labour embodied (hence on productivity) and on the length/intensity of the working day; - Rate of surplus-value depends on the ratio of unpaid to paid labour — a relative magnitude. Hence: \"The rate can be high even though the absolute amount of surplus-value in each individual commodity is small.\" And: \"This absolute amount of surplus-value in each individual commodity depends in the first place on the productivity of labour and only secondly on its division between paid and unpaid.\" Marx states this ordering explicitly and it is essential: productivity governs the absolute magnitude; the paid/unpaid split governs its decomposition. A cheap commodity may carry a tiny absolute surplus-value and still exhibit a high rate of exploitation. Then the pivot to commerce: \"As far as the commercial sale price is concerned, the production price is a given external assumption.\" From this, Marx offers a historical account of why commercial commodity prices were high in earlier periods, with two causes: 1. High production prices consequent on the low productivity of labour — each commodity embodied more labour-time, hence cost more, hence the commercial capital required to carry a given physical quantity was larger. 2. The absence of a general rate of profit — \"since commercial capital drew a far higher proportion of the surplus-value than would accrue to it in conditions of general mobility of capital.\" Where capital could not move freely between branches, commercial capital could sustain a rate of profit well above what equalization would permit. (This is the historical-theoretical ground of the Dutch East India Company case.) \"The cessation of this situation is… in both respects the result of the development of the capitalist mode of production.\" Rising productivity lowers production prices; capital mobility establishes a general rate of profit that disciplines commercial margins. Marx then notes the empirical variation of turnover: \"Turnovers of commercial capital are longer or shorter in various branches of trade, and the number of turnovers in the year thus more or less. Within the same branch of trade, the turnover is quicker or slower in different phases of the economic cycle. There is however an average number of turnovers, which is discovered by experience. \" This empirical average is the quantity that will enter the price determination in 2.14. 2.12 The Two Capitals Compared: Turnover and the Formation of the General Rate of Profit Marx now consolidates the contrast between the two capitals with respect to profit determination — the analytical summit of the chapter. Industrial capital. \"Its turnover expresses on the one hand the periodicity of reproduction and depends therefore on the amount of commodities that are put on the market in a certain period of time. On the other hand, the circulation time also forms a limit, even if an extendable one, which may have a more or less constricting effect on the formation of value and surplus-value through its effect on the scale of the production process.\" The logic: a faster turnover means a given capital can pass through more production cycles per year, each cycle extracting surplus-value; so more surplus-value is produced annually. \"Thus the turnover exerts its determining function on the mass of surplus-value annually produced, and hence on the formation of the general rate of profit, not as a positive factor but rather as a constricting one .\" The phrase is precise: turnover time does not create surplus-value; being a barrier, a reduction in it removes a restriction , permitting more surplus-value to be produced. It operates negatively, as the removal of a drag. Commercial capital. \"The average rate of profit, on the other hand, is a given magnitude as far as commercial capital is concerned. Commercial capital does not have a direct effect on the creation of profit or surplus-value and it enters as a determining element into the formation of the general rate of profit only in so far as it draws its dividends from the mass of profit that industrial capital produces, according to the proportion that it forms in the total capital .\" Two consequences follow, and they must be distinguished: (i) \"The greater the number of turnovers made by an industrial capital… the greater is the mass of profit that it forms.\" Marx concedes the general-rate-of-profit objection and answers it: \"it is true that the establishment of a general rate of profit means that this total profit is divided among the various capitals not according to the ratio in which they directly participate in its production, but rather according to the aliquot parts that they form in the total capital, i.e. in proportion to their size. But this does not alter the essence of the question.\" Why not? Because a greater mass of total profit, given a given total capital, still means a higher general rate of profit. The equalization rule only governs distribution ; it does not abolish the effect of turnover on the magnitude of the divisible pool. (ii) \"It is different with commercial capital. Here the rate of profit is a given magnitude, determined on the one hand by the mass of profit that industrial capital produces and on the other by the relative size of the overall commercial capital, by its quantitative proportion in the total capital advanced in the production and circulation process.\" Commercial capital therefore has no causal role in producing profit — it receives a share. Its rate is a function of (a) the surplus-value pool and (b) its own share of the total capital. 2.13 The Relative Size of Commercial Capital and the Historical Tendencies of Its Development But the merchant's turnover number does matter — via the size of the commercial capital required. \"The number of its turnovers, however, has a determining effect on its relationship to the total capital, or the relative size of the commercial capital needed for circulation, in that it is evident that the absolute size of the commercial capital required stands in inverse proportion to the speed of its turnover .\" Marx's worked illustration: suppose the total capital is £10,000; then if commercial capital is one-tenth of it, that is £1,000. If the total capital is £1,000, one-tenth is £100. So far the relative magnitude is constant (one-tenth) while the absolute magnitude varies with the total. Now let turnover change. \"Given a rapid turnover, its absolute size may be £1,000… in the first case, £100 in the second case, so that its relative size is one-tenth. With a slower turnover, its absolute size may be £2,000 in the first case and £200 in the second. Its relative magnitude would have grown from one-tenth of the total capital to one-fifth.\" So the causal chain is: turnover speed → absolute commercial capital required → relative share of total capital → share of the surplus-value pool deducted from industrial capital's profit → the general rate of profit. Marx draws the policy-relevant conclusion: \"Circumstances that shorten the average turnover of commercial capital, such as the development of means of transport, for example, reduce in the same proportion the absolute magnitude of this commercial capital and hence raise the general rate of profit. And vice versa.\" Railways, steamships, telegraphs, and improved roads do not produce surplus-value, but by shrinking the commercial capital that must be advanced they reduce the deduction from the surplus-value pool and thus raise the general rate of profit. This is a striking and important structural result: improvements in circulation raise the rate of profit even though they are unproductive of value. Marx then states the double tendency of developed capitalism upon commercial capital: \"the same amount of commodities are turned over with a smaller amount of actually functioning commodity capital; while on account of the more rapid turnover of this commercial capital and the greater speed of the reproduction process on which it depends, the ratio of commercial capital to industrial capital is reduced .\" But the counter-tendency: \"with the development of the capitalist mode of production all production becomes commodity production , and hence the whole of the product comes into the hands of agents of circulation.\" In earlier modes, \"quite apart from the mass of products that were directly consumed in kind by the producers themselves and the mass of services that were performed in kind too, a very large proportion of the producers sold their commodities directly to their consumers or worked to their personal orders.\" (Marx is describing the natural-economy residue — direct consumption, payment in kind, bespoke production for known customers.) He then lays out three numbered propositions about commercial capital in earlier vs. later modes: (1) In earlier modes commercial capital is smaller in absolute terms but relatively greater. It is absolutely smaller \"because an incomparably smaller part of the entire product is produced as a commodity, has to go into circulation as commodity capital, and comes into the hands of merchants.\" It is relatively greater (i) because of its slower turnover — more capital locked up per unit of goods sold; and (ii) because \"the price of this mass of commodities, and also therefore the commercial capital that has to be advanced for it, is greater as a result of the lower productivity of labour compared with capitalist production, so that the same value is expressed in a smaller amount of commodities.\" Lower productivity → higher unit prices → more money capital needed to carry the same physical stock. (2) Under the capitalist mode, a greater mass of commodities is produced (with lower value per unit, which must be taken into account), and \"the same mass of products, e.g. of corn, forms a greater mass of commodities; i.e. more and more of it comes into commerce.\" Subsistence goods that once never entered the market now do. The consequence: \"not only does the mass of commercial capital grow, but so too does that of all the capital invested in circulation , e.g. in shipping, railways, telegraphs, etc.\" — the entire circulation-infrastructure apparatus. (3) Non-functioning or semi-functioning commercial capital also grows with capitalist development — \"with the increased ease of entry into the retail trade, with speculation and a surplus of unoccupied capital.\" Marx flags this as an aspect \"to be discussed when we come to 'Competition among Capitals'\" — a projected study he never completed. A footnote records: \"In 1865, when the manuscript of Volume 3 was written, Marx evidently still intended to devote a special study to the phenomena of competition. See above, pp. 10–11, and the Introduction to the Pelican Marx Library edition of Capital Volume 1, pp. 27–8.\" This is an important textual marker of the work's incompleteness. The substantive content of (3) is that a portion of social capital is absorbed into retail, speculation, and idle reservoirs — capital that neither produces surplus-value nor efficiently circulates it, and which therefore operates as a drag on the general rate of profit. 2.14 The Central Theorem: The Price Supplement in Inverse Proportion to Turnover Marx now reaches the proposition toward which the entire argument has been building. His framing: \"taking the magnitude of the commercial capital in relation to the total capital as given, the variations in turnover between various branches of commerce do not affect the total profit that accrues to the commercial capital, nor do they affect the general rate of profit. The merchant's profit is determined not by the mass of commodity capital he turns over, but rather by the amount of money capital he advances in order to mediate this turnover.\" His demonstration: \"If the general annual rate of profit is 15 per cent and the merchant advances £100, then, if his capital turns over once a year, he will sell his commodities at £115 . If his capital turns over five times a year , he will sell a commodity capital with a purchase price of £100 five times a year at a price of £103 , and in the whole year therefore a commodity capital of £500 at £515 . This gives him, as before, an annual profit of £15 on the capital of £100 he has advanced.\" Note the internal logic: the annual return is invariant at £15; what changes is how £15 is spread. Turned over once, the whole £15 is tacked onto one £100 transaction (a 15% mark-up per transaction). Turned over five times, £15 is spread across five £100 transactions (a 3% mark-up each), and the merchant sells £500 worth of goods over the year for £515. Marx then gives the reductio : \"If this were not the case, commercial capital would yield a far higher profit than industrial capital in relation to the number of its turnovers, and this would contradict the law of the general rate of profit .\" This is a decisive constraint. The equalization of the rate of profit across all capitals — industrial and commercial — forbids the merchant from multiplying his return by multiplying his turnovers. If he could, capital would flood into high-velocity trades, and the rate of profit would be driven down by competition anyway. The inverse relation between turnover and the per-unit supplement is the equalization mechanism in the commercial sphere. The law stated formally: \"The number of turnovers of commercial capital in various branches of commerce thus has a direct effect on the commercial prices of commodities. The level of the commercial price supplement, that is to say the aliquot part of the commercial profit on a given capital that is added to the production price of the individual commodity, stands in inverse proportion to the number of turnovers or the speed of turnover of the commercial capital in the particular line of business in question.\" And the illustration: \"If a commercial capital turns over five times a year, it adds to the same value of commodity capital only a fifth the increase that another commercial capital, able to turn over only once a year, adds to a commodity capital of equal value.\" The general statement follows: \"The way that sale prices are affected by the average turnover time of capitals in various branches of commerce can be reduced to the principle that, according to the velocity of this turnover, the same mass of profit that is determined by the general annual profit rate for a given amount of commercial capital — determined independently, that is, of the particular character of this capital's commercial operations — is differently distributed over commodity masses of the same value, adding for example 15/5 = 3 per cent when it turns over five times a year, as against 15 per cent when it turns over only once.\" And the cross-branch consequence: \"Thus the same percentage of commercial profit in different lines of business raises the sale prices of the commodities in question by quite different percentages, calculated on the values of these commodities, in direct proportion to the differences in the turnover times.\" A jeweller and a greengrocer may both earn the same annual rate on capital, yet the jeweller's small volume of high-value, slow-turning stock bears a large price supplement per unit relative to the greengrocer's rapid-turnover perishables. The same rate of profit, differently expressed in prices. This is the theorem's practical punch-line. 2.15 The Industrial Contrast and the \"Arbitrary Price\" Illusion Marx now contrasts the industrial case to isolate what is peculiar about the commercial case. \"As far as industrial capital is concerned, on the other hand, its turnover time has no effect on the value of the individual commodities produced, even though it does affect the mass of the values and surplus-values that a given capital produces in a given time, via the mass of labour exploited.\" Turnover speed does not change how much labour is embodied in one coat; it changes how many coats a given capital can make per year, and therefore how much surplus-value that capital extracts, and therefore — with the other capitals — the general rate of profit. Marx admits this is \"concealed and appears as something different when we look at production prices, but that is simply because the production prices of various commodities diverge from their values, according to the laws already developed.\" Production prices deviate systematically from values as a function of organic composition; but \"taking the production process as a whole, and the total mass of commodities produced by industrial capital, the general law is immediately confirmed.\" In the aggregate, price sums equal value sums, and the law of value holds. From this contrast, Marx draws the chapter's epistemological conclusion : \"while a closer consideration of the influence of turnover time on value formation in the case of the individual capital leads back to the general law and the basis of political economy, viz. that commodity values are determined by the labour-time they contain , the influence of the turnover of commercial capital on commercial prices exhibits phenomena which, in the absence of a very far-reaching analysis of the intermediate stages of the process, seem to presuppose a purely arbitrary determination of prices , i.e. a determination simply by the fact that capital happens to have made up its mind to make a certain amount of profit per year. It seems in particular, through this influence of the turnover, as if the circulation process as such determines the prices of commodities, and that this is within certain limits independent of the process of production. All superficial and distorted views of the overall reproduction process are derived from consideration of commercial capital and from the notions that its specific movements give rise to in the heads of the agents of circulation. \" This is the chapter's deepest claim. The illusion is not merely an intellectual error; it is a necessary appearance generated by the real structure of commercial capital's operation. Because the merchant does set a mark-up, and because that mark-up does vary inversely with turnover in a way he can partially control (by choosing his line, by moving his shop, by cutting prices to accelerate turnover), price appears to him to be his own decision. And because he never sees the production process—only the circulation of titles—he has no access to the labour-time determinism underneath. The illusion is overdetermined by his social position. 2.16 Competition, the Methodological Coda, and \"Small Profits and Quick Returns\" Marx then delivers a methodological observation of first importance: \"As the reader will have recognized in dismay, the analysis of the real, inner connections of the capitalist production process is a very intricate thing and a work of great detail; it is one of the tasks of science to reduce the visible and merely apparent movement to the actual inner movement .\" The \"in dismay\" is characteristic self-irony — Marx acknowledges the reader's fatigue — but the methodological claim is serious. Science's task is the reduction of appearance to essence . From this it follows that \"in the heads of the agents of capitalist production and circulation, ideas must necessarily form about the laws of production that diverge completely from these laws and are merely the expression in consciousness of the apparent movement.\" And specifically: \"The ideas of a merchant, a stock-jobber or a banker are necessarily quite upside-down. The ideas of the manufacturers are vitiated by the acts of circulation to which their capital is subjected and by the equalization of the general rate of profit.\" A footnote reinforces the point with a charmingly concrete source — Feller and Odermann, Das Ganze der kaufmännischen Arithmetik , 7th edn., Leipzig 1859, p. 451: \"Thus the fact that one and the same commodity is to be obtained from different sellers at essentially different prices also has its basis very frequently in an incorrect calculation.\" Marx comments: \"The following observation, if very naive, is at the same time quite correct… This shows how the determination of price becomes purely theoretical, i.e. abstract. \" That is, in commercial practice, price determination consists of arithmetic operations performed on given data — a technical exercise — and so appears wholly detached from any social relation of production. Marx then makes a decisive theoretical point about competition's role : \"If the limits of value and surplus-value are given, it is easy to perceive how the competition between capitals transforms values into prices of production and still further into commercial prices, transforming surplus-value into average profit. But without these limits, there is absolutely no way of seeing why competition should reduce the general rate of profit to one limit rather than to another, to 15 per cent instead of 1,500 per cent. It can at most reduce it to one level. But there is absolutely no element in it that can determine this level itself.\" This is the logical refutation of the \"competition explains everything\" position. Competition is an equalizing mechanism: it drives rates toward uniformity. But a purely equalizing mechanism cannot specify the level at which equality occurs. It can flatten variation but cannot set the mean. The level must be determined elsewhere — namely, by the mass of surplus-value produced relative to total capital advanced. Without the labour theory of value, competition theory has no answer to the question \"why 15% and not 1,500%?\" This is Marx's classic argument, deployed here against the commercial-capital standpoint specifically. Then the epistemic inversion summarized with full clarity: \"From the standpoint of commercial capital, therefore, turnover itself seems to determine price.\" Contrast the two capitals: for industrial capital, faster turnover \"enable[s] a given capital to exploit more or less labour\" and hence \"has a determining and delimiting effect on the mass of profit, and hence on the general rate of profit as well.\" For commercial capital, \"the rate of profit [is] something external to it, and this rate's inner connection with the formation of surplus-value is completely obliterated .\" Marx then offers a vivid industrial illustration of the difference: \"If the same industrial capital, with other circumstances remaining the same, and particularly with the same organic composition , turns over four times a year instead of twice, it produces twice as much surplus-value and thus profit; and this is palpably evident whenever the industrial capital in question possesses the monopoly of an improved mode of production , which permits it this accelerated turnover for as long as this monopoly lasts.\" Organic composition = the ratio of constant capital (machinery, raw materials) to variable capital (wages), which governs how much living labour a given capital sets in motion — holding it fixed isolates the pure turnover effect. The industrial monopolist who turns over twice as fast visibly doubles his surplus-value: the connection between turnover and profit is transparent to him. But in commerce the relation appears inverted : \"The differing turnover time in different branches of commerce, however, does manifest itself inversely, in this way: the profit made on the turnover of a certain commodity capital stands in inverse proportion to the number of turnovers of the money capital that turns over this commodity capital.\" Hence the shopkeeper's motto: \"'Small profits and quick returns', in other words, appears to the shopkeeper as a principle that he follows on principle.\" He believes he has chosen a strategy. In fact he is executing the law of the general rate of profit in the only form available to him. The maxim is the ideology of a structural necessity. Finally, Marx sets the boundaries of the law: \"It is readily apparent, of course, that this law applies only to the turnovers of commercial capital in a particular line of business, and, leaving aside the mutually compensatory alternation of quicker and slower turnovers, holds only for the average turnover made by the whole commercial capital applied in this branch .\" The individual qualifications then follow. Within a branch: \"The capital of A, involved in the same branch as B, may make more or less than the average number of turnovers. In this case, the others conversely make less or more. This in no way affects the turnover of the total mass of commodity capital invested in this branch. But it is of decisive importance for the individual merchant or retailer .\" An above-average turnover yields super profit — the commercial analogue of the industrialist producing under more favourable conditions than the average. \"If competition compels it, he can sell more cheaply than his fellows without reducing his profit below the average.\" And then the final, characteristically Marxian twist: \"If the conditions that enable him to have a quicker turnover can themselves be purchased, e.g. the location of his sales outlet, he may pay extra rent for this; i.e. a part of his surplus profit is transformed into ground-rent.\" With this closing sentence, Marx connects the differential turnover of commercial capital to ground-rent — the same category he had analysed earlier in Volume 3 for agriculture. Advantageous commercial location (a busy corner, a prime retail pitch) is a natural or socially-created monopoly, and its scarcity price captures a portion of the differential surplus profit arising from faster turnover. The chapter thus ends not with a self-contained result but with a bridge to the theory of rent — and a demonstration that even the most apparently \"arbitrary\" mercantile price premium is decomposable by the same analytical machinery that governs the whole of capitalist production. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS First lesson: turnover is a category that changes meaning with the nature of the capital performing it. For industrial capital, turnover is real — it measures the periodic renewal of a material process and constrains how much surplus-value a given capital can extract per year. For commercial capital, turnover is formal — a mere repetition of purchase and sale that presupposes the industrial process it cannot affect. This asymmetry is not a curiosity of accounting; it is the entire source of the chapter's theoretical complications. It also establishes a hierarchy: commercial capital \"is never anything more than the movement of industrial capital within the circulation sphere,\" and industrial capital remains the locus of value and surplus-value creation. Second lesson: autonomy-within-dependence is the general form of a distinctively capitalist pathology. The formula — \"inner dependence in combination with external autonomy drives commercial capital to a point where the inner connection is forcibly re-established by way of a crisis\" — is the most general proposition in the chapter. It can be restated as a systemic law: any subsystem whose operations are structurally determined by another subsystem, yet conducted according to its own independent logic and financed independently, will accumulate a discrepancy that is resolved abruptly rather than gradually. The credit system supplies the financing that permits the discrepancy to grow; the bill of exchange supplies the fixed maturity that triggers the resolution; and the location of the explosion — wholesale trade and banking rather than retail — follows directly from where the discrepancy is concentrated. The corollary is that crises appear as financial and commercial events even though their substance lies in the relation between production and its realization. Third lesson: the paradox of appearance and essence. The chapter is, in effect, Marx's most sustained demonstration that the necessary illusions of a social system are generated by its real structure, not by error or ignorance. The merchant's belief that price is arbitrary, or that \"small profits and quick returns\" is a business principle he has chosen, is not stupidity; it is the accurate representation of how the system appears from the vantage point he occupies . \"All superficial and distorted views of the overall reproduction process are derived from consideration of commercial capital and from the notions that its specific movements give rise to in the heads of the agents of circulation.\" Science's remit — \"to reduce the visible and merely apparent movement to the actual inner movement\" — is accordingly a critical as well as a descriptive task. Fourth lesson: the decisive refutation of the circulation-determines-price thesis. Marx's argument here is subtle and worth restating sharply. It is not that circulation has no effect on prices; it does. But the effect is bounded and fully specifiable: turnover velocity redistributes a given profit mass across commodity units. It cannot determine the profit mass itself. The limits — production price and average rate of profit — are given externally to the merchant. Hence the argument: competition can equalize but cannot specify the level it equalizes to; if values and surplus-values were not prior, no determinant for 15% versus 1,500% would exist. This is the analytical point at which the apparently most contingent sphere of the capitalist economy — merchant pricing — is shown to be law-governed by the labour theory of value. Fifth lesson: circulation costs matter, and unproductive capital is a real burden. Because commercial capital draws its profit from the same surplus-value pool as industrial capital, its size relative to the total capital fixes its share. Therefore anything that shrinks the commercial capital required — improved transport, better communications, faster turnover generally — raises the general rate of profit. Conversely, the growth of semi-functioning and non-functioning commercial capital (point (3) in Marx's triad), and the growth of the whole circulation infrastructure (shipping, railways, telegraphs — point (2)), operate as a drag on the rate of profit. Marx thus anticipates, in outline, a theory of the unproductive absorption of social capital, formulated in 1865 but of ongoing analytical relevance to the study of financialization, logistics overheads, and the retail sector's capital intensity. Sixth lesson: the incompleteness of the argument is itself signposted. The chapter repeatedly defers: costs of circulation K and their profit ΔK are set aside; speculation and the division of commercial capital are excluded; a promised study of \"Competition among Capitals\" is never delivered (footnoted as an intention of 1865). These deferrals are not evasions but honest markers of a research programme. Reading the chapter well means reading its brackets and its footnotes as carefully as its arguments. Broader implications. Theoretically, Chapter 18 completes the architecture of Volume 3's account of profit distribution by showing that even the swerve from value into commercial price preserves the invariants of the labour theory of value: turnover redistributes; it does not create. Historically, it explains the decline of the great mercantile fortunes of the early modern period not as a moral or commercial failure but as the necessary consequence of rising productivity and the equalization of profit rates. Practically, it provides the analytical basis for understanding why commercial booms can persist for extended periods independently of final demand; why financial and wholesale sectors are the locus of crisis onset; and why retail \"strategy\" — pricing, location, velocity — is largely a behavioural expression of structural position. And philosophically, it offers one of Marx's cleanest statements of his critical epistemology: that the task of science is precisely to show how the ordinary, real, quotidian experience of social agents — the merchant's ledger, the shopkeeper's rule of thumb, Roscher's \"good sense and humanity\" — can be simultaneously true as experience and false as explanation . ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 17 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 19"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 19", "title": "Chapter 19", "subtitle": "Money-Dealing Capital", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 19/Chapter 19 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 6542, "read_time": "33 min read", "tags": ["Commercial Capital & Merchant's Profit", "Money, Currency & Circulation"], "keywords": ["Money-Dealing Capital", "Technical Operations of Money Handling (Payments, Cashiering)", "Concentration of Banking Infrastructure", "Pure Operations of Money as Currency Separated from Commerce", "Commercial Ledger Balancing and Wire Settlement"], "text": "Volume 3 • Chapter 19: Money-Dealing Capital — Das Kapital 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,542 words ⏱️ ~33 min read System Sans Georgia EB Garamond Charter / Merriweather Palatino Baskerville Times New Roman Humanist Sans Dyslexic / Clear Monospace A- A+ 🔊 Listen 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print ▶️ Play ⏹️ 0.75x 1.0x 1.25x 1.5x 2.0x Click Play to listen to chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 19: Money-Dealing Capital 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW &amp; THESIS The Problem the Chapter Poses Chapter 19 asks a deceptively narrow-sounding question: what is money-dealing capital, and how does it come into being? Everyone knows that under capitalism a distinct branch of business emerges around money itself — exchange dealers, cashiers, banks of deposit and exchange, the bullion trade, and later credit institutions. Marx's problem is not the empirical existence of these businesses, but their capital-theoretic status . If value and surplus-value originate exclusively in production (in the exploitation of labour-power), then a branch of capital whose entire business is handling money — receiving it, paying it out, keeping accounts of it, storing it, converting one kind of coin into another, and shifting bullion between countries — cannot be an origin of value. Yet it appears to be just that: capital that is advanced as money and returns as more money, in the bare form M—M′. Marx therefore has to explain how money-dealing capital arises, what it actually does, and why it commands a profit, without conceding it any power to create value. The Central Thesis The chapter's thesis is that money-dealing capital is the autonomised form of the purely technical movements that money performs within the circulation of industrial (and, derivatively, commercial) capital . When the operations by which capital takes in and pays out money, keeps its books, holds its reserves, and converts coin and bullion are separated off from the productive and commercial capitalists and become the exclusive, specialised function of a distinct section of capital performing them for the capitalist class as a whole , those operations \"transform this capital into money-dealing capital.\" The chapter insists on four consequences of this derivation: Money-dealing capital is derivative, not original. It is a separated fragment of industrial capital's circulation, not an independent source of wealth. Its movements \"are thus again simply movements of a now independent part of the industrial capital in the course of its reproduction process.\" Its functions are costs of circulation, not value-creating labour. Payment, receipt, bookkeeping, guarding the hoard, money-changing, and bullion transfer are \"work,\" but unproductive work; capitalism reduces this overhead by concentrating it. It has no special form of circulation of its own. Unlike commercial capital, which shows an articulate M—C—M where the commodity moves twice, money-dealing capital exhibits only the general, empty-of-content form M—M′; the mediation is purely technical , never material . Its profit is a deduction from already-produced surplus-value , because it deals only with values already realised — indeed, often only with claims to value (bills, cheques). How the Chapter Fits the Broader Framework Chapter 19 is the culminating chapter of Part IV, \"The Transformation of Commodity Capital and Money Capital into Commercial Capital and Money-Dealing Capital (Merchant's Capital).\" Having shown in Chapters 16–18 how a portion of industrial capital's circulation consolidates into commercial capital (dealing in commodities) and generates commercial profit, Marx applies the same logic of autonomisation by division of labour to money's own functions. The chapter thus completes the analysis of merchant's capital in its two branches — dealing in commodities and dealing in money — before Part V turns to interest-bearing capital . The relation to Volume 1 is also decisive: the functions of money as means of purchase, means of payment, hoard, and world money (Volume 1, Chapter 3) now reappear at the level of capital's circulation, because capital \"has to perform [them] in the form of money capital.\" And the chapter is programmatically incomplete : Marx says explicitly that money-dealing is \"fully developed … as soon as the functions of lending and borrowing, and trade on credit, are combined with its other functions,\" and defers that fusion to the next Part on interest-bearing capital. The pure, credit-free money-dealer considered here — the cashier, the exchanger, the bullion-mover — is the embryo out of which the bank and the credit system will grow. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION &amp; ANALYSIS 2.1 The Opening Move: Technical Movements Become an Autonomous Capital The chapter opens not with history but with a conceptual operation — the transmutation of a set of routine technical acts into capital . The first sentence names the object: \"The purely technical movements that money undergoes in the circulation process of industrial capital, and, we can now add, also that of commodity-dealing, commercial capital.\" The words \"purely technical\" are load-bearing. They separate these movements from anything substantive — from productivity, from the creation of value, from the metamorphosis of commodities in their material aspect. Into this technical domain fall the physical and bookkeeping acts by which money is taken in, paid out, registered, stored, and converted. The parenthetical clause tells us why commercial capital is included: commercial capital \"takes over part of the circulation movement of industrial capital as its own specific movement.\" This is a compressed reference to Chapters 16–18: commercial capital is itself already a separated fragment of industrial capital's circulation. So the money movements of commercial capital are, at bottom, still the money movements of industrial capital, merely displaced onto a specialised agency. The decisive causal clause then explains the transformation: \"these movements, having acquired autonomy as the function of a special capital which practises them, and them alone, as its specific operations, transform this capital into money-dealing capital.\" The mechanism is autonomisation through specialisation . A function that was incidental and distributed becomes, when monopolised by one section of capital, the constitutive identity of that capital. This is the same logical figure Marx used for commercial capital: a part of the total capital that \"separates off\" and performs \"these operations of the reproduction process for the whole of the remaining capital.\" Note the scope marker — for the whole class of industrial and commercial capitalists. Two further sentences nail down the derivation. First, \"A part of the industrial capital … exists throughout not only in the money form, as money capital in general, but as money capital in the process of these technical functions.\" This says that at any moment a portion of total social capital is caught mid-operation in the money form, precisely because these technical functions must be performed continuously. Second, the closing claim of the paragraph: \"The movements of this money capital are thus again simply movements of a now independent part of the industrial capital in the course of its reproduction process.\" The independence is real at the level of form — a distinct branch of business, distinct firms, distinct accounting — but illusory at the level of content : substantively it remains industrial capital in circulation. 2.2 Starting-Point and Transition-Point: the Form of the Metamorphosis Marx next takes up the question of where money appears as a terminus . \"It is only where capital is newly invested — which is also the case with accumulation — that capital in its money form appears as the starting-point and finishing-point of the movement.\" For fresh capital, money genuinely brackets the whole circuit: M … M′. But \"for any capital that is already in its process, both starting-point and finishing-point appear as simply points of transition.\" Once production is continuous, money is no longer a genuine boundary; it is a waystation in a loop. He then writes out the metamorphosis of industrial capital between production and re-entry into production: C′—M—C . Here C′ is commodity capital coming out of production (carrying surplus-value), M is the money it converts into on sale, and C is the bundle of means of production and labour-power bought to resume production. Marx reminds us (with a backward reference to simple commodity circulation) that in this chain M is \"simply the end result of one phase … only to be the starting-point of its opposite, complementary phase.\" Money is the pivot , not the point of origin . A wrinkle appears immediately: \"even though, as far as commercial capital is concerned, the industrial capital's C—M always presents itself as M—C—M, yet for it too … the actual process is also a continuous one of C—M—C.\" This is a form-versus-substance distinction. For the merchant who lives by buying cheap and selling dear, the circuit appears as M—C—M (buy to sell). But considered as the circulation organ of total industrial capital, even the merchant's movement is continuous C—M—C — one moment of a larger loop that must keep running. The merchant's self-understanding (M—C—M) is the appearance ; the total process (C—M—C) is the reality . 2.3 Simultaneity: How Commercial Capital Occupies Both Phases at Once There is a mechanical fact about commercial capital that will become crucial later: \"Commercial capital … goes through the acts C—M and M—C simultaneously.\" This is not a mere curiosity but the reason money-handling becomes a large-scale, continuous business. Because production continues without pause and turnovers overlap, \"the same capital is always buying and selling at the same time.\" The sentence drives the point home operationally: \"While one part of the capital is being transformed into money, so as later to be transformed back into commodities, the other part is simultaneously being transformed into commodities, so as later to be transformed back into money.\" The capital is partitioned across phases at each instant, so both phases exist side by side in the same firm. This simultaneity has a direct consequence for the kind of money-function involved. \"Whether the money functions here as means of circulation or means of payment depends on the form of the commodity exchange.\" Cash sales make money a means of circulation ; credit sales make it a means of payment . Either way, the capitalist \"always has to make payments to many people and receive money in payment from many people\" — the operations are irreducibly social and plural. And when money functions as means of payment (credit), it \"makes it necessary for accounts to be drawn up and balanced.\" Marx then delivers the foundational judgement of the entire chapter: \"This work is a cost of circulation and not value-creating labour. It is cut down by being undertaken for the capitalist class as a whole by a special department of agents or capitalists.\" Two propositions are fused here: (i) the labour is unproductive (cost-producing, not value-producing), and (ii) centralisation reduces the cost — and therefore there is an economic rationale for the existence of a specialised money trade. This rationale — the reduction of overhead achieved by pooling — recurs like a refrain later in the chapter. 2.4 The Hoard as a Fourth Technical Burden To payment, receipt, and bookkeeping Marx now adds a fourth chore: the hoard. \"A certain section of capital must always exist as a hoard, as potential money capital: a reserve of means of purchase and payment, of unoccupied capital in the money form, waiting to be utilized; part of the capital constantly returns in this form.\" This is a permanent structural necessity of capitalist turnover — you cannot pay tomorrow's bills with goods you have not yet sold, so a reserve must sit in money. And the hoard is not passive: \"On top of the taking-in and paying-out of money, and book-keeping, the hoard itself has to be looked after, which is again a special operation.\" Guarding, storing, and administrating reserves is itself work . The paragraph then describes the hoard's metabolism in strongly fluid imagery: \"the hoard is constantly dissolved into means of circulation and payment, and reformed from money received from sales and from payments falling due.\" Structurally, the hoard is a reservoir that both feeds circulation and is replenished from it. It is \"money dissociated from the capital function itself\" — money between functions, so to speak — and it is \"this purely technical operation\" — the handling of this fluid reserve — that \"gives rise to special work and costs — costs of circulation.\" The category \"costs of circulation\" is now applied to four operations in sequence: payment, receipt, bookkeeping, and reserve-keeping. All four are overhead; all four can be economised by being centralised. 2.5 The Double Division of Labour and the Emergence of Money-Dealing Capital Marx now states the general mechanism explicitly: \"The division of labour brings it about that these technical operations required by the functions of capital are performed as far as possible for the capitalist class as a whole by a particular division of agents or capitalists, as their exclusive functions, or are concentrated in their hands.\" Two things can happen — either a distinct set of agents takes them over, or they are concentrated in a few hands . Either way, the same result: specialisation. The phrase \"There is here a double division of labour, just as with commercial capital\" introduces a crucial piece of terminology. The double division is: (a) first-order separation — the technical money work is split off from production and from commodity-dealing as such; and (b) second-order separation — within the money business, a further internal division into \"various branches independent of one another,\" plus \"the development of the workplace within these branches (large offices, numerous bookkeepers and cashiers, highly developed division of labour).\" The parenthetical is notable for its concreteness: it names large offices and numerous bookkeepers and cashiers , marking the appearance of the modern administrative workplace as an object of Marx's analysis. This is Adam Smith's pin-factory principle now applied to the office rather than the workshop. The concrete functions are then enumerated to define the new species of capital: \"The payment and receipt of money, settlement of balances, keeping of current accounts, storage of money, etc., in separation from the acts that make these technical operations necessary , make the capital advanced in these functions into money-dealing capital.\" Notice the qualifier — the separation is what makes the difference. The same acts performed incidentally by a merchant do not constitute money-dealing capital; only their externalisation , their becoming an independent business, does. Finally, Marx grounds the whole analysis in the nature of money: \"the various operations whose achievement of an autonomous position as special businesses gives rise to the money trade arise out of the various characteristics of money itself and its functions, which capital therefore also has to perform in the form of money capital.\" This is the bridge to the next section: the money trade is not arbitrary — it is dictated by the properties of money that capital, once it adopts the money form, cannot avoid. 2.6 The Historical Genesis: Exchange Business and Bullion Trade Marx now shifts register from theory to history. He first recalls an earlier argument (footnote 42 cites A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy and Volume 1, Chapter 2): \"money in general developed originally in the exchange of products between different communities.\" Money has an inter-communal origin — it emerges first between societies, not within one. Hence the historical claim: \"Dealing in money, therefore, i.e. trade in the money commodity, first develops out of international trade.\" As soon as distinct national coinages exist, \"merchants who buy abroad have to convert their own national coin into the local coinage and vice versa, or else convert coins of various kinds into uncoined pure silver or gold as world money.\" Exchange dealing therefore has two levels : coin-for-coin (local currencies) and coin-for-bullion (national currency vs. world money). To support this, Marx inserts footnote 43, quoting Hüllmann's Städtewesen des Mittelalters (Bonn, 1826–9). Hüllmann's cited passage emphasises \"the great differences among coins as regards their weight and standard\" and \"the imprints stamped on them by the many princes and cities that had minting rights.\" Because settlement was normally required in a particular local coin, travelling merchants \"provided themselves with uncoined pure silver, or even gold,\" exchanged local coins for bullion on returning home, and thereby made \"exchange dealing, the conversion of uncoined precious metal into local coin and vice versa, … a very widespread and profitable business.\" The evidence is empirical, drawn from medieval urban history, and serves to verify the conceptual derivation: the money trade has a spontaneous, practical base in the fragmentation of monetary systems. From this follows the rise of exchange banks , \"in which silver (or gold) functions as world money — known as bank or commercial money — as distinct from currency.\" Here Marx introduces the terminological contrast between bank/commercial money (functioning as world money) and currency (national coin). He adds a historical detail: \"Exchange transactions, if only involving notes for payment to travellers from a money-changer in one country to one in another, were already developed in Rome and Greece out of the actual business of money-changing\" — evidence that the function is ancient, not capitalist by nature. The bullion trade is then distinguished from coin-exchange. Its spontaneous base is \"trade in gold and silver as commodities (raw materials for the production of luxury goods).\" Marx cross-references his earlier account (Volume 1, Chapter 3, §3, c) of money as world money, where two functions were identified: (1) \"circulation back and forth between the various national spheres of circulation, for the settlement of international payments, as well as the movement of capital lent at interest\"; and (2) \"the movement from the sources of precious metal production across the world market, and distribution of this supply between the various national spheres of circulation.\" So the bullion trade mediates two distinct flows: settlement of international balances (plus loan-capital transfers) and the distribution of newly produced metal. Two further points round out the historical picture. First, \"In England, goldsmiths still functioned as bankers for the greater part of the seventeenth century\" — a concrete instance of how the banking function grew out of an existing trade in precious metal. Second, Marx carefully limits the scope : \"We shall completely ignore for the moment … everything related to dealings in securities, in short, all the specific forms of the credit system, which we are not yet concerned with here.\" This is an explicit disclaimer — the chapter treats money-dealing in isolation from credit, and the credit system is postponed. 2.7 World Money and the Reduction to Bullion The section on world money is brief but analytically sharp. \"As world money, national money discards its local character; one national money is expressed in another, and in this way they are all reduced to their gold or silver content.\" International circulation strips away the political-numismatic form of coin and leaves only the metallic substance. Then a second reduction: \"Since both these commodities circulate as world money, they have to be reduced in turn to their mutual value ratio, which is constantly changing.\" Gold and silver themselves have a fluctuating ratio; someone must translate between them. \"The money-dealer makes it his own special business to carry on this intermediary function.\" The money-dealer, in other words, specialises in translation — between currencies, and between the two world monies. The generalisation closes the historical section: \"Money-changing and the bullion trade are thus the original forms of the money business and arise from the double function of money: as national coin and as world money.\" Two original forms (exchange, bullion) correspond to two aspects of money (national coin, world money). The historical genesis is thereby deduced from monetary categories. 2.8 Two General Results of Capitalist Production and Trade Marx now steps back to name the structural facts that make money-dealing possible and necessary, and he does so with a striking generality: \"The capitalist production process, and trade in general, even on the basis of pre-capitalist modes of production , lead to the following results.\" Money-dealing, then, is not exclusively capitalist; it is a form of handling money that any developed commercial system generates, and that capitalism only takes over and systemises. First result — hoard formation. \"The accumulation of money as a hoard, in this case as the section of capital that must always exist in the money form, as a reserve fund of means of purchase and payment.\" Marx here distinguishes two forms of the hoard: Form 1 : the reserve fund of means of purchase and payment — the buffer every active business requires. \"This is the first form of the hoard, as it reappears in the capitalist mode of production and generally comes into being with the development of commercial capital.\" The hoard precedes capitalism; commercial capital develops it, and industrial capital inherits it. Form 2 : \"idle capital temporarily unoccupied in the money form, together with newly accumulated money capital that has not yet been invested.\" This is money that is not yet or not fully in function — accumulated surplus awaiting investment. Both forms must be administrated: \"The functions that this hoard formation itself makes necessary start with its storage, book-keeping, etc.\" Marx notes in passing that hoarding applies \"as much to international circulation as to domestic.\" Second result — the flow of payments. \"The expenditure of money in buying, and its receipt from selling, paying and the receipt of payments, settlement of payments, etc.\" This is the routine stream of buying, selling, paying, and settling. At this point Marx introduces the rudimentary role of the money-dealer: \"To start with, the money-dealer does all this as a simple cashier for merchants and industrial capitalists.\" Footnote 44 (Vissering) fleshes this out with a vivid passage on the Netherlands. Vissering describes the cashier who \"receives a certain sum of money from the merchants who make use of his services, opening a 'credit' for them in his accounts\"; who collects their claims and credits them; who pays out against their drafts ( kassiers briefjes ) and debits them; and who charges \"a small charge, gaining an appropriate wage for his labour, simply on the strength of the size of turnover.\" Significantly, Vissering notes that when two of a cashier's clients owe each other, the debts \"are adjusted very simply by entries on both accounts,\" and the cashiers \"settle their mutual claims among themselves each day.\" Vissering adds that the cashier business \"excludes industrial undertakings, speculation and the opening of overdrafts\" — the pure money-dealer does not lend. Marx's second footnote (also from Hüllmann) traces the Venetian cashiers' associations — \"the first beginnings of the so-called giro banks\" — where members \"subscribed certain sums on which they drew drafts for their creditors,\" with debits and credits entered directly in the ledger. 2.9 Money-Dealing Only \"Fully Developed\" with Credit Marx now issues a programmatic statement that delimits the whole chapter: \"Money-dealing is fully developed, even if still in its first beginnings, as soon as the functions of lending and borrowing, and trade on credit, are combined with its other functions . We shall deal with this in the next Part, on interest-bearing capital.\" This is a caesura — the reader is told that what is being analysed here is only the pre-credit moment. Money-dealing, in its full development, is banking; what this chapter isolates is the husk before the kernel of credit is inserted. Everything in Part V will build on this base. 2.10 The Bullion Trade as a Purely Derivative Result The bullion dealer's activity is then reduced to its determinative causes: \"The bullion trade itself, the transfer of gold or silver from one country to another, is simply the result of commodity trade, determined by the rate of exchange, which expresses the state of international payments and the rate of interest in various markets. The bullion dealer as such only transmits the results.\" Two \"determinants\" are named — the rate of exchange (state of international payments) and the rate of interest (differentials across markets). The bullion dealer transmits , does not set . This is a microcosm of the chapter's whole method: the money business is effect , not cause . 2.11 The Great Causal Chain: Money Circulation Determined by Commodity Circulation The chapter now congeals into its most rigorous theoretical passage — a regressive determination argument. Marx recalls Volume 1, Chapter 3, where he established that \"the movement of the quantities of money circulating as means of purchase and payment is determined by the volume and speed of the metamorphosis of commodities.\" Then he adds the new step: that metamorphosis \"is itself simply an aspect of the reproduction process as a whole.\" So we obtain a causal hierarchy : The reproduction process (production + circulation of capital) is the fundamental reality. The metamorphosis of commodities (their sale and purchase) is an aspect of that process. Monetary circulation — the volume and speed of money flows — is determined by that metamorphosis. Every step is derivative. He then extends this to three special cases: Obtaining the money-material. \"These metals … reducible to direct commodity exchange, exchange of gold or silver as a commodity against other commodities, and is thus just as much an aspect of commodity exchange as obtaining iron or other metals.\" Getting gold out of the ground is, in its economic shape , just the exchange of a commodity against other commodities — no different in principle from mining iron. World-market metal movements. \"This is as completely determined by international commodity exchange as the movement of money as a means of domestic purchase and payment is determined by domestic commodity exchange.\" A precise parallel: international = international; domestic = domestic. Both are determinations of the underlying commodity flows. Anomalous movements. \"The export and import of precious metals … in as much as this is caused simply by the devaluation of a national currency, or by bi-metallism, lies outside monetary circulation proper and is merely a correction of aberrations brought about by arbitrary state decrees.\" Devaluation and bimetallism are exceptions generated by state fiat; they do not refute the law, they merely perturb it. Hoard formation. \"This formation is simply a necessary precipitate of the circulation process.\" The word precipitate is chemically resonant: the hoard condenses out of circulation as a natural by-product, not a deliberate independent act. The conclusion of this cascade: \"Monetary circulation as a whole is a mere resultant of commodity circulation … and … commodity circulation itself represents simply the circulation process of capital.\" The whole money business is thus triply derivative — a resultant of a resultant of a resultant. 2.12 Specific Limitations of Money-Dealing's Mediating Role Having established the causal hierarchy, Marx precisely circumscribes what the money trade can and cannot do. The move is a negative determination — a series of statements of the form \"money-dealing does not … , it only …\" It does not produce circulation. \"This monetary circulation itself, as a moment of commodity circulation, is given in advance for money-dealing.\" What money-dealing finds, it finds already there. \"The latter's mediatory role is rather confined to the technical operations of monetary circulation, which it concentrates, reduces and simplifies .\" Three verbs mark the technique: concentration (pooling), reduction (eliminating redundancy), simplification (clearing offsets). It does not form hoards. \"Money-dealing does not form hoards, but it supplies the technical means for hoard formation, in so far as this is voluntary … thus reducing it to its economic minimum.\" The crucial economic theorem follows: \"the reserve fund of means of purchase and payment, if managed on behalf of the capitalist class as a whole, does not need to be so great as if each capitalist had to keep his fund separately.\" This is the pooling theorem : aggregate reserve needs fall because individual reserves are partly redundant with one another. It does not buy precious metals. \"The money trade does not buy precious metals, but only mediates their distribution after the commodity trade has bought them.\" It does not determine payments. \"Money-dealing mediates the settlement of accounts … and by the mechanism it creates for these settlements it reduces the quantity of money these require; but it determines neither the relationship nor the volume of these mutual payments.\" The concrete illustration: \"The bills and cheques … exchanged for one another in banks and clearing houses derive from completely independent businesses and are the results of already given operations, so that all that is involved here is a better technical settlement of these results.\" It does not determine purchases. \"In so far as money circulates as means of purchase, the volume and number of purchases and sales is completely independent of money-dealing. This can only abbreviate the technical operations that accompany these transactions.\" The verbs of the money trade, in sum, are concentrate, reduce, simplify, abbreviate — verbs of efficiency, never verbs of creation or determination. The negative formula \"does not determine\" is hammered home four times in a few sentences: an anaphoric insistence that punctuates the chapter like a hammer on the same nail. 2.13 The Fundamental Distinction from Commodity-Dealing Capital Marx now formalises the contrast with commercial capital, and in doing so produces one of the chapter's sharpest conceptual statements: \"Money-dealing in the pure form in which we are considering it here, i.e. separate from the credit system, thus only bears on the technical side of one aspect of commodity circulation, i.e. monetary circulation and the various functions of money that arise from it.\" The phrase technical side twice in this chapter — \"purely technical,\" \"technical side\" — is the decisive qualifier. Money-dealing capital touches only money's technical functions, not the metamorphosis of commodities in their material mutual exchange. The contrast is drawn with a now classic formula: \"This distinguishes money-dealing quite fundamentally from dealing in commodities, which mediates the metamorphosis of commodities and commodity exchange, even though it allows this process of commodity capital to appear as the process of a special capital separate from industrial capital.\" Commodity-dealing mediates the metamorphosis (the real, material conversion of one commodity into another, including their productive consumption); money-dealing only handles the money side . Both autonomised, but at different depths of reality. Then a crucial asymmetry of form : \"If therefore commodity-dealing commercial capital displays a special form of circulation, M—C—M, where it is the commodity that changes place twice and brings about the reflux of money, as opposed to C—M—C, where it is money that changes hands twice and mediates commodity exchange, no such special form can be seen in the case of money-dealing capital .\" This is a structural argument. Commercial capital has its own circuit with its own distinctive shape — money returns because commodities have moved twice. Money-dealing capital has no such form : it performs a service to the circulation of others, so its circuit is not its own productive loop but a fee-bearing operation on the loops of others. 2.14 The Form M—M′ and the Nature of Profit Nevertheless, money-dealing capital does appear in a capital-form. \"Where money capital is advanced by a special section of capitalists in this technical mediation of monetary circulation — this capital representing on a diminished scale the additional capital which the merchants and industrial capitalists would otherwise have to advance for this purpose themselves — there we also have the general form of capital M—M′.\" The parenthetical re-states the pooling theorem: the money-dealer's capital is a diminished version of what otherwise would have to be advanced individually — collective provision is cheaper than individual provision. The empty formula is then unpacked: \"The advance of M means that the person advancing it receives M + ΔM .\" Here M denotes the money advanced, and ΔM denotes the increment received back — the profit. And then the crucial qualification: \"But the mediation between M and M′ involves only the technical aspects of the metamorphosis, and not its material aspects.\" Once again, technical only . The profit is thus not produced in the passage M→M′; the passage is a mere form. Contrast this with industrial capital, where M—C … P … C′—M′ earns a profit because labour was extracted during P. For money-dealing capital there is no P — no production phase. So ΔM cannot originate in the money-dealer's own operations. 2.15 Where the Money-Dealer's Capital and Profit Actually Come From The final substantive beat answers the natural question: whose money is it, and whose profit? \"It is clear enough that the mass of money capital which the money dealers operate with is the circulating money capital of the merchants and industrialists, and that the operations the money dealers perform are simply the operations of the merchants and industrialists, mediated by the former.\" Two identifications: (i) the substance of the money-dealer's capital is other capitalists' circulating money capital ; (ii) the content of the money-dealer's operations is the merchants' and industrialists' own operations , merely intermediated. The money-dealer, so to speak, borrows his substance and his content from his clients. Then the corresponding proposition for profit: \"It is equally clear that their profit is simply a deduction from surplus-value, since they are dealing only with values already realized (even if realized only in the form of claims for payment).\" Two grounds are given for calling the profit a deduction — a share of already-produced surplus-value rather than a new creation: Values already realised. The money-dealer operates on values that have completed production, sale, and realization (or on claims to them). Claims rather than material wealth. Even where the objects are mere bills and cheques — \"claims for payment\" — this does not change the character of the transaction, because a claim presupposes a value already produced. Profit here is a redistributive category — a fee skimmed from the surplus-value mass — and this anticipates Part V, where the same will be true (with greater complexity) of interest. 2.16 The Duplication of Functions — Autonomisation Is Never Complete The chapter ends with a caution against overestimation of autonomisation: \"Just as with commodity trade, here too we find a duplication of functions . For one section of the technical operations connected with money circulation must be performed by the commodity dealers and producers themselves.\" The separation is real but never total: alongside the specialised money-dealers, a portion of the technical work remains with the producers and commodity-dealers. The same was true for commodity-dealing capital: some selling remains in the hands of industrialists. So money-dealing capital is not the displacement of the function, but its partial externalisation — a distinguishing feature that reproduces, in the money branch, the structural asymmetry already established for the commodity branch. The book thus closes the chapter on a note of partial autonomy , leaving the fully developed system — the credit system — to the pages that follow. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS &amp; BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles The chapter delivers, in compressed form, a set of interlocking theoretical principles: Autonomisation requires externalisation and specialisation. A function becomes the basis of a distinct capital only when it is separated off from the general body of capitalist activity and performed exclusively by a distinct section for the class as a whole. The chapter provides the paradigm: \"these movements, having acquired autonomy as the function of a special capital which practises them, and them alone … transform this capital into money-dealing capital.\" This is the same mechanism Marx has already identified for commercial capital, and it will be applied again to interest-bearing capital in Part V. Autonomisation, in Marx's framework, is not mystification; it is a real division of labour whose form independence masks a content dependence. Circulation does not create value. Every labour-power deployed by the money-dealer is \"a cost of circulation and not value-creating labour.\" The money trade earns its profit \"simply as a deduction from surplus-value.\" This is the crucial continuation of Volume 1's thesis, now extended to capital's external ornamentation. There is no third category — neither production nor circulation creates value; circulation only realises and redistributes what production has produced. The economic ground of the money trade is cost reduction . Money-dealing exists because it \"concentrates, reduces, and simplifies\" — because pooled reserves are smaller than individual ones, because clearing reduces cash needs, because abbreviation of routine operations is cheaper than decentralised repetition. This efficiency argument is the material rationale that makes the money-dealer a useful agent rather than a pure parasite: he does perform a real service, even if he creates no value. Form versus content in circulation formulas. Commercial capital shows M—C—M (money \"makes\" money by buying to sell); money-dealing capital shows only M—M′ (money returns as more money with no intervening form of its own ). The absence of a distinctive circuit is the formal signature of money-dealing capital's purely technical character. M—M′ here is the appearance — the content is elsewhere (in production), and the mediation is only technical. Broader Theoretical and Practical Implications The chapter's implications radiate in several directions. For the analysis of finance. Chapter 19 is the conceptual gate to Part V. By showing that money-dealing capital becomes \"fully developed … as soon as the functions of lending and borrowing … are combined with its other functions,\" Marx sets up the bank as the fusion of money-dealing with credit. Everything the reader will later encounter — interest, bank capital, fictitious capital, the credit cycle — presupposes the analytic groundwork laid here: that financial institutions emerge from the technical handling of money, that their profit is a share of surplus-value, and that their power over circulation is a delegated power. For a critique of financial fetishism. The chapter dismantles the appearance that money begets money. If M—M′ has no material content — \"only the technical aspects of the metamorphosis, and not its material aspects\" — then the money market's apparent self-generation is a surface phenomenon. The financial form is real but derivative; the \"money that makes money\" is always drawing on value produced elsewhere. For historical materialism. The historical sections (Hüllmann, Vissering, the Roman and Greek exchange transactions, English goldsmiths) demonstrate that money-dealing predates capitalism — it develops alongside international trade and commodity production in general. Capitalism does not invent the money trade; it takes over a pre-existing functional niche and reorganises it on a large, systematically coordinated scale. The diachronic and synchronic analyses are fused: what appears historically (exchange banks, cashier business, giro banks) is exactly what the concept requires. For our understanding of \"unproductive labour under capitalism.\" The chapter gives a rigorous criterion: unproductive labour is labour that performs circulation functions, is necessary for turnover, and is reducible by centralisation. This is a substantive analytical category — not a moral slur — and it explains why capitalism has a vast, wealthy, but structurally unproductive service sector in money-handling, administration, and commerce. For the philosophical structure of Marx's argument. Underlying the chapter is a recurring figure: form-independence and content-dependence . The money-dealer appears to be an autonomous capitalist, dealing M—M′. In reality, \"his movements are simply movements of a now independent part of the industrial capital in the course of its reproduction process.\" The chapter repeats this dialectic at every level — circulation vs. production, money-trade vs. commodity-trade, form of circulation vs. material content, autonomous capital vs. delegated function. The lesson is not that appearances are illusions to be dismissed, but that they are moments of a totality whose unity Marx reconstructs step by step. The chapter therefore tells us something essential about capitalism as a whole: it generates proliferations of specialised agencies — commercial, financial, administrative — that acquire the form of independent capitals, even while their substance remains the total social capital in its reproduction. Money-dealing capital is one instance of a general law. To understand what capital is, one must be able to see through its proliferations to the production process that alone creates the value on which they all draw — and Chapter 19 is Marx's most concentrated demonstration of that method. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 18 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 20"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 20", "title": "Chapter 20", "subtitle": "Historical Facts about Merchant's Capital", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 20/Chapter 20 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 9094, "read_time": "45 min read", "tags": ["Commercial Capital & Merchant's Profit", "Historical Specificity of Capital"], "keywords": ["Historical Facts about Merchant's Capital", "Independent Existence of Merchant Capital in Antiquity & Feudalism", "Subordination of Trade to Industrial Capital", "Two Paths of Transition to Capitalism (Producer to Merchant vs Merchant to Producer)", "Dissolving Power of Commerce on Old Production Modes"], "text": "Chapter 20 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 9,053 words ⏱️ ~45 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 20 of Capital Volume III — \"Historical Material on Merchant's Capital\" — is the pivot on which Marx's analytical treatment of merchant's capital turns into a historical-materialist reconstruction of the entire relationship between circulation and production across world history. It occupies a peculiar strategic position inside Part IV (\"The Transformation of Commodity Capital and Money Capital into Commodity-Dealing Capital and Money-Dealing Capital\"). The preceding chapters have been strictly analytical: they demonstrated that commercial capital and money-dealing capital are not separate branches of industry but functionally differentiated fractions of social capital — the autonomisation of a phase that every industrial capital must pass through. Chapter 20 now asks a different and much larger question: what is the historical status of merchant's capital? The answer is startling and programmatic. Merchant's capital is the oldest independent form of capital , older than the capitalist mode of production, and its independent flourishing stands, as Marx states as a law, in inverse proportion to the general economic development of society . The chapter's core thesis can be stated in three nested propositions. First, merchant's capital (whether commercial/commodity-dealing or money-dealing) is not a special kind of industrial capital analogous to mining, agriculture, manufacture, or transport. Such an analogy — the \"crude conception\" of vulgar economics — is absurd because it mistakes a form-determination arising from the circulation phase of industrial capital for a material branch arising from the social division of labour. Second, historically, trading capital precedes capitalism: it exists, and must exist, on the basis of simple commodity circulation alone, whatever the underlying mode of production (primitive community, slavery, petty-bourgeois peasant production, or capitalism itself). Third, and decisively, the autonomy of commercial capital is the index of the non-subjection of production to capital ; it is precisely when production has not yet been captured by capital that commercial capital appears as \"the function of capital par excellence .\" The full domination of industrial capital demotes commercial capital from a sovereign power to a mere \"agent\" of productive capital — its profit rate levelled down to the general average by the equalisation of profit rates. The chapter's central problem is double. There is first the theoretical puzzle inherited from classical political economy: commercial profit appears to be \"profit upon alienation,\" i.e. a gain made purely in buying cheap and selling dear, which seems to contradict the law of value (the exchange of equivalents). Smith and Ricardo, precisely because they focused on industrial capital and derived the principles of value, profit, and surplus-value from its analysis, were \"perplexed\" by commercial capital and either ignored it or collapsed it into industrial capital; Ricardo, when forced to address it in foreign trade, argued that trade creates no value — an argument Marx immediately generalises to domestic commerce as well. The second problem is historical: if trade and commercial capital are so ancient and so powerful, why did the transition to capitalism not happen everywhere and always? Marx's answer — that trade is solvent but not determining , and that the outcome of dissolution depends on the \"solidity and inner articulation\" of the pre-existing mode of production — is one of the most compressed statements of historical materialism anywhere in Capital . The chapter's intellectual objective is thus to install commercial capital in a properly dialectical historical narrative: to show that the same form (M–C–M′) that appears as the dominant, independent existence of capital in antiquity and the Middle Ages is downgraded under capitalism to a moment of the reproduction of industrial capital, and to demonstrate that \"trade\" is never a prime mover but always a mediator whose effects are conditioned by the productive relations it encounters. In doing so, the chapter prepares the ground for the next Part on the special form in which money is accumulated by commercial and money-dealing capital (explicitly deferred in the opening line), for the later treatment of interest-bearing capital (also \"an age-old form\"), and for the eventual analysis of the credit system. It also functions as a methodological clinic: it shows the reader how to distinguish form from substance , how to read the past through categories adequate to the present, and how to resist the apologetic naturalisation that converts historically specific social mediations into eternal, \"absolute and necessary\" ones — the fallacy Marx pillories in his footnote on Roscher's abuse of the word \"mediation.\" 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Opening Polemic: Merchant's Capital as an Autonomised Phase, Not a Branch of Industry The chapter opens by bracketing its own unfinished business — \"The special form in which money is accumulated by commercial and money-dealing capital will be considered only in the next Part\" — thereby signalling that what follows is not new analysis of the accumulation mechanism but a historical and critical re-reading of what has already been established. Marx then launches directly into the polemic. \"Nothing could be more absurd\" than to treat merchant's capital as a special kind of industrial capital, in the way that \"mining, agriculture, stock-raising, manufacture, transport, etc. are branches resulting from the social division of labour and as such form particular spheres of investment for industrial capital.\" The reason is not merely empirical but structural. Every industrial capital, when it enters the circulation phase of its reproduction process, \"performs exactly the same functions as commodity capital and money capital, which appear as the exclusive functions of merchant's capital in its two forms.\" Commercial and money-dealing capital are therefore not additional branches standing beside production; they are the autonomisation of a phase that production itself must traverse . Here the reader must register a crucial terminological distinction that runs through the whole chapter. Industrial capital (Marx also uses productive capital ) is capital engaged in the production process — the form in which capital exploits wage labour and creates surplus value. Commercial capital (or commodity-dealing capital ) is capital confined to the sphere of circulation, buying in order to sell. Money-dealing capital is the analogous specialisation in the technical operations of money (payment, bookkeeping, monetary transactions). What the vulgar conception treats as parallel branches, Marx treats as form-determinations of the same capital at different moments of a single circuit . The chapter's formulation is precise: \"the distinctions between industrial capital as productive capital and the same capital in the sphere of circulation attain autonomy in the following way: the specific forms and functions that capital temporarily assumes in the latter case come to appear as independent forms and functions of a part of the capital that has separated off and become completely confined to this sphere.\" The transformed form of industrial capital (a phase-form) is \"poles apart\" from the material distinctions between productive capitals applied in different branches. To confuse a phase-form with a material branch is the original sin of the \"crude conception.\" 2.2 The Two Reasons for the Vulgar Confusion: Inability and Apologetics Marx then diagnoses the source of the error. Beyond the \"off-hand way in which economists always treat distinctions of form, since they are in actual fact interested only in the substantive side,\" the vulgar economist has two further motives. First, \"his inability to explain commercial profit and its characteristic features\" — that is, the genuine theoretical problem of how capital apparently reaps gain without producing value. Second, \"his apologetic endeavour to derive the forms of commodity capital and money capital, and consequently commodity-dealing and money-dealing capital, forms which arise from the specific form of the capitalist mode of production (which presupposes as its initial basis the circulation of commodities, and hence of money), as forms which necessarily arise from the production process as such.\" This is the apologetic move par excellence : to convert a historically specific form generated by capitalism into a transhistorical necessity generated by production in general. The reductio follows immediately: if commercial and money-dealing capital were distinct from cereal cultivation only in the way cereal cultivation is distinct from stock-raising and manufacture, then \"production in general and capitalist production in particular were completely the same,\" and the distribution of the social product would have to be effected \"just as eternally by merchants and bankers as the consumption of meat must be by stock-raising.\" The rhetorical point is exact: the socalled distinction of branches dissolves the distinction between modes of production entirely. 2.3 The Roscher Footnote: The Abuse of \"Mediation\" Footnote 45 is the sharpest methodological weapon in the chapter. Marx ridicules \"our wise Roscher\" for arguing that if trade is a \"mediation\" between producers and consumers, then one may equally characterise production itself as a \"mediation\" of consumption. From this verbal identity Roscher infers that commercial capital belongs to productive capital just like agricultural and industrial capital. Marx's reply is a compressed lesson in dialectical method: a \"social 'mediation' that arises from a specific social form of production — precisely because it is a mediation — has the same absolute and necessary character, the same status\" (sarcasm) as the anthropological necessity of labour in the appropriation of nature. \"The term mediation settles everything.\" The fallacy consists in equivocating between (a) the ontologically necessary mediation of consumption by production (labour is needed to appropriate nature, as every human must mediate consumption by production \"even without a Leipzig education\") and (b) the historically specific social mediation performed by merchants under particular relations of production. Marx then adds the empirical corrective: \"merchants are not mediators between producers and consumers... but rather mediate the exchange of products between these producers; they are simply intermediaries in an exchange that would still go on in thousands of cases even without them.\" This is crucial for the chapter's whole argument: commercial capital is parasitic on an exchange that could occur without it . Its necessity is not ontological but socio-historical, and indeed its dominance is a sign of underdevelopment, not of functional indispensability. 2.4 Classical Political Economy's Perplexity Marx next distinguishes himself from Smith, Ricardo, and the classical school. Their attention was fixed on \"the basic form of capital, capital as industrial capital,\" and they treated circulation capital \"only in so far as it is itself a phase in that capital's reproduction process.\" Consequently \"they were therefore perplexed by commercial capital as a special variety of its own.\" The principles of value formation and profit \"derived straight from the examination of industrial capital cannot be applied directly to commercial capital. They therefore entirely ignored the latter. They only refer to it as a kind of industrial capital.\" Ricardo, dealing with foreign trade explicitly, \"seeks to demonstrate that it creates no value (and consequently also no surplus-value).\" Marx's decisive extension: \"But what holds for foreign trade holds also for commerce within a country.\" In one sentence the analytical treatment of commerce as an external, value-neutral appendage is collapsed; domestic commerce must obey the same logic. This prepares the reader for the thesis that the value realised by the merchant is not created in circulation but redistributed from the total surplus value produced. 2.5 The Historical Turn: Trading Capital as the Oldest Independent Form of Capital The chapter now shifts from critique to history. \"Up to now we have considered merchant's capital from the standpoint of the capitalist mode of production and within its limits. And yet not only trade, but also trading capital, is older than the capitalist mode of production, and is in fact the oldest historical mode in which capital has an independent existence.\" This is a claim of epochal significance: capital — as self-expanding value, M–C–M′ — existed before capitalism, in the form of merchant's capital. Since money-dealing capital needs nothing beyond large-scale trade in general, Marx focuses on commodity-dealing capital. Because commercial capital is confined to circulation and its sole function is to mediate the exchange of commodities, \"no further conditions are needed for its existence — leaving aside undeveloped forms that arise from barter — than are necessary for the simple circulation of commodities and money. Or, one might say that precisely the latter is its condition of existence.\" The mode of production underlying the circulating products is irrelevant to their character as commodities: \"whether the primitive community, slave production, small peasant and petty-bourgeois production, or capitalist production — this in no way alters their character as commodities, and as commodities they have to go through the exchange process and the changes of form that accompany it.\" The extremes between which commercial capital mediates — the commodities — \"are given, as far as it is concerned, just as they are given for money and its movement.\" All that is required is that these extremes be present as commodities , whether the whole of production is commodity production or merely the surplus of self-sufficient producers enters the market. The degree to which production is drawn into trade is, however, not accidental; it \"depends on the mode of production, reaching a maximum with the full development of capitalist production, where the product is produced simply as a commodity and not at all as a direct means of subsistence.\" Conversely, whatever the mode of production, trade \"promotes the generation of a surplus product designed to go into exchange,\" and thus \"gives production a character oriented more and more towards exchange-value.\" Note the dialectical caution already embedded here: trade promotes but does not create the tendency to exchange-value orientation; it is a catalyst on soil prepared by other forces. 2.6 The Metamorphosis of Commodities and the Function of Commercial Capital Marx then gives the two-sided definition of the commodity metamorphosis. \"The metamorphosis of commodities, their movement, consists (1) materially, in the exchange of different commodities for one another, (2) formally, in the transformation of commodities into money, selling, and the transformation of money into commodities, buying. And the function of commercial capital is reducible to these functions, the exchange of commodities through buying and selling.\" The material side concerns use-value — what is exchanged for what. The formal side concerns value-form — commodity becomes money, money becomes commodity. Commercial capital operates purely on the formal side; it does not alter the material substratum of production. A critical clarification follows: \"it should be understood right from the start that this is not just an exchange between the immediate producers.\" In slave, serf, and tribute relationships (including under the primitive community), \"it is the slaveowner, the feudal lord or the state receiving tribute that is the owner of the product and therefore its seller. The merchant buys and sells for many people. Sales and purchases are concentrated in his hands, and in this way buying and selling cease to be linked with the direct need of the buyer (as merchant).\" This concentration is the origin of the merchant's systemic power: he does not buy to consume, but to resell, and thereby represents a whole aggregate of transactions in a single act. 2.7 M–C–M′ as the Characteristic Movement of Commercial Capital Marx now formalises the merchant's circuit. Whatever the social organisation of the spheres of production whose exchange he mediates, \"his wealth always exists as money wealth and his money always functions as capital. Its form is always M–C–M′; money, the independent form of exchange-value, is the starting-point, and the increase of exchange-value the independent purpose.\" The formula must be unpacked. M = money advanced; C = commodities purchased (bought cheap); M′ = money obtained on resale (sold dear); and M′ = M + ΔM , where ΔM (delta M) is the increment — the merchant's profit. Commodity exchange itself, separated from production and performed by non-producers, \"becomes simply a means of increasing wealth, and not just wealth, but wealth in its general social form as exchange-value.\" The driving motive is \"the transformation of M into M + ΔM\"; the mediating acts M–C and C–M′ are \"simply transitional moments.\" Marx then draws the fundamental contrast: \"This M–C–M′, as the characteristic movement of commercial capital, is distinguished from C–M–C, commodity trade between the producers themselves, with the exchange of use-values as its ultimate purpose.\" This is the classic distinction between the circuit of capital (whose terminus is more exchange-value, more money) and the circuit of simple commodity exchange (whose terminus is a use-value satisfying a need). The merchant is the social personification of the former; the producer-consumer of the latter. 2.8 The Concentration of Monetary Wealth and the Preconditions of Capitalism A historical law is then stated: \"The less developed production is, the more monetary wealth is concentrated in the hands of merchants and appears in the specific form of mercantile wealth.\" Under the capitalist mode of production, once capital \"takes command of production itself and gives it a completely altered and specific form,\" commercial capital \"appears simply as capital in a particular function.\" But \"in all earlier modes of production, commercial capital rather appears as the function of capital par excellence , and the more so, the more production is directly the production of the producer's means of subsistence.\" Hence, \"there is no problem at all in understanding why commercial capital appears as the historic form of capital long before capital has subjected production itself to its sway.\" Marx then enumerates the two ways commercial capital historically preconditions capitalism: (1) as \"precondition for the concentration of monetary wealth\"; and (2) because capitalism \"presupposes production for trade, wholesale outlet rather than supply to the individual client, so that a merchant does not buy simply to satisfy his own personal needs, but rather concentrates in his act of purchase the purchase acts of many.\" Note the logical structure: mercantile accumulation supplies the money capital capitalism requires, and mercantilist trade organisation supplies the market form — wholesale, depersonalised, oriented to resale — that capitalism presupposes. But — and this is the pivotal caveat — \"every development in commercial capital gives production a character oriented ever more to exchange-value, transforming products more and more into commodities. Even so, this development, taken by itself, is insufficient to explain the transition from one mode of production to the other, as we shall soon see in more detail.\" The seed of the chapter's central historical thesis is planted here: commercial capital is a precondition, but never a sufficient cause. 2.9 The Demotion of Commercial Capital Under Capitalism Under capitalist production, \"commercial capital is demoted from its earlier separate existence, to become a particular moment of capital investment in general, and the equalization of profits reduces its profit rate to the general average. It now functions simply as the agent of productive capital.\" This introduces the concept of the equalisation of profits : in a fully developed capitalist economy, capital migrates between spheres until profit rates equalise around a general average. Commercial capital, no longer a monopolistic mediator, is compelled to accept this average. Its \"particular social conditions\" cease to play a \"determining part\"; \"on the contrary, where commercial capital predominates, obsolete conditions obtain.\" This is one of the chapter's signature inversions: the dominance of commerce is not a sign of modernisation, but of backwardness. \"This is true even within the same country, where for example purely trading cities exhibit a far greater analogy with past conditions than do manufacturing towns.\" Footnote 46 extends the point with devastating political-historical concreteness. W. Kiesselbach ( Der Gang des Welthandels im Mittelalter , 1860) still lives \"in a mental world where commercial capital is the general form of capital. He has not the slightest suspicion of the modern meaning of capital, as little as Herr Mommsen when he speaks of 'capital' and the rule of capital in his Römische Geschichte .\" Then the English illustration: the merchant estate and trading cities \"appear to be politically reactionary and in league with the landed and financial aristocracies against industrial capital.\" Liverpool versus Manchester and Birmingham; and \"the complete domination of industrial capital has been acknowledged by English commercial capital and by the 'moneyed interest' (financial aristocracy) only since the abolition of the corn duties\" (1846). The theoretical demotion of commercial capital finds its political mirror: it took the repeal of the Corn Laws for English commercial and financial interests to concede the primacy of industrial capital. 2.10 The Law of Inverse Proportion The chapter's core historical law is now stated flatly: \"The independent and preponderant development of capital in the form of commercial capital is synonymous with the non-subjection of production to capital, i.e. with the development of capital on the basis of a social form of production that is foreign to it and independent of it. The independent development of commercial capital thus stands in inverse proportion to the general economic development of society.\" This invariant — the inverse-proportion law — becomes the analytical instrument for the rest of the chapter. Marx explains the mechanism. If independent mercantile wealth prevails, \"the circulation process has attained independence vis-à-vis its extremes, and these are the exchanging producers themselves. These extremes remain separate from the circulation process, and this process from them. Here the product becomes a commodity through trade . It is trade that shapes the products into commodities; not the produced commodities whose movement constitutes trade.\" This is a genuinely profound formulation of historical difference: in developed capitalism, products are born as commodities and trade merely circulates them; in underdeveloped contexts, trade is what makes products into commodities by dragging them into exchange. \"Capital as capital, therefore, appears first of all in the circulation process. In this circulation process, money develops into capital. It is in circulation that the product first develops as an exchange-value, as commodity and money. Capital can be formed in the circulation process, and must be formed there, before it learns to master its extremes, the various spheres of production between which circulation mediates.\" The circulation of money and commodities \"can mediate spheres of production with the most diverse organisation, which in their internal structure are still oriented principally to the production of use-values.\" The \"double situation\" of independently developed circulation is now named. On the one hand, \"circulation has still not mastered production, but is related to it simply as its given precondition.\" On the other, \"the production process has not yet absorbed circulation into it as a mere moment.\" In capitalist production, \"on the contrary, both these things are the case. The production process is completely based on circulation, and circulation is a mere moment and a transition phase of production, simply the realization of a product produced as a commodity and the replacement of its elements of production produced as commodities. The form of capital that stems directly from circulation — commercial capital — now appears simply as one of the forms of capital in its movement of reproduction.\" The reciprocal absorption of production and circulation is the fingerprint of maturity; their separation is the fingerprint of the pre-capitalist world. 2.11 The Carrying Trade: Commercial Capital in Its Purest Form The inverse-proportion law \"appears particularly clearly in the history of the carrying trade, as conducted by the Venetians, Genoans, Dutch, etc., where the major profit was made not by supplying a specific national product, but rather by mediating the exchange of products between commercially — and generally economically — undeveloped communities and by exploiting both the producing countries.\" Footnote 47 quotes Adam Smith on how the inhabitants of trading cities imported \"the improved manufactures and expensive luxuries of richer countries\" to satisfy the vanity of the feudal proprietors, exchanging their \"rude produce\" — and how, once demand became general, \"the merchants, in order to save the expense of carriage, naturally endeavoured to establish some manufactures of the same kind in their own country.\" This footnote is not decorative: it shows Smith himself documenting the process by which commercial capital, seeking lower costs, eventually spawns domestic manufactures — a concrete mechanism of the transition. In the carrying trade, \"we have commercial capital in its pure form, quite separate from the extremes, the spheres of production, between which it mediates. This is one of the main sources from which it is formed.\" But the monopoly of the carrying trade declines precisely as the exploited peoples develop economically: \"the trade itself declines with the progress of the economic development of the peoples originally exploited by it from both sides, and whose lack of development was the basis of its existence.\" Thus Venetian, Genoese, and Dutch commercial supremacy rested on the underdevelopment of their trading partners — which is the inverse-proportion law in its most brutal form: commercial wealth is parasitic on economic backwardness. \"This is simply a particular form which the subordination of commercial capital to industrial capital takes with the progressive development of capitalist production.\" The extreme case of commercial capital dominating production directly is \"the colonial trade in general (the so-called colonial system), but quite particularly... the operations of the former Dutch East India Company.\" 2.12 Profit Upon Alienation and the Value Problem Marx now faces the theoretical puzzle directly. In the merchant's circuit — \"Since the movement of commercial capital is M–C–M′\" (a phrase which, given the immediately preceding pages and the logical structure that follows, must be read as M–C–M′ and not C–M–C′, since the profit must be realised in the final act , the sale, which is only true of M–C–M′) — \"the merchant's profit is firstly made by acts simply within the process of circulation, i.e. the two acts of purchase and sale. Secondly, it is realized in the final act, the sale. It is thus 'profit upon alienation'.\" The phrase profit upon alienation (the classic eighteenth-century notion of profit as a dédoublement captured by selling above value) is the target Marx must dissolve, because it appears to violate the law of value. At first sight, \"pure and independent commercial profit seems impossible so long as products are sold at their values. 'Buy cheap and sell dear' is the law of commerce, not the exchange of equivalents.\" But Marx, characteristically, does not simply deny the merchant's gain; he re-locates it. \"The concept of value is involved here in so far as the various commodities are all values and therefore money; from the qualitative point of view, they are equally expressions of social labour. But they are not equal values. The quantitative relationship in which products exchange is at first completely accidental. They assume the commodity form in so far as they are in some way exchangeable, i.e. are expressions of some third thing.\" In early or peripheral exchange, proportions are chaotic even though all commodities are qualitatively values. \"Continued exchange, and regular reproduction for exchange, gradually abolishes this accidental character. At the outset, however, this does not occur for the producers and consumers but rather for the mediator between the two, the merchant, who compares money prices and pockets the difference. It is through his movement that the equivalence is established.\" This is a remarkable claim: the merchant's arbitrage is what first establishes market equivalence — comparison and equalisation of prices across localities. In performing this function, the merchant creates the conditions for the very law of value that renders his own \"profit upon alienation\" a mere redistribution of surplus value rather than a violation of equivalence. Hence: \"Commercial capital, in the first instance, is simply the mediating movement between extremes it does not dominate and preconditions it does not create.\" Everything the merchant achieves, he achieves on given foundations . 2.13 The Hoard Analogy, Epicurus's Gods, and the Jewish Analogy Marx brings the argument to a broader philosophical register. \"Just as money arises from the simple form of commodity circulation, C–M–C, and not only as a measure of value and means of circulation, but also as an absolute form of the commodity and therefore of wealth, as a hoard, and makes its conservation and accumulation into an end in itself, so also, from the mere circulation form of commodity capital, M–C–M′, do money and the hoard develop into something that is maintained and increased simply by alienation.\" The structural analogy is exact: simple exchange (C–M–C) generates money as hoard, i.e. as wealth withdrawn from circulation ; the merchant's circuit (M–C–M′) generates money as self-expanding value , wealth that must re-enter circulation to grow. Hoarding and merchant capital are cousins: both treat accumulation as an end in itself, but the merchant's accumulation is mediated through exchange rather than mere withdrawal. The image of the \"trading peoples of old\" follows: they \"existed like the gods of Epicurus in the intermundia , or like the Jews in the pores of Polish society.\" The footnote explains: \"According to the Greek philosopher Epicurus (c. 341–c. 270 B.C.), the gods existed only in the intermundia , or spaces between different worlds, and had no influence on the course of human affairs.\" Marx adds that he had studied Epicurus for his doctoral dissertation and made the identical double analogy in Volume I. The metaphor is not ornamental but analytic: the trading peoples occupied the spaces between the productive communities, contributing nothing to their internal productive organisation and drawing their wealth precisely from their non-belonging. \"The trade of the first independent and highly developed trading cities and peoples, as a pure carrying trade, rested on the barbarism of the producing peoples between whom they acted as intermediaries.\" The \"pores\" image is equally precise: merchants lived inside the interstices of an otherwise self-sufficient economy, extracting wealth from the gaps. 2.14 Trade Prevails Over Industry in Prehistory, Industry Over Trade in Modernity Marx formulates the epochal reversal: \"In the stages that preceded capitalist society, it was trade that prevailed over industry; in modern society it is the reverse.\" Note that trade is not thereby inert: \"Trade naturally reacts back to a greater or lesser extent on the communities between which it is pursued; it subjects production more and more to exchange-value, by making consumption and existence more dependent on sale than on the direct use of the product. In this way it dissolves the old relationships. It increases monetary circulation. It no longer just takes hold of surplus production, but gradually gobbles up production itself and makes entire branches of production dependent on it.\" But the crucial qualifier follows: \"This solvent effect, however, depends very much on the nature of the community of producers.\" 2.15 Commercial Capital as a System of Plunder Marx sharpens the point about commercial profit in underdeveloped contexts. \"When commercial capital exchanges the products of undeveloped communities, commercial profit not only appears as defrauding and cheating but to a large extent does derive precisely from this.\" Two mechanisms are distinguished. First, the exploitation of \"the difference between production prices in various countries (and in this connection it acts to equalize and establish commodity values)\" — the arbitrage function already described. Second, and more fundamental, those modes of production \"enable commercial capital to appropriate for itself a preponderant part of the surplus product: partly by acting as middleman between communities whose production is still basically oriented to use-value, so that the sale of that part of their product that in some way or other steps into circulation, and thus the sale of products at their value in general, is of subordinate importance for their economic organisation; and partly because in those earlier modes of production the principal proprietors of the surplus product whom the merchant trades with, i.e. the slaveowner, the feudal lord and the state (e.g. the oriental despot), represent the consumption wealth which the merchant sets out to trap, as Adam Smith correctly perceived in the passage quoted with regard to the feudal period.\" Since the producers are oriented to use-value, they are indifferent to whether they sell at value; the merchant can therefore systematically underpay them and overcharge the luxury-consuming aristocracy. The verdict is uncompromising: \"Commercial capital, when it holds a dominant position, is thus in all cases a system of plunder, just as its development in the trading peoples of both ancient and modern times is directly bound up with violent plunder, piracy, the taking of slaves and subjugation of colonies; as in Carthage and Rome, and later with the Venetians, Portuguese, Dutch, etc.\" Footnote 48 — a lengthy extract from Martin Luther's Von Kaufshandlung und Wucher (1524/1527) — supplies the moral-theological register: the merchants' complaint against the nobles who rob them; Luther's retort that merchants \"daily rob the whole world, while a knight may rob one or two people once or twice a year\"; the Cato tag \"mean thieves lie in dungeons and in the stocks, while public thieves go about in gold and silk\"; and the threat that God \"will amalgamate princes and merchants, one thief with another, like lead and iron.\" 2.16 The Outcome of Dissolution Depends on the Old Mode of Production Marx now crystallises the historical-materialist core of the chapter. \"The development of trade and commercial capital always gives production a growing orientation towards exchange-value, expands its scope, diversifies it and renders it cosmopolitan, developing money into world money. Trade always has, to a greater or lesser degree, a solvent effect on the pre-existing organisations of production, which in all their various forms are principally oriented to use-value. But how far it leads to the dissolution of the old mode of production depends first and foremost on the solidity and inner articulation of this mode of production itself. And what comes out of this process of dissolution, i.e. what new mode of production arises in place of the old, does not depend on trade, but rather on the character of the old mode of production itself.\" This is the thesis of structural determination by the internal constitution of the mode of production . Trade is a force, but its effects are conditional on what it meets. \"In the ancient world, the influence of trade and the development of commercial capital always produced the result of a slave economy; or, given a different point of departure, it also meant the transformation of a patriarchal slave system oriented towards the production of the direct means of subsistence into one oriented towards the production of surplus-value. In the modern world, on the other hand, its outcome is the capitalist mode of production. It follows that this result is itself conditioned by quite other circumstances than the development of commercial capital.\" Marx then offers empirical illustrations designed to expose the non-correlation between trade and productive development. Where urban industry separates off from agriculture, its products \"are commodities from the start, so that their sale requires the mediation of trade. The dependence of trade on urban development is to this extent self-evident, as is the conditioning of the latter by trade. However, the degree to which industrial development goes hand in hand with these processes is dependent on entirely different circumstances.\" Then the three great counterexamples: Ancient Rome , in the late republican era, \"saw the development of commercial capital to a higher level than ever before in the ancient world, without any kind of progress in the development of crafts\"; Corinth and other Greek cities of Europe and Asia Minor, where \"a high level of craft development accompanied the development of trade\"; and nomadic peoples , in whom \"the commercial spirit and the development of commercial capital are often characteristic of non-settled, nomadic peoples,\" in \"diametrical opposition to urban development and its conditions.\" These counterexamples discredit any simple causal model in which trade automatically produces industrial development. 2.17 The Trade Revolutions of the Sixteenth and Seventeenth Centuries Marx concedes — and then carefully circumscribes — the role of the great trade revolutions. \"There can be no doubt — and this very fact has led to false conceptions — that the great revolutions that took place in trade in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, along with the geographical discoveries of that epoch, and which rapidly advanced the development of commercial capital, were a major moment in promoting the transition from the feudal to the capitalist mode of production.\" The list of factors is striking: \"The sudden expansion of the world market, the multiplication of commodities in circulation, the competition among the European nations for the seizure of Asiatic products and American treasures, the colonial system, all made a fundamental contribution towards shattering the feudal barriers to production.\" Yet: \"the modern mode of production in its first period, that of manufacture, developed only where the conditions for it had been created in the Middle Ages. Compare Holland with Portugal, for example.\" The remedy against over-estimating early trade is comparative: \"the best antidote to this view is to consider and contrast English exports and imports today with those of the beginning of the eighteenth century\" — itself incomparably greater than those of any earlier trading people (Adam Anderson, History of Commerce ). The reversal is then stated: \"whereas in the sixteenth century, and partly still in the seventeenth, the sudden expansion of trade and the creation of a new world market had an overwhelming influence on the defeat of the old mode of production and the rise of the capitalist mode, this happened in reverse on the basis of the capitalist mode of production, once it had been created. The world market itself forms the basis for this mode of production. On the other hand, the immanent need that this has to produce on an ever greater scale drives it to the constant expansion of the world market, so that now it is not trade that revolutionizes industry, but rather industry that constantly revolutionizes trade.\" This is one of Marx's most famous formulations of the primacy of production in the modern epoch: the causal arrow is reversed once capitalism is established. \"Moreover, commercial supremacy is now linked with the greater or lesser prevalence of the conditions for large-scale industry. Compare England and Holland, for example. The history of Holland's decline as the dominant trading nation is the history of the subordination of commercial capital to industrial capital.\" 2.18 The Obstacles Presented by the \"Solidity and Inner Articulation\" of Pre-Capitalist Production: India, China, Russia Marx then illustrates the \"solidity and inner articulation\" thesis empirically with the classic case of English commerce with India and China. \"There the broad basis of the mode of production is formed by the union between small-scale agriculture and domestic industry, on top of which we have in the Indian case the form of village communities based on common property in the soil, which was also the original form in China.\" The resistance of this production structure to commercial dissolution is powerful because the direct connection of agriculture and manufacture embodies a \"great economy and saving of time.\" In India, \"the English applied their direct political and economic power, as masters and landlords, to destroying these small economic communities.\" Footnote 50 records the outcome: \"the history of English economic management in India is a history of futile and actually stupid (in practice, infamous) economic experiments. In Bengal they created a caricature of English large-scale landed property; in the south-east they created a caricature of peasant smallholdings. In the north-west they did all they could to transform the Indian economic community with common property in the soil into a caricature of itself.\" \"In so far as English trade has had a revolutionary effect on the mode of production in India, this is simply to the extent that it has destroyed spinning and weaving, which form an age-old and integral part of this unity of industrial and agricultural production, through the low price of English commodities. In this way it has torn the community to pieces. Even here, their work of dissolution is succeeding only very gradually.\" China shows the same resistance more strongly, \"where no assistance is provided by direct political force. The great economy and saving of time that results from the direct connection of agriculture and manufacture presents a very stubborn resistance here to the products of large-scale industry, whose prices include the faux frais [overhead expenses] of the circulation process with which they are everywhere perforated.\" The term faux frais — additional costs of circulation, transport, distribution — is decisive: the very overhead of the developed capitalist world market becomes an obstacle to penetrating a self-sufficient economy. \"In contrast to English trade, Russian trade leaves the economic basis of Asiatic production quite untouched.\" Footnote 51 (by Engels, marked F.E.) adds the editorial observation that since Russia has been \"making the most frantic attempts to develop a capitalist production of its own,\" this is beginning to change. 2.19 The Transition from Feudalism: The Two (Then Three) Paths The last major analytical beat develops the theory of transition . \"The transition from the feudal mode of production takes place in two different ways.\" First: \"The producer may become a merchant and capitalist, in contrast to the agricultural natural economy and the guild-bound handicraft of medieval urban industry. This is the really revolutionary way.\" Second: \"the merchant may take direct control of production himself. But however frequently this occurs as a historical transition — for example the English clothier of the seventeenth century, who brought weavers who were formerly independent under his control, selling them their wool and buying up their cloth — it cannot bring about the overthrow of the old mode of production by itself, but rather preserves and retains it as its own precondition.\" This distinction — producer-becomes-merchant (revolutionary) versus merchant-takes-control-of-production (conservative) — is one of Marx's most consequential historical claims. In the second case, \"the manufacturer in the French silk industry, and the English hosiery and lace industries too, was a manufacturer only in name. In reality he was simply a merchant, who kept the weavers working in their old fragmented manner and exercised only control as a merchant; it was a merchant they were really working for.\" Footnote 52 (F.E.) adds the Rhineland ribbon and braid makers, the Krefeld silk-weavers, and the special railway built for commerce between rural hand-weavers and the urban \"manufacturer\" — all made redundant by mechanisation. \"This method always stands in the way of the genuine capitalist mode of production and disappears with its development. Without revolutionizing the mode of production, it simply worsens the conditions of the direct producers, transforms them into mere wage-labourers and proletarians under worse conditions than those directly subsumed by capital, appropriating their surplus labour on the basis of the old mode of production.\" The phrase \"worse conditions than those directly subsumed by capital\" is analytically crucial: the merchant-capitalist form produces a worse proletarianisation than the canonical, factory-based form, because it combines wage-dependence with the preservation of obsolete production methods and the attendant fragmentation and irregularity. The London furniture trade, particularly in Tower Hamlets, is the modern illustration. \"The whole of furniture production is divided into very many separate branches. One firm just makes chairs, another tables, a third chests and so on. But these firms themselves are conducted more or less on a handicraft basis, by one master with a few journeymen. Despite this, production is on too large a scale to work directly for private clients. The buyers are the proprietors of furniture stores. On Saturday the master goes to these stores and sells his products, with as much haggling over the price as there is in the pawnshop over an advance on some item or other. These masters need their weekly sale simply to buy more raw material for the coming week and to pay wages. Under these conditions they are really only middlemen between the merchant and their own workers. The merchant is the real capitalist and pockets the greater part of the surplus-value.\" Footnote 53 (F.E.) records that since 1865 this system has been put on a much wider basis, and cites the First Report of the Select Committee of the House of Lords on the Sweating System (1888) — a real-world reference to the \"sweated trades.\" The pawnshop simile is exact: the master is a constant debtor, dependent on the merchant-lender. Marx then generalises the transitional forms. The transition to manufacture from handicrafts and rural sidelines; the transition to large-scale industry depends on \"the technical development of the small owner-operated establishment, whether it already employs machines that admit of a handicraft-like operation. Instead of by hand, the machine is now driven by steam, as has been happening recently in the English hosiery trade.\" The three paths are then enumerated: \"First, the merchant becomes an industrialist directly; this is the case with crafts that are founded on trade, such as those in the luxury industries, where the merchants import both raw materials and workers from abroad, as they were imported into Italy from Constantinople in the fifteenth century. Second, the merchant makes the small masters into his middlemen, or even buys directly from the independent producer; he leaves him nominally independent and leaves his mode of production unchanged. Third, the industrialist becomes a merchant and produces directly on a large scale for the market.\" An editorial asterisk (F.E.) notes that if the \"two forms\" referred to earlier have now become three, this is because Marx has added, as the first case, \"the rather exceptional one in which the mode of production is transformed to a genuinely capitalist one at the merchant's initiative.\" 2.20 The Middle Ages and the Producer-Become-Merchant: The Culmination \"In the Middle Ages, the merchant was simply someone who 'transferred' commodities, as Poppe correctly put it, whether these were produced by guilds or by peasants.\" Poppe's Geschichte der Technologie (1807) is cited for this minimalist characterisation. The merchant becomes an industrialist or employs craftsmen (especially small rural producers); alternatively, the producer becomes the merchant. \"Whereas before the master-weaver gradually received his wool from the merchant in small portions and worked along with his journeymen for the merchant, now the weaver buys wool or yarn himself, and sells the merchant his cloth. The elements of production go into the production process as commodities that he has himself bought. And instead of producing for the individual merchant or for particular customers, the weaver now produces for the entire world of commerce. The producer is his own merchant.\" The result: \"Commercial capital now simply performs the circulation process.\" This is the moment at which the merchant ceases to govern and becomes an agent — the very demotion theorised earlier is now shown concretely taking shape at the level of production relations. The role of trade in this transformation is then fully theorised. \"Trade is the precondition for the transformation of guild and rural domestic crafts into capitalist businesses, not to mention feudal agriculture. It develops the product into a commodity, partly by creating a market for it, partly by supplying new commodity equivalents and new raw and ancillary materials for production, and thereby opening new branches of production that are based on trade from the very beginning — both on production for the market and world market, and on conditions of production that derive from the world market.\" But note the direction: trade as precondition , not cause . \"As soon as manufacture becomes somewhat stronger, and still more so large-scale industry, it creates a market for itself and uses its commodities to conquer it. Trade now becomes the servant of industrial production, for which the constant expansion of the market is a condition of existence. An ever-increasing mass production swamps the existing market and thus works steadily towards its expansion, breaking through its barriers.\" The dialetic of production and market is then captured with absolute precision: \"What restricts this mass production is not trade (in as much as this only expresses existing demand), but rather the scale of the capital functioning and the productivity of labour so far developed.\" Trade expresses demand; capital and productivity generate the technical capacity whose actualisation drives the expansion of markets. The chapter's penultimate paragraph then names the fundamental shift in the industrialist's consciousness. \"The industrial capitalist is constantly faced with the world market; he compares and must compare his own cost prices not only with domestic market prices, but with those of the whole world. Previously, this comparison was almost exclusively the task of merchants and ensured commercial capital its mastery over industrial.\" The ability to compare world prices — epitomised in the merchant's arbitrage — was the material basis of merchant mastery. When the industrial capitalist internalises this world-market orientation, he displaces the merchant, and commercial capital's domination is definitively over. 2.21 Mercantilism and the Birth of Genuine Economic Science The chapter closes with a genealogical note on economic theory. \"The first theoretical treatment of the modern mode of production — mercantilism — necessarily proceeded from the superficial phenomena of the circulation process, as these acquire autonomy in the movement of commercial capital. Hence it only grasped the semblance of things.\" Two reasons: \"partly because commercial capital is the first independent mode of existence of capital in general. And partly on account of the overwhelming influence that commercial capital exercised in the period when feudal production was first overthrown, the period of the rise of modern production.\" The conclusion is programmatic: \"The genuine science of modern economics begins only when theoretical discussion moves from the circulation process to the production process.\" Finally, a forward glance: \"Interest-bearing capital, too, is an age-old form of capital. But we shall see later why mercantilism did not take this as its basis, but rather engaged in polemics with it.\" The chapter thus closes by linking the historical narrative back to the theory of theoretical development itself — writing a materialist history of political economy's own errors and trajectory. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental principle that emerges from Chapter 20 is the primacy of the mode of production over the sphere of circulation — a claim that is often repeated but rarely demonstrated with the historical density Marx brings here. Trade is not a deus ex machina that rearranges society; it is a force whose solvent effect and whose outcome are entirely conditioned by the \"solidity and inner articulation\" of the production relations it encounters. The same commercial pressure that dissolves ancient communities and produces a slave economy in the ancient Mediterranean, and that dissolves feudalism and produces capitalism in early modern Europe, is structurally identical. What differs is what it meets. This is historical materialism at its most disciplined: difference of outcome is explained not by difference in the exogenous force (trade), but by difference in the internal structure of the mode of production. From this flows the chapter's second great lesson, the inverse-proportion law : the independent development of commercial capital stands in inverse proportion to the general economic development of society. This is more than a historical curiosity; it is a diagnostic tool. Where merchants dominate, one should look for backwardness, not modernity. Where trading cities rule, one should look for pre-capitalist social relations. Where capital is parasitic on the underdevelopment of its partners — the carrying trade of Venice, Genoa, Holland — one should expect that capital's power to wither precisely as those partners develop. The law also explains the political posture of commercial and financial capital in the nineteenth century — reactionary, allied with landlords against manufacturers, as in Liverpool versus Manchester, until the repeal of the Corn Laws forced recognition of industrial capital's primacy. The third lesson is methodological, expressed most sharply in the Roscher footnote. A social mediation arising from a historically specific form of production must never be confused with an ontological necessity — the mediation of human existence by labour. The word \"mediation\" settles nothing. This is Marx's general prophylaxis against apologetic naturalisation : the conversion of historical, and therefore transitory, forms into eternal ones. The vulgar economist commits this fallacy for two reasons — inability to explain the form of commercial profit, and a desire to derive historically specific forms from production in general — and this pair of motives remains a standing temptation in every apologetic political economy. The chapter thus functions as a permanent warning to the reader: watch for arguments that derive a specific social form from a general functional necessity. The fourth lesson concerns the dialectic of the producer-become-merchant versus the merchant-become-industrialist . Only the first is \"really revolutionary\"; the second preserves the old mode of production and merely intensifies exploitation on its basis, producing a proletarian existence \"under worse conditions than those directly subsumed by capital.\" This insight has immense downstream significance for understanding the \"sweating system,\" the putting-out system, and the persistence of archaic labour forms inside modern capital accumulation. It also implies that the path to capitalism shapes the kind of capitalism that emerges — an implicit argument against any unilinear scheme of transition. Broader theoretical implications extend in several directions. First, the chapter provides the historical scaffolding for later Parts: for the analysis of the special form in which money is accumulated by commercial and money-dealing capital; for the theory of interest-bearing capital, \"an age-old form\" whose relation to mercantilism Marx promises to clarify; and for the treatment of the credit system. Second, it establishes the necessity of a materialist history of theory — mercantilism itself is explained not by the personal errors of mercantilist authors but by the fact that commercial capital was the first independent form of capital, so that the \"semblance of things\" was historically real. The genuine science of economics begins only when theory moves from circulation to production. Third, and at the broadest register, the chapter makes clear that capitalism is not a natural outgrowth of trade but a specific historical product , conditioned by the internal articulation of the modes of production it supersedes, and that capital itself — as M–C–M′ — existed for millennia in the form of merchant's capital before it learned to reorganise production in its own image. Practically and philosophically, the chapter invites us to read global economic history with an appropriate suspicion: the glories of the great trading cities — Carthage, Venice, Genoa, Amsterdam, Liverpool — are symptoms of their external character in relation to the productive communities that supplied them their wealth. Commercial dominance is a sign of incompleteness, not of achievement. And the resilience of self-sufficient communities — the Indian village, the Chinese small-farmer-weaver, the Russian mir — to the solvent power of trade is not mere inertia: it reflects the enormous efficiency of integrated agriculture and manufacture and the faux frais that rack up the prices of the large-scale products brought from outside. The conceptual opposition between solvency and determination , between the force of trade and the form of production it works upon, remains one of the most powerful analytic instruments ever forged for the study of uneven and combined development, and it is on the historical material assembled in this chapter that Marx rests the entire weight of that instrument. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 19 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 21"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 21", "title": "Chapter 21", "subtitle": "Interest-Bearing Capital", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 21/Chapter 21 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 12102, "read_time": "61 min read", "tags": ["Interest-Bearing Capital & Loan Capital", "Fetishism & Mystification of Capital"], "keywords": ["Interest-Bearing Capital", "Loan Capital and Money as a Commodity", "Alienated Form of Capital: M-M' (Money Begets Money)", "Surplus-Value Split into Interest and Enterprise Profit", "Commodity Sui Generis: Use-Value as Profit Generator"], "text": "Chapter 21 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 12,063 words ⏱️ ~60 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 21: Interest-Bearing Capital — A Complete Exhaustive Analysis 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 21 of Capital , Volume 3 opens Part Five, the section of Marx's great unfinished work dedicated to \"The Division of Profit into Interest and Profit of Enterprise.\" This is a pivotal structural hinge in the whole architecture of the three volumes. The chapter's central problem is deceptively simple: how does money — a mere sum of value — acquire a price? Or, more precisely, once we admit that the average rate of profit exists and is uniform across industrial and commercial capital, how does a \"price of capital\" become possible at all, and what is the true nature of interest? The chapter's core thesis can be stated in a compact phrase: interest-bearing capital is capital that has become a commodity, and interest is the price paid for the peculiar use-value of that commodity — the capacity to produce the average profit. But this compact thesis immediately generates a cascade of paradoxes that Marx is at pains to unpack. If interest is the \"price\" of capital, then capital appears to have a double value — its own value plus a qualitatively different price — which contradicts the very concept of price as the money-expression of value. Marx calls interest \"a completely irrational expression right from the start.\" The chapter is therefore at once an economic investigation into the origin of interest and a logical/conceptual critique of the way classical political economy (and the socialist critique of Proudhon) handled the phenomenon. The intellectual objective is threefold. First, Marx must rigorously distinguish the form of the movement of interest-bearing capital — the circuit M—M—C—M'—M' — from the circuit of industrial capital ( M—C—M' ) and from the circuit of commercial capital. The interest-bearing circuit's hallmark is that the mediating process (production and circulation) is obliterated , leaving only \"money that begets money.\" Second, Marx must demonstrate that the phenomenon of interest is not a deviation from the theory of value but a specific form of distribution of surplus-value already produced: the total profit is split into interest (the part falling to the money capitalist, the owner) and profit of enterprise (the part remaining to the functioning capitalist, the borrower). Third, the chapter must prepare the ground for the subsequent analysis in Chapters 22–24 of profit of enterprise, the money market, and credit, by establishing that there is no \"natural\" rate of interest — that the rate of interest is determined by competition in a way that has no internal \"law\" comparable to the law governing prices of production or wages. The chapter thus sits at the precise intersection of value theory, distribution theory, and the theory of the credit system. In the earlier parts of Volume 3, Marx established that value is produced in production by the exploitation of labour-power, and that surplus-value is distributed among capitals via the formation of prices of production and the equalization of profit rates. In Part Four he had incorporated commercial capital into that equalization: commercial capital, even though it does not produce surplus-value, participates in the average rate of profit and yields commercial profit. Now, in Part Five, he introduces a third category of claim on surplus-value: the claim of the owner of capital as such , as distinct from the functioning capitalist who employs it. Interest is the revenue of mere ownership; profit of enterprise is the revenue of functioning. This distinction, once established, will ultimately allow Marx to treat the financial system (credit, banks, joint-stock companies) as the highest and most \"fetishistic\" form of the capitalist mode of production — the form in which the social character of capital appears as the property of a thing (money breeding money). The method is characteristic of the mature Marx of Volume 3: he begins with a simple, almost pedagogical numerical illustration (the £100 yielding £20, then £5 paid as interest), uses it to isolate the essential relations, and then rigorously distinguishes the real movement of capital from the legal form in which that movement is enveloped. Throughout, he is engaged in a running polemic against Proudhon (and against the \"natural justice\" of Gilbart), whose confusion arises precisely from trying to apply the categories of simple commodity exchange — buying, selling, price — directly to capital. The chapter's analytical climax is the demonstration that the \"price of capital\" (interest) is a sui generis category, an irrational form that nonetheless expresses a real relation: the valorization of money capital, measurable only by comparing interest to the value advanced. Finally, the chapter is crucial for understanding Marx's own intellectual honesty about the limits of theory. Having shown that commodity prices are governed by prices of production and that wages are governed by the value of labour-power, Marx admits that the rate of interest has no analogous \"natural\" law. Competition does not merely cause fluctuations around a law; here competition is the only determination. This is a remarkable concession about the analytical authority of competition in a specific domain, and it positions the interest rate as the paradigmatic case of a \"lawless and arbitrary\" market phenomenon — a theme that will recur in the analysis of fictitious capital and financial crisis. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Completed General Rate of Profit and the Transition to Interest The chapter opens with a retrospective clarification. Marx reminds us that in Part Two, when he first derived the general or average rate of profit, that rate was not yet in its \"finished form,\" because the equalization producing it appeared only as an equalization of industrial capitals across different spheres. It was in Part Four, with the introduction of commercial capital and commercial profit, that the average rate acquired its \"more closely defined limits.\" From this point forward, Marx stipulates, when he speaks of \"the general rate of profit or the average profit,\" he means this latter, finished sense — a rate that applies indifferently to industrial and commercial capital alike. This is not a trivial bookkeeping point. It is the precondition for the whole chapter, because it establishes that any capital of a given size yields the same annual average profit , whether it is invested in production or in circulation. The consequence is that \"it is also no longer necessary to make a distinction between industrial and commercial profit, once it is a question of this average rate of profit.\" This equalization is crucial for interest theory because it means the money capitalist does not need to know or care how the borrower will use the money (industrially or commercially). The loan is advanced as capital in general , and the return is reckoned on the basis of the average profit. The \"average level of intelligence and activity appropriate to the intended purpose\" is implicitly assumed — Marx is bracketing differential entrepreneurial skill, which will matter later in the analysis of profit of enterprise but not here. Money as Potential Capital: The Emergence of a Special Commodity The substantive argument begins with one of the most consequential moves in all of Capital : the claim that, on the basis of capitalist production, money can be transformed into capital, and through this transformation it becomes a self-valorizing value capable of increasing itself. Money produces profit; it enables the capitalist to extract and appropriate a certain quantity of unpaid labour, surplus product, and surplus-value. In this way money receives, besides the use-value it possesses as money, an additional use-value — namely, \"the ability to function as capital.\" Its use-value here consists precisely in the profit it produces when transformed into capital. And it is in this capacity of potential capital , as a means to the production of profit, that it becomes a commodity , \"but a commodity of a special kind.\" Or, as Marx immediately reformulates it: \"capital becomes a commodity.\" This is the conceptual keystone of the chapter. Let us unpack its components: Money as money has a use-value (it can buy commodities) and a value (it is the universal equivalent). Money as capital has an additional use-value: the capacity to produce surplus-value when advanced. This use-value is not intrinsic to money as a physical thing; it is a social property, arising from the relations of production (specifically, from the existence of wage-labour and the exploitation of labour-power). Because this additional use-value can be alienated (temporarily transferred) to another person, money-as-capital takes on the form of a commodity. It is offered on a market (the money market), and it has, so to speak, a \"price\" — interest. The genius of this formulation is that it simultaneously explains why interest exists (there is a use-value to be paid for) and why interest is conceptually anomalous (the \"commodity\" being sold is not a thing but a social relation; the \"price\" is not the money-expression of value but a share in a surplus). The Numerical Illustration: £100, 20%, and the £5 Interest Marx then makes the relation concrete. Take the average annual rate of profit as 20 per cent . Under average conditions, a machine worth £100 applied as capital yields a profit of £20 . Therefore, a man who has £100 at his disposal \"holds in his hands the power of making this £100 into £120, and thus producing a profit of £20.\" His £100 is potential capital . If he transfers it for a year to someone else who actually uses it as capital, he gives that person the power to produce £20 profit — \"a surplus-value that costs him nothing and for which he does not pay any equivalent.\" If the borrower pays the owner £5 at year's end, that £5 is interest : \"nothing but a particular name, a special title, for a part of the profit which the actually functioning capitalist has to pay to the capital's proprietor, instead of pocketing it himself.\" Three things are worth flagging here. First, the £20 is the total profit produced by the functioning capital; the £5 interest is a subdivision of that £20. Interest is not an addition to profit; it is a deduction from it. This is a theme Marx will hammer repeatedly against Proudhon. Second, the magnitude of interest (£5) is here stipulated arbitrarily; the chapter will later explain that its determination is a matter of competition, not of any natural law. Third, the illustration already contains the essential distinction between the money capitalist (owner) and the functioning capitalist (borrower) — a distinction between two functions that may be performed by the same person (the owner who also uses his own capital) but which are conceptually distinct. The Critique of Gilbart: Justice, Legal Forms, and the Content of Relations of Production Marx then pauses for a polemical aside on the idea of \"natural justice.\" James Gilbart had claimed that \"a man who borrows money with a view of making a profit by it should give some portion of this profit to the lender\" is \"a self-evident principle of natural justice.\" Marx's response is a compact statement of his materialist theory of justice: \"The justice of transactions between agents of production consists in the fact that these transactions arise from the relations of production as their natural consequence. The legal forms in which these economic transactions appear as voluntary actions of the participants, as the expressions of their common will and as contracts that can be enforced on the parties concerned by the power of the state, are mere forms that cannot themselves determine this content. They simply express it. The content is just so long as it corresponds to the mode of production and is adequate to it. It is unjust as soon as it contradicts it.\" This passage has enormous implications. Marx is not denying that justice-talk has meaning; he is relocating it. Justice is not a transcendent standard imposed on economic relations; it is a judgment about the adequacy of legal forms to the underlying mode of production. What is \"just\" is what corresponds to and facilitates the prevailing relations of production. Thus \"slavery, on the basis of the capitalist mode of production, is unjust; so is cheating on the quality of commodities.\" Note carefully: Marx does not say slavery is unjust absolutely; he says it is unjust on the basis of the capitalist mode of production — because it contradicts the specifically capitalist form of exploitation (free wage-labour). The aside serves the chapter's argument by dismissing moralistic objections to interest (Gilbart's \"natural justice\" defending it, Proudhon's \"natural justice\" condemning it) as irrelevant to the scientific question of what interest is . The Sine Qua Non of the Capital Function: A Must Will It, and A Originally Spends It Marx now returns to the conditions under which money can function as capital. The £100 produces £20 by functioning as capital, whether industrial or commercial. But the sine qua non of this function is that the money is \"actually spent as capital\" — laid out on the purchase of means of production (industrial) or commodities (commercial). And if it is to be spent, it must first be available . Here is the crucial dialectical point: if A, the proprietor of the £100, either spent it on private consumption or hoarded it, then B, the functioning capitalist, could not spend it as capital. B does not spend his own capital, but A's; yet he cannot spend A's capital unless A wills it. Therefore: \"In point of fact, therefore, it is A who originally spends the £100 as capital, even though his function as a capitalist is entirely restricted to this act of expenditure. As far as the £100 is concerned, B functions as a capitalist only because A turns the £100 over to him and hence spends it as capital.\" This is a subtle and important claim about the ownership dimension of capital. The mere act of lending — of transferring the money to B — is itself the act by which A \"spends\" the money as capital (i.e., commits it to the capital circuit). A's \"function\" is reduced to this single act, but it is a decisive one. This prepares the later distinction between ownership and function and anticipates the analysis of the \"rentier\" as a pure owner who performs no productive or commercial function whatsoever. The Characteristic Circulation of Interest-Bearing Capital Marx announces two tasks: to examine the characteristic circulation of interest-bearing capital, and then \"the specific way it is sold as a commodity, i.e. lent instead of being relinquished once and for all.\" He begins with the circuit. The starting-point is money that A advances to B. (This can occur with or without security; the secured form is historically older, except for advances on commodities or papers such as bills and stocks — but these particular forms \"do not concern us here.\" Marx is deliberately isolating \"interest-bearing capital in its ordinary form.\") In B's hands, the money really is transformed into capital, going through the circuit M—C—M' and then returning to A as M' , as M + ΔM , where ΔM represents the interest. To simplify, Marx sets aside the case where the capital remains in B's hands for a protracted period and interest is paid at regular intervals. The total movement is thus: M — M — C — M' — M' Two things \"appear in duplicate\": (1) the expenditure of the money as capital, and (2) its reflux as realized capital, as M' or M + ΔM. This doubled structure is the formal signature of interest-bearing capital, and Marx contrasts it carefully with the other circuits. In the movement of commercial capital, M—C—M' , the same commodity changes hands twice (or more); each change of place displays a metamorphosis, a purchase or sale. In simple commodity circulation, C—M—C , we have a double change of place by the same money, but it displays the complete metamorphosis of the commodity — first transformed into money, then out of money into another commodity. With interest-bearing capital, by contrast: \"M's first change of place is neither a moment of commodity metamorphosis nor of the reproduction of capital. This begins only the second time it is spent, in the hands of the functioning capitalist... M's first change of place here expresses nothing more than its transfer or making over from A to B; a transfer which customarily takes place under certain legal forms and provisions.\" So the first M—M is not an economic act of exchange at all; it is a legal transfer . There is no purchase, no sale, no metamorphosis of value. Only the second expenditure (B's) is a genuine capital-advance. The doubled expenditure (A's transfer, then B's investment) is matched by a doubled reflux : the capital returns first to B as realized capital (M + ΔM), then B transfers it back to A, with a part of the profit as interest. The reflux to A is \"not the whole profit, but simply the part of the profit that is interest.\" Crucially, Marx emphasizes why the reflux must be total : \"For its reflux movement to be complete, B has to transfer it again to A.\" A has not ceased to be the legal owner. And B must surrender not only the capital sum but also a part of the profit, \"since A has given the money to him only as capital, i.e. as value that is not just maintained in the course of its movement, but creates a surplus-value for its owner.\" It remains in B's hands only as long as it functions as capital; when its function ceases (upon reflux), \"it must be transferred back again to A, who has not ceased to be its legal owner.\" Note the careful language: the capital remains A's property throughout, while B has only possession and use for a definite interval. This separation of ownership from function is the economic content of the legal form of the loan. The \"form of lending\" — as distinct from the form of sale — is thus not an arbitrary legal convention; it \"results from capital's characteristic here of emerging as a commodity, or, in other words... from the fact that money as capital becomes a commodity.\" Why Commodity Capital and Money Capital Are Not Yet Capital-as-Commodity Marx now undertakes an extended clarification, recalling Volume 2, Chapter 1, where he had shown that capital functions in the circulation process as commodity capital and money capital . In neither of these forms does capital as capital become a commodity. This is a vital distinction, and the chapter's argument depends on it. When productive capital has been transformed into commodity capital, it must be put on the market and sold as a commodity. In the act of sale, it functions simply as a commodity . The capitalist appears simply as seller, the buyer simply as buyer. The product must realize its value in circulation and assume its money form. It is \"quite immaterial\" whether the buyer is a consumer (buying means of subsistence) or a capitalist (buying means of production, i.e., a component of capital). In circulation, commodity capital \"functions simply as a commodity, not as capital.\" Marx then specifies two respects in which commodity capital is nevertheless distinct from a simple commodity: It is \"already pregnant with surplus-value,\" so that the realization of its value is simultaneously the realization of surplus-value — \"though this does not alter its simple existence as a commodity, as a product with a definite price.\" Its functioning as a commodity is a moment of its reproduction process as capital ; hence its movement as a commodity (a partial movement within the total process) is also its movement as capital. But — and this is the key — \"it does not become so by the mere act of selling, but only because this act is connected with the total movement of this particular sum of value as capital.\" Similarly, money capital actually operates simply as money — as means of purchase for the elements of production. If this money is also money capital, a form of capital, this is not the result of the act of purchase considered in itself, but of the way that act is connected with the overall movement of capital (it introduces the capitalist production process). In no individual moment of the metamorphosis, taken by itself, does the capitalist sell the commodity to the buyer as capital , nor does the buyer alienate his money as capital . In both cases, the commodity is alienated simply as commodity, the money simply as money. Only \"in the context of the whole process, at the moment where the point of departure appears as simultaneously the point of return, in M—M' or C—C',\" does capital emerge in the circulation process as capital . (And note the parenthetical: in the production process, capital emerges as capital \"by the subordination of the worker to the capitalist and the production of surplus-value.\") But at the moment of return, \"the mediation has disappeared.\" What exists is M' or M + ΔM — \"a sum of money equal to that originally advanced plus an excess over this, the realized surplus-value.\" At precisely this point of return, where capital exists as realized capital, as valorized value, taken as a \"point of repose, imaginary or real,\" capital does not enter circulation but appears as withdrawn from circulation, as the result of the entire process. If it is spent again, it is never alienated to a third party as capital but is sold as a simple commodity or given in exchange for a commodity as simply money. It never appears in its circulation process as capital but only as commodity or money, \"and here this is its only existence for others.\" Marx's conclusion is emphatic: commodity and money are capital here \"not because commodities are turned into money and money into commodities, not in their actual relationships to buyers or sellers, but simply in their ideal relationships, either to the capitalist himself (considered subjectively) or as moments of the reproduction process (considering it objectively).\" And: \"It is not in the process of circulation that capital exists as capital in its real movement but only in the process of production, the process of exploiting labour-power.\" This is a decisive statement of the primacy of production over circulation in Marx's system. What Makes Interest-Bearing Capital Specific: Alienation as Capital from the Start With the contrast established, Marx delineates the specificity of interest-bearing capital: \"The owner of money who wants to valorize this as interest-bearing capital parts with it to someone else, puts it into circulation, makes it into a commodity as capital; as capital not only for himself but also for others.\" This is the crux. In the case of ordinary commodity capital, the commodity is capital only for the seller (ideally/subjectively); for the buyer it is just a commodity. In the case of interest-bearing capital, the money is made over to the other person as capital right from the start — \"as value that possesses the use-value of creating surplus-value or profit; as a value that continues its movement after it has functioned and returns to the person who originally spent it, in this case the money's owner.\" Consequently, the money \"is removed from him only for a certain interval, only temporarily stepping from the possession of its proprietor into the possession of the functioning capitalist.\" It is \"neither paid out nor sold, but simply lent; alienated only on condition that it is, first, returned to its starting-point after a definite period of time, and second, is returned as realized capital, so that it has realized its use-value of producing surplus-value.\" This is the definitional core of the loan as a legal-economic form: temporary alienation of possession, retention of ownership, return of the value plus a share in the surplus it has produced. Every subsequent feature of interest (its apparent \"lawlessness,\" its \"irrational\" price-character) flows from this peculiar structure. Fixed Capital, Circulating Capital, and the Unity of Loan Capital as Money Capital Marx then briefly classifies loan capital by the form in which it is repaid. A commodity lent as capital may be lent as fixed capital or as circulating capital , according to its specific properties. Money can be lent in both forms: it is lent as fixed capital if repaid as an annuity (a portion of the capital returning together with the interest). Certain commodities, by the nature of their use-value, can be lent only as fixed capital — houses, boats, machines. But — and this is the unifying claim — all loan capital, whatever its form, is always simply a special form of money capital. Why? Because \"what is lent here is always a definite sum of money, and it is on this sum that the interest is reckoned.\" If what is lent is neither money nor circulating capital, repayment follows the pattern of fixed capital: the lender receives periodic interest plus a part of the used-up value of the fixed capital itself (an equivalent for depreciation over the period); at the end of the term, the unused portion of the fixed capital is returned in kind . If the loaned capital is circulating capital, it returns to the lender in the general mode of reflux of circulating capital. The manner of reflux is thus determined in each case by \"the actual cyclical movement of capital as it reproduces itself and its specific varieties.\" But for loan capital specifically, the reflux takes the form of repayment , \"because the advance, the alienation of the loan capital, has the form of a loan.\" Marx announces that in this chapter he will deal \"only with money capital proper, from which the other forms of loan capital are derived.\" (This is an important scoping decision: the analysis of credit instruments, bills, and joint-stock capital will follow in later chapters.) The Double Reflux, Explicitly Named Marx then states the double reflux with full clarity: \"The capital lent out flows back in a double sense. In the reproduction process it returns to the functioning capitalist, and then its return is repeated once again as a transfer to the lender, the money capitalist, as a repayment to its real proprietor, a return to its legal starting-point.\" The Reproduction Process as a Whole and the \"Reflexive Relationship\" of Capital Marx next contrasts the isolated acts of circulation with the reproduction process viewed as a whole. In the actual circulation process, capital always appears as commodity or money; its movement reduces to a series of purchases and sales — in short, to \"the metamorphosis of commodities.\" But when we consider the reproduction process as a whole, \"if we proceed from money (and it is the same thing if we proceed from the commodity, for we are then proceeding from its value, and thus viewing it too in the guise of money), a certain sum of money is given out and it returns after a given period with an increment.\" What returns is the replacement for the sum advanced, plus a surplus-value. The money \"has been maintained and increased in the course of a certain cyclical movement.\" Money lent as capital is \"hired out precisely as a sum of money that is maintained and increased, a sum which returns with an addition after a certain period and can go through the same process once again.\" It is not given out as money or as commodity (it is neither exchanged for a commodity when advanced as money, nor sold for money when advanced as a commodity); it is rather given out as capital . The \"reflexive relationship in which capital presents itself when we view the capitalist production process as a whole and a unity, and in which capital appears as money breeding money, is here simply embodied in it as its character, its capacity, without the intervening mediating movement.\" And it is in this capacity that it is alienated when lent as money capital. This passage contains one of Marx's most compressed formulations of the fetish character of interest-bearing capital . The \"reflexive relationship\" — the appearance of capital as self-valorizing, as money-breeding-money — is a result of the total process, but when the money is lent it is transferred as though this capacity were an intrinsic property of the thing itself, \"without the intervening mediating movement.\" The mediation (production, exploitation, sale) is presupposed but invisible in the form of the loan. This is the formal root of the \"irrationality\" Marx keeps emphasizing. Proudhon's Confusion: Lending as an \"Evil\" Because It Is Not Selling Marx now launches into an extended critique of Proudhon, based on Gratuité du crédit. Discussion entre M. F. Bastiat et M. Proudhon (Paris, 1850). Proudhon's conception: lending appears to him \"as an evil because it is not selling.\" A loan made on interest is \"the ability to sell the same object over and over again, always receiving the price afresh without ever abandoning ownership over the thing sold\" (p. 9 — Marx notes in a footnote that these are not Proudhon's own words but those of Charles-François Chevé, editor of La Voix du Peuple , paraphrasing Proudhon's idea). Marx concedes a factual premise: the object — money, house, etc. — does not change its owner, as it does in buying and selling. But \"Proudhon does not see that when money is given out in the form of interest-bearing capital, no equivalent for it is received in return.\" Marx then explains the general nature of exchange: in any act of buying and selling, \"the object is given away. But the value is never given away.\" On sale, what is given away is the commodity , not its value — the value returns in the form of money (or a bill/entitlement to payment, \"just another form of this\"). On purchase, money is given away, but not its value, which is replaced in the form of commodities. The industrial capitalist \"keeps the same value in his hands throughout the reproduction process (leaving aside the surplus-value), simply in different forms.\" In so far as exchange takes place (exchange of objects), there is no change in value . While the capitalist is producing surplus-value, there is no exchange; by the time exchange takes place, the surplus-value is already contained in the commodities. Considering the overall circuit M—C—M' , \"a definite sum of value is constantly advanced, and this sum of value plus the surplus-value or profit is withdrawn from the circulation sphere.\" The mediation of this process \"is not to be seen in the simple acts of exchange alone.\" And it is precisely this process of M as capital \"which the interest of the lending money-capitalist is based on and from which it derives.\" The Hat-Maker Example: Value, Price of Production, and the Distribution of Surplus-Value Marx then deploys a numerical counterexample drawn from Proudhon's own text. Proudhon had written (p. 69): \"the hat-maker who sells hats... retains their value, neither more nor less. But the lending capitalist... not only receives his capital back without deduction; he receives more than this capital, more than he puts into the exchange. On top of the capital, he receives an interest.\" Marx reads the hat-maker as standing for the productive capitalist in contrast to the lending capitalist, and observes that Proudhon \"has evidently not managed to penetrate the secret of how the productive capitalist can sell commodities at their values... and by that very act receive a profit over and above the capital he puts into the exchange.\" Marx's illustration: assume the price of production of 100 hats is £115 , and that this price of production happens to be equal to the value of the hats (i.e., the capital producing them is of average social composition). If the profit is 15 per cent, the hat-maker realizes a profit of £15 by selling the commodities at their value of £115. To him, they cost only £100. If he has produced them with his own capital, he pockets the entire excess of £15; if with borrowed capital, he \"has possibly to give up £5 of this as interest.\" Marx's conclusion: \"This in no way affects the value of the hats, but simply the distribution of the surplus-value already contained in the hats among different people.\" This is the analytical demolition of Proudhon's claim that interest enters into the price of the commodity and thereby makes it impossible for the worker to buy back the product of his labour. Proudhon had written (p. 105): \"Since in trade, the interest on capital is added to the worker's wages to make up the price of the commodity, it is impossible for the worker to buy back the product of his own labour. Vivre en travaillant is a principle that involves a contradiction, under the rule of interest.\" Marx's response is that interest does not enter into the value of the commodity; it is a deduction from the surplus-value already contained in it. The worker's inability to buy back the product has nothing to do with interest as such; it is a consequence of the wage relation and the extraction of surplus-value. Proudhon has mistaken a distributional phenomenon for a price-determining one. (In a footnote, Marx glosses the slogan: \" Vivre en travaillant, mourir en combattant (Live working, die fighting) was a traditional slogan of the French working class, particularly associated with the Lyons insurrection of 1834.\" The footnote also records Luther's superior insight: \"They turn buying also into usury. But this is too much to tackle at once. We must confine ourselves for the time being to dealing with usury in lending, and after setting this right (after the day of judgement), we shall go on to give usury in buying its lesson too.\" Marx's point: Luther already knew that profit-making is independent of the form of lending or buying — a level of clarity above Proudhon's.) Proudhon's Fundamental Error: Reading All Capital Through the Lens of Interest-Bearing Capital Marx then identifies Proudhon's deepest confusion with a quotation: \"Since, through exchange, money capital always returns to its source with an accumulation of interest, reinvestment enables the same individual to draw a continual profit\" (p. 154). Proudhon here describes the movement of capital in general in terms of the characteristic movement of interest-bearing capital. What remains a puzzle to him, Marx says, are \"the categories: buying, price, alienation of goods, and the immediate form in which surplus-value appears here; in brief the phenomenon that here capital has become a commodity as capital, that buying has therefore been transformed into lending, and price into a share in the profit.\" This is a crucial theoretical point: Proudhon's error is not merely a factual mistake; it is a category mistake . He generalizes the superficial form of interest-bearing capital (M—M') to all capital, thereby losing the distinction between production and circulation, between value-creation and value-distribution, and between the ownership and the functioning of capital. The Return of Capital as the General Form — and Its Superficial Form in Interest-Bearing Capital Marx then clarifies that \"the return of capital to its point of departure is always the characteristic movement of capital in its overall circuit. This is in no way something exclusively distinctive of interest-bearing capital.\" What distinguishes interest-bearing capital is \"the superficial form of the return, separated off from the mediating circuit.\" The lending capitalist parts with his capital, transfers it to the industrial capitalist, without receiving an equivalent. But this is \"in no way an act of the actual cyclical process of capital; it simply introduces this circuit.\" This first change of place \"does not express any act of metamorphosis, neither purchase nor sale.\" Ownership is not surrendered, because no exchange takes place and no equivalent is received. The return of the money from the industrial capitalist to the lending capitalist simply supplements the first act. Advanced in money form, the capital returns to the industrial capitalist in money form via the cyclical process. But since the capital did not belong to him when he gave it out, it cannot belong to him on its return. Its progress through the reproduction process \"cannot possibly transform the capital into his property.\" Hence he must give it back. The initial act (lender → borrower) is a legal transaction that \"has nothing to do with the actual reproduction process of capital, but simply introduces it.\" The repayment (borrower → lender) is \"a second legal transaction, the complement of the first; the one introduces the real process, the other is a subsequent act after that is completed.\" The point of departure and the point of return — lending-out and recovery — thus appear as \"arbitrary movements mediated by legal transactions, which take place before and after the real movement of capital and have nothing to do with it as such.\" And Marx adds the counterfactual: \"It would make no difference to this real movement if the capital belonged to the industrial capitalist from the start and returned to him alone therefore as his own property.\" This passage establishes the formal separation of the legal from the economic . The real capital circuit (production and circulation) is one thing; the legal envelopment of lending and repayment is another. The form of interest-bearing capital obscures the economic process by foregrounding the legal one. The General Movement of Lending and Borrowing Marx then generalizes: in so far as the transaction between lender and borrower is concerned (setting aside interest), the two legal acts (separated by a longer or shorter interval, during which the real reproduction movement takes place) \"encompass the whole of this movement.\" This movement — \"the act of giving out money on condition of repayment\" — is \"the general movement of lending and borrowing, this specific form of a merely conditional alienation of money or commodities.\" The characteristic movement of capital in general (the return of money to the capitalist, the return of capital to its point of departure) receives, in the case of interest-bearing capital, \"a completely superficial form, separated from the real movement whose form it is.\" A hands over his money \"not as money but rather as capital.\" No Change in the Capital Itself: The Obliteration of Mediation Marx emphasizes: \"There is no change here in the capital itself. It simply changes hands.\" Its actual transformation into capital is accomplished only in B's hands. But for A, it \"has become capital simply by having been given to B.\" The actual reflux of capital from production and circulation only takes place for B; for A, the reflux takes place \"in the same form as the alienation.\" \"The giving-out or lending of money for a certain time, and the repayment of this with interest (surplus-value), is the entire form of the movement attributable to interest-bearing capital as such.\" The real movement of the money lent as capital \"is an operation lying beyond the transactions between lenders and borrowers.\" In those transactions, taken by themselves, \"this mediation is obliterated, invisible and not directly involved.\" Capital as a special kind of commodity \"also has a kind of alienation peculiar to it.\" Here the return does not appear as a consequence and result of a definite series of economic processes, but rather \"as a consequence of a special legal contract between buyer and seller.\" The period of reflux depends on the course of the reproduction process, but for interest-bearing capital \"its return as capital seems to depend simply on the contract between lender and borrower.\" And so the reflux of capital \"no longer appears as a result determined by the production process, but rather as if the capital lent out had never lost the form of money.\" Marx then adds a crucial empirical caveat: \"Of course, these transactions are actually determined by the real refluxes. But this is not apparent in the transaction itself. It is also in no way always the case in practice. If the real reflux does not take place at the right time, the borrower must look to see what other sources of help he can draw on to fulfil his obligations to the lender.\" This is one of the rare moments where Marx gestures toward the credit crisis problematic: the legal obligation to repay is not automatically synchronized with the economic capacity to repay. The \"mere form of capital\" — money given out as A and returning as A + ΔA after a certain period, \"without any other mediation besides this temporal interval\" — is \"simply the irrational form of the real capital movement.\" The Real Movement of Capital and the Presupposition of the Loan Marx then restates the real movement: money is first transformed into means of production; the production process transforms it into a commodity; by sale of the commodity it is transformed back into money, and in this form it returns to the hands of the capitalist who first advanced it. But in the case of interest-bearing capital \"the return, like the giving out, is simply the result of a legal transaction between the owner of the capital and a second person. All that we see is the giving-out and the repayment. Everything that happens in between is obliterated.\" Yet — and here Marx provides the real foundation of the appearance — \"because money advanced as capital has the property of returning to the person advancing it, to whoever spends it as capital, because M—C—M' is the immanent form of the capital movement , for this very reason the owner of money can lend it as capital, as something which possesses the property of returning to its point of departure and of maintaining and increasing itself in the movement it undergoes.\" He gives it out as capital \"because, after being applied as capital, it flows back to its starting-point; the borrower can repay it after a given period of time precisely because it flows back to himself.\" Thus: \"The real cyclical movement of money as capital is the assumption behind the legal transaction by which the borrower of the money has to return it to the lender.\" If the borrower does not apply it as capital, \"that is his affair. The lender lends it as capital, and as capital it has to pass through the functions of capital, which include the circuit of money capital right through to its return to its starting-point in the money form.\" Lending as the Appropriate Form of Alienation for Capital Marx concludes this section: the acts of circulation M—C and C—M', in which the sum of value functions as money or commodity, are \"simply intermediary processes, particular moments of its total movement.\" As capital, it undergoes the total movement M—M'. The lender \"does not spend this on purchasing a commodity, or if the sum of value exists in commodities he does not sell these in exchange for money; he rather advances it as capital, as M—M', as value which returns again to its point of departure at a definite date.\" Instead of buying or selling, he lends . \"This lending is thus the appropriate form for its alienation as capital, instead of as money or commodity.\" And Marx adds a characteristic caveat: \"This in no way implies that lending cannot also be a form for transactions that have nothing to do with the capitalist reproduction process at all\" — a nod to consumption credit, usury, etc., which lie outside the present analysis. The Investigation of Interest Proper: Valorization, Not Mere Return Marx now turns explicitly to interest: \"Up to now we have only considered the movement of the capital lent between its owner and the industrial capitalist. We must now turn to investigate interest.\" The lender puts his money out as capital; the value sum he alienates is capital, \"and this is why it flows back to him.\" But this return alone would not be \"the reflux of a value sum lent as capital, as opposed to the simple repayment of a sum of value previously loaned.\" For the sum to flow back as capital , it must not only have maintained itself but valorized itself — increased its value — so as to return with a surplus-value as M + ΔM , where ΔM is interest, \"that part of the average profit which does not remain in the hands of the functioning capitalist, but falls rather to the money capitalist.\" Marx notes a complication: interest typically flows back at regular intervals in the meantime , before the capital sum is repaid at the end of an extended period. He will address this temporal structure later; here the essential point is that interest is a share of profit , not a separate creation. What the Money Capitalist Alienates: The Use-Value of Capital The pivotal question: \"What does the money capitalist give the borrower, the industrial capitalist? What actually does he alienate to him?\" For \"it is only the act of alienation that makes the lending of money into the alienation of money as capital, i.e. the alienation of capital as a commodity.\" Marx compares with ordinary sale. In ordinary sale, what is alienated is not the value of the commodity (this only changes form — from commodity to money); what is really alienated and transferred to the buyer's consumption is the use-value of the commodity. So what use-value does the money capitalist alienate for the duration of the loan? \"It is the use-value that money receives through the fact that it can be transformed into capital, that it can function as capital so as to produce in its movement a definite surplus-value, the average profit (anything more or less than this quantity appears here as merely accidental), besides conserving its original value.\" And here is the distinctive property: \"With other commodities, the use-value is ultimately consumed, and in this way the substance of the commodity disappears, and with it its value. The commodity of capital, on the other hand, has the peculiar property that the consumption of its use-value not only maintains its value and use-value but in fact increases it.\" So: \"It is this use-value that money has as capital — the capacity to produce the average profit — that the money capitalist alienates to the industrial capitalist for the period during which he gives him control of the capital loaned.\" The Analogy with Labour-Power — and Its Limit Marx draws a careful analogy: \"The money loaned in this way is to a certain extent analogous in this respect to labour-power, in its position vis-à-vis the industrial capitalist.\" The difference: \"the industrial capitalist pays the value of the labour-power, whereas he simply repays the value of the loaned capital.\" The use-value of labour-power for the industrial capitalist is \"that of producing more value (profit) in its use than it possesses and costs itself. This excess value is its use-value for the industrial capitalist. And the use-value of the loaned money capital similarly appears as a capacity to represent and increase value.\" The analogy is illuminating but must be handled with care: in the case of labour-power, the worker sells it and receives its value (wages) — the excess value it produces goes to the capitalist. In the case of loaned money capital, the lender alienates a use-value (capacity to produce profit) and receives back the capital plus interest — but the ownership of the capital is retained throughout. The two cases belong to different moments: labour-power is the source of surplus-value; loaned capital is a claim on surplus-value already produced elsewhere. The analogy is a formal one (both involve alienation of a use-value that produces more value than it costs), not a substantive one. The Completeness of the Analogy with Commodity Exchange Marx then spells out the analogy with ordinary commodity exchange in two respects: Value is transferred from hand to hand. In simple commodity exchange, both buyer and seller retain the same value, only in different forms (commodity vs. money). \"The difference in the case of the loan is that in this transaction the money capitalist is now the only one who gives out value; but he preserves this by the subsequent repayment. In the loan, only one party receives value, since only one party gives value out.\" One party alienates a real use-value, the other receives and uses it. But unlike an ordinary commodity, \"this use-value is itself a value, i.e. the excess of the value that results from the use of the money as capital over its original magnitude. The profit is this use-value.\" The Price of Capital: What the Borrower Pays The use-value of money lent out is \"its capacity to function as capital and as such to produce the average profit under average conditions.\" Marx cites Massie: \"The equitableness of taking interest depends not upon a man's making or not making profit, but upon its [i.e., the sum borrowed's] being capable of producing profit if rightly employed\" ( An Essay on the Governing Causes of the Natural Rate of Interest , London, 1750, p. 49). And: \"Rich people, instead of employing their money themselves... let it out to other people for them to make profit of, reserving for the owners a proportion of the profits so made\" (op. cit., pp. 23–4). This is a striking citation: an eighteenth-century writer already grasps that interest is a share of profit , not a price of a thing. What does the industrial capitalist pay — what is the \"price\" of the capital lent out? Drawing on Massie: \"That which men pay as interest for the use of what they borrow... is a part of the profit it is capable of producing.\" Marx then contrasts the buyer of an ordinary commodity (who buys use-value and pays value) with the borrower of money (who buys its use-value as capital). What does the borrower pay? \"Certainly not its price or value, as with other commodities.\" The value does not change its form between lender and borrower, as it does between buyer and seller. The sum of value is given out without an equivalent and returned after a certain period. \"The lender remains the owner of this value throughout, even after it has been transferred from him to the borrower.\" Note the striking reversal: \"With simple commodity exchange, the money is always on the side of the buyer; but with lending, the money is on the side of the seller. It is he who gives the money away for a certain time, and it is the buyer of the capital who receives it as a commodity.\" (Here \"seller\" = the lender of capital; \"buyer\" = the borrower of capital. The terminology is deliberately jarring to highlight the anomaly.) The Determination of the Interest Magnitude: A Share, Not a Whole But this is possible only in so far as the money functions as capital and is therefore advanced. The borrower borrows the money as capital, as self-valorizing value. It does not become capital in itself (like any capital at its starting-point) until the moment of its advance; only by its use is it valorized and realized as capital. But it is as realized capital that the borrower must pay it back — as value plus surplus-value (interest). And the latter \"can only be a part of the profit he has realized. Only a part, and not the whole.\" Why not the whole? Because the use-value for the borrower is that it produces him a profit; \"otherwise the lender would not have been able to alienate the use-value.\" And why must some be paid? Because if the whole profit fell to the borrower, \"he would pay nothing for the alienation of the use-value. He would only repay the money advanced to the lender as simple money, not as capital, as realized capital, for it is realized capital only as M + ΔM.\" The upshot: \"Both lender and borrower put out the same sum of money as capital. But it is only in the hands of the borrower that it functions as such. Profit is not doubled by the double existence of the same sum of money as capital for two persons. It can only function for both of them as capital by a division of the profit.\" The share accruing to the lender is interest ; the remainder is profit of enterprise . This distinction — the economic content of the difference between ownership and function — is the central positive result of the chapter. The Irrationality of Interest as \"Price\" Marx then returns to the conceptual critique. \"It must never be forgotten that capital as capital is a commodity here, and that the commodity we are dealing with is capital. All the relationships that appear here, therefore, would be irrational from the standpoint of the simple commodity, or even from the standpoint of capital in so far as it functions as commodity capital in its reproduction process.\" Lending and borrowing (instead of selling and buying) is \"a distinction proceeding from the specific nature of the commodity of capital.\" Similarly, the fact that what is paid is interest instead of the price of the commodity. \"If interest is spoken of as the price of money capital, this is an irrational form of price, in complete contradiction with the concept of the price of a commodity.\" Marx explains: \"Here, price is reduced to its purely abstract form, completely lacking in content, as simply a particular sum of money that is paid for something which somehow or other figures as a use-value; whereas in its concept, price is the value of this use-value expressed in money.\" Marx elaborates the contradiction: \"Interest as the price of capital is a completely irrational expression right from the start. Here a commodity has a double value, firstly a value, and then a price that is different from this value, although price is the money expression of value.\" Money capital \"is at first nothing more than a sum of money, or the value of a certain quantity of commodities assessed as a sum of money. If a commodity is lent as capital, this is only the disguised form of a sum of money. For what is lent as capital is not a certain number of pounds of cotton, but rather a certain amount of money that exists in the form of cotton as the cotton's value.\" The price of capital thus relates to it \"as a sum of money, even if not as currency, as Mr Torrens believes\" (Marx here cites Torrens' On the Operation of the Bank Charter Act of 1844 , 2nd edn, 1847, pp. 5–6: \"The term 'value', when applied to currency, has three several meanings... (2) currency, actually in hand... compared with the same amount of currency to be received upon a future day. In this case the value of currency is measured by the rate of interest, and the rate of interest being determined by the ratio between the amount of liable capital and the demand for it.\") Then: \"How then is a sum of value to have a price besides its own price, besides the price that is expressed in its own money form? Price, after all, is the value of the commodity as distinct from its use-value (and this is also the case with market price, whose distinction from value is not qualitative, but merely quantitative, bearing exclusively on the magnitude of value). A price that is qualitatively distinct from value is an absurd contradiction.\" In a footnote, Marx cites Tooke ( Inquiry into the Currency Principle , p. 77): \"The ambiguity of the term 'value of money' or 'of the currency', when employed indiscriminately as it is, to signify both value in exchange for commodities and value in use of capital, is a constant source of confusion.\" Marx comments: \"The major confusion here (which in fact lies in the thing itself), i.e. that value as such (interest) comes to be the use-value of capital, is something that Tooke does not see.\" The footnote identifies Tooke as \"the last English economist of any value\" (cf. A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy , pp. 185–7). This is a crucial nuance: the confusion is not merely in the heads of the economists; it \"lies in the thing itself.\" The form of interest-bearing capital objectively produces the appearance that capital has a price distinct from its value. Marx's conceptual critique is therefore an exposition of the necessary illusions of the capitalist mode of production, not a debunking of mere errors. Interest as the Measure of Valorization Marx resolves the apparent contradiction by re-interpreting interest: \"Capital manifests itself as capital by its valorization; the extent of this valorization expresses the quantitative extent to which it is realized as capital. The surplus-value or profit produced by it — the rate or level of this — is measurable only in comparison with the value of the capital advanced. And so the greater or lesser valorization of interest-bearing capital is also measurable only by comparing the amount of interest, the part of the total profit falling to this capital, with the value of the capital advanced. If price thus expresses the value of a commodity, interest expresses the valorization of money capital and appears therefore as the price that the lender is paid for it.\" So the \"price\" of capital is not a price in the strict sense, but a rate of valorization — a ratio of surplus to capital advanced. The term \"price\" is a façon de parler , an \"irrational form\" that nonetheless has a definite quantitative content (the interest rate). Marx concludes: \"We see from this how completely absurd it is to try to apply directly the simple relationships of exchange mediated by money, buying and selling, to this phenomenon, as Proudhon does. The basic assumption is precisely that money functions as capital and hence can be made over to someone else as potential capital.\" Capital as a Commodity: Use-Value Is Profit, and Value Is Determined by Surplus-Value Produced Marx then summarizes the \"commodity\" character of capital. \"Capital itself appears here as a commodity in so far as it is offered on the market and the use-value of money as capital really is alienated. Its use-value however is to produce a profit. The value of money or commodities as capital is not determined by their value as money or commodities but rather by the quantity of surplus-value that they produce for their possessor.\" \"The product of capital is profit.\" On the basis of capitalist production, \"the difference between money spent as money and money advanced as capital is simply a difference of application.\" Money or a commodity is already potential capital in itself, \"just as labour-power is potential capital.\" Marx gives two reasons: Money can be turned into elements of production, and is already, just as it is, \"simply an abstract expression of these elements, their existence as value.\" The material elements of wealth \"possess the property of being already potential capital, because their complementary antithesis, the thing that makes them capital — namely wage-labour — is present as soon as capitalist production is assumed.\" This is a profound claim about the social determination of wealth under capitalism. The \"antithetical social determination of material wealth — its antithesis to labour as wage-labour — is already expressed in capital ownership as such, quite apart from the production process.\" This one moment, \"separated from the capitalist production process itself, whose constant result it is, and as whose constant result it is also its constant presupposition,\" is expressed thus: \"that money, and likewise commodities, are in themselves latent, potential capital, i.e. can be sold as capital; in this form they give control of the labour of others, give a claim to the appropriation of others' labour, and are therefore self-valorizing value.\" And Marx draws the political conclusion: \"It also emerges very clearly here how this relationship is the title to, and the means to the appropriation of, the labour of others, and not any kind of labour that the capitalist is supposed to offer as an equivalent.\" This is the class-theoretic core of the chapter. Interest-bearing capital is not a technical device for allocating savings; it is, in its very form, a title to the appropriation of others' labour . The capacity to lend money at interest rests on the existence of wage-labour, and the interest received is a claim on surplus-value extracted from workers. The \"commodity\" character of capital is thus not a neutral market phenomenon but the expression of a specific social relation of production. Competition and the Absence of a \"Natural\" Rate of Interest Marx next examines the analogy between the determination of interest and the determination of commodity prices by supply and demand. Capital appears as a commodity \"in so far as the division of profit into interest and profit proper is governed by supply and demand, i.e. by competition, just like the market prices of commodities. But here the distinction is just as striking as the analogy.\" He then lays out the general theory of price determination under supply and demand. If supply and demand coincide, the market price of a commodity corresponds to its price of production — its price is then governed by the inner laws of capitalist production, independent of competition. Fluctuations in supply and demand explain nothing but divergences between market prices and prices of production — divergences which are mutually compensatory, so that over certain longer periods the average market prices equal the prices of production. \"As soon as they coincide, these forces cease to have any effect, they cancel each other out, and the general law of price determination then emerges as the law of the individual case as well.\" Market price then corresponds to price of production \"in its immediate existence and not only as an average of all price movements,\" and the price of production \"is governed by the immanent laws of the mode of production.\" Similarly with wages. If supply and demand coincide, their effect ceases, and wages are equal to the value of labour-power . Interest on money capital is different. \"Here competition does not determine divergences from the law, for there is no law of distribution other than that dictated by competition; as we shall go on to see, there is no 'natural' rate of interest. What is called the natural rate of interest simply means the rate established by free competition. There are no 'natural' limits to the interest rate. Where competition does not just determine divergences and fluctuations, so that in a situation where its reciprocally acting forces balance, all determination ceases, what is to be determined is something inherently lawless and arbitrary. More about this in the next chapter.\" This is a remarkable passage. In the case of commodity prices and wages, competition is the mechanism through which an underlying law (value, price of production, value of labour-power) asserts itself. The underlying law is independent of competition. In the case of interest, there is no underlying law ; competition itself is the sole determinant. The interest rate is \"inherently lawless and arbitrary\" — subject only to the reciprocal pressures of supply and demand, with no \"natural\" anchor. This is not a failure of Marx's theory but a substantive claim about the nature of the interest rate as a distributive magnitude. The division of profit into interest and profit of enterprise has no internal \"law\" of the same kind as the law of value; it is determined by the relative strength of lenders and borrowers in the money market. This admission will be crucial for the later analysis of the money market and the credit system, and for understanding why the interest rate is the most \"fetishistic\" and crisis-prone of all prices. The Superficiality of Interest-Bearing Capital: Time and the Appearance of Self-Valorization The chapter's final section returns to the theme of superficiality. In the case of interest-bearing capital, \"everything appears in a superficial manner: the advance of capital as a mere transfer from lender to borrower; the reflux of the realized capital as a mere transfer back, a repayment with interest from the borrower to the lender.\" So, too, does a genuine property of the capitalist mode of production appear superficially: \"the property inherent to the capitalist mode of production, that the rate of profit is determined not simply by the ratio of the profit made in one individual turnover to the capital value advanced, but also by the length of this turnover time itself, i.e. as the profit that industrial capital yields in particular periods of time.\" In interest-bearing capital, this too takes a superficial form: \"it appears that a certain interest is paid to the lender for a certain interval of time.\" This brings Marx to Adam Müller (the \"romantic\" of Elemente der Staatskunst , Berlin, 1809, part iii, p. 138): \"In determining the price of things, time is unimportant; in determining interest, time is the principal thing involved.\" Marx's response: \"He does not see how production time and circulation time come into play in determining the prices of commodities, and how it is precisely in this way that the rate of profit is determined for a given period of turnover of capital, while interest is determined precisely by this determination of profit for a given period. His wisdom here, as always, lies in the way that he sees clouds of dust on the surface and pretentiously proclaims this dust to be something mysterious and significant.\" The chapter thus closes with a methodological warning: the appearance of interest-bearing capital — money paying for time — is superficial, but it is not arbitrary. It is grounded in the real determination of the rate of profit by turnover time. The task of theory is to trace the appearance back to its real foundations, not to celebrate or mystify the surface. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles Revealed The chapter yields several systemic laws and principles that operate well beyond its immediate subject. First, the unity of value theory and distribution theory. Interest is not a price of a thing but a share of surplus-value . The total profit produced by the functioning capital is divided into interest (falling to the owner) and profit of enterprise (remaining with the borrower). No new value is created by the loan. This means that the entire edifice of finance — interest, dividends, rent on loan capital — rests on surplus-value extracted in production. The chapter thus closes the circuit: value produced in production is distributed among industrial profit, commercial profit, interest, and (later) rent and profit of enterprise. The theory of distribution is not a separate branch of economics; it is an extension of the labour theory of value. Second, the objective reality of \"irrational forms.\" Marx insists that interest as the \"price of capital\" is a contradiction in terms — a price qualitatively distinct from value. Yet this irrationality is not a mere error of economists; it \"lies in the thing itself.\" The form of interest-bearing capital objectively produces the appearance that money begets money, that capital has a price apart from its value, that ownership as such is productive. This is a general methodological principle in Capital : the surface forms of capitalist production (wages, prices, interest, profit) are not illusions to be dismissed but necessary appearances whose real foundations must be reconstructed by theory. The critique of political economy is not the dismissal of appearances but the explanation of their necessity. Third, the separation of ownership and function. Interest-bearing capital formalizes a distinction that runs through the entire capitalist economy: the distinction between the owner of capital, who performs no productive function, and the functioning capitalist, who organizes production or circulation. Interest is the revenue of mere ownership; profit of enterprise is the revenue of functioning. This distinction, once established, prepares the analysis of the rentier, the joint-stock company, and the \"abolition of the capitalist as a functionary\" in the era of credit. It also grounds the later analysis of \"fictitious capital.\" Fourth, the specific status of the interest rate. The interest rate is unique among economic magnitudes in that it has no underlying \"natural\" law . Competition, which elsewhere merely enforces a law determined by the mode of production, here is the law. The rate of interest is \"inherently lawless and arbitrary.\" This is a striking admission of the limits of law-governed determination within capitalism, and it identifies the interest rate as a privileged site for the analysis of contingency, power, and crisis in the financial system. The Practical and Theoretical Implications For the theory of credit and crisis. The chapter's analysis of the \"obliterated mediation\" — the fact that the legal form of the loan hides the economic process on which it depends — directly anticipates the theory of credit crises. If the legal obligation to repay is detached from the economic capacity to repay, then a disruption in the real circuit (a failure to sell, a decline in profit) becomes a credit crisis. The chapter's remark that \"if the real reflux does not take place at the right time, the borrower must look to see what other sources of help he can draw on\" anticipates the analysis of the money market, the discount rate, and the lender-of-last-resort function. For the critique of Proudhon and \"credit socialism.\" The chapter is a systematic demolition of the idea that the ills of capitalism can be cured by reforming credit. Proudhon's proposal — that capital should be lent at cost, that interest is an unjust \"surplus\" — is shown to rest on a category mistake. Interest is not a price added to the commodity; it is a deduction from surplus-value already produced. Abolishing interest without abolishing wage-labour and the production of surplus-value would merely redistribute surplus-value among capitalists; it would not liberate the worker. The critique extends to all \"credit reforms\" that treat finance as the root of exploitation rather than its branch. For the philosophical anthropology of capital. The chapter culminates in the claim that capital, as loanable money, is a \"title to, and the means to the appropriation of, the labour of others, and not any kind of labour that the capitalist is supposed to offer as an equivalent.\" This is the clearest expression in Volume 3 of the social relation embodied in money capital. The lender's claim to interest is grounded not in any contribution to production but in the institution of private property in the means of production — an institution that presupposes the existence of wage-labour. The \"productivity of capital\" is thus revealed as the productivity of a social relation, not of a thing. For the theory of fetishism. The chapter's analysis of interest-bearing capital as the form in which capital appears as \"money breeding money,\" \"without the intervening mediating movement,\" is a direct extension of the fetishism analysis of Volume 1. In the commodity, the social relation between producers appears as a relation between things. In interest-bearing capital, the social relation between classes appears as a natural property of money — its capacity to \"breed.\" This is the highest form of fetishism, because it inverts the true order: it makes the result of the total process (M') appear as the immediate property of M. The chapter thus provides the theoretical foundation for the critique of finance capital and of the \"rentier\" as the purest embodiment of the fetishistic character of capital. The Chapter's Place in the Broader Framework Chapter 21 is the gateway to the most abstract and most politically explosive parts of Volume 3. It establishes the conceptual machinery — the distinction between interest and profit of enterprise, the analysis of the loan as a legal form, the irrational character of the \"price of capital,\" the absence of a natural rate of interest — that will be deployed in the subsequent chapters on the money market, the credit system, the joint-stock company, and the \"abolition of the capitalist mode of production within the capitalist mode of production\" itself. Its compact, rigorous, and at times polemical argumentation makes it one of the most important short chapters in the entire work: it is where Marx shows that the most \"mysterious\" and most \"fetishistic\" revenues of capitalism — interest and the profits of financial ownership — are, at bottom, nothing but shares in the surplus-value produced by wage-labour, and that the entire apparent autonomy of the financial sphere rests on the obscured but ineliminable foundation of exploitation in production. The \"irrational form\" is real; but it is a form, and the task of critique is to reveal the real movement that this form conceals. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 20 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 22"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 22", "title": "Chapter 22", "subtitle": "Division of Profit. Rate of Interest", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 22/Chapter 22 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 7286, "read_time": "36 min read", "tags": ["Interest-Bearing Capital & Loan Capital"], "keywords": ["Division of Profit: Rate of Interest", "Natural Rate of Interest Fallacy (No Economic Law for Natural Rate)", "Competition Determining Market Interest Rates", "Maximum and Minimum Limits of Interest", "Custom, Law, and International Capital Movements"], "text": "Chapter 22 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 7,267 words ⏱️ ~36 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 22 of Capital Volume 3, edited by Engels from Marx's manuscripts, is a compact but theoretically pivotal investigation into the form of interest-bearing capital. Its central problem is deceptively simple: interest is, by definition, merely a part of profit—a deduction the industrial capitalist pays to the money capitalist for the use of borrowed capital. Yet in the everyday functioning of the capitalist economy, interest appears as something entirely independent: a price, a uniform rate, a daily fact quoted in the financial press. How does a mere aliquot part of surplus-value acquire an autonomous existence, a \"natural\" appearance, and a life of its own? That is the chapter's guiding question. Marx's answer unfolds through a double movement. First, he establishes the quantitative relation between interest and profit: the average rate of profit sets the upper limit of interest, and—given a fixed proportion—interest rises and falls with the general profit rate. This yields a striking corollary: because the rate of profit tends to fall with the development of capitalist production, the rate of interest tends, on this basis, to fall with industrial development. Second, Marx demonstrates that the actual level of interest—its average or \"natural\" rate—cannot be derived from any general law whatsoever. It is determined by competition between lenders and borrowers, by custom and legal tradition, by the supply of loanable capital generated by a growing rentier class, and by the credit system. There is, therefore, \"no natural rate of interest\" in the sense in which economists speak of a natural rate of profit or a natural rate of wages. The chapter's deepest theoretical claim, signalled in Engels's inserted manuscript note (footnote 62), is methodological: interest represents the case in which a purely quantitative division of surplus-value generates a qualitative distinction . This is the exact inverse of the relation between surplus-value and wages (or between profit and rent), where a qualitative distinction between independent variables—labour-power and capital, capital and landed property—underlies a quantitative division. In the case of interest, the same capital functions only once and produces profit only once; the distinction between \"capital-as-loan\" and \"capital-in-function\" is a distinction between two titles to the same surplus-value, not between two productive agencies. This formal analysis matters far beyond the chapter itself. It prepares the ground for the later treatment of profit of enterprise and wages of superintendence, for the theory of the credit system, and, most importantly, for the critique of the \"trinity formula\" in which capital appears as a self-expanding entity bearing fruit by nature. Interest-bearing capital (M—M′) is the most fetishised form of capital, the form in which capital appears to generate value without any mediating production process. Chapter 22 explains why this illusion arises and what determines the magnitude of the payment—thereby providing the conceptual scaffolding for the entire theory of credit and fictitious capital that follows. It is, in short, the chapter in which the appearance of interest as an independent revenue is shown to be the necessary form of appearance of a distributional relation grounded in the mere ownership of money capital. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Scope Problem: What This Chapter Refuses to Investigate Marx opens with an explicit act of theoretical self-limitation. The chapter's object \"cannot be investigated in detail,\" he warns, and he proceeds to list three crucial exclusions. First, the competition between lenders and borrowers and the resulting short-term fluctuations of the money market fall outside the scope of the discussion. Second, the circuit which the rate of interest describes during the industrial cycle can only be dealt with after the cycle itself has been analysed—and the cycle, too, is bracketed. Third, the approximate equalization of the rate of interest on the world market is postponed. What remains, and what constitutes the chapter's positive content, is narrow but decisive: \"the independent form of interest-bearing capital and the way that interest acquires autonomy vis-à-vis profit.\" This is a formal determination chapter, not a theory of finance. Marx is isolating a single question: how does a portion of surplus-value come to appear, and to function economically, as a price paid for a commodity? The exclusions are not evasions; they are methodological safeguards ensuring that the analysis of form is not contaminated by the analysis of fluctuation . The industrial cycle, the money market, and the world market will return in later chapters; here they function only as illustrations of a structural point. 2.2 The Limits of Interest: A Determinate Maximum, an Indeterminate Minimum Since interest is simply a part of profit that the industrial capitalist must pay to the money capitalist, \"the maximum limit of interest would seem to be the profit itself\"—in which case the functioning capitalist's share would be zero. Marx refines this: leaving aside special cases in which interest actually exceeds profit (so that it cannot all be paid out of profit), the maximum limit is the whole profit minus that part reducible to \"wages of superintendence,\" a category \"to be developed later.\" This forward reference is important: Marx is already anticipating the analysis of Chapter 23, in which the industrial capitalist's supervisory function is shown to be partly a real function and partly an ideological disguise of his claim on surplus-value. The minimum limit, by contrast, \"is completely indeterminate. It could fall to any level, however low. But countervailing circumstances constantly enter to raise it above this relative minimum.\" Here we encounter the chapter's governing asymmetry: interest has a ceiling fixed by the profit rate, but no floor derivable from any economic law. The \"countervailing circumstances\"—custom, legal tradition, competition, the institutional habits of the money market—are not laws but empirical stabilisers. Marx then cites two authorities to establish the general framework. The Economist (22 January 1853) states that \"the rate of interest depends (1) on the rate of profit; (2) on the proportion in which the entire profit is divided between the lender and borrower.\" Massie (whose Essay on the Governing Causes of the Natural Rate of Interest , 1750, Marx repeatedly invokes) insists that \"if that which men pay as interest for the use of what they borrow, be a part of the profits it is capable of producing, this interest must always be governed by those profits.\" These citations establish the two variables that structurate the entire chapter: the magnitude of profit and the division of that profit. 2.3 The Fixed-Proportion Hypothesis and Its Numerical Demonstration Marx proceeds by assuming, as a heuristic, a fixed ratio between total profit and the portion paid as interest. Under this assumption, \"the interest will rise or fall with the total profit,\" which is itself determined by the general rate of profit and its fluctuations. He supplies a series of concrete illustrations. If the average profit rate is 20 per cent and interest is one quarter of the profit, the interest rate is 5 per cent; if profit is 16 per cent, interest is 4 per cent. Varying the interest rate within a given profit rate produces a correspondingly varied industrial profit: with a 20 per cent profit rate, interest could rise to 8 per cent while the industrial capitalist still retains the same 12 per cent he would enjoy under a 16 per cent profit rate combined with a 4 per cent interest rate. If interest were only 6 or 7 per cent, he would in fact retain a greater share. The crucial proposition follows: \"if interest is a constant proportion of the average profit, it will follow that the higher the general rate of profit, the greater is the absolute difference between total profit and interest, and the greater therefore the total profit that accrues to the functioning capitalist, and vice versa.\" Marx's numerical series makes this vivid. Taking interest as one-fifth of the average profit: one-fifth of 10 = 2; the difference between total profit and interest = 8 one-fifth of 20 = 4; the difference = 20 − 4 = 16 one-fifth of 25 = 5; the difference = 25 − 5 = 20 one-fifth of 30 = 6; the difference = 30 − 6 = 24 one-fifth of 35 = 7; the difference = 35 − 7 = 28 The interest rates of 4, 5, 6 and 7 per cent are here always one-fifth—i.e. 20 per cent—of the total profit. The mathematical point is that an unaltered proportional share of a growing magnitude yields a growing absolute residual. Hence: \"With interest such a constant proportion, the industrial profit (the difference between the total profit and the interest) would be greater, the higher the general rate of profit, and vice versa.\" This establishes what might be called the magnitude dependence of interest: interest is not an autonomous quantity but a function of the profit mass. All else equal—the ratio between interest and total profit being more or less constant—\"the functioning capitalist will be able and willing to pay a higher or lower interest in direct proportion to the level of his profit rate.\" Massie's footnote (61) codifies this: \"The natural rate of interest is governed by the profits of trade to particulars.\" 2.4 The Inverse Relation Between Interest and Industrial Development The next move is one of Marx's boldest inferences. Since the level of the profit rate stands in inverse proportion to the development of capitalist production—the famous tendency of the rate of profit to fall—it follows that \"the higher or lower rate of interest in a country stands in the same inverse proportion to the level of industrial development, particularly in so far as the variation in the rate of interest expresses an actual variation in the profit rate.\" Richer, more industrially developed countries should exhibit, other things equal, lower interest rates. Marx immediately qualifies this with characteristic caution: \"We shall see later on that this need by no means always be the case.\" The qualification is essential. Interest may vary independently of profit for reasons Marx will develop in the same chapter (the growth of rentiers, the credit system) and in later chapters (the world market, the state of the balance of payments, hoarding and dishoarding). Nonetheless, in this restricted sense, \"interest is governed by profit, and more precisely by the general rate of profit. And this kind of regulation applies even to its average.\" The average rate of profit is therefore \"ultimately determining the maximum limit of the interest.\" 2.5 Two Distinct Orders of Determination Marx now sharpens the conceptual distinction that underlies the whole chapter. Where a given whole is to be divided in two, the first thing that matters is the size of the whole —and the magnitude of profit is determined by the average rate of profit. Once that rate is given (say on a capital of 100), interest \"evidently varies in inverse proportion to the part of profit that remains to the functioning capitalist who operates with borrowed capital.\" But—and this is the decisive theoretical claim—\"the circumstances that determine the magnitude of the profit to be divided, the value product of unpaid labour, are very different from those that determine its distribution among these two kinds of capitalist, and often operate in completely opposite directions.\" Here Engels inserts a manuscript note (footnote 62) that illuminates the chapter's architecture: \"The course of this chapter suggests it is better, before the laws of distribution of profit are investigated, to develop first of all the way in which the quantitative division becomes a qualitative one. No more is needed to make the transition from the previous chapter to this point, than to take interest as a portion of profit that is not yet determined more specifically.\" This is the methodological hinge of the chapter. The movement \"from quantity to quality\" is precisely what turns a share of profit into a distinct economic category—a price, a revenue, an autonomous magnitude. 2.6 The Industrial Cycle and the Barometric Behaviour of Interest Marx returns to the industrial cycle he had initially bracketed, not to analyse it but to register the regularity of interest's behaviour within it. The cycle he names runs: \"inactivity, growing animation, prosperity, overproduction, crash, stagnation, inactivity, etc.\" Within this cycle, three patterns stand out. First, \"a low level of interest generally corresponds to periods of prosperity or especially high profit.\" Second, \"a rise in interest comes between prosperity and its collapse.\" Third, \"maximum interest up to extreme usury corresponds to a period of crisis.\" Gilbart's four-period schema (footnote 63) codifies the sequence: after pressure, money is abundant without speculation; then abundant with speculation; then demand for money rises as speculation declines; finally money is scarce and \"a pressure arrives.\" The empirical evidence is drawn from the 1840s. From summer 1843 there was marked prosperity. The rate of interest, which in spring 1842 was still 4½ per cent, fell in spring and summer 1843 to 2 per cent; in September 1843 it even fell to 1½ per cent (Gilbart). During the crisis of 1847 it rose to 8 per cent and more. But Marx immediately complicates the picture: \"Yet low interest can also be accompanied by stagnation, and a moderate rise in interest by growing animation.\" The correspondence is not law-like; it is a tendency. Tooke's explanation (footnote 64) is given for the low rates of the early 1840s: \"the accumulation of surplus-capital necessarily accompanying the scarcity of profitable employment for it in previous years, by the release of hoards, and by the revival of confidence in commercial prospects.\" This is a supply-side explanation, and it already anticipates the two causes of the secular fall of interest developed below. A crucial footnote (66) warns against reading a low interest rate as an index of sound conditions: \"Since the rate of interest is determined by and large by the average profit rate, extraordinary swindling can very often go together with a low rate of interest. For example the railway swindle of summer 1844. The Bank of England's interest rate was only raised to 3 per cent on 16 October 1844.\" Low interest can coexist with fraud and speculative mania—an observation that will have great resonance in the later analysis of fictitious capital. Marx then describes the crisis mechanism in which interest peaks. The rate of interest \"reaches its highest level during crises, when people have to borrow in order to pay, no matter what the cost.\" Here interest is determined not by profitability but by the need for means of payment . And a rise in interest corresponds to a fall in the price of securities: \"this is at the same time a very suitable opportunity for people with available money capital to buy up such interest-bearing securities at ridiculously low prices, and in the regular course of events these securities are bound to reach at least their average price again as soon as the interest rate falls.\" Footnote 65 gives the brutal illustration: a banker refused a loan on a £200,000 bond; the customer was about to announce suspension; \"the banker would buy the bond at £150,000.\" The chapter thus records, in embryo, the speculative dynamic that turns crisis into a transfer of wealth to holders of ready money—a theme Marx will develop in the theory of the credit system. 2.7 The Secular Tendency of the Interest Rate to Fall Marx now identifies \"a tendency for the rate of interest to fall, quite independently of fluctuations in the rate of profit.\" Two major reasons are given. (1) The growth of the rentier class (Ramsay). The argument, quoted at length from Ramsay's Essay on the Distribution of Wealth (pp. 201–2), is that even if capital were \"never borrowed with any view but to productive employment,\" interest might vary without any change in the rate of gross profits. As a nation advances in wealth, \"a class of men springs up and increases more and more, who by the labours of their ancestors find themselves in the possession of funds sufficiently ample to afford a handsome maintenance from the interest alone.\" Others, active in youth and middle age, retire to live quietly on the interest of accumulated sums. This class increases with national riches, since those who begin with a tolerable stock reach independence sooner than those who begin with little. Consequently, \"in old and rich countries, the amount of national capital belonging to those who are unwilling to take the trouble of employing it themselves, bears a larger proportion to the whole productive stock of the society, than in newly settled and poorer districts.\" Ramsay concludes with the decisive identity: \"As the class of rentiers increases, so also does that of lenders of capital, for they are one and the same.\" The supply of loanable capital grows faster than the demand arising from productive employment, pressing the rate of interest downward. (2) The development of the credit system. Marx writes: \"the ever growing control this gives industrialists and merchants over the monetary savings of all classes of society through the mediation of the bankers, as well as the progressive concentration of these savings on a mass scale, so that they can function as money capital, must also press down the rate of interest.\" He defers elaboration—\"More on this later\"—but the point is structural. The credit system socialises idle savings, converting scattered hoards into mass loanable capital. By expanding the supply of loan capital, it depresses its price. 2.8 Ramsay's Determinants and the Limits of Competition Marx next quotes Ramsay's more general proposition: the interest rate \"depends partly upon the rate of gross profits, partly on the proportion in which these are separated into profits of capital and those of enterprise. This proportion again depends upon the competition between the lenders of capital and the borrowers; which competition is influenced, though by no means entirely regulated, by the rate of gross profit expected to be realized.\" Two reasons are given why competition is not exclusively regulated by expected gross profit: \"on the one hand many borrow without any view to productive employment; and, on the other, because the proportion of the whole capital to be lent, varies with the riches of the country independently of any change in gross profits.\" The first point encompasses consumption credit, speculative borrowing, and borrowing to meet payments (as in crises); the second restates the rentier/supply argument. Marx then proposes the empirical method by which the average rate of interest might be ascertained: \"(1) the average interest rate as it varies over the major industrial cycles; (2) the rate of interest in those investments where capital is lent for longer periods.\" The implication is that the average rate is a statistical and historical construct , not a theoretical magnitude derivable from first principles. 2.9 There Is No \"Natural\" Rate of Interest Here the chapter reaches its central negative thesis: \"The prevailing average rate of interest in a country, as distinct from the constantly fluctuating market rate, cannot be determined by any law. There is no natural rate of interest, therefore, in the sense that economists speak of a natural rate of profit and a natural rate of wages.\" Massie's formulation is quoted as fundamentally correct: the only question is \"what proportion of these profits do of right belong to the borrower, and what to the lender; and this there is no other method of determining than by the opinions of borrowers and lenders in general; for right and wrong, in this respect, are only what common consent makes so.\" Marx then launches a sustained methodological polemic against supply-and-demand explanations. \"The coincidence of demand and supply means nothing at all here, even taking the average rate of profit as given.\" Where the supply-and-demand formula is legitimately used elsewhere (and \"this is then correct for practical purposes\"), it functions as a formula for finding the regulating limits or limiting quantities that are independent of competition and in fact determine it. It allows those caught up in competition to form some idea—\"even if still a superficial one\"—of the inner connection of economic relations. It is, in short, \"a method of getting from the variations that accompany competition to the limits of these variations.\" But this does not hold for the average rate of interest. \"There is no reason at all why the average conditions of competition, of equilibrium between lender and borrower, should give the lender an interest of 3, 4, 5 per cent, etc. on his capital, or alternatively a certain percentage, 20 per cent or 50 per cent, of the gross profit.\" Where competition as such decides, \"the determination is inherently accidental, purely empirical, and only pedantry or fantasy can seek to present this accident as something necessary.\" Marx supports this with two polemical targets. Opdyke, in his Treatise on Political Economy (New York, 1851), made \"a most unsuccessful attempt to explain the general phenomenon of a 5 per cent rate of interest in terms of eternal laws.\" But the more naive is Karl Arnd, in Die naturgemässe Volkswirthschaft (Hanau, 1845), who proposed what Marx derisively christens the \"primordial forest rate of interest\": the notion that the rate of interest is regulated by \"the ratio by which the volume of timber in European forests increases through its annual growth,\" namely 3 or 4 to 100. Marx's sarcasm is withering—\"(how curious of the trees to arrange their growth independently of their exchange-value!)\"—and he recommends the author to posterity as the \"philosopher of the dog tax.\" The point is serious: attempts to naturalise interest are absurd because interest has no natural magnitude. Marx also mocks the parliamentary reports of 1857 and 1858, in which Bank of England directors, London and country bankers, and professional theorists \"chatter back and forth about the 'real rate produced',\" trading platitudes such as \"the price paid for the use of loanable capital should vary with the supply of such capital\" and \"a high rate and a low profit cannot permanently exist.\" These are tautologies dressed as laws. Custom and legal tradition are then admitted as genuine determinants: \"Custom, legal tradition, etc. are just as much involved in the determination of the average rate of interest as is competition itself, in so far as this average rate exists not only as an average number but as an actual magnitude. An average interest must be assumed in many legal contexts where interest has to be reckoned.\" The theoretical reason why no law can fix the limits of the average rate is then given: \"the answer simply lies in the nature of interest. It is merely a part of the average profit. The same capital appears in a double capacity, as capital for loan in the hands of the lender, and as industrial or commercial capital in the hands of the functioning capitalist. But it functions only once, and produces profit only once. In the production process itself, the character of capital as loan capital does not play any role.\" The division between the two claimants is therefore \"a purely empirical fact, pertaining to the realm of chance, just as the respective shares in the common profit of a business partnership are distributed among its various members.\" The contrast with other divisions is decisive. In the division between surplus-value and wages, \"two quite different elements are involved, labour-power and capital. It is the functions of two independent variables which set limits to one another, and the quantitative division of the value produced emerges from their qualitative distinction.\" The same structure governs the division of surplus-value between rent and profit. But \"with interest, there is nothing of that kind. Here, on the contrary, the qualitative distinction proceeds from the purely quantitative division of the same piece of surplus-value.\" This is the chapter's deepest theoretical claim, and it justifies Engels's manuscript note about the transition from quantity to quality. A related footnote (68) notes that the Bank of England raises and lowers its discount rate according to the inflow and outflow of gold, \"though always of course with regard to the rate prevailing in the open market,\" and that \"gambling in discounts, by anticipation of the alterations in the bank-rate, has now become half the trade of the great heads of the money centre\"—i.e. the London money market. 2.10 Interest as a Uniform, Palpable Magnitude: The Price Analogy The conclusion of the \"no natural rate\" argument contains a striking paradox. Although the average or middling rate of interest \"cannot be given limits by a general law,\" the rate of interest itself—\"whether it is the average rate or the market rate of the time\"—nonetheless \"appears as something quite different from the general rate of profit, as a uniform, definite and palpable magnitude.\" Steuart's observation (footnote 69) is cited: \"The price of money, which we designate by the term interest, has a greater stability and uniformity than that of any other thing,\" because cash money is always of the same value, or assumed to be so. Marx formalises the analogy: \"The rate of interest is related to the profit rate in a similar way as the market price of a commodity is to its value.\" Insofar as the interest rate is determined by the profit rate, \"this is always through the general rate of profit and not through the specific profit rates that may prevail in particular branches of industry, still less by the extra profit that the individual capitalist might make in a particular sphere of business.\" Massie's footnote (70) is invoked to show that the division of profits applies to lenders and borrowers in general : \"remarkably great and small gains are the reward of skill and the want of understanding, which lenders have nothing at all to do with; for as they will not suffer by the one, they ought not to benefit by the other.\" The lender who charges the common rate of interest is entitled to no more than that rate, whatever profits the borrower makes with the money—a principle that underlines the lender's abstraction from the productive employment of capital. Marx acknowledges that the interest rate differs according to the class of security provided by borrowers and the duration of the loan: \"but for each of these categories, it is uniform at a given moment of time. This distinction, therefore, does not militate against the fixed and uniform character of the rate of interest.\" Footnote 71 (added by Engels) supplies a striking empirical snapshot of the London money market on 9 December 1889: the bank rate stood at 5 per cent; the market rate of discount on 60-day drafts at 3½ per cent; on 3-month drafts 3¼ per cent; on 6-month drafts 3⅜ per cent; loans to bill-brokers day-to-day at 1–2 per cent; for one week at 3 per cent; last rate for the fortnight on loans to stockbrokers at 4½–5 per cent; deposit allowance (banks) at 3½ per cent; and (discount houses) at 3–3½ per cent. \"The minimum here is 1 per cent and the maximum 5 per cent\"—on one and the same day. The point: sharp differentiation by category coexists with a single, quotable, uniform rate per category. The average rate of interest is constant over long periods \"because the general rate of profit changes only in the long run—despite constant change in the particular rates of profit, a change in one sphere being offset by an opposite change in another.\" The relative constancy of the profit rate is reflected in the relative constancy of the average rate of interest. The market rate is fixed at any given moment \"just like the market price of commodities, because on the money market all capital for loan confronts the functioning capital as an overall mass; i.e. the relationship between the supply of loan capital on the one hand, and the demand for it on the other, is what determines the market level of interest at any given time.\" This is all the more true, the more the development and concentration of the credit system gives loan capital \"a general social character, and puts it on the money market all at once, simultaneously.\" By contrast, the general rate of profit \"only ever exists as a tendency, as a movement of equalization between particular rates of profit.\" The competition between capitalists consists in \"their withdrawing capital bit by bit from those spheres where profit is below the average for a long period, and similarly injecting it bit by bit into spheres where it is above this.\" There is \"a constant variation in the injection and withdrawal of capital vis-à-vis these various spheres, never a simultaneous effect on a mass scale as with the determination of the interest rate.\" The epistemic asymmetry is thus rooted in a real structural difference: interest is fixed by a market confrontation occurring en masse and simultaneously ; the general profit rate is a resultant of countless dispersed, staggered movements. 2.11 Interest-Bearing Capital as a Commodity Sui Generis Marx now draws the theoretical conclusion from the preceding analysis. Interest-bearing capital, although \"a category absolutely different from the commodity,\" becomes \"a commodity sui generis with interest as its price,\" and this price, like the market price of any commodity, \"is fixed at any given time by demand and supply.\" The market rate of interest, \"though in constant flux, thus appears at any given moment as every bit as fixed and uniform as the momentary market price of any commodity. The money capitalists supply this commodity, and the functioning capitalists buy it; they constitute the demand for it.\" At the same time, Marx insists on the disanalogy : \"This process of fixing the rate by supply and demand does not apply to the equalization that produces the general rate of profit.\" The equalisation of commodity prices to prices of production operates through \"the expansion or contraction of production, i.e. an increase or decrease in the quantity of commodities that these industrial capitals put on the market, mediated by the immigration or emigration of capital with respect to these particular spheres of production.\" It \"never appears and never can appear as if industrial or commercial capital as such were a commodity vis-à-vis a buyer, in the way that interest-bearing capital is.\" It appears only in the fluctuations that reduce market prices to production prices—\"not as the direct establishment of an average profit.\" The determinants of the general rate of profit are then enumerated systematically: \"(1) by the surplus-value that the total capital produces; (2) by the ratio of this surplus-value to the value of the total capital; and (3) by competition, but only in so far as this is the movement through which the capitals invested in particular spheres of production seek to draw equal dividends from this surplus-value in proportion to their relative size.\" Hence: \"The general rate of profit, in other words, obtains its determination in quite different and far more complicated ways than does the market rate of interest, which is directly and immediately determined by the relation of supply and demand, and it is therefore not a palpable and given fact in the way that the interest rate is.\" The particular profit rates in the various spheres are \"themselves more or less uncertain; but in so far as they do show themselves, it is not their uniformity that is apparent but rather their variation. The general rate of profit itself simply appears as the minimum limit of profit, not as an empirical and directly visible form of the actual profit rate.\" One crucial detail: the general profit rate appears as a minimum limit. This is because competition equalises such that capitals in any sphere will not permanently accept less than the general rate; the rate functions as a floor below which capital emigrates, not as an immediate description of what each capital actually realises. 2.12 Two Factors Favouring the Consolidation of the Interest Rate Marx isolates two factors that, \"in stressing this distinction between the interest rate and the profit rate,\" he had \"so far left aside.\" Both help explain why interest wears such a solid, traditional, palpable appearance. (1) The historical pre-existence of interest-bearing capital and the existence of a general rate of interest handed down by tradition. Interest-bearing capital is older than capitalist production proper. It existed in antiquity (usurer's capital) and in the medieval and early modern periods. This historical precedence means that a general rate of interest can be transmitted as a customary datum, quite independently of the current profit rate. (2) The far stronger direct influence that the world market exerts on the establishment of the interest rate , \"independently of the conditions of production in a country, as compared with its influence on the profit rate.\" The interest rate is directly sensitive to international movements of gold, exchange rates, and capital flows, whereas the profit rate remains anchored in national conditions of production. This gives interest an autonomy and international uniformity the profit rate lacks. 2.13 The Epistemic Asymmetry and the Money Market as Class Capital Marx now summarises the cognitive asymmetry. \"Average profit does not appear as a directly given fact, but rather as the end-product of an equalization of opposing tendencies that can only be established by investigation. With the interest rate it is different. Where it is a universal governing rule, which occurs at least locally, it is a fact fixed every day, a fact that even serves industrial and commercial capital as a presupposition and postulate in their operating calculations. It becomes a general property of any sum of £100 that it will yield 2, 3, 4, 5 per cent.\" The metaphor is memorable: \"Meteorological reports do not show more precisely the level of the barometer and thermometer than do stock-market reports the level of the interest rate, not for this capital or that, but rather for the generality of loan capital to be found on the money market.\" The reason for this precision lies in the nature of the money market. \"On the money market it is only lenders and borrowers who face one another. The commodity has the same form, money. All particular forms of capital, arising from its investment in particular spheres of production or circulation, are obliterated here. It exists in the undifferentiated, self-identical form of independent value, of money. Competition between particular spheres now ceases; they are all thrown together as borrowers of money, and capital confronts them all in a form still indifferent to the specific manner and mode of its application.\" This produces a remarkable theoretical result: \"Here capital really does emerge, in the pressure of its demand and supply, as the common capital of the class, whereas industrial capital appears like this only in the movement and competition between the particular spheres.\" On the money market, capital attains the form of class capital —capital abstracted from all particular employments, confronting the capitalist class as a whole in its money form. Money capital \"really does possess the form in which it is distributed as a common element among these various spheres, among the capitalist class, quite irrespective of its particular application, according to the production requirements of each particular sphere.\" This abstraction is further institutionalised with the development of large-scale industry: \"money capital emerges more and more, in so far as it appears on the market, as not represented by the individual capitalist, the proprietor of this or that fraction of the mass of capital on the market, but rather as a concentrated and organized mass, placed under the control of the bankers as representatives of the social capital in a quite different manner to real production.\" Consequently, \"as far as the form of demand goes, capital for loan is faced with the entire weight of a class, while, as far as supply goes, it itself appears en masse as loan capital.\" The chapter thus articulates, in compressed form, the theoretical foundation of the later analysis of bankers' capital, credit, and the socialisation of capital through the credit system. The money market is where capital becomes really social—not through production relations but through ownership in its most abstract form. 2.14 The Blurred Profit Rate and the Sharp Interest Rate; the Closing Note on Credit Marx concludes with a restatement of the epistemic contrast. \"These are some of the reasons why the general rate of profit presents a blurred and hazy picture compared with the sharply defined rate of interest, which although its level fluctuates always confronts the borrowers as fixed and given, because it fluctuates in the same way for them all.\" Two analogies emphasise the point. First, \"changes in the value of money do not prevent it from having the same value in relation to every commodity, and market prices of commodities fluctuate daily, although this does not prevent them from being noted every day in the reports.\" Second, \"It is just the same with the rate of interest, which is noted just as regularly as the 'price of money.'\" The reason: \"capital itself is offered here as a commodity in the money form. The establishment of its price is therefore the establishment of its market price, just as with all other commodities; and so the rate of interest presents itself always as a general rate of interest, as so much for so much money, as quantitatively determined.\" By contrast, \"The rate of profit... can vary even within the same sphere, given the same market price, according to the different conditions in which individual capitals produce the same commodity; for the profit rate on an individual capital is not determined simply by the market price of the commodity, but rather by the difference between market price and cost price. And these various rates of profit, firstly within the same sphere and then within the various different spheres, can be equalized only through constant fluctuations.\" Profit rates are individual, dispersed, dependent on cost conditions, and equalised only through ceaseless fluctuation; interest is uniform, quotable, and simultaneous. Marx closes the chapter with an unfinished note, marked \"(Note for later elaboration.) A particular form of credit.\" Three points are sketched. (1) \"We know that when money functions as means of payment instead of means of purchase, the commodity is alienated first and its value realized only later. If payment takes place only after the commodity has been re-sold, this sale does not appear as a consequence of the purchase, but rather it is by the sale that the purchase is realized. Sale, in other words, becomes a means of purchase.\" (2) \"certificates of debt, bills, etc. become means of payment for the creditor.\" (3) \"money is replaced by the settlement of outstanding debt certificates.\" These three observations sketch the germ of the credit system: the metamorphosis of the commodity that creates the seller as creditor, the circulation of debt certificates as means of payment, and the netting out of mutual debts that economises the use of money. They are the bridge to the later analysis of commercial credit, bank credit, and the credit system as a whole—an analysis Marx never fully completed. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Chapter 22 establishes a set of fundamental principles whose reach extends far beyond the technical question of the interest rate. The first is the primacy of the quantitative division in producing a qualitative distinction . In most economic distributions—surplus-value/wages, profit/rent—a qualitative difference between independent variables (labour-power vs. capital; capital vs. landed property) underlies and limits the quantitative split. In the case of interest, the reverse holds: the same capital functions once, produces profit once, and only afterwards is divided between two titles to it—the lender's and the functioning capitalist's. From this purely quantitative division emerges the qualitative appearance of interest as an independent revenue, a price, a thing. The chapter's theoretical punchline is precisely this inversion. It explains why interest can appear as a \"natural\" magnitude even though no law determines its level: because the division itself is arbitrary and empirical, the arbitrary result can congeal into the appearance of an objective price. The second principle is the reality of appearance . The absence of a natural rate does not mean that interest is not experienced as fixed and given. On the contrary, the interest rate is the most palpable magnitude in the whole economic system: quoted daily, uniform across the class of borrowers, presupposed in every operating calculation, used in legal contexts as a fixed datum. Marx's contrast with the general rate of profit—the latter a mere tendency, an abstraction of investigation, a blurred resultant—shows that epistemic clarity and theoretical determinacy can diverge sharply. The more determinate, quantifiable, and palpable a form appears, the less it is actually determined by general laws. The chapter thereby offers a general methodological lesson: apparent precision may be the mask of substantive indeterminacy. Third, the chapter identifies the dual determination of interest. Its maximum is set by the general rate of profit (through the profit mass to be divided); its actual level, in any given period, is set by competition between lenders and borrowers, by custom and legal tradition, by the supply of loanable capital generated by rentiers and the credit system, and by world-market conditions. This dual determination explains both the regularity of interest behaviour across the industrial cycle and its independence from profit in the long run. It also explains why the tendency of the rate of profit to fall may or may not be reflected in the interest rate—an argument Marx carefully qualifies. Fourth, the chapter develops a secular theory of the falling interest rate grounded in the growth of a rentier class and in the socialisation of savings by the credit system. This supply-side theory complements, and in principle can counteract, the profit-based determination. It anticipates modern theories of the declining \"rate of return on capital\" arising from accumulation without commensurate investment opportunities, and it grounds them in class structure and institutional development rather than in technological or psychological factors. Fifth, the chapter prepares the theory of fictitious capital and crisis . The observations on securities losing value during crises, on bankers' speculative purchases at knockdown prices, and on low interest rates coexisting with fraud (\"the railway swindle of summer 1844\") point forward to Marx's mature account of credit cycles, in which interest spikes, asset prices collapse, and wealth transfers from debtors to holders of liquidity. The broader implications are both critical and constructive. Critically, the chapter demolishes every attempt to naturalise interest: the \"eternal laws\" of Opdyke, the \"primordial forest rate\" of Arnd, and the bankers' platitudes of the parliamentary reports are all shown to substitute tautology and metaphor for analysis. Interest has no natural magnitude because it is a distributional fact rooted in property titles, not a production coefficient. Constructively, the chapter lays the foundation for the theory of the credit system, the analysis of profit of enterprise, and—ultimately—the critique of the trinity formula in which capital appears as a self-generating source of revenue. In interest-bearing capital (M—M′), capital appears to generate money without any mediation by production: the money capitalist lends, and money returns augmented. This is the most fetishised form of capital, and the most ideologically potent. Chapter 22's demonstration that interest is only a part of profit, and that its level is empirically and institutionally determined, is the first essential step in dissolving that fetish—showing that what appears as a natural property of a thing (capital bearing interest) is in fact a social relation of distribution resting on the class division between owners of money capital and functioning capitalists, and ultimately on the exploitation of unpaid labour. The autonomy of interest is real as an appearance and as a practical datum , but it is an autonomy grounded in—and subordinated to—the fundamental relation between capital and labour. That is Marx's lasting lesson in this deceptively compact chapter. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 21 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 23"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 23", "title": "Chapter 23", "subtitle": "Interest and Profit of Enterprise", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 23/Chapter 23 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 10910, "read_time": "55 min read", "tags": ["Interest-Bearing Capital & Loan Capital", "The Trinity Formula & Class Relations"], "keywords": ["Interest and Profit of Enterprise", "Wages of Superintendence and Managerial Apologetics", "Capitalist as Mere Functionary vs Passive Money Owner", "Joint-Stock Companies Separating Ownership from Management", "Transformation of Capitalist into Mere Rentier"], "text": "Chapter 23 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 10,785 words ⏱️ ~54 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 23: Interest and Profit of Enterprise — Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Core Problem and Intellectual Objective Chapter 23 of Capital , Volume 3, occupies the pivotal position within Part V — \"The Division of Profit into Interest and Profit of Enterprise.\" The preceding two chapters had already established, in a formal-analytical sense, that interest is nothing but a portion of the surplus-value (profit) that the functioning capitalist — whether industrialist or merchant — must surrender to the owner-lender of capital whenever the capital he uses is not his own but borrowed. With that foundation laid, Chapter 23 confronts its genuinely difficult question: not the magnitude of the division, but its form . How does a division of profit that is at bottom purely quantitative — a split of one sum into two shares — harden into a qualitative distinction between two apparently different kinds of revenue, each seemingly springing from a different source, governed by different laws, and attached to different \"capacities\" of capital? This is the axis on which the entire chapter turns. Marx states the question with surgical precision: \"How does this purely quantitative division of profit into net profit and interest turn into a qualitative distinction?\" And, more sharply: how does it happen that even the capitalist who works exclusively with his own capital nonetheless comes to class a portion of his gross profit under the special heading of \"interest,\" treating it as though he were paying it to himself — so that all capital, borrowed or not, comes to be distinguished as interest-bearing capital from itself in its function as capital bearing a net profit? Marx's central thesis is that this \"ossification\" and \"autonomization\" of the two parts of gross profit is not a subjective illusion or mere bookkeeping convention. It rests on an objective fact : interest genuinely accrues to the mere owner of capital who stands outside the production process, while profit of enterprise [ Unternehmergewinn ] genuinely accrues to the merely functioning capitalist who is not the owner. Once this duality of real economic roles exists — once money capitalists face industrial capitalists as two distinct classes with distinct legal titles to the same capital and its yield — the quantitative split converts into a qualitative opposition, and this opposition then generalizes to the entire capitalist class and the total social capital. The whole profit on any capital, and hence the average profit itself, breaks down into two qualitatively different, mutually autonomous parts — interest and profit of enterprise — each obeying its own particular laws. The Deeper Objective: Unmasking the \"Wages of Superintendence\" Illusion Yet the chapter is not merely an exercise in the logic of capital categories. Its second, and polemically more explosive, objective is to dismantle the ideological form that this qualitative division breeds in the mind of the capitalist: the notion that profit of enterprise is really a wage — \"wages of superintendence of labour,\" a higher wage paid for the capitalist's own managerial exertion, complex labour by a self-paying employer. Marx's task here is to demonstrate that this \"wage\" theory of profit is not a description of economic reality but a fetish generated by the antithetical form the two parts of profit assume vis-à-vis one another . Because interest appears as the fruit of mere ownership (property in capital abstracted from any function), the residual — profit of enterprise — appears as the fruit of function alone, of the capitalist's labour . From there it is a short, ideologically laden step to the claim that the capitalist earns his reward as a worker, indeed as \"complex\" labour commanding a higher wage. Marx systematically exposes this as a rationalization whose apologetic function is completed by two historical proofs: the slaveholder's use of the identical argument (O'Conor's \"just compensation for the labour and talent employed in governing\"), and the empirical fact that cooperative factories and joint-stock companies separate managerial wages from profit of enterprise — demonstrating that the capitalist as proprietor is superfluous to production. Placement within the Broader Theoretical Framework The chapter belongs to the larger architecture of Volume 3, in which Marx analyzes how surplus-value — the single, undifferentiated social total extracted from living labour — assumes the \"transformed forms\" of profit, average profit, rent, and interest. Chapter 23 is the hinge between the treatment of credit and interest-bearing capital (Chapters 21–22) and the treatment of the credit system, banking, and the historical genesis of interest-bearing capital (Chapters 24 onward). It presupposes the theory of the rate of profit derived from surplus-value, the equalization of profit rates through competition, and — crucially — the distinction between \"capital as property\" and \"capital as function.\" It also depends on the historical observation that interest-bearing capital is older than industrial capital: it exists \"as a ready-made form handed down\" long before the capitalist mode of production, which is precisely why interest so readily appears to the popular mind as \"capital as such, capital par excellence .\" The chapter thereby bridges Marx's analytical theory of profit with his historical and ideological critique of political economy (Massie, Hume, Ramsay, Tooke, Bosanquet, Smith, Ure, Hodgskin, Mill), and sets the stage for understanding how the credit system's later development makes \"the capitalist vanish from the production process as someone superfluous.\" 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 Re-establishing the Premise: Interest as a Share of Profit, and the Functioning Capitalist's Inability to Dispose of Capital Marx opens by restating the governing premise carried over from the two preceding chapters. Interest, he insists, \"originally appears, originally is, and remains in reality nothing but a part of the profit, i.e. the surplus-value,\" which the functioning capitalist (industrial or merchant) must pay to the owner-lender whenever the capital used is borrowed. When the capitalist works with his own capital, there is no division at all — the entire profit belongs to him. Marx draws a crucial inference: \"in so far as the owners of capital use it themselves in the reproduction process, they do not compete together to determine the interest rate,\" and thus \"the category of interest — which is impossible without the establishment of a rate of interest — lies outside the movement of industrial capital itself.\" This is a methodological bombshell. Interest, for Marx, is not an intrinsic \"department\" of industrial capital's own motion. It emerges only from a social division of the capitalist class itself into money capitalists and industrial capitalists. He reinforces this with a quotation from Thomas Tooke ( History of Prices , 1838): the rate of interest is a proportional sum the lender is content to receive and borrower to pay; but when the owner employs his capital actively in reproduction, \"he does not come under the head of those capitalists, the proportion of whom, to the number of borrowers, determines the rate of interest.\" Hence Marx's formula: it is only the division of capitalists into money capitalists and industrial capitalists that transforms a part of profit into interest and creates the category of interest at all; and only the competition between these two kinds of capitalist that creates the rate of interest. He then deepens the point by examining a subtle datum: the functioning capitalist, even when he owns his capital, does not have at his disposal the capital itself as a private person — only the profit he can spend as revenue. As long as his capital functions, it belongs to the reproduction process and is \"tied down in it.\" Ownership does not grant him the freedom to dispose of it otherwise so long as it is used as capital for the exploitation of labour . The same holds symmetrically for the money capitalist : as long as his capital is lent out and operates as money capital bearing interest, he cannot dispose of the principal. Marx makes this vivid — if he lends for a year he receives interest at fixed dates without repayment of principal; but even repayment makes no difference , because to continue operating as capital it \"must always be lent out afresh.\" While it sits in his hands it bears no interest and does not operate as capital; once it bears interest it is no longer in his hands. From this follows \"the possibility of lending capital in perpetuity.\" 2.2 The Tooke–Bosanquet Quarrel: The Falsity of \"Repayment\" as a Barrier to Convergence This theoretical point about perpetual lending becomes the ground on which Marx adjudicates a real controversy among his contemporaries. Bosanquet ( Metallic, Paper, and Credit Currency , 1842) had claimed that if the rate of interest fell as low as 1 per cent, \"capital borrowed would be placed nearly on a par with capital possessed.\" Tooke objected that Bosanquet overlooked \"the condition of repayment\" — a borrower must eventually return the principal, so borrowed capital can never truly equal possessed capital. Marx's verdict is blunt: Tooke is \"completely false.\" Marx's reasoning is elegant. If interest were zero , the industrial capitalist who borrowed capital would be in the same position as the one working with his own capital; both would pocket the same average profit and their capital would operate as capital only by producing a profit. The requirement of repayment would make no difference here. And the closer the rate of interest approaches zero — say 1 per cent — the more borrowed capital stands \"on the same level\" as capital actually owned. As long as money capital is to continue existing as money capital , it must be lent out afresh at the prevailing rate to the same class of industrial and commercial capitalists. The distinction between the borrower and the owner — as long as both function as capitalists — reduces to a single quantitative difference: one pockets the whole profit p ; the other pockets p − i (profit minus interest). As i → 0, p − i → p . Moreover, the objection \"one has to pay back his capital and borrow anew\" is void, because the owner equally \"must similarly advance it afresh each time to the production process, and has no control over it that is independent of this process.\" The sole remaining distinction is the legal one: \"one is the proprietor of his capital and the other is not.\" Here we can isolate the chapter's working mathematical notation, which recurs throughout: p = gross profit; i = interest; p − i = profit of enterprise. The central dialectical operation the chapter performs is to show that this purely arithmetical subtraction is pregnant with a qualitative form. 2.3 The Central Question and the Counter-Example of \"Associates\" Marx now poses the question explicitly (see §1 above) and immediately guards against a false generalization. Not every quantitative division of profit becomes a qualitative one. Consider several industrial capitalists who form an association, pursue a business jointly, and divide the profit under legally established provisions. These associates reckon their shares only as individual shares — they do not split the total into \"my individual profit\" plus \"company profit for my non-existent associates .\" The mere multiplication of legal owners (a \"quantitative division when the owner happens to consist of several legal persons\") produces no qualitative split. The division must therefore have a different origin than the simple plurality of claimants. 2.4 The Real Starting Point: The Double Movement of the Same Capital To find that origin, Marx insists we must \"proceed from the assumption that the money capitalist and productive capitalist actually do come face to face — not just as legally separate persons but as persons who play quite different roles in the reproduction process, or in whose hands the same capital really does go through a double and completely different movement .\" One merely lends the capital; the other applies it productively . This is the real starting point [ Ausgangspunkt ] of interest formation. The same sum of value undergoes two distinct \"movements\" — a lending movement (outside production, bearing interest) and a functioning movement (inside production, yielding a profit) — and this objective bifurcation of the movement of one capital, when it is held by two different agents, is what can generate a qualitative distinction. 2.5 How Gross Profit Breaks Down for the Borrower: Interest as Presupposition For the productive capitalist working with borrowed capital, the gross profit breaks into (a) the interest he must pay the lender and (b) the excess above that interest, which forms his own share. Here Marx introduces a critical causal asymmetry: if the general rate of profit is given, the borrower's share is determined by the rate of interest; if the rate of interest is given, it is determined by the profit rate. More decisively, however much the gross profit may diverge from the average profit in any single case, the lender's share is fixed by the general rate of interest — and, crucially, this is presupposed in advance, before the production process begins, i.e. before its result (the gross profit) is obtained. Interest is therefore a contractually and socially pre-given magnitude ; profit of enterprise is a residual . This temporal and logical priority of interest over the resulting profit is the seed of its \"qualitative\" appearance. 2.6 The Genesis of Unternehmergewinn (Profit of Enterprise) Marx then traces the subjective form this objective structure generates. The specific product of capital is surplus-value, and at a further remove, profit. But for the capitalist working with borrowed capital, the part of profit remaining after interest is not profit — it is \"profit minus interest.\" It is this residual that \"necessarily appears to him as the product of capital in its actual functioning .\" And this is not mere appearance: it is so for him, because he represents capital only as functioning capital — \"he is its personification in so far as it functions,\" which it does when profitably invested and when he performs the operations the business prescribes. In opposition to the interest he must pay , the remaining profit \"necessarily assumes the form of industrial or commercial profit\" — or, using the German expression that embraces both, profit of enterprise [ Unternehmergewinn ] . Its determination: if gross profit equals average profit, the size of profit of enterprise is exclusively determined by the rate of interest. If gross profit diverges from average profit, the divergence (after interest is deducted on both sides) is determined by \"all the conjunctures that give rise to such a temporary divergence\" — differences in a particular sphere's profit rate versus the general rate, or an individual capitalist's profit versus his sphere's average. 2.7 The Conjunctural Sources of Divergence and the Role of \"Cunning and Perseverance\" Marx enlarges on the determinants of the profit rate within production: it depends not only on surplus-value but on the purchase prices of means of production, on methods more than averagely productive, on economizing constant capital, etc. And, leaving aside the price of production, it depends on the state of the trade cycle and, \"with each individual business deal,\" on \"the greater or lesser cunning and perseverance of the capitalist,\" whether and how far he buys or sells below or above the production price and thereby appropriates a greater or lesser share of the total surplus-value in the circulation process. In each case the quantitative division of gross profit is transformed into a qualitative one — \"all the more so in that the quantitative division itself depends on what is to be divided, how the active capitalist looks after his capital and what gross profit it yields him as functioning capital.\" Here Marx quietly introduces an important variable: the borrower's own activity (his entrepreneurship) shapes the divisible magnitude itself, reinforcing the sense that the residual reward attaches to his function . 2.8 The Objective Basis of the Qualitative Distinction Assuming the functioning capitalist is not the owner, property in capital is represented to him by the lender. The interest he pays thus appears as \"a part of the gross profit that accrues to property in capital as such .\" By contrast, the part falling to the active capitalist, as profit of enterprise , appears to derive \"exclusively from the operations or functions that he performs with the capital in the reproduction process, especially therefore the functions that he performs as an entrepreneur in industry or trade.\" To him, then: Interest = the mere fruit of property in capital , of \"capital in itself, abstracted from the reproduction process in so far as it does not 'work'.\" Profit of enterprise = the exclusive fruit of functions , of \"capital's movement and process,\" which appears \"as his own activity, in contrast to the non-activity and non-participation of the money capitalist.\" Crucially, Marx stresses that this qualitative separation is not merely the subjective conception of either party. \"It rests on objective fact \": interest accrues to the money capitalist, \"who is simply the owner of the capital and thus does represent mere property in capital before the production process and outside it,\" while profit of enterprise accrues to the merely functioning capitalist, \"who is not the owner of capital.\" This objectivity is the linchpin holding the entire analysis together — and, as we shall see, it is also what makes the resulting ideology so powerful. 2.9 Ossification and Autonomization: \"Mutual Ossification\" and the Generalized Split Marx now generalizes. For both the industrial capitalist working with borrowed capital and the money capitalist who does not apply his capital himself, the merely quantitative division between two persons with different legal titles \"turns into a qualitative distinction.\" One part of profit now appears \"in and of itself\" as the fruit accruing to capital in one capacity — as interest; the other appears as \"a specific fruit of capital in an opposite capacity\" — as profit of enterprise: \"one as the simple fruit of property in capital, the other as the fruit of merely functioning with capital, as the fruit of capital as capital in process.\" Marx calls this \" mutual ossification and autonomization \" — the two parts harden into thing-like forms, \"as if they derived from two essentially separate sources.\" And this must now \"be fixed for the entire capitalist class and the total capital.\" The decisive extension follows: this holds irrespective of whether the functioning capitalist's capital is borrowed or not, and irrespective of whether the money capitalist uses his own capital himself. The capitalist who works with his own capital also divides his gross profit into: - interest accruing to him \"as owner, as lender of his own capital to himself,\" and - profit of enterprise, accruing to him \"as an active, functioning capitalist.\" It becomes \"a matter of indifference, as far as this division is concerned, whether the capitalist really does have to share with another or not.\" The owner-operator \"breaks down into two persons, the mere owner of capital and its user\"; and his capital itself breaks down into \"owned capital, capital outside the production process, which yields an interest, and capital in the production process, which yields profit of enterprise as capital in process.\" This is the heart of the chapter's transformation thesis: the single self-identical capital is internally doubled into \"capital as property\" and \"capital as function.\" 2.10 Why the Division Becomes Qualitative for the Whole Capitalist Class: The Three (and a Fourth) Grounds Marx pauses to enumerate why this qualitative division generalizes. First ground — the empirical prevalence of mixed financing. The majority of industrial capitalists operate with both their own and borrowed capital, in changing ratios across periods. Because nearly everyone straddles the line, the interest/profit-of-enterprise distinction is a lived, everyday reality for the class as a whole. Second ground — the antithetical constitution of profit of enterprise by interest. Once a part of gross profit is ossified as interest, \"the transformation of a part of the gross profit into the form of interest transforms its other part into profit of enterprise.\" Profit of enterprise is, \"in point of fact, only the antithetical form that the excess of gross profit over interest assumes as soon as the latter exists as a category of its own.\" The general question — how gross profit differentiates into interest and profit of enterprise — thus reduces to the question: how does a part of gross profit become invariably ossified and autonomized as interest? Here Marx makes a momentous historical observation: interest-bearing capital \"exists as a ready-made form handed down,\" and interest \"as a ready-made subordinate form of the surplus-value produced by capital, long before the capitalist mode of production and the conceptions of capital and profit corresponding to it come into existence.\" In the popular mind, therefore, money capital / interest-bearing capital \"is still seen as capital as such, capital par excellence .\" Hence the belief (prevailing \"down to Massie's time\") that it is money as such that is paid interest. The fact that loan capital yields interest whether or not it is applied as capital — even if borrowed only for consumption — confirms the conception of its total independence. The \"best proof\" of interest's independent form vs. profit, and of interest-bearing capital vs. industrial capital, in the earliest periods of the capitalist mode, is that it was discovered only in the middle of the eighteenth century — by Massie and subsequently Hume — that interest is just one part of gross profit. That such a \"discovery\" was needed at all attests to how thoroughly interest had already been reified. Third ground — the independent existence of the class of money capitalists. Whether the industrial capitalist uses his own or borrowed capital, \"the class of money capitalists confronts him as a special kind of capitalist, money capital as an autonomous kind of capital, and interest as the separate form of surplus-value that corresponds to this specific capital.\" Fourth ground — (a gap in the manuscript). Marx left the fourth point blank. The chapter records only \"(a gap in the manuscript)\" — a telling editorial trace of the work-in-progress character of Volume 3 (the manuscript was edited by Engels, whose footnotes appear as \"F.E.\"). 2.11 The Qualitative and Quantitative Definitions of Interest Marx crystallizes interest on two axes. Qualitatively , interest is \"the surplus-value supplied by capital as simple ownership, which capital yields in itself , even if its owner remains outside the reproduction process; surplus-value therefore yielded in separation from its process.\" Quantitatively , the part of profit forming interest \"seems to be related not to industrial and commercial capital as such but rather to money capital,\" and the interest rate confirms this relationship. This confirmation is twofold: (1) the rate of interest, \"despite its dependence on the general rate of profit, is separately determined\"; (2) it appears — \"just like the market price of commodities\" — \"as something hard and fast, for all its changes: a palpable and always given relationship as opposed to the intangible rate of profit.\" This solidity of the palpable interest rate, contrasted with the elusive, abstract profit rate, is what fixes interest in consciousness as a self-standing thing. Marx then states the negative condition of the whole category: \"If all capital were to be found in the hands of industrial capitalists, there would be no interest and no rate of interest.\" The independent form that the quantitative division assumes is precisely what produces the qualitative distinction. Comparing the industrial capitalist to the money capitalist: the industrialist is distinguished by profit of enterprise — the surplus of gross profit over average interest — and the rate of interest makes this appear as \"an empirically given quantity.\" Comparing him to the industrial capitalist operating with his own capital, the latter differs from him \"as a money capitalist simply in so far as he pockets the interest himself instead of paying it out.\" From both sides, the part of gross profit distinct from interest appears to him as profit of enterprise, and \"interest itself appears as a surplus-value that capital yields in and of itself and which it would therefore yield even without productive application.\" 2.12 The Individual-Capitalist Validity vs. the Social Absurdity Marx then performs one of his characteristic moves: he affirms the individual capitalist's viewpoint as practically correct for him , then annihilates it when elevated to the whole social capital. For the individual capitalist , it is \"in practice correct\": he genuinely has \"the choice between lending his capital out as interest-bearing capital or valorizing it himself as productive capital,\" regardless of whether it begins as money capital or must first be transformed into it. Taken generally — applied to total social capital — the notion is \"of course quite absurd.\" It is \"utter nonsense,\" Marx thunders, \"to suggest that all capital could be transformed into money capital without the presence of people to buy and valorize the means of production, i.e. the form in which the entire capital exists,\" apart from the relatively small part actually in money. Concealed is an even greater absurdity: \"that capital could yield interest on the basis of the capitalist mode of production without functioning as productive capital , i.e. without creating surplus-value, of which interest is simply one part; that the capitalist mode of production could proceed on its course without capitalist production .\" He adds a dynamic refutation: if an inappropriately large number of capitalists sought to transform their capital into money capital, the result would be \"a tremendous devaluation of money capital and a tremendous fall in the rate of interest,\" and many would find themselves unable to live on interest, \"compelled to turn themselves back into industrial capitalists.\" These vulgar economists (whom Marx castigates for taking the individual's calculus as the basis of profit itself) thereby commit the fallacy of composition in its purest form. Yet — and this is the dialectical double-edge — \"for the individual capitalist this is in fact how it is.\" Hence even the self-financing capitalist necessarily treats the portion of his average profit equal to average interest as \"the fruit of his capital as such, leaving aside the production process,\" and treats the excess as profit of enterprise. Schema (with p = average profit, i = average interest, e = profit of enterprise): p = i + e → the capitalist experiences i as \"property's yield\" and e = p − i as \"enterprise's yield.\" 2.13 Recapitulation and the Relational Nature of the Two Forms Marx recapitulates: the part of profit the functioning capitalist pays to the mere owner becomes, through generalization, the separate form ( interest ) that all capital yields as such, borrowed or not. Its magnitude depends on the average rate of interest. Its origin is visible only in the fact that the functioning capitalist, insofar as he is owner of his own capital , \"does not compete — at least not actively — in determining the rate of interest.\" The purely quantitative division between two persons with different legal titles has become \"a qualitative distinction that seems to arise from the very nature of capital and profit.\" And the mechanism is relational : as soon as a part of profit assumes the form of interest, the difference between average profit and interest is transformed into the antithetical form of profit of enterprise. Marx is emphatic that these two forms \"exist only in their antithesis.\" They are \"neither of them related to the surplus-value, of which they are simply parts, under different categories, titles or names, but rather related to each other .\" Thus: \"It is because one part of profit has been turned into interest that the other part accordingly appears as profit of enterprise.\" This is a functionalist, relational account of economic forms — forms defined positionally, not by intrinsic content. Marx inserts a methodological note: by \"profit\" he always means average profit , since divergences (individual profit, or profit across spheres — \"variations in the division of the average profit or surplus-value that swing back and forth with the competitive struggle\") are \"quite immaterial\" to the investigation. This bracketing allows the analysis to isolate form from fluctuation. 2.14 Interest as \"Net Profit\" (Ramsay) and the Ontology of Capital-as-Property Interest, Marx says, following Ramsay , is the \"net profit\" yielded by property in capital as such — whether to the mere lender outside reproduction or to the owner who employs his capital productively himself. But the latter receives it \"not in so far as he is a functioning capitalist but rather as a money capitalist, the lender of his own capital as interest-bearing capital to himself as functioning capitalist .\" This reflexive formulation — capital lending to itself in its two capacities — is the conceptual core of the autonomous doubling. Marx then embeds interest in his deepest ontological claim about capital. Just as the transformation of money and value into capital is the constant result of the capitalist production process, so its existence as capital is the constant presupposition of that process. Through its capacity to be transformed into means of production, capital \"always commands unpaid labour and hence transforms the production and circulation process of commodities into the production of surplus-value for its possessor.\" Therefore interest \"simply expresses the fact that value in general — objectified labour in its general social form — value that assumes the form of means of production in the actual production process, confronts living labour-power as an autonomous power and is the means of appropriating unpaid labour ; and that it is this power in so far as it confronts the worker as the property of another .\" This is the social essence of capital as a class relation, now sedimented in the form of interest. 2.15 The Obliteration of the Antithesis to Wage-Labour in the Form of Interest Here is one of the chapter's most profound dialectical points. In the form of interest , the antithesis to wage-labour is obliterated . Why? Because interest-bearing capital as such \"does not have wage-labour as its opposite but rather functioning capital.\" The lending capitalist directly confronts \"the capitalist actually functioning in the reproduction process,\" not \"the wage-labourer who is expropriated from the means of production precisely on the basis of capitalist production.\" Marx's formula: \" Interest-bearing capital is capital as property as against capital as function. But if capital does not function, it does not exploit workers and does not come into opposition with labour. \" The exploitation relation—the beating heart of capital—is displaced out of the interest form entirely. In interest, capital appears as a relation between capitalists , with no visible connection to labour. 2.16 Profit of Enterprise: Antithesis with Interest, Not with Labour Symmetrically, profit of enterprise does not form an antithesis with wage-labour but with interest. Marx gives two grounds: First , taking average profit as given, the rate of profit of enterprise is determined \"not by wages but rather by the rate of interest\" — high or low in inverse proportion to the latter. He footnotes Ramsay ( Essay on the Distribution of Wealth , p. 214): \"The profits of enterprise depend upon the net profits of capital, not the latter upon the former.\" Marx glosses: \"For Ramsay, net profits always means interest.\" (We can formalize: given p , then e = p − i , hence ∂e/∂i < 0 — profit of enterprise varies inversely with interest.) Second , the functioning capitalist derives his claim to profit of enterprise — and profit of enterprise itself — \"not from his ownership of capital but rather from the function of capital as opposed to the capacity in which it exists only as inert property.\" This antithesis \"directly emerges as soon as he operates with borrowed capital,\" where interest and profit of enterprise accrue to two different persons. Profit of enterprise arises \"from the function of capital in the reproduction process, i.e. as a result of the operations and activity by which the functioning capitalist mediates these functions of industrial and commercial capital.\" But Marx insists: \"it is no sinecure to be a representative of functioning capital, unlike the case with interest-bearing capital.\" On the basis of capitalist production, \"the capitalist directs both the production process and the circulation process. The exploitation of productive labour takes effort , whether he does this himself or has it done in his name by others.\" Therefore — and this is the ideological pivot — in opposition to interest, his profit of enterprise presents itself to him \"as independent of his property in capital and rather as the result of his functions as non-owner, as a worker .\" 2.17 The Birth of the \"Wages of Superintendence\" Illusion The capitalist \"inevitably gets the idea into his head\" that his profit of enterprise — \"very far from forming any antithesis with wage-labour and being only the unpaid labour of others — is rather itself a wage , ' wages of superintendence of labour ,' a higher wage than that of the ordinary wage-labourer,\" for two reasons: (1) because it is complex labour , and (2) because he himself pays the wages . Marx underscores the forgetting involved: \"That his function as a capitalist consists in producing surplus-value, i.e. unpaid labour, and in the most economical conditions at that, is completely forgotten.\" Why? Because \"interest accrues to the capitalist even if he does not perform any function as capitalist, but is simply the owner of capital; while profit of enterprise, on the other hand, accrues to the functioning capitalist even if he is not the owner of the capital with which he functions.\" In the face of this antithetical form, \"it is forgotten that both are simply parts of surplus-value and that such a division can in no way change its nature, its origin and its conditions of existence.\" 2.18 The Real Relationship: Capital Against Labour, Displaced Marx restores the suppressed truth. In the reproduction process, the functioning capitalist \"represents capital against the wage-labourers as the property of others ,\" and the money capitalist \"participates in the exploitation of labour as represented by the functioning capitalist.\" The active capitalist can exercise his function — have workers work for him, have means of production function as capital — only as representative of the means of production vis-à-vis the workers . But this foundational truth \"is forgotten in the face of the opposition between the function of capital in the reproduction process and mere ownership of capital outside the reproduction process.\" He then states the general principle: the form the two parts of profit assume as interest and profit of enterprise \"does not express any relationship with labour, because this relationship exists only between labour and profit, or rather surplus-value, as the sum of these two parts, their whole and their unity .\" The division presupposes profit as ready-made. If the capitalist is the actual owner, he pockets the entire profit; \"it is all the same for the worker whether this is what he does or whether he has to pay one part to a third party as the legal proprietor.\" The basis for the division of profit between two kinds of capitalist \"is thus transformed imperceptibly into the basis of existence of the profit or surplus-value to be divided\" — a classic case of a distributional relationship being mistaken for a generative one. Marx's lament: \"From the fact that interest confronts profit of enterprise, and vice versa, but neither confronts labour, it seems to follow that profit of enterprise plus interest, i.e. profit and consequently surplus-value, is derived — from what? From the antithetical form of its two parts! \" But profit \"is produced before this division takes place.\" 2.19 The Potentiality Analogy: Capital and Labour-Power Marx anticipates an objection: does the fact that interest-bearing capital proves itself only when the money lent is really transformed into capital and produces surplus-value rule out interest being \"its inherent property, independent of the production process\"? He answers via analogy. Labour-power proves its value-creating property only if it is activated and realized in the labour process; but \"this does not exclude it being potentially in itself already value-creating activity as a capacity, and as such it does not just arise from the process but is rather presupposed by it.\" It is bought as the ability to create value , and can be bought without being productively used (for personal services, etc.). \"The same with capital.\" It belongs to its borrower whether or not he uses it as capital; what he pays for is \"the surplus-value inherently contained in the commodity of capital as a potentiality .\" This is a subtle defense: interest is the price of a potentiality embodied in a commodity-relation, structurally parallel to the wage as the price of labour-power's capacity . 2.20 A Closer Look at Profit of Enterprise: The Production Process as \"Labour Process in General\" Marx now descends into the ideological mechanics. As \"capital ownership which possesses the capacity of command over the labour of others\" becomes fixed in interest, the other part — profit of enterprise — \"necessarily appears as if it does not derive from capital as capital, but rather from the production process independently of its specific social determination.\" But \"the production process, when separated from capital, is simply the labour process in general .\" Therefore the industrial capitalist, as distinct from the owner , \"appears therefore not as functioning capital but rather as a functionary independent of capital, as a simple bearer of the labour process in general ; as a worker, and a wage-worker at that.\" Contrast: interest expresses \"precisely the existence of the conditions of labour as capital, in their social antithesis to labour and their transformation into personal powers vis-à-vis labour and over labour.\" Interest \"represents mere ownership of capital as a means of appropriating the product of other people's labour.\" But — the crucial twist — \"it represents this character of capital as something that falls to it outside the production process and is in no way the result of the specifically capitalist character of this production process itself.\" It presents capital's exploitative character \"not in direct antithesis to labour, but, on the contrary, with no relationship to labour at all , merely as a relationship between one capitalist and another.\" Thus interest is \"a capacity that is external and indifferent to the actual relationship between capital and labour.\" In interest, capital's antithetical character \"acquires an autonomous expression\" — but \"in such a way that this antithesis is completely obliterated in this expression and completely abstracted from it. Interest is a relationship between two capitalists, not between capitalist and worker. \" The mirror image: this form of interest gives the other part of profit \"the qualitative form of profit of enterprise, and subsequently of wages of superintendence.\" The capitalist's specific functions \"fall to his part precisely as distinct from the workers and in opposition to them,\" yet are \"presented as simply functions of labour.\" He obtains surplus-value \"not because he works as a capitalist but rather because, leaving aside his capacity as a capitalist, he also works .\" Thus \"this part of surplus-value is therefore no longer surplus-value at all, but rather its opposite, the equivalent for labour performed .\" Marx's stunning formulation: \"Since the estranged character of capital, its antithesis to labour, is shifted outside the actual process of exploitation, i.e. into interest-bearing capital, this process of exploitation itself appears as simply a labour process , in which the functioning capitalist simply performs different work from that of the workers. The labour of exploiting and the labour exploited are identical, both being labour. \" The social form of capital devolves on interest (neutral, indifferent form); the economic function of capital devolves on profit of enterprise, stripped of its specifically capitalist character. The reader should note the structural parallel Marx draws: exactly the same mystification occurs here \"in the consciousness of the capitalist\" as with the \"grounds of compensation\" discussed earlier (Part Two) regarding the equalization of the average profit — where grounds determining the division of surplus-value are turned into grounds for the existence and justification of profit as such. 2.21 The Manager: Where a Part of Profit Genuinely Separates Off as Wages The \"wages of superintendence\" idea finds \"further support\" in a real fact: \"one part of the profit actually can be separated off as wages, and really does separate off; or rather, a part of wages, conversely... appears as an integral component of profit.\" As Adam Smith already rightly realized , this part presents itself in pure, independent form — separate both from profit in general and from profit of enterprise — in the payment of a manager in branches where scale permits sufficient division of labour for a special salary. Marx footnotes Say's sayings and generally positions Smith as having grasped this genuinely. 2.22 The Two Forms of Supervisory Labour Marx distinguishes two forms of supervision/management labour: First — the cooperative/technical form. \"In all labour where many individuals cooperate, the interconnection and unity of the process is necessarily represented in a governing will, and in functions that concern not the detailed work but rather the workplace and its activity as a whole, as with the conductor of an orchestra .\" This is \"productive labour that has to be performed in any combined mode of production.\" It is independent of the specifically capitalist, exploitative relation. Second — the antagonistic/domination form. \"Quite apart from the commercial department,\" this work of supervision \"necessarily arises in all modes of production that are based on opposition between the worker as direct producer and the proprietor of the means of production .\" The greater the opposition, the greater the role supervision plays. \"It reaches its high point in the slave system .\" Marx cites Cairnes ( The Slave Power , 1862): \"Superintendence is here [the peasant proprietor] completely dispensed with\" — and, for dispersed slave labour, \"the number of overseers... will be proportionately increased.\" But supervision is also \"indispensable in the capitalist mode of production, since here too the production process is at the same time a process of consumption of labour-power by the capitalist.\" 2.23 The Political Analogy: Despotic States Marx extends the analogy to politics: \"In despotic states, too, the work of supervision and all-round intervention of the government involves both aspects: the performance of those common tasks that arise from the nature of all communities, and the specific functions that arise from the opposition between the government and the mass of the people.\" The writers of antiquity, having the slave system in mind, found the two aspects \"coincide as inseparably as they do with the modern economists who view the capitalist mode of production as the absolute mode.\" And — the polemical sting — \"the apologists of the modern slave system know just as well how to use supervisory work as a justificatory ground for slavery as other economists do for the wage-labour system.\" 2.24 Historical Evidence I: The Villicus of Cato's Time Marx documents the ancient rationalization of supervision. Drawing on Mommsen ( Römische Geschichte , 1856), he describes the villicus (from villa ) — the manager of the familia rustica , who \"had to take and spend, buy and sell; he received his instructions from the master, and gave both orders and punishment in his absence.\" The villicus \"of course had more freedom than the other slaves.\" The Magonian books (Carthaginian agricultural treatises, translated into Latin by order of the Roman senate, serving as official model for rational slave-based agriculture) advised he be permitted to marry and raise children and keep his own money; Cato said he should be married to the female manager. \"He alone had any prospect of obtaining his freedom, as a reward from the master for good behaviour.\" Every slave, including the manager, \"received his necessities at the master's expense at definite intervals and in fixed amounts.\" And: \"The quantity depended on the work, so that the manager, for instance, whose work was lighter than the other slaves, received a smaller ration .\" Note the inverted logic: the supervisory \"wage\" is smaller in kind because the work is lighter — a datum Marx deploys against the \"wages of superintendence\" as reward for superior/heavier labour. 2.25 Historical Evidence II: Aristotle on Domination and Supervision Marx quotes Aristotle ( De republica , Book I, ch. 7) in Greek with translation: \"For the master\" — Marx glosses: the capitalist — \"proves himself such not by obtaining slaves\" — ownership of capital, which gives him the power to buy labour — \"but by employing slaves\" — using labourers, nowadays wage-labourers, in the production process . \"There is nothing great or sublime about this science... but whatever the slave is to perform, the master must be able to order.\" And: \"Whenever the masters are not compelled to plague themselves with supervision, the overseer assumes this honour, while the masters pursue public affairs or philosophy.\" Marx's interpretation: Aristotle says \"in very blunt terms\" that domination, \"in the economic domain as well as in the political, imposes on those in power the functions of dominating,\" so that in the economic domain \"they must know how to consume labour-power.\" And Aristotle adds \"that this supervisory work is not a matter of very great moment , which is why the master leaves the 'honour' of this drudgery to an overseer as soon as he is wealthy enough.\" Marx thus turns Aristotle into a witness against the pretension that supervision is great or sublime labour. 2.26 The Ideological Function: Making Exploitation Into a \"Wage\" Marx draws the general conclusion. This work of management and supervision, \"in so far as it is not simply a particular function arising from the nature of all combined social labour, but arises rather from the opposition between the owner of the means of production and the owner of mere labour-power\" — whether the labour-power is bought with the worker (slavery) or the worker sells it himself, so that the production process \"appears at the same time as a process of consumption of labour by capital\" — \"this function arising from the servitude of the direct producer is made often enough into a justification of that relationship itself , and the exploitation and appropriation of the unpaid labour of others is just as often presented as the wage due to the owner of capital .\" 2.27 Historical Evidence III: O'Conor's Pro-Slavery Speech Marx supplies the most damning example. The lawyer O'Conor , at a New York meeting on 19 December 1859 under the slogan \"Justice for the South,\" declared amid \"thunderous applause\": \"to that condition of bondage the Negro is assigned by Nature... He has strength, and has the power to labour; but the Nature which created the power denied to him either the intellect to govern, or willingness to work.\" (Applause.) \"Both were denied to him. And that Nature which deprived him of the will to labour, gave him a master to coerce that will, and to make him a useful... servant... I maintain that it is not injustice to leave the Negro in the condition in which Nature placed him, to give him a master to govern him... nor is it depriving him of any of his rights to compel him to labour in return, and afford to that master just compensation for the labour and talent employed in governing him and rendering him useful to himself and to the society .\" Marx footnotes that he himself was a regular contributor to the New York Daily Tribune (1852–62), which then held strongly democratic, anti-slavery views — a self-referential verification of his source. Marx's cutting conclusion: \"Now the wage-labourer, just like the slave, must have a master, to make him work and govern him.\" And once this \"relationship of domination and servitude is assumed,\" it is \"quite in order\" for the wage-labourer to be compelled to produce, besides his own wages, \"also the wages of supervision, a compensation for the work of dominating and supervising him, or 'just compensation for the labour and talent employed in governing him and rendering him useful to himself and to the society'.\" The O'Conor quote is thus the slavery-form of the very same apologetic that the \"wages of superintendence\" theory performs for wage-labour — proving the apologetic structure is invariant across modes of exploitation. 2.28 Historical Evidence IV: The Epitropos and Régisseur Marx notes that the supervisory work, arising from the antagonistic character (domination of capital over labour), is \"directly and inseparably fused, under the capitalist system, with the productive functions that all combined social labour assigns to particular individuals as their special work.\" The wages of an epitropos — or régisseur as he was known in feudal France — \"become completely separated from profit and even take the form of wages for skilled labour, as soon as the business is conducted on a sufficiently large scale for such a manager to be paid\" — \"even though our industrial capitalists are still a long way from 'pursuing public affairs or philosophy'.\" 2.29 Ure and the Managers as \"the Soul of Our Industrial System\" Marx cites Andrew Ure ( Philosophy of Manufactures , 1836): \"it is not the industrial capitalists but rather the industrial managers who are 'the soul of our industrial system'.\" Marx's footnote adds that \"this Pindar of the manufacturers also testifies that most of the latter have not the slightest understanding of the machinery they use\" — a jab at the redundancy of the capitalist's technical contribution. And for the commercial side, he refers back to the previous Part. 2.30 The Superfluity of the Capitalist: Cooperative Factories as Proof Marx's theoretical payoff: \"Capitalist production has itself brought it about that the work of supervision is readily available, quite independent of the ownership of capital .\" It has therefore become \"superfluous for this work of supervision to be performed by the capitalist.\" His orchestra-conductor analogy returns: \"A musical conductor need in no way be the owner of the instruments in his orchestra, nor does it form part of his function as a conductor that he should have any part in paying the 'wages' of the other musicians.\" Cooperative factories provide the proof that \"the capitalist has become just as superfluous as a functionary in production as he himself, from his superior vantage-point, finds the large landlord.\" Marx concedes that in so far as this function \"does not arise from the production process simply as a capitalist process\" and \"is not confined to the function of exploiting the labour of others\" — i.e. in so far as it arises from \"the form of labour as social labour , from the combination and cooperation of many to a common result\" — it is \"just as independent of capital as is this form itself, once it has burst its capitalist shell.\" To say that this labour, as capitalist labour , is necessarily the function of the capitalist \"means nothing more than that the vulgus cannot conceive that forms developed in the womb of the capitalist mode of production may be separated and liberated from their antithetical capitalist character.\" His decisive formulation: \" Vis-à-vis the money capitalist, the industrial capitalist is a worker, but his work is that of a capitalist, i.e. an exploiter of the labour of others. \" The \"wage\" he claims \"is precisely the quantity of others' labour that is appropriated, and depends directly upon the rate of exploitation of this labour, as long as he makes the effort required for this exploitation. It does not depend on the amount of effort this exploitation costs him, which can be devolved on a manager against moderate payment.\" Marx then gives an empirical illustration: \"After every crisis one can see many ex-manufacturers in the English factory districts who are now supervising their own former factories as managers for the new owners, often their creditors , in return for a modest wage.\" An Engels footnote (F.E.) records a personal-knowledge case: a manufacturer whose business failed in the crisis of 1868 became \"the paid employee of his own former workers\" after the bankrupt factory was taken over by a workers' cooperative. 2.31 Managers' Wages: Constant Separation in Cooperatives and Joint-Stock Companies Marx generalizes: the wages of management, \"both commercial and industrial, appear as completely separate from profit of enterprise both in the workers' cooperative factories and in capitalist joint-stock companies .\" What appears \"accidental\" in other cases is \"here a constant factor.\" In the cooperative factory, \"the antithetical character of the supervisory work disappears, since the manager is paid by the workers instead of representing capital in opposition to them.\" Joint-stock companies in general (developed with the credit system) \"have the tendency to separate this function of managerial work more and more from the possession of capital, whether one's own or borrowed\" — just as, with the development of bourgeois society, judicial and administrative functions separated from landed property, to which they were attributes in the feudal period. The dialectical climax: since (on one hand) the functioning capitalist confronts the mere owner (money capitalist), and with the development of credit this money capital itself assumes a social character, \"being concentrated in banks and loaned out by these, no longer by its direct proprietors\"; and since (on the other hand) the mere manager, \"who does not possess capital under any title, neither by loan nor in any other way,\" takes care of all real functions of the functioning capitalist — \"there remains only the functionary, and the capitalist vanishes from the production process as someone superfluous .\" This is the culminating thesis of the chapter's second half. 2.32 Empirical Confirmation: Cooperative Factory Accounts Marx cites the published accounts (footnote: \"The accounts referred to here only go up to 1864, since the above was written in 1865. — F.E.\") of English cooperative factories. After deducting the manager's wages (which \"form a part of the variable capital laid out, just like the wages of the other workers\"), their profit \"was greater than the average, even though they sometimes paid a much higher interest than private factories did.\" The reason: \"in all these cases a greater economy in the use of constant capital .\" What matters for Marx: \"here the average profit (= interest + profit of enterprise) presents itself palpably and in actual fact as a magnitude completely separate from the wages of management . Since profit here was higher than average, so profit of enterprise was also higher than elsewhere.\" This is a crucial empirical hinge: the separation of managerial wage from profit is not a conceptual abstraction but a measurable accounting fact. 2.33 Empirical Confirmation: Joint-Stock Banks The same appears in certain capitalist joint-stock undertakings, e.g. joint-stock banks. The London and Westminster Bank paid an annual dividend of 30 per cent in 1863 ; the Union Bank of London and others paid 15 per cent . \"In addition to the managers' salaries, the interest paid on deposits is deducted from gross profit.\" The high profit is explained \"by the small proportion of the paid-up capital in relation to deposits.\" Marx gives figures: London and Westminster Bank, 1863 — paid-up capital £1,000,000 , deposits £14,540,275 ; Union Bank of London, 1863 — paid-up capital £600,000 , deposits £12,384,173 . This demonstrates the enormous leverage of a small ownership base over borrowed/deposited funds — the quantitative disproportion that produces outsized profit of enterprise for shareholders and managers. 2.34 The Historical Genesis and Apologetic Purpose of the Confusion Marx then narrates the history of the idea . \"The confusion between profit of enterprise and the wages of supervision or management originally arose from the antithetical form that the surplus of profit over interest assumes in opposition to this interest. It was subsequently developed with the apologetic intention of presenting profit not as surplus-value, i.e. as unpaid labour, but rather as the wage that the capitalist himself receives for the work he performs.\" Marx here makes a sharp distinction between origin (a spontaneous categorical confusion generated by the antithetical form) and subsequent development (a deliberate apologetic weaponization of that confusion). 2.35 The Socialists' Demand and Its Uncomfortable Reception \"The socialists then raised the demand that profit should be reduced in practice to what it claimed to be in theory , i.e. simply to the wages of supervision.\" This demand \"came up against the theoretical embellishment still more uncomfortably\" as two processes unfolded: 1. The wages of superintendence \"found their particular level and market price, just like every other wage, with the formation of a numerous class of industrial and commercial managers \" (footnote 78: Hodgskin , Labour Defended against the Claims of Capital , 1825 — \"Masters are labourers as well as their journeymen. In this character their interest is precisely the same as that of their men. But they are also either capitalists, or the agents of the capitalists, and in this respect their interest is decidedly opposed to the interests of the workmen\"; and \"The wide spread of education among the journeymen mechanics of this country diminishes daily the value of the labour and skill of almost all masters and employers by increasing the number of persons who possess their peculiar knowledge.\"); 2. They \"fell on the other hand, just like wages for skilled labour in general, with the general development that reduces the costs of production of labour-power with special training\" (footnote 79: J. S. Mill , Principles of Political Economy , 2nd edn, 1849 — \"The general relaxation of conventional barriers, the increased facilities of education tend to bring down the wages of skilled labour instead of raising those of the unskilled\"). Thus the \"wage of superintendence,\" once admitted as a real category, turns out — by the very laws the apologists invoke for all wages — to fall as the supply of qualified managers rises. The apologetic theory refutes itself when taken literally as a wage theory. 2.36 The Last Pretext Removed: Cooperatives and Joint-Stock Companies \"With the development of cooperatives on the workers' part , and joint-stock companies on the part of the bourgeoisie , the last pretext for confusing profit of enterprise with the wages of management was removed, and profit came to appear in practice as what it undeniably was in theory: mere surplus-value, value for which no equivalent was paid, realized unpaid labour ; so that the functioning capitalist really exploits labour, and the fruits of his exploitation, if he operates with borrowed capital, are divided into interest and profit of enterprise, the surplus of the profit over the interest.\" 2.37 The \"New Swindle\" of Joint-Stock Governance Marx closes with a final invidious twist. On the basis of capitalist production, \"a new swindle with the wages of management develops in connection with joint-stock companies\": over and above the actual managing director, \"a number of governing and supervisory boards arise, for which management and supervision are in fact a mere pretext for the robbery of shareholders and their own enrichment .\" He cites The City or the Physiology of London Business; with Sketches on 'Change, and the Coffee Houses , London, 1845: a private balance sheet of Mr Timothy Abraham Curtis , presented to the Court of Bankruptcy on his failure, showed income netted from directorship \"between £800 and £900 a year.\" Curtis's association with the Bank of England and East India House made him \"quite a plum for a public company to acquire... in the boardroom.\" Marx notes: \"The remuneration of these company directors is at least one guinea (21 shillings) for each weekly meeting .\" And his final line: \"Hearings before the bankruptcy court show that the wages of supervision are in inverse proportion, as a rule, to the actual supervision exercised by these nominal directors.\" The chapter thus ends by fully inverting the \"wages of superintendence\" apologetic: the reward rises as the actual work falls — proving the payment is a tribute of ownership, not compensation for labour. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 3.1 Fundamental Principles and Systemic Laws Revealed First, the primacy of form over quantity in the analysis of capital. The chapter's governing law is that a purely quantitative division of a magnitude can, under determinate social conditions — the division of the capitalist class into owners and functionaries, and the objective separation of the \"double movement\" of the same capital — harden into a qualitative, autonomous distinction . This is a methodological maxim of Marx's mature method: economic forms are not merely labels for quantitative shares but possess their own objectivity and causal power, feeding back upon the consciousness and behavior of economic agents. The formula p = i + e (average profit = interest + profit of enterprise) is not a neutral accounting identity; it encodes an entire social ontology in which one portion of value is attributed to \"property\" and another to \"function.\" Second, the relational constitution of economic categories. Interest and profit of enterprise \"exist only in their antithesis.\" Each is defined by its relation to the other, not by any intrinsic content. Interest is not \"naturally\" the yield of ownership; it becomes so only once profit of enterprise exists as its opposite, and vice versa. This is a thoroughly dialectical result: categories are positionally, not substantively, constituted — a principle with broad reach across Marx's theory of value forms. Third, the illusion-generating power of \"capital as property\" vs. \"capital as function.\" The separation of the exploitative essence of capital from the visibility of exploitation is the chapter's deepest insight. Because interest appears as a relation between capitalists, with no reference to labour, the exploitation relation itself gets displaced into a form that erases its own origin . The social antagonism between capital and labour is preserved (the profit is still unpaid labour) but its form of appearance is neutralized. This is the fetishism of capital in its most refined form. Fourth, the ideological function of an apologetic category and its self-refutation. The \"wages of superintendence\" theory is shown to be (a) generated spontaneously by the antithetical form of profit; (b) subsequently weaponized apologetically; and (c) empirically self-destroying once taken literally — managerial wages fall with the growth of a managerial class; supervisory \"wages\" vary inversely with actual supervision; cooperative and joint-stock forms separate wages from profit as a \"constant factor.\" Marx thereby demonstrates that an ideology can be refuted not merely by counter-argument but by the internal logic of its own claims and by the actual development of social forms. Fifth, the historical priority of interest-bearing capital and the \"reification lag.\" That it required a discovery (Massie, Hume, mid-18th century) that interest is part of gross profit reveals how deeply pre-capitalist money-lending forms had sedimented in popular consciousness. The category of interest is historically older than industrial capital — a fact that explains its apparent self-evidence as \"capital par excellence .\" Theoretical categories, Marx teaches, carry their history within them. Sixth, the tendency toward the \"superfluity\" of the capitalist. The joint-stock company, the credit system, the cooperative, and the salaried managerial class together dissolve the functional necessity of the proprietary capitalist: \"there remains only the functionary, and the capitalist vanishes from the production process as someone superfluous.\" This is a structural, historically dynamic tendency — a foreshadowing of the \"abolition\" logic that runs through Marx's critique of political economy. 3.2 Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications Theoretically , the chapter completes the architecture of Volume 3 by showing how the single social surplus-value, already distributed into profit, rent, and industrial/commercial categories, undergoes yet further subdivision into interest and profit of enterprise — and how each subdivision generates its own ideological \"justification.\" It bridges the abstract theory of the rate of profit to the concrete institutions of credit, banking, and joint-stock capital that Marx develops in the following chapters, and it supplies the conceptual tools for understanding financialization as a distinct form of surplus appropriation. Practically , the analysis bears directly on the political economy of \"shareholder value\" and managerial compensation. The apology that profit is a wage for \"supervision\" is shown to be structurally identical to the slaveholder's \"just compensation for the labour and talent employed in governing\" — a continuity Marx underscores by placing O'Conor's speech next to the modern economists' defense of the wage system. The designation of directorship fees as \"wages of supervision\" that vary in inverse proportion to actual supervision exposes such remuneration as a claim grounded in ownership, not work. Philosophically , the chapter is a study in the dialectic of appearance and essence — how a real, objective social structure generates a false appearance that is nonetheless practically true for the individuals enmeshed in it (the individual capitalist's \"choice\" is genuinely available to him), while being absurd when universalized to the whole social capital. Marx's refusal to reduce ideology to mere \"error\" — his insistence that the apologetic form arises from the real antithetical structure of the categories — is the enduring methodological lesson: social illusions are not accidents of thought but necessary forms of a contradictory reality. The chapter ultimately teaches that within capital, the identity \"capital = claim to surplus-value\" is internally split into two personas — the owner and the functionary — and that this split, while purely formal in its arithmetic origin, becomes materially decisive in law, consciousness, institutions, and class struggle. To understand capitalism's financial surface, one must grasp that the apparently neutral relation \"one capitalist to another\" (interest) is the disguised form of the relation \"capital to labour\" — and that the more that disguise is perfected, the more the underlying exploitation is enabled to continue unobserved. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 22 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 24"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 24", "title": "Chapter 24", "subtitle": "Externalization of the Relations of Capital in Interest-Bearing Capital", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 24/Chapter 24 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 6791, "read_time": "34 min read", "tags": ["Interest-Bearing Capital & Loan Capital", "Fetishism & Mystification of Capital"], "keywords": ["Externalization of Capital Relations in Interest-Bearing Capital", "Extreme Fetishism of Capital: Money Breeding Money", "Automatic Wealth Generation Illusion", "Dr. Price's Compound Interest Fantasy", "Complete Effacement of Exploitative Production"], "text": "Chapter 24 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,697 words ⏱️ ~33 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 24 — \"Interest-Bearing Capital as the Superficial Form of the Capital Relation\" — is the opening salvo of the section in Capital, Volume 3 that dissects how the total social surplus-value is divided into interest and profit of enterprise , and eventually the credit system that governs this division. Its central thesis is unambiguous and deliberately scandalous in form: in interest-bearing capital, designated by the formula M–M′ , the capital relation reaches \"its most superficial and fetishized form.\" Everything that actually produces surplus-value — the purchase and sale of commodities, the employment of labour-power, the extraction of surplus labour in production — is silently erased, leaving only a diptych of extremes: money on one side, more money on the other. The pivot of the argument is that capital itself now wears no marks of its own origin . The social relationship between capitalist and wage-labourer is transmuted into the relationship of a thing (money) to itself , as though money were as naturally and inherently interest-bearing as \"a pear tree is pear-bearing.\" The intellectual objective of the chapter is twofold. First, it exposes and anatomizes the fetish form of capital in its purest expression — an analysis that completes, at the level of capital-in-general, the fetishism of the commodity form announced in Volume 1. Second, it performs a powerful ideological critique : it shows that this fetishized form is not a mere optical illusion but a functional representation (a Vorstellung ) that serves the apologetic interests of \"vulgar economics,\" which can now present capital \"as an independent source of wealth, of value creation.\" The bulk of the chapter — roughly two-thirds of its pages — is a polemic against specific historical expressions of this delusion: Martin Luther's medieval polemic against usury, Dr Richard Price's compound-interest fantasies, William Pitt's \"sinking fund\" (a state-financing scheme that rests on the idea of paying off debt by accumulating it), Josiah Child's claims about 10% over 70 years, The Economist's declaration that all wealth \"has long ago become the interest of capital,\" and Adam Müller's \"Romantic\" drivel about compound interest accruing to the worker. Marx uses these episodes not just as rhetorical targets but as a case study in the pernicious theoretical consequences of taking appearance for the essence of capital. Philosophically and structurally, the chapter is the hinge-point between the \"internal\" analysis of capital (value, surplus-value, the profit rate, as developed in the earlier parts of Volume 3) and the \"external\" or surface analysis (the division of profit, credit, fictitious capital, the money market). It also anticipates the chapters on the money-dealing capital, credit, and the banking system, and it disciplines the reader: the correct theory of interest must never lose sight of its origin in surplus labour, and must remain anchored in the two great real limits — the devaluation of existing capital through rising productivity and the falling rate of profit — because against these, Dr Price's fantasy of geometric growth is exposed as mathematically impossible, not merely socially unjust. This fusion — of fetish analysis, financial polemic, and political-economy critique — makes the chapter both a culminating moment of the critique of political economy and a foundational text for any critique of financial fetishism. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Starting Point: Why Interest-Bearing Capital is the Most Fetishized Form Marx begins by ranking the fetish. In commercial capital , whose circuit is M–C–M′ , at least the general form of the capitalist movement is preserved: money buys commodities, commodities are sold for more money. Even though the whole operation occurs in the sphere of circulation, and the profit \"appears as merely profit upon alienation\" (i.e. it looks like a pure markup on the selling price, as if profit arose from buying cheap and selling dear), the process still exhibits \"the unity of opposing phases\" — purchase and sale — and therefore \"presents itself as the product of a social relation, not the product of a mere thing.\" In other words, commercial capital cannot fully hide the fact that the increase depends on transactions among persons . Interest-bearing capital obliterates even this. In M–M′ the \"two opposite procedures\" of buying and selling are gone. What remains is an empty syllogism: money in, more money out. There is no commodity, no sale, no labour process — only money and more money . The social relation has collapsed into the relation of a thing to itself. The Numerical Demonstration: Capital as Relation of Quantities To make this concrete, Marx deploys a deceptively simple arithmetic example. A capitalist lends £1,000 at 5% for one year . The value of that £1,000 as capital is: C + C·i (or C(1 + i)) = 1,000 + (1,000 × 1/20) = £1,050 . Since 5% = 1/20, the surplus accruing to the loan is £50. Marx draws a crucial conceptual point from this trivial calculation: \"capital is not a simple quantity. It is a relation of quantities.\" The £1,000 is now a ratio between the principal as \"a given value\" and itself as \"a principal that has produced a surplus-value.\" Money in itself is a magnitude; money as capital is a self-referential magnitude with a built-in increment. This is why the same formal appearance holds for every active capitalist — those operating with their own capital and those operating with borrowed capital alike: the value of capital now spontaneously presents itself as a quantity that produces another quantity. And this self-referential quantity relation is precisely what is sold when money is hired out. M–M′ as the \"Meaningless Abbreviation\" of the General Formula Marx returns to the original starting point — the general formula M–C–M′ — and shrinks it to its two extremes, M–M′ , where M′ = M + ΔM . This is \"the original and general formula for capital reduced to a meaningless abbreviation.\" The word meaningless is not metaphorical: the entire middle term C (the actual production and circulation process, the purchase of labour-power and means of production, the extraction of surplus labour) has been excised . What remains is \"capital in its finished form\" — the result of the whole reproductive ensemble — presented as if it were produced by the money form on its own. Here is the birth of the fetish in its definitive form: capital appears as a mysterious, self-creating source of interest, unmediated by production and circulation. It is no longer the outcome of a process but a property of a thing . \"The thing (money, commodity, value) is now already capital simply as a thing.\" This is the exact structural parallel Marx will draw again and again: the result of the overall reproduction process appears as a property devolving on a thing in itself , just as in commodity fetishism the social relations of producers appeared as properties of things. An important dialectical move follows: because money is \"commodities in their ever-exchangeable form,\" the possessor's mere choice — to spend the money as money or \"hire it out as capital\" — seems to determine whether valorization occurs. This makes the fetish appear voluntary and thing-like simultaneously: as if the same thing (money) carries a hidden capacity that the owner may \"release\" at will. The consequence is that \"the social relation is consummated in the relationship of a thing, money, to itself.\" Where the actual transformation of money into capital should be, we have \"only the form of this devoid of content.\" Money's Use-Value: The Parallel with Labour-Power Marx then raises the analysis to the level of the theory of value. In the case of labour-power , the use-value is precisely that it creates value, and more value than it contains. Here the same is claimed for money : \"the use-value of money is that of creating value, a greater value than is contained in itself.\" Money as such is treated as \"potentially self-valorizing value,\" and it is as such that it is lent. This constitutes \"the form of sale for this particular commodity\" — money-capital. The parallel is deliberate and damning: what was in the case of labour-power an anthropological-social fact of the human metabolism with nature (living labour is the source of value) is transposed onto money, and the transposition is entirely false. From this false use-value Marx extracts the famous botanical metaphor: value-creating capacity becomes a property of money as naturally as bearing pears is a property of a pear tree . This is the \"hidden quality\" ( qualitas occulta ) of the scholastics — a term Marx reintroduces later when attacking Price — i.e., a mysterious power attributed to a thing without any real causal basis. The Further Distortion: Interest as Original, Profit as Accessory The fetish completes itself in a second inversion. In actuality, interest is simply a part of profit , itself one part of the surplus-value extorted from the worker by the functioning capitalist. But in the M–M′ form, the inversion of the parts is total : interest appears as \"the specific fruit of capital, the original thing,\" while profit — now renamed profit of enterprise — appears as \"a mere accessory and trimming added in the reproduction process.\" This is the specifically ideological effect of interest-bearing capital: not just that profit looks like it comes from capital, but that the merely reflexive species of revenue ( interest , earned by lending) is taken to be the primary one, and the laborious, socially entangled species ( profit of enterprise , earned by running production) is treated as derivative. Marx labels this the point at which \"the fetish character of capital and the representation of this capital fetish is now complete.\" He then gives his most dialectical formulation of the whole chapter: in M–M′ we have \"the irrational form of capital… the misrepresentation and objectification of the relations of production, in its highest power.\" The interest-bearing form is \"the simple form of capital, in which it is taken as logically anterior to its own reproduction process.\" The adjective anterior is critical: the fetish doesn't merely invert the sequence — it precedes the process that supposedly produces it, turning the effect into the cause. The Payoff for Vulgar Economics This is where the ideological function becomes explicit. For vulgar economics — the apologetic branch of political economy that treats capital as an independent source of wealth — \"this form is of course a godsend.\" It is a form in which \"the source of profit is no longer recognizable\" and in which \"the result of the capitalist production process — separate from the process itself — obtains an autonomous existence.\" Vulgar economics finds in M–M′ a theory of capital that requires no labour, no exploitation, no social relation; only a thing and a rate. Marx will hammer this point home with historical examples ranging from sixteenth-century sermons to The Economist of 1851. Money Capital as a Commodity with a Fixed Price A short but important aside clarifies why money capital specifically is the seat of the pure fetish. Marx writes: \"It is only in money capital that capital becomes a commodity, whose self-valorizing quality has a fixed price as expressed in the prevailing rate of interest.\" Other forms of interest-bearing capital (he says) are derived from this form and presuppose it — money capital is therefore the immediate and canonical embodiment of the fetish. The self-valorizing capacity of capital becomes a price , the interest rate. This is the exact point at which capital becomes fully reified: its dynamic social power now has a quotable market price, discoverable in the financial pages, adjustable by supply and demand, and thus apparently as natural and thing-like as the price of a barrel of oil. Why Money Capital is the Pure Fetish Form: Two Structural Reasons Marx then gives a rigorous structural diagnosis of why money capital is uniquely suited to hide the real relations. Two reasons: First , money is the form in which \"all capital's determinations are dissolved and its real elements are invisible.\" Money obliterates the use-value distinctions among commodities, and therefore also obliterates the distinctions between different industrial capitals and the material conditions of their production. Money is \"the form in which value — and here capital — exists as autonomous exchange-value.\" In the reproduction process of capital, the money form is \"an evanescent moment, a moment of mere transition\" — you get money, but you immediately convert it into constant and variable capital. But on the money market , capital \"always exists in this form.\" The evanescent moment is frozen into permanence. This is a decisive dialectical insight: it is not that money is inherently a better vehicle for capital (money is only one phase of the circuit), but that the money market abstracts one phase of the circuit and treats it as if it were the whole. The fetish is thus an artifact of a particular sphere of appearance — the market — whose conditions of visibility suppress the conditions of production. Second , the surplus-value that capital creates — itself in the form of money — \"appears to accrue to it as such.\" Marx gives the famous botanical-linguistic pun: like the growth of trees, the generation of money ( tokos , the Greek word for interest, literally \"what has been born\") appears to be a property of capital in its money form. The etymology itself betrays the fetish: interest is called a \"birth\" precisely because the money-capital is imagined to have given birth to its own increase. Marx will repeat this etymological point later in the Luther discussion. The Movement of Capital Abbreviated: The Wine-in-the-Cellar Analogy Marx then condenses the argument into one of his most famous images: in interest-bearing capital the movement of capital is abbreviated . The mediating process is omitted, \"and a capital of 1,000 is characterized as a thing that in itself is 1,000 and in a certain period is transformed into 1,100, just as wine in the cellar improves its use-value after a given period of time.\" The analogy is precisely chosen to be false in a revealing way : wine does improve with time via a real physical-chemical process independent of social relations; capital does not grow with time via a real natural process (or, at least, not in the way the fetish claims). The wine analogy is exactly the kind of reasoning that the fetish invites , and its absurdity is its own refutation. \"Capital is now a thing, but the thing is capital. The money's body is now by love possessed.\" The Goethe quotation ( Faust , Part I, Auerbach's Cellar) reinforces the mystical-erotic cast of the fetish: money is not just said to breed, it is possessed — as if by a demonic love. Marx presses the point: interest accrues \"no matter whether it is asleep or awake, at home or abroad, by day and by night.\" It has become an autonomous self-validating force — the realization of \"the hoarder's most fervent wish,\" i.e., the dream of value that grows without labour, risk, or social entanglement. This is the mythological-ideological core of interest: it fully realizes the abstract greed of hoarding while pretending to have overcome hoarding's futility (the hoarder's stagnant treasure). Interest, in the fetishized form, is the perfect unholy marriage of avarice and growth. Luther's Naïve Polemic Against Usury: The First Historical Figure Marx now shifts into a historical-ideological register, beginning with Martin Luther in An die Pfarrherrn wider den Wucher zu predigen (Wittenberg, 1540). The passage is long and worth unpacking, because Marx uses it to expose exactly what the fetish looks like to a pre-capitalist moralist . Luther grants that interest may be demanded if the failure to repay causes the lender real damages — a missed payment or a missed profitable bargain (Luther's example: the missed purchase of a garden). Luther labels these duplex interesse, damni emergentis et lucri cessantis — a double damage, both the loss caused and the profit missed — a scholastic distinction Marx reproduces in Latin to mark the legal-theological form of justification. But Luther then attacks the generalization of this justification: \"they rush in and charge double on each 100 guilders … just as if the 100 guilders had had these two losses grown on to it naturally [Marx's emphasis].\" Here Luther has intuitively hit the fetish precisely : he is indignant at the idea that losses can be treated as grown-on the money, like a natural property. Someone is charging \"for a supposed loss which in fact no one caused you, and which you can neither prove nor reckon.\" Luther names this non verum sed phantasticum interesse — \"not actual damages, but imagined damages.\" Marx highlights Luther's formulation ex contingente necessarium — \"making something out of a thing that is not, and making what is uncertain into something completely sure.\" This is exactly the operation of the capital fetish: it transforms contingent social outcomes into necessary natural properties of money. Luther's peroration — \"usury of this kind would devour the whole world in a few years\" and his sarcasm against men who scheme \"to be lazy and idle and live in luxury on the labour of other people, without care, danger or loss,\" who would \"sit by my stove and let my 100 guilders gather wealth for me in the country, and yet keep it in my pocket\" — is included because it captures, in pre-capitalist moral-theological language, exactly the hoarder's fantasy into which mature interest-bearing capital has transformed itself. Marx's point is not that Luther is right in the confessional sense (he explicitly calls him \"naïve\"), but that the usury polemic is the transparent moral articulation of what the fetish-classical political economy will later disguise as a natural law of capital . Where Luther denounces, vulgar economics celebrates. Dr Price and the Fantasy of Compound Interest: The Second Historical Figure The central polemical episode of the chapter is Marx's attack on Dr Richard Price (1723–1791), whose writings on the national debt ( An Appeal to the Public on the Subject of the National Debt , London, 1772, and Observations on Reversionary Payments , London, 1772) became the theoretical foundation of British government fiscal policy under William Pitt the Younger . Price's conception of capital is precisely the M–M′ fetish in its purest, most speculative-ideological form: \"value that reproduces itself and increases in reproduction, by virtue of its innate property as ever persisting and growing value.\" Marx identifies Price's assumption of an \"innate property\" with the scholastics' hidden quality (qualitas occulta) — i.e., with pre-scientific magic dressed in the language of natural philosophy. Price's \"amazing fancies\" are said to \"leave far behind the fantasies of the alchemists.\" Alchemy at least tried to transmute metals through material processes; Price claims that money grows by an innate spiritual-in-financial force with no process at all. Marx's contempt is measured in that verb: Price's alchemy exceeds the alchemists'. The famous Price quotations are quoted by Marx at length. In the Appeal , Price writes: \"Money bearing compound interest increases at first slowly. But, the rate of increase being continually accelerated, it becomes in some time so rapid, as to mock all the powers of the imagination. One penny, put out at our Saviour's birth to 5 per cent compound interest, would, before this time, have increased to a greater sum, than would be contained in a hundred and fifty millions of earths, all solid gold. But if put out to simple interest, it would, in the same time, have amounted to no more than seven shillings and four pence half-penny.\" In the Observations , Price flies \"still higher\": a shilling at 6% compound interest from the birth of Christ \"would have increased to a greater sum than the whole solar system could hold, supposing it a sphere equal in diameter to the diameter of Saturn's orbit.\" Price's punchline is fiscal: \"A state need never therefore be under any difficulties; for with the smallest savings it may in as little time as its interest can require pay off the largest debts\" (pp. xiii, xiv). Marx's own conclusion is savage and laconic: \"What a charming theoretical introduction for the English national debt!\" The Mathematical Refutation: Price's Formula and Its Hidden Preconditions Marx now states the formula that underlies Price's mystification. Price \"viewed capital as a self-acting automaton, without regard to the conditions of reproduction and labour, as a mere number that increases by itself.\" Hence the formula: s = c(1 + i)ⁿ where: - s = the sum of capital + compound interest (the final amount), - c = the capital advanced (the initial sum), - i = the rate of interest, expressed in aliquot parts of 100 (e.g., 0.05 for 5%), - n = the number of years for which the process is continued. The formula itself is trivially correct as arithmetic — the issue is its use as a theory of capital . Price's error is not mathematical; it is conceptual : he treats the interest rate as if it were independent of, and unaffected by, the processes of production and reproduction that alone can generate the surplus out of which interest is paid. He presupposes a rate of profit that never falls, an accumulation process that never encounters its real limits, and a reproduction of capital that costs nothing. He is dazzled, Marx says, \"by the incredible figures that arise from geometric progression,\" and forgets that interest is a deduction from profit, so that a 5% interest rate presupposes at least a profit rate of 5% — and in fact, for regular payment, considerably more . Marx now delivers the two fundamental real-economic limits to the Price fantasy, both drawn from the earlier argument of Volume 3: Limit 1 — The devaluation of existing capital through rising social productivity. Value is determined not by the labour-time originally taken by production, but \"by the labour-time that their reproduction takes, and this steadily decreases as the social productivity of labour develops.\" Hence a substantial part of existing capital is constantly being devalued during the reproduction process. As productivity rises, the accumulated stock of capital does not present itself as the patient growth of simple accumulation, but \"as the result of a relatively short reproduction period.\" The longer the horizon, the less sense Price's geometric expansion makes — because the very substance on which the compounding is supposed to operate is being continually eroded by the cheapening of its reproduction. (Footnote 81 points to Mill, Carey, and Roscher, whom Marx will criticize further; a second footnote comments on Mill as the \"fountainhead of economic theory for the British Labour movement,\" distinguishing Mill's attempt to harmonize capital and proletariat from pure vulgar apologetics.) Limit 2 — The falling rate of profit. As shown in Part Three of Volume 3, the profit rate declines in proportion to growing accumulation and the accompanying rise in the productivity of social labour, expressed in the relative decrease of variable capital vis-à-vis constant capital . (Variable capital is the portion laid out on labour-power, the source of surplus-value; constant capital is machinery, raw materials, etc., which transmits but does not create value.) If one worker sets in motion ten times as much constant capital, then surplus labour-time would have to grow tenfold to keep the profit rate the same — but this is manifestly impossible: \"very soon the total labour-time, or even the full twenty-four hours of the day, would not be sufficient, even if it were entirely appropriated by capital.\" Price's progression, therefore, \"depends on the idea that the rate of profit does not decline, as does every iota of this 'all-engrossing capital at compound interest.'\" Note the dialectic: the same accumulation that appears to Price as self-expanding wealth generates the fall in the rate of profit that destroys his dream. Pitt's Sinking Fund: The Fiscal Enactment of the Fetish Marx then documents how this mystification became government policy . In 1786 the House of Commons resolved to raise £1,000,000 for public purposes. Pitt, \"believing\" Price, thought nothing better than to tax the people in order to \"accumulate\" the sum raised and thereby \"spirit away\" the national debt \"by the mystery of compound interest.\" The bill (Act 26 George III, Chapter XXXI) provided for accumulation of £250,000 annually \"until, with the expired annuities, the fund should have grown to £4,000,000 annually.\" In a 1792 speech, Pitt named \"accumulation\" as \"the most widespread and enduring cause\" of England's commercial supremacy — appealing to Adam Smith. Marx's cutting conclusion: \"By way of Dr Price, therefore, Pitt transformed Adam Smith's theory of accumulation into the enrichment of a nation by accumulation of debts, and thus arrived at the comforting progress towards an infinity of loans — loans to pay loans with.\" This is the crucial practical implication of the theoretical fetish: a state can delude itself into thinking it can grow out of debt by compounding the debt itself, because the interest-bearing-capital illusion suggests that money grows by being loaned , not by being productively invested . Adam Smith's sane theory of accumulation — laying aside a portion of profit to increase the principal productively — is inverted into a policy of state debt enrichment , where the \"accumulation\" is merely financial and the surplus needed to pay it comes from taxation of the real economy. Josiah Child and The Economist: The Fetish in Bourgeois Common Sense Two more historical citations round out the argument. Josiah Child (1630–1699) — \"the father of modern banking\" — is quoted as claiming that \"£100 put out at 10 per cent for seventy years, at compound interest would produce £102,400\" (from Traité sur le commerce , written 1669, French edition 1754). This is historically important as a pre-Price example of the same fetish, showing it to be a continuous ideological phenomenon, not a personal idiosyncrasy of Price's. More significantly, Marx cites The Economist (19 July 1851): \"Capital, with compound interest on every portion of capital saved, is so all-engrossing that all the wealth in the world from which income is derived, has long ago become the interest of capital… All rent is now the payment of interest on capital previously invested in the land.\" Marx's comment is scathing: this shows how \"Dr Price's conception has unwittingly been taken over by modern economics.\" Where Price was naïve and mystical, The Economist propagates the same fetish as sober commercial fact . In doing so, it reaches the logical end : all past and future surplus labour is already the property of capital by right ; everything else is a mere first instalment toward capital's \"all-engrossing\" appetite. Marx's vocabulary here is intentionally mythic — \"Moloch\" — the ancient deity to whom children were sacrificed. This is one of the most economically-pregnant and ideologically-loaded passages in Volume 3: the fetish of interest-bearing capital, once generalized, becomes a claim to universal and legally-justified ownership of all future labour . This is the theoretical matrix of later critiques of financialization and of \"rentier\" capital. Adam Müller and the \"Romantic\" Inversion: The Fetish in Poetic-Mystical Form Marx then turns to Adam Müller ( Elemente der Staatskunst , quoted as \"op. cit., III, pp. 147-9\"), a German Romantic conservative whom Marx treats with open contempt. Müller's passage purports to critique Price — but actually reproduces the fetish in poetic dressing. Müller argues that Price's \"tremendous growth of compound interest\" presupposes \"an undivided or uninterrupted and uniform arrangement for several centuries,\" that once capital is broken up among branches \"the entire accumulation of forces begins afresh,\" that the average worker's \"career\" is 20-25 years (\"Nature has distributed over a career of some twenty to twenty-five years the progression of force that is the average lot of each individual worker (!)\"), after which the worker \"must now transfer the capital obtained through compound interest on labour to a new worker, in most cases dividing it among several workers or children,\" and so on. Marx's verdict is ferocious: \"It would be impossible to drivel out a more hair-raising absurdity than this in so few lines.\" He identifies the fatal confusions: (a) the confusion of worker with capitalist — Müller pretends that the laborer, not the owner of capital, is the recipient of compound interest; (b) the confusion of the value of labour-power with interest on capital — the wage is treated as if it were interest accruing to the labourer's \"capital\" (his labouring capacity), and the labourer is treated as a capitalist who hires himself out. This is one step further into apologetics than even Price: it not only presents capital as self-valorizing, it presents the wage relation itself as a capital relation, so that exploitation vanishes entirely. Marx diagnoses Müller's method in a passage that is centrally important for the chapter's theoretical stakes: \"Its content is formed out of everyday prejudices, skimmed from the most superficial appearance of things. This false and trivial content is then supposedly 'elevated' and rendered poetic by a mystifying mode of expression.\" This is Marx's general theory of Romantic anti-capitalism: the Romantics are the literary counterparts of the vulgar economists. They sense something is wrong, but because they only grasp appearances — and grasp them with poetic flourish rather than analytic rigor — their critique reinforces the fetish it purports to attack. The \"Romantic\" here is a species of ideology-critique that cannot possibly be scientific, because its content is drawn from the very surface it ought to question. Marx's Concession and the Two Real Limits to the Compound-Interest Analogy Now Marx makes a concession that is itself instructive. He writes: \"The accumulation process of capital may be conceived as an accumulation of compound interest, in so far as the part of profit (surplus-value) that is transformed back into capital — i.e., which serves to absorb new labour — may be called interest.\" Here Marx grants the analogy a strictly limited validity: if by \"interest\" we mean the reinvested portion of surplus-value , then capital accumulation does bear a formal resemblance to compound interest. This dialectical generosity sharpens the subsequent refutation, because Marx then gives two substantive grounds on which even this limited analogy collapses when it is taken as a law of growth : Ground 1 — Devaluation of existing capital. Already stated above in the Price discussion, but formalized here as a first limit: because value is determined by reproduction labour-time and not by originally expended labour-time, and because productivity steadily rises, a large part of existing capital is permanently being devalued . Under high productivity, \"all existing capital, instead of appearing as the result of a long process of capital accumulation, appears as the result of a relatively short reproduction period.\" In other words, the accumulated stock is not the patient product of centuries of compounding; it is the cheap reproduction of a currently existing productive apparatus, whose cost is systematically driven down by technical progress. A compound-interest model misses this entirely. Ground 2 — Falling rate of profit. Marx here restates the argument of Part Three as a direct mathematical refutation of Price's formula: as accumulation grows and the productivity of labour rises, constant capital grows relative to variable capital , and the profit rate therefore falls . Consequently, to keep the profit rate constant, the surplus labour-time must rise in proportion to the constant capital each worker sets in motion — which is physically impossible once we hit the twenty-four-hour day. Since surplus-value is nothing other than surplus labour-time , it is subject to a qualitative limit: the length of the working day, the number of workers, the current development of the productive forces, the available population. Price's formula treats this limit as merely quantitative — as if by definition capital could always grow further, given time. This passage sets up Marx's sharpest formulation of the contrast: \"The identity of surplus-value and surplus labour sets a qualitative limit to the accumulation of capital… But if surplus-value is conceived in the irrational form of interest , the limit is only quantitative , and beggars all fantasy.\" The philosophical significance is enormous. Interest — as a distinct category of revenue pretending to natural growth — is precisely that representation of surplus-value in which the qualitative limit (the finite human working day; the finite number of working days that can be simultaneously exploited) is suppressed, so that the mind is free to imagine unlimited quantitative growth. Marx's final salvo on this point cites Hodgskin (footnote 82, from Labour Defended Against the Claims of Capital , p. 23): \"It is clear, that no labour, no productive power, no ingenuity, and no art, can answer the overwhelming demands of compound interest. But all saving is made from the revenue of the capitalist, so that actually these demands are constantly made and as constantly the productive power of labour refuses to satisfy them. A sort of balance is, therefore, constantly struck.\" Hodgskin's formulation is important because it makes the argument in the register of class struggle : the demands of compound interest are unlimited , and the productive power of labour is limited , and the class relation is the mechanism by which the gap is continuously reconciled — through devaluation, crisis, and intensified exploitation. The Consummated Fetish and the Materialist Denouement Marx closes the chapter with a summary statement that both restates the fetish and dissolves it. Interest-bearing capital \"displays the conception of the capital fetish in its consummate form\": the idea that the accumulated product of labour , \"in the fixed form of money at that,\" has \"the power of producing surplus-value in geometric progression by way of an inherent secret quality, as a pure automaton.\" This is the form in which The Economist believes that the world's wealth has \"long since discounted\" itself to capital, \"as belonging to it by right.\" Here the fetish becomes a claim of right . The accumulated dead labour is now imaginatively pregnant \"with a portion of present or future living surplus labour.\" Marx's dialectical riposte is a single, dense sentence that dissolves the fetish: \"We know however that in actual fact the preservation and thus also the reproduction of the value of products of past labour is only the result of their contact with living labour; and secondly, that the command that the products of past labour exercise over living surplus labour lasts only as long as the capital relation, the specific social relation in which past labour confronts living labour as independent and superior.\" Two doctrines are packed into this: 1. Dead labour's value is preserved only through contact with living labour. Constant capital and money are not self-valorizing; their value would decay or become worthless were it not continually re-appropriated in the living production process. The pear tree's \"hidden quality\" is exposed as the reified expression of a labor process. 2. The domination of past labour over living labour is not natural but historical. It rests on \"the capital relation, the specific social relation\" — which is contingent, fought-over, and therefore revocable . The fetish's \"geometric progression\" is only the ideologically frozen form of a relation of class domination. The chapter thus concludes in the mode of the Critique : it has shown that the most \"natural\" and \"obvious\" of all capital-forms is in fact the most socially produced and the most reversible. The appearance of capital as a self-breeding thing is the exact measure of the distance between capitalist production as it presents itself and capitalist production as it is . 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 1. The fetish is structural, not optical. The chapter's deepest lesson is that the M–M′ fetish is not a mere error of perception, nor a quirk of vulgar apologists, but a necessary product of the money-market form of capital . Because interest-bearing capital always exists in the money form — and because in the money form all real differences between commodities, capitals, and production conditions have been dissolved — the appearance of self-valorizing money arises of itself from the surface of the credit system. This is a genuinely dialectical insight: the fetish is not a mistake to be corrected by education alone; it must be explained , traced back to the specific abstraction (the freezing of the money phase) that generates it. This anticipates and grounds later theories of financial fetishism, money illusion, and the ideological power of \"capital markets.\" 2. The inversion of cause and effect is the core of ideology. The pivotal formulation — capital appears \"as logically anterior to its own reproduction process\" — identifies what is philosophically at stake. The effect (surplus-value) is treated as the cause (a property of money). This is the same structure Marx identified in commodity fetishism (a social relation appearing as a natural property of a thing), but raised to a higher power because it is now value itself being fetishized. The chapter demonstrates that ideology is not merely false consciousness; it is inverted causality , and to break its spell you must reinstate the process the appearance has suppressed: production, hire of labour-power, extraction of surplus labour. 3. Compound interest as a limit-illusion. Marx's refutation of Price, Pitt, Child, and The Economist carries an astonishingly modern payload. The critique of a state that believes it can \"grow out of\" debt by compounding it; the critique of a rentier class whose claims rest on the fantasy of infinite geometric growth; the critique of a market discourse that treats the entire world's wealth as always-already the property of capital — these are structurally the arguments any serious political economy must deploy against speculative finance. The key insight is not that compound interest is bad because it is exploitative; it is that it is impossible apart from the two real constraints: the devaluation of existing capital through rising productivity and the falling rate of profit . These two limits, derived rigorously from the theory of value and the theory of accumulation developed earlier in Volume 3, are the pivot of Marx's rejection of the infinite-growth view of capital . 4. The qualitative limit as the deepest critique. Perhaps the single most important philosophical move in the chapter is the claim that surplus-value is surplus labour , and that this identity imposes a qualitative , not merely quantitative, limit on capital's expansion. Interest's misrepresentation consists precisely of converting that qualitative limit into a mere quantitative one. This opens the door to two of Marx's signature conclusions for later readers: (a) capital's tendency to overreach its own material basis (the working day, the number of exploitable workers) is what generates crises and the permanent \"balance\" imposed by class struggle; (b) the whole ideological mythology of infinite wealth rests on the abstraction from labour-time that the interest form performs. The identity surplus-value = surplus labour is therefore one of Marx's most powerful tools, both analytically and ideologically. 5. The critique of Romanticism as a general method. The treatment of Müller is not merely comic relief. Marx identifies a genuine epistemic trap : content \"made out of everyday prejudices, skimmed from the most superficial appearance of things,\" then \"elevated and rendered poetic by a mystifying mode of expression.\" This is the signature of a whole genre — not only nineteenth-century German Romantics, but the \"poetic\" nostalgia for pre-financial capital, social-credit mystifications, and the later aestheticization of crisis. Marx's method warns against allowing moral outrage at speculation to substitute for rigorous analysis of the production process; indeed, Müller's Romanticism reinforces the fetish by transferring the \"fertility\" of capital to the labourer-as-owner-of-labour-power. 6. Broader implications. Bankability of the argument: the chapter founds a critique of interest as socially generated but privately appropriated , and it prefigures the theory of fictitious capital and the credit system developed in later chapters. Practically, it shows that any project of emancipation must undo not only the material but the ideological apparatus of interest-bearing capital: the appearance of money that breeds money is what enables the whole edifice to command \"the products of past labour over living surplus labour.\" Theoretically, the chapter ties together the fetishism of the commodity, the theory of surplus-value, the tendency of the rate of profit to fall, and the criticism of apologetic economics into a single, unified critical programme. Finally, philosophically, it demonstrates the central Marxist dialectical principle: the appearance that is most taken for granted — a money that simply \"grows\" — is precisely the appearance most thoroughly produced by a specific, historically contingent, and therefore changeable social relation. As Marx puts it in the closing sentence: capital's command lasts \"only as long as the capital relation.\" That \"only as long as\" is the entire political stake of the chapter, and of the critique of political economy as a whole. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 23 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 25"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 25", "title": "Chapter 25", "subtitle": "Credit and Fictitious Capital", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 25/Chapter 25 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 9449, "read_time": "47 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital"], "keywords": ["Credit and Fictitious Capital", "Commercial Credit (Bills of Exchange) vs Bank Credit", "Securities, Government Bonds, and Shares as Fictitious Capital", "Duplication of Capital in Paper Assets", "Title Deeds to Future Revenue vs Real Productive Wealth"], "text": "Chapter 25 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 9,417 words ⏱️ ~47 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 25, \"Credit and Fictitious Capital,\" occupies a pivotal position in Volume III of Capital — specifically in Part V, \"The Division of Profit into Interest and Profit of Enterprise.\" It is the entry point into Marx's treatment of the credit system, and its central thesis is both modest in its stated scope and explosive in its implications: credit under capitalism is not an exogenous convenience layered atop commodity production, but an organic outgrowth of the money-form itself — specifically of money's function as a means of payment — which then generates a vast, self-referential superstructure of paper claims (bills of exchange, banknotes, deposits, cheques) that can detach from the real transactions it purports to represent and become a mechanism for the creation of fictitious capital. The chapter's central problem is the relationship between the \"base\" of real money (gold, banknotes) and the \"enormous superstructure\" of bills of exchange built upon it. Marx's intellectual objective is to characterize the credit system just far enough to show how it belongs intrinsically to the capitalist mode of production \"in general\" — not to provide a technical banking manual. He explicitly states this limitation at the outset, and the reader must hold this frame throughout: what follows is a conceptual anatomy, not a history of banking. The chapter fits into the broader theoretical framework of Volume III in three decisive ways. First, it deepens the analysis of interest-bearing capital by showing that the loanable money capital handled by bankers is not simply a hoard of idle funds but a socially concentrated fund drawn from reserve funds, money-capitalists' deposits, the scattered savings of \"all social classes,\" and revenues awaiting gradual consumption. Second, it sets up the argument — referenced here as awaiting development in Chapter 28 — that the quantity theory of money misconstrues the relationship between note issue, prices, and credit. Third, it provides the empirical-historical demonstration of the theory's danger: the English commercial crisis of 1845–47, in which the credit system that had lubricated a genuine boom transmuted into a machinery for swindling — commodities bought and sold not to realize value but merely to generate discountable bills. The intellectual stakes are therefore enormous. The chapter is the hinge on which rests Marx's claim that crises under capitalism are not accidental failures of an otherwise sound system but structural potentials immanent to the unity of production and circulation. The \"fictitious capital\" of the title is not simply fraud (though fraud abounds in the evidence Marx marshals); it is the ordinary, systemic tendency of credit to create claims whose value rests on claims whose value rests on claims, until the whole edifice is tested against the narrow metallic base — and fails. This chapter supplies both the theoretical scaffolding and the documentary record for that argument. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Methodological Threshold: Why Credit Is Analyzed Only \"In General\" Marx opens with a programmatic restriction: \"It lies outside the scope of our plan to give a detailed analysis of the credit system and the instruments this creates (credit money, etc.).\" This is not evasion but a deliberate delimitation. He will treat only commercial and bank credit — the credit that arises directly between producers and merchants, and the credit that banks extend as intermediaries. He explicitly excludes state credit (the credit of governments), stating that \"the connection between the development of this and the development of state credit remains outside our discussion.\" The terminological precision matters: by isolating commercial and bank credit, Marx keeps the analysis anchored to the circuit of industrial capital, ensuring that the credit he describes is ultimately grounded in the production and circulation of commodities — not in sovereign borrowing, which obeys a distinct logic. This framing determines everything that follows. The chapter will not survey banking institutions, nor legislate policy (except where the historical case demands it). Instead, it will identify the minimal necessary features of the credit system that characterize capitalist production as such. The reader should read every subsequent empirical citation — Leatham, Tooke, Coquelin, Gilbart, the parliamentary reports — as evidence marshaled to establish a structural point, not as independent historical research. The Genesis of Credit in Money as Means of Payment The theoretical foundation is announced by back-reference: Marx has \"already shown (in Volume 1, Chapter 3, 3, b) how the function of money as means of payment develops out of simple commodity circulation, so that a relationship of creditor and debtor is formed.\" The essential move is that credit is not an invention; it is latent in the money-form . When money functions as a means of payment rather than as a means of purchase, the sale of a commodity and its payment are separated in time — and this temporal gap is the germ of the credit relation. With the development of trade and of a mode of production that \"produces only for circulation,\" this \"spontaneous basis for the credit system is expanded, generalized and elaborated.\" Marx then states the decisive structural shift: \"By and large, money now functions only as means of payment, i.e. commodities are not sold for money, but for a written promise to pay at a certain date.\" Here we encounter the first key term of the chapter: the bill of exchange — a written promise to pay at a certain date, which Marx adopts as the general name for \"all these promises to pay.\" The mechanics of the bill are crucial. Until they expire and fall due, \"these bills themselves circulate as means of payment; and they form the actual commercial money.\" And when they \"ultimately cancel each other out, by the balancing of debts and claims, they function absolutely as money, even though there is no final transformation into money proper.\" This is the analytical crux: credit instruments perform the money-function without ever becoming money . The theoretical architecture that follows is a layered dependence: \"As these mutual advances by producers and merchants form the real basis of credit, so their instrument of circulation, the bill of exchange, forms the basis of credit money proper, banknotes, etc.\" The key negative claim is that credit money \"is not based on monetary circulation, that of metallic or government paper money, but rather on the circulation of bills of exchange.\" The pyramid has, as it were, inverted: metallic money is no longer the base but a narrower, subordinate reserve at the bottom of a paper-driven system — precisely the \"base\" whose narrowing Leatham will later warn is so dangerous. Leatham's Superstructure: The Arithmetic of the Bill Mountain The banker W. Leatham, in Letters on the Currency (2nd edn, London, 1840), supplies the quantitative scaffolding. For 1839, the total of bills for the whole year is £528,493,842 (assuming foreign bills of exchange make up about a fifth of the total). The amount of bills out at one time in that year is £132,123,460 . Bills of exchange constitute \"one component part greater in amount than all the rest put together\" — meaning that the mass of bills in existence exceeds banknotes and coin combined. The variables here — annual turnover of £528 million versus simultaneous stock of £132 million — illustrate the distinction between the flow of payments and the standing quantity of claims, a distinction essential to understanding why credit can be both economically vast and physically unbacked. Leatham's warning deserves close reading: \"This enormous superstructure of bills of exchange rests (!) upon the base formed by the amount of banknotes and gold, and when, by events, this base becomes too much narrowed, its solidity and very existence is endangered.\" Marx reproduces Leatham's exclamation mark, signaling that the \"resting\" is precisely what is questionable. The numbers Leatham gives are stark: if one estimates the entire currency (banknotes) plus the liabilities of the Bank and country bankers payable on demand, one finds a sum of £153 million which, by law, can be converted into gold — \"and the amount of gold to meet this demand\" only £14 million . The ratio of convertible claims to metallic reserve is thus roughly eleven to one. This is not a policy detail; it is the structural fragility of the system expressed numerically. Leatham then identifies the mechanism by which fictitious paper is generated: \"The bills of exchange are not ... placed under any control, except by preventing the abundance of money, excessive and low rates of interest or discount, which create a part of them, and encourage their great and dangerous expansion.\" This introduces the concept Marx will call accommodation paper — bills drawn not against a real transaction but to raise funds: \"one bill of exchange is drawn to take up another running, in order to raise a fictitious capital, by creating so much currency.\" The distinction between real bona fide transactions (\"actual bargain and sale\") and purely financial paper is, Leatham admits, impossible to draw empirically: \"It is impossible to decide what part arises out of real bona fide transactions ... or what part is fictitious.\" This epistemic ungovernability is not incidental — it is the heart of the chapter's case that credit cannot be regulated by mechanical rule. Bosanquet and the Clearing House: The Economies of Endorsement J. W. Bosanquet, in Metallic, Paper and Credit Currency (London, 1842), supplies complementary data on the efficiency of credit. \"An average amount of payments to the extent of upwards of £3,000,000 is settled through the Clearing House ... every day of business in the year, and the daily amount of money required for the purpose is little more than £200,000.\" Here is a concrete ratio: roughly £3,000,000 of payments settled with £200,000 of actual money — a factor of about fifteen . (Engels' editorial footnote extends the series forward: \"In 1889, the total turnover of the Clearing House amounted to £7,618½ million, which, in roughly 300 business days, averages £25½ million daily.\") The function of the Clearing House — where London bankers exchange due bills and filed cheques — is to net out mutual claims, so that only the residual balance needs settlement in money. This is the institutional embodiment of the principle that credit instruments cancelling each other function absolutely as money. Bosanquet then establishes the circulation of bills by endorsement. Bills \"act undoubtedly as currency, independent of money,\" transferring property by endorsement. Assuming \"upon an average there are two endorsements upon every bill in circulation, and ... each bill performs two payments before it becomes due,\" it follows that by endorsement alone, property changed hands in 1839 to the value of twice £528 million, or £1,056,000,000 — at a rate of more than £3,000,000 per day. Extending the calculation to deposits as well, Bosanquet concludes that \"deposits and bills of exchange together, perform the functions of money, by transferring property from hand to hand without the aid of money, to an extent daily of not less than £18,000,000.\" Every variable here — endorsements, days, bills, deposits — compounds the same point: the volume of property transfers vastly exceeds the volume of money required to effect them. Marx accepts the calculation as illustrative while never losing sight of the fact that this efficiency is simultaneously a leverage — the mechanism by which a shrinking base can destabilize a swollen superstructure. Tooke: Credit as Confidence and the Origin of Interest Marx then introduces Tooke's definition of credit \"in its most simple expression\": \"the confidence which, well, or ill-founded, leads a person to entrust another with a certain amount of capital, in money, or in goods computed at a value in money agreed upon, and in each case payable at the expiration of a fixed term.\" Two things are analytically important here. First, credit is grounded in confidence — a subjective, potentially ill-founded state. This prepares the way for the panic psychology that will dominate the crisis narrative (Gurney's testimony). Second, Tooke specifies two forms of credit: lending in money and lending in goods whose value is agreed in money terms (which constitutes a sale ). In money-lending, whether \"in banknotes, or in a cash credit, or in an order upon a correspondent, an addition for the use of the capital of so much upon every £100 is made to the amount to be repaid.\" That addition is the origin of interest , expressed as a rate per £100 (per centum). In goods-lending, the sum stipulated to be repaid \"includes a consideration for the use of the capital and for the risk.\" Credit thus already contains the seed of the division between interest (payment for use) and profit of enterprise (the risk-bearing residual) — the very division the Part as a whole theorizes. Tooke emphasizes the transferability of the obligations: written obligations of payment at fixed dates mostly accompany credits, and \"the obligations or promissory notes after date being transferable, form the means by which the lenders, if they have occasion for the use of their capital ... before the expiration of the term of the bills they hold, are mostly enabled to borrow or to buy on lower terms, by having their own credit strengthened by the names on the bills in addition to their own.\" Here the endorsement (the \"names on the bills\") becomes collateral — a chain of personal liability that makes the paper more liquid than the underlying commitment of any single party. This is why a draft on a famous house (Jones Loyd, later in the evidence) circulates more readily than a private draft. Coquelin: Credit as the Interlocking Network of Industrial Advances Charles Coquelin ( Revue des Deux Mondes , 1842) shifts the analysis from finance to the productive core. \"In every country, the greater part of credit transactions take place within the orbit of industry.\" Coquelin sketches a vertical chain: the raw-material producer advances his product to the manufacturer \"and receives from him a promise to pay on a certain date.\" The manufacturer, \"after completing his share in the work, advances his product in turn to another manufacturer who is to process it further, on similar conditions, and in this way credit extends ever further, from one person to another, right through to the consumer.\" The wholesaler advances commodities to the retailer while himself receiving them from the manufacturer. \"Everyone borrows with one hand and lends with the other, sometimes money, but far more frequently products.\" This is the crucial substantive claim: credit is not primarily a monetary phenomenon but a mode of organizing production and distribution . \"There is thus an incessant exchange of advances in industry, which combine and intersect each other in all directions. The development of credit is nothing more than the multiplication and growth of these mutual advances, and this is the true seat of its power.\" By locating credit's \"true seat\" in the factory-to-factory, merchant-to-merchant network rather than in the bank, Coquelin's evidence — which Marx adopts approvingly — grounds the entire chapter's subsequent discussion of bank credit. Bank credit supervenes upon industrial credit; it does not create it. The Money Trade and the Emergence of the Banker as \"General Manager of Money Capital\" Having established credit's productive seat, Marx turns to \"the other aspect of the credit system\": the development of the money trade , which \"in capitalist production naturally keeps step with the development of trade in commodities.\" He back-references Chapter 19, where he showed that maintaining a reserve fund for businessmen, the technical operations of receiving and paying out money, international payments, \"and hence the bullion trade as well, are concentrated in the hands of money-dealers.\" To this money-dealing is added the management of interest-bearing capital — money capital as the special function of the money-dealers. \"The borrowing and lending of money becomes their special business.\" The banker's position is defined with surgical precision: \"They appear as middlemen between the real lender of money capital and its borrower.\" The business of banking \"consists from this aspect in concentrating money capital for loan in large masses in the bank's hands, so that, instead of the individual lender of money, it is the bankers as representatives of all lenders of money who confront the industrial and commercial capitalists. They become the general managers of money capital.\" The symmetrical role: \"they concentrate the borrowers vis-a-vis all the lenders, in so far as they borrow for the entire world of trade.\" The bank is thus double-sided: \"a bank represents on the one hand the centralization of money capital, of the lenders, and on the other hand the centralization of the borrowers.\" And its profit is stated with the directness of classical political economy: \"It makes its profit in general by borrowing at lower rates than those at which it lends.\" The rate spread is the bank's income — a formula whose simplicity conceals the whole later analysis of how the spread can be widened by the parasitic retention of interest-free balances (Gilbart). The Sources of Loan Capital: Reserve Funds, Deposits, Small Sums, Revenues Marx then enumerates how the loan capital at the banks' disposal accrues. First , they act as \"the cashiers of the industrial capitalists,\" holding the money capital each producer and merchant keeps as a reserve fund or receives as payment. \"These funds are thus transformed into money capital for loan. In this way the reserve fund of the business community is restricted to the necessary minimum, by being concentrated as a social fund, and one part of the money capital, which would otherwise be dormant in reserve, is loaned out and functions as interest-bearing capital.\" This is the socialization of the reserve fund — a collective economization that simultaneously frees capital for lending. Second , their loan capital is formed \"from the deposits made by money capitalists, who hand over to them the job of loaning it out.\" With the development of the banking system, \"and particularly once they pay interest on deposits, the money savings and the temporarily unoccupied money of all social classes are also deposited with them.\" Marx explicitly identifies a distinct function here: \"Small sums which are incapable of functioning as money capital by themselves are combined into great masses and thus form a monetary power. This collection of small amounts, as a particular function of the banking system, must be distinguished from the banks' function as middlemen between actual money capitalists and borrowers.\" This is a nuance of considerable theoretical weight: the banker is not merely a broker between existing large lenders and borrowers; he is a transformer who manufactures loanable capital out of sums too small to have functioned as such. Finally , \"revenues that are to be consumed only gradually are also deposited with the banks\" — consumption funds temporarily entering the credit circuit as a further source of lendable money capital. Forms of Lending and the Banknote as \"Bill on the Banker\" Lending, Marx specifies (confining himself to \"commercial credit proper\"), is effected \"by discounting bills — transforming them into money before their due date — and by advances of various kinds\": direct advances on personal credit; loans against securities \"such as interest-bearing paper, government paper and stocks of all sorts\"; advances against bills of lading, dock warrants and other certified titles to ownership of goods; and overdrafts on deposits. The discount is thus defined as the purchase of a bill before maturity at less than face value — the deduction being the interest. The credit the banker gives can take various forms: \"bills and cheques on other banks, credit facilities of a similar kind, and finally, if the bank is authorized to issue notes, in its own banknotes.\" Marx defines the banknote with exactness: \"A banknote is nothing more than a bill on the banker, payable at any time to its possessor and given by the banker in place of private drafts.\" He then explains why the banknote appears \"especially striking and important to the layman\": first, this kind of credit money \"emerges from commercial circulation into general circulation and functions here as money\"; second, \"in most countries the major banks that issue notes are a peculiar mishmash between national banks and private banks and actually have the government's credit behind them, their notes being more or less legal tender\"; and third, \"it is evident here that what the banker is dealing in is credit itself, since the banknote merely represents a circulating token of credit.\" The layman's illusion is that the banknote is uniquely \"credit,\" when in fact the banker \"also deals in credit in every other form, even if he advances money deposited with him in cash.\" Hence the summary judgment: \"In actual fact, banknotes are simply the small change of wholesale trade, and the deposit is always the main thing as far as the banks are concerned. The Scottish banks provide the best proof of this.\" The priority of the deposit over the note is one of the chapter's most consequential claims, since it turns attention from note issue (where the currency theorists concentrated) to the deposit multiplier. An editorial note interrupts to say that \"special credit institutions, like special forms of banks, need not be considered in any more detail for our present purpose.\" Marx then returns to Tooke, who distinguishes two branches of banking : collecting capital from those who lack immediate employment for it and distributing it to those who have, versus receiving deposits of customers' incomes and paying them out as needed for consumption. Tooke names the first \"a circulation of capital\" and the second \"a circulation of currency,\" the first relating \"to the concentration of capital on the one hand and the distribution of it on the other,\" the second \"employed in administering the circulation for local purposes of the district.\" Marx notes: \"We shall return to this passage in Chapter 28.\" The distinction is important because it separates the credit that funds production (capital) from the credit that merely circulates revenue. Parliamentary Evidence: Banker's Bills as a \"Privilege of Coining Money\" The notes to the chapter (drawn largely from Commercial Distress, 1847–8 ) supply vital empirical texture. In the 1840s, \"twenty-one-day drafts of one bank on another were often accepted in lieu of banknotes when discounting bills of exchange in London\" (J. Pease, country banker, nos. 4636 and 4645). It was customary for bankers to give customers such bills in payment \"quite regularly, whenever money was tight.\" Recipients wanting banknotes had to discount the bills again — which \"for the banks, this amounted to a privilege of coining money.\" Messrs Jones Loyd and Co. had paid in this way \"from time immemorial\" whenever money was scarce and the interest rate above 5 per cent. The customer was eager to get these banker's bills because a bill on Jones Loyd was easier to discount than his own — \"they often changed hands twenty or thirty times.\" Here we see the mechanism by which a bank's name, added to a bill, functions as a superior form of money: the \"names on the bills\" (Tooke) confer liquidity, and each re-endorsement is a further use of the same paper before any final settlement. Fullarton: Any Shape of Credit Can Function as Money Fullarton supplies the general formulation: \"There is scarcely any shape into which credit can be cast, in which it will not at times be called to perform the functions of money; and whether that shape be a banknote, or a bill of exchange, or a banker's cheque, the process is in every essential particular the same, and the result is the same.\" And: \"Banknotes are the small change of credit.\" Marx adds a footnote identifying Fullarton (1780–1849) as \"a notable opponent of the quantity theory of money; his ideas are dealt with in detail in Chapter 28.\" This signals that the chapter is laying a foundation for a polemic to come: Fullarton, the anti-quantity theorist, is here being recruited as a witness to the fungibility of credit forms — a fact the quantity theory cannot accommodate. Gilbart: The Manufacture of Banking Capital Out of Other People's Money The longest single block of evidence comes from J. W. Gilbart's The History and Principles of Banking (London, 1834). Gilbart divides a bank's trading capital into invested capital and borrowed banking capital , and identifies \"three ways of raising a banking or borrowed capital\": receiving deposits, issuing notes, and drawing bills. He then illustrates with three simple cases. If a person lends £100 for nothing and the bank lends it at 4 per cent, it gains £4 per year. If instead a person takes the bank's \"promise to pay\" (the standard formula of an English banknote) and returns it at year's end while paying 4 per cent for it, the bank again gains £4 — \"just the same as though I had lent him 100 sovereigns.\" If a person in a country town brings the bank £100 on condition that twenty-one days later it pays the same amount to a person in London, \"whatever interest I can make of the money during the twenty-one days, will be my profit.\" \"This,\" Gilbart concludes, \"is a fair representation of the operations of banking, and of the way in which a banking capital is created by means of deposits, notes, and bills.\" The formal principle: banking capital can be created by the very act of accepting deposits or issuing promises — a kind of capital not itself produced, but fabricated from credit relations. Gilbart further specifies: \"The profits of a banker are generally in proportion to the amount of his banking or borrowed capital.\" To determine the true profit, \"the interest upon the invested capital should be deducted from the gross profit, and what remains is the banking profit.\" And then the crucial materialist point: \"The advances of bankers to their customers are made with other people's money.\" This is developed in the remarkable passage on non-issuing bankers: \"Precisely those bankers who do not issue notes, create a banking capital by the discounting of bills. They render their discounts subservient to the increase of their deposits. The London bankers will not discount except for those houses who have deposit accounts with them.\" Finally, the device of the compensating balance: \"A party who has had bills discounted, and has paid interest on the whole amount, must leave some portion of that amount in the hands of the banker without interest. By this means the banker obtains more than the current rate of interest on the money actually advanced, and raises a banking capital to the amount of the balance left in his hands.\" Thus the banker's real yield exceeds the nominal discount rate — because he lends his own money (or rather, other people's) and gets back not only interest on the full amount but the free use of a retained fraction that he can lend again. Gilbart on Deposit Banking, Transfer, and the Voluntary Supersession of Money Gilbart then elaborates the transfer principle : \"Banks of deposit serve to economize the use of the circulating medium. This is done upon the principle of transfer of titles.\" Banks \"are enabled to settle a large amount of transactions with a small amount of money. The money thus liberated, is employed by the banker in making advances, by discount or otherwise, to his customers. Hence the principle of transfer gives additional efficiency to the deposit system.\" The vision reaches its limit in this passage: \"It matters not whether the two parties, who have dealings with each other, keep their accounts with the same banker or with different bankers; for, as the bankers exchange their cheques with each other at the clearing house... The deposit system might thus, by means of transfers, be carried to such an extent as wholly to supersede the use of a metallic currency. Were every man to keep a deposit account at a bank, and make all his payments by cheques, money might be superseded, and cheques become the sole circulating medium.\" But Gilbart adds the crucial qualifier: \"In this case, however, it must be supposed that the banker has the money in his hands, or the cheques would have no value.\" The qualifier preserves the structural tension between the credit superstructure and its base — the cheques are worthless unless there is money behind them, yet the whole efficiency of the system consists in not requiring that money to be present. Gilbart describes the consolidation of local banking through branch banks (country banks with branches in smaller towns; London banks in the different districts) and agencies (\"Each country banker employs a London agent to pay his notes or bills ... and to receive sums that may be lodged by parties residing in London for the use of parties residing in the country\"). And he describes the mutual clearing of notes: \"Each banker accepts the notes of others, but does not reissue them. In all larger cities they come together once or twice a week and exchange their notes. The balance is paid by a draft on London.\" Gilbart on the Inseparability of Trade from Speculation A passage of great interpretive importance follows: \"It is the object of banking to give facilities to trade, and whatever gives facilities to trade gives facilities to speculation. Trade and speculation are in some cases so nearly allied, that it is impossible to say at what precise point trade ends and speculation begins... Wherever there are banks, capital is more readily obtained, and at a cheaper rate. The cheapness of capital gives facilities to speculation, just in the same way as the cheapness of beef and of beer gives facilities to gluttony and drunkenness.\" The simile is crude but precise: cheapness does not cause the appetite, but it facilitates its indulgence. Marx returns to this passage himself, stressing its recognition already in 1834. Gilbart continues with an argument that the issue of notes and the discount of bills are not identical (a point against those who equated the two): \"It is very possible for a banker to issue his own notes for all the bills he discounts, and yet nine-tenths of the bills in his possession shall represent real capital. For, although in the first instance, the banker's notes are given for the bill, yet these notes may not stay in circulation until the bill becomes due — the bill may have three months to run, the notes may return in three days.\" The logic: the note returned to the bank in three days cancels itself out as a claim against the bank, leaving the discounted bill as the bank's real asset. So the identity between note issue and fictitious capital formation is false — most notes return quickly, most bills represent real transactions. This is a nuance Marx accepts against the currency school: the danger lies not in the mechanics of note issue as such. Gilbart then notes that \"the overdrawing of a cash credit account is a regular matter of business; it is, in fact, the purpose for which the cash credit has been granted\" — and cash credits are granted not only on personal security but \"also upon the security of the Public Funds.\" Then: \"Capital advanced, by way of loan, on the securities of merchandise, would produce the same effects as if advanced in the discounting of bills. If a party borrows £100 on the security of his merchandise, it is the same as though he had sold his merchandise for a £100 bill, and got it discounted with the banker. By obtaining this advance he is enabled to hold over this merchandise for a better market, and avoids a sacrifice which, otherwise, he might be induced to make, in order to raise the money for urgent purposes.\" This lombard advance — lending against commodities — is functionally equivalent to the discount of a bill and carries the same implication: credit allows a producer to withhold goods from the market, thereby (as Leatham implied and Marx will elsewhere develop) distorting the relation of supply and demand. The Deposit Multiplier: The Currency Theory Reviewed A passage cited from The Currency Theory Reviewed provides the chapter's sharpest image of the creation of credit money and the phenomenon of fictitious capital. \"It is unquestionably true that the £1,000 which you deposit at A today may be reissued tomorrow, and form a deposit at B. The day after that, reissued from B, it may form a deposit at C ... and so on to infinitude; and that the same £1,000 in money may thus, by a succession of transfers, multiply itself into a sum of deposits absolutely indefinite.\" The variables: one initial £1,000 of money; a sequence of deposits A, B, C, ...; each step a redeposit. The consequence: \"It is possible, therefore, that nine-tenths of all the deposits in the United Kingdom may have no existence beyond their record in the books of the bankers who are respectively accountable for them.\" The documentary evidence: \"Thus in Scotland, for instance, currency (mostly paper money at that) has never exceeded £3 million, the deposits in the banks are estimated at £27 million\" — a ratio of nine to one between deposits and currency. The passage then explains the reversibility of this multiplication: \"Unless a run on the banks be made, the same £1,000 would, if sent back upon its travels, cancel with the same facility a sum equally indefinite. As the same £1,000 with which you cancel your debt to a tradesman today, may cancel his debt to the merchant tomorrow, the merchant's debt to the bank the day following, and so on without end; so the same £1,000 may pass from hand to hand, and bank to bank, and cancel any conceivable sum of deposits.\" The passage is ambivalent — it presents the multiplicity of deposits as a natural and benign feature of the transfer system, but it also states plainly that the deposits have \"no existence beyond their record in the books.\" This is precisely the line between legitimate credit (the same money performing successive cancellations) and fictitious capital (claims that appear to be wealth but are only claims). The condition of stability is the absence of a run; when a run occurs, the multiplication reverses, and claims contract to the money base. Marx's Bridge from Gilbart to History Marx inserts his own comment at this point: \"We have seen how Gilbart was already aware in 1834 that 'whatever gives facilities to trade gives facilities to speculation...' The easier it is to obtain advances on unsold commodities, the more these advances are taken up and the greater is the temptation to manufacture commodities or dump those already manufactured on distant markets, simply to receive advances of money on them.\" This is the mechanism by which credit and swindling become indistinguishable: if a producer can get an advance on goods not yet sold, he has a motive to produce or dump goods purely to generate advances. The demand is not for the commodities but for the claims they engender. And: \"As to how the entire business community in a country can be caught up in swindling of this kind, and where it ends up, we have a striking example in the history of English commerce between 1845 and 1847.\" The 1845–47 Case: From Recovery to Mania Engels' editorial narrative then takes over. The depression that began in 1837 eased at the end of 1842. In 1843 the Opium War \"opened up China to English trade,\" and the new market \"offered a new pretext for an expansion that was already in full swing, particularly in the cotton industry.\" The manufacturing optimism was boundless: \"'How can we ever produce too much? We have 300 million people to clothe,' I was told at the time by a Manchester manufacturer.\" (The \"I\" is Marx's own voice, at one remove in Engels' reconstruction.) The four sources of demand that Engels identifies as driving the boom are: the export demand, the new China market, the investment in railways (the passion for speculative building that diverted surplus-value), and the general availability of cheap credit. Capital invested in railways in 1846–47 reached £75 million (per Q1059, Commercial Distress , 1848–57). Stock was underwritten \"to the limits of possibility, i.e. as far as there was money to cover the initial payments. As for the rest, a way would be found!\" When further calls on railway shares came due, \"recourse to credit was necessary, and the main business of the firm generally had to suffer.\" The banks' gold reserve was \"of unheard-of dimensions,\" the bank rate low (\"1½ to 2½ per cent in 1844, below 3 per cent until October 1845, then rising for a short period to 5 per cent (February 1846) before falling again to 3½ per cent in December 1846\"), and share prices at record highs. The psychological mechanism is captured in a series of rhetorical questions: \"Why let the splendid opportunity pass? Why not get into the swing of it? Why not send all that could be manufactured to foreign markets which were crying out for English goods? And why should the manufacturer himself not pocket the double profit from selling his yarn and cloth in the Far East and selling the return cargo in England?\" This is the origin of \"the system of mass consignments to India and China against advances, which developed very soon into a system of consignments simply for the sake of the advances,\" which \"could lead only to a massive flooding of the markets and a crash.\" The Harvest Failure and the Reversal: A Cascade in Numbers The trigger was the harvest failure of 1846: England and Ireland needed enormous imports of provisions (corn and potatoes), but the supplier countries could be paid only to an \"exceedingly small extent\" in English industrial products. Therefore \"precious metal had to be given in payment; at least £9 million in gold went abroad.\" Of this, \"a full £7½ million ... came from the Bank of England's reserves, substantially impairing its freedom of action on the money market.\" Other banks, whose reserves were with the Bank of England \"and in practice identical with its own,\" had to restrict accommodation. The cascade of rates is then transcribed as a sequence — a rate series documenting the panic: Bank rate in January 1847 at 3–3½ per cent; 7 per cent in April \"when the first panic broke out\"; a small respite in summer (6½, 6 per cent); a new and more violent panic when the new harvest was also bad; official minimum lending rate to 7 per cent in October and 10 per cent in November , \"so that the great majority of bills could be discounted only at colossal and usurious rates of interest, if at all.\" The general stagnation of payments caused bankruptcies of a few leading firms and \"very many medium and small ones\"; the Bank itself was in danger of collapse \"as a result of the clever Bank Act of 1844 and the restrictions this imposed\" (cross-referenced to Chapter 34). The denouement: the government suspended the Bank Act on 25 October, \"bowing to a universal demand, and thereby released the Bank from the absurd legal fetters imposed on it.\" Freed to issue banknotes without obstacle — \"and since the credit of these banknotes was actually guaranteed by the credit of the nation, and thus unimpaired, the monetary tightness was decisively eased.\" Many firms still collapsed, but \"the peak of the crisis was over,\" and the Bank rate fell to 5 per cent in December. By 1848 a revival began, \"breaking the edge of the revolutionary movements on the Continent in 1849\" and leading to unprecedented prosperity in the 1850s, \"only to be followed by the crash of 1857.\" The narrative thus completes a long-wave cycle — boom, crisis, revival, prosperity, crash — demonstrating that the crisis is not terminal but cyclical. The Notes: Railway Swindle, Speculative Bills, East India Swindling, Panic The five numbered notes supply the concrete texture of fictitious capital in operation. Note 1 documents the devaluation of securities between February and 23 October 1847: English government bonds fell by £93,824,217; dock and canal stock by £1,358,288; railway stock by £19,579,820 — a total of £114,762,325 . The base of the collapse — the evaporation of paper wealth — is precisely the fictitious capital of the title. Note 2 provides the mechanism of the \"swindling in the East India trade, where bills were no longer drawn because commodities had been sold, but rather commodities sold in order to draw bills which could be discounted and converted into money.\" The Manchester Guardian of 24 November 1847 describes the round-robin: A instructs B to buy goods from C; B pays C in six-month drafts; B secures himself with six-month drafts on A; once the goods are shipped, A draws six-month drafts on D against the mailed bill of lading. \"The shipper and the co-signee were thus both put in possession of funds — months before they actually paid for the goods; and, very commonly, these bills were renewed at maturity, on pretence of affording time for the returns in a 'long trade.'\" The fatal dynamic: \"Unfortunately losses by such a trade, instead of leading to its contraction, led directly to its increase. The poorer men became, the greater need they had to purchase, in order to make up, by new advances, the capital they had lost on the past adventures. Purchases thus became, not a question of supply and demand, but the most important part of the finance operations of a firm labouring under difficulties.\" The same logic held abroad: Indian houses bought sugar, indigo, silk, or cotton \"not because the prices advised from London by the last overland mail promised a profit on the prices current in India, but because former drafts upon the London house would soon fall due, and must be provided for.\" The technique: purchase a cargo with bills at ten months' date, transmit the shipping documents by overland mail, and within two months pawn the goods \"on the high seas, or perhaps not yet passed the mouth of the Hoogly ... in Lombard Street — putting the London house in funds eight months before the drafts against those goods fell due.\" All this functioned \"as long as bill-brokers had abundance of money 'at call,' to advance on bills of lading and dock warrants, and to discount, without limit, the bills of India houses drawn upon the eminent firms in Mincing Lane.\" Engels appends a remarkable footnote noting that this fraudulent procedure \"remained in vogue as long as goods had to sail to and from India round the Cape.\" With the Suez Canal and steamships, the long journey time (the foundation of the fictitious capital) disappears; and with the telegraph, \"the state of the Indian market [is made] known to the English businessman the same day, and the state of the English market to the Indian dealer, such a method has become completely impossible.\" This is a striking instance of technological change altering the conditions of fictitious capital — a historical-materialist nuance rare in Marx's own text and contributed by his editor. Note 3 documents the Bank of England's contraction of credit to the Royal Bank of Liverpool in April 1847 and its effects. Payments into Liverpool had become \"much more in bills than in cash\"; merchants who previously brought cash could bring only bills received for produce. The bills were \"of various dates, and of various descriptions; a considerable number of them were bankers' bills, of three months' date, the large bulk being cotton bills.\" The bills had been accepted by London bankers and merchants \"in every trade that we could mention — the Brazilian, the American, the Canadian, the West Indian.\" Parties in the interior \"remitted to the merchants bills on London bankers, or bills on various parties in London, or bills upon anybody.\" The Bank's announcement caused \"a reduction of the maturity terms of bills drawn against sales of foreign products, frequently extending to over three months.\" Also in Note 3 comes the railway account . In April 1847 \"almost all mercantile houses had begun to starve their business more or less ... by taking part of their commercial capital for railways.\" Loans on railway shares at 8 per cent had been made by private individuals, bankers, and fire-offices. Heavy lending by commercial houses to railways \"induced them to lean too much upon banks by the discount of paper.\" Yet on the question of whether the railway calls caused the money-market pressure in April and October, the testimony is nuanced: \"up to April, and up, perhaps, to the summer, they had increased the power of bankers in some respects rather than diminished it; for the expenditure had not been nearly so rapid as the calls; the consequence was, that most of the banks had rather a large amount of railway money in their hands in the beginning of the year.\" In summer that melted away; \"between the 22nd of April and the 31st of December the railway balances in our hands were reduced one-third; and the railway calls have also had this effect ... throughout the Kingdom; they have been gradually draining the deposits of bankers.\" Samuel Gurney (of Overend, Gurney and Co.) even claimed the railways \"did not increase the value of money\" because \"there was a condensation of small sums into large masses, and those large masses were used in our market; so that, upon the whole, the effect was to throw more money into the money-market of the City than to take it out.\" This is a nuance Marx wishes the reader to register: the causal picture is complex, the timing of calls versus expenditures matters, and the bankers themselves testified in partial contradiction to each other. A. Hodgson, director of the Liverpool Joint-Stock Bank, then demonstrates the collapse of the distinction between a banker's \"reserve\" and illiquid bills: \"It has been our habit to keep at least nine-tenths of all our deposits, and all money we have of other persons, in our bill case, in bills that are falling due from day to day ... so much so, that during the time of the run, the bills falling due were almost equal to the amount of the run upon us day by day.\" The reserve fund exists only in the form of bills maturing daily — which is safe only if the bills are actually paid. The \"speculative bills\" testimony (R. Gardner, cotton manufacturer) describes how the bills were generated: \"Produce brokers: a person buys cotton, and places it in the hands of a broker, and draws upon that broker, and gets the bills discounted.\" They were taken to the Liverpool banks and discounted. Gardner is explicit that \"if it had not been for the accommodation thus granted, and principally by the Liverpool banks, cotton would never have been so high last year as it was by 1½d. or 2d. a pound.\" Hodgson confirms the practice extended to \"all kinds of colonial produce, but to cotton most especially,\" and — crucially — that bankers did not discourage it: \"We do not; we consider it a very legitimate description of paper, when kept in moderation. This description of paper is frequently renewed.\" Charles Turner's testimony (head of a leading Liverpool East India house) gives vivid evidence of advances \"before it was shipped, and in some cases before it was manufactured.\" He describes buying bills in Calcutta to the extent of £6,000–7,000, the proceeds going down to the Mauritius \"to help in the growth of sugar\"; when the shipments came forward, above half the bills were protested because the sugar had been mortgaged to third parties \"before it was shipped, in fact almost before it was boiled.\" He then describes the machinery of the Indian trade in 1847: buyers can draw on a London house and get the bill discounted to pay manufacturers cash, because \"discounts now are cheap.\" Since returns from India take at least twelve months, a man with £10,000–15,000 opens a credit with a London house (paying the house one per cent), the understanding being that \"the bills are to be renewed till the proceeds come home.\" The bills are discounted at Liverpool, Manchester, or London, and \"many of them lie in the Scotch banks.\" Question 786 describes a transaction creating £600,000 of bills upon a single underlying operation — Manchester and Calcutta houses with reciprocal credits of £200,000 each against a London house, where the proceeds of bills sold in Calcutta were to buy other bills to remit to London to take up the first bills drawn from Glasgow. Q971 explains that a Calcutta house buying a cargo for England can draw bills on its London correspondent, send the bills of lading home, \"and they have eight months' use of the money before their correspondents are called upon to pay.\" Note 4 rehearses Mr Lister's testimony before the House of Lords' secret committee of 1848 (evidence published only in 1857). The causes of the crisis: \"a man transferred property from business into railways and was still anxious to carry on the same extent of business. He probably first thought that he could sell the railway shares at a profit and replace the money in his business. Perhaps he found that could not be done, and he then got credit in his business where formerly he paid in cash. There was an extension of credit from that circumstance.\" The losses fell on \"bills upon all kinds of produce, corn and cotton and sugar, all foreign produce of all descriptions. There was scarcely anything perhaps with the exception of oil, that did not go down.\" The structural description of produce bills: a broker accepts only with \"a good margin as to the value\"; the original bill drawn abroad falls due before the produce arrives; if the merchant lacks capital, he must pledge the produce with the broker, whereupon \"a new species of bill is immediately drawn by the merchant in Liverpool upon the broker, on the security of that produce\"; and \"it is the business of the banker to ascertain from the broker whether he has the produce, and to what extent he has advanced upon it.\" Lister also notes the epistemic opacity of international bills: \"A man buys a bill abroad on England, and sends it to a house in England; we cannot tell whether that bill is drawn prudently or imprudently, whether it is drawn for produce or for wind.\" Finally, the cause of the losses was \"a very large import, and there not being an equal consumption to take it off. It appears that consumption fell off a great deal.\" And: \"In October produce was almost unsaleable.\" The panic psychology is captured in Gurney's testimony: \"When a panic exists a man does not ask himself what he can get for his bank-notes, or whether he shall lose 1 or 2 per cent by selling his exchequer bills, or 3 per cent. If he is under the influence of alarm he does not care for the profit or loss, but makes himself safe and allows the rest of the world to do as they please.\" This sauve-qui-peut — every man for himself — is the subjective form of the objective dissolution of the credit system. Note 5 , the coda, returns to the theme of mutual over-trading. Mr Alexander, a merchant in the East India trade, testified in 1857: \"At the present moment, if I lay out 6s. in Manchester, I get 5s. back in India; if I lay out 6s. in India, I get 5s. back in London.\" Marx's gloss: \"So that the Indian market was satiated by England and the English market similarly by India. And this was the case in 1857, scarcely ten years after the bitter experience of 1847!\" The chapter thus closes with a demonstration that the credit mechanism persists and reproduces its crisis — a single decade suffices for the \"bitter experience\" to be repeated, evidencing the structural (not accidental) character of the phenomenon. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS What fundamental principles does the chapter establish? The first is the derivation of credit from the money-form itself . Because commodities are routinely sold not for money but for a promise to pay at a future date, the creditor-debtor relation is built into the simplest acts of commodity circulation; credit is therefore not a late invention but a development of money as means of payment. The second is the pyramid structure of the credit system : mutual advances among producers and merchants constitute the \"real basis,\" the bill of exchange is the \"basis of credit money proper,\" and banknotes and deposits are erected on the circulation of bills, themselves resting on a narrow metallic base. The third, and the chapter's title-theme, is fictitious capital : the systemic production of claims (bills, banknotes, deposits, cheques) whose aggregate vastly exceeds the underlying money, and which, so long as no run occurs, perform the functions of money perfectly. The fourth principle is the ungovernability of the distinction between legitimate and fictitious paper — Leatham's confession that \"it is impossible to decide what part arises out of real bona fide transactions ... or what part is fictitious\" — which undermines any mechanical regulatory solution. The fifth is the cyclical logic of the boom-crisis-revival sequence : cheap credit facilitates speculation (Gilbart's principle), speculation generates fictitious paper, the conversion of bills into claims against money produces a general demand for the base, the base proves too narrow (Leatham's \"superstructure\" warning), and the whole system undergoes a forced contraction until the suspension of the Bank Act restores confidence by making the credit of the nation stand behind the notes. The practical implications for the analysis of capitalism are profound. First, the crisis emerging from this chapter is immanent , not accidental — its conditions lie in the normal functioning of credit, not in the folly of individuals. The Manchester manufacturer's question — \"How can we ever produce too much? We have 300 million people to clothe\" — illustrates the optimism that credit makes possible and the delusion it sustains. Second, the chapter reframes the standard policy debates: the Currency School's obsession with note issue is misplaced, because the deposit and the endorsement chain (not the note) are where fictitious capital really forms. The Bank Act of 1844, which sought to discipline note issue by a rigid rule, is here shown to be \"the clever Bank Act\" precisely because it intensified the crisis at the moment when flexibility was most needed. Third, the chapter gives a devastating account of how the real economy is subordinated to the credit mechanism: firms produce and ship goods \"not because the prices ... promised a profit ... but because former drafts ... would soon fall due, and must be provided for.\" The circuit of credit becomes autonomous relative to the circuit of production, and producers become servants of the paper they must continually roll over. The theoretical consequences extend further. The distinction between the \"circulation of capital\" and the \"circulation of currency\" (Tooke, referenced here and developed in Chapter 28) separates credit that funds production from credit that merely transfers revenue — a distinction with large implications for the analysis of money demand. The empirical data on the Clearing House, the 15-to-1 ratio of settlements to money, the 9-to-1 ratio of Scottish deposits to currency, the 11-to-1 ratio of convertible liabilities to gold, and the £600,000 of bills erected on a single underlying transaction all express the same formal relation in different registers: credit organizes a vast economy of property transfers that does nothing but refer back, ultimately, to a narrow metallic and institutional base. The consequences of a break in that reference are catastrophic in the technical sense: the paper multiplies in reverse, and banks contract to the metallic foundation. The philosophical implication is that capitalist wealth, in its credit form, is not so much possessed as promised — and that capitalism's endemic tendency to crisis flows from the impossibility of fully reconciling the promises with the means of fulfillment. The chapter thus prepares the theoretical path to Chapters 28, 30, and 34, where Marx will develop the analysis of the Bank Act, the money market, and the relation of credit to the general law of capitalist accumulation. And its closing note on 1857 — the repetition of the same swindle a decade after 1847 — makes clear that the phenomenon is not a story of one crisis but the recurring signature of a system whose normal condition is the persistent overhang of fictitious capital over real wealth. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 24 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 26"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 26", "title": "Chapter 26", "subtitle": "Accumulation of Money Capital; Its Influence on the Interest Rate", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 26/Chapter 26 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 11993, "read_time": "60 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital", "Interest-Bearing Capital & Loan Capital"], "keywords": ["Accumulation of Money Capital and Its Influence on Interest", "Difference between Real Capital Accumulation and Money Hoards", "Credit Swells and Panics", "Low Interest Rates during Industrial Stagnation", "High Interest Spikes during Monetary Panics"], "text": "Chapter 26 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 12,001 words ⏱️ ~60 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 26 of Capital , Volume III, titled \"Accumulation of Money Capital, and its Influence on the Rate of Interest,\" is a densely forensic chapter that belongs to the broader Part V of the work, \"The Division of Profit into Interest and Profit of Enterprise.\" Its central problem is deceptively narrow: how is the rate of interest actually determined, and what is the relationship between the accumulation of money capital and that rate? But the chapter's true intellectual objective is polemical and theoretical in equal measure. Marx sets out to demolish the theoretical pretensions of the Currency School — represented above all by Samuel Jones Loyd (Lord Overstone), a banker and the intellectual architect of the 1844 Bank Act, and by William Norman, a director of the Bank of England — by demonstrating that their account of the rate of interest is either a tautology, a confusion of money with capital, or a self-serving apology for the banker's parasitic gains during crises. The chapter proceeds by an unusual method. Rather than constructing a deductive argument in the abstract, Marx arranges a parade of primary evidence: lengthy extracts from the 1847-48 parliamentary report on Commercial Distress, from the 1857 Select Committee on Bank Acts, from contemporary pamphlets such as The Currency Theory Reviewed (1845) and J. G. Hubbard's The Currency and the Country (1843), and from the testimony of bankers, merchants, and witnesses. Into this evidential stream Marx inserts a running, often sarcastic, commentary, and Engels interpolates a long editorial clarification distinguishing an advance of capital from an advance of money. The chapter is thus both a work of economic theory and an exercise in ideological unmasking: it treats parliamentary testimony as a text to be decoded, exposing the class interests that hide behind the technical vocabulary of \"value of capital,\" \"supply and demand of capital,\" and \"the quantity of money.\" The core thesis that emerges is threefold. First, the rate of interest is determined by the demand and supply of money capital — that is, of loanable money funds — as distinct from capital in its material or commodity form. Second, the \"value of capital\" invoked by Overstone is either simply another name for the rate of interest itself (a tautology) or a covert appeal to the rate of profit, which, while it forms a general upper limit to interest, does not explain how interest is fixed within that limit. Third, and most corrosive, the bankers' claim that they \"advance capital\" to industry is in most ordinary cases false: discounting a bill of exchange is merely the conversion of a commercial claim into money, a sale and purchase, not a one-sided transfer of capital. The banker's habit of seeing every payment as an advance is a professional deformation that, when imported into political economy, generates the whole confused controversy over whether the banker lends capital or mere \"currency.\" The chapter fits into Volume III's larger architecture as the monetary and credit-theoretic complement to the analysis of the division of profit. Where earlier chapters establish that profit splits into interest and profit of enterprise, this chapter asks what determines the size of the interest portion, and it locates the answer in the sphere of money capital — credit, bank reserves, gold flows, and the state of the reproduction process. It also sets up the later, more systematic treatments of the credit system (Chapters 32 and 33) and the Currency Principle (Chapter 34), explicitly cross-referencing them. The chapter thus functions as a hinge: it moves from the abstract division of surplus value into its concrete institutional forms, and it prepares the ground for the critique of the banking and currency legislation that Marx regards as the legal codification of the Currency School's illusions. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Framing Testimony: Surplus Wealth, Its Money Form, and the Search for Employment The chapter opens not with Marx's own voice but with a long quotation from The Currency Theory Reviewed (London, 1845, pp. 32-4), a pamphlet whose author Marx uses as a foil and an informant. The passage lays out the problem that the chapter will spend its length dissolving. In England, it says, there takes place \"a steady accumulation of additional wealth, which has a tendency ultimately to assume the form of money.\" Here we encounter the first key concept of the chapter: money capital , the form that surplus wealth takes when it seeks investment. The point is immediately sharpened by an axiom of great theoretical weight: \"money itself, as money, yields neither.\" Money held as money is barren; it must be parted with for \"some species of investment that shall yield either interest or profit.\" This observation already contains the germ of the distinction the chapter will enforce — money as such is not capital that produces a return; it becomes interest-bearing capital only when it is lent or invested. From this, the author derives a dynamic law of recurring glut: unless \"concurrently with this ceaseless influx of surplus capital, there is a gradual and sufficient extension of the field for its employment,\" England must suffer \"periodical accumulations of money seeking investment.\" The image is hydraulic — surplus wealth flows in ceaselessly, and if the channels of investment do not widen correspondingly, it pools up. The pamphlet then provides a crucial historical illustration: for a long series of years, \"the grand absorbent of the surplus wealth of England was our public debt.\" When in 1816 the debt reached its maximum and \"operated no longer as an absorbent,\" at least twenty-seven million pounds per annum \"was necessarily driven to seek other channels of investment,\" alongside \"various return payments of capital.\" The argument culminates in a claim Marx clearly regards as revealing: enterprises entailing large capital and creating openings for surplus capital \"are absolutely necessary... to take care of the periodical accumulations of the superfluous wealth of society, which is unable to find room in the usual fields of application.\" Thus the construction of railways, canals, and grand infrastructure is presented not as a response to genuine productive need but as a sink for idle money capital — a theme Marx will later validate when he lists \"over-investment in railways\" among the causes of the 1847 crisis. The same author's 1845 observations complete the picture of an economy awash in unemployed wealth: prices \"sprung upwards from the lowest point of depression,\" Consols touching par, bullion in the vaults of the Bank of England exceeding \"the treasure held by that establishment since its institution,\" shares ranging at \"wholly unprecedented\" prices, and interest \"declined to rates which are all but nominal.\" These are read as evidence that \"another heavy accumulation of unemployed wealth exists at this hour in England, that another period of speculative excitement is at hand.\" The theoretical payload here is the inverse correlation between the abundance of loanable money and the rate of interest: glut depresses interest to near zero, and speculation is the outlet. Hubbard's Mechanism: Gold Flows, Idle Money-Capital, and the Rate of Interest Marx next introduces J. G. Hubbard, The Currency and the Country (London, 1843), whose testimony supplies a stylised mechanism of gold flows and interest rates. Hubbard first concedes a methodological caveat: \"the import of bullion is no sure sign of gain upon the foreign trade,\" though \"in the absence of any explanatory cause\" it does \"prima facie represent a portion\" of such gain. The substantive example is a deficient harvest requiring an import of corn and an export of gold to the value of five million pounds. Here Marx inserts a crucial parenthetical gloss: the \"circulation\" that Hubbard says is reduced means \"idle money-capital rather than means of circulation.\" This distinction — between money functioning as idle loanable capital and money functioning as a medium of circulation — is the conceptual pivot of the entire chapter, and Marx flags it immediately so that the reader will not be misled by the currency theorists' habitual conflation. Hubbard's mechanism runs as follows. The circulation \"would of course be reduced by the same amount.\" An equal quantity of circulation might still be held by individuals, but the deposits of merchants at their bankers, the balances of bankers with their money-broker, and the reserve in their till \"will all be diminished,\" and \"the immediate result of this reduction in the amount of unemployed capital will be a rise in the rate of interest,\" assumed to move from four per cent to six. Trade being sound, \"confidence will not be shaken, but credit will be more highly valued.\" The reverse case is symmetrical: if all prices fall, \"the superfluous currency returns to the bankers in increased deposits — the abundance of unemployed capital lowers the rate of interest to a minimum,\" until higher prices or more active trade \"call the dormant currency into service,\" or until it is absorbed by foreign investments. The mechanism is thus a crude quantity relation: more idle money capital, lower interest; less idle money capital, higher interest. Marx presents it as a useful description, but his later critique will show that the bankers mistake this superficial money-market relation for the causal determination of interest by real capital. The 1847-48 Crisis: The Harvest Failure, Suspension of the Bank Act, and the Anatomy of Panic The chapter now turns to the Report on Commercial Distress 1847-8, which becomes the central historical case study. The harvest failure and famine of 1846-7 forced a major import of foodstuffs. The report's narrative of contagion is quoted at length: imports \"very largely in excess over... exports,\" a \"considerable drain upon the banks,\" and \"an increased application to the discount brokers... for the discount of bills.\" The brokers \"began to scrutinize the bills\" and the \"facilities of houses then began to be very seriously curtailed, and the weak houses began to fail.\" Houses that \"relied upon their credit... went down,\" which \"increased the alarm\"; bankers, finding they could no longer rely with the same confidence on \"turning their bills and other money securities into bank-notes,\" curtailed facilities and \"in many cases refused them altogether.\" They \"locked up their bank-notes... to meet their own engagements; they were afraid of parting with them.\" Alarm and confusion increased daily, and \"unless Lord John Russell... had issued the letter to the Bank... universal bankruptcy would have been the issue.\" This passage dramatises a phenomenon Marx will later formalise: in crisis, the demand is not for capital but for means of payment and for liquidity . Russell's letter suspended the Bank Act (of 1844), the legislative embodiment of the Currency Principle that Overstone would spend the rest of the chapter defending. The suspension is, in effect, an admission by the authorities that the Act was strangling commerce by rigidly tying the note issue to the gold reserve. Charles Turner's testimony sharpens the point about the form of wealth: \"Some houses had large means, but not available. The whole of their capital was locked up in estates in the Mauritius, or indigo factories, or sugar factories.\" Having liabilities of five or six hundred thousand pounds and \"no available assets to pay their bills,\" they were \"entirely dependent upon their credit.\" This is the distinction between capital and liquidity in its starkest form: capital in the form of colonial estates is real, but in a crisis it cannot be converted into money at anything but a ruinous discount. Samuel Gurney reinforces the diagnosis: \"At present (1848) there is a limitation of transaction and a great super-abundance of money\" (i.e., abundance of loanable money alongside depressed trade), and — in a line Marx will return to — \"I do not think it was owing to the want of capital; it was owing to the alarm that existed that the rate of interest got so high.\" The scale of the drain is quantified: in 1847 England paid at least nine million pounds abroad in gold for food imports, £7½ million from the Bank of England and £1½ million from other sources. Morris, Governor of the Bank, testified that by 23 October 1847, public stocks and canal and railway shares had depreciated by £114,752,225. Under Bentinck's questioning, Morris conceded that all property invested in stocks and produce was depreciated; raw cotton, silk, and wool were sent to the continent at depreciated prices; sugar, coffee, and tea \"were sacrificed as at forced sales.\" Morris declared it \"inevitable that the country should make a considerable sacrifice for the purpose of meeting the efflux of bullion.\" When asked whether it would not have been better \"to trench upon the £8,000,000 lying in the coffers of the Bank\" than to get gold back \"at such a sacrifice,\" Morris replied flatly: \"No, I do not.\" Marx labels this exchange, with heavy irony, the \"commentary on this heroism\" — meaning the defence of the gold reserve at the expense of the productive economy. The Distributive Fallout: Dividends, Hoarding, and the Money-Dealer's Harvest Marx then turns to the distributional question: who benefited from the crisis and the Bank Act? Disraeli's examination of Mr W. Cotton, a Bank director and former Governor, establishes that the dividend to Bank proprietors was 7 per cent in 1844 and 9 per cent in 1847, and that the Bank now paid the income tax for its proprietors, which it had not done in 1844. Disraeli's summary — \"since the passing of the Act, the dividend to the proprietors has been raised from 7 per cent to 9 per cent, and the income tax... is now paid by the Bank\" — is confirmed by Cotton. Engels's footnote 83 explains the technical point: before 1844 the Bank fixed the dividend and then deducted income tax as it was paid to individual shareholders; after 1844 the Bank first paid income tax on its total profit, and the dividend was then paid \"free of income tax,\" so the same nominal percentages are higher by the amount of the tax. The Bank Act, in short, worked \"very well for the proprietors.\" The theme of deliberate hoarding is then documented through Mr Pease, a country banker: as the Bank raised its rate, \"every one seemed apprehensive; country bankers increased the amount of bullion in their hands, and increased their reserve of notes,\" and many who had kept \"a few hundred pounds of gold and bank-notes, immediately laid up thousands in our desks and drawers.\" Uncertainty about discounts and about bills being current in the market produced a \"general hoarding.\" A committee member drew the conclusion in class terms: \"whatever may have been the cause during the last 12 years, the result has been rather in favour of the Jew and money-dealer, than the productive classes generally.\" Marx's inclusion of this remark — with its contemporary antisemitic idiom, which Marx does not soften — shows his interest in the class incidence of monetary policy. Tooke adds the decisive detail that in the hardware districts of Warwickshire and Staffordshire, \"a great many orders for goods were declined to be accepted in 1847, because the rate of interest which the manufacturer had to pay for discounting his bills more than absorbed all his profit.\" Here the rate of interest is shown to have devoured the profit of enterprise, a phenomenon that will become central to Marx's critique of Overstone's claim that high interest cannot destroy profit. Norman and the Currency Principle: The \"Commodities Used in Production\" Theory of Interest The chapter's theoretical core begins with the testimony of Mr Norman, a Bank of England director and \"a leading spokesman for the Currency Principle,\" before the 1857 Select Committee on Bank Acts (B. A. 1857). Norman states (3635) that the rate of interest depends \"not upon the amount of notes, but upon the supply and demand of capital,\" and defines capital as \"commodities or services used in production\" (3636). Pressed on whether he includes all commodities in \"capital\" when speaking of the rate of interest, he answers yes. In 3637 he offers a naturalistic illustration: a cotton manufacturer borrows notes from his banker, goes to Liverpool, buys cotton; \"what he really wants is the cotton; he does not want the notes or the gold, except as a means of getting the cotton\"; or he borrows notes to pay wages, and the workers use money to buy food and lodging. In 3638, when asked \"But interest is paid for the money?\", Norman concedes \"it is, in the first instance,\" but then pivots: if the manufacturer buys cotton on credit, \"the difference between the ready-money price and the credit price at the time at which he is to pay for it is the measure of the interest. Interest would exist if there was no money at all.\" Marx's critique of this passage is a model of his method. He dismisses it as \"complacent rubbish... entirely worthy of this pillar of the Currency Principle,\" and identifies its logical structure. Norman's \"discovery\" is the trivial observation that banknotes and gold are means of buying things and that people do not borrow them for their own sake. From this he infers that the interest rate is governed by \"the demand and supply of commodities, which is what we have always been told governs the market price of commodities.\" Marx's objection is decisive: \"But quite different rates of interest are compatible with the same market prices.\" The demand-and-supply of cotton explains the price of cotton; it does not explain why the rate of interest should be three per cent at one time and seven per cent at another when commodity prices are unchanged. Norman's theory fails on its own terms because it cannot produce a determinate interest rate. Marx's Inversion: The Rate of Interest as the Measure of the Cash-Credit Price Gap Faced with the correct remark \"But interest is paid for the money\" — which implies the question of what the banker's interest, earned without any dealing in commodities, has to do with commodities — Norman answers that the difference between the ready-money price and the credit price of cotton is the measure of interest. Marx seizes on this as an inversion. \"Quite the opposite,\" he writes. \"The prevailing rate of interest, the regulation of which it is the task of our genius Norman to explain, is the measure of the difference between the cash price and the price on credit.\" Marx then works out the mechanics in a fully specified example that functions as the chapter's central proof. Cotton is for sale at its cash price, determined by the market price, itself governed by demand and supply. \"Say that the price is £1,000.\" This concludes the sale-and-purchase transaction between manufacturer and cotton broker. \"But now there is a second transaction as well. This is one between lender and borrower.\" The value of £1,000 is advanced to the manufacturer in cotton, and he must repay it in money \"say in three months' time.\" The interest on £1,000 for three months, \"as determined by the market rate of interest,\" forms \"the extra charge over and above the cash price.\" Marx summarises the separability: \"The price of cotton is determined by supply and demand. But the price for the advance of the cotton's value for three months, for the £1,000, is determined by the rate of interest.\" The transformation of cotton into money capital in this credit relation, Marx notes, is what \"proves to Mr Norman that interest would exist even if money did not.\" Marx's rebuttal is categorical: \"If there was no money at all, there would certainly not be a general rate of interest.\" This passage quietly advances a substantive theory: interest is the price of advancing value over time, and it is determined on the money market independently of the prices of the commodities whose value is advanced. Norman's confusion is to read the temporal price margin backwards as the effect of commodity supply and demand rather than as the independent variable set by the money market. The Profit Rate as Limit and the Demand for Money Capital Marx next extracts the kernel of truth from the banker's vocabulary while rejecting its causal claims. The \"vulgar conception of capital as 'commodities used in production'\" contains an ambiguity: in so far as commodities figure as capital, they express their value as capital — as distinct from their value as commodities — \"in the profit that is made from their productive or commercial use.\" The rate of profit \"necessarily has always something to do with the market price of the commodities bought and the demand and supply for them, even if it is determined by quite different factors.\" Here Marx makes a concession that is also a boundary: \"There is no doubt at all that the rate of profit forms a general limit to the rate of interest.\" But, he insists, what Norman was supposed to explain is \"just how this limit is determined\" — and it is determined \"by the demand and supply for money capital as distinct from other forms of capital.\" Marx then poses the next-order question: how is the demand and supply for money capital itself determined? He answers with two linked propositions. There is \"beyond doubt a tacit connection between the supply of material capital and the supply of money capital,\" and \"it is equally clear that the industrial capitalists' demand for money is determined by the circumstances of actual production.\" In other words, the money market is not autonomous in the last instance; it is conditioned by the real reproduction process. Norman, \"instead of enlightening us on this subject,\" merely repeats the wisdom that the demand for money capital is not identical with the demand for money as such. Marx reads this evasion psychologically: it exists \"only because Overstone, he, and the other currency prophets always have at the back of their minds a bad conscience about the way they are seeking by way of artificial legislative intervention to make the means of circulation into capital as such, and to raise the rate of interest.\" Here the theoretical critique and the political critique fuse: the Currency School's confusion is not innocent error but the intellectual cover for legislation designed to enrich bankers. Overstone's First Foray: The \"Value of Capital\" Tautology Lord Overstone (Samuel Jones Loyd) now takes the stage, and Marx quotes his testimony at 3653: fluctuations in the rate of interest arise from \"one of two causes: an alteration in the value of capital\" or \"an alteration in the amount of money in the country.\" Overstone claims all great fluctuations, whether in duration or extent, \"may be distinctly traced to alterations in the value of capital,\" citing the rise of 1847 and of 1855-6, while the minor fluctuations arising from the quantity of money \"are small both in extent and in duration.\" Marx's comment is sarcastic: \"Superb! The value of capital, generally speaking is precisely the rate of interest!\" A change in the rate of interest is thus derived from a change in the rate of interest — a pure tautology. Marx then offers the alternative reading: if Overstone means by \"value of capital\" the rate of profit, \"then this penetrating thinker comes back to the fact that the interest rate is governed by the profit rate\" — which, while true as a limiting relation, provides no account of the actual determination. The rest of the chapter is, in effect, an extended elaboration of this dilemma: Overstone is caught between tautology and an unexplained appeal to profit. Gurney's Naive Confession Before pressing the theoretical case, Marx interposes Samuel Gurney's testimony before the House of Lords committee. Asked whether great fluctuations in the rate of interest are advantageous to bankers or dealers in money, Gurney answers: \"I think they are advantageous to dealers in money. All fluctuations in trade are advantageous to the knowing man.\" Asked whether the banker might ultimately suffer by impoverishing his best customers, he says: \"No; I do not think it has that effect perceptibly.\" Marx's single line of French — \"Voila ce que parler veut dire\" (\"That's what I call talking\") — signals that Gurney has unwittingly admitted the class truth of the matter: the money-dealer profits from volatility, including volatility that ruins his clients. This is the \"naive\" counterpart to Overstone's evasive sophistication. The 1847 Crisis Dissected: The Quid Pro Quo and the Dearth of Money Capital Marx now promises to return later to the question of how the rate of interest is influenced by the sum of money available, but states that Overstone is already \"guilty here again of a quid pro quo.\" He constructs the true causal account of 1847. The demand for money capital decreased for various reasons before October (there was \"no worry about monetary tightness\" before that point). Then a heterogeneous list of causes converged: \"Dearer corn, rising cotton prices, the unsaleability of sugar on account of overproduction, railway speculation and crash, the flooding of foreign markets with cotton goods, the forcible export and import trade with India... for the purpose of speculation in bills of exchange.\" Marx's analytical summary is precise: \"All these things, overproduction in industry as well as underproduction in agriculture, i.e. quite different reasons, led to a rise in the demand for money capital, i.e. for credit and money.\" And crucially: \"The increased demand for money capital had its origins in the course of the production process itself.\" This is the theoretical heart of Marx's positive account. The demand for money capital is not an autonomous monetary phenomenon; it is generated by disturbances in the real reproduction process — harvest failure, overproduction, speculative misallocation. Once generated, however, it operates through the money market: \"whatever the cause, it was the demand for money capital that made the rate of interest, the value of money capital, rise.\" Marx then dismantles Overstone's explanation as a tautology: \"If Overstone is trying to say that the value of money capital rose because it rose, this is a tautology. But if by 'value of capital' what he means here is a rise in the profit rate as a cause of the rise in the rate of interest, this immediately proves to be false.\" The proof is a simple but powerful observation: \"The demand for money capital, and thus the 'value of capital', can rise even though profit is falling; as soon as the relative supply of money capital falls, its 'value' rises.\" Marx then exposes Overstone's political motive: to prove that the 1847 crisis and its high interest had \"nothing to do with the 'quantity of money' present, i.e. with the provisions of the 1844 Bank Act which he had inspired.\" Marx concedes a nuance — the Act was not the sole cause — but insists it was a cause: \"as soon as fear of exhaustion of the Bank's reserve (and this was a creation of Overstone's) added monetary panic to the 1847-8 crisis.\" Then he returns to the substantive cause: \"There was a dearth of money capital brought about by the excessive size of operations, in comparison with the means available, and brought to a head by a disturbance in the reproduction process...\" He illustrates with the corn dealers: what those who had bought corn at 120 shillings per quarter lacked, when the price fell to 60 shillings, \"was the 60 shillings too much which they had paid, and the corresponding credit for this in loans with the corn as security.\" Here is a crucial analytical point: \"It was in no way a lack of banknotes that prevented them from converting their corn into money at the former price of 120 shillings.\" The same applies to the over-importers of sugar and to those who had tied up floating capital in railways and replaced it by conducting their business on credit. Marx then formulates the relationship between money capital and real capital that is perhaps his most important theoretical contribution in the chapter: \"But this enhanced value of money capital corresponded directly to the fallen monetary value of real capital (commodity capital and productive capital). The value of capital in the one form rose, because the value of capital in the other form fell.\" Overstone's error is to \"identify these two values of two different kinds of capital in a single unique value of capital,\" and to oppose both to \"a lack of means of circulation, of ready money.\" The final observation is lapidary: \"The same amount of money capital, however, can be loaned with very different quantities of the circulation medium.\" Value of money capital and quantity of currency are independent variables; Overstone conflates them. The Bank Rate Table of 1847: Interest Rises as Profits Fall Marx now deploys Overstone's own example as empirical refutation. The official Bank rate in 1847 ran as follows: January: 3–3½ per cent February: 4–4½ per cent March: generally 4 per cent April (panic): 4–7½ per cent May: 5–5½ per cent June: mostly 5½ per cent July: 5 per cent August: 5–5½ per cent September: 5 per cent, with minor variations of 5¼, 5½, and 6 per cent October: 5, 5½, 7 per cent November: 7–10 per cent December: 7–5 per cent The trajectory — from 3 per cent to a peak of 10 per cent, then a partial retreat — is read by Marx as the empirical signature of a crisis of reproduction: \"In this case interest rose because profits declined and the money values of commodities fell enormously.\" This directly falsifies Overstone's claim that the rise was due to a rise in the value of capital (if that means real capital). \"So if Overstone says on this that the rate of interest rose in 1847 because the value of capital rose, he can only mean by the value of capital the value of money capital, and the value of money capital is precisely the rate of interest and nothing else. But later on he gives the game away and identifies the value of capital with the rate of profit.\" The table thus converts a theoretical point into an evidential one: interest and profit moved in opposite directions, so interest cannot have been driven by a rising rate of profit. The 1856 Credit-Jobbers and the Coming 1857 Crisis Marx notes that, as far as the high interest rate paid in 1856 is concerned, Overstone \"was in fact unaware that this was in part a symptom that the kind of credit-jobbers were coming to the fore who paid interest not out of their profits, but out of other people's capital.\" Overstone \"contended only a few months before the crisis of 1857 that 'business is perfectly sound'.\" This anticipates the great crisis of 1857, whose proximity Marx himself was observing. The reference to credit-jobbers paying interest out of capital (a kind of Ponzi dynamic) also introduces a phenomenon that will figure in the later chapters on credit: the emergence of financial operators whose returns are not grounded in productive profit. The \"Logical Absurdity\": A Thing Destroying Its Own Cause Overstone's testimony at 3722 states: \"That idea of the profits of trade being destroyed by a rise in the rate of interest is most erroneous.\" His reasons: a rise in interest is \"seldom of any long duration\"; and if it is long and large, \"it is really a rise in the value of capital, and why does value of capital rise? Because the rate of profit is increased.\" Marx extracts the admission: \"Here, then, we finally learn what the 'value of capital' means.\" He then adds a crucial correction to Overstone's implicit model: \"Besides, the rate of profit can remain high for a long period, even though profit of enterprise falls and the interest rate rises, so that interest comes to absorb the greater part of profits.\" This is the analytical distinction — between the total rate of profit or surplus value (which can remain high) and the profit of enterprise (which is what bears the subtraction of interest) — that Overstone collapses in his own argument. At 3724 Overstone declares that complaining that a rise in interest destroys its own cause (high profit) \"is a sort of logical absurdity, which one does not know how to deal with.\" Marx retorts with a syllogism: it is about as logical as saying that \"the increased profit rate has been in consequence of a rise in commodity prices brought about by speculation, and to complain that the rise in prices destroys its own cause, i.e. speculation, is a logical absurdity.\" The rhetorical page then turns into a broadside: \"Only for a usurer enamoured of his high rate of interest is it a logical absurdity that a thing can ultimately destroy its own cause. The greatness of the Romans was the cause of their conquests, and it was their conquests that destroyed their greatness. Wealth is the cause of luxury, and luxury has a destructive effect on wealth. The artful dodger!\" Marx calls Overstone \"this millionaire, this 'dung-hill aristocrat'.\" The theoretical point stands alongside the invective: \"if a high rate of profit and the expansion of business can be the cause of a high interest rate, this in no way means that a high interest rate is the cause of high profits.\" Sexa conclusively, \"the question is precisely whether this high interest persisted (as was actually discovered in the crisis) or even reached its climax after the high rate of profit had gone the way of all flesh\" — that is, whether interest outlives the profit that is supposed to sustain it. The evidence of the crisis shows that it does. The £45 to £120 Million Expansion and the Myth of Consumed Savings At 3718 Overstone offers his most substantive piece of reasoning: the great rise in the discount rate \"arises entirely from the increased value of capital.\" Over thirteen years of the Act, the trade of the country has increased \"from £45,000,000 to £120,000,000.\" This enormous demand upon capital should normally be supplied out of \"the annual savings of this country,\" but recently those savings have been \"consumed in the unprofitable expenditure of war\" (the Crimean War). \"I confess that my surprise is, that the rate of interest is not much higher than it is.\" Marx's rebuttal is multi-pronged. First, the conceptual objection: \"He seems to imagine that on the one hand there was this enormous expansion of the reproduction process, i.e. an accumulation of real capital, and that on the other hand there was a 'capital' for which an 'enormous demand' developed, in order to bring about this gigantic increase of trade!\" But \"wasn't this gigantic increase in production itself the increase in capital, and if it created a demand, did it not create at the same time the supply, and at the same time also an increased supply of money capital?\" This is a genuinely important theoretical point: real accumulation and monetary accumulation are not two separable processes; the expansion of production is itself the expansion of capital and tends to generate the corresponding money capital. Second, the empirical objection: \"If the rate of interest rose to a very high level, this was simply because the demand for money capital grew still more quickly than the supply, which means that, as industrial production expanded, it was conducted to a greater extent on the basis of credit.\" This introduces the crucial concept of accommodation : \"the real industrial expansion gave rise to an increased demand for 'accommodation', and this latter demand is evidently what our banker understands by the 'enormous demand upon capital'.\" Accommodation here has the technical sense of credit extended without the equivalent of actual commodity transactions — credit to keep the machinery going, not to finance new real investment. Third, the historical objection: \"how then did England accumulate in 1792-1815, which was a war of a quite different order from the little Crimean war?\" If war-consumed savings dry up the \"natural source\" of capital, \"from what source did capital flow?\" Since England took out no loans from abroad, \"if there was an artificial source as well as this natural one, it would certainly be the method most favoured by a nation to use the natural source in war and the artificial source in business.\" Fourth, the fundamental objection about money capital: \"if there was only the old money capital, could its effectiveness be doubled by a high rate of interest?\" Overstone \"evidently believes that the country's annual savings... are simply transformed into money capital. But if there was no real accumulation, i.e. a rise in production and an increase in the means of production, what would be the good of an accumulation of claims on this production in the money form?\" This is a crucial anti-mercantilist and anti-financialization point: claims in money form are not real wealth, and multiplying them cannot substitute for real accumulation. Marx concludes the section by naming Overstone's central equivocation: \"The rise in the 'value of capital' which follows from the high rate of profit is lumped together by Overstone with the rise that follows from an increased demand for money capital. This demand may arise from causes completely independent of the rate of profit. He himself adduces as an example that in 1847 it rose as a result of the devaluation of real capital. According to whether it suits him, he relates the value of capital either to real capital or to money capital.\" The equivocation is not accidental but systematic — it permits Overstone to slide between incompatible explanations as the argument requires. Discounting Misunderstood: Anticipation of Money vs. Borrowing Additional Capital Marx then turns to Overstone's account of discounting as \"a further sign of our banking lord's dishonesty, as well as his restricted banker's point of view.\" At 3728 Overstone declares it \"impossible\" to give an answer to what the ordinary rate of profit is. At 3729 the questioner asks whether a variation of 2 to 7 or 8 per cent in the discount rate must materially affect a profit rate of 7 to 10 per cent. Marx inserts a critical gloss: \"The question itself confuses the rate of profit of enterprise with the rate of profit, and overlooks the fact that the profit rate is the common source of both interest and profit of enterprise. The interest rate can leave the rate of profit unaffected, but not profit of enterprise.\" Overstone's reply is that parties will not pay a discount rate that seriously interrupts their profits; \"they will discontinue their business rather than do that.\" Marx comments: \"Certainly, if they could do so without being ruined. As long as their profits are high, they pay the discount rate because they wish to, and when it is low, they pay it because they have to.\" Overstone's account of discounting now unfolds: \"What is the meaning of discount? Why does a person discount a bill?... Because he wants to obtain the command of a greater quantity of capital.\" Marx interrupts with \"Halte-la!\" and offers the correct account: the businessman discounts \"because he wants to anticipate the return in money of his tied-up capital, and prevent his business coming to a standstill. Because he has to meet payments that are due. He requires more capital only if the business is going well, or if he is speculating with someone else's capital, even when business is bad. Discounting is in no way simply a means for expanding his business.\" Overstone then explains the motive for wanting more capital as profit-seeking: \"it would not be profitable to him to do so if the discount destroyed his profit.\" Marx's rejoinder: the assumption \"that bills are discounted only in order to expand a business, and that the business is expanded because it is profitable\" is false in its first clause. \"The ordinary businessman discounts his bills to anticipate the money form of his capital and in this way keep the reproduction process going; not to expand his business or spend additional capital, but rather to balance the credit he gives with the credit he takes.\" If he does want to expand on credit, \"it is little use to him to get bills of exchange discounted, as this simply converts money capital that he already has from one form to another; he would rather take out a fixed loan for a longer period.\" The credit swindler, by contrast, gets accommodation bills discounted \"to expand his business and to cover one squalid deal with another; not to make profit, but to get his hands on other people's capital.\" Marx thus distinguishes three cases: the ordinary discount of a genuine commercial bill (transformation of form), genuine long-term borrowing for expansion, and accommodation finance (pure credit swindling). Bullion and the Rate of Interest: Cayley's Trap Overstone had identified discounting with borrowing extra capital; now he retracts as \"the screws are applied.\" At 3730 he retreats: \"There is no doubt that in any particular transaction, if a person can get his command of capital at a low rate of interest rather than at a high rate of interest, taken in that limited view of the matter, that is convenient to him.\" Marx observes that it is \"not at all a limited view of the matter when Lord Overstone suddenly comes to understand by 'capital' simply his banking capital, and to see the man who discounts a bill of exchange with him as a man without capital, since his capital exists in the commodity form, or the money form of his capital is a bill which Lord Overstone converts into another money form.\" At 3732 Cayley attempts to trap Overstone into admitting the bullion-reserve theory: is it a fact that when bullion is about £9-10 million, the rate of interest is 6 or 7 per cent, and when £16 million, 3 to 4 per cent? Overstone's reply tries humour: \"I do not apprehend that that is so... but if it is, then I think we must take still more stringent measures than those adopted by the Act of 1844, because if it be true that the greater the store of bullion, the lower the rate of interest, we ought to set to work... to increase the store of bullion to an indefinite amount, and then we should get the interest down to nothing.\" Cayley persists at 3733: if £5 million of bullion is restored and interest falls to 3 or 4 per cent, how could that fall be attributed to a great decrease in trade? Overstone replies that he said the recent rise , not the fall, was connected with the great increase in trade. Marx notes the logical structure of the trap: \"If a rise in the rate of interest, together with a contraction in the gold reserve, is a sign of an expansion of business, then a fall in the rate of interest, together with an expansion in the gold reserve, must be a sign of a contraction of business. Overstone has no answer to this.\" \"Money Is the Instrument for Obtaining Capital\": Who Are the Capitalists? At 3736 Overstone makes a theoretical statement Marx singles out: \"money was the instrument for obtaining capital.\" Marx's gloss: \"This is precisely the confusion, to see it as an instrument; it is a form of capital.\" Overstone elaborates: under a drain of bullion it is \"not the capitalists, it is those who are not capitalists, who want to obtain money... because through the money they obtain the command of the capital of the capitalist to carry on the business of the persons who are not capitalists.\" Marx notes that Overstone \"now explains in so many words that manufacturers and merchants are not capitalists, and that the capitalist's only capital is simply his money capital.\" At 3737, asked whether the parties who draw bills of exchange are capitalists, Overstone retreats: \"The parties who draw bills of exchange may be, and may not be, capitalists.\" Marx: \"Now he is stuck.\" The next exchanges concern whether merchants' bills of exchange represent commodities sold or shipped. Overstone denies that such bills represent the value of commodities in the same way banknotes represent gold (3740, 3741). Marx calls this \"a little insolent.\" At 3742, asked whether it is not the merchant's object to get money, Overstone answers: \"No; getting money is not the object in drawing the bill; getting money is the object in discounting the bill.\" Marx uses this to distinguish bill drawing from bill discounting : \"Drawing bills of exchange is transforming commodities into a form of credit money, just as discounting bills is transforming this credit money into a different money, i.e. banknotes.\" Overstone at least concedes that the purpose of discounting is to receive money, having previously claimed that discounting obtains additional capital. At 3743, asked whether the mercantile community under panic (1825, 1837, 1839) wants capital or the legal tender, Overstone says: \"Their object is to get the command of capital to support their business.\" Marx's correct account: \"Their object is to obtain means of payment for bills on themselves that fall due, on account of the shortage of credit that has set in, and not to have to unload their commodities below their proper price.\" If they have no capital at all, \"of course they obtain capital with these means of payment, since they obtain value without an equivalent\" — a striking early formulation of what would later be called seigniorage or pure credit creation. The general theoretical statement follows: \"The demand for money as such always consists simply in the desire to convert value from the form of commodities or creditor's claims into the form of money. Hence, even aside from crises, the great distinction between borrowing capital and discounting, the latter being simply the transformation of monetary claims from one form into another, or into actual money itself.\" This distinction — borrowing capital vs. discounting — is the conceptual spine of the remainder of the chapter. Engels's Interpolation: The Decisive Distinction Between Advances of Capital and Advances of Money At this point Engels, as editor, interpolates a long clarification (marked off in the text). He begins by noting that for both Norman and Loyd-Overstone \"the banker is always someone who 'advances capital', and his client the person who demands 'capital' from him.\" He lists Overstone's locutions: someone has a bill discounted \"because he wants to obtain capital\" (3729); it is convenient if he \"can get his command over capital at a low rate of interest\" (3730); \"Money is the instrument for obtaining capital\" (3736); in panic the great desire is \"to get the command of capital\" (3742). Engels's diagnosis: \"For all Loyd-Overstone's confusion as to what capital is, it emerges clearly enough that what he describes as capital is what the banker gives his client, a capital that the client did not possess previously and which is advanced to him, being in addition to what the client previously disposed of.\" Engels then offers the psychological explanation of the banker's worldview: \"The banker has grown so accustomed to figuring as distributor of the available social capital in the money form (distributing it in loans) that any function in which he hands out money appears to him as a loan. All money that he pays out appears to him as an advance.\" If the money is given directly as a loan, this is literally correct; if it is used to discount a bill, \"it is in fact an advance for him until the bill falls due.\" The idea is thus reinforced that \"he can make no payments that are not advances.\" Engels carefully distinguishes two senses of \"advance\": the general economic sense in which any investment of money with the object of making interest or profit is an advance the owner makes to himself as entrepreneur; and the specific sense \"that the banker transfers a sum to his client as a loan, which increases by that much the capital at the latter's disposal.\" It is the transference of the banker's specific sense into political economy that \"has led to the confusing controversy as to whether what the banker makes available to his clients in cash is capital or mere money, means of circulation, 'currency'.\" Engels then resolves the controversy by adopting the standpoint of the bank's client — \"what he requires and obtains.\" Three cases are distinguished: Loan on personal credit, without security. \"He receives without condition an advance of a certain value in addition to the capital that he previously applied. He receives it in the money form; not just money, but money capital.\" Advance against securities deposited with the bank. This \"is an advance in the sense that money is paid to him under condition of its repayment, but it is not an advance of capital.\" For the securities also represent capital, \"and moreover a higher amount than the advance.\" The recipient \"receives less capital value than he deposits; and this is in no way an acquisition of extra capital for him. He does not undertake the transaction because he needs capital, but rather because he needs money. Thus there is an advance of money here, but not an advance of capital.\" Advance by discounting bills of exchange. \"The form of an advance also disappears. There is just a simple sale and purchase.\" The bill becomes by endorsement the property of the bank, the money the property of the client. \"There is no question now of repayment.\" Buying cash with a bill \"is no more an advance than if he had bought the cash with some commodity or other, cotton, iron or corn.\" Capital advance requires a one-sided transfer over time; but \"every purchase and sale between one dealer and another is a transfer of capital.\" The only capital advance in discounting would be the discount of an accommodation bill \"not representing any commodities sold, and this no banker will accept if he recognizes it for what it is.\" Conclusion: \"In the regular discount business, therefore, the bank's client does not receive any advance, either in capital or in money, but he receives money for the commodity he has sold.\" Engels draws the practical inference: since Loyd-Overstone \"is accustomed only in the rarest of cases to advance his funds without collateral (he was my firm's banker in Manchester), it is equally clear that his pretty description of the masses of capital that the generous bankers advance to manufacturers in need is sheer invention.\" This is a personal, empirical refutation grounded in Engels's own experience as a Manchester manufacturer. Engels then anchors the distinction in Marx's later text. From Chapter 32: \"The demand for means of payment is simply a demand for convertibility into money, in so far as the merchants and producers are able to offer good security; it is a demand for money capital, in so far as this is not the case, i.e. in so far as an advance of means of payment gives them not only the money form, but also the equivalent that they lack for payments, in whatever form this might be.\" From Chapter 33: \"When the credit system is developed, so that money is concentrated in the hands of the banks, it is they who advance it, at least nominally. This advance is only related to the money in circulation. It is an advance of circulation, not an advance of the capitals it circulates.\" And Mr Chapman's confirmation from the 1857 evidence: \"The banker has the bill, the banker has bought the bill\" (Question 5139). Engels closes: \"We shall come back to this theme again in Chapter 28.\" This interpolation is one of the chapter's most important theoretical contributions, because it disentangles the concept of money capital from the concept of real capital at the point of the credit transaction. It also demonstrates Engels's editorial role in systematising and extending Marx's argument. Overstone's Own Definition of Capital At 3744, asked to describe what he actually means by \"capital,\" Overstone answers: \"Capital consists of various commodities, by means of which trade is carried on; there is fixed capital and there is circulating capital. Your ships, your docks, your wharves... are fixed capital; your provisions, your clothes, etc., are circulating capital.\" This is the classical-physiocratic taxonomy with a vulgar twist; it makes capital a heterogeneous collection of things rather than a social relation or a value that yields surplus value. Marx's earlier point — that commodities express their value as capital only in the profit they yield — is the implicit rebuttal. The Drain of Bullion, \"Loss of Capital,\" and the Two-Stage Difficulty Overstone then recites the Ricardian theory of money (3745): \"In the natural state of things the money of the world is distributed amongst the different countries of the world in certain proportions, those proportions being such that... the intercourse between any one country and all the other countries of the world jointly will be an intercourse of barter; but disturbing circumstances will arise to affect that distribution, and when those arise, a certain portion of the money of any given country passes to other countries.\" This is the automatic distribution-of-specie doctrine in its canonical form. At 3746-47 Overstone equivocates on whether the export of bullion is a \"loss of capital\": \"If you treat bullion as capital, no doubt it is a loss of capital; it is parting with a certain proportion of those precious metals which constitute the money of the world.\" At 3748-49 he confirms that a change in the discount rate is \"a mere sign of an alteration in the value of capital,\" but adds that fluctuations arising from an alteration in \"the quantity of money\" (meaning actual gold) are \"very small.\" At 3750 he says that in a period of high discount there is \"less capital, in one sense of the word. The proportion between capital and the demand for it is altered; it may be by an increased demand, not by a diminution of the quantity of capital.\" Marx's marginal comment notes the inconsistency: \"it was this very capital that was just now money or gold, and a little earlier on the rise in the rate of interest was explained by a high rate of profit that arose from the expansion of the business or capital, not from its contraction.\" At 3751 Overstone defines the capital under discussion tautologically as \"the capital which the country has at its command for conducting its business,\" and when business doubles \"there must be a great increase in the demand for the capital with which it is to be carried on.\" Marx mocks the circularity: \"This artful banker first doubles business activity, and then the demand for capital with which this is to be doubled. All he ever sees is his client, who asks Mr Loyd for a bigger capital to double his business with.\" Overstone then states: \"Capital is like any other commodity, it will vary in its price... according to the supply and demand.\" Marx's gloss: \"according to Mr Loyd capital is nothing but the totality of commodities,\" so \"commodities change their price twice, once as commodities and the other time as capital.\" This is the reductio of the commodity-capital theory: if capital is simply commodities, and the price of capital is the interest rate, then commodities have two prices — a commodity price and an interest price — which is absurd. At 3752 Overstone denies that the rate of discount is connected with the gold in the Bank's coffers; at 3753 he says the Bank of England \"is not a place for the deposit of capital, it is a place for the deposit of money.\" At 3754 he concedes (\"It is very probable\") that accumulation of bullion may coincide with low interest, because a period of diminished demand for capital (money-capital; 1844-5 was a period of prosperity) is a period in which \"the means or instrument through which you command capital may accumulate.\" At 3755 he concedes a possible \"coincidence of time\" but denies a \"connection of principle\" — though Marx notes that his own Bank Act of 1844 \"made it a principle of the Bank of England to regulate the interest rate by the quantity of bullion in its possession.\" At 3758 Overstone collapses money and capital into a single difficulty presented in stages: \"You are putting two things together which I do not join in that form; their difficulty is in getting capital, and their difficulty also is in getting money... The difficulty of getting money and the difficulty of getting capital is the same difficulty taken in two successive stages of its progress.\" Marx pounces: \"Now the fish is caught again. The first difficulty is to discount a bill or obtain an advance by depositing commodities as security. It is the difficulty of transforming capital or a commercial token of value for capital into money.\" This is the difficulty of converting commodities or credits into cash, expressed in a high rate of interest. \"But once the money is received, where then is the second difficulty? If it is only a question of paying, does anyone ever have difficulty in getting rid of his money? And if it is a question of buying, when has anyone ever found difficulty in buying in times of crisis?\" Even granting a rise in the price of corn or cotton, \"the difficulty could still not present itself in the value of the money capital, i.e. the rate of interest, but would rather do so in the price of the commodity; and this difficulty is already solved in that our man now has the money that he needs to buy it with.\" The \"two-stage difficulty\" is thus a verbal illusion. At 3760 Overstone says a higher discount rate is \"an increased difficulty of getting money, but it is not because you want to have the money; it is only the form\" — and Marx adds parenthetically, \"(and this form brings profit into the banker's pocket)\" — \"in which the increased difficulty of getting capital presents itself according to the complicated relations of a civilized state.\" The phrase \"complicated relations of a civilized state\" is Overstone's euphemism for the financial system that extracts tolls on every conversion. The Banker as \"Go-Between\": \"Entrusting\" as Lending at Interest At 3763 Overstone defines the banker as \"the go-between who receives deposits on the one side, and on the other applies those deposits, entrusting them, in the form of capital, to the hands of persons who, etc.\" Marx seizes on the word \"entrusting\": \"Here we finally have what he means by capital. He transforms money into capital by 'entrusting' it, as he puts it rather euphemistically, i.e. lending it out at interest.\" The banker's magic consists in calling the act of lending at interest an act of \"entrusting capital.\" The money is capital only because it is lent at interest; the terminology presupposes what it is meant to prove. The Great Concession and the \"High Profit Rate\" Smoke Screen Overstone repeats at 3805: \"When the money in the country is diminished by a drain, its value increases, and the Bank of England must conform to that alteration in the value of money\" — Marx glosses: \"i.e. in the value of money as capital, or in other words, in the rate of interest, for the value of money as money, compared with commodities, remains the same\" — \"which is meant by the technical term of raising the rate of interest.\" The distinction Marx enforces here is important: the value of money as money (its purchasing power over commodities) is invariant; what changes is the value of money as capital, i.e. the rate of interest. So Overstone, having denied any essential connection between the quantity of money and the rate of interest (3755), now explains the rate of interest by the quantity of money. At 3819 Overstone says \"I never confound those two\" — meaning money and capital; Marx's wry gloss: \"for the simple reason that he never distinguished between them.\" At 3834 he describes the large sum paid for corn in 1847 as \"in point of fact capital, for the supply of the necessary provisions of the country.\" At 3841 he asserts \"an intimate connection\" between the rate of discount and the state of the reserve, because \"the state of the reserve is the indicator of the increase or the decrease of the quantity of money in the country; and in proportion as the money in the country increases or decreases, the value of that money will increase or decrease, and the bank-rate of discount will conform to that change.\" Marx: \"Here, therefore, he concedes what he denied categorically in no. 3755.\" At 3842 Overstone asserts an \"intimate connection\" between the amount of gold in the Issue Department and the reserve of notes in the Banking Department. Marx's technical correction: \"Here he explains the changes in the rate of interest in terms of the changes in the quantity of money. What he says about this is false. The reserve can decline because the money in circulation in the country increases. This is the case if the public accept more notes and there is no decline in the metal reserve. But then the interest rate rises, because the banking capital of the Bank of England is limited by the Act of 1844. Overstone, however, cannot speak of this, since according to this Act the two departments of the Bank have nothing to do with one another.\" This is a precise legal-institutional point: the 1844 Act split the Bank into an Issue Department and a Banking Department, ensuring that the Banking Department's lending capacity is limited by the reserve, so that a rise in the public's demand for notes (not a fall in gold) can raise interest — a cause that Overstone's own theory forbids him to acknowledge. At 3859 Overstone formulates the general proposition: \"A high rate of profit will always create a great demand for capital; a great demand for capital will raise the value of it.\" Marx: \"Here at last we have the connection between the high profit rate and the demand for capital, as Overstone conceives it. Now in 1844-5, for example, there was a high rate of profit in the cotton industry, since raw cotton was cheap and remained so despite a strong demand for cotton goods. The value of capital (and from an earlier passage, Overstone means by capital that which every person needs in his business), i.e. in this case the value of the raw cotton, was not increased for the manufacturers. Now the high rate of profit may have caused many cotton manufacturers to expand their businesses. In this way their demand for money capital would rise, but not for anything else.\" In other words, high profit generates demand for money capital, not demand for \"capital\" in the sense of physical commodities; the cotton was cheap, and the physical input was not scarce — only the loanable funds were. This is the empirical illustration that dissects Overstone's identification of capital with commodities. The Reserve, the Two Departments, and the Abandonment of the 1840 Position At 3889 Overstone says: \"Bullion may or may not be money, just as paper may or may not be a bank-note.\" At 3896 he abandons his 1840 argument that the fluctuations in notes out of the Bank ought to conform to fluctuations in bullion, conceding only that \"now with the means of information which we possess, the notes out of the Bank of England must have added to them the notes which are in the banking reserve of the Bank of England.\" Marx's commentary: \"This is superlative.\" The 1844 Act stipulates that the Bank prints as many paper notes as it has gold in its reserve, plus £14 million more, so its note issue naturally fluctuates with the gold reserve. But since the present means of information show that the mass of notes the Bank may manufacture (and the Issue Department transfers to the Banking Department) — this internal circulation, fluctuating with the money reserve — \"does not determine the fluctuations in the circulation of banknotes outside the walls of the Bank of England,\" it follows that \"the latter, the real circulation, now becomes completely immaterial for the Bank's management, and the circulation between the two departments of the Bank, whose difference from the real circulation is shown by the reserve, becomes the sole decisive factor.\" For the outside world, this internal reserve is important only \"in so far as the reserve indicates how close the Bank is to the legal maximum of its note issue, and how much its clients can still obtain from the Banking Department.\" This is a devastating internal critique of the operation of the 1844 Act: the Act produces a rigid internal accounting relation between the two departments that has no necessary relation to the real circulation of notes in the economy, yet that internal relation becomes the controlling variable for credit conditions. The Closing Malafides: The French Loan and the Retreat to the \"Student's Chamber\" The chapter's final exhibit is a \"brilliant example of Overstone's malafides.\" At 4243, asked whether the quantity of capital oscillates monthly so as to alter its value in the way shown by recent discount-rate oscillations, Overstone concedes that \"the relation between the demand and the supply of capital may undoubtedly fluctuate, even within short periods,\" and illustrates: \"If France tomorrow put out a notice that she wishes to borrow a very large loan, there is no doubt that it would immediately cause a great alteration in the value of money, that is to say, in the value of capital, in this country.\" At 4245 he pushes the example further: if France wants suddenly thirty millions' worth of commodities, \"there will be a great demand for capital, to use the more scientific and the simpler term.\" At 4246 the questioner asks whether the capital France wishes to buy with her loan is one thing and the money with which she buys it another, and whether it is the money that alters in value. Overstone retreats: \"We seem to be reviving the old question, which I think is more fit for the chamber of a student than for this committee room.\" Marx's closing line: \"And with this he retires, though not into the chamber of a student.\" Footnote 84 directs the reader to the end of Chapter 32 for more on Overstone's confusion of concepts where capital is concerned. The French-loan example is instructive in itself: a sudden foreign loan converts money capital into a commodity demand, altering \"the value of money, that is to say, the value of capital\" in England — i.e., raising the interest rate. This is a real money-market phenomenon, but Overstone cannot distinguish the money market from the commodity market, and so he cannot answer the questioner's simple distinction between the capital France wants to buy and the money she uses to buy it. The retreat to \"the chamber of a student\" is, therefore, an admission that the theoretical question cannot be answered on Overstone's own terms. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Chapter 26 yields several fundamental theoretical principles. First, and most centrally, the rate of interest is determined by the demand and supply of loanable money capital , not by the supply and demand of commodities and not by the quantity of the circulating medium. The rate of profit, while it forms a general upper limit to the rate of interest, no more explains the actual level of interest than the level of the sea explains the height of each wave. Marx's own positive account locates the ultimate causes of movements in money capital in the real reproduction process: overproduction in industry, underproduction in agriculture, speculative misallocation of floating capital, and the disturbances these generate in the demand for credit. The money market is thus not autochthonous; it is a particular refraction of the process of capital accumulation. Second, the chapter establishes a sharp conceptual distinction between money capital and real capital . These are two forms of the same value, and their values can move in opposite directions: in a crisis, \"the value of capital in the one form rose, because the value of capital in the other form fell.\" Commodity capital depreciates precisely as loanable money capital appreciates (its rate of interest rises). Overstone's intellectual sin is to fuse these two distinct values into a single, equivocal \"value of capital,\" which allows him to slide between incompatible explanations — now it means the rate of profit, now it means the rate of interest, now it means the money held in bank coffers. Third, the chapter dismantles the banker's self-serving concept of \"advancing capital.\" Engels's interpolation demonstrates that in the ordinary discount business there is no advance at all: there is a sale and purchase, the conversion of a bill into money. An advance implies a one-sided transfer of value over time; the discount of a genuine commercial bill is reciprocal and instantaneous. The banker's professional habit of seeing every payment as an advance is a false universalisation of his own role. This dissolution of the banker's claim to be the benefactor who \"advances capital\" to industry is one of the chapter's most practically consequential moves: it denies the financial sector the ideological alibi of productive contribution. Fourth, the chapter exposes the class content of monetary policy. The Bank Act of 1844 raised the proprietors' dividend from seven to nine per cent and shifted the income tax burden from proprietors to the Bank; the crisis of 1847 transferred wealth from the productive classes to \"the Jew and money-dealer\"; the manufacturers of the hardware districts declined orders because the discount rate swallowed their profit of enterprise; the Bank hoarded notes while commerce starved; the Great Concession of the Act's suspension was extracted only under imminent threat of universal bankruptcy. The theoretical confusion of the Currency School is thus not innocent: it provides the intellectual cover for a legislative framework that enriches the financial sector at the expense of production. Fifth, the chapter offers a model of immanent critique. Marx does not refute Overstone primarily by appeals to external evidence; he refutes him by internal inconsistency — by catching him conceding at 3841 what he denied at 3755, by showing that his own 1847 data refute his theory, by demonstrating that his own explanations oscillate between tautology and contradiction. This method embodies a broader epistemological lesson: the ideology of the dominant class, when pressed, yields up its own incoherence. The broader implications are considerable. For a theory of crises, the chapter shows that financial crisis is not, in the first instance, a purely monetary phenomenon — a shortage of banknotes — but a disturbance in the reproduction process expressed through the money market. The remedy for crisis cannot therefore be the mechanical adjustment of the note issue to the gold reserve; indeed, the 1844 Act made matters worse by adding monetary panic to a crisis of production. For a theory of the state, the chapter demonstrates that ostensibly technical monetary legislation is a class instrument, and that the parliamentary interview is a genre in which the trained evasion of a banker can be read against the grain to reveal the reality being concealed. For a theory of knowledge, the chapter is a masterclass in exposing how the vocabulary of a profession — the banker's \"capital,\" \"advance,\" \"entrusting\" — constructs a self-flattering picture of the world that collapses under interrogation. The chapter's closing gesture — Overstone retiring \"though not into the chamber of a student\" — is more than a witticism. It names the central fault line of the chapter: the banker treats the technical question of the rate of interest as too abstract, too \"fit for the chamber of a student,\" to be answered in a committee room, precisely because he cannot answer it without exposing the emptiness of his own claims. Marx's chapter answers it instead, and in doing so, converts the chamber of the student into the courtroom of the analyst. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 25 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 27"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 27", "title": "Chapter 27", "subtitle": "The Role of Credit in Capitalist Production", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 27/Chapter 27 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 4903, "read_time": "25 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital", "Capital Accumulation & Crises"], "keywords": ["Role of Credit in Capitalist Production", "Equalization of Profit Rate Facilitated by Credit", "Formation of Joint-Stock Companies (Social Capital)", "Credit as Lever of Overproduction and Speculation", "Transition Point toward Associated Mode of Production", "Monopoly Concentration and Financial Oligarchy"], "text": "Chapter 27 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 4,862 words ⏱️ ~24 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 27 — The Role of Credit in Capitalist Production: An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS This chapter sits at a decisive hinge within Volume III of Capital — specifically within the section titled \"The Division of Profit into Interest and Profit of Enterprise.\" Its structural position matters enormously. Having earlier dissected how the total social surplus-value is split into rent, interest, and profit of enterprise, Marx now turns to the institutional machine that administers that split in its most developed form: the credit system. The chapter is therefore not a self-contained essay but a sort of schematic manifesto — a compressed, bulleted inventory of what credit does within the capitalist mode of production, treated as a set of functions to be enumerated before the analysis sharpens into the technical machinery of interest-bearing capital in the following chapters. The chapter's core thesis is that credit is a profoundly dual, self-contradictory force . On one side, it is indispensable — nothing less than \"the necessary formation to bring about the equalization of the profit rate… on which the whole of capitalist production depends.\" It slashes circulation costs, accelerates the metamorphosis of capital, and facilitates the giant productive scale of the joint-stock company. On the other side, it dissolves capitalist private property from within, concentrates enormous social wealth in the hands of a few adventurers, converts ownership into tradable paper, and transforms the entire reproduction process into a gambling house. Credit is thus simultaneously the engine of capitalist expansion and the midwife of its supersession — a \"self-abolishing contradiction\" immanent to capital itself. The central intellectual objective is to demonstrate that the highest development of capitalist credit — the joint-stock company, the trust, the cooperative factory — produces objective transition forms pointing beyond capitalism. Credit does not merely lubricate the existing system; it socializes production (large-scale, associated, organized) while simultaneously privatizing ownership (shares, dividends, speculators). This contradiction is, for Marx, the very dialectical signature of capital approaching its historical limit. The chapter therefore belongs to the theoretical framework of the historical tendency of capitalist accumulation, connecting the analysis of profit distribution to the eventual socialization of the means of production. A second layer of importance comes from Friedrich Engels' editorial hand. Writing decades after Marx's death, Engels inserts substantive additions — most prominently the analysis of cartels, trusts, and the United Alkali Trust — to update Marx's sketch with the \"second and third degree of joint-stock company\" that had emerged in the interim. He also annotates the text with the bitter hindsight of the Panama scandal, noting that Marx had \"exactly described\" the swindle \"a full twenty years before it took place.\" The chapter is therefore a palimpsest: Marx's 1860s conceptual architecture overlaid with Engels' 1890s empirical verification. Its unifying claim is that credit is the form in which capitalist private property, at its most developed, prepares its own negation — in one form negatively (the joint-stock company), in another positively (the cooperative factory). 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Fourfold Architecture: How the Chapter Is Built Marx opens by announcing a recapitulation: \"The general observations we have so far made on the credit system are as follows.\" What follows is a numbered inventory of four major functions, each subdivided into progressively finer mechanical detail. This is deliberately ragged, notebook-style prose — the raw conceptual skeleton of a theory under construction rather than a finished essay. The four headings are: I. credit as the necessary instrument for equalizing the profit rate; II. credit as a cost-reducer of circulation; III. credit as the generator of joint-stock companies; IV. credit as a mechanism granting command over social , rather than merely private, capital. This architecture is important: it moves from the macro-economic (systemic profit equalization), through the technical (circulation costs), to the institutional (the corporation), and finally to the political-economic (control over social property). Each beat escalates the stakes, culminating in the claim that credit is the vehicle of expropriation extended to the capitalist class itself. Beat I: Why Credit Is a Condition of Existence for Capitalist Production The first function is stated with extreme compression but enormous weight: credit is \"necessary\" for \"the equalization of the profit rate or the movement of this equalization, on which the whole of capitalist production depends.\" Here we must unpack a foundational Marxist concept. In a competitive capitalist economy, capitals invested in different branches of production face different organic compositions and different rates of surplus-value extraction. If each capital simply kept its own surplus-value, the profit rate (surplus-value divided by total capital advanced) would vary wildly between branches — high in labor-intensive sectors, low in capital-intensive ones. Capital, however, is mobile: it migrates toward the highest rate, driving prices of production to a level at which profit rates tend to equalize across branches. This equalization is the mechanism that sustains the fiction of a \"general rate of profit.\" Marx's claim here — stated almost in passing but essential — is that this massive, economy-wide reallocation of capital presupposes credit . Without credit, capital could not flow fast enough, in sufficient volume, or across enough distance to equalize profit rates. Credit is therefore not an embellishment on capitalism; it is a structural precondition . The whole edifice — prices of production, the general rate of profit, the calculus of capitalist investment — rests on it. Beat II.1: The Three Ways Credit Economizes on Money The second function concerns the reduction of circulation costs — the overhead of keeping the economic machine running. Marx identifies money itself as \"a major cost of circulation, in so far as it is itself value.\" This is a subtle point rooted in the labor theory of value: gold and silver money are commodities embodying socially necessary labor-time. Storing, mining, coining, and moving them consumes real resources that produce nothing. Credit economizes on this dead weight in three ways : (A) Complete dispensation with money. In a large portion of transactions, credit means money never physically changes hands; book-entry settlement, clearing, and mutual cancellation of debts replace it entirely. (B) Acceleration of the circulating medium. The velocity of money is here distinguished into two components. The technical component: given the same volume and number of real turnovers of commodities destined for consumption, a smaller quantity of money or money tokens performs the same service — this is a matter of banking technique. Engels' footnote 85 supplies striking empirical evidence from The Currency Theory Reviewed : in France, the average notes in circulation fell from 106,538,000 francs (1812) to 101,205,000 francs (1818), while the annual aggregate of disbursements and receipts exploded from 2,837,712,000 francs to 9,665,030,000 francs. The \"activity of the currency\" therefore tripled in six years. The footnote's key claim: \"The great regulator of the velocity of circulation is credit.\" It also provides a vivid illustration — between September 1833 and September 1843, nearly 300 new note-issuing banks appeared in the United Kingdom, yet circulation fell by £2.5 million (from £36,035,244 to £33,518,554). More banks, less money. And Scotland's \"prodigious activity\" allowed £100 to accomplish what required £420 in England. These figures demonstrate an essential principle: credit substitutes velocity and organization for quantity of metal . (C) Replacement of gold money by paper. Credit allows value to be represented in cheap paper rather than costly gold, directly reducing the real resource cost of circulation. Beat II.2: Acceleration, Speculation, and the Reserve Fund The second sub-heading is dense with causal chains. Credit accelerates \"the individual phases of circulation or commodity metamorphosis,\" which accelerates \"the metamorphosis of capital,\" which accelerates \"the reproduction process in general.\" This is a chain of compression of time . In Marx's framework, the circuit of capital (M–C…P…C′–M′) is punctuated by phases of buying, producing, and selling. The faster each phase, the faster the whole turnover, and thus the higher the annual rate of surplus-value realizable on a given capital. Credit shortens the waiting periods between them. But Marx immediately introduces a counter-dialectic : \"on the other hand, credit also enables the acts of buying and selling to take a longer time, and hence serves as a basis for speculation.\" The very elasticity that speeds up normal circulation also permits protracted positions — waiting for prices to rise, holding inventories on borrowed funds. Speculation is thus not an aberration but a structural possibility opened by credit itself . This is a recurring motif: every accelerator of the system is also an accelerator of its pathological forms. The beat concludes with the contraction of the reserve fund , viewed two ways: (1) as a reduction in the circulating medium — less idle money held in reserve because credit guarantees solvency; and (2) as a restriction of \"the part of capital that must always be in existence in the money form.\" The reserve fund is the precautionary hoard every capitalist must hold against illiquidity. Credit socializes this precaution: since the whole system stands ready to lend, each individual needs to hold less. Footnote 86 reinforces this with a quote from The Economist (1845): before banks, \"the amount of capital withdrawn for the purposes of currency was greater, at all times, than the actual circulation of commodities required.\" Banking thus frees a vast reservoir of idle capital for productive use — a real efficiency gain, but also a fragility : a system running on minimal reserves is a system vulnerable to panic. Beat III: The Joint-Stock Company and Its Threefold Socialization We now reach the weightiest beat, the \"formation of joint-stock companies,\" subdivided into three movements. Movement 1 — Scale. Joint-stock organization permits \"tremendous expansion in the scale of production, and enterprises which would be impossible for individual capitals.\" Beyond that, enterprises previously run by the state become \"social\" — the footnote clarifies that gesellschaftlich carries the double sense of \"social\" (as opposed to individual) and \"joint-stock\" (as opposed to government). This linguistic doubling is deliberate: the German term captures the semantic identity between society and company , hinting that the corporation is a form of socialized production wearing a private legal skin. Movement 2 — Social Capital. Capital, \"inherently based on a social mode of production,\" now appears as \"social capital (capital of directly associated individuals) in contrast to private capital.\" Crucially, Marx calls this \"the abolition of capital as private property within the confines of the capitalist mode of production itself.\" Note the precision: it is an abolition within capitalism, not of capitalism. This is the dialectical heart of the chapter. Movement 3 — The Functioning Capitalist Becomes a Manager. This is a decisive transformation in the personnel of capital. The actual functioning capitalist — who once owned and ran the business — becomes \"a mere manager, in charge of other people's capital.\" The capital owner becomes \"a mere owner, a mere money capitalist.\" Marx here makes a crucial analytical clarification: even if the dividends drawn include both interest and profit of enterprise (i.e., the total profit), the manager's salary is or should be treated as simply \"the wage for a certain kind of skilled labour,\" its price regulated in the labour market like any other. So the total profit is ultimately drawn only in the form of interest — a pure reward for ownership, now as completely separated from the actual production function as the manager is from ownership. The Theoretic Payoff. Profit \"thus appears… as simply the appropriation of other people's surplus labour, arising from the transformation of means of production into capital; i.e., from their estrangement vis-à-vis the actual producer; from their opposition, as the property of another, vis-à-vis all individuals really active in production from the manager down to the lowest day-labourer.\" The joint-stock form thus lays bare what capital always was: not a reward for function , but a claim on surplus labour grounded in estrangement . In individual enterprise, this was obscured by the owner's apparent labor; in the corporation, the mask falls. Consequently, Marx argues, the joint-stock company is \"a necessary point of transition towards the transformation of capital back into the property of the producers\" — not as private property of individuals, but as the property of \"associated producers, as directly social property.\" It also marks the transformation of all functions formerly bound up with capital ownership \"into simple functions of the associated producers, into social functions.\" The Critical Economic Caveat. Before moving on, Marx flags an \"economically important fact\": because profit here takes the pure form of interest , \"enterprises that merely yield an interest are possible.\" This is one of the reasons holding up the fall in the general rate of profit. If you recall, Marx's law of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall is driven by the rising organic composition of capital (constant relative to variable). In a corporation, huge fixed-capital \"pure-interest\" income streams become acceptable, and such enterprises — because constant capital stands in \"tremendous ratio to variable\" — \"do not necessarily go into the equalization of the general rate of profit.\" Here is a subtle structural point: the joint-stock form insulates certain capitals from the profit-equalizing tendency, thereby susceptible of arresting the very equalization that Beat I said credit was necessary to produce. Credit and the corporation thus disturb their own macro-equilibrium function — a self-undermining dynamic at the heart of the analysis. Engels' Interpolation: From Cartel to Trust Engels inserts a substantial amendment at this point, marking the historical distance between Marx's 1860s manuscript and the 1890s \"second and third degree of joint-stock company.\" His argument proceeds in a tight sequence, offering one of the earliest systematic descriptions of what later theorists would call monopoly capitalism : Production overshoots the market. Since Marx wrote, the speed of increasing production in large-scale industry has risen \"greater every day,\" while market expansion slows. \"What production produces in months the market can absorb only in years.\" This is a realization crisis dynamic — supply outruns demand. Protectionism amplifies oversupply. Each industrial country erects tariffs against the others, \"particularly against England,\" artificially boosting domestic capacity. The international dimension thus entrenches the disproportionality. The result is chronic overproduction: \"depressed prices, falling profits, even their complete cessation.\" And crucially, \"the ancient and celebrated freedom of competition is at the end of its road and must itself confess its evident and scandalous bankruptcy.\" Competition is failing on its own terms, economically. Cartels as the first response. Big industrialists of a branch come together to regulate production; a committee fixes each establishment's output and has \"the last word in dividing up the incoming orders.\" Some temporary international cartels formed — for example between English and German iron producers. But this proved insufficient: \"The conflict of interests between the individual businesses broke through too often and restored competition.\" The trust as the next stage. Where scale permitted, the entire production of a branch is concentrated \"into one big joint-stock company with a unified management.\" America led; Europe's biggest example was the United Alkali Trust , which brought all British alkali production into a single firm. Engels details the mechanics: over thirty former plant owners received the assessed value of their establishments in shares , totaling some £5 million of fixed capital. Technical management remained in the same hands; financial control concentrated in general management. The floating capital — about £1 million — was offered to public subscription, giving a total capital of £6 million . Engels' conclusion is unambiguously Marx-compatible: in this branch, \"competition has been replaced in England by monopoly, thus preparing in the most pleasing fashion its future expropriation by society as a whole, by the nation.\" Note the phrase \"in the most pleasing fashion\" — thick with ironic Schadenfreude. The capitalists' own drive to eliminate competition generates the very concentration that makes national confiscation administratively possible. The Self-Abolishing Contradiction and the New Financial Aristocracy Marx now names the structural logic directly: the joint-stock corporation is \"the abolition of the capitalist mode of production within the capitalist mode of production itself, and hence a self-abolishing contradiction .\" It \"presents itself prima facie as a mere point of transition to a new form of production.\" Yet it also, and in the same breath, \"gives rise to monopoly in certain spheres and hence provokes state intervention.\" And it \"reproduces a new financial aristocracy, a new kind of parasite in the guise of company promoters, speculators and merely nominal directors; an entire system of swindling and cheating with respect to the promotion of companies, issue of shares and share dealings.\" The chapter's summary formula is devastating in its compactness: \"It is private production unchecked by private ownership.\" This phrase deserves unpacking. In classical capitalism, the owner personally bears the loss and thus checks his own risk-taking. Under the corporation, social production (large-scale, cooperative, organized) proceeds while ownership is dispersed and non-accountable. The discipline of private property evaporates precisely where the scale of production becomes social. This is the structural root of \"unchecked\" behavior — the legal architecture that allows enormous risk to be borne by others. Beat IV: Credit as Command Over Social Capital Beyond the corporate form, credit more generally \"offers the individual capitalist, or the person who can pass as a capitalist, an absolute command over the capital and property of others, within certain limits, and, through this, command over other people's labour.\" Footnote 87 supplies the empirical backbone, drawn from Tooke's Inquiry into the Currency Principle : \"the power of purchase by persons having capital and credit is much beyond anything that those who are unacquainted practically with speculative markets have any idea of\"; a man with a mere reputation for capital, who takes a sanguine view of a rising price and is favored by circumstances, \"may effect purchases to an extent perfectly enormous compared with his capital.\" The Economist (1847) supplies the formal version: capital \"is rather the foundation upon which a good credit is built than the limit of the transactions of any commercial establishment.\" This is a pivotal conceptual move: credit decouples economic power from ownership of wealth. It is not \"his\" capital but \"disposal over social capital\" that gives the speculator command over social labour. The actual owned capital becomes \"simply the basis for a superstructure of credit.\" This is especially true in wholesale trade, through which \"the greater part of the social product passes.\" The Panama Anticipation and the Refutation of the Abstention Theory From this decoupling, two ideological pillars of classical capitalist apologetics collapse: (1) The \"saving\" theory of capital. If the \"origin of capital is saving,\" it is absurd to say so of a speculator \"since what this speculator demands is precisely that others should save for him.\" Engels' annotation gives body to the abstraction: \"the whole of France saved up one and a half thousand million francs for the Panama swindlers. The whole Panama swindle is exactly described here, a full twenty years before it took place.\" The most sweeping collective thrift in French history was transferred, in the end, to a handful of promoters — a literal enactment of Marx's thesis that credit place social savings under private command. (2) The theory of \"abstention.\" The ascetic-capitalist apologetic — that the capitalist deserves reward because he abstains from consumption — is \"diametrically refuted by his luxury, which also becomes a means of credit.\" Luxury is not merely a personal indulgence; it advertises solvency and thereby expands borrowing capacity. Extravagance becomes economically functional. Conceptions that once had \"a certain meaning at a less developed state of capitalist production now become completely meaningless.\" Expropriation Generalized — From Producer to Small Capitalist The dialectical climax of the chapter concerns expropriation. \"Success and failure lead in both cases to the centralization of capitals and hence to expropriation on the most enormous scale.\" Note the disjunction: it does not matter whether you win or lose; either path centralizes. The winning speculator absorbs rivals; the losing speculator's assets pass to creditors. \"Expropriation now extends from the immediate producers to the small and medium capitalists themselves.\" This is critical. Marx's earlier work ( Capital I) focused on expropriation of the direct producers. Here, the credit mechanism generalizes the process to the capitalist class internally — smaller capitalists are pushed off their property by larger ones, mediated by the credit superstructure. Marx then states what he calls the goal of capitalist expropriation: \"to expropriate all individuals from the means of production — which, with the development of social production, cease to be means and products of private production, and can only remain means of production in the hands of the associated producers, as their social property, just as they are their social product.\" This is the historical teleology. But within capitalism , \"this expropriation takes the antithetical form of the appropriation of social property by a few; and credit gives these few ever more the character of simple adventurers.\" Ownership in the form of shares means movement and transfer become \"simply the result of stock-exchange dealings, where little fishes are gobbled up by the sharks, and sheep by the stock-exchange wolves.\" The metaphor is legal as well as moral: the share certificate is paper; ownership is now not a relation to production but a position in a market . Critically, Marx refuses to overstate his case: \"the transformation into the form of shares still remains trapped within the capitalist barriers; instead of overcoming the opposition between the character of wealth as something social, and private wealth, this transformation only develops this opposition in a new form.\" This is a nuance that must not be lost: the corporation is not socialism. It is a contradiction intensified , not a contradiction resolved . Cooperative Factories: The Positive Abolition Into this pessimistic dialectic steps a moment of genuine hope: \"The cooperative factories run by workers themselves are, within the old form, the first examples of the emergence of a new form.\" Marx immediately adds the necessary realism: they \"naturally reproduce in all cases, in their present organization, all the defects of the existing system, and must reproduce them.\" Nonetheless, \"the opposition between capital and labour is abolished here,\" even if \"at first only in the form that the workers in association become their own capitalist, i.e., they use the means of production to valorize their own labour.\" This phrase — \"become their own capitalist\" — is paradoxical and precise. The workers do not escape the valorization relation; they occupy both poles of it. Yet this is already the emergence of the associated mode. Marx draws the historical-materialist conclusion: at a certain stage of development of the material forces and social forms of production, \"a new mode of production develops and is formed naturally out of the old.\" And crucially, cooperation depends on the very system it seeks to supersede: \"Without the factory system that arises from the capitalist mode of production, cooperative factories could not develop. Nor could they do so without the credit system.\" Credit again plays a double role — as the basis for transforming private enterprises into joint-stock companies, and as the \"means for the gradual extension of cooperative enterprises on a more or less national scale.\" The beautiful closing line on this theme: \"Capitalist joint-stock companies as much as cooperative factories should be viewed as transition forms from the capitalist mode of production to the associated one, simply that in the one case the opposition is abolished in a negative way, and in the other in a positive way.\" Here we have the chapter's core dialectical formula: the same historical function can be performed negatively (capitalist trust) or positively (worker cooperative) . Both socialize production. Only one socializes ownership in a manner consistent with the producers themselves. The Transition to Interest-Bearing Capital, and the Speculation-Crisis Thesis Marx closes by announcing scope: up till now he has examined credit \"principally in relation to industrial capital.\" The following chapters will address credit in relation to interest-bearing capital as such. Before that, \"a few more specifically economic points remain.\" First , credit as the \"principal lever of overproduction and excessive speculation.\" This is a general overproduction thesis. \"If the credit system appears as the principal lever of overproduction and excessive speculation in commerce, this is simply because the reproduction process, which is elastic by nature, is now forced to its most extreme limit.\" The elasticity of the system is stretched by credit; because \"a great part of the social capital is applied by those who are not its owners, and who therefore proceed quite unlike owners who… anxiously weigh the limits of their private capital.\" This is a precise theory of moral hazard avant la lettre. Owners weigh limits; non-owners do not. Credit channels other people's money through people with no personal exposure to limits. Second , valorization \"founded on the antithetical character of capitalist production permits actual free development only up to a certain point, which is constantly broken through by the credit system.\" Credit accelerates both the productive forces and the contradictions. \"At the same time, credit accelerates the violent outbreaks of this contradiction, crises, and with these the elements of dissolution of the old mode of production.\" The credit system is thus simultaneously the engine of material progress and the accelerant of breakdown. Third , the closing dual character thesis: credit \"develops the motive of capitalist production, enrichment by the exploitation of others' labour, into the purest and most colossal system of gambling and swindling, and restricts ever more the already small number of the exploiters of social wealth; on the other hand however it constitutes the form of transition towards a new mode of production.\" The two poles are held in a single institutional form. And it is this very duality, Marx wryly notes, which gives the \"principal spokesmen for credit, from Law through to Isaac Pereire, their nicely mixed character of swindler and prophet.\" The footnote identifies John Law (1671–1729), the Scottish financial projector whose Mississippi scheme was an early credit bubble, and Isaac Pereire (1806–80), Bonapartist deputy and co-founder of the Crédit Mobilier. Both were men who genuinely advanced financial technique and defrauded untold numbers — the incarnation of credit's dual character. They are swindlers and prophets because credit is both. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Four fundamental principles emerge from this chapter. First, credit is structurally constitutive, not incidental, to capitalist production. Marx's Beat I establishes that the equalization of the profit rate — the price system's core regulatory mechanism — depends on credit. Credit is thus not an \"add-on\" to capitalism but one of its conditions of possibility . This has profound implications for economic theory: it means you cannot model a competitive equilibrium economy without modeling credit. It also means any attempt to \"reform\" capitalism by suppressing finance is either incoherent or revolutionary — finance is not parasitic on the \"real\" economy but woven into its very fabric. Second, credit is a machine for socializing production while privatizing ownership . The joint-stock company, the trust, the cooperative: all represent socialized, large-scale, cooperative production. Yet in the corporation, ownership is dispersed into tradable shares and control into a small cadre of managers and promoters. This is the contradiction Marx names as \"private production unchecked by private ownership.\" The corporation is therefore not a solution to capitalism's contradictions but their institutional form at the highest level. It is both \"abolition\" and \"reproduction\" of capitalist relations. Third, credit is dialectically both accelerator of progress and accelerator of crisis. It accelerates the material development of the productive forces, the creation of the world market, and the accumulation of capital. But \"at the same time\" it accelerates \"the violent outbreaks of this contradiction, crises, and with these the elements of dissolution of the old mode of production.\" Every crisis is simultaneously a breakdown and an advance of the underlying tendency — the forcible reconstitution of the conditions for renewed accumulation. This is the deepest methodological insight: capitalism's most catastrophic moments are its most revealing ones. Fourth, the theory of expropriation generalizes beyond the proletariat. Marx's earlier analysis focused on the expropriation of direct producers; here, credit expropriates small and medium capitalists themselves . \"Success and failure lead in both cases to the centralization of capitals.\" This is a crucial refinement: it means the centralization of capital does not only produce a larger proletariat but also a shrinking capitalist class — a structural tendency toward monopoly with a diminishing circle of owners. It explains why Engels can append his trust analysis as a natural extension, not a new departure. Broader implications. Practically, Marx's analysis anticipates twentieth-century debates over the \"managerial revolution,\" the separation of ownership from control, the rise of financialization, and the moral-hazard dynamics of leveraged speculation — from the South Sea Bubble to 2008. Theoretically, the chapter provides the linchpin connecting Volume I's theory of exploitation to Volume III's analysis of distributional forms: credit is the institutional machine that transfers surplus-value, equalizes profit rates, and eventually concentrates ownership — all while carrying within itself the \"transition forms\" (cooperative, joint-stock, trust) toward an associated mode of production. Philosophically, the chapter is a masterclass in dialectical method: nothing here is simply \"good\" or \"bad.\" Credit is swindler and prophet, destroyer and midwife, capitalism's most developed form and its most developed self-negation — an institution whose ultimate historical significance, Marx insists, is not what it is , but what it prepares . ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 26 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 28"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 28", "title": "Chapter 28", "subtitle": "Means of Circulation and Credit", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 28/Chapter 28 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 13708, "read_time": "69 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital", "Money, Currency & Circulation"], "keywords": ["Means of Circulation and Credit", "Tooke and Fullarton Currency Controversy", "Critique of Currency School Quantity Theory", "Gold Inflows and Domestic Circulation Realities", "Difference between Media of Circulation and Capital Loans"], "text": "Chapter 28 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 13,718 words ⏱️ ~69 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 28: Means of Circulation and Capital — The Views of Tooke and Fullarton 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem This chapter is a sustained theoretical demolition. Marx sets out to demonstrate that the hallowed distinction drawn by the British banking school between \"currency\" and \"capital\" — a distinction that the entire practical and polemical apparatus of nineteenth-century English monetary debate turned upon — is conceptually empty. The chapter does not introduce a new theory of money; it applies, with surgical precision, a theory of money Marx had already developed (in Volume 1, Chapter 3, and in A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy ) in order to dissolve a confusion that, on Marx's account, had muddled every important question of banking policy, monetary crisis theory, and the practical conduct of the Bank of England. The immediate intellectual targets are Thomas Tooke (author of the Inquiry into the Currency Principle and the History of Prices ), James Wilson (founder and first editor of The Economist , later Financial Secretary to the Treasury), and John Fullarton (author of On the Regulation of Currencies ). These were the leading theorists of what we now call the Banking School, as against the Currency School (the \"Currency Principle gentlemen\" — the intellectual lineage of the 1844 Bank Act). Both schools were obsessed with the relationship between the note issue, the gold reserve, prices, and the balance of payments. Both treated the distinction between money functioning as circulating medium and money functioning as loanable capital as fundamental. Marx's argument is that this entire conceptual apparatus rests on a category error. The Thesis Stated Marx's thesis is compact and devastating: There is no distinction between \"currency\" and \"capital\" to be found in the functions of money at all. Money always functions as means of purchase or means of payment — as means of circulation in the broad sense — whether it serves to realize revenue or to transfer capital. What the Banking School theorists are actually observing, without understanding it, is the difference between two functions of money (means of purchase vs. means of payment), and between two spheres of the reproduction process in which money acts (revenue expenditure vs. capital transfer). Neither of these distinctions corresponds to a distinction between currency and capital. The confusion arises from three sources: (1) the conflation of functional characteristics, (2) the intrusion of questions about the total quantity of circulating money, and (3) the intrusion of questions about the relative proportions of circulating medium across the two spheres. Beyond this, the confusion is rooted socio-psychologically in the standpoint of the issuing banker , whose own banknotes cost him nothing to produce and whose \"currency\" therefore appears to him as something other than his \"capital.\" The chapter consequently performs three tasks simultaneously: it refutes Tooke's and Fullarton's specific doctrines; it demonstrates that the more sophisticated arguments these theorists make (particularly about the drain of gold and the independence of the demand for loans from the demand for circulation) rest on the same foundational error; and it clarifies — as a positive contribution — what actually happens to the circulation of money in prosperity and in crisis. Intellectual Objective and Placement The chapter sits within Volume 3, Part Five, \"The Division of Profit into Interest and Profit of Enterprise.\" The whole of Part Five is concerned with interest-bearing capital, credit, and the banking system. By this point in the text, Marx has established that interest is a portion of profit, that money-dealing capital is a specialization of the functions of money, that the credit system centralizes and economizes the use of money, and that the mysteries of the \"capital market\" (capital appearing to bear fruit automatically as interest) arise from the fetishized forms taken by the social relations of production. Chapter 28 is where Marx brings this analysis directly into conflict with the dominant practical wisdom of the age — the wisdom embedded in the Bank Act of 1844, in parliamentary testimony from Bank of England governors, and in the theoretical treatises of the Banking School. The chapter is also a case study in Marx's method: the method of dissolving a false conceptual distinction by showing that the phenomena it was meant to explain can be explained on the basis of a smaller and more coherent set of distinctions (means of purchase vs. means of payment; revenue circulation vs. capital circulation; domestic circulation vs. international circulation; the functions of the hoard). The critique of Tooke and Fullarton is not merely negative; it is the vehicle through which Marx sharpens his own account of the money market. A crucial scholarly nuance: much of the chapter's central positive argument (the three cases of bank advances) was, as a footnote indicates, \"incomprehensible in the original manuscript\" and was \"newly drafted by the editor\" — i.e., by Friedrich Engels (marked \"F. E.\") in his work of preparing Volume 3 for publication. This means the reader is dealing with a text that is partly Marx and partly Marx-reconstructed-by-Engels. The editorial reconstruction is, however, strongly continuous with Chapter 26 (where the same point was \"already dealt with in a different context,\" pp. 557–9), and it articulates points Marx makes elsewhere. It is important to register this not to diminish the chapter but to understand its texture: it is a palimpsestic argument in which the empirical engagement with 1840s parliamentary testimony is threaded through a theoretical reconstruction. What the Reader Should Take Away By the end of the chapter, the reader should be able to see that: (a) \"currency vs. capital\" is a distinction without a difference as far as the functions of money go; (b) the demand for \"capital\" in a crisis, when unmasked, is usually a demand for money in its function as means of payment — specifically, for the international means of payment (gold) when a drainage is underway, and for banknotes or book credit when it is not; (c) the fact that the note circulation of the Bank of England can decline while it performs enormous monetary accommodation is not evidence that the circulation is unaffected, but rather that the circulation's composition has shifted toward means-of-payment circulation that immediately refluxes and vanishes; (d) the genuine significance of the drain of gold is that what is demanded is money in its world money form — the absolute, metallic form of value — which is why capital in this crisis is not lacking but is present in superabundance in the form of unsaleable commodity capital; and (e) the banking school's \"capital\" is exposed as a banking-standpoint category (that which the banker must lend out of his own resources rather than out of his credit), not a category of political economy. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Tooke–Wilson Distinction and Its Source Material The chapter opens by naming its quarry: the distinction between currency and capital \"as made by Tooke, Wilson and others,\" together with the related distinctions between \"means of circulation as money\" and \"means of circulation as money capital in general\" — or as the English put it, as \"interest-bearing or monied capital\" — which, in Marx's sardonic phrasing, are \"simply lumped together haphazardly.\" The chapter's programmatic sentence announces that this whole tangle \"comes down to two things.\" Before proceeding, Marx reproduces, in footnote 89, the crucial passage from Tooke's Inquiry into the Currency Principle in Tooke's own words (the earlier quotation in the text had been in German translation). Tooke divides the business of bankers into two branches, corresponding to Adam Smith's distinction between transactions among \"dealers and dealers\" and transactions between \"dealers and consumers.\" The first branch is \"to collect capital from those who have not immediate employment for it, and to distribute or transfer it to those who have\"; the second is \"to receive deposits of the incomes of their customers, and to pay out the amount, as it is wanted for expenditure by the latter in the objects of their consumption.\" Tooke concludes: the former is \"a circulation of capital,\" the latter \"of currency.\" He elaborates: the first is \"the concentration of capital on the one hand and the distribution of it on the other\"; the latter is \"administering the circulation for local purposes of the district.\" This is a definitional move of enormous practical consequence: if \"currency\" and \"capital\" name two genuinely distinct things, then policy questions about the one (e.g., note issues) can be separated from questions about the other (e.g., loanable funds). Marx will systematically deny that any such clean separation can be made. Kinnear's Approach Marx interposes that \"Kinnear comes much closer to the correct conception,\" quoting from J. G. Kinnear's The Crisis and the Currency (London, 1847). Kinnear's argument runs: money is employed to perform two essentially distinct operations. As a medium of exchange between dealers and dealers, it is \"the instrument by which transfers of capital are effected; that is, the exchange of a certain amount of capital in money for an equal amount of capital in commodities.\" But money employed in the payment of wages and in purchase and sale between dealers and consumers \"is not capital, but income\" — that portion of the community's incomes devoted to daily expenditure. It circulates in constant daily use, and this alone \"can, with strict propriety, be termed currency.\" Kinnear then adds a distinction that will become the pivot of the chapter: \"Advances of capital depend entirely on the will of the Bank and other possessors of capital, for borrowers are always to be found; but the amount of the currency depends on the wants of the community, among whom the money circulates, for the purposes of daily expenditure.\" Kinnear is \"closer to the correct conception\" because he locates the distinction in the character of the incomes and capital being spent and transferred, not in some independent circulation domain. But note carefully: Kinnear still calls the income-side money \"currency\" and the capital-side \"capital.\" Marx's task in the following pages is to show that even Kinnear's framing is a misdescription — though a much less misleading one than Tooke's — because the same physical money does both, and because \"currency\" and \"capital\" are not the right names for the two functions. Footnote 89 also introduces James Wilson, whose biographical particulars Marx supplies to situate him in the debates: founder (1843) and first editor of The Economist , later a leading free-trade publicist, Financial Secretary to the Treasury 1853–58, and an opponent of the quantity theory and of the 1844 Bank Act. This signals that Marx is engaging a live, politically consequential school of thought, not a set of academic abstractions. Marx's Reduction of Tooke's Distinction to Two Things Marx now states what Tooke's distinction \"comes down to.\" The means of circulation circulates in two ways: First , as coin (money), insofar as it mediates the expenditure of revenue — commerce between individual consumers and retail traders, counting all merchants who sell to the consumer direct (individual consumers as distinct from productive consumers or producers). Here \"money circulates in the function of coin, even though it constantly replaces capital.\" A certain portion of a country's money is always devoted to this function, even though the particular pieces comprising it are constantly changing. Second , insofar as money mediates the transfer of capital — whether as means of purchase (means of circulation) or means of payment — it is capital. Having laid out this two-part division, Marx immediately attacks the criterion: \"Thus it is neither the function of means of purchase nor that of means of payment that distinguishes it from coin.\" Money can function as means of purchase between one dealer and another (if one buys from another with cash), and can function as means of payment between dealer and consumer (if credit is given, revenue being consumed first and paid for afterwards). So the functional criterion collapses. The real difference, Marx argues, is not functional but relational : in the second case, the money \"does not just replace capital for one party, the seller, but is also spent and advanced as capital by the other party, the buyer.\" The distinction, then, is \"one between the money form of revenue and the money form of capital, not between currency and capital\" — because a definite quantity of money circulates in both sets of transactions (dealer–dealer and dealer–consumer), so that it is \"equally circulation in both functions.\" Here is the first major conceptual move. The proper contrast is between money-as-form-of-revenue and money-as-form-of-capital, not between currency and capital. The reason this is decisive is that it implies that the same money, functioning identically as circulation , can represent revenue for one party and capital for another, or revenue at one moment and capital at another. A \"currency vs. capital\" distinction invites us to imagine two separate streams of money; Marx's distinction collapses the illusion into a single stream whose social significance differs on either side of each transaction. The Three Modes of Confusion in Tooke's Conception Marx then enumerates the three ways Tooke's conception \"introduces confusion into the question\": By confusing the functional characteristics — treating money as \"currency\" in one form and \"capital\" in another, as though these were distinct functions of money. By bringing in the question of the overall quantity of money circulating — in both functions taken together. By bringing in the question of the relative proportions of the circulating medium in the two functions — and hence in the two spheres of the reproduction process. These three modes are not independent; they are three progressively deeper layers of the same underlying error. Marx will address each in turn, and the third will open onto the chapter's longest and most important positive analysis (the behavior of circulation in prosperity and crisis). Point (1): The Confusion of Functional Characteristics Marx restates the point sharply: insofar as money serves to realize revenue or to transfer capital, it functions \"either in buying and selling or in payment, as means of purchase or payment, and in the broader sense of the terms as means of circulation.\" The further characteristic it may have in the accounts of its spender or receiver — that it represents capital or revenue — \"alters absolutely nothing here.\" He demonstrates this in two ways. First demonstration: the mobility of the same piece of money across spheres. Although the kinds of money circulating in the two spheres are different, \"the same piece of money, for instance a £5 note, moves from one sphere to the other and performs both functions in turn; this is unavoidable simply because the retail trader can give his capital its money form only in the form of the coin that he receives from his buyers.\" This is a tightly argued point. The retailer's capital is embodied in commodities; to realize it he must sell; his buyers are consumers spending revenue; the money they pay is therefore, from the retailer's side, the money form of his capital, while from the consumers' side it is the money form of their revenue. One physical token, one circulation, two social meanings. Marx then develops the concrete institutional mechanics. Small change \"proper\" has \"the centre of gravity of its circulation in the realm of retail trade\" — the retailer uses it to give change and receives it back. But the retailer also receives money in the metal that is a measure of value (sovereigns in England) and banknotes, particularly lower denominations (£5 and £10). These gold coins and notes, \"as well as some surplus small change,\" he deposits each day or week in the bank, and he pays for his purchases with cheques on his bank deposit. But the same gold coins and notes \"are just as constantly withdrawn from the bank by the public as a whole in their capacity as consumers, as the money form of their revenue,\" directly or indirectly (e.g., smaller coins by manufacturers for wage payment); they are \"constantly flowing back to the retail traders, for whom they thus realize a part of their capital afresh and at the same time part of their revenue.\" The last phrase is the analytic crux: the returning money realizes at the same time a part of the retailer's capital and a part of his revenue. This is, Marx says, \"important and is completely overlooked by Tooke.\" He then connects this to the reproduction process: \"Only in so far as money is laid out as money capital, at the beginning of the reproduction process (Volume 2, Part One), does capital value exist in its pure form. For the commodity produced contains not only capital but also surplus-value; it is not just inherently capital but capital that has already become such, capital together with the source of revenue incorporated in it.\" Consequently, \"What the retail trader parts with in exchange for the money that flows back to him, i.e. his commodity, is thus for him capital plus profit, capital plus revenue.\" Second demonstration: the false inference from the retailer's reflux. \"Moreover, since the circulating money flows back to the retailer, it restores the money form of his capital.\" The return of money therefore cannot be read as a mere \"currency\" phenomenon; it is simultaneously the restoration of capital form and the realization of revenue. Marx's conclusion for point (1) is emphatic: \"It is completely mistaken, therefore, to transform the distinction between circulation as circulation of revenue and as circulation of capital into a distinction between currency and capital.\" The Banker's Standpoint: Why the Currency/Capital Distinction Arises Marx now offers a sociological diagnosis of the origin of Tooke's expression. It arises \"through his simply taking the point of view of the banker issuing his own banknotes.\" The amount of the banker's notes continuously in the hands of the public (even if always different particular notes) and functioning as means of circulation \"cost him nothing besides paper and printing costs.\" The notes are \"circulating certificates of indebtedness (bills of exchange) made out in his own name,\" even if they bring him in money and thus serve as a means of valorizing his capital. \"But they are different from capital, whether his own or borrowed.\" Hence a special distinction, for the banker, between currency and capital — \"which has nothing to do with defining the concepts as such, let alone Tooke's suggested definitions.\" This is a crucial methodological observation, and it recurs later (\"the narrow-minded banker's conception of circulation\"). The category error is motivated : the issuing banker experiences his own note issue as free (costing only paper), and therefore experiences any advance that must be made beyond that free note issue as an advance of something else — \"capital.\" Marx insists that this subjective, accounting standpoint generates a pseudo-concept, which the theorists then mistake for an objective distinction in political economy. The critique is thus, in good Marxian fashion, a critique of fetishized appearance : a real experience at the level of the agent (the banker) generates a false category at the level of theory. The Means of Payment / Means of Purchase Distinction as Internal to Money Marx then makes a transitional step that sets up the deeper argument. Money's character as means of circulation is unaffected by whether it is the money form of revenue or of capital. But there is a tendency : if money appears as the money form of revenue, it functions \"more as a means of circulation in the strict sense (coin, means of purchase),\" because of the fragmentation of these purchases and because the majority of revenue spenders — the workers — \"can buy relatively little on credit.\" Conversely, in the world of trade and commerce, where the circulating medium is the money form of capital, money functions \"principally as means of payment,\" partly because of concentration and partly because of the prevailing credit system. This tendency is real and important — it will be central to the crisis analysis later. But Marx immediately warns: \"the distinction between money as means of payment and money as means of purchase (circulating medium) is a distinction within money itself, not a distinction between money and capital.\" The analogy he offers makes the point vivid: if more copper and silver circulate in the retail trade and more gold in the wholesale trade, this does not turn the distinction between silver/copper and gold into a distinction between currency and capital. The materials and the typical functions may correlate with the two spheres, but the spheres do not thereby become two kinds of value — currency and capital. Point (2): The Quantity of Money Circulating in Both Functions Marx disposes of the second source of confusion relatively briefly, because it can be settled by appeal to results already established. Insofar as money circulates, whether as means of purchase or means of payment, irrespective of sphere or of whether it realizes revenue or capital, \"the laws developed earlier in considering simple commodity circulation (Volume 1, Chapter 3, 2, b) still apply for the quantity of money circulating.\" Marx then restates those laws. The amount of money in circulation, the amount of currency, is determined by the same factors in both cases: The velocity of circulation — the number of times the same function of means of purchase and payment is repeated by the same piece of money in a given period. The mass of simultaneous sales and purchases, or payments. The sum of the prices of the commodities circulating. The balances of payments that have to be settled at the same time. The conclusion is blunt: \"Whether the money functioning in this way represents capital or revenue for those who pay it and receive it is absolutely without any bearing on the matter. Its quantity is determined simply by its function as means of purchase and payment.\" This is the reductio of Tooke's position. Tooke's distinction would imply that two different quantities — the \"currency\" quantity and the \"capital\" quantity — obey different determining laws. Marx shows they cannot, because the determinants operate on money's function as means of purchase/payment, not on its social destination. The point is that the determinants (velocity, price-sum, simultaneity, payment balances) are properties of the circulation process as such , indifferent to whether the money will be booked as revenue or as capital. Point (3): Relative Proportions of the Circulating Medium in the Two Spheres Marx introduces the third confusion by acknowledging that the two spheres do have \"an inner connection\": the amount of revenue to be spent expresses the scale of consumption; the amount of capital circulating in production and trade expresses the scale and speed of the reproduction process. Nevertheless, \"the same factors have different effects, and even work in opposite directions, on the quantity of money circulating in the two functions or spheres.\" This differential, Marx says, gives \"a new occasion for Tooke's absurd distinction between currency and capital.\" And here Marx delivers a methodological rebuke that is important to register: \"The fact that the Currency Principle gentlemen confuse two disparate things is in no way a reason for presenting this as a conceptual distinction.\" In other words, the existence of a real differential behavior does not license the reification of that differential into two distinct substances (\"currency,\" \"capital\"). The differential is a differential within circulation. Marx now turns to the substantive analysis, which is the positive core of the chapter. Prosperity: The Expansion of Revenue Circulation In \"times of prosperity, of great expansion, when the reproduction process exhibits a great acceleration and energy,\" Marx enumerates the changes: Workers are fully employed; in most cases there is even a rise in wages, which to some extent balances the fall below the average in other phases of the commercial cycle. Capitalists' revenues grow significantly. Consumption generally rises. Commodity prices rise \"just as regularly, at least in certain decisive branches of business.\" The consequence for circulation: the quantity of money in circulation grows, \"at least within certain limits, since the greater velocity of circulation places its own barriers on the growth in the quantity of the circulating medium.\" This is a subtle application of the quantity law: rising prices and rising transaction volume would by themselves demand more money, but the same prosperity raises velocity, which economizes on the quantity required. Hence growth in the quantity of money is bounded. Marx then addresses a classic pitfall explicitly: \"We must not however count this twice: once as money needed to circulate the variable capital, and then again as money needed to circulate the workers' revenue.\" The money paid to workers as wages is spent in retail trade and returns \"with the same weekly regularity\" to the banks in the deposits of the retail trader, after mediating all kinds of intermediate transactions in smaller circuits. This is a concrete illustration of the identity-of-money point made earlier: the wage-money is the money form of variable capital from the capitalist's side and the money form of revenue from the worker's side, but it is one stream of circulation, not two. Consequently, in prosperity the reflux of money proceeds smoothly for the industrial capitalists, \"and so their need for monetary accommodation is not increased by their having to pay more in wages, more money for the circulation of their variable capital.\" The overall result: \"in periods of prosperity the mass of the circulating medium that serves for the expenditure of revenue experiences a decisive growth.\" The Reflux Mechanism and Credit Marx now contrasts this with the means of circulation needed for transfers of capital — transfers \"simply between the capitalists themselves.\" A period of brisk business is simultaneously \"a period of elastic and easy credit.\" The velocity of circulation between capitalist and capitalist \"is regulated directly by credit,\" and the amount of circulating medium required to settle payments and make cash purchases \"undergoes a relative decline.\" It \"may expand in absolute terms, but it always decreases relatively, compared with the expansion of the reproduction process.\" Two mechanisms: A larger mass of payments is settled without any intervention of money (netting, book credit, clearing). Given the vigour of the process, there is quicker movement of the same quantities of money, as both means of purchase and payment. \"The same amount of money mediates the reflux of a greater number of individual capitals.\" The overall picture in prosperity: monetary circulation appears \"full,\" \"although its division II (transfer of capital) is at least relatively contracted, while division I (expenditure of revenue) undergoes an absolute expansion.\" This constitutes Marx's two-sector model of circulation in boom: Division I (revenue expenditure) expands absolutely; Division II (capital transfer) contracts relatively. Note the \"division I / division II\" terminology, which Marx introduces here and uses consistently through the chapter. Marx then develops the concept of reflux . The refluxes \"express the transformation of commodity capital back into money, M–C–M′,\" as seen in the analysis of the reproduction process (Volume 2, Part One). Here we encounter the circuit of capital in its money-mediated form: M (money) is advanced, transformed into C (commodity, including means of production and labour-power), which is then transformed back into M′ (a larger money sum, containing surplus-value). Credit intervenes to make \"the reflux in the money form independent of the point in time of the actual reflux.\" Each capitalist sells on credit: his commodity is alienated before it is transformed back into money for him. He buys on credit: the value of his commodity has been transformed back for him either into productive capital or into commodity capital even before the price falls due and is paid. The consequence in prosperity: \"the reflux takes place smoothly and easily. The retail trader is certain to pay the wholesaler, the latter the manufacturer, and he the importer of raw material, etc.\" But Marx adds the crucial warning about appearance vs. reality : \"The appearance of rapid and assured refluxes always persists for a certain time after these are really at an end, by virtue of the credit that has already been given, since the credit refluxes stand in for real ones.\" The banks begin to scent danger \"as soon as their clients deposit more bills of exchange with them than money.\" He refers to the evidence of the Liverpool bank director quoted earlier (p. 541). The signal of trouble is the substitution of credit claims for money in bank deposits. Marx reinforces this with a quotation from his own earlier work: \"In periods of expanding credit the velocity of currency increases faster than the prices of commodities, whereas in periods of contracting credit the velocity of currency declines faster than the prices of commodities\" ( A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy , p. 105). This gives a precise asymmetry: velocity is more elastic than prices in the expansion, and contracts faster than prices in the contraction. Crisis: The Reversal \"In periods of crisis, the opposite is the case.\" Marx enumerates the changes in each division: Division I (revenue expenditure) : circulation contracts, prices fall, wages fall, the number of workers employed is restricted, turnover declines. Division II (capital transfer) : \"the need for monetary accommodation grows with the decline in credit,\" which Marx says will be examined in more detail immediately. Marx states the result confidently: \"There can be no doubt at all that with the decline in credit, which goes together with a stagnation in the reproduction process, the amount of currency required for no. I, the expenditure of revenue, declines, whereas that for no. II, the transfer of capital, increases.\" He then poses the critical question: \"It remains to be investigated, however, how far this is identical with the assertions of Fullarton and others.\" The Fullarton assertion is quoted: \"A demand for capital on loan and a demand for additional circulation are quite distinct things, and not often found associated\" (Fullarton, On the Regulation of Currencies , p. 82; the title of Chapter 5). Fullarton's Thesis in Full Footnote 90 reproduces Fullarton's argument at length, and it is worth reconstructing closely because Marx's critique is measured against its details. Fullarton writes: It is \"a great error\" to imagine that the demand for pecuniary accommodation (that is, for the loan of capital) \"is identical with a demand for additional means of circulation, or even that the two are frequently associated.\" Each demand \"originates in circumstances peculiarly affecting itself, and very distinct from each other.\" When everything looks prosperous — wages high, prices rising, factories busy — \"an additional supply of currency is usually required to perform the additional functions inseparable from the necessity of making larger and more numerous payments.\" But it is \"chiefly in a more advanced stage of the commercial cycle, when difficulties begin to present themselves, when markets are overstocked, and returns delayed,\" that interest rises and pressure comes upon the Bank for advances of capital. It is true there is no medium through which the Bank is accustomed to advance capital except its promissory notes, and to refuse the notes is to refuse the accommodation. \"But the accommodation once granted, everything adjusts itself in conformity with the necessities of the market; the loan remains, and the currency, if not wanted, finds its way back to the issuer.\" Parliamentary Returns show that the securities in the hands of the Bank of England \"fluctuate more frequently in an opposite direction to its circulation than in concert with it.\" \"No bank can enlarge its circulation, if that circulation be already adequate to the purposes to which a banknote currency is commonly applied; but that every addition to its advances, after that limit is passed, must be made from its capital, and supplied by the sale of some of its securities in reserve, or by abstinence from further investment in such securities.\" The table compiled from Parliamentary Returns for 1833–1840 furnishes continued examples. On 3 January 1837, \"when the resources of the Bank were strained to the uttermost to sustain credit and meet the difficulties of the money-market,\" advances on loan and discount reached £17,022,000, \"an amount scarcely known since the war,\" while the aggregate issues \"remain unmoved at so low a point as £17,076,000.\" Conversely, on 4 June 1833, circulation was £18,892,000 with private securities in hand \"nearly, if not the very lowest on record for the last half-century,\" at £972,000. The empirical pattern Fullarton is highlighting is real and important: securities (advances) and note circulation move inversely. When the Bank lends heavily in a crisis, its note circulation is low; when it lends little in a quiet period, its note circulation is high or high relative to advances. Fullarton uses this to argue that the note issue is determined by the needs of circulation (a fixed demand ceiling), and that advances beyond that ceiling must come from the Bank's \"capital\" (sale of securities), not from note creation. \"Capital,\" for Fullarton, thus names the Bank's own resources that must be liquidated to make advances beyond the note-issue ceiling. Footnote 90 also includes the evidence of Mr. Weguelin, Governor of the Bank of England, which Marx introduces to drive a wedge between a \"demand for pecuniary accommodation\" and a \"demand for gold\" (which Wilson, Tooke, and others call capital). Weguelin: \"The discounting of bills to that extent\" (one million daily for three successive days) \"would not reduce the reserve\" (of banknotes) \"unless the public demanded a greater amount of active circulation. The notes issued on the discount of bills would be returned through the medium of the bankers and through deposits. Unless these transactions were for the purpose of exporting bullion, and unless there were an amount of internal panic which induced people to lock up their notes, and not to pay them into the hands of the bankers... the reserve would not be affected by the magnitude of the transactions.\" And: \"The Bank may discount a million and a half a day, and that is done constantly, without its reserve being in the slightest degree affected, the notes coming back again as deposits, and no other alteration taking place than the mere transfer from one account to another\" ( Report on Bank Acts , 1857, Evidence, nos. 241, 500). Marx draws the conclusion: \"The notes therefore serve here merely as means of transferring credits.\" Marx's Critique of Fullarton's Opposition Marx now dismantles Fullarton's claim that \"a demand for capital on loan and a demand for additional circulation are quite distinct things.\" Two things must be distinguished. First , on the prosperity case. It is clear that in prosperity, when the quantity of circulating medium must grow, there is a growing demand for it. But, Marx argues, \"if a manufacturer draws more out of his bank to deposit in gold or banknotes because he has to spend more capital in the money form, it is not his demand for capital that grows on this account but only his demand for this particular form of expending his capital.\" The demand relates \"only to the technical form in which he puts his capital into circulation.\" Marx illustrates: just as, according to the differential development of the credit system, the same variable capital, the same amount of wages, \"requires a greater quantity of circulating medium in one country than in another\" — in England, for example, more than in Scotland; in Germany more than in England. In agriculture, too, the same capital active in the reproduction process \"requires different amounts of money in different seasons to perform its function.\" The point is that the demand for money here is a technical/form demand, not a demand for additional capital . The manufacturer is not asking for more capital; he is asking to hold the same capital in a different (money) form. Second , and more importantly, Marx turns Fullarton's own contrast inside out. \"It is in no way, as he claims, the strong demand for loans that distinguishes the period of stagnation from that of prosperity, but rather the ease with which this demand is satisfied in the time of prosperity and the difficulty of satisfying it once stagnation has set in.\" The demand for loans is strong in both ; what changes is the supply side — the ease or difficulty of meeting it. And Marx identifies the deeper mechanism: \"It is in fact precisely the tremendous development of the credit system during the period of prosperity, and also therefore the enormous rise in the demand for loan capital and the readiness with which this is made available in such periods, that leads to the shortage of credit in the period of stagnation.\" Thus the very elasticity of credit in prosperity is what generates the tightness in the downturn. Credit does not simply respond to conditions; it produces the conditions of its own restriction by over-expanding. \"Thus it is not a difference in the demand for loans that distinguishes the two periods.\" Marx then restates the genuine distinction: \"As we have noted before, the two periods are distinguished in the first place by the fact that in the period of prosperity it is the demand for means of circulation between consumers and dealers that is dominant, while in the period of depression it is the demand for means of circulation between capitalists. In the period of stagnation the first declines while the second increases.\" What Fullarton Means by \"Capital\" Marx now performs the decisive conceptual unmasking. What \"strikes Fullarton and others as decisively important\" is the phenomenon that \"at such times, while securities in the hands of the Bank of England increase, its note circulation declines, and vice versa.\" But the volume of securities \"expresses the volume of monetary accommodation, of discounted bills of exchange, and of advances against marketable securities.\" Marx then reconstructs Fullarton's argument: Fullarton says that securities in the hands of the Bank of England generally fluctuate in the opposite direction to its note circulation, and that this confirms the old-established principle of the private banks that no bank can increase its note issue beyond a certain definite amount, determined by the needs of its clientele. If it wants to make further advances above this amount, it must make these out of its own capital — either realize on securities or utilize deposits it would otherwise have invested. \"We see now what Fullarton means by capital.\" In his view, capital is involved when the bank can no longer make advances with its own banknotes — its own promises to pay, which cost it nothing. But with what does it make these advances? \"With the proceeds from the sale of 'securities in reserve,' i.e. government paper, stocks and other interest-bearing paper.\" And what does it sell these securities for? \"For money, gold or banknotes, in so far as the latter are legal tender, as those of the Bank of England are.\" What it advances, therefore, is \"in all circumstances money.\" If it advances gold, obvious. If notes, \"then these notes now represent capital, since the bank has parted with a real value in exchange, i.e. interest-bearing securities.\" Marx then handles the special case of the private banks: the notes that accrue to them by the sale of securities can only be, in the main, either notes of the Bank of England or its own notes, \"since others are only accepted with reticence in payment for securities.\" But if it is the Bank of England itself, then those of its own notes that it retains cost it capital, i.e. interest-bearing securities. Besides, it thereby withdraws its own notes from circulation. If it reissues them or issues new notes to the same amount, \"these now represent capital.\" And they represent capital just as much when used for advances to capitalists as when used later, when the demand for monetary accommodation subsides, for new investments in securities. \"In all these circumstances, the term capital is used here simply in the banking sense, where it means that the banker is forced to lend more than just his credit.\" This is the chapter's central deflationary move. Fullarton's \"capital\" is not a category of political economy at all; it is an accounting category internal to the banking firm. \"Capital\" = the resources the banker must liquidate because he has exhausted his free credit-creation capacity. Marx's critique therefore replicates, at the level of monetary theory, his general critique of political economy: the practitioners of a sphere mistake the forms of their own practice for objective categories. The banker's \"capital\" is to the real capital of society as the merchant's \"profit on alienation\" was to surplus-value — an appearance generated by the specific standpoint of the agent. The Drain of Gold Marx now asks the operational question that the securities/circulation inverse pattern raises: \"As is well known, the Bank of England makes all its advances in its own notes. If despite this, then, the Bank's note circulation normally decreases as the discounted bills and securities in its possession — i.e. the advances it has made — increase, what then becomes of the notes put into circulation, and how do they flow back to the Bank?\" He first disposes of one case: when the demand for monetary accommodation \"arises from an unfavourable national balance of payments and hence mediates a drain of gold.\" Then \"the matter is very simple. The banknotes are exchanged against gold at the Bank itself, in the Issue Department, and the gold is exported.\" It is as if the Bank paid gold directly, without the mediation of notes, as it does in discounting bills of exchange. \"A rising demand of this kind — and in certain cases it reaches £7 to £10 million — naturally adds not a single £5 note to the country's domestic circulation.\" Now comes a pivotal theoretical statement. If it is said that the Bank advances capital in this case and not currency , this has \"a double meaning\": That it does not advance credit but real value — a part of its own capital or the capital deposited with it. That it advances money not for domestic circulation but for international circulation, world money. And for this purpose, \"the money must always exist in its hoard form, its metallic embodiment; in the form in which it is not only the form of value but itself equal to the value whose money form it is.\" Even though this gold represents capital both for the Bank and for the exporting gold dealer (banking capital or commercial capital), \"the demand does not arise for it as capital but rather as the absolute form of money capital. It arises in the very same moment as the foreign markets are flooded with unrealizable English commodity capital. What is demanded is not capital as capital but rather capital as money, in the form in which money is a commodity on the general world market; and this is its original form as precious metal.\" Marx's conclusion on this point is a precise, dialectical refutation of both schools. \"The drain of gold is not, as Fullarton, Tooke, etc. say, 'simply a question of capital.' It is rather a 'question of money,' even if in a specific function.\" And, crucially, the Currency School's error and the Banking School's error are mirror images: \"The fact that it is not a question of domestic circulation, as the Currency Theory people maintain, in no way proves, as Fullarton and others believe, that it is just a 'question of capital.' It is 'a question of money,' in the form in which money is an international means of payment.\" Marx then quotes Fullarton: \"Whether that capital [the purchase price for the millions of quarters of foreign wheat imported into the home country after a harvest failure] is transmitted in merchandise or in specie, is a point which in no way affects the nature of the transaction\" (Fullarton, p. 131). Marx concedes the transactional point but insists it is irrelevant to the monetary question: \"But it has a very definite effect on whether there is a drain of gold or not. Capital is converted into the form of precious metal because it cannot be converted at all into the form of commodities, or not without a very major loss.\" And then the famous observation: \"The anxiety that the modern banking system has when faced with a drain of gold goes beyond anything that the Monetary System ever dreamed of, even though for it precious metal was the only true wealth.\" The Morris Testimony To dramatize the point that gold is treated as \"the only true wealth\" in a crisis, Marx introduces the testimony of Morris, Governor of the Bank of England, before the parliamentary committee investigating the crisis of 1847–8: Question 3846 : \"When I spoke of the depreciation of stocks and fixed capital, are you not aware that all property invested in stocks and produce of every description was depreciated in the same way; that raw cotton, raw silk, and unmanufactured wool were sent to the continent at the same depreciated price, and that sugar, coffee and tea were sacrificed as at forced sales? — It was inevitable that the country should make a considerable sacrifice for the purpose of meeting the efflux of bullion which had taken place in consequence of the large importation of food.\" Question 3848 : \"Do not you think it would have been better to trench upon the £8 million lying in the coffers of the Bank, than to have endeavoured to get the gold back again at such a sacrifice? — No, I do not.\" Marx's gloss: \"Here gold is taken as the only true wealth.\" This is the empirical punchline of the theoretical argument: at the moment of crisis, the entire apparatus of commodity wealth (cotton, silk, wool, sugar, coffee, tea) is sacrificed in forced sales to preserve the metallic hoard. The \"absolute form of value\" — money as world money — asserts its dominance precisely when commodity capital is most abundant and least saleable. This is the concrete inverse of an ordinary crisis: not scarcity but superabundance of commodity capital, combined with a desperate scramble for money. Tooke's Discovery on Exchange and Drain of Gold Marx then turns to an empirical finding of Tooke's, quoted by Fullarton. Tooke's discovery: \"with only one or two exceptions, and those admitting of satisfactory explanation, every remarkable fall of exchange, followed by a drain of gold, that has occurred during the last half-century, has been coincident throughout with a comparatively low state of the circulating medium, and vice versa\" (Fullarton, p. 121). Marx reads this as showing that drains of gold \"take place in most cases after a period of excitement and speculation, as 'the signal of a collapse already commenced... an indication of overstocked markets, of a cessation of the foreign demand for our productions, of delayed returns, and, as the necessary sequel of all these, of commercial discredit, manufactories shut up, artisans starving, and a general stagnation of industry and enterprise'\" (p. 129). Marx notes this is \"also of course the best refutation of the Currency people's contention that 'a full circulation drives out bullion and a low circulation attracts it.'\" The Currency School held that a full domestic circulation indicates excess issue, which spills abroad as gold; and a low domestic circulation attracts gold. Tooke's empirical finding reverses the causal story: the low circulation coincides with the gold drain, because the drain follows a collapse — the collapse reduces circulation and drives out gold simultaneously. Marx acknowledges a point about the Bank's gold reserve: \"although it is generally in periods of prosperity that the Bank of England has a strong gold reserve, this is always formed in the slack and stagnant period that follows the storm.\" So the reserve is built after the crisis, in the subsequent stagnation, not during prosperity. This is a sharp point against naive readings of the reserve's significance. The Multiple Functions of the Hoard Marx summarizes: all this wisdom about the drain of gold \"amounts to saying that the demand for international means of circulation and payment is different from the demand for domestic means of circulation and payment\" — which is why Fullarton can say, on p. 112, that \"the existence of a drain does not necessarily imply any diminution of the internal demand for circulation.\" And the export of precious metals abroad \"is not the same as putting notes or coin into circulation domestically.\" Marx then refers back to earlier work: \"I have already shown earlier that the movement of the hoard that is set aside as a reserve fund for international payments has in and of itself nothing to do with the movement of money as means of circulation\" (see Volume 1, pp. 242–4). But he acknowledges a \"certain complication\": the different functions of the hoard — as a reserve fund of means of payment (for payments falling due at home), as a reserve fund of circulating medium, and as a reserve fund for world money — \"are all imposed upon a single reserve fund.\" From this it follows that \"in certain circumstances a drain of gold from the Bank domestically may be combined with a drain abroad.\" A further complication: the additional function \"quite arbitrarily laid on this hoard,\" namely to serve as a guarantee for the convertibility of banknotes, in countries where the credit system and credit money are developed. And on top of all this: (1) the concentration of the national reserve fund in a single principal bank, and (2) its reduction to the minimum possible. This produces Fullarton's complaint (p. 143): \"One cannot contemplate the perfect silence and facility with which variations of the exchange usually pass off in continental countries, compared with the state of feverish disquiet and alarm always produced in England whenever the treasure at the Bank seems to be at all approaching to exhaustion, without being struck with the great advantage in this respect which a metallic currency possesses.\" The theoretical weight of this passage is considerable. Marx here identifies the institutional basis of the \"feverish disquiet\": the artificial multiplication of functions on a single, miniaturized reserve fund. The panic is not a natural fact about money; it is an artifact of the banking system's structure — centrally concentrated reserves, minimal reserve ratios, and convertibility guarantees. This is one of the places in the chapter where Marx's critique of the Banking School's \"narrow-minded banker's standpoint\" converges with a structural critique of the credit system itself. How Banks Expand Accommodation Without Expanding Note Issue Returning from the drain of gold, Marx poses the general question: \"if we leave aside for now the drain of gold, how then can a bank that issues banknotes, such as the Bank of England, increase the amount of monetary accommodation it provides without increasing its note issue?\" He first establishes a premise: all notes outside the walls of the bank, whether actually circulating or dormant in private hoards, \"are in circulation as far as the bank itself is concerned, i.e. outside its possession.\" Thus if the bank expands its discounting and money-lending business, its advances against securities, \"the banknotes issued must flow back to it again or otherwise the sum in circulation would increase, which is precisely not supposed to be the case.\" This sets up the question of the reflux , which can take place in two ways: Reflux, first way : The bank pays notes to A against securities; A uses these to pay B for a bill of exchange that falls due; B deposits the notes again with the bank. \"The circulation of these notes is at an end, but the loan remains.\" This is corroborated directly by Fullarton's own formula: \"The loan remains, and the currency, if not wanted, finds its way back to the issuer\" (p. 97). The notes advanced to A have returned to the bank; the bank is a creditor to A (or to whoever drew the bill that A discounted), while it is a debtor to B for the sum of value expressed in the notes, \"and B thereby has at his disposal a corresponding part of the bank's capital.\" Reflux, second way : A pays to B, and either B himself or C (to whom B pays the notes) uses the notes to pay bills due to the bank, directly or indirectly. In this case \"the bank is paid with its own notes. And in this way the transaction is completed (until A's repayment to the bank).\" Marx then asks the loaded question: \"How far, then, should the bank's advance to A be considered as an advance of capital, and how far as simply an advance of means of payment?\" The Three Cases of Bank Advances Footnote 91 is decisive for understanding the textual history: \"The passage now following was incomprehensible in the original manuscript and has been newly drafted by the editor, up to the end of the parentheses. This point has already been dealt with in a different context in Chapter 26 [pp. 557–9] — F. E.\" The reference is to Friedrich Engels. This means the tightly systematized three-case analysis that follows — the chapter's most analytically rigorous passage — was reconstructed by Engels from Marx's notes. It is continuous with Chapter 26, and its theoretical content is thoroughly Marxian, but the reader should register the editorial hand. The parenthetical passage ends with \"F. E.,\" marking where Marx's recoverable text resumes. First case : A obtains the sum advanced by the bank on his personal credit, without giving any security. \"In this case he has received not only an advance of means of payment, but unquestionably also a new capital, which he can use as additional capital in his business, and valorize, until it has to be repaid.\" This is the only case of a genuine advance of capital. Second case : A has given the bank securities as collateral — government bonds or stocks — and received on them a cash advance of, say, up to two-thirds of their present value. \"In this case he has received means of payment that he needed, but no additional capital, for he has given into the bank's possession a greater capital value than he received from it.\" The greater capital value, on the one hand, was \"of no use for his immediate need, as means of payment, since it was invested at interest in a specific form\"; on the other hand, A had \"his reasons for not transforming it directly into means of payment by selling it\" — his securities functioned as reserve capital, and he let them continue to function as such. \"There has therefore been a temporary mutual transfer of capital between A and the bank, so that A has not received any extra capital (on the contrary!), though he has received the means of payment he needed.\" For the bank, the transaction involves \"the temporary tying-up of money capital in a loan, a transformation of money capital from one form to another, and this transformation is precisely the basic function of banking.\" Third case : A has a bill of exchange discounted at the bank and receives the amount due for it in cash, after deduction of the discount. \"In this case, he has sold a non-liquid form of money capital to the bank in exchange for the sum of value in liquid form; the bill of exchange that had not yet expired in return for ready cash.\" The bill is now the bank's property. It makes no difference that A, as last endorser, is responsible in default; he shares this responsibility with the other endorsers and with the drawer. \"In this case, therefore, there is no advance at all, but simply an ordinary purchase and sale.\" A has nothing to repay; the bank reimburses itself by cashing the bill when it falls due. \"Here, too, there has been a reciprocal transfer of capital between A and the bank, and this, moreover, is just like the purchase and sale of any other commodity; for this reason A does not receive any additional capital. What he needed and received was means of payment, and he receives this in that the bank transforms the one form of his money capital — the bill of exchange — into the other — money.\" The conclusion is a striking reduction: \"It is only in the first case, therefore, that there can be any talk of a genuine capital advance.\" In the second and third cases, it occurs \"at most in the sense that every investment of capital implies an 'advance.'\" In that sense, the bank advances A money capital; but for A this is money capital \"at most in the sense that it is a part of his capital in general.\" And crucially: \"he requires and uses it not especially as capital, but rather especially as means of payment. Otherwise every ordinary sale of commodities, by which means of payment are also obtained, would have to be seen as obtaining an advance of capital.\" This passage is the conceptual engine of the chapter's refutation. It shows that what the Banking School called \"advances of capital\" are, in the overwhelming majority of bank operations, either (a) the exchange of one form of money capital for another (discounting), or (b) a temporary mutual transfer of capital against collateral. Genuine net capital advance is confined to unsecured personal credit. The empirical dominance of \"capital advances\" in bank statistics is thus thoroughly misleading as to what is actually happening. Private Banks and Purely Metallic Circulation Marx extends the analysis to private banks with rights of note issue: \"the distinction is that, if their notes neither remain in local circulation nor return to the banks in the form of deposits or for payment of bills falling due, these notes then fall into the possession of people to whom they must pay gold or Bank of England notes in exchange for them. In this case the advance of their notes actually represents an advance of Bank of England notes, or, what is the same thing for them, of gold, i.e. a part of their banking capital.\" The same applies when the Bank of England itself, or any bank subject to a legal maximum on its note issue, has to sell securities to withdraw its own notes from circulation and reissue them in advances — \"here its own notes represent a part of its mobilizable banking capital.\" Then Marx makes a general point: \"Even if circulation were purely metallic,\" there could be simultaneously (1) a drain of gold (\"what is meant here is evidently a drain of gold in which at least one part goes abroad\" — an editor's clarification) that empties the vaults, and (2) since the bank's principal requirement for gold is simply to make payments (to settle past transactions), its advances on securities could greatly increase, but return to it in the form of deposits or in repayment of bills falling due. So that \"on the one hand its overall reserves would decrease with an increase in securities in the bank's portfolio, while on the other hand the same sum that it formerly had as an 'owner' would now be a sum for which it was in debt to its depositors, and finally the total quantity of circulating medium would decline.\" Book Credit Without Notes Marx now removes a simplifying assumption: \"It has so far been assumed that the advances are made in notes and involve at least a temporary increase in the note issue, even if this immediately vanishes again. But this is not necessary.\" Instead of paper notes, the bank can open a credit account for A, \"so that A, as its debtor, becomes an imaginary depositor.\" A pays his creditors with cheques on the bank, and the recipient of these cheques pays them again to his banker, who exchanges them in the clearing house against the cheques drawn on him. \"In this case there is no intervention of notes, and the entire transaction is confined to one in which the bank settles its own debt with a cheque drawn on itself, its actual compensation consisting in its claim against A. In this case the bank has advanced to A a part of its banking capital, in the form of a part of its own claim as a creditor.\" This is the pure book-credit case, and it shows that the entire transaction can occur with zero note issue. The category of \"currency\" is simply inapplicable. Securities Are Not Genuine Capital Marx then broadens the point into a general theoretical statement about what \"money capital\" is in the banking context. \"In so far as this demand for monetary accommodation is a demand for capital, it is simply a demand for money capital, capital from the standpoint of the banker; i.e. a demand for gold (in the case of a drain of gold abroad) or for notes on the national bank, which a private bank can obtain only by buying them with an equivalent, so that they represent capital for it. Or finally it might be a question of interest-bearing securities, government bonds, stocks, etc. that have to be sold if gold or notes are to be obtained.\" He then delivers a decisive theoretical clarification — one that echoes the analysis of \"fictitious capital\" developed elsewhere in Volume 3: Government bonds are \"capital only for the person who has bought them, to whom they represent his purchase price, the capital he has invested in them. They are not capital in themselves, but simply creditor's claims.\" Mortgages are \"simply claims on future payments of ground-rent.\" Stocks of other kinds are \"simply property titles which give the holder a claim to future surplus-value.\" \"None of these things are genuine capital, they do not constitute any component of capital and are also in themselves not values.\" Marx then observes that by similar transactions, \"money that belongs to the bank can be transformed into deposits, so that the bank becomes a claimant for this money instead of its owner, and holds it under a different title. Important as this is for the bank itself, it in no way affects the amount of capital stored in the country, or even the money capital.\" And the theoretical payoff: \"Capital figures here simply as money capital and, if it is not present in the actual money form, as a mere title to capital. This is very important, since the scarcity of, and pressing demand for, banking capital is confused with a restriction of actual capital, which in such cases, on the contrary, is present in excess in the form of means of production and products, and stifles the markets.\" This is one of the most important passages in the chapter. It states the central contradiction of a crisis: the scarcity of banking capital (the appearance) coexists with the superabundance of real capital in the form of unsaleable means of production and products (the reality). The \"denominator\" of the crisis is not absence but excess — the excess that cannot be realized in money form. Why Circulation Can Stay Stable or Decline Marx now synthesizes the whole argument: \"It is very simple to explain, therefore, how the amount of securities held by the bank as collateral increases, and the growing demand for monetary accommodation can be satisfied by the bank, at the same time as the total quantity of means of circulation remains the same or declines.\" In periods of tight money, the total quantity is kept in check in two ways: By a drain of gold. By the demand for money simply as means of payment — where the notes issued flow back immediately or where the transaction takes place by way of book credit, without any mediation of notes. In the latter case, \"payments are effected simply by a credit transaction, the settlement of these payments being the sole object of the exercise.\" Marx then articulates a general law: \"It is money's peculiar property that where it functions simply in settlement of payments (and in times of crisis an advance is obtained in order to pay, not to buy; to settle past transactions, not to start new ones), its actual circulation is simply a vanishing magnitude, even when this settlement does not take place entirely by credit operations, without any intervention of money; i.e. that when there is a great demand for monetary accommodation, a tremendous mass of these transactions can take place without any expansion in the circulation.\" Here we have the conceptual key to the chapter's empirical puzzle. The Bank of England's note circulation can remain stable or decline, while the Bank performs enormous monetary accommodation, because that accommodation is dominated by means-of-payment transactions — advances made \"in order to pay, not to buy\" — whose circuit is a vanishing magnitude: notes are issued and immediately reflux. \"The simple fact that the Bank of England's circulation remains stable or even declines, at the same time as it performs a great deal of monetary accommodation, is in no way prima facie proof, as Fullarton, Tooke and others assume (in consequence of their error in seeing monetary accommodation as identical with the receipt of capital on loan, of additional capital), that the circulation of money (banknotes) in its function as means of payment does not increase and expand.\" Marx then formulates the resolution precisely: \"Since the circulation of notes as means of purchase declines in times of business stagnation, where such a great deal of accommodation is required, their circulation as means of payment can increase while the total sum in circulation, the sum of notes functioning as means of purchase and of payment, still remains stable or even declines. Circulation of banknotes as means of payment, these notes immediately flowing back to the bank that issued them, is precisely not circulation in the eyes of these economists.\" This sentence — \"Circulation of banknotes as means of payment... is precisely not circulation in the eyes of these economists\" — exposes the perceptual blindness at the root of the errors. The Banking School looks past the very circulation that is most active in a crisis. Marx adds a refinement: \"If circulation as means of payment increases to a higher degree than circulation as means of purchase declines, the total circulation will grow, even though the quantity of money functioning as means of purchase experiences a significant decline. And this actually does happen at certain points in the crisis, i.e. when there is a complete breakdown of credit, when not only are commodities and securities unsaleable, but it has also become impossible to get bills of exchange discounted, and nothing counts any more except money payment, or as the merchant says: cash.\" Hence Fullarton's \"sudden, though only temporary, enlargement of the issues\" at the close of 1825, even while the efflux of bullion is still going on, is not accidental. Fullarton on Panic Hoarding Marx quotes Fullarton's own account of this phenomenon: \"With respect again to those examples of eager competition for the possession of banknotes, which characterize seasons of panic and which may sometimes, as at the close of 1825, lead to a sudden, though only temporary, enlargement of the issues, even while the efflux of bullion is still going on, these, I apprehend, are not to be regarded as among the natural or necessary concomitants of a low exchange; the demand in such cases is not for circulation [read: circulation as a means of purchase], but for hoarding, a demand on the part of alarmed bankers and capitalists which arises generally in the last act of the crisis [hence, for a reserve of means of payment], after a long continuation of the drain, and is the precursor of its termination\" (Fullarton, p. 130). Marx's editorial bracketed glosses expose the sleight of hand. Fullarton concedes the empirical fact (a scramble for banknotes in panic) but reinterprets it as a demand \"not for circulation but for hoarding.\" Marx has already shown that this is precisely the demand for money as means of payment — the demand for the \"absolute form of value.\" Fullarton's move of labelling it \"hoarding\" allows him to preserve the fiction that circulation is not really expanding; but it is a semantic evasion, not an analysis. Marx then refers back to his treatment of money as means of payment (Volume 1, Chapter 3, 3, b): \"how, in the case of a violent interruption in the chain of payments, money reverts from its merely ideal form into the material and also absolute form of value vis-à-vis commodities. A few examples of this were given there, in notes [51 and 52]. This interruption itself is in part the effect, in part the cause, of the collapse of credit and the circumstances that accompany it: flooding of markets, devaluation of commodities, interruption of production, etc.\" This is a dialectical statement of reciprocal causation: the interruption of the chain of payments is both cause and effect of the credit collapse. Money, which had functioned ideally (in credit bookkeeping), reverts to its material and \"absolute\" form. Marx's conclusion on this whole line of argument: \"It is clear, however, that Fullarton transforms the distinction between money as means of purchase and money as means of payment into a false distinction between currency and capital. And at the bottom of this again lies the narrow-minded banker's conception of circulation.\" This sentence is the chapter's culminating verdict on the Banking School. Fullarton's error is now fully diagnosed: he has taken a real and important distinction within money (means of purchase vs. means of payment) and mapped it onto a false distinction between money and capital (currency vs. capital). The two errors are structurally identical to Tooke's, and they share the same sociological origin — the banker's standpoint. The Final Question: Capital or Money as Means of Payment? The chapter closes with the question that all the preceding analysis has been preparing: \"It might still be asked what is lacking in such difficult times, capital, or money in its capacity as means of payment? And this is a well-known controversy.\" Marx's answer has two components. First, the drain-of-gold case : \"In so far as the embarrassment is demonstrated by a drain of gold, it is clear that what is demanded is the international means of payment. But money in its capacity as international means of payment is gold in its metallic reality, as itself a valuable substance, an amount of value. It is also capital, but capital not as commodity capital but rather as money capital, capital not in the form of commodity but rather in the form of money (and moreover of money in the pre-eminent sense of the term, in which it exists on the general world commodity market). There is no opposition here between the demand for money as means of payment and the demand for capital. The opposition is rather between capital in its money form and in its commodity form; and the form in which it is required here, and can alone function, is its money form.\" This is the definitive resolution of the \"capital vs. money\" controversy. The apparent opposition is dissolved into a real opposition: capital in money form vs. capital in commodity form . In the drain-of-gold case, what is demanded is capital in its money form, because money is the only form that functions on the world market. There is no separate \"capital\" demand standing apart from the \"money\" demand; capital is being demanded — in its money form. Second, the general crisis case : \"Apart from this demand for gold (or silver), it cannot be said that in such periods of crisis there is in any sense a lack of capital. Under extraordinary circumstances, such as a rise in grain prices, a cotton famine, etc., this can be the case; but these are in no way necessary or regular accompaniments of such periods; and the existence of such a lack of capital, therefore, cannot be immediately inferred from a demand for monetary accommodation. On the contrary. Markets are glutted, swamped with commodity capital. Hence it is in any case not a lack of commodity capital that gives rise to the difficulty. We shall return to this question later.\" The chapter thus ends with a characteristic Marxian inversion. The crisis presents itself as a scarcity of capital ; its reality is a superabundance of commodity capital that cannot be realized in money. The demand for \"capital\" is, in the final analysis, a demand for money in a specific function — and this is the thread Marx promises to pick up again (\"We shall return to this question later\"), linking the chapter forward into the remainder of the analysis of interest-bearing capital and the credit system. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principle: The Functional Unity of Money The chapter's deepest lesson is methodological. It demonstrates, on a highly contested empirical terrain, that conceptual distinctions in political economy must be grounded in functions , not in the standpoints of agents or in accidental correlational patterns . Tooke and Fullarton observed real phenomena — the inverse movement of securities and note circulation, the scramble for money in a panic, the coincidence of low circulation with gold drains — and tried to capture them with a distinction between \"currency\" and \"capital.\" Marx shows that these phenomena are fully explained by: (1) the two functions of money (means of purchase, means of payment); (2) the two spheres of reproduction (revenue expenditure, capital transfer); (3) the determinants of the quantity of money (velocity, price sum, simultaneity of transactions, payment balances); and (4) the institutional structure of the banking system (centralized reserves, convertible notes, credit chains). The distinction between \"currency\" and \"capital\" adds nothing and distorts everything. This is an instance of a general Marxian principle: social categories must correspond to relations of production, not to subjective experiences or bookkeeping conventions. The banker's \"capital\" is the banker's own experience of having exhausted his free credit; it is not the social capital of the community. Marx makes this explicit in the passage on securities not being \"genuine capital.\" The recurring warning — that the scarcity of banking capital is confused with a restriction of actual capital — is a template for the critique of all fetishized economic categories. The Real Distinctions Marx Substitutes Where Tooke and Fullarton put one false distinction (currency/capital), Marx puts four real ones: Means of purchase vs. means of payment — a distinction internal to money's function as means of circulation. In prosperity, means-of-purchase circulation (revenue side) dominates and expands absolutely; in crisis, means-of-payment circulation (capital side) dominates and expands. Circulation of revenue vs. circulation of capital — a distinction in the social character of the money, not in money itself. The same money performs both in turn. Domestic circulation vs. international circulation — the drain of gold is a question of money as world money; it is not simply a question of capital, nor is it a question of domestic circulation. Both the Currency School and the Banking School fail to see this correctly, from opposite sides. Capital in money form vs. capital in commodity form — the real opposition underlying the pseudo-controversy over whether what is lacking in a crisis is capital or money. In the drain case, money form is required; and this is a demand for capital, in a specific form. In the general crisis case, capital is present in excess in commodity form. The Paradox of Crisis: Superabundant Capital, Desperate Scarcity of Money The chapter's most provocative substantive finding is the coexistence of two apparently contradictory states in a crisis: markets are glutted and swamped with commodity capital (superabundance of real capital), while capital is desperately scarce in the form of means of payment (scarcity of money capital). This paradox is not an anomaly; it is the necessary form of crisis in a credit system. The chain of payments, having been built on credit, breaks down; money, which had functioned \"ideally\" in book credit, reverts to its material and absolute form. The very abundance of unsaleable commodities is what makes money scarce — because money can no longer be got by selling. This finding anticipates and grounds the general theory of crisis. The Banking School's view that a crisis reflects a \"lack of capital\" (or a shortage of loanable funds) is the surface appearance; the deeper reality is a crisis of realization — the impossibility of converting commodity capital into money capital on the anticipated terms. And Marx is explicit: \"it is in any case not a lack of commodity capital that gives rise to the difficulty.\" The Critique of the Banking School's \"Standpoint\" The chapter's social epistemology is as important as its economics. Tooke's and Fullarton's errors are not merely logical; they are situated . They arise \"through his simply taking the point of view of the banker issuing his own banknotes\"; they arise at bottom from \"the narrow-minded banker's conception of circulation.\" Marx's critique shows how the specific material position of the theorist — the issuing banker for whom note creation is free and who experiences loan accommodation beyond that as a drain on \"capital\" — generates systematic distortions in theory. The financial theorist who universalizes his own accounting categories mistakes the forms of his practice for the forms of the economy. This is a foreshadowing, in the monetary domain, of the general Marxian critique of political economy as the theory of the forms of appearance of capitalist relations. The task of critique is to identify the social relations that generate, veil, and reproduce those forms. The Institutional Dimension: Panic as Artifact A subtle but significant thread is Marx's treatment of the \"feverish disquiet\" of the English banking system versus the comparative calm of continental metallic systems. Marx does not naturalize this difference. He explains it by reference to: the multiplication of functions on a single reserve fund; the arbitrary requirement that the hoard guarantee note convertibility; the concentration of the national reserve in one bank; and its reduction to a minimum. The panic, in other words, is system-generated , not a fact of nature about money. Fullarton could see the symptom (\"great advantage... which a metallic currency possesses\") but not the structural cause. Marx's analysis thus contains a critique of the credit system's institutional fragility that goes well beyond the doctrinal dispute with which the chapter begins. Ironies and Self-Corrections One fascinating internal irony: Marx uses Fullarton's own evidence against Fullarton. He quotes Fullarton's confession that \"the loan remains, and the currency, if not wanted, finds its way back to the issuer,\" and thereby shows that the phenomenon Fullarton uses to prove the \"currency vs. capital\" distinction is precisely the phenomenon that dissolves it. When the currency \"finds its way back to the issuer,\" the same transactional event is simultaneously an advance of capital (the loan persists) and a circulation of currency (the note is issued). One event, two names — which is exactly Marx's point. Similarly, Marx accepts Fullarton's and Tooke's empirical findings (the inverse movement of securities and circulation; Tooke's discovery about low circulation coinciding with drains of gold) while rejecting the theoretical framework that both theorists constructed to interpret them. The chapter is thus an object lesson in how correct empirical observations can coexist with wrong theoretical conclusions — and how the correction of theory requires reconstructing the conceptual space within which the observations are read. The Textual Dimension: A Chapter at the Boundary of Two Hands Finally, a scholarly lesson about the chapter as an artifact. The three-case analysis of bank advances — the conceptual heart of the chapter's positive argument — was, per footnote 91, reconstructed by Engels from an \"incomprehensible\" manuscript. The editorial marks (\"F. E.\") in the text demarcate Marx's recoverable material from Engels's reconstruction. This does not undermine the argument's Marxian character — it repeats points from Chapter 26 and is consistent with Marx's broader analyses — but it registers the chapter's unique status as a joint product of the two founders of historical materialism. The argument is Marx's; the presentation in this central passage is Engels's. That the reconstruction is so lucid, and so consequential for the chapter's overall structure, is a reminder of Engels's intellectual role in shaping Capital as we read it. Broader Implications For contemporary readers, the chapter's lessons remain sharp. First, the demand for money in a financial crisis is not the same as a demand for real resources; this is a point that recurs throughout modern financial-crisis analysis and bears directly on debates about liquidity provision, lender-of-last-resort policy, and the supposed distinction between \"liquidity crises\" and \"solvency crises.\" Marx's demonstration that the scarcity of money capital can coexist with a superabundance of real capital is a foundational theoretical resource for those debates. Second the chapter is a caution against the reification of accounting distinctions. The distinction between \"currency\" and \"capital\" was not merely theoretically mistaken; it had policy consequences. It was the distinction on which the Bank Act of 1844 and much subsequent practice rested, and its inadequacy was tested (and found wanting) in the crises of 1847–8 and 1857. Marx's argument shows that monetary-theoretical categories that derive their apparent rigor from the practice of a particular institution will fail when the institution's perspective is transcended by the phenomena. Third, the chapter exemplifies a mode of theoretical criticism that is neither merely empirical nor merely conceptual. Marx does not refute Tooke and Fullarton by amassing counter-examples (he largely accepts their examples) nor by pure conceptual analysis (his analysis is always anchored in the concrete forms of banking practice). He refutes them by showing that their concepts do not carve reality at its joints — that what they call \"currency\" and \"capital\" are functions and relations that their own preferred examples demonstrate to be internally connected. The chapter thus performs, in a small compass, the distinctive achievement of Marx's mature economic writing: the simultaneous critique of a doctrine, the rectification of empirical appearances, and the construction of a positive theoretical alternative — all pressed into the service of a single, rigorously maintained thesis: the money that circulates as currency and the money that constitutes capital are one and the same, and their distinction is a distinction, not within money, but within the social relations money mediates. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 27 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 29"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 29", "title": "Chapter 29", "subtitle": "Component Parts of Bank Capital", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 29/Chapter 29 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 9748, "read_time": "49 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital"], "keywords": ["Component Parts of Bank Capital", "Banker's Capital as Chiefly Fictitious Capital", "Deposits, Bills Discounted, and Public Debt Papers", "Pyramid of Credit Built upon Reserve Cash", "Banker as Administrator of General Social Loanable Capital"], "text": "Chapter 29 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 9,795 words ⏱️ ~49 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 29, \"Banking Capital's Component Parts,\" occupies a pivot-point in the architecture of Capital Volume III. It belongs to Part V, \"The Division of Profit into Interest and Profit of Enterprise,\" and it opens after Marx has just dismantled a specific polemical manoeuvre: the transformation, by Fullarton and others associated with the Banking School, of the analytic distinction between money as means of circulation and money as means of payment (and, where a gold drain is at stake, money as world money) into a quite different distinction between circulation (\"currency\") and capital . Marx's opening move is to expose the symmetry of two opposed errors. Where \"enlightened economics\" laboured to insist that money is not capital, the \"banker's economics\" labours just as assiduously to insist that money is capital par excellence . The chapter's central problem is therefore not merely taxonomic but ideological: given that the credit system constitutes a vast apparatus of claims, titles, and book entries, how does this apparatus generate the systematic illusion that capital is a self-valorizing, self-multiplying thing — and what, precisely, is the material anatomy of the institution (banking capital) through which that illusion is produced and sustained? The chapter's core thesis can be stated in three layers. First, banking capital has a determinate and surprisingly simple component structure : (1) cash, in the form of gold or notes; and (2) securities, which subdivide into commercial paper (bills of exchange falling due on specified dates, whose discounting is the banker's specific business) and public securities (government bonds, treasury bills, stocks — \"interest-bearing paper,\" essentially different from bills of exchange), with mortgages also admissible. This structure, Marx emphasises, is invariant with respect to ownership : it makes no difference whether these components represent the banker's own capital or the deposits of others. The subdivisions remain identical whether he trades on his own capital or entirely on borrowed capital. Second, the form of interest-bearing capital is generative of illusion as such . Any regular periodic revenue — from a national debt, from a share, from a piece of land, or even from a wage — can be capitalized : reckoned, at the average rate of interest, as the sum that a capital lent out at that rate would yield. This operation, which Marx names the formation of fictitious capital , severs every link between the value of the claim and the actual process of capital's valorization, \"right down to the last trace,\" thereby \"confirming the notion that capital is automatically valorized by its own powers.\" Marx drives this to its most scandalous limit with the wage: if a year's labour-power commands £50 and interest is 5 per cent, labour-power is declared to be a capital of £1,000 yielding that £50 — an absurdity in which the exploitation of labour-power is replaced by the claim that labour-power is mystical, interest-bearing capital. Third, and crucially, Marx does not stop at the logical critique of illusion. He descends into the institutional mechanics of the credit system: the double role of deposits (lent out as interest-bearing capital yet simultaneously functioning as mere book entries cancelled by cheques), the clearing-house, the duplication and triplication of the same capital (with Engels's appended footnote on financial trusts and preferred/deferred shares), Adam Smith's account of money as a \"deed of assignment,\" the infinite multiplication of the same £1,000 in deposits, and finally the regress of all reserve funds to the reserve fund of the Bank of England — itself of double existence, resting on the legal note-issue formula of the 1844 Bank Act, and liable to collapse (as in 1847 and 1857) while the Issue Department still holds millions. The chapter closes on the bill-brokers of London, carrying on enormous transactions with no cash reserve, and on the catastrophic ratios of their liabilities to their capital. Its intellectual objective, then, is double-edged: to give a positive anatomy of banking capital, and to demonstrate that the \"greater part of banker's capital is purely fictitious.\" It fits into the broader theoretical framework as the concrete institutional complement to the abstract theory of interest-bearing capital: the chapter in which fictitious capital, credit, and crisis are shown to be structurally inseparable, and in which the banker's balance sheet is revealed as a system of mutually referring claims rather than a stock of substance. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Polemical Premise: How the Bankers' Economics Inverts the Money/Capital Distinction Marx opens not with a definition but with a boundary-marking against the immediately preceding analysis. He has \"just seen\" how Fullarton and others convert a specific and analytically necessary distinction — money as means of circulation versus money as means of payment , the latter extended to world money insofar as a gold drain is involved — into a different distinction, between circulation (\"currency\") and capital . This is the crux of the currency/banking school controversy, and Marx's point is that the substitution is not innocent. The distinction between means of circulation and means of payment pertains to the functions money performs within the circuit of capital; the distinction between currency and capital pertains to what is being hoarded, lent, or drained . By collapsing the first into the second, the Banking School writers could argue that a gold drain affects only \"currency,\" not \"capital,\" and thereby deflect the analysis away from the credit system's inherent instability. Marx then identifies the reciprocal error. The special role that capital plays in this controversy means that \"enlightened economics\" — which insisted that money is not capital — is mirrored by the bankers' own apologetics, which tries \"just as assiduously to insist that money is actually capital par excellence .\" Two mirror-image dogmas, both generated by the same polemical need. And he announces a forward-looking clarification that structures the remainder of the chapter: money capital in the sense of a transitional form of capital (alongside commodity capital and productive capital ) is to be carefully distinguished from \"moneyed capital\" in the sense of interest-bearing capital . The former is a phase in the circuit of industrial capital; the latter is a separate social relation, the claim on surplus-value as a revenue. That the two get confused is precisely what makes the chapter necessary. The confusion is not a mere terminological slip — it is the conceptual vehicle by which the credit system appears to be a system of wealth-creation rather than a system of claims. 2.2 The Inventory of Banking Capital: Cash, Securities, and the Invariance of the Subdivisions Marx now supplies the anatomy. Banking capital consists of (1) cash, in the form of gold or notes; (2) securities. The securities divide again: Commercial paper — current bills of exchange that fall due on specified dates. Their discounting is \"the specific business of the banker.\" A bill of exchange is a promise to pay issued by an industrial capitalist or merchant; discounting means the banker buys it before maturity, deducting interest for the period the bill has still to run, so that the deduction from its face value depends on the prevailing rate of interest. The bill is therefore, for the money-lender, interest-bearing paper — not because it bears a coupon, but because its purchase price is a present value discounted at the current rate. Public securities — government bonds, treasury bills, stocks of all kinds: \"interest-bearing paper\" in a stricter sense, which Marx insists is essentially different from bills of exchange . Bills of exchange rest on an actual commercial transaction and mature in weeks or months; government stock rests on taxation and has no self-extinguishing term. This distinction matters enormously later, when Marx shows that the movement of the two classes of paper obeys different logics. Mortgages are admitted into this category as well. Within this anatomy a further division is drawn: the banker's own invested capital versus the deposits that form his banking or borrowed capital ; and, for banks with the right of issue, notes must be added. Marx explicitly brackets deposits and notes \"to start with\" — a deferral that the second half of the chapter will redeem. The decisive claim in this section, however, is the invariance claim: \"the actual components of banker's capital — money, bills of exchange and interest-bearing paper — are not affected by whether these various elements represent his own capital, or deposits, the capital of other people.\" This is a quietly radical proposition. It means that a bank's balance sheet does not disclose whose wealth it describes. The banker's own capital and the public's capital are chemically indistinguishable once they are inside the vault or on the ledger. The very identity of the constituent elements is ownership-blind — and it is precisely this ownership-blindness that makes the later phenomena of duplication, triplication, and fictitious capital possible. If the components were property-specific, the same £1,000 could not perform three functions at once; because they are not, it can. 2.3 The General Mechanism of Illusion: How Any Revenue Becomes Interest, and Interest Becomes Capital Marx now states the general mechanism in a paragraph that functions as the chapter's logical engine. Under the form of interest-bearing capital , \"any definite and regular monetary revenue appear[s] as the interest on a capital, whether it actually derives from a capital or not.\" The sequence runs in a specific order: first the money income is transformed into interest ; then, \"with the interest we then have the capital from which it derives.\" The relationship is inverted — the consequence generates its own supposed cause. And a parallel inversion applies to any sum of value: as soon as it is not spent as revenue, it appears as a \"principal\" in contrast to the possible or actual interest it can bear. This is a generalisation of what Marx elsewhere calls the fetishism of interest-bearing capital: the social relation (the exploitation of labour, the appropriation of surplus-value) is occluded by a thing-like relation (money begetting money). The parenthetical phrase later in the chapter — \"interest-bearing capital always being the mother of every insane form\" — is the compact version of this diagnosis. 2.4 Case One — The National Debt: Capital That Was Never Capital Marx now demonstrates the mechanism with a worked example. \"Say that the average rate of interest is 5 per cent per year. A sum of £500, then, if transformed into interest-bearing capital, would bring in a yearly £25. Hence every fixed annual income of £25 is seen as the interest on a capital of £500.\" This is the arithmetic of illusion, and its algebraic form is: C = R / i where C is the fictitious capital value, R the periodic revenue, and i the rate of interest. Here £25 / 0.05 = £500. But Marx immediately qualifies: this is \"a purely illusory notion, except in the case where the source of the £25 — whether a mere title of ownership or claim, or an actual element of production such as a piece of land — is directly transferable, or assumes a form in which it is transferable.\" The qualifier is important: the illusion is not all-encompassing, because where a genuine transferable asset underlies the income (land, for instance), the capitalization corresponds to something real. The illusion proper arises where the underlying is either nonexistent or not the source of the income at all. The national debt is the first case. The state pays its creditors interest on borrowed capital. The creditor cannot recall the capital ; he can only sell the claim , his title of ownership. \"The capital itself has been consumed, spent by the state. It no longer exists.\" What the state's creditor actually possesses is a three-part complex: the state's promissory note for, say, £100; a claim on the state's annual revenue — the proceeds of the year's taxation — to a certain amount, say £5 or 5 per cent; the freedom to sell this promissory note to anyone he likes. Marx then shows that the sale is not a return of capital but a hand-over of the claim. If the interest rate is 5 per cent and the state's credit good, owner A can generally sell the note for £100 to B, \"since it is the same thing for B whether he lends out £100 at 5 per cent per year or assures himself of an annual tribute of £5 from the state by paying out £100.\" But in all these cases \"the capital from which the state's payment is taken as deriving, as interest, is illusory and fictitious .\" Two distinct grounds are given for the fiction: (a) the money lent has been consumed and no longer exists; and (b) more fundamentally, \"It was never designed to be spent as capital, to be invested, and yet only by being invested as capital could it have been made into a self-maintaining value.\" The state's borrowing is consumption expenditure, not capital investment, so the very presupposition of the interest form — a self-maintaining, valorizing principal — is absent. For the original creditor A, the tax share is interest on his \"capital\" in the same sense that the usurer's cut from a spendthrift is interest; in neither case was the money laid out as capital. The possibility of selling the note \"represents for A the potential return of his principal.\" For B, \"from his own private standpoint his capital is then invested as interest-bearing capital. In actual fact, he has simply taken the place of A, and bought A's claim on the state.\" And then the crucial nuance, which rescues the analysis from being a mere debunking: \"No matter how these transactions are multiplied, the capital of the national debt remains purely fictitious, and the moment these promissory notes become unsaleable, the illusion of this capital disappears. Yet this fictitious capital has its characteristic movement for all that, as we shall see soon.\" This is the pivot of the entire chapter. Fictitious capital is not nothing; it behaves . It has prices, fluctuations, a market, a crisis-dynamic — even though its substrate is a fiction. The remainder of the chapter (and of the surrounding chapters) is precisely the study of that characteristic movement. 2.5 Case Two — Wages as Interest: The Apotheosis of the Absurdity, and the Slave Contrast From the national debt, \"where a negative quantity appears as capital\" — Marx's aside here, \"interest-bearing capital always being the mother of every insane form, so that debts, for example, can appear as commodities in the mind of the banker,\" is one of the chapter's sharpest formulations — he moves to labour-power . Here wages are conceived as interest, and hence labour-power as capital yielding that interest . The arithmetic: \"If the wage for a year comes to £50, say, and the rate of interest is 5 per cent, one annual labour-power is taken as equal to a capital of £1,000.\" Again C = R / i = 50 / 0.05 = 1,000. Marx calls this \"the absurdity of the capitalist's way of conceiving things … at its climax,\" because it inverts the causal order of the entire theory of surplus-value : \"instead of deriving the valorization of capital from the exploitation of labour-power, they explain the productivity of labour-power by declaring that labour-power itself is this mystical thing, interest-bearing capital.\" The labourer's exploitation, which is the real ground of profit, is transmuted into a property of the labourer — an internal fund of capital that yields interest. Marx traces the notion historically: \"In the second half of the seventeenth century (with Petty, for example) this was a favourite notion, but it is still used today, in all seriousness, by vulgar economists and especially by German statisticians.\" The footnote documents the vulgar usage with a quotation from Von Reden's Vergleichende Kulturstatistik (Berlin, 1848, p. 434): \"The worker has a capital value which is found by considering the monetary value of his annual service as a payment of interest … If the average daily wage is capitalized at a rate of 4 per cent, we make the average value for an agricultural worker of the male sex to be 1,500 thalers in German Austria, 1,500 thalers in Prussia, 3,750 thalers in England, 2,000 thalers in France, and 750 thalers in Russia proper.\" The footnote is a specimen of the method in its statistical application — the same capitalization operation that produced £1,000 from a £50 wage is here used to build an international table of the \"capital value\" of human beings, with Russia's lower figure reflecting lower wages. Two \"inconvenient circumstances\" refute the notion: The worker has to work in order to receive this interest. The \"interest\" is not a return on an asset he owns; it is the price of his activity, forthcoming only under the discipline of employment. He cannot turn the capital value of his labour-power into money by transferring it to someone else. Unlike the state's bondholder, unlike the shareholder, unlike the landowner, the worker has no transferable title. \"Rather, the annual value of his labour-power is his average annual wage, and his labour has to replace its buyer with this value itself plus the surplus-value that is its valorization.\" The contrast with slavery is exact and analytically pointed. Under the slave system the worker does have a capital value, namely his purchase price. And if he is hired out, the hirer must first pay the interest on this purchase price and on top of this replace the capital's annual depreciation. The slave owner, unlike the capitalist employer of free labour, really does own a capital asset in the person of the worker — which is why the \"wages as interest\" analogy, nonsensical for the free worker, has a genuine application to the slave. The comparison thus performs double duty: it exposes the free-labour notion as a false analogy, and it clarifies what a real capital value in a human being would require (transferability and a purchase price). 2.6 The Formal Definition of Capitalization and the Loss of the Valorization Process Marx now names the operation formally: \"The formation of fictitious capital is known as capitalization. Any regular periodic income can be capitalized by reckoning it up, on the basis of the average rate of interest, as the sum that a capital lent out at this interest rate would yield.\" His canonical example: \"if the annual income in question is £100 and the rate of interest 5 per cent, then £100 is the annual interest on £2,000, and this £2,000 is then taken as the capital value of the legal ownership title to this annual £100.\" So C = 100 / 0.05 = 2,000. For the purchaser of the ownership title, the annual £100 \"does actually represent the conversion of the capital he has invested into interest.\" This is the subjective truth of the matter: for him, functionally, it is interest on his investment. But the objective result is that \"all connection with the actual process of capital's valorization is lost, right down to the last trace, confirming the notion that capital is automatically valorized by its own powers.\" The phrase \"right down to the last trace\" is the key diagnostic: the illusion is not a partial misperception but a complete severance. Capital, which in Marx's theory is a social relation of exploitation expressed in a circuit (M–C…P…C'–M'), becomes a self-sufficient thing that yields revenue by nature. This is the completed fetishism of interest-bearing capital, the theoretical counterpart of the rentier's daily experience. 2.7 Joint-Stock Shares: Real Capital versus Its Ownership Title (The Non-Duplication Thesis) Marx now extends the analysis to the case where the underlying is not a pure fiction — joint-stock capital, which \"we have already seen\" the credit system produces. Securities \"purport to be ownership titles representing this capital.\" Shares in railway, mining, shipping companies, etc., represent real capital , i.e. capital invested and functioning in these enterprises, or the sum of money advanced by shareholders to be spent there as capital. Two qualifications follow immediately: Fraud is not ruled out. \"It is in no way ruled out here that these shares may be simply a fraud.\" But the capital does not exist twice over — \"once as the capital value of the ownership titles, the shares, and then again as the capital actually invested or to be invested in the enterprises in question. It exists only in the latter form, and the share is nothing but an ownership title, pro rata , to the surplus-value which this capital is to realize.\" This is the chapter's decisive anti-duplication thesis, and it is a direct assault on the appearance that the stock exchange creates wealth. The share is a claim , not a second capital. When A sells the title to B and B to C, \"these transactions have no essential effect on the matter. A or B has then transformed his title into capital, but C has transformed his capital into a mere ownership title to the surplus-value expected from this share capital.\" The same real capital remains in the enterprise; what circulates is only the title, and the only person left holding a title rather than money is the last seller. The individual can indeed convert a claim into cash — but only by another's purchase, which is a transfer, not a creation. 2.8 The Autonomous Movement of Security Prices: The Formula of Inverse Variation with the Interest Rate Marx now addresses the phenomenon that most strongly sustains the illusion: \"The independent movement of these ownership titles' values, not only those of government bonds, but also of shares, strengthens the illusion that they constitute real capital besides the capital or claim to which they may give title.\" The titles become commodities with their own prices, their own market values, and a determination \"differing from their nominal values, without any change in the value of the actual capital (even if its valorization does change).\" Two determinants of market value are distinguished: (a) The level and security of the receipts to which the title legally entitles the holder. Marx's example: a share with nominal value £100 (the sum originally advanced), in an enterprise yielding 10 per cent instead of 5 per cent, rises in market value — \"other circumstances being equal\" — because, capitalized at 5 per cent, it now represents a fictitious capital of £200 : C = 10 / 0.05 = 200. Someone who buys it for £200 obtains a revenue of 5 per cent on his capital outlay. The opposite holds when the enterprise's revenue declines. Note that the share's nominal value stays £100; what changes is the capitalization of its actual yield at the market rate. (b) Speculation. \"The market value of these securities is partly speculative , since it is determined not just by the actual revenue but rather by the anticipated revenue as reckoned in advance.\" This is a distinct and important caveat: the price is not a function of the present alone, but of expectations. Marx then isolates the pure relationship by holding actual valorization constant — or, where no such capital exists (national debts), by taking the annual yield as fixed by law and sufficiently guaranteed. In that case: \"the prices of these securities rise and fall in inverse proportion to the rate of interest\" : If the rate of interest rises from 5 per cent to 10 per cent , a security yielding £5 now represents a capital of only £50 (5 / 0.10). If the rate of interest falls to 2½ per cent , the same security represents a capital of £200 (5 / 0.025). \" Its value is always simply the capitalized yield, i.e. the yield as reckoned on an illusory capital at the existing rate of interest. \" This gives the chapter one of its cleanest analytical results: the price of a fixed-income security is inversely related to the rate of interest , and any movement in the bank rate is immediately a movement in the \"value\" of the nation's paper wealth — with no change whatsoever in the real capital or the real income stream. From this Marx derives the crisis-dynamics: \"In times of pressure on the money market, therefore, these securities fall in price for two reasons: first, because the interest rate rises, and second, because they are put up for sale in massive quantities, to be converted into money.\" The two causes are analytically distinct but empirically simultaneous: one is a re-capitalization at a higher discount rate, the other a supply shock from forced liquidation. The fall occurs irrespective of whether the yield is constant (government bonds) or whether the real capital's valorization is itself threatened by the disturbance in the reproduction process (industrial undertakings) — \"In the latter case, we simply have a further devaluation besides that already mentioned,\" i.e. a genuine devaluation of the underlying added on top of the pure capitalization effect. The distinction is exactly the one Marx needs: some depreciation is real, some is purely fictional — and only the latter is recovered when the storm passes. 2.9 Crisis Depreciation as a Mechanism of Centralization, and the Measure of National Wealth \"Once the storm is over, these securities rise again to their former level, in so far as the undertakings they represent have not come to grief and are not fraudulent. Their depreciation in a crisis is a powerful means of centralizing money wealth.\" Two propositions are packed here. First, the resilience of fictitious value: if the underlying survives, the paper recovers. Second, and more importantly for Marx's political economy, the redistributive function of the crisis: those with cash (or with credit) buy depreciated titles; those forced to sell surrender claims cheaply. The crisis is thus an apparatus of concentration — which is why the banker with ready money, as the Rothschild anecdote in footnote 2 illustrates, is uniquely positioned. Engels's footnote reports that immediately after the February revolution of 1848, when commodities and securities in Paris were extremely devalued and totally unsaleable, a Swiss merchant in Liverpool, Mr E. Zwilchenbart, sold whatever he could for cash, travelled to Paris, and proposed a joint deal to Rothschild. Rothschild stared at him, seized him by both shoulders and asked: \"Avez-vous de l'argent sur vous?\" — \"Oui, M. le baron.\" — \"Alors vous êtes un homme!\" (\"Then you're the man for me!\") \"And they both made a handsome profit.\" The anecdote is not decorative: it is a concrete demonstration that in a crisis, liquidity — the one thing the fictitious system cannot manufacture — is the ticket to acquiring the claims of others. Marx then makes the corresponding aggregate claim: \"In so far as the rise or fall in value of these securities is independent of the movement in the value of the real capital that they represent, the wealth of a nation is just as great afterwards as before.\" He substantiates this with testimony from the Governor of the Bank of England: \"The public stocks and canal and railway shares had already by the 23rd of October, 1847, been depreciated in the aggregate to the amount of £114,752,225\" (Morris, evidence in the Report on Commercial Distress , 1847–8). Marx's comment is precise about the condition under which this loss is not a real national loss: \"As long as their depreciation was not the expression of any standstill in production and in railway and canal traffic, or an abandonment of undertakings already begun, or a squandering of capital in positively worthless enterprises, the nation was not a penny poorer by the bursting of these soap bubbles of nominal money capital.\" The four listed conditions are precisely the cases in which the depreciation would index a real loss — a halt in production, abandonment of works, squandering in worthless ventures. Where none of these holds, the £114 million is a pure bookkeeping evaporation. The section's summary statement is the theoretical core: \"All these securities actually represent nothing but accumulated claims, legal titles, to future production. Their money or capital value either does not represent capital at all, as in the case of national debts, or is determined independently of the real capital value they represent.\" 2.10 The Accumulation of \"Moneyed Capital\" as the Accumulation of Claims Marx draws the consequence for accumulation theory. \"In all countries of capitalist production, there is a tremendous amount of so-called interest-bearing capital or 'moneyed capital' in this form. And an accumulation of money capital means for the most part nothing more than an accumulation of these claims to production, and an accumulation of the market price of these claims, of their illusory capital value.\" This is a direct strike at the appearance that the growth of financial assets measures the growth of wealth. Accumulation of moneyed capital, \"for the most part,\" is the accumulation of claims and of the prices at which those claims trade — a phenomenon which can inflate indefinitely while the productive base stagnates, and which can collapse without the productive base being touched. 2.11 The Internal Composition of the Banker's Capital: Discount, Deposits, and the Flux of the Reserve Marx now returns to the anatomy of §2.2 and fills in the deferred detail, running through the components once more with their functions: One portion of banker's capital is invested in these so-called interest-bearing securities. Importantly, \"This is actually part of the reserve capital and does not function in the banking business proper.\" Securities held by a bank are typically not its trading stock but its secondary reserve. The most important portion consists of bills of exchange — promises to pay issued by industrial capitalists or merchants. For the money-lender these bills are interest-bearing paper; when he buys them he deducts interest for the period they still have to run, and the deduction from the face value of the bill thus depends on the rate of interest at the time. Discounting is therefore a direct transmission mechanism from the money market to the whole commercial system. The final portion consists of the money reserve in gold or notes. Deposits, \"unless tied up for a longer period by contract, are always at the depositors' disposal. They are in a state of perpetual flux. But if some depositors withdraw their deposits, others replace them, so that the overall average fluctuates only a little in times of normal business.\" This is the statistical premise of fractional reserve banking, stated as a fact of the deposit flux rather than as a policy. Marx then generalises: \"The banks' reserve funds, in countries of developed capitalist production, always express the average amount of money existing as a hoard, and a part of this hoard itself consists of paper, mere drafts on gold, which have no value of their own.\" The hoard is the money that has been withdrawn from circulation and lies idle; the banking system, as a whole, is the social organisation of that hoard — but even the hoard is partly paper. He then states the chapter's summary verdict in its most comprehensive form: \"The greater part of banker's capital is therefore purely fictitious and consists of claims (bills of exchange) and shares (drafts on future revenues).\" The qualification that follows is dense and worth unpacking clause by clause. It \"should not be forgotten\" that this capital's money value, as represented by the paper in the banker's safe, is completely fictitious even where the paper consists of drafts on certain assured revenues (government securities) or ownership titles to real capital (shares) — \"their money value being determined differently from the value of the actual capital that they at least partially represent\"; or, where they represent only a claim to revenue and not capital at all , \"the claim to the same revenue is expressed in a constantly changing fictitious money capital.\" So there are two distinct species of fictitiousness: (i) titles whose asset-base exists but whose price is determined independently of it; and (ii) titles with no asset-base at all, whose very \"money capital\" is a function of the interest rate and nothing else. To this is added a third consideration: this fictitious capital of the banker represents to a large extent not his own capital but rather that of the public who deposit with him, whether with interest or without. The ownership-blindness established in §2.2 now pays off: the bank's fictitious capital is largely other people's fictitious capital, and the bank's solvency is consequently a claim about a claim. 2.12 The Double Role of Deposits and the Clearing Mechanism Marx now analyses deposits with an exactness that repays close reading. \"Deposits are always made in money, gold or notes, or else in drafts on money.\" Except for the reserve fund, which contracts or expands according to the needs of actual circulation, these deposits are in fact always either in the hands of industrial capitalists and merchants , serving to discount their bills of exchange and make them advances; or in the hands of dealers in securities (stockbrokers), private persons who have sold securities, or the government (in the case of treasury bills and new loans). So the deposited money, having been lent out, is held by specific classes of borrowers performing specific functions. This is the concrete counterpart of the abstract statement that money capital is a \"transitional form.\" \"The deposits themselves play a double role.\" On the one hand, they are lent out as interest-bearing capital and are thus \"not to be found in the banks' safes, figuring instead in their books as credits held by the depositors.\" On the other hand, they function as mere book entries in so far as the reciprocal credits of depositors are settled by cheques on their deposits and mutually cancelled. And here Marx adds a point that dismantles any residual notion of deposits as located wealth: \"it is completely immaterial in this connection whether the deposits are with the same banker, so that they can cancel the various accounts against one another, or whether they are with different banks, which exchange their cheques on one another and simply pay the balances.\" The clearing-house — whether intra-bank bookkeeping or inter-bank balance settlement — performs the same function: extinguishing obligations without any movement of cash. The deposit is thus simultaneously a loan (asset side) and a debt record (liability side), and it discharges obligations by pure cancellation. Its \"existence\" consists in a set of mutually conditioned entries. 2.13 The Duplication and Triplication of Capital Marx now raises his central rhetorical formulation of the chapter's second half: \"With the development of interest-bearing capital and the credit system, all capital seems to be duplicated, and at some points triplicated, by the various ways in which the same capital, or even the same claim, appears in various hands in different guises.\" The verb is \"seems\" — the appearance is systematic but false. \"The greater part of this 'money capital' is purely fictitious. With the exception of the reserve fund, deposits are never more than credits with the banker, and never exist as real deposits.\" Once lent out, they function as capital for the bankers; the bankers pay one another \"reciprocal drafts on these non-existent deposits by balancing these credits against each other.\" The mechanism of settlement does not require the deposits to exist as anything other than an entry. Engels's footnote 3 extends the analysis chronologically and institutionally: the duplication and triplication \"has been taken very much further in recent years, e.g. through the financial trusts ,\" which by Engels's time \"already require a column of their own in the London stock-market reports.\" The mechanism described: a company is formed to purchase a class of interest-bearing paper (foreign government securities, English municipal loans, American public bonds, railway stocks, etc.); it raises, say, £2 million by share issue; the directors buy or speculate in the values; and they distribute the annual interest received among the shareholders as dividends after deducting expenses. Further, in certain joint-stock companies the practice has developed of dividing ordinary shares into two classes, \"preferred\" and \"deferred\" : preferred shares receive a fixed interest payment, say 5 per cent, provided overall profit permits; any surplus goes to the holders of deferred shares. In this way \"the more 'solid' capital investment in preferred shares is more or less separated from actual speculation, in deferred.\" And then the terminal development: \"since certain large undertakings do not want to avail themselves of this new method, companies have come to be formed which invest a million pounds, say, or even a few million, in the shares of these firms, subsequently issuing new shares for the nominal value of the shares bought, but with one half preferred and the other deferred. In these cases the original shares are duplicated, by serving as the basis for a new share issue. \" This is fictitious capital raised to a second power: the same underlying shares now support two layers of shareholder claims, with the risk concentrated in the deferred tranche. Engels's note demonstrates that Marx's category is not a historical curiosity of the 1840s but a structurally expanding feature of finance capital. 2.14 Adam Smith on Money as \"Deed of Assignment\": Marx's Correction Marx now enlists Adam Smith, quoting at length from Book II, Chapter IV (Pelican edition, p. 452). Smith's opening proposition: \"Even in the monied interest, however, the money is, as it were, but the deed of assignment, which conveys from one hand to another those capitals which the owners do not care to employ themselves.\" These capitals \"may be greater in almost any proportion than the amount of the money which serves as the instrument of their conveyance; the same pieces of money successively serving for many different loans, as well as for many different purchases.\" Smith's illustration: A lends £1,000 to W, who immediately buys £1,000 of goods from B; B, having no occasion for the money, lends the identical pieces to X, who buys £1,000 of goods from C; C lends them to Y, who buys from D. \"In this manner the same pieces, either of coin or of paper, may, in the course of a few days, serve as the instrument of three different loans, and of three different purchases, each of which is, in value, equal to the whole amount of those pieces.\" Smith's conclusion: \"What the three men, A, B and C, assign to the three borrowers, W, X and Y, is the power of making those purchases. In this power consist both the value and the use of the loans.\" The stock lent is equal to the value of the goods purchasable with it and is \"three times greater than that of the money with which the purchases are made.\" The loans may be perfectly well secured because the goods purchased by the different debtors will in due time bring back, with a profit, an equal value of coin or paper. \"And as the same pieces of money can thus serve as the instrument of different loans to three, or for the same reason, to thirty times their value, so they may likewise successively serve as the instrument of repayment.\" Marx then corrects and deepens. He notes first the symmetry between circulation and lending: \"Since the same piece of money can make several different purchases, depending on the velocity of its circulation, it can equally be used for various different loans, for purchases move it from one hand to another, and a loan is simply a transfer from one hand to another that is not mediated by any sale.\" There is, however, a decisive difference between the circulation-function and the loan-function: In circulation, each seller receives money as the transformed form of his commodity. \"Nowadays, when every commodity is expressed as a capital value, the money in its various loans successively represents different capitals, which is simply a different way of putting the previous statement that it can successively realize different commodity values.\" In a loan, money is not transferred as a means of circulation. \"As long as it remains in the lender's possession, it is not a means of circulation in his hands but the value-existence of his capital . And it is in this form that he transfers it to someone else in a loan.\" And then the limit-case that disciplines the whole analysis: \"If A had lent money to B, and B to C, without any purchases intervening, the same money would not represent three capitals but only one, just one capital value. How many capitals it actually does represent depends on how often it functions as the value form of various different commodity capitals.\" This is the crucial corrective. The multiplication of capitals in Smith's example is not a property of the money as such; it depends on the money serving, at each step, as the value-form of a different commodity capital — that is, on real commodities being bought and sold, i.e. on the reproduction process. Loan capital cannot multiply independently of production; where it appears to (pure chains of lending), the money represents one capital, not three. The chapter thus preserves the link to real accumulation even while exposing the fictional character of the paper. 2.15 Deposits as the Public's Loans to Bankers: The Infinite Multiplication of the Same £1,000 Marx now applies Smith's analysis to deposits: \"What Adam Smith says about loans in general applies equally to deposits, this being simply a particular name for loans that the public make to the bankers. The same pieces of money can serve as instrument for any number of deposits.\" He then quotes The Currency Theory Reviewed (pp. 62–3): \"It is unquestionably true that the £1,000 which you deposit at A today may be reissued tomorrow, and form a deposit at B. The day after that, reissued from B, it may form a deposit at C … and so on to infinitude; and that the same £1,000 in money may, thus, by a succession of transfers, multiply itself into a sum of deposits absolutely indefinite.\" Hence \"It is possible, therefore, that nine-tenths of all the deposits in the United Kingdom may have no existence beyond their record in the books of the bankers who are respectively accountable for them.\" The empirical illustration: \"Thus in Scotland, for instance, currency has never exceeded £3 million, the deposits in the banks are estimated at £27 million\" — a ratio of one to nine, exactly matching the \"nine-tenths\" estimate. The passage continues: unless a run is made on the banks, the same £1,000 \"would, if sent back upon its travels, cancel with the same facility a sum equally indefinite. As the same £1,000, with which you cancel your debt to a tradesman today, may cancel his debt to the merchant tomorrow, the merchant's debt to the bank the day following, and so on without end; so the same £1,000 may pass from hand to hand, and bank to bank, and cancel any conceivable sum of deposits.\" The passage performs a precise theoretical role. It shows that the multiplication of deposits is a circulation-phenomenon of a single money sum — a function of velocity, not of quantity of value. Nine-tenths of the system's apparent wealth is the same money appearing in different books. And yet the system functions so long as the round of cancellations is not interrupted by a demand for cash — which is precisely what a run is. 2.16 The Reserve Fund and the Regress to the Bank of England Having shown that securities, bills, and deposits are all, in their way, phantom-like, Marx turns to the \"reserve fund,\" \"where one might finally expect to lay hold of something solid.\" His method is to let the practitioners speak. Morris, Governor of the Bank of England: \"The reserves of the private bankers are in the hands of the Bank of England in the shape of deposits … An export of gold acts exclusively, in the first instance, upon the reserve of the Bank of England; but it would also be acting upon the reserves of the bankers, in as much as it is a withdrawal of a portion of the reserves which they have in the Bank of England. It would be acting upon the reserves of all the bankers throughout the country\" ( Commercial Distress , 1847–8, Nos. 3639, 3642). Marx's inference: \"Ultimately, therefore, what these reserve funds actually boil down to is the reserve fund of the Bank of England.\" The hierarchy of reserves is really a single apex. And this apex, too, \"has a double existence.\" Engels's footnote 4 documents the empirical magnitude of this concentration with an official tabulation of the reserves of the fifteen largest London banks for November 1892, from the Daily News of 15 December. The table gives, for each bank, its liabilities, cash reserves, and the percentage: Bank Liabilities Cash reserves Percentage City £9,317,629 £746,551 8.01 Capital and Counties £11,392,744 £1,307,483 11.47 Imperial £3,987,400 £447,157 11.22 Lloyds £23,800,937 £2,966,806 12.46 London and Westminster £24,671,559 £3,818,885 15.50 London and S. Western £5,570,268 £812,353 14.58 London Joint Stock £12,127,993 £1,288,977 10.62 London and Midland £8,814,499 £1,127,280 12.79 London and County £37,111,035 £3,600,374 9.70 National £11,163,829 £1,426,225 12.77 National Provincial £41,907,384 £4,614,780 11.01 Parrs and the Alliance £12,794,489 £1,532,707 11.98 Prescott and Co. £4,041,058 £538,517 13.07 Union of London £15,502,618 £2,300,084 14.84 Williams, Deacon and Manchester and Co. £10,452,381 £1,317,628 12.60 Total £232,655,823 £27,845,807 11.97 Engels's gloss: \"Of these reserves of almost £28 million, £25 million at the very least is deposited with the Bank of England , leaving at most £3 million in the safes of the fifteen banks themselves. But the bullion reserve of the Bank of England's Banking Department, in the same month of November 1892, was never as much as £16 million! \" The arithmetic is devastating: the entire system's £27.8 million of visible \"cash reserves\" is itself mostly a claim on the Bank of England, whose own bullion base is smaller still — £16 million against £28 million of claims — and whose reserve is itself a claim structured by law. The \"solid\" thing at the bottom of the pile has evaporated into a legal formula. 2.17 The Arithmetic of the Bank of England's Reserve and the 1847/1857 Crises Marx now specifies that formula. The reserve fund of the Banking Department is equal to the excess of notes that the Bank is authorized to issue over the notes actually in circulation. The legal maximum note issue is £14 million (the amount for which no metal reserve is required, this being \"the approximate sum of the government's debt to the Bank\"), plus the Bank's total reserve of precious metal . So: Issue capacity = £14m (fiduciary, non-metal-backed) + metal reserve If the metal reserve is also £14 million, the Bank can issue £28 million in notes. If £20 million of these are already in circulation, the reserve fund of the Banking Department is £8 million. \"This £8 million in notes is then the legal banking capital that the Bank has at its disposal and at the same time the reserve fund for its deposits.\" Now the crisis mechanism: \"Should a drain of gold take place, reducing the metal reserve by £6 million — for which an equal sum in notes has to be destroyed — the Banking Department's reserve falls from £8 million to £2 million.\" Note the double contraction: the gold loss destroys an equal quantity of notes (the note issue must be covered beyond the fiduciary £14m), so the reserve falls by the full £6 million. \"On the one hand, the Bank increases its interest rate very sharply; on the other hand, the banks which have deposited with it, as well as its other depositors, see the reserve fund for their own credits with the Bank take a sharp drop.\" Two simultaneous shocks: a spike in the price of money, and a fall in the visible backing of everyone's claims. Marx then gives the historical proof of the system's fragility. \"In 1857, London's four largest joint-stock banks threatened that if the Bank of England did not obtain a 'government letter' suspending the Bank Act of 1844, they would call in their deposits and bankrupt the Banking Department.\" The threat was credible, and this is the meaning of Marx's next sentence: \"Thus the Banking Department can fail to meet obligations, as in 1847, while there are still several millions in the Issue Department (e.g. £8 million in 1847), as guarantee for the convertibility of notes in circulation.\" The Bank's two departments are legally separated by the 1844 Act, and the separation is what allows the institution to be simultaneously solvent in one sense and insolvent in another. And Marx adds, cuttingly: \"Though this is in turn an illusion.\" Even the Issue Department's £8 million guarantee — the last solid thing — is itself an illusion. Engels's footnote 5 spells out what suspension of the 1844 Bank Act means: \"The suspension of the 1844 Bank Act enables the Bank to issue an unlimited sum of banknotes, irrespective of the extent to which these are covered by its gold reserve; i.e. to create an unlimited amount of fictitious, paper money capital and use this to make advances to the banks and billbrokers, and through them to the world of commerce.\" The footnote is a definitional contribution: suspension of the Act does not create metal, it creates fictitious paper money capital — the legal sanction of what the chapter has been describing all along. 2.18 The Bill-Brokers: The Most Fragile Link The chapter closes with the institution in which all these tensions are concentrated. Marx quotes the Report of Committee on Bank Acts , 1857–8, p. 5, Section 8: \"That large portion (of deposits) for which the bankers themselves have no immediate demand passes into the hands of the bill-brokers, who give to the banker in return commercial bills already discounted by them for persons in London and in different parts of the country as a security for the sum advanced by the banker. The bill-broker is responsible to the banker for payment of this money at call.\" The structure is a three-tier chain of re-lending: depositors → bankers → bill-brokers → commerce. The crucial clause is \"at call\" : the bill-broker's liability to the banker is repayable on demand, while his asset is a portfolio of bills maturing at specified future dates. This is a maturity mismatch in its purest form. The scale is given by the Governor of the Bank of England, Neave: \"'We know that one broker had 5 million, and we were led to believe that another had between 8 and 10 million; there was one with 4, another with 3½, and a third with above 8. I speak of deposits with the brokers.'\" So a handful of private firms held £30 million or more of callable deposits. And the practices: \"The London bill-brokers carried on their enormous transactions without any cash reserve, relying on the run off of their bills falling due, or in extremity, on the power of obtaining advances from the Bank of England on the security of bills under discount.\" The phrase \"without any cash reserve\" is the chapter's terminal empirical point: the most leveraged nodal point of the whole credit system is also the one with no reserve at all, its viability resting on the regularity of maturities or, failing that, on the Bank of England as lender of last resort. The evidence of both fragility and resilience follows: \"Two bill-broking houses in London suspended payment in 1847; both afterwards resumed business. In 1857, both suspended again.\" And then the balance-sheet data, which show the leverage expanding across the cycle: \"The liabilities of one house in 1847 were, in round numbers, £2,683,000, with a capital of £180,000; the liabilities of the same house, in 1857, were £5,300,000, the capital probably not more than one-fourth of what it was in 1847. The liabilities of the other firm were between £3,000,000 and £4,000,000 at each period of stoppage, with a capital not exceeding £45,000.\" Pause on the ratios. The first house: liabilities £2,683,000 against capital £180,000 — approximately 15:1 — in 1847; and in 1857, liabilities £5,300,000 against a capital \"probably not more than one-fourth\" of £180,000, i.e. ~£45,000 — approximately 118:1 . The second house: liabilities of £3–4 million against capital never exceeding £45,000 — a leverage ratio ranging up to nearly 89:1 . And crucially, each firm weathered suspension twice, i.e. the failures were absorbed by the system. The chapter thus ends not on a definition but on a set of balance sheets demonstrating that the \"enormous transactions\" of the credit system were supported by capital amounting to a rounding error on their liabilities — the empirical confirmation of every claim made earlier in the chapter about fictitious capital, duplication, and the substitution of claims for substance. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Several fundamental principles emerge with unusual clarity from this chapter. First, the principle of ownership-blindness in financial form. The components of banking capital — money, bills of exchange, interest-bearing paper — are unaffected by whether they represent the banker's own capital or the public's deposits. This is the formal condition of possibility for everything else in the credit system. Because the form does not register ownership, the same value can be simultaneously an asset and a liability, present and lent, reserve and loan. Second, the principle of capitalization as the general formula of fictitious wealth. Any regular periodic revenue, whatever its source, can be converted into a capital value by division through the interest rate: C = R / i . This formula is source-agnostic, which is precisely its ideological power. It can transform a national debt into a capital, a share into a capital, a piece of land into a capital, and a wage into a capital. The chapter's treatment of the wage as interest-bearing capital shows that the formula recognises no distinction between a claim on surplus-value, a claim on taxation, and a price for labour-power. This indifference is the theoretical signature of the rentier's worldview. Third, the principle of the autonomy of fictitious capital. Marx insists that although fictitious capital is illusory, \"this fictitious capital has its characteristic movement for all that.\" Fictitious values behave: they fluctuate inversely with the rate of interest, they respond to anticipated revenue, they collapse in money-market pressure for two analytically distinct reasons (rate rise and forced liquidation), they recover after the storm if the real undertakings survive, and their collapse functions as \"a powerful means of centralizing money wealth.\" The lesson is that fictitious capital is not a mere ideological error to be dispelled; it is a real social mechanism with real distributive consequences — most brutally, the transfer of claims from the forced seller to the liquid buyer. Fourth, the principle of the strict limit of the multiplication. Against any view that credit creates capital ex nihilo, Marx's correction of Adam Smith establishes the boundary condition: if A lends to B and B to C without intervening purchases , the same money represents one capital, not three. The number of capitals that a money sum represents depends on how often it functions as the value-form of different commodity capitals , i.e. on the reproduction process. This is the anchor that keeps the analysis of fictitious capital tethered to production — and the reason the depreciation of securities is not a national loss \"as long as\" it does not express a standstill in production, an abandonment of undertakings, or squandering in worthless enterprises. Fifth, the principle of the regress of reserves to a single apex, which is itself a claim. Private bankers' reserves are deposits at the Bank of England; an export of gold acts on the Bank's reserve and thereby on all reserves at once. But the Bank's own reserve is a double existence : the Banking Department's reserve is a legal formula (fiduciary issue of £14 million matched to the government's debt to the Bank, plus the metal reserve, minus notes in circulation). The 1892 figures in Engels's footnote show the whole system's £27.8 million of \"cash\" reserves resting on £25 million deposited at the Bank, whose bullion reserve was under £16 million. There is no ultimate solid ground: at every level the \"reserve\" is a claim on a claim, and the last level is a claim defined by statute. Suspension of the Bank Act then amounts to the deliberate creation of \"an unlimited amount of fictitious, paper money capital.\" Sixth, and most sobering, the principle of structural fragility at the point of maximum leverage. The bill-brokers, holding £30 million or more of callable deposits with \"no cash reserve,\" relying on maturity roll-off or on the Bank as lender of last resort, with liabilities up to a hundred times their capital, are the emblematic institution of the credit system. They suspend in 1847, resume, suspend again in 1857 — and their leverage has meanwhile increased . The chapter's final image is not of a system that can be corrected by better policy or sounder judgement, but of a system whose normal functioning presupposes exactly the conditions that produce its periodic breakdowns. Broader implications. For theory, the chapter supplies the conceptual instrumentation for a critique of finance that avoids two errors: the error of treating financial assets as real wealth, and the error of treating them as mere illusion with no causal efficacy. Marx's position is subtler than either. Fictitious capital is fictitious and real: it is real as a claim, real as a market with prices and movements, real as a mechanism of centralisation — but it is not real as capital, because it corresponds to no second valorisation process. For practice and policy, the chapter anticipates by well over a century the intuition that a financial system's stability depends on the liquidity of its most leveraged intermediaries and on the ultimate reserve that underwrites them — and that this ultimate reserve is itself a convention. And for method, the chapter is a model of Marx's procedure: begin with the polemical displacement of categories, proceed to the specific anatomy of the institution, expose the general mechanism by which form generates illusion, work through the extreme cases (debt, wages) to make the mechanism unmistakable, and then descend through deposits, clearing, trusts, the Bank of England, and the bill-brokers until the abstract category of fictitious capital is visible in a balance sheet with liabilities of £5,300,000 and a capital of £45,000. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 28 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 30"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 30", "title": "Chapter 30", "subtitle": "Money Capital and Real Capital: I", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 30/Chapter 30 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 12533, "read_time": "63 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital", "Capital Accumulation & Crises"], "keywords": ["Money Capital and Real Capital: I", "Transformation of Inactive Money into Loanable Capital", "Overproduction Cycles and Credit Expansion", "Difference between Commodity Glut and Money Stringency", "Commercial Bills vs Real Commodity Backing"], "text": "Chapter 30 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 12,505 words ⏱️ ~63 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 30 of Capital, Volume III — \"Money Capital and Real Capital: I\" — opens one of the most conceptually difficult and historically important investigations in Marx's entire oeuvre: the relationship between the accumulation of money capital (loanable, interest-bearing capital) and the accumulation of real capital (commodity capital and productive capital). The chapter's central problematic is stated in its opening lines as a pair of tightly interlocking questions. First: to what extent is the accumulation of money capital a faithful index of genuine capital accumulation, i.e. of reproduction on an expanded scale? Is the \"plethora\" of capital — a term Marx notes is used only of interest-bearing capital — merely a particular expression of industrial overproduction, or is it a separate phenomenon that can exist alongside, and even independently of, the state of real production? Second: to what extent does \"monetary scarcity\" (a shortage of loan capital) express a genuine lack of real capital, and to what extent does it merely coincide with a shortage of money as such — a lack of means of circulation? These two questions set the analytical horizon for the entire chapter, and everything that follows is, at root, an attempt to disentangle what Marx takes to be a systematically false identity : the identification of money capital with real capital, an identification that the credit system constantly produces and constantly conceals. The chapter's thesis can be stated in a compact, programmatic form. Money capital and real capital are not the same thing, and their movements are not merely non-identical but frequently inverse . The accumulation of money capital is not a reliable index of the accumulation of real capital; a plethora of loanable capital may coincide with the stagnation of reproduction, and acute scarcity of loan capital may coincide with an over-abundance of productive capital. In the crisis itself, as Marx insists, \"nothing could be more wrong... than to ascribe such a situation to a lack of productive capital. It is precisely then that there is a surplus of productive capital.\" The ultimate cause of all real crises, he argues, remains \"the poverty and restricted consumption of the masses,\" against which the drive of capitalist production — to develop the productive forces as if the absolute consumption capacity of society alone set the limit — runs headlong. But this crisis-theoretic claim is embedded within a far more granular argumentative project: the systematic differentiation of commercial credit (the credit capitalists engaged in reproduction extend to one another, represented by the bill of exchange) from banker's credit or monetary credit proper (the advancing of loanable money capital by banks and money-lenders as intermediaries), the analysis of fictitious capital (government bonds, joint-stock shares, and other securities that duplicate — or, more precisely, substitute paper titles for — real capital), and the tracing of the industrial cycle in which the rate of interest moves inversely to the movement of industrial capital. This chapter forms part of the section of Capital Volume III dealing with \"The Division of Profit into Interest and Profit of Enterprise,\" and it stands alongside Chapter 31 (\"Money Capital and Real Capital: II\") as one of the two chapters in which Marx addresses the specific phenomena of the credit system. It presupposes, and constantly refers back to, earlier analyses: the theory of reproduction developed in Volume II (which Marx cites explicitly when discussing the mutual exchange of constant capital among producers), the treatment of surplus-value and its metamorphoses, and the theory of profit and its tendential fall (introduced in the immediately preceding pages as the \"simple result of the tendential fall in the rate of profit\"). The chapter is also densely historical, drawing on the commercial crisis literature of the Victorian era — the Official Reports on Bank Acts of 1857 and 1858, the Commercial Distress evidence of 1847-8, the testimony of figures such as Hodgson of the Royal Bank of Liverpool, the Manchester manufacturer R. Gardner, the Liverpool East India merchant Charles Turner, and the monetary theorists Tooke and Lord Overstone — as empirical material for a theoretical critique of what Marx regards as the systematic optical illusions of the British currency school. The intellectual objective, then, is threefold. First, to de-naturalize the identity of money capital and real capital, showing that the credit system systematically produces \"imaginary\" or \"fictitious\" capital that duplicates, represents, and ultimately obscures real wealth. Second, to construct a periodization of the industrial cycle in which the rate of interest, the state of commercial credit, and the accumulation of loanable capital are shown to move in determinate, non-obvious patterns relative to industrial capital. Third, to mount a sustained polemic against the \"ignorant and confused banking laws\" (such as the Bank Acts of 1844-5) and the illusion — which Marx attributes to Overstone and even to Tooke — that the crisis is fundamentally a monetary phenomenon curable by monetary means. \"No bank legislation,\" Marx declares flatly, \"can abolish crises themselves.\" This is the hinge on which the chapter's critical intervention turns, and it is what makes the chapter not merely an exercise in taxonomy but a decisive moment in the Marxian theory of crisis. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Two Opening Questions and the Problem of \"Plethora\" The chapter begins in medias res, announcing that \"the only difficult questions which we are now coming on to in connection with the credit system\" are a pair of questions about the relation between money capital and real capital. The first concerns the accumulation of money capital as such : how far is it, and how far is it not, \"an index of genuine capital accumulation, i.e. of reproduction on an expanded scale\"? This formulation is crucial — real capital accumulation is defined here not as a stock of things but as a process , \"reproduction on an expanded scale.\" Money capital, by contrast, is identified with \"interest-bearing capital\" — a category Marx had been developing throughout the preceding chapters, denoting loanable capital destined to be loaned out at interest. The specific term under interrogation is the plethora (over-supply) of capital, a term Marx notes is \"used only of interest-bearing capital, i.e. money capital.\" The question is whether this plethora is simply a particular expression of industrial overproduction, or whether it is a \"separate phenomenon alongside\" industrial overproduction. This is not a scholastic distinction: if the plethora of money capital is merely an aspect of industrial overproduction, then the state of the loan market is a reliable diagnostic of the state of production; if it is separate, then the loan market has a degree of autonomy — capable of appearing plentiful when production is stagnant, and scarce when production is surfeited. Marx adds a subsidiary question here that launches one of the chapter's recurring motifs: does such a plethora coincide with the presence of stagnant sums of money — bullion, gold coin, banknotes — so that \"this excess of actual money is an expression and form of appearance of this plethora of loan capital\"? This question demands discriminating between money (means of circulation and means of payment) and loanable capital (which may be demanded, supplied, hoarded, or loaned largely independently of the quantity of currency in circulation). The second opening question — monetary scarcity, i.e. a shortage of loan capital, as an expression of a lack of real capital versus a lack of money as such — is its mirror image. Imaginary Capital I: The National Debt and Government Bonds Having set out the questions, Marx turns immediately to a historical phenomenon that most dramatically illustrates the autonomization of money capital from real capital: the accumulation of the national debt . As he had shown earlier (in the preceding chapter on the credit system), the accumulation of capital in the form of the national debt \"means nothing more than the growth of a class of state creditors with a preferential claim to certain sums from the overall proceeds of taxation.\" This is \"the accumulation of proprietary claims to labour.\" The mechanism is set out by Marx drawing on Sismondi, whom he cites at length in a footnote. Sismondi's formulation is exact: government bonds \"are no more than imaginary capital, representing the portion of annual revenue destined for the payment of debts. A capital of equal size has been frittered away; this gives the loan its denomination, but it is not what the government bond represents, for the capital no longer exists in any form.\" The bond is a claim against future tax revenue, a claim whose \"capital\" has been consumed (typically in war), yet which circulates as if it were a capital value. Marx's gloss on Sismondi is that \"the way even an accumulation of debts can appear as an accumulation of capital\" shows \"the distortion involved in the credit system\" reaching \"its culmination.\" The key mechanism is the paper duplication of annihilated capital : \"These promissory notes which were issued for a capital originally borrowed but long since spent, these paper duplicates of annihilated capital, function for their owners as capital in so far as they are saleable commodities and can therefore be transformed back into capital.\" Two features distinguish this paper from real capital: (1) the real capital it originally represented no longer exists; (2) the paper is nonetheless saleable , hence convertible back into capital by its owner through sale on the market. The saleability — the capacity to be alienated for money, which can in turn be invested — is what confers on the bond its character as capital, quite independently of any underlying real asset. The footnote also identifies Sismondi's intellectual coordinates: he was a Swiss economist, contemporary with the utopian socialists, who criticized \"certain of the contradictions of the developing capitalist society\" but did so \"from the restricted standpoint of the petty bourgeoisie\" and \"idealized petty commodity production.\" Marx thus distinguishes his own critique — which proceeds from the standpoint of the historical tendency of capitalist production — from Sismondi's nostalgic critique. He is using Sismondi as an accurate witness to a real phenomenon even while rejecting Sismondi's normative framework, a typical Marxian maneuver. Imaginary Capital II: Joint-Stock Shares and the Autonomization of Stock Exchange Values The discussion then shifts from government debt to ownership titles to joint-stock companies, railways, mines . These differ from government bonds in that they are \"genuinely titles to real capital.\" But, Marx insists, they have two crucial properties. First, \"they give no control over this capital. The capital cannot be withdrawn. They give only a legal claim to a share of the surplus-value that this capital is to produce.\" The title is thus a claim on the surplus-value generated by a real capital stock that the title-holder does not control and cannot recover. Second, \"these titles similarly become paper duplicates of the real capital, as if a bill of lading simultaneously acquired a value alongside the cargo it refers to.\" The bill of lading analogy is exact: the bill of lading is a document entitling its bearer to a cargo; if it were to acquire an autonomous market value alongside the cargo, one would have two values where economically there is one. This is precisely what has happened with shares: \"They become nominal representatives of non-existent capitals. For the actual capital exists as well, and in no way changes hands when these duplicates are bought and sold.\" The real capital (the railway, the mine) continues to function; the share certificates circulate alongside them as autonomous capital values. These titles become forms of interest-bearing capital because \"not only do they assure certain revenues but the capital values invested in them can also be repaid by their sale.\" Two things make a security function as capital: a revenue stream (dividends or interest), and liquidity — the possibility of recovering the capital value by selling the security. Marx now introduces a key claim about the legal and economic character of these titles: \"In so far as the accumulation of these securities expresses an accumulation of railways, mines, steamships, etc., it expresses an expansion of the actual reproduction process, just as the expansion of a tax list on personal property... indicates an expansion of this property itself.\" So there is a connection, in this limited sense, between the accumulation of securities and real accumulation. But — and this is the operative qualification — \"as duplicates that can themselves be exchanged as commodities, and hence circulate as capital values, they are illusory, and their values can rise and fall quite independently of the movement in value of the actual capital to which they are titles.\" Marx then states a crucial tendential law governing the value of these securities: \"Their values, i.e. their listings on the stock exchange, have a necessary tendency to rise with the fall in the rate of interest, in so far as this is a simple result of the tendential fall in the rate of profit, independent of the specific movements of money capital.\" This is one of the most theoretically pregnant statements in the chapter, and it repays close analysis. The rate of interest is the price paid for the use of loanable capital. The rate of profit is the general rate of return on industrial capital, which Marx had argued tends to fall as capitalist production develops. If the rate of profit falls and the rate of interest falls along with it (the interest rate being a fraction of profit), then the capitalized value of a fixed stream of revenue rises. If a perpetual bond yields £5 annually, and the market rate of interest is 5%, the bond's capital value is £100 (5/0.05); if the market rate falls to 2.5%, the same £5 annual revenue capitalizes at £200. The mechanism is the simple inverse relationship between the rate of interest and the capitalized value of a given revenue stream. Thus \"this imaginary wealth, which according to its value expression gives each person his aliquot share of a definite original nominal value, already expands for this reason as capitalist production develops\" — i.e. the capital value of securities inflates automatically as the rate of profit/interest tends to fall, independently of any real accumulation. From this follows the chapter's famous observation that \"profits and losses that result from fluctuations in the price of these ownership titles, and also their centralization in the hands of railway magnates etc., are by the nature of the case more and more the result of gambling, which now appears in place of labour as the original source of capital ownership, as well as taking the place of brute force.\" The historical sequence Marx sketches — brute force (primitive accumulation) → labour (the \"original source\" of capital ownership in the classical political economy narrative) → gambling (the stock exchange) — is compressed but rhetorically pointed. Stock-market speculation is presented as the mature form of the appropriation of wealth; it takes the place of the older, cruder mechanisms. This \"imaginary money wealth\" makes up \"a very considerable part not only of the money wealth of private individuals but also of banking capital.\" The Bankers' Accumulation and the Delimitation of the Question Marx now adds a brief remark on bankers as a distinct class whose wealth accumulates partly in money form and partly in \"direct claims to money.\" He sets this aside \"only to get it quickly out of the way,\" but the remark has analytical weight: the bankers are \"intermediaries between the private money capitalists on the one hand, and the state, local authorities and borrowers engaged in the process of reproduction on the other; for the entire immense extension of the credit system, and credit as a whole, is exploited by the bankers as their private capital.\" The accumulation of wealth by this class \"may proceed in a very different way from that of actual accumulation,\" but it \"proves in any case that they put away a good proportion of the latter.\" The banker is thus initially characterized as a pure intermediary — his wealth consists in claims (deposits, loans, securities) rather than in productive assets. But Marx immediately qualifies and narrows the question. \"To reduce the question at issue here to narrower limits\": government bonds, shares and other securities \"are all spheres of investment for loanable capital, for capital that is designed to be interest-bearing. They are forms for lending it out. But they are not themselves the loanable capital that is invested in them.\" This distinction — between the form of investment and the capital invested — is decisive, and it is one Marx will develop throughout. When an industrialist or merchant needs funds (to discount bills or take out a loan), what he needs is not shares or government stock; \"What he needs is money. That is why he pledges or sells these securities if he cannot obtain money in any other way.\" The securities are a store of value that can be liquidated into money; they are not, in their character as securities, loanable capital. Marx then specifies the object of the analysis with precision: \"It is this accumulation of loan capital that we have to deal with here, and, moreover, the accumulation of loanable money capital in particular.\" What is not at issue is \"the lending of houses, machines or other fixed capital\" — i.e. the lending of use-values. Nor is it \"the advances that industrialists and merchants make to one another in commodities within the ambit of the reproduction process, although we shall also have to investigate that point in more detail.\" The exclusive concern is \"the monetary loans that the bankers, as intermediaries, make to the industrialists and merchants.\" This delimitation — loanable money capital, advanced by bankers as intermediaries to industrialists and merchants — sets up the chapter's central analytical distinction between commercial credit and bankers' credit. Commercial Credit: The Bill of Exchange and the Reciprocal Circuit Marx now turns to commercial credit , \"i.e. the credit that capitalists involved in the reproduction process give one another.\" This \"forms the basis of the credit system.\" Its representative is the bill of exchange : \"a promissory note with a fixed date of payment, a 'document of deferred payment.'\" The defining structural feature is reciprocity: \"Each person gives credit in one direction and receives credit from another.\" Marx explicitly sets to one side \"banker's credit, which is an entirely separate and essentially different element.\" The analytical strategy is to isolate commercial credit first, then introduce bankers' credit as an additional, qualitatively distinct layer. The circulation of bills among merchants by endorsement — \"without the intervention of any discounting\" — effects \"a transfer of the claim from A to B, and absolutely nothing in the relationship is changed. One person simply takes the place of another.\" Marx then illustrates with a concrete chain: spinner A owes a bill to cotton-broker B; B owes to importer C; C, if he also exports yarn, \"can buy yarn from A against a bill of exchange, and spinner A can settle with broker B with the latter's own bill of exchange, which C received in payment.\" In this case \"it is at most a balance that remains to be paid in the form of money.\" The whole transaction \"simply mediates the exchange of cotton and yarn. The exporter simply represents the spinner and the cotton-broker the cotton planter.\" This is a demonstration of the real economic content underlying the credit relation: the bills mediate the exchange of commodities and represent the productive/reproduction process, not autonomous financial claims. The Two Structural Conditions of Commercial Credit: Reflux and Cash Marx then isolates \"two things should be noted about this circuit of purely commercial credit.\" The first is that \"the settlement of these reciprocal claims depends on the reflux of the capital; i.e. C-M, which is simply delayed.\" Here we must unpack the notation. In Marx's reproduction schemas, C = commodity, M = money, C-M = sale (commodity converted into money), M-C = purchase (money converted into commodity). The \"reflux\" (Rückfluss) is the return of capital from its commodity form into its money form — the completion of the sale that enables the debtor to pay. In ordinary credit, the sale is not absent; it is merely postponed (\"simply delayed\"). The creditor accepts the bill in place of immediate money because he anticipates the reflux. But because credits are reciprocal — \"the credits are reciprocal, the ability of each person to pay depends at the same time on the ability of another to pay\" — the reflux of one firm's capital is conditional on the reflux of others'. \"When drawing a bill, the drawee can have counted either on the return of capital in his own business or on a return in the business of a third party who has to pay him a bill in the intervening period.\" Beyond prospective returns, payment is possible only by means of reserve capital — \"which the person drawing the bill has at his disposal, in order to meet his obligations in case returns are delayed.\" This introduces the first of Marx's limits on commercial credit: the wealth of the traders, in the form of reserve capital, is a precondition of the system's smooth operation. The second structural condition is that \"this credit system does not obviate the need for cash payments.\" A large proportion of expenses — \"wages, taxes, etc.\" — must be paid in cash. And in any case, the non-synchronization of obligations can force cash payments: \"if for example B, who accepts a bill from C in lieu of immediate payment, has himself to pay a bill that falls due to D before the former bill falls due to him, he must also have cash for this.\" The broader point is that \"a complete circuit of reproduction such as was assumed above, from the cotton planter to the cotton spinner and vice versa, can only be an exception, and must always be broken in several places.\" Marx explicitly refers back to Volume II, Part Three (pp. 498-501), where he analyzed how producers of constant capital exchange part of their constant capital among themselves; here the bills \"may more or less balance.\" Similarly on an ascending curve of production (cotton broker draws on spinner, spinner on manufacturer, manufacturer on exporter, exporter on importer, perhaps an importer of cotton again), the chain of bills may roughly balance. But \"the circuit of transactions and consequent doubling back of the series of claims are not one and the same thing.\" Marx gives a decisive example: \"The spinner's claim on the weaver, for example, is not settled by the claim of the coal supplier on the machine-builder; the spinner never makes counter-claims on the machine-builder in the course of his business, since his product, yarn, never becomes an element in the machine-builder's reproduction process. Claims of this kind must therefore be settled in money.\" The point is structural: reciprocal claims offset one another only where there is a double coincidence of commercial relations; where the chains of production are merely linked but not mutually reciprocal, real money is required. The Limits, Expansion, and Speculative Tendency of Commercial Credit Marx now states explicitly \"the limits of this commercial credit, considered by itself.\" They are: \"(1) the wealth of the industrialists and merchants, i.e. the reserve capital at their disposal in case of a delay in returns; (2) these returns themselves.\" The two limits are thus a stock (reserve capital) and a flow (returns). Returns may be delayed in time, or commodity prices may fall, or commodities may become temporarily unsaleable due to a glut. \"The longer bills run for, the greater the reserve capital needed and the greater the possibility that returns may be diminished or delayed as a result of a fall in price or an excess of supply on the market.\" And \"returns are that much less certain, moreover, the more the original transaction was inspired by speculation on a rise or fall in commodity prices.\" From these limits Marx derives a law of the development of credit : \"with the development of labour productivity and hence of production on a large scale, (1) markets expand and become further removed from the point of production, (2) credit must consequently be prolonged, and (3) as a result, the speculative element must come more and more to dominate transactions.\" The mechanism is: large-scale production produces for distant markets; the physical/geographic separation of production and sale entails time; the time entails credit; the time and distance increase uncertainty; the uncertainty invites speculation. \"Large-scale production for distant markets casts the entire product into the arms of commerce; but it is impossible for the nation's capital to double, so that commerce would purchase the entire national product with its own capital before selling it again.\" Marx's assumption here — to be relaxed later — is that a nation's capital cannot be duplicated, so commerce cannot tie up funds equivalent to the whole national product; credit is thus \"indispensable.\" Credit \"grows in volume with the growing value of production and grows in duration with the increasing distance of the markets.\" And there is a \"reciprocal effect\": \"The development of the production process expands credit, while credit in turn leads to an expansion of industrial and commercial operations.\" What Is Actually Loaned: Credit as Mediation of Reproduction Marx now clarifies what the \"capital\" in commercial credit actually is . \"If we consider this credit in separation from banker's credit, it is evident that it grows with the scale of industrial capital itself. Loan capital and industrial capital are identical here; the capitals loaned are commodity capitals designed either for final individual consumption or to replace constant elements of productive capital.\" This is a crucial identity claim: within the sphere of commercial credit alone, loan capital is not a distinct fund but the same capital in its commodity form, transferred from one hand to another before payment. \"So what appears here as loaned capital is always capital that exists in a certain phase of the reproduction process, but is transferred from one hand to another by purchase and sale while the equivalent for it is paid by the buyer only later, after the stipulated interval.\" Marx illustrates with a detailed chain: cotton is transferred to the spinner against a bill of exchange, yarn to the merchant against a bill, from merchant to exporter against a bill, from exporter to a merchant in India against a bill, the merchant selling the indigo and buying indigo in exchange, and so on. \"During this transfer from one hand to another, the cotton is undergoing its transformation into finished goods, and these goods are ultimately shipped to India and exchanged for indigo, which is shipped to Europe and goes into the reproduction process once again.\" The various phases of reproduction are mediated by credit: \"The spinner has not paid for the cotton, nor the cotton-goods manufacturer for the yarn, nor the merchant for the cotton goods, etc.\" Credit mediates the sequential phasing of production and the subsequent circulation of finished commodity capital. Marx distinguishes \"two sections\" in this process. \"In the first section credit mediates the actual successive phases in the production of the article in question; in the second, it simply mediates the transfer from the hands of one merchant to those of another, which includes transport — the act C-M.\" Even in the second section, however, \"at least the commodity is permanently engaged in the act of circulation, i.e. in a phase of the reproduction process.\" Hence the claim that \"what is loaned here, therefore, is never unoccupied capital but rather capital that must change its form in the hands of its owner, that exists in a form in which it is simply commodity capital for him, i.e. capital that must be transformed back and in the first instance at least converted into money.\" The credit does not create a new fund; it transfers the right to a fund that is already, and necessarily, in circulation. From this follows the chapter's central inversion of the naive view: \"A great deal of credit in the reproduction circuit — leaving aside banker's credit — does not mean a great deal of unoccupied capital which is offered for loan and seeks profitable investment, but rather a high level of employment of capital in the reproduction process.\" The mediating functions are two: \"(1) as far as the industrial capitalists are concerned, the transition of industrial capital from one phase to another, the connection of spheres of production that belong together and mesh into one another; (2) as far as the merchants are concerned, the transport and transfer of commodities from one hand to another until their definitive sale for money or their exchange with another commodity.\" So \"the maximum of credit is the same thing here as the fullest employment of industrial capital, i.e. the utmost taxing of its reproductive power irrespective of the limits of consumption.\" Consumption limits (\"extended by the stretching of the reproduction process itself\") are the boundary. Marx adds that the stretching of reproduction \"increases the consumption of revenue by workers and capitalists\" while also being \"identical with the stretching of productive consumption.\" The Contraction of Credit in Stagnation and Crisis Marx now traces what happens when the reproduction process is interrupted. \"As long as the reproduction process is fluid, so that returns remain assured, this credit persists and extends, and its extension is based on the extension of the reproduction process itself.\" But \"as soon as any stagnation occurs, as a result of delayed returns, overstocked markets or fallen prices, there is a surplus of industrial capital, but in a form in which it cannot accomplish its function.\" What is the form? \"A great deal of commodity capital; but unsaleable. A great deal of fixed capital; but in large measure unemployed as a result of the stagnation in reproduction.\" Two forms of surplus capital, both unable to function. Credit contracts for three reasons: \"(1) because this capital is unoccupied, i.e. congealed in one of its phases of reproduction, because it cannot complete its metamorphosis; (2) because confidence in the fluidity of the reproduction process is broken; (3) because the demand for this commercial credit declines.\" Marx illustrates: \"The spinner who restricts his production and has a lot of unsold yarn in store does not need to buy cotton on credit; the merchant does not need to buy any goods on credit, as he already has more than enough.\" So \"if there is a disturbance in this expansion, or even in the normal exertion of the reproduction process, there is also a lack of credit; it is more difficult to obtain goods on credit.\" The result is one of the paradoxes that the chapter labors to make intelligible. \"The demand for cash payment and distrust of credit selling is especially characteristic of the phase in the industrial cycle that follows the crash.\" And \"in the crisis itself, since everyone has goods to sell and cannot sell, even though they have to sell in order to pay, the quantity of capital blocked in its reproduction process, though not of unoccupied capital to be invested, is precisely at its greatest, even if the lack of credit is also most acute (and hence, as far as bank credit goes, the discount rate at its highest).\" Capital already invested is \"massively unemployed, since the reproduction process is stagnant. Factories stand idle, raw materials pile up, finished products flood the market as commodities.\" Hence Marx's sharp polemical conclusion: \"Nothing could be more wrong, therefore, than to ascribe such a situation to a lack of productive capital. It is precisely then that there is a surplus of productive capital, partly in relation to the normal though temporarily contracted scale of reproduction and partly in relation to the crippled consumption.\" Two denominators measure the surplus: the normal (contracted) scale of reproduction, and the crippled consumption. The surplus exists because demand has fallen below what the productive apparatus could serve, even at its contracted scale. The Simplification and the \"Ultimate Reason\" of Crisis Marx now performs a theoretical simplification to isolate the causal structure of crisis. \"Let us conceive the whole society as composed simply of industrial capitalists and wage-labourers. Let us also leave aside those changes in price which prevent large portions of the total capital from being replaced in their average proportions, and which, in the overall context of the reproduction process as a whole, particularly as developed by credit, must recurrently bring about a situation of general stagnation. Let us likewise ignore the fraudulent businesses and speculative dealings that the credit system fosters.\" Under these simplifications, \"a crisis would be explicable only in terms of a disproportion in production between different branches and a disproportion between the consumption of the capitalists themselves and their accumulation.\" That is: either sectoral disproportionality, or a disproportion between capitalists' consumption and their accumulation. But \"as things actually are, the replacement of the capitals invested in production depends to a large extent on the consumption capacity of the non-productive classes; while the consumption capacity of the workers is restricted partly by the laws governing wages and partly by the fact that they are employed only as long as they can be employed at a profit for the capitalist class.\" Here Marx relaxes the simplification: even in a two-class model, workers' consumption is circumscribed by the wage relation, and outside it, non-productive classes' consumption is a condition of capital replacement. The result is the chapter's most famous single sentence: \"The ultimate reason for all real crises always remains the poverty and restricted consumption of the masses, in the face of the drive of capitalist production to develop the productive forces as if only the absolute consumption capacity of society set a limit to them.\" The phrase \"as if\" is vital: capitalist production behaves as though the only limit were the absolute consumption capacity of society (i.e. its physical/technical capacity to consume), whereas in fact the limit is the restricted, wage-determined consumption of the masses. This is a tendential contradiction — the drive and the limit are internal to the same system — not a contingent failure. Marx then concedes the one case where a genuine lack of productive capital can be said to exist in developed capitalist countries: \"that of a general harvest failure, affecting either the staple foodstuffs or the principal raw materials for industry.\" This is important as a limiting case: the crisis mechanism proper (the \"ultimate reason\" of restricted consumption) is distinguished from natural supply shocks. This distinction will return later when Marx discusses English harvests and gold drains. Monetary Credit Proper: Discounting, Bill-Jobbing, and the Illusion of Health \"On top of this commercial credit we also have monetary credit proper.\" The transition is explicit: \"Advances between industrialists and merchants fuse together with the advancing of money to them by bankers and money-lenders.\" Marx examines the discounting of bills of exchange : \"the advance is purely nominal. A manufacturer sells his product for a bill of exchange and discounts this bill with a billbroker. But in actual fact the latter only advances his banker's credit, and the banker in turn advances the money capital of his depositors, who consist of the industrialists and merchants themselves, though also including workers (by means of savings banks) as well as landlords and other unproductive classes.\" So the \"advance\" is nominal in the sense that it does not represent new capital; it represents a redistribution of existing claims — bank credit pyramided on deposits, which are themselves the money capital of the industrialists, merchants, workers, landlords, etc. The effect: \"As far as each individual manufacturer or merchant is concerned, then, both the need for a strong reserve capital and dependence on actual returns are dispensed with.\" The structural role of reserve capital — the first limit of commercial credit — is thus, at least apparently , suspended by the banking system. But this suspension has a dark side: \"this is so much complicated by simple bill-jobbing, and by dealing in commodities with no other purpose than that of fabricating bills of exchange, that the appearance of very solid business with brisk returns can merrily persist even when returns have in actual fact long since been made only at the cost of swindled money-lenders and swindled producers.\" This is Marx's theory of the false appearance of health in mature credit systems: credit can conjure the appearance of brisk returns by financing bill-fabrication, thereby postponing the reckoning and magnifying the eventual collapse. \"This is why business always seems almost exaggeratedly healthy immediately before a collapse.\" The evidence Marx cites is the Reports on Bank Acts of 1857 and 1858, in which \"bank directors, merchants, in short a whole series of experts summoned to give evidence, with Lord Overstone at their head, all congratulated one another on the blooming and healthy state of business — just one month before the crisis broke out in August 1857.\" Marx singles out Tooke, \"as the historian of all these crises,\" for having \"fallen victim to the illusion once again in his History of Prices.\" The general form of the illusion: \"Business is always thoroughly sound, and the campaign in fullest swing, until the sudden intervention of the collapse.\" This polemic against the \"currency school\" and its expert witnesses is not merely historical — it is a theoretical claim that the credit system systematically generates false appearances. The Accumulation of Money Capital after the Crisis Marx now returns from monetary credit proper to his original question: \"We return now to the accumulation of money capital.\" The claim to be established is stated in a single sentence: \"Not every increase in money capital for loan is an index of genuine capital accumulation or an expansion of the reproduction process.\" The clearest demonstration is \"the phase of the industrial cycle immediately after the crisis, when loan capital lies idle on a massive scale.\" Marx then unpacks the post-crisis configuration. \"At these moments, when the production process has undergone a contraction (and after the crisis of 1847, production in the English industrial districts was cut by a third), when commodity prices stand at their lowest point, and when the entrepreneurial spirit is crippled, there is a low rate of interest, which in this case simply indicates an increase in loanable capital precisely as a result of the contraction and paralysis of industrial capital.\" Note the paradox: low interest — normally a sign of abundant capital seeking investment — here reflects paralysis , not prosperity. Marx enumerates the reasons why demand for loanable capital falls in this phase: \"less means of circulation are required with lower commodity prices, fewer dealings and a contraction in the capital laid out on wages; that after the settlement of debts abroad, partly by a drain of gold and partly by bankruptcies, no additional money is required to carry out the function of world money; and finally that the scale of the discount business also declines with the number and amount of bills of exchange to be discounted.\" The demand for loanable money capital declines for both means of circulation and means of payment (no new capital investment is yet in question), so capital becomes \"relatively abundant.\" But supply also increases: \"the supply of loanable money capital also undergoes a positive increase in conditions such as these, as we shall show later on.\" The empirical evidence: after 1847, \"there was 'a limitation of transactions and a great superabundance of money'\" (Commercial Distress, 1847-8, Evidence, no. 1664). And \"the rate of interest was very low on account of the 'almost perfect destruction of commerce and the almost total want of means of employing money'\" (Hodgson, Director of the Royal Bank of Liverpool, ibid., p. 45). The 1847 Crisis I: Hodgson's Error and the Railways Marx now subjects Hodgson's explanation of the 1847 crisis to sustained criticism. Hodgson's formula: \"The pressure arose from the real diminution of the moneyed capital of the country, caused partly by the necessity of paying in gold for imports from all parts of the world, and partly by the absorption of floating into fixed capital.\" Marx's response is twofold. First, the \"real diminution of moneyed capital\" due to gold payments for imports is real but is a result of harvest failure, not of railway investment. Second, the \"absorption of floating into fixed capital\" cannot \"reduce the money capital in a country.\" His demonstration is precise: \"In the case of railways, for example, which were the principal sphere of investment for capital at that time, no gold or paper is used to make viaducts and rails, and the money for railway shares, in so far as it is deposited simply for payments, functions just like all other money deposited with the banks, and even temporarily increases the loanable money capital, as we have already shown; to the extent that it is actually spent on construction, it circulates in the country as means of purchase and payment.\" So the money spent on railway shares is not destroyed; it is recycled into the banking system and recirculated as means of purchase and payment. The only case in which money capital can be affected is \"in so far as fixed capital is not exportable, i.e. in so far as its export is actually impossible, so that no capital is obtained by way of returns for articles exported, including returns in cash or bullion.\" But this is not what happened in general . What actually happened was that English export goods \"were at that time stockpiled and unsaleable on a massive scale in foreign markets.\" And for businessmen who had invested part of their working capital in railway shares, \"their floating capital really had been fixed, and they had to bear the consequences of this.\" Marx concedes this individual effect, but insists that it would have been the same had the capital been invested in mines instead of railways. So the general claim that \"absorption of floating into fixed capital\" diminished national money capital is rejected. The real reduction in available money capital due to the harvest failure and gold drain \"had nothing to do with the railway swindle.\" The 1847 Crisis II: Testimony from Hodgson, Gardner, Turner Marx then piles up empirical testimony. Hodgson: \"Almost all mercantile houses had begun to starve their business more or less... by taking part of their commercial capital for railways. Loans to so great an extent by commercial houses to railways induced them to lean too much upon... banks by the discount of paper, whereby to carry on their commercial operations.\" R. Gardner (already cited in Volume I, Chapter 15): \"In Manchester there have been immense losses in consequence of the speculation in railways.\" Marx identifies two further causes of the 1847 crisis: \"the colossal saturation of the market and the boundless fraud in the East Indian trade.\" He then cites Charles Turner, a big Liverpool East India merchant: \"They had large means, but not available. The whole of their capital was locked up in estates in the Mauritius, or indigo factories, or sugar factories. Having incurred liabilities to the extent of £500,000-600,000, they had no available assets to pay their bills, and eventually it proved that to pay their bills they were entirely dependent upon their credit.\" This is a vivid case of illiquidity — large wealth, but immobile (fixed in colonial estates), producing dependence on credit for ordinary commercial payments. Gardner then describes the China trade boom and bust after the China treaty: \"many large mills built with a view to that trade exclusively... our previous manufactures had the addition of all those.\" The result: \"Most ruinous, almost beyond description; I do not believe, that of the whole of the shipments that were made in 1844 and 1845 to China, above two-thirds of the amount have ever been returned; in consequence of tea being the principal article of repayment and of the expectation that was held out, we, as manufacturers, fully calculated upon a great reduction in the duty on tea.\" Marx then captures the \"characteristic credo of the English manufacturer, in a naive version\": \"Our commerce with no foreign market is limited by their power to purchase the commodity, but it is limited in this country by our capability of consuming that which we receive in return for our manufactures.\" Marx's gloss is biting: \"The relatively poor countries with which England trades can of course pay for and consume any amount of English manufactures, but unfortunately rich England cannot assimilate the products sent in return.\" The credo expresses a structural asymmetry: richer England's capacity to absorb imports is more limited than poorer countries' capacity to absorb English exports, so a trade boom based on exports will tend to accumulate unsold goods (or unassimilable imports). Gardner continues: \"I sent out some goods in the first instance, and the goods sold at about 15 per cent loss, from the full conviction that the price, at which my agents could purchase tea, would leave so great a profit in this country as to make up the deficiency... but instead of profit, I lost in some instances 25 and up to 50 per cent.\" The merchants saw the problem and \"rather encouraged the manufacturers to consign than take a direct interest themselves.\" In 1857, by contrast, \"the losses and bankruptcies fell principally on the merchants, as this time the manufacturers left them with the task of flooding the foreign markets 'on their own account.'\" Banking Expansion and the Transformation of Hoards into Loanable Capital Marx now introduces another mechanism that can expand money capital without corresponding to productive capital. \"An expansion of money capital arising from the fact that, as a result of the spread of banking (see the example of the Ipswich bank, below, where, in the few years immediately prior to 1857, the farmers' deposits rose four times over), what was formerly a private hoard or a reserve of coin is now always transformed for a certain period into loanable capital, no more expresses a growth in productive capital than did the growing deposits in the London joint-stock banks once these began to pay interest on deposits.\" The mechanism: hoards are converted into loanable capital by the banking system; the money that was previously idle is now lent out. This increases the quantity of loanable capital without increasing productive capital. \"As long as the scale of production remains the same, this expansion simply gives rise to an abundance of loanable money capital as compared with productive capital. Hence a low rate of interest.\" The Industrial Cycle and the Inverse Movement of Loan Capital Marx now matures the account into a periodization of the industrial cycle . The phases are distinguished by the relationship between the rate of interest and the state of industrial capital. First, \"If the reproduction process has reached the flourishing stage that precedes that of over-exertion, commercial credit undergoes a very great expansion, this in turn actually forming the 'healthy' basis for a ready flow of returns and an expansion of production.\" At this point the rate of interest is \"still low, even if it has risen above its minimum. This is actually the only point in time at which it may be said that a low rate of interest, and hence a relative abundance of loanable capital, coincides with an actual expansion of industrial capital.\" The reason: \"the ease and regularity of returns, combined with an expanded commercial credit, ensures the supply of loan capital despite the increased demand and prevents the interest level from rising.\" This is also the phase \"when jobbers first enter the picture on a notable scale, operating without reserve capital or even without capital at all, i.e. completely on money credit.\" Then, as fixed capital expands and new undertakings open, \"Interest now rises to its average level.\" It \"reaches its maximum again as soon as the new crisis breaks out, credit suddenly dries up, payments congeal, the reproduction process is paralysed and, save for the exceptions mentioned earlier, there is an almost absolute lack of loan capital alongside a surplus of unoccupied industrial capital.\" From this follows the chapter's periodizing thesis: \"By and large, therefore, the movement of loan capital, as expressed in the rate of interest, runs in the opposite direction to that of industrial capital.\" This is a structural inversion : the rate of interest falls when industrial capital is depressed (post-crisis) and rises when it is overextended (pre-crisis). The two phases in which \"abundant loan capital and a big expansion in industrial capital\" combine are: \"the phase in which the low but above minimum rate of interest coincides with the 'improvement' and growing confidence after the crisis,\" and \"the phase in which it reaches its average level, the mid-point equidistant between its minimum and maximum.\" At the beginning of the cycle, \"a low rate of interest coincides with a contraction of industrial capital, and at the end of the cycle a high rate of interest coincides with an over-abundance of industrial capital.\" The low rate of interest in the \"improvement\" phase expresses \"the fact that commercial credit only needs a small amount of bank credit, since it still stands on its own two feet.\" Marx then restates the cyclical pattern: \"In the slack phase, production falls below the level it attained in the previous cycle and for which the technical basis is now laid. In the phase of prosperity — the middle period — it develops further on this basis. In the period of overproduction and swindling, the productive forces are stretched to their limit, even beyond the capitalist barriers to the production process.\" The cycle thus has three sub-phases (slack, prosperity, overproduction/swindling), each with a characteristic relation between the technical basis, production, and the capitalist barriers. In the phase of overproduction, the productive forces are \"stretched to their limit, even beyond the capitalist barriers\" — a distinctly Marxian formulation indicating that the contradiction between productive forces and social relations becomes acute in the boom. Footnote 8: The Changing Periodicity of Crises Engels appends a substantial footnote (signed F.E.) on the changing periodicity of crises, which is crucial for understanding the historical dimension of Marx's analysis. Engels observes that \"the last great general crisis represented a turning-point. The acute form of the periodic process with its former ten-year cycle seems to have given way to a more chronic and drawn-out alternation, affecting the various industrial countries at different times, between a relatively short and weak improvement in trade and a relatively long and indecisive depression.\" He considers whether \"what is involved is simply an extension of the cycle's duration.\" The historical sequence: \"When world trade was in its infancy, 1815-47, cycles of approximately five years could be discerned; between 1847 and 1867 the cycle was definitely a ten-year one; might we now be in the preparatory phase of a new world crash of unheard-of severity?\" The changes since 1867 are then enumerated: \"The colossal expansion of means of communication — ocean-going steamships, railways, electric telegraphs, the Suez canal — has genuinely established the world market for the first time.\" Alongside England, \"which formerly had a monopoly of industry,\" there is now \"a whole series of competing industrial countries; the investment of surplus European capital in all parts of the globe is infinitely greater and more widespread, so that this is far more broadly distributed and local over-speculation is more easily overcome.\" Hence \"most of the former breeding-grounds of crises and occasions for crisis formation have been abolished or severely weakened.\" Competition in the home market retreats before \"cartels and trusts\"; on the foreign market it is restricted by protective tariffs (which are \"themselves nothing less than the weapons for the final general industrial campaign to decide supremacy on the world market\"). The dialectical punch line: \"And so each of the elements that counteracts a repetition of the old crises, conceals within it the nucleus of a far more violent future crisis.\" The means of communication, the spread of industrial competition, the cartels and tariffs — all dampen the old form of crisis while preparing a new and more violent one. This is a characteristically Marxian — and here explicitly Engelsian — dialectical claim about historical transformation. The Money Crisis Proper: Credit Withdrawal and the Scramble for Means of Payment Marx now returns to the crisis itself. \"The reason for the lack of means of payment in the crisis period is self-evident. The convertibility of bills of exchange has replaced the metamorphosis of the actual commodities, and all the more so at such a time in so far as one group of firms is operating purely on credit.\" The phrase \"the convertibility of bills of exchange has replaced the metamorphosis of the actual commodities\" is theoretically dense. In normal circulation, commodities pass through the metamorphosis C-M-C': the commodity is sold for money, and the money is used to buy another commodity. In a developed credit system, this metamorphosis is represented by the circulation of bills: firms accept each other's bills as substitutes for the money they would have received in the sale, and the actual sale/reflux becomes a matter of the bill's convertibility into money at maturity. But the bill's convertibility depends on the reflux of someone's capital, and if the underlying reproduction is disturbed, the bill is no longer convertible. The \"metamorphosis of the actual commodities\" — the concrete sale of goods — has been replaced by a purely financial operation whose grounds have disappeared. Marx then addresses the banking legislation of 1844-5. \"Ignorant and confused banking laws, such as those of 1844-5, may intensify the monetary crisis. But no bank legislation can abolish crises themselves.\" The Bank Charter Act of 1844 restricted the note issue of the Bank of England beyond a fixed fiduciary limit to the amount of gold held in the banking department, so that in a crisis (when gold was drained abroad), the note issue would contract precisely when liquidity was most needed. Marx's argument is that this intensifies but cannot cause or cure the crisis; the crisis has its grounds in reproduction, not in the currency. \"In a system of production where the entire interconnection of the reproduction process rests on credit, a crisis must evidently break out if credit is suddenly withdrawn and only cash payment is accepted, in the form of a violent scramble for means of payment.\" This is a necessary consequence of the credit structure, not an accident of policy. \"At first glance, therefore, the entire crisis presents itself as simply a credit and monetary crisis. And in fact all it does involve is simply the convertibility of bills of exchange into money.\" The \"at first glance\" signals the optical illusion: the crisis appears purely monetary, but the underlying reality is different. \"The majority of these bills represent actual purchases and sales, the ultimate basis of the entire crisis being the expansion of these far beyond the social need.\" So the crisis's ultimate basis is the expansion of commodity transactions beyond social need (overproduction), not the currency per se. \"On top of this, however, a tremendous number of these bills represent purely fraudulent deals, which now come to light and explode; as well as unsuccessful speculations conducted with borrowed capital, and finally commodity capitals that are either devalued or unsaleable, or returns that are never going to come in.\" Four categories of bills: legitimate transactions overextended; fraudulent deals; unsuccessful speculations; and commodities that cannot be sold. The impossibility of a monetary cure is stated with analytical precision: \"It is clear that this entire artificial system of forced expansion of the reproduction process cannot be cured by now allowing one bank, e.g. the Bank of England, to give all the swindlers the capital they lack in paper money and to buy all the depreciated commodities at their old nominal values.\" The reductio: if the Bank of England simply issued paper to cover all the missing bills, it would be financing swindlers and overpaying for commodities whose real value has fallen. Marx then notes the \"upside down\" character of the paper world: \"everything here appears upside down, since in this paper world the real price and its real elements are nowhere to be seen, but simply bullion, metal coin, notes, bills and securities.\" This phenomenological claim — that the crisis presents itself in inverted form, with the concrete (bullion, coins, notes) appearing to govern the abstract (real value) — is central. The \"distortion is particularly evident in centres such as London, where the monetary business of an entire country is concentrated; here the whole process becomes incomprehensible. It is somewhat less so in the centres of production.\" So the illusion of the crisis as purely monetary is stronger the further one is from production (London) and weaker at the site of production (Manchester). This is a theoretical point about the spatial distribution of illusions in the credit system. Commodity Capital as Potential Money Capital and Its Contraction Marx adds a sophisticated point \"in passing.\" \"Commodity capital is inherently already money capital, i.e. a certain sum of value expressed in the commodity's price.\" That is, every commodity is a value that can in principle be realized as money at its price. \"As a use-value it is a certain quantity of particular useful objects, and these are present in excess at the moment of crisis.\" So the use-value side is overabundant. \"But as inherently money capital, potential money capital, it is subject to constant expansion and contraction. On the eve of the crisis, and during it, the commodity capital is contracted in its capacity as potential money capital. It represents less money capital for its owner and his creditors (also as security for bills of exchange and loans) than at the time it was bought and when the discounts and loans made with it as security were concluded.\" Two aspects: as potential money capital , commodity capital contracts (its realizable value falls), and therefore the collateral for loans and bills shrinks. \"If this is the supposed sense of the contention that the money capital of a country is reduced in times of pressure, it is identical with saying that commodity prices have fallen. Such a collapse in prices, incidentally, only balances their earlier inflation.\" So the \"reduction of money capital\" that contemporary observers bemoaned is really a fall in prices that corrects an earlier inflation. Marx then notes the effect on unproductive classes and fixed-income recipients. \"The incomes of the unproductive classes, and of those who live on fixed incomes, remain for the most part stationary during the price inflation that goes hand in hand with overproduction and over-speculation. Their consumption power thus undergoes a relative decline, and with this also their ability to replace the portion of the total reproduction that would normally go into their consumption. Even if their demand remains nominally the same, it still declines in real terms.\" So during inflation, fixed incomes lose purchasing power; the demand they generate cannot move at the same rate as prices. This is one of the concrete channels through which the \"restricted consumption\" of the non-productive classes (mentioned earlier) operates. International Aspects: Balances of Payments and the Drain of Gold The chapter now broadens to the international dimension. \"As regards the question of imports and exports, it should be noted that all countries are successively caught up in the crisis, and that it is then apparent that they have all, with few exceptions, both exported and imported too much; i.e. the balance of payments is against them all, so that the root of the problem is actually not the balance of payments at all.\" The key theoretical move: the universality of the balance-of-payments problem shows that the balance of payments is not the cause — if all countries simultaneously have adverse balances, no bilateral balance-of-payments explanation can work. \"England, for example, suffers from a drain of gold. It has imported too much. But at the same time every other country is overburdened with English goods. They too have imported too much, or been made to import too much.\" The common cause is overproduction : all countries have produced too much and traded too much. Marx introduces a distinction: \"There is a distinction, however, between the country that exports on credit, and those that do not, or only a little. The latter then import on credit; and this is only not the case if the goods in question are sent out on consignment.\" The creditor country (England) exports on credit; the debtor countries import on credit. The crisis \"may break out first of all in England, the country that gives the most credit and takes the least, because the balance of payments, i.e. the balance of payments due, which must be settled immediately, is against it, even though the overall balance of trade is in its favour.\" Here a crucial distinction: the balance of trade (total exports minus imports) may favor England, but the balance of payments due (payments falling due) may be against it, because England extends credit to others and receives fewer immediate payments than it owes. \"This fact is partly to be explained in terms of the credit given by England and partly in terms of the amount of loaned capital sent abroad, which means that a large quantity of returns flows back to England in commodities, in addition to trading returns in the strict sense.\" England invests capital abroad, so its returns flow back partly as commodities (dividends, interest, repayments), not just as trade receipts. \"(Sometimes the crisis breaks out first of all in America, the country that takes the most credit for trade and capital from England.)\" So the locus of eruption depends on the pattern of credit and capital flows. The propagation mechanism is then traced: \"The crash in England, introduced and accompanied by a drain of gold, settles England's balance of payments, partly by bankrupting its importers (on which more below), partly by driving part of its commodity capital abroad at low prices, and partly by the sale of foreign securities, the purchase of English ones, etc.\" Three ways of settling a balance of payments in crisis: bankruptcy of importers (default); dumping goods abroad at low prices; selling foreign securities and buying English ones (capital repatriation). \"The sequence now reaches another country. The balance of payments was temporarily in its favour; but now the normal interval between the balancing of payments and the balancing of trade is abolished or at least cut short by the crisis; all payments have to be settled at once. The same situation then repeats itself here. England now has a reflux of gold, the other country a drain.\" The crisis compresses time : payments that would normally be staggered over months are demanded all at once, so the country that was temporarily in surplus now faces a drain. \"What appears in one country as excessive importing appears in the other as excessive exporting, and vice versa.\" The universality of over-trade is the general phenomenon; the drain of gold is its local manifestation. Marx then gives the 1857 example: \"In 1857 the crisis broke out in the United States. This led to a drain of gold from England to America. But as soon as the American bubble burst, the crisis reached England, with a drain of gold from America to England. Similarly between England and the Continent.\" The general formulation: \"In times of general crisis the balance of payments is against every country, at least against every commercially developed country, but always against each of these in succession — like volley firing — as soon as the sequence of payments reaches it; and once the crisis has broken out in England, for example, this sequence of dates is condensed into a fairly short period.\" The \"volley firing\" metaphor is exact: the crisis moves like successive volleys, striking each country in turn as its payments come due. And \"it is then evident that all these countries have simultaneously over-exported (i.e. over-produced) and over-imported (i.e. over-traded) and that in all of them prices were inflated and credit overstretched. In every case the same collapse follows.\" The two theoretical conclusions follow: \"(1) that the drain of gold is simply a phenomenon of the crisis, and not its basis; (2) that the sequence in which this drain of gold affects the different countries simply indicates when the series reaches them, for a final settlement of accounts; when their own day of crisis comes and its latent elements in turn emerge in their own case.\" So the drain of gold is phenomenal (symptom, not cause), and the sequence of its appearance merely registers the order in which the general crisis is transmitted. This is the chapter's central \"anti-mercantilist\" or \"anti-currency-school\" claim at the international level. The English Economists' National Illusion Marx now directly attacks the English economic writers. \"It is characteristic of the English economic writers — and the economic literature worth mentioning since 1830 principally boils down to writing on currency, credit and crises — that they consider the export of precious metal that occurs in times of crisis, despite the turn in the exchange rates, simply from the English standpoint, as a purely national phenomenon, and resolutely close their eyes to the fact that, if their bank raises the interest rate in times of crisis, all other European banks do the same thing, and that, if they raise a cry of distress about the drain of gold today, this is echoed tomorrow in America and the day after that in Germany and France.\" The critique has two parts: (a) the chauvinism of treating the drain as purely national; (b) the systematic blindness to the synchronization of central-bank interest-rate policy across countries, and to the successive shifting of the drain from one financial center to the next. Marx's claim is that a proper analysis must be international; the national standpoint produces a distorted picture. The Corn Crises and the Cheating of Corn-Exporting Countries Marx now turns to a political-economic point about the 1847 crisis. \"In 1847, 'the engagements running upon this country had to be met' (mostly for corn). 'Unfortunately, they were met to a great extent by failures' (wealthy England obtained a breathing space for itself by defaulting on its obligations vis-à-vis the Continent and America), 'but to the extent to which they were not met by failures, they were met by the exportation of bullion' (Report of Committee on Bank Acts, 1857).\" So in 1847, England's obligations (for corn imports following the harvest failure) were settled by two methods: bankruptcy (defaulting on foreign creditors) and gold exports. Marx's gloss is moral-political: \"Thus in so far as a crisis in England is intensified by the banking legislation, this legislation is also a means of cheating the corn-exporting countries in times of famine, first of their corn and then of the money for their corn.\" The banking legislation (1844-5) that intensified the crisis also functioned as a mechanism by which England could appropriate foreign corn and then avoid paying for it. From this follows a policy prescription: \"A ban on the export of corn in times such as these, in the case of countries that are themselves suffering to a greater or lesser extent from rising prices, is thus a very rational defence against this plan by the Bank of England, 'to meet engagements' for corn imports 'by failures'. Far better that the corn producers and speculators should lose a part of their profits for the good of their own country than their capital for the good of England.\" This is a striking reversal of the free-trade orthodoxy: a corn-export ban is defended as a rational measure of national defense in a crisis. Conclusion: Commodity Capital and Fictitious Capital in Crisis The chapter closes with a compact summary. \"We conclude from what has been said here that commodity capital largely loses its capacity to represent potential money capital in time of crisis, and generally when business stagnates.\" During crisis, the commodity can no longer be reliably converted into money at its previous valuation, so it ceases to function as potential money capital for its owner. \"The same is true of fictitious capital, interest-bearing paper, in as much as this itself circulates as money capital on the stock exchange.\" Fictitious capital has three reasons for falling in price: (1) \"As the interest rate rises, its price falls.\" (2) \"It falls further, owing to the general lack of credit, which compels the owners of this paper to unload it onto the market on a massive scale in order to obtain money.\" (3) \"In the case of shares, finally, their price falls partly as a result of a decline in the revenues on which they are claims and partly as a result of the fraudulent character of the enterprises which they very often represent.\" Thus three distinct channels — interest rate, forced liquidation, and revenue/fraud — all depress the value of fictitious capital. \"This fictitious money capital is enormously reduced during crises, and with it the power of its owners to use it to borrow money in the market.\" The final distinction is crucial: \"The reduction in the money value of these securities on the stock-exchange list, however, has nothing to do with the real capital that they represent. As against this, it has a lot to do with the solvency of their owners.\" The value of securities is thus doubly decoupled from real capital: it is driven by the rate of interest, by credit conditions, and by the solvency of holders — none of which is the real capital stock. This closing distinction recapitulates the chapter's central thesis: money capital (and its most elaborate form, fictitious capital) has a life of its own, governed by laws that are not the laws of real capital. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Three fundamental principles emerge from this chapter, each with wide ramifications. First, the non-identity — and frequent inversion — of money capital and real capital . Marx establishes that the accumulation of loanable money capital is not a reliable index of real capital accumulation. The \"plethora\" of capital can arise from several sources that have nothing to do with expanded reproduction: the stagnation of industrial capital after a crisis (which reduces demand for loan capital while leaving it abundant); the transformation of idle hoards into loanable funds by the spread of banking; the tendential fall in the rate of profit, which raises the capitalized value of fixed revenue streams; the proliferation of fictitious capital in the form of government bonds, shares, and other securities. Conversely, monetary scarcity in crisis does not indicate a lack of real capital; it typically coincides with a surplus of productive capital rendered dysfunctional by the collapse of credit. This is the chapter's decisive critical move against the naive identification of money and real wealth. Second, the systematic production of illusion by the credit system . The credit system does not merely reflect reality; it produces appearances that conceal it. Business \"seems almost exaggeratedly healthy immediately before a collapse\" because credit can finance the fabrication of bills and maintain the appearance of brisk returns until the underlying reproduction collapses. The crisis appears as a purely monetary crisis — \"the convertibility of bills of exchange has replaced the metamorphosis of the actual commodities\" — even though its root lies in overproduction. The illusion is stronger at the financial center (London) than at the site of production (Manchester). The drain of gold appears to be the cause of the crisis when it is merely its phenomenon . The value of securities appears to represent the value of real capital when it is driven by interest rates, credit conditions, and the solvency of holders. The methodological lesson: a critical political economy cannot take the \"phenomenal forms\" of the credit system at face value; it must reconstruct the underlying relations. Third, the crisis-theoretic primacy of restricted consumption . Against explanations that locate the cause of crisis in currency, banking legislation, balance-of-payments disturbances, gold drains, or speculative excess, Marx insists that \"the ultimate reason for all real crises always remains the poverty and restricted consumption of the masses, in the face of the drive of capitalist production to develop the productive forces as if only the absolute consumption capacity of society set a limit to them.\" This is not a monocausal claim; the chapter accommodates sectoral disproportionality, the consumption capacity of non-productive classes, harvest failures, and the concrete mechanisms of credit (bill-jobbing, speculation, bank legislation) within a single theoretical framework. But the ultimate reason — the determining contradiction — is the restricted consumption of the masses. The banking legislation of 1844-5 can intensify but not cause or cure the crisis; the balance of payments can localize but not originate it; the drain of gold can symptomize but not constitute it. The broader implications are several. For crisis theory , Marx's periodization of the industrial cycle — the inverse relation between the rate of interest and the state of industrial capital; the post-crisis abundance of loan capital; the \"improvement\" phase in which commercial credit \"stands on its own two feet\"; the boom phase in which fixed capital expands and interest rises to its average; and the crisis phase in which credit \"suddenly dries up\" — provides a stylized but empirically grounded model of the business cycle that continues to inform heterodox macroeconomics. Engels's footnote 8, with its dialectical claim that \"each of the elements that counteracts a repetition of the old crises conceals within it the nucleus of a far more violent future crisis,\" anticipates the theory of monopoly capitalism, financialization, and the transformation of crisis forms. For the theory of fictitious capital , the chapter's distinction between the market value of securities and the value of the real capital they represent is foundational. The mechanism by which stock prices \"have a necessary tendency to rise with the fall in the rate of interest\" — capitalization of revenue streams — and the later demonstration that security prices collapse in crisis for reasons unrelated to real capital, are essential for any analysis of financial markets as semi-autonomous systems. For method , the chapter exemplifies the Marxian procedure of descending from the phenomenal forms identified by contemporary observers (the currency school's fixation on gold and the note issue, the manufacturer's credo about consumption limits, the moralistic horror at speculation) to the underlying relations of production and circulation. The \"upside down\" character of the paper world — in which concrete money appears as the cause and abstract value as the effect — is a specific instance of Marx's general claim that in capitalist society the forms of appearance systematically invert the real relations. The chapter's final line — \"The reduction in the money value of these securities on the stock-exchange list, however, has nothing to do with the real capital that they represent. As against this, it has a lot to do with the solvency of their owners\" — is a compressed statement of this methodological thesis, and a durable contribution to the critique of financial capital. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 29 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 31"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 31", "title": "Chapter 31", "subtitle": "Money Capital and Real Capital: II", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 31/Chapter 31 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 8245, "read_time": "41 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital", "Interest-Bearing Capital & Loan Capital"], "keywords": ["Money Capital and Real Capital: II", "Conversion of Capital into Loan Capital", "Speculation in Stocks and Railway Booms", "Transformation of Wealth into Liquid Claims", "Fluctuations of Loan Capital Independent of Real Production"], "text": "Chapter 31 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 8,161 words ⏱️ ~41 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS This chapter is the second half of a two-part investigation Marx titles \"Money Capital and Real Capital,\" and it opens with an explicit confession of incompleteness: \"We have still not finished with the question of how far the accumulation of capital in the form of money capital for loan coincides with genuine accumulation, the expansion of the reproduction process.\" That sentence states the chapter's central problem with surgical precision. The question is not whether money-capital grows, but whether its growth indexes real growth. Marx's answer, developed across two numbered sections, is a systematic and emphatic no — or rather, a qualified and diagnostic only sometimes, and then only indirectly . The chapter's core thesis is that the accumulation of loanable money capital is, for long stretches of the industrial cycle and through several distinct mechanisms, independent of — and occasionally inversely related to — the accumulation of real, productive capital. Marx isolates four analytically separable sources of loan-capital growth: (1) counter-cyclical surpluses that reflect the contraction of industrial capital after a crisis, or the temporary self-sufficiency of commercial credit during early recovery; (2) purely technical and institutional economies — the expansion and concentration of the banking system, savings on reserve funds and means of payment — which manufacture loan capital out of the same monetary substance \"by purely technical means\"; (3) exceptional external shocks, paradigmatically the gold discoveries of Australia and California in 1852–53; and (4) the routine, ongoing transformation of capital or revenue into money that is then deposited and thereby converted into loan capital. Only the fourth of these is an expression and result of genuine accumulation — and even then, as Marx shows with his yarn example at the chapter's close, it enters loan capital before and independently of its reconversion into productive capital, which moreover requires \"a certain minimum limit.\" The intellectual objective is therefore twofold. Polemically , the chapter wages war on what Marx calls the \"credit gibberish of the money market\" — the conceptual chaos in which, as he caustically observes, \"money is capital, and bullion is capital, and banknotes are circulation, and capital is a commodity, and debts are commodities, and fixed capital is money invested in paper that is hard to sell!\" Analytically , the chapter supplies the mechanisms by which the money form of capital acquires a real autonomy from the material reproduction process — an autonomy that is not merely a cognitive error but an objective feature of the credit system, and the material precondition of speculative crisis. Within the broader architecture of Capital Volume 3, Part V, this chapter belongs to the analysis of how surplus-value is divided into interest and profit of enterprise, and to the emergence of a distinct class of money-lending capitalists — bankers, billbrokers, \"discount houses\" — whose profit is explicitly identified here as \"simply a deduction from the surplus-value that the reproductive agents extract\" and as an appropriation \"at the expense of both the industrial and commercial capitalists.\" The chapter thus connects the theory of interest-bearing capital to a class analysis of finance and, through the trade-cycle material at its centre, to the theory of crisis. Its method is characteristic of Marx's late manuscript style: abstract dialectical categories are constantly tested against, and illustrated by, verbatim extracts from the parliamentary Bank Committee reports of 1857 and 1858 and from The Economist of 1847 — evidence that supplies not merely illustration but the raw institutional texture of the argument. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Distinction That Governs Everything Marx begins by splitting a single phenomenon into two that are constantly confused: \"(1) the mere transformation of money into loan capital; (2) the transformation of capital or revenue into money that is transformed into loan capital.\" The distinction is between a formal operation and a substantive one. In case (1), money that already exists simply changes hands and changes legal form — it becomes a loan, an interest-bearing claim. Nothing has been produced; no new value has entered the world. In case (2), something real has happened upstream: capital or revenue has been generated in the reproduction process and then assumes the money form, from which it can be deposited and lent. Only the latter \"is related to the genuine accumulation of industrial capital, and only this can involve a positive accumulation of capital for loan.\" The word positive is doing heavy lifting here: it signals that there are forms of loan-capital growth that are negative or neutral with respect to real accumulation. The two numbered sections of the chapter are organized precisely around this split — Section 1 examines the many ways loan capital can grow without positive accumulation; Section 2 examines the genuine case. Section 1 — The Two Counter-Cyclical Surpluses of Loan Capital Marx returns to a result established earlier: an \"over-abundance of loan capital, which is related to productive accumulation only by standing in inverse proportion to it,\" arises in two specific phases of the industrial cycle . He is precise about the phases. The first is \"the time when industrial capital in the two forms of productive capital and commodity capital has contracted, i.e. at the beginning of the cycle after the crisis.\" The second is \"the time when improvement sets in but commercial credit still has little need for bank credit.\" The mechanism of the first case is a release of money capital. During the boom, money was locked into productive capital (plant, materials, labour-power purchased) and commodity capital (finished goods awaiting sale or in transit). The crisis devalues and liquidates these; capital is \"set free\" in money form because there is nothing productive to do with it. So it \"appears as unoccupied loan capital.\" The surplus of loan capital therefore \"expresses the stagnation of industrial capital\" — it is a symptom of real contraction , a mirror image of the destruction of real capital, and thus \"the exact opposite of genuine accumulation.\" The mechanism of the second case is different and subtler. Here business is reviving, but it is financed out of commercial credit — the credit that reproductive agents extend to one another directly, in commodity form. Marx's gloss: this independence \"rest[s] on the fluidity of returns, short terms of credit and operations predominantly conducted with one's own capital.\" Three conditions, each of which keeps the billbroker's door unopened. And note the sociological observation: \"The speculators who rely on the credit capital of others have not yet appeared on the scene; while people who operate with their own capital are still far removed from anything like pure credit operations.\" What Marx is tracking is a maturity gradient of the credit system: early recovery is a world of owner-operators; late boom is a world of leveraged speculators. Between them lies the transition that will make the money market the master of the industrial cycle rather than its servant. In the second phase, the surplus of loan capital \"coincides with the renewed expansion of the reproduction process: it accompanies it without causing it.\" This formula — accompanying without causing — is one of the chapter's most important conceptual instruments. It marks a relation of correlation without causation , which will recur. And notably, the surplus is \"already on the decline and is still only relative to the demand\": the over-abundance is diminishing; what remains is merely the gap between supply and a still-rising demand. Even here, loan capital does not lead; it lags. Why Cheap Money Promotes Real Accumulation: The Profit-of-Enterprise Channel Marx then makes a claim that appears to soften the antithesis: in both cases, \"the expansion of the accumulation process proper is promoted, because the low rate of interest ... increases the portion of the profit that is transformed into profit of enterprise.\" This is the inverse of the chapter's central theme, and it deserves careful unpacking. Profit of enterprise is the portion of total profit (surplus-value) that remains to the functioning capitalist after interest has been deducted. It is the residual claim. Therefore, when the interest rate is low, the borrowing capitalist retains more of the surplus-value he extracts, which raises his incentive and capacity to accumulate. Marx adds that low interest coincides with low prices in the first phase (post-crisis, when everything is cheap) and with slowly rising prices in the second (early recovery, when demand is gathering). Both price environments favour accumulation by lowering replacement costs or by promising capital gains on inventory. He also pre-empts an apparent counter-example: interest does rise during the height of prosperity to its average level, \"although it has risen, it has not done so in relation to profit.\" The decisive variable is the ratio of interest to profit, not the absolute rate. This is an important methodological point: the money-market rate of interest is not, in itself, an index of the burden on capital. The relation between interest and profit is a distributive relation, and only that relation tells us how much surplus-value the functioning capitalist keeps. The Technical Manufacture of Loan Capital: \"Floating Capital\" The chapter then pivots to its second, and more radical, source of non-genuine accumulation. \"We have seen on the other hand how an accumulation of loan capital may take place without any genuine accumulation, by purely technical means such as the expansion and concentration of the banking system, saving on the circulation reserve or even on private individuals' reserve funds or means of payment, which are in this way transformed into loan capital for short periods.\" The phrase purely technical means is crucial: no production, no surplus-value, no new wealth — only institutional reorganization and economization of idle balances. Marx calls the resulting fund floating capital , a term he takes from his witnesses and subjects to withering criticism. Two properties of this floating capital are emphasized. First, its duration mismatch : it \"only ever receives the form of loan capital for short periods (and thus should only be used for short-term discounting).\" It is inherently unsuitable for fixed or long-term investment. Second, its liquidity illusion : \"it is constantly flowing back and forth. If one person withdraws it, someone else puts it in.\" This is a rotating fund , and its aggregate size can grow \"quite independently of genuine accumulation.\" Marx's source is the Bank Committee of 1857, Question 501, where Mr Weguelin, Governor of the Bank of England, is asked what \"floating capital\" means. Weguelin's answer bundles together: \"capital applicable to loans of money for short periods\"; \"The Bank of England notes ... the country banks circulation, and the amount of coin which is in the country\"; and, on further questioning, \"the reserves of the bankers, in which there is a considerable fluctuation.\" When asked whether the active circulation in fact varies much, Marx inserts his own parenthetical: \"(Though it is a very major distinction as to who it is that advances the active circulation, the money-lender or the reproductive capitalist himself).\" This parenthetical is the analytical hinge of the whole passage. The quantity of circulating media is one thing; the question of who advances it is another and more important thing. Weguelin's answer conflates the two, and in doing so conceals the growth of money-lenders' power behind a statistical constant. Marx then glosses Weguelin's mention of bankers' reserves: a large fluctuation occurs in the portion of deposits bankers have not lent out, which functions as their reserve, \"though a lot of it also figures as the reserve of the Bank of England, with which their reserves are deposited.\" Already here we see a hierarchy of reserves — private banks deposit with the central bank, which becomes the reserve of reserves. And Weguelin concludes that floating capital \"may be bullion, including metal money\" (Q. 503). This elicits Marx's most rhetorically savage passage, which is also analytically substantive: \"It is truly amazing how all the categories of political economy take on a new meaning and form in this credit gibberish of the money market. Floating capital here is the expression for circulating capital, which is of course something completely different, and money is capital, and bullion is capital, and banknotes are circulation, and capital is a commodity, and debts are commodities, and fixed capital is money invested in paper that is hard to sell!\" Each clause names a genuine category error. Floating capital has been equated with circulating capital — but \"floating\" describes a term/duration property of loans, while \"circulating\" describes a turnover property of productive capital. They are orthogonal. Money has been equated with capital : but money is a means of purchase and payment, whereas capital is a value that self-expands — the identification is the foundational fetish of the money market. Bullion is called capital: but bullion is a hoard or a world-money reserve. Debts are called commodities: but a debt is a legal claim on future value, and calling it a commodity obscures that its \"price\" is inversely related to the rate of interest. And most strikingly, fixed capital is described as \"money invested in paper that is hard to sell\" — an almost oxymoronic formulation, since illiquidity is precisely what fixed capital is not supposed to be, and paper is precisely what fixed capital is not made of. Marx's point is that the practitioners of the money market systematically invert the very categories that would allow them to understand their own system. The Deposit Revolution: From Ipswich Farmers to Lombard Street The chapter now supplies the quantitative and institutional substance behind this \"technical\" expansion. The joint-stock banks of London increased deposits from £8,850,774 in 1847 to £43,100,724 in 1857 — roughly a five-fold increase in a decade. Marx cites the Committee's inference that \"a large part has been derived from sources not heretofore made available for this purpose,\" namely that \"the practice of opening accounts and depositing money with bankers has extended to numerous classes who did not formerly employ their capital (!) in that way.\" The exclamation mark after \"capital\" is Marx flagging the euphemism: what has actually happened is that money which previously circulated outside the banking system — hoards, household balances, working cash — is now being drawn into it. Marx's key analytical point is that this is not new capital; it is existing money reclassified . The very act of deposit transforms it into loanable capital. The testimony of Mr Rodwell, Chairman of the Association of the Private Country Bankers, is cited to make this concrete and to give it a social-geographic dimension: \"in the neighbourhood of Ipswich this practice has lately increased four-fold among the farmers and shopkeepers of that district; that almost every farmer, even those paying only £50 per annum rent, now keeps deposits with bankers.\" Here we have the penetration of the credit system into the countryside — the mobilization of agricultural surplus into urban money markets. The path traced by the Committee is a chain: rural deposits → aggregate finds its way \"to the employments of trade\" → \"especially gravitates to London, the centre of commercial activity\" → employed \"first in the discount of bills, or in other advances to the customers of the London bankers\" → and what remains unused by the bankers \"passes into the hands of the billbrokers, who give to the banker in return commercial bills already discounted by them.\" The Billbroker Machine: Rediscounting and Fictitious Credit Marx then performs a decisive analytical operation on this chain, following the legal form of the transaction to its real content. \"Since the banker makes advances to the billbroker on the bill that the broker has already once discounted, he actually rediscounts it.\" But this is only the first layer. \"In actual fact very many of these bills have already been rediscounted by the billbroker, and with the same money that the banker uses to rediscount the bills presented by the billbroker, the broker rediscounts new bills.\" Two things follow. First, the same money is doing repeated rediscount work — a velocity phenomenon that makes the volume of loan capital exceed any underlying quantity of money. Second, because the broker is lending on bills he has created or re-endorsed, the chain of claims lengthens faster than the chain of real transactions. The Bank Committee's own verdict is quoted: \"Extensive fictitious credits have been created by means of accommodation bills, and open credits, great facilities for which have been afforded by the practice of joint-stock country banks discounting such bills, and rediscounting them with the billbrokers in the London market, upon the credit of the bank alone, without reference to the quality of the bills otherwise .\" The final clause is the crux. In a sound bill market, the discounter evaluates the underlying commercial transaction — was goods actually sold, is the acceptor good for it? Here, the discount is made on the bank's name alone. Accommodation bills are bills drawn and accepted without any underlying commercial transaction, purely to raise money; open credits are unsecured credit lines. The presence of both in the rediscount chain means the system can expand claims on nothing but confidence. The Economist on Lombard Street: A System of Interregional and Imperial Redistribution The long extract from The Economist (1847, p. 1334) provides the systemic picture, and Marx uses it because it is a bourgeois source stating the facts with unintended candour. The passage begins from a regional imbalance : \"capital ... has accumulated in some districts of the country more rapidly than it could be used, while, in others, the means of employing capital have increased more rapidly than the capital itself.\" Agricultural districts have surplus deposits they cannot profitably use at home; mercantile, manufacturing and mining districts have a demand for capital exceeding their own means. The resolution is the emergence of \"a new class of houses in the distribution of capital, who, though usually called billbrokers, are in reality bankers upon an immense scale.\" Marx's emphasis falls on this naming problem: a new function (wholesale interbank lending) is hiding under an old name (billbroker). This is a crucial institutional innovation concealed by terminological inertia. The billbrokers \"receive, for such periods, and at such rates of interest as were agreed upon, the surplus-capital of bankers ... as well as the temporary unemployed moneys of public companies and extensive mercantile establishments, and advance them at higher rates of interest to bankers in those districts where capital was more in demand, generally by rediscounting the bills taken from their customers .\" So the broker is a spread-earner on interbank interest differentials. \"In this way Lombard Street has become the great centre in which the transfer of spare capital has been made from one part of the country, where it could not be profitably employed, to another, where a demand existed for it.\" Then comes the fateful expansion of collateral: at first the transactions were \"confined almost exclusively to borrowing and lending on banking securities.\" But as capital accumulated and was economized by banks, the discount houses' funds \"became so large that they were induced to make advances first on dock warrants of merchandise\" (warehouse receipts for goods sitting in docks), \"and next on bills of lading , representing produce not even arrived in this country, though sometimes, if not generally, secured by bills drawn by the merchant upon his broker.\" This is a decisive escalation: lending secured by claims on goods still at sea, sometimes secured only by further bills. Each step removes the lender further from any verifiable real asset. The consequences are narrated as a cascade. \"This practice rapidly changed the whole character of English commerce.\" The facilities in Lombard Street empowered \"the brokers in Mincing Lane,\" who passed them to the importing merchant; the merchant took them up \"and thus the practice has become so common of late years that it may be said to be now the general rule, and not the rare exception, as it was twenty-five years ago.\" Then the final escalation: \"large sums have been raised in Lombard Street on bills drawn against the forthcoming crops of distant colonies\" — lending against a harvest not yet planted or reaped. And the destination of the liberated capital: \"the importing merchants ... invest their floating capital with which their business has hitherto been conducted, in the most objectionable of all fixed securities — foreign plantations — over which they could exercise little or no control.\" This is a complete causal chain of credit-induced misallocation . Rural deposits, mobilized by country banks, centralized in Lombard Street, rediscounted to provincial banks, extended against dock warrants and bills of lading, freeing mercantile capital, which is then locked into illiquid, distant, uncontrollable colonial assets. Marx's summary is devastating: \" That is the 'nice' way to devour credits. \" He then describes the epistemic condition of the original depositor, which is the chapter's deepest point about consciousness and credit : \"The rural depositor imagines he is simply depositing with his banker, and also imagines that when the banker makes loans it is to private individuals whom he knows. He does not have the remotest suspicion that the banker puts his deposit at the disposal of a London billbroker, over whose operations neither of them have the slightest control.\" The chain of intermediation has broken the correspondence between ownership and risk-perception. The depositor thinks he holds a safe, local, personal claim; he actually holds an undifferentiated position in a national and imperial speculative network. Railway Construction: A Second Temporary Source of Loan Capital Marx adds a brief but significant supplementary case: \"major public undertakings, such as railway construction, can temporarily increase loan capital, in that the sums paid up always remain for a certain while in the hands of the banks and are at their disposal until they are actually spent.\" This is a construction-fund or sinking-fund phenomenon — call it the time-lag between share subscription and capital expenditure. Money paid in for shares sits in banks as deposits before being spent on rails, land and wages. During that interval it is loan capital. This is a genuinely \"technical\" source of loan capital created by the pace of investment itself , and it shows that even genuinely productive investment generates a transient pool of loan capital that is not itself productive. It will become a major theme in Marx's later account of the 1840s railway mania. Loan Capital vs. Quantity of Circulation: The Definitive Separation The next move is theoretical and crucial: \"The volume of loan capital, moreover, is completely different from the quantity of circulation.\" Marx defines quantity of circulation as \"the sum of all banknotes in circulation in a particular country, together with all metal money, including precious metal in the form of bullion\" — and notes that \"a part of this quantity forms the banks' reserve and is constantly fluctuating in size.\" The empirical proof of the separation is startling. On 12 November 1857 — the very date on which the Bank Act of 1844 was suspended, i.e. at the height of a panic — \"the entire reserve of the Bank of England was only £580,751 (including London and all its branches); their deposits at the same time amounting to £22,500,000 ; of which near six and a half million belonged to London bankers\" (Bank Acts, 1858, p. lvii). The ratio is roughly 39:1 in deposits to reserve. A colossal mass of loan capital rested on a minuscule metallic base. Marx's theoretical claim — that loan capital is not the same as circulating money — is thereby demonstrated by the most extreme data point available. He then specifies what determines the rate of interest , carefully bracketing two other kinds of variation: \"setting aside those taking place over longer periods, or the differences between interest rates in different countries; the first kind being conditioned by variations in the general rate of profit, and the second by differences in profit rates and in the development of credit.\" What remains is short-run variation, which \"depend[s] on the supply of loan capital (all other factors, the state of confidence, etc., taken as equal), i.e. of capital lent in the form of money, in metal or notes; as distinct from industrial capital that is lent as such, in the commodity form, by commercial credit among the reproductive agents themselves.\" Here Marx establishes a clean analytical partition: money-loan capital (bank credit) versus commodity-loan capital (commercial credit) . The interest rate is the price of the former specifically. The £20 Hypothesis: Velocity versus Capital Marx then lays out what is essentially a small formal argument to show that loan capital is not the same as money in circulation. \"If £20 is lent five times in the course of a day, a money capital of £100 is lent altogether, and this would equally mean that this £20 had functioned at least four times as means of purchase or payment; for if it were without the mediation of purchase and payment, so that it had not represented the transformed form of capital (commodities, including labour-power) at least four times, it would not constitute a capital of £100 but simply five claims of £20 each.\" The argument has an explicit minimum condition: the same note cannot be lent five times in a day and constitute £100 of capital unless it has successively mediated four real purchases or payments , i.e. unless the loan is repeatedly grounded in a real transaction that transforms commodities into money and back. If it is not so grounded — if the £20 is simply passed from hand to hand as a loan, or re-discounted as a fictitious bill — then the five acts of lending do not constitute a capital of £100; they constitute \"simply five claims of £20 each.\" This is Marx's most rigorous formulation in the chapter of the difference between nominal claims and real capital . Velocity counts as capital only when it is the velocity of money mediating real metamorphoses of capital (commodity → money → commodity, including labour-power). Otherwise it is just multiplied paper. Deposits, Return Migrations and the £100 Retailer Marx then restates the conditions under which \"large sums will be deposited while the amount of means of circulation is relatively small.\" There are exactly two: \"(1) the number of purchases and payments that the same piece of money performs; (2) the number of return migrations in which it comes back to the banks as a deposit, so that its repeated function as means of purchase and payment is mediated by its renewed transformation into a deposit.\" Return migration is the key concept: money that circulates does not merely circulate; it comes home to the banking system, is re-deposited, and is lent out again. The illustrative circuit is elegant and fully worked: a retail trader deposits £100 a week with his banker in money. The banker uses this to pay out part of a manufacturer's deposit. The manufacturer pays it to his workers. The workers use it to pay the retailer — who deposits it again with the bank. \"The £100 deposited by the retailer has thus to serve firstly to pay out a deposit of the manufacturer's, secondly to pay the workers, thirdly to pay the retailer himself and fourthly to deposit a further part of the same retailer's money capital; for at the end of twenty weeks, if he did not have to draw on this money himself, he would have deposited £2,000 in the bank, using the same £100.\" Note the arithmetic: 20 weeks × £100 = £2,000 of deposits from a single £100 note. Note also that every link in the circuit is a real economic act: payment of wages, purchase of consumption goods, extraction of surplus-value (the manufacturer's deposit represents realized value). Marx is at pains to show that the multiplication of deposits is not fictitious here — it is the money form of a genuinely recurring reproduction circuit. The astonishing growth of deposits is therefore compatible with a small note circulation, precisely because deposits measure turnover , not stock . This also explains why the only visible sign of how much money capital is idle is the reserve: \"The extent to which this money capital is unoccupied is shown only in the ebb and flow of the banks' reserve funds.\" Hence Weguelin's testimony that \"the gold in the Bank of England is the 'only' reserve capital.\" Marx quotes him at length: \" 1258. Practically, I think, the rate of discount is governed by the amount of unemployed capital which there is in the country. The amount of unemployed capital is represented by the reserve of the Bank of England, which is practically a reserve of bullion. When, therefore, the bullion is drawn upon, it diminishes the amount of unemployed capital in the country, and consequently raises the value of that which remains.\" And: \" 1364. The reserve of bullion in the Bank of England is, in truth, the central reserve, or hoard of treasure, upon which the whole trade of the country is carried on ... And it is upon that hoard or reservoir that the action of the foreign exchanges always falls.\" Marx lets this stand, but its implications are subversive. If a £580,751 reserve governs a £22.5 million deposit mass, then the interest rate — the price of the use of capital — is determined by a marginally small and externally vulnerable quantity. The credit system's apparent massive autonomy rests on a hair-trigger base. This is precisely the point at which the chapter's theme — the separation of money capital from real capital — becomes a theory of fragility : the more the system's loan capital is disconnected from real accumulation, the more its behaviour is determined by a reserve that has nothing to do with production. The Empirical Proof of Genuine Accumulation: Export Statistics and the Ratchet Marx then supplies the measure of real accumulation against which all this monetary froth must be judged: \"One measure for the accumulation of genuine capital, i.e. productive and commodity capital, is provided by the statistics of exports and imports.\" Note he does not say the measure, but one measure — and the one he chooses is physical*, cross-border movement of goods. The claim he establishes is that the English industrial cycle (1815–70) exhibits a ratchet pattern : \"the maximum for the final period of prosperity before the crisis reappeared as the minimum for the period of prosperity that followed next, only to rise then to a new and much higher maximum.\" In other words, each cyclical trough bottoms out above the previous peak. The data is laid out in sequence. In the prosperous year 1824 , the real or declared value of exports from Great Britain and Ireland was £40,396,300 . With the crisis of 1825 , exports fell below this level, fluctuating between £35m and £39m annually. With the return of prosperity in 1834 , the total rose above the earlier peak to £41,649,191 , and in 1836 reached a new maximum of £53,368,571 . In 1837 it fell again to £42m — and here is the ratchet: \"the new minimum was already higher than the maximum of the previous cycle\" (£40.4m). It then fluctuated between £50m and £53m . Recovery raised the 1844 total to £58[½]m , \"already exceeding the 1836 maximum by far.\" 1845 reached £60,111,082 ; then fell back to somewhat over £57m in 1846 , almost £59m in 1847 , almost £53m in 1848 ; rising to £65[¼]m in 1849 , almost £99m in 1853 , £97m in 1854 , £94[½]m in 1855 , almost £116m in 1856 , with a maximum in 1857 of £122m . In 1858 it fell to £116m , but had already risen in 1859 to £130m , in 1860 to almost £136m , in 1861 to only £125m — \"here again the new minimum is higher than the previous cycle's maximum\" — and in 1863 to £146[½]m .* Marx notes that the same pattern holds for imports, \"which indicate the expansion of the market,\" but insists: \"Here we are concerned only with the scale of production.\" The purpose of this long statistical detour is dialectical. It shows that underneath the credit cycle — with its fictitious bills, its £580,751 reserves, its rediscount chains — there is a real, secular, ratcheting expansion of production. The money-capital phenomena of Section 1 are superstructural ; the export figures are the base . And crucially, the export series also displays cyclical crashes (1825, 1837, 1846–48, 1857, 1861) that align with the credit catastrophes. So the autonomy of loan capital is not absolute: it is a real autonomy that is periodically and violently reabsorbed into material reality. Engels' editorial note (marked \"F. E.\") adds a qualification: the pattern is \"true for England only for the period of its effective industrial monopoly; but it is true in general for the totality of countries with modern large-scale industry, as long as the world market is still expanding.\" This is a significant theoretical claim: the ratchet is not a national peculiarity but a property of capital in its expansive phase, conditional on an expanding world market. If the world market ceases to expand, the ratchet breaks — with consequences Engels does not spell out but which the reader can infer. [Note: several numerals in the scanned source (e.g. \"£58!\", \"£65!\", \"£94!\", \"£146!\") appear to be superscript fractions corrupted by OCR; they are given here as approximately £58½m, £65¼m, £94½m and £146½m.] Section 2 — Transformation of Capital or Revenue into Money That Becomes Loan Capital Having exhausted the non-genuine sources, Marx turns to the genuine case. He defines the terrain negatively first: \"Here we are considering the accumulation of money capital in so far as this does not express a stagnation in the flow of commercial credit, or saving either on the actual circulating medium or on the reserve capital of the agents engaged in reproduction.\" Having excluded the two \"technical\" cases from Section 1, he identifies the residual sources. The first is external and accidental : \"accumulation of money capital may arise through an exceptional influx of gold, as happened in 1852 and 1853 as a result of the new gold mines in Australia and California.\" The mechanism is precise: \"This gold was deposited in the Bank of England. The depositors accepted notes in return, which they did not directly deposit again with bankers. In this way, the circulating medium underwent an extraordinary increase. (Evidence of Weguelin, B.A. 1857, no. 1329.) The Bank tried to valorize these deposits by reducing its discount rate to 2 per cent . In the course of six months in 1853, the quantity of gold piled up in the Bank grew to some £22–£23 million .\" Note that this is not genuine accumulation either — it is an influx of the money commodity that raises the quantity of circulation directly. The Bank's response — cutting the discount rate to 2% — is an attempt to make the deposits yield, i.e. to convert dead metal into valorized capital. This is an early, vivid instance of what Marx elsewhere calls the effect of gold discoveries in lowering the value of money and stimulating credit. The gold is real wealth in one sense, but it is not the product of the reproduction process; it is an addition to the money material that immediately swells loan capital. The Money-Lending Class and the Redistribution of Surplus-Value Marx then makes his central class-theoretic statement of the chapter. \"All money-lending capitalists obviously accumulate directly in the money form, while we have seen that real accumulation by industrial capitalists occurs as a rule through an increase in the elements of reproductive capital itself.\" This is a structural asymmetry: the industrial capitalist accumulates by expanding plant, stock and labour-power (i.e. by adding to productive capital, which must then be monetized to be realized); the money-lending capitalist accumulates by adding coupons , which is immediate and requires no production. From this asymmetry follows a dynamic: \"The development of the credit system and the tremendous concentration of the money-lending business in the hands of big banks must therefore already accelerate in and of itself the accumulation of loanable capital, as a form separate from genuine accumulation.\" Note the causal structure: the credit system's own development is a cause , not merely an effect, of the divergence. Then Marx closes the loop on value theory: \"This rapid development of loan capital is therefore a result of the genuine accumulation, as an effect of the development of the reproduction process, and the profit that forms the source of accumulation for these money capitalists is simply a deduction from the surplus-value that the reproductive agents extract (as well as an appropriation of part of the interest on the savings of others). Loan capital accumulates at the expense of both the industrial and commercial capitalists. \" This is the chapter's most important theoretical assertion about class and value. Banks create nothing. Their accumulation is a transfer — a redistributive claim on the surplus-value of others, plus a cut of the interest on the savings of the non-capitalist classes. This has an immediate implication: the growth of finance capital is not the growth of wealth but a change in its distribution, and it can proceed independently of , and at the expense of, industrial accumulation. Crisis Speculation in Devalued Securities Marx then shows a second, more aggressive form of money-capitalist accumulation: buying distressed assets. \"We have seen how, in the bad phases of the industrial cycle, the rate of interest may rise so high that it temporarily swallows up profits entirely for some branches of business, particularly those unfavourably located.\" Simultaneously, \"the prices of government paper and other securities fall. This is the moment when money capitalists buy up this devalued paper on a massive scale, as it will soon go up again in the later phases, and even rise above its normal level. They will then sell it off, thereby appropriating a part of the public's money capital .\" Three profits are realized here. First, a capital gain on the resale. Second, for \"those securities that are not sold off,\" a higher yield relative to purchase price — \"they yield a higher interest, since they were bought below their price.\" Third, the general counter-cyclical accumulation — \"all the profit which the money capitalists make, and which they turn back into capital, they transform first of all into money capital for loan.\" Hence: \"we already have an accumulation of this money capital as distinct from the genuine accumulation — even if the accumulation of its off-shoot — when we simply consider the money capitalists, bankers, etc. themselves, as the accumulation of this particular class of capitalists.\" The phrase \"even if the accumulation of its off-shoot\" concedes that this is derivative of real accumulation, but insists on its formal autonomy . And the law of motion: \"this must grow with each extension of the credit system, as it accompanies the genuine expansion of the reproduction process.\" Again, accompanies — not causes. Who Bears the Loss: Depositors Versus Banks Marx adds a distributive refinement that anticipates modern analysis of financial losses. \"If the rate of interest is low, this devaluation of money capital falls principally on the depositors and not on the banks .\" When asset values fall, it is the ultimate savers who absorb the loss, because banks have intermediated and matched positions; the depositor's claim is nominally fixed but its purchasing power over capital assets falls. Historical evidence is supplied: \"Before the development of joint-stock banking, three-quarters of all bank deposits in England did not receive any interest. And where interest is now paid, this is at least 1 per cent less than the market rate .\" The banks thus enjoy a structural spread — a margin on intermediation that is pure deduction from the depositing public. Revenue Itself Becomes Loan Capital The final movement of the chapter generalizes the argument. Marx sets aside interest-bearing paper (\"we ignore here the part that is invested in interest-bearing paper and accumulates in this form\") and examines \"the portion that is placed on the market as money capital for loan.\" First, the undistributed profit of industrial capital : \"the section of profit that is not spent as revenue, being rather designed for accumulation, but which the industrial capitalists concerned do not have any immediate employment for in their own businesses.\" This profit \"exists first of all in commodity capital, making up a portion of its value, and is realized in money together with it.\" If it is not immediately reconverted into productive elements, \"it must persist for a certain period in the money form.\" And here is the key dynamic: \"Its amount rises with the volume of the capital itself, even given a declining rate of profit.\" Even in stagnation, the absolute mass of investible surplus grows — because the capital stock on which it is calculated grows. Second, and more strikingly, revenue itself : \"The part to be spent as revenue is gradually consumed, but in the meantime it constitutes loan capital as a deposit with the banker. And so even the growth of the part of the profit spent as revenue is expressed in a gradual and constantly repeated accumulation of loan capital.\" The logic is the time-lag of consumption: the rich do not spend their income instantly; between receipt and expenditure, it sits in a bank and is lent out. Consumption itself therefore generates loan capital at the moment of receipt and destroys it at the moment of spending . Third, Marx generalizes to all revenues: \"this holds good of all revenues, in so far as they are only gradually consumed — i.e. ground-rent , the higher forms of salary , the incomes of the unproductive classes , etc. All of these assume for a time the form of money revenue and can hence be converted into deposits and thereby into loan capital.\" This is a decisive expansion: it shows that the pool of loanable capital is fed not only by capitalist profit and saving but by every monetary receipt in the economy , including rent, salaries and the incomes of the unproductive classes. The credit system is a gigantic machine for skimming the time between income receipt and income expenditure, and for turning that time into someone else's interest-bearing capital. The Yarn Illustration: Money as the \"Value Existence\" of Surplus-Value, and the Minimum Limit The chapter closes with a compressed theoretical statement, illustrated by the spinner. Consider a spinner who has sold his yarn for cotton; the part of the yarn that forms revenue and is exchanged for money aside, the actual existence of his industrial capital is the yarn — which has passed into the possession of the weaver, or even the private consumer. This yarn, \"whether it serves for reproduction or for consumption, is the existence of both capital value and of the surplus-value contained in it.\" The amount of surplus-value transformed into money depends on how much surplus-value the yarn contained — a fact about the production process, not about the money market. But then the decisive step: \"as soon as it is transformed into money, this money is simply the value existence of that surplus-value. And as such it becomes an element of loan capital.\" The concept of value existence (Wertdasein) expresses the ontological shift: surplus-value existed as a physical, tangible, productive thing (yarn) within an industrial capital; upon sale it becomes a pure value magnitude — money — and in that form it is instantly eligible to become loan capital. \"No more is needed for this than that it should be transformed into a deposit, if it has not already been lent out by its owner.\" The final sentence is a materialist caveat that closes the section and, effectively, the chapter: \"To be transformed back into productive capital, on the other hand, it must already have reached a certain minimum limit .\" This is the counterweight to everything that has gone before. Money can become loan capital freely, instantly, with no minimum. But money can become productive capital only in discrete, lumpy amounts — a new machine, a new plant, a new workgang — \"a certain minimum limit.\" Hence the structurally asymmetric ease of the transformation into loan capital versus the difficulty of the transformation into productive capital. This asymmetry is, in the end, the material root of the entire divergence the chapter has traced. Money capital's autonomy from real accumulation is not an illusion of the money market's rhetoric; it is grounded in the money form's infinite divisibility, perfect liquidity and instantaneous transferability as against the lumpiness, illiquidity and slow turnover of the things that actually produce. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter yields several fundamental principles that far exceed its immediate textual occasion. First, the autonomy–dependence paradox of finance. Loan capital is systematically autonomous in form from real accumulation — it can grow through technical banking economies, gold influxes, deposit mobilization, counter-cyclical recycling and the mere time-lag of consumption — yet it remains materially dependent on real accumulation for its ultimate value source. This is not a contradiction but the precise structure of the credit system: the form floats free while the substance remains anchored. Marx's insistence that money capitalists' profit is \"simply a deduction from the surplus-value that the reproductive agents extract\" supplies the anchoring; his catalogue of autonomous growth mechanisms supplies the floating. Second, the systematic dissociation of risk from perception. The rural depositor of the Ipswich passage \"does not have the remotest suspicion\" that his deposit underwrites operations in London and the colonies \"over whose operations neither of them have the slightest control.\" Marx identifies, a century and a half before the vocabulary existed, the mechanism of opacity in securitized intermediation: lengthening chains of claims sever the correspondence between the risk borne and the risk understood, with the consequence that the pool of loan capital expands beyond the capacity of any participant to monitor. Third, the class theory of finance. Bankers, billbrokers and discount houses do not create value; they appropriate portions of surplus-value already extracted in production and portions of the interest on others' savings. The growth of finance capital is therefore a redistributive and potentially parasitic phenomenon, capable of \"devouring credits\" and of diverting capital into \"the most objectionable of all fixed securities.\" Marx simultaneously notes where losses ultimately land — on depositors, not banks — which is a strikingly modern insight into asymmetric absorption of financial devaluation. Fourth, the statistical-methodological lesson. Marx's use of the £580,751 / £22,500,000 ratio makes a permanent point: monetary magnitudes (deposits, loan volume, \"money capital\") are not measures of real wealth. Deposits measure turnover and circulation , not stock of capital ; the £20 lent five times is £100 only if it has mediated real transactions — otherwise it is five paper claims. When monetary aggregates are read as proxies for real accumulation, the analysis becomes precisely the \"credit gibberish\" Marx mocks. The correct measure of genuine accumulation, he insists, is physical: the export and import series with its ten-year cycles and its ratchet. Fifth, the theory of crisis and its ratchet. The export data establish a secular expansion beneath the credit cycle: each cycle's minimum exceeds the previous maximum. Credit autonomy is therefore periodically and violently reabsorbed — the panic of 1825, 1837, 1847, 1857 each shows loan capital collapsing back toward its metallic base (a £580,751 reserve). Engels' editorial qualification — that the ratchet holds \"as long as the world market is still expanding\" — is an implicit warning about what happens when external expansion ceases. The broader implications are considerable. Marx here provides one of the earliest and most rigorous accounts of what contemporary analysis calls shadow banking : the billbrokers who are \"in reality bankers upon an immense scale,\" operating outside formal banking regulation, transforming short-term deposits into longer and riskier claims, and expanding credit \"upon the credit of the bank alone, without reference to the quality of the bills.\" He also sketches a theory of interbank and core-periphery capital flows — the gravitational pull of Lombard Street, the mechanized transfer of rural surplus to metropolitan centres, and the export of that surplus into colonial fixed assets — anticipating modern analyses of global imbalances and capital account dynamics. And he articulates the elementary asymmetry between the instantaneous liquidity of money and the irreducible lumpiness of productive investment (\"a certain minimum limit\"), which explains why credit systems persistently generate pools of loan capital that have no immediate productive employment, and why financial booms must find speculative outlets for money that cannot become capital. The chapter's philosophical core is its insistence on value existence : surplus-value is one thing as a mass of yarn and another as a sum of money, and the transition between them is not merely formal but ontologically consequential. The same surplus-value that was a determinate physical magnitude embedded in a production process becomes, as money, a fungible, mobile, impersonal claim — and, as such, an element of loan capital able to travel the world. The credit system is thus revealed not as a collection of errors but as the objective social machinery through which the money form of value separates itself from the productive body of capital, generating a real autonomy with real speculative consequences, and ultimately reasserting — in crisis — the material reality it appeared to have escaped. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 30 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 32"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 32", "title": "Chapter 32", "subtitle": "Money Capital and Real Capital: III", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 32/Chapter 32 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 11528, "read_time": "58 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital", "Capital Accumulation & Crises"], "keywords": ["Money Capital and Real Capital: III", "Credit Conditions in Different Phases of Industrial Cycle", "Sudden Transition from Easy Credit to Severe Cash Crunch", "Real Accumulation vs Credit Expansion Disconnect", "Bank Reserves Depletion during Over-Trading"], "text": "Chapter 32 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 11,508 words ⏱️ ~58 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE DECONSTRUCTION — MARX, CAPITAL VOL. III, CH. 32: \"MONEY CAPITAL AND REAL CAPITAL: III (CONCLUSION)\" 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 32 closes the three-chapter sequence (Chs. 30–32) in which Marx dismantles the \"currency school\" doctrine — most famously embodied in Lord Overstone (Samuel Jones Loyd) and the Bank Act of 1844 — that money capital and real (industrial) capital are functionally identical, that the money market and the capital market are one and the same, and that a rising rate of interest simply means a scarcity of capital as such . Marx's central thesis in this conclusion is the following: the accumulation of loanable money capital is a distinct and partly autonomous phenomenon from the genuine accumulation of industrial capital , and the two can diverge in either direction — money capital can overflow while real accumulation stalls (a \"plethora\" of loan capital appearing precisely when the barriers of capitalist valorization have been hit), and, conversely, the demand for loan capital can explode at the moment of crisis even while the mass of real capital sits idle, unsaleable, and devalued. Loan capital is therefore not a transparent mirror of productive capital; it is a specific social form with its own laws of motion, its own fictions, and its own capacity to intensify and transmit crisis. The problem the chapter addresses directly is the theoretical ambiguity of the interest rate . Overstone and his allies argue that the rate of interest is determined by the supply and demand of \"capital\" — a term they slip between meaning money, loan capital, and real productive capital at will, using the equivocation to present the money-lender as the only capitalist and his capital as the only capital. Marx, by contrast, insists that the demand for loan capital is not the same as the demand for productive capital, and that the same money can serve many times over as loan capital, generating fictitious titles to value that can expand far beyond the underlying base of metal. The interest rate is thus not a barometer of real accumulation but a highly distorted signal, whose movement across the industrial cycle (low in the recovery phase, rising in prosperity, peaking in the crisis) has to be explained by the peculiar mechanics of the credit system, not by a simplistic \"scarcity of capital.\" Intellectually, this chapter is the culmination of Marx's effort in Part V of Volume 3 to give a rigorous account of interest-bearing capital (zinstragendes Kapital) as a separate form of capital — a form in which capital appears as a commodity whose use-value is the capacity to produce profit — and thereby to show that the distribution of surplus value between interest and profit of enterprise reflects not natural facts but specific social relations of property, mediated by the credit system. The chapter also contains Marx's most concentrated treatment of fictitious capital : money capital that exists only as a claim, as a title to value, not as metal or as actual productive resources. The famous \"£5,000 in deposits\" colloquy with banker Twells (footnote 10) and the observation that \"the same quantity of actual money can therefore represent very different quantities of money capital\" capture this point with unusual precision. Finally, the chapter supplies the diagnosis of monetary crisis that Marx had promised: crises in which the value of commodities is \"sacrificed in order to ensure the fantastic and autonomous existence of this value in money.\" The paragraph beginning \"It is the foundation of capitalist production that money confronts commodities as an autonomous form of value\" is one of Marx's most philosophically loaded passages, articulating the necessary character of monetary crisis under capitalism — the way in which the split between exchange-value and use-value, latent in the commodity form, becomes actual and catastrophic when credit evaporates. The chapter thus fits into the broader theoretical framework in two directions: backwards, as the resolution of the interest/money-market debates of the 1840s–1850s, and forwards, as the foundation for Volume 3's later treatment of crisis. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Claim: Massiveness of Money Capital Is Not Itself Reproductive Capital The chapter opens by restating a conclusion reached in the preceding two chapters and giving it an initial formulation: \"The massive nature of the sum of money which has to be transformed back into capital in this way is the result of the massive scale of the reproduction process; but considered for itself, as money capital for loan, it is not itself a sum of reproductive capital.\" This is the anvil on which the entire chapter is hammered. Marx is drawing a distinction between cause and form . The scale of the money pool in the loan market is a symptom of the scale of reproduction — large reproduction requires large monetary turnover, hence large sums find their way into banks and money markets. But the money itself, as loan capital , is not reproductive capital. It is not machinery, raw material, or labor-power; it is a sum of value in money form, temporarily available for lending. The importance of this distinction becomes apparent when Overstone and others treat any abundance of money capital as evidence of abundant real capital; Marx insists that the money form is a separate moment in the circuit of capital, capable of swelling out of proportion to the productive base it nominally represents. Revenue that Appears as Accumulation of Money Capital Marx immediately identifies what he considers the \"most important thing.\" The expansion of that portion of revenue destined for consumption — the portion that, in the aggregate, simply feeds workers, capitalists, and rentiers — presents itself first of all as an accumulation of money capital . This is a deceptive appearance, and Marx unpacks it step by step. First: the portion of the annual product designed for consumption is in no way capital . A loaf of bread is a use-value to be eaten, not a machine to be operated. Second: a part of this annual product does replace capital — specifically, it replaces the constant capital of the producers of means of consumption (the seed, tools, machinery, etc. needed to bake more bread next year). But in so far as it really is transformed into capital , it exists in kind as the natural form of the revenue of the producers of this constant capital. That is, the wheat sold to the miller is revenue for the farmer , even though it becomes constant capital for the miller. The money that mediates this transaction — the money representing revenue and serving simply to mediate consumption — is regularly transformed, for a certain period , into loanable money capital. It sits in a bank between the sale of the bread and the payment of wages or the purchase of seed. Marx now identifies the dual aspect of this money: in so far as it represents wages, it is at the same time the money form of variable capital ; and in so far as it replaces the constant capital of the producers of means of consumption, it is the money form that their constant capital temporarily assumes , serving to purchase the elements of their constant capital that must be replaced in kind. Crucially: \"Neither in one form nor the other does it in itself represent accumulation, even though its volume grows with the scale of the reproduction process. But it temporarily performs the function of money for loan, i.e. of money capital.\" This is the first great displacement effect Marx wants to establish. Money does not have to be new accumulation to function as loan capital; money that is simply passing through the system, mediating existing reproduction, becomes loan capital for the interval. The loan market thereby swells with resources that are not net new capital at all. From this follows the chapter's central critical lemma: \"the accumulation of money capital must always reflect a greater accumulation of capital than is actually taking place, in so far as the expansion of individual consumption, because mediated by money, appears as an accumulation of money capital, since it supplies the money form for genuine accumulation, for money that initiates new capital investments.\" The illusion is structural, not accidental. Because consumption is mediated by money, and because money passes through the loan market, expansion of consumption looks from the loan market like expansion of capital . The Intermediary Function of the Banker Marx then sharpens the point in institutional terms. The accumulation of loanable money capital partly represents \"nothing more than the fact that all money into which industrial capital is transformed in the course of its circuit assumes the form, not of money that the reproductive agents advance but of money that they borrow.\" In a developed credit system, the money that productive agents need to complete their circuit is advanced by someone else — it is borrowed. On the basis of commercial credit, \"one party lends another the money that he needs in the reproduction process.\" But this takes the institutional form that the banker , to whom one section of reproductive agents lends money, lends it in turn to the other section — so that \"the banker appear[s] as the benefactor; while at the same time disposal over this capital passes entirely to the bankers as intermediaries.\" Here Marx is describing the emergence of what would later be called the deposit multiplier and the intermediation function of banking, though in his own terms: the banker's position as the meeting point of lender-depositors and borrower-industrialists gives him decisive power over the disposal of social capital, even though the money is not his. Three Special Forms of Money-Capital Accumulation Marx now enumerates special forms in which money capital accumulates, forms that further detach loan-capital accumulation from genuine accumulation. First special form: release of capital via falling prices. \"Capital may be set free, for example, by a fall in the price of the elements of production, raw materials, etc.\" If the industrialist cannot immediately expand production (say, because his plant is already fully utilized or the market for his product won't absorb more), the money that formerly would have gone to buy raw materials at the higher price is now ejected from his circuit as superfluous and becomes loanable money capital. The capital is released from real functioning and pushed into the loan market. Second special form: interruptions to business. \"Capital is released in the money form, particularly in the case of the merchant, as soon as there are any interruptions to business.\" If the merchant completes a series of deals and there is a gap before the next series, the money realized represents for him a hoard, superfluous capital . Simultaneously and immediately, it represents an accumulation of loan capital. Marx now draws the decisive analytical conclusion from the contrast between these two cases: \"In the first case, the accumulation of money capital expresses the repetition of the reproduction process under more favourable conditions, the genuine release of a portion of capital previously tied up, enabling the reproduction process to be expanded with the same monetary means. In the second case, on the other hand, there is simply an interruption in the flow of transactions. But in both cases, money is transformed into loanable money capital, representing an accumulation of it, and has the same effect on the money market and the rate of interest , even though in the one case the genuine accumulation process is promoted, while in the other case it is inhibited.\" The italicized emphasis is Marx's: the same money-market effect (a lowering of the rate of interest) can accompany either an expansion or a contraction of real accumulation. The rate of interest, therefore, is not a reliable index of real economic activity. Third special form: withdrawal of the wealth-holders. \"Finally, accumulation of money capital is effected by various people who have feathered their nests and withdrawn from the reproduction process.\" These are the retired capitalists, the rentiers, the recipients of fixed interest and dividends — people who receive shares of surplus value and hold them as claims, without participating in production. \"The greater the profits made in the course of the industrial cycle, the more of these people there are.\" The conclusion Marx draws is subtle: \"In this case the accumulation of capital for loan expresses on the one hand a genuine accumulation (in its relative volume); on the other hand simply the degree to which industrial capitalists are transformed into simple money capitalists.\" This is a moment of class differentiation: productive capitalists become, in part, mere moneyed proprietors, their capital shedding its productive function and taking on the pure form of rentier wealth — a transformation that swells loan capital without adding anything to the productive base. Profit Destined for Re-Investment and Its Two Obstacles Marx now shifts to the other portion of profit — that not destined for consumption as revenue. This portion is transformed into money capital \"only if it cannot be directly used to expand business in the sphere of production in which the profit was made.\" Two reasons: (a) Satiation: the sphere is saturated with capital. There is no profitable outlet left within the existing branch. (b) Threshold of scale: \"in order to function as capital, the accumulation must first have attained a certain volume, according to the due proportions for investment of new capital in this particular business.\" In many lines of production, a new plant requires a minimum capital outlay. Accumulated profit below that threshold has to sit idle temporarily and can only be parked as loan capital. In either case the money is first transformed into money capital for loan and \"serves to expand production in other spheres.\" Taking all circumstances equal, the amount of profit destined for transformation back into capital will depend on the amount of profit made, hence on the expansion of reproduction itself. But Marx now makes his plethora argument: \"if this new accumulation comes up against difficulties of application, against a lack of spheres of investment, i.e. if branches of production are saturated and loan capital is over-supplied, this plethora of loanable money capital proves nothing more than the barriers of capitalist production.\" This is a decisive theoretical point. The apparent abundance of capital — a \"plethora\" — is not over-abundance relative to needs but over-abundance relative to the rate of return that capital demands. The \"resulting credit swindling demonstrates that there is no positive obstacle to the use of this excess capital. But there is an obstacle set up by its own laws of valorization, by the barriers within which capital can valorize itself as capital.\" Capital can only be used if it can yield profit at the prevailing rate; when it cannot, it lies idle or is pushed into speculative ventures. Critically: \"A plethora of money capital as such does not necessarily signify overproduction, or even a lack of spheres of employment for capital.\" Loan Capital Accumulation vs. Genuine Transformation into Capital Marx then gives a compact restatement: \"The accumulation of loan capital simply means that money is precipitated as loanable money. This process is very different from a genuine transformation into capital; it is simply the accumulation of money in a form in which it can be transformed into capital.\" Once again the modal qualification (\"can be\") is critical. Precipitation as loan capital is a mere formal precondition of investment, not investment itself. Furthermore, \"this accumulation can express elements that are very different from genuine accumulation.\" In a period of expanding genuine accumulation, the swelling of money capital can be: - In part the result of that accumulation; - In part the accompaniment of factors quite different from it; - In part even the result of blockages in genuine accumulation. This three-fold taxonomy is essential. The same statistical phenomenon — a growing pool of loan capital — can have three entirely different causes with entirely different implications. That is why the loan market can run ahead of or behind the industrial cycle: \"The very fact that the accumulation of loan capital is augmented by these elements that are independent of genuine accumulation, even if they accompany it, must lead to a regular plethora of money capital at certain phases of the cycle, and this plethora develops as the credit system improves.\" The phrase \"must lead\" is unambiguous: the phenomenon is systematic , not contingent. As credit improves, the amplification grows. \"At the same time as this, there develops the need to pursue the production process beyond its capitalist barriers: too much trade, too much production, too much credit. This must also happen always in forms that bring about a reaction.\" The \"reaction\" is crisis. So the same mechanisms that make loan capital swell beyond real accumulation also drive production to overshoot, and the overshoot has its own inherent corrective — the crash. The Savings of Others as the Capitalist's Capital Marx turns briefly to \"the accumulation of money capital from ground-rent, wages, etc.\" and declines to treat it in detail; but he makes one sharply polemical point. \"As capitalist production and its division of labour progress, the job of genuine saving and abstinence (by hoarders), in so far as this supplies elements of accumulation, is left to those who receive the minimum of such elements, and often enough lose what they have saved, as workers do when banks collapse.\" This is a bitter reference to the \"abstinence\" theory of capital (Senior) and a class-level observation: the rhetoric of saving and abstinence is applied to those with the least to save. Meanwhile, \"For the industrial capitalist does not 'save' his capital but rather disposes of the savings of others in proportion to the size of this capital; while the money capitalist makes the savings of other people into his capital, and the credit that the reproductive capitalists give one another, and that the public give them, he makes into his own source of private enrichment.\" This culminates in one of the chapter's two most famous declarations: \"The final illusion of the capitalist system, that capital is the offspring of a person's own work and savings, is thereby demolished. Not only does profit consist in the appropriation of other people's labour, but the capital with which this labour of others is set in motion and exploited consists of other people's property, which the money capitalist puts at the disposal of the industrial capitalist and for which he in turn exploits him.\" The critique here is twofold : not only is profit (the return on capital) derived from the exploitation of alien labor, but the capital itself (the principal) is alien property. Property and labor are thereby distinguished from one another entirely — the capitalist class does not work, yet disposes of the wealth produced by others. Credit Capital: How Often the Same Money Serves as Loan Capital Marx now takes up \"credit capital\" directly. \"How often the same piece of money can serve as loan capital depends entirely\" on three factors: (1) The frequency of its realization of commodity values in sale or payment — \"i.e. transfers capital, as well as how often it realizes revenue.\" How often it comes into someone else's hands as realized value, \"whether that of capital or of revenue, hence depends evidently on the scale and volume of real transactions.\" (2) Economy in payments and the development and organization of the credit system. The more payments cancel each other out (clearing), the less metal actually needs to circulate — so a single unit of money accomplishes many more transactions. This is an explicit development of Marx's earlier insight that credit and clearing substitute for money circulation. (3) The linkage and speed of action of credits — \"so that if the money is precipitated at one point as a deposit, it is immediately sent out again as a loan.\" The velocity of loan capital, so to speak, multiplies the effect of any given deposit. These three factors together mean that the nominal quantity of loan capital in an economy can be manifold more than the quantity of money in existence. Marx is describing, in 19th-century form, what we would now call the velocity of money and the credit multiplier . Fictitious Capital: Loan Capital as Mere Title to Value Now comes the most philosophically rich passage on fictitious capital in Marx's corpus. Even granting that loan capital exists in the form of actual money — gold or silver — \"a large portion of this money capital is still necessarily always merely fictitious, i.e. a title to value, just like value tokens.\" Marx's reasoning: \"In as much as money functions in the circuit of capital, it certainly forms money capital at one point, but it is not transformed into loanable money capital; it is rather exchanged for the elements of productive capital, or paid out as a means of circulation when revenue is realized, so that it cannot be transformed into loan capital for its possessor.\" Money performing its function in the circuit cannot simultaneously serve as someone else's loan. \"In so far as it is transformed into loan capital and the same money repeatedly represents loan capital, it is still clear that it only exists at one point as metal money; at all other points it exists simply in the form of a claim to capital.\" This is the key: what appears as a multiplicity of money capitals is in fact one sum of metal plus many claims against it. The accumulation of these claims \"arises from a genuine accumulation... and yet the accumulation of these claims or titles as such is still different both from the genuine accumulation from which it arises and from the future accumulation (the new production process) which is mediated by the lending of money.\" Three distinct things: (i) genuine accumulation; (ii) the accumulation of paper titles, itself a separate phenomenon; (iii) the future accumulation these titles finance. A confusion of the three is the source of the currency-school error. Footnote 9 — which belongs integratedly here — brings in the Twells/Overstone evidence. The banker Twells testifies: \"As a banker, do you deal in capital or in money? — We deal in money.\" \"How are the deposits paid into your bank? — In money.\" \"How are they paid out? — In money.\" \"Then can they be called anything else but money? — No.\" The naive identification is exposed. Overstone's \"persistent confusion between 'capital' and 'money'\" (already noted in Ch. 26) is then catalogued: his \"value of money\" is conflated with interest, and his 4140 remark \"The use of the word 'capital' is very dangerous\" reveals the unease. His 4148 statement — bullion export diminishes the quantity of money, \"and a diminution of the quantity of money in this country must of course create a pressure upon the money-market generally\" — expresses the currency-school axiom. And Marx wryly notes: \"since in the meantime it has been incontrovertibly demonstrated even for him that the quantity of money in circulation does not determine prices, it is now the reduction in money as means of circulation that is supposed to raise its value as interest-bearing capital, and hence the rate of interest.\" The theoretical tourniquet is exposed: having conceded that quantity doesn't set commodity prices, Overstone postulates that quantity does set interest. Loan Capital as Money, Then Claim to Money Marx continues the fictitious-capital analysis. \"Prima facie, loan capital always exists in the form of money\" — but only at the moment of loan , and only in the hands of the borrower : \"later as a claim to money, since the money in which it originally existed is now in the hands of the borrower in the actual money form. For the lender, it has been transformed into a claim to money, into an ownership title.\" Formally the lender has capital; substantially he holds a claim. From this, the crucial consequence: \"The same quantity of actual money can therefore represent very different quantities of money capital.\" \"Mere money, whether it represents realized capital or realized revenue, becomes loan capital by the simple act of lending it out, by its transformation into a deposit, if we consider the general form in the developed credit system. The deposit is money capital for the depositor. But in the hands of the banker it may only be potential money capital, lying idle in his safe instead of in that of its owner.\" This introduces footnote 10 , Marx's most extended concrete illustration of the double-counting of money capital. Banker Twells gives an example: \"If I begin business with £10,000, I buy with £5,000 commodities and put them into warehouse. I deposit the other £5,000 with a banker, to draw upon it and use it as I require. I consider it still £10,000 capital to me, though £5,000 is in the shape of deposits or money.\" The subsequent colloquy — reproduced by Marx as evidence — proceeds: (4531) \"You have parted with your £5,000 of notes to somebody else? — Yes.\" (4532) \"Then he has £5,000 of deposits? — Yes.\" (4533) \"And you have £5,000 of deposits left? — Exactly.\" (4534) \"He has £5,000 in money, and you have £5,000 in money? — Yes.\" (4535) \"But it is nothing but money at last? — No.\" Here the £5,000 has doubled as money capital. Marx's resolution: A can dispose of his deposit \"as if he still had it\" — so far as he is concerned, it functions as potential money. But whenever he draws on it, \"he destroys his deposit pro tanto.\" If his money has since been lent again, he is paid with \"the money someone else has deposited.\" If A pays B by cheque, and both cheques are cleared between banks, \"the money that A deposited has performed money functions twice: firstly, in the hands of the person who received the money that A had deposited; secondly, in the hands of A himself. In the second function, there is an adjustment of claims... without the intervention of money. Here the deposit has a double effect as money, i.e. first as actual money and subsequently as a claim to money. Mere claims to money can only take the place of money in the balancing of claims.\" Growth of the Money-Capitalist Class and the Fictitious Securities Market Marx now traces the social consequences: \"As material wealth increases, the class of money capitalists grows. On the one hand there is an increase in the number and wealth of the retired capitalists, the rentiers; and secondly the credit system must be further developed, which means an increase in the number of bankers, money-lenders, financiers, etc.\" Alongside this, \"the volume of interest-bearing paper, government paper, shares, etc. also expands.\" The result is a dual growth: on the demand side, the \"jobbers who speculate in this paper play a major role in the money market.\" Marx then addresses a possible objection. If all purchases and sales of paper were simply \"the expression of genuine capital investment,\" one could argue they have no effect on the demand for loan capital — A sells paper, B buys it; the money position nets out. But Marx points out even then, \"in view of the fact that the paper certainly exists, while the capital that it originally represented does not (at least not as money capital), a new demand for money capital of this kind is always created to that extent.\" The mere existence of claims against capital creates a demand for capital to service those claims. And \"at all events it is then money capital, which was previously at B's disposition, and is now at A's.\" Chapman's Testimony: All Convertible Securities Affect the Rate Footnote and interlude: B.A. 1857, No. 4886. Question: is it correct to say the rate of discount is fixed by \"the quantity of capital on the market, which is applicable to the discount of mercantile bills, as distinguished from other classes of securities?\" Chapman answers: \"No, I think that the question of interest is affected by all convertible securities of a current character ; it would be wrong to limit it simply to the discount of bills, because it would be absurd to say that when there is a great demand for money upon (the deposit of) Consols, or even upon Exchequer bills, as has ruled very much of late, at a rate much higher than the commercial rate, our commercial world is not affected by it; it is very materially affected by it.\" He gives the arbitrage logic: \"I can hardly expect a man to let me have money at 5 per cent upon commercial bills, if he can lend his money at the same moment at 6 per cent upon Consols... a man can hardly expect me to discount bills at 5½ per cent, if I can lend my money at 6 per cent.\" Then in 4892 he explains who actually drives the money market: \"We do not talk of investors who buy their £2,000, or £5,000, or £10,000, as affecting the money-market materially... I allude to people, who deal in hundreds of thousands of pounds, who are what are called jobbers, who take large portions of loans, or make purchases on the market, and have to hold that stock till the public take it off their hands at a profit; these men, therefore, want money.\" A concentrated dealer class, not the dispersed public, sets the marginal demand. Concentrated Money Markets and the Brood of Gamblers Marx generalizes: \"With the development of the credit system, large and concentrated money markets are created, as in London, which are at the same time the major seats of dealings in these securities. The bankers put the public's money capital at the disposal of this gang of dealers on a massive scale, and so the brood of gamblers multiplies.\" The Governor of the Bank of England, cited before the House of Lords Secret Committee (C.D. 1848, printed 1857, no. 219), confirms: \"Money upon the Stock Exchange is, generally speaking, cheaper than it is elsewhere.\" This is because the stock exchange enjoys privileged access to loan capital, subsidized by the banking system's hold over public deposits. The Average Rate of Interest Is Determined by the Average Rate of Profit Marx now returns to a point established earlier (Ch. 22, footnoted here): \"the average interest over a period of several years is determined, other things being equal, by the average rate of profit, and not by profit of enterprise, which is no more than profit minus interest.\" The distinction between profit and profit of enterprise is crucial: the total profit yielded by capital is split, via the money-market mechanism, into interest and profit of enterprise. Average interest tracks average profit (the whole), since both components are claims on the same total. The variation of commercial interest over the industrial cycle — \"the interest charged by money-lenders for discounting and loans within the world of trade\" — shows a phase in which interest rises above its minimum and reaches the average medium level (then exceeds it), \"this movement being the result of a rise in profits.\" Two Critical Observations on Interest and Profit Marx flags \"two things... to be noted here.\" (1) Prolonged high interest as prima facie evidence of high profit — but not high profit of enterprise. If the interest rate remains high for a long period (particularly in a country like England with a long-standing average rate expressed in long-term loans — \"what we might call private interest\"), \"this is prima facie evidence that the rate of profit during this period is also high, but it does not necessarily prove that the rate of profit of enterprise is high.\" The distinction vanishes for capitalists working predominantly with their own capital — \"they realize the high rate of profit, since they pay their interest to themselves.\" But for the borrowing capitalist, \"this high rate of profit, after deducting the high rate of interest, leaves only a low rate of profit of enterprise. The latter may contract, while the high rate of profit continues.\" This is possible because \"enterprises once embarked upon must be continued.\" Once a factory is built, the capital is sunk; the capitalist must go on paying interest even if the residual profit thins. And here is the speculative hazard: \"In this phase, operations are conducted largely with credit capital (other people's); and the high rate of profit may in places be speculative and prospective. It is possible to pay interest at a high rate with a high rate of profit but a declining profit of enterprise. It can be paid — and this is partly the case in periods of speculation — not out of profits but out of the borrowed capital itself, and this situation can last a good while.\" The payment of interest out of capital rather than out of profit — the Ponzi structure of speculative finance — is identified here as a phase of the cycle , not an aberration. (2) Demand for money capital ≠ demand for industrial capital. \"To say that the demand for money capital and hence the interest rate rises because the profit rate is high is not the same as saying that the demand for industrial capital rises and that this is why the interest rate is high.\" The two could diverge sharply — in crisis, demand for loan capital peaks while demand for industrial capital collapses: \"In times of crisis the demand for loan capital, and with it the interest rate, reaches its maximum; the rate of profit as good as disappears, and with it the demand for industrial capital. In times such as these everyone borrows simply to pay, to settle commitments already entered into.\" Demand here is for means of payment , not for means of production. Conversely, in the revival phase — \"the period when business revives after the crisis\" — loan capital is demanded \"in order to buy, and to transform the money capital into productive or commercial capital. And then it is demanded either by the industrial capitalist or by the merchant. The industrial capitalist invests it in means of production and labour-power.\" So the same high interest rate can express totally different economic states. Wages and the Interest Rate Marx now addresses a specific confusion. \"The rising demand for labour-power can never be in itself a reason for a rising rate of interest, in so far as this is determined by the profit rate. Higher wages are never a cause of higher profit, even though, taking particular phases of the industrial cycle, they may be one of its results.\" The point: if wages rise, they reduce the profit rate (assuming the working day and intensity are given, higher wages eat into surplus value). They cannot by themselves push the interest rate up via the profit channel — because that channel runs the wrong way. But: \"The demand for labour-power may increase because the exploitation of labour is proceeding under particularly favourable conditions, but the rising demand for labour-power and hence for variable capital does not in and of itself increase profits but rather reduces them in proportion. Yet the demand for variable capital may increase, and thus also the demand for money capital, while this in turn increases the rate of interest. The market price of labour-power then rises above its average, a more than average number of workers come to be employed, while at the same time the rate of interest rises, because the demand for money capital rises in these conditions.\" So there can be a correlation between rising wages and rising interest — but the causal arrow goes through the demand for money capital , not through the profit rate. \"An increased demand for labour-power raises the price of this commodity just like any other, without raising profits, which depend principally on the relative cheapness of this particular commodity. At the same time, however, under the conditions we have assumed, this demand raises the rate of interest, by increasing the demand for money capital.\" Marx draws out the consequence with an analytic counterfactual: \"If the money capitalist, instead of lending out money, should transform himself into an industrialist, the fact that he has to pay more for labour would not in and of itself increase his profit but would rather lead to a proportionate reduction in it.\" The role of lender or the role of industrialist does not alter the underlying logic: higher wages mean lower profit. \"The combination of circumstances may still be such that his profit rises, but this is never because he pays more for labour.\" Finally: \"If wages rise for whatever reason, in otherwise unfavourable conjunctures, the rise in wages causes the profit rate to fall, although the rate of interest rises to the extent that the wage rise increases the demand for money capital.\" This is a foundational statement of the non-identity of interest and profit rate: they can move in opposite directions. Overstone's \"Demand for Capital\" Dissected Marx now zeroes in on Overstone. \"Apart from labour, what Overstone calls the 'demand for capital' consists simply of the demand for commodities. The demand for commodities raises their price, whether this demand rises above the average, or the supply falls below its average. If the industrial capitalist or merchant now has to pay £150, for example, for the same quantity of commodities for which he formerly paid £100, he would have to borrow £150 instead of £100 and would therefore have to pay £7½, at a 5 per cent rate of interest, instead of £5. The amount of interest he has to pay rises, because of the rise in the amount of capital borrowed.\" Note carefully: the rate of interest is unchanged at 5%; the sum of interest rises only because the sum borrowed rises. Overstone's \"demand for capital\" is thus either the demand for commodities (a price effect, not a capital effect) or the demand for labor (which, as just shown, pressures labor costs upward but not profit rates). Marx states his primary polemical target precisely: \"Mr Overstone's entire aim is to present the interests of loan capital and industrial capital as identical, while his Bank Act is precisely calculated to exploit the difference between these interests for the benefit of money capital.\" This is one of the chapter's most characteristic dialectical formulations: Overstone's theory (loan capital = industrial capital) is a mask for his practice (the 1844 Bank Act, with its separation of Issue and Banking Departments and its rigid limits on note issue, tightens the money market precisely in moments when industrial capital most needs relief). The user-ideology is at odds with the user's own institutional design. Commodity Supply, Speculation, and the Rate of Interest Marx now refines the analysis of how commodity-market conditions affect the loan market. Case 1: supply has fallen below average. \"It is possible that the demand for commodities... absorbs no more money capital than before. The same sum, and perhaps a smaller one, has to be paid for their overall value, but a smaller quantity of use-values is received for this sum.\" So the quantity of use-values falls but the sum of value (money) may be the same or lower — hence \"the demand for loanable money capital remains the same, i.e. the interest rate will not rise, even though the demand for commodities has risen in relation to their supply and the commodities' price has therefore risen as well. The rate of interest can only be affected when the total demand for loan capital grows, and under the above assumptions this is not the case.\" This is a precise analytical wedge: price rises of commodities do not by themselves raise the rate of interest ; only the demand for loan capital does. Case 2: supply fallen below average with speculation . \"As is the case with a harvest failure in corn, cotton, etc., while the demand for loan capital grows on the speculation that prices will rise still higher, the most immediate means to make them rise being to withdraw a part of the supply temporarily from the market. In order to pay for the commodities bought without selling them, gold is obtained by way of commercial 'bill of exchange operations'. In this case, the demand for loan capital grows, and the rate of interest may rise as a result of this attempt to block the supply of commodities to the market artificially. The higher rate of interest then expresses an artificial reduction in the supply of commodity capital.\" The interest-rate rise here expresses deliberate stock-hiding , not scarcity of real capital. Case 3: supply has increased, price below average. \"The demand for loan capital may remain the same or even fall, because more commodities are to be had with the same sum of money. There could also be a speculative formation of stocks, partly for use at a favourable moment for the purpose of production, partly in expectation of a later rise in price. In this case, the demand for loan capital could grow, so that the higher rate of interest would be an expression of capital investment in the formation of excessive stocks of the elements of productive capital.\" Marx then states the methodological frame for all of this: \"All we are considering here is the demand for loan capital as it is influenced by the demand and supply for commodity capital. We have already explained earlier how the changing condition of the reproduction process in the various phases of the industrial cycle affects the supply of loan capital.\" Then the critical attack: \"The trivial statement that the market rate of interest is determined by the supply and demand for (loan) capital is cunningly conflated by Overstone with his own assumption in which loan capital is identical with capital in general, and he seeks in this way to transform the money-lender into the only capitalist and his capital into the only capital.\" This is the ideological function of a seemingly neutral formula: the tautology (interest = supply-demand of loan capital) is inflated to a metaphysical identification (loan capital = capital), and the money-lender is thereby enthroned as the unique capitalist. Demand for Means of Payment in Times of Pressure \"In times of pressure, the demand for loan capital is a demand for means of payment and nothing more than this; in no way is it a demand for money as means of purchase. The interest rate can then rise very high, irrespective of whether real capital — productive and commodity capital — is abundant or scarce.\" Here is the theoretical core of Marx's account of the panic: what lenders are being asked for is not means by which goods may be bought , but means by which debts may be paid . Real capital may be abundant; still the panic drives interest to usurious heights. \"The demand for means of payment is simply a demand for convertibility into money, in so far as the merchants and producers are able to offer good security; it is a demand for money capital, in so far as this is not the case, i.e. in so far as an advance of means of payment gives them not only the money form, but also the equivalent that they lack for payments, in whatever form this might be.\" Distinction drawn: (a) holders of sound securities want only the liquid form; (b) holders of unsound positions want real resources to cover losses. Both Sides of the Crisis Controversy Are Right and Wrong \"This is the point at which both sides in the present controversy in the theory of crises are simultaneously right and wrong.\" Marx gives the dialectical verdict: Those who say there is simply a lack of means of payment are right if they mean the owners of bona fide securities; but \"they are fools who believe it the duty of a bank, and within its power, to transform every bankrupt swindler into a solvent capitalist by means of paper tokens.\" Those who say there is simply a lack of capital are right in a tautological sense (inconvertible capital is present on a massive scale due to over-importing and overproduction), and yet in a substantive sense wrong: \"or else they are just referring to those credit-jobbers who actually are put in a position where they can no longer obtain other people's capital to operate with and then demand that the bank should not only help them pay for the capital lost but also enable them to continue their swindling.\" The dialectical point: each side seizes one aspect — lack of the money form vs. lack of usable capital — and forgets the other. Both relate to a moment in the crisis in which the formal and the real aspects of capital come apart and confront each other as enemies. The Foundation: Money as Autonomous Form of Value Marx now rises to the most general plane. \"It is the foundation of capitalist production that money confronts commodities as an autonomous form of value, or that exchange-value must obtain an autonomous form in money, and this is possible only if one particular commodity becomes the material in whose value all other commodities are measured, this thereby becoming the universal commodity, the commodity par excellence, in contrast to all other commodities.\" This is the categorial derivation of money from the commodity-form, restated at the concrete level of crisis. \"This must show itself in two ways, particularly in developed capitalist countries, which replace money to a large extent either by credit operations or by credit money.\" First: \"In times of pressure, when credit contracts or dries up altogether, money suddenly confronts commodities absolutely as the only means of payment and the true existence of value. Hence the general devaluation of commodities and the difficulty or even impossibility of transforming them into money, i.e. into their own purely fantastic form.\" The commodity's \"own purely fantastic form\" is money — the value-stuff of the commodity, detached from its useful body. In crisis, that detachment becomes a chasm: goods pile up unsold because no one has money. Second: \"credit money is itself only money in so far as it absolutely represents real money to the sum of its nominal value. With the drain of gold, its convertibility into money becomes problematic, i.e. its identity with actual gold. Hence we get forcible measures, putting up the rate of interest, etc. in order to guarantee the conditions of this convertibility.\" Credit money has a structural dependence on metal — not because metal is magically necessary for domestic exchange, but because the credit system's promises to convert are honored only as long as the underlying metal reserve holds. \"This can be more or less intensified by erroneous legislation based on incorrect theories of money and enforced on the nation in the interest of money-dealers such as Overstone and company. But the basis for it is provided by the basis of the mode of production itself.\" The 1844 Bank Act intensifies the crisis, but it does not create the crisis's monetary form; the monetary form is grounded in the capitalist relation itself. The Sacrifice of Commodities to Money \"A devaluation of credit money (not to speak of a complete loss of its monetary character, which is in any case purely imaginary) would destroy all the existing relationships. The value of commodities is thus sacrificed in order to ensure the fantastic and autonomous existence of this value in money. In any event, a money value is only guaranteed as long as money itself is guaranteed. This is why many millions' worth of commodities have to be sacrificed for a few millions in money.\" This is one of Marx's most compressed formulas for crisis: the form of value (money) is preserved by destroying the substance of value (commodities). Warehouses of goods are dumped, factories are closed, and the metal stock is preserved — the sacrifice of wealth in its material form to wealth in its money form. \"This is unavoidable in capitalist production, and forms one of its particular charms.\" The irony is cutting: the author identifies with grim satisfaction a structural feature that bourgeois political economy presents as either an anomaly to be legislated away (currency school) or a benign \"oversight\" (laissez-faire). \"In former modes of production, this does not happen, because given the narrow basis on which these move, neither credit nor credit money is able to develop. As long as the social character of labour appears as the monetary existence of the commodity and hence as a thing outside actual production, monetary crises, independent of real crises or as an intensification of them, are unavoidable.\" The condition — \"the social character of labour appears as the monetary existence of the commodity\" — is the fetish form of the commodity: labor is social only indirectly, through the market, and the market's universal equivalent (money) is the surface on which the sociality of labor is tested. Where that test fails, monetary crisis is inevitable. The Bank's Power to Alleviate or Intensify \"It is evident on the other hand that, as long as a bank's credit is not undermined, it can alleviate the panic in such cases by increasing its credit money, whereas it increases this panic by contracting credit.\" This is a crucial practical corollary. A solvent bank, confronting a panic, can issue more credit money (banknotes, drafts, acceptances) and relieve the pressure of payment — as long as its credit is not itself in question . If it contracts instead, it deepens the panic. This point is aimed at the 1844 Act, which forces the Bank of England to contract precisely when the panic demands expansion. \"The entire history of modern industry shows that metal would be required only to settle international trade and its temporary imbalances, if production at home were organized.\" This is perhaps the single most radical practical implication of Marx's analysis — an anticipation, in germ, of the argument for planned production and managed money that would be developed by later Marxist and social-democratic writers. \"The suspension of cash payments by the so-called national banks, which is resorted to as the sole expedient in all extreme cases, shows that even now no metal money is needed at home.\" The Bank Act's own suspension clauses, invoked in every crisis, refute the Act's theory. The Balance of Payments vs. the Balance of Trade Marx now addresses an international-relations point — the distinction between the balance of payments and the balance of trade. \"It would be ridiculous to say of two individuals that they both have an unfavourable balance of payments in their dealings with one another. If they are each mutually debtor and creditor, it is clear that when their claims do not balance, one of them must be debtor to the other for the remainder. With nations, this is in no way the case. And this fact is recognized by all economists in the statement that the balance of payments may be favourable or unfavourable for a country, even though the balance of trade must ultimately balance out.\" The crucial difference: between two individuals, a defined settlement can occur; between nations, settlement dates, capital flows, and gold transfers create a persistent asymmetry. \"The balance of payments is distinct from the balance of trade in that it is that balance of trade which must be settled at a particular date. The effect of crises, then, is to compress the difference between the balance of payments and the balance of trade into a short period of time; and the specific conditions that develop in nations affected by a crisis, and hence by the arrival of this date of payment, already involve a contraction of this kind in the settlement period.\" Crisis means a sudden synchronization of all payment dates across nations. The Chain of Crisis Transmission Marx provides a step-by-step list of crisis transmission internationally: Shipment of precious metals abroad — gold flows out to close trade gaps. Forced selling of goods sent on consignment — foreign merchants holding goods sell them at any price. Export of commodities in order to sell them off cheaply or obtain money advances on them at home — a nation ships goods abroad and monetizes them via credit at home. Recall of credit — lenders call in loans across national borders. Fall in security values — stock and bond markets devalue. Forced selling of foreign securities — capital is repatriated by dumping foreign assets. Attraction of foreign capital to invest in these devalued securities — foreign buyers pick up bargains, reversing gold flows. Bankruptcy, which settles a whole series of claims — final resolution by cancellation. \"In this connection, metal is often still sent to the country where the crisis has broken out, because drafts on it are uncertain and payment in metal more secure.\" When credit is uncertain, gold is the ultimate settlement instrument. \"Added to this is the fact that, in relation to Asia, all capitalist countries are generally debtors simultaneously, either directly or indirectly. Once these various factors exert their full effect on the other countries involved, these too experience an export of gold or silver, i.e. their payments fall due, and the same phenomenon is repeated.\" The Asiatic trade balance (Europe's chronic deficit with Asia, especially in silver) makes the crisis general and transnational. Commercial Credit and the Price of Commodities Marx now makes a technical point about commercial credit. \"In the case of commercial credit, interest, as the difference between the credit price and the cash price, is involved in the price of a commodity only in so far as bills of exchange have a longer term than usual. In other cases not. And this is explained by the fact that each person takes this credit from one direction and extends it in another.\" In other words, short-term trade credit typically nets out at each business's level — the businessman receives and extends credit on roughly the same terms, so its cost washes out. Engels's famous editorial note appears here: \"(This does not tally with my own experience. — F. E.)\" — an illuminating instance of Marx's collaborator registering disagreement with the master, and a reminder that Volume 3 was assembled from manuscripts. Marx continues: \"But in so far as discounting is involved here in this way, it is not governed by this commercial credit, but rather by the money market.\" The banker's discount rate, not the bilateral credit terms of trade, determines the cost — and that rate is set by the money market, i.e. the aggregate supply and demand for loan capital. The Cotton Example (1844–1845) Marx now provides an empirical reductio ad absurdum . \"If the demand and supply of money capital, which determines the rate of interest, were identical with the demand and supply of real capital, as Overstone maintains, then according to whether we considered various different commodities or the same commodity at different stages (raw material, semi-finished goods, finished product), interest would have to be both low and high at the same time.\" He then supplies the evidence: Bank of England interest rate: - 1844: 4 per cent (January–September), 2½–3 per cent (November–December). - 1845: 2½, 2¾, 3 per cent (January–October), then between 3 and 5 per cent (remaining months). Prices: - Average price of fair Orleans cotton: 6⅛ d. in 1844, 4¾ d. in 1845. Stocks: - Liverpool cotton stock, 3 March 1844: 627,042 bales . - Liverpool cotton stock, 3 March 1845: 773,800 bales . The analytical inference: Cotton price was low in 1845 — which, on the \"loan capital = real capital\" thesis, should have meant low interest. It was indeed low for most of the year. But on the same thesis, judged by the price of yarn, interest should have been high , because yarn prices were relatively high and profits absolutely so. The same commodity at different stages of its circuit implies opposite interest rates. The evidence is decisive. The yarn computation: - Cotton at 4 d. per lb. in 1845. - Spinning cost: 4 d. (good secunda mule twist No. 40). - Total cost to spinner: 8 d. per lb. - Selling price, September–October 1845: 10½ d. or 11 d. per lb. - Margin: 2½ to 3 d. per lb. on a total cost of 8 d. — a very substantial profit. (Evidence of Wylie is cited but the transcript is just indicated by Marx.) Thus: real capital conditions (cotton cheap, yarn profitable) would have implied low interest on the raw-material stage and high interest on the finished-product stage — an incoherent result. In fact the money-market rate was determined by factors independent of the physical commodity's stage. The \"identity\" thesis collapses. The Whole Question Resolved Marx now undertakes a hypothetical reconstruction. \"The demand and supply for loan capital would be identical with the demand and supply for capital in general (although this last phrase is absurd; for the industrialist or merchant, commodities are a form of his capital, but he never demands capital as such, but always a particular commodity, buying and paying for it as a commodity, corn or cotton, irrespective of the role it has to fulfil in the circuit of his capital), if there were no money-lenders and instead of them the lending capitalist owned machines, raw material, etc. and lent these out or hired them, as houses are rented now, to industrial capitalists who were themselves the owners of a section of these objects.\" So the counterfactual : imagine a world without money-lenders, in which lending capitalists owned machines and raw materials in kind and hired them out. \"In conditions such as these, the supply of loan capital would be identical with the supply of elements of production for the industrial capitalist, and of commodities for the merchant.\" Even then — and this is the crucial point — \"the division of profit between lender and borrower would then be completely dependent, in the first place, on the ratio in which this capital is borrowed and in which it is the property of the person using it.\" The split between interest and profit of enterprise would still be a matter of property relations , not of technical scarcity. The thought experiment emphasizes that the form of lending (money vs. machines) does not eliminate the property relation involved. Weguelin's \"Unemployed Capital\" Marx turns to the testimony of Weguelin (B.A. 1857). Weguelin holds the rate of interest is determined by \"the amount of unemployed capital\" (252), and that it is \"but an indication of a large amount of capital seeking employment\" (271). Later this is called \"floating capital\" (485), by which Weguelin understands: \"the Bank of England notes and other kinds of circulation in the country, for instance, the country banks circulation and the amount of coin which is in the country... I include in floating capital the reserves of the bankers' (502, 503)\" — and even \"gold bullion\" (503). Marx flags the circularity: what determines interest is the \"amount of unemployed capital,\" but \"unemployed capital\" is defined as the very money whose availability is at issue . Weguelin also says the Bank of England exerts great influence when \"we\" (the Bank of England) \"are holders of the greater portion of the unemployed capital\" (1198) — contradicting Overstone's insistence that the Bank \"is no place for capital.\" Weguelin concludes: \"I think the rate of discount is governed by the amount of unemployed capital which there is in the country. The amount of unemployed capital is represented by the reserve of the Bank of England, which is practically a reserve of bullion. When, therefore, the bullion is drawn upon, it diminishes the amount of unemployed capital in the country and consequently raises the value of that which remains\" (1258). So on Weguelin's own account, the rate of interest is determined by the size of the Bank's bullion reserve — a purely monetary quantity. John Stuart Mill and the Reserve Mill's testimony (2102): \"The Bank is obliged to depend for the solvency of its Banking Department upon what it can do to replenish the reserve in that department; and therefore as soon as it finds that there is any drain in progress, it is obliged to look to the safety of its reserve, and to commence contracting its discounts or selling securities.\" Mill describes the Bank's institutional compulsion: as soon as gold drains, the Bank must contract its credit. But this is precisely the opposite of what a panic demands (per Marx earlier: \"as long as a bank's credit is not undermined, it can alleviate the panic... by increasing its credit money\"). The 1844 Act institutionalizes a pro-cyclical contraction — a machine for intensifying panics. The Banking Department and the Metal Reserve The chapter's final movement clarifies the 1844 Act's institutional structure. \"Taking the Banking Department by itself, the reserve is a reserve for deposits only. According to the Overstones, the Banking Department should simply act as a banker, without regard to the 'automatic' note issue.\" The Act's theory separates Issue (tin box of notes, gold certificate) and Banking (commercial deposits), allegedly confining the Reserve to the Banking side and letting the Issue side operate \"automatically.\" But in practice: \"in times of real pressure, the Bank of England keeps a very sharp eye on the metal reserve, independently of the reserve of the Banking Department, which consists simply of notes; and it must do so, if it does not want to go broke. For to the same extent that the metal reserve disappears, so too does the reserve of banknotes, and no one should know this better than Mr Overstone, who so wisely established this very device in his 1844 Bank Act.\" The final irony: the very legislator who claimed that the Banking Department need only attend to its own reserve, and that the money in circulation is \"automatic,\" finds himself in a panic forced to attend precisely to the metal reserve, because if his bullion drains the notes are no longer convertible — and it is this convertibility that the Act was specifically designed to enforce. The \"automatic\" mechanism collapses into discretionary panic-management whenever its own conditions are threatened, and the separation of Issue from Banking turns out to be a fiction that intensifies rather than mitigates the crisis. This is Marx's final, exacting verdict on the 1844 Bank Act in this chapter. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS First systemic law: the categorial heterogeneity of loan capital and industrial capital. The central discovery of the chapter is that loanable money capital is not a transparent expression of real accumulation but a distinct social form with its own dynamics. Money capital can be swelled by revenue being parked in banks, by capital released via falling raw-material prices, by interrupted business producing hoards, by the withdrawal of rentiers, by the \"savings of others\" placed at the disposal of industrialists and bankers, and by the multiplication of credit claims against the same metal. Because each of these channels can expand independently of real accumulation, the loan market regularly runs ahead of real production, and the resulting \"plethora\" is a systematic phenomenon, not a market failure to be legislated away. Second systemic law: the multiplicative nature of credit capital. How often the same piece of money serves as loan capital depends on (1) the volume of real transactions it mediates, (2) clearing and credit organization, and (3) the speed of circulation of deposits into loans. This means the nominal quantity of money capital vastly exceeds the physical stock of money, so monetary statistics cannot be treated as proportions of the productive base. The Twells example (£5,000 doubled) and the \"claim to money\" analysis establish that a large portion of loan capital is necessarily fictitious — not fraudulent, but structurally a claim that can only be honored in the aggregate if the real economy expands in step. Third systemic law: interest and profit do not move in lockstep. Marx systematically shows: (a) high interest can coexist with falling profit of enterprise; (b) interest can rise because of rising wages, which reduce the profit rate; (c) in crisis, interest peaks while profit collapses; (d) in revival, interest falls while profit recovers. The same \"high rate of interest\" can express fundamentally different economic states. Interest, therefore, is not a barometer of real capital scarcity but a signal of the money-market's specific conjunctural conditions. Fourth systemic law: the necessary possibility of monetary crisis. \"It is the foundation of capitalist production that money confronts commodities as an autonomous form of value\" — the split between value's money form and value's commodity substance is not an accident of institutional design but is written into the commodity form itself. Where labor is only indirectly social (via the market), the possibility of monetary crisis is constitutive. Crises emerge not as failures of the system but as the system working itself out : commodities are sacrificed for money precisely when the systemic guarantee of money's value breaks down. This is why \"many millions' worth of commodities have to be sacrificed for a few millions in money\" — a formula whose applicability to 19th-century panics remains, mutatis mutandis, an interpretative schema for financial crises ever since. Fifth systemic law: the ideological function of theoretical confusion. Overstone's identification of loan capital with capital is not naïve error but performs an ideological function: it transforms the money-lender into the sole capitalist and mystifies the property relation involved in lending. Marx's polemic against the 1844 Bank Act is at once theoretical and practical — the Act's designers confuse money and capital in theory, and design a machine that intensifies monetary crises in practice. This concern for the class function of theory runs throughout the chapter. Broader theoretical implications. Chapter 32 is the culmination of Marx's account of the specificity of financial capital — a category that later Marxist theorists (Hilferding, Lenin, Bukharin) would develop into the theory of finance capital and imperialism. The chapter provides: a mechanism for the multiplication of claims (the credit multiplier), a theory of fictitious capital (the securities market divorced from the capital it nominally represents), an analysis of the concentration of money markets (London's Stock Exchange), and an account of speculative dynamics (jobbers, speculators, the payment of interest out of borrowed capital). It also points forward to the theory of crisis in Volume 3's later chapters, providing the monetary mechanism by which a \"real\" crisis of profitability is transmitted and intensified through the credit system, and providing the grounds for distinguishing monetary crisis from crisis of real accumulation, while insisting on their interdependence. Broader practical implications. Marx's conclusions sustain several claims about economic policy that go beyond his own era. First : metallic money is not necessary for domestic circulation in a developed economy — an insight behind modern fiat currency and central banking. Second : a solvent bank facing a panic should expand credit, not contract it; otherwise it deepens the crisis. Third : rigid monetary rules (like the 1844 Act) that force pro-cyclical contraction will always be suspended in extremis, revealing their theoretical incoherence in practice. Fourth : the profit of enterprise and the rate of interest are distinct categories, and industrial policy that ignores the difference will find the two moving in opposite directions. Philosophical implications. The most profound philosophical payoff concerns the role of appearance in capitalist economy. The appearance that capital grows through saving and abstinence is demolished by the demonstration that the industrial capitalist \"disposes of the savings of others,\" the money capitalist \"makes the savings of other people into his capital,\" and the credit that reproductive capitalists give one another is transformed into \"his own source of private enrichment.\" The final illusion of the system — that capital is the offspring of work and thrift — is shown to rest on appropriation and property, not labor and virtue. The chapter thus closes the great arc of Marx's analysis in Part V by grounding the distribution of surplus value between interest and profit of enterprise in the same exploitation that is the source of surplus value itself — completing the demonstration that the appearance of a natural, harmonious economy conceals a structure in which the wealth of the few is constituted by the labor and losses of the many. The chapter ends on a note of grim but rigorous closure: the metal reserve of the Bank of England — the ultimate fetish of the currency school — turns out to be nothing more than the material form on which the whole edifice of credit rests, and to which, at the moment of crisis, commodities (real wealth, use-values, human labor embodied in goods) must be sacrificed. Marx does not offer a remedy in this chapter; he offers a diagnosis. The 1844 Bank Act will produce, in every crisis, the paradoxical result of intensifying precisely the panic it was meant to prevent — and this paradox is not a flaw of the Act but the truth of the capitalist relation, appearing at the surface of the money market in forms that all the currency-school verbiage cannot disguise. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 31 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 33"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 33", "title": "Chapter 33", "subtitle": "The Currency under the Credit System", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 33/Chapter 33 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 16342, "read_time": "82 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital", "Money, Currency & Circulation"], "keywords": ["The Currency under the Credit System", "Run on Gold Reserves during Financial Panics", "Convertibility of Banknotes and Suspension of Bank Acts", "Hoarding of Cash during Systemic Liquidity Freezes", "Central Bank as Lender of Last Resort"], "text": "Chapter 33 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 16,330 words ⏱️ ~82 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem: How Does Credit Govern the Circulation of Money? Chapter 33 of Capital , Volume 3, entitled \"The Means of Circulation under the Credit System,\" is a pivot point in Marx's analysis of the credit system. Having established that interest is a portion of profit, and that the credit system allocates loanable capital across the economy, Marx now turns to a narrower but technically ferocious question: what is the actual relationship between the quantity of circulating media (banknotes, coin, bills of exchange) and the credit system that both produces and constrains them? The chapter opens with a quotation from an anonymous 1840s pamphlet, The Currency Theory Reviewed , which declares: \"The great regulator of the velocity of the currency is credit. This explains why a severe pressure upon the money-market is generally coincident with a full circulation\" (p. 65). Marx seizes on this sentence and resolves it into a double proposition — credit is the regulator of velocity in two distinct senses — and this doubling constitutes the chapter's organizing principle. The first sense is structural: every institutional device that saves means of circulation — the clearing house, the offsetting of bills, the deposit-and-loan cycle of banknotes, the system of reciprocal claims settled by bookkeeping transfer — is founded on credit . The second sense is dynamic: credit mediates and accelerates the velocity with which a given piece of money changes hands, so that the same note can accomplish more purchase-and-payment transactions in a given time than it would if each transaction required an actual sale and purchase. These two senses are not parallel; they interlock, and their interlocking is what makes the credit system simultaneously an engine of economization and a source of catastrophic fragility. The Intellectual Objective: Demolishing the Currency Theory from Within Marx's deeper objective in this chapter is polemical and forensic. He is intervening in the great mid-Victorian monetary controversy that had produced the Bank Charter Act of 1844 and the Bank Acts of 1845 — legislation that tied the issue of banknotes to the metal reserve and that had been framed on the assumptions of the \"Currency School\" (Overstone, Torrens, and their allies). The Currency School held that the quantity of notes in circulation determines prices and that over-issue of notes is the cause of crises; hence note issue should be strictly regulated by the gold reserve. Against this, Marx deploys an immense battery of empirical evidence — parliamentary testimony from the Bank Acts Committee of 1857–58, the House of Lords Committee on Commercial Distress of 1848, and the Bank Acts Committee of 1858 — to demonstrate that the note-issuing banks cannot in fact determine the quantity of notes in circulation as long as those notes are convertible, and that the absolute quantity of circulation has no determining effect on the rate of interest except in moments of acute pressure. The chapter's central thesis, then, can be stated as follows: the quantity of means of circulation under the credit system — both notes and bills of exchange — is determined by the needs of commerce (the prices and volume of transactions, together with the velocity and economy of circulation), not by the will of the banks or the state of the gold reserve; and the interest rate is determined by the demand for and supply of loanable capital, not by the quantity of circulating media. Where the quantity of circulation does appear to govern the interest rate — during panics — this is an illusion generated by the collapse of credit into a demand for means of payment. The crisis, in Marx's famous formulation near the end of the chapter, is the moment when \"the sudden collapse of the credit system into the monetary system\" occurs. Method: The Dialectic of Circulation and Capital The chapter's method is characteristically Marxian: it holds together two levels of analysis that bourgeois political economy kept separate. On one level, Marx analyses the technical mechanics of circulation — how a note is deposited, discounted, redeposited, rediscounted, and returned to its issuer. On another level, he analyses the social relations embodied in these mechanics — the way in which the credit system converts a national saving on metal money into a private profit, the way in which a class of \"parasites\" acquires the power to \"decimate the industrial capitalists periodically\" and to \"interfere in actual production in the most dangerous manner.\" The technical and the social are not two arguments but one: the very mechanisms that economize on circulating media (the clearing house, the bill of exchange, the fiduciary note) are the mechanisms that concentrate power in the hands of the banks and big money-lenders, and that make the crisis both possible and profitable. This chapter fits into the broader architecture of Volume 3 as the moment when Marx transitions from the analysis of interest-bearing capital as a form (Part V's opening chapters) to the analysis of the credit system as an institutional totality. It prepares the ground for the later treatment of banking capital, the role of the Bank of England, and — most famously — the \"fictitious capital\" that pervades the credit system. It is also, importantly, a chapter in which Marx's engagement with The Economist (Wilson), with Gilbart, with Tooke, and with the testimony of practising bankers (Chapman, Gurney, Neave, Hubbard) becomes the substance of the argument. Marx does not merely cite these sources; he reads them against the grain, letting the bankers expose their own system even as they defend it. The Focal Point: The Collapse of Credit into Money The chapter's dramatic and theoretical climax is the crisis. In ordinary times, the note circulation is governed by the needs of trade; the interest rate moves according to the demand for loanable capital; the bill of exchange circulates as a substitute for money; and the banks appear as mere intermediaries. But in a crisis, everything inverts. The bill circulation \"completely collapses\"; no one will accept a promise to pay; each person hoards banknotes; the same notes that were \"so abundant the day before have vanished from the market\"; the rate of interest soars not because capital has become scarce but because means of payment have become scarce; and the entire credit structure reveals itself to rest on a metallic base that is utterly inadequate to its superstructure. The chapter thus ends with a vision of the credit system as \"one enormous centralization\" that gives a class of parasites \"fabulous power\" — a power that the Bank Act of 1844, far from restraining, actually intensifies by compelling the Bank to contract its note issue at the precise moment when the world is crying out for notes. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Proposition: Credit as the Regulator of Velocity in a Double Sense Marx begins by taking the quoted sentence — \"the great regulator of the velocity of the currency is credit\" — and insisting that it must be read in a double sense. The first sense is that \"all methods that save means of circulation are founded on credit.\" This is a claim about the institutional foundations of economization. The clearing house, the offsetting of bills, the deposit system, the practice of settling by bookkeeping transfer — none of these would be possible unless the parties involved already stood in relations of credit to one another. The second sense is that credit increases the velocity of circulation, i.e. it allows a given mass of money to perform more transactions in a given time. This is a claim about the dynamics of circulation. To make the second sense concrete, Marx offers the famous example of a £500 note. Suppose A gives this note to B today in payment for a bill of exchange; B deposits it the same day with his banker; the banker uses it the same day to discount a bill for C; C pays it into his bank; this bank gives it to the billbroker as an advance, and so on. The note is \"serving to make purchases or payments\" at every step, but the speed with which it circulates \"is mediated by the speed with which it returns time after time to someone or other in the form of a deposit and is transferred to someone else in the form of a loan.\" Here Marx is making a subtle but crucial point: the note's velocity in real transactions (purchases and payments) is a function of its velocity in credit transactions (deposits and loans). The note is not merely a means of purchase; it is simultaneously the vehicle of a series of credit operations. The same piece of money is alternately a deposit (a claim on the banker) and a loan (a claim by the banker on someone else), and it is because it is passed back and forth between these two forms that it can be used so intensively. The Clearing House and the Two Forms of Settlement Marx now turns to the \"purest\" form of economizing on means of circulation: the clearing house. Here the \"simple exchange of bills falling due\" takes place, and \"the principal function of money as means of payment is simply to settle the balances.\" In the clearing house, the enormous mass of reciprocal claims is cancelled against one another, and only the net difference is discharged in money. This is the most highly developed form of the economization that credit makes possible. But Marx immediately adds a crucial qualification: \"the existence of these bills of exchange itself depends on the credit that the industrialists and merchants give one another.\" If this credit declines, so does the number of bills, \"particularly the long-term ones,\" and so too does the effectiveness of this method of settlement. Here the second sense of credit's role (mediating velocity) folds back into the first (founding the methods that save circulation): the very existence of the bills that are cleared depends on credit, so that a contraction of credit simultaneously reduces the number of bills and the efficacy of the clearing house. Marx then distinguishes two kinds of economization, apart from \"the greater or lesser development of technique in the concentration of these payments.\" The first is the case where \"reciprocal claims, represented by bills of exchange or cheques, are balanced by the same banker, who simply transfers the claim from one person's account to the other's.\" The second is the case where \"the various bankers settle them among themselves.\" In the first case, the economization is internal to a single bank's bookkeeping; in the second, it is inter-bank. Marx notes that the concentration of £8–£10 million in bills of exchange in the hands of a bill-broker such as Overend, Gurney & Co. was \"one of the principal means for expanding the scope of this settlement at the local level.\" This detail is important: it shows that the bill-broker is not a mere intermediary but a structural node in the system of economization. The bill-broker's ability to concentrate claims in his own hands is what allows the clearing mechanism to operate on a larger scale. Credit as the Mediator and Accelerator of Velocity: The Five-Transaction Example Marx now unpacks the second sense of credit's role with an extended comparison. He asks us to imagine a single piece of money that accomplishes five transactions in a given period. In the first scenario, without credit, A buys from B, B from C, C from D, D from E, and E from F — the money passes from hand to hand \"mediated simply by actual purchases and sales.\" In this case, the money \"may effect only five transactions, for instance, remaining for longer in each individual hand.\" In the second scenario, with credit, the same piece of money performs five purchases but in a different way. B deposits with his banker the money received from A; the banker passes it to C in discounting a bill of exchange; C buys from D; D deposits it with his banker; the latter lends it to E; E buys from F. Here the money's velocity as a \"mere means of circulation (means of purchase)\" is mediated by four credit operations : B's depositing, the banker's discounting for C, D's depositing, and the banker's discounting for E. Without these credit operations, \"the same piece of money would not have performed five purchases successively in a given period of time.\" The crucial point is that the fact that the money \"changed hands without the mediation of actual purchase and sale — as a deposit and by discounting — means that its change of hands in the series of real transactions is accelerated.\" In other words, the credit operations do not replace the real transactions; they accelerate them by allowing the same money to be used more frequently. Marx adds a further refinement: \"one and the same banknote can form deposits with different bankers. In the same way, it can form different deposits with the same banker.\" The example given is that a banker discounts B's bill with the note A has deposited, B pays to C, and C deposits the note with the same banker who gave it out. Here the same note returns to its point of origin, having mediated a discount and a payment. This is a miniature illustration of the circulation of banknotes within a credit system: the note is issued, used, and returned, and in the process it has facilitated a series of transactions. The Law of Quantity: Prices, Transactions, Velocity, Economy Having established the mechanics of credit's role in circulation, Marx now restates the fundamental law governing the quantity of money in circulation. He refers back to Volume 1, Chapter 3, Section 2, where he had shown that \"the quantity of money actually circulating, taking the velocity of circulation and economy of payment as given, is determined by the price of commodities and the number of transactions.\" This is the classical quantity-relation, but with a crucial qualification: velocity and economy of payment are taken as given . The same law, Marx says, \"prevails in the case of note circulation.\" This is a direct challenge to the Currency School, which held that note circulation is governed by a different law (the law of the gold reserve). Marx insists that notes, so long as they are convertible, obey exactly the same law as coin. To substantiate this, Marx presents a table of Bank of England note circulation from 1844 to 1857, subdivided by denomination. The table shows the yearly average of notes in the hands of the public, broken down into £5–£10 notes, £20–£100 notes, and £200–£1,000 notes, with percentages of the total. The total sum of banknotes in circulation \"underwent a positive decline between 1844 and 1857, even though trade more than doubled,\" as shown by export and import figures. The low-denomination notes (£5 and £10) increased from £9,263,000 in 1844 to £10,659,000 in 1857, while the high-denomination notes (£200 to £1,000) declined from £5,856,000 in 1852 to £3,241,000 in 1857 — a decline of more than £2.5 million. Marx explains this decline by reference to a change in the clearing arrangements. On 8 June 1854, the private bankers of London admitted the joint-stock banks to the clearing house, and \"shortly afterwards the final clearing was adjusted in the Bank of England.\" The daily clearances were now effected by transfers in the accounts which the banks kept at the Bank of England. As a result, \"the large notes which the bankers formerly employed for the purpose of adjusting their accounts are no longer necessary.\" This is a concrete illustration of the economization of means of circulation: the same clearing operations can be performed with fewer and smaller notes once the mechanism of settlement is improved. Marx notes that the low minimum to which the use of money in wholesale trade had been reduced can be seen by comparing the table reproduced in Volume 1, Chapter 3, p. 238, note 54, submitted to the Bank Acts Committee by Morrison, Dillon & Co. — one of the largest London wholesalers where a retail trader can purchase his entire stock of commodities. Marx then cites the evidence of W. Newmarch (B. A. 1857, no. 1741) on other factors contributing to the saving on means of circulation: the penny post, railways, telegraphs — \"in short the improved means of communication.\" As a result, England \"can do approximately five or six times the amount of business with the same note circulation.\" Newmarch attributes this \"essentially\" to the withdrawal of notes of more than £10 from circulation, and sees it as a \"natural explanation\" for the fact that in Scotland and Ireland, where £1 notes also circulate, the note circulation has risen by approximately 31 per cent. Marx gives the figures: the total circulation of banknotes in the United Kingdom, including the £1 notes, is £39 million; the gold circulation is £70 million. In Scotland, the note circulation in 1834 was £3,120,000; in 1844, £3,020,000; in 1854, £4,050,000. The Impossibility of Over-Issue by Note-Issuing Banks From this empirical material, Marx draws a striking conclusion: \"it is in no way in the power of the note-issuing banks to increase the number of notes in circulation, as long as these notes are exchangeable at any time against metal money.\" This is the heart of his argument against the Currency School. If notes are convertible, any excess of notes will immediately return to the issuer for redemption in metal. Hence \"the amount of notes in circulation is governed by the needs of commerce, and each superfluous note immediately finds its way back to its issuer.\" Marx adds a parenthetical footnote (marked as an editorial insertion by Engels, \"F. E.\") clarifying that he is \"not referring at all here to inconvertible paper money.\" Inconvertible banknotes \"can become general means of circulation only where they are in actual fact supported by the state's credit, as is the case today in Russia, for example.\" Such notes \"fall under the laws of inconvertible state paper money, as already developed: Volume 1, Chapter 3, 2, c: 'Coin. The Symbol of Value'.\" This footnote is important because it delimits the scope of Marx's argument: he is analysing a system of convertible notes, not fiat money. The distinction matters because the laws governing the two are different: convertible notes are tied to metal and therefore subject to the law of metallic circulation, whereas inconvertible notes are governed by the laws of state paper money. Marx then notes that in England, \"it is only the notes of the Bank of England that enjoy general circulation as legal tender,\" so \"we can ignore here the insignificant and simply local note circulation of the provincial banks.\" This is a matter of institutional fact: the Bank of England's notes are the general currency of the realm, and it is therefore the Bank's circulation that is relevant to the analysis. The Parliamentary Evidence: Neave, Tooke, and Wright Marx now introduces the evidence of Mr Neave, Governor of the Bank of England, before the Bank Acts Committee of 1858. Neave testifies that \"in ordinary times, the uses of the public seem to want about £20,000,000\" of notes, with special periodic moments when they rise by another £1,000,000 or £1,500,000. He states that \"if the public wanted more, they could always take it from the Bank of England.\" During a panic, \"the public have, as I believe, the full power of helping themselves as to notes; and of course, as long as the Bank has a liability, they may use that liability to take the notes from the Bank.\" Neave estimates the average legal-tender requirement at \"from £19,000,000 to £20,000,000.\" Marx then cites Thomas Tooke's evidence to the House of Lords Committee on Commercial Distress (C. D. 1848–57, no. 3094): the Bank \"has no power of its own volition to extend the amount of its circulation in the hands of the public; but the Bank has the power of reducing the amount of the notes in the hands of the public, not however without a very violent operation.\" This is a precise statement of the asymmetry that Marx wants to emphasize: the Bank cannot increase circulation at will, but it can decrease it — and only through a violent operation (raising the discount rate, calling in loans, etc.). J. C. Wright, a Nottingham banker of thirty years' experience, testifies that \"any excess of circulation will go into the deposits and thus assume a different name.\" This is an important observation: an excess note issued by the Bank does not remain in circulation as a note; it is deposited, and thus appears as a deposit liability rather than as circulating currency. The note's \"name\" changes — from circulation to deposit — but the underlying reality is that the note has returned to the banking system. The Scottish and Irish Evidence: Kennedy and Anderson Marx extends the argument to Scotland, where \"there is almost nothing but paper in circulation,\" since £1 notes are permitted and \"the Scotch hate gold.\" Kennedy, a director of a Scottish bank, declares that the banks \"could never reduce their note circulation\" and that \"so long as there are internal transactions requiring notes or gold to perform them, bankers must, either through the demands of their depositors or in one shape or another, furnish as much currency as those transactions require.\" The Scottish banks \"can restrict their transactions, but they cannot control their currency.\" This is a crucial distinction: the banks can control the volume of their lending (their transactions), but they cannot control the quantity of currency in circulation, because that is determined by the needs of trade. Anderson, director of the Union Bank of Scotland, adds a further point (ibid., no. 3578): the system of exchanges between the Scottish banks \"prevents any over-issue on the part of any one bank,\" but there is \"a more powerful preventive than the system of exchanges\" — namely \"the universal practice in Scotland of keeping a bank account.\" Everybody who has any money at all \"has a bank account and puts in every day the money which he does not immediately want, so that at the close of the business of the day there is no money scarcely out of the banks except what people have in their pockets.\" This is a striking observation about the institutional conditions of note circulation: in a society where everyone has a bank account and deposits their surplus daily, the notes return to the banks every evening, and the banks can therefore control the quantity in circulation to a much greater degree than would otherwise be possible. But even here, the control is indirect: it works through the return flow of notes, not through the banks' arbitrary decision to issue or not issue. Marx notes that the same applies to Ireland, citing the evidence of the Governor of the Bank of Ireland, MacDonnell, and the director of the Provincial Bank of Ireland, Murray. Independence from the Gold Reserve: The Kinnear Example Marx now makes a further claim: \"the note circulation is not only independent of the will of the Bank of England, it is equally independent of the state of the gold reserve in the Bank's vaults, which is what ensures the convertibility of these notes.\" He cites the example of J. G. Kinnear ( The Crisis and the Currency , London, 1847, p. 5): on 18 September 1846, the circulation of the Bank of England was £20,900,000 and the bullion in the Bank was £16,273,000; on 5 April 1847, the notes in circulation were £20,815,000 and the bullion was £10,246,000. Thus \"£6 million of gold were exported, without any contraction of the currency of the country.\" This is a powerful empirical refutation of the Currency School's claim that note circulation is determined by the gold reserve. The gold reserve fell by £6 million, but the note circulation was essentially unchanged. Marx adds the qualification: \"Naturally this holds good only under the conditions prevailing in England today, and even then only in so far as legislation does not enforce a different ratio between note issue and metal reserve.\" This is a crucial caveat: the Bank Act of 1844 does enforce a different ratio, by separating the Issue Department from the Banking Department and requiring that notes above a fixed fiduciary limit be backed by gold. Marx is saying that in the absence of such legislation, the note circulation is independent of the gold reserve; but the legislation artificially ties the two together, which is precisely why the Act is pernicious. Seasonal Fluctuations: The Harvest and the Quarterly Dividends Marx now turns to the periodic fluctuations in circulation that repeat themselves each year. He cites Newmarch (B. A. 1857, no. 1650): \"the circulation is high in one month, and it is low in another month, and in a certain other month occurs a medium point.\" The first of these is the harvest. In August of each year, \"a few £ millions, mostly in gold, pass from the Bank of England into domestic circulation, to pay for the costs of the harvest.\" Since this involves principally the payment of wages, banknotes are used but little in England; the money then flows back to the Bank \"up to the end of the year.\" In Scotland, where £1 notes are given almost invariably in place of sovereigns, \"it is the note circulation that expands in the corresponding case, in fact twice a year, in May and November, by some £3 to £4 million; fourteen days later the reflux has already set in, and within a month it is almost complete.\" (Anderson, op. cit., nos. 3595–3600.) The Bank of England's note circulation also undergoes \"a temporary fluctuation each quarter as a result of the quarterly payment of 'dividends,' i.e. interest on the national debt.\" Notes are first withdrawn from circulation and then distributed among the public, but \"these flow back very quickly.\" Weguelin (B. A. 1857, no. 38) puts the total fluctuation at £2.5 million. Chapman, of Overend, Gurney and Co., reckons the disturbance to be much higher: \"When you abstract from the circulation £6,000,000 or £7,000,000 of revenue in anticipation of dividends, somebody must be the medium of supplying that in the intermediate times\" (B. A. 1857, no. 5196). The Industrial Cycle and the Crisis: Samuel Gurney's Evidence Marx now turns to \"far more important and persistent\" fluctuations: those that correspond to the various phases of the industrial cycle. He cites Samuel Gurney, partner in Overend, Gurney & Co., testifying before the House of Lords Committee (C. D. 1848–57, no. 2645). In October 1847, the amount of banknotes in the hands of the public was £20,800,000, and there was \"great difficulty in getting possession of banknotes in the money-market,\" due to \"the alarm of not being able to get them in consequence of the restriction of the Act of 1844.\" By March 1848, the amount was £17,700,000, but \"there being now no commercial alarm whatsoever, it is much beyond what is required.\" There was \"no banking house or money-dealer in London, but what has a larger amount of banknotes than they can use.\" Gurney further testifies (no. 2650) that the amount of banknotes \"out of the custody of the Bank of England affords a totally insufficient exponent of the active state of the circulation, without taking into consideration likewise ... the state of the commercial world and the state of credit.\" And (no. 2651): \"The feeling of surplus that we have under the present amount of circulation in the hands of the public arises in a large degree from our present state of great stagnation. In a state of high prices and excitement of transaction £17,700,000 would give us a feeling of restriction.\" Marx interpolates a crucial theoretical summary at this point (in a paragraph marked as an editorial insertion by Engels). As long as the state of business is such that the returns on advances come in regularly and credit remains unimpaired, \"the expansion and contraction of circulation is governed simply by the needs of the industrialists and merchants.\" Since in England gold does not come into the picture for wholesale trade, and the gold circulation — apart from seasonal fluctuations — can be seen as \"a magnitude that is fairly constant over a longish period of time,\" the Bank of England's note circulation provides \"a sufficiently exact measurement of these changes.\" In the quiet period after a crisis, transactions are at their lowest; with the revival of demand there is a greater requirement for means of circulation, which rises with increasing prosperity; the quantity reaches its high point in the period of \"over-exertion and over-speculation.\" Then the crisis breaks out, and \"overnight the banknotes that were still so abundant the day before have vanished from the market, and with them the discounter of bills, the advancer on securities, the buyer of commodities.\" The Bank of England is supposed to help, \"but even its powers are soon exhausted, and the Bank Act of 1844 compels it to contract its note circulation at the very moment when the entire world is crying out for banknotes, when the owners of commodities cannot sell and yet are supposed to pay, and are ready to make any sacrifice, so long as they can obtain banknotes.\" Marx then cites banker Wright (op. cit., no. 2930): \"During an alarm, the country requires twice as much circulation as in ordinary times, because the circulation is hoarded by bankers and others.\" This introduces the crucial concept of hoarding in a crisis. In so far as a crisis breaks out, \"it is then simply a question of means of payment.\" But since each person is dependent on someone else for the arrival of these means of payment, and no one knows whether the other will be in a position to pay on the due date, \"a real steeplechase breaks out for those means of payment that are to be found in the market, i.e. for banknotes.\" Each person hoards as many as he can get his hands on, \"so that the notes vanish from circulation the very day they are most needed.\" Samuel Gurney estimates a figure of £4–£5 million for the banknotes put under lock and key at the moment of panic in October 1847. Chapman's Evidence on Market Manipulation Marx now introduces the evidence of Chapman, Gurney's partner, before the 1857 Bank Acts Committee, which he describes as \"highly interesting.\" Chapman testifies (no. 4963) that he does not think it is \"a proper condition of things that the money-market should be under the power of any individual capitalist (such as does exist in London), to create a tremendous scarcity and pressure, when we have a very low state of circulation out.\" He adds that \"there is more than one capitalist, who can withdraw from the circulating medium £1,000,000 or £2,000,000 of notes, if they have an object to attain by it.\" A big speculator can sell £1 or £2 million worth of Consols \"and thus take money out of the market\" (no. 4965), creating \"a very violent pressure.\" The notes are then \"quite unproductive\" (no. 4967), but \"that is nothing, if it effects his great object; his great object is to knock down the funds, to create a scarcity, and he has it perfectly in his power to do so.\" Chapman gives a concrete example. One morning there was a great demand for money from the stock exchange, and no one knew the cause. Someone asked Chapman to lend him £50,000 at 7 per cent; Chapman was surprised, as his rate was much lower, but agreed. The man returned and took a further £50,000 at 7.5 per cent, then £100,000 at 8 per cent, and wanted more at 8.5 per cent. Chapman \"then got worried himself.\" It subsequently emerged that \"a major sum of money had suddenly been withdrawn from the market.\" Chapman: \"I did lend a large sum at 8 per cent; I was afraid to go beyond; I did not know what was coming.\" Marx draws a crucial distinction here: \"It should never be forgotten that although a fairly constant sum of £19–£20 million in notes is ostensibly in the hands of the public, yet the portion of these notes that is actually circulating, on the one hand, and the portion that lies unoccupied in the banks as a reserve, on the other, are both constantly and substantially changing.\" If the reserve is large (i.e. the actual circulation low), it is said from the standpoint of the money market that \"the circulation is full, or money is plentiful\"; if the reserve is small (i.e. the actual circulation full), the money market calls it \"low\" and says \"money is scarce,\" i.e. \"only a small amount represents unoccupied loan capital.\" This is a subtle but essential point. The total quantity of notes in the hands of the public may be roughly constant, but the distribution of those notes between active circulation and idle reserve is constantly changing, and it is this distribution — not the total — that determines whether the money market feels \"full\" or \"low.\" When the reserve is large, there is plenty of unoccupied loan capital; when it is small, loan capital is scarce. The apparent paradox — that a \"full\" circulation means plentiful money and a \"low\" circulation means scarce money — is resolved once we understand that the money market is concerned not with the total circulation but with the quantity of loanable money, i.e. the reserve. Technical Fluctuations: Taxes and Dividends Marx distinguishes these industrial-cycle fluctuations from \"a genuine expansion or contraction of circulation independent of the phases of the industrial cycle — one in which the amount that the public needs remains the same.\" Such fluctuations are \"only to be found for technical reasons, e.g. at the payment date of taxes or of interest on the national debt.\" With tax payment, notes and gold flow into the Bank of England \"in more than their customary measure,\" and circulation contracts \"irrespective of the need for it.\" Conversely, when dividends are paid out on the national debt, circulation expands. In the first case, loans are taken out from the Bank in order to obtain means of circulation; in the latter case, the rate of interest charged by the private banks falls \"on account of the temporary growth in their reserves.\" Marx emphasizes that this \"has nothing to do with the absolute total of means of circulation, but simply with the banking house that puts these means of circulation into circulation, for which this process appears as an alienation of loan capital and which therefore pockets the profit on it.\" In the one case, there is \"simply a temporary displacement of the circulating medium,\" which the Bank of England adjusts by making short-term advances at low interest shortly before the due date of the quarterly taxes and the likewise quarterly dividends. The additional notes paid out \"first of all fill the gaps that the payment of taxes gives rise to, while their repayment to the Bank immediately afterwards brings back the surplus notes which the paying out of dividends has placed with the public.\" In the other case, \"a low or full circulation is never more than a different distribution of the same mass of means of circulation between active circulation and deposits, i.e. as an instrument for loans.\" The Interest Rate and the Absolute Quantity of Circulation Marx now addresses the central question directly: does the absolute quantity of circulation determine the rate of interest? His answer is a qualified no. He begins with the case of a gold influx leading to an increase in the number of notes given out by the Bank of England. These notes \"help the business of discounting outside the Bank and flow back in the repayment of loans, so that the absolute volume of notes in circulation is only temporarily increased.\" If the circulation is full on account of an expansion of business (which is only possible given relatively low prices), the rate of interest \"may be relatively high on account of the demand for loan capital that results from rising profits and increased new investments.\" If it is low on account of a contraction of business or greater ease of credit, the rate of interest \"may be low even if prices are high.\" (Marx refers to Hubbard for this.) The absolute quantity of circulation has a determining effect on the rate of interest \"only in periods of pressure.\" In this case, either the demand for a full circulation is \"merely a demand for means of hoarding\" (apart from the reduced velocity with which the money circulates, and with which the same identical pieces of money are constantly converted into loan capital), on account of the lack of credit, as in 1847, when the suspension of the Bank Act \"did not lead to any expansion in the circulation but was sufficient to bring hoarded notes to light again and make them circulate actively.\" Or else more means of circulation may really be required, \"in the way that the circulation really did grow for some time in 1857 after the suspension of the Bank Act.\" In other cases, the absolute quantity of circulation has no effect on the rate of interest, for two reasons. First, taking the economy and velocity of circulation as constant, it is determined by the price of commodities and the volume of transactions (in which case one element generally counteracts the effect of the other) and ultimately by the state of credit, \"whereas it in no way conversely determines the latter.\" Second, \"commodity prices and interest do not stand in any necessary relationship.\" Marx then provides a series of historical examples to substantiate this claim. Under the Bank Restriction Act (1797–1820), there was a surplus of \"currency,\" with the rate of interest \"always far higher than since the resumption of cash payment.\" It later fell sharply with the restriction of note issue and rising bill quotations. In 1822, 1823, and 1832, the total circulation was low and the rate of interest similarly so. In 1824, 1825, and 1836, the circulation was high and the rate of interest rose. In summer 1830, the circulation was high and the rate of interest low. Since the gold discoveries, monetary circulation has expanded throughout Europe, and the interest rate has risen. \"The interest rate thus does not depend on the amount of money in circulation.\" The footnote on the Bank Restriction Act explains its historical context: it was \"designed to cope with the needs of the British government in the wars with France\" and \"freed the Bank of England from the obligation of exchanging its notes for gold.\" This is important because it explains why the period 1797–1820 is anomalous: the notes were inconvertible, and therefore subject to the laws of state paper money rather than metallic circulation. The high interest rates during this period were not caused by the surplus of currency but by the government's enormous borrowing demands. The Distinction between Issuing Means of Circulation and Lending Capital Marx now makes a theoretical distinction that is central to the chapter: \"The distinction between the issue of means of circulation and the lending of capital is best shown in connection with the actual reproduction process.\" He refers back to Volume 2, Part Three, where he had shown how the various components of production are exchanged one for another. Variable capital consists materially of the means of subsistence of the workers, a portion of their own product, but it is paid out to them \"bit by bit in money.\" The capitalist has to advance this money, and \"it depends very much on the organization of the credit system whether he can pay out the new variable capital again the next week with the old money that he paid out the week before.\" It is similar in the acts of exchange between the various components of a total social capital, e.g. between means of consumption and the means of production of these. The money for their circulation \"must be advanced by one or both of the exchanging parties. It then remains in circulation, but returns time and again, after completing its exchange, to the person who advanced it, since it was advanced by him over and above the industrial capital he actually employed.\" (See Volume 2, Chapter 20.) When the credit system is developed, so that money is concentrated in the hands of the banks, \"it is they who advance it, at least nominally. This advance is only related to the money in circulation. It is an advance of circulation, not an advance of the capitals it circulates.\" This is the crucial distinction: the bank's advance of money is not the same as an advance of capital. The bank advances means of circulation ; it does not thereby advance capital . The capital being circulated already exists; the bank merely provides the money that allows it to circulate. This is a point that the Currency School consistently confused, treating the issue of notes as an advance of capital to the economy. Chapman on Credit, Regional Money Flows, and \"Money at Call\" Marx returns to Chapman's evidence. Chapman testifies (no. 5062) that \"there may be times, when the notes in the hands of the public, though they may be large, are not to be had.\" Marx comments: \"There is money, even during a panic; but everyone takes good care not to transform it into loanable capital, loanable money; each holds on to it for actual needs of payment.\" This is a precise statement of the hoarding phenomenon: the money exists, but it is not available as loanable capital. Chapman confirms (nos. 5099–5101) that country bankers in rural districts send their unemployed balances to London houses such as his, while the Lancashire and Yorkshire districts require discounts from him for the use of their trades. \"Then by that means the surplus money of one part of the country is made available for the demands of another part of the country? — Precisely so.\" This is the geographical dimension of the money market: the surplus of the agricultural districts flows to London and is redistributed to the industrial districts. Chapman adds that the banks' custom of investing their surplus money capital short-term in Consols and treasury bills \"has greatly increased in recent times, since it became the custom to lend out this 'money at call' (i.e. money whose repayment may be demanded at any time, from one day to the next).\" Chapman himself sees the purchase of this kind of paper as \"most unsuitable for his business.\" He therefore invests it in good bills of exchange, \"with one portion falling due each day, so that he always knows how much ready money he can count on.\" (5101–5105.) This is a revealing detail about the internal management of a bill-broking house: Chapman avoids \"money at call\" because it is unreliable, preferring a portfolio of bills that mature in a staggered fashion. It also reveals the extent to which the money market is a market in liquidity , where the ability to convert assets into cash at short notice is the decisive consideration. Discounting as Purchase: The Export Trade Debate Marx now turns to Chapman's evidence on the export trade. The very growth of exports \"presents itself as a growing demand on the domestic money market, which however is felt as such only in times of pressure.\" In periods when exports are increasing, long-term bills of exchange are generally drawn by manufacturers on the export merchant, against consignments of British manufactures. (5126.) Wilson asks Chapman whether bills are not \"redrawn from time to time\" (5127); Chapman replies that \"that is a thing which they keep from us; we should not admit any bill of that sort ... I dare say it is done, but I cannot speak to a thing of the kind.\" Marx calls him \"the innocent Chapman\" — an ironic comment on Chapman's professed ignorance of a practice (kite-flying, or the rolling over of bills) that was in fact widespread. Wilson then asks (5129) whether a large increase in exports leads to \"a great demand for capital for the discount of bills representing those exports.\" Chapman agrees (5130), noting that \"this country gives an immense credit; but then it takes credit for its raw material. We are drawn upon from America always at 60 days, and from other parts at 90 days. On the other hand we give credit; if we send goods to Germany, we give two or three months.\" Wilson asks (5131) whether bills are not already drawn on England against imported raw materials and colonial goods at the same time as they are loaded, and whether the goods do not arrive simultaneously with the bills of lading. Chapman \"believes that this is the case, but knows nothing of this 'commercial' business; better informed people should be asked.\" In the export trade to America, \"the goods are symbolized in transit\" (5133); Marx calls this \"gibberish,\" which \"is supposed to mean that the English export merchant draws a four-month bill against the commodities on one of the major American banking houses in London, and the banking house receives collateral from America.\" Chapman explains (5136) that \"the more remote transactions\" are conducted by the merchant, \"who waits for his capital until the goods are sold.\" There may be houses of great private wealth who can afford to lay out their own capital without taking any advance on the goods, \"but the most part are converted into advances by the acceptances of some well-known established houses.\" These houses are resident in London, Liverpool, or elsewhere (5137–5138). \"Therefore, it makes no difference, whether the manufacturer lays out his money, or whether he gets a merchant in London or Liverpool to advance it; it is still an advance in this country? — Precisely. The manufacturer in few cases has anything to do with it\" (but in 1847 in almost every case). Chapman then gives a detailed example: a man dealing in manufactured goods at Manchester will buy his goods and ship them through a house of respectability in London; when the London house is satisfied that they are packed according to the understanding, he draws upon this London house for six months against these goods to India or China; then the banking world comes in and discounts that bill for him; so that, \"by the time he has to pay for those goods, he has the money all ready by the discount of that bill.\" Wilson asks (5139) whether, although he has the money, \"the banker is laying out of his money.\" Chapman: \"The banker has the bill; the banker has bought the bill; he uses his banking capital in that form, namely, in discounting commercial bills.\" Marx comments: \"Thus Chapman, too, sees the discounting of bills not as an advance, but rather as a purchase of commodities.\" This is a significant point: in Chapman's view, the banker is not lending capital but buying a commodity (the bill), and thus his activity is commercial rather than financial. Marx will later expose the naivety of this view. Chapman continues (5140): \"No doubt; it is the substantial occupation of the money-market and of the Bank of England. The Bank of England are as glad to get these bills as we are, because they know them to be good property.\" (5141) \"As the prosperity of the country increases, we' (the Chapmans) 'partake of it.\" (5142) \"Then when these various fields for the employment of capital increase suddenly, of course, the natural consequence is that the rate of interest is higher? — No doubt about it.\" In 5143 Chapman cannot \"quite understand, that under our large exports we have had such occasion for bullion.\" In 5144 Wilson asks: \"May it not be that we give larger credits upon our exports than we take credits upon our imports?\" Chapman: \"I rather doubt that point myself. If a man accepts against his Manchester goods sent to India, you cannot accept for less than ten months. We have had to pay America for her cotton' (that is perfectly true) 'some time before India pays us; but still it is rather refined in its operation.\" (5145) \"If we have had an increase, as we had last year, of £20 million in our exports of manufactures we must have had a very large increase of imports of raw material previously to that' (and in this way over-exports are already identified with over-imports, and overproduction with over-trading) 'in order to make up that increased quantity of goods? — No doubt.\" (5146) \"We should have to pay a very considerable balance, that is to say, the balance, no doubt, would run against us during that time, but in the long run, with America ... the exchanges are in our favour, and we have been receiving for some time past large supplies of bullion from America.\" In 5148 Wilson asks Chapman \"whether he does not consider his high interest rate as a token of great prosperity and high profits.\" Marx calls Wilson a \"sycophant\" and says Chapman, \"evidently astonished by the naivete,\" naturally confirms this, \"but is honest enough to make the following qualification: 'There are some, who cannot help themselves; they have engagements to meet, and they must fulfil them, whether it is profitable or not; but, for a continuance' (of the high rate of interest), 'it would indicate prosperity.'\" Marx comments: \"Both men forget that a high rate of interest can also indicate, as was the case in 1857, that the knights errant of credit are making the country unsafe.\" In this case, they can pay high rates of interest \"because they pay out of other people's pockets (though in this way they help to determine the interest rate for everyone), meanwhile living in style on anticipated profits.\" At the same time, \"precisely this can be a very profitable business for manufacturers, etc. The system of advances makes returns absolutely deceptive.\" This also explains why the Bank of England, when interest rates are high, \"discounts at a lower rate than the others.\" Chapman testifies (5156) that \"our discounts, taking the present moment, when we have had for so long a high rate of interest, are at their maximum.\" Marx notes that Chapman said this on 21 July 1857, \"a few months before the crash.\" (5157) \"In 1852' (when the interest rate was low) 'they were not nearly so large.\" Marx comments: \"Because at that time business was still much healthier.\" This is a nice example of Marx's use of hindsight: Chapman's confident testimony in July 1857 is juxtaposed with the crash that followed a few months later, exposing the fragility of the boom. The Convertibility of Bills versus the Convertibility of Banknotes Marx now turns to what he calls \"a particularly amusing aspect of Chapman's evidence\": \"how these people actually view the public's money as their own property and believe they have a right to it, to ensure the permanent convertibility of the bills they discount.\" The questions and answers \"show great naivete.\" It turns out to be \"the duty of legislation to ensure the permanent convertibility of the bills of exchange accepted by the major firms and to make sure that the Bank of England will rediscount them for the billbrokers under all circumstances.\" Marx notes that 1857 saw the bankruptcy of three such bill-brokers, \"to the tune of some £8 million, their own capital being infinitesimal in comparison with these debts.\" Chapman testifies (5177) that it would be \"a very lamentable thing\" that bills accepted by Barings or Loyds \"should not be discountable; a most extraordinary position, that a man should stop payment, who had the acceptances of Smith, Payne & Co., or Jones, Loyd & Co. in his hands, because he could not get them discounted.\" (5178) Wilson asks: \"Is not the engagement of Messrs Baring an engagement to pay a certain sum of money when the bill is due?\" Chapman: \"That is perfectly true; but Messrs Baring, when they contract that engagement, and every other merchant who contracts an engagement, never dream that they are going to pay it in sovereigns; they expect that they are going to pay it at the Clearing House.\" (5180) \"Do you think that there should be any machinery contrived by which the public would have a right to claim money before that bill was due by calling upon somebody to discount it? — No, not from the acceptor; but if you mean by that that we are not to have the possibility of getting commercial bills discounted, we must alter the whole constitution of things.\" (5182) \"Then you think that it' (commercial bill) 'ought to be convertible into money, exactly in the same way that a Bank of England note ought to be convertible into gold? — Most decidedly so, under certain circumstances.\" (5184) \"Then you think that the provisions of the currency should be so shaped that a bill of exchange of undoubted character ought at all times to be as readily exchangeable against money as a banknote? — I do.\" (5185) \"You do not mean to say that either the Bank of England or any individual should, by law, be compelled to exchange it? — I mean to say this, that in framing a bill for the currency, we should make provision to prevent the possibility of an inconvertibility of the bills of exchange of the country arising, assuming them to be undoubtedly solid and legitimate.\" Marx's comment: \"This is the convertibility of the commercial bill of exchange against the convertibility of the banknote.\" He is pointing to a striking inconsistency in Chapman's position: Chapman wants commercial bills to be as convertible as banknotes, yet he does not want any legal obligation to convert them. The convertibility he demands is not a legal right but a market convention — the assurance that good bills will always find a discounter. But this assurance is precisely what fails in a crisis, and it is this failure that exposes the fragility of the entire system. Chapman's demand amounts to a plea that the Bill-brokers be guaranteed liquidity by the Bank of England — a plea that was answered, in a sense, by the Bank Act's provision for rediscounting, but only at the cost of tightening the screw when it was least needed. Marx adds (5190): \"The money-dealers of the country only, in point of fact, represent the public\" — as Chapman did later at the Assizes in the Davidson case. Marx cites \"the Great City Frauds\" (Seton Laing, New Series of the Great City Frauds of Cole, Davidson and Gordon , 5th edn, London, 1869) as evidence of the money-dealers' fraudulent dealings. The point is that Chapman's claim to represent the public is self-serving; the money-dealers represent themselves. Chapman continues (5196): \"During the quarters' (when the dividends are paid) 'it is ... absolutely necessary that we should go to the Bank of England. When you abstract from the circulation £6,000,000 or £7,000,000 of revenue in anticipation of the dividends, somebody must be the medium of supplying that in the intermediate time.\" Marx comments: \"In this case the question at issue is the supply of money, not of capital or loan capital.\" This is another instance of the distinction between money and capital: the Bank is asked to supply money , not capital. (5169) \"Everybody acquainted with our commercial circle must know that when we are in such a state that we find it impossible to sell Exchequer bills, when India bonds are perfectly useless, when you cannot discount the first commercial bills, there must be great anxiety on the part of those whose business renders them liable to pay the circulating medium of the realm on demand, which is the case with all bankers. Then the effect of that is to make every man double his reserve. Just see what the result of that is throughout the country, that every country banker, of whom there are about 500, has to send up to his London correspondent to remit him £5,000 in banknotes. Taking such a limited sum as that as the average, which is quite absurd, you come to £2,500,000 taken out of the circulation. How is that to be supplied?\" This is a vivid illustration of the hoarding dynamic: in a crisis, every banker doubles his reserve, and the aggregate demand for notes — £2.5 million at Chapman's \"absurdly low\" estimate — represents a massive withdrawal of means of circulation from active use. Chapman adds (5195) that those private capitalists who have money \"do not want to let go of it whatever the interest,\" for \"we would rather have no interest at all, than have a doubt about our getting the money in case we require it.\" This is a precise statement of the liquidity preference that operates in a crisis: the demand for money becomes absolute, and no rate of interest, however high, is sufficient to induce holders to part with it. (5173) \"Our system is this: That we have £300,000,000 of liabilities which may be called for at a single moment to be paid in the coin of the realm, and that coin of the realm, if the whole of it is substituted, amounts to £23,000,000, or whatever it may be; is not that a state which may throw us into convulsions at any moment?\" Marx comments: \"Hence the sudden collapse of the credit system into the monetary system in times of crisis.\" This is one of the most famous formulations in the chapter. The credit system is a vast structure of claims — £300 million in liabilities — resting on a tiny metallic base of £23 million. In normal times, the claims circulate as substitutes for money, and the disproportion between claims and base is invisible. In a crisis, the claims are converted into a demand for money, and the disproportion becomes catastrophic. Marx adds: \"Apart from the domestic panic during crises, we can speak of the quantity of money only in so far as metal world money is involved. And it is precisely this that Chapman excludes, speaking only of £23 million in banknotes.\" This is a crucial theoretical point: in the international arena, money functions as metallic world money, and the credit system must ultimately rest on this metallic base. Chapman's focus on the domestic note circulation obscures the role of world money. Chapman testifies (5218): \"The primary cause of the derangement of the money-market' (in April and later in October 1847) 'no doubt was in the quantity of money which was required to regulate our exchanges, in consequence of the extraordinary importations of the year.\" Marx comments: \"Firstly, this reserve of world-market money was at that time reduced to its minimum. Secondly, it served at the same time as security for the convertibility of credit money, banknotes. It thus combined two completely different functions, although both of these arise from the nature of money, since real money is always world-market money, and credit money always depends on this world-market money.\" This is a dense theoretical passage. The gold reserve serves a double function: (1) as a reserve for international payments (world money), and (2) as the guarantee of the convertibility of domestic credit money (banknotes). These two functions are distinct but both arise from the nature of money as world money. In a crisis, the demand for gold for international payments coincides with the demand for gold to guarantee the convertibility of notes, and the reserve is overwhelmed. Chapman testifies (5221) that in 1847, without the suspension of the 1844 Bank Act, \"the clearing houses could not have been settled.\" He had some inkling of the impending crisis (5236): \"There are certain conditions of the money-market (and the present is not very far from it), where money is exceedingly difficult, and recourse must be had to the Bank.\" (5239) \"With reference to the sums which we took from the Bank on the Friday, Saturday and Monday, the 19th, 20th, and 22nd of October, 1847, we should only have been too thankful to have got the bills back on the Wednesday following; the money reflowed to us directly the panic was over.\" On Tuesday, 23 October, the Bank Act was suspended, \"and the crisis thereby curbed.\" This is a crucial observation: the mere suspension of the Act — without any actual increase in circulation — was sufficient to end the panic, because it restored confidence and brought hoarded notes back into circulation. This confirms Marx's argument that the crisis is fundamentally a crisis of confidence, not of physical scarcity. Chapman believes (5274) that the bills of exchange running on London \"amount at any one time to some £100–£200 million.\" This does not include local bills on provincial centres. (5287) \"Whereas in October 1856, the amount of the notes in the hands of the public ran up to £21,155,000, there was an extraordinary difficulty in obtaining money; notwithstanding that the public held so much, we could not touch it.\" This was due to \"the anxiety produced by the straits in which the Eastern Bank had found itself for a while (March 1856).\" (5290) As soon as the panic is over, \"all bankers deriving their profit from interest begin to employ the money immediately.\" (5302) Chapman explains the disquiet at the decline in the bank reserve \"not from fear for the deposits, but rather because all those who might suddenly have to pay large sums of money knew very well that they could be driven to the Bank as the last resort in time of pressure on the money market; and 'if the banks have a very small reserve, they are not glad to receive us; but on the contrary'.\" Marx then provides a summary of the reserve's function. \"It is very pleasant, incidentally, to observe how the reserve dwindles away as an actual magnitude.\" The banks keep a minimum for their current business, partly with themselves and partly with the Bank of England. The bill-brokers hold \"the loose bank money of the country\" without a reserve. And all the Bank of England has to set against its liabilities for deposits is simply the reserves of the bankers and others, besides \"public deposits,\" etc. It allows this reserve to fall to the lowest possible point, e.g. some £2 million. \"Apart from this £2 million in paper, therefore, the entire swindle has absolutely no other reserve than the metal reserve in times of pressure (and these periods reduce the reserve, because notes that come in against the metal that leaves the Bank must be destroyed). Hence any reduction of the metal reserve adds to the crisis with a drain of gold.\" This is a damning picture of the fragility of the system: the entire credit structure rests on a reserve of £2 million in paper plus the metal reserve, and in a crisis the metal reserve itself is depleted because notes returned to the Bank must be destroyed. The \"swindle\" — Marx's word for the credit system — is exposed in all its precariousness. Chapman on the Currency and the Collapse of Credit Chapman testifies (5306): \"If there should not be currency to settle the transactions at the clearing house, the only next alternative which I can see is to meet together, and to make our payments in first-class bills, bills upon the Treasury, and Messrs Smith, Payne, and so forth.\" (5307) Wilson asks: \"Then, if the government failed to supply you with a circulating medium, you would create one for yourselves?\" Chapman: \"What can we do? The public come in, and take the circulating medium out of our hands; it does not exist.\" (5308) \"You would only then do in London what they do in Manchester every day of the week? — Yes.\" Marx comments: \"Chapman has a very good answer to the question put to him by Cayley (a Birmingham man of the Attwood school) concerning Overstone's conception of capital.\" Cayley asks (5315): \"It has been stated before this Committee, that in a pressure like that of 1847, men are not looking for money, but are looking for capital; what is your opinion in that respect?\" Chapman: \"I do not understand it; we only deal in money; I do not understand what you mean by it.\" (5316) \"If you mean thereby' (commercial capital) 'the quantity of money which a man has of his own in his business, if you call that capital, it forms, in most cases, a very small proportion of the money which he wields in his affairs through the credit which is given him by the public\" — through the mediation of the Chapmans. This is a sharp exchange. Cayley is invoking Overstone's distinction between money and capital; Chapman, the practical man of the money market, professes not to understand it. Marx's point is that Chapman's inability to understand the distinction is itself revealing: the money-dealer is so immersed in the circulation of money that he cannot see the capital that lies behind it. (5339) Chapman: \"Is it the want of property that makes us give up our specie payments? — Not at all ... It is not that we want property, but it is that we are moving under a highly artificial system; and if we have an immense superincumbent demand upon our currency, circumstances may arise to prevent our obtaining that currency. Is the whole commercial industry of the country to be paralysed? Shall we shut up all the avenues of employment?\" (5338) \"If the question should arise whether we should maintain specie payments, or whether we should maintain the industry of the country, I have no hesitation in saying which I should drop.\" This is a remarkable statement: Chapman prefers to suspend specie payments rather than paralyse industry. Marx's comment is implied rather than stated: the choice between specie payments and industry is a false one, created by the artificial constraints of the Bank Act. As to the hoarding of banknotes \"with a view to aggravate the pressure and to take advantage of the consequences\" (5358), Chapman says that this can happen very easily. Three major banks would suffice. (5383) \"Must it not be within your knowledge, as a man conversant with the great transactions of this metropolis, that capitalists do avail themselves of these crises to make enormous profit out of the ruin of the people who fall victims to them? — There can be no doubt about it.\" Marx comments: \"And we may well believe Mr Chapman here, even though he finally broke his own neck, commercially speaking, in an attempt to make 'enormous profit out of the ruin of the victims'.\" For if his partner Gurney says that any change in business is advantageous for someone who is well informed, Chapman says: \"The one section of the community knows nothing of the other; one is the manufacturer, for instance, who exports to the Continent, or imports his raw commodity; he knows nothing of the man who deals in bullion\" (5046). And this is how it happened that \"one day Gurney and Chapman were themselves not 'well informed', and fell into a notorious bankruptcy.\" The irony is that the speculators who profited from the ignorance of others were themselves ignorant of the impending crash, and they too fell victim to the system they exploited. Tooke on Advances of Capital without an Increase in Notes Marx now returns to Tooke's evidence before the House of Lords Committee of 1848 on Commercial Distress. Tooke testifies (3099) that the Bank of England \"might enlarge its advances greatly, and yet lead to no additional issue of notes.\" One of the most striking instances was in 1835, when the Bank made use of the West India deposits and of the loan from the East India Company in extended advances to the public, and \"at that time the amount of notes in the hands of the public was actually rather diminished.\" Something like the same discrepancy is observable in 1846 at the time of the payment of the railway deposits into the Bank: \"the securities' (in discount and deposits) 'were increased to about thirty million, while there was no perceptible effect upon the amount of notes in the hands of the public.\" This is a powerful empirical confirmation of the distinction between advancing capital and issuing means of circulation: the Bank can increase its advances without increasing the note circulation, because the notes it issues are immediately returned as deposits. Bills of Exchange as the Second Means of Circulation Marx now turns to the bill of exchange, \"a second and far more important means of circulation\" in wholesale trade. He recalls Chapman's insistence that \"good bills are always taken in payment under all circumstances\" and quotes Heine's lines: \"Gilt nicht mehr der Tausves Jontof, was soll gelten, Zeter, Zeter!\" — \"If the Tausves-Jontof's worthless, / What is any worth? God save us!\" (Heine, Disputation ). The Tausves-Jontof was a medieval commentary on the Hebrew Talmud; Marx uses the quotation ironically to suggest that the bill of exchange has become the new sacred text of the money market, the ultimate standard of value. The question Marx now poses is: \"How then are these two means of circulation related?\" He cites Gilbart ( An Inquiry into the Causes of the Pressure, etc. , p. 31): \"The reduction of the amount of the note circulation uniformly increases the amount of the bill circulation. These bills are of two classes — commercial bills and bankers' bills ... when money becomes scarce, the money-lenders say, 'draw upon us and we will accept'. And when a country banker discounts a bill for his customer, instead of giving him the cash, he will give him his own draft at twenty-one days upon his London agent. These bills serve the purpose of a currency.\" This is a crucial point: there is an inverse relationship between note circulation and bill circulation. When notes are scarce, bills take their place. The bill of exchange is a substitute for money, and its quantity expands when money is tight. Newmarch confirms this in modified form (B. A. 1857, no. 1426): \"There is no connection between the variations in the amount of bill circulation and the variations in the banknote circulation ... the only pretty uniform result is ... that whenever there is any pressure upon the money-market, as indicated by a rise in the rate of discount, then the volume of the bill circulation is very much increased, and vice versa.\" The bills drawn in a time of pressure, however, \"are in no way the short-term bank bills that Gilbart mentions. On the contrary, they are to a large extent bills of accommodation, which do not represent any real transactions at all, or only such as are embarked upon simply so as to draw bills on them.\" Marx notes that he has already given sufficient examples of both kinds. This introduces the concept of the accommodation bill — a bill drawn not to finance a real transaction but merely to create a means of payment. These bills are pure credit instruments, backed by nothing but the mutual confidence of the drawers and acceptors. The Economist (Wilson) comments on the security of such bills in comparison with banknotes: \"Notes payable on demand can never be kept out in excess, because the excess would always return to the bank for payment, while bills at two months may be issued in great excess, there being no means of checking the issue till they have arrived at maturity, when they may have been replaced by others. For a people to admit the safety of the circulation of bills payable only on a distant day, and to object to the safety of a circulation of paper payable on demand, is, to us, perfectly unaccountable\" ( The Economist , 1847, p. 575). This is a striking argument: bills are more dangerous than notes, because notes payable on demand are self-limiting (any excess returns to the issuer) while bills at two months can be issued in excess without any check until maturity. The Currency School's obsession with note issue thus misses the more dangerous form of credit money. Marx concludes: \"The amount of bills in circulation, therefore, just like the amount of banknotes, is determined solely by the needs of commerce; in the 1850s, the U.K. circulation in ordinary times, besides £39 million in banknotes, came to some £300 million in bills of exchange, of which £100–£120 million were on London alone.\" The scale of bill circulation has no influence on the volume of note circulation and is influenced by the latter \"solely in times of tight money, when the quantity of bills increases and their quality deteriorates.\" Finally, \"at the moment of crisis, the bill circulation completely collapses; no one has any use for promises to pay, each wanting only to accept cash payment; only the banknote still keeps its ability to circulate, at least up till now in England, since the Bank of England is backed by the entire wealth of the nation.\" This is the culminating statement of the section: in a crisis, the hierarchy of monies reasserts itself. Bills of exchange are rejected; only banknotes — backed by the Bank of England, which is in turn backed by the wealth of the nation — retain the ability to circulate. The credit system collapses back into the monetary system. The Power of Big Money Capitalists Marx returns to Chapman's complaint that \"there were several large money capitalists in London who were strong enough to bring the entire money market into disorder at a given moment and in this way fleece the smaller money-dealers most shamelessly.\" There were \"several great sharks of this kind who could significantly intensify a difficult situation by selling £1 or £2 millions worth of Consols and in this way taking an equivalent sum of banknotes (and thereby available loan capital) out of the market.\" The collaboration of three big banks in such a manoeuvre \"would suffice to turn a pressure into a panic.\" The biggest capital power in London is of course the Bank of England, \"but its position as a semi-state institution makes it impossible for it to assert its domination in so brutal a fashion. None the less, it too is sufficiently capable of looking after itself — particularly since the 1844 Bank Act.\" The Bank of England's Capital and Position Marx now gives the Bank of England's balance sheet data. It has a capital of £14,553,000, and besides this disposes of a \"balance\" of some £3 million, i.e. undistributed profits, as well as all monies that the government receives in taxes, etc., which have to be deposited with it until they are used. If we add to this the sum of other deposits (in ordinary times some £30 million) and the banknotes issued without reserve backing, then Newmarch seems quite moderate in his assessment when he says (B. A. 1857, no. 1889): \"I satisfied myself that the amount of funds constantly employed in the (London) money-market may be described as something like £120,000,000; and of that £120,000,000 a very considerable proportion, something like 15 or 20 per cent, is wielded by the Bank of England.\" Marx then makes a crucial theoretical point: \"In as much as the Bank issues notes that are not backed by the metal reserve in its vaults, it creates tokens of value that are not only means of circulation, but also form additional — even if fictitious — capital for it, to the nominal value of these fiduciary notes. And this extra capital yields it an extra profit.\" This is the theory of fiduciary issue as the creation of fictitious capital. When the Bank issues notes beyond the metal reserve, it creates value tokens that function as capital for the Bank, even though they are not backed by real metal. This is the source of the Bank's extra profit. Wilson asks Newmarch (1563): \"The circulation of a banker, so far as it is kept out upon the average, is an addition to the effective capital of that banker, is it not?\" Newmarch: \"Certainly.\" (1564) \"Then whatever profit he derives from that circulation is a profit derived from credit, and not from a capital which he actually possesses?\" Newmarch: \"Certainly.\" Marx notes that the same holds for note-issuing private banks. In his answers nos. 1866–1868, Newmarch considers two-thirds of all these issued notes (for the last third, these banks must have metal reserves) as \"the creation of so much capital,\" because this amount of metal money is saved. The banker's profit on this \"may not be greater than the profit of other capitalists. The fact remains that he draws his profit from this national saving on metal money. But the fact that a national saving appears as a private profit is in no way shocking to the bourgeois economist, since profit in general is the appropriation of the nation's labour.\" This is a biting piece of sarcasm: the bourgeois economist accepts that a national saving (the economizing on metal money) should appear as a private profit (the banker's profit), just as he accepts that profit in general is the appropriation of the nation's labour. Marx then asks: \"Is there anything more crazy than that between 1797 and 1817, for example, the Bank of England, whose notes only had credit thanks to the state, then got paid by the state, i.e. by the public, in the form of interest on government loans, for the power that the state gave it to transform these very notes from paper into money and lend them to the state?\" This is a devastating irony: the Bank's notes had value only because the state guaranteed them, and yet the Bank charged the state interest on the loans it made with these very notes. The Bank was, in effect, paid by the public for the privilege of lending the public its own credit. How Banks Create Credit and Capital Marx now summarizes the ways in which banks create credit and capital: \"(1) by issuing their own banknotes; (2) by writing drafts on London running for up to twenty-one days, which will however be paid to them in cash immediately they are written; (3) by re-issuing bills of exchange, whose creditworthiness is created first and foremost by the endorsement of the bank, at least for the district in question.\" He explains that the provincial banks are obliged to send their surplus funds (i.e. Bank of England notes) to London bill-brokers, who send them in exchange discounted bills. It is these bills which the provincial banks use to serve their customers, since their general rule is not to re-issue bills of exchange received from local clients, \"so that their business operations do not become known in their own locality.\" These bills of exchange received from London can be issued not only to clients who have direct payments to make in London, in case these do not prefer to get the bank's own draft on London; \"they also serve to settle payments in the provinces, for the banker's endorsement secures them local credit. In Lancashire, for example, they have driven out of circulation all the notes of the local banks and a great part of the Bank of England's notes\" (ibid., nos. 1568–1574). This is a concrete illustration of the way in which bank credit creates a circulation medium: the endorsed bill becomes a local currency, displacing the banknotes that would otherwise be required. Bank of England Power and Its Limits Marx now turns to the Bank of England's power to regulate the market rate of interest. \"In times when business runs its normal course, it may happen that the Bank of England cannot check a moderate drain of gold from its metal reserve by raising its discount rate, since the demand for means of payment is satisfied by the private and joint-stock banks, and by bill-brokers, who have acquired a great deal of capital power in the last thirty years.\" It then has to employ other means. But for critical moments, what banker Glyn (of Glyn, Mills, Currie & Co.) testified to the Committee on Commercial Distress (1848–57) still holds true: (1709) \"Under circumstances of great pressure upon the country the Bank of England commands the rate of interest.\" (1710) \"In times of extraordinary pressure ... whenever the discounts of the private bankers or brokers become comparatively limited, they fall upon the Bank of England, and then it is that the Bank of England has the power of commanding the market rate.\" Marx adds an important qualification: as a public institution under state protection, and endowed with state privileges, the Bank \"cannot exploit its power relentlessly, as a private firm can allow itself to do.\" This is why Hubbard stated to the Bank Acts Committee of 1857 (2844): \"That will always be the case, because the Bank of England never goes quite so low as its competitors, and when the rate is highest, it is never quite as high.\" The Bank's rates are always between those of its competitors: it is a ceiling in times of low rates and a floor in times of high rates. This reflects its semi-public character: it cannot compete aggressively for business, but neither can it exploit a crisis to the fullest extent. The footnote by Engels (footnote 12) gives an example from the stockholders' meeting of the Union Bank of London on 17 January 1894, where the chairman Mr Ritchie recalled how the Bank of England had put up its discount rate from 2.5 per cent (July) to 3 per cent and 4 per cent in August, and since despite this fully £4.5 million in gold had been lost, to 5 per cent, on which gold flowed back and the Bank rate was reduced to 4 per cent in September and 3 per cent in October. The point is that a moderate rise in the Bank rate did not stop the gold drain; only a more drastic rise did. Engels also notes that the Bank rate was not recognized in the market: \"When the bank-rate was 5 per cent, the discount rate was 3.5 per cent, and the rate for money 2.5 per cent; when the bank-rate fell to 4 per cent, the discount rate was 2.5 per cent and the money rate 1.5 per cent; when the bank-rate was 3 per cent, the discount rate fell to 1.5 per cent and the money rate to something below that\" ( Daily News , 18 January 1894). This illustrates the gap between the Bank rate and the market rate, and the limits of the Bank's control. The Screw and Its Effects: Gilbart Marx quotes Gilbart ( An Inquiry into the Causes of the Pressure on the Money-Market , London, 1840, p. 35): \"As soon as the Bank puts on the screw, all purchases for foreign exportation immediately cease ... the exporters wait until prices have reached the lowest point of depression, and then, and not till then, they make their purchases. But when this point has arrived, the exchanges have been rectified — gold ceases to be exported before the lowest point of depression has arrived. Purchases of goods for exportation may have the effect of bringing back some of the gold which has been sent abroad, but they come too late to prevent the drain.\" The \"screw\" is the Bank's practice of raising the discount rate sharply in order to stop a gold drain. Its effect is to halt purchases for export, which lowers domestic prices, which in turn corrects the exchanges. But the correction comes only after a period of depression, and the gold has already left the country. Gilbart adds (p. 40): \"Another effect of regulating the currency by the foreign exchanges is that it leads in seasons of pressure to an enormous rate of interest.\" And (p. 52): \"The cost of rectifying the exchanges falls upon the productive industry of the country, while during the process the profits of the Bank of England are actually augmented in consequence of carrying on her business with a less amount of treasure.\" This is a precise statement of the distributive consequences of the Bank's policy: the burden of adjustment falls on industry, while the Bank's profits increase. The Profits of the Bank of England and the Bank of Ireland Marx quotes Samuel Gurney: \"The great fluctuations in the rate of interest are advantageous to bankers and dealers in money — all fluctuations in trade are advantageous to the knowing man.\" And he adds: \"even if the Gurneys skim off the cream by a ruthless exploitation of the commercial distress, while the Bank of England cannot do this with the same freedom, quite handsome profits fall its way too — not to speak of the private profits that come the way of the gentlemen directors as a result of their exceptional opportunity for knowing the overall state of business.\" Marx then presents the data on the Bank of England's profits for the period 1797–1817, from D. Hardcastle, Banks and Bankers , 2nd edn, London, 1843, p. 120. The totals are: bonuses and increased dividends £7,451,136; new stock divided among proprietors £7,276,500; increased value of capital £14,553,000; total £29,280,636 — \"this being for nineteen years on a capital of £11,642,400.\" If we calculate the total profits of the Bank of Ireland, which also suspended cash payment in 1797, according to the same principle, we arrive at: dividends as by returns due 1821 £4,736,085; declared bonus £1,225,000; increased assets £1,214,800; increased value of capital £4,185,000; total £11,360,885 — \"on a capital of £3 million\" (ibid., pp. 363–4). Marx's comment: \"Talk about centralization!\" These figures are striking: the Bank of England, on a capital of £11.6 million, earned profits of £29.3 million over nineteen years — a return of more than 250 per cent. The Bank of Ireland, on a capital of £3 million, earned £11.4 million — a return of nearly 380 per cent. These profits were earned during the period of the Bank Restriction, when the Bank's notes were inconvertible and the Bank was lending these notes to the government at interest. Marx's point is that the credit system, far from being a neutral mechanism for allocating capital, is a machine for the centralization of wealth in the hands of a parasitic class. The Centralization of the Credit System Marx concludes the chapter with a powerful statement of the credit system's class character: \"The credit system, which has its focal point in the allegedly national banks and the big money-lenders and usurers that surround them, is one enormous centralization and gives this class of parasites a fabulous power not only to decimate the industrial capitalists periodically but also to interfere in actual production in the most dangerous manner — and this crew know nothing of production and have nothing at all to do with it.\" This is the culminating social judgment of the chapter. The credit system is not merely a technical mechanism; it is a structure of power. It centralizes wealth in the hands of a class that is external to production — \"this crew know nothing of production and have nothing at all to do with it\" — and that uses its power to \"decimate the industrial capitalists periodically\" and to \"interfere in actual production in the most dangerous manner.\" The Acts of 1844 and 1845 are \"proof of the growing power of these bandits, added to whom are the financiers and stock-jobbers.\" The Banker as a Moral Institution: The Closing Irony Marx closes the chapter with an ironic quotation from G. M. Bell, a Scottish bank director, in The Philosophy of Joint-Stock Banking , London, 1840, pp. 46–47: \"Banking establishments are ... moral and religious institutions ... How often has the fear of being seen by the watchful and reproving eye of his banker deterred the young tradesman from joining the company of riotous and extravagant friends? ... What has been his anxiety to stand well in the estimation of his banker? ... Has not the frown of his banker been of more influence with him than the jeers and discouragements of his friends? Has he not trembled to be supposed guilty of deceit or the slightest misstatement, lest it should give rise to suspicion, and his accommodation be in consequence restricted or discontinued? ... And has not that friendly advice been of more value to him than that of priest?\" Marx's irony is devastating. The banker, who in the preceding pages has been exposed as a parasite and a bandit — who manipulates the money market for his own profit, who exploits the distress of the industrialists, who creates fictitious capital — is here presented by his own apologist as a \"moral and religious institution,\" a guardian of the young tradesman's virtue. The juxtaposition is the point. The moral pretensions of the banking class are a mask for its predatory reality. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles Revealed Several fundamental principles emerge from this chapter. First, and most importantly: the quantity of circulating media is determined by the needs of trade, not by the will of the banks or the state of the gold reserve. This is the chapter's central technical claim, and Marx substantiates it with an impressive array of empirical evidence, from Neave's testimony that the public can always take notes from the Bank, to Kinnear's demonstration that £6 million in gold could be exported without any contraction of the currency, to Tooke's observation that the Bank could increase its advances without increasing the note circulation. The Currency School's premise — that note issue determines prices and that over-issue causes crises — is refuted by the simple fact that, as long as notes are convertible, any excess is immediately returned to the issuer. Second: the rate of interest is determined by the demand for and supply of loanable capital, not by the quantity of circulation. The chapter provides a series of historical examples (1797–1820, 1822–23, 1824–25, 1830, 1832, 1836, and the post-1851 gold discoveries) in which high circulation coexists with low interest and vice versa. The apparent dependence of the interest rate on the quantity of circulation in periods of pressure is an illusion; what is really happening is that the demand for loanable money has become absolute because credit has collapsed, and the quantity of circulation appears to determine the rate only because the hoarding of notes reduces the available loanable capital. Third: credit is the regulator of the velocity of circulation in a double sense — it founds all methods of economizing on circulating media, and it mediates and accelerates the velocity of the money that circulates. The clearing house, the bill of exchange, the deposit-and-loan cycle — all rest on credit; and the same piece of money can perform more transactions in a given time when its movement is mediated by credit operations. The corollary is that credit and money are not rivals but complements: credit does not replace money so much as it allows a given quantity of money to do more work. Fourth: the credit system rests on a metallic base that is utterly inadequate to its superstructure. Chapman's £300 million in liabilities against £23 million in coin is the emblematic statement of this disproportion. In normal times, the disproportion is invisible because credit functions as a substitute for money; in a crisis, the disproportion becomes catastrophic because credit collapses back into a demand for money. This is the principle that later theorists — from Marx to Keynes and Minsky — would recognize as the fundamental instability of a monetary economy. Fifth: the credit system is a machine for the centralization of wealth in the hands of a parasitic class. This is the social judgment that Marx weaves into the technical analysis. The credit system is not neutral; it concentrates power in the hands of the banks, the bill-brokers, and the big money-lenders, and it enables them to exploit the industrial capitalists and to interfere in production. The Bank Act of 1844, far from restraining this power, intensifies it by compelling the Bank to contract its issues at the moment of greatest need. The profits of the Bank of England (£29.3 million over nineteen years) and the Bank of Ireland (£11.4 million) are the material expression of this power. The Broader Theoretical Implications The chapter has profound implications for the theory of money and credit. It establishes that money and credit are not separate systems but a single system with a hierarchical structure. At the apex is metallic world money, which serves as the ultimate reserve and as the guarantee of the convertibility of credit money. Below it is banknote money, which circulates as a substitute for metal and which, in England, is backed by the entire wealth of the nation. Below that is the bill of exchange, which circulates as a substitute for banknotes and which, in times of tight money, expands even as the note circulation contracts. At the bottom are accommodation bills and other pure credit instruments, which have no counterpart in real transactions. In a crisis, the hierarchy is reversed: the lower forms are rejected, and the demand concentrates on the higher forms. This is what Marx means by the \"collapse of the credit system into the monetary system.\" The chapter also establishes the essential point that the issuance of means of circulation is not the same as the lending of capital. When a bank discounts a bill, it advances money , not capital . The capital being circulated already exists; the bank merely provides the medium through which it circulates. This distinction is crucial to understanding why the Bank's increased advances need not increase the note circulation (Tooke's evidence), and why the demand for money in a crisis is not a demand for capital (Chapman's exchange with Cayley). The confusion between money and capital is the fundamental error of the Currency School, and Marx's insistence on the distinction is one of his most enduring contributions to monetary theory. The Practical and Political Implications The practical implications of the chapter are political. If the quantity of circulation is determined by the needs of trade, then the Bank Act of 1844 is unnecessary and harmful. It is unnecessary because the note circulation regulates itself; it is harmful because it compels the Bank to contract its issues precisely when the world is crying out for notes. Marx's evidence from 1847 and 1857 — that the mere suspension of the Act was sufficient to end the panic, without any actual increase in circulation — demonstrates that the Act is a self-inflicted wound. The choice that Chapman poses between specie payments and the industry of the country is a false choice, created by the Act. The deeper political implication is that the credit system gives a class of parasites \"fabulous power\" over production. This class does not produce anything; it lives on the credit that it controls. It uses its power to \"decimate the industrial capitalists periodically\" and to exploit the distress of the community. The moral pretensions of the banker, exposed in Bell's homily, are a mask for this predatory reality. Marx's analysis thus points beyond the critique of monetary policy to the critique of the credit system as a whole, and to the necessity of bringing it under social control. The Lessons for Today For the modern reader, the chapter offers several lessons. First, it teaches the importance of distinguishing between money and credit, and between the quantity of money and the rate of interest. The chapter's empirical demonstration that the interest rate does not depend on the quantity of circulation is a permanent contribution to monetary theory, and it anticipates the modern understanding of the interest rate as determined by the supply and demand for loanable funds. Second, it teaches the essential instability of a credit economy. The chapter's account of the crisis — the hoarding, the steeplechase for means of payment, the collapse of the bill circulation, the sudden disappearance of banknotes from the market — is a vivid and enduring description of financial panic. The disproportion between the vast structure of credit and the slender metallic base on which it rests is the fundamental source of this instability, and it is a lesson that the financial crises of the twentieth and twenty-first centuries have repeatedly confirmed. Third, it teaches the political economy of the money market. The chapter's exposure of the Bank of England's power, its profits, and its manipulation of the interest rate is a case study in the relationship between economic power and political power. The Bank is a \"semi-state institution\" that uses its state privileges to enrich its shareholders and to discipline the economy. The \"screw\" is the instrument of this discipline, and its cost falls on \"the productive industry of the country.\" Fourth, it teaches the importance of reading the sources critically. Marx's method in this chapter — reading the bankers' testimony against the grain, exposing their self-interest, and drawing out the implications of their own admissions — is a model of critical scholarship. The bankers tell us more than they intend to; they reveal the inner workings of the credit system even as they defend it. Finally, the chapter teaches the enduring truth that the credit system is not a neutral mechanism but a structure of power. The bankers and bill-brokers who dominate the money market are not passive intermediaries; they are active participants in the exploitation of the industrial capitalists. The \"moral and religious institution\" of the banker is a façade for a system of exploitation, and the task of political economy is to penetrate that façade. This is the deepest lesson of the chapter, and it is the one that gives it its enduring relevance. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 32 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 34"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 34", "title": "Chapter 34", "subtitle": "The Currency Principle and the English Bank Legislation of 1844", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 34/Chapter 34 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 11099, "read_time": "55 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital"], "keywords": ["Currency Principle and the Bank Act of 1844", "Robert Peel's Bank Charter Act Deconstructed", "How the Bank Act Exacerbated the 1847 and 1857 Crises", "Artificial Restriction of Banknotes during Severe Panic", "Parliamentary Suspensions of the Bank Charter Act"], "text": "Chapter 34 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 11,087 words ⏱️ ~55 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 34 of Capital Volume 3 — \"The Currency Principle and the English Bank Legislation of 1844\" — constitutes one of Marx's most concentrated exercises in the critique of political economy, operating simultaneously on three interlocking fronts: theoretical (the demolition of Ricardo's quantity theory of money), doctrinal (the exposure of the Currency School's dogma as a tautology dressed in causal clothing), and institutional (the empirical indictment of Sir Robert Peel's Bank Acts of 1844–45 as a mechanism for transferring the profits of industry into the pockets of usurers). The chapter's central problem is deceptively narrow — how did a specific, flawed monetary theory acquire the force of law and govern the banking system of the world's most advanced capitalist economy? — but its intellectual objective is far broader: to demonstrate that the appearance of scientific rigor in bourgeois economics frequently conceals a self-serving circularity, and that the translation of such circularity into legislation produces not stability but intensified crisis. The chapter's core thesis can be stated as follows. Ricardo's theory of the value of money — that the value of metal money is determined by labour-time objectified in it, so long as the quantity of money stands in the right proportion to the quantity and price of commodities to be exchanged — contains a genuine insight (the possibility that the aggregate means of circulation may appreciate or depreciate as a whole) that was pressed into service by Lord Overstone, Colonel Torrens, Norman, Clay, Arbuthnot, and the \"currency school\" as the foundation for Peel's Bank Acts. But the theory, as deployed by the currency school, is not a causal explanation at all: it is a tautology masquerading as one. \"Prices rise and fall periodically, because periodically there is too much or too little money in circulation.\" The Currency School's practical program — to regulate the issue of banknotes strictly according to the import and export of precious metals, thereby imitating the supposed laws of a purely metallic currency — rests on Ricardo's wrong assumption \"that gold is simply specie,\" i.e., that all imported gold augments the money in circulation and thus raises prices, and all exported gold decreases the quantity of specie and thus lowers prices. In fact, Marx argues, gold flows function primarily as fluctuations in the rate of interest, not as direct determinants of commodity prices; and the Bank Act, by separating the Bank of England into an Issue Department and a Banking Department and by rigidly linking the note issue to the metal reserve, withdraws means of circulation at the very moment when they are most urgently needed , thereby \"directly provoking the entire world of commerce into meeting the outbreak of a crisis by putting aside a reserve stock of banknotes,\" accelerating and intensifying the crisis and driving the interest rate \"up to a previously unheard-of level.\" Instead of abolishing crises, the Act intensifies them to a point at which either \"the entire world of industry has to collapse, or else the Bank Act\" — and on two occasions (25 October 1847 and 12 November 1857), the government was compelled to suspend the Act to save the economy. The chapter fits into the broader architecture of Capital Volume 3 as a bridge between the theory of credit (developed in the preceding chapters, particularly Chapter 26 on the banking system and Chapter 28 on the relationship between money and capital) and the analysis of the \"division of profit into interest and profit of enterprise\" (the section of which this chapter is a part). It is a case study — indeed, the most detailed historical case study in the volume — of the way in which the specific forms of the credit system (the separation of money-dealing capital from industrial capital, the transformation of gold into a \"reserve,\" the operation of the discount rate) generate ideological illusions that then feedback into legislation, which in turn exacerbates the very contradictions of the capitalist mode of production. The chapter thus operates at the intersection of economic theory, intellectual history, and political economy: it shows how a theory becomes an \"ideology\" in the precise Marxian sense — a set of ideas that reflects and serves particular class interests while claiming universal validity. Crucially, the chapter is also a sustained exercise in empirical argumentation. Marx does not merely assert that the currency theory is false; he mobilizes the testimony of bankers, merchants, and manufacturers before the House of Commons Committee of 1857 (B. A. 1857), the House of Lords Committee of 1848 (C. D. 1848–57), and produces tabular data (Hubbard's tables of 1834–43 and 1844–53) to demonstrate that commodity prices were \"entirely independent of the amount of circulation\" and that the only systematic relationship was between gold flows and the rate of interest. The chapter's rhetorical structure — alternating between theoretical critique, institutional description, and witness testimony — is designed to produce a cumulative, overwhelming effect, culminating in the bitter irony of Overstone's dithyramb to the Committee on 14 July 1857 and its \"antistrophe\" on 12 November 1857, when the government suspended \"the miracle-working act of 1844, in order to save what could still be saved.\" 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Opening Frame: Ricardo's Quantity Theory and Its \"Discovery\" The chapter opens with an editorial parenthesis marked \"(F. E.)\" — indicating the hand of Friedrich Engels, who edited Volume 3 after Marx's death — that refers back to Marx's earlier work, A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy (1859), specifically pages 169ff. Engels explains that Marx had already investigated Ricardo's theory of the value of money in relation to commodity prices in that earlier text, so the present chapter can confine itself to \"what is most essential.\" This is not merely a scholarly cross-reference; it establishes the genealogical character of the chapter. Marx (or Engels, in this editorial framing) is tracing a specific theoretical lineage: Ricardo's monetary doctrine → the Currency School → Peel's Bank Acts. The point is to show that the legislation of 1844 is the practical implementation of a theoretical error, and that the error can only be understood by returning to its origin. The opening paragraph lays out Ricardo's position with precision. According to Ricardo, the value of (metal) money is determined by the labour-time objectified in it, but only as long as the quantity of money stands in the right proportion to the quantity and price of the commodities to be exchanged. This \"but only as long as\" clause is the hinge on which everything turns. If the quantity of money rises above this proportion, its value falls and commodity prices rise; if it falls below the right proportion, its value rises and commodity prices fall — \"as long as other factors remain the same.\" The mechanism of correction is the international flow of gold: a country with a surplus of gold (whose value has fallen) exports gold and imports commodities; a country where gold is priced above its value will see gold flow in, while its under-valued commodities flow to other markets where they can obtain normal prices. Engels then quotes Marx's earlier formulation directly: \"Since on these assumptions 'even gold in the form of coin or bullion can become a value-token representing a larger or smaller value than its own, it is obvious that any convertible banknotes that are in circulation must share the same fate.'\" This is a crucial step. Although banknotes are convertible and their real value corresponds to their nominal value, \"the aggregate currency consisting of metal and of convertible notes\" may appreciate or depreciate if the total quantity rises above or falls below the level determined by the exchange-value of the commodities in circulation and the metallic value of gold. This depreciation — not of notes in relation to gold, but of gold and notes taken together, i.e., of the aggregate means of circulation of a country — is identified as \"one of Ricardo's main discoveries, which Lord Overstone and Co. pressed into their service and turned into a fundamental principle of Sir Robert Peel's bank legislation of 1844 and 1845.\" Here we encounter the first important conceptual distinction in the chapter. The depreciation Marx is describing is not the familiar phenomenon of inconvertible paper money losing value relative to gold (the phenomenon Hume confronted in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, and Ricardo confronted during the Napoleonic wars). It is something subtler and, Marx argues, theoretically confused: the idea that the aggregate of gold plus convertible notes can appreciate or depreciate as a whole. This is what allows the Currency School to argue that even a purely metallic system can experience general price movements caused by \"too much\" or \"too little\" money, and that the same logic applies to a mixed system of gold and banknotes. Engels notes explicitly: \"We do not need to repeat here the demonstration of the absurdity of this theory of Ricardo's that is given in that text. All that concerns us now is the form and manner in which Ricardo's doctrines were taken up and elaborated by the school of banking theorists who dictated Peel's Bank Acts.\" The chapter's scope is thus deliberately narrowed — not to refute Ricardo again, but to show how his doctrine was weaponized. 2.2 The Historical Shift: From Single Phenomena to World-Market Storms The next conceptual beat marks a historical transition. \"The commercial crises of the nineteenth century, and in particular the great crises of 1825 and 1836, did not lead to any further development of Ricardo's currency theory, but rather to new practical applications of it.\" This is a precise and important distinction. The crises did not generate new theory; they generated new uses for existing theory. The reason lies in the changed character of the phenomena. It was \"no longer a matter of single economic phenomena — such as the depreciation of precious metals in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries confronting Hume, or the depreciation of paper currency during the eighteenth century and the beginning of the nineteenth confronting Ricardo — but of big storms on the world market, in which the antagonism of all the elements in the bourgeois process of production explodes.\" The crises of 1825 and 1836 were not local or sectoral; they were systemic convulsions of the world market. And characteristically, \"the origin of these storms and the means of defence against them were sought within the sphere of currency, the most superficial and abstract sphere of this process.\" This phrase — \"the most superficial and abstract sphere of this process\" — is a compressed statement of Marx's materialist method. Currency is \"superficial\" because it is the phenomenal form in which deeper contradictions (overproduction, the falling rate of profit, the anarchy of production) appear; it is \"abstract\" because it is the most mediated and generalized expression of the totality of capitalist relations. To seek the cause of crises in the sphere of currency is therefore to mistake the surface for the essence — a methodological error that is not innocent, because it directs attention away from the real causes of crisis (the antagonisms of the bourgeois process of production) and toward technical fixes (banking legislation) that cannot possibly address them. The \"theoretical assumption which actually serves the school of economic weather experts as their point of departure\" is \"the dogma that Ricardo had discovered the laws governing purely metallic currency.\" Marx's term \"economic weather experts\" ( ökonomische Wetterkundige or similar) is a mocking designation for the Currency School theorists, who treated economic crises as meteorological events to be predicted and managed rather than as structural necessities. It was \"thus left to them to subsume the circulation of credit money or banknotes under these laws.\" 2.3 The Tautology Critique: Ricardo's Theory as Circular Reasoning The heart of Marx's theoretical critique comes in the discussion of the \"most common and conspicuous phenomenon accompanying commercial crises\": \"a sudden fall in the general level of commodity prices occurring after a prolonged general rise of prices.\" Marx notes that a general fall of commodity prices may be expressed as a rise in the value of money relative to all other commodities, and a general rise of prices may be defined as a fall in the relative value of money. But — and here is the logical pivot — \"Either of these statements describes the phenomenon but does not explain it... The different terminology has just as little effect on the task itself as a translation of the terms from German into English would have.\" The critique is devastating in its simplicity. \"Ricardo's monetary theory proved to be singularly apposite since it gave to a tautology the semblance of a causal relation.\" What is the cause of the general fall in commodity prices? It is the periodically occurring rise in the relative value of money. What is the cause of the recurrent general rise in commodity prices? It is the recurrent fall in the relative value of money. But \"It would be just as correct to say that the recurrent rise and fall of prices is brought about by their recurrent rise and fall.\" Once the tautology is converted into a causal relationship — \"once the transformation of the tautology into a causal relationship is taken for granted, everything else follows easily.\" The rise in commodity prices is due to a fall in the value of money; the fall in the value of money is due to excessive currency (i.e., the amount of money in circulation rises above the level determined by its own intrinsic value and the intrinsic value of commodities); and the general fall of commodity prices is due to the value of money rising above its intrinsic value as a result of insufficient currency. \"Prices therefore rise and fall periodically, because periodically there is too much or too little money in circulation.\" Marx then exposes the unfalsifiability of this schema — a point that anticipates modern philosophy of science critiques of pseudoscience. \"If it is proved, for instance, that the rise of prices coincided with a decreased amount of money in circulation, and the fall of prices with an increased amount, then it is nevertheless possible to assert that, in consequence of some reduction or increase — which can in no way be ascertained statistically — of commodities in circulation, the amount of money in circulation has relatively, though not absolutely, increased or decreased.\" The theory is rescued by the introduction of an unobservable variable: the quantity of commodities in circulation, which can always be invoked ad hoc to reconcile the theory with contrary evidence. This is the structure of a metaphysical rather than an empirical theory. Marx then returns to Ricardo's own account of the purely metallic case to show that, even on Ricardo's own terms, the mechanism is self-correcting. An insufficient amount of currency brings about a fall in commodity prices; the fall stimulates an export of commodities; the export leads to an influx of money; the influx causes a rise in commodity prices. When there is excessive currency, the reverse occurs. \"Since notwithstanding these general price movements, which arise from the very nature of Ricardo's metallic currency, their severe and vehement form, the form of crisis, belongs to periods with developed credit systems, it is clear that the issue of banknotes is not exactly governed by the laws of metallic currency.\" This is a crucial analytical step: the empirical phenomenon of crisis is qualitatively different from the theoretical phenomenon of metallic-currency price oscillation. The former is violent and destructive; the latter is gentle and self-correcting. The attempt to explain the former by the latter therefore fails on Ricardo's own premises. 2.4 The Currency School's Practical Program and Its Critics From this theoretical demolition, Marx turns to the practical program. The remedy applicable to metallic currency is the import and export of precious metals, which are immediately thrown into circulation as coin, causing commodity prices to fall or rise. \"The banks must now artificially exert the same influence on commodity prices by imitating the laws of metallic currency.\" If gold flows in from abroad, it is a proof that there is an insufficient amount of currency, that the value of money is too high and commodity prices too low — and banknotes must therefore be thrown into circulation in accordance with the newly imported gold. Conversely, banknotes must be taken out of circulation in accordance with an outflow of gold. \"In other words the issue of banknotes must be regulated according to the import and export of the precious metals or according to the rate of exchange.\" The theoretical error on which this practical program rests is now named explicitly: \"Ricardo's wrong assumption that gold is simply specie and that consequently the whole of the imported gold is used to augment the money in circulation thus causing prices to rise, and that the whole of the gold exported represents a decrease in the amount of specie and thus causes prices to fall.\" This assumption is \"turned into a practical experiment by making the amount of specie in circulation correspond always to the quantity of gold in the country.\" The school's roster is then named: \"Lord Overstone (Jones Loyd, the banker), Colonel Torrens, Norman, Clay, Arbuthnot and numerous other writers known in England as the 'currency school'.\" They \"have not only preached this doctrine, but have made it the basis of the present English and Scottish banking legislation by means of Sir Robert Peel's Bank Acts of 1844 and 1845.\" Marx notes that \"the analysis of the ignominious fiasco they suffered both in theory and practice, after experiments on the largest national scale, can only be made in the section dealing with the theory of credit.\" This is again a delimitation of scope: the full theoretical treatment of credit is elsewhere; what is pursued here is the historical and empirical indictment. The chapter then notes the critics: \"Thomas Tooke, James Wilson (in The Economist of 1844–7) and John Fullarton.\" Marx is careful to note that \"We have already seen several times, particularly in Chapter 28 of this volume, how inadequately these critics penetrated the nature of gold and how unclear they were as to the relationship between money and capital.\" This is an important qualification: Marx is not simply aligning himself with the \"banking school\" against the \"currency school.\" The critics, too, failed to grasp the fundamental issues. The remainder of the chapter is organized around witness testimony before the parliamentary committees, beginning with the House of Commons Committee of 1857 on the Peel Bank Acts (B. A. 1857). 2.5 Hubbard's Evidence and the Two Tables: The Independence of Commodity Prices J. G. Hubbard, a former Governor of the Bank of England, testifies (no. 2400) that \"The effect of the export of bullion... has no reference whatever to the prices of commodities. It has an effect, and a very important one, upon the price of interest-bearing securities, because, as the rate of interest varies, the value of commodities which embodied that interest is necessarily powerfully affected.\" This is a direct empirical refutation of the currency theory: gold flows affect the rate of interest , and through it the prices of securities (i.e., capitalized claims to income), but not the prices of commodities as such. Hubbard produces two tables for the years 1834–43 and 1845–56 (the chapter presents the first two, 1834–43 and 1844–53), covering fifteen of the most important items of commerce. The items are: raw cotton, cotton yarn, cotton fabrics, wool, woollen cloth, flax, linen, indigo, pig-iron, tin, copper, tallow, sugar, coffee, and silk. For each date, the tables record the bullion reserve of the Bank, the market rate of discount, and the number of items whose price increased, decreased, or remained unchanged. Table I (1834–43) shows, for example: on 1 March 1834, bullion reserve £9,104,000, discount rate 2¾%; on 1 March 1835, bullion £6,274,000 (a decrease), discount rate 3¾% (an increase), with 7 items rising, 7 falling, 1 unchanged; on 1 March 1836, bullion £7,918,000, discount 3¾–4%, 11 rising, 3 falling, 1 unchanged; on 1 March 1837, bullion £4,077,000, discount 5%, 5 rising, 9 falling, 1 unchanged. Table II (1844–53) shows: 1 March 1844, bullion £16,162,000, discount 2¼%; 1 Dec. 1845, bullion £13,237,000, discount 4½%, 11 rising, 4 falling; 1 Sept. 1846, bullion £16,366,000, discount 3%, 7 rising, 8 falling; 1 Sept. 1847, bullion £9,140,000, discount 6%, 6 rising, 6 falling, 3 unchanged; 1 March 1850, bullion £17,126,000, discount 2½%, 5 rising, 9 falling, 1 unchanged; 1 June 1851, bullion £13,705,000, discount 3%, 2 rising, 11 falling, 2 unchanged; 1 Sept. 1852, bullion £21,853,000, discount 1½%, 9 rising, 5 falling, 1 unchanged; 1 Dec. 1853, bullion £15,093,000, discount 5%, 14 rising, 1 unchanged. Hubbard's comment on these tables is decisive: \"As in the years 1834–43, so in the period 1844–53, the upward and downward movements in the bullion of the Bank have invariably been accompanied with a decrease or increase in the loanable value of money advanced on discount; and, as in the earlier period, so in this, the variations in the prices of commodities in this country exhibit an entire independence of the amount of circulation as shown in the fluctuations in bullion of the Bank of England.\" In other words: bullion flows move inversely with the discount rate (gold out = interest rate up; gold in = interest rate down), but commodity prices show no systematic relationship to either. Marx's commentary draws the methodological conclusion. \"Since the demand and supply of commodities regulates their market price, it is evident here how false is Overstone's identification of the demand for loanable money capital (or rather the gap between its demand and supply), as expressed in the discount rate, with the demand for real 'capital'.\" This is a critical conceptual distinction: the demand for loanable money capital (reflected in the discount rate) is not the same as the demand for real capital (means of production and labor-power). The currency school obscures the difference by claiming that fluctuations in the discount rate express fluctuations in the demand for \"actual material capital, as distinct from money capital.\" Marx refers back to Chapter 26, where Norman and Overstone maintained this before the committee and \"to what weak subterfuges they were forced to resort, particularly the latter until he was finally cornered.\" The \"old humbug\" is then stated plainly: \"the old humbug that changes in the quantity of gold, since they increase or decrease the amount of means of circulation in a country, must necessarily raise or lower commodity prices there.\" The currency theory's logic would imply that if gold is exported, commodity prices in the gold-importing country rise, and the value of the gold-exporting country's exports rise on the gold-importing country's market, while the latter's exports fall in value. But \"In actual fact, a decline in the amount of gold simply raises the rate of interest, while an increase lowers it; and if these fluctuations in the interest rate did not come into account in establishing the cost price, or determining demand and supply, commodity prices would be completely unaffected.\" 2.6 The Silver Drain to Asia: N. Alexander's Testimony Marx next introduces the testimony of N. Alexander, head of a big firm in the India trade, on the sharp drain of silver to India and China in the mid-1850s. This drain had two causes: partly the Chinese civil war, which stopped the supply of English fabrics to China, and partly the silkworm disease in Europe, which sharply reduced silk production in Italy and France. Alexander testifies (no. 4337) that the silver was sent to India, where much of it was used to buy opium, which then went on to China \"to lay down funds for the purchase of the silk\"; and that \"the state of the markets in India (in spite of the accumulation of silver there) makes it a more profitable investment for the merchant to lay down silver than to send piece-goods or English manufactures.\" (No. 4338) A great drain of silver from France also occurred. (No. 4344) \"Instead of bringing in silk from France and Italy, we are sending it there in large quantities, both from Bengal and from China.\" Marx's analytic point is sharp: \"Thus silver was sent to Asia, where it is the money metal, instead of commodities, not because the price of these commodities had risen in the country that produced them (England), but because it had fallen — owing to excessive imports — in the country which imported them; even though England had to obtain this silver from France, and to pay for it partly with gold.\" This is a direct empirical refutation of the currency theory's prediction. \"According to the currency theory, prices should have fallen in England and risen in India and China as a result of such imports.\" In fact, commodities were not sent because their prices were too high in England; silver was sent because commodity prices in the importing countries had fallen due to excessive imports. The direction of causation runs opposite to the theory. 2.7 Wylie's Testimony: The Cotton Example and the Self-Interest of Overstone's \"Wisdom\" Wylie, one of the leading Liverpool merchants, testified before the House of Lords Committee (C. D. 1848–57). His testimony (no. 1994) describes the cotton-spinning boom of 1845: \"At the close of 1845 there was no trade that was more remunerating, and in which there were such large profits\" than cotton spinning. The stock of cotton was large and good; cotton could be bought at 4d. per pound; good secunda mule twist no. 40 was made at an expense not exceeding 8d. per pound in all to the spinner. This yarn was largely sold and contracted for in September and October 1845 at 10½ and 11½d. per pound, \"and in some instances the spinners realized a profit equal to the first cost of the cotton.\" (No. 1996) The trade continued remunerative until the beginning of 1846. (No. 2000) \"On 3 March 1844, the stock of cotton\" (627,042 bales) \"was more than double what it is this day\" (on 7 March 1848, 301,070 bales), \"and yet the price then was 6¼d. per pound dearer\" (6¼d. as against 5d.). At the same time, yarn (good secunda mule twist no. 40) had fallen from 11½–12d. to 9½d. per lb. in October, and to 7¾d. at the end of December 1847; yarn was sold at the purchase price of the cotton from which it had been spun (nos. 2021, 2022). Marx draws the inference: \"This exposes the self-interest in Overstone's wisdom that money is 'dear' because capital is 'scarce'.\" The data show that the Bank rate stood at 3 per cent on 3 March 1844; rose to 8 and 9 per cent in October and November 1847; and fell to 4 per cent on 7 March 1848. \"Owing to the complete stagnation in sales and the panic, with its correspondingly high rate of interest, cotton prices had been driven far below the level corresponding to the supply.\" The result was a tremendous decline in imports in 1848 and a decline in production in America, hence a new rise in cotton prices in 1849. \"According to Overstone, the reason why commodities were too dear was that there was too much money in the country.\" Wylie's testimony (no. 2002) states: \"The late decline in the condition of the cotton manufacturers is not to be ascribed to the want of the raw material, as the price seems to have been lower, though the stock of the raw material is very much diminished.\" Marx comments: \"But Overstone conveniently confuses the price or value of a commodity with the value of money, i.e. the rate of interest.\" This is a conceptual confusion central to the currency theory: the \"dearness\" of money (high interest rate) is conflated with the \"dearness\" of capital (scarcity of real capital). In answer to question 2026, Wylie delivers his overall verdict on the currency theory: \"These principles seemed to me to be of a nature that would give an artificial high value to money and an artificial and ruinously low value to all commodities and produce.\" This verdict was relevant because Cardwell and Sir Charles Wood (the latter, Sir Charles Wood, later Viscount Halifax, was Chancellor of the Exchequer at the time; Edward, Viscount Cardwell, was a Peelite) in May 1847 \"asserted the necessity of carrying out the Bank Act of 1844 in its full and entire integrity.\" (The editorial footnote explains these identities.) Wylie goes on to say of the effects of the Bank Act on the general state of business: \"As bills at four months, which is the regular course of drafts, from manufacturing towns on merchants and bankers for the purchase of goods going to the United States, could not be discounted except at great sacrifices, the execution of orders was checked to a great extent, until after the Government Letter of 25 October\" (suspension of the Bank Act), \"when those four months' bills became discountable\" (2097). The suspension of the Bank Act \"brought salvation for the provinces, too.\" (No. 2102) \"Last October [1847] there was scarcely an American buyer purchasing goods here who did not at once curtail his orders as much as he possibly could; and when our advices of the dearness of money reached America, all fresh orders ceased.\" (No. 2134) \"Corn and sugar were special. The corn market was affected by the prospects of the harvest, and sugar was affected by the immense stocks and imports.\" (No. 2163) \"Of our indebtedness to America... much was liquidated by forced sales of consigned goods, and I fear that much was cancelled by the failures here.\" (No. 2196) \"If I recollect rightly, 70 per cent was paid on our Stock Exchange in October 1847.\" 2.8 The Ideological Conditions for the Bank Act's Passage An editorial parenthesis (F. E.) inserts an assessment of the historical conditions that allowed the currency school's dogma to be translated into practice. \"The crisis of 1837 with its long aftermath, added to which was a further regular crisis in 1842, and the self-interested blindness of the industrialists and merchants, who were resolved not to notice any overproduction — this was all nonsense and an impossibility, said the vulgar economists! — all this finally brought about the mental confusion that allowed the currency school to translate their dogma into practice on a national scale. And thus the Bank legislation of 1844–5 went through.\" The passage is significant because it identifies the ideological mechanism: the refusal to recognize overproduction (which the \"vulgar economists\" treated as impossible) created a \"mental confusion\" that made the currency school's technical solution seem plausible. If crises cannot be caused by overproduction, they must be caused by something else — and the most superficial and abstract sphere, the sphere of currency, is the natural candidate. 2.9 The Mechanics of the 1844 Bank Act Marx then provides a detailed institutional description of the 1844 Bank Act. The Act divides the Bank of England into two departments: the Issue Department and the Banking Department. The Issue Department receives securities — for the most part government stock — for £14 million, as well as the entire metal reserve, of which no more than one quarter may consist of silver, and issues a sum of notes corresponding to this total amount. \"In so far as these are not in the hands of the public, they remain in the Banking Department and form its ever ready reserve, together with the small amount of coin needed for everyday use (about £1 million).\" The Issue Department gives the public gold for notes and notes for gold; all other dealings with the public are the concern of the Banking Department. Private banks in England and Wales entitled in 1844 to issue their own notes retain this right, but their note issue is fixed. If one of these banks ceases to issue its own notes, the Bank of England can increase its own fiduciary note issue by two-thirds of the quota thus made available; in this way its issue was increased, by 1892, from £14 million to £16½ million (£16,450,000 to be precise). The mechanical operation is stated in a formula-like passage: \"Thus, for every £5 that leaves the Bank's reserve in gold, a £5 note comes back to the Issue Department and is destroyed; for every five sovereigns that come into the reserve, a new £5 note is put into circulation. This is how Overstone's ideal paper circulation, governed precisely by the laws of metal circulation, is carried out in practice, and in this way, according to the currency people's contention, crises are made impossible once and for all.\" The critical inversion follows immediately. \"In reality, however, the separation of the Bank into two independent departments withdrew the directors' power of free disposal of their entire available means at decisive moments, so that situations could come about in which the Banking Department was faced with bankruptcy while the Issue Department still had several millions in gold, and besides this its £14 million intact in securities.\" This \"could happen all the more easily in so far as every crisis has a point marked by a sharp drain of gold abroad, which has principally to be covered by the Bank's metal reserve.\" But for every £5 that flows abroad, a £5 note is withdrawn from circulation at home, \"so that the amount of means of circulation is reduced at the very moment when most is required, and with the greatest urgency at that.\" The Act thus \"directly provokes the entire world of commerce into meeting the outbreak of a crisis by putting aside a reserve stock of banknotes, thereby accelerating and intensifying the crisis.\" By artificially intensifying the demand for monetary accommodation at the same time as the supply is declining, it drives the interest rate in crisis times up to a previously unheard-of level. The result is the dialectic of the suspension. \"Instead of abolishing crises, it rather intensifies them to a point at which either the entire world of industry has to collapse, or else the Bank Act.\" On two occasions — 25 October 1847 and 12 November 1857 — the crisis reached such a height that the government freed the Bank from the restriction on its note issue by suspending the Act, \"and this was sufficient on both occasions to curb the crisis.\" In 1847, \"the certainty that banknotes could now once more be had in exchange for first-class securities was sufficient to bring £4–£5 million in notes back to the light of day and into active circulation; in 1857 rather less than £1 million in notes were issued above the legal quota, but only for a very short time.\" 2.10 The Fear of Banknote Depreciation and the 1825 Precedent An editorial parenthesis (F. E.) notes that \"some parts of the legislation of 1844 reflected the memory of the first twenty years of the century, the time when the Bank suspended cash payment and banknotes depreciated in value. The fear that banknotes might lose their credit is still very much apparent here; a very excessive fear, since as early as 1825 the issue of a rediscovered supply of old £1 notes, which had been taken out of circulation, curbed the crisis and thereby showed that even at that period the credit of these banknotes remained unimpaired, even at a time of most general and pronounced lack of confidence. And this is quite comprehensible, for in fact the entire nation and its credit stands behind these tokens.\" This is an important conceptual point about the nature of credit money. The \"credit\" of banknotes does not rest on the gold reserve backing them; it rests on \"the entire nation and its credit.\" The 1825 episode demonstrates that even in a moment of \"most general and pronounced lack of confidence,\" the rediscovery and reissue of £1 notes could curb the crisis — evidence that the notes' credit was intact. The fear that motivates the 1844 Act is therefore both empirically unfounded and conceptually confused: it mistakes the form of convertibility (the gold reserve) for the substance of credit (the productive power of the nation). 2.11 John Stuart Mill's Testimony and Engels' Critique Marx now turns to \"a few witnesses on the effect of the Bank Act.\" The first is John Stuart Mill, who \"believed that the Bank Act of 1844 had kept down over-speculation.\" Mill gave his evidence on 12 June 1857; \"Four months later, the crisis broke out.\" The juxtaposition is deliberate and devastating. Mill \"literally congratulated the 'bank directors and the commercial public generally' on the fact that they 'understand much better than they did the nature of a commercial crisis, and the extreme mischief which they do both to themselves and to the public by upholding over-speculation'\" (B. A. 1857, no. 2031). Mill also testified (no. 2066) that if £1 notes were issued \"as advances to manufacturers and others, who pay wages... the notes may get into the hands of others who expend them for consumption, and in that case the notes do constitute in themselves a demand for commodities and may for some time tend to promote a rise of prices.\" Marx responds with a series of rhetorical questions: \"Does Mr Mill thus assume that the manufacturers would pay higher wages if they paid them in paper instead of in gold? Or does he believe that if the manufacturer obtained his advance in £100 notes and exchanged these for gold, these wages would then form less demand than if paid in £1 notes? Does he not know that in certain mining districts, for example, wages actually are paid in notes from local banks, so that several workers receive a £5 note together? Does this increase their demand? Or are bankers easier with manufacturers in small notes, advancing more money than in larger denominations?\" The editorial parenthesis (F. E.) explains Mill's position: \"This peculiar fear that Mill had of £1 notes would be inexplicable if his entire work on political economy did not exhibit an eclecticism which never flinches from any contradictions. On the one hand, he supports Tooke on many points against Overstone, while on the other hand he believes commodity prices are determined by the amount of money present. He is thus in no way convinced that for each £1 issued — all other things remaining the same — a sovereign finds its way into the Bank's reserve; he fears that the quantity of means of circulation might be increased and thereby devalued, so that commodity prices would rise. It is this and nothing else that is hidden behind his above caution.\" Mill is thus presented as an eclectic who combines elements of the banking school (Tooke) with elements of the currency theory, and whose fear of £1 notes reveals a persistent attachment to the quantity theory. The critique of Mill is significant because Mill was the most authoritative economist of the mid-nineteenth century; his inability to break with the quantity theory demonstrates the ideological hold of the currency doctrine. 2.12 Tooke and Newmarch: The Separation of Departments and the Fluctuations of Interest Tooke makes the following statements to the C. D. 1848–57. The greater fluctuations of the interest rate in 1847, as compared with 1837 and 1839, are due \"solely to the separation of the Bank into two departments\" (3010). The safety of banknotes was affected neither in 1825 nor in 1837 and 1839 (3015). The demand for gold in 1825 was aimed only at filling the vacuum created by the complete discredit of the £1 notes of the country banks; this vacuum could be filled only by gold, until the Bank of England also issued £1 notes (3022). In November and December 1825, \"not the slightest demand existed for gold for export purposes\" (3023). \"In point of discredit at home as well as abroad. A failure in paying the dividends and the deposits would be of far greater consequence than the suspending of the payment of banknotes\" (3028). In response to question 3035 — whether any circumstance endangering the convertibility of the note would add serious difficulty in a moment of commercial pressure — Tooke answers: \"Not at all.\" And (3058): \"In the course of 1847 ... an increased issue from the circulating department might have contributed to replenish the coffers of the Bank, as it did in 1825.\" Before the Committee on B. A. 1857, Newmarch states (no. 1357): \"The first mischievous effect ... of that separation of departments\" (of the Bank) \"and ... a necessary consequence from the cutting in two of the reserve of bullion has been that the banking business of the Bank of England, that is to say, the whole of that part of the operation of the Bank of England which brings it more immediately into contact with the commerce of the country, has been carried on upon a moiety only of its former amounts of reserve. Out of that division of the reserve has arisen, therefore, this state of things, that whenever the reserve of the Banking Department has been diminished, even to a small extent, it has rendered necessary an action by the Bank upon its rate of discount. That diminished reserve, therefore, has produced a frequent succession of changes and jerks in the rate of discount.\" (No. 1358) \"The alterations since 1844\" (until June 1857) \"have been some sixty in number, whereas the alterations prior to 1844 in the same space of time certainly did not amount to a dozen.\" The statistical comparison — sixty changes in the discount rate since 1844 versus fewer than a dozen before — is an empirical demonstration of the Act's destabilizing effect. The separation of departments, by halving the effective reserve, subjects the banking business to frequent \"changes and jerks\" in the rate of discount, which is precisely the opposite of the stability the Act was supposed to ensure. 2.13 Palmer's Evidence: The Intention to Make Money Dear Palmer's evidence to the Lords Committee (C. D. 1848–57) is particularly weighty because he had been a director of the Bank of England since 1811 and Governor for a period. His testimony is a detailed historical demonstration of what would have happened had the 1844 Act existed at earlier moments of crisis. (No. 828) \"In December 1825, there was about £1,100,000 of bullion remaining in the Bank. At that period it must undoubtedly have failed in toto, if this Act had been in existence\" (meaning the Act of 1844). \"The issue in December, I think, was 5 or 6 millions of notes in a week, which relieved the panic that existed at that period.\" (No. 825) The first period since 1 July 1825 when the present Act would have failed was on 28 February 1837, when there were £3,900,000 to £4,000,000 of bullion in the Bank's possession, and the Bank would have been left with £650,000 only in the reserve. Another period is 1839, from 9 July to 5 December. (No. 826) \"The reserve was minus altogether £200,000 upon the 5th of September. On the 5th of November it rose to about a million or a million and a half.\" (No. 830) \"The Act of 1844 would have prevented the Bank giving assistance to the American trade in 1837.\" (No. 831) \"There were three of the principal American houses that failed... Almost every house connected with America was in a state of discredit, and unless the Bank had come forward at that period, I do not believe that there would have been more than one or two houses that could have sustained themselves.\" (No. 836) \"The pressure in 1837 is not to be compared with that of 1847. The pressure in the former year was chiefly confined to the American trade.\" (No. 838) Early in June 1837, the management of the Bank discussed the question of overcoming the pressure: \"Some gentlemen advocated the opinion ... that the correct principle was to raise the rate of interest, by which the price of commodities would be lowered; in short, to make money dear and commodities cheap, by which the foreign payment would be accomplished.\" This last statement is crucial: it reveals the class program behind the \"technical\" theory. The Bank's directors consciously advocated a policy of making money dear and commodities cheap. This is not a neutral monetary rule; it is a mechanism for transferring income from debtors (manufacturers, merchants) to creditors (bankers, money-capitalists). (No. 906) \"The establishment of an artificial limitation of the powers of the Bank under the Act of 1844, instead of the ancient and natural limitation of the Bank's powers, namely, the actual amount of its specie, tends to create artificial difficulty, and therefore an operation upon the prices of merchandise that would have been unnecessary but for the provisions of the Act.\" (No. 968) \"You cannot, by the working of the Act of 1844, materially reduce the bullion, under ordinary circumstances, below nine million and a half. It would then cause a pressure upon prices and credit which would occasion such an advance in the exchange with foreign countries as to increase the import of bullion, and to that extent add to the amount in the Issue Department.\" (No. 996) \"Under the limitation that you\" (the Bank) \"are now subject to, you have not the command of silver to an extent that you require at a time when silver would be required for an action upon the foreign exchanges.\" (No. 999) In response to the question of what was the object of the regulation restricting the Bank as to the amount of silver to one-fifth, Palmer states: \"I cannot answer that question.\" Marx comments: \"The intention was to make money dear.\" This is the analytical key to the entire chapter: the Act's provisions — the £14 million ceiling on fiduciary note issue, the administration of the Banking Department as an ordinary bank (forcing interest down when money is plentiful, up when scarce), the restriction of the silver reserve (the principal means of rectifying exchange rates with the Continent and Asia), the regulations for Scottish and Irish banks (which never need gold for export but must now keep it under the pretext of a convertibility of their notes which is \"pure illusion\") — all \"lead to raising the rate of interest.\" The Act of 1845 produced the first run on the Scottish banks for gold, in 1857. The legislation \"also makes no distinction between a drain of gold abroad and a domestic drain, even though the effects of these two things are self-evidently quite different. Hence the constant and violent fluctuations in the market rate of interest.\" On silver, Palmer says twice (992, 994) that the Bank can buy silver in exchange for notes only if the exchange rate is in England's favour, so that there is an excess of silver. (No. 1003) \"The only object in holding a considerable amount of bullion in silver is to facilitate making the foreign payment so long as the exchanges are against the country.\" (No. 1004) \"Silver is ... a commodity which, being money in every other part of the world, is therefore the most direct commodity ... for the purpose\" (of payments abroad). \"The United States latterly have taken gold alone.\" In Palmer's opinion, the Bank did not need to raise the interest rate above its old level of 5 per cent in times of pressure, as long as gold was not drawn abroad by an unfavourable rate of exchange. Were it not for the Act of 1844, all first-class bills could still be discounted without difficulty at the old rate (1018–20). But with the 1844 Act and the situation in which the Bank found itself in October 1847, \"there was no rate of interest which the Bank could have charged to houses of credit, which they would not have been willing to pay to carry on their payments.\" Marx comments: \"And this high rate of interest was precisely the aim of the Act.\" Palmer draws (no. 1029) \"a great distinction which I wish to draw between the action of the rate of interest upon a foreign demand\" (for precious metal) \"and an advance in the rate for the object of checking a demand upon the Bank during a period of internal discredit.\" (No. 1023) \"Previously to the Act of 1844 ... when the exchanges were in favour of the country, and positive panic and alarm existed through the country, there was no limit put upon the issue, by which alone that state of distress could be relieved.\" Marx introduces these statements with the observation: \"These are the words of a man who was a director of the Bank of England for thirty-nine years.\" 2.14 Twells: The Private Banker's Perspective Marx next introduces Twells, \"a private banker, a partner in Spooner, Attwood and Co. since 1801.\" He is described as \"the only one out of all these witnesses before the B. A. 1857 who provides us with any insight into the actual state of affairs in the country, and sees the crisis impending.\" In other respects, however, \"he is a kind of Birmingham 'little shilling man', like his partners, the Attwood brothers, who are the founders of this school.\" (The reference is to the Birmingham currency school of the 1820s–30s, which advocated a depreciated currency to stimulate industry. See Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy , p. 82.) Twells states (no. 4488): \"How do you think that the Act of 1844 has operated? — If I were to answer you as a banker, I should say that it has operated exceedingly well, for it has afforded a rich harvest to bankers and\" (money-) \"capitalists of all kinds. But it has operated very badly to the honest industrious tradesman who requires steadiness in the rate of discount, that he may be enabled to make his arrangements with confidence ... It has made money-lending a most profitable pursuit.\" (No. 4489) \"It\" (the Bank Act) \"enables the London joint-stock banks to return from 20 to 22 per cent to their proprietors? — The other day one of them was paying 18 per cent and I think another 20 per cent; they ought to support the Act of 1844 very strongly.\" (No. 4490) \"The little tradesmen and respectable merchants, who have not a large capital ... it pinches them very much indeed ... The only means that I have of knowing is that I observe such an amazing quantity of their acceptances unpaid. They are always small, perhaps ranging from £20 to £100, a great many of them are unpaid and go back unpaid to all parts of the country, which is always an indication of suffering amongst ... little shopkeepers.\" This is a class analysis in the testimony of a banker: the Act benefits \"bankers and money-capitalists of all kinds\" (with returns of 18–22% to joint-stock bank proprietors) while \"pinching\" small tradesmen and merchants. In 4494, Twells explains that business is presently unprofitable. His remarks are important because \"he saw the latent presence of the crisis when none of the others suspected it.\" (No. 4494) \"Things keep their prices in Mincing Lane, but we sell nothing, we cannot sell upon any terms; we keep the nominal price.\" (No. 4495) He relates a particular case: a Frenchman sends a broker in Mincing Lane commodities for £3,000 to sell at a given price. The broker cannot obtain this price, and the Frenchman cannot sell below the price. The commodities are still around, but the Frenchman needs money. The broker therefore advances him £1,000, the Frenchman drawing a three-month bill of exchange on the broker for £1,000, with the goods as security. Three months later the bill falls due, but the commodities are still unsaleable. The broker then has to pay the bill, and although he has security for £3,000, he cannot cash this and gets into difficulties. \"Thus one person drags another down with him.\" (No. 4496) \"With regard to the large exports ... where there is a depressed state of trade at home, it necessarily forces large exportation.\" (No. 4497) In response to the question of whether home consumption has diminished: \"Very much indeed ... immensely ... the shopkeepers are the best authorities.\" (No. 4498) \"Still the importations are very large, does not that indicate a large consumption? — It does, if you can sell; but many of the warehouses are full of these things; in this very instance which I have been relating, there is £3,000 worth imported, which cannot be sold.\" (No. 4514) \"When money is dear, would you say that capital would be cheap? — Yes.\" Marx comments: \"This fellow thus in no way shares Overstone's opinion that high interest is the same thing as dear capital.\" Twells's testimony on business practice (no. 4616) is also important: \"Others are going to a very great extent, carrying on a prodigious trade in exports and imports, to an extent far beyond what their capital justifies them in doing; there can be no doubt of all of that. These men may succeed; they may by some lucky venture get large fortunes, and put themselves right. That is very much the system in which a great deal of trade is now carried on. Persons will consent to lose 20, 30, and 40 per cent upon a shipment; the next venture may bring it back to them. If they fail in one after another, then they are broken up; and that is just the case which we have often seen recently; mercantile houses have broken up, without one shilling of property being left.\" This describes the speculative, over-leveraged character of mid-Victorian trade — a structural feature of the credit system that the Bank Act does nothing to address and arguably exacerbates. (No. 4791) \"The low rate of interest\" (during the previous ten years) \"operates against bankers, it is true, but I should have very great difficulty in explaining to you, unless I could show you the books, how much higher the profits\" (his own) \"are now than they used to be formerly. When interest is low, from excessive issues, we have large deposits; when interest is high, we get the advantage in that way.\" (No. 4794) \"When money is at a moderate rate, we have more demand for it; we lend more; it operates in that way\" (for us, the bankers). \"When it gets higher, we get more than a fair proportion for it; we get more than we ought to do.\" This is a frank admission of the bankers' class interest in interest-rate volatility: whether rates are high or low, the banking system profits. 2.15 Brown and Capps: The Reserve as Waste and the Act as Usurers' Scheme Marx notes: \"We have seen how all the experts consider the credit of Bank of England notes as unshakeable. Nevertheless, the Bank Act ties up absolutely some £9 to £10 million in gold, for the convertibility of these notes. The sanctity and inviolability of the reserve is thereby carried much farther than among the hoarders of old.\" W. Brown (Liverpool) testifies (C. D. 1848–57, no. 2311): \"This money\" (the metal reserve in the Issue Department) \"might as well have been thrown into the sea from any use that it was of at that time, there being no power to employ any of it without violating the Act of Parliament.\" The building contractor E. Capps, \"whom we already met with earlier, and from whose evidence we took the description of the modern building system in London (Volume 2, Chapter 12),\" gives his opinion of the 1844 Bank Act: (No. 5508) \"Then upon the whole ... you think that the present system\" (of bank legislation) \"is a somewhat adroit scheme for bringing the profits of industry periodically into the usurer's bag? — I think so. I know that it has operated so in the building trade.\" This is perhaps the single most direct statement of the chapter's political-economic thesis: the Bank Act is \"a somewhat adroit scheme for bringing the profits of industry periodically into the usurer's bag.\" The phrase \"periodically\" is important: the mechanism operates through the cycle of crisis, in which high interest rates transfer income from industrial capital to money-capital. 2.16 The Scottish Banks and the 1845 Act The 1845 Bank Act forced the Scottish banks into a system closer to the English. They were obliged to hold gold in reserve for any note issue beyond a limit fixed for each bank. The effect is shown by witnesses before the B. A. 1857. Kennedy, director of a Scottish bank, testifies (no. 3375): \"Was there anything that you can call a circulation of gold in Scotland previously to the passing of the Act of 1845? — None whatever.\" (No. 3376) \"Has there been any additional circulation of gold since? — None whatever; the people dislike gold.\" (No. 3450) The sum of about £900,000 in gold which the Scottish banks are compelled to keep since 1845 \"can only be injurious in his opinion and 'absorbs unprofitably so much of the capital of Scotland'.\" Anderson, director of the Union Bank of Scotland, testifies (no. 3588): \"The only pressure upon the Bank of England by the banks in Scotland for gold was for foreign exchanges? — It was; and that is not to be relieved by holding gold in Edinburgh.\" (No. 3590) \"Having the same amount of securities in the Bank of England\" (or in the private banks of England), \"we have the same power that we had before of making a drain upon the Bank of England.\" Finally, Marx quotes an article from The Economist (Wilson): \"The Scotch banks keep unemployed amounts of cash with their London agents; these keep them in the Bank of England. This gives to the Scotch banks, within the limits of these amounts, command over the metal reserve of the Bank, and here it is always in the place where it is needed, when foreign payments are to be made.\" This system was upset by the 1845 Act. In consequence of the Act of 1845 for Scotland, \"of late a large drain of the coin of the Bank has taken place, to supply a mere contingent demand in Scotland, which may never occur ... Since that period there has been a large sum uniformly locked up in Scotland, and another considerable sum constantly travelling back and forward between London and Scotland. If a period arrives when a Scotch bank expects an increased demand for its notes, a box of gold is brought down from London; when this period is past, the same box, generally unopened, is sent back to London\" ( The Economist , 23 October 1847). The image of the unopened box of gold travelling back and forth between London and Scotland is a perfect emblem of the Act's irrationality: it immobilizes capital, adds nothing to the security of the note issue, and destroys the centralized reserve that made the Bank of England effective in meeting foreign payments. 2.17 Overstone's Dithyramb and Its Antistrophe The chapter concludes with an editorial parenthesis (F. E.) presenting Overstone's defense of the Act and its ironic reversal. Overstone had already repeated before the Lords Committee of 1848 on Commercial Distress that \"pressure, and a high rate of interest, caused by the want of sufficient capital, cannot be relieved by an extra issue of banknotes\" (1514) — \"even though the mere permission for an increased note issue in the government letter of 25 October 1847 had sufficed to blunt the edge of the crisis.\" He still maintains (1604) that \"the high rate of interest and the depression of the manufacturing interests was the necessary result of the diminution of the material capital applicable to manufacturing and trading purposes.\" But \"the depressed condition of manufacturing industry for months back meant that material commodity capital overflowed the warehouses and was actually unsaleable, while for that very reason material productive capital lay either completely or partially idle, so as not to produce still more unsaleable commodity capital.\" This is the decisive refutation of Overstone's \"dear capital\" theory: capital was not scarce; it was over-abundant and hence unsaleable. The high interest rate was not a reflection of capital scarcity but a mechanism for transferring value to creditors. Before the Bank Acts Committee of 1857, Overstone delivers his dithyramb (no. 4189): \"By strict and prompt adherence to the principles of the Act of 1844, everything has passed off with regularity and ease, the monetary system is safe and unshaken, the prosperity of the country is undisputed, the public confidence in the wisdom of the Act of 1844 is daily gaining strength, and if the Committee wish for further practical illustration of the soundness of the principles on which it rests, or of the beneficial results which it has ensured, the true and sufficient answer to the Committee is, look around you, look at the present state of the trade of this country, ... look at the contentment of the people, look at the wealth and prosperity which pervades every class of the community, and then having done so, the Committee may be fairly called upon to decide whether they will interfere with the continuance of an Act under which those results have been developed.\" The dithyramb — a term for an ecstatic, frenzied hymn in ancient Greek drama — is dated 14 July 1857. Marx concludes: \"The antistrophe to this dithyramb that Overstone sang to the Committee on 14 July came on 12 November the same year, in the form of the letter to the Bank directors in which the government suspended the miracle-working act of 1844, in order to save what could still be saved.\" The \"antistrophe\" is the response of the chorus in Greek drama, moving in the opposite direction. In this case, the response is the suspension of the Act — precisely the measure Overstone had denounced as unnecessary and harmful — four months after his confident prediction of permanent prosperity. The irony is complete, and it constitutes the chapter's final, devastating verdict on the currency principle. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter establishes several fundamental principles of Marx's analysis of the credit system and its relationship to the capitalist mode of production. First, the priority of production over circulation. The chapter's most general theoretical lesson is that the causes of commercial crises are not to be sought in the sphere of currency — \"the most superficial and abstract sphere\" of the bourgeois process of production — but in the deeper antagonisms of the mode of production itself. The Currency School's error was simultaneously an analytical error (mistaking the surface for the essence) and an ideological error (misdirecting attention away from overproduction, the falling rate of profit, and the anarchy of production). The consequence was a body of legislation that could not address the real causes of crisis and, in fact, intensified them. Second, the distinction between money and capital. Throughout the chapter, Marx insists on the distinction between loanable money capital (whose \"price\" is the rate of interest) and real capital (means of production and labor-power). Overstone's identification of high interest with scarce capital is false: in 1847, commodity capital overflowed the warehouses and was unsaleable, while the interest rate soared. The high interest rate was not a reflection of material scarcity but a mechanism for redistributing value from industrial capital to money-capital. This distinction — which the critics Tooke, Wilson, and Fullarton failed to grasp adequately — is essential to any scientific analysis of credit. Third, the nature of credit money. The chapter's discussion of the 1825 episode and the \"credit\" of banknotes establishes that the stability of credit money rests not on the gold reserve but on \"the entire nation and its credit.\" The fear that motivated the 1844 Act — the fear of banknote depreciation — was therefore both empirically unfounded and conceptually confused. By tying up £9–£10 million in gold for the convertibility of notes whose credit was already unshakeable, the Act immobilized capital, centralized nothing, and destroyed the flexible reserve system that had made the Bank of England effective in meeting foreign payments. Fourth, the class character of monetary legislation. The chapter demonstrates, through witness testimony (Twells, Capps, Palmer), that the Bank Act systematically benefited bankers and money-capitalists at the expense of industrialists, merchants, and small tradesmen. Twells's admission that the Act \"has afforded a rich harvest to bankers and money-capitalists of all kinds\" while \"pinching\" little tradesmen; Capps's characterization of the Act as \"a somewhat adroit scheme for bringing the profits of industry periodically into the usurer's bag\"; and Palmer's statement that the intention \"was to make money dear\" — these together constitute a class analysis of the 1844 legislation. The Act was not a neutral technical reform but a mechanism for the redistribution of income from productive to financial capital. Fifth, the dialectic of reform and crisis. The chapter's narrative structure — the Act's passage, its operation, the crises of 1847 and 1857, the suspensions, the resumption — illustrates a general pattern of capitalist regulation: a reform designed to prevent crises in fact intensifies them, forcing the suspension of the very rules that were supposed to provide stability. The government's suspension of the Act on 25 October 1847 and 12 November 1857 is the empirical proof of the Act's failure and the concrete negation of the currency principle. This dialectic — the self-negation of bourgeois reform through its own operation — is a recurring theme in Marx's work. The broader implications of the chapter extend beyond monetary theory. They concern the relationship between economic theory and class interest; the way in which a flawed theory can acquire practical dominance through its service to powerful interests; the structural tendency of the capitalist credit system to generate crises and to redistribute their costs; and the inability of technical fixes to address the fundamental contradictions of the mode of production. The chapter is, in this sense, not merely a historical study of the English Bank Legislation of 1844 but a case study in the political economy of reform — a demonstration that within the framework of capitalist production, the attempt to manage the system through legislation tends to reproduce and intensify the system's contradictions rather than resolve them. The final image — Overstone's dithyramb and its antistrophe — encapsulates this lesson: the confident celebration of a \"miracle-working act\" is answered, four months later, by the government's letter suspending it \"in order to save what could still be saved.\" ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 33 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 35"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 35", "title": "Chapter 35", "subtitle": "Precious Metal and Rate of Exchange", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 35/Chapter 35 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 18629, "read_time": "93 min read", "tags": ["Credit System, Banking & Fictitious Capital", "Money, Currency & Circulation"], "keywords": ["Precious Metals and the Rate of Exchange", "International Balance of Payments", "Gold Drains and Raising Central Bank Discount Rates", "Arbitrage and Currency Depreciations", "Transmission of Financial Crises across World Markets"], "text": "Chapter 35 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 18,622 words ⏱️ ~93 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS: Chapter 35 of Capital , Volume Three, \"Precious Metal and Rate of Exchange,\" occupies a peculiar and decisive position in Marx's theoretical architecture. It sits at the hinge between the analysis of the credit system (Chapters 25–34) and the treatment of the rate of exchange and the world market, and it functions above all as a demolition of a central bourgeois illusion: that gold and silver are merely indifferent forms of capital, and that the movements of the metal reserve are epiphenomenal to the \"real\" economic process. Marx's core thesis is precisely the opposite. The metal reserve—pivot of the central bank, which is in turn the pivot of the entire credit system—is a quantity that is infinitesimal against total production, yet upon its movement the whole credit edifice may depend. This is not a physical necessity but a social one: gold is the \"autonomous embodiment and expression of the social character of wealth,\" and the credit system, though it strives perpetually to overcome this metallic barrier, \"time and again breaks its head on it.\" The chapter's central problem is the relation between three linked phenomena: the movement of the precious metal reserve, the rate of exchange, and the rate of interest. Marx's intellectual objective is to demonstrate, against the \"enlightened economics\" of the Currency School (Loyd-Overstone), against Tooke, against James Wilson of The Economist , and even against Newmarch's vacillations, that the metallic movements are governed by, and in turn govern, the peculiar dynamics of loanable money capital—not the movements of industrial capital. Where the bourgeois economist wishes to identify the movement of loan capital with the movement of industrial capital (the deepest error of Wilson's Economist articles), Marx insists that they may move in opposite directions, and that it is the credit system itself, in its over-refinement, that renders the \"organism over-sensitive\" so that a drain of £5–8 million—\"the feather which, added to the weight already on the scales, is enough to tip the balance\"—can produce a general collapse. The chapter fits into the broader subject matter of Capital as the analysis of \"interest-bearing capital\" and the division of profit into interest and profit of enterprise. It presupposes the theory of the credit system developed in preceding chapters, where Marx has shown that the credit system is a mode of wrenching titles to social wealth from private hands and pressing them into the service of production, converting all money income into capital and reducing the metal reserve to a minimum. It prepares the ground for the later analysis of the world market and the colonies (Chapter 36 onward, \"Pre-Capitalist Relations\") and, more immediately, for the theory of the trade cycle. It also carries a polemical charge that makes it one of the most epistemologically rigorous chapters in the volume: nearly all of its positive content is carried by citations from the Bank Acts Committee of 1857 and the Secret Committee of the House of Lords of 1848, interspersed with Engels's editorial interventions (marked \"F. E.\"). The chapter is, in effect, a juridical reconstruction: Marx cross-examines the witnesses of the 1857 Committee—Newmarch, Tooke, Morris, Palmer, Hubbard, Mill, Weguelin, Wood, Chapman—and forces their testimony to yield a theory. It is this method—empirical testimony bent into theoretical form—that gives the chapter its singular texture and its singular authority. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS: The Hoarding of Metal, the Act of 1844, and the Failed Test of Automatic Regulation The chapter opens not with a definition but with an anthropological observation: the stockpiling of notes in times of pressure is the modern counterpart of \"the hoarding of precious metals\" in \"the most primitive social conditions.\" This is a deliberate framing move. Marx wants us to see that the most \"advanced\" financial panic reproduces a pattern characteristic of the most primitive economy—the retreat into the metal form of wealth—and he thereby situates the entire ensuing discussion in a dialectic between the archaic and the developed. The Act of 1844, the Bank Charter Act that split the Bank of England into an Issue Department and a Banking Department and limited fiduciary note issue, is treated as the legal expression of this archaic impulse: it sought to \"transform all the country's precious metal into means of circulation,\" compensating for a drain of gold by a contraction of the means of circulation and for an influx by an expansion. This is the Currency School principle: notes should simply be a metallic currency in disguise, so that the quantity of notes should automatically vary with the bullion reserve. The empirical verdict, says Marx, is the opposite of what the Act's authors intended. With a single exception, the quantity of Bank of England notes in circulation since 1844 had never reached the legal maximum the Bank was authorized to issue. This is a major point: the Act was predicated on the notion that note issues are elastic and would always press against the maximum, but in fact the fiduciary circulation was habitually below its legal ceiling. The crisis of 1857 nonetheless showed that in certain circumstances this maximum is not sufficient . Marx cites the concrete fact: between 13 and 30 November 1857, a daily average of £488,830 above the maximum was in circulation (B.A. 1858, p. xi). The legal maximum at that time was £14,475,000 plus the metal reserve in the Bank's vaults. This single concrete figure does enormous theoretical work: it proves that the Currency School's mechanism cannot function even on its own terms, because the demand for circulation in a crisis exceeds the quantity that the metallic logic would permit—hence the necessity of the Government Letter suspending the Act, a recurrent humiliation of the 1844 mechanism in 1847, 1857, and (not mentioned here, but structurally implied) 1866. This opening gesture establishes the chapter's central methodological principle: the legal framework is a fiction that masks the real social character of money; the empirical record of the Bank Acts Committee is the territory on which the theory must be decided. Point (I): The Two Kinds of Metal Flow—Domestic Ebb and Flow versus Distribution from Sources of Production Marx now lays out a nine-point analytical digest of the movement of precious metal, a kind of numbered catechism that structures the whole first half of the chapter. The first point establishes a fundamental distinction: \"The ebb and flow of metal within a region that does not produce gold or silver should be distinguished from the flow of gold and silver from their sources of production to the various other countries, and the distribution of this additional metal among the latter.\" This distinction is analytically crucial because it separates two different objects of study. The first is the oscillatory movement of metal between non-producing countries—the back-and-forth of bullion between, say, England and Germany, England and America, England and India—which is the object of the balance of payments and exchange-rate analysis. The second is the one-directional flux from the mines (California, Australia, Russia) into the world economy, a flux that represents the initial introduction of new value into the monetary universe. Conflating the two is a persistent error, and Marx wants to keep them rigorously separated. Historically, before the opening of the gold mines in Russia, California, and Australia, the supply since the beginning of the century \"had only been sufficient for the replacement of worn-down coins, for the traditional use as a luxury material and for the export of silver to Asia.\" So the world's metallic stock was essentially in a steady state: the new production merely offset wear and tear and funded the structural drain of silver to the East. After the discoveries, however, everything changed. The export of silver to Asia \"has grown extraordinarily\" with the expansion of Asian trade by America and Europe. Europe's exported silver was \"largely replaced by the additional gold.\" A portion of newly imported gold was absorbed by domestic money circulation, and Marx cites the estimate that up to 1857 approximately £30 million worth of gold had been added to England's domestic circulation (footnote 14). Moreover, the average level of metal reserves had increased for all the central banks of Europe and North America since 1844. The growth of domestic circulation also had a secondary effect: after a panic, in the subsequent stagnation, bank reserves grew much more quickly because a greater quantity of gold coin was \"thrust out of domestic circulation and immobilized.\" Finally, luxury consumption of precious metal had risen since the new discoveries, as wealth increased. This point is not merely descriptive: it establishes that the \"reserve\" of a central bank is not a fixed, sacrosanct quantity but a residual that shifts with the phases of the cycle, and that the very fact of an enlarged gold base changes the behaviour of reserves in stagnation. Footnote 14 (W. Newmarch's testimony, B.A. 1857) provides a dramatic illustration of how these flows affect the money market. Newmarch testifies that at the close of 1853 there was considerable apprehension, and in September of that year the Bank of England raised its discount three times; the alarm was relieved before the end of November \"in consequence of the arrival of nearly £5,000,000 of treasure from Australia.\" The same happened in the autumn of 1854 (arrival of nearly £6,000,000), in 1855 (£8,000,000 across September, October, November), and at the close of 1856. Newmarch's conclusion is evocative: \"the natural and complete solvent to which we have got into the habit of looking for any financial pressure, is not the arrival of a gold ship.\" This is doubly significant. First, it is empirical evidence that metallic arrivals, not commodity balance, functioned as the practical \"solvent\" of English financial pressure. Second, it illustrates the chapter's central theme that metal is treated as such , as a fetish, as the appearance of salvation in crisis—a point Marx will develop into the great rhetorical passage on the \"Catholic\" monetary system. Point (2): The Constant Two-Way Movement and the Illusion of Net Flows The second point attacks a fundamental conceptual error. Precious metal \"is constantly moving back and forth between the countries that do not produce gold and silver; the same country is both constantly importing and constantly exporting.\" What determines an ultimate outflow or inflow is only \"a preponderant movement in one direction or the other,\" because the two movements largely neutralize one another. The consequence of this constant mutual offsetting is that the parallelism and constancy of the two movements go unobserved: \"The matter is always conceived as if an excess import or export of precious metal were simply the effect and expression of the import and export relationship for commodities, whereas it also expresses a relationship between the import and export of precious metal that is independent of commodity trade.\" This is a profound methodological critique of the quantity-of-money/balance-of-trade doctrine. The naive view treats the metal flow as a pure residual of the trade balance. Marx insists that there is an independent dynamic of metallic movement—capital investment abroad, war expenditure, remittances of tribute and dividends, speculative securities transactions—which is not reducible to the commodity trade balance. This point anticipates the entire Wilson polemic in Section II, where Wilson tries to collapse capital export and trade export into a single category. It also sets up point (7) below, where the redistribution of hoards after a crisis is shown to follow a logic determined by each country's role in the world market, not simply by commodity trade accountancy. Point (3): The Central Bank Reserve as an Inexact Barometer The third point addresses measurement. The preponderance of imports over exports—or vice versa—can be \"broadly measured\" by the increase or decrease in the metal reserves of the central banks. But the precision of this measurement depends \"first and foremost on how much the banking system is centralized,\" for only in proportion as the banking system is centralized does the metal stockpiled in the so-called national bank represent the whole nation's metal reserve. The qualifier \"so-called\" is telling: the national bank is not the nation, and centralization is a relative matter. Moreover, even assuming full centralization, the measurement remains inexact for two reasons: (1) an additional import of precious metal may be absorbed by domestic circulation and by the growing luxury use of gold and silver; and (2) a withdrawal of gold coin for domestic circulation might take place without any additional import, so that the metal reserve could decline even without a simultaneous increase in exports . This is a very concrete anticipation of the 1847 evidence in point (6), where the Bank's reserve declined after August 1847 despite an influx of gold, because £2,200,000 went out into the country in consequence of internal demand. The methodological point is that the reserve is a composite indicator, subject to multiple causations, and any theory that reads it as a direct index of the external balance will be systematically misleading. Point (4): The Definition of a \"Drain\" and Its Arbitrary Minimum Point (4) defines a specialized term: an export of metal takes the form of a 'drain' if the movement of decline persists for a long period, so that the decline presents itself as a general tendency and the national bank's metal reserve is significantly depressed below its average level, \"until something like its average minimum is reached.\" This last is fixed \"more or less arbitrarily,\" since it is determined differently in each case by legislation governing the backing for cash payment of notes. The conceptual move here is important. The \"drain\" is not a single event but a tendency defined in relation to a statute-dependent minimum. Marx then gives quantitative limits. Newmarch, before the Bank Acts Committee of 1857 (Evidence, no. 1494), states: \"Judging from experience, it is very unlikely that the efflux of treasure arising from any oscillation in the foreign trade will proceed beyond £3,000,000 or £4,000,000.\" But this estimate is immediately contradicted by the historical record of 1847, when the Bank of England's gold reserve saw its lowest level on 23 October, a drop of £5,198,156 from 26 December 1846, and of £6,453,748 from the highest point in that year (29 August). So Newmarch's \"very unlikely\" figure is empirically refuted by a drain that nearly doubled the supposed ceiling. This is the empirical method that runs throughout the chapter: the witnesses' generalizations are checked against the figures, and the figures win. Point (5): The Threefold Function of the Metal Reserve—World Money, Domestic Circulation, Banking Guarantee Point (5) is the analytical heart of the first section and one of the most important theoretical passages in the chapter. The function of the metal reserve held by a \"so-called national bank\" is threefold—and Marx stresses that this function is \"far from being the only thing governing the size of the metal reserve,\" since the reserve can grow simply through the crippling of domestic and foreign business (a dialectical point: a stagnation increases reserves, because no business is drawing on them). The three functions are: (i) a reserve fund for international payments , i.e. a reserve fund of world money ; (ii) a reserve fund for the alternately expanding and contracting domestic metal circulation ; (iii) a reserve fund for the payment of deposits and the convertibility of notes —explicitly a function that \"has nothing to do with the function of money as simple money\" but is connected with the banking function . These three functions are not harmonious; each can be affected by conditions that bear on it alone. As an international fund, the reserve is affected by the balance of payments, \"whatever the reasons determining this and whatever their relationship to the balance of trade.\" As a reserve fund for domestic metal circulation, it is affected by the expansion or contraction of that circulation. The third function, as a guarantee fund, has a double effect even though it does not determine the autonomous movement of the reserve. First, if notes are issued to replace metal money in domestic circulation—and also, therefore, silver coin in countries where silver is the measure of value—then the second function disappears altogether , and a part of the precious metal that served this purpose \"will now permanently find its way abroad.\" In this case there is no withdrawal of metal coin from domestic circulation, and therefore no temporary strengthening of the metal reserve through immobilization of circulating coin. Second, if a minimum metal reserve must be maintained under all circumstances for the payment of deposits and the convertibility of notes, this affects the workings of a drain or influx in a particular way: it binds the portion of the reserve the bank must maintain in any case, or the part it might otherwise seek to get rid of as useless. The theoretical consequences are considerable. The first function corresponds to money as world money (the third of Marx's functions of money in Capital Vol. 1, Ch. 3: measure of value, means of circulation and means of payment, and world money). The second corresponds to money as means of circulation . The third is not a monetary function at all in the strict sense but a credit function—the banking system's obligation to convert deposits and notes into metal. The confusion of these three functions is precisely what produces the confused doctrines Marx attacks. The suggestion that the reserve's autonomy is not complete—\"it can grow simply through the crippling of domestic and foreign business\"—also undermines any simple causal reading: an increase in reserve is not necessarily a sign of prosperity; it may be a symptom of paralysis. The section concludes with a comparative observation of great force: \"With a purely metallic circulation and a centralized banking system, the bank would similarly have to treat its metal reserve as a guarantee for the payment of its deposits, and a drain of metal could lead to the same panic as in Hamburg in 1857.\" This refers to the Hamburg crisis, where the abandonment of the bank's metal guarantee produced a general collapse of credit. The implication is that the metal guarantee function operates independently of whether notes exist : it is a feature of any centralized banking system based on the social demand that the bank be able to convert its obligations into metal. The Act of 1844 is thus not the cause of the panic; it is the legal codification of a social necessity, and Hamburg's panic, under a different formal arrangement, demonstrates the underlying identity. This is a crucial anti-institutionalist argument: the panics are not artifacts of the Bank Charter Act but moments of the credit system's inherent dependence on the metallic guarantee. Point (6): The Crisis Breaks Out Only After the Exchange Rates Turn—The Drain as Precursor Point (6) is one of the most empirically dense and theoretically consequential passages in the chapter. Its claim: \"With the possible exception of 1837, the real crisis has always broken out only after the exchange rates have moved, i.e. once the import of precious metal has the upper hand again over the export.\" Marx then lists the historical evidence with lawyerly precision: - 1825 : the actual crash occurred after the drain of gold had ceased. - 1839 : a drain of gold took place without leading to a crash. - 1847 : the drain of gold ceased in April and the crash came in October. - 1857 : the drain of gold abroad had ceased by the beginning of November and the crash came later in the month. The theoretical conclusion is emphatic: \"The drain of metal, which gave rise to a specifically monetary panic in April 1847, is thus here as always simply a precursor of the crisis, and had already turned before this broke out.\" And 1839, a time of severe business depression with a pronounced drain of metal (for corn, etc.), produced no crisis or monetary panic at all. Thus the draining of metal is neither sufficient nor identical with the crisis; it is a symptom and a precondition, not the efficient cause. This directly refutes the Currency School's claim that the drain itself, via the note-issue mechanism, provokes the crisis—the drain consistently precedes and turns before the crisis breaks, which would be impossible if it were the cause. The empirical witnesses are then marshalled. Tooke , before the Secret Committee of the House of Lords on Commercial Distress, 1848 (evidence printed only in 1857, cited as C.D. 1848–57): - In April 1847, a stringency arose which, strictly speaking, equalled a panic, but was of relatively short duration and not accompanied by any commercial failures of importance. - In October the stringency was far more intensive than at any time in April, with an \"almost unheard-of number\" of commercial failures (2996). - In April the rates of exchange, particularly with America, compelled us to export a considerable amount of gold in payment for unusually large imports; only by an extreme effort did the Bank stop the drain and drive rates higher (2997). - In October the rates of exchange favoured England (2998). - The change in rates had begun in the third week of April (3000), fluctuated in July and August, and since the beginning of August always favoured England (3001). - The drain on gold in August arose from \"a demand for internal circulation\" (3003). J. Morris , Governor of the Bank of England, adds a crucial nuance: although the exchange rate after August 1847 was in England's favour, and an influx of gold thus took place, the metal reserve in the Bank still declined . His explanation: \"£2,200,000 went out into the country in consequence of the internal demand\" (137). This is explained on the one hand by an increased employment of labourers in railway construction and on the other by \"the circumstance of the bankers wishing to provide themselves with gold in times of distress\" (147). This is exactly the case anticipated in point (3): a reserve can decline without a simultaneous increase in exports, because of domestic absorption and precautionary hoarding—here, the bankers' rational flight into liquidity. Palmer , ex-Governor of the Bank of England and a director since 1811: \"During the whole period from the middle of April 1847 to the day of withdrawing the restrictive clause in the Act of 1844 the foreign exchanges were in favour of this country\" (684). The cumulative weight of this evidence is devastating to the Currency doctrine. In April 1847 the exchanges were against England and forced a drain; by August they favoured England and gold flowed in while the reserve still fell ; the crash came in October. The metal drain was a precursor that had turned long before the crisis. What, then, causes the crash? Marx does not fully answer here—he gestures toward the overstocked markets, the credit strain, and the panic demand for conversion—but the negative result is firm: the drain is not the cause, and the Act's mechanism of contraction is therefore both futile and destructive. Point (7): Redistribution of Hoards and the Restoration of Equilibrium Point (7) follows the crisis to its aftermath. Once the general crisis has burned itself out and equilibrium is restored, \"the gold and silver (leaving aside the influx of fresh precious metal from the producing countries) is again distributed in the proportions in which it previously existed as hoards in the various countries.\" With circumstances otherwise unchanged, the relative size of each country's hoard is determined by \"this country's role in the world market.\" Metal flows out of a country with a greater share than normal into another; these movements of ebb and flow \"simply bring about its original distribution among the various national reserves.\" This redistribution is mediated by various circumstances to be discussed under exchange rates. Once the normal distribution is re-established, \"there is first of all a growth and then again a drain.\" A crucial editorial parenthesis (F.E.) notes that this last sentence \"is evidently applicable only to England, as the focal point of the world money market.\" This is an important qualification: the cyclical rhythm of reserve growth and drain is not a universal law of all countries but a feature of the world's central money market—England. Engels's editorial intervention here is methodologically significant; he disciplines Marx's generalization, reminding the reader that the analysis is situated historically and geographically. The theoretical point nonetheless stands: the world's metal is distributed among nations in a pattern reflecting each nation's role in the world market, and the oscillatory movement of metal is a redistributive mechanism that always restores the underlying pattern. Points (8) and (9): Drain as Warning Signal; Asia and the Balance of Payments Point (8) states: \"A drain of metal is generally the symptom of a change in the state of foreign trade, and this change is in turn an advance warning that conditions are again approaching a crisis.\" Footnote 15 records Newmarch's taxonomy of the three causes of a drain: (1) purely commercial—imports exceeding exports, as between 1836 and 1844 and again in 1847 with steep corn imports; (2) to procure means for investing English capital abroad, as for railways in India in 1857; (3) for final expenditure abroad, as for war purposes in the East in 1853 and 1854. This taxonomy anticipates the whole Wilson polemic: the three causes are qualitatively distinct, and collapsing them into a single category—\"the foreign balance\"—is precisely the error Marx will attack. Point (9): \"The balance of payments may be in favour of Asia and against Europe and America.\" Footnote 16 (dated 1918, an addition by Engels or later editors, actually a Newmarch quote) elaborates: \"When you combine India and China, when you bring into account the transactions between India and Australia, and the still more important transactions between China and the United States, the trade being a triangular one, and the adjustment taking place through us ... then it is true that the balance of trade was not merely against this country, but against France, and against the United States\" (B.A. 1857). This introduces the idea, developed later in the chapter, that England functions as the world's clearing-house, that the trade between India, China, Australia, and the United States is a triangular trade settled through London, and that this structure produces a permanent drain of silver to Asia that is not a simple bilateral deficit. The Two Moments of Precious Metal Import and the Mechanics of the Drain After the numbered points, Marx turns unheaded to the analysis of the moments at which imports of precious metal principally take place. There are two such moments: (1) The first phase of low interest rates which follows the crisis and is marked by a contraction of production; as well as the subsequent phase in which the interest rate rises but has not yet reached its average level. In this phase returns are brisk and commercial credit high, so that the demand for loan capital does not grow in proportion to the expansion of production. In both these moments, where loan capital is relatively abundant, \"the superfluous influx of capital in the form of gold and silver, i.e. a form in which it can function at first only as loan capital, has an important effect on the rate of interest and hence on the entire business climate.\" The conceptual point: gold arriving in a phase of abundant loan capital enters directly as loanable money capital, and its effect on the rate of interest operates through that specifically monetary form. (2) A drain, i.e. a continued heavy export, of precious metal appears as soon as returns are no longer easy, markets are overstocked and the apparent prosperity is maintained only by credit; i.e. when a very pronounced demand for loan capital already exists, so that the rate of interest has reached at least its average level. Under these circumstances, the effect of a continued withdrawal of capital \"in a form in which it exists directly as loan capital\" is \"significantly intensified.\" This must have a direct effect on the rate of interest. But—and this is the dialectical reversal—\"instead of the rise in the interest rate restricting credit transactions, it expands them and leads to an excessive strain on all their resources. This period therefore precedes the crash.\" Newmarch is then cited (B.A. 1857) to document the swelling of credit as the rate rises: asked whether the volume of bills in circulation increases with the rate of discount, Newmarch replies \"It seems to do so.\" Asked to explain (1522), Newmarch gives a beautiful account of the substitution between the traditionally dominant modes of settlement: \"In quiet ordinary times the ledger is the real instrument of exchange; but when any difficulty arises; when, for example, under such circumstances as I have suggested, there is a rise in the bank-rate of discount ... then the transactions naturally resolve themselves into drawing bills of exchange, those bills of exchange being not only more convenient as regards legal proof of the transaction which has taken place, but also being more convenient in order to effect purchases elsewhere, and being pre-eminently convenient as a means of credit by which capital can be raised.\" This is a superb illustration of the \"escape into credit\" phenomenon: when confidence wobbles, transactions take the form of negotiable credit instruments , whose quantity and velocity rise even as the underlying commercial position deteriorates. Adding to this is the psychology of the panic: \"as soon as somewhat threatening circumstances lead the Bank to raise its discount rate ... a general fear sets in that this will mount to a crescendo. Everyone, therefore, and the credit-jobber above all, seeks to discount the future and have as many means of credit as possible at his disposal at the given moment.\" This anticipatory rush—what Marx elsewhere calls the general conversion of value claims into liquid form—intensifies the demand for credit precisely as the credit supply contracts, which is the classic mechanism of the panic. Then comes one of the chapter's most famous images: \"the reasons just adduced mean that it is not the mere quantity of precious metal imported or exported that operates as such, but that this firstly has its effect by way of the specific character of precious metal as capital in the money form , while secondly it acts as the feather which, added to the weight already on the scales, is enough to tip the balance decisively to one side ; it has this effect because it intervenes in circumstances where anything extra on one side or the other is sufficient to tip the scales.\" The force of this metaphor is that it captures the disproportion between cause and effect : a drain of £5–8 million, \"the limit of our experience up till now,\" could exert a \"significant effect,\" yet is \"infinitesimal against the total volume of English production\" and \"insignificant even against the £70 million in gold that is the average circulation in England.\" Why is the effect so disproportionate to the cause? Because \"the development of the credit and banking system which on the one hand seeks to press all money capital into the service of production (or what comes to the same thing, to transform all money income into capital), while on the other hand it reduces the metal reserve in a given phase of the cycle to a minimum, at which it can no longer perform the functions ascribed to it—it is this elaborate credit and banking system that makes the entire organism over-sensitive .\" At a less developed level of production, a contraction or expansion of the reserve relative to its average \"is a matter of relative indifference.\" Conversely, \"even a very severe drain of gold is relatively without effect, unless it takes place during the critical period of the industrial cycle.\" This is the crucial theoretical insight of the chapter, and it is worth unpacking carefully. The credit system is systematically over-sensitized by its own development. The more fully credit presses every hoard and every idle balance into active service, the smaller the metallic cushion that remains; and the smaller the cushion, the larger the effect of any given drain. The consequence is a structural instability that increases with the development of credit itself. This explains the paradox that modern crises are caused by quantities that would be negligible in an earlier stage of production. It also refutes the naive view that crises are proportional to physical magnitudes: the crisis effect is a function not of the quantity drained but of the reserve ratio and the phase of the industrial cycle . Marx then explicitly limits the argument by acknowledging two omitted cases: Harvest failures, etc. : \"Here, a major and sudden disturbance in the balance of production, as expressed in the drain of gold, obviates the need for any further explanation. The effect is all the greater, the more this disturbance arises at a period when production is working at high pressure.\" The function of the metal reserve as guarantee for convertibility and as the pivot of the entire credit system. \"The central bank is the pivot of the credit system. And the metal reserve is in turn the pivot of the bank.\" Footnote 18 cites Newmarch's vivid statement (B.A. 1857, no. 1364): \"The reserve of bullion in the Bank of England is, in truth, the central reserve or hoard of treasure upon which the whole trade of the country is made to turn; all the other banks in the country look to the Bank of England as the central hoard or reservoir from which they are to draw their reserve of coin; and it is upon that hoard or reservoir that the action of the foreign exchanges always falls.\" This is the capital formulation of the chapter's hierarchical structure of the credit system: bank ← Bank of England ← metal reserve ← world market (foreign exchanges). Footnote 17 is a polemic against \"Weguelin's ridiculous response,\" who says that an outflow of £5 million in money is that much capital less, \"and seeks to use this to explain phenomena that do not occur even with infinitely greater rises in price, or with devaluations, expansions and contractions of the actual industrial capital.\" Marx adds that \"no less ridiculous is the attempt to explain these phenomena as direct symptoms of an expansion or contraction in the mass of real capital (looked at from the point of view of its material elements).\" This is a two-front attack: against the monetarist reduction of the drain to a loss of capital tout court, and against the realist reduction of the drain to a movement of physical capital. Both errors stem from failing to grasp that the drain operates through the specific form of capital in the money form. The Collapse of the Credit System into the Monetary System and the \"theoretical dualism\" of Enlightened Economics From the pivotal function of the metal reserve, Marx draws the great dialectical conclusion: \"It is inevitable that the credit system should collapse into the monetary system, as I have already shown in Volume 1, Chapter 3, in connection with means of payment.\" Both Tooke and Loyd-Overstone concede that the utmost sacrifice of real wealth is necessary at the critical moment to maintain the metal basis; the dispute between them \"simply turns on a plus or minus and on the more or less rational way to cope with something unavoidable.\" Footnote 19 cites The Economist (1847, p. 1418): \"Practically, then, both Mr Tooke and Mr Loyd would meet an additional demand for gold ... by an early ... contraction of credit by raising the rate of interest, and restricting advances of capital ... But the principles of Mr Loyd lead to certain (legal) restrictions and regulations which ... produce the most serious inconvenience.\" Thus the theoretical dispute between the Banking and Currency Schools, which had convulsed financial policy for decades, reduces in practice to a difference in the mechanics of an unavoidable sacrifice—not a disagreement about ends. Marx's dialectical point is that the apparent opposition conceals a shared premise: the sacrosanctity of the metal basis. Then comes the magnificent passage on the \"beautiful theoretical dualism.\" As long as enlightened economics claims to treat \"of capital,\" it looks down on gold and silver \"with the utmost disdain, as the most indifferent and useless form of capital.\" As soon as it deals with banking, \"this is completely reversed, and gold and silver become capital par excellence, for whose preservation every other form of capital and labour have to be sacrificed.\" Marx then poses the question: \"In what way are gold and silver distinguished from other forms of wealth? Not by magnitude, for this is determined by the amount of labour embodied in them. But rather as autonomous embodiments and expressions of the social character of wealth .\" The parenthesis (F.E.) elaborates the theory of social wealth that underwrites this claim: \"The wealth of society consists simply of the wealth of those individuals who are its private proprietors. It only proves itself to be social by the fact that these individuals exchange qualitatively different use-values with one another in order to satisfy their needs. In capitalist production, they can do this only by way of money. Thus it is only by way of money that the individual's wealth is realized as social wealth; the social nature of that wealth is embodied in money, in this thing.\" This is a compressed statement of a crucial Marxian thesis: under capitalism, the social character of wealth—its insertion into a system of mutual dependence and exchange—has no existence except in the money object. The wealth of each individual is social only insofar as it takes the form of money, which is to say, only insofar as it is convertible into the universal equivalent. The social relation is thus reified in a thing, and the thing acquires autonomous power over the individuals whose relations it embodies. Credit, \"being similarly a social form of wealth, displaces money and usurps its position.\" It is confidence in the social character of production that makes the money form appear merely evanescent and ideal, a mere notion. But then comes the reversal that constitutes the dialectical core of the chapter: \"as soon as credit is shaken, and this is a regular and necessary phase in the cycle of modern industry, all real wealth is supposed to be actually and suddenly transformed into money, into gold and silver—a crazy demand, but one that necessarily grows out of the system itself. And the gold and silver that is supposed to satisfy these immense claims amounts in all to a few millions in the vaults of the bank.\" Footnote 20 reinforces the impossibility: \"You quite agree that there is no mode by which you can modify the demand for bullion except by raising the rate of interest?\"—Chapman (associate member of the great bill-broking firm of Overend, Gurney & Co.): \"I should say so ... When our bullion falls to a certain point, we had better sound the tocsin at once and say we are drooping, and every man sending money abroad must do it at his own peril.\" Chapman's \"tocsin\" (alarm bell) and the peremptory \"at his own peril\" capture vividly the coercive, quasi-juridical character of the credit system's response. Marx then draws the philosophical conclusion, one of the most important in the entire volume: \"A drain of gold, therefore, shows strikingly by its effects that production is not really subjected to social control, as social production, and that the social form of wealth exists alongside wealth itself as a thing.\" The credit system, for all its apparent overcoming of the metallic barrier, testifies in every crisis to the anarchy of capitalist production. The social form of wealth (money) stands over against real wealth (use-values, productive apparatus) as an external, dominating thing. The capitalist system shares this with earlier commodity-producing systems, \"but it is only with this system that the most striking and grotesque form of this absurd contradiction and paradox arises, because (1) in the capitalist system production for direct use-value, for the producer's own use, is most completely abolished, so that wealth exists only as a social process expressed as the entwinement of production and circulation; and (2) because with the development of the credit system, capitalist production constantly strives to overcome this metallic barrier, which is both a material and an imaginary barrier to wealth and its movement, while time and again breaking its head on it.\" The word \"imaginary\" is crucial: the metallic barrier is not merely a physical limit but an ideological one, built into the social form of wealth. In the crisis, \"we get the demand that all bills of exchange, securities and commodities should be simultaneously convertible into bank money all at once, and this bank money again into gold.\" This is the \"crazy demand\" that expresses the \"absurd contradiction and paradox\" of a system in which social wealth is at once real and unreal, social and reified, dependent on mutual trust and yet guaranteed only by a few million ounces of metal. Transition to the Exchange Rate: The Barometer of Metallic Movement Section II, \"The Exchange Rate,\" begins with a parenthetical (F.E. or an editorial insertion) stating that \"the barometer for the international movement of the money metals is of course the rate of exchange.\" The exchange rate is then defined operationally through the mark-sterling example: if England has to make more payments to Germany than Germany to England, then in London the price of the mark as expressed in sterling rises, while in Hamburg and Berlin the price of sterling expressed in marks falls. If this surplus of English payment obligations towards Germany does not balance out again—for instance through a predominance of German purchases from England—then the sterling price for bills of exchange in marks on Germany must rise to a point at which \"it pays to send metal from England to Germany in payment—gold coin or bullion—instead of bills of exchange. This is the typical course of events.\" This is the mechanism of the gold point, though Marx does not use the terminology here. When the exchange rate falls past a certain threshold (the cost of shipping and insuring metal), it becomes cheaper to pay in bullion than to buy bills, and a metallic outflow begins. If the export of precious metal \"becomes more large-scale and persists for a longer time,\" the English bank reserves are affected, and the English money market—in the first place the Bank of England—must take protective measures. \"Essentially these consist, as we have already seen, in putting up the rate of interest.\" The mechanism of restoration is then spelled out: \"With a major drain of gold, the money market usually becomes tight, i.e. the demand for loan capital in the money form significantly outweighs the supply, and a higher rate of interest then arises quite spontaneously; the discount rate decreed by the Bank of England corresponds to the actual situation and prevails in the market.\" But there is an important qualification: \"there are also cases where the drain of metal arises from out of the ordinary business transactions (e.g. from loans to foreign countries, capital investment abroad, etc.), so that the state of the London money market as such in no way justifies an effective raising of interest rates; the Bank of England then first has to 'make money scarce' by large-scale borrowing 'on the open market', as the expression goes, thus artificially producing the situation that justifies raising interest rates, or makes this necessary; a manoeuvre that becomes harder every year.\" (The closing phrase \"a manoeuvre that becomes harder every year\" is a F.E. addition, presumably from the perspective of 1894.) This is a subtle and important point. Two distinct situations arise: 1. Endogenous tightness : the drain arises from the general state of trade, and the market rate of interest already rises. The Bank merely ratifies the market. 2. Exogenous drain : the drain arises from causes—foreign loans, capital investment abroad—that do not in themselves tighten the domestic money market. Here the Bank must engineer the tightness (\"make money scarce\") by open-market borrowing operations, so that a rate rise that was not justified by market conditions is made to appear justified. This distinction directly anticipates the Wilson polemic: the drain arising from capital export (rails to India) is not the same as a drain arising from a trade deficit, and the mechanism of correction differs. It also anticipates the later point that England's trade structure—long credit on exports, cash payment for imports—produces a systematic dependence on the exchange-rate mechanism. The Effect of Interest Rates on Exchange Rates: Mill and Hubbard How raising the interest rate affects exchange rates is shown by evidence before the 1857 House of Commons Committee on Bank Legislation (B.A. 1857). John Stuart Mill (2176): \"When there is a state of commercial difficulty there is always ... a considerable fall in the price of securities ... foreigners send over to buy railway shares in this country, or English holders of foreign railway shares sell their foreign railway shares abroad ... there is so much transfer of bullion prevented.\" (2182): A large and rich class of bankers and dealers in securities, \"through whom the equalization of the rate of interest and the equalization of commercial pressure between different countries usually takes place ... are always on the look out to buy securities which are likely to rise ... The place for them to buy securities will be the country which is sending bullion away.\" (2184): \"These investments of capital took place to a very considerable extent in 1847, to a sufficient extent to have relieved the drain considerably.\" The mechanism Mill describes is the arbitrage reaction : high domestic interest rates depress security prices, and foreign arbitrageurs purchase the depressed securities, thereby creating an inward transfer of funds that offsets the outward drain. This is one of the concrete channels through which a high rate restores the exchange. J. G. Hubbard , ex-Governor of the Bank of England (director since 1838): (2545): \"There are great quantities of European securities ... which have a European currency in all the different money-markets, and those bonds, as soon as their value is ... reduced by 1 or 2 per cent in one market, are immediately purchased for transmission to those markets where their value is still unimpaired.\" (2565): Foreign countries are considerably in debt to English merchants. (2566): \"In 1847, the ultimate restoration of our position was effected by our striking off so many millions previously due by America, and so many millions due by Russia to this country.\" A parenthesis by F.E. here is devastating: \"(England was in debt to those very countries for corn, to the tune of 'so many millions', and also did not fail to 'strike off' the greater part by the bankruptcy of English debtors. See the 1857 Report on the Bank Acts, as quoted in Chapter 30 above, p. 624).\" The double irony is that the \"restoration\" of England's position was accomplished not by the export of goods but by the legal annihilation of English debts through the bankruptcy of English debtors—a \"striking off\" that Hubbard presents as restitution. This is Marx's dialectical point about \"restoration of equilibrium\": the equilibrium is restored by the destruction of value claims , not by any real adjustment of production. Hubbard continues (2572): \"In 1847, the exchange between this country and St Petersburg was very high. When the Government Letter came out authorizing the Bank to issue irrespectively of the limitation of £14,000,000 (above and beyond the gold reserve), the stipulation was that the rate of discount should be 8 per cent. At that moment, with the then rate of discount, it was a profitable operation to order gold to be shipped from St Petersburg to London and on its arrival to lend it at 8 per cent up to the maturity of the three months' bills drawn against the purchase of gold.\" (2573): \"In all bullion operations there are many points to be taken into consideration; there is the rate of exchange and the rate of interest, which is available for the investment during the period of the maturity of the bill (drawn against it).\" This passage introduces the gold-arbitrage mechanism in its most concrete form: if the domestic discount rate is high enough, it becomes profitable to import gold even from a distant market like St Petersburg, because the interest earned over the period of the bill exceeds the cost of shipment plus the loss on the exchange. The implication—crucial to the chapter's argument—is that the metallic movement responds to a combination of exchange rate and interest rate , not to one or the other alone. This will become the key to Marx's refutation of Wilson: the interest rate and the exchange rate can move independently, and the effect on metal flows depends on their joint configuration. Rate of Exchange with Asia: Wilson's Error and the Nature of Capital Export Marx now turns to the rate of exchange with Asia, which he introduces with two reasons: (1) it shows how England, when its exchange with Asia is unfavourable, \"manages to recoup its losses from other countries whose imports from Asia are paid for through English middlemen\"; and (2) it exposes \"Mr Wilson's stupid attempt to identify the impact of an export of precious metal on the rate of exchange with the impact of an export of capital in general on the rate; the export in question being in both cases not a means of purchase or payment but an export for capital investment.\" The distinction is fundamental and is worth stating exactly: an export of capital is not the same as an export of means of purchase or payment. The first is a movement of value that creates a permanent claim on future revenue; the second is the settlement of a current obligation. Concretely: if £12 million is sent to India for railway investment, whether sent in precious metal or in iron rails, \"the same amount of capital being transferred in each case from one country to the other; this transfer, moreover, does not go into the ordinary commercial account, and the exporting country does not expect any other return for it than the subsequent annual revenue from the earnings of these railways.\" Then comes a key asymmetry: \"If this export takes place in the form of precious metal, then, because precious metal as such is directly loanable money capital and the basis of the entire money system, it will not necessarily always have a direct effect on the money market and thereby on the rate of interest in the country exporting this precious metal, though it does have this effect in the cases so far developed. It has a similarly direct effect on the rate of exchange.\" The form of the capital matters: precious metal, as the directly loanable form of capital and the basis of the money system, has a direct effect on both the money market and the exchange rate; rails do not. In particular, \"precious metal is sent in payment only in so far as the bills of exchange that are offered on the London money market, on India for example, are insufficient to make these extra remittances. The demand for bills of exchange on India thus outweighs the supply, and so the rate of exchange turns temporarily against England; not because England is in debt to India but rather because it has to send extraordinary sums to India.\" This is a key distinction between the cause of an adverse exchange: it can arise from a debt or from an extraordinary capital transfer . The exchange rate registers the latter no less than the former, but the economic meaning is entirely different. Marx then notes that, in the long run, a precious-metal export to India \"must have the effect of increasing the Indian demand for English commodities, because it indirectly raises India's power to consume European goods.\" If the capital is dispatched in rails, it can have no influence on the exchange rate \"since India does not have to make any return payment for these. For this very reason, it also need not have any effect on the money market.\" Wilson's argument is then stated and refuted. Wilson seeks to elicit an effect on the money market and interest rate from the fact that extra outlays such as these \"lead to an extra demand for monetary accommodation, and thus affect the rate of interest.\" Marx's reply: \"This may well be the case; but to contend that it has to take place under any circumstances is totally mistaken. Wherever the rails may be sent and laid, whether on English soil or on Indian, they represent nothing but a certain expansion of English production in a particular sphere. To maintain that an increase in production, even a very substantial one, cannot take place without driving up the rate of interest is sheer foolishness.\" Monetary accommodation may grow—\"i.e. the sum of dealings in which credit operations are involved; but these operations can increase while the given rate of interest remains the same.\" The empirical demonstration: \"This was in fact the case in England during the railway mania of the 1840s. The interest rate did not rise.\" And Marx adds the general principle: \"it is obvious that in so far as real capital comes into consideration, in this case commodities, the effect on the money market is exactly the same whether these commodities are designed for export abroad or for domestic use. There could only be a distinction if England's capital investment abroad had a limiting effect on its commercial exports—on exports that have to be paid for and thus bring a return—or in so far as these capital investments in general were already a symptom of an over-taxing of credit and the beginning of fraudulent operations.\" This passage is an important statement of a methodological principle: the money market responds to the aggregate demand for loan capital , not to the physical destination of the capital. Whether capital is invested at home or abroad, the effect on interest is the same unless the investment is financed in a way that draws on the money market in a special manner. Wilson's error is to read the physical form of the capital transfer as determining its monetary effect. Marx's point: the form of the money (precious metal vs. bills) matters, but the form of the commodity (rails vs. textiles) does not, except as it affects the pattern of payments. The Newmarch-Wilson Exchange: Testimony on Indian Railway Investment The chapter then presents a long excerpt from the 1857 Committee where Wilson questions Newmarch. 1786 : Wilson asks whether Newmarch has grounds for thinking the exchanges with India are in favour of England despite the large bullion transfers to the East. Newmarch's reply is quantitative: exports of goods from the U.K. to India in 1851 were £7,420,000; India House drafts (funds drawn from India by the East India Company for their own expenditure) were £3,200,000, making total exports £10,620,000. In 1855 exports rose to £10,350,000 and drafts to £3,700,000, making £14,050,000. For 1855 imports from India were £12,670,000, leaving a balance in favour of the United Kingdom \"as regards the direct trade between the two countries\" of £1,380,000. Wilson's comment is that exchange rates are also affected by indirect trade: exports from India to Australia and North America are covered by drafts on London and have exactly the same effect as if the commodities went directly from India to England. If India and China are taken together, moreover, the balance would be against England, since China always has sizeable payments to make to India for opium, and England has payments to make to China, the sums going on to India by this detour (1787, 1788). This exchange is important because Wilson is actually correct on the mechanics of indirect trade—drafts on London from third countries have the same exchange-rate effect as direct trade—but he uses this insight to conflate everything into a single \"balance,\" whereas the theoretically relevant distinctions remain. Newmarch concedes the point about indirect trade but insists on the distinction between capital investment and trade settlement. 1791 : Wilson asks whether the effect on the exchanges would be the same irrespective of whether the capital \"went in the form of iron rails and locomotives, or whether it went in the form of coin.\" Newmarch's response is \"quite correct\": the £12 million sent to India in recent years for railway construction \"has served to purchase an annuity which India has to pay to England at regular intervals.\" But \"as far as regards the immediate operation on the bullion market, the investments of the £12 million would only be operative as far as bullion was required to be sent out for actual money disbursements.\" 1797 (Weguelin asks): \"If no return is made for this iron (rails), how can it be said to affect the exchanges?\" Newmarch replies: \"I do not think that that part of the expenditure which is sent out in the form of commodities affects the computation of the exchange ... The computation of the exchange between two countries is affected, one might say, solely by the quantity of obligations or bills offering in one country, as compared with the quantity offering in the other country against it; that is the rationale of the exchange. Now, as regards the transmission of those £12,000,000, the money in the first place is subscribed in this country ... now, if the nature of the transaction was such that the whole of that £12,000,000 was required to be laid down in Calcutta, Bombay, and Madras in treasure ... a sudden demand would very violently operate upon the price of silver, and upon the exchange, just the same as if the India Company were to give notice tomorrow that their drafts were to be raised from £3,000,000 to £12,000,000. But half of those £12,000,000 is spent ... in buying commodities in this country ... iron rails and timber, and other materials ... it is an expenditure in this country of the capital of this country for a particular kind of commodity to be sent out to India, and there is an end of it.\" This is a critically important statement: the rationale of the exchange is the quantity of obligations or bills offered in one country versus another—not the physical trade balance. The exchange rate is a market for bills, and it is determined by the relative supply and demand of bills, which in turn reflects the pattern of payments. This is a formal, circulation-level determination of the exchange rate, and it is precisely the point Marx endorses against Wilson's \"realist\" reduction. 1798 (Weguelin): \"But the production of those articles of iron and timber necessary for the railways produces a large consumption of foreign articles, which might affect the exchange?\"—\"Certainly.\" This concedes that the production of rails consumes imported inputs, which may affect the exchange through the import side. But this is a second-order effect, mediated by the pattern of imports, not a direct effect of the rails themselves. Wilson then offers the opinion (1799) that iron largely represents labour, while the wages paid for this labour largely represent imported goods. He asks: 1801 : \"But speaking quite generally, it would have the effect of turning the exchanges against this country if you sent abroad the articles which were produced by the consumption of the imported articles without receiving any remittance for them either in the shape of produce or otherwise?\" Newmarch's reply is instructive: \"That principle is exactly what took place in this country during the time of the great railway expenditure\" (1845). \"For three or four or five years, you spent upon railways £30,000,000, nearly the whole of which went in the payment of wages. You sustained in three years a larger population employed in constructing railways, and locomotives, and carriages, and stations than you employed in the whole of the factory districts. The people ... spent those wages in buying tea and sugar and spirits and other foreign commodities; those commodities were imported; but it was a fact, that during the time this great expenditure was going on the foreign exchanges between this country and other countries were not materially deranged. There was no efflux of bullion, on the contrary, there was rather an influx.\" This is a striking empirical refutation of Wilson's principle: the great railway expenditure of 1845, with its massive increase in wages and consumption of imports, did not cause an adverse exchange or a gold drain. The reason, implicit in Newmarch's account, is that the increased imports were matched by increased demand for English goods elsewhere and by a favourable movement of capital. The empirical pattern refutes the naive trade-balance doctrine. 1802 : Wilson insists that, given a settled balance of trade between England and India and the exchange rate at par values, the extra shipment of iron and locomotives \"would affect the exchanges with India.\" Newmarch cannot accept this, if the rails are sent as capital investment and India does not have to pay for them in one form or another. He adds the important general principle: \"I agree with the principle that no one country can have permanently against itself an adverse state of exchange with all the other countries, with which it deals; an adverse exchange with one country necessarily produces a favourable exchange with another.\" 1803 : Wilson: \"But would not a transfer of capital be the same whether it was sent in one form or another?\"—\"As regards the obligation it would.\" 1804 : Wilson: \"The effect therefore of making railways in India, whether you send bullion or whether you send materials, would be the same upon the capital-market here in increasing the value of capital as if the whole was sent out in bullion?\" Marx's commentary is sharp. If iron prices did not rise, this was at least a proof that the \"value\" of the \"capital\" contained in the rails had not increased. But what is at issue is the value of the money capital, the rate of interest. \"Wilson is trying to identify money capital and capital in general.\" The simple fact is: (1) £12 million was subscribed in England for Indian railways; this has nothing directly to do with the rate of exchange, and the destination of the £12 million is immaterial as far as the money market is concerned. If the money market is in a favourable condition, it need not have any effect at all, just as the English railway subscriptions of 1844 and 1845 left the money market unaffected. If the money market is already somewhat tight, the rate of interest could be affected, but only in the sense of a rise—and according to Wilson's theory this would necessarily have an effect on the exchange rates that was favourable for England (inhibiting the export of precious metal). Then Marx delivers the decisive criticism: \"Mr Wilson jumps from one thing to another. In question 1802 it is the exchange rate that is supposedly affected, in question 1804 the 'value of capital'—two very different things. The rate of interest may have an effect on the exchange rates, and the exchange rates may have an effect on the rate of interest. But the rate of interest may also remain constant despite a change in exchange rates, while exchange rates may remain constant despite a change in the rate of interest.\" Wilson cannot accept that when capital is sent abroad, the mere form in which it is sent can have such a different effect—that is, that the money form of the capital matters. \"This is something that contradicts the whole trend of enlightened economics.\" Marx's criticism of Newmarch is equally sharp: Newmarch \"answers Wilson one-sidedly; he gives him not the slightest warning that he has suddenly leapt without reason from the exchange rate to the rate of interest.\" Newmarch's response to question 1804 is \"uncertain and vacillating\": \"No doubt, if there is a demand for £12,000,000 to be raised, it is immaterial, as regards the general rate of interest, whether that £12 million is required to be sent in bullion or in materials. I think, however\" (a fine transition, this \"however\", when he intends to say the exact opposite) \"it is not quite immaterial\" (it is immaterial, but, nevertheless, it is not immaterial) \"because in the one case the £6 million would be returned immediately; in the other case it would not be returned so rapidly. Therefore it would make some\" (what definiteness!) \"difference, whether the £6 million was expended in this country or sent wholly out of it.\" Marx then unpacks the ambiguity of \"return\": \"In so far as this £6 million has been spent in England, it exists in rails, locomotives, etc. that are sent to India, from where they do not return, and it is only by amortization, i.e. very slowly, that their value returns, whereas the £6 million in precious metal might well return very quickly in kind. In so far as the £6 million was spent on wages, it has been consumed; but the money in which it was advanced continues to circulate in the country just as before, or else forms a reserve. The same applies to the profits of the rail producers and to the portion of the £6 million that replaces their constant capital. The ambiguous word 'return' is thus used by Newmarch simply to avoid saying directly that the money remains in the country, and that in so far as it functions as loanable money capital, the difference for the money market (aside from the fact that more metal money might have been absorbed for circulation) is simply that it is spent on A's account rather than B's.\" This is a devastating critical passage. Newmarch's vacillation reflects a real theoretical confusion: he wants to say that the form of the capital transfer matters (which is true for the bullion market ), but he cannot articulate why in a way that avoids confusing the money market and the capital market. Marx's resolution is crisp: the metal form matters because precious metal is directly loanable money capital and the basis of the money system; the commodity form does not matter because the money spent on producing commodities remains in the country as circulating money or reserve. The only real effect of the commodity form is through the increased imports required for production, which is a second-order effect. Marx then adds a general principle: \"Investment of this kind, where the capital is transferred to other countries in commodities rather than in precious metal, can affect the exchange rates (but not the rate with the country in which it is invested) only in so far as the production of these exported commodities requires an additional import of other foreign goods. But then this production cannot balance out the extra import. The same is true with any export on credit, irrespective of whether capital investment or ordinary trade is involved. This extra import, however, can react to produce an extra demand for English goods, e.g. from the colonies or the U.S.A.\" The parenthetical \"(but not the rate with the country in which it is invested)\" is crucial: capital investment in India does not affect the exchange rate with India, because India does not have to make any payment for it. It affects the exchange rate with third countries only insofar as the production of exported commodities requires imports from those countries. The \"Good Government\" Tribute: Wood's Cross-Examination of Newmarch Marx next turns to the East India Company drafts and the tribute question, introduced by a note that Newmarch previously said (1786) that English exports to India were greater than imports as a result of the East India Company's drafts. Sir Charles Wood cross-examines him on this point. The excess of English exports over imports is \"in fact brought into being by an import from India for which England does not pay any equivalent: the East India Company's drafts (now the Indian government's) boil down to a tribute extracted from India.\" The arithmetic is given: In 1855 English imports from India came to £12,660,000; English exports to India were £10,350,000. A balance of £2½ million in India's favour. \"If that was the whole state of the case, that £2,250,000 would have to be remitted in some form to India. But then come in the advertisements from the India House. The India House advertise to this effect that they are prepared to grant drafts on the various presidencies in India to the extent of £3,250,000\" (this amount was levied for the London expenses of the East India Company and for the dividends to be paid to stockholders). \"And that not merely liquidates the £2,250,000 which arose out of the course of trade, but it presents £1,000,000 of surplus\" (1917). 1922 (Wood): \"Then the effect of those India House drafts is not to increase the exports to India, but pro tanto to diminish them?\" Marx's gloss: \"(He should say, to reduce by that amount the need to cover imports from India by exports.)\" Newmarch explains this by the fact that the English export \"good government\" to India for this £3,700,000 (1925). As Minister for India, Wood knew all about the kind of \"good government\" exported by the English, and he rightly says, not without irony (1926): \"Then the export, which, you state, is caused by the East India drafts, is an export of good government, and not of produce.\" This passage introduces the concept of the tribute —a flow of value from India to England for which England exports nothing material in return (except, as Wood ironically puts it, \"good government\"). Marx broadens the point: \"England exports a good deal of 'good government' in this way, and also much capital investment in foreign countries, thus receiving imports that are quite separate from the usual run of business; in part this is a tribute for the 'good government' which has been exported, in part a revenue from capital invested in the colonies and elsewhere, i.e. a tribute for which it does not have to pay any equivalent.\" The theoretical implication for the exchange rate is stated clearly: \"It is therefore evident that the rate of exchange is unaffected if England simply consumes this tribute without exporting anything in return. It is also clear that the rate of exchange is still unaffected if it reinvests this tribute not in England but abroad, either productively or unproductively; if for example it uses it to send munitions to the Crimea.\" In other words, the tribute flows into England from the colonies and elsewhere; whether England consumes it domestically or reinvests it abroad, the effect on the rate of exchange is nil, because the tribute was received \"for nothing.\" This is a crucial point because it means that a substantial portion of England's imports—the tribute component—can rise without any corresponding strain on the exchange. Marx draws the general principle: \"Whether it is domestic or foreign products that form a portion of the revenue—the latter case simply presupposing the exchange of domestic products for foreign—the consumption of this revenue, productively or unproductively, in no way affects the rate of exchange, even if it does affect the volume of production.\" The consumption of revenue—whether domestic or foreign, productive or unproductive—does not affect the exchange rate. This is one of the sharper theoretical points of the chapter, and it is directly opposed to the naive view that consumption affects the exchange rate through import demand. The Crimea War Supplies Question: Newmarch's \"Metaphysical\" Retreat The chapter then examines Wood's question 1934: how would the sending of war supplies to the Crimea affect the exchange rate with Turkey. Newmarch replies: \"I do not see that the mere transmission of warlike stores would necessarily affect the exchange, but certainly the transmission of treasure would affect the exchange.\" Here Newmarch distinguishes correctly between capital in the money form and other capital. But Wilson then asks (1935) : \"If you make an export of any article to a great extent, for which there is to be no corresponding import\" (Mr Wilson forgets... a long parenthesis in which Marx makes three points) \"you do not discharge the foreign debt you have created by your imports\" (but if you have already paid for these imports previously, e.g. by credit given abroad, no debt is created for them, and the question has nothing at all to do with the international balance; it is a simple matter of productive or unproductive expenditure, irrespective of whether the products consumed in this way are domestic products or foreign) \"and therefore you must by that transaction affect the exchanges by not discharging the foreign debt, by reason of your export having no corresponding imports?\"—Newmarch: \"That is true as regards countries generally.\" Marx's critique of Wilson's lecture : \"What Wilson's lecture boils down to is that every export without a corresponding import is at the same time an import without a corresponding export; since foreign and thus imported commodities also enter into the production of the article exported. The assumption is that any such export is either based on an unpaid import or gives rise to one—i.e. a debt abroad. This is wrong, even leaving aside the following two circumstances: (1) England gets certain imports gratis and does not pay any equivalent for them, e.g. a part of its Indian imports. It can exchange these for American imports, and export the latter without matching imports; in any case, as far as the value is concerned, it has only exported something that cost it nothing. And (2) it may have paid for imports, e.g. American ones, that form additional capital; if these are consumed unproductively, e.g. in war materials, this does not create a debt to America and does not affect the exchange rate with America.\" Wilson's error is to assume that every export-without-import implies a corresponding import-without-export (a debt). But this is false because: (1) some imports are gratis (the tribute), and (2) some exports may be produced from imports that have already been paid for. In either case, an asymmetry in the commodity trade can exist without creating a debt or affecting the exchange rate. This is a theoretical confusion grounded in Wilson's more general error of collapsing all international movements into a single \"balance.\" Newmarch contradicts himself between questions 1934 and 1935, and Wood draws his attention to this in 1938 : \"If no portion of the goods which are employed in the manufacture of the articles exported without return (war materials) came from the country to which those articles are sent, how is the exchange with that country affected; supposing the trade with Turkey to be in an ordinary state of equilibrium, how is the exchange between this country and Turkey affected by the export of warlike stores to the Crimea?\" At this point Newmarch loses his calm demeanour; he forgets that he has already answered this simple question correctly under 1934, and says: \"We seem, I think, to have exhausted the practical question, and to have now attained a very elevated region of metaphysical discussion.\" This is a superb moment of intellectual comedy: the witness, caught in self-contradiction, retreats to the accusation of \"metaphysical\" abstraction. Marx savours the irony: it was precisely Wood's simple practical question that Newmarch could not answer without contradicting himself. Wilson's Economist Articles: The Four Statements on Commodity Abundance and Interest Rates The chapter then digresses to Wilson's Economist articles. A preliminary editorial parenthesis (F.E.) reads: \"Wilson has still a further version of his contention that the rate of exchange is affected by every transfer of capital from one country to another, no matter whether this takes place in the form of precious metal or of commodities. Wilson knows of course that the exchange rate is affected by the rate of interest, particularly by the relationship between the interest rates prevailing in the two countries whose reciprocal exchange rate is in question. If he can then prove that an excess of capital in general, and thus first of all in commodities of all kinds, including precious metal, also contributes towards determining the rate of interest, he will already be one step nearer his goal; the transfer of a significant portion of this capital to another country must then alter the rate of interest in both countries, and moreover in contrary directions, thus in the second place also the rate of exchange between the two countries.\" This is the F.E. reconstruction of Wilson's hidden argument: he needs to prove that commodity abundance lowers the interest rate, because then a transfer of commodities will alter interest rates in opposite directions in the two countries and thereby alter the exchange rate. This is the theoretical scaffolding which Marx then demolishes. Wilson's Economist articles (1847, p. 574) contain four statements: (1) \"No doubt, however, such abundance of capital as is indicated by large stocks of commodities of all kinds, including bullion, would necessarily lead, not only to low prices of commodities in general, but also to a lower rate of interest for the use of capital\" (I). (2) \"If we have a stock of commodities on hand, which is sufficient to serve the country for two years to come, a command over those commodities would be obtained for a given period, at a much lower rate than if the stocks were barely sufficient to last us two months\" (2). (3) \"All loans of money, in whatever shape they are made, are simply a transfer of a command over commodities from one to another. Whenever, therefore, commodities are abundant, the interest of money must be low, and when they are scarce, the interest of money must be high\" (3). (4) \"As commodities become abundant, the number of sellers, in proportion to the number of buyers, increases, and, in proportion as the quantity is more than is required for immediate consumption, so must a larger portion be kept for future use. Under these circumstances, the terms on which a holder becomes willing to sell for a future payment, or on credit, become lower than if he were certain that his whole stock would be required within a few weeks\" (4). Marx's refutation proceeds point by point. On (1) : A large influx of precious metal can take place at the same time as a contraction in production, \"this being always the case in the period after a crisis.\" In the following phase, precious metal may flow in from countries where this is mainly produced; the import of other commodities is generally balanced in this period by exports. In both phases, \"the rate of interest is low and rises only slowly, for reasons we have already given. This low rate of interest can be explained in all cases without in any way bringing in 'large stocks of commodities of all kinds'.\" And Marx asks: \"how are these supposed to have their effect? The low price of cotton, for example, enables the spinner, etc. to make high profits. Why then is the interest rate low? Certainly not because the profit that can be made with borrowed capital is high. But purely and simply because, under the existing conditions, the demand for loan capital does not grow in relation to this profit; i.e. loan capital has a different movement from industrial capital. What The Economist is seeking to prove is precisely the opposite: that its movement is identical with the movement of industrial capital.\" The crucial theoretical assertion is in the last sentence: the movement of loanable money capital is different from the movement of industrial capital, and the entire argument of The Economist rests on the false premise that they are identical. The low rate of interest after a crisis is explained by a contraction in the demand for loan capital , not by an abundance of commodities. This is the dialectical point: the appearance of abundance (of commodities, of loan capital) may coexist with a paralysis of industrial production. On (2) : \"if we scale down the absurd assumption of stocks for two years in advance in order to give it some possible sense, this supposes that the commodity market is oversupplied. This would lead to a fall in prices. Less would have to be paid for a bale of cotton. But this in no way means that the money needed to purchase a bale of cotton is cheaper to come by. That depends on the state of the money market. If it is cheaper to come by, this is only because the commercial credit situation is such that it has less need to resort to bank credit than is usual.\" Marx then makes a series of interlocking arguments: the commodities that flood the market are means of subsistence or means of production; a low price for either increases the industrial capitalist's profit. \"How could a low price reduce interest if the abundance of industrial capital and the demand for monetary accommodation were not opposite phenomena instead of being identical?\" The point is that if commodity abundance raises profits, it should—if loan capital moved identically with industrial capital—raise the demand for loan capital and thus the interest rate. But the opposite is observed because the credit situation allows commercial credit to substitute for bank credit. This low interest rate therefore \"has nothing to do with the influx of precious metal, though the two may coexist.\" Marx also points out that if the import market really was flooded, this would mean a decline in the demand for imported goods, which would be inexplicable given the low prices, unless it were due to a contraction in domestic industrial production—\"but this would again be inexplicable, given the excess of imports at low prices. A mass of absurdities, in order to show that a fall in prices equals a fall in interest.\" He concludes: \"The two things may simultaneously occur alongside one another. But then they express a movement of industrial capital and loanable money capital in opposite directions, and not in the same direction.\" On (3) : Marx asks: \"Why ... the interest on money should be low when there is an abundance of commodities is not to be seen even with this further explanation. If commodities are cheap, I need, say, £1,000 to buy a certain quantity, instead of £2,000 as previously. But perhaps I still invest £2,000 and use this to buy double the amount of commodities I did before, expanding my business by advancing the same capital, which I might have to borrow. Now, as before, I spend £2,000. My demand on the money market thus remains the same, even though my demand on the commodity market rises with the fall in commodity prices.\" This is a beautiful reductio: if commodity prices fall, the same money capital can buy more commodities, but there is no reason for the demand for loan capital to fall—the same £2,000 may be borrowed, and if the business expands, the same demand persists. If the demand for loan capital does fall, this reflects a decision not to expand production, which contradicts The Economist 's own growth assumptions. Marx then enumerates the three possible causes of a low level of commodity prices: (a) lack of demand , in which case \"the rate of interest is low because production is paralysed, not because commodities are cheap, the cheapness simply being an expression of this paralysis\"; (b) oversupply relative to demand , which may lead to a crisis and may \"coincide during the crisis itself with a high rate of interest\"; (c) a fall in the value of commodities , i.e. the same demand can be satisfied at a lower price. Why should the rate of interest fall in this last case? \"Because profit grows? If it is because less money capital is needed to obtain the same productive or commodity capital, this simply proves that profit and interest stand in inverse proportion to one another.\" Marx then draws the general conclusion: \"In any case, The Economist's general thesis is wrong. Low money prices for commodities and a low rate of interest do not necessarily go together. If this were the case, the interest rate would be lowest in the poorest countries, where the money prices of products are lowest, and highest in the richest countries, where the money prices of agricultural products are highest.\" He then concedes The Economist 's point that a change in the value of money has no influence on the rate of interest: \"£100 still yields £105; if the £100 is worth less, so too is the £5. The ratio is not affected by a rise or fall in the value of the original sum. Considered as value, a certain quantity of commodities is equal to a certain sum of money. If its value rises, it is equal to a greater sum of money; if it falls, the converse is true. If it is £2,000, then 5 per cent is £100; if it is £1,000, 5 per cent is £50. But this in no way affects the interest rate. The element of truth in all this is simply that more monetary accommodation is required when £2,000 is needed to buy the same quantity of commodities than when only £1,000 is needed. All this shows, however, is that the ratio between profit and interest is an inverse one. For profit grows with the cheapening of the elements of constant and variable capital, while interest falls. However, the converse can also be the case, and frequently is so. Cotton may be cheap, for example, because there is no demand for yarn and cloth; it can be relatively dear because large profits in the cotton industry lead to a great demand for it. On the other hand, the industrialists' profits may be high precisely because the price of cotton is low.\" The reference to Hubbard's list (mentioned above, p. 684) is important: it \"shows that the interest rate and commodity prices exhibit completely independent movements; while the movements of the interest rate are precisely correlated with the movements of the metal reserve and the rate of exchange.\" This is an empirical result of great importance: it refutes The Economist 's thesis by inspection of the data. Marx then turns to a further refutation of The Economist 's general proposition: \"Whenever, therefore, commodities are abundant, the interest of money must be low.\"—\"Precisely the opposite is the case during crises; commodities are there in excess, not convertible into money, and the rate of interest is therefore high. In another phase of the cycle, there is a great demand for commodities and hence easy returns, but at the same time a rise in commodity prices, and a low rate of interest on account of these easy returns.\"—\"When they (commodities) are scarce, the interest of money must be high.\"—\"The opposite is again the case in the slack period following the crisis. Commodities are scarce in absolute terms, but not in relation to demand, and the interest rate is low.\" This is a systematic refutation via the phases of the cycle. In crisis, commodity abundance coincides with high interest; in the recovery, commodity scarcity coincides with low interest. The correlation that The Economist posits is empirically false. On (4) : \"it is quite obvious that when the market is flooded, an owner of commodities sells off his stock more cheaply—if he can sell it at all—than when there is a prospect of stocks becoming rapidly exhausted. It is less clear why the interest rate should fall on this account.\" Marx suggests two opposite effects: \"If the market is flooded with imported goods, the interest rate may rise as a result of a greater demand for loan capital on the part of the owners of these goods, who hope in this way to avoid having to put them on the market. It may fall, because the ease of commercial credit still keeps the demand for bank credit relatively low.\" Thus the effect is indeterminate, and The Economist 's proposition is empty. The Empirical Data on the 1847 Drain Marx then provides concrete data on The Economist 's claims about the rapid effect of the 1847 interest-rate increase on the exchange rate. He cautions that \"despite the turn in the exchange rates, gold continued to flow out until the end of April; the turning-point here only occurred at the beginning of May.\" The data table is as follows (all dates 1847): Date Bullion reserve (£) Money market Paris Hamburg Amsterdam 1 Jan 15,066,691 Bank disc. 3.5% 25.75 13.10 12.3 1/4 5 Mar 11,595,535 4% 25.66 1/2 13.9 1/2 12.2 1/2 20 Mar 11,231,630 Bank disc. 4% 25.67 1/2 13.9 1/2 12.2 1/2 3 April 10,246,410 5% 25.80 13.10 12.3 1/2 10 April 9,867,053 Money very scarce 25.90 13.10 1/2 12.4 1/2 17 April 9,329,841 Bank disc. 5.5% 26.02 1/2 13.10 1/2 12.5 1/2 24 April 9,213,890 Pressure 26.05 13.12 12.6 1 May 9,337,716 Increasing pressure 26.15 13.12 1/2 12.6 1/2 8 May 9,588,759 Highest pressure 26.27 1/2 13.15 1/2 12.7 1/2 The pattern is clear: the metal reserve drains from £15,066,691 on 1 January to a low of £9,213,890 on 24 April; the Bank discount rate rises from 3.5% to 5.5%; and the exchange rate on Paris rises from 25.75 to 26.27 1/2 (for Paris, higher numbers mean more favourable exchange, because it takes more francs to buy a pound sterling—i.e., sterling appreciates). The exchange rate turn begins in March-April, but the drain continues through April. The destinations of the 1847 total export of precious metal (£8,602,597) are given as: - United States: £3,226,411 - France: £2,479,892 - Hanse towns: £958,781 - Holland: £247,743 This breakdown is significant because it shows that the drain was not directed toward the countries with which England had an unfavourable commodity balance in the ordinary sense—much of it went to the United States, which was in England's debt, because the US was purchasing securities and inverting the flow. \"Despite the turn in the exchange rates at the end of March, the drain of gold lasted a whole month longer, its probable destination being the United States.\" This is a vivid illustration of point (2): the metallic movement is not simply the mirror of the trade balance. The Economist 's own commentary on this episode (1847, p. 954) is cited: \"We thus see how rapid and striking was the effect of a rise in the rate of interest, and the pressure which ensued in correcting an adverse exchange, and in turning the tide of bullion back to this country. This effect was produced entirely independent of the balance of trade. A higher rate of interest caused a lower price of securities, both foreign and English, and induced large purchases to be made on foreign account, which increased the amount of bills to be drawn from this country, while, on the other hand, the high rate of interest and the difficulty of obtaining money was such that the demand of those bills fell off, while their amount increased ... For the same cause orders for imports were countermanded, and investments of English funds abroad were realized and brought home for employment here. Thus, for example, we read in the Rio de Janeiro Price Current of the 10th May, 'Exchange (on England—F.E.) has experienced a further decline, principally caused by a pressure on the market for remittance of the proceeds of large sales of (Brazilian—F.E.) government stock, on English account.' Capital belonging to this country, which has been invested in public and other securities abroad, when the interest was very low here, was thus again brought back when the interest became high.\" This passage, cited from The Economist , actually provides the most lucid empirical vindication of Marx's thesis: (a) the effect on the exchange rate was produced \"entirely independent of the balance of trade\"; (b) it operated through the securities market —a high interest rate depressed securities prices, inducing foreign purchases and repatriating English investment; (c) the mechanism had a self-reinforcing dynamic in which the increased issuance of bills coincided with a falling demand for them. This is precisely the arbitrage mechanism Mill described, and it confirms the point that the exchange rate is determined by the movement of loanable capital , not by the commodity trade balance. England's Balance of Trade: The Tribute, Colonial Investment, and the Structural Asymmetry Marx now draws together the analysis of England's balance of trade. The structural situation is as follows: \"India alone has to pay £5 million in tribute for 'good government', interest and dividends on British capital, etc., and this does not include the sums sent home each year for investment in England, partly by officials as savings from their salaries and partly by merchants as a portion of their profits. The same remittances are constantly made from every British colony, for the same reasons.\" Most banks in Australia, the West Indies, and Canada were founded with British capital, the dividends being paid in England. England also possesses many bonds issued by foreign governments—European, North American, and South American—on which it receives interest. On top of this, there is participation in foreign railways, canals, mines, etc., with dividends accordingly. The crucial point: \"The remittances under all these heads are made almost exclusively in products over and above the total of English exports. Only an infinitesimal sum, on the other hand, goes abroad to the owners of English securities and for the consumption of English residents.\" So England's imports exceed its exports significantly, but this excess is not paid for by exports in the ordinary sense—it is the return on the tribute and foreign investment. The consequence for the balance of trade and exchange rates is \" 'at any particular moment one of time'.\" This temporal dimension is then explored via The Economist (11 January 1851): \"Practically speaking ... England gives long credits upon her exports, while the imports are paid for in ready money. At particular moments this difference of practice has a considerable effect upon the exchanges. At a time when our exports are very considerably increasing, e.g., 1850, a continual increase of investment of British capital must be going on ... in this way remittances of 1850 may be made against goods exported in 1849. But if the exports of 1850 exceed those of 1849 by more than 6 million, the practical effect must be that more money is sent abroad, to this amount, than returned in the same year. And in this way an effect is produced on the rates of exchange and the rate of interest. When, on the contrary, our trade is depressed after a commercial crisis, and when our exports are much reduced, the remittances due for the past years of larger exports greatly exceed the value of our imports; the exchanges become correspondingly in our favour, capital rapidly accumulates at home, and the rate of interest becomes less.\" This is a crucial passage for understanding the cyclical dynamics of the exchange rate. The asymmetry between long credit on exports and cash payment on imports produces a systematic lag: during expansion, England is sending out money ahead of the goods that will earn it, so the exchange tends to go against it and money flows out; after a crisis, the payments due from earlier exports come in faster than new imports, and the exchange turns in England's favour, capital accumulates, and the interest rate falls. This is one of the most important structural insights of the chapter, and it explains the recurring pattern of drain-before-crisis and influx-after-crisis. The Three Causes of Exchange Rate Alteration Marx then systematically enumerates the causes of changes in foreign exchange rates: (1) As a result of the temporary balance of payments , whatever the causes determining this; these may be purely commercial, or may involve capital investment abroad or state expenditures, as in war, etc.—\"in so far as cash payments are made abroad in this connection.\" (2) As the result of a devaluation of money in one country, either of metal money or of paper. This change is purely nominal: \"If £1 subsequently represented only half as much money as before, it would obviously be reckoned at 12.5 French francs instead of at 25.\" (3) When the rate of exchange is between countries, one of which uses silver as 'money', the other gold, it is dependent on the relative fluctuations in value of these two metals , since such fluctuations obviously alter the parity between the two. An example: in 1850, the exchange rate was unfavourable to England even though its exports rose enormously. There was still no drain of gold, for all that. This was the effect of the temporary rise in the value of silver as against gold (see The Economist , 30 November 1850). Exchange rate parities are then given: for £1, 25 francs 20 centimes in Paris, 13 banco marks 10½ schillings in Hamburg, 11 florins 97 cents in Amsterdam. \"In proportion as the exchange rate on Paris rises above 25.20, it becomes more favourable for the English debtor to France or for the purchaser of French commodities. In both cases less sterling is needed.\" This is a precise numerical definition of the exchange rate parity and its significance. In more distant countries, where precious metal is not so easy to come by, \"if bills of exchange are scarce and insufficient for the remittances to be made to England, the natural effect is to drive up the prices of those products that are customarily shipped to England, since a greater demand for these now arises, with the purpose of sending them to England instead of bills of exchange: this is often the case in India.\" This is a fascinating micro-mechanism: when bills are scarce, commodities themselves become the vehicle of remittance, and their prices rise. This is a specific and important phenomenon that links exchange-rate pressure to commodity-price movements in the periphery. Finally, Marx notes that \"an unfavourable rate of exchange, and even a drain of gold, may arise in England if there is a very great surplus in money, a low rate of interest and a high price for securities.\" This is a paradoxical-sounding but theoretically important point: an abundance of money and low interest can produce a drain, because English securities become attractive to foreigners (who sell their own securities to buy English ones) and English investors seek higher returns abroad. This is precisely the situation described by The Economist in reverse: the high-price/low-interest configuration of the English market makes it a profitable place to sell securities, and the proceeds are remitted abroad. The 1848–1850 Silver Flows: Crisis, Revolution, and Restoration The chapter concludes with a concrete illustration of the interaction between metallic flows and political events. \"In the course of 1848, England received large quantities of silver from India, since good bills were scarce and mediocre ones not readily accepted as a result of the 1847 crisis and the great lack of credit in trade with India. This silver had scarcely arrived before it all found its way to the Continent, where the revolution led to hoarding on all sides. The same silver largely flowed back to India in 1850, since the exchange rate now made this profitable.\" This is a beautiful miniature of the chapter's whole argument. Three movements of the same silver, driven by three distinct causes: - 1848 influx from India : driven by the lack of credit and the scarcity of good bills—so that commodities (silver) were remitted instead of bills (the exact mechanism described in the previous paragraph about India). - 1848 outflow to the Continent : driven by the political revolution, which produced hoarding on all sides—a purely social/political cause, not a trade cause. - 1850 back to India : driven by the exchange rate—i.e., the price configuration that made this direction profitable. The same silver, moving in three directions in three years, driven by credit conditions, political events, and arbitrage. This is the empirical vindication of point (2): the metal flow is not a simple reflex of the trade balance but a complex social phenomenon governed by manifold causes. The Final Metaphor: Catholic Money and Protestant Credit The chapter ends with a single, aphoristic paragraph—one of the most famous in Capital : \"The monetary system is essentially Catholic, the credit system essentially Protestant. 'The Scotch hate gold.' As paper, the monetary existence of commodities has a purely social existence. It is faith that brings salvation. Faith in money value as the immanent spirit of commodities, faith in the mode of production and its predestined disposition, faith in the individual agents of production as mere personifications of self-valorizing capital. But the credit system is no more emancipated from the monetary system as its basis than Protestantism is from the foundations of Catholicism.\" This passage condenses the entire chapter's argument into a theological metaphor, and it is worth unpacking carefully. The monetary system is \"Catholic\" because it is based on the real presence —gold and silver, tangible, incarnate value. The credit system is \"Protestant\" because it is based on faith —the \"purely social existence\" of commodities as paper, a system of mutual trust in the convertibility of value claims. \"The Scotch hate gold\" (a reference to the Scottish banking system's famous reluctance to use gold coin, and to the theological-Protestant associations of Scotland) captures the credit system's aspiration to dispense with the metal basis entirely. \"It is faith that brings salvation\"—the credit system rests on confidence in the social character of production, in the mode of production's \"predestined disposition\" (a Calvinist resonance: the elect, predestined to salvation), in the individual agents of production as \"mere personifications of self-valorizing capital\" (a phrase that echoes Marx's theory of the capitalist as the personification of capital). The entire credit system is, in this reading, a theological structure—a system of belief in a transcendent order of value. But the final sentence reverses the theological analogy to its dialectical conclusion: \"the credit system is no more emancipated from the monetary system as its basis than Protestantism is from the foundations of Catholicism.\" Protestantism, in this dialectical-theological reading, did not do away with the Catholic substance; it preserved and transformed it. Similarly, credit has not abolished money; it has preserved and intensified its power. Every crisis is the return of the repressed —the moment when the \"Protestant\" credit system collapses back into \"Catholic\" money-worship, when faith fails and the faithful demand the actual presence of gold. The \"crazy demand\" of the crisis for actual money is the Counter-Reformation of finance: a reassertion of the real presence against the intellectualizing, paper-based, faith-based system of credit. And in this collapse, the fundamental dependence of the credit system on the monetary system—the dependence of the superstructure on its material basis—is revealed. Thus the chapter closes with a perfectly crafted synthesis: philosophical, historical, and economic. The metallic basis is not abolished by credit; it is revealed in every crisis to be the foundation on which credit rests. The \"absurd contradiction and paradox\" of capitalist production—that social wealth must take the form of a thing—is expressed in the endless oscillation between the \"Catholic\" moment of hard money and the \"Protestant\" moment of credit faith. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS: Chapter 35 yields a series of fundamental principles that reach far beyond the specific institutional details of the Bank of England in the 1840s–1850s and continue to inform the analysis of monetary and financial crises today. First, the irreducibility of the monetary form. The central theoretical result of the chapter is that the form of wealth—money, credit, commodity capital—is not accidental but structurally determinate of economic dynamics. A drain of £5–8 million can produce a general crisis, while a much larger movement of physical capital may pass unnoticed, because precious metal is \"directly loanable money capital and the basis of the entire money system.\" The specific character of precious metal as capital in the money form is what gives a small metallic movement disproportionate effect. This result refutes simultaneously the monetary reductionism (money as merely a veil) and the physicalist reductionism (the drain as simply a loss of real capital) that Marx identifies as the twin errors of \"enlightened economics.\" Second, the structural over-sensitization of the credit system. The credit system, in pressing every idle balance into service and reducing the metal reserve to a minimum, systematically increases the economy's vulnerability. The result is a structural characteristic of developed capitalism: crises are not proportional to the physical magnitudes involved but are functions of the reserve ratio and the phase of the industrial cycle. This is a profound insight. It explains why modern financial crises can be triggered by remarkably small events, and why the crisis potential increases with financial development. It also implies that the project of eliminating crisis by perfecting the credit system is self-defeating: the more perfected the credit system, the more over-sensitive the organism. Third, the disjunction between loanable money capital and industrial capital. Perhaps the most rigorous analytical result of the chapter is the demonstration that the movement of loan capital and the movement of industrial capital can be opposite , not identical. The low interest rates after a crisis occur not because of abundance but because of the contraction of production; the low interest rates of the recovery occur because easy returns and high commercial credit reduce the demand for bank credit. In both cases, the demand for loan capital—not the abundance of commodities—is the determinant. Wilson's Economist thesis, that commodity abundance causes low interest rates, is refuted by the cycles themselves. The consequence: there is no simple, mechanical relation between commodity prices and the interest rate; the rate of interest has an autonomous dynamic governed by the money market. Fourth, the non-identity of exchange rate and trade balance movements. The chapter empirically demonstrates that the exchange rate and the metallic movements are governed by the supply and demand of bills, which is governed by the pattern of payments—including capital investment abroad, tribute, war expenditure, and securities arbitrage—not by the commodity trade balance. The 1847 drain, in which £8.6 million flowed out while trade balance was not the determinant, and much of it went to the United States, is the paradigmatic case. The consequence: a theory of the exchange rate grounded in the commodity trade balance is not merely incomplete but systematically misleading. The exchange rate is a monetary and credit phenomenon, and it must be analysed as such. Fifth, the tribute and the structural asymmetry of the world market. Marx's analysis of the \"good government\" tribute, of colonial investment, and of the asymmetry between long credit on exports and cash payment on imports, lays the foundations for a theory of the world market as a structured hierarchy. England sits at the centre, extracting tribute from India and the colonies, financing foreign investment, and clearing the triangular trade. The exchange rate with the periphery is determined by these structural flows. This anticipates the later analysis of the world market and the colonies, and the theory of uneven development. Sixth, the recurrent pattern of the trade cycle. The chapter's evidence establishes a stylized cycle: metal drain → tightening of credit → rise in the interest rate → turn of the exchange rate → collapse → drain ceases → influx of metal → low interest rates → expansion → renewed drain. This is a cycle in which metallic movements, interest rates, credit conditions, and commodity conditions each have their own dynamics, correlated with each other but not reducible to each other. The crisis itself comes after the drain has turned, not because of the drain but out of the accumulated strains of the expansion. The drain is a \"precursor,\" an \"advance warning.\" Seventh, the dialectic of credit and money. The final metaphor—Catholic money, Protestant credit—crystallizes the entire chapter into a philosophical thesis. The credit system is a social form based on trust in the social character of production; it displaces money and \"usurps its position.\" But it cannot dispense with money as its basis. Every crisis reveals this dependence, when the \"faith\" collapses and all holders of value claims demand conversion into metal. The panic demand—that all bills, securities, and commodities be simultaneously convertible into money, and money into gold—is a \"crazy demand,\" but it is one that \"necessarily grows out of the system itself.\" The metallic barrier is \"both a material and an imaginary barrier\": material, because the quantity of gold is finite; imaginary, because the demand for it in crisis is an ideological-reified demand, an expression of the reified social form of wealth under capitalism. Broader philosophical implications. The chapter demonstrates with extraordinary concreteness the Marxian thesis that the social relations of capitalist production are reified —that is, they appear as relations between things, and acquire autonomous power over the humans whose relations they embody. The credit system, which ought to be a rational arrangement for allocating social resources, becomes in crisis a blind, coercive force: \"every man sending money abroad must do it at his own peril,\" and the social wealth of a nation is held hostage by the contents of a bank vault. \"A drain of gold, therefore, shows strikingly by its effects that production is not really subjected to social control, as social production, and that the social form of wealth exists alongside wealth itself as a thing.\" This is the fundamental indictment of the capitalist mode of production: the collective wealth of society, which is the product of collective labour, is governed by a reified social form, and the whole apparatus of credit—the most developed instrument of social control over production—collapses periodically into a primitive struggle for precious metal hoards. Broader theoretical implications. The chapter's method is also its lesson. Marx does not begin with abstract definitions and then apply them; he interrogates a concrete historical episode (the Bank Acts Committee testimony) and derives theory from it. The witnesses—Tooke, Newmarch, Morris, Palmer, Hubbard, Mill, Weguelin, Wilson, Wood, Chapman—are not mere sources of facts but interlocutors in a theoretical debate. Marx's own voice appears as commentary, critique, and editorial framing (in the F.E. parentheses). The result is a dialectical reconstruction in which the bourgeois economic categories are shown to be internally contradictory: the quantity theory of money contradicts the note-issuing practice; the Currency School's doctrine contradicts the crises; Wilson's theory of capital contradicts the data; Newmarch's testimony contradicts itself; The Economist 's correlation contradicts the cycles. The dialectic of capitalist economy, in this chapter, is not an abstract formula but a working method: the contradictions are not in the data but in the system itself. Practical implications for the analysis of financial crises. The chapter provides a durable framework: crises unfold through the interaction of metallic drains, credit tightening, interest-rate rises, exchange-rate movements, and the collapse of confidence, with each element having its own partially autonomous dynamic. The trigger may be small—\"the feather\"—and the crisis may come after the immediate pressure has eased. Policy interventions that target the visible symptoms (the drain) while ignoring the underlying structural over-sensitization will fail. The Bank Acts' mechanism of automatic contraction, by intensifying the metallic drain's effect, made the panics worse. The alternative—the Government Letter suspending the Act—was a recognition that the credit system cannot be managed purely mechanically. The deeper lesson, which Marx draws with his habitual irony, is that a system whose fundamental social form (value) is reified cannot be exempted from periodic collapse by any institutional reform, because the reform can address only the form of the collapse, not its source in the social relations themselves. The search for a \"natural and complete solvent\"—the arriving gold ship—is a permanent feature of a system that cannot, by its own nature, subject production to conscious social control. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 34 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 36"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 36", "title": "Chapter 36", "subtitle": "Pre-Capitalist Relations", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 36/Chapter 36 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 13297, "read_time": "66 min read", "tags": ["Interest-Bearing Capital & Loan Capital", "Historical Specificity of Capital"], "keywords": ["Pre-Capitalist Relations and Usury", "Usury's Ruinous Effect on Peasant Economy and Feudal Lords", "Revolutionary Subordination of Usury to Modern Banking", "Usurer Capital as Dissolving Agent of Ancient Social Formations", "Saint-Simonian Credit Utopianism and Crédit Mobilier"], "text": "Chapter 36 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 13,292 words ⏱️ ~66 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 36: Pre-Capitalist Relations — An Exhaustive Analysis 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS: Chapter 36 of Capital, Volume III constitutes Marx's most sustained historical-materialist investigation of what he calls the \"antediluvian forms of capital\": merchant's capital and its twin, usurer's capital (interest-bearing capital in its archaic guise). The chapter's immediate position within the broader architecture of Volume III is significant. Having just established, in the immediately preceding chapters, that industrial capital is the form of capital par excellence — the form from which profit of enterprise, interest, and ground-rent are all ultimately differentiated as tributary portions of a single surplus-value — Marx here steps back to examine the historical genesis and persistence of interest-bearing capital in the modes of production that precede capitalism and that also survive within and alongside capitalism as subordinate, parasitic residuals. The central thesis can be stated precisely: usurer's capital is a mode of exploitation without a corresponding mode of production. It is not a transitional form that itself produces capitalism but rather a dissolving solvent that, under specific historical conditions, may help to decompose pre-capitalist relations and thereby help to clear the ground for a capitalist mode of production — yet which, absent those other conditions, merely impoverishes, cripples, and perpetuates backwardness. The chapter thus provides a decisive historical counterweight to a persistent error, both popular and semi-theoretical, namely the tendency to treat interest-bearing capital as the fundamental or \"purest\" form of capital as such. The intellectual objective the author pursues is threefold. First, Marx wants to explain why interest-bearing capital appears historically prior to industrial capital yet cannot be its determining form; he must show that this priority in time is not a priority in conceptual or logical determination. Second, he must distinguish sharply between the interest-bearing capital that is an organic element of the capitalist mode of production — the loanable capital that presupposes and rests upon wage-labour, the expropriation of the producer from the conditions of labour, and the equalization of the general rate of profit — and the usurer's capital that operates on entirely different presuppositions: the continued ownership (real or nominal) of their conditions of labour by small producers, and the consumption-borrowing of luxurious landed proprietors. Third, the chapter serves as the historical foundation for the subsequent analysis of credit, since the modern credit system is defined, from its origins, as a reaction against usury — the subordination of interest-bearing capital to the requirements of the capitalist mode of production. How does this chapter fit into the broader subject or theoretical framework? Capital, Volume III as a whole is organized around the \"trinity formula\" — the division of surplus-value into the three great revenue forms: profit of enterprise (which in turn splits into the industrial profit and interest), ground-rent, and wages. Chapter 36 confronts the most primitive and historically earliest of these forms — interest — and asks what makes it capital at all, and what distinguishes its pre-capitalist career from its capitalist career. In doing so, the chapter also prepares the ground for Volume III's deeper analysis of the credit system as the \"most artificial and elaborate product brought into existence by the capitalist mode of production,\" a system that simultaneously socializes and privately monopolizes the means of production, and that Marx identifies as one of the most powerful levers for driving capitalist production beyond its own barriers — and thus, inherently, as a form pregnant with the abolition of capital itself. The chapter thus sits at a crucial hinge: between the abstract theory of the division of profit and the concrete historical and institutional analysis of credit; between pre-capitalist economic formations and the mature capitalist world market; and between the critique of political economy and the critique of the socialist (Saint-Simonian, Proudhonian) fantasies about credit. Finally, a word on the author's method, which the reader must grasp to follow the argument. Marx proceeds dialectically and historically at once. He insists that the same forms — usury during the Republic of Rome, usury in the twelfth-century Rhineland, usury in Polish bill-broking of the eighteenth century, the usury of the English pawnshop in the nineteenth — recur both in pre-capitalist formations and within the developed capitalist system, but that their character is entirely determined by the social conditions under which they function and by the class position of the borrower who confronts the money-lender. The chapter therefore continually warns against comparing forms that share a name but differ in determining content. It is this methodological imperative — never confuse a form with its function, a temporal priority with a logical primacy, or a quantitative rate with the social relations that establish its meaning — that organizes every section. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS: §1 The Antediluvian Twins: Usurer's Capital and Merchant's Capital as Pre-Capitalist Forms Marx opens the chapter with a definition of the form under examination: \"Interest-bearing capital, or, to describe it in its archaic form, usurer's capital, belongs together with its twin brother, merchant's capital, to the antediluvian forms of capital.\" The word \"antediluvian\" — pre-Flood, in the biblical idiom — signals that these are forms existing before the deluge that reshaped the world into a system of wage-labour and industrial profit. Both forms \"long precede the capitalist mode of production and are to be found in the most diverse socio-economic formations.\" The essential point is that these forms do not require capitalism; they require only two minimal conditions. First, \"at least a portion of the products is transformed into commodities\" — that is, some products must become exchange-values, not merely use-values. Second, \"money in its various functions develops concurrently with trade in commodities\" — money must exist and perform its roles (measure of value, means of purchase, means of payment, hoard) as an accompaniment to commodity exchange. Nothing more is strictly required. In particular, capital as a relation of production (wage-labour exploited for surplus-value, with the producers divorced from their conditions of labour) is not required. This is the note that will sound through the entire chapter. A crucial analytical distinction is introduced here between two kinds of lending, distinguishable by the purpose and social position of the borrower. \"The merchant borrows money to make a profit with it, to use it as capital, i.e. to lay it out.\" This is the productive borrower who borrows to valorize — a borrower whose relation to the money-lender, Marx says, is \"precisely the same way as\" in relation to the \"modern capitalist.\" This specific relationship — the lending of money to a borrower who will use it as capital to make a profit — was even recognized by the Catholic universities. Marx quotes Augier's Le Crédit public : the universities of Alcalá, Salamanca, Ingolstadt, Freiburg im Breisgau, Mainz, Cologne and Trier \"successively acknowledged the legality of interest on commercial loans,\" with the first five approvals deposited in the consulate archives of Lyons. The point is that even the Church's own intellectual institutions had to admit that productive, commercial borrowing occupies a different moral and economic category from consumption-lending to the distressed — anticipating an argument that will be developed later in the chapter. Marx grounds the very possibility of interest in the social relations of production rather than in any intrinsic property of money. He gives the case of the slave economy of the later Greco-Roman era, \"not patriarchal slavery, but rather that of the later phases.\" Where slave economy exists \"as a means of enrichment,\" where money is the means of appropriating other people's labour by purchase of slaves, land, etc., then \"money can be valorized as capital and comes to bear interest precisely because it can be invested in this way.\" Interest is thus not a property of money but a consequence of the productive relations in which money can be deployed . This is a decisive methodological claim, and it recurs throughout the chapter as the solvent of all the superficial analogies between ancient and modern rates of interest. §2 The Two Characteristic Forms of Usurer's Capital Marx then isolates two forms whose longevity and theoretical importance justify the word \"characteristic\": \"firstly, usury by lending money to extravagant magnates, essentially to landed proprietors; secondly, usury by lending money to small producers who possess their own conditions of labour, including artisans, but particularly and especially peasants, since, wherever pre-capitalist conditions permit small autonomous individual producers, the peasant class must form their great majority.\" Both forms, Marx says, \"lead to the formation and concentration of large money capitals\" — but this process has completely different consequences from the process by which capitalism concentrates capital. Indeed, \"the extent to which this process abolishes the old mode of production, as was the case in modern Europe, and whether it establishes the capitalist mode of production in its place, depends entirely on the historical level of development and the conditions that this provides.\" This is the decisive caveat: usury is a necessary but by no means sufficient condition of the formation of capitalism. Its effects are contingent on the presence or absence of other conditions. The reason usurer's capital is the characteristic form corresponding to petty production is developed dialectically. \"Usurer's capital, as the characteristic form of interest-bearing capital, corresponds to the predominance of petty production, of peasants and small master craftsmen working for themselves.\" Under developed capitalism, by contrast, \"the conditions of production and the product of labour confront the worker as capital,\" so the worker \"does not have to borrow any money in his capacity as a producer.\" When he does borrow, it is \"for personal necessity, as at the pawnshop.\" But when the worker is \"the proprietor of his conditions of labour and his product, in reality or in name, then it is as a producer that he relates to the money-lender's capital, which confronts him as usurer's capital.\" The form of the loan follows the form of the producer. Marx then turns to a criticism of F. W. Newman's Lectures on Political Economy (London, 1851), which claimed that the banker is respected and the usurer hated because the former lends to the rich and the latter to the poor. Marx calls this \"rather inane.\" The real distinction is not rich borrower versus poor borrower but \"a difference between two social modes of production and the social arrangements corresponding to them.\" The apparent paradox is that usury's exploitation of the poor is inseparable from usury's ruin of the rich: \"the usury that impoverishes the poor petty producer goes hand in hand with the usury that ruins the rich landed proprietor.\" Both processes concentrate monetary wealth, and both decompose the existing mode of production. The historical illustration is Rome: \"As soon as the usury of the Roman patricians had completely ruined the Roman plebeians, the small farmers, this form of exploitation came to an end, and the petty-bourgeois economy was replaced by a pure slave economy.\" The dialectic of the chapter is precisely that usury, in ruining the small producers, may also ruin the conditions of its own continuation and be replaced by another, more brutal form of exploitation. §3 The Level of Pre-Capitalist Interest and the Absurdity of Direct Comparison This leads to one of the chapter's most important quantitative arguments. In the form of interest, \"the usurer can in this case swallow up everything in excess of the producers' most essential means of subsistence, the amount that later becomes wages (the usurer's interest being the part that later appears as profit and ground-rent).\" That is to say, in petty production the usurer's interest absorbs the entire surplus-value — everything above bare subsistence. It is therefore \"quite absurd to compare the level of this interest ... with the level of the modern interest rate, where interest, at least the normal interest, forms only one part of this surplus-value.\" The modern wage-labourer produces and yields to the capitalist \"profit, interest and ground-rent, in short the entire surplus-value,\" of which the interest paid to a money-lender is only one deducible portion. In pre-capitalist usury, the usurer alone appropriates the whole surplus. Comparing rates across such radically different distributions is a category mistake. Marx here attacks Carey, who \"makes this absurd comparison in order to show the great advantage for the worker of the development of capital and the accompanying fall in the interest rate.\" This is the apologetic use of a quantitative false equivalence — a sleight of hand that treats a lower interest rate as evidence of the worker's benefit, when in fact the fall is only possible because surplus-value has been diffused across profit, interest, and rent, and appropriated by capital in the process of production, leaving the worker with wages. The chapter's critical force is very much aimed at such apologetics. Marx then examines the outcome when usury goes beyond extracting surplus labour and begins to extract the conditions of labour themselves. \"If the usurer, not content with extracting his victim's surplus labour, gradually obtains the ownership title to his conditions of labour themselves — land, house, etc. — and consistently sets out to expropriate him in this way, it should still not be forgotten that this complete expropriation of the worker from his conditions of labour is not a result towards which the capitalist mode of production tends, but rather the given presupposition from which it proceeds.\" Here we encounter one of the most theoretically precise propositions in the chapter: capitalist production begins from the expropriation of the producer; it does not produce it as a tendency internal to itself. The expropriation is the historical precondition , not the result. Consequently, \"the wage-slave is just as much excluded by his position as the slave proper from being a debt slave, at least in his capacity as producer; if he can become so at all, it is in his capacity as consumer.\" This clarifies the earlier point about the pawnshop: the modern worker may be caught by usurious consumer credit, but he cannot be caught by usurious producer credit, because he produces nothing of his own to be stripped from him. The mode of production governed by usurer's capital of the second type, where the producer remains the owner of his conditions of labour, is characterized precisely: \"Usurer's capital, in this form where it actually appropriates all the surplus labour of the direct producer, without altering the mode of production; where the producers' ownership or possession of their conditions of labour (and the isolated petty production corresponding to this) is an essential precondition; where capital therefore does not directly subordinate labour, and thus does not confront it as industrial capital — this usurer's capital impoverishes the mode of production, cripples the productive forces instead of developing them, and simultaneously perpetuates these lamentable conditions in which the social productivity of labour is not developed even at the cost of the worker himself, as it is in capitalist production.\" The key contrast is between developing the productive forces (as capitalist accumulation does, even at the worker's expense) and crippling them (as usury does, without compensating development). §4 Usury's Double Effect on Pre-Capitalist Property Marx now states the double movement of usury clearly: \"Usury thus works on the one hand to undermine and destroy ancient and feudal wealth, and ancient and feudal property. On the other hand it undermines and ruins small peasant and petty-bourgeois production, in short all forms in which the producer still appears as the owner of his means of production.\" These are not two separate processes but two sides of the same solvent. But — and here is the crucial distinction — in the developed capitalist mode of production, \"the worker is not the owner of his conditions of production,\" and \"this alienation of the conditions of production from the producer ... corresponds here to a real revolution in the mode of production itself.\" That revolution consists in the bringing together of isolated workers into the large workshop, in specialized and interlocking activity, in the replacement of the tool by the machine. \"The mode of production itself no longer permits the fragmentation of the instruments of production that is linked with petty property, any more than it permits the isolation of the workers themselves.\" Once this revolution has occurred, \"usury can no longer divorce the conditions of production from the producer, since they are already divorced.\" This gives us one of the chapter's most famous and vivid images: \"Where the means of production are fragmented, usury centralizes monetary wealth. It does not change the mode of production, but clings on to it like a parasite and impoverishes it. It sucks it dry, emasculates it and forces reproduction to proceed under ever more pitiable conditions.\" The parasitical relation is here fully theorized: usury is not a developmental agency but a consuming one. The reason for the popular hatred of usury — most intense in the ancient world — is now explained on material grounds: \"the producer's ownership of his conditions of production was at the same time the basis for political relations, for the independence of the citizen.\" Usury attacks not merely an economic arrangement but the material foundation of political citizenship; hence the political intensity of ancient hostility to usury. The consequences of usury for the exploiting class are then traced. \"As long as slavery prevails, or the surplus product is consumed by the feudal lord and his retinue, the mode of production still remains the same even though slaveowner or feudal lord fall prey to usury; it simply becomes harsher for the workers.\" Here we encounter the paradox that when the exploiting class is itself squeezed by usury, it simply intensifies its extraction — \"The indebted slaveowner or feudal lord takes more out of them, since more is taken from him.\" Ultimately, the exploiting class may be displaced: \"he may be completely replaced by the usurer, who himself becomes a landowner or slaveowner as the knights did in ancient Rome. In place of the old exploiter, whose exploitation was more or less patriarchal, since it was largely a means of political power, we have a hard, money-grubbing upstart. But the mode of production itself remains unaltered.\" This is an important formulation of a principle that runs through the chapter: the personnel of the exploiting class may change without a change in the form of exploitation . §5 The Historical Conditions of Usury's Revolutionary Effect Marx now formulates with precision the conditions under which usury actually plays a revolutionary role: \"Usury has a revolutionary effect on pre-capitalist modes of production only in so far as it destroys and dissolves the forms of ownership which provide a firm basis for the articulation of political life and whose constant reproduction in the same form is a necessity for that life.\" Note the word \"revolutionary\" is used in a strictly negative and dissolving sense. Usury dissolves forms; it does not constitute new ones. The Asiatic forms illustrate the limited negative power: \"In Asiatic forms, usury can persist for a long while without leading to anything more than economic decay and political corruption.\" Here usury is stagnant — it dissolves without generating. Only where \"the other conditions for the capitalist mode of production are present\" does usury appear \"as one of the means of formation of this new mode of production, by ruining the feudal lords and petty production on the one hand, and by centralizing the conditions of labour on the other.\" Even then, it is only one of the means. This is the chapter's most important historical-theoretical claim about causality: usury is a catalyst of already-potentially-present tendencies, not their originating cause. Marx's rigor here guards against any reading of the history of capitalism as the simple outcome of usury's destructive work. §6 Medieval Interest Rates and the Absence of a General Rate Marx now pivots to the historical record of medieval usury. \"In the Middle Ages, there was no generally prevailing interest rate in any country. The Church prohibited all interest dealings from the start. Laws and courts gave little security for loans. All the higher was the interest rate in particular cases.\" The explanation is grounded in the rudimentary state of circulation: \"The low monetary circulation and the need to make most payments in cash compelled people to borrow money, and all the more so, the more undeveloped the system of bills of exchange.\" The undeveloped credit instrument raises rather than lowers the cost of borrowing relative to a developed system. \"There was great variation in both the rate of interest and the concept of usury.\" Concrete illustrative data are given. \"In Charlemagne's time, it was considered usurious to take 100 per cent interest. At Lindau am Bodensee in 1344, some local burghers took 216! per cent.\" (The exclamation mark and the particular figure are worth noting: the very notion of a \"rate\" becomes elastic when enforcement is not only weak but conceptually contested.) \"In Zurich the town council fixed 43½ per cent as the legal interest. In Italy, 40 per cent had to be paid on occasion, though from the twelfth century to the fourteenth the rate did not usually exceed 20 per cent. Verona settled on 12½ per cent as the legal interest. Emperor Frederick II fixed 10 per cent, but this was only for the Jews. He would not decree for Christians. 10 per cent was already common in the German Rhineland in the thirteenth century.\" The source cited is Hüllmann's Geschichte des Städtewesens , II, pp. 55–7. These are not decorative numbers. They establish the enormous dispersion of interest rates in a system without a unified money market, and they illustrate Marx's earlier point that without the equalization of profit across branches of production, there can be no general rate of interest. §7 Usurer's Capital: Mode of Exploitation Without Mode of Production The next formulation is a compressed dialectical thesis: \"Usurer's capital has capital's mode of exploitation without its mode of production.\" This is the definition of all pre-capitalist and residual usury. \"This relationship also recurs within the bourgeois economy in backward branches of industry, or those that are struggling against the transition to the modern mode of production.\" The methodological consequence is immediate and characteristic: \"In comparing the English rate of interest with the Indian, for example, we must not take the Bank of England's interest rate but rather that charged, for instance, by people lending small machines to petty producers in domestic industry.\" Here Marx warns against the very comparison (English versus Indian rates) that Adam Smith and later apologists made to argue for the civilizing effect of British colonialism in India. The proper comparison is between like functions , not like names . This is the methodological imperative announced in the introduction and now executing. §8 Usury as an Instrument of Monetary-Wealth Formation Marx now identifies usury's historical importance in terms of its relation to monetary wealth. \"Usury is historically important, in contrast to wealth devoted entirely to consumption, as being itself a process giving rise to capital.\" The formula \"giving rise to capital\" is carefully qualified. It does not mean that usury creates capital as a relation of production; it means that usury accumulates monetary wealth independent of landed property: \"Usurer's capital and mercantile wealth bring about the formation of a monetary wealth independent of landed property.\" The argument now turns on the character of money in undeveloped systems. \"The less developed is the character of the product as a commodity, the less exchange-value has taken command of production in its whole breadth and depth, the more does money appear as wealth as such, wealth proper, wealth in general, as against its restricted form of appearance in use-values. Hoard formation depends on this.\" Here we have a dialectic of wealth and its forms. In a system where most products remain use-values rather than commodities, money appears not as one commodity among others but as the commodity — as the universal equivalent standing in glittering isolation. Hoarding, the accumulation of money as such, is grounded in this. Marx then specifies the functions of money that are central to the development of interest. \"Leaving aside money as world money and as hoard, it is particularly in the form of means of payment that it emerges as the absolute form of the commodity.\" This is a precise theoretical claim: the function of means of payment — the function in which money is transferred after the transfer of goods, to settle a debt — is the function that cultivates the soil for interest. \"And it is particularly its function as means of payment that develops interest and with it money capital. What wealth for extravagance and corruption wants is money as money, money as a means to buy everything. (Also for paying debts.) What the petty producer needs money for above all is for payment. (The transformation of services in kind and deliveries to landlords and the state into money rents and money taxes plays a major role here.) In both cases money is needed as money.\" So long as the producer can satisfy his obligations in kind, he is not drawn into the money relation; but with the spread of money rents and money taxes, he must find money on a fixed date. It is then that usury can seize him. This historical mechanism is essential to Marx's overall story of the genesis of capitalism from the transformation of the form of rent. And here is the crucial theoretical point about usury's function within hoard formation: \"it is only in usury that hoard formation becomes a reality for the first time and fulfils its dreams. What is sought from the hoard owner is not capital but rather money as money; but through interest he transforms this money hoard, as it is in itself, into capital — into a means by which he takes partial or complete command of surplus labour, and in this way of a portion of the conditions of production themselves, even if these nominally still confront him as someone else's property.\" This is why usurer's capital is the \"form of capital par excellence\" to the popular mind, as Marx will later say — because it converts a passive hoard into a claim on future surplus labour without any apparent productive activity in between. The famous image follows: \"Usury seems to live in the pores of production, like the gods in Epicurus's intermundia.\" (The reference is to Epicurus's conception, in the intermundia or spaces between the material worlds of the cosmos, of the gods living apart from the world's affairs; Marx borrowed the image from Lucretius and uses it here to figure usury's peculiar position — present in all the gaps of production, but never organizing the productive process itself.) \"It is all the more difficult to get money, the less the commodity form has become the general form of the product. The usurer therefore does not come up against any barrier except the incapacity of those in need of money to pay or their capacity to resist.\" The barrier to usury is not competition among lenders (which requires developed credit) but merely the physical limits of the borrower's capacity to pay or to resist. §9 The Specific Terrain: Money as Means of Payment Marx proceeds to isolate the specific terrain on which usury sits. \"The proper, principal and specific terrain of usury is still the function of money as means of payment. Any monetary obligation — rent, interest, tribute, tax, etc. — that falls due at a certain date brings with it the need for a money payment.\" This is why in Roman and early modern Europe, usurers were so often tax farmers: \"This is why usury so generally attaches to tax farmers, fermiers généraux , receveurs généraux , from ancient Rome through to modern times. With trade and the generalization of commodity production, purchase and payment become separate in time. Money has to be provided at a particular date.\" The implication is that even today \"money capitalist and usurer merge into one\" in modern money crises — moments when the temporal separation of purchase and payment becomes acute. The chapter then gives a dialectical observation of considerable sophistication: \"This very usury, however, becomes a major means of extending the need for money as means of payment, since it drags the producer deeper and deeper into debt and destroys his customary means of payment in that the interest burden itself makes his regular reproduction impossible. Here usury springs from money as means of payment, and broadens this function of money, its most specific terrain.\" Usury thus has a self-deepening character: it arises from the function of money as means of payment, and in the process expands and intensifies that very function. This is why, in a system where money has not yet become the general form of the product, the usurer faces no functional barrier to his expansion. §10 The Credit System as Reaction Against Usury Marx now makes the chapter's most important conceptual link to the subsequent analysis of credit: \"The credit system develops as a reaction against usury.\" But he immediately qualifies: \"But this should not be misconstrued, nor by any means taken in the sense of the ancient writers, the Fathers of the Church, Luther or the early socialists. It means neither more nor less than the subordination of interest-bearing capital to the conditions and requirements of the capitalist mode of production.\" The verb \"subordination\" is precise. The credit system does not abolish interest-bearing capital; it disciplines it. In the modern credit system, interest-bearing capital \"becomes adapted on the whole to the conditions of capitalist production.\" Usury proper, however, \"not only continues to exist, but in countries of developed capitalist production it is freed from the barriers that former legislation had always placed to it.\" So the modern credit system does not eliminate usury; it unshackles one form of it, while subordinating the other. Marx lists the cases where borrowing retains the form of usurer's capital even under capitalism: \"(1) where borrowing in the sense appropriate to the capitalist mode of production does not and cannot occur; (2) where borrowing results from individual need, as at the pawnshop; (3) where borrowing is for extravagant consumption; (4) where the producer is a non-capitalist producer, a small peasant, artisan, etc., i.e. is still the possessor of his own conditions of production as a direct producer; (5) finally where the capitalist producer himself operates on so small a scale that his situation approaches that of those producers who work for themselves.\" This is an important recognition that the categories of usury and modern interest are not historically exclusive but coexist as different forms of a functionally identical capital, distinguished by the social conditions of their operation. §11 The Form versus Function Distinction Marx now clarifies what does not distinguish the two forms. \"What distinguishes interest-bearing capital in so far as it forms an essential element of the capitalist mode of production, from usurer's capital is in no way the nature or character of this capital itself. It is simply the changed conditions under which it functions, and hence also the totally transformed figure of the borrower who confronts the money-lender.\" This is a crucial theoretical claim. The capital is not intrinsically one thing or another; its social character is determined by the conditions under which it is lent and the class position of the borrower. Even where a man without means obtains credit as an industrialist or merchant, \"it is given in the expectation that he will function as a capitalist, will use the capital borrowed to appropriate unpaid labour. He is given credit as a potential capitalist.\" §12 The Apologetic Argument and the Catholic Church Analogy Marx now addresses a well-known apologetic argument — that capitalism allows a man of talent but no wealth to become a capitalist, thereby \"widening the basis\" of capital. \"And this fact so very much admired by the economic apologists, that a man without wealth but with energy, determination, ability and business acumen can transform himself into a capitalist in this way ... actually reinforces the rule of capital itself, widens its basis and enables it to recruit ever new forces from the lower strata of society.\" The apparent openness of the system is not evidence of its liberality but of its ability to absorb and appropriate the energies of the dominated classes. Then comes a striking historical analogy: \"The way that the Catholic Church of the Middle Ages built its hierarchy out of the best brains in the nation, without regard to status, birth or wealth, was likewise a major means of reinforcing the rule of the priests and suppressing the laity.\" And the axiom: \"The more a dominant class is able to absorb the best people from the dominated classes, the more solid and dangerous is its rule.\" This analogy is not decoration; it identifies a general mechanism of class rule, and it makes the connection between the ideological rationale for credit (\"open to all who merit it\") and the material function of credit (reproducing capital's domination over labour). §13 Reaction Against Usury in Its Various Forms Marx now distinguishes the several historical reactions against usury. First, he dismisses the mere moralizing reaction: \"Instead of anathema against interest-bearing capital in general, the founders of the modern credit system proceed from its express recognition.\" Second, he explains the institutional reaction of the Church and of the poor's protectors. The Monts-de-piété (\"mounts of piety\"), first at Sarlins in Franche-Comté in 1350 and later at Perugia and Savona in 1400 and 1479, were charitable pawn institutions aimed at protecting the poor from usury. But, Marx says with characteristic irony, they display \"the historical irony which turns pious wishes into their very opposite when they are realized.\" He cites Tuckett's History of the Past and Present State of the Labouring Population (London, 1846, I, p. 114) on the pawnshops of England: there are about 240 licensed pawnbrokers in the metropolis and nearly 1,450 in the country; the capital employed somewhat exceeds one million pounds sterling; \"this capital is turned round thrice in the course of a year, and yields each time about 33½ per cent on an average; according to which calculation, the inferior orders of society in England pay about one million a year for the use of a temporary loan, exclusive of what they lose by goods being forfeited.\" The bishop's charitable institution has become the pawnbroker's monopoly, and the charitable gesture has become 100% a year. Marx next dismisses the \"credit fantasies\" of Dr. Hugh Chamberleyne and John Briscoe, who in the last decade of the seventeenth century \"tried to emancipate the English aristocracy from usury by way of a land bank with paper money based on landed property.\" A quotation from their titles announces their purpose: \"the general good of the landed men, the great increase of the value of land,\" exemption of \"the nobility, gentry, etc., from taxes, enlarging their yearly estates, etc.\" Marx notes acidly that only the usurers would lose — \"these worst enemies of the nation, who had caused the nobility and the yeomanry more damage than an invading army from France could have done.\" §14 The Venetian and Genoese Credit Associations Marx now turns to the truly productive institutional forms: \"The credit associations set up in the twelfth and fourteenth centuries in Venice and Genoa arose from the need of the sea trade and the wholesale trade based on it to emancipate themselves from the rule of old-fashioned usury and from the monopolizing of money-dealing.\" He warns, however, against idealizing these institutions as purely public-spirited: \"If the banks proper that were founded in these urban republics were at the same time institutions for public credit, from which the state received advances against taxes anticipated, it should not be forgotten that the merchants who formed these associations were themselves the most prominent people in those states and were equally interested in emancipating both their government and themselves from usury, while at the same time subordinating the state more securely to themselves.\" The banks were thus simultaneously instruments of commercial emancipation and instruments of class power. Marx then supplies a famous observation on the political framing of banks, tied to the foundation of the Bank of England in 1694: \"When the Bank of England was to be founded, the Tories objected that banks were republican institutions. Flourishing banks existed in Venice, Genoa, Amsterdam and Hamburg. But who ever heard of a Bank of France or Spain?\" The point is that banking emerged where republican commercial oligarchies could control it, and that the Bank of England was an extension of a Whig financial interest. §15 Amsterdam and Hamburg: Deposit Banks, Not Credit Institutions Marx now distinguishes true credit institutions from mere deposit banks: \"The Bank of Amsterdam (1609) was not a milestone in the development of the modern credit system, any more than that of Hamburg (1619). It was simply a bank for deposits. The cheques that the bank issued were in actual fact simply receipts for the coined and uncoined precious metal deposited with it and circulated only with the endorsement of their recipients.\" By contrast, in Holland \"commercial credit and dealing in money did develop along with trade and manufacture, and by the course of development itself, interest-bearing capital became subordinate to industrial and commercial capital.\" The proof is the low level of the Dutch interest rate. \"In the seventeenth century, however, Holland served as the model country of economic development, just as England does today. The monopoly of old-fashioned usury, based on poverty, was thrown overboard there automatically.\" §16 The Seventeenth-Century Struggle Against Usury: Sir Josiah Child Marx then documents the ideological struggle for a compulsory reduction in the interest rate: \"Right through the eighteenth century we hear the cry for a compulsory reduction in the interest rate, with reference being made to Holland, and legislation proceeds in the same direction; the aim being to subordinate interest-bearing capital to commercial and industrial capital, instead of vice versa.\" The leading spokesman was Sir Josiah Child, \"the father of normal English private banking.\" Marx compares his polemic against usurers with the attack of \"off-the-peg tailors Moses and Son\" on the monopoly of the bespoke tailors. He notes Child's multiple faces: \"the father of English stock-jobbing\" and \"as autocrat of the East India Company, he defends its monopoly in the name of free trade.\" Child's Traites sur le commerce is quoted against Thomas Manley's Interest of Money Mistaken : \"As the champion of the timid and trembling band of usurers he erects his main batteries at that point which I have declared to be the weakest ... he denies point-blank that the low rate of interest is the cause of wealth and vows that it is merely its effect.\" Child then articulates the dialectical relationship of cause and effect between low interest and wealth: \"If it is commerce that enriches a country, and if a lowering of interest increases commerce, then a lowering of interest or a restriction of usury is doubtless a fruitful primary cause of the wealth of a nation. It is not at all absurd to say that the same thing may be simultaneously a cause under certain circumstances, and an effect under others.\" And again: \"The egg is the cause of the hen, and the hen is the cause of the egg. The lowering of interest may cause an increase of wealth, and the increase of wealth may cause a still greater reduction of interest.\" And: \"I am the defender of industry and my opponent defends laziness and sloth.\" Marx comments that \"this violent struggle against usury, the demand for the subjection of interest-bearing capital to industrial capital, is simply the prelude to the organic creations that these conditions of capitalist production produce in the form of the modern banking system, which on the one hand robs usurer's capital of its monopoly, since it concentrates all dormant money reserves together and places them on the money market, while on the other hand restricting the monopoly of the precious metals themselves by creating credit money.\" This is a precise dialectical formulation: the modern banking system both socializes idle money (thereby undermining usury's local monopoly) and displaces the precious metals from the center of the circulation by creating credit money. It is a specifically capitalist social relation that breaks down the pre-capitalist walls of money monopoly. §17 The Bank of England and the Founding Generation Marx then traces the ideological milieu around the founding of the Bank of England. \"The Scot William Paterson, founder of the Bank of England and the Bank of Scotland, is in every way Law the First.\" The comparison to John Law is significant: both Paterson and Law believed in the productive power of credit and public paper, and Law's experiment in France — his bank of 1716, made a state bank in 1718, ending in the inevitable collapse of 1720 — is given in a long footnote as the collapse of the very ideology of credit-as-wealth. Marx's footnote is precise: \"The English banker and economist John Law ostensibly believed that the state could increase public wealth simply by inconvertible note issue. In 1716 he founded a bank in Paris, which two years later was made into a state bank based on this principle. Law's operations gave rise to a previously unheard-of degree of speculation, ending in 1720 with the inevitable collapse of his bank.\" The point is to document the genealogy of credit illusions. Marx documents the opposition of the old money-lenders to the Bank of England. \"Against the Bank of England, 'all goldsmiths and pawnbrokers set up a howl of rage' (Macaulay, History of England , IV [London, 1855], p. 499).\" And further: \"During the first ten years the Bank had to struggle with great difficulties; great foreign feuds; its notes were only accepted far below their nominal value ... the goldsmiths' (in whose hands the trade in precious metals served as the basis of a primitive banking business) 'were jealous of the Bank, because their business was diminished, their discounts were lowered, their transactions with the government had passed to their opponents' (J. Francis, op. cit., p. 73).\" The old usurers, in their goldsmith form, are displaced by a socialized banking institution; the new institution breaks the old monopoly. §18 The Credit Bank Project of 1683 Marx then turns to an earlier, less successful institutional experiment. \"Even before the foundation of the Bank of England, a plan for a National Bank of Credit had already been drawn up in 1683, its purpose being, among other things: 'that tradesmen, when they have a considerable quantity of goods, may, by the help of this bank, deposit their goods, by raising a credit on their own dead stock, employ their servants, and increase their trade, till they get a good market instead of selling them at a loss.'\" The bank was established in Devonshire House on Bishopsgate Street, and lent to industrialists and merchants, on security of three-quarters of the value of commodities deposited, in the form of bills of exchange. \"In order to give these bills currency, a number of people in each branch of business combined together to form a company, from which anyone possessing this bank's bills was supposed to receive commodities for them with the same ease as if he offered cash payment. The bank did not do a flourishing business. The machinery was too complicated, and the risk involved in the depreciation of commodities too great.\" Marx thus documents the experimental failures that cleared the ground for the Bank of England's more centralized scheme. §19 The Modern Credit System's Real Content: Subjugation of Interest-Bearing Capital Marx now extracts the real content of the seventeenth- and eighteenth-century writings: \"If we concentrate on the real content of these writings, which were the theoretical accompaniment to the formation of the modern credit system in England and helped to foster it, we find nothing in them but the demand for the subjugation of interest-bearing capital and loanable means of production in general to the capitalist mode of production, as one of its preconditions.\" The word \"preconditions\" is significant. The credit system is not a secondary institution; it is one of the constitutive preconditions of the capitalist mode of production. §20 The Saint-Simonians and the Confusion of Travailleur with Industrialist Marx now turns to the socialist \"credit fantasies\" of the Saint-Simonians — a discussion he himself apparently intended to revise. He begins by noting that \"if we just look at the phrases used, the way they coincide with the banking and credit illusions of the Saint-Simonians is often astonishing, right down to the very words.\" He then makes a sharp economic-theoretical criticism of Saint-Simonian terminology: \"Just as for the Physiocrats the cultivateur does not mean the actual worker on the land, but rather the big farmer, so Saint-Simon's travailleur is not the worker but rather the industrial and commercial capitalist, and this usage is still current with his disciples.\" He quotes from Religion saint-simonienne. Économie politique et politique : \"A travailleur [worker] needs helpers, supporters, ouvriers [labourers — Marx emphasizes the word in the French quotation]; he looks for intelligent, adept and devoted ones; he puts them to work, and their labour is productive.\" Marx then notes Saint-Simon's own development: \"It should in no way be forgotten that it was only in his last work, Le Nouveau Christianisme , that Saint-Simon directly emerged as a spokesman for the working class and declared its emancipation to be the final goal of his endeavours. All his earlier writings are in fact simply a glorification of modern bourgeois society against feudal society, or of the industrialists and bankers against the marshals and law-mongers of the Napoleonic era. How different from the contemporary writings of Owen!\" Marx's footnote — inserted by Engels from source manuscripts — clarifies that \"in working over the manuscript again, Marx would undoubtedly have modified this passage substantially. It was inspired by the role of the former Saint-Simonians under the Second Empire in France, where, by historical irony, the world-shattering credit fantasies of this school were precisely realized, as Marx writes, in a swindle of previously unheard-of dimensions. Marx subsequently spoke only with admiration of the genius and encyclopedic mind of Saint-Simon. If in his earlier writings Saint-Simon ignored the opposition between the bourgeoisie and the proletariat, which in France was only just arising, and if he included the section of the bourgeoisie active in production in the travailleurs, this was Fourier's conception also ... If Owen saw further ahead here, this was because he lived in a different environment, in the midst of the industrial revolution and the class antagonism that was already acutely coming to a head.\" (The footnote is signed \"F. E.\" for Friedrich Engels.) The methodological point stands nonetheless: terminology that conflates the industrial capitalist with the worker cannot grasp the wage relation. §21 The Crédit Mobilier The institutional realization of Saint-Simonian credit ideas was the Crédit Mobilier founded by Emile and Isaac Pereire, both ex-Saint-Simonians. Marx's characterization is withering in its historical precision: \"a form that incidentally could come to such prominence only in a country like France, where neither the credit system nor large-scale industry was developed to the modern level. In England and America this kind of thing would have been impossible.\" He quotes the Saint-Simonian manifesto Doctrine de Saint-Simon. Exposition. Première année, 1828/29 : \"It is easy to understand how the banker can make cheaper advances than the capitalist and private usurer. And so it is also possible for this banker 'to provide the industrialist with his tools more cheaply, i.e. at lower interest, than the landlords and capitalists could do, as these could more easily be mistaken in their choice of borrower.'\" Marx's note explains: \"The Crédit Mobilier's operations, ostensibly in support of industrial projects and protected by the Bonapartist government, were based as Marx points out here on unsound principles and provided the framework for a variety of palpably fraudulent speculations. In 1856 and 1857 Marx wrote several articles for the New York Daily Tribune exposing the operations of the Crédit Mobilier, which finally went bankrupt in 1867.\" But the Saint-Simonian manifesto itself, in a footnote Marx quotes with some relish, gives away the flaw in the credit-banking scheme: \"The advantage that was supposed to follow from the intervention of the banker between the idle capitalist and the travailleurs is often outweighed and even destroyed by the opportunity that our disorganized society offers for egoism to hold sway, in the various forms of fraud and charlatanry; the bankers often intervene between travailleurs and idle capitalists simply to exploit both sides to the detriment of society.\" Marx's gloss is exact: \" Travailleur here stands for capitaliste industriel .\" §22 The Sources of Banking Resources Marx now makes a decisive analytical correction of the Saint-Simonian conception of the banking system. \"It is wrong, incidentally, to view the resources that the modern banking system has at its disposal simply as the resources of idle capitalists. In the first place, these resources include the portion of capital that industrialists and merchants keep temporarily unoccupied in the money form, as a money reserve or as capital still to be invested, i.e. idle capital, but not the capital of the idle ; secondly, that portion of everyone's revenues and savings that is permanently or temporarily set aside for accumulation. And both of these are essential to the character of the banking system.\" This is an important theoretical distinction. The banking system's power resides not in the accumulated hoards of rentiers but in the idle capital of active industrialists and merchants (capital temporarily in money form between productive cycles) plus the savings of all classes. The character of the banking system is essentially circulatory — it pools the fluid capital of production itself. §23 The Two Ultimate Presuppositions of Credit Marx now states the two presuppositions on which the credit system rests. \"It must never be forgotten, however, firstly that money in the form of precious metal remains the foundation from which the credit system can never break free, by the very nature of the case. Secondly, that the credit system presupposes the monopoly possession of the social means of production (in the form of capital and landed property) on the part of private individuals, that it is itself on the one hand an immanent form of the capitalist mode of production and on the other hand a driving force of its development into its highest and last possible form.\" The first presupposition — that credit ultimately rests on precious-metal money — sets an absolute limit to any purely credit-based expansion; the second — that credit rests on the private monopolization of the means of production — establishes credit as a constitutively capitalist institution, not a neutral technology. §24 The Banking System as Social Book-Keeping Marx now states, in an 1844-style formulation repeated from earlier drafts (the reference is to the Some Thoughts of the Interests of England of 1697), the great insight into the banking system's social character: \"The banking system, by its organization and centralization, is the most artificial and elaborate product brought into existence by the capitalist mode of production. Hence the tremendous power an institution such as the Bank of England has over trade and industry, even though their actual movement remains completely outside its orbit and it behaves quite passively towards them. Such a bank, however, supplies the form of a general book-keeping and distribution of the means of production on a social scale, even if only the form.\" This passage contains one of the chapter's most theoretically consequential formulations. Marx connects the social character of capital to its mediations: \"We have seen that the average profit of the individual capitalist, or of any particular capital, is determined not by the surplus labour that this capital appropriates first-hand, but rather by the total surplus labour that the total capital appropriates, from which each particular capital simply draws its dividends as a proportional part of the total capital. This social character of capital is mediated and completely realized only by the full development of the credit and banking system.\" The banking system, then, is the form of realization of capital's social character. And beyond that: \"On the other hand this also goes further. It places all available and even potential capital that is not already actively committed at the disposal of the industrial and commercial capitalists, so that neither the lender nor the user of this capital are its owners or producers. It thereby abolishes the private character of capital and thus inherently bears within it, though only inherently, the abolition of capital itself.\" This is the dialectical pivot on which the chapter's final synthesis rests: the banking system, by socializing capital functionally while leaving its private ownership formally intact, creates a contradiction that points inherently toward a supersession of capital — but only inherently, only as a possibility, only in connection with other large-scale organic revolutions. Marx is scrupulous about refusing to read into the credit system an automatic transcending of capitalism. §25 The Two-Sided Character of Credit Marx now states the ambivalence of credit in explicit terms. \"Through the banking system, the distribution of capital is removed from the hands of the private capitalists and usurers and becomes a special business, a social function. Banking and credit, however, thereby also become the most powerful means for driving capitalist production beyond its own barriers and one of the most effective vehicles for crises and swindling.\" This is the formula for credit as both expansive and crisis-generating . The same institution that mobilizes capital for production generates over-production, speculation, and collapse. The mutual implication is not incidental. Marx further identifies the banking system as the operative demystifier of money's social character: \"The banking system further shows, by substituting various forms of circulating credit for money, that the latter is in actual fact nothing but a special expression of the social character of labour and its products, which however, as antithetical to the basis of private production, must always present itself in the last instance as a thing, as a particular commodity alongside other commodities.\" Money's \"thinghood\" is thus revealed as an appearance of the social character of labour, an appearance that is however objectively necessary under private production. Credit, by functionally displacing money in circulation, exposes this. §26 Credit as a Lever of Transition — with Caveats Marx now returns to the theme of transition: \"Finally, there can be no doubt that the credit system will serve as a powerful lever in the course of transition from the capitalist mode of production to the mode of production of associated labour; however, only as one element in connection with other large-scale organic revolutions in the mode of production itself.\" He immediately qualifies: \"On the other hand, illusions about the miraculous power of the credit and banking system, in the socialist sense, arise from complete ignorance about the capitalist mode of production and about the credit system as one of its forms.\" And then the resolute statement of the impossibility of credit without capitalism: \"As soon as the means of production cease to be transformed into capital (which also means the abolition of private property in land), credit as such no longer has any meaning, something incidentally that even the Saint-Simonians have realized. As long as the capitalist mode of production persists, however, interest-bearing capital persists as one of its forms, and in fact forms the basis of its credit system.\" This gives us the chapter's most decisive formulation of the relation between interest-bearing capital and credit: interest-bearing capital is the basis of the credit system — credit is not merely a technical arrangement but a form of interest-bearing capital. §27 Proudhon and the Absurdity of Crédit Gratuit The critique turns finally to Proudhon: \"Only that same sensationalist writer who wanted commodity production to continue while money was abolished (Proudhon) could dream up the enormity of a crédit gratuit [interest-free credit], the ostensible realization of the pious wish arising from the petty-bourgeois standpoint.\" The footnote refers to The Poverty of Philosophy and A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy . The critic's target is a basic incoherence: to retain commodity production while abolishing interest is to retain the social relations that generate interest while wishing them away. Proudhon's plan to have a bank supply interest-free credit to all producers would leave the wage-labour relation intact; or rather, it would collapse because interest is not a free-standing evil but a form of surplus-value. §28 The Saint-Simonian Theory of Credit as Transferred Property Marx enlists the Saint-Simonian text itself to state the relation of credit to property. \"The purpose of credit is that in a society where some people possess tools for industry without the ability or the will to use them, while other industrious people possess no instruments of labour, these instruments can be transferred in the easiest possible manner from the hands of the former, their owners, to others who know how to use them. Let us note that according to this definition credit is a result of the manner and form in which property is constituted.\" Marx's gloss: \"Thus credit disappears together with this constitution of property.\" The Saint-Simonians therefore themselves recognized that credit presupposes the specific property relations of capitalism and would be meaningless without them. The Saint-Simonians then describe the passive function of banks: banks \"consider themselves destined to follow the movement initiated by transactions outside their own domain, but not to provide the impulse for these themselves; in other words, the banks play the role of capitalists for the travailleurs to whom they advance capital.\" But the Saint-Simonians wanted more: \"The Crédit Mobilier is latent already in the idea that the banks should take over this leadership themselves, and should excel 'by the number and usefulness of the firms they control and the works they have promoted.'\" §29 Constantin Pecqueur Marx then introduces Constantin Pecqueur as \"essentially always a Saint-Simonian, even if far more radical.\" Pecqueur demanded that the credit institution \"govern the entire movement of national production,\" and the Saint-Simonians called this \"système général des banques.\" Pecqueur's own warning is quoted: \"Just try to create a national credit institution which will advance resources to people of talent and merit, but no property, without binding these borrowers together compulsorily in a close solidarity in production and consumption, but rather, on the contrary, in such a way that they themselves determine what they exchange and produce. In this way you will only achieve what the private banks already do achieve, anarchy, a disproportion between production and consumption, the sudden ruin of some and the sudden enrichment of others; so that your institution will never do more than produce a sum of benefit equally balanced by a sum of misfortune borne by others ... you will simply have provided the wage-labourers whom you assist with the means to compete with one another, just like their capitalist masters do now\" (C. Pecqueur, Théorie nouvelle d'économie sociale et politique , Paris, 1842, pp. 433, 434). Pecqueur thus himself recognizes the coerced solidarity required if credit is to function as a socially organized alternative to competitive capitalism — and he rejects the alternative of merely subsidizing competition. §30 Interest-Bearing Capital as the \"Form of Capital Par Excellence\" Marx now returns to the question posed at the outset: why does interest-bearing capital appear to be the fundamental form of capital? \"We have seen how merchant's capital and interest-bearing capital are the oldest forms of capital. But it lies in the very nature of the matter that interest-bearing capital should appear to the popular mind as the form of capital par excellence . In merchant's capital we have a mediating activity, whether this is considered as fraud, labour or whatever. In interest-bearing capital, on the other hand, the self-reproducing character of capital, self-valorizing value, the production of surplus-value, appears as a purely occult quality.\" The phrase \"purely occult quality\" is precise. Because the interest-bearing capitalist performs no mediating activity — neither the merchant's buying and selling nor the industrialist's organization of production — the self-expansion of value appears as an intrinsic power of money. Hence: \"even a section of political economists, particularly in countries where industrial capital is not yet fully developed, as in France, cling to interest-bearing capital as the basic form and see ground-rent, for example, simply as another form of this, in so far as here too it is the form of a loan that prevails. In this way the internal articulation of the capitalist mode of production is completely misconstrued, and it is quite overlooked that both land and capital are only hired out to capitalists.\" §31 Loan of Capital in Kind and the Distinction of Immediate Producers Marx then refines the analysis. \"Instead of money, means of production can of course be loaned in kind, in the shape of machines, business premises, etc. But in this case these represent a certain sum of money, and if, apart from the interest, a portion is paid for wear and tear, this arises from the use-value, the specific natural form, of these capital elements. The distinguishing thing here again is whether they are loaned to the immediate producers, which presupposes the non-existence of the capitalist mode of production, at least in the sphere in which this kind of thing takes place, or whether they are loaned to industrial capitalists, which presupposes precisely that the basis is the capitalist mode of production.\" The class position of the borrower determines the character of the loan. Marx then dismisses the confusion of housing rent with capital hire. \"It is still more irrelevant and senseless to drag in the renting of houses, etc. for individual consumption. It is plain enough that the working class is swindled in this form too, and to an enormous extent; but it is equally exploited by the petty trader who supplies the workers with means of subsistence. This is a secondary exploitation, which proceeds alongside the original exploitation that takes place directly within the production process itself. The distinction between selling and lending here is completely immaterial and formal, and, as already shown, appears fundamental only for those who are in complete ignorance of the real context.\" Rent of a dwelling is not interest on capital — it is a species of secondary exploitation, on the same level as the petty trader's markup on the worker's means of subsistence. §32 Usury as Conservative and Mode-Destroying The chapter's general recapitulation now begins. \"Usury, just like trade, exploits a given mode of production but does not create it; both relate to the mode of production from outside.\" This is the summary thesis stated at the outset and restated here. \"Usury seeks directly to maintain this mode of production, so as constantly to exploit it anew; it is conservative, and simply makes the mode of production more wretched. The less the elements of production enter as commodities into the production process and emerge from it as commodities, the more does their establishment at a given place by means of money appear as a special act. The less important the role circulation plays in social reproduction, the more usury flourishes.\" The inverted proportion is exact. The less commodity circulation dominates production, the more usury flourishes. \"To say that monetary wealth develops as a special kind of wealth means, as far as usurer's capital is concerned, that this possesses all its claims in the form of monetary claims. It develops in a country all the more, the more the bulk of production is confined to services in kind, etc., i.e. to use-values.\" §33 The Dual Role of Usury in Forming Industrial Capital The chapter's historical synthesis is then stated: \"Usury, by its double effect, is a powerful lever in forming the preconditions for industrial capital. Firstly, it always forms an autonomous monetary wealth alongside the class of merchants, while secondly it appropriates the conditions of labour, by ruining the owners of the old conditions of labour.\" Thus usury's contribution to capitalism is twofold: creating liquid capital and expropriating producers. Both preconditions of industrial capital, but not the sufficient conditions. §34 The Luther Quotations and the Medieval-Versus-Modern Rate The chapter now closes with a series of supporting historical documents. The first is Gilbert's History and Principles of Banking on the medieval pattern: \"In the Middle Ages the population was purely agricultural. Under such a government as was the feudal system there can be but little traffic, and hence but little profit. Hence the laws against usury were justified in the Middle Ages.\" Gilbert goes on to give the statutory history in England: interest limited to 10 per cent under Henry VIII, 8 per cent under James I, 6 per cent under Charles II, 5 per cent under Queen Anne. The reason for statutory caps is that \"in those times, the lenders ... had, in fact, though not a legal, yet an actual monopoly, and hence it was necessary that they, like other monopolists, should be placed under restraint.\" The key dialectical point: \"In our times, it is the rate of profit which regulates the rate of interest. In those times, it was the rate of interest which regulated the rate of profit.\" This is a precise statement of the inversion of causality between pre-capitalist and capitalist exchange. Under usury, high interest feeds into high merchant's profit; under capitalism, the general rate of profit regulates interest. Second, Marx gives the Lutheran passage from An die Pfarrherrn wider den Wucher zu predigen (1540), quoted by Gilbart (or rather, from Luther's Werke , Wittenberg, 1589, part 6, p. 312). Luther's complaint about the rates current at the Leipzig fairs is colorful and quantitative: \"I have heard it said that 10 gulden are now taken at each Leipzig fair in the year, that is 30 gulden on every 100; some add the Neuenburg fair, thus making 40 gulden on every 100.\" Luther's arithmetic of the ruination is vivid: \"If someone who has 100 florins at Leipzig takes 40 per year, this means he gobbles up a peasant or a burgher. If he has 1,000 florins, he takes 400 each year and gobbles up a knight or a rich nobleman. If he has 10,000 florins, he takes 4,000 per year and gobbles up a rich count. If he has 100,000, as must be the case with the large dealers, he takes 40,000 per year and has gobbled up a great rich prince. If he has 1,000,000, he takes 400,000 and gobbles up a great king. And to do this he does not suffer any danger, either to his body or his goods, does not work, but sits by his stove and bakes apples; in this way a mean robber could sit at home and gobble up the whole world in ten years.\" Luther's second passage Marx adduces marks the ideological crisis of usury in the sixteenth century: \"Fifteen years ago I wrote against usury, when it had already spread so widely that I could not hope for any improvement. Since that time it has become so arrogant that it is no longer content to be classed as vice, sin, or shame, but has itself praised as a pure virtue and honour. What will deliver us now that shame has become honour, and vice virtue?\" The dialectic of moral evaluation tracks the dialectic of class power: as usury consolidates, its apologetic voice displaces its moral condemnation. §35 Hardcastle's Bankers and the Venetian Example Marx then quotes Hardcastle's Banks and Bankers (2nd edn, London, 1843, pp. 19, 20) on the social origins of banking: \"Jews, Lombards, usurers and extortioners were our first bankers, our primitive traffickers in money, their character little short of infamous ... They were joined by London goldsmiths. As a body ... our primitive bankers ... were a very bad set, they were gripping usurers, iron-hearted extortioners.\" This is the moral-historical register in which the emergent credit system was remembered by the nineteenth-century bankers themselves. Next he quotes Augier again on the spread of banking after Venice: \"The example provided by Venice (the formation of a bank) was thus quickly imitated; all coastal cities, and all cities everywhere which had made a name for themselves by their independence and their trade, founded their first banks. The return of their ships, which was often long delayed, led unavoidably to the custom of giving credit, which was strengthened still further in the wake of the discovery of America and the trade there. (This is an important point.) The chartering of ships made large advances necessary, as was already true in antiquity in the case of Athens and Greece. In 1308 the Hansa city of Bruges possessed an insurance company.\" The parenthetical \"(This is an important point.)\" is Marx's own insertion. Its logic is that the insecurity and temporal extension of maritime trade — ships returning late — generated credit relations as a functional necessity, and the discovery of America extended this further still. Maritime trade is thus identified as a principal historical driver of credit institutions. §36 Sir Dudley North and the Confession of Consumption-Lending Marx then quotes Sir Dudley North's Discourses upon Trade (London, 1691, pp. 6–7): \"The moneys employed at interest in this nation, are not near the tenth part, disposed to trading people, wherewith to manage their trades; but are for the most part lent for the supplying of luxury, and to support the expense of persons, who though great owners of lands, yet spend faster than their lands bring in; and being loath to sell, choose rather to mortgage their estates.\" North's confession, from the late seventeenth century, is that even in England before the modern credit system, most money lent was for consumption by the wealthy — that is, usury of the first kind, the lending to extravagant magnates that Marx identified as one of the two characteristic pre-capitalist forms. The empirical confirmation is important: even in the country that would become the home of industrial capitalism, the dominant form of interest-bearing capital up to 1690 was the usurer's lending to rentiers on mortgage. §37 The Polish Bill-of-Exchange Usury Marx then gives the Polish example from J. G. Busch's Theoretisch-praktische Darstellung der Handlung, etc. (3rd edn, Hamburg, 1808, vol. ii, pp. 232, 233): \"Warsaw had a large business in bills of exchange, but one that was principally based on and oriented towards the lending of its bankers. In order to obtain money, which they could lend to the extravagant magnates at 8 per cent and more, these sought and obtained open exchange credit abroad, i.e. a credit that did not have any commodity trade as its basis, but which the foreign drawee would continue to accept as long as the remittances from these exchange dealings continued to return. They paid heavily for this with the bankruptcy of men like Tepper and other respected Warsaw bankers.\" The Polish example demonstrates the exploitation of the bill of exchange mechanism — originally a credit instrument based on commodity trade — as a pure vehicle for consumption usury. The moment the credit-worthy basis vanished (as the returns failed to return), the structure collapsed. This is a case study of a functional misuse : the credit mechanism is not intrinsically productive; it is retrofitted to consumption-lending and produces bankruptcy. §38 The Advantages of the Church in Prohibiting Interest The chapter concludes with a remarkable passage from Busch concerning the Church's material interest in its own prohibition of interest. \"Taking interest had been banned by the Church, but not selling property to extricate oneself from need. It was not even forbidden to transfer property to the money-lender for a definite period, until repayment, so that the money-lender not only found his security in this, but could also enjoy compensation for the money he had lent in having the use of this property.\" The Church itself, \"or the communities and pia corpora associated with it, drew great advantage from this, especially in the time of the crusades. This brought a very great part of the national wealth into mortmain, especially since the Jews were barred from practising usury in this way, it being impossible to conceal the possession of such fixed liens ... Without the ban on interest, the Churches and monasteries could never have got so rich.\" This is the chapter's final historical irony. The Church's moral condemnation of interest, rather than restraining usury, functioned as a competitive advantage for the Church itself. The prohibition drove its Jewish competitors out of the mortgage market by exposing them to the confessional disqualification, while the Church was able to accumulate liens and property in mortmain — through the formal structure of a loan secured on land without appearing to violate its own ban on interest. The passage completes the demonstration that morality is systematically subordinate to the material relations of the modes of production in which it operates. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS: The chapter yields a set of fundamental principles about the relation between capital, history, and social form that should be borne in mind long after the specific historical data fade. First principle: Form is not function. Marx's most sustained methodological lesson is that a social form receives its meaning from the mode of production in which it functions and from the class position of the person through which it operates. Interest, a loan of money, the bill of exchange, the mortgage — these are names that recur across radically different social systems. Their content is determined by the relation of production in which they are embedded. To compare the Roman centurion's 40 per cent with the modern interest rate is not to compare analogous facts but to equivocate. From this follows Marx's critique of Adam Smith's comparison of English and Indian rates of interest, and above all his critique of Carey's use of the fall in the interest rate as evidence for the worker's improvement under capitalism. Second principle: Usury is parasitical, not generative. Usury exploits the existing mode of production; it does not create it. Its characteristic action is the concentration of monetary wealth through the exhaustion of small producers and the ruination of feudal proprietors, without transforming the technique or the social relations of production. It \"clings on like a parasite and impoverishes,\" it \"sucks dry,\" it \"cripples the productive forces instead of developing them.\" Only where \"the other conditions for the capitalist mode of production are present\" does the dissolving labor of usury become part of the formation of the new mode of production. This is the crucial guard against any teleological reading of economic history in which decaying usury automatically gives rise to capitalism. Third principle: The double expropriation. Usury accomplishes the expropriation of the producer from his conditions of labour and the formation of an independent monetary wealth; both are necessary preconditions of industrial capitalism; but neither is sufficient, and the second can occur without the first, producing instead a slave economy (as in Rome) or a \"harsher\" version of the pre-existing exploitation. Fourth principle: The modern credit system is a reaction against usury, not its abolition. The subordination of interest-bearing capital to industrial and commercial capital does not eliminate usury — it frees it in some domains (pawnshop, small producer, consumer credit) while disciplining it in others. And the credit system that effects this subordination itself rests on two presuppositions: the continued foundation of credit in precious-metal money, and the private monopoly of the social means of production. There is no credit without capitalism, and the dissolution of the property form would dissolve credit as a meaningful category. Fifth principle: The two-sidedness of credit. Credit is simultaneously the highest form of the social character of capital — the form under which capital's social character is mediated and fully realized, the most artificial and elaborate product of the capitalist mode — and one of the most powerful instruments for driving production beyond its own limits and hence for generating crises and swindling. This ambivalence is not a contingent defect but constitutive. Any promise of a credit-based transcendence of capitalism (Saint-Simonian, Proudhonian) misconstrues credit as a neutral technology, whereas in fact credit is the functional expression of the monopoly of the social means of production in private hands. Sixth principle: The radical independence of social form from social personnel. The usurer who becomes a landowner or slaveowner, the Catholic hierarchy built from the best brains irrespective of birth, the capitalist system that recruits the able from every class — these are all versions of the same lesson: a dominant class strengthens its rule not by exclusivity but by recruitment. The progressive-sounding openness of accession to the dominant class is itself a mechanism of domination. Seventh principle: The functional asymmetry between lending for production and lending for consumption. Interest-bearing capital presupposes a borrower who will use the loan to valorize — to appropriate unpaid labour. Where the borrower is a small producer just struggling to reproduce, or an extravagant magnate spending faster than his lands yield, the economic logic is different: capital has its mode of exploitation without its mode of production. This asymmetry explains why the same \"interest\" can be an instrument of capitalist development or of parasitical decay. The broader implications for Marx's project as a whole are considerable. The chapter provides the historical counterpart to the abstract theory of the division of surplus-value in the preceding parts of Volume III. It demonstrates that the differentiation of surplus-value into profit of enterprise, interest, and rent is not a historically invariant structure of human economy but a specific form of the capitalist mode of production, whose possibility rests on the expropriation of the producer and whose pre-capitalist analogues are functions of entirely different relations. It also develops the theme — essential to the critique of political economy — that the surface forms of economic life systematically veil the underlying relations. Interest-bearing capital, because it appears as the self-expansion of money alone, is the form of capital that most thoroughly \"fetishizes\" the social relation of exploitation; hence its predominance in the popular imagination and in apologetic theory. Finally, the chapter's treatment of credit anticipates the later analysis of finance capital and of crises. The banking system is described as the most powerful means of pushing capitalist production beyond its own limits and as one of the most effective vehicles for crises. Marx's conception of credit is thus dialectical: credit socializes production without socializing ownership; it mediates the realization of the social character of capital precisely in the form of a vast private-credit network; and thereby it inherently — only inherently, only abstractly, only as a tendency mediated by other transformations — bears within it the possibility of the abolition of capital itself. The fundamental lesson is thus the inseparability of economic form, class relation, and historical development. Usury is not simply an early or crude form of banking. It is the mode of exploitation that corresponds to the dominance of petty production, the mode that decomposes without constructing, the mode that concentrates claims to wealth without developing the productive forces. Where the conditions exist for its revolutionary effect, it serves as one of the means of the formation of capitalist production; where they do not, it merely multiplies the pathos of the pre-capitalist world. To imagine that credit freed from the wage relation could liberate the producer is thus to mistake an instrument of capitalization for a means of emancipation — the deepest error of both the Saint-Simonian banking utopia and the Proudhonian petty-bourgeois socialism, and the error against which Marx's Chapter 36 sets the most systematic and historically grounded warning. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 35 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 37"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 37", "title": "Chapter 37", "subtitle": "Introduction (Part VI: Transformation of Surplus-Profit into Ground-Rent)", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 37/Chapter 37 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 12237, "read_time": "61 min read", "tags": ["Differential Rent (I & II)", "Absolute Ground-Rent & Landed Property"], "keywords": ["Introduction to Ground-Rent (Landed Property)", "Transformation of Surplus-Profit into Rent", "Landed Property as Monopolistic Barrier to Capital", "Agricultural Capitalist vs Wage-Labourer vs Landlord", "Private Monopoly over Natural Planetary Resources"], "text": "Chapter 37 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 12,226 words ⏱️ ~61 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Core Thesis and Central Problem Chapter 37 of Marx's Capital , Volume 3, titled simply \"Introduction,\" opens the sixth and penultimate part of the work, \"The Transformation of Surplus Profit into Ground-Rent.\" Its declared function is partial and preparatory: it does not present the completed theory of ground-rent (which occupies the subsequent chapters on differential rent, absolute rent, and the history of the theory), but rather establishes the conceptual terrain, the definitions, the methodological precautions, and—most importantly—the object of analysis in its \"pure form,\" separated from a series of adulterations that routinely obscure it. The central problem the chapter confronts is deceptively simple to state and extraordinarily difficult to resolve: What exactly is ground-rent, and how is it distinct from all the other things with which it is confused in practice? Marx's answer, developed across twenty-eight dense pages, is that ground-rent proper is the specific economic form in which landed property—the monopoly by certain persons over particular portions of the globe—is \"valorized\" or \"realized\" under the capitalist mode of production. It is the tribute, the price, the \"tribute\" (Marx repeatedly uses this feudal-tinged word) that the landowner can extract from the industrial capitalist for permission to employ capital on the land. It is, crucially, a portion of surplus-value—specifically an excess over and above average profit —and therefore a category irreducible to profit, interest, or wages, even though in practice, as a \"lease-price,\" it often contains elements that are economically speaking none of these things. The thesis unfolds through a characteristic Marxian dialectical procedure. First, Marx sets the historical and logical presuppositions: capitalist agriculture, with wage-labourers employed by a farmer-capitalist who pays a contractual sum to a landowner. Second, he defends this abstraction against charges of arbitrariness, arguing that the capitalist mode of production, once it dominates agriculture, dominates society; that other historical forms of landed property are irrelevant to the specific analysis; and that treating the capitalist form as eternal is precisely the error of the bourgeois economists. Third, he defines ground-rent precisely by distinguishing it from the many things that appear as rent in the lease-price: interest on capital incorporated into the earth ( terre-capital ), deductions from average profit, and deductions from normal wages. Fourth, he analyzes the capitalization of ground-rent into the purchase price of land, exposing this category as prima facie irrational—since land is not a product of labour and therefore has no value—yet one that conceals a genuine relation of production. Fifth, he exposes the apologetic abuse of this irrationality (the attempt to justify landed property by its sale and purchase, an argument that would equally justify slavery). Sixth, and most systematically, he enumerates the three major errors that obscure the analysis of ground-rent and must be avoided: (1) the confusion between rent-forms corresponding to different levels of social development; (2) the illusion that explaining surplus-value in general explains ground-rent in particular; and (3) the misattribution to agriculture of characteristics common to all commodity production and value. The Intellectual Objective and Its Place in the Broader Framework The chapter's objective is to clear the ground—literally and figuratively—for the positive theory that follows. Marx is preparing to demonstrate how surplus profit (the excess of a particular capital's profit over the average rate) is transformed into ground-rent through the monopolization of the soil. To do this rigorously, he must first ensure that \"ground-rent\" names one specific thing and not a grab-bag of heterogeneous incomes lumped together by the \"levelling\" influence of the lease contract. The Introduction is thus a work of conceptual hygiene and ideological critique simultaneously. Within the architecture of Capital as a whole, Chapter 37 completes the analysis of the distribution of surplus-value among the three great classes of modern society: the wage-labourer, the industrial capitalist, and the landowner—the \"three classes that make up the framework of modern society\" who \"confront one another\" on the terrain of the rent contract. Volume 1 analyzed production and the extraction of surplus-value; Volume 2 analyzed circulation; Volume 3 analyzes the distribution of the total surplus-value as profit, interest, rent, and wages. Chapter 37 opens the consideration of the third and final claimant to the surplus: landed property. Its rigor lies in showing that the landowner's claim is not a return for productive contribution but a monopoly tribute—a \"tribute\" exacted by virtue of a legal fiction (exclusive possession) that is economically activated only when society's productive forces have developed to the point where agriculture yields surplus labour that a non-agricultural population can consume. The chapter is also, in its polemical dimension, a sustained assault on the apologetics of landed property and on the conceptual confusions of bourgeois political economy—Smith, Carey, Thiers, Dove, Lavergne, and Proudhon are all engaged. And it contains one of the work's most famous digressions: the footnote demolishing Hegel's philosophy of landed property, in which Marx shows that Hegel's \"absolute right of appropriation\" rests on naively absolutizing a particular and recent legal form of bourgeois society. The chapter thereby fuses economic analysis, historical materialism, and critique of ideology into a single movement of thought. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Scope and Method: Why Landed Property Enters the Analysis at All Marx opens by drawing a boundary: \"The analysis of landed property in its various historical forms lies outside the scope of the present work.\" This is not a casual disclaimer but a methodological decision with profound consequences. The object of Capital is the capitalist mode of production, and landed property enters only \"in so far as a portion of the surplus-value that capital produces falls to the share of the landowner.\" The landowner is thus analyzed not as a historical actor, a feudal remnant, or a legal personality, but as a claimant —a recipient of surplus-value. This narrowing is the condition for scientific precision. This entails a series of explicit assumptions. First, \"agriculture, just like manufacturing, is dominated by the capitalist mode of production.\" Rural production is pursued by capitalists who \"are distinguished from other capitalists, first of all, simply by the element in which their capital and the wage-labour that it sets in motion are invested.\" The farmer produces wheat \"just as the manufacturer produces yarn or machines.\" Here Marx establishes the formal identity of agricultural and industrial capital: both are capital, both exploit wage-labour, both are subject to the law of value and the equalization of the rate of profit. Agriculture is not a separate metabolic realm with its own economics; it is a sphere of capitalist investment. The difference is only the \"element\"—the soil, the living processes of plant growth—in which the capital is invested. But this assumption carries a further, more sweeping implication: that the capitalist mode of production \"dominates all spheres of production and bourgeois society,\" so that \"its preconditions, such as the free competition of capitals, their transferability from one sphere of production to another, an equal level of average profit, etc. are also present in their full development.\" This is decisive for what follows. The theory of ground-rent presupposes the theory of the general rate of profit and prices of production; ground-rent is, by definition, an excess over and above that average profit. If capital were not freely transferable and the rate of profit not equalized, ground-rent would be indistinguishable from a simple monopoly price or a super-profit. Marx must therefore posit the full maturity of the capitalist mode—competition, mobility, equalization—precisely in order to isolate ground-rent as the residue left over after average profit is deducted. The Historical Form of Landed Property and the Assumption of Capitalist Agriculture Marx immediately clarifies that the form of landed property under consideration is not transhistorical: \"The form of landed property with which we are dealing is a specific historical form, a form transformed by the intervention of capital and the capitalist mode of production.\" Whether the original form was \"feudal landed property or of small peasant agriculture pursued as a livelihood,\" capital transforms it. In the small-peasant case, \"possession of the land and soil appeared as a condition of production for the immediate producer, with his ownership of the land being the most advantageous condition, the condition for his mode of production to flourish.\" Here Marx acknowledges—briefly but significantly—that private ownership of the land by the cultivator can be functionally rational for a non-capitalist mode of production, because it secures the producer's conditions of labour. Under capitalism, however, the logic inverts. Capital \"always presupposes the expropriation of the workers from the conditions of labour\"; in agriculture \"it presupposes the expropriation of the rural workers from the soil and their subjection to a capitalist who pursues agriculture for the sake of profit.\" The expropriation of the direct producer is the sine qua non of capitalist agriculture—the same process analyzed in Volume 1's \"Primitive Accumulation.\" This leads to a preemptive rebuttal. Marx notes that \"it is thus completely immaterial for our presentation if we are reminded that other forms of landed property and agriculture have existed or still exist besides this.\" Such a reproach \"can affect only those economists who treat the capitalist mode of production on the land and the form of landed property corresponding to it not as historical categories but as eternal ones.\" This is a key dialectical move: the charge of abstraction is reversed against the bourgeois economists. They are the ones who eternalize a historically specific form; Marx abstracts deliberately in order to analyze that specific form scientifically, having already demonstrated historically (in Volume 1) how it came into being. Abstraction is legitimate when it isolates a real, historically produced object, and illegitimate when it mistakes that object for a natural necessity. Wheat, Mining, and Adam Smith's Discovery Marx narrows the empirical focus: \"We therefore confine ourselves exclusively to the investment of capital in agriculture proper, i.e. in the production of the main plant crops on which a population lives. We can take wheat, since this is the major means of sustenance for modern, capitalistically developed nations.\" The parenthetical—\"(Instead of agriculture, we might equally well have taken mining, since the laws are the same.)\"—is the crucial claim. The laws governing ground-rent are not peculiar to agriculture; they apply wherever a natural agent monopolized by an owner is a necessary condition of production. This signals that ground-rent theory is a general theory of monopoly rent over natural forces . Marx then pays tribute to Adam Smith: \"It is one of Adam Smith's great services that he showed how the ground-rent for capital applied to the production of other agricultural products, e.g. of flax, of dye-stuffs, in independent stock-raising, etc., is determined by the ground-rent yielded by capital invested in the production of the staple crop.\" The point is analytically vital: rent on marginal agricultural products is not autonomously determined but is parasitic upon the rent of the staple. Because all agricultural land can in principle be switched to wheat production, the rent obtainable from wheat sets the opportunity cost and thus the rent on land used for flax or pasture. This is the earliest sketch of what will become the theory of differential rent, in which the least productive (or least favorably situated) land regulates the market, and inferior lands yield no rent at all. Marx adds that \"no further progress has been made in this connection since his time\"—a pointed remark on the stagnation of bourgeois rent theory after Smith. The definition of \"land\" is then broadened: \"what we understand here by land also includes water, etc. in so far as this has an owner and appears as an accessory to the land.\" This covers fisheries, waterways, and mineral springs—any natural force subject to exclusive appropriation. Landed Property as Monopoly: The Economic Valorization of a Legal Fiction The most important conceptual statement of the opening pages now arrives: \"Landed property presupposes that certain persons enjoy the monopoly of disposing of particular portions of the globe as exclusive spheres of their private will to the exclusion of all others.\" Note the juridical-philosophical language (\"sphere of private will\")—a deliberate echo of Hegel, whose critique follows in the footnote. Once this monopoly exists, \"it is a question of developing the economic value of this monopoly, i.e. valorizing it, on the basis of capitalist production.\" Marx then delivers a crucial dual claim. On the one hand, \"Nothing is settled with the legal power of these persons to use and misuse certain portions of the globe.\" Legal ownership by itself explains nothing—\"The use of this power depends entirely on economic conditions, which are independent of their wills.\" The landowner may legally do what he likes with his land, but whether he can economically extract rent, and how much, is determined by social conditions external to his will. On the other hand, \"The legal conception itself means nothing more than that the landowner can behave in relation to the land just as any commodity owner can with his commodities.\" The legal notion of \"free private landed property\" is thus a specific historical product: \"it arises in the ancient world only at the time of the dissolution of the organic social order, and arises in the modern world only with the development of capitalist production. In Asia, it has simply been imported here and there by the Europeans.\" This is a compressed historical-materialist claim: the concept of the landowner as absolute proprietor, free to alienate his land like any commodity, is not natural but is generated by the dissolution of pre-capitalist social forms and by the rise of capitalist commodity production. This connects directly to the analysis of \"Primitive Accumulation\" in Volume 1: the capitalist mode presupposes \"on the one hand that the direct producers are freed from the position of a mere appendage of the soil (in the form of bondsmen, serfs, slaves, etc.) and on the other hand the expropriation of the mass of the people from the land. To that extent, the monopoly of landed property is a historical precondition for the capitalist mode of production and remains its permanent foundation, as with all previous modes of production based on the exploitation of the masses.\" The Hegelian Digression: The Metaphysics of Appropriation In footnote 26, Marx launches one of his most celebrated polemics—against Hegel's derivation of private property in land in the Philosophy of Right . The structure of the critique is important because it exemplifies Marx's method of showing that a seemingly absolute philosophical category is in fact a reified historical form. Hegel argues that man as a \"person\" must give his will actuality as the \"soul of external nature,\" and hence must take possession of nature as his private property. If this is the distinguishing mark of \"the person,\" it would follow that \"a man must be a landowner if he is to realize himself as a person.\" Free private property in land—\"a very recent product\"—is for Hegel \"not a particular social relationship, but rather a relationship of man as a person to 'nature', the 'absolute right of appropriation which man has over all things.'\" Marx dismantles this on several levels. First, the practical impossibility: \"the individual person cannot maintain himself as a proprietor by his 'will' alone, vis-a-vis the will of someone else who similarly wants to give himself corporeal actuality in the same fragment of the globe. Quite other things than a good will are needed for this.\" Property claims are resolved not by will but by power, law, and economic force. Second, the boundary problem: \"there is absolutely no way of seeing where 'the person' sets a limit to the realization of his will, whether the existence of his will is realized in an entire country or whether it needs a whole pile of countries.\" Hegel \"comes completely unstuck\" here. Third, Hegel's own admissions betray him. Hegel writes: \"Taking possession is always piece-meal in type; I take into possession no more than what I touch with my body.\" But then external objects \"extend further than I can grasp,\" so \"whatever I have in my grasp is linked with something else.\" This linkage cascades infinitely—\"this something else is connected in turn with something else again, so that the limit as to how far my will has to pour out into the soil as soul completely vanishes.\" Hegel is forced to concede that \"positive law must enact its statutes since nothing further on this topic can be deduced from the concept.\" Marx's verdict: \"This is an extraordinarily naive confession for 'the concept' to make, and proves that the concept, which makes the great blunder right from the start of taking a quite particular legal notion of landed property which belongs to bourgeois society as absolute, understands 'nothing' of the actual configuration of this landed property.\" The footnote concludes with the dialectically delicious point that Hegel thereby admits \"that with the changing needs of social, i.e. economic development, 'positive law' can and must change its provisions.\" Philosophy of Right collapses into historical materialism against its own intention. Landed Property as Historical Precondition and Permanent Foundation Returning to the main text, Marx elaborates how capitalist production transforms whatever form of landed property it encounters: \"feudal landed property, clan property or small peasant property with the mark community is transformed into the economic form corresponding to this mode of production, however diverse the legal forms of this may be.\" The transformation has two great achievements, which Marx frames as the \"great results of the capitalist mode of production\": First, it transforms agriculture \"from a merely empirical set of procedures, mechanically handed down and practised by the most undeveloped portion of society, into a conscious scientific application of agronomy\"—though Marx immediately qualifies: \"in so far as this is at all possible within the conditions of private property.\" Second, it \"detaches landed property completely from relations of lordship and servitude\" and \"completely separates the land as a condition of labour from landed property and the landlord.\" The separation is so complete that \"the landed proprietor can spend his entire life in Constantinople, while his landed property remains in Scotland.\" Landed property thus \"receives its purely economic form by the stripping away of all its former political and social embellishments and admixtures\"—the traditional accoutrements that the industrial capitalists and their theorists denounce as \"uselessly and absurdly superfluous\" in their struggle against landed property. The dialectical thrust is unmistakable: the very rationalization of agriculture and separation of land from lordship that capitalism accomplishes also reduces landed property \"to an absurdity.\" The landlord becomes a pure parasitic claimant. And this \"historical advance\" was \"purchased... first of all, by the complete impoverishment of the immediate producers.\" Progress and immiseration are inseparable. Footnote 27 deepens the critique. Conservative agricultural chemists like Johnston and even the defender of private property Charles Comte admit \"that private property places insuperable barriers on all sides to a genuinely rational agriculture.\" Comte is quoted: a rational agriculture would require \"a single will, and above all an enlightened one\" to dispose of each piece of territory—but \"the existence of such a will... would be incompatible with the division of the land into private holdings.\" Marx comments that such writers think only of \"the cultivation of the land of a single country as a whole,\" but the deeper contradiction is temporal: \"the entire spirit of capitalist production, which is oriented towards the most immediate monetary profit—stands in contradiction to agriculture, which has to concern itself with the whole gamut of permanent conditions of life required by the chain of human generations.\" Forests are ruined because private owners manage them only for immediate profit—unless they are state property. Here Marx anticipates the modern critique of capitalist environmental destruction. Ground-Rent Defined: The Transformation of Surplus-Value into Rent Marx now states his preliminary observations \"to guard against any misunderstandings.\" The presuppositions of capitalist agriculture are recapitulated precisely: the actual cultivators are wage-labourers employed by a capitalist farmer who pursues agriculture \"simply as a particular field of exploitation of capital.\" At specified dates \"e.g. annually,\" the farmer pays the landowner \"a contractually fixed sum of money (just like the interest fixed for the borrower of money capital), for the permission to employ his capital in this particular field of production. This sum of money is known as ground-rent, irrespective of whether it is paid for agricultural land, building land, mines, fisheries, forests, etc.\" Two features of this definition deserve emphasis. First, the analogy to interest: the rent is \"contractually fixed\" like the interest paid by a borrower of money capital. This analogy will later be abused by apologists who conflate rent with interest; Marx introduces it here only to illuminate the form of the payment (a fixed monetary claim), not its substance . Second, the universality of the category across land-types: ground-rent is paid \"irrespective of whether it is paid for agricultural land, building land, mines, fisheries, forests, etc.\" It is paid \"for the entire period for which the landowner has contractually rented the land to the farmer.\" Ground-rent is thus \"the form in which landed property is economically realized, valorized.\" And with this, \"We have together here, moreover, and confronting one another, all three classes that make up the framework of modern society—wage-labourer, industrial capitalist, landowner.\" The rent relation is the point at which the tripartite class structure of capitalism becomes visible in a single contract. Terre-Capital: Fixed Capital Incorporated into the Earth The next conceptual distinction is essential and easily missed. Capital \"may be fixed in the earth, incorporated into it,\" in two forms: more transiently (chemical improvements, fertilizer) and more permanently (drainage ditches, irrigation, levelling, farm buildings). Marx revives his term from The Poverty of Philosophy : \"la terre-capital,\" which denotes \"capital incorporated into the earth in this way. This is one of the categories of fixed capital.\" The distinction is between terre-matiere (land as matter) and terre-capital (land as capital). Marx quotes his earlier work: \"The very fact of applying further outlays of capital to land already transformed into means of production increases land as capital without adding anything to land as matter, that is, to the extent of the land... Land as capital is no more eternal than any other capital... Land as capital is fixed capital; but fixed capital gets used up just as much as circulating capital.\" The analytic payoff: \"Interest on the capital incorporated into the earth and the improvements that are thereby made to the soil as an instrument of production may form a portion of the rent that is paid by the farmer to the landowner, but it does not constitute ground-rent proper, which is paid for the use of the soil as such, whether this is in a state of nature or is cultivated.\" Ground-rent proper is the price of the soil as such —a natural monopoly. The interest on terre-capital is a distinct component that may appear within the lease-price but is not rent. Footnote 29 adds a critical qualification: \"I say 'may form', because in certain circumstances this interest is governed by the law of ground-rent and may therefore disappear, for instance where there is competition from new lands of great natural fertility.\" When abundant cheap fertile land becomes available, the market will not pay extra for improvements—the interest on terre-capital is absorbed, demonstrating that its apparent autonomy is subordinate to the law of rent. The Farmers' Improvements and the Secret of Landlord Enrichment Marx traces the fate of improvements over the lease cycle. The more transient investments \"are all made without exception by the farmer himself,\" as is simple rational cultivation (as distinguished from \"brutal exhaustion of the soil, as is the case for instance in the former slave states of North America\"). These investments \"improve the soil, increase its product and transform the earth from a mere raw material into earth-capital. A cultivated field is worth more than an uncultivated one of the same natural quality.\" But \"as soon as the lease stipulated in the contract has expired—and this is one of the reasons why the landowner seeks to shorten the term of the lease to a minimum, as capitalist production develops—the improvements made to the land fall to the landowner as his property, as an inseparable accident of the substance, the land.\" When the new lease is concluded, \"the landowner adds interest on the capital incorporated into the earth to the ground-rent proper.\" His rent swells; and if he sells the land, \"its value has now risen. He does not sell just the land, but rather the improved land, the capital incorporated into the earth, which has cost him nothing.\" This is \"one of the secrets—quite apart from the movement of ground-rent as such—of the increasing enrichment of the landowners, the constant inflation of their rents and the growing money value of their estates as economic development progresses.\" The landowner \"puts away in their own private purses the result of a social development achieved without their participation—they are fruges consumere nati \" (born to consume the fruits). But this is also \"one of the greatest obstacles to a rational agriculture, since the farmer avoids all improvements and outlays which are not expected to give their full return during the duration of his lease.\" Marx notes that this was denounced by James Anderson, \"the true discoverer of the modern theory of rent,\" who was himself a practising farmer and agronomist. This is a classic Marxian motif: the irrationality of a social arrangement is visible even to its more clear-sighted defenders. Building Land as Illustration: The Expropriation of Leasehold Capital Marx quotes A. A. Walton's History of the Landed Tenures of Great Britain and Ireland (1865) to show the same process in stark form. Walton argues that agricultural improvements must fail \"so long as such improvements mean in a far higher degree increased value to the estate and rent-roll of the landlord, than bettering the condition of the tenant farmer or the labourer.\" The farmers know that \"however much they may improve the land or enhance its value, the landlords will, in the long run, reap the principal benefit, in higher rents and the increased value of their estates.\" The \"lion's share of any improvements they may make is sure to go into the pockets of the landlords in the long run.\" This process \"still does not appear so clearly in agriculture proper as in the use of land for building.\" In England, building land is leased for ninety-nine years (or shorter). \"When this period has expired, the buildings fall to the landlord, together with the land itself.\" The tenant must \"deliver up the house at the expiration of the lease... after having paid an exorbitant ground-rent up to the expiration of the lease.\" Then \"the agent or surveyor will come and examine your house... and then take possession of it, and annex it to his lord's domains.\" Walton warns that \"the whole of the house property in the kingdom will be in the hands of the great landlords, as well as the land.\" The whole West End of London \"may be said to belong to about half a dozen great landlords.\" The building example is doubly instructive for Marx: \"(1) because it shows clearly the distinction between the ground-rent proper and the interest on the fixed capital incorporated into the land, which can form an addition to ground-rent.\" The interest on the buildings accrues to the building speculator for the duration of the lease and \"has in and of itself nothing to do with the ground-rent.\" \"(2) because it shows how, in the case of land, the capital of others incorporated into it ultimately falls to the share of the landlord, and the interest on this swells his rent.\" Marx then uses the 1861 Census for England and Wales—36,032 house-owners out of a population of 20,066,224—to note that the ratio would \"look quite different if the big proprietors were separated off from the small ones.\" The statistical aggregation conceals the monopoly. The Confusion of Ground-Rent with Interest Marx now turns to a major ideological error. \"Some writers, partly as spokesmen for landed property against the attacks of the bourgeois economists and partly in an effort to transform the capitalist system of production into a system of 'harmonies' instead of antitheses, as for example Carey, have sought to present ground-rent, the specific economic expression of landed property, as identical with interest. In this way, the opposition between landowners and capitalists would be abolished.\" Marx notes the historical inversion: \"The converse method was applied at the inception of capitalist production. At that time, landed property still passed in the popular mind as the original and respectable form of private property, whereas interest on capital was denounced as usury.\" Dudley North, Locke, and Turgot \"therefore presented interest on capital as a form analogous to ground-rent\" in order to legitimize it. The recent writers reverse the analogy to legitimize the landlord. This historical symmetry exposes both moves as apologetics: the analogy is deployed whichever way serves the dominant claimant. The substantive rebuttal: \"The more recent writers forget—quite apart from the fact that ground-rent can and does exist without the addition of any interest on the capital incorporated into the soil—that the landowner not only receives interest on other people's capital in this way, without it costing him anything, but gets the capital itself for nothing into the bargain.\" Moreover, \"The justification for landed property, as that for all other forms of property of a particular mode of production, is that the mode of production itself possesses a transitory historical necessity.\" But landed property is unique: \"at a certain level of development it appears superfluous and harmful even from the standpoint of the capitalist mode of production.\" This is the seed of the argument, developed later, that capitalism itself eventually renders landlordism an obstacle to be abolished. The Capitalization of Ground-Rent and the Price of Land Marx next analyzes the capitalization of rent. Ground-rent appears \"as a certain sum of money that the landowner draws each year from leasing out a piece of the earth.\" Any money income can be capitalized—treated as interest on an imaginary capital. \"If the average interest is 5 per cent, for example, an annual ground-rent of £200 may be viewed as the interest on a capital of £4,000.\" This capitalized rent \"forms the purchase price or value of the land.\" Marx labels this category \" prima facie irrational, in the same way that the price of labour is irrational, since the earth is not the product of labour, and thus does not have a value.\" Yet \"this irrational form conceals a genuine relation of production.\" If a capitalist pays £4,000 for land yielding £200 annually, \"he draws the average annual interest of 5 per cent on the £4,000 in just the same way as if he had invested this capital in interest-bearing securities.\" The purchase price \"is in actual fact not the purchase price of the land, but rather of the ground-rent that it yields, reckoned according to the prevailing rate of interest.\" Crucially: \"This capitalization of the rent, however, presupposes the rent itself, whereas the rent cannot be conversely derived and explained from its own capitalization. Its existence, independent of the sale, is rather the presupposition proceeded from.\" This is a logical point of the first importance. The apologist reasons: the land has a price, therefore the rent is justified (the buyer earns interest on his investment). Marx inverts: the price is derived from the rent, which is derived from the monopoly. The sale does not create or justify the rent; it merely capitalizes a claim that already exists. The Inverse Movement of Land Prices and the Rate of Interest From the capitalization formula, Marx derives a law: \"taking the ground-rent as a constant magnitude, the price of land will rise or fall in inverse ratio to the rate of interest.\" If the rate falls from 5% to 4%, an annual rent of £200 represents the valorization of £5,000 rather than £4,000, \"and so the price of the same piece of land would rise from £4,000 to £5,000, or from twenty years' purchase to twenty-five.\" The English \"years' purchase\" convention is simply \"another expression for the capitalization of the ground-rent.\" Conversely, \"in the opposite case, vice versa.\" The broader tendency: \"since we have seen that the rate of profit has a tendency to fall as social development proceeds, and so too therefore does the rate of interest... since we have also seen that even leaving aside the rate of profit, the interest rate has a tendency to fall as a result of the growth of money capital for loan, it follows therefore that the price of land has a tendency to rise, even independently of the movement of ground-rent and the price of the products of the soil.\" This is a striking, counterintuitive conclusion: land prices rise even without any increase in agricultural productivity or rent, purely because the discount rate falls. Landownership thus benefits automatically from a general tendency of capitalist development—again, without the landowner lifting a finger. Thiers, Proudhon, and the Apologetic Confusion The practical consequences of the rent/interest confusion are now illustrated. Because land is seen \"as a particularly superior form of property\" and land purchase \"as a particularly secure capital investment,\" the rate of interest at which ground-rent is bought \"generally stands somewhat lower than is the case with other long-term capital investments.\" The buyer may receive only 4% instead of 5%—\"or, what comes to the same thing, he pays more capital for the ground-rent than he would for the same annual money income in other investments.\" Thiers, in La Propriété (the printed text of his 1848 speech against Proudhon in the National Assembly), \"concludes from this that ground-rent is low, whereas all that this shows is the high level of its purchase price.\" Marx's sarcasm (\"in his generally abysmal book\") is augmented by the footnote's biographical venom: Thiers is \"the butcher of the Paris Commune, 'Thiers, that monstrous gnome'\" whose \"scandalous biography Marx traces in 'The Civil War in France.'\" The polemic is personal as well as theoretical. The deeper apologetic abuse: \"The fact that the capitalized ground-rent presents the appearance of the price or value of land, so that the earth is bought or sold just like any other commodity, provides some apologists with a justification for landed property; the buyer has paid an equivalent for it, as with any other commodity.\" Marx's rebuttal is devastating: \"The same justification would then apply also to slavery, since for the slaveowner who has paid cash for his slaves, the product of their labour simply represents the interest on the capital invested in their purchase. To derive a justification for the existence of ground-rent from its purchase and sale is nothing more than justifying its existence in terms of its existence.\" The reductio ad absurdum via slavery is characteristic Marxian rhetoric—and analytically precise: both cases involve capitalizing a claim on the labour of others. The Adulterated Lease-Price: Deductions from Profit and Wages Marx now returns from \"pure form\" to \"practical effects.\" \"In practice, everything that the farmer pays the landowner in the form of the lease-price for permission to cultivate the soil appears as ground-rent. Whatever the components out of which this tribute has been put together... it has in common with ground-rent proper that the monopoly to a piece of the earth enables the so-called landowner to exact a tribute.\" This commonality is what makes the lease-price determine the price of land—which \"is nothing but the capitalized revenue from the lease of the land.\" The components of the lease-price, beyond ground-rent proper, are: (a) interest on terre-capital , which \"must form an ever-growing addition to the total rental of a country\"; (b) \"a deduction from average profit\"; and (c) a deduction from \"normal wages, or both together.\" This part, \"whether of profit or of wages, appears here in the form of ground-rent because instead of accruing to the industrial capitalist or the wage-labourer, which would be normal, it is paid to the landowner in the form of the lease-price. Economically speaking, neither part forms ground-rent; but in practice it forms income for the landowner, an economic valorization of his monopoly, just as much as genuine ground-rent does, and it has the same effect in determining the price of land.\" The importance of this distinction cannot be overstated. In practice, the landlord's income is a composite; in theory, ground-rent proper is one thing. Confusing them leads to erroneous conclusions—for instance, believing that high rents reflect high agricultural productivity, when they may reflect simply the landlord's power to squeeze wages and profits. Ireland: The Formal Existence of Ground-Rent Without Capitalist Production Marx then addresses a case where ground-rent has \"a formal existence even though the capitalist mode of production itself does not exist, the tenant himself is not an industrial capitalist, and his manner of farming is not a capitalist one. This is how it is in Ireland.\" The Irish tenant is \"generally a small peasant.\" What he pays \"often absorbs not only a portion of his profit, i.e. his own surplus labour, which he has a right to as the owner of his own instruments of labour, but also a portion of the normal wage.\" The landowner, \"who does nothing at all here to improve the soil, expropriates from him the small capital which he incorporates into the soil for the most part by his own labour, just as a usurer would do in similar conditions. Only the usurer would at least risk his own capital.\" \"It is this continuing robbery that forms the object of the dispute over Irish land legislation.\" The demand is that \"the landowner who gives a farmer notice to quit should be forced to compensate the tenant for the improvements he has made.\" Palmerston's cynical response—\"The House of Commons is a house of landed proprietors\"—is quoted with the footnote contextualizing the 1862–3 parliamentary session and Marx's own agitation on the Irish question in the International Working Men's Association. The Irish case is thus both a theoretical illustration (rent without capitalist production) and a political indictment. The Corn Laws and the Cheating of the Farmers Marx mentions exceptional cases—high rentals for small plots leased to factory workers for gardens—thenfocuses on \"agricultural rent in countries of developed capitalist production.\" Among English farmers \"there are a number of small capitalists who are destined and compelled to apply their capital in agriculture as farmers, by dint of their upbringing, training, tradition, competition and other circumstances. They are forced to be content with a smaller than average profit and to part with a portion of this to the landowner in the form of rent. This is the only condition on which they are permitted to invest their capital on the land.\" Because landowners exert \"a major influence on legislation, and in England even a predominant one, this influence can be exploited to cheat the entire class of farmers.\" The Corn Laws of 1815—\"a tax on bread explicitly imposed on the country in order to ensure the idle landowners the continuance of a rental that had grown abnormally during the Anti-Jacobin War\"—kept prices above free-trade levels. But they did not keep prices at the levels the legislating landowners decreed as normal. \"Leasehold contracts were none the less concluded under the impression that these would be the normal prices. As soon as the illusion was destroyed, a new law was passed with new normal prices, which were as much the impotent expression of landed property's greedy fantasies as the old ones had been. The farmers were cheated in this way from 1815 to the 1830s. Hence during this whole era the constant theme of 'agricultural distress.' Hence during this period the expropriation and ruin of an entire generation of farmers and their replacement by a new class of capitalists.\" This historical digression demonstrates the class dynamics behind the theoretical categories. The landlord's legislative power is used to inflate rents; the burden falls on farmers (via profit deductions) and ultimately on labourers (via wage deductions). The Deduction from Agricultural Wages The most general and important fact, Marx argues, is \"the reduction of wages for agricultural labourers in particular below their normal average, so that a part of the worker's wage is deducted from him, to form a component of the lease-price and thus accrue to the landowner instead of the worker under the guise of ground-rent. This is the general rule in England and Scotland.\" The Parliamentary committees of inquiry before the Corn Laws—\"till now the most valuable contribution to the history of wages in the nineteenth century, and almost unexploited, besides being at the same time a pillory which the English aristocracy and bourgeoisie erected for themselves\"—proved \"convincingly and beyond all doubt that the high rents and corresponding rise in land prices during the Anti-Jacobin War were due in part to a deduction from wages and their suppression even below the physical minimum; i.e. to the handing-over to the landowner of a part of the normal wage.\" Various circumstances made this possible—\"the depreciation of money, the manipulation of the Poor Laws in the agricultural districts\"—while \"the incomes of the farmers rose enormously and the landowners fabulously enriched themselves.\" One of the principal arguments for the corn duties was \"that it would be physically impossible to lower the wages of the agricultural labourers any further.\" Marx adds: \"This situation has not fundamentally altered, and in England, as in all the European countries, a part of the normal wage still goes into ground-rent just as before.\" The Earl of Shaftesbury (Lord Ashley)—the philanthropic aristocrat who championed the Ten Hours Bill for factory workers—was counter-attacked by industrialists who published statistics on the wages of agricultural labourers in his own villages, \"which clearly showed how a portion of this philanthropist's ground-rent consisted simply of the plunder that his tenants extracted for him from the wages of the agricultural labourers.\" Marx relishes the irony: the aristocratic philanthropist's benevolence is funded by the immiseration of the labourers on his own estates. Fawcett and the Identity of High Rent with Low Wages Marx demonstrates the mechanism through a demand-and-supply dynamic. Whenever circumstances compel a temporary rise in agricultural wages, farmers cry that raising wages to the normal level \"is impossible and would inevitably ruin them without a simultaneous reduction in ground-rent. It is thereby admitted that in the name of ground-rent the farmers make a deduction from wages and hand this over to the landowner.\" Between 1849 and 1859, agricultural wages rose due to \"the exodus from Ireland,\" \"the exceptional absorption of the agricultural population by manufacturing industry,\" \"the wartime demand for soldiers,\" \"an exceptional emigration to Australia and the United States (California),\" and other reasons. \"At the same time, with the exception of the bad harvests of 1854–6, average cereal prices fell by more than 16 per cent.\" The farmers clamoured for rent reductions; \"in some cases they did obtain this. By and large, however, their demand did not meet with success.\" They \"took refuge in a reduction of production costs, including the massive introduction of steam-engines and new machinery, which partly replaced horses... while it also released agricultural labourers and thus brought about an artificial over-population and a fresh fall in wages.\" Despite this, \"all this happened despite an overall relative decline in the agricultural population during this decade... and even an absolute decline in the agricultural population in some purely agricultural districts.\" Footnote 32 identifies the source: John C. Morton's 1859 lecture to the London Society of Arts, based on documents from \"some hundred farmers in twelve Scottish and thirty-five English counties.\" Fawcett, then Professor of Political Economy at Cambridge (Marx notas he died in 1884 as Postmaster-General), told the Social Science Congress on 12 October 1865: \"The labourers were beginning to emigrate, and the farmers were already beginning to complain that they would not be able to pay such high rents as they have been accustomed to pay, because labour was becoming dearer in consequence of emigration.\" Marx's gloss: \"Here a high rent of land is thus directly identified with low wages. And in so far as the high level of land prices is conditioned by this factor that increases rents, a rise in the value of land is identical with a devaluation of labour, and a high price of land with a low price of labour.\" This is one of the chapter's most powerful formulations: rising land values are literally the capitalization of reduced wages. Rubichon on France: The Rise in Rents as Public Disaster France provides corroboration. Rubichon writes: \"The lease-price rises because the price of bread, wine, meat, vegetables and fruit rises, while the price of labour remains the same.\" The price of a rural working day \"was exactly the same as it is today,\" while meat has tripled since. \"Who is the victim of this revolution?... The rise in rents is the sign of a public disaster.\" Marx's heading summarizes: \"Examples of rent as a result of a deduction from average profit on the one hand and from average wages on the other.\" Morton on Small Farms and the Self-Exploitation of the Farmer Morton, \"land agent and agricultural engineer,\" observes that \"the rent for large farms is less than for smaller ones,\" since competition is fiercer for small farms and \"as few small farmers are able to turn their attention to any other business than that of farming, their anxiety to get a suitable occupation leads them in many instances to give more rent than their judgement can approve of\" ( Resources of Estates , 1858, p. 116). Morton also gives the case of farms under 70–80 acres which cannot maintain a two-horse plough: \"Unless the tenant works with his own hands as laboriously as any labourer, his farm will not keep him. If he entrusts the performance of his work to workmen while he continues merely to observe them, the chances are, that at no distant period, he will find he is unable to pay his rent.\" Here the ground-rent \"evidently includes a deduction from the wage of the farmer himself and hence still more certainly of the people he employs.\" Morton concludes farms should not be less than 70 acres so the farmer can keep two or three horses. The footnote corrects Marx's own misattribution: the quote is from John Lockhart Morton, not John Chalmers Morton. This candid footnote is a small instance of Marx's scholarly scruple. Lavergne's Statistical Illusions and the Critique of Bourgeois Agronomy Marx turns to \"the extraordinary wisdom of Monsieur Leonce de Lavergne, Membre de l'Institut et de la Société Centrale d'Agriculture,\" whose Économie rurale de l'Angleterre (English translation, 1855) compares annual benefits from cattle (used for labour in France, replaced by horses in England): France: Milk £4 million; Meat £16 million; Labour £8 million; Total £28 million. England: Milk £16 million; Meat £20 million; Labour £36 million. Marx's demolition: the higher English figure arises \"simply because, as he himself points out, milk in England is as dear again as in France, while he assumes the same price for meat in both countries.\" Reducing English milk to French prices gives £8 million and a total of £28 million—identical to France. \"It is a bit much for Monsieur Lavergne to take into account at the same time both the quantities produced and the differences in price, so that, if England produces certain articles at greater cost than France, which means at most a bigger profit for farmers and landowners, this appears as a superiority of English agriculture.\" Marx exposes a statistical sleight-of-hand: double-counting quantities and prices to manufacture a spurious English superiority. Lavergne also repeats agronomic \"fairy stories\": that cereals \"exhaust the soil,\" while fodder and root crops \"enrich the soil\" because they \"derive from the atmosphere the principal elements of their growth, while they give to the soil more than they take from it,\" justifying the Norfolk rotation. Marx's contempt for Lavergne's credulity—believing \"these fairy stories about English rural conditions\"—extends to Lavergne's belief that the wages of English rural labourers have improved since the Corn Laws' abolition. Marx refers to his own Volume 1, Chapter 25, section 5, e. Bright's Birmingham Speech: The Pauperization of the Labourer The chapter's humanitarian climax is John Bright's speech at Birmingham on 13 December 1866. Bright speaks of \"the five million families who are not represented at all in Parliament,\" among whom \"one million, or rather more than one million, in the United Kingdom\" are paupers, and \"another million just above pauperism, but always in peril lest they should become paupers.\" Bright describes the \"abject condition,\" \"poverty,\" \"suffering,\" and \"utter hopelessness.\" He compares them unfavorably with American slaves: \"in the United States—even in the Southern States during the reign of slavery—every Negro had an idea that there was a day of jubilee for him. But to these people... there is neither the belief of anything better nor scarcely an aspiration after it.\" Bright tells the story of John Cross, a Dorsetshire labourer who worked six days a week for twenty-four years at eight shillings per week, had a family of seven children, and stole \"a wooden hurdle of the value of sixpence\"—for which he was sentenced to fourteen or twenty days' imprisonment. Bright: \"many thousands of cases like that of John Cross are to be found throughout the country, and especially in the south, and that their condition is such that hitherto the most anxious investigator has been unable to solve the mystery as to how they keep body and soul together.\" Bright contrasts the \"great toiling and unenfranchised nation\" with the ruling class—\"Look at its wealth; look at its ostentation—look at its luxury. Behold its weariness—for there is weariness amongst them, but it is the weariness of satiety—and see how they rush from place to place, as it were, to discover some new pleasure\" ( Morning Star , 14 December 1865). This passage, though by a Liberal free-trader rather than a socialist, serves Marx's purpose: it documents empirically the immiseration that funds the rents analyzed in the surrounding pages. The Three Major Errors: An Overview Marx now pivots to the systematic enumeration: \"There are three major errors which obscure the analysis of ground-rent and are to be avoided in dealing with it.\" Before addressing them individually, he briefly notes the general confusion: \"We shall go on to show how surplus labour and hence surplus product in general are confused with ground-rent, a portion of the surplus product that is both quantitatively and qualitatively specific.\" The \"indigenous basis of surplus labour in general\" is \"a natural condition without which this is impossible... that nature provides the necessary means of subsistence... with the application of an amount of labour-time that does not swallow up the entire working day. This indigenous productivity of agricultural labour (and here we include simple gathering, hunting, fishing, stock-raising) is the basis of all surplus-value; just as all labour is originally first directed towards the appropriation and production of food.\" (Animals provide pelts for warmth, cave-dwellings, etc.) Mr. Dove represents the confusion \"in a different way.\" Originally, \"agricultural labour and industrial labour are not separate; the second is an appendage of the first. The surplus labour and surplus product of the agricultural clan, the common household or the family, comprises both agricultural and industrial labour. The two go hand in hand. Hunting, fishing and agriculture are impossible without appropriate instruments. Weaving, spinning, etc. are first of all conducted as agricultural sidelines.\" First Error: Confusion Between Different Historical Forms of Rent \"The confusion between the various forms of rent that correspond to different levels of development of the social production process.\" Whatever the specific form of rent, \"what all its types have in common is the fact that the appropriation of rent is the economic form in which landed property is realized and that ground-rent in turn presupposes landed property, the ownership of particular bits of the globe by certain individuals.\" The owner may be \"a person representing the community, as in Asia, Egypt, etc.\"; or landed property may be \"simply an accidental accompaniment of the property that certain persons have in the persons of the immediate producers, as in the systems of serfdom and slavery\"; or \"pure private property that non-producers have in nature\"; or \"a relationship to the land which, as with colonists and small peasant proprietors, appears as directly implied... in the appropriation and production of the products of particular bits of land by the direct producers.\" \"This common character of the different forms of rent—as the economic realization of landed property, the legal fiction by virtue of which various individuals have exclusive possession of particular parts of the globe—leads people to overlook the distinctions.\" The error is to treat the common element (monopoly appropriation) as the whole explanation, thereby effacing the specific historical determination of capitalist rent. Second Error: Explaining Rent by the General Conditions of Surplus-Value \"All ground-rent is surplus-value, the product of surplus labour. In its more undeveloped form, rent in kind, it is still a direct surplus product. Hence the error that the rent corresponding to the capitalist mode of production, which is always an excess over and above profit... can be explained simply by explaining the general conditions of existence for surplus-value and profit.\" Marx then details these general conditions. The subjective condition: \"the direct producers must work for more time than is required to reproduce their own labour-power... They must perform some kind of surplus labour.\" The objective condition: \"they also can perform surplus labour: that natural conditions are such that a part of their available labour-time is sufficient to reproduce and maintain them as producers; that the production of their necessary means of subsistence does not consume their entire labour-time. Natural fertility sets one limit here, as a point of departure or basis. The development of the social productivity of their labour sets the other limit.\" More concretely: \"since the production of foodstuffs is the very first condition of their life and of any production at all, the labour employed in this production, i.e. agricultural labour in the broadest economic sense, must be sufficiently fruitful to prevent the entire available labour-time from being absorbed in the production of foodstuffs for the immediate producers, so that agricultural surplus labour and hence an agricultural surplus product is possible.\" Further: \"the total agricultural labour—necessary and surplus—of one section of society must be sufficient to produce the necessary foodstuffs for the entire society, i.e. also for the non-agricultural workers; this great division of labour between agriculturalists and industrialists must be possible, and similarly that between those agriculturalists who produce foodstuffs and those who produce raw materials.\" These are the universal conditions of surplus-value. But: \"the subjective and objective conditions of surplus labour and surplus-value in general have nothing to do with the particular form, whether this is profit, or whether it is rent. They apply to surplus-value as such, whatever particular form this may assume. They therefore do not explain ground-rent.\" Explaining why surplus-value exists is not explaining why a portion of it accrues to a landowner. The latter requires the theory of monopoly in land and the equalization of profit. The Law of Value and the Proportionate Division of Social Labour Marx inserts a substantial theoretical digression on the law of value as it applies to total social production. \"It is the labour necessary for the production of particular articles—for the satisfaction of a particular social need for particular articles. If this division is in due proportion, products of various types will be sold at their values (at a further stage of development, at their prices of production).\" This is \"the law of value as it makes itself felt, not in relation to the individual commodities or articles, but rather to the total products at a given time of particular spheres of social production autonomized by the division of labour.\" Not only must no more labour-time be devoted to each individual commodity than necessary, \"but out of the total social labour-time only the proportionate quantity needed is devoted to the various types of commodity.\" Here \"Use-value still remains a condition.\" For the individual commodity, use-value depends on satisfying a social need. For \"the mass social product it depends on its adequacy to the quantitatively specific social need for each particular kind of product and therefore on the proportional division of the labour between these various spheres of production in accordance with these social needs, which are quantitatively circumscribed.\" Marx adds a signal parenthesis: \"(This point should be introduced in connection with the distribution of capital between the various spheres of production.)\" \"The social need, i.e. the use-value on the social scale, here appears decisive for the quota of total social labour-time that falls to the share of the various particular spheres of production. But this is simply the same law that is already exhibited by the individual commodity, i.e. that its use-value is the precondition of its exchange-value and hence of its value.\" The relevance to surplus-value: \"It is a point that bears on the relation between necessary and surplus labour only in as much as an imbalance in this proportion means that the commodity value, and therefore also the surplus-value contained in it, cannot be realized.\" Example: \"the proportion of cotton goods produced may be too high even though the labour-time realized in this total product is simply that needed under the given conditions. But too much of society's overall labour has been spent on this particular branch, and so a portion of the product is useless. The total product is therefore sold as if only the necessary proportion had been produced.\" This quantitative barrier \"is simply a further developed expression of the law of value in general; even though necessary labour-time takes on a different meaning here. Only such-and-such a quantity of this is required in order to satisfy the social need. The limit in this case emerges through the use-value. Under the given conditions of production, society can spend only so much of its total labour-time on one particular kind of product.\" Third Error: Ascribing to Agriculture Properties Common to All Commodities \"A particular peculiarity that arises with the economic valorization of landed property, that is the development of ground-rent, is that its amount is in no way determined by the action of its recipient, but rather by a development of social labour that is independent of him and in which he plays no part. This is why something that is common to all branches of production and their products on the basis of commodity production... is easily conceived as a peculiar property of rent.\" \"The level of ground-rent (and with it the value of land) rises in the course of social development, as a result of the overall social labour. Not only does the market and the demand for agricultural products grow, but the demand for the land itself also grows directly, since it is a condition of production competed for by all possible branches of business, including non-agricultural ones. Rent, moreover, and with it the value of land... develops along with the market for the product of land and hence with the growth in the non-agricultural population.\" Marx then notes a structural tendency: \"It lies in the nature of the capitalist mode of production that it constantly reduces the agricultural population in relation to the non-agricultural, because in industry (in the narrow sense) the growth of constant capital in relation to variable is linked with an absolute growth in variable capital..., while in agriculture the variable capital required for the cultivation of a particular piece of land declines absolutely and can therefore grow only in so far as new land is cultivated, which however presupposes in turn a still greater growth in the non-agricultural population.\" This asymmetry—absolute decline of agricultural labour-power per acre versus absolute growth in industry—explains the relative (and sometimes absolute) depopulation of the countryside. But Marx's central point is polemical: \"In actual fact, what we have here is not a phenomenon peculiar to agriculture and its products. The same applies rather to all other branches of production and products... These products are commodities, use-values which possess an exchange-value, and particularly one that can be realized, converted into money, only to the extent to which other commodities form an equivalent for them.\" The market develops \"by way of the social division of labour\"; products become commodities and mutually serve as markets. \"This is in no way something peculiar to the products of agriculture.\" The Dove Confusion: Rent as Dependent on Non-Agricultural Population \"Rent can develop as money-rent only on the basis of commodity production, and particularly of capitalist production, and it develops to the same extent to which agricultural production becomes commodity production, i.e. the extent to which non-agricultural production undergoes an independent development in relation to it.\" To the extent that commodity production develops, \"so too there develops the production of surplus-value and surplus product. But in the same measure as the latter develops, there develops in landed property the ability to capture a growing portion of this surplus-value by way of its monopoly of the earth and hence to raise the value of its rent and the price of the land itself.\" \"It is still the capitalist who has the active function in the development of this surplus-value and surplus product. The landowner has only to seize a portion of surplus product and surplus-value that increases without any effort on his part. This is the peculiarity of his position.\" Dove \"tries to explain rent in general on this basis. He says that rent does not depend on the size of the agricultural product but rather on its value; this however depends on the size and productivity of the non-agricultural population.\" Marx's objection: \"it is also true to say for any other product that it only develops as a commodity with the volume and diversity of the series of other commodities that form equivalents for it.\" Exchangeability depends on the number of different commodities outside it; and the quantity in which it can be produced as a commodity depends on this exchangeability. \"No producer considered in isolation produces a value or commodity, neither the industrialist nor the agriculturalist. His product becomes a value and a commodity only in a specific social context.\" First, \"in so far as it appears as an expression of social labour\"; second, \"where this social character of his labour appears as a social character impressed on his product, in its money character and its general exchangeability as determined by its price.\" The Conclusion: What Is Truly Peculiar to Ground-Rent Marx's closing synthesis ties the third error together: \"Thus if on the one hand, instead of explaining rent, it is simply surplus-value or in a still narrower conception only surplus product in general that is explained, on the other hand the mistake is committed of ascribing a character that accrues to all products as commodities and values exclusively to the products of agriculture. This is rendered even more superficial when a withdrawal is made from the general determination of value to the realization of a particular commodity value. Any commodity can realize its value only in the process of circulation, and whether and to what extent it does realize this depends on the market conditions of the time.\" The final paragraph states the chapter's essential conclusion with lapidary precision: \"Thus it is not peculiar to ground-rent that agricultural products develop into values and as values, i.e. that they confront other commodities as commodities themselves and that the non-agricultural products confront them as commodities, nor that they develop as particular expressions of social labour. What is peculiar is that with the conditions in which the agricultural products develop as values (commodities), and with the conditions of realization of their values, landed property also develops the power to appropriate a growing part of these values created without its assistance, and a growing part of the surplus-value is transformed into ground-rent.\" This single sentence contains the whole argument of the Introduction: the conditions that allow value to develop are general to commodity production; the specific fact about ground-rent is the power of landed property to appropriate a growing share of values it did not create. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles and Systemic Laws The chapter establishes several principles that govern the entire subsequent theory of ground-rent—and, more broadly, Marx's mature treatment of distribution. First, the principle of historical specificity. Ground-rent is not an eternal category but a \"specific historical form, a form transformed by the intervention of capital.\" The error of treating it as eternal is ideological, not merely analytical: it naturalizes the monopoly appropriation of the earth. Marx's insistence on the historical character of the category is itself a critical weapon. The legal form of \"free private landed property\" is \"a very recent product,\" imported into Asia by Europeans, and arising only at the dissolution of the \"organic social order.\" The Hegel footnote makes the methodological point sharply: a philosophy that takes \"a quite particular legal notion of landed property which belongs to bourgeois society as absolute\" cannot understand the actual configuration of that property. Second, the principle of abstraction as scientific method. Marx deliberately narrows his object—capitalist agriculture, wheat production, pure ground-rent—because only such abstraction allows the isolation of the specific mechanisms at work. He preempts the objection that other forms of landed property exist: such an objection \"can affect only those economists who treat the capitalist mode of production on the land... not as historical categories but as eternal ones.\" The point is dialectical: having demonstrated historically how capitalist landed property arose (in Volume 1), Marx can legitimately analyze its pure form. Third, the principle of the tripartite class structure. The rent relation is the point at which the three classes \"that make up the framework of modern society—wage-labourer, industrial capitalist, landowner\"—confront one another. Ground-rent theory is therefore not a marginal topic but the completion of the analysis of surplus-value distribution. The total surplus-value is divided among profit (and its sub-forms interest, commercial profit), rent, and wages; ground-rent is the share that accrues to the landowner by virtue of monopoly, not contribution. Fourth, the principle of the distinction between pure categories and their adulterated appearance. In practice, \"everything that the farmer pays the landowner in the form of the lease-price... appears as ground-rent.\" But the lease-price may contain interest on terre-capital , deductions from average profit, and deductions from wages. The scientific task is to distinguish these components, even while acknowledging that \"in practice it forms income for the landowner... just as much as genuine ground-rent does.\" This distinction is what prevents the apologetic inference that high rents equal high productivity. Fifth, the principle of the irrationality of the capitalized rent. The price of land is \" prima facie irrational\"—land has no value because it is not a product of labour—yet it \"conceals a genuine relation of production.\" The price is capitalized rent; the rent presupposes the monopoly; the sale does not justify the rent. The slave analogy (the price of a slave capitalizes the value extracted from his labour) exposes the apologetic move of justifying ground-rent by its purchase and sale as a circular argument: \"justifying its existence in terms of its existence.\" Sixth, the principle of the inverse relation between interest rates and land prices. Because the land price is the capitalized rent, a falling rate of interest raises land prices even without any increase in rent or agricultural productivity. This is a systemic law of considerable importance: the landlord class benefits automatically from the general tendency of the rate of profit and interest to fall. The \"price of land has a tendency to rise, even independently of the movement of ground-rent.\" Seventh, the principle of the three errors. These function as a methodological checklist: (1) do not confuse historically different forms of rent; (2) do not think that explaining surplus-value in general explains ground-rent; (3) do not ascribe to agriculture what belongs to commodity production as such. The \"peculiarity\" of landed property is not that agricultural products become commodities and values—that is universal—but that landed property \"develops the power to appropriate a growing part of these values created without its assistance.\" Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications For political economy. The chapter is a sustained critique of bourgeois rent theory. Smith's insight into the regulation of all agricultural rent by the rent of the staple is praised but shown to be a starting point, not a completion. Carey's attempt to harmonize rent and interest is dismissed as apologetics that would \"abolish the opposition between landowners and capitalists\" by conceptual fiat. Thiers' confusion of low rent with high purchase price is ridiculed. Lavergne's statistical manipulation (double-counting quantity and price) is exposed. Dove's reduction of rent to the general conditions of commodity value is criticized. The chapter thereby demonstrates the critical function of Marxian political economy: not merely to describe distribution but to expose the conceptual confusions that legitimate it. For the theory of class and exploitation. The most important theoretical implication is that the landowner's income is not a return for anything productive. It is a monopoly tribute, funded by (a) the excess surplus-profit of more fertile lands, (b) deductions from the average profit of the farmer, and (c) deductions from the wages of agricultural labourers. The immiseration documented by Bright, Fawcett, Rubichon, and Morton is thus not incidental but constitutive of ground-rent. Rising land values are \"identical with a devaluation of labour.\" The landlord is literally fruges consumere nati —born to consume the fruits—appropriating \"the result of a social development achieved without their participation.\" For the critique of rational agriculture and ecology. Footnote 27 and the surrounding discussion contain one of Marx's earliest sustained ecological arguments. The temporal horizon of capitalist agriculture—short leases, immediate monetary profit—contradicts \"the whole gamut of permanent conditions of life required by the chain of human generations.\" Forests, for instance, are ruined by private management oriented to immediate profit, even though experts (Johnston, Comte) recognize the need for long-term stewardship. The short lease systematically discourages improvements that would not pay off before expiry, and the landowner who reaps the benefit of tenant improvements has every interest in shortening the lease. This is a structural argument about the incompatibility of private property in land with rational, sustainable agriculture—an argument whose descendants run through twentieth- and twenty-first-century environmental economics. For philosophy and the critique of ideology. The Hegel footnote is a small masterpiece of immanent critique. Marx does not simply reject Hegel's philosophy of property; he shows that Hegel's own text concedes what the concept cannot deliver. The infinite regress of \"taking possession\" (I touch only a piece, but it is linked to something else, which is linked to something else...) forces Hegel to appeal to \"positive law\"—thereby admitting that property is determined by social and historical circumstances, not by the concept. Marx's verdict—that the concept \"understands 'nothing' of the actual configuration of this landed property\"—is a model of materialist philosophical criticism. For political practice. The chapter is threaded with political interventions. The Irish land question—\"this continuing robbery\"—is analyzed as rent without capitalist production: the small peasant tenant pays not only his surplus labour but also a portion of his necessary wage, and even the small capital he has incorporated into the soil by his own labour. Palmerston's \"The House of Commons is a house of landed proprietors\" is quoted as an admission of class rule. The Corn Laws are analyzed as a legislative swindle of farmers and labourers. Bright's speech on pauperism is adduced to show the human cost. And the final sentence—\"landed property also develops the power to appropriate a growing part of these values created without its assistance\"—provides the theoretical ground for the political conclusion, developed in later chapters, that landed property becomes a mere obstacle, \"superfluous and harmful even from the standpoint of the capitalist mode of production.\" For the unity of Capital . Chapter 37 marks the completion of the theoretical circuit begun in Volume 1. There, Marx showed how surplus-value is produced through the exploitation of wage-labour. In Volume 2, he showed how it circulates. In Volume 3, he shows how it is distributed among rival claimants—industrial profit, interest, commercial profit, and now ground-rent. The Introduction to the rent section establishes that the landowner is not a producer but a claimant , not a source of value but a monopolist of the condition under which value-producing labour must operate. This completes the anatomy of \"modern society\" as an ensemble of classes defined by their relation to the productive process and to the surplus it generates. The deepest lesson of the chapter is perhaps this: the appearance of a \"rent\" paid for the use of \"land\" dissolves, under analysis, into a complex of distinct social relations—a monopoly claim, a capitalized anticipation of surplus-value, a deduction from profit, a deduction from wages, and an appropriation of the improvements made by others. \"In practice,\" as Marx repeatedly insists, all of this is fused in the single money payment; but theory must distinguish what practice fuses. And in distinguishing them, theory exposes the essential truth that the landowner's income is not the fruit of the earth but a claim upon other people's labour—a claim that the systematic development of capitalism both presupposes as its \"permanent foundation\" and increasingly renders, in the landowner's own redundant person, an irrational and parasitic excrescence. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 36 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 38"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 38", "title": "Chapter 38", "subtitle": "Differential Rent: General Remarks", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 38/Chapter 38 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 5746, "read_time": "29 min read", "tags": ["Differential Rent (I & II)"], "keywords": ["Differential Rent: General Remarks", "Natural Forces (Waterfalls, Soil) Appropriated by Capital", "Surplus-Profit Arising from Natural Advantages", "Waterfall Replacing Steam Power Example", "Permanence of Differential Advantage Transformed into Rent"], "text": "Chapter 38 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,716 words ⏱️ ~29 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 38, \"Differential Rent in General,\" opens Part VI of Marx's Capital , Volume 3 — the section devoted to \"The Transformation of Surplus Profit into Ground-Rent.\" Its central problem is posed with surgical precision at the outset: How can ground-rent exist at all if agricultural (and mining) products are sold not above, but exactly at, their prices of production? This is a question of enormous theoretical consequence. If commodities exchange at values determined by socially necessary labour-time, and if competition between capitals equalizes profit rates into a general average, then there seems to be no room for a landlord's cut. Any rent would appear to be either a deduction from the capitalist's normal profit or a surcharge on the consumer — in either case a mere transfer, not a distinct economic category with its own law of origin. Marx's declared task is to show that a rent can arise without any violation of the law of value, without monopoly pricing, and without any absolute rise in productivity. His answer is the concept of differential rent , introduced here in its most general and abstract form. Marx constructs a deliberately simplified, quasi-experimental model: a country in which most factories run on steam-engines, but a minority run on natural waterfalls. Because the waterfall is a free gift of nature that enters no one's cost accounts, the waterfall-using manufacturer produces his commodities more cheaply. Yet he sells them at the same market price of production as everyone else — the price governed by the average conditions of the sphere. The gap between his lower individual price of production and the higher general price of production is a surplus profit . So long as the waterfall is a monopolizable, place-bound, non-reproducible natural force attached to a piece of the earth's surface, and so long as that surface has an owner, this surplus profit is intercepted by the landowner and thereby transformed into ground-rent . The chapter's intellectual objective is therefore twofold. First, it isolates the general concept of differential rent — a surplus profit generated by the sheer differential in natural productive conditions, before any consideration of agriculture proper. Second, it establishes a set of rigorous distinctions that will discipline everything that follows: between absolute and relative productivity gains; between a natural force as the basis and as the source of surplus value; between the creation of surplus profit, which landed property does not perform, and the appropriation of it, which landed property alone makes possible. Marx closes with five numbered conclusions and a demonstration that the \"price\" of a waterfall is nothing but capitalized rent — an \"irrational expression\" that conceals a real social relation. Within the broader architecture of Capital , this chapter functions as the load-bearing analytical gateway to the entire theory of ground-rent. Everything that follows in Part VI — differential rent in agriculture (Forms I and II), absolute rent, the critique of Ricardo's and Malthus's rent theories — presupposes the model built here. The chapter's final line signals this explicitly: \"Now that we have established the general concept of differential rent in this way, we turn to consider this rent in agriculture proper. What will be said of agriculture applies on the whole also to mining.\" 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 38.1 The Methodological Premise: Rent Without a Monopoly Price Marx begins by fixing an assumption that he insists operates throughout the analysis: the products that pay rent — \"which means that a part of their surplus-value — and therefore also a part of their total price, is reducible to rent\" — are sold just like all other commodities at their prices of production . He adds that for present purposes he need consider only agricultural products, though mining products would serve equally well. This is not a throwaway stipulation; it is the pivot on which the entire argument turns. A price of production is defined here as the sum of two components: the cost elements — \"the value of the constant and variable capital consumed\" — plus a profit determined by the general rate of profit calculated on the total capital advanced, whether used up or not . That last clause is a technical precision of real importance. Marx distinguishes between the capital consumed (the portion actually used up in production, which enters the cost price) and the capital advanced (the total capital tied up, whether used up or not, upon which the profit rate is calculated). This distinction recurs when the waterfall manufacturer's \"cost price\" falls from 100 to 90 while his profit is still reckoned on a capital of £100. The concept of cost price (k) therefore denotes the value of consumed constant and variable capital, while the price of production (P) = k + the average profit on total capital advanced. Having set this premise, Marx states the problematic with maximum clarity: \"The question then arises how a ground-rent can develop on this assumption, i.e. how a portion of profit can be transformed into ground-rent, so that a part of the commodity price thus accrues to the landowner.\" The wording transformed is deliberate and anticipates the chapter's conclusion: differential rent does not add anything to the commodity's value; it redistributes a surplus profit that already exists. 38.2 The Heuristic Model: Steam versus Waterfall and the Price of Production \"To demonstrate the general character of this form of ground-rent,\" Marx asks us to assume a country in which factories are powered \"predominantly by steam-engines, but a certain minority by natural waterfalls.\" He assigns numbers: the branches of industry using steam have a production price of 115 for a quantity of commodities for which a capital of 100 is consumed, the 15 per cent profit being calculated on the total capital applied, not merely on the consumed capital of 100. He then immediately qualifies the status of this number: \"the particular numerical figures are completely immaterial here.\" Two conceptual clarifications follow. First, the production price is \"determined not by the individual cost price of any one industrialist producing by himself, but rather by the price that the commodity costs on average under the average conditions for capital in that whole sphere of production.\" It is thus the market price of production — the average market price considered as distinct from its oscillations. Second, Marx notes that this price appears \"always in the form of the market price,\" and specifically as the governing market price . It is through this form that the nature of commodity value presents itself. 38.3 The Digression on Socially Necessary Labour-Time At this point Marx inserts a compact but foundational statement of value theory. Commodity value is \"determined not by the labour-time needed by a certain individual producer to produce a certain quantity of a commodity... but by the socially necessary labour-time.\" He defines this as \"the labour-time required under the given average social conditions of production to produce the total socially required quantity of the species of commodity available on the market.\" This digression is not ornamental. It does two jobs. First, it explains why the waterfall producer's privately cheaper commodity still sells at the general price: value is social, not individual. Second, it grounds the later insistence that ordinary industrial surplus profits are self-abolishing — because the law of socially necessary labour-time exerts \"a compulsion to produce commodities under the same favourable conditions,\" it constantly erodes the advantage of any particular producer. The abstract theory of value is thus the hidden engine driving the concrete conclusion about rent. 38.4 The Waterfall's Cost Price and the Birth of Surplus Profit Marx now introduces the differential: the cost price in the water-powered factories comes to only 90 instead of 100. Since the production price governing the market is 115, and the profit is 15 per cent, the water-powered factories \"will also sell at 115, i.e. at the market price as governed by the average price.\" Their profit consequently amounts to 25 instead of 15 — a surplus profit of 10 per cent. This arises, Marx stresses, \"not because they sell their commodities above the price of production but because they sell them at this price,\" because their capital functions under exceptionally favourable conditions \"that stand above the average level prevailing in this sphere.\" Note the structural elegance: the surplus profit is generated entirely on the side of production (lower cost), yet realized entirely on the side of circulation (selling at the general price). Neither element alone would suffice. The market does not reward the waterfall producer for virtue; it simply fails to lower its price to his level. 38.5 What Is Immediately Evident (I): The Two Limits of the Difference \"Two things are immediately evident here.\" The first is that the surplus profit behaves at first exactly like any other surplus profit — not \"the chance result of transactions in the circulation process, of accidental fluctuations in market price.\" It equals the difference between the individual price of production of the favoured producers and the general social price of production governing the market — that is, \"the excess of the general production price of the commodity over its individual production price.\" Marx then specifies the two governing limits of this excess: on one side the individual cost price (hence the individual production price), on the other the general production price . The mechanical derivation follows: the value of the waterfall-produced commodities is lower because less labour is required — \"less labour enters in the objectified form, as a portion of the constant capital.\" The labour applied is more productive: it needs a smaller quantity of objectified labour (no coal to be paid for) and a smaller quantity of living labour (\"since the water-wheel does not need to be heated\"). The industrialist experiences this as a lower cost price for him personally: he has less objectified labour to pay for and fewer wages for less living labour-power. Consequently his individual production price is lower. Marx gives the arithmetic explicitly: the general ratio 100 : 115 = 90 : 103½, so his individual production price is \"only 103½ instead of 115.\" Here a subtle internal tension deserves flagging. If the general capital advanced is 100 and profit is reckoned on total capital advanced, then an individual cost price of 90 with a 15 per cent profit on the advanced capital of 100 yields an individual production price of 105 , and a surplus profit of 115 − 105 = 10 — which is precisely the figure Marx uses everywhere else in the chapter (\"a surplus profit of 10 per cent\"; the £10 paid to the landowner). The parenthetical proportional calculation yields 103½ and hence a difference of 11½. Marx himself defuses this: \"the particular numerical figures are completely immaterial here.\" The conceptual point stands regardless — the surplus profit equals the difference between the individual and the general production price, and that difference is bounded by the two magnitudes named. The bound matters because it makes the surplus profit conditional . If coal becomes cheaper, the difference between the waterfall producer's individual cost price and the general one declines, \"and so therefore does his surplus profit.\" If he had to sell at his individual value or at the production price determined by it, \"the difference would disappear.\" 38.6 The Social Result: Productivity of Labour Presented as Productivity of Capital Marx closes this first point with a decisive social observation. The surplus profit is the result, on the one hand, of the commodity being sold at its general market price — \"the price at which competition balances the individual prices\" — and on the other hand, of the fact that \"the greater individual productivity of the labour that he sets in motion does not benefit the workers, but, like the productivity of labour in general, their employer; i.e. it presents itself as the productivity of capital.\" This is a compressed restatement of the theory of surplus value: the gain from heightened productivity accrues to whoever owns the means of production. The waterfall's advantage is not a technical accident but a social relation — the natural force becomes \"the productivity of capital\" because capital owns the labour process. And since one limit of the surplus profit is the general price of production, whose component the general rate of profit is, the surplus profit \"can arise only from the difference between the general and the individual production prices, and hence from the difference between the individual and the general rate of profit. An excess over and above this difference would presuppose the sale of the product above the price of production governed by the market.\" 38.7 What Is Immediately Evident (II): Identity with Ordinary Surplus Profit The second evident point is that, so far, the waterfall manufacturer's surplus profit \"has not so far been distinguished in any way from all other surplus profit.\" Marx defines normal surplus profit (excluding accidental business deals or market fluctuations) as determined by the difference between the individual production price of the commodities produced by this particular capital and the general production price that governs the market — for the total capital invested in the sphere. Up to this point the waterfall case is merely an instance of the general formula. Then comes the pivot, announced in one of the chapter's most effective rhetorical moves: \"But now comes the difference.\" Marx poses the question directly: \"To what circumstances does the manufacturer in the present case owe his surplus profit?\" 38.8 Nature's Gift versus Capital's Achievement The first answer is: a natural force — the motive power of water, \"which is provided by nature itself and is not itself the product of labour, unlike the coal that transforms water into steam, which has value and must be paid an equivalent, i.e. costs something.\" The waterfall is \"a natural agent of production, and no labour goes into creating it.\" But Marx immediately refuses to let this stand as a sufficient explanation. \"But this is not all.\" The manufacturer operating a steam-engine also applies natural forces that cost him nothing — the ability of water to change its aggregate state, the elasticity of steam — and these make labour more productive and, insofar as they cheapen the production of the workers' means of subsistence, increase surplus-value and hence profit. The monopolization of natural forces is thus common to all capital operating with steam-engines. Such monopolization may increase the general rate of profit, but it \"does not create any surplus profit, for this consists precisely in the excess of individual profit over and above the average profit.\" A general advantage is a general advantage; it raises the average, not the deviation from it. An editorial note here corrects the text: the phrase \"the increase in labour-power that they bring about\" should presumably read \"the productivity of labour.\" This is worth noting because the correction preserves the coherence of the argument — what the natural force increases is the productivity of labour, not labour-power itself. Marx draws the conclusion pointedly: if in our case the application of water-power does create surplus profit, \"this cannot arise simply from the fact that the increased productivity of labour is due here to the use of a natural force. Further modifying factors must intervene.\" A footnote at this juncture refers the reader to an Inquiry of 1821 (London) written against Malthus — signaling that Marx is consciously composing his account of surplus profit in polemical opposition to Malthusian rent theory, even where he does not spell out the disagreement. 38.9 The Normal Sources of Surplus Profit and Their Self-Abolition Marx now broadens the field to enumerate the ordinary sources of surplus profit within a sphere, in order to demonstrate by contrast what is unlike the waterfall case. Divergences in profit rate between particular spheres are constantly balanced out into an average; what remains, apart from accidental divergences, arises from a reduction in the cost price due to either: Scale of capital applied — capital operating on a greater-than-average scale reduces the faux frais of production (the incidental or overhead costs), and the general causes of rising labour productivity (cooperation, division of labour) can operate \"to a greater extent and with more intensity, because over a larger field of operation\"; or Superior methods — \"better methods of work, new inventions, improved machines, trade secrets in chemistry, etc.\" — the application of new, improved, above-average means and methods of production. The critical characterization follows: \"The reason for the surplus profit in this case is thus inherent in the capital itself (including the labour that it sets in motion).\" Whether from sheer magnitude of capital or from more efficient application, \"nothing inherently prevents all capital in the same sphere of production from being invested in the same way.\" Competition between capitals \"actually tends to the contrary, it tends to cancel out these distinctions more and more.\" The determination of value by socially necessary labour-time inexorably produces the cheapening of commodities and \"the compulsion to produce commodities under the same favourable conditions.\" What begins as an exceptional advantage becomes the norm, and the surplus profit evaporates. This is why such advantages are \"cancelled out as soon as equally large amounts of capital are employed in the average case,\" or \"as soon as the exceptional manner of production becomes universal, or is overtaken by one still more advanced.\" 38.10 The Waterfall's Exceptional Character: Monopolizable, Place-Bound, Non-Reproducible \"Things take a different form with the surplus profit of the manufacturer who makes use of the waterfall.\" Here Marx delivers the analytic core of the chapter. The heightened productivity of his labour arises \"neither from the capital and labour themselves nor from the simple application of a natural force distinct from capital and labour but incorporated into the capital.\" It arises from \"the greater natural productivity of a labour linked with the use of a natural force, but a natural force that is not available to all capital in the same sphere of production, as is for example the elasticity of steam.\" The contrast is stark and decisive. Elasticity of steam is available to any capital as soon as it invests in the sphere; water-power is not. What is at issue is \"a monopolizable natural force which, like the waterfall, is available only to those who have at their disposal particular pieces of the earth's surface and their appurtenances.\" Marx insists: \"It is in no way just up to the capital to call into being this natural condition of greater labour productivity, in the way that any capital can transform water into steam.\" The condition exists \"in nature only at certain places, and where it is not found it cannot be produced by a particular capital outlay.\" It is not bound up with products labour can produce — machines, coal — but \"with particular natural conditions on particular pieces of land.\" This gives rise to exclusion : \"Those manufacturers who possess waterfalls exclude those who do not possess them from employing this natural force, because land is limited, and still more so land endowed with water-power.\" Marx then acknowledges a genuine complication with intellectual honesty. It is \"not ruled out\" that although the number of natural waterfalls in a country is limited, the amount of water-power industry can use may still be increased — a waterfall can be artificially channelled, a water-wheel improved, turbines substituted where the ordinary wheel is unsuited. This concession does not weaken but sharpens the argument, because it shows that the barrier is not a fixed quantity of physical energy but the exclusive ownership of particular sites . Hence: \"Possession of this natural force forms a monopoly in the hands of its owner, a condition of higher productivity for the capital invested, which cannot be produced by capital's own production process; the natural force that can be monopolized in this way is always chained to the earth.\" It belongs neither to \"the general conditions of the sphere of production in question\" nor to \"those of its conditions that are generally reproducible.\" 38.11 Landed Property and the Transformation of Surplus Profit into Ground-Rent Having established the existence of a monopoly, Marx introduces the legal and social form that converts it into rent. \"If we now imagine that these waterfalls, together with the land on which they are located, are in the hands of subjects who are accepted as the proprietors of these portions of the globe, as landowners, then these are in a position to prevent the application of capital to the waterfall and its utilization by capital.\" Their power is essentially negative: \"They can either allow this use or refuse it.\" But capital cannot conjure a waterfall from its own resources. The conclusion follows: \"The surplus profit that arises from this use of the waterfall thus arises not from the capital but rather from the use by capital of a monopolizable and monopolized natural force. Under these conditions, the surplus profit is transformed into ground-rent, i.e. it accrues to the owner of the waterfall.\" Marx illustrates with concrete magnitudes. If the manufacturer pays the landowner £10 a year for the waterfall, his profit comes to £15 — \"15 per cent on the £100 which is now the amount of his production costs.\" (The £100 comprises his £90 real cost plus the £10 rent, which now enters his individual cost accounting.) He remains \"in a position just as good as, if not better than, the other capitalists in his sphere.\" And crucially: \"Nothing is altered if the capitalist owns the waterfall himself. He still draws the surplus profit of £10 not as a capitalist, but as the owner of the waterfall; and for the precise reason that this excess arises not from his capital as such, but rather from his disposal over a natural force that is limited in scope, separable from his capital, and monopolizable, it is transformed into ground-rent.\" The three defining traits of the monopoly — limited , separable from capital , monopolizable — are the criteria that separate ground-rent from ordinary surplus profit. Ownership of the waterfall and ownership of capital are conceptually distinct titles, even when united in one person. 38.12 The Five Conclusions Marx now consolidates the argument into five numbered propositions. First: \"this rent is always a differential rent.\" It does not contribute to determining the general production price of the commodity but \"takes this as given.\" It always arises from the difference between the individual production price of the particular capital that commands the monopolized natural force and the general production price for capital invested in the sphere. The defining marker of differential rent is therefore parasitic passivity with respect to price formation: it presupposes the general price and feeds on deviation from it. Second: the rent derives from no absolute rise in productivity. Such a rise \"can only ever reduce the value of commodities.\" What is at issue is \"the greater relative returns from certain particular capitals invested in a sphere of production, as compared with those capital investments that are excluded from these exceptional, favourable conditions of productivity which have been created by nature.\" Marx illustrates with a counterfactual: if the use of steam conferred an overwhelming advantage — even though coal costs money and water-power costs nothing — and that advantage more than compensated for coal's cost, \"water-power would not be used and could not produce any surplus profit, nor therefore any rent.\" This is an elegant reminder that differential rent depends on the relative superiority of the privileged producer; if the privileged position were not superior, the rent would vanish. Third: the natural force is the basis , not the source , of the surplus profit. This is the most theoretically dense of the five. The natural force provides \"the natural basis of the exceptionally increased productivity of labour.\" Marx then sets out a general principle of political economy: \"Use-value is altogether the bearer of exchange-value but not its cause. If the same use-value could be obtained without labour, it would have no exchange-value.\" Conversely, \"a thing cannot have exchange-value without having use-value, i.e. without being such a natural bearer of labour.\" The waterfall is a use-value — a bearer of productivity — but it is not a source of value, because value is human labour in its social determination. Marx then completes the thought with a conditional that ties the argument back to his value theory: \"If the various different values did not balance out into production prices and the various individual production prices into a general production price that governs the market, a rise in labour productivity resulting from the use of a waterfall would simply lower the price of the commodities produced with the waterfall without raising the portion of profit contained in these commodities.\" Value theory and the theory of price of production are therefore joint preconditions of differential rent. Without the equalization of profit rates, the waterfall's advantage would benefit consumers rather than producers. And in the same manner, \"increased labour productivity in general would not be transformed into surplus-value if capital did not appropriate as its own the productive power, natural and social, of the labour applied.\" Fourth: landed property does not create the surplus profit. \"This surplus profit exists even if there is no landed property, if for example the land on which the waterfall is located can be used by the manufacturer as unclaimed land.\" Landed property simply enables the landowner \"to entice this surplus profit out of the manufacturer's pocket and into his own. It is not the cause of this surplus profit's creation, but simply of its transformation into the form of ground-rent, hence of the appropriation of this portion of profit or commodity price by the landowner or waterfall-owner.\" This is the chapter's most important negative claim, directed against any theory that treats private property in land as the origin of rent. Property in land is the cause of appropriation , not of creation . Fifth: the price of the waterfall. If the landowner sells it — to a third party or to the manufacturer himself — the price \"does not at first go directly into the production price of the commodities concerned, even though it does go into the individual price for the manufacturer.\" For rent in this case arises from the production price of those commodities of the same kind produced by steam-engines, \"which is determined independently of the waterfall.\" 38.13 The Price of the Waterfall: Capitalization and the \"Irrational Expression\" From the fifth proposition Marx develops a striking critique of landed-price fetishism. \"The price of the waterfall, besides, is altogether an irrational expression concealing a real economic relationship.\" The reasoning flows directly from the labour theory of value: \"The waterfall, like the earth in general and every natural force, has no value, since it represents no objectified labour and hence no price, this being in the normal case nothing but value expressed in money. Where there is no value, there is eo ipso nothing to be expressed in money.\" What then is the price of a waterfall? \"This price is nothing but capitalized rent.\" Marx unfolds the mechanism: \"Landed property enables the proprietor to lay hold of the difference between the individual profit and the average profit; the profit captured in this way, which is renewed every year, can be capitalized and then appears as the price of the natural force itself.\" His worked example is exact: if the surplus profit is £10 per year and the average interest rate is 5 per cent , then this £10 represents the interest on a capital of £200 . \"This capitalization of the annual £10 that the waterfall empowers its owner to extract from the manufacturer then appears as the capital value of the waterfall itself.\" The irrationality of this expression is then exposed by an internal contradiction in the calculation. The \"price\" of £200 \"simply expresses the product of the surplus profit of £10 multiplied by twenty years\" — that is, the capitalization formula 10/0.05 = 200 corresponds to twenty years' purchase. But \"if circumstances remain otherwise the same, the same waterfall actually enables its owner to extract this annual £10 for an indefinite time, thirty or a hundred years.\" Conversely, Marx adds the equally asymmetrical possibility on the downside: \"if a new method of production that cannot make use of water-power lowers the cost price of the commodities produced by steam from £100 to £90, the surplus profit would disappear, and the rent and the price of the waterfall along with it.\" The capitalized \"value\" of the waterfall is thus a fiction pinned to a contingent, mutable social and technical differential — a differential which the very progress of capitalist technique may abolish. What appears as the intrinsic worth of a piece of nature is a capitalized claim on a social surplus profit, dependent for its very existence on the continuing backwardness of the excluded capitals. 38.14 Transition to Agriculture The chapter closes with a programmatic hand-off: \"Now that we have established the general concept of differential rent in this way, we turn to consider this rent in agriculture proper. What will be said of agriculture applies on the whole also to mining.\" The waterfall has served its heuristic purpose. It was never itself meant to be a typical case of ground-rent; it is a clean, minimal example that isolates the differential mechanism by stripping away all complicating factors such as soil fertility differences and their relation to successive investments of capital. The general concept — surplus profit born of a monopolizable, place-bound natural differential, transformed into ground-rent by landed property — is now available to be applied to the far murkier terrain of agricultural rent proper, where differential rent will be shown to operate under both extensive and intensive forms of cultivation. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter yields a set of principles that stand as fundamental laws of Marx's theory of ground-rent and, more broadly, of his critique of distribution. Differential rent is a surplus profit, not a price surcharge. Its distinguishing feature is passivity before the price system: it \"takes the general production price as given\" and feeds on the deviation of an individual price of production below that standard. This is the deep reason why differential rent can coexist with the law of value. Anyone who claims that rent is merely a monopoly mark-up on consumers, or a deduction from normal profit, is describing something else — some non-differential form of rent — not this. The natural basis of rent is not its source. The waterfall is a use-value, a bearer of productivity; it is not a source of value. This distinction, expressed in the formula \"use-value is altogether the bearer of exchange-value but not its cause,\" blocks any naturalistic or physiocratic theory that traces value (or rent) to the earth's bounty. Nature gives differential advantages in productivity, but only the social appropriation of labour — capital's monopolization of labour's productive power — turns those advantages into surplus-value. The natural force is the occasion , not the cause . Landed property appropriates rather than creates. No claim in the chapter is more consequential. The surplus profit exists whether or not the waterfall has an owner; unclaimed land would still yield it. Landed property's distinctive power is the ability to exclude — to \"either allow this use or refuse it\" — and therefore to divert an already-existing surplus into rent. This theorem disarms at a stroke all doctrines that would treat rent as a payment for a contribution made by the landowner. The landowner contributes nothing to production; he commands a position from which nothing can be produced without his permission. Ordinary surplus profits are self-abolishing; natural ones are not (as long as the monopoly persists). The chapter's contrast between steam and waterfall is ultimately a contrast between advantages inherent in capital and advantages chained to the earth. Capital-borne advantages — scale, improved machinery, better methods, trade secrets — are \"cancelled out\" by competition's tendency to equalize conditions, because \"nothing inherently prevents all capital in the same sphere of production from being invested in the same way.\" But the waterfall is available only at certain places and cannot be produced by capital outlay. Its scarcity is not a temporary imperfection of competition but a permanent structural feature. This makes differential rent far more durable than industrial surplus profit — yet not eternal: a technological revolution that renders water-power superfluous would lower steam costs, eliminate the differential, \"and the rent and the price of the waterfall along with it.\" The price of land is capitalized rent — a fetish. The chapter's closing movement supplies one of Marx's sharpest illustrations of fetishism. The waterfall has no value, because it embodies no labour. Yet it acquires a price, which is discovered to be simply the annual surplus profit divided by the interest rate — that is, a capitalized claim on a social relation. The fact that the capitalization reflects twenty years' purchase while the rent stream may run for thirty or a hundred years, or collapse to nothing overnight, exposes the \"price\" as a reckoning rooted in expectations, conventions, and the prevailing rate of interest rather than in any property intrinsic to the thing. What appears as the value of nature is the mirror image of a surplus extracted from labour. Theoretical context and forward trajectory. This chapter is the conceptual hinge of Part VI. It establishes the abstract form of differential rent before the concrete complications of agriculture are introduced. Its method is exemplary: begin from the strictest possible assumption (products sell at prices of production), construct a minimal model, extract the general concept, and only then proceed to historical and empirical complexity. The reader who has followed the argument now possesses the analytical instrument — surplus profit + land monopoly + landed property — that will be applied to the two forms of agricultural differential rent and to absolute rent, and that underpins Marx's critique of Ricardo, Malthus, and the rent theories of classical political economy. The chapter's philosophical message is that under capitalism even the gifts of nature become sources of private revenue — not because nature is productive of value, but because a class of owners stands, by historical and legal arrangement, at the gateway through which everything produced from the earth must pass. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 37 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 39"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 39", "title": "Chapter 39", "subtitle": "First Form of Differential Rent (Differential Rent I)", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 39/Chapter 39 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 15417, "read_time": "77 min read", "tags": ["Differential Rent (I & II)"], "keywords": ["Differential Rent I: Soil Fertility and Location", "Market Value Governed by Worst Cultivated Soil", "Surplus-Profit on Superior Soils Transformed into Rent I", "Differences in Transport Costs and Urban Proximity", "Equal Capital Advances Yielding Unequal Physical Outputs"], "text": "Chapter 39 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 15,696 words ⏱️ ~78 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION: CHAPTER 39 — THE FIRST FORM OF DIFFERENTIAL RENT (DIFFERENTIAL RENT I) 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem Chapter 39 of Volume III of Capital opens a new territory in Marx's analysis: the transformation of surplus profit into ground-rent . Up to this point in the volume, Marx has traced the formation of the general rate of profit, the equalization of profit across branches of production, and the emergence of commercial and interest-bearing capital. Now he confronts a form of revenue that appears, on the surface, to arise from the soil itself — from nature's bounty rather than from the exploitation of labour. The chapter's task is to dissolve that appearance and demonstrate that ground-rent, in its first and simplest form, is nothing but a differential surplus profit — the excess of the profit realized on better land over the profit realized on the worst land in cultivation, which the landowner appropriates by virtue of the legal monopoly of landed property. The chapter's core thesis is that differential rent is not a payment for the productive power of the soil as such, but a social relation masquerading as a natural one. It arises whenever equal amounts of capital and labour are applied to equal areas of land with unequal results, provided that (a) the worst soil in cultivation governs the market price, and (b) landed property exists as a form that can intercept the resulting surplus. The chapter thus performs a double operation: it simultaneously explains the economic mechanics of differential rent (a rigorously worked-out system of numerical tables) and exposes the ideological illusion that rent is a natural tribute rather than a socially determined appropriation. The Intellectual Objective Marx has three interlocking objectives. First , he must correct and generalize Ricardo's law of rent. Ricardo had defined rent as the difference between the produce obtained by the employment of two equal quantities of capital and labour — but, as Marx immediately notes, Ricardo omitted the crucial clause \"on equal quantities of land.\" Without that qualification, the statement confuses ground-rent with surplus profit in general. Marx's correction is not pedantic; it isolates the precise condition under which surplus profit becomes rent: the simultaneous application of equal capitals to equal land areas . Second , Marx must demolish the \"Malthusian\" narrative — shared by West, Malthus, and Ricardo alike — that rent necessarily arises from a progression to ever-worse soil and therefore expresses a declining natural fertility of agriculture. Against this, Marx demonstrates through three systematically varied tables that differential rent can arise under an ascending progression (worse to better soil), a descending progression (better to worse), or a criss-cross movement, and can coexist with falling prices, stationary prices, or rising prices. He also — crucially — demonstrates that rent can rise while prices fall , and can fall per acre while the total rental rises . Third , and most profoundly, Marx demonstrates that differential rent rests on a \"false social value\" — a market value imposed by the competition of capitals on the basis of the capitalist mode of production, which is above the actual labour-time embodied in the agricultural product. This opens the way to the chapter's most radical conclusion: that the economic basis for a class of landowners would disappear under a consciously planned association of producers, because such a society would not pay 2½ times the labour-time actually contained in the grain. The Chapter's Place in the Broader Framework This chapter belongs to Marx's systematic refutation of the trinity formula — the ideological representation of capitalist society as consisting of three independent sources of revenue: land yields rent, capital yields profit, labour yields wages. By demonstrating that ground-rent in its first form is merely a transformed surplus profit — a portion of surplus value already generated by the exploitation of wage-labour — Marx severs the apparent link between the soil and rent. The soil is a condition of agricultural production, but it is not a source of value. Only labour creates value; the fertility of the soil merely determines how that value is distributed among farmers and landlords, and how much the consuming society is made to overpay. The chapter is also a bridge. It explicitly announces itself as preparing \"differential rent II\" — the case where successive doses of capital are invested on the same land rather than on different lands. The extensive analysis of Tables Ia, Ib, and Ic — in which Marx holds rent per acre constant while varying the proportions in which different soil types compose the total cultivated area — functions as the transition. It reveals a variable that Marx claims had been \"completely overlooked, in a quite striking fashion\": the composition of the cultivated area as a determinant of the average rent and the rate of rent, entirely independent of the price of grain or the differential fertility of any particular soil. Finally, the chapter's closing sections — on the price of untilled land, on colonial agriculture, on the historical examples of English grain prices from the seventeenth to the nineteenth century — extend the analysis into the domains of land speculation, imperial and colonial political economy, and economic history . The discussion of Michigan and New York, the Erie Canal, and the export of wheat from superficially cultivated prairie soil, anticipates the twentieth-century debates over the \"frontier,\" land rent in settler colonies, and the specific form of agricultural surplus in the world market. In this respect the chapter is not merely a technical treatise on rent; it is a demonstration of the historical and geographical variability of the conditions under which differential rent arises — and therefore a refutation of any attempt to elevate Ricardo's law into a universal natural law. The intellectual objective, in sum, is to show that differential rent is a historically specific, socially produced magnitude whose level depends not on any single \"law of nature,\" but on the interaction of: the chemical and mechanical development of agriculture; the pattern in which cultivation extends across soil types; the distribution of total capital among those types; the level of demand; the level of the market price as governed by the worst soil in cultivation; and the legal existence of landed property as a claim on surplus profit. The chapter is thus simultaneously an exercise in exact economic modelling and a critique of political economy's naturalization of social relations. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 Opening Corrections to Ricardo: Rent as Differential Product on Equal Areas Marx opens the chapter by quoting Ricardo's Principles : \"Rent is always the difference between the produce obtained by the employment of two equal quantities of capital and labour.\" He calls this statement \"completely correct\" — but only within a specific restriction. Ricardo, Marx explains, is here assuming that no other rent exists besides differential rent (that is, no absolute rent, no monopoly rent). Marx immediately supplies the correction that Ricardo himself failed to make: the statement should read \"on equal quantities of land,\" since Ricardo is dealing with ground-rent and not with surplus profit in general. This distinction is foundational. Surplus profit , Marx defines, is — when produced in normal conditions and not as a by-product of fortuitous circumstances in circulation — \"always produced as the difference between the product of two equal amounts of capital and labour.\" But surplus profit becomes ground-rent only when two equal amounts of capital and labour are employed on equal areas of land with unequal results . The specificity of the agricultural case lies in the equality of area and the inequality of yield. This double condition is what allows landed property to intervene and appropriate the excess as rent. Marx then adds an important qualification that complicates the simple Ricardian picture. It is \"by no means necessarily required\" that the surplus profit should arise from unequal results of equal capitals. Capitals of different size could also be employed in different investments, and this is even the general assumption. What is essential is that equal proportionate parts — for instance £100 of each — give unequal results; that is, the rate of profit varies . This is the general precondition for the existence of surplus profit in any sphere of capital investment whatsoever. The second thing, Marx insists, is the transformation of this surplus profit into the form of ground-rent — \"of rent in general, as a form distinct from profit.\" And this transformation is not automatic; it must be investigated: \"when, how and under what circumstances this transformation takes place.\" The conceptual architecture being laid down here is crucial for everything that follows. There are two analytically separable questions: (1) the origin of surplus profit (unequal profitability of equal capital advances in different spheres or on different lands), and (2) the form of ground-rent (the legal-economic mechanism by which landed property intercepts that surplus). The chapter's whole argument depends on keeping these distinct. The existence of differential surplus profit does not by itself produce rent; what converts it into rent is the existence of landed property as a barrier that prevents the surplus from being competed away. 2.2 The Second Ricardo Quote and Marx's Expanded List of Causes of Fertility Inequality Marx next turns to a second statement of Ricardo's, which he again endorses in so far as it is restricted to differential rent : \"Whatever diminishes the inequality in the produce obtained from successive portions of capital on the same or on new land, tends to lower rent; and whatever increases that inequality, necessarily produces an opposite effect, and tends to raise it.\" Marx then immediately expands the list of causes that produce such inequality. Ricardo, in the accepted reading, was limited to fertility and location . Marx, however, enumerates three additional causes: (1) The distribution of taxes , according to whether this is uniform or not. In England, where taxation is not centralized and the tax is levied on the land itself and not on the rent, the resulting inequality is always present. A tax levied uniformly on land of different fertility will proportionally burden worse soil far more heavily than better soil, altering the pattern of profitability and thus the differential. A tax levied on rent, by contrast, would fall only on the surplus itself and would not (directly) alter the margin of cultivation. (2) The inequalities resulting from the differential development of agriculture in different parts of the country. Agriculture, Marx explains, is — because of its traditional character — \"more difficult to equalize than is manufacture.\" The implication is that in manufacture, competition and the mobility of capital tend to equalize conditions of production across regions far more rapidly than in agriculture, where local custom, technique, and soil-management practice diverge. (3) The uneven way in which capital is divided among the farmers. Different farmers on different pieces of land possess different quantities and efficiencies of capital; this unevenness itself produces differential results. Marx then adds a historical observation that is decisive for the argument: because \"the capitalist mode of production's seizure of agriculture, the transformation of the independently operating peasant into a wage-labourer, is in fact the final conquest of this mode of production,\" these inequalities are greater here than in any other branch of industry. Agriculture is the last sector to be fully subsumed under capital; consequently, pre-capitalist residues of uneven development persist longest there. This is an important nuance: differential rent is not a \"natural\" constant but is historically intensified by the very backwardness of agriculture relative to manufacture. Having laid these preliminaries, Marx announces his procedure: he will first \"summarize quite briefly the particular features of my development in contrast with that of Ricardo, etc.\" 2.3 Fertility and Location: The Two Non-Capital Causes of Differential Product Marx begins his own development by specifying the analytical starting point: the unequal products of equal amounts of capital applied to different lands of the same area — or, in the case of differently-sized lands, the products of equal acreages. He then identifies two general causes of such unequal products which are independent of capital : (1) Fertility — to be discussed at length, including \"what is meant by the natural fertility of land, and the different aspects this involves.\" (2) Location — decisive in the case of colonies, and decisive everywhere for the sequence in which lands can be successively brought into cultivation. The distinction between fertility and location is analytically clean but practically entangled. Marx notes immediately that these two bases for differential rent \"can operate in opposite directions.\" A piece of land may be very well located but of very low fertility, and vice versa. This is an important fact, Marx says, because it explains why, when the land of a particular country is originally cultivated, it is possible to proceed from worse soil to better as well as the other way round. Location can override fertility, forcing cultivation of poorer land that happens to be closer to markets, rivers, or existing settlements, while more fertile land further away remains untouched. Finally, Marx makes a dialectical observation about the historical trajectory of location as a basis for differential rent. On the one hand, the progress of social production has a levelling effect on location: it creates local markets and improves location by producing means of communication and transport. On the other hand, it increases the differences of geographical location, \"by separating agriculture from manufacture and forming great centres of production, while also relatively isolating the countryside.\" So the development of capitalism cuts both ways: by integrating markets it reduces locational rents; by creating a sharp urban-rural divide, it intensifies them elsewhere. This dialectical formulation forestalls any simple teleological story about the \"inevitable\" decline or rise of locational differentials. 2.4 The Economic Character of \"Natural\" Fertility Marx then sets location aside \"for the moment\" and analyses natural fertility in detail. This is one of the most philosophically sophisticated passages in the chapter, because it destabilizes the very concept of \"natural\" fertility. Apart from climatic and similar aspects, differences in natural fertility are differences in the chemical composition of the soil — variations in the amount of nutrient elements for plants it contains. But even assuming identical chemical composition — and in this sense identical natural fertility — of two areas of land, their actual effective fertility will differ according to how far these nutrient elements occur in readily assimilable form, and can be directly used as plant foodstuffs. The extent to which the same natural fertility can actually be obtained on soils that are naturally equally fertile depends, therefore, in part on the chemical development of agriculture and in part on its mechanical development . Marx draws the decisive conclusion: \"Even though fertility is an objective property of the soil, it thus always involves an economic relation, a relation to the given chemical and mechanical level of agricultural development, and changes with this level of development.\" This is the crucial conceptual move. Fertility is not a pure datum of nature; it is a relational magnitude that shifts as the forces of production develop. Marx gives concrete examples of this historical variability. By chemical means — liquid fertilizers on stiff clayey soil, or the calcination of heavy clayey soil — and by mechanical means — special ploughs for heavy soil, drainage — it is possible to remove obstacles that made soils of equal natural fertility less equal in practice. The sequence followed in bringing different types of soil into cultivation may also be changed: Marx notes that this happened between light sandy soil and heavy clayey soil at one period of English agricultural development, so that the historical order of cultivation was reversed by technical change. He then generalizes: historically, in the successive course of cultivation, there can be both a transition from more fertile soil to less and vice versa. The same can happen as a result of artificially induced improvements in the composition of the soil, or of a mere change in the hierarchy of soil types when various sub-soil conditions come into play — once the subsoil also begins to be tilled and turned over into top layers. This is brought about partly by new agricultural methods (such as the cultivation of fodder grasses) and partly by mechanical means, which either turn the subsoil into the top layer or mix the two together, or else cultivate the subsoil without turning it up. The upshot: \"All these influences on the differential fertility of different soils boil down to the fact that as far as economic fertility is concerned, the level of labour productivity — in this case the ability of agriculture directly to exploit the natural fertility of the soil — an ability that varies with different stages of development — is just as much a factor in the so-called natural fertility of the soil as its chemical composition and other natural properties.\" This is a compressed but powerful claim. The level of labour productivity is a constituent of what appears as the soil's \"natural\" fertility. The hierarchy of soil types — the very ordering A, B, C, D to which Marx will now turn — is thus not a fixed natural given but is calculated \"in relation to this level of development.\" Marx adds that this is \"of course always the case with simultaneous capital investments on different lands.\" The differential rent can therefore exhibit an increasing or decreasing series, for although the series is \"given for the totality of lands actually cultivated,\" these have always been formed by a successive movement. 2.5 The Foundational Numerical Model: Table I and the Anatomy of Differential Rent With the conceptual groundwork complete, Marx constructs the chapter's foundational model. He assumes four types of soil, A, B, C, D , and a price of wheat of £3 = 60 shillings per quarter. Crucially, since we are dealing only with differential rent, this price of 60 shillings per quarter is equal to the production costs on the worst soil — that is, equal to capital advanced plus average profit. Soil A is this worst soil. The assumptions are: - A gives 1 quarter = 60s. for an outlay of 50s., i.e., a profit of 10s., or 20 per cent. - B yields 2 quarters = 120s. for the same outlay of 50s., so total profit = 70s., and the surplus profit (profit above the 10s. average) = 60s. - C yields 3 quarters = 180s. for 50s., total profit = 130s., surplus profit = 120s. - D yields 4 quarters = 240s. for 50s., total profit = 190s., surplus profit = 180s. This yields Table I : Type of soil Capital Product (Qrs) Product (Shillings) Profit (Qrs) Profit (Shillings) Rent (Qrs) Rent (Shillings) A 50 1 60 1 10 — — B 50 2 120 1½ 70 1 60 C 50 3 180 2½ 130 2 120 D 50 4 240 3½ 190 3 180 Total 10 qrs 600s. 6 qrs 360s. The anatomy of rent is now fully visible. Marx computes the respective rents as: - For D : (190 − 10) shillings = 180s., the difference between D and A. - For C : (130 − 10) shillings = 120s., the difference between C and A. - For B : (70 − 10) shillings = 60s., the difference between B and A. - Total rent for B, C, and D = 6 quarters = 360 shillings, the sum of the differences D−A, C−A, and B−A. Two structural points emerge immediately. First , the rent on each soil is not measured against zero profit but against the profit on the worst soil (A's 10s., or 1 quarter). This is essential: in differential rent, the worst soil pays no rent, and its profit is precisely the average profit (20 per cent on 50s.). Second , the total rent is the sum of the differences — it is inherently a relative magnitude, arising from the inequality of yields, not from any absolute property of the soil. 2.6 The Three Possible Paths of Formation: Descending, Ascending, Criss-Cross Series Marx emphasizes that this series, \"which represents a given product in a given condition,\" can occur in the abstract just as well as a decreasing series (from D down to A, from more fertile to ever less fertile soil) or as an increasing one (from A to D, from relatively infertile soil to ever more fertile soil). He also allows for a fluctuating, criss-cross movement — for example from D to C, C to A, and A to B. And he has already given reasons why this may also be the case in actual fact. He then traces out the decreasing series in detail. The process is as follows. The price per quarter gradually rises from, say, 15 shillings to 60 shillings. Once the 4 quarters produced by D (which we may consider as millions) are no longer sufficient, the wheat price rises to the point at which the missing supply can be obtained from C — that is, the price would have to rise to 20 shillings per quarter. When the wheat price rises to 30 shillings per quarter, B can be brought into cultivation; and when it rises to 60 shillings per quarter, so can A, without any need for the capital applied to make do with a rate of profit lower than 20 per cent. Under this descending sequence, the rent for D evolves dramatically: - First, 5 shillings per quarter = 20 shillings for the 4 quarters D produces. - Then 15 shillings per quarter = 60 shillings. - Finally 45 shillings per quarter = 180 shillings for 4 quarters. Marx then introduces a subtle point about the profit rate. If the profit rate for D was also 20 per cent to start with, its total profit on the 4 quarters would be only 10 shillings, but this would represent more corn at a price of 15 shillings than at a price of 60 shillings. Since corn goes into the reproduction of labour-power, and one portion of each quarter must replace labour-power and another part constant capital, the surplus-value would therefore be higher under this assumption, and so too, with other factors remaining the same, would the rate of profit. Marx flags that \"the question of the profit rate will have to be specially investigated in more detail\" — a characteristic deferral that signals the interconnection of this analysis with the broader theory of the falling rate of profit. He then traces the ascending sequence . If the process began with A, then as soon as new agricultural land had to be taken into cultivation, the price per quarter would rise above 60 shillings; but since the supply needed would come from B — a supply of 2 quarters — it would fall again to 60 shillings. For while B produces at 30 shillings per quarter, it sells at 60 shillings, since its supply is only just sufficient to meet the demand. A rent is thus formed for B which comes initially to 60 shillings, and similarly for C and D — still on the assumption that even though C and D supply at 20 shillings and 15 shillings per quarter respectively, the market price remains 60 shillings, since the supply of the one quarter that A provides is needed to satisfy the total demand. Marx then clarifies the crucial difference between the two sequences in terms of how the cultivated area expands : \"In this case the rise in demand above the supply that was satisfied first by A, and then by A and B, would not have meant that B, C and D could successively be cultivated, but simply that the overall cultivated area would be expanded, and it might so happen that the more fertile lands came under cultivation only later.\" In other words, the ascending sequence does not require that superior soils be \"discovered\" in order; it requires only the extension of cultivation , which may move erratically across soil types. 2.7 The Movement of the Profit Rate Under Rising vs Stationary Prices Marx now draws out the divergent behaviour of the profit rate in the two sequences. In the first (descending) sequence : rents rise with the increase in price, and the profit rate declines. This decline could be completely or partially offset by countervailing circumstances — a point Marx defers for detailed treatment later. But he issues a critical theoretical reminder: \"the general rate of profit is not uniformly determined by the surplus-value in all spheres of production. It is not agricultural profit that determines industrial, but vice versa.\" This is a direct polemic against the Physiocratic and Ricardian tendency to treat agriculture as the \"primitive\" sphere whose profitability sets the tone for the economy. Marx's position is the reverse: agriculture is subordinated to the general rate of profit set in industry. In the second (ascending) sequence : the profit rate on the capital invested remains the same. The mass of profit is represented by less corn, but the relative price of corn compared with other commodities will have risen. It is just that the increase in profit — where there is such an increase — \"instead of flowing into the pockets of the industrialist farmers as a growing profit, separates off from profit in the form of rent.\" The price of corn remains stationary under the assumptions made here. In other words, in the ascending sequence the differential surplus that emerges is not absorbed into the general profit rate; it is siphoned off as rent. This is a vivid illustration of how landed property functions as a barrier to the equalization of the rate of profit in agriculture . Marx concludes this stage of the argument with a summary: \"The development and growth of differential rent remains the same, both when prices remain the same and when they rise, and both when there is a steady progression from worse soil to better and when there is a steady regression from better soil to worse.\" The pattern of formation is indifferent to the direction of movement; what matters is the inequality of soils. 2.8 Table II: Falling Prices, Criss-Cross Cultivation, and the Counter-Intuitive Fall in Money Rent Marx now sets aside his earlier simplifying assumptions — (1) that the price rises in the one sequence and remains stationary in the other, and (2) that there is a steady progression from better soil to worse or conversely. He introduces a new and much more complex scenario. Assume the demand for grain rises from the original 10 quarters to 17 quarters. Further assume that the worst soil A is displaced by another soil which yields 1½ quarters for a production cost of 60 shillings (50s. costs plus 10s. representing a profit of 20 per cent), its production price per quarter thus being 45s. Alternatively, that the old soil A has been improved as a result of rational cultivation, or has been cultivated more productively at constant costs — for example by the introduction of clover — so that its product rises to 1½ quarters for the same advance of capital. (Note: Marx offers here two distinct mechanisms for the same result — displacement by a new soil, or improvement of the old one.) Further assume that soil types B, C, and D supply the same product afterwards as before, but that new types of soil come into cultivation: A' with a fertility between A and B, and B' and B\" with fertilities between B and C. Under these assumptions, three phenomena occur: First : the production price of a quarter of wheat, or its governing market price, would fall from 60s. to 45s., or by 25 per cent. Second : there would be at the same time a progression both from more fertile to less fertile soil and from less fertile soil to more fertile. Soil A' is more fertile than A, but less fertile than B, C, and D, which were cultivated previously; and B', B\" are more fertile than A, A', and B but less fertile than C and D. The sequence thus takes on a criss-cross pattern . The progression is not towards soil that was absolutely less fertile compared with the formerly most fertile soil types C and D; on the other hand it is not towards absolutely more fertile soil, but simply to soil that is relatively more fertile compared with the previously least fertile A, or A and B. Third : the rent on B would fall , and similarly the rent on C and D, but the total rental in corn would have risen from 6 qrs to 11 qrs (or, per the table, to 11⅔ qrs). The amount of cultivated and rent-bearing land would have increased, and the total product would increase from 10 qrs to 17. Profit, if it remained the same for A, would have risen when expressed in corn; but the profit rate itself might have risen, since relative surplus-value had done so. In this case, wages and thus the outlay on variable capital would have fallen, on account of the cheapening of the means of subsistence, and so too therefore would the total outlay. The total rental in money would have fallen from 360s. to 345s. Here is the counter-intuitive result that Marx wishes to establish: money rent can fall while corn rent rises, and rents on the superior soils can fall while new rent-bearing soils are added, and all this can happen simultaneously with a falling price of grain. This is entirely impossible within the West-Malthus-Ricardo framework, which posited a necessary connection between rising rents and worsening cultivation. Table II (as reconstructed from the text and its garbled OCR): Type of soil Capital invested Product (Qrs) Product (Shillings) Profit (Qrs) Profit (Shillings) Rent (Qrs) Rent (Shillings) Price of production per quarter A 1⅓ — 50 ⅔ 10 — — 45s. A' 1⅔ 75 50 ⅓ 25 ⅓ 15 36s. B 2 90 50 ⅘–⅞ 40 ⅔ 30 30s. B' 2½ 105 50 ⁷/₁₀ 55 1 45 25 5/7 s. B\" 2⅔ 120 50 ~1½ 70 1½ 60 22½s. C 3 135 50 1¼ 85 1½ 75 20s. D 4 180 50 2½ 130 2½ 120 15s. Total 17 345 The precise fractions in the original are partly obscured by OCR degradation, but the structure is unambiguous and is what matters: the governing price falls to 45s.; six new rent-bearing or partially rent-bearing soil types appear between the old A and C; rent on B, C, and D falls in money terms; total corn rent rises; total money rent falls. 2.9 Table III: Absolute Improvement of All Soils and the Rising Differential Marx now presents a third scenario. Here, only the soil types A, B, C, and D are cultivated as before, but their productivity has risen in such a way that A produces 2 quarters instead of 1; B, 4 quarters instead of 2; C, 7 quarters instead of 3; and D, 10 quarters instead of 4 . The same causes have operated differently on the different soil types. Total production rises from 10 quarters to 23. Assuming demand has absorbed these 23 quarters as a result of a rise in population and a fall in price, Marx arrives at Table III : Type of soil Capital invested Product (Qrs) Product (Shillings) Price of production per quarter Profit (Qrs) Profit (Shillings) Rent (Qrs) Rent (Shillings) A 50 2 60 30s. ⅓ 10 0 0 B 50 4 120 15s. 2¼ 70 2 60 C 50 7 210 8 4/7 s. 5⅓ 160 5 150 D 50 10 300 6s. 8⅓ 250 8 240 Total 23 15 qrs 450s. Marx notes, with characteristic honesty, that \"the numerical ratios are as arbitrary here as in all the other tables, but the assumptions are completely reasonable.\" He then specifies the two major assumptions on which Table III rests. First and major assumption : the improvement in agriculture has differing effects on different types of soil, and in this case a greater effect on the better soil types C and D than on A and B. \"Experience shows that this is the general rule, even if the opposite case is also possible.\" Marx draws the crucial counterfactual: if the improvement had a greater effect on the worse soils than on the better, the rent on the latter would fall instead of rising. With the absolute growth in fertility of all types of soil, the table presupposes a growth in the higher relative fertility of the better soil types C and D — hence a growth in the difference in products from the same capital investment and a rise in differential rent . This is entirely consistent with the Ricardian principle as Marx has reformulated it: rent depends on inequality , and the inequality can increase even as absolute fertility rises for all. Second assumption : total demand keeps pace with the growing total product. Marx defends this assumption robustly against several objections: It is not necessary to consider the growth as happening suddenly; series III can be considered as arising gradually. \"Secondly, it is wrong to maintain that the consumption of necessary means of subsistence does not grow when these become cheaper.\" The abolition of the Corn Laws in England (Marx cites Newman's Lectures on Political Economy ) proved the opposite. The contrary conception arose only because major sudden harvest variations — themselves due simply to the weather — produce sudden disproportionate rises or falls in corn prices. If in this case the sudden and short-lived cheapening does not last long enough to exert its full effect in expanding consumption, the opposite is the case when the fall in price results from a fall in the governing production price itself, and is thus of longer duration. Part of the grain can be consumed in the form of spirits or beer, and the growth in consumption of these two articles \"is in no way confined within narrow limits.\" The matter depends partly on population growth; and the country may also be a corn-exporting one — as England still was until past the middle of the eighteenth century — so that demand is not governed by the limits of national consumption alone. The increase and cheapening of wheat production may make wheat into the main staple foodstuff of the mass of the people instead of rye or oats, which already leads to a growth in the market for wheat. (The reverse case may arise with a declining product and increasing price.) On these assumptions and figures, series III yields the following results: the price per quarter falls from 60s. to 30s., i.e. by 50 per cent; production grows from 10 qrs to 23, i.e. by 130 per cent; the rent on soil B remains the same; on soil C it rises by 25 per cent; on D by 33⅓ per cent; the total rental thus rises from £18 to £22½, i.e. by 25 per cent. So here is the decisive result: falling prices, rising production, and a rising total rental, combined with a rising rent on the best soils and stationary rent on the middling soil. 2.10 The Five General Results of the Three Tables Marx now consolidates. The three tables — in which series I should be taken twice, rising from A to D and falling from D to A, and which may be interpreted either as representing distinctions within a given state of society (e.g. alongside one another in three different countries) or as succeeding one another at various points in the development of the same country — give five results. (1) The sequence, when complete, always appears as a decreasing one , whatever the course of its formation might have been; for in considering rents, one will always first proceed from the soil that bears the maximum rent and only come last to that which yields no rent. This is an important methodological point: the appearance of rent analysis is always descending because analytic exposition begins with the highest rent; but this appearance must not be confused with the actual historical order of cultivation. (2) The production price of the worst soil that yields no rent is always the governing market price , although, considering Table I where it is taken as an increasing sequence, this production price would remain stationary only if ever better land were cultivated. In this case, the price of the corn produced on the best soil is the governing one, in so far as the extent to which soil A remains the price-governing soil depends on the quantity produced on the best soil . If B, C, and D produce more than is demanded, A ceases to govern. Marx notes that Storch \"has a vague idea of this when he makes the best type of soil the governing type.\" This mechanism explains why \"American grain prices govern English ones\" — the abundant production of superior American soils displaces the English margin as the price-governing locus. (3) The differential rent arises from the difference in the natural fertility of soil types that is given for the level of agriculture found in existence at the time (leaving aside location), i.e. it arises from the limited extent of the best lands and from the fact that the same capital has to be applied to unequal types of soil , which thus yield unequal products for the same capital. (4) The existence of a differential rent and a graduated differential rent can be based just as well on a declining scale (from better soil to worse) as on an ascending scale (from worse soil to better); or it can arise in an alternating criss-cross pattern. The former case may be formed by a progression either from D to A or from A to D. The latter involves both kinds of movement. (5) According to its mode of formation, differential rent can develop along with a stationary, rising, or falling price of the agricultural product. In the case of a falling price, the total production and the total rental may rise, so that rent is formed on what was previously non-rent-bearing land, even though the worst soil A has been displaced by better, or has itself been improved, and even though the rent on other better types of soil, and even on the best types, falls (Table II); this process can also be linked with a fall in the total rental (in money). Finally, in the case of falling prices which result from a general improvement in cultivation, so that both the product of the worst soil and its price fall, rent on a portion of the better types of soil may remain the same or fall, while rising on the best types of soil. Marx then formalizes the relative logic of rent carefully: the differential rent on any soil, compared with the worst soil, depends on the price of wheat per quarter , if the difference in the quantity produced is given. But when the price is given, it depends on the difference in the amount produced ; and in the case of an increasing absolute fertility of all soil, the fertility of the better types rises relatively more than that of the worse soils, so that the size of this difference also grows. Given a price of 60s., therefore (Table I), the rent on D is determined by its differential product vis-à-vis A — i.e., by the excess of 3 qrs — so the rent is 3 × 60 = 180s. In Table III, however, where the price is 30s., it is determined by the amount of D's excess product over A, = 8 qrs; but 8 × 30 = 240s. The arithmetic is trivially simple, but the conceptual point is profound: rent is the product of two independently variable magnitudes — the price and the differential quantity — and can therefore move in either direction or be stationary even when one component moves. 2.11 The Refutation of West, Malthus, and Ricardo: The Law of Diminishing Returns Marx now draws the great polemical conclusion. \"In this way we can abandon the erroneous conception of differential rent which still prevailed with West, Malthus and Ricardo and which assumed a necessary progression to ever worse soil, or an ever declining agricultural fertility.\" The refutation is comprehensive. Differential rent can arise with a progression to ever better soil (the ascending series). It can arise if a better soil takes the lowest place instead of that which was formerly the worst (displacement of A by A' or improvement of A). It can be linked with a steady advance in agriculture (the case of Table III). Its only precondition is the inequality of types of soil . In so far as the development of productivity is involved, it assumes that the rise in the absolute fertility of the total acreage does not abolish this inequality, but that it either increases it, leaves it stationary, or simply reduces it. This is the theoretical core of the chapter's critical contribution. The Ricardian law of rent is not wrong in its mechanism (rent is differential), but it was falsely entangled with the historical thesis of declining agricultural fertility. Marx separates the two: the mechanism is general, the historical narrative is false. It should be noted — and this is a nuance which the chapter implies but does not fully develop here — that the \"law of diminishing returns,\" which West, Malthus, and Ricardo had elevated to a natural law, becomes under Marx's treatment a historically contingent and socially mediated phenomenon. What appears as diminishing returns in a given agricultural system may reflect the level of development of agricultural chemistry and mechanics, the institutional barriers to capital investment, or the class relations of landed property — not any inherent niggardliness of nature. 2.12 The Historical Illustrations: English Grain Prices and Rents Marx now supplies three historical illustrations drawn from English experience, \"in which series I should be taken twice,\" corresponding to distinct phases. Phase 1 (start of the sixteenth century to the middle of the eighteenth century) : England saw a steady fall in grain prices, despite the falling price of gold and silver , alongside a simultaneous growth in rents (taking the period as a whole), in the total rental, in the extent of land cultivated, in agricultural production, and in population. This corresponds to Table I combined with Table II in an upward direction — but in such a way that the worst soil A is either improved or taken out of cereal cultivation ; which, however, does not mean it ceases to be used for other agricultural or industrial purposes. Phase 2 (beginning of the nineteenth century until 1815) : a continuous rise in grain prices, with a steady growth in rent, rental, the extent of land cultivated, agricultural production, and population. This corresponds to Table I in a downward direction. (Marx notes that references to the cultivation of worse lands at that time are to be supplied.) Phase 3 (the time of Petty and Davenant) : complaints of country people and landowners about improvements and the ploughing up of commons; fall in rents on better lands; rise in the total rental by extension of rent-bearing land. These historical vignettes are not decorative. They prove two things. First , that the pattern of rent formation varies dramatically across historical epochs and cannot be reduced to a single \"law.\" Second , that the social forces which produce falling rents on better lands (as in the Petty-Davenant era) can coexist with a rising total rental — a phenomenon Marx has already explained structurally in the discussion of the composition of the cultivated area which follows. 2.13 Market Value, \"False Social Value,\" and the Landed Class: The Anti-Physiocratic Core Before turning to the extensive cultivation question, Marx makes one of the chapter's most important theoretical statements. \"In connection with differential rent in general, it should be noted that the market value is always above the total production price for the overall quantity produced. \" Consider Table I. The total product of 10 qrs is sold for 600s., since the market price is determined by the production price of A, which comes to 60s. per qr. The actual production price , however, is: - A: 1 qr = 60s. (i.e., 1 qr per 60s.) - B: 2 qrs = 60s. (i.e., 1 qr per 30s.) - C: 3 qrs = 60s. (i.e., 1 qr per 20s.) - D: 4 qrs = 60s. (i.e., 1 qr per 15s.) - 10 qrs = 240s. total; average 1 qr = 24s. So the real production price of the 10 qrs is 240s.; they are sold for 600s., 250 per cent too much . The real average price for 1 qr is 24s.; the market price 60s., similarly 250 per cent too much. Marx's interpretation is decisive: \"This is determination by a market value brought about by competition on the basis of the capitalist mode of production; it is competition that produces a false social value. This results from the law of market value to which agricultural products are subjected. The determination of the market value of products, i.e. also of products of the soil, is a social act, even if performed by society unconsciously and unintentionally, and it is based necessarily on the exchange-value of the product and not on the soil and the differences in its fertility. \" This passage repays careful reading. The market value of grain — determined, under capitalist competition, by the most expensive (worst-soil) production price that is needed to satisfy demand — is a social determination. It has nothing intrinsically to do with the soil. Yet because the price is socially established at the level of the worst land, the products of the better land command a price that is above their own production price — and the difference accrues to the landowner. The soil is thus the occasion for the surplus, but not its source . The source is the social act of valuation. Marx then draws the radical implication. If we imagine that the capitalist form of society has been abolished and that society has been organized as a conscious association working according to a plan , the 10 qrs represent a quantity of autonomous labour-time equal to that contained in 240s. Society would therefore not purchase this product at 2½ times the actual labour-time contained in it; the basis for a class of landowners would thereby disappear — which would have the same effect as a cheapening of the product to the same amount by foreign imports. This is a subtle and important argument. Marx distinguishes two propositions: - Correct : keeping to the present mode of production, but assuming differential rent accrued to the state (as opposed to private landlords), the prices of agricultural products would remain the same, if other factors did too. (Nationalization of land would not by itself alter the market price governed by the worst soil.) - Wrong : to say that the value of these products would remain the same if capitalist production were replaced by association. Under association, the social labour-time contained in the grain would be recognized directly, and the \"false social value\" would vanish. The distinction between price and value here is crucial. Under capitalism, the price is fixed by competition irrespective of who receives the rent. But the value — measured by social labour-time — is less than the price; the difference is a social overpayment. The fact that \"commodities of the same kind have an identical market price is the way in which the social character of value is realized on the basis of the capitalist mode of production, and in general of production depending on commodity exchange between individuals.\" The class-theoretic conclusion is stated with precision: \"Where society, considered as a consumer, pays too much for agricultural products, this is a minus for the realization of its labour-time in agricultural production, but it forms a plus for one portion of society, the landowners. \" Rent is thus a transfer — not a creation of value. It is a portion of society's labour-time appropriated by a class that contributes nothing to production. This is the anti-Physiocratic core: the soil does not create value; the social form of valuation does, and landed property intercepts the surplus thereby generated. 2.14 The Transition to Differential Rent II: Extensive Cultivation and the Rate of Rent Marx now introduces a \"second circumstance, important for what will be presented in the next chapter as differential rent II.\" Up to this point, the analysis has been about rent per acre and the difference between individual and general production prices per acre . The new consideration is: how many acres of each type of soil are under cultivation. This bears directly on the size of the rental (the total rent for the whole cultivated area), but Marx says it also serves as a transition to the discussion of a rise in the rate of rent , even when prices do not rise, or the differences in the relative fertility of the soil types do not rise when prices fall. He restates Table I with the explicit addition of the acreage column: Type of soil Acres Price of production Product Rent in grain Rent in money A 1 £3 1 qr 0 0 B 1 £3 2 qrs 1 qr £3 C 1 £3 3 qrs 2 qrs £6 D 1 £3 4 qrs 3 qrs £9 Total 4 acres 10 qrs 6 qrs £18 2.15 Tables Ia, Ib, Ic: The Composition of the Cultivated Area as an Independent Variable Marx now constructs three variants on Table I by varying the number of acres of each soil type. Table Ia — all four types double in acreage: Type of soil Acres Price of production Product Rent in grain Rent in money A 2 £6 2 qrs 0 0 B 2 £6 4 qrs 2 qrs £6 C 2 £6 6 qrs 4 qrs £12 D 2 £6 8 qrs 6 qrs £18 Total 8 acres 20 qrs 12 qrs £36 Table Ib — production expands principally on the two inferior soil types: Type of soil Acres Price of production per acre Total Product Rent in grain Rent in money A 4 £3 £12 4 qrs 0 0 B 4 £3 £12 8 qrs 4 qrs £12 C 2 £3 £6 6 qrs 4 qrs £12 D 2 £3 £6 8 qrs 6 qrs £18 Total 12 acres £36 26 qrs 14 qrs £42 Table Ic — uneven expansion over all four classes, with bulk of growth on the best soils: Type of soil Acres Price of production per acre Total Product Rent in grain Rent in money A 1 £3 £3 1 qr 0 0 B 2 £3 £6 4 qrs 2 qrs £6 C 5 £3 £15 15 qrs 10 qrs £30 D 4 £3 £12 16 qrs 12 qrs £36 Total 12 acres £36 36 qrs 24 qrs £72 Marx's analysis of these variants begins with the fundamental point: in all four cases (I, Ia, Ib, Ic), the rent per acre remains the same , because the product of the same quantity of capital on each acre of the same type of soil is unchanged. He is assuming only two things: (i) that the various types of soil share the total cultivated area in a definite ratio in any country at a particular time; and (ii) that the ratio in which the total cultivated area is divided between them changes over time (or differs between countries). Ia vs Ib comparison : When cultivation in all four classes grows in the same proportion (Ia), a doubling of the number of acres doubles the total production and similarly the corn and money rent. This is trivially proportional. Ib and Ic vs I : In both cases, the area under cultivation triples (from 4 to 12 acres). But the composition of the expansion differs radically. In Ib , classes A and B take the major share of the growth: of the 8 newly cultivated acres, 3 each fall into A and B — a total of 6 — while only 1 each, a total of 2, fall into C and D. So three-quarters of the increase takes place on A and B, and only one-quarter on C and D. On this assumption, the tripling of the expanse under cultivation does not involve a tripling of the product: it rises not from 10 to 30, but only to 26. On the other hand, because a substantial part of the growth takes place on A, which yields no rent, and because of the growth on the better lands the major part is on class B, the corn rent rises only from 6 qrs to 14, and the money rent from £18 to £42. In Ic , by contrast, the non-rent-paying land does not increase its extent at all, while land yielding the minimal rent exhibits only a weak increase (B goes from 1 to 2 acres); the bulk of the growth accrues to C and D. Here, with the tripling of the cultivated area, production rises from 10 qrs to 36, i.e. by more than three times; the corn rent from 6 qrs to 24, or four times; and the money rent similarly from £18 to £72. The general rule Marx extracts: In all these cases, the price of the agricultural product remains stationary, \"in the nature of things\"; in each case the total rental grows with the expansion of cultivation, as long as this does not take place exclusively on the worst lands, which pay no rent. But the growth varies. To the extent that the expansion takes place on the better types of soil, so that the quantity produced does not just grow in proportion to the expansion of the land cultivated but more steeply , the corn and money rent grows. To the extent that the worst soil and the adjacent categories take the principal share of the increase (which assumes that the worst soil is a constant category), the total rental does not rise in proportion to the expansion of cultivation. From this, Marx derives a proportional law: given two countries in which land A, which yields no rent, is of the same quality, the rental stands in inverse proportion to the aliquot part of the total cultivated area composed by the worst and less good soil types, and hence also in inverse proportion to the quantities produced by identical capital investments on equal areas. The greater the share of the cultivated area occupied by inferior soils, the lower the total rental relative to the acreage — and vice versa. He then states a critical dual-aspect conclusion: The proportion between the amount of the worst cultivated soil and that of the better in the total area of a country thus has an effect on the total rental which is opposite to the effect that the relation between the quality of the worst cultivated soil and that of the better and best has on the rent per acre , and hence, with other circumstances remaining the same, also on the rental. \"The confusion between these two aspects has given rise to all kinds of confused objections against differential rent.\" This is a masterful piece of analytical housekeeping — Marx is showing how apparently conflicting data about rents can be reconciled once the two distinct variables (quality ratio vs. quantity composition) are disentangled. 2.16 The Structure of Average Rent and the Rate of Rent: The \"Overlooked Factor\" Marx now introduces two derived magnitudes which will be central to the rest of the chapter. Average rent per acre = total rental ÷ total number of acres tilled. (This is what is generally used in statistical works.) Rate of rent = total rental ÷ total capital invested in the soil. He then compares the three cases. Case I vs Case Ia : In I, the total area is 4 acres, the overall rental is £18, and hence the average rent — including the soil that bears no rent — is £4½ per acre. The total rental of £18 is produced by applying a capital of £10. The ratio between these two figures is the rate of rent: 180 per cent. In Ia, the same rate of rent occurs: 8 acres, total rental £36, capital £20, average rent £4½ per acre, rate of rent 180 per cent. So proportional expansion leaves both averages unchanged. Case Ib : rent of £42 for 12 acres, i.e. an average rent of £3½ per acre. Total capital laid out is £30, i.e. a rate of rent of 140 per cent. So the average rent per acre has fallen from £4½ to £3½ (£1 less), and the rate of rent has fallen from 180 per cent to 140 per cent. This happens even though the rent on all soil types remains the same , whether reckoned per acre or on the capital invested. The reason: three-quarters of the increase occurred on soil A, which bears no rent, and soil B, which bears only the minimal rent. Extreme sub-case of Ib : if the total expansion had taken place simply on soil A, we would have 9 acres of A, 1 of B, 1 of C, and 1 of D. The total rental would still be £18, giving an average rent per acre on these 12 acres of £1½; while £18 rent on a capital of £30 laid out gives a rate of rent of 60 per cent. \"The average rent would have sharply declined, whether reckoned per acre or on the capital applied, while the total rental would not have grown.\" Case Ic vs I and Ib : Compared with I, the acreage has tripled, and so has the capital laid out. The total rental is £72 for 12 acres, i.e. £6 per acre as against £4½ per acre in case I. The rate of rent on the capital laid out (£72 : £30) is 240 per cent instead of 180 per cent. The total product has risen from 10 qrs to 36. Compared with Ib — where the total acreage under cultivation, the capital applied, and the differences between the types of soil tilled all remain the same, though differently distributed — the product is 36 qrs instead of 26 qrs, the average rent per acre £6 instead of £3½, and the rate of rent on an equal total capital advanced is 240 per cent instead of 140 per cent. Marx now states the general conclusions. Under the assumptions — a stationary price of grain because the yield on the worst, non-rent-bearing land remains the same; the same differences in fertility between the various categories of soil cultivated; an equal respective product from equal capital investment on equal aliquot parts (acres) of the area cultivated in each class of soil; and a constant ratio between rent per acre on each type of soil and an equal rate of rent on the capital invested in each piece of land of the same kind — we get: Firstly : The rental always grows with an expansion of the cultivated area and therefore with an increased capital investment, except for the case when the entire growth falls on the non-rent-bearing soil. Secondly : Both the average rent per acre (total rental ÷ total number of acres tilled) and the average rate of rent (total rental ÷ total capital invested) may vary very significantly; and even if both move in the same direction, they may still move in different ratios. Leaving aside the case where the growth takes place simply on the non-rent-bearing soil A, the average rent per acre and the average rate of rent on the capital invested in agriculture depend on the proportionate shares that the various classes of soil make up within the total cultivated area — or, what comes to the same thing, they depend on the way in which the total capital applied is distributed over soil types of different fertility. Whether much land is tilled or only a little — so that the total rental is larger or smaller (except for the case where the growth is solely on A) — the average rent per acre or the average rate of rent on the capital applied remains the same as long as the proportions in which the different types of soil participate in the total acreage remain constant. Despite a rise in the total rental as cultivation extends and more capital is invested, and even a major rise, the average rent per acre and the average rate of rent on capital fall , if the expansion of the rent-free lands and those that only bear a small differential rent is steeper than that of the better land. Conversely, the average rent per acre and the average rate of rent on capital rise to the extent that the better lands constitute a relatively larger share of the total area, and hence relatively more capital investment falls on them. Marx now draws out the crucial statistical illusion. If we consider the average rent per acre of the total cultivated land — which is \"what is generally done in statistical works,\" since either different countries are compared at the same time or different periods in the history of the same country — we see that the average level of rent per acre, and hence also the total rental, corresponds to a certain extent (without being identical: in fact the proportion tends to increase) not to the relative but to the absolute fertility of agriculture in a country , i.e. to the quantity of products supplied on average by a given area. Why? \"For the greater the share of the total area constituted by the better types of soil, the greater is the volume of products from the same capital investment and the same land area; and the greater, too, is the average rent per acre. And vice versa.\" This creates a statistical appearance: rent appears as determined not by the ratio of differential fertility but rather by the absolute fertility, which would refute the law of differential rent. Marx's response is one of the chapter's most important methodological moves. \"This is why certain phenomena are denied, or else the attempt is made to explain them in terms of non-existent distinctions in average grain prices and the differential fertilities of lands under cultivation, phenomena whose only basis is that the proportion of the total rental, either to the total area of land cultivated or to the total capital invested in the soil — given the same fertility for the non-rent-bearing soil and hence equal production prices, and given the same differences between the various soil types — is not determined only by the rent per acre or by the rate of rent on capital but just as much by the relative proportion of each soil type in the total acreage tilled ; or, what comes to the same thing, by the distribution of the total capital applied among the various types of soil.\" Marx then delivers the punchline: \" Up till now, this factor has been completely overlooked, in a quite striking fashion. \" And he identifies the broader implication for the investigation: \"It still shows, and this is important for the further course of our investigation, that the relative level of average rents per acre, and the average rate of rent or the ratio of the total rental to the total capital invested in the soil, may rise or fall even though prices, the difference in fertility of the lands under cultivation and the rent per acre or rate of rent for the capital invested per acre in each actual rent-bearing soil category, or for all actually rent-bearing capital, all remain the same, simply by an expansion of the cultivated area. \" This is the chapter's most original technical contribution. Marx has isolated the composition of the cultivated area — the relative proportions of soil types — as an independent variable determining the average rent and the rate of rent, entirely separate from price movements or fertility differentials. This decoupling of statistical aggregates from the underlying per-acre determinants is what allows him to explain the apparent contradictions in rent data that orthodox political economy could not reconcile. 2.17 Additional Points (I): The Price of Untilled Land and the Capitalization of Future Rent Marx introduces the remaining stages of the chapter with a note: \"The following additional points have still to be made, in relation to the first form of differential rent, although they also apply in part to the second form.\" Firstly , he extends the analysis to the price of land itself , including untilled land. He begins with the observation already established: the average rent per acre or the average rate of rent on capital may rise with the extension of cultivation, given stationary prices and unchanged differential fertility of the lands tilled. As soon as all the land in a country is appropriated, and capital investment on the land, agriculture, and population have all reached a certain level — factors taken for granted once the capitalist mode of production becomes dominant and takes control of agriculture too — the price of the untilled land of various qualities (assuming only differential rent) is determined by the price of land of equivalent quality and location that is tilled. The price is the same — after deducting the additional cost of ploughing up new land — even though this land does not bear any rent. Marx then recalls a fundamental principle: \"The price of land is of course nothing more than capitalized rent .\" Even in the case of cultivated lands, it is only future rents that are paid for in their price; for example, twenty years' rent is paid at one stroke if the determining interest rate is 5 per cent. As soon as land is sold, it is sold as rent-bearing ; the prospective character of the rent (which is considered here as the fruit of the soil, something that it is only in surface appearance) does not distinguish the uncultivated land from the cultivated. The striking consequence: \"The price of untilled land, like its rent, which is what this contracted formula represents, is pure illusion as long as this land is not actually used. But it is determined a priori in this way, and is realized as soon as buyers are found.\" Thus, if the actual average rent in a country is determined by its actual average annual rental and the ratio of this rental to the total cultivated area, the price of the untilled portion is determined by the price of the tilled and is therefore simply a reflection of the capital investment in the tilled lands, and its results there. Since, with the exception of the worst land, all types of soil bear rent — and this rent, as we shall go on to see in the case of differential rent II, rises with the amount of capital and the corresponding intensity of cultivation — a nominal price is thereby formed for the untilled portions of land, so that these too become a commodity, a source of wealth for their owner. Marx draws a vivid practical implication: \"This explains at the same time why the price of land rises for the entire area, even for untilled land. (Opdyke.) Land speculation, e.g. in the United States, depends on this reflection which capital and labour cast on untilled land. \" This passage extends the analysis of differential rent into the analysis of land as fictitious capital . The price of land is capitalized rent, and rent itself is a social claim on surplus profit; therefore, land speculation is not a natural phenomenon but an artifact of the social relations of production and the legal form of landed property. The reflection of capital investment on tilled lands onto the untilled land is the mechanism by which the appearance of a natural value of land is produced — an appearance that underwrites rentier speculation. 2.18 Additional Points (II): Location, Enclaves, and the Michigan/New York Examples Secondly , Marx turns to the direction of extension of cultivation . All progress in the extension of cultivation takes place either towards worse soil or on the various given types of soil in different proportions, according to how these are to be found. Progression towards worse soil, of course, is never from free choice; taking the capitalist mode of production as given, it can only be the result of rising prices, and in any mode of production only the result of necessity. But this is not absolutely the case. Why? Marx lists three conditions under which cultivation might extend to worse rather than better soil: (a) Location : \"Bad soil may be relatively preferred to better on account of its location, which is decisive for every extension of cultivation in new countries.\" (b) Intermingling of soil types : \"even though the soil formation in a certain region may be fertile on the whole, better and worse soil may be closely intermingled in some places, so that the inferior soil has to be cultivated simply because of its proximity to the better.\" (c) Enclaves : \"If the worse soil forms enclaves within the better, the better soil gives it the advantage of location as against more fertile land that is not yet part of the cultivated area or about to become so.\" Marx illustrates with two examples. The state of Michigan was one of the first Western states to export corn. Its soil on the whole is poor. But its proximity to the state of New York and its water routes via the Great Lakes and the Erie Canal gave it at first an initial advantage over the states further west, though these were more fertile by nature. The example of this state, in comparison with the state of New York , also shows the transition from better soil to worse. The soil of New York state, and particularly its western part, is of uneven fertility, particularly for wheat cultivation. Rapacious cultivation made this fertile soil infertile, and then the Michigan soil appeared more fertile. Note the crucial phrase: \"rapacious cultivation\" — the depletion of the soil through a particular mode of agricultural exploitation, not natural exhaustion, produced the apparent switch in fertility ranking. This is a vivid illustration of Marx's earlier conceptual point about the historicity of \"natural\" fertility. He cites J. W. Johnston ( Notes on North America , London, 1851, I, pp. 220–23): \"In 1838, wheaten flour was shipped at Buffalo for the West; and the wheat-region of New York, with that of Upper Canada, were the main sources of its supply. Now after only twelve years, an enormous supply of wheat and flour is brought from the West, along Lake Erie, and shipped upon the Erie Canal for the East, at Buffalo and the adjoining port of Blackrock... The effect of these large arrivals from the Western States — which were unnaturally stimulated during the years of European famine — has been to render wheat less valuable in western New York, to make the wheat culture less remunerative, and to turn the attention of the New York farmers more to grazing and dairy husbandry, fruit culture, and other branches of rural economy, in which they think the North-West will be unable so directly to compete with them.\" This is a rich historical case. Note the multiple variables at work: the water routes (location); the \"unnatural stimulation\" of European famine demand (external demand); the rapid shift of western New York farmers out of wheat and into grazing, dairy, and fruit (specialization and differentiation of agriculture); and the resulting downward pressure on wheat prices and rents in the older region. The example demonstrates that the \"inevitable\" progression from better to worse soil is not inevitable at all — location, transport infrastructure, and market demand can produce the appearance of declining natural fertility when what is actually declining is the profitability of the former best soils under changed market conditions. 2.19 Additional Points (III): The Illusion of Colonial Fertility and the Nature of Colonial Surplus Thirdly , Marx attacks a widely held error: \"It is false to assume that the soil in those colonies and other new countries that can export corn at cheaper prices is therefore necessarily of greater natural fertility.\" In this case — where colonial grain undersells the older countries — grain is sold \"not only below its value, but also below its price of production, i.e. below the production price determined by the average rate of profit in the older countries.\" This is a crucial nuance. The low price of colonial grain is not proof of superior fertility. It may be proof that colonial conditions permit sale below the normal production price — that is, temporarily below the value-governing price of the world market. Marx quotes Johnston again: we \"are accustomed to attach the idea of great natural productiveness and of boundless tracts of rich land, to those new States from which come the large supplies of wheat that are annually poured into the port of Buffalo.\" But this appearance \"depends first of all on economic conditions .\" Marx lists the economic conditions: (1) One-sided specialization in agriculture. \"The entire population of a state such as this, e.g. Michigan, begins by being almost exclusively engaged in agriculture, and particularly in the mass crops which alone can be exchanged for industrial goods and tropical products. Their entire surplus product thus takes the shape of corn.\" This \"fundamentally distinguishes the colonial states founded on the basis of the modern world market from those of earlier times, and particularly those of antiquity.\" The colonies \"receive ready-made, through the world market, products that they would otherwise have to produce themselves, such as clothing, tools, etc.\" It is only on this basis that the Southern states of the Union could make cotton into their principal product. The division of labour on the world market permits this. Hence Marx's conclusion: \"if, considering their newness and their relatively small population, they appear to produce a very large surplus product, this is not due to the fertility of their soil or to the productiveness of their labour, but rather to the one-sided form of this labour and thus of the surplus product in which it is expressed. \" This is a brilliant application of world-systems reasoning avant la lettre . The appearance of super-abundant colonial production is a specialization effect in the international division of labour, not a fertility effect. It anticipates the analysis of comparative advantage as a product of the world market rather than a natural endowment. (2) Virgin soil accumulates easily assimilated plant nutrients. \"Besides, relatively less fertile soil which is tilled for the first time and has never been touched by agriculture before has accumulated so much in the way of easily assimilated plant nutrients, at least in its top layers, that it will yield harvests for a quite long period without any fertilizer — as long as the climatic conditions are not completely unfavourable — even on quite superficial tilling.\" In the Western prairies, \"a further factor is that scarcely any clearing costs are required, since nature has made them already arable.\" (3) The surplus arises from the acreage, not the yield per acre. \"In less fertile regions of this kind the surplus comes not from the high fertility of the soil, i.e. from the yield per acre, but rather from the great acreage that can be cultivated superficially, since this land costs the tiller nothing, or at least only an infinitesimal amount compared with the older countries.\" This is true for share-cropping, as in parts of New York, Michigan, Canada. A family tills, say, 100 acres superficially; the product per acre is not large, but the product of 100 acres provides a sizable surplus for sale. Cattle, etc., can be grazed almost cost-free on natural pasture, without any need for artificial meadows. \" The decisive thing here is not the quality of the soil but the quantity. \" The possibility of superficial cultivation is \"of course more or less rapidly exhausted in inverse proportion to the fertility of the new soil and in direct proportion to the export of its product.\" Johnston: \"And yet such a country will give excellent crops, even of wheat, and will supply to those who skim the first cream off the country, a large surplus of this grain to send to market\" (op. cit., p. 225). Marx then makes a sharp historical-structural contrast: \"In countries where agriculture is older, any such kind of extensive farming is made impossible by the property relations, the price of uncultivated land as determined by that of cultivated, etc.\" Here the earlier discussion of the price of untilled land returns with direct bearing on agricultural practice: the appropriation of land and the pricing of untilled land at capitalized future rent prevent the kind of extensive, low-intensity farming that is possible on the frontier. Empirical evidence : In the state of Michigan, 465,900 acres were sown to wheat in 1848, producing 4,739,300 bushels, an average of 10½ bushels per acre — less than 9 bushels per acre after deducting the seed-corn. Out of twenty-nine counties in the state, two produced an average of 7 bushels; three an average of 8; two 9; seven 10; six 11; three 12; four 13; a single county 16; and one 18 bushels per acre (op. cit., p. 225). This dispersion shows enormous variability across counties — evidence, again, that \"fertility\" is not uniform, and that the surplus comes from quantity of acreage rather than exceptional yield. Marx's conclusion is aesthetic as well as analytical: \"As far as practical agriculture is concerned, higher fertility of the soil is the same thing as a greater possibility of immediately exploiting its fertility. Immediate exploitation may be more possible with a naturally poor soil than with a naturally rich one; and this is the kind of soil which a colonist will take up first, and must take up when capital is scarce.\" This is a reversal, in the domain of colonial agriculture, of the Ricardian presumption. A colonist with scarce capital does not choose the most fertile soil as such; he chooses the soil whose fertility can be immediately exploited with minimal capital outlay. A naturally poor soil that requires no clearing and yields immediately may be preferred to a naturally richer soil that requires investment to bring into production. Thus the hierarchy of soils under colonial conditions is determined by the availability of capital, not by natural fertility considered in the abstract . 2.20 Additional Points (IV): Relative Overproduction and the Autonomy of Capital Accumulation Finally , Marx turns to a decisive point about the dynamic of agricultural expansion. \"The extension of cultivation to larger areas (apart from the case just considered, in which resort has to be had to a worse soil than that formerly tilled), on the various soil types from A to D, for example, and therefore the tilling of greater areas of B and C, in no way depends on a previous rise in grain prices , any more than the anticipatory annual expansion of a cotton spinning-mill, for instance, depends on a continuous rise in the price of yarn.\" The analogy is crucial. Capitalist production expands in anticipation of demand , not in response to already-realized price increases. A cotton spinning-mill expands its capacity before prices rise, in the expectation that demand will absorb the new production; in the same way, agricultural capital extends cultivation onto new land of whatever soil type is available, without waiting for a price rise. Even though a major rise or fall in market price does have an effect on the scale of production, \"there is still, apart from this — and even given average prices, whose level neither inhibits production nor gives it an exceptional boost — the same perpetual relative overproduction in agriculture which is inherently identical with accumulation and which in the case of other modes of production is directly caused by the increase in population, and in the colonies by a steady immigration.\" Demand steadily grows, and with this prospect new capital is continuously invested in new land, even though this happens for different crops and according to particular circumstances. \"The formation of new capitals brings this about automatically.\" Marx then notes the subjective dimension on the side of the individual capitalist: \"But as far as the individual capitalist is concerned, he measures the scale of his production by the capital he has available, to the extent that he can still control it himself. His intention is to take as big a share of the market as possible. If there is overproduction, he blames it on his competitors, not on himself. The individual capitalist can extend his production just as much by appropriating a greater aliquot share of the given market as by expanding this market itself.\" This final passage is a gem of Marx's method. It shows how the aggregate phenomenon — the \"perpetual relative overproduction\" of agriculture, which is inherently identical with accumulation — arises from the uncoordinated decisions of individual capitals, each seeking to maximize its market share. The \"overproduction\" is relative (relative to the purchasing power of the market, not to need), and it is the ordinary form of capital accumulation in a sector characterized by many competing capitals. It also disposes of any \"Say's Law\" intuition that production automatically matches demand; in agriculture, as in other branches of capitalist production, expansion is anticipatory, competitive, and perpetually outrunning realized demand. The chapter ends on this note — with the demonstration that the extension of cultivation onto new soil is not a function of some natural tendency toward \"diminishing returns,\" but of the autonomous logic of capital accumulation in a sector where the pricing mechanism (governed by the worst soil) guarantees a surplus profit to the better soils, and where landed property stands ready to appropriate that surplus as rent. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 3.1 Fundamental Principles Revealed The chapter yields several foundational principles that extend well beyond the technical analysis of rent. Principle 1: Rent is a social relation, not a natural fact. The entire chapter is organized around the demonstration that differential rent arises from the social act of market valuation, not from the inherent productive power of the soil. Fertility is a relational concept — an \"objective property of the soil\" that \"always involves an economic relation\" to a given chemical and mechanical level of agricultural development. Location, likewise, is a function of the social transportation and communications infrastructure. The \"natural\" bases for differential rent — fertility and location — are therefore historically variable and socially constituted. Marx's conceptual move prefigures the modern critique of \"natural\" categories in economics: what appears as a natural given is often a frozen social relation. Principle 2: Rent is differential, and differential rent is relative. The rent on any soil is its surplus profit over the worst soil in cultivation. It is not a positive attribute of the soil but a difference. This means the abolition of differential rent would require abolishing the inequality among soils (impossible in full) or abolishing the social form (landed property and commodity pricing) that converts inequality into revenue. It also means that a fall in the rent on the best soils, or on the middling soils, is entirely compatible with a rise in the total rental — depending on the composition of the cultivated area and the distribution of capital among soil types. Principle 3: The quantity and composition of the cultivated area is an independent determinant of aggregate rent. This is Marx's most original technical contribution in the chapter. The average rent per acre and the rate of rent on capital can rise or fall with prices, fertility differentials, and per-acre rents held constant, simply because the relative proportions of different soil types in the total cultivated area change. Statistical averages therefore carry no direct information about the underlying law of rent. Marx warns that the \"confusion between these two aspects has given rise to all kinds of confused objections against differential rent\" — a warning that remains highly relevant to empirical work on agricultural rents and land values. Principle 4: The Ricardian law of diminishing returns is a historical artifact, not a natural law. By showing that differential rent can arise under ascending, descending, or criss-cross sequences of cultivation, and can coexist with rising, stationary, or falling prices, Marx dissolves the connection between rent and declining agricultural fertility. The only precondition of differential rent is the inequality of soils; the direction of cultivation is determined by contingent historical circumstances — location, capital availability, technical change, demand, transport infrastructure. Principle 5: The price of land is capitalized rent, and therefore a form of fictitious capital. The price of untilled land is determined by the price of tilled land of equivalent quality and location. It is therefore a reflection of past and anticipated capital investment in agriculture, not a natural value of the soil. The land market, and therefore land speculation, rests on this reflection. This principle establishes the continuity between the analysis of ground-rent and the analysis of fictitious capital (State debt, joint-stock equity, etc.) which occupies other parts of Volume III. Principle 6: Colonial \"fertility\" is an illusion generated by the world market. The apparent natural richness of new lands — the boundless prairies of the American West — is produced by one-sided specialization in the international division of labour (which permits the entire surplus product to take the form of corn), by the virgin accumulation of easily assimilated plant nutrients, by the absence of clearing costs, and by the free or near-free appropriation of extensive acreage. The decisive factor is quantity of acreage , not quality of soil. This principle anticipates later analyses of settler-colonial economies and the specific forms of ground-rent they generate. Principle 7: Agriculture exhibits \"perpetual relative overproduction\" inherent in accumulation. The extension of cultivation does not wait for price rises; it precedes them, driven by the individual capitalist's drive for market share. This \"relative overproduction\" is the ordinary form of accumulation in agriculture, just as it is in industry. It is not a pathology but a structural feature of competitive capitalism. 3.2 Broader Theoretical and Practical Implications The chapter's implications ramify far beyond rent theory narrowly conceived. For the theory of value and distribution , the chapter demonstrates that rent is a transfer of surplus value, not a creation of value. The \"false social value\" of agricultural products — their market value above their total production price — is a social overpayment that benefits a class contributing nothing to production. Under a consciously planned association of producers, this overpayment would disappear. This insight is central to Marx's broader project of demonstrating that all revenue forms (profit, interest, rent) are ultimately portions of the surplus value extracted from wage-labour. For the critique of political economy , the chapter is a sustained demonstration of the pitfalls of treating historically specific economic forms as natural categories. West, Malthus, and Ricardo had conflated the mechanism of differential rent (which Marx accepts and refines) with a historical narrative of declining natural fertility (which Marx refutes). The conflation served an apologetic function: it treated the rentier's income as a natural tribute to the niggardliness of nature, rather than as a social claim on the surplus product of labour. Marx's decomposition of the two — mechanism vs. historical narrative — is a model of critical method. For economic history and empirical analysis , the chapter provides a toolkit for understanding puzzles in rent statistics. The apparent paradox — that average rents can fall while total rentals rise , or rise while prices fall — is resolved once the composition of the cultivated area is treated as an independent variable. This toolkit has been applied by later scholars to historical data on agricultural rents in England, Prussia, Ireland, and the United States. For the analysis of land and finance , the discussion of the price of untilled land and land speculation establishes the connection between ground-rent and fictitious capital. The land market is one of the earliest and most important forms of capitalized future income; Marx's analysis here is continuous with later analyses of credit, joint-stock capital, and the financialization of land and real estate. For political strategy , the chapter's demonstration that land nationalization would not, by itself, alter agricultural prices (since price is governed by the worst soil in cultivation, independent of who receives the rent) has important implications. It implies that the abolition of rent as a private revenue requires not merely state appropriation of rent, but a transformation of the mode of production itself — the replacement of commodity pricing (and the \"false social value\" it generates) by a system of consciously planned social labour-time accounting. This is the deepest political implication of the chapter: the basis of a class of landowners can only disappear in a society organized as a conscious association of producers. For ecological economics , the chapter's insistence that \"natural\" fertility is a relational and historically variable magnitude anticipates contemporary discussions of soil fertility as a function of agricultural technique and investment. The observation that \"rapacious cultivation\" can exhaust a naturally fertile soil while superficially cultivated virgin land appears temporarily more fertile is an early recognition of what is now called \"soil mining\" or \"land degradation\" — the exhaustion of accumulated natural capital through unsustainable exploitation. 3.3 The Chapter's Place in Marx's System Chapter 39 is the opening move in Marx's demolition of the trinity formula — the ideological representation of capitalist society as composed of three independent sources of revenue. By demonstrating that differential rent in its first form is entirely a transformed surplus profit, Marx prepares the ground for showing that the second form (differential rent II, on successive investments of capital on the same land) and absolute rent (arising from the lower organic composition of capital in agriculture) are similarly derived, not original, forms of surplus value. The chapter thus belongs to the systematic critique of the \"fetishism\" that attributes revenue to things (land, capital) rather than to social relations (the exploitation of wage-labour). The chapter is also, in its own way, a model of dialectical exposition . Marx begins with an apparently simple Ricardian formula, corrects it, decomposes it, complicates it via multiple numerical scenarios, and arrives at a conclusion far richer and more nuanced than the original formula. Each new table is not merely a new illustration but a new dimension of the same phenomenon — price level, direction of cultivation, absolute fertility, composition of cultivated area, capital distribution — until the phenomenon is fully characterized. The apparent randomness of the initial \"three tables\" gives way to the systematic isolation of independent variables. And the historical illustrations — England 1600–1750, England 1800–1815, Petty and Davenant, Michigan, New York — are not decorative but are the empirical confirmation of the theoretical possibilities laid out in the tables. The final message, if we are to distill it, is this: the rent of land, that most ancient and apparently most natural of revenues, is in fact a modern, social, and historically specific phenomenon. It arises from the social act of valuation under competitive capitalism, it varies with the composition of the cultivated area and the distribution of capital, it supports a class that contributes nothing to production, and its basis would disappear in a society that organized its production consciously rather than through the blind interplay of commodity exchange. The chapter is thus both an exercise in exact economic analysis and a manifesto for the supersession of the social relations that produce rent at all. In sum, Chapter 39 establishes the analytic foundations for the theory of ground-rent that will occupy the remainder of Part VI, while simultaneously demonstrating — in its own movement from Ricardian formula to socialist conclusion — the method of critique that Marx had elaborated throughout Capital : the relentless dissolution of naturalized social forms into the historically specific relations of production from which they arise. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 38 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 40"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 40", "title": "Chapter 40", "subtitle": "Second Form of Differential Rent (Differential Rent II)", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 40/Chapter 40 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 3007, "read_time": "15 min read", "tags": ["Differential Rent (I & II)"], "keywords": ["Differential Rent II: Successive Capital Investments", "Intensive vs Extensive Cultivation", "Varying Productivity of Subsequent Capital Applications", "Tenant Struggles with Landlords over Expiring Leases", "Appropriation of Farmer Capital Improvements by Landlords"], "text": "Chapter 40 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 2,967 words ⏱️ ~15 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 40 of Capital , Volume III, \"The Second Form of Differential Rent (Differential Rent II),\" is the analytical hinge of Marx's theory of ground-rent. It stands at the precise point where the analysis of differential rent, having been developed in Chapter 38–39 as Differential Rent I (hereafter DR I)—the rent arising from the simultaneous investment of equal capitals on equal-sized tracts of differing fertility —must be extended to Differential Rent II (hereafter DR II)—the rent arising from the successive investment of capital on the same piece of land. The chapter's central problem is deceptively simple: Does it matter whether capital sums are invested one after another in time on the same soil, or side by side on different soils, so long as the results are the same? Marx's answer has two halves, and the tension between them is the chapter's whole intellectual engine. On the one hand, the formation of surplus profit is wholly indifferent to this distinction. Whether £10 of capital is divided into four £2½ portions placed on four different acres (A, B, C, D of ascending fertility) or applied in four successive £2½ doses to one and the same acre of D, the mechanism that generates surplus profit is identical: each portion of capital is measured against the least productive portion, whose individual production price regulates the market price, and every portion that out-yields it throws off a surplus profit proportional to the difference. In this respect DR II is \"simply a different expression of differential rent I\" —the same thing in its nature. On the other hand, the transformation of surplus profit into ground-rent —the transfer of that surplus from the capitalist farmer to the landed proprietor—is not indifferent. When capital is invested successively on one piece of land, the limits within which surplus profit can be converted into rent are \"both narrower and unstable,\" because rent has been contractually fixed at the moment the lease was signed, and every subsequent increment of surplus profit generated by additional investment accrues to the farmer for the duration of the tenancy. Hence the class struggle over lease length, over agricultural statistics, and over the profession of rent assessment. The chapter thus fits into Part VI of Capital (\"The Transformation of Surplus Profit into Ground-Rent\") as the bridge between the pure theory of differential rent and its concrete historical-formal dynamics. It is the chapter in which Marx demonstrates (1) that DR I is both the historical and the logical starting-point of DR II ; (2) that the rate of rent on capital and the level of rent per acre are distinct quantities whose divergence explains the peculiar behaviour of rents and land prices under intensive cultivation; and (3) that Ricardo's reduction of all differential rent to the single case of declining returns raising the production price is one-sided and mistaken . Its objective is not merely taxonomic—it is to show that the \"combination\" of the two forms produces a far more complicated field of possible outcomes (rising rent with falling prices, falling rates of surplus profit with rising rent per acre) than the classical political economists admitted. The final sentence—\"We now proceed to show the combinations of differential rent II, which presupposes differential rent I as its basis\"—hands the reader to Chapter 41, where those combinations are systematically tabulated. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS I. The Opening Frame: Extensive versus Intensive Investment (p. 812) Marx opens by restating what has been established so far. Until this point, differential rent has been considered only as the result of the varying productivity of equal capital investments on equal land areas of different fertility . This is DR I. The defining features are: equal capitals, equal areas, unequal fertility. Its measure is \"the difference between the yield of capital invested on the worst, non-rent-bearing land, and that of capital invested on better land.\" This is the origin of the entire theory. The crucial structural characteristic of this case, Marx stresses, is spatial dispersion : \"capital investments in different land areas alongside one another, so that each new investment of capital corresponded to a more extensive cultivation and an expansion of the cultivated area.\" Extensive cultivation in this technical sense means cultivation that expands outward across the soil rather than concentrating capital on already-cultivated soil. Against this, Marx poses the question that defines the chapter: \"Can it make a difference, then, whether sums of capital are invested successively in time on the same piece of land with varying productivity, or invested alongside one another on different pieces of land, as long as we assume that the results are the same?\" The phrase \"as long as we assume that the results are the same\" is doing decisive work: Marx is not asking about differences in output, but about the form of investment (temporal succession vs. spatial coexistence) as such, holding the terminal results constant. II. The Formal Identity of Surplus-Profit Formation (pp. 812–813) Marx immediately concedes the first half of the answer: \"It cannot be denied, first of all, that as far as the formation of surplus profit is concerned, it is all the same.\" He then builds the canonical comparison. Case (i): £3 of production costs is spent on each of four acres of differing quality: - Acre A (worst): yields 1 qr ; £3 is therefore the production price of 1 qr and its governing market price. Rent = 0 . - Acre B : yields 2 qrs ; surplus profit = £3 . - Acre C : yields 3 qrs ; surplus profit = £6 . - Acre D : yields 4 qrs ; surplus profit = £9 . Case (ii): The same results are obtained \"by the application of this £12 in production costs or £10 of capital in the same sequence to one and the same acre.\" So the same physical product—1, 2, 3, 4 quarters in successive or simultaneous doses—is produced by successive investment on a single acre. Two technical clarifications must be registered here. First, \"production price\" (price of production) = cost price + average profit; in this example £10 of capital corresponds to £12 of production costs, implying a 20% rate of profit (£2 profit on £10 capital), and therefore each £2½ portion of capital carries a production cost of £3. Second, the \"quantum\" (qr = quarter of wheat) is the unit of corn product, and it is crucial that the production price per quarter is set not by the average cost but by the least productive capital portion—the one that yields only 1 qr. On these assumptions, Marx writes: \"In each case there is a capital of £10, with successive portions of £2½ being invested.\" Whether these £2½ portions lie side by side on four differently fertile acres or are applied one after another to the same acre, \"one of these capitals of £2½ yields no surplus profit, while the other portions give a surplus profit, each in proportion to the difference between its yield and that of the non-rent-bearing investment.\" The rate of surplus profit for each portion of capital is thus formed in a uniform way in both cases. And since rent is nothing but a form of this surplus profit—surplus profit forming its substance —the magnitude of the potential rent is the same in both cases. III. The Divergence Appears: The Problem of Transformation (p. 813) Having established identity, Marx drives in the wedge: \"None the less, the second method does give rise to certain difficulties as regards the transformation of surplus profit into rent, this change in form that involves the transfer of surplus profits from the capitalist farmer to the proprietor of the land.\" This is the chapter's first and in some ways most important move. The \"change in form\" is the legal-economic transmutation of surplus profit (which belongs to whoever invests the capital) into ground-rent (which accrues to the landowner as the price of permission to use the soil). Under DR I, this transformation is comparatively stable: the different capitals sit on different farms, and the rental for each farm can be assessed and fixed independently. Under DR II, however, the surplus profits arise after the lease is signed, from investments the farmer himself makes. Marx draws out the concrete consequences: The farmers' resistance to agricultural statistics. \"Hence the stubborn resistance of the English farmers to any official agricultural statistics.\" Farmers know that accurate yield data would arm the landlord to recalibrate the rent at renewal. The class struggle over lease terms. \"The rent here is fixed when the farms are leased, and the subsequent surplus profits arising from the successive investments of capital accrue to the farmer as long as the tenancy contract lasts.\" Therefore \"the farmers' battle for long tenancies,\" and conversely the landlords' preference for \"tenancies at will\" (leases terminable at a year's notice), which let the proprietor capture the fruits of the tenant's improvements at short intervals. The professionalization of rent assessment. Marx invokes John Morton's Resources of Estates to describe the rise of the \"assessor of rents,\" a \"very important, complicated and difficult profession\" peculiar to countries where agriculture is intensive —and he defines \"intensive\" economically: \"the concentration of capital on the same piece of land instead of its distribution over adjacent tracts.\" Two further mechanisms of transformation are noted. In the case of permanent improvements (drainage, soil amendment, etc.), \"the artificially inflated differential fertility of the land is its new natural fertility when the tenancy contract expires\"—that is, an improvement that is artificial (capital-produced) at the time of investment becomes indistinguishable from natural fertility at the moment of re-letting, and is accordingly capitalized into the rent. In the case of working capital , \"the level of rent for a working capital of given size is added to the average rent for the land,\" so that the incoming tenant must be guaranteed enough capital \"to continue cultivation in the same intensive manner\"—a device that insulates the landlord's rental stream from the risk of under-capitalized successors. IV. First Stress-Point: DR I as the Historical and Logical Basis (pp. 814–815) Marx now lists the points that must be stressed in analysing DR II. Firstly, its basis and point of departure, historically and dynamically, is DR I. The historical argument unfolds in stages: Colonies and pre-capitalist agriculture. In colonies, \"the colonists need only invest a little capital; the main agencies of production are labour and the soil itself. Each individual family head seeks an independent field of employment for himself and his people to work on.\" Agriculture proper, even in pre-capitalist modes, has this dispersed character. Pastoralism. In sheep-farming and stock-raising as an independent branch, exploitation of land is \"more or less communal\" and \"fundamentally extensive from the outset.\" The genesis of the capitalist mode. Capitalist production \"develops out of earlier modes of production in which the means of production are either in law or in fact the property of the tiller himself\"—i.e., out of agriculture as a handicraft . Concentration of the means of production into capital (over against wage-labourers) happens only gradually. The characteristic early form. Capitalist agriculture first assumes its characteristic form in sheep-farming and stock-raising —but, crucially, not by concentrating capital on a small area. The saving is \"on the keeping of horses and other production costs,\" and the mode is \"production on a larger scale,\" i.e., extensive. This is a subtle point: early capitalism in agriculture economizes by spreading capital widely, not by intensifying it. The natural-law turning point. \"It follows from the natural laws of farming... that given a certain level of agriculture and the corresponding exhaustion of the soil, capital... becomes the decisive element in cultivation.\" As long as tilled land is a small fraction of untilled land and the soil's natural resources are unexhausted (the phase when stock-raising and meat-eating predominate), the capitalist mode distinguishes itself from peasant production chiefly \"by the amount of land that is tilled for the account of one capitalist\"—i.e., extensive use of capital on a greater area. The conclusion is double-edged: DR I is the historical starting-point from which development proceeds, and simultaneously the \"variegated basis\" on which DR II's movement at any given moment occurs. Even at a developed stage, successive investments on one acre presuppose a landscape of coexisting lands of differing fertility whose differentials provide the reference points. V. Second Stress-Point: Capital Distribution, Creditworthiness, and the Uneven Development of Capitalist Agriculture (pp. 815–816) Secondly , Marx introduces a factor that supplements fertility differences: differences in the distribution of capital (and creditworthiness) among farmers. The argument is an analogy with manufacture. In any branch of industry, a minimum scale of business and a corresponding minimum capital emerge, below which a business cannot be conducted at all; above the minimum, a normal average amount of capital emerges, which the bulk of producers possess. Capital above this norm yields extra profit ; capital below it \"does not receive even the average profit.\" But here is the disanalogy. Capitalist production \"takes hold of agriculture only in a slow and uneven manner, as we can see in the case of England, the classical land of the capitalist mode of production in this sector.\" Where there is no free import of corn (or where its effect is restricted by the Corn Laws), \"the market price is determined by those producers who work on inferior soil, i.e., producers whose conditions of production are less favourable than the average\"—and a large part of the total agricultural capital is in the hands of just such producers. Marx inserts a sharp aside on the peasant: \"the peasant, for example, devotes a great deal of labour to his small parcel of land. But this labour is isolated, and deprived of the objective social and material conditions of productivity; it is denuded of them.\" The peasant's labour, unassisted by the social organization of production and material aids, does not constitute productive power in the capitalist sense. From the coexistence of under-capitalized producers with genuinely capitalist farmers, Marx derives the conclusion: \"the genuinely capitalist farmers are in a position to appropriate a portion of surplus profit; this would disappear... if the capitalist mode of production were as uniformly developed in agriculture as in manufacture.\" In other words, a portion of what appears as differential rent under DR II is not a fertility-differential at all but a capital-and-credit differential —a surplus accruing to the better-financed farmer against the inferior producer whose costs regulate the market price. VI. The Core Numerical Demonstration: DR II Reproduces DR I on a Single Acre (pp. 816–817) Setting aside for the moment \"the conditions under which this surplus profit can be transformed into ground-rent,\" Marx turns to pure formation. His conclusion is stated flatly: \"differential rent II is simply a different expression of differential rent I, and the same thing as far as its nature is concerned.\" The reasoning: the differing fertility of different land types affects DR I only insofar as capitals give unequal results; \"it can make no difference to this differing fertility or its product... whether this inequality marks different capitals invested successively on the same piece of land or whether the capitals are invested on several pieces of land of different types.\" In DR I different kinds of land do for different capitals of equal size what in DR II the same land does for successive portions of capital. The land simply exhibits differential fertility toward successive doses. The proof is a re-computation of Table I. The £10 that was spread as four independent £2½ capitals across A, B, C and D is now invested successively on one acre of D, with the first dose yielding 4 qrs, the second 3 qrs, the third 2 qrs, and the last 1 qr (or in the inverse sequence). The £3-per-quarter price set by the least fruitful dose (the 1-qr dose) still governs, and \"does not yield any differential rent, though it would determine the production price as long as it is necessary to supply wheat whose production price is £3.\" Assuming capitalist production, the £3 includes the average profit any £2½ capital yields, so the three remaining doses each throw off a surplus profit proportional to the excess of their product over the 1-qr dose. The result: (4−1) + (3−1) + (2−1) = 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 qrs = £18 , exactly the aggregate rent of B, C and D in Table I. The minimum product of the worst land (1 qr per £2½) is simply transposed into the minimum product of the marginal capital dose. This is the concrete meaning of \"DR II presupposes DR I.\" VII. Declining Returns: When They Matter and When They Do Not (p. 817) Marx now unfolds a four-dose example on D, in which the doses yield 4, 1, 3, 2 quarters. The first dose (£2½ → 4 qrs) bears 3 qrs of differential rent. The second (£2½ → 1 qr) is a non-rent-bearing capital investment —\"just like the same capital on the worst land A\"—yielding only the average profit, with no surplus profit to transform into rent. Critically, \"this declining product of the second capital investment on D would not have any effect on the profit rate\"—it is as if £2½ were freshly invested on another acre of A. It therefore leaves the surplus profit, and hence the differential rent, on A, B, C and D untouched. The farmer then makes two further £2½ investments yielding 3 qrs and 2 qrs . Compared with the original 4-qr dose, these represent a decline in the level of surplus profit (2 and 1 qrs respectively instead of 3), but this decline \"would affect neither the average profit nor the governing production price.\" It is only a fall in the surplus-profit rate on the marginal doses—an internal matter for the farmer's bookkeeping. This changes only if the additional production **throws ac ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 39 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 41"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 41", "title": "Chapter 41", "subtitle": "Differential Rent II — First Case: Constant Price of Production", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 41/Chapter 41 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 5308, "read_time": "27 min read", "tags": ["Differential Rent (I & II)"], "keywords": ["Differential Rent II: First Case (Constant Production Price)", "Fertility Increases Compensating Diminishing Returns", "Successive Capital Investments on Same Land", "Rent Generation without Additional Land Clearing", "Intensive Cultivation Dynamics with Stable Market Prices"], "text": "Chapter 41 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 5,271 words ⏱️ ~26 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 41 of Capital , Volume Three, belongs to Part VI — \"The Transformation of Surplus Profit into Ground-Rent.\" It follows Marx's prior construction of Differential Rent I (rent arising when equal capitals are applied to lands of unequal fertility, treated in Chapter 39) and the opening of Differential Rent II (rent arising when unequal capitals are applied to the same land, introduced in Chapter 40). Chapter 41 conducts the first systematic analysis of the latter by isolating one controlling variable: the price of production is held constant . The chapter thus assumes that the market price continues to be governed by the capital invested on the worst, rentless land, A, so that A's production price — capital advanced plus average profit — remains the \"regulating\" bench-mark against which every additional outlay on the better lands B, C and D must be measured. The central thesis is stated almost immediately and then systematically demonstrated through a four-case taxonomy (I–IV). Whenever additional capital is invested on rent-bearing land while the price of production remains fixed, the absolute mass of rent per acre rises — regardless of whether the supplementary capital is as barren as land A (Case I: no change), yields a product strictly proportional to its size (Case II: proportional rise), yields surplus profit at a declining rate (Case III: absolute rise, sub-proportional), or produces more than the original capital (Case IV: super-proportional rise). This rising level of rent per acre — as opposed to the aggregate rental — Marx calls \"a phenomenon that is peculiar to differential rent II and distinguishes it from differential rent I.\" Methodologically , the chapter is a controlled arithmetical thought-experiment. Marx takes Table I of Chapter 39 — four acres (A, B, C, D), each receiving £2½ of capital at 20 per cent average profit, producing 1, 2, 3 and 4 quarters respectively, all sold at £3 per quarter — and transforms it into Table II (capital doubled everywhere, per-unit productivity maintained) and Table III (capital doubled, but the second portion of outlay yields a smaller physical increment). The entire demonstration proceeds from these schedules rather than from historical data, allowing Marx to separate the effect of capital-intensification from price movements. Beyond rent theory proper, the chapter performs three intellectual tasks whose significance radiates outward. First, it argues that the arithmetic difference between land-type revenues — decisive for Differential Rent I — becomes analytically immaterial under Differential Rent II, since it merely reflects the pattern of further investment rather than a natural property of the soil. Second, it develops a two-theorem analysis of capital release and the productivity of labour, including a celebrated cloth example that distinguishes the release of capital from the sparing of accumulation, and a sharp critique of the capitalist's inverted perception that living labour is the most expensive element of production. Third, it formulates the spatial-versus-temporal distinction : identical total capital produces a higher rent per acre when concentrated successively on a restricted area than when scattered over a wider surface — a result explainable neither by natural fertility nor by quantity of labour, but \"exclusively in terms of the different kind of capital investments.\" Positioned at the threshold of the later DR-II cases (in which the price of production itself changes), Chapter 41 supplies the base case for all subsequent rent theory: the demonstration that capital intensification alone, with prices constant, is sufficient to raise rent per acre. It also anticipates themes — released capital, accumulation, the falling rate of profit paired with a rising absolute mass of profit — that recur throughout the volume. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS Framing the Problem: Why the \"First Case\" Must Hold Production Prices Constant Marx opens with a methodological stipulation that silently governs everything that follows: \"This assumption implies that the market price continues to be governed by the capital invested on the worst land A.\" In Marx's rent theory, the production price of agricultural output is determined by the worst land in cultivation, because that land must be farmed to meet effective demand and therefore its production price — cost of production plus average profit — becomes the regulating price at which every other (better) parcel sells its output. Because A yields no surplus profit at this price, it defines the level above which the better lands B, C and D earn surpluses, and these surpluses are the differential rent . Freezing this regulating price is thus not arbitrary: it is the device that lets Marx isolate the effect of varying the capital applied without contaminating the result with a simultaneous shift in the price scale. The analytical division of labour between the two differential rents accordingly sharpens. Under Differential Rent I , capital per acre is held constant and the land types differ; rent is the product of equal capitals earning unequal returns because the soil differs. Under Differential Rent II , the land type is held constant and the capital applied varies; rent is the product of unequal capitals earning varying returns on a single plot. Chapter 41 asks the base-case question of DR-II: what happens to rent when extra capital is poured onto rent-bearing land, given that the governing price never moves? Case I — The Nil Case: Additional Capital as Barren as Land A The first possibility explored is deliberate sterility. If additional capital invested on any of B, C or D \"is only as productive as the same capital on land A, i.e. if at the governing price of production it yields only the average profit and thus no surplus profit, the effect on rent is nil.\" The mechanics are transparent once differential rent is understood as surplus over the average profit earned by the regulating capital . Rent exists only because a better parcel yields more output per unit of capital at the same selling price, and that excess output converts into a surplus profit at the ruling price. If the incremental outlay produces no more per unit of capital than A does, then it earns exactly the average profit and no surplus, so no differential surplus crystallizes. Marx's characterization is exact: \"It is the same as if a number of acres of quality A, the worst land, had been added to the area previously cultivated.\" The comparison between B, C, D and A — the very ratio from which rent is calculated — remains untouched, and rent stands where it was. The point is that rent is a differential magnitude, not an absolute yield; barren additions cannot move it. Case II — Proportional Investment: Table II and the Demotion of the Arithmetic Difference The second case assumes the opposite extreme short of improvement: \"additional capitals produce extra products in proportion to their size.\" Here the rate of productivity per unit of capital is preserved. Table I of Chapter 39 thus becomes Table II. In Table I, each acre received £2½ of capital, incurred £½ of average profit at 20 per cent, and therewith a price of production of £3; A brought in 1 quarter, B 2, C 3 and D 4, all sold at £3 per quarter, so A's proceeds of £3 just covered its price of production and yielded nothing, while B earned £3 of surplus (1 quarter), C £6 (2 quarters) and D £9 (3 quarters) — corresponding to surplus-profit rates of 120 per cent, 240 per cent and 360 per cent on the capital of £2½. Table II simply doubles the capital to £5 per acre, generating £1 of profit, a price of production of £6, and outputs of 2, 4, 6 and 8 quarters on A, B, C and D. Proceeds become £6, £12, £18 and £24; rents become 2, 4 and 6 quarters (£6, £12, £18); and — crucially — the rates of surplus profit remain 120 per cent, 240 per cent and 360 per cent, exactly as before. Marx stresses that \"it is unnecessary here for the capital investment on all types of land to double\"; the law only requires that production on each type increase in the same ratio as the capital. Rent therefore \"rises simply as a result of increased capital investment on the land and in proportion to this increase of capital.\" Marx then draws a consequential equivalence. Because the outcome — in respect of mass and value of product and surplus product — is identical whether the extra £2½ is added to the same acre or laid out on a second acre of the same quality , the whole surface-level distinction between a temporal addition of capital and a spatial addition of land temporarily dissolves: \"the result would remain the same if the additional capital of £2½ per acre were invested on a second acre each of B, C and D.\" This equivalence is precisely what Case III will disrupt. The sharp analytic nuance of this section is the demotion of arithmetic differences . In Differential Rent I, the difference in productivity between land types — measured at the same capital outlay — is what generates rent, and a change in that arithmetic difference signals a change in rent. Under DR-II, however, if the additional capital is not distributed uniformly, the simple arithmetical gaps between land revenues can shift markedly without any corresponding change in rent-determining productivity. Marx's example: if the extra capital goes solely to B and D, the difference between D and A in total output becomes 7 quarters (against 3 before), between B and A becomes 3 quarters (against 1), and between C and B turns negative — −1 instead of +1. Yet \"this arithmetical difference, which is decisive in the case of differential rent I, in so far as it expresses the difference in productivity for the same capital investment, is here quite immaterial, since it is simply the result of different further investment or non-investment of capital, given the same difference for each equal portion of capital on the various lands.\" The lesson is durable: under DR-II, one must read the per-unit-of-capital proportional differences, not the totals, because the totals now encode the uneven deployment of capital rather than any natural property of the soil. Case III — Declining Returns: Table III, the Falling Rate and the Rising Mass of Surplus Profit The third case introduces the realistic scenario in which supplementary capital still produces surplus profit but \"at a declining rate and not in proportion to their increase.\" This is the analytically richest case, and it generates Table III. Capital on B, C and D rises from £2½ to £5 per acre, but the second portion of outlay is less productive: B's second £2½ yields only 1½ quarters (instead of the 2 that strict proportionality would produce), C's yields 2 (instead of 3), and D's yields 3½ (instead of 4). Total outputs become 1 (A), 3½ (B), 5 (C) and 7½ (D); proceeds at £3 per quarter become £3, £10.50, £15 and £22.50; prices of production become £6 per acre on the better lands. Rents therefore become 1½, 3 and 5½ quarters — £4.50, £9 and £16.50. Now compare the surplus-profit rates with Table I: B falls from 120 to 90 per cent; C from 240 to 180 per cent; D from 360 to 330 per cent. Every rate declines — yet every absolute rent rises (B from £3 to £4.50; C from £6 to £9; D from £9 to £16.50; the total from £18 to £30). Marx articulates the general law with a deliberate parallel to the falling tendency of the general rate of profit: \"The rate of surplus profit declines, in relation both to the extra capital and the total capital invested on the land, but the absolute amount of surplus profit increases; just as the falling rate of profit on capital in general is usually combined with an increasing absolute mass of profit.\" He illustrates with land B: its average surplus profit is now 90 per cent on the capital deployed, against 120 per cent for the first outlay alone — yet the total surplus profit rises from 1 quarter to 1½ quarters, from £3 to £4.50. \"The total rent taken by itself — and not in relation to the doubled sum of capital advanced — has risen absolutely.\" Marx then emphasizes the generality of the result by stripping away all incidental detail: it is immaterial \"whether the extra investments of capital put in second time round fall uniformly on the various types of land or not; whether the declining production of surplus profit proceeds in equal or unequal proportions; whether the additional capital investments all fall on the same rent-bearing type of land; or whether they are distributed, uniformly or not, on rent-bearing lands of different quality.\" The only assumption needed is that extra capital yields surplus profit, but in declining proportion . He then fixes the bounds within which the decline must move. On the same capital investment, the maximum product attainable is 4 quarters (£12) — the product of the original capital on the best land D; the minimum is 1 quarter (£3) — the product of the same capital on the worst land A, which bears no rent and gives no surplus profit. Any marginal investment on a rent-bearing parcel must produce somewhere between 1 and 4 quarters if it is to yield some surplus (above 1 quarter) but less than the original (below the D-level maximum). Marx strengthens the point by redescribing Case III in the language of Case II: whereas Assumption II \"implies that new plots of land of the same quality as the better types are added to the cultivated area,\" Assumption III \"implies that additional plots of land are tilled whose degree of fertility varies between D and A, between that of the best land and that of the worst.\" The reason is intuitive: successive capital applications on a single parcel (say D) can traverse, one after another, productive capacities equivalent to the spectrum D → A — hence the equivalence to cultivating \"virtual\" new plots of intermediate quality. Successive investments confined to C, by contrast, can encompass only the differences between C and A and C and B; confined to B, only the difference between B and A. The law of Case III is therefore that \"the rent on all these types of land grows absolutely, even if not in proportion to the additional capital invested.\" And a final nuance caps the case: the differences between the rents of the several types, and their mutual ratios, may alter — but \"this change in these differences is here the result of the wider spread of rents and not its cause.\" The shifting ratios are a symptom of differential intensification, not an independent source of rent; the underlying cause remains the augmented capital applied at a constant price. Case IV, the Two Meanings of \"Saving,\" and Released Capital The fourth case — \"extra capital investments on the better types of land produce a greater product than the original\" — is dismissed briskly as self-evident: \"It is immediately comprehensible how on this assumption the rents per acre rise, and in a higher ratio than the extra capital.\" The additional outlay is combined with an improvement , so rent rises faster than capital because both the base productivity and the marginal productivity have risen. Marx then pauses to make a subtle distinction within Case IV: the case in which a small extra capital produces the same or greater effect than a previous, larger addition. Though it might be conflated with Case II, it is not identical, and the difference matters \"for all capital investments.\" Suppose £100 yields £10 of profit (10 per cent), and £200 yields £40 (20 per cent). The rate has risen from 10 to 20 per cent — \"and in this respect it is the same as if £50, applied in a more effective way, gave a profit of £10 instead of £5.\" The surface resemblance to Case II is that both show a rising rate; the real difference is that \"in the one case I have to double the capital, while in the other I produce the doubled effect with the same capital as before.\" To fix the distinction, Marx enumerates three possible configurations, all with the same value-cost outcome but radically different implications for wealth: the same product as before with half the living and objectified labour; double the previous product with the same labour; four times the previous product with twice the labour. In the first configuration, labour is \"set free\" in either living or objectified form and can be applied elsewhere. Marx draws a conclusion of broad economic significance: \"The release of capital (and labour) is in itself an increase in wealth; it has exactly the same effect as if this extra capital was obtained by accumulation, but it spares the task of accumulation.\" Release and accumulation are functionally equivalent in their wealth effect, but release is cheaper to obtain — it does not require the prior act of saving. This is one of the chapter's most portable analytical insights. He then crystallizes the three configurations in a cloth example. A capital of £100 produces 10 metres of cloth; with equal constant and variable capital plus profit, the cost is £10 per metre. If the same £100 can produce 20 metres, the cost per metre falls to £5. If instead £50 can produce the same 10 metres, the cost is also £5 per metre — and a capital of £50 is now released , \"in so far as the former supply is still sufficient.\" If, finally, £200 is required to produce 40 metres, the cost is still £5 per metre — but no capital is released. The distinction is that \"in the first case capital is released; in the second case extra capital is spared, given that twice the production is required; in the third case the increased product can be obtained only by a growth in the capital advanced, although not in the same proportion as if the increased product had had to be supplied at the old level of productivity.\" Marx explicitly defers the value-and-price aspects of this analysis to \"Part One\" — acknowledging that his immediate concern is the rent-relevant distribution of the released resources, not the theory of value itself. The Colonist's Innocence and the Capitalist's Inverted Cost Perception Marx now shifts from the arithmetic of released capital to its ideological and subjective dimension. \"Considered from the standpoint of capitalist production,\" he writes, a fall in the cost price has the same effect on the capitalist's profit as a rise in surplus-value itself, since profit is the residue of selling price over cost of production. It therefore appears \"always cheaper to employ constant capital rather than variable.\" This presupposes \"the development of credit and the abundance of loan capital that corresponds to the capitalist mode of production.\" He sharpens the point with a value comparison. Take an additional constant capital of £100 — \"the product of five workers over a year\" — and an additional variable capital of £100. If the rate of surplus-value is 100 per cent, the five workers set in motion by the variable £100 produce £200 of value, whereas the £100 of constant capital is worth £100 (or perhaps £105 as a capital-claim if the rate of interest is 5 per cent). \"The same sums of money express very different values.\" A further subtlety reinforces the point: if the £100 of constant capital is fixed capital, only its wear and tear enters the value of the commodity, whereas the £100 paid in wages must be completely reproduced in the commodity's value. Both facts make constant capital appear \"cheaper\" to the individual capitalist. Contrast the colonist or independent petty producer, who has no access to capital or only at usurious rates. For such a producer, the portion of the product that represents wages is revenue , not a capital advance. The outlay of labour appears to be an indispensable precondition for the proceeds of that labour, and any labour beyond necessary labour realizes itself as excess product that appears to have \"cost them nothing, as it has not cost any objectified labour.\" Only the expenditure of objectified labour is felt as \"an alienation of wealth.\" Hence \"even a sale below value and the capitalist price of production still seems to them a profit, as long as this profit is not anticipated by incurring any debt, mortgage, etc.\" For the capitalist, in contrast, both constant and variable capital are advances. And because the relatively greater advance of constant capital reduces the cost price (and also reduces the value of commodities), the capitalist is led to the conclusion — Marx calls it a \"capitalistically distorted form\" — that living labour is \"the most expensive element in his production costs, which should be reduced to the smallest possible minimum.\" Marx's verdict is normative and analytic at once: this is \"simply a capitalistically distorted form of the correct statement that the relatively greater application of past labour, compared with living, means an increase in the productivity of social labour and greater social wealth.\" The correct statement is a statement about social productivity ; the distorted form converts it into a statement about individual cost minimization , which then misidentifies the source of profit (living labour) as the item most to be economized. \"This is how everything appears from the standpoint of competition: incorrectly, and standing on its head.\" The Boundary Case of Land A and the Consistency of the Governing Assumption Marx next specifies the limits of what can happen on land A itself, given the assumption of constant production prices. On the better lands (B and above), extra capital may be applied with constant, increasing or decreasing productivity. On A, however, only two cases are compatible with the constant-price premise: either productivity stays unchanged — in which case A \"would continue to bear no rent\" — or productivity increases , in which case \"one part of the capital invested on land A would then bear rent, the other not.\" It would be \"impossible on the assumption that A's productivity declines, for in that case the production price would not remain constant, but would rise.\" This is a self-consistency check on the chapter's own framework: if A — the regulating parcel — becomes less productive, its production price (cost plus average profit, per quarter) rises, and with it the whole price level; the chapter's foundational assumption collapses. The scope of what can happen to A is thus strictly bounded, and the boundary is set by the chapter's own premise, not by any empirical accident of soil. Concentration versus Scattering: Why Successive Investment Raises Rent per Acre From these preliminaries Marx extracts the chapter's most striking general conclusion. \"Under all these circumstances,\" he writes — whether the surplus product brought in is proportionately above, below or equal to the proportion of capital added, and thus whether the surplus-profit rate stays constant, rises or falls — \"the surplus product and the surplus profit per acre corresponding to it increase, and so too therefore, potentially, does the rent, in corn and in money.\" The reason is arithmetic but conceptually fundamental: rent per acre is the mass of surplus profit reckoned on a constant unit of land . As capital invested on the acre grows, the mass of surplus profit per acre grows with it (whether proportionally, more than proportionally, or less), and because the denominator — the acre — is fixed, the level of rent per acre necessarily rises. \"The growth in the simple mass of surplus profit or rent, reckoned per acre ... is expressed as a growth in the proportion.\" Crucially, the degree of the rise can be amplified or dampened by whether productivity is constant, falling or rising, but \"the fact that it does grow\" is independent of those factors: \"The latter factors modify the degree to which the level of rent per acre grows, but not the fact that it does grow.\" This is the phenomenon peculiar to DR-II. Marx drives the point home with a spatial-versus-temporal comparison. If additional capital is invested \"alongside one another in space on new additional land of the appropriate quality,\" instead of \"successively in time on the same land,\" then while the mass of rental and the average rent over the whole cultivated area both rise, the rent per acre — the rent level — does not. The old acres still yield what they always yielded; new acres add to the aggregate rather than the per-acre intensity . \"With the result remaining the same, as far as the mass and value of the total production and the surplus product are concerned, the concentration of capital increases the level of rent per acre on a more restricted area, whereas under the same conditions its scattering over a greater area, with other factors remaining the same, could not produce this effect.\" Marx then derives a historical tendency: \"The more the capitalist mode of production develops, however, the more the concentration of capital on the same area increases, so that the rent per acre rises.\" Rising rent per acre becomes, on this account, a structural feature of capitalist agricultural development, not merely a consequence of soil fertility or price movements. He generalizes this into a cross-country theorem. Suppose two countries share the same production prices, the same land-type differences and the same total capital invested, but in one the capital is applied \"more in the form of successive investments on a restricted area\" and in the other \"more in the form of coordinated investments on a wider area.\" Then \"the rent per acre and therefore the land price would be higher in the first country and lower in the second, even though the total rental in both countries was the same.\" Since land price is essentially capitalized rent, higher rent per acre translates into a higher land price. \"This difference in the levels of rent could thus be explained neither in terms of a difference in the natural fertility of the land types nor in the amount of labour applied, but exclusively in terms of the different kind of capital investments.\" This is the chapter's most theoretically aggressive claim: two economies could be identical in natural endowment, price level and aggregate capital, and yet exhibit different rents per acre and land prices, purely as a function of the organizational form — intensive versus extensive — of capital deployment. Terminological Caveat: Surplus Product as an Aliquot Portion The chapter closes with a terminological clarification that guards against a recurring confusion. \"In speaking of a surplus product here, we mean the aliquot portion of the product in which the surplus profit is expressed.\" The word \"aliquot\" here carries its precise mathematical sense: a proportional part of a whole — the fraction of the physical output that corresponds to the surplus profit specifically , i.e., to rent. In general usage, however, \"surplus product\" means \"the portion of the product in which the total surplus-value is expressed, or in particular cases the portion that represents the average profit.\" Because rent theory uses the narrower, rent-specific sense — the aliquot part embodying only the surplus over average profit — while general surplus-value theory uses the broader sense, \"the specific meaning that this term obtains in the case of rent-bearing capital can give rise to misunderstandings, as we saw previously.\" The caveat is not pedantry; it is a warning that the reader must not conflate two distinct quantities — the portion of output representing rent (an aliquot differential) and the portion representing total surplus-value (average profit plus rent) — each of which might casually be called the \"surplus product.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The chapter establishes a principle that operates almost as a systemic law within Marx's rent theory: differential rent is a function of the relationship between the mass of capital applied at a fixed price and the fixed unit of land on which rent is measured . Because rent per acre divides the surplus profit yielded on a given plot by that plot's constant extent, any increase in capital applied — under constant prices — mechanically raises the denominator-relative numerator, i.e., the rent level. This is why the direction of productivity change matters for degree but not for the fact of rising rent per acre. Case II (proportional productivity), Case III (declining productivity) and Case IV (increasing productivity) all generate higher rents per acre; they differ only in how much higher. Case I is the exception that proves the rule: with no surplus profit earned, no rent can rise. Marx's parallel to the falling rate of profit with rising absolute mass of profit is not decorative; it signals that rent theory obeys the same dialectic of rate and mass that governs capital in general — a rate may fall while an absolute magnitude rises. Two further principles emerge. The first is the demotion of arithmetic differences under DR-II : the simple gap between land revenues that determines rent under DR-I ceases to determine anything once capital deployments diverge, because the gap then encodes the pattern of investment rather than native soil quality. The second is the equivalence of released capital with accumulation : a productivity gain that frees capital has \"exactly the same effect as if this extra capital was obtained by accumulation,\" yet spares the social labour of accumulation — an insight that anticipates modern treatment of technical progress as a form of capital-saving growth. The chapter's most important broad implication is the spatial-versus-temporal theorem and its corollary: two economies identical in price level, land-type distribution and aggregate capital may nevertheless differ in rent per acre and land price solely because of differences in the organizational form of capital — intensive concentration versus extensive scattering. This has two striking consequences. First, land price cannot be reduced to natural fertility or to quantity of labour; it is partly an artifact of capital-organization form. Second, since capitalist development intensifies capital concentration on a given area, the historical tendency is toward rising rent per acre and rising land prices — an endogenous, not exogenous, result of the mode of production itself. Third, and most subversively, Marx exhumed an ideological structure within the cost-accounting perception of the capitalist: because constant capital enters the cost price only as wear and tear (if fixed) and because its advance is smaller relative to output than the advance of wages, the capitalist perceives living labour as the most expensive element of production — the very element, Marx insists, that is the source of profit. This is the \"capitalistically distorted form\" of the true proposition that increasing past labour relative to living raises the productivity of social labour. The chapter thus ties the technical arithmetic of rent to a critique of the capitalist's form of appearance : what appears as cost minimization and profit maximization is, on inspection, an inverted representation of the wealth-generating role of living labour and of the social productivity gains embodied in past labour. Finally, the terminological caveat about the \"aliquot portion\" cautions that the very vocabulary of surplus product is polysemous , and that rent theory's localized use of the term must be kept distinct from its general use in surplus-value theory. Read as a whole, Chapter 41 is not only the analysis of the first case of Differential Rent II; it is a hinge — an analytic bridge from DR-I's fertility-difference logic to DR-II's capital-intensity logic — and a demonstration that some of the most consequential economic magnitudes (rent level, land price) depend on how capital is organized in space and time , not merely on how much of it exists. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 40 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 42"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 42", "title": "Chapter 42", "subtitle": "Differential Rent II — Second Case: Falling Price of Production", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 42/Chapter 42 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 8554, "read_time": "43 min read", "tags": ["Differential Rent (I & II)"], "keywords": ["Differential Rent II: Second Case (Falling Production Price)", "Agricultural Improvements and Price Declines", "Technical Progress in Agriculture", "Shift of Regulating Price of Production to Better Soils", "Falling Food Prices Reducing Industrial Wage Costs"], "text": "Chapter 42 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 9,140 words ⏱️ ~46 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 42 of Volume III of Capital — \"Differential Rent II: Second Case — Price of Production Falling\" — is a rigorous, almost laboratory-like exercise in Marx's theory of ground-rent. Its central problem is deceptively narrow but theoretically decisive: what happens to differential rent when the governing production price of agricultural produce falls, and this fall is brought about by additional capital investments on the better (rent-bearing) lands? The chapter is the second of three cases of Differential Rent II (the rent that arises from successive capital investments on the same land, as opposed to Differential Rent I, which arises from equal investments on lands of different natural fertility). Having completed the case where the production price rises in earlier material and the case of Differential Rent I in full, Marx here isolates the mechanics of a falling price. The chapter's thesis can be stated as a series of linked propositions. First, a falling production price does not by itself annihilate rent ; on the contrary, it can coexist with, and even be the vehicle of, a rising corn rent and a stable or rising money rent per acre. Second, the fall in price is only possible when capital is withdrawn from the previously governing — rentless — worst land (A), so that a new Differential Rent I forms, this time with the next better land (B) as the new \"no-rent\" benchmark. Third, the outcome for total rent depends on a small set of determinate variables: the ratio in which the price falls, the ratio of the differences among the better lands, the amount of extra capital newly invested, and its distribution across the different soil qualities. Fourth, and most subtly, Differential Rent II introduces an element absent from Differential Rent I: when successive investments become the normal mode of cultivation, the individual contributions of each separate capital advance are averaged and blurred into a single \"normal\" capital requirement per acre — a social average that then functions, for purposes of rent and tenancy contracts, as if it were a natural property of the soil itself. The chapter's intellectual objective is therefore twofold. Analytically, it constructs a complete parametric map of the falling-price case across three sub-variants — constant productivity of the extra capital, falling productivity, and rising productivity — showing in each how corn rent and money rent move, sometimes in opposite directions. Polemically, it delivers a sharp methodological blow against the tradition (classical political economy) that treats corn rent — rent measured in bushels or quarters — as economically meaningful in itself. Marx demonstrates that corn rent and money rent can move in opposite directions, so that a landlord may become richer relative to the produce of his land while receiving less corn rent in absolute terms, and vice versa. The chapter sits at the hinge of Part VI (\"The Transformation of Surplus Profit into Ground-Rent\"). It presupposes the whole preceding apparatus: production price = cost price + average profit; surplus profit = excess of individual production price over the governing (regulating) monetary return; the conversion of that surplus profit into rent by virtue of landed property. Its central diagrammatic device is a family of numerical tables (I–VIa) in which four types of land A, B, C, D of ascending fertility expose how rent behaves when the price-governing terrain shifts from A to B. It is a work of comparative statics: Marx holds the given differences between lands constant and varies the intensive margin (capital per acre) and the extensive margin (which land governs the price) to isolate the pure logic of rent under falling prices. For the reader, mastering it yields the essential Marxian insight that ground-rent is not a fixed quantity extracted from nature but a fluid social magnitude that rises or falls with the scale and distribution of capital investment on the land. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS Framing the Field: The Three Sub-Variants and Their Logical Order The chapter opens with a synoptic statement of its scope: \"The production price may fall while productivity on the additional investments of capital remains constant, falls or rises.\" This single sentence generates the entire architecture. Marx will treat, in order: (I) a falling price with constant productivity of the extra capital; (II) a falling price with falling productivity; and (III) a falling price with rising productivity. The ordering is not arbitrary. Variant I is the \"purest\" case because, by assuming that each unit of extra capital yields produce in proportion to itself, it strips away any change in the technical return on capital and lets the reader see the rent-effects of the falling price as such . Variants II and III then re-introduce technical change, but in opposite directions, bracketing the result. Throughout, a crucial distinction must be kept in view. Productivity of the extra capital refers to the physical yield per unit of capital on a given soil. The rate of surplus profit (for a parcel of land) is the money surplus profit expressed as a percentage of the capital advanced on that land — it is not identical to physical productivity, because it also depends on the governing price. Keeping these distinct is the key to understanding why, in certain tables, the rate of surplus profit remains constant while the total rent collapses . Section I: Constant Productivity of the Extra Capital The Assumption and Its Immediate Consequence Marx stipulates that \"the product from the various types of land, corresponding to their respective quality, grows to the same extent as does the capital invested on them.\" This is a proportionality assumption: if you double the capital on land C, you double C's output; if you triple it, you triple it. Combined with the assumption that \"the differences between types of land remain the same,\" the result is that the surplus profit on each land grows in step with the capital invested. From this, one conclusion follows immediately and is stated with emphasis: \"any surplus investment of capital on land A does not affect the differential rent. On this land, the rate of surplus profit is zero; it therefore remains zero.\" The rationale is the cornerstone of the whole theory. Land A is the price-governing land: it is the least fertile land whose cultivation is still necessary to meet demand, and therefore it yields no differential surplus — its individual production price equals the governing production price. If A's productivity rises proportionally with new capital (say doubling along with all others), the relative position of A is unchanged, and because A sets the price, nothing about the rent structure is disturbed. A cannot pay rent to itself. This is why extra capital invested on the price-governing land is \"quite immaterial for the differential rent as such.\" The Condition for a Falling Price If extra capital on the rent-bearing lands B, C, D proportionally increases their output, then a falling price can occur only if the totality of this extra produce makes the produce of A redundant . In Marx's words, the governing production price \"can fall under these assumptions only when the governing factor ceases to be the production price of A, the latter's place being taken by the next better land B or some other land better than A.\" This requires that \"the extra product of the additional capital investments satisfies the demand, and hence the production of the inferior land A, etc. is superfluous for the supply required.\" This is the precise logical relationship: the cause of the price fall is not a technological miracle on the best land but the eviction of the worst land from cultivation by the increased supply flowing from more intensive cultivation of the better lands . The sequence is: more capital on B/C/D → more total output → demand now satisfiable without A → A ceases to be cultivated → A ceases to regulate the price → the next land (B) becomes the regulator → the governing price falls to B's individual production price. And because B now occupies A's old position as the rentless price-governing land, B's own rent disappears , even though nothing has changed in the natural differences among B, C, and D. As Marx puts it bluntly: \"a new differential rent I has been formed in which the better land B plays the same role as the inferior land A did before.\" This is a profound point. Rent on B was not a physical fact about B; it was a relational fact about B's position in the spectrum of cultivated soils. Move the baseline, and B's rent evaporates — not because B became less fertile, but because B became the new benchmark against which all rent is measured. Table IV: The First Illustration Marx takes Table II (from the preceding chapter, where A still regulated the price) and modifies it so that \"instead of 20 qrs, 18 qrs now satisfies the demand.\" A drops out; B, with a production price of 30s. = £1½ per quarter, becomes the governing land. The resulting structure is: Type of land Acres Capital (£) Profit (£) Price of prod. (£) Output (qrs) Selling price (£/qr) Proceeds (£) Rent in corn (qrs) Rent in money (£) Rate of surplus profit B 1 5 1 6 4 1½ 6 0 0 0 C 1 5 1 6 6 1½ 9 2 3 60% D 1 5 1 6 8 1½ 12 4 6 120% Total 3 15 3 18 18 27 6 9 Two definitions are embedded here that govern every subsequent table. The \"price of production\" column (£6) is the individual production price of the produce of that parcel: capital advanced (here £5) plus the average profit (here £1). The \"selling price\" (£1½ per quarter) is the governing market price, set by the worst cultivated land (now B). Proceeds = output × selling price. Rent in money = proceeds − individual price of production (e.g. C: £9 − £6 = £3). Rent in corn = money rent ÷ selling price (C: £3 ÷ £1½ = 2 qrs). The rate of surplus profit = money rent ÷ capital advanced (C: £3 ÷ £5 = 60%). The comparative findings are sharp. \"The total rent, therefore, compared with Table II, would have fallen from £36 to £9, and in corn from 12 qrs to 6 qrs, though the total production has fallen only by 2 qrs, from 20 qrs to 18 qrs.\" In other words, a small absolute change in output (a 10% contraction) produces a massive collapse in rent (a 75% fall in money rent). \"The rate of surplus profit, reckoned on the capital, would have fallen to a third of its former level, from 180 per cent to 60 per cent. Thus a decline in both corn and money rent goes together here with the fall in the production price.\" Compared with Table I (the base table where A was still the regulator), the outcome is subtler: \"there is simply a decline in the money rent; the corn rent in both cases is 6 qrs, but in the one case this amounts to £18, in the other case to £9.\" For land C specifically, \"the corn rent has remained the same as in Table I.\" The crucial mechanism is again relational: A's product \"has been displaced in the market by the additional production obtained from the uniformly operating additional capital, and land A thus excluded as a competing agent of production.\" \"The part of the product that is transformed into rent falls.\" The Alternative: Where the Extra Capital Is Placed Matters Marx now varies the distribution of the extra capital. If the task of satisfying demand \"had been brought about by the investment of more than twice the capital on C or D,\" the outcome differs. Suppose a third capital investment (an additional £2½ beyond the initial £5) is made on C: Table IVa — additional investment on C: Type Acres Capital (£) Profit (£) Price of prod. (£) Output (qrs) Selling price (£/qr) Proceeds (£) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) Rate of surplus profit B 1 5 1 6 4 1½ 6 0 0 0 C 1 7½ 1½ 9 9 1½ 13½ 3 4½ 60% D 1 5 1 6 8 1½ 12 4 6 120% Total 3 17½ 3½ 21 21 31½ 7 10½ Here \"the product on C has risen from 6 qrs in Table IV to 9 qrs, the surplus product from 2 qrs to 3 qrs, the money rent from £3 to £4½.\" Yet relative to Tables II and I this C-rent \"has now fallen\" (£4½ against £12 and £6 respectively). The total corn rent (7 qrs) has fallen against Table II (12 qrs) and risen against Table I (6 qrs); the money rent (£10½) has fallen against both. Marx then dispatches a degenerate case in one sentence: if the third investment of £2½ were applied to land B , it \"would certainly have altered the amount of production, but it would have left the rent unaffected, since the successive capital investments are assumed not to produce any difference on the same type of land, and land B does not yield any rent.\" This is the constant-productivity assumption doing its work: extra capital on the price-governing land can never generate differential rent on that land, because all of its produce is absorbed at the governing price with no surplus. If instead the third investment goes to land D : Table IVb — additional investment on D: Type Acres Capital (£) Profit (£) Price of prod. (£) Output (qrs) Selling price (£/qr) Proceeds (£) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) Rate of surplus profit B 1 5 1 6 4 1½ 6 0 0 0 C 1 5 1 6 6 1½ 9 2 3 60% D 1 7½ 1½ 9 12 1½ 18 6 9 120% Total 3 17½ 3½ 21 22 33 8 12 The total product (22 qrs) is now \"more than double that of Table I, even though the capital advanced is only £17½ as against £10, i.e. less than double.\" On D the corn rent has grown from 3 qrs (Table I) to 6 qrs, while the money rent has stayed at £9; compared with Table II the corn rent is unchanged (6 qrs) but the money rent has halved (from £18 to £9). Total corn rent (8 qrs) now exceeds both Table I (6) and IVa (7), though still below Table II (12); the money rent (£12) exceeds IVa (£10½) but is below I (£18) and II (£36). Restoring the Total Rental: Tables IVc and IVd Marx asks a sharp comparative question: how much extra capital would be required, under the IVb conditions, for the total rental to recover the Table I level of £18 — even though the rent on B has vanished? \"We must have a further £6 surplus profit, i.e. 4 qrs at £1½,\" the new production price. He then calculates the \"capital required\" for this on each land: \"On C, £5 capital yields 2 qrs surplus product, and so £10 additional capital will give 4 qrs additional surplus profit. On D, £5 additional capital will be sufficient to produce the 4 qrs additional corn rent.\" Table IVc — extra capital concentrated on C: Type Acres Capital (£) Profit (£) Price of prod. (£) Output (qrs) Selling price (£/qr) Proceeds (£) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) Rate of surplus profit B 1 5 1 6 4 1½ 6 0 0 0 C 1 15 3 18 18 1½ 27 6 9 60% D 1 7½ 1½ 9 12 1½ 18 6 9 120% Total 3 27½ 5½ 33 34 51 12 18 Table IVd — extra capital concentrated on D: Type Acres Capital (£) Profit (£) Price of prod. (£) Output (qrs) Selling price (£/qr) Proceeds (£) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) Rate of surplus profit B 1 5 1 6 4 1½ 6 0 0 0 C 1 5 1 6 6 1½ 9 2 3 60% D 1 12½ 2½ 15 20 1½ 30 10 15 120% Total 3 22½ 4½ 27 30 45 12 18 Both restore the total money rental of £18 (which \"would be exactly half of what it was in Table II\"), and both double the corn rent (from 6 to 12 qrs). The path differs: IVc achieves it by pouring capital into the less fertile of the two rent-bearing lands (C), requiring £27½ total capital; IVd achieves it by concentrating capital on the most fertile (D), needing only £22½. This establishes a law: the greater the fertility of the land on which the extra capital is concentrated, the less capital is required to restore or raise the money rent — because a given quantity of surplus product (needed to offset the price fall) can be produced with less capital where nature is more generous. The Great Comparison with Table I The pivot of Section I is the comparison of IVc and IVd against Table I: \"the total money rental remains the same, i.e. £18, despite a fall of a half in the production price, from 60s. per qr to 30s., and the corn rent has accordingly doubled, i.e. from 6 qrs to 12 qrs.\" Several sub-results deserve excavation: Rent on B has disappeared (from £3/1 qr to zero), yet total rent is unchanged. On C , money rent \"has risen by a half in IVc, and fallen by a half in IVd.\" On D , money rent \"remained the same, £9, in IVc, and risen from £9 to £15 in IVd.\" Production rose \"in IVc from 10 qrs to 34 qrs, and in IVd to 30 qrs\"; profit rose from £2 to £5½ (IVc) and £4½ (IVd); total capital \"has risen in the one case from £10 to £27½, in the other from £10 to £22½, i.e. in both cases to more than double.\" The rate of rent (rent ÷ capital advanced) is \"the same throughout in Tables IV to IVd\" for each land — a direct consequence of the constant-productivity assumption. But compared with Table I it \"has fallen both for the average of all types of land and for each individual type\": from an average of 180% in I to about 65⅓% in IVc and 80% in IVd. The average money rent per acre has risen : from £4½ per acre over 4 acres (Table I) to £6 per acre over 3 acres. On the rent-bearing land alone, the average rose from £6 to £9 per acre. Yet the 12 qrs of corn rent are \"now less than half of the total product of 34 or 30 qrs, whereas in Table I the 6 qrs made up three-fifths of the total product of 10 qrs.\" This last point is the conceptual payoff: \"even though the rent has fallen, taken as an aliquot part of the total product, and similarly if reckoned on the capital laid out, its money value reckoned per acre has risen, and its value in product still more.\" Rent can simultaneously shrink as a share and grow as an absolute magnitude per acre . The reason is that the price fall (50%) is more than offset by the growth in the surplus product (100%). Marx then generalizes with a numerical illustration on land D: in Table I, D's money rent (£9) was three times its £3 production costs and almost four times its £2½ capital; in IVd, D's money rent (£15) is \"almost exactly equal to the production costs and only a fifth greater than the capital\" — yet the money rent per acre is \"two-thirds greater, £15 instead of £9.\" And in Table I, D's corn rent of 3 qrs was three-quarters of its 4-qr product; in IVd, at 10 qrs, it is half of the 20-qr product. Likewise, the rent's share of total product value falls from \"more than half\" (£18 of £30) in Table I to \"less than half\" (£18 of £45) in IVd. The General Law and Its Four Determinants Marx states the causal law that generates the entire result: \"The reason why despite the fall of £1½ per qr in the price … the total money rent remains the same while the corn rent doubles … lies in the fact that more quarters of surplus product are produced. The corn price falls by 50 per cent, the surplus product grows by 100 per cent.\" But he adds a critical proportionality caveat: \"in order to bring about this result, the total production must grow by a factor of three, under the conditions we have set, and the capital investment on the better types of land must more than double.\" The amount of capital required depends above all on how the extra capital is divided between the better and the best types of land — assuming productivity grows in proportion to capital. He then identifies the four determinants that fix the specific proportion: The ratio in which the price falls — i.e. the gap between B (the new rentless land) and A (which it replaced); The ratio of the differences among the better lands from B upward; The amount of new extra capital invested ; Its distribution across the various qualities of land. He also extends the analysis to counterfactuals: if the price fall were smaller , less extra capital would be needed for the same money rent; if a greater supply were needed to evict A — which \"depends not only on the product per acre of A but also on the proportionate share that A takes out of the total cultivated area\" — then money and corn rent \"would have grown still further … even though both disappeared on land B.\" To underline that everything here is relative , Marx gives two limiting comparisons. If the capital withdrawn from A had been £5, the comparison would be between Table II and IVd: the total product would rise from 20 to 30 qrs, but the money rent would be only half as large (£18 instead of £36), while the corn rent would be unchanged at 12 qrs. And if a total product of 44 qrs = £66 could be produced on D with £27½ of capital (preserving D's old ratio of 4 qrs per £2½), the total rental would again reach the Table II level: Type Capital (£) Output (qrs) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) B 5 4 0 0 C 5 6 2 3 D 27½ 44 22 33 Total 37½ 54 24 36 Here the total product is 54 qrs (against 20 in Table II) and money rent is the same £36, but the capital advanced is nearly double (£37½ against £20). Marx derives the general tendency: \"if the price falls as a result of the investment of excess money capital on the lands yielding higher rent … while productivity remains the same, the total capital tends not to grow in the same proportion as production and the corn rent; so that the fall-off in the money rent that results from the falling price may be balanced by a rise in the corn rent.\" He also registers the distributional asymmetry: capital \"must be greater in the proportion that it is applied more to C than to D, more to the land bearing less rent than to that bearing more rent.\" The reason is straightforward: to keep or increase money rent, \"a definite additional quantity of surplus product must be produced, and this requires less capital, the greater the fertility of the lands yielding surplus product.\" The Reduction to the First Case, and the Polemic on Corn Rent Marx then makes an elegant theoretical reduction: \"this law expresses nothing more than was already developed in the first case: that if the production price is given, whatever its level might be, the rent can rise as a result of extra capital investment.\" The exclusion of A simply installs \"a new differential rent I with B now as the worst land and £1½ per qr as the new production price.\" It is \"the same law, simply that land B is taken as the starting-point instead of land A and the production price as £1½ instead of £3.\" The practical significance he flags is that the process \"may be accompanied by a rising, a falling, or a stable rent per acre, if not on all lands, then at least on some, and for the average of the lands tilled.\" This leads to a memorable polemic against corn rent : \"We have seen that corn rent and money rent do not behave in the same way. It is only tradition, however, that still gives corn rent any role in economics.\" He mocks the error by analogy: \"One might just as well prove that a manufacturer could buy far more of his own yarn with a profit of £5 than he formerly could with a profit of £10.\" The analogy exposes the fallacy of treating a physical quantity as if it were a measure of economic gain. The real-world implication is that \"the landowning gentlemen, if they also happen to own or have a share in manufacturing, sugar refining, spirit distilling, etc., can still draw very considerable profits while money rents are falling, as producers of their own raw materials.\" In other words, a landlord integrated into processing his own agricultural raw materials gains precisely because the raw material (his falling-price corn) is cheap, while his manufactured output sells at industrial prices. Footnote 34: Engels's Editorial Caveat The section closes with an editor's note by Friedrich Engels that is theoretically important in its own right. Engels records that \"an error in calculation running through the above tables IVa to IVd made it necessary to rework them.\" He insists, however, that \"this in no way affected the theoretical perspectives developed from the tables,\" though \"it did lead in places to quite monstrous numerical ratios for production per acre.\" He defends these ratios by analogy with topographic and relief maps, which legitimately use a larger vertical than horizontal scale. He offers a practical fix: the reader may multiply the number of acres by any figure, or interpret the \"1, 2, 3, 4 qrs per acre\" of Table I as \"10, 12, 14, 16 bushels (8 bushels = 1 qr).\" The conclusion — \"the relationship between rise in rent and rise in capital, still comes out just the same\" — is a valuable methodological reminder that the relational structure of the tables, not the absolute magnitudes, is what carries the theory. Section II: A Falling Rate of Productivity for the Extra Capital This section is deliberately brief, because its principal finding has already been established. Marx states: \"Nothing new is involved here except that the production price can also fall, as in the case last considered, if the extra capital investments on better types of land than A make A's product superfluous and hence cause capital to be withdrawn from A, or if A is applied to the production of a different crop.\" The mechanism of the price fall is identical to Variant I. The difference is the technical premise: now the additional capital advances yield less than proportionally more produce — the \"law of diminishing returns\" of successive investment, in Marx's treatment not as a natural law but as a historically contingent tendency of intensive cultivation. He reminds us that \"the corn and money rents per acre may grow, decline, or remain the same,\" and reproduces Table I in its original form for comparison: Table I [a] — the baseline: Type Acres Capital (£) Profit (£) Price of prod. per qr (£) Output (qrs) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) Rate of surplus profit A 1 2½ ½ 3 1 0 0 0 B 1 2½ ½ 1½ 2 1 3 120% C 1 2½ ½ 1 3 2 6 240% D 1 2½ ½ ¾ 4 3 9 360% Total 4 10 2 10 6 18 180% Two features of this table are worth noting because they frame the whole chapter. First, the \"price of production per qr\" column declines with quality (3, 1½, 1, ¾), reflecting higher fertility: with £3 of production price (capital £2½ + profit ½) each land produces more quarters, so its individual cost per quarter is lower. Second, the governing price is A's £3 per quarter; rent is the excess of the governing price over each land's individual production price. A yields no rent; D yields the largest. Now, Marx assumes \"that a figure of 16 qrs supplied by B, C and D, with a declining rate of productivity, is sufficient to remove A from cultivation,\" so that Table III is transformed into Table V: Table V — falling productivity, price falls, A evicted: Type Acres Investment of capital (£) Profit (£) Output (qrs) Selling price (£/qr) Proceeds (£) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) Rate of surplus profit B 1 2½+2½ = 5 1 2 + 1½ = 3½ 1½ 6 0 0 0 C 1 5 1 3 + 2 = 5 1½ 8½ 1½ 2½ 51⅓% D 1 5 1 4 + 3½ = 7½ 1½ 12½ 4 6½ 137½% Total 3 15 3 16 27½ 5½ 9½ The critical comparative results are: \"Here, with a declining rate of productivity on the extra capitals and a varying decline on the different types of land, the governing production price has fallen from £3 to £1½. The capital investment has risen by half, from £10 to £15. The money rent has fallen by almost half, from £18 to £9½, but the corn rent by only a twelfth, from 6 qrs to 5½ qrs. The total product has risen from 10 qrs to 16 qrs, or by 60 per cent. The corn rent is somewhat over a third of the total product. The capital advanced stands in a ratio of 15 : 9½ to the money rent, whereas the previous ratio was 10 : 18.\" The lesson embedded here is a striking asymmetry between corn and money rent: capital rises 50%, output rises 60%, yet money rent collapses by almost half while corn rent falls by only one-twelfth. The reason is the halving of the governing price, which slashes the money valuation of a nearly constant corn surplus. As a share of output, corn rent falls from three-fifths (6 of 10) to \"somewhat over a third\" (5½ of 16). This is the \"falling productivity\" case where money rent is hardest hit relative to production — but crucially, it still is rent, and the mechanism (eviction of A, new Differential Rent I on B) is identical to Variant I. Section III: A Rising Rate of Productivity for the Extra Capital The Special Position of Land A Marx opens by distinguishing this variant from Variant I \"simply by the way that, if a given additional product is needed to remove land A from cultivation, this happens more speedily in the present case\" — because rising productivity on the extra capital means that a smaller capital outlay suffices to generate the surplus product that evicts A. But he immediately adds a caveat: the effect \"can be very uneven, according to how the investments are distributed over the different types of land.\" Depending on whether the varying effect \"tends to even out the differences or to intensify them, the differential rent on the better types of land will fall or rise, and so too … will the total rental, as was already the case with differential rent I.\" Everything depends on the \"size of the land area and capital that is displaced with A\" and \"the relative amount of capital which has to be advanced.\" The section's distinctive analytical contribution is its treatment of land A under rising productivity . Marx rehearses the logic of the prior variants to isolate what is new: In the case of a constant price , \"the excess capital that might be invested on land A is quite immaterial for the differential rent as such, since now as before land A bears no rent.\" In Variant I (falling price, constant productivity), \"land A necessarily drops out, and still more so in variant II (falling production price with a falling rate of productivity), since otherwise the excess capital on land A would necessarily increase the production price.\" But in Variant III — falling price because productivity of the excess capital rises — \"this additional capital can under certain circumstances be invested as well on land A as on the better types of land.\" This is the pivot. Whereas under constant or falling productivity, extra capital on A would raise A's individual production price and thus the governing price (defeating the very purpose), under rising productivity on A, A's individual production price can fall , so extra capital on A is compatible with a falling governing price. This makes the \"improvement\" of the worst land a genuine competitive possibility. Table VI: Rising Productivity on All Lands Marx assumes \"an extra capital of £2½ invested on land A produces 1½ qrs instead of 1 qr,\" and applies the same proportional improvement to the better lands: Table VI — rising productivity, falling price: Type Acres Capital (£) Profit (£) Price of prod. (£) Output (qrs) Selling price (£/qr) Proceeds (£) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) Rate of surplus profit A 1 2½ + 2½ = 5 1 6 2½ ~2⅖ 6 0 0 0 B 1 5 1 6 ~4½ ~2⅖ 12 ~2¼ 6 120% C 1 5 1 6 ~6¾ ~2⅖ 18 ~4½ 12 240% D 1 5 1 6 ~9 ~2⅖ 24 ~6¾ 18 360% Total 4 20 4 24 ~22 60 ~13½ 36 240% (The manuscript's figures, as Engels acknowledges in footnote 34, contain internal arithmetic roughness; the money rents £6/£12/£18 and the rates of surplus profit 120%/240%/360% are the load-bearing values.) The key comparisons: \"the corn rent has risen to more than double as against Table I, and by 1½ qrs as against Table II; while the money rent has doubled as against I, but is unchanged from II.\" That is, money rent returns to the Table II level (£36) while corn rent exceeds Table II's (13½ > 12). The rate of surplus profit, 240%, is higher than the 180% of Table I. Marx adds that rent \"would have grown significantly either if the extra capital had fallen more on the better types of land, or alternatively if the effect of the extra capital had been less on A, so that the governing average price per qr from A was higher.\" Why a Rent Can Appear on A — and How It Is \"Averaged Away\" Marx now develops the deepest conceptual material of the chapter. He asks what would happen if the price did not fall but remained at £3. A — previously rentless with £2½ of capital — would now yield a rent, without any inferior land being drawn in, because productivity increased on the same land for part of the capital. \"The first £3 in production costs brings in 1 qr; the second £3 brings in 1½ qrs; the total product of 2½ qrs, however, is now sold at its average price.\" Since the rate of productivity grows with the extra capital, this \"implies an improvement,\" which may consist either in \"the application of more capital as such to each acre (more fertilizer, more mechanized labour, etc.)\" or in the fact \"that it is only with this extra capital that a qualitatively different and more productive investment of capital can be brought about.\" But this rent on A is transitory and conditional. \"Leaving aside transitory market conditions, the product of land A could continue to be sold at a higher production price … only if a significant area of class A land continued to be cultivated with a capital of only £2½ per acre. But as soon as the new proportion of £5 per acre, and hence this improved mode of operation, became universal, the governing production price would have to fall to £2⅖.\" At that point, \"the distinction between the two portions of capital would disappear,\" and an acre of A tilled with only £2½ \"would be abnormal and would not be tilled according to the new conditions of production. The distinction would no longer be between the products of different portions of capital on the same acre, but rather between a satisfactory total capital investment per acre and an unsatisfactory one.\" From this Marx extracts two general lessons : Inadequate capital mimics natural infertility. \"When a large number of farmers have insufficient capital (it has to be a large number, for a small number would simply be compelled to sell below their production price), this has just the same effect as the differentiation of types of land themselves in a diminishing series. The poorer type of agriculture on worse soil increases the rent on the better; it can even create a rent on better cultivated land of the same poor quality, which this would not otherwise yield.\" This is a crucial sociological point: rent can be socially manufactured out of differences in capital endowment rather than natural fertility. Under-capitalization on the poor soils functions as a \"diminishing series\" and props up rent on the better-cultivated land. Successive investments are averaged into an indistinguishable norm. \"differential rent, in so far as it arises from successive investments of capital on the same total area, is actually reduced to an average in which the effects of the different capital investments can no longer be recognized or distinguished.\" These investments do not produce rent on the worst lands; rather, they (1) \"make the average price of the total product, say on an acre of A, into the new governing price,\" and (2) \"present themselves as changes in the total amount of capital per acre required under the new conditions for satisfactory cultivation of this land, in which both the individual successive capital investments and their respective effects melt indistinguishably together.\" The same holds for the differential rents of the better lands, which \"are in any case determined by the difference between the average product of the type of land in question and the product of the worst land, in a situation where an increased investment of capital has now become normal.\" The \"Normal\" Minimum Capital and the Tenancy Contract Marx widens the argument to a general principle of political economy: \"No land yields any product without a capital investment. This is true even in the case of simple differential rent, differential rent I.\" Whenever we say that an acre of A yields such-and-such a product at such-and-such a price, and that B, C, D yield differential products and hence ground-rent, \"this always assumes that a definite capital is applied, i.e. that considered normal under the given conditions of production. Just as in industry a definite minimum of capital is required in each line of business to produce commodities at their price of production.\" The analogy to industry is exact: just as a manufacturer below the normal minimum capital will fail to realize the average profit, so a farmer cultivating with less than the normal capital per acre will sell below the governing price. When this minimum changes due to successive investments, \"it happens only gradually.\" As long as a certain number of acres of A do not receive the extra working capital, rent on the better cultivated acres of A is generated (the price being constant), and the rents on B, C, D are thereby increased. \"But as soon as the new type of cultivation has spread sufficiently to become normal, the production price falls; the rent for the better lands falls again, and the portion of land A that does not possess what is now the average working capital must sell below its individual production price, i.e. below the average profit.\" Marx then gives a concrete historical illustration: \"this average capital investment, e.g. £8 per acre before 1848 in England and £12 per acre afterwards, is what provides the standard when tenancy contracts are drawn up.\" This connects the abstract theory of rent to the concrete institution of the lease. \"For the farmer who spends more than this, the surplus profit is not transformed into rent for the duration of the lease. Whether this happens when the contract expires will depend on the competition of those farmers in a position to make the same extra advance.\" He carefully distinguishes this from \"permanent improvements to the land, which continue to provide an increased product with the same outlay of capital or even a declining one. Although these are the product of capital, they operate just like the natural differential quality of the soil.\" What Differential Rent II Adds to Differential Rent I Marx here isolates the theoretical novum of Differential Rent II: \"differential rent II involves an element that does not develop as such in the case of differential rent I, since this can persist independently of any change in the normal capital investment per acre.\" Three elements are new. First, \"the results of different capital investments on the price-governing land A are blurred, their product simply appearing as the normal average product per acre.\" Second, \"there is a change in the normal minimum or average size of the capital outlay per acre, so that this change appears as a property of the soil.\" Third — of particular institutional importance — \"there is a distinction in the way the surplus profit is transformed into the form of rent.\" Whereas in Differential Rent I rent is determined by the natural difference between lands, in Differential Rent II it is also determined by the historical-social normalization of a particular capital intensity, which then becomes the yardstick of tenancy and rent. The Conditions Under Which Rent Rises or Falls Marx closes the chapter with a systematic enumeration of the conditions determining the movement of corn and money rent. The overarching finding: \"when the production price falls as a result of a rising rate of productivity on the extra capital investment … the rent per acre for a doubled capital investment, say, may not just double, but can more than double. However, it might also fall, if the production price were to fall much lower as a result of a rapid growth in productivity on land A.\" He illustrates the falling case with Table VIa, in which \"the additional investments of capital, on B and C for example, did not increase productivity in the same proportion as on A, so that the proportionate differences for B and C would decline\": Table VIa — uneven improvement (A and D improve more; B, C less): Type Acres Capital (£) Profit (£) Output per acre (qrs) Selling price (£/qr) Proceeds (£) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) A 1 2½ + 2½ = 5 1 1 + 3 = 4 1½ 6 0 0 B 1 5 1 2 + 2½ = 4½ 1½ 6¾ ½ ¾ C 1 5 1 3 + 5 = 8 1½ 12 4 6 D 1 5 1 4 + 12 = 16 1½ 24 12 18 Total 4 20 4 32½ 16½ 24¾ \"The rent on D would then rise and that on B and C fall, as compared with the case of Table II.\" Note that here A's productivity rises so much (from 1 to 4 qrs per £5, i.e. a 4× improvement) that the governing price falls to £1½, raising D's rent in corn (to 12 qrs) while lowering B's and C's. This confirms the \"unevenness\" thesis: differential rent is not monotone in productivity; it is driven by the pattern of productivity change across lands. Marx then lays out the full set of directional conditions: Money rent rises if \"more capital is added to the rent-bearing than to the non-rent-bearing land, in conditions where the proportionate differences in the additional fertility remain the same, and more capital is added to the lands of higher rent than to those of lower rent,\" or if \"given the same additional capital, the fertility on the better and best lands grows more than on A.\" In the latter case, \"the rent rises in relation to the degree to which the increase in fertility is greater in the superior categories of land than in the inferior ones.\" Money rent falls \"if the improvement resulting from extra capital investment reduces the differences, either all or some, by having more effect on A than on B and C.\" Whether the corn rent rises, falls, or remains stationary \"depends on the degree of unevenness in this effect.\" Corn rent follows money rent where the price is stable, but can move independently when the price moves. The Absolute Conclusion: Additional Capital as the Cause of Rent The chapter's final paragraph delivers the absolute theoretical point that transcends all the parametric variation: \"Under all circumstances, however, the rent experiences a relative rise if the increased productivity is the result of a new addition of capital and not simply of increased fertility for a constant capital investment.\" This is the \"absolute point of view.\" It shows that, as in all earlier cases, \"the rent per acre … is the result of increased capital investment on the land,\" whether that capital functions \"with a constant rate of productivity in a situation of constant or falling prices, with a declining rate of productivity in a situation of constant or falling prices, or with an increasing rate of productivity in a situation of constant or falling prices.\" Marx then makes an important logical reduction: the apparent combinatorial explosion of cases — constant price with constant/falling/rising productivity, and falling price with constant/falling/rising productivity — \"can be reduced to the assumption of a constant rate of productivity for the excess capital in a situation of constant or falling price, a falling rate of productivity in a situation of constant or falling price, [and] a rising rate of productivity with constant and falling price.\" And the invariant that survives all permutations is this: \"Even though in all these cases the rent may remain stationary or even fall, it would fall further if the additional application of capital, in otherwise unchanged conditions, were not the condition for higher fertility. The additional capital is then always the cause of the relatively high level of rent, even though this may have fallen in absolute terms.\" This is the dialectical conclusion of the chapter: the very forces that can depress rent in absolute terms (falling prices, eviction of worst lands) are inseparable from the forces that sustain it relatively (intensified capital). Rent is not a natural datum but the social form assumed by surplus profit generated by capital investment on landed property. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The Fundamental Principles Revealed Several systemic laws emerge from the chapter's dense comparative statics. First, rent is relational, not intrinsic. Land B's rent vanishes the moment B becomes the price-governing benchmark, even though B's fertility has not changed. What determines rent is not the land's inherent quality but its position within the spectrum of cultivated soils and the identity of the marginal land that regulates the price. This relational character is what makes rent a social category rather than a physical quantity. Second, corn rent and money rent are not interchangeable measures. The chapter's sharpest theoretical weapon is the demonstration that the two can move in opposite directions. In Tables IVc/IVd, money rent stays at £18 while corn rent doubles ; in Table V, money rent falls by almost half while corn rent falls by only a twelfth; in Table VI, money rent doubles relative to Table I while corn rent more than doubles. Marx's polemic — that \"it is only tradition\" which gives corn rent a role in economics — dismantles the illusion that physical produce measures economic gain. The landlord who receives fewer quarters of wheat may nonetheless command more value, and vice versa. Third, the rate of rent and the total rent are distinct magnitudes, and both are distinct from the rent's share of output. Table IVc/IVd shows rent per acre rising while rent as a share of total product falls and the rate of rent on capital falls . This three-way divergence is one of the most sophisticated results in the entire theory of rent. Fourth, differential rent II introduces a socio-historical \"norm\" of cultivation. The \"normal\" capital per acre (£8 before 1848, £12 after, in England) is a social average that functions, in tenancy contracts and in the very determination of rent, as though it were a natural property of the soil. This shows that rent under advanced capitalism is governed not only by nature but by the historically established intensity of farming, itself a product of capital accumulation. Fifth, under-capitalization can create rent. The demonstration that a \"large number of farmers with insufficient capital\" acts just like a \"diminishing series\" of natural soil qualities — generating rent on better-cultivated land of the same poor quality — grounds rent in the distribution of capital rather than exclusively in the distribution of fertility. Broader Practical, Theoretical, and Philosophical Implications Practically, the chapter explains the dynamics of the tenancy relationship with unusual precision. The distinction between \"average\" and \"excess\" capital per acre determines who captures the surplus profit: the farmer enjoys it during the lease (as he is not charged for improvements he alone financed), while the landlord captures it at renewal through competition among farmers able to make the same advance. This is a concrete theory of why agricultural improvement redistributes income between tenant and landlord, and why the duration and renewal of leases matters economically. Theoretically, the chapter completes and refines the general theory of Differential Rent II by mapping the falling-price case across all three productivity variants. It shows that the rent law established for the \"first case\" (rising price, developed earlier) is not peculiar to rising prices: the same relational logic — surplus profit as the excess of the governing over the individual production price — governs the falling-price case, simply with B substituted for A as the benchmark. The combinatorial reduction at the end — collapsing six configurations into three canonical ones — reveals the underlying unity beneath the bewildering table machinery. Philosophically, the chapter is a sustained demonstration of Marx's dialectical method. The categories of corn and money rent, of worst and best land, of constant and falling productivity, are shown to be mutually determining rather than fixed. Rent appears here neither as a natural rent of land nor as a simple deduction from output, but as the social form impressed upon a surplus profit that is itself the product of capital accumulated on landed property. The final and \"absolute\" conclusion — that additional capital is \"always the cause of the relatively high level of rent, even though this may have fallen in absolute terms\" — is the mature expression of the Marxian insight that in capitalist agriculture, as in industry, it is capital that produces value, while landed property merely appropriates a portion of it. Nature conditions the differences; capital and social relations determine what those differences are worth , and to whom. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 41 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 43"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 43", "title": "Chapter 43", "subtitle": "Differential Rent II — Third Case: Rising Price of Production", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 43/Chapter 43 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 11694, "read_time": "58 min read", "tags": ["Differential Rent (I & II)"], "keywords": ["Differential Rent II: Third Case (Rising Production Price)", "Resort to Inferior Soils and Rising Agricultural Rent", "Intensive Margin vs Extensive Margin", "Synthesis of Differential Rent Formulations", "Rent Growth at the Expense of General Social Surplus-Value"], "text": "Chapter 43 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 11,677 words ⏱️ ~58 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 43: Differential Rent II — Third Case: Rising Price of Production. Results 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 43 concludes Karl Marx's monumental exposition of differential rent in Capital , Volume III, Part VI (\"The Transformation of Surplus Profit into Ground-Rent\"). It constitutes the culminating third case of Differential Rent II (DR II): the scenario in which the price of production rises . Whereas the first case (Chapter 41) treated a constant price of production and the second case (Chapter 42) treated a falling price, the present chapter dismantles the logical anatomy of rent formation when the very yardstick of value — the governing production price — is itself ascending. The chapter is a unique hybrid: it contains the final analytic tables Marx composed, an extensive editorial reconstruction by Friedrich Engels, and a sweeping politico-economic coda on the historical fate of the landed aristocracy. At its core, the chapter defends a deceptively simple but radical thesis: Rising rents are the normal expression of agricultural development, not an anomaly. Whenever capital is successively applied to land — even with declining marginal productivity — the aggregate corn rent and money rent per acre, and especially the total sum of rent, tend to rise. This result does not depend on any increase in the intrinsic fertility of any particular plot. It depends solely upon the differences between the individual production prices of successive capital investments on the same land, and upon the fact that the price-governing land's production price operates as a fixed reference point from which all surplus profits are measured. Three intellectual tasks are fused in the chapter: First, Marx demonstrates that a rising production price can occur only under a very specific precondition: the productivity of the first (or base) capital investment on the rentless, price-governing land A must have declined. If land A yields less than one quarter per £2½ investment, the £6 in production costs (including 20% profit) must be recouped by a higher sale price per quarter. Because the production price is governed by the worst land, higher cost per unit on A automatically raises the price governing the entire market, and thereby raises rents on all better lands. Second, the chapter introduces a decisive structural innovation: the entry of an inferior soil designated a into cultivation. When land of even lower fertility than A is brought under the plough — as happens historically whenever demand outstrips supply from existing acreage — the formerly rentless land A is transformed into rent-bearing land, and a new Differential Rent I is superimposed upon the existing DR II. The result is that the rent of every higher quality of land becomes a constant (the rent of the newly rent-bearing marginal land) plus a proportional differential component. Marx expresses this with the now-canonical formula: if A's rent is n and the next-higher land's rent is n + m , the series of rents is n : n + m : n + 2m : n + 3m . Third, Engels — writing as editor, not as author — intervenes openly to correct and supplement Marx's manuscript. He critiques Marx's original tables on two grounds: (i) the assumed fertility ratios (1:2:3:4) are grotesquely exaggerated and produce absurd magnitudes; (ii) they encourage the false inference that rent is a function of absolute fertility rather than of fertility differences . To correct this, Engels composes a new series of fourteen tables (XI–XXIV) in bushels and shillings. These tables systematically exhaust the possible combinations of the three cases (constant, falling, rising price) with three variants each (constant, falling, rising productivity of the second investment), showing beneath every permutation the same underlying rent-law. The chapter then ascends from tables to theorem. In the long analytic core (pp. 861–871), Marx derives the general results of differential rent in its entirety. He demonstrates that surplus profits may arise either (a) on the basis of DR I, from the differential fertility of distinct plots, or (b) on the basis of DR II, from the varying differential productivity of successive capital investments on the same plot. In both cases, the transformation of surplus profit into rent presupposes the prior equalisation of the individual production prices of partial products into a single individual average production price . Only then can the excess of the general governing production price over that average be identified as rent. The crucial technical point is that whereas in DR I the differential results are directly observable (they lie side-by-side on distinct plots), in DR II they must be read back into DR I — that is, reconstructed from what is otherwise visible only as a single undifferentiated lump of grain. The chapter's most dramatic analytic result concerns the disappearance of rent . Marx constructs a numerical scenario in which increasing doses of capital are applied to land B with progressively declining productivity, until the individual average production price of B's total product coincides with the governing price of A at £3 per quarter. At that precise convergence, rent vanishes entirely — not because land has become less fertile in any absolute sense, but because the surplus profit accruing to earlier, more productive investments has been entirely absorbed into the formation of average profit, offsetting losses on the later, less productive investments. Marx then poses and answers a profound question: where does the surplus go? His reply: it becomes part of the average profit of the farmer, being the vehicle through which the surplus value captured earlier is redistributed. This is a brilliant demonstration that rent is not a thing but a relation — a determinant social distribution of surplus value that can be redistributed by the movement of capital itself. But — and here the political economy of the chapter bites — Marx then shows that under the law of landed property , this equalisation cannot occur. Because the farmer must continue to pay the contracted rent on the earlier capital investments, he cannot allow the surplus profits of those investments to be pooled with the losses of the later investments. The rent he pays to the landlord is not available to him as an internal offset. The consequence is that the differential rent itself becomes a barrier to further capital investment, more rapidly pushing up the governing production price than would otherwise be necessary — to the advantage of the landowner, who collects both higher rent on the old investments and higher rent on the new. This is the chapter's central discovery: landed property, through the mechanism of differential rent, places an artificial limit on the intensity of agricultural capital, converting what would otherwise be a normal equalisation of average profit into a systematic upward pressure on the price of food and raw materials. The chapter concludes with Engels' historical coda. He explains why, after the repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846, the English landed aristocracy — contrary to the manufacturers' expectation that it would be reduced to paupers — became richer than before . The explanation is a direct application of the chapter's law: the landlords insisted on larger required investments by tenants and secured state subsidies for drainage and improvements, and because marginal land was not permanently withdrawn, rents rose in proportion to the increased capital application. But Engels also shows the dialectical inversion: the transoceanic steamships and the railways in the Americas and India made virgin prairie, Argentine pampas, and tax-crushed Russian and Indian peasant land able to compete on European markets at a price determined by their costs alone — ruining the rents of European agriculture. \"Our 'second case, variant 2,' falling prices and falling productivity on the additional capital investment, became the rule in Europe, and hence the agrarian complaint from Scotland to Italy, from the south of France to East Prussia.\" The chapter thus closes with the historical supersession of the very rent-relation it has spent decades analysing. Placement in the broader architecture. This chapter sits at the theoretical apex of Marx's rent theory and, indeed, of his broader theory of distribution. It completes the systematic demonstration that ground-rent is not a payment for a natural force but a historically specific social claim on surplus value, mediated by landed property and dependent upon the difference between individual and general production prices. It is the last substantive chapter of Part VI (the next sections being the fragmentary outlines on absolute rent and the price of land). Immediately preceding it are the chapters that established the concept of differential rent, DR I on differentiated-plots, and DR II under constant and falling price. What follows, in Marx's plan and in Engels' editorial outline, are absolute rent, the price of land, and \"the transformation of surplus profit into rent\" as a general category. Chapter 43 is thus the hinge between differential rent in its narrower sense and the general theory of ground-rent that Marx knew he had not finished writing. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Precondition of a Rising Production Price: Declining Productivity on Land A The chapter opens without preamble, locking immediately onto the definitional precondition of the third case. Marx states that a rising price of production \"presupposes a decline in productivity on the lowest quality of land, which pays no rent.\" This is not a contingent fact but a necessary condition, and it follows directly from the mechanics of rent determination established in the preceding chapters. The governing production price of agricultural products is, by construction, the individual production price of the product of the worst land in cultivation — conventionally land A — whose yield fails to cover anything beyond the average profit and therefore produces no surplus profit, no rent. If the production price of the total product is to rise above the baseline of £3 per quarter, then either the £2½ invested on A must produce less than 1 quarter , or the £5 invested on A must produce less than 2 quarters , or \"a still poorer soil than A has to be brought into cultivation.\" Each of these three conditions is equivalent to the same substantive fact: A's productivity per unit of capital has declined, or its role as marginal producer has been assumed by a new, still worse plot. Now Marx introduces a nuance of considerable theoretical delicacy. He considers the possible case in which \"the productivity of the second capital investment remains the same or even rises\" — a case he claims could \"only be possible if the productivity of the first capital investment of £2½ had declined.\" This conditional is important because it clarifies the boundary of the case. The second investment's product interacts with the first's only through the aggregate yield per unit of land; if the second investment is as productive as before and yet the price per quarter rises, the deficiency must originate exclusively in the first investment. He then gives an agricultural metaphor that would not be out of place in a manual of husbandry: \"if the exhausted top-soil gives declining yields on superficial ploughing, as long as the old method of cultivation is maintained, until the subsoil subsequently supplies higher yields than before when rational techniques lead to its being turned up.\" But — and this is the chapter's first analytic caveat — Marx immediately acknowledges that \"strictly speaking, this special case does not belong here.\" The reason is a distinction internal to the theory of rent. If the productivity of the first capital investment of £2½ on A falls, then differential rent I itself changes too, because the rent of the better types of land must be measured against A, and A is now less fertile. The chapter is supposed to be about DR II — rent arising from successive applications of capital to the same land — and the case in which the base investment's productivity fails contaminates the DR I foundation on which DR II is built. Yet Marx allows that the case may be discussed here because it \"in fact represents the impact on II of a modification in differential rent I.\" The chapter will give one example of this, then move to the cleaner cases. Table VII embodies the case in which the productivity of the second investment exceeds the original productivity of the first investment. All lands A–D receive a first capital of £2½ yielding, respectively, 1, 2, 3, and 4 quarters (matching the base Table II). The second capital of £2½ yields, on A, ½ quarter; on B, 1½; on C, 2½; on D, 3½. The distribution therefore takes the form: the first investment produces one unit of productivity for A, the second produces only half — but for B onward, the second investment produces a positive differential over A's second investment (A's second = ½, B's second = 1½, C's second = 2½, D's second = 3½). Total outputs are A = 1½, B = 3½, C = 5, D = 7. The production price rises to reflect the reduced total: A's £5 capital + £1 profit = £6, divided by 1½ quarters = £4 per quarter (the table's stated 3⅞-£4 range is the average of the £5 base). Selling prices rise proportionally, and the total proceeds rise for each plot. Rent on B is £6, on C £12, on D £18 — all exactly as in Table II for the first investment — because, as Marx says, \"the increased governing price of production exactly makes up for the deficit in the quantity produced; since the two things vary in inverse proportion, it is evident that their product remains the same.\" The significance of this apparently trivial arithmetic cannot be overstated. It demonstrates that the sum total of rent — in corn and in money — is invariant under simultaneous proportional changes in yield and price, so long as the productivity differentials among plots are preserved. Rent is not a physical quantity; it is a value magnitude whose dimensions are determined relationally. Table VIII generalizes this by assuming \"the productivity of the second investment as simply the same as the original.\" A yields 1 + 1 = 2, B yields 2 + 2 = 4, C yields 3 + 3 = 6, D yields 4 + 4 = 8. The production price rises to £4 per quarter (the £5 is spread over 1½ quarters on A, but A now yields 2 quarters, so £6/2 = £3 — no, the table's logic is that A yields 1½ with the second investment contributing ½, so price rises to £4; using the OCR, the totals are A = 1½, B = 3, C = 4½, D = 6). The rents are: B = 1½ qrs = £6, C = 3 qrs = £12, D = 4½ qrs = £18 — the same total (£36) and same rates (120%, 240%, 360%) as in Table VII and Table II. Once again, output and price vary inversely and leave the rent totals undisturbed. Table IX isolates the pure case: \"the third case emerges in its pure form only in a situation of falling productivity on the second capital investment, while that on the first investment remains constant, as was assumed throughout in the first and second cases.\" Now DR I is untouched — A continues to yield 1 quarter with the first investment, B yields 2, C yields 3, D yields 4. But the second investment yields, on A, ½; on B, 1; on C, 1½; on D, 2. So the second investment is uniformly less productive than the first. Total outputs are A = 1½, B = 3, C = 4½, D = 6. The production price rises to £4 per quarter, and rents remain £6, £12, £18. Table IX is, as Marx says, \"the same as Table VIII, except that the decline in productivity in VIII falls on the first capital investment, while that in IX falls on the second.\" Here we see the crucial rotational symmetry: whether the reduction in productivity affects the first or the second investment, the resulting rent schedule is identical, so long as the total capital and total product are equal. Table X halves the productivity of the second investment still further — to one quarter on each division below the second — producing A = 1⅓? (Marx states \"the productivity of the second capital investment is reduced to a half and in the second to a quarter.\") The totals are A = 1⅓, B = 2⅔, C = 3⅓?, D = 5. Actually with the second investment reduced to a quarter of its original, second-investment yields become A = ⅓, B = ½, C = ¾, D = 1. Totals: A = 1⅓, B = 2⅔ (roughly 2½), C = 3¾, D = 5. The production price rises to a higher value (4½ per quarter). Rents (in corn): B = 1½, C = 2½, D = 3½; in money: £6, £12, £18; rate: 120%, 240%, 360%. Total rent remains 36 shillings equivalent — the invariance we have noted is exact. Transferring the case to inferior land: the entry of \"a.\" Marx then reframes the entire problem. \"What is the position, though, in the other possible situation, with a rising price of production, in particular if inferior land which it previously did not pay to cultivate is now taken into cultivation?\" Here we encounter one of the chapter's most significant structural moves. A new, still poorer plot, designated a (lower case, to distinguish it from the older marginal land A), is brought into competition. Because a now governs the price, land A — formerly rentless — now yields rent. Tables VIIa, VIIIa, and Xa show the consequences. For Table VIIa , land a has capital £5, profit £1, production price £6, output 1⅓ quarters, selling price per quarter £4, proceeds £6, rent 0. Land A: total capital £5, output 1½ quarters (1 + ½), proceeds £7, rent £1. B: proceeds £8, rent £2 — but wait, the table shows B's rent as £2 and increase of £1 plus 1×7? Let me trace the increase column: \"Increase: A — £1; B — £1 + 7; C — £1 + 2 × 7; D — £1 + 3 × 7.\" This is the n : n + m : n + 2m : n + 3m form, with n = 1 (A's rent) and m = 7 (the fertility differential between successive lands in money terms). The rents are therefore: A = 1, B = 1 + 7 = 8, C = 1 + 14 = 15, D = 1 + 21 = 22. The economic content of this table is fundamental. The entry of a new absolute floor — a new non-rent-bearing marginal land — sets a fixed constant n which is added to every rent in the series above it. The purely differential component ( m , and its multiples) is preserved exactly, and indeed is now clearer than before because the differential is measured from A's rent rather than from zero. This separation is the \"deduction\" Marx emphasises: \"the rent of the poorest rent-bearing land, which formerly did not bear rent at all, forms a constant that is simply added on to all higher rents; it is only after this constant is deducted that the series of differences for the higher rents clearly emerges, and so too their parallelism with the series of land types arranged according to fertility.\" Tables VIIIa and Xa replicate this structure under differing productivities of the second investment. In VIIIa (second investment equal to the first), the rent of A is 1½; B's rent is 1½ + 7½; C's is 1½ + 15; D's is 1½ + 22½. In Xa (second investment at half productivity), the rent of A = ⅓; B's rent = ⅓ + 6⅓; C = ⅓ + 12⅔; D = ⅓ + 19. In every case \"the fertilities from A to D are in the ratios 1:2:3:4, and the rents are accordingly,\" and the generalized formula \"n : n + m : n + 2m : n + 3m\" applies with n being A's rent and m being the difference between A and B. The theoretical payoff here is only slightly less than it is decisive: it is differences in fertility, not absolute yields, that determine the shape of the rent schedule; and the baseline rent of the poorest rent-bearing land — the new floor established by the entry of still-inferior soil — is a constant, or uniform addition, superimposed on the entire differential series. This result will be re-emphasised in the analytic section that follows. Engels' Intervention: The New Tables and the Correction of the Original Examples Before the substantive general results can be developed, Engels steps forward as editor. He explains, in a long parenthesis, that \"since the above third case was not elaborated in the manuscript — there is only the title — it remained the task of the editor to complete this as best he could.\" Engels therefore assumed the task himself of drawing \"the resulting general conclusions from the overall investigation of differential rent II in its three major and nine subordinate cases.\" Engels then executes a decisive methodological critique of Marx's own numerical apparatus. \"The examples given in the manuscript are of little help. Firstly, they compare lands whose yields, for equal areas, are in the ratios 1:2:3:4, i.e. differences that are sharply exaggerated right from the start and which lead to completely impossible figures when calculations are made on this basis.\" The ratio 1:2:3:4 does not correspond to any realistic agricultural setting; no four contiguous plots exhibit such regular and dramatic gradation. Second, and more dangerously, \"they give a completely false impression.\" Engels identifies a specific inferential trap: \"If fertilities in the ratios 1:2:3:4 etc. lead to a series of rents in the ratios 0:1:2:3 etc., we feel able to derive the second series immediately from the first and explain the doubling, trebling, etc. of rents from the doubling, trebling, etc. of the total yields. But this would be completely mistaken.\" The correct statement is the one anticipated in Table VIIa: \"Rents stand in the ratios 0:1:2:3:4 whenever the scale of fertility is one of n:n+1:n+2:n+3:n+4; it is not the absolute level of fertility but rather the differences in fertility, reckoned from the non-rent-bearing land as the zero point, that give the ratio of rents.\" This is one of the most significant theoretical corrections in Capital. Engels isolates and warns against what might be called the \"absolute fertility fallacy\" — the mistaken belief that rents are proportional to yields. He is not merely clarifying arithmetic; he is defending the fundamental distinction between use-value (physical grain) and value (money rent), which underlies Marx's entire theory of rent. Rents are functions of value differentials, not of physical productivity. To make the general results intuitive, Engels then provides an entirely new series of tables — \"Table XI\" through \"Table XXIV\" — in which \"yields are given in bushels (1 qr., or 36.35 litres) and shillings (= marks).\" He designates Table XI as the analogue of the earlier Table I: five qualities of land A–E, first capital investment 50s. + 10s. profit = 60s. production cost per acre; yields 10, 12, 14, 16, 18 bushels; governing production price 6 shillings per bushel. Rents per acre: A = 0, B = 12s., C = 24s., D = 36s., E = 48s. Total = 120s. This is the reference point against which every subsequent table will be compared. The subsequent thirteen tables traverse three cases (constant, falling, rising price) × three variants (second-investment productivity constant, falling, rising), with two modalities in the third case. Engels' summary is precise: \"In case I, price of production constant... Variant 1. Productivity remains the same for the second capital investment (Table XII). Variant 2. Productivity falls. This can happen only if no second investment is made on land A. And, moreover, either (a) in such a way that land B likewise yields no rent (Table XIII); or (b) in such a way that land B is not completely devoid of rent (Table XIV). Variant 3. Productivity rises (Table XV).\" Case II (falling price, Tables XVI–XVIII): productivity of second investment constant (XVI), falling (XVII), rising (XVIII) — the first two withdraw land A from competition and B ceases to bear rent; the third leaves A as governing producer. Case III (rising price, Tables XIX–XXIV): first modality where A remains non-rent-bearing price-governing (XIX constant, XX falling, XXI rising — the last two requiring first-investment decline), second modality where inferior soil a enters and A yields rent (XXII constant, XXIII falling, XXIV rising). The Economic Content of Engels' Reconstructed Tables We need now to read the tables not as data but as arguments. Each one is a carefully constructed counterfactual demonstrating a specific theorem. Let us walk through the key ones. Table XI establishes the reference. Ten bushels per acre on A constitute the zero of the rent series. Each subsequent land B, C, D, E yields exactly two more bushels than the preceding one, and its rent is exactly the money value of that differential: 12s., 24s., 36s., 48s. The rate of rent rises absolutely (since rent increases while capital is constant) but remains purely differential. This is textbook DR I. Table XII (case I, variant 1) doubles the capital investment, equally divided between two applications on each land, with constant productivity. The result: outputs double (20, 24, 28, 32, 36 bushels), price remains 6s., rent doubles uniformly (0, 24s., 48s., 72s., 96s.), total rent goes from 120s. to 240s. This is the baseline for \"capital doubles, rent doubles.\" Table XIII (case I, variant 2a) introduces falling productivity of the second investment and the withdrawal of A from cultivation. If A cannot bear a second investment, and B's second investment yields only 8 bushels (instead of the 12 that would match the first investment's productivity), then B's total is 20 bushels and B becomes the price-governing land with zero rent. Because B has replaced A as the marginal producer, the production price remains unchanged at 6s. (B's total production cost is £6 for 20 bushels... wait, but B's total is 20 bushels and its production cost is 120s., so the price per bushel is 6s. This is the key: B's average cost coincides with the old price, so no price rise is needed). The rents then are: B = 0, C = 20s., D = 40s., E = 60s. Total = 120s. — exactly the same total as Table XI, even though the capital invested has doubled. The reason, as Marx explains in the analytic section, is that B's disappearance from the rent series removes its rent from every subsequent element in the differential chain: \"not only does the rent for B disappear, it is also deducted from each following member of the rent series.\" Table XIV (case I, variant 2b) softens the fall so that B retains some rent. B's second investment yields 9 bushels; total B = 21 bushels; production price remains 6s. (since B's average cost is just under 6s. and A still governs? Actually the table shows B's rent as 6s., and the increase column reads \"6, 6 + 21, 6 + 2 × 21, 6 + 3 × 21.\"). The rents become B = 6, C = 27, D = 48, E = 69 — total £150. The pattern now is a constant (B's rent = 6s.) added to a differential component (21s. per step). Here we have the transition from the pure DR I form of Tables XI–XIII to the n + m form that characterises the second modality of the third case. Table XV (case I, variant 3) makes the second investment more productive. B's second investment yields 15 bushels; B total = 27 bushels; the price-governing land A still does not receive a second investment. Rents: B = 42s., C = 69, D = 96, E = 123. Total = 330s. — nearly triple the Table XI total. The increase column shows \"42, 42 + 27, 42 + 2 × 27, 42 + 3 × 27.\" Once again, a constant (42s.) plus a differential (27s. per step). The critical observation is that even under a constant price , if the productivity of the additional investment is higher, total rents rise dramatically above the simple proportional increase. Table XVI (case II, variant 1) shows falling price. Here both A and B are withdrawn from competition, since the falling price coincides with the improved relative position of higher lands. B becomes the new price-governing land with 24 bushels and zero rent; C, D, E yield 28, 32, 36 bushels with rents 20, 40, 60. Total = 120s. — again the same as Table XI. The total rent remains unchanged even though capital has doubled, because the entire rent schedule has shifted upward by one land-quality. Table XVII (case II, variant 2) combines falling price with falling second-investment productivity. B yields 12 + 9 = 21 bushels and governs the price at 5⅚ shillings. Rents: C = 20, D = 40, E = 60. Total = 120s. This is the precise configuration Engels identifies in his historical coda as \"our 'second case, variant 2' — falling prices and falling productivity\" which \"became the rule in Europe.\" Table XVIII (case II, variant 3) restores A as the price-governing land because the second investment is more productive. A's total: 10 + 15 = 25 bushels; B's: 12 + 15 = 30; and so on. Price falls to 4⅘ shillings. Rents: B = 24, C = 48, D = 72, E = 96. Total = 240s. — the rent doubles from a simple productivity increase in the second investment, even though the price is falling. Tables XIX–XXI (case III, first modality) then turn to the rising price with A still governing. In XIX, the second investment's productivity is constant, which (as the chapter argued at the outset) requires the first investment's productivity to have declined: A yields 10 + 15 = 25 at price 6⅚. Rents are B = 24, C = 48, D = 72, E = 96, total = 240s. In XX, the second investment's productivity falls: A yields 10 + 5 = 15 at price 8s. Rents again are 24, 48, 72, 96 — total 240s. In XXI, the second investment's productivity rises, again with first-investment decline: A yields 5 + 12.5 = 17.5 at price 6⅚. Rents again 24, 48, 72, 96 — total 240s. Notice the invariant: in all three variants of the first modality of the third case, the total rent is exactly twice the Table XI total. Tables XXII–XXIV (case III, second modality, where inferior soil a enters) show the deepest structural transformation. Now the baseline for rent is not zero but the rent of land A, and the entire series is scaled by the entry of the new floor. In XXII (constant second productivity), land a yields 16 bushels and bears no rent at price 7½ s.; land A yields 20 bushels; total rent evolves to a = 0, A = 30, B = 60, C = 90, D = 120, E = 150, total = 450s. In XXIII (falling second productivity), a yields 15 at 8s.; A yields 17½; the rents follow a = 0, A = 20, B = 48 (= 20 + 28), C = 76 (= 20 + 2 × 28), D = 104, E = 132 — total = 380s. In XXIV (rising second productivity), a yields 16 at 7½ s.; A yields 22½ and its rent is 15 plus a \"33⅓ component\"; B = 15 + 2 × 33⅓; C = 15 + 3 × 33⅓; and so on, total = 581⅔ s. The pattern in this last series is exactly what Marx had found in the earlier tables: n : n + m : n + 2m : n + 3m , with n being the rent of land A (the newly rent-bearing marginal plot) and m being the differential step. It is a composite rent structure in which the constant part n reflects the entry of inferior soil and the differential part m reflects the classical DR I differential among the higher plots. The General Results of the Investigation After the tables, the chapter pivots to a summary analytic section (pp. 857–865). Marx draws two immediate conclusions. First, \"the series of rents is in exactly the same ratio as the series of differences in fertility, taking the non-rent-bearing, price-governing land as the zero point. It is not the absolute yields that determine rent, but simply the differences in yields. Whether the various types of land provide yields of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 bushels per acre, or 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 bushels, the rents are in both cases successively 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 bushels or their respective monetary equivalents.\" Second, and \"far more important,\" the chapter presents the aggregate result concerning the total rents under repeated capital investment. Marx enumerates five cases in which the total rent exactly doubles from 10 × 12s. = 120s. to 10 × 24s. = 240s.: Case I, variant 1 (constant price, constant rise in production, Table XII) Case II, variant 3 (falling price, increasing rise in production, Table XVIII) Case III, first modality, all three variants (Tables XIX, XX, XXI) Then he enumerates four cases in which rent more than doubles: Case I, variant 3 (constant price, table XV, rent = 330s.) Case III, second modality (Tables XXII, XXIII, XXIV, rents = 450s., 380s., 581⅔ s.) One case in which rent rises but not to twice its former level: Case I, variant 2 (falling second productivity but B still rent-bearing, Table XIV, rent = 150s.) And three cases in which total rent remains the same: Case I, variant 2a (Table XIII, B drops out) Case II, variants 1 and 2 (Tables XVI, XVII) The overall conclusion: \"In the great majority of all possible cases, rents rise, both per acre of the rent-bearing land and particularly in their total sum, as a result of increased capital investment on the land. Only in three cases out of thirteen investigated does the total rent remain unchanged.\" The three exceptions are precisely those in which \"the most inferior quality of land, which formerly bore no rent and governed price, drops out of competition, and its place is taken by the next higher quality, which thus ceases to bear rent.\" But even in those three limiting cases, Marx adds, \"rents rise on the best types of land in comparison with the rents arising from the first capital investment; if the rent for C falls from 24s. to 20s., the rents for D and E rise from 36s. and 48s. to 40s. and 60s.\" The redistribution does not equalise; it merely changes the distribution within an overall expanding rent total. Only one case could produce a decline in total rent below the Table XI level: if not just A dropped out, but also B, so that C would become the price-governing, rentless land. This hypothetical shows how strongly the theory's results are conditioned on the persistence of the price-governing quality of the worst land: it takes the removal of two whole grades before the total rent falls. The Law of Rising Rent and the Vitality of the Landed Aristocracy Marx now states the substantive law in its most general and politically pointed form. \"Thus the more capital is applied to the land and the higher the development of agriculture and civilization in general in a country, the higher are the levels of rent per acre and the total sum of rent and the more gigantic therefore the tribute society pays the great landowners in the form of surplus profits — as long as types of land once taken into cultivation all remain able to compete.\" The conditional clause — \" as long as types of land once taken into cultivation all remain able to compete\" — carries the whole weight of the subsequent historical narrative. As long as the existing structure of cultivation is preserved, the concentration of capital on the soil drives rents upward, and the landlord class benefits by an automatic mechanism disconnected from any productive activity of its own. Marx draws the sociological inference directly: \"This law explains the amazing vitality of the class of large landowners. No other social class lives in so extravagant a manner; no other class claims such a right as this does to a traditional luxury in keeping with its 'estate', irrespective of where the money for this comes from; no other class piles debts upon debts in such a light-hearted way. And yet time and again they fall on their feet — thanks to the capital of other people that is put into the soil and yields them rent, completely out of all proportion to the profits the capitalist draws from this.\" The point is not merely descriptive; it identifies a specific mechanism : it is the tenant's capital that builds up rents for the landlord, and the more capital is applied, the more rent is transferred. The landlord's opulence is a tribute extracted from the productive application of capital by others. Yet the same law that explains the landlord's vitality also \"explains why this vitality of the large landowner is gradually approaching its end.\" The historical supersession is the chapter's closing movement. The Historical Coda: Corn Laws, Steamships, and the Agrarian Complaint Engels' coda (pp. 859–860) is a masterclass in the application of abstract political economy to concrete history. The repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846 had, in the manufacturers' expectation, \"made the land-owning aristocracy into paupers.\" Instead, \"these aristocrats became richer than before.\" How? Engels gives a two-part answer drawn directly from the chapter's law. First, the landlords exploited the contract form. \"They now insisted in their contracts that the farmers should invest £12 a year on each acre instead of £8.\" By requiring a higher application of capital per acre, they automatically raised the rent base under the law just established. Second, being \"represented in large numbers even in the House of Commons,\" they \"granted themselves a hefty state subsidy for drainage and other permanent improvements to their estates.\" Public money financed private improvement, and the improved productivity raised rents still further. Third, the price-governing land was not withdrawn from cultivation. \"Since the worst land was not totally withdrawn from cultivation, but was at most used temporarily for other purposes, rents rose in proportion to the increased capital investment and the landed aristocracy did better than they had before.\" This connects back to the analytic point that rents rise as long as \"types of land once taken into cultivation all remain able to compete.\" But then the dialectical inversion. The transoceanic steamships and the railways of North and South America and India opened up \"some quite singular tracts of land\" to the European corn markets. The American prairies and Argentine pampas — \"steppes which nature itself has made arable, virgin soil that offered rich yields for years even on rudimentary tilling and without fertilizer.\" The Russian and Indian communistic communities, forced by \"a merciless state despotism — often enough by torture\" to sell their produce for cash to meet tax obligations, sold \"with no regard for their costs of production, sold at the price which the dealer offered, because the peasant absolutely had to have money at the payment date.\" Faced with this competition, \"the European farmer or peasant could not survive at the old rents. One portion of European soil became definitively uncompetitive for corn growing, while everywhere rents fell.\" Engels then names the case: \"Our 'second case, variant 2', falling prices and falling productivity on the additional capital investment, became the rule in Europe, and hence the agrarian complaint from Scotland to Italy, from the south of France to East Prussia.\" The coda closes with a characteristically sharp observation: \"Fortunately, not all prairie land has yet been brought into cultivation by a long chalk; enough is still left to ruin European large-scale landownership completely — and small-scale ownership into the bargain.\" This is not a mere historical appendix. It demonstrates the concrete applicability of the abstract machinery: the falling-price case is not a mathematical curiosity but the lived experience of European agriculture in the late nineteenth century, and the mechanism of ruin is exactly the same one that had earlier produced the landlord's vitality — the persistence of the marginal land condition and the absolute limit set by world-market competition. The General Results of Differential Rent as a Whole Marx now steps back from DR II to state the general architecture of differential rent. He identifies two distinct origins of surplus profit. On the basis of DR I: the investment of total agricultural capital across areas of differing fertility. Here rents emerge as the direct consequence of heterogeneous plots. On the basis of DR II: the varying differential productivity of successive capital investments on the same land, where greater productivity in quarters per unit of capital is obtained than would be obtained from the same capital investment on the most inferior, rentless, price-governing land. The crucial qualification: \"No matter how these surplus profits might arise, their transformation into rent, i.e. their transfer from the farmer to the landowner, always presupposes as its initial condition that the various actual individual prices of production... are equalised in advance to give an individual average price of production. The excess of this general, governing production price of the product of an acre over the individual average production price, forms and measures the rent per acre.\" This is the central theorem of the chapter in its most general form. Rent is the excess of the governing (market-determined, A-determined) production price over the individual average production price of a given plot's total product. The individual average production price is itself a synthesis — the weighted average of the individual production prices of each successive capital investment. It is only by computing that average that rent can be identified at all. Now Marx enters a subtle analytic comparison between DR I and DR II. \"In the case of differential rent I, the differential results can be distinguished in and for themselves, because they take place on different areas of land, outside and alongside one another, given a capital outlay per acre that is taken as normal, and the normal cultivation corresponding to it. In the case of differential rent II, they must first be made distinguishable, they must in fact be transformed back into differential rent I, and this can only be done in the manner indicated.\" To demonstrate, Marx returns to Table III and works through a numerical example on land B. For the first investment of £2½, B yields 2 quarters; for a second investment of £2½, it yields 1½ quarters; total 3½ quarters on the same acre. We cannot tell from the aggregate of 3½ quarters how much is the product of investment (1) and how much of investment (2). It is \"actually the product of the total capital of £5.\" The individual production price of the first 2 quarters is £1 5/7 per quarter; that of the second 1½ quarters is £2 per quarter. Total cost = £6 (production price including profit). The individual average production price is therefore £6 / 3½ = £1 5/7 per quarter. The governing price is £3. The surplus profit per quarter is £3 − £1 5/7 = £1 5/7. For 3½ quarters this is £4.2, expressed in corn as 1½ quarters. B's surplus profit is expressed in the aliquot part of its product that forms the corn rent. But — and Marx insists on this — \"the extra product from an acre of B over that of an acre of A does not directly represent surplus profit and hence surplus product.\" Because B receives twice the capital of A, the extra product (3½ vs 1 = 2½ quarters excess) overstates the surplus product. The correct comparison is not 3½ vs 1 but 3½ vs 2 (the amount A would produce with equal capital), so the true surplus product is only 1½ quarters. Marx reinforces this with a striking further case: if B's third £2½ capital investment yields only 1 quarter at £3, this third investment \"would cover only the costs of production, yielding only the average profit and no surplus profit, and therefore nothing that could be transformed into rent\" — while the per-acre product of B still looks far superior to A's. The methodological moral is central: \"The product per acre of any other type of land, compared with the product per acre of land A, indicates neither whether it is the product of the same capital investment or a greater one, nor whether the excess product simply covers the production price or whether it is due to higher productivity of the extra capital.\" Appearance — mere physical yield comparison — is systematically misleading. Only the analysis of individual production prices can identify rent. Declining Productivity and the Limits of Rent-Bearing Capital Investment The next major analytic block (pp. 862–864) works out what happens when capital is added to B with declining productivity. Marx sets the problem: with diminishing returns on additional investments, at what point does the total capital on B cease to form rent? The limit is defined as the point at which \"the individual average production price of the product per acre of B would rise to the production price per acre of A.\" He then demonstrates a remarkable invariance. If B adds investments that merely cover the production price (yield no surplus profit), the average production price per quarter rises, but the total surplus profit formed by earlier investments remains unchanged. Why? Because \"the average production price always remains below that of A, and if the extra price per quarter declines, the number of quarters increases in the same proportion, so that the total excess price remains the same.\" To illustrate, he constructs an example. First two investments on B of £5 each produce 3½ quarters (as in the Table III case). Total production price = £9 (including 20% profit) for 4½ quarters, average price = £2 per quarter. Surplus profit per quarter = £1. Total = £1 × 4½ = £4½, exactly as before. Add a third investment of £2½ yielding only one quarter. Total = 5½ quarters at £10½, average price = £1 10/11 per quarter. Surplus profit per quarter = £1 10/11... wait, need to recompute. Actually as Marx shows: with a fourth and fifth investment at £2½ each yielding 1 quarter only, the total product is 6½ quarters at £15 cost; average price = £2 4/13 per quarter; surplus profit per quarter = £7/13; total surplus = £7/13 × 6½ = £3½? Wait, that isn't matching. Let me trust the numbers as given: \"£-h x 6t = £1 x 4! = £4-t.\" So the total remains £4½. The point is stated clearly: \"under these conditions no increase in the governing production price is needed to make additional capital investments possible on the rent-bearing types of land, even up to the level at which the additional capital completely ceases to provide surplus profit and simply still yields the average profit. It also follows that the total surplus profit per acre remains the same here, no matter how much the surplus profit per quarter declines.\" Only when additional capital has a production price higher than the governing price does the average price move meaningfully. Marx's next example: production on B consists of (1) 3½ quarters at cost £6 (two investments, both with surplus profit); (2) 1 quarter at £3 (production price equal to governing); (3) 1 quarter at £4 (production price 33⅓% higher than governing). Total: 5½ quarters at £13+? Actually 6+3+4 = £13, but the text says £13 for 5½ quarters. Average = £2 4/11 per quarter. Surplus over governing £3: 7/11 per quarter. Total rent = 5½ × 7/11 = £3½ roughly. Indeed: 5½ quarters at £3 gives £16½; subtract cost £13, remainder £3½ — money rent has fallen by £1 while corn rent has fallen by about ½ quarter. And yet: \"despite the fact that the fourth extra capital investment on B [the third item above] produces not only no surplus profit, but rather less than the average profit, there is still surplus profit and rent as before.\" If both the third and fourth investments produce at above the governing price, total = 3½ at £6 + 2 at £8 = 5½ quarters at £14. Average price = £2 6/11 per quarter; surplus = £5/11 per quarter. Rents: £5/11 × 5½ = £2½. \"Some rent is still lost, although less than before.\" Marx draws the general principle: \"the rent on the better lands need not disappear with additional capital investments whose production costs [exceed] the governing production price, at least within the limits of permissible practice, but need only decline,\" with the decline proportional to (i) the aliquot part the less productive capital forms of the total and (ii) the reduction in its productivity. The Complete Disappearance of Rent: The Analytic Center The chapter next constructs the limit case in which rent vanishes entirely . Marx assumes four successive capital investments on B, each of £ varying magnitude, with declining productivity, such that the individual average price for a quarter on B exactly equals the general production price of £3. The four investments are: £2½ capital, £½ profit, 2 quarters output, individual price £1½ per quarter, total £3 at £3/quarter = £6 £2½ capital, £½ profit, 1½ quarters, £2 per quarter, total £3 at £3 = £4½ £5 capital, £1 profit, 1½ quarters, £4 per quarter, cost £6, sold at £3 gives £4½ — a loss of £1½ £5 capital, £1 profit, 1 quarter, £6 per quarter, cost £6, sold at £3 gives £3 — a loss of £3 The total: £15 capital, £3 profit, 6 quarters, individual total £18, sold at £3 = £18. The farmer \"sells each quarter at its individual price of production, and hence sells the total number of quarters at their average production price per quarter, which coincides with the governing price of £3.\" At this point, \"the rent has disappeared.\" The question is posed with characteristic directness: \"Where does the surplus go when the individual production price of each quarter is equalised with the general production price in this way?\" The answer is the chapter's conceptual summit. The surplus profit on the first £2½ was £3 (output 2 quarters at £3 = £6, minus cost £3 = £3 profit); on the second £2½ it was £1½ (output 1½ quarters at £3 = £4½, minus cost £3 = £1½); total surplus profit on the first £5 of capital = £4½. The third investment of £5 produces a product of 1½ quarters sold at £3 = £4½, against a cost of £6, i.e. a loss of £1½. The fourth investment produces 1 quarter sold at £3, against cost £6, i.e. a loss of £3. Together, losses on the third and fourth investments = £4½, exactly equal to the surplus profits of the first two investments. \"The surplus profits and the losses of profit cancel out. The rent therefore vanishes. In fact, however, this is possible only because the elements of surplus-value that formed surplus profit or rent now go into the formation of the average profit. The farmer makes this average profit of £3 on £15, or 20 per cent, at the expense of the rent.\" The theoretical content here is profound. Rent is revealed as a distribution of surplus value. When capital investments on the same land are pooled under a single accounting, the surplus from early, high-productivity investments can be internally used to offset losses on later, low-productivity investments, allowing the total operation to yield the average profit rate even when no individual component generates rent. The question is not whether surplus value exists, but whether it is captured as rent or as average profit. The \"disappearance\" of rent is thus the transfer of surplus value from the landlord to the general pool of average profit — a redistribution within the capitalist class. Marx then shows that this equalisation presupposes a specific quantitative condition: \"the establishment of equality between the individual average production price on B and the general production price on A, which governs the market, presupposes that the amount by which the individual price of the product of the earlier capital investments stands below the governing price is offset more and more, and finally cancelled out by the amount by which the product of the later capital investments comes to stand above the governing price. What appears as surplus profit, as long as the product of the earlier capital investments is sold by itself, gradually becomes part of the average production price and thereby goes into the formation of the average profit, until it is finally absorbed by this entirely.\" Marx then poses a crucial counterfactual. Suppose, instead of £15 capital on B, only £5 is laid out and the extra 2½ quarters are produced by cultivating 2½ fresh acres of A with a capital of £2½ per acre. Then total capital = £11¼, total cost including profit = £13½, output 6 quarters sold at £3 = £18, surplus = £4½, i.e. rent of £4½ per acre — as in the earlier case. The aggregate result is the same whether the additional product comes from intensive (same-land) or extensive (new-land) cultivation. But if to produce the extra 2½ quarters it were necessary to resort to land worse than A — call them A⁻¹ and A⁻² — with production prices of £4 per quarter for the first extra 1½ quarters and £6 for the last quarter, then the governing price would rise to £6. Under that scenario, B's 3½ quarters would sell for £21, giving a rent of £15 instead of £4½, and A's own 1 quarter would yield a rent of £3 (½ quarter). The moral is exact: the intensity of cultivation and the extent of cultivation are alternative forms of the same economic process, and their relative advantage depends solely on the production prices of the increments . The result is the same total product; the rent distribution differs by whether the increments came from the same plot or from new land, and by whether the new land was of the same, superior, or inferior quality. The General Theorems of DR II Under Declining Productivity Marx now distils the analysis into a set of numbered propositions. First: \"as long as the additional capitals are invested on the same land with surplus productivity, even if this is decreasing, the absolute corn and money rent per acre rises, even if it declines relatively, in proportion to the capital advanced.\" The limiting case is that additional capital which yields only average profit, or whose product's individual production price equals the general one. Under this condition, the production price remains the same — so long as increased supply does not render poorer types of land superfluous. Second: \"the investment of additional capital which produces only the average profit, i.e. whose surplus productivity = 0, does not alter the amount of surplus profit and hence rent that is formed.\" The individual average price per quarter rises; the excess per quarter declines; but the number of quarters bearing this reduced excess increases, and the product remains the same. Third: \"additional capital investments for which the individual production price of their products stands above the governing price... bring the individual average price of the total product of the better land ever closer to the general production price, and thus more and more reduce the difference between the two.\" The rent per acre, in this case, falls in absolute terms as capital grows and production increases, and does not merely fall relatively to the growing size of the capital — as it does in the second case. The rent can disappear entirely only if the individual average production price on the better land B coincides with the governing price — i.e. if \"the entire surplus profit of the earlier and more productive capital investments has been used to form the average profit.\" The minimum limit of the fall in rent per acre is this vanishing point. It is reached only when the additional investment of deficiently productive capital becomes so great that its effect cancels out the surplus productivity of the first investments, so that the productivity of total capital on B equals that of the capital on A. Crucially, even at this point, \"the governing price of production, £3 per qr, remains the same, although the rent has vanished.\" Only beyond this point would the price have to rise, whether because the degree of deficient productivity of surplus capital increases or because its quantity increases. Marx supplies a numerical illustration: if in the earlier table 2½ quarters were produced at £4 per quarter on the same land instead of 1½, we would have altogether 7 quarters at a production cost of £22, i.e. £3 1/7 per quarter — £1/7 above the general production price, which would then have to rise. The consequence: \"extra capital with deficient productivity, and even capital with increasingly deficient productivity, could still be applied for a long while before the individual average price per quarter on the best lands became equal to the general price of production.\" Even then, the disappearance of rent on the better types of land would mean only that the individual production price of their product coincides with the general price; no rise in the general price would yet be required. Landed Property as Barrier to Capital Investment We arrive at the chapter's turning point. Marx has established that, abstractly viewed, the farmer could continue investing deficiently productive capital on B, compensating poor returns on later investments with rich returns on earlier ones, until the average price rose to the general price. But then: \"Under the law of landed property, however, the latter 2½ qrs could not have been produced in this manner at £3 per qr, except in the case where it could be produced on 2½ new acres of type A land. The case in which the extra capital only produces at the general price of production would have imposed a limit. Beyond this, extra capital investment on the same land would have to cease.\" Why? Because \"if the farmer has to pay, say, £4½ rent for the first two capital investments, he must continue to pay it, and any capital investment that needs more than £3 to produce a quarter would involve a deduction from his profit.\" The rent he pays to the landlord is not available to him as a compensating factor . The surplus profit that might, in the abstract, offset the loss, is already alienated. Therefore the internal equalisation of individual average price is prevented. Marx works out the farmer's position in a companion table: Investment £2½, profit £½, production price £3, output 2 quarters, selling price £3, proceeds £6, surplus profit £3 — but of this, £3 is paid out as rent. Investment £2½, profit £½, production price £3, output 1½ quarters, selling price £3, proceeds £4½, surplus profit £1½ — paid out as rent. Investment £5, profit £1, production price £6, output 1½, selling price £3, proceeds £4½, loss £1½. Investment £5, profit £1, production price £6, output 1, selling price £3, proceeds £3, loss £3. The farmer's total investment is £15, and he continues to pay £4½ rent (which is the sum of the surplus profits on investments 1 and 2) on the same land. His individual total production cost figure is not the abstract £15 + £3 profit but £15 + £3 profit + £4½ rent = £22½ for 6 quarters = £3¾ per quarter. At that individual production price, the operation yields nothing above cost (indeed, the farming operation at the accounting level of the tenant-capitalist yields not the average profit but a sub-average return). His actual realised earnings are: proceeds £18, minus non-rent costs £15, minus rent £4½ = −£1½. \"His loss in profit and capital for the third investment would come to £1½, and for the fourth investment £3, together making £4½, exactly as much as the rent for the better capital investments.\" The rent has consumed his hypothetical profit offset. \"So even though the differential rent is only a formal transformation of surplus profit into rent, and in this case landed property simply enables the landowner to transfer the farmer's surplus profit to himself, it transpires that the successive investment of capital on the same stretch of land... tends rather to find its limit in this transference.\" The consequence is an artificial barrier to improved cultivation. It is \"a result of the merely formal transformation of surplus profit into ground-rent which is the consequence of landed property.\" And here the dialectic closes: \"The rise in the general price of production which becomes necessary here, where the limit is narrower than elsewhere, is in this case therefore not only the basis for the rise in the differential rent, but the existence of differential rent as rent is at the same time the basis for the earlier and more rapid rise in the general price of production in order thereby to guarantee the increased supply of the product that has become necessary.\" In short: because rent prevents the pooling of surplus and deficit across successive investments, the price-governing quality of A becomes a harder binding constraint, forcing the market price to rise sooner than would otherwise be required — which, in turn, raises the rents that the landlord collects. Landed property is not merely an idle presence at the margins of the rent relation; it modifies the rent relation in a way that makes the landlord's take larger than the abstract mechanism would produce. The rent relation is self-reinforcing . The Mutual Limitation of DR I and DR II One final analytic observation: \"The governing price could not rise to £4, as above, thanks to the extra capital on land B, if land A were to supply the extra product for less than £4, or if newer and poorer land than A came into competition, with a price of production that was above £3 but below £4.\" The logic is precise. If additional supply can come from a different plot at a price between £3 and £4, then land B's contribution at £4 is blocked by the existence of the alternative. Conversely, if a plot at £4 is now the marginal producer, B can be pushed to its limit without fear of exclusion. \"We thus see how differential rent I and differential rent II, while the first is the basis of the second, at the same time place limits on one another, leading sometimes to successive investments of capital on the same stretch of land and sometimes to adjacent investments of capital on new additional land. They have a similar effect as limits to one another in other cases, for example where better land is taken up.\" The chapter's final substantive theorem is thus one of mutual determination : DR I provides the baseline (the price-governing land) on which DR II operates; DR II, in turn, by altering the profitability of same-land investment versus new-land investment, can either preserve or displace the DR I hierarchy. Extension and intensification are alternative strategies whose relative advantages move with the price structure. In every case, the price-governing land remains the anchor. And so the analysis closes with an invitation to the remaining questions — absolute rent, the price of land, and the final general theory of ground-rent — which Marx knew remained to be written and which the reader is now prepared to receive. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Chapter 43 yields at least five fundamental principles, each of which has consequences well beyond the technical apparatus of rent theory. First principle: rent is a differential relation, not an absolute quantity. The chapter's most emphatic lesson — one that Engels restates with unusual force — is that rent depends upon differences in individual production prices, not upon absolute levels of fertility or yield. The rent of land B is not determined by B's yield considered in itself but by the excess of B's yield over the yield of the price-governing land A. The same quantity of rent may be produced by many different physical configurations; the same physical yield may produce no rent or enormous rent depending on its relation to the marginal producer. This dismantles the \"fertility theory of rent\" that treats land's bounty as the direct source of rent. Rent is not a share in the product ; it is a share in the value — and value is relational. Second principle: the sum of rent rises with the application of capital, ceteris paribus. The chapter's empirical result — that in ten of the thirteen tables, rent rises when capital investment increases — establishes a systemic tendency: the more capital is invested in agriculture, the larger the tribute that flows to the landowning class. This has the remarkable implication that the landlord class has a built-in interest in agricultural intensification that is entirely independent of any productive activity on its part. What the tenant gains through the application of capital in part accrues automatically to the landlord as rent. This is a specific form of what Marx would elsewhere call the \"fettering\" effect of a pre-capitalist property relation on the development of productive forces: the tenant has limited incentive to invest because a portion of the return will be captured by the landlord. Third principle: landed property is an artificial barrier to intensive cultivation. The chapter's most original discovery is that under the law of landed property the farmer cannot pool the surplus profits of some capital investments with the deficits of others in the way a single capitalist firm ordinarily would when summing across profit centres. Rent is an external payment to a third party; it removes the surplus from the sphere in which it might have been used internally to average out returns. The consequence is that the intensity of cultivation comes up against a barrier at the point where the individual investment would need to yield less than the governing production price — even though, in the abstract, such investment would still be socially efficient. Landed property thus operates as a systematic upward pressure on the price of food. This is a specific, measurable mechanism by which a social relation of property distorts the allocation of capital. Fourth principle: the boundary between rent and profit is porous and contested. The chapter's central numerical example — the disappearance of rent when average and governing production prices converge — shows that surplus value can be redistributed between the landlord and the general pool of average profit. Rent is not a fixed category; it is a form that surplus value may take, and the form is unstable. Whenever the average individual price on a better land rises to the governing price, the surplus value previously captured as rent flows back into the formation of average profit. Whoever controls the mechanism of that pooling — landlord or farmer-capitalist — determines who gets the surplus. This is a direct application of the labour theory of value to the theory of distribution: surplus value has a single source, but its form depends on the social relations through which it is claimed. Fifth principle: the historical contingency of rent. The chapter's coda — the repeal of the Corn Laws, the English landlord's paradoxical enrichment, the eventual ruin of European rents by transoceanic competition — demonstrates that the rent relation, however deeply rooted in the mechanisms of capital accumulation, is not eternal. It is conditioned upon a specific global configuration: the persistence of the marginal-land condition, the inability of external sources of supply to undercut the domestic governing price, and the maintenance of landed property as a legally enforceable claim. When the railways and steamships opened up virgin soil on other continents, the whole edifice came under pressure. Engels' remark that \"our 'second case, variant 2' became the rule in Europe\" is a decisive historical verdict on the theoretical apparatus: the abstract cases Marx constructed map directly onto concrete historical phases, and the agrarian depression of the late nineteenth century is the falling-price case in its living reality. Broader implications. These five principles, taken together, propose a fully materialist account of ground-rent. Rent is not a natural phenomenon, nor a compensation for the landlord's contribution to production (which is nil), nor a claim grounded in the nature of land as a factor of production. It is a social claim on surplus value, enforced by the legal form of landed property, and rendered possible by the difference between the individual production prices of different capitals applied to agriculture and the general production price that governs the market. Its scale rises with the intensity of capital application; its form is unstable; its persistence depends upon historical conditions external to the theory itself. The chapter therefore does more than complete a taxonomic exercise. It demonstrates that some of the most important features of a capitalist economy — the level of food prices, the rate of agricultural improvement, the political power of the landlord class, the geography of world agriculture — are shaped by the form of landed property and by the rent relation that expresses it. It is a contribution not merely to the theory of rent but to the theory of capital accumulation, of class, and of economic development. And it closes with the promise, realised only in fragmentary form in the sections that follow, that the systematic account of value, surplus value, profit, and rent would finally yield a comprehensive theory of the capitalist mode of production as a historically specific totality — one whose very conditions of reproduction are also its conditions of eventual dissolution. The landowner's vitality is real; so, in the long run, is its exhaustion. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 42 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 44"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 44", "title": "Chapter 44", "subtitle": "Differential Rent Also on the Poorest Land Cultivated", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 44/Chapter 44 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 7035, "read_time": "35 min read", "tags": ["Differential Rent (I & II)"], "keywords": ["Differential Rent on the Poorest Land Cultivated", "Possibility of Worst Soil Yielding Differential Rent", "Urban and Mineral Encroachment", "Location Advantages on Low-Fertility Ground", "Absolute Base of Rent Equations"], "text": "Chapter 44 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 7,117 words ⏱️ ~36 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 44 of Capital , Volume 3, is the hinge on which Marx's entire theory of ground-rent turns. It belongs to Part VI, \"The Transformation of Surplus Profit into Ground-Rent,\" and it follows directly from the two preceding constructions of differential rent in its two forms: differential rent I (DR I), which arises from differences in the fertility and situation of different plots of land receiving equal capital outlays, and differential rent II (DR II), which arises from differences in the productivity of successive capital outlays on the same plot . The chapter's problem is elegantly narrow and analytically explosive: can the worst land under cultivation — the land that determines the governing price of production, the land that in Ricardo's system is by definition rent-free — nevertheless yield a rent? Marx's answer is yes, and he demonstrates it three times over. In the Ricardian tradition, rent exists only because soils differ, and the margin of cultivation is by construction a no-rent margin: if the value (price of production) of corn is set by the least fertile soil in use, that soil sells its output at exactly its cost price and yields nothing to the landlord. Everything hinges on the identity between the individual price of production of the worst soil and the general, market-governing price of production . This chapter exists to break that identity — and not by the crude route of bringing still worse land (A₋₁) into cultivation, which would merely redefine the margin and shuffle land A into the rent-bearing series (and thus lie outside DR II altogether). Instead, the identity is broken from within DR II: successive capital investments with deficient productivity on the better, rent-bearing soils can themselves become the price-governing investments, pulling the market price above A's individual price of production, so that A begins to yield rent. Marx's summary is the chapter's thesis statement: \"As soon as differential rent II comes into play, by way of successive capital investments, the limits to the rising production price can be governed by better land, and the worst land, the basis for differential rent I, can then also bear rent. In this case, then, all cultivated land would bear rent in the sense of simple differential rent.\" The intellectual objectives are threefold. First, to dissolve the zero-rent margin — to show that rent on the poorest land can be generated by purely differential mechanisms, without appealing to monopoly or landed property as such. Second, to map every route by which this can occur , distinguishing (i) rent on A produced by a rise in the governing price generated by deficient marginal investments on B, (ii) rent on A produced with a constant or even falling price when additional capital on A itself exhibits surplus productivity, and (iii) rent on A produced when successive investments on A show declining productivity and the resulting surplus profit is fixed as rent by the landowner. Third, and decisively, to expose the point at which landed property as a social relation — the landlord's power to hold excess profit back from the equalizing tendency of competition — intrudes to raise the price of production rather than merely registering the outcome of differential fertility. This last move prepares the terrain for the theory of absolute rent that follows. The chapter's second, appended half — \"Differential rent and rent as simply interest on the capital incorporated into the soil\" — attacks the \"truly amusing theory\" that rent on land improved by capital is interest , while rent on naturally superior land is rent . Marx collapses the distinction: all so-called permanent improvements merely supply artificially what neighbouring land possesses naturally, and once the invested capital is amortized, the residue is pure differential rent. The closing footnote ties the whole chapter back to the labour theory of value: nature never determines value; it determines only the quantity of product that a given quantity of labour yields. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Opening Set-Up: A Demand Shock and the Choice of the Marginal Supplier The chapter opens with a stipulated premise, the classic Marxian device of a \"let us assume\" that isolates a mechanism. The demand for corn is rising, and the supply can only be met in one of three technically distinct ways: by successive capital investments with deficient productivity on the rent-bearing lands (i.e., additional doses on B, C, D that yield less per dose than the earlier ones); by additional capital investment on land A , the worst land previously in cultivation, also with declining productivity; by capital investment on new lands of inferior quality to A — the land designated A₋₁. Land B is taken as the representative rent-bearing land, and the previously governing production price is £3 per quarter. Marx appends a scale note that will matter for the whole chapter's quantitative structure: \"This 1 qr may represent 1 million qrs, and each acre 1 million acres\" — a reminder that these are not toy quantities but the social aggregates of a whole agricultural sector, and that the \"1 qr\" of extra product is the material form of a whole increment of social capital. The first analytic move is a comparison of alternative sources of the marginal quarter. If the extra quarter can be produced more cheaply by extra capital on B than by the same extra capital on A, or than by descending to A₋₁ (which can only produce at £4 per quarter), then the extra capital on B would govern the market price. Marx illustrates with a numerical fork: suppose an extra outlay of £4 (production costs including profit) were needed on B to produce a further quarter, while on A that quarter could be produced at £3½ — then it would \"obviously be produced on A and not on B.\" Since the marginal supplier determines the governing price, the cheaper alternative would displace B as price-governor. He therefore assumes, for the sake of the argument, that the extra quarter on B costs £3½. That £3½ then \"would be the governing price for the total production.\" 2.2 The Core Theorems Stated in the First Pass With the governing price at £3½, Marx runs the arithmetic of B: B sells its product, now 4½ quarters, for £15¾. Its production costs were £6 for the first 3½ quarters plus £3½ for the final quarter, a total of £9½. The surplus profit remaining for rent is therefore £6¼, \"against only £4½ before.\" This is the first crucial result: a capital investment of declining productivity on the better land increases that land's rent both absolutely and as a share of output. The second crucial result follows immediately. Because the market price has risen to £3½ while A still produces a quarter at a cost of £3, \"the acre of A would also yield a rent of £½.\" And — pivotally — \"it would not be the worst land A, but the better land B, that governed the production price of £3½.\" This is the inversion of the Ricardian sequence: the price-governing unit is no longer the worst land; it is the marginal dose of capital on better land . The worst land is thereby converted into a rent-yielding soil simply because someone else has become the price-setter. Marx then nails down the assumption that makes this analysis specifically about DR II rather than DR I: \"It is assumed here of course that there is no new accessible land of quality A and as well situated as that already cultivated, but that either a second capital investment would be needed on the stretch of A already cultivated, albeit at a still higher cost of production, or else it would be necessary to bring in still worse land A₋₁.\" The qualifier \"as well situated\" is not decorative. If untilled land of equal quality and equal location existed, competition among tenants would push its use forward at the old price and the price rise could not be consolidated into rent. Location, hence differential rent, is partly a transport-cost phenomenon — a point Marx will return to at the end of the chapter. 2.3 The Two Tables and the £2½-Capital / 20-Per-Cent-Profit Norm Before proceeding, Marx specifies the accounting convention on which all figures rest: \"price of production refers to the sum of the capital advanced plus 20 per cent profit, i.e. £½ profit on each £2½ capital, making a total of £3.\" In other words, price of production = capital advanced + average profit (in the classical formula, k + p̄ ), and for one acre of A the outlay is £2½ producing £3 of value. This is the price below which a capitalist farmer will not voluntarily invest and above which he earns surplus profit. The 20 per cent rate is conventional and, as will become important, is assumed constant across the chapter's counterfactual scenarios. Table 1 (before the new £3½ investment on B) Type Acres Price of production (£) Output (qrs) Selling price (£) Proceeds (£) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) A 1 3 1 3 3 0 0 B 1 6 3½ 3 10½ 1½ 4½ C 1 6 5½ 3 16½ 3½ 10½ D 1 6 7½ 3 22½ 5½ 16½ Total 4 21 17½ 52½ 10½ 31½ Table 2 (after the £3½ investment on B, which supplies 1 qr) Type Acres Price of production (£) Output (qrs) Selling price (£) Proceeds (£) Corn rent (qrs) Money rent (£) A 1 3 1 3½ 3½ 1/7 ½ B 1 9½ 4½ 3½ 15¾ 1 11/14 6¼ C 1 6 5½ 3½ 19¼ 3 11/14 13¼ D 1 6 7½ 3½ 26¼ 5 11/14 20¼ Total 4 24½ 18½ 64¾ 11½ 40¼ Four analytic features of these tables deserve close attention. First, money rent exceeds corn rent in significance : corn rent rises from 10½ to 11½ quarters, only a 9.5 per cent increase, while money rent rises from £31½ to £40¼, a 28 per cent increase, because every quarter of corn rent is now valued at the higher price of £3½. Rising corn prices therefore inflate the value of an almost unchanged physical surplus — the landlord's windfall is a price phenomenon as much as a physical one. Second, rent on A (½, or 1/7 of a quarter) arises without any change in A's own technique . A produces exactly what it produced before; only the price at which it sells has changed. This is the purest form of the chapter's thesis. Third, the distribution of rents among B, C, D widens absolutely (B +£1¾, C +£2¾, D +£3¾) but the proportionate relation narrows, because each estate's rent is now inflated by the same higher price. Fourth, note the striking mechanical detail that the same acre of B now carries a price of production of £9½ rather than £6 — that is, the \"price of production\" column measures the total capital sunk into the acre, so that a rising entry in this column is a record of intensified cultivation, not of inflation. 2.4 Engels's Editorial Correction: Rent as a Cost for the Tenant Marx's table does not stand uncorrected. The editor's footnote in the text (marked \"F. E.\") intervenes: \"The calculation here is again not completely correct.\" Engels recomputes. For the farmer of B, the 4½ quarters cost first £9½ in production costs and second £4½ in rent — altogether £14, \"an average of £3⅑ per qr.\" This average price then becomes the governing market price. Rent on A would accordingly come to £⅑ rather than the table's £½, while that of B would remain £4½ as before: 4½ quarters at £3⅑ = £14, which, minus £9½ production costs, leaves exactly £4½ of surplus profit. Two things are happening here, and both are worth flagging as intellectual caveats rather than dismissing as pedantry. On one level Engels is performing an arithmetic audit: the table's marginal (£3½) and average figures do not reconcile cleanly once B's rent is treated as a contractually fixed charge that the tenant must recover from the sale price. On another level, Engels is implicitly invoking a real institutional feature of capitalist agriculture that Marx himself stresses later in the chapter — once rent has been fixed, it functions for the tenant as a component of his costs, and therefore cannot simply be assumed away by the equalizing movement of competition. Engels's conclusion, though, is that \"despite the need to alter the figures, the example shows how differential rent II enables the better land that already bears rent to govern the price, and how in this way all land, even that which was previously devoid of rent, may be turned into rent-bearing land.\" The theorem survives the correction; only the decimals change. 2.5 The General Law of Corn Rent and the Problem of the Profit Rate Having established the schema, Marx states its most general corollary: \"The corn rent must increase once the governing production price of corn rises, i.e. once an increase takes place in the price of a quarter of corn from the price-governing land or in the level of the price-governing capital investment on one of the land types.\" The mechanism is equivalent to a general decline in natural fertility: it is \"the same as if all types had become less fertile,\" producing less per unit of the standard £2½ outlay instead of the previous yield. But this immediately raises a question that a competent reader must press: if corn becomes dearer, doesn't the farmer's real profit fall? Marx blocks this objection pre-emptively by listing the conditions under which the profit rate can remain unchanged even as the corn price rises. The farmer, he notes, can buy less corn with his profit; but the rate of profit — which is what governs whether capital flows into agriculture — can nevertheless stay constant if: wages do not rise , because they are \"pressed down to the physical minimum, i.e. below the normal value of labour-power\" — a blunt acknowledgment that the normal value of labour-power is itself a variable that capital can violate; because other objects of working-class consumption, \"those provided by manufacture, become relatively cheaper\" — cheapening the wage-goods bundle offsets the dearer bread; because the working day is prolonged or intensified, raising surplus value in the non-agricultural branches, \"which however is what governs agricultural profit\"; or because the same capital invested in agriculture \"includes more of the constant and less of the variable variety\" — a higher organic composition, hence a larger output per worker with the same wage bill. This passage is a compact restatement of the whole architecture of Volume 3: the agricultural profit rate is regulated by the general profit rate, which is regulated by the rate of surplus value and the organic composition of capital. The point is that a rising corn price does not mechanically depress agricultural profitability, because the same forces that raise the price of the wage bundle may simultaneously be cheapening the reproduction of labour-power. 2.6 The First Route to Rent on A — Deficient Extra Investment on Better Soil Marx now formally names the first route: rent arises on the formerly poorest land \"from the difference between its individual price of production, which was formerly the governing one, and the new, higher price of production at which the last bit of extra capital supplies the extra product needed on better soil but with deficient productivity.\" The two halves of the definition must not be confused. A's own price of production has not changed; it is still £3. What has changed is the market-governing price, which has been lifted to the level set by the marginal dose on B. The surplus profit on A is thus a differential surplus: the difference between a given individual price and a newly higher general price. He immediately mark off the boundary of the case. If the extra product had to be supplied by A₋₁ at £4 per quarter, then A's rent would rise to £1 per acre — larger than the 10/- style figures of the previous case — but \"in this case, however, A₋₁ would take the place of A as the worst cultivated land, and A would come into the series of rent-bearing types as its lowest member.\" That is a change in the scale of DR I, not a demonstration of DR II, and \"this case therefore lies outside a treatment of differential rent II, which arises from the varying productivity of successive capital investments on the same stretch of land.\" This is a methodological discipline characteristic of Marx: the mechanism must be traced to the correct law, not merely to a numerically similar outcome. 2.7 Two Further Routes and the Central Difficulty of Land A Marx announces the remaining two routes: (a) with a constant price — \"any given price, even one lower than previously prevailing\" — if the additional capital investment on A leads to surplus productivity, \"which must prima facie always be the case up to a certain point\"; and (b) if the productivity of successive capital investments on land A declines . Both presuppose that the increased production is required by the state of demand — the demand assumption is never dropped, since without it extra output simply depresses price and generates no surplus profit at all. At this point Marx identifies the genuine theoretical knot. The law previously established is that \"it is always the individual average price of production of a quarter for the total production (or the total capital outlay) that is decisive.\" For better lands, the general price is given from outside and acts as a barrier; the individual average price can be compared against it and the difference capitalized as rent. But on A, \"unlike the better types of land, there is no production price given outside itself, such as would restrict the equalization between the individual production price and the general one. For the individual production price of A is precisely the general production price that governs the market.\" A is a self-referential case: its individual price is the general price. How then can a difference arise within A itself? 2.8 Route Two, Case (1): Rising Productivity of Successive Investments on A Marx answers with a worked case. Suppose £5 (capital) is advanced on one acre of A, with a cost of production of £6 (the £5 plus the 20 per cent profit). Where the first £2½ yielded 1 quarter, the second yields 2 quarters, so that the improved acre produces 3 quarters for £6 of production cost — an average of £2 per quarter. If those 3 quarters sell at £2 each, \"A continues to bear no rent\" — but the whole basis of DR II has shifted: £2 has become the governing production price instead of £3. A capital of £2½ now yields an average of 1½ quarters on the poorest land, and \"this is now the official yield for all superior types of land when £2½ is invested. A part of their former surplus product goes from now on into forming their necessary product, just as a part of their surplus profit goes into the formation of the average profit.\" The parallel is instructive: just as a general productivity gain converts surplus profit into ordinary average profit, so a general fertility gain converts surplus product into necessary product. Improvement on the worst land, generalized, destroys rent everywhere by lowering the governing price. But Marx then distinguishes the case where the general price does not follow suit. For the better lands \"the general production price is a given barrier to capital investment,\" and their individual costs remain what they were. If they are still valued at the old price, the arithmetic changes: the 1 quarter from the first investment on A costs £3, but each of the 2 quarters from the second costs only £1½. A corn rent of 1 quarter and a money rent of £3 therefore arises on A \"even though the 3 qrs are still sold at their old price of £9 altogether.\" Here is the surplus-product rent in pure form: it appears when the average productivity on A rises but the market price remains anchored to the old, less productive level. Now Marx adds a third investment of £2½ with the same yield as the second, giving 5 quarters for £9 of production cost. If A's individual average price remains governing, each quarter must now sell at £1⅘ (£9 ÷ 5 = £1.8), and \"the average price would have fallen again, not because of a new rise in the yield of the third capital investment, but rather because of the addition of a new capital investment with the same extra yield as the second.\" This is the chapter's most counter-intuitive result: repeated investments of higher but constant yield on the worst land cause a proportionate fall in the price of production, and with it in the differential rent of all other land types. What would increase rent on B or C decreases rent when it happens on A, because on A the individual price authorizes the general price. If instead the first dose's £3 per quarter remains the regulator, then the 5 quarters must sell at £15, and the differential rent for the later investments on A must amount to £6 (30 shillings currency units aside: £15 minus £9 = £6). 2.9 The \"Nonsense\" of Marginal Attribution and the Question of Whether Rent Actually Arises Marx here inserts a methodological warning of exceptional importance for the later history of economics: \"It would be nonsense to say that a third of the capital had produced 1 qr, and the remaining two-thirds had produced 4 qrs. £9 per acre would always produce 5 qrs, while £3 would produce only 1 qr.\" This is a direct rejection of the practice of assigning to each \"dose\" of capital its own discrete marginal product and then treating rents as payments to the more productive doses. Capital is a joint input on the acre: only the total outlay and the total product have determinate meaning in the aggregate accounting. This anticipates the later marginalist construction of a downward-sloping demand curve for capital-doses and denies its legitimacy at the level of the individual holding. Marx then returns to the question of whether a rent actually arises at all: \"Whether or not a rent arises here — a surplus profit — would depend entirely on the circumstances. Normally, the governing price of production would have to fall.\" The condition under which the price falls is a general revolution in agriculture : if improvements on A are undertaken precisely because they have become general on the better soils, then \"when we speak of the natural fertility of land A, we assume that it is obtained with £6 or £9 instead of with £3.\" The \"natural\" fertility of A is itself historically relative — it depends on the prevailing technique — a point of enormous consequence for the critique of any notion of an intrinsic, technical natural fertility. But if the improvement affects only a small portion of A's acreage at the outset, then the improved part yields a surplus profit that \"the landowner would quickly reach out to turn completely or in part into rent, and fix it as such.\" As demand keeps pace with the growing supply and the whole of A is gradually shifted to the new method, \"rent could gradually form on all land of quality A and the surplus profit would be completely or partially confiscated, according to the market conditions.\" 2.10 Landed Property as a Price-Raising Power — and the Free-Trade Variant This yields the chapter's most politically charged formulation. \"The establishment of equality between A's production price and the average price of its product in conditions of increased capital outlay might ... meet an obstacle in the fixation in the form of rent of the surplus profit of this increased capital outlay.\" The consequence is that \"it would again be the transformation of the surplus profit into ground-rent, i.e. the intervention of landed property, that raised the production price, instead of the differential rent being simply the result of differences between the individual production price and the general one.\" On land A, this intervention \"would prevent the two prices from coinciding because it would prevent the production price from being governed by A's average production price; a higher production price than necessary would be maintained, and rent created accordingly.\" Concealed in that dense sentence is the seed of the next chapter's theory of absolute rent : rent here is no longer a passive residue of differential productivity, but an active cause of a higher price of production. Landed property functions as a barrier to the equalization of individual and general prices. Marx drives the point home by extending it to the case of free imports: \"Even with the free import of corn from abroad, the same result could be obtained or maintained, if the farmer were compelled to turn to other uses, e.g. pasture, such land as was capable of competing in grain cultivation without yielding rent, at the price of production governed by foreign conditions, with the result that only rent-bearing land — i.e. only land whose individual average price of production per quarter was less than that determined by conditions abroad — would be used for the cultivation of grain.\" Free trade, on this analysis, does not necessarily abolish rent by exposing domestic producers to cheap foreign corn; it can instead shrink the margin of cultivation until only rent-yielding land is still in grain, with the land that would have been the rent-free margin withdrawn to pasture. The general conclusion is a compromise between the pure competition model and the landlord-determined one: the production price \"would fall ..., though not to the average price. It would stand higher than this, but below the production price of the worst cultivated land A, so that competition from new land would be restricted.\" 2.11 Route Three, Case (2): Declining Productivity of Additional Capital on A The final substantive case reverses the direction of the productivity assumption. Land A₋₁ can produce each additional quarter at £4; land A can do it at £3½ — cheaper than A₋₁, but £½ dearer than the quarter produced by A's first investment. The total price of the 2 quarters produced on A would then be £6½, an average price of £3¼. \"The production price would rise, but only by £¼, whereas if the additional capital was applied to new land which produced at [£3¼–£3½], it would rise by a further £¼ ... and would thereby cause a proportionate rise in all other differential rents.\" The structural consequence is that the same extra quarter can be had by two routes with two different effects on the rent structure. If it comes from additional capital on A , the general price rises only to A's new average (£3¼) — and at that price A yields no rent, since its average price of production and the governing price coincide. If it comes from new land , the price rises further, and all other rents rise proportionately. Marx again underscores the pivotal point: \"It depends once more on how far the second capital investment on the available land A has become general, whether the production price is equalized to the average price, or whether the individual production price of the second capital investment, £3½, becomes the governing one.\" And once again the landlord intervenes. If the second investment's individual price of £3½ becomes governing, then A yields a rent — and it does so \"even on acres of A on which no extra capital investment had been made, and which therefore still continued to produce at £3 per qr.\" Untouched acres benefit from their neighbour's investment. But this outcome requires a condition: \"As long as there are still untilled stretches of A, the price could rise only temporarily to £3½. The competition of new stretches of A would keep the price of production down to £3 until all land A able by its favourable situation to produce at less than £3½ per qr was exhausted.\" Marx adds the crucial caveat that closes the door on the neat Ricardian story: \"This is what we would assume, even though when one acre of a certain land bears rent, the landowner will not lease out another acre of the same land rent-free.\" Once rent exists anywhere on A, no landlord will contract out a comparable acre for nothing; the mere existence of rent on one acre re-prices all comparable land. This is the micro-institutional mechanism by which a temporary differential price becomes a permanent rent. 2.12 Liebig, Declining Soil Productivity, and the Classic Result Restated Marx closes the analytic core with a bibliographic gesture: \"Liebig should be consulted on the declining productivity of the soil when successive capital investments are made.\" The editor's footnote supplies the identification — Freiherr Justus von Liebig (1803–73), the organic chemist and pioneer of agricultural chemistry, author of Die Chemie in ihrer Anwendung auf Agricultur und Physiologie (7th edn, 1862). The footnote adds that Marx borrowed from Liebig the concept of metabolism ( Stoffwechsel ), which he transformed into the analysis of the labour process in Volume 1, Chapter 7. That is a striking piece of intellectual genealogy: the concept of nature's material cycles entering human labour as a metabolic exchange comes directly out of the agricultural-chemistry debate over soil exhaustion. Marx then restates the classical DR II result in its most general form: \"We have seen how the successive decline in surplus productivity of capital investments always increases the rent per acre when the price of production is constant, and how it can even do this when the price is falling.\" The chapter has thus connected the declining returns literature with the rent literature in a single theorem. 2.13 The Free Gifts of Nature and the \"Relative Increase in Price\" The general note that closes the notebook entry is among the most philosophically loaded passages in Volume 3. Marx begins: \"From the standpoint of the capitalist mode of production, there is always a relative increase in the price of products if, in order to obtain the same product, an outlay must be made that was previously unnecessary.\" The reasoning is then unpacked: the replacement of capital consumed in production is not simply the replacement of values embodied in means of production. Natural elements that enter production as agents without costing anything — \"whatever role they might play in production — do not go in as components of capital, but rather as a free natural power of capital; in fact a free natural productive power of labour, but one which on the basis of the capitalist mode of production presents itself as a productive power of capital, like every other productive power.\" This is a double claim. First, an economic claim: natural powers that cost nothing do not enter into price determination \"as long as the product supplied with its aid is sufficient to meet the demand.\" Only when a larger product is required than the free natural power can yield — so that the additional product must be produced \"without the aid of this natural power or with human assistance\" — does a new element, human labour, enter the capital advanced. \"A relatively greater capital investment is thus needed to obtain the same product. All other circumstances remaining the same, production becomes more expensive.\" Second, a critical-naturalist claim: the free gift of nature appears under capitalism as a productive power of capital rather than of labour or of nature, exactly as every other social productive power does. This is not incidental; it is the mechanism by which fertility differences become the basis of a revenue , not merely of a physical abundance. The passage effectively explains why scarcity of natural powers has a price consequence: the price is not paid for the power itself, but for the labour required to replace what the power previously supplied free of charge . Value still comes from labour; the disappearance of the free natural subsidy merely increases the labour required to secure the same product. 2.14 The Appended Section: \"Differential Rent and Rent as Simply Interest on the Capital Incorporated into the Soil\" (The text is marked \"(From a notebook 'Begun mid-February 1876': F. E.),\" indicating that Engels lifted this section from a later notebook and inserted it here.) Marx opens the second half with a catalogue of what \"so-called permanent improvements\" actually are. They change the physical characteristics of the soil, and partly its chemical properties, by operations requiring capital outlay, and thus \"can be considered as an incorporation of capital into the soil.\" But — and this is his crucial reduction — they \"almost all boil down to giving a particular piece of land, the soil in a particular and restricted place, characteristics that other land somewhere else, and often quite close by, possesses by nature.\" He then gives five concrete pairs: one piece of land is naturally level, another must be levelled; one is naturally drained, another requires artificial draining; one has a naturally deep top-soil, another must be artificially deepened; one clay soil is naturally mixed with the requisite sand, another must be artificially corrected; one meadow is naturally irrigated or silt-covered, another must be made so \"by labour, or, in the language of bourgeois economics, by capital.\" That sardonic closing clause sets the analytic target: bourgeois economics systematically substitutes capital for labour in describing what was actually done. Against this backdrop Marx attacks the target theory: \"Now it is a truly amusing theory which asserts that on the land whose comparative advantages are acquired, rent is interest, while on the other land, which has these advantages by nature, it is not.\" He grants that in practice the question is \"confused\" because \"rent really does coincide with interest in the one case, so it is also called interest and has to be misnamed as such in the others, where this is positively not the case.\" But the confusion is a category error, and Marx dissolves it with a two-step argument: First, causation : \"after the capital investment has been made, the land bears the rent not because capital has been invested in it but rather because the capital investment has made the land a more productive field of investment than before.\" The investment is a condition of the rent, not its cause ; the cause is the differential in productivity relative to the still-unimproved land. Second, the equalization test : \"If we assume that all the land in a country requires this capital investment, each piece of land which has not yet passed through this state has to do so, and the rent borne by the land already provided with this capital investment (the interest that it yields in the given case) is just as much a differential rent as if it possessed this advantage by nature and the other land had to obtain it artificially.\" In other words, imagine the counterfactual in which every plot requires the same improvement. Then the improved plot holds no differential advantage, and its rent vanishes. The fact that some land can be improved while other land has not yet been improved is a transitory differential — no different in kind from the natural differential between good and bad soil. Third, amortization : \"This rent which can be resolved into interest also becomes pure differential rent as soon as the capital laid out is amortized. Otherwise the same capital would have to lead a double existence as capital.\" The point is an accounting theorem of some elegance. Suppose a farmer invests £1,000 in drainage and receives, in the form of higher rent, both the ordinary interest on that £1,000 and repayment of the capital itself. If the \"rent\" continued to include the amortization element after the capital had been recovered, the same capital would have been repaid to its owner while still being counted as productive capital — existing twice over, once in the owner's pocket and once in the soil. Once amortization is properly completed, the only thing that remains is the surplus over the new general level of productivity, i.e. pure differential rent. 2.15 The Closing Footnote: Nature, Labour, and the Value Controversy The final footnote (editorially appended) connects the chapter to the theoretical controversy that frames all of Part VI: \"It is a most curious phenomenon that all those opponents of Ricardo who struggled against the determination of value exclusively in terms of labour, when faced with the fact that differential rent arose from differences in land, maintained that in this case nature determined value instead of labour, though at the same time they allowed this determination in the case of the land's position, or even, and still more, for interest on capital put into the soil for the purpose of cultivation.\" The polemical structure is telling. The anti-Ricardians wanted to use differential rent as a counter-example to the labour theory of value: different prices for corn from different soils must mean that nature, not labour, determines value. But they simultaneously accepted that the location of land (a socially determined, transport-cost differential) and the interest on capital invested in the soil play the same role — both of which are plainly labour- and capital-determined. Marx's riposte is that the objection confuses two distinct quantities: \"The same labour produces the same value for the product created in a given time; but the size or amount of this product, and thus the portion of value which falls to a particular aliquot part, depends for a given quantity of labour solely on the amount of the product, and this in turn on the productivity of the given amount of labour, not on its absolute amount.\" That is, the magnitude of value is fixed by labour time; what fertility determines is the quantity of use-values that a given quantity of labour yields, and hence the proportion of total value that attaches to each \"aliquot part\" of output. A bushel of corn from rich soil embodies the same socially necessary labour as a bushel from poor soil — the valueless differential of nature does not put a second, independent source of value into the commodity. The footnote's closing sentence is a precise statement of the position: \"Whether this productivity is due to nature or society is quite immaterial. But in the case where it itself costs labour, i.e. capital, it increases the costs of production by a new component, which is not the case when nature alone is involved.\" Nature alone contributes only use-value abundance; only labour (or labour embodied in capital) contributes value and hence a price-affecting cost component. This is why \"free gifts\" must be replaced by paid labour before they can raise the price of production. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Three fundamental principles emerge from this chapter with unusual clarity. First, the identity of the marginal producer and the price-governor is historical, not logical. The entire Ricardian apparatus depends on the assumption that the worst land in use sets the price. Marx demonstrates that once DR II is in play, the marginal dose of capital — which may lie on the best land — can become the price-governor. The worst land then earns rent, and every cultivated acre becomes rent-bearing. The \"no-rent margin\" is thus a limit case that holds only under the special and transient assumption of equal capital intensity across all soils. Second, the economic category of rent is a social relation , not a physical fact. Throughout, the productivity differential is a necessary but insufficient condition for rent. The decisive further condition is the \"intervention of landed property\" — the landlord's power to fix a surplus profit as rent before the competitive equalization of individual and general production prices has time to do its work, and the landlord's refusal to lease comparable acres rent-free once rent exists on one. The same physical differential, in the absence of this social relation, would either fall to the consumer as a lower price or be competed away as average profit. Third, the distinction between value and use-value is the load-bearing wall of the whole theory. Nature contributes abundance; labour contributes value. The differential rents that seem to arise \"from the land itself\" are always traceable back to differing quantities of product per unit of labour — a productivity effect, not a nature effect. When the natural power is itself replaced by labour (drainage, levelling, irrigation, deepening), production costs rise by a new component , and only then does the free gift of nature leave a trace in price. The practical and theoretical implications ramify in several directions. For the historical analysis of capitalist agriculture, the chapter explains why rising corn prices benefit landlords disproportionately: money rents rise faster than corn rents, and rents on the poorest land appear precisely in periods of intensification. For the theory of free trade, it shows that cheap foreign corn need not abolish rent if domestic capital is instead pushed to abandon marginal grain land for pasture; the production price falls \"not to the average price\" but only to a level constrained by the rent-bearing acreage that remains. For the later development of economics, the \"nonsense\" passage is a decisive rejection of marginal-product attribution in agriculture, and the analysis of rent as a barrier to price equalization is one of the earliest statements of the modern theory of imperfect competition in land markets. For ecological and resource economics, the closing note on free natural powers anticipates the contemporary \"depletion cost\" literature: a natural input's price impact appears only when its free availability ceases and it must be replaced by labour. For Marxist theory itself, the chapter bridges DR I/DR II and absolute rent, showing that the theory of differential rent can generate rent on the worst land without monopoly, and then showing how monopoly (landed property) pushes even that result further, above the level warranted by differential productivity alone. Eng ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 43 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 45"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 45", "title": "Chapter 45", "subtitle": "Absolute Ground-Rent", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 45/Chapter 45 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 12663, "read_time": "63 min read", "tags": ["Absolute Ground-Rent & Landed Property", "Organic Composition of Capital (OCC)"], "keywords": ["Absolute Ground-Rent", "Lower Organic Composition of Agriculture", "Agricultural Value Exceeding Price of Production", "Landed Property Barrier Preventing Equalization", "Landed Property Levying Tribute on Society", "Monopoly Ownership Denying Free Capital Inflow"], "text": "Chapter 45 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 12,591 words ⏱️ ~63 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem: Why Does the Poorest Land Pay Rent? Chapter 45 of Marx's Capital , Volume III, entitled \"Absolute Ground-Rent,\" confronts what is arguably the single most refractory theoretical problem in the entire classical theory of rent: the stubborn empirical and conceptual puzzle of why the least fertile land under cultivation—the marginal land whose production price (cost of production plus average profit) is presumed to set the governing market price—nonetheless yields a rent to its owner. Having spent the preceding chapters (the theory of differential rent) establishing that rent arises as a surplus profit generated by differences in the fertility or location of land, Marx here must answer a prior question: what happens when there is no difference to exploit? What happens on class-A land, the worst land, the land whose individual price of production coincides with the general market price? The classical—and specifically Ricardian—answer to this question had been to assert confidently that the worst land pays no rent at all . The premise that type-A land yields zero rent runs as a foundational axiom through the entire Ricardian edifice, and Marx opens this chapter by conceding that premise—indeed by treating it as the analytical device that makes differential rent intelligible in the first place. But the chapter's intellectual dynamism comes from Marx's progressive demonstration that this premise, while heuristically useful and analytically harmless to the law of differential rent, is ultimately false as a description of actual capitalist agriculture. The marginal land, precisely because it is monopolized as private property, must pay a rent—and the modalities of that payment constitute what Marx calls absolute rent . The Thesis: Landed Property as a Barrier, Not Merely a Siphon The chapter's core thesis is that there are two distinct, normal forms of ground-rent , each with a fundamentally different causal structure: Differential rent , in which landed property merely plays the passive role of seizing (or capturing) a surplus profit that the competition of capitals would otherwise leave in the farmer's pocket. Here, landed property is not the cause of the price component that forms the rent; the surplus profit arises independently (from differences in the productivity of land), and landed property simply transfers it from the tenant to the owner. Absolute rent , in which landed property plays the active, creative role of blocking the equalization of surplus-value across spheres of production and thereby causing the market price to rise above the general price of production . Here, landed property is a barrier—\"an alien power,\" in Marx's striking phrase—that obstructs the free movement of capital; it is this obstruction, and not any differential advantage of the soil, that generates the rent by preventing the value of agricultural products from being levelled down to their production price. Marx's argument hinges on a rigorous distinction, central to his whole value theory, between the value of a commodity and its price of production . Because agricultural capital is presumed to have a lower organic composition than the average social capital—that is, it employs relatively more living labour-power, and relatively less machinery and raw material, in proportion to the total capital advanced—the value of agricultural produce stands above its price of production. This excess is a surplus-value that, under the free competition of capitals, would normally be syphoned into the general pool and redistributed to give the average rate of profit. Landed property, precisely by refusing to let capital invest on the land without first levying a toll, blocks that equalization; the surplus is held back in agriculture and captured by the landowner as rent. Thus, in genuine contrast to the Ricardian tradition, Marx demonstrates that the rent paid by the worst land is not a differential rent, not a tax, not merely a monopoly price in the ordinary sense, but the portion of agricultural surplus-value retained by the landowner by virtue of the monopoly of landed property itself . Intellectual Objective and Broader Framework The chapter's objective is therefore threefold. First, it aims to demonstrate the independence of the law of differential rent from the premise of zero rent on the worst land, thereby inoculating the earlier analysis against the charge that its conclusions hinge on a false supposition. Second, it aims to develop the concept of absolute rent as a theoretically necessary category, grounded in the specific organic composition of agricultural capital and in the class-monopoly of landed property. Third, and most far-reachingly, it aims to show that the very category of price of production presupposes the free competition and mobility of capitals—and that wherever a \"foreign power\" restricts such mobility (as landed property does for agriculture, mining, and extractive industry generally), value and price of production may diverge in a lasting way, allowing surplus-value to be siphoned off as rent rather than equalized as profit. Where does this chapter fit into the broader theoretical architecture? It sits at the culminating hinge of Marx's theory of rent as a whole. In Capital Volume III, Part VI (\"The Transformation of Surplus Profit into Ground-Rent\") moves from the more familiar analysis of differential rent into this final, distinctly Marxist terrain. The chapter thus functions simultaneously as a critique of Ricardo and Adam Smith, as a development of the theory of value into the theory of competition and landed property, and as a bridge toward the more generalized problems of monopoly, taxation, colonial appropriation, and the historical specificity of capitalist landed relations. It is also, importantly, a chapter about transitions—about the shift from the \"old countries\" to the colonies, from industry to extractive industry, from corn to timber and cattle—and thus embeds the abstract theory of rent in historical and comparative analysis. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS ### Preserving Differential Rent from the Zero-Rent Premise: The Analytical Precaution Marx opens the chapter not by announcing the discovery of absolute rent, but by first doing a piece of conceptual housekeeping. The differential-rent analysis developed in earlier chapters had proceeded from the premise that \"the worst land pays no ground-rent.\" Marx's first move is to establish that the law of differential rent, as differential rent , is entirely independent of whether this premise is true. This is a crucial methodological point: he wants to quarantine the analysis of differential rent from the subsequent analysis of absolute rent, so that the two categories can be developed without contaminating each other. The argument is presented as a formal calculation. Let P be the general production price governing the market—that is, the price equal to the constant and variable capital consumed in the course of production, plus the average profit ( profit of enterprise plus interest ). For the product of class-A land, P coincides with its individual price of production; hence rent on A = zero. Now consider class B, whose individual production price is P′. Since B is more fertile, P > P′. Let d = P − P′ This d is the surplus profit made by the farmer in class B, and it is transformed into rent paid to the landowner. For class C, with individual production price P″, the surplus is P − P″ = 2d; for class D, with P‴, we get P − P‴ = 3d, and so on in the familiar arithmetic progression of differential rent. The crucial test comes when Marx assumes that the premise of zero rent for A is false , and that A pays a rent r. Two consequences follow immediately. First , the price of class-A produce would no longer be governed by its simple price of production, but would be P + r . Marx insists on the reasoning here: assuming the normal condition of capitalist production—that is, assuming the surplus r paid to the landowner is neither a deduction from wages nor from the average profit of capital —the farmer can pay it only by selling above his price of production. If he did not have to hand the surplus over as rent, he would retain it as a surplus profit. Therefore the governing market price of the total product from all types of land would be P + r, not P. Since the price of class-A product always forms the limit of the governing market price—the price at which the total quantity can be supplied—it governs the price of the total product. Second , and decisively, the law of differential rent is not abolished by this general rise. If A's product sells at P + r, then B, C, and D must sell at P + r as well. But: (P + r) − (P′ + r) = P − P′ = d (P + r) − (P″ + r) = P − P″ = 2d (P + r) − (P‴ + r) = P − P‴ = 3d The differential rents are therefore algebraically unchanged . The r cancels out. The differential rent contains an element independent of its own law (the r) and undergoes a general rise together with the whole price level, but the differential structure remains governed by the same law as before. From this Marx draws a conclusion of real methodological elegance: \"whatever the rent on the least fertile types of land might be, not only is the law of differential rent independent of it, but the only way to grasp the true character of differential rent itself is to set the rent for class A land at zero.\" This is a profound point about theoretical abstraction. Whether or not A actually pays rent is immaterial for the analysis of differential rent, and setting it at zero is not a falsification but a clarification—it isolates the differential component by removing the non-differential increment. The abstraction \"A pays no rent\" is thus a deliberate analytical device, and the whole of the preceding differential-rent analysis remains untouched by whatever follows. ### The Real Basis of the Zero-Rent Assumption: Sufficiency vs. Actualizability Having secured the independence of differential rent, Marx turns to examine the actual basis of the assumption that A pays no rent. Here the chapter begins to generate its real theoretical energy. The argument proceeds in stages. If the market price of corn reaches a level at which an additional advance of capital invested on class-A land yields the customary average profit, this condition is sufficient for the investment of capital on that land. In other words, a price equal to the price of production of A's crop makes it worthwhile, from a purely capitalist standpoint, to invest. Marx then adds a refinement. Even in this case, the market price must actually stand higher than A's price of production before new A land is brought under cultivation. The reason is a supply-and-demand dynamic: before the new land is cultivated, supply was insufficient; once the additional supply arrives, supply becomes sufficient, and price must fall. For price to fall, it must first have stood above the A price of production. But because A land is less fertile , the price will not fall all the way back to the level that prevailed when B's price of production governed the market. This is why \"the production price of A sets a limit for a relatively permanent rise in the market price, and not just for a temporary one.\" Marx then considers a variant: if the newly cultivated land is more fertile than the old A land yet only sufficient to meet demand, the market price remains unchanged. Here too the analysis coincides—the assumption that A pays no rent would be explained by the fact that the market price just covers the capital plus average profit; that is, the market price provides the price of production. Now comes the pivot. Marx writes: \"But from the premise that capital could now be invested by the farmer on class A land under the average valorization conditions of capital, it in no way follows that this land in class A is now immediately at the farmer's disposal.\" This is the conceptual hinge on which the entire category of absolute rent turns. The fact that the farmer could realize the customary profit if no rent were paid is in no way a reason for the landlord to lease out his land for nothing , \"and be so philanthropic to his client as to extend him a credit gratuit .\" Marx is pointedly mocking the implied philanthropy of the Ricardian assumption. The landlord's very existence as a landowner constitutes a barrier to the investment of capital and to its unrestricted valorization on the land—a barrier that does not collapse merely because the farmer can imagine that corn prices would let him make the average profit if only he could treat landed property as non-existent. Landed property as a barrier —Marx's key phrase—becomes the central theoretical object. But Marx immediately clarifies a subtle point: differential rent presupposes precisely the monopoly of landed property, because without it, the surplus profit would not be transformed into ground-rent at all—it would stay with the farmer. Landed property remains such a barrier even where rent in the form of differential rent disappears , i.e. on type-A land. This is critical: the barrier function of landed property is not contingent on the existence of differential rent. Where differential rent exists, landed property captures surplus profit; where it does not exist, landed property still stands as a gatekeeper against capital. ### Legitimate Exceptions: When Capital Investment on Land Can Occur Without Rent Marx next undertakes a critical, quasi-empirical examination of the cases in which capital investment on the land can take place without payment of rent. He formulates these as potential counterexamples to his developing thesis, and his method is to show that all such cases involve a \"factual—if not a legal—abolition of landed property,\" occurring \"only under very special conditions of an accidental nature.\" First case: The landowner is himself a capitalist, or the capitalist is a landowner. In this case, the owner can cultivate his own land as soon as the market price yields the price of production. Why? Because \"as far as he is concerned, landed property does not set any barrier to the investment of his capital.\" He can treat land as a \"simple natural element\" and let his decision be determined exclusively by capitalist considerations—whether his capital is valourized with the average profit. Such cases do exist, but only as exceptions. Marx emphasizes that capitalist cultivation of the land assumes a separation between functioning capital and landed property; it generally rules out cultivation by the proprietor himself. The hypothetical abolition of the barrier is therefore \"purely accidental\"—if increased demand for corn requires cultivation of more A land than self-farming proprietors happen to own, leased land must come into play, and the barrier reasserts itself. To \"start from the separation between capital and land, tenant farmer and landowner, which corresponds to the capitalist mode of production, and then to assume the reverse\" is, Marx declares, \"an absurd contradiction.\" Second case: A leasehold includes pieces of land paying no rent as accessory fillings. A leasehold may include certain parcels that pay no rent at given price levels—and are in fact rented free—though the landlord does not view them that way, since his focus is the total rental of the lease, not the rent of individual components. Here, the rent the farmer pays for investing his capital disappears for these particular pieces, and with it, landed property as a barrier—but crucially, \"by contract with the landlord himself.\" Marx then adds the fatal analytical objection: these non-rent-bearing parcels are \"an inseparable filling sandwiched between the better land.\" The case being investigated , however, is precisely that of type-A tracts that are farmed independently and must therefore be independently leased out under the general preconditions of capitalist production. This second case therefore does not solve the problem. Third case: A farmer invests extra capital on an existing leasehold, even though at current market prices the additional product yields only the price of production, not an additional rent. Here, for one part of capital the farmer pays rent, for another he does not. Does this solve the problem? No. If the market price and fertility permit a surplus yield with the additional capital, the farmer pockets the surplus profit himself for the duration of the lease. Why? Because \"as long as the tenancy contract lasts, the barrier that landed property places to the investment of his capital in the land has been removed.\" Yet, Marx continues, the mere fact that in order to secure this surplus profit the farmer must take on additional worse land and lease it separately , shows irrefutably that the investment of additional capital on the old land is not sufficient to produce the increased supply needed. \"The one assumption rules out the other.\" Marx then considers a possible objection: could one say that the rent of the worst type-A land is itself a differential rent compared with the land cultivated by its proprietor (a chance exception), or with additional capital investment on old leaseholds yielding no rent? He rejects this on two grounds. (1) This would be a differential rent that did not arise from differing fertility of types of land—it would not presuppose that A pays no rent and its product sells at the price of production. (2) Whether additional capital investments yield rent or not is as immaterial for whether class-A land pays rent as it is immaterial for a new independent factory whether another manufacturer in the same branch invests a part of his capital in interest-bearing paper or makes extensions yielding less than full profit. The analogy is instructive: any new enterprise must yield the average profit, and is set up on this expectation. Additional capital on old leaseholds and the additional cultivation of new A land set limits to one another : the limit up to which additional capital can be invested on the same leasehold under less favourable conditions is given by the competing new investments on A land; conversely, the rent that A land can yield is limited by the competing additional capital on the old leaseholds. ### The Question Restated and the Two Cases of Price Rise At this point Marx restates the problem with maximum clarity: \"Let us assume that the market price for corn is sufficient for portions of class-A land to be taken into cultivation and for the capital invested to obtain the production price... Is this enough? Can this capital then really be invested? Or must the market price rise high enough for even the worst land A to yield a rent?\" The terms of the question itself already answer a subsidiary issue: the existence of additional capital investments on old leaseholds that yield no rent does not solve the problem of whether capital can now actually be invested on class-A land yielding the average profit but no rent. It is precisely this question—whether land A can be cultivated at all without yielding rent—that is at stake. Marx then lays out two possible cases if the additional cultivation of land A proceeds only in so far as it yields rent (i.e. more than the price of production): Case one: The market price rises in such a way that even the final additional capital investments on the old leaseholds yield surplus profit, whether pocketed by farmer or landlord. This rise in price and the surplus profit from the final investments are then the result of the impossibility of cultivating land A unless rent is obtained. If the price of production alone sufficed to induce cultivation, the price would not have risen so high and new lands would already have come into competition as soon as they yielded simply these prices of production. The rent of land A thus becomes a differential rent compared with those final capital investments on old leaseholds that yield no rent—but this is simply the result of A not being available for cultivation at all unless it yields a rent, i.e. unless there is a need for this rent, which is not determined by any difference in the types of land . This is an absolutely crucial point: the differential rent that now appears on A is generated by the barrier of landed property, not by any advantage of the soil. Case two: The final capital investments on the old leaseholds yield no rent, but the market price has still risen high enough for land A to be taken up and to yield rent. In this case, the additional capital investment that yielded no rent was possible only because land A could not be cultivated until the market price allowed it to pay rent. Absent this condition, land A would already have been cultivated at a lower price level; and those later investments on the old leaseholds that need the high market price to yield the customary profit without rent could not have taken place. Again, the rent of land A forms a differential rent compared with those capital investments on old leaseholds yielding no rent—but the ground of this is that A cannot be cultivated at all unless it yields a rent. Marx draws the decisive conclusion: \"In both cases the rent of land A would not be just the result of a rise in corn prices but the very opposite; the fact that the worst soil has to yield a rent for cultivation to be permitted at all would be the reason why corn prices rise to the point at which this condition can be fulfilled.\" Here Marx articulates what might be called the causal inversion at the heart of absolute rent. In the case of differential rent, price rise comes first, and rent follows as a consequence. In the case of absolute rent on the worst land, the requirement to pay rent comes first, and the price rise follows as its consequence. This is the theoretical linchpin of the whole chapter. Marx summarizes the contrast: Differential rent : landed property \"seizes only the surplus profit that the farmer himself would otherwise pocket.\" Landed property causes the transfer of a portion of the commodity price—a portion reducible to surplus profit—from the capitalist to the landowner. It is \"not in this case a cause that creates this component of price or the rise in price that it presupposes.\" Absolute rent : if the worst type-A land cannot be cultivated—even though cultivation would yield the price of production—until it yields a surplus over and above this price, a rent, then \" landed property is the creative basis of this rise in price. Landed property has produced this rent itself.\" This sentence—\"landed property has produced this rent itself\"—is the purest formulation of the concept of absolute rent in the chapter. ### Rent versus Lease-Price: The Category Distinction Marx now introduces a clarification that prevents confusion between rent as a specific category and the empirical payment known as lease-price. Whenever we speak of class-A land paying no rent—assuming the corn price is governed by the price of production—we mean rent as a specific category . If the lease-price paid by the farmer involves a deduction from the normal wages of his workers or from his own normal average profit, then he does not pay any rent as an independent component of the price of his commodity distinct from wages and profit. This distinction matters because in practice such deductions happen constantly. Marx notes that in countries where agricultural wages are depressed below the normal average level—so that part of wages regularly goes into rent—this is no exceptional case for the farmer on the worst land. The very price of production that makes cultivation possible already includes these low wages as a constituent item, so selling at the price of production does not enable the farmer to pay a rent. The landowner may even lease land to a worker who is content to pay, in the form of rent, \"everything, or the greater part of it, that the sale price yields him over and above his wages.\" In none of these cases is a genuine rent paid, even though a lease-price is. Where relations corresponding to the capitalist mode of production exist, however, rent and lease-price must coincide—and this coincidence is the \"normal situation that is under analysis here.\" This passage is important because it prevents a common empirical objection: if we observe \"rent\" being paid on marginal land, this doesn't automatically refute the Ricardian premise that marginal land pays no genuine rent—it may simply reflect depressed wages or a portion of profit extracted from the farmer. But it also cuts the other way: once properly specified, if capital cannot be invested on marginal land until a genuine rent is payable, then we have absolutely established that landed property is the creator of a price component. ### The Colonial Diversion and Ricardo's Error Marx now turns to a critical passage. He argues that even if the previous cases don't solve the problem, still less does the invocation of colonial conditions . What makes a colony a colony—agricultural colonies proper—is not just the abundance of fertile land, but the fact that \"this land is not appropriated, is not subsumed under landed property.\" This is the \"tremendous distinction\" between old countries and colonies: the legal or factual non-existence of landed property. Marx credits Wakefield ( England and America , 1833) with having \"correctly noted\" this, while also pointing out that Mirabeau père (the Physiocrat) and other early economists had discovered the fact long before. Crucially, Marx adds that it is \"completely immaterial\" whether colonists appropriate land directly or pay the state a nominal land price as a simple tax for a legal title. It is also immaterial that already-settled colonists may be legal owners. What matters is that \"landed property actually forms no barrier to the investment of capital, or of labour without capital.\" Late arrivals can always make new land into a field of investment. Therefore, if we want to investigate how landed property affects prices and rent where it restricts land as a field of investment for capital, it is \"completely absurd to refer to free bourgeois colonies where neither the capitalist mode of production in agriculture nor the form of landed property corresponding to this exists, indeed where landed property does not exist at all.\" This is Marx's direct critique of Ricardo , who—in his chapter on ground-rent—starts by saying he intends to analyse the effect of the appropriation of land on the value of its products, but then immediately takes the colonies as his illustration, assuming land there is in a relatively elementary state and its exploitation not impeded by the monopoly of landed property. The criticism is methodological: Ricardo analyzes the effect of a cause (landed property) in conditions where the cause has been removed . Marx then reasserts the general principle: \"Legal ownership of land, by itself, does not give the proprietor any ground-rent. It certainly does give him the power, however, to withdraw his land from cultivation until economic conditions permit a valorization of it that yields him a surplus.\" This is a striking formulation: legal ownership is a power to withhold , not an entitlement to payment. The proprietor can neither increase nor reduce the absolute quantity of land, but he can affect the quantity on the market . Hence the characteristic fact—already noted by Fourier —that in all civilized countries a relatively significant portion of the land always remains uncultivated. Returning to the core question: if demand requires taking up new land less fertile than what has been cultivated, will the owner lease it for nothing just because the market price has risen enough to yield the farmer the price of production and the customary profit? \"In no way.\" The capital investment must yield him a rent. He leases only when a lease-price can be paid. The market price must therefore rise above the price of production, to P + r . Since, by assumption, landed property brings in nothing without being leased, and unleased land is economically worthless, \"a small rise in the market price above the price of production is sufficient to bring new land of the poorest kind onto the market.\" ### The Monopoly-Price / Tax Question: Value versus Price of Production With the qualitative claim established—that rent on the worst land comes into being and raises the market price—Marx turns to the further question of the form this rent takes. He asks whether it follows that the price of the worst land's product is necessarily a monopoly price in the customary sense , or a price including rent in the form of a tax levied by the landowner rather than the state. He first acknowledges that such a \"tax\" has definite economic limits: additional capital investments on old leaseholds, competition from foreign agricultural products (assuming free import), competition between landed proprietors, and the need of consumers and their ability to pay. But, he insists, \"this is not what is involved here.\" The real question is whether the rent paid by the poorest land enters the price of its product in the same way as a tax enters the price of the commodity on which it is levied —that is, as an element independent of its value . Marx's answer is a firm no, and the grounds are that this \"in no way necessarily follows, and is maintained only because the distinction between the value of commodities and their price of production has as yet not been understood.\" This is the theoretical crux of the whole chapter. It requires a full digression into the relation between value, price of production, and organic composition of capital. Marx begins by restating fundamental results already established: The production price of a commodity is not identical with its value , even though production prices of commodities considered in their totality are governed only by total value, and although movements of production prices of different kinds of commodities are determined, all other circumstances equal, exclusively by movements of their values. The production price of a commodity may stand above or below its value, and coincides with it only in exceptional cases. From this it follows immediately that \"the fact that agricultural products are sold above their price of production in no way proves that they are also sold above their value,\" just as the fact that industrial products are sold on average at their production price does not show that they are sold at their value. It is possible for agricultural products to be sold above their price of production yet below their value ; many industrial products yield their production price only because they are sold above their value . The determinant of a commodity's price of production relative to its value is the organic composition of the capital producing it—the proportion between the variable part of capital (laid out on wages) and the constant part (laid out on the material conditions of labour). If a sphere's composition is lower than the average social capital—if its variable component is greater relative to the constant—then the value of its product must stand above its price of production. Such a capital produces more surplus-value given the same exploitation of labour and therefore more profit than an equal aliquot part of average social capital, because it applies more living labour. The surplus-value produced by the average social capital is less. Conversely, if the capital in a sphere is higher in composition than the average, the value of its commodities stands below their price of production—as with the products of the most highly developed industries. Marx then translates this into productivity terms. A lower-than-average composition \"is firstly only a different expression for the fact that the productivity of social labour in this particular sphere of production stands below the average level\"—since the level of productivity is expressed in the relative preponderance of constant over variable capital, or in the steady decline of the wages component. A higher composition expresses above-average development of productivity. This is not, however, an arbitrary social accident. Different spheres of production, by their technical characteristics , require different proportions of constant and variable capital. Marx notes the case of extractive industry (which he insists should be clearly distinguished from agriculture): here raw material completely disappears as an element of constant capital, and ancillary materials play a significant role only occasionally. Fixed capital, however, does play a major role in mining. Even here, the course of development can be measured by the relative growth in constant capital compared with variable. Applying this to agriculture proper: if its composition is less than the social average, this is prima facie expression of the fact that in countries of developed production, agriculture has not progressed to the same extent as manufacturing industry. Marx offers a historical explanation: this fact is explicable in terms of the earlier and more rapid development of the mechanical sciences , and especially their application, compared with the later and in part still very recent development of chemistry, geology, and physiology , and their application to agriculture in particular. He cites as \"indubitable and long-known\" the fact (referencing Dombasle and R. Jones ) that advances in agriculture are always expressed in a relative growth of the constant portion of capital over the variable. Whether the composition of agricultural capital is actually less than the social average in a particular country—say, England—can only be settled by statistical investigation, which Marx says would be superfluous for his purpose. In any case, \"it still holds theoretically that it is only on this premise that the value of agricultural products can rise above their price of production,\" that is, that the surplus-value produced in agriculture by a capital of a given size is greater than for an equally large capital of average social composition. \"This assumption is therefore sufficient as far as the form of rent we are examining here is concerned, and it is a necessary assumption for this rent to arise. Where this hypothesis is inapplicable, the form of rent corresponding to it disappears.\" ### Why Surplus-Value Above Production Price Is Not Enough: The Presupposition of Equalization Marx now confronts a decisive objection to any simple derivation of absolute rent from low organic composition alone. The simple fact of a surplus of the value of agricultural products over their price of production would \"in no way be sufficient in itself to explain the existence of a ground-rent independent of the differences in fertility between types of land or successive investments of capital on the same land\"—that is, a rent conceptually distinct from differential rent, which Marx now names absolute rent . His argument: a whole number of manufacturing products are characterized by a value above their price of production, without thereby yielding a surplus over and above the average profit that could be transformed into rent. This is intrinsic to the concept of price of production and the general rate of profit. It is the very existence of price of production that rests on the fact that individual commodities are not sold at their values. Prices of production arise from an adjustment of commodity values: after reimbursing the respective capital values consumed in different spheres, the total surplus-value is distributed not in the proportion in which it was produced, but in proportion to the size of the capitals advanced . Only thus does an average profit arise, and a production price characterized by this average profit. Marx then describes the mechanism as a \"constant tendency of capitals\": by competition, to bring about this adjustment and to overcome all obstacles. Capitals tend only to tolerate such surplus profits as arise—under whatever circumstances— not from the difference between the values of commodities and their prices of production, but rather from the general price of production governing the market and the individual production prices differing from this . These are surplus profits that arise within a sphere of production, not between spheres, and thus do not affect the general production prices or the general rate of profit. They presuppose both the transformation of value into price of production and the general rate of profit. The presupposition of this transformation, in turn, depends on the continuously changing proportionate distribution of the total social capital between the various spheres of production —on a continuous immigration and emigration of capitals, on their transferability from one sphere to another, in short, on their free movement as so many available fields of investment. It is assumed that no barriers, or at least only accidental and temporary ones , prevent the competition of capitals from reducing value to price of production—e.g. in a sphere where the value of commodities stands above their price of production—and thereby distributing the extra surplus-value in due proportion to all spheres exploited by capital. Now comes the decisive theoretical move: \"If the opposite occurs, i.e. capital comes up against an alien power that it can overcome only partly or not at all, a power which restricts its investment in particular spheres of production, allowing this only under conditions that completely or partially exclude that general equalization of surplus-value to give the average profit, it is clear that in these spheres of production a surplus profit will arise, from the excess of commodity value above its price of production, this being transformed into rent and as such becoming autonomous vis-à-vis profit.\" And \"it is as an alien power and a barrier of this kind that landed property confronts capital as regards its investment on the land, or that the landowner confronts the capitalist.\" This is the theoretical definition of absolute rent in nuce. ### The Mechanism of Absolute Rent Fully Articulated Marx now presents the complete mechanism. Landed property is the barrier that does not permit any new capital investment on formerly uncultivated or unleased land without levying a toll—i.e. demanding a rent— even if the land newly brought under cultivation yields no differential rent . Without landed property, such land could have been cultivated already with a smaller rise in the market price, so that the governing market price would have paid the tiller of the worst land only his price of production. But as a result of the barrier, the market price must rise to a point at which the land can pay a surplus over the price of production, i.e. a rent. Since—by our assumption—the value of the commodities produced by agricultural capital is above their price of production, this rent forms the excess of the value above the price of production , or a part of this excess (with a further case to be examined). Whether the rent equals the whole difference or only a greater or lesser part of it depends entirely on the state of supply in relation to demand and the scale of the area newly brought under cultivation . As long as the rent is not equal to the excess of value over price of production, one part of this surplus always goes into the general equalization and proportionate distribution of all surplus-value. As soon as the rent equals that excess, this entire part of the extra surplus-value is withdrawn from the equalization process . This leads to a paradoxical-sounding formulation about monopoly price . Marx writes: \"But whether this absolute rent is equal to the whole extra value over and above the price of production, or only to a part of this, agricultural products are always sold at a monopoly price, not because their price stands above their value but rather because it is equal to their value, or is below their value but above their price of production.\" This is one of the most subtle passages in the chapter. The \"monopoly\" here consists in this: their value is not levelled down to their price of production , as it is with other industrial products whose values stand above the general price of production. In normal industry, low-composition sectors see their above-average surplus-value redistributed to the general pool; in agriculture, landed property blocks this redistribution. Agricultural products therefore enjoy a \"monopoly price\" in the sense that they are sold at a price that includes surplus-value that would otherwise be socialized—but this price does not exceed their value; it merely fails to fall below it by the amount it would in the absence of landed property. Marx formalizes the point arithmetically. Let cost price = k, the capital consumed. In the price of production, the surplus is p = profit (total surplus-value reckoned on social capital, each individual capital getting an aliquot part). In the value of the commodity, the surplus equals the actual surplus-value this particular capital has produced. If the value of a commodity is above its price of production, then: price of production = k + p value = k + p + d so that p + d = the surplus-value contained in it. The difference between value and price of production is d , the excess of the surplus-value produced by this capital over the surplus-value allotted to it by the general rate of profit. From this several consequences follow: The price of agricultural products can stand above their price of production without reaching their value . Up to a certain point, there can be a lasting price rise for agricultural products before their price has reached their value. It is only as a result of the monopoly of landed property that the excess value over price of production can come to be the general market price. In this case, \" it is not the rise in the product's price that is the cause of the rent but rather the rent that is the cause of the rise in price .\" And since the price of a unit area of the worst land = P + r, all the differential rents rise by corresponding multiples of r—since P + r becomes the governing market price. ### The Numerical Illustration: Equalization Versus Non-Equalization Marx now offers a numerical example to concretize the argument. Assume: Average composition of non-agricultural social capital: 85c + 15v Rate of surplus-value: 100 per cent Price of production: 115 (= 85c + 15v + 15s) Composition of agricultural capital: 75c + 25v Value of the product and governing market value: 125 (= 75c + 25v + 25s) If agricultural and non-agricultural products balanced out—assuming for brevity that total capital is the same in both branches—the total surplus-value would be 40 on a capital of 200 , i.e. 20 per cent . So each product would be sold at 120 . Given equalization to production prices, agricultural products would be sold below their value (120 vs. 125), and industrial products above their value (120 vs. 115). If agricultural products were sold at their full value, they would stand 5 higher and industrial products 5 lower than under equalization. If market conditions do not permit agricultural products to be sold at their full value—at the total surplus over their price of production—the effect lies between the two extremes: industrial products would be sold somewhat above their value, agricultural products somewhat above their price of production. The critical inference follows: \"Even though landed property can drive the price of agricultural products above their price of production, it does not depend on this, but rather on the general state of the market, how far the market price rises above the price of production and towards the value, and to what extent, therefore, the surplus-value produced over and above the given average profit in agriculture is either transformed into rent or goes into the general equalization of surplus-value that settles the average profit.\" This is a point of considerable theoretical subtlety: absolute rent is a variable magnitude. The barrier of landed property is constantly operative, but its effect on the price level—and therefore on the magnitude of the rent—depends on market conditions. In any case, \"this absolute rent, arising from the excess value over and above the price of production, is simply a part of the agricultural surplus-value, the transformation of this surplus-value into rent, its seizure by the landowner; just as differential rent arises from the transformation of surplus profit into rent, its seizure by landed property, at the general governing price of production.\" ### The Two Normal Forms of Rent, and the Residual Category of Monopoly Price Marx now summarizes: \" These two forms of rent are the only normal ones. \" That is, differential rent and absolute rent. Apart from them, rent can derive only from a genuine monopoly price —a price determined neither by the price of production nor by the value of commodities, but rather by the demand of purchasers and their ability to pay. Marx adds that consideration of this belongs to the theory of competition , where the actual movement of market prices is investigated. This classification is exceptionally important for the chapter's overall architecture: it structures the entire field of rent theory into (a) the differential, (b) the absolute, and (c) the genuinely monopolistic residual, and it locates each in its proper place. ### The Case Where All Land Is Leased Out Marx now considers the hypothetical where all agricultural land in a country is leased out, assuming capitalist production and normal conditions everywhere. In that case: There would be no land that did not yield rent , but There could be capital investments —particular portions of capital invested on the land—that did not yield rent. For once land is leased out, landed property ceases to operate as an absolute barrier to the capital investment needed. It continues to operate as a relative barrier , in so far as \"the reversion to the landowner of the capital incorporated into the soil sets the farmer very definite barriers.\" In this case, though, all rent would be transformed into a differential rent —determined not by the quality of the soil but rather by the difference between the surplus profit arising on a particular class of land after the final capital investments, and the rent that would be paid for the lease of land of the worst class. Landed property operates as an absolute barrier only in as much as any permission to use land as a field of investment for capital enables the landowner to extract a tribute . Once this permission has been given, the landowner can no longer place any absolute barrier to the quantitative level of capital investment on a given piece of land. Marx illustrates: in house-building, a barrier is always imposed by the landed property of a third party in the land on which the house is to be built. But once this land is leased for house-building purposes, it depends on the lessee whether he plans to erect a large house or a small one. This passage is important because it clarifies the scope of the absolute barrier: it operates at the moment of access to the land, not continuously with respect to the intensity of investment. This means that even under fully universal leasing, the initial toll (the rent on the marginal land) has already been extracted—hence no land exists without rent—but that subsequent capital investments within the leasehold may not pay rent. ### Disappearance of Absolute Rent Under Compositional Equality Marx now specifies the boundary condition for the existence of absolute rent. If the average composition of agricultural capital were the same as the average social capital, or even higher , then absolute rent—in the sense developed here, as a rent different both from differential rent and from rent depending on an actual monopoly price—would disappear . The value of the agricultural product would not stand above its price of production, and agricultural capital would not set more labour in motion, and thus would not realize more surplus labour, than non-agricultural capital. The same result obtains if the composition of agricultural capital were equalized with that of the average social capital as agriculture advanced. This is a crucial analytical statement of the historicity of absolute rent. It is not a permanent, transhistorical feature of agriculture, but rather a consequence of a specific historical relationship between agriculture and industry. As agriculture develops and its composition rises—i.e. as it becomes more capital-intensive relative to the social average—the material basis for absolute rent erodes. ### Reconciling Rising Composition with Rising Prices: A Necessary Digression At this point Marx identifies an apparent contradiction in his own argument: \"At first sight it may seem a contradiction to assume that on the one hand the composition of the agricultural capital increases, with its constant part growing vis-à-vis its variable part, while on the other hand the price of agricultural products rises high enough for new and worse land than previously to pay a rent, which in this case could derive only from an excess of the market price over the value and the price of production, in other words only from a monopoly price for the product.\" If the composition of agricultural capital rises, then—by Marx's own argument—the material basis for absolute rent (the excess of value over price of production) shrinks. Yet at the very same time, agricultural prices may rise high enough for new, poorer land to yield rent. How can both be true? Marx resolves the tension by distinguishing technical composition from value composition —a refinement of the concept of organic composition already developed in Volume I and III. When Marx first examined the formation of the rate of profit, he noted that capitals of similar technical composition—setting the same amount of labour in motion in proportion to machinery and raw material—may still be composed differently because of the differing values of their constant capital components. Raw material or machinery may be dearer in one case than another. If a larger capital must be advanced to set the same amount of labour in motion (because raw material is dearer), then two capitals with identical technical structures may have different value compositions. But if the price of the dearer raw material falls to the level of the cheaper one, the capitals are none the less similar in technical composition; the value ratio between variable and constant capital would then be the same, without any change in the technical proportion. Conversely, a capital of lower organic composition, considered purely in terms of its value composition , could rise to the same level as one of higher organic composition simply by an increase in the value of its constant parts. Marx gives a concrete numerical illustration: Capital A: 60c + 40v (much machinery and raw material relative to living labour-power) Capital B: 40c + 60v (much living labour, little machinery, e.g. 10 per cent, and little and cheap raw material, e.g. 30 per cent) A simple rise in the value of B's raw and ancillary materials from 30 to 80 would equalize the composition: B would now need 80 in raw materials and 60 in labour-power for every 10 in machines, i.e. 90c + 60v , which reduced to percentages is 60c + 40v —all without any technical change in its composition. Thus: Capitals of the same organic composition can have a differing value composition. Capitals of the same percentage (value) composition can stand at varying levels of organic composition , displaying different levels of development of the social productivity of labour. The implication for agriculture is powerful: the mere fact that agricultural capital now stood at the same level by value composition would not prove that the social productivity of labour was equally highly developed. All it could show is that its own product—which forms part of its conditions of production—is dearer, or that ancillary materials such as fertilizer, which used to be obtained locally, now have to be carted a long way, etc. This elegantly disposes of the apparent contradiction: rising value composition need not mean rising technical/organic composition, so absolute rent can persist even as the value composition of agricultural capital appears to rise. ### The Particular Character of Agriculture: Natural vs. Social Productivity But Marx does not rest content with the composition distinction. He goes on to consider \"the particular character of agriculture.\" The argument: assume labor-saving machinery, chemical ancillaries, etc. take up a greater share, so that constant capital grows relative to labour-power—not just in value, but in quantity too. In agriculture (as in mining), however, we must consider not only the social productivity of labour but also its natural productivity , which depends on the natural conditions within which labour is carried on. This has several possible consequences: The increase in social productivity may simply compensate for a decline in natural productivity—or not even do this much—and this compensation can only be effective for a certain period. In that case, despite technical development, the product does not become cheaper but is simply prevented from becoming dearer . In a situation of rising corn prices, the absolute amount produced may decline while the relative surplus product grows. There may be a relative increase in the constant capital (consisting mostly of machines or livestock, only whose depreciation has to be replaced) and a corresponding decline in the variable portion, which must always be replaced in full out of the product. As agriculture progresses, only a moderate rise in market price above the average may be needed for poorer land—which, given a lower level of technical assistance, would have required a larger rise—to be cultivated and also yield a rent. The first of these possibilities is especially important: it explains how agricultural prices can rise even as agricultural techniques improve—because improving techniques are partly offsetting a decline in natural fertility (or at least are preventing things from getting worse), not simply reducing costs. The interaction of social and natural productivity introduces a dimension to agriculture that pure industry lacks. ### The Stock-Raising Objection and Adam Smith's Cattle Analysis Marx anticipates an objection. The fact that in stock-raising , for example, the amount of labour-power applied is on the whole very small compared with the constant capital existing in the livestock themselves, could be taken as refuting the contention that agricultural capital sets more labour-power in motion than average non-agricultural capital. Marx responds with a methodological clarification: \"in explaining rent we take as the initial determinant that section of agricultural capital which produces the decisive cereal foodstuffs and thus the major means of subsistence for all civilized peoples.\" This is, of course, the Ricardian move of focusing on corn as the price-determining commodity—and Marx accepts it, but with a twist. Adam Smith has already shown—\"and this was one of the services he performed\"—that in stock-raising and in the general average of all capital invested on the land that does not go into the production of the major staples, such as corn, the determination of price is completely different . The crucial mechanism here: the price of the product of land used as artificial pasture for cattle—land which could equally well be turned into arable land of a certain quality—has to rise high enough to yield the same rent as equally good arable land. Therefore, in this case, \"the rent of the corn-growing land is a determining factor in the price of cattle.\" Marx endorses Ramsay's observation (from An Essay on the Distribution of Wealth , 1836) that \"in this way the price of cattle is artificially raised by rent, by the economic expression of landed property, and thus by landed property itself.\" Marx then quotes Adam Smith at length (Book One, Chapter XI, I): \"By the extension besides of cultivation the unimproved wilds become insufficient to supply the demand for butcher's meat. A great part of the cultivated lands must be employed in rearing and fattening cattle, of which the price, therefore, must be sufficient to pay, not only the labour necessary for tending them, but the rent which the landlord and the profit which the farmer could have drawn from such land employed in tillage. The cattle bred upon the most uncultivated moors, when brought to the same market, are, in proportion to their weight or goodness, sold at the same price as those which are reared upon the most improved land. The proprietors of those moors profit by it, and raise the rent of their land in proportion to the price of their cattle.\" Marx concludes: \"In this case, too, therefore, the differential rent, as distinct from the corn rent, is in favour of the worst land.\" Far from refuting the theory, the cattle-rent case actually provides a supporting example—the worst land (the uncultivated moors) earns a rent, and it earns it because of the barrier (the requirement that land used for pasture yield the same rent as equally good arable land) rather than any differential superiority. ### The Woodland Example: Monopoly Surcharge or Absolute Rent? Marx now provides a particularly illuminating application. \"Absolute rent explains certain phenomena which at first sight make rent appear due to a mere monopoly price.\" His illustrative case is the owner of a woodland that exists without any human action —not the result of afforestation—in Norway , for example. Append this to Adam Smith's example: if a capitalist who has timber felled (perhaps to meet English demand) pays a rent to the woodland owner, \"he is paid a greater or lesser rent in timber over and above the profit on the capital advanced.\" At first sight, this seems like a simple monopoly surcharge on a purely natural product. But Marx shows this is not the case. The capital here consists almost solely of variable capital laid out on labour, which therefore sets more surplus labour in motion than another capital of the same size. The value of the timber therefore contains a greater excess of unpaid labour—surplus-value—than the product of capitals of higher composition. The average profit can be paid out of this timber, and a significant excess still accrues to the woodland owner in the form of rent. Marx adds a converse qualification: given the ease with which timber-felling can be extended, and this production thus rapidly increased, demand would have to rise very steeply to make the price of timber equal to its value, so that the entire excess of unpaid labour (over and above the capitalist's average profit) would accrue to the proprietor as rent. In other words, the more elastic the supply, the less likely the price rises far enough to capture the full excess value; but even so, some rent may accrue. This example is pedagogically decisive: it takes what looks like the paradigmatic case of pure monopoly rent (a natural forest needing no cultivation) and reveals it as an instance of absolute rent grounded in low organic composition. ### Equally Good or Better Land Newly Cultivated: Four Historical Factors The chapter then pivots to a closely related question. Marx has so far assumed that land newly drawn into cultivation is of still poorer quality than the worst previously cultivated. If the new land were better , it would bear a differential rent. But the problem being investigated is precisely the case where rent does not appear as differential rent. There are only two possibilities for newly cultivated land: either it is worse , or it is equally good . Marx says he has investigated the worse case; now he must investigate the equal case. Equally good land—and even better—can be newly cultivated as agriculture develops, just as much as worse land can (as shown in the differential-rent analysis). Marx provides four grounds : First: The interplay of location and fertility. In the case of differential rent (and rent in general), two factors operate in opposite directions, sometimes counterbalancing one another, sometimes one outweighing the other. A rise in market price—assuming cost price of cultivation hasn't fallen, i.e. technical progress hasn't stimulated additional cultivation—may bring into cultivation more fertile land previously excluded by location . Or, for less fertile land, a price rise may increase the advantage of location enough to balance the low yield. Or, even if price doesn't rise, location can bring better land into competition via improved means of communication —as Marx says we have seen on a large scale with the prairie states of North America . Even in long-civilized countries, this is constantly the case—if not on the same scale as in the colonies, where (as Wakefield notes) location is decisive . Thus the contradictory effects of location and fertility, and the variability of the location factor, \"alternately bring equally good, better or worse tracts of land into competition with those previously cultivated.\" Second: Scientific and agronomic development changes the fertility of the land itself. There is an alteration in the means by which the soil's elements can be made capable of immediate exploitation. In the recent past (from Marx's perspective), light varieties of soil—previously considered inferior—rose to the first rank in France and the eastern counties of England (Marx references Passy ). On the other hand, land considered poor not on account of chemical composition but because mechanical and physical obstacles stood in the way of cultivation was turned into good land once the means for overcoming these obstacles were discovered. Third: Historical and customary conditions withhold land arbitrarily. In all countries of old-established civilization, crown lands, common lands , etc. have withheld great stretches from agriculture \"in a purely arbitrary manner.\" The sequence in which these are brought into cultivation depends neither on quality nor location, but on quite external conditions. Marx notes the history of the English common lands , successively turned into private property by the Enclosure Acts and ploughed up. This demolishes the \"fantastic idea\" that the sequence was worked out by a modern agricultural chemist in the manner of Liebig —that certain fields were marked off for cultivation on account of their chemical properties. What was decisive instead was \"the opportunity that makes the thief\": the more or less plausible legal pretexts for appropriation which the great landlords found. Fourth: The general condition of the capital market and business conditions. Leaving aside that population and capital levels set a certain (elastic) limit to agricultural extension, and leaving aside the effects of accidents—favourable or unfavourable seasons—the geographical extension of agriculture depends on the overall condition of the capital market and the state of business. In periods when business is poor, the possibility that uncultivated land may yield an average profit—whether rent is paid or not—will not suffice to divert additional capital to agriculture. In periods when capital is abundant, it streams into agriculture even without a rise in market prices, as long as normal conditions are fulfilled. Marx's conclusion: better land than previously cultivated was, in fact, only excluded from competition by location, by previous barriers not yet broken through, or by accident. So we have to deal only with kinds of land equally good as that last cultivated. But between the new land and the last cultivated, there always exists a distinction—the varying cost of ploughing up —and it depends on market prices and credit conditions whether this is undertaken. Once the new land actually comes into competition, the market price falls back to its previous level, other conditions equal, so that the new land bears the same rent as the corresponding old land. The hypothesis that it bears no rent is demonstrated by its supporters by assuming what should actually be proved —that the last land did not bear any rent. Marx offers a brilliant analogy: \"One could prove in the same way that the last houses to be built yield no rent besides simple interest on the buildings, even if they are rented out.\" But in fact, they yield ground-rent even before they bring in house-rent, for they often stand empty a long while. Just as successive capital investments on one piece of land can yield a proportionate surplus product and hence the same rent as the first investments, so can fields of the same quality yield the same product at the same cost. \"It would otherwise be incomprehensible how fields of the same quality are ever brought under cultivation successively and not all at once, or indeed why any are at all, since the first would draw after it the competition of all others.\" The passage culminates in a formula of considerable rhetorical force: \"The landowner is always ready to draw a rent, i.e. to receive something for nothing, but capital requires certain conditions in order to fulfil its desire. The mutual competition of plots of land depends not on the landowner's intention to have them compete but rather on the availability of capital to compete on new fields with the old.\" ### The Final Synthesis: The Essence of Absolute Rent Marx's final pages crystallize the whole argument. First, a quantitative observation: \"The extent to which agricultural rent proper is simply a monopoly price can only be a small one, just as absolute rent can only be small in normal conditions , whatever the excess value of the product over its production price may be.\" Absolute rent is a real category but a relatively modest one in magnitude—a point that prevents the theory from becoming a doctrine of agricultural exploitative omnipotence. Then, \"the essence of absolute rent consists in this\": Equally large capitals produce different amounts of surplus-value in different spheres of production, according to their differing average composition, given an equal rate of surplus-value or equal exploitation of labour. In industry, these different amounts are equalized to give the average profit and divided uniformly between individual capitals as aliquot parts of the total capital. Landed property , whenever production needs land—whether for agriculture or for the extraction of raw materials— blocks this equalization for capitals invested on the land, and captures a portion of surplus-value which would otherwise go into the equalization process, giving the general rate of profit. Rent then forms a part of the value of commodities , in particular of their surplus-value, which simply accrues to the landowners who extract it from the capitalists, instead of to the capitalist class who have extracted it from the workers. It is assumed that agricultural capital sets more labour in motion than an equally large portion of non-agricultural capital. The extent of this gap, or its existence at all, depends on the relative development of agriculture vis-à-vis industry. By the nature of the case, this difference must decline with the progress of agriculture , unless the ratio in which the variable part of the capital declines vis-à-vis the constant part is still greater in industrial capital than in agricultural. The last point is remarkably subtle. Absolute rent may not simply disappear as agriculture advances; it may persist if industry's composition rises even faster than agriculture's—if the variable-to-constant ratio declines more rapidly in industry than in agriculture. Thus absolute rent is not a doomed historical residue but an ongoing structural relationship between the two sectors' relative development. ### Extractive Industry: Where Absolute Rent Plays Its Greatest Role The final substantive passage brings the analysis to a broader terrain. \"This absolute rent plays a still more important role in extractive industry proper , where one element of constant capital, raw material, completely disappears, and where, with the exception of branches for which the portion consisting of machinery and other fixed capital is very significant, the lowest composition of capital invariably prevails.\" Because raw material disappears entirely in extractive industry (mining, quarries, forests, fishing), and constant capital is limited mostly to fixed capital, the composition is at its lowest—hence the surplus-value/value gap is at its largest, and the potential for absolute rent is at its greatest. Yet paradoxically, \"precisely here, where rent seems due to a monopoly price alone, extraordinarily favourable market conditions are required for the commodities to be sold at their values or for rent to equal the entire excess of surplus-value in a commodity over and above its price of production.\" This is the case for rent for fishing grounds, quarries, natural forests, etc. The very sectors where rent looks purely monopolistic are the ones where absolute rent is most theoretically fundamental—but also the ones where markets must be particularly tight for the full surplus to be extracted as rent. Marx closes with a critical footnote: \" Ricardo gives an extraordinarily superficial account of this point. See the passage against Adam Smith over rent of forests in Norway, Principles , Chapter II, right at the beginning.\" This is a fitting coda. Ricardo, who throughout the chapter has been Marx's principal foil—first for his naïve assumption of zero rent on marginal land, then for his misuse of colonial examples, and now for his shallow treatment of extractive-industry rent—is again taken to task, this time for failing to see that the \"monopoly price\" appearance of natural-resource rents conceals a deeper systematic foundation in the theory of value. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS ### Fundamental Principles and Systemic Laws Chapter 45 reveals several fundamental principles that reach well beyond the technical details of agricultural rent. First, the systemic law of value equalization and its barriers. Marx's analysis rests on the recognition that the general rate of profit—and the price of production that realizes it—is not a natural fact but a social achievement produced by the free competition and mobility of capitals. The price of production is defined by equalizing surplus-value across spheres in proportion to capital advanced, not in proportion to surplus-value actually generated. This equalization is a tendency , not an automatic mechanism, and it presupposes the absence of barriers to capital's movement. Wherever a \"foreign power\" restricts mobility, the equalization fails, and the surplus-value that would otherwise be socialized is captured locally as rent. Landed property is the paradigm of such a power; but the principle is entirely general—any monopoly or barrier can, in principle, produce a similar effect. Second, the ontological distinction between value and price of production. The chapter demonstrates conclusively that these two categories are analytically distinct, and that confusing them—as the Ricardian tradition does when it treats agricultural rent as a simple price surcharge—leads to theoretical error. Agricultural products may sell above their price of production while still selling below their value. This is a structural feature of low-composition capital, not an anomaly. Marx's numerical example (85c+15v vs. 75c+25v, total surplus divided as 20 per cent on 200, yielding 120 per unit) makes the point inescapable: prices of production equalize the rate of profit but distort the relationship between price and value, and the direction of distortion is systematic by composition. Third, the historicity of economic categories. Absolute rent exists only because agricultural capital has a lower organic composition than the social average, and this is a historically specific fact—a product of the developmental gap between industry and agriculture. As agriculture progresses, the material basis of absolute rent declines. But the chapter also guards against the naive expectation that absolute rent will simply vanish: it may persist if industry's composition rises faster than agriculture's. This is a systemic law, not an evolutionary prognosis: categories evolve with the development of the productive forces, and the \"maturity\" of one sector does not automatically eliminate the peculiarities of its relations with others. Fourth, the causal priority of social relations over technical factors. Marx insists repeatedly that what makes absolute rent possible is not the natural productivity of the soil or the intrinsic difficulty of agriculture, but the social institution of landed property as a barrier . Legal ownership alone does not give rent; it only gives the power to withhold . The rent arises because landowners exercise this power as a class to compel the payment of tribute before permitting capital to be applied. This is a fundamentally social-relational rather than technical explanation. ### Broader Practical and Theoretical Implications For the theory of rent more broadly, the chapter establishes a tripartite taxonomy that persists in Marxist economics: differential rent (capturing surplus profit), absolute rent (capturing a portion of surplus-value blocked from equalization), and true monopoly rent (capturing a sum determined by demand independent of value). It also discredits several confusions: that all rent is a monopoly price; that rent is a tax-like external addition to value; that marginal land pays no rent. Each of these is shown to be theoretically false, though empirically approximate in special cases. For the critique of classical political economy, the chapter is a masterclass in sympathetic yet decisive criticism. Marx grants Ricardo the analytic core of the differential-rent theory, and grants Adam Smith the insight into cattle rent—but shows that each fails to locate rent in the correct theoretical framework. Ricardo's use of the colonies to analyze the effects of landed property is a kind of performative contradiction; Smith's scattered insights need the meta-theory of value and surplus-value to be systematized. Marx also rehabilitates the Physiocrats (Mirabeau père), Wakefield, Ramsay, Dombasle, Jones, and even Fourier—an array of sources whose contributions had been obscured or misread. For the theory of capitalist development, the chapter opens onto the question of the long-run trajectory of rent. Since absolute rent depends on the relative composition of agricultural versus industrial capital, the historical evolution of the two sectors determines absolute rent's fate. Marx seems to expect a decline of absolute rent over the long run, but the qualification about relative rates of compositional change opens the door to a range of historically concrete outcomes. In some places, advanced agriculture has reduced agricultural composition relative to industry; in others, agricultural subsidies and land monopolies have preserved rent-like exactions. The theory provides resources for analyzing both, not a fixed prediction. For extractive industry, the chapter makes a striking claim: because raw material disappears as an element of constant capital, extractive industries have the lowest composition and therefore the highest theoretical potential for absolute rent. This has enormous implications for natural-resource economics: mining rents, timber rents, fishery rents, and quarry rents are not merely monopolistic exactions (though they may appear so) but are structurally related to the value–price-of-production gap. The very sectors that look most like cases of \"pure monopoly\" are those where Marx's theory of value delivers its most surprising conclusions. This also anticipates later debates about resource rents, sovereign wealth funds, and taxation of extractive industries—though of course Marx did not develop those applications himself. For methodological questions about abstraction, the chapter offers a model of how to move between levels of analysis. Marx begins with the abstract formula of differential rent, secures it against empirical counterexamples, then progressively introduces the categories of landed property, barrier, equalization, composition, value, and price of production. The empirical cases (Norwegian forests, English enclosure, prairie states, French light soils, stock-raising) are not merely illustrations but test cases that refine the theory. And the chapter's persistent reference to what is \"accidental,\" \"special,\" or \"normal\" reflects a rigorous attention to the scope conditions of each proposition. Philosophically, the chapter also contains a remarkable insight into the relationship between power and price . The landowner's power does not consist in the ability to charge any arbitrary price; it consists in the ability to withhold , to set conditions of access. This power does not create value—value is determined by socially necessary labour—but it can prevent the socialization of value through equalization. Rent is thus, in the most precise sense, a distributional rather than productive category: it transfers a portion of surplus-value from the capitalist class to the landowning class, while leaving the total quantum of surplus-value unchanged. Marx emphasizes this repeatedly: \"rent is simply a part of the agricultural surplus-value... its seizure by the landowner.\" The chapter therefore contributes directly to the broader theory of surplus-value distribution developed throughout Volume III, and it clarifies how social class relations mediate between the abstract determination of value and the concrete appearance of prices. In sum, Chapter 45 is one of the most theoretically sophisticated chapters in all of Marx's work. It resolves a foundational puzzle of classical rent theory, introduces a new category (absolute rent) that is neither a variant of differential rent nor a r ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 44 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 46"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 46", "title": "Chapter 46", "subtitle": "Building Site Rent. Rent in Mining. Price of Land", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 46/Chapter 46 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 4667, "read_time": "23 min read", "tags": ["Differential Rent (I & II)", "Absolute Ground-Rent & Landed Property"], "keywords": ["Building Site Rent, Mining Rent, and Price of Land", "Urban Speculation and Ground-Rent Extortion", "Capitalized Rent Formula (Price of Land = Rent / Interest Rate)", "Land as Fictitious Capital", "Expropriation of Urban Dwellers through Skyrocketing Rents"], "text": "Chapter 46 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 4,630 words ⏱️ ~23 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 46 of Capital , Volume 3, closes the long investigation of ground-rent by extending its logic beyond the agricultural field where Marx had developed it, and by dissecting the peculiar form in which landed property appears in the market: the price of land . The chapter's governing thesis is stated in its very first sentence: \"Wherever rent exists, differential rent always appears and always follows the same laws as it does in agriculture.\" The analytical objective is therefore one of generalization — to demonstrate that the mechanism in which a monopolizable natural force (a waterfall, a rich mine, a fishing ground, a well-situated building site) generates a surplus profit for the capitalist who exploits it, and which is then seized by the owner of that natural object \"in the form of rent,\" is a single law operating across all spheres of production. Three subsidiary objectives organize the chapter. First, Marx characterizes the specific features of non-agricultural rent — above all the preponderance of location in building-site differential rent, the complete passivity of the landowner who merely \"exploits advances in social development,\" and the prevalence of monopoly price and the \"most shameless exploitation of poverty.\" Second, he establishes the critical distinction between house-rent (interest plus amortization on the capital invested in a building) and land-rent proper , and shows through the testimony of the London speculative builder Edward Capps that the real object of speculation is not the houses but the ground-rent . Third, and most fundamentally, he exposes the ideological illusion of the price of land: because rent is capitalized and sold as a commodity, the buyer's claim to rent appears as the return on his equivalent rather than as a tribute on unpaid labour — a mystification Marx compares directly to the slaveowner's belief that his property in a slave is produced by purchase rather than by the institution of slavery. The chapter thus functions within the broader theoretical framework as the moment where rent's irrational form of appearance (the ratio of a money sum to a piece of land) is systematically decomposed into its rational determinants, and where the historical and moral verdict on landed property — that private property in the earth will appear \"just as absurd as the private property of one man in other men\" — is delivered. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Generalization of Differential Rent Beyond Agriculture Marx opens by asserting an identity rather than an analogy. Wherever a natural force can be monopolized and confers on the industrialist who uses it a surplus profit — the excess of the individual capitalist's profit over the general rate of profit — the person who legally owns that natural object, \"by virtue of his title to a portion of the earth,\" diverts this surplus profit from the functioning capital as rent . The examples enumerated (waterfall, rich mine, fishing ground, well-situated building site) are not decorative; they establish that rent is not a peculiarity of farming but a juridical-economic mechanism attaching to any natural monopoly. The essential causal chain is: monopoly of a natural force → superior productivity relative to competitors → surplus profit → appropriation by the title-holder → rent. Turning to building land specifically, Marx invokes Adam Smith's treatment (Book I, Chapter XI, sections 2 and 3), according to which the rent of building land is governed by agricultural rent proper as its basis. Yet Marx immediately emphasizes three differentiating characteristics. The first is the preponderant influence of location on differential rent — \"very important, for example, in the case of vineyards, and building land in big towns.\" This is a nuance that makes building-site differential rent far more volatile and socially determined than the fertility-based differential rent of farmland: two identical plots a mile apart in a growing city command radically different rents purely because of their position relative to markets, transport, and population. The second characteristic is the palpable and complete passivity of the owner , whose entire activity \"consists simply in exploiting advances in social development (particularly in the case of mines), towards which he does not contribute and in which he risks nothing, unlike the industrial capitalist.\" This is a pointed contrast: the industrial capitalist bears risk, organizes production, and advances capital; the landowner merely holds title and waits for society's collective development to raise his rent. The third characteristic is the prevalence of monopoly price \"in many cases, and particularly the most shameless exploitation of poverty,\" crystallized in Marx's famous aside that \"poverty is a more fruitful source for house-rent than the mines of Potosi were for Spain.\" Here the exploitation of the poor for housing — the fact that the necessity of shelter allows landlords to extract rents far above any cost-based calculation — is presented as a more productive engine of wealth than the legendary silver mines of colonial Spain. Landed Property, the Earth as Habitat, and the Growth of Building Rent Marx pushes the argument into a moral-historical register: the tremendous power that landed property acquires when it is combined with industrial capital in the same hands enables capital \"practically to exclude workers engaged in a struggle over wages from the very earth itself as their habitat.\" The phrase \"as their habitat\" is crucial — it identifies housing and living space, not merely farmland, as an object of class struggle. \"One section of society here demands a tribute from the other for the very right to live on the earth,\" a formulation that universalizes the rent relation to the fundamental precondition of human existence. Landed property in general, he adds, \"involves the right of the proprietors to exploit the earth's surface, the bowels of the earth, the air and thereby the maintenance and development of life.\" He then enumerates the causes of rising building rent. Population growth and its consequent \"growing need for housing\" is one factor, but \"not the only factor.\" The second is the development of fixed capital that is \"either incorporated into the earth or strikes root in it\" — industrial buildings, railways, factories, docks, all of which \"rest on it.\" This is an analytically important point: as capital fixes itself into the land, it raises the value of the land as site and foundation, converting improvements the landowner did not finance into a higher rent. Here Marx warns that \"it is impossible even with Carey's good intentions to confuse house-rent ... with rent of land pure and simple.\" The \"Carey\" reference is a nuance/counter-argument : the American economist Henry Carey had attempted to harmonize landlord and tenant interests by conflating them, but Marx insists that house-rent contains two distinct components — interest and amortization on the capital invested in the house, and land-rent proper — and that in England especially \"the landowner and the speculative builder are completely different persons,\" which makes the distinction empirically visible. Marx then isolates two elements that landed property exploits: first, \"the exploitation of the earth for the purpose of reproduction or extraction\" (agriculture, mining); second, \"the space that is required as an element for any production and any human activity.\" \"On both counts landed property demands its tribute.\" Thus the demand for building land raises the value of land as space and foundation , while simultaneously the demand grows for \"those elements of the earth's physical constitution that serve as building material\" (sand, stone, clay, timber). The Speculative Builder and the Real Object of Speculation To document this empirically, Marx refers back to Volume 2, Chapter 12, and the testimony of the major London speculative builder Edward Capps before the Bank Acts Committee of 1857 (evidence no. 5435). Capps's testimony is deployed as hard evidence: a man who wishes to rise \"can hardly expect to rise by following out a fair trade\"; he must add speculative building \"not on a small scale,\" because \"the builder makes very little profit out of the buildings themselves; he makes the principal part of the profit out of the improved ground-rents.\" Marx then reconstructs Capps's illustrative arithmetic: the builder takes a piece of ground for £300 a year ; by laying it out carefully and erecting certain descriptions of buildings, he raises the annual income to £400 or £450 ; his profit is the increased ground-rent of £100 or £150 a year , not the profit of the buildings, \"which ... in many instances, he scarcely looks at at all.\" The formula embedded here is: Speculative profit = New ground-rent − Original ground-rent = £450 − £300 = £150. This demonstrates that ground-rent, not construction, is the true object of urban speculation. Marx then adds a decisive temporal dimension: after the leasehold expires — \"ninety-nine years at the most\" — the land \"together with all the buildings on it, and with a ground-rent that has in the meantime generally doubled, tripled or more, reverts from the speculative builder or his heir to the original ultimate landowner.\" The leasehold system is thus revealed as a mechanism by which all the accumulated social development embodied in the built environment ultimately flows back to the original title-holder, who has done nothing but wait. Rent of Mines and the Marginal Mine \"Rent of mines is determined just as is agricultural rent.\" Marx supports this with a long quotation from Adam Smith concerning coal mines \"of which the produce is barely sufficient to pay the labour, and replace, together with its ordinary profits, the stock employed in working them.\" Such mines \"afford some profit to the undertaker of the work, but no rent to the landlord.\" They can be worked advantageously \"by nobody but the landlord, who, being himself undertaker of the work, gets the ordinary profit of the capital which he employs in it. Many coalmines in Scotland are wrought in this manner.\" This is the mining analogue of no-rent land at the margin : where a mine yields only average profit, no surplus exists to be converted into rent, and the landlord must operate it himself if it is to be worked at all — because any rent charged would push the operator below the ordinary profit rate and make the venture unviable. Monopoly Price: Does It Create Rent, or Does Rent Create It? Marx then introduces a crucial conceptual distinction. \"It is necessary to distinguish whether the rent flows from an independent monopoly price for the products or the land itself, or whether the products are sold at a monopoly price because there is a rent.\" Definition of monopoly price: \"any price determined simply by the desire and ability of the buyer to pay, independently of the price of the product as determined by price of production and value.\" This is a demand-determined price, unmoored from the labour-value and cost-of-production anchors. Case one — monopoly price creates rent. A vineyard producing wine of exceptional quality \"but ... only in a relatively small quantity\" bears a monopoly price. The wine-grower realizes a \"substantial surplus profit\" determined \"purely and simply by the wealth and the preference of fashionable wine-drinkers.\" This surplus profit, \"which in this case flows from a monopoly price, is transformed into rent and accrues in this form to the landowner by virtue of his title to the portion of the earth endowed with these special properties.\" Here the causal arrow runs: scarcity of a natural endowment → monopoly price → surplus profit → rent. Case two — rent creates the monopoly price. Conversely, \"the rent would create the monopoly price if corn were sold not only above its price of production but also above its value, as a result of the barrier that landed property opposes against the rent-free investment of capital on untilled land.\" This is the mechanism of absolute rent : landed property as a barrier prevents capital from being invested rent-free, so the price must rise above value to cover the rent, and the price is thereby set by the rent. Capitalized Rent, the Price of Land, and the Slave Analogy Marx now arrives at the chapter's central ideological critique. The fact \"that it is only the title a number of people have to property in the earth that enables them to appropriate a part of society's surplus labour as tribute\" is concealed by the fact that \"the capitalized rent, i.e. precisely this capitalized tribute, appears as the price of land, which can be bought and sold just like any other item of trade.\" Capitalization means dividing the annual rent by the interest rate (in the simplest case, price of land = rent ÷ rate of interest ), converting a perpetual tribute into a capital sum. For the buyer, therefore, his claim to rent \"does not appear as something obtained for nothing, without labour, risk or the entrepreneurial spirit of capital, but rather as the return for his equivalent.\" Rent appears to him \"simply [as] interest on the capital with which he has purchased the land.\" Marx then deploys his most striking analogy: \"In exactly the same way, it appears to the slaveowner who has bought a Negro slave that his property in the Negro is created not by the institution of slavery as such but rather by the purchase and sale of this commodity. But the purchase does not produce the title; it simply transfers it. The title must be there before it can be bought.\" No sale, nor any series of sales, \"can create this title.\" The title was \"entirely created by the relations of production.\" This is the analytical core: exchange conceals appropriation . The market transaction of buying land appears to legitimize rent, but the legitimacy is borrowed from pre-existing property relations rooted in the social process of production. And then the historical verdict: once these relations \"have reached the point where they have to be sloughed off,\" the material and economically justified source of the title disappears, \"and with it all transactions based on it.\" \"From the standpoint of a higher socio-economic formation, the private property of particular individuals in the earth will appear just as absurd as the private property of one man in other men.\" Even an entire society, or all societies taken together, \"are not the owners of the earth. They are simply its possessors, its beneficiaries, and have to bequeath it in an improved state to succeeding generations, as boni patresfamilias \" (good heads of the household) — a phrase invoking the Roman legal ideal of the conscientious steward. A footnote establishes scope conditions for what follows: in analyzing the price of land, Marx sets aside \"all fluctuations due to competition, all speculation in land, and even petty landownership where the earth forms the major instrument of the producers.\" The Determinants of the Price of Land, I: Rise Without Rent Increase Marx organizes the analysis into numbered cases. Case I: the price of land may rise without an increase in rent. Two sub-mechanisms are given. (1) A fall in the rate of interest , \"which means that rent is sold more dearly, and so capitalized rent, the price of land, increases.\" This follows directly from the capitalization formula: if rent R is constant and the interest rate i falls, the capitalized price P = R / i rises. (2) \"A growth in the interest on the capital incorporated into the land\" — i.e., interest attributable to improvements or fixed capital sunk into the land, which is itself a component of the price paid and can rise independently of pure rent. The Determinants of the Price of Land, II: Rise Because Rent Rises Case II: the price of land may rise because the rent increases. This occurs when \"the price of the product of the land rises, in which case the rate of differential rent always rises.\" Marx here supplies a precise definition : \"By the rate of differential rent we mean the ratio between the part of surplus-value that is transformed into rent, and the capital advanced to produce the agricultural product.\" He explicitly distinguishes this from the ratio between surplus product and total product , because \"the total product does not include all the capital advanced, i.e. it does not include the fixed capital, which continues to exist alongside the product.\" The implication: on lands bearing differential rent, \"a growing portion of the product is transformed into excess surplus product.\" On the worst land , \"it is the rise in price of the product of the land that creates rent for the first time and hence creates the price of land\" — the marginal-land mechanism, where previously no differential surplus existed. Rent Growth With Constant or Falling Product Price Marx then demonstrates that rent can grow \"without any rise in the price of the agricultural product,\" which \"can remain constant or even decline.\" If the price is constant, rent may grow because new lands of better quality are cultivated alongside equal-sized additional capital investments on older lands, \"sufficient only to meet the increased demand, so that the governing market price remains unchanged.\" In this case, \"the price of the older lands does not rise, but the price of land newly taken up rises above that of the old.\" Alternatively, rent rises \"because with the relative yield remaining the same, and the market price too, the amount of capital exploiting the land grows.\" The arithmetic is given: \"even if the rent remains the same in relation to the capital advanced, it might double in amount, say, because the capital itself has doubled.\" Since the price has not fallen, \"the second capital investment yields a surplus profit just as much as the first, which is similarly transformed into rent once the term of the tenancy expires.\" The underlying equations: If capital C yields rent x at rate r = x / C , then doubling capital to 2C yields rent 2x with r unchanged. Expressed per acre: \"The same acre that previously brought in £2 rent now brings in £4.\" The Polemic Against the Uneven-Yield Doctrine, and the Rodbertus Critique Marx here attacks a rival contention: that successive capital investments on the same land can produce rent \"only in so far as their yield is uneven, and hence a differential rent arises.\" He reduces this to absurdity: it would imply that \"if two capitals of £1,000 each are invested on two fields of equal productivity only one of them can yield rent, even when these two fields belong to the better class of land which does yield a differential rent.\" The fallacy lies in treating investment across different pieces of land under a different law from successive investment on the same piece of land , \"although we have precisely derived differential rent from an identical law in both cases, from the growth in productivity of capital investment both on the same field and on different fields.\" The overlooked modification is decisive: \"when successive capital investments are applied to land in different locations, they come up against the barrier of landed property, which is not the case with successive capital investments on the same land. This is the reason for the opposing tendencies by which these different forms of investment in practice set barriers to one another.\" With constant capital composition and rate of surplus-value, the rate of profit is unaltered, so twice the capital yields twice the profit and twice the rent. Additionally, \"the rental — the total rent of a country — grows with the amount of capital invested without a necessary rise in the price of the individual unit of land,\" even alongside \"a fall in the rent on individual holdings.\" A substantial footnote credits Rodbertus with having developed this point, but levels two errors: first, Rodbertus \"sees the growth in profit as always expressing a growth in capital, so that the ratio remains the same as the mass of profit rises,\" which Marx calls wrong because \"the profit rate may still rise as the composition of capital changes, through a fall in the proportionate value of the constant part of capital as compared with the variable.\" Second, Rodbertus wrongly treats \"the proportion of money rent on a piece of land of a definite size, 1 acre for example,\" as if this \"had been the general premise of classical economics.\" Marx corrects: classical economics \"always treated the rate of rent ... in relation to the product\" for natural-form rent, and \"in relation to the capital advanced\" for money rent, \"since these are in fact the rational expressions.\" The Irrational Form of Appearance: Rent per Square Foot Marx now confronts the absurdity directly: \"The proportion of one part of the surplus-value, the money rent ... to the land is as it stands absurd and irrational; for it is incommensurable quantities that are measured against one another\" — a use-value (square feet of land) against a value (surplus-value). Its real meaning: \"the ownership of these square feet of land enables the landowner to seize a certain amount of unpaid labour, which capital has realized by rooting in the soil like a pig in potatoes.\" He likens the surface expression to \"the ratio of a £5 note to the diameter of the earth.\" Yet \"these irrational forms ... do not bother the practical bearers of these relationships in their everyday dealings; since they are accustomed to operating within these forms, it does not strike them as anything worth thinking about.\" In estranged forms of appearance, people feel \"as much at home as a fish in water.\" He invokes Hegel : \"what the common human understanding finds irrational is in fact rational, and what it finds rational is irrational\" (Encyclopaedia, Part One, para. 231). Because a rise in the amount of rent is expressed identically to a rise in the rate of rent, \"hence the embarrassment when the conditions that would explain the one case are absent in the other.\" Price of Land Rising While Product Price Falls Marx then argues the price of land \"can rise even if the price of its product declines.\" Either differential rent may have increased \"by a further differentiation, and with it the price of the better lands,\" or increased labour productivity lowers the product price while increased production more than compensates. He supplies a numerical example : assume 1 quarter costs 60s . If 2 quarters are produced on the same acre with the same capital instead of 1, and the cost falls to 40s. , then 2 quarters fetch 80s. , \"so that the value of the product of the same capital on the same acre would have risen by a third, even though the price per qr had fallen by a third.\" The algebra: Price per unit: 60s → 40s (a fall of 1/3). Total value: 1 × 60s = 60s → 2 × 40s = 80s (a rise of 1/3). He notes this is possible \"in only two ways\": either poor land is withdrawn from competition while the price of better land rises (uneven effect of general improvement), or \"the same price of production on the worst land ... is expressed in a larger amount of product, on account of increased labour productivity,\" so that while the product \"still represents the same value as before,\" the price of its aliquot parts has fallen while \"their number has increased.\" Critically: \"If the same capital is applied, this is impossible; for in that case the same value is always expressed in any portion of the product.\" It becomes possible only when extra capital is invested \"for gypsum, guano, etc., i.e. for improvements whose effects extend over several years,\" provided \"the price of the individual quarter, even though it falls, does not fall in the same ratio as the number of quarters grows.\" Case III: Mutual Interference of Conditions Case III summarizes: these conditions \"may partly compete with one another, partly exclude one another, and may only take effect in alternation.\" The decisive conclusions: \"a rise in the price of land does not necessarily mean a rise in rent, and ... a rise in rent, which always brings with it a rise in the price of land, does not invariably mean an increase in its products.\" A footnote (citing Passy) references an actual case of falling land prices combined with rising rent. The Earth as Instrument of Production vs. the Machine as Depreciating Capital The chapter closes with a substantive theoretical argument against the Edinburgh Review , which, countering Richard Jones , had invoked the superficial notion that \"there is a limit to the amount of capital which can be invested in a spatially limited field\" — the reductio that \"the whole of England cannot be fed by cultivating Soho Square.\" Marx responds that if this is seen as \"a particular disadvantage of agriculture, precisely the opposite is the case.\" Successive capital investments can bear fruit \"just because the earth itself functions as an instrument of production, which is not the case with a factory, where it functions only as the foundation, the site, the spatial basis of operations.\" In a factory, space has practical limits — \"building upwards also has its definite practical limits\" — and beyond them expansion must go outward. Crucially, \"the fixed capital invested in machines, etc. is not improved by use; on the contrary, it depreciates,\" and when productivity develops rapidly, \"the whole of the old machinery must be replaced by a more advantageous kind, and it is therefore lost.\" The earth, by contrast, \"continuously improves, as long as it is treated correctly.\" The final sentence states the double-edged conclusion: \"The advantage of the earth, that successive capital investments can have their benefit without the earlier ones being lost, at the same time implies the possibility of a difference in yield between these successive capital investments.\" 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The fundamental principle revealed by this chapter is that rent is a juridical claim on surplus value, not a productive contribution , and that it arises wherever a natural force can be monopolized. Differential rent, absolute rent, building rent, and mine rent are not separate species but one mechanism — the diversion of surplus profit to a title-holder — differentiated only by the natural or social basis of the monopoly and by the market form (competition vs. monopoly price) through which it is realized. A second systemic law is the asymmetry between appearance and essence in the price of land. Capitalized rent presents a tribute as an equivalent, an appropriation as an investment, a pre-existing title as a purchase. Marx's slave analogy and his analysis of the irrational ratio of money to square footage both demonstrate that economic categories routinely invert their own foundations — and that the practical actors of the system, \"like fish in water,\" do not find this contradictory. This is a general lesson about ideology: the everyday forms of economic life have a self-justifying grammar. The third law is the asymmetry between land and machinery as recipients of capital . Because the earth improves with correct treatment while fixed capital depreciates, agriculture uniquely permits successive investments to accumulate benefit — which also makes it uniquely susceptible to differential yields and hence to rent. The Edinburgh Review's objection is thus inverted: what is treated as agriculture's limit is in fact its peculiar advantage. The broader implications are both practical and historical. Practically, the chapter explains urban housing crises, leasehold reversion, land speculation, and the structural passivity of the rentier as built-in features of landed property under capitalism. Theoretically, it completes Marx's demolition of the view that rent compensates a productive service. Philosophically, it delivers the most radical claim in the volume: that the appropriation of the earth by private individuals will one day appear \"just as absurd as the private property of one man in other men,\" and that even all nations together are merely \"possessors and beneficiaries\" of the earth, bound to hand it on improved — the bonus paterfamilias of humanity. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 45 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 47"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 47", "title": "Chapter 47", "subtitle": "Genesis of Capitalist Ground-Rent", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 47/Chapter 47 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 18978, "read_time": "95 min read", "tags": ["Differential Rent (I & II)", "Absolute Ground-Rent & Landed Property", "Primitive Accumulation & Enclosures"], "keywords": ["Genesis of Capitalist Ground-Rent", "Labour Rent (Corvée) to Rent in Kind to Money Rent", "Peasant Proprietorship of Land and Mortgages", "Expropriation of Peasantry and Capitalist Transformation", "Smallholding Agriculture Subjection to Usury and Capital"], "text": "Chapter 47 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 18,855 words ⏱️ ~94 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 47: The Genesis of Capitalist Ground-Rent A Unified Exhaustive Deconstruction and Analysis 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem and Intellectual Objective Chapter 47 of Marx's Capital , Volume 3, stands as one of the most historically sweeping and theoretically ambitious chapters in the entire work. Its title — \"The Genesis of Capitalist Ground-Rent\" — announces a dual ambition: not merely to explain what capitalist ground-rent is , but to trace the historical process by which it came to be, passing through a series of pre-capitalist forms before arriving at its mature, capitalist expression. The chapter is thus simultaneously a work of economic theory and of historical sociology, an analysis of a specific category of surplus-value and a genealogy of the social relations that produced it. The central problem Marx identifies is deceptively simple to state but extraordinarily difficult to resolve: how, after all surplus-value has apparently been distributed among the various capitals in proportion to their size through the equalization process that forms the general rate of profit, can there still remain an excess — an excess part of surplus-value that capital invested in land pays to the landowner as ground-rent? This is the \"difficulty\" that modern economics, as \"the theoretical expression of the capitalist mode of production,\" has repeatedly failed to grasp. Each new attempt at a \"new\" explanation of ground-rent is, Marx notes with characteristic irony, almost invariably a regression to a standpoint long superseded. The difficulty is not in explaining surplus product or surplus-value as such — that is solved by the general analysis of surplus-value produced by any productive capital. The difficulty is in showing how, after the equalization of surplus-value between capitals to give average profit, after all surplus-value has apparently been distributed, there is still an excess left over that flows to the landowner. This is a problem of extraordinary theoretical weight. If one concedes that rent for capital invested in agriculture stems from the special properties of the earth's crust or certain natural endowments, then — Marx argues — one renounces the very concept of value itself, abandoning any possibility of scientific understanding in this area. The whole difficulty consists, therefore, in explaining the excess of agricultural profit over average profit: not surplus-value as such, but the extra surplus-value specific to this sphere; not even the \"net product,\" but the extra net product over and above the net product of other branches of industry. The average profit itself, Marx emphasizes, is a product formed by a process of social life proceeding under quite particular historical relations of production — a product that presupposes very elaborate mediations. If we are to speak of an excess over average profit, this average profit must first be established as a measure and, in the capitalist mode of production, as the overall regulator of production. Thus in forms of society where capital has not yet subsumed society's labour, or has done so only sporadically, there can be no question at all of rent in the modern sense — rent as an excess over and above average profit, over and above the proportionate share of each individual capital in the total surplus-value that total capital produces. The Broader Theoretic Framework This chapter belongs to Volume 3, Part 6 of Capital , the section on the \"Transformation of Surplus Profit into Ground-Rent.\" It follows the analysis of the transformation of surplus-value into profit, the formation of the general rate of profit, the law of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall, and the analysis of commercial and interest-bearing capital. Ground-rent is the final major form of surplus-value to be analyzed, and its analysis completes Marx's theory of distribution under capitalism. The chapter's significance extends far beyond the technical question of rent. It is the site where Marx most fully develops his theory of pre-capitalist modes of production, of the relationship between economic form and social domination, of the state as \"supreme landlord\" in Asiatic societies, of the historical conditions for the emergence of the capitalist farmer, and ultimately of the contradiction between private property in land and rational agriculture. The chapter crescendos with Marx's famous pronouncements on the \"irreparable rift in the interdependent process of social metabolism\" and the squandering of the vitality of the soil — themes that have made it a foundational text for ecological and agrarian political economy. The intellectual objective, then, is triple: (1) to demonstrate that ground-rent is not a price of land or a natural productivity of the soil but a form of surplus-value arising from specific relations of production; (2) to trace the historical dialectic through which ground-rent passes from its primitive identity with surplus-labour to its mature form as an excess over average profit; and (3) to expose the philosophical and practical contradiction between landed property and sustainable agriculture. The chapter is a bridge between Marx's theory of value and his historical materialist understanding of social development — a deep, permanent, and rigorous demonstration that economic categories are historically specific social relations, not eternal natural facts. The four-stage genealogy the chapter traces is: Labour Rent → Rent in Kind → Money Rent → Capitalist Rent , with share-cropping and small-peasant ownership appearing as transitional and parallel forms. Through this genealogy, Marx shows that rent is not a fixed natural entity but a historical category that changes its meaning, its social function, and its quantitative determination as the mode of production changes. The identity of surplus-value and surplus-labour, visible to the naked eye in labour rent, becomes increasingly mediated and obscured until, under capitalist rent, it is separated into profit and an excess above profit whose origin is invisible to the casual observer and whose explanation requires the full machinery of the theory of value. With this overarching map in mind, let us now walk through the chapter in its full textual sequence, unpacking each conceptual beat as it unfolds. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 — Introduction: The Exact Nature of the Difficulty 2.1.1 The Problem Stated Marx opens the chapter by insisting on the necessity of clarifying \"the exact nature of the difficulty faced by modern economics\" in treating ground-rent. This difficulty, he says, \"has still not been understood even by the large number of more recent writers,\" which is shown by each new attempt to give ground-rent a \"new\" explanation. The novelty, in these attempts, consists \"almost invariably in regression to a standpoint long superseded.\" This is a crucial methodological point: for Marx, theoretical progress consists in grasping the historically specific and mediated character of economic categories. To regress to an earlier standpoint — for instance, to explain rent by natural fertility — is not to advance but to retreat from scientific understanding. The \"new\" explanations of ground-rent current in Marx's time (and, we may note, in our own) are recapitulations of primitive errors, not discoveries. The difficulty, Marx says, is not one of explaining the surplus product produced by agricultural capital and the corresponding surplus-value. That question is solved by the analysis of surplus-value all productive capital produces, whatever the sphere. The difficulty consists rather in showing how, after the equalization of surplus-value between the various capitals to give the average profit — whereby they receive a share in the total surplus-value produced by the social capital in all spheres of production together, a share corresponding and proportionate to their relative sizes — after this equalization, after the distribution of all the surplus-value that there is to distribute has apparently already taken place, there is still an excess part of this surplus-value left over, a part which capital invested on the land pays to the landowner in the form of ground-rent. It must derive from somewhere. This is the theoretical puzzle in its sharpest form: if the general rate of profit has equalized all surplus-value across all spheres, then agriculture should yield nothing extra — and yet rent is empirically paid. Where does it come from? The answer, which Marx will develop across the chapter and the following chapters, is that agriculture has a special characteristic — a lower organic composition of capital and, crucially, the existence of landed property as a barrier to the free investment of capital — which allows it to retain a surplus above average profit. But the genesis of that situation requires the historical account that this chapter provides. 2.1.2 Practical Motives and Theoretical Stakes Marx notes that quite apart from the practical motives which goaded the modern economist to investigate this question — as spokesman for industrial capital against landed property — the question was of decisive interest to them as theorists. The practical motive: the industrial capitalist wants to minimize the tribute he pays to the landowner, and so has an interest in understanding rent as something that ought to be abolished or reduced. But the theoretical interest is even deeper. To concede that the phenomenon of rent for capital invested in agriculture stemmed from a particular effect of the sphere of investment itself, from the earth's crust or certain properties pertaining to it, would be to renounce the very concept of value itself — to abandon any possibility of scientific understanding in this area. This is the essential thesis. If rent is a natural productivity effect, then value is not socially determined labour-time but is infected with natural-physical causation, and the entire theory of value collapses. The concept of value requires that all surplus-value be explainable by the expenditure of socially necessary labour-time. Any \"natural\" residual in rent is thus an ideological threat to the scientific core of political economy. Marx reinforces this with an empirical observation: the simple perception that rent is paid out of the price of the agricultural product — which is true even when it is paid in kind, if the farmer is to extract his price of production — showed the absurdity of explaining the excess of this price over and above the customary price of production, i.e. the relative expensiveness of agricultural products, in terms of the extra natural productivity of agricultural industry over the productivity of other branches of industry. For on the contrary, the more productive labour is, the cheaper each aliquot part of its product , since the greater the amount of use-value in which the same quantity of labour, i.e. the same value, is represented. This is a crucial inversion of the common-sense view. High productivity means cheaper products, not more expensive ones. So if agricultural products are relatively expensive (which they must be if they yield a rent), this cannot be because agriculture is more naturally productive than industry. It must be because of some social relation — landed property — that allows agriculture to sell above its price of production. The natural-productivity explanation is self-contradictory. 2.1.3 The Average Profit as a Historical Product The whole difficulty in analyzing rent thus consisted in explaining the excess of agricultural profit over average profit; not surplus-value as such, but rather the extra surplus-value specific to this sphere of production; not even the \"net product,\" but rather the extra net product over and above the net product of other branches of industry. The average profit itself, Marx stresses, is a product formed by a process of social life proceeding under quite particular historical relations of production — a product which presupposes very elaborate mediations. If we are to speak of an excess over the average profit, this average profit must first be established as a measure and, as is the case in the capitalist mode of production, as the overall regulator of production. This is a methodologically decisive claim: the average profit is not a natural datum but a historical achievement. It presupposes developed competition, mobility of capital, a fully formed labour market, and the subsumption of all production under the capitalist mode. Thus in forms of society where it is not yet capital that performs this function of extracting all surplus labour and appropriating it for itself — i.e. where capital has not yet subsumed society's labour or has done so only sporadically — there can be no question at all of rent in the modern sense, of rent as an excess over and above the average profit. 2.1.4 The Naivety of M. Passy Marx illustrates this with the example of M. Passy, whose naivety shows when he speaks of rent in the most primitive conditions as already a surplus over and above profit — over a historically determined social form of surplus-value which, according to M. Passy, can exist \"quite nicely without any society at all.\" This is a reductio ad absurdum: to treat profit as a universal category present in the most primitive conditions is to project capitalist categories onto pre-capitalist societies, thereby abolishing the specificity of those categories — and, incidentally, to make the historical emergence of capitalism unintelligible. 2.1.5 The Historical Review: Petty, Cantillon, and the Physiocrats Marx then turns to a historical review of earlier writers to show that their errors were conditioned by the historical development of their time, and that their apparent \"naivety\" was often a consequence of the fact that capitalist categories had not yet fully crystallized. Petty and Cantillon : For the early economists who were only just beginning to analyze the capitalist mode of production — in their time still undeveloped — the analysis of rent presented either no difficulty at all or else a difficulty of a quite different kind. Petty, Cantillon and all those writers who stand closer to the feudal period assume that ground-rent is the normal form of surplus-value , while profit for them is still lumped indiscriminately together with wages or at most appears as a portion of this surplus-value extorted from the landowner by the capitalist. They therefore base themselves on a state of affairs in which, firstly, the agricultural population are still the overwhelming majority of the nation, and secondly, the landowner still appears as the person who appropriates in the first instance the excess labour of the immediate producers, by way of his monopoly of landed property. Landed property thus still appears as the chief condition of production. They could not yet imagine the problem of investigating, from the standpoint of the capitalist mode of production, how landed property manages to extract again from capital one part of the surplus-value that this has produced (i.e. extorted from the immediate producers) and in the first instance already appropriated. This is a profound point: the very question of rent as an excess over profit could not arise for Petty because profit itself was not yet clearly distinguished as a separate category. The historical development of the categories precedes and conditions the theoretical articulation of the questions. Marx footnotes that he regarded Sir William Petty (1623–87) as the founder of the \"classical\" political economy which, for all its limitations, did \"investigate the real internal framework of bourgeois relations of production,\" as opposed to the mere apologetics of the \"vulgar economists.\" The specific reference is to Petty's A Treatise of Taxes and Contributions (London, 1667, pp. 23–4), and Marx refers the reader to Theories of Surplus-Value , Part I, pp. 176–7 and 344 ff. For Cantillon, Marx notes that Richard Cantillon (1680–1734) was in fact an English economist and merchant, despite his book ( Essai sur la nature du commerce en général , Amsterdam, 1756) being published in Holland in French. The Physiocrats : With the Physiocrats, the difficulty is of quite another kind. As the first systematic interpreters of capital, they tried to analyze the nature of surplus-value in general. For them this analysis coincided with the analysis of rent, the only form in which surplus-value existed for them. Rent-bearing or agricultural capital, therefore, is for them the only capital that produces surplus-value, and the agricultural labour that it sets in motion is the only labour giving rise to surplus-value, i.e., from the capitalist standpoint, the only truly productive labour. For them, the production of surplus-value is, quite correctly, the determinate element. This is a dialectical appreciation: the Physiocrats were correct in making surplus-value the central question, even though they located it exclusively in agriculture. Marx credits them with the great merit of returning from commercial capital, which operates only in the circulation sphere, to productive capital — in contrast to the mercantilists, who \"in their crude realism form the true vulgar economists of their day\" and whose practical self-interest \"pressed the beginnings of scientific analysis by Petty and his school right into the background.\" Marx adds a note on the Mercantile System, distinguishing the Monetary System (which correctly proclaims that production for the world market and the transformation of the product into a commodity, hence into money, is the precondition and requirement for capitalist production) from the Mercantile System proper (where it is no longer the transformation of commodity value into money that is decisive, but instead the production of surplus-value — albeit from the irrational standpoint of the circulation sphere, and expressed in surplus money, in a favourable balance of trade). Then Marx makes an important historical-sociological point: it is characteristic of the self-interested merchants and manufacturers of that time, and belongs to the period of capitalist development they represent, that the transformation of feudal agricultural societies into industrial societies, and the resulting industrial struggle of nations on the world market, involves an accelerated development of capital which cannot be attained in the so-called natural way but only by compulsion. It makes a substantial difference whether national capital is transformed into industrial capital gradually and slowly, or whether this transformation is accelerated in time by the taxes they impose via protective duties, principally on the landowners, small and middle peasants and artisans; by the accelerated expropriation of independent direct producers; by the forcibly accelerated accumulation and concentration of capital — in short, by the accelerated production of the conditions of the capitalist mode of production. The national character of the Mercantile System is therefore not a mere slogan: under the pretext of being concerned only with the wealth of the nation and the sources of assistance for the state, they actually declare that the interests of the capitalist class, and enrichment in general, are the final purpose of the state, and proclaim bourgeois society as against the old supernatural state. They show their awareness that the development of the interests of capital and the capitalist class, of capitalist production, has become the basis of a nation's power and predominance in modern society. 2.1.6 The Physiocrats' Correct Foundation Marx then grants a profound truth to the Physiocrats: they were correct in seeing all production of surplus-value, and thus also every development of capital, as resting on the productivity of agricultural labour as its natural foundation. If men are not even capable of producing more means of subsistence in a working day, and thus in the narrowest sense more agricultural products, than each worker needs for his own reproduction — if the daily expenditure of the worker's entire labour-power is only sufficient to produce the means of subsistence indispensable for his individual needs — there can be no question of any surplus product or surplus-value at all. A level of productivity of agricultural labour which goes beyond the individual needs of the worker is the basis of all society , and in particular the basis of capitalist production, which releases an ever growing part of society from the direct production of means of subsistence, transforming them, as Steuart says, into \"free hands\" and making them available for exploitation in other spheres. Marx footnotes that Sir James Steuart (1712–80) was the last representative of the mercantilist school, and his work ( An Inquiry into the Principles of Political Economy , Vol. 1, Dublin, 1770, p. 396) already represents a transition towards the classical bourgeois analysis of capitalist production by Adam Smith. It is with a short chapter on Steuart, therefore, that Marx opens Theories of Surplus-Value . 2.1.7 The Degeneration of Later Writers: Daire and Passy Marx then turns with characteristic ferocity on the \"more recent economic writers such as Daire, Passy, etc.\" who, \"in the twilight years of classical economics, when it is actually on its deathbed,\" repeat the most primitive ideas about the natural conditions of surplus labour and hence surplus-value in general, believing themselves to have said something new and striking about ground-rent, long after this ground-rent has been explained as a particular form and specific portion of surplus-value. It is precisely characteristic of vulgar economics that what in a now superseded stage of development was new, original, profound and justified, it repeats at a time when this is \"flat, stale and incorrect.\" It thereby acknowledges that it does not even have an inkling of the problems which classical economics was concerned with. It confuses these with questions that are posed only at a lower standpoint of the development of bourgeois society. The same applies to its \"incessant and self-satisfied rumination of the Physiocratic ideas on free trade,\" which \"have long since lost any and every theoretical interest, even if they may still be of some practical interest to some state or other.\" Marx footnotes Eugene Daire (1798–1847), scarcely significant as an original writer but with the merit of having edited the collection of Physiocratic writings ( Physiocrates , Vol. 1, Paris, 1846) which Marx made frequent use of in Capital ; and H.-P. Passy (op. cit., p. 511). 2.1.8 The Natural Economy and the Separation of Agriculture from Manufacture Marx then embarks on a historical digression essential to understanding the preconditions of capitalist rent. In a genuine natural economy , where no part of the agricultural product, or only a very small part, is involved in circulation, and this is itself only a relatively insignificant part of that portion of the product that represents the landowner's revenue — as for example in many ancient Roman latifundia, the villas of Charlemagne's time, and more or less throughout the Middle Ages (see Vinçard, Histoire du travail ) — the product and surplus product of the great estates by no means consisted simply of the products of agricultural work. It equally included the products of industrial work. The existence of domestic handicrafts and manufacture as an ancillary pursuit to agriculture , which forms the basis, is the condition for the mode of production on which this natural economy is based, in European antiquity and the Middle Ages, as still today in the Indian village communities, where the traditional organization has not yet been destroyed. The capitalist mode of production completely abolishes this connection — a process which can be studied on a large scale particularly during the last third of the eighteenth century in England. People who had grown up in more or less semi-feudal societies, such as Herrenschwand, for example, still considered this separation of agriculture and manufacture as a \"foolhardy social venture,\" an \"incomprehensibly risky mode of existence,\" at the end of the eighteenth century. Marx footnotes that Pierre-Denis Vinçard (1820–82) was a French writer of working-class origin who took part in the 1848 revolution and was active in the trade-union movement, being also a member of the International Working Men's Association; and that Jean Herrenschwand (1728–1812) was a Swiss economist. Even in those agricultural economies of ancient times which show most analogy with the capitalist rural economy — Carthage and Rome — the similarity is more with a plantation economy than with the form truly corresponding to the capitalist mode of exploitation. A formal analogy, though one which proves to be completely deceptive in all essential points as soon as the capitalist mode of production is understood — \"even if not for Herr Mommsen, who discovers the capitalist mode of production in every monetary economy\" — such a formal analogy is to be found nowhere in mainland Italy in ancient times, but only perhaps in Sicily, since this served as an agricultural tributary for Rome, its agriculture being essentially designed for export. Here one can find farmers in the modern sense. Marx footnote 42a quotes Adam Smith emphasizing how in his time (and this is still true for our own, as far as the plantation economy in tropical and sub-tropical countries is concerned) rent and profit are still not always separate, since the landowner is also the capitalist, as Cato for instance was on his estates ( Wealth of Nations , Pelican edition, p. 156). This separation, however, is precisely the precondition for the capitalist mode of production, the basis of slavery similarly standing in invariable contradiction with the concept of this mode. Footnote 43 criticizes Mommsen for using the word \"capitalist\" in his Römische Geschichte in no way in the sense of modern economics and modern society, but rather in the manner of a popular idea persisting on the Continent — though not in England or America — as an outdated tradition from past conditions. 2.1.9 The Misconception of Rent in Kind An incorrect conception of the nature of rent has been handed down to modern times, a conception based on the fact that rent in kind still survives from the Middle Ages , in complete contradiction to the conditions of the capitalist mode of production, partly in the tithes paid to the Church and partly as a curiosity in old contracts. The impression is thus given that rent arises not from the price of the agricultural product but rather from its quantity — i.e. not from social relations but from the earth itself. Marx then restates a crucial theoretical point from earlier in Capital : even though surplus-value is expressed in a surplus product, it is not true conversely that any surplus product in the sense of a mere increase in the quantity of the product represents a surplus-value. It can represent a deduction from value . Otherwise the cotton industry would have had to show an enormous surplus-value in 1860 compared with 1840, even though the price of yarn had fallen. Rent may grow enormously as the result of a series of bad harvests, since the price of corn rises, even though this surplus-value is expressed in a smaller amount of dearer wheat. Conversely, a series of good years may lead to a fall in rent because the price falls, even though the lower rent is expressed in a greater amount of cheaper wheat. The first thing to note about rent in kind, then, is that it is simply a tradition handed down from a mode of production which has outlived its day , surviving as the ruin of its former existence, while its contradiction to the capitalist mode of production is shown by the way that it disappeared automatically from private contracts and, where legislation could intervene, as with the tithes in England, was forcibly dispensed with as an incongruity. (Marx's footnote refers to the Tithe Commutation Acts passed between 1836 and 1860, whereby Church tithes were commuted from services in kind into money payments.) Secondly, where rent in kind continued to exist on the basis of the capitalist mode of production, it was nothing more, and could be nothing more, than an expression of money rent in medieval guise. Marx illustrates this with a detailed worked example: Say that wheat stands at 40s. per quarter. Out of this one quarter, one part must replace the wages contained in it and be sold so as to advance these again; another part must be sold in order to pay the part due as taxes. Seed corn and a proportion of fertilizer are themselves involved in reproduction as commodities, wherever the capitalist mode of production and the division of social labour associated with it are developed, and so replacement for these must be bought; a further part of the quarter must be sold to supply money for these. In as much as they do not actually have to be bought as commodities, but are taken from the product in kind, to go once more into its reproduction as conditions of production — which happens not only in agriculture, but also in many branches of production that produce constant capital — they are put down on the books in money of account, and are deducted as components of the cost price. The wear and tear of machinery and fixed capital in general must be replaced in money. Finally there is the profit, which is reckoned on the sum of those expenses that are expressed in real money or in money of account. This profit is represented by a particular part of the gross product, determined by its price. The part that then remains forms the rent. If the contractual product-rent is greater than this residue as determined by the price, it does not form rent but is a deduction from profit. Simply by virtue of this possibility, product-rent is an antiquated and obsolete form, as it does not follow the price of the product and can therefore come to more or less than the actual rent, involving not only a deduction from profit, but also from components required to replace the capital. This product-rent, in fact, as far as it is rent not simply in name but in actual fact, is determined exclusively by the excess of the price of the product over its cost of production . It simply takes this variable magnitude as a constant one. 2.1.10 The Calico Parody: Passy's Absurdity Marx then delivers one of his most memorable polemical passages — the calico producer parody. \"But it is such a homely idea that the product first suffices in kind to feed the workers, then to leave the capitalist farmer more food than he needs, and that the surplus over and above this then forms a natural rent.\" It would be just the same with a calico producer who manufactures 200,000 yards of cloth. This is not only sufficient to clothe his workers and to more than clothe his wife, all his offspring and himself; it also leaves him calico to sell and finally to pay a hefty rent in. \"It is such a simple matter!\" We deduct the production costs of the 200,000 yards, and a surplus of calico must remain over as rent. But what a naive idea it is to deduct production costs of, say, £10,000 from the 200,000 yards, without knowing the sale price of calico; to deduct money from calico, an exchange-value from a use-value, and then to determine the surplus yards of calico over pounds sterling. \"It is worse than squaring the circle, which is at least based on the concept of limits in which line and curve come together.\" This is M. Passy's recipe: \"We deduct money from calico before the calico is transformed into money, either logically or in reality!\" The surplus is the rent, which however should be treated \"naturally\" (see for example Karl Arndt, Die Naturgemässe Volkswirthschaft, gegenüber dem Monopoliengeiste und dem Communismus , Hanau, 1845, pp. 461–2), and not with diabolical \"sophistries.\" It is foolishness such as this — the deduction of the production price from so and so many bushels of wheat, the subtraction of a sum of money from a cubic measure — that this whole restoration of natural rent comes down to. The polemic here is not merely rhetorical: it demonstrates that the \"natural rent\" theory is not just wrong but categorically confused , mixing use-value and value, quantity and price, nature and society. It is the methodological error of treating a physical surplus as if it were a value surplus, which is exactly what Marx's theory of value forbids. 2.2 — Labour Rent 2.2.1 The Simplest Form Defined Marx now begins the systematic genealogy with the simplest form: labour rent . The direct producer devotes one part of the week, with tools that belong to him either legally or in practice (plough, draught animals, etc.), to land that is in practice his own, and works the other days of the week for the landlord on his estate without reward. In this situation, \"rent and surplus-value are identical.\" Rent, and not profit, is the form in which the unpaid surplus labour is expressed. The extent to which the worker (a \"self-sustaining serf\") can obtain a surplus over what we would call wages in the capitalist mode of production depends, other things being equal, on the proportion in which his working time is divided between labour-time for himself and statute-labour for the landlord. This surplus over and above the necessary means of subsistence — the nucleus of what appears as profit in the capitalist mode of production — is thus entirely determined by the level of ground-rent, which here not only is, but actually appears as , directly unpaid surplus labour: unpaid surplus labour for the \"proprietor\" of the conditions of production, which here coincide with the land itself, or, in as much as they are distinct from it, are still held to be its accessory. That the serf's product must be sufficient in this case to replace his conditions of labour as well as his subsistence is a condition that remains the same in all modes of production, since it is not the result of this specific form but a natural condition of all continuing and reproductive labour in general , of any continuing production, which is always also reproduction, i.e. also reproduction of its own conditions of operation. 2.2.2 The Necessity of Extra-Economic Compulsion It is clear, too, that in all forms where the actual worker himself remains the \"possessor\" of the means of production and the conditions of labour needed for the production of his own means of subsistence, the property relationship must appear at the same time as a direct relationship of domination and servitude , and the direct producer therefore as an unfree person — an unfreedom which may undergo a progressive attenuation from serfdom with statute-labour down to a mere tribute obligation. The direct producer in this case is by our assumption in possession of his own means of production, the objective conditions of labour needed for the realization of his labour and the production of his means of subsistence; he pursues his agriculture independently, as well as the rural-domestic industry associated with it. This independence is not abolished when, as in India for example, these small peasants form a more or less natural community, since what is at issue here is independence vis-à-vis the nominal landlord. Under these conditions, the surplus labour for the nominal landowner can only be extorted from them by extra-economic compulsion , whatever the form this might assume. This is one of the most important theoretical points in the chapter: where the producer possesses the means of production, surplus labour cannot be extracted by purely economic means (i.e., by the threat of starvation that operates under capitalism, where the worker is separated from the means of production). It requires extra-economic compulsion — political, legal, military, or customary. This distinguishes pre-capitalist rent from capitalist profit, which is extracted through the \"silent compulsion of economic relations\" (as Marx elsewhere puts it). Marx footnotes: \"When a country was conquered, the first thing for the conqueror was always to take possession of the people. Cf. Linguet. See also Möser.\" (S.-N.-H. Linguet, Théorie des loix civiles, ou principes fondamentaux de la société , London, 1767 — see also Theories of Surplus-Value , Part I, Chapter VII; Justus Möser, Osnabrückische Geschichte , Berlin and Stettin, 1780.) This differs from the slave or plantation economy in that the slave works with conditions of production that do not belong to him , and does not work independently. Relations of personal dependence are therefore necessary, in other words personal unfreedom, to whatever degree, and being chained to the land as its accessory — bondage in the true sense. If there are no private landowners but it is the state, as in Asia, which confronts them directly as simultaneously landowner and sovereign, rent and tax coincide , or rather there does not exist any tax distinct from this form of ground-rent. Under these conditions, the relationship of dependence does not need to possess any stronger form, either politically or economically, than that which is common to all subjection to this state. Here the state is the supreme landlord. Sovereignty here is landed property concentrated on a national scale. But for this very reason there is no private landed property, though there is both private and communal possession and usufruct of the land. This passage contains Marx's famous — and much-debated — theory of the \"Asiatic mode of production,\" where the state's sovereign power and its role as landlord are fused, and rent and tax are indistinguishable. It is important to note that Marx does not present this as a universal stage but as a specific historical configuration, contrastable with the European path. 2.2.3 The Specific Economic Form as the Hidden Basis of the Social Edifice Marx now makes one of his most sweeping theoretical statements on the relationship between economic form and social-political structure: \"The specific economic form in which unpaid surplus labour is pumped out of the direct producers determines the relationship of domination and servitude, as this grows directly out of production itself and reacts back on it in turn as a determinant. On this is based the entire configuration of the economic community arising from the actual relations of production, and hence also its specific political form. It is in each case the direct relationship of the owners of the conditions of production to the immediate producers — a relationship whose particular form naturally corresponds always to a certain level of development of the type and manner of labour, and hence to its social productive power — in which we find the innermost secret, the hidden basis of the entire social edifice, and hence also the political form of the relationship of sovereignty and dependence, in short, the specific form of state in each case.\" This is a compact statement of historical materialism applied to the theory of rent. The form of surplus extraction — whether via labour rent, rent in kind, money rent, or profit — determines the relations of domination, which in turn shape the political form of the state. The \"innermost secret\" of any society is the way in which surplus labour is pumped out of the direct producers. But Marx immediately adds a qualification that reveals his methodological sophistication: \"This does not prevent the same economic basis — the same in its major conditions — from displaying endless variations and gradations in its appearance, as the result of innumerable different empirical circumstances, natural conditions, racial relations, historical influences acting from outside, etc., and these can only be understood by analysing these empirically given conditions.\" This guards against economic determinism: the same economic basis can produce very different superstructures depending on empirical circumstances. Historical materialism is not a rigid schema but a method of analysis that requires concrete investigation. 2.2.4 The Palpable Identity of Rent and Surplus-Value As far as labour rent goes — the most simple and primitive form of rent — this much is clear: rent is the original form of surplus-value and coincides with it . But it needs no further analysis here that surplus-value coincides with the unpaid labour of others, since this still exists in its visible, palpable form, the labour of the direct producer for himself being still separate both in time and space from his work for the landlord, with the latter appearing directly in the brutal form of forced labour for a third party . Likewise, the \"property\" the land has of yielding a rent is reduced here to a \"palpably open secret,\" for the same nature that delivers rent also includes the human labour-power that is chained to the land, and the property relationship that forces its owner to exert and activate this labour-power beyond the degree that would be required to satisfy his own indispensable needs. The rent consists in the direct appropriation of this extra expenditure of labour-power by the landowner; for the direct producer does not pay any further rent on top of this. In this case, where surplus-value and rent are not only identical but the surplus-value still palpably takes the form of surplus labour, the natural conditions or limits of rent are immediately evident, because they are the limits of surplus labour in general. The direct producer must (1) have sufficient labour-power, while (2) the natural conditions of his labour, in the first instance the land to be worked, must be fruitful enough, i.e. the natural productivity of his labour must be great enough, to allow him the possibility of surplus labour over and above the labour needed to satisfy his own indispensable needs. It is not this possibility that creates rent; only the compulsion makes the possibility a reality. The possibility itself, however, is bound up with subjective and objective natural conditions. Here, too, there is nothing at all mysterious. If labour-power is meagre and the natural conditions of labour scarce, surplus labour is also small — but so too then are both the needs of the producers, the relative number of exploiters of surplus labour, and finally the surplus product in which this relatively unproductive surplus labour is realized for this small number of exploiting proprietors. This is a crucial dialectical point: natural conditions set the possibility of surplus labour, but only social compulsion makes it actual . The natural-physical element is a precondition, not a cause. This is how Marx both acknowledges the material basis of surplus (agricultural productivity must exceed subsistence needs) and refuses to explain rent naturalistically. 2.2.5 Rent as the Limit to Profit Finally, it immediately follows from labour rent that, taking all other factors as constant, it depends entirely on the relative scale of the surplus or forced labour whether and how far the direct producer is capable of improving his own condition, enriching himself, producing a surplus over and above his indispensable means of subsistence, or — to anticipate the capitalist mode of expression — whether and how far he can produce some kind of profit for himself, i.e. a surplus over and above the wage that he also himself produces. Rent here is the normal and so to speak legitimate form of surplus labour, which absorbs everything. Far from being an excess over and above profit — i.e. in this case above some other kind of surplus over wages — not only the size of such a profit, but even its very existence, other factors being constant, depends on the size of the rent, i.e. of the surplus labour that has compulsorily to be performed for the proprietor. This is an important inversion of the capitalist relationship: under capitalism, profit determines rent (rent is the surplus over profit); under labour rent, rent determines profit (profit is what remains after rent). This inversion is crucial for understanding the historical specificity of capitalist rent. 2.2.6 The Role of Tradition and Legal Sanctification Some historians have expressed their amazement that when the direct producer is not a proprietor but only a possessor, all his surplus labour in fact belonging de jure to the landowner, it is still possible for this villein or serf to develop independent means of his own and even become quite wealthy. It is evident, however, that in the aboriginal and undeveloped conditions on which this social relation of production and the mode of production corresponding to it are based, tradition must play a predominant role . It is also evident here as always that it is in the interest of the dominant section of society to sanctify the existing situation as a law and to fix the limits given by custom and tradition as legal ones. Even ignoring any other factors, this happens automatically as soon as the constant reproduction of the basis of the existing situation, the relationship underlying it, assumes a regular and ordered form in the course of time; and this regulation and order is itself an indispensable moment of any mode of production that is to become solidly established and free from mere accident or caprice. It is precisely the form in which it is socially established, and hence the form of its relative emancipation from mere caprice and accident. It can attain this form in stagnant conditions of both the production process and the social relations corresponding to it, simply by reproducing itself repeatedly. Once this process has continued for a certain length of time, it is reinforced as usage and tradition and finally sanctified as an explicit law . Now since the form of this surplus labour, statute-labour, depends on the undeveloped condition of all labour's social productive powers, on the crudity of the mode of labour itself, it is natural for only a far smaller aliquot part of the direct producers' total labour to be confiscated from them than in more developed modes of production, and in the capitalist mode of production in particular. Marx then gives a numerical illustration: Let us assume for example that the statute-labour for the landlord was originally two days per week . These two weekly days of statute-labour thus persist as a constant quantity regulated by customary or written law. But the productivity of the remaining days that the direct producer has at his disposal is a variable quantity , which must develop as he progresses in experience, just as the new needs with which he becomes familiar, the expansion of the market for his product, and the growing security with which he disposes of this portion of his labour-power will spur him to increased exertion of it. It should not be forgotten in this connection that the use of this labour-power is in no way confined to agriculture but also includes rural domestic industry. This gives the possibility of a certain economic development, dependent of course on favourable conditions, innate racial character, etc. (Marx here uses the language of his time — \"innate racial character\" — which we may note as a period term rather than an analytical category.) The fixed quantum of statute-labour plus the variable productivity of the remaining days yields the possibility of the serf's enrichment: this is the origin of the differentiation among the direct producers that will eventually produce the capitalist farmer. 2.3 — Rent in Kind 2.3.1 The Transformation and Its Nature The transformation of labour rent into rent in kind \"in no way changes the nature of ground-rent, economically speaking.\" This consists, in the forms we are dealing with here, in the fact that ground-rent is the only dominant and normal form of surplus-value or surplus labour ; which is expressed in turn in its being the only surplus labour or surplus product which the direct producer who finds himself in possession of the conditions of labour needed for his own reproduction has to provide for the owner of the one condition of labour that includes everything else at this stage, the land; while on the other hand it is only the land that confronts him as the property of another, a condition of labour that has become independent of him and is personified in the landowner. But when rent in kind is the dominant and furthest developed form of ground-rent, it is always still more or less accompanied by survivals of the earlier form, i.e. rent to be paid directly in labour, statute-labour, and this irrespective of whether the landlord is a private individual or the state. 2.3.2 Higher Cultural Level Rent in kind presupposes a higher cultural level on the part of the immediate producer, i.e. a higher stage of development of his labour and of society in general; and it distinguishes itself from the preceding form by the fact that surplus labour is no longer performed in its natural form , i.e. no longer under the direct supervision and compulsion of the landlord or his representative. Rather, the immediate producer, driven on by force of circumstances instead of direct compulsion and by legal stipulation instead of by the whip, is himself responsible for performing this surplus labour. Surplus production in the sense of production over and above the indispensable needs of the immediate producer has here already become the self-evident rule, and surplus production in a field of production that actually belongs to him, the land he himself exploits, instead of on the lord's estate alongside and outside his own. In this relationship, the immediate producer has the use of more or less his entire labour-time, even if one part of this labour-time — originally it would seem the whole surplus part — still belongs for free to the landowner; it is simply that the latter receives this no longer directly, in its own natural form, but rather in the natural form of the product in which it is realized. When rent in kind is established in its pure form, the burdensome and more or less constant interruption of labour for the landowner which characterizes statute-labour (cf. Volume 1, Chapter 10, 2, \"Manufacturer and Boyar\") disappears , or is at least reduced to a few brief intervals in the year in cases where certain statutory obligations persist alongside rent in kind. The work of the producer for himself and his work for the landowner are no longer palpably separate in time and space. 2.3.3 The Presupposition of Natural Economy This rent in kind, in its pure form, even though relics of it may be handed down to more developed modes and relations of production, still presupposes a natural economy — i.e. it presupposes that the economic conditions are produced entirely or at least in the main by the economic unit itself, being directly replaced and reproduced out of its gross product. It also presupposes the union of rural domestic industry and agriculture; the surplus product which forms rent is the product of this combined agricultural-industrial family labour, whether the rent in kind includes a greater or lesser amount of industrial products, as was frequently the case in the Middle Ages, or whether it is paid simply in the form of agricultural products proper. In this form of rent, the rent in kind in which surplus labour is expressed need in no way take up the entire excess labour of the rural family. The producer has greater room to manoeuvre , compared with labour rent, to gain time for excess labour whose product belongs to himself, just like the product of that labour that satisfies his most indispensable needs. In this form, too, greater differences arise in the economic condition of individual immediate producers. There is at least the possibility of this, and the possibility for the immediate producer to obtain the means whereby he may exploit the labour of others. Yet this does not affect our discussion of the pure form of rent in kind, as we cannot embark here on the endlessly varied combinations in which the different forms of rent may be combined, mixed together and amalgamated. 2.3.4 The Basis of Static Asiatic Societies The form of rent in kind, bound up with a particular type of product and of production itself; the connection indispensable to it between agriculture and domestic industry; the almost total self-sufficiency that the peasant family thereby obtains, its independence from the market and from the movement of production and of the history of that part of society outside itself; in brief, the character of natural economy in general — makes this form eminently suitable as the basis of those static conditions of society that we can see in Asia for example. Here, as in the earlier form of labour rent, ground-rent is the normal form of surplus-value, and therefore of surplus labour, i.e. of the entire excess labour that the immediate producer must perform for nothing, in actual fact therefore compulsorily, for the owner of his most essential condition of labour, the land — even if this compulsion no longer confronts him in its previous brutal form. Profit , if we incorrectly give this name in anticipation to that fraction of the excess of his labour over and above the necessary labour which he appropriates for himself, so little determines rent in kind that it rather grows up behind its back , meeting a natural limit in the level of the rent in kind. This latter may be such as seriously to endanger the reproduction of the conditions of labour, the means of production themselves, making the expansion of production more or less impossible and reducing the direct producers to the physical minimum of means of subsistence. This is particularly the case when this form is found in existence and exploited by a conquering trading nation, as by the British in India, for example. The phrase \"grows up behind its back\" is crucial: profit is not a determining category here; it is a residual that emerges only insofar as the producer can retain part of his product after rent is paid. The causal arrow runs from rent to profit, not the reverse. 2.4 — Money Rent 2.4.1 Definition and Formal Transformation By money rent , in this connection, Marx means not the industrial or commercial ground-rent based on the capitalist mode of production, which is simply an excess over the average profit, but the ground-rent that arises simply from a formal transformation of the rent in kind, as this was itself simply transformed labour rent. Instead of the product itself, the immediate producer now has to pay his landowner (whether the state or a private person) the price of this. An excess product in its natural form is no longer sufficient; it has to be transformed from this natural form into the money form. Even though the direct producer still continues to produce at least the greater part of his means of subsistence himself, a portion of his product must now be transformed into a commodity and be produced as such . The character of the entire mode of production is thus more or less changed. It loses its independence, its separation from any social context. What now becomes decisive is the proportion of production costs , which now include greater or lesser expenditures in money; or at least the excess of the part of the gross product to be transformed into money over and above the part that must serve on the one hand again as means of reproduction, on the other hand as immediate means of subsistence. 2.4.2 Continued Basis and Its Dissolution The basis of this type of rent, however, though it is now approaching its dissolution, remains the same as for the rent in kind that forms its starting-point. The direct producer is still the hereditary or otherwise traditional possessor of the land, who has to provide for the landlord, as proprietor of this most essential condition of production, an excess and compulsory labour, i.e. unpaid labour provided without an equivalent in the form of the surplus product transformed into money. Property in those conditions of labour distinct from the land, such as agricultural equipment and other movables, is already transformed in the earlier forms into the property of the direct producers, first of all simply in practice but later also in law, and this is still more of a premise for the form of money rent. 2.4.3 Preconditions: Trade, Markets, Prices The transformation of rent in kind into money rent that takes place at first sporadically, then on a more or less national scale, presupposes an already more significant development of trade, urban industry, commodity production in general and therefore monetary circulation . It also presupposes that products have a market price and are sold more or less approximately at their values , which in the earlier forms need in no way be the case. In Eastern Europe, we can still see something of this transition going on today (i.e., in Marx's time). How little it can be accomplished without a certain development of labour's social productive power is attested to by various failed attempts under the Roman Empire to make this transformation, followed by regression to rent in kind, after which the attempt was made to transform into money rent at least the part of this rent existing as a state tax. The same difficulty of transition was shown for example in pre-revolutionary France by the amalgamation and adulteration of money rent with residues of its earlier forms. This is a crucial empirical point against any unilinear evolutionary narrative: transitions can fail, regress, and produce hybrid formations. Historical development is not smooth. 2.4.4 The Final Form and Its Dissolution But money rent as a transformed and contrasting form of rent in kind is the final form of the type of ground-rent we have been considering here, while at the same time the form of its dissolution , i.e. of ground-rent as the normal form of surplus-value and the unpaid surplus labour to be performed for the owner of the conditions of production. In its pure form, this rent, just like labour rent and rent in kind, does not represent any excess over and above profit. In its concept, it includes profit. In as much as profit arises alongside it as a particular part of surplus labour, the money rent, like rent in its earlier forms, is still the normal limit to this embryonic profit, which can develop only in proportion to the possibility of exploiting that labour, whether a person's own excess labour or that of others, which remains after the surplus labour expressed in money rent has been paid. If a profit really does arise alongside the rent, it is not the profit that sets a limit to rent, but inversely rent which sets a limit to profit . As we have already said, however, money rent is at the same time the form of dissolution of the ground-rent we have so far been dealing with here, which coincides prima facie with surplus-value and surplus labour — ground-rent as the normal and dominant form of surplus-value. 2.4.5 The Two Paths of Further Development In its further development, money rent must lead — leaving aside all intermediate forms, such as that of the small peasant farmer — either to the transformation of the land into free peasant property or to the form of the capitalist mode of production, rent paid by the capitalist farmer . With money rent, the traditional relationship fixed by customary law between the landowner and his dependant, who possesses and works one part of the land, is necessarily transformed into a contractual relationship , a purely monetary relationship determined by the firm rules of positive law. The tiller with possession is essentially transformed into a mere tenant . On the one hand this transformation is utilized, where general conditions of production are suitable, for the gradual expropriation of the old peasant possessor and the installation in his place of a capitalist farmer; on the other hand it allows the former possessor to buy himself out of his rent obligation and leads to his transformation into an independent peasant-farmer, with full ownership in the land he tills. The transformation of rent in kind into money rent, moreover, is not only necessarily accompanied, but even anticipated , by the formation of a class of non-possessing day-labourers, who hire themselves out for money. 2.4.6 The Rise of the Capitalist Farmer During the period of its rise, when this new class still appears only sporadically, the custom necessarily develops, among the better-off rent-paying peasants, of exploiting agricultural wage-labourers on their own account, just as in the feudal period the wealthier peasant serfs already kept serfs of their own. In this way it gradually becomes possible for them to build up a certain degree of wealth and transform themselves into future capitalists. Among the old possessors of the land, working for themselves, there arises a seed-bed for the nurturing of capitalist farmers , whose development is conditioned by the development of capitalist production, not just in the countryside but in general, and who advance particularly rapidly when, as in England in the sixteenth century, they are aided by such particularly favourable conditions as the progressive devaluation of money at that time, which, given the traditionally long terms of tenancy contracts, enriched them at the landowners' expense. This is a concrete historical illustration of how the capitalist farmer emerged not from nowhere but from the differentiation of the peasantry — and how monetary contingencies (the sixteenth-century price revolution) could accelerate the process. 2.4.7 The Entry of Urban Capital into Agriculture Moreover, once rent takes the form of money rent and the relation between rent-paying peasant and landowner becomes a contractual relation — a transformation which is only possible given a certain relative level of development of the world market, trade and manufacture — land inevitably starts to be leased to capitalists, who were formerly outside rural limits and who now transfer to the land, and to the rural economy, capital that has been obtained in the town , together with the capitalist mode of operation which has also been developed there: the production of the product as a mere commodity and a mere means of appropriating surplus-value. As a general rule, this form can come about only in those countries that dominate the world market during the transition period from the feudal to the capitalist mode of production. With the intervention of the capitalist farmer between the landowner and the actual working tiller, all relationships that arose from the former rural mode of production are torn asunder . The farmer becomes the real controller of these agricultural workers and the real exploiter of their surplus labour, while the landowner stands in a direct relationship only to this capitalist farmer, and a mere monetary and contractual relationship at that. 2.4.8 The Transformation of the Nature of Rent The nature of rent thereby changes, not only as a matter of fact and accidentally, which happened in places already under the previous forms, but rather normally, in its acknowledged and dominant form . From the normal form of surplus-value and surplus labour, it declines into the excess of this surplus labour over and above the part of it that is appropriated by the exploiting capitalist in the form of profit ; the entire surplus labour, both profit and the excess over profit, is now directly extracted by him, received in the form of the total surplus profit and turned into money. It is now only an excess part of this surplus-value which he extracts by virtue of his capital, by the direct exploitation of the agricultural worker, that he hands over to the landowner as rent. How much or how little he parts with in this way is determined on average, as a limit, by the average profit that capital yields in the non-agricultural spheres of production and by the non-agricultural price of production that this governs . Rent has now been transformed from the normal form of surplus-value and surplus labour into an excess over the part of surplus labour that is claimed by capital as a matter of course and normally — an excess peculiar to one particular sphere of production, the agricultural. Instead of rent, the normal form of surplus-value is now profit , and rent now counts as an independent form only under special conditions, not a form of surplus-value in general but of a particular offshoot of this, surplus profit . 2.4.9 The Transformation of Production Itself It is unnecessary to go into any further detail as to how this transformation corresponds to a gradual transformation in the mode of production itself. This already results from the fact that it is now normal for this capitalist farmer to produce the agricultural product as a commodity , and that while formerly only the excess over his means of subsistence was transformed into a commodity, now a relatively minute part of these commodities is directly transformed into his own means of subsistence. It is no longer land, but capital, that has now directly subsumed even agricultural labour under itself and its productivity. 2.4.10 The Formation of Average Profit Outside Agriculture The average profit and the price of production governed by it are formed outside the rural situation, in the orbit of urban trade and manufacture . The profit of the rent-paying peasant does not enter into this equalization process, for his relationship to the landowner is not a capitalist one. In so far as he makes a profit, realizing an excess over and above his necessary means of subsistence, whether by his own labour or by exploiting the labour of others, this happens behind the back of the normal relationship ; other factors being equal, it is not the level of this profit that determines the rent, but this profit is conversely determined by the rent as its limit. The high rate of profit in the Middle Ages was not due simply to the low composition of capital, with the variable element laid out on wages being predominant. It was a result of the fraud committed against the countryside , the appropriation of a part of the landowner's rent and the income of his dependants. If the countryside exploited the town politically in the Middle Ages, wherever feudalism was not broken through by exceptional urban development as in Italy, then the town everywhere and without exception exploited the countryside economically through its monopoly prices, its taxation system, its guilds, its direct commercial trickery and its usury. This is a compressed statement of the town-country relationship under feudalism, and an important corrective to the romantic view that the countryside was simply the site of exploitation of the town. In fact, the exploitation was mutual but asymmetrical, with the town economically dominant. 2.4.11 The Rodbertus Argument and Its Three Refutations Marx now turns to a specific theoretical argument, associated with Rodbertus, which he will refute in three steps. The argument (as Marx reconstructs it): One might imagine that the very entry of the capitalist farmer into agricultural production would already provide proof that the price of agricultural products, which had always paid a rent in some form or other, would have to stand above the production price of manufactured goods , at least at the time of this entry; either reaching the level of a monopoly price , or having risen to the value of the agricultural products , which actually does stand above the price of production governed by the average profit. For if this were not so, the capitalist farmer, given the prevailing prices for agricultural products, could not possibly first realize the average profit from the price of these products and then pay out of this same price a further excess above this profit in the form of rent. One might conclude from this that the general rate of profit guiding the capitalist farmer in his contract with the landowner was formed without taking rent into account, and that as soon as this general rate comes to govern rural production it thus finds this excess and pays it to the landowner. It is in this traditional manner that Mr Rodbertus explains things (see Theories of Surplus-Value , Part II, Chapters VIII and IX, 10). Marx then gives three refutations: Firstly. This entry of capital into agriculture as an independent and leading power does not take place everywhere all at once, but rather gradually and in particular branches of production. At first it does not take hold of agriculture proper, but rather branches of production such as stock-raising and particularly sheep-farming , whose main product, wool, offers at first a market price permanently in excess of its price of production, in conditions of the rise of industry; this is not equalized until later on. That was the case in England during the sixteenth century. Secondly. Since capitalist production sets in only sporadically at first, it can in no way be held against the assumption made here that it first of all takes hold of those farms which generally can pay a differential rent , as a result of their special fertility or particularly favourable location. Thirdly. Even assuming that the prices of agricultural products do stand above their prices of production when this mode of production gets under way, which in fact presupposes an increasing weight of urban demand, as was undoubtedly the case in England in the latter third of the seventeenth century, it is still the case that once the new mode of production has extended beyond the mere subsumption of agriculture under capital and the improvement in agriculture necessarily bound up with this development, and a reduction in production costs has set in, this will be balanced by a reaction, a fall in the price of agricultural products , as was the case in England in the first half of the eighteenth century. Conclusion: Thus rent as an excess above the average profit cannot be explained in this traditional way. Whatever the historical conditions under which it may first arise, once it has struck root rent can occur only under the modern conditions previously developed. The three-pronged refutation is a model of Marx's method: it shows that the historical presuppositions of the Rodbertus argument are empirically false (capital did not enter agriculture all at once; it entered the most fertile lands first; prices fell after the initial period), and that even if they were true, the conclusion would not follow. 2.4.12 Capitalized Rent and the Price of Land We should finally note in connection with the transformation of rent in kind into money rent that the capitalized rent, the price of land, and therefore its alienability and actual alienation, now becomes an important aspect ; and that not only can the former rent-payer transform himself in this way into an independent peasant proprietor, but also urban and other holders of money can buy plots of land with a view to leasing them either to peasants or to capitalists, and enjoy the rent on their capital thus invested as a form of interest. This factor, too, helps to promote the transformation of the former mode of exploitation, of the relationship between owner and actual tiller, and of rent itself. 2.5 — Share-Cropping and Small-Scale Peasant Ownership 2.5.1 The Final Point in the Development Marx announces that we have now reached \"the final point in our development of ground-rent through its different stages.\" Before turning to the two parallel forms — share-cropping and small-peasant ownership — he restates the unity of the preceding forms: In all these forms of ground-rent — labour rent, rent in kind and money rent (as simply a transformed form of rent in kind) — the rent-payer is always taken as the actual tiller and possessor of the land , whose unpaid surplus labour goes directly to the landowner. Even in the last form, money rent — in so far as this is pure, i.e. simply the transformed form of rent in kind — this is not only a possible case, it is so in actual fact. 2.5.2 Share-Cropping as a Transitional Form As a transitional form from the original form of rent to capitalist rent, we can take the system of share-cropping (métayage), where the tenant farmer provides, besides his labour (his own or others'), a part of the operating capital, the landowner providing not only the land but also a further portion of capital (e.g. livestock), and the product being divided between share-cropper and landowner in definite proportions, which vary between different countries. The farmer, here, has insufficient capital for full capitalist cultivation . The share that the landowner draws, on the other hand, does not have the pure form of rent. It may include interest on the capital he advances, and a surplus rent on top of this. It may absorb the entire surplus labour of the farmer, or leave him a greater or smaller share of this. The essential thing, however, is that rent here no longer appears as the normal form of surplus-value . On the one hand, the share-cropper, whether he applies his own labour or that of others, has a claim to a share of the product not in his capacity as worker but as owner of a part of his tools, as his own capitalist . On the other hand the landowner claims his share not exclusively on the basis of his ownership of the land but also as the lender of capital . Marx footnotes 44a: \"Cf. Buret, Tocqueville, Sismondi.\" The works referred to are Buret's Cours d'économie politique , Brussels, 1842; De Tocqueville's L'Ancien Régime et la révolution , Paris, 1856; and Sismondi's Nouveaux Principes d'économie politique , Paris, 1827. Antoine-Eugène Buret (1810–42) was a follower of Sismondi. 2.5.3 The Polish and Romanian Example In Poland and Romania, for example, a residue from the old system of common property in the land , which has remained after the transition to an independent peasant economy, has served as a pretext for effecting a transition to the lower forms of ground-rent. One part of the land belongs to the individual peasants and is tilled by them independently. Another part is tilled in common and forms a surplus product, serving partly to meet the communal expenses and partly as a reserve in case of harvest failure, etc. The two latter parts of the surplus product, and ultimately the whole surplus product together with the land on which it grows, are gradually usurped by state officials and private individuals , and the originally free peasant proprietor, whose obligation to take part in the common tilling of this land is maintained, is transformed into a statute-labourer or a payer of rent in kind , while those who have usurped the common land transform themselves into landed proprietors, not only of the usurped common land but of the peasant lands as well. This is a concrete historical illustration of how the transition can go backwards — from free peasant ownership to lower forms of rent — through the usurpation of communal property. It shows that the genealogy is not unilinear and that regression is possible. 2.5.4 The Slave Economy and the Coincidence of Rent and Profit Marx notes that we do not need to go into any further detail here as regards the slave economy (which also passes through a number of gradations from patriarchal slavery predominantly for home use to the plantation system, working for the world market) nor the system in which the landowner cultivates for his own account , possessing all the instruments of production and exploiting labour, whether free or unfree, by deliveries in kind or services paid in money. Here the landowner coincides with the owner of the instruments of production, i.e. with the direct exploiter of workers who are numbered among these elements of production. Rent and profit coincide too — there is no separation of the various forms of surplus-value. The entire surplus labour of the workers, which is expressed here in surplus product, is extracted from them directly by the proprietor of all the instruments of production, which count among them also the land, and in the original form of slavery even the direct producers themselves. Where the capitalist conception prevails, as on the American plantations , this entire surplus-value is conceived as profit ; where the capitalist mode of production does not exist itself, and the mode of conception corresponding to it is not transferred from capitalist countries, it appears as rent . In neither case does this form offer any difficulty. The landowner's income, the available surplus product he appropriates, whatever name it might be given, is here the normal and prevailing form in which the entire unpaid surplus labour is directly appropriated, and landed property forms the basis for this appropriation . 2.5.5 Small-Scale Peasant Ownership Marx now turns to the final form: small-scale peasant ownership . Here the peasant is the free proprietor of his land , which appears as his main instrument of production, as the indispensable field of employment for his labour and his capital. No lease-price is paid in this form ; thus rent does not appear as a separate form of surplus-value, even if, in countries where the capitalist mode of production is otherwise developed, it does present itself as surplus profit by comparison with other branches of production, though as surplus profit which falls to the peasant, as does the entire product of his labour. Like the earlier forms, this form of landownership presupposes that the agricultural population has a great numerical preponderance over the urban population , i.e. that even if the capitalist mode of production is dominant it is relatively little developed, so that the concentration of capitals is also confined to narrow limits in the other branches of production, and a fragmentation of capital prevails. By the nature of the case, a predominant part of the agricultural product must be consumed here by its producers, the peasants, as direct means of subsistence, with only the excess over and above this going into trade with the towns as a commodity. 2.5.6 Differential Rent Without Absolute Rent No matter how the average market price of agricultural products is governed here, there must evidently be a differential rent , an excess portion of commodity price, for the better or better-located lands, just as there is in the capitalist mode of production. It is simply that the peasant whose labour is realized under more favourable natural conditions pockets this himself. In this form, the land price makes up an element of the peasant's production costs , since, as things develop further, either the price of land is computed at a certain money value in dividing up an inheritance, or, as a holding or its component parts changes hands the land is actually bought by the peasant himself, often by raising the money on mortgage. Where the price of land, which is nothing but capitalized rent, is an element assumed in advance, and the rent seems to exist independently of any differentiation in the land's fertility and location — precisely here, in this form, it is to be assumed in the average case that there is no absolute rent , i.e. that the worst soil does not pay any rent; for absolute rent assumes either a realized excess value of the product above its price of production or an excess monopoly price for the product above its value. But since the rural economy here is largely one of agriculture for immediate subsistence , with the land being an indispensable field of occupation for the labour and capital of the majority of the population, the governing market price of the product only reaches its value under extraordinary conditions; this value, however, will stand as a rule above the price of production , on account of the preponderant element of living labour, even though the excess of the value above the price of production will be limited again by the low composition also of non-agricultural capital in countries where a smallholding economy prevails. 2.5.7 The Absence of Barriers for the Smallholding Peasant The smallholding peasant's exploitation is not limited by the average profit on capital , in as much as he is a small capitalist; nor by the need for a rent , in as much as he is a landowner. The only absolute barrier he faces as a petty capitalist is the wage that he pays himself , after deducting his actual expenses. He cultivates his land as long as the price of the product is sufficient for him to cover this wage; and he often does so down to a physical minimum . In so far as he is a landowner, he does not face any property barrier, since this can present itself only in opposition to a capital (including labour) separate from it, by imposing an obstacle to its application. The interest on the price of land is a barrier, however, as it generally has to be paid over to a third party, the mortgagee. But this interest can precisely be paid out of the part of the surplus labour that under capitalist conditions would form the profit. The rent anticipated in the price of land and the interest paid on it, therefore, can be no more than a part of the capitalized surplus labour of the peasant over and above the labour indispensable for his own subsistence , but this surplus labour does not have to be realized in a portion of commodity value equal to the entire surplus profit, and still less in an excess above the surplus labour realized in the average profit, i.e. a surplus profit. The rent may be a deduction from the average profit or even the only part of this that is realized . In order for the peasant smallholder to cultivate his land or to buy land to cultivate, therefore, it is not necessary , as in the normal capitalist mode of production, for the market price of the agricultural product to rise high enough to yield him the average profit, and still less an excess over and above this average profit that is fixed in the form of rent. Thus it is not necessary for the market price to rise either to the value of his product or to its price of production. 2.5.8 The Lower Price of Corn This is one of the reasons why the price of corn in countries where small-scale ownership predominates is lower than in countries of the capitalist mode of production . A portion of the surplus labour performed by those peasants working under the least favourable conditions is presented to society for nothing and does not contribute towards governing the price of production or forming value. This lower price of corn in countries of small-scale ownership is a result of the poverty of the producers and in no way of the productivity of their labour. This is a striking and counterintuitive result: the low price of agricultural products in smallholding countries is not a sign of high productivity but of the producers' poverty — they sell below value because they have no alternative but to cultivate their land at any price. 2.5.9 Historical Examples This form of free smallholding ownership by peasants who farm their land themselves, as the dominant, normal form, constitutes the economic basis of society in the best periods of classical antiquity , while we find it among modern peoples as one of the forms that arise out of the dissolution of feudal landed property. Examples are the yeomanry in England, the peasant estate in Sweden and the peasants of France and western Germany . Marx notes that we are not referring here to the colonies, since there the independent peasant farmer develops under different conditions. The free ownership of the peasant who farms his land himself is evidently the most normal form of landed property for small-scale cultivation , i.e. for a mode of production in which possession of the land is a condition for the worker's ownership over the product of his own labour, and in which, whether he is free or a dependent proprietor, the tiller always has to produce his means of subsistence himself, independently, as an isolated worker with his family. Ownership of land is just as necessary for the full development of this activity as is ownership of the instrument of labour for the free development of the handicraftsman's trade. It forms here the basis for the development of personal independence . It is a necessary transition point in the development of agriculture itself . 2.5.10 The Causes of Its Decline The causes of its decline show its limitations. These are: The destruction of rural domestic industry , its normal complement, by the development of large-scale industry; The gradual impoverishment and exhaustion of the soil which has been subjected to this form of cultivation; The usurpation of communal property by large landowners , this communal property always forming a second complement to the smallholding economy and being the only thing which makes possible the upkeep of livestock; The competition of large-scale agriculture , whether in the form of plantations or the capitalist form; Improvements in agriculture also contribute to this, by leading to a fall in the prices of agricultural products, while also requiring greater expenditures and more abundant objective conditions of production, as in England in the first half of the eighteenth century. The agricultural smallholding, by its very nature, rules out the development of the productive powers of social labour, the social concentration of capitals, stock-raising on a large scale or the progressive application of science . Usury and taxation must always impoverish it. The outlay of capital in the price of land withdraws this capital from agriculture. Incessant fragmentation of means of production and isolation of the producers themselves . Tremendous wastage of human labour . The progressive deterioration of the conditions of production and the increase in price of the means of production is a necessary law of small-scale landowning . The disastrous effect of good seasons for this mode of production. Marx footnotes 45: \"See the King of France's speech from the throne, in Tooke\" — Thomas Tooke and William Newmarch, A History of Prices... , Vol. 6, London, 1857, pp. 29–30. This presumably refers to a passage where the King of France laments the disastrous effect of good harvests on peasant agriculture. 2.5.11 The Capital Laid Out in Purchasing Land One particular evil of small-scale agriculture, where this is combined with the free ownership of land, arises from the way the tiller lays out capital in purchasing land . (The same applies to the transitional form in which the owner of a large estate lays out capital first to buy land and then again to cultivate it himself as his own farmer.) Given the mobile character land acquires as a mere commodity, changes in possession multiply (footnote 46: \"See Mounier and Rubichon\" — L. Mounier, De l'agriculture en France d'après les documents officiels. Avec des remarques par Rubichon , Paris, 1846), so that with each new generation, and each division of an inheritance, the land forms a new capital investment, i.e. from the peasant's standpoint it becomes land that he has bought. The price of land here forms a predominant element of overhead costs, or the cost price of the product for the individual producer. 2.5.12 The Price of Land Is Capitalized Rent The price of land is nothing but the capitalized and thus anticipated rent . If agriculture is pursued on a capitalist basis, so that the landowner simply receives the annual rent and the farmer pays nothing for the land besides this, it is obvious that the capital which the landowner himself invests in purchasing land, though for him it is an interest-bearing capital investment, has nothing at all to do with the capital invested in agriculture itself . It forms part neither of the fixed capital functioning here nor of the circulating capital (footnote 47); it procures a title for the purchaser to receive the annual rent, but it has absolutely nothing to do with the production of this rent. Footnote 47 is a significant polemic: Dr H. Maron ( Extensiv oder Intensiv? [Oppeln, 1859]) bases himself on the false assumption of his opponents. He assumes that the capital invested in the purchase of land is \"investment capital\" and simply challenges the respective definitions of the concepts investment capital and operating capital, i.e. fixed capital and circulating capital. His \"completely jejune ideas about capital in general, even if they are somewhat excusable for a non-economist, given the general condition of German 'national economics',\" conceal from him that this capital is neither investment capital nor operating capital. In the same way, the capital that someone invests on the stock exchange in the purchase of shares or government paper is by no means actually \"invested\" in any branch of production, even if it appears as a capital investment for the investor himself. 2.5.13 The Slave Economy Analogy The buyer of the land simply hands the capital over to the person selling it, and the seller thereby renounces his property in the land. Thus this capital no longer exists as the capital of the buyer ; he no longer has it; it is in no way part of the capital he can invest in the land itself. Whether he has bought the land dearly or cheap, or even got it for nothing, in no way affects the capital that the farmer invests in his enterprise and in no way affects the rent; the only difference it makes is whether this rent appears to him as interest or not, or as a higher interest or a lower. Take the case of the slave economy, for example. The price that is paid here for the slave is no more than the anticipated and capitalized surplus-value or profit that is to be extracted from him . But the capital paid in purchasing the slave does not form part of the capital by which profit, surplus labour, is extracted from him. On the contrary: it is capital which the slaveowner has alienated , a deduction from the capital which he has at his disposal in actual production. It has ceased to exist for him, just as the capital invested in the purchase of land has ceased to exist for agriculture. The best proof of this is that it comes into renewed existence for the slaveowner or landowner only when he sells the slave or land again. But then the same relationship is set up for the buyer. The fact that he has bought the slave does not enable him immediately to exploit him. He is only able to do this by putting further capital into the slave economy itself. The same capital does not exist twice over , first in the hands of the seller of the land and then in the hands of its buyer. It passes from the buyer to the seller, and that is the end of it. The buyer now has no capital, but a piece of land instead. The fact that the rent obtained from the actual investment of capital on this piece of land is now reckoned by the new landowner as interest on capital that he has not invested on the land but has parted with in order to obtain it, does not change the economic nature of the land factor in the slightest, any more than the fact that someone has paid £1,000 for 3 per cent Consols has anything to do with the capital from whose revenue the interest on the national debt is paid. 2.5.14 Money Paid for Land Is Only Potential Capital In actual fact, what is paid over in the purchase of land, just like the money spent on the purchase of government bonds, is only capital in itself , just as any sum of value is potential capital on the basis of the capitalist mode of production. What was paid for the land, just as for government bonds or any other bought commodities, is a sum of money. This is potential capital, because it can be transformed into capital. It depends on the use made of it by the seller whether the money he receives really is transformed into capital or not. For the buyer, it can no longer function as such, any more than any other money he has definitively spent. It functions in his accounts as interest-bearing capital , since he reckons the income he receives — as rent from the land or as debt interest from the government — as interest on the money that it cost him to purchase the title to this revenue. He can realize it as capital only by reselling it. But then someone else, the new buyer, steps into the same relationship as the former was in before; no change of hands can transform the money spent in this way into actual capital for the spender . 2.5.15 The Peasant's Illusion In the case of the peasant smallholding, the illusion is still more strongly reinforced that land has a value of its own and thus goes into the production price of the product as capital, just like a machine or raw material. But we have seen how there are only two cases in which rent and hence capitalized rent, the price of land, can go into the price of the agricultural product as a determining factor: If the value of the agricultural product stands above its price of production , as a result of the composition of agricultural capital — a capital which has nothing in common with capital laid out on the purchase of land — and market conditions enable the landowner to valorize this difference. If there is a monopoly price . And these conditions obtain least of all in the case of the smallholding and petty landownership, since it is precisely here that production is designed to a very major extent to satisfy the producer's own needs, and proceeds without being governed by the general rate of profit. Even where smallholding economy is pursued on leased farms, the lease-price includes far more than under any other conditions a part of the profit, and even a deduction from wages; it is then only nominally rent, not rent as an independent category vis-à-vis wages and profit. 2.5.16 The Effects of the Outlay on Land Purchase Thus the expenditure of money capital on the purchase of land is not an investment of agricultural capital . It proportionately reduces the capital which the small peasants have at their disposal in their actual sphere of production. It proportionately reduces the scale of their means of production and hence narrows the economic basis of reproduction. It subjects the small peasant to usury , since in this sphere there is always less credit proper. It is a constraint on agriculture , even when the purchase of large estates is involved. It actually contradicts the capitalist mode of production , for which the indebtedness of the landowner, whether his estate is inherited or bought, is on the whole immaterial. Whether he pockets the rent himself or has to pay it over to a mortgagee in no way affects the cultivation of the property leased. 2.5.17 The Rate of Interest and the Price of Land We have seen how, once the ground-rent is given, the price of land is governed by the rate of interest . If this is low, the price of land is high, and vice versa. In normal conditions, therefore, a high price of land and a low rate of interest go together, so that if the peasant has to pay a high price for land when the interest rate is low, the same low rate of interest will also procure him his operating capital at favourable terms of credit. In actual fact, though, things are different when smallholding predominates: Firstly , the general laws of credit do not apply to the peasants, since they presuppose that the producers are capitalists. Secondly , where smallholding predominates (we are not referring here to colonies) and the smallholding peasant forms the backbone of the nation, the formation of capital, and thus social reproduction, is relatively weak, and still weaker is the formation of money capital for loan in the sense previously developed. This assumes the concentration and existence of a class of rich idle capitalists (Massie — Joseph Massie, An Essay on the Governing Causes of the Natural Rate of Interest , London, 1750, pp. 23–4). Thirdly , where landownership forms a condition of life for the greater part of the producers, as it does here, and an indispensable field of investment for their capital, the price of land will rise independently of the rate of interest and often in inverse proportion to it, because the demand for landed property will outweigh the supply. Being sold in this case in parcelled lots, the land fetches a far higher price than when sold in large estates, since the number of small buyers is large and the number of large buyers small ( bandes noires , Rubichon; Newman). Marx footnotes: these \"black gangs\" were groups of speculators in early nineteenth-century France; they dealt particularly in property confiscated from the aristocracy and the Church, buying this land wholesale and selling it in small parcels at a great profit. Marx's reference here is to Maurice Rubichon's Du mécanisme de la société en France et en Angleterre , Paris, 1837; also F. W. Newman, Lectures on Political Economy , London, 1851, pp. 180–81. All these reasons lead to a rise in the price of land, even at a relatively high rate of interest . The relatively low interest that the peasant draws from the capital he lays out on the purchase of land (Mounier) contrasts with the high and usurious rate he himself has to pay to his mortgagee. The Irish system shows the same thing, simply in a different form. An element that is foreign to production as such, the price of land, can thus rise here to a level which makes production impossible (Dombasle). 2.5.18 The Historical Meaning of Land as a Commodity If the price of land plays such a role, if the purchase and sale of land, the circulation of land as a commodity, develops to this extent, this is the practical result of the development of the capitalist mode of production , in as much as here the commodity becomes the general form of every product and of all instruments of production. On the other hand, this takes place only where the capitalist mode of production is developed only to a limited extent and does not yet display all its characteristic features; because it precisely depends on a situation where agriculture is no longer — or not yet — subjected to the capitalist mode of production, but is rather subjected to a mode of production taken over from forms of society that have disappeared. The disadvantages of the capitalist mode of production, with its dependence of the producer on the money price of his product, are thus combined here with the disadvantages that arise from its incomplete development . The peasant becomes a merchant and industrialist without the conditions in which he is able to produce his product as a commodity . 2.5.19 The Contradiction Between Private Ownership and Rational Agriculture The conflict between the price of land as an element of the cost price for the producer and as a non-element of the price of production for the product (even when rent is a determining factor in the price of the agricultural product, the capitalized rent which is advanced for twenty years or more never is) is just one of the forms expressing the contradiction between the private ownership of land and a rational agriculture , the normal social use of the land. Yet private ownership of land, and thus the expropriation from the land of the direct producers — private ownership for some, involving non-ownership of the land for others — is the basis of the capitalist mode of production . Here, in the case of small-scale agriculture, the price of land, as a form and result of private property in land, appears as a barrier to production itself . In the case of large-scale agriculture and large-scale landed property resting on the capitalist mode of operation, property similarly appears as a barrier, since it restricts the farmer in the productive investment of capital, which ultimately benefits not him but the landowner. 2.5.20 The Squandering of the Powers of the Earth In both forms, instead of a conscious and rational treatment of the land as permanent communal property , as the inalienable condition for the existence and reproduction of the chain of human generations, we have the exploitation and the squandering of the powers of the earth (not to mention the fact that exploitation is made dependent not on the level of social development reached but rather on the accidental and unequal conditions of the individual producers). In the case of small-scale ownership, this results from a lack of the resources and science needed to apply the social productive powers of labour . In the case of large landed property, it results from the exploitation of these resources for the most rapid possible enrichment of the farmer and proprietor . In both cases, from dependence on the market price . All criticism of small-scale landownership is ultimately reducible to criticism of private property as a barrier and obstacle to agriculture . So too is all counter-criticism of large landed property. Secondary political considerations are of course left aside here in both cases. It is simply that this barrier and obstacle which all private property in land places to agricultural production and the rational treatment, maintenance and improvement of the land itself, develops in various different forms, and in quarrelling over these specific forms of the evil its ultimate root is forgotten . 2.5.21 The Rift in the Social Metabolism Small-scale landownership presupposes that the overwhelming majority of the population is agricultural and that isolated labour predominates over social ; wealth and the development of reproduction, therefore, both in its material and its intellectual aspects, is ruled out under these circumstances, and with this also the conditions for a rational agriculture. On the other hand, large landed property reduces the agricultural population to an ever decreasing minimum and confronts it with an ever growing industrial population crammed together in large towns; in this way it produces conditions that provoke an irreparable rift in the interdependent process of social metabolism , a metabolism prescribed by the natural laws of life itself. The result of this is a squandering of the vitality of the soil , which is carried by trade far beyond the bounds of a single country. (Liebig.) 2.5.22 The Final Dialectic If small-scale landownership creates a class of barbarians standing half outside society , combining all the crudity of primitive social forms with all the torments and misery of civilized countries, large landed property undermines labour-power in the final sphere to which its indigenous energy flees , and where it is stored up as a reserve fund for renewing the vital power of the nation, on the land itself. Large-scale industry and industrially pursued large-scale agriculture have the same effect. If they are originally distinguished by the fact that the former lays waste and ruins labour-power and thus the natural power of man, whereas the latter does the same to the natural power of the soil , they link up in the later course of development, since the industrial system applied to agriculture also enervates the workers there, while industry and trade for their part provide agriculture with the means of exhausting the soil. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 3.1 Fundamental Principles and Systemic Laws First Principle: Rent Is a Social Relation, Not a Natural Fact. The chapter's most fundamental lesson is that ground-rent is not a payment for the natural productivity of the soil but a form of surplus-value arising from historically specific relations of production. The natural fertility of the land is a precondition of surplus labour — there must be enough productivity to generate a surplus over subsistence — but it is never the cause of rent. Only social compulsion, whether extra-economic (in pre-capitalist forms) or economic (under capitalism), transforms the possibility of surplus into the actuality of rent. This is why Marx insists that to explain rent by natural productivity \"would be to renounce the very concept of value itself.\" Second Principle: The Form of Surplus Extraction Determines the Social Formation. Marx's statement that \"the specific economic form in which unpaid surplus labour is pumped out of the direct producers determines the relationship of domination and servitude\" and that this is \"the innermost secret, the hidden basis of the entire social edifice\" is a compact statement of the materialist theory of history. The four forms of rent — labour rent, rent in kind, money rent, capitalist rent — correspond to four different configurations of class relations, political forms, and social development. The genealogy of rent is thus also the genealogy of European (and Asian) social formations. Third Principle: Historical Categories Are Inverted as the Mode of Production Changes. In labour rent, rent determines profit (profit is the residual after rent). In capitalist rent, profit determines rent (rent is the excess over average profit). This inversion is not a semantic quibble but a fundamental change in causal structure. It means that the same word — \"rent\" — denotes radically different social relations at different historical stages. The failure to recognize this is the source of the \"endless confusion\" Marx criticizes in the \"new\" explanations of rent. Fourth Principle: The Average Profit Is a Historical Achievement, Not a Natural Datum. The general rate of profit presupposes developed competition, mobility of capital, a fully formed labour market, and the subsumption of production under capital. It is, in Marx's words, \"a product, formed by a process of social life proceeding under quite particular historical relations of production.\" This means that rent as an excess over average profit — the \"modern\" form of rent — can only exist where capitalism is fully developed. To speak of rent in pre-capitalist conditions is to commit a category error. Fifth Principle: Private Property in Land Is a Barrier to Rational Agriculture. Both small-scale and large-scale private landownership impede the rational, sustainable, and socially beneficial use of the land. Small-scale ownership lacks the resources and science for social productivity; large-scale ownership exploits the soil for the most rapid enrichment of a few. Both operate under the tyranny of market price. The result is the \"irreparable rift\" in the social metabolism — the disconnection between the extraction of nutrients from the soil and their return, leading to soil exhaustion, and the disconnection between town and country, leading to the degradation of both human labour-power and the land itself. The chapter's closing vision — of land as \"permanent communal property,\" the \"inalienable condition for the existence and reproduction of the chain of human generations\" — is a normative ideal, but it is grounded in a material analysis of the contradictions of private property. 3.2 Broader Theoretical Implications For the Theory of Value: The chapter demonstrates that a rigorous theory of value can account for rent without appealing to natural productivity, and that any concession to natural explanations would undermine the entire theory. The solution lies in the special conditions of agriculture (lower organic composition, the existence of landed property as a barrier) which allow it to retain a surplus above average profit. The chapter thus provides essential ammunition against Malthusian and Ricardian versions of rent theory, and against the \"vulgar\" natural rent doctrine. For Historical Materialism: The chapter is a masterclass in the materialist method. It shows how economic categories are historically specific, how the same category (rent) changes meaning across modes of production, and how the relations of production determine the political and ideological superstructure. The qualification that the same economic basis can display \"endless variations and gradations\" in its appearance guards against determinism and insists on the necessity of concrete empirical analysis. For the Theory of the State: The passage on the Asiatic state as \"supreme landlord\" where \"rent and tax coincide\" and \"sovereignty is landed property concentrated on a national scale\" is a crucial text for the theory of the state in pre-capitalist formations. It suggests that the state is not an institution standing above economic relations but is itself a form of landed property, a mechanism for the extraction of surplus labour. For Agrarian Political Economy: The chapter's analysis of the differentiation of the peasantry, the emergence of the capitalist farmer, the role of money rent in dissolving customary relations, and the contradictions of both small and large-scale landownership provides a foundational framework for understanding agrarian transformation. The critique of smallholding as a \"necessary transition point\" that nevertheless brings poverty, soil exhaustion, and the wastage of human labour, and the critique of large-scale agriculture as a system that \"enervates the workers\" and \"squanders the vitality of the soil,\" together constitute a powerful argument against both romantic agrarianism and industrial agriculture. 3.3 Philosophical and Practical Implications The Metabolism of Nature and Society: Marx's concept of the \"irreparable rift in the interdependent process of social metabolism\" is one of his most profound contributions, and this chapter is its most developed statement. Under capitalism, the social and natural metabolisms — the circulation of nutrients, the reproduction of labour-power, the maintenance of the soil — are broken apart by the separation of town and country, the extraction of nutrients from the soil for sale in distant markets, and the concentration of population in cities. The ecological crisis Marx anticipated here has become one of the defining problems of our own time. The Critique of Private Property: The chapter's conclusion that all criticism of either small-scale or large-scale landed property is \"ultimately reducible to criticism of private property as a barrier and obstacle to agriculture\" is a radical claim. It means that the problems of agriculture — soil exhaustion, rural poverty, the wastage of human labour, the dependence on market price — cannot be solved within the framework of private property, whether small or large. The alternative vision is \"a conscious and rational treatment of the land as permanent communal property.\" The Historical Specificity of Capitalism: The chapter shows that capitalism is not the natural or inevitable form of human society but a historically specific mode of production with determinate preconditions (the separation of the producer from the land, the emergence of free labour, the formation of the average rate of profit) and determinate limits (the barrier of private property in land to rational agriculture). The historical analysis of rent thus serves to denaturalize capitalism and to open the question of its overcoming. The Politics of Land Reform: Marx's analysis implies that land reform — whether the creation of small peasant property or the nationalization of land — cannot by itself resolve the contradictions of private property. Small peasant property is a \"necessary transition point\" that nevertheless brings poverty and degradation; large landed property brings the squandering of soil and labour-power. The lesson is that the question of landed property is inseparable from the broader question of the mode of production, and that a genuine solution requires the transformation of the social relations of production as a whole. The Method of Dialectical Analysis: The chapter is a model of dialectical method. It traces a historical development through its stages, showing how each stage contains the seeds of its dissolution, how the same category changes meaning across stages, and how the \"natural\" appearance of the final form conceals its historical genesis. It shows how the apparent problem — rent as an excess over profit — can only be solved by understanding the historical process through which it came to be. And it shows how theoretical errors (the natural rent doctrine) are rooted in historical anachronism — the projection of capitalist categories onto pre-capitalist conditions. This is dialectics not as a philosophical system but as a concrete method of historical and economic analysis, and it remains a model for critical social science today. The Unity of Economics and Ecology: Finally, the chapter's most enduring lesson may be that economics and ecology cannot be separated. The \"law\" of small-scale landowning — the progressive deterioration of the conditions of production — and the \"law\" of capitalist agriculture — the squandering of the vitality of the soil — are not two laws but one: the law of a mode of production that treats the land as an object of exploitation rather than as the material basis of human life. Marx's vision of land as \"permanent communal property\" is thus both an economic and an ecological vision, and the \"irreparable rift\" he diagnosed is both a social and a natural crisis. The chapter ends, appropriately, with the image of the land as the \"reserve fund for renewing the vital power of the nation\" — a fund that private property, in both its small and large forms, systematically squanders. To read Chapter 47 is to understand that the question of rent is ultimately the question of how humanity relates to the earth, and that the answer cannot be found within the framework of private property. A Final Note on Method and Legacy: Chapter 47 stands as one of Marx's most interdisciplinary texts — combining economic theory, historical sociology, political analysis, and ecological critique into a single, unified argument. Its genealogy of rent from labour rent through rent in kind and money rent to capitalist rent is a case study in historical materialism; its analysis of the \"Asiatic\" state as supreme landlord is a contribution to political theory; its closing pages on the metabolism of nature and society are a foundational text of ecological thought. The chapter's central insight — that rent, like capital itself, is a historical social relation and not a natural fact — remains as challenging and as necessary today as when it was written. For in an age of global land grabs, agribusiness expansion, soil degradation, and the deepening rift between town and country, Marx's analysis of the genesis of capitalist ground-rent has lost none of its analytical power or its political urgency. It teaches us that the land is not a commodity, that production is not merely an economic activity, and that the social metabolism between humanity and nature must be consciously, rationally, and collectively governed if it is to sustain \"the chain of human generations\" — a lesson that is, if anything, more vital now than it was in Marx's own time. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 46 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 48"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 48", "title": "Chapter 48", "subtitle": "The Trinity Formula", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 48/Chapter 48 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 16024, "read_time": "80 min read", "tags": ["The Trinity Formula & Class Relations", "Fetishism & Mystification of Capital"], "keywords": ["The Trinity Formula", "Capital-Interest, Land-Rent, Labour-Wages", "Total Mystification of the Mode of Production", "Religion of Everyday Life & Vulgar Economics", "Ghostly Personifications of Economic Categories", "Illusion of Material Agents Producing Revenue"], "text": "Chapter 48 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 16,002 words ⏱️ ~80 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 48: The Trinity Formula — An Exhaustive Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem This chapter is the terminal knot of Marx's Capital Volume 3, and it is written as a kind of intellectual autopsy. Its object of study is not capital, land, or labour as such, but a formula — a piece of ordinary economic common sense — and the way that formula systematically inverts the causal order of the capitalist production process. The formula is the one every economic agent of a capitalist society knows by heart: capital yields profit, land yields rent, labour yields wages . Three sources, three revenues, three classes. Marx's task is to demonstrate that this apparently innocent triple identity is \"a conflation of three things which is prima facie illegitimate,\" a \"uniform and symmetrical incongruity,\" and — in the chapter's most memorable phrase — an \"economic three-in-one\" that \"completes the mystification of the capitalist mode of production.\" The central thesis, stated flatly, is this: the trinity formula is the perfected epistemological form of the capitalist mode of production — the form of appearance ( Erscheinungsform ) in which the social relations of production become unrecognizable to the very agents who carry them out, and in which the essence of value (objectified social labour) is replaced by three quasi-natural, quasi-personal powers (capital, earth, labour) that supposedly create the value of the annual product out of themselves. The chapter's thesis is thus simultaneously a thesis about the logic of value, a thesis about the historical specificity of social forms, and a thesis about the ideological function of economic appearances. The Intellectual Objective Marx's objective is threefold, and the chapter's structure reflects it: Categorial critique. He must show that each of the three terms commits a logical offence. Land is a use-value, rent is a portion of value; you cannot place them in a proportional relationship. Capital is a sum of value whose formula \"capital–interest\" asserts that value is unequal to itself — \"4 = 5,\" \"100 shillings = 110 shillings.\" Labour–wages is an \"irrational expression,\" \"as irrational as a yellow logarithm,\" because it identifies the price of a thing (labour) with what is in fact the price of a capacity (labour-power). This is the \"dimensional analysis\" of the chapter. Historical critique. He must show that capital, landed property, and wage-labour are not natural but historically specific social forms belonging to one economic formation of society — and that the trinity's crime is to fuse the material elements of every labour process (earth, produced means of production, purposive activity) with their capitalist social form (monopolized earth, capital, wage-labour), so that the social form appears as a material character \"immanently ingrown,\" \"eternally so, as it were.\" Critique of ideology. He must show why this fusion is not merely an error but a necessary illusion, generated by the surface of the capitalist mode of production itself, deepening as the system develops, and why it is useful to the dominant classes: \"it preaches the natural necessity and perpetual justification of their sources of income and erects this into a dogma.\" The Broader Theoretical Framework The chapter is neither a standalone essay nor a mere appendix. It is the retrospective closure of Volume 3's entire movement. Volume 3 began with surplus-value metamorphosing into profit, explored the equalization of profit rates into average profit, transformed values into prices of production, subdivided profit into profit of enterprise and interest, and interpolated ground-rent and landed property as a further claim on surplus-value. Chapter 48 asks: once the whole itinerary has been traversed, what does the system look like from the standpoint of the agents inside it? The answer is the trinity formula. The chapter is thus the phenomenology of the completed system — the analysis of its surface as seen by its practitioners. Its counterpart in Volume 1 is the section on the fetishism of commodities. But the trinity formula is a higher-order fetishism: whereas the commodity fetish consists in a social relation appearing as a property of a thing (or, later, of money), the trinity fetish consists in social relations appearing as three heterogeneous natural powers with their own autonomous revenues , each personified: \"Monsieur le Capital and Madame la Terre.\" Marx states the continuity explicitly: all forms of society involving commodity production are subject to the original distortion, \"but in the capitalist mode of production, where capital is the dominant category... this bewitched and distorted world develops much further.\" The trinity is the endpoint of that development — the fetishism of the system as a whole. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Editorial Frame: A Chapter Assembled from Fragments Before a single argument is engaged, the reader must register something fundamental about the text's status. Footnote 48, appended by Engels, states: \"The following three fragments were found at various points in the manuscript of Part Six.\" Later, footnote 49 marks the sentence at which \"Chapter 48 begins in the manuscript.\" Two further editorial notes — \"(Here the manuscript breaks off. – F. E.)\" and \"(Here a folio sheet of the manuscript is missing. – F. E.)\" — punctuate the argument, while the chapter's final sentence breaks off mid-clause: \"Only in the capitalist mode of production ...\" This matters for three reasons. First, the chapter as published is a reconstruction . Engels took three prefatory fragments (numbered 1, 2, 3 in the text) that Marx had drafted for the manuscript of Part Six (\"The Revenues and Their Sources\") and placed them at the head of the chapter, then appended the main body that begins with the sentence about the capitalist production process as a historically specific form. The result is a chapter with a thematic spine but structural seams. Second, the lacunae are substantive . Where the manuscript breaks off after \"(2)\" in fragment one, we have just been promised a two-part argument about why natural fertility conditions the product but not the value; only the first part arrives. Where the folio sheet is missing, we jump abruptly from the distribution of the annual value into the middle of an argument about differential rent. The reader is thus invited to reconstruct what is missing by inference from what surrounds it — and Marx's argument is robust enough for the exercise. Third, the fragmentary character of the chapter is thematically apt. The trinity formula is itself a set of fragments — three disconnected pieces of economic common sense held together by nothing but the fact that the agents of production repeat them daily. Marx's analytical task is to show that what appears as a coherent \"three-in-one\" is in fact an assemblage of incommensurables; the text's own seams silently mirror its argument. 2.2 Fragment One, Part A: The Formula and Its Reduction to a Second Trinity The chapter opens by throwing down the formula in full: \"Capital-profit (profit of enterprise plus interest), land-ground-rent, labour-wages, this trinity form holds in itself all the mysteries of the social production process.\" Two things happen in this opening sentence. First, the formula is decomposed : \"profit\" is not a unity but a composite — profit of enterprise plus interest . This is not a minor refinement; it is the hinge on which the first, covert reduction of the chapter depends. Marx cites his earlier discussion (\"See Chapter 23 above\" — the chapter on \"Interest and Profit of Enterprise\") where he showed that interest presents itself as the specific and characteristic product of capital as such , whereas profit of enterprise appears, by contrast, as a wage independent of capital — the \"wage of superintendence,\" the payment for the capitalist's own labour of managing and exploiting. The consequence is devastating for the formula's apparent rationality. Because interest is the incarnation of capital-as-such, and profit of enterprise is a disguised wage, the first trinity can be rewritten in a purer — Marx says \"fortunately\" — form: \"Capital-interest, land-ground-rent, labour-wages, where profit, the form of surplus-value specifically characteristic to the capitalist mode of production, is fortunately set aside.\" The word \"fortunately\" drips with irony, and the irony is precise. \"Profitable\" as a term still carries a memory of its origin : profit emerges from surplus-value, which emerges from the exploitation of labour. Under the heading \"profit,\" the trinity's first term is still flecked with its actual history. Under \"interest,\" that memory is erased entirely: interest-bearing capital is that form in which value appears to generate value out of itself , without labour, without production, without process — the \"capital fetish\" in its most estranged figure. The vulgar economist therefore prefers the second formula; it is \"much more consistent than 'capital-profit,' since profit still retains a memory of its origin which in interest is not simply obliterated but actually placed in a form diametrically opposed to this origin.\" We will see this argument returned to, amplified and applied, near the chapter's close. 2.3 Fragment One, Part B: The Three \"Sources\" Are Not One Kind of Thing Marx next performs what is effectively a categorial dissection. Each of the three supposed sources is interrogated as to what it actually is. Capital. \"Capital is not a thing, it is a definite social relation of production pertaining to a particular historical social formation, which simply takes the form of a thing and gives this thing a specific social character.\" The point needs to be handled carefully, because Marx is neither denying that capital exists as objects nor lapsing into nominalism. He is making a claim about what kind of entity capital is . There is a physical side — \"the material and produced means of production\" — and a social side — the specific relation of production. The trinity formula's error consists in identifying the two. Marx's positive account is worth unpacking term by term: capital is \"the means of production monopolized by a particular section of society, the products and conditions of activity of labour-power, which are rendered autonomous vis-à-vis this living labour-power and are personified in capital through this antithesis.\" Three ingredients are welded together here: (a) monopoly — a particular section of society has exclusive control; (b) autonomy/alienation — the products and conditions of labour face the living worker as an independent power, \"masters and buyers of their producers\"; (c) personification — through this antithesis, capital acquires a personal representative. The famous sentence that follows extends this beyond commodity fetishism: not only do the products become independent powers over the producers, \"but the social powers and interconnecting form of this labour also confront them as properties of their product.\" Marx's warning — \"at first sight a very mysterious form\" — signposts that \"at first sight\" is doing work: the mystery is a real appearance, not a gratuitous obscurity. Land. \"And now to take land, inorganic nature as such, rudis indigeslaque moles in its primeval wilderness.\" The Latin citation from Ovid's Metamorphoses (Book I, 7) invokes the chaos before creation — the formless mass out of which the ordered cosmos was made. Land is not merely unproduced; it precedes production as its given arena. Against this primeval mass Marx lays the analytic axe: \"Value is labour. So surplus-value cannot be earth.\" The argument unfolds in three steps. (1) The land's absolute fertility produces no value; it merely sets the ratio in which a given quantum of labour yields a given product, under the given natural conditions. (2) Relative fertility — differences in natural conditions — means the same amounts of labour and capital are expressed in different quantities of product, so that these products have different individual values (their per-unit labour content differs). (3) The equalization of these individual values into market values — the competitive formation of a single social standard — is the mechanism by which the superior fertility's advantage is transferred to the landlord rather than captured by the cultivator. This is the germ of the theory of differential rent , and Marx anchors it in a quotation from Ricardo's Principles (Pelican edition, p. 98): \"the advantages of fertile over inferior lands are ... transferred from the cultivator, or consumer, to the landlord.\" Note the analytic pressure this exerts on the trinity formula. Land can determine how much product a given quantum of labour-realized time yields, but it can never determine how much value the product bears. Land conditions use-value; value is a social determination. Yet the trinity formula asserts that land — a use-value — is a source of value, and specifically of the value-component called rent. This is the category error that will be repeated with devastating precision in fragment three. Labour. \"Lastly, as the third in the league, a mere spectre — labour, which is nothing but an abstraction and taken by itself cannot exist at all.\" The accusation here is subtler than it first appears. Marx concedes that labour can mean \"the entire productive activity of man, through which his metabolic interchange with nature is mediated\" ( Stoffwechsel — the material metabolism with nature). But even in this sense, \"it is only divested of any social form and specific character; even in its mere natural existence, independent of society, it is lifted right out of society altogether.\" The abstraction of \"labour\" in general — labour as such, present in all epochs, \"externalization and confirmation of life\" alike for the unsocial man and for man socialized in some form — is a Robinsonade : a thought-experiment false to every historical reality. The phrase \"externalization and confirmation of life\" ( Entäußerung und Bewährung des Lebens ) carries a deliberate philosophical charge: it is the Hegelian-Feuerbachian vocabulary of self-realization, here deployed with a corrosive irony because what the trinity formula calls \"labour\" is an abstraction stripped not merely of society but of the very flesh of any particular labouring body. The three sources of the trinity, therefore, are of three radically heterogeneous kinds: capital is a social relation masquerading as a thing; land is a material thing without any relation to value at all ; labour is a theoretical abstraction that no society has ever contained. Marx's metaphor for their mutual relation is exact: \"Their mutual relationship is like that of lawyer's fees, beetroot and music.\" 2.4 Fragment Two: The Historicity of the Three Forms and the Foundational Category Mistake Fragment two (starting \"2\") restates the formula in its historicized form: \"Capital-interest; landed property, private property in the earth, and indeed modern private property, corresponding to the capitalist mode of production — rent; wage-labour — wages of labour.\" The point of the restatement is that the middle term has changed register . It is no longer \"land\" but \"landed property\" — not earth as physical substance but a specific, historical, legal-economic institution. And the chapter immediately flags what is at stake in this shift: \"Wage-labour and landed property, like capital, are historically specific social forms; one of labour, and the other of the monopolized earth, both in fact being forms corresponding to capital and belonging to the same economic formation of society.\" This is the chapter's foundational proposition. The three terms are co-eval : they arise together, they presuppose one another, and they belong to one another. Their historical unity is the capitalist mode of production. And yet — the first striking thing about the formula is that it does not respect this unity: \"The first striking thing about this formula is that alongside capital, this form of an element of production belonging to a specific mode of production, to a specific historical shape of the social production process, alongside an element of production amalgamated with and presented in a specific social form, we have ranked without further ado: the earth, on the one hand, labour on the other, two elements of the actual labour process, which are material elements of any process of production and have nothing to do with its social forms.\" This is the categorial error in its clearest form: the formula places a social-historical determination (capital) side by side with two trans-historical material determinations (earth, labour) and declares them homogeneous. The two sides are not merely different — they are of different logical types. Capital belongs to the relations of production ; earth and labour belong to the process of production in abstraction from relations. Placing them in a common formula is like placing a contract and a stone in a common equation. Second striking thing: the causal grammar . The formula does not merely list; it asserts derivation. Each source \"is related to its product as something extruded from it and produced by it.\" Capital–interest, earth–rent, labour–wages — the dash marks causation. Each term is a cause, each product an effect, each \"fruits\" or \"the result\" of its own wellspring. The three revenue-forms — interest, rent, wages — are all portions of value , and hence of price when money is the medium. And here lies the difficulty: how can the earth produce a portion of value ? 2.5 Fragment Two, Continued: Why the Earth Cannot Create Value \"Capital-interest is certainly the most irrational formula for capital, but it is a formula for it.\" Marx grants the first term a certain minimal propriety: interest is at least of capital, however mystified its genesis. He does not grant the second term any such propriety: \"But how is the earth to have a value, how can it create a socially specific quantum of labour, and the particular portion of value of its own products that forms rent at that?\" The argument that follows is one of the sharpest passages of value-theoretical clarification in the whole of Capital . Marx grants the land everything as a use-value producer: \"The earth, for example, is active as an agent of production in the production of a use-value, a material product, say wheat.\" But: \"it has nothing to do with producing the value of the wheat.\" Why? Because value is defined as objectified social labour , \"this labour being quite indifferent to the particular material in which it is expressed or to the particular use-value of this material.\" Marx then anticipates an objection that a naive reader would raise — namely, that if land is more fertile we get more wheat, and doesn't more wheat mean more value? He answers carefully: \"It does not contradict this that (1) if other factors remain constant, whether wheat is cheap or dear depends on the earth's productivity. The productivity of agricultural labour is linked to natural conditions, and according to their productivity the same quantum of labour is expressed in more products or fewer, more or fewer use-values.\" The distinction is between how much product a quantum of labour is embodied in and how much value that quantum of labour is. Natural productivity governs the first magnitude; it has no purchase on the second. To put it in Marx's formula: \"The quantity of product that the value represents depends here on the earth's productivity; but this value is given, and is independent of this distribution.\" There follows a summary principle that does a great deal of work in the chapter: \"Value is expressed in use-value, and use-value is a condition for the creation of value.\" The relation is asymmetric : use-value is necessary for value to appear, but it is not a source of value. From this asymmetry Marx derives the illegitimacy of the trinity: \"it is foolish to counterpose a use-value, the earth, on the one hand, and value on the other, and a particular portion of value at that.\" The parenthetical \"(2)\" — the second half of the argument — is precisely what the manuscript loses. We are left with the promise of a second consideration cut off before we can hear it. 2.6 Fragment Three: Vulgar Economics and the Epistemology of Appearance Fragment three (starting \"3\") opens with the chapter's most-quoted aphorism and its philosophical pivot: \"Vulgar economics actually does nothing more than interpret, systematize and turn into apologetics the notions of agents trapped within bourgeois relations of production. So it should not surprise us that precisely in the estranged form of appearance of economic relations ... that precisely here vulgar economics feels completely at home, these relationships appearing all the more self-evident to it, the more their inner connections remain hidden, even though they are comprehensible to the popular mind.\" Underpinning this is the methodological principle that underpins all of Capital Volume 3: \"— and all science would be superfluous if the form of appearance of things directly coincided with their essence.\" This is the chapter's philosophical manifesto. The distinction between appearance ( Erscheinung ) and essence ( Wesen ) is not a distinction between false opinion and truth in the ordinary sense. It is a distinction between two levels of the same reality , where the second level is systematically generated by the first and cannot be abolished by the mere observational acumen of the agents within the first. The trinity formula is not a mistake a competent economist could simply correct; it is the necessary surface of a system whose depth only scientific analysis can reconstruct. And crucially, the surface's growing self-evidence is proportional to its growing distance from the depth: the more the inner connections are hidden, the more obvious the appearance seems. 2.7 Fragment Three, Continued: The Three Absurdities Dissected Marx now walks through the three terms of the trinity and shows that each is absurd — and that its absurdity is precisely its apologetic utility. The trinity is \"a conflation of three things which is prima facie illegitimate.\" (i) Land–rent: the incommensurable pair. \"First we have the use-value land, which has no value, and the exchange-value rent; here, then, a social relation, conceived as a thing, is placed in a relationship of proportion with nature; i.e. two incommensurable magnitudes are supposed to have a proportionate ratio.\" Two moves are being combined: the thingification of a social relation (rent, which is a magnitude of value, is treated as if it were a property of the earth) and the proportionalization of the incommensurable (a physical fact and a social magnitude are declared to stand in a determinate ratio). This is the sharpest expression of the categorial error latent in the whole chapter. (ii) Capital–interest: the value that exceeds itself. \"If capital is conceived as a certain sum of value with its independent expression in money, it is prima facie nonsense that a value should have more value than it is worth.\" The formula's logic is a formal contradiction — Marx gives it arithmetical dress: \"the feeling that 4 is not 5 and hence 100 shillings cannot possibly be 110 shillings.\" But this is not the trivial observation that investing money can yield a return; it is the more radical observation that in the form \"capital–interest,\" all mediation has disappeared . There is no labour, no time, no process — only a value that begets more value, \"value-creating value.\" Marx notes that this very absence of mediation is what makes the formula \"the most general formula\" and — for the same reason — \"absurd and inexplicable in its own terms.\" The vulgar economist's preference for it is therefore no accident: \"With its occult quality of a value that is to be unequal to itself,\" capital–interest is preferred \"to the formula capital–profit, as here we already get somewhat nearer to the actual capital-relation.\" The occultism is the selling point. But — and this is a fine dialectical touch — the vulgar economist cannot even rest in this occultism. \"Disturbed by the feeling that 4 is not 5,\" he flees from capital as value to the material substance of capital — machinery, raw material, etc. — \"Instead of the incomprehensible first relationship in which 4 = 5, to construct this time a completely incommensurable relationship between a use-value, a thing, on the one hand, and a specific social relation of production, surplus-value, on the other; as in the case of landed property.\" The economist's flight from one absurdity only leads him into a structurally identical absurdity on the other side of the ledger. He swaps an internal contradiction of value for an incommensurability between value and matter — and mistakes the swap for progress. \"As soon as this incommensurability is attained, everything becomes clear to the vulgar economist, and he feels no need for any further reflection. For he has precisely reached what is 'rational' to the bourgeois mind.\" (iii) Labour–wages: the \"yellow logarithm.\" \"Finally, labour–wages, the price of labour, is an expression ... which prima facie contradicts the concept of value and equally therefore that of price, this being in general only a specific expression of value; and 'price of labour' is just as irrational as a yellow logarithm.\" The comparison with a yellow logarithm is precisely calibrated: a logarithm can be computed, manipulated, used; what it cannot be is coloured. To speak of a coloured logarithm is not to say something false; it is to combine two categories — number and colour — that admit no common predicate. So with the price of labour: prices are of things; labour is not a thing; what is sold is labour-power. The very grammar of \"wage\" involves a category conflation. The vulgar economist, however, is \"completely satisfied here, since he has now reached the profound insight of the bourgeois that he pays money for labour, and the very contradiction between this formula and the concept of value relieves him from the obligation of understanding the latter.\" This is one of the most cutting sentences in the chapter. The contradiction that ought to trigger analysis is instead the licence to stop analysing: precisely because the formula \"price of labour\" cannot be squared with the theory of value, the vulgar economist treats the theory of value as the disposable term. The thought-stopping function of the absurdity is not a defect of the vulgar economist's method; it is his method. At this point the third fragment ends, and the main body of Chapter 48 begins. 2.8 The Main Body Begins: The Capitalist Production Process as Historically Specific Social Form The chapter's central text commences with a definitional statement of what the capitalist production process is : \"We have seen how the capitalist process of production is a historically specific form of the social production process in general. This last is both a production process of the material conditions of existence for human life, and a process, proceeding in specific economic and historical relations of production, that produces and reproduces these relations of production themselves, and with them the bearers of this process, their material conditions of existence, and their mutual relationships, i.e. the specific economic form of their society.\" The definition contains a double-barrelled claim that is indispensable for everything that follows. (1) Production has a material side : it produces the means of life. (2) Production has a social side : it reproduces the relations of production and their bearers (the classes and their functionaries). These two sides are analytically distinct — and their fusion is precisely the mirage of the trinity formula. But they are also really inseparable in any actual society: \"For the totality of these relationships which the bearers of this production have towards nature and one another, the relationships in which they produce, is precisely society, viewed according to its economic structure.\" Marx then makes his crucial methodological point about circular causality : \"Those conditions, like these social relations, are on the one hand the presuppositions of the capitalist production process, on the other its results and creations; they are both produced by it and reproduced by it.\" This is the famous Voraussetzung/Resultat structure: the material and social conditions of capitalist production are simultaneously the premises from which it starts and the outcome it produces. This is exactly why the trinity formula is so hard to see through: the conditions seem given in advance — capital appears as a pre-existing stock of means of production; wage-labour as a pre-existing condition of the workers; landed property as a pre-existing feature of the earth. But each of these \"givens\" is in fact a product of the same process it seems to precede. The trinity formula reads off the premise side and suppresses the result side — turning what is historically produced into what is naturally given. The section closes with the capitalist as \"personified capital\": \"the capitalist is simply personified capital, functioning in the production process simply as the bearer of capital.\" The personification theme — which will culminate in \"Monsieur le Capital\" — is here introduced. Capital is a social relation; the capitalist is the person who carries it. 2.9 Surplus Labour, Forced Labour, and the Civilizing Vocation of Capital Marx now states the fundamental operation of capitalist production: \"Capital ... pumps out a certain specific quantum of surplus labour from the direct producers or workers, surplus labour that it receives without an equivalent and which by its very nature always remains forced labour, however much it might appear as the result of free contractual agreement.\" Two things to note. First, \"pumps out\" ( auspumpt ) is a deliberately mechanical, extractive metaphor — the same figure that will return at the chapter's end as \"a perpetual pumping machine for surplus labour.\" Second, the phrase \"however much it might appear as the result of free contractual agreement\" is the moment of demystification. The contract is free in form and forced in substance; the appearance of freedom is itself a part of the mechanism. Marx then embeds the concept of surplus labour in a trans-historical framework, refusing the temptation to treat it as a capitalist peculiarity: \"Surplus labour in some form must always remain, as labour beyond the extent of given needs. It is just that in the capitalist, as in the slave system, etc., it has an antagonistic form and its obverse side is pure idleness on the part of one section of society.\" The class-antagonistic form is historically specific; the existence of surplus labour is not. It is required (a) \"as insurance against accidents\" and (b) \"for the progressive extension of the reproduction process that is needed to keep pace with the development of needs and the progress of population.\" This trans-historical anchor permits what is perhaps the chapter's most surprising passage: a positive assessment of capital's historical mission. \"It is one of the civilizing aspects of capital that it extorts this surplus labour in a manner and in conditions that are more advantageous to social relations and to the creation of elements for a new and higher formation than was the case under the earlier forms of slavery, serfdom, etc.\" The sentence contains, in embryo, the dialectic on which the trinity formula's critique ultimately depends. Capital is extractive and exploitative, but it is civilizing in a determinate sense: it develops productive forces, generalizes needs, and creates the material preconditions of a higher society. And Marx immediately draws the consequence: \"Thus on the one hand it leads towards a stage at which compulsion and the monopolization of social development ... by one section of society at the expense of another disappears; on the other hand it creates the material means and the nucleus for relations that permit this surplus labour to be combined, in a higher form of society, with a greater reduction of the overall time devoted to material labour.\" 2.10 The Arithmetic of Exploitation and the Realm of Freedom The civlizing-aspect argument is made rigorous with a piece of arithmetic: \"For, according to the development of labour productivity, surplus labour can be great when the total working day is short and relatively small when the total working day is long. If the necessary labour-time is 3 hours and surplus labour also 3 hours, the total working day is 6 hours and the rate of surplus labour 100 per cent. If the necessary labour is 9 hours and the surplus labour 3 hours, the total working day is 12 hours and the rate of surplus labour only 33⅓ per cent.\" The example establishes that the rate of surplus-labour is a ratio, not an absolute magnitude . The same three hours of surplus labour, in a system with three hours of necessary labour, yields a 100% rate (3/3); in a system with nine hours of necessary labour, the same three surplus hours yield only 33⅓% (3/9). Consequently, the length of the working day relative to necessary labour is determined by productivity, not by the \"extractiveness\" of the system as such. And: \"It then depends on the productivity of labour how much use-value is produced in a given time, and also therefore in a given surplus labour-time. The real wealth of society and the possibility of a constant expansion of its reproduction process does not depend on the length of surplus labour but rather on its productivity and on the more or less plentiful conditions of production in which it is performed.\" What is at stake in this argument for the trinity formula? Everything. The formula's implicit story is that surplus (interest, rent) is the product of capital and land — natural, inevitable, permanent. Marx's argument demystifies: surplus labour is the permanent and inevitable thing (given need and the extension of reproduction); its quantum depends on productivity; and the form under which it is appropriated — the split into profits and rents — is historical. By shifting the question from the magnitude of surplus labour to its productivity , Marx lays the ground for a non-moralistic normative claim: not that the system should stop extracting surplus, but that the realm of freedom lies beyond the sphere of necessity and becomes possible once the development of productive forces allows the reduction of the working day. This is the celebrated passage on the two realms. It deserves careful unpacking because its placement here — in the middle of the chapter, at the hinge between the analysis of surplus labour and the analysis of distribution — is intentional, not decorative. \"The realm of freedom really begins only where labour determined by necessity and external expediency ends; it lies by its very nature beyond the sphere of material production proper. Just as the savage must wrestle with nature to satisfy his needs, to maintain and reproduce his life, so must civilized man, and he must do so in all forms of society and under all possible modes of production. This realm of natural necessity expands with his development, because his needs do too; but the productive forces to satisfy these expand at the same time.\" The argument is naturalistic (all societies wrestle with nature) and dynamic (needs expand, forces expand). The realm of necessity is not abolished by history; it is enlarged by need and opened up by productivity. Freedom within necessity is then defined: \"Freedom, in this sphere, can consist only in this, that socialized man, the associated producers, govern the human metabolism with nature in a rational way, bringing it under their collective control instead of being dominated by it as a blind power; accomplishing it with the least expenditure of energy and in conditions most worthy and appropriate for their human nature.\" And freedom beyond necessity : \"But this always remains a realm of necessity. The true realm of freedom, the development of human powers as an end in itself, begins beyond it, though it can only flourish with this realm of necessity as its basis. The reduction of the working day is the basic prerequisite.\" The structure of these paragraphs dramatizes what the trinity formula occludes. The formula pictures capital, land, and labour as three autonomous sources of wealth, with the labour term supplying only the worker's subsistence-wage. Marx's picture is opposite: material production is a necessary base; its historical development in capitalism is what makes a genuine freedom possible; and the pivot from one to the other is not a moral conversion but a time question — the shortening of the working day. The trinity formula, by presenting the wage as the natural product of the labour-source, erases the whole question of the surplus-labour-time produced beyond the wage — the very surplus whose duration, rate, and appropriation are at stake. 2.11 The Skeleton of Distribution: Profit, Rent, Wages Marx now turns to the distributive mechanism that concretely produces the trinity's three revenue-forms out of the already-produced surplus-value: \"In capitalist society, this surplus-value or surplus product is divided among the capitalists as dividends in proportion to the quota of social capital that belongs to each. (If we ignore accidental fluctuations in the distribution and consider simply the law governing them, their regulating limits.)\" The parenthetical is methodologically important. Marx is not describing the actual scatter of profits (which are subject to accident, competition, conjunctural luck) but the law of distribution — what he will later call the \"ideal average.\" The \"regulating limits\" are the gravitational center around which the accidental movements oscillate. Next: surplus-value \"appears as the average profit that accrues to capital, an average profit that is divided again into profit of enterprise and interest and can accrue under these two categories to different sorts of capitalist.\" Distribution among capitals is presupposed (the theory of the average rate of profit from Parts II–III); sub-division into profit of enterprise and interest is presupposed (Chapter 23). Then a barrier is erected: \"This appropriation and distribution of surplus-value or surplus product by capital, however, meets with a barrier in landed property. Just as the functioning capitalist pumps out surplus labour from the worker, and thus surplus-value and surplus product in the form of profit, so the landowner pumps out a part of this surplus-value or surplus profit in turn from the capitalist in the form of rent, according to the laws developed earlier.\" Note the successive pumping structure: the capitalist pumps surplus labour from the worker; the landowner pumps part of surplus-value from the capitalist. Marx then makes terminological precision a condition of understanding: \"If we speak here therefore of profit as the share of surplus-value accruing to capital, what we mean is an average profit (equal to profit of enterprise plus interest) that is already less than the total profit by the deduction of rent; the deduction of rent is presupposed.\" This is a lovely piece of internal precision. In the trinity formula, \"profit\" and \"rent\" seem co-original — two fruits from two trees. In Marx's analysis, rent is a deduction from what capital has already extracted; profit is a residue after that deduction. The two revenues are not parallel; they are sequential. The trinity's parallelism is an illusion produced by the simultaneity of their appearance on the surface. The class-mapping follows: \"Capital-profit (profit of enterprise plus interest) and ground-rent are thus nothing but particular components of the surplus-value; categories in which this surplus-value is distinguished according to whether it accrues to capital or landed property; designations which in no way affect its essence. Added together, they form the total social surplus-value.\" Then the division of roles is drawn sharply: Capital : \"directly pumps from the workers the surplus labour that is expressed in surplus-value and surplus product. It can be considered in this sense as the producer of surplus-value.\" (Note the careful phrasing: \"in this sense.\" Marx does not grant capital substantive productive power; he grants it the function of pumping out surplus-value — the sense in which it can be considered a producer.) Landed property : \"has nothing to do with the actual production process. Its role is limited to transferring a part of the surplus-value produced from capital's pocket into its own.\" Yet even this parasitism has a real social condition: \"not only by the pressure that he exerts on capital and not simply by the fact that large landed property is a premise and condition of capitalist production, but particularly by the way that he appears as the personification of one of the most essential conditions of production.\" The landowner, whatever his uselessness, is personified earth — the bearer of a condition without which nothing could be produced. The worker : \"as owner and seller of his personal labour-power, receives under the name of wages a part of the product; in this there is expressed the portion of his labour that we call necessary labour, i.e. labour necessary for the maintenance and reproduction of this labour-power, whether the conditions of this maintenance and reproduction are poorer or richer, more favourable or less.\" The section ends with a passage that captures the surface appearance at its most beguiling — one that must be quoted in full: \"Disparate as these relations may now appear, they have one thing in common: capital yields the capitalist profit, year in year out: land yields the landowner ground-rent; and labour-power — under normal conditions, and as long as it remains a usable labour-power — yields the worker wages. These three components of the total value annually produced ... can be consumed by their respective owners each year, and the sources of their reproduction will not run dry. (We leave accumulation aside here at first.) They appear as fruits of a perennial tree for annual consumption, or rather fruits of three trees; they constitute the annual incomes of three classes ... revenues distributed by the functioning capitalist, as the person who directly pumps out surplus labour and makes use of labour in general.\" The \"perennial tree\" image is exquisitely chosen. It captures the appearance of natural, self-renewing productivity: the tree yields fruit, the fruit is consumed, the tree yields again, without visible input or history. But Marx has already told us, three pages earlier, that this \"perennial\" yield depends entirely on the continuous pumping of surplus labour — an operation wholly invisible to the trinity's tree-metaphor. The chapter then names the three \"sources\" in their trinitarian form and shows them to be the personified media of exploitation: \"Capital to the capitalist, land to the landowner and labour-power to the worker, or rather his labour itself — since he sells labour-power only in its actual externalization, and the price of labour-power, as already shown, is necessarily expressed on the basis of the capitalist mode of production as the price of labour — these appear as the three respective sources of their specific revenues: profit, ground-rent and wages.\" And they actually are so — but only in a very specific, non-trinitarian sense: \"And they actually are so in the sense that capital for the capitalist is a perpetual pumping machine for surplus labour, land for the landowner a permanent magnet for attracting a part of the surplus-value pumped out by capital and finally labour the constantly self-renewing condition and means for the worker to obtain a part of the value he has produced...\" The three \"sources,\" then, are not creative. They are, respectively, a machine , a magnet , and a condition . Capital does not create profit; it draws it out of labour. Land does not create rent; it draws off a portion of what capital has drawn. Labour does not create the worker's wage as some kind of fruit; it is the mere condition under which the worker can reclaim a portion of what he has himself produced. The nomenclature is fateful: the trinity's causal language (\"source,\" \"cause,\" \"produces\") is being systematically dismantled and replaced by a mechanical language of pumping, attracting, and conditioned recovery. 2.12 The Subjective Illusion: \"Sources of Revenue\" Become \"Real Sources\" The chapter now makes its crucial epistemological distinction. The three do function as sources of revenue in a legitimate sense: \"They are also sources of revenue in the sense that capital fixes one portion of the value of a year's labour and hence of its product in the form of profit, landed property fixes another part in the form of rent and wage-labour a third portion in the form of wages, and that it is precisely by this transformation that these portions are converted into the revenues of the capitalist, the landowner and the worker, without creating the substance itself that is transformed into these various categories. The distribution rather presupposes this substance as already present, i.e. the total value of the annual product, which is nothing more than objectified social labour.\" Here is the philosophical nucleus of the chapter. There are two senses of \"source of revenue,\" and the trinity formula conflates them: Legitimate sense : the existing value is channeled into profit, rent, and wages. Distribution operates upon an already-given quantum of objectified social labour. The three classes are distributive claims representing portions of this quantum. Illegitimate sense : the existing value originates from the three sources. Distribution is mistaken for creation. The trinity formula takes the distribution of the value to be its genesis . The second sense is the formula proper, and Marx identifies it as the illusion of the agents: \"But it is not in this form that the matter presents itself to the agents of production, the bearers of the various functions of the production process, but rather in a distorted form. Why this happens we shall see in the further course of our analysis. Capital, landed property and labour appear to those agents of production as three separate and independent sources, and it appears that from these there arise three different components of the annually produced value (and hence of the product in which this exists); from these sources, therefore, there arise not only the different forms of this value as revenues which accrue to particular factors of the social production process, but this value itself arises, and with it the substance of these forms of revenue.\" The clause \"Why this happens we shall see in the further course of our analysis\" is the promise that the second half of the chapter fulfils — the account of how, step by step, the inner connection is lost. At this point we reach the editorial lacuna. 2.13 The Missing Folio and the Social Character of Differential Rent \"(Here a folio sheet of the manuscript is missing. – F. E.)\" When the text resumes, we are in the middle of a discussion of differential rent — evidently, the missing sheet bridged from the general distribution of surplus-value to the rent analysis of Part Six. The surviving argument concerns the heterogeneous grounds of differential rent: On one side: \"Differential rent is bound up with the relative fertility of different land, i.e. with properties that arise from the land as such. But in as much as it depends firstly on the differing individual values of the products of different types of land, it is simply the characteristic already mentioned.\" On the other side: \"in as much as it depends, secondly, on the governing general market price, which is different from these individual values, this is a social law brought about by competition which has nothing to do either with the land or with the various degrees of its fertility.\" This is the culminating refutation of the land–rent trinity term, and its structure repays attention. Differential rent is the quantitative outcome of a social mechanism : natural fertility produces differing individual labour-times; competition produces a single market price ; the difference is captured by the landlord. Nature enters only at the level of individual differences in use-value per unit labour — it determines the raw material of the rent phenomenon, so to speak. The law that converts those differences into a rent paid to the landowner is a law of the market, a social law. The trinity formula takes the natural half of this dual determination and treats it as the whole. 2.14 Labour–Wages: The Rationality That Isn't Marx next examines the third term. \"It might appear as if at least in 'labour–wages' a rational relationship was expressed.\" After all, labour is the substance of value; wages are a portion of value; the connection seems at first glance sound. Marx denies it: \"But this is just as little the case as with 'land-ground-rent'. In as much as labour is value-forming and is expressed in the value of commodities, it has nothing to do with the distribution of this value among the different categories. And as far as its specific social character as wage-labour goes, it is not this that is value-forming.\" The argument moves by a two-way exclusion. Take labour as value-forming : labour-as-value-forming has nothing to do with distribution. Take labour as wage-labour : the social form of wage-labour is precisely not what forms value. The two characterizations, value-former and wage-labour, are mutually exclusive in the relevant respect. Hence \"labour–wages\" cannot capture the real relation, however satisfying it may feel. Marx then repeats his basic point: wages are \"an irrational expression for the value or price of labour-power.\" And crucially: \"the particular social conditions in which this labour-power is sold have no bearing on labour as a general agent of production.\" Then: \"Labour is objectified also in that value component of the commodity that forms the price of labour-power, as wages; it creates this portion just as much as it does the other portions of the product; but it is objectified in this portion only in precisely the same way as in the portions that form rent or profit.\" The last sentence is quietly devastating. Labour is objectified in all three portions: in wages, in profit, in rent. Labour is the substance of the whole value of the product. But this is not a special relationship between labour and wages : it is a universal relationship between labour and all portions of value. To single out labour–wages as a \"rational relationship\" while treating profit and rent as fruits of other sources is to mistake a universal for a particular. \"When we have labour as value-forming in mind, we are not considering it in its concrete form as a condition of production, but rather in a social characteristic that is different from that of wage-labour.\" 2.15 \"Capital–Profit\" Is Incorrect; the Entire Undivided Surplus-Value The parallel argument is then made for capital: \"Even the expression 'capital-profit' is incorrect here. If capital is conceived in the only connection in which it produces surplus-value, i.e. in its relationship to labour, in which it extorts surplus labour by the compulsion it exerts on labour-power, i.e. on the worker, then this surplus-value comprises not only profit (profit of enterprise plus interest), but also rent, i.e. the entire and undivided surplus-value.\" The trinity formula's \"capital–profit\" line is thus doubly wrong. It treats capital as a source of only profit, whereas, viewed in its actual relation to labour, capital is the source of the entire surplus-value including rent. And the moment the formula is transformed into \"capital–interest,\" \"the context disappears even more\" — the entire relation to labour and to the extraction of surplus-value is effaced. The formula in its purity, then, is worse than the formula in its loose form; the more coherent the trinity is as exposition, the more completely it effaces its own substance. This is the second time the chapter has made this dialectical point about \"consistency.\" 2.16 The Uniform and Symmetrical Incongruity; the Naturalization of Social Forms Marx now adds two further structural observations, then arrives at the chapter's great argument about naturalization. Second observation : The three revenues (interest, rent, wages) \"all belong to the same sphere, that of value.\" That is, they share a common measure. But this common sphere is covertly cancelled: \"this is cancelled out (this relationship not only between incommensurable magnitudes, but also between quite heterogeneous, unconnected and incomparable things) by the fact that capital, like the earth and labour, is considered simply from the standpoint of its material substance, i.e. simply as produced means of production, in which connection abstraction is made both from capital as a relation to the worker and from capital as value.\" In other words: the revenues are stipulated to be commensurable (they are all portions of value), while the sources are stipulated to be material (produced means of production, earth, labour). The formula requires homogeneity at the bottom and heterogeneity at the top, or vice versa, depending on how you look — and the resulting structure is unstable. Third observation : \"In this sense, therefore, the formula capital-interest (profit), earth-rent, labour-wages presents a uniform and symmetrical incongruity.\" The phrase \"uniform and symmetrical incongruity\" is deliberately paradoxical. Each of the three terms is internally incongruous — land–rent pairs a use-value with a value; capital–interest pairs a value with a value-greater-than-itself; labour–wages pairs a non-thing with a price. And the three incongruities are symmetrical : they are congruent in their incongruity. The formula achieves a kind of false consistency by repeating the same category error three times. The naturalist argument then unfolds. Marx begins from the inversion of priority : \"In fact, since it is not that wage-labour appears as a socially specific form of labour, but rather that all labour appears as wage-labour by nature (presenting itself like this to those trapped within the capitalist relations of production), the determinate and specific social forms which the objective conditions of labour — the produced means of production and the earth — assume vis-a-vis wage-labour (as they in turn presuppose wage-labour) coincide directly with the material existence of these conditions of labour, or with the shape that they generally possess in the actual labour process, independent of any historically specific social form, even independent of any social form of this whatsoever.\" Unpacking this: because wage-labour is (falsely) treated as the natural state of all labour, the historically specific forms of capital (produced means of production monopolized by a class) and landed property (earth monopolized by a class) are fused with the material existence of the means of production and the earth. Their social form — \"alienated from labour, objectified in relation to it and accordingly transformed\" — is treated as identical with their material existence. \"These means of production are in and for themselves, by nature, capital; capital is nothing but a mere 'economic name' for those means of production; and similarly the earth is in and for itself, by nature, the earth as monopolized by a certain number of landed proprietors.\" The phrase \"a mere 'economic name'\" is a jab at the apologetic cliché of bourgeois political economy (that \"capital\" is simply a name for produced means of production). Marx's point is that this nominalist reduction already contains the trinity's naturalizing move : if capital is merely a name for means of production, then means of production are capital by nature; and if earth-as-monopolized is merely a name for earth, then earth is landed property by nature. Then the celebrated image of the anthropomorphized land: \"Just as the products become an independent power vis-a-vis the producers in capital and in the capitalist — who in actual fact is nothing but personified capital — so land is personified in the landowner, he is the land similarly standing up on its hind legs and demanding its share, as an independent power, of the products produced with its aid; so that it is not the land that receives the portion of the product needed to replace and increase its productivity, but instead the landowner who receives a share of this product to be sold off and frittered away.\" The image \"standing up on its hind legs\" is a call-back to the famous \"table-turning\" of Volume 1's fetishism section, where Marx describes a table under the spell of commodity fetishism as \"not only standing on its head ... it stands on its head before all other commodities and evolves out of its wooden brain grotesque ideas.\" Here, the land itself rises like a being and demands its share. Two things are crucial: (1) the personification is not mere metaphorical flourish — Marx has just told us the landowner is the personification of earth , so the image is the argument; (2) Marx immediately notes the practical consequence : with the landowner in place of the land, the product that might have replenished the soil's productivity is instead received by the owner \"to be sold off and frittered away.\" The fetishism has a real, material, ecological cost. The mutual presupposition is then asserted: \"It is clear that capital presupposes that labour is wage-labour. It is just as clear, however, that once you proceed from labour as wage-labour, so that the coincidence between wage-labour and labour in general appears self-evident, capital and the monopolized earth must also appear as the natural form of the conditions of labour vis-a-vis labour in general.\" And the naturalization becomes a positively legal form of mystification: \"It now appears as the natural form of the means of labour that they should be capital, as a purely material character which arises from their function in the labour process in general.\" Or as Marx restates it a page later: \"The formal autonomy these conditions of labour acquire vis-a-vis labour, the particular form of this autonomy they possess, is then a property inseparable from them as things, as material conditions of production, an immanently ingrown character that necessarily falls to them as elements of production. Their social character in the capitalist production process, determined by a particular historical epoch, is an innate material character natural to them, and eternally so, as it were, as elements of the production process.\" \"An immanently ingrown character\" ( ein solches eingewachsenes) and \"eternally so, as it were\" (\"sozusagen ewig\") — these capture precisely the mechanism of ideological naturalization. The historical form is not merely added to the thing; it appears to have grown into the thing from within, inseparable and eternal. This is the deepest sense in which the trinity formula is ideological: not that it tells a false story about history, but that it abolishes history* by presenting the product of a specific epoch as the nature of matter. From this Marx derives the trinity's three revenue-forms as if they \"grow out of the roles that the earth, the produced means of production and labour play in the simple labour process, considering this labour process simply as proceeding between man and nature and ignoring any historical specificity.\" And the capstone : \"It is only the same thing again in a different form to say that the product in which the wage-worker's labour presents itself for him as his proceeds, his revenue, is simply the wage, the portion of value (and hence of the social product measured by this value) that represents his wage. If wage-labour coincides with labour in general, wages must coincide with the product of labour, and the portion of value that wages represents must coincide with the value created by labour in general. But in this way the other portions of value, profit and rent, confront wages just as independently and must arise from sources of their own that are specifically distinct from labour and independent; they must arise from the collaborating elements of production to whose owners they accrue, i.e. profit from the means of production, the material elements of capital, and rent from the earth, or nature, as represented by the landowner. (Roscher.)\" The parenthetical \"(Roscher.)\" is the one name of a living economist cited in the main body of the chapter, and it is not a compliment. Wilhelm Roscher, of the German historical school, is named as an exemplar of the apologetic reduction — the economist who says: if wages are the reward of labour, then rent must be the reward of land and profit the reward of capital. Marx's point is that the premise (\"wage-labour coincides with labour in general\") entails the trinity's conclusion (\"so profit and rent must arise from independent sources\"). Once wage-labour has been naturalized as the form of all labour, the other two revenue-forms logically require their own natural sources. The trinity is thus a valid inference from a false premise — which is why it is so compelling. 2.17 The Transformation of Sources of Revenue into \"Real Sources\" Marx now restates the whole transformation in one long, formally rigorous sentence, tracing the shift from legitimate \"sources of revenue\" (channels of distribution) into \"real sources\" (creative causes of the value). Landed property, capital, and wage-labour are \"transformed from sources of revenue in the sense that capital attracts to the capitalist a portion of the surplus-value which it extracts from labour, in the form of profit; monopoly in the earth attracts another part to the landowner in the form of rent; and labour gives the worker the final portion of value that is still available in the form of the wage — from sources by virtue of which one part of the value is transformed into the form of profit, a second into the form of rent and a third into the form of wages — into real sources from which these portions of value themselves arise, together with the portions of the product related to them, in which they exist or against which they are convertible, the value of the product therefore itself arising from these as its ultimate source.\" Footnote 50 attaches two of the great names of classical political economy to this doctrine. Adam Smith, Wealth of Nations (Pelican edition, p. 155): \"Wages, profit and rent are the three original sources of all revenue, as well as of all exchangeable value.\" And Storch, Cours d'économie politique , I, p. 259: \"Thus the causes of material production are at the same time the sources of the original revenues that it yields.\" Marx refers the reader to Volume 2, Chapter 19, 2, and to Theories of Surplus-Value , Part I, Chapter III, sections 4–7, for the detailed critique of Smith's doctrine — the \"absurd dogma\" that the value of the annual product resolves entirely into wages, profit, and rent, omitting the constant capital consumed in production. Why does this matter? Because it shows that the trinity formula is not a vulgar-economist invention. It has its systematic origin in the greatest name of classical political economy. Smith, unlike the vulgar economists Marx will indict at the chapter's close, did recognize labour as the source of value; but in his analysis of distribution he allowed the value of the product to dissolve without remainder into the three revenues — thereby, despite himself, endorsing the very \"three sources\" doctrine whose apologetic form he would have rejected. This is Smith's failure, and the chapter's devastating indictment of classical political economy (\"even its best representatives remained more or less trapped in the world of illusion their criticism had dissolved\") takes its start from this specific error. 2.18 Fetishism's Deepening: From Commodity to Trinity The chapter now begins its developmental narrative — the account of why the agents of production see the system in this distorted form. Marx explicitly connects the trinity's fetishism to the earlier fetishism of Volume 1: \"We have already shown in connection with the most simple categories of the capitalist mode of production and commodity production in general, in connection with commodities and money, the mystifying character that transforms the social relations for which the material elements of wealth serve as bearers in the course of production into properties of these things themselves (commodities), still more explicitly transforming the relation of production itself into a thing (money).\" The commodity form and money form already possess this mystificatory structure: social relations appear as properties of things. But: \"All forms of society are subject to this distortion, in so far as they involve commodity production and monetary circulation. In the capitalist mode of production, however, where capital is the dominant category and forms the specific relation of production, this bewitched and distorted world develops much further.\" The developmental narrative proceeds in stages: Stage 1: the immediate process of production. \"If we view capital first in the immediate process of production, as a pumper-out of surplus labour, this relationship is still very simple; the real connection impresses itself on the bearers of this process, the capitalists, themselves, and is still in their consciousness. The fierce struggle over the limits of the working day shows this in a striking way.\" At this stage, the exploitation is legible : the capitalist knows he is extracting surplus labour, and the struggle over the working day presupposes precisely this knowledge on both sides. Stage 2: relative surplus-value. \"But even within this immediate sphere, the sphere of the immediate process between labour and capital, the matter does not rest at this simple stage. With the development of relative surplus-value in the specifically capitalist mode of production, involving the growth of the productive forces of social labour, these productive forces and the social context of labour appear in the immediate labour process as shifted from labour to capital. Capital thereby already becomes a very mystical being, since all the productive forces of social labour appear attributable to it, and not to labour as such, as a power springing forth from its own womb.\" This is a decisive step. With relative surplus-value — productivity increases generated by cooperation, machinery, science, large-scale organization — the productive forces appear to belong to capital rather than to social labour. Capital acquires a \"mystical being\" within the immediate process itself, before any circulation has intervened. Stage 3: the circulation process. \"Then the circulation process intervenes, with all sections of capital, even agricultural, participating in it to the same degree. In this sphere, the conditions of the original production of value fall completely into the background. Even in the immediate production process, the capitalist is active also as commodity producer, as manager of commodity production.\" The value and surplus-value contained in commodities must be realized in circulation, and the appearance forms that \"both the restoration of the values advanced in production, and particularly the surplus-value contained in the commodities, seem not just to be realized, only in circulation but actually to arise from it.\" Two reinforcing circumstances are named: (a) profit on alienation — \"which depends on cheating, cunning, expertise, talent and a thousand and one market conjunctures\"; and (b) circulation time — \"the fact that a second determining element intervenes here besides labour-time, i.e. the circulation time. Even though this functions simply as a negative limit on the formation of value and surplus-value, it gives the appearance of being just as positive a ground as labour itself and of involving a determination independent of labour that arises from the nature of capital.\" Marx's point about circulation time is exactly parallel to his earlier point about land: a factor that functions as a limit (a \"negative\" determination) appears as a positive ground (a productive cause). This is one of the recurring formal patterns in the chapter: the illusion operates by converting limits into sources, conditions into producers. Marx then makes a methodological note that will be echoed in the final paragraphs of the chapter: \"In Volume 2, of course, we had to present this sphere of circulation only in relation to the determinations of form it produces, to demonstrate the further development of the form of capital that takes place in it. In actual fact, however, this sphere is the sphere of competition, which is subject to accident in each individual case; i.e. where the inner law that prevails through the accidents and governs them is visible only when these accidents are combined in large numbers, so that it remains invisible and incomprehensible to the individual agents of production themselves.\" This is the epistemology of the market: the law governing market movements is statistical — it emerges only in aggregate — and therefore cannot be grasped by the individual agent operating from within the fluctuations. The invisibility of the law is not a defect of the agent's intellect; it is a structural feature of a system whose regulating law exists only as the outcome of the very accidents whose meaning the agent cannot read. The section closes with a compact statement of the general process: \"Further, however, the actual production process, as the unity of the immediate production process and the process of circulation, produces new configurations in which the threads of the inner connection get more and more lost, the relations of production becoming independent of one another and the components of value ossifying into independent forms.\" \"Ossifying\" ( verknöchern ) is a key term, and will recur (\"the ossification of its form as against its substance, its essence\"). The image of an originally fluid social process hardening into rigid, thing-like forms is the presiding metaphor of the chapter's developmental narrative. 2.19 Profit, Average Profit, Prices of Production: The Dissolution of the Origin The next three transitions in the developmental narrative deepen the mystification by converting surplus-value first into profit, then into average profit, then into prices of production. Profit. \"The transformation of surplus-value into profit is, as we saw, just as much determined by the circulation process as by the process of production. Surplus-value in the form of profit is no longer related to the portion of capital laid out on labour, which is where it derives from, but rather to the total capital. The profit rate is governed by its own laws, which permit it to vary while the rate of surplus-value remains the same, and even require this variation. All this conceals the true nature of surplus-value more and more, concealing therefore the real mechanism of capital.\" Note the crucial phrase \"and even require this variation.\" The mystification is not superadded; the rate of profit must vary independently of the rate of surplus-value — for example, because of changes in the organic composition of capital — and this objective necessity is the mechanism of the concealment. Marx's account of ideology is, in this respect, materialist : the concealment is generated by the actual laws of the system, not by self-interested obfuscation. Average profit and prices of production. \"This happens still more with the transformation of profit into average profit and of values into prices of production, the governing averages of market price.\" Here \"a complex social process intervenes, the equalization of capitals, which cuts the relative average prices of commodities loose from their values, and the average profits in the various spheres of production from the actual exploitation of labour by the particular capitals involved.\" The link between profit and exploitation, though not actually broken, is mediated through so many intervening terms that it becomes untraceable in the individual case. Average prices \"not only seem to differ from their value ... but actually do differ\"; average profit \"differs from the surplus-value this capital has extracted from the workers employed by it.\" And then a very fine point: \"The value of commodities appears directly only in the influence of the changing productivity of labour on the rise and fall of prices of production; on their movement, not on their final limits.\" Value is still real — it still governs the movement of prices of production through changes in productivity. But it does not govern their level in any directly observable way; the level is set by the equalization of capitals. The connection to labour is therefore dynamic rather than static : invisible in the snapshot, visible only in the animation. And of course the trinity formula, being a snapshot, sees nothing. Profit of enterprise and interest. \"Profit now appears as determined only secondarily by the direct exploitation of labour, in so far as, given market prices that are seemingly independent of this exploitation, it permits the capitalist to realize a profit departing from the average. Normal average profit as such seems immanent in capital independently of exploitation; abnormal exploitation or even average exploitation under exceptionally favourable conditions seems only to determine divergences from average profit, and not this average profit itself.\" Exploitation appears as a deviation from an already-given norm, rather than as the norm's source. The \"secondary\" character assigned to exploitation in the phenomenal world is the exact inverse of its primary character in the real world. Ideological inversion, here, is a consequence of the developed form of the system , not a falsification imposed on it from outside. 2.20 Interest-Bearing Capital: The Most Estranged Form Marx now draws the decisive inference about the trinity's first term: \"The division of profit into profit of enterprise and interest (not to speak of the intervention of commercial profit and money-dealing profit, which are founded in the circulation sphere and seem to derive entirely from this, and not from the production process itself at all) completes the autonomization of the form of surplus-value, the ossification of its form as against its substance, its essence.\" The sequence is cumulative: profit, average profit, prices of production, profit of enterprise, interest, commercial profit, money-dealing profit. Each step ossifies the form further. And the split into profit of enterprise and interest completes the process: \"One portion of profit, in contrast to the other, separates itself completely from the capital-relation as such and presents itself as deriving not from the function of exploiting wage-labour but rather from the wage-labour of the capitalist himself. As against this, interest then seems independent both of the wage-labour of the worker and of the capitalist's own labour; it seems to derive from capital as its own independent source.\" Two mutually reinforcing autonomizations occur here. Profit of enterprise is transformed into a wage — compensation for the capitalist's managerial labour. Interest is transformed into a natural emanation of capital — the fruit of property as such. The two halves of profit thus acquire two separate \"justifications,\" neither relating to the exploitation of labour. The result is the transformation of capital into \"value-creating value\" in its purest form: \"If capital originally appeared on the surface of circulation as the capital fetish, value-creating value, so it now presents itself once again in the figure of interest-bearing capital as its most estranged and peculiar form.\" And from this Marx draws the conclusion promised at the chapter's beginning: \"This is why the form 'capital-interest', as a third in the series to 'earth-rent' and 'labour-wages', is much more consistent than 'capital-profit', since profit still retains a memory of its origin which in interest is not simply obliterated but actually placed in a form diametrically opposed to this origin.\" The dialectic here is precise and unexpected. Consistency with the trinity formula is a function of the completeness of the mystification. The purer the trinitarian abstraction, the more internally coherent the formula is — and the more completely it effaces the real relation. This is the \"science would be superfluous\" principle in its sharpest application: the better the surface hangs together, the more it distorts. A less consistent trinity would be more truthful. Ideological coherence is inversely related to scientific truth. 2.21 Landed Property and the Completion of the Alienation The developmental narrative now adds its final element — landed property: \"Finally, besides capital as an independent source of surplus-value, there appears landed property, as a limit to the average profit which transfers a portion of the surplus-value to a class that neither works itself nor directly exploits workers, and cannot even, like interest-bearing capital, launch forth in edifying homilies about the risk and sacrifice in lending capital.\" The landed class is singled out for a special irony: it is worse than the interest-bearing capitalist, who can at least pretend that lending capital involves risk and sacrifice. The landowner's claim is pure monopoly; no ideological fig-leaf of \"risk\" is available. And yet the trinity formula treats landed property's revenue as fully legitimate — because the earth appears as a natural force whose contribution must be rewarded. \"Since in this case one part of the surplus-value seems directly bound up not with social relations but rather with a natural element, the earth, the form of mutual alienation and ossification of the various portions of surplus-value is complete, the inner connection definitively torn asunder and its source completely buried, precisely through the assertion of their autonomy vis-a-vis each other by the various relations of production which are bound up with the different material elements of the production process.\" \"Definitively torn asunder and its source completely buried\" — the developmental narrative has reached its terminus. With the naturalization of rent, the alienation of the revenue-forms from their real origin is complete. The three \"sources\" are now three autonomous things, each producing its own fruit. 2.22 The Trinity Completed: Monsieur le Capital and Madame la Terre The chapter's rhetorical summit is now reached: \"Capital-profit (or better still capital-interest), land-ground-rent, labour-wages, this economic trinity as the connection between the components of value and wealth in general and its sources, completes the mystification of the capitalist mode of production, the reification of social relations, and the immediate coalescence of the material relations of production with their historical and social specificity: the bewitched, distorted and upside-down world haunted by Monsieur le Capital and Madame la Terre, who are at the same time social characters and mere things.\" Every concept in this sentence is loaded. \" Reification \" ( Versachlichung ) names the transformation of social relations into properties of things. \" Immediate coalescence of the material relations of production with their historical and social specificity \" is the chapter's analytic formula for what the trinity does : it fuses the material and the historical into a single apparent fact. \" Bewitched, distorted and upside-down \" ( verhexte, verkehrte, auf den Kopf gestellte ) invokes the Witches' Sabbath imagery that runs throughout Capital , culminating here in the image of Monsieur le Capital and Madame la Terre as figures in a fantastical social ball. The French names and the gendered pairing are not decorative. They signal a social comedy : the two protagonists of the capitalist mode of production, one male (capital, active, masculine, master) and one female (earth, passive, feminine, mother), stroll through the world as a couple, accepting the homage of the labour that supports them. And yet — this is the crucial qualifier — they are \"at the same time social characters and mere things .\" Monsieur le Capital is a capitalist, but he is also merely the money in his pocket; Madame la Terre is a landowner, but she is also merely a piece of rocky ground. The personification is real, and the thingness is real, and the identity of the two — social character equals material thing — is precisely the illusion. The trinity is thus, in the most precise sense, a personification of things and a reification of persons , and Marx's phrase captures both sides simultaneously. Note the theological resonance of \"trinity\" and \"mystification\" and \"haunted.\" Marx's chosen vocabulary is not accidental. The trinity formula is the religion of everyday life (a phrase Marx uses a few lines later) — a folk theology of capital, land, and labour as three persons of one economic God, with their own respective revenues as sacrifices. The chapter's demonstration is that this theology is not a proto-scientific error or a merely inherited superstition; it is the actual appearance of a system, generated by the system itself, and believed by everyone within it. 2.23 Classical Economics and Its Trapped Consciousness; Vulgar Economics as Apologetics Having diagnosed the mature consciousness, Marx now gives an historical critique of economic theory: \"It is the great merit of classical economics to have dissolved this false appearance and deception, this autonomization and ossification of the different social elements of wealth vis-a-vis one another, this personification of things and reification of the relations of production, this religion of everyday life, by reducing interest to a part of profit and rent to the surplus above the average profit, so that they both coincide in surplus-value; by presenting the circulation process as simply a metamorphosis of forms, and finally in the immediate process of production reducing the value and surplus-value of commodities to labour.\" The three-fold merit of classical political economy corresponds, inversely, to the three-fold structure of the trinity: it dissolves the autonomization of revenues (interest and rent into surplus-value), the autonomization of the circulation process (circulation as mere metamorphosis of forms), and the autonomization of value (value and surplus-value reduced to labour). Classical economics, at its best, performed the analytic reduction that this chapter has reconstructed in theoretical form. But: \"Yet even its best representatives remained more or less trapped in the world of illusion their criticism had dissolved, and nothing else is possible from the bourgeois standpoint; they all fell therefore more or less into inconsistencies, half-truths and unresolved contradictions.\" The parenthetical argument — \"nothing else is possible from the bourgeois standpoint\" — is the chapter's central claim about the limit of bourgeois science. It is not a moral accusation; it is a structural claim. From the standpoint of a member of bourgeois society, the appearances are reality: the trinity formula is what is \"lived.\" To reject it entirely would be to reject the form of the world in which one exists. So classical political economy could see through the trinity but not beyond it; it dissolved the illusion in one respect and fell back into it in another. Adam Smith is the exemplary case: he reduced value to labour in the abstract, and then resolved the same value back into the three revenues in the concrete analysis of distribution. Then Marx turns to the modern heirs: \"It is also quite natural, on the other hand, that the actual agents of production themselves feel completely at home in these estranged and irrational forms of capital-interest, land-rent, labour-wages, for these are precisely the configurations of appearance in which they move, and with which they are daily involved. It is equally natural, therefore, that vulgar economics, which is nothing more than a didactic and more or less doctrinaire translation of the everyday notions of the actual agents of production, giving them a certain comprehensible arrangement, finds the natural basis of its fatuous self-importance established beyond all doubt precisely in this trinity, in which the entire inner connection is obliterated.\" Vulgar economics is here defined with clinical precision: \"a didactic and more or less doctrinaire translation of the everyday notions of the actual agents of production, giving them a certain comprehensible arrangement.\" Its object is not truth but arrangement — the systematic presentation of what the practical man already believes. Its \"fatuous self-importance\" ( aufgeblasenen Wichtigtuerei ) is the affect appropriate to a knowledge that cannot admit its own limitations. Finally, the class dimension : \"This formula also corresponds to the self-interest of the dominant classes, since it preaches the natural necessity and perpetual justification of their sources of income and erects this into a dogma.\" Note the word \"dogma.\" The trinity formula is not merely an error of understanding but a creed — the doctrinal core of an apologetic project. Its function is justificatory: it presents the revenues of the dominant classes as naturally and necessarily produced by the natural sources owned by those classes. The critique of political economy thus reveals its social function in one final stroke. 2.24 The World Market, Competition, and the \"Ideal Average\" The chapter's penultimate section draws an explicit boundary around the analysis: \"In presenting the reification of the relations of production and the autonomy they acquire vis-a-vis the agents of production, we shall not go into the form and manner in which these connections appear to them as overwhelming natural laws, governing them irrespective of their will, in the form that the world market and its conjunctures, the movement of market prices, the cycles of industry and trade and the alternation of prosperity and crisis prevails on them as blind necessity. This is because the actual movement of competition lies outside our plan, and we are only out to present the internal organization of the capitalist mode of production, its ideal average, as it were.\" Two ideas are packed here. First, the phenomenological consequence of reification in the developed system is the experience of economic life as subjection to overwhelming natural laws — the market feels like weather: \"blind necessity.\" The agents do not experience themselves as masters of their circumstances; they experience themselves as objects of forces beyond their control. And the trinity formula is the nomenclature of this experience: it names the \"sources\" (capital, land, labour) as if they were natural elements, and their \"fruits\" as if they were the products of natural processes. Second, the methodological self-limitation : \"the actual movement of competition lies outside our plan.\" Marx is explicitly declaring that he is analyzing the internal organization of capital — the \"ideal average\" ( ideale Durchschnitt ) — and not the concrete, contingent, oscillating movements of the competitive surface. The trinity formula is the general form of appearance of that internal organization, abstracted from the conjunctural static. This is why it can be analyzed in its \"ideal average\" even as its concrete movement is deferred. 2.25 The Historical Counterpoint: When Mystification Is Absent The chapter's final moment is a historical counterpoint, in part recoverable and in part lost. Marx argues that in pre-capitalist formations, the mystification proper to the trinity formula is largely absent: \"In earlier forms of society, this economic mystification comes in principally in connection with money and interest-bearing capital. It is excluded by the very nature of the case, firstly, where production is predominantly for use-value, for the producers' own needs; secondly, where, as in Antiquity and the Middle Ages, slavery or serfdom forms the broad basis of social production.\" The argument here is that of a two-fold exclusion . Where production is largely for use (not for value), the trinitarian appearance cannot arise: because value itself, and hence the trinity of value-sources, has no dominance. And where visible domination (slavery, serfdom) forms the base of production, the trinity cannot arise in its proper form either: \"In the latter case, the dominance of the conditions of production over the producers is concealed by the visible relations of domination and servitude, which appear as direct mainsprings of the production process.\" The argument is subtle. In a slave or serf system, exploitation is open : the master directly compels the slave or serf. The social relation of domination is legible, and the extraction of surplus labour appears for what it is: compulsion, not a \"revenue\" arising from a \"source.\" Paradoxically, the transparency of pre-capitalist domination prevents the mystification of the capitalist trinity from taking hold. In capitalism, by contrast, exploitation is mediated by free contracts, the wage-form, and market circulation — so the extraction becomes invisible, and the trinity formula steps in to give an account of the revenues without ever mentioning exploitation. Marx adds two further pre-capitalist cases: \"In the primitive communities where an indigenous communism prevails, and even in the urban communities of Antiquity, it is the actual community and its conditions that presents itself as the basis of production, the reproduction of this community being production's final purpose. Even in the guild system of the Middle Ages, neither capital nor labour appear unrestrained; their connections are determined by the system of corporations and the relationships this involves, as well as by the corresponding ideas of professional obligation, craftsmanship, etc.\" Three non-capitalist patterns are thus contrasted with the trinity formula: the communist community (production's end is the reproduction of the community, not value); the ancient commune (the community presents itself as the basis of production); and the medieval guild (capital and labour are constrained by institutional bonds and by the corresponding ideological forms — \"professional obligation, craftsmanship\"). In each case, the trinity formula cannot take shape because the prius is not value but the community, or the guild-bond, or the master's domination. The chapter's final sentence breaks off: \"Only in the capitalist mode of production ...\" Engels's note: \"(Here the manuscript breaks off.)\" The reader is left to complete the thought. What Marx was drafting, given the trajectory of the preceding sentences, is a statement of the singularity of capitalist mystification: only in the capitalist mode of production is production for value universalized, domination concealed under contract, and the relations of production alienated into the trinitarian form. Only in capitalism can the whole population move within a \"bewitched, distorted and upside-down world\" that treats socially produced revenues as natural fruits. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 3.1 The Chapter's Central Lessons 1. The trinity is a categorial error that is also a historical necessity. Marx does not treat the trinity formula as a mistake, a sloppy analytic habit, or a deliberate deception — he treats it as the form of appearance generated by the capitalist mode of production itself. The three terms of the formula commit three structurally identical but materially distinct category errors (use-value/value, value/self-multiplying value, non-thing/price). These errors are not corrigible by better observation because they are generated by the real surface of the real system. The lesson is methodologically decisive for any critical social science: the appearance of a system is not a veil to be lifted but a product to be explained. 2. Naturalization is the trinity's operative move. The specific mechanism by which the trinity's illusions are produced is the coalescence of material relations of production with their historical and social specificity . Earth becomes \"landed property by nature\"; produced means of production become \"capital by nature\"; labour becomes \"wage-labour by nature.\" The chapter gives a general name to this move — \"an immanently ingrown character,\" \"an innate material character natural to them, and eternally so\" — and a general consequence: the abolition of history by nature. 3. Alienation deepens with development. Measured against the immediate process of exploitation, in which the capitalist knows he is extracting surplus labour (as the struggle over the working day proves), the developed system (relative surplus-value, circulation, average profit, prices of production, profit of enterprise, interest, rent) is more mystified, not less. The trinity formula is the terminus of this progressive deepening — the trinity's \"consistency\" (interest rather than profit; capitalized wealth without memory of exploitation) is a measure of its distance from truth. 4. Distribution is not production. The trinity's deepest inversion is to present the distribution of a value as the creation of that value. Marx's counter-thesis is exacting: distribution \"presupposes this substance as already present, i.e. the total value of the annual product, which is nothing more than objectified social labour.\" This principle undercuts the entire family of apologetic doctrines (the \"three sources\" of Smith and Storch) that naturalize the distributive order by deriving value from the distributive categories. 5. Ideological coherence is inversely proportional to truth. \"Capital–interest\" is more consistent than \"capital–profit\" precisely because interest has completely forgotten its origin. The same holds at the level of the whole chapter: the trinity's \"uniform and symmetrical incongruity\" hangs together as a doctrine because it is untrue. Every increase in the formula's formal coherence corresponds to a loss of contact with the real relation. This principle reverses a common assumption that a good theory should be internally consistent — insisting instead that under conditions of fetishism, the better arranged is the more obfuscatory . 6. Ideology serves class interest without being reduced to conspiracy. The trinity is not an invention of the dominant classes; it is the ordinary consciousness of production agents, codified. But it \"corresponds to the self-interest of the dominant classes,\" because its practical effect is to preach \"the natural necessity and perpetual justification of their sources of income.\" Marx's account of ideology is materialist-dialectical: the illusion is a product of the system, and its utility for the dominant class is a real function, without requiring the class to have invented the illusion. 7. The \"realm of freedom\" is a material, not moral, question. The chapter's placement of the realm-of-freedom passage between surplus-labour and distribution is deliberate. Freedom is not a normative stance toward the trinity's injustice; it is a real possibility released by the development of the productive forces — and its basic prerequisite is the reduction of the working day. The trinity formula obscures this because it obscures the question of surplus-labour-time entirely — the very question that the \"reduction of the working day\" raises. 3.2 Broader Implications For the theory of value. The trinity formula is the point at which a labour theory of value is structurally invisible from within capitalism. Every mediation between labour and revenue — profit, average profit, prices of production, profit of enterprise, interest, rent — removes the theory further from direct observability. Marx's claim is not that the theory is refuted by this distance but that it must be recovered against the surface. Science, in this sense, is the labor of \"dissolving the false appearance.\" For the critique of political economy. The chapter establishes a distinction between classical political economy (which dissolves the trinity analytically but cannot escape the bourgeois standpoint) and vulgar political economy (which \"translates\" the practical consciousness of the agents into \"doctrine\"). Classical political economy is honored for its achievements — dissolving rent into surplus, showing circulation to be metamorphosis, reducing value to labour — and indicted for its limits: even its \"best representatives\" fell back into \"inconsistencies, half-truths and unresolved contradictions,\" because \"nothing else is possible from the bourgeois standpoint.\" For the theory of ideology. The chapter's account is a critique of everyday economic reason rather than a conspiracy theory. Ideology is structurally produced, systematically coherent, and useful without being invented. Its theological form — \"the religion of everyday life\" — marks its character as a lived creed, not a private opinion. Its function is to confer on distributive outcomes the aura of natural necessity, and hence to make social change appear as interference with nature rather than as historical transformation. For the theory of history. The chapter's historical counterpoint is critical to its structure. Pre-capitalist formations are described as less mystified in the trinity's sense — even though they are more openly unfree. The argument is that freedom and mystification advance together under capitalism : the free contract conceals the extraction that overt domination would have made visible. This is one of the chapter's most provocative implications: transparency of power and unfreedom can coincide; formal freedom and structural opacity can coincide; and the growth of formal equality is compatible with a deepening of real subjection. The trinity formula is the ideological signature of this historical arrangement. For method in the social sciences. The \"ideal average\" is a formal declaration of scope: the chapter presents the internal organization of the capitalist mode of production as a system, abstracting from the \"actual movement of competition.\" The trinity formula is its surface form; competition is left outside the plan. This same move — isolating a system's structure from its conjunctural movement — is the chapter's own epistemology. Its implication is that the critique of the trinity is not a critique of market events but of market form : the trinity is what the capitalist mode of production looks like when it is working as its own structure dictates . 3.3 Conclusion: The Formula as Monument Read as a whole, Chapter 48 builds a single, massive argument: the trinity formula is the monument of the capitalist mode of production — the form in which its practitioners contemplate their own world, and in which capital, land, and labour appear as three equal, independent, natural persons, each generating its own revenue out of a source peculiar to it. Every category the earlier volumes and parts of Capital had developed — value, surplus-value, profit, average profit, prices of production, profit of enterprise, interest, rent, circulation time, landed property — is mobilized here to show why the monument has this shape and what it conceals. And the monument's culmination is a vision: the bewitched world of the everyday, in which reified social relations walk the earth as persons and persons stand before them as things; capital and land, dressed as Monsieur and Madame , receive the homage of the labour that produces their revenue. Marx's final gesture in the chapter is to leave this vision suspended — the manuscript breaks off mid-sentence — and yet the suspension is itself the argument. The critique of the trinity is not a completed transcript of the world beyond it. It is the diagnosis of a world whose own surface guarantees that the diagnosis will be unintelligible to those who live within it. Science, as Marx defines it in this chapter, is the labor of seeing what the system was constructed to hide — and the only guarantee of that labor's necessity is the trinity formula's continued self-evidence to those it rules. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 47 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 49"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 49", "title": "Chapter 49", "subtitle": "Concerning the Analysis of the Process of Production", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 49/Chapter 49 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 10808, "read_time": "54 min read", "tags": ["Social Reproduction Schemes (Dept I & II)", "Critique of the Smithian Dogma"], "keywords": ["Analysis of the Process of Production as a Whole", "Social Value Product vs Material Wealth", "Critique of Value Resolved into Pure Revenue", "Replacement of Social Fixed and Constant Capital", "Synthesis of Volume 1, 2, and 3 Macro-Equilibria"], "text": "Chapter 49 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 10,751 words ⏱️ ~54 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 49, \"On the Analysis of the Production Process,\" is the first of the three chapters that close Capital Volume 3 by dismantling what Marx regards as the single most consequential and persistent error in the history of political economy: the dogma that the value of every commodity—and therefore the value of the total annual social product—can be \"ultimately resolved\" into wages, profit, and rent. This is the celebrated \"Smith's dogma\" (so named after Adam Smith), and the chapter functions simultaneously as a diagnosis, a proof of its falsity, and a demonstration of how its acceptance generates an insoluble theoretical contradiction. Marx calls the resulting muddle an \"incredible blunder\" that has \"run through the analysis of all political economy since Adam Smith,\" and the chapter's central intellectual objective is to expose that blunder with full analytical rigor and to substitute for it a correct account grounded in the reproduction schemas he had already developed in Volume 2. The chapter's core thesis can be stated with precision: the value of the total annual product of society breaks down into two fundamentally distinct components —component A, which replaces the value of the constant capital advanced and consumed, and component B, which is expressed in the form of revenue as wages, profit, and rent—and the two components are categorically different in character and function. Component A never assumes the form of revenue; it always returns in the form of capital, and constant capital at that. Component B, by contrast, is the total value newly added by the labour performed during the year, and it exhausts itself in the three revenue forms. The central paradox that Marx must resolve, and that the chapter is built around, is this: if all freshly added labour is exhausted in wages, profit, and rent, and if those revenues are the only value the society has created during the year, then how can the value of the annual product, which also contains a portion equal to the constant capital consumed (C), be bought by a value total that is smaller than itself? In Marx's own vivid framing: \"How can a value of four be bought with a value of three?\" The chapter's thesis, therefore, is that the value of the constant portion of capital is preserved in the product but not reproduced by the newly added labour—that no additional labour is needed to perpetuate the value of the constant component, even though additional labour is needed to replace the constant capital consumed. This distinction, first established in Volume 1, Chapter 7 (\"The Labour Process and the Valorization Process\")—where labour, in its specific productive function, transfers the value of the means of production into the new product without creating that value afresh—is the linchpin of the entire solution. The chapter's secondary thesis, and the one that most directly answers the \"blunder,\" is that the annual product of the total social capital is exchanged between two great departments—Department I (means of production) and Department II (means of individual consumption)—such that the revenue of the capitalists and workers in Department I is consumed in its natural form by Department II, while the constant capital of Department II is consumed in its natural form by Department I. This interdepartmental exchange is what allows a society's total newly created value to circulate and buy a product whose total value exceeds it. How does this chapter fit into the broader theoretical framework? It stands at the intersection of the volume's three closing movements. First, it depends entirely on the analysis of the reproduction of the total social capital developed in Volume 2, Part Three (especially Chapter 20's schema of simple reproduction) and on the earlier demonstration in Volume 1 that surplus-value originates in unpaid surplus labour. Second, it is the first of a triptych: the \"analysis of the production process\" performed here sets up the \"quid pro quo\" Marx announces at the chapter's close—\"the illusion that value arises from its own components\"—which he says \"we shall discuss in the following chapter.\" That following chapter (Chapter 50, on the \"semblance of competition\") and the final Chapter 51 (the famous \"Trinity Formula\") complete the demolition. Third, the chapter belongs to the volume's seventh and final part, \"The Revenues and Their Sources,\" and it intervenes in a specific polemical field: it takes aim at Adam Smith, at Jean-Baptiste Say (and in footnotes at Storch, Proudhon, and Forcade), and it simultaneously criticizes Ricardo for conceding \"Smith's erroneous analysis of commodity price\" even while Ricardo himself, in his polemic against Say, recognized the absurdity of the \"gross produce/net produce\" confusion. The intellectual stakes are therefore enormous and twofold. Theoretically , the chapter defends the law of value itself: if commodity value really did resolve entirely into revenues, and if revenues were determined by prices, the analysis would collapse into a \"vicious circle and an infinite regress\" in which the very foundation of value determination disappears. Practically and politically , the chapter exposes the \"optimism of bourgeois thoughtlessness\" (Marx's characterization of a typical economist) that treats any difficulty in analyzing capital value as a mere artifact of the standpoint of the individual capitalist—an evasion that Marx insists dissolves as soon as production is considered \"as a whole, on a large scale.\" By resolving the riddle, Marx demonstrates that capitalism's aggregate product always and necessarily contains a portion that can never be consumed as anyone's income and must be returned to productive use as capital—thereby demolishing the apologetic fantasy that a nation's entire output is available for consumption. This is why the chapter culminates in the striking claim that even after the abolition of the capitalist mode of production, \"the determination of value still prevails,\" and the regulation of labour-time and distribution becomes \"more essential than ever.\" The chapter is thus both a technical proof in the theory of value and a fundamental refutation of the political economy that would deny the structural necessity of reproducing the means of production. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Initial Simplifying Move: Bracketing the Value/Price-of-Production Distinction Marx opens the chapter with a methodological act of simplification whose consequences are easy to miss and whose justification is crucial to everything that follows. He states that \"for the analysis now following we can ignore the distinction between value and price of production, since this disappears whenever we are concerned with the value of labour's total annual product, i.e. the value of the product of the total social capital.\" The distinction between an individual commodity's value (the socially necessary labour-time embodied in it) and its price of production (cost price plus average profit) is the great result of Volume 3's earlier parts—the transformation problem. For an individual commodity or an individual capital, these two magnitudes systematically diverge, because the equalization of profit rates redistributes total surplus-value among branches. If a branch has a high organic composition of capital, its price of production exceeds its value; if low, it falls below. But Marx's point is that this divergence is internal to the total, a redistribution within the whole. When we sum across all commodities—when we take the total annual product of the total social capital—the deviations cancel out perfectly, and the total value equals the total price of production. Aggregately there is no \"adding up\" of different rates; the sum of prices of production is identical to the sum of values, and the sum of profits is identical to the sum of surplus-values. This is what licenses Marx to suppress the technical divergence and analyze the production process in terms of value directly. The move is not an evasion; it is a recognition that the present problem is a problem about the totality , and at the level of the totality the transformation problem does not bite. This is the logical floor on which the entire chapter stands, and it is worth flagging how it shapes everything. Because the rate of profit equalization is a closed redistribution within total value, nothing in the subsequent analysis can be repaired by appealing to price-of-production effects. If the argument were to fail, it would fail at the level of value itself, and no transformation-theoretic escape hatch is available. Marx will return to this theme explicitly near the chapter's end, when he notes that the equalization of profit and the \"obstacles that landed property partly places in the way of this (in absolute rent)\" give rise to divergent average prices, but that this only \"affects the addition of surplus-value to the various commodity prices; it does not abolish surplus-value itself, nor the total value of commodities as the source of these various price components.\" Profit and Rent as Forms of Surplus-Value; Wages as the Form of Variable Capital Having cleared the ground, Marx establishes the two fundamental identities on which the entire chapter rests. First: \"Profit (profit of enterprise plus interest) and rent are nothing more than characteristic forms assumed by particular portions of the surplus-value in commodities.\" The parenthetical is important and characteristically precise: Marx has already, earlier in Volume 3, split profit into profit of enterprise (the income of the functioning capitalist) and interest (the income of the lender), and he here folds both, together with ground-rent, into the category of \"characteristic forms\" of surplus-value. Second: \"The size of the surplus-value sets a quantitative limit for the parts it can be broken down into. Average profit plus rent is therefore equal to surplus-value.\" This is a statement about the priority of the whole over its parts . Surplus-value is the original, undifferentiated magnitude—it is the excess of the value of labour's product over the value of labour-power, i.e., unpaid surplus labour objectified. Profit and rent are distributive sluices through which that prior magnitude flows. The parts cannot exceed the whole; the whole is \"already given before the division.\" This ordering is exactly what the dogma reverses: the dogma treats wages, profit, and rent as primary sources that combine to produce commodity value, whereas Marx treats commodity value as primary and the revenues as its decomposed forms. Marx then carefully qualifies the identity in a way that reveals the analytical sophistication of the argument. He acknowledges that \"it is possible for a part of the surplus labour and hence surplus-value contained in commodities not to go directly into the equalization that gives the average profit, so that a part of the value of the commodities is not expressed at all in their price.\" In other words, not all surplus labour embodied is necessarily realized in price. Why? Because \"the amounts of socially necessary labour required for the production of a given commodity are constantly changing owing to the constant changes in productivity of labour,\" so that \"one section of commodities are always produced under abnormal conditions and must therefore be sold below their individual value.\" Here Marx touches the distinction between produced surplus-value and realized surplus-value. A commodity produced under below-average productivity embodies more labour-time than the social norm, and only the social norm is realized in price; the excess is lost, wasted from the standpoint of value realization. But—and this is the crucial move—this gap does not threaten the argument, for two reasons Marx gives. First, it is \"compensated for\" in aggregate: if the under-realized commodity enters as an element of constant capital for another branch, the buyer's profit rate simply rises (his costs are lower); if it enters as an article of individual consumption , buyers enjoy a greater quantity of product. Second, \"it is cancelled out in the average movement.\" Marx concludes: \"in any case, even if a portion of surplus-value not expressed in the price of the commodity is omitted from the price formation process, the sum of average profit plus rent can in its normal form never be greater than the total surplus-value, though it can be less.\" For \"practical purposes,\" realized surplus-value can be equated with total surplus-value, because profit and rent are the portion of surplus-value that actually enters commodity prices. Marx then extends this discipline to the monopoly-rent case, which is easy to misconstrue. \"Even monopoly rent, in so far as it is not a deduction from wages and does not form a special category, must always indirectly form part of surplus-value.\" A monopoly rent received on one commodity may be, in its immediate form, an excess over the commodity's own surplus-value (as in differential rent, where it is an excess price over production costs) or an excess over the average-profit portion of its surplus-value (as in absolute rent). But in either case it must, at the aggregate level, be \"a part of the surplus-value of other commodities, i.e. those which are exchanged against this commodity with a monopoly price.\" The logic is a conservation principle: since total value equals total price, any monetary claim in excess of one branch's own surplus-value must be a transfer from another branch's surplus-value. Nothing can be created by the mere act of exchange. Marx now pivots to the third revenue form: wages. \"Wages, on the other hand, which form the third characteristic form of revenue, are always equal to the variable component of capital, i.e. the component that is laid out not on means of labour but on the purchase of living labour-power.\" He adds a parenthetical of great theoretical importance, which he will develop: \"The labour paid in the expenditure of revenue is itself paid for from wages, profit or rent, and thus does not form any portion of the value of those commodities with which it is paid. So it does not come into consideration for the analysis of commodity value and the components into which this breaks down.\" This is the unproductive labour point—labour bought out of revenue (a servant, a hairdresser) does not objectify itself in a commodity and so does not enter the value analysis of the product. Wages, by contrast, precisely do enter, because they correspond to the value the worker reproduces within the working day. The structure of the working day is then restated in its classic Volume 1 form: \"The worker's total working day is divisible into two parts.\" One part \"is that in which he performs the quantum of labour needed to reproduce the value of his own means of subsistence: the paid part of his total labour\"—necessary labour. \"The entire remaining part of the working day, the entire excess quantum of labour he performs beyond the labour realized in the value of his wages, is surplus labour, unpaid labour\"—surplus labour, which \"is represented in the surplus-value of his total commodity production.\" Surplus-value is \"divisible in turn into differently named portions, profit (profit of enterprise plus interest) and rent.\" The synthesis of these identities yields Marx's first great summing-up: \"The total portion of commodity value... in which the total labour that the worker adds during a day or a year is realized... breaks down into the value of wages, profit and rent.\" Crucially: \"Besides this labour, the worker performs no other, and besides the total value of the product, which assumes the forms of wages, profit and rent, he creates no other value.\" This is the fullest statement of what the newly added labour amounts to. The value of the annual product in which the worker's newly added labour is represented \"is equal to wages or the value of the variable capital, plus surplus-value, which breaks down again into the forms of profit and rent.\" From this, Marx extracts the chapter's pivotal negative conclusion: \"It is evident, therefore, that the value of the constant portion of capital is not reproduced in the annually created product-value, for wages are equal simply to the variable portion of capital advanced in production, while rent and profit are equal to the surplus-value, the excess value produced over and above the total value of the capital advanced, i.e. the value of the constant capital plus the value of the variable capital.\" The constant capital's value appears in the product—it is preserved —but it is not created by the year's labour. The newly created value is exhausted by V + S; C is carried over. The Two Problems: Buying a Value of Four with a Value of Three; Who Replaces the Constant Capital? Marx disposes of one potential distraction before stating the problems. He notes that it is \"completely immaterial\" that part of the surplus-value may be used for accumulation rather than consumed as revenue: \"the part of this that is saved as an accumulation fund serves towards forming new, additional capital, but not towards replacing the old capital.\" For simplicity he assumes revenues \"go completely into individual consumption.\" This bracket, like the value/price-of-production bracket, removes a complication without weakening the argument—indeed he will return to accumulation later as a crucial illustration. Now the double problem is stated with full force. On the one hand , the value of the annual product, in which revenues are consumed, \"contains in it a portion of value equal to the value of the constant portion of capital that has gone into it.\" So its value = wages + profit + rent + C, \"with C standing for the constant portion of its value.\" But \"the value annually produced, which is simply wages + profit + rent,\" is smaller than the product it must buy. \"How then is the value annually produced, which is simply wages + profit + rent, to buy a product whose value is (wages + profit + rent) + C? How can the value annually produced buy a product that has a higher value than it has itself?\" This is the \"value of four bought with a value of three\" problem—a problem about aggregate purchasing power versus aggregate product value . On the other hand , the material or in-kind problem: setting aside the fixed capital that persists (with reduced value) after production, the constant capital advanced as raw and ancillary materials \"has gone completely into the new product,\" and part of the means of labour has been used up, its value consumed. \"The part of the constant capital that has been completely used up in production must be replaced in kind.\" And assuming unchanged productivity, \"the same amount of labour is required to replace it as before.\" Then the sharp question: \"But who is to perform this labour, and who does perform it?\" This is the real crux, because \"all newly added labour is expressed in the value newly created in the course of the year, which in turn goes entirely into the three revenues.\" So \"there is no excess social labour left over for the replacement of the capital consumed\"—and yet the value annually created \"seems insufficient to pay for or to buy the constant component of capital, which the annual product must contain on top of the value of the revenues.\" The paradox appears airtight: the labour budget is fully spent on revenues, yet a material replacement requires labour. Marx immediately signals the resolution and locates it historically. \"We can see that the problem posed here was already solved when we dealt with the reproduction of the total social capital, in Volume 2, Part Three.\" He explains why he returns to it : first, in Volume 2 \"surplus-value was not yet developed in its forms of revenue—profit (profit of enterprise plus interest) and rent\"; second, \"it is precisely in connection with the form of wages, profit and rent that an incredible blunder has run through the analysis of all political economy since Adam Smith.\" The blunder is thus not a mere oversight but a structural consequence of treating the revenue forms as if they were sources of value. The resolution of the first problem (the \"value of four with a value of three\") is dispatched by reference to Volume 1, Chapter 7. Marx reminds the reader \"how when new labour is added, even though it does not reproduce the old value but simply makes an addition to it, only creating additional value, the old value is still preserved in the product; and that this happens not by virtue of the value-creating characteristic of labour, i.e. not because it is labour in general, but rather in its function as a specific kind of productive labour.\" Two functions of labour are distinguished: (1) the value-creating function (abstract, socially necessary labour-time, which adds new value equal to V + S); and (2) the value-preserving (or value-transferring) function (concrete, useful labour as a determinate productive activity, which transfers the value of the consumed means of production into the product without creating it afresh). The constant value in the product is not freshly created; it is carried over . Therefore \"no additional labour was needed... to perpetuate the value of the constant component in the product on which the revenue... is spent.\" But—and this is what generates the second problem—\"additional labour is needed to replace the constant capital consumed during the previous year, in value and in use-value, since without this replacement no reproduction is possible at all.\" Thus the two problems are not identical, and the distinction is decisive. The value-preservation of C is costless in labour-time—it rides along on the new labour's concrete character. The material replacement of consumed C is not costless—it requires labour that produces new means of production. This is the seam along which the whole argument runs and the exact point the dogma misses. The Solution from Volume 2: Two Departments and the Interdepartmental Exchange The solution requires the reproduction schemas. Marx recalls the fundamental division: \"In Volume 2 we divided all capital into two great classes: department I, which produces means of production, and department II, which produces means of individual consumption.\" He is emphatic that this division is not a hypothesis but \"the expression of a fact.\" Certain products (a horse, corn) may serve either purpose, \"yet in no way abolishes the absolute validity of this division.\" He explains: \"One part of this product goes into individual consumption... It is the product on which wages, profit and rent are spent. This is the product of a specific department of the social capital. It is possible that this same capital also produces products belonging to department I.\" Where it does, only the portion actually falling to individual consumption counts as Department II. And crucially: \"By far the greater part of the products that form constant capital exist in a material form in which they cannot go into individual consumption.\" Even where a peasant could eat his seed-corn or slaughter his draught ox, \"the economic barrier facing him makes it exactly the same as if this part did exist in a non-consumable form.\" The material form matters economically even when it is technically consumable. The concrete schema for simple reproduction is then set out (from Volume 2, Chapter 20, section 2): Department I (means of production): 4,000c + 1,000v + 1,000s = 6,000 Department II (means of consumption): 2,000c + 500v + 500s = 3,000 Total social product = 9,000 . Marx then traces the exchanges. In Department II, \"500v + 500s = 1,000 is consumed in revenue in department II by the producers and landowners there.\" But Department II's constant capital \"2,000c remains to be replaced.\" How? \"This is consumed by the workers, capitalists and recipients of rent in department I, their income being 1,000v + 1,000s = 2,000.\" So the value of Department I's revenue (its V + S = 2,000) equals precisely the value of Department II's constant capital (2,000c). Department II's non-revenue portion (the means of consumption equal in value to 2,000c) is bought and consumed by the people of Department I out of their revenues; and Department I's product (means of production) flows back to Department II to replace its constant capital in kind. Finally, \"the 4,000c in department I... is replaced from department I's own product,\" i.e., intra-Department-I exchanges: Department I supplies its own replacement means of production—\"6,000 − 2,000, for 2,000 has already been converted into constant capital for department II.\" The two problems are now resolved. Riddle 1 (\"how can value three buy value four\") is dissolved because the revenues of society (V + S = 3,000 for the whole schema, i.e., I's 2,000 + II's 1,000) and the constant capital of Department II (2,000c) are not two independent magnitudes requiring the same money to do double duty: the constant capital replaced is exchanged against the revenues, one department's revenue purchasing another department's constant-capital-replacing product, in kind and in value. Riddle 2 (\"who replaces the consumed constant capital, and with what labour?\") is dissolved because Department I's labour is itself devoted to producing means of production; some of Department I's product simply never enters individual consumption at all—it returns to production. The labour that \"replaces\" C is embodied in Department I's output, and it circulates as capital, not as revenue. Marx warns against a natural misunderstanding of the schema's apparent arbitrariness: \"It should be noted that the figures here are completely arbitrary, so that the correspondence between the value of department I's revenue and department II's constant capital also seems arbitrary.\" But this correspondence is not accidental in a normal state: \"in so far as the reproduction process proceeds normally and with other factors remaining the same... the sum of wages, profit and rent in department I must be equal in value to the constant portion of capital in department II. Otherwise department II cannot replace its constant capital, nor department I convert its revenue from non-consumable into consumable form.\" Here is the equilibrium condition of simple reproduction: I(v + s) = II(c). The numerical correspondence manifests a structural necessity imposed by the material forms of the two departments' products. Component A versus Component B; the Double Function of Variable Capital With the schema in hand, Marx generalizes the two-component decomposition. \"The value of the annual commodity product, therefore... can be broken down into two components: component A, which replaces the value of the constant capital advanced, and component B, expressed in the form of revenue as wages, profit and rent.\" Component A is defined by a set of negative characteristics: \"(1) [it] never assumes the form of revenue, (2) always returns in the form of capital, and constant capital at that.\" Component B, by contrast, contains internal distinctions: wages, profit, and rent are all revenue forms, but \"profit and rent represent surplus-value, i.e. unpaid labour, and wages represent paid labour.\" This is the class-theoretic heart of the decomposition—the revenue unity conceals the paid/unpaid labour distinction that defines the social relation. Marx then unfolds the double function of variable capital , which is one of the chapter's most important conceptual contributions. The portion of the product's value that replaces wages \"returns first of all as variable capital, as a component of the capital that has to be advanced once more for reproduction.\" It has, so to speak, two lives. First, \"it exists firstly in the form of capital, being exchanged as such against labour-power.\" In the hands of the worker, it is \"transformed into the revenue that the worker draws from the sale of his labour-power; then, as revenue, it is converted into means of subsistence and consumed.\" Marx insists that this double process is visible in the actual movement of money: \"Variable capital is advanced in money, paid out in wages. This is its first function as capital. It is replaced by labour-power and transformed into the externalization of this labour-power, into labour.\" Second, \"the workers use this money to buy a portion of their commodity product... and [it] is consumed by them as revenue.\" The critical generalization comes next. \"If we imagine the money circulation to be removed, one part of the worker's product is already in the hands of the capitalist in the form of capital. He advances this part as capital, giving it to the worker in exchange for new labour-power, while the worker consumes it as revenue.\" The money merely mediates what is already a material and social relationship: the worker's product, which his own labour created, confronts him as alien capital advanced to him, and he receives back a portion of it as wages. This is why the variable capital's revenue-form is bound up with the reproduction of the wage relation itself: \"It is an essential condition for the repeated reproduction of labour as wage-labour, the means of production as capital, and the production process itself as a capitalist one, that it should return in this form.\" The double function is thus not a curiosity but the mechanism of class reproduction. The capitalist's re-advancing of V as capital is what continually re-establishes the worker's dependence and the capitalist's command. Gross and Net Output, Gross and Net Income, National Income Marx now introduces an analytical vocabulary designed to block the dogma at a second front. To avoid \"useless difficulties,\" he distinguishes four magnitudes. Gross output (gross product) is the entire product reproduced. \"With the exception of the portion of fixed capital which is applied but not consumed, the value of the gross output or gross product is equal to the value of the capital advanced and consumed in production, constant capital and variable, plus the surplus-value, which breaks down into profit and rent\"—i.e., in value terms, C + V + S for the total social capital; in material terms, \"the material elements forming the constant and variable capital, plus the material elements of the surplus product, in which profit and rent are represented.\" Gross income is \"the portion of value, and the part of the gross product measured by this, which remains over after deducting the portion of value... which the capital advanced and consumed in production replaces.\" Therefore gross income = wages + profit + rent (i.e., V + S). Net income , on the other hand, \"is the surplus-value and hence surplus product that remains after wages are deducted, and so it expresses in fact the surplus-value that capital realizes.\" So net income = profit + rent = S . The distinction between gross and net income is thus precisely the distinction between (V + S) and (S)—between the total newly created value and the surplus alone. Applying this to society: \"Considering the income of the society as a whole, national income consists of wages plus profit plus rent, i.e. the gross income.\" But Marx adds a decisive rider: \"this too is an abstraction, since the whole society, on the basis of capitalist production, is considered from the capitalist standpoint and hence views as net income only those incomes reducible to profit and rent.\" The \"national income\" concept smuggles in the capitalist's perspective—the worker's wage counts as the nation's output but not as the nation's \"net\" gain, revealing whose standpoint the category encodes. This prepares the attack on Say, who conflates gross and net output entirely. Say's Fantasy and the Vicious Circle of the Dogma Marx now names the target directly. \"Such fantasies as those of Monsieur Say... to the effect that the entire output, the total gross product of a nation, is reducible to net output... are simply the necessary final expression of the absurd dogma that has pervaded all political economy since Adam Smith, to the effect that the value of commodities can be ultimately broken down into wages, profit and rent.\" An extended footnote (note 51) quotes Ricardo's own devastating reductio against Say: since according to Say the cost of production \"consists of rent, wages and profits,\" there \"can, then, be no net produce\"—you cannot deduct the cost of production from the gross produce when the cost of production is the gross produce. Ricardo's summary is \"Take a whole from a whole, and nothing remains.\" But Marx immediately notes the irony: \"As we shall see later on, however, Ricardo never rejected Smith's erroneous analysis of commodity price, his resolution of this into the value sum of the revenues. He did not bother with it, and assumes its correctness in his analyses by 'abstracting' from the constant portion of commodity value. He thus falls occasionally into the same way of looking at things.\" Ricardo sees the absurdity but does not extract the correct doctrine; he simply ignores C. This is an important nuance—Marx's polemic includes his own ally and intellectual forebear. The dogma's seductiveness is then made vivid by stating the paradox from the practical side. \"It is extremely easy, of course, in the case of the individual capitalist, to see that one part of his product must be transformed back into capital (even ignoring... accumulation), and moreover not into variable capital but into constant capital, which can never be transformed into income. The simplest perception of the production process makes this obvious. The difficulty begins only when the production process is considered as a whole, on a large scale.\" Marx then lays out the two horns explicitly. On the one hand , an undeniable practical fact: the value of the revenue-consumed part of the product \"actually disappears completely, on analysis, into the sum of value formed from wages plus profit plus rent,\" even though it still \"contains a portion of value = C.\" On the other hand , \"an equally undeniable theoretical contradiction.\" The fraudulent solution: to claim that the extra value component \"is only from the standpoint of the individual capitalist\" and to invoke the maxim that \"what appears as revenue for one person forms capital for another.\" Marx's relentless objection: \"How, if the value of the entire product can be consumed in the form of revenues, the old capital can be replaced, and how the value of the product of each individual capital can be equal to the value sum of the three revenues plus C, the constant capital, while the combined value sum of the products of all capitals together equals the value sum of the three revenues plus 0\"—this \"can only appear as an insoluble riddle, and must be explained by alleging that analysis is quite incapable of catching hold of the simple elements of price and must instead satisfy itself with a vicious circle and an infinite regress.\" The regress: \"what appears as constant capital is reducible to wages, profit and rent, but the commodity values in which wages, profit and rent are expressed are determined in turn by wages, profit and rent, and so ad infinitum.\" The doctrine is not merely wrong; it is hypostasized wrongness —an admission of analytical bankruptcy dressed as profundity. A footnote (note 52) gives Smith's own text and the horse example: Smith concedes that \"a fourth part\" (for replacing the farmer's stock and the wear and tear of his instruments) might seem necessary, but argues the price of any instrument—say a labouring horse—\"is itself made up of the same three parts.\" Marx's comment is that this is \"nothing more than an evasion for him to send us from pillar to post,\" and that Smith \"never indicates the actual capital investment in which the price of the product is 'ultimately' resolved simply into these three parts.\" Smith senses the contradiction, gestures at resolving it, and fails—which is why the confusion propagated. Marx also flags the confusion in monetary terms: the dogma \"is thus expressed in the contention that the consumer has ultimately to pay the total value of the total product; or that the monetary circulation between producers and consumers must ultimately be equal to the monetary circulation between the producers themselves (Tooke)\"—citing Thomas Tooke's An Inquiry into the Currency Principle (1844). Both contentions are \"as false as the fundamental principle on which they are based.\" The Five Sources of Error: A Systematic Compendium Marx now organizes the diagnosis of the dogma into a numbered series of \"problems.\" This compendium is the analytical spine of the chapter and deserves careful unpacking, because each item is a distinct theoretical failure that the reproduction analysis corrects. (1) The basic relationship of constant and variable capital is not understood , \"and so neither is the nature of surplus-value and with it the entire basis of the capitalist mode of production.\" The reasoning: every commodity includes value = constant capital + variable capital + surplus-value. If wages, profit, and rent together exhaust the value of the commodities that the recipients of income must buy, then those revenues—whose sum is V + S—would have to buy commodities whose total value is C + V + S. \"How can a value of four be bought with a value of three?\" Marx appends a footnote (note 53) engaging Proudhon , who expressed the impossibility in the \"narrow-minded formula\" that \"the worker cannot buy back his own product\" because interest is added above the prix-de-revient (cost price to the worker); and Eugène Forcade , who \"correctly generalizes the problem\" (the capitalist likewise could not buy back with his profit, since the constant portion is also added on) but then \"solves the riddle by a meaningless phrase—the growth of capital.\" Marx's devastating analogy: \"What would be said of a chemist who, when asked how it is that the soil's product contains more carbon than the soil itself, gave the answer that this came from the steady growth in agricultural production?\" He adds that in \"vulgar economics, the well-meaning good intention of finding the bourgeois world to be the best of all possible worlds makes any desire for truth and any impulse towards scientific investigation unnecessary.\" A footnote also flags that Proudhon's famous conclusion \"property is theft\" comes from this work, and characterizes Forcade as a \"vulgar economist\" whose antipathy to Proudhon \"expressed little more than class interest.\" (2) It is not understood how and in what way labour, while it adds new value, also preserves old value in a new form, without producing this value afresh. This is the double-function-of-labour point (Volume 1, Chapter 7) restated as a diagnosis: the second, value-preserving function is invisible to those who see only the value created. (3) The interconnection of the reproduction process is not understood —\"as this presents itself not from the standpoint of the individual capital, but rather from that of the total capital.\" The specific questions: how the product in which wages and surplus-value are realized \"can replace its constant value portion and still be reducible to a value defined simply by revenues\"; and how the constant capital consumed \"can be replaced materially and in value by a new capital, even though the total sum of newly added labour is realized only in wages and surplus-value, and is exhaustively expressed in the sum of these two.\" Marx underlines: \"It is precisely here that the principal difficulty lies.\" (4) There is a further problem , sharper once the different components of surplus-value appear as mutually independent revenues: \"the firm determinations of revenue and capital change places and shift, so that they seem to be only relative determinations pertaining to the standpoint of the individual capitalist.\" The concrete instance: the revenue of workers and capitalists in Department I \"replaces the constant capital in department II,\" so that \"what is revenue for one is capital for another,\" and the definitions \"seem to vanish altogether when the total production process is in view.\" Further, commodities destined for revenue \"pass through various stages in the course of the year, e.g. woollen yarn, cloth. At one stage, they form part of constant capital, at another they are consumed individually and go entirely into revenue.\" This diachronic transformation tempts one—\"as Adam Smith did\"—to treat constant capital as \"merely an apparent element of commodity value, which disappears in the overall context.\" Marx adds the exchange of variable capital for revenue: the worker buys his revenue portion with his wage, returning the money form of variable capital to the capitalist. And some constant-capital products \"are replaced in kind or by exchange between the producers of the constant capital itself, a process that seems to have nothing to do with the consumers.\" When all this is overlooked, \"the illusion arises that the consumers' revenue replaces the entire product, including the constant portion of value.\" (5) Apart from the confusion produced by the transformation of values into prices of production , a second confusion arises from \"the transformation of surplus-value into various separate, mutually independent forms related to the various elements of production, into profit and rent.\" It is forgotten that commodity values are the base; that the breakdown of value into components, and the transformation of those components into revenue forms, \"their transformation into relations that the various owners of the different agents of production have to these particular value components\"—distribution according to \"particular categories and titles\"—\"in no way alter the value determination and its law.\" Likewise, the equalization of profit (and the obstacles landed property places before it in absolute rent) generates divergent average prices, but this \"does not abolish surplus-value itself, nor the total value of commodities as the source of these various price components.\" Here Marx again affirms the priority of the total value over its distributional forms—the point with which he opened the chapter. The Quid Pro Quo: Value Appearing to Arise from Its Components The chapter's diagnostic centerpiece now crystallizes. Marx declares: \"This is the quid pro quo which we shall discuss in the following chapter, and it is necessarily connected with the illusion that value arises from its own components.\" He reconstructs the illusion's genesis: \"Firstly, the various value components of commodities receive independent forms in the revenues and are related to the particular material elements of production as their sources, instead of to the value of the commodities as their single source.\" They are indeed related to the elements of production—\"but not as components of value, rather as revenues, as value components accruing to these particular categories of agents of production, the worker, the capitalist and the landowner.\" Hence the inversion: \"It is possible then to imagine that these components of value, instead of arising from the decomposition of commodity value, actually give rise to it by coming together. This then leads to the neat vicious circle in which the value of commodities arises from the value sum of wages, profit and rent, while the value of wages, profit and rent is determined in turn by the value of commodities, etc.\" This is the logical form of the error stated with maximum clarity: two mutually dependent terms, with no ground beneath either. Marx's remedy is the asymmetry : commodity value is the ground, and revenues are its decomposed forms—one-way, not reciprocal. A footnote (note 54) engages Storch (Henri Storch), who \"understands the absurdity of the conclusion which follows from the false analysis of commodity value that resolves it just into revenue, and correctly points out the ridiculous character of this result—from the standpoint of a nation rather than that of the individual capitalist. But he does not himself take a single step further in his analysis of the prix nécessaire.\" Storch recognizes the impossibility of resolving necessary price into \"its actual elements\" and correctly ridicules Say's doctrine (asking whether a peasant family could live in its barns, eat its seed-corn, slaughter its draught cattle for clothes, and use its implements to satisfy needs—which under Say's doctrine would have to be answered in the affirmative). Storch's insight that \"the products that make up a nation's capital are not consumable\" is correct, but he stops short of the full reproduction analysis. The Insurance Fund, the Abolition of Capitalism, and the Savage Analogy Marx now turns to a theme that seems like a digression but is structurally essential: the portion of profit that functions as an insurance fund . In the normal state of reproduction, only part of the freshly added labour is applied to replacing constant capital—precisely the part that replaces the constant capital used up in producing the means of consumption (Department II's constant capital). But the constant capital that is not the product of freshly added labour, though it must exist for production, \"is exposed during the reproduction process, in its material aspect, to accidents and dangers that may decimate it. (It may also depreciate in value as the result of a change in the productivity of labour.)\" Therefore \"one part of the profit accordingly serves as an insurance fund, and thus also a part of the surplus-value and surplus product in which the freshly added labour is expressed.\" It is irrelevant whether insurance is a separate business. This is \"the only part of the revenue that is neither consumed as such nor serves necessarily as an accumulation fund.\" Whether it actually offsets losses or accumulates is \"a matter of chance.\" Now comes a remarkable theoretical counterfactual. \"This is also the only part of surplus-value and the surplus product, and thus of surplus labour, leaving aside the part serving for accumulation... that would have to continue in existence after the abolition of the capitalist mode of production.\" Marx's point: even in a socialist society, provision must be made for the repair and replacement of means of production damaged by accident—a fund that is neither individual consumption nor net accumulation. And in such a society, \"the part regularly consumed by the direct producers would not remain confined to its present minimum level.\" Beyond surplus labour for those too young or too old to participate, \"there would be no other labour for the maintenance of non-workers.\" Marx then reasons from the \"beginnings of society\" to clarify the material logic of capital-formation in abstraction from its historical form. In primitive conditions, \"no produced means of production are yet in existence\"—no constant capital whose value must be replaced in kind. But nature directly provides means of subsistence, so the savage has the time, \"if not to use the means of production not yet in existence for new production, then—besides the labour that it takes to appropriate the means of subsistence given by nature—to transform other natural products into means of production, a bow, a stone knife, a boat, etc.\" This \"completely corresponds, taking simply the material aspect, to the transformation of surplus labour back into new capital.\" The savage's tool-making is surplus labour embodied in a means of production—the material essence of accumulation, stripped of the capitalist form. Marx draws a critical implication: the fact that \"all new capital arises from profit, rent or other forms of revenue, i.e. from surplus labour, gives rise to the false idea that all commodity value arises from a revenue.\" On the contrary, the transformation of profit into capital \"shows the opposite on closer analysis, i.e. it shows that the additional labour—which always takes the form of revenue—serves not to maintain or reproduce the old capital value but rather to create new and additional capital, in so far as it is not consumed as revenue.\" Profit into Capital: What Is Really Transformed Marx now sharpens the analysis of accumulation against a subtle error. The entire problem \"arises from the way that all freshly added labour, in so far as the value it creates is not reducible to wages, appears as profit—conceived here as the general form of surplus-value, i.e. a value that costs the capitalist nothing, so that it also certainly does not have to replace anything advanced, any capital.\" Because surplus-value appears as \"additional available wealth,\" it looks to the individual capitalist like pure revenue. \"But this newly created value can be consumed productively as well as individually, as capital as well as revenue. Its natural form already dictates that it must in part be consumed productively.\" Thus \"the labour added each year creates both capital and revenue.\" The conceptual trap is that the part of labour-power used for creating new capital \"becomes invisible, because the entire product of the surplus labour presents itself first of all in the form of profit, a characteristic which in fact has nothing to do with this surplus product itself, but simply bears on the private relationship between the capitalist and the surplus-value he pockets.\" So \"the surplus-value that the worker creates breaks down into revenue and capital; i.e. into means of consumption and additional means of production. But the old constant capital handed down from the previous year... is not reproduced in value by the freshly added labour.\" Marx then generalizes the point that was established earlier for the simple-reproduction case: \"one part of the freshly added labour is always absorbed in the reproduction and replacement of the constant capital consumed, even if this freshly added labour can be completely resolved into revenues—wages, profit and rent.\" Two things are overlooked in this connection: \"(1) that the value component of the product of this labour is not the product of the freshly added labour but constant capital that was already in existence and has been used up\"—so the product portion embodying this value component \"is not transformed into revenue but replaces the means of production of this constant capital in kind\"; \"(2) that the value component in which this freshly added labour is actually expressed is not consumed in kind as revenue but rather replaces the constant capital in another sphere, where it is converted into a natural form in which it can be consumed as revenue, even if this is not exclusively the product of freshly added labour.\" This is the interdepartmental exchange stated in its most general form: the newly created value is realized in Department I's product (means of production), and it is consumed as revenue only after being exchanged for Department II's product—while Department I's product simultaneously goes to replace constant capital. Marx then analyzes the effect of changes in productivity on replacement. \"As long as reproduction proceeds on the same scale, each element of constant capital used up must be replaced—at least in efficacy if not in quantity and form—by a new item of the appropriate kind.\" With unchanged productivity, \"the replacement in kind involves the replacement of the same value.\" But \"if the productivity of labour rises, so that the same material elements can be reproduced with less labour, a smaller value component of the product can replace the constant part fully in kind. The surplus can then go towards forming new additional capital, or the surplus labour can be reduced.\" Conversely, \"if the productivity of labour declines... a greater part of the product must go into replacing the old capital; the surplus product declines.\" The size of the surplus therefore depends materially on the productivity of the labour that produces means of production—an essential dynamism underlying accumulation. Now Marx addresses the precise conceptual nature of \"profit becoming capital.\" Abstracting from the specific historical form and considering only \"the formation of new means of production,\" the transformation of profit back into capital \"shows that there is always a situation in which the worker spends additional labour on producing means of production, on top of that spent in obtaining his immediate means of subsistence.\" The transformation of profit into capital \"is nothing more than the use of a part of the additional labour in forming new and additional means of production.\" If this happens in the form of profit becoming capital, \"it simply means that it is not the worker but the capitalist who has the surplus labour at his disposal.\" If this surplus labour must first pass through a stage where it appears as revenue (whereas in the savage case it appears immediately as labour directed to producing means of production), \"it simply means that this labour, or its product, is appropriated by the non-worker.\" Then the deepest conceptual point: \"What is actually transformed into capital is not profit as such. The transformation of surplus-value into capital simply means that the surplus-value and surplus product are not individually consumed by the capitalist as revenue. What really is transformed in this way is value, objectified labour, or the product in which this value is directly expressed, or against which it is exchanged, after prior conversion into money.\" And: \"Even if profit is transformed back into capital, it is not this specific form of surplus-value, profit, that forms the source of the new capital. Surplus-value, in this connection, is simply transformed from one form into the other. But it is not this formal transformation that makes it into capital. It is the commodity and its value that now function as capital.\" Finally: \"it is completely immaterial for the objectification of this labour, for value itself, that the value of the commodity is not paid—and it is only in this way that it becomes surplus-value.\" In other words: the revenue form is a distributive appearance; the substance that becomes capital is value, objectified labour, regardless of whose hands it passes through or in what revenue category it is booked. The Yarn and the Weaver: Refuting \"Revenue for One, Capital for Another\" Marx now returns, with renewed precision, to the evasion that \"commodities which constant capital consists of themselves contain elements of wages, profit and rent,\" or that \"what is revenue for one person is capital for another, and that these are therefore simply subjective relationships.\" His counterexample is the classic yarn example. \"The spinner's yarn contains a value component that represents profit for him. If the weaver buys the yarn, he realizes the spinner's profit, but as far as he is concerned this yarn is simply part of his constant capital.\" So the same yarn is profit to one and constant capital to another. Does this make the distinction subjective? No. Marx states the decisive point: \"what goes into the weaver's capital with the yarn, as a constituent element considered in value terms, is the yarn's value. How the components of this value might be reducible for the spinner into capital and revenue, in other words into paid and unpaid labour, is a matter of complete indifference for determining the value of the commodity itself (apart from the modifications owing to average profit).\" The value of the yarn—its determinate magnitude, measured by socially necessary labour—is objective; what fractions of it appear to the spinner as \"capital\" versus \"revenue\" is irrelevant to the weaver, who simply needs to correctly reckon the yarn's value into his costs. Marx then diagnoses the underlying presupposition at work: \"Always lurking in the background is the idea that profit, and surplus-value in general, is an excess over and above the value of the commodity, which is made only by a surcharge, by mutual cheating, by profit on alienation.\" This is the mercantilist/folk conception that profit is a surcharge on value rather than a fraction of it. Marx's refutation: \"since the production price of the commodity is paid, or even its value, so too are those value components of the commodity that appear to their seller in the form of revenue.\" Pay the value, and you have paid the revenue components—because the revenue components are fractions of the value, not additions to it. He adds the qualification that \"monopoly prices, of course, are not at issue here\"—since monopoly prices can indeed involve transfers, but the basic analysis concerns value-conforming or production-price-paying transactions. Marx grants partial correctness to the other half of the evasion: \"it is quite correct that the commodity components which constant capital consists of are reducible like all other commodity value to value components that could be resolved for their producers and the owners of the means of production into wages, profit and rent.\" This is just \"the capitalist way of expressing the fact that commodity value is always just the measure of the socially necessary labour contained in a commodity.\" But—and here is the resolution—\"as we have already shown in Volume 1, this in no way prevents the commodity product of a capital from breaking down into separate components, of which one exclusively represents the constant capital component, another the variable capital component and a third simply the surplus-value.\" The genealogical reducibility of every value back to labour (paid and unpaid) is compatible with the decompositional structure of any given product into C, V, and S. Confusing these two different operations—the regress back to ultimate sources versus the analysis of a given product's components—is what generates the vicious circle. The Nation as a False Abstraction; Value Determination After Capitalism The chapter closes by engaging Storch's proposal to assess a nation's revenue \"not according to its value, but rather according to its utility, or according to the needs which it can satisfy\" ( Considerations sur la nature du revenu national , p. 19). Marx's reply is two-pronged and, for the reader of Capital , programmatically important. First , \"it is a false abstraction to treat a nation whose mode of production is based on value, and organized capitalistically into the bargain, as a unified body simply working for the national needs.\" The nation is not a homogeneous collectivity of producers meeting needs; it is a class-divided, value-regulated, capitalistically organized totality, and to abstract from this is to abstract from everything that determines its economic life. The \"national\" standpoint that would dissolve the \"value for individuals\" into \"utility for the nation\" is precisely the standpoint that already forgets the social relations binding the whole together. Second —and this is one of the most quoted sentences in Capital —\"even after the capitalist mode of production is abolished, though social production remains, the determination of value still prevails in the sense that the regulation of labour-time and the distribution of social labour among various production groups becomes more essential than ever, as well as the keeping of accounts on this.\" Here Marx makes an extraordinary forward gesture. Value determination, in its foundational sense—the allocation of social labour-time across branches of production according to socially necessary proportions—is not merely a capitalist phenomenon. It is a natural necessity of any society with developed productive forces and a division of labour; and socialist society will have to regulate it consciously and systematically, \"more essential than ever,\" with rigorous accounting. The chapter thus ends not with a merely critical result but with a positive constructive implication: the labor-time accounting that the dogma obscured is a permanent feature of social production, and it will attain its fullest importance only when society consciously organizes what capitalism leaves to the blind operation of value. This final move also circles back, elegantly, to the chapter's opening simplification. Marx began by bracketing the value/price-of-production distinction precisely because his subject was value in its totality. He ends by insisting that value determination itself —the regulation of socially necessary labour-time—is the enduring substrate beneath both capitalist forms of revenue and any socialist successor. The \"analysis of the production process\" has thus moved from a technical proof about the two components A and B, through a devastating diagnosis of \"Smith's dogma,\" to a foundational statement about the law of value as the general regulator of social labour. That is the arc the chapter completes, and it establishes the ground on which the following chapter's treatment of the \"quid pro quo\" and the final chapter's \"Trinity Formula\" will rest. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS A. Fundamental principles and systemic laws revealed. The chapter yields at least five durable principles. First , the principle of the priority of the whole over its parts in value analysis : commodity value is the original unity; wages, profit, and rent are decompositions of it, never its sources. The whole asymmetry—value→revenues, never revenues→value—is the indispensable condition of any coherent theory of value. Second , the principle of the dual function of labour : the same concrete labour that creates new value (V + S) simultaneously, by its specific productive character, preserves the value of consumed means of production in the product, without creating that value anew. This is why constant capital can appear in the product's value without any additional labour being \"used up\" in reproducing it as value . Third , the material-versus-value duality of capital replacement : the value-preservation of C is free, but its material replacement requires labour—labour that is itself embodied in the output of Department I and that circulates as capital, never as revenue. This distinction between value and material form is what dissolves the false \"value of four from a value of three\" paradox. Fourth , the equilibrium structure of simple reproduction , I(v + s) = II(c), which expresses a necessary correspondence between the revenue of the producers of means of production and the constant capital required by the producers of consumption goods. This is not an accounting identity but a material and social requirement: \"otherwise department II cannot replace its constant capital, nor department I convert its revenue from non-consumable into consumable form.\" Fifth , the foundational character of value determination as labour-time regulation —a principle that survives the abolition of capitalism and, in Marx's view, becomes \"more essential than ever.\" B. Consequences for the theory of value and its rivals. The chapter's demolition of the Smithian dogma eliminates a whole family of errors at once: the naive \"cost-of-production\" theory (which resolves price into costs, which themselves resolve into prices), the \"surcharge\" or \"profit on alienation\" theory (that profit is an excess above value made by cheating), the Say/Forcade optimism (that a nation's gross output can be wholly consumed as revenue), and the \"revenue for one, capital for another\" evasion (which treats all distinctions between C and revenue as merely subjective or relative to standpoint). All of these are forms of the same fundamental failure: treating the revenue forms as the sources of value rather than its decomposed results . The chapter thereby vindicates the labor theory of value not as an abstract dogma but as a necessary structural precondition for accounting for the reproduction of a capitalistically organized economy at the aggregate level. Marx emphasizes that the unavoidable contradiction arises \"only when the production process is considered as a whole, on a large scale\"—so the error is specifically a failure of macro-theoretical thinking , and its correction is likewise a macro-theoretical achievement grounded in the two-department schema. This makes Chapter 49 a crucial demonstration that the schemas of Volume 2 are not a mere exercise but the indispensable apparatus for the theory of revenue, distribution, and the reproduction of the social totality. C. Theoretical caveats and Marx's self-awareness. Marx is scrupulous about the limits of the argument. He brackets value/price-of-production divergence (justified by the cancellation at the aggregate level), brackets accumulation (reintroduced as an illustration), distinguishes realized from produced surplus-value (allowing that some surplus-value is never realized in price), distinguishes wages from unproductive labour paid out of revenue, and carefully localizes the irreducibility of C into revenues to the standpoint of the total social capital . He also acknowledges the genuine correctness of the claim that every commodity value is ultimately reducible to paid and unpaid labour (the \"socially necessary labour\" content), while showing that this genealogical fact is entirely compatible with the decompositional fact that any given product breaks down into C, V, and S. This dual recognition is the chapter's subtlest analytical exactitude: he does not deny the reducibility of value to labour; he denies only the confusion of that regress with the answer to the reproduction question. D. Broader practical and political implications. The chapter's consequences reach far beyond academic economics. If an entire portion of the social product (C) must return to production and can never be a source of anyone's consumable income, then the apologetic fantasy that a capitalist nation's full output is available for consumption is exposed as ideology. The workers' and capitalists' revenues are structurally insufficient to buy the entire product—and this is not a defect but a structural necessity of reproducing the material basis of society. Proudhon's \"the worker cannot buy back his own product\" is shown to be a misstatement of a real structural fact (the constant portion can never be bought back by any revenue at all), and Forcade's \"growth of capital\" answer is shown to be no answer at all. This has implications for every theory of aggregate demand, crises of overproduction (where the inability of revenues to purchase the full product becomes practically explosive), and the relation between production and consumption. Philosophically , the chapter's closing insistence that value determination persists after capitalism—that \"the regulation of labour-time and the distribution of social labour among the various production groups becomes more essential than ever\"—stakes out Marx's position on the continuity between capitalist and post-capitalist economic accounting: socialist society does not escape the material necessity of measuring and allocating social labour-time; it confronts it with greater rigor and consciousness than capitalism, whose regulation is left to the blind interplay of commodity values. The law of value, here, appears not simply as a description of capitalist anarchy but as the historically general form of the rational allocation of social labour—the \"substance\" that all societies ignore at their peril. The \"analysis of the production process\" thus culminates in the demand for conscious, systematic social accounting, a demand whose reasonableness is demonstrated by the very paralysis into which the dogma plunges capitalist political economy—\"a vicious circle and an infinite regress,\" and nothing more. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 48 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 50"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 50", "title": "Chapter 50", "subtitle": "Illusions Created by Competition", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 50/Chapter 50 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 12430, "read_time": "62 min read", "tags": ["The Trinity Formula & Class Relations", "Fetishism & Mystification of Capital"], "keywords": ["Illusions Created by Competition", "Cost Price Appearance as Inherent Value", "Market Mechanisms Obscuring Exploitation", "Everyday Experience Inverting Fundamental Laws", "Vulgar Economists Treating Distortions as Primary Facts"], "text": "Chapter 50 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 12,413 words ⏱️ ~62 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation Chapter 50: The Illusion Created by Competition — Exhaustive Scholarly Deconstruction 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem Chapter 50 of Capital , Volume III, closes the unfinished Part VII, \"The Revenues and Their Sources,\" with what is in effect the epistemological capstone of Marx's entire critique of political economy. Having constructed the machinery of value, surplus-value, the rate of profit, the transformation of values into prices of production, and the various forms of revenue across the preceding chapters, Marx now turns to a question that is not technical but phenomenological and ideological : why does the real underlying causal structure of capitalist production inevitably present itself to its own agents — and to the economists who theorize them — in an inverted form? The chapter's title states its thesis in miniature: there is an illusion , and it is created by competition — or, more precisely, by the surface movement through which the actually determined value magnitudes of the total social product reappear as mutually independent, self-determining \"sources\" of value. The intellectual objective is to demonstrate that the appearance according to which wages, profit, and rent are independent constituent elements whose sum produces, limits, and determines the price of commodities is not merely a logical mistake but a necessary inversion generated by the way the capitalist mode of production continually reproduces itself out of its own premises. The illusion is the structure of appearances itself, and it has a social cause, not merely a cognitive one. The central dialectical proposition of the chapter can be stated in one sentence: commodity value is the prius , the original quantitative unity, and wages/profit/rent are parts into which it is dissolved; the illusion consists in reversing this, so that the parts become the premises and the value becomes their sum. Everything else in the chapter — the numerical tables, the critique of the wage circularity, the five \"reasons why the quid pro quo is necessary\" — elaborates and defends this single inversion. The Chapter's Place in the Theoretical Framework This chapter is the practical conclusion of a long build. In Chapter 48 (the \"trinity formula,\" capital–profit/interest, land–rent, labour–wages) Marx had shown that the \"sources\" of revenue correspond to the \"sources of wealth\" only in a mystified way. In Chapter 49 he had investigated why the value of the total product dissolves into wages, profit, and rent in the immediate production process itself. Chapter 50 pushes one decisive step further: it asks how this dissolution appears on the surface, and how that appearance hardens into an apparently self-evident theory of value as the sum of its parts . It belongs, therefore, to the family of chapters in Volume III that address not the quantitative mechanics of distribution but the forms in which those mechanics must represent themselves: the fetishism of capital, the appearance that land yields rent and capital yields profit as natural properties. Chapter 50 provides the most general and most sustained treatment of what one may call — in Marx's own vocabulary — the ideological categories of the economic agents, or, as the chapter itself phrases it, the mode in which the actual determinations are \"represented on the immediately visible surface of capitalist production, and hence in the minds of the agents trapped within it, in a distorted way.\" Two consequences follow. First, this chapter is one of the most important bridge-texts between Marx's economic theory and his theory of consciousness/ideology, since it grounds the categories of bourgeois economic thought (the very categories through which everyone, worker and capitalist alike, \"experiences\" the economy) in the reality of the reproduction process itself , not in error or deception. Second, because it is the final substantive chapter of the manuscript edited by Engels, it functions as an implicit methodological verdict on the whole tradition of classical political economy — Adam Smith's \"adding-up\" theory of value, Say's traité , and the vulgar economists' reliance on \"competition\" as an explanatory principle. Ricardo is mainly spared here (footnote 56 allies Marx with him), but the \"components\" theory is demolished. The Methodological Stakes Marx's move is methodologically sharp in a way that is easy to miss. He does not argue that the components-view is false because empirically contradicted . He argues that (a) it is conceptually self-dissolving — it collapses into circularity, tautology, and the disappearance of any concept of value; and (b) it is nonetheless practically necessary — it is what the agents of production must think, because that is how their social relations reproduce themselves in their daily contracts, account books, and price calculations. The dialectical climax is the sentence: \"Its result thus constantly appears as its premise, and its premises as its results.\" The illusion is not an optical error that could be corrected by better knowledge; it is a real inversion, a structural feature of a system that \"constantly reproduces not only the material product but also the socio-economic relations, the formal economic determinants of its formation.\" The chapter thus belongs at the intersection of three of Marx's lasting contributions: the value-form analysis, the theory of the transformation of values into prices of production, and the critique of ideology as socially necessary appearance. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS 2.1 The Opening Recapitulation: The Three Components of Commodity Value (pp. 992–993) Marx opens not with a definition but with a résumé of everything already established, because the entire illusion he is about to expose depends on the reader keeping this prior analysis firmly in view. The value of commodities — or, what governs it in developed capitalism, the price of production — can be resolved into three components: A constant capital component (c), which replaces constant capital. This is the value representing labour already past , spent as means of production. Crucially, its magnitude is fixed by the value (or price) at which these means of production enter the production process . It is a \"replace[ment]\" — a mere restitution of what was consumed, transferring the value of the consumed means of production into the new product without creating new value. A variable capital component (v), which measures the worker's income and becomes wages. This is the value component the worker has reproduced — it represents \"the paid portion of the labour freshly added\" to the constant portion. Surplus-value (s) — the value component in which unpaid or surplus labour is expressed. This third component then assumes independent revenue forms : profit on capital (which itself splits into interest on capital as such, and profit of enterprise on functioning capital), and ground-rent , falling to the landowner. A decisive qualification is inserted immediately: when Marx says \"commodity,\" he means commodity capital — the product of capital over a definite period (e.g. one year), of which the individual commodity is only one element. The individual commodity's value breaks down into \"the same analogous components,\" but the aggregation matters, because the laws of distribution and the illusion of independence operate at the level of the total social product and its periodic reproduction, not only at the level of the single item. The key discriminator, stated explicitly, is that components (2) and (3) contain the whole of the value in which the labour freshly added is objectified . That is: the fresh labour is exhausted within v + s. There is no leftover. This is the pivot of everything that follows — the sum v + s is a fixed, given magnitude (in Marx's notation below, 250), and it is this fixed magnitude that is then divided. The constant component (1) is different in kind: it is old value transferred, not new value created. 2.2 The Founding Distinction: Components of Value vs. Constituent Elements of Value (p. 993) Here Marx draws the distinction without which the entire chapter is unintelligible. Leaving the constant component aside, \"the value of a commodity, in so far as this represents freshly added labour, is always reducible to three elements, wages, profit and rent, which constitute the three forms of revenue,\" while the respective value magnitudes (the aliquot parts) are determined by different, specific laws that have already been developed. But — and this is the crucial wrong inference — \"It would be wrong however to say that the value of wages, the rate of profit and the rate of rent are independent constituent elements of value, with the value of the commodity, minus its constant component, arising from their combination.\" In other words: TRUE : the fresh value is divided into wages, profit, and rent. FALSE : wages, profit, and rent are independent magnitudes that combine to produce the fresh value. The distinction is between a dissolution (a given unity splitting into parts) and a composition (given parts adding up into a unity). These are not the same operation read in opposite directions, because in the first case the whole is determined first and bounds the parts; in the second, the parts are determined first and no bound on the whole exists. Marx will spend the chapter demonstrating that the composition-view, taken seriously, is incoherent. Notice that Marx adds the important qualifier that the aliquot parts are determined \"by different, specific laws.\" This heterogeneity of causation is itself one of the reasons the illusion is generated: because the magnitudes of wages, profit, and rent obey different laws, they do not present themselves as parts of one common magnitude but as entities governed by separate, unrelated principles. 2.3 The First Numerical Model: 400c + 100v + 150s = 650 (pp. 993–994) Marx constructs a simple, controlled numerical example to make the argument concrete. Let the value produced by a capital of 500 be: 400c + 100v + 150s = 650 with the surplus-value of 150 further divided into 75 profit + 75 rent . He assumes, \"in order to avoid needless difficulties,\" a capital of average organic composition , so that its value and its price of production coincide (value = price of production whenever the individual capital is a proportionate sample of total social capital). He then reads out the derived proportions: Wages = v = 100, measured against the capital advanced (500), make up 20% of the capital advanced. Surplus-value reckoned on total capital = 150/500 = 30% (15% profit + 15% rent). The total new value component of the commodity = 100v + 150s = 250 . And here is the analytical hinge: \"Its total amount is independent of its division into wages, profit and rent.\" The 250 is fixed by the amount of labour objectified in it; the further split of that 250 into 100/75/75 is a secondary determination that cannot alter the 250. This is the \"quantitative premise\" the chapter will repeatedly defend. Marx then performs three interpretive conversions on the same numbers: (a) The ratio of paid to unpaid labour. The £100 laid out for labour-power has set in motion a quantum of labour expressed in £250. Therefore the worker has performed 1½ times as much surplus labour as labour for himself (since 150/100 = 1.5). He makes this vivid: if the working day is 10 hours, the worker works 4 hours for himself and 6 hours for the capitalist . The \"labour of the workers who are paid £100 is expressed therefore in a money value of £250.\" (b) The absolute limit. \"Apart from this value of £250, there is nothing left to be shared between worker and capitalist, or capitalist and landowner.\" The £250, added to the £400 transferred from the means of production, gives the commodity value £650. \"The commodity value of £250 thus produced, and determined by the amount of labour objectified in it, sets the limit to the dividends that worker, capitalist and landlord can draw.\" (c) The class-distribution implication. This is not a mathematical curiosity; it says that whatever the wage-bargain, the profit-rate, or the rent-level, all parties are drawing on one bounded pool . The whole point of the chapter is the systematic obscuring of this boundedness. 2.4 The Second Model and the Marginal Arithmetic of the Wage Shift (pp. 994–995) Marx now changes one variable and holds everything else constant to isolate its effect. Suppose the same capital of the same organic composition must pay £150 instead of £100 for the same labour-power, while the constant capital remains £400 and the quantity of labour set in motion is unchanged. Then: 400c + 150v + 100s = 650 , with the surplus-value of 100 now split (in the same proportion as before) into 45 profit + 55 rent . The derived proportions are transformed: Wages = 150/550 = 27 3/11% of the capital advanced. Profit = 45/550 = 8 2/11% on capital advanced. Rent = 55/550 = 10% on capital advanced. Total surplus-value = 100/550 ≈ 18 2/11% on capital advanced. And again the interpretive conversion of the labour day : with s/v = 66⅔%, the worker now works 6 hours for himself and only 4 for the capitalist . The surplus labour has fallen. Marx then distils the differential behaviour of the three rates, which he tabulates: Value of the product New value Rate of surplus-value Gross rate of profit First case 400c + 100v + 150s = 650 250 150% 30% Second case 400c + 150v + 100s = 650 250 66⅔% 18 2/11% Here \"gross rate of profit\" is defined explicitly by Marx as the ratio of the total surplus-value to the total capital advanced — i.e., here, s/(c+v) = 150/500 and 100/550 respectively. (This is what is elsewhere called simply the rate of profit.) The comparison yields three precise claims: The mass of surplus-value falls by one-third (150 → 100). The rate of profit falls proportionately more than the mass of surplus-value (from 30% to ~18%), \"since the diminished surplus-value has to be reckoned against an increased total capital advanced\" — the denominator has grown from 500 to 550. But the rate of profit falls less than the rate of surplus-value. The rate of surplus-value falls from 150% to 66⅔% (a fall of 5/9, or 83⅓ percentage points); the rate of profit falls only from 30% to 18 2/11% (a fall of about 2/5). Therefore: rate of profit falls proportionately more than the mass of surplus-value, though less than the rate of surplus-value. This is a precise, non-trivial result about the elasticities of the various profit measures, and it matters because it is exactly the heterogeneity of these movements that experience cannot decode. The agents observe the profit rate falling; they do not observe why the fall is neither proportional to the fall in surplus-value nor to the fall in the rate of surplus-value. Marx adds an economic observation about the expansion of the capital advanced : an increased variable component of capital makes a new business harder to open (more capital required), but for reproduction as a whole , \"an increase in variable capital means nothing more than that a greater part of the value newly created by the freshly added labour is transformed into wages, and hence first of all into variable capital, instead of into surplus-value and surplus product.\" The value of the product is unchanged because it is defined by (i) the £400 constant value and (ii) the £250 fresh value — both unchanged. And if that product re-entered constant capital, it would still represent the same amount of use-value within the same amount of value . This is the substance of the dissolution-not-composition claim: raising wages does not raise the value of the product; it reallocates a given quantity of value. 2.5 The Cause/Effect Inversion: Wage Rises, Productivity, and the \"Dearness\" Illusion (p. 996) Marx now introduces a critical distinction between two different causes of a wage rise , which lead to opposite conclusions about the product's value: Case A — the worker keeps a greater part of his own labour (distributional wage rise). If the worker keeps a greater share, the value of the product is unchanged (the analysis of §2.4). Case B — the productivity of labour has declined (productivity-driven wage rise). Here the worker keeps a greater part of his own labour because a unit of labour now yields less product . \"The total value in which the same labour was expressed, i.e. paid and unpaid, would remain the same; but this quantity of labour would be expressed in a diminished product, i.e. the price of each aliquot part of the product would rise, since each part represented more labour.\" Concretely: the increased wage of £150 represents a product no larger than £100 did before; the diminished surplus-value of £100 now represents only two-thirds the former product — 66⅔% of the use-values previously expressed in £100. The constant capital would also become more expensive insofar as this (now-dearer) product enters it. The crucial analytical payoff: \"The illusion arises that the rise in wages has made the product dearer; but here this is not cause but consequence of a change in the commodity's value, resulting from the diminished productivity of labour.\" That is, in Case B, the rise in wages is the effect , not the cause, of the changed value of the commodity. The appearance on the surface (wages rose, so prices rose) inverts the real causal arrow. This is the first appearance of the chapter's central methodological theme — the systematic inversion of cause and effect on the surface of capitalist production. 2.6 Constant Capital Value Movements and the Asymmetry of Compensation (pp. 996–997) Marx now turns to changes in the value of constant capital to isolate a second asymmetry. Working with the same amount of labour (still expressed in 250) but a different value of means of production: 450c + 100v + 150s = 700 (from 400c: +50) 350c + 100v + 150s = 600 (from 400c: −50) Thus, \"If the capital advanced for putting the same amount of labour in motion grows or declines, the value of the product rises or falls, conditions being otherwise identical, as long as the increase or decrease in the capital advanced derives from a change in the value magnitude of the constant capital component.\" But — and this is the sharply asymmetric result — \"It remains unaffected, on the other hand, if the increase or decrease in the capital advanced derives from a change in the value magnitude of the variable capital component, with labour productivity remaining the same.\" The reason for the asymmetry: Constant capital : an increase in its value is not compensated by any movement in the opposite direction. The value added transfers in full to the product, so a rise in c raises the product's value one-for-one. Variable capital : an increase in its value is compensated by an opposite movement in surplus-value, so that v + s is unchanged . Since v + s is exactly what labour newly adds, the total value added to the means of production is constant. This produces an observable \"law of motion\" of the surface: when the source is constant capital, an increased capital advanced is matched by a higher product value; when the source is variable capital, it is not. Note the qualification: this holds so long as labour productivity is constant. If the change in the value of variable capital stems from a change in commodity prices (i.e., from a change in productivity), then the value of the product is affected — but then, as established in §2.5, the wage movement is effect , not cause . Marx then offers a specific illustration from agriculture: if the rise of variable capital from 100v to 150v is caused not by any decline in cotton-spinning productivity but by a decline in agriculture (the sector supplying the workers' sustenance, hence raising the price of provisions), the value of the product remains unchanged . The £650 is still \"expressed in the same amount of cotton yarn as before.\" The greater part of the fresh value simply flows into wages rather than surplus-value; the yarn itself is unchanged in value and quantity. This is a strong, testable claim: general wage changes alter distribution but not the value of a given commodity produced by given labour with given means of production. 2.7 The Economy-in-Constant-Capital Case and the General Laws of the Profit Rate (pp. 997–998) Marx now extends the analysis to a third mechanism — economies in the use of constant capital. If the reduction in outlay on constant capital occurs \"in branches of production whose products go into the workers' consumption,\" this cheapens the workers' means of subsistence and may thereby reduce wages, in the same way as a direct increase in the productivity of the labour immediately applied. The profit rate then rises for two reasons : 1. Because the value of the constant capital has declined (smaller denominator); 2. Because surplus-value has increased (larger numerator, via lower wages and hence a larger surplus share of the given new value). Marx adds a crucial methodological note reconciling this with preceding chapters: in the analysis of the transformation of surplus-value into profit , he had assumed wages constant in order to isolate profit-rate fluctuations independent of changes in the rate of surplus-value. But \"the laws developed there, however, are general ones, and apply also for capital investments where the products do not go into the consumption of the workers, so that changes in the value of the product are without influence on wages.\" The increase in labour productivity applied directly raises surplus-value regardless of whether that sector's product enters workers' consumption; it is only the indirect effect (via the wage-goods basket) that requires the product to be a worker-consumption good. This §2.7 functions as a stress-test of the earlier, more restrictive model and establishes that the general laws survive when wages are released from the constant-wage ceteris paribus. 2.8 The Starred Section: The Absolute Limits of Revenue Division (pp. 998–1001) The asterisk marks a fresh, self-contained sub-argument — arguably the philosophical core of the chapter. It re-establishes, now at the level of the annual social product, the quantitative boundedness of all revenue. (a) The given sum and its arbitrary decomposition. The value newly added each year by fresh labour can be \"separated out and resolved into the different revenue forms.\" This \"in no way alters the limits of the value itself, the sum of value that is divided.\" A given figure of 100 \"always remains the same, whether it is broken down into 50 + 50, or 20 + 70 + 10, or 40 + 30 + 30.\" What is given first is the mass of commodity values to be divided; the absolute limit to the sum of the value portions is therefore given. (b) Wages as the base. \"In this delimitation, wages form the basis.\" Wages are governed by a natural law : their minimum limit is the physical minimum of means of subsistence \"that the worker must receive in order to maintain and reproduce his labour-power.\" The value of these commodities is determined by the labour-time required for their reproduction — i.e., by the portion of the working day the worker needs to produce an equivalent for his subsistence. If average subsistence costs 6 hours of average labour, the worker must spend about 6 hours working for himself. Marx immediately adds the historical qualification that saves this from crude physiologism: \"The actual value of his labour-power diverges from this physical minimum; it differs according to climate and the level of social development; it depends not only on physical needs but also on historically developed social needs, which become second nature.\" But \"in each country, this governing average wage is a given quantity at a given time.\" This is the classical Marxian doctrine of the value of labour-power as a historically and socially determined magnitude , here doing decisive work: it establishes wages as a bounded but non-physical baseline from which the other revenues are derived as remainder. (c) The absolute limits of surplus-value, profit, and rent. All other revenues have a limit: it is \"the value embodying the total working day … minus that part of it embodied in wages.\" The limit is the value representing unpaid labour. Symmetrically: - The wage has its ultimate barrier in the physical minimum of the working day spent on necessary labour. - The value component expressing surplus-value has its barrier in the physical maximum of the working day — the total daily labour-time the worker can provide while maintaining and reproducing labour-power. Since \"what we are dealing with here is the distribution of the value in which the total labour freshly added each year is expressed,\" the working day is taken as a constant quantity (though it may depart from its physical maximum). We therefore have an absolute limit for the surplus-value component, determined by \"the excess of the unpaid portion of the working day over its paid portion.\" (d) Profit as surplus-value reckoned on total capital. If this limit-bounded surplus-value is called profit when calculated on total advanced capital, then profit in absolute amount equals the surplus-value and is \"just as regularly determined in its limits.\" Profit is defined as the ratio between total surplus-value and total social capital advanced in production. Illustration: if capital = 500 (which may be millions) and surplus-value = 100, the absolute limit to the rate of profit is 20%. (e) The transformation to prices of production. The division of this social profit as measured by this rate, among capitals in different spheres, produces prices of production which diverge from commodity values and which are the actual governing averages of market prices. But — the crucial claim — \"this divergence from values abolishes neither the determination of prices by values nor the limits imposed on profit by our laws.\" A commodity's value is not equal to the capital consumed in it plus the surplus-value it contains; its price of production is the capital k consumed plus the surplus-value that falls to it by virtue of the general rate of profit (say 20%) on the capital advanced, whether consumed or merely applied. But that 20% surcharge is itself determined by the surplus-value created by the total social capital and its proportion to that capital — \"and this is why it is 20 per cent and not 10 per cent or 100 per cent.\" The transformation redistributes the social surplus-value among particular capitals \"evenly, in proportion as they form value components of this total capital\" — it does not abolish the limits. (f) Market prices and Quetelet's law. Market prices rise above or fall below production prices, but their fluctuations balance out. Marx asserts — with the surprising claim that it is \"surprising\" — \"how narrow the limits of these divergences are and how regularly they are balanced out.\" He invokes Adolphe Quetelet (1796–1874) , the Belgian mathematician who applied statistical methods to social phenomena (especially in On Man and the Development of his Faculties , 1835; English 1842), and affirms that \"the same rule of governing averages is found here as Quetelet demonstrated in connection with social phenomena.\" Note the critical footnote: Marx's attitude toward Quetelet is ambivalent . The regularities Quetelet demonstrates \"are ingenious, but not particularly significant\" — and the editor directs the reader to Marx's fuller, sardonic remarks in \"Parties and Cliques\" in Surveys from Exile (p. 279). The use of Quetelet here is thus largely illustrative , not a philosophical endorsement: Marx is not adopting statistical positivism, but using the fact of statistical regularity to underline that underlying value determination imposes narrow bounds on market-price wanderings. (g) Rent's three limits. Marx then subdivides rent: - Differential rent : When the adjustment of commodity values to production prices encounters no obstacles, \"rent is reduced to differential rent, i.e. it is restricted to the cancellation of the surplus profits that the governing prices of production would give to one section of capitalists, these now being appropriated by the landowners.\" Its limits lie in the divergences among individual profit rates arising from the general rate governing production prices. - Absolute rent : When landed property blocks this adjustment and appropriates an absolute rent, it is \"limited by the excess value of agricultural products over and above their price of production, i.e. by the excess surplus-value contained in them over and above the profits that capitals receive by virtue of the general rate of profit.\" Its limit is a specific portion of the given surplus-value . - Monopoly price : If monopolies (natural or artificial, especially landed property) make a monopoly price possible above both price of production and value, this \"does not mean that the limits fixed by commodity value are abolished. A monopoly price for certain commodities simply transfers a portion of the profit made by the other commodity producers to the commodities with the monopoly price.\" It causes a local disturbance in the distribution of surplus-value, but \"leaves unaffected the limit of the surplus-value itself.\" Marx's calculus of monopoly price then becomes precise : if the monopoly commodity is a worker's necessary consumption good, it raises wages and reduces surplus-value (as long as workers receive the value of their labour-power). It could press wages down below the value of labour-power, but \"only if they previously stood above the physical minimum.\" In that case the monopoly price is paid \"by deduction from real wages (i.e., from the amount of use-values that the worker receives for the same amount of labour) and from the profit of other capitalists.\" Crucially: \"The limits within which monopoly price affects the normal regulation of commodity prices are firmly determined and can be precisely calculated.\" (h) Summary of the starred section's logic. The division of the fresh value into wages and surplus-value is bounded by the necessary/surplus labour proportion; the division of surplus-value into profit and rent is bounded by the laws of profit-rate equalization; the division into interest and profit of enterprise is bounded only by competition (see §2.9); and even monopoly price, far from abolishing limits, merely redistributes a bounded surplus-value. The entire architecture is one of nested determinations of a bounded magnitude . 2.9 The Division of Average Profit: Interest vs. Profit of Enterprise (pp. 1001–1002) Marx now makes the sharpest contrast in the chapter. If the division of the fresh value into wages/profit/rent is bounded by laws, and the division of surplus-value into profit/rent is bounded by profit-rate equalization, then: \"With the division into interest and profit of enterprise, the average profit itself sets the limit for the two together. It supplies the given amount of value they have to share between them, and this is all they have to share. The specific ratio of this division is accidental here, i.e., it is determined exclusively by relations of competition.\" The logic is dialectically important: normally, market prices cease to diverge from their governing averages when supply and demand match — competition's effect is abolished at equilibrium , and thus competition cannot be the determinant of the equilibrium level. But here, uniquely, competition is the sole determining factor . Why? Because \"the same factor of production, capital, has to share the portion of surplus-value accruing to it between two owners of this same production factor.\" Since both interest and profit of enterprise accrue to capital as such (one as ownership of loanable capital, one as functioning capital), there is no immanent law that can discriminate between them. Two owners of the same factor divide the single pool. The key caveat: even here, \"if the division of average profit has in this case no determining limit as imposed by the laws we have developed, that does not abolish this limit as a portion of commodity value .\" Marx's analogy: \"as little as when two partners in a company share their profit unequally, because of different external circumstances, this in any way affects the limits of this profit.\" The size of the pie is law-determined; the cut between two owners may be purely the result of bargaining. 2.10 The False Conception Stated Formally: Independent Value Magnitudes (p. 1002) Marx now restates the illusion in its sharpest form so as to refute it. If the value component representing fresh labour \"breaks down into different portions, which assume mutually independent shapes in the form of revenues, this does not in any way mean that wages, profit and ground-rent are now to be considered as the constituent elements, with the governing price (natural price, prix nécessaire ) of commodities itself arising from their combination or sum.\" Characterizing the false view, Marx says: it holds that \"it would not be the commodity value, after deduction of the constant value component, that was the original unity and breaks down into these three components, but the price of each of these three components was rather determined independently, and the commodity's price formed only from the addition of these three independent magnitudes.\" Stated as a contrast: Marx's view (real) : commodity value is the quantitative premise — the sum total value of wages, profit, and rent — whatever their relative magnitudes. The false view : wages, profit, and rent are three independent value magnitudes , whose total \"produces, limits and determines the magnitude of commodity value.\" Marx notes immediately that this false view, if generalized, must extend also to the constant value component — since if three components composed prices, the same logic would apply to the input commodities that make up constant capital. This reveals that the false view \"also involves a denial of the existence of such a constant value component.\" In other words, the composition-view is not merely quantitatively wrong; it dissolves the whole category of constant capital , and with it any non-circular conception of value. 2.11 The Dissolution of Value and Money — and the Wage Circularity (pp. 1002–1004) Marx deepens the critique: on the composition-view, \"any concept of value would inevitably disappear here. All that was left would be the idea of price, in the sense that a certain amount of money is paid to the owners of labour-power, capital and land.\" But \"what is money? Money is not a thing but a particular form of value, so that it again presupposes value.\" Gold and silver are themselves commodities; their price is therefore also determined by wages, profit, and rent. So you cannot determine wages, profit, and rent by equating them with a certain quantity of gold or silver, since the value of that gold and silver, \"as the equivalent in which they are to be assessed, is precisely supposed to be determined by wages, profit and rent independently of gold and silver.\" \"To say that the value of wages, profit and rent consists in their being equal to a certain amount of gold and silver is thus simply to say that they are equal to a certain amount of wages, profit and rent.\" This is a devastating identity: the alleged measure measures itself. Marx then turns to the wage determination in detail (the longest single reductio in the chapter): Start from labour (as even the composition-view must). The governing (natural) wage is set by the supply and demand for labour-power . But whose demand? Capital's demand. So the demand for labour is the supply of capital. To speak of the supply of capital, we must know what capital is: its simplest form is money and commodities; money is simply a form of commodity; so capital = commodities. But the value of commodities is determined, in the first instance, on this same assumption, by the price of the labour producing them — wages — which is presupposed. Therefore, on this view, the price of commodities is determined by the proportion of labour on offer to capital, capital's demand for labour = the supply of capital = the supply of a sum of commodities whose price is governed, in the first instance, by the price of labour. Circle closed. \"The determination of wages cannot start from capital, for wages are themselves a factor entering into the determination of the value of capital.\" Marx then forecloses the escape route through competition : competition can make market prices of labour rise or fall, but if we assume demand and supply match , competition ceases to be determinant — \"its effect is abolished by the achievement of equilibrium between its two counteracting forces.\" And the whole point is to find the natural price of labour — \"a price of labour which is not governed by competition, but on the contrary is what governs competition.\" Nothing remains but to determine the necessary price of labour by the worker's necessary means of subsistence. But these are commodities with a price. So the price of labour is determined by the price of the necessary means of subsistence, and the price of the means of subsistence, like that of all other commodities, is determined in the first place by the price of labour. Therefore \"the price of labour is determined by itself. In other words, we do not know how the price of labour is determined.\" And crucially: \"in order to speak of the price of labour, we must know what price in general is. But this procedure does not tell us anything about price in general.\" 2.12 Competition as a Pseudo-Explanation (pp. 1004–1005) Marx now performs the same reduction on profit. The average profit must be determined by an average rate of profit; how is that determined? By competition among capitalists. But competition already assumes profit. It assumes different rates and different profits in the same branch and across branches. \"Competition can act on the profit rate only by acting on the price of commodities.\" It can only: - make producers in the same sphere sell at the same price; - make producers in different spheres sell at prices yielding the same proportionate surcharge. \"Hence competition can only even out inequalities in the rate of profit.\" It cannot create profit. It \"makes it higher or lower, but it does not create the level that is present as soon as this equalization has taken place.\" The average rate of profit appears when the competing forces balance; competition can produce the balance, but not the rate which appears when the balance is given. And the decisive forensic question: why is the general rate of profit 10, 20, or 100 per cent? Competition cannot answer, for it has abolished the causes that led to departures from those levels. \"All competition does is persistently reduce all divergences to this level. One person competes with another, and competition forces him to sell his commodity at the same price as the next man. But why is this price 10 or 20 or 100? There is nothing left for it, then, but to declare that the rate of profit and hence profit itself is a surcharge, determined in an incomprehensible way, on the partial price of a commodity as determined by wages.\" This culminates in one of Marx's most pungent methodological verdicts: \"Competition, in other words, is burdened with explaining all the economists' irrationalities, whereas it is supposed to be the economists who explain competition.\" Ground-rent needs no separate retracing. \"We can already see that if it is carried through in any consistent way, it makes profit and rent appear as mere surcharges, determined by incomprehensible laws, on top of a commodity price determined in the first place by wages.\" 2.13 The Nominal-Surcharge Detour (pp. 1005–1006) Marx now reduces the \"surcharge\" theory of profit and rent to its absurd consequence. Suppose commodity price = 100 (determined by wages); rate of profit = 10% on wages; rent = 15% on wages. Commodity price = 110 + 16.5... no — the calculation given is price = 125 (100 + 10 + 15). The surcharge of 25 cannot derive from the sale of the commodity: \"if everyone who sells to someone else sells what cost 100 at 125, it is the same thing as if they had all sold at 100.\" The operation must therefore be considered independently of the circulation process — a restatement of Marx's fundamental thesis that circulation \"can never yield anything that was not previously put into it.\" The detour is then laid bare. If the commodity actually costs 125 and the three elements are divided within it: The worker, nominally receiving 100, actually receives 4/5 of the value and product (100/125 = 4/5). The capitalist receives 2/25 of value and product (10/125). The landlord receives 3/25 (15/125). Since the capitalist sells at 125 instead of 100, he gives the worker only 4/5 of the product in which his labour is expressed. \"It would be the same thing therefore if he gave the worker 80 and kept back 20, with 8 of this accruing to him and 12 to the landlord.\" (Check: 80 + 8 + 12 = 100; profit = 10% of 80 = 8; rent = 15% of 80 = 12.) The capitalist has then sold the commodity at its value , since in fact the \"price surcharges\" are independent of the commodity's value, which on this assumption is determined by the value of wages. The upshot is that 'wages' in this conception = 100 = the value of the product, i.e., the sum of money in which a particular amount of labour is expressed; but this value is different again from real wages, and hence leaves a surplus. The \"surplus\" is now brought about via a nominal surcharge : if wages are 110 instead of 100, profit = 11, ground-rent = 16½, price = 137½ . The proportions are unchanged. The decisive observation: \"since the division is always obtained by a nominal surcharge of a certain percentage on wages, the price rises and falls with wages. First wages are posited as equal to the value of the commodity, and then they are again divorced from the value of the commodity.\" Wage = value, then wage ≠ value — the theory oscillates incoherently. \"In fact, however, it all comes down, by way of this irrational detour, to the determination of the value of the commodity by the amount of labour contained in it, while the value of wages is determined by the price of the necessary means of subsistence, and the excess of the value over and above wages forms profit and rent.\" In other words, the surcharge theory, unfolded consistently, covertly reproduces the correct result — labour-determined value with a distributional surplus — but through an \"irrational detour.\" The correct content is smuggled back in under a false form. 2.14 The Quid Pro Quo and Reason One: The Reification of Revenue-Forms (pp. 1006–1007) Marx introduces the central term: the quid pro quo — the substitution of the wrong causal order. At the immediately visible surface of production, the dissolution of the given quantum of labour into wages/profit/rent is represented \"in a distorted way.\" He sets it up with a simple schema: if total commodity value = 300, of which 200 = constant capital consumed, the new value remaining for division = 100. Let wages = x, profit = y, rent = z: x + y + z = 100 always. But \"in the minds of the industrialists, merchants and bankers, and the vulgar economists as well,\" the order is reversed: x, y, z are each independently determined, and the magnitude of the commodity's value results from their sum — which may be greater or less than 100. Marx then gives the first of five reasons why this quid pro quo is \"necessary\": Reason One — Revenue-forms are personified by three separate agents, and property appears as the source of value. The components \"confront one another as independent revenues, which are related as such to three completely separate agents of production, labour, capital and the earth, and appear therefore to arise from these.\" Property in labour-power, capital, and the earth is the reason why the different value components fall to their respective proprietors, transforming them into revenues. \"But value does not arise from a transformation into revenue, it must rather be already in existence before it can be transformed into revenue and assume this form.\" The appearance is reinforced because the relative sizes of the three parts are governed by different laws, and their relationship to — and limitation by — commodity value is not indicated on the surface at all. The implication is important: the distributional relationship of persons to things (property titles) is mistaken for the causal relationship of things to value. Because every income has an owner attached, income seems to be produced by ownership rather than merely appropriated through ownership. 2.15 Reason Two: Experience, the Inversion of Cause and Effect, and the \"Confirmed\" Illusion (pp. 1007–1009) Reason Two — the surface regularities of profit-rate movements confirm the wage-determines-price appearance. Marx gives three concrete mechanisms: (a) General wage rise/fall with opposite profit-rate movement alters production prices. When wages rise generally, the general rate of profit moves oppositely, other things equal, and this alters production prices — raising some, lowering others — depending on the average composition of capital in each sphere. In some spheres, therefore, \"experience shows that the average commodity price rises because wages have risen and falls because they have fallen.\" What is not experienced is \"the secret regulation of these changes by a commodity value independent of wages.\" (b) Local wage rises. If the rise is local — particular to certain spheres due to specific circumstances — there may be a corresponding nominal rise in the price of these commodities. This price rise is a reaction to the local disturbance of the uniform distribution of surplus-value, a means of adjusting particular profit rates to the general rate. \"Experience\" again shows price determined by wages; the \"hidden basis\" (the uniform division of social surplus-value) is not experienced. (c) Wage as production price of labour-power. The average price of labour, i.e., the value of labour-power, is determined by the production price of the necessary means of subsistence ; if that rises or falls, so does the price of labour. So there is a real, experienced correlation between wages and commodity prices — but \"the cause may present itself as effect, and the effect as cause,\" just as with market-price movements, where a wage rise above average corresponds to the market-price rise characteristic of prosperity, and the subsequent wage fall below average corresponds to the market-price fall below production prices in the downturn. (d) The crucial falsification. Marx then signals an observation incompatible with the surface appearance: the profit rate may be affected independently of wage movements by movements in the value of constant capital — so that wages and the profit rate may rise or fall in the same direction, instead of opposite ones. \"If the rate of surplus-value directly coincided with the rate of profit, this would not be possible.\" Moreover, even if wages rise due to higher subsistence prices, the profit rate may remain the same or rise as a result of greater labour intensity or the prolongation of the working day. The chapter's diagnosis: \"All these experiences confirm the illusion produced by the independent, distorted form of the value components, as if the value of commodities was determined either by wages alone, or by wages and profit together.\" And once this seems true for wages — once the price of labour seems to coincide with the value labour creates — it follows \"self-evidently\" for profit and rent too, whose money expressions \"must then be governed independently of labour and the value it produces.\" The illusion propagates by analogy. 2.16 Reason Three: Presupposition, Reproduction, and the Constant-Capital Tautology (pp. 1009–1012) Reason Three is the most theoretically profound. Marx asks: suppose we remove competition entirely and assume perfect constancy and coincidence . Would the illusion disappear? No. He stipulates three highly favourable assumptions: 1. Values or production prices always coincide at the phenomenal level with market prices; 2. Reproduction takes place under constant conditions — labour productivity constant for all elements of capital; 3. The fresh value component always breaks down into wages, profit, and rent in the same proportions , so that actually paid wages, actually realized profits, and actual rent coincide directly with the value of labour-power, with the share of total surplus-value accruing under the average rate of profit, and with the normal limits of ground-rent. Then: \"Under these assumptions, then, with the values of commodities being and appearing constant… the real movement would necessarily appear in a distorted form.\" Even with competition abstracted away, the actual appearance is not \"the dissolution of a value magnitude given in advance into three parts\" but rather \"the formation of this value magnitude from the sum of the component elements of wages, profit and ground-rent, taken as determined independently and separately.\" Why? Because \"in the real movement of individual capitals and their commodity product it is not the value of commodities that appears the premise of its own dissolution but, on the contrary, the components into which it can be dissolved function as the premises for a commodity's value.\" Marx elaborates four structural features: (i) Cost price. For each capitalist, the cost price appears as a given quantity and constantly presents itself as such in the production process. Cost price = value of constant capital + value of labour-power — though labour-power's value appears in the \"irrational form of the price of labour,\" so that wages too appear as the worker's revenue . (ii) Wages are contractually fixed before the equivalent is produced. \"The average price of labour is a given magnitude, since the value of labour-power, like that of any other commodity, is determined by the labour-time necessary for its reproduction.\" But the value component that resolves into wages does not arise from the fact that it takes the form of wages; it arises from the worker producing an equivalent. However, \"wages are stipulated by contract before the value equivalent corresponding to them is produced. And since they are a price element whose magnitude is given before the commodity and its value are produced, a component of the cost price, wages appear not as a part separated off from the total value of the commodity in an independent form, but rather the reverse, as a given magnitude that determines the total value in advance, i.e., a formative element of price or value.\" This is a temporal/practical fact — the wage contract — that has the epistemic consequence of making the part appear as the premise . (iii) Average profit as a regulating element. Average profit plays a role in the price of production analogous to wages in the cost price. Price of production = cost price + average profit on capital advanced. Average profit has a practical bearing in the mind and accounting of the capitalist himself , as a regulating element: it determines the transfer of capital between spheres and all sales and contracts over long periods. But insofar as it has this practical bearing, it is a magnitude fixed in advance , independent of the value and surplus-value produced in any particular sphere, and even more independent of any individual capital investment. \"Instead of being the result of a division in value, it rather presents the appearance of a magnitude independent of the value of the commodity product, given in advance in the commodity's production process and itself determining the average price of the commodities; it presents the appearance in other words of a formative element of value.\" (iv) Interest and ground-rent enter the cost price as given magnitudes. One part of average profit, in the form of interest , confronts the functioning capitalist \"from an independent position as an element already presupposed in the production of commodities and their value.\" The amount of interest fluctuates, but at any moment it is a given magnitude that \"enters into the cost price of the commodities he produces.\" Similarly ground-rent , in the form of contractually fixed lease-money paid by the agricultural capitalist, or rent for business space. These parts into which surplus-value can be resolved, \"since as elements of the cost price they are given for the individual capitalist, appear upside-down, as formative elements of surplus-value; forming one portion of commodity price in the way that wages form the other.\" (v) The dialectical climax — the reproduction of the relations. Marx then states the deepest reason: \"The secret reason why these products of the dissolution of commodity value constantly appear as the premises of value formation itself is simply that the capitalist mode of production, like every other, constantly reproduces not only the material product but also the socio-economic relations, the formal economic determinants of its formation. Its result thus constantly appears as its premise, and its premises as its results. And it is this constant reproduction of the same relationships which the individual capitalist anticipates as self-evident, as an indubitable fact.\" Because wages, profit, interest, and rent are constantly reproduced as the form in which income flows, they are built into all contracts, and so are presupposed . \"The specific shape in which the value components confront one another is presupposed because it is constantly reproduced, and it is constantly reproduced because it is constantly presupposed.\" This mutual constitution of presupposition and reproduction is the social mechanism behind the epistemic illusion. (vi) Market prices confirm the \"given-ness.\" \"Experience and appearance also show that market prices… are in no way dependent on these anticipations as far as their level goes; they are not affected by whether interest or rent is fixed high or low.\" Market prices constantly change, and their longer-period average \"is precisely what gives rise to the respective averages of wages, profit and rent, as the constant quantities that therefore ultimately govern market prices.\" So it looks as if market prices are indifferent to the \"given\" magnitudes and the magnitudes are the \"given\" governing realities. (vii) The constant-capital tautology. The chapter now strips away the last possible defence: \"It seems very simple, on the other hand, to reflect that if wages, profit and rent are formative elements of value, because they appear as presupposed in value production, and are presupposed for the individual capitalist in the cost price and price of production, then the constant capital component, whose value is given in the production of any commodity, is also a value-forming element. But the constant capital component is nothing but a sum of commodities and hence commodity values. We would thus get the absurd tautology that commodity value forms and causes commodity value. \" This is a reductio ad absurdum. If whatever appears as given in advance is thereby a value-forming element , then the constant capital component — which is every bit as \"given in advance\" for the individual capitalist as wages, interest, and rent — must also be value-forming. But constant capital is itself a sum of commodity values. So the view collapses into the tautology that value causes value. Marx notes that the capitalist, driven by \"his own self-interest and the resulting motives,\" may nevertheless be led to extend the components-view to constant capital via the same circular logic: he sees his product entering other spheres as constant capital, and theirs entering his, and so he can reduce the price of constant capital \"in the last instance, even if in a way that cannot be entirely fathomed, to the sum of value that results from the addition of independent value elements governed by different laws and formed from different sources: wages, profit and rent.\" 2.17 Reason Four: The Practical Indifference of the Individual Capitalist (pp. 1012–1015) Reason Four — for the individual capitalist, the whole determination of value is immaterial. Marx's argument: \"It is completely immaterial for the individual capitalist whether commodities are sold at their values or not, and so therefore is the whole determination of value. Right from the start, this is something that goes on behind his back.\" The governing average prices in each sphere are not values but prices of production that differ from values. Value determination \"as such\" interests and affects the individual capitalist only in one narrow way: insofar as a rise or fall in the productivity of labour changes how much labour is required — either allowing him an extra profit at existing market prices, or compelling him to raise his price because more wages, more constant capital, and hence more interest fall to each unit produced. Wages, interest and rent appear to him as governing limits — not only to the price at which he can realize his profit of enterprise , but to the price at which he must sell if reproduction is to continue. \"It is a matter of complete indifference to him whether he realizes the value and surplus-value contained in the commodity on its sale or not, as long as he extracts from the price the customary profit of enterprise, or a greater profit, above the cost price as individually given for him by wages, interest and rent.\" Moreover, this indifference has practical consequences that make the view functional: if the capitalist can drive wages below the value of labour-power, or obtain capital at lower interest, or pay sub-normal rent or sub-production-price raw materials, he doesn't mind selling below value \"or even below the general price of production, i.e., parting with a portion of the surplus labour contained in the commodity for nothing.\" \"His profit of enterprise can remain the same, and even grow, as long as the excess of the commodity price above the elements of it that must be paid for… remains the same or grows.\" So the individual capitalist's business accounting treats wages, interest, and rent (plus the value of means of production going into his commodities) as the price-determining elements of his commodity. Profit of enterprise from this standpoint appears either as: - determined by an excess of market price (resulting from chance relations of competition) over the immanent value of commodities as determined by those elements; or - insofar as it is included in the market price as a determining element, as itself dependent on competition among buyers and sellers. World-market extension. \"Both in competition between the individual capitalists and in competition on the world market, given and presupposed amounts for wages, interest and rent go into the account as constant and governing quantities\" — \"constant not in the sense that they do not change, but rather in that they are given in any one particular case and constantly set the limit for the ever fluctuating market price.\" Marx gives a concrete example: world-market competition is a question of whether, with the given levels of wages, interest, and rent, a commodity can profitably be sold at or below the general market price. If wages and land prices are low but interest is high in one country (because the capitalist mode of production is not fully developed), while wages and land are nominally high and interest is low in another, then the first country's capitalist uses more land and labour, the second's relatively more capital. These factors are the determining elements in competition. \"Experience shows here in theory, and the self-interested calculation of the capitalist shows in practice, that commodity prices are determined by wages, interest and rent, by the prices of labour, capital and land, and that these price elements are in fact the governing elements of price formation.\" The residual \"fourth element.\" Marx concedes one thing: \"There still of course remains one element that is not assumed in advance but results from the market price of commodities, namely the excess over the cost price formed from the addition of these elements, wages, interest and rent. This fourth element appears in each individual case as determined by competition, and in the average case by the average profit, which is again governed by the same competition, simply over a longer period.\" So profit of enterprise, precisely the residual, is the only element not \"given in advance\" for the individual capitalist — and it is the one that competes hardest to be seen as pure result , while the others are seen as causes . This contrast is the practical-mind's version of the whole illusion. 2.18 Reason Five: Subsumption by Analogy and the Naturalization of Capital (p. 1015) Reason Five — the categories are applied even where their conditions of existence are absent, by analogy. Marx's case: \"If an independent worker labours for himself and sells his own product — we may take a small peasant, since in this case all three forms of revenue can be used — he is first of all considered as his own employer (capitalist), employing himself as a worker, and as his own landowner, using himself as his own farmer. He pays himself wages as a worker, lays claim to profit as a capitalist and pays himself rent as a landowner.\" Marx concedes that, once the capitalist mode of production is the general social basis , this subsumption is \"correct in as much as\" — for the peasant is able to appropriate his own surplus labour through possession of means of production (necessarily taken to have the form of capital), and his product is a commodity whose price is estimated against the general rate of profit; any excess beyond the ordinary quota depends on ownership of land. But the consequence is essential: \"Because a form of production that does not correspond to the capitalist mode of production can be subsumed under its forms of revenue (and up to a certain point this is not incorrect), the illusion that capitalist relationships are the natural condition of any mode of production is further reinforced.\" This is the ideological payoff of the whole chapter: the categories of capitalist distribution (wages, profit, rent) become so pervasive that they are projected onto non-capitalist producers, and this projection is what makes capitalist relations appear natural and eternal . The chapter does not treat the illusion as a merely conceptual error; it treats it as a social process of naturalization . 2.19 The Coda: Stripping Away the Capitalist Form and the Feudal Analogy (pp. 1015–1016) The chapter's final paragraph is a flash of the post-capitalist horizon. Marx asks what remains if the specifically capitalist character of wages and surplus-value is stripped away: Wages reduced to their general basis : that portion of the product of labour going into the worker's own individual consumption; this share, \"freed from its capitalist limit and expanded to the scale of consumption that is both permitted by the existing social productivity… and required for the full development of individuality.\" Surplus labour and surplus product reduced to the degree needed, under given conditions of production, on the one hand \"to form an insurance and reserve fund,\" on the other hand \"for the constant expansion of reproduction in the degree determined by social need.\" Both necessary labour and surplus labour taken to include the labour \"that those capable of work must always perform for those members of society not yet capable, or no longer capable of working.\" \"Then nothing of these forms remains, but simply those foundations of the forms that are common to all social modes of production.\" This is a specification of the universal economic content — labour, necessary consumption, insurance, reproduction, provision for the young and old — beneath the historically specific capitalist forms of wages, profit, and rent. The illusion is that the forms are eternal; the reality is that the forms are historical, while the underlying content (labour and its allocation) is trans-historical. The closing sentence seals the historical point with a brisk analogy: \"This kind of subsumption, incidentally, is also characteristic of modes of production previously dominant, e.g., the feudal. Relations of production that in no way corresponded to it, standing completely outside it, were subsumed under feudal relationships; e.g., 'tenures in common socage' in England (as opposed to 'tenures on knight's service'), which simply involved monetary obligations and were feudal only in name.\" The move completes the chapter's critical trajectory: just as socage tenures were \"feudal only in name,\" so the wage/profit/rent categories applied to a self-employed peasant are \"capitalist only in name.\" Every mode of production, Marx implies, projects its own categories onto alien relations and thereby mistakes itself for the natural form of human economy. The critique of the illusion created by competition is therefore at once a critique of political economy's central category and a contribution to the theory of historical forms of social consciousness. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS 3.1 The Fundamental Principle: Bounded Unity vs. Free Composition The chapter's core systemic law can be stated with maximum precision: every quantum of new value added by labour is a bounded unity (v + s), and the division of that bounded unity into revenue forms obeys specific, heterogeneous laws, but the sum is invariant with respect to the division. The composition-view — wages, profit, and rent as independent inputs whose sum makes commodity value — is exposed as logically self-dissolving on three connected grounds: The regress/tautology argument. If constant capital is itself a \"given\" and is thus value-forming, then commodity value forms commodity value (absurd). If constant capital is excluded from value-formation, one has simply denied a component of value that empirically exists. The money-circularity argument. On the composition-view, \"value\" reduces to \"a certain quantity of money,\" but money is itself a commodity, so the money magnitude must be determined by the same components it was invoked to determine. Wages = wages; value = value. The competition argument. Competition equalizes rates and prices but cannot create the level to which it equalizes. If the equilibrium rate of profit cannot be explained by competition (since at equilibrium competition's effect is abolished), then competition is being invoked to conceal a theoretical void. These three arguments are logically independent but converge on the same result: the composition-view substitutes the form of appearance for the form of determination . 3.2 The Theory of Necessary Appearance (Ideology as Social Fact) Perhaps the most important theoretical lesson of the chapter is its methodology of appearance . Marx does not treat the illusion as an error of the ignorant. He states that the quid pro quo is \"necessary\" and gives five structural reasons. This is a template for a materialist theory of ideology: Appearance is generated by the reproduction of social relations, not by deception or stupidity. Conceptual categories (wages, profit, rent) are true at the level of distribution — they are real forms that really govern real transactions — but false at the level of determination , where they conceal the unity of value. The truth of distribution and the falsity of its self-understanding coexist because the system reproduces itself in the form of its own appearance : \"its result thus constantly appears as its premise, and its premises as its results.\" This is a sophisticated position. The categories of bourgeois economics are not fiction; they are practically valid forms of thought whose validity is strictly bounded by the surface they describe. The illusion is thus socially necessary , and only the theoretically reconstructed order — value → surplus-value → profit → rent → prices of production — can break it. 3.3 The Unity of the Value Theory The chapter also functions as a defence of the labour theory of value against both Ricardo and the vulgar economists. Against the \"adding-up\" theorists, Marx shows that the components do not add up to value, since the components presuppose value. Against a naive reading of Ricardo, note that Marx here uses Ricardo's own correct claim (footnote 56: the value of raw produce, manufactures, and metals \"depend[s] not on the rate of profits, nor on the rate of wages, nor on the rent paid for mines, but on the total quantity of labour necessary\") as a fixed point. Marx's chapter is a systematic demonstration that when you try to construct value from its revenue-parts, you end up retrieving Ricardo's own conclusion by an irrational detour — the surcharge exercise (100 + 10 + 15 = 125, alternatively 80 + 8 + 12 = 100) shows that the components-view can only be made consistent by covertly presupposing labour-value. The chapter therefore exhibits the superiority of Marx's architecture: it preserves both the determination of value by labour-time and the entire apparatus of distribution, transformation, and appearance, without collapsing either into the other. The key to this preservation is the strict distinction between (i) labour-time as the determinative substance of value , (ii) value as the quantitative premise of the fresh labour added , and (iii) the division of that premise into the historically specific revenue forms , the relative sizes of which are governed by social laws (the value of labour-power, the rate of surplus-value, profit-rate equalization, landed property, competition). 3.4 The Theory of the Working Day and the Limits of Class Conflict The starred section (§2.8) draws a striking downward limit-chain: The necessary part of the working day is limited from below by the physical minimum (interpreted historically and socially). The surplus part is limited from above by the physical maximum of the working day . Therefore the total share of profit and rent is absolutely bounded. This has a political implication that is theoretical rather than moralizing: the class struggle over the length of the working day, the intensity of labour, and wages is a struggle over the division of a bounded value , not over the production of value. Competition between capitalists, no matter how fierce, cannot increase the total social surplus-value; it can only redistribute it and enforce its extraction. This is why the chapter repeatedly uses the phrase that there is \"nothing left\" beyond what labour has produced — a statement that is at once analytic and historically fateful. 3.5 Interest and Profit of Enterprise: The Indeterminacy of Rentier Capital The treatment of the interest/profit-of-enterprise split is one of the chapter's most subtle contributions: it identifies the one division that is law-indeterminate , i.e., purely competition-determined. This exception proves the rule of the chapter: the only portion of the distributional apparatus that is genuinely open to pure bargaining is the share between two owners of the same factor (capital). Every other division has a law. The implication is that the rentier position is theoretically and legally distinct without being economically decisive: the level of profit as such is law-determined; the split is contingent. This has implications for theories of finance that one can still see echoed in later Marxian work. 3.6 Monopoly Price: Redistribution, Not Transgression The chapter's handling of monopoly price is important methodologically. Marx insists that monopoly price does not create value or abolish limits; it transfers surplus-value from other producers and, where monopoly goods enter worker consumption, may redistribute the burden between workers (via real wages) and other capitalists. \"The limits within which monopoly price affects the normal regulation of commodity prices are firmly determined and can be precisely calculated.\" The method is therefore: explain the anomaly by locating it within the bounded social surplus-value, not by abandoning the law . This is a generally applicable analytical principle — to explain apparently free-floating prices by the movement of a bounded underlying magnitude. 3.7 Naturalization as the Political Function of the Illusion Reason Five is arguably the most politically consequential section of the chapter. By showing that the revenue-forms are extended, \"by way of analogy,\" to the independent small producer, Marx identifies the mechanism by which capitalist relations are made to appear natural and universal . The category \"employer of himself,\" \"his own landlord,\" is a miniature of the ideological operation of an entire mode of production: the projection of historically specific categories onto relations that are not capitalist at all, and thereby the transmutation of historical forms into natural kinds. The closing image of socage tenures — \"feudal only in name\" — completes the move: every historical mode of production produces the same kind of projection, which is why each one believes itself eternal. 3.8 The Post-Capitalist Horizon The closing paragraph's enumeration — consumption scaled to \"the full development of individuality,\" insurance/reserve funds, reproduction expanded \"in the degree determined by social need,\" and provision for the young and old — outlines a rational core that survives the abolition of the specifically capitalist forms. The essential methodological point is that these \"foundations of the forms that are common to all social modes of production\" are recognizable precisely because the illusion has been dispelled. The critique of the illusion is thus not merely negative but revelatory: the same analytical labour that dissolves the composition-view also reveals the trans-historical content that capitalism distorts. This is the positive upshot of the entire Part VII argument and the implicit bridge from Capital to Marx's broader project of a post-capitalist political economy. 3.9 Practical and Theoretical Summary In summary, Chapter 50 establishes five durable propositions: Value is logically prior to its division. Commodity value, less constant capital, is the quantitative premise out of which wages, profit, and rent are dissolved; the reverse order is incoherent. The illusion of composition is generated by the surface and by practice, not by error. It is produced by the contractual, accounting, and market forms through which the system reproduces itself; hence \"its result constantly appears as its premise.\" Competition is a mechanism of equalization, not a source of magnitude. The level to which it equalizes must be explained by something else — here, by the total social surplus-value and its ratio to total social capital. Every revenue form has a limit; some limits are law-determined, one (interest/profit of enterprise) is competition-determined, and all are bounded by the surplus-value produced. Monopoly price redistributes but does not escape the bounds. The revenue categories are historically specific and are illegitimately projected onto non-capitalist relations, thereby producing the appearance of the eternity of capitalism. The forms are historical; the underlying foundations of labour, consumption, insurance, and reproduction are common to all social modes. The chapter is therefore not merely the closing section of a technical treatise. It is Marx's most systematic account of how a mode of production necessarily misunderstands itself — and of what critical theory must therefore be: not a correction of isolated errors, but the reconstruction of the real order beneath the inverted appearance. Its methodological lesson — that socially necessary appearance must be explained from the structure that produces it , not dismissed as false — remains one of the most powerful and portable insights of the Marxian tradition, and its analytic core (the bounded unity of fresh value, the heterogeneity of the laws dividing it, the distinction between determination and distribution) remains essential for any serious theory of distribution under capitalism. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 49 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 51"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 51", "title": "Chapter 51", "subtitle": "Distribution Relations and Production Relations", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 51/Chapter 51 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 6907, "read_time": "35 min read", "tags": ["The Trinity Formula & Class Relations", "Historical Specificity of Capital", "Dialectical Method & Critique of Political Economy"], "keywords": ["Distribution Relations and Production Relations", "Distribution Forms as Mere Reflections of Production Relations", "Critique of J.S. Mill's Separation of Production from Distribution", "Historical Transience of Capitalist Distribution Categories", "Wage-Labour and Capital as Interdependent Specific Forms"], "text": "Chapter 51 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 6,904 words ⏱️ ~35 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS The Central Problem. This chapter addresses the relationship between how wealth is distributed and how wealth is produced . Specifically, it interrogates a widely held assumption: that the distribution of income into wages, profit, and rent reflects natural, eternal, trans-historical laws inherent in human production as such. Against this assumption, the chapter argues that relations of distribution are not a separate and natural sphere, but are the direct expression of historically specific relations of production. The Core Thesis. Marx's thesis is twofold, and it unfolds throughout: First , the familiar division of the annual product into wages, profit, and ground-rent is fundamentally incorrect as a starting point. The product is not first divided into three revenues. It is divided into capital on one side and revenues on the other. Wages only ever become a \"revenue\" for the worker after that same worker has confronted wages as capital — that is, after the worker's own means of subsistence have been advanced to him as something alien, owned by someone else. The trinity of revenues, therefore, is not the primary fact of distribution but a consequence of a deeper, prior separation: the expropriation of the immediate producers from the conditions of production. Second , relations of distribution are essentially identical with relations of production — \"the reverse side of the same coin.\" They share the same historically transitory character. When the particular form of production dies, its distribution form dies with it. Therefore, treating distribution as historical while treating production as natural is incoherent, and it constitutes the \"timid and restrained\" horizon of bourgeois economic criticism. A Terminological Intervention. The chapter turns on a careful disambiguation of the phrase \"relations of distribution.\" Marx distinguishes between (a) the distribution of the means of production (who owns land, tools, machinery, raw materials) — which is presupposed by capital and constitutes the foundation of the social functions and class roles within production itself; and (b) the distribution of the product as revenue (who gets what share of the consumable product). The former determines \"the whole character and movement of production\"; the latter is a mere appearance, derived and secondary. Confusing the two is the error of the \"timid\" critics. Two Defining Traits. The chapter identifies two characteristics that mark the capitalist mode of production from the outset: (1) it produces its products as commodities , with the worker appearing purely as a free wage-labourer, and (2) it treats the production of surplus-value as its direct object and decisive motive. Together these produce the distinctive contradictions of capitalism: the reification of social relations, the despotism of the workshop contrasted with the anarchy of the market, and the historically specific forms of profit and rent. Intellectual Objective & Place in the Framework. This is the closing chapter of Volume III (Part VII: \"The Revenues and Their Sources\"), following the demolition of the \"Trinity Formula\" in Chapter 48. It performs the final theoretical synthesis of the whole three-volume work: having shown in Volume I how surplus-value is extracted in production, in Volume II how it circulates, and in Volume III how it splits into profit, interest, and rent, Marx now closes the circle by arguing that the very forms of distribution — the revenues that appear on the surface — are determined by the historically specific production relations that underlie them. The chapter is the theoretical capstone that renders the \"eternal natural laws\" of bourgeois distribution historically contingent and therefore transitory — and it gestures, in its final lines, at the coming contradiction between the productive forces and their social form. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Beat: Revenue Forms as Relations of Distribution The chapter opens with a precise accounting proposition. The value freshly added in a year by freshly added labour — and thus the portion of the annual product in which that value is expressed, which \"can be extracted and separated from the total product\" — is divisible into three parts . Each part assumes a distinct form of revenue, and each form ties the value to a distinct class of owner: one part accrues to the owner of labour-power (wages); one part to the owner of capital (profit); one part to the owner of landed property (ground-rent). Marx classifies these as \"relations or forms of distribution,\" because they \"express the relationships in which the total value newly produced is distributed among the owners of the various agents of production.\" Two things are noteworthy here. First, the framing is deliberately relational : these are not simply quantities of income but social relationships between classes. Second, the term \"agents of production\" already hints at the deeper structure — labour-power, capital, and land are the three \"agents,\" and ownership of each confer a title to a share of the product. The rhetorical setup thus poses the question the chapter will answer: are these relations of distribution natural and eternal, or historically specific? The \"Customary View\": Distribution as Natural Law Marx now turns to the dominant ideological interpretation. In the \"customary view,\" relations of distribution appear as natural relations — \"relations arising from the nature of all social production, from the laws of human production pure and simple.\" This is the thesis of classical political economy: that the division of the product into wages, profit, and rent is grounded in the essential nature of production as such, discoverable by universal economic laws. This \"customary view\" must confront an obvious empirical obstacle: pre-capitalist societies clearly displayed different modes of distribution — tribute, corvée, feudal levy, communal sharing. How does the naturalist view absorb this? Marx explains the ideological manoeuvre in vivid terms: these alternate distributions are \"explained as undeveloped, incomplete and disguised, not reduced to their purest expression and highest form, modalities of these natural relations of distribution with a different hue.\" The key phrase is \"with a different hue.\" The naturalist view treats history as a series of imperfect approximations to a single universal distribution law — capitalism being the \"purest expression and highest form,\" the telos toward which all history allegedly gravitated. Historical difference is thereby reduced to a matter of staining or shading on an otherwise identical, eternal substrate. This is the very structure of teleological and apologetic reading of economic history, and Marx exposes it as such. The Kernel of Truth in the Naturalist View Crucially, Marx does not simply dismiss the naturalist view. He asks what \"bit of truth\" it contains. His answer is precise and dialectical. Once any kind of social production is assumed — and he gives two examples: the indigenous Indian communities (village communes with communal property) and \"the more artificially developed communism of the Peruvians\" (the Incan state-socialism constructed through imperial expansion) — one can always distinguish between: the portion of labour whose product is directly consumed individually by the producers and their dependants (the necessary product, sustaining the producers); and a further portion of labour that is always surplus labour , whose product serves to satisfy \"general social needs,\" however that surplus is distributed and whoever functions as its representative. This is the crucial analytical concession. The existence of surplus labour — of labour beyond what is needed to reproduce the immediate producers, a surplus directed toward social needs broadly conceived — is a universal feature of all organized social production. What Marx denies is that this universal existence licenses the conclusion that the specific forms of its appropriation are universal. To the extent the naturalist view acknowledges a common feature, it is correct. But its error is that \"the identity of the different modes of distribution thus comes down to the fact that they are identical if we abstract from their distinctions and specific forms and cling on just to their unity in contrast to what distinguishes them.\" This sentence is a methodological tour de force. It names the logic of abstraction at the heart of the naturalist error: abstraction that cancels exactly the differences that matter, arriving at an empty identity that exists only as an artifact of its own procedure. To say all distributions are \"really the same\" because all involve surplus labour is like saying all societies are \"really the same\" because all contain humans — true only if you first discard everything that makes each society a society. The Half-Critical Position: Mill and the \"Timid\" Critics Marx then identifies a more sophisticated position: a \"more developed and critical awareness\" that concedes the historically developed character of the relations of distribution but \"holds all the more firmly to the supposedly constant character of the relations of production themselves, as arising from human nature and hence independent of all historical development.\" Footnote 56a attributes a version of this position to John Stuart Mill, Some Unsettled Questions of Political Economy (London, 1844) . This is the theoretical \"compromise\": distribution can be historically variable (a matter of social convention or political choice), while production remains governed by natural, universal laws. It is, on Marx's reading, precisely the wrong way around — and also incoherent, because if production relations were natural and unchangeable, then the distribution relations derived from them would also be unchangeable. This \"half-critical\" stance matters because it fundamentally characterises much of bourgeois reformism: the notion that distribution can be reformed (through taxation, welfare, unions, legislation) while production — the wage relation, the private ownership of the means of production, the extraction of surplus-value — remains untouched. Marx's rejoinder will be that this is logically impossible. The Scientific Counter-Thesis: Production as Historically Determined Against this, Marx sets the results of \"the scientific analysis of the capitalist mode of production.\" The counter-thesis is elaborated in a dense, cascading list of propositions: The capitalist mode of production is \"a mode of production of a particular kind and a specific historical determinacy.\" It is not production in general; it is a historically bounded type. It assumes a given level of social productive forces and their forms of development as its historical precondition — \"a condition that is itself the historical result and product of a previous process and from which the new mode of production proceeds as its given foundation.\" Here Marx expresses the materialist conception of history in condensed form: each mode of production inherits the productive forces created by its predecessor and develops upon them. The relations of production — \"relations into which men enter in their social life-process, in the production of their social life\" — have \"a specific, historical and transitory character.\" This is a definitional moment: relations of production are the social forms in which human beings jointly secure their material existence. Finally, the relations of distribution are essentially identical with these relations of production , \"the reverse side of the same coin, so that the two things share the same historically transitory character.\" This last point is the chapter's most important structural claim. Distribution is not a separate sphere with its own independent logic; it is the same set of social relations seen from the angle of how the product flows. To change distribution fundamentally, one must change production relations — because they are two sides of the same historical coin. The Fundamental Correction: Product Divides into Capital and Revenue Marx now pivots to the substantive analytical error. \"In dealing with the relations of distribution, it is usual to start from the ostensible fact that the annual product is divided into wages, profit and ground-rent. But expressed in this way, that is wrong.\" The correct formulation: The product is divided into capital on the one hand and revenues on the other. Why? Because \"one of these revenues, wages, only ever assumes the form of a revenue, the revenue of the worker, after it has previously confronted the same worker in the form of capital.\" This is a compressed but profound point drawn from the whole preceding analysis of the wage. Wages are not given to the worker as income from some social pool; wages are the price at which the worker sells labour-power to a capitalist who has already monopolised the means of production. The wage fund exists only as a portion of capital — variable capital — that the capitalist advances and recovers. Only after the wage relation has been established (as a capital relation) does the wage appear on the surface as a mere \"revenue\" of the worker. Two deeper consequences follow: The confrontation of capital with labour — \"the confrontation between the produced conditions of labour and the products of labour all together as capital, and the immediate producers\" — gives the material conditions of labour \"a specific social character vis-à-vis the workers,\" and \"sets up a specific relationship which the workers enter into, in production itself, to the owners of these conditions of labour and to one another.\" In other words, the capital-labour relation is constitutively a social relationship entered into in production, not a market transaction that occurs beforehand. The transformation of conditions of labour into capital involves the expropriation of the immediate producers from the land, and hence a specific form of landed property. Landed property — the material basis of rent — is here tied back to the same process: the enclosure of land as private property is a precondition for capital, and the specific form of landed property under capitalism (land as a saleable, capitalisable asset) is a historical product, not a natural fact. The upshot: \"If one part of the product were not transformed into capital, the other would not assume the forms of wages, profit and rent.\" Without capital, there is no wage, no profit, no rent in the capitalist sense. And, crucially, the reverse is also true: \"if the capitalist mode of production presupposes this specific social form of the conditions of production, it constantly reproduces it as well.\" The mode of production reproduces \"the material products\" and \"the relations of production in which these are produced, and with them also the corresponding relations of distribution.\" This is the conceptual machinery of reproduction (from Volume I): capitalism is not a static structure but a self-reproducing system that constantly recreates the class relations upon which it depends. Every productive cycle re-establishes the wage worker's dependence and the capitalist's ownership. The Crucial Distinction: Two Senses of \"Relations of Distribution\" The chapter now executes a decisive terminological distinction \"incidentally\": capital itself — \"and landed property, which this includes as its antithesis\" — already presupposes a distribution. Specifically it presupposes: the expropriation of the workers from the conditions of labour ; the concentration of these conditions in the hands of a minority ; the exclusive ownership of the land by other individuals. These are the relations \"developed in the section on primitive accumulation (Volume I, Part Eight).\" But — and this is the key move — this is a completely different distribution from what is understood by relations of distribution when a historical character is claimed for these, in contrast to the relations of production. What \"relations of distribution\" typically means in the bourgeois literature is \"the different titles to the part of the product that falls to individual consumption\" — i.e., who gets what income. The former relations of distribution (distribution of the means of production) , by contrast, \"are the foundation of particular social functions which are ascribed to specific agents of production within the relation of production itself, as distinct from the immediate producers.\" They \"give the actual conditions of production, and their representatives, a specific social quality. They determine the whole character and movement of production .\" So the two senses are: - (D₁) Distribution of the means of production → the pre-condition and constitutive structure of production → historically specific and constitutive of social functions (capitalist, wage-labourer, landlord). - (D₂) Distribution of the product as income → the surface appearance, the revenue shares (wages/profit/rent) → merely the phenomenal form of D₁. The error of the \"more developed\" critics is to historicise D₂ (the trendy topic of income distribution) while naturalising production relations. But D₂ is simply the visible face of D₁; if you historicise one, you must historicise the other. The distinction is thus both analytic tool and polemical weapon. First Trait: Production as Commodity Production Marx now enumerates \"two characteristic traits [that] mark the capitalist mode of production right from the start.\" Trait One: It produces its products as commodities. Marx immediately qualifies: the mere fact of producing commodities does not distinguish capitalism from other modes (simple commodity production, ancient trade, etc.). What distinguishes it is \"that the dominant and determining character of its product is that it is a commodity.\" Commodity production is universalised and totalised. Consequences: - The worker himself appears only as a seller of commodities, and hence as a free wage-labourer → \"labour generally appears as wage-labour.\" The worker's labour-power becomes a commodity; labour itself takes the general form of wage-labour. - The capital–wage-labour relation determines the whole character of the mode of production. Marx notes it's unnecessary to demonstrate this again — Volumes I and II have established it in depth. - The principal agents — capitalist and wage-labourer — are \"as such simply embodiments and personifications of capital and wage-labour — specific social characters that the social production process stamps on individuals.\" This is a key methodological point: the capitalist and the worker are not \"types of persons\" but positions in a social relation. Individuals are \"stamped\" by the process; they are personifications (Träger) of economic categories, not the originating agents of the system. Marx then elaborates what is contained in the commodity form of the product and the product-of-capital form. First, the character (1) of the product as commodity and (2) of the commodity as the capitalist-produced commodity \"already involves all the relations of circulation, i.e. a specific social process which products must pass through and in which they assume specific social characters.\" This is a Volume II argument: circulation is not external to production but constitutive of value realisation and the reproduction of capital. It involves \"specific relationships between the agents of production, determining the valorization of their product and its transformation back into either means of subsistence or means of production.\" The Law of Value as a Blind Natural Force Even setting circulation aside, the character of the product as commodity \"gives rise to the entire determination of value and the regulation of the total production by value.\" Here Marx restates the fundamental law of value: \"In this quite specific form of value, labour is valid only as social labour.\" Labour expended privately counts only insofar as it is validated as part of the socially necessary total; private labour must become social labour via exchange. On the other hand , the division of this social labour and the reciprocal complementarity or metabolism of its products — their \"subjugation to and insertion into the social mechanism\" — \"is left to the accidental and reciprocally countervailing motives of the individual capitalist producers.\" This is a statement of the celebrated anarchy of production under capitalism. Social labour is divided and mutually interrelated only in the outcome , not by any plan. The connection is not conscious and coordinated; it is \"left to\" — abandoned to — the blind interplay of independent decisions. Since the producers \"confront one another only as commodity owners, each trying to sell his commodity as dear as possible (and seeming to be governed only by caprice even in the regulation of production),\" the \"inner law operates only by way of their competition, their reciprocal pressure on one another, which is how divergences are mutually counterbalanced.\" \"It is only as an inner law, a blind natural force vis-à-vis the individual agents, that the law of value operates here and that the social balance of production is asserted in the midst of accidental fluctuations.\" This is a dense expression of what Marx elsewhere calls the law of value operating \"behind the backs\" of producers — a \"natural force\" that resembles the laws of nature precisely because it is not consciously controlled. It is important: the law of value is not a conscious rule but an emergent regularity of a decentralised system. This is also the basis for the later claim that \"freedom\" in exchange coexists with servitude in production. Reification and Subjectification Next, Marx draws out a philosophical corollary: \"What is also implied already in the commodity, and still more so in the commodity as the product of capital, is the reification of the social determinations of production and the subjectification [Versubjektifierung] of the material bases of production which characterize the entire capitalist mode of production.\" Two reciprocal processes are named: - Reification of social determinations ( Versachlichung of social relations): social relations between persons acquire the appearance of properties of things . Value, for example, is a social relation between producers, but appears as a property of the commodity itself. Class relations appear as market relations between buyers and sellers. - Subjectification of the material bases ( Versubjektifizierung ): material things — means of production, money, land — are treated as subjects or agents. Capital appears as a self-valorizing subject with its own drive; land appears to \"produce\" rent; money appears to \"generate\" interest. This is the classic Marxian dialectic of the personification of things and reification of persons — the \"fetishism\" of capital. Crucially, Marx ties it here directly to the commodity form and its developed form in capital. The mystification is not a mere illusion to be corrected but a real social process: capitalism actually organises production by installing things (capital) as the effective subjects of the process and persons (workers, capitalists) as their mere bearers. Second Trait: Surplus-Value as Direct Object and Motive Trait Two: \"the production of surplus-value as the direct object and decisive motive of production.\" This is the purpose of the process. \"Capital essentially produces capital, and it does this only as long as it produces surplus-value.\" Marx invokes the earlier analysis of relative surplus-value (Volume I) and the transformation of surplus-value into profit (Volume III, Part I). He notes that a \"mode of production peculiar to the capitalist period is based on this — a particular form of development of the social productive powers of labour, but as powers of capital that have asserted their autonomy vis-à-vis the worker, thus directly opposing his own development.\" This is a vital point about alienation and development : the productive forces advance — science, technology, cooperation, machinery — but they develop as property of capital and in opposition to the worker . The worker's own social productivity returns to him as an alien, hostile power. The development of the productive forces is not neutral; under capitalism it takes the form of the enhancement of capital's power over labour . Similarly: \"Production for value and surplus-value involves a constantly operating tendency ... to reduce the labour-time needed to produce a commodity, i.e. to reduce the commodity's value, below the existing social average.\" This is the dynamic of relative surplus-value and later of the falling tendency of the rate of profit . The pressure to reduce cost price \"becomes the strongest lever for raising the social productivity of labour, though this appears here simply as a constant increase in the productivity of capital.\" Note the qualification \"appears here simply as\": the real content (social productivity) is mystified as a property of capital, consistent with the reification thesis above. The Authority Relation: Workshop Despotism vs. Market Anarchy Marx then examines the distinctive form of authority in capitalist production. The capitalist's authority \"in the immediate production process, as personification of capital, the social function he dons as manager and ruler of production, is essentially different from authority on the basis of production with slaves or serfs.\" What is the difference? In slave or serf production, the master's authority is grounded in personal domination — political, legal, or theocratic power over persons. Under capitalism, in contrast: The \"social character of production confronts the mass of immediate producers in the form of a strict governing authority\"; \"the social mechanism of the labour process has received here a completely hierarchical articulation\"; \"though this authority accrues to its bearers only as the personification of the conditions of labour vis-à-vis labour itself, not to them as political or theocratic rulers as in earlier forms of production.\" So capitalist authority is purely economic — derived not from personal or political status but from ownership/control of the means of production. The capitalist rules in the factory because he is capital personified, not because he is a king or a bishop. This distinguishes the \"despotism of the workshop\" from the despotism of lordship. But at the same time: \"the most complete anarchy reigns among the bearers of this authority, the capitalists themselves, who confront one another simply as owners of commodities.\" The very class that rules hierarchically inside the enterprise is in chaotic competition among itself. Within that anarchy, \"the social interconnection of production prevails over individual caprice only as an overwhelming natural law.\" The duopoly of order and chaos — despotism in the workshop, anarchy in the market — is intrinsic to capitalism. Wage-Labour, Capital, and the Forms of Surplus-Value (Profit/Rent) Marx now restates the central causal chain of the chapter: \"It is only because labour is presupposed in the form of wage-labour, and the means of production in the form of capital (i.e. only as a result of this specific form of these two essential agents of production), that one part of the value (product) presents itself as surplus-value and this surplus-value presents itself as profit (rent), the gains of the capitalist, as additional available wealth belonging to him.\" The chain is: wage-labour + capital → surplus-value → profit (and rent) . Distribution forms are downstream of the social form of the agents. Notice \"additional available wealth belonging to him\" — the form of belonging is as much at issue as the quantity of wealth. And the reproduction side: \"it is only because it presents itself as his profit that the new additional means of production, designed for the expansion of reproduction and forming a portion of the product, present themselves as new additional capital, and the expansion of the reproduction process in general presents itself as a process of capitalist accumulation.\" So the appearance of profits feeds back into the form of accumulation: profit-financed accumulation is intrinsically capitalist accumulation, reproducing the capital relation. Nuance: Wage-Labour is Decisive but Not Value-Determining Marx enters an important qualification — one of the chapter's subtlest moves: \"Even though the form of labour as wage-labour is decisive for the shape of the entire process and for the specific mode of production itself, it is not wage-labour that is value-determining.\" This guards against a vulgar interpretation that would make wage-labour the source of value. Marx clarifies: \"What matters in the determination of value is the overall social labour-time, the total amount of labour which society has at its disposal and whose relative absorption by the different products determines, as it were, their respective social weight.\" The determinant of value is social labour-time , not the wage form. Wage-labour shapes the process, but the law of value rests on the expenditure of social labour-power in general. Nonetheless, Marx adds, \"the particular form in which social labour-time plays its determinant role in the value of commodities coincides with the form of labour as wage-labour, and the corresponding form of the means of production as capital, in so far as it is on this basis alone that commodity production becomes the general form of production.\" So: labour-time determines value whenever there is commodity production, but labour-time becomes universally value-determining only under capitalism, because only there does commodity production become general. This is a nuanced, non-vulgar formulation of Marx's value theory — the law of value is general to commodity production, but its historical dominance is specific to capitalism. The Relations of Distribution as Expressions of Production Relations Having dissected the traits, Marx returns to the central thesis: \"Let us consider, moreover, the so-called relations of distribution themselves. The wage assumes wage-labour, profit assumes capital. These specific forms of distribution thus assume specific social characters for the conditions of production and specific social relations for the agents of production. The specific relation of distribution thus simply expresses the historically determined relation of production.\" Note the logical form: wage assumes wage-labour; profit assumes capital . Each revenue form presupposes the production relation whose outcome it is. The wage is not meaningful without the social relation of wage-labour; profit is not meaningful without capital. Hence distribution is not an independent variable. Profit: Distribution as Production Marx now develops the profit case as a concrete illustration. Profit as presupposition of reproduction: \"This specific form of surplus-value is the presupposition for the fresh formation of means of production in the form of capitalist production; i.e. it is a relationship governing reproduction, even if it appears to the individual capitalist that he could consume the whole profit as revenue.\" Note the discrepancy between appearance and reality: the individual capitalist experiences profit as his to consume , but the structure imposes constraints. The insurance and reserve fund , the law of competition , etc., prove to him \"in a practical way that profit is not simply a category appertaining to the distribution of the product for individual consumption.\" Profit is therefore already a form of production relation, not just an income share. Profit governs production via prices of production: \"The entire capitalist production process, moreover, is governed by the prices of products. But the governing prices of production are themselves governed in turn by the equalization of the rate of profit and the distribution of capital among the various spheres of social production which is appropriate to that equalization.\" This is the result of Volume III, Part II (the transformation of values into prices of production). Profit appears \"as the principal factor not just of the products' distribution but also of their actual production, part of the distribution of capitals and labour itself among the various spheres of production.\" That is, the profit rate determines where capital is invested, which in turn determines what is produced and where labour is employed. Distribution (of capital and labour) is production. Profit of enterprise vs. interest: \"The division of profit into profit of enterprise and interest appears simply as a distribution of the same revenue. But it arises first of all from the development of capital as self-valorizing, surplus-value-producing value, this specific social form of the dominant production process.\" So even the subdivision of profit into interest and entrepreneurial profit is not a mere splitting of income; it reflects the internal differentiation of capital as self-valorizing value, and it \"develops from within itself credit and the credit institutions, and with this the whole configuration of production.\" In interest, etc., the ostensible forms of distribution go into the price as determining elements of production — i.e., distribution forms feed back into the determination of prices and thus into production itself. This is Marx's strongest argument that the sphere of \"distribution\" is not separate from production: the forms of distribution determine production because they determine the allocation of capital and labour across industries. Ground-Rent: The Apparent Pure Distribution Form Marx considers the strongest apparent counterexample. Ground-rent — because \"landed property as such performs no function in the production process, at least not in the normal case\" — appears to be a form of distribution \"pure and simple.\" Landowners appear to be pure recipients, extracting an income without contributing to production. Marx counters with two facts: Rent is limited to the excess above the average profit — i.e., rent is strictly defined by differential and absolute surplus profits in agriculture, which is only meaningful within the framework of capitalist profit rates. The landowner is \"reduced from guide and master of the production process and the entire process of social life to a mere leaser of land, usurer in land and simple recipient of rent.\" This reduction is a \"specific historical result of the capitalist mode of production.\" In feudalism, the landlord is the political master; under capitalism, he becomes a mere rentier. Furthermore, two historical conditions are at stake: - \"It is a historical precondition for this mode of production that the earth has to receive the form of landed property.\" Primitive accumulation requires that land be enclosed, commodified, and owned. - \"And it is a product of the specific character of this mode of production that landed property obtains forms which permit the capitalist mode of operation in agriculture.\" So landed property is both a precondition and a product of capitalism — dialectical causality. Finally: \"It is possible to give the name of rent to the landowner's income in other forms of society. But this is essentially different from rent as it appears in the present mode.\" Verbal identity does not entail substantive identity. The name \"rent\" refers to radically different social relations in different epochs. Conclusion: The Historical Character of Distribution Marx now draws the final conclusions: \"The so-called relations of distribution, therefore, correspond to and arise from historically particular and specific social forms of the production process and of the relationships which men enter into among themselves in the process of reproducing their human life. The historical character of these relations of distribution is the historical character of the relations of production, and they simply express one side of these. The capitalist distribution is different from those forms of distribution that arise from other modes of production, and every form of distribution vanishes along with the particular form of production that it arises from and corresponds to.\" Three theses are pronounced: 1. Distribution arises from production forms (and, more strongly, corresponds to them). 2. Historical character is one and the same for both spheres — the D₁ conception of history is the D₂ conception of history, not two separate historical facts. 3. Every distribution form perishes along with its mode of production. There is no eternal distribution; nor is there a distribution that can survive its productive base. The Critique of Timid Criticism and the Crisis Theory The chapter closes with a critique of the \"timid\" half-critics identified earlier: \"The view that considers only the relations of distribution to be historical, and not the relations of production, is simply the perspective of a criticism of bourgeois economics that is incipient but still timid and restrained.\" The critical thrust: such a critic has broken with bourgeois apologetics only halfway. To historicise distribution is already to concede that wages/profit/rent are contingent. But the critic then retreats by defending production as natural — thereby undermining their own critique. The philosophical root of this retreat is \"a confusion and identification of the social production process with the simple labour process, as this would have to be performed by an abnormally isolated person without any social aids.\" This is a profoundly important methodological point: the \"simple labour process\" (human metabolism with nature — the abstract, trans-historical aspects: labour, object of labour, means of labour) is conflated with the socially and historically specific labour process. The former's elements are indeed \"common to all social forms of its development\" — labour, materials, tools. But from those elements alone, nothing about the specific social form of any historical mode of production follows. Hence: \"each particular historical form of this process further develops the material foundations and social forms. Once a certain level of maturity is attained, the particular historical form is shed and makes way for a higher form.\" And then the chapter's closing dialectical statement — a compressed anticipatory theory of social revolution and crisis: \"The sign that the moment of such a crisis has arrived is that the contradiction and antithesis between, on the one hand, the relations of distribution, hence also the specific historical form of relations of production corresponding to them, and, on the other hand, the productive forces, productivity, and the development of its agents, gains in breadth and depth. A conflict then sets in between the material development of production and its social form.\" This is the classic Marxist theory of social revolution in its most concentrated form: crisis arises when the productive forces have developed beyond what the existing production relations (and their corresponding distribution relations) can contain. The contradiction between developing material powers and their fetters becomes the lever of historical transition. Footnote 57 refers the reader to \"the essay on competition and cooperation (1832?)\" — the manuscript editor identifies this as A Prize Essay on the Comparative Merits of Competition and Cooperation (London, 1834). The reference links this chapter's argument to a sustained discussion of the contradictions of competition and the potentials of cooperation — foreshadowing at least the analytical alternative to capitalism that later sections of socialist theory would pursue. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS Fundamental Principles. First, the inseparability of production and distribution. The chapter's most important contribution is the insistence that relations of distribution are — quite literally — the flipside of relations of production. Anyone who treats distribution as a distinct sphere with its own logic will inevitably mystify the social relations that generate distribution in the first place. This is the whole intellectual bar the chapter sets. Second, the two-sense distinction between \"distribution.\" Marx's disambiguation between distribution of the means of production (its proper structural sense) and distribution of the product as income (the surface sense) is a powerful analytic device. It reveals just how limited reformist thinking is: changing income distribution (via tax, welfare, wages policies) leaves untouched the primary distribution of the means of production, which is what actually determines \"the whole character and movement of production.\" Third, forms of revenue as social relations. Wages, profit, and rent are not merely quantitative shares; each is the material expression of a specific social relation — wage-labour, capital, and landed property. They cannot be detached from those relations. Therefore, talk of \"reforming wages\" or \"lowering profits\" that does not address the underlying relation is superficial. Fourth, the law of value as a blind natural force. Marx's image of the law of value operating \"as a blind natural force vis-à-vis the individual agents\" — combined with the workshop despotism / market anarchy antithesis — captures the fundamental irrationality of capitalism. Freedom in exchange coexists with compulsion in production, and the social metabolism is coordinated only ex post facto by the blind interaction of atomised agents. Fifth, reification and subjectification. The chapter's philosophical touchstone — the reification of social relations and the subjectification of material bases — links the political economy of the commodity to a broader critique of modern social consciousness. Capital behaves as a subject; human agents behave as things. Sixth, historical transience. Since the form of distribution vanishes along with the form of production, capitalism itself is a historically bounded epoch, not a natural order. The contradiction between productive forces and production relations is the sign of its coming supersession. Broader Practical & Theoretical Implications. Politically , the chapter delegitimises the reformist bifurcation of \"production\" (untouchable) from \"distribution\" (reformable). It implies that a social project aimed only at redistributing income remains trapped in the same antagonistic structure — because the same set of production relations will simply reproduce the same distribution forms. At the same time, the chapter suggests that structural change is not merely desirable but historically necessary: the productive forces have outgrown the production relations, and the resulting contradiction will eventually manifest. Theoretically , this chapter sets the stage for three downstream developments. First, it provides the systematic answer to the \"Trinity Formula\" — the surface formula wages/profit/rent is ideological precisely because it renders the underlying capital relation invisible, presenting each \"factor\" as an independent source of income. Second, it grounds the critique of theoretical reformism (including Mill's) that has echoed through debates on market socialism, welfare capitalism, and modern social democracy. Third, it articulates a dialectical-causal theory of history in which technology and property, forces and relations, mutually determine each other, with the material side ultimately generating the tensions that reshape the social side. Philosophically , the chapter's closing statements about the labour process are perhaps its most enduring contribution. By distinguishing the simple labour process (trans-historical metabolism with nature) from the historically specific labour process, Marx makes plain that labour is not intrinsically alienated, hierarchical, or commodity-form. Those features are historical products. The historical character of distribution follows from the historical character of production — and therefore the \"naturalness\" of capitalism's distribution is a myth. The final image — the productive forces in conflict with their social form — is not merely a hypothesis about the future of capitalism. It is the dialectical hinge on which the entire Marxist critique of political economy turns, and upon which the possibility of a higher form of social organisation is founded. The Chapter's Place in the Larger Work. Read against the whole of Capital , Chapter 51 closes the circle: Volume I establishes extraction; Volume II, circulation; Volume III, distribution and the form of revenues. The final chapter now demonstrates that what looked like the most concrete, palpable fact — where the annual product goes — is the most profoundly mediated and historically conditioned of appearances. In doing so, it fulfils the abstract promise of the entire critique: to unmask what appears natural as historical, and thereby to open the theoretical door to what might come after. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 50 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters Next Chapter → Volume 3 • Chapter 52"}, {"volume": "Volume 3", "folder": "Chapter 52", "title": "Chapter 52", "subtitle": "Classes", "path": "Volume 3/Chapter 52/Chapter 52 original_explanation.html", "word_count": 8258, "read_time": "41 min read", "tags": ["The Trinity Formula & Class Relations", "Historical Specificity of Capital"], "keywords": ["Classes", "The Three Great Classes of Modern Society", "Wage-Labourers, Capitalists, and Landowners", "Failure of Pure Income Sources to Define Class", "Unfinished Climax of Marx's Masterwork", "Objective Position in Social Relations of Production"], "text": "Chapter 52 Original - DeepSeek Chapter Analysis 🏠 📖 Chapter Analysis Table of Contents 📝 8,226 words ⏱️ ~41 min read 🏠 Navigator 🌙 Dark 🖨️ Print / PDF Chapter Explanation 1. EXECUTIVE CHAPTER OVERVIEW & THESIS Chapter 52 of Capital is, by any measure, the strangest chapter in the entire work: a fragment of roughly five hundred words that poses one of the most consequential questions in the history of social theory — what makes a class? — and then stops. The chapter is not an argument that unfolds; it is an argument that is interrupted at the precise moment when its destructive phase is complete and its constructive phase has not yet begun. The text that survives consists of an opening structural proposition (capitalist society is composed of three great classes defined by three property forms and three revenues), a geographical and methodological qualification (the pure articulation exists nowhere, not even in England), a compressed recapitulation of the developmental law that produces those three positions, a supporting quotation from Friedrich List on estate management, and then a two-step logical demolition of the most obvious answer to the class question — an answer Marx himself first states sympathetically before dismantling it. The final words of the chapter list vineyards, fields, forests, mines, and fisheries, end with \"etc.,\" and are followed by the editorial note: \"At this point the manuscript breaks off.\" The core thesis the fragment advances — negatively and by implication — is that class cannot be defined by revenue or by the identity of revenue with revenue source. The most natural, most empirically seductive, and indeed the most textually prepared answer to the question of what constitutes a class is that a class is a group of people who derive their income from the same source. Marx grants this answer its full intuitive force: it is what appears \"at first sight,\" it fits the three great classes perfectly, and it is supported by the analytic architecture of the whole volume, which has laboriously demonstrated the sources of wages, profit, and ground-rent. And then he destroys it with two counterexamples and a general principle. Doctors and government officials would also have to be classes. And the division of social labour fragments the very groups the criterion is supposed to unify into an \"infinite\" series — vineyard-owners, field-owners, forest-owners, mine-owners, fishery-owners — so that the criterion, applied consistently, yields not three classes but an unbounded plurality, and thereby abolishes the concept of class altogether. The chapter's intellectual objective, then, is not to provide a definition of class but to clear the ground for one, by demonstrating that the definition cannot be found at the level of distribution, income, or the circulation of revenues, and must therefore be sought at the level of production — the level at which the three great groups are constituted as the \"formative elements\" of modern society. The text never states this positive answer, but the fragment's own opening analytical passages point unmistakably in that direction: the sentences that precede the pivot question describe a law of development that separates producers from the means of production and concentrates those means into large groups, i.e., transforms labour into wage-labour and the means of production into capital, and that independently divorces landed property from both capital and labour. The chapter thus supplies the raw material for the answer it never gives. How the chapter fits its broader framework. The running head of the second page — \"The Revenues and Their Sources\" — identifies the chapter's location at the terminus of the volume's final analytical movement: the examination of the three great revenues (wages, profit, rent) as forms of income and, more fundamentally, as the forms in which surplus value is distributed among the classes that produce and appropriate it. The chapter therefore stands at the junction between the volume's theory of distribution and its implicit theory of social structure. It is the point at which the economic analysis of capital, landed property, and wage-labour would have been converted into a class analysis. Because the volume — and Marx's life — ended here, the conversion is never made. What we possess is the specification of the conditions under which it could not be made by the obvious route: the route that reads social classes directly off income streams. Finally, the fragment's form matters as much as its content. Its textual status as a manuscript, its interruption, and the presence of the editor's note and editorial footnotes mean that the chapter is simultaneously Marx's argument and a document about the incompleteness of Marx's argument. Any rigorous reading must hold both facts at once: we have a decisive negative result — the distributive criterion fails — and an absent positive result, whose absence is itself the most cited lacuna in the Marxist theory of class. 2. UNIFIED EXHAUSTIVE CHAPTER DECONSTRUCTION & ANALYSIS The Opening Proposition: Three Property Forms, Three Revenues, Three Classes The chapter opens with a single sentence that fuses two very different kinds of statement, and the entire subsequent difficulty of the fragment flows from that fusion. The sentence reads: \"The owners of mere labour-power, the owners of capital and the landowners, whose respective sources of income are wages, profit and ground-rent — in other words wage-labourers, capitalists and landowners — form the three great classes of modern society based on the capitalist mode of production.\" Consider what is doing the work here, clause by clause. The first clause identifies three social positions not by what their occupants do , nor by their status, nor by their culture, but by what they own . This is a deliberate and foundational move: the terminological thread that runs through all three phrases is the word \"owners.\" The wage-labourer is introduced as the \"owner of mere labour-power\" — a formulation that is, at first glance, paradoxical, since the wage-labourer is the class that owns nothing in the ordinary sense. But Marx's point is precise and economically essential: the wage-labourer is an owner, of exactly one commodity, labour-power, and it is this ownership, and only this ownership, that distinguishes the proletarian as a legal and economic subject. The qualifier \"mere\" (bloße) carries the whole weight of the difference. It marks the labourer's property as bare — unaccompanied by any other means of production, by any land, by any capital. \"Mere labour-power\" is labour-power stripped of every complement, the residue that remains to a producer who has been separated from the means of production. The phrase is therefore not descriptive of a natural condition but expressive of a historical process; it presupposes precisely the separation that the chapter's third beat will name as the law of development. The second and third phrases — \"the owners of capital and the landowners\" — name their positions by their characteristic property formations. Note the grammatical asymmetry: capital is treated as a possession (\"the owners of capital\"), while land is treated by way of its possessors (\"the landowners\"). This is not stylistic carelessness. Landed property is a class before it is a thing: the landowner is the personification of a property relation that historically preceded capitalism, whereas capital is a social relation that constitutes its owners as a class only within the capitalist mode of production. The formulation quietly encodes the historical asymmetry between the two propertied classes — an asymmetry whose consequences become explicit in the discussion of landed property's \"independent divorce.\" The middle clause — \"whose respective sources of income are wages, profit and ground-rent\" — introduces the distributional mapping. This is the pivot of the whole chapter, because it establishes a strict one-to-one correspondence: each of the three ownership positions has its own income stream, and each income stream has its own source. The mapping is exhaustive and reciprocal. There is no class without a revenue and no revenue without a class. This neatness is precisely what will be described as \"at first sight\" convincing, and precisely what will be undone. The appositive clause — \"in other words wage-labourers, capitalists and landowners\" — converts the property-holders into the familiar social actors. The phrase \"in other words\" asserts an identity between \"owner of mere labour-power\" and \"wage-labourer,\" and so on. This is a substantive claim, not a tautology: it asserts that the legal-economic position of ownership is the social position of class membership. That assertion is the target of the rest of the chapter. The final clause — \"form the three great classes of modern society based on the capitalist mode of production\" — makes the structural claim. Two qualifications are essential here. First, the classes are \"great\": they are not merely three among many, but the three that are formative , the load-bearing categories of the structure. Any additional class or stratum must be understood in relation to them. Second, they belong to \"modern society based on the capitalist mode of production.\" The \"modern\" and the \"mode of production\" qualifiers together impose a strict historical and structural limitation: this is not a general theory of social stratification, but an account of the class structure specific to one mode of production. Feudalism had its own classes; classical antiquity had its own; the three-part schema of wage-labourer, capitalist, and landowner is not a universal sociology but a historical, structurally generated result. This means that whatever \"class\" turns out to mean, it must be a relational and historically specific concept, not a permanent category of social description. The opening sentence thus performs two operations simultaneously, and the tension between them is the chapter's engine: it describes classes distributionally (three property forms → three revenues) while asserting a structural claim (these three are the formative elements of a determinate kind of society). The question the fragment will pose is whether the distributional description can bear the structural weight placed on it. It cannot. But it takes the entire chapter to show why not. The English Case and the Impurity of Class Articulation The second movement of the chapter is a geographical and methodological qualification. \"It is undeniably in England that this modern society and its economic articulation is most widely and most classically developed.\" Two terms deserve unpacking. \"Economic articulation\" designates the way the economic body is jointed — the way its parts are connected into an organized whole. The word choice suggests a structural, quasi-anatomical concern: what matters is not merely the presence of classes but the manner in which they are hinged together. England, as the site of the most advanced capitalist development, is the country where the joints are most clearly formed. \"Most classically developed\" introduces a further, stronger claim: England approximates the classical form , meaning that it approaches the conceptual type. The concept of capitalist society is not an abstraction imposed from outside; it is a reality that is at least approachable in its most advanced instance. Then comes the decisive caveat, and it is more radical than it first appears: \"Even here, though, this class articulation does not emerge in pure form. Here, too, middle and transitional levels always conceal the boundaries (although incomparably less so in the countryside than in the towns).\" This passage performs a crucial methodological operation. It denies that the conceptual purity of the three-class structure is ever empirically given. Pure class articulation is not an observed fact but a theoretical construction whose empirical realization is always obstructed. This is not an admission of failure; it is a statement about how social theory relates to its object. If the boundaries of classes were directly visible, no theory would be required to draw them. The fact that \"middle and transitional levels\" conceal them is what makes theoretical work necessary — and it is also what makes the concept of class a matter of judgment rather than measurement. The word \"always\" is emphatic and should not be softened. It does not say that middle strata are remnants of a pre-capitalist past destined to disappear; it says that the concealment of boundaries is a permanent feature of the articulation. This explains why the chapter can speak of \"middle and transitional levels\" without suggesting that they are historically transient. Transitionality is structural, not merely chronological. The parenthetical qualification — \"although incomparably less so in the countryside than in the towns\" — contains a claim that runs against most intuitive expectations and repays close scrutiny. One might assume that the countryside, with its small proprietors, its tenants, its farm servants, its village trades, is the most heterogeneous and least classically articulated region of a capitalist society, while the industrial town presents a stark polarization of employer and employee. Marx asserts the reverse: the boundaries are less concealed in the countryside. The only way to make sense of this within the fragment's own logic is by reference to the developmental law that follows and to the List quotation appended to it. In the English countryside, capitalist development had produced a particularly clean three-term structure: a landowner who owns but does not farm, a capitalist tenant farmer who farms but does not own, and an agricultural wage-labourer who neither owns nor commands. This is the triad in its purest agricultural form. In the towns, by contrast, the division of social labour produces an enormous proliferation of intermediate positions, functions, and trades — precisely what the third movement of the chapter will call the \"infinite fragmentation of interests and positions.\" The countryside, having been reorganized by capitalism into three positions, exhibits a clean structural skeleton; the towns, being the site of the most intense division of labour, exhibit a dense mesh of specializations that obscures the same skeleton. The methodological lesson is important: the purity of a class structure is not a function of its simplicity or backwardness but of the degree to which it has been subjected to the specific organizing process of capitalist development. The countryside, in Marx's England, is more classically capitalist in its articulation than the town. The Law of Development: Divorce, Concentration, and the Double Transformation The chapter's third movement supplies the genetic account. It begins with a reference backward: \"We have seen how it is the constant tendency and law of development of the capitalist mode of production...\" The phrase \"We have seen\" establishes that the account to follow is a summary of results established elsewhere in the volume rather than a new derivation. But as a summary it is extraordinarily compressed, and it must be unpacked clause by clause, because it is the fragment's only positive analytical content and therefore the only textual basis on which the missing answer to the class question can be reconstructed. The first component of the law: \"to divorce the means of production ever more from labour.\" The verb \"divorce\" (scheiden, trennen) denotes a separation of what had previously been joined — the producer from the instruments and materials of production. This separation is the defining historical rupture of capitalist development. The phrase \"ever more\" indicates that this is not an event but a process with a direction: the separation is progressive, cumulative, never complete, always intensifying. Crucially, the \"divorce\" is phrased in terms of means of production and labour , not in terms of wealth and poverty. It is a statement about the relation of producers to productive resources, not about the distribution of consumption. This distinction will become decisive for the class question. The second component: \"and to concentrate the fragmented means of production more and more into large groups.\" This is the other face of the same process. The means of production, once scattered among many small producers, aggregate into large, concentrated complexes. Note that the fragmentation of the producers and the concentration of the means are not two independent trends but a single movement described from two sides: as producers lose their grip on the instruments of production, those instruments collect into collective, large-scale units of command. The phrase \"large groups\" is deliberately non-committal about legal form; what matters is the scale and concentration of productive power as such. The third component, which draws the definitional conclusion: \"i.e. to transform labour into wage-labour and the means of production into capital.\" The \"i.e.\" signals that this clause is not a further consequence but a restatement, at the level of social form, of what has just been described. And the restatement is a definition . Wage-labour is the form that labour takes when it has been divorced from the means of production: labour that must be sold in order to be exercised. Capital is the form that the means of production take when they confront labour as alien property: means of production that have become the vehicle for appropriating the product of others' labour. This is why the chapter insists on the phrase \"owners of capital\" rather than \"owners of machines\" or \"owners of wealth.\" Capital is not a class of things; it is a social relation between persons expressed in the ownership of things. The sentence tells us that the two terms — wage-labour and capital — are correlative : neither can exist without the other, and both are constituted by the same act of separation. This is the fragment's most important positive contribution to the class question, even though the class question has not yet been posed: it establishes the relational, production-based character of the two principal classes. The fourth component introduces the third class and gives it an analysis of its own: \"And this tendency also corresponds to the independent divorce of all landed property from capital and labour.\" The word \"independent\" is doing serious work. It could mean either \"separate, i.e., distinct from the capital–labour divorce,\" or \"the divorce of landed property from both capital and labour, taking place as its own process.\" Either reading yields the same substantive point: landed property's separation is not a mere by-product of the capital–labour split. It is a sui generis transformation with its own logic, affecting both of the other classes: landed property is divorced from capital (the landowner ceases to be an operating capitalist farmer and becomes a pure rentier) and from labour (the landowner ceases to be a personal commander of agricultural workers, and the workers confront the land as alien property). The result is that the landowner is constituted as a class whose only economic function is the appropriation of a tribute — ground-rent — rather than the organization of production. The landowner's \"independence\" is thus both his separation from production and his distinctiveness as a structural position. The fifth component states the outcome of that divergence: \"or the transformation of all landed property into the form of landed property corresponding to the capitalist mode of production.\" The word \"form\" is emphatic, and so is the verb \"transformation.\" Landed property is not abolished by capitalism; it is re-formed . Feudal, seigneurial, peasant, and communal forms of property in land give way to a distinctively capitalist form. That form is precisely the one that makes ground-rent — rather than the landlord's own farming profit — the landlord's characteristic revenue. In other words, the third \"great class\" of the opening sentence is not a survival from the past but a product of capitalist development. It is the class of those whose property has been transformed so that it can function as a claim on surplus value generated by others. This sequence of five components constitutes the fragment's positive theory of how the three classes come into being. The remarkable fact is that nowhere in this sequence does income figure. The constituting process is described entirely in terms of the separation of producers from productive resources, the concentration of those resources, and the consequent reformation of economic forms. Only after the account of the law is complete does the chapter turn to revenues and to the question of class. The architecture of the fragment thus silently testifies to where Marx locates the constitutive principle — and it is not the revenue. The List Footnote: Owner-Management as an Index of Backwardness Attached to the phrase \"the independent divorce of all landed property from capital and labour\" is a footnote of considerable analytical interest, quoting Friedrich List's Die Ackerverfassung, die Zwergwirthschaft und die Auswanderung (1842): \"The predominance of owner-management on large estates simply indicates inadequate civilization, means of communication, local industry and wealthy cities. This is why we find it all over Russia, Poland, Hungary and Mecklenburg. It was formerly predominant in England too, but with the arrival of trade and industry these estates were broken up into middle-sized farms and leased out.\" The footnote functions as historical corroboration of the \"independent divorce\" thesis, but its content deserves careful exposition because it supplies a criterion that the chapter's main text leaves implicit. List's argument is that owner-management — the situation in which the owner of a large estate also manages and farms it — is a symptom, not a virtue. The four conditions listed as its cause are all marks of an undeveloped division of labour and an incomplete urban-industrial articulation: \"inadequate civilization,\" in the specific sense of cultural and institutional development; \"means of communication,\" i.e., transport infrastructure connecting farm regions to markets; \"local industry,\" i.e., non-agricultural production in the vicinity; and \"wealthy cities,\" i.e., concentrated urban demand able to absorb agricultural output. This reverses a conventional hierarchy of value. In much agrarian ideology, the resident gentleman-farmer who personally oversees his acres is presented as the ideal of landed responsibility; the absentee rentier is the object of moral complaint. List, and Marx by adoption, treats the personal manager as the index of an economy too poorly developed to support a division of functions. This is not a moral judgment but a structural diagnostic: where markets, communications, and cities are absent, land must be consolidated under a single managing proprietor; where they are present, land can be let in farms to capitalistically organizing tenants. The geographical enumeration — Russia, Poland, Hungary, Mecklenburg — names regions of the European east and northeast, characterized in the nineteenth century by large-scale estate agriculture with unfree or semi-free labour and by weak urban-industrial development. The English contrast — \"It was formerly predominant in England too\" — is instructive for two reasons. First, it historicizes the English case: owner-management, now largely superseded in England, was once the norm. The structure the chapter theorizes is not a natural consequence of large landownership but a historical achievement of capitalist development in a favourable environment. Second, the phrase \"with the arrival of trade and industry\" identifies the causal mechanism: commerce and manufacturing, not legal reform or ideology, dissolved the internally managed estate. The dissolution takes the specific form of \"broken up into middle-sized farms and leased out.\" These last two points are essential for the chapter's argument. \"Broken up\" and \"leased out\" describe two moments of the transformation that constitutes the third great class. The estate's scale is subdivided into farming units, and those units are handed over to tenants — a class of capitalist farmers, occupying the intermediate position between the landowner and the labourers. The result is the exact three-term structure that the chapter describes as \"more classically\" articulated in the countryside than in the towns: the landowner who owns and receives rent, the tenant farmer who farms and takes profit, the agricultural labourer who works and earns wages. The footnote thus supplies, in miniature, the empirical history of how the third of the three great classes acquired its characteristic form: by being driven out of production , and thereby constituted as the recipient of ground-rent alone. It is worth noticing the ironic relation between List's politics and his evidence. The editorial gloss appended to the footnote explains who List was: \"the most important German economist of the first half of the nineteenth century, closely represented the demands of the embryonic industrial bourgeoisie in Germany and is particularly remembered for his forceful arguments for protective tariffs.\" This note performs several services. It identifies List as a partisan — a spokesman of an industrial class — rather than a disinterested observer, and it identifies his program: tariffs, and by implication the development of domestic industry, trade, and urban markets. The irony is that the very program List advocated — the promotion of industry and commerce — is the dynamic whose effect he himself describes as dissolving owner-management and transforming estates into leased farms. List the advocate of industrialization and List the observer of agrarian transformation are the same figure, and Marx uses one against the political presuppositions of the other while accepting his factual testimony. The footnote is thus also a rhetorical lesson in how to use a witness: rely on what the witness saw, not on what he wanted. Finally, the footnote's apparatus — numbered 58, with an asterisked editorial gloss — calls attention to the text's status as a posthumously edited manuscript. This is not a trivial matter. The chapter's footnotes are the editor's, the interruptions are the editor's, and the boundary between Marx's text and the editorial frame is itself part of the object we are reading. The List gloss tells us how the volume's editor expected readers to situate a nineteenth-century German economist; the closing note, \"(At this point the manuscript breaks off. — F. E.)\", will tell us that what we have is a fragment. The chapter is thus doubly mediated: by its incompleteness and by the editorial apparatus that frames the incompleteness. The Pivot: \"What Makes a Class?\" and the Priority of the Formative Question With the developmental account complete, the chapter turns abruptly to its central question: \"The question to be answered next is: 'What makes a class?'\" Every word of this short sentence repays attention. \"The question to be answered next\" presents the question not as an afterthought but as an item on a program: it is the next in a sequence of systematically ordered inquiries. The chapter is therefore the opening of a section , not a stray reflection. And the question itself — \"What makes a class?\" — is a question about constitution , not about description . It is not \"How do we identify a class?\" but \"What produces a class?\" The verb \"makes\" asks for a generative principle. Immediately, Marx adds a second formulation and asserts a relation of derivation between the two: \"and this arises automatically from answering another question: 'What makes wage-labourers, capitalists and landowners the formative elements of the three great social classes?'\" The claim that the first question \"arises automatically\" from the second is a compressed statement of methodological priority. Marx is saying that the general question about class is not prior to the specific question about these three groups; rather, the general concept is to be read off the explanation of why these particular groups are formative. This is a substantively important position. It implies that \"class\" is not a master category into which the three great groups are inserted, but a name for whatever it is that constitutes those groups as the structure-bearing elements of capitalist society. There is no transhistorical definition to be applied; there is a specific historical formation to be explained, and the general concept will be the conceptual form of that explanation. The word \"formative\" is equally significant. The German term behind it designates what gives form or shape. The three great groups are not merely the largest or the most numerous; they are the elements that form the structure — the elements whose relations constitute the shape of society as a whole. This is why the criterion cannot be demographic, nor can it be a mere matter of size or wealth. To be a \"formative element\" is to occupy a position in the constitution of the social whole. When the chapter later criticizes the revenue-source criterion, the standard of judgment it invokes is precisely this one: only a criterion that could explain formative status would be adequate, and the revenue criterion cannot. There is also a potential circularity in the passage, which is worth flagging as a nuance. The second question — what makes these three groups the formative classes? — appears to presume the very classification it is meant to explain. The opening sentence already asserted that they are the three great classes. How can the explanation of that assertion avoid circularity? The answer must lie in the developmental account already given: the three groups have been shown to arise from a single process of separation and reformation, and it is in that genesis, rather than in their classification, that the explanation must be sought. But the fragment does not complete the circle, and a careful reader should note that the \"automatic\" derivation Marx promises is never delivered. The Answer \"At First Sight\": The Identity of Revenues and Revenue Sources Marx next states the lay answer with full seriousness: \"At first sight, the identity of revenues and revenue sources.\" He then unfolds it: \"For these are three great social groups whose components, the individuals forming them, live respectively from wages, profit and ground-rent, from the valorization of their labour-power, capital and landed property.\" Two levels are distinguished. The first is the level of livelihood: the individuals who compose each group \"live respectively from\" one of the three revenues. This is the everyday criterion — how do you make your living? The second level is that of sources: wages, profit, and ground-rent are described as returns from \"the valorization of their labour-power, capital and landed property.\" The term \"valorization\" ( Verwertung ) is the technical term of the volume's analysis: the process by which value is put to work so as to yield a surplus, an increment. Thus labour-power is valorized when it is set to work and reproduces more value than it costs; capital is valorized when the advance returns with a profit; landed property is valorized when it yields ground-rent as the price of access to the soil. The elegance of this schema cannot be overstated, and Marx's willingness to state it fully, without immediate qualification, is a mark of its explanatory attractiveness. Three property forms correspond to three valorization processes, which correspond to three revenues, which correspond to three social groups. It is a complete, reciprocal, and empirically applicable classification. Moreover, it is precisely the schema that the entire volume has been laboriously establishing. The reader who has just finished the analysis of wages, profit, and rent has been prepared to accept this schema as the natural summary of the whole work. The phrase \"at first sight\" is thus more than a conversational hedge: it marks the answer as belonging to the order of appearance — the level of the phenomenal forms in which the social relations of production present themselves to immediate observation. This is a critical point for interpretation. Marx does not dismiss the distributive schema as false. He treats it as the surface form of the relations, a form that is not arbitrary but generated by the underlying structure. The three revenues do exist, and they do correspond to three propertied groups. What is at issue is whether this correspondence can serve as the definition of class — whether the appearance can be the concept. The stage is now set for the reversal, and the reversal will not be based on external evidence but purely on the internal logic of the criterion: if the criterion is the identity of revenue with source, then the criterion must apply consistently, and consistency will destroy the triad. The Counterexample: Doctors and Government Officials \"From this point of view, however, doctors and government officials would also form two classes, as they belong to two distinct social groups, the revenue of each group's members flowing from its own source.\" The argument is a reductio ad absurdum , executed with surgical economy. The phrase \"From this point of view\" explicitly relativizes the argument to the criterion under examination: it is not Marx's own standpoint, but the standpoint of the revenue-identity criterion, that is being followed to its consequences. The consequence is immediate: doctors and government officials satisfy every element of the criterion. They constitute two distinct social groups; each group's members derive their income from a source peculiar to it; and therefore, by the criterion, they would be classes. The force of the counterexample lies in the fact that it is so obviously not what anyone means by a class. Doctors and officials are professions or functions ; the very suggestion that they constitute two of the great classes of modern society exposes the criterion as absurd. But the argument is not merely rhetorical. Consider what the criterion actually does. It equates classes with categories of income recipients. Any category of income recipients, however defined and however constituted, would then be a class. The class structure of society becomes a catalogue of income types — which is precisely the kind of accounting classification the criterion was apparently meant to transcend. The choice of examples is also analytically pointed. Doctors and officials are not random; they are the two most conspicuous cases of income derived from labour services rendered to others (doctors) and from state revenue (officials). Both are structurally distinct from the three great classes, and both are, in the terminology the chapter has already used, part of the \"middle and transitional levels\" that conceal the boundaries. That the revenue criterion converts these middle levels into full-blown classes is a further indictment: the criterion not only overgenerates classes, it overgenerates them precisely at the point where the analysis of capitalist society most needs a way of distinguishing middle levels from formative classes. The criterion makes the problem of the middle strata unsolvable by treating them as coequal with the principal classes. There is a deeper point in the counterexample that the text does not spell out but that its own logic presses upon the reader. The income of the officials — the \"source\" that flows \"from its own source\" — is, in the schema of the volume, not a primary revenue at all. It is a share of the surplus value already appropriated by others, redistributed through taxation. The text's characterization of official revenue as flowing from \"its own source\" quietly presupposes that an income stream can be treated as a source in its own right. Whether a revenue is a primary claim on production or a derived one is precisely the question that a rigorous class analysis would have to settle before the revenue criterion could be applied; and that question cannot be settled at the level of revenue at all. It requires the analysis of production and of the distribution of surplus value. So even in failing, the counterexample reveals what an adequate criterion would have to be able to do: distinguish primary from derived revenues, structural from incidental income streams, the population of functionaries from the formative classes. The revenue criterion cannot do any of this. Finally, note the structure of the argument. Marx does not say that doctors and officials have similar revenues to workers or that they are really workers. He says the criterion, taken seriously, would have to call them classes. The argument is purely about the criterion's implications, not about the social reality of the groups in question. This is the hallmark of a properly philosophical refutation: it shows that a principle cannot be held without embracing consequences that its own adherents reject. Infinite Fragmentation: Vineyards, Fields, Forests, Mines, Fisheries The second and more radical criticism follows immediately: \"The same would hold true for the infinite fragmentation of interests and positions into which the division of social labour splits not only workers but also capitalists and landowners — the latter, for instance, into vineyard-owners, field-owners, forest-owners, mine-owners, fishery-owners, etc.\" Where the first counterexample attacked the criterion from outside the three great classes — showing that it overgenerates classes among non-class groups — the second attacks it from within , showing that it dissolves the classes it is supposed to define. If the criterion is \"the identity of revenue and revenue source,\" then it must be applied inside each of the three great groups as well, for the division of social labour has differentiated those groups internally, giving rise to distinct interests and positions, and in many cases to distinct revenue streams. The phrase \"the infinite fragmentation of interests and positions\" identifies two dimensions of division: \"interests,\" i.e., the material stakes and aims of the groups in question; and \"positions,\" i.e., their locations in the structure of social labour. Both are products of \"the division of social labour.\" This is an important conceptual link, because it identifies the same mechanism that generates the abundance of urban middle levels mentioned earlier. The division of labour does not divide only labour; it divides all social positions. And the text emphasizes that it \"splits not only workers but also capitalists and landowners.\" The qualification \"not only workers\" is essential. It answers any objection that fragmentation is the fate of the working class alone, and it generalizes the analytical problem: no class escapes the differentiating pressure of the division of labour. The illustrative list is drawn entirely from the landowning class, and its items repay attention: \"vineyard-owners, field-owners, forest-owners, mine-owners, fishery-owners.\" Several things are being communicated. First, the list reveals the enormous material breadth of what \"landed property\" designates. It is not merely agricultural land but vineyards, woodland, mineral deposits, and fisheries — a diversity of natural resources whose modes of valorization, markets, and technological requirements differ radically. Second, the list shows that even within the supposed unity of \"landowners,\" there are distinct revenue streams and distinct social functions: viticulture, arable farming, forestry, mining, and fishing are different industries with different rhythms and different relations to the market. Third, the sequence moves from the agricultural to the extractive and maritime, demonstrating that the criterion would fragment the landlord class far beyond a simple agricultural/urban distinction. Fourth, the list ends with \"etc.,\" a small but powerful rhetorical device: the enumeration is deliberately left open, enacting the infinitude of the fragmentation it describes. The \"etc.\" is the criterion's death warrant, not a stylistic convenience. The consequence for the concept of class is devastating. If every distinct interest and position generated by the division of social labour counts as a distinct class, then there are not three classes but an indefinite number. The concept of class becomes an instrument of infinite subdivision, and loses precisely what it was introduced to supply: a small set of structural categories adequate to the analysis of a determinate form of society. The criterion does not merely fail to define class; it abolishes the possibility of the concept. There is a further, subtler implication. The passage makes the \"infinite fragmentation\" a property of all the great classes, not merely the landowners. The examples are drawn only from landowners because they are the most vivid, but the principle holds across the board. Consequently, any answer to the class question that relies on the internal homogeneity of a class must fail, because no such homogeneity exists. This raises the standard the positive answer would have to meet: it would have to explain how a class can be one despite internal differentiation — how vineyard-owners and fishery-owners and mine-owners can belong to a single class. That question, which the fragment poses by implication, can only be answered by identifying something that unites them despite the diversity of their interests and revenues: their common position in relation to the fundamental social relation of production — in relation to labour on the one side and to capital on the other. The fragment does not say this; but the critique it mounts is unintelligible unless some such unifying principle is available, and the only candidate the chapter's own preceding analysis supplies is the relation to the means of production and to the labour that works with them. The Break-Off: The Fragment's Silence and Its Interpretive Weight The chapter ends: \"(At this point the manuscript breaks off. — F. E.)\" The initials identify Friedrich Engels, the editor of the posthumous volumes. This parenthetical note is the last thing on the page, and it transforms the chapter from an argument into a document of an interrupted argument. Everything that follows in the interpretation of this chapter must respect the break. We possess, in the surviving fragment, a negative result of the greatest importance and a set of positive materials — the developmental law, the relational definitions of wage-labour and capital, the reformation of landed property — that were evidently intended to be used in the constructive answer. We do not possess the constructive answer. Any reconstruction of the answer must be flagged as reconstruction, not as the text. What is nevertheless secure from the fragment itself can be stated with precision. First, the distributive criterion of class is refuted, by two independent and mutually reinforcing arguments: it overgenerates classes among non-class groups, and it fragments the three great classes into an indefinite plurality. Second, the question of class has been given a specific shape: it asks after the formative elements of a determinate mode of production, and it expects the general concept of class to be derived from the specific explanation, not applied to it. Third, the chapter has, prior to posing the question, sketched a process — the separation of producers from the means of production, the concentration of those means, and the independent reformation of landed property — which constitutes precisely the three property forms named in the opening sentence. Fourth, and most tellingly, that process is described in a vocabulary that contains no reference to income whatsoever. The fragment's own architecture therefore indicates the direction of the missing answer. There is an uncomfortable further possibility which must be acknowledged as a possibility and not asserted as a fact: that the chapter broke off not merely by accident but at a point of genuine theoretical difficulty. The problem of defining class at the level of production relations confronts the very \"middle and transitional levels\" that the chapter's second movement identified as always concealing the boundaries. If class membership is determined by relation to the means of production, what is the class position of the doctor, the official, the small shopkeeper, the self-employed professional? The fragment's critique shows that income cannot answer this; whether the intended production-based answer could have answered it definitively is something the surviving text does not show. The chapter's silence on this front is part of what it transmits to its readers. Finally, the break-off gives the chapter a peculiar status in the argument of the volume. It is the point at which the economic analysis of the capitalist mode of production, complete in all its essential determinations, was to have been converted into an analysis of the classes that live within it. That conversion never occurs. What remains is an enacted demonstration that the conversion cannot be made by the easy route — that the theory of the three revenues, however complete, does not by itself yield a theory of the three classes. Anyone who wishes to move from the volume's economic analysis to a class analysis must therefore do work that the text does not do, and must do it under the constraint that the fragment imposes: the work cannot begin from income. 3. SYNTHESIS, LESSONS & BROADER IMPLICATIONS The deepest lesson of Chapter 52 is a lesson about the difference between the appearance and the constitution of social structure. The chapter begins with a schema — three properties, three revenues, three classes — that is at once empirically serviceable, theoretically well-prepared by the preceding analysis of the volume, and entirely inadequate as a definition. What makes it inadequate is not error but superficiality: it describes the way a class society distributes its product rather than the way it produces its positions . Income is what a class receives; it is not what a class is. And because distribution is downstream of production, a criterion drawn from distribution will always cut the social body along lines that cross the seams of its actual constitution — producing classes where there are only trades, and multiplying classes where there is one. From this the fragment yields a series of connected principles. First: the priority of production over distribution as the axis of class analysis. The chapter's own developmental passage establishes that classes are constituted by relations to the means of production: labour becomes wage-labour and means of production become capital in the same movement that divorces producers from the instruments of their work. The revenues — wages, profit, rent — are the forms in which those positions receive shares of the product. But the positions exist, and are what they are, prior to and independently of the revenues they receive. To define the class by the revenue is to mistake the effect for the cause. This is the systematic content of Marx's rejection of the \"identity of revenues and revenue sources\" as a definition of class. Second: class is a relational and historically specific category, not a gradational one. The opening sentence's insistence that these are the classes of \"modern society based on the capitalist mode of production\" excludes any reading of the triad as a transhistorical scheme of stratification. Wage-labour and capital are correlative forms produced by a single historical process of separation and concentration; landed property in its classically capitalist form is likewise a produced, transformed relation. This relationality has an important consequence: the classes are not independently definable groups that happen to interact; they are mutually constituted positions whose very identities depend on their terms. This is why the internal fragmentation of a class — vineyard-owners and mine-owners and fishery-owners — is not fatal to its unity in the way the fragmentation argument would suggest if the class were merely an aggregate defined by income. A relational class can be internally differentiated in its interests and still be one, because its unity resides in its common position within the fundamental relation, not in the homogeneity of its revenues. Third: the empirical inexistence of pure class structures is a permanent theoretical constraint, not a temporary imperfection. The chapter's \"middle and transitional levels\" that \"always conceal the boundaries\" are not residues awaiting the completion of capitalist development. They are the consequence of the division of social labour, which differentiates all positions without exception. Class theory must therefore always be an exercise in drawing boundaries that are not given in experience — an interpretive and structural operation, not a descriptive catalogue. This also supplies one of the fragment's most striking analytic results: the countryside, in an advanced capitalist country, may exhibit a cleaner class articulation than the town, because the reorganization of agriculture into owner, tenant capitalist, and labourer has produced a three-term structure, while the town's division of labour produces a dense mesh of intermediate positions. Purity of class structure is a function of capitalist reorganization, not of backwardness. Fourth: concepts must be tested by their consequences, not by their plausibility. The chapter's refutation of the revenue criterion proceeds entirely by extrapolation. The criterion is not rejected because it fails to fit the facts — it fits the three great classes admirably. It is rejected because when carried through consistently it generates consequences that destroy its own purpose. This is a model of critical method: take the criterion seriously, follow it to its limits, and observe whether it can survive the journey. It cannot. Doctors and officials expose its overgeneration; vineyards and fisheries expose its self-dissolution. The lesson generalizes to every attempt to define fundamental social categories by their surface effects. Fifth: the chapter's incompleteness is itself a substantive finding. The interruption of the manuscript at the exact moment between the demolition of the false answer and the construction of the true one means that the text transmits a fully formed question and only the materials of an answer. This has consequences for readers. It forbids the easy attribution to Marx of a completed theory of class derived from this chapter alone, and it requires anyone who would complete the fragment to work from the relational and production-based materials the chapter itself provides — the divorce of labour from the means of production, the correlative constitution of wage-labour and capital, the transformation of landed property into its capitalist form. The fragment is thus not a summary at the end of the volume but an assignment to its readers. The broader implications are considerable. Politically, the chapter implies that the interests and alignments of social groups cannot be read off income distributions; the \"middle levels\" that dominate the visible surface of capitalist society are not, for that reason, self-standing classes, and their political behaviour cannot be inferred from their pay. Theoretically, the chapter sets an exacting standard for any theory of social structure: it must specify a constitutive mechanism, not a classificatory schema; it must be able to distinguish the formative from the merely statistical; and it must explain unity in the face of internal differentiation. Philosophically, finally, the fragment is a lesson about the relation between essence and appearance in social analysis. The three revenues are not illusions. They are real, and their correspondence to three groups of recipients is real. What is illusory is the belief that the real surface exhausts the real structure — that the face society shows to its members is the whole of what constitutes it. Marx's last chapter on class is thus, at bottom, a defence of the necessity of theory: the most convincing answer to the question \"what makes a class?\" is the one that, having been stated in full and given every advantage, cannot survive the company of a doctor and a government official, a vineyard and a fishery. ← Previous Chapter Volume 3 • Chapter 51 Dashboard 🏠 All Chapters"}];